pmc:AegyptiaArcanumAmenti a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Vollziehe dies vor keinem Menschen, denn es ist ein überaus großes Geheimnis"@de,
        "Perform this before no human being, because it is an exceedingly great mystery"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 137A, rubric, Papyrus of Nu, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London 1901, vol. II, pp. 417-418",
        "Book of the Dead, chapter 161, rubric, from the Turin text, in the English of Peter le Page Renouf and Édouard Naville, The Egyptian Book of the Dead, London: Society of Biblical Archaeology, 1904" ;
    skos:definition "The secrecy clause of the same rubric: thou shalt not perform this ceremony before any human being except thine own self, or thy father, or thy son, because it is an exceedingly great mystery of Amentet and is a type of the hidden things of the underworld. The rubric adds that these things shall be done secretly in the underworld, and that they are mysteries of Neter-khert."@en ;
    skos:note "No consequence is stated for breaking the clause in either translation. That is the ordinary state of this corpus and is recorded rather than filled in."@en,
        "The exception is the finding, and it is edition-dependent. Budge admits the father and the son to the ceremony; Renouf, translating the Turin text under chapter 161, has the same clause exclude them by name — reveal it to no one, may he be thy father or thy son, except thyself. The two readings put the boundary of the transmission in different places, one at the lineage and one at the single person, and no reading of the Egyptian was available to this run to decide between them. The node therefore reports both and asserts neither."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:AegyptiaQuattuorFlammae ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmc:AegyptiaAriEmAbF a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Ari-em-ab-f"@de,
        "Ari-em-ab-f"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 125, the address to the forty-two gods, address 35, Papyrus of Ani, in the transliteration and English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London: British Museum, 1895, plates XXXI-XXXII, pp. 198-203",
        "Budge, A Hieroglyphic Vocabulary to the Theban Recension of the Book of the Dead, London: Kegan Paul, 1911, s.v. Ari-em-ab-f, p. 52" ;
    skos:altLabel "Ari-en-ab-f (Vocabulary, 1911)"@en ;
    skos:definition "The thirty-fifth address of chapter 125 in the Papyrus of Ani. Budge renders the name working in his heart. The god is addressed as coming forth from Tebu, and the address is bound to one denial: I have not fouled water."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:nameForm "ari-em-ab-f" ;
    pm:nameRole "one of the forty-two gods of the hall of the two Maati" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmc:AegyptiaAriNefTchesef a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Ari-nef-tchesef"@de,
        "Ari-nef-tchesef"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 99, the names of the parts of the boat, Papyrus of Nu (British Museum No. 10,477, sheets 21 and 22), in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II, pp. 299-300",
        "Budge, A Hieroglyphic Vocabulary to the Theban Recension of the Book of the Dead, London: Kegan Paul, 1911, s.v. Ari-nef-tchesef" ;
    skos:definition "One of the eight names answered together when the planks in the hull say tell us our names. The eight are given in one breath and in one order: Mesthi, Hapi, Tuamautef, Qebh-sennuf, Haqau, Thet-em-Aua, Maa-an-tef, Ari-nef-tchesef. The first four are the sons of Horus, who are named elsewhere in the corpus; the last four are named here and, so far as this run could find, nowhere else in it."@en ;
    skos:note "Budge glosses the name in the chapter: he who made himself. His vocabulary to the same recension carries the headword separately and gives it a different sense — name of a plank or peg in the magical boat — so the volume that elsewhere identifies a bearer here identifies a piece of timber. The two readings are recorded side by side and neither is preferred."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:nameForm "ari-nef-tchesef" ;
    pm:nameRole "one of the eight planks in the hull of the boat of chapter 99" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmc:AegyptiaAritaeEfficacia a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Rezitation an den sieben Arits bringt den ungehinderten Durchgang hervor"@de,
        "The recitation at the seven arits yields unimpeded passage"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 147, closing rubric of the Papyrus of Thenna, printed as a footnote in E. A. Wallis Budge, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II, p. 479" ;
    skos:note "Entered as the claim of the transmission and not as a finding. The claim names a condition — that the words be recited at the seven arits — and an outcome, and it names no term, no sign and no way for the outcome to fail. It is the standard shape of this corpus; see pmc:AegyptiaRubricaScientiae."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AegyptiaSeptemAritae ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AegyptiaAritaeTransitus .

pmc:AegyptiaAtekTauKehaqKheru a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Atek-tau-kehaq-kheru"@de,
        "Atek-tau-kehaq-kheru"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 147, the sixth arit, Papyrus of Ani (British Museum No. 10,470, sheets 11 and 12), in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II, pp. 473-479" ;
    skos:definition "One of the three officers of the sixth of the seven arits. The chapter gives the three names and nothing else about them: the dead man is told the name of the doorkeeper, the name of the watcher and the name of the herald, and then says his address."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
