    pm:nameRole "doorkeeper of the sixth arit" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmc:AegyptiaAtesHraAriShe a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Ates-hra-ari-she"@de,
        "Ates-hra-ari-she"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 147, the sixth arit, Papyrus of Ani (British Museum No. 10,470, sheets 11 and 12), in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II, pp. 473-479" ;
    skos:definition "One of the three officers of the sixth of the seven arits. The chapter gives the three names and nothing else about them: the dead man is told the name of the doorkeeper, the name of the watcher and the name of the herald, and then says his address."@en ;
    skos:note "Budge prints Ates-hra-[ari]-she; the middle element is his restoration and stands in square brackets in the edition."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:nameForm "ates-hra-ari-she" ;
    pm:nameRole "herald of the sixth arit" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmc:AegyptiaBasti a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Basti"@de,
        "Basti"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 125, the address to the forty-two gods, address 13, Papyrus of Ani, in the transliteration and English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London: British Museum, 1895, plates XXXI-XXXII, pp. 198-203",
        "Budge, A Hieroglyphic Vocabulary to the Theban Recension of the Book of the Dead, London: Kegan Paul, 1911, s.v. Basti, p. 129" ;
    skos:definition "The thirteenth address of chapter 125 in the Papyrus of Ani. Budge carries the name over untranslated. The god is addressed as coming forth from the secret place, and the address is bound to one denial: I have not eaten my heart."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:nameForm "basti" ;
    pm:nameRole "one of the forty-two gods of the hall of the two Maati" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmc:AegyptiaBennuTransformatio a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Verwandlung in den Bennu-Vogel, und was sie erlaubt"@de,
        "Transformation into the Bennu bird, and what it permits"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 83, with its rubric, Papyrus of Nu, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London 1901, vol. II, pp. 268-269" ;
    skos:definition "The speaker declares that he has germinated like the things which germinate, that he is of the germs of every god, that he is Yesterday of the four quarters and of the seven uraei which came into existence in Amentet, that he has come by day and risen in the footsteps of the gods, and that he is the god Khensu who driveth back all that oppose him. The rubric states what the state permits: he shall come forth pure by day after his death, he shall perform whatsoever transformations his heart desireth, he shall be in the following of Un-nefer, he shall be satisfied with the food of Osiris and with sepulchral meals, he shall see the disk, he shall be in good case upon earth before Ra, he shall be triumphant before Osiris, and no evil thing whatsoever shall have dominion over him for ever and ever."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered singly and not in a list with the other transformations, because the harvest rule counts capacities strictly and because these are not one claim restated: each spell names a different form and a different set of permissions. Of the thirteen transformation spells only three were read at the wording in this run, and only two of those carry a rubric of their own."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmc:AegyptiaErtatSebanqa a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Ertat-Sebanqa"@de,
        "Ertat-Sebanqa"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 146, the third pylon, Papyrus of Nu (British Museum No. 10,477, sheet 25), in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II, pp. 464-472" ;
    skos:definition "The doorkeeper of the third of the pylons of chapter 146. The dead man says that he knows the pylon, its name and the name of the god who guards it; the pylon's own name is then given as a long epithet, and the doorkeeper's name is given last and alone."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:nameForm "ertat-sebanqa" ;
    pm:nameRole "doorkeeper of the third pylon of the house of Osiris in Sekhet-Aanru" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmc:AegyptiaExaminatioRerum a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Namensprüfung ist die allgemeine Form des Korpus, nicht die Form seiner Tore"@de,
        "The naming examination is the corpus's general form, not the form of its gates"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-19" ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapters 99, 125, 146 and 147, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II, pp. 297-302, 375-377, 464-472 and 473-479" ;
    skos:definition "Four places in the corpus put the same examination, and only two of them are gates. At the hall of the two Maati every element of the doorway refuses passage unless told its name. At the seven arits three officers are named at each. At the secret pylons a god and, for the first ten, a doorkeeper are named. And in chapter 99 the parts of a boat ask, one after another, to be told their names. The common form is exact: the thing addressed asks, the answer is supplied in the same passage, and the knowing of the whole is what the attached rubric rewards."@en ;
    skos:note "Systematizing rather than generalizing, and the distinction matters here. This is one tradition's arrangement of its own material, put together from four places in one corpus, and it is asserted as the shape of that corpus and of nothing else. A claim about transmissions in general would need witnesses outside this one and a counter-search behind it; that is the work at generalizations/ and it is not done here."@en,
        "The compiler named below is this project and not an Egyptian. The four places are the corpus's; putting them in one row is an act of this holding, dated, and the class exists so that a reader is not left to mistake the row for a testimony."@en,
        "What this settles about the first two files. The forty-two and the twenty-one were entered as the names of gatekeepers. They are better read as instances of a procedure the corpus applies to whatever the dead man must get past or use, gods and rigging alike, and the procedure is the object worth holding. See pmc:AegyptiaRubricaScientiae for what the knowing is said to obtain and pmc:AegyptiaPortaeNomina for the one place where the failure is stated."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:compiledBy "prima-materia" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmc:AegyptiaFenti a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Fenti"@de,
        "Fenti"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 125, the address to the forty-two gods, address 3, Papyrus of Ani, in the transliteration and English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London: British Museum, 1895, plates XXXI-XXXII, pp. 198-203",
        "Budge, A Hieroglyphic Vocabulary to the Theban Recension of the Book of the Dead, London: Kegan Paul, 1911, s.v. Fenti, p. 157" ;
