    skos:definition "The twenty-sixth address of chapter 125 in the Papyrus of Ani. Budge renders the name babe. The god is addressed as coming forth from Uab, and the address is bound to one denial: I have not made myself deaf to the words of right and truth."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:nameForm "nekhennu" ;
    pm:nameRole "one of the forty-two gods of the hall of the two Maati" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmc:AegyptiaPuritasOperantis a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Diese Dinge soll ein Mann vollziehen, der rein und zeremoniell lauter ist"@de,
        "These things shall be performed by a man who is clean and ceremonially pure"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 137A, the fourth of the four appended brick rubrics, Papyrus of Nu, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II" ;
    skos:definition "The condition attached to the four brick rubrics of chapter 137A, stated once at the end of the fourth and governing all of them: these things shall be performed by a man who is clean and is ceremonially pure, one who hath eaten neither meat nor fish, and who hath not recently had intercourse with women; and offerings of cakes and ale shall be made and incense burnt on the fire of these gods."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at prescribed order rather than at stated necessity, for the same reason as pmc:AegyptiaPuritasRecitantis: three abstinences are named and no consequence of breaking any of them is stated. The two conditions are worded almost identically in chapters 64 and 137A, which makes the shared silence a feature of the form and not of one passage."@en,
        "The condition attaches to the four bricks and not to the four flames, although the two sets stand in the same chapter and the flames carry the secrecy clause instead. The corpus divides its conditions between the two halves of one chapter rather than applying both to both."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:AegyptiaLaterSeptentrionalis ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:AegyptiaLaterOccidentalis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmc:AegyptiaPuritasRecitantis a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Zu sprechen von einem, der rein ist und weder Fleisch noch Fisch gegessen noch Frauen berührt hat"@de,
        "Recited by one who is clean and hath not eaten flesh or fish or touched women"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 125, rubric, Papyrus of Nu, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London 1901, vol. II, pp. 377-378",
        "Book of the Dead, chapter 64, the three rubrics, Papyrus of Nu and Papyrus of Mes-em-neter, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London 1901, vol. I, pp. 217-218 and 221-222" ;
    skos:definition "The condition attached to chapter 64 in all three of its rubrics: this chapter shall be recited by a man who is ceremonially clean and pure, who hath not eaten the flesh of animals or fish, and who hath not had intercourse with women. Chapter 125 sets a longer list of the same kind before its own recitation: cleansed and purified, arrayed in apparel, shod with white leather sandals, the eyes painted with antimony, the body anointed with unguent of anti, and offerings of oxen, fowl, incense, cakes, ale and garden herbs; and the representation is to be drawn on a new tile moulded from earth upon which neither a pig nor other animals have trodden."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity, and the reason is the finding. The corpus sets the condition three times over in the same chapter and never says what follows if it is broken. A reader who took the strength from the insistence rather than from the wording would enter a necessity the text does not state."@en,
        "The tile untrodden by a pig is the one condition in the passage that a second person could have checked before the fact. Everything else on the list is a state of the reciter."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:AegyptiaRubricaScientiae ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:AegyptiaPeretEmHeru ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmc:AegyptiaPylonorumNomina a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die geheimen Pylone: was an jedem gewusst wird, und wo der Pförtner aufhört"@de,
        "The secret pylons: what is known at each, and where the doorkeeper stops"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 146, Papyrus of Nu (British Museum No. 10,477, sheet 25), in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II, pp. 464-472" ;
    skos:definition "The form of chapter 146. At each pylon of the house of Osiris in Sekhet-Aanru the dead man says that he has made his way, that he knows the pylon, that he knows its name, and that he knows the name of the god who guards it. The pylon's name is then given as a long epithet — lady of tremblings with lofty walls, fire the lady of flames, blazing fire whose flame cannot be quenched — and after it, for the first ten pylons only, the name of the doorkeeper."@en ;
    skos:note "How many pylons there are depends on the manuscript, and Budge says so in a footnote at the eleventh: the Papyrus of Ani enumerates ten only, the version Naville published has twenty vignettes, and the Turin papyrus gives fifteen. The chapter as printed runs to twenty-one. This is the same finding as the one at pmp:EgyptianAssessorNameSearch in another place — the number is a property of the copy and not of the thing counted."@en,
        "The chapter has two halves and the seam is at the eleventh pylon. Up to the tenth the dead man knows the name of the god who guardeth you and a doorkeeper is named. From the eleventh he knows instead the name of her who is within thee, no doorkeeper is named at all, and every pylon closes with the same fixed clause: she hath the judgment of the feeble bandaged one. Two different things have been joined under one chapter number, and the join is visible in the wording."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmc:AegyptiaQebhSennuf a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Qebh-sennuf"@de,
        "Qebh-sennuf"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 99, the names of the parts of the boat, Papyrus of Nu (British Museum No. 10,477, sheets 21 and 22), in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II, pp. 299-300" ;
    skos:definition "One of the eight names answered together when the planks in the hull say tell us our names. The eight are given in one breath and in one order: Mesthi, Hapi, Tuamautef, Qebh-sennuf, Haqau, Thet-em-Aua, Maa-an-tef, Ari-nef-tchesef. The first four are the sons of Horus, who are named elsewhere in the corpus; the last four are named here and, so far as this run could find, nowhere else in it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:nameForm "qebh-sennuf" ;
    pm:nameRole "one of the eight planks in the hull of the boat of chapter 99" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmc:AegyptiaQererti a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Qererti"@de,
        "Qererti"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 125, the address to the forty-two gods, address 11, Papyrus of Ani, in the transliteration and English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London: British Museum, 1895, plates XXXI-XXXII, pp. 198-203",
        "Budge, A Hieroglyphic Vocabulary to the Theban Recension of the Book of the Dead, London: Kegan Paul, 1911, s.v. Qerti, p. 418" ;
    skos:altLabel "Qerti (Vocabulary, 1911)"@en ;
    skos:definition "The eleventh address of chapter 125 in the Papyrus of Ani. Budge carries the name over untranslated. The god is addressed as coming forth from Amentet, and the address is bound to one denial: I have not committed fornication."@en ;
