    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmc:AegyptiaRubricaeAnatomia a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Bau des Nachschriftenkorpus: dreiundfünfzig Nachschriften, zwei Arten, keine Probe"@de,
        "The anatomy of the rubric corpus: fifty-three postscripts, two kinds, no test"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-19" ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, all fifty-three rubrics of the Theban recension, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: An English Translation of the Chapters, Hymns, etc., of the Theban Recension, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vols. I to III" ;
    skos:definition "All fifty-three passages headed RUBRIC in the three volumes of Budge 1901 were located and read. They fall into two kinds with no overlap. Thirty-four are execution rubrics: recite over an object, name its material, name where it is placed, sometimes name the day. Nineteen are knowledge rubrics: if this chapter be known, such and such follows, with no object and no act. Every execution rubric names at least a material and a placement; not one of the fifty-three names a sign, a term, or a condition under which the prescription would have failed."@en ;
    skos:note "Compiled by this project from the fifty-three places, not stated anywhere by the corpus. The count and the two kinds are in the pages; the reading of what the count means is the act recorded here, with its date."@en,
        "The proportion is the first result and it corrects the first reading of this corpus. The run that entered pmt:EgyptianRitual reported a tradition whose operation consists in knowing a text. That is true of nineteen rubrics. Thirty-four prescribe handling a thing, and they are the more specified of the two: the knowledge rubrics vary in what they promise, while the execution rubrics vary in stone, metal, cloth, colour, orientation and day."@en,
        "The second result is the silence, and it is uniform. Thirty-four prescriptions name matter to the point of naming which lake the water came from and which quarter a figure faces, and none of them says what the officiant should see if the rite took. A tradition that can specify a brick of green clay with incense in its middle, smeared with pitch and set alight, and walled into the south wall facing north, is not a tradition that lacked the vocabulary for a criterion."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:compiledBy "prima-materia" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmc:AegyptiaScarabaeusEfficacia a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der Skarabäus hält das Herz davon ab, seinem Träger genommen zu werden"@de,
        "The scarab keeps the heart from being driven from its owner"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 30B with its rubric, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London 1901, vol. I, pp. 150-152" ;
    skos:note "The title of the chapter states the claim as plainly as any in this holding: the chapter of not letting the heart of the deceased be driven from him in the underworld."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AegyptiaScarabaeusCordis ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AegyptiaCorNeTestetur .

pmc:AegyptiaSecretumOperis a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Vollziehe es vor niemandem: die vier Geheimhaltungsklauseln und wie weit sie auseinandergehen"@de,
        "Perform it before no one: the four secrecy clauses and how far they differ"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapters 137A, 148, 156 and 161, rubrics, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vols. II and III" ;
    skos:definition "Four rubrics restrict who may be present, and the four draw the line in four different places. Chapter 137A: thou shalt not perform this ceremony before any human being except thine own self, or thy father, or thy son. Chapter 148: thou shalt not recite this book of Un-nefer in the presence of any person except thine own self. Chapter 156: thou shalt not let any person who is with thee see it. Chapter 161: let none who is outside know it; thou shalt not do this in the presence of any person except thy father or thy son, or thyself alone."@en ;
    skos:note "Chapter 161 is the strongest of the four and the least securely attested: Budge adds its rubric from the Saite recension and it is not in his Theban base text. A secrecy clause that reaches the graph only through a later recension is worth marking as such."@en,
        "None of the four states a consequence of breaking the clause. They are entered as cautions because that is what they are in form, and the missing half is the same missing half as everywhere else in this corpus."@en,
        "Put side by side the four disagree about the lineage. Chapters 137A and 161 admit the father and the son; chapter 148 admits nobody at all. And Renouf, translating the Turin text of the first of these under chapter 161, has it exclude the father and the son by name. The corpus does not hold one rule about who may watch; it holds four, and a fifth reading of one of them in another edition. The Renouf divergence is argued at pmc:AegyptiaArcanumAmenti."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:AegyptiaFibulaCarneoli,
        pmp:AegyptiaQuattuorVentorum,
        pmp:AegyptiaTabulaCibi ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

