pmc:AffectCouplingProtocol a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Verschiebung eintritt, und ob die Behauptung etwas über sie sagt"@de,
        "Whether the shift occurs, and whether the claim says anything about it"@en ;
    skos:definition "An examination of whether a strong affect shifts attention, readiness and the threshold for risk, and of what remains of the claim once it does."@en ;
    skos:note "A convergence worth following separately. Several transmissions warn against exactly this coupling on the ground that it deceives the practitioner about his own position, and traditions/patanjala-yoga.ttl already carries pmc:SiddhisAsObstacle in that shape. If the direction of the shift is unknowable in advance, the warning and the finding say something close to the same thing from opposite ends, and that is a candidate for a convergence node rather than a remark here."@en,
        "Counter-search, carried and not favourable to either side. A registered replication of the key studies in a large online sample supports the framework only in part: the association between the emboldening affect and both risk-seeking and optimistic estimates holds, while the association between the cautioning affect and risk-seeking does not reliably appear. The opposition of directions survives; its symmetry does not."@en,
        "On the state. This protocol is the case that made pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate necessary, and it is deliberately not recorded as cases without deviation. There the claim says something and the cases bear it out; here they bear it out because the claim does not say enough to be borne out against anything. Filing it as an ordinary confirmation would have added a success to a record that had in fact found the claim empty."@en,
        "That the shift occurs is not in doubt and the claim is borne out in that narrow sense. Strong affect measurably moves risk judgement and the choices that follow from it, in induced and in naturally occurring form alike."@en,
        "The consequence for anyone acting on the claim, which is the reason to enter this node at all. Whoever couples an intended state to a strong affect in order to shift his own threshold does not know from the claim whether he has raised or lowered it. Confirmation at this level of generality is not usable guidance, and the node says so rather than recording a success."@en,
        "What the evidence takes away in the same motion. The direction of the shift is set by the particular affect and not by its strength. Fear and anger are both strong and both unpleasant, and they move the threshold for risk in opposite directions: the fearful judge risk pessimistically and choose cautiously, the angry judge it optimistically and choose boldly, with the angry closer to the cheerful than to the fearful. A claim that names only the strength of the affect therefore cannot say which way anything moves, and is compatible with every outcome."@en ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Carried. A registered replication of the key studies in a large online sample supports the framework only in part: the association between the emboldening affect and both risk-seeking and optimistic estimates holds, the one between the cautioning affect and risk-seeking does not reliably appear. The finding is in the notes above. Not searched: later meta-analytic work on the framework as a whole."@en ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "J. S. Lerner and D. Keltner, Beyond valence: toward a model of emotion-specific influences on judgement and choice, Cognition and Emotion 14 (2000), no. 4, pp. 473-493",
        "J. S. Lerner and D. Keltner, Fear, anger, and risk, Journal of Personality and Social Psychology 81 (2001), no. 1, pp. 146-159, doi:10.1037/0022-3514.81.1.146" ;
    pm:examinationState pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate ;
    pm:examinedBy "Not devised here. The protocol reads the finding off experimental work on specific emotions and their appraisal tendencies, and off a registered replication of it" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "Strong affect, induced or occurring, leaves risk judgement and the choices following from it unmoved against a neutral condition."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:AffectCouplingShiftsTheCarrier ;
    pm:undividedOutcomes "A threshold for risk that has been raised, and one that has been lowered. The claim covers both alike, so acting on it cannot tell the practitioner which of the two he has brought about."@en .

pmc:AgrippaCensuraInEodemVolumine a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Widerruf, gedruckt in dem Band, den er widerruft"@de,
        "The retraction printed inside the volume it retracts"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Agrippa, De occulta philosophia, the censure or retraction printed after book III (trans. J. F., London 1651)" ;
    skos:definition "A structural fact of the volume, read at the wording. After the conclusion of the third book and two letters of the author there follows a piece headed as the censure or retraction of Henry Cornelius Agrippa concerning magic, after his declamation of the vanity of the sciences and the excellency of the word of God. It is not a withdrawal of the contents: it opens by saying that this place requires that we speak of magic, that he who professes magic without astrology does nothing but is altogether in error, and it then divides magic into natural and ceremonial and treats natural magic as the highest power of the natural sciences, naming the peoples who chiefly excelled in it."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because the arrangement decides how the work is to be read and is regularly reported the other way round. The retraction is not a later document set against the book; it is bound into the book, after the conclusion, in the same volume, and what stands under its heading is a further exposition of magic. A reader told that the author withdrew the work will find the withdrawal printed as one of its parts."@en,
        "What was read is the heading, its place in the sequence, and the opening sections on magic in general and on natural magic. The rest of the piece was not read, and the declamation on the vanity of the sciences from which it is drawn was not opened."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeOccultaPhilosophia ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RenaissanceMagic .

pmc:AgrippaCredulitas a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Beständige Leichtgläubigkeit wirkt Wunder in Meinungen und falschen Verrichtungen"@de,
        "Constant credulity works miracles in opinions and false operations"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Agrippa, De occulta philosophia III.4 (trans. J. F., London 1651)" ;
    skos:definition "The third book states of superstition — which it defines as a resemblance of religion, imitating its miracles, sacraments, rites and observations — that it gets no small power from that imitation and no less strength from the credulity of the operator; that a constant credulity can do so great things as even to work miracles in opinions and false operations; and that whosoever in his religion, though false, yet believes most strongly that it is true, and raises his spirit by that credulity until it is assimilated to the leading spirits of that religion, may work those things which nature and reason do not discern. It adds that incredulity and diffidence weaken every work, not only in superstition but also in true religion, and enervate the desired effect even of the strongest experiments."@en ;
    skos:note "It is not a critic's observation reported by the work; it is the work's premise for a practical warning. The chapter goes on to say that the old authors of the art were Chaldaeans, Egyptians, Assyrians, Persians and Arabians whose religion was polluted idolatry, and that their errors must not be allowed to war against the Catholic religion. The argument runs: since credulity works either way, the choice of what to be credulous about is a matter of piety and not of efficacy."@en,
        "This is the sharpest sentence the holding carries on the point, and it is the work's own. The transmission states in its instructions that the truth of the doctrine is not what decides the outcome, and that the same condition produces the effect inside a religion its author holds to be false. Once that is said, no result can tell for or against any particular doctrine of the work, and the author says it while arguing that the reader should therefore be careful which religion he imitates."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeOccultaPhilosophia ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RenaissanceMagic .

pmc:AgrippaDignificatio a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Viele treiben diese Kunst vergeblich, weil ihnen die Würdigung fehlt"@de,
        "Many prosecute this art in vain, for want of being dignified to it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Agrippa, De occulta philosophia III.3 (trans. J. F., London 1651)" ;
    skos:definition "The requirement the third book calls the beginning, the perfection and the key of all magical operations. The faculty requires in a man a wonderful dignifying, because the understanding, the highest faculty of the soul and the only worker of wonders, is not worthy of the command of divine substances when it is overwhelmed by too much commerce with the flesh; therefore, the work says, many prosecute this art in vain. Two things are to be meditated: how to leave carnal affections, frail sense and material passions, and by what means to ascend to an intellect pure and conjoined with the powers above. Natural dignity is the best disposition of the body and its organs and proceeds from the situation and influence of the celestial bodies at a man's generation; whoever is not born such must recompense the defect of nature by education and the right use of natural things."@en ;
    skos:note "A second clause of the same kind was read in the third book, in the chapter on holy names: the manner and rules delivered by the first institutor must be observed, for they who are ignorant of these things lose their labour and work in vain. Which rules those are, and where they are delivered, is not said at that place."@en,
        "The condition is partly fixed before birth, and the work says so in astrological terms: whether a man has it depends on the state of his ninth house at his generation. What is offered to the man who lacks it is a recompense by education with no stated point at which the recompense is complete. Between that and the reader's dulness of intellect at the conclusion, the work has assigned a failure to the operator's constitution at both ends."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:AgrippaPraeparatioOraculi ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeOccultaPhilosophia ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RenaissanceMagic .

pmc:AgrippaDuaeRationesEfficaciae a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Wirkung den Glauben des Handelnden verlangt, innerhalb eines Werkes bestritten"@de,
        "Whether the effect requires the operator's belief, disputed inside one work"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Agrippa, De occulta philosophia I.20 (trans. J. F., London 1651)",
        "Agrippa, De occulta philosophia I.66 (trans. J. F., London 1651)" ;
    skos:definition "The two sides the volume carries without joining them. On one side the operation requires a firm belief in the worker, doubt breaks the virtue of his mind, and a constant credulity works even in false operations. On the other side the virtue is seated in the substance of a thing or in one of its members and acts by mere touch or by being seen, with nothing required of anybody. The dispute is not between parties: it is between two chapters of the same book, and neither refers to the other at the places read."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a dispute and not as a remark because the two sides have different consequences for everything else in the work. If the belief condition governs, then every failure is accounted for in advance and no operation of the work can be tested; if the substance doctrine governs, then a good part of the first book states claims that could be tried on a bench. A reader who wants to know which the work means has to decide, and the work does not decide for him at the places read."@en,
        "The two sources of this node are two places in one volume rather than two works, which is unusual for this class in the holding and is the honest form here: what is in dispute is stated twice in one book. Whether the Latin of 1533 or the manuscript of 1510 joins them somewhere is open, and it is the first thing a run with the critical edition should look at, because the chapters carrying the belief condition are among those the manuscript is reported not to contain."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:AgrippaFides ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeOccultaPhilosophia ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RenaissanceMagic .

pmc:AgrippaHoraePlanetariae a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die ungleichen Stunden, und die Nachlässigkeit, durch die viele ihrer Wirkung beraubt werden"@de,
        "The unequal hours, and the neglect by which many are defrauded of their effect"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Agrippa, De occulta philosophia II.34 (trans. J. F., London 1651)" ;
    skos:definition "The ground of the planetary hours as the second book gives it. Two things are to be observed: the motions, ascensions and windings of the stars as they truly are in the eighth sphere, through the neglect of which, the work says, it happens that many err in fabricating the celestial images and are defrauded of their desired effect; and the times of choosing the planetary hours, which are formed by dividing the span from sunrise to sunset into twelve equal parts and the span from sunset to sunrise likewise, and distributing those hours to the planets in order, the first hour of the day going always to the lord of that day."@en ;
    skos:note "A named cause of failure that lies neither in the operator's belief nor in a third party but in a calculation, and it is the only one of that kind among the places read. It is also the only one a later reader could in principle audit: whether an image was made at a given hour, and whether that hour was computed in the eighth sphere, are questions about a record rather than about a state of mind."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeOccultaPhilosophia ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RenaissanceMagic .

pmc:AgrippaNonApprobo a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Das Tor des Acheron ist in diesem Buch, und ich billige sie nicht"@de,
        "The gate of Acheron is in this book, and I do not approve of them"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Agrippa, De occulta philosophia, the address to the reader (trans. J. F., London 1651)" ;
    skos:definition "The author's warning against his own book, in the address to the reader. Those who would take the name of magic in the worse sense are advised not to read his writings, nor understand them, nor remember them, for they are pernicious and full of poison; the gate of Acheron is in this book, and it speaks stones, so let them take heed that it does not beat out their brains. To the reader who comes without prejudice he says that if he finds anything that may not please him, he should let it alone and make no use of it, for the author does not approve of those things but declares them to him; and that he has written many things rather narratively than affirmatively."@en ;
    skos:note "A self-warning standing inside the passage that publishes the thing, which is the shape this holding already carries from several transmissions; what is unusual here is that it is a warning of non-approval rather than of danger alone. The author reserves, in advance and without naming which, a portion of his own contents that he reports without endorsing. That leaves no way to tell an endorsed instruction from a reported one, and it leaves the author right whatever the reader finds."@en,
        "The same address carries the retraction and the objection to it in the author's own voice: he says that being become a man he retracted what he did as a boy, and that in his book on the vanity of the sciences he for the most part retracted this book; he then puts the reader's obvious question — you wrote it young and have retracted it, what then have you published — and answers not by withdrawing anything but by an account of how an unfinished copy got loose in Italy, France and Germany. So the work is published as a thing its author has retracted, and the reason given for publishing is the control of the text and not its truth."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:AgrippaPraeparatioOraculi ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeOccultaPhilosophia ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RenaissanceMagic .

pmc:AgrippaVirtutesInSubstantia a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kräfte in der ganzen Substanz oder in einem Teil, wirkend durch Berührung oder Blick"@de,
        "Virtues seated in the whole substance or in a part, working by touch or by sight"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Agrippa, De occulta philosophia I.20 (trans. J. F., London 1651)" ;
    skos:definition "The doctrine of the first book about where a natural virtue is seated. In some things it is in the whole substance or in all their parts — the little fish that is said to stop a ship by its mere touch, the plant good for the sight no more in the root than in the leaves and the seeds — and in other things only in a member: in the eyes of the basilisk a power to kill as soon as it sees, in the eyes of certain wolves a power to take away a man's voice if they see him first. The work adds that those who work by witchcraft use the eyes of such animals in their waters for that reason, and gives a list of further cases: ants fleeing the heart of one bird and not its head or eyes, the gall of one animal gathering weasels, the gall of another gathering frogs."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because it is the other theory of the same work and it is not reconciled with the first. Here the virtue is in the substance and acts by touch or by being seen; nothing is required of an operator, nothing is said of his belief, and in the clearest cases — the fish, the basilisk, the wolf — there is no operator at all. Set beside the condition of firm belief and the clause about credulity in false operations, the volume carries two accounts of how its own effects come about and joins them nowhere that was read."@en,
        "The reason for keeping both and not choosing is that the choice is exactly what a later reader would supply. A holding that entered only the belief condition would report a psychological magic; one that entered only the substances would report a natural philosophy; the work is both, and which of the two a modern presentation quotes is the first thing to check about that presentation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeOccultaPhilosophia ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RenaissanceMagic .

pmc:AhwalAreNotByEffort a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Zustände werden nicht durch Anstrengung erlangt"@de,
        "The states are not attained by effort"@en ;
    dcterms:source "al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:notByEffort ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:Ahwal ;
    pm:situationGround "The handbooks describe these as descending on the wayfarer as a gift and distinguish them from the stations precisely by their not being attainable through effort. That is the criterion this coordinate names, stated by the source itself."@en .

pmc:AhwalAreTransient a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Zustände vergehen"@de,
        "The states pass"@en ;
    dcterms:source "al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:transientState ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:Ahwal ;
    pm:situationGround "The same passage has them pass as well as descend, and the two are separate claims: a gift may be kept. The Sufi material answers both questions the same way, which is why one axis sufficed until a tradition answered them differently."@en .

pmc:AjivikaGosalaAttributio a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Makkhali Gosāla lehrte die Läuterung durch das Umherwandern"@de,
        "Makkhali Gosāla taught purification by the round"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphalasutta 19.1-21.7 (Mahāsaṅgīti Tipiṭaka Buddhavasse 2500; segments dn2:19.1-21.7), read with the translations of Bhikkhu Sujato and Sabbamitta" ;
    skos:note "How hostile the witness is, in its own words and from the same canon: the Kesakambalasutta calls the doctrine of the bamboo-staffed ascetic the worst of all the doctrines of the various ascetics and brahmins, as a hair blanket is the worst woven cloth, summarises it as there is no deed, no action, no energy, and says that the man has come into the world as a trap for humans, for the harm and suffering of many beings. Read in the Pali at an3.137:1.3-3.2. That is the stance of the source of every node in this file, and it is recorded here rather than at a node of its own because the verdict belongs to the Theravāda transmission and not to this one."@en,
        "So the only witness to the doctrine reports it as an evasion of a question about results, and the question it evades is the project's own question. That is the part a later run should weigh before using this report as evidence of what the Ājīvikas held: a hostile source that frames the doctrine as a non-answer has a motive for the shape it gives it."@en,
        "The ascription and its frame in one node, because the frame is the whole value of the witness. King Ajātasattu reports that he asked the bamboo-staffed ascetic Gosāla about the fruit of the ascetic life visible in the present life, and that Gosāla answered with purification by the round; and he says it was as if a man asked about a mango answered with a breadfruit, or asked about a breadfruit answered with a mango. He adds that he neither approved nor rejected what was said, and left without taking the words up."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:AjivikaSamsarasuddhi ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DighaNikaya ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ajivika .

pmc:AjivikaNaTapasaParipaka a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Keine Bußübung bringt eine unreife Tat zur Reife"@de,
        "No austerity ripens an unripe deed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphalasutta 20.8 (Mahāsaṅgīti Tipiṭaka Buddhavasse 2500; segment dn2:20.8), read with the translations of Bhikkhu Sujato and Sabbamitta" ;
    skos:definition "The clause that denies the form of claim this holding collects, in the words of the report: and therein there is no such thing as this, that by this precept or by this vow or by this austerity or by this holy life I shall ripen an unripened deed, or wear away a ripened deed by experiencing it piece by piece; it is not so."@en ;
    skos:note "The Pali is tattha natthi imināhaṁ sīlena vā vatena vā tapena vā brahmacariyena vā aparipakkaṁ vā kammaṁ paripācessāmi, paripakkaṁ vā kammaṁ phussa phussa byantiṁ karissāmī ti hevaṁ natthi. The four named means are precept, vow, austerity and the holy life, which is the standard fourfold list of what the competing movements of the same period said did work, so the denial is pointed and not general."@en,
        "This is the node that makes a yielding claim impossible for this transmission, and it is why the file has none. A reader who wants the holding's one clear counter-position to every pm:Yielding in it should start here."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DighaNikaya ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ajivika .

pmc:AjivikaSituatAcquisitio a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Das Ende des Leidens kommt nicht durch Anstrengung"@de,
        "The end of suffering comes not by effort"@en ;
    skos:note "The clearest case on this axis in the holding, and worth the placement for that reason: most sources leave the acquisition to be inferred from a prescription, and this one argues for the negative value directly."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:notByEffort ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:AjivikaSamsarasuddhi ;
    pm:situationGround "The source states the acquisition negatively and at length: there is no cause and no condition for the purification of beings, no precept or vow or austerity or holy life ripens an unripe deed, there is no increasing or diminishing of the round, and fools and the wise alike make an end of suffering by running through it. The coordinate is the compiler's; the source names no axis."@en .

pmc:AjivikaSituatAgentia a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Unterscheidung der Handlungsträgerschaft wird verweigert, nicht beantwortet"@de,
        "The agency distinction is refused, not answered"@en ;
    skos:note "This is the use the declining value was introduced for, in the terms SPEC 17 sets: a transmission that rejects the distinction itself, held apart from a source that was asked and said nothing. Before this placement the four consciousness axes carried no instance of a declining value from a transmission that argues for the refusal."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:agencyDeclined ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:AjivikaNiyati ;
    pm:situationGround "The axis asks who acts according to the source, and the source denies both ends of it in one sentence: natthi attakāre, natthi parakāre, natthi purisakāre, there is no act of one's own, no act of another, no act of a person. Neither pm:ownAgency nor pm:cededAgency would be the report, and pm:agencyNotStated would be false, because the question is put and answered with a refusal of the distinction."@en .

pmc:AjivikaSuttagulaUpama a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das geworfene Garnknäuel, das läuft, bis es abgewickelt ist"@de,
        "The ball of thread thrown, which runs until it is unwound"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphalasutta 20.10-20.11 (Mahāsaṅgīti Tipiṭaka Buddhavasse 2500; segments dn2:20.10-20.11), read with the translations of Bhikkhu Sujato and Sabbamitta" ;
    skos:definition "The image the report gives for the fixed round: just as a ball of thread, when thrown, runs on only while it unwinds, so fools and the wise alike, having run on and wandered through, will make an end of suffering."@en ;
    skos:note "Kept as its own node because the simile carries something the enumeration does not: the end is certain, it is reached by running out rather than by arriving, and nothing the thread does alters when it stops. That is a different claim from the bare count, and it is the part a later reader can hold against transmissions that make the end depend on the practice."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DighaNikaya ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ajivika .

pmc:AkasagamanaPresupposesDivineEye a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Luftreise setzt das göttliche Auge voraus"@de,
        "Travel through the air presupposes the divine eye"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.100-101 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, p. 460; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:note "It is the second consequence that makes this more than a traffic rule. The thing to be avoided is being seen, and the corpus that teaches the flight is the corpus in which the same act, done in front of laypeople, is an offence — see pmc:DisplayOfPowersRefused, and the bowl fetched from the pole in the fifth chapter of the Cūḷavagga. The handbook does not repeat the prohibition here. It supplies the technique for not being caught by it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The handbook gives two consequences in two sentences. Without the divine eye he does not see the hills and trees on his way, whether of the temperature order or created out of jealousy by serpents or by the fairy birds, and must meet them unprepared. And without it he cannot pick his landing place: if he comes down at an unsuitable one, at a bathing place, a landing place or a village gate, he reveals himself to the populace, and the handbook directs him to look with the divine eye, avoid such a place and descend elsewhere." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:AkasenaGamana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Dibbacakkhu ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:AkasakasinaVivada a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Kasiṇa-Erreichung überhaupt nötig ist, und was es entscheidet"@de,
        "Whether the device attainment is needed at all, and what settles it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.100 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, p. 460; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.89 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, p. 457; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "An elder of the same school, named in the handbook as the one who knew the three Baskets, objects twice. At the wall he asks what the use of the space device is, and argues from a case the chapter itself supplies: one who wants to create elephants and horses does not enter an elephant device or a horse device, so is not mastery of the eight attainments after the preliminary work in any device whatever the whole criterion? At the air journey he objects again, in the same words: what is the use of entering the attainment, should not his mind be concentrated, so that whatever place he resolves upon becomes space? The monks answer him the first time that the space device stands in the text and hence must certainly be so; and the handbook, having reported the second objection, closes it by saying that one should do as in the going through the wall."@en ;
    skos:note "It is entered as a dispute and not as a note because both sides are reported, both are attributed, and the chapter is explicit about the ground on which it decides. The two sources are the two places where the same elder objects; there is no second work behind them, and none is claimed."@en,
        "The dispute is about technique and is settled by the location of a word. Neither side offers a case, an outcome or an attempt; the objector argues from the internal consistency of the procedure, the answer is that the procedure stands written. In a corpus whose subject is what is to be done in order to bring an effect about, this is the transmission showing what it takes to be decisive, and it is not the effect."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim "That the operator must enter the attainment of the space device before going through a wall, and the earth device before travelling through the air, rather than resolving from mastery of the eight attainments in any device whatever." ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:AnapanasatiDukkara a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Der Atemgegenstand wird im Kapitel, das ihn lehrt, als kein Feld für geringe Personen erklärt"@de,
        "The breathing subject is declared no field for inferior persons, in the chapter that teaches it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga VIII.211-212, Pali PTS [284]-[285] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, pp. 326-327; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "In the middle of the longest exposition the handbook gives to any single meditation subject, it says of that subject that it is heavy and hard to develop, a fit subject of attention for Buddhas, silent Buddhas and sons of the Buddhas, that it is not a small thing and not to be practised by inferior persons, and that the more it is attended to the calmer and subtler it becomes, so that strong mindfulness and understanding are required. It grounds this on a saying of its founder, that he does not declare the development of mindfulness of breathing for one who is forgetful and unknowing, and illustrates it with the needle and the instrument for boring the needle's eye."@en ;
    skos:note "It is the second of the two places in these readings where the handbook warns against its own prescription, and the two do it differently. Of the powers it says they impede the goal; of this subject it says the aspirant is probably not equal to it. The first can be obeyed by cutting the thing off, the second cannot be obeyed at all."@en,
        "The warning stands in the prescribing passage and not in a commentary on it, and it is addressed against the practice the passage is teaching. The handbook does not withdraw the instruction; it goes on to give the sixteen steps, the counting, the connexion and the fixing."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Anapanasati ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:AnapanasatiYieldsPiti a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Atemachtsamkeit bringt Verzückung hervor"@de,
        "Mindfulness of breathing yields rapture"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.94ff. (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga VIII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:note "Rapture appears in the handbook as a factor of absorption reached through the meditation subject, not as something aimed at directly. The pairing is recorded at that strength."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Anapanasati ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:Piti .

pmc:AnswersFromAnUncontrolledOutcome a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Antworten am Ausgang, den der Betreiber nicht beherrscht"@de,
        "Answers fixed to an outcome the operator does not control"@en ;
    skos:note "A third case was considered and not used. The Sámi drum answers by the travel of a ring that the operator does not steer, and the reading rule is attested twice ninety years apart (pmc:SunCourseRule). It is not entered here because that node states a rule of reading and not a claim that the reading comes true, and pm:generalizedFrom must point at claims and not at descriptions. Seen and left out, with the reason."@en,
        "The Zande transmission has a feature that makes this generalization examinable rather than merely stateable: it prescribes a control, the second consultation that must reverse the first. It also names eight causes to which a failure of that control is attributed, so the condition never falls against the oracle itself. Both stand in traditions/azande.ttl, and both belong in any protocol devised against this node."@en,
        "The comparison class is the whole of the claim and is the part to attack. An oracle that answers yes or no is measured against one half; a system of two hundred and fifty-six figures against one two-hundred-and-fifty-sixth. A protocol that does not fix the admissible set before the consultation measures nothing, because the set can always be narrowed afterwards to whatever came true."@en,
        "Two attested claims from two transmissions with no route between them that this holding has established: the Zande poison oracle and the casting of the Ifá figures. The mechanism is the same in both and is the reason they are taken together — the answer is not read off the operator's judgement but off an outcome he does not steer."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:BengeYieldsIdentificationOfTheWitch,
        pmc:CastingYieldsForeknowledge ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Procedures that fix an answer to the outcome of a physical process the operator cannot control name the state of affairs later established more often than an answer drawn at random from the same set of admissible answers."@en .

pmc:ApanaForcedAgainstItsCourse a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der abwärts gerichtete Wind wird mit Gewalt aufwärts getrieben"@de,
        "The downward wind driven upward by force"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.62 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "The wind whose course is downward is said to be made to go upward by force; the manual states the natural course and the reversal in the same verse."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the direct reversal of any scheme that assigns downward carriage to an operation in the lower abdomen. The manual has the abdominal operation carry upward, and has the operation at the throat press the other wind down. Both assignments are the opposite of the one the modern draft makes. See prima-materia#28."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:ApatheiaPresupposesAscesis a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Apatheia setzt asketische Arbeit voraus"@de,
        "Apatheia presupposes ascetic labour"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 81" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Apatheia is called the flower of ascetic labour and ascetic labour is defined as the keeping of the commandments, so that where the labour is absent the freedom from the passions does not appear at all rather than appearing in a lesser degree." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Apatheia ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Praktike ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:DesertMonasticism .

pmc:ApertioIsNotByEffort a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Öffnung des Inneren liegt bei nicht-durch-Anstrengung"@de,
        "The opening of the interiors is placed at not-by-effort"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Swedenborg, Arcana coelestia, n. 9438-9439 (The Heavenly Arcana, trans. Potts, Standard Edition, vol. XI, pp. 359-360)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:notByEffort ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArcanaCoelestia ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:SwedenborgLoquelaCumSpiritibus ;
    pm:situationGround "Every statement of the opening in the passages read is passive and has the same agent: the interiors of my spirit have been opened by the Lord; it has been granted me; to no one is it given unless he can be consociated. The corpus supplies no operation at all — the search stands at pmp:SwedenborgPraxisSearch — so there is nothing on the acquired side of this axis for it to be placed at. The coordinate is read off the grammar and off the absence, and both were checked."@en .

pmc:AppearancesAreSelfArising a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Erscheinungen sind Selbst-Erscheinung"@de,
        "The appearances are self-arising"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bar do thos grol (Karma Lingpa recension, 14th century; trans. Gyurme Dorje 2006), the intermediate state of reality" ;
    skos:definition "The teaching that the figures met in the intermediate state are appearances of the one who meets them and not independently existing beings, and that recognising them as such is what frees."@en ;
    skos:note "Tibetan rang snang, self-appearance. A dating warning belongs on this node: the work is traditionally ascribed to Padmasambhava in the eighth century but is a treasure text of the fourteenth, revealed by Karma Lingpa; the older ascription is not held in the scholarship. Note also the scope. The doctrine holds of all appearance in this tradition, waking included, and is therefore not a statement about the threshold state in particular."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:TibetanTantric .

pmc:AraqielTeachesSignsOfTheEarth a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Araqiel offenbart die Zeichen der Erde"@de,
        "Araqiel discloses the signs of the earth"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:SignsOfTheEarth ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Araqiel ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:AristeasJourney a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Seelenreise, Aristeas zugeschrieben"@de,
        "The soul-journey ascribed to Aristeas"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Herodotus, Histories IV.13-16",
        "Pliny, Naturalis Historia VII.174" ;
    skos:definition "A capacity ascribed to a figure who is not available to report it, in a historian's account of what was told of him."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:SoulJourney ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HerodotiHistoriai,
        pmw:NaturalisHistoria ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GraecoRoman .

pmc:ArmarosTeachesLoosingOfSpells a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Armaros offenbart das Lösen von Beschwörungen"@de,
        "Armaros discloses the loosing of spells"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:LoosingOfSpells ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Armaros ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:ArsNotoriaCrimenKautel a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Wer durch ein Verbrechen behindert ist, darf sie nicht sprechen"@de,
        "No one impeded by crime may presume to say it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), pp. 46-47, ed. Peterson" ;
    skos:definition "The warning that no man impeded or corrupted with any crime ought to presume to say the oration; on a day on which he is so impeded he may only call it to mind in his heart."@en ;
    skos:note "The caution is the closest thing in this witness to a condition of failure, and it is worth naming what it does not do: it names who may not say the words, not what happens to one who says them anyway. The distinction decides whether the condition is examinable or absorbing, and the text leaves it on the safer side of that line."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ArsNotoriaOratio ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:ArsNotoriaHorae a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Tagesstunden und die Zahl der Wiederholungen"@de,
        "The hours of the day and the count of repetitions"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), pp. 13-14, ed. Peterson" ;
    skos:definition "The division of the day the orations follow: once in the morning, once about the third hour, once about the ninth hour and once at evening; where a great work presses, twice in the morning and twice about the ninth hour."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:ArsNotoriaPresupposesLunationes a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Wirkung setzt den bestimmten Tag voraus"@de,
        "The effect presupposes the appointed day"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), § 14, p. 12, ed. Peterson" ;
    skos:note "Prescribed order and not stated necessity. The text says the orations are to be pronounced at certain determinate times of the moon and are not to be undertaken without consideration of the end; it does not say what follows if the day is missed. The strength is set to what the passage carries and no higher."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ArsNotoriaFacundia ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ArsNotoriaLunationes ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:ArsNotoriaPresupposesSolitudo a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Wirkung setzt Alleinsein und die gesenkte Stimme voraus"@de,
        "The effect presupposes solitude and the lowered voice"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), p. 14, ed. Peterson" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ArsNotoriaFacundia ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ArsNotoriaSolitudo ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:ArsNotoriaRepetitioYieldsGradatio a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die wiederholte Oration bringt Beredsamkeit nach Maß hervor"@de,
        "The repeated oration yields eloquence by measure"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), pp. 46-47, ed. Peterson" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ArsNotoriaOratioRepetita ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ArsNotoriaGradatioFacundiae .

pmc:ArsNotoriaTerminusMortisDisputatus a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der Tod auf den folgenden Tag oder nur auf bald gesetzt ist"@de,
        "Whether the death is set for the morrow or only soon"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), § 30a, p. 25, ed. Peterson",
        "Ars notoria, the Latin of the Beringos printing appended to the Agrippa Opera, § 30a, printed beside the translation in the edition of Peterson" ;
    skos:note "Held as a dispute and not resolved. Resolving it would need the medieval recensions, which were not opened; that the two readings stand side by side in one edition is a reason to record the divergence, not to prefer either."@en,
        "The Latin of the printing Turner worked from says of the sick man who answers badly that he is proculdubio in proximo moriturum, that is that he will die shortly, with no day named. Turner's English says: then doubtless conclude, He will dye on the morrow. Both stand in the edition read, on facing sides of the same passage."@en,
        "The difference falls on the only part of the claim that could be missed. A death that must come tomorrow can be watched for and can fail to arrive; a death that will come shortly has no last day, and no outcome ever refutes it. The English is therefore the examinable version and the Latin is not, and a protocol has to say which of the two it tests."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:ArsNotoriaInterrogatioAegroti ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:ArsNotoriaVirtusNonExNatura a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Wunder geht nicht aus deiner eigenen Natur hervor"@de,
        "The miracle proceeds not from your own nature"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), § 30b, p. 25, ed. Peterson" ;
    skos:definition "The statement the text makes about its own procedure: that this miracle proceeds not from the operator's nature but from the nature and virtues of the holy angels, it being a part of their office wonderfully to reveal these things to him."@en ;
    skos:note "A transmission that says outright where it locates the acting is uncommon, and this one says it in the middle of an instruction rather than in a prologue. It is why the placement on the agency axis beside this file rests on the wording and not on a reading of it."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:AscentPresupposesTheRungs a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Schau der Schönheit selbst setzt den Aufstieg über die Stufen voraus"@de,
        "The vision of beauty itself presupposes the ascent by rungs"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Plato, Symposium 211c" ;
    skos:note "The word rendered as rungs stands in the base text at 211c, which is why this counts as a stated ordering and not as a modern reading of a sequence of paragraphs."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The ascent is described as rightly conducted only when it proceeds as by rungs of a ladder, from one body to two, from bodies to pursuits, from pursuits to studies, and thence to beauty itself; the one who does not go this way does not arrive at the final vision." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:BeautyItself ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:BeautyOfOneBody ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Platonic .

pmc:AsipuAnointingYieldsProtection a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Salbung hält Schaden vom Beschwörer ab"@de,
        "The anointing keeps harm from the exorcist"@en ;
    dcterms:source "KAR 31 (VAT 8803) rev. 30-31 (M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons, BAM 8, De Gruyter 2016, p. 40)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AsipuRamanaPasasu ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AsipuMimmaLemnuLaTehi .

pmc:AsipuCautionAgainstUnprotectedApproach a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Nicht ungeschützt zum Kranken gehen"@de,
        "Do not go to the patient unprotected"@en ;
    dcterms:source "KAR 31 (VAT 8803) rev. 29-31 (M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons, BAM 8, De Gruyter 2016, p. 40)" ;
    skos:note "A caution that a transmission addresses to its own operator rather than to the person operated on, and one that names what it protects against by list. The list is the same list of demons the incantations of the series drive away from the patient, which makes the exorcist, for the length of the visit, a second patient."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:AsipuLemnuAnaAsipiTehu ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmc:AsipuLearnsTheRitesByDivineDecision a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Götter beschlossen, dass er ihre Riten lernen solle"@de,
        "The gods decided that he should learn their rituals"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Udug-hul 12: 39 (canonical Utukku Lemnutu; critical edition M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons, BAM 8, De Gruyter 2016)" ;
    skos:definition "The claim the twelfth tablet puts in the speaker's mouth: they decided about me that I should learn their rituals and their ways."@en ;
    skos:note "Acquisition and gift stand in one sentence and neither cancels the other: the learning is his and the decision that he should learn is not. That is a shape the holding's axes cannot yet place, because the acquisition axis asks whether the source presents the state as earned or as given and this source presents it as both, at two different levels. The node is entered without an axis placement rather than forced onto one; no placement is a different record from a placement on the not-stated value, and here the source does state something."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmc:AsipuMashultuppuYieldsRemoval a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der Sündenbock trägt das Übel fort"@de,
        "The scapegoat carries the evil away"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Udug-hul 12: 77-112 (canonical Utukku Lemnutu; critical edition M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons, BAM 8, De Gruyter 2016)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AsipuMashultuppu ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AsipuLemnuIntoTheNetherworld .

pmc:AsipuProvisionedDismissalYieldsDeparture a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die mit Zehrung versehene Entlassung schickt die Geister in die Steppe"@de,
        "The provisioned dismissal sends the ghosts to the steppe"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Udug-hul 7: 152-156 (canonical Utukku Lemnutu; critical edition M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons, BAM 8, De Gruyter 2016)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AsipuNaruqqaLeqe ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AsipuLemnuAnaSerika .

pmc:AsipuSelfProtectionBeforeApproach a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Salbung geht dem Herantreten voraus"@de,
        "The anointing comes before the approach"@en ;
    dcterms:source "KAR 31 (VAT 8803) rev. 29-31 (M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons, BAM 8, De Gruyter 2016, p. 40)" ;
    skos:note "The strength is set at stated necessity and the reason for the doubt is recorded rather than hidden. The text gives a purpose clause and not a threat: anoint yourself, so that nothing bad will approach you. The consequence of omission is therefore the contrapositive of what stands written and not a sentence of the transmission, and a later reader who holds that this is one step short of a stated necessity would be arguing about the same words and not about a missing reading. What tips it is the rubric immediately above, which names the harm by list and by name; a purpose clause backed by a named harm says what is at stake if the step is left out."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The ritual states the anointing as what secures that nothing bad approaches the exorcist when he approaches the patient; what is claimed for the omission is therefore that the demons of the rubric — Sheriff and Bailiff demon, epilepsy, the accessory to evil, the Alu-demon, the falling illness — approach him instead."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:AsipuAnaMarsiTehu ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:AsipuRamanaPasasu ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmc:AsipuSpellOnSelfBeforeApproach a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Selbstbeschwörung geht dem Herantreten voraus"@de,
        "The spell on oneself comes before the approach"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Sakikku (diagnostic series) Tablet 2, colophon (R. Labat, Traite akkadien de diagnostics et pronostics medicaux, Paris and Leiden 1951, pp. 18-19 n. 27)" ;
    skos:note "Prescribed order and not stated necessity, although the wording is the sharper of the two: it is a prohibition and not a recommendation. What it lacks is the consequence. The colophon says you should not approach, and says nothing about what befalls the one who does; the ritual of the Assur tablet, which is weaker in form, is the one that names the harm. The pair is worth keeping side by side for that reason, because it shows that the firmness of a wording and the presence of a stated consequence vary independently in one and the same craft."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:AsipuAnaMarsiTehu ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:AsipuSiptaAnaRamaniNadu ;
    pm:readVia "M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons: Canonical Udug-hul Incantations (BAM 8, Boston/Berlin: De Gruyter 2016), p. 40" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmc:AsiputuLamaduYieldsIsipputu a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Studium bringt die Beherrschung des Reinigungskorpus hervor"@de,
        "The study yields mastery of the corpus of purification"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The Exorcist's Manual, KAR 44: 36 (Geller, in Steinert (ed.), Assyrian and Babylonian Scholarly Text Catalogues, BAM 9, De Gruyter 2018, p. 301)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AsiputuLamadu ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AsiputuIskaruMasmassuti .

pmc:AsiputuMasteryBeforeJudgement a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Korpus wird beherrscht, bevor die Ansichten gewogen werden"@de,
        "The corpus is mastered before the views are weighed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The Exorcist's Manual, KAR 44: 36-37 (Geller, in Steinert (ed.), Assyrian and Babylonian Scholarly Text Catalogues, BAM 9, De Gruyter 2018, p. 301)" ;
    skos:note "The order is given by the word afterwards standing at the head of the second stage, and by the clause until you master the entire corpus closing the first. No consequence is stated for taking the second stage first, so the strength stays at prescribed order; what the manual does state, at the end, is the reward for completing both, namely that the protective deity establish the student and that his name be mentioned forever."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:AsiputuSitulti ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:AsiputuLamadu ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmc:AsiputuSitultiYieldsMithurtu a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die zweite Stufe bringt das Wägen widerstreitender Ansichten hervor"@de,
        "The second stage yields the weighing of conflicting views"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The Exorcist's Manual, KAR 44: 37-40 (Geller, in Steinert (ed.), Assyrian and Babylonian Scholarly Text Catalogues, BAM 9, De Gruyter 2018, p. 301)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AsiputuSitulti ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AsiputuMithurtu .

pmc:AtharvanMedha a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Weisheit, zuerst gerufen und gebeten einzutreten"@de,
        "Wisdom called first, and asked to enter"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Atharvaveda Saṃhitā 6.108 verses 1 to 4, in the translation of W. D. Whitney, revised by C. R. Lanman, Harvard Oriental Series 7, Cambridge, Mass. 1905" ;
    skos:definition "A short hymn for wisdom. Do thou, O wisdom, come first to us, with kine, with horses, thou with the sun's rays; I call first, unto the aid of the gods, wisdom filled with brahman, quickened by brahman, praised by seers, drunk of by Vedic students; the wisdom that the Ṛbhus know, that the Asuras know, the excellent wisdom that the seers know — that do we cause to enter into me; and, with that wisdom do thou make me today, O Agni, possessed of wisdom."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a capacity claimed and not as a claim that a practice produces it, and the distinction is the reason there is no yielding node here. The hymn is a petition throughout: wisdom is called, invited and asked to enter, and the one operative clause names Agni as the agent. What the manual makes of it — its use in the rite for begetting wisdom and at the initiation — was not read in the Sanskrit and is not entered."@en,
        "Whitney records that the Paippalāda has only verses 1, 2 and 5 of this hymn. The verse carrying the three classes who know wisdom is therefore transmitted in one recension of the two."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Atharvavedic .

pmc:AtrarpePatraic a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Patrick verwarf sie, und wer sie übt, hat weder Himmel noch Erde"@de,
        "Patrick abolished these, and whoever practises them has neither heaven nor earth"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Prologue to the Senchas Már, in Ancient Laws of Ireland, vol. I (Dublin: HMSO, 1865), p. 45",
        "Sanas Cormaic s.v. Imbas forosnai, Irish text in Stokes, Three Irish Glossaries (London 1862), p. 25, English in Cormac's Glossary, trans. O'Donovan (Calcutta 1868), p. 95" ;
    skos:definition "The sentence the glossary appends to the procedure. Patrick abolished this and the teinm laegda, and he adjudged that whoever should practise them should have neither heaven nor earth, because it was renouncing baptism. The law prologue states the same abolition of the three things among the poets when they believed, as they were profane rites, and gives the ground that teinm laegha and imus forosna could not be performed without offering to idol-gods."@en ;
    skos:note "The consequence is stated and it falls on the operator: neither heaven nor earth. It is a consequence of performing and not of performing badly, which is the opposite direction from every other stated consequence in this file."@en,
        "The ground is the offering and never the outcome. Neither witness says that the procedures do not work, that they had failed, or that what they revealed was false; both say that they cannot be performed without an offering to demons and that the offering is what is forbidden. A prohibition of that shape is an attestation of efficacy by the prohibiting party, and the search that establishes there is no denial anywhere in what was read stands at pmp:CelticEfficacyDenialSearch."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InsularCeltic .

pmc:AttainmentRelievesOnlyTheOutwardCondition a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Was die Erlangung erlassen darf, ist die außerhalb des Übenden festgesetzte Bedingung"@de,
        "What attainment is allowed to waive is the condition fixed outside the practitioner"@en ;
    skos:note "Falsified by a passage in any operative transmission granting an experienced practitioner relief from a bodily or conduct condition — a fast, a term of continence, a washing, a dietary rule — on the ground of his attainment, stated as a waiver of a rule the same text imposes on others. One such passage and the statement is gone. It is deliberately easy to break, because the interest of the statement is entirely in the asymmetry: a transmission that calls its astrology a pedagogy for beginners and will not say the same of its fasting is telling a reader which of the two it believes is doing the work."@en,
        "The counter-search was carried against the compiled holding before this was written, for waivers granted on attainment and for any case in which the thing waived is a rule of the body. The nearest case found does not refute the statement and does bound it: the Mahānirvāṇa Tantra divides practitioners into the bound, the heroic and the divine and makes the division govern which rites a person is competent for, so that the bound one may neither look at a Śūdra nor think of a woman while the heroic one performs a rite with wine, meat, fish, grain and union. That is not one practitioner released from a condition the transmission sets for all; it is a transmission that sets different conditions for different classes from the outset, and the wording above says relieves of one of its own prescribed conditions in order to keep the two apart. Whether the distinction holds under pressure is the first thing a later run should attack."@en,
        "The third witness is the negative half and it is the one that makes the statement more than a coincidence of two. The Zhen'gao states the loss from emission in a countable form and adds that even a person entered in the jade registers is not thereby exempt. There the question of a waiver for the advanced is raised explicitly, about a bodily condition, and answered no."@en,
        "The witnesses. The Key of Solomon says that so exact a preparation of days and hours is not necessary for those who are adepts in the art, and gives as the ground that beginners lack faith in the experiments; it waives nothing else, and a search of both books for a corresponding relief from the nine days, the fast, the bath or the confession came back empty. The eighteenth chapter of the Baopuzi says that one who can guard the One need not divine the day or choose the hour, no longer consults the siting calendars, and does not avoid the year star or the taboos of his year and fate. Both waivers are granted for attainment and both fall on the calendar."@en,
        "What it does not say. It does not say the waived condition is idle, nor that the unwaived one is effective; both are claims about efficacy and this is a claim about how transmissions talk. And the count is two positive witnesses and one negative, which is thin. The two are separated by nine centuries and by any route anyone has proposed, which is worth something; it is not worth a general law, and the statement is worded as a regularity in what gets waived and not as a rule about all transmissions."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:ClaviculaAdeptiExempti,
        pmc:ShangqingYijieQingyao,
        pmc:ShouyiMianLiri ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where an operative transmission relieves a practitioner of one of its own prescribed conditions on the ground of what he has attained, the condition relieved is one fixed outside him — a day, an hour, a calendar, a star — and not one bearing on his body or his conduct. Where the same transmissions speak of a bodily condition and attainment together, they say that the attainment does not relieve it."@en .

pmc:AttharasaSenasanaDosa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die achtzehn Fehler eines Klosters, jeder mit seiner Folge"@de,
        "The eighteen faults of a monastery, each with its consequence"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.2-18, Pali PTS [118]-[122] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, pp. 138-142; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "Eighteen properties that disqualify a monastery for one who is to develop concentration: largeness, newness, dilapidation, nearness to a road, a water tank, edible leaves, flowers, fruits, being sought after, nearness to a town, to timber, to arable fields, the presence of people of incompatible views, nearness to a port, to a border district, to the boundary of the realm, unsuitability, and the absence of a good friend within reach."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as one node and not as eighteen, and the reason is the shape of the passage and not economy. What the handbook states once is the disqualification of a place; the eighteen are the grounds it gives for it, with their consequences and their releases, and the claim a later reader would want to contest is the disqualification. The two that carry the sharpest consequence are named in pmc:SamadhiPresupposesSenasana, where they do work. Whoever wants the eighteen counted separately should reopen the chapter; the pages are named."@en,
        "For most of the eighteen the handbook also states the condition under which the fault is not a fault: a large monastery is admissible where all the duties are done and no noise reaches the ear; a new one where the other monks say they will do the work; and so on. The dilapidated one is given no such release. So the list is a set of defeasible conditions, and only one of them is stated without a way out."@en,
        "The handbook runs each of the eighteen with the harm it does, and the harms are practical and not spiritual: in a large one the duties are left undone, so he goes late into the village, so the almsgiving is over and he gets nothing, and the omission is itself an offence of wrongdoing; in a dilapidated one the meditation subject of the man doing the repairs suffers; where there is fruit, people come, are refused, grow angry, take by force, abuse him and try to have him expelled. The seventeenth, unsuitability, is the only one stated in two ways, by objects of the other sex and by the place being occupied by non-human beings, and it is the one the handbook illustrates with a case."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:AvibhavaTirobhava a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Er wird sichtbar und er wird unsichtbar"@de,
        "He becomes visible and he becomes invisible"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.69-87 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, pp. 452-456; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "He lights up a dark place, reveals what is hidden, brings into focus what is out of sight; and in the other direction he turns light into darkness, the unhidden into the hidden, what is within sight into what is out of it. Others at a distance then see as he himself sees, if he so wishes; or others near at hand do not see him, and he does not see them, if he so wishes."@en ;
    skos:note "Both directions are governed by the operator's wish, and the handbook says so twice in the same paragraph. That is the same switch that stands at the diving into earth and the walking on water, here applied to seeing rather than to a surface."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:Awenyddion a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die awenyddion, befragt über einen zweifelhaften Ausgang"@de,
        "The awenyddion, consulted upon a doubtful event"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Giraldus Cambrensis, Descriptio Kambriae I.16, in The Historical Works of Giraldus Cambrensis, trans. Thomas Wright (London: Bohn, 1863), pp. 501-502" ;
    skos:altLabel "awenyddion"@en,
        "awenydhyon"@en ;
    skos:definition "What the Welsh archdeacon reports of them in 1194. When consulted upon any doubtful event they roar out violently, are rendered beside themselves, and become as it were possessed by a spirit. They do not deliver the answer in a connected manner, but the person who skilfully observes them will find, after many preambles and many incoherent though ornamented speeches, the desired explanation conveyed in some turn of a word. They are then roused from their ecstasy as from a deep sleep and compelled as it were by violence to return to their senses; they do not recover until violently shaken by other people; and they cannot remember the replies they have given. The gifts are usually conferred upon them in dreams: some seem to have sweet milk or honey poured on their lips, others that a written schedule is applied to their mouths, and on waking they publicly declare that they have received the gift."@en ;
    skos:note "Reported by an outsider who is a churchman and who supplies his own aetiology, deriving the gift from Trojan descent and comparing it to Calchas and Cassandra. The report is entered for what it describes and not for what it explains, and the mode is set accordingly."@en,
        "Three elements match the Irish procedure closely enough to be worth naming and not close enough to be joined: a state entered for a question put by someone else, a return from it compared to waking from deep sleep, and a second party who must shake the practitioner out of it. The differences are as sharp: nothing is chewed, nothing is offered, there is no term, and the gift is not acquired by a procedure at all but conferred in a dream."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InsularCeltic .

pmc:AwenyddionAithchuinge a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zweimal zum selben Punkt befragt, antworten sie ganz verschieden"@de,
        "Consulted twice on the same point, they answer in wholly different terms"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Giraldus Cambrensis, Descriptio Kambriae I.16, in The Historical Works of Giraldus Cambrensis, trans. Thomas Wright (London: Bohn, 1863), pp. 501-502" ;
    skos:definition "The sentence the same chapter adds after the description: if consulted a second or third time upon the same point, they will make use of expressions totally different; perhaps they speak by the means of fanatic and ignorant spirits."@en ;
    skos:note "A stated failure of reproducibility, put by the reporter as an observation and not as a rule of the art, and it is the rarest sentence in this file. The repeated question is the cheapest test there is, somebody evidently ran it, and what came back is recorded as having come back different. The transmissions this holding carries mostly forbid the repeated question; here it was asked and answered, and the answer did not hold."@en,
        "It is an outsider's report and he attaches his own explanation to it, that they may speak by ignorant spirits. The explanation is his; the result of the repetition is what the node holds."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InsularCeltic .

pmc:AyurvedaAcararasayanaPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Verhalten allein bringt hervor, was die Drogen hervorbringen sollen"@de,
        "Conduct alone yields what the drugs are said to yield"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.4.57, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXV, Calcutta 1905, p. 1066" ;
    skos:note "The claim is stated in the next verse and not in the list: he who, endued with these high attributes, uses rasayana, enjoys all the effects enumerated of rasayana. Read with the verse after it, which says that one who has not cast off the graver faults never reaps the effects, the conduct is doing two jobs at once — it is a route to the result and it is the condition on every other route. The lesson does not separate them and this holding should not pretend it does."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AyurvedaAcararasayana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AyurvedaDirghayus .

pmc:AyurvedaAindrirasayanaPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Aindri-Rasayana bringt Unangreifbarkeit durch Beschwörung, Unglück und Gift hervor"@de,
        "The Aindri rasayana yields immunity from incantation, from adversity and from poison"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.3.28-33, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, p. 1048" ;
    skos:note "It is the foremost of all those things which generate ojas; its potency has been well tested; no incantation according to Atharvan rites can injure a man who takes this medicine; Alaksmi cannot approach him; poison cannot affect him; neither does he succumb to any disease. Four immunities and a claim of testing in five consecutive clauses."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AyurvedaAindrirasayana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AyurvedaKrtyapratighata,
        pmc:AyurvedaOjovrddhi,
        pmc:AyurvedaSmrtimedha,
        pmc:AyurvedaVisavighata .

pmc:AyurvedaAmalakacurnaPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Amalaka-Pulver bringt hundert Jahre ohne ein Zeichen des Verfalls hervor"@de,
        "The amalaka powder yields a hundred years without a sign of decrepitude"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.2.22, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, p. 1034" ;
    skos:note "Stated by reference, in the same formula as the extract before it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AyurvedaAmalakacurna ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AyurvedaDirghayus .

pmc:AyurvedaAmalakaghrtaSahasrapakaPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das hundertfach gekochte Ghee bringt hundert Jahre hervor, mit bis zuletzt ungeschwächten Geisteskräften"@de,
        "The hundred-cooked ghee yields a hundred years with the powers of mind unimpaired to the last"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.2.13-16, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, pp. 1032-1033" ;
    skos:note "One lives a hundred years without disease; the powers of mind remain in full vigour for the entire period; all diseases disappear; the power of sexual congress is retained throughout life; the body has the strength of adamant and the senses are unimpaired; the voice resembles the roar of the clouds or of the surging ocean; the children are many and long-lived. The claim about the children is the only one in the lesson that would be settled in a generation and not in a century."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AyurvedaAmalakaghrtaSahasrapaka ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AyurvedaDehasamhanana,
        pmc:AyurvedaDirghayus,
        pmc:AyurvedaSmrtimedha .

pmc:AyurvedaAmalakarasayanaCaturthaPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das vierte Amalaka-Rasayana bringt den Sehern ihre Jugend und viele Jahrhunderte Leben hervor"@de,
        "The fourth amalaka rasayana yields the seers their youth and many centuries of life"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.1.74, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIII, Calcutta 1904, p. 1027" ;
    skos:note "Through the use of this rasayana the seers got back their youth and succeeded in living for many centuries, freed from disease and endued with great strength of body, of understanding and of the senses; it was through it that they were able to practise severe penances. The claim is again attached to the past and to no living user."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AyurvedaAmalakarasayanaCaturtha ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AyurvedaDirghayus,
        pmc:AyurvedaVayahsthapana .

pmc:AyurvedaAmalakavalehaDvitiyaPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der zweite Amalaka-Extrakt bringt hundert Jahre ohne Merkmale des Verfalls hervor"@de,
        "The second amalaka extract yields a hundred years without symptoms of decrepitude"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.2.26, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, p. 1036" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AyurvedaAmalakavalehaDvitiya ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AyurvedaDirghayus .

pmc:AyurvedaAmalakavalehaPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der Amalaka-Extrakt bringt hundert Jahre ohne ein Zeichen des Verfalls hervor"@de,
        "The amalaka extract yields a hundred years without a sign of decrepitude"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.2.20, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, p. 1034" ;
    skos:note "The first of the preparations whose claim is stated by reference: one lives a hundred years without any sign of decrepitude, and as regards its other virtues they agree with those of the rasayana spoken of before. The formula recurs five times in this section and once in the third, and each time it transfers a whole list of capacities to a new preparation without restating any of them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AyurvedaAmalakavaleha ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AyurvedaDirghayus .

pmc:AyurvedaAmalakayasarasayanaPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Amalaka-Eisen-Rasayana bringt Freiheit von Ermüdung, Krankheit und Verfall hervor, und Leben, die die Zeit nicht berührte"@de,
        "The amalaka and iron rasayana yields freedom from fatigue, disease and decrepitude, and lives Time did not touch"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.3.8-12, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, pp. 1044-1045" ;
    skos:note "The verse after it is the one place in the lesson where the transmission accounts for those who had no rasayana. There were no great seers before that time; there was no rasayana; the men of those days were boorish, engaged in acts other than penances, and of souls entirely unrestrained; hence success they had not. The failure of an earlier age is thus assigned to the conduct of its people and to the absence of the drug at once, which is the same double account the fourth section gives of a living user."@en,
        "Vasistha, Kasyapa, Angiras, Jamadagni, Bharadvaja, Bhrgu and others like them, having taken this rasayana under proper restraints, became free from the fear of fatigue, disease and decrepitude, and their lives did not come under the touch of Time. The translator glosses the last phrase: they lived as long as they liked and death did not come to them until they desired it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AyurvedaAmalakayasarasayana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AyurvedaDirghayus,
        pmc:AyurvedaVayahsthapana .

pmc:AyurvedaBhallatakaksaudraPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der Bhallataka-Saft mit Honig bringt hundert Jahre ohne Verfall hervor"@de,
        "The bhallataka juice with honey yields a hundred years without decrepitude"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.2.43, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, p. 1040" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AyurvedaBhallatakaksaudra ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AyurvedaDirghayus .

pmc:AyurvedaBhallatakaksiraPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Bhallataka-Kur in Milch bringt hundert Jahre ohne Verfall hervor"@de,
        "The bhallataka course in milk yields a hundred years without decrepitude"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.2.40, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, p. 1039" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AyurvedaBhallatakaksira ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AyurvedaDirghayus .

pmc:AyurvedaBhallatakamatra a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Kein Übermaß über dreißig, und nicht mehr als tausend Früchte"@de,
        "No excess above thirty, and not more than a thousand fruits"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.2.38 and I.2.46, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, pp. 1039 and 1041" ;
    skos:definition "In the administration of the bhallatakas there should be no excess above the number thirty, nor should one take more than a thousand fruits agreeably to the method indicated. The ceiling is stated as two numbers, a daily one and a total, and it stands immediately after the ramp that reaches the first of them."@en ;
    skos:note "A caution with two exact numbers and no stated penalty is a rarer thing than it looks, and it is the shape a later reader should test against the other direction: the same lesson states a ceiling here and states no ceiling anywhere else, including for the aconite it measures in sesame seeds."@en,
        "The only quantitative limit in the lesson and the only place where a rasayana is spoken of as something a body can have too much of. The lesson says why the drug needs one, in the verses that close the section: bhallatakas are keen, they promote digestion, they are like fire, and administered according to the ordinances laid down they become like nectar. The consequence of exceeding the ceiling is not stated."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:AyurvedaBhallatakaksira ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmc:AyurvedaBhallatakatailaPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Bhallataka-Öl bringt hervor, was die zwei Zubereitungen vor ihm hervorbringen"@de,
        "The bhallataka oil yields what the two preparations before it yield"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.2.44, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, p. 1041" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AyurvedaBhallatakataila ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AyurvedaDirghayus .

pmc:AyurvedaBhallatakavidhiPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Recht gegebenes Bhallataka bringt die Linderung jeder Schleimkrankheit und jeder Verstopfung hervor"@de,
        "Bhallataka rightly administered yields the alleviation of every disease of phlegm and every constipation"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.2.46-48, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, pp. 1041-1042" ;
    skos:note "There is no disease born of phlegm and no form of constipation which bhallataka properly administered cannot alleviate promptly; and it enhances mental powers and the digestive fire. The claim is universal over a class of disease and it is guarded by the clause properly administered, which is the guard the fourth section generalizes."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AyurvedaBhallatakavidhi ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AyurvedaSmrtimedha .

pmc:AyurvedaBrahmarasayanaDvitiyaPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das zweite Brahmya-Rasayana bringt ein Gedächtnis hervor, das alles Gehörte behält, und ein Gift, das seine Kraft verliert"@de,
        "The second Brahmya rasayana yields memory that retains all it hears, and poison that loses its force"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.1.58-60, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIII, Calcutta 1904, pp. 1023-1024" ;
    skos:note "The fullest list in the first section: freedom from disease, longevity, great strength, the love and affection of all creatures, the fruition of all desires, a complexion like the moon or the sun, memory improved so that he retains all he hears, a mind like that of the seers, a body compact as a mountain, powers like the wind, and poison that loses its force on contact."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AyurvedaBrahmarasayanaDvitiya ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AyurvedaDehasamhanana,
        pmc:AyurvedaSmrtimedha,
        pmc:AyurvedaVisavighata .

pmc:AyurvedaBrahmarasayanaPrathamaPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das erste Brahmya-Rasayana bringt unermessliche Lebensdauer und einen gegen einen neuen getauschten Körper hervor"@de,
        "The first Brahmya rasayana yields immeasurable longevity and a body exchanged for a new one"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.1.53-56, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIII, Calcutta 1904, p. 1022" ;
    skos:note "Freed from decayed bodies they obtained bodies that were foremost of their kind, as also youthfulness of years; freed from langour and fatigue and shortness of breath, without disease of any kind, with minds concentrated. The claim is made of ascetics of the remote past and then transferred to the reader in the next verse: one desirous of longevity should use this rasayana, and by so doing is sure to attain to long life."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AyurvedaBrahmarasayanaPrathama ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AyurvedaVayahsthapana .

pmc:AyurvedaCyavanaprasaPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Cyavanaprasa bringt einem schon sehr Alten die Jugend hervor"@de,
        "Cyavanaprasa yields youth to one already very old"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.1.70-73, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIII, Calcutta 1904, pp. 1025-1026" ;
    skos:note "The lesson calls it the foremost of all rasayanas and adds a list of diseases it cures — cough, asthma, loss of voice, diseases of the chest and heart, leprosy, thirst, diseases of the urine and of the vital seed — so that the same preparation is claimed both as a rasayana for the healthy and as a remedy. The translator's note supplies the story of Cyavana and the Asvins from the epic; the lesson gives only the name."@en,
        "Through administration of this medicine Cyavana, who had become very old, once more became youthful; and if one that is even decrepit enters the chamber spoken of and takes this rasayana, he soon casts off his decrepit form and acquires that of prime of youth. The second sentence is the only place in the lesson where the hut is made a condition of a named preparation's strongest claim rather than a route to rasayana in general."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AyurvedaCyavanaprasa ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AyurvedaVayahsthapana .

pmc:AyurvedaDosaprakopahetu a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Was die Fehler erregt und vor dem Beginn des Rasayana aufzugeben ist"@de,
        "What excites the faults, and must be given up before the rasayana is begun"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.2.5-9, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, pp. 1030-1031" ;
    skos:definition "The second section opens with a long list of what excites the faults of the body: sour, saline, pungent, alkaline and dry food, certain pulses, sesame, flesh and cakes; sprouted or new rice; incompatible, unassimilable or secretion-suppressing food; filthy, heavy, putrid or stale food; irregular meals and eating before the last is digested; sleep by day, women and wine; shocks from excessive exercise or untoward postures; fear, wrath, cupidity, heedlessness, grief and severe toil. From the excitement of the faults the flesh becomes lax, the joints slacken, the blood heats, the adeps escapes, the marrow does not adhere to the bones, semen is not generated and the ojas wastes; and such a person does not attain the full limit of the period of life assigned to human beings."@en ;
    skos:note "A causal chain with seven named bodily consequences and one stated outcome, and it runs in the opposite direction from everything else in the lesson: here the transmission says what shortens a life rather than what lengthens one. The negative claim is the more nearly checkable of the two and it is the one the lesson does not return to."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:AyurvedaRasayana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmc:AyurvedaDosatyagavivada a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der fehlerhafte Anwender nichts erhält oder nur den gröberen Teil"@de,
        "Whether the faulty user gets nothing or only the grosser part"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.4.58, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXV, Calcutta 1905, p. 1066, with the translator's note",
        "Gangadhara Kaviraja Ray, Jalpakalparu, commentary on Cikitsasthana I.4.58, as reported in A. C. Kaviratna's note at that place, part XXXV, Calcutta 1905, p. 1066" ;
    skos:definition "Two readings of the same verse, and they differ on exactly the point that decides whether the condition is an excuse or a rule. Gangadhara, in his commentary, takes it to mean that one who uses rasayana without casting off the faults of body and mind does not obtain the superior or subtler virtues of rasayana although he may reap its grosser virtues. The translator rejects that reading and takes the verse to say that if one takes rasayana while indulging in all sorts of evil practices, one can never be benefited by it at all; he grounds the rejection on common sense and on the verse that follows."@en ;
    skos:note "Both sides are in one book and neither is a separate transmission: the commentator is of the seventeenth to nineteenth century and the translator of 1905. The dispute is entered because it is about the reading of a passage this file carries and because the choice was made in the only edition read; it is not evidence of a disagreement between traditions."@en,
        "Worth a node because the two readings are not a nuance. On Gangadhara's reading the condition grades the result and a user who got a little has the rule confirmed; on the translator's it is a switch, and a user who got a little has a case against the rule. The wording of the failure clause therefore decides whether the claim can be met by any outcome at all, and the transmission as it reaches a reader in English has already had that decision made for it by an editor who says so."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:AyurvedaDosatyagapurvakatva ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmc:AyurvedaDronipravesikaPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Sechs Monate im Gefäß bringen zehntausend Jahre hervor, Rede, die das Vergangene offenbart, und tausend Yojana in einem Zug"@de,
        "Six months in the vessel yield ten thousand years, speech that reveals the past and a thousand yojanas at a stretch"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.4.27-29, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, p. 1061" ;
    skos:note "When the process is continued for full six months one becomes like unto a celestial in age, complexion, voice, shape, strength and lustre; one's speech reveals even the past; one's eyes and ears become like those of the immortals; one can traverse a thousand yojanas at a stretch; one's period of life is extended to full ten thousand years, uninterrupted by any ailment."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AyurvedaDronipravesika ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AyurvedaAtitajnana,
        pmc:AyurvedaDuragamana,
        pmc:AyurvedaVayahsthapana .

pmc:AyurvedaDronipunarudbhava a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zu nichts aufgezehrt, und binnen sechs Monaten wieder gewachsen"@de,
        "Attenuated into nothingness, and grown again within six months"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.4.26, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, p. 1061" ;
    skos:definition "The course of the procedure in the wooden vessel, given as what happens rather than as what is done: one subjecting oneself to this process becomes attenuated into nothingness; one grows again within six months; goat's milk is one's sustenance."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered among the signs because it is what the transmission offers a user in place of a criterion: a described course whose stages he would pass through, announced beforehand, and observable by him and by whoever feeds him. It is the most extreme statement in the lesson and it is also the one that would be settled soonest, which is why it is worth having the wording rather than a summary of it."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmc:AyurvedaHaritakirasayanaPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Haritaki-Rasayana bringt einen von der Furcht vor Beschwörungen befreiten Körper hervor"@de,
        "The haritaki rasayana yields a body freed from the fear of incantations"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.1.75, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIII, Calcutta 1904, p. 1027" ;
    skos:note "With body freed from decrepitude, disease, sin and fear of incantations, and acquiring extraordinary strength of understanding and the senses, with all efforts crowned with success, one would attain to a long life. Sin and the fear of incantations stand in the same list as decrepitude and disease, without a change of register."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AyurvedaHaritakirasayana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AyurvedaDirghayus,
        pmc:AyurvedaKrtyapratighata .

pmc:AyurvedaIndropadistarasayanaPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Sechs Monate von Indras Paste bringen Lebensdauer, bewahrte Jugend und gestärktes Gedächtnis hervor"@de,
        "Six months of Indra's paste yield longevity, preserved youth and a strengthened memory"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.4.15-17, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, p. 1059" ;
    skos:note "It promotes longevity, preserves youth, prevents disease, confers wealth of voice and complexion, sharpens the understanding, strengthens the memory, gives great strength and produces other desirable effects; and it is a well-tried preparation. The fourth of the five claims of trial in the lesson."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AyurvedaIndropadistarasayana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AyurvedaDirghayus,
        pmc:AyurvedaSmrtimedha,
        pmc:AyurvedaVayahsthapana .

pmc:AyurvedaKevalamalakaPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Tausend Jahre Jugend für jede gegessene Frucht"@de,
        "A thousand years of youth for each fruit eaten"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.3.17-18, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, p. 1046" ;
    skos:note "One will live in the enjoyment of youth for as many thousands of years as the number of fruits one eats according to this method; by eating them until hunger is gratified one becomes like unto an immortal; and Sri, the Vedas and Speech in her embodied form come to such a person. The claim is the only one in the lesson that is stated as a proportion, and the quantity it is proportional to is the one thing in the whole procedure the user controls."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AyurvedaKevalamalaka ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AyurvedaVayahsthapana .

pmc:AyurvedaKosthasuddhilinga a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Darm klar und der Stuhl geregelt"@de,
        "The bowels clear and the stools regular"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.1.26-27, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIII, Calcutta 1904, p. 1019" ;
    skos:definition "The sign at which the preliminary cleansing is complete and the rasayana may be given: the barley gruel is continued for three, five or seven nights till the bowels become clear and the stools become regular, and physicians then ascertain that the cleansing has been properly done."@en ;
    skos:note "A sign of a preliminary and not of the operation, which is why it is entered separately from the one above. It is nonetheless the only place in the lesson where a physician other than the user certifies a state of the user's body, and it is the model of what the operation itself lacks."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmc:AyurvedaLauhadirasayanaPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Jahr Eisen-Rasayana bringt einen Menschen hervor, der weder Gewalt noch Krankheit, Alter oder Tod erliegt"@de,
        "A year of the iron rasayana yields a man who does not succumb to violence, disease, age or death"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.3.26-27, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, p. 1047" ;
    skos:note "The person who takes rasayana made of iron for a whole year becomes endued with the strength of an elephant and possessed of senses in their full vigour; he never succumbs to aggressions of violence committed on his person, or to diseases of any kind, or to the decrepitude of age, or to death itself; whatever he utters becomes accomplished; whatever he hears even once he remembers. The strongest claim of the lesson, attached to the most ordinary of its procedures."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AyurvedaLauhadirasayana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AyurvedaDehasamhanana,
        pmc:AyurvedaSmrtimedha,
        pmc:AyurvedaVaksiddhi .

pmc:AyurvedaLauhaghrtaPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Drei Jahre Eisen-Ghee bringen hundert Jahre hervor, einen steinharten Körper und einen von keinem Geschöpf Angreifbaren"@de,
        "Three years of the iron ghee yield a hundred years, a body compact as stone, and a person unassailable by all creatures"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.1.76, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIII, Calcutta 1904, p. 1028" ;
    skos:note "He lives for a hundred years freed from decrepitude or decay; he succeeds in retaining what he hears; no disease overtakes him; if poison comes in contact with his body it fails to affect him; his body becomes as compact as stone; he becomes irresistible to view and unassailable by all creatures. Six claims in one verse, of which the third and the fourth would be settled by a single instance and the first by none."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AyurvedaLauhaghrta ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AyurvedaDehasamhanana,
        pmc:AyurvedaDirghayus,
        pmc:AyurvedaSmrtimedha,
        pmc:AyurvedaVisavighata .

pmc:AyurvedaMedhakararasayanaPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Verstandes-Rasayanas bringen Verstandesstärke hervor, und eines von ihnen besonders"@de,
        "The understanding-rasayanas yield strength of understanding, and one of them especially"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.3.35, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, p. 1049" ;
    skos:note "These rasayanas promote longevity, destroy disease, increase strength and improve digestion, complexion and voice; they also invigorate the understanding; and of these, that made of sankhapuspi especially conduces to strength of understanding. A comparative judgement between four preparations of the same kind, given without a ground."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AyurvedaMedhakararasayana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AyurvedaDirghayus,
        pmc:AyurvedaSmrtimedha .

pmc:AyurvedaNagabalarasayanaPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Jahr der Nagabala-Wurzel bringt hundert Jahre ohne Verfall hervor"@de,
        "A year of the nagabala root yields a hundred years without decrepitude"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.2.29-30, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, p. 1037" ;
    skos:note "By taking this medicine continuously for one year one lives for a hundred years without any indication of decrepitude; the other virtues are those of the medicines mentioned before. A year of daily observance is the condition and the verification lies ninety-nine years beyond it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AyurvedaNagabalarasayana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AyurvedaDirghayus .

pmc:AyurvedaPippalirasayanaPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Langpfeffer-Rasayana heilt eine benannte Liste von vierzehn Leiden"@de,
        "The long pepper rasayana cures a named list of fourteen disorders"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.3.37-39, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, p. 1049" ;
    skos:note "Bronchitis, phthisis, asthma, consumption, hiccup, all diseases of the throat, piles, the disease of the grahani, chlorosis, obstinate fever, hoarseness, inflammation of the nasal membrane with loss of smell, inflammatory swellings, abdominal tumours, and all diseases born of excited wind and phlegm. A list of conditions is the most nearly checkable form any claim in the lesson takes, and it is attached to the preparation with the smallest dose and the longest term."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AyurvedaPippalirasayana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AyurvedaOjovrddhi .

pmc:AyurvedaPippalivardhamanaPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die steigende Langpfeffer-Kur bringt Kraft, Stimme und Lebensdauer hervor und wirkt dem Alter entgegen"@de,
        "The rising course of long pepper yields strength, voice and longevity and counteracts age"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.3.44, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, p. 1051" ;
    skos:note "It promotes strength, improves the voice and conduces to longevity; it alleviates enlarged spleen and all other diseases of the stomach; it counteracts the effects of age and sharpens intellect. The three grades of the course are ranked by efficacy without a ground being given for the ranking, and the grade is chosen by the strength of the user."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AyurvedaPippalivardhamana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AyurvedaDirghayus,
        pmc:AyurvedaSmrtimedha,
        pmc:AyurvedaVayahsthapana .

pmc:AyurvedaPranavamadhurya a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Frucht wird in der Hand süß, während die Silbe rezitiert wird"@de,
        "The fruit turns sweet in the hand while the syllable is recited"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.3.15-17, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, pp. 1045-1046" ;
    skos:definition "The one sign in the lesson that is announced before the operation and is perceived during it. Holding the plucked fruits, one mentally recites the pranava until nectar comes into them; while the syllable is being recited nectar will come into the fruits for at least a short space of time, and at that time the fruits will become as sweet as sugar or honey, juicy and soft, in consequence of their union with nectar. The translator adds that this implies their astringent taste will disappear."@en ;
    skos:note "It is the only criterion in Lesson I that a user could apply on the day, and the thing it turns on is a taste. An amalaka is astringent; the sign is that it stops being so. Announced in advance, reported by the person who wants it, and settled by nobody else — the shape the separate counting of signs exists for."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmc:AyurvedaRasayanapradanavidhi a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Wem die Lektion nicht mitgeteilt werden darf"@de,
        "To whom the lesson may not be imparted"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.4.60, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXV, Calcutta 1905, p. 1066" ;
    skos:definition "All this that has been said of rasayana should not be imparted to those persons who have slain their selves, or to those who are free from ailments, or who do not belong to the twice-born classes, or who have no desire to hear it. The translator glosses the first as one addicted to evil practices of every kind or who disregards all duties, and leaves open whether the second qualifies the third, in which case the excluded would be the healthy of the fourth class."@en ;
    skos:note "The restriction stands inside the operative text, at the end of the section that gives the procedures, and it names the classes of excluded recipient rather than the permitted one. It gives no ground, unlike the transmissions elsewhere in this holding that attribute the restriction to the authority of the contents; the lesson simply forbids."@en,
        "The second exclusion is the strange one and the translator marks it as unclear. Rasayana is defined at the head of the lesson as the medicine for the healthy; a rule excluding the healthy from hearing about it would contradict the definition, which is presumably why the reading that ties it to the fourth class is offered."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:AyurvedaRasayana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:AyurvedaAcararasayana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmc:AyurvedaSamsodhanapurvakatva a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Reinigung muss vollständig sein, bevor das Rasayana gegeben wird"@de,
        "The cleansing must be complete before the rasayana is given"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.1.24-27, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIII, Calcutta 1904, pp. 1018-1019" ;
    skos:definition "The order the lesson sets and the only one it makes a physician certify. Oil and sweating, then the corrective powders in hot water, then light food, then barley gruel with ghee for three, five or seven nights until the bowels are clear and the stools regular; and only when physicians conversant with age, constitution and power of assimilation have ascertained that the bowels are properly cleansed is the rasayana administered."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity, because the lesson gives the sequence and the certification and does not say at this place what follows if the sequence is broken. What it does say, two sections later and in general terms, is that disease sets in from a deviation; that is a separate node and it is not read back into this one."@en,
        "The translator supplies the reason in a footnote at the fourth preparation — before beginning to take rasayana one requires one's body to be corrected or purified — and that is his gloss on the same rule, not a second statement of it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:AyurvedaRasayana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:AyurvedaSamsodhana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmc:AyurvedaSilajatukulattha a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Hülsenfrucht, die mit Silajatu untersagt ist, und der angegebene Grund"@de,
        "The pulse that is interdicted with silajatu, and the reason given"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.3.66-67, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, pp. 1054-1055" ;
    skos:definition "While using silajatu one should avoid food that causes an acrid or burning sensation and food that is heavy; and the seeds of kulattha should as a rule be avoided. Since these seeds are exceedingly hostile to it, they are pre-eminently piercers of stone, as seen by men; hence their use is interdicted. The translator sets out the inference: silajatu is the product of stone, kulattha is seen to break stone, therefore kulattha is hostile to silajatu."@en ;
    skos:note "The one caution in the lesson that gives its ground, and the ground is an argument from a likeness which the text marks as something men have observed. It is also the only prohibition here that could be examined, because both of its terms are things and one of the two assertions about them is a claim about what kulattha does to stone."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:AyurvedaSilajaturasayana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmc:AyurvedaSilajaturasayanaPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Es gibt auf Erden keine heilbare Krankheit, die Silajatu nicht bezwingen könnte"@de,
        "There is no curable disease on earth which silajatu cannot conquer"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.3.69, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, p. 1055" ;
    skos:note "Entered without a protocol and without an examination state, because no protocol was opened on it in this run. It is named here as the clearest candidate the lesson offers for the state in which a claim treats a result and its opposite alike."@en,
        "The widest claim in the lesson and the one that guards itself twice in a single sentence. There is no such curable disease on earth which silajatu cannot conquer; administered to a healthy person, according to the ordinances in respect of its combinations, it imparts excessive vigour to him. The word curable exempts every case that does not recover, and the clause according to the ordinances exempts every case that was not done right; between them nothing is left that could count against the claim."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AyurvedaSilajaturasayana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AyurvedaDehasamhanana,
        pmc:AyurvedaDirghayus,
        pmc:AyurvedaSmrtimedha .

pmc:AyurvedaSuvarnarajatarasayanaPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Gold- und Silber-Rasayanas verlängern das Leben und vertreiben Krankheiten aller Art"@de,
        "The gold and silver rasayanas extend life and dispel all sorts of disease"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.3.25, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, p. 1047" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AyurvedaSuvarnarajatarasayana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AyurvedaDirghayus .

pmc:AyurvedaSvarasarasayanaPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Presssäfte bringen hervor, was die Nagabala-Wurzel hervorbringt"@de,
        "The expressed juices yield what the nagabala root yields"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.2.31, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, p. 1038" ;
    skos:note "The virtues of the two medicines are equal — the translator's words for the text's reference. Ten of the nineteen drugs are named by the technical term for arresting age, so the capacity is carried by the classification of the drug and not by a statement about this preparation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AyurvedaSvarasarasayana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AyurvedaDirghayus,
        pmc:AyurvedaVayahsthapana .

pmc:AyurvedaTriphalarasayanaPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Jahr der drei Myrobalanen bringt hundert vom Alter unberührte Jahre hervor"@de,
        "A year of the three myrobalans yields a hundred years untouched by age"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.3.46 and I.3.50-51, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, pp. 1051-1052" ;
    skos:note "By its use one would live for a full hundred years without being influenced by age and without being afflicted by disease; used for a full year any of these would sharpen the understanding and the memory, infuse strength, impart longevity, counteract the effects of age and alleviate diseases."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AyurvedaTriphalarasayana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AyurvedaDirghayus,
        pmc:AyurvedaSmrtimedha .

pmc:AyurvedaVaidikarasayanaPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das vedische Rasayana bringt das Vermögen hervor, das Vergangene zu wissen, und wendet Gift und Unglück ab"@de,
        "The Vedic rasayana yields the power of knowing the past and turns aside poison and adversity"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.4.46-48, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXV, Calcutta 1905, pp. 1064-1065" ;
    skos:note "It is alleviative of all sorts of diseases; it generates semen; it promotes longevity; it strengthens mind, memory, body, digestive fire, intellect and senses; it is the foremost of all medicines that impart vigour; it improves the complexion; it counteracts the action of poisons and the genius of adversity; and it imparts the power of knowing the past. The easiest of the three routes carries the second-widest set of claims in the lesson."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AyurvedaVaidikarasayana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AyurvedaAtitajnana,
        pmc:AyurvedaKrtyapratighata,
        pmc:AyurvedaOjovrddhi,
        pmc:AyurvedaVisavighata .

pmc:AyurvedaVidangavalehaPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der Vidanga-Extrakt bringt hervor, was die Zubereitungen vor ihm hervorbringen"@de,
        "The vidanga extract yields what the preparations before it yield"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.2.23, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, p. 1035" ;
    skos:note "The claim is made entirely by reference — the effects are the same as those of the medicines mentioned above — and the preparation shares no ingredient with the amalaka extracts it is referred to. A reader who wanted to know what this one does has to read the promise off a different recipe."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AyurvedaVidangavaleha ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AyurvedaDirghayus .

pmc:AzazelTeachesAdornment a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Azazel offenbart Schmuck und Kosmetik"@de,
        "Azazel discloses adornment and cosmetics"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:1 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:AdornmentAndCosmetics ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Azazel ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:AzazelTeachesMetalworking a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Azazel offenbart Metallverarbeitung"@de,
        "Azazel discloses metalworking"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:1 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:Metalworking ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Azazel ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:BadMideCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Der böse Midē, der die Vermögen zum Schaden wendet"@de,
        "The bad Midē who turns the powers to harm"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), pp. 168-169" ;
    skos:definition "The society names a figure of its own kind who employs the powers for evil purposes, who can assume the form of any animal and in that shape destroy the life of his victim. The warning is not against an outsider but against a member who has passed the same degrees."@en ;
    skos:note "A tradition that catalogues capacities and warns against their use is the shape this collection meets in Pātañjala Yoga as well, and the two warnings are not the same warning. There the capacities are called obstacles to the goal of the practice; here they are called dangerous in the hands of a man who wants harm. One is a caution about the practitioner's direction, the other about his intention."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:MideDistantTouch,
        pmc:MideSoulSeizing ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin .

pmc:BaopuziAgainstTheZhuangzi a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob Laozi und Zhuangzi den Weg zu den Unsterblichen tragen"@de,
        "Whether the Laozi and the Zhuangzi carry the way to the transcendents"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 8 (Shizhi), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 8",
        "Zhuangzi 4, in James Legge (trans.), The Sacred Books of China: The Texts of Tâoism, Part I, Sacred Books of the East vol. XXXIX (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1891), Book IV, par. 2, pp. 208-210" ;
    skos:definition "The eighth chapter sets itself against the books this file also rests on. Of the five thousand words it says that they are general and cursory throughout and nowhere set out a matter from head to tail so that it could be taken up and followed, and that to recite the classic by heart without the essential way is labour thrown away. Of Wenzi, Zhuangzi and Yin Xi it says that their writing derives from the Yellow Lord and Laozi and follows their broad intent but never states the decisive word, and that where they level life and death, call them no different, treat being alive as a corvée and dying as a rest, they are already a hundred thousand million li away from the transcendents. Their parables it allows for small uses only."@en ;
    skos:note "This is a dispute inside one tradition and inside one file, and it is worth a node because the graph would otherwise carry the Zhuangzi passage and the Baopuzi passage side by side as though they belonged together. The most systematic practitioner in the corpus says they do not. Whoever builds a convergence on Daoist material has to say which of the two sides he is counting."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:EmptyingOfTheHeart ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BaopuziNeipian,
        pmw:Zhuangzi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:BaraqelTeachesAstrology a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Baraqel offenbart die Astrologie"@de,
        "Baraqel discloses astrology"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:Astrology ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Baraqel ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:BaraqelTeachesSignsOfLightning a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Baraqel offenbart die Zeichen der Blitze"@de,
        "Baraqel discloses the signs of the lightning flashes"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)" ;
    skos:note "Stands in the position the Ethiopic fills with astrology."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:SignsOfLightning ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Baraqel ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:BarasnumExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Reinigung, die es schlimmer macht"@de,
        "The purification that makes it worse"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Vendidad, fargard 9.47-49, trans. James Darmesteter, Sacred Books of the East 4, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon, 1895)" ;
    skos:definition "A protocol taken from the transmission's own reasoning. The claim is not that an unqualified purification is ineffective but that it increases sickness and death in the world beyond what they were. That is a directional claim about an outcome outside the practitioner, and it separates two states of affairs that a later observer could tell apart."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at procedure without cases. No series has been collected, and none is available from within the transmission: the man who would provide the case is executed before the outcome could be recorded, which is itself a feature of the protocol worth stating rather than passing over."@en,
        "The transmission supplies the falsifying condition without meaning to. It could have said that the wrong rite simply does not cleanse, which nothing would contradict; instead it named an effect in the opposite direction from the one intended, and thereby excluded an outcome. Where a claim of failure predicts nothing, this one predicts an increase."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, protocol stated from the witness; no case series has been collected" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A recorded series of purifications carried out by persons known to be unqualified, after which sickness and death in the affected place did not rise above the rate where the rite was performed correctly or not at all. The transmission asserts an increase, so a flat or falling rate falls due against it." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:BarasnumCaution .

pmc:BengeAtFirstContact a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Giftprobe beim ersten dauerhaften Kontakt"@de,
        "The poison ordeal at the first sustained contact"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Georg Schweinfurth, The Heart of Africa, trans. Ellen E. Frewer (London: Sampson Low, Marston, Low and Searle, 1873), vol. II, chapter XIII, the account of the Niam-niam praying-machine and auguries" ;
    skos:definition "The earliest European account, from travels of eighteen sixty-eight to eighteen seventy-one, already describes the whole apparatus: an oily preparation from a red wood, given to a hen, whose death or survival decides the question; the rubbing-board consulted before any undertaking of consequence; and the use of both to establish the guilt or innocence of the accused, with suspected witches tried by the same ordeal. It records a case in which a hostile chief called off an attack because his fowl had died."@en ;
    skos:note "The corroborative second test is not in this account. That is an argument about the account and not about the transmission: the writer was a botanist passing through with an ivory company, not an observer of consultations, and he reports the outcome of single fowls because that is what he saw. What the silence does establish is that the double test is not attested before the monograph in anything read here, and the question of when it can first be shown is left open as an issue."@en,
        "The passage is the reason the contact route on this tradition is not simply documented. The oracles stand in the record at the first sustained contact with the Islamic north and cannot have come in with it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:BengeCorroborationProtocol a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Rate, mit der der Bestätigungstest scheitert"@de,
        "The rate at which the corroborative test fails"@en ;
    skos:definition "A count of the corroborated consultations printed in the source: how often the second test fell opposite to the first, as the transmission's rule of validity requires, and how often it fell alike and voided the verdict."@en ;
    skos:note "Of the two seances printed, the first carries two corroborated pairs and both are recorded as contradictory: a question about taking on the taboos of vengeance against a question about the questioner's own survival under them, and a question about a man's safety in a named settlement against its explicit corroboration. The second seance carries one explicitly corroborative question, and it agreed; the recorder calls that seance a wholly successful consultation. Three pairs, two of them void by the transmission's own rule."@en,
        "Status of what follows. The state above is procedure without cases, set when the counter-search requirement was introduced, because this node does not record whether a search for opposing work was made. Its notes are older than the requirement and are kept unchanged; whoever documents the search restores the case-bearing state. See prima-materia#386."@en,
        "The count is the recorder's material and the counting is this graph's. He states outright that the seances are given so that the reader may judge for himself the proportion of fowls that die and the order of deaths and survivals, so the arithmetic is invited by the source and is not imposed on it."@en,
        "The state is cases with deviation and not cases without, and the deviation is from the transmission's own stated requirement rather than from a base rate of chance. A base rate is deliberately not entered: the poison is administered in a number of doses that varies with the size of the fowl and with the operator's reading of its earlier answers, so the probability that a given fowl dies is not fixed and no honest chance rate can be stated from what was read."@en,
        "Three pairs decide nothing about a rate and the number is entered so that a later reader can see how small it is. What it does establish is that the falsifying condition is not hypothetical: it occurred in two of the three cases this graph has actually read, and the source says elsewhere that the oracle frequently kills or spares both fowls."@en,
        "What the deviation is not. It is not evidence against the oracle in the transmission's own terms, because the transmission assigns every occurrence of the condition to one of eight causes that leave the instrument untouched. That is why the finding of this protocol is about the standing of the falsifying condition inside the transmission and not about the oracle's accuracy, which nothing read here measures."@en ;
    pm:caseCount 3 ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchNotCarried ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Not documented. Predates the requirement; see the note at pmc:TummoHeatExamination for why the undocumented case is treated as uncarried."@en ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), the two seances printed at pp. 141-143" ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "The Azande, by the corroborative second test their own rule prescribes; the two consultations counted here were recorded and printed at length by E. E. Evans-Pritchard, who was present at both" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "The two tests of one consultation fall alike, both fowls dying or both surviving, so that by the transmission's own rule the verdict is contradictory and void."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:BengeYieldsIdentificationOfTheWitch .

pmc:BhastrikaPresupposesLevelNeckAndTrunk a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Blasebalgatem setzt einen gerade gehaltenen Hals und Rumpf voraus"@de,
        "The bellows breath presupposes neck and trunk held level"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.60 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:note "The verse binds the lotus seat, a level neck and trunk, and a carefully closed mouth into the one instruction that opens the bellows breath. It is the only place in the breath chapter where the alignment of the spine is stated as a condition of a breath practice, and it is stated for one practice, not for the chapter. See prima-materia#29."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:HathaPranayama ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ErectTrunkInSwastikasana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:BhavaClassification a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die drei Dispositionen: paśu, vīra, divya"@de,
        "The three dispositions: paśu, vīra, divya"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Mahānirvāṇa Tantra I.50-57 (trans. A. Avalon, Tantra of the Great Liberation, London 1913)",
        "Mahānirvāṇa Tantra IV.19-20 (trans. A. Avalon, Tantra of the Great Liberation, London 1913)" ;
    skos:definition "A division of practitioners into three kinds, the bound, the heroic and the divine, which governs the rites a person is competent for. The source describes the divine as all but a god, pure of heart, alike towards opposites and unattached, and the bound as one who must gather his own offerings and who may neither look at a Śūdra nor think of a woman."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the tradition's own answer to the question why one person takes one way and another another: not by choosing, but by what he is held competent for. The text presses the point polemically — it argues that if the bound disposition cannot exist in this age, the divine cannot either, and that only the heroic rites bear visible fruit. A record that reports the two paths as a choice between camps reports the later Western reading and not this one. See prima-materia#54."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmc:BiansuiDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob irgendein Ausgang gegen die Kunst zählen könnte"@de,
        "Whether any outcome could count against the art"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Wang Chong, Lunheng, the treatise on distinguishing calamities (Bian sui), juan 24, Sibu congkan facsimile",
        "Zangshu, miscellaneous section, in the recension of Wu Cheng, one juan, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text" ;
    skos:definition "The same treatise sets out, in the chapter on distinguishing calamities, what happens to a counter-example put to a practitioner of these arts, and it does so by carrying one out three times. Suppose ten people in one household who move nothing, break no ground, change no dwelling, and choose auspicious days for every sacrifice, marriage and journey from spring to winter, offending against no prohibition: in a hundred years, can they avoid dying? The diviner, the treatise says, will then answer that a dwelling has its flourishing and its decay, and that the year-breaker and the direct-symbol were not avoided. Suppose then that they consult the practitioners at each turn, stay where the dwelling flourishes and leave where it decays, and move the whole household whenever the year-breaker falls: in a hundred years, can they avoid dying? He will then answer that moving offends against the time and that going and coming were not auspicious. Suppose they consult on that too: in a hundred years, can they avoid dying? He will then answer that the allotted span was simply at an end. The treatise concludes that on that showing life and death have their own term after all, and that no offence against year or month brought them about. It names the interest behind the regress in the same chapter: seeing a misfortune, the practitioners of the art point to the taboo and say nothing of the rest; hearing of a fortune, they keep it back and let it go unreported; they heap up misfortunes to alarm the careless and set out fortunes to hearten those who fear the times. And it puts three mass cases against the doctrine: the city of Liyang, which sank in one evening and became a lake, whose people had not all offended against the year and the month; the forty thousand of the Zhao army buried alive at Changping, who had not all left home at an unchosen hour; and the thousand prisons of the empire with ten thousand inmates, who had not all transgressed a taboo."@en ;
    skos:note "The second argument, about which cases the practitioners report, is a statement of selection in the record. It is the one argument in the treatise that a modern protocol could take over unchanged, and it is prior to any count: where only the confirmations are collected, a count of confirmations establishes nothing."@en,
        "The three mass cases are weaker than they look and the treatise does not notice why. Each says that a large group shared an outcome without sharing a cause, which refutes a doctrine claiming that the cause is necessary; the burial book does not claim that, and its annotation would be free to say that each of the drowned of Liyang had his own reason. The regress above is the stronger instrument precisely because it needs no case at all."@en,
        "This is the most valuable thing read in the treatise and the reason for opening it. It is a first-century description of an immunising regress, carried out step by step against a living practitioner, and it ends where such regresses end, at a clause that covers every remaining case. The graph holds the same machinery from the inside, at the six adversities of the burial book, whose annotation enumerates the ways in which nothing happens. Here it is described from the outside, six hundred years earlier, by someone who put it to a practitioner."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:ZangYieldsYin ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination .

pmc:BirchBarkRecord a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Birkenrindenkarte"@de,
        "The birchbark chart"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), pp. 164-165" ;
    skos:definition "The record the priests keep on birchbark, incised in fine lines, giving pictorially the ground plan of the degrees to which the owner is entitled."@en ;
    skos:note "Two things follow from this and both bear on how the rest of the file is to be read. The society keeps a written record by its own means, which is unusual among the transmissions this graph registers as lacking a corpus. And the capacities of the degrees are stated as readings of marks on such a chart, so what the report carries is an informant's exegesis of a pictograph and not a free verbal account; that is a different kind of witness, and arguably a firmer one, because the marks were there before the question was asked."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin .

pmc:BonByangChubSems a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Erheben des Gedankens zur Erleuchtung als Grundabsicht"@de,
        "Raising the Thought towards Enlightenment as the basic intent"@en ;
    dcterms:source "gZi brjid, the Way of the Shen of Prediction (phya-gshen theg-pa), on setting to work, in D. L. Snellgrove, The Nine Ways of Bon: Excerpts from gZi-brjid, London Oriental Series 18, London: Oxford University Press, 1967" ;
    skos:definition "The condition the first Way sets before any of its four parts is begun: at the start of the process of setting to work, raise your Thought towards Enlightenment and keep compassion as your basis, with your mind intent on benefiting living beings. The same requirement is repeated at the head of the fourth part for the physician, who is to raise his thought to the four immeasurable virtues before he diagnoses."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GziBrjid ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bon .

pmc:BonMaNorBa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Es gibt kein Verfehlen des Ziels und keinen Fehler in der Berechnung"@de,
        "There is no mistaking the objective and no error in calculation"@en ;
    dcterms:source "gZi brjid, the Way of the Shen of the Visual World (snang-gshen theg-pa), at the close of the four portals, in D. L. Snellgrove, The Nine Ways of Bon: Excerpts from gZi-brjid, London Oriental Series 18, London: Oxford University Press, 1967" ;
    skos:definition "The claim the second Way makes for the sequence of the Nine Ways at the close of its four portals: as one traverses upwards through the vehicles there is no falling back, there is no mistaking the objective and no error in calculation."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because it is the counter-weight to everything above it in this file and stands in the same work. The transmission that states four conditions of failure for its own procedures also states, of the order those procedures belong to, that it contains no error. The two are not in contradiction — one is about the operations, the other about the scheme — and that is the finding: what the work holds open to failure is the practice, and what it holds closed is the system."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GziBrjid ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bon .

pmc:BonPresupposesByangChubSems a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ohne den Gedanken der Erleuchtung erlangst du die höheren Wirkungen nicht"@de,
        "Without the Thought of Enlightenment you will not gain the higher effects"@en ;
    dcterms:source "gZi brjid, the Way of the Shen of Prediction (phya-gshen theg-pa), on setting to work, in D. L. Snellgrove, The Nine Ways of Bon: Excerpts from gZi-brjid, London Oriental Series 18, London: Oxford University Press, 1967" ;
    skos:definition "The consequence the work attaches to the condition, stated as a general rule over both halves of its own system: if as a general rule both in the Bon of Cause and the Bon of Effect you do not raise your Thought towards Enlightenment as your basic intent, you will not gain anywhere the higher effects of the worldly causes; so how should one obtain the highest truth? Although one is concerned here with the Bon of Cause, keep going all the time with the Thought of Enlightenment."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a concept and not as a pm:Presupposing with a stated necessity, and the reason is a limit of the source rather than of the class. The clause makes the want of the condition cost the higher effects of the operation, not the operation: the sortilege, the calculation, the rite and the diagnosis are still to be performed and are nowhere said to fail without it. A pm:Presupposing with pm:dependentStep on one of the four practices would therefore claim more than the passage says."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GziBrjid ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bon .

pmc:BonRtagsMtshan a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Diagnostiziere aus dem Aussehen alle Zeichen des Todes und Zeichen der Heilung"@de,
        "Diagnose from the appearance all signs of death and signs of cure"@en ;
    dcterms:source "gZi brjid, the Way of the Shen of Prediction (phya-gshen theg-pa), the fourth of its four parts, on diagnosis, in D. L. Snellgrove, The Nine Ways of Bon: Excerpts from gZi-brjid, London Oriental Series 18, London: Oxford University Press, 1967" ;
    skos:definition "The third of the three diagnoses of the fourth part, and the one that states both outcomes. From the connecting channels the disease is identified and from the urine what is of benefit and what of harm; from the general appearance the physician is to diagnose the signs of death and the signs of cure. The list of the four kinds of diagnosis at the head of the Way gives the same part as diagnosis of the patient's appearance to discover whether he will die or be cured."@en ;
    skos:note "A prognostic with both sides named, on a patient, with a verdict that the next weeks settle without the physician doing anything further. It is the strongest thing in the first Way and the work takes it back in the following sentence, which is entered at pmc:BonLasChad."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GziBrjid ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bon .

pmc:BonSpangBa a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Vermeide Techniken, in denen du unerfahren bist"@de,
        "Avoid techniques in which you are inexperienced"@en ;
    dcterms:source "gZi brjid, the Way of the Shen of Prediction (phya-gshen theg-pa), on setting to work, in D. L. Snellgrove, The Nine Ways of Bon: Excerpts from gZi-brjid, London Oriental Series 18, London: Oxford University Press, 1967" ;
    skos:note "A list of some twenty avoidances given to the diviner before the four parts are described, of which the operative ones are these: avoid unskilful precipitancy; avoid the self-esteem of thinking one knows; avoid the pride of thinking one is clever; avoid acts which do not fit the occasion; avoid ritual items which are unsuitable; avoid untruths of things unseen; avoid news of where you have not been; avoid techniques in which you are inexperienced; avoid unsuitable activities. Beside them stand social rules of a different kind, on how to meet an angry man, a flatterer and a liar."@en,
        "Three of the avoidances warn against the practitioner's own confidence and two against reporting what he has not seen, which makes this a self-warning of the art and not a warning about a danger in the world. It is the kind of clause SPEC §15 lists as owed, and the reason it is worth a node here rather than a line is that the transmission addresses it to the diviner before he is taught the divination."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:BonMo ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GziBrjid ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bon .

pmc:BonTsheBsluYieldsLoGsum a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der Lebensloskauf bringt den dreijährigen Aufschub des Todes"@de,
        "The life-ransom brings the three-year delay of death"@en ;
    dcterms:source "gZi brjid, the Way of the Shen of Prediction (phya-gshen theg-pa), the third of its four parts, on the rites, in D. L. Snellgrove, The Nine Ways of Bon: Excerpts from gZi-brjid, London Oriental Series 18, London: Oxford University Press, 1967" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:BonTsheBslu ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GziBrjid ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bon ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:BonTsheLoGsum .

pmc:BoranChabbannaBuddharasmi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die sechs Farbkunstgriffe, die sechs Strahlen des Buddha-Glanzes genannt"@de,
        "The six colour devices, called the six rays of the Buddha's radiance"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Yogāvacara's Manual, the exercise by devices (trans. Woodward 1916, p. 78-79)" ;
    skos:definition "In the exercise by devices the ten kasiṇas yield six colour devices, and the work names them the six rays of the Buddha's radiance: blue-green, yellow, crimson, light red or orange-madder, white, and radiant. They are practised singly and then for the six colours at one and the same time; the light device is explained by the translator as a circle of light thrown upon a wall."@en ;
    skos:note "The naming is the point. The colours a practitioner is to induce with clay frames are identified with the rays said to have come from the Buddha's body, so the standard against which he measures what he sees is a canonical description of somebody else."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:YogavacarasManual ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BoranKammatthana .

pmc:BoranDasananaVidha a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die zehn Erkenntnisformen, jede mit ihrem Gleichnis und ihrem Element"@de,
        "The ten forms of knowledge, each with its simile and its element"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Yogāvacara's Manual, the ten forms of knowledge (trans. Woodward 1916, p. 129-135)" ;
    skos:definition "The last and highest series. Knowledge of calm and insight; insight into the rise and fall of things, taught with the lute whose sound neither stays nor departs; insight into disruption, taught with rain-bubbles bursting on a tank; insight into what is to be feared, where every world and every seat of consciousness seems a source of fear as lions and tigers to a coward; insight into evil, where the aggregates appear as a forest of wild beasts, a tank of water-sprites, an armed enemy, poisoned food, a blazing house; insight of disgust, with the golden swan loath to dwell in the outcast's pit; insight arising from desire for freedom, with the fish in the net and the frog in the snake's mouth; the insight of reflection; contemplation with indifference; and contemplation of adaptation. Each is attained with a preparation spoken as impermanence, suffering, there is no soul, and each has one element appearing to the practitioner."@en ;
    skos:note "The similes are of dangers and the dangers are the content of the insight, not hazards of the practice. That distinction was checked by search across the whole work and is recorded at pmp:BoranPericulumSearch: the word danger occurs in the manual only inside these similes and once in the translator's theosophical footnote, and nowhere as a warning to the practitioner."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:YogavacarasManual ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BoranKammatthana .

pmc:BoranKasinaDosa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Flecken des Kunstgriffs erscheinen wie Rauch oder weiße Wolken"@de,
        "The blots of the device appear like smoke or white clouds"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Yogāvacara's Manual, breathing exercise for mental clarity (trans. Woodward 1916, p. 67)",
        "Yogāvacara's Manual, the preamble (trans. Woodward 1916, p. 2)" ;
    skos:definition "The first appearance in the series, and the only one the work presents as a defect. When the meditation proceeds, any blots or blemishes in the induction-artifice, such as marks of froth or scratches in the mark of upholding, will look like smoke or like white clouds in the sky. In the breathing exercise the same blemishes appear as water mixed with foam and bubbles in waves, or as finger-marks and scratches in the earth device, or as steam passing through a tube."@en ;
    skos:note "It is the nearest thing in the work to a negative sign and it is not one. The blemishes are a stage on the way, announced in advance like everything else, and the work has them fade before the clear image enters: the dim image fades as if by the passing of the blemishes. So the defect is a station and not a fault, and nothing follows from its persisting."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:YogavacarasManual ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BoranKammatthana .

pmc:BoranPancaDasaVara a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Nicht weniger als fünf und nicht mehr als zehn Mal, und die Wahrnehmung erscheint"@de,
        "Not less than five times nor more than ten, and the perception appears"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Yogāvacara's Manual, breathing exercise for mental clarity (trans. Woodward 1916, p. 70)" ;
    skos:definition "The one counted threshold in the work, stated in the breathing exercise: to him who practises concentration by in-breathing and out-breathing, doing it not less than five times nor more than ten times, the perception of the mental states appears, the adverting of the door of the mind; and he is then to observe that perception in the colour of light blue and practise it in both directions."@en ;
    skos:note "Held beside pmc:CountedThresholdNeverFallsDue in generalizations/counted-thresholds.ttl and deliberately not entered there as a witness, because it is not one. That statement says a transmission fixing such a number provides in advance for the number being reached without the effect, either by prescribing continuation past it or by assigning the shortfall to an act of the operator. This work provides neither. It is therefore a silence of the same kind that file already records of the Notory Art, and a sharper one, because the Notory Art's count has no ceiling and this one does. Recorded as a note there rather than as a case, since the statement's own falsifier requires the transmission to say that the completed count establishes something, and this one says nothing."@en,
        "It is a closed count, which is rarer than an open one and is what makes the node worth having. A threshold with only a lower bound never falls due, because the practitioner may always do it once more. This one has an upper bound: at the tenth repetition the perception has either appeared or it has not, and the work says nothing whatever about the second case."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:YogavacarasManual ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BoranKammatthana .

pmc:BoranPancapitiVanna a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die fünf Freuden, jede mit ihrem Element und ihrer Dreierfarbe"@de,
        "The five raptures, each with its element and its triad of colours"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Yogāvacara's Manual, the meditation of the fivefold zest (trans. Woodward 1916, p. 6-10)",
        "Yogāvacara's Manual, the preamble (trans. Woodward 1916, p. 2-3)" ;
    skos:definition "The catalogue the transmission is known for. Each of the five raptures is bound to an element and each has three colours, one for the ecstasy, one for the preamble and one for the access. The lesser thrill, with the element of extension: the ecstasy like the light of a firefly, self-lit by day and night; the preamble like the mild rays of the morning sun; the access like blue flowers and yellow flowers, named in Sinhala as katarolu and hirikaṇikā blossom. The momentary flash, with the element of heat: the ecstasy the colour of the morning star; the preamble golden; the access like the young sun rising in the east. The flooding rapture, with the element of cohesion: the ecstasy the brightness of the full moon with his sixteen parts complete; the preamble the colour of a lotus; the access a yellow flower, kinihiri. The transporting rapture, with the element of mobility: the ecstasy like the midday sun; the preamble light red like madder; the access the colour of the rain-cloud. The all-pervading rapture, with the element of space: the ecstasy like idda, jasmine and lotus flowers; the preamble like a sheaf of peacock's feathers; the access black as a beetle. Each triad is then withdrawn from the nose-tip, fixed in the heart and placed in the navel, and afterwards one inch above the station before."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted with the signs and carried separately from anything else, as SPEC §15 requires, and the reason is exactly the case this work makes. A sign announced in advance is reported because it was announced; where the same work also states a condition of failure, the announcement can at least be measured against it. Here there is no such condition anywhere in the work, so the catalogue is the whole of what the transmission says about how progress is known."@en,
        "The translator records that the Sinhala nīla covers blue and green without distinction and that he renders it blue-green, which is a fact about the standard and not about the vision. It is noted here and not built into a claim."@en,
        "Three things about the catalogue decide how it should be read, and all three are in the text. It is announced to the practitioner before he sits: the formula of request names the rapture he is to attain, and the rubric that follows states which element appears and in which colours. It is stated in the indicative throughout — the element appears to him, the two images appear to him — and never as something to look for. And the colours are defined by local flowers rather than by abstract colour words, so the standard is a plant somebody in Ceylon could hold up beside the vision."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:YogavacarasManual ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BoranKammatthana .

pmc:BoranParikammaAwarenessLocus a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Vorbereitung, auf dem Ort des Bewusstseins verortet"@de,
        "The preparation, situated on where the awareness is put"@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:embodiedLocus ;
    pm:situatedNode pmp:BoranParikamma ;
    pm:situationGround "Every station is placed somewhere in the body and the places are given in inches. The thought-forms are withdrawn from the nose-tip, fixed in the heart, then in the navel, and then each successive rapture one inch above the one before, up to a point an inch from the heart; the elements are located in the body and the thirty-two parts are gone through one by one. Where the extension ladder of the breathing exercise carries the sign out to Mount Meru and the Brahmā worlds, it brings it back to a single inch and places it in the nostril, so even the widest excursion ends in the body. Nothing in the work has the practitioner's awareness leave it."@en .

pmc:BoranParikammaSensoryAnchoring a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Vorbereitung, auf der Sinnesbindung verortet"@de,
        "The preparation, situated on sensory anchoring"@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:withdrawnAbsorption ;
    pm:situatedNode pmp:BoranParikamma ;
    pm:situationGround "The work has the practitioner fix the sight on the nose-tip and then grasp that he sees it with the sight-mind rather than with the eye, and everything afterwards is an appearance arising within: images, elements, colour triads, lights. The clock is audible precisely because he cannot see it, which is the plainest evidence in the work that the eye is out of use during the station. The ordinary sense channel is therefore not the locus of the operation, and the appearances are expressly not colours seen as physical eyes see colours. The value is the withdrawn one and the placement is a reading of the work, not a term the work uses."@en .

pmc:BoranPatibhaganimitta a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Nachbild: ein juwelenbesetzter Fächer, ein Kristallspiegel, tausendfach gereinigt"@de,
        "The after-image: a jewelled fan, a crystal mirror, cleansed a thousand times"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Yogāvacara's Manual, breathing exercise for mental clarity (trans. Woodward 1916, p. 67)" ;
    skos:definition "The third appearance, given in the breathing exercise under a question the work asks itself: thus closely practising the way of in-breathing and out-breathing the two images appear to him — what is the token thereof? Then the image of the mark appears as a jewelled fan, as a crystal mirror, or the moon breaking through the clouds, or a flock of cranes before the clouds, as if piercing the mark of upholding, freed from the blemishes of the earth device, and cleansed a hundred, nay, a thousand times therefrom."@en ;
    skos:note "The question the work puts — what is the token thereof — is the only place in the whole manual where it asks how something is known, and the answer is a list of four similes for an appearance. It asks for a token and gives an appearance, and it does not ask the question the other way round anywhere."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:YogavacarasManual ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BoranKammatthana .

pmc:BoranSisyaUmmattako a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Der Schüler, den ein nicht voll bewanderter Lehrer unterrichtete, wurde wahnsinnig und starb"@de,
        "The pupil taught by a guru not fully versed went mad and died"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Yogāvacara's Manual, translator's prefatory note (Woodward 1916, p. xviii-xix)" ;
    skos:definition "The one caution this transmission is reported to carry, and it is not in the manual. A Sinhala informant related that the last bhikkhu in Ceylon known to hold the paramparā yoga, Doratiyaveye Thera of the Hangurangketa Wihāra, still living in 1900, had received the practice from an aged guru after a test of obedience, did not practise it himself, and taught it to one of his pupils, who went mad and died; and the informant's judgement was that this would probably happen unless the guru himself were fully versed in the methods and able to see clairvoyantly exactly what effect each meditation was having on his pupil. The same informant held that nobody in Ceylon then either knew or practised the exercises."@en ;
    skos:note "Carried with pm:fieldAttestation and not with pm:textualAttestation because the account is not in the work; it is an oral report first written down in the volume that translates the work, which is the case SPEC §10 provides the mode for. Whether the judgement about the guru is de Silva's, Woodward's or the transmission's cannot be separated in the printed sentence, and the field record says so."@en,
        "Its position in the holding is the finding. The manual states no caution, no danger and no condition of failure — established by reading the whole of it and searched for term by term at pmp:BoranPericulumSearch. The transmission's last living memory of itself states the gravest caution a meditation corpus in this holding carries: that the practice drove a pupil mad and killed him, and that a teacher unable to watch it clairvoyantly should not give it. The written manual and the oral lineage therefore disagree about whether the practice is dangerous, and the disagreement is not between parties but between a text and the people who last held it."@en,
        "Not entered as a pm:Disputing node, and the reason is a rule and not a preference: a dispute needs two sources, and what stands against this report is the manual's silence. A silence is not a source. The pair is held instead as this note plus the search node that establishes the silence over the whole work."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:BoranParikamma ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by G. Sagaris de Silva, Sinhala pandit of Mahinda Buddhist College, Galle, to F. L. Woodward, principal of that college, who recorded it in his translator's prefatory note dated Galle, 7 August 1916, and printed it in English. De Silva is named; the guru and the pupil are not, and Doratiyaveye Thera is named as the intermediate holder. No date is given for the pupil's death and no place. The language of the telling is not stated and no Sinhala wording is carried in the volume, so what stands is Woodward's English. This is the first fixation of the account: no earlier written source for it is named, and the volume gives it as an oral communication."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WoodwardManualOfAMystic ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BoranKammatthana .

pmc:BoranUggahaNimitta a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Bild des Merkmals: ein aus der Hülle gezogener Spiegel, ein Kranichschwarm vor dunklen Wolken"@de,
        "The image of the mark: a mirror drawn from its case, a flock of cranes before dark clouds"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Yogāvacara's Manual, the preamble (trans. Woodward 1916, p. 2)" ;
    skos:definition "The second appearance. Forthwith the image of the mark arises in him like a mirror drawn from its case, like a conch-shell scoured clean, like the moon as he slips from the clouds, or like a flock of cranes before dark clouds. The work states it in the indicative, as what arises, and gives four similes without saying that any one of them decides anything."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:YogavacarasManual ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BoranKammatthana .

pmc:BothMethodsReachSiddhiForKamakhya a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Für Kāmeśvarī und Kāmākhyā erreicht jede der beiden Methoden die Kraft"@de,
        "For Kāmeśvarī and Kāmākhyā either method attains the power"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Kālikāpurāṇa, at the verses on worshipping Kāmeśvarī and Kāmākhyā by either method (139b-140a) (text, introduction and translation in English by B. N. Shastri, part III, Nag Publishers, Delhi 1991)" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that the goddesses Kāmeśvarī and Kāmākhyā may be worshipped at will following either the orthodox or the heterodox method, and that the doer attains siddhi doing it either way."@en ;
    skos:note "The passage that most directly rules out reading the pair as two camps. The choice is left to the worshipper, both methods reach the same attainment, and the text sees no need to rank them. Whatever the division does in this work, it does not sort people into parties and it does not make one method the true one."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmc:BouitiDibohaMayNotReturn a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Erst nach drei Tagen kommt er etwas zu sich, wenn er nicht überhaupt nie zurückkommt"@de,
        "Only after three days does he come a little to himself, unless he never comes back"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Le Scao, Au pays de Sette Cama: la religion des habitants. Les fétiches: le Bouiti, Messager du St Esprit, 1908-1909, the section on the initiation" ;
    skos:definition "The outcome the procedure names for itself: after three days the postulant comes a little to himself, à moins qu'il ne revienne jamais - unless he never comes back. The mediator gives a case from the year of writing, a boy of fourteen who was to be initiated at Koja, who drank the Diboha and lost consciousness, and who when they tried to bring him round was nothing but a corpse."@en ;
    skos:note "It is not a condition of failure in the sense SPEC section 15 asks for. A death ends the candidate and says nothing about whether the initiation works; what would be a condition of failure is a mark by which a living initiate could be shown not to have been received, and the criterion at pmc:BouitiMirrorReportDecidesAdmission is constructed so that no such mark exists."@en,
        "The clause and the case are two different kinds of statement and the node keeps them apart. The clause is what the transmission says of its own procedure, in the same breath as the recovery; the case is the recorder's report of a death, dated to the year and placed at a named village, and it is the only outcome in these pages that anybody outside the society could have observed. Together they are the one place where this transmission names a cost it does not place on the candidate's merit."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:BouitiDibohaInitiation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AuPaysDeSetteCamaLeBouiti ;
    pm:readVia "H. Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan (Münster i. W.: Aschendorffsche Verlagsbuchhandlung, 1912), p. 51" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bwiti .

pmc:BouitiDindimbaStopsTheLikoundou a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Dindimba-Liane wurde zu hoch für einen Menschen gebunden und hält den Likoundou auf"@de,
        "The Dindimba liana was tied too high for a man and stops the Likoundou"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Le Scao, Au pays de Sette Cama: la religion des habitants. Les fétiches: le Bouiti, Messager du St Esprit, 1908-1909, the section on the prodigies" ;
    skos:definition "The proof the transmission offers to an unbeliever. To convince you, they will take you to some Dindimba. A Dindimba is a liana joining the tops of two tall trees at a height where the branches appear incapable of bearing the weight of a man. Who but Bouiti could have tied this liana so high? And when the Likoundou wants to pass that way to go and eat somebody's soul, this liana stops it."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because of the structure of the argument and not because of the claim. It is the one place in the read passages where the transmission produces a physical object as evidence and names what the object is supposed to establish, and the two halves of it are of opposite kinds. That the liana is there and is high is checkable by anyone, and the transmission invites the check. That nobody but Bouiti could have put it there is an argument from the absence of an alternative, and that it stops the Likoundou is unobservable by construction, since what it stops is invisible."@en,
        "The inference is therefore the thing to record, and the holding has the shape elsewhere: a visible warrant is produced for an invisible effect, and the warrant is genuine. Whether a man can climb that high is a question the passage treats as settled by the look of the branches, which is the whole load-bearing step. No search was made in this run for a Gabonese report of climbing to tie such a liana; the question is named here rather than answered."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AuPaysDeSetteCamaLeBouiti ;
    pm:readVia "H. Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan (Münster i. W.: Aschendorffsche Verlagsbuchhandlung, 1912), p. 51" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bwiti .

pmc:BouitiDisclosureIsCertainDeath a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Verrieten sie etwas, ist es der sichere Tod für sie"@de,
        "If they revealed anything it is certain death for them"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Le Scao, Au pays de Sette Cama: la religion des habitants. Les fétiches: le Bouiti, Messager du St Esprit, 1908-1909, the section on the initiation" ;
    skos:definition "The sanction that holds the secret of the criterion. The initiates and even the converts are afraid to speak of what is seen in the mirror; if they revealed anything, it is certain death for them."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as its own node because of what it does rather than what it threatens. This is the clause that makes the admission criterion unauditable, and it is therefore part of the mechanism of pmc:BouitiMirrorReportDecidesAdmission and not a side remark about danger. A secret enforced by a death penalty cannot be checked by the method the rest of this holding uses, which is to ask a second witness the same question."@en,
        "Who carries out the sanction is not said, nor whether it was ever carried out. The mediator reports the fear and not a case, which is the opposite way round from the caution at pmc:BouitiDibohaMayNotReturn, where he reports a case and the transmission supplies the clause."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:BouitiMirrorReportDecidesAdmission ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AuPaysDeSetteCamaLeBouiti ;
    pm:readVia "H. Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan (Münster i. W.: Aschendorffsche Verlagsbuchhandlung, 1912), p. 51" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bwiti .

pmc:BouitiSuspensionFromTheInitiation a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Schweben wird Eingeweihten zugeschrieben, also denen, die der Spiegel aufnahm"@de,
        "The suspension is ascribed to initiates, that is to those the mirror received"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Le Scao, Au pays de Sette Cama: la religion des habitants. Les fétiches: le Bouiti, Messager du St Esprit, 1908-1909, the section on the prodigies" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that the capacity belongs to those who have been through the procedure. The passage ascribes the suspension to initiés de Bouiti and to no one else, which makes the initiation the condition of it; it does not say that the initiation is sufficient, how long after admission the capacity appears, or that every initiate has it."@en ;
    skos:note "A weak yielding claim and entered as one. What the passage supplies is an ascription to a class of persons defined by the procedure, which is the least a yielding claim can be; what it does not supply is any step between the procedure and the capacity. The holding carries stronger forms of the same shape, and this one is kept because the capacity is unusually checkable and because a reader who saw only the capacity node could not tell whether anything connected it to the initiation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:BouitiDibohaInitiation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AuPaysDeSetteCamaLeBouiti ;
    pm:readVia "H. Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan (Münster i. W.: Aschendorffsche Verlagsbuchhandlung, 1912), p. 51" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bwiti ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:BouitiSuspensionAboveTheGround .

pmc:BrahmalokeKayavasavattana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Er übt die Körpergewalt bis zur Brahmāwelt"@de,
        "He exercises mastery with the body as far as the world of Brahmā"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.119-136 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, pp. 465-469; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "He may go to the world of Brahmā with a visible body, bending the mind by means of the body so that the mind goes at the pace of the body; or with an invisible body, bending the body by means of the mind so that the body goes at the pace of the mind. In either case he descends into the perception of bliss and the perception of buoyancy, and the body becomes light as cotton wool, and he goes as an arrow goes from the bow, borne by the vehemence of the resolve."@en ;
    skos:note "The handbook draws a line here that it draws nowhere else, and it is a line about what counts as having done the thing. Seeing Brahmā with the divine eye, hearing him, knowing his mind, making the far near, going to that world visibly or invisibly — of every one of these the chapter says that he has not yet exercised mastery with the body. Only when he creates a body in Brahmā's presence is the mastery exercised. The capacity is defined by its last step and not by the journey."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:BrowContactProtocol a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der Ort der Berührung einen Unterschied macht"@de,
        "Whether the place of contact makes a difference"@en ;
    skos:definition "An examination of whether contact at the brow differs from contact elsewhere on the head in what the recipient reports."@en ;
    skos:note "Procedure without cases. The design is the same as for the palms and inherits the same controls, with one addition that matters more here: the brow is the place the recipient has most often been told about, so an effect found without blinding him to the site would be an effect of the telling."@en,
        "What a negative result would and would not settle. It would touch the draft's addition and leave pmc:Saktipata untouched: a descent of power that names no place cannot be refuted by showing that a named place does nothing. Keeping the two apart is why this node tests the posit and not the transmission."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "Not devised here. The falsifying condition follows from the claim naming a site" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "Recipients blinded to the site of contact report no difference between contact at the brow and contact at another site on the head beyond chance."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:BrowContactOpensTheBrow .

pmc:BudgeOrderingOfEthiopianAmulets a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Budges Ordnung der äthiopischen Amulette unter der Macht der Namen"@de,
        "Budge's ordering of the Ethiopian amulets under the power of names"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1900" ;
    dcterms:source "Budge (ed.), The Miracles of the Blessed Virgin Mary, and the Life of Hanna (Saint Anne), and the Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), introduction, pp. L-LIII" ;
    skos:definition "The account given in the introduction to the volume, in which the amulets are explained by a single principle: that wherever the name of a good or evil spirit, or of a man, existed, there also was the presence of that spirit or man, so that the presence of a name is equivalent to the presence of the being; and that the Ethiopians, having become Christians outwardly, retained their fear of demons and could resist one only by obtaining the protection of another of greater power."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as an ordering act and not as a claim of the transmission because none of the rolls printed in the volume says any of it. The formula about the presence of the name is the editor's, it is the sentence most often repeated about this material, and it is regularly given as an Ethiopian doctrine."@en,
        "The same introduction summarises the sixth section of the roll written for Za-Walda Hawaryat as declaring that the man who wears this amulet on his breast shall be delivered from the spear of any who would attack him. The translation printed sixty pages later gives two forms, the spoken one first, and attaches the reservation by the will of God to the worn one only. A reader of the summary gets the guarantee without the reservation and without the alternative, which is the part of the sentence a protocol turns on."@en ;
    pm:compiledBy "E. A. Wallis Budge" ;
    pm:ordersClaim pmc:EthiopianAsmat .

pmc:Camac a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Camac, der angerufene Macher"@de,
        "Camac, the maker that is invoked"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V, the invocation of the huaca; cap. VII, the pacarina of each lineage; cap. XIV, the third question of the visitor's interrogatory" ;
    skos:definition "The name under which the power is addressed at the moment of asking. When they invoke the huaca they call it Runapcamac, maker of men, and ask it for life, health and food, and they ask it nothing for any other life. Each lineage names its own pacarina, the place it says it came out of, and calls that Camac, maker; and each says it has its own maker, one a hill, one a spring."@en ;
    skos:note "The term is entered at the strength the three passages give it and no higher. All three are the same recorder, and all three show him using the word as evidence that the Indians give to a stone what is due to God alone. What can be taken from them is that camac was the word used in address and that it was understood as making rather than as owning or protecting. What cannot be taken from them is a doctrine of camac as a substance or a force, which is a reading of the Quechua of Huarochirí and stands on a document this run could not open."@en,
        "The third of the three places is a question of the interrogatory the visitor was to put in every village. That the word had to be asked for by name shows that it was the recorder's own diagnostic and not an incidental report, which strengthens the evidence that it was in use and weakens the evidence about what it meant."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmc:CandimasuriyaParamasana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Er berührt und streicht Mond und Sonne mit der Hand"@de,
        "He touches and strokes the moon and the sun with his hand"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.102-105 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, pp. 460-461; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "The moon and the sun are called mighty because they move at a height of forty-two thousand leagues and light three islands at once. He may go to them and feel them; or, seated or lying here on the earth, he may resolve that they come to the side of his hand, and they come like a palm fruit cut from the stalk; or he may make his hand grow until it reaches them."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also the only one for which the transmission records a technical question about the mechanism rather than about the procedure. A second named elder asks whether the derived matter of the hand grows and shrinks, offering as evidence that a monk who passes through a keyhole must have made it small and that one who enlarged his body must have made it large. The answer given is that the underived grows in dependence on the derived. The discussion is about how the body could do it, and nobody in it asks whether it happens."@en,
        "The handbook states no device order for this one, unlike every other operation in the chapter. It is the only capacity here effected from the absorption bases alone."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:CariyaNidanaVivada a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Ursachen eines Temperaments frühere Gewohnheit, die Elemente und die Körpersäfte sind"@de,
        "Whether the causes of a temperament are past habit, the elements and the humours"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga III.80-82, Pali PTS [102]-[103] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, pp. 119-120; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Upatissa, Vimuttimagga, chapter 6 on temperaments, Taishō 410a10-410a22, in the English of Ehara, Soma and Kheminda as revised by Nyanatusita, The Path of Freedom, Buddhist Publication Society, pp. 116-117" ;
    skos:definition "Two handbooks of the same practice give incompatible answers to the question on which the assignment of meditation subjects rests. The earlier states three causes: former habits, so that one who had abundant agreeable undertakings becomes a person of greed temperament and one reborn from an animal state a person of delusion temperament; the elements, so that the prominence of earth and water makes a person of delusion temperament, of fire and wind a person of hate temperament, and the equality of all four a person of greed temperament; and the humours, so that a predominance of phlegm makes a person of greed temperament and of wind a person of delusion temperament, with the addition that some say the reverse. The later handbook reports all three as what some say and then refutes them: not all of those who had agreeable undertakings or came from a heaven are of greed temperament, there is no such law of prominence of the elements, only two of the three roots are given in the law of humours and that determination contradicts itself in its sequence, and no cause at all is given for the temperaments of faith, intelligence and thinking; therefore, it concludes, these statements are indecisive. It then puts the commentarial doctrine of past root-cause in their place."@en ;
    skos:note "It also settles a question about counting. The two works cannot be two witnesses of anything, because the later demonstrably read the earlier, and the subcommentary names it. Whoever finds this holding agreeing with itself across the Visuddhimagga and the Vimuttimagga has found one witness and a reader of it."@en,
        "The identification of the opponent is not this file's inference. The later handbook says only some, and the subcommentary of Dhammapāla states at that very place that some is said with reference to the Elder Upatissa, for it is put in this way by him in the Vimuttimagga. Both translations read for this run carry the identification in a note, and the passage referred to was then read in the Vimuttimagga itself and is the one paraphrased above."@en,
        "Why this dispute is worth more than the doctrine at issue. It is a disagreement about the cause of a failure condition, inside the layer of failure conditions, between two works of one practice — and the later one wins by an argument and not by authority: it counts the cases the rule does not cover, points out that the rule contradicts itself, and notes that it explains only half the list. That is a transmission examining one of its own explanations, and it is the kind of passage this holding is built to find."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim "That a person's temperament, which decides which meditation subject can work for him, is caused by his habits in former existences, by a prominence among the four elements, and by a predominance among the bodily humours." ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Vimuttimagga,
        pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:CariyaVavatthanaPariyanta a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Tafel, welcher Gegenstand zu welchem Temperament passt, und die Klausel, die sie zurücknimmt"@de,
        "The table of which subject suits which temperament, and the clause that takes it back"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga III.121-122, Pali PTS [114]-[115] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, p. 134; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga III.97-102, Pali PTS [107]-[110] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, pp. 125-128; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "The handbook assigns the forty meditation subjects to six temperaments: eleven to the person of greed temperament, eight to the person of hate temperament, mindfulness of breathing to the deluded and to the thinking temperament, the first six recollections to the faithful, four to the intelligent, and the remaining devices and the four immaterial states to all; with the further rule that a limited device suits the thinking temperament and an unlimited one the deluded. It states the exclusions positively too: a limited object of the size of a tray or a pot lid does not suit the deluded, because in a cramped space the mind falls the more into confusion; and an open lodging from which parks, groves, ponds, villages and blue-shimmering hills are seen does not suit the thinking temperament, because such a lodging is the cause of the scattering of thought. And then, in the next paragraph, it withdraws the force of the whole table: all this is said by way of direct opposition and extreme suitability, for there is no meritorious culture that does not throw off lust and the rest and that is not conducive to faith."@en ;
    skos:note "The withdrawal is the finding and not the table. A transmission that assigns practices to persons has made a claim that could fail — this man was given this subject and it did not work — and the clause in the next paragraph removes the assignment from the reach of any such case, because every subject is then said to work against everything. The handbook supports the clause with a canonical passage and does not notice that it has cancelled its own criterion."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:CatequillaPrediction a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Huaca, die einen Tod voraussagte und dafür verbrannt wurde"@de,
        "The huaca that foretold a death and was burned for it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. II, on the idol Catequilla" ;
    skos:definition "A famous idol of one province was consulted through its priests by an Inca passing with an army, about whether he would return victorious from a battle or die in it; the answer was that he would die, and so it fell out. From this the huaca came to have so great a name that people came from very remote provinces to consult it and to make offerings, and it grew rich and had a sumptuous temple. His son, passing later and seeing that greatness, and learning that it was the huaca which had announced his father's death, ordered the temple and all in it to be burned; the priests stole the idol out of the fire and carried it elsewhere and built it another temple."@en ;
    skos:note "A stated hit, with its consequence stated too: the reputation of the huaca is derived from a single prediction that came true, by the recorder and presumably by the tradition he heard it from. It is the only place in this file where a claim about accuracy is made about a particular occasion rather than about a method."@en,
        "Entered as a claim standing in the work and not as a report of an event. Its chain is long — an extirpator writing in sixteen twenty-one about what another visitor was told about a consultation before the conquest — and no part of it was checked against another witness in this run."@en,
        "The second half is the more useful. The correct answer cost the huaca its temple, because the person it was correct about was the father of the man who burned it. A transmission in which an accurate oracle can be destroyed for its accuracy is not one in which reputation tracks accuracy, and the story says both things at once."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmc:CautionAgainstCarryingAnotherShamansSoul a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die letzte Überführung nicht im Zweifel übernehmen"@de,
        "Do not undertake the last transport in doubt"@en ;
    dcterms:source "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXX, sect. 141, p. 382" ;
    skos:definition "When a shaman dies among the Northern Tungus his soul must be taken to the lower world by another shaman who is stronger than the deceased. The spirits left behind often want to keep the dead man in their company, so the one who performs the operation must first fight them. If he is not certain of being able to master the spirits of the deceased, he must not undertake it."@en ;
    skos:note "A prohibition addressed to the practitioner and conditioned on his own estimate of his strength, which is unusual: most cautions in this graph are addressed to the layman or to the beginner. If no sufficiently strong shaman exists, the soul is said not to reach the world of the dead and to disturb the clansmen without rest."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:TungusShamanizing ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SiberianShamanism .

pmc:CautionAgainstOperatingAcrossTheSexes a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Frauen sind von Frauen zu magnetisieren"@de,
        "Women are to be magnetized by women"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. VI, pp. 107-108, and chap. VIII, pp. 161-164" ;
    skos:definition "Women ought to be preferred as operators upon women and, outside cases where plain good sense makes it indifferent, they alone ought to be charged with it; in chronic complaints requiring long treatment the practice between persons of different sexes is to be proscribed, the only men who may undertake the treatment of a young woman being her father or her husband. Four reasons are given: the processes wound decency; somnambulism generally imparts to the subject a lively affection for the operator which continues after the treatment ends; there are symptoms and questions about which a woman in that state will not speak to a man; and the crises are such that a man ought not to witness them nor could employ the processes best calculated to soothe them."@en ;
    skos:note "A caution of the ordinary kind, and it is entered for a reason that has nothing to do with propriety: it prescribes, for the great majority of the cases the practice treated, an operator drawn from the patient's own household and instructed by the patient's own book. The transmission thereby makes its typical operator a person with no independent means of judging the result, which bears on every case report in the literature."@en,
        "The same chapters apply the rule to the hospitals, where the source says young women and girls with nervous diseases were being chosen in preference because they were believed more susceptible and more likely to present curious phenomena. That sentence is a report about how the examinations of the day were in fact staffed, and it is the strongest thing in the file about the composition of the evidence."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:MagnetismeGrandsCourans ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:CautionAgainstOpinionAboutHiddenThings a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Warnung davor, sich über die verborgenen Dinge eine Meinung zu bilden"@de,
        "The caution against forming opinions about hidden things"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), pp. 19-20" ;
    skos:definition "The reported attitude that these are great and difficult questions best left alone, that men know so little beyond food and sleep and rest that it might easily seem presumptuous to form any opinion about hidden things, and that people who are content should not trouble themselves with thinking."@en ;
    skos:note "A tradition warning against the very enquiry the rest of it makes possible. It is entered because a record that carries only the capacities and not the cautions misreports what it holds, and because this caution is directed at the enquirer and not at the practitioner: it does not say the hidden things are unreal, it says that opinion about them is presumption."@en,
        "The passage also gives a second and worldlier ground for the same reticence, which the ethnographer reports of himself and not of the tradition: that his hosts feared their séances and their taboo observances would be laughed at. Both grounds are in the same pages, and separating them is the reader's work."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:InuitHiddenThings ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InuitAngakkuq .

pmc:CautionAgainstProlongedSomnambulisme a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Das somnambule Vermögen nach der Heilung nicht erhalten"@de,
        "Do not keep the somnambulic faculty after the cure"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. VIII, pp. 173-174" ;
    skos:definition "Persons long somnambulists retain, even after recovery, a nervous susceptibility so high that the slightest action may return them to an imperfect somnambulism, and some have been seen habitually in the magnetic state. The operator is therefore to operate no longer than the somnambulist says is necessary, never to speak to him after waking of what he said, to discharge the fluid and wake him perfectly at the end of each sitting so that no intermediate point remains, and as soon as the patient is cured to will that the faculties cease."@en ;
    skos:note "The rule closes the practice's one route to a body of repeatable observations, and the source says as much without drawing the consequence: many operators keep somnambulists after their cure hoping to serve other patients through them, and it is wrong to have confidence in such somnambulists, who are often used for experiments of curiosity. A transmission that dismisses its trained subjects on recovery cannot accumulate cases."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:MagnetismeSomnambulisme ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:CautionAgainstQuestionsOutsideHealth a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Den Somnambulen nicht über seine eigene Gesundheit hinausschicken"@de,
        "Do not send the somnambulist beyond his own health"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. VIII, pp. 174 and 185-188" ;
    skos:definition "The somnambulist is to be interdicted absolutely from all religious, metaphysical and political discussion. Where he is required to see the dead or spirits, compelled to go into distant times or places, to discover things lost, to announce the future, to name the numbers that will be fortunate in a lottery, or interrogated about political affairs, much mischief will be done him and he might even be made idiotic."@en ;
    skos:note "The named consequence is severe, and the list is worth reading as a list: every item on it is a claim that could be checked against an outside record, and the lottery number is the only one for which a plain arithmetic of success exists. The prohibition therefore falls, item by item, on exactly the class of claims that would have been decidable."@en,
        "The source's reason for the prohibition is not that the faculty fails there but that the somnambulist is harmed and led into error, and he grounds it in a disorder of proportion: one faculty is exalted more than the others, harmony no more exists, the prejudices of childhood operate afresh, and imagination can realise the phantoms it has created. That is a psychological account of false report offered by a believer, and it is the most acute thing in the chapter."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:MagnetismeLucidite ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:CautionAgainstSeekingSomnambulisme a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Den Somnambulismus niemals herbeiführen wollen"@de,
        "Never seek to produce somnambulism"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. V, pp. 70-71" ;
    skos:definition "The first advice given: never seek to produce somnambulism, but let it come naturally in order to profit by it when it takes place. Operators who charge the head in order to produce it often succeed in obtaining a forced slumber, a reflux of blood towards the brain, and partial crises which are of no utility; and the method is not without danger."@en ;
    skos:note "A self-warning of the strongest kind — the transmission's own prohibition of the effect for which it is known, placed at the head of the chapter about that effect. Beside it stands the statement that somnambulism too much prolonged would of itself become a disease."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:MagnetismeGrandsCourans ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:CautionAgainstSelfMagnetizing a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Wer den Zustand allein herbeiführen kann, soll es niemals tun"@de,
        "Those who can enter the state alone ought never to do it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. VI, pp. 112-113" ;
    skos:definition "Among persons often magnetized there are some who can of their own accord throw themselves into the magnetic state; the source holds it a faculty they ought never to employ, because by exercising it they acquire the habit of concentration, which may fatigue the nervous system and become very injurious. An operator in good health may relieve a local pain of his own by applying the processes to himself, but a person with a general disease, a fever or an organic affection cannot draw the remedy from himself, since the fluid he would use no longer has the necessary qualities."@en ;
    skos:note "Carried because it closes the last door by which a claim of this tradition could be checked cheaply and often. A subject who can enter the state alone could be examined without an operator present, which would remove the one figure whose disposition every excuse in this file appeals to; the transmission forbids it, on grounds of the subject's health."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:MagnetismeSomnambulisme ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:CautionAgainstTheWrongPresence a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die falsche Anwesenheit zerstört die Kraft der Sache"@de,
        "The wrong presence destroys the power of the thing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Frances Densmore, Teton Sioux Music (Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 61, Washington, 1918), p. 252",
        "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 143 and p. 145 and p. 92" ;
    skos:definition "Both recorders report that a ceremony or a medicine is undone by who is present. The first states that no ceremony will be efficacious if a woman is present during her menstrual flow or if the influences that surround her at that time are present, that such influences remain about a tipi she has occupied until it is taken down and set up again, and that the conductor of the Buffalo ceremony walked the lodge scanning the women to find such a one and the men to find an unworthy one, and would have ordered either to withdraw. The second states that the presence of the wrong kind of person in the lodge was believed to affect the efficacy of a medicine, and that if it were exposed to such influence for any considerable time its power would be entirely destroyed."@en ;
    skos:note "The caution is entered against the imparted potency rather than against any single rite, because that is the thing both sources say is destroyed: the first speaks of a ceremony that will not be efficacious, the second of a medicine whose power is entirely destroyed, and what the two have in common is the potency the rite is supposed to put to work."@en,
        "The measure the first source prescribes is worth recording exactly, because it is physical and datable: the tipi in which the young woman had lived alone was taken down by her mother and immediately set up again on the same spot, and that act is what made it fit for the ceremony."@en,
        "This is the failure condition that does the most work in the file, because it is prior to every other: it can be invoked after the fact against any rite that did not take, and it needs no evidence beyond the assertion that somebody was there. Entered as a caution and not as a mere doctrine because both sources give it in the imperative, with an action to be taken."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:Sicun ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by named Oglala shamans, chiefly Sword, Tyon, Ringing-shield, Bad-wound and No-flesh, and by Finger on 25 March 1914; recorded by the agency physician J. R. Walker at Pine Ridge over his years of service there, closed December 1916; given in Lakota, part of it written out in Lakota by Sword and Tyon and translated by Burt Means, Thomas Mills, Bruce Means and Antoin Herman; the paper prints the translations only, so the Lakota wording is not carried." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LakotaRitual .

pmc:CautionAgainstTouchingTheBurialGear a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Was am Grab bleibt, nicht anrühren"@de,
        "Do not touch what is left at the grave"@en ;
    dcterms:source "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXX, sect. 141, p. 382" ;
    skos:definition "Among the Tungus of Transbaikalia the shaman's paraphernalia are laid with the body or hung on a tree standing nearby. It is not recommended to touch them: those who do may get mental diseases, they may lose their minds."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:TungusParaphernalia ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SiberianShamanism .

pmc:CautionAgainstTrustingThePrescription a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Der Verordnung des Somnambulen nicht ungeprüft folgen"@de,
        "Do not follow the somnambulist's prescription without examining it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. V, pp. 83-86, and chap. VIII, pp. 175-177 and 183-184" ;
    skos:definition "Against the blind confidence of enthusiastic operators, who believe their somnambulists infallible, suppose that remedies which did not succeed were not exactly followed, and regard remedies that did mischief as a necessary crisis. Where the prescription appears unsuitable the operator is to object, have the organs examined in succession, ask the reasons for the choice, present the medicine to be touched and tasted, and have the dose shown as a quantity and not only named; if after all that the somnambulist persists, he may be depended upon. Where the prescription would put life in imminent danger the operator is evidently not to conform to it."@en ;
    skos:note "A caution the transmission addresses to its own adherents, naming the two devices by which they absorb failure — non-compliance and the necessary crisis — and rejecting both. That is a transmission criticising the unfalsifiability of its own practitioners, and it is the reason this file does not read as apologetics."@en,
        "It stops short of the step that would matter. The prescriptions are to be submitted to a candid physician before being taken, and the physician is to preserve his supremacy — but he is not to be put in consultation with the somnambulist, and his judgement is therefore never recorded against the somnambulist's in a form anybody could count."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:MagnetismeAutoDiagnostic ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:CautionAgainstWakingSuddenly a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Den Kranken niemals plötzlich erwecken"@de,
        "Never wake the patient suddenly"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. III, pp. 48-49" ;
    skos:definition "The patient is never to be woken suddenly, nor anyone permitted to trouble him, nor is he to be left while the induced state lasts. Where a person in profound sleep is roused roughly the source has seen it produce convulsions or violent pains, render the return of somnambulism impossible, and so change the patient's condition that he could afterwards not endure the action at all, his restoration being left to time and regimen; persons put into somnambulism for the first time have been known to lose the faculty of entering it again by being rudely roused."@en ;
    skos:note "Named consequences of a rough waking, and among them one a bystander could establish: that the state cannot afterwards be induced in that person again. That is a prediction about a particular individual on a particular date and it is the kind of thing a register of cases would settle. None is kept."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:MagnetismeSomnambulisme ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:CautionAtrarpePatraic a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Gegen die beiden Verfahren, wegen des Opfers"@de,
        "Against the two procedures, on the ground of the offering"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Sanas Cormaic s.v. Imbas forosnai, in Stokes, Three Irish Glossaries (London 1862), p. 25, and in Cormac's Glossary, trans. O'Donovan (Calcutta 1868), p. 95" ;
    skos:note "A warning transmitted in the same entry as the prescription it forbids, by the hand that copied the prescription. The glossary sets out the procedure in full and in the present tense and then abolishes it, and neither the setting out nor the abolition is abridged for the other's sake."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ImbasForosnai ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InsularCeltic .

pmc:CautionOnTheCandidatesConduct a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die acht Regeln, die einen Kandidaten binden"@de,
        "The eight rules that govern a candidate"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 71 and p. 93" ;
    skos:definition "The invariable rules taught to a candidate for the second, third or fourth form: he must subordinate himself to his mentor, meditate continually on his undertaking, speak little with others than his mentor, and use only his consecrated implements and utensils; he must not become angry, must not hear ribald speech, must not go into the water, and must not have sexual intercourse. A candidate who disregards any of them must do such penance as his mentor prescribes before he can proceed."@en ;
    skos:note "A warning with a remedy rather than a forfeit: the breach does not void the undertaking, it interrupts it until a penance set by another man has been done. That is a different shape from the taboos which are said to destroy an instrument outright, and the two stand in the same corpus."@en,
        "Two further restrictions belong to the same regime and are entered here rather than as separate nodes: the consecrated clothing, implements and utensils may be used by no one else until the dance is danced, and from the beginning of his last day in the sacred lodge the candidate must fast and take no drink until he has danced. Only a shaman can release him from any requirement of his conduct in the sacred lodge."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:WiwanyangWacipi ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by named Oglala shamans, chiefly Sword, Tyon, Ringing-shield, Bad-wound and No-flesh, and by Finger on 25 March 1914; recorded by the agency physician J. R. Walker at Pine Ridge over his years of service there, closed December 1916; given in Lakota, part of it written out in Lakota by Sword and Tyon and translated by Burt Means, Thomas Mills, Bruce Means and Antoin Herman; the paper prints the translations only, so the Lakota wording is not carried." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LakotaRitual .

pmc:CentumAutumni a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die hundert Herbste und das Maß, das vor ihnen nicht wieder genommen werden darf"@de,
        "The hundred autumns, and the measure that may not be taken again before them"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Atharvaveda Saṃhitā 19.67 and 18.2 verse 38, in the translation of W. D. Whitney, revised by C. R. Lanman, Harvard Oriental Series 8, Cambridge, Mass. 1905" ;
    skos:definition "The term this corpus sets on a human life, stated twice in the hymns opened and in two different postures. As a petition, in eight one-line verses: may we see a hundred autumns, may we live, may we wake, may we ascend, may we prosper, may we be, may we adorn a hundred autumns, more autumns than a hundred. And as a stipulation at a funeral, where a measure is drawn on the ground: this measure do we measure, so that one may not measure further; in a hundred autumns, not before."@en ;
    skos:note "The second passage is a term with a lower bound and is the more interesting of the two. It does not ask for a hundred autumns; it fixes a hundred autumns as the interval within which the act may not be repeated, which makes the number a rule about the living rather than a wish for them."@en,
        "Whitney records that the hymn of the hundred autumns is not found in the Paippalāda at all, and that its first two lines are the last two padas of a Rigvedic verse which several later manuals extend differently. The formula is old and the hymn as a hymn is one recension's."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Atharvavedic .

pmc:Chilan a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Chilan"@de,
        "Chilan"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Diego de Landa, Yucatan Before and After the Conquest, translated by William Gates (Baltimore: The Maya Society, publication 20, 1937), section XXVII, pp. 46-47" ;
    skos:altLabel "chilam"@en,
        "chilanes"@es ;
    skos:definition "The office of the speaker who gave out the oracles to those of the locality. The witness reports that the respect shown them was so great that they did not ordinarily leave their houses except carried on litters."@en ;
    skos:note "The same section gives the office of the priest beside it: to teach the sciences, to indicate calamities and the means of remedying them, to preach at the festivals and to celebrate the sacrifices. Indicating a calamity and naming its remedy are one office in this transmission, which is why the remedy for a foretold year below is not a separate institution."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MayaRitual .

pmc:ChimaiGuanTongzi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Rote Gefäße über der Pupille, nach Jahren gezählt"@de,
        "Red vessels crossing the pupil, counted for years"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 70 (Han re), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 10",
        "Huangdi neijing lingshu 74 (Lun ji zhen chi), in Lingshu jing, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text, juan 11" ;
    skos:definition "Asked how life and death are decided in the scrofulous cold-and-heat swellings, the chapter says to turn the eye back and look: where there are red vessels within it running from above down across the pupil, one vessel means death in a year, one vessel and a half a year and a half, two vessels two years, two and a half two and a half, three vessels three years. And where red vessels are seen that do not run down across the pupil, the case can be treated."@en ;
    skos:note "The last clause is the reason this is the most examinable claim read in this corpus. The prognosis names the finding under which it predicts nothing, which is what a claim must do to be able to fail; and the finding itself is visible on a living eye, countable, and prior to the outcome by a year or more. Almost nothing else here has all three."@en,
        "The passage stands twice in the Lingshu, and the doublet is not verbatim. In the chapter on cold and heat the whole rule is given, including the clause about the vessels that do not cross; in the chapter on examining the forearm the same count is given as one item in a long list of skin and pulse signs, with the count intact and without the treatable case. What repeats is the number and what does not repeat is the exception, which is the half that carries the examinability."@en,
        "The two places were read in two different editions, and the reason is a defect of the digitisation and not of the work. In the Sibu congkan text used here, the eleventh juan breaks off inside the chapter on examining the forearm and a block of commentary belonging to a Suwen chapter stands in its place; the passage is absent there. It was read instead in the Wenyuange Siku quanshu text of the Lingshu, and both citations say which."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmc:ChinToChestInTheChiefSeats a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das an die Brust gedrückte Kinn in den beiden bevorzugten Sitzhaltungen"@de,
        "The chin pressed to the chest in the two seats the manual prefers"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I.37 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I.46 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I.48 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "In the two seats the manual singles out as chief, the chin is set firmly against the chest and the gaze is fixed between the brows or at the tip of the nose."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the counterweight to any claim that the manual requires an erect spine throughout. The seats it recommends most warmly close the neck rather than extend it, and the same posture chapter has the tongue set against the roots of the upper teeth while the wind is drawn upward. Uprightness of the trunk is attested; uprightness carried through the neck and head is contradicted in the same work. See prima-materia#29."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:ChuiqiJinshe a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Qi gegen Schlangen blasen, nah und aus der Ferne"@de,
        "Blowing the qi at snakes, near and at a distance"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 17 (Dengshe), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 17" ;
    skos:definition "Before entering, the practitioner learns the interdiction at home and holds in mind the sun and moon and the four directional animals to guard his body. In the undergrowth he takes three breaths on the left, holds them, and blows them over the vegetation, intending the qi to be red and to fill several tens of li like cloud and mist; those with him, however many, are drawn up in a line and blown over likewise, and thereafter he treads on a snake and it does not stir, and mostly meets none. Meeting one, he takes three breaths on the left facing the sun, holds them, props the tongue against the palate, pinches the gate with the hand, shuts the gate of heaven and stops the door of earth, presses down the snake's head with something and winds it about with the hand, drawing a prison on the ground to hold it; and it will not bite, and until he releases the interdiction by blowing out the qi it cannot leave the prison. Where another has been bitten, he takes three breaths on the left and blows, and the man is healed and the pain stops; and where the man is ten and more li away he may make the qi from afar, calling the man's surname and name, invoking over his own left hand for a man and his own right for a woman, and that man too is healed."@en ;
    skos:note "The last clause repeats the rule of the eighth chapter word for word in its operative part: the practitioner blows and incants over his own hand, left for a male patient and right for a female, at a distance, on someone he does not see. It is therefore not a single formula of one chapter but a rule the work states twice, in two different operations, and it is what a search for the palms as an organ of transmission finds instead."@en,
        "The sending organ in both places is the breath, and the hand is where the breath is directed. The chapter has the qi do the work throughout — over the grass, over the snake, over the wound, over the distance — and gives the hand no office except in the distant case, where it is the thing blown upon."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BaopuziNeipian ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:ChukcheeColdWaterEndsInspiration a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kaltes Wasser auf den Kopf beendet die Eingebung"@de,
        "Cold water on the head ends the inspiration"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Waldemar Bogoras, The Chukchee, Memoir of the American Museum of Natural History vol. VII (Leiden: E. J. Brill, 1904-1909), p. 433" ;
    skos:definition "The stated means of breaking off the state: the application of cold water to the head of a practitioner in the very heat of his performance would suddenly bring his inspiration to an end, and at ceremonials his neighbours may sprinkle him for that purpose when he practises too long or seems excited to a dangerous pitch."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because it is a failing condition of the rarest kind: one that a bystander can bring about deliberately, in the middle of the act, with a stated and immediate effect. Most conditions in this holding are set before the act and can only be observed afterwards."@en,
        "The recorder marks his own distance at this one place: he was told this, where the surrounding paragraphs report what he saw. The node keeps the difference rather than levelling it into the rest of the account."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChukcheeShamanism .

pmc:CiceroNullaTabulaObservationum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Eine Behauptung von vierhundertsiebzigtausend Jahren Aufzeichnung, und keine Aufzeichnung"@de,
        "A claim of four hundred and seventy thousand years of records, and no record"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Cicero, De divinatione II.46-47, in the English of C. D. Yonge, The Treatises of M. T. Cicero, London: Bohn 1878, pp. 240-241" ;
    skos:definition "Some pretend, the passage says, that the Chaldean astrologers have verified the nativities of children by calculations and experiments in the cases of all the children born for four hundred and seventy thousand years; and this is a mistake, for had they been in the habit of doing so they would never have given up the practice, and as it is no author remains who knows of such a thing being done now or ever having been done. The chapter closes with the counterpart from the other side: how many Chaldean prophecies were repeated to Pompey, Crassus and Caesar, according to which not one of them was to die except in old age, in domestic felicity and perfect renown."@en ;
    skos:note "It has a counterpart in a corpus that could not have known it, and the pairing is the reason this node is separate. The rasāyana lesson of the Caraka states five times that its combinations are well tested by experience and reports no case; here a critic names the same gap in somebody else's practice and draws the consequence the Indian handbook does not draw about its own. Two corpora, one shape, and only one of them is looking at itself. See pmp:AyurvedaRasayanaPariksaSearch."@en,
        "The argument is not that the record is bad but that it does not exist, and the ground given is behavioural: a practice that had such a record would still be keeping it. That is a test of a claim of verification by the presence of its own apparatus, and it is worth having as a form."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeDivinatione ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GraecoRoman .

pmc:CinwadPuhl a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Činwad-Brücke"@de,
        "The Chinvat bridge"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The Book of Arda Viraf, ed. and trans. Martin Haug, E. W. West and Hoshang Jamaspji Asa (Bombay and London: Trübner, 1872), 4.7 and 5.1" ;
    skos:altLabel "Chinvat"@en,
        "Cinvat"@en,
        "Cinwad puhl"@en ;
    skos:definition "The bridge at which the accounting falls due. In the account of the journey under mang it is called very wide and strong, and its width for the traveller who has passed becomes nine javelin-lengths."@en ;
    skos:note "The measure is stated in a unit and is therefore comparable, which most of what the transmissions in this graph say about such passages is not. What the chapters read do not give is the counter-measure, the width for the one who fails; that stands in another book and was not opened here."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Zoroastrian .

pmc:CizhenPresupposesJingshen a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Nadelung setzt voraus, dass der Geist da ist"@de,
        "Needling presupposes that the spirit is present"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 8 (Ben shen), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 2",
        "Huangdi neijing suwen 14 (Tang ye lao li lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 4" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Two passages state it and both state a consequence rather than a degree. Where the essence-spirit does not go forward and the intent is not ordered, the illness cannot be cured, and the body worn out and the blood exhausted the treatment establishes nothing. Where the five, essence, spirit, hun, po and the intent, are damaged, the needle cannot treat him at all."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:Cizhen ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Jingshen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmc:CizhongIntervalDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Wie lange nach dem Treffer ins Depot der Tod eintreten soll"@de,
        "How long after a depot is struck death is said to follow"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 16 (Zhen yao jing zhong lun), with the Xin jiao zheng collation notes, in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 4",
        "Huangdi neijing suwen 52 (Ci jin lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 14",
        "Huangdi neijing suwen 64 (Si shi ci ni cong lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 18",
        "Huangfu Mi, Zhenjiu jiayi jing, juan 5 (Zhen jiu jin ji), in the Wenyuange Siku quanshu text" ;
    skos:definition "Four places in the same canon give differing intervals for the same strike, and a further witness gives another set. The needling-prohibition chapter has the kidney at six days, the lung at three and the spleen at ten. The chapter on the essentials of examination has the heart dying within one circuit rather than in one day, the spleen at five days, the kidney at seven, the lung at five, and adds a strike through the diaphragm after which the patient dies within the year however well he seems to recover. The chapter on needling with and against the four seasons repeats the whole list a third time, agreeing with the needling-prohibition chapter at heart, liver, lung and spleen, giving the kidney's movement sign as sneezing and yawning where the other has sneezing alone, omitting the gall bladder, and adding a summary clause the others do not have: that whoever in needling injures a man's five depots kills him for certain, and that his death is to be known from the movement sign of the depot concerned. The Zhenjiu jiayi jing has the kidney at three days, the spleen at fifteen, and gives the liver's sign as a different character from the one the received Suwen prints. The Song editors record in their collation notes that the older recension of Quan Yuanqi agreed with the Jiayi jing on both numbers, and that Wang Bing changed the liver's sign."@en ;
    skos:note "The commentators do not treat the numbers as observations. The note beside the spleen argues from ten being the completion number of earth, and other commentators have wanted to correct liver and lung to the generation numbers of wood and metal. A number defended from a numerology is not thereby false, but it is not evidence of a counted interval either, and a protocol on this claim would have to begin there."@en,
        "The consequence for this graph is narrower than the dispute. Nothing here says that the intervals are wrong; what it says is that a citation naming one chapter conceals that the same canon carries three answers, and that the earliest reachable witness disagrees with the received text at two of the six places."@en,
        "The dispute is entered because the transmitted text states it about itself and states a verdict on it. The collation note printed with the chapter on the essentials of examination says that these three discourses are all the words of Qi Bo and that their differing is an error of transmission. That is one party's account of the divergence and not a neutral finding, and the graph keeps it as such."@en,
        "The third Suwen chapter was added on 2026-09-10 after being read at the wording, and it changes the balance rather than adding a fourth set of numbers. Two of the three Suwen chapters now agree on five of the six intervals, so the divergence inside the received text is narrower than the earlier state of this node suggested; what remains is the chapter on the essentials of examination against the other two, and the Jiayi jing against all three. The clause that whoever injures the five depots kills for certain stands only in the added chapter, and it is the sentence that turns a list of intervals into a claim that admits no surviving case."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:CizhongWuzang ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmc:ClaviculaNovemDies a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Neun Tage Enthaltung vor dem Kreis, davon die letzten drei ein Fasten"@de,
        "Nine days of continence before the circle, of which the last three are a fast"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Clavicula Salomonis, Book I chapter III, with Book II chapters IV and XIII, in the English of S. L. MacGregor Mathers, The Key of Solomon the King, London: George Redway, 1889" ;
    skos:definition "Before commencing, master and disciples must abstain with great and thorough continence for nine days from sensual pleasures and from vain and foolish conversation; the second book repeats the term and adds that during the last three days one eats fasting diet once a day, better bread and water only, and on the last day nothing at all. Within the same nine days every instrument must be made, consecrated and exorcised, so that nothing remains to be prepared when the day and hour of the work arrive."@en ;
    skos:note "The dependent step and the prior step are the same practice because the book makes the preparation part of the operation rather than a separate act with its own name. What the node holds is the term: nine days, with three inside them, fixed in advance and countable by anyone present. It is entered at stated necessity and not at prescribed order because the consequence is quoted and not inferred — but the consequence is the general clause, not a penalty peculiar to this step, and that is the whole difficulty this file is about."@en,
        "The term is not the same length in every witness of this corpus. Mathers notes that the paragraph carrying the nine days is omitted in Lansdowne 1202. The Heptameron printed in the Turner collection of 1655 sets nine days and three within them in the same shape, which was read at the wording in that book; the routes between these texts are documented, so the agreement is reception and not a second witness."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "If thou doest ill in one single point, these experiments or these Arts will not be verified (Book II chapter I); and, of the order of hours and days and of the position of the Moon, without the operation of which thou canst effect nothing (Book I chapter I)."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ClaviculaOperatioCirculi ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ClaviculaSalomonis ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ClaviculaOperatioCirculi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SolomonicGrimoire .

pmc:ClaviculaScotDisputatio a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die salomonischen Beschwörungen überhaupt etwas bewirken"@de,
        "Whether the Solomonic conjurations do anything at all"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Clavicula Salomonis, Book I chapter I, in the English of S. L. MacGregor Mathers, The Key of Solomon the King, London: George Redway, 1889",
        "Reginald Scot, The Discoverie of Witchcraft, Book XV chapter 5, in the reprint of the edition of 1584 edited by Brinsley Nicholson, London: Elliot Stock, 1886, pages 329-330" ;
    skos:definition "The book holds that whoever fears God and prepares the order of hours and days and the position of the moon may easily and thoroughly arrive at the effect and end he desires, and that the spirits, being conjured, come and give what is asked. A work printed in London in 1584 denies it and gives a ground that is neither scriptural nor demonological but a count of the practitioners: he that can be persuaded that these things are true may soon be brought to believe that the moon is made of green cheese; of whom yet was never seen any rich man, or at least that gained any thing that way, or any unlearned man that became learned by that means, or any happy man that could with the help of this art either deliver himself or his friends from adversity, or add unto his estate any point of felicity — yet these men, in all worldly happiness, must needs exceed all others, if such things could be accomplished as it is presupposed."@en ;
    skos:note "The same chapter of 1584 elsewhere reproduces a necromancy of the Solomonic shape — first fast and pray three days and abstain from all filthiness — in order to expose it, and glosses the fasting term of another handbook with the remark that so the cozenage may best be handled. The reading of the term as stagecraft is that author's and is not entered as a node."@en,
        "The second side is worth having because of the form of its argument and not its verdict. It does not dispute that the spirits exist or that the ceremonies are impious; it takes the capacities the books promise — wealth, learning, deliverance — and observes that the people who work the art do not have them. That is an outcome the transmission's own claims predict and it is checkable from outside the session, which is more than the transmission offers anywhere except at the mark on the thief's face."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:ClaviculaOperatioYieldsImperium ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ClaviculaSalomonis,
        pmw:ScotDiscoverieOfWitchcraft .

pmc:ClaviculaSociorumPericulum a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Der Schüler, der nicht gehorcht, leidet und stirbt zuweilen"@de,
        "The disciple who disobeys suffers, and sometimes dies"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Clavicula Salomonis, Book II chapters III and XIII, in the English of S. L. MacGregor Mathers, The Key of Solomon the King, London: George Redway, 1889" ;
    skos:definition "The companions are to be bound by oath to do all that the master orders. Those who shall act otherwise shall suffer and endure many pains and labours, and run into many dangers, which the spirits will cause and procure for them, and for this cause sometimes they shall even die. Conversely, wearing the consecrated pentacles at the breast, they shall be exempt and free from all perils and dangers, provided that they obey the commands of the master and do all that he ordains them. Within the circle they are on no cause whatsoever to move from their assigned places."@en ;
    skos:note "The protection and the penalty share one condition and it is obedience to a person present. That makes the master, not the procedure, the party whose account decides afterwards whether a harm that occurred was a breach or a visitation — and a harm is the only outcome of the two that leaves evidence."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ClaviculaOperatioCirculi ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ClaviculaSalomonis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SolomonicGrimoire .

pmc:ClaviculaThesauriPericulum a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Erdgeister warnen den Schatzgräber und töten ihn, wenn er weitermacht"@de,
        "The earth-spirits warn the treasure-digger, and kill him if he persists"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Clavicula Salomonis, Book I chapter XIV, in the English of S. L. MacGregor Mathers, The Key of Solomon the King, London: George Redway, 1889" ;
    skos:definition "Men who undertake a search for hidden treasures are molested and sometimes put to death by the spirits called gnomes. This is not done through avarice, a spirit being incapable of possessing anything; the spirits are enemies of the passions and foresee the evil ends for which the treasure will be used. When they slightly disturb the workers it is a warning to cease from the work, and if the greedy importunity of the workers obliges them to continue notwithstanding the warnings, the spirits, irritated by their despising the same, frequently put the workmen to death."@en ;
    skos:note "A caution that supplies its own reading of the evidence in both directions. A disturbance during the digging is a warning; a death is the penalty for having ignored one. Nothing that can happen at the site is left over to count against the account, and the men who would have to report the warnings are the men the workers are told not to be disturbed by."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ClaviculaCeteraeVirtutes ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ClaviculaSalomonis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SolomonicGrimoire .

pmc:ClearLightIsAtTheSleepThreshold a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Das klare Licht liegt an der Schlafschwelle"@de,
        "The clear light is placed at the sleep threshold"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005), the yoga of the clear light of sleep" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:sleepThreshold ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:ClearLightOfSleep ;
    pm:situationGround "The treatise places the luminosity at the moment of falling asleep and trains the practitioner to hold it rather than pass through it unconscious. The file's own note already separates this from a body whose motor system has shut down while the mind stays awake; both fall on this coordinate, and the difference between them belongs in the nodes, not in the axis."@en .

pmc:CmBaibashijie a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Ohne die hundertachtzig Gebote kann der Vollzieher keine Geister bändigen"@de,
        "Without the hundred and eighty precepts the officiant cannot control spirits"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Lu Xiujing, Lu xiansheng daomen kelüe 007b (DZ 1127), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "The code cites a scripture to the effect that a Daoist who has not received the hundred and eighty precepts of the Lord Lao has no virtue in his person, is therefore not a Daoist, may not accept the obeisance of the people, and cannot take in hand the control of ghosts and spirits."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "He is not a Daoist, may not accept the obeisance of the people, and cannot take in hand the control of ghosts and spirits." ;
    pm:dependentStep "That an officiant can take in hand the control of ghosts and spirits" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LuxianshengDaomenKelue ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep "That he has received the hundred and eighty precepts of the Lord Lao" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CelestialMasters .

pmc:CmBukaoRiwen a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Nicht den Tag erwählen und nicht nach der Stunde fragen"@de,
        "Do not divine the day or ask after the hour"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Lu Xiujing, Lu xiansheng daomen kelüe 008a-008b (DZ 1127), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "The code states that under the pure covenant one does not divine the day or enquire after the hour for dwelling, for settling a tomb, for moving or for any undertaking, but acts as the heart directs, with nothing avoided and nothing sought; and that the books of siting and the geomancy of tombs, dwellings and foundations are things to be driven out by petition. It names among the present abuses the further keeping of a calendar and the picking of days and hours, calls it a folly grown worse, and says the correct code has made the matter plain and men will not follow it."@en ;
    skos:note "A transmission refusing the condition that most of this holding's calendar-bound transmissions impose, and refusing it as a mark of identity rather than by silence. The refusal is placed in a list whose other members are sacrificing to ghosts, telling fortunes and observing taboos, so the calendar is classed with the practices the transmission defines itself against."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout "Choosing days and hours for undertakings, and consulting the books of siting" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LuxianshengDaomenKelue ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CelestialMasters .

pmc:CmBuxindaoDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der ungeheilte Fall den fehlenden Glauben des Kranken zeigt oder die fehlende Wirkung des Verfahrens"@de,
        "Whether the unhealed case shows the patient's want of faith or the method's want of effect"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Sanguozhi 8.28a-28b, Dianlüe quoted in Pei Songzhi's commentary, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text (Zhonghua shuju 8.264)",
        "Zhengyi fawen tianshi jiaojie kejing 018b (DZ 789), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "The Dianlüe reports the rule and rejects it in the same paragraph: the master had the sick person knock his head and reflect on his faults and then gave him talisman water to drink, and of those whose illness was of few days' standing and who recovered it was said that this man trusted the Way, while of those who did not recover it was said that they did not trust the Way; and the household of the sick person put out five pecks of rice as the standing charge. The chronicler's judgement follows directly: in truth it was of no benefit in the treatment of illness and was merely licentious delusion, yet the small people were benighted and foolish and vied with one another in serving it. The admonitions state the transmission's own version of the same rule, assigning the relapsing patient to badness of character and withdrawing treatment from him."@en ;
    skos:note "One distinction the passage forces and the literature often blurs. The Dianlüe states the faith rule of Zhang Jue's way of great peace, and says of Zhang Xiu's way of five pecks of rice that its method was roughly the same with the quiet chamber added. The rule is therefore reported of this transmission by way of an equation the chronicler makes, and the admonitions supply the transmission's own parallel rule directly. Both are cited here and the difference is stated rather than smoothed."@en,
        "The objection that this holding would raise against such a rule is here raised inside the third century, by a witness contemporary with the movement, and in the same words the holding would use: an outcome and its opposite are both accounted for by the disposition of the patient, and the chronicler names that as the reason the thing is of no benefit. The dispute is entered rather than the judgement, because the Dianlüe is a hostile source and its verdict is a verdict; what is not in question is that the rule was in use and that a contemporary saw what it did."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim "Whether a patient's failure to recover after confession and talisman water shows that he did not trust the Way" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Sanguozhi,
        pmw:ZhengyiFawenTianshiJiaojieKejing ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CelestialMasters .

pmc:CmDaobujiu a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Wiewohl er auf den Schutz des Dao hofft, rettet das Dao ihn nicht"@de,
        "Though he looks to the Dao for protection, the Dao does not save him"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhengyi fawen tianshi jiaojie kejing 006b-007a (DZ 789), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "The admonitions ask why a household that serves the Dao nevertheless meets with calamity, illness, bereavement, summonses and quarrels, and answer that it is because the people are foolish and not diligent in the observance, knowing the Dao to be honourable and wishing to serve it while their disposition and conduct are no different from the common sort. Such a one, the text continues, is enrolled in name as a servant of the Dao and hopes that the Dao will protect him, and the Dao does not receive him; when calamity and sickness fall on his body, though he looks to the Dao for protection the Dao does not save him; and when evil spirits assail him the Dao does not drive them off."@en ;
    skos:note "The passage is the transmission's own answer to the objection that its adherents suffer like everyone else, and it is given before the objection is raised against it. The condition it names — diligence, and a disposition different from the common sort — is not one that can be established independently of the outcome it explains."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout "Serving the Dao in name while the disposition and conduct remain those of the common sort" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ZhengyiFawenTianshiJiaojieKejing ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CelestialMasters .

pmc:CmJiatuoGuishen a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Nicht die Autorität der Geister borgen, um Bedrängte zu schrecken"@de,
        "Do not borrow the authority of ghosts and spirits to frighten people in difficulty"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhengyi fawen tianshi jiaojie kejing 008b (DZ 789), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "The admonitions forbid using one's wits to cheat the ignorant, trading on one's standing to make false claims, borrowing the authority of ghosts and spirits, and frightening people who are in straits; and forbid, in the same list, having a little cleverness and making a great name of it, or slighting the ignorant."@en ;
    skos:note "A self-warning of the kind SPEC 15 singles out: the transmission prohibits, as an abuse, the precise use to which its own apparatus lends itself, and it does so inside the document that establishes that apparatus."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout "Invoking ghosts and spirits in order to frighten people who are in difficulty" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ZhengyiFawenTianshiJiaojieKejing ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CelestialMasters .

pmc:CmJingshi a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die stille Kammer, vom Haus getrennt und mit vier Dingen darin"@de,
        "The quiet chamber, separate from the house and holding four things"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Lu Xiujing, Lu xiansheng daomen kelüe 004b-005a (DZ 1127), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile",
        "Sanguozhi 8.28a, Dianlüe quoted in Pei Songzhi's commentary, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text (Zhonghua shuju 8.264)" ;
    skos:definition "The code states that the quiet chamber is the place where sincerity is brought, that its outside stands apart and does not adjoin any other building, that its inside is empty and holds nothing extraneous, that its door is not opened and shut carelessly, that it is swept and kept as though a god lived there, and that it holds four things and no more: an incense burner, an incense lamp, a petition table and a writing knife. It adds that its plain bareness can be had for a hundred-odd cash."@en ;
    skos:note "The chamber is also the oldest datable element of the procedure. The Dianlüe of the third century gives it as Zhang Xiu's addition to a method otherwise shared with Zhang Jue: he added the setting up of a quiet chamber and had the sick person sit in it and reflect on his faults. Where this code states the rule, the chronicle reports the innovation, and the two are two centuries apart."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep "That the petition is sent up from a place fit to receive sincerity" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LuxianshengDaomenKelue,
        pmw:Sanguozhi ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep "That a chamber is kept separate, empty and holding only the four objects" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CelestialMasters .

pmc:CmMingjiBenshi a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Eingabe erreicht nichts, wenn sie nicht über den eigenen Meister geht"@de,
        "The petition goes nowhere unless it is sent through the petitioner's own master"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Lu Xiujing, Lu xiansheng daomen kelüe 003b-004a (DZ 1127), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "The code states that on the day of illness people go not to their own master but petition another officer; that the other officer does not trace where they belong and simply makes a petition for them; and that since the sick body was not in the register before and now suddenly has a petition, it does not belong to the household-guarding officer's charge, the three masters do not hold it, the register of the three heavens is empty of it and the Director of Destinies has no such name — so that the petitioner smashes his head on the ground to no purpose, the files pile up, and because the thing was not done according to the rule the Dao does not deliver."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "He smashes his head on the ground to no purpose; the files pile up; it is not according to the rule, and the Dao does not deliver." ;
    pm:dependentStep "That a petition sent up for illness is acted on" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LuxianshengDaomenKelue ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep "That the petitioner stands in the register of his own master, through whom the petition is sent" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CelestialMasters .

pmc:CmMingxinBudao a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das jährliche Pfand hält den Namen im Register, und das Register hält den Schutz"@de,
        "The yearly pledge keeps the name in the register, and the register keeps the protection"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Lu Xiujing, Lu xiansheng daomen kelüe 004a-004b (DZ 1127), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "The code states that if the pledge of destiny does not arrive, the register of destiny is not sent up; that no separate additional pledge and no kitchen-feast makes good that lack; and that where a household has gone years without sending its pledge, the three heavens strike its name from the register, the household-guarding officer returns to the offices of heaven, the pneuma of the Dao no longer covers it, ghosts and robbers injure it, and bereavement, sickness, untimely death and blocked fortune follow — while the household never becomes aware of this and instead blames the master and holds a grievance against the Dao."@en ;
    skos:note "The last clause is the part that bears on examination and not on doctrine. The transmission states that the people to whom the protection has lapsed cannot tell that it has lapsed, and that their reaction to the resulting misfortune will be to blame the master. It has thereby described in advance, and dismissed in advance, the only report a practitioner could make against it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The three heavens strike the name from the register; the household-guarding officer returns to the offices of heaven; the pneuma of the Dao no longer covers the house; ghosts and robbers injure it; bereavement, sickness and untimely death follow, and the household never perceives the cause." ;
    pm:dependentStep "That the household-guarding officer continues to protect the household against calamity" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LuxianshengDaomenKelue ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep "That the pledge of destiny arrives at the parish by the fifth day of the tenth month, so that the register of destiny is sent up" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CelestialMasters .

pmc:CmMoburShengquan a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Keiner von ihnen bleibt nicht am Leben"@de,
        "Not one of them fails to be kept alive"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Lu Xiujing, Lu xiansheng daomen kelüe 001b-002a (DZ 1127), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "The work states that where the sick person cannot bear decoction and needling, he takes only the talisman in water and confesses the faults committed from the year of birth onward; that those whose faults deserve death are all pardoned; and that of the long-sick and the gravely ill, not one fails to be kept whole and alive."@en ;
    skos:note "The strongest efficacy claim in the file and the one with no failure side attached to it at the place where it stands. It is made in the sentence immediately before the author turns to the present state of the observance and announces that he will set out its illnesses, so that the unconditional claim and the catalogue of everything that goes wrong with it stand one line apart. Both are entered."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:CmShoufuYinshui ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LuxianshengDaomenKelue ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CelestialMasters ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity "Recovery from established and grave illness, and the remission of faults otherwise punishable by death" .

pmc:CmRiwenDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Werk der Überlieferung verbietet die Tagwahl, ein anderes füllt vierzig Blatt damit"@de,
        "One work of the transmission forbids choosing the day, another fills forty folios with it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chisongzi zhangli 2.8a, 2.12b-13a and 2.16b (DZ 615), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 2",
        "Lu Xiujing, Lu xiansheng daomen kelüe 008a-008b (DZ 1127), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "The code states, as part of what distinguishes the covenantal authority from heterodox practice, that in dwelling, in settling a tomb, in moving and in every undertaking one does not divine the day or enquire after the hour, but acts as the heart directs with nothing avoided and nothing sought; and it names the keeping of a calendar and the picking of days and hours as a folly grown worse, which the correct code has made plain and which men will not follow. The petition manual of the same transmission prints, for the sending of petitions, a monthly table of forbidden days, the days of the quarters and the full and new moons, the days of heaven's father on which no petition is sent up, the days of heaven's mother, the days of the killing of heaven which a petition must avoid, the days on which heaven's gate is closed to audience and no petition may be presented, and the days on which heaven and earth are shut."@en ;
    skos:note "Bears on pmc:AdmissibilityBoundToTheCalendar, and it is the first case in the holding in which a transmission states the refusal as doctrine. The internal alchemy recorded there declines to bind and locates the condition in the practitioner; this code forbids the binding and assigns it to the opposing party. The petition manual is at the same time a new witness for the statement itself."@en,
        "The two works are a matter of centuries apart and the manual is the later, so this may be a change in the transmission rather than a disagreement within it at one time. What makes it worth a node either way is the ground the code gives: it does not merely omit the calendar, it makes its absence the criterion by which the transmission is told apart from what it rejects. A later work of the same transmission then adopts the thing the criterion excluded, for the central rite, at length."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim "Whether the rite of this transmission is bound to a calendar of permitted and forbidden days" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ChisongziZhangli,
        pmw:LuxianshengDaomenKelue ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CelestialMasters .

pmc:CmSandeThreeGrades a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Drei Stufen der Erreichung für den Dienst"@de,
        "Three grades of attainment for the observance"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Lu Xiujing, Lu xiansheng daomen kelüe 002a (DZ 1127), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile",
        "Zhengyi fawen tianshi jiaojie kejing 003b (DZ 789), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission grades what the observance yields: the highest virtue becomes a divine immortal, the middle doubles its lifespan, the lowest extends its years. The admonitions give the same three and add the negative form, that by the precepts one who does evil does not live out his span but dies untimely."@en ;
    skos:note "The grading is the same in both works and the wording of the third grade differs in a way worth keeping: the code says the lowest virtue lengthens its years, the admonitions say the lowest preparation increases its years and does not die untimely. The second is a claim about the absence of an event and is the weaker of the two in exactly the way this holding cares about — nobody can be shown not to have died untimely."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:CmShoufuYinshui ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LuxianshengDaomenKelue,
        pmw:ZhengyiFawenTianshiJiaojieKejing ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CelestialMasters ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity "Immortality, a doubled lifespan, or added years according to the grade of the practitioner's virtue" .

pmc:CmShibuShouqian a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Gottheiten essen und trinken nicht, und der Meister nimmt kein Geld"@de,
        "The gods do not eat and drink, and the master does not take money"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Lu Xiujing, Lu xiansheng daomen kelüe 001b and 007b-008a (DZ 1127), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "The code states the rule twice in the same words: the gods do not eat and drink and the master does not take money, and this is what is called the pure covenant. At its second statement the rule is the criterion by which the covenantal authority is distinguished from what the code calls the inverted methods of heterodox and sorcerous practice, which sacrifice to ghosts and spirits and pray for blessings."@en ;
    skos:note "The code reports in the same breath that the rule is broken. At 007b it describes officiants who indulge their greed and their appetite, who carry out the law of the Way without aiming at merit, whose intention on the day of a healing is not mercy but the hope of profit, and whose thought is on wine and food; at 008a it has them drunk at the altar and vomiting beside the table. The rule and the report of its breach are one paragraph apart, which is what this work is for."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout "Taking money for the rite, and feeding the spirits, as the mark by which the transmission is distinguished from the practice it rejects" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LuxianshengDaomenKelue ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CelestialMasters .

pmc:CmWangqiuZhizhang a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Wer eine Eingabe ohne das zugehörige Unglück erbittet, wird zu Arbeit verurteilt"@de,
        "Asking for a petition without the calamity it is for is punished by labour"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chisongzi zhangli 2.19a (DZ 615), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 2" ;
    skos:definition "The manual states that where there is no such calamity or illness, this petition may not be recklessly asked for, and that one who offends is judged at the second grade of punishment; it then sets out the grades in labour, the second being to sweep the road and repair bridges, the fifth a fine of three thousand bundles of thatch for roofing the Celestial Master's parish, the sixth two thousand roof tiles, and gives the first as twenty days of road-sweeping."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout "Requesting a petition for a calamity that has not occurred" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ChisongziZhangli ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CelestialMasters .

pmc:CmXijiaoHu a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Dao geht nach Westen und übergibt den Barbaren das Gesetz des Weges"@de,
        "The Dao goes west and hands the barbarians the law of the Way"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhengyi fawen tianshi jiaojie kejing 013b-014a (DZ 789), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "The admonitions state that after the five thousand words were composed and entrusted to the keeper of the pass, the Dao went west among the barbarians and handed them the law of the Way, with the prohibition made especially severe there — no union of yin and yang, no killing of living things — because the barbarians could not be brought to trust the Way; that it then changed into a true immortal; and that the barbarians knocked their heads ten thousand times, shaved their heads and trimmed their beards, and asked to trust the true man, whereupon the true Way arose among them."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because it is a statement about a contact route made from inside, and it is the reason the route of this tradition stays disputed rather than being settled either way. A transmission that claims another's teaching descends from its own founder has said that it knows the other teaching; it has not said which way anything was borrowed, and its claim is the one a party would make. The passage is also the corpus's own account of why the two bodies of practice resemble one another, offered before anyone outside had raised the question."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity "The founding of the teaching that the barbarians received, by the same Dao that issued the five thousand words" ;
    pm:ascribedTo "The Dao, in its western journey" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ZhengyiFawenTianshiJiaojieKejing ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CelestialMasters .

pmc:CmYehuiKe a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Befleckungsfristen, in Tagen gezählt, während deren keine Eingabe gesendet werden darf"@de,
        "The pollution intervals, counted in days, during which no petition may be sent"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chisongzi zhangli 2.23a-23b (DZ 615), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 2" ;
    skos:definition "The manual cites the code of pollution: a death in the house, whether of an adult or a child, and a woman's childbirth, and a great mourning, pollute for a hundred days; a woman injured in her body at birth, a hundred days; entering the room of a woman who has given birth, three days; the death of a small child or of a bondservant, one month; the death of a domestic animal, one day; and one who was elsewhere is not polluted. A year's mourning pollutes for forty days; the lesser mournings, within the month; the pollution lifts when the month goes out. Going to a mourning house and weeping there pollutes for that day; an old mourning does not pollute. The food of a mourning household's offering, and the food of a woman in her three days and at her full month, are not to be eaten. And under all of these, no fast may be kept, no offering set out and no petition sent up; one who meets such a case elsewhere releases it with talisman water."@en ;
    skos:note "The most exactly quantified condition in this file, and the one that behaves least like the others: the states it names are public, dated and countable, they are fixed before any outcome is known, and neither party to the rite can adjust them afterwards. A transmission that suspends its own remedy for a hundred days after a death in the house has stated a condition that could be checked against a register of cases."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep "That a fast, an offering or a petition may be performed at all" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ChisongziZhangli ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep "That the stated interval of pollution has elapsed, counted from the death, the birth or the entry" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CelestialMasters .

pmc:CmZhangjueXiedao a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Korpus trennt sich von der Bewegung, deren Schrift ihm zugeschrieben wurde"@de,
        "The corpus separates itself from the movement whose scripture it was credited with"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Sanguozhi 8.28a, Dianlüe quoted in Pei Songzhi's commentary, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text (Zhonghua shuju 8.264)",
        "Zhengyi fawen tianshi jiaojie kejing 014a (DZ 789), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "The admonitions place the Yellow Turban rising among the causes of the ruin they are written after: men delighted in disorder, and so Zhang Jue and the Yellow Turbans made their rebellion, after which the dead were to be counted in the millions; and the text names a heterodox way that set the later heirs to dividing the pneuma and governing the people for forty-odd years in Hanzhong. The outside chronicle, by contrast, reports the two as one kind, describing Zhang Jue's way of great peace and Zhang Xiu's way of five pecks of rice in a single sentence and saying that the second's method was roughly the same as the first's."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a dispute and not as a finding because the two sides are of unequal kinds and the graph should not decide between them by preferring the one it read second. The corpus is a party and has an interest in the separation; the chronicle is an outside observer with no stake in it but with the distance of a hostile onlooker who calls both movements bandits. What the dispute settles for this holding is narrower and sufficient: the transmission whose file this is does not own the Taiping jing, because it says of that movement's founder that he was a heterodox way."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim "Whether the way of great peace and the way of five pecks of rice are one transmission or two" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Sanguozhi,
        pmw:ZhengyiFawenTianshiJiaojieKejing ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CelestialMasters .

pmc:CmZhangxin a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Ohne das Pfand nehmen die Gottheiten die Eingabe nicht an"@de,
        "Without the pledge the gods do not accept the petition"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chisongzi zhangli 1.1b-2a (DZ 615), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 1" ;
    skos:definition "The manual states that whoever sends up a petition must first prepare the pledge, and that if there is no pledge at all the gods do not accept, holding the sender to a mean and ungiving heart. It adds that a petition without the proper pledge is to be increased for the wealthy and reduced for the poor, and that a rich household which hopes for the merit but begrudges its goods and puts out the pledge of a poor man gains nothing by what it does."@en ;
    skos:note "The manual immediately supplies the exception, and it is worth recording beside the rule because it is the one place in this file where the transmission relieves a party of a stated condition: the code, it says, provides that for one living in cold poverty and want who asks for a petition or an offering, the master puts out the ritual pledge on his behalf. The condition is therefore on the pledge's arrival and not on the petitioner's ability to pay it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The gods do not accept, and hold the sender to a mean and ungiving heart; a pledge below the sender's means is of no benefit to what he is doing." ;
    pm:dependentStep "That the gods accept a petition sent up on someone's behalf" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ChisongziZhangli ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep "That the pledge appointed for that petition is prepared and presented, or put out by the master for one too poor to present it" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CelestialMasters .

pmc:CmZhangziGuoduo a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Zu viele Zeichen in der Eingabe, und die Beamten verderben die Sache"@de,
        "Too many words in the petition and the officers bungle the business"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chisongzi zhangli 2.19a (DZ 615), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 2" ;
    skos:definition "The manual fixes the length of the declaration of merits by the gravity of the case: three or four hundred characters for a great matter, up to five hundred where the petitioner is at the point of death, one or two hundred for a small one; and states that where there are more, the correct pneuma is scattered in clamour and the officers and soldiers bungle the business."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered among the cautions and not among the failure assignments because of the direction it runs in. It names a defect in the performance that is present, countable and knowable before the outcome — the characters can be counted — and attaches a stated consequence to it. That is the rarer of the two shapes this file holds."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout "Exceeding the prescribed length of the declaration of merits in a petition" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ChisongziZhangli ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CelestialMasters .

pmc:CmZhengyiMengwei a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Dao übergibt die Bündnisvollmacht der rechten Einheit"@de,
        "The Dao hands down the covenantal authority of orthodox unity"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhengyi fawen tianshi jiaojie kejing 014a and 020b (DZ 789), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "The corpus states that the Dao brought out the way of the covenantal authority of orthodox unity on the first day of the fifth month of the first year of Han'an, at the Red Stone citadel of Qu Ting in Linqiong county of Shu commandery, making a bond with heaven and earth and setting up twenty-four parishes to distribute the mysterious, primal and initial pneumas and govern the people; and that the recipient was Zhang Daoling, appointed Celestial Master because the people feared ghosts and did not trust the Way."@en ;
    skos:note "The date is given twice and not identically framed. At 014a it is the Dao that brings the way out, with Zhang Daoling named as the one the Lord Lao handed it to; at 020b the speaker gives it in the first person, receiving the way and its pneuma from the divine pneuma of the First Emperor of Han and taking five pecks of rice as the pledge. The second is the founding as the transmission's own voice tells it, and the pledge is in it from the start."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity "The receiving of a covenantal authority from the Dao, and with it the office of Celestial Master and the right to establish parishes" ;
    pm:ascribedTo "Zhang Daoling" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ZhengyiFawenTianshiJiaojieKejing ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CelestialMasters .

pmc:CmZhidaoBaibu a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Hundert Schritt ausgebesserter Weg tilgen die Verfehlung"@de,
        "A hundred paces of road repaired removes the fault"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Sanguozhi 8.28b, Dianlüe quoted in Pei Songzhi's commentary, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text (Zhonghua shuju 8.264)" ;
    skos:definition "The chronicle reports that Zhang Lu taught his people to keep their small faults to themselves, and that one who had committed a small fault was to repair a hundred paces of road, whereupon the fault was removed."@en ;
    skos:note "A quantified penance, and the only one in this file whose performance and completion are both public and countable. It is entered under the tradition although the witness is an outside chronicle, because the chronicle is here describing the movement's own rule rather than judging it; the judging sentences of the same passage are entered separately at the dispute below."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:CmShoufuYinshui ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Sanguozhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CelestialMasters ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity "Removal of a small fault" .

pmc:CmZhiliaoDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Zwei Werke einer Überlieferung beantworten den ungeheilten Fall entgegengesetzt"@de,
        "Two works of one transmission answer the unhealed case in opposite ways"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chisongzi zhangli 2.18b-19a (DZ 615), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 2",
        "Zhengyi fawen tianshi jiaojie kejing 018b (DZ 789), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "The admonitions instruct the libationer to stop: where the illness clears and returns, the patient is a bad person and is not to be treated further. The petition manual instructs him to continue: where the petition does not heal, a further and different petition is to be sent up, through some fourteen named stages, with no stage at which the series ends in a verdict."@en ;
    skos:note "The two are not reconcilable by saying that one addresses the patient and the other the procedure, because both are answering the same question — what to do when the treatment has not worked — and they give opposite instructions about the same next step. The works are of different dates and the manual is the later, so the transmission may simply have changed; nothing in either text says so, and this node records the disagreement rather than resolving it by a chronology neither work states."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim "Whether a failure to heal terminates the treatment or requires the next petition in the series" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ChisongziZhangli,
        pmw:ZhengyiFawenTianshiJiaojieKejing ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CelestialMasters .

pmc:CmZhongweiliDong a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Vom Gewinn bewegt mitten im Werk, ist aller bisherige Verdienst verworfen"@de,
        "Moved by profit in the middle of the work, all the merit before it is discarded"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhengyi fawen tianshi jiaojie kejing 012a (DZ 789), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "The admonitions state that a man of the Way should be furnished with a hundred practices and marked by a thousand good deeds, and that though he have nine hundred and ninety-nine good deeds, if the one remaining is not completed and he is in the middle moved by profit, the whole of the merit before it is discarded; only by governing himself, keeping his attention on it and never letting the precepts lapse does he pass beyond the age."@en ;
    skos:note "The clause is the sharpest statement in this file of the shape that makes such a claim hard to hold against anything: the account is cumulative and the balance can be reset to zero at any point by an internal event nobody else can witness, so that a practitioner who has done everything and received nothing has been told in advance where the fault lies."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout "Being moved by profit before the count of good deeds is complete" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ZhengyiFawenTianshiJiaojieKejing ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CelestialMasters .

pmc:CmZongfuXiuqin a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Fleiß hilft nicht, wo die Weihe unordentlich war"@de,
        "Diligence does not help where the ordination was irregular"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Lu Xiujing, Lu xiansheng daomen kelüe 006b (DZ 1127), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "The code states of those who received register and parish without a master's registration, or from a master they had long since ceased to belong to, or as laymen who attached themselves to a Daoist without being received by the prescribed procedure, that such persons are wholly false and are Daoists in vain; and that though they cultivate diligently and perform good works, the three heavens have no name for them, and they are therefore not exempt from unjust and untimely death."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the condition under which the transmission says its own practice fails for a practitioner who does everything right. The defect lies in a past transaction that is not recoverable by any amount of present effort, and the sign of it is the absence of protection, which is the same absence the practice was undertaken to remove."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The three heavens hold no name for him; though he cultivate diligently and do good, he is not exempt from unjust and untimely death." ;
    pm:dependentStep "That diligent practice yields protection from unjust and untimely death" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LuxianshengDaomenKelue ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep "That register and parish were received in the prescribed order, through a master in whose registration the recipient stands" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CelestialMasters .

pmc:CoCendNomaide a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Bis zum Ende einer nómad, oder zweier, oder dreier"@de,
        "To the end of a nómad, or of two, or of three"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Sanas Cormaic s.v. Imbas forosnai, Irish text in Stokes, Three Irish Glossaries (London 1862), p. 25, English in Cormac's Glossary, trans. O'Donovan (Calcutta 1868), p. 94" ;
    skos:definition "The term the entry puts on the sleep. The poet is watched until everything he is engaged upon is revealed to him, to the end of a nómad or of two or of three, or as long as he was reckoned to be at the offering."@en ;
    skos:note "A counted term with a second clause that uncounts it. The first half gives a unit and three lengths in it; the second half replaces the count with a quantity fixed by something else entirely, the length of time he was supposed to be at the offering. A practitioner whose revelation has not come at the end of the third unit is therefore not out of time, because the alternative clause is still running, and this holding has the same construction from other transmissions under the generalisation that the counted threshold never falls due."@en,
        "The unit is not established here, and the disagreement between the two obtainable translations is recorded at the tradition instance rather than resolved. What is established is that the transmission counts something, that the count runs to three, and that it supplies a second measure that makes the count non-binding."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InsularCeltic .

pmc:ColdAsSourceProtocol a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob Kälte Quelle oder Widerstand ist"@de,
        "Whether cold is a source or a resistance"@en ;
    skos:definition "An examination of whether exposure to cold supplies the practitioner with something, in the physical sense the word energy carries, or draws on him."@en ;
    skos:note "A second reading, that the claim reports an experience of being charged, is not established here and the correction matters. This node first cited an intervention study as showing that cold exposure raises circulating catecholamines. Read at the wording, that study attributes the rise to the breathing technique — cyclic hyperventilation with breath retention, producing intermittent respiratory alkalosis and hypoxia — and its participants practised breathing, not cold immersion, during the measurement. Cold was one component of a ten-day training and is not the variable the epinephrine measurements isolate. The claim's experiential reading therefore has no evidence in this file, and the study is retained only for what it does show."@en,
        "Status of what follows. The state above is procedure without cases, and everything below is written in the voice of a finding. It is not one. The notes record what the evidence appeared to show before any search for opposing work was made, and they are kept in that voice because rewriting them would destroy the reasoning that has to be checked. Read them as the expected result of an examination nobody has finished, not as its outcome. See prima-materia#386."@en,
        "The claim has two readings and they come out in opposite directions, so the protocol keeps them apart as prima-materia#341 requires. Read physically, it is turned around: cold exposure raises energy expenditure rather than supplying it. The heat produced in the brown adipose tissue that the cold recruits comes from oxidising the body's own substrate, and the deposit is depleted by the work, not filled by it. Cold costs; it does not deliver."@en,
        "The source transmission stands on the other side, and the issue that opened this said so before any evidence was consulted. The tibetan inner-heat literature sets cold as the resistance against which the heat is raised, never as what the heat is raised from. Where the draft and its transmission disagree, the measurements agree with the transmission. That is the reverse of the expected result and is the reason this node is worth having."@en ;
    pm:baseRate "Energy expenditure and substrate use at thermoneutrality in the same participants."@en ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchNotCarried ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Not carried. No search was made for work disputing the thermogenesis account or failing to replicate it. The reversal recorded in the notes is therefore one side of a question nobody has asked here."@en ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "A. M. Cypess and others, Identification and importance of brown adipose tissue in adult humans, New England Journal of Medicine 360 (2009), no. 15, pp. 1509-1517, doi:10.1056/NEJMoa0810780",
        "M. Kox and others, Voluntary activation of the sympathetic nervous system and attenuation of the innate immune response in humans, Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 111 (2014), no. 20, pp. 7379-7384, doi:10.1073/pnas.1322174111",
        "V. Ouellet and others, Brown adipose tissue oxidative metabolism contributes to energy expenditure during acute cold exposure in humans, Journal of Clinical Investigation 122 (2012), no. 2, pp. 545-552" ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "Not devised here. The deviation is read off the thermogenesis literature; the second reading off the intervention study cited below" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "Practitioners exposed to cold are found to expend less energy, or to end the exposure with greater substrate reserves, than matched participants at thermoneutrality."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:EnergyTakenFromCold .

pmc:CommunicationMustBeInterpretedByAShaman a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Mitteilung setzt einen Deuter voraus"@de,
        "The communication presupposes an interpreter"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), pp. 68-69" ;
    skos:note "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity: the source says that the seeker ought to consult a shaman even where the communication is apparently intelligible and easily understood, and that a candidate for the Sun dance must consult his mentor and be guided by him, but it names no consequence for the man who interprets his own. What it does name is one possible content of the interpretation, and that is entered in the definition because it is a stop rule."@en,
        "The stop rule: the communication may prohibit the candidate from dancing the Sun dance, and if it does he is to proceed no further in the matter. A ceremony that can be called off by the very thing it is undertaken to obtain is unusual enough in this graph to be recorded where it stands."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Wowihanble ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by named Oglala shamans, chiefly Sword, Tyon, Ringing-shield, Bad-wound and No-flesh, and by Finger on 25 March 1914; recorded by the agency physician J. R. Walker at Pine Ridge over his years of service there, closed December 1916; given in Lakota, part of it written out in Lakota by Sword and Tyon and translated by Burt Means, Thomas Mills, Bruce Means and Antoin Herman; the paper prints the translations only, so the Lakota wording is not carried." ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ShamanicInterpretation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LakotaRitual .

pmc:CompletionPresupposesGeneration a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Vollendungsstufe setzt die Erzeugungsstufe voraus"@de,
        "The completion stage presupposes the generation stage"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)" ;
    skos:note "The order is set by the arrangement of the treatise, which takes the generation-stage meditations before the completion-stage yogas. That the six yogas belong to the completion stage places tummo after the generation stage as well."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:CompletionStage ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:GenerationStage ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:TibetanTantric .

pmc:ConcealmentFromTheRecorder a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Praxis wird dem Fragenden verborgen"@de,
        "The practice is concealed from the questioner"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulos I and VI, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces" ;
    skos:definition "The recorder states that the people of this land, like those of Peru, hide the matter with the greatest diligence; that they deny it stubbornly even when accused and convicted, until they die impenitent; and that neither the fear of God nor an oath nor anything else avails to make them confess."@en ;
    skos:note "The comparison with Peru is the recorder's and is not evidence of any connection between the two. It is entered because it shows he was generalising across two colonial situations, which is one of the ways a report acquires content its informants never supplied."@en,
        "This is the most valuable sentence in what was read, and it cuts two ways. It records a gate: the transmission was held closed against an outsider who had the power to punish, which is a fact about the tradition. And it records the limit of the whole manual as a witness, in the words of the man who wrote it: what he has is what could not be hidden from him. Every other node in this file is to be read against it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NahuaRitual .

pmc:ConcentrationPresupposesVirtue a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Sammlung setzt Sittlichkeit voraus"@de,
        "Concentration presupposes virtue"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.1 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.7-I.8 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:note "The handbook is built as a commentary on a canonical verse in which the one established in virtue develops mind and understanding, and it glosses the order accordingly."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Samadhi ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Sila ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:ConfessionYieldsAverting a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Beichte wendet den Zorn ab"@de,
        "The confession turns aside the anger"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ConfessionBeforeTheIchuri ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AvertingTheAngerOfTheHuacas .

pmc:ConsciousnessCalibrationProtocol a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob sich eine Bewusstseinsstufe am Muskeltest ablesen lässt"@de,
        "Whether a level of consciousness can be read off a muscle test"@en ;
    skos:definition "An examination of the claim that the position of a mental or emotional state on a numbered scale can be established by testing the strength of a subject's muscle, as the scale's author reports having done over some quarter of a million times."@en ;
    skos:note "A second reason for caution about the direction of the finding. The source itself names conditions under which its procedure is said to fail: synthetic clothing on the subject, metal jewellery, the voice of the tester. A procedure whose stated interferences are all channels through which an expectation could travel is one whose null result would be expected on ordinary grounds, and whose positive result would need to exclude them. That observation belongs in the protocol and not in a verdict."@en,
        "The counter-search is owed and has not been carried out. Work claiming reliability for manual muscle testing exists, some of it from within the field, and a rejection that has read only one side is by SPEC §13 a one-sided dispute and not a refutation. Until it is read, the state of this protocol stays where it is."@en,
        "What the available evidence covers. Three controlled studies bear on the muscle test as a diagnostic instrument, all with blinding, and none of them supports it. A test-retest study on wasp-venom allergy with four examiners, seven patients and twenty bottles each reports an overall kappa of 0.03 with sensitivity around forty per cent and specificity around sixty; a dental double-blind study on materials testing reports the test unreliable; a double-blind randomised study on therapy localisation reports the same. The first states plainly that as a diagnostic tool the method is no more useful than guessing."@en,
        "What the available evidence does not cover, and this is the point. Every one of those studies asks whether the muscle test can identify a substance or a bodily site. None asks whether it can place a mental state on a numbered scale. The evidence therefore strikes the presupposition, that the test yields answers better than chance, and not the claim itself. No blinded replication of the calibrations is known, and the claim is unexamined in the strict sense even though its foundation is not."@en,
        "Why this claim and not another. Almost every claim in this graph is about an inner state for which no procedure exists, and pm:noProcedureDevised is accordingly the commonest state in the holding. This one names its own procedure, the procedure requires no instrument, and blinding it is cheap. Among the claims the graph carries it is the most nearly testable, and that is the reason it was opened."@en ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchNotCarried ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Not carried, and the debt is stated in the notes above. Work claiming reliability for manual muscle testing exists and has not been read; until it is, the negative reading rests on one side."@en ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "H.-J. Staehle, M. J. Koch and T. Pioch, Double-blind Study on Materials Testing with Applied Kinesiology, Journal of Dental Research 84 (2005), no. 11, doi:10.1177/154405910508401119",
        "R. Lüdtke, B. Kunz, N. Seeber and J. Ring, Test-retest-reliability and validity of the Kinesiology muscle test, Complementary Therapies in Medicine 9 (2001), pp. 141-145",
        "S. A. Schwartz and others, A Double-Blind, Randomized Study to Assess the Validity of Applied Kinesiology as a Diagnostic Tool and as a Nonlocal Proximity Effect, Explore 10 (2014), no. 2" ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "Not devised here. The procedure is the one the source prescribes; the falsifying condition is the ordinary requirement of inter-rater and test-retest agreement, and nobody has yet applied it to the calibrations themselves" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "Blinded testers, given the same states without knowing which, do not agree on their placement above chance; or the same tester, blinded to his earlier results, does not reproduce his own placements."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:HawkinsConsciousnessScale .

pmc:ConsultationPresupposesQualifyingWood a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Befragung setzt sonnenläufiges Holz voraus"@de,
        "Consulting the drum presupposes wood that turns with the sun"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, Of the magicall Ceremonies of the Laplanders, pp. 47-48" ;
    skos:note "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity: the source says the drum must be of such wood, and does not say what follows from using a drum that is not. The strength is the strength the text gives, not the strength the requirement sounds like."@en,
        "This is the condition the graph nearly lost. It was read at the wording in the same run as the rest of this file but reached only a local working copy, not the branch, and was recovered only because an uncommitted file was noticed. Recorded as a process finding in prima-materia#351."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:RunicDrumConsultation ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:DrumWoodTurningWithTheSun ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SamiNoaidi .

pmc:ConsultationPresupposesTaboos a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Befragung setzt die Tabus voraus"@de,
        "The consultation presupposes the taboos"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 132" ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity: the source does not merely prescribe the abstinence but says what its want does to the instrument. An unclean man may still address the oracle from a distance, which is a graded rather than an absolute exclusion and is recorded here rather than smoothed away."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Contact of an unclean person with the oracle is certain to destroy its potency, and even the close proximity of an unclean person may have that result."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:BengeConsultation ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:BengeTaboos ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:ConvergingDescentAcquires a pm:Converging ;
    rdfs:label "Was die Seele trägt, wurde beim Abstieg aufgenommen"@de,
        "What the soul carries was taken on during the descent"@en ;
    skos:definition "The correspondence between the hermetic account of the descent, in which each of the seven rulers gives Man a share of his own ordering, and the ophite account in which the ascending soul must give back or show what belongs to each ruler's realm."@en ;
    skos:note "Recorded because the asymmetry is the finding. A reader who meets the ophite speeches alone cannot tell whether the symbols are acquired, innate or conferred by a rite, and the received accounts settle that question with a doctrine borrowed from the other side of this node."@en,
        "This is the assumption that makes the seven-station ascent intelligible at all, and only one of the two witnesses states it. The hermetic treatise says outright that the rulers gave shares and that Man became a sharer in their nature; the ophite speeches presuppose that something is owed or shown at each gate and never say where it came from. The correspondence is therefore asymmetrical in evidence, not only in wording."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:convergingConcept pmc:HermeticDescensusPerHarmoniam,
        pmc:OphiteSymbolumOstensum ;
    pm:transmissionPath "Same milieu as above: Graeco-Egyptian, second to third century. No priority is asserted between the two accounts, and the dating of the Poimandres is itself disputed." .

pmc:ConvergingGatedAscentInOneWitness a pm:Converging ;
    rdfs:label "Dieselbe Sieben-Tor-Architektur, zweimal in einem Buch, und nur einmal mit Planeten"@de,
        "The same seven-gate architecture, twice in one book, and only once with planets"@en ;
    skos:definition "The correspondence between the Mithraic ladder of metal gates and the ophite gate speeches: both divide the ascent of the soul into gates passed one at a time in a fixed order, and both are preserved in the sixth book of one refutation, nine chapters apart. Only the Mithraic account assigns a planet to each gate; only the ophite account gives the words spoken there."@en ;
    skos:note "The order is what a transfer would have to explain. Lead-Saturn, tin-Venus, bronze-Jupiter, iron-Mercury, alloy-Mars, silver-Moon, gold-Sun is not the sequence in which the ophite speaker meets his gates, and it is not the received order by distance either. A borrowing that reversed or reshuffled the order while keeping the pairing is a larger claim than a borrowing, and nobody has made it."@en,
        "There is no independence here of any kind. One author set the two side by side to make a polemical point, and a second author preserved both while calling the pairing tendentious. The whole correspondence therefore lies inside a single channel, and it counts as one occurrence for any purpose that counts witnesses."@en,
        "This node exists to keep the two apart, not to join them. The architecture is shared and the halves are complementary in a way that invites exactly the merger this graph refuses: one account has the planets and no speeches, the other the speeches and no planets, and a table that seats each ophite ruler on a planet is the merger, performed silently. See prima-materia#595."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:convergingConcept pmc:MithraicKlimaxHeptapylos,
        pmc:MithraicPylaiPlanetarum,
        pmc:OphiteTransitusLiber ;
    pm:transmissionPath "Both accounts stand in Origen, Contra Celsum VI, at chapters 22 and 31, and both are quoted from the same lost work of Celsus, who adduces the Mithraic ladder expressly so that the Christian rites may be examined alongside it. No transmission between the two cults is claimed; what is documented is that the pairing itself is the work of the reporting author." .

pmc:ConvergingGradedAscent a pm:Converging ;
    rdfs:label "Die Seele durchläuft sieben Stationen einzeln, und an jeder wird etwas verhandelt"@de,
        "The soul passes seven stations one at a time, and something is transacted at each"@en ;
    skos:definition "The correspondence between the ophite gate speeches and the hermetic surrender of energies: in both, the ascent after death is divided into seven stations that are passed singly and in order, and at each one a particular thing is transacted rather than the passage being granted as a whole."@en ;
    skos:note "A second difference decides whether either can be examined. The hermetic passage names the seven things; the ophite speeches name the gates and never the passions. Every table that pairs an ophite archon with a vice is therefore doing what this node refuses to do: filling one witness from the other."@en,
        "The correspondence is asserted by the compiler and by neither source. What is shared is the form — seven, singly, in order, with a transaction at each — and it is worth naming because it is not the trivial part. A doctrine of ascent does not have to be graded: it could be one passage, or a judgement, or a threshold. These two divide it the same way."@en,
        "What is not shared is what is transacted, and the difference runs in opposite directions. The ophite passer brings something and names it: a symbol of life, his own beard, the spirit of a virgin, the symbol of the mother. The hermetic one leaves something and it is named for him: growth and waning, the device of evils, the guile of the desires, arrogance, daring, the striving for wealth, ensnaring falsehood. One ascends by asserting what he has, the other by being emptied of what he was given. Reading the two as one doctrine of shedding garments takes the hermetic half and supplies it to the other."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:convergingConcept pmc:HermeticRedditioEnergiarum,
        pmc:OphiteSymbolumOstensum,
        pmc:OphiteTransitusLiber ;
    pm:transmissionPath "Both corpora are Graeco-Egyptian of the second and third centuries and circulate in the same milieu; the hermetic writings and the diagram Origen obtained are separated by neither language, region nor period, and the ophite material carries names Origen himself derives partly from magic and partly from the Hebrew scriptures, which is the same pool the hermetic and papyrological material draws on. No transmission from one text to the other is claimed; what is documented is that no route needs to be found." .

pmc:CrookedLimbsIfTheOfficeIsRefused a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das verweigerte Amt wird am Leib bezahlt"@de,
        "The office refused is paid for in the body"@en ;
    dcterms:source "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXVIII, sect. 128, p. 349" ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity, and the consequence is quoted rather than paraphrased because it is the sharpest one in the file. The case is a single one, among the Khingan Tungus: a girl of fifteen entered by a clan spirit, taught by an old shaman to distinguish the questions asked by the assistants, a shaman at the time of the report for some twenty-five years."@en,
        "One case is a case and not a rate. What makes it worth a node is that the transmission states a specific bodily outcome for the refusal, which is the shape a condition of failure has, and not a general misfortune."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "If she had not become a shaman, her arms or legs or neck, or back would have been crooked for life."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:TungusCandidateCondition ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:TungusMasteringOfSpirits ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SiberianShamanism .

pmc:Daena a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Daēnā"@de,
        "Daena"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hadhoxt Nask, fragment 2.7-11, trans. James Darmesteter, Sacred Books of the East 23 (Oxford: Clarendon, 1883)" ;
    skos:altLabel "daena"@en,
        "den"@en ;
    skos:definition "What meets the soul at the end of the third night after death, in the shape of a woman. The oldest witness read here fixes three things a later reader can hold the transmission to: the moment, the end of the third night, after a wind that comes sweet-scented from the south; the appearance, of the size of a maid in her fifteenth year, fair, bright, white-armed, strong, tall-formed, high-standing, thick-breasted, beautiful of body, noble, of a glorious seed; and the answer she gives when asked who she is, that she is the soul's own conscience."@en ;
    skos:note "The account of the journey under mang describes the same encounter and gives the figure no age at all. The fifteenth year stands in the older Avestan fragment and not in the Pahlavi book, and the two are kept apart here rather than merged, because a reader who takes the age from one and the setting from the other is reading a text no witness gives."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Zoroastrian .

pmc:DaenaCounterpart a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Gegenstück, das die Seele des Bösen trifft"@de,
        "The counterpart met by the soul of the wicked"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hadhoxt Nask, fragments 2.15 and 3.25-33, trans. James Darmesteter, Sacred Books of the East 23 (Oxford: Clarendon, 1883)" ;
    skos:definition "The same structure with every term reversed: at the end of the third night a foul-scented wind from the north, and four steps that lead to the hell of evil thought, of evil word, of evil deed, and to endless darkness, against the four that lead to the paradise of good thought, good word, good deed, and endless lights."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because the symmetry is the point. A transmission that gives both outcomes in the same form, with the same number of steps and the same sequence, is making a claim with two branches rather than a promise; the passages read state the second as fully as the first."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Zoroastrian .

pmc:DancingPresupposesOfferingAndFlowingBlood a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Tanz setzt eine Gabe und eine blutende Wunde voraus"@de,
        "The dance presupposes an offering and a wound that bleeds"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 61 and p. 93" ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity: the corpus does not merely order the two before the dance but says what their absence costs. The wound may be as small as the skin of a louse in the first form and as large as the dancer wills."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Before beginning to dance the candidate must make an acceptable offering to the Sun and have a wound that will cause his blood to flow while he dances; when the blood flows as a token it is the surest guarantee of sincerity, and without such a guarantee the people or the Sun may doubt the professed purposes of the dancer."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:WiwanyangWacipi ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by named Oglala shamans, chiefly Sword, Tyon, Ringing-shield, Bad-wound and No-flesh, and by Finger on 25 March 1914; recorded by the agency physician J. R. Walker at Pine Ridge over his years of service there, closed December 1916; given in Lakota, part of it written out in Lakota by Sword and Tyon and translated by Burt Means, Thomas Mills, Bruce Means and Antoin Herman; the paper prints the translations only, so the Lakota wording is not carried." ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:OfferingAndWoundBeforeTheDance ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LakotaRitual .

pmc:DanshaHuanfan a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zinnober wird zu Quecksilber und kehrt zurück"@de,
        "Cinnabar goes to mercury and comes back"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 4 (Jindan), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 4" ;
    skos:definition "The observation on which the chapter rests its case for minerals over plants. Grasses and trees, burnt, are ash at once; cinnabar, burnt, becomes mercury, and by accumulated transformation it becomes cinnabar again. Therefore, the chapter argues, it is far removed from common plants and can make a man long-lived. It adds that the common people do not know that mercury comes out of cinnabar, and that told so they will not believe it, objecting that cinnabar is a red thing and asking whence it should become this white thing, and that cinnabar is a stone, and that burnt stones all become ash."@en ;
    skos:note "The chapter also reports the objection it met, and the objection is the same kind of argument as its own: cinnabar is a stone, and stones burn to ash. Both sides reason from what a substance does in the fire to what it will do in a man. The one that happened to have looked was right about the fire."@en,
        "The observation is correct and repeatable, which is rare in this collection and is what makes the passage worth a node of its own. What it does not carry is the step: a substance that returns to its own form after transformation is not thereby a substance that keeps a body from returning to earth. The chapter states the observation and the conclusion and leaves the step between them unstated."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Waidan .

pmc:DasaIddhiyo a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die zehn Arten des Gelingens, mit dem Gaukler und dem Pflüger darunter"@de,
        "The ten kinds of success, with the conjuror and the ploughman among them"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.22-39 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, London 1931, pp. 438-444; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "The handbook takes over from the Paṭisambhidā a list of ten: success by resolve, success as transformation, success as the mind-made body, success by the intervention of knowledge, success by the intervention of concentration, the Noble Ones' success, success born of the result of action, success of the meritorious, success through the sciences, and success in the sense of succeeding through right exertion in this or that work. Only the first three are reached by the course this chapter teaches. The seventh covers the flight of every bird, the eighth the riches of named householders, the ninth the conjurors who recite a spell and go through the sky and display armies in the firmament, and the tenth, on the commentary's reading, any work brought off by an art or a science, the acquiring of the three Vedas and of the three Baskets, and ploughing and sowing."@en ;
    skos:note "The ninth member is the one that costs this corpus something, and the chapter does not notice. The conjuror who goes through the sky by a spell is admitted as a genuine member of the class; and the discourse at Dīgha Nikāya 11, which this file already holds at pmc:DisplayOfPowersRefused, refuses the marvel of power precisely because an onlooker may put it down to a spell of that kind. What the discourse offers as the reason for distrusting the marvel, the handbook enters as a species of it."@en,
        "The word the chapter is about does not mean power. The handbook derives it from succeeding, and the tenth member is defined by that sense outright; the list is a list of things that come off, and the meditator's feats sit in it beside a good harvest. A reader who comes to this chapter expecting a catalogue of marvels has to be told that the transmission does not sort it that way."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:DasaPalibodha a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die zehn Hemmnisse, die vor dem Beginn der Übung abzuschneiden sind"@de,
        "The ten impediments to be severed before the training begins"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga III.29-30, Pali PTS [90] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, pp. 105-106; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "Ten things the handbook names in a mnemonic verse as impediments to be cut off by one about to develop concentration: a dwelling, a family or body of supporters, the gains that are the four requisites, a class of pupils, building work, travel, kin, illness, the books, and the supernormal powers. The list is closed and the handbook treats each in turn."@en ;
    skos:note "The rule the handbook states immediately after the list is what makes it a finding rather than a catalogue: this is not an impediment to everyone. A dwelling impedes only the man who falls into anxiety over its repairs, or who has accumulated belongings in it, or whose mind is caught by some business connected with it; for another it is no impediment. The same qualification is made for the family, the gains, the books and the kin, each with its own criterion and most with a story attached. So nine of the ten are stated as relations between a man and a thing and not as properties of the thing, and a reader cannot establish from outside whether any of them obtains."@en,
        "The tenth is the exception and is entered separately at pmc:IddhiPannayaPalibodha, because it is stated as a property of the powers themselves and because the handbook devotes two later chapters to what it here tells the aspirant to cut off."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:DavisFonsDisputatus a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Band beantwortet die Frage nach seiner Urheberschaft zweimal und verschieden"@de,
        "The volume answers its own authorship question twice and differently"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Davis, The Principles of Nature, 3rd edn., New York 1847, Scribe's Introduction, pp. xvi-xvii, with the footnote at p. 41",
        "Davis, The Principles of Nature, 3rd edn., New York 1847, pp. 43-46" ;
    skos:definition "Two parties, both inside one book, and the title page carries both: the work is BY AND THROUGH Andrew Jackson Davis. The scribe holds that the book must have been dictated by some other and higher source and names it the Spiritual World, with the spirits and angels of the Second Sphere in sympathetic communication with the subject. Davis holds the opposite about the source and says so three times in nine pages: there I do not have any counsellor or informer, but I receive the reality of what I request; my information is not derived from any persons that exist in the sphere into which my mind enters, but is the result of a Law of truth; and the process is one of spiritual analysis, in which a desire attracts the particular kind of truth and causes it to flow into the mind. He names Aristotle, Demosthenes, Galen and Swedenborg as having had impressions of the same species through a different medium, and places Swedenborg below himself for not having been in independent clairvoyance."@en ;
    skos:note "A dispute between an author and his editor about what the book is, settled by neither and printed together. It is entered because the difference is not a nuance: on the scribe's account the volume is testimony from elsewhere and on Davis's it is the output of a human mind in an exalted state, and every later corpus in the channelled block takes the scribe's reading. The scribe himself supplies the mediating formula in a footnote — all the inhabitants of the second Sphere are considered as united and forming one, so that the Mind referred to is the General Mind of the Second Sphere — which reconciles the two only by making the speaker a collective nobody."@en,
        "Which side a later reader takes decides what the corpus can be counted for, and that is why this is a node rather than a note. On the scribe's reading this is a received text and belongs with the Ra material and the Seth material; on Davis's reading it is a first-person report of a state, which is a different attestation mode and a different kind of evidence. The holding keeps both and asserts neither."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:DavisReceptio ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DavisPrinciplesOfNature ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SpiritualistAutomaticWriting .

pmc:DavisInterferentiaInsensibilis a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Der Zustand kann unbemerkbar ausfallen, und das Werk sagt es"@de,
        "The state can fail unobservably, and the work says so"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Davis, The Principles of Nature, 3rd edn., New York 1847, Scribe's Introduction, pp. xix-xx",
        "Davis, The Principles of Nature, 3rd edn., New York 1847, p. 52" ;
    skos:definition "Stated in one sentence with a parenthesis that is the whole of it. Physical causes incidental to the external system sometimes indirectly interfere with the medium of sensibility, preventing the free expansion of the mind; and when this occurs — and it can not be perceived by the senses — impressions can not be received, because the mind has not become thoroughly independent."@en ;
    skos:note "A stated condition of failure whose own statement removes every way of establishing that it obtained. The parenthesis is not a hedge added by a later reader; it is in the author's own exposition of his method, in the chapter the scribe names as where the claims are set out. A transmission that names a failure mode and declares it imperceptible has put every unsuccessful sitting beyond examination in advance, and the holding should be able to count transmissions that do this."@en,
        "The scribe builds the other half of the shelter around it in the Introduction: the clairvoyant never lectured when from physiological causes or surrounding circumstances he was rendered incapable of that perfect disconnexion which was necessary, and at such times as his interior perceptions were not duly expanded he refused to proceed with his dictations. So the undetectable interference is also declared never to have been allowed to produce a lecture. The two statements together are what SPEC §3 means by a claim that does not discriminate, and no protocol is entered because none was carried."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:DavisIndependentClairvoyance ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DavisPrinciplesOfNature ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SpiritualistAutomaticWriting .

pmc:DavisManipulatorNecessarius a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Kein Eintritt ohne Operateur, und die Strafe für den Alleingang ist der Tod"@de,
        "No entry without an operator, and the penalty for entering alone is death"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Davis, The Principles of Nature, 3rd edn., New York 1847, Scribe's Introduction, p. xvii",
        "Davis, The Principles of Nature, 3rd edn., New York 1847, p. 44" ;
    skos:definition "Stated by Davis with its consequence, twice and in his own voice. It is impossible for any one to enter voluntarily that state in which he can view with clearness things belonging to a sphere higher than the natural world; were such a thing to take place, that moment death would necessarily and inevitably ensue, because the state cannot be entered without a loss of one of the controlling forces of the system, and if that force is not supplied by the system of another the natural functions would cease and the spirit could not re-enter. Independent clairvoyance, therefore, must be induced by the action of another system. The scribe states the same in the other direction: if while the subject is in this condition the magnetizer should by any means lose connexion with him, the vital movements of the body would cease and the spirit would be incapable of re-entering it."@en ;
    skos:note "It also fixes the social shape of the transmission. The state requires a second person, and the Address names three more as witnesses and a fourth as scribe, with the reason for the number given as a correspondence to a trinity. So the work makes five people structurally necessary to its own production, and says so before it says anything else."@en,
        "The strongest form a stated necessity can take and the one least open to test, which is why both halves of it are quoted. The consequence is death, so no practitioner survives to report the condition having been skipped; and the scribe adds that the claim rests on the subject's own information, as he himself has informed us. A necessity whose only witness is the person it protects is a different thing from one a bystander could check, and the holding should be able to tell them apart."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Death ensues at that moment: the natural functions of the organization cease and the spirit cannot re-enter the body." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:DavisIndependentClairvoyance ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DavisPrinciplesOfNature ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:DavisMagnetization ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SpiritualistAutomaticWriting .

pmc:DavisNonInfallibilis a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Das Buch verbietet, als unfehlbarer Maßstab genommen zu werden"@de,
        "The book forbids being taken as an infallible standard"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Davis, The Principles of Nature, 3rd edn., New York 1847, An Address to the World, p. 1",
        "Davis, The Principles of Nature, 3rd edn., New York 1847, Scribe's Introduction, pp. xix-xx" ;
    skos:definition "The scribe's closing instruction to the reader, and it is addressed against the authority the volume otherwise claims. Reader, do not consider this book as a standard so infallible as to relieve you from the exercise of your own reason; although it is the production of a mind immensely exalted and having access to the knowledge of a higher world, it is still the production of a human mind, and if the work will not stand upon its own intrinsic merits, let it be rejected. The Address to the World sets the same rule from the other side: no arbitrary or external standard shall be recognised as suitable to test the truth of what is presented, and the verdict of Nature and not of men is required."@en ;
    skos:note "The two sentences do not say the same thing and the difference is the finding. The scribe submits the work to the reader's reason; the author withdraws it from every standard outside Nature, which is a standard nobody can apply. Set beside each other they leave the work open to rejection and closed to refutation, and the next clause of the scribe's own sentence completes the figure: if the contents commend themselves to the approbation of your interior being, let them be considered as true, even infallibly so."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:DavisIndependentClairvoyance ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DavisPrinciplesOfNature ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SpiritualistAutomaticWriting .

pmc:DavisParticulariaNonFirma a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Auf das Allgemeine ist zu bauen, auf Einzelheiten nicht"@de,
        "Generals may be relied on, particulars and minutiae may not"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Davis, The Principles of Nature, 3rd edn., New York 1847, Scribe's Introduction, p. xx",
        "Davis, The Principles of Nature, 3rd edn., New York 1847, p. 52" ;
    skos:definition "A rule of reading that the work lays down about itself. Particulars and minutiae correspond to incidentals and can not be relied on as producing the same unchangeable effects, for they stand in correspondence with incidental intervening circumstances of a subordinate character; generals are always manifest, corresponding with truth and inward reality, and can be depended upon as inevitably manifesting inward truth. In the same passage Davis withdraws the optical reading of his own commonest phrase: the expression I see, literally understood, would convey a wrong impression, and means simply I know."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a caution rather than as a near-miss of pmc:SelfWarningStandsInsideThePrescription, and the distinction is worth stating because both this node and the Hippocratic one entered on the same day fail that generalization on the same word. That statement is about a transmission warning the practitioner against valuing a practice for the wrong reason. This one warns the reader against relying on a class of the work's own contents, which relieves the work rather than the reader, and the Hippocratic case at pmc:HippocratesNonIntegrisDiebus relieves the author of the exactness he had just claimed. Three such places now stand in the holding and a successor statement about transmissions that exempt their own checkable part would have witnesses; it is not written here because the third one was read on the same day as the second and SPEC §16 requires the counter-search over the holding before the statement, not after."@en,
        "The scribe applies the rule to the volume at once and in the reader's direction: inasmuch as the author establishes that particulars and minutiae can not be depended upon, it can scarcely be expected that the many particulars and minutiae involved in this work of eight hundred pages are in every individual case nicely accurate, and whatever inaccuracies may be discovered can not affect the great general principles. So the only part of the work that could be checked against anything is exempted from the claim before the first page of it is read."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:DavisIndependentClairvoyance ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DavisPrinciplesOfNature ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SpiritualistAutomaticWriting .

pmc:DavisPlanetaeDatum a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Schreiber datiert die Planetenstelle vor die Beobachtung des Planeten"@de,
        "The scribe dates the planet passage to before the planet was seen"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Davis, The Principles of Nature, 3rd edn., New York 1847, Scribe's Introduction, pp. xv-xvi",
        "Davis, The Principles of Nature, 3rd edn., New York 1847, scribe's footnote at p. 161" ;
    skos:definition "A footnote by the scribe, set at the place, which turns the passage into a claim about a capacity and supplies the date it needs. Numerous witnesses can testify that what is said about an eighth and ninth planet was in manuscript in March 1846, and months before Le Verrier's calculations and conclusion had been announced in this country; the eighth planet was first actually observed in September 1846. These facts, the footnote continues, cannot but be regarded as important, proving as they do the ability of the clairvoyant to obtain knowledge of things beyond the reach of the natural senses, and hence as imparting confidence in his revealments which can not be verified by any process of sensuous investigation."@en ;
    skos:note "The ascription and its purpose are in the same footnote, and the purpose is the part worth entering. The dated manuscript is offered as a warrant for the statements that cannot be checked — which is the reverse of the usual direction, where a checkable claim is tested and an untestable one left alone. The scribe says plainly what the one is for: it imparts confidence in the revealments which can not be verified."@en,
        "The date rests on one document and one witness, and the footnote names neither at the place. The Introduction gives the detail elsewhere, quoting a pamphlet by James Victor Wilson, one of the twenty-three occasional witnesses and by then dead, for the manuscript dates 15 and 16 March 1846. So the chain is: a footnote by the scribe, resting on a pamphlet by a deceased witness, resting on sheets the Introduction declares to be preserved. Whether the sheets exist is the question, and this holding cannot answer it."@en,
        "The same footnote also admits something about itself, and it is the sentence that makes the ascription weaker than it looks: at first I did not understand this expression, and it is but recently that it occurred to me that it evidently has reference to the conclusions which Le Verrier had already established in his own mind from mathematical calculations, and which the clairvoyant saw. The scribe is reading the passage as foreknowledge of another man's unpublished reasoning, and he arrived at that reading after the discovery rather than before it."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:DavisIndependentClairvoyance ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DavisPrinciplesOfNature ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SpiritualistAutomaticWriting .

pmc:DavisPlanetaeNondumVisi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zwei Planeten jenseits des Uranus, einer davon beschrieben"@de,
        "Two planets beyond Uranus, one of them described"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Davis, The Principles of Nature, 3rd edn., New York 1847, pp. 160-161 and 167, with the footnote at p. 169" ;
    skos:definition "The work states that the sun's atmosphere extended to the orbit that the extreme planet occupies and traverses as a cometary body, that this is one more planet than is now known and has not yet been detected by observation, and that the existence of eight planets has been determined upon as nearly beyond all doubt — still the eighth and ninth are not yet recognised as bodies belonging to the Solar System. Of the eighth it then gives particulars: its density is four fifths that of water; its atmosphere is exceedingly rare, containing little oxygen but being mostly composed of fluorine and nitrogen; no organic constitution existing upon the earth could exist there alive for one moment; and it has, like Uranus, six satellites. Of the diameter and the period it says that the one is unnecessary to determine and the other can be inferred analogically from Uranus."@en ;
    skos:note "No protocol is entered and the omission is deliberate rather than an oversight. A pm:Testing node on this claim requires a modern work read at the wording under pm:evidenceFrom and a counter-search carried over it, and this run opened no astronomical literature and no critical literature on this work. What the holding has is the claim with its place; what it does not have is a protocol, and the absence says so."@en,
        "The nearest thing to a verdict is reported and was not read here, and the distinction matters more at this node than anywhere else in the file, because this is the one claim an outside judgement could settle. The research that preceded this run gives Podmore, Modern Spiritualism, London 1902, vol. I, as holding the density figure approximately correct but unremarkable for that, since it applies as well to Uranus, and the fluorine atmosphere preposterous; and as pointing out that the work counts only four planetoids although Astraea had been found in 1845. Podmore was not opened in this run. He is in any case secondary literature arguing rather than a procedure with an outcome, so even read he would enter only at a pm:Testing node and not here — but the reason he is absent from the graph is the rule, and the reason he is quoted as a report is that nobody in this run turned his pages."@en,
        "The work shelters the particulars in advance and the shelter is visible at the place. Where the eighth planet's orbit and diameter would be, a footnote states that it is contrary to the clairvoyant's invariable practice to relieve mental effort by imparting information on subjects accessible by ordinary processes, which is the reason given for withholding the two numbers an astronomer could have checked immediately. What is given instead is a density, an atmosphere and a satellite count, and the satellite count is given as the same as the one then attributed to Uranus."@en,
        "This is the only claim found in the part of the volume that was read whose outcome is fixed by something outside the work, and it is entered for that reason and not because the holding needs a cosmology. The claim divides: an eighth planet beyond Uranus, a ninth beyond that, and a set of particulars about the eighth. A later reader can take each separately."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DavisPrinciplesOfNature ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SpiritualistAutomaticWriting .

pmc:DescentPresupposesDepth a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Abstieg setzt die Tiefe voraus, die den Atem sammelt"@de,
        "The descent presupposes the depth that gathers the breath"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Xingqi yupei ming, in D. Harper, Early Chinese Medical Literature (1998), p. 125" ;
    skos:note "Recorded at stated necessity because a consequence is quotable, which is unusual: most sequences in this collection state an order and leave the consequence unsaid. The strength rests on the closing line, not on the chain of conditionals, which on its own would carry only a prescribed order."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The inscription closes by setting the two outcomes against each other: to go with the sequence is to live, to go against it is to die. The consequence is stated for the order as a whole, and every stage is joined to the one before it by the same conditional form." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:XingqiDescent ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XingqiYupeiMing ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:XingqiDepth ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:DibbacakkhuParipantha a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Das himmlische Auge ist eine Gefahr für den gewöhnlichen Menschen, und die Gefahr ist Wahnsinn"@de,
        "The divine eye is a danger to the average man, and the danger is madness"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XIII.100, Pali PTS [429] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, p. 497; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "Immediately after describing how the divine eye comes to pass, the handbook states that it is a danger to the average man, and says why: a mass of light appears through earth, ocean and mountains wherever he resolves, he is overcome by fear as he sees fearful forms such as ogres and demons, and the outcome is that he becomes distracted in mind and mad through the absorption. Therefore, it concludes, he is not to be negligent in the matter of seeing objects."@en ;
    skos:note "The two translations diverge at the outcome and the divergence is the reason both are cited. Pe Maung Tin has him become distracted in mind and mad through the absorption; Ñāṇamoli has his mind distracted and his jhāna lost. The first is an injury to the man, the second a failure of the operation, and the difference decides whether this passage is a caution or a failure condition. This file keeps it as a caution on the reading of the translation read first, and names the other."@en,
        "This is a stated harm to the practitioner from the exercise of the capacity the same chapter teaches, with a named mechanism and a named outcome, and this holding carries very few of those. What it does not carry is a remedy: the instruction given is to be careful, and the chapter goes on."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:Dibbacakkhu ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:DieshiYieldsZhilai a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Abzählen bringt das Wissen des Kommenden hervor"@de,
        "The counting yields the knowledge of what is coming"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhouyi, Xici A, the section beginning with the number of the great expansion and the section on prognostication, in the Wang Bi and Han Kangbo recension; read in the digital text of the Kanseki Repository, which names no printing" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Dieshi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JishuZhilai .

pmc:DirectKnowledgesPresupposeConcentration a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die höheren Kenntnisse setzen die Sammlung voraus"@de,
        "The direct knowledges presuppose concentration"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.11 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The handbook states that the six kinds of direct knowledge are arrived at with the support of perfected concentration and by nothing besides that." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Cetopariyanana,
        pmc:Dibbacakkhu,
        pmc:Dibbasota,
        pmc:Iddhividha ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Samadhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:DirectionIsNotThePhaseOfBreathing a pm:Converging ;
    rdfs:label "Die Richtung trägt eine gestufte Bahn, nicht die Atemphase"@de,
        "Direction is carried by a graded course, not by the phase of breathing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.47 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Xingqi yupei ming, in D. Harper, Early Chinese Medical Literature (1998), p. 125",
        "Zhangjiashan Yinshu, slips 36 to 37, in Vivienne Lo (trans.), How to do the Gibbon Walk: A Translation of the Pulling Book (ca. 186 BCE), Needham Research Institute Working Papers 3 (Cambridge: Needham Research Institute, 2014)" ;
    skos:definition "Two traditions assign direction inside the body to something other than inhaling and exhaling. One assigns it to two winds driven against each other in a single operation; the other to a place in a graded sequence, with descent in the middle of it and ascent at its end. Neither pairs a direction with a phase of the breath."@en ;
    skos:note "A second correspondence sits alongside this one and is deliberately not asserted as a separate node yet: both traditions sanction the wrong handling of the breath with death, the one saying that the breath kills the practitioner who forces it, the other that going against the sequence is to die. Two death sanctions are a thinner finding than they look, since a warning of death is the commonest form a strong prohibition takes anywhere, and the two do not agree on what the fault is: one names haste and force, the other names reversal of order. Recorded here so that the next reader can weigh it rather than rediscover it."@en,
        "A third member was added on 2026-09-16 and it changes the asymmetry the note above reports. The Pulling Book from tomb 247 at Zhangjiashan states a direction taken inside the body and the outcome of its taking it: with a staff in one hand, the face to a wall and the breath held, one foot treading the wall, when the qi of the head flows downward the foot will not be numb, the head will not swell and the nose will not be blocked (pmc:YinshuQiXiaXing). The breath is held throughout, so the direction is not carried by a phase of it; what is varied is the posture and the side. The agreement is on the denial and not on what takes the denial's place — the manual has two winds driven against each other, the jade inscription a graded course, and this one a posture — and the node is worded for the denial, so the third member belongs to it."@en,
        "The independence is asymmetric and the node says so rather than claiming more than it has. The jade inscription is dated to the fourth or third century before the common era on the reading this collection follows, which is earlier than any documented transmission of Indian breath technique into China; for that witness there is no route of contact to be had. The manual is fifteenth-century and stands downstream of centuries of Buddhist traffic in both directions, so its side of the correspondence cannot be declared contact-free with the same confidence. What the two share is therefore better read as one early independent witness and one late witness of uncertain independence, which is weaker than a convergence of two early ones and stronger than a citation chain."@en,
        "What the third member is worth is its date and the way its date is known. The jade inscription is dated on a reading of its script and its content, which is a scholarly judgement and can be revised. This manuscript is dated by the tomb it was sealed in, between 186 and 156 before this era, and nothing was written into it after that. For the independence question that is a different kind of ground: the earlier reasoning had to argue that no route of contact is documented, and this one can say that no route could have reached the object after a fixed year. It does not make the fifteenth-century manual independent, and the asymmetry below still holds; what it does is put two early witnesses on the side that was carrying one."@en ;
    pm:convergingConcept pmc:OpposedDirectionsOfPranaAndApana,
        pmc:XingqiDescent,
        pmc:YinshuQiXiaXing ;
    pm:independenceGround "One side carries the claim and the other has been withdrawn from it. The jade inscription is dated to the fourth or third century before the common era, earlier than any documented transmission of Indian breath technique into China, and for that witness there is no route of contact to be had. The manual is fifteenth-century and stands downstream of centuries of Buddhist traffic in both directions; it cannot be declared contact-free, and it was listed here as an independent attestation until this ground was required, which is what the requirement is for. Neither witness is an ethnographic gathering, so no recorder's feedback is at issue." ;
    pm:independentAttestation pmt:Daoist .

pmc:DistantPerceptionAgainstAControlProtocol a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der Zustand eine Kontrollbedingung bei derselben Aufgabe schlägt"@de,
        "Whether the state beats a control condition on the same task"@en ;
    skos:definition "An examination of the comparison clause: not whether free-response reports about distant targets exceed chance, but whether reports made from a deliberately entered inner state exceed reports made without that state, at the same task and from the same prior information."@en ;
    skos:note "A note on the evidence that could not be opened, because the state of this protocol depends on it. The two founding papers of the controversy, the critical appraisal of 1985 and the reply to it, and the joint communique of 1986, are in a journal with no open access and were not read; what this protocol says about them is therefore nothing. The same holds for the meta-analysis of free-response studies without altered states of 1997, whose reported effect size is higher than the ganzfeld figure of the study read here and which would bear directly on the comparison. A later run with access to that journal should read those four before the state above is moved in either direction."@en,
        "One of those two omitted conditions is the sharpest single fact this examination turned up, and it is a fact about the comparison and not about psi. An experiment in the meta-analysis ran two conditions, one under hypnosis and one without: 45 hits in 120 four-choice trials under hypnosis, 37.5 percent, and 29 hits in 120 trials without it, 24.2 percent. The meta-analysis included the hypnosis condition and not the control. The one study in the set that had built the state comparison into a single experiment therefore entered the category contrast with only its state arm. Whether that was deliberate is not decided here and does not need to be: a meta-analysis that sorts studies by the state of the receiver, and takes one arm of a within-study state comparison, has measured something other than what it reports."@en,
        "That study has to be read exactly, because it is the one most easily overstated in either direction. The authors themselves say what it does and does not settle: since no effect appeared in either condition, they cannot reach even a tentative conclusion about a differential advantage of the ganzfeld. A comparison between two nulls is uninformative about which would be larger if either were positive. What it does establish is that the comparison has been attempted with a design built for it, and that it did not come out; and that its own post hoc finding, a correlation of hits across the two conditions, points away from the method mattering and toward the person."@en,
        "The case for the statement, and it is one case. Storm, Tressoldi and Di Risio sorted free-response studies from 1992 to 2008 into three groups by the state of the receiver: ganzfeld, other noise-reduction techniques such as hypnosis, meditation, relaxation and dreaming, and standard free response with no state induction at all. The three groups take the same task and the same chance level. Ganzfeld returned a mean effect size of 0.142 and, on trial counts, 483 hits in 1,498 trials, a 32.2 percent hit rate; the standard free-response group returned a mean effect size of -0.029 and 455 hits in 1,872 trials, a 24.3 percent hit rate. The contrast is tested and not merely displayed: an analysis of variance gives F(2, 53) = 6.08, p = .004 two-tailed, and a Tukey test gives the difference between those two groups at p = .005. That is the comparison clause, supported, at pp. 475 to 477 of the article."@en,
        "The consequence for the generalization itself is a wording change and not a withdrawal, and it is proposed here rather than made, because the statement belongs to the node and this is its protocol. The clause people who have not entered such a state is doing more work than the evidence supports: in the one data set where the contrast holds, it holds only for participants selected for belief or practice, which means the contrast in hand is between selected-people-in-a-state and unselected-people-not-in-one. A statement that named that would be examinable by an experiment that exists in principle and, as far as this examination can tell, has been run once."@en,
        "The first deviation is inside that same article and is not mentioned in its abstract. The category difference does not hold for the participants the transmissions would be about. Splitting by participant type, for unselected participants there is no difference between the three conditions, F(2, 27) = 1.44, p = .255; for participants selected as believers, meditators and the like there is, F(2, 26) = 6.83, p = .004, with the difference again between ganzfeld and standard free response. The authors state it themselves: the effect is attributable to participants in the ganzfeld only, and within the ganzfeld selected participants beat unselected ones, t(27) = -3.44, p = .002. So the state advantage in this data set is not separable from who was in the state. The generalization says a practitioner enters a state; it does not say that only practitioners already disposed to succeed do so."@en,
        "The fourth line of evidence does not bear on the comparison at all but decides how much the supporting side is worth. A Bayes factor reanalysis of the very data set that carries the category contrast finds that studies randomising by hand outperform studies randomising by computer by about 6,350 to 1, and reads that as a flaw in the manual studies rather than as an effect, since no anomaly is needed to produce dependencies between trials that a hand procedure leaves in. With those studies excluded and two omitted conditions restored, the support for any psi effect in the set falls to at most 330 to 1."@en,
        "The second deviation is the decade that followed. The same two authors carried the same three-way comparison forward with studies from 2009 to 2018 and reported the outcome against themselves. In the new data alone the standard free-response category, which is the control, returns a mean effect size of 0.050 while the non-ganzfeld state category returns 0.045 — the control outperforms the state. On the combined databases the control category returns 0.027 with a 95 percent confidence interval of -0.024 to 0.078, which includes zero, and the analysis of variance on the categories falls to F(2, 74) = 2.58, p = .082. The ganzfeld-versus-control contrast survives in the post hoc Tukey test at p = .012 and becomes clear again only in a reduced model that drops the other factors. A contrast that was p = .004 in 2010 and p = .082 in 2020, on more data, is not a finding that has been strengthened by replication."@en,
        "The third deviation is the only experiment this examination found that runs the comparison the statement actually describes: the same people, the same task, with and without the state. Forty-one participants, each tested once in a ganzfeld condition and once in a condition equal to it in every respect except that the halved ping-pong balls, the red light, the headphones and the white noise were absent; the order of conditions randomised across participants; one target pool of eighty pictures; a hardware random number generator; a three-experimenter plan; and the methodological criticism of the preceding decade worked into the design on purpose. The success criterion was fixed in advance and had two parts, a significant difference between conditions and significant hitting in the ganzfeld condition alone. The result: no difference between the conditions, Wilcoxon T = 344.5, z = -0.11, p greater than .90, and no evidence of psi in either condition."@en,
        "What the examination does not find, and the absence is the reason the state is deviation rather than refutation. It does not find a body of work that has tested the comparison and come out against it. It finds one direct test, null in both arms; one between-studies contrast that supported it in 2010 and weakened to marginal by 2020; a reanalysis that removes most of the weight from the supporting side; and, beneath all of it, a main effect whose own replication record is contested — thirty studies from seven laboratories under the agreed standards giving Stouffer z = 0.70, p = .24. The generalization is not refuted. It is close to untested, and the literature that looks as though it tested it mostly did not: the canonical series compares performance against chance, which is a different question with a similar shape."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:protocolledPractice ;
    pm:baseRate "The score of the control condition at the same task. In the four-choice free-response design that carries most of this literature, chance is 25 percent by construction, and the standard free-response database of the meta-analysis read here returns 24.3 percent over 1,872 trials, which is the empirical control figure rather than the arithmetic one."@en ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Carried on 2026-09-20 and it found opposition of three kinds, each read at the wording. First, a direct null: the only experiment found that runs the comparison within subjects reports no difference between the two conditions, and no effect in either of them. Second, a methodological attack on the one meta-analysis that does draw the contrast: a Bayes factor analysis of the same data set finds that studies with manual randomisation outperform those with computerised randomisation by a factor of about 6,350 to 1, which the authors read as a flaw in the manual studies rather than as an effect, and that two conditions from included reports were left out of the meta-analysis. Third, a large replication series that fails the underlying main effect altogether: thirty ganzfeld studies from seven laboratories following the agreed standards, Stouffer z = 0.70, p = .24 one-tailed, mean effect size 0.013. What the search did not find is a preregistered multi-laboratory experiment with a control condition; none appears to exist."@en ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "A. C. van Dalen, L. R. B. Dias, J. M. J. Murre and S. A. Schouten, A prototypical ganzfeld psi experiment with a control condition, Parapsychology Laboratory, University of Utrecht, convention abstract, pp. 40-43, read in the declassified copy CIA-RDP96-00792R000700960003-3",
        "J. Milton and R. Wiseman, Does psi exist? Lack of replication of an anomalous process of information transfer, Psychological Bulletin 125(4) (1999), pp. 387-391",
        "J. N. Rouder, R. D. Morey and J. M. Province, A Bayes factor meta-analysis of recent extrasensory perception experiments: Comment on Storm, Tressoldi, and Di Risio (2010), Psychological Bulletin 139(1) (2013), pp. 241-247, doi:10.1037/a0029008",
        "L. Storm and P. E. Tressoldi, Meta-analysis of free-response studies 2009-2018: Assessing the noise-reduction model ten years on, Journal of the Society for Psychical Research 84(4) (2020), pp. 193-219, read in the author preprint doi:10.31234/osf.io/3d7at",
        "L. Storm, P. E. Tressoldi and L. Di Risio, Meta-analysis of free-response studies, 1992-2008: Assessing the noise reduction model in parapsychology, Psychological Bulletin 136(4) (2010), pp. 471-485, doi:10.1037/a0019457" ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:examinedBy "Not devised here. The comparison is the design of van Dalen, Dias, Murre and Schouten, who state the requirement in their own words, and the category contrast is the first hypothesis of Storm, Tressoldi and Di Risio" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "An experiment in which the same participants perform the same free-response task, with the same target pool and the same judging procedure, once in the named state and once in a condition differing from it only by the absence of that state, and in which the state condition does not score higher than the control condition."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-20" ;
    pm:tests pmc:DistantPerceptionFromAVoluntaryState .

pmc:DistinctionDesEsprits a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Unterscheidung guter und schlechter Geister"@de,
        "Telling a good spirit from a bad one"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Allan Kardec, Le Livre des Médiums, seconde partie, chapitre XXIV, sections 263-264" ;
    skos:altLabel "distinction des bons et des mauvais Esprits"@fr ;
    skos:definition "The capacity the whole system rests on, since every claim in it arrives through a communication that may be false. The criterion given is not evidential but linguistic and moral: spirits are judged as men are judged, by their language, and goodness and benevolence are held to be the essential attributes of purified spirits."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Spiritism .

pmc:DistinctivenessAgainstDissolution a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Unterschiedenheit als Streben des Geschöpfs gegen die Auflösung"@de,
        "Distinctiveness as the striving of the creature against dissolution"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1916" ;
    dcterms:source "C. G. Jung, Septem Sermones ad Mortuos, Sermo I (English translation of the 1916 private printing, Gnostic Society Library electronic edition)" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that a creature which ceases to distinguish falls into the undifferentiated fullness, ceases to be a creature and is given over to dissolution in nothingness, so that its natural striving runs towards distinctiveness against a primeval and perilous sameness; and that the striving is nevertheless not after difference but after the creature's own being."@en ;
    skos:note "Placed on the telic axis as pm:individuatingTelos. The placement is a reading and rests on the two refusals the passage makes in the same breath: dissolution is called the death of the creature, and the striving is said not to be after difference. Stated here rather than left implicit so that it can be contradicted."@en,
        "Recorded because it is the only position located so far that names both ends of the axis as a loss and then states a third movement. Dissolution into the fullness is called the death of the creature outright, which rules out the unitive end; and the striving is expressly not after difference as it is thought, which rules out the separative one. What remains is a striving after one's own being, and the source gives it the name principium individuationis."@en,
        "The attribution is why this is an origination and not a witness of gnostic transmission. The author printed the text under the name of a second-century teacher of Alexandria who did not write it, and it continues to be read and cited as gnostic teaching. pm:circulatesAs records that, and the node makes no judgement about the author's own reading of the ancient material."@en,
        "The god named above the god of the pairs of opposites belongs to the same argument and not to a separate doctrine: the qualities of the fullness are given as pairs, and what is set above them unites the full and the empty. A reading that takes this for a third party in a pantheon takes the figure for the argument."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:circulatesAs "A gnostic text of the second century, printed under the name of Basilides of Alexandria and read as ancient teaching rather than as a work written in 1916." ;
    pm:originatedBy "C. G. Jung" ;
    pm:telicOrientation pm:individuatingTelos .

pmc:DivineDreamIsNotOrdinarySleep a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Der göttliche Traum ist nicht der gewöhnliche Schlaf"@de,
        "The divine dream is not ordinary sleep"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Iamblichus, De mysteriis III.2-3 (ed. des Places 1966)" ;
    skos:definition "The warning that the mass of dreams occurring in full sleep carries no divination, and that only the state at the edge of sleep is treated as a sign of divine presence."@en ;
    skos:note "Load-bearing against any claim that sleep as such is an access. The source that comes closest in the whole collection to naming the threshold state is also the one that refuses the wider claim."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:Theopemptos ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NeoplatonicTheurgy .

pmc:DiviningPresupposesPupilage a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Befragen setzt die Lehrzeit voraus"@de,
        "Divining presupposes the pupilage"@en ;
    dcterms:source "James Johnson, Yoruba Heathenism, the account of the training of a candidate for the office of Babalawo" ;
    skos:note "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity, and the reason is a property of the passage rather than of the tradition. The source sets the pupilage, its minimum length and the division of its course, and says nothing about what befalls a man who divines without it. A stated consequence would raise this to a necessity; none stands in what was read."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:IfaCasting ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:BabalawoPupilage ;
    pm:readVia "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), appendix, extract from Yoruba Heathenism" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ifa .

pmc:DivyaCaksusIsWithdrawnAbsorption a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Das göttliche Auge wird in abgezogener Vertiefung geübt"@de,
        "The divine eye is exercised in withdrawn absorption"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.1-4",
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.26" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:withdrawnAbsorption ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:DivyaCaksus ;
    pm:situationGround "The sūtra reads bhuvanajñānaṃ sūrye saṃyamāt: knowledge of the worlds from saṃyama on the sun. The operator is named in the sūtra itself, and its state is fixed at III.1-4."@en .

pmc:DivyaSrotraIsWithdrawnAbsorption a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Das göttliche Ohr wird in abgezogener Vertiefung geübt"@de,
        "The divine ear is exercised in withdrawn absorption"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.1-4",
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.41" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:withdrawnAbsorption ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:DivyaSrotra ;
    pm:situationGround "The sūtra has the divine ear follow from saṃyama on the relation between the organ of hearing and the ether. The operator is named in the sūtra itself, and its state is fixed at III.1-4."@en .

pmc:DogonSiriusExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Steht die Kenntnis des Begleiters in der Überlieferung?"@de,
        "Is the knowledge of the companion found in the transmission?"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol addressing the claim that Dogon transmission carries knowledge of an invisible companion of Sirius. It asks whether the star names and the doctrine can be found among Dogon outside the circle of the original informants, and whether any account of them leads anywhere but back to the recording work."@en ;
    skos:note "Status of what follows. The state above is procedure without cases, set when the counter-search requirement was introduced, because this node does not record whether a search for opposing work was made. Its notes are older than the requirement and are kept unchanged; whoever documents the search restores the case-bearing state. See prima-materia#386."@en,
        "The counter-testimony has not been read at the wording in this run. A reply appeared in the same journal in the following volume, and a further exchange in the two years after; the deviation recorded here therefore rests on one side of a controversy that has two. See prima-materia#252. Under SPEC §13 this state is a one-sided dispute waiting for its second side, not a refutation, and the state below is set on the cases and not on the conclusion."@en,
        "The one trace that remains is itself the finding. Informants in that village say that in former times people knew of a star appearing in the year of the ceremony, and further enquiry leads back to the recorder. What looks like independent survival is feedback from the published account into the field it describes, and any later confirmation must be tested against that path before it counts."@en,
        "The re-study reports the ethnographic facts as straightforward. Dogon know Sirius as a star and call it the hunter's star; knowledge of the stars matters neither in daily life nor in ritual, the sun and the phases of the moon being what reckoning runs on. No Dogon outside the circle of the original informants had heard the star names the account turns on, and no one, not even inside that circle, had heard or understood that Sirius was double. The scheduling of the ceremony is done in several ways in the village the account draws on, none of them astronomical."@en,
        "The re-study's explanation of how the account arose is not adopted here as a finding, only recorded: that an informant spoke of stars of different generations, meaning two adjacent visible stars held as father and son to Sirius as grandfather, and that the recorder, who had read astronomy in Paris, made a double and triple system of it. That is a reconstruction of an intention and cannot be examined; the absence of the doctrine in the field can."@en ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchNotCarried ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Not documented. Predates the requirement; see the note at pmc:TummoHeatExamination."@en ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "Walter E. A. van Beek, Dogon Restudied: A Field Evaluation of the Work of Marcel Griaule, Current Anthropology 32/2 (1991), pp. 149-150 with its footnote 10, and pp. 156-157" ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "Walter E. A. van Beek, field re-study among the Dogon of Sanga and Yugo Doguru, reported 1991" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A report of the star names or of the doctrine of an invisible companion from Dogon speakers outside the original circle of informants, where the chain of the report can be shown not to run back through the published account; or a fixation of the doctrine in any text or record datable before nineteen thirty-one. Either would return the claim to contested and undo the deviation recorded here." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-01"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:DogonKnowledgeOfSiriusCompanion .

pmc:DoingPresupposesBeing a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "Es wird nichts getan, solange niemand da ist, der tut"@de,
        "Nothing is done until there is someone to do it"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1949" ;
    dcterms:source "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching (London 1950), in the first talks on the human machine and in the chapter on the absence of unity" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that a person is a machine governed by outward influences, that everything happens and nobody does anything, and that in order to do it is first necessary to be; together with the account of a person as lacking unity and consisting of a multiplicity of I's rather than one."@en ;
    skos:note "It bears directly on the disagreement recorded in convergences/left-hand-path-divergence.ttl. Both sides there dispute what becomes of the self at the end; this source holds that at the beginning there is no single self to be kept or surrendered, and that one has to be made first. That is not a third answer to their question but a claim that the question has a precondition neither states."@en,
        "The interest for this graph is the order of precedence. The claim is not that people do the wrong thing but that the doer is not yet assembled, which puts every question of method downstream of a question about who is applying it. A path chosen by a multiplicity is chosen by whichever part happens to be present."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:circulatesAs "A finding of an older school, transmitted rather than argued." ;
    pm:originatedBy "G. I. Gurdjieff, in the record of P. D. Ouspensky" .

pmc:DreamDivinationPresupposesPurityOfTheVehicle a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Traumdivination setzt die Reinheit des Fahrzeugs voraus"@de,
        "Dream divination presupposes purity of the vehicle"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Synesius of Cyrene, De insomniis" ;
    skos:note "The condition is on the state of the seer, not on a technique of sleeping. Recorded as order rather than necessity because the treatise makes the clarity of the dream depend on the vehicle without stating at that place what befalls one who proceeds with an unpurified one. Chapter divisions vary between editions; the work and its subject are given so that the passage can be found in whichever edition is at hand."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:DreamDivination ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:PneumaticVehicle ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NeoplatonicTheurgy .

pmc:DreamYogaIsPerformedInDreaming a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Traumyoga wird im Traum ausgeführt"@de,
        "Dream yoga is performed within the dream"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005), the yoga of the dream state" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:dreamingWithin ;
    pm:situatedNode pmp:DreamYoga ;
    pm:situationGround "The exercises are set out as apprehending, multiplying and transforming what appears within the dream, which places the performance inside the state and not at its edge. That the practitioner knows he dreams is what the training is for and is not a separate coordinate."@en .

pmc:DreamYogaPresupposesClearLight a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Traumyoga setzt das Halten des klaren Lichts voraus"@de,
        "Dream yoga presupposes holding the clear light of sleep"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)" ;
    skos:note "The treatise takes the recognition of the clear light before the training in the dream. It does not state at that place what befalls one who begins elsewhere, which is why the strength is order and not necessity."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:DreamYoga ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ClearLightOfSleep ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:TibetanTantric .

pmc:DrumMustBeInherited a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Trommel muss ererbt sein"@de,
        "The drum must be inherited"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Leem, An Account of the Laplanders of Finmark, in John Pinkerton (ed.), A General Collection of the Best and Most Interesting Voyages and Travels, vol. I (London: Longman, Hurst, Rees and Orme, 1808), chap. XXI, p. 474" ;
    skos:note "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity: the source says the drums were not thought sufficiently fit for their uses unless got by inheritance, and does not say what follows from using one that was not. The rule sits oddly beside the same recorder's report of a practitioner explaining how his own drum was made, and the oddity is left standing rather than resolved."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:RunicDrumConsultation ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:SunCourseRule ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SamiNoaidi .

pmc:DurationTracksDistance a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Dauer des Liegens folgt der Entfernung und endet bei einem Tag"@de,
        "The time he lies still follows the distance, and stops at a day"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, p. 57" ;
    skos:definition "How long he lies is stated to depend on how near or far the place is of which he makes enquiry — an hour or two for a near one — and to have an upper bound: the time never exceeds twenty-four hours, let the place be at never so great a distance."@en ;
    skos:note "A quantity that varies with a measurable quantity, and a ceiling that does not. Both halves are unusual: the first makes the claim comparable across cases, and the second makes it refutable by a single case that runs longer. Nothing else in this file states a number."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SamiNoaidi .

pmc:DureSantikeKarana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Er macht das Ferne nah, das Nahe fern, das Viele wenig und das Wenige viel"@de,
        "He makes the far near, the near far, the many few and the few many"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.119-135 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, pp. 465-468; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "Four resolves of one kind, each with its named performer. A distant place is made near and a near place distant; many are made few and few many. Named beside them are two more of the same sort: a sweet thing made not sweet and a thing not sweet made sweet. The reported cases are of alms and of journeys — a river turned to cream for monks who had got nothing but dry food, an elder who contracted the earth so that seven hundred monks went to another country for their begging round and asked afterwards where the ocean had been."@en ;
    skos:note "The one restriction the handbook puts on the whole group is a statement about who is allowed to have done it: the elders who seize the distance and make it near are aged elders, and the answer to the monks who ask where the ocean went is that aged elders make the great small. The qualification is age and standing and not any state of the operator that the chapter has been teaching."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:Dvadasanta a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Ende der Zwölf"@de,
        "The end of the twelve"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Vijñānabhairava 28 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)",
        "Vijñānabhairava 50 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)",
        "Vijñānabhairava 51 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)" ;
    skos:definition "A limit named as the end of twelve, at which the mind or the risen power comes to rest. The verses give the number and leave the unit unstated."@en ;
    skos:note "The count itself fluctuates. What is verse 50 in the numbering used here appears as 51 in other editions, and the following verse shifts with it. A reader working from a different edition should expect the two verses cited here to sit one number further on."@en,
        "Two things this node deliberately does not say. It does not fix the unit: the base text says end-of-twelve, and the reading that makes this twelve finger-breadths from the tip of the nose belongs to the commentarial layer, not to the verse. And it does not fix the place: the exegesis of Lakṣmaṇjoo counts several such limits in the body, at the crown, the brows, the throat, the base of the heart and the navel, and the verse that follows says outright that it may be reached in any manner and in any place. Nothing here yields a fixed radius. See prima-materia#38."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmc:EaAndMardukInstructTheExorcist a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Ea unterwies den Beschwörer und legte ihm die Beschwörung in den Mund"@de,
        "Ea instructed the exorcist and put the incantation in his mouth"@en ;
    dcterms:source "KAR 31 (VAT 8803) obv. 1-4 and rev. 26; Udug-hul 8: 25-26 and 30-31 (M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons, BAM 8, De Gruyter 2016, pp. 38-40)" ;
    skos:note "Stated by the exorcist about himself, in the first person, inside the text he recites: I am the messenger of the great gods, Ea and Marduk have instructed me comprehensively, I orally render their pure incantation, I carry out the words they say. The eighth tablet has him say it of himself in the same words and then name the transfer exactly: the great lord Ea has sent me and made his incantation acceptable for my mouth."@en,
        "The ascription is to a god and the attesting mode is textual and not third-party, because what was read is the passage in which the claim stands and not a report that somebody made it. Whether the office was in fact instructed by Ea is not a question this graph can put; that the transmission grounds the office there rather than in a lineage of teachers is."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:AsipuKapirigEridu ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Ea ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmc:EighthHogonTeaching a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Der achte Häuptling lehrte die Menschen die Merkmale des Sterns"@de,
        "The eighth chief taught men the characteristics of the star"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 282-284" ;
    skos:definition "The account that the eighth supreme chief, having discovered the star, escaped the sacrifice his seven predecessors had undergone by feigning death, returned claiming to have been inside the star and to know its secrets, and then taught men its characteristics and reordered the reckoning of time."@en ;
    skos:note "An ascription inside a myth of origin, and the article gives it as such: it is offered expressly as an indication of the changes and combinations of reckoning that appear in the course of what the recorders put in quotation marks as the history of the Blacks."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:KnowledgeOfSeedStarCharacteristics ;
    pm:ascribedTo "the eighth supreme chief, in the Dogon account of the origin of the sixty-year reckoning" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Dogon .

pmc:EjiOgbeFirstOfTheSigns a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Eji Ogbe als erstes der Zeichen"@de,
        "Eji Ogbe as the first of the signs"@en ;
    dcterms:source "James Johnson, Yoruba Heathenism, the account of the Odu and their number" ;
    skos:definition "The sign held to be the principal one, called the leader, the commander and the king. Its appearance on the divining bowl is taken to mean that the message delivered is of very great weight."@en ;
    skos:note "A stated order of rank among the signs, and therefore a place where a later reader can check whether a modern list has kept the tradition's own order or imposed another."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), appendix, extract from Yoruba Heathenism" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ifa .

pmc:EmptyingOfTheHeartIsWithdrawnAbsorption a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Das Fasten des Herzens liegt bei abgezogener Vertiefung"@de,
        "The fasting of the heart is placed at withdrawn absorption"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhuangzi 4, in James Legge (trans.), The Sacred Books of China: The Texts of Tâoism, Part I, Sacred Books of the East vol. XXXIX (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1891), Book IV, par. 2, pp. 208-210" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:withdrawnAbsorption ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Zhuangzi ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:EmptyingOfTheHeart ;
    pm:situationGround "The three steps are a graded withdrawal from the sensory channel and are stated as such: not to wait for the hearing of the ears, then not even for the hearing of the mind. The practitioner is awake throughout; what is withdrawn is the reliance on what the ears report. That is the criterion this coordinate names."@en .

pmc:EthiopianAsmatGuardAgainstMen a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Namen schützen vor Menschen"@de,
        "The names guard against men"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers written for Za-Walda Hawaryat (London: W. Griggs, 1900), section 4, p. LVII" ;
    skos:definition "The claim of the fourth section of the same roll: that the names shall enable a man to guard himself against evil men, liars, calumniators, men of wrath, the hand of him that would attack him, and the feet of those who would rush upon him by means of magical powers; and, in the petition that follows, that those who would oppose him in the time of judgment shall not do so."@en ;
    skos:note "Set beside the outcome of the sixth section because the two are of different kinds. Guarding oneself against liars has no moment at which it can be seen not to have happened; a spear turned back has one."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic .

pmc:EthiopianBarya a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Barja"@de,
        "Barya"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, prayers I, II, III, VI, VII, VIII, pp. 216-220" ;
    skos:altLabel "Borya"@en ;
    skos:definition "The being named more often than any other in the Meux roll, against whose evil eye the first, second, third, sixth, seventh and eighth prayers ask deliverance, and against whom the diseases enumerated in them are directed."@en ;
    skos:note "The edition read prints Barya and Borya for what is plainly one name and does not say which spelling stands in the parchment. Held as one naming with the second form as an alternate label rather than split into two, because nothing in the volume supports two bearers."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:nameForm "Barya" ;
    pm:nameRole "the bringer of the evil eye against which the prayers are said" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic .

pmc:EthiopianOathOfWerzelya a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Eid, auf dem der geschriebene Schutz ruht"@de,
        "The oath that grounds the written protection"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, prayer VII, p. 219" ;
    skos:definition "The undertaking sworn by the pierced child-killer, by the seven ranks of the archangels, that she will not contend against the Name, will never journey along the path where it is to be met with, or enter any church wherein it is commemorated, or any house or place whatsoever, and that this prayer shall be placed upon children, young men or the full grown, unto the end of the world."@en ;
    skos:note "The reason for a node of its own is that the roll states its own mechanism, which is uncommon. The protection is not a power the wearer acquires and not a favour asked each time; it is the enforcement of a promise extracted once from the adversary, and the written object is what puts the wearer inside the scope of that promise. A protocol that wanted to unsettle the claim would have to unsettle the oath, and there is no proceeding for that."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic .

pmc:EthiopianOwnerNamedInEveryPrayer a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Rolle nennt die Person, für die sie geschrieben wurde"@de,
        "The roll names the person it was written for"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, prayers I-IX, pp. 216-220; the supplementary rolls, pp. LV-LXIV" ;
    skos:definition "The condition, visible in every prayer of the roll and never stated as a rule in it, that the protection is asked for a named person: each of the nine prayers of the Meux roll closes by naming the woman Aheta Mikael, and the three supplementary rolls each name their own owner in the same place."@en ;
    skos:note "Read off the wording and not from a statement about it. The volume nowhere says that a roll must carry its owner's name; it prints four rolls, and all four do it in every section. What follows for any protocol is that a failure is always available for explanation as a wrong or missing name, and that this escape is built into the object rather than argued for."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic .

pmc:EthiopianSucklingProtectionPresupposesTheWearing a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Bewahrung setzt das Tragen über die Frist voraus"@de,
        "The preserving presupposes the wearing through the term"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, prayer VII, p. 218" ;
    skos:note "Prescribed order and not stated necessity. The prayer says the writing down and the wearing must be done and gives the term, and it names no consequence of breaking off, so nothing here carries a citable consequence of skipping."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:EthiopianProtectionOfTheSuckling ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:EthiopianWearingUntilChildbearingEnds ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic .

pmc:EthiopianSusenyos a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Susenjos"@de,
        "Susenyos"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, prayer VII, p. 219" ;
    skos:altLabel "Socinius"@en ;
    skos:definition "The being named in the seventh prayer as the man whose child was killed, who mounted his horse Cherubim, took a spear in his right hand, was granted power from a voice out of heaven, and slew the killer."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:nameForm "Susenyus" ;
    pm:nameRole "the saint on horseback whose prayer is written down and worn" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic .

pmc:EthiopianWerzelya a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Werzelja"@de,
        "Werzelya"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, prayer VII, p. 219" ;
    skos:altLabel "Ursula"@en ;
    skos:definition "The being named in the seventh prayer of the Meux roll as the one who came in the matter of the first child and killed him, and who after being pierced swears never again to approach where the Name is met with."@en ;
    skos:note "The edition read renders the name as Ursula in the introduction and prints Werzewelya in the translation. The identifier keeps the transliterated form of the roll and not the editor's substitution, which is a Latin name for a being the roll does not call by one."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:nameForm "Werzewelya" ;
    pm:nameRole "the killer of the child at the breast, bound by her own oath" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic .

pmc:EthiopianWornPrayerYieldsProtectionOfTheSuckling a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das getragene Gebet bewahrt den Säugling"@de,
        "The worn prayer preserves the suckling child"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, prayer VII, p. 218" ;
    skos:note "The list of harms is long and the wording is a petition rather than a guarantee: it says preserve and protect, and it names the woman it is for. What makes the claim examinable at all is not the list but the term beside it, and what makes it hard is that infant death from any of the named causes can be assigned to a lapse in the wearing."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:EthiopianSusenyosPrayerWorn ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:EthiopianProtectionOfTheSuckling .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicFallendeKrankheitAusschluss a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Mittel gilt nur für den, der noch nicht ins Feuer oder Wasser gefallen ist"@de,
        "The remedy holds only for one who has not yet fallen into fire or water"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 27",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 17" ;
    skos:note "An exclusion in the heading of a prescription is a different thing from an excuse after a failure. It stands before the application, it is determinable on the sufferer beforehand, and it divides the cases into two groups for which the book claims different things. That is what SPEC §15 asks of a failing condition and it is rare in this holding, which otherwise carries conditions that bite only afterwards."@en,
        "Whether the division is prognostic or self-protective is not decided here and cannot be decided from the wording. Falling into fire or water is the mark of a severe and manifest seizure disorder, so the clause may exclude exactly the cases in which the remedy would visibly fail. The book gives no reason, and the alternative is left standing rather than filled in."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The heading states the exclusion as the scope of the prescription: it is a remedy for the falling sickness when one has not yet fallen into fire or water. Outside that case the book claims nothing, and the same heading stands a second time in the index of the Reading printing, so it is not a compositor's slip."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicFallendeKrankheitHeilung ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1820,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850 ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicFallendeKrankheitZettel ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicFallendeKrankheitYieldsHeilung a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der angehängte Zettel nimmt die fallende Krankheit"@de,
        "The hung slip takes away the falling sickness"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 27",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 17" ;
    skos:note "The book carries a second remedy for the same affliction, without any such condition, some pages further on; the exclusion below therefore belongs to this prescription and not to the affliction."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicFallendeKrankheitZettel ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1820,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicFallendeKrankheitHeilung .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicGichterPresupposesMondfenster a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Knüpfen setzt das Fenster aus Wochentag und Mond voraus"@de,
        "The knotting presupposes the window of weekday and moon"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 29",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 18" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicGichterHeilung ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1820,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850 ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicMondfenster ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicGichterPresupposesUnbeschrauen a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Knüpfen setzt voraus, dass es unbeschrauen geschieht"@de,
        "The knotting presupposes that it is done unbeschrauen"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 29",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 18" ;
    skos:note "Prescribed order and not stated necessity, because the passage gives the condition as part of the instruction and says nothing about what follows if it is broken. The book has consequences elsewhere and none here, and the difference is kept rather than filled in."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicGichterHeilung ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1820,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850 ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicUnbeschrauen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicGichterYieldsHeilung a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Knüpfen vertreibt die Gichter"@de,
        "The knotting drives out the fits"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 29",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 18" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicGichterKnoten ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1820,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicGichterHeilung .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicKuhstallYieldsUebergang a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die geteilte Luft trägt die Krankheit auf die Kuh hinüber"@de,
        "The shared air carries the disease over to the cow"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Ephrata, Pa., 1828), Anhang, S. 71-72",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), Appendix, pp. 59-60" ;
    skos:note "A handbook of charms citing a newspaper as its warrant is itself a finding about the transmission, and the warrant it gives is an appeal to European physicians and to repeated trials rather than to a charm. It is recorded here and not in dcterms:source, where SPEC §10 does not admit it."@en,
        "The passage is not Hohman's. The Reading printing of eighteen twenty has no appendix; the piece stands first in the Ephrata printing of eighteen twenty-eight, introduced as a remedy made public in the Lancaster newspapers in the year eighteen twenty-eight and closed with the name of the paper, the German-language Lancaster Adler. The Harrisburg translation of eighteen fifty renders that name as Lancaster Eagle, which is how the sheet is usually looked for and why it is usually not found."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicKuhstallUebertragung ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1828,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850,
        pmw:RomanusBuechlein ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicKuhFaelltTodt .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicRheumaBriefFrist a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "An welchem Freitag des Mondes der Brief angehängt wird"@de,
        "Which Friday of the moon the letter is hung on"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 53",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 32" ;
    skos:note "Kept as a dispute and not resolved. Resolving it would require the intervening American printings, which were not opened; that the translation is called literal on its own title page is a reason to record the divergence, not to prefer either side."@en,
        "The eighteen twenty printing sets the hanging on at the last Friday in the old light, that is at the end of the waning moon. The eighteen fifty translation sets it at the first Friday in the decreasing moon, which is the other end of the same quarter. The two cannot both be followed, and the difference falls on the only part of the prescription that could be missed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicRheumaBrief ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1820,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicSchelwurzelYieldsVorzeichen a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Auflegen bringt das Vorzeichen des Ausgangs hervor"@de,
        "The laying on brings out the sign of the outcome"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Abschnitt »Die Schelwurzel«",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 20" ;
    skos:note "The same paragraph attaches two further and quite different effects to the same herb: carried with the mole's heart it overcomes and ends all strife, and boiled and filtered its water is a wash for dim or shining eyes. Only the middle one is a prediction, and it is kept apart from the other two here rather than gathered with them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicSchelwurzelProbe ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1820,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicSchelwurzelVorzeichen .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicVitriolZaehlungStrittig a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "An wem die sechzig Anwendungen geschahen"@de,
        "On whom the sixty applications were made"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 46",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 28" ;
    skos:note "The eighteen twenty printing says the author drove away his own severest toothaches more than sixty times. The eighteen fifty translation says he has cured the severest toothache more than sixty times and names no patient, which reads as a series over other people. The number is the same and the population it runs over is not, and a count whose population is unsettled cannot be compared with anything."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicVitriolYieldsStillung ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1820,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicWachholderKautel a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Den Busch wieder losmachen, sobald das Gut zurück ist"@de,
        "Loose the bush again once the goods are back"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 90",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 57",
        "Romanus-Büchlein, in Johann Scheible (ed.), Das Kloster, weltlich und geistlich, vol. III (Stuttgart 1846), pp. 489-523, here p. 516" ;
    skos:definition "The warning to watch for the return of the stolen goods and, when they are back, to carry the stone to the place it lay and to loose the bush again."@en ;
    skos:note "A caution about what the operator owes after the effect has occurred, not about the risk of performing it. Both printings and the older booklet carry it in the same order and with the same clause; no consequence of leaving the bush bent is named in any of them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicWachholderbusch ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1820,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850,
        pmw:RomanusBuechlein ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

