    dcterms:source "The Sword of Moses, part III, no. 130 in the editor's numbering (ed. and trans. Moses Gaster, The Sword of Moses, London: David Nutt, 1896)" ;
    skos:definition "The bone of a dead man and dust from under him put in a pot, tied in a woven rag with saliva, the words said in his name, and buried in the cemetery."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HarbaDeMoshe ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hekhalot .

pmc:HarbaDeMoshe131 a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Diebe und Räuber binden"@de,
        "To bind thieves and robbers"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The Sword of Moses, part III, no. 131 in the editor's numbering (ed. and trans. Moses Gaster, The Sword of Moses, London: David Nutt, 1896)" ;
    skos:definition "Said with the little finger in the ear."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HarbaDeMoshe ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hekhalot .

pmc:HarbaDeMoshe132 a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sie lösen"@de,
        "To release them"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The Sword of Moses, part III, no. 132 in the editor's numbering (ed. and trans. Moses Gaster, The Sword of Moses, London: David Nutt, 1896)" ;
    skos:definition "Said with the finger taken out of the ear."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HarbaDeMoshe ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hekhalot .

pmc:HarbaDeMoshe133 a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Dein Haus vor Dieben hüten"@de,
        "To guard thy house from thieves"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The Sword of Moses, part III, no. 133 in the editor's numbering (ed. and trans. Moses Gaster, The Sword of Moses, London: David Nutt, 1896)" ;
    skos:definition "Said over a cup of water poured out round the roof."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HarbaDeMoshe ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hekhalot .

pmc:HarbaDeMoshe134 a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Haus vor Räuberbanden hüten"@de,
        "To guard a house from robber bands"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The Sword of Moses, part III, no. 134 in the editor's numbering (ed. and trans. Moses Gaster, The Sword of Moses, London: David Nutt, 1896)" ;
    skos:definition "Earth from an ant-hill strewn round the roof with the words repeated."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HarbaDeMoshe ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hekhalot .

pmc:HarbaDeMoshe135 a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Dich vor schädigenden Geistern hüten"@de,
        "To guard thyself from harmful spirits"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The Sword of Moses, part III, no. 135 in the editor's numbering (ed. and trans. Moses Gaster, The Sword of Moses, London: David Nutt, 1896)" ;
    skos:definition "Said in the name of the first five sections, that the operator pass in peace and not in hurt; the same is used to excommunicate them on meeting them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HarbaDeMoshe ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hekhalot .

pmc:HarbaDeMoshe136 a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Für jede andere Sache, die nicht genannt ist"@de,
        "For every other thing not mentioned"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The Sword of Moses, part III, no. 136 in the editor's numbering (ed. and trans. Moses Gaster, The Sword of Moses, London: David Nutt, 1896)" ;
    skos:definition "The general clause that closes the list: for anything at all that the foregoing has not named, the operator says this section and reads on to the end of the Sword."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HarbaDeMoshe ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hekhalot .

pmc:HarbaDeMosheBenedictioAnteOrationem a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Sprich den Segen vor dem Gebet, damit sie nicht vom Feuer hinweggerafft werden"@de,
        "Say the blessing before praying, lest they be swept away by the fire"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The Sword of Moses, ed. and trans. Moses Gaster (London: David Nutt, 1896), the framing part, the letters communicated to Moses" ;
    skos:definition "The angels charge Moses to command the generations after him to say a named blessing before their prayer, lest they be swept away by the fire. The blessing itself is given in the text."@en ;
    skos:note "The second of the two stated consequences in the whole work, and the only one attached to the framing devotions. The danger is named and the remedy is named; what is not named is any way of telling, before or afterwards, whether it was incurred."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:HarbaDeMoshe ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HarbaDeMoshe ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hekhalot .

pmc:HarbaDeMosheCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Was den trifft, der sich nicht daran hält"@de,
        "What comes to the man who does not act accordingly"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The Sword of Moses, ed. and trans. Moses Gaster (London: David Nutt, 1896), the close of part III after no. 136" ;
    skos:definition "The penalty the handbook states, and the act it is stated for. Upon every amulet written from this Sword the user must first write a named formula, that the Sword be effectual to do his services and that the powers be delivered over to him as they were to Moses. If he will not act accordingly, the angels of wrath, ire, fury and rage will come near him to minister to him, will lord over him, strangle him and plague him all over, and will make his body like unto a dunghill. Four leaders are named: Mzpopiasaiel over the angels of wrath, Zkzoromtiel over those of ire, Kso'ppghiel over those of fury, N'mosnikttiel over those of rage, and the angels under them are numberless."@en ;
    skos:note "Corrected on 2026-09-24 against the whole work. This node said the penalty was for not keeping the preparation. It is not. The sentence stands at the very end of part three, immediately after the instruction about what to write first on an amulet, and the words not act accordingly point back at that instruction and not at the three days. The difference is the whole value of the node: what the work sanctions is a defect of form in a written object, and it sanctions no breach of purity, no misuse, and no failure of any of its hundred and thirty-six operations."@en,
        "It is a stated consequence and not a condition of failure. What is threatened is an injury to the operator; nothing here says the amulet would not work. The work ends without ever saying what an unsuccessful use of it would look like, and the search at pmp:HarbaDeMosheDefectusSearch establishes that for all hundred and thirty-six operations."@en,
        "The harm named is bodily and immediate and falls on the practitioner himself, which distinguishes this caution from the Hawaiian case in traditions/hawaiian.ttl, where the ill-luck of a fault falls on the party the chant honours. Where the sufferer is the one who could report the fault, the tradition can in principle be contradicted from inside."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:HarbaDeMosheGeneralisClausula,
        pmp:HarbaDeMoshe ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HarbaDeMoshe ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hekhalot .

pmc:HarbaDeMosheNuntiusNeminemInvenit a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Bote suchte einen Menschen, der es empfangen durfte, und fand keinen"@de,
        "The messenger looked for a man fit to receive it and found none"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The Sword of Moses, ed. and trans. Moses Gaster (London: David Nutt, 1896), the framing part, the charge to the swift messenger" ;
    skos:definition "God commands the swift messenger to make the Sword known to men who are pious, good, pure, righteous and faithful, whose heart is not divided and in whose mouth is no duplicity, who do not lie with their tongues nor deceive with their lips, who do not grasp with their hands nor are lustful with their eyes, who do not run after evil, keep aloof from every uncleanness, depart from every defilement, keep themselves holy from contamination and do not approach woman. The messenger goes down, asks where such a man is, and says in the work's own words that he thought in his heart there was no man who would do all this, and found none, and it was heavy unto him. He hands the Sword over only because God then binds him to it by an oath."@en ;
    skos:note "The clause about not approaching woman belongs here, in the charge to the messenger, and not in the preparation at pmp:HarbaDeMoshe, where this file carried it until 2026-09-24."@en,
        "The condition and the admission that nobody meets it stand in the same passage, three sentences apart, and the work goes on to deliver the Sword anyway. That is not a reader's objection smuggled into the text; it is the text's own account of how the transmission began."@en,
        "What it does to the rest of the file. Every operation below is offered to a reader whom the work has already said it could not find, and every failure of an operation therefore has an account waiting for it that the work supplied before the first recipe. No node states that account, because the work does not connect the two; the connection would be a reading and is left to whoever makes it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HarbaDeMoshe ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hekhalot .

pmc:HarbaDeMosheUsePresupposesPreparation a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Gebrauch des Schwertes setzt die dreitägige Vorbereitung voraus"@de,
        "Using the Sword presupposes the three days' preparation"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The Sword of Moses, ed. and trans. Moses Gaster (London: David Nutt, 1896), the opening instructions" ;
    skos:note "Corrected on 2026-09-24 and taken down from stated necessity to prescribed order. The consequence that stood here — the angels of wrath and the rest — was read off the sentence at the close of part three, and that sentence is about the formula at the head of an amulet and not about the preparation; see pmc:HarbaDeMosheCaution. With the consequence removed the strength cannot be held. The handbook does say, at the end of the framing part, that the user is not to touch this Sword before he has done all these things, which is an imperative and not a consequence."@en,
        "The prior step named here is the secrecy and purity condition rather than one of the counts, because the handbook binds them together in a single sentence."@en,
        "What is left standing after the correction is a finding and not a loss. A work that fences its own handling with three days of purity, a secrecy rule and a prohibition on touching states no penalty for any of them, and states one for a defect of form on an amulet. The care is all on the side of the operator's condition and none on the side of what he does with it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:HarbaDeMoshe ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HarbaDeMoshe ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:HarbaDeMosheSecrecy ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hekhalot .

pmc:HathaChapterOrder a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Handbuch ordnet Haltung, Atem, Siegel und Versenkung"@de,
        "The manual orders posture, breath, seal and absorption"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I-IV (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:note "Recorded at the weakest strength on purpose. The four chapters run posture, breath, seal, absorption, and that arrangement is often cited as though it were itself a rule. The rule is in II.1; the chapter order is only an arrangement."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:NadiPurification ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:presentationOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Asana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:HathapradipikaRecensionDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Textbestand und Verszählung des Handbuchs hängen von der Ausgabe ab"@de,
        "The text and the verse numbering of the manual are edition-dependent"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhapradīpikā, critical edition, Kaivalyadhama S.M.Y.M. Samiti, Lonavla",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhapradīpikā, critical edition, ed. J. Mallinson, J. Birch, J. Hanneder, M. Demoto and N.-J. Liersch (2024)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā, ed. and tr. P. Sinh (1915)" ;
    skos:definition "A disagreement among editors about what belongs to the manual and how its verses are numbered. The widely circulated editions descend from single manuscripts and differ in the count and extent of their chapters; a critical edition drawn from more than two hundred manuscripts, with the oldest dated 1496, settles neither question in favour of the numbering that most citations use."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as a node because every citation in this file carries a verse number, and a number is only as stable as the recension it comes from. The numbering used throughout this file follows the widely circulated vulgate text; a reader working from the critical edition may find the same passage under a different number. Naming the manual without naming the edition conceals that anything is at issue."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:HauAsSpiritOfTheThing a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Gabe trägt einen Geist, der ihre Erwiderung erzwingt"@de,
        "The gift carries a spirit that compels its return"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1925" ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Mauss, The Gift, translated by Ian Cunnison (London: Cohen and West, 1966), pp. 8-10" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that a thing given holds a spiritual power belonging to its giver, that this power seeks to return to its place of origin, and that the obligation to make a return gift follows from it. The claim is put forward in a work of nineteen twenty-five, on the strength of the single Māori passage above."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as an origination and not as a Māori claim because the generalisation is the author's. The letter speaks of one kind of valuable and of a forest rite; the obligation of the return gift as a universal law of archaic exchange begins here. That the reading of the passage was itself contested from nineteen twenty-nine onward is recorded in prima-materia#107, because those sides were not read at the wording in this run."@en ;
    pm:circulatesAs "the Māori doctrine of the spirit of the gift, and beyond it a universal principle of archaic exchange" ;
    pm:originatedBy "Marcel Mauss" .

pmc:HauOfTheGiftWordingDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Wessen Hau: der Wortlaut des Ranapiri-Briefes"@de,
        "Whose hau: the wording of the Ranapiri letter"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Elsdon Best, Maori Forest Lore, part III, Transactions and Proceedings of the New Zealand Institute 42 (1909), p. 439",
        "Tamati Ranapiri, letter to Elsdon Best, 23 November 1907, Polynesian Society Papers, Alexander Turnbull Library, MS-Papers-1187, folder 271, as collated in Hēnare, Journal of the Polynesian Society 127/4 (2018), pp. 455-457" ;
    skos:definition "A disagreement about what the source text says, not about how to interpret it. The manuscript reads the possessive of the second person, which makes the hau that of the giver and keeps the giver attached to the thing after it has changed hands. The printed version of 1909 has the definite article in both key sentences, which makes the hau that of the object. Every later reading of the passage rests on one of the two."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a dispute over the text rather than over its meaning, because that is the part a later reader can check and because the substitution was made silently. Both sides were read in the same study that reports the substitution, so the collation itself has not been verified against the manuscript or against the 1909 printing in this run."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:HauOfTheGift .

pmc:HawaiianKoCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Zuckerrohr ist der Hula-Schar verboten"@de,
        "Sugar cane is forbidden to the hula company"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Nathaniel B. Emerson, Unwritten Literature of Hawaii: The Sacred Songs of the Hula, Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 38 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1909), chapter IV, p. 30" ;
    skos:definition "A prohibition the teacher justifies in a sentence that turns on the word itself: if one indulged in sugar cane his work as a practitioner would amount to nothing, aohe e ko ana kana mau hana, his work will not be accomplished. The recorder adds a practical ground beside it, that chewing the stalk roughens the voice."@en ;
    skos:note "Two grounds for one rule, and they are of different kinds: one is a pun and the other is a physical effect on a singer's instrument. The transmission gives both without ranking them. Where a rule carries a homophonic justification, the graph should not read the justification as the reason the rule exists."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Ailolo ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HawaiianRitual .

pmc:HeatWithoutBreathProtocol a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die sanftere Form dieselbe Hitze erzeugt"@de,
        "Whether the gentler form produces the same heat"@en ;
    skos:definition "An examination of whether the effect measured in the practice survives the removal of its forceful-breath component."@en ;
    skos:note "No new evidence was gathered for this protocol and none was needed. The measurements already held at pmc:TummoHeatExamination separate the two components and report the temperature rise as depending on the forceful breath together with the visualisation, with the visualisation alone yielding the lesser effect. The claim that the breath may be dropped is therefore addressed by evidence that has stood in this file since the twenty-eighth of August."@en,
        "Status of what follows. The state above is procedure without cases, and everything below is written in the voice of a finding. It is not one. The notes record what the evidence appeared to show before any search for opposing work was made, and they are kept in that voice because rewriting them would destroy the reasoning that has to be checked. Read them as the expected result of an examination nobody has finished, not as its outcome. See prima-materia#386."@en,
        "The finding matters more than its size. Where a draft and its source transmission differ on a component, and the difference is exactly the component the measurements isolate, the draft is testable at that seam and almost nowhere else. Removing a prescribed element and claiming the effect survives is the one kind of modification that instruments can settle."@en,
        "What is not settled. That the gentler form yields less heat is not that it yields none, and the studies were not designed to find the floor. The deviation recorded here is from the claim of sameness and not from the claim that the gentler form does anything at all."@en ;
    pm:baseRate "Temperature change under the visualisation alone, against the change under visualisation together with the forceful breath, in the same participants."@en ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchNotCarried ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Not carried. The component separation was read off a single study and no search was made for work that failed to reproduce it."@en ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "M. Kozhevnikov et al., Neurocognitive and somatic components of temperature increases during g-tummo meditation, PLoS ONE 8(3) (2013), e58244, doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0058244" ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "Not devised here. The separation of the two components is the design of the second study already cited in this file" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "Practitioners of the gentler form show the same rise in measured body temperature as practitioners of the form with the forceful breath."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-02" ;
    pm:tests pmc:HeatWithoutForcefulBreath .

pmc:HedanTiaojian a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Bedingungen, unter denen das Elixier nicht entsteht"@de,
        "The conditions under which the elixir will not form"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 4 (Jindan), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 4" ;
    skos:definition "The compounding is to be done in a famous mountain, in a place without people, with companions not exceeding three; with a fast of a hundred days beforehand and washing in the five fragrances; with no approach to pollution and no coming and going with common people. Beyond that the chapter names two conditions that lie outside the operator's hands. Unbelievers must not be let to know of it, for if they slander the divine drug the drug will not form. And whoever has not the bone of an immortal cannot so much as get sight of this way. The transmission itself is bound by an oath: a gold man and a gold fish, or a gold man of eight ounces, are thrown into an eastward-flowing stream and blood is smeared for the covenant, and whoever steals the recipe and works it without the proper method will never succeed. Before the gold that the elixir makes may be used, eighty-eight catties of it are to be sacrificed to named powers, and twelve more put in a good leather bag and abandoned in silence in a crowded market on a good day, walking away without looking back; whoever uses the gold before sacrificing it will certainly meet with calamity."@en ;
    skos:note "The prescribed sacrifice of gold stands in the same chapter as the sentence that the way of long life is not in sacrifice and in serving ghosts and spirits. Both are printed as instructions and neither is qualified. The file records the contradiction rather than choosing a side, because a transmission that carries both is telling something about how it was assembled."@en,
        "Three of these conditions cannot be checked before the attempt and cannot be cleared after it. Whether an unbeliever heard of the work, whether anyone slandered it, and whether the operator has the bone of an immortal are all settled by whether the elixir formed. Together with the reseal-and-refire rule at the assay, they leave the recipe with no way of being wrong."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Jindan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Waidan .

pmc:HermeticDaemonUltor a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Der rächende Daimon tritt an die Stelle des Mind"@de,
        "The avenging daimon takes the place of Mind"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Corpus Hermeticum I.23 (trans. G. R. S. Mead)" ;
    skos:definition "The warning that to the mindless, the wicked and depraved, the envious and covetous, Mind is far off and yields its place to the avenging daimon, who sharpens the fire and torments him, rushes upon him through his senses, renders him readier for transgressions so that he meets with greater torment, and leaves him never ceasing to desire inordinate appetites, insatiably striving in the dark."@en ;
    skos:note "It also inverts the usual reading of such warnings. The daimon does not punish the transgression; he makes the transgression more likely so that the punishment increases. The mechanism is a spiral and not a sentence, and a summary that calls it hell loses that."@en,
        "The counterpart of the ascent and the nearest this treatise comes to a stated outcome of failure. It is worth keeping apart from the seven zones: the tormented one is not turned back at a gate, he is never described as arriving at one."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:HermeticVitaPia ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hermetic .

pmc:HermeticDissolutioAnte a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Aufstieg setzt die Auflösung des Körpers voraus"@de,
        "The ascent presupposes the dissolution of the body"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Corpus Hermeticum I.24-25 (trans. G. R. S. Mead)" ;
    skos:note "Prescribed order and not stated necessity. The treatise sets the sequence — first the body is surrendered, then the way of life to the daimon, then the senses return to their sources, and thereafter the man speeds upward through the Harmony — and never says what would follow from taking it in another order, because it does not envisage another order."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:HermeticRedditioEnergiarum ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:HermeticDissolutioCorporis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hermetic .

pmc:HermeticOgdoas a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die achte Natur, und was über ihr liegt"@de,
        "The eighth nature, and what lies above it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Corpus Hermeticum I.26 (trans. G. R. S. Mead)" ;
    skos:definition "The state reported after the seven: stripped of all the energisings of the harmony and clothed in his proper power, the ascending one comes to the nature belonging to the Eighth and there hymns the Father with those that are; they welcome him, he is made like to them, and he further hears the powers above the eighth nature singing to God in a language of their own."@en ;
    skos:note "Two stages and not one, and the second is only heard. The passage marks a boundary the ascent does not cross by the same means: what lies above the Eighth is audible before it is entered, and entering it is described in the next sentence as a surrender of self rather than a further passage."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hermetic .

pmc:HermeticPolarityOfMentalStates a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Polarität der Gemütszustände"@de,
        "The polarity of mental states"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1912" ;
    dcterms:source "Three Initiates, The Kybalion (Chicago: Yogi Publication Society, 1912), chapter X, Polarity" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that every mental state is a rate of vibration, that states fall into innumerable classes each of which has two opposite poles, that the positive pole of a class stands at a higher degree than its negative, and that a state may be moved along its own scale but never across into another class."@en ;
    skos:note "Opened for prima-materia#12, to date the step between the theosophical colour key of nineteen hundred and two and the numbered scale of nineteen ninety-five. Read at the wording, chapter ten."@en,
        "The finding is a denial, and it is sharper than a confirmation would have been. The vibration vocabulary is fully present and every mental state carries a rate. But the text states outright that the states belong to innumerable classes, each with its own two poles, and that movement is possible only within a class: love and hate are transmutable into one another, so are fear and courage, and fear cannot be turned into love. That is many two-ended scales and expressly not one scale carrying all feelings. A ranking of unlike feelings against each other is therefore not merely absent here, it is ruled out."@en,
        "What the later scales do inherit is the direction. The positive pole is said to stand at a higher degree than the negative and to dominate it, and a practitioner is described as raising his own vibration and inducing the change in another. Sixty years separate that from a numbered ladder, and the step is still undated."@en ;
    pm:circulatesAs "A study of the Hermetic philosophy of ancient Egypt and Greece, so described on its own title page and taken by its readers for a transmission from Hermes Trismegistus rather than for a work of its own decade." ;
    pm:originatedBy "Three Initiates, the pseudonymous authorship under which the work appeared" .

pmc:HermeticSeptemGubernatores a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die sieben Herrscher, deren Herrschaft die Menschen Schicksal nennen"@de,
        "The seven rulers whose rule men call Fate"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Corpus Hermeticum I.9 (trans. G. R. S. Mead)" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that a second Mind, itself brought forth by the Mind that is God, and being of fire and spirit, formed seven rulers who enclose the world the senses perceive, and that what men call Fate is the ruling of these seven."@en ;
    skos:note "The identification is made by the source and not by a reader: Fate is not likened to the seven, it is said to be their rule. That is the reason this node stands here rather than as a compiler's reading of a cosmology."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hermetic .

pmc:HermeticVitaPiaYieldsAscensus a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das fromme Leben bringt den Aufstieg und die Einung hervor"@de,
        "The pious life yields the ascent and the union"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Corpus Hermeticum I.22 and I.26 (trans. G. R. S. Mead)" ;
    skos:note "The claim is stated as the good end for those who have gained gnosis, and the treatise names both what precedes it and what happens to those who do not. That is more than the ophite witness gives, and it is still not a falsifying condition: nothing here could be observed to fail, because the whole of it is placed after death."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:HermeticVitaPia ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hermetic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:HermeticFinisBonus .

pmc:HeyokaFromTheWingedGod a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Mitteilung, die den Empfänger zum widernatürlichen Handeln verpflichtet"@de,
        "The communication that obliges the receiver to act contrarily"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 68 and pp. 83-84" ;
    skos:definition "A communication that appears in the form of one of the messengers of the Winged God is held to have been granted by him, and the one to whom it speaks must thereafter become heyoka and speak and act contrarily, or as a buffoon, for the rest of his life. A candidate to dance the Sun dance who receives such a communication must act as a clown in the dance lodge, appear to enjoy the tortures and make sport of his fellow dancers. Seeing the substance of the Winged God himself has the same effect; seeing the god Heyoka, who may appear in the form of a bent man, does not."@en ;
    skos:note "An obligation imposed by the outcome of a procedure and binding for life, entered because it is the sharpest cost this transmission attaches to a communication. The heyoka must also set the door of his tipi so that it does not face the centre of the camp circle."@en,
        "The list of the messengers is given twice in the same paper and the two lists do not agree. The first names the dog, the shore lark, the swallow, the night hawk, the frog, the lizard and the dragon fly; the second names the dog, the swallow, the snowbird, the night hawk, the lizard, the frog and the dragon fly. Whether the shore lark and the snowbird are one bird under two English names, or whether one of the two places is a slip, is not decidable from the paper and is held as an issue rather than smoothed away here."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LakotaRitual .

pmc:HicksEmotionalGuidanceScale a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Skala der emotionalen Führung"@de,
        "The emotional guidance scale"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2004" ;
    dcterms:source "E. Hicks and J. Hicks, Ask and It Is Given (2004), ch. 22" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that emotions form a graded ladder that indicates how near a person stands to a desired state, with joy, knowledge, empowerment, freedom, love and appreciation at its top rung."@en ;
    skos:note "The diagnostic marker is the top rung and its sequence: joy, knowledge, empowerment, freedom, love, appreciation. Six terms, and the fourth of them is the one that gives the sequence away, because a list built from an older stock has no reason to place freedom between empowerment and love. A later list carrying this block, in this order, carries it from here."@en,
        "The upper block of this scale reappears almost word for word in later lists that present themselves as older. Recorded so that the wording has a dated first appearance to be checked against."@en ;
    pm:circulatesAs "A law of the emotions, presented as a description of how feeling works rather than as one author's arrangement." ;
    pm:originatedBy "Esther Hicks and Jerry Hicks" .

pmc:HippocratesCerebrumVulneratum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Zeichen, das einer schweren Hirnwunde folgen muss"@de,
        "The sign that must follow a severe wound of the brain"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, Aphorisms VI.50, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. II, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849" ;
    skos:definition "When the brain is severely wounded, fever and vomiting of bile necessarily supervene. The claim is not about the outcome but about a sign, and it is made with a necessity: these two things follow, and not for the most part."@en ;
    skos:note "A necessity is cheap to refute and expensive to state, which is why the form is worth recording: a severely wounded brain without fever and without bilious vomiting is outside the sentence."@en,
        "This is the nearest the section comes to what the Suwen does at every one of its six organs, where each interval is paired with a bodily movement by which the strike is to be recognised. Here the sign stands alone, with no interval beside it, and it is stated of one of the seven parts the list of fatal wounds names."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AphorismiHippocratis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine .

pmc:HippocratesEvacuatioCeleris a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Entleerung, die durch ihre Schnelligkeit tötet"@de,
        "The evacuation that kills by being quick"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, Aphorisms VI.27, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. II, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849" ;
    skos:definition "Those cases of empyema or dropsy which are treated by incision or the cautery, if the water or the pus flow rapidly all at once, certainly prove fatal. The claim is about the manner of the operation and not about the disease: the same incision kills or does not kill according to the rate at which it empties."@en ;
    skos:note "A second caution of the same family stands nine aphorisms earlier and is not entered separately: when a person has been cured of chronic hemorrhoids, unless one be left, there is danger of dropsy or phthisis supervening. Both sentences say that the complete removal is the dangerous one."@en,
        "The sharpest caution in the section and the only one that puts the outcome on the operator's hand. It is the Greek counterpart of what the Susruta states about quantity in its lesson on venesection, where an excessive flow is forbidden and a remnant of the diseased blood is to be left standing on purpose."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:HippocratesIncisioEtCauterium ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AphorismiHippocratis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine .

pmc:HippocratesFaciesInterroganda a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Das tödliche Gesicht ist erst zu befragen, und es entscheidet sich in einem Tag und einer Nacht"@de,
        "The deadly face must first be asked after, and it is decided in a day and a night"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, The Book of Prognostics 2, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, p. 236" ;
    skos:definition "The treatise defeats its own strongest sign in the sentence after stating it. Inquiry must be made whether the patient has long wanted sleep, whether his bowels have been very loose, and whether he has suffered from want of food; if any of these be confessed to, the danger is to be reckoned so far less, and it becomes obvious in the course of a day and a night whether or not the appearance proceeds from these causes. Only if none of these is said to exist, and the symptoms do not subside in that time, is death to be known for certain to be at hand."@en ;
    skos:note "The caution is what makes the sign a sign rather than a verdict, and it carries its own counted term: one day and one night, within which the alternative explanation either holds or falls. The holding has no other place where a sign is given a stated interval for being wrong."@en,
        "Two further defeating conditions stand in the same treatise and are not entered separately. Chapter 2 excepts the sleeper who does not close his lids from habit, and the case where the open-lidded sleep goes with diarrhoea or a purgative; chapter 12 says the urine prognosis does not hold if the bladder itself is diseased. All three have the same form: the sign is withdrawn where another cause for it is available."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:HippocratesFaciesMoribunda ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PrognosticonHippocratis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine .

pmc:HippocratesIncisioTemporisVetita a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Kein Schnitt an der Schläfe, und der Krampf wechselt die Seite"@de,
        "No incision at the temple, and the convulsion crosses over"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, On Injuries of the Head 13, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, pp. 457-458" ;
    skos:definition "The one prohibition in the treatise that comes with a named and lateralised consequence: incisions may be practised with impunity on other parts of the head, with the exception of the temple and the parts above it, where there is a vein that runs across the temple, in which region an incision is not to be made. For convulsions seize on a person who has been thus treated; and if the incision be on the left temple the convulsions seize the right side, and if on the right side they take place on the left side."@en ;
    skos:note "The same chapter carries the other half of the local régime and it is not entered separately: in a wound of the head you must not apply anything liquid, not even wine, nor a cataplasm, nor conduct the treatment with tents, nor apply a bandage, unless the wound be on the forehead or the bare part or about the eyebrow and eye — and even there only while the inflammation and swelling last. Four prohibitions with one exception and no consequence stated for any of them."@en,
        "Worth entering for the form of the consequence rather than its content. A caution whose stated outcome is a crossing — left incision, right convulsion — excludes an outcome, because a convulsion on the same side would not be the thing announced. The nineteenth chapter states the same crossing for the neglected wound, so the corpus holds it twice and in two different causal settings."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:HippocratesIncisioCapitis ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeVulneribusCapitis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine .

pmc:HippocratesIntestinumTransfixum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein durchbohrter Dünndarm wächst nicht zusammen"@de,
        "A transfixed small intestine does not unite"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, Aphorisms VI.24, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. II, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849" ;
    skos:definition "Stated of the part and not of the patient: if any of the small intestines be transfixed, it does not unite. The neighbouring aphorism says the same of a bone, a cartilage, a nerve and the slender part of the jaw, which once cut out do not grow again."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered beside the list of fatal parts because it is the same operation at one remove. It names a site, it names an outcome, and the outcome is an absence — the wound does not close — which is a thing an observer could in principle be wrong about and no term is set for the observation."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AphorismiHippocratis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine .

pmc:HippocratesMeninxNonDenudanda a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Nicht in einem Zug bis zur Hirnhaut durchsägen"@de,
        "Do not saw through to the membrane at one sitting"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, On Injuries of the Head 21, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, pp. 465-466" ;
    skos:definition "Stated with two consequences, and conditional on who has had the case. If you have had the management from the first you must not at once saw the bone down to the meninx, for it is not proper that the membrane should be laid bare and exposed to injuries for a length of time, as in the end it may become fungous; and there is another danger, that in the act of sawing you should wound the meninx. When only a very little of the bone remains to be sawed and the bone can be moved, you must desist and leave the bone to fall out of itself. Where the case is taken over from another after a time, the opposite is prescribed: saw at once down to the meninx with a serrated trepan."@en ;
    skos:note "A rule indexed on the surgeon's history with the case rather than on the state of the injury is a shape the holding should be able to count, and this is the second place in this file that has it. The first is pmc:HippocratesVulnusCapitisCulpaMedici, where the counted death is attached to the surgeon's omission. Both put the operator inside the conditions of the operation's success."@en,
        "Entered as a necessity because the consequence is stated twice and in the text's own words, and because the instruction reverses on a circumstance that has nothing to do with the patient: who was in charge from the beginning. The same reversal governs the perforator at the end of the chapter — if you have had the charge from the first you must not penetrate to the membrane but leave a thin scale of bone — and the three-day window in chapter 14 carries the same condition."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The membrane is laid bare and exposed for a length of time and may in the end become fungous, and it may be wounded in the act of sawing." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:HippocratesTrepanatio ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeVulneribusCapitis ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:HippocratesOssisRadulatio ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine .

pmc:HippocratesNonIntegrisDiebus a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Keine dieser Fristen lässt sich in ganzen Tagen zählen"@de,
        "None of these periods can be counted in whole days"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, The Book of Prognostics 20, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, p. 251; the Greek from Hippocrates, vol. II, ed. and trans. W. H. S. Jones, Loeb Classical Library 148, London 1923, p. 42" ;
    skos:altLabel "οὐ δύναται δὲ ὅλῃσιν ἡμέρῃσιν ἀριθμεῖσθαι οὐδὲν τούτων ἀτρεκέως"@grc ;
    skos:definition "The sentence that follows the series, in the same chapter and in the same breath: none of these can be truly calculated by whole days, for neither the year nor the months can be numbered by entire days. The treatise states the numbers and then withdraws their precision, giving as its ground the incommensurability of the calendar itself."@en ;
    skos:note "A warning against the thing the passage prescribes, standing inside the prescribing passage, in the mouth of the writer who gave it, two sentences later. It is entered as its own node because it does what no other counted threshold in the holding does: it removes in advance the outcome that would have told against the series, namely a crisis that falls a day beside a stated day."@en,
        "It is deliberately NOT added to the derivation of pmc:SelfWarningStandsInsideThePrescription, and the near-miss is worth recording rather than passing over. That statement is about a transmission warning the practitioner against valuing a practice for the wrong reason. This warns against the exactness of a count and not against a valuing, and the one who is relieved by it is the author. A successor statement about transmissions that state a number and withdraw its precision in the same passage would take this as a witness; the existing one does not, and stretching it to fit would make it unfalsifiable in exactly the way its own notes guard against."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:HippocratesDiesCritici ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PrognosticonHippocratis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine .

pmc:HippocratesPronoia a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Vorauskenntnis, wie dieser Korpus das Geschäft des Arztes bestimmt"@de,
        "Foreknowledge, as this corpus defines the physician's business"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, The Book of Prognostics 1, in the English of Francis Antony Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, pp. 234-235; the Greek from Hippocrates, vol. II, ed. and trans. W. H. S. Jones, Loeb Classical Library 148, London 1923, pp. 6-8" ;
    skos:altLabel "πρόνοια"@grc ;
    skos:definition "The treatise opens by naming a capacity and its scope: it is an excellent thing for the physician to cultivate foreknowledge, for by foreseeing and foretelling in the presence of the sick the present, the past and the future, and by explaining the omissions the patients have been guilty of, he will the more readily be believed to know their circumstances, so that men will have confidence to entrust themselves to him. The capacity is stated as covering all three tenses and not the future alone."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because the corpus states the capacity before stating any sign, and states it in terms of what it produces in the patient rather than in terms of what it establishes about the disease. The sentence that follows puts the order plainly: it is impossible to make all the sick well, and this would have been better than to be able to foretell what is going to happen. The capacity is named second-best by its own treatise."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PrognosticonHippocratis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine .

pmc:HippocratesPronoiaAnaitios a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Ansagen soll den Ansagenden unbelangbar machen"@de,
        "The announcing is said to make the announcer blameless"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, The Book of Prognostics 1, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, p. 235" ;
    skos:definition "Stated in one clause of the opening chapter: by foreknowing and foretelling those who will die and those who will be saved, the physician would be blameless. The practice is the public announcement, the thing arrived at is the position of not being held the cause, and the treatise names no other return on the announcing in that chapter."@en ;
    skos:note "A yielding claim whose product falls on the operator and not on the patient, which is rare in the holding and is why it is entered as one. Nothing in the chapter says the announcing changes the course of the disease; the next sentence says the physician will be the better able to treat aright those who can be saved, and that is attributed to having anticipated everything, not to having said it aloud."@en,
        "Its failure condition is weak and the treatise supplies no term for it. A wrong announcement is not said to cost the physician anything, and chapter 25 widens the signs to cover diseases it does not name, so there is no case in which the announcing is said to have been inadmissible. That is the shape SPEC §3 records as pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate, and no protocol is entered here because none was carried."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:HippocratesPrologesis ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PrognosticonHippocratis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:HippocratesAnaitios .

pmc:HippocratesPueriOssaTenuiora a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Knochen des Kindes vereitert früher und das Kind stirbt früher"@de,
        "A child's bone suppurates sooner and the child dies sooner"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, On Injuries of the Head 18, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, p. 463" ;
    skos:definition "The eighteenth chapter states the claim as a graded comparison with no number in it: the bones of children are thinner and softer because they contain more blood, and are porous and spongy and neither dense nor hard; wounded to a similar or inferior degree by weapons of the same or inferior power, the bone of a young person more readily and quickly suppurates, and in accidents which are to prove fatal the younger person will die sooner than the elder. The operation is not withheld — blood is to be given issue by perforating with a small trepan — but with the greatest precautions, because the bone of young persons is thinner and more superficial."@en ;
    skos:note "A third variable that orders the duration without being given a number, beside the site and beside the season which does get one. The treatise is therefore consistent in its arithmetic: three things make the dying faster or slower, and only the calendar is counted."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeVulneribusCapitis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine .

pmc:HippocratesQuadragintaDies a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die gichtische Entzündung geht in vierzig Tagen zurück"@de,
        "The gouty inflammation subsides in forty days"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, Aphorisms VI.49, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. II, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849" ;
    skos:definition "Stated without condition and without a remedy attached: in gouty affections, the inflammation subsides in the course of forty days."@en ;
    skos:note "The same section says, two aphorisms later, that gouty affections for the most part rankle in spring and in autumn, which is a second claim about the same disease on a different clock. The two are not reconciled in the text and are not reconciled here."@en,
        "The second counted term in the section, and the cleaner of the two, because nothing is said to intervene and nothing is said to excuse a longer course. A claim of this form is settled by counting, and the aphorism offers no account of a case that runs past the fortieth day."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AphorismiHippocratis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine .

pmc:HippocratesSeptemDies a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Tod binnen sieben Tagen, wenn nicht Fieber hinzutritt"@de,
        "Death in seven days, unless fever come on"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, Aphorisms VI.51, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. II, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849" ;
    skos:definition "The one counted term in the section attached to a sudden event in a previously healthy person: persons in good health suddenly seized with pains in the head, and straightway laid down speechless and breathing with stertor, die in seven days — unless fever come on."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also the reason the silence at VI.18 counts. This corpus does state intervals, and when it does it states them with an exception; what it never does in this section is state one for a wound."@en,
        "The structure is the whole value of the node. A term is named, so a moment arrives at which the claim has either held or not; and a release is named in the same sentence, so the case in which the patient is alive on the eighth day is covered in advance if a fever appeared. The transmission put both halves in one line."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AphorismiHippocratis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine .

pmc:HippocratesSignaOmniTempore a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Zeichen werden für jedes Jahr, jede Jahreszeit und jede nicht genannte Krankheit beansprucht"@de,
        "The signs are claimed for every year, every season and any disease not named"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, The Book of Prognostics 25, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, p. 256; the Greek from Hippocrates, vol. II, ed. and trans. W. H. S. Jones, Loeb Classical Library 148, London 1923, pp. 54-55" ;
    skos:definition "The closing chapter extends the signs twice. In place: they are said to have held true in Libya, in Delos and in Scythia, from which it is said to be known that in the same regions many more things than these can be learned. In coverage: you should not complain because the name of any disease may happen not to be described here, for you may know all such as come to a crisis in the aforementioned times by the same symptoms."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because it is the sentence that removes the last way the treatise could have been caught out. A sign catalogue that names its diseases can be tested against a disease it fails on; one that covers the diseases it does not name cannot. The three places are the other half: they are named as verification and no observation from any of them is reported."@en,
        "The geographical claim is the only dating hook in the treatise, and what this run knows about the dispute over it is second hand. The research that preceded the run reports Littré, in the Avertissement to his second volume, giving Petersen's reading of Libya, Delos and Scythia as a travel record and holding the verification in those places at the time of writing to be very doubtful. Littré's volume was fetched in this run and measured for the state of its Greek, and the Avertissement was not read; Petersen was not opened at all. So the dispute is named here and entered nowhere, and the correction of this note is itself a finding of the run's own closing check."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PrognosticonHippocratis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine .

pmc:HippocratesSuturaeNonTrepanandae a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Niemals den Trepan auf die Nähte selbst"@de,
        "Never the trepan on the sutures themselves"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, On Injuries of the Head 12, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, p. 456" ;
    skos:definition "Stated at the end of the twelfth chapter, after a long account of why an injury at a suture is both worse and harder to detect than one elsewhere: many of these require trepanning, but you must not apply the trepan to the sutures themselves, but on the adjoining bone."@en ;
    skos:note "The prohibition is absolute in its wording and no consequence is given with it, which is why it is a caution and not a prerequisite of stated necessity. Adams reports that the rule is departed from twice in the sixth book of the Epidemics, and that Porralius gives three grounds for it; both were read in Adams's notes and neither work was opened, so neither is entered."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:HippocratesTrepanatio ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeVulneribusCapitis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine .

pmc:HippocratesTrepanatioTriduum a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Innerhalb der drei Tage, nicht danach"@de,
        "Within the three days, and not after them"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, On Injuries of the Head 14, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, p. 459" ;
    skos:definition "The window the fourteenth chapter sets on the operation: when you scrape the bone with the raspatory and it appears that the wound in the bone requires the operation, you must not postpone it for three days but do it during this period, more especially if the weather be hot, and you have had the management of the treatment from the commencement. The scraping that establishes the indication is itself placed on the day after the wound is laid open and tented."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity, and the reason is a decision about reading rather than caution. The chapter states no consequence for exceeding the three days. The nearest stated consequence in the same work is the term in chapter 19, and that one is attached to the injury never having been discovered at all, which is a different omission from an operation performed late. Reading chapter 19 as the consequence clause of chapter 14 would manufacture a stated necessity out of two places that do not refer to each other, and SPEC §3 keeps the scale apart for exactly that reason."@en,
        "The season appears here as an intensifier — more especially if the weather be hot — and not as a boundary, and the second condition is about the surgeon and not the patient: the rule is given for the case where he has had the management from the commencement. Chapter 21 makes the same distinction govern the whole technique."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:HippocratesTrepanatio ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeVulneribusCapitis ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:HippocratesOssisRadulatio ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine .

pmc:HippocratesTrepanumRefrigerandum a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Trepan wird herausgenommen und in kaltes Wasser getaucht"@de,
        "The trepan is taken out and dipped in cold water"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, On Injuries of the Head 21, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, p. 465" ;
    skos:definition "Stated with its consequence in the same sentence: in trepanning you must frequently remove the trepan on account of the heat in the bone and plunge it in cold water, for the trepan being heated by running round, and heating and drying the bone, burns it and makes a larger piece of bone around the sawing to drop off than would otherwise do. The chapter repeats the instruction for the case where the surgeon saws at once down to the membrane."@en ;
    skos:note "The one stated necessity in the treatise whose consequence is both specific and observable at the time: a larger piece of bone comes away. It is also the place where this corpus is at its most physical — the failure is a property of the tool running hot, not of the patient and not of the day."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The instrument burns the bone and a larger piece of bone around the sawing drops off than would otherwise do." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:HippocratesTrepanatio ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeVulneribusCapitis ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:HippocratesTrepanumRefrigeratio ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine .

pmc:HippocratesUvulaAlvusAperienda a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Vor dem Schnitt an der Uvula den Leib öffnen, wenn Zeit bleibt"@de,
        "Open the bowels before the uvula is cut, if there is time"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, The Book of Prognostics 23, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, p. 254" ;
    skos:definition "The order the chapter gives and the two conditions it attaches: it will be better to open the bowels gently before proceeding to the operation, if time will permit and the patient be not in danger of being suffocated. No consequence of omitting it is stated anywhere in the treatise."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at the weakest strength the scale carries a consequence for, and that is the finding about it. The chapter says better and not necessary, and it names two circumstances in which the step is to be dropped; a reading that made this a necessity would be lending ancient warrant to a sequence the source left conditional. SPEC §11 names that error directly."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:HippocratesUvulaeResectio ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PrognosticonHippocratis ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:HippocratesAlvusAperienda ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine .

pmc:HippocratesUvulaRubraNonSecanda a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Nicht, solange sie rot und groß ist"@de,
        "Not while it is red and large"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, The Book of Prognostics 23, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, p. 254" ;
    skos:definition "Stated with its consequence in the same sentence: it is very dangerous to cut off or scarify enlarged uvulae while they are red and large, for inflammations and haemorrhages supervene; one should try to reduce such swellings by some other means at this season. When the change named in the same chapter has occurred, it will be safe to operate."@en ;
    skos:note "A prohibition whose condition is a state of the part and not a date, which is the form the holding has least of. The stated consequence is specific enough to be recognised by a bystander, which is what distinguishes it from a caution that can never be seen to have been justified."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:HippocratesUvulaeResectio ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PrognosticonHippocratis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine .

pmc:HippocratesVenaeSectioVere a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Im Frühjahr zur Ader und abführen"@de,
        "Bleed and purge in spring"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, Aphorisms VI.47, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. II, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849" ;
    skos:definition "Persons who are benefited by venesection or purging should be bled or purged in spring. The season is stated as the time of the operation and no consequence is given for operating outside it."@en ;
    skos:note "A season without a stated penalty. The holding carries a number of claims that bind an operation to a calendar and the useful distinction among them is whether anything is said to follow from acting outside the window; here nothing is, and the node is entered at that strength rather than at a stronger one."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:HippocratesVenaeSectio ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AphorismiHippocratis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine .

pmc:HippocratesVulnusCapitisCulpaMedici a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der gezählte Tod folgt aus dem Versäumnis des Arztes und nicht aus der Wunde"@de,
        "The counted death follows from the surgeon's omission and not from the wound"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, On Injuries of the Head 19, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, pp. 463-464",
        "Hippocrates, On Injuries of the Head 5 and 10, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, pp. 449 and 452-453" ;
    skos:definition "The condition the nineteenth chapter puts on its own term, stated before the numbers: when a bone is broken or cleft or contused or otherwise injured, and when by mistake it has not been discovered, and neither the raspatory nor the trepan has been applied as required, but the case has been neglected as if the bone were sound. The seven and fourteen days are the terms of that neglect. The chapter then names the escape: if you perceive that fever is coming on and any of these symptoms accompany it, you must not put off, but having sawed the bone to the membrane or scraped it, you must apply the other treatment."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim is entered because of where it puts the failure. The treatise opens its prognostic chapter by saying the physician may form an opinion of the patient's approaching dissolution and foretell what is to happen — which is the announcing of the Prognostic — and the case it describes is one the physician produced by missing the injury. So the same corpus that gives the announcing as what makes the announcer blameless gives, in the other treatise, a counted death whose stated cause is the announcer's own mistake. Both halves are in the holding now and neither is a reading of the other."@en,
        "What would tell against this reading. The chapter can be read as describing an unrecognisable injury rather than a negligent surgeon: chapters 5 and 10 say at length that contusion and fine fissures cannot be recognised by sight and that the sounding probe does not show them. On that reading the mistake is excusable and the term is a property of the injury after all. The chapter's own words are by mistake it has not been discovered and neglected as if the bone were sound, which name an omission; the two readings are not reconciled here and both places are cited."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeVulneribusCapitis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine .

pmc:HippocratesVulnusCapitisLocus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Ort ordnet die Dauer des Sterbens, und bekommt keine Zahl"@de,
        "The site orders how long the dying takes, and is given no number"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, On Injuries of the Head 2, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, pp. 446-447" ;
    skos:definition "The second chapter grades the skull by site and states the consequence of each grade comparatively. At the bregma the bone is thinnest and the flesh over it least and the brain beneath it greatest, so that from a similar or even smaller wound the bone is more contused, fractured and depressed, injuries there are more deadly and more difficult to cure, and a man will die in a shorter space of time than from a wound in any other part of the head. Behind the vertex and the ear the bone is stronger and the flesh deeper, so that in a fatal accident the patient will live longer than when the wound is elsewhere, and pus takes longer to form and penetrate to the brain. The temple is named as the other weak place, for the jaw joint, the organ of hearing and a hollow and important vein. Not one of these gradations carries a day-count."@en ;
    skos:note "The gradation is contested in the surgical literature and the contest is reported through the translator, so no dispute node is entered. Adams records that the posterior part being less dangerous is at variance with the experience of certain modern authorities and sets Guthrie's experience beside it as consonant; the Latin commentators Arantius and Porralius are quoted against chapter 18 on children in the strongest terms, that none was ever saved on whom the section was used. Both were read in Adams's notes and neither work was opened."@en,
        "This is the node the convergence at pmc:TheIntervalAfterTheWound needed and it is not the node the earlier reading expected. The Greek corpus does couple the wounded site to the duration of the dying; what it does not do is express the duration as a number. It says longer and shorter and sooner and later, over a graded list of sites, and it stops there."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeVulneribusCapitis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine .

pmc:HippocratesVulnusCapitisSignaAequalia a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Zeichen gelten gleich, ob die Wunde alt oder frisch ist"@de,
        "The signs are declared the same whether the wound is old or recent"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, On Injuries of the Head 19, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, p. 464" ;
    skos:definition "After the sequence of signs that precede death in a neglected head wound — the wound losing its colour, the inflammation dying in it, the wound becoming glutinous and of a tawny and somewhat livid colour, the bone sphacelating and turning black and then pale, small blisters forming on the tongue, death in delirium, convulsions on the side opposite the wound, and in some an apoplexy — the chapter states: and these symptoms indicate, in the same manner, whether the wound be older or more recent."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered separately because it is an explicit invariance claim and the holding has few. It says that one variable which an observer would expect to matter does not, and it says it of the sign-set and not of the term. Together with the season clause it fixes what this treatise thinks the duration depends on: the time of year, and not the age of the wound and not the place of it."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeVulneribusCapitis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine .

pmc:HittAkkiskittari a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Anhaltendes Sterben im Land, von einer feindlichen Gottheit verursacht"@de,
        "Continual dying in the land caused by a hostile deity"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Uḫḫamuwa, CTH 410, HT 1 Vs. II 17-19 (critical edition, exemplar A, hethiter.net/: CTH 410, textus 2013-12-09)" ;
    skos:definition "The condition named in the incipit: if there is continual dying inside the land, and if some hostile deity has brought it about, the practitioner acts as follows."@en ;
    skos:note "Two conditions joined, and only the first is observable. That a hostile deity has caused the dying is a second condition in the grammar of the sentence and an assumption in the practice: nothing in the rite establishes it, and the whole procedure presupposes it. A transmission that makes an unverifiable second condition part of its entry has made its own non-application unprovable, since a rite that failed can always have been performed in a case where the second condition did not hold."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Uhhamuwa ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual .

pmc:HittHantitassuAgencySituating a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Worte des Ḫantitaššu-Ritus liegen außerhalb des Ausführenden"@de,
        "The words of the Ḫantitaššu rite are placed outside the officiant"@en ;
    skos:note "This is the one placement in the file that the source nearly states rather than describes, and it is worth marking as the easy case. The axis asks who acts according to the presentation of the source, and here the source denies that the speaker acts. A reader could still dispute it by holding that the disclaimer is a formula of modesty and that the officiant is the agent throughout; the placement is the compiler's and carries that exposure."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:cededAgency ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:HittNattaAmmelUddar ;
    pm:situationGround "The officiant states that the words he has just spoken are not his but those of the Sun-god and of Kamrušepa. The source therefore locates the agency of the operative speech with the deities named and does so in the text of the speech itself, which is as explicit as this axis gets."@en .

pmc:HittHantitassuCounts a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Zahlen, die der Ritus festsetzt und für die er nichts vorsieht"@de,
        "The counts the rite fixes and does not provide for"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Ḫantitaššu, CTH 395.1, KBo 11.14 Vs. II 19, Vs. II 27, Rs. III 17'-18', Rs. III 24', Rs. III 35', Rs. IV 15'-23' (critical edition, exemplar A, hethiter.net/: CTH 395.1, textus 2015-06-16)" ;
    skos:note "Five counts, all in the imperative or the indicative of prescription: the recitation three times, the incantation seven times, the libation three times in the pit and three times before each offering and once at the last, the enchanted things carried seven days, the whole concluded on the ninth. Not one of them has a clause attached for the case of the count being completed without effect, and the fourth column in which such a clause would have to stand is preserved to the end of the colophon in both exemplars. The first and second columns and the third have gaps, so the absence is established for the end of the rite and not for the whole tablet. This is the case that the protocol in examinations/the-count-that-does-not-fall-due.ttl was opened against."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:HittMuNininkantes ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hantitassu ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:HittHukmaiHantitassu ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual .

pmc:HittHarsiyalliUlMa a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Ist ein Vorratsgefäß vorhanden, und wenn nicht, ein Stein oder ein Bild"@de,
        "If a storage vessel is available, and if not, a stone or a statue"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Paškuwatti, CTH 406, KUB 7.8+KUB 9.27(+)KUB 7.5 Rs. IV 11-18 (critical edition, exemplar A, hethiter.net/: CTH 406, Expl. A, textus 2012-05-07)" ;
    skos:note "After the sign the man takes the deity as his own, and the installation is provided for in two cases: if a storage vessel is good for him he installs her in a storage vessel, and if not he installs her at a stone, or else makes a statue of her. The alternative is in the preserved text and the negation is not a restoration. And the new table that stood through the invocation becomes the deity's own."@en,
        "The provision is not about failure but about means, and it is entered because it shows the same hand as the last paragraph: wherever the rite could run into a case it cannot meet, this text names the case and supplies a way on. The pair of clauses, one for a missing vessel and one for a missing sign, stand eight lines apart."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:HittMugessarUliliyassi ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Paskuwatti ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:HittZashiyaTuekkissi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual .

pmc:HittHassatarNuGal a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Mann, der keine Nachkommenschaft hat oder einer Frau gegenüber kein Mann ist"@de,
        "A man who has no procreation or is not a man toward a woman"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Paškuwatti, CTH 406, KUB 7.8+KUB 9.27(+)KUB 7.5 Vs. I 1-5 (critical edition, exemplar A, hethiter.net/: CTH 406, Expl. A, textus 2012-05-07)" ;
    skos:altLabel "ḫaššatar NU.GÁL"@en ;
    skos:definition "The condition named in the incipit: if some man has no ḫaššatar, or is not a man toward a woman, the practitioner offers to Uliliyašši for him and invokes her for three days."@en ;
    skos:note "Two indications joined by or, and they are not the same thing: the absence of offspring is a fact about a household over time, and not being a man toward a woman is a fact about the man. The text puts them side by side without saying that the first is caused by the second."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Paskuwatti ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual .

pmc:HittHuddakUwar a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein früher Tod unter dem, was am Tor abgenommen wird"@de,
        "An early death among the things taken off at the gate"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Tunnawiya, CTH 409, KUB 7.53+KUB 12.58 Rs. III 39-42 (Goetze–Sturtevant, AOS 14, 1938, pp. 19-21)" ;
    skos:definition "The last item in the list the Old Woman has taken off the patient as he passes through the gate of ḫatalkeššar wood: evil uncleanness, sorcery, sin, the anger of the gods, curses, the gossip of the crowd, and an early death."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered separately because it is the one item in that list that is not a condition but an outcome, and it is the only place in the five works read where a rite claims to avert death as such. It is also the item that puts a date on nothing: a death averted is never observed, and the claim has no term after which it would have come true or failed."@en,
        "The same tablet asks in its closing addresses for life, good health and vigour, and for the patient to be covered like a fruit tree and to fill the house with grandchildren and great-grandchildren. So the rite claims both the removal of an early death and a long and fertile life, and neither claim names a moment at which it could be checked."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Tunnawiya ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual .

pmc:HittKisibApiyakkuDisputatum a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die einstigen Könige wiederkommen und das Siegel geöffnet wird, oder fortgehen und die Tafel niedergelegt wird"@de,
        "Whether the olden kings come back and the seal is opened, or depart and the tablet is laid down"@en ;
    dcterms:source "A. Goetze, Ritual against Domestic Quarrel, in J. B. Pritchard (ed.), Ancient Near Eastern Texts Relating to the Old Testament, 3rd ed., Princeton 1969, p. 351, on the same four lines",
        "F. Hrozný, Hethitische Keilschrifttexte aus Boghazköi. In Umschrift, mit Übersetzung und Kommentar, Boghazköi-Studien 3, Leipzig: J. C. Hinrichs 1919, p. 89, on KBo 2.3 Rs. IV 10-13 with his notes 7 to 9" ;
    skos:note "Four lines, and the two translations differ at every joint. The editio princeps of 1919 renders the kings as going away rather than returning, the sealed object as a stone tablet rather than a seal, the verb as laying down rather than opening, and the restrictive adverb as there rather than only then; its author asks in a footnote whether the paragraph is urging the Hittite kings to spread these prescriptions abroad. The 1969 translation renders the kings as returning and examining the state of the land, and the clause as a seal to be broken then and then only. The later reading is the one the subsequent literature follows, and the earlier one is not a careless reading but a different construal of a text whose vocabulary was then new."@en,
        "What the original wording settles and what it does not. The words for the kings, for the sealing of the horn in the preceding paragraph and for the restrictive particle are read the same way by both; the verb of motion, the designation of the sealed thing and the verb of the last line are what the two construe differently. A third treatment that would decide between them is in print."@en,
        "Why it is entered rather than settled by preferring the later translation. What this holding wants from the clause is whether the transmission set the undoing of its own rite beyond reach, and on the 1919 construal it did no such thing — the paragraph would then be a direction about the tablet and not about the rite's reversal. The claim at pmc:HittKisibApiyakku therefore rests on a reading, and a reader who takes the other one is entitled to strike it. That is a stronger statement of the holding's position than a node that reports only the received translation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:HittKisibApiyakku ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Mastigga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual .

pmc:HittKolophonHantitassuDisputatum a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob Ḫantitaššu eine Frau oder ein Mann war"@de,
        "Whether Ḫantitaššu was a woman or a man"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Ḫantitaššu, CTH 395.1, KBo 11.14 Rs. IV 24, colophon (critical edition, exemplar A, hethiter.net/: CTH 395.1, textus 2015-06-16)",
        "Ritual of Ḫantitaššu, CTH 395.1, KUB 43.57+KBo 18.174 Rs. IV 23, colophon (critical edition, exemplar B, hethiter.net/: CTH 395.1, textus 2015-06-16)" ;
    skos:note "Not decided here, and the direction of any decision would matter. A scribe who wrote the female determinative and then the masculine classifier made a slip in one of the two, and which one is the slip is the question; no reachable treatment of the problem was found, and the two editions that discuss this ritual are in print."@en,
        "Two colophons of one ritual, and they do not agree. The general version writes the determinative that marks a female personal name before the name and then the sign for man after it, so that the contradiction stands inside a single line of a single manuscript; the version made for the king writes the female determinative and then the sign for woman. The editors of the critical edition state the divergence in their remarks on the exemplars."@en,
        "Why this is more than a scribal curiosity for this holding. The registration of this tradition says its rituals are transmitted under the names of the practitioners who dictated them, most of them women, and that clause is the only general statement the graph makes about the corpus. It is supported by four of the five works read and contradicted, in one manuscript, for the fifth. The dispute is therefore about the quantity the registration asserts and not only about one person."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:HittMuNininkantes ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hantitassu ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual .

pmc:HittMastiggaYielding a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der Ritus soll die Worte des Streits vom Paar abschneiden"@de,
        "The rite is said to cut off the words of the quarrel from the pair"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Maštigga ritual against domestic quarrel, CTH 404.1.II, KBo 2.3+ Vs. I 1-4 with Vs. I 26-37 (critical edition, exemplar A, hethiter.net/: CTH 404.1.II, Expl. A, textus 2012-05-07)" ;
    skos:note "What the rite is said to do is stated in the operative speeches and not in a summary: let the tongues of these days be cut off, let the words of these days be cut off; be clean henceforth of mouth and tongue. The claim is about the words that were said and not about the disposition of the two persons, and nothing in the tablet says that they will not quarrel again."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:HittTaksanTittanumar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Mastigga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:HittHalluwai .

pmc:HittNakkussi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Sündenbock, der nicht getötet wird und der Alten Frau zufällt"@de,
        "The scapegoat that is not killed and goes to the Old Woman"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Maštigga ritual against domestic quarrel, CTH 404.1.II, KBo 2.3+ Vs. II 47'-53' with Rs. III 25 (critical edition, exemplar A, hethiter.net/: CTH 404.1.II, Expl. A, textus 2012-05-07)" ;
    skos:altLabel "nakkušši-"@en ;
    skos:definition "A further sheep is driven up and called the scapegoat; the Old Woman presents it to the Sun-god as a substitute in the pair's place, with mouth and tongue, breaks a sweet loaf and pours a libation of wine; and this sheep they do not kill, but the Old Woman herself takes it."@en ;
    skos:note "The practitioner's fee stands inside the operative text and is paid in the animal that carries the evil. It is the only one of the four animals of this rite that survives: the white sheep is buried, the black sheep is burnt, the piglet is buried, and the scapegoat is given to the Sun-god in word and to the operator in fact. The same tablet gives her the pair's fine garments a column later."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Mastigga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual .

pmc:HittPaprahtaTriplex a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob vor den Göttern, vor den Toten oder vor den Menschen"@de,
        "Whether before the gods, before the dead, or before mankind"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Tunnawiya, CTH 409, KUB 7.53+KUB 12.58, Vs. II, paragraphs 18 and 19 of the edition (Goetze–Sturtevant, AOS 14, 1938, pp. 12-15)" ;
    skos:definition "The disjunction with which the Old Woman covers the setting in which the uncleanness may have been incurred: whoever made this person dark and stiff and made him or her unclean — whether anyone made him or her unclean before the gods, or before the dead, or before mankind — for that person I am here performing the ritual of uncleanness."@en ;
    skos:note "Found on a reading of the middle of this tablet that the run's first pass had skipped, and the correction is recorded in the pull request rather than only here. The locus is given by paragraph and page because the optical transcription of this copy does not carry the column and line numbers of these paragraphs reliably; the paragraph division and the page are in the printed edition."@en,
        "This is a third candidate for the device at pmc:TheProvisionForNotKnowing, found in the same corpus as the second and in a work read in the same run, and it is the closest of the three in form to the Ugaritic one at pmc:UgarHta: a triple disjunction inside the operative speech. It is not entered as a witness of that claim, for the reason recorded at pmp:HittProvisionSearch — the independence of this tradition from the Mesopotamian witness already standing there is the open question of its own contact route."@en,
        "Three settings named at once, and between them they exhaust the places where a Hittite could have incurred anything: the gods, the dead, the living. The agent is covered by the same construction and is likewise not named. So the rite needs neither the culprit nor the occasion, and it says so in the operative formula rather than leaving the question open."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Tunnawiya ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual .

pmc:HittPapratar a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Unreinheit als Zustand, in den ein Mensch gesetzt ist"@de,
        "Uncleanness as a condition a person is placed in"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Tunnawiya, CTH 409, KUB 7.53+KUB 12.58 Vs. I 1-6 (A. Goetze with E. H. Sturtevant, The Hittite Ritual of Tunnawi, American Oriental Series 14, New Haven 1938, pp. 4-5)" ;
    skos:altLabel "paprannaš"@en,
        "papratar"@en ;
    skos:definition "The condition the ritual of the river is performed against: a person, man or woman, is placed in some uncleanness, or someone else has named him or her for uncleanness, or a woman's children keep dying, or they are born before term, or the sexual parts of a man or a woman are disabled in consequence of a formula of uncleanness."@en ;
    skos:note "Five indications in one conditional sentence, and they are of two kinds. Three are states a second person could observe: children that keep dying, children born before term, parts of the body disabled. The other two are not. That a person is placed in uncleanness is the diagnosis, not the sign of it; and that someone else has named him for uncleanness makes a third party's accusation an indication in its own right, with nothing in the text by which the accusation could be checked. A transmission whose entry conditions include an accusation can be entered without a case ever having been observed, and the text supplies no step that would exclude it."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Tunnawiya ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual .

pmc:HittPaskuwattiAgencySituating a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Der Ausgang des Paškuwatti-Ritus liegt bei der Gottheit und nicht beim Mann"@de,
        "The outcome of the Paškuwatti rite is placed with the deity and not with the man"@en ;
    skos:note "The ceded agency sits oddly beside pmc:HittSaklaiLutar, where the practitioner says in the first person and in the perfect that she has taken womanhood away and given manhood back. That assertion is the practitioner's own act and would be placed at own agency if it were placed separately. Two placements for one rite would be the honest result and the second is not entered, because what is being placed here is the yielding claim and not the gate ceremony."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:cededAgency ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:HittPaskuwattiYielding ;
    pm:situationGround "The invocation asks the deity to come down to the man, to be a wife to him for a child, to assign him her handmaid and to let him take his wife; and the sign of success is an act of the deity in the dream and not an act of the man. The man's own part is to bathe, to walk behind the rations, to pass under the gate, to lie down and to report. The source thus assigns the operative act to the deity throughout."@en .

pmc:HittPaskuwattiSleepSituating a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Das Zeichen des Paškuwatti-Ritus wird an der Schlafschwelle genommen"@de,
        "The sign of the Paškuwatti rite is taken at the sleep threshold"@en ;
    skos:note "The placement is the compiler's and the source names no coordinate. It is disputable without touching the sign itself: a reader who held that a dream in which a deity is present should sit at dreaming-within rather than at the sleep threshold would be disputing this node and not pmc:HittZashiyaTuekkissi. The ground for the threshold rather than the dream state is that what the rite arranges is the lying down and the collecting of the report, and both are at the edges of the sleep and not inside it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:sleepThreshold ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:HittZashiyaTuekkissi ;
    pm:situationGround "The tablet prescribes that a bed be made for the ritual patron in front of the table with the garment that lay on the rations spread out, that he lie down and sleep, and that the sign be whatever he sees in the dream and reports on waking. The decisive state is therefore sleep, and what is wanted from it is a dream; the sign is not taken in any waking condition and is not taken in absorption."@en .

pmc:HittRiverBankAnnouncement a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Dem Genius des Flussufers wird angesagt, bevor der Lehm genommen wird"@de,
        "The genius of the river bank is told before the clay is taken"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Tunnawiya, CTH 409, KUB 7.53+KUB 12.58 Vs. I 24-33 (Goetze–Sturtevant, AOS 14, 1938, pp. 5-7)" ;
    skos:note "The Old Woman goes to the river bank at nightfall for the express purpose of announcing the rite, breaks bread, pours wine and asks the genius to take the clay into its hand and cleanse the patient with it, and only then takes the clay. The material of the rite is thus obtained by request and not simply dug; no consequence of taking it without the request is stated."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:HittSiskurPaprannas ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Tunnawiya ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:HittRiverBankGenius ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual .

pmc:HittSahturUlNepis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Was das Ferkel nicht wiedersehen wird"@de,
        "What the piglet will not see again"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Maštigga ritual against domestic quarrel, CTH 404.1.II, KBo 2.3+ Vs. I 56-Vs. II 2, with Vs. II 7-13 (critical edition, exemplar A, hethiter.net/: CTH 404.1.II, Expl. A, textus 2012-05-07)" ;
    skos:definition "The ground the Old Woman states for the piglet's efficacy: it has been fattened with grass and grain, and it will not see the sky again and will not see the other piglets again; so let the evil curses likewise not come to the two ritual patrons."@en ;
    skos:note "The analogy runs on a fact about the animal that the operator is about to make true, and the certainty of the premise is the whole of the argument. The same structure recurs twice in this tablet: the clay does not return to the clay pit and the cumin does not turn white and cannot be used for seed a second time. In each case what guarantees the claim is an irreversibility that the operator produces."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Mastigga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual .

pmc:HittTarpalli a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Tier als Ersatz für Köpfe und Körper des Paares"@de,
        "The animal that stands as substitute for the heads and bodies of the pair"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Maštigga ritual against domestic quarrel, CTH 404.1.II, KBo 2.3+ Vs. I 47-55 (critical edition, exemplar A, hethiter.net/: CTH 404.1.II, Expl. A, textus 2012-05-07)" ;
    skos:altLabel "tarpalliš"@en ;
    skos:definition "The black sheep the Old Woman lifts over the two quarrellers and declares to be the substitute of their heads and of their whole bodies, with the curses in its mouth and on its tongue; the pair then spit into its mouth, and it is cut up, dismembered and burnt on the hearth with honey and oil poured over it."@en ;
    skos:note "The transfer is made by an act of the patients and not of the operator: the spitting is theirs. Three animals carry it in sequence in this version — a white sheep, then the black sheep, then a piglet — and the first two are both named substitute, so the substitution is not a single act but a graded series."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Mastigga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual .

pmc:HittTentSitePresupposed a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Zelt steht, wo kein Anbau ist und der Pflug nicht hinkommt"@de,
        "The tent stands where there is no cultivation and the plough does not come"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Tunnawiya, CTH 409, KUB 7.53+KUB 12.58 Vs. I 39-42 (Goetze–Sturtevant, AOS 14, 1938, pp. 7-9)" ;
    skos:note "The text puts the condition as a question and answers it: where do they make the tent — where there is no cultivation near by and the plough does not come, there the tent is made. It states no consequence of making it elsewhere, so the strength is prescribed order and not stated necessity. What the condition is about is not said either; the rite handles uncleanness by moving it into clay, animals and figures that are afterwards disposed of, and an uncultivated place is where such things can be left."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:HittSiskurPaprannas ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Tunnawiya ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:HittTentSite ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual .

pmc:HittTunnawiyaYielding a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Flussritual soll die zwölf Teile reinigen und die Fortpflanzung wiederherstellen"@de,
        "The ritual of the river is said to purify the twelve parts and restore procreation"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Tunnawiya, CTH 409, KUB 7.53+KUB 12.58 Vs. I 1-9 with Rs. IV 34-38 (Goetze–Sturtevant, AOS 14, 1938, pp. 4-5, 25)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:HittSiskurPaprannas ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Tunnawiya ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:HittParkunuwarUzuUr .

pmc:HittUhhamuwaYielding a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der bekränzte Widder soll die seuchenbringende Gottheit in Gnade zurückwenden"@de,
        "The crowned ram is said to turn the plague-bringing deity back in favour"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Uḫḫamuwa, CTH 410, HT 1 Vs. II 24-33 (critical edition, exemplar A, hethiter.net/: CTH 410, textus 2013-12-09)" ;
    skos:note "The claim is checkable in principle and in a way most of this holding is not: an epidemic either abates or does not. What makes it unexaminable in fact is the second entry condition at pmc:HittAkkiskittari and the unnamed addressee at pmc:HittKuisDingirKurLukur, and neither is an accident of this tablet: the first says the rite applies only where a hostile deity caused the deaths, and the second says nobody need know which."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:HittUdukranzaPenniyawar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Uhhamuwa ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:HittKuisDingirKurLukur .

pmc:HittUlAraEnSiskur a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Es ist nicht recht, dass der Ritualherr ihn selbst ausführt"@de,
        "It is not right for the ritual patron to perform it himself"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Ḫantitaššu, second version, CTH 395.2.1, KUB 57.79 Rs. IV 40-42 (critical edition, hethiter.net/: CTH 395.2.1, textus 2012)" ;
    skos:note "The clause in the second version is in two parts: the lord of the word performs this rite in another house, and for the ritual patron to perform it is not ārā. The Hittite term is the standing one for what is permitted or in order, and its negation is the corpus's own way of marking a transgression without naming a penalty. No consequence is stated here, which is why this is a caution and not a prerequisite with a stated necessity."@en,
        "The first version says the same thing from the other side and adds a second restriction: the lord of the word performs this rite in another's house, by himself alone; he does not perform it in the palace; and a clause follows about the king which the reading does not settle. The verb of that clause is an iterative that could be of to step or of to put, and without a translation beside the original the two cannot be decided between. What the line contains, in both exemplars of the first version, is the king, the negation and that verb; the construal is left open here deliberately."@en,
        "The restriction is sharpened by what the editors say about the manuscripts: exemplar B of the first version is the copy made for the king, and it is the one whose fourth column names the king as the one who prostrates, who takes the ox-gut and who has carried the enchanted things for seven days. So the royal copy makes the king the ritual patron and still carries the clause that the rite is not performed in the palace."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:HittHukmaiHantitassu ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hantitassu ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual .

pmc:HorripilationCorrespondence a pm:Converging ;
    rdfs:label "Die Entsprechung der Haaraufrichtung"@de,
        "The horripilation correspondence"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.94ff. (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Nāṭyaśāstra VII" ;
    skos:definition "The observation that the raising of the body hair is codified as a sign of an inner state in two Indian genres that have no common subject matter: a meditation handbook and a treatise on dramatic performance."@en ;
    skos:note "The strongest item in this file, and still not what it first appears. The independence is generic and not cultural: both texts belong to the Indian sphere, so a common background cannot be excluded. What can be excluded is that one borrowed the observation from the other, since neither is about the other's subject. A universal physiology codified twice is a finding about the physiology."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:convergingConcept pmc:KhuddikaPiti,
        pmc:Romanca ;
    pm:independenceGround "Here the ground is not a date and the node says so rather than borrowing an argument it does not have. The commentary is of the fifth century of the common era and the treatise on performance is older, so priority runs the wrong way for an argument from age. What carries the independence is that neither text treats the other's subject: a handbook of meditation had no occasion to take an observation about gooseflesh from a manual for actors, or the reverse. Both are received texts, so no gathering situation is at issue. A common Indian background remains available and is not excluded by this ground." ;
    pm:independentAttestation pmt:SanskritDramaturgy,
        pmt:Theravada ;
    pm:transmissionPath "Both codifications arise within the Indian cultural sphere; independence holds at the level of genre and subject matter, not at the level of culture." .

pmc:HowManyTheGreatMysteriousAre a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Wie viele die Große Geheimnisvolle sind, und ob die Sonne der Große Geist ist"@de,
        "How many the Great Mysterious are, and whether the Sun is the Great Spirit"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 153",
        "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), pp. 154-155" ;
    skos:definition "Two informants in the same volume give the count differently. The one whose written Lakota underlies the doctrinal texts gives sixteen names in four groups of four and says that the term of the shamans differs from the common term only in considering all the benevolent gods, four of each of four classes, as one whole. The other, questioned directly, names eight, then says there is but one, then reduces the eight to four by pairing them, saying that the Sun and the Moon are the same, the Sky and the Wind the same, the Rock and the Winged the same, and the Earth and the Beautiful Woman the same. On a second point the same informant contradicts the people rather than the other informant: many of the people hold that the Sun and the Moving are one and the same, and he says that the shamans know it is not so, because the Sun is in the sky only half the time and the Moving is there all the time."@en ;
    skos:note "The argument the second informant gives for his denial is worth keeping separate from the denial: it is an argument from the observed availability of the two, not from authority or from lineage. That form is rare in this corpus."@en,
        "The dispute is entered because the graph would otherwise hold the scheme of the first informant as the doctrine of the transmission, which the second informant does not confirm and the compiler himself declines to claim. It is a disagreement between two witnesses of the same transmission and not between two traditions, which is the commoner and less visible kind."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:WalkerSystematizationOfTheGods .

pmc:HuacaAsAddressedPower a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Huaca als angesprochene Macht"@de,
        "The huaca as a power that is addressed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. II" ;
    skos:definition "What is addressed falls into two kinds. The fixed, which cannot be taken away because they do not move: the sun, the moon, the Pleiades, the lightning, the sea, the earth, springs, rivers, snow peaks, high hills and certain very large stones, and the pacarina, the place each lineage says it came out of. And the movable, which are the ordinary huacas of a place: mostly stone, mostly without a figure, each with its own name, each belonging to a lineage, each with its own priests. Beside them stand the malquis, the bones or whole bodies of the ancestors, held to be children of the huacas and given the same offerings; and the conopas, held in each house and inherited by the eldest son, which are the same worship made private."@en ;
    skos:note "The division is the recorder's and it is his working distinction as an extirpator: the movable ones could be seized and burned and the fixed ones had to be taken out of the heart instead. It is entered because it is also a real division in the practice — a lineage huaca is public and a conopa is secret — and not because his purpose was neutral."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmc:HulaRetentionPresupposesKeptVows a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Behalten der Gesänge setzt das Halten der Gelübde voraus"@de,
        "Keeping the songs presupposes keeping the vows"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Nathaniel B. Emerson, Unwritten Literature of Hawaii: The Sacred Songs of the Hula, Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 38 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1909), chapter V, p. 34" ;
    skos:note "A memory sanction is a rare shape and it is nearly self-confirming, which is worth marking here rather than discovering later. A pupil who forgets is by that fact convicted of having been heedless, and a pupil who remembers proves the rule; no outcome is excluded. The claim is therefore weaker than it sounds even though the consequence is quotable."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The pupils who are heedless of their vows are told that the songs they have learned will fly away. The sanction is a loss of memory and not a punishment laid on by anyone; it is stated as what follows." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:HulaKnowledgeRetained ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:HalauConduct ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HawaiianRitual .

pmc:HundredArtsPresupposeYubu a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die hundert Künste setzen den Schritt des Yu voraus"@de,
        "The hundred arts presuppose the pace of Yu"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 17 (Dengshe), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 17" ;
    skos:note "Entered at prescribed order and not higher because the chapter says one ought to know it and states no consequence for not knowing it. The dependent step is named as the operation this chapter is about; the text's own claim is wider and covers the hundred arts, which the graph cannot express as a single edge and does not try to."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:MingjingRushan ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BaopuziNeipian ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:Yubu ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:IddhiAdhigamaVivada a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Erlangungen, die das Handbuch lehrt, noch zu haben sind"@de,
        "Whether the attainments the handbook teaches are still to be had"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Anāgatavaṃsa, recension B of Minayeff's edition, Journal of the Pali Text Society 1886, pp. 33-34, in the English of Henry Clarke Warren, Buddhism in Translations, Harvard University Press 1896, §102, pp. 482-483",
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.1-12 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, pp. 432-436; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "Two works of one transmission, read on the same day, say incompatible things about the availability of the same attainments. The handbook sets out the whole course for a meditator in the present tense, names the seven thresholds at which most fall away and the one class exempt from them, and nowhere says that the course has lapsed or that its subject belongs to a closed age; a search of its twenty-three chapters for any such statement came back with nothing. The work on what is to come sets a term: a thousand years for the analytical sciences, then a descending series of lesser fruits, then nothing, with the trances named among the things the disciples will become unable to realise."@en ;
    skos:note "Neither work names the other and neither is answering the other; this is not a controversy the transmission conducted. What it is, and the reason it is entered as a dispute rather than as two notes, is that the two statements cannot both be acted on. A reader who takes the handbook as instruction and the other work as chronology has to decide which of them applies to him, and the transmission gives him nothing with which to decide it."@en,
        "The asymmetry is worth stating because it decides where a reader should look next. A statement that the attainments have lapsed can be refuted by one attested case after the term; the handbook's silence cannot be refuted at all, because it is a silence. The search that established it stands at pmp:VisuddhimaggaDeclineSearch, and what it exhibits is a gap: in twenty-three chapters of instruction there is no sentence saying that any of it has become unavailable."@en,
        "The step from the one to the other is an inference and is named as one here. The work on what is to come does not mention the supernormal powers. It names the trances, and the handbook makes the fourth trance the base of every operation of its twelfth chapter and the fourteenfold mastery the condition of beginning; if the trances lapse, the operations built on them lapse with them. That much follows. What does not follow is any date for the powers themselves, and none is claimed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:disputedClaim "That a person now living who completes the training the handbook prescribes can reach the states it prescribes it for." ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Anagatavamsa,
        pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:IddhiIsExercisedInWithdrawnAbsorption a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Die übernatürlichen Kräfte werden in abgezogener Vertiefung ausgeübt"@de,
        "The supernormal powers are exercised in withdrawn absorption"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.1 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.49 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:withdrawnAbsorption ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:Iddhividha ;
    pm:situationGround "The handbook assigns the powers to a meditator who has reached the fourth absorption and calls that absorption their natural plane. The absorption is one of the four described as awake and turned from the senses to a single object, which is the criterion this coordinate names. The placement is of the exercise, not of the training that leads to it."@en .

pmc:IddhiOccultanda a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die zwei Anweisungen, nicht entdeckt zu werden"@de,
        "The two instructions for not being found out"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.101 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, p. 460; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.68 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, p. 452; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "The chapter twice tells the practitioner how to keep the operation from becoming known. He is to look with the divine eye and descend where the populace will not see him. And, among the reasons for returning from many forms to one, it gives this: out of fewness of wishes he reflects that there are only a few monks in the monastery, that arriving guests would ask where so many monks resembling each other had come from, and would thus find him out; and for that reason he resolves to be one again within a fixed time."@en ;
    skos:note "Both instructions are practical and neither is a moral warning; the chapter nowhere repeats the rule of the order against display. They are entered as a caution because that is what they operate as: the thing to be guarded against is the observation of the operation by anyone who is not the operator, which is the same property that pmc:TassaYevaSaPathavi builds into the operation itself."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:AkasenaGamana,
        pmc:EkoHutvaBahu ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:IddhiPannayaPalibodha a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Kräfte sind ein Hemmnis für die Einsicht und werden vom Geringsten gebrochen"@de,
        "The powers are an impediment for insight and are broken by the slightest thing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga III.56, Pali PTS [97] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, p. 113; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "The tenth impediment. The supernormal powers are those of an average man; they are hard to manage, like a child lying on its back or like tender corn, and the slightest thing breaks them. They are an impediment for insight and not for concentration, since they are obtained when concentration is obtained. Therefore one who seeks insight is to cut off the impediment of the powers, and another man only the remaining nine."@en ;
    skos:note "This is a self-warning of an unusual kind: the handbook tells the aspirant, in its third chapter, to cut off the thing to which it devotes its twelfth and thirteenth. It does not withdraw the instruction later and it does not reconcile the two places. The ground it gives here is also the ground on which the eleventh chapter counts the powers among the five benefits of developing concentration — that they are obtained through concentration — so the same fact is made the reason for pursuing them and the reason for cutting them off."@en,
        "What it warns against is not the exercise of the powers but the keeping of them: they are said to break at the slightest thing, and the two similes are of things that cannot be set down and left. The display of the powers is refused elsewhere and on another ground, and that stands at pmc:DisplayOfPowersRefused; the two cautions do not overlap."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:Iddhividha ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:IddhiPresupposesFourteenfoldMastery a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Übernatürliche Kraft setzt die vierzehnfache Beherrschung voraus"@de,
        "Supernormal power presupposes the fourteenfold mastery"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.8 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The handbook states that it is not possible for a meditator even to begin to accomplish transformation by supernormal power without having first completed the development of control of the mind in those fourteen ways." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Iddhividha ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:FourteenfoldMastery ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:IddhiPresupposesFourthJhana a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Übernatürliche Kraft setzt die vierte Vertiefung voraus"@de,
        "Supernormal power presupposes the fourth absorption"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.1 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.12-13 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:note "The handbook assigns the task to a meditator who has reached the fourth absorption and calls that absorption the natural plane for obtaining the power. It does not, at these places, say what happens to one who attempts it from elsewhere."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Iddhividha ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:FourthJhana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:IfaGeomanticIdentificationDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die sechzehn Figuren der Apparat der Geomantie sind"@de,
        "Whether the sixteen figures are the apparatus of geomancy"@en ;
    dcterms:source "A. B. Ellis, The Yoruba-Speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1894), chapter II, the section on Ifa",
        "J. A. Abayomi Cole, Astrological Geomancy in Africa, the account of the sixteen palm nuts, read in R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), appendix" ;
    skos:definition "A disagreement about what the sixteen figures of the oracle are. One account has them as the base figures of the system itself, called mothers, from whose columns every further figure is compounded, and gives the practice no derivation from outside Yorubaland. The other, written by a Sierra Leonean author and reprinted in the same appendix as the bishop's extract, counts the sixteen as twelve houses of the heavens, two witnesses, one judge and a further judge got by combining the judge with the figure of the first house, and calls the operations of the diviners geomantic throughout."@en ;
    skos:note "Neither side of this was read in a modern study at the wording in this run. What the twentieth-century literature makes of the question — whether the figures were taken over and grafted on an older practice, whether the sand science itself has North African roots, and how either is dated — is untouched here and is the first thing the next run on this tradition should open."@en,
        "The second account is the reason pm:contactRoute on this tradition stands at disputed. Twelve houses, two witnesses and a judge are the working parts of the Arabic sand science and of the European geomancy descended from it, and a system that has them is not without a route to the Mediterranean and Islamic material this graph already holds. The first account describes the same casting and the same sixteen without any of those parts."@en,
        "The second account was read at second hand, in the appendix that reprints it, and carries no imprint there. It is entered as a side of a dispute and not as a finding about the history of the system."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:IfaSixteenMothers .

pmc:IfaMemorisationStandardUnmet a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der als unerreicht berichtete Gedächtnismaßstab"@de,
        "The memorisation standard reported as unattained"@en ;
    dcterms:source "James Johnson, Yoruba Heathenism, the account of the stories attached to the Odu" ;
    skos:definition "The same extract that states the standard states that it is not reached: scarcely anyone has been found who has learnt the whole of the narratives and can recite them."@en ;
    skos:note "The node exists because the shortfall stands in the same breath as the demand. A record that carried the demand alone would report a feat of memory that the tradition does not claim is performed, and this is one of the few places where a transmission states the distance between its own rule and its practitioners."@en,
        "This is the point at which the tradition touches the exactness material already in the graph, and it touches it from the other side: there the rule is enforced, here the rule is conceded to be unfulfilled. No convergence node is written for it. The contact route of this tradition stands at disputed, so an agreement counts as neither reception nor independent attestation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), appendix, extract from Yoruba Heathenism" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ifa .

pmc:IfaOduEnumeration a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Zählung der Zeichen"@de,
        "The enumeration of the signs"@en ;
    dcterms:source "James Johnson, Yoruba Heathenism, the account of the Odu and their number" ;
    skos:definition "The count of the signs as the bishop's extract gives it: behind each of the sixteen representative palm nuts stand sixteen subordinate powers, and each of the whole number is itself called an Odu, which is to say a head; this gives two hundred and fifty-six. A further sixteen are counted behind each of those, which gives four thousand and ninety-six."@en ;
    skos:note "The two accounts read in this run do not generate the count the same way. The account of eighteen ninety-four compounds the further figures from the columns of two of the sixteen; the extract used here subordinates sixteen to each of the sixteen. Both arrive at a system built on sixteen, and neither states the other's derivation. The difference is recorded rather than smoothed, because which derivation a source gives is one of the things that would distinguish a locally grown scheme from a borrowed one."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), appendix, extract from Yoruba Heathenism" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ifa .

pmc:IfaRuleOfInterpretationClosed a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Deutungsregel ist fest und verschlossen"@de,
        "The rule of interpretation is fixed and closed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "A. B. Ellis, The Yoruba-Speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1894), chapter II, the section on Ifa" ;
    skos:definition "The reading of a figure is reported to follow an established rule, and the rule is reported to be known only to those initiated into it. The fee paid to a priest for being taught the art is reported as very heavy."@en ;
    skos:note "Two gates named in one sentence, and they are of different kinds. That the rule is fixed is a claim about the system and is checkable against any later account of it; that it is known only to the initiated is a claim about access, and the fee gives its price. The account is an outsider's and reports what he was not told, which is a weaker witness for the content of the rule than for the fact of its closure."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ifa .

pmc:IfaVerseCorpus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die den Zeichen anhängenden Erzählungen"@de,
        "The narratives attached to the signs"@en ;
    dcterms:source "James Johnson, Yoruba Heathenism, the account of the stories attached to the Odu" ;
    skos:definition "The body of narratives through which a sign delivers its answer, given in parables. One thousand six hundred and eighty are counted to each Odu, and everyone aspiring to the office of babalawo is expected to commit them, together with those of the other signs, to memory."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), appendix, extract from Yoruba Heathenism" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ifa .

pmc:ImbasForosnaiYieldsFaillsiugud a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Verfahren bringt die Offenbarung des Gesuchten"@de,
        "The procedure brings the revelation of what is sought"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Sanas Cormaic s.v. Imbas forosnai, in Stokes, Three Irish Glossaries (London 1862), p. 25, and in Cormac's Glossary, trans. O'Donovan (Calcutta 1868), pp. 94-95" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ImbasForosnai ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InsularCeltic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ImbasForosnaiFaillsiugud .

pmc:ImbasPresupposesIdbart a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Opfer geht dem Schlaf voraus"@de,
        "The offering comes before the sleep"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Sanas Cormaic s.v. Imbas forosnai, in Stokes, Three Irish Glossaries (London 1862), p. 25" ;
    skos:note "Prescribed order and not stated necessity. The entry sets the steps in a fixed sequence and says outright that the procedure is done in this way; it states nothing about what befalls a poet who omits the offering or takes the steps out of order. The one place in this material where an omission carries a stated consequence is the prohibition, and there the consequence attaches to doing the thing and not to doing it wrong."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ImbasForosnaiFaillsiugud ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ImbasForosnai ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InsularCeltic .

pmc:ImbasVidhiDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Drei Beschreibungen, wie das benannte Verfahren ausgeführt wird"@de,
        "Three descriptions of how the named procedure is performed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Prologue to the Senchas Már, in Ancient Laws of Ireland, vol. I (Dublin: HMSO, 1865), pp. 44-45",
        "Sanas Cormaic s.v. Imbas forosnai, in Stokes, Three Irish Glossaries (London 1862), p. 25",
        "Senchas Már in the codex H. 3. 18, printed and translated by Kuno Meyer, Finn and the Man in the Tree, Revue Celtique 25 (1904), pp. 344-347" ;
    skos:definition "The transmitted literature describes the operation under this name in three ways that do not reduce to one another. The glossary has the poet chew raw flesh, lay it behind the door, offer it, sleep with his palms on his cheeks and be watched. The prologue to the Senchas Már says that before Patrick's time the poet placed his staff upon the person's body or upon his head and found out his name and the name of his father and mother and discovered every unknown thing proposed to him, and that this is teinm laegha or imus forosna, the same thing being revealed by means of them, but performed in different ways, a different kind of offering being made at each. The version of the Senchas Már in the vellum codex H. 3. 18 gives a narrative as an example of the practice called imbas forosnai in which Finn puts his thumb into his mouth and, on taking it out, his imbas illumines him and he chants."@en ;
    skos:note "The third description is the one a reader is most likely to miss, because it is not in a glossary or a tale but in a legal manuscript, printed by its editor with the remark that it is given there as an example of the practice of incantation called imbas forosnai and has not been preserved elsewhere."@en,
        "This is not a dispute between a source and a modern reader. All three descriptions are in the transmitted material, two of them in the same law book, and the law prologue itself acknowledges that the two named operations were performed after a different manner without saying which manner belongs to which. A holding that entered one of the three as the procedure would be choosing among its own witnesses."@en,
        "What the divergence costs and what it does not. It does not touch the term, the watching or the prohibition, which stand in the glossary alone and are not contradicted anywhere. It touches exactly the part a person would have to do, which is the part that would have to be fixed before the operation could be tried."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmp:ImbasForosnai ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InsularCeltic .

pmc:ImmediateReturnProtocol a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Rückkehr sich von der Hinreise unterscheidet"@de,
        "Whether the return differs from the outward journey"@en ;
    skos:definition "An examination of the asymmetry the claim asserts between the journey out and the journey back."@en ;
    skos:note "Against the claim stands the same passage as at the neighbouring node, in a weaker way. If the time lying still follows the distance and ends at a day, the account being given is of an absence with a duration, not of an absence with a slow half and an instant half. The transmission says nothing about the return specifically; it is the shape of the whole report that sits awkwardly with a claim of asymmetry."@en,
        "Procedure without cases rather than no procedure, and this is the one of the three where that holds. A comparison of reported durations for the outward and the return passage, gathered from practitioners not told which half the question is about, needs nothing that does not exist. It has not been done."@en,
        "The asymmetry is where this claim is reachable, and it is reachable in principle without settling anything about the journey itself. Whatever the outward passage is, the claim says the return is not like it, and a report that describes both would show whether the practitioner experiences them as different."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "Not devised here. The falsifying condition follows from the claim asserting a difference between two things the practitioner can be asked about separately" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "Practitioners asked about both passages, without being told that the asymmetry is at issue, report durations for the return that match those for the outward journey."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:ReturnIsImmediate .

pmc:IndoTibetanReception a pm:Converging ;
    rdfs:label "Indo-tibetische Rezeption, keine Konvergenz"@de,
        "Indo-Tibetan reception, not convergence"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)" ;
    skos:definition "The correspondence between the Indian channel-and-heat material and its Tibetan form, held as a node in order to be counted out: the route is documented, so the agreement is reception and carries no weight as evidence of an independently arrived-at structure."@en ;
    skos:note "Recorded because a graph that only holds findings cannot show its own exclusions. Any later count of independent attestations must skip this pair."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:convergingConcept pmc:InnerHeat,
        pmc:Kundalini,
        pmc:Rtsa ;
    pm:transmissionPath "The six yogas reach Tibet from the Indian mahāsiddha Nāropa through Marpa the translator; the transmission is named in the Tibetan sources themselves." .

pmc:InipiPresupposesTheCustomaryBuild a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Hütte muss nach dem Brauch gebaut sein, sonst wirkt die Zeremonie nicht"@de,
        "The lodge must be built after the custom or the ceremony is of no avail"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 156" ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity because the source does not prescribe the build and stop there: it names what follows if the build is wrong, and what follows is total. The sentence is from the text written in Lakota by Sword and translated, not from the compiler's exposition."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The ceremony must be performed in an ini ti; the ini ti must be made according to Lakota custom, otherwise the ceremony would be of no avail."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:Inipi ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by named Oglala shamans, chiefly Sword, Tyon, Ringing-shield, Bad-wound and No-flesh, and by Finger on 25 March 1914; recorded by the agency physician J. R. Walker at Pine Ridge over his years of service there, closed December 1916; given in Lakota, part of it written out in Lakota by Sword and Tyon and translated by Burt Means, Thomas Mills, Bruce Means and Antoin Herman; the paper prints the translations only, so the Lakota wording is not carried." ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:IniTiConstruction ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LakotaRitual .

pmc:InitiationPresupposesSaktipata a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Initiation setzt die Herabkunft der Kraft voraus"@de,
        "Initiation presupposes the descent of power"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Abhinavagupta, Tantrasāra XI",
        "Abhinavagupta, Tantrāloka XIII" ;
    skos:note "The exegesis asks who is qualified for initiation and answers by taking up the descent of power, which places the descent before the rite and before the path. Later and other traditions have read the descent as the fruit of practice instead; the direction recorded here is the one Abhinavagupta sets."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Diksa ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Saktipata ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmc:InnerHeatFromBreathAndDirectedAttention a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wärme aus gehaltenem Atem und gerichteter Aufmerksamkeit"@de,
        "Heat from a held breath and attention directed into the trunk"@en ;
    skos:note "It is nonetheless the most examinable generalization the holding can currently form, and for a reason that has nothing to do with the number of witnesses: it names a quantity an instrument reads. The two clauses of the statement are separately refutable — a rise that a matched untrained person shows from the breathing alone refutes the second without touching the first."@en,
        "The weakness is a route and it is stated rather than smoothed over. The Tibetan material received the Indic; these are not two independent witnesses but one transmission at two stations, and the count here is therefore one and not two. The class does not require independence, but a reader ordering the queue by the spread of the traditions under a generalization must see that this one has none."@en,
        "Two attested claims: the Tibetan tummo instruction and the root seal of the Haṭhayogapradīpikā. Two is the floor the class sets and not a recommendation, and this node stands on the floor."@en,
        "Two protocols in examinations/tummo-heat.ttl already address the tradition-level claim and the question whether the gentler form works without the forceful breath. Neither points here, and neither is extended by this node: a protocol on a generalization has to be devised against this wording, and the difference between the two levels is exactly what the wording adds."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:MulabandhaYieldsInnerFire,
        pmc:TummoYieldsInnerHeat ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Practices that combine a retained or forcefully driven breath with attention directed to a named region of the trunk raise the practitioner's measured peripheral temperature above what the same person shows at rest under the same ambient conditions, and by more than the breathing alone produces in a person not trained in the practice."@en .

pmc:InuitHelpingSpirits a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Hilfsgeister"@de,
        "Helping spirits"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), pp. 24, 28" ;
    skos:definition "Spirits belonging to a particular shaman, called up by him and reported to act in the world on his behalf: to guard a child, to raise a barrier against the dead, to find what has been lost."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InuitAngakkuq .

pmc:InuitSecondSightIsAbiding a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Das zweite Gesicht bleibt"@de,
        "Second sight abides"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 29" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:abidingState ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:InuitSecondSight ;
    pm:situationGround "A standing property of a person is not an episode. This is the placement that broke the older single axis: not attained and not passing either, which the two ends of that axis could not hold at once. See prima-materia#439."@en .

pmc:InuitSecondSightIsNotByEffort a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Das zweite Gesicht wird nicht durch Anstrengung erlangt"@de,
        "Second sight is not attained by effort"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 29" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:notByEffort ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:InuitSecondSight ;
    pm:situationGround "The narrative reports the capacity as a standing property of a person and expressly not as the outcome of an exercise. No training is named for it anywhere in the portion read."@en .

pmc:InuitSpiritCallingAnchoringIsNotStated a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Das Geisterrufen gibt keine Sinnesbindung an"@de,
        "The spirit calling states no sensory anchoring"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), pp. 24, 28" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:anchoringNotStated ;
    pm:situatedNode pmp:InuitSpiritCalling ;
    pm:situationGround "The portion read gives the occasions on which the session is held and the outcomes claimed for it, and not the procedure; the node's own note already records that nothing was found there about posture, words or darkness. A session held in the household on the occasion that requires it is not thereby placed on this axis, and the silence is worth recording because the later chapters of the same volume are known to describe what this portion does not."@en .

pmc:IuratusIuramentumKautel a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die neun Eide über den Inhaber des Buches"@de,
        "The nine oaths on the holder of the book"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber iuratus Honorii, prologue (Sloane MS 3854, fols. 112vb-113ra), section 3 of Boudet's critical edition, at p. 872: Nos moti furore et iracundia ista fecimus juramenta" ;
    skos:definition "The oaths the prologue says the masters swore, from which the book takes its name. That the book be given to no one until its master is at the point of death; that it be copied three times only; that it be given to no woman, nor to any man unless mature, most upright and faithful, and whose character and condition the master has known for a year; that it not be destroyed but returned to the giver or his successors; that if no sufficient man is found to receive it, the master have it buried with him in his tomb, binding his executors by the strongest oaths, or himself bury it somewhere cleanly in his lifetime and reveal the place to no one by any circumstance; that the disciples not fear to suffer death in fulfilling the master's precepts; that the holder not enquire into his master's words or deeds nor reveal to anyone that his master knows such things; that master and disciples be bound in concord and love, none revealing another's secret; and ninthly that the receiver swear and thereafter observe all of these."@en ;
    skos:note "The device is the same one the holding already carries at pmc:ArsNotoriaSecretumKautel for the first work of this tradition, and the two are not independent of each other. What this witness adds is the count, the year of acquaintance, the exclusion of women and the burial clause, which the first work does not have."@en,
        "This is the gate the criterion of SPEC 0 names, stated by the transmission in its own operative text and in nine clauses rather than one. It stands here as a claim of the tradition and not as an instruction: the Latin has been in a critical edition since two thousand and two and the English since well before."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:IuratusOpusVisionis ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberIuratusHonorii ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:IuratusLunationes a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Mondtage der ersten Reinigung und ihre Zahlen"@de,
        "The days of the moon of the first purification and their counts"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber iuratus Honorii, cap. LI, the first purification (Royal MS 17 A. xlii, fols. 45v-46r; Latin of Sloane MS 3854 as given in Peterson's edition)" ;
    skos:definition "The timetable that follows a granted petition: on the fourth, eighth, twelfth, sixteenth, twentieth, twenty-fourth, twenty-eighth and thirty-second of the moon the named orations are said once about the third hour, once about the ninth and once at evening; and a second oration is said four times on the first day of the moon, three times on the third, twice on the sixth, and in rising counts on the ninth, twelfth, fifteenth, eighteenth, twenty-first, twenty-third, twenty-sixth, twenty-ninth and thirtieth."@en ;
    skos:note "The same device as the lunation table of the Ars Notoria at pmc:ArsNotoriaLunationes, in a second work of the same tradition and with a different set of days. Two works of one transmission fixing different days is a finding about the device and not about the days."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberIuratusHonorii ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:IuratusMundacioYieldsConcessus a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die drei Tage bringen das Urteil in der vierten Nacht"@de,
        "The three days yield the verdict on the fourth night"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber iuratus Honorii, cap. L (Royal MS 17 A. xlii, fol. 44v), ed. Peterson" ;
    skos:note "The term and the sign are in one clause, which is what prima-materia#640 found missing across seven works. The Ars Notoria of the same tradition promises the sciences within one month and names nothing that would show the month had passed unfulfilled; this work promises an answer on a particular night and says what the answer is."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:IuratusMundacioPrima ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberIuratusHonorii ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:IuratusConcessusVelRepulsa .

pmc:IuratusNonagintaTriaCapitula a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die dreiundneunzig Kapitel und ihre fünf Werke"@de,
        "The ninety-three capitula and their five works"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber iuratus Honorii, prologue and cap. II, the capitula of the five works (Sloane MS 3854, fols. 112vb-113vb), sections 3 and 4 of Boudet's critical edition, at pp. 872-874, and in the English of Royal MS 17 A. xlii, fols. 4r-5v, ed. Peterson" ;
    skos:definition "The catalogue the book puts before itself, which Honorius says he drew out of seven volumes of the magical art: ninety-three capitula, divided over five works. The first work holds six, from the composition of the seventy-two-letter name to the redemption of three souls out of purgatory, with the divine vision as its second. The second holds twenty-seven, on the heavens and their angels and on knowing things present, past and to come. The third holds fifty-four, from the constraining of spirits by words to the appearance of every pleasure, and it is the part that names invisibility, the opening of locks, the destruction of a kingdom, the curing and the giving of any sickness, and the killing of anyone. The fourth holds the freeing of prisoners, the opening of locks and prisons, hidden treasure, and the appearance of dead bodies speaking. The fifth is the exposition of the four before it."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as the catalogue and not as ninety-three capacities, and the reason is a property of the witnesses rather than of the run. The catalogue is read at the wording and in the Latin, so each entry is a claimed use of the art with its name; but the operative chapter is read for the first work only. For capitula seven to ninety-three the witnesses carry the title and not the procedure: the English of Royal MS 17 A. xlii breaks off in the second treatise and Boudet's edition prints the table and then the chapters he selects. A yielding node per title would claim a reading of ninety-three prescriptions that did not happen, which is the error SPEC 15 names on the other axis from prima-materia#747. The omission is named in the pull request of this harvest and is the work the next run on this witness has to do."@en,
        "The count itself is a finding. The oldest witnesses say ninety-three and one manuscript says ninety-two; Honorius says he preserved the flower of seven volumes and left the bark to others. So the work presents itself as a selection with a number, and the number is in the prologue rather than in a later table of contents."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberIuratusHonorii ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:IuratusObiectioNonVidebitMe a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Einwand aus der Schrift, erhoben und durch Verengung beantwortet"@de,
        "The objection from scripture, raised and answered by narrowing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber iuratus Honorii, cap. CI (Royal MS 17 A. xlii, fol. 64r), ed. Peterson; Latin of Sloane MS 3854 in Boudet's edition, section 15, at pp. 880-881" ;
    skos:definition "The objection the book puts to itself immediately after the vision, and its answer. Someone will say: since the Lord says that man shall not see me and live, it follows that whoever sees God must die bodily, and therefore will not rise again until the day of judgement, since no one dies bodily twice. The answer is that it is false that anyone dies bodily in the divine vision: the spirit is caught up into heaven and the body is refreshed in the earth with angelic food. For we read of many caught up in spirit to whom many celestial secrets were revealed, and at the passion of our Lord many bodies rose again."@en ;
    skos:note "Not added to that generalization in this run. The generalization is untested and SPEC 16 requires the counter-search against the holding before it is enlarged; the search that would have to run is whether the holding carries transmissions that state a counter-observation and let it stand, and that search was not carried here."@en,
        "The structure is the one the holding generalizes at generalizations/the-observation-answered-by-narrowing.ttl from three witnesses: the transmission states the counter-observation in full and then moves the claim so that the observation no longer touches it. Here the move is precise and small: the claim is narrowed from seeing God to the spirit's being caught up while the body stays alive, and the scriptural sentence is left standing."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberIuratusHonorii ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:IuratusOctoTerminiSineLunatione a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die acht Termini achten weder Tag noch Mondphase noch notwendig die Stunde"@de,
        "The eight terms regard neither day nor lunation nor, of necessity, hour"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber iuratus Honorii, cap. CII (Royal MS 17 A. xlii, fol. 65v), and in the Latin of Sloane MS 3854, prologue and first book, section 16 of the critical edition of Jean-Patrice Boudet, Mélanges de l'École française de Rome. Moyen Âge 114/2 (2002), pp. 851-890, at p. 881" ;
    skos:definition "The exemption the book grants its own calendar: for the eight terms said with the ten orations numbered twenty-three to thirty-two it is not necessary to regard the day, nor the lunation, nor of necessity the hour; they are said chiefly about the morning, about the third hour and about midday, and they are said to set right whatever a man has done amiss through his frailty, the more and the oftener the more the fault is amended."@en ;
    skos:note "A negative witness, and it is worth keeping for that. The holding carries the claim that efficacy is bound to the calendar at generalizations/efficacy-bound-to-the-calendar.ttl, and here is a work that states the binding for one part of its own apparatus and lifts it for another, in the same chapter and with the lifting given as a reason rather than a concession. A transmission that exempts part of its own procedure is harder to read as a transmission for which the calendar carries the effect."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberIuratusHonorii ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:IuratusOpusYieldsVisio a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die dreizehn Tage und der Schlaf bringen die Schau der Gottheit"@de,
        "The thirteen days and the sleep yield the sight of the Deity"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber iuratus Honorii, cap. CI (Royal MS 17 A. xlii, fol. 64r), ed. Peterson" ;
    skos:note "The vision is placed in sleep and after silence, which is why it is given a placement on the sensory axis rather than left to the reader. What the text does not give is any sign by which a sleep that produced nothing would be told from one that produced the vision; the sign it does give, the grant or the refusal of pmc:IuratusConcessusVelRepulsa, falls thirteen days earlier and is about the petition, not about the sight."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:IuratusOpusVisionis ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberIuratusHonorii ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:IuratusVisioDivina .

pmc:IuratusPresupposesChristianus a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Wahrhaftes Wirken setzt den christlichen Ausführenden voraus"@de,
        "True operation presupposes the Christian operator"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber iuratus Honorii, sections 8-9 of Boudet's critical edition of Sloane MS 3854, at pp. 875-876" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Because they have a bad faith, their works are null: ideo opera eorum nulla; and the spirits, though compelled to come, will not answer truly one who is not signed with the sign of the cross and of the faith."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:IuratusVisioDivina ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberIuratusHonorii ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:IuratusSolusChristianus ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:IuratusPresupposesFidesCatholica a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Schau setzt den katholischen Glauben voraus, bei Strafe des Verderbens"@de,
        "The vision presupposes the catholic faith, on pain of perishing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber iuratus Honorii, cap. XIII, oratio 7 (Royal MS 17 A. xlii), ed. Peterson" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Which faith unless a man believe faithfully and firmly he shall perish and shall not have the divine vision."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:IuratusVisioDivina ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberIuratusHonorii ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:IuratusFidesCatholica ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:IuratusPresupposesSequestratio a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Schau setzt Absonderung von den weltlichen Dingen voraus"@de,
        "The vision presupposes sequestration from worldly things"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber iuratus Honorii, cap. CI (Royal MS 17 A. xlii, fol. 62r), ed. Peterson" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "No man that is troubled with worldly things can do this thing, for the soul, by means of earthly blindness, is utterly sequestrate from all godly secrets."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:IuratusVisioDivina ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberIuratusHonorii ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:IuratusSequestratio ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:IuratusPresupposesSinePeccatoMortali a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Schau setzt Freiheit von Todsünde voraus, bei Strafe des Wahnsinns"@de,
        "The vision presupposes freedom from deadly sin, on pain of madness"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber iuratus Honorii, cap. CI (Royal MS 17 A. xlii, fol. 64v), ed. Peterson; Latin in Boudet's edition, section 15, at p. 880" ;
    skos:note "Stated necessity and not prescribed order, because the consequence is quotable and is quoted. The prior step is entered as the clause itself rather than as a state of cleanness, since what the source makes binding is the warning with its outcome and not a purification it names elsewhere."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "He that shall work must beware that he be in no deadly sin, for if he be he shall be mad ever after: de cetero esset insanus, and as regards his body there will be in him no human rationality."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:IuratusVisioDivina ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberIuratusHonorii ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:IuratusInsania ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:IuratusRepulsaIteratio a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Was geschieht, wenn die Abweisung kommt"@de,
        "What is done when the refusal comes"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber iuratus Honorii, cap. L (Royal MS 17 A. xlii, fols. 45r-45v; Latin of Sloane MS 3854 as given in Peterson's edition: in orationibus latinis tunc oporteret peticionem mutare, scilicet peticione concessus in peticionem miseracionis)" ;
    skos:definition "The provision for the refusal, in three steps. First the three days are done again as before, except that on the Sunday alms to three poor people take the place of the fast. Then the same again on the first moon, that is the Monday following, and likewise on the second, the third and the fourth, and so on to the end, that peradventure God will have mercy. And in the later prayers the petition is changed: the operator is no longer to desire that God grant his petition but only that God have mercy on him. Alongside it stands the instruction that on being put back he ought not to despair, but to confess truly, search his conscience, give alms largely, have masses said and entreat with bitter tears, for so the wise men were wont to come to their purpose."@en ;
    skos:note "Held apart from the clause it would otherwise be confused with. The failure is not denied and not explained away as the operator's misreading; the refusal is accepted as a refusal. What is removed is only its cumulative force. That makes this a different device from the ones the holding carries at generalizations/no-failed-run-counts.ttl and generalizations/the-repair-without-a-sign.ttl, and whether it is the same device in a new dress is a question for a run that puts this witness against those; it is not settled here."@en,
        "This is the node the run was looking for and it is the opposite of what the search expected. The book states a falsifiable outcome and then, in the next breath, states the procedure by which no number of refusals ever amounts to one: the sequence restarts monthly without end, and at the point where the count would begin to tell against the art the petition itself is exchanged for a petition that has no outcome. A request for mercy cannot be refused in a way anybody could observe."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberIuratusHonorii ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:IuratusSituatVisioAgentia a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Schau wird nicht erzwungen, sondern erbeten"@de,
        "The vision is not compelled but prayed for"@en ;
    skos:note "Worth the placement because the contrast is inside one work: the agency is the operator's where spirits are constrained and ceded where God is asked, and a transmission that holds both is a different thing from one that holds either."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:cededAgency ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:IuratusVisioDivina ;
    pm:situationGround "The source says the sight of the Deity will not be had without perfect pureness, for he will not be constrained but prayed unto and entreated: quia non est super ipsum coactio immo preces. The same book constrains spirits by words, seals and tables in its third work, so the ceding is stated for this operation specifically and not as a general stance."@en .

pmc:IuratusSituatVisioSomnus a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Das Werk der Schau steht an der Schlafschwelle"@de,
        "The work of the vision stands at the threshold of sleep"@en ;
    skos:note "The placement is on the threshold rather than on dreaming within, because the text puts the going to sleep and the silence as the last prescribed acts and makes the sight follow them, without saying that what is seen is seen as a dream. The grant or the refusal of pmc:IuratusConcessusVelRepulsa is said in somnis and is a different act thirteen days earlier."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:sleepThreshold ;
    pm:situatedNode pmp:IuratusOpusVisionis ;
    pm:situationGround "The operation ends with the instruction to sleep and thereafter not to speak, and the vision is said to follow from that: tunc dormiat et de cetero non loquatur, et sic fiet quod videbit celeste palatium. The source describes the state as sleep entered after the whole apparatus is in place and does not name a coordinate; the placement is the compiler's."@en .

pmc:IuratusSpiritusNeuterBonusNeuterMalus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Weder gut noch böse: die Einordnung, die das Buch verweigert"@de,
        "Neither good nor bad: the classification the book declines"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber iuratus Honorii, third treatise, proem on the aerial spirits (Sloane MS 3854, fol. 132ra), section 26 of Boudet's critical edition: eos neque bonos neque malos volumus appellare" ;
    skos:definition "The book's refusal to classify the spirits of the air. In the air are spirits which holy mother Church calls damned, but they themselves assert the opposite to be true, and therefore we do not wish to call them either good or bad."@en ;
    skos:note "The refusal is reported as a refusal and not as a doctrine: the book names both parties, names that they disagree, and declines to decide. Kept as a concept of the tradition rather than as a dispute node, because one place reporting two sides is one source and a dispute in this holding carries two."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberIuratusHonorii ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:IuratusSpiritusTerreniKautel a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Warnung gegen die Operation, die dasselbe Kapitel vorschreibt"@de,
        "The warning against the operation the same chapter prescribes"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber iuratus Honorii, fourth treatise, on the earthly angels (Sloane MS 3854, fol. 137va), section 36 of Boudet's critical edition: Et de istis nunquam vel raro christianus se intromittit, nec eorum consilia sunt credenda" ;
    skos:definition "The clause that closes the operation on the earthly spirits, in the chapter that has just given it: a Christian never or very rarely meddles with these, nor are their counsels to be believed; and of these the pagans chiefly operate and Christians most rarely."@en ;
    skos:note "A self-warning inside the prescription, in the strict sense: the sentence that tells the operator how to end the rite is the sentence that tells him not to perform it. The holding carries this device as a generalization from four witnesses at generalizations/self-warning-inside-the-prescription.ttl; this is a fifth place of the same shape and it has not been added there, because the first work of this same tradition is already among the four and two works of one transmission are not two witnesses."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:IuratusOpusVisionis ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberIuratusHonorii ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:JainaAlocanaYieldsFreedomFromThorns a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Bekenntnis entfernt die drei Dornen"@de,
        "Confession removes the three thorns"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX.5 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:JainaAlocana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JainaTrisalyaMukti .

pmc:JainaAnuprekshaYieldsShortening a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Nachsinnen verkürzt das Karman"@de,
        "Pondering shortens the karman"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX.22 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:JainaAnupreksha ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JainaKarmasthitiHrasa .

pmc:JainaArhatanivarana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Kraft kann auf einen Arhat nicht wirken"@de,
        "The power can have no effect on an Arhat"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Viyāhapannatti (Bhagavaī) XV, Calcutta edition p. 1234a" ;
    skos:definition "The answer Mahāvīra gives when Ānanda, having been threatened, comes and asks him whether Gosāla really possesses powers of destruction. He admits the power, and adds that it could have no effect on an Arhat, because the powers of the latter are still greater."@en ;
    skos:note "A condition of failure stated in advance of the case that tests it, which is rare enough in this holding to be the reason for the node. The book states the rule at the Calcutta page 1234a and then, some pages later, has Gosāla shoot the power at Mahāvīra and has it fail; the rule is therefore not read off the outcome but laid down before it."@en,
        "What it costs on the other side: the rule makes the one test that a bystander could witness unavailable in advance. Any failure against an Arhat confirms the rule, any success would show the target not to have been one, and the transmission supplies no mark of Arhatship independent of the outcome. That is the same clause this file already carries at pmc:JainaLingamNaTrayate, working the other way round: there the failure of the practice is assigned to the practitioner, here the failure of the attack is assigned to the target."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "A. F. Rudolf Hoernle, The Uvāsagadasāo, vol. II, Appendix I (Bibliotheca Indica, Calcutta 1888), p. 5" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaAtapanabhumi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Mit erhobenen Armen und aufwärts gewandtem Gesicht in der Sonnenglut sitzend"@de,
        "Sitting with upraised arms and upturned face in the glare of the sun"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Viyāhapannatti (Bhagavaī) XV, Calcutta edition p. 1220" ;
    skos:altLabel "ātāpanā"@en ;
    skos:definition "The posture in which the book shows the ascetic Vesiyāyana when Gosāla first sees him, outside the town of Kummāgāma: sitting with upraised arms and upturned face in the glare of the sun, his body swarming with lice. It is the posture, and the only part of any discipline in this episode, that the abridged translation prints."@en ;
    skos:note "Stated of the man who has the power and not of the acquiring of it, and the node keeps that difference. The narrative shows the posture, then has Gosāla ask how the power was acquired, then says that Mahāvīra explained the discipline without saying what it was. A reader may join the two; the transmission, in this translation, does not."@en,
        "The secondary literature reports a fuller formula in the Prakrit of this book, with continuous two-day fasts, a handful of beans and a cupped handful of water beside the sun-exposure. None of that is in either translation read and none of it is entered here. What became of that claim stands at pmp:JainaTejolesyaVidhiSearch and in prima-materia#722."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "A. F. Rudolf Hoernle, The Uvāsagadasāo, vol. II, Appendix I (Bibliotheca Indica, Calcutta 1888), p. 3" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaBhaktapratyakhyanaYieldsFewerBirths a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der Verzicht auf alle Nahrung verhindert Hunderte von Geburten"@de,
        "The renunciation of all food prevents hundreds of births"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX.40 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:JainaBhaktapratyakhyana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JainaJanmashatanivarana .

pmc:JainaCautionAgainstDevamaya a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Traue der trügerischen Macht der Götter nicht"@de,
        "Do not trust the delusive power of the gods"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ācārāṅga Sūtra I.7.8.24 (Jacobi, SBE 22, Oxford 1884; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 1964)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:JainaDevamaya ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaCautionAgainstNimittaAndCikitsa a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Gegen das Ausüben der Künste und gegen das Heilen"@de,
        "Against professing the arts and against healing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XV.7-8 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)",
        "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XX.45 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:note "Repeated in a second work with a stated outcome rather than a definition: he who practises divination from bodily marks and dreams, who is well versed in augury and superstitious rites, who gains a sinful living by practising magic tricks, will have no refuge at the time of retribution."@en,
        "That second passage stands in the block of the twentieth lecture which the editor of the edition read holds to be apparently a later addition, on the grounds that its subject is unconnected with what precedes and that its metre differs. The caution is carried by the fifteenth lecture without it; the repetition is recorded with its doubt rather than counted as a second independent place."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:JainaCikitsavidya,
        pmc:JainaNimittavidya ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaCautionAgainstOutwardTokens a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Gegen das Verwechseln des Abzeichens mit der Sache"@de,
        "Against taking the badge for the thing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Sūtrakṛtāṅga Sūtra I.2.1.9 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)",
        "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra V.21-22 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:note "A self-warning in the strict sense: the text warns against the very marks it prescribes, and it does so without withdrawing the prescription."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:JainaLingamNaTrayate ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaCautionAgainstPapashramana a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Gegen das Werden zum schlechten Asketen"@de,
        "Against becoming the bad ascetic"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XVII.20 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:note "The lecture closes the warning with a judgement rather than a penalty: such a monk, who like the heretics does not protect himself from sins and who though having the appearance of a monk is the lowest among his worthy brethren, is despised in this world like poison, and he is nobody in this world and in that beyond."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:JainaPapashramana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaCautionAgainstThePleasantThings a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die angenehmen Dinge sind wie das Gift Tālapuṭa"@de,
        "The pleasant things are like the poison Tālapuṭa"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XVI.11-14 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:definition "The verse that closes the enumeration: a lodging frequented by women, their pleasant talk, their company, looking at their charms, their screeching, screaming, singing and laughing, eating and sleeping together with them, well-dressed food and drink or partaking of them to excess, and ornaments and finery — these pleasant things, which are hard to leave, are like the poison Tālapuṭa for a man who seeks after the true Self."@en ;
    skos:note "The comparison is to a poison the commentary of the edition read glosses as killing by merely touching the palate, while the translator notes that the etymology behind that gloss is doubtful. The gloss is not entered; what is entered is that the transmission chose a poison and not a snare for the image, which puts the harm in the contact and not in the pursuit."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:JainaDashaBrahmacaryasamadhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaCautionAgainstWishing a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Verlange weder nach Leben noch nach Tod"@de,
        "Yearn after neither life nor death"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ācārāṅga Sūtra I.7.8.4, with I.7.8.23 (Jacobi, SBE 22, Oxford 1884; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 1964)" ;
    skos:note "Entered beside the further clause at the end of the same lesson, which forbids attachment to the transitory pleasures and to the greater ones and forbids nourishing desire and greed. The translation of that verse is difficult at its last words and the difficulty is left visible rather than smoothed: what stands in the edition read is that he should not nourish desire and greed, looking only for eternal praise, and whether the Prakrit forbids the seeking of praise or enjoins it is a question for the critical edition and not for this node."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:JainaJivitamaranaAkanksha ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaDashaYieldsSamadhi a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die zehn Bedingungen bringen die Bewahrung der Keuschheit"@de,
        "The ten conditions bring the guarding of chastity"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XVI, opening formula (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:JainaDashaBrahmacaryasamadhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JainaBrahmacaryasamadhiPhala .

pmc:JainaDvadashaTapas a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die zwölf Askesen, sechs äußere und sechs innere"@de,
        "The twelve austerities, six outward and six inward"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXX.7-8 and XXX.30-36 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:definition "The division the thirtieth lecture sets out. The six external: fasting, abstinence, collecting alms, abstention from dainty food, mortification of the flesh, and taking care of one's limbs. The six internal: expiation of sins, politeness, serving the teacher, study, meditation, and the abandoning of the body."@en ;
    skos:note "Expiation stands inside austerity here and not beside it. That places the whole apparatus of penances for the ascetic's own failures among the practices that shed Karman, which means that in this transmission the correction of a failed observance is itself an observance."@en,
        "The count is the transmission's own and the translator notes that the general division agrees with the other canonical list he compares it to while the subdivisions differ in many details. The list is entered as this lecture gives it and not harmonised."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaDvadashavarshaBeforeKevala a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die zwölf Jahre stehen vor der Erlangung"@de,
        "The twelve years stand before the attainment"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Kalpa Sūtra 117 and 120 (Jacobi, SBE 22, Oxford 1884; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 1964)" ;
    skos:note "Presentation order and nothing stronger, deliberately. The work narrates the twelve years and then the thirteenth year in which the attainment fell; it does not say that twelve years are required, and it states no consequence for a shorter term. The introduction to the edition read asserts that twelve years of penance were always held essential and that every ascetic has to undergo a course of that length; that is the editor writing in 1884 and it is recorded here as his and not entered as the transmission's."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:JainaKevalaprapti ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:presentationOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:JainaDvadashavarshaNirodha ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaGlaneAharaPunah a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Wird der Mönch krank, so nehme er wieder Nahrung"@de,
        "If the mendicant falls sick, let him take food again"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ācārāṅga Sūtra I.7.8.3, with I.7.8.6 (Jacobi, SBE 22, Oxford 1884; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 1964)" ;
    skos:definition "The clause that stands in the middle of the instruction for the death by fasting: subduing the passions and living on little food he should endure hardships, and if a mendicant falls sick, let him again take food."@en ;
    skos:note "An abort condition written into the prescription itself, and the rarest thing in this holding: an instruction that names the circumstance under which it is to be broken off, inside the same passage that gives it. It is not a warning about a danger but a rule for stopping, and it is addressed to the practitioner rather than to anyone watching him."@en,
        "It sits beside a second clause with the same direction, four verses later: whatever means one knows for calming one's own life, that a wise man should learn in order to gain time for continuing penance. The two together make the preservation of the life a duty of the man who is fasting to death, which is the point at which this practice separates from the act it otherwise resembles."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:JainaSamlekhanaItvara ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaJalasiddhiVivada a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob Wasser oder Feuer irgendjemanden vollenden können"@de,
        "Whether water or fire can perfect anyone"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Sūtrakṛtāṅga Sūtra I.7.12-13 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)",
        "Sūtrakṛtāṅga Sūtra I.7.14-19 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:definition "The refutation the same lecture mounts, and it is built by taking the claim at its word and reading a consequence off it. If perfection could be obtained by contact with water, many beings living in water must have reached perfection: fishes, tortoises, aquatic snakes, cormorants, otters, and the demons living in water. If water did wash off the impure Karman it must take off merit too. If water did wash off the sins of him who committed them, some would have obtained perfection who killed water-beings. And if perfection could be obtained through fire, mechanics also, who use fire, would be liberated. The lecture concludes that perfection cannot be established by such gratuitous assertions."@en ;
    skos:note "Both citations are the Jain text's, one for the claim and one for the refutation, so the dispute is reported by one side only and the graph holds no witness for the other. That is recorded rather than concealed, as at pmc:SuiyueSuoshiDispute, where the same situation arises for the same reason: the practice attacked has left no transmission this holding has opened."@en,
        "It has the weakness of its strength, and the lecture does not notice it. The same reasoning would tell against the austerity this transmission itself prescribes, since beings who fast by nature are not reported as perfected either; the reply available to it is that the fast works by intention and not by contact, which is exactly the reply a defender of the ablutions would give. The argument therefore refutes a reading of the rival practice rather than the practice."@en,
        "This is the most transferable thing in either volume. The argument is not that the rival practice is impious; it is that the rival's own rule, applied without exception, entails an outcome in a class of beings who satisfy the rule by nature, and that the outcome is not there. This holding already carries the same instrument from the other end of Asia, in the first-century Chinese treatise that puts consequences of the day-choosing doctrine to its own practitioners. Two transmissions, no contact route between them at that date, one argument form."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:JainaJalasiddhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaMahaviraCikitsanirodha a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Stifter begehrte keine Heilbehandlung"@de,
        "The founder desired no medical treatment"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ācārāṅga Sūtra I.8.4.1-2 (Jacobi, SBE 22, Oxford 1884; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 1964)" ;
    skos:definition "What the same lecture states of him immediately before the regimen: he was able to abstain from indulgence of the flesh though never attacked by diseases, and whether wounded or not wounded he desired no medical treatment; purgatives and emetics, anointing of the body and bathing, shampooing and cleansing of the teeth do not behove him, after he learned that the body is something unclean."@en ;
    skos:note "The list of what does not behove him is the same list, item for item, that the fifteenth lecture of the Uttarādhyayana forbids the monk to practise on others. The refusal to be treated and the prohibition of treating are two sides of one rule in this transmission, and both are entered here because only the second was expected."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaNimittaVivada a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Manche Vorhersagen treffen zu, die Prophezeiungen anderer erweisen sich als falsch"@de,
        "Some forecasts are true and the prophecies of others prove wrong"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Sūtrakṛtāṅga Sūtra I.12.10 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:definition "The opposing position the next verse reports and rejects: the opponents say that some forecasts are true and the prophecies of others prove wrong, and that therefore they do not study those sciences, but profess to know the world, fools though they be."@en ;
    skos:note "A discrimination argument reported inside a transmission and attributed to its opponents: the art is not to be studied because its results do not agree with one another. It is the only argument of that form anywhere in what was read, and this transmission is on the other side of it."@en,
        "The reading is not secure and the insecurity is recorded rather than hidden. The edition notes a variant on which the line says instead that one must give up science altogether, and the translator marks the whole verse as the opponents' speech by a supplement of his own in brackets. What the node holds is what stands in the edition read, and a critical edition may move it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaNimittajnanaBhavishya a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die die Künste studiert haben, wissen das Kommende"@de,
        "Those who have studied the arts know the future"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Sūtrakṛtāṅga Sūtra I.12.9 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:definition "The premise the twelfth lecture of the first book uses against the philosophers who hold the world to be unreal: many men in this world who have studied astrology, the art of interpreting dreams, divination from diagrams, augury, divination from bodily marks and from portents, and the eight branches of divination from omens, know the future."@en ;
    skos:note "Stated as a fact and not as a concession, and used as a premise: the edition read notes that this would be impossible if the whole world were unreal. The same transmission that forbids its monks to live by these arts and threatens the birth among Asuras to those who do, here grants that they yield knowledge of what is to come, and grants it in order to win a different argument."@en,
        "That is not a contradiction and should not be entered as one. The prohibitions are about who may live by the arts and on what motive; this sentence is about whether the arts inform. A transmission can hold both, and this one does. What it costs the transmission is the easiest ground for the prohibition, which it therefore never takes."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaPapavidyaYieldsAsuratva a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Leben von den Künsten bringt die Geburt unter den Asuras hervor"@de,
        "Living by the arts produces the birth among Asuras"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Sūtrakṛtāṅga Sūtra II.2.27 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:note "Entered as a yielding claim although what is yielded is a penalty, because the form of the sentence is the form the class holds: this practice, carried out in this way, produces this. Nothing in the class requires the outcome to be desirable, and a holding that entered only the desirable ones would report the promises of the transmissions and not their claims."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:JainaPapavidyabhyasa ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JainaPapavidyaphala .

pmc:JainaPratyagatatejolesya a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Kraft prallt zurück und verbrennt den, der sie sandte"@de,
        "The power rebounds and burns the one who sent it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Viyāhapannatti (Bhagavaī) XV, Calcutta edition p. 1249b" ;
    skos:definition "What the book reports when Gosāla, reproved by Mahāvīra, draws back a few paces and shoots his power of destruction at him: it rebounds harmlessly from him as from a rock and returns, burning Gosāla himself."@en ;
    skos:note "The failure is not a nullity but a reversal, and the difference matters for what could be observed. A power that comes to nothing leaves no trace; a power that returns upon its sender leaves a man with a fever, which is what the book then reports and what the town is then said to argue about."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "A. F. Rudolf Hoernle, The Uvāsagadasāo, vol. II, Appendix I (Bibliotheca Indica, Calcutta 1888), p. 6" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaSakamamaranaNaSarvesham a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der gute Tod fällt nicht jedem Mönch zu"@de,
        "The good death does not fall to every monk"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra V.19-20 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:definition "What the fifth lecture says after distinguishing the death against one's will from the death with one's will: such a death does not fall to the lot of every monk, nor of every householder, for the morality of householders is of various character and that of monks is not always good throughout; and some householders are superior to some monks in self-control, though the saints are superior to all householders."@en ;
    skos:note "An explicit refusal of the guarantee, stated by the transmission about its own order. It is also the passage that opens the good death to the layman, which means the outcome is not reserved to those who carry the marks and cannot be read off them in either direction."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaSammattaparakkama a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Streben in der Rechtschaffenheit, als dreiundsiebzig Fragen gestellt"@de,
        "The exertion in righteousness, stated as seventy-three questions"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX, with the list of subjects at its head and the seventy-three answers following (Hermann Jacobi, Jaina Sūtras Part II, Sacred Books of the East 45, Oxford: Clarendon 1895; read in the Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:altLabel "sammattaparakkama"@en,
        "samyaktvaparākrama"@en ;
    skos:definition "The form the twenty-ninth lecture takes: a numbered list of seventy-three items, each put as a question and answered in the same words. Sir, what does the soul obtain by X? By X the soul obtains Y. The list runs from the longing for liberation and the disregard of worldly objects through confession, study, control and austerity to the renunciation of company, of food and of the body, and ends at the straight upward motion of the freed soul."@en ;
    skos:note "The answers are almost always chained: X obtains Y, and by Y he obtains Z. Eleven of the seventy-three end in the same terminal formula, that he obtains perfection, enlightenment, deliverance and final beatitude and puts an end to all misery. What the chain never does is name a perceptible event, and that is the subject of the search at examinations/searches-jain.ttl."@en,
        "The question form is the point and not the ornament. A transmission that states its path as a list of what each step obtains has committed itself to an answer at every step, and each answer can be read on its own and weighed against the next. The translation drops the question from the third item onward for brevity and says so; the lecture carries it throughout."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaSamvegaYieldsBoundedRebirth a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Verlangen nach Erlösung begrenzt die Zahl der Geburten"@de,
        "The longing for liberation bounds the number of births"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX.1 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:note "The chain in the text runs through two further terms before it arrives at the bound: the longing obtains an intense desire of the Law, the desire destroys anger, pride, deceit and greed, the soul acquires no bad Karman and becomes possessed of right faith, and the bound is stated of the purity of that faith. The node names the two ends of the chain and the source carries the middle."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:JainaSamvega ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JainaTribhavaAvadhi .

pmc:JainaSamyamaJnanaPurva a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Beherrschung und Wissen gehen dem religiösen Tod voraus"@de,
        "Control and knowledge come before the religious death"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ācārāṅga Sūtra I.7.8.1-2 (Jacobi, SBE 22, Oxford 1884; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 1964)" ;
    skos:definition "The condition the lesson opens with: the wise ones who attain in due order to one of the unerring states, those who are rich in control and endowed with knowledge, knowing the incomparable religious death, should continue their contemplation."@en ;
    skos:note "Prescribed order and not stated necessity: the verse states who does this and in what condition, and names nothing that befalls the man who does it otherwise. The edition read carries in a footnote the scholiast's rule that the death is normally permitted only after twelve years of preparatory penance and that the ascetic must ask and get the permission of his teacher; both are the commentary and neither stands in the verses, so neither is entered."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:JainaTrividhamaranakrama ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:JainaBhaktapratyakhyana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaSitalatejolesya a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der kühle Strahl, der den heißen lähmt"@de,
        "The cool flash that paralyses the hot one"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Viyāhapannatti (Bhagavaī) XV, Calcutta edition p. 1220" ;
    skos:definition "What the book has Mahāvīra do when Vesiyāyana, provoked past bearing, shoots his power at Gosāla. Mahāvīra, taking pity on Gosāla, interposes with a power of his own; Leumann's extract renders it: I interceded, and paralysed the hot flash of the ascetic's power by a cool flash of mine. The other, seeing that his power had remained without effect through Mahāvīra's, says twice that he sees."@en ;
    skos:note "A stated cause of a failure, and it lies in neither of the two parties to the operation. The power was correctly directed by a man who had it at a man who could be hurt by it, and it produced nothing because a third person interposed a contrary one. This holding carries the shape of that account under pm:Generalizing as the absent rival; here the rival is present, named, and on the side of the victim."@en,
        "It is also the one place in this episode where a temperature is asserted of the power in both directions. The destroying power is hot, the counter-power is cool, and the nullification is described as a paralysing rather than as a shielding."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "Ernst Leumann, Appendix I: Extracts from Bhagavatī XV, in W. W. Rockhill, The Life of the Buddha (London: Trubner 1884), p. 251; and A. F. Rudolf Hoernle, The Uvāsagadasāo, vol. II, Appendix I (Bibliotheca Indica, Calcutta 1888), p. 3" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaSuddhapanayaPresupposesSanmasa a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die sechs Monate gehen der Erscheinung der letzten Nacht voraus"@de,
        "The six months come before the appearance on the last night"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Viyāhapannatti (Bhagavaī) XV, Calcutta edition p. 1257" ;
    skos:note "Prescribed order and not stated necessity, although the passage does carry a quotable consequence, and the reason for the lower strength is worth stating because it could easily have gone the other way. What the sentence attaches an outcome to is a choice made on the last night, whether to submit to the touch; it attaches nothing to the omission of the six months. A man who never began the regimen is nowhere in the passage, and a strength of stated necessity would have put him there."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:JainaSuddhapanayaAntyaratri ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:JainaSuddhapanaya ;
    pm:readVia "A. F. Rudolf Hoernle, The Uvāsagadasāo, vol. II, Appendix I (Bibliotheca Indica, Calcutta 1888), pp. 8-9" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaSuddhapanayaYieldsMarana a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das halbjährige Regime bringt das Feuer und den vollendenden Tod"@de,
        "The six-month regimen brings the fire and the death that perfects"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Viyāhapannatti (Bhagavaī) XV, Calcutta edition p. 1257" ;
    skos:note "The claim is reported of another sect by this one, and the node holds it as this book's claim about that regimen. No transmission of the Ājīviyas has been opened in this holding and none survives independently, so there is no second side to put beside it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:JainaSuddhapanaya ;
    pm:readVia "A. F. Rudolf Hoernle, The Uvāsagadasāo, vol. II, Appendix I (Bibliotheca Indica, Calcutta 1888), pp. 8-9" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JainaSuddhapanayaAntyaratri .

pmc:JainaTapasYieldsTejolesya a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die strenge Askese bringt die Feuerkraft in sechs Monaten"@de,
        "The severe discipline brings the fiery power in six months"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Viyāhapannatti (Bhagavaī) XV, Calcutta edition p. 1222" ;
    skos:note "The yielding is claimed of a course whose content this holding does not have. That is a weak node and it says which part is weak: the practice, the period and the result are all attested, the description of the practice is not."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:JainaTejolesyaTapas ;
    pm:readVia "A. F. Rudolf Hoernle, The Uvāsagadasāo, vol. II, Appendix I (Bibliotheca Indica, Calcutta 1888), p. 4; and Ernst Leumann, Appendix I: Extracts from Bhagavatī XV, in W. W. Rockhill, The Life of the Buddha (London: Trubner 1884), p. 251" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JainaSanmasaTejolesyaprapti .

pmc:JainaTejolesya a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die ausgesandte Feuerkraft"@de,
        "The fiery power that is shot forth"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Viyāhapannatti (Bhagavaī) XV, Calcutta edition pp. 1220, 1247a, 1248a, 1249b" ;
    skos:altLabel "tejoleśyā"@en,
        "teya-lessā"@en ;
    skos:definition "The capacity the fifteenth book has its persons obtain, use and suffer: a power of destruction that one ascetic shoots forth at another from a few paces away, that burns what it strikes, and that a bystander can see to have been used because the man it was aimed at is destroyed by it. The book uses it four times in its narrative: twice by Gosāla against disciples of Mahāvīra, once by the ascetic Vesiyāyana against Gosāla, and once by Gosāla against Mahāvīra."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered from the narrative and not from a definition, because neither translation read carries a definition of it in the text itself. What both carry is a power with a direction, a range and an effect on a third party, which is more than most capacities in this holding have."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "A. F. Rudolf Hoernle, The Uvāsagadasāo, vol. II, Appendix I: The History of Gosāla Maṅkhaliputta, briefly translated from Bhagavatī saya XV (Bibliotheca Indica, Calcutta 1888), pp. 3-7" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaTejolesyaVyakhya a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Eine Kraft, die über viele Yojana brennt und im Leib gespeichert werden kann"@de,
        "A force that burns over many yojanas and can be stored in the body"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Malayagiri, commentary on the Sūryaprajñapti, as cited at Bhagavatī p. 318 of the Calcutta edition" ;
    skos:definition "The gloss the commentary gives for the standing epithet saṅkhitta-viula-teya-lesse, Sanskrit saṅkṣipta-vipula-tejoleśyaḥ: a kind of fiery force acquired as the reward of distinguished asceticism, of such a nature as to be able to burn up objects situated over an area extending over many yojanas, yet compressible and so storable within the small compass of the body."@en ;
    skos:note "The epithet is formulaic and not peculiar to the fifteenth book. It stands in the same corpus as a fixed attribute of ascetics who are not in that story at all, which is the reason for holding the gloss apart from the narrative nodes: what the fifteenth book relates is one use of a capacity the corpus names elsewhere as a standing possession."@en,
        "Two quantities in one epithet, and they are the reason the gloss is worth a node: a range over which the power reaches and a volume into which it is withdrawn. Neither is given a number, so neither carries a measurement, but a transmission that describes a capacity in terms of extent and compression has said something that a later account either keeps or drops."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "A. F. Rudolf Hoernle, The Uvāsagadasāo, vol. II (Bibliotheca Indica, Calcutta 1888), note 140, reporting Malayagiri's commentary on the Sūryaprajñapti" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaVaidyanamVifalata a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Ärzte, die Zauber und Wurzeln kannten, konnten ihn von seinen Schmerzen nicht befreien"@de,
        "The physicians who knew spells and roots could not rid him of his pains"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XX.21-33 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:definition "The case the twentieth lecture reports in the first person. The best physicians came to his help, who cure by their medical art and by spells, who were versed in their science and well knew spells and roots; they tried to cure him according to the fourfold science which they had been taught, but they could not rid him of his pains. Nor could his father with all he possessed, nor his mother, brothers, sisters or wife. He then resolved that if he once got rid of these great pains he would become a houseless monk; while he thought so he fell asleep, and after that night his pains had vanished."@en ;
    skos:note "A named craft named as having failed, inside the tradition that reports the case, with the practitioners described as competent and the remedy attempted in full. That is a rarer thing in this holding than a claim of success, and it is rarer still that the recovery is then reported as following a resolution and a night's sleep rather than any treatment."@en,
        "What the passage does not do is generalise. It is an argument for the helplessness of every protector, made in a lecture about the want of protection, and the physicians stand in it beside father, mother and wife. The failure of the craft is the premise and never the point, which is why it can be read as a report rather than as a polemic."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaVaiyavrittyaYieldsTirthakaragotra a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der Dienst bewirkt die Stellung eines Tīrthakara"@de,
        "Service brings about the station of a Tīrthakara"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX.43 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:JainaVaiyavrittya ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JainaTirthakaranamagotra .

pmc:JainaVedanaPurvakarma a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Weder erfreut über die Heilung noch sie verbietend"@de,
        "Neither pleased with the cure nor forbidding it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ācārāṅga Sūtra II.13.22-23 (Jacobi, SBE 22, Oxford 1884; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 1964)" ;
    skos:definition "The rule the thirteenth lecture of the second book gives for the monk who is being treated: one should neither be pleased with nor prohibit it if another tries to cure him by pure charms, or by impure charms, or by digging up and cutting living bulbs, roots, rind or sprouts for the sake of a sick monk. The reason the lecture gives is that sensation is the result of former actions and that all sorts of living beings experience sensation."@en ;
    skos:note "It stands beside the reported failure at pmc:JainaVaidyanamVifalata and the two do not sit easily together. There the craft is described as having been tried and having failed, which presupposes that an outcome was at stake; here no outcome is at stake at all. Both are entered because both are in the corpus, and the difference is in the literary form: a story admits a failure a doctrine cannot."@en,
        "The rule does not say that the charms fail; it says that the monk is to have no view about the outcome, because the sensation was determined before the treatment began. A doctrine of that shape removes every case from the reach of the question: a cure and a failure to cure are equally what the former actions required, and the transmission has said so in the sentence that gives the reason."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaYoganirodhakrama a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Erst der Geist, dann die Rede, dann der Leib, zuletzt der Atem"@de,
        "Mind, then speech, then body, then the breath"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX.72 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:definition "The order the seventy-second item states for the last stage: he first stops the functions of his mind, then the functions of speech, then those of the body, and at last he ceases to breathe."@en ;
    skos:note "Prescribed order and not stated necessity, and the reason is the same throughout this file: the lecture names the sequence and never names what happens to the man who takes it in another order. The strength is set at what the wording carries and the search for a stated consequence of omission stands at examinations/searches-jain.ttl."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:JainaPanchahrasvaksharakala ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:JainaBhaktapratyakhyana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JeuAdversariiCruoris a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Gruppe, die benannt, beschrieben und ausgeschlossen wird"@de,
        "The group that is named, described and excluded"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Das zweite Buch Jeû, Handschriftenseite 55, deutsche Übersetzung in Carl Schmidt, Gnostische Schriften in koptischer Sprache aus dem Codex Brucianus, Texte und Untersuchungen VIII 1-2, Leipzig 1892, S. 194" ;
    skos:definition "The one competing practice the text names: those who serve the eighth power of the great archon, that is, those who consume the blood of the menstruation of their uncleanness and the seed of men, saying, we possess the true knowledge and pray to the true God. The text answers that their god is evil, that he is the third power of the great archon, that his name is Taricheas son of Sabaoth Adamas, the enemy of the kingdom of heaven, and that his face is that of a swine, his teeth standing outside his mouth, with a second, lion's face behind."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as one side and not as a dispute, because only one side has been read. The other side is reported by a hostile outsider in Epiphanius, Panarion 26, and the two reports would have to be set beside each other at their own wordings before a pm:Disputing node could be written. What this node carries is that the transmission identifies a rival by its rite, assigns that rite a named god with a rank inside its own cosmology, and forbids passing the mysteries to its adherents."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu .

pmc:JeuAeonDuodecimus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der zwölfte Äon, und die Vorhänge vor dem unsichtbaren Gott"@de,
        "The twelfth aeon, and the curtains drawn before the invisible God"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Das zweite Buch Jeû, Handschriftenseiten 82-83, deutsche Übersetzung in Carl Schmidt, Gnostische Schriften in koptischer Sprache aus dem Codex Brucianus, Texte und Untersuchungen VIII 1-2, Leipzig 1892, S. 219-220" ;
    skos:definition "What the text places at the twelfth aeon: the invisible God, Barbelos and the unbegotten God; the invisible God alone in a place within the twelfth aeon, with curtains drawn before him; and many other gods in that aeon who are called archons in the light-treasure, the great archons who rule over all the aeons and who serve the invisible God, Barbelos and the Unbegotten. Its archons are named, sealed against, and scattered, and the text says that they scatter because the passer has received the twelve apologies of the twelve aeons."@en ;
    skos:note "The reason given for the scattering changes here. At the earlier stations the archons scatter because they hear the names; at the twelfth the text says they scatter because the twelve apologies have been received, that is, because of a completed set and not because of the formula of that station."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu .

pmc:JeuAeonTertiusDecimus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der dreizehnte Äon, und die vierundzwanzig, die zugreifen"@de,
        "The thirteenth aeon, and the twenty-four that step forward to seize"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Das zweite Buch Jeû, Handschriftenseiten 83-84, deutsche Übersetzung in Carl Schmidt, Gnostische Schriften in koptischer Sprache aus dem Codex Brucianus, Texte und Untersuchungen VIII 1-2, Leipzig 1892, S. 220" ;
    skos:definition "What the text places at the thirteenth aeon: the great invisible God, the great virginal spirit, and the twenty-four emanations of the invisible God. The twenty-four step before the passer in order to seize him on account of the mysteries he has received, and the text gives their imperishable names in order, the first to the twenty-fourth."@en ;
    skos:note "The ground of the seizure is the possession of the mysteries and not their lack, and it is stated in that form twice on the same leaf. Nothing that was read says what the twenty-four do if they succeed."@en,
        "The list is legible for the first seventeen names and then decays: Schmidt prints the eighteenth and the last three with gaps in the letters. This node therefore records that the list has twenty-four members and does not carry them."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu .

pmc:JeuChrismaNonTraditum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die geistige Salbung wird einmal angekündigt und nie gegeben"@de,
        "The spiritual anointing is announced once and never given"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Das zweite Buch Jeû, Handschriftenseiten 56-57 und 77, deutsche Übersetzung in Carl Schmidt, Gnostische Schriften in koptischer Sprache aus dem Codex Brucianus, Texte und Untersuchungen VIII 1-2, Leipzig 1892, S. 195 und 214" ;
    skos:definition "What the text promises and what it performs. The announcement lists, in one sentence, the three baptisms of water, fire and the holy spirit, then the mystery of removing the wickedness of the archons from the disciples, and then the mystery of the spiritual anointing. Four of the five are afterwards carried out and described in full. The anointing is never described, and the recapitulation, which lists what the disciples have received, names the mystery of the twelve aeons, the baptism of the water of life, the baptism of fire, the baptism of the holy spirit and the removal of the wickedness — and not the anointing."@en ;
    skos:note "A promise that the transmission itself drops is a different thing from a lacuna, and this witness does not let the two be told apart with certainty: the manuscript is damaged in places and its end is lost. What can be said from what was read is that the announcement occurs once, that the recapitulation four leaves later lists four rites and not five, and that the missing one is the one for which no apparatus is anywhere given. The search is at pmp:JeuChrismaSearch."@en,
        "It also corrects the premise of the issue that sent this run at the corpus. That issue names the three baptisms and the anointing together as the precondition of the ascent; the text performs the three baptisms and, in place of the anointing, a fourth rite against the wickedness of the archons. Whoever repeats the received account of this corpus repeats a rite the corpus does not carry."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu .

pmc:JeuCognitioYieldsLiberatio a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Erkennen des Wortes bringt die Freiheit vom Archon hervor"@de,
        "Knowing the word yields freedom from the ruler"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The First Book of Jeu, chapter 2 (trans. V. MacDermot), in the excerpt published by the Gnostic Society Library" ;
    skos:note "The mechanism given is not a passage and not a password. The ruler does not fight because he finds nothing of his own in the one he faces: what decides is a constitution and not a credential. That is the same reading the ophite speeches invite and never state, and here it is stated."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:JeuVerbumCognoscere ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JeuLiberatioAbArchonte .

pmc:JeuCustodesAdPortas a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die drei Wächter stehen am Tor und gehören zu keinem Rang"@de,
        "The three watchers stand at the gate and belong to no rank"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The First Book of Jeu, chapters 6 and 8 (trans. V. MacDermot), in the excerpt published by the Gnostic Society Library" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that a rank is set up corresponding to the treasuries and placed as watchers at the gate, three of them standing there, and that these watchers do not belong to the ranks of the treasuries of the light."@en ;
    skos:note "Read again in Schmidt's translation at the sixtieth treasury, where the number of watchers is given as nine and the rule behind it as three at each gate, each of them with a name of his own. Schmidt 1892, manuscript page 43, page 183."@en,
        "The exclusion is stated and is the interesting half. A gatekeeper who is not a member of what he guards cannot be passed by belonging to it, and that is precisely the move the ophite speeches make when the passer names what he already bears. The two corpora are in the same milieu and give the gate two different logics."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu .

pmc:JeuDynamisMissa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Eine Lichtkraft wird hinter den Aufsteigenden ausgesandt, und die Schätze ziehen sich zurück"@de,
        "A light-power is sent out behind the ascending and the treasuries withdraw"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Das zweite Buch Jeû, Handschriftenseite 76, deutsche Übersetzung in Carl Schmidt, Gnostische Schriften in koptischer Sprache aus dem Codex Brucianus, Texte und Untersuchungen VIII 1-2, Leipzig 1892, S. 213" ;
    skos:definition "The last station of the ascent that the text reaches, and it works unlike all the others. The imperishable names are invoked so that the true God may send out a great light-power to follow the twelve, since they have received the mystery of the forgiveness of sins and are therefore not to be held back from approaching the light-treasure; the true God sends out a power whose name is given; it comes out behind the disciples and makes the light-treasures and their ranks withdraw until the disciples pass into the inner and reach the treasury of the true God, who gives them his great mystery, his great seal and his great name. He then praises and invokes the unapproachable God, who thrusts out a light-power that comes to them at the place of the true God, gives them the character of that treasury, completes them in all fullness, and makes them a rank within it."@en ;
    skos:note "Every other station in the ascent has the passer produce something and the beings of the station withdraw. Here a power is sent out from above and behind him, and it is the power that makes the stations withdraw. The passer supplies nothing at this station, and the ground the text gives for the sending is not a token he holds but the mystery of the forgiveness of sins he is said to have received. That is the one place where the corpus makes the withheld mystery do the work the apparatus does everywhere else."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu .

pmc:JeuHomoCaelestis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der irdische Sinn hört auf, und ein himmlischer steht an seiner Stelle"@de,
        "The earthly mind ceases and a heavenly one stands in its place"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The First Book of Jeu, chapter 3 (trans. V. MacDermot), in the excerpt published by the Gnostic Society Library" ;
    skos:definition "The interpretation the living Jesus gives of sending the earth up to heaven: that he who hears the word of gnosis has ceased to have the understanding mind of a man of earth and has become a man of heaven, his understanding mind having ceased to be earthly and become heavenly; and that because of this he is saved from the ruler of this aeon, who becomes the Midst, because it is nothing."@en ;
    skos:note "A change of what someone is, stated as such and not as an achievement or a permission. The text is asked how heaven is brought down and answers by describing a state rather than an operation, twice in the same passage."@en,
        "The last clause resists paraphrase and is left as it stands: the ruler becomes the Midst, because it is nothing. Whether the Midst is a place, a rank or a privation is not decidable from what was read, and the corpus uses the term elsewhere in chapters that were not opened."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu .

pmc:JeuKakiaRemotaYieldsAthanasiam a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Beseitigung der Unbill der Archonten bringt Unsterblichkeit hervor"@de,
        "Removing the wickedness of the archons yields immortality"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Das zweite Buch Jeû, Handschriftenseiten 67-69, deutsche Übersetzung in Carl Schmidt, Gnostische Schriften in koptischer Sprache aus dem Codex Brucianus, Texte und Untersuchungen VIII 1-2, Leipzig 1892, S. 204-206" ;
    skos:note "The claim is not circular in the way the qi claims of this holding often are: the practice is a sealing and a prayer, and what it is said to produce is a change in what the disciples can do. It is also stated without a condition of any kind, which makes it the strongest single assertion in the corpus and the one with nothing attached by which it could be missed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:JeuMysteriumContraKakian ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JeuAthanasiaDiscipulorum .

pmc:JeuKautelIgnorantiae a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Das Gewand tragen, ohne ihn zu erkennen"@de,
        "Bearing the garment without knowing him"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The First Book of Jeu, chapter 4 (trans. V. MacDermot), in the excerpt published by the Gnostic Society Library" ;
    skos:definition "The warning that one who bears his virginity and his garment without understanding and knowing him, and who blasphemes his name, is given over to destruction, and has become an earthly son because he has not known the word with certainty."@en ;
    skos:note "It also answers a question the ophite material leaves open. There the passer shows a symbol and the gate opens; here having the garment and lacking the knowledge is an explicit case, and it goes badly. Whether the two corpora would agree about it is not asserted."@en,
        "The nearest thing to a condition of failure that the whole ascent material of this graph has produced so far, and it is worth being exact about how near. It names an outcome for a specific case — the bearer who does not know — and thereby says that bearing the garment is not sufficient. It does not name a sign by which the bearer or anyone else would recognise the case, so it still cannot be missed. The passage is damaged at this point in the excerpt read."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:JeuVerbumCognoscere ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu .

pmc:JeuMundumCrucifigere a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wer die Welt gekreuzigt hat"@de,
        "He who has crucified the world"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The First Book of Jeu, chapters 1 and 3 (trans. V. MacDermot), in the excerpt published by the Gnostic Society Library" ;
    skos:definition "The saying with which the teaching opens and the gloss given to it: blessed is he who has crucified the world and whom the world has not crucified; and he who has crucified it is he who has found the word of the speaker and has fulfilled it according to the will of him who sent him."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because the gloss identifies the act with a finding and a fulfilling and with nothing else — no rite, no seal, no name. In a corpus whose reputation rests entirely on its apparatus, the opening states the condition without any of it."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu .

pmc:JeuMysteriaNonTradenda a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Verratet sie an niemanden, und die Liste der Gemeinten"@de,
        "Betray them to no one, and the list of those meant"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Das zweite Buch Jeû, Handschriftenseiten 55-56, deutsche Übersetzung in Carl Schmidt, Gnostische Schriften in koptischer Sprache aus dem Codex Brucianus, Texte und Untersuchungen VIII 1-2, Leipzig 1892, S. 194-195" ;
    skos:definition "The command of secrecy as the text states it: keep these mysteries and betray them to no man unless he is worthy of them; not to father nor mother, not to brother nor sister nor kinsman, not for food nor drink, not for a woman, not for gold nor silver, nor for anything at all of this world, nor for the goods of this whole world; not to any woman or any man who stands in any faith in the seventy-two archons or who serves them; nor to those who serve the eighth power of the great archon; and do not tell them the place of the light and what is in it."@en ;
    skos:note "The restriction stands inside the operative text, in the mouth of the speaker who is about to perform the rites, and it names the class of permitted recipient in the same breath at pmp:JeuMysteriaTradere. It is not a rule imposed on the text from outside, and it is not a later gloss."@en,
        "What it does not carry is a consequence. The passage says what must not be done and does not say what happens to the one who does it, which is why this is a caution and not a prerequisite with a stated necessity. The nearest thing to a consequence anywhere in what was read is the warning of pmc:JeuKautelIgnorantiae, and that falls on the unknowing bearer and not on the betrayer. The search is at pmp:JeuPoenaProditionisSearch."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:JeuMysteriaTradere ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu .

pmc:JeuNomenMagnumYieldsTransitum a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Sagen des einen Namens öffnet alle Stationen zugleich"@de,
        "Saying the one name opens every station at once"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Das erste Buch Jeû, Handschriftenseiten 45-47, deutsche Übersetzung in Carl Schmidt, Gnostische Schriften in koptischer Sprache aus dem Codex Brucianus, Texte und Untersuchungen VIII 1-2, Leipzig 1892, S. 185-186" ;
    skos:note "The text sets this beside the station-by-station passage of pmc:JeuThesaurusTransitusForma and says nothing about the relation between them. It does not say that the one name replaces the sixty seals, and it does not say why a passer who has it would learn them. The sequence in the text runs the other way round: the disciples ask for the single name early, are told they must first be shown every place, and get it only after the whole survey."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:JeuNomenMagnum ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JeuTransitusOmniumThesaurorum .

pmc:JeuNumerusThesaurorum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sechzig Schätze, an beiden Enden benannt und in der Anrufung gezählt"@de,
        "Sixty treasuries, named at the two ends and counted in the invocation"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Das erste Buch Jeû, Handschriftenseiten 43 und 47, deutsche Übersetzung in Carl Schmidt, Gnostische Schriften in koptischer Sprache aus dem Codex Brucianus, Texte und Untersuchungen VIII 1-2, Leipzig 1892, S. 183 und 186" ;
    skos:definition "The count the text gives for the treasuries it has surveyed: the survey is declared complete from the treasury of the true God, whose name is given, to the treasury of Kazaezo; and the formula of the great name addresses the watchers of the sixty treasuries within and without, the ranks of the treasuries, the curtains of the treasuries and the places of the streets of their whole fatherhood."@en ;
    skos:note "The number is in the operative formula and not only in a hymn, which is what makes it citable. It is also the number against which what the manuscript actually carries is to be measured: the diagrams run to twenty-eight, the thirteenth is missing without a gap in the leaves, and the narrated passage covers only the last six or seven treasuries. Whatever the transmission claimed, the witness holds a small part of it."@en,
        "Two abbreviation signs stand in this sentence that Schmidt could not resolve, and he says so in a footnote. The sentence therefore gives the number sixty once and leaves a second place blank."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu .

pmc:JeuPraeceptaMoralia a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Was dem aufzutragen ist, der die Mysterien empfängt"@de,
        "What is to be laid on whoever receives the mysteries"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Das zweite Buch Jeû, Handschriftenseiten 57-58, deutsche Übersetzung in Carl Schmidt, Gnostische Schriften in koptischer Sprache aus dem Codex Brucianus, Texte und Untersuchungen VIII 1-2, Leipzig 1892, S. 196" ;
    skos:definition "The list the disciples are to impose before all things on the one to whom they give the mysteries: not to swear falsely nor to swear at all, not to fornicate, not to commit adultery, not to steal, not to covet another's goods, not to love silver nor gold, not to invoke the name of the archons nor the name of their angels for any matter, not to rob, not to curse, not to slander falsely nor to accuse; but their yes shall be yes and their no no, and in a word they shall carry out the good commandments."@en ;
    skos:note "One item in the list is not ethical and is the reason the whole list is here: the prohibition on invoking the name of the archons or of their angels for any matter. A handbook that teaches the naming of archons at every station of an ascent forbids the naming of the same beings for any worldly purpose, and it puts that prohibition in the same list as theft and perjury."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu .

pmc:JeuPsephoiStationum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Ziffern der Stationen, wie die Ausgabe sie druckt"@de,
        "The ciphers of the stations, as the edition prints them"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Das zweite Buch Jeû, Handschriftenseiten 77-86, deutsche Übersetzung in Carl Schmidt, Gnostische Schriften in koptischer Sprache aus dem Codex Brucianus, Texte und Untersuchungen VIII 1-2, Leipzig 1892, S. 215-223" ;
    skos:definition "The numbers the text assigns to the stations that were read, each to be grasped with the hands at its own station: 1119 at the first aeon, 2219 at the second, 4559 at the tenth, 5558 at the eleventh, 9885 at the twelfth, and 4554 at the place of the archons of the light beyond the fourteenth aeon; besides these, 9879 at all three baptisms, called the cipher of the seven voices, 530 at the mystery against the wickedness of the archons, called the cipher of the first Amen, and 7856 with the one great name of the first book."@en ;
    skos:note "No rule relates them. They are not a series, they are not derived from the number of the station, and the text states no consequence for holding a wrong one. What it does state is where each belongs and that it is to be in the hands while the name is said."@en,
        "The figures are Schmidt's reading of Coptic-Greek letter numerals, and he marks several of the ones from the stations that were not read with a question mark of his own. The two ciphers of the first book he leaves untransliterated. A node that gave these numbers without saying that would be claiming a precision the witness does not have."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu .

pmc:JeuRemissioYieldsPurificationem a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der einmalige Vollzug bringt die Austilgung jeder Sünde hervor"@de,
        "The single performance yields the blotting out of every sin"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Das zweite Buch Jeû, Handschriftenseite 58, deutsche Übersetzung in Carl Schmidt, Gnostische Schriften in koptischer Sprache aus dem Codex Brucianus, Texte und Untersuchungen VIII 1-2, Leipzig 1892, S. 197" ;
    skos:note "The practice this points at is the one the corpus does not transmit. That is not a defect of the node but its content: a yielding claim whose practice is withheld is exactly what a transmission looks like when the written apparatus is declared insufficient."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:JeuMysteriumRemissionis ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JeuRemissioOmniumPeccatorum .

pmc:JeuSignaTriaPetita a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Drei erbetene Zeichen, drei berichtete Zeichen, und kein Wort für ihr Ausbleiben"@de,
        "Three signs asked for, three signs reported, and no word for their absence"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Das zweite Buch Jeû, Handschriftenseite 66, deutsche Übersetzung in Carl Schmidt, Gnostische Schriften in koptischer Sprache aus dem Codex Brucianus, Texte und Untersuchungen VIII 1-2, Leipzig 1892, S. 204",
        "Das zweite Buch Jeû, Handschriftenseiten 61-62, deutsche Übersetzung in Carl Schmidt, Gnostische Schriften in koptischer Sprache aus dem Codex Brucianus, Texte und Untersuchungen VIII 1-2, Leipzig 1892, S. 199-200",
        "Das zweite Buch Jeû, Handschriftenseiten 64-65, deutsche Übersetzung in Carl Schmidt, Gnostische Schriften in koptischer Sprache aus dem Codex Brucianus, Texte und Untersuchungen VIII 1-2, Leipzig 1892, S. 202" ;
    skos:definition "In the second, the prayer asks, on the same condition, for a sign in the fire of the fragrant incense; and in the same moment the sign happened in the fire. In the third, it asks, on the same condition, for a sign in the offering; and in the same moment the wonder happened. In none of the three does the text say what the absence of the sign would show, or what is to be done if it does not come."@en,
        "The form each of the three baptisms takes at its decisive point. In the first, the prayer asks that, if the Father has heard, has had pity, has counted the disciples among the heirs of the kingdom of light, has forgiven their sins and blotted out their misdeeds, then a wonder may happen and Zorokothora come and bring the water of the baptism of life out into one of the wine vessels; and in the same moment the wonder happened and the wine to the right of the sacrifice became water."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also an event no party to the rite can produce at will — wine becoming water, a sign in a fire, a sign in an offering — and it is announced to the participants in advance, in the prayer they hear. That combination is what makes this passage the case which the protocol at examinations/both-sides-of-the-criterion.ttl had named as its own falsifying condition and had not found. The search behind the negative half is at pmp:JeuSignumAbsensSearch."@en,
        "The conditional is the whole point and it runs one way only. The sign is asked for as the evidence that the petition has been granted, so a sign that failed to come would report that the sins were not forgiven; the text draws that consequence nowhere, contemplates the case nowhere, and prescribes nothing for it. What it does instead is narrate the successful performance in the same passage that prescribes the rite, three times."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu .

pmc:JeuThesauriStructura a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Was jeder Rang der Schatzkammern trägt"@de,
        "What each rank of the treasuries carries"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The First Book of Jeu, chapters 5-10 (trans. V. MacDermot), in the excerpt published by the Gnostic Society Library" ;
    skos:definition "The repeating unit of the account: each Jeu has a name, a character, three watchers, and twelve emanations; there are twelve ranks in each treasury, six heads on one side and six on the other turned towards each other, twelve heads in each rank and twelve in each emanation, and the ranks stand one outside the other endlessly."@en ;
    skos:note "The count that circulates for this corpus, sixty treasuries, did not come from anything read when this node was written. It has since been read at two places in the text, once as the end of the survey and once inside the operative formula of the great name, and it stands at pmc:JeuNumerusThesaurorum."@en,
        "The unit repeats without variation through the chapters that were read, and the excerpt states that it goes on repeating for the twenty-four chapters that were not. Whatever else this text is, it is an inventory, and an inventory of this shape presupposes that someone was meant to hold the entries apart."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu .

pmc:JeuVoxEmanans a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Stimme ist die Emanation, nicht ihre Anrufung"@de,
        "The voice is the emanation, not an invocation of it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The First Book of Jeu, chapters 5-6 (trans. V. MacDermot), in the excerpt published by the Gnostic Society Library" ;
    skos:definition "The account of how the ranks come to be: a power of the Father moves the true God and radiates within him, he gives voice, and when he had given voice there came forth this voice which is the emanation; the three lines drawn in the diagram are said to be the voices he gives out when he is commanded to sing praises to the Father, so that he brings forth emanations."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the strongest thing the excerpt carries and it is easy to lose in the diagrams. The voice does not summon the emanation and does not name it: the voice is it. A tradition that holds this cannot treat a spoken name as a key to something else, and it puts the vowel chains of this corpus in a different class from the addresses of the ophite gates before any comparison of their sounds. See prima-materia#607."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu .

pmc:JhanaPresupposesAbandoningTheHindrances a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Vertiefung setzt das Aufgeben der Hemmnisse voraus"@de,
        "Absorption presupposes the abandoning of the hindrances"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)" ;
    skos:note "The discourse places the abandoning of the five immediately before entry into the first absorption in a fixed formula, without stating at that place what befalls one who enters otherwise."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:FourAbsorptions ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DighaNikaya ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:FiveHindrances ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:JiaqiuWaiwu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Festigkeit, von einem äußeren Ding geborgt"@de,
        "Firmness borrowed from an outer thing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 4 (Jindan), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 4" ;
    skos:definition "The mechanism the chapter states. Gold entering the fire is not consumed by a hundred smeltings, and buried to the end of heaven it does not decay; the golden elixir, the longer it is burnt the finer its transformations. Taking these two drugs refines the body, and so makes a man neither age nor die. This, the chapter says expressly, is to seek from an outer thing in order to make oneself firm: as fat feeds a fire so that it does not go out, and as copper-green smeared on the feet keeps them from rotting in water, borrowing the strength of the copper to guard the flesh. But the elixir, entering the body, soaks the constructive and the defensive qi, and is not merely an outward application like the copper-green."@en ;
    skos:note "The argument is a transfer of a property, and it names its own weakest point in the act of making it. Both illustrations are of surfaces: the fat is outside the flame it feeds, the copper-green outside the foot it guards. The chapter sees this and answers it by saying that the elixir goes inside, which asserts the disanalogy it was asked about rather than removing it."@en,
        "This is the claim that the whole file rests on, and it is the one place where an examination could take hold without waiting out a term: it says that an ingested mineral confers on tissue the incorruptibility the mineral has in fire and in earth. It is stated as a mechanism and not as a report, and nothing in the chapter offers a case of it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Waidan .

pmc:JibaroMaikoaDivination a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Antwort vor dem Angriff, und die Hälfte, die niemand prüft"@de,
        "The answer before the attack, and the half of it nobody tests"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Rafael Karsten, Blood Revenge, War, and Victory Feasts among the Jibaro Indians of Eastern Ecuador, Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 79 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1923), pp. 16-17" ;
    skos:definition "Those preparing a feud never omit to consult the spirits first, and the spirits let them know whether the planned attack will succeed. After the three days and nights of fasting in the dreaming ranch the arutama speak and tell the warrior whether he will be able to kill the enemy or whether he will perhaps be killed himself. The two answers are printed as sentences: that sorcerer who has bewitched your father you will conquer and kill; or, if you make war against that sorcerer now, you will not succeed in killing him, but you will be killed yourself."@en ;
    skos:note "A second divination stands beside it and is of the opposite kind. When a chief or a great warrior dies, a medicine-man drinking natema establishes not only that the death was caused by witchcraft but who the wizard is, and the killing follows from that finding. Nothing in the work states how such an identification could be wrong, and the whole feud system rests on it."@en,
        "The asymmetry is not a reproach. A man who is told he will die and attacks anyway would be testing an oracle with his life, and no transmission in this holding asks that. What makes it worth a node is that the asymmetry is structural: the answers that could fail are filtered out by the rule for acting on them, so a perfect record is what this procedure would produce whether or not it had any power."@en,
        "This is as near a prediction as anything in this holding comes. The outcome is public, settled within days, and not in the operator's gift: either the enemy is dead or he is not. And the transmission removes one of the two answers from the reckoning in the same paragraph — in the unfavourable case the avenger desists from his plan, at least for the present, so the prediction that he would be killed is never put to the event. What remains checkable is the favourable answer alone, which is the half that gets acted on."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:KarstenBloodRevenge ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JibaroShuara .

pmc:JibaroMaikoaYieldsArutama a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der Trank und die drei Tage bringen die Alten"@de,
        "The drink and the three days bring the Old Ones"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Rafael Karsten, Blood Revenge, War, and Victory Feasts among the Jibaro Indians of Eastern Ecuador, Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 79 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1923), pp. 2-3 and 17" ;
    skos:note "Stated with a condition rather than as an effect, and the condition is the finding: if the dreams are good the arutama appear. So an operation that produces no dream, or a bad one, is covered in advance and is not a case of the drink having failed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:JibaroMaikoaUmartinyu ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by Rafael Karsten among the Jibaro of the Pastaza, Morona and Upano-Santiago in eastern Ecuador and published by the Bureau of American Ethnology in 1923, the manuscript transmitted in February 1921. No informant is named at this place and no date of gathering is given; the account is the recorder's own and the Jibaro terms, maikoa and arutama and ayámdai, are carried in his transcription beside the English, with no continuous text in the language."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:KarstenBloodRevenge ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JibaroShuara ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JibaroArutamaSeen .

pmc:JibaroNatemaOmen a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Was den Trinkern angesagt wird, dass sie es sehen werden"@de,
        "What the drinkers are told they will see"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Rafael Karsten, Blood Revenge, War, and Victory Feasts among the Jibaro Indians of Eastern Ecuador, Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 79 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1923), pp. 72-73" ;
    skos:definition "The object of the drinking of the natema at the trophy feast is to ascertain whether everything will turn out favourably for the slayer — whether he will have a long life, attain material prosperity and be lucky in his undertakings. What the slayer and his relatives are to see in the dream is stated in advance: his house surrounded by large and flourishing plantations of manioc and bananas, his swine and chickens numerous and fat; and the spirit shows him the tracks of the swine and lets him hear their grunting. The drinkers are said to be benefited for their own part as well, purified of impure and disease-bringing matters and gaining strength for their work. After sleeping until the afternoon they bathe in the river, return, and tell the older Indians what dreams they have had."@en ;
    skos:note "Held apart from the conditions of failure as SPEC §15 requires, and this one shows exactly why the separation is needed. The content of the favourable dream is announced before the drink is taken, the dreamer reports it himself afterwards to the old men, and the thing he is to see is the same thing the trophy is said to bring about. A sign in that position can only confirm."@en,
        "One detail makes it worse rather than better. Every drinker empties three dishes and vomits each one, so the dose that acts is unknown and uncontrolled, and a drinker who sees nothing has an explanation to hand that the account does not need to supply."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:KarstenBloodRevenge ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JibaroShuara .

pmc:JibaroPrickingNotToDie a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Stechen am Abend der Tötung"@de,
        "The pricking on the evening of the killing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Rafael Karsten, Blood Revenge, War, and Victory Feasts among the Jibaro Indians of Eastern Ecuador, Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 79 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1923), p. 34" ;
    skos:note "Entered because the rule names the mechanism by which it works and the mechanism is a sentence the practitioner's own soul is to be able to say. The sores are not a sign of success but the means: they are what makes the refusal true. A protocol is not available — the event is a dream — but the dependency is stated with unusual precision, and the step that is left out is one an observer could see being done."@en,
        "The prior step named here is formal. The pricking does not depend on the making of a shield; it depends on there having been a killing, and this holding has no step for that. The dependency the node states is in the consequence clause, which is where the text puts it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "On the evening of the day the enemies have been killed, every warrior who took part pricks himself slightly over the whole body — arms, legs, shoulders, breast and stomach — with a pointed blowpipe arrow, and this is held to protect him against the spirit of the killed enemy. In the dream the slayer meets the spirit, who says: come, let us dance together, and his own soul answers: no, I cannot dance, for I have my body full of sores. If he has not pricked himself in the evening he will in the dream obey the exhortation to dance, and the consequence will be that the warrior soon dies. The Jibaros say that they prick themselves in order not to die."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:JibaroIhambratinyu ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by Rafael Karsten among the Jibaro of eastern Ecuador and published by the Bureau of American Ethnology in 1923. No informant is named and no date of gathering is given; the exchange in the dream is printed in English only and the Jibaro wording is not carried at this place."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:KarstenBloodRevenge ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:JibaroTandaraMaking ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JibaroShuara .

pmc:JibaroSoulInTheMadeObject a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Was ein Mann isst und tut, geht in das Ding über, das er macht"@de,
        "What a man eats and does passes into the thing he makes"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Rafael Karsten, Blood Revenge, War, and Victory Feasts among the Jibaro Indians of Eastern Ecuador, Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 79 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1923), pp. 13-14" ;
    skos:definition "The Jibaro is reported as not distinguishing his own person from the things he has made himself. Making a shield, a drum, a blowpipe or another delicate object, he has to diet and observe abstinence, because he puts something of his own person, his own soul, into the object; and his properties — the essential and habitual ones and those occasionally acquired by eating a certain food — are thereby transferred to that object. The same principle is stated of persons: when one member of a family is sick the rest have to keep the same diet, for if they eat unsuitable food it is as if the patient had eaten it and his condition would grow worse; and after a child is born the parents fast and observe abstinence for a couple of years, until the child is named."@en ;
    skos:note "Reported by the recorder as the Indians' own view and in his vocabulary of souls and persons, so the wording of the principle is his. What is not his are the three consequences, each of which he gives as a rule the Jibaros keep."@en,
        "The mechanism behind the shield rule and worth having separately, because it is a general claim with several distinct consequences and because two of them are checkable in different ways. A shield is inspected; a sick relative either worsens or does not, and the rest of the family's diet is a matter of record in a household."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:KarstenBloodRevenge ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JibaroShuara .

pmc:JibaroTandaraAbstinence a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Schild setzt das Fasten und die Enthaltsamkeit seines Verfertigers voraus"@de,
        "The shield presupposes the fast and the continence of the man making it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Rafael Karsten, Blood Revenge, War, and Victory Feasts among the Jibaro Indians of Eastern Ecuador, Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 79 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1923), pp. 6-7" ;
    skos:note "The prior step named here is the slayer's fasting, which is the only other observance of the same kind in this file. The dependency the text states is on the maker's own fast and continence during the days of the work, and those days are not a practice of their own in this holding."@en,
        "The reason this node exists. Almost everything this holding carries puts the consequence of a broken rule inside the practitioner — he falls ill, he dies, the power does not come, the spirits withdraw — and none of that is settled by anyone but him. Here the consequence is the state of a wooden disc: split, warped, worm-eaten or sound. It is announced before the work begins, it arrives within days, and anybody can look. Of the material read in this run it is the only claim that would cost nothing to put to the test."@en,
        "The recorder adds an explanation of his own to the first prohibition — that the belief is due to the sweet potato having the same appearance as moth-eaten or rotten wood — and calls the rule a superstition. His explanation is his; the rule and its stated consequence are reported as the transmission's."@en,
        "What weakens it, and it should be said in the same place. All three outcomes named are the ordinary ways a piece of green tropical wood fails, so a shield that splits confirms the rule and a shield that holds confirms the observance, and nobody in the account is reported as having kept a count. The claim is testable in form and has not been tested; that is the difference this holding exists to show."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Named for each prohibition separately and in terms of the object. If the maker eats the sweet potato called inchi the shield will be attacked by moth, rot and become useless. If he lies with a woman during those days the shield will burst or get crooked. For the Canelos the same work and the same three prohibitions — the forenoon fast, the strict silence, the continence through four days and nights — carry one joint consequence: if he infringes these rules the shield will get humpy or burst, or be attacked by moth."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:JibaroTandaraMaking ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by Rafael Karsten among the Jibaro of eastern Ecuador and, for the second set of rules, among the Quichua-speaking Canelos, published by the Bureau of American Ethnology in 1923. No informant is named and no date of gathering is given; the Jibaro and Canelos names of the shield wood, kamaka and lanchama, and the Jibaro names of the shield and of the sweet potato, tandara and inchi, are carried in the recorder's transcription."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:KarstenBloodRevenge ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:JibaroIhambratinyu ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JibaroShuara .

pmc:JibaroTsantsaYieldsPlenty a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das gehaltene Fasten bringt die Mehrung, das gebrochene den Verlust"@de,
        "The kept fast brings the increase, and the broken one the loss"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Rafael Karsten, Blood Revenge, War, and Victory Feasts among the Jibaro Indians of Eastern Ecuador, Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 79 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1923), pp. 46-47" ;
    skos:note "The structure in full, because it is the most complete one this run found. An operation; a term of about two years set by the growth of a pig; an outcome anyone in the household can see; a failure named as plainly as the success; and a cause assigned to the failure that is always the practitioner's negligence in the fasting, so that a bad harvest is evidence about the man and never about the trophy. The account says in as many words that it is of paramount importance that the ceremonies be carried out properly and that the slayer continue strictly to observe the rules, and that calamities are the operations of the revengeful spirit against whom sufficient precautions have not been taken."@en,
        "What would have to be done with it. The base rate is knowable here in a way it almost never is: pigs and manioc have ordinary rates of thriving and failing, and the claim is of an extraordinary force beyond the normal. A protocol would compare households with a trophy against households without, and the work reports no such comparison and no count."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:JibaroIhambratinyu ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by Rafael Karsten among the Jibaro of eastern Ecuador and published by the Bureau of American Ethnology in 1923. No informant is named and no date of gathering is given; the terms tsantsa, wakáni and suamartinyu are carried in the recorder's transcription beside the English, and the account of the two outcomes is the recorder's prose."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:KarstenBloodRevenge ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JibaroShuara ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JibaroTsantsaPlenty .

pmc:JibaroWakaniTakesHisLife a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Leben des Töters setzt das Halten seines Fastens voraus"@de,
        "The slayer's life presupposes his keeping of the fast"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Rafael Karsten, Blood Revenge, War, and Victory Feasts among the Jibaro Indians of Eastern Ecuador, Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 79 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1923), pp. 19 and 34" ;
    skos:note "A long observance, a counted term of two or three months, and one named penalty for the whole of it. The penalty is the death of the slayer, which arrives at no stated time and has no stated upper bound, so a slayer who breaks the diet and lives has not refuted anything: the avenging soul follows him constantly and the account sets no date by which it must have acted."@en,
        "Set beside pmc:JibaroTandaraAbstinence this is the contrast the two nodes exist for. Both are observances of fasting and continence; one is answered by a piece of wood within days and the other by the practitioner's eventual death. The transmission has both forms and uses the checkable one on an object and the uncheckable one on a man."@en,
        "The prior step named here is the maikoa drinking, because the fast begins from the killing that the divination precedes. It is the order the work gives and not a dependency of efficacy."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "If the rules are infringed by the slayer, the soul of the killed enemy, who constantly follows him thirsting for revenge, will take his life. The same is stated of two details of the diet: the food on the way home would become impure if the slayers touched it with hands polluted with blood, and they would expose themselves to death."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:JibaroIhambratinyu ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by Rafael Karsten among the Jibaro of eastern Ecuador and published by the Bureau of American Ethnology in 1923. No informant is named and no date of gathering is given; the names of the dishes and of the men's room, nauma, sangu, ambi, yankipi, kayuka, chumakai shachma and tangámasha, are carried in the recorder's transcription beside the English."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:KarstenBloodRevenge ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:JibaroMaikoaUmartinyu ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JibaroShuara .

pmc:JindanFeiyong a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Was die Unsterblichkeit kostet, und die kleinste Menge, die gelingt"@de,
        "What immortality costs, and the smallest batch that works"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 4 (Jindan), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 4" ;
    skos:definition "The chapter prices the operation. For the golden liquor only the gold is hard to get: a catty by the old weight is two catties by the present one and costs not more than some three hundred thousand; the accessory drugs are easier to assemble, and no fire is raised, so that the days in the flowery pool suffice. Altogether about four hundred thousand yields one preparation, and one preparation is enough to make eight men immortal. Against that stands a rule of scale: those who compound somewhat less than the stated amount find that its force is not sufficient to effect the transformation, as brewing a few sheng of rice will certainly yield no wine."@en ;
    skos:note "A stated price is a rare thing to find in this collection and it does two things at once. It puts the operation inside the economy, where it can be reached by anyone with the sum, and it explains why the author of the chapter never reached it. He says elsewhere in the same chapter that he received the scriptures more than twenty years ago and had not so much as a picul of means to do it with."@en,
        "The minimum-batch rule closes the cheapest way to attempt the operation and does so with an analogy that is true of brewing. It is also a second condition under which a failed run says nothing about the recipe, since a run that failed can be assigned to a batch that was too small."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Waidan .

pmc:JingmaiQijue a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Abreißen des Leitbahn-Qi, sein Zeichen und sein Stammtag"@de,
        "The severing of a channel's qi, its sign and its stem day"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 10 (Jing mai), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 3" ;
    skos:definition "For each of five yin channels a sign of the qi having been severed, a part of the body said to die first, and a pair of stem days on which the case grows grave and on which death follows, each with a reason from the sequence in which the phases overcome one another. Hand taiyin: skin and hair scorched, the hair breaking, the hair dies first; grave at bing, dead at ding, fire overcoming metal. Hand shaoyin: the vessel not passing, the face black as lacquered kindling, the blood dies first; grave at ren, dead at gui, water over fire. Foot taiyin: the flesh soft, the tongue withered, the lips turned back, the flesh dies first; grave at jia, dead at yi, wood over earth. Foot shaoyin: the bone dried, the teeth grown long and foul, the hair without lustre, the bone dies first; grave at wu, dead at ji, earth over water. Foot jueyin: the sinews taut, the lips blue and the tongue curled and the testicles drawn up, the sinews die first; grave at geng, dead at xin, metal over wood. Two collective cases follow: all five yin severed together, the eye connection turns and the eyes roll, the intent dies first, and death is at most a day and a half away; the six yang severed, yin and yang part, the interstices open and the sweat of dying comes out, and one reckoned in the morning dies in the evening, one reckoned in the evening dies in the morning."@en ;
    skos:note "The interest of this passage for the graph is that it shows the corpus deriving a term rather than reporting one. The day is not a number of days from the observation; it is a named day of the sexagenary cycle, and the text gives the derivation openly in each case, from the phase that overcomes the phase of the channel. Whatever the intervals of Suwen 52 are, these are not counted intervals, and the chapter does not pretend otherwise."@en,
        "The two collective cases are of the other kind. A day and a half, and the morning-for-evening rule, are stated as durations from the finding, with no calendar in them. One chapter therefore carries both forms, which is the comparison a protocol on the intervals of this corpus would have to begin from."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmc:JinyeFenliang a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Goldsaft, nach Unzen"@de,
        "The golden liquor, by the ounce"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 4 (Jindan), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 4" ;
    skos:definition "For the golden liquor the dose is graded and each grade has its own outcome. A hundred days of compounding make it. One ounce taken, and one is an immortal. Half an ounce, and one has long life without death, and the ten thousand harms and the hundred poisons cannot injure; and at that grade one may keep a wife and children, hold office and rank, and do as one wishes, with nothing forbidden. Whoever then wishes to ascend fasts again and takes the further ounce, and flies off. Whoever wishes to ascend to heaven at once must first cut off grain for a year before taking it. The scripture adds a sign: when the golden liquor enters the mouth, the body becomes gold in colour."@en ;
    skos:note "The colour of the body is the only immediately observable sign the chapter names for any of its preparations. Everything else is a term to be waited out; this one is a report a bystander could make on the day. It is also the item most easily produced by the preparation itself rather than by any effect of it."@en,
        "The half-ounce grade is the part of this file that a modern reader will most easily misplace. It does not describe a lesser immortal but a stated condition in which the ordinary life goes on unchanged and no prohibition applies. A transmission that names a dose at which its own discipline lapses is saying something about what the discipline was for, and it is the opposite of what the rest of the chapter demands."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Waidan .

pmc:JiudanMingshu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die neun Elixiere, jedes mit Namen, Gabe und Frist"@de,
        "The nine elixirs, each with its name, its dose and its term"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 4 (Jindan), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 4" ;
    skos:definition "Nine preparations are named in order, and each carries a term. The first, Cinnabar Flower, is made in thirty-six days and taken; in seven days one is an immortal. The second, Divine Elixir, also called Divine Tally: in a hundred days; three knife-points destroy the three corpses and the nine worms and heal the hundred illnesses, and smeared under the feet it carries one over water. The third, also Divine Elixir: one knife-point, a hundred days, and jade women and the spirits of mountains and rivers attend in human shape; given to the six domestic animals they too do not die. The fourth, Returned Elixir: one knife-point, a hundred days; written with it above a man's eyes, the hundred demons flee. The fifth, Bait Elixir: thirty days. The sixth, Smelted Elixir: ten days. The seventh, Pliant Elixir: one knife-point, a hundred days, and taken with the juice of quepen it lets a man of ninety father children. The eighth, Prostrate Elixir: the same day. The ninth, Cold Elixir: one knife-point, a hundred days, and one flies without wings. Of these nine, the chapter says, one need only obtain a single one to become an immortal; making all of them is a matter of preference and not of necessity."@en ;
    skos:note "The clause that any one of the nine suffices is what makes the list examinable rather than a counsel of perfection. A transmission that required the whole set could meet every failure by naming the elixir that was not made; this one has closed that exit in advance, in its own words."@en,
        "The terms run from the same day to a hundred days, and the ordering of the list does not follow them. The eighth acts on the day it is taken and the ninth takes a hundred, so the sequence is not a scale of potency, and whatever orders the nine is not stated."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Waidan .

pmc:JiuhouSishi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Todesstunde, aus dem Puls und seiner Jahreszeit gelesen"@de,
        "The hour of death read from the pulse and the season it stands for"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 20 (San bu jiu hou lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 6" ;
    skos:definition "Where all nine positions of the pulse are sunken, fine and cut off, that is yin and rules winter, so death comes at midnight; where they are full, restless and hurried, that is yang and rules summer, so death comes at noon. Then by illness: one with a cold-and-heat illness dies at first light; heat within and heat illness, at noon; wind illness, at evening; water illness, at midnight; and where the pulse is now sparse now frequent, now slow now quick, death comes on the day that rides the four seasons. The chapter adds two rules that cut across the scheme: where the flesh is already gone, the patient dies although the nine positions are in order; and where the seven signs appear but the nine positions all run with the grain, he does not die."@en ;
    skos:note "The derivation is stated and is the point. The hour is not observed and then recorded; it is read off a correspondence, pulse to yin or yang, yin or yang to a season, season to an hour of the day. The chapter says so in the order of its own clauses, which is why this is entered beside the stem days of Lingshu 10 rather than beside the day counts of Suwen 52."@en,
        "The second of the two crossing rules is the more interesting and the harder to use. It says that the seven signs may be present and the patient not die, provided the nine positions run with the grain, and the chapter immediately qualifies even that by naming illnesses that resemble the seven signs and are not them. A prognosis with an exception whose scope is settled by the prognosticator is what the graph holds here."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmc:JiuzhuanRishu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die neun Umwandlungen und die Tage, die sie sparen"@de,
        "The nine cyclings and the days they save"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 4 (Jindan), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 4" ;
    skos:definition "For the Great Clarity elixir the chapter gives a table in which the labour of preparation and the wait after taking stand in inverse proportion. The elixir of one cycling, taken, gives immortality in three years; of two cyclings, in two years; of three, in one year; of four, in half a year; of five, in a hundred days; of six, in forty days; of seven, in thirty days; of eight, in ten days; of nine, in three days. The chapter then states the reason: where the cyclings are few the days of firing are many and the drug's force is not sufficient, so that taking it uses many days and immortality comes slowly; where the cyclings are many the drug's force is complete, so that taking it uses few days and immortality comes fast."@en ;
    skos:note "The relation is stated as inverse but not as proportional, and the numbers are not. Three years, two years, one year, half a year, a hundred days, forty, thirty, ten, three: the fall is steep at the top and shallow in the middle, and no rule generates it. What the chapter offers as a reason accounts for the direction of the table and not for its values."@en,
        "The same passage carries the chapter's own answer to the charge of empty writing: were these empty writings, it asks, how could the nine cyclings and nine transformations come out by the counted days exactly as the recipe says. The check offered is therefore the agreement of the process with its own timetable and not the arrival of the outcome, which is a lower bar than the one the table itself sets."@en,
        "This is the most quantitatively committed claim located in this collection. It is not a list of outcomes but a stated law relating a preparation variable to an interval, with a mechanism given for the relation, and both ends of the scale named. Anything of this shape can be got wrong in a way that a catalogue of capacities cannot."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Waidan .

pmc:Kandu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kandu, das Zeichen, das den Fluch ankündigt"@de,
        "Kandu, the token that announces a curse"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), p. 239" ;
    skos:definition "A hoe handle with cassava cores tied to it, a stick daubed with colours, an old basket hung in a fruit tree: things set where property is to be protected. The corpus states that these are not themselves charms but tokens or warnings, put up to inform a thief that a curse lies on the thing and what complaint he will get if he risks it, and it gives the reason in the form of a rule of fairness, that it is not considered fair to curse the stealing of an article without indicating it in some way."@en ;
    skos:note "A stated rule of fairness inside an operative system is rare in this graph and is the reason for the node. It also has a consequence for what the transmission can claim: a curse that must be announced in advance is one whose effects the announcement itself could produce, and the corpus records elsewhere the case of a man who could not eat what his inherited prohibition forbade only so long as he feared it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:KongoNkisi .

pmc:KasdejaTeachesStrikingOfSpirits a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Kasdeja offenbart das Schlagen der Geister"@de,
        "Kasdeja discloses the striking of spirits"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 69:12 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
        "J.T. Milik, The Books of Enoch: Aramaic Fragments of Qumrân Cave 4 (Oxford, 1976)" ;
    skos:note "Book of Parables; no Aramaic fragment of chapters 37-71 is attested at Qumran."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:StrikingOfSpirits ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Kasdeja ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:KaulaAboveTheOtherWays a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die kaulische Verehrung als der in diesem Zeitalter tragende Weg"@de,
        "Kaula worship as the way that succeeds in this age"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Mahānirvāṇa Tantra IV.20 (trans. A. Avalon, Tantra of the Great Liberation, London 1913)",
        "Mahānirvāṇa Tantra XIV.203 (trans. A. Avalon, Tantra of the Great Liberation, London 1913)" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that in the present age success is attained by Kaula worship alone, and that the mantra received from a Kaula brings knowledge of Brahman, where the mantra received from one of the lower dispositions reproduces that disposition in the receiver."@en ;
    skos:note "Kaula is set above the other ways here, not beside them. That is the same order Wilson translates from a passage he ascribes to the Kulārṇava, in which the left hand stands above the right and Kaula above all — an order the Western pair of names keeps while reversing its valuation. Two witnesses, one of them located by chapter and verse in a named translation and one not; the second is still to be found. See prima-materia#56."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmc:KevalaKumbhakaHoldsOwnAgency a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Das alleinige Anhalten behält die eigene Trägerschaft"@de,
        "Retention alone holds its own agency"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.74 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:ownAgency ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:KevalaKumbhaka ;
    pm:situationGround "The verse has the practitioner able to hold the breath at his own pleasure, and what follows is credited to that holding. Nobody else acts in these verses: there is no descent, no possession and no conferral, only a capacity said to be at the practitioner's disposal once the practice has taken."@en .

pmc:KevalaKumbhakaIsByEffort a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Das alleinige Anhalten wird durch Anstrengung erlangt"@de,
        "Retention alone is attained by effort"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.72-73 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:byEffort ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:KevalaKumbhaka ;
    pm:situationGround "The verse directs that the accompanied retention be practised for as long as it takes until the unaccompanied one succeeds, and the next verse names the condition by the success: it is called retention alone once it can be done without the emptying and the filling. The manual gives no other way to it, and the counted regimen of the same chapter sets a number of retentions to be performed at four times of the day."@en .

pmc:KevalaKumbhakaIsWithdrawnAbsorption a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Das alleinige Anhalten liegt bei abgezogener Vertiefung"@de,
        "Retention alone is placed at withdrawn absorption"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.71-74 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:withdrawnAbsorption ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:KevalaKumbhaka ;
    pm:situationGround "Retention held at will without the filling and the emptying that ordinarily frame it. A suspension of the breath held as the accomplished form of the practice is the case this coordinate names by its own definition; the practitioner is awake and the ordinary sensory traffic has stopped being the point."@en .

pmc:KevalaKumbhakaLocusNotStated a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Das alleinige Anhalten gibt keinen Ort an"@de,
        "Retention alone states no locus"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.71-74 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:locusNotStated ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:KevalaKumbhaka ;
    pm:situationGround "The verses were read for this question and are silent on it. They describe the breath and what the practitioner can do with it, and say nothing about where the awareness is held to be; the seated posture the chapter requires places the body and not the awareness."@en .

pmc:KevalaPresupposesSahita a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das alleinige Anhalten setzt das Anhalten mit Füllen und Leeren voraus"@de,
        "Retention alone presupposes retention with filling and emptying"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.72 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:note "The instruction is to keep practising the accompanied form until the unaccompanied one succeeds. Recorded at prescribed order because the verse sets a sequence without saying what befalls one who skips it. The direction of the breath drops out at the top of this sequence, which is what makes the claim that inhalation draws and exhalation expels a claim about the lower rungs only. See prima-materia#27."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:KevalaKumbhaka ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:HathaPranayama ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:KhecariYieldsRetention a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die über den Gaumen gelegte Zunge bringt das Zurückhalten des Nektars hervor"@de,
        "The tongue set above the palate yields the retention of the nectar"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.42 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.51-52 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:note "The contact at the palate is attested, and what it is said to do is stop a descent. It is not bound to a phase of breathing anywhere in the chapter, and it is not said to carry anything upward. The same holds for the seal at the throat, which is described as closing off a downward flow. A scheme that pairs the palate with inhalation and with upward carriage takes a stopping and reports it as a conveyance. See prima-materia#28."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Khecari ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:RetentionOfTheDescendingNectar .

pmc:Kinyambi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kinyambi, das Wasser gegen den gewöhnlichen Tod"@de,
        "Kinyambi, the water that was to prevent an ordinary death"@en ;
    dcterms:source "W. Holman Bentley, Pioneering on the Congo (London: Religious Tract Society, 1900), vol. I, p. 292" ;
    skos:definition "In eighteen eighty-five men came from Luanda with the story of a fish that begged not to be killed and promised that whoever drank the water from its mouth, or water it had lain in, would never die by fair means or from natural causes, but only by witchcraft. The water was hawked through the country and widely bought, even at San Salvador."@en ;
    skos:note "The corpus notes that the promise conceded almost nothing, since every death is in any case attributed to witchcraft, and that it was nonetheless a great success. That is a claim that discriminates almost nothing by construction, and the transmission that made it is the one that noticed."@en,
        "The outcome is the reason the node stands beside pmc:Kiyoka: when it was seen that the purchasers died like ordinary mortals, the traffic ceased. The same corpus, thirteen years earlier and in the same districts, had recorded a failure that was explained away instead. The two responses to a failed promise stand two pages apart, and neither is the rule of the transmission."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:KongoNkisi .

pmc:KiyokaProtocol a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Protokoll: das Versprechen des allgemeinen Verbrennens"@de,
        "Protocol: the promise of the general burning"@en ;
    dcterms:source "W. Holman Bentley, Pioneering on the Congo (London: Religious Tract Society, 1900), vol. I, pp. 290-292" ;
    skos:definition "A protocol against the claim carried by the movement of about eighteen hundred and seventy-two, that the destruction of every charged thing would end sickness and death."@en ;
    skos:note "The case is the movement itself and is entered as one case and not as a count. What the source records is that the deaths went on as before and that the people themselves drew that conclusion; it gives no number, no period and no comparison, so the deviation is qualitative and rests on the summary of one resident observer."@en,
        "The response to the failure is the part worth keeping. The claim was not withdrawn: the failure was assigned to hidden charms, an unobservable condition that can absorb any outcome, and the theory was left standing. That is the same move the transmission makes at the level of a single charged thing in pmc:FailureIsSulkinessOrLostPower, carried out at the scale of a country."@en ;
    pm:caseCount 1 ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundNothing ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Searched for a second and independent record of the outcome of the kiyoka movement in the other works opened in this run: the term does not occur in Weeks 1914 or in Dennett 1906; it occurs once in Weeks, Folk-Lore vol. XX, pp. 57-58, but there as a quotation from the same compiler's dictionary appendix of 1895, pp. 848-849, and therefore not as a second witness. Nothing was found that reports the movement as having succeeded, and nothing that gives a count of deaths on either side."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia compiler, from the wording of Bentley 1900, vol. I, pp. 290-292, with the search stated in the counter-search note" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A district in which the general destruction of charged things was carried out and sickness and death did not cease."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:Kiyoka .

pmc:KnowingTheTextAsTheCondition a pm:Converging ;
    rdfs:label "Die Erlangung hängt am Wissen eines Textes, und das Scheitern am Nichtwissen"@de,
        "The attainment is attached to knowing a text, and the failure to not knowing it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aitareya Brāhmaṇa iii,7 to iii,11 (xi,7 to xi,11), in the English of Arthur Berriedale Keith, Rigveda Brahmanas, Harvard Oriental Series 25, Cambridge, Massachusetts 1920, pp. 169-172",
        "Book of the Dead, chapters 64, 83, 86 and 125, rubrics and the address to the gods of the hall of the two Maati, Papyrus of Nu, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: An English Translation of the Chapters, Hymns, etc., of the Theban Recension, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. I, pp. 217-218 and 221-222, and vol. II, pp. 269, 275-277 and 375-378",
        "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa XI,5,3,8-12 and XI,5,6,9 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, The Śatapatha-Brāhmaṇa, Sacred Books of the East 44, Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1900, pp. 82-85 and 99" ;
    skos:definition "Two transmissions state the condition of their attainments in the same unusual place. Neither says that the man who performs the rite obtains the result; both say that the man who knows obtains it. The Egyptian corpus closes chapter after chapter with the clause that if this chapter be known by the deceased, such and such will follow — coming forth by day, passage at every gate, transformation into whatever form he pleases, flourishing children and the favour of the king. The Vedic corpus closes exposition after exposition with the clause that he who knows this attains a full life, offspring, cattle, prosperity, or escape from the death that comes again. And both state the reverse in the same terms. At the hall of the two Maati every part of the doorway refuses the dead man passage unless he tells its name; in the Vedic dialogue on the fires that go out, the eldest son dies, the master of the house dies, the cattle die, but only in the case of him who would not know this, while the man who knows has by dint of knowledge prevailed."@en ;
    skos:note "A third witness stands near and is deliberately not counted. The Hekhalot literature in this graph has doorkeepers at the palaces, a stated fitness, and a penalty of violence for the descender who fails at the sixth gate; but what that gate tests is silence and not knowledge of a name, and the passages where names and seals are the condition of passage were not read at the wording in this run. It is named here so that the next run knows where the third leg would come from, and it is not in pm:convergingConcept."@en,
        "Counter-material was looked for in this holding before the node was written, and one kind was found. Where a transmission deliberately keeps part of its operative content out of writing, the condition moves off the text: the internal alchemy says that the sages transmit the medicine and do not transmit the fire, and that the one aperture cannot be settled by one's own notion but must be handed on from mouth to mind. That does not contradict this correspondence, which is about two corpora that put everything into the text; it bounds it, and the bound is the useful part. It is stated as a claim at generalizations/the-text-and-what-is-withheld.ttl."@en,
        "What the two do not share, and it is worth as much as what they do. The Egyptian condition is knowledge of a particular wording that can be written on a roll and buried with the man, and the corpus is that roll. The Vedic condition is knowledge of an identification — that this breath has entered that breath, that the year is Death — which the corpus states but which is not a formula to be recited. One is a password, the other an interpretation. A run that wanted to press the correspondence further would have to ask whether the Egyptian names are ever glossed as meanings rather than as names."@en,
        "Why the correspondence is worth stating at all, given that caveat. It is not that two transmissions promise things for knowing — many do. It is that both of them state the failure in the same place and in the same grammar, so that the whole apparatus turns on one unobservable fact about a man. In the Egyptian case the gates ask a question whose answer the buyer has already purchased; in the Vedic case the same mishap kills one man's son and not another's, and what decides it is an equivalence held in the mind. In both, no third party at any time could tell the two cases apart."@en ;
    pm:convergingConcept pmc:AegyptiaPortaeNomina,
        pmc:AegyptiaRubricaScientiae,
        pmc:VaidikaJnanenaJitam,
        pmc:VaidikaYaEvamVeda ;
    pm:independenceGround "Not a claim of priority in time. The Egyptian witness is the older by a wide margin: the Book of the Dead is attested from about sixteen hundred before this era and its clause-bearing predecessors earlier still, while the Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa in the Mādhyandina recension belongs to the first half of the following millennium. What carries the independence is that no route of contact has been established, in anything this run read or searched, between the Egyptian mortuary literature and the Vedic ritual exegesis for this feature; the disputed contact route entered on the Egyptian instance concerns the Graeco-Roman and hermetic material of a much later period and the demotic manuscripts of the Roman era, not the Indian side, and the documented route entered on the Vedic instance concerns its own Indic and Eurasian neighbourhood. Two things this ground does not settle and does not hide. Both corpora stand in one connected Old World in which contact of other kinds is abundantly documented, and absence of an established route for a feature is not the same as an established absence of route. And the feature is of a kind for which the structural alternative is strong: a transmission whose product is a text has a reason of its own to make knowing the text the condition, and that reason is available to anybody independently of anybody else. See the note below and generalizations/the-text-and-what-is-withheld.ttl, where the structural reading is what is actually put at risk." ;
    pm:independentAttestation pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmc:KnowledgePresupposesContemplation a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Gotteserkenntnis setzt Naturschau voraus"@de,
        "Knowledge of God presupposes contemplation of nature"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 1" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Theologike ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Physike ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:DesertMonasticism .

pmc:KokabelTeachesConstellations a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Kokabel offenbart die Sternbilder"@de,
        "Kokabel discloses the constellations"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:Constellations ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Kokabel ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:KokabelTeachesSignsOfTheStars a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Kokabel offenbart die Zeichen der Sterne"@de,
        "Kokabel discloses the signs of the stars"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)" ;
    skos:note "Stands in the position the Ethiopic fills with the constellations."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:SignsOfTheStars ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Kokabel ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:KrtyapratiharanaYieldsRedeuntem a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der Ritus des Zurückwenders bringt hervor, dass der Zauber auf seinen Urheber zurückkommt"@de,
        "The rite of the returner brings the spell back upon its maker"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Atharvaveda Saṃhitā 5.14 verses 4 to 13, in the translation of W. D. Whitney, revised by C. R. Lanman, Harvard Oriental Series 7, Cambridge, Mass. 1905" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Krtyapratiharana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Atharvavedic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:KrtyaReditAdAuctorem .

pmc:Kumulipo a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kumulipo"@de,
        "Kumulipo"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Martha Warren Beckwith, The Kumulipo: A Hawaiian Creation Chant (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), chapter 1, the prose note" ;
    skos:definition "The chant the transmission names as an ancient prayer for the dedication of a high chief: a birth chant that establishes rank by reciting a descent that runs from the beginning of the world to the living. What it is held to accomplish is therefore a standing, not a cure or a protection."@en ;
    skos:note "The prose note prefixed to the chant makes two datable claims beside the genealogical ones: that the chant was recited to Alapai-wahine at Koko on Oahu while Keeaumoku lay dying, and that it is the chant Puou recited to Cook at the heiau of Hikiau at Kealakekua. Those are historical assertions and not claims of efficacy, and they are the only part of the text that an outside record could meet or miss."@en,
        "The text this edition rests on is disputed, and the dispute is not entered as a node because neither side was read at the wording in this run. It stands at prima-materia#472."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HawaiianRitual .

pmc:LacAcidumSignum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ist die Milch sauer geworden, ist die Handlung gelungen"@de,
        "If the milk has turned, the performance is a success"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Kauśikasūtra 19.15-21" ;
    skos:definition "The manual prescribes a stable rite: the owner drinks the new milk of a cow that has thrown her second calf, mixed with the spittle of the calf; he presents a cow to the Brahman; he pours a vessel of water into the stable; having swept the moistened dung together and laid his left hand upon it, he scatters half with his right; having put lumps of excrement, bdellium and salt into milk from a cow whose calf is of her own colour, he buries the mixture behind the fire; on the fourth morning he eats of it. Then: if the milk has turned, the performance is a success."@en ;
    skos:note "Owed under the separate count for signs of success and kept apart from the conditions of failure, which this rite has none of. The criterion is announced in advance, it is checked by the performer, and the outcome it asks for is what buried milk does in four days by itself. The editor through whom it was read says so in a footnote of three words: cheap magic, the milk is sure to turn."@en,
        "Read at second hand and the mode says so. There exists no translation of the Kauśikasūtra into any European language; the edition that carries the sūtra prints it in Devanāgarī, and the passage was read in the editor's English rendering inside his commentary on the hymn. The work that carries the claim is named in the source; the work in which it was read is named with pm:readVia. This is an open item under SPEC §10: whoever can read the sūtra text raises the mode."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "Maurice Bloomfield, Hymns of the Atharva-Veda, Sacred Books of the East 42, Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1897, commentary on 2.26, page 303" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Atharvavedic .

pmc:LapseInSelfRememberingGoesUnnoticed a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Der Aussetzer wird nicht bemerkt, solange er dauert"@de,
        "The lapse is not noticed while it lasts"@en ;
    dcterms:source "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching, chapter seven, at the walk along the Nevsky and the waking two hours later (one-volume English text as scanned in the Internet Archive full-text mirror; edition left unstated)" ;
    skos:definition "The warning that the exercise fails without the practitioner noticing: the recorder reports holding the divided attention along a street, losing it at a single ordinary thought, and coming to two hours later in another part of the city, having in the meantime left a shop, made a telephone call and written two letters, and only then remembering that he had forgotten to remember himself."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the shape of failure condition the project looks for, and it is unusually strong: the failure is not merely predicted but predicted to be invisible from inside, and the report performs it. It rules out the obvious procedure. Asking practitioners how long they sustained the exercise measures what they remember of it, and the claim says exactly that this is the faculty which fails."@en,
        "What it does not yield on its own is a duration. The account gives one lapse and no figure for how long the state can be held, so nothing here fixes a threshold a later examination could measure against. See prima-materia#654."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:SelfRemembering ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:FourthWay .

pmc:LeDouteAffaiblitLaPuissance a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Zweifel an der eigenen Macht schwächt sie"@de,
        "Doubt of one's own power enfeebles it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. III, pp. 42-43" ;
    skos:definition "Where the first patient undertaken is absolutely insensible to the action, the beginner is apt to imagine he has not conducted the process aright, or else he doubts his own power; and in proportion as one doubts it, it really becomes enfeebled. The source states as the happiest thing that can happen to a first attempt that the subject be one who is not insensible and who nevertheless feels only slight and gradual effects."@en ;
    skos:note "The practical advice that follows is the part worth recording for anyone examining the tradition: the beginner is to choose a first subject who is neither insensible nor strongly affected. Selection of that kind, stated as counsel, bears on every case series the literature produced — and a second work of the tradition, read at the wording for pmc:HuitJoursConsecutifs below, states it as a rule instead of counsel."@en,
        "The third cancelling clause, and the one that closes the circle. A failure produces doubt, doubt reduces the power, and reduced power produces further failure — so a sequence of null results is accounted for by the first of them. The transmission states the mechanism plainly and does not notice that it has made a failed series self-explaining."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:LeftHandPathTwoBranches a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "Der linke Pfad, über sein Ziel bestimmt, mit zwei nach der Methode unterschiedenen Zweigen"@de,
        "The left-hand path defined by its goal, with two branches differing in method"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1997" ;
    dcterms:source "S. E. Flowers, Lords of the Left-Hand Path: A History of Spiritual Dissent (Smithville 1997), in the chapter setting out the criteria and the two branches" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that the left-hand path is constituted by the goal of self-deification rather than by any particular practice, and that it falls into two branches: an immanent one, materialistic in outlook and pronounced in its transgression, and a transcendental one, centred on intellect and eternal forms."@en ;
    skos:note "That is the case this graph was given a separate method axis for. In the same work the goal is held constant across both branches while the degree of transgression varies between them — an author varying one axis and holding the other fixed, without drawing the axes. See prima-materia#52 and prima-materia#67."@en,
        "The passage that matters most for this record is the one that denies a methodological definition outright. The source states that there are no categorical methodological proscriptions on the path in any of its traditions, eastern or western, and that methods are chosen pragmatically; the antinomian element is described as often strong, not as constitutive. The author who is usually cited as fixing the path by transgression therefore says the opposite of what he is cited for."@en,
        "The transcendental branch is the standing candidate for the quadrant that a one-axis scheme cannot name: a goal of self-deification without transgression as its means. Whether it actually sits there is not settled by this node and is the question of prima-materia#72."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:circulatesAs "The old distinction of the Indian tantric traditions, reported as though the two-criteria definition were carried by them rather than formulated in 1997." ;
    pm:originatedBy "Stephen E. Flowers" .

pmc:LegalActionPresupposesPoisonOracle a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Rechtsverfolgung setzt das Giftorakel voraus"@de,
        "Legal action presupposes the poison oracle"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 164" ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity because the consequence is stated flatly and is not a matter of degree: the action cannot be brought. The passage dates from the period of the monograph, in which the declarations of the oracle had already ceased to be recognized as evidence in the courts the administration had set up, so the necessity is the transmission's own and not the colonial law's."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Legal action cannot be taken without a decision of the poison oracle."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:BengeValidVerdictRule ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:BengeConsultation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:LightKnowledgeCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Das veröffentlichte System ist eine Enthüllungsstufe, nicht das System"@de,
        "The published system is a stage of revelation, not the system"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), p. 274" ;
    skos:definition "The recorders state once and for all that what they print represents one stage of the revelations granted to initiates of high standing who were not themselves responsible for the calculations concerning this part of the sky, and that the stage is deliberately distorted in both directions."@en ;
    skos:note "A caution that cuts against the account carrying it, which is why it is held as a node. It makes the text unfalsifiable in the direction its later readers wanted: any discrepancy can be assigned to the withheld stage. It also means the article cannot be cited as the Dogon system without citing this sentence, and the reception has cited it without."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:LightKnowledge ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Dogon .

pmc:LightningStrikeElection a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der vom Blitz Getroffene gilt als erwählt"@de,
        "The lightning-struck is taken as elected"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Cristóbal de Molina, Relación de las fábulas y ritos de los incas, ed. Paloma Jiménez del Campo, transcr. Paloma Cuenca Muñoz (Madrid: Iberoamericana / Frankfurt: Vervuert, 2010), fols. 6v-7r, on the camascas",
        "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III" ;
    skos:definition "When a man struck by lightning is left alive, injured but living, he is held to be as if divinely chosen for the service of the huacas. The earlier report gives the claim in the first person of the transmission: those called camascas said that the grace and virtue they had was received from the Thunder — that when lightning fell and left a man in terror, he said on coming to himself that the Thunder had shown him that art, whether curing with herbs or giving answers to what was asked; and the same was said of a man who escaped a river or a great danger."@en ;
    skos:note "The two do not say the same thing about what is received. The later gives a status — he is as if elected. The earlier gives a content — the Thunder showed him an art, and names two arts. The difference is recorded rather than merged, because a status can be conferred by the community after the fact and a taught art cannot."@en,
        "Two recorders, forty-six years apart, in different regions, on different sides of the colonial administration, one writing in Cuzco and one in the archdiocese of Lima, give the same claim. Neither cites the other. This is the best-attested single claim in the file, and the only one for which the internal date spread is wide enough to be worth stating."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmc:LimbOrderDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Glieder der Reihe nach zu durchlaufen sind"@de,
        "Whether the limbs must be traversed in order"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra II.29",
        "Vyāsa, Yogabhāṣya on Yogasūtra II.29" ;
    skos:definition "A disagreement about whether the eight limbs form a sequence that must be climbed. The sūtra that names them enumerates and says nothing about order; the commentarial layer introduces the categories of outer and inner limb, and later teaching hardens these into a rule of practice."@en ;
    skos:note "The disagreement is not between two traditions but between a base text and its readers, which is the commonest shape a dispute takes in this collection and the easiest to lose, because the readers usually win by default."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:AstangaLimbs .

pmc:LimbPracticeYieldsLightOfKnowledge a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Übung der Glieder bringt das Leuchten der Erkenntnis hervor"@de,
        "Practice of the limbs yields the light of knowledge"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra II.28" ;
    skos:note "The sūtra makes the arising conditional on the wearing away of impurity, which is itself said to come from the practice. The condition is part of the claim and is not to be dropped when the claim is quoted."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:PracticeOfTheLimbs ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PatanjalaYoga ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:LightOfKnowledge .

pmc:LonnrotJarjestys a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die achtzehn Formelklassen und die Reihenfolge des Vortrags"@de,
        "The eighteen classes of formula and the order of recitation"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1880" ;
    dcterms:source "John Abercromby, The Pre- and Proto-historic Finns, vol. II (London: David Nutt, 1898), pp. 2-6" ;
    skos:definition "The ordering the collector set over the material and the translator reports from him. The formulae are divided into eighteen classes by occasion, among them the preliminary, the defensive, those against envy, those to discover the cause, the reparation of harm, the expulsion, the posting, the reproaching, the falling into ecstasy, the boasting, the menacing, the exorcism and the making fast. The order of recitation is given as: the vapour formula where there is an open wound, then the preliminary and defensive formulae and those against envy; then, where the injury is of a known kind, the recitation of its origin, and where it is not, the formula to discover the cause; and thereafter the remaining classes in no particular order, as the operator remembered them or thought them useful. The account adds that the work was done in the vapour-bath house, heated as secretly as possible so that no envious person should get wind of it."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as an ordering act and not as a claim of the transmission, for the reason the registration of this tradition already gave about the Kalevala. The collector states himself that it is hard to say in what order the songs were recited and that if there was an order at all it was not everywhere uniform. An ordering offered with that reservation is exactly what this class is for."@en,
        "It is the source of two things this file needs and could not take from a song: that the menacing formula has its occasion after a failed expulsion, and that the discovery formula has its place before the origins. Both are entered above with this node standing behind them, and a later reader who wants the songs alone should subtract them."@en ;
    pm:compiledBy "Elias Lönnrot, in the preface to Suomen Kansan Muinaisia Loitsurunoja (Helsinki: SKS, 1880), reported and translated by John Abercromby in 1898" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:FinnicIncantation .

pmc:LoquelaPresupponitConsociationem a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Rede mit Engeln setzt eine Vergesellschaftung in Glauben und Liebe voraus"@de,
        "Speech with angels presupposes a consociation in faith and love"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Swedenborg, Arcana coelestia, n. 9438 (The Heavenly Arcana, trans. Potts, Standard Edition, vol. XI, pp. 359-360)" ;
    skos:note "The consequence has a second edge that belongs to a holding of failure conditions. The same passage makes the conjunction the protection: a man who is not conjoined and whose interiors were nevertheless opened would be exposed to the attack of spirits who, as the caution says, desire nothing more vehemently than to destroy him. The corpus does not say that this happens, because it says the interiors of such a man cannot be opened at all — so the protection is guaranteed by the impossibility of the case in which it would be needed."@en,
        "The necessity is stated and the condition is not establishable by the man it is stated of, which is the finding rather than the necessity. Faith and love are the two things this corpus elsewhere says are not felt in the natural degree, and the safety from evil spirits that the passage promises follows from the conjunction and not from anything the man does in the encounter. See pmc:SwedenborgNullumSignumInVita."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The corpus states the consequence twice in the same paragraph and in the strongest form it uses anywhere. To no one is it given to speak as a spirit and angel with angels and spirits unless he is of such a character that he can be consociated with them in respect to faith and love, and he cannot be so consociated unless he has faith in the Lord and love to the Lord; and when he has been conjoined with Him he is safe from the attack of evil spirits who are from hell. With others the interiors cannot be opened at all, because they are not in the Lord."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:SwedenborgLoquelaCumSpiritibus ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArcanaCoelestia ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:SwedenborgTresGradus ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Swedenborgian .

pmc:LovePresupposesApatheia a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Liebe setzt Apatheia voraus, Erkenntnis die Liebe"@de,
        "Love presupposes apatheia, and knowledge presupposes love"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 81",
        "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 84" ;
    skos:note "The chain is stated as generation and not as sequence: each term is the child or the flower of the one before it. That is why the strength here is necessity, and why a consequence can be quoted at all."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Love is called the offspring of apatheia and is said to keep the door to the contemplation of created things, so that without it the door is not passed." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Agape,
        pmc:Physike ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Agape,
        pmc:Apatheia ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:DesertMonasticism .

pmc:LuciditePresupposesUndividedAttention a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Lucidité setzt ungeteilte Aufmerksamkeit voraus"@de,
        "Lucidity presupposes an undivided attention"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. V, pp. 75-76 and 84" ;
    skos:definition "For the proper exercise of clairvoyance the somnambulist must concentrate his faculties upon a single object, without distraction, without trouble, and without the intervention of any foreign influence to change the direction of his mind; the more his attention is distracted by various subjects, the less of it he gives to the essential object."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity on a consequence the source gives in plain terms: made to talk beyond the seat of his own disorder he will furnish only vague and perhaps erroneous views, and questions of curiosity excite his vanity, after which nothing he says can be depended upon. The greater part of somnambulists, even in good hands, are said to have lost a portion of their faculties because many persons in succession were allowed to see them."@en,
        "This is the prerequisite from which the whole apparatus of exclusions below follows, and it is the load-bearing member of the generalization formed from this file: the condition of the capacity is stated in such a way that any observation of it destroys it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "If you make him talk beyond this, you will obtain from him only vague and perhaps erroneous views; and when you have once awakened in him this sentiment of vanity, to which somnambulists are in general very much inclined, you can no more depend upon any thing."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:MagnetismeLucidite ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:MagnetismeRapport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:MagnetiserPresupposesTheOperatorsHealth a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Operation setzt die Gesundheit des Magnetiseurs voraus"@de,
        "The operation presupposes the operator's own health"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. I, § 15, p. 23, and chap. VIII, pp. 171-172" ;
    skos:definition "Because the fluid emanating from the operator exercises a physical influence on the patient, the operator must be in good health; and because the influence acts in time on the patient's moral condition, he must also be worthy of esteem for the uprightness of his mind and the honesty of his character."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity on a consequence the source states twice and illustrates with a case: rheumatic pains, nervous affections and especially organic diseases are communicated from the operator to the patient in proportion to the thoroughness of the communication, and a person whose lungs are delicate cannot without danger operate on one whose lungs are affected. The case is of an operator with an inflammation of the larynx who sent a magnetized handkerchief each evening; the patient contracted the same disease and was in the greatest danger when he died."@en,
        "The source also reports, of one operator, that she commonly takes the disease of the person she operates on — having the sensation and symptoms of it for several days — and instances gout and an ophthalmia, which he notes are not contagious. He says he cannot account for it. A claim of that shape is checkable by ordinary means and the transmission does not check it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Rheumatic pains, nervous affections, and especially organic diseases, are communicated from the magnetizer to the person magnetized, with facility proportioned to the thoroughness of the communication between them."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:MagnetismeGrandsCourans ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:MagnetismeRapport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:MagnetiserPresupposesWillConfidenceBenevolence a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Operation setzt Wille, Zutrauen und Wohlwollen zusammen voraus"@de,
        "The operation presupposes will, confidence and benevolence together"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. I, §§ 4, 9, 14, 26, pp. 21-26" ;
    skos:definition "The first condition of action is to exercise the will; the second is the confidence the operator has in his own powers; the third is benevolence, the desire of doing good. One of these may supply the others to a certain point, but for the action to be at once energetic and salutary the three must be united."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity because the source states the outcome of the want in the same numbered series: if the desire of doing good be not united to the will to act, there will be some effects, but these effects will be irregular. That is a consequence, and it is also the escape route — irregular effects are what an unsuccessful trial produces anyway, so the condition can absorb every failure without ever being violated in a way a reader could point to."@en,
        "The same series makes confidence unnecessary in the patient: one can act equally on those who believe and those who do not, it sufficing that the patient yields himself passively without resistance. That asymmetry is the transmission's own answer to the charge of imagination and it is the claim the 1784 commission's blindfold design was built against."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "If the desire of doing good be not united to the will to act, there will be some effects, but these effects will be irregular."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:MagnetismeGrandsCourans ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:MagnetismeRapport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:MagnetismeConsultationPourAbsent a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Beratung für einen Abwesenden über einen getragenen Gegenstand"@de,
        "Consultation for an absent person by a worn object"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. V, p. 82, and chap. VIII, p. 178" ;
    skos:definition "Some somnambulists consult for persons absent and unknown to them: hair of the patient, or something worn for several days on the stomach next to the skin, suffices to put them in close communication with him, whereupon they describe his physical and moral condition exactly and minutely. The object is to be enveloped in paper and the packet not opened when presented."@en ;
    skos:note "Before introducing a patient to a somnambulist the operator is to let him touch something the patient has worn, so that he may say whether he feels repugnance to it or sees danger in being put in communication. The same object thus serves as the channel of the diagnosis and as the ground for refusing to make it."@en,
        "The one claim in this file that is cleanly testable by a procedure the transmission itself prescribes, and the procedure is already half a blind: the packet is closed, the operator does not open it, and a second packet from a person with a different complaint would complete the design. The source does not take that step and nothing in the work suggests it occurred to him."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:MagnetismePerceptionParLOperateur a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Magnetiseur, der den Sitz der Krankheit wahrnimmt"@de,
        "The operator who perceives the seat of the disease"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. VI, pp. 113-114" ;
    skos:definition "A capacity claimed for the operator and not the subject: among those at Paris who devote themselves to the practice the source says he knows some who perceive the seat of the disease and modify their action accordingly, and some who pass into a sort of demi-somnambulism during which they operate with much discernment."@en ;
    skos:note "Kept as its own node because it is the only capacity in the file whose bearer is the person making the report, and because it removes the one structural advantage the tradition had. Everywhere else the claim is checkable in principle: one person acts, another reports, and the reports can be collected. Here the operator both acts and reports, and the transmission has arrived at the shape of every other tradition in this holding."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:MagnetismePrevision a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Vermögen der Vorschau und seine ausgesprochene Grenze"@de,
        "The faculty of prevision and its stated limit"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. VIII, pp. 185-188" ;
    skos:definition "The most inconceivable of the faculties, said never to extend beyond a certain number of objects and to be conditional; and of which the source states that if there are facts enough to prove its existence, there is not a case where the exactitude of its application can be reckoned upon."@en ;
    skos:note "The same chapter forbids consulting somnambulists on political affairs, on the dogmas of religion and on metaphysics, and reports men otherwise well informed becoming the dupes of their visions and predictions. The prohibition and the limit are one thought."@en,
        "The sentence is the sharpest self-limitation in the file and it is worth more than the claim it limits. A faculty whose existence is asserted and whose application is declared never reckonable cannot be brought to any trial, and the source says so without appearing to notice that it has said so. Braid would make the same point against the tradition thirty years later as an objection; here it stands inside the manual as a counsel of prudence."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:MaidenDeclarationDecidedByASnake a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Eine bestrittene Erklärung entscheidet eine Schlange"@de,
        "A contested declaration is decided by a snake"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 99" ;
    skos:definition "The maidens who wish to attend the dancers are called by name and each declares that she has never had carnal intercourse with a man. Anyone may challenge the declaration. If the challenged maiden is silent she is held not to be a maiden; if she repeats the declaration and bites a snakeskin or the effigy of a snake and the challenger then keeps silence, her declaration is held true. If the challenge is repeated the challenger must bite the snake too; if he does not, his challenge is held a slander. If he does, the decision is held in abeyance until a snake decides it by biting the one who gave false testimony, as the corpus says a snake will surely do."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also the only ordeal in this file in which both parties are put at risk, and the only one in which the transmission provides that the accuser may lose by refusing the same test."@en,
        "The most nearly falsifiable procedure in this file. It has a named outcome, a named agent, and a defined pair of contestants, and it does not resolve until the outcome occurs. What it does not have is a term, and that is the whole difference between it and the Sun dance promise on the node above: the verdict of the snake can be waited for indefinitely, so no length of silence tells against it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LakotaRitual .

pmc:MakutuCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Nicht spucken in Gegenwart eines Kundigen"@de,
        "Not to spit in the presence of one skilled in the art"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 31" ;
    skos:definition "The witness reports that a person would not dare to spit in the presence of anyone he feared might be disposed to injure him, if that person had a reputation for skill in this art."@en ;
    skos:note "A caution that names its own detection problem: the material the art needs is produced involuntarily, so the guard is against being present rather than against acting."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Makutu ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Maori .

pmc:Mana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Mana"@de,
        "Mana"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), pp. 54 and 62" ;
    skos:definition "An efficacy that belongs to someone. In the passages read it is always somebody's: a father's, which may inspire the son so that he becomes the living representative of that mana and of the karakia with it; or an opponent's, which is asked for by name in an invocation before a stroke is made."@en ;
    skos:note "What the witness does not show is the thing the term is famous for elsewhere. In these passages mana is never a quantity of impersonal force at large in the world; it is held, inherited, represented, and asked for from a named holder. The reading that made it an impersonal power belongs to a later literature and to a different archipelago, and it is not entered here because that literature has not yet been opened at the wording. See prima-materia#103 for the origination and prima-materia#105 for the philological dispute over the word itself."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Maori .

pmc:MandaeanAblatioSacramentorum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Gebete, Totenmesse und Taufe werden ihnen abgenommen"@de,
        "The prayers, the masiqta and the baptism are taken away from them"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ginzā, right side, book 15, piece 7 (text 319,21-320,16), in the German of Mark Lidzbarski, Ginzā (Göttingen 1925), pp. 323-324" ;
    skos:definition "What follows in the same piece for the unworthy priest. Driven back from the gate of Abathur, he will not behold the knowledge; it will be taken from him, and he will sink down to the nature of the place of darkness. The hidden prayers he understands from the world will be taken from him, and the hidden prayer-orders, and the well-appointed ships; the masiqta and the baptism will be taken from him, and these will ascend to the place from which they came; the hidden treasure will be taken from him and placed with the great Jur, the treasurer."@en ;
    skos:note "No sign accompanies the removal and none is asked for. Nothing in the passage tells the man, or anyone watching him, that his baptism has been taken; the loss is established at the gate and nowhere earlier."@en,
        "The rite is treated as a possession that can be repossessed, and the text says where it goes: back up, to where it came from. Read together with pmc:MandaeanNomenEtSignum this closes a circle inside the corpus. The pass at the watch-houses is the baptism; the baptism can be taken away; and the man it is taken from is turned back at the gate that all the stations lead to."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GinzaRabba ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean .

pmc:MandaeanAndirunaLibriDesunt a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zwei Rollen fehlen, und die Handlung ist ungültig"@de,
        "Two scrolls absent, and the proceedings are void"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), p. 153" ;
    skos:definition "All the sacred books that can be found must be placed in the reed hut in which the novice and his master keep vigil, and two of them in particular, the Diwan Alma Rishaya and the Diwan Malkutha Ileytha. If these are not present, the proceedings are invalid."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because of the form and not the content. The condition is an observable state of the room, it is stated in advance, and the consequence named for failing it is not a penalty but voidness: the rite does not become expensive, it does not count. A holding that wants to know which transmissions can be shown to have failed needs the cases where the transmission itself says what would make a performance no performance."@en,
        "The books are named and the reason they are named is not given. Whether they are required because they are read from, because they are the warrant of the office, or for some third reason, the field record does not say, and no node here supplies it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by E. S. Drower in Iraq and published in 1937, in her account of the consecration as related to her by priestly informants; no speaker is named at this place."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean .

pmc:MandaeanBaptismataMultiplicata a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Zahl wird durch Vervielfachung der Vollzieher abgetragen"@de,
        "The count is discharged by multiplying the celebrants"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), pp. 175-176" ;
    skos:definition "How a tariff of many baptisms is actually worked off. In the purification the recorder attended, each act of the baptism was received from three priests in turn, so that every action was performed nine times and the whole operation was repeated at noon and before sunset, giving the defiled priest nine baptisms in one day. A polluted ganzibra was served by seven priests and got through sixty-three baptisms in a day; performed by one priest once daily they would have taken a whole year, during which he would have been excommunicate."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also the one place in the file where the recorder writes as an eyewitness of a penalty being paid, and the count she gives is arithmetic she did herself."@en,
        "The number is the thing, and the time is not. A penalty stated as a count of repetitions turns out to be payable in a day by adding celebrants, and the transmission does this openly and by arrangement rather than as an evasion. Whoever wants to read the counts in this file as a measure of severity has to reckon with the fact that the transmission itself treats them as divisible."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Observed by E. S. Drower at Litlata in 1934, at the beginning of the purification of an assistant priest, and published in English in 1937; the ganzibra's figure is reported to her rather than seen."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean .

pmc:MandaeanCautioAdversusDivinos a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Gehet nicht zu den Wahrsagern und Chaldäern der Lüge"@de,
        "Go not to the soothsayers and the Chaldeans of falsehood"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ginzā, right side, book 1, commandment 143 (text 19,25-21,3), and book 2, first piece, commandment 55 (text 37,16-38,22), in the German of Mark Lidzbarski, Ginzā (Göttingen 1925), pp. 22 and 37" ;
    skos:definition "The commandment forbidding recourse to diviners: go not to the soothsayers and the Chaldeans of falsehood, for they will be lodged in the darkness. The parallel recension adds that the soothsayers and Chaldeans themselves will be lodged in the darkness, so that the ground given is their end and not the falsity of what they say."@en ;
    skos:note "The prohibition is not that divination fails. Neither recension says the soothsayers are wrong; both say where they will end up. A transmission that wanted to make its own procedures the only working ones had an easy sentence available here and did not use it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:MandaeanCautioAdversusMagiam ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GinzaRabba ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean .

pmc:MandaeanDiesVacuiRepetiti a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die sechzig Tage werden selten in sechzig Tagen vollendet"@de,
        "The sixty days are rarely accomplished in sixty days"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), pp. 155-156 and 173" ;
    skos:definition "The rule governing the period of purity after the week in the hut. The chance visit or touch of a person or thing in a state of uncleanness may render that day void, and another day with the devotions that should have been performed in it is added at the end of the sixty; thus, the record says, the sixty days of purity are rarely accomplished in sixty days. For the candidate to the high priesthood the corresponding period is forty-five days, called the period assigned for a passage through the purgatories, and there the rule is harder: if pollution occurs in spite of precaution, the seclusion must be begun again."@en ;
    skos:note "Its relation to pmc:CountedThresholdNeverFallsDue is worth stating and is not identity. That generalization is about a count at which an effect is to arrive; here the count is a term to be served, and the effect at the end of it, the priesthood, is established by other means. What the two share is the provision made in advance for the count not completing, and a run that wants to widen the generalization to terms as well as thresholds should start here."@en,
        "Two devices for the same problem and they are not equally strong. A voided day is replaced; a voided seclusion is restarted. In both, a count that the transmission itself fixed is prevented from falling due by an event outside the candidate's control, and in both the transmission provides for that in advance rather than meeting it when it comes."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by E. S. Drower in Iraq and published in 1937, from priestly informants; the remark that the sixty days are rarely accomplished in sixty is her own and follows the rule in the same sentence."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean .

pmc:MandaeanHamraEodemDie a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Priester muss das Hamra am selben Tag bereiten, sonst ist es unnütz"@de,
        "The priest must prepare the hamra the same day, or it is useless"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), p. 68" ;
    skos:definition "The rule governing the sacramental fluid made by kneading white raisins or grapes and dates into water: the priest must prepare the hamra the same day, or it is useless."@en ;
    skos:note "A term of hours attached to a substance, with the consequence stated in the same word the transmission uses of a dead day. It is the smallest falsifiable claim in this file and the cheapest to check, and no sign is given by which a useless hamra would be told from a good one."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by E. S. Drower in Iraq and published in 1937, in her account of a wedding she attended, where the preparation is described item by item as she saw it laid out."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean .

pmc:MandaeanIngirthaNafaqa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Botschaft, die ein Sterbender trägt"@de,
        "The message carried by a dying man"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), pp. 169-171" ;
    skos:definition "The condition on which a priest may be raised to the high priesthood. It can take place only when some pious aged person of ritually pure and priestly stock, married and not childless, is near death. That person is brought to the cult enclosure, washed, dressed in the death garment, and given a sealed phial of oil in the pocket of the shirt; the candidate says to him, I have brought it to thee, and bear thou it to Abathur; and the dying person is thereby purified of all past transgressions and passes into the worlds of light without a sojourn in the watch-houses."@en ;
    skos:note "One condition of the rite is stated and then relaxed in the same sentence, which is worth keeping because the rest of this file is so exact: the consecration must be performed by a ganzibra, two priests and two acolytes, and in cases of dire necessity one priest has sufficed. The transmission that prices a broken incense-holder at a baptism by three priests also knows how to make an exception, and says so without a tariff attached."@en,
        "The recorder adds, without comment, that the shock of the cold water and the excitement and exertion usually hasten the end."@en,
        "Two claims run together in one rite and the graph should keep them apart. One is about the candidate: his office depends on a death. The other is about the dying man: carrying the message exempts him from the stations the soul otherwise passes. The second is the only exemption from the watch-houses this file carries, and it is bought by dying at the right moment for somebody else's advancement."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by E. S. Drower in Iraq and published in 1937, from priestly informants and in part from her own presence at Mandaean rites of the period; the formula is given in her transliteration with the priests' own English rendering beside it."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean .

pmc:MandaeanMatarata a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Wachthäuser auf dem Weg nach oben"@de,
        "The watch-houses on the way up"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ginzā, right side, book 5, piece 3 (text 180,11-184,8), in the German of Mark Lidzbarski, Ginzā: Der Schatz oder das große Buch der Mandäer (Göttingen: Vandenhoeck und Ruprecht, 1925), pp. 183-186" ;
    skos:definition "The stations the soul passes between the earthly world and the world of light, each in the charge of a named keeper, and each holding fast a named class of the dead: at one the shedders of blood, at another those who fornicated and stole and swore falsely, and so on. The soul reports the journey in the first person, asks at each station whose watch-house it is and who is bound in it, is answered, and passes."@en ;
    skos:note "The piece is a first-person itinerary and not a doctrine about an afterlife, which is why it is entered as a node: it states what is required at each station and reports the outcome of meeting the requirement, and it does so in a fixed formula repeated station by station."@en,
        "The souls held there are said to want a second death and not to get it. The transmission thereby closes off, for the class it is most severe about, the one outcome that would end the matter."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GinzaRabba ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean .

pmc:MandaeanSacerdosRitualiterMortuus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der befleckte Priester gilt als rituell tot"@de,
        "The defiled priest is treated as ritually dead"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), p. 175" ;
    skos:definition "What the purification of a defiled priest opened with, as the recorder saw it at Litlata in 1934. A blessed oblation was read in his name by a ganzibra and two priests; he wore an entirely new garment, held a new staff, and stayed mute while his three colleagues ate for the dead in his name, for he is considered ritually dead; and he sat facing south instead of north. The baptism followed, and when fifty baptisms had been performed upon him a second oblation was read over him and his purification was complete."@en ;
    skos:note "The transmission does not repair the fault, it kills and restores the man. Two marks make the death a formal state and not a metaphor: the meal for the dead is eaten in his name by others, and he is seated at the orientation of the dead. The completion of the count is the only thing that ends it, and there is no sign attached to that completion."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Observed by E. S. Drower at Litlata in 1934 and published in English in 1937; she was present at the beginning of the purification and describes the rite step by step as she watched it, with the Mandaic terms in her transliteration."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean .

pmc:MandaeanScientiaSineDignitate a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Das Verständnis des Schatzes bringt ihnen keinen Nutzen"@de,
        "Understanding the Treasure brings them no benefit"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ginzā, right side, book 15, piece 7 (text 319,1-21), in the German of Mark Lidzbarski, Ginzā (Göttingen 1925), p. 323" ;
    skos:definition "The warning addressed to the transmission's own priestly class. The Nasoraeans who are not worthy of the House of Life have committed grave sin, their body stood in transgression and there was defect and blemish in them; and though they understand the Treasure in high degree from the earth, it brings them no benefit; when they arrive at the great gate of the house of Abathur they will be driven back from it and will not behold the place of light."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also a counter-case to the first clause of pmc:TheTextAndWhatIsWithheld, which says that a transmission putting its operative content into an obtainable text states the condition of attainment as knowing the text and names no further condition a possessor of the text could fail to meet. Here a further condition is named, in the text, against the possessors. Entered as a node and not worked into that generalization by this run, because a counter-case belongs in a protocol and not in the statement it bears on; the protocol is the work of a later run and the address is this node."@en,
        "The translator marks the sentence as uncertain in his footnote and says that the word he renders as the Treasure appears here already to mean a collection of religious writings. The node keeps his doubt rather than quoting past it."@en,
        "This is the passage that makes the transmission interesting to a holding built around the difference between a claim and its warrant. A corpus that puts its whole operative content into books, and requires its priests to know those books by heart, states in one of them that knowing them confers nothing by itself. The condition it adds is worthiness, which is not observable and not countable, and which the same corpus nowhere makes into a test."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:MandaeanNomenEtSignum ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GinzaRabba ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean .

pmc:MandaeanTaghaLapsus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Fällt die Krone, ist er wieder Novize"@de,
        "Should the crown fall, he is a novice again"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), p. 34" ;
    skos:definition "The tagha, the crown worn beneath the turban, is the chief part of the priest's insignia and the consecration is spoken of as the setting up of the crown on him. Should the crown fall accidentally from the head or from the hand of a priest while he is officiating, he becomes a novice and must undergo a form of reconsecration."@en ;
    skos:note "An office lost by an accident of gravity, with no fault, no intention and no further condition. It is the purest case in this file of the transmission attaching a consequence to an observable physical event rather than to a state of the person, and the consequence is the largest it has: not a penalty but the loss of the rank itself."@en,
        "The same image carries the same meaning in the scripture, in a place this run did not read at the wording and therefore does not enter: Lidzbarski quotes, in his notes to the Book of John, a passage saying of proud priests and bishops that the crown has fallen from their head. Whether that is the same formula in the same sense is a question for whoever opens the Drāšā d-Yahia."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by E. S. Drower in Iraq and published in 1937, in her chapter on ritual dress and insignia; the rule is given as current practice, and the sentence of the Alf Trisar Šuialia about the crown and kingliness being set on the head stands beside it as her quotation from the manual."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean .

pmc:MandaeanTaxatioRerum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Preisliste für ein zerbrochenes Kultgerät"@de,
        "The price list for a broken cult object"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), p. 154" ;
    skos:definition "The counted penalties attaching to accidents to cult objects during the week in the hut and the sixty days of purity that follow it. If the clay table breaks, the novice incurs sixty baptisms and his master the same; if the fire-basin, both must be baptized by five priests; if the incense-holder, by three; if the staff floats away or breaks, both have fifty; if the ring is lost, fifty; if the crown, sixty; if the trousers, turban, girdle or stole are injured or lost, the novice and the ganzibra must receive a triple baptism from three priests."@en ;
    skos:note "Seven items and five different prices, and the price varies with which object was damaged rather than with how it happened or whose hand was on it. The transmission is doing something unusual here: it treats a ritual fault as a quantity, and it fixes the quantity by a property of the thing rather than by a property of the act. The graph should record that, because it is the precondition of the question a later reader will want to ask, namely whether the numbers stand in any relation to each other at all."@en,
        "What the field record does not say, and it would be the first thing to look for in the priestly manual: what happens if the penalty is not paid. The numbers are stated; the consequence of leaving them unpaid is not."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by E. S. Drower in Iraq and published in 1937, from priestly informants; the list is given as a list, without a case attached to any item of it."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean .

pmc:MandaeanVigiliaSeptemDierum a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Weihe setzt eine Woche ohne Schlaf voraus"@de,
        "The consecration presupposes a week without sleep"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), pp. 154 and 172" ;
    skos:note "Entered as a prerequisite rather than as a caution because the transmission names what follows from failing it, and names it in numbers. What it does not name is any sign by which the failure would be known to anyone but the candidate, which makes the whole apparatus rest on his own report."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The consequence is stated as a graded count of baptisms and, at the earliest stage, as postponement of the whole consecration for a year. The sleeplessness is not asceticism and the record says so: it is prescribed because of what sleep may bring, and the household arranges itself around it, beating drums, singing and entertaining guests all night so that the candidate shall not doze."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:MandaeanTarmidaTarasa ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by E. S. Drower in Iraq and published in 1937, from priestly informants; the same rule is reported again, with three days instead of seven, for the candidate for the high priesthood."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:MandaeanTaxatioPollutionis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean .

pmc:MangIWistaspanLocus a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Reise verortet das Bewusstsein außerhalb des Körpers"@de,
        "The journey places the awareness away from the body"@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:displacedLocus ;
    pm:situatedNode pmp:MangIWistaspan ;
    pm:situationGround "The body stays on the carpet and is watched for seven days by named persons, while what travels sees, is addressed and is guided elsewhere, and returns to that body on the seventh day. The transmission does not treat the two as one place at any point of the account." .

pmc:ManguMayRemainInoperative a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Substanz kann kühl bleiben"@de,
        "The substance may remain cool"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 4" ;
    skos:definition "The doctrine holds that a man may carry the substance and never use it: it may remain inoperative, cool, throughout his life, and a man whose witchcraft never functions is hardly counted a witch. In practice the question asked of the oracles is never whether a man is a witch but whether at this moment he is bewitching the questioner."@en ;
    skos:note "The clause is what keeps the post-mortem from telling against the inheritance rule, and it belongs in the record beside that rule for exactly that reason. A finding of the substance where no injury was ever ascribed costs the doctrine nothing, and the absence of the substance is the only outcome that decides anything."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:ManguSexLinkedInheritance a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Hexerei geht auf die Kinder desselben Geschlechts über"@de,
        "Witchcraft passes to the children of the same sex"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 2-3" ;
    skos:definition "The substance is held to be transmitted by unilinear descent from parent to child of the same sex: all the sons of a male witch are witches and none of his daughters, all the daughters of a female witch are witches and none of her sons."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim is checkable in a way few in this graph are, because the transmission itself draws consequences from it in practice and then declines the widest of them: in theory every clansman of a proven witch would be one, and in practice only close paternal kinsmen are so regarded."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:ManguSubstance a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Hexerei als Substanz im Leib"@de,
        "Witchcraft as a substance in the body"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 1-2" ;
    skos:definition "Mangu, described as an oval blackish swelling attached to the edge of the liver and lying beneath the xiphoid cartilage, in which small objects are sometimes said to be found. A witch performs no rite, utters no spell and possesses no medicines: the act is held to proceed from the substance itself. The location is known because the substance was formerly extracted by autopsy."@en ;
    skos:note "The recorder adds that he believes the organ in question to be the small intestine in certain digestive periods. That is his identification and not the transmission's, and it is recorded here as his."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:ManomayaIddhi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Er zieht einen zweiten Körper aus diesem wie das Rohr aus der Scheide"@de,
        "He draws another body out of this one as a reed from its sheath"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.138-139 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, pp. 470-471; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "Success as the mind-made body, and the procedure for it. He rests from the basic absorption, adverts to the body and resolves let it be a hollow, and it is a hollow; he adverts to another body within himself, does the preliminary work and resolves, and there is another body within him; and he draws it out as a reed from its sheath, a sword from its scabbard, a snake from its slough."@en ;
    skos:note "The holding already carries pmc:Manomayakaya for the claim itself, from the second discourse of the Dīgha and from this chapter without a place. This node is the procedure and its limits, read at the place."@en,
        "Two limits on the created body stand in the chapter and are entered with it. It has the appearance of eye and ear and the rest but no sentient organ; and the bodies created by a disciple do only what the creator does, while only what a Buddha creates does other things and does them at his wish. The second is a rank distinction inside the capacity and not a caution."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:ManomayakayaIsDisplaced a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Der geistgemachte Körper ist ein verlagerter Ort"@de,
        "The mind-made body is a displaced locus"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta, DN i.77 (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:displacedLocus ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DighaNikaya ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:Manomayakaya ;
    pm:situationGround "The source has a second body, complete in its faculties, produced from this one and going where this one does not. That is the criterion for the displaced value; whether anything of the sort occurs is a separate question and not this one."@en .

pmc:MaoriEfficacyPresupposesExactRecitation a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Wirksamkeit einer Karakia setzt die fehlerfreie Wiederholung voraus"@de,
        "The efficacy of a karakia presupposes its repetition without error"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 11" ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity because the consequence is quotable. The same sentence is the tradition's own falsifying condition and is what makes the claim examinable at all; the protocol stands at pmc:RecitationExactnessExamination in convergences/recitation-exactness.ttl."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The witness says that to make an error, or even to hesitate, in repeating a karakia was held fatal to its efficacy. The consequence is stated for the whole address and not for a part of it, and hesitation is named alongside error." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:MaoriKarakiaEfficacy ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:MaoriExactRecitation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Maori .

pmc:MaqamatAreAbiding a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Stationen bleiben"@de,
        "The stations abide"@en ;
    dcterms:source "al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya, chapter on the stations" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:abidingState ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:Maqamat ;
    pm:situationGround "The handbooks have the station held by the one who reaches it, which is what makes the enumeration an ascent rather than a list of episodes."@en .

pmc:MaqamatAreByEffort a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Stationen werden durch Anstrengung erlangt"@de,
        "The stations are attained by effort"@en ;
    dcterms:source "al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya, chapter on the stations" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:byEffort ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:Maqamat ;
    pm:situationGround "The handbooks describe these as attained by effort and enumerated in a set order. The order is why prerequisite claims are found here and not at the other end of the axis."@en .

pmc:Marduk a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Marduk"@de,
        "Marduk"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Udug-hul 8: 26; KAR 31 (VAT 8803) obv. 2 and rev. 26 (M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons, BAM 8, De Gruyter 2016)" ;
    skos:altLabel "Asalluhi"@en ;
    skos:definition "The god who in the recurring dialogue of the series asks Ea what is to be done and is told to go and do it, and whose part the exorcist takes when he performs the instructed rite."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:nameForm "Marduk" ;
    pm:nameRole "the god sent by Ea to carry out the rite, and the one in whose role the exorcist acts" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmc:MayaAjSignExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Das gepflanzte Rohr als Meldung eines Todes andernorts"@de,
        "The planted cane as a report of a death elsewhere"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Popol Vuh: The Sacred Book of the Ancient Quiché Maya, English version by Delia Goetz and Sylvanus G. Morley from the Spanish translation of Adrián Recinos (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1950), part II, chapter 7" ;
    skos:definition "A protocol the transmission states for itself and states completely: a plant in one place is held to report the death or survival of a person in another, and both readings are fixed before the event. The claim is about a correspondence between two separated states of affairs, so it is decidable in a way that a claim about an inner state is not."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a protocol rather than as a remark because the tradition supplies every part a protocol needs and supplies it in advance: the observable, the two readings, the reader, and the moment of reading. What it does not supply is a case in which the reading was checked against the death."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, protocol stated from the witness; no case series has been collected" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A case in which the cane dried while the person who planted it lived, or sprouted again while he was dead, with the state of the plant recorded before the news arrived." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:MayaAjSign .

pmc:MayaIdolCarvingCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Schnitzer entzogen sich dem Auftrag"@de,
        "The carvers excused themselves from the commission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Diego de Landa, Yucatan Before and After the Conquest, translated by William Gates (Baltimore: The Maya Society, publication 20, 1937), section XL, pp. 76-77" ;
    skos:definition "A warning that comes from the practitioners and not from the priesthood. The witness reports that the craftsmen always excused themselves from the work, because they believed that either they or someone of their household would die, or would suffer heart attacks or strokes."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the rarer kind of caution: not a rule laid on the practice from above but a refusal by the people who would have to carry it out, with the harm they expected named. The named harms are sudden, bodily and unrelated to the work, which is what makes the belief hard to disappoint."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:MayaIdolCarving ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MayaRitual .

pmc:MayaIxYearRemedyAgency a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Abwehr der Übel wird dem Gott zugeschrieben, nicht den Feiernden"@de,
        "The averting of the foretold ills is credited to the god, not to the celebrants"@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:cededAgency ;
    pm:situatedNode pmp:MayaIxYearRemedy ;
    pm:situationGround "The witness does not say that the offerings work, but that they were held to be very acceptable to the idols and remedial through that acceptance. The efficacy is placed with the receiving party; the celebrants supply the occasion." .

pmc:MayaOfficePresupposesZuyuaNaat a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Matte und Thron setzen das Verstehen der Sprache von Zuyua voraus"@de,
        "The mat and the throne presuppose understanding the language of Zuyua"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ralph L. Roys, The Book of Chilam Balam of Chumayel (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1967), chapter IX, pp. 91-93" ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity because the consequence is quotable and because the text sets no lesser outcome beside it. There is no passage naming a second attempt, a number of chiefs to be examined, or a remedy for failure."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The source states the consequence for the whole procedure and states it as death, not as exclusion: those who die are those who do not understand, those who live will understand it. The punishments are named in a sequence. The hands of those who fail are bound before them to a wooden collar and they are pulled along with the cord; they are taken to the house of the ruler; they shall be hung by the neck, the tips of their tongues shall be cut off, their eyes shall be torn out. The chapter adds an omen for the case that the chiefs of the towns do not understand: ill-omened is the star adorning the night." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:PopDzam ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ZuyuaNaat ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MayaRitual .

pmc:MayaSafetyPresupposesUnbrokenFast a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Unversehrtheit setzt voraus, ein begonnenes Fasten nicht zu brechen"@de,
        "Remaining unharmed presupposes not breaking a fast once begun"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Diego de Landa, Yucatan Before and After the Conquest, translated by William Gates (Baltimore: The Maya Society, publication 20, 1937), section XL, p. 70" ;
    skos:note "The evil is not specified, which is why this prerequisite is examinable only as far as its own wording reaches: a rule whose penalty has no described shape cannot be found to have failed. That is a property of the passage and is worth having on the record beside the passages that do name a shape."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The witness states it as the reason the fasts were not broken: those who began these fasts did not dare to break them, for they believed it would bring evil upon them or upon their houses. The consequence attaches to the breaking of a fast begun and not to the omission of the fast." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:MayaHouseholdUnharmed ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:MayaNewYearFast ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MayaRitual .

pmc:MayaXibalbaSurvivalPresupposesUnconsumedTorch a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das lebende Verlassen setzt die unverbrauchte Rückgabe der Fackel voraus"@de,
        "Coming out alive presupposes returning the torch unconsumed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Popol Vuh: The Sacred Book of the Ancient Quiché Maya, English version by Delia Goetz and Sylvanus G. Morley from the Spanish translation of Adrián Recinos (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1950), part II, chapter 9" ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity because the consequence is reported for a named case with the condition quotable beside it, not because a rule is pronounced. Where a transmission gives the precedent instead of the rule, the precedent is what a later reader can check."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The transmission names the case and its outcome. Hun-Hunahpú and Vucub-Hunahpú burned the torch and the cigars through, and were sacrificed at once and buried at Pucbal-Chah. The consequence is not stated as a rule but exhibited as a precedent, and the later pair act on it." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:XibalbaSurvival ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:QuequmaHa ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MayaRitual .

pmc:MedicineEatingYieldsSeeing a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Essen der Medizinen bringt das Sehen hervor"@de,
        "Eating the medicines yields the seeing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 88-89" ;
    skos:note "The claim is not circular in the sense SPEC §11 forbids: the medicines are vegetable preparations named by their plants and the capacity is a perception ascribed to the eater, so the practice is not the capacity under another name."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:WitchDoctorMedicineEating ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:SeeingWitchcraft .

pmc:MideDegreesPresupposeOneAnother a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Jeder Grad setzt den darunter liegenden voraus"@de,
        "Each degree presupposes the one below it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), pp. 164-170" ;
    skos:note "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity, and the reason is a property of the passages read rather than of the society. The report sets the sequence, names the payment and the feasts due before each step, and says nothing about what happens to a man who takes a degree out of turn or without the preparation. A consequence would raise this to a necessity; none was found in the pages read. See prima-materia#119."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:MideInitiationSecondDegree ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:MideInitiationFirstDegree ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin .

pmc:MidewiwinAgeDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Gesellschaft dem europäischen Kontakt vorausgeht"@de,
        "Whether the society precedes European contact"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Harold Hickerson, The Chippewa and Their Neighbors: A Study in Ethnohistory (New York: Holt, Rinehart and Winston, 1970), p. 63, as quoted in Kidd 1981, p. 42",
        "Kenneth E. Kidd, A Radiocarbon Date on a Midewiwin Scroll from Burntside Lake, Ontario, Ontario Archaeology 35 (1981), pp. 41-43" ;
    skos:definition "A disagreement about the age of the society. One side reads it as a nativistic formation of the contact period, assembled from older elements in response to epidemic, trade and mission. The other points to a birchbark scroll fragment from an archaeological context, radiocarbon dated to about the middle of the sixteenth century, which is earlier than any documented European contact with the central Algonkians."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as a node because everything else in this file depends on it. If the society is a formation of the contact period, its agreement with anything the graph already holds has to be weighed differently, and the value at pm:contactRoute changes with the outcome."@en,
        "Only one side was read at the wording in this run. The dating, the laboratory reference and the argument against the nativistic reading were read in the note of nineteen eighty-one; the nativistic reading itself was read only as that note quotes it, and the paper of nineteen sixty-two that states it in full was not obtained. See prima-materia#118."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmt:Midewiwin .

pmc:MidewiwinOrigin a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Ursprung der Gesellschaft und die Kette ihrer Übertragung"@de,
        "The origin of the society and the chain of its transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), pp. 166-167" ;
    skos:definition "The account by which the society traces itself: the culture hero instructed the otter in its mysteries, gave him the sacred rattle and the drum, shot the shell into his body, and conferred the secrets on him."@en ;
    skos:note "The chain runs through an animal and not through a lineage of men, which is why the society's own account of its age cannot be read as a genealogy and does not bear on the dispute recorded above."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin .

pmc:Migis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Mī'gis"@de,
        "Mī'gis"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 167" ;
    skos:definition "The sacred shell of the society, said in the origin account to have been shot into the body of the first recipient so that he might have immortality and be able to confer the secrets on his kinsmen."@en ;
    skos:note "The shell is not a token of membership but the vehicle of the transfer: the account makes the entry of the shell into the body the act by which the capacity passes, and the same act is repeated at every degree."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin .

pmc:MithraicComparatioImposita a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Vergleich der beiden Diagramme wird vom Zeugen selbst verlangt"@de,
        "The comparison of the two diagrams is demanded by the witness"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.22 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV)" ;
    skos:definition "The frame in which both accounts reach us: Celsus adduces the Persian mysteries in order to say that whoever examines the Christian mysteries alongside them, and unveils the rites of the Christians, will see the difference; Origen answers that the comparison was chosen for polemical use, that Eleusis, Aegina, Egypt, Cappadocia, Thrace and Rome would have served as well, and that Celsus never says which sect uses the diagram he describes."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as a node because it decides how much the two accounts can be worth to each other. They are not two independent reports that happen to agree; they are one author's pairing, preserved by a second author who thought the pairing tendentious. Any correspondence between them is therefore at most a second occurrence in one channel, never a second witness — the same rule that keeps the star-formula of PGM IV apart from the ophite speeches."@en,
        "Origen's complaint carries a fact worth keeping: Celsus, who names sects wherever he can, does not name the one whose diagram he has. The ophite attribution of that diagram is not in the witness."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mithraic .

pmc:MithraicDisputatioGraduum a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Drei Zeugen, drei unvereinbare Auskünfte über die Grade"@de,
        "Three witnesses, three incompatible accounts of the grades"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Jerome, Epistula 107.2 (trans. A. S. Geden, London 1925)",
        "Nonnus the Mythographer, commentary on Gregory Nazianzen (trans. A. S. Geden, London 1925)",
        "Porphyry, De abstinentia IV.16 (trans. A. S. Geden, London 1925)" ;
    skos:definition "The conflict between the accounts of initiation this graph holds: seven named grades in a Latin letter of about 400, four animal ranks including women in a Greek report drawn from a lost history of the cult, and eighty ascending and descending disciplines in a Greek commentary of the sixth or seventh century."@en ;
    skos:note "None of the three knows of the others, and none is arguing. The conflict is the compiler's observation, and it is recorded because the seven-grade list is quoted everywhere as if it were the account rather than one of three. The others are not obviously worse witnesses: the four-name list comes from someone who had read a multi-volume history of the cult that is now lost, and the seven-name list from a bishop making a rhetorical point about a relative's baptism."@en,
        "The wall brings its own disagreement with it. Its planetary guardianships do not match those of the mosaic floor at Ostia, so the material channel is not the single voice the literary one lacks. See pmc:MithraicTutelaePlanetarum."@en,
        "What would decide it is not another text. The grades are attested outside the literary channel altogether, on a mosaic floor and in painted inscriptions, and one of those is now read: the painted procession at Santa Prisca has seven, labelled one by one, which settles the number for that room in 220 and against the eighty. It does not settle the four animal ranks, which name women and may describe another thing entirely."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:MithraicGradusSeptem ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mithraic .

pmc:MithraicLeonticaMelle a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Honig über die Hände derer, die in die Löwenweihe aufgenommen werden"@de,
        "Honey poured on the hands of those initiated in the rites of the Lion"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Porphyry, De antro nympharum, on honey in the Leontic rites (trans. Thomas Taylor, London 1917, section 7 of that edition's division)" ;
    skos:definition "The purification Porphyry reports from within the cult: those initiated into the Leontic rites have honey poured over their hands in place of water, and are charged by the initiator to keep their hands clean of everything harmful, injurious and abominable; and their tongue is likewise purified of evil with honey. Other initiators into the same mysteries use fire instead, as a purifying agent."@en ;
    skos:note "A named grade, a named substance, a stated substitution and a stated charge, all in one sentence — and the charge is a condition laid on the initiate rather than a promise made to him. That is rarer in this graph than any vision. What the passage does not say is what follows if the charge is broken."@en,
        "The substitution is the operative part. Water is what hands are washed with; honey is what preserves against decay, which is the property Porphyry has spent the surrounding pages establishing. The gesture therefore says that these hands are to be kept from corruption, not merely cleaned, and the alternative purification by fire says the same thing with the other means."@en,
        "Two further Mithraic details stand in the same passage and are held here rather than as separate nodes: the Persians offer honey to the guardian of fruits as a symbol of a preserving and warding power, and a bowl stands beside Mithras in place of a spring."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mithraic .

pmc:MithraicNamaAcclamatio a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Heil den Vätern, vom Aufgang bis zum Untergang"@de,
        "Hail to the fathers, from the east to the west"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Mithraeum of Santa Prisca, Rome, upper-layer dipinto above the seated figure, south wall (CIMRM 480, after Ferrua and Vermaseren-van Essen 1965)" ;
    skos:definition "The form of the painted captions: a Persian word of greeting, then the grade in the dative plural, then the guardianship. The one at the head of the procession extends the greeting in space — hail to the fathers from the east to the west, under the guardianship of Saturn."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also the cult using a Persian word in a Latin sentence, in Rome, on a wall painted by Syrians. The word is not translated and not explained, which is how a term of address behaves when everyone in the room knows it."@en,
        "The grammar is the finding. The acclamation is addressed to the grade and not spoken by its holder, and it is addressed to all of them at once, in the plural: not to this father in this room but to the fathers, as far as the sun goes. What the wall records is therefore not a formula the initiate says but the standing of the rank he is entering, said over him."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mithraic .

pmc:MithraicNatalisInitiationis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Geboren beim ersten Licht, am Saturntag, der Mond achtzehn Tage alt"@de,
        "Born at first light, on Saturn's day, the moon eighteen days old"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Mithraeum of Santa Prisca, Rome, graffito beside the cult niche (CIMRM 476, after Vermaseren-van Essen 1965)" ;
    skos:definition "The graffito scratched beside the cult niche of the same mithraeum: born at first light, in the consulship of the two Augusti Severus and Antoninus, on the twelfth day before the kalends of December, the day of Saturn, the eighteenth of the moon. It records an initiation, dated as a birth, on 20 November 202."@en ;
    skos:note "The most concrete single thing this tradition has left in its own hand, and three of its properties are worth keeping. It calls the initiation a birth without qualifying the word. It records the moment to the hour, with the consular year, the weekday and the age of the moon — that is the apparatus of a horoscope, cast for the second birth rather than the first. And it does not give a name."@en,
        "The omission of the name is what makes it more than a date. A man who writes his birth on a wall and leaves himself out of it is recording the event and not himself, and that is consistent with everything else this cult wrote down: the wall greets the fathers, not a father."@en,
        "The reckoning is exact enough to be checked against an ephemeris, which is unusual in this graph. Whether the eighteenth of the moon actually fell on that Saturday in 202 has not been checked here."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mithraic .

pmc:MithraicNominaAnimalium a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein anderer Satz von Gradnamen, und Frauen darunter"@de,
        "A different set of grade names, and women among them"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Porphyry, De abstinentia IV.16, reporting Eubulus (trans. A. S. Geden, Select Passages Illustrating Mithraism, London 1925)" ;
    skos:definition "The account Porphyry takes from Eubulus, who wrote a history of Mithraism in many books: that those who take part in the rites are called lions, the women lionesses or hyenas, the servants crows, and the fathers eagles and hawks; that the one invested with the character of the lion takes on the various forms of living creatures; and that the animal names signify the passage of human souls through various bodies, which the mysteries hold in common with the doctrine of the three grades of magi."@en ;
    skos:note "The reason given for the names is the transmigration of souls, and it is given as the cult's own reason rather than the reporter's. That makes the animal names a doctrine and not a taxonomy of ranks — an entirely different thing from a ladder of degrees, and one more reason not to merge this list with the other."@en,
        "This list is not Jerome's list. It has four ranks where his has seven, it names the fathers eagles and hawks, and it includes women — which the received account of this cult denies. Either the witness is describing a different stage of the cult, or a local form, or he is wrong; nothing in the text decides, and the graph does not decide either."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mithraic .

pmc:MithraicSedesAequinoctialis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Mithras sitzt am Äquinoktium, zwischen den beiden Toren"@de,
        "Mithras is seated at the equinox, between the two gates"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Porphyry, De antro nympharum, on the seat of Mithras at the equinoctial circle (trans. Thomas Taylor, London 1917, section 11 of that edition's division)" ;
    skos:definition "The position Porphyry assigns him: a place beside the equinoctial circle as his proper seat, holding the sword of Aries, a martial sign, and borne on the Bull, which is the sign of Venus, because Mithras like the Bull is demiurge and lord of generation; he stands with the northern parts on his right hand and the southern on his left."@en ;
    skos:note "Held apart from the ladder of Contra Celsum VI.22 on purpose. That account has seven gates in a line and a soul that traverses them; this one has two gates at the solstices and a god who does not traverse anything. They are two cosmologies for one cult, reported by two outsiders a generation apart, and the graph does not reconcile them."@en,
        "The two gates in this treatise are Cancer for the descent of souls and Capricorn for their ascent, and they are the doctrine of the theologians Porphyry follows — Numenius and Cronius — not a Mithraic teaching. What is Mithraic in the passage is only the seat: the god is placed at the crossing between the two, that is, at neither gate but at the point where descent and ascent are equal."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mithraic .

pmc:MithraicSpelaeumImagoMundi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Höhle ist ein Abbild der Welt, und ihre Ausstattung sind deren Gegenden"@de,
        "The cave is an image of the world, and its furnishings are its regions"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Porphyry, De antro nympharum, on the cave consecrated by Zoroaster (trans. Thomas Taylor, London 1917, section 2 of that edition's division)" ;
    skos:definition "The report Porphyry takes from Eubulus: that Zoroaster first consecrated to Mithras a naturally formed cave in the mountains of Persia, flowering and with springs, as a likeness of the world Mithras had made; and that the things placed in the cave were arranged at commensurate intervals, so as to be symbols of the elements and regions of that world. Wherever the god was known, Porphyry adds, he was approached in a cave, and the initiate of the Persians is admitted in a place they call a cavern, which signifies the descent of the soul into the sublunary and its return."@en ;
    skos:note "The chain of hands is three long and the middle link is lost. Porphyry reports Eubulus, whose work on Mithras does not survive; Eubulus reports the Persians. The graph holds the outermost link only, and every statement here about what Zoroaster did is at that distance."@en,
        "This is the only statement in the graph that a ritual space is laid out to scale. It does not merely say that the cave means the world; it says the interval between two things in it corresponds to the interval between two things in the world. A claim of that shape is checkable against a surviving sanctuary in a way that an allegory is not, and the check has not been made here — the seven semicircles of the mithraeum at Ostia are the obvious place to make it, and they are unread. See prima-materia#642."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mithraic .

pmc:MithraicTutelaePlanetarum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Jeder Grad unter der Obhut eines Planeten, neben seinen Träger gemalt"@de,
        "Each grade under the guardianship of one planet, painted beside its bearer"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Mithraeum of Santa Prisca, Rome, upper-layer dipinti of the south wall (CIMRM 480, after Ferrua and Vermaseren-van Essen 1965)" ;
    skos:definition "The captions painted above the procession of the seven grades on the south wall of the mithraeum beneath Santa Prisca in Rome, each naming a grade in the dative and the planet whose guardianship it stands under: the father under Saturn, the sun-runner under the Sun, the Persian under Mercury, the lion under Jupiter, the soldier under Mars, the bridegroom under Venus; the raven, of whom only traces remain, under the Moon."@en ;
    skos:note "And the wall does not agree with the other wall. At Ostia the Persian stands under the Moon and the raven under Mercury; here the two are exchanged, the Persian under Mercury and the raven under the Moon. The catalogue entry records the divergence in a footnote and moves on. It is not a small thing: the received table follows Ostia, drops this one, and presents the result as the Mithraic order."@en,
        "This is where the pairing of grade and planet actually comes from. No literary source has it: Jerome names the grades without stars, and the only ancient text that distributes planets over a sevenfold Mithraic structure, Contra Celsum VI.22, distributes them over gates and not over grades. Every modern table that seats a grade on a planet is reading a wall."@en,
        "Three Mithraic orderings are now in this graph and no two agree. The ladder of Contra Celsum VI.22 ascends Saturn, Venus, Jupiter, Mercury, Mars, Moon, Sun. This wall descends Saturn, Sun, Mercury, Jupiter, Mars, Venus, Moon. Ostia gives a third by exchanging two of them. Whatever governs the sequence, it is not one fixed doctrine, and an argument that derives any other seven-station scheme from the Mithraic order has to say which of the three it means. See prima-materia#595."@en,
        "What was read: the transcription of the seven dipinti in the catalogue entry for this monument, which itself depends in part on Ferrua's record of details already lost when the modern excavation began. Not the wall, not a photograph, and not the excavation report. Three of the seven captions are restored in part, and the seventh is a reconstruction from traces."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mithraic .

pmc:MouthToMouthOnly a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Wesentliche geht von Mund zu Mund und wird nicht geschrieben"@de,
        "The essential is passed from mouth to mouth and not written"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 8 (Shizhi), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 8" ;
    skos:definition "Of the bedchamber arts the chapter says that the great essential lies in one thing, and that this method is passed by the true men from mouth to mouth and is fundamentally not written down; that whoever takes the famed medicines without knowing this essential does not attain long life either; and that the older authorities set down only the coarse matters and never put the essential on paper."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because it is a statement the graph can use about its own limits rather than about a capacity. A transmission that says its decisive step was never written is telling every later reader, including this project, that the written corpus is not the transmission. The claim is unfalsifiable and its being so is the point; it is recorded as what the source says, not as an obstacle discovered here."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BaopuziNeipian ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:Mpangu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Mpangu, das ererbte Verbot"@de,
        "Mpangu, the inherited prohibition"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), pp. 245-246" ;
    skos:definition "A prohibition inherited in a family, with a penalty stated in advance and matched in kind to the thing forbidden: hippopotamus flesh and elephantiasis, a fish with opal eyes and diseased eyes, the great bull frog and bulging eyes, spotted animals and a spotted skin disease. A parent who keeps the prohibition is said to bless his children with immunity from that disease and one who breaks it to curse them with liability to it."@en ;
    skos:note "The corpus records its own counter-witness at the same place, and the node exists as much for that as for the rule. Some people told the recorder there are no such prohibitions in their families; and a well-informed man told him that anyone could eat what his inherited prohibition forbade without evil consequence, provided he ate it fearlessly and did not boast of it afterwards. That is a stated condition of failure for the prohibition itself, and it is stated by a member of the transmission."@en,
        "The same corpus notes that the animals under prohibition are not treated as omens and are not respected: they are killed like any others and only not eaten."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:KongoNkisi .

pmc:Nahualli a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Nahualli"@de,
        "Nahualli"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulo I, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces" ;
    skos:definition "The word as the recorder of sixteen twenty-nine glosses it: one veiled or disguised under the appearance of an animal. He connects the name with calendars he had found among such people, which distribute the names of animals across the days."@en ;
    skos:note "The gloss is the recorder's and is given in Spanish; the Nahuatl word is his lemma. That he offers an etymology at all is worth noting, because it shows he had the word from the people and not from a predecessor's book."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NahuaRitual .

pmc:NahualliGivenAtBirth a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Tier wird bei der Geburt zugeteilt"@de,
        "The animal is assigned when the child is born"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulo I, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces" ;
    skos:definition "The assignment is made at birth and not chosen later: the child is dedicated or subjected to the animal at the time it is born, on the strength of an agreement the recorder attributes to the parents."@en ;
    skos:note "The recorder frames the agreement as a pact with the devil, which is his own theology and not the tradition's account of itself. What is left when that frame is subtracted is the timing — at birth — and the fact that someone else contracts it for the child. Both are entered; the frame is not."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NahuaRitual .

pmc:NavajoEfficacyPresupposesExactness a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Wirksamkeit der Zeremonie setzt die Genauigkeit der Gesänge voraus"@de,
        "The efficacy of the ceremony presupposes exactness in the songs"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Washington Matthews, Navaho Legends, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society V (Boston and New York: Houghton, Mifflin, 1897), section 41, p. 24" ;
    skos:note "The consequence is graded rather than absolute: an error may be fatal, and only in some cases does a single syllable do irreparable harm. That grading is worth keeping, because the parallel claims elsewhere are usually reported without it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The witness says an error made in singing a song may be fatal to the efficacy of a ceremony, and that in some cases the error of a single syllable works an irreparable injury." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:NavajoCeremonyEfficacy ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:NavajoSongExactness ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NavajoCeremonial .

pmc:NavajoPublicErrorDetection a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Zuhörerschaft als Fehlerinstanz"@de,
        "The audience as the detector of the error"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Washington Matthews, Navaho Legends, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society V (Boston and New York: Houghton, Mifflin, 1897), section 42, pp. 24-25" ;
    skos:definition "The practice, at the public night ceremony, that many of those present know the songs well enough to catch a deviation, whereupon the ceremony is broken off as fruitless and the gathering disperses."@en ;
    skos:note "The part of the claim that makes it examinable rather than merely asserted. The tradition does not only say that an error destroys the effect; it names who notices, when, and what visibly follows. Neither of the two parallel witnesses in this bundle carries that."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NavajoCeremonial .

pmc:NeidanDuoshu a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Zwanzigtausend Marken gegen dreißigtausend Jahre"@de,
        "Twenty thousand marks against thirty thousand years"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhang Boduan (ascr.), Jindan sizi, preface, with Huang Ziru's commentary (DZ 1081), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, fol. 3b-4a" ;
    skos:definition "The preface states an exchange rate between the work and time. Within the twenty thousand marks one may seize the number of thirty thousand years of heaven; the reason given is that the labour of a single mark has in it the seasons of a whole year. It restates the same in two further forms: inwardly one refines in a year the elixir of thirty thousand marks, outwardly one body seizes the number of thirty thousand years; at the larger scale a single day binds an embryo of thirteen thousand five hundred breaths, at the smaller the twelve double-hours carry the breath eighty-four thousand li."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because it is a stated quantity and not a figure of speech: the text does the arithmetic twice and corrects its own rounding, giving the year as thirty-six thousand marks against the ten months' thirty thousand. What it asserts is not an experience but a rate, and a rate is the kind of claim that could be wrong."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:NeidanHuohou ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Neidan ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:NeidanShengtaiCapacity .

pmc:NeidanGuaxiangDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Hexagramme ein Werkzeug oder ein Verfahren sind"@de,
        "Whether the hexagrams are an instrument or a method"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhang Boduan, Wuzhen pian, in Wuzhen pian zhushi (DZ 145), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 2, fol. 5b-6a",
        "Zhang Boduan, Wuzhen pian, with Weng Baoguang's commentary (DZ 145), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 2, fol. 6a-6b" ;
    skos:definition "The poem holds that the images set in the hexagrams were models, that having the image one forgets the words and the sense is plain of itself, and that later deluded followers are mired in the images and practise by the hexagram-breaths in the hope of flying up. The commentary that stands under it does the opposite of dismissing the apparatus: it lays it out in full, the tripod and furnace as Qian and Kun, the medicine as Kan and Li, those four placed at the centre and exempt from the turning, the remaining sixty distributed through a month at two a day, ending and beginning again like the turning of a wheel, three hundred and sixty-four lines answering to a year with its intercalary remainder and to the three hundred and eighty-four grains of the catty — and only then says that once the fire phases are understood the images may be forgotten."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered separately from the dispute about the calendar, because it is a different question and the answers do not line up. Here the apparatus is granted and its use is said to be provisional; there the timings are either binding or empty. A transmission can hold the images to be a ladder to be kicked away and still set the fire by the solstice, and this one does."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:NeidanHuohouBuyongshi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Neidan .

pmc:NeidanJiunan a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die neun Schwierigkeiten"@de,
        "The nine difficulties"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhong-Lü chuandao ji, Lun monan 17, in the Chongkan Daozang jiyao recension (JY 149), juan 3, fol. 52a-53a" ;
    skos:definition "Nine conditions are named under which the work does not succeed, and every one of them is a circumstance of the practitioner's life rather than a state of his body or a fault in the method: the pressure of food and clothing, since before the great medicine is made he still feels cold and heat and hunger; the obstruction of elders, where duty to parents forbids leaving; the entanglement of affection for wife and children; the binding of name and profit; the intervention of calamity and illness, whether from a weak constitution or from a hidden reckoning; the constraint of a blind master, taken for an immortal on the strength of a ready tongue or an ancient face and found out later to be a man after gain; the discrepancy of doctrines, where blind masters and deluded friends point at side-tracks and small methods contradict one another; the slackening of will, which begins in the morning and is altered by evening; and the squandering of the years, losing the month within the year and the day within the month while the young forget nothing of name and profit and the old think of nothing but grandchildren."@en ;
    skos:note "The chapter closes the list with a clause that makes it all-or-nothing: only when these nine have been escaped can one serve the Way, and if one or two of the nine still cannot be dealt with, the labour is vain and the work cannot succeed. A list of nine ordinary human circumstances, of which none may remain, covers any course that did not succeed."@en,
        "The list is remarkable for what it does not contain. There is no item about the instructions being wrong, none about the method being unsuited to the person, and none about the outcome not arriving. Every clause is a reason why the practitioner did not get to practise properly."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:NeidanZhengyanXingchi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Neidan .

pmc:NeidanLiubuke a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die sechs Dinge, die nicht getan werden dürfen"@de,
        "The six things that may not be done"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhang Boduan (ascr.), Jindan sizi, preface, with Huang Ziru's commentary (DZ 1081), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, fol. 4a" ;
    skos:definition "Immediately before the passage on the aperture the preface sets six prohibitions in a row, and they cancel one another in pairs: one may not hold fast to non-action, and one may not give it shape in deliberate doing; one may not be mired in visualisation, and one may not cling to holding and guarding; one may not sit withered with a heart of dead ashes, and one may not practise blindly and refine in the dark. The reason given is that one fears not knowing where the material comes from, and fears not knowing the measure of the fire phases."@en ;
    skos:note "A set of prohibitions in which each pair excludes both a course and its contrary leaves no described course open, and the passage that follows says why: what is left is the aperture, which cannot be reasoned to and must be received orally. The six are therefore not six errors but the argument for the transmission."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:NeidanHuohou ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Neidan .

pmc:NeidanSanwanke a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Dreißigtausend Marken, und eine genügt"@de,
        "Thirty thousand marks, and one of them is enough"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhang Boduan (ascr.), Jindan sizi, preface, with Huang Ziru's commentary (DZ 1081), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, fol. 3b",
        "Zhang Boduan (ascr.), Jindan sizi, verse 17, with Huang Ziru's commentary (DZ 1081), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, fol. 11a" ;
    skos:definition "The preface reckons the work by the water-clock. A year has twelve months, a month thirty days, a day a hundred marks; a month is three thousand marks and ten months thirty thousand. Through the whole of that span the breath is to be held to whether walking, standing, sitting or lying. And then the condition: of those thirty thousand marks every single one must be brought into accord, and if one single mark departs from it the material is consumed and the fire phases fall short, whence the saying that a hair's breadth of difference makes no elixir. The seventeenth quatrain says the same in verse and the commentary repeats the reckoning."@en ;
    skos:note "The form is the same as the elixir-makers' clause at pmc:JinchengYaocheng, reached by a different route. There the failed batch is refired and always succeeds; here the failed run is referred to a fault too small to have been noticed. In both the outcome that would have told against the recipe has been given somewhere else to be."@en,
        "The most exactly quantified immunizing clause in this holding. It states a number of opportunities to fail, sets the tolerance at nil, and gives no way of telling from outside which mark went wrong or whether any did. Any run that produced nothing is covered by it, and the practitioner cannot show that it is not, because he cannot account for thirty thousand quarter-hours."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:NeidanHuohou ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Neidan .

pmc:NeidanShimo a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die zehn Dämonen, und die Regel, sie nicht anzuerkennen"@de,
        "The ten demons, and the rule that they are not to be acknowledged"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhong-Lü chuandao ji, Lun monan 17, in the Chongkan Daozang jiyao recension (JY 149), juan 3, fol. 53a-54a" ;
    skos:definition "Ten appearances are named, in three classes according to where they are met: present outside the body, in dreams, and in inner observation. The six thieves of the senses; wealth, as towers and painted beams and coral over the floor; rank, as gilt saddles and the tokens of office; the six passions, as mild days and then storm, thunder, music and weeping; affection, as kinsfolk in trouble, children ill, parents dead, brothers scattered, wives parted; calamity, as falling into a cauldron of fire, dropping from a height, poison, meeting brigands, dying under the law; the sages and worthies, as the thearchs and the high gods arriving in state; weapons, as massed cavalry, blades like frost, bows drawn and no escape; the music of women; and the beauty of women. The rule for all ten is one clause: the only right course is not to acknowledge them, for once acknowledged there is attachment, once attached there is grasping, and it is exactly this by which people fail to attain the Way."@en ;
    skos:note "The chapter tells the practitioner what to do when the appearance comes in inner observation, and the instruction is diagnostic in form: examine whether it is empty or solid, discriminate the true from the false, do not follow the wave and take the thief for your son, and raise the true fire of absorption to burn the body, at which the whole crowd of demons scatters at one sweep. What it does not supply is any mark by which the true would be told from the false, so the discrimination it demands has no criterion."@en,
        "The seventh is the one to keep. The appearance of the sages and the high gods in full procession is listed among the demons on the same footing as the appearance of wealth or of armed men, so that the arrival of what the practice promises is itself a form of its failure. A transmission that puts its own goal into the list of deceptions has stated something that costs it, and it is entered here for that reason."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:NeidanNeiguan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Neidan .

pmc:NeidanYizaiShengtai a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Zehn Monate Tragen und zwei Monate Baden ergeben ein Jahr"@de,
        "Ten months carrying and two months bathing make one year"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhang Boduan (ascr.), Jindan sizi, verse 20, with Huang Ziru's commentary (DZ 1081), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, fol. 11b" ;
    skos:definition "The last quatrain gives the term: the husband and wife meet, the bridal chamber makes cloud and rain, in one year a child is born and every one of them can ride the crane. The commentary breaks the year down — ten months of carrying the embryo and two months of bathing together make the one year — and says the child comes out through the upper cinnabar field."@en ;
    skos:note "The only term in this file that is short enough for a person to keep. A year is a span a practitioner could reach and a bystander could ask after, unlike the nine years and three years that stand elsewhere in the literature; and the corpus disputes those, as the node on the disagreement below records."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:NeidanHuohou ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Neidan ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:NeidanShengtaiCapacity .

pmc:NeidanZhuMi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das nach Körnern gewogene Elixier"@de,
        "The elixir weighed out, grain by grain"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhang Boduan (ascr.), Jindan sizi, verse 14, with Huang Ziru's commentary (DZ 1081), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, fol. 9b" ;
    skos:definition "The fourteenth quatrain has the two components seized into one place and refined with fire, one grain and then another, from the minute to the manifest. The commentary makes it a schedule by weight: one grain the size of a millet seed is produced each day, so that in thirty days the weight is thirty-eight grains and four hundredths, and in three hundred days three hundred and eighty-four grains, which is a square inch and weighs one catty."@en ;
    skos:note "The number is the Cantong qi's, and the file says so: three hundred and eighty-four is the count of the lines of the hexagrams, which Weng Baoguang's commentary on the Wuzhen pian gives in the same breath as the three hundred and eighty-four grains of a catty. What is being weighed is therefore a correspondence and not a substance, and the daily increment cannot be checked because nothing is on a scale."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Neidan .

pmc:NimittaVinasa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Verlust des Zeichens, seine benannten Ursachen und das Nachgehen, das es zurückholt"@de,
        "The loss of the sign, its named causes, and the retracing that fetches it back"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.29, Pali PTS [124]-[125] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, pp. 145-146; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.34, Pali PTS [126] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, p. 147; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga VI.59-63, Pali PTS [188]-[189] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, pp. 215-216; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "The handbook treats the loss of the sign three times and each time with a remedy. If the new concentration vanishes through some unsuitable encounter, he is to put on sandals, take a walking stick, go back to the place of the device and apprehend the sign again. The sign is to be guarded as one would guard the embryo of a world ruler: to him who guards it what he has got is not lost, and he who fails to guard it loses everything he got. And at the meditation on a corpse, where return is barred because the place is haunted by non-human beings and ranged by wild animals, or because the bloated corpse has discoloured after a day or two, he is to sit down and recollect the way of going and coming station by station — which gate, which quarter, the rock, the ant hill, the tree, the bush — until the sign appears as though set before him."@en ;
    skos:note "The one term in the passage is a property of the material and not of the practice: a bloated corpse discolours after a day or two, and after that the sign cannot be re-apprehended at its source. The handbook says of this subject that of all the meditation subjects none is so hard to get."@en,
        "What the three passages have in common is that none of them ends. Every loss of the sign is met with a further operation, and the handbook nowhere names a point at which the practitioner is to conclude that the sign will not come back. The search that established that stands at pmp:VisuddhimaggaTerminusSearch."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:NineYearsForTheSuccessor a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Nachfolger binnen neun Jahren"@de,
        "The successor within nine years"@en ;
    dcterms:source "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXX, sect. 141, pp. 382-383" ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity: a period is named and the outcome of letting it pass is named with it. A dated deadline with a stated consequence is the material a condition of failure is made of, and this graph holds few of them."@en,
        "The observed cost of an unfilled interval is reported separately in the same section: the free clan spirits try one person after another and make them abnormal, sometimes for years. The recorder watched such a case daily for nearly two months among the Khingan Tungus."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The new shaman who gathers the spirits left free must appear not later than nine years after the shaman's death; otherwise there will be many people suffering from the spirits."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:TungusFormalRecognition ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:TungusMasteringOfSpirits ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SiberianShamanism .

pmc:NippurAbraxas a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Abraxas, Hüter der guten Geister"@de,
        "Abraxas, guardian of good spirits"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, texts 7: 9, 19: 11 and 34: 9, with introduction § 11, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 57, 147, 196 and 231)" ;
    skos:note "Three bowls of three different dialects invoke him. The editor notes that the numerical value of the letters, which is what the name was built on, has worn away in these spellings, so that the word is used as a name without the reckoning behind it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:nameForm "Abrakas",
        "Abrakis" ;
    pm:nameRole "the great Abraxas, the guardian of good spirits and destroyer of evil ones; in a Syriac bowl the mighty lord by whose seal the binding is made" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurAgzariel a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Agzariel, der ohne Erbarmen ist"@de,
        "Agzariel, who is without compassion"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 19: 11, with introduction § 13, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 100 and 196)" ;
    skos:note "Entered because of the rule the editor states for this nomenclature, that the first element of the name expresses the object desired in the incantation; this is the case where the characterisation is spelt out in the clause instead of being left in the name."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:nameForm "Agzariel" ;
    pm:nameRole "an angel invoked in the pagan bowl and characterised by the one thing wanted of him, that he is without compassion" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurAngelsWhoseWordNoneTransgresses a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Negoznai, Be'odai, Buznai und Darwa"@de,
        "Negoznai, Be'odai, Buznai and Darwa"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, texts 38: 6-8 and 40: 17-19, with introduction § 13, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 96, 243 and 253)" ;
    skos:note "One of these names is shared with a lilith in the same series of bowls: the lilith charmed in the second Mandaic text is Buznai, and the word that charms her is the word of the granddaughter of the angel Buznai. The editor remarks that a number of the Mandaic names of this collection are not found in the known Mandaic literature."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:nameForm "Be'odai",
        "Buznai",
        "Darwa",
        "Negoznai" ;
    pm:nameRole "the angels of the two Mandaic bowls, by whose word and command the lilith is charmed and by whose seal she is bound; of the second of each pair it is said that his word none transgresses" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurArionSonOfZand a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Arion, Sohn des Zand"@de,
        "Arion son of Zand"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, texts 19: 13 and 19: 17 and 34: 8, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 196-197 and 231)" ;
    skos:note "Named in two bowls of two different dialects, in both at the point where the sealing rather than the charming is assigned. The name is one of the places where the editor says it is impossible to decide whether a man, a deity or a mere charm-word is meant."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:nameForm "Arion son of Zand" ;
    pm:nameRole "the bearer of a signet by which a house is sealed, and in the pagan bowl the one by whom the sealing of the second of each pair of operations is done: charmed by the great gods and sealed by Arion son of Zand" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurBagdana a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Bagdana, König der Dämonen"@de,
        "Bagdana, king of the demons"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, texts 11: 5 and 19: 10 and 19: 13, with introduction § 13, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 100, 170 and 196)" ;
    skos:note "Named in a Rabbinic bowl as the one whose binding the charm is for, and in the long pagan invocation as a lord with a cult: the seventy exalted priests of Bagdana are invoked beside him. The editor notes that in the Mandaic bowls of another collection the same name appears as a demon, Abugdana."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:nameForm "Bagdana" ;
    pm:nameRole "their king and ruler, the king of demons and devils and the great ruler of the liliths; in a pagan bowl of the same collection he is Bagdana son of Habal, that is of destruction, and has seventy exalted priests" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurBanAscribedToJoshuaBarPerahia a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Joshua bar Perahia sandte den Bann und schrieb den Brief"@de,
        "Joshua bar Perahia sent the ban and wrote the writ"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, texts 8: 6-9, 17: 8-10 and 32: 4-5, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 153, 189-190 and 224)" ;
    skos:note "The ascription is stated inside the operation and in the perfect tense: it is announced to you that Rabbi Joshua bar Perahia has sent against you the ban; and thus has spoken to us Rabbi Joshua bar Perahia; and in the Syriac bowl, a work which has been made like that which Rab Jesus bar Perahia sat and wrote against them. The operator of the present bowl does not claim the act, he claims to be repeating one."@en,
        "The mode is textual and not third-party ascription, because what was read is the passage in which the claim stands and not a report that someone made it. Whether a master of the first century before this era wrote such a writ is a question this holding cannot put; that six bowls of three dialects ground their authority in him is one."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmp:NippurGetAgainstDemons ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:NippurJoshuaBarPerahia ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurBowlNotToBeBroken a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Vergreife dich nicht an dieser Presse"@de,
        "Do not transgress against this press"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 6: 11-12 with the commentary, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 142-143)" ;
    skos:definition "The one penalty clause in the corpus, and the only caution in it that protects the operation itself rather than the client. Whoever will transgress against this press and does not accept these rites shall split asunder violently and burst in the midst, and the sound of him shall resound with the resonance of brass in the spheres of heaven, and his abode shall be in the seventh hell of the sea, from this day and forever."@en ;
    skos:note "The editor reads the close of the text as a threat against any who destroy the bowl and ignore its ban, which makes the clause run against two different readers at once: the demons who are to accept the rites, and the human being who might lift the vessel out of the ground. A charm whose whole efficacy lies in an object buried a hand's breadth under the floor has exactly one physical vulnerability, and this is the sentence that answers it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:NippurBowlInversion ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurChildbirthNames a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Sanui, Sansanui und Semniglaph"@de,
        "Sanui, Sansanui and Semniglaph"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 42 with the commentary, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 259-260)" ;
    skos:note "Entered because the order of what follows them is itself a finding the editor marks: Adam, Yhwh, Kadmon, in that sequence, and then Life and Lilith. He also takes a further pair of words in the heading, one of them reduplicated, to mean divorced."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:nameForm "Sansanui",
        "Sanui",
        "Semniglaph" ;
    pm:nameRole "the three names standing at the head of the childbirth amulet, before Adam, Yhwh, Kadmon, Life and Lilith; the editor says the first two are common in childbirth charms and reads the second as the inscribed Name" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurCommissionAscribedToTheFiveDeities a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Fünf Gottheiten beauftragten, sandten, befahlen, sprachen und gaben Macht"@de,
        "Five deities charged, sent, commanded, spoke and gave power"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 36: 3-6, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 237-238)" ;
    skos:note "Five verbs for five powers, in the first person, inside the text the operator recites. The capacity ascribed is not a skill but a licence, and the bowl treats the licence as the thing that makes the expulsion possible: because he has given me a name by which I shall drive thee forth."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:NippurCommissionFromTheFiveDeities ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:NippurFiveCommissioningDeities ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurCommissionBeforeExpulsion a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Beauftragung durch die Götter geht dem Austreiben voraus"@de,
        "The commission of the gods comes before the driving out"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 36: 2-6, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 237-238)" ;
    skos:note "The clearest chain in the corpus, because the text names the transfer that licenses the act and then names the act: the lord Sames has charged me against thee, Sin has sent me, Bel has commanded me, Nannai has said to me, and Nirig has given me power to go against the evil spirit; and three lines later, because he has given me a name by which I shall drive thee forth, Evil Spirit, go from the presence of these angels. What must come first is not a purification of the operator but an authority conferred on him, and the name is part of what is conferred."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:NippurText36BridalMotherhoodSecured ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:NippurCommissionFromTheFiveDeities ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurCountedSealings a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die gezählten Sprüche und Siegel einer Bindung"@de,
        "The counted spells and seals of a binding"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, texts 5: 2-3, 6: 7-8, 19: 4 and 23: 3, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 137-142, 196 and 202)" ;
    skos:definition "Several texts state the strength of a binding as a count. One binds with the seven spells and seals with the seven seals. One charms and seals and counterseals with the seven spells which may not be loosed and with the eight seals which may not be broken. One seals a house with three signets and counterseals it with seven seals. One presses the demons down by means of seven words, each attached to a named power, by which heaven and earth are charmed."@en ;
    skos:note "No text read says what follows if a count falls short, and none prescribes a repetition of the operation. The counts are the measure of the binding and not a dose to be kept."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurCounterCharmAsCauseOfFailure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Gegenverrichtung, die das Werk auflöst"@de,
        "The rival operation that undoes the work"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, texts 6: 2 and 6: 9, 12: 9, 16: 9, 32: 8 and 39: 7, with introduction § 11, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 53, 141, 174, 188, 225 and 248)" ;
    skos:definition "The third condition of failure in this corpus, and the only one that names a cause rather than a sign: the work can be undone by another working, and the bowls list such workings among what they must break. Counter-charms stand in the lists of things pressed down, frustrated and banned in four bowls; one Syriac bowl says of the master it works under that he climbed to the height of heaven and learned all counter-charms; and one Mandaic bowl names the rival operations outright, the evil mysteries, the magic circle of malignant masters and sages and doctors, and the melting of wax figures of him who is alive."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure and not as a caution, because what it names is why an operation would not take. It is also the one of the three that the corpus treats as ordinary: a counter-charm is not a warning about a mistake but a standing feature of the field in which this craft works."@en,
        "The editor states the same thing from the vocabulary: the exorcist's effort for his client includes his defence and his supernatural arming, and involves the breaking of counter charms and the wiles of the devils, for which he lists eight roots; and he notes that in the Talmud the verb for breaking a charm is the technical opposite of the verb for binding one."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurDateBeforeTheWrit a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Datum steht am Kopf des Scheidebriefs"@de,
        "The date stands at the head of the writ"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 17: 1-2 with the commentary to 8: 7 and introduction § 11, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 55, 159 and 189-191)" ;
    skos:note "One divorce bowl opens with a form of date, this day above any day, years and generations of the world, and the first person then divorces. The editor notes that a date was a requisite in the legal writ and that the mutilated opening of the parallel bowl is the same formula misunderstood. The two things the holding would otherwise keep apart, the singled-out moment of the operation and the date of a legal instrument, are here the same words."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:NippurGetAgainstDemons ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:NippurPropitiousSeason ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurDemonTakesABorrowedShape a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Was erscheint, kann eine geliehene Gestalt tragen"@de,
        "What appears may be wearing a borrowed shape"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, texts 1: 12-13, 6: 3-4 and 39: 9-10, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 117-118, 139 and 247)" ;
    skos:definition "A caution the texts state about recognition and not about conduct. The liliths appear to mankind, to men in the likeness of women and to women in the likeness of men, and with mankind they lie by night and by day; they attach themselves to a household, dwell in its archways, lurk by its thresholds, and appear to it in one form and another; and in the sharpest case a lilith appears to a pregnant woman in the shape of her sister's daughter, who is named."@en ;
    skos:note "The caution names no mark by which the borrowed shape could be told from the person it copies. That is what makes it a caution and not a test: it tells the client that an appearance may be false and gives nothing with which to decide a particular appearance. The Mandaic bowl also has the lilith appear in dreams of night and visions of day, which is the same class of event the charms elsewhere forbid."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:NippurDemonsDepartAndDoNotReturn ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurDlibat a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Dlibat die Leidenschaftliche"@de,
        "Dlibat the passionate"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 28: 5, with introduction § 13, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 100 and 211)" ;
    skos:note "Invoked in the same breath as an angel with a Hebrew name, in a bowl the editor calls definitely pagan. The pairing is the corpus's and not a later conflation: one clause names the angel, the next names the planet-goddess and the lords of the mysteries."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:nameForm "Dlibat" ;
    pm:nameRole "the passionate one, invoked with the gods, the lords of all the mysteries, in the love-charm; a Semitic Venus by the editor's identification" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurDodibTheStrangler a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Dodib, den man den Erwürger nennt"@de,
        "Dodib, whom they call the Strangler"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 36: 4-5, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, p. 237)" ;
    skos:note "The one being in this collection whose harm is stated as a stage of life rather than as an act: it is named for what it does to the unborn, and the charm that names it is made out for a bride, before there is a child to protect."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:nameForm "Dodib" ;
    pm:nameRole "the evil spirit called the Strangler, who kills the young in the womb of their mothers; such spirits are called Slayer and their fathers Destroyer" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurEditorsArrangement a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Anordnung des Korpus nach Dialekt und Nummer"@de,
        "The arrangement of the corpus by dialect and number"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1913"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, introduction §§ 1, 4 and 14, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 15, 103 and the preface at p. 11)" ;
    skos:definition "The order in which this corpus is read is the editor's and not the corpus's. Forty bowls selected from a collection of over 150 numbers are numbered 1 to 40 and grouped by the language and script of their inscriptions: texts 1 to 30 in the dialect of the Babylonian Talmud, texts 31 to 37 in a Syriac dialect, texts 38 to 40 in Mandaic, with an inscribed skull as 41 and a form of the Lilith legend as 42. The three groups are set out in one script in print because two of the three types were not to be had in an American printing house, which the editor says would have been more correctly presented in their several scripts."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because the alternative is that a later reader takes the three groups for three traditions. The numbering itself is the locus of every source string in this file and is carried as such, not as a second scheme of identity."@en,
        "The grouping is a classification and not a stratification, and the editor says so against his own arrangement: the same persons and families appear in all three classes, so no Judaic, Syriac and Mandaic layers representing different ages or racial elements are to be supposed. The order of reading therefore divides what the find does not."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:compiledBy "James A. Montgomery, in the edition of 1913" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurElijaMeetsTheLilith a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Elija der Prophet, der der Lilith auf dem Weg begegnet"@de,
        "Elija the prophet, who meets the Lilith on the road"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 42, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 259-260)" ;
    skos:note "The frame is a narrative and is entered because it carries the operative clause of the amulet: the Lilith answers that every time that they repeat or she sees her names written, it will not be in the power of her or of all her band to do evil or harm. Her own account of what she was going to do is as exact as the charm against it: to give the woman the sleep of death, to take the child, to suck his blood, to suck the marrow of his bones and to devour his flesh."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:nameForm "Elija the prophet" ;
    pm:nameRole "the one who meets the Lilith with all her band on the road, asks her where she is going, restrains her with a ban from the Name, hears her oath, and then adjures her and her band not to come to the woman in childbirth" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurFifteenNamesOfTheLilith a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Die fünfzehn Namen, die die Lilith von sich angibt"@de,
        "The fifteen names the Lilith gives of herself"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 42 with the commentary, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 259-261)" ;
    skos:note "The list is given by the being bound, not by the operator, and the text makes the writing of it the whole working part of the amulet. The editor identifies the second as the Mandaic genius Abatur, prefers the alternative reading of the copy on the strength of the Greek parallels, and reads the fourth as the Greek word for the shapeless."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:nameForm "Abikar",
        "Abitar",
        "Abnukta",
        "Ailo",
        "Amorpho",
        "Batzeh",
        "Hakas",
        "Kali",
        "Kephido",
        "Kitsa",
        "Lilith",
        "Matrota",
        "Odam",
        "Satriha",
        "Taltui" ;
    pm:nameRole "the names the Lilith names of herself when she is restrained, and whose writing is the condition on which she and her band have no power to do evil or harm" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurFourBowlsBeforeTheCircuit a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Vier Niederlegungen gehen dem Umkreis des Hauses voraus"@de,
        "Four deposits are wanted before the house stands in a circuit"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 4: 1-2 with introduction § 8 and texts 21-23, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 42, 132 and 202-204)" ;
    skos:note "The text itself declares the spirits bound and sealed in each one of the four corners of the house, so the four deposits are stated and not inferred. What is the editor's is the reading of the four points as a circle of magical influence about the dwelling, and the inference that the three surviving duplicates of one client are three of such a set."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:NippurFourCornerCircuit ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:NippurBowlInversion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurIbbolAndIbboleth a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Herr Ibbol und unsere Herrin Ibboleth"@de,
        "Lord Ibbol and our lady Ibboleth"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 19: 5-6 and 19: 16-17, with introduction § 13, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 100 and 196-197)" ;
    skos:note "A male and a female form of one name, which the editor takes for the Gnostic Aeons in pairs. What the text does with them is more exact than the identification: the two halves of the operative formula, the charming and the sealing, are assigned to the two of them separately, and the same division runs through the whole series of repetitions in that bowl."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:nameForm "Ibbol",
        "Ibboleth" ;
    pm:nameRole "the great king of the Bagdani and the great queen of the goddesses, by whom the charming and the sealing of the same operation are divided: charmed by Ibbol and sealed by Ibboleth" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurIshtars a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Die Ischtars und die mächtige Istar"@de,
        "The Ishtars, and the mighty Istar"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, texts 38: 9-10, 40: 21 and 37: 6 and 37: 11, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 241-253)" ;
    skos:note "The same name stands on both sides of the operation in one collection: a thing to be bound in the two Mandaic bowls, a source of peace in the mutilated pagan bowl that closes with peace from the male gods and from the female Ishtars. The editor reads the mighty Istar of the first Mandaic bowl as possibly identical with the named lilith of the same text."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:nameForm "Ishtars",
        "Istar" ;
    pm:nameRole "in the Mandaic bowls a power to be charmed, confined, restrained and hobbled away from the client's body together with the gods, temple-spirits, shrine-spirits and idol-spirits; in the pagan Syriac bowl the female Ishtars from whom peace is wished" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurLilith a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Lilith"@de,
        "Lilith"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, texts 8: 2-4 and 17: 3-4, with introduction § 10, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 49, 153 and 189)" ;
    skos:definition "The generic name is differentiated by species inside the formula, so that one address covers them all: thou Lilith, male Lili and female Lilith, Hag and Ghul; and then the three of you, the four of you, the five of you. The same bowl moves between singular and plural on purpose, to take in both the particular lilith and the whole brood."@en ;
    skos:note "The editor's remark on why these predominate: where women are the clients, the greater part of the magic has to do with the maladies of the sexual life, and the lilis and liliths are personifications of them. That is his reading and not the texts'. What the texts do state is the list of harms: appearing in dream and slumber, lying with the client, killing the unborn and the newborn."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:nameForm "Lili",
        "Lilith" ;
    pm:nameRole "the chief object of exorcism in this corpus: a spirit that attaches itself to a married person, lies with them by night and by day, and kills their children" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurLilithHalbas a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Lilith Halbas, Enkelin der Lilith Zarni"@de,
        "Lilith Halbas, granddaughter of Lilith Zarni"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 11: 6, with the commentary to 18: 5, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 170 and 193)" ;
    skos:note "The one place in the collection where a lilith is adjured by her own name and her grandmother's. The genealogy is there because the instrument requires it: a writ names both parties with their parentage, so the demon must be given one. The editor notes that the name of the grandmother is the one element of this text whose tradition is accurate across all four of its duplicates."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:nameForm "Halbas" ;
    pm:nameRole "a named individual lilith, given a genealogy so that the divorce-writ can name her as a party: she dwells in the house of the named woman and plagues boys and girls" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurLilithParentsPalhasAndPelahdad a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Palhas der Vater und Pelahdad die Mutter der Lilithen"@de,
        "Palhas the father and Pelahdad the mother of the liliths"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, texts 8: 6-9 and 17: 6-11, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 153-155 and 189-191)" ;
    skos:note "Two bowls give the pair, the second with the mother's name in the form Palhan, and both adjure the liliths by the glory of the father and the name of the mother. The naming rule the craft applies to its clients is here applied to the demons, which is what allows the writ to be drawn at all."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:nameForm "Palhan",
        "Palhas",
        "Pelahdad" ;
    pm:nameRole "the parentage by which the liliths are identified in the divorce-writ, announced to them for their advisement and their terrification, and sworn by in the adjuration" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurLoveAngels a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Rahmiel, Habbiel und Hanniniel, die Engel der Liebe"@de,
        "Rahmiel, Habbiel and Hanniniel, the angels of love"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, texts 13: 3-7 and 28: 5, with introduction § 13, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 100, 177 and 211)" ;
    skos:note "The editor states the rule these names follow: the first, verbal element of an angel's name expresses the object desired in the incantation, so the three names of the love-charm are three words for loving. The first of them is invoked again in the second love-charm of the collection, beside a Semitic Venus."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:nameForm "Habbiel",
        "Hanniniel",
        "Rahmiel" ;
    pm:nameRole "the three angels of the love-charms, whose office is to pity and love and compassionate and embrace the woman who commissions the bowl, to sit with her on this side and on that, and to clothe her in the garment of the grace of God" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurMightyDestroyer a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Der mächtige Verderber, der Mann von Frau trennt"@de,
        "The Mighty Destroyer who parts husband from wife"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 3: 2-3, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 126-128)" ;
    skos:note "The name is given in consonants the edition prints without vowels, and the role is stated in a single clause repeated in both directions. The whole of that bowl is the adjuration of this one being, with the great name set against it four times over."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:nameForm "SP'SK" ;
    pm:nameRole "the Mighty Destroyer, who kills a man from the side of his wife and a woman from the side of her husband, and sons and daughters from their father and from their mother, walking by day and by night" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurNamingBeforeBinding a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die genaue Nennung geht der Bindung voraus"@de,
        "The exact naming comes before the binding"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, introduction § 10, with the opening lines of texts 1, 10 and 12, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 49-50, 117, 163 and 173)" ;
    skos:note "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity: every text read opens with the names and no text says what happens to a charm whose names are wrong or missing. That the individuals must be exactly specified is the editor's sentence, drawn from the invariance of the practice across forty-two texts."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:NippurBowlInversion ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:NippurNamingOfClientAndMother ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurNoNameExhaustsTheBeing a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Kein einzelner Name schöpft das Beschworene aus"@de,
        "No one name exhausts what is being conjured"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, texts 14: 6, 29: 9 and 35: 8, with introduction § 11, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 58, 182, 217 and 235)" ;
    skos:definition "The craft's warning against its own instrument. Since the whole operation rests on naming, the texts provide for the case that the name is not enough: the blast-demons and evil injurers are bound whose names are recorded in this bowl and whose names are not recorded in this bowl; the injurers are adjured whose names I have mentioned and whose names I have not mentioned; and the idol-spirits are guarded against who are known and who are not known by name."@en ;
    skos:note "The editor states the principle behind the clause: both Jewish and pagan magic required the accumulation of as many names of the deity or demon as possible, for fear lest no one name exhaust the potentiality of the spiritual being conjured. He sets the fifty names of Marduk and the hundred names of Allah beside it. The clause is also what makes the long name-lists of this corpus a provision against failure rather than ornament."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:NippurNamingOfClientAndMother ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurOperatorArmedWithIronAndFire a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Handelnde kommt in Eisen und Feuer gewappnet, in Hermes gekleidet"@de,
        "The operator comes armed in iron and fire, vested in Hermes"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, texts 2: 1-2 and 27: 3-4, with introduction § 9, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 47, 120 and 211)" ;
    skos:note "Stated in the first person at the head of the bowl: again I come, I Pabak bar Kufithai, in my own might, on my person polished armour of iron, my head of iron, my figure of pure fire; I am clad with the garment of Armasa and the Word, and my strength is in him who created heaven and earth. The second half of the same bowl has the other party say it for him, so the two men work the charm for each other in turn."@en,
        "The mode is first-person report and not textual attestation, because what the place carries is the operator's own account of his state at the moment of the operation. The editor sets it beside a Palestinian mortuary charm in which the magician introduces himself with the wand of Moses, the plate of Aaron, the seal of Solomon, the shield of David and the mitre of the chief priest; that charm was not opened here."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:NippurOperatorNamesHisOwnWork ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:NippurArmasaHermes ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurPalsaPelisa a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Palsa-Pelisa, Scheider-Geschiedener"@de,
        "Palsa-Pelisa, Divorcer-Divorced"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 8: 7, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, p. 153)" ;
    skos:note "A name built out of the act it performs, in the active and the passive of the same root, and set in the writ at exactly the point where a court would name the authority under which the instrument issues."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:nameForm "Palsa-Pelisa" ;
    pm:nameRole "the power in whose name the writ renders the demon her divorce and her separation; the editor glosses the double name as Divorcer-Divorced" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurPowerInheritedInTheFamily a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Handelnde beruft sich auf die Kraft seines mächtigen Verwandten"@de,
        "The operator claims the virtue of his mighty relative"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 34: 2 with the commentary and text 8: 11, with introduction §§ 1 and 9, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 14, 47, 155, 229 and 232-233)" ;
    skos:note "One Syriac bowl seals a house by power of the virtue of Jesus the healer and by the virtue of my mighty relative, and a Rabbinic bowl of the same collection has the sorcerer call himself a cousin of the master and refer to his house, which the editor takes for his school. The claim is not to a borrowed name but to a descent, and it is the nearest thing to a line of transmission this corpus states about its own operators."@en,
        "What stands against reading it as a lineage: the operator of this corpus is otherwise anonymous, the editor judges that some of the more illiterate texts were written by lay people, and he notes that a layman who wrote his own bowl saved the exorcist's fee. The two readings are not decided here."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:NippurOperatorNamesHisOwnWork ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:NippurJoshuaBarPerahia ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurReappearanceTest a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Wiedererscheinen des Geistes, das die Zauber verbieten"@de,
        "The reappearance of the spirit, which the charms forbid"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, texts 6: 9-11, 8: 11 and 8: 16, 11: 8, 20: 4 and 26: 5-6, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 142, 155-156, 170, 201 and 209)" ;
    skos:definition "A protocol this corpus states for itself, and states in the form of a prohibition rather than of a sign. The things the bowls forbid the spirits to do are observable by the client and are named one by one: appearing in dream by night or in slumber by day, appearing by day or by night or at any evening or morning, approaching the right side or the left, killing the children, having power over the property, returning at all from this day and forever. Any one of them occurring after the bowl is laid is an outcome the wording of the bowl has excluded."@en ;
    skos:note "No case series exists and none is proposed here. The forty-two texts name no instance of a bowl that failed, no second attempt, no renewal of an expired charm and no complaint of a client."@en,
        "What makes this a protocol rather than a wish is that the corpus itself picks the observables, and that they are all events the household would notice without being told what to look for. What weakens it is the same corpus's formulae of perpetuity: a binding that holds until the dissolution of heaven and earth, seals that may not be broken, a charm from which none ever goes forth. Those clauses set no interval, so a reappearance can always be assigned to a term not yet reached."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, protocol stated from the witness; no case series has been collected" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A household for which a bowl was laid in which a child afterwards died, or the property was afterwards lost, or the lilith afterwards appeared in sleep, reported from the same source that reports the laying of the bowl. The corpus states the five events as excluded from this day and forever, so one recorded occurrence after a laying falls due against the claim." ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-10-01"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:NippurDemonsDepartAndDoNotReturn .

pmc:NippurSealOfSolomon a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "König Salomo, Sohn Davids, und sein Siegel"@de,
        "King Solomon son of David, and his seal"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, texts 34: 8-9 and 39: 10-11, with introduction §§ 8 and 11, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 41, 53, 231 and 247)" ;
    skos:note "Two bowls use the seal, one Syriac and one Mandaic. The editor sets the usage in the Jewish legends of Solomon's power to confine demons in vessels, which is the clue by which the purpose of the inverted bowl was first understood at all; the seventy seals of Solomon stand in a bowl of another collection and not in these."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:nameForm "Solomon son of David" ;
    pm:nameRole "the king by whose seal the Oppressors and the Latbe were sealed, and whose seal seals the defiling ghosts away from a pregnant woman" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurSealsOfAdamAndNoah a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Der erste Adam und Noah als Siegler"@de,
        "The First Adam and Noah, as sealers"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 10: 3-5 with the commentary, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 163-166)" ;
    skos:note "A sealing justified by two earlier sealings, which is the form this corpus uses whenever it wants a warrant: the same bowl that invokes them seals a woman, her husband, her son, her house and her whole threshold. The editor observes that it is the Babylonian story and not the biblical one in which the hero shuts himself in."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:nameForm "Noah",
        "the First Adam" ;
    pm:nameRole "the two precedents one bowl names for its own sealing: the seal with which the First Adam sealed Seth his son so that he was preserved from demons, devils, tormentors and satans, and the seal with which Noah sealed the ark from the waters of the Deluge" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurSealsThatCannotBeLoosed a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sprüche, die nicht gelöst, und Siegel, die nicht gebrochen werden können"@de,
        "Spells that may not be loosed and seals that may not be broken"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, texts 19: 4 and 19: 14, 32: 5 and 32: 9, 34: 11, 38: 8 and 40: 19, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 196, 224-226, 231, 245 and 253)" ;
    skos:definition "The formulae with which this craft forecloses the undoing of its own work. A body is charmed with the seven spells which may not be loosed and with the eight seals which may not be broken, and of the same bowl it is said that from whose charm none ever goes forth and from whose control none sallies forth. A Syriac bowl declares its ban-writ charmed and sealed and countersealed even as ancient runes fail not. A second declares the binding to hold until the dissolution of heaven and earth, and two Mandaic bowls seal by an angel whose word none transgresses. The ordinary close of a Rabbinic bowl is from this day and forever."@en ;
    skos:note "Taken together these clauses do what a condition of failure would undo. A binding that holds until the dissolution of heaven and earth sets no term after which nothing more is to be expected; a seal that may not be broken denies in advance that a later operation could break it; and a charm from which none ever goes forth makes the demon's reappearance a thing the text has already excluded rather than a thing it could be measured against. The corpus states perpetuity where the holding looks for an interval."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurServiceBeforeDeparture a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Brief wird übergeben und genommen, bevor der Dämon ausfährt"@de,
        "The writ is served and taken before the demon goes out"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, texts 26: 5-6, 11: 8 and 8: 10, with the commentary to 8: 7, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 155, 159, 170 and 206)" ;
    skos:note "The order is in the wording and runs the same way in three bowls: take thy writ, then flee from their presence; take thy divorce from the woman, and do not appear to her; hear, obey and go forth. The editor reads the sentence commanding the demon to take the writ as the one that consummates the process, which is the step a writ needs in the court it is copied from."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:NippurDemonsDepartAndDoNotReturn ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:NippurGetAgainstDemons ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurSimulacrumBeforeTheAngelActs a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die geknetete Form geht dem Vereiteln der Zauber durch den Engel voraus"@de,
        "The kneaded shape comes before the angel frustrates the charms"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 12: 5-9, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 173-175)" ;
    skos:note "The order is the order of the lines: the adjuration of the descending angel, the rubric for the horn-shape and the honey, four lines on what that angel is, and then the clause saying that these will frustrate and ban the whole list of hostile powers. No consequence is stated for omitting the shape, and the text does not say who performs it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:NippurText12OffspringAndPreservation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:NippurAngelCompelledByHornAndHoney ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurSoundOfTheBurstingTransgressor a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der bronzene Laut dessen, der sich an der Presse vergriff"@de,
        "The brazen sound of the one who transgressed the press"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 6: 11-12, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 141-142)" ;
    skos:definition "The one perceptible sign this corpus names for its own operation taking effect, and it is a sign of the penalty and not of the cure. Whoever will transgress against this press and does not accept these rites shall split asunder violently and burst in the midst, and the sound of him shall resound with the resonance of brass in the spheres of heaven, and his abode shall be in the seventh hell of the sea."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted on the sign side and kept apart from the three conditions of failure, per SPEC section 15. It earns its place there and barely: it is a stated observable attached to the working of the charm, and it is announced in advance, which is exactly the property that makes a sign of success poor evidence. It is also placed where no client stands: the sound resounds in the spheres of heaven and the abode is in the sea."@en,
        "That the count stands at one sign against three conditions of failure is itself the finding about this corpus, and it runs the other way from most of the holding. Forty-three statements of effect are made in forty-one texts, and not one of them names a light, a warmth or an appearance that the client is told to look for as a sign that the bowl has worked. What they name is the absence of what was forbidden, which is why the weight of this corpus falls on the failure side."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurSprinklingBeforeTheBurning a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Bestreuen geht dem Entbrennen des Mannes voraus"@de,
        "The sprinkling comes before the man burns"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 28: 3-4, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 211-212)" ;
    skos:note "The text puts the order in a clause of its own: she shall sprinkle them upon this man until that he be inflamed and burn after the woman. It is the one place in the corpus where an operation on a substance stands between the words and the stated effect, and it is also the most broken passage in the collection."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:NippurText28ManInflamedWithLove ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:NippurHotHerbsOnTheImage ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText10HouseholdSealedWithTheSealsOfAdamAndNoahClaimed a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Text 10 erlangt es"@de,
        "Text 10 obtains it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 10: 1-6, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, p. 165)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:NippurBowlInversion ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:NippurText10HouseholdSealedWithTheSealsOfAdamAndNoah .

pmc:NippurText11WomanSavedFromFeverBarrennessAndAbortionClaimed a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Text 11 erlangt es"@de,
        "Text 11 obtains it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 11: 1-9, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, p. 170)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
