    rdfs:label "Text 6 erlangt es"@de,
        "Text 6 obtains it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 6: 1-11, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 141-142)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:NippurBowlInversion ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:NippurText6DemonsPressedDownFromACouple .

pmc:NippurText7LifeHouseAndBodyBoundAndSealedClaimed a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Text 7 erlangt es"@de,
        "Text 7 obtains it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 7: 1-9, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 147-148)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:NippurBowlInversion ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:NippurText7LifeHouseAndBodyBoundAndSealed .

pmc:NippurText8LilithsDivorcedFromAMarriedPairClaimed a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Text 8 erlangt es"@de,
        "Text 8 obtains it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 8: 1-17, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 155-156)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:NippurGetAgainstDemons ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:NippurText8LilithsDivorcedFromAMarriedPair .

pmc:NippurText9DivorcesWrittenForAllTheLilithsClaimed a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Text 9 erlangt es"@de,
        "Text 9 obtains it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 9: 1-11, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 161-162)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:NippurGetAgainstDemons ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:NippurText9DivorcesWrittenForAllTheLiliths .

pmc:NippurThreeHundredAndSixtyBroods a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die dreihundertsechzig Bruten der Lilith"@de,
        "The three hundred and sixty broods of the lilith"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, texts 38: 5-11 and 40: 17-22, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 243-253)" ;
    skos:definition "A number the two Mandaic bowls give to what a named lilith must be charmed with: the lilith and all her broods, even the three hundred and sixty broods; and in the second bowl the lilith and all the goddesses and the three hundred and sixty tribes. Both texts then say of the same number that the operator has dismissed them from the client one after the other."@en ;
    skos:note "The one count in this corpus that is not a count of the operator's acts but of what he is working against, and the only place where the texts say the dismissal is serial rather than simultaneous. Nothing read says how the serial dismissal is performed or how far it has got at any point."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurThreeNamedOffices a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Michael der Heiler, Rofiel der Linderer, Gabriel der Knecht des Herrn"@de,
        "Michael the healer, Rofiel the reliever, Gabriel the servant of the Lord"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 34: 7, with introduction § 13, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 96-97 and 231)" ;
    skos:note "The only place in the corpus where the three common angels are given separate functions rather than listed. The editor notes that Michael has no settled precedence in these texts, that as often Gabriel stands first, and that these bowls stand on the borderland of Jewish angelology rather than within its orthodox development."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:nameForm "Gabriel",
        "Michael",
        "Rofiel" ;
    pm:nameRole "three angels each given a distinct office in one clause of one Syriac bowl: Michael the healer, Rofiel the reliever and Gabriel the servant of the Lord" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurWritFromHeavenBeforeItBindsSpirits a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Scheidebrief kommt vom Himmel herab, bevor er einen Geist bindet"@de,
        "The writ comes down from heaven before it binds a spirit"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, texts 8: 7 and 8: 9, 17: 10, with the commentary to 8: 7, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 153, 159 and 190)" ;
    skos:note "The provenance is in the texts: a divorce-writ has come down to us from heaven, and in the next sentence of the same bowl, a divorce writ has come for you from across the sea. The reason for it is the editor's: against spiritual powers divine authority was necessary, and a writ from a foreign country had its own special forms in the law. Entered at prescribed order for that reason."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:NippurGetAgainstDemons ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:NippurWritOfHeavenlyProvenance ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurWrittenNamesTest a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Bedingung, die die Lilith für ihre eigene Fesselung angibt"@de,
        "The condition the Lilith states for her own restraint"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 42, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 259-260)" ;
    skos:definition "The one place in this corpus where the condition of efficacy is stated as a condition and by the being bound. The Lilith, having sworn, says: and every time that they repeat or I see my names written, it will not be in the power of me or of all my band to do evil or harm. The restraint is therefore not unconditional and not perpetual; it holds where the names are repeated or seen written, and the text says nothing of the case where they are not."@en ;
    skos:note "Read as a protocol this is the strongest thing the corpus offers, because it makes a stated act of the client the variable: whether the names were written, and whether they were there to be seen. Read as a defence it is also the most complete, because any harm that occurs can be assigned to a lapse in the writing. The holding enters it as the former and records the latter."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, protocol stated from the witness; no case series has been collected" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A birth at which the written names were present and read, and at which the mother or the child came to the harm the text enumerates. The text makes the writing sufficient, so one clean case with the writing in place falls due against it." ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-10-01"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:NippurText42ChildbedProtectedByTheWrittenNames .

pmc:NkasaVerdictRule a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Urteilsregel des Ordals"@de,
        "The verdict rule of the ordeal"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), p. 263",
        "W. Holman Bentley, Pioneering on the Congo (London: Religious Tract Society, 1900), vol. I, pp. 276-277" ;
    skos:definition "Vomiting acquits and retention condemns, with a confirming step: should the accused vomit three times he is given a fourth dose, and if he vomits that he is held to have proved beyond doubt that he is not a witch. He does not clear himself if he fails to vomit, or if there are signs of blood or of green matter in what he brings up, or if he has bad diarrhoea; in that case he is brought out of the hut and killed and the body left on the hill."@en ;
    skos:note "A fourth dose after three successful vomits is a repetition of the trial by the transmission itself, and the graph holds only one other case of a prescribed repetition, the second consultation of the Zande poison oracle. The difference is that the Zande control must reverse the first answer and this one must repeat it."@en,
        "The second witness records that the rule is not the same everywhere, and the variants are the finding. In some districts the vomit is examined and guilt inferred from something in it; in others the man who has vomited must run down into the valley and up the next hill, which he is usually in no condition to do, so that the vomiting has proved nothing. The same witness states that in some districts the ordeal-giver loses his fee if the accused vomits and may be punished, and that he is sometimes bribed to prepare a mild dose that will ensure one."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:KongoNkisi .

pmc:NkasaYieldsDetectionOfTheNdoki a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Ordal bringt das Aufdecken des ndoki hervor"@de,
        "The ordeal yields the detection of the witch"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John H. Weeks, Notes on Some Customs of the Lower Congo People, in Folk-Lore, vol. XX (London: The Folk-Lore Society, 1909), p. 51" ;
    skos:note "Entered because this is the form of claim the graph already holds from another African transmission, at pmc:BengeYieldsIdentificationOfTheWitch in traditions/azande.ttl, and because the two differ in a way that matters: there the poison is given to fowls and the question is put twice in opposite form, here the accused drinks it himself and no reversal is prescribed. Whether the two may be counted as separate witnesses is not decided here and is held as an issue; the contact route of this transmission is entered as documented on other grounds, and that value governs."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:BauKiaNkasa ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by Bakongo of the Lower Congo, unnamed in this passage; recorded by the Baptist missionary John H. Weeks in the districts of San Salvador and Ngombe Lutete and published in 1909; given in Kikongo, printed in English with the Kikongo terms." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:KongoNkisi ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:DetectionOfTheNdoki .

pmc:NkisiAKiniambe a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Nkisi a kiniambe, der Kult, der den Tod aufhob"@de,
        "Nkisi a kiniambe, the cult that promised no death"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), p. 234",
        "John H. Weeks, Notes on Some Customs of the Lower Congo People, in Folk-Lore, vol. XX (London: The Folk-Lore Society, 1909), p. 57" ;
    skos:definition "A practitioner who appeared with a new charged thing at the towns round San Salvador and held a rite in the form of a communion, with slices of cassava and peanuts in palm wine. The recipient paid first, one string of beads for a child and five for an adult, then confessed every evil desire he had had for the sickness or death of another, then received the cassava, the peanut and a drop of the wine, and with them the promise that he should never die."@en ;
    skos:note "The name is analysed by the recorder himself: the word for God on the Lower Congo is Nzambi and the form kiniambe is an upper river name for God, so a new charged thing was made under a borrowed divine name."@en,
        "The same recorder gives two different outcomes in two works, and the discrepancy is the finding. In nineteen hundred and nine he writes that when the people died the practitioner said it was because they had not made a full confession, and that he and his accomplices made a large sum out of the fear of death. In nineteen fourteen he writes that when the clients died like others the bubble burst and that cult and its charged thing fell into disfavour and neglect. Immunisation and collapse are the two possible responses to a failed promise, and here one witness reports both about one cult; the file records the discrepancy rather than choosing between them."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:KongoNkisi .

pmc:NkisiYieldsItsOffice a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das geladene Ding verrichtet sein Amt"@de,
        "The charged thing performs the office it was made for"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), p. 235" ;
    skos:note "Not circular in the sense SPEC §11 forbids: the practice named is the composing and charging of an object by a named practitioner, and the capacity is an effect in the world outside it, on a thief, a gun, a disease or a birth. The transmission also states what is to be concluded when the effect fails to appear, and that stands on the neighbouring node."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Kimenga ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by Bakongo of the Lower Congo at San Salvador and in the Ngombe Lutete district, mostly unnamed in this passage; recorded by the Baptist missionary John H. Weeks over some thirty years of residence, the book closed in 1913; given in Kikongo, of which the work prints the terms in the text while the accounts themselves are printed in English only." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:KongoNkisi ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:Nkisi .

pmc:Nlongo a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Nlongo, das auferlegte persönliche Verbot"@de,
        "Nlongo, the imposed personal prohibition"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), pp. 246-247" ;
    skos:definition "A prohibition laid on one person by the practitioner who treats him, sometimes for life and sometimes for a stated term. The corpus states that the thing forbidden bears no relation to the complaint and is chosen at the practitioner's discretion, and it gives instances: a woman with fits forbidden to look in a mirror or at her reflection in a stream, men forbidden cassava in any form for the rest of their lives. Eating the forbidden thing is said to break the protective spell and to bring the complaint back. Where the prohibition is for a term, say six months, the practitioner removes it at the end and takes his fee."@en ;
    skos:note "Held apart from pmc:Mpangu because the two differ in exactly the way that matters here: the inherited prohibition names its penalty in advance and in kind, and the imposed one names no relation at all between the forbidden thing and the disease. The second cannot fail, since any return of the complaint is evidence that something was eaten."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:KongoNkisi .

pmc:NoQuantitativeVerification a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Keine Zählung gegen den Zufall in den alten Quellen"@de,
        "No count against chance in the old sources"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ignatius of Loyola, Spiritual Exercises 313-336",
        "John Cassian, Collationes I.20-22" ;
    skos:definition "A negative finding, held as a node so that it is not lost: the old traditions test a claimed movement by its origin and its fruits, and none of those examined here tests a claimed capacity by counting hits against the rate obtainable by guessing."@en ;
    skos:note "Any protocol that counts outcomes against a base rate is a modern addition and is to be recorded as one, not as the recovery of an ancient method."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:TestingOfThoughts .

pmc:NoTwoKindsOfDoing a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Es gibt nicht zwei Arten des Tuns"@de,
        "There are not two kinds of doing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching, chapter eleven, at the answer on black magic and the two kinds of doing (one-volume English text as scanned in the Internet Archive full-text mirror; edition left unstated)" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that there is no red, green or yellow magic: there is mechanics, which is what happens, and there is doing, which is magic, and doing can be of one kind only. What looks like a second kind is an imitation of the outward appearance of doing and yields no objective result."@en ;
    skos:note "It stands beside pmc:FourWaysAtConstantGoal, where the same source varies only the means and holds the goal fixed. Both point the same way: this transmission classifies by completeness and by genuineness of operation, and it declines to classify by direction of the will. See prima-materia#65."@en,
        "Recorded because it is this tradition's answer to the question the right-hand and left-hand path terms are built to ask, and the answer is that the question has no second term. The refusal is directed at a division of kinds of operation, not at a norm about means and not at an end state, so it is held as a claim in its own right rather than as a value on pm:MethodOrientation or pm:TelicOrientation."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:FourthWay .

pmc:Noa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Noa"@de,
        "Noa"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 26" ;
    skos:definition "The state of being free or common, that is, not under the restriction of tapu. The witness states that persons and things under tapu can be brought into this state by ceremonies, so that the two are not fixed conditions but ends of an operation."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Maori .

pmc:NoaidiJourneyAnchoringIsNotStated a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Reise gibt keine Sinnesbindung an"@de,
        "The journey states no sensory anchoring"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 55-56" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:anchoringNotStated ;
    pm:situatedNode pmp:NoaidiJourney ;
    pm:situationGround "The passage was read for this question. It describes the practitioner from outside — he casts himself down, grows black in the face, lies as if dead, sweats — and says nothing about what he perceives while he lies there. An outside description of immobility is not a statement about the sensory channel, and reading one into the other would put a coordinate in the record that the source does not carry."@en .

pmc:NoaidiJourneyIsDisplaced a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Reise ist ein verlagerter Ort"@de,
        "The journey is a displaced locus"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 55-56" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:displacedLocus ;
    pm:situatedNode pmp:NoaidiJourney ;
    pm:situationGround "The practice is named for it: the journey is made while the body lies still, and a token is brought back from where the body was not. The placement records the account the tradition gives and not a verdict on it — the same compilation carries three positions on what actually lies there, and its author rejects the departure of the soul on doctrinal grounds rather than from anything he was told. See pmc:WhatLiesStillWhileHeTravels."@en .

pmc:NorseAtjandaKautel a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Alles ist besser, was einer allein kann"@de,
        "All is better that one alone knows"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hávamál 163 (Guðni Jónsson; 164-165 in Bellows), in Eddukvæði. Sæmundar-Edda, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:definition "The caution the catalogue closes on: the eighteenth song is never taught to maid nor to another man's wife, except to the one who lies in the speaker's arms or to his sister, for all is better which one alone knows."@en ;
    skos:note "A caution about disclosure and not about performance, and the only one in the transmission that names the persons to whom disclosure is allowed. It is also the exact opposite of this project's own criterion, which is why it is worth having in the holding: here is a transmission stating in its own words that the gate is the point."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:NorseLjodatal ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmc:NorseAtjandaLjod a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das achtzehnte, das ich niemals lehre"@de,
        "The eighteenth, which I never teach"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hávamál 163 (Guðni Jónsson; 164-165 in Bellows), in Eddukvæði. Sæmundar-Edda, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:definition "The eighteenth song, of which the poem states no effect at all and states instead that the speaker teaches it neither to maid nor to any man's wife, save to her alone who holds him in her arms or who is his sister; with the ground that all is better which one alone knows, and the remark that this follows the end of the songs."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered although it names no effect, because leaving it out would make the catalogue seventeen and the poem counts eighteen. What it carries instead of an effect is a rule of transmission, and that rule is the one testable thing about the whole list: a catalogue whose last member is withheld cannot be complete in any hand but the speaker's."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmc:NorseFimbulljodNiu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die neun Zauberlieder und der Trunk aus Óðrerir"@de,
        "The nine mighty songs and the draught of Óðrerir"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hávamál 140-141 (Guðni Jónsson; 141-142 in Bellows), in Eddukvæði. Sæmundar-Edda, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:definition "Nine mighty songs taken from the famed son of Bǫlþorn, Bestla's father, and a draught of the precious mead poured out of Óðrerir, after which the speaker began to thrive and to be wise, and to grow and to fare well."@en ;
    skos:note "A second acquisition beside the runes and from a named giver, which the runes were not. Nine again, as the nights were nine, and the number is the only thing the two acquisitions have in common in the wording."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmc:NorseFleinnPresupposesSjonum a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Anhalten des Schaftes setzt voraus, dass er mit Augen gesehen wird"@de,
        "The staying of the shaft presupposes that it is seen with the eyes"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hávamál 150 (Guðni Jónsson; 151 in Bellows), in Eddukvæði. Sæmundar-Edda, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:note "One of only two stated necessities this transmission carries, and the only one in the catalogue. Seventeen of the eighteen songs name an effect without a condition; this one names a condition that can be absent at the moment of use and is determinable by the user."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The stanza sets the condition as the limit of the claim: it flies not so hard that I do not stay it, if I see it with my eyes. Outside sight the song claims nothing, and the clause stands in the same line as the effect rather than beside it."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:NorseFleinnStodvadr ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:NorseLjodatal ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmc:NorseHamskiptiAnchoring a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Der Gestaltwechsel wird an der Schlafschwelle verortet"@de,
        "The shape-changing is placed at the sleep threshold"@en ;
    skos:note "Placed on the sleep threshold rather than in withdrawn absorption because the source's own comparison is sleep and death, not an inward gathering of the senses. A source that had meant absorption had the word for it and did not use it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:sleepThreshold ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:NorseHamskiptiYieldsFjarlaegLond ;
    pm:situationGround "The body is said to lie sem sofinn eða dauðr, as if asleep or dead, for the duration of the operation. The source gives the condition by comparison with sleep and gives no other."@en .

pmc:NorseHamskiptiLocus a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Der Gestaltwechsel wird am versetzten Ort verortet"@de,
        "The shape-changing is placed at a displaced locus"@en ;
    skos:note "The placement is the compiler's and the source names no coordinate. It is unusually well carried here, because the text states the condition of the abandoned body rather than leaving it to be inferred, which is what most sources on this axis do not do."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:displacedLocus ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:NorseHamskiptiYieldsFjarlaegLond ;
    pm:situationGround "The source separates the two explicitly and in one sentence: the trunk lay as if asleep or dead, and he himself was then a bird or a beast, a fish or a serpent, and went to far lands. What acts is not where the body is."@en .

pmc:NorseHangaPresupposesFasta a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Aufnehmen setzt weder Brot noch Horn voraus"@de,
        "The taking up presupposes neither bread nor horn"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hávamál 139 (Guðni Jónsson; 140 in Bellows), in Eddukvæði. Sæmundar-Edda, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:note "Held apart from the nine nights because it is a different kind of condition and because it is the one the poem states negatively: they cheered me with no loaf nor with any horn. A withholding by others, not an act of the sufferer and not an injury."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:NorseRunarUppNema ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:NorseHangaAVindgaMeidi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmc:NorseHangaPresupposesNiuNaetr a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Aufnehmen setzt neun Nächte, den Speer und die Selbstgabe voraus"@de,
        "The taking up presupposes nine nights, the spear and the self-offering"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hávamál 138 (Guðni Jónsson; 139 in Bellows), in Eddukvæði. Sæmundar-Edda, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:note "Prescribed order and not stated necessity, because the poem narrates a single case and names no consequence of a shorter hanging or of a hanging without the wound. It says what was done and what followed, not what would have followed otherwise."@en,
        "The self-offering is doubled in the wording and the doubling is the point: given to Óðinn, oneself to oneself. Giver, receiver and offering are the same, so the exchange the next caution below makes the measure of all offering is here reduced to one party."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:NorseRunarUppNema ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:NorseHangaAVindgaMeidi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmc:NorseHangaYieldsRunar a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Hängen bringt die Runen hervor"@de,
        "The hanging yields the runes"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Henry Adams Bellows, The Poetic Edda (New York: American-Scandinavian Foundation, 1923), Hovamol 139-140",
        "Hávamál 138-139 (Guðni Jónsson; 139-140 in Bellows), in Eddukvæði. Sæmundar-Edda, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:note "Attested as a first-person report and not as a textual attestation of a doctrine, because the stanzas are in the first person throughout and the speaker is the god: I know that I hung, I peered down, I took up the runes, I fell back. The poem gives no third-person account of the event anywhere beside it."@en,
        "The stated conditions are all deprivations or injuries and none of them is an action: nine nights, a spear-wound, being given, no bread, no horn, and a tree whose root is unknown. What the practitioner does is peer and take; everything before that is what is done to him or withheld from him. That is unusual in this holding, where a prerequisite is normally something performed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:NorseHangaAVindgaMeidi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:NorseRunarUppNema .

