pmc:NorseOfblotit a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Besser ungebeten als zu viel geopfert"@de,
        "Better unasked than over-offered"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Henry Adams Bellows, The Poetic Edda (New York: American-Scandinavian Foundation, 1923), Hovamol 146",
        "Hávamál 145 (Guðni Jónsson; 146 in Bellows), in Eddukvæði. Sæmundar-Edda, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:definition "The caution the poem sets against the four operations: better it is unasked than that it be over-offered, ever does a gift look to its return; better unsent than that it be over-slain."@en ;
    skos:note "The consequence named is not a punishment but a reversal of sign: excess is worse than omission. Between the two halves of the stanza stands the ground, that a gift ever looks to its return, so the measure is the exchange and excess is what destroys it while omission does not."@en,
        "Two paired verbs and not four separate ones. Asking pairs with offering, sending with slaying, and the caution is built twice over the same shape. A transmission that states the failure of a rite as too much rather than too little is uncommon in this holding, where cautions overwhelmingly name a shortfall."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:NorseBidjaOkBlota ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmc:NorseSeidrYieldsAudsyni a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der Vollzug macht das Verborgene offenbar"@de,
        "The performance makes the hidden plain"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Eirik the Red's Saga, chapter 4, translated by J. Sephton (Liverpool: D. Marples, 1880)",
        "Eiríks saga rauða, chapter 4, in Íslendinga sögur, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:note "The narrative marks the before and after without comment. When the seeress arrives, the farmer asks her to run her eyes over household, herd and buildings, and she is fámálug um allt, sparing of speech about everything. Nothing is told until the song has been sung."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:NorseFremjaSeidinn ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:NorseAudsynir .

pmc:NorseSeidrYieldsOrlog a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Kunst bringt Wissen um Schicksale und Macht über Menschen hervor"@de,
        "The craft yields knowledge of fates and power over men"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Snorri Sturluson, Ynglinga saga, chapter 7, in Heimskringla, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:NorseSeidrIthrott ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:NorseSeidrOrlogOkUordna .

pmc:NorseSeidrYieldsSpa a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der Vollzug bringt die Vorhersage mit ihrer Frist hervor"@de,
        "The performance yields the prediction with its term"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Eiríks saga rauða, chapter 4, in Íslendinga sögur, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:note "Held apart from the general making-plain above, because a term is a different kind of claim from a state of knowledge. The saga reports the answer and, in this chapter, not the outcome; whether the spring came as told is not said here."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:NorseFremjaSeidinn ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:NorseHallaerisSpa .

pmc:NorseSpakonuMegin a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Hellere Strahlen, als ich die Kraft habe, sie deutlich zu sehen"@de,
        "Brighter rays than I have strength to see clearly"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Eirik the Red's Saga, chapter 4, translated by J. Sephton (Liverpool: D. Marples, 1880)",
        "Eiríks saga rauða, chapter 4, in Íslendinga sögur, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:definition "The limit the seeress states on her own sight, in the fate she gives Guðríðr: over your kindreds shine brighter rays than I have strength to see such a thing clearly."@en ;
    skos:note "A transmission stating where its own instrument ends, in the mouth of the practitioner and at the height of the performance. That is a different thing from a caution: nothing is warned against, and no condition is set. What is given is a ceiling."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmc:NothingTiedMayEnterTheTown a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Nichts Gebündeltes darf in den Ort"@de,
        "Nothing tied in a bundle may enter the town"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), pp. 220-221" ;
    skos:definition "One prohibition is attached to the town charm and the corpus says it must be scrupulously observed: nothing tied in a bundle may enter the town, or the charm becomes ineffective. Women returning with firewood untie their bundles before they reach the post, men carrying thatching grass unfasten them, carriers loosen their cords or make a wide detour, and people take off their girdles."@en ;
    skos:note "Recorded because it is the clearest case in the file of a condition that is public, physical, continuously in force, and observable by anyone. It is also the condition most easily satisfied after the fact: any misfortune in the town can be traced to a bundle somebody carried in."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:NkindaAEvata ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by Bakongo of the Lower Congo at San Salvador and in the Ngombe Lutete district, mostly unnamed in this passage; recorded by the Baptist missionary John H. Weeks over some thirty years of residence, the book closed in 1913; given in Kikongo, of which the work prints the terms in the text while the accounts themselves are printed in English only." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:KongoNkisi .

pmc:ObjectiveConsciousnessPresupposesSelfConsciousness a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Der objektive Zustand setzt den dritten voraus"@de,
        "The objective state presupposes the third"@en ;
    dcterms:source "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching, chapter eight, at the passage on the only right way to objective consciousness (one-volume English text as scanned in the Internet Archive full-text mirror; edition left unstated)" ;
    skos:note "Recorded at stated necessity and not at prescribed order, because the source names what befalls the one who arrives without the prior step rather than merely setting a sequence. The consequence is a loss of the result and not a punishment, which is the form a prerequisite claim takes when it is about capacity."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "An ordinary person brought into the objective state artificially and returned will remember nothing of it and will believe he lost consciousness for a time; only from within the third state can flashes of the fourth be retained."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:FourStatesOfConsciousness ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:SelfRemembering ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:FourthWay .

pmc:OfficePresupposesTheFast a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Amt setzt das Fasten voraus"@de,
        "The office presupposes the fast"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III" ;
    skos:note "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity, and the reason is a property of the passage. The source sets the fast, its lengths and its abstinences, and says nothing about what befalls a man who takes the office without it. A stated consequence would raise this; none stands in what was read. The variation between one month, six and a year is itself against reading a necessity into it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:OfficeEnteredThreeWays ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:FastBeforeTakingOffice ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmc:OloliuhquiYieldsAnswer a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Trinken bringt die Antwort hervor"@de,
        "The drinking yields the answer"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulo VI, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:OloliuhquiConsultation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NahuaRitual ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:OloliuhquiAnswer .

pmc:OphiteAdonaeus a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Adonaeus, in der Liste genannt und in den Reden abwesend"@de,
        "Adonaeus, named in the list and absent from the speeches"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.32 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)" ;
    skos:altLabel "Adonaios"@de ;
    skos:note "He is the only one of the seven for whom the witness carries no speech. What is known of him here is his name and its derivation, and nothing else; the role recorded above is what the passage gives and is deliberately empty of office."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:nameForm "Adonaeus" ;
    pm:nameRole "one of the seven names of the hebdomad, taken from the Hebrew scriptures, where the text is Adonai; no office and no gate are given him" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:OphiteGnosis .

pmc:OphiteAloaeus a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Aloaeus, Herrscher des zweiten Tores"@de,
        "Aloaeus, governor of the second gate"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.31 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)" ;
    skos:altLabel "Ailoaios"@de,
        "Eloaeus"@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:nameForm "Aloaeus" ;
    pm:nameRole "governor of the second gate, to whom the speaker brings the symbol of his mother, a grace hidden by the powers of the realms" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:OphiteGnosis .

pmc:OphiteAstaphaeus a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Astaphaeus, Herrscher des dritten Tores"@de,
        "Astaphaeus, ruler of the third gate"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.31 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)" ;
    skos:altLabel "Astaphanos"@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:nameForm "Astaphaeus" ;
    pm:nameRole "ruler of the third gate, overseer of the first principle of water, who sees the essence of the world and before whom the speaker is purified with the spirit of a virgin" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:OphiteGnosis .

pmc:OphiteDescensusPerSeptem a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Abstieg durch die sieben Himmel in der Gestalt ihrer Söhne"@de,
        "The descent through the seven heavens in the likeness of their sons"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Irenaeus, Adversus Haereses I.30.12 (trans. A. Roberts and W. Rambaut, Ante-Nicene Fathers I, Buffalo 1885)" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that Christ descended through the seven heavens, having assumed the likeness of their sons, and emptied them of their power by degrees, so that the whole besprinkling of light rushed to him."@en ;
    skos:note "The counter-movement to the ascent of the speeches, and the only passage in either witness that says what passing through a sphere does to it. It works by likeness: the descending one takes the form of what holds each heaven, and that is the same logic the speeches use when the speaker names what he already bears. Whether the two are one doctrine seen from both ends is not asserted here."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:OphiteGnosis .

pmc:OphiteDiagrammaDescriptum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Kreise des Diagramms und ihre Inschriften"@de,
        "The circles of the diagram and their inscriptions"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.25, VI.33 and VI.38 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)" ;
    skos:definition "What Origen reports of the drawing itself: ten circles, distinct from one another and bound by one circle said to be the soul of all things and called Leviathan, whose name stands twice, at the circumference and at the centre; Behemoth placed beneath the lowest circle; a thick black line called Gehenna or Tartarus; a greater and a lesser circle inscribed Father and Son along their diameter; a barrier in the shape of a hatchet; short circles inscribed Love and Life; a rhomboid figure called the foresight of wisdom, with the nature of wisdom at the point of common section, and Knowledge and Understanding above and below it; and, in what Celsus called the square pattern, a flaming sword drawn as the diameter of a flaming circle, guarding the tree of knowledge and of life."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a description and not as a reconstruction. Origen gives the parts and almost never their positions relative to one another, and every published drawing of the diagram supplies an arrangement that this passage does not contain."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:OphiteGnosis .

pmc:OphiteDuaeHebdomades a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zwei Siebenheiten in einem Diagramm, nicht eine"@de,
        "Two heptads in one diagram, not one"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.30-31 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)" ;
    skos:definition "The finding that the diagram holds two distinct groups of seven, given in two separate chapters and never equated: the ruling demons in animal shape with angelic and barbarous names, and the named rulers of the gates to whom the speeches are addressed. No name occurs in both lists."@en ;
    skos:note "The single sentence that connects them is the remark that the star Phaenon is in sympathy with the lion-like ruler, placed directly after the speech to Ialdabaoth. It joins one member of each list and leaves the other six pairs unstated. A table that runs the two heptads together into one column of seven is doing what the source does not do."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:OphiteGnosis .

pmc:OphiteErataoth a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Erataoth, der Hundegestaltige"@de,
        "Erataoth the dog-like"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.30 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:nameForm "Erataoth" ;
    pm:nameRole "sixth of the seven ruling demons of the diagram, with the face of a dog" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:OphiteGnosis .

pmc:OphiteGabriel a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Gabriel, der Adlergestaltige"@de,
        "Gabriel the eagle-like"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.30 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:nameForm "Gabriel" ;
    pm:nameRole "fourth of the seven ruling demons of the diagram, eagle-like" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:OphiteGnosis .

pmc:OphiteGenerationesIrenaei a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Sieben als Zeugungskette"@de,
        "The seven as a chain of generations"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Irenaeus, Adversus Haereses I.30.5 (trans. A. Roberts and W. Rambaut, Ante-Nicene Fathers I, Buffalo 1885)" ;
    skos:definition "The account in which the same seven names appear not as gatekeepers but as a descent: the first offspring of the mother is Ialdabaoth, from him Iao, from him Sabaoth, the fourth Adoneus, the fifth Eloeus, the sixth Oreus, and the seventh and last Astanphaeus. They are said to sit in heaven in the order of their generation and to rule invisibly over things celestial and terrestrial; the mother holds the eighth place."@en ;
    skos:note "The witness carries no speeches and no gates. What the first witness gives as a liturgy of passage, this one gives as a genealogy, and the only thing common to both is the set of names. That is the finding: the names are attested twice, the practice once."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:OphiteGnosis .

pmc:OphiteHebdomasInferior a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die untere Hebdomas der herabgestürzten Schlange"@de,
        "The lower hebdomad of the cast-down serpent"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Irenaeus, Adversus Haereses I.30.8 (trans. A. Roberts and W. Rambaut, Ante-Nicene Fathers I, Buffalo 1885)" ;
    skos:definition "The second group of seven in this witness: the serpent, cast down into the lower world, subdues the angels there and begets six sons, himself forming the seventh, after the example of the hebdomad that surrounds the father. These are called the seven mundane demons, said always to oppose and resist the human race."@en ;
    skos:note "The doubling is therefore attested twice and independently of the diagram. Where the first witness has two lists side by side in one drawing and never equates them, this one states outright that the lower seven are formed after the pattern of the upper seven and are not the same beings. See pmc:OphiteDuaeHebdomades."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:OphiteGnosis .

pmc:OphiteHebdomasPlanetae a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die heilige Hebdomas sind die sieben Sterne, die sie Planeten nennen"@de,
        "The holy hebdomad is the seven stars they call planets"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Irenaeus, Adversus Haereses I.30.9 (trans. A. Roberts and W. Rambaut, Ante-Nicene Fathers I, Buffalo 1885)" ;
    skos:definition "The identification the second witness makes: that the holy hebdomad is the seven stars which they call planets. It is made of the group as a whole and is never broken down; no name in the list is paired with a star."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the exact state of the evidence that the received tables overwrite in both directions. Whoever says the ophites had no planetary doctrine has not read this sentence; whoever prints a column pairing Ialdabaoth with Saturn and Horaios with the Moon has supplied what the sentence withholds. A collective identification without a distribution is a third thing, and it is the thing the witnesses give."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:OphiteGnosis .

pmc:OphiteHebdomasTheriomorpha a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die sieben Herrscher in Tiergestalt"@de,
        "The seven ruling demons in animal shape"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.30 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)" ;
    skos:definition "A second set of seven in the same diagram, each with a name and an animal form: Michael the lion-like, Suriel the bull-like, Raphael the serpent-like, Gabriel the eagle-like, Thauthabaoth the bear-like, Erataoth the dog-like, and Onoel or Thartharaoth, somewhat asinine in appearance."@en ;
    skos:note "Origen states the division of labour between his source and Celsus outright: Celsus gave the shapes and not the names, and the names come from the diagram Origen had. The first four are the four living creatures of the merkabah under angel names, the last three are formed like the names of the other heptad."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:OphiteGnosis .

pmc:OphiteHoraeus a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Horaeus, der über den Feuerwall sprang"@de,
        "Horaeus, who overleapt the rampart of fire"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.31 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)" ;
    skos:altLabel "Horaios"@de ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:nameForm "Horaeus" ;
    pm:nameRole "ruler of the first gate, who fearlessly overleapt the rampart of fire, and who beholds the symbol sculptured on the figure of the tree of life" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:OphiteGnosis .

pmc:OphiteIaldabaoth a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Ialdabaoth, erster und siebter"@de,
        "Ialdabaoth, first and seventh"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.31 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)" ;
    skos:altLabel "Ialdabaoth"@de,
        "Jaldabaoth"@en ;
    skos:note "The one place in the passage where a ruler is tied to a star: immediately after this speech Origen reports that the star Phaenon is said to be in sympathy with the lion-like ruler. Phaenon is Saturn, and the lion-like one belongs to the other heptad, so the sentence is at once the sole planetary datum in the text and the sole bridge between its two sets of seven."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:nameForm "Ialdabaoth" ;
    pm:nameRole "ruling spirit of the seventh gate, addressed as first and seventh, rational ruler of a pure mind and a perfect work to son and father, who closed a gate against his own kingdom" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:OphiteGnosis .

pmc:OphiteIao a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Iao, der bei Nacht scheint"@de,
        "Iao, who shines by night"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.31-32 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)" ;
    skos:note "No gate number is given for him, only the ordinal second. Origen says elsewhere that the name comes from the Hebrew scriptures, where it is Iao or Jah."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:nameForm "Iao" ;
    pm:nameRole "second, who shines by night, ruler of the secret mysteries of son and father, first prince of death and portion of the innocent" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:OphiteGnosis .

pmc:OphiteMichaelDisputatus a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob Michael ein Herrscher des Diagramms ist oder ein Name der gefallenen Schlange"@de,
        "Whether Michael is a ruler of the diagram or a name of the fallen serpent"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Irenaeus, Adversus Haereses I.30.9 (trans. A. Roberts and W. Rambaut, Ante-Nicene Fathers I, Buffalo 1885)",
        "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.30 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)" ;
    skos:note "Origen's diagram gives Michael as the first of the seven ruling demons, lion-like, and adds that scripture honours him as the angel of the Creator. Irenaeus says the serpent that was cast down bears two names, Michael and Samael. The same name therefore stands once at the head of the ruling seven and once on the adversary they were formed against."@en,
        "The disagreement is worth keeping because it falls on the name and not on a concept. A name is a harder identity than a resemblance, and two transmissions putting one name in two incompatible places is a finding about the transmissions rather than about the bearer."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:OphiteMichael ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:OphiteGnosis .

pmc:OphiteNullaOrdinatioPlanetaria a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Zeuge ordnet den Toren keine Planeten zu"@de,
        "The witness assigns no planets to the gates"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.22 and VI.30-32 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)" ;
    skos:definition "The finding that the passage nowhere assigns a planet to a gate or to a named ruler. The rulers are counted by gates and realms; the only celestial body named in the whole of the diagram material is Phaenon, and it is put in sympathy with the lion-like ruler of the other heptad."@en ;
    skos:note "The planetary ladder does stand in the same book, and it is expressly other material. At VI.22 Celsus reports it from the mysteries of Mithras, with seven gates of metals assigned to Saturn, Venus, Jupiter, Mercury, Mars, the Moon and the Sun, and Origen treats the quotation as an intrusion from a foreign cult. Celsus himself adds that the order is explained by musical reasons rather than by the planets. Reading the ophite gates through that ladder joins two things the text keeps apart, and it changes the order into the bargain: the Mithraic sequence is not the sequence of the gates."@en,
        "This is a statement about one witness and not about the transmission. The second witness does make the identification, and it makes it in a form nobody reports: Irenaeus has the holy hebdomad be the seven stars they call planets, collectively, and never says which name belongs to which star. See pmc:OphiteHebdomasPlanetae. What is unattested in both witnesses is therefore not the planetary reading as such but the table that pairs each name with one planet. See prima-materia#595."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:OphiteGnosis .

pmc:OphiteOnoel a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Onoel, der Eselsgestaltige"@de,
        "Onoel the ass-like"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.30 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)" ;
    skos:altLabel "Thaphabaoth"@en,
        "Thartharaoth"@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:nameForm "Onoel" ;
    pm:nameRole "seventh of the seven ruling demons of the diagram, asinine in appearance; Celsus gives the name as Thaphabaoth or Onoel, the diagram Origen read as Onoel or Thartharaoth" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:OphiteGnosis .

pmc:OphiteOratioYieldsTransitus a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Rede am Tor erwirkt den Durchgang"@de,
        "The speech at the gate obtains the passage"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.31 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)" ;
    skos:note "No condition of failure is stated anywhere in the passage. The witness gives what is to be said and never what happens to one who says it without having what the words claim, nor any sign by which the passer would know he had been refused. The claim is therefore not examinable from this source, and that is a property of the source and not of the claim."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:OphiteTransitusOratio ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:OphiteGnosis ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:OphiteTransitusLiber .

pmc:OphiteOrdoDisputatus a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Wer von den Sieben zuletzt steht"@de,
        "Which of the seven stands last"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Irenaeus, Adversus Haereses I.30.5 (trans. A. Roberts and W. Rambaut, Ante-Nicene Fathers I, Buffalo 1885)",
        "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.31 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)" ;
    skos:note "Irenaeus makes Ialdabaoth the first and Astanphaeus the seventh and last, in a chain where each is begotten by the one before. Origen has the soul address Ialdabaoth as first and seventh, and puts Astaphaeus at the third gate, with Horaeus at the first. The two orders cannot both be read off one diagram: one counts downwards from the mother, the other counts gates upwards from the earth, and Ialdabaoth stands at the far end in each."@en,
        "Whether that is a real disagreement between two branches or the same scheme read from two directions is not decidable from the translations used. It is entered as a dispute rather than harmonised, because a resolution here would settle by preference what the witnesses settle differently."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:OphiteHebdomasNominata ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:OphiteGnosis .

pmc:OphiteProphetaeDistributi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Verteilung der Propheten auf die Sieben"@de,
        "The prophets distributed among the seven"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Irenaeus, Adversus Haereses I.30.11 (trans. A. Roberts and W. Rambaut, Ante-Nicene Fathers I, Buffalo 1885)" ;
    skos:definition "The assignment of the Hebrew prophets to the seven: Moses, Joshua, Amos and Habakkuk to Ialdabaoth; Samuel, Nathan, Jonah and Micah to Iao; Elijah, Joel and Zechariah to Sabaoth; Isaiah, Ezekiel, Jeremiah and Daniel to Adonai; Tobias and Haggai to Eloi; Michaiah and Nahum to Oreus; Esdras and Zephaniah to Astanphaeus. Each is said to glorify his own father as god."@en ;
    skos:note "The only place in either witness where the seven are individuated by content rather than by rank. It is also the only distribution the sources actually make across the seven, and it is a distribution of prophets and not of planets — which is worth saying beside pmc:OphiteHebdomasPlanetae, where a distribution is exactly what is missing."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:OphiteGnosis .

pmc:OphiteRaphael a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Raphael, der Schlangengestaltige"@de,
        "Raphael the serpent-like"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.30 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:nameForm "Raphael" ;
    pm:nameRole "third of the seven ruling demons of the diagram, serpent-like; in Celsus amphibious and frightfully hissing" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:OphiteGnosis .

pmc:OphiteSabaoth a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Sabaoth, Herrscher des fünften Reiches"@de,
        "Sabaoth, governor of the fifth realm"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.31 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:nameForm "Sabaoth" ;
    pm:nameRole "powerful governor of the fifth realm, defender of the law of his own creatures, who are liberated by his grace through a more powerful pentad" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:OphiteGnosis .

pmc:OphiteSeptemOrationes a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sieben Reden, sechs davon an einen benannten Herrscher"@de,
        "Seven speeches, six of them to a named ruler"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.31-32 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)" ;
    skos:definition "The count as the witness gives it. Seven speeches stand in the passage: one to a one-formed king, from whom the beginnings of the ogdoad are said to derive, and then in order to Ialdabaoth, Iao, Sabaoth, Astaphaeus, Aloaeus and Horaeus. Six of the seven names of the hebdomad therefore have a speech, and Adonaeus has none."@en ;
    skos:note "The count is worth entering because it is regularly given otherwise. The secondary literature speaks of eight speeches to seven named archons; the translation read carries seven speeches and six names. Whether the difference lies in the Greek, in the numbering of the chapters, or in the counting is unchecked here, because neither Koetschau nor Chadwick nor the study making that claim was opened. See prima-materia#596."@en,
        "The gap falls where the numbering also breaks. Gates one, two and three are named, the fifth realm is named, Ialdabaoth is first and seventh, and the fourth and sixth are never mentioned. Whether the missing speech is a loss in transmission, a diagram Origen copied incompletely, or a system that never had one is not decidable from this witness."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:OphiteGnosis .

pmc:OphiteSigillum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Siegel und die zwei Parteien am Sterbebett"@de,
        "The seal, and the two parties at the deathbed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.27 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)" ;
    skos:definition "The exchange reported around what ecclesiastical writers call the seal: he who impresses it is called father and he who is sealed young man and son, and the sealed one answers that he has been anointed with white ointment from the tree of life. With it a number is given: seven angels attach themselves to either side of the soul of the dying body, the one party called angels of light, the other archontics, whose ruler is termed the accursed god."@en ;
    skos:note "Origen says of the dialogue that he never heard of such a thing even among the heretics, and he attacks the name given to the ruler at length. Both the rite and his disbelief in it are recorded; the passage is the closest the witness comes to something done in life rather than after death."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:OphiteGnosis .

pmc:OphiteSuriel a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Suriel, der Stiergestaltige"@de,
        "Suriel the bull-like"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.30 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:nameForm "Suriel" ;
    pm:nameRole "second of the seven ruling demons of the diagram, bull-like" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:OphiteGnosis .

pmc:OphiteThauthabaoth a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Thauthabaoth, der Bärengestaltige"@de,
        "Thauthabaoth the bear-like"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.30 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:nameForm "Thauthabaoth" ;
    pm:nameRole "fifth of the seven ruling demons of the diagram, bear-like" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:OphiteGnosis .

pmc:PancaAntaradhanani a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Erlangungen gehen zuerst, und die Unterscheidungswissen währen tausend Jahre"@de,
        "The attainments go first, and the analytical knowledges last a thousand years"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Anāgatavaṃsa, recension B of Minayeff's edition, Journal of the Pali Text Society 1886, pp. 33-34, in the English of Henry Clarke Warren, Buddhism in Translations, Harvard University Press 1896, §102, pp. 482-483" ;
    skos:definition "Asked about the Buddha to come, the work first describes the decline of the religion in five disappearances: of the attainments, of the method, of learning, of the symbols, of the relics. Of the first it says that only for a thousand years from the passing of the Blessed One will the monks be able to acquire the analytical sciences; then, as time goes on, the disciples will attain only to never returning, to once returning, to conversion; and with the death of the last disciple that attained to conversion the attainments will have disappeared. Of the second it says that the disciples, being unable to realise the trances, the insights, the paths and the fruits, will keep only the four purities of conduct, and then only the rule against the four deadly sins."@en ;
    skos:note "Read on 2026-09-24 for one question: whether the transmission anywhere states that what the handbook teaches is no longer to be had. It does, in the only form in which such a statement can be made, by putting a date on it. The trances that the handbook makes the base of every operation of its twelfth chapter are named in the second disappearance among the things the disciples will be unable to realise."@en,
        "The recension is a manuscript copy and the count of years is the thing most likely to differ between copies. It is entered with the recension named for that reason, and a later reader who wants to press the date should press it there and not here."@en,
        "What the passage does not say, and the node does not supply it. It does not name the supernormal powers. It names the analytical sciences under the first disappearance and the trances under the second, and the powers of the twelfth chapter stand on the fourth trance without being listed anywhere here. Whether the passage reaches them is an inference and is not made in this node; it is stated as an inference at pmc:IddhiAdhigamaVivada."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Anagatavamsa ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:ParacittaJnanaIsWithdrawnAbsorption a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Kenntnis fremden Bewusstseins wird in abgezogener Vertiefung geübt"@de,
        "Knowledge of another mind is exercised in withdrawn absorption"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.1-4",
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.19",
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.20" ;
    skos:note "The sūtra immediately following adds a limit that belongs to the claim: what supports the notion is not thereby known, being out of the practitioner's reach. The capacity is bounded in the base text and not only by its readers."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:withdrawnAbsorption ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:ParacittaJnana ;
    pm:situationGround "The sūtra reads pratyayasya paracittajñānam: knowledge of another mind from the presented notion, with saṃyama carried over from the sūtras before it. The state is the operator's, fixed at III.1-4, and the placement is inherited from the binding the source itself makes."@en .

pmc:ParakayaPravesaIsDisplaced a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Der Eintritt in einen anderen Körper ist ein verlagerter Ort"@de,
        "Entering another body is a displaced locus"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.38 (Sanskrit sūtra text; received four-pāda numbering)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:displacedLocus ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:ParakayaPravesa ;
    pm:situationGround "The sūtra has the seat of awareness transferred into a body other than the practitioner's own. The awareness is thereby located away from the body it started in, which is the criterion this value names."@en .

pmc:ParakayaPravesaIsWithdrawnAbsorption a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Der Eintritt in einen anderen Körper geschieht in abgezogener Vertiefung"@de,
        "Entering another body is performed in withdrawn absorption"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.1-4",
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.38 (Sanskrit sūtra text; received four-pāda numbering)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:withdrawnAbsorption ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:ParakayaPravesa ;
    pm:situationGround "The sūtra makes the entry follow from saṃyama, and the state saṃyama names is fixed by III.1-4. The placement is inherited from the operator the source itself binds the capacity to, and not read off the capacity."@en .

pmc:ParalysisUniversalityProtocol a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Lähmung allgemein ist und ihr Bemerken selten"@de,
        "Whether the paralysis is universal and its noticing rare"@en ;
    skos:definition "An examination of the posited claim in two parts: whether the motor paralysis of the sleep threshold is a feature of every sleeper, and whether the rate at which it is noticed is low enough that a universal mechanism could go unreported."@en ;
    skos:note "A word on why the claim is posited and not sourced. It comes from an unpublished method draft, which SPEC §10 excludes as a witness, and the exclusion is not a formality: a draft that entered as a source could be confirmed by the very research it was written from. Holding it as a posit keeps the direction right, and the direction is that the draft is examined and never grounded."@en,
        "Status of what follows. The state above is procedure without cases, and everything below is written in the voice of a finding. It is not one. The notes record what the evidence appeared to show before any search for opposing work was made, and they are kept in that voice because rewriting them would destroy the reasoning that has to be checked. Read them as the expected result of an examination nobody has finished, not as its outcome. See prima-materia#386."@en,
        "The claim has two halves and they are carried by two different bodies of evidence, which is the reason this protocol is worth entering at all. The universality is a matter of mechanism: the atonia of the relevant sleep stage is produced by an identified transmitter and receptor pathway and is a regular feature of that stage rather than a fault in it. The rarity of noticing is a matter of prevalence: a systematic review over thirty-five studies and more than thirty-six thousand participants finds that some seven and a half per cent of the general population report at least one episode in a lifetime, with markedly higher figures among students and psychiatric samples."@en,
        "The two halves fit, and that is the finding. A mechanism present in every sleeper and an experience reported by roughly one in thirteen are not in contradiction: they are exactly what the claim asserts, namely that what is rare is not the paralysis but the waking awareness that coincides with it. The state is therefore cases without deviation."@en,
        "What this does not settle, and the limits are sharper than the fit. The prevalence figure counts self-reported episodes and not measured ones, so it is a floor for noticing and not a ceiling for occurrence. The mechanism evidence establishes the atonia of the sleep stage and not that every reported episode coincides with it; the two are joined here by the ordinary explanation and not by a study that measured both in the same subjects. And a claim confirmed in this shape says nothing about what any transmission builds on the state, which is the part this graph actually cares about."@en ;
    pm:baseRate "7.6 per cent of the general population report at least one lifetime episode, aggregated over 35 studies and 36,533 participants; 28.3 per cent among students and 31.9 per cent among psychiatric patients" ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchNotCarried ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Not carried. Neither the prevalence review nor the mechanism study was checked against later work, and a systematic review of 2011 is old enough that the question is not idle."@en ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "B. A. Sharpless and J. P. Barber, Lifetime prevalence rates of sleep paralysis: a systematic review, Sleep Medicine Reviews 15 (2011), no. 5, pp. 311-315, doi:10.1016/j.smrv.2011.01.007",
        "P. L. Brooks and J. H. Peever, Identification of the transmitter and receptor mechanisms responsible for REM sleep paralysis, Journal of Neuroscience 32 (2012), pp. 9785-9795, doi:10.1523/JNEUROSCI.0482-12.2012" ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "Not devised here. The protocol assembles two existing bodies of evidence, one on the mechanism and one on the prevalence, and states the joint they leave open" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A population of ordinary sleepers is found in whom the atonia of the sleep stage is absent; or reported episodes are measured and found not to coincide with it."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:ParalysisPrecedesItsNotice .

pmc:ParityDivination a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wahrsagung durch gerade und ungerade"@de,
        "Divination by odd and even"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Cristóbal de Molina, Relación de las fábulas y ritos de los incas, ed. Paloma Jiménez del Campo, transcr. Paloma Cuenca Muñoz (Madrid: Iberoamericana / Frankfurt: Vervuert, 2010), fol. 6v, on the achicoc",
        "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III, on the diviner by maize" ;
    skos:definition "The diviner by maize makes small heaps of grains without counting them, then takes one away from one side and one from another, and according as they are left even or odd the lot is good or bad. The earlier report gives the same mechanism under another name and with another material: they cast lots with maize and llama dung, and if they came out even or odd they gave their answers accordingly."@en ;
    skos:note "In one village a practitioner of this office produced instead a bag of small stones which he said were called by a particular name and that he had inherited them from his grandfather for this purpose. The variant is recorded because it shows the office surviving a change of instrument."@en,
        "Two recorders, forty-six years and several hundred leagues apart, name different practitioners and the same mechanism. The count is deliberately not made before the drawing in the later account, which means the outcome is not determined by anything the diviner knows; that is the same feature the poison-oracle material in this graph rests on, and it is stated here of a maize heap."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmc:PatanjalaCapacityDurationNotStated a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Für die Vermögen wird keine Dauer angegeben"@de,
        "The capacities are given no duration"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.19",
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.26",
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.37",
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.38 (Sanskrit sūtra text; received four-pāda numbering)",
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.41" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:durationNotStated ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:ParakayaPravesa ;
    pm:situationGround "The sūtras that carry the capacities of this chapter were read for the question and are silent on it: each names an object of saṃyama and the knowledge or entry that follows, and none says whether what follows passes or remains. The sūtra that weighs the capacities weighs them for what they are worth and not for how long they last."@en .

pmc:PathaviyaUmmujjanimujja a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Er taucht in die Erde und aus ihr auf wie in Wasser"@de,
        "He dives in and out of the earth as if it were water"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.92-94 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, pp. 458-459; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "He enters the water device, emerges, marks off as much ground as he wants to become water, does the preliminary work and resolves; and the marked ground becomes water. He does not only dive: he bathes in it, drinks from it, washes his face and his clothes in it. And not water only — butter, oil, honey, molasses, whatever he chooses; and when he draws these off into jars the butter remains butter and the water water. If he wishes to be wet he is wet, and if not, not."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:PatibhagaNimitta a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Gegenzeichen, hundertmal reiner und ohne Farbe oder Gestalt"@de,
        "The counterpart sign, a hundred times purer and without colour or shape"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.31, Pali PTS [125]-[126] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, p. 146; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga VIII.214-215, Pali PTS [285] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, pp. 327-328; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "The second sign, arising as the hindrances are suppressed and the mind reaches access concentration. It differs from the learning sign in that any fault of the device is apparent in the learning sign and not in this one, which appears as if breaking out of it and a hundred times, a thousand times more purified, like a looking-glass disk drawn from its case, a washed mother-of-pearl dish, the moon coming out from a cloud, cranes against a thundercloud. And it has neither colour nor shape, for if it had it would be cognizable by the eye, gross, and stamped with the three characteristics; it is born only of perception in one who has obtained concentration, being a mere mode of appearance."@en ;
    skos:note "Sign of success, counted apart. What it does for a project that collects failure conditions is worth stating: a mark that has neither colour nor shape and is born only of perception cannot be described wrongly by anyone who reports it, and the handbook supplies thirteen descriptions of it in another chapter."@en,
        "The negative determination is the load-bearing half and the handbook does not keep to it. Chapter III counts mindfulness of breathing among the subjects that have a counterpart sign, and Chapter VIII then describes that sign as appearing to some like a star, a cluster of gems or pearls, to others rough like a cotton seed or a peg of heartwood, to others like a long cord, a wreath of flowers, a puff of smoke, a spider's web, a film of cloud, a lotus, a chariot wheel, the moon's disk or the sun's disk. The handbook marks the conflict — so some say, this however is the decision of the commentaries — and resolves it by the variety of perception, which accounts for the differences between practitioners and not for a sign having a shape at all when it was said to have none."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:PdmAcaciaFructus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Mittel, eine Frau einen Mann lieben zu machen, Akazienfrucht"@de,
        "A medicament for making a woman love a man, fruit of acacia"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso III/14-16, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A medicament for making a woman love a man: fruit of acacia ground with honey, with which the phallus is anointed, and the operator lies with the woman."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmAcaciaPodum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Eine Frau ihren Mann lieben machen, Akazienschoten"@de,
        "To cause a woman to love her husband, pods of acacia"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso XIII/10-11, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "To cause a woman to love her husband: pods of acacia pounded with honey, with which the phallus is anointed, and he lies with the woman."@en ;
    skos:note "The only erotic prescription of the work whose stated beneficiary is the existing marriage, and it is three lines from the one that tears a woman name out of Egypt. The ingredient is the same acacia as at verso III/14-16, there for making a woman love a man."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmAmorPerPiscem a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Eine Frau einen Mann lieben machen durch den in Öl getränkten Fisch"@de,
        "Making a woman love a man by the fish drowned in oil"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XII/1-27, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "Opobalsamum, malabathrum, kusht, a scented ingredient and merue, each one stater, are pounded with two lok of genuine oil in a clean vessel one day before the lunar period. A black fish of nine fingers, another copy says seven, is laid in the oil for two days while the formula is recited to it at dawn before leaving the house and before speaking to any man on earth; then a vine-shoot taken before it has ripened grapes, taken with the left hand and passed to the right, is carried home, the fish is hung by its tail from the vine-wood over the oil-vessel standing on a new brick until it has dripped, for three days; the fish is then embalmed with myrrh, natron and fine linen and hidden, two further days pass, and the oil is recited to for seven days. The operator anoints his phallus and his face and lies with the woman."@en ;
    skos:note "The longest chain of conditions in the non-divinatory half of the work: a date in the lunar month, a count of days at three separate points, a hand of the operator, a brick that must be new, and two clauses of silence. Not one of them carries a stated consequence of being skipped, which is why the node carries no pm:Presupposing beside it."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmAmorPerPiscemAlter a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die zweite Methode mit dem schwarzen Fisch von neun Fingern"@de,
        "The second method with the black fish of nine digits"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XII/27-31, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A second prescription for the same end, given immediately after the first. A black fish of nine digits is drowned in an ointment of roses, hung up by its head, then placed on a glass vessel with a little water of sisymbrium and a little amulet-of-Isis, pounded; the invocation is recited to it seven times for seven days opposite the rising sun. The operator anoints his head with it in the hour when he lies with any woman, and embalms and buries the fish with myrrh and natron in his chamber or a hidden place."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as its own node because it is a second prescription and not a variant invocation: the ingredient, the vessel, the count of days and the part anointed all differ. The outcome it claims is the one the preceding recipe claims, which is the case SPEC §15 has in view when it says a recipe is a capacity: two recipes, one effect, and the count of recipes is what measures the manual."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmAmorPerStercora a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Reiße ihren Namen aus Ägypten für vierzig Tage, dreiunddreißig Monate, 175 Tage"@de,
        "Tear her name out of Egypt for forty days, thirty-three months, 175 days"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso XII/1-XIII/9, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "The long eroticon of the verso. The invocation asks that the named woman waste away, that her sleep be taken, and that her name be torn out of Egypt for forty days, thirty-three months, 175 days, the complement of six months. Dung of crocodile, a little placenta of a she-ass, sisymbrium, seven oipi of antelope dung, the gall of a male goat and first-fruits of oil are heated with stalks of flax; the invocation is recited to it seven times for seven days, the operator anoints his phallus with it, lies with the woman and anoints her breast also."@en ;
    skos:note "Four durations in one clause, one of which is given twice in different units, and the whole of it a measure of how long something is to be absent rather than of when the operator is to look. Entered because the terms of a transmission are the material of a condition of failure, and this is the longest string of them in the work."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmAnulusCordisMuris a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Goldring mit dem Herz der Spitzmaus, für Gunst"@de,
        "The gold ring with the heart of the shrew-mouse, for favour"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XIII/20-21 and col. verso XXXII/12-13, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "The heart of the shrew-mouse is put into a seal-ring of gold worn on the hand, and the operator goes anywhere; then it brings him favour, love and reverence. The verso repeats it: you put its heart in a ring of gold and put it on your hand, then it gives you great praise, love and respect."@en ;
    skos:note "The second doublet of the same animal, and the same outcome as the wax ape of col. XI reaches by a formula and a wreath. Two prescriptions with nothing in common but the end, which is the count SPEC §15 wants kept."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmAnulusLeonum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Ring mit zwei Löwen auf der Lampe, dann kommt sie sogleich"@de,
        "The ring of two lions on the lamp, then she comes at once"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso XIX/1-7, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A spell for bringing a woman out of her house. A part of a wild she-cat is dried and a heel-tendon of a drowned animal taken; a ring is fashioned whose bezel is variegated with gold in the form of two lions with open mouths facing each other. To bring a woman at any time, the ring is placed on the upper part of a lighted lamp and the demand spoken that she be brought to this place quickly in these moments of to-day. Then she comes at once."@en ;
    skos:note "At any time and she comes at once together: the prescription claims to be independent of the calendar and immediate in its effect, which is the strongest combination in the work and the one with the least said about what would show it had not worked."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmAnulusMuris a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Ring mit dem Spitzmausschwanz, dass jede Frau, die du berührst, sich hingibt"@de,
        "The ring of the shrew-mouse tail, that any woman you touch gives herself"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XIII/13-17, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A living shrew-mouse is set on a Syrian pot, placed on the backbone of a donkey, its tail put in a Syrian pot or a glass, and let loose alive within the door of the woman bath; the tail is gilded and embalmed, pounded myrrh added, and put in a gold ring worn on the finger after the charms have been recited to it. Then any woman whom the operator takes hold of gives herself to him. It is done when the moon is full."@en ;
    skos:note "A date is set and no sign is announced, so there is nothing in the prescription that a performance at full moon could fail to show."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmAquaMulierisPrima a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das erste Mittel, Wasser bei einer Frau zu heilen"@de,
        "The first remedy to cure water in a woman"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso VI/1, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A remedy to cure water in a woman, the first remedy: salt and oil pounded and applied daily for two days."@en ;
    skos:note "The head of the only prescription in the work that is numbered as a stage of a course: four entries follow it over two columns, each headed as the next, each with its own interval. The series is entered as four nodes because the work counts them as four."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmAquaMulierisQuarta a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das vierte, die Raute, von der Dämmerung bis zum Mittag gezogen"@de,
        "The fourth, the rue steeped from dawn till midday"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso VII/4-7, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "Another to follow: a new dish with ten measures of old sweet wine and half a kite of fresh rue standing from dawn till midday; she bathes, comes out and drinks it, and in the evening honey is put on her as above again for seven days."@en ;
    skos:note "Four stages, with intervals of two, three or four, seven and seven days, and not one word at any stage about what is to be seen before the next is begun. A course this long with no criterion between its stages is the clearest instance in the work of a procedure that cannot end in a failure."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmAquaMulierisSecunda a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das zweite Mittel, nach den zwei Tagen"@de,
        "The second remedy, after the two days"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso VI/2-8, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "After the two days, the second remedy: white lead pounded very carefully with a little pigment from an oil-dealer, true oil of fine quality and an egg; a fine-spun strip of linen is dipped in it, she bathes and washes in good wine, the medicated strip is drawn in and out of her for a short time until the medicament spreads, then removed and she is left till evening; in the evening a bandage dipped in genuine honey is put on her until morning, for three days, another copy saying four."@en ;
    skos:note "The one place in the whole manuscript where a stage is expressly timed from the end of the one before, after the two days. The variant reading three or four days is the compiler own, like the competing invocations of the recto."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmAquaMulierisTertia a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das dritte, danach, der Gurkensaft am Mittag"@de,
        "The third, to follow it, the cucumber juice at midday"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso VII/1-4, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "Another to follow it: a ladleful of juice of a rubbed-down cucumber and a ladleful of water of the ears of a named animal, with a measure of good wine, drunk at midday before she has eaten anything whatever and after bathing; in the evening the rag with honey is put on her as above for seven days."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmAurisAquosaPrima a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Mittel für ein wässriges Ohr"@de,
        "A medicament for an ear that is watery"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso IV/1-3, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A medicament for an ear that is watery: salt heated with good wine, applied to it after cleansing it first."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmAurisAquosaSecunda a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Dasselbe noch einmal, vier Tage lang aufgelegt"@de,
        "The same again, applied for four days"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso IV/4-5, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "The second entry for the same ear: salt scraped and heated with wine, applied to it for four days."@en ;
    skos:note "A term without a question and without a sign: four days are named, and nothing is said about what is to be seen at the end of them. The gout treatment at pmc:PdmPodagraInterrogatio is the only place in the work where a term of this kind carries a question."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmCaecitasPerCerevisiam a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Blendung durch das, was einem Menschen ans Auge gelegt wird"@de,
        "Blinding by what is put to the eye of a man"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XIII/26, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A single clause closing the series: you put beer, the reading uncertain, to the eye of a man, then he is blinded."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmCaecitasPerMurem a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Blendung durch die ertränkte Spitzmaus"@de,
        "Blinding by the drowned shrew-mouse"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XIII/11-12, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A shrew-mouse is drowned in some water and the man is made to drink of it; then he is blinded in his two eyes."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmCaecitasSanguineCaprimulgi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Blendung durch Ziegenmelkerblut im Auge"@de,
        "Blinding by night-jar blood in the eye"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XXIV/31, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "Another: a night-jar blood is put into his eye; then he is blinded."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmCaecitasSanguineVespertilionis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein weiteres, Fledermausblut, so geht es wieder"@de,
        "Another, a bat blood, this is the manner of it again"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XXIV/32, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "Another: a bat blood; this is the manner of it again. The entry gives the ingredient and refers back to the preceding prescription for the manner, so the eye and the outcome are carried over and not restated."@en ;
    skos:note "An entry whose whole content is a substitution in the recipe before it. Entered because a prescription that works by reference is still a prescription, and because the back-reference is the mechanism by which the series grows without any of its claims being stated twice."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmCataplexiaCapitisAsini a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Vier Tage, dann schläft er, sieben Tage, dann stirbt er"@de,
        "Four days he sleeps, seven days he dies"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XXIII/1-8, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A spell to inflict catalepsy. An ass head is placed between the operator feet opposite the sun in the morning and again in the evening; the right foot is anointed with set-stone of Syria and the left with clay, the hands with ass blood, and the charms uttered towards the sun in the morning and evening of four days, then he sleeps. If the operator wishes to make him die, he does it for seven days, with a thread of palm-fibre bound to his hand and a mat of wild palm-fibre to his phallus and his head. The invocation is to Typhon Set. The recipe closes very excellent."@en ;
    skos:note "The one prescription of this work in which the count of days is the whole difference between two outcomes, and therefore the one place where the manual makes a quantity answerable: four days are to produce sleep and not death, seven death. It belongs to the witnesses of generalizations/counted-thresholds.ttl."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmCataplexiaDactylorum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Gegen Starre, Mehl wilder Datteln im Wein"@de,
        "For catalepsy, flour of wild dates in wine"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XXIV/1a-5a, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "For catalepsy, another: flour of wild dates beaten up with milk, with a further ingredient lost, made up together into a ball and put in the wine. The entry stands in the margin of the column, numbered separately by the editors."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmCataplexiaFelCerastae a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Mittel gegen Starre, Hornvipergalle und westliche Äpfel"@de,
        "A medicament for catalepsy, gall of cerastes and western apples"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XXIV/27-28 and col. verso III/1-3, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A medicament for catalepsy: gall of cerastes, pips of western apples and herb of klo pounded together, made into a pill and put into the food. The verso repeats the same three ingredients with the same instruction, there made into a ball and put into wine and drunk."@en ;
    skos:note "A doublet between the recto pharmacology and the drug list of the verso, with the vehicle changed from food to wine and the drinker from the victim to an unnamed subject. The two places are the reason the drug list of the verso is not a separate collection but the same material entered twice."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmCautioAspectus a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Lass ihn nirgends anders hinsehen, sonst fürchtet er sich"@de,
        "Do not let him look elsewhere, or he is afraid"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XVII/16-20, with col. XIV/27-30, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "The one instruction in the work whose breach is given a named consequence in the medium. The boy is to look towards the lamp and towards nothing else; if he does not look towards it, then he is afraid. Where that has happened the operator is to do all these things, cease from the inquiry, return, make the boy close his eyes, and recite a further invocation over him if the gods go away and the boy ceases to see them, asking that the boy not be frightened, terrified or scared and be made to return to his original path. A separate formula dismisses the god to his home at the end of a successful session, and the lamp, the vessel and the cloth are then taken off the child."@en ;
    skos:note "The caution is about the medium and not about the operator or the god, and the harm it names is fright. It is the only place in the work where an inquiry is broken off, and the reason given is the state of the boy; a run ended this way is therefore not an inquiry that produced nothing but a boy who was not fit at that moment."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:PdmShenHbs ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmCorMulierisConversum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Herz einer Frau einem Mann nachsetzen, in einem Augenblick getan"@de,
        "Putting the heart of a woman after a man, done in one moment"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XXV/23-37 and col. XXVI/1-18, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A method to put the heart of a woman after a man, which the work says is done in one moment and comes to pass instantly. A living swallow and a living hoopoe are anointed on their heads with lotus ointment and with blood of a male ass and blood of the tick of a black cow, a cry is uttered before the sun at its rising, their heads are cut off, the hearts taken out of the right ribs, anointed again with the same blood, put in an ass skin and laid in the sun four days; when dry they are pounded and kept in a box in the house. To use it, a shaving of a pleasure-wood is taken, the named words recited over it, and it is put in a cup of wine or beer and given to the woman. It is done on the fourteenth of the lunar month. Very excellent. Two further invocations for the same cup follow, the second marked as taken from another book."@en ;
    skos:note "Done in one moment and comes to pass instantly is the only interval in the non-divinatory half of the work short enough to be watched, and it is the strongest claim in it for that reason. Nothing is named that would show it had not come to pass."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmDonatoresNominati a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Zusammensteller nennt, wer ihm das Verfahren gab"@de,
        "The compiler names who gave him the procedure"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. I/1, col. VIII/12, col. XIV/1 and col. XVIII/7, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "The work repeatedly says where a recipe came from, in the first person. A vessel-divination which a physician of the nome of Pemze gave to me; another vessel-inquiry which a physician in the Oxyrhynchus nome gave me; that which another man said to me; a direct inquiry by the voice of Pasash the priest of Kes, he the informant telling it. Beside the donors it cites competing copies: another roll says, as says another book, another volume saith, another says, another book gives this name instead."@en ;
    skos:note "A compiler who marks his sources and his variants is doing in the third century what this graph does now, and the marks are the reason several of its claims can be attributed at all. Two of the four named donors are physicians, which places the divination and the medicine of the verso in the same hands and not merely in the same manuscript."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmFebrisRemota a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Fieber bei Nacht und das Fieber bei Tag nehmen"@de,
        "Removing the fever by night and the fever by day"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso XXXIII/1-9, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "The last column of the roll. Horus, going up a hill at midday on a horse, refuses the food of the gods because he is ill in his head and his body, a fever has taken hold of him and a south wind has seized him; he asks whether Isis has ceased to make magic and Nephthys to give health, and whether the gods sit eating until they remove the fever from the head of the son of Isis and from the head of the named patient, the fevers by night, the fevers by day, the headache, this burning, this heat. It is said over genuine oil seven times, and his hand, his body and his feet are anointed while the words are pronounced to him."@en ;
    skos:note "A healing claim about a condition that remits on its own, made in a narrative in which the sufferer is the god. The work ends on it, and it names no interval: the fevers are to be removed, with nothing said about when or about what a fever on the following day would mean."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmFoliumMulieris a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Binsenblatt auf dem Mund einer Mumie, dass es eine Frau bringe"@de,
        "The rush-leaf on the mouth of a mummy, that it bring a woman"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso XVII/5-8, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "The dream-sending prescription continues: it brings a woman also. A name is written on the rush-leaf with the blood of a named bird or a hoopoe, the hair of the woman is put in the leaf, the leaf laid on the mouth of a mummy, and the name written on the earth with the demand that the named woman be brought to the sleeping-place in which the named man is."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as its own node and not with the dream-sending at pmc:PdmResponsumPerSomnium because the work marks it as a further use, with a different ingredient, a different place for the leaf and a different claimed outcome."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmGratiaAnteOmnes a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Lob, Liebe und Ehrfurcht vor jedem Menschen und vor dem König"@de,
        "Praise, love and reverence before every man and before the king"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XI/1-26, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A spell of giving favour, said over an ape of wax. An oxyrhynchus fish is put in prime lily or moringa oil with styrax, frankincense and seeds of the great-of-love plant in a metal vase; a wreath of flowers is anointed with the oil and the spell recited over it seven times before the sun in the morning, before speaking to any man on earth; the operator anoints his face with it, takes the wreath in his hand and goes among any people. The claim is that it brings him great praise among them exceedingly, and in the invocation praise, love and reverence before a named man, before the king and his host, and that the named man will do everything the operator says to him."@en ;
    skos:note "The recipe closes with a comparative and a pedigree rather than a sign: this scribe feat is that of King Darius, there is no better than it. Neither names what a worse one lacked, which is the absence the search at pmp:PdmReworkingSearch found across the whole work."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmIctusScorpionis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Formel für den Stich, mit der Zunge geleckt und täglich geölt"@de,
        "The formula for the sting, licked with the tongue and oiled daily"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XX/1-27, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A spell spoken to the sting, in which Isis tells the young Anubis to lick the edges of the wound with his tongue and to swallow what he licks up rather than spit it on the ground. The operator licks it with his tongue while it is bleeding, immediately; thereafter he recites to a little oil seven times, puts it on the sting daily and soaks a strip of linen to lay on it. A second spell is given for the oil, said seven times, which ends twice with the question whether the oil will not make it well."@en ;
    skos:note "The daily application has no stated end, unlike the dog-bite formula that runs until it is well. The question put to the oil, wilt thou not make it well, is the nearest the work comes to admitting that a remedy might not, and it is put to the ingredient rather than to the outcome."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmInimicusMedicatus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Deinen Feind betäuben, mit einem Käfer, in Styrax verbrannt"@de,
        "To drug your enemy, with a beetle burnt with styrax"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso II/16-20, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "To drug your enemy: a named beetle is burnt with styrax and pounded together with one drachma of apple and a further ingredient; the remainder of the entry is lost."@en ;
    skos:note "The entry stands between two blocks of bare mineral names, which is how the verso is arranged throughout: the prescriptions are not collected but interleaved with the glossary. That is the reason a run that reads the verso for its drug names will pass over them."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmInsaniaPerAccipitrem a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Jeden Mann oder jede Frau wahnsinnig machen, durch den lebend freigelassenen Falken"@de,
        "To make mad any man or any woman, by the hawk released alive"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso XXIX/1-6, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A spell to make mad any man or any woman. The hair of the person is taken together with the hair of a dead or murdered man, the two tied to each other and tied to the body of a hawk, and the hawk released alive. If the operator wishes to do it for some days, he puts the hawk in a place and feeds it in his house."@en ;
    skos:note "The duration is set by keeping the bird, so the operation continues as long as the operator keeps feeding it. That is a term the operator controls at both ends, which is the opposite of the criterion that no party controls."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmIraSuperiorisAversa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Vor einen Vorgesetzten treten, der mit dir streitet und nicht verhandeln will"@de,
        "Going to meet a superior who fights with you and will not parley"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XV/24-31, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A spell said seven times, given first in Greek and then in its demotic translation, in which the operator declares that he is carrying the mummy of Osiris to Abydos to deposit it in Alkhah, and that if the named man deals blows at him he will cast it at him."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because the threat is conditional and names the condition: the operator does not claim the superior will relent but states what he will do if blows come. That is the shape of a condition of failure without being one, since what follows the condition is a further act of the operator and not an end to the operation."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmLucernaAdductionis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Lampe, die von Abend bis Morgen brennt, eine Frau zu bringen"@de,
        "The lamp that burns from evening till morning to bring a woman"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso XVI/1-7, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "Named words asking that the woman be brought out of her abodes to the house of the named man are written in myrrh ink on a strip of clean fine linen and put in a clean new lamp filled with genuine oil, which stands in the operator house from evening till morning. If a hair of the woman can be found to put in the wick, it is excellent."@en ;
    skos:note "A single night is the interval, which makes this one of the few prescriptions in the work whose claim falls due at a named time. A row of figures, three scarabs, three hawks and three goats, is drawn at the head of the column in the papyrus itself."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmMedicamentumScammoniae a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Mittel, einen Menschen zu betäuben, erprobt"@de,
        "A medicament to drug a man, tested"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XXIV/1-5, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A medicament, when you wish to drug a man, marked tested: scammony root one drachm, opium one drachm, pounded with milk, made into a ball and put into some cooked food, and he is let eat it; then he is upset."@en ;
    skos:note "One of the six places where the work marks a recipe as tried, collected at pmc:PdmProbatumNovies. This is the only one of the six that stands over a prescription with weights, and the mark says nothing about who tried it or what followed."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmMedicamentumSiccum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das trockene Mittel aus Alaun, Pfeffer und Satyrion"@de,
        "The dry medicament of alum, pepper and satyrium"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso XIV/1-7, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A prescription whose heading breaks off after to make: alum one drachm, pepper one drachm, a named dried ingredient four drachms and satyrium four drachms, pounded together into a dry medicament, with which the operator does his business as he knows with any woman."@en ;
    skos:note "The heading is lost and the outcome therefore unknown; what the node carries is the prescription and the fact that its end is not readable, which is different from an entry that states none."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmMorbusCutisInsanabilis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Eine Hautkrankheit, die nicht geheilt werden soll"@de,
        "A skin disease that shall not be healed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XIII/24-25, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "Two named lizards are cooked with oil and the man washed with them, for the stated purpose of producing a skin disease on a man and that it shall not be healed. A further clause follows for making it troublesome, with the ingredient lost in a lacuna."@en ;
    skos:note "The only prescription in the work that states its own permanence as part of the claim, and the only one whose end is that another operation shall not take effect. The verso carries no cure for it; the healing prescriptions of the work address bites, stings, bones, blood, feet, ears, eyes and fever."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmMorsPerAccipitrem a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Tod durch den im Wein ertränkten Falken"@de,
        "Death by the hawk drowned in wine"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XIII/21-22 and col. XXIV/33-34, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A hawk is drowned in a measure of wine and the man made to drink it; then he dies. The same prescription stands a second time in the series of col. XXIV, there with a jar of wine and with the outcome given as then it does its work."@en ;
    skos:note "One of five prescriptions that stand once with a named outcome at col. XIII and once at col. XXIV without one. The five are at pmc:PdmMorsPerAccipitrem, pmc:PdmMorsPerFelMuris, pmc:PdmMorsPerFelMustelae, pmc:PdmMorsPerLacertam and pmc:PdmPotioMurisAmatoria."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmMorsPerFelMuris a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Tod auf der Stelle durch die Galle der Spitzmaus im Wein"@de,
        "Death at once by the gall of the shrew-mouse in wine"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XIII/19-20, col. XXIV/35-36 and col. verso XXXII/10-11, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "The gall of the shrew-mouse is put into a measure of wine and the man drinks it; then he dies at once. Or it is put into any piece of food. The verso gives the same with the woman as the one who drinks and the outcome she dies instantly, or put it in meat or some food; a third place, among the series of animal preparations of col. XXIV, gives the gall added to wine with the outcome left unnamed, then it does its work very much."@en ;
    skos:note "The three places together are the clearest instance in this work of what the manual does with an outcome. Twice it is named and immediate, once it is replaced by the formula that it does its work; the operator reading the third passage cannot tell from it what is supposed to happen, and the first two show that the compiler could have said."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmMorsPerFelMustelae a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Tod durch die Galle des alexandrinischen Wiesels in der Speise"@de,
        "Death by the gall of the Alexandrian weasel in food"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XIII/22-23 and col. XXIV/37, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "The gall of an Alexandrian weasel is put into any food; then he dies. The same stands at col. XXIV in the series of animal preparations, with the outcome given as then it does its work, and again a little earlier in the soporific series, where it is offered as another medicament for making a man sleep."@en ;
    skos:note "The same ingredient is offered for death and for sleep within thirteen lines, which is the limit of what the compiler distinguishes. The soporific use is entered separately at pmc:PdmSomniferumFelMustelae because the stated end is different."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmMorsPerLacertam a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Tod durch die zweischwänzige Eidechse, in Öl gekocht"@de,
        "Death by the two-tailed lizard cooked in oil"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XIII/23-24 and col. XXIV/37-39, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A two-tailed lizard is put into the oil and cooked, and the man anointed with it; then he dies, the translators marking the verb as uncertain. The same prescription stands at col. XXIV with the outcome given as then it does its work, and once more in the soporific series as the bare entry another, a two-tailed lizard."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmMorsPerMuremEsam a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Tod durch den in Speise geriebenen Leib der Spitzmaus"@de,
        "Death by the body of the shrew-mouse ground into food"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XIII/12-13, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "The body of the shrew-mouse is ground with any piece of food and the man made to eat it; then he swells up and he dies."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmMorsSanguineCameli a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Tod durch Kamelblut mit dem Blut eines Toten im Wein"@de,
        "Death by camel blood with the blood of a dead man in wine"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XXIV/29-30, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "Another: camel blood with the blood of a dead man is put into the wine and the man made to drink it; then he dies."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmMorsusCanisPrimus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die erste Formel für den Hundebiss, täglich gesprochen, bis er heil ist"@de,
        "The first formula for the bite of a dog, addressed daily until it is well"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XIX/1-9, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A spell spoken to the bite of the dog, in which the operator threatens to take the dog up to the court of the temple of Osiris if it does not extract its venom; then garlic is pounded with kmou and put on the wound, and the wound is addressed daily until it is well."@en ;
    skos:note "Until it is well is a stopping rule of a kind the divinatory half of the work never states: the operation ends at an observable condition of the patient and not at a count of repetitions. The search at pmp:PdmFailureConditionSearch found no such clause anywhere in the inquiries."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmMorsusCanisSecundus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die zweite Formel für den Hundebiss, mit Salz auf der gereinigten Wunde"@de,
        "The second formula for the bite of a dog, with salt on the cleansed wound"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XIX/32-40, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A second spell spoken to the bite of the dog, named as the exorcism of Amen and Thriphis; the wound is cleansed, salt is pounded with an ingredient lost in a lacuna, and applied to him."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmMorsusCanisTertius a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das dritte gegen den Hundebiss, Raute mit Honig"@de,
        "The third for the bite of a dog, rue with honey"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XIX/40, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "Another: rue is pounded with honey and applied; the spell is also said to a cup of water and the man made to drink it, the translators marking the last clause as uncertain."@en ;
    skos:note "Three prescriptions for one injury within forty lines, which is the highest count of alternatives the work gives for anything outside the inquiry, and none of the three says what to do if the first fails."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmNominaFurisEtNavis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die vier Namen, die einen Verbrecher herbeibringen und ein Schiff vor dem Untergang bewahren"@de,
        "The four names that bring in a criminal and keep a ship from foundering"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso XV/1-7, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "Four names of gods are given for bringing in a criminal by vessel-questioning; the same name is to be uttered before a ship that is about to founder, on account of the names of Dioskoros which are within, and it is safe. The names are recited to the bowl of Adonai, which is written outside, and the work says it will do a mighty work in bringing in a criminal."@en ;
    skos:note "One prescription with two unconnected applications, one of which is the only claim in the work about an event outside the room: a ship about to founder either founders or does not, and the clause and it is safe is the plainest statement of outcome in the whole manuscript. It is entered as one node because the work gives one set of names, and both ends are named here so that neither is lost."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmOphthalmiaSanata a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Augenentzündung bei einem Menschen heilen, mit dem Öl und dem geschriebenen Amulett"@de,
        "To heal ophthalmia in a man, with the oil and the written amulet"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso XX/1-7, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "To heal ophthalmia in a man, the reading of the complaint uncertain. A spell is said to a little oil with salt and nasturtium seed added, and the man anointed with it; the same is written on a new papyrus and made into a written amulet on his body, with the words thou art this eye of heaven and a drawn eye with rays."@en ;
    skos:note "The prescription puts the remedy in two forms at once, an application and an amulet, with no order between them and no sign for either. The drawing stands in the papyrus itself and is the only figure the editors reproduce inside a healing entry."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmOsInGutturePrimum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die erste Formel für einen im Hals steckenden Knochen"@de,
        "The first formula for a bone stuck in the throat"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XIX/21-32, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A spell said seven times, then invocation over a little oil; the man face is put upwards and the oil poured into his mouth, the operator places finger and nail to the two muscles of his throat, makes him swallow the oil and start up suddenly, and the oil is ejected immediately; then the bone comes up with the oil."@en ;
    skos:note "A stated outcome at a stated moment, produced by a mechanical act, and the only prescription of the work whose claimed result is visible to everyone in the room at the moment it is claimed. It belongs beside the pregnancy test at pmc:PdmProbatioGraviditatis and the assay of the stone at pmc:PdmProbatioLapidis: all three stand outside the divinatory half."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmOsInGuttureSecundum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die zweite Formel, einen Knochen aus dem Hals zu holen"@de,
        "The second formula to fetch a bone out of a throat"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XX/27-33, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A spell spoken seven times to a cup of water, which the patient is made to drink; the invocation names bone of god, bone of man, bone of bird, bone of fish, bone of animal, bone of everything, and asks that what is in the belly come to the heart, what is in the heart to the mouth, and what is in the mouth to the operator hand here to-day."@en ;
    skos:note "Unlike the formula at col. XIX/21-32 nothing mechanical is done, and the claimed result is placed in the operator hand to-day, which is an interval. The second of two prescriptions for one condition, and the one without the manual act."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmPesFicuum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein weiteres für den Fuß, Sykomorenfeigen mit Akazien- und Perseafrucht"@de,
        "Another for the foot, sycomore figs with acacia and persea fruit"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso XI/10-11, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "Another: sycomore figs of an unread kind, fruit of acacia and persea fruit pounded together and applied to him. No outcome is stated."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmPesLuxatus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Mittel für einen stark verstauchten Fuß, sehr vortrefflich"@de,
        "The remedy for a badly sprained foot, very excellent"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso XI/7-9, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A remedy for a foot which is much sprained, marked very excellent: his foot is washed with juice of cucumber and it is rubbed well on his foot."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmPesUnctus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Mittel für einen Fuß, mit kaltem Wasser abgewaschen, wenn es trocken ist"@de,
        "The remedy for a foot, washed off with cold water when it is dry"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso XI/1-6, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A remedy for an afflicted foot, the term lost: garlic, frankincense, an old ingredient and genuine oil pounded together and used to anoint him; when it is dry it is washed off with cold water; then he recovers."@en ;
    skos:note "A sequence with an observable step inside it, when it is dry, and an outcome after it. The dryness orders the procedure and does not decide it, which is the same construction as the three days of questioning in the gout treatment."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmPodagraCataplasma a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Umschlag gegen Gicht, auf die schmerzende Stelle gelegt"@de,
        "The poultice for gout, applied to the painful part"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso IX/1-9, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "Another for gout: one kite of euphorbia, half a kite of pepper, a stater each of pyrethrum and adarces, a stater of native sulphur, six staters of any wine and genuine oil, pounded and made into a poultice, applied to the part which is painful."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmPodagraInterrogatio a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Du fragst den Mann drei Tage lang, ob es gehört hat"@de,
        "You ask the man for three days whether it has hearkened"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso VIII/1-8, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "The gout treatment of the verso sets the man down with clay under his feet, and then: you ask the man, saying, Has it hearkened? for three days. Thereafter a further course of anointing follows, with an ant cooked in oil of henna, then figs, dried grapes and potentilla pounded with wine, and the operator blows on him with his mouth."@en ;
    skos:note "A term and a question, which is more than any inquiry in the work carries, and it is put to the patient rather than to a sign. What follows the three days is not a verdict but the next remedy, so the interval orders the treatment and does not close it. It is entered because a stated interval with a stated question is the raw material of a condition of failure, and because the work has three of them in its medical half and none in its divinatory one."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmPodagraLamella a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Amulett für den gichtigen Fuß, angebunden, wenn der Mond im Löwen steht"@de,
        "The talisman for the gouty foot, bound on when the moon is in Leo"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso X/1-12, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "Another talisman for the foot of the gouty man: named words are written on a strip of silver or tin, put on a deer-skin and bound to the foot of the named man, with the request that he recover from every pain which is in his feet and two legs. It is done when the moon is in the constellation of Leo."@en ;
    skos:note "The third prescription for gout within three columns, and the one that turns the condition into a date. Beside it the work puts a star under the earth at the moon in Scorpio, and the ring of the shrew-mouse at full moon; three dates in the whole manuscript, none of them with a consequence for the wrong one."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmPotioCapillorumOccisi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Trank aus dem Haar eines gewaltsam Gestorbenen"@de,
        "The potion of the hair of a man who died a violent death"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XV/1-20, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A shaving of the head of a man who has died a violent death, seven grains of barley buried in a grave, nine or ten measures of apple-seeds, blood of a worm of a black dog, blood of the second finger of the left hand and the operator semen are pounded together, put in a cup of wine with three uteh of the first-fruits of the vintage before anyone has tasted it, invoked over seven times and given to the woman to drink; the skin of the parasite is tied with a band of byssus to the operator left arm. What is asked is that she feel love, longing and madness, seeking him everywhere, a flame of fire in her heart in the moment of not seeing him."@en ;
    skos:note "The invocation asks for a state in a second person that the operator cannot inspect and sets no interval. It is the longest erotic prescription of the recto and the only one whose ingredients are three times taken from the dead."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmPotioMurisAmatoria a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Trank aus getrockneter Spitzmaus und dem Blut des Herzfingers"@de,
        "The potion of the dried shrew-mouse and the blood of the heart-finger"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XIII/17-19 and col. verso XXXII/1-9, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "The dried body of the shrew-mouse is pounded, a little of it mixed with a little blood of the second finger, that of the heart, of the left hand, put in a cup of wine and given to the woman to drink; then she has a passion for the operator. The work gives the prescription twice, the second time with the gall and the heart of the same animal set aside first for the two uses that follow."@en ;
    skos:note "A doublet inside one manuscript, at two unconnected places, with the same ingredient, the same finger, the same cup and the same outcome. The verso version prefaces it by taking out the gall and the heart and putting each in a place of its own, so the three uses of the one animal stand there as a series where the recto has them as three consecutive clauses."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmPotioUnguis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der zweite Trank, aus Nagelspan und Apfel"@de,
        "The second potion, of the nail-paring and the apple"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XV/21-23, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "Another method of doing it again: the paring of the operator nail from an apple-fruit with blood of the finger named before; the apple is pounded, the blood put on it, the whole put in the cup of wine, invoked seven times, and the woman made to drink it at the moment named."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmProbatumNovies a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Erprobt, und neunmal erprobt"@de,
        "Tested, and tested nine times"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. IV/23, col. V/3, col. VIII/12, col. XIV/31, col. XXIV/1 and col. XXIX/1, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "The work marks certain procedures as having been tried, and twice attaches a number. A question-form, tested. A method of lucky-shadows, that is tested. A direct inquiry by the voice of Pasash the priest of Kes, he tells it, saying it is tested, nine times. You do it also by vessel-inquiry alone, excellent, tried, tested nine times. Behold a form of inquiry of the sun, of which they say it is well tested. A medicament, when you wish to drug a man — tested. Elsewhere the marks are bare praise: very excellent, very good, there is nothing in the world better than it, excellent and tried."@en ;
    skos:note "Nowhere does the work say who tried, on what question, against what afterwards, or what an unsuccessful trial would have looked like. The mark is attached to the recipe and never to an outcome, and it sits in the same manuscript as the graded compulsions that make an unsuccessful trial unrecordable. A claim of nine trials from a transmission that has no way of counting a failure is a claim about nine performances and not about nine results."@en,
        "The editors read the phrase as a formula rather than as a count: nine times tried seems proverbial, they note, comparing the two places where it stands. That reading is theirs and is entered as theirs; what the text carries is the number, twice, at two unconnected recipes, and once as a report of what somebody else said about a third."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmProsperitasManus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Dass alles, was du tust, in deiner Hand gedeihe"@de,
        "That everything you do shall prosper in your hand"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XVI/18-21, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "Another method of it again. The operator rises early from his bed on the day on which he will do it, or any day, and pronounces a named invocation before Phre three times or seven times, in order that everything which he will do shall prosper in his hand, he being pure from every abomination."@en ;
    skos:note "The purity clause here carries no consequence of its own, unlike the one at col. XVII/26 which says in plain words that it does not succeed. Two clauses of purity in one manuscript, one with a stated consequence and one without, and the one with it belongs to the inquiry."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmProsperitasOperis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Anrufung vor Phre, dass gelinge, was du tust"@de,
        "The invocation before Phre that what you do may succeed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XVI/15-17, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "An invocation of two names pronounced seven times before Phre in the morning, before reciting to the boy, in order that that which the operator does may succeed. It stands inside the lamp-divination of col. XVI but claims no apparition and nothing of the inquiry."@en ;
    skos:note "The nearest thing in the work to a claim about outcomes in general, and it is unfalsifiable in the plainest way: no act is named, no interval is set, and there is no result that would not fall under it. Entered because the examinability-driven entrance of SPEC §14 needs the negative cases counted as well as the positive ones."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmResponsumPerSomnium a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Antwort, die statt dessen im Schlaf kommt"@de,
        "The answer that comes in sleep instead"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. V/6-8 and col. V/29-31, with col. verso XVII/3-4, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A second outlet the same procedures provide: after the invocation the operator lies down on a rush mat without speaking to anyone on earth, and the god makes answer to him by dream. In one recipe the two are offered as alternatives in one sentence — either the figure of a god stands behind the lamp and speaks concerning the question, or the operator lies down and the god comes to him. A separate short recipe writes a name on a rush-leaf, places it under the head, and is said both to make dreams and to send them, the sending being done by laying the leaf on the mouth of a mummy."@en ;
    skos:note "The alternative is what makes the session hard to end empty. A god who has not appeared at the lamp has not yet failed to answer, because the sleep that follows is part of the same procedure and its outcome is reported by the sleeper the next day. This is the same construction as the graded compulsions at pmc:PdmCompulsioNonAdventus, reached by a different route."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmResponsumPresupponitPuritatem a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Antwort setzt die Reinheit voraus"@de,
        "The answer presupposes purity"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XVII/26, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity because the source states the failure in the same clause as the requirement and in the plainest available form: it does not succeed. This is the rare case in this graph where a general condition is given a general consequence rather than being left to be read off a recipe."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "If it be that you do not apply purity to it, it does not succeed; its chief matter is purity."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:PdmResponsumVerum ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:PdmPuritas ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmSanguisSistendusPrimus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Mittel, Blut zu stillen, vor dem Essen genommen"@de,
        "A medicament to stop blood, taken before she has eaten"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso V/1-3, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A medicament to stop blood: juice of the plant called Great Nile together with beer, which the woman is made to drink in the morning before she has eaten; then it stops."@en ;
    skos:note "Then it stops is a stated outcome on a process that would have been visible to the patient within the day, and the condition on an empty stomach is the kind of clause that lets a failure be assigned to the taking rather than to the remedy."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmSanguisSistendusSecundus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das zweite, Blut zu stillen, Blatt von Skeiska und von Fliegenbronze"@de,
        "The second to stop blood, leaf of skeiska and of fly-bronze"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso V/9-11, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A second medicament to stop blood: leaf of skeiska and fresh leaf of the plant called fly-bronze, pounded and put on the operator, who then lies with the woman."@en ;
    skos:note "The remedy is applied to the man and the condition is the woman, which is the construction the whole erotic series of this work uses. Entered because the same heading as the preceding entry here produces a prescription of an entirely different shape, and a list node would have hidden that."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmSanguisSistendusTertius a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das dritte, Myrrhe und Knoblauch mit Gazellengalle"@de,
        "The third, myrrh and garlic with the gall of a gazelle"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso V/11-13, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "Another: myrrh, garlic and gall of a gazelle pounded with old scented wine, put on the operator, who lies with her."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmScarabaeusInVino a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Skarabäus im Weinbecher, eine Frau einen Mann lieben machen"@de,
        "The scarab of the cup of wine, to make a woman love a man"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XXI/10-20, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A small hornless scarab with three plates on the front of its head is taken at sunrise, the operator bound with a cloth on his upper back and a strip of palm-fibre on his face, and addressed before the sun seven times; it is drowned in milk of a black cow and left till evening, spread on sand under a circular strip of cloth for four days with frankincense burnt before it, then divided down the middle with a bronze knife. The right half with the nails of the right hand and foot is cooked on a new potsherd with vine wood, pounded with nine apple-pips and the operator urine or sweat, made into a ball, put in the wine, spoken over seven times and given to the woman to drink; the left half with the nails of the left side is bound in fine linen with myrrh and saffron and bound to the operator arm."@en ;
    skos:note "Every count in it is exact and none of them is answerable: a four-day drying, nine pips, seven addresses, two halves. The recipe divides the material between the woman and the operator, which is the construction that makes a failure attributable to either."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmSeparatioConiugum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Einen Mann von einer Frau und eine Frau von ihrem Mann trennen"@de,
        "Separating a man from a woman and a woman from her husband"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XIII/1-10, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "Gum and myrrh with wine are made into a figure of Geb holding a was-sceptre, and the invocation calls the fury of every god and goddess on a named man and a named woman. What is asked is hatred in his heart and quarrel to his face, nagging and squabbling and fighting at all times, until he casts her out of his abode and they are separated from each other without agreeing again for ever."@en ;
    skos:note "The only prescription of the work whose stated end is a duration without end, and the strongest case in it of an outcome that no interval can bring due: nothing is to be looked for at a named time, and nothing short of a reconciliation would count against it."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmShenHnePresupponitPuerum a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Gefäßbefragung mit Medium setzt den unberührten und geprüften Knaben voraus"@de,
        "The vessel-inquiry with a medium presupposes the untouched and tested boy"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. III/10-15 and col. X/14-15, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:note "Entered at prescribed order and not higher, deliberately. The work says who is to be brought and it says that the child must have been tested in his ears beforehand, but it nowhere says what happens if an untested or an unchaste boy is used; the nearest thing to a consequence is the grading of the test itself, which says that a boy whose left ear speaks is bad, and that is the outcome of the test and not of skipping it. The distinction is the one SPEC §17 keeps between an order and a necessity, and it is the commonest way a modern reading acquires a warrant the text does not give."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:PdmShenHne ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:PdmPuerIntactus ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmShenHneYieldsResponsum a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Gefäßbefragung bringt den Gott zum Antworten"@de,
        "The vessel-inquiry brings the god in to answer"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. II/24-III/5, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:note "Entered as the claim of the transmission and not as a result. What makes the pairing worth a node of its own is that the claimed effect is an event in one person's visual field, reported by that person, and that the person is chosen and graded by the operator before the session."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:PdmShenHne ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:PdmResponsumVerum .

pmc:PdmSignaAdventus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Was dem Knaben vorher angesagt wird, dass er es sehen werde"@de,
        "What the boy is told beforehand that he will see"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. II/1-3 and II/24-27, col. VI/6, col. XIV/24-25, col. XVII/12-15, col. XIX/25-32 and col. XXVII/16-19, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "The signs announced in advance, in the order in which the recipes expect them. The light appears and grows in the flame of the lamp; the boy says I see the light; Anubis comes in and takes his stand; the shadow of the god appears about the lamp; the gods are brought in, seated at a table, and eat and drink; the god who will answer stands up and tells his name; and in the variant done alone, the operator sees the god behind him while standing or lying down. In one recipe the boy is first asked what he sees and answers that he sees a darkness, and is then told the sentence he is to say next."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered under the separate count SPEC §15 requires for signs of success. Every one of them is told to the medium before the session by the person who will afterwards ask him whether it happened, and in the darkness-answer the text supplies the boy's next line as well. Read as evidence they can only confirm; the reason they are here is that a transmission which announces its signs in advance, in this detail, is itself a finding about the transmission."@en,
        "The count for this work, which is the point of keeping the two apart. Seven or eight announced signs of arrival against the conditions of failure listed at pmc:PdmCompulsioNonAdventus, of which not one is a verdict on the operation. The two procedures in this file that do state a verdict on both sides — the pregnancy test and the assay of the stone — are the two that have nothing to do with an apparition."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmSomniferumFelMustelae a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein weiteres Schlafmittel, die Galle eines alexandrinischen Wiesels in jeder Speise"@de,
        "Another soporific, the gall of an Alexandrian weasel in any food"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XXIV/25-26, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "Another, standing in the series of soporifics: gall of an Alexandrian weasel, added to any food."@en ;
    skos:note "The same ingredient and the same vehicle as the killing prescription at pmc:PdmMorsPerFelMustelae, under a different heading and with no quantity in either place. Entered separately because the stated end differs, and the pair is the finding."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmSomniferumLacertae a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein weiteres Schlafmittel, eine zweischwänzige Eidechse"@de,
        "Another soporific, a two-tailed lizard"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XXIV/26, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "Another, in the same series, consisting of the words a two-tailed lizard and nothing else: no preparation, no vehicle, no quantity and no outcome of its own."@en ;
    skos:note "The thinnest entry of the work, and it is entered for that reason. A series in which one member is a bare ingredient name shows what the compiler took to be self-evident, and the count of such entries is a measure of the manual that a list node makes unobtainable."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmSomniferumQuartum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das vierte, Apfelkerne mit Mehl zum Kuchen gestoßen"@de,
        "The fourth, apple pips pounded with flour into a cake"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XXIV/14-16, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "Another, the fourth: pips of apple, one stater and one kite, pounded with flour and made into a cake, which the man whom the operator wishes is made to eat. No outcome is stated beyond the heading of the series."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmSomniferumVini a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Mittel, einen Menschen schlafen zu machen, im Glas aufbewahrt"@de,
        "A medicament for making a man sleep, kept in a glass"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XXIV/17-22, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A medicament for making a man sleep, very good: pips of apple one stater and one drachma, mandragora root four drachmas, ivy four drachmas, pounded together, fifteen uteh of wine added, put into a glass vessel and kept; when it is to be given, a little is put into a cup of wine and given to the man."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmSomnusBiduanus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Einen Menschen zwei Tage schlafen machen"@de,
        "To make a man sleep for two days"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XXIV/6-14, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "Another, when you wish to make a man sleep for two days: mandragora root one ounce, liquorice one ounce, hyoscyamus one ounce, ivy one ounce, pounded with a measure of wine; done carefully, four portions of each with an uteh of wine, moistened from morning to evening, clarified, and given to drink; very good."@en ;
    skos:note "The one soporific of the series that names a duration, and therefore the one whose claim a bystander could have found false by the third day. Nothing in the entry says what a shorter sleep would mean."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmSpumaEquina a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Schaum aus dem Maul eines Hengstes"@de,
        "Foam of a stallion mouth"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso XIII/11-13, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A prescription whose end the editors leave in Latin, that a woman may love her own intercourse: foam of a stallion mouth, with which the phallus is anointed, and he lies with the woman."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmStercusAccipitris a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Falkendung mit Salz und zwei Pflanzen, zweifach vortrefflich"@de,
        "Hawk dung with salt and two plants, excellent twice over"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso XXV/1-8, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "Hawk dung, salt, the asi plant and the del plant are pounded together and the phallus anointed with it, and he lies with the woman; if it is dry, a little of it is pounded with wine and used in the same way. The entry closes excellent twice over."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmStercusAnseris a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Dung einer Smoune-Gans, dann fällt ihr Leib"@de,
        "Dung of a smoune-goose, then her body falls"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso XXX/1-2, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "The first entry of the dung series, its opening clause lost: dung of a smoune-goose, then her body falls."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmStercusAsini a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Auch Eselsdung, diese Art der Behandlung"@de,
        "Ass dung also, this method of treatment"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso XXX/11, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "The last entry of the series, consisting of the words ass dung also and this method of treatment."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmStercusCumMelle a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Dung mit Honig gestoßen, wie oben wieder"@de,
        "Dung pounded with honey, as above again"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso XXX/5-6, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "Another: dung of an animal whose name is lost is pounded with honey and the phallus anointed with it as above again."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmStercusHyaenae a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Hyänendung mit Rosensalbe"@de,
        "Dung of hyaena with ointment of roses"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso XXX/7-8, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "Another: dung of hyaena, the reading uncertain, with ointment of roses, as above again."@en ;
    skos:note "Four consecutive entries in which the whole prescription is an ingredient and the words as above again. They are entered one by one because the series is what a count of the manual measures: four claims, no outcome restated, and a back-reference doing the work of the instruction."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmStercusKel a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Dung eines Kel, dann fühlt sie deine Liebe"@de,
        "Dung of a kel, then she feels thy love"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso XXX/3-4, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "Another: the phallus is anointed with dung of a kel and he lies with the woman, then she feels his love."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmStercusUstum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Getrockneter und verbrannter Dung, zwei Maß, mit Hennaöl und Honig"@de,
        "Dung dried and burnt, two measures, with henna oil and honey"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso XXIII/10-12, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "The surviving end of a fragmentary eroticon: dung dried and burnt, two measures, pounded with oil of henna and honey, with which the phallus is anointed, and he lies with her. The first nine lines of the column are lost."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmSuffimenIchneumonis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Eine Frau mit Ichneumondung ausräuchern während der Blutung, dann ist sie geheilt"@de,
        "Fumigating a woman with ichneumon dung during menstruation, then she is cured"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso XXX/9-10, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "Another: a woman is fumigated with ichneumon dung when the menstruation is on her; then she is cured."@en ;
    skos:note "The one entry of the dung series that claims a healing and not an effect on desire, and it stands between two erotic ones with the same formula of reference. What she is cured of is not named."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmVenenumCordis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Gift aus dem Herzen eines Menschen ziehen, dem man einen Trank zu trinken gab"@de,
        "Extracting the venom from the heart of a man who has been made to drink a potion"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XIX/10-21, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A spell spoken over a cup of wine to which fresh rue is added, invoked seven times, and the man made to drink it in the morning before he has eaten. The invocation asks that he be healed of all poison, pus and venom which have been put to his heart, and that they be cast up."@en ;
    skos:note "The counterpart inside the same manuscript to the erotic potions of cols. XII, XV, XXI and XXV, which are administered in a cup of wine; the manual carries both the drugging and the antidote, and the antidote names a sign, that they be cast up."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmVirtutesCeterae a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die weiteren Vermögen, die der Papyrus beansprucht"@de,
        "The further capacities the papyrus claims"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XI, col. XIII, col. XV/24-31, col. XIX-XX, col. XXIII/1-20, col. XXIV, and col. verso V, VIII-X, XV, XVII, XX, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "Beside the inquiries the work claims, each with its own recipe: to obtain praise, love and reverence before every man, and before a king and his host; to avert the anger of a superior who will not parley; to see the bark of Phre; to make the moon or a god in the moon appear and speak; to see the shadow of every god and every goddess; to put the heart of a woman after a man and to separate a man and a woman; to bring a woman at once by a ring laid on a lighted lamp; to blind, to send a skin disease that shall not be healed, to make a man sleep for two days, and to kill; to heal the bite of a dog, a sting, poison in the heart, a bone in the throat, gout, water in the ear, ophthalmia and fever; to save a ship about to founder; and to know whether a woman will conceive."@en ;
    skos:note "The reason for the change, kept at the node because the node was the error. The run of 2026-09-18 drew them together on the ground that a recipe with an ingredient list and one clause of effect carries no chain of prerequisites, no caution and no condition of failure. SPEC §15 withdrew that ground in the same session: a recipe is a capacity, and that a recipe carries no examining layer is the finding about the recipe, countable only if the recipe is there. Debt filed as prima-materia#747 and discharged on 2026-10-09."@en,
        "Three claims of this work stand apart from the recipes because they state both sides of a criterion: the pregnancy test at pmc:PdmProbatioGraviditatis, the assay of the stone at pmc:PdmProbatioLapidis, and the double form announced at pmc:PdmRamshauFormaDuplex."@en,
        "What this node still claims and what it no longer carries. The finding in it is the range: the same manual that carries the divination carries the poisons, the erotica and the medicine, and the editors own table of contents sets them side by side under six headings, so the boundary between a procedure that claims an apparition and one that claims a physiological effect is not a boundary this transmission draws. The individual prescriptions are no longer held here. They stand one by one in traditions/demotic-recipes.ttl, seventy-seven nodes over the recto and the verso, each with its own column and line and its own stated outcome or the absence of one."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmVulnusSanatum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das trockene Pulver des Widderhornkrauts, auf jede Wunde gelegt"@de,
        "The dry powder of the ram-horn herb, applied to any wound"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso IV/10-14, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "Inside the plant entry for the herb called ram horn, whose Greek name is given as kephalike: the herb is pounded when dry, gathered and made into a dry powder, and applied to any wound; then it is cured."@en ;
    skos:note "A prescription hidden inside a glossary entry, with an outcome stated flatly and no condition at all. It is the widest healing claim of the work, any wound, and it stands where nothing but a description of a leaf and a stem was expected."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PenemueTeachesBitterAndSweet a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Penemue offenbart bitter und süß"@de,
        "Penemue discloses bitter and sweet"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 69:8 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
        "J.T. Milik, The Books of Enoch: Aramaic Fragments of Qumrân Cave 4 (Oxford, 1976)" ;
    skos:note "Book of Parables; no Aramaic fragment of chapters 37-71 is attested at Qumran."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:DiscernmentOfBitterAndSweet ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Penemue ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:PenemueTeachesWriting a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Penemue offenbart das Schreiben mit Tinte"@de,
        "Penemue discloses writing with ink"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 69:9 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
        "J.T. Milik, The Books of Enoch: Aramaic Fragments of Qumrân Cave 4 (Oxford, 1976)" ;
    skos:note "Book of Parables; no Aramaic fragment of chapters 37-71 is attested at Qumran."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:WritingWithInk ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Penemue ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:PerformancePresupposesTheMirror a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Vorstellung setzt den Spiegel voraus"@de,
        "The performance presupposes the mirror"@en ;
    dcterms:source "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXIII, sect. 99, p. 278" ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity because the source states the outcome of the want in plain terms and in the same sentence as the requirement. The substitute is admitted and is said not to be considered as good as the original; that qualification stands here rather than in the strength, because it weakens the object and not the necessity."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Where there is no mirror or its substitute, no performance is possible."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:TungusShamanizing ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:TungusToli ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SiberianShamanism .

pmc:PgmIvExtasisSolitarii a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Allein sprichst du das Wort des Gottes wie ein Ausser-sich-Seiender"@de,
        "Alone, you speak what the god said as one out of himself"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the instruction for magical use (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 16 lines 11-12)" ;
    skos:definition "The clause for the operator working without a companion: if you are alone and undertake the things spoken by the god, you speak them as one in ecstasy, uttering them as one possessed."@en ;
    skos:note "The clause distinguishes the solitary run from the run with a second person and says that the difference falls on the manner of speaking. With a companion the words are said over his head so low that he cannot hear them; alone they are said in ecstasy. The same words, and the transmission specifies a different delivery for each case."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PgmIvIdentitasAstri a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ich bin ein Stern, dessen Bahn eure Bahn ist"@de,
        "I am a star whose course is as your course"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.475-829, second utterance (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1907)" ;
    skos:definition "The second utterance, said after silence and before the disk expands: that the speaker is a star whose course runs with the course of those addressed, shining anew out of the depth."@en ;
    skos:note "A bare statement of what the speaker is, addressed to powers, with no request attached. It is the same shape as the ophite speeches without their closing petition, and it stands in a corpus with a documented route to that one rather than at a distance from it — so it is a second occurrence and not a second witness."@en,
        "The claim is of kinship in motion and not in rank: the course is the same course. Whether that is what the ophite speaker means when he names the symbol he already bears is exactly the question this file cannot settle, and it is why the two are held apart."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PgmIvImmortalitasHorae a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Unsterblichkeit für eben diese Stunde"@de,
        "Immortality for this same hour"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.475-829, fifth and tenth utterances and invocatory logos (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1907)" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity claimed: that the operator, one out of so many tens of thousands, is transformed to immortality in this same hour; that his human soul-power subsists for a little while in purity and will afterwards be transmitted back to him; and, in the closing salutation, that being born again he passes away in being made great, and having been made great he dies."@en ;
    skos:note "The last sentence is not a flourish. Being made great and dying are put in sequence and the second follows from the first, which places the death inside the attainment rather than after it."@en,
        "The promise carries its own term, and the term is short. A rite that says the state lasts an hour and then returns the operator to his necessity is making a smaller claim than the ascents this graph holds elsewhere, and a more checkable one: it says the practitioner will be back."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PgmIvOraculumIsCeded a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Beim Antworten ist das Handeln abgegeben"@de,
        "At the answering the agency is ceded"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the close of the rite (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 16 lines 1-7)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:cededAgency ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:PgmIvMemoriaInfallibilis ;
    pm:situationGround "The appendix states of this one moment that the operator is drained in soul and not in himself while he is being answered, and that he stands there speechless; the taking in of the oracle is said to happen of itself. Everywhere else in the piece the source has him act, so the placement is of the answering and not of the rite."@en .

pmc:PgmIvPetitioLibera a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Bitte den Gott, was du willst, und er wird es dir geben"@de,
        "Ask the god what you will, and he will give it to you"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the teaching of the operation (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 18 line 10)" ;
    skos:definition "The promise standing between the anointing and the constituting: ask of the god what you wish, and he will give it to you."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because its unconditionality is the finding and not a triviality. One sentence in this piece states a forfeiture and one sentence states a promise with no condition of any kind attached, and they are four pages apart in the same appendix. A reader who wants to know what the transmission thinks can be relied on has both."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PgmIvProsPhilous a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "An die Freunde, naemlich ein Homervers"@de,
        "For friends, being one verse of Homer"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the closing charms (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 21 lines 7-8)" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity claimed for a single hexameter set under the heading towards friends: the line asking that the enemy not be given cause to rejoice over them."@en ;
    skos:note "The same shape as the restrainer of anger and the same silence, and the two together are what a handbook of this kind looks like when it is not writing a liturgy: a purpose, a line, and no account of either."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PgmIvSalutatioNominatim a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Vierzehn Mächte, jede mit ihrem eigenen Namen begrüßt"@de,
        "Fourteen powers, each greeted by its own name"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.475-723, the ninth and tenth utterances (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wünsch, Leipzig 1910)" ;
    skos:definition "The two ranks met after the gates open: seven maidens in linen with serpent faces and golden sceptres, called the Fates of heaven and guardians of the four pillars, and seven gods with the faces of black bulls, in linen aprons and with seven golden diadems, called the pole-rulers. Each rank is greeted first as a body and then member by member, and the text requires that each be addressed with its own name, given in order from first to seventh."@en ;
    skos:note "The greeting is not a request. The maidens are saluted as holy and good; the pole-rulers are told what they do — they turn the axle of heaven at a single command and hurl thunder, lightning, earthquakes and bolts at the tribes of the impious — and are then asked for health, bodily wholeness, keenness of hearing and sight, and freedom from disturbance for the good hours of this day. A passage is not asked for and no passage is granted."@en,
        "This is the nearest thing in the corpus to a gate formula that names its keeper. The requirement is explicit — each is to be greeted with the name proper to it — and the order is fixed, so what is transmitted is a sequence of fourteen names in two ranks, not an invocation that could be paraphrased. In the translation read first, all fourteen stand as obeli; they are in the edition."@en,
        "Whether the names belong to the rite is the question the edition itself raises and does not close. Its editor holds that names must have stood in exactly these two places, which is a reconstruction from the shape of the passage rather than a mark in the manuscript, and he holds most other barbarous names in the piece to be interchangeable stock. One of the fourteen is read as Egyptian and as certainly belonging to the received text; that is the only one of them with an argument attached."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PgmIvScarabaeusMoritur a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Er kommt und frisst, und wenn er gefressen hat, stirbt er sogleich"@de,
        "It comes and eats, and when it has eaten it dies at once"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the teaching of the operation (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 17 lines 11-12)" ;
    skos:definition "The outcome the appendix states for the first step of the consecration: the operator will at once see the beetle coming and eating, and when it has eaten, it dies immediately."@en ;
    skos:note "A stated observable inside a preparation, and it is of the plainest kind this holding carries: an animal either comes and eats and dies or it does not. The text puts it as what will happen and gives no alternative, so a run in which the beetle refuses the cake or lives is a run the wording does not provide for. That is not a sign of success told in advance for the operator to look out for; it is a step whose completion is visible, and the difference is that this one can be seen to fail."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PgmIvSignaSuccessus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die geordnete Liste dessen, was zu sehen sein wird"@de,
        "The ordered list of what will be seen"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.475-829, first through eighth instructions and utterances (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1907)" ;
    skos:definition "The sequence of announced sights, each given before the stage at which it is to occur: the disposition of the gods, some ascending and some descending; the pipe depending from the disk from which the wind comes; the gods bearing down and then desisting; a thunder-clap that startles; the disk expanding; a host of five-pointed stars filling the air; an infinite encircling with doors of fire fast closed; the doors thrown open; a god with locks of flame in a white tunic and scarlet mantle; seven virgins with serpent faces and golden sceptres; seven gods with the faces of black bulls; and a descending god holding the golden shoulder of the calf."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as one node and kept apart from anything that could be tested, for the reason SPEC §15 gives: these are signs of success, they are told to the operator in advance, and there is no base rate for a sight nobody measures without the telling. A corpus this rich in announced sights and this empty of stated failures is itself the finding."@en,
        "The second half of that finding is withdrawn. This node said until 2026-09-09 that the rite carries no condition of failure at all, and that was a statement about the translation that had been read, not about the text: Mead breaks off with the liturgy, and the forfeiture clause stands in the magical appendix that follows it in the papyrus. What holds is the imbalance, and it is large — a dozen announced sights against one clause. What does not hold is the zero. See pmc:PgmIvTerInAnno and prima-materia#643."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PgmIvSilentiumAvertit a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Schweigenswort wendet die Götter ab"@de,
        "The word of silence turns the gods aside"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.475-829, first utterance (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1907)" ;
    skos:definition "The exchange at the first threshold: the operator sees the gods gazing intently at him and bearing down upon him, lays his right finger on his lips and says Silence three times, calling silence the symbol of the living god beyond decay and asking it to protect him, then hisses long and puffs; whereupon the gods are said to gaze benignly and to bear down no longer, going about their proper order of doings."@en ;
    skos:note "A formula with an announced effect on a hostile power, and the effect is not passage but the withdrawal of pressure. The gods are not opened, moved or persuaded: they return to their own business. Beside the ophite gates, where a speech obtains a passage, this is a smaller and more precise claim."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PgmIvSpiritusYieldsSublatio a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der dreifach eingezogene Atem hebt den Handelnden empor"@de,
        "The threefold indrawn breath raises the operator"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.475-829, first instruction (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1907)" ;
    skos:note "The pairing is as tight as this graph holds anywhere: a named bodily act, a stated count, and an outcome that arrives in the same hour. What it still lacks is the other half — the text nowhere says what the operator sees or fails to see if it does not work."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:PgmIvSpiritusTractus ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:PgmIvSublatio .

pmc:PgmIvSublatioIsDisplaced a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Erhebung ist ein verlagerter Ort"@de,
        "The raising is a displaced locus"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.475-829, first instruction (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1907)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:displacedLocus ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:PgmIvSublatio ;
    pm:situationGround "The source has the operator see himself lifted and passing upward, so that he seems to be in the midst of the air, and later has him stand among the rays and meet ranks of powers coming out of a depth. The awareness is put somewhere the body is not, which is the criterion for this value. Whether anything of the sort occurs is a different question and not this one."@en .

pmc:PgmIvSublatioIsWithdrawn a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Erhebung liegt bei abgezogener Vertiefung"@de,
        "The raising is placed at withdrawn absorption"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.475-829, first instruction (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1907)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:withdrawnAbsorption ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:PgmIvSublatio ;
    pm:situationGround "The source states the withdrawal before the vision: in that hour the operator hears nothing, neither man nor beast, and sees nothing of the things upon the earth. That is the criterion this coordinate names, and the source supplies it as an instruction about what will be the case and not as a description of a mood. The operator is awake throughout and is acting — drawing breath, speaking, pressing his side — so none of the sleep or dream values applies."@en .

pmc:PgmIvSystasisPraerequisitum a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die drei Tage der Reinheit stehen vor dem Zusammentreten"@de,
        "The three days of purity stand before the constituting"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the constituting of the great god (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 18 lines 15-16)" ;
    skos:note "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity, because the text gives the span and does not say what follows from skipping it. That is the whole of the difference: an operator who came on the second day would find nothing in the wording telling him what he had forfeited."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:PgmIvSystasisMagni ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:PgmIvPurificatioTriduana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PgmIvThymokatochon a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Zornbaendiger, naemlich ein Homervers"@de,
        "A restrainer of anger, being one verse of Homer"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the closing charms (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 21 lines 5-6)" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity claimed for a single hexameter set under the heading restrainer of anger: the line in which the hearer is asked whether he will dare to lift the monstrous spear against Zeus."@en ;
    skos:note "A recipe of the shortest possible kind — one heading, one line, nothing else — and it is a capacity like any other. No dose, no time, no condition, no sign, and no statement of what would show the anger unrestrained."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PgmIvTraditioSubmissa a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Halte ihn fuer wuerdig, dann sprich es ueber seinem Kopf, damit er es nicht hoert"@de,
        "Judge him worthy, then say it over his head so that he cannot hear it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the instruction for magical use (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 16 line 13 to p. 17 line 4)" ;
    skos:definition "The procedure for admitting a second person. The operator is first to judge whether that man is safely worthy, using the method by which he would be judged himself in the immortalization; he then puts the first utterance to him, whose opening is given; and the remainder he speaks as an initiate over the man's head in a toneless voice, so that he may not hear it, anointing his face with the mystery."@en ;
    skos:note "It sits with pmc:PgmIvTraditioClausa, which restricts the handing on to one person by one person, and it is the sharper of the two, because a rule of secrecy can be broken by a copyist and this cannot. A reader of the papyrus has the words; the man who was initiated by its owner did not."@en,
        "The judgement of worthiness is left to the operator with no criterion attached beyond the words safely, as a man, and a back-reference to the way he would be judged himself. Where a criterion is missing the decision falls to whoever holds the text, which is the arrangement this project's own criterion is aimed at."@en,
        "The transmission restricts its own passing on inside the operative text, and here the restriction is put into the performance rather than into a rule about it. The candidate undergoes the rite and does not receive the words: he is anointed, the greater part is recited above him inaudibly, and what he is given is the effect and not the text."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:PgmIvSpiritusTractus ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PgmIvUnctioYieldsVisus a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Salbung des Gesichts gibt jenem das weithin leuchtende Sehen"@de,
        "The anointing of the face gives that person the far-shining sight"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the teaching of the operation (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 18 lines 6-9)" ;
    skos:note "Named act, named material, named recipient, announced effect, and no term and no condition of failure. The pairing is tight at the input end and open at the output end, which is the opposite arrangement from the beetle that dies when it has eaten."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:PgmIvUnctioOculorum ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:PgmIvVisusTelaugos .

pmc:PgmIvUsusRepetitusEtCorrectio a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Vielfach habe ich es gebraucht und mich gewundert, und der Gott hiess mich die Salbe lassen"@de,
        "Having used it often I marvelled, and the god told me to stop using the ointment"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the teaching of the operation (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 19 lines 1-6)" ;
    skos:definition "The author's report of his own practice and of a change in his prescription. He says that having used the operation many times he marvelled exceedingly; and that the god said to him: use the ointment no longer, but throw it into a river, and operate wearing the great mystery of the scarab revived through the twenty-five living birds, and operate once a month at the full moon instead of three times in the year."@en ;
    skos:note "It also shows that somebody was following the rule. A frequency is not altered in a text nobody worked from, and the alteration is made in the first person by the writer of the handbook and not by a later hand set apart in the manuscript."@en,
        "The shape of the correction is what makes it worth a node of its own rather than a remark at the clause it changes. A revision attributed to the revealing power cannot be wrong in the way a revision attributed to experience can: nothing the author saw is offered as the reason, no fault of the discarded ointment is named, and the new frequency is not derived from anything. So a text that does record repeated use records it without letting the use bear on the rule. The clause the correction overrides stands at pmc:PgmIvTerInAnno."@en,
        "Two claims stand here and they are of different kinds. That the author used the operation repeatedly is a report of his own practice, which is the scarcest thing in a handbook of this sort and is what separates a copied recipe from a working one. That the prescription was then corrected is a revision of the rule, and the authority given for it is the same power the rule came from."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PgmIvVersusHomerici a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Homerverse als Zauber gebraucht, einer davon ohne genannten Zweck"@de,
        "Verses of Homer used as charms, one of them under no stated purpose"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the amulets and the closing charms (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 20 lines 9-12 and p. 21 lines 3-4)" ;
    skos:definition "The practice the appendix closes with: single hexameters of the Iliad are set down as operative formulae, each under a heading naming what it is for. Four stand with the amulets; one stands under the heading common, which names no effect; two more carry effects and are entered separately. The editor notes that the same verses stand in the papyrus immediately before the immortalization as well as after it, so that the rite is enclosed by them on both sides."@en ;
    skos:note "That verses of an epic are used at all is the finding beside the voces magicae. Where the barbarous names assert of themselves that they have never been uttered by a human tongue, these lines were the most widely known poetry in the language, and the same appendix puts them to work in the same way."@en,
        "The heading common is entered as it stands and not filled in. A heading that names no effect is not a gap in the reading: it says that the transmission had a use for a verse that it did not need to specify, and a reader who supplied a purpose for it would be supplying the only thing that could have been tested."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PgmIvVocesSepties a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Vokalkette, siebenmal durchgesprochen"@de,
        "The vowel chain, uttered through seven times"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.475-829, third utterance (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1907)" ;
    skos:definition "The one chain of vowels the translation prints in full, invoked as the deathless names innate with life which have not yet descended to mortal nature nor been made articulate by human tongue, with the instruction to utter them all with fire and spirit once to the end and then begin again, until the seven immortal gods of the cosmos are completed."@en ;
    skos:note "A dose and a stopping rule, which is rare. The chain is not said once but run through until a count of seven is reached, and the count is given by the gods rather than by the operator; the announced consequence is thunder and crashing in the surround, felt in the body at every crash."@en,
        "The claim the words make about themselves is worth separating from their use. They are called names that have not yet come down to mortal nature and have never been made articulate by a human tongue — that is, the text asserts that what it prints cannot be what is meant. Any comparison of these syllables with the chains of the Jeu material or of the Pistis Sophia runs into that before it runs into the transliterations. See prima-materia#607."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PgmIvVocesSineLege a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Vokalgruppen der Sieben folgen keiner auffindbaren Regel"@de,
        "The vowel groups of the seven follow no discoverable rule"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.475-723, the third utterance and the editor's apparatus to it (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wünsch, Leipzig 1910)" ;
    skos:definition "The state of the chain as the edition prints it: the seven vowels recur in groups through the invocation of the seven immortal gods of the cosmos, and the groups are not built by any pattern the editor could establish. He states that he put them to a mathematician and that no law of permutation was found in their arrangement."@en ;
    skos:note "That bears directly on any comparison with the chains of the Books of Jeu and the Pistis Sophia. A comparison of two sequences is worth something if the sequences are generated; if one of them is unstructured, matching it against another matches accidents. See prima-materia#607."@en,
        "The contrast is what makes this worth a node. Where a comparable arrangement is inscribed for the seven archangels of the planets in a theatre in Asia Minor, the rule is trivial and visible at a glance: the leading vowel of each row moves to the end of the next. The chain in this papyrus does not do that, and it does not do anything else that has been found. Either it is corrupt, or its rule is not positional, or there is none and the sequence is the point."@en,
        "The recitation carries a manner as well as a count: the whole is to be said with fire and with breath, run through to the end and begun again until the seven are completed. The prescribed non-verbal sounds sit in the same layer — a long hiss and a clucking of the lips — and are reported by the edition as an old stratum of address, attested for lightning in fifth-century Athens and for calling snakes, not as an invention of the magicians."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PingrenJuegu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sieben Tage ohne Nahrung, und die Rechnung hinter der Zahl"@de,
        "Seven days without food, and the reckoning behind the number"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 32 (Ping ren jue gu), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 6" ;
    skos:definition "Asked why a person dies who does not eat for seven days, the chapter answers with a measurement and an arithmetic. It gives the dimensions and capacities of stomach, small intestine, returning intestine and wide intestine, and their combined length and combined capacity. In the healthy person, it says, the stomach is full when the intestines are empty and the intestines full when the stomach is empty, alternately empty and full, so that the qi may go up and down. Of what is held, two dou of grain and one dou five sheng of water stay in the tract; a healthy person voids twice a day, two and a half sheng each time, five sheng in a day; seven days at five sheng is three dou five sheng, and the retained food and water are gone. Therefore, the chapter concludes, a healthy person who does not eat or drink for seven days dies, because the essence-qi of food and water, the humours and the fluids are all exhausted."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because of the derivation and not the number. The seven days are not reported from a case; they are computed from a stated capacity and a stated daily rate, and both figures are given, so the claim can be attacked at either of them without touching the conclusion. Very little in this graph exposes its own arithmetic that way."@en,
        "It is also the one claim of this corpus that is about any human being rather than about a patient, an operator or a practitioner, and therefore the one on which a protocol would not have to construct its own population."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmc:PistisSophiaAngeliTransgressi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Zaubereien wurden von den übertretenden Engeln herabgebracht"@de,
        "The sorceries were brought down by the angels who transgressed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pistis Sophia 15 and 18 (trans. C. Schmidt, GCS 13, Leipzig 1905)" ;
    skos:definition "The ascription of the invocations used by men to the angels who transgressed, who are said to have brought them down and taught them for the completion of lawless deeds in the mystery of their sorcery."@en ;
    skos:note "The same derivation the enochic material makes for the taught arts, in a work of another century and another circle, and worth holding apart from it rather than merging: here it is stated in passing, to explain what the taking of the third was aimed at, and the arts are not enumerated."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PistisSophia .

pmc:PistisSophiaAnimaeExFaece a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Seelen, aus dem Übriggebliebenen gemacht"@de,
        "Souls made from what is left over"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pistis Sophia 25 (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1921)" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that while the light is purified and carried into the treasury, the servitors of the rulers gather all the matter from them, and the servitors of fate and of the sphere below the aeons take it and fashion it into souls of men and of cattle, reptiles, wild beasts and birds, and send them down into the world of mankind; the material refuse is fashioned according to the circle of the rulers of that sphere and the configurations of its revolution."@en ;
    skos:note "Held because of what it does to the rest of this graph's ascent material. If a human soul is what remains after the light has been taken out, then the ascent is not a return of something that fell but a recovery by something that was made from the residue. The hermetic descent, where the seven give shares out of love, and this account of the same interval cannot both be right, and both are held."@en,
        "The fashioning is said to follow the configurations of the revolving sphere. That makes astrology a statement about the composition of the residue rather than about influence, and it stands beside the claim two chapters earlier that the casters of nativities can no longer announce anything — the same work removes the practice and keeps the mechanism."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PistisSophia .

pmc:PistisSophiaDuaeVires a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Eine Kraft anstelle einer Seele"@de,
        "A power in place of a soul"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pistis Sophia 7-8 (trans. C. Schmidt, GCS 13, Leipzig 1905)" ;
    skos:definition "The account of what was put into the bodies of John and of the disciples: into Elizabeth a power taken from the little Jao of the midst, and in place of the soul the soul of the prophet Elijah, brought by the light-virgin and her receivers and cast into her womb; into the disciples the twelve powers of the twelve saviours of the treasury, in place of souls of the rulers, so that they were born without souls of the rulers in them."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim is that what the disciples have instead of a soul of the archons is a power from the treasury, and that this is why they are not of the world. It is the same reading the Books of Jeu give at their own second chapter — the ruler finds nothing of his own in the one he faces — arrived at from the other end, by construction rather than by knowing."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PistisSophia .

pmc:PistisSophiaFormulaQuinqueVerborum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Mysterium der fünf Worte, mit der Auflösung, die der Text selbst gibt"@de,
        "The mystery of five words, with the interpretation the text gives it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pistis Sophia 10 (trans. C. Schmidt, GCS 13, Leipzig 1905)" ;
    skos:definition "The formula found written on the light-garment in five words of those of the height, which the text then resolves at length: an address to the mystery that is outside in the world, for whose sake the All came into being, that is the whole going-out and the whole coming-up, which emanated all emanations and everything in them; it is told that the speakers are its fellow-members, that they and it are one and the same, and that it is the first mystery which existed in the ineffable before it came forth, and whose name they all are."@en ;
    skos:note "The division of the five words is not certain. Schmidt prints them as five and notes in the apparatus that another division is possible, so any count of syllables built on this passage rests on an editor's spacing. See prima-materia#607."@en,
        "The one formula in this graph's ascent material that arrives with its own interpretation attached. It is not an address to a gatekeeper: the words are found written on the garment, and what they say is that the addressee and the addressers are the same thing under different names. Whatever the syllables are, the text does not treat them as an unintelligible password."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PistisSophia .

pmc:PistisSophiaIeuVincula a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Herrscher waren von Anfang an vom Aufseher des Lichts gebunden"@de,
        "The rulers were bound from the beginning by the overseer of the light"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pistis Sophia 25 (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1921)" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that before the proclamation was made to them, all the rulers of the aeons, of fate and of the sphere were already bound in their bonds, their spheres and their seals, as the overseer of the light had bound them from the beginning; that each remained in his own order; and that each travelled his course as the overseer had established it."@en ;
    skos:note "It also settles what the seals are here, and it is not what the later handbooks mean by the word. In this passage a seal is part of what binds the ruler, not an instrument the practitioner carries; the seals of traditions/books-of-jeu.ttl are held by the one who passes. Two uses of one term in one circle of writings, and they point in opposite directions."@en,
        "The consequence is the one this material is usually read against. The rulers are not autonomous adversaries whom an ascending soul must outwit: they are held in an arrangement made by someone else, and their courses were set for them. A formula addressed to such a ruler is not a negotiation with a power but an appeal to the order that constituted him — and the same work says two chapters earlier that a third of that power was taken away, which is an act inside the same arrangement rather than a victory over it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PistisSophia .

pmc:PistisSophiaLuxIntolerabilis a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Welt könnte das Licht nicht ertragen, wie es ist"@de,
        "The world could not bear the light as it is"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pistis Sophia 14 (trans. C. Schmidt, GCS 13, Leipzig 1905)" ;
    skos:definition "The statement that the light in the twelve aeons was many times greater than the light that had been on him in the world, and that the world would not be able to bear the light as it is in its truth, since otherwise the world and everything on it would be destroyed together."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a caution and not as cosmology because of what it is used for: it explains why the manifestation was reduced, and it makes the reduction a mercy rather than a limit. A transmission that says its own highest state is unbearable below has stated a condition on its own transmissibility."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:PistisSophiaVestisNominum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PistisSophia .

pmc:PistisSophiaMagiaFrustrata a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der genannte Zweck: dass die Anrufungen nicht mehr wirken"@de,
        "The stated purpose: that the invocations should stop working"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pistis Sophia 15 and 18 (trans. C. Schmidt, GCS 13, Leipzig 1905)" ;
    skos:definition "The reason the text gives for taking the third: so that when the men who are in the world call upon the rulers in their mysteries — those which the angels who transgressed brought down, that is their sorceries — they should no longer be able to complete them; and, as Mary restates it, so that from that hour the casters of nativities and the questioners and those who announce to men what is to happen should no longer be able to announce anything."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the closest thing to a falsifying condition in the whole of this graph's ascent material, and it is unusual in a second way: it is a claim about somebody else's practice failing. A transmission says here that from a stated hour, invocation and astrology stopped delivering, and it says which practices it means."@en,
        "What keeps it from being examinable is not vagueness but reach. No sign is given by which a practitioner would notice the difference, and the change is dated to an event no observer could time. It is entered as a claim and not as a protocol; what a protocol would need is a rate before and after, and the text offers no before."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PistisSophia .

pmc:PistisSophiaMelchisedecPurgatio a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Licht wird den Herrschern zu einer bestimmten Zahl entzogen"@de,
        "The light is carried away from the rulers at an appointed number"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pistis Sophia 25 (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1921)" ;
    skos:definition "The account of the extraction: when the time of the number of Melchisedec, the great receiver of the light, came, he would come into the midst of the aeons and of all the rulers bound in the sphere and in fate, and carry away the purification of the light from them; he set in motion the hastener who is over them and made them turn their circles swiftly, and the hastener carried away the power that was in them, and the breath of their mouth, and the tears of their eyes, and the sweat of their bodies."@en ;
    skos:note "The procedure is stated as recurring and as timed by a number, not as a single event, and the mechanism is acceleration: the circles are made to turn faster and the powers come off. Whatever else it is, it is described as an extraction with a schedule, which is rarer in this corpus than the extraction itself."@en,
        "The receivers of the sun and of the moon are given the same office at a lower level: when they look up and see the configurations of the paths of the aeons, of fate and of the sphere, they take the light-power from them, make it ready and hold it until they hand it to the receivers of Melchisedec. The passage therefore has the light travelling upward through two named stations, and it is the closest thing in this witness to a machine."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PistisSophia .

pmc:PistisSophiaMixtura a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Anteil in der Mischung"@de,
        "The portion in the mixture"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pistis Sophia 8 (trans. C. Schmidt, GCS 13, Leipzig 1905)" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that the last helper blew a portion of power into the mixture, that this power is mixed with all the invisibles, all the rulers and all the aeons, and that every human being holds a portion of it; the chain is given step by step, from the first commandment through the great light and the five helpers into the mixture, which is called the world of corruption."@en ;
    skos:note "A doctrine of a portion present in everyone, stated as a transmission through named stages rather than as a general truth. It is the counterpart of the previous node: the disciples are said to have something more, and this says what everyone has."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PistisSophia .

pmc:PistisSophiaTertiaPars a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Drittel der Kraft von den Herrschern genommen"@de,
        "A third of the power taken from the rulers"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pistis Sophia 15-16 and 18 (trans. C. Schmidt, GCS 13, Leipzig 1905)" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that when the tyrants of all the aeons made war on the light they were all made powerless and fell down like the dead, and that a third of the power of each was taken, and that fate and the sphere over which they rule were turned, so that they spend six months turned to the left and complete their influences and six months looking to the right and complete theirs."@en ;
    skos:note "The chapter reference given for this in the received reports is chapter 20. In the edition read it stands at chapters 15 and 16 and is restated by Mary at chapter 18. Anyone citing chapter 20 for it from a summary is citing a place the passage is not."@en,
        "The turning of fate is stated twice with a correction attached: the same passage says that Jeu, the overseer of the light, had originally set the spheres to look left at all times, so what is described is the reversal of an arrangement and not the invention of one."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PistisSophia .

pmc:PolarityHarvestThresholds a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "Die zwei Schwellen der Polarität und der Zusammenbruch der negativen"@de,
        "The two thresholds of polarity and the collapse of the negative one"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1981/1984" ;
    dcterms:source "The Law of One (The Ra Material), session 36.12 (L/L Research, Louisville 1984)",
        "The Law of One (The Ra Material), session 78.25 (L/L Research, Louisville 1984)",
        "The Law of One (The Ra Material), session 85.11 (L/L Research, Louisville 1984)",
        "The Law of One (The Ra Material), sessions 17.32 and 17.33 (L/L Research, Louisville 1984)" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that a being becomes harvestable on one of two opposed paths at unequal thresholds — a slight majority of service to others on the one, an approach to totality of service to self on the other, with a region of indifference between them — and that the negative path is nevertheless abandoned at the density of unity, because it rests on an omission and what is not cannot endure."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as an origination rather than as the teaching of a tradition. The material was produced between 1981 and 1984 by three named people in a stated procedure, and it presents itself as received rather than composed. That presentation is what pm:circulatesAs records; the node makes no claim about the source of the material, only about where the text begins."@en,
        "One caution for anyone opening the passages. The published wording of the omission argument names the wrong polarity at its first occurrence, and the correction is made in the exchange itself; a reader who quotes the sentence as printed quotes a slip. And the passage about abandoning the negative polarity in the early sixth density stands at 36.12, not at 78.25 as an earlier note in prima-materia#62 had it. 78.25 carries the different statement that the two paths must take in each other."@en,
        "The two halves of the claim do not sit easily together, and the record keeps them apart rather than choosing. Read from the thresholds alone the system is a dualism with two valid outcomes; read from the argument about the omission it is a monism in which one outcome is provisional. Whoever cites only the thresholds reports a symmetry the same text withdraws. See prima-materia#69."@en ;
    pm:circulatesAs "A cosmology of great antiquity, received from a source outside the authors, rather than a text composed between 1981 and 1984." ;
    pm:originatedBy "Carla Rueckert as instrument, with Don Elkins as questioner and Jim McCarty as scribe (L/L Research)" .

pmc:PotencyPresupposesTheSongsThatBelongToIt a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Gebieten über eine Potenz setzt ihre Lieder voraus"@de,
        "Commanding a potency presupposes its songs"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Frances Densmore, Teton Sioux Music (Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 61, Washington, 1918), p. 211",
        "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 158 and pp. 158-159" ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity: two informants in the same volume make obedience conditional on the songs and not merely customary, and the second witness reports the same rule attached to a particular stone, whose song was transferred with it and was to be sung whenever a request was made of it. The consequence is stated as a condition on the obedience rather than as a punishment, which is the strongest form this corpus uses."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The chosen potency must do as it is directed by the one who chose it, but the chooser must know the songs that belong to it; a man should know the songs, and if he sings them his potency will do as he wishes."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Sicun ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by named Oglala shamans, chiefly Sword, Tyon, Ringing-shield, Bad-wound and No-flesh, and by Finger on 25 March 1914; recorded by the agency physician J. R. Walker at Pine Ridge over his years of service there, closed December 1916; given in Lakota, part of it written out in Lakota by Sword and Tyon and translated by Burt Means, Thomas Mills, Bruce Means and Antoin Herman; the paper prints the translations only, so the Lakota wording is not carried." ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:SingingTheSongsOfAPotency ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LakotaRitual .

pmc:PracticePresupposesInitiation a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Praxis setzt die Initiation voraus"@de,
        "The practice presupposes initiation"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Abhinavagupta, Tantrāloka XV",
        "Mālinīvijayottaratantra VIII" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:SaktipataTransmission ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Diksa ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmc:PranayamaPresupposesAsana a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Atemregulierung setzt Festigkeit in der Haltung voraus"@de,
        "Breath regulation presupposes firmness in posture"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.1 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:note "The verse opens the breath chapter by requiring that the practitioner first be established in posture and in a measured diet, and that the breath practice then follow the instruction of a teacher. The condition is in the base text, not in a commentary."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:HathaPranayama ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Asana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:PrayerOfTheHeartIsUnbound a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Das Herzensgebet ist an keinen Zustand gebunden"@de,
        "The prayer of the heart is bound to no state"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Philokalia IV, Gregory of Sinai, On Stillness and the Two Ways of Prayer",
        "Philokalia IV, Nikephoros the Monk, On Watchfulness and the Guarding of the Heart" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:anchoringUnbound ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:PrayerOfTheHeart ;
    pm:situationGround "The prayer is prescribed as continuous, which is the point of it: not a set-apart exercise but something carried through whatever the practitioner is doing. The tradition is not declining the distinction between waking and sleeping; it requires the prayer across it."@en .

pmc:PrayerPresupposesWatchfulness a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Herzensgebet setzt Wachsamkeit und Führung voraus"@de,
        "The prayer of the heart presupposes watchfulness and guidance"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Philokalia I, Hesychios of Sinai, On Watchfulness and Holiness",
        "Philokalia IV, Gregory of Sinai, On Stillness and the Two Ways of Prayer",
        "Philokalia IV, Nikephoros the Monk, On Watchfulness and the Guarding of the Heart" ;
    skos:note "The writers of this corpus set watchfulness and the direction of a spiritual father before the prayer, and describe the way as passing from purification through illumination to perfection. The chapter divisions vary between the editions of the individual texts; the works and their titles are given so that the passages can be found in whichever edition is at hand."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:PrayerOfTheHeart ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Watchfulness ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hesychasm .

pmc:PresqueUnMois a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Beinahe ein Monat, und was dann"@de,
        "Nearly a month, and then what"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. VI, pp. 111-112" ;
    skos:definition "Addressed to the patient and not to the operator. If you do not sleep, one of three things will occur: you will feel no effects, you will experience either relief or one of the encouraging effects described, or you will grow worse. In the first case you will try nearly a month; in the second you will continue with patience so long as your operator is not wearied; in the third, which is said to be very rare, you will renounce the practice after some days and make use of ordinary medicine."@en ;
    skos:note "A term of nearly a month set for the case of no effect at all — and the paragraph immediately following removes the third case, which is the only one of the three that could end the treatment quickly: it requires careful attention before one can say with certainty that the disease is rendered worse, a person might be deceived by appearances and renounce the practice at the moment when it is on the point of doing the most good."@en,
        "The first case is not closed either. The text does not say what is to be done when the month has passed without effect; it says to try nearly a month. A rule that names a term and not an outcome at the term is a rule about how long to persevere and not a rule about when to stop, and this is the transmission's own strongest statement read as such."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:ProcedureJudgedByItsOwnWorking a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Verfahren, nach seinem eigenen Arbeiten beurteilt"@de,
        "The procedure judged by its own working"@en ;
    skos:note "Fourth witness, added on 2026-09-27, and it is the first from a craft rather than from a divination. Zosimos states that there are two grades of fitting tinctures according to whether one works on the raw or on the cooked species, and judges between them on three grounds, all three properties of the procedure: the cooking is free of a great fatigue, it needs a great skill, and it is shorter. Nothing is said about the fitness, the rank or the initiation of whoever works it, and the judgement is attributed to a named earlier authority, the divine Mary, rather than claimed by the writer."@en,
        "The limit of the derivation. Three claims, each a single passage, and in two of the three the comparison is reported by an outside recorder rather than stated in the transmission's own words for its own purposes. That the ground is a property of the procedure is clear in all three; that the transmissions would put it that way if asked is not established, and this node does not claim it."@en,
        "The three come from transmissions between which no route has been proposed. The Zande material holds the rubbing-board inferior to the other oracles because the man who works it can cheat with it and only he may work it, and records that of the poison oracle a manipulation is never even suggested; the ground is that one instrument's outcome is under one party's control and the other's is not. The Andean report says of the fire-readers, and of no other class of specialist in its list, that they were the least esteemed because they always lied; the ground is a record of being wrong. The Lakota material ranks the routes by which a disease is driven out — sweat best and easiest, then breath, then defecation, then urine, with vomiting very hard though some diseases come out no other way — and adds a rule matching the kind of healer to the kind of cause; the ground is the working of the route."@en,
        "What it adds beyond a count is the third ground. The witnesses this statement was built on ground their judgements in the outcome lying in the hand of the operator and in a procedure being repeatedly wrong; here one of the three grounds is the time the procedure takes, which is a property of the procedure that has nothing to do with whether it is right. The statement holds, and the kind of property it covers is wider than the first three witnesses showed."@en,
        "What the counter-search found, and it is the reason for the scope clause. The holding carries at least two graded orders whose ground is not the working of anything: the ācāra ladder, which sets the Veda lowest and ascends through the Vaiṣṇava, the Śaiva, the right hand, the left hand and the Siddhānta to the Kaula, and the fivefold ranking of the primary devotional relations. Both are rankings, both stand inside their transmissions, and neither gives a reason that any outcome could bear on. Without the scope clause they would refute the statement on the first reading; with it they are simply a different kind of judgement, and the difference between the two kinds is itself worth having."@en,
        "What would refute it as it stands: a transmission that compares two of its own procedures for how well they work and grounds the comparison in the persons who work them — that this instrument is less reliable because the men who handle it are of bad descent, or impure, or less well initiated. The holding contains judgements of exactly that form about individuals, and they are worth keeping apart from this. The Yoruba material has a king declare an elder a cheat and a liar and bar him from official consultations, and the Zande material has a witch-doctor called a cheat because his medicines are poor and a liar because he has no witchcraft in him. Both locate the fault in a man; neither is a comparison of procedures, and so neither settles the statement either way."@en,
        "Why this is worth stating at all. The projects that gather this material commonly assume that a transmission explains a failure by something about the person: impurity, wrong descent, a broken taboo, insufficient resolve. That assumption is well supported and this holding is full of instances of it. What these three show is that the same transmissions are capable of the other move as well, and that they make it precisely where they have two procedures to set against each other. A comparison needs a common measure, and the measure they reach for is the procedure and not the man."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:CautionAgainstTheFireReaders,
        pmc:CautionAgainstTheRubbingBoard,
        pmc:TheOrderOfExitsForADisease,
        pmc:ZosimosDuoGradusTincturarum ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission runs more than one procedure of the same kind and states a comparative judgement of how well they work, the ground it gives for the judgement is a property of the procedure — that its outcome lies in the operator's own hand, that it is repeatedly wrong, that one route is easier than another — and not a property of the persons who work it. This holds of judgements about working; it does not hold of rankings by dignity or by metaphysical order, which the same holding carries and which are grounded otherwise."@en .

pmc:ProceedingTooFastIsSaidToInjure a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Schneller als in den gesetzten Graden vorzugehen soll den Körper schädigen"@de,
        "Proceeding faster than the stated degrees is said to injure the body"@en ;
    skos:note "Falsified by a graded bodily technique whose earliest witness attaches no penalty to haste, or attaches one that is social rather than bodily. Also weakened, without being refuted, if the injury named turns out in each case to be unrelated to what the technique treats."@en,
        "The folk charm is the interesting witness precisely because it is the least literary. It is an operator's instruction inside a working text, not a doctrine about the body, and it carries no theory of degrees at all — which makes it the case most likely to break the claim on a closer reading."@en,
        "The third clause is the one that carries the weight and the one that only the first witness states outright: the injury is the disorder the technique treats. Whether the other two say it or only imply it is the first thing an examination should settle, and if they do not, the statement is to be narrowed rather than defended."@en,
        "Three witnesses of very unequal standing, which is why the statement is worded around what they share. The Haṭhayogapradīpikā has the breath mastered by degrees as a lion or an elephant is mastered, says it destroys whoever proceeds otherwise, and adds that the practice performed wrongly generates the very ailments it removes when performed rightly. A Pennsylvania German charmbook prints inside the spoken formula a warning to the operator not to drive the wheel too hard. The Lingshu gives twelve prohibitions on needling as a two-sided rule, with what is said to follow from breaking them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicRadKautel,
        pmc:ForcedBreathCaution,
        pmc:ShierjinZhongshi ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Transmissions prescribing a bodily technique in graded steps state that proceeding faster than the grades injures the practitioner bodily, and name the injury as the very disorder the technique is said to remove when carried out at the stated pace."@en .

pmc:ProphesyingPresupposesMedicineEating a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Weissagen setzt das Essen der Medizinen voraus"@de,
        "Prophesying presupposes the eating of medicines"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 96" ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity: the source states a disqualification and not only an order. A novice who has eaten only a few times dances at the seance but does not divine, and the quantity eaten is what the source names as the reason."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "A man who has eaten the medicines only a few times is not qualified to take a prominent part in a seance or to prophesy at it."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:SeeingWitchcraft ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:WitchDoctorMedicineEating ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:ProvisionForTheQuestThatYieldsNothing a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Was geschieht, wenn keine Mitteilung gewährt wird"@de,
        "What is done when no communication is granted"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 68" ;
    skos:definition "The seeker is to wait until he receives the communication or until he is so exhausted that he cannot wait longer without danger of losing his life. One who receives it returns to his tipi singing a song of victory; one to whom it is not granted is to come away from the quest meekly and as privately as possible."@en ;
    skos:note "It also fixes the abort condition on the practitioner and not on the clock: the quest ends at exhaustion, not after a set number of days. The corpus read here gives no number of days for this quest, which is the point at which the popular reports of it differ most sharply from it."@en,
        "The passage is the reason this transmission was opened before others in the registry. A procedure that names an exit for its own non-occurrence, and prescribes a different bearing for it, is stating that the non-occurrence happens and is expected. Nothing else read in this run says as plainly that the promised thing may fail to come."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LakotaRitual .

pmc:PubbayogaVisesa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wer große Vorarbeit hinter sich hat, ist vom ganzen Weg befreit"@de,
        "Those of great past endeavour are exempt from the whole course"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.11 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, p. 436; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "Immediately after the seven thresholds the handbook names a class for whom none of them holds. In Buddhas, in silent Buddhas and in chief disciples, who have vast previous endeavour behind them, the transformation by supernormal power and the other special qualities such as the discriminations come to success with the attainment of sanctity alone, without the course of taming the mind just described."@en ;
    skos:note "The exemption is granted on a ground that the aspirant cannot acquire, cannot verify and cannot fail to lack: what the exempt class has is endeavour in previous existences. Every member of it named by the transmission is dead and is of a rank the transmission holds to be no longer filled. The waiver therefore removes nobody from the seven thresholds who is alive to read the passage."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:PunaEkoHoti a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die vielen werden wieder einer, wenn die gesetzte Zeit abläuft"@de,
        "The many become one again when the set time runs out"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.68 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, p. 452; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "Having become many he becomes one again. He may do it by a second resolve; or, the handbook adds, he becomes one of himself with the lapse of the time he determined beforehand, without doing anything further."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the first of three places in the chapter where a determined period ends the effect by itself. The other two stand at the diving into earth and at the walking on water. In all three the reversion is automatic, nothing is required of the operator to close the operation, and no sign marks the end except the end."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:QiListeningPresupposesEmptying a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Hören mit dem Qi setzt das Leeren des Herzens voraus"@de,
        "Attending with the qi presupposes the emptying of the heart"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhuangzi 4, in James Legge (trans.), The Sacred Books of China: The Texts of Tâoism, Part I, Sacred Books of the East vol. XXXIX (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1891), Book IV, par. 2, pp. 208-210" ;
    skos:note "Reopened in this run and confirmed at the wording, with one correction. The consequence stood here as a paraphrase of the dialogue and is now quotable from the edition named. What the earlier wording added and the passage does not say is that the hearing which stops at the ears and the mind does not reach the qi at all; the text says the pupil is to wait for the third hearing and that the first two are to rest where they are, which is an instruction and not a statement about what fails to arrive."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The pupil sets out three ways of proceeding and is refused each time; the master answers that he has too many plans, that adhering to them may keep him free from transgression but that this will be all the result, that he cannot in this way produce the transformation he desires, and that all it shows is the mind of a teacher. Only when the pupil says he can go no further and asks for the method is he told that the method is the fasting."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Qi ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Zhuangzi ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:EmptyingOfTheHeart ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:QuestionDEpreuve a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Prüfungsfrage"@de,
        "The test question"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Allan Kardec, Le Livre des Médiums, seconde partie, chapitre XXVI, sections 286 and 288, item 3" ;
    skos:altLabel "question d'épreuve"@fr ;
    skos:definition "A question put in order to try the perspicacity of the spirits. The transmission classes it with the useless questions and with those put out of curiosity, and states the outcome: to those the spirits do not reply, and they withdraw. The founder warns against them expressly when he sets out how a question is to be framed."@en ;
    skos:note "The French word épreuve carries both senses in this literature and the transmission uses both: the trial a soul undergoes in an incarnation, which it affirms, and the test question, which it forbids. A translation that renders them alike loses the distinction at the point where it matters most."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Spiritism .

pmc:QuimbyAbsensWhitney a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Aus der Ferne zur verabredeten Stunde behandelt, mit drei vorher gezählten Zeugen"@de,
        "Treated at a distance at an appointed hour, with three witnesses counted in advance"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Horatio W. Dresser (ed.), The Quimby Manuscripts, 2nd ed. (New York: Thomas Y. Crowell, 1921), pp. 105-106" ;
    skos:definition "The editor's account of an absent treatment, told from a witness of his own and from the patient's household. Being in Lancaster, New Hampshire, the healer remarked at the appointed time that he must go to Wayne to visit a patient; he retired to the parlour for an hour, returned, and said he had got the lady up from her bed, that she walked, and that three persons were present in the room who witnessed it. On writing to the husband it was found that he and two friends who had come home with him from meeting were present at that time and saw her walk. The husband's own letter reports that he attended church not expecting her to rise, and found her up and dressed on his return, and that between eleven and twelve she rose, walked across the room, and the next morning took breakfast with the family."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered for the shape and not for the result, and the shape is the rarest in this file: a claim made before the fact, at a named hour, about a discrete event in another place, with a count of the persons who would see it, checkable by a party who had no part in making it. Almost nothing in this holding is stated that way. A run that wants to know what this transmission would look like if it had been examined has this case and no other."@en,
        "It is also the one case in this file bearing on the question of the protocol at examinations/magnetic-action-without-the-subjects-knowledge.ttl, because the household reports that a later absent treatment was given at the husband's request without informing the wife. That is the condition that protocol exists to test, arrived at from a transmission it was not built for."@en,
        "Its weaknesses are as plain as its shape and they are not small. Every element reaches the record through the editor, sixty years later, in a volume written to establish the healer's priority; the count of three persons is reported by him as the healer's word and then reported as confirmed by him; the woman in Lancaster who supplies the independent half is unnamed; and nobody wrote anything down at the time. The correct reading is that the transmission made a claim of a checkable kind and that the check was not conducted, not that the check was passed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:QuimbyManuscripts ;
    pm:readVia "The editor's connecting narrative and newspaper scrap-book in Horatio W. Dresser (ed.), The Quimby Manuscripts, 2nd ed. (New York 1921), pp. 104-106, which prints the husband's letter and adds the editor's own corroborating enquiry" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought .

pmc:QuimbyAbsurditasCurat a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Geheilt durch die Absurdität der Heilung"@de,
        "Cured by the absurdity of the cure"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Horatio W. Dresser (ed.), The Quimby Manuscripts, 2nd ed. (New York: Thomas Y. Crowell, 1921), pp. 34-35" ;
    skos:definition "The operator's first-person account of his own recovery. He had been told his kidneys were partly consumed and his lungs ulcerated and believed it. The subject, asked in the mesmeric state, described the pain, said one kidney was half consumed with a piece three inches long separated from it and hanging by a thread, and then offered to put the piece on so that it would grow; he laid on his hands, said he had united the pieces, and the next day said they had grown together. From that day the operator never felt the pain again. His own explanation: the absurdity of the remedy made him doubt that the kidneys were diseased at all, and the disease vanished by the absurdity of the cure."@en ;
    skos:note "The founding case of the transmission is a cure the transmission itself explains by the worthlessness of the remedy. A parallel case is printed on the following page: the subject told a man that his lungs looked like a honeycomb and his liver was covered with ulcers, prescribed a simple herb tea, and the patient recovered; the attending physician credited the medicine, and the operator concluded that the physician had made the disease and that faith in the boy had changed the patient's mind."@en,
        "What makes it more than an anecdote of scepticism is the direction of the inference. The remedy is inert, the cure happened, and instead of concluding that something else cured the man, the practitioner concludes that the disease was never there. That move keeps the effect and discards the illness, and it is the step on which the whole later doctrine rests: the sequel is not that treatment is useless but that the doctor's opinion is what has to be treated."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:QuimbyManuscripts ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought .

pmc:QuimbyAmiciIncreduli a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Der skeptische Freund, vom Herausgeber als Schwierigkeit der Geheilten benannt"@de,
        "The sceptical friend, named by the editor as the difficulty of the cured"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Horatio W. Dresser (ed.), The Quimby Manuscripts, 2nd ed. (New York: Thomas Y. Crowell, 1921), p. 115" ;
    skos:definition "An editorial footnote to the patients' letters: the chief difficulty encountered by former patients who depended solely on the new Science was in avoiding old fears and other mental associations readily called up when meeting sceptical friends. The letters it annotates bear it out in their own words — one correspondent, whose case was well known, reports that the doctors are sceptical and sometimes angry and that she must maintain her faith against opposition, and that at times she can hardly call herself well."@en ;
    skos:note "It is the editor's sentence and not the founder's, written sixty years later by a man defending the founder's priority, and it is entered at that weight. What makes it worth entering at all is its position: it stands as a footnote under the testimonials, which is exactly where a reader counting cures would arrive at the ones that did not hold."@en,
        "The excluding condition, gone from the operation, reappears at the far end of it: not as a cause of the cure failing but as a cause of the cured relapsing. It is located in a third party who is present after the fact, and no criterion is offered by which a relapse with a sceptical friend nearby could be told from a relapse without one."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:QuimbyExhibitioPublica ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:QuimbyManuscripts ;
    pm:readVia "The editor's connecting narrative in Horatio W. Dresser (ed.), The Quimby Manuscripts, 2nd ed. (New York 1921), which summarises the mesmeric years from the papers, the journal and the notices rather than printing them at the place" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought .

pmc:QuimbyDiagnosisEstOpinio a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die hellsichtige Diagnose war die Meinung eines Anwesenden"@de,
        "The clairvoyant diagnosis was the opinion of somebody in the room"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Horatio W. Dresser (ed.), The Quimby Manuscripts, 2nd ed. (New York: Thomas Y. Crowell, 1921), p. 32" ;
    skos:definition "After a time the operator became convinced that whenever the subject examined a patient, his diagnosis of the case would be identical with what either the patient himself or someone present believed, instead of the subject really looking into the patient and giving the true condition of the organs; that he was reading the opinion of someone rather than stating truth acquired by himself. Having satisfied himself of this, he gave up the subject altogether and began what became mental healing."@en ;
    skos:note "A discriminating observation with a stated criterion — agreement with a belief held in the room — run by the practitioner against the instrument on which his reputation rested, and acted on by abandoning it. The holding carries protocols on distant perception against a control; none of them was devised by the practitioner and none of them ended the practice. This one did both."@en,
        "The editor will not let it stand unqualified and the qualification is kept here rather than trimmed, because a file that reports only the sharp half of its source is reporting badly. He adds that the subject also discerned at other times the actual state of the body, that there is documentary evidence of descriptions of the body after death, and that events at a distance were described when the subject was put in communication with someone in the audience who thought of the place. The conflict between the practitioner's conclusion and his editor's reservation is not resolved in the volume and is not resolved here."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:QuimbyManuscripts ;
    pm:readVia "The editor's connecting narrative in Horatio W. Dresser (ed.), The Quimby Manuscripts, 2nd ed. (New York 1921), which summarises the mesmeric years from the papers, the journal and the notices rather than printing them at the place" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought .

pmc:QuimbyProbaAdAegrum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Er ließ sich die Beschwerden nicht sagen und benannte sie"@de,
        "He would not be told the feelings, and named them"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Horatio W. Dresser (ed.), The Quimby Manuscripts, 2nd ed. (New York: Thomas Y. Crowell, 1921), pp. 301-302" ;
    skos:definition "A patient's account of a first interview, printed among the manuscripts. Asked whether he could cure spinal disease, the healer answered that he never wished a patient to tell him his feelings; asked what the patient should do, he said nothing but listen. He then sat down, took the patient's hand, passed his hand to one vertebra and said there was a very sharp pain in it at that moment, then to the left temple and said there was a very bad pain there affecting the sight of the left eye. The patient confirmed both. Only then did the healer explain his theory."@en ;
    skos:note "A demonstration addressed to a doubter and placed before the treatment rather than inside it. The patient had come after hearing the healer called a spiritualist, a mesmerist and an enemy of religion, and says so; the opening move of the procedure is a statement about the patient's body that the patient alone can confirm or deny, made before he has been allowed to say anything."@en,
        "It is the third such demonstration in this holding and the pattern across the three is now worth naming. The demotic handbook assays its white stone, the Romanus-Büchlein tells the man who will not believe to stab an animal, and here a healer declines to be told and reports the pain. All three put the operation at risk, all three are offered to somebody who does not believe, and none of them is a step the convinced practitioner would have any occasion to perform. That the shape recurs in a New England consulting room in 1860 is what makes it a candidate for a statement rather than a coincidence of two manuscripts; the question is registered at pmc:TheTestOnTheIngredientExamination and is not settled here."@en,
        "What the demonstration is worth as evidence is another matter and the file does not inflate it. The healer is holding the patient's hand and watching his face, the pains named are the commonest sites of complaint in a patient who has come about the spine, and the report reaches print through the patient's own recollection in a volume edited to defend the healer's priority. It is entered for its form and not for its result."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:QuimbyManuscripts ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought .

pmc:QuimbySericumOperantis a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Seide war der Glaube des Operateurs und nicht die Seide"@de,
        "The silk was the operator's belief and not the silk"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Horatio W. Dresser (ed.), The Quimby Manuscripts, 2nd ed. (New York: Thomas Y. Crowell, 1921), pp. 60-61" ;
    skos:definition "The operator's own account of how he found the cause of an effect he had located in a material. He believed that silk would attract the subject, a belief he says he held in common with mankind; so if anyone present had any silk about him, a lady with a silk apron for instance, the subject's hand would be affected by it and would move towards her even if she were behind him. He reports the finding in general terms: his own belief, as distinct from his thought, produced effects in the subject whether he was thinking of it or not."@en ;
    skos:note "An assay of a material that came out empty, reported by the practitioner against his own practice, with the cause relocated from the material to the operator's mind. The first limb of pmc:TheTestOnTheIngredient says that operative handbooks assay their materials; here the assay is not in a handbook but in a memoir, and its result is not that the material is genuine or counterfeit but that no material was involved. That is a third outcome the statement does not provide for."@en,
        "The asymmetry the operator draws and the reason it matters for reading this whole file: he distinguishes what he thought from what he believed, and says the effect followed the belief. A transmission which locates its variable in the operator's belief has made that variable unreachable from outside, and it has done so while sounding as though it had localised the cause more precisely, not less."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:QuimbyManuscripts ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought .

pmc:QuimbyTonitruumNonObstat a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das nicht bemerkte Gewitter: eine Bedingung, die ein für sie blinder Durchgang beseitigte"@de,
        "The storm he did not notice: a condition removed by a run that was blind to it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Horatio W. Dresser (ed.), The Quimby Manuscripts, 2nd ed. (New York: Thomas Y. Crowell, 1921), pp. 30-31, with the list of dropped conditions at p. 50" ;
    skos:definition "While he held that the phenomenon was animal magnetism and had to do with electricity, the operator was never able to get satisfactory results when the conditions were not right as he believed they should be, and during a thunderstorm his trials would prove failures. One evening, after making experiments with excellent results, he found that there had been a severe thunderstorm throughout, and, being so interested in the experiments, he had not noticed it. From that time he could produce as good results during a storm as in fair weather."@en ;
    skos:note "It belongs beside pmc:NoFailedRunCountsAgainstTheOperation rather than against it. That statement is about what a transmission supplies, and this transmission supplies an operator-condition in its mature form all the same. What the case adds is the mechanism by which such a condition can be removed at all, and it turns out to be a narrow one: the condition has to be of a kind the operator can be unaware of while it holds. Steel in the pocket, a sceptic in the chair and the purity of an officiant are not of that kind, and none of the fourteen witnesses of that statement names a condition that could have been tested this way."@en,
        "The one place in this holding where a stated condition of failure was tested by a run in which the operator was blind to the condition, and the transmission then dropped the condition instead of the case. He did not learn that storms are harmless by reasoning about storms; he learned it because he had not known that one was going on. Nobody arranged this and it is not an experiment; it is an accident with the structure of one."@en,
        "Two neighbouring conditions went the same way and are recorded here rather than as nodes of their own, because the volume states them in a list and not at length. Pointing the sharp end of a steel instrument at the subject made him start as if pricked with a pin, while the blunt end left him unmoved; after the revision the operator could make him start by simply pointing a finger. And so long as he believed that steel had to do with his failures he had to keep all steel away from him. What connects the three is that each was a material condition whose falsity became visible only once the operator stopped believing it, which is the awkward part of the case and the reason it is entered as a claim and not as a proof."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:QuimbyManuscripts ;
    pm:readVia "The editor's connecting narrative in Horatio W. Dresser (ed.), The Quimby Manuscripts, 2nd ed. (New York 1921), which summarises the mesmeric years from the papers, the journal and the notices rather than printing them at the place" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought .

pmc:RasaAbhrakaPurvaSvarna a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Glimmer vor Gold, und beides vor der inneren Verflüssigung"@de,
        "Mica before gold, and both before the internal liquefaction"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, swallowing of the essence of mica by mercury, pp. 97-98, and garbhadruti, p. 130" ;
    skos:definition "Mica is first of all to be swallowed by mercury, and then gold, and last of all is to be effected the internal liquefaction; the chemist who does not know this makes a waste of his money. Those chemists who score partial success by making mercury swallow gold and other metals but not mica are as frugal as divers who reach the bottom of the ocean and are satisfied with cowries; nothing but the essence of mica can cut the wings of mercury."@en ;
    skos:note "The consequence named is expense and not injury, which places this prerequisite between the two kinds the volume otherwise uses. It is also the place where the transmission argues for an ingredient by a property — mica alone stops the mercury subliming — so the order is given a reason and not only an authority."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The chemist who does not know this makes a waste of his money, and the mercury, its wings uncut, cannot be killed."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:RasaGarbhadruti ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:RasaGrasana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RasaAmrtapaniDagdhapani a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Der nach seiner Handfläche gewählte Chemiker, und der zu meidende"@de,
        "The chemist chosen by his palm, and the one to be avoided"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. I, p. 16" ;
    skos:definition "For the performance of the operations one should engage a chemist who is pious, truthful, learned, a worshipper of Śiva and Keśava, kind-hearted, and who has on his palm a flag, a vessel, a lotus, a fish, a bow and a line below the ring finger; such a chemist is called nectar-palmed. A chemist who is unfortunate, cruel, greedy, uninitiated and has black spots on his palm is called burnt-palmed and should therefore be avoided in metallurgical operations."@en ;
    skos:note "A disqualification stated before the work and readable on the body, which is a different device from the clauses that assign a failure afterwards: this one can be applied in advance and by a third party. The sentence that follows it goes the other way and is the ordinary immunising form — invariably successful are the operations carried on by those who are pure in body and mind, truthful, believers in God, learned and full of optimism — so the two stand together, one testable and one not. See pmc:RasaSiddhiPurusagunayatta."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:RasaShodhana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RasaApathyaviparyaya a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Das Syndrom, an dem ein Anwender erkennt, dass er die Regeln gebrochen hat"@de,
        "The syndrome by which a user knows he has broken the rules"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, how to cure symptoms due to an improper use of mercury, pp. 206-208" ;
    skos:definition "The violation of the rules relating to the taking of mercury results in a failure to digest the mercury, accompanied by a colic pain on the lower part of the navel, inactivity, drowsiness, lethargy, fever, shortness of eyesight, pains all over the body, aversion to food, and inflammation of the whole body. To remove these symptoms one takes for three days suvarchala salt with cow's urine and the juice of karkati root, or the juice of matulunga with rock salt and dried ginger."@en ;
    skos:note "Ten signs and a three-day course against them, and the signs are stated as the mark of a transgression and not of the medicine. That is the direction the clause runs in and it is worth stating plainly: the same ten signs are listed again on the next page as the signs of indigestion of mercury, so the transmission has one syndrome and two readings of it — the user broke a rule, or the mercury was not absorbed — and in both readings the medicine is exonerated."@en,
        "This is the volume's closest approach to a condition of failure that a user could apply to himself while the course runs, and it is a condition on the user's conduct. Nothing in the volume tells him at what point to conclude that the mercury is not working."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:RasaRasasevana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RasaAstadasaSamskara a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die achtzehn Operationen am Quecksilber, als eine nummerierte Reihe gesetzt"@de,
        "The eighteen operations on mercury, set out as one numbered series"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1926" ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, the list of the eighteen operations, p. 40, with the preface at pp. ix and vii" ;
    skos:definition "Purification, boiling, rubbing, raising, sublimation, confinement, restraint, stimulation, rehabilitation, the swallowing of metals, swooning, movement, internal liquefaction, exhaustion, killing, incineration, dyeing, and the transformation of base metals — eighteen operations numbered from one to eighteen, each treated in the order given and each with its own processes."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as an ordering act and not as a doctrine, because the volume is a compilation and its order is the compiler's. He says so twice: the instructions received from his preceptor enabled him to arrange methodically the materials found in the existing books, which were mostly in a chaotic state; and the present-day physicians of Bengal are ignorant of the eighteen mercurial operations, which can safely be considered the pivot round which the whole of Indian chemistry rotates. The eighteen are older than he is; that they stand here as one consecutive series with the eight indispensable ones marked off inside it is his arrangement and is what this node records."@en,
        "What hangs on the number. The text states twice that a man who performs all eighteen is the greatest of operators, free from physical decay; that mercury of two of the five kinds becomes a panacea if subjected to the eighteen; and separately that eight of them, those from boiling to rehabilitation, are indispensable after purification. Three different counts of the same series — eighteen as the measure of the operator, eighteen as what makes bad mercury usable, eight as what any medicinal mercury must have passed — and the volume does not reconcile them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:compiledBy "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasacharya, principal of the Bengal College of Ayurveda, Calcutta" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RasaAstadosa a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Acht natürliche Fehler des Quecksilbers und die acht Krankheiten, die jeder von ihnen erzeugt"@de,
        "Eight natural faults of mercury and the eight diseases each of them causes"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, natural blemishes of mercury, p. 35" ;
    skos:definition "The natural defects of mercury are the evil properties of lead, of tin, impurity from foreign matter, the evil properties of fire, subtleness, the evil properties of poison, the evil properties of stone, and the inability to stand the heat of fire. These blemishes give rise to the following ailments in one who takes impure mercury, respectively: carbuncles, leprosy, swoon, inflammation, loss of vigour, death, inertness, and eruptions."@en ;
    skos:note "Set beside the corresponding search in examinations/searches-rasashastra.ttl. The volume states the pairing and reports no case of any of the eight, which is why the mapping is held here as a caution and not as an examination."@en,
        "The word respectively is the whole of the finding. This is not a list of things that may go wrong but a mapping of eight causes onto eight effects in a fixed order, and each fault has its own removal by its own drug in the first operation. A transmission that pairs its faults with its diseases one to one has made a claim of a kind that could be checked against a case: a man poisoned by this material should show the condition his particular fault is named for."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:RasaShodhana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RasaAstasamskaraPurvakatva a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die acht Operationen nach der Reinigung, bevor Quecksilber überhaupt gebraucht wird"@de,
        "The eight operations after purification, before mercury is used at all"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, the eighteen operations, p. 40" ;
    skos:definition "The eight operations commencing with boiling should be resorted to as soon as the process of purification is finished; the use of mercury, though purified, is not commendable unless it is subjected to these eight kinds of operations. One exception is stated in the same paragraph, in favour of mercury extracted in the prescribed manner from cinnabar, which is applicable for all medicinal purposes without them."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity. The text says not commendable and states no harm at this place; the harms it does state stand at the blemishes and at the classes of solidification, and they are not read back into this sentence. The exception is the more interesting half: a route that skips eight of the eighteen operations stands in the same paragraph as the rule, which means the transmission holds both that the series is indispensable and that one starting material makes it dispensable."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:RasaGrasana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:RasaShodhana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RasaAtivijnana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Überlieferung erklärt, ihr Wissen stamme nicht aus Beobachtung und lasse sich nicht vermehren"@de,
        "The transmission states that its knowledge does not come from observation and cannot be added to"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, preface, pp. ix-x, with p. xiv" ;
    skos:definition "It is not a science in the ordinary sense of the term. It is not a collection of experiential truths or inductive generalizations, based on observation and experiment. It is a super-science, and as such is based on something higher than observation and experiment — call it inspiration, revelation, or what you will. It is not possible, even for a very careful and devoted student, to explain in many cases why a certain line of procedure was prescribed with a view to attain an end in view. Neither is it possible for us to make any material addition to what has been transmitted to us from time out of memory."@en ;
    skos:note "A transmission saying in its own words what this holding would otherwise have to infer about it, and saying both halves: the claims did not come from trial, and nothing may be added to them. The second half matters more for the examining layer than the first. A transmission that forbids addition has declared in advance that no later station will attach a term, a caution or a condition of failure to what it received, which is exactly the material a falsifier is made of; and the same volume disproves it in its own footnotes, where the compiler cuts an old dose to a quarter and adds a contraindication against a modern drug."@en,
        "The compiler sets his own central claim against the same sentence, and the two do not fit. He calls the science independent of observation and experiment, and then says of the swallowing of gold without gain of weight that the statement is based on a practical experience and an arduous research carried on for several years, and asks his readers to perform the operations faithfully and form their own judgement. That is an appeal to trial made three pages after trial has been declared beside the point."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RasaAyogyabandha a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Mit Blei, Zinn, Gift, Harn oder Samen verfestigtes Quecksilber ist nicht zu geben"@de,
        "Mercury solidified with lead, tin, poison, urine or semen is not to be given"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, pp. 196 and 206-207" ;
    skos:definition "Mercury solidified with lead, tin, poison, semi-poison, urine or semen is to be avoided for medicinal purposes; and a man who takes by mistake mercury containing lead or tin should take rock salt with cow's urine and the tuber of karabella, or a decoction of karkoti, guduchi, myrobalan and sharapunkha boiled in cow's urine and reduced to an eighth."@en ;
    skos:note "The prohibition and its antidote stand a page apart, and the antidote is what makes the caution more than a rule: the transmission expects the mistake to happen, names the wrong material by name, and states a three-day course against it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:RasaRasasevana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RasaBalukaAgniPariksa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Grasprobe für die Hitze des Sandapparats"@de,
        "The grass test for the heat of the sand apparatus"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. VI, baluka jantram, p. 259" ;
    skos:definition "In the baluka jantram the heating is to be stopped when it is found that the external surface of the earthen basin at the top of the apparatus has got so heated as to reduce to ashes a piece of dried grass placed upon it."@en ;
    skos:note "An endpoint stated as an observation and not as a duration, in a volume that otherwise counts everything in days and watches. A later run that wants to know whether these procedures could be repeated by a stranger should start here: this is the only place where a heat is specified by something the operator can see happen."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RasaBandhaKaladana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Heilung binnen einer Woche und binnen drei Wochen, nach der Menge des verschluckten Glimmers"@de,
        "Cure within a week and within three weeks, by the count of mica swallowed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. VII, pp. 316-317" ;
    skos:definition "Mercury exhausted with an equal quantity of mica and then reduced to ashes is bala baddha and can cure and prevent all sorts of diseases. Exhausted with double its quantity of mica it is kumara baddha and can cure in three weeks all the diseases due to serious vices, leprosy among them. Having swallowed four times its weight of mica it is taruna baddha, the best of all rasayanas, and can cure in a week all sorts of diseases. Having swallowed six times its weight it is briddha baddha and is used in curing and in the incineration of metals."@en ;
    skos:note "Four grades, a counted ingredient, and two stated terms — a week and three weeks — attached to named conditions. These are the only cure-times in the volume and they are short enough to be watched. A course of taruna baddha in a patient with a condition the transmission names, followed for eight days, is the cheapest examination anywhere in this file; that is why the terms are entered as their own node and not left inside the class list."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RasaBandhabheda a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Fünfundzwanzig Klassen verfestigten Quecksilbers, unterschieden nach Aussehen und Wirkung"@de,
        "Twenty-five classes of solidified mercury, told apart by what they look like and what they do"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. VII, different classes of solidification of mercury, pp. 311-314" ;
    skos:definition "The commentators are said to describe twenty-five ways of solidifying mercury, and the volume names them and distinguishes them by how the material behaves. Solidification of mercury not properly purified is hatha bandha and gives rise to death or a very serious disease; of properly purified mercury, arota bandha, commendable for internal use and slow to cure. Mercury that will not solidify but sublimates and returns to its original condition when put to the putam is a third class. Mercury solidified with materials not properly purified is kriyahina, and taking it with improper diets gives rise to several after-effects of a troublesome nature. Mercury that sublimates out of the crucible during incineration is sajiva baddha and does not possess the properties of incinerated mercury."@en ;
    skos:note "A taxonomy of outcomes in which four of the first classes are failures, each with an observable and a consequence. This is the volume's nearest thing to a diagnostic table for its own work, and the criteria are all of the same kind: what the material did in the fire."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RasaBhasmalaksana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die fünf Merkmale veraschten Quecksilbers"@de,
        "The five marks of incinerated mercury"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, characteristics of incinerated mercury, p. 175, with sajiva baddha at ch. VII, p. 314" ;
    skos:definition "Incinerated mercury is devoid of lustre; it is light, white, a killer of metals, devoid of subtleness, and it does not sublimate if placed upon fire."@en ;
    skos:note "An assay of the transmission's own operation and not of a material it bought, and that is its interest. The last mark is a test anybody could carry out in a minute — put it on the fire and see whether it rises — and the sentence attaches to the negative outcome neither a repetition rule nor a cause outside the procedure. The volume names the failing case separately and calls it sajiva baddha, where mercury sublimates out of the crucible during the incineration and is declared not to possess the properties of incinerated mercury and not to be efficacious."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RasaDirghayus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Als Zahlen angegebene Lebensspannen"@de,
        "Lifespans stated as figures"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, dose of incinerated mercury, p. 196" ;
    skos:definition "One who takes one pala of incinerated mercury exhausted with steel or copper lives a lakh of years; one who takes ten palas lives for a kalpa; one who takes one pala of incinerated mercury exhausted with gold lives for a crore of years. The dose from which these follow is stated separately and is one rati a day."@en ;
    skos:note "The figures are of a size that removes them from any test, and that is itself the finding: the same volume states its cure-times in weeks and its antidote courses in days, so it is perfectly capable of a checkable number and does not use one where the central promise stands. A lakh of years and a crore of years are not two claims of different strength; they are the same claim made twice."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RasaGandhakaPurvakatva a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Schwefel wird zuerst verschluckt, sonst entsteht kein Appetit"@de,
        "Sulphur is swallowed first, or no appetite is roused"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, order of swallowing of metals by mercury, p. 93" ;
    skos:definition "Metals are not to be swallowed by mercury unless sulphur is first of all made to be swallowed; otherwise no appetite is roused in mercury. Sulphur is therefore to be swallowed first of all, for the purpose of increasing the appetite of mercury."@en ;
    skos:note "A necessity with its consequence in the same sentence, and the consequence is stated in the material and not in the operator: the mercury will not take the metal. That makes it the one prerequisite in the volume whose failure would be visible at the bench within the operation itself."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Otherwise no appetite is roused in mercury, and the metals are not swallowed."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:RasaGrasana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:RasaDipana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RasaGandhakamatraKrama a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sechs Stufen verschluckten Schwefels, sechs Anzeigen"@de,
        "Six grades of swallowed sulphur, six indications"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, grasanam, pp. 76-77" ;
    skos:definition "The potency of pure mercury increases a hundred times if it consumes an equal quantity of pure sulphur; it cures all sorts of leprosy at twice its weight of sulphur, all sorts of inertness at three times, the premature falling of teeth and greyness of hair at four times, consumption at five, and all sorts of diseases at six. All this is said to have been spoken by Mahādeva to Indra. The same section grades the other swallowed materials: a hundredfold for an equal quantity of mica, a thousand attributes for gold, and for diamond it is said that Śiva alone knows."@en ;
    skos:note "A dose-response table in which the variable is counted and the outcomes are named conditions. It is the form of a claim that a series of cases could contradict, and the transmission gives it for its preparatory operation rather than for its promise: the six grades govern what the mercury is capable of, not how long the patient lives."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RasaGrasakramalaksana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Vier Bissen, vier Erscheinungen"@de,
        "Four morsels, four appearances"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, quantity of the morsels, p. 140" ;
    skos:definition "The four morsels swallowed in the exhaustion are a sixteenth, an eighth, a quarter and a half of the mercury, and each is given its own look: the first makes the mercury look very beautiful, the second gives it the appearance of a leech, the third makes it appear like a crow's stool, and the fourth makes it look like butter milk."@en ;
    skos:note "A counted series in which each step has a stated appearance, so that an operator can say at a glance how far he has come. This is the transmission stating a progress mark for its own procedure, which it does here and does not do anywhere for the course a patient takes."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RasaGrasanaParyaptiPariksa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Gewichtsprobe: das Quecksilber muss wiegen, was es wog"@de,
        "The weight test: the mercury must weigh what it weighed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, pp. 86 and 134, with the preface, p. xiv" ;
    skos:definition "If iron, mica and the rest with which mercury was rubbed are properly swallowed, the mercury retains its original quantity and can stand the heat of fire; in case they are not properly swallowed, the mercury is to be sublimated with sour asava and then rubbed and boiled again as before. In the fifth process of swallowing the same rule is given as an instruction: if the weight of the mercury is greater than what it was originally, it is again to be boiled until it reverts to its original weight."@en ;
    skos:note "That invitation is what makes this the most examinable claim in the holding's Indian material: an outcome stated in a measurable quantity, a procedure printed in full, and an author who asks to be tested on it. The rule of repetition attached to the failing case — boil it again until the weight comes back — is the clause that would have to be counted against it, and it is a rule about the material rather than an excuse for the operator."@en,
        "The one test in the volume that is carried out with a balance, and the claim behind it is the compiler's central one, stated in the preface in his own person: mercury can be made to swallow a considerable quantity of gold or other metals without any appreciable increase in the weight of the swallowing mercury. He says he expects the statement to appear dogmatic, paradoxical and revolting against the common sense of rational beings, that it rests on practical experience and years of research, and he invites the reader to perform the operations faithfully and then form his own judgement."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RasaJaranaPurvaMarana a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Quecksilber wird erschöpft, bevor es getötet wird"@de,
        "Mercury is exhausted before it is killed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, before the exhaustion of mercury, pp. 131-132" ;
    skos:definition "The man who kills mercury without exhausting it, and without making it swallow a sufficient quantity of gold, mica and the rest, is a great sinner and is to be taken for an enemy of the great God; hence mercury is to be exhausted very carefully before it is killed."@en ;
    skos:note "The consequence is stated and it is religious and not material: what the offender incurs is sin and enmity with the god, not a failed preparation. It is entered at stated necessity because a consequence is quoted, and the kind of consequence is recorded here so that nobody reads it as a claim about the substance. The volume is able to state a material consequence when it has one, as it does at the swallowing of sulphur."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "He is a great sinner and is to be taken for an enemy of the great God."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:RasaMarana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:RasaJarana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RasaJaranaVarjya a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Stoffe, die bei der Erschöpfung nicht gebraucht werden dürfen, und der Schaden, den sie anrichten"@de,
        "Substances that must not be used in the exhaustion, and the harm they do"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, first process of exhaustion of mercury, p. 134" ;
    skos:definition "Iron rust, aconite, urine, rasaka, realgar, orpiment and sulphur are not to be made use of in the exhaustion of mercury, since mercury exhausted with these things gives rise to leprosy, boils and the like."@en ;
    skos:note "A prohibition on named materials with a named consequence in the user's body, standing inside the procedure that prescribes several of the same materials elsewhere: sulphur is swallowed six times over in the tenth operation and forbidden in the fourteenth. The transmission is therefore claiming a difference between a substance taken up at one stage and the same substance at another, and it states no sign by which the resulting mercury could be told apart."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:RasaJarana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RasaKamamada a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die eine Schwierigkeit, die die Überlieferung einräumt, und ihre Frist ist drei Wochen"@de,
        "The one difficulty the transmission admits, and its term is three weeks"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, special directions for use of mercury, pp. 196-197" ;
    skos:definition "There is only one difficulty arising out of taking incinerated mercury, namely that the man gets intoxicated, in three weeks, with sexual desires. He gets indigestion if he avoids all connection with women while taking it; a sexual intercourse on the other hand leads to the waste of semen and proves very dangerous; it is therefore desirable to dally with women without having any intercourse. A yogi who observes absolute celibacy is said to be helped by the same medicine towards samadhi."@en ;
    skos:note "The only adverse effect in the volume that carries a term. Three weeks is a checkable number and the effect is one the user would notice, which makes this the volume's one falsifiable statement about what its own medicine does to a body. The sentence immediately after it closes the exit on both sides — abstinence brings indigestion, intercourse is dangerous — and prescribes a state between them that nobody could be shown to have kept."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:RasaRasasevana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RasaKarpuraDusta a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Zwei benannte Präparate, falsch hergestellt, und die Krankheiten, die sie geben"@de,
        "Two named preparations, wrongly made, and the diseases they give"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, pp. 209-210" ;
    skos:definition "Rasakarpuram or white mercury, if not prepared in the prescribed manner, gives rise to leprosy, gout and excess of phlegm, and is therefore to be prepared and taken very carefully; the ailments from it are removed by the watery portion of the stool of a she-buffalo taken with water, or by dhanya with sugar candy. Rasasindura prepared out of impure mercury gives rise to all the evil effects of unpurified mercury, and these are remedied by clarified butter with powdered black pepper for seven days."@en ;
    skos:note "Named product, named fault in the making, named disease, named antidote, and in the second case a term of seven days. This is the transmission at its most exact about its own harms, and it is worth putting beside the lifespans of a lakh and a crore of years in the same chapter: the promise is unbounded and the damage is dated."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:RasaKarpuraRasa ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RasaKarpuraRasaPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Karpura-rasa soll Syphilis mit Komplikationen heilen und die Kraft für hundert Frauen geben"@de,
        "Karpura-rasa is claimed to cure syphilis with complications and to give the power of a hundred women"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, pp. 109-110" ;
    skos:note "Taken with cloves, sandal, musk and saffron it brings about a quick cure of syphilis with complications, increases appetite and semen, imparts nutrition and strength; one who takes the medicine at regular intervals acquires the ability to enjoy a hundred women. A kindred white preparation, sudhanidhi-rasa, is said to cause purgation within six hours if taken in the morning with a quarter tola of powdered cloves, which is the shortest stated interval in the volume."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:RasaKarpuraRasa ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:RasaSarvarogahara .

pmc:RasaKhecaratva a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Vermögen, sich in der Luft zu bewegen"@de,
        "The power of moving in the air"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. III, pp. 28 and 31-32, with the four colours at p. 34" ;
    skos:definition "Mercury, if itself tied down, is said to give a human being the power of flying in the space like a bird; and both air and mercury, if bound and rendered incapable of movement, are said to render the body light enough to move in the atmosphere. Of the four colours of mercury the black is the one preferred for the attainment of this power."@en ;
    skos:note "The one capacity in the volume for which the compiler adds a footnote of his own asserting present-day cases: the ability of expert yogis to walk upon the surface of water and to ascend the atmosphere is traditional in this country, and such miracles are reported to have been worked even in our own time. The footnote reports report and names nobody, and it is the compiler's and not the verse's."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RasaKshetrikaranaPurvakatva a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Ohne die Vorbereitung des Körpers schadet das Mittel, und die Handlung führt zum Tod"@de,
        "Without the preparation of the body the medicine harms, and the action leads to death"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, pp. 194 and 207" ;
    skos:definition "The man who takes mercury without preparing the soil of his physical system in the aforesaid manner does not derive any benefit from the medicine, which rather proves harmful. Eleven pages later the same rule is stated at its sharpest: a man attains success in taking mercury if prior to his so doing he prepares his system by purgation, vomiting and the rest; otherwise, the action leads to death."@en ;
    skos:note "The strongest necessity in the volume and the only one whose stated consequence is death. It is also the clause that makes every account of a failed course ambiguous in the transmission's favour: a user in whom nothing happened either did not prepare the field or did, and only he can say which."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "He derives no benefit from the medicine, which rather proves harmful; and the action leads to death."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:RasaRasasevana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:RasaKshetrikarana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RasaLohavedha a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Verwandlung unedler Metalle in Gold und Silber"@de,
        "The transformation of base metals into gold and silver"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. V, pp. 234 and 237-238, and ch. VIII, pp. 321-337" ;
    skos:definition "The eighteenth and last of the operations, and the one the transmission states in multiples: mercury prepared in the stated way turns a hundred, a thousand, a hundred thousand or ten million times its own weight of base metal into gold or silver, and at the highest grade does it by touch, by smoke, by sight or by sound."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the claim of the volume that a stranger could test without taking anything, and the volume knows it. It distinguishes the metal that is really transformed from the metal that is only made to appear like gold, it gives the fineness of the product in sixteenths, and it instructs that transformed metal be kept buried in the earth for a fortnight before it is taken to market."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RasaMakaradhwajaKvinin a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die eigene Gegenanzeige des Verfassers: Makaradhwaja mit Chinin"@de,
        "The compiler's own contraindication: makaradhwaja with quinine"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, footnote, p. 208" ;
    skos:definition "A footnote in the compiler's own voice, attached to the rule that mercury is purged out of the body by sour, pungent and bitter things: the allopaths who have been using makaradhwaja should take note of this, since the administration of makaradhwaja and quinine at the same time is found to be not only abortive but pernicious."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered separately from the cautions of the verse because it is an addition of the station and not of the transmission: it names a modern drug, it is addressed to practitioners of another system, and it stands in a footnote. Its form is that of a reported finding — is found to be — with no case, no series and no observer named. Where the volume's verses give a harm they at least give an antidote; this one gives neither."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:RasaMakaradhwaja ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RasaMakaradhwajaPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Makaradhwaja soll Alter und Tod verhüten und alle Fieber heilen"@de,
        "Makaradhwaja is claimed to prevent old age and death and to cure all fevers"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, eleventh process, p. 116" ;
    skos:note "If taken regularly it prevents premature old age and death and produces many effects with a suitable accompaniment; in particular it cures all sorts of fever, loss of appetite and aversion to food. This is the one preparation of the volume that the compiler says is in use outside the transmission, by the allopaths, and it is the one he attaches a contraindication to."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:RasaMakaradhwaja ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:RasaAjaraAmara,
        pmc:RasaSarvarogahara .

pmc:RasaMantraSuddhiUpakarana a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Jedes Gerät wird durch den Spruch gereinigt, sonst werden die Kräfte fortgenommen"@de,
        "Every vessel is purified by the hymn, or the potencies are taken away"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. I, equipments of the laboratory, pp. 15-16" ;
    skos:definition "Crucibles, mud, husk, cotton, cowdung cakes, the three kinds of medicine, fuel, jars and bowls of glass, iron, mud and cowrie, the winnowing plate, the bamboo vessels and all the cutlery — these and all other articles necessary from the sweeping of the laboratory to the termination of a metallurgical operation are to be purified by the supreme mantram, otherwise Bhairavas might take away the inherent potencies of all these things. The hymn is to be chanted frequently all the time a mercurial operation is carried on."@en ;
    skos:note "A necessity whose stated consequence falls on the equipment and not on the operator: the instruments keep their form and lose their power, and nothing at the bench would show it. That is the shape this holding elsewhere records as a condition placed beyond any observer, reached here through the furniture."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Bhairavas might take away the inherent potencies of all these things."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:RasaShodhana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:RasaAghoraMantra ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RasaMatrahrasa a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Der Verfasser kürzt eine alte Dosis für heutige Körper auf ein Viertel"@de,
        "The compiler cuts an old dose to a quarter for present-day bodies"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. II, footnote, p. 19" ;
    skos:definition "A footnote to the prescription of one tola of purified sulphur with ghee every morning for three weeks: this dose was prescribed more than five thousand years ago, and the present day human beings can stand only one fourth to half of the dose prescribed in those days."@en ;
    skos:note "The station correcting the text it prints, and doing it by an argument that cannot be checked from either side: the men of five thousand years ago are not available and the dose that makes a present-day body ill is not reported. It is recorded here because it is the clearest case in the volume of a later hand altering a quantity, which is the kind of thing this holding counts when it asks whether a transmission is a copying chain or a working practice."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:RasaKalinipatni ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RasaMrtasanjivaniPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die ein Jahr gehaltene Kugel soll einen Sterbenden zurückholen und den Körper waffenfest machen"@de,
        "The ball held a year is claimed to restore a dying man and to make the body proof against weapons"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, p. 212" ;
    skos:note "Held for one year inside the mouth it serves to restore a dying man to life and strength, to make the body so strong as to baffle the enemies' arms, and to cure senility. This is the only preparation in the volume that is not swallowed, and the only one whose course is stated as a year."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:RasaMrtasanjivaniGutika ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:RasaAjaraAmara,
        pmc:RasaVajradeha .

pmc:RasaParpatiPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Parpati soll alle Krankheiten heilen, und die der Kinder vor allem"@de,
        "Parpati is claimed to cure all diseases, and children's above all"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, p. 113, and ch. VII, p. 313" ;
    skos:note "May be used in all sorts of diseases with a suitable accompaniment; the chapter on solidification adds that the same preparation, there called pota or parpati, is the curer of all diseases, especially of children."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:RasaParpati ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:RasaSarvarogahara .

pmc:RasaPathyapathya a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Kost und Lebensführung dessen, der Quecksilber nimmt"@de,
        "The diet and conduct of one who takes mercury"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, dietary regulations, pp. 198-205, with the two clauses at pp. 208-209" ;
    skos:definition "Salutary: mudga juice, milk, ghee, sali rice, punarnava, rock salt, long pepper, musta, lotus root, wheat, barley, jangala meat juice, a daily bath in comfortable water. Injurious: mustard oil on the body, kanji, oil, alcohol, curd, sour and pungent and bitter and saline things, garlic, onion, radish, pulses, brinjal, mushrooms, rubbing of the limbs, late hours, sleeping by day, warm food, ice water, grief, a current of air, exposure to the sun, anger, meditation, and the showing of courage. Two further lists give the forbidden foods whose names begin with the letter K, and a third names camphor, the four spices, the trikatu, nutmeg and betel nut as things that counteract the medicine."@en ;
    skos:note "Some sixty prohibitions, of which several are states of mind and one is meditation. A course hedged this way cannot come out against the medicine: any user in whom nothing happened has certainly broken one of them, and the transmission says in the same section that mercury loses its potency by an excess of sour and salty things and that abstinence from food destroys the appetite so that the mercury is not absorbed, which is the failure of the cure assigned to the diet twice over."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:RasaRasasevana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RasaRasasinduraPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Rasasindura soll jede Krankheit heilen und Alter und Tod verhüten"@de,
        "Rasasindura is claimed to cure every disease and to prevent old age and death"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, sixth process, p. 104" ;
    skos:note "Can be used with the suitable accompaniment in all sorts of diseases; it prevents premature old age and death. Dose three ratis a day. The dose and the promise stand in consecutive sentences, which is the pattern of every preparation in this chapter."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:RasaRasasindura ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:RasaAjaraAmara,
        pmc:RasaSarvarogahara .

pmc:RasaSaptakancuka a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Sieben Hüllen aus dem Boden, in dem das Quecksilber lag, jede mit ihrer eigenen Schädigung"@de,
        "Seven coverings from the ground mercury lay in, each with its own injury"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, blemishes of mercury due to its environment, pp. 35-38" ;
    skos:definition "Mercury acquires three kinds of blemish from contact with soil, rock and water, called its seven coverings: parpati, causing roughness of the skin; patali, causing ruptures in the flesh; bhedi, causing holes in the body; dravi, causing decomposition of the body; malakari, causing an increase of the three humours; andhakari, causing blindness; and dhwankhshi, causing darkness of the skin. The blemish from contact with soil gives inertness and that from water a derangement of air, heightened by the first two natural blemishes."@en ;
    skos:note "Seven more named injuries, each tied to a named covering, and the summary sentence of the section states the position of the whole transmission on its own material: purified, mercury becomes nectar itself, whereas in its impure condition it is no better than poison. The transmission thus holds both halves of the case against it and answers the second with the first operation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:RasaShodhana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RasaSarvangasundaraPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Sarbangasundara-rasa soll alle Krankheiten heilen und Schönheit, Reichtum und Kinder geben"@de,
        "Sarbangasundara-rasa is claimed to cure all diseases and to give beauty, riches and children"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, eighth process, p. 111" ;
    skos:note "It increases appetite and cures all sorts of diseases; it prevents the arrival of premature old age and gives rise to riches and happiness; it gives enthusiasm, beauty and children. Riches stand in the same list as the cure of disease and are not marked off from it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:RasaSarvangasundara ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:RasaAjaraAmara,
        pmc:RasaSarvarogahara .

pmc:RasaSiddhiPurusagunayatta a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Operationen sollen bei Männern eines bestimmten Charakters unfehlbar gelingen"@de,
        "The operations are said to succeed invariably for men of a certain character"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. I, p. 16, and ch. II, pp. 24-25" ;
    skos:definition "Invariably successful are the metallurgical operations carried on by those who are pure in body and mind, are truthful, believers in God, learned, and full of optimism. Mercurial operations prove successful by the grace of God if carried on by those who are persevering, instructed by preceptors, patronised by kings, who avoid the following of too many pursuits, who regard mercury as the greatest of all earthly objects, who are rich, who are encouraged by the public, and who are efficient in the knowledge of doses, apparatus and heating. And: if the preceptor is pleased Śiva is pleased, if Śiva is pleased mercury is pleased, and if mercury is pleased all metallurgical operations become undoubtedly successful."@en ;
    skos:note "Three statements of the same shape at three places, and together they make every failed operation a statement about the operator: he was not pure, or not optimistic, or not rich, or his preceptor was not pleased. None of the conditions can be established except after the fact and by the person himself. This is the standard immunising clause, and what makes its presence here worth a node is that the same volume also states conditions of the other kind — the burnt palm read before the work, the weight taken with a balance after it — so the transmission is not incapable of a checkable criterion and uses both."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RasaSuddharasalaksana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Aussehen gereinigten Quecksilbers"@de,
        "The look of purified mercury"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, appearance of purified mercury, p. 38" ;
    skos:definition "The mercury which is deep blue inside and as bright as the mid-day sun outside is to be considered commendable, on account of its having been duly purified; whereas that which is smoky, yellowish and multi-coloured is not to be made use of in the preparation of medicines unless it is properly purified again."@en ;
    skos:note "An assay of the material with two named outcomes and a rule for the failing one, and it decides whether the first of the eighteen operations has been done. Nothing in it is deferred and nothing excuses a negative result; the failing material is simply put through the operation again."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RasaSvarnakaranaNisedha a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Goldherstellung ist gewöhnlich verboten"@de,
        "The making of gold is ordinarily forbidden"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. VIII, footnote, p. 337, with the preface, p. xiii" ;
    skos:definition "Preparation of gold is ordinarily prohibited; it is only for the bare subsistence of life that one may have recourse to it. The rule stands as the compiler's footnote in the middle of the chapter that gives sixteen processes for making gold and silver, and is stated again in the preface of the ascetic chemists who prepare from time to time only as much real gold as is actually necessary for bare subsistence."@en ;
    skos:note "A prohibition on the use and not on the operation, and it is the one restriction in the volume that would limit how often the transmission's most checkable claim gets made in public. Whether that is its purpose is not stated and is not inferred here."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:RasaVedhana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RasaSvarnasinduraPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Swarna-sindura soll das Alter verhüten und Kraft, Gedächtnis und Verdauung mehren"@de,
        "Swarna-sindura is claimed to prevent old age and to increase strength, memory and digestion"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, twelfth process, p. 116" ;
    skos:note "It prevents premature old age and is a destroyer of diseases; it is nutritious and increases strength, retentive faculties, power of digestion, beauty and sexual power. The dose is the only one in the chapter adjusted to the person rather than stated as a weight."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:RasaSvarnasindura ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:RasaAjaraAmara .

pmc:RasaTalakaPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Rasa-talaka soll achtzehn Arten von Aussatz und den Star heilen"@de,
        "Rasa-talaka is claimed to cure eighteen kinds of leprosy and cataract"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, tenth process, p. 114" ;
    skos:note "It cures fever, increases appetite and the retention of semen, cures eighteen different kinds of leprosy and cataract, serves as a tonic, increases longevity and the retentive faculties, and prevents premature old age. The count of eighteen kinds of leprosy is the transmission's own classification and belongs to the medical literature it shares with the Ayurvedic corpus."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:RasaTalaka ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:RasaAjaraAmara,
        pmc:RasaSarvarogahara .

pmc:RasaVajrapanjaraPhala a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Bajra-panjara soll die retten, die von Krankheit, Alter und drohendem Tod überwältigt sind"@de,
        "Bajra-panjara is claimed to save those overwhelmed with disease, senility and impending death"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, pp. 190-191" ;
    skos:note "This medicine, called bajra panjara, is a saviour of people overwhelmed with diseases, senility and impending death. The preparation immediately following it, panchamrita, made of pyrites, magnetic iron, mica, pearls and diamond rubbed a week with gold-swallowed mercury, is said to be more efficacious than this one if taken with honey and ghee."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:RasaVajrapanjara ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:RasaAjaraAmara,
        pmc:RasaVajradeha .

pmc:RasaVedhakrama a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Fünf Stufen der Verwandlung, gezählt im Gewicht des Samens"@de,
        "Five grades of transformation, counted in the weight of the seed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. V, pp. 234 and 241-242" ;
    skos:definition "Mercury exhausted with an equal quantity of gold or silver becomes śatabedhi and transforms a hundred times its weight of base metal; with double, sahasrabedhi, a thousand times; with four times, lakṣabedhi, a hundred thousand times; with eight times, koṭibedhi, ten million times; with sixteen or thirty-two times its weight it becomes sparśamaṇi, the philosopher's stone, and transforms any metal by mere touch. Exhausted with sixty-four times its weight it is said to transform by its smoke, its touch, its sight and its sound."@en ;
    skos:note "The scale runs from a claim a workshop could weigh out in an afternoon to one that needs no contact at all, and the two ends are given in the same list and in the same grammar. The first grade is the examinable one: a hundred times the weight of the prepared mercury, in a metal whose fineness the same chapter states in sixteenths."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RecitationExactnessExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Prüfprotokoll zur Genauigkeitsregel"@de,
        "Protocol on the exactness rule"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Washington Matthews, Navaho Legends, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society V (Boston and New York: Houghton, Mifflin, 1897), section 42, pp. 24-25" ;
    skos:definition "An attempt to fail the claim that a misrecited ritual utterance does not obtain its effect. The claim is unusually well placed for this, because two of the three traditions name an outward event that follows a lapse, and one of them names who notices it and when."@en ;
    skos:note "The Navajo witness supplies the sharpest form of the test and it needs no laboratory: at the public ceremony many present know the songs, an error is caught, and the ceremony is broken off as fruitless. The condition can therefore be failed by the tradition's own reporting rather than by an outside measurement, which is what the falsifier below states."@en,
        "The examination is entered at procedure without cases and not at no procedure devised, and the difference is the whole point of the scale. A condition has been stated here; nobody has yet collected the cases against it."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, protocol drawn from the wording of the three witnesses; no cases collected" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A recorded case, from within one of these transmissions, of a ritual utterance known to have been misrecited and nonetheless reported by that transmission as having obtained its effect; or, on the Navajo side, a recorded public ceremony in which a detected error did not break off the proceeding." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:ExactnessAsConditionOfEfficacy .

pmc:RecognitionPresupposesMasteredSpirits a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Anerkennung setzt eine Mindestzahl gemeisterter Geister voraus"@de,
        "Recognition presupposes a minimum of mastered spirits"@en ;
    dcterms:source "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXII, sect. 95 B, pp. 271-272" ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity: the source states both that a candidate must have a certain minimum before being recognised and what follows where he has not, and it adds that the loss of recognition on this ground is met with among all Tungus groups. That the condition is reported as sometimes failing after the fact, and not only before it, is what makes it a condition and not a form of words."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "He would not be recognized as a real shaman by his clansmen and outsiders, and it is very likely that he would give up his practice of shamanism."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:TungusFormalRecognition ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:TungusListOfSpirits ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SiberianShamanism .

pmc:RedCollarOfTheSurvivedOrdeal a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der rote Kragen derer, die die Rinde genommen haben"@de,
        "The red collar worn by those who took the bark"@en ;
    dcterms:source "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), p. 73 and pp. 74-75 and p. 77" ;
    skos:definition "A collar of red list bound with white tape, worn in Loango by a person who has taken the bark and survived it, to show the people that he did so successfully. The figures worked on it are read out by the wearer: signs for the common descent of the parties, for the strife that divided them, for the witnesses called and saluted, for the accusers set aside, and for the place given to the wearer in the town where he took it."@en ;
    skos:note "A sign of success that is public, durable and worn, and the file holds it apart from the conditions of failure for the reason SPEC §15 gives: signs of success are announced in advance and are reported by the person to whom they were announced. Two cases are recorded by name and date, an elderly woman who visited the recorder wearing one, and Matueka on the fifth of January nineteen hundred, who explained each figure on his."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:KongoNkisi .

pmc:RespiratioInternaIsCededAgency a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Die innere Atmung liegt bei abgegebener Handlungsträgerschaft"@de,
        "The internal respiration is placed at ceded agency"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Swedenborg, Arcana coelestia, n. 3884 (The Heavenly Arcana, trans. Potts, Standard Edition, vol. V, pp. 237-238)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:cededAgency ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArcanaCoelestia ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:SwedenborgRespiratioInterna ;
    pm:situationGround "The source says that the operation led his respiration gently but from within, so that he had no need to draw breath or respire by any exertion of his will. Who acts is therefore stated in the passage and it is not the man; that is the criterion this coordinate names. The placement is of the respiration in that state and not of the man's ordinary breathing, which the same passage treats as the external respiration kept going by influx."@en .

pmc:ReturnPresupposesTheSewingProhibition a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Wiederaufleben setzt die Einhaltung des Nähverbots voraus"@de,
        "The return after death presupposes that the prohibition was kept"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), pp. 28-29" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that the attempt to bring back a dead man failed because a prohibition had been broken, and not because the shamans were unable."@en ;
    skos:note "The structure of this passage matters more than its content, and it is the reason the node is here. A failure of the practice is reported, and the account assigns the failure to a third party's breach of a prohibition rather than to the practitioners or to the claim. That move makes the underlying claim unfailable from inside the tradition: any outcome confirms it, since success shows the power and failure shows the breach. Recording the immunisation is not an objection to the passage; it is the part a later protocol has to work against."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The narrator relates that the moon would not let the man come to life again, because his mother had sewn garments of new caribou skin on the island of Oqshoriaq, which is not allowed there; the two shamans who tried to catch the returning corpse did not succeed." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:InuitReturnAfterDeath ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:InuitSewingProhibitionOnOqshoriaq ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InuitAngakkuq .

pmc:ReturnPresupposesUnbrokenSinging a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Rückkehr setzt voraus, dass der Gesang nicht abbricht"@de,
        "The return presupposes that the singing does not stop"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 56-57" ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity because the source states the outcome of the want in plain terms and twice over: the singing must not cease and no one may stir him, and no fly or living creature is suffered to touch him. The recorder adds that the watching is for this reason and not, as another writer held, out of fear that the devil would take the body away; he names that writer and rejects him. The correction is his and is recorded as his."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "He would never recover out of the trance if they either ceased singing, or any one stirred him with hand or foot."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:DistantSight ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:NoaidiJourney ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SamiNoaidi .

pmc:RevelatioImmediataDisputata a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob ein Mensch durch unmittelbare Offenbarung belehrt wird"@de,
        "Whether a man is taught by immediate revelation"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Swedenborg, Arcana coelestia, n. 9439 (The Heavenly Arcana, trans. Potts, Standard Edition, vol. XI, p. 360)",
        "Swedenborg, Sapientia angelica de divino amore et de divina sapientia, n. 249 (Angelic Wisdom concerning the Divine Love and the Divine Wisdom, trans. Swedenborg Society, London 1912, p. 90)" ;
    skos:definition "Two works of one author say incompatible things, and neither mentions the other. The work of 1763 says that every man is taught of the Divine precepts by others who know them from religion, and that he is not taught by immediate revelations, referring the reader to a third work for the demonstration. The work of 1749 to 1756 says that because in the church there is no faith in the life after death and scarcely any in heaven, therefore the interiors of the author's spirit have been opened by the Lord so that he might be with the angels in heaven while in the body, not only speak with them but see amazing things there and describe them, lest hereafter people should say: who has come to us from heaven and told us that it exists, and what is there?"@en ;
    skos:note "One side of the dispute names a work that was not opened. The 1763 passage refers for its demonstration to the Doctrine of the New Jerusalem concerning the Sacred Scripture, numbers 114 to 118, and that work was not read in this run. Whoever presses this dispute should open it first; it is the place where the rule is argued rather than asserted."@en,
        "The same pair of works carries the tension a second time and from the other side, which is why this is not a slip of wording. At Arcana 10751 appearances are said to be rare now lest they compel belief, because a compelled faith does not cleave; at Arcana 9439 the opening is justified precisely by the need for somebody to come and tell. The same consideration, the effect of evidence on faith, is made to carry the withholding in one place and the granting in the other."@en,
        "The two are compatible only if the author's own case is an exception to the rule he states, and neither passage says so. The 1763 work states the rule without reservation and about every man; the earlier work gives the reason for the opening in exactly the terms the rule denies, that somebody had to come from heaven and tell. Entered as a dispute rather than as two notes because a reader cannot act on both."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:disputedClaim "That the Divine precepts reach a man by immediate revelation rather than through other men who know them from religion." ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArcanaCoelestia,
        pmw:DivinoAmoreEtSapientia ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Swedenborgian .

pmc:ReversedRuleForTheftAndAdultery a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Bei Diebstahl und Ehebruch gilt die umgekehrte Regel"@de,
        "For theft and adultery the rule is reversed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), p. 264",
        "W. Holman Bentley, Pioneering on the Congo (London: Religious Tract Society, 1900), vol. I, p. 279" ;
    skos:definition "The same bark is used in serious accusations of theft and in bad cases of denied adultery, given in small doses in the town by a practitioner of divination, and there the rule runs the other way: if the accused retains the ordeal he is guiltless of the charge and may demand compensation from his accusers, and if he vomits he pays the fines for his crime and the matter ends."@en ;
    skos:note "The exact inversion of the witchcraft rule with the same substance, in the same transmission, recorded on the following page. It is the strongest single argument in the file against reading the ordeal as a belief about a poison, and the strongest for reading it as a procedure whose reading rule is chosen with the charge. A second witness has the milder dose for theft too and gives the reason in physiological terms, that the retained poison then only causes illness."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:KongoNkisi .

pmc:RomanusFurtiTempus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Donnerstag früh, vor Sonnenaufgang, unter freiem Himmel"@de,
        "Thursday morning, before sunrise, under the open sky"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Romanus-Büchlein, in Johann Scheible (ed.), Das Kloster, weltlich und geistlich, vol. III (Stuttgart 1846), pp. 489-523, here pp. 497, 499 and 516" ;
    skos:definition "The three conditions the booklet sets on its thief-bindings, printed as a rubric above the charm: this blessing is to be spoken on Thursday morning early, before the rising of the sun, under the open sky. The same conditions recur at the two procedures for making a thief bring the goods back — the nails held against the sunrise, taken before sunrise at a pear tree, and the juniper bent towards the rising sun before sunrise."@en ;
    skos:note "A day of the week, a boundary of the day and a place, and none of the three carries a consequence for being missed. Entered as a statement about the booklet's conditions and not as a prerequisite chain: a first draft of this node made the fire bundle the prior step of the juniper, which is a relation the text does not assert anywhere, and it was withdrawn rather than kept on the weakest strength the scale allows. What the booklet arranges in an order is not thereby a chain."@en,
        "The conditions are the calendar family this holding collects, and they arrive here from the source rather than from the receiving handbook: the thief-binding of the Reading printing of 1820 carries them too. That the earlier witness has them settles that they are not an American addition."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RomanusBuechlein ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:RomanusNonusDies a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wieder gerade bis auf den neunten Tag"@de,
        "Straight again by the ninth day"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Romanus-Büchlein, in Johann Scheible (ed.), Das Kloster, weltlich und geistlich, vol. III (Stuttgart 1846), pp. 489-523, here p. 518-519" ;
    skos:definition "The blessing for a sheep or other beast with a broken leg. The break is blessed on this present day so that it shall be straight again by the ninth day, and the wound shall not swell and not suppurate until the mother of God bears another son. A plaster is required with it: a good charge of powder finely pounded, leaven to the bulk of half an egg and the whites of two eggs mixed together and laid over — and of that plaster the booklet says it is approved."@en ;
    skos:note "The one term in the booklet that falls due. A broken leg either bears weight on the ninth day or it does not, the beast is at hand, and the claim is made of an animal whose owner is counting. Set against it, in the same blessing, is the second clause: the wound shall not swell until the mother of God bears another son, which is a term that never falls due at all. The booklet puts a checkable deadline and an unreachable one in consecutive lines."@en,
        "The word approved stands on the plaster and not on the blessing. Where the booklet labels something as tried it labels the pharmacological half, and the blessing beside it carries the deadline; whether that division is regular across the text was searched and the search is at pmp:RomanusApprobatumSearch."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RomanusBuechlein ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:RomanusProbatioPastoris a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Schäfer hat die Probe an demselbigen Edelmanne gemacht"@de,
        "A shepherd made the trial on that same nobleman"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Romanus-Büchlein, in Johann Scheible (ed.), Das Kloster, weltlich und geistlich, vol. III (Stuttgart 1846), pp. 489-523, here p. 511-512" ;
    skos:definition "The closing sentence of the instruction for cutting a stick with which a man may be beaten however far away he is: the stick is cut at the new moon on a Tuesday before sunrise, facing the rising sun, with two prescribed addresses to it and three written words; a garment is laid on a mound of clods, the stick is struck on the garment and the man's name is named. Instead of the mound the threshold under the door will serve, the booklet says, as a shepherd of a named place made the trial on that same nobleman."@en ;
    skos:note "A reported case, with a station, a party who performed it and a party it was performed on, and it is the only one in the booklet. It is not a report of an outcome: what is said to have been tried is the substitution of the threshold for the mound, so the sentence vouches for a variant of the procedure and says nothing about what happened to the nobleman."@en,
        "That is the finding worth keeping, because it is the shape a reported trial takes here. The booklet has a place to record that somebody did this, it uses that place once in thirty-five pages, and it uses it to authorise a change of apparatus rather than to state an effect."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RomanusBuechlein ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:RomanusSolutioBanni a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Jede Bindung des Büchleins ist mit ihrer Lösung gedruckt"@de,
        "Every binding in the booklet is printed with its release"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Romanus-Büchlein, in Johann Scheible (ed.), Das Kloster, weltlich und geistlich, vol. III (Stuttgart 1846), pp. 489-523, here p. 498-499, 509 and 516" ;
    skos:definition "Three releases, each printed immediately after the binding it undoes. The thief held standing is freed by bidding him go in the name of Saint John. The very swift stelling of rider and footman, which must be spoken three times, is followed by a paragraph headed Release again, which sends them riding off in the name of Jesus Christ; a second such charm closes with the clause that with the same words by which they were stelled they are loosed again. And the bent juniper carries the instruction to watch for the return of the stolen goods and then to carry the stone back to where it lay and loose the bush."@en ;
    skos:note "A binding with a printed release is a claim with a stated end, and that is rarer in this holding than a claim with a stated beginning. The release also supplies the only observable the operation has: the juniper is to be loosed when the goods are back, so the operator is told to watch for an event outside himself and to act on it."@en,
        "What no release carries is a consequence for omitting it. Nothing is said about the thief who is never sent off, or the bush left bent, and the holding already records that gap at pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicWachholderKautel from the receiving handbook. It is now confirmed in the source: the omission is not Hohman's."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicWachholderbusch ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RomanusBuechlein ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:RomanusVisioVeneficarum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Du wirst nicht allein alle Hexen sehen, es wird auch dem Vieh geholfen"@de,
        "You will not only see all the witches, the cattle will also be helped"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Romanus-Büchlein, in Johann Scheible (ed.), Das Kloster, weltlich und geistlich, vol. III (Stuttgart 1846), pp. 489-523, here p. 496-497" ;
    skos:definition "The announced outcome of the second charm for a cow whose milk has been taken: three slips carrying the prescribed lines are written, milk from the sick cow is taken with them and something is scraped from the skull of a poor sinner, all of it sealed in a pot and boiled properly, whereupon the witch must burst. The slips may instead be taken into the mouth, spoken three times outside the eaves-drip and then given to the beast, and then, the booklet says, you will not only see all the witches but the cattle will also be helped."@en ;
    skos:note "Two outcomes in one clause and they are of different kinds. That the cattle are helped is the effect sought and has no sign attached; that the operator will see all the witches is an announced perception, told in advance, of a thing nobody else in the room can check. The first is unfalsifiable because no criterion is given, the second because the criterion is private, and the booklet puts them in the same sentence joined by not only."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RomanusBuechlein ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:RosicrucianAurumParergonPraecedentia a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Kenntnis der Natur muss der Metalltinktur vorausgehen"@de,
        "The knowledge of nature must precede the tincture of metals"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Confessio Fraternitatis, in the English of Thomas Vaughan (London, 1652), the passage on the tincture of metals" ;
    skos:note "Prescribed order and not stated necessity. The Confessio says that excellent wits ought not to be drawn to the tincture of metals before they be exercised well in the knowledge of nature, and states no consequence of the wrong order; what follows in the passage is an argument about how such a man ought then to live, which is not a consequence of skipping. The prior step is entered as the self-examination because that is the practice this corpus names for the preparation of a person, the knowledge of nature being a state and not an act."@en,
        "The strength is set at what the wording carries, and the weakness of the pairing is admitted: the steps named here are the nearest this corpus has to the two the sentence puts in order, and a later reader who finds a passage prescribing the knowledge of nature as an operation should replace the prior step rather than raise the strength."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:RosicrucianOpusTurris ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ConfessioFraternitatis ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:RosicrucianExaminatioAnteNuptias ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmc:RosicrucianAvisVinciendaAnteCibum a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Den Vogel festmachen, ehe man ihn füttert"@de,
        "Make the bird fast before feeding it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chymische Hochzeit Christiani Rosencreutz (Strasbourg, 1616), the Fourth Storey of the Tower, Sixth Day" ;
    skos:definition "A caution inside the procedure, with its consequence stated and then borne out in the same paragraph: before the hatched bird is given anything to eat it is to be made fast, otherwise it would give the workers work enough. After the first feeding it bites and scratches so that, could it have had its will upon any of them, it would have despatched him."@en ;
    skos:note "Worth entering because of the class it belongs to and not because of the danger. The consequence is stated before the event and is then reported as having occurred, so the work supplies both the rule and the case for it; most cautions in this holding supply one or the other."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:RosicrucianOpusTurris ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:NuptiaeChymicae ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmc:RosicrucianCantusQuiLapidesTrahit a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Gesang, der Perlen statt Felsen zieht und Fürsten statt Pluto bewegt"@de,
        "Singing that draws pearls instead of rocks and moves princes instead of Pluto"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Confessio Fraternitatis, in the English of Thomas Vaughan (London, 1652), the passage of the six questions" ;
    skos:definition "The fifth capacity, and the only one of the six with a named means: that one could so sing that instead of stony rocks one draws to oneself pearls and precious stones, instead of wild beasts spirits, and instead of hellish Pluto moves the mighty princes of the world."@en ;
    skos:note "The construction is a threefold correction of the Orpheus story, and each limb replaces the object Orpheus is said to have moved with a greater one. That makes the sentence a claim about the singing and also a claim about the older account, and the work gives no term, no interval and no sign by which the singer would know it had worked."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ConfessioFraternitatis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmc:RosicrucianCuratioGratis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Kranken heilen, und zwar unentgeltlich"@de,
        "Curing the sick, and for nothing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Fama Fraternitatis, in the English of Thomas Vaughan (London, 1652), the six articles of agreement, and the passage on the death of I. O." ;
    skos:definition "The capacity the Fama states in the form of an obligation rather than a promise: the first of the six articles is that none of the brethren should profess any other thing than to cure the sick, and that gratis. The work reports one case, that the brother I. O. cured a young Earl of Norfolk of leprosy in England, and it reports as an obstacle to the first four brethren the unspeakable concourse of the sick."@en ;
    skos:note "The reported case is the only named cure in the three works and it names a disease with a visible course, which makes it the one claim of this corpus a contemporary could in principle have checked. The work gives no date, no name for the earl, and no account of the means."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:FamaFraternitatis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmc:RosicrucianCustosPortae a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Pförtner, der nur frei wird, wenn ein anderer sich gleich schwer vergeht"@de,
        "The gatekeeper who is freed only when another transgresses as highly"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chymische Hochzeit Christiani Rosencreutz (Strasbourg, 1616), the Seventh Day, the porter's supplication and the sentence upon it" ;
    skos:definition "The condition of the porter of the first gate, given by the king in answer to a direct question on the seventh day. He is a famous and rare astrologer, formerly in high regard, who once committed a fault against Venus and saw her in her bed of rest; the punishment imposed is that he wait at the first gate until someone releases him from it. Asked whether he may then be released, the king answers that if anyone can be found that has transgressed as highly as himself, he must take his place and the other shall be free. The narrator, who has committed the same fault, confesses it, is told that the king cannot transgress his ancient usages, is put in the porter's place with the porter's ring, and is told that no release is to be hoped for until the marriage feast of the king's future son."@en ;
    skos:note "The porter's own supplication is the other half and is of a different kind. He asks to be present at the supper on the ground that his observations cannot lie to him — Venus has already been uncovered by one of the guests — and undertakes, if a strict enquiry should show otherwise, to remain before the gate all the days of his life. An astrologer staking his whole penalty on a prediction being confirmed is the nearest thing to a wager this corpus contains, and the work reports that he was right."@en,
        "The structure is worth having exactly. The penalty is not remitted, not served out and not transferable at the offender's choice: it is a post that must be occupied, and it passes only when a second person commits the same offence and says so. That makes the release of one man conditional on the fall of another, inside the operative narrative and stated by the authority of the place."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:NuptiaeChymicae ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmc:RosicrucianDignitasNonExCuraHominum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Würdigkeit wird nicht nach menschlicher Sorgfalt beurteilt, und die Unwürdigen werden nicht gehört"@de,
        "Worthiness is not judged by man's carefulness, and the unworthy are not heard"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Confessio Fraternitatis, in the English of Thomas Vaughan (London, 1652), the passage on offering the treasures freely to all men" ;
    skos:definition "How the Fraternity says admission is decided. The worthiness of those who shall be accepted is not esteemed and known of them by man's carefulness, but by the rule of their revelation and manifestation; wherefore if the unworthy cry and call a thousand times, or offer and present themselves a thousand times, God has so commanded their ears that they should hear none of them. And no violence or force can be done to them, for God has so compassed them about with his clouds that they can neither be seen nor known by anybody, except he had the eyes of an eagle."@en ;
    skos:note "Taken together with the Confessio's promise that no man's uprightness and hopes shall deceive him whosoever shall make himself known to them under the seal of secrecy, this gives the applicant a procedure with no criterion and a guarantee with no failure. The unanswered applicant is the unworthy applicant by construction."@en,
        "The thousand of this passage is a rhetorical thousand and should not be read against the one in a thousand of the cedars, which is a distribution. They are entered in one file and are not two statements of one rate."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ConfessioFraternitatis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmc:RosicrucianDistributioPonderum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sieben, einundzwanzig, fünfunddreissig, fünfunddreissig, einundzwanzig, sieben, einer"@de,
        "Seven, twenty-one, thirty-five, thirty-five, twenty-one, seven, one"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chymische Hochzeit Christiani Rosencreutz (Strasbourg, 1616), the Third Day, the passage on the number of the prisoners, p. 52 of the 1616 print" ;
    skos:definition "The work publishes the outcome of its own trial as a count. Of the prisoners, those who weighed one were seven; those who weighed two, twenty-one; those who weighed three, thirty-five; those who weighed four, thirty-five; those who weighed five, twenty-one; those who weighed six, seven; and he who came to the seventh and yet could not well raise it was one only. Besides these, of them who wholly failed there were many, and of those who drew all the weights from the ground, but few. The narrator says he numbered them as they stood before him and noted them in his table-book, that among the hundred and twenty-six who weighed anything none was equal to another, and that he would name them all with each man's weight were it not as yet forbidden him."@en ;
    skos:note "The numbers are in the 1616 print as they are in the English, and they are the binomial coefficients of seven taken one to six with the seventh case set at one. So the distribution is constructed and not counted, and the narrator's claim to have counted it stands beside a series no counting produces. The work asserts both in the same paragraph."@en,
        "Two further rate statements belong beside it. After the gentry, the learned and the unlearned had passed, in each condition perhaps one, it may be two, but for the most part none, was found perfect. And of the cheaters, the most part could not abide the trial and were jerked out with whips, so that of so great a throng so few remained that the narrator says he is ashamed to reveal their number."@en,
        "Whatever the numbers are, they are the thing this holding has almost none of: a transmission stating how many of its candidates failed and in what proportions. Read as a report it is falsified by its own arithmetic; read as a statement of what the transmission holds about its candidates it says that most fail, that the failures are graded, and that no two candidates fail alike. The second reading is the one the nodes of this file rest on, and it does not need the numbers to be true."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:NuptiaeChymicae ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmc:RosicrucianEquitesLapidisAurei a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Über Unwissenheit, Armut und Krankheit gesetzt, mit ihnen nach Belieben zu verfahren"@de,
        "Set over Ignorance, Poverty and Sickness, to handle them at pleasure"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chymische Hochzeit Christiani Rosencreutz (Strasbourg: Lazarus Zetzner, 1616), the Seventh Day, the installation after the five articles; read in the modern reset English and checked against the 1616 print" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity conferred at the end of the Chymische Hochzeit, at the installation of the guests as Knights of the Golden Stone: among other privileges they are set over Ignorance, Poverty and Sickness, to handle them at their pleasure. The three are the same three the Confessio's first promise names, now given as an office rather than as a condition."@en ;
    skos:note "It is conferred by ceremony and after an oath, not by a procedure, and the work states no means by which the office is exercised. That makes it the corpus's own statement of what the whole seven days were for, and it is worth noting that the thing conferred is authority over three privations and not any of the six capacities of the Confessio."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:NuptiaeChymicae ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmc:RosicrucianFundatorSineMorbo a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Stifter von über hundert Jahren, der nie eine Krankheit erfuhr"@de,
        "A founder over a hundred years old who never experienced disease"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Fama Fraternitatis, in the English of Thomas Vaughan (London, 1652), the elogium at the end of the book I; with Confessio Fraternitatis, the passage on the year 1378" ;
    skos:definition "What the elogium in the vault claims of the founder, and it is the corpus's only claim about a body. He died aged more than a hundred, not driven by any disease — which he himself had never experienced in his body, nor suffered to infest others — but called by the spirit of God, and rendered his illuminated soul amid the embraces and last kisses of the brethren. The Confessio gives him the years: born in 1378, and a hundred and six years of life whose alterations he left to the brethren to peruse."@en ;
    skos:note "Never experienced disease in his body and never suffered it to infest others are two claims and the second is about other people. The Fama has already reported one cure by another brother and the concourse of the sick as an obstacle to the work of the first four, so the second clause is the corpus's strongest statement about what the curing did, and it stands in a funerary inscription and not in an account of a practice."@en,
        "The dates are internally consistent — 1378 plus 106 is 1484, and 1484 plus 120 is 1604, ten years before the Fama was printed and six before the manuscript Haslmayr had. The vault inscription and the printing of the work are therefore not far apart on the corpus's own reckoning, which is a thing a reader should be able to see from the nodes."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:FamaFraternitatis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmc:RosicrucianHoraMortisNonOstenditur a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ihr eigenes Unglück und ihre Sterbestunde werden ihnen nicht gezeigt"@de,
        "Their own misfortunes and hour of death are not shown to them"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Fama Fraternitatis, in the English of Thomas Vaughan (London, 1652), the passage on the deaths of the brethren and the Book M" ;
    skos:definition "A limit the Fama states of its own highest capacity, publicly and in the first person plural: whatever secret they have learned out of the Book M, although before their eyes they behold the image and pattern of all the world, yet there are not shown unto them their misfortunes, nor the hour of their death, the which only is known to God himself, who thereby would have them keep in a continual readiness."@en ;
    skos:note "The two sentences stand within a few lines of each other, and the second is the ground on which the first has to be read: one brother's death was foretold by another, and no brother's own death is shown to him. The limit is therefore on self-knowledge and not on foreknowledge, and the work does not say so — this node says which of the two the wording carries and no more."@en,
        "This is the rarest kind of statement in this holding and it should be marked as such: a transmission naming something its own method cannot do, in its own voice, with no adversary in view and no failure to explain away. It is stated in the passage on the brethren's deaths, where the Fama has just reported that the first of them died in England as brother C. long before had foretold him."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:FamaFraternitatis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmc:RosicrucianLiberUnusProOmnibus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Buch gelesen, und damit alle Bücher verstanden und behalten"@de,
        "One book read, and all books thereby understood and remembered"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Confessio Fraternitatis, in the English of Thomas Vaughan (London, 1652), the passage of the six questions" ;
    skos:definition "The fourth capacity: that one could read in one only book and withal by reading understand and remember all that which in all other books — those that have been, those that are now, and those that shall hereafter come out — has been, is and shall be learned and found out."@en ;
    skos:note "The forward reach of the clause is the part that admits of no evidence and is stated anyway: books not yet written are included. It is also the clearest case in this corpus of a capacity given without any procedure, and the Fama's Book M, which the founder is said to have translated in a year, is the nearest thing to an object for it."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ConfessioFraternitatis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmc:RosicrucianLibriFictiFalsorumChymistarum a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Legt die Bücher der falschen Alchemisten weg"@de,
        "Put away the books of the false alchemists"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Confessio Fraternitatis, in the English of Thomas Vaughan (London, 1652), the conclusion; with Fama Fraternitatis, the passage on books set forth under the name of Chymia, and Chymische Hochzeit (Strasbourg, 1616), the Third Day, the sentence" ;
    skos:definition "The Confessio's closing admonition, and the Fama's promise that goes with it. The reader is earnestly admonished to put away, if not all, yet the most books written by false alchemists, who misuse the holy Trinity, deceive people with most strange figures and dark sentences, and cozen the simple of their money; the enemy of man's welfare mingles such books among the good seed to make the truth more difficult to be believed, truth being in herself simple, easy and naked, while falsehood is proud, haughty and coloured. The Fama promises a catalogue or register of such books to the pure-hearted, and the Chymische Hochzeit has the king promise the dismissed lords a catalogue of heretics or Index Expurgatorius and announce that he will rummage his own library and offer up the seductive writings to Vulcan, asking the lords to do the same with theirs."@en ;
    skos:note "A transmission that announces its own index is announcing a criterion, and all three works announce one and none supplies it. The promised catalogue is the register of what the corpus disowns, it is promised twice in the manifestos and once inside the narrative, and its absence is the reason a later reader cannot tell which of the Rosicrucian literature the Rosicrucian manifestos would have rejected."@en,
        "The Chymische Hochzeit has the cheaters confess that they forged false fictitious books, fooled and cheated others, and made use of ungodly deceitful figures, sparing not even the Divine Trinity — which is the Confessio's sentence put in the mouths of the condemned. The narrative therefore executes the manifestos' judgement on a class the manifestos never enumerate."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:RosicrucianOpusTurris ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ConfessioFraternitatis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmc:RosicrucianLinguaMagica a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Eine gemachte Sprache, in der die Natur aller Dinge ausgedrückt ist"@de,
        "A made language in which the nature of all things is expressed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Confessio Fraternitatis, in the English of Thomas Vaughan (London, 1652), the passage on the characters and letters; with Fama Fraternitatis, the passage on the first four brethren" ;
    skos:definition "The Fraternity states that its first four members made a magical language and writing with a large dictionary, which it says it uses daily; and the Confessio adds that the language was formed out of the characters God imprinted in the creation, that in it the nature of all things is expressed and declared, and that this is why the Fraternity is not eloquent in other languages, those being disagreeing with the languages of Adam and Enoch and hidden through the confusion of Babel."@en ;
    skos:note "Two claims stand together here and only the second is a capacity: that such a language was made, and that it expresses the nature of what it names. The second is the one that could fail, and the failure condition the work itself supplies is its own: a language of that kind would make its speakers bad at other languages, which the Confessio offers as an explanation and which is also a prediction about them."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ConfessioFraternitatis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmc:RosicrucianNihilOccultumUltraGangen a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Nichts verborgen bei denen jenseits des Ganges oder in Peru"@de,
        "Nothing hidden by those beyond the Ganges or in Peru"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Confessio Fraternitatis, in the English of Thomas Vaughan (London, 1652), the passage of the six questions" ;
    skos:definition "The third capacity: that dwelling in one place, neither the people who dwell beyond the river Ganges in the Indies could hide anything, nor those who live in Peru be able to keep their counsels secret from you. The capacity is stated as knowledge of what distant persons conceal, not as sight of distant places."@en ;
    skos:note "No practice is named for it anywhere in the three works, which is why this node carries no pm:Yielding. The search that establishes that absence stands at pmp:RosicrucianOperandiModusSearch."@en,
        "The distinction is worth keeping because it is the work's own. What is named is the failure of concealment and not the perception of a scene, and the two are differently testable: the first requires knowing what somebody meant to keep back, which no arrangement of a viewing can supply."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ConfessioFraternitatis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmc:RosicrucianNonTimereFamemMorbumSenectutem a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Hunger, Armut, Krankheit und Alter nicht mehr fürchten müssen"@de,
        "Not having to fear hunger, poverty, sickness or age"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Confessio Fraternitatis, in the English of Thomas Vaughan, The Fame and Confession of the Fraternity of R: C: (London, 1652), the passage of the six questions, and again at the passage on the tincture of metals" ;
    skos:definition "The first of the capacities the Confessio names, and it names all four together: that one would not need to care nor to fear hunger, poverty, sickness and age. The work returns to it later in its own voice, saying of the man who has come so far that neither poverty nor sickness can hurt him that he must be an insatiable creature if he then gives himself to idle things."@en ;
    skos:note "The second passage is the reason this is entered as a capacity and not as a figure of speech. A work that argues about how such a man ought then to behave has treated the state as obtained."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ConfessioFraternitatis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmc:RosicrucianNuptiaePraesupponuntExaminationem a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Selbstprüfung muss der Reise vorausgehen"@de,
        "The self-examination must precede the journey"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chymische Hochzeit Christiani Rosencreutz (Strasbourg, 1616), the First Day, the verse of the invitation, with the sentence of the Third Day" ;
    skos:note "Stated necessity and not prescribed order, because the consequence is quotable and stands in the same five lines as the requirement. The dependent step is named as the work of the tower rather than as attendance at the wedding: attendance is what the weighing decides, and the work of the tower is the thing only the weighed survivors are admitted to."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The wedding may work thy bane; bane comes to him who faileth here. Stated in the invitation before the journey begins, and made concrete on the third day, where those found too light are stripped, branded according to which weights they failed, hunted out with rods, whips or dogs, or punished in body and life."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:RosicrucianOpusTurris ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:NuptiaeChymicae ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:RosicrucianExaminatioAnteNuptias ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmc:RosicrucianOpusTurrisYieldsImagines a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Werk des Turms bringt die zwei lebenden Körper hervor"@de,
        "The work of the tower produces the two living bodies"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chymische Hochzeit Christiani Rosencreutz (Strasbourg, 1616), the Sixth and Seventh Days" ;
    skos:note "Entered as the work states it and not as the reader may wish to take it. The narrative is a first-person report of a dream, so the mode is textual attestation of what the work claims and not a first-person report of a practice; nothing in the work says that the writer performed this anywhere but in the vision."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:RosicrucianOpusTurris ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:NuptiaeChymicae ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:RosicrucianImaginesAnimatae .

pmc:RosicrucianPortaAdCampanamClausa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Tor schliesst beim Glockenschlag, und der Pförtner kann es nicht wieder öffnen"@de,
        "The gate shuts at the bell and the porter cannot open it again"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chymische Hochzeit Christiani Rosencreutz (Strasbourg, 1616), the Second Day, the second gate and the closing of it at the bell" ;
    skos:definition "The failure stated at the token-issuing gate. As the traveller lingers with the second porter it begins to ring in the castle, the porter tells him to run or else all the pains and labour he has hitherto undergone will serve to no purpose, and the lights above begin to be put out. He gets in beside the virgin who extinguishes them; the gate is suddenly clapped to, a part of his coat is locked out and has to be left behind, and neither he nor they who stood ready without and called at the gate could prevail with the porter to open it again. The porter then delivers the keys to the virgin, who takes them into the court."@en ;
    skos:note "A second failure at the same gates is stated in passing and is of the same kind: the first porter demands the letter of invitation, and the narrator says how glad he was to have brought it, for how easily might he have forgotten it, as it also chanced to others, as the porter himself told him. That is a reported class of applicants who came to the gate and lacked the token the transmission had supplied them."@en,
        "Four features of this passage decide what it is worth. The station is one that hands over its own token. The failure is at that station and not before it. It is named as having happened to persons — those who stood without and called — and not stated as a rule. And the refusal is put beyond the officiant: the porter cannot reopen, and he gives the keys away. A run that wanted a token-handing gate with a stated failure could not construct a cleaner one."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:NuptiaeChymicae ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmc:RosicrucianPostCXXAnnosPatebo a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Nach hundertzwanzig Jahren werde ich mich öffnen"@de,
        "After a hundred and twenty years I shall open"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Fama Fraternitatis, in the English of Thomas Vaughan (London, 1652), the relation of the finding of the vault" ;
    skos:definition "The Fama's account of the finding of the founder's tomb. A brother repairing the house of the Spirit draws out a great nail from a brass memorial table, the nail takes a stone out of the plastering with it and uncovers a hidden door, on which is written in great letters Post 120 annos patebo, with the year of the Lord under it. The vault behind it has seven sides, each five foot broad and eight foot high, is lit by an inner sun set in the centre of the ceiling, has a round altar for a tombstone, ten squares with figures and sentences on each wall, a chest behind each wall containing the brethren's books, and beneath the altar the body of the founder whole and unconsumed, holding a parchment book called I."@en ;
    skos:note "The Fama draws the consequence itself and widens it past the vault: for like as our door was after so many years wonderfully discovered, also there shall be opened a door to Europe when the wall is removed, which already doth begin to appear. The second door has no term."@en,
        "The vault is the corpus's own demonstration and it is built as one: an inscription naming a term, a term elapsed, and a discovery at the end of it. What it demonstrates is that the founder foresaw the date of the finding, which is the only prediction of this corpus with a date attached, and the date is supplied by the same work that reports the finding."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:FamaFraternitatis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmc:RosicrucianPraescientiaObscurationum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Verfinsterungen der Kirche vorherwissen und wie lange sie dauern"@de,
        "Foreknowing the obscurations of the Church and how long they will last"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Confessio Fraternitatis, in the English of Thomas Vaughan (London, 1652), the passage on the characters and letters" ;
    skos:definition "A capacity stated with its own analogy and its own ground: as the mathematician or astronomer can long before see and know the eclipses which are to come, so the Fraternity may verily foreknow and foresee the darkness or obscurations of the Church, and how long they shall last. The ground given is the characters and letters which God has imprinted in the creation and in the Bible, from which the Fraternity says it borrowed its magical writing."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the one capacity in the corpus that states a testable form. An eclipse analogy commits the claimant to a date and a duration, and the work draws the analogy itself rather than having it drawn for it. No date and no duration is given anywhere in the three works, and the search for one is at pmp:RosicrucianTerminusSearch."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ConfessioFraternitatis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmc:RosicrucianQuiNimisLevis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Schaden hat, wer hier versäumt; hüte sich, wer zu leicht ist"@de,
        "Harm to him who fails here; let him beware who is too light"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chymische Hochzeit Christiani Rosencreutz (Strasbourg, 1616), the First Day, the verse of the invitation" ;
    skos:definition "The failure condition of the invitation, stated in the invitation itself and in advance of everything it invites to: if thou shouldst not bathe thoroughly, the wedding may work thy bane; bane comes to him who faileth here; let him beware who is too light. The German of 1616 reads Schad hat, wer hie verzeucht, Huet sich wer ist zu Leicht."@en ;
    skos:note "The last line is the reason the whole of the third day is anticipated here. Too light is not a figure: on the third day the guests are put on a golden balance against seven weights, and the ones who cannot raise them are said in the same words to have been found too light. So the invitation names the criterion of a trial the invited will not see for two days, and it names it in the term the trial itself uses."@en,
        "This is a failure condition in the sense SPEC 15 asks for and not a caution: it states an outcome, it is prior to the operation, and the operation supplies the measurement. What it does not state is what too light means in advance of the measurement, and nothing in the work supplies that. The search stands at pmp:RosicrucianDignitasSearch."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:NuptiaeChymicae ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmc:RosicrucianQuinqueArticuliEquitum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die fünf Artikel der Ritter des Goldenen Steins"@de,
        "The five articles sworn by the Knights of the Golden Stone"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chymische Hochzeit Christiani Rosencreutz (Strasbourg, 1616), the Seventh Day, the five articles" ;
    skos:definition "Read out by Atlas on the seventh day and sworn by the king's sceptre. That they shall at no time ascribe their order to any devil or spirit, but only to God their creator and his handmaid Nature. That they will abominate all whoredom, incontinency and uncleanness, and not defile their order with such vices. That they will through their talents be ready to assist all that are worthy and have need of them. That they desire not to employ this honour to worldly pride and high authority. That they shall not be willing to live longer than God will have them do. The narrator records that at the last article the company could not choose but laugh, and that it may well have been placed after the rest only for a conceit."@en ;
    skos:note "The fourth article is the fourth weight by another name, and the narrator's parenthesis at the banquet — that naming his place in the order might be interpreted as pride in him, which is expressly against the fourth weight — is the only bridge the work builds between the two lists. Whether the seven weights are the articles is exactly the question the work leaves open."@en,
        "The reported laughter is entered because it is a judgement inside the source on one of its own rules, and the class of thing this holding loses most easily. The article laughed at is the one that cannot be broken by an act."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:NuptiaeChymicae ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmc:RosicrucianRedeasDumLicet a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Weisst du dich fehlerhaft, so kehre heim, solange es noch geht"@de,
        "If you know yourself faulty, go home while you still can"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chymische Hochzeit Christiani Rosencreutz (Strasbourg, 1616), the Second Day, the Latin tablet on the three cedars" ;
    skos:definition "The closing sentence of the same tablet, and it is a self-warning against the thing the tablet has just invited to: but, ho, beware, you know not with how much danger you commit yourself to this way, for if you know yourself to be obnoxious by the smallest fault to the laws of our King, I beseech you, while it is still possible, to return swiftly to your house by the way you came."@en ;
    skos:note "The caution supplies an exit and attaches a term to it — while it is still possible — and the same tablet has already said that once a way is entered there is no going back save at peril of life. So the exit exists only before the choice, and the narrator loses it by accident rather than by decision."@en,
        "The standard of the fault is the smallest fault against laws the traveller has not been shown, and the judgement of whether he is guilty of one is left to him. That is the corpus's characteristic construction: the applicant is asked to apply a criterion he is not given."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:RosicrucianQuatuorViae ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:NuptiaeChymicae ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmc:RosicrucianSecretumCentumAnnorum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Beschränkung der Weitergabe, im operativen Text selbst"@de,
        "The restriction on passing it on, inside the operative text"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Fama Fraternitatis, in the English of Thomas Vaughan (London, 1652), the six articles and the description of the vault; with Chymische Hochzeit (Strasbourg, 1616), the First, Second, Third, Sixth and Seventh Days" ;
    skos:definition "Where the three works restrict their own transmission. The Fama's sixth article binds the Fraternity to remain secret one hundred years, and its fourth makes each brother responsible for choosing a worthy successor; the brethren's burial places were to be kept secret as far as possible, and the floor of the vault is described as parted in a triangle whose contents are not manifested for fear of the abuse by the evil and ungodly world. The Chymische Hochzeit carries the restriction as a series of refusals by the narrator: the name on the trumpet he could well read but is as yet forbidden to reveal; the words above the first portal he was earnestly forbidden to relate; the names and weights of the hundred and twenty-six he would willingly give were it not as yet forbidden him; the sentence upon the prisoners none of the knights dared open his mouth about; and what the virgin did with the companions in the seventh room he cannot tell, they being most earnestly forbidden to speak of it."@en ;
    skos:note "The sharpest case is the library and the sepulchre of the third day, because there the restriction is enforced against a breach that had already happened. Both were shown to the narrator and his companion by a page who held the keys; a second page then came running, the keys were taken away, and the first page declared that the king would by no means permit either to be seen by any man and besought them, as they cared for his life, to reveal it to no man, he having already utterly denied it. The penalty for the disclosure falls on the page and not on the two who saw."@en,
        "Two features put this beside the other transmissions of this holding that restrict their own passing on. The restriction stands inside the operative text and not in an external rule, and the class of permitted recipient is named — a worthy successor chosen by a brother, and the pure-hearted who are to receive the catalogue. What is different is the ground: the Fama gives fear of abuse by the ungodly world for the floor of the vault, and the Chymische Hochzeit gives no ground at all, only that the narrator is forbidden."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:FamaFraternitatis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmc:RosicrucianSententiaGradata a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Urteil, abgestuft nach den versäumten Gewichten"@de,
        "The sentence graded by which weights were failed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chymische Hochzeit Christiani Rosencreutz (Strasbourg, 1616), the Third Day, the sentence read in the garden, p. 52 of the 1616 print" ;
    skos:definition "The judgement read out on the same day, and it is assigned by the combination of weights each guest failed. The lords and potentates keep their lives and are dismissed, expressly rejected by the Order and told never henceforth to covet an entrance so inconsiderately. Those who did not stand up to the first, third and fourth weight are stripped stark naked and so sent forth. Those found too light in the second and fifth are besides stripping branded with one, two or more brand-marks according as each was lighter or heavier. Those drawn up by the sixth or seventh and not by the rest are dealt with somewhat more graciously; and for every combination there was a certain punishment ordained, which the narrator says is too long to recount. Those who the day before separated themselves of their own accord go out at liberty without any blame. The convicted cheaters who could move none of the weights are punished in body and life, with the sword, halter, water and rods, and the execution is to be inviolably observed as an example to others."@en ;
    skos:note "At the gate each of the dismissed lords is given out of a cup a draught of forgetfulness, so that he might have no further memory of misfortune. A trial that erases the memory of its own failures in the same passage in which it grades them is worth recording as such."@en,
        "This is graded exclusion and not graded attainment, and the difference is the whole of what it costs a generalization this holding already holds. Every grade is a way of being put out of the castle: dismissed with a request never to come again, stripped, branded, hunted, drowned or hanged. Nobody who failed a weight receives a lesser rank inside the system, and the work says in the dismissal itself that the lords are rejected by this our Order."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:NuptiaeChymicae ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmc:RosicrucianSexArticuli a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die sechs Artikel, zu denen sich die ersten Brüder verbanden"@de,
        "The six articles the first brethren bound themselves to"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Fama Fraternitatis, in the English of Thomas Vaughan (London, 1652), the six articles; the German of the 1615 print consulted at the same place" ;
    skos:definition "The agreement of the eight, as the Fama states it. First, that none of them should profess any other thing than to cure the sick, and that gratis. Second, that none of the posterity should be constrained to wear one certain kind of habit, but therein to follow the custom of the country. Third, that every year upon the day C. they should meet together at the house S. Spiritus, or write the cause of his absence. Fourth, every brother should look about for a worthy person who after his decease might succeed him. Fifth, the word C. R. should be their seal, mark and character. Sixth, the Fraternity should remain secret one hundred years."@en ;
    skos:note "Five of the six are rules of a society and the first is a rule about a capacity: it forbids the brethren to profess anything but curing, and forbids payment for that. The fourth makes each member responsible for producing his own replacement, which is how a transmission with no institution keeps going, and the third supplies the one thing a secret society can be caught out on — a fixed annual date and place."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:FamaFraternitatis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmc:RosicrucianTesseraeHospitum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Drei Tore, und an jedem ein Zeichen, das das Tor selbst ausgibt"@de,
        "Three gates, and at each a token the gate itself issues"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chymische Hochzeit Christiani Rosencreutz (Strasbourg, 1616), the Second Day, the three gates; the tablets Procul hinc, procul ite profani and Date et dabitur vobis" ;
    skos:definition "The graded passage into the castle. At the first gate the porter demands the letter of invitation, is satisfied by it, sells the traveller a golden token bearing S.C. for his bottle of water, and gives him besides a sealed letter to the second porter. At the second gate the second porter reads that letter, drives back a chained lion, and sells a token bearing S.M. for the traveller's salt. At the third gate the name is written in a vellum book and the true guest token is given, smaller than the others and much heavier, bearing S.P.N.; new shoes are given and the old ones are to be given away to the poor."@en ;
    skos:note "That is what makes the next node a counter-case rather than a detail, and it is examined at examinations/what-the-royal-wedding-refutes.ttl."@en,
        "The arrangement is the one this holding has from the ascent literature: the token of the next station is issued at the last, and the first token is the invitation, which the bridegroom sent. Nothing the traveller brought of himself opens any gate; what he gives up at each is a consumable he was carrying — water, salt — and in the third case his shoes."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:NuptiaeChymicae ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmc:RosicrucianTrialPresupposesLibra a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Wägung muss überstanden sein, ehe der König erreicht wird"@de,
        "The weighing must be survived before the king is reached"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chymische Hochzeit Christiani Rosencreutz (Strasbourg, 1616), the Third Day, the sentence read in the garden" ;
    skos:note "Stated necessity, and the consequence is not inferred from the narrative but read out as a sentence by a herald with a trumpet. The dependent step is the work of the tower for the same reason as at pmc:RosicrucianNuptiaePraesupponuntExaminationem: the survivors of the weighing are the persons who go on to perform it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Rejection by the Order and dismissal from the castle, graded by the combination of weights failed: dismissal with a request never to return, stripping, branding with one or more marks, hunting out with rods, whips or dogs, and for those who could raise no weight at all, punishment in body and life with the sword, halter, water and rods."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:RosicrucianOpusTurris ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:NuptiaeChymicae ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:RosicrucianLibraAurea ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmc:RosicrucianVeniaSponteConfessis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die freiwillig Bekennenden werden ohne Strafe gewogen"@de,
        "Those who confessed their unworthiness are weighed without penalty"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chymische Hochzeit Christiani Rosencreutz (Strasbourg, 1616), the Second Day, the virgin's address on the scales, and the Third Day, the weighing of the nine" ;
    skos:definition "The waiver the same day grants, and it is granted for a declaration and not for an attainment. On the evening before the trial the guests are told that whoever does not trust himself should stand aside, that he whose conscience pricks him shall be left in the hall that day and be freed by the morrow but must come hither never again. Nine stay; they are bound with cords that none could cut and left in darkness. In the morning they are unbound and placed where they can see the scales, and after the whole Inquisition is finished a captain asks that these poor men who acknowledged their misunderstanding be set upon the scale too, without their incurring any danger of penalty, and only for recreation's sake. Most of them miscarry, and are neither laughed at nor scourged but peaceably placed on one side. The narrator outweighs all seven."@en ;
    skos:note "It also supplies the one thing a trial needs to have a failing grade at all: a control group whose outcome differs. The nine who confessed and the guests who did not were put on the same balance under different stakes, and the work reports the results of both."@en,
        "Two things are waived and they are not the same. The penalty of the trial is waived for those who declared themselves unworthy, and the trial is not: they are weighed. What is not waived at all is the exclusion for those who took the offered exit and left — they go free without blame and may never return in the same fashion. So the transmission prices the admission of unworthiness at loss of the penalty and loss of the future, and charges nothing for the measurement."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:NuptiaeChymicae ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmc:RosicrucianVitaAbInitioAdFinem a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Leben, als hätte man von Anfang der Welt an gelebt und lebte bis an ihr Ende"@de,
        "Living as though from the beginning of the world to its end"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Confessio Fraternitatis, in the English of Thomas Vaughan (London, 1652), the passage of the six questions" ;
    skos:definition "The second capacity of the same series: that one could always live so as if one had lived from the beginning of the world, and moreover as one should still live to the end of it. The work does not say whether this is length of life or knowledge of the whole span, and the two readings are not separated in the wording."@en ;
    skos:note "The ambiguity is the reason this node says what it says and no more. The Fama elsewhere reports the founder dying at over a hundred and having never experienced disease in his body, which would support the first reading; the Confessio's own next question is about reading one book and thereby knowing all books, which would support the second. Nothing in either work decides it, and a node that decided it would be reporting a preference."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ConfessioFraternitatis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmc:RubbingBoardSecondTestSeldomMade a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der zweite Test am Reibbrett wird selten gemacht"@de,
        "The second test at the rubbing-board is seldom made"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 171" ;
    skos:definition "Strictly the rubbing-board requires the same corroborative second test as the poison and termites oracles, with the opposite outcome for the verdict to be valid. In fact the second test is very seldom made, and important questions are taken to the poison oracle instead."@en ;
    skos:note "The passage is a self-warning of the transmission against a rule it lays down, and it is the sharpest one this file carries: the control procedure exists for the instrument that is used most often and is precisely the one it is not applied to. The recorder adds the inference that the Azande must be aware that the second test always confirms the first. That is his conclusion and not a reported Zande statement, and it is not entered as a claim of the transmission."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:RushanJinri a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Jahre, Monate und Tage, an denen man nicht ins Gebirge geht"@de,
        "The years, months and days on which one may not enter the mountains"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 17 (Dengshe), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 17" ;
    skos:definition "Three calendars laid over one another. The third and the ninth month are the months in which the mountain opens, and within them a good day and hour is to be chosen. Each of the five peaks is closed in the years and months of the phase that governs it: not the eastern peak in a jia, yi, yin or mao year in the first or second month; not the southern in a bing, ding, si or wu year in the fourth or fifth; not the western in a geng, xin, shen or you year in the seventh or eighth; not the central in a wu or ji year in the last month of a season; not the northern in a ren, gui, hai or zi year in the tenth or eleventh, and the ban covers every side of the peak in question. Then a list of forbidden days by month, and a second list of good ones. Then a third list, from two named works, of six days in a long month and five in a short one. The consequence is stated for the last: whoever enters the mountains on such a day will assuredly be tested by the spirit of the mountain, will not obtain what he sought and will not accomplish what he set out to do; and not only the practitioner but the ordinary man who enters on those days meets with harm and falls in with tigers, wolves and venomous creatures."@en ;
    skos:note "The chapter also names what the calendar is a defence against, and it is not an outcome of the work but the tester: whoever does not know the art of entering meets illness, wounding, fright, lights, strange voices, great trees falling without wind, rocks dropping without cause and men going astray into ravines. The prohibition therefore protects against the mountain and not against a failure of the practice, which distinguishes it from the hours of dead qi in the eighth chapter."@en,
        "The prohibition binds an operation to a date and states what follows from breaking it, which is the shape this graph tracks. What makes it worth more than the shape is that the author declares his own position on it in the next breath, and declares it as uncertainty: the state of heaven and earth and the fortune and misfortune of yin and yang are vast and hard to make out, he says, and he neither insists that there is anything in it nor dares guarantee that there is not. He then gives his reasons for transmitting it anyway, which are the authorities that used it and the fact that the classics have a calendar and that kings keep an office for it. A transmission that hands on a calendrical doctrine and prints its transmitter's suspension of judgement beside it is rare enough in this holding to be recorded."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:MingjingRushan ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BaopuziNeipian ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:SacrificiumNonPeractum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Opfer zu vollbringen begehrend, haben wir es nicht vollbracht"@de,
        "Desirous of accomplishing the sacrifice, we have not accomplished it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Atharvaveda Saṃhitā 6.114 verses 2 and 3, with 6.115 verses 1 and 2, in the translation of W. D. Whitney, revised by C. R. Lanman, Harvard Oriental Series 7, Cambridge, Mass. 1905" ;
    skos:definition "A hymn against disability in sacrifice, which is the one place in the hymns opened where the corpus speaks in its own voice of a rite that did not come off. By right of right, O Ādityas, worshipful ones, release ye us here, in that, O ye carriers of the sacrifice, we, desirous of accomplishing the sacrifice, have not accomplished it. And: sacrificing with what is rich in fat, making oblations of sacrificial butter with the spoon, without desire, to you, O all gods, desirous of accomplishing we have not been able to accomplish. The hymn beside it covers the case where the fault is not known: if knowing, if unknowing, we have committed sins, do ye free us from that."@en ;
    skos:note "The doctrine that would name the cause — that a defective recitation voids the act — was not found in the hymns opened and belongs to the layer above them. That the search was carried and came back empty is recorded at pmp:AtharvanRecitationFaultSearch rather than asserted here."@en,
        "The failure is admitted and it is not explained. The hymn names no cause, no sign by which the shortfall was recognised, and no rule by which a later rite could be told to have worked; what it supplies is a release, and the neighbouring hymn extends the release to faults the performer did not notice. A transmission that provides an expiation for the unnoticed fault has made the unnoticed fault the standing account of a rite that produced nothing."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Atharvavedic .

pmc:SadampuspaYieldsVisum a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das immerblühende Amulett bringt das Sehen des Verborgenen hervor"@de,
        "The ever-flowering amulet brings the seeing of the concealed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Atharvaveda Saṃhitā 4.20, in the translation of W. D. Whitney, revised by C. R. Lanman, Harvard Oriental Series 7, Cambridge, Mass. 1905" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Sadampuspa ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Atharvavedic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:OccultaVidere .

pmc:SakhyaRatiIntensification a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die fünf aufsteigenden Stufen der Freundesliebe"@de,
        "The five rising stages of love as friendship"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Rūpa Gosvāmin, Bhaktirasāmṛtasindhu 3.3.106 (trans. Haberman 2003)" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that love in the mode of friendship passes through five increasingly higher stages: friendship itself, attachment, supreme love, tenderness, and passion."@en ;
    skos:note "Given in the text as increasingly higher, so the ordering is asserted and the direction is stated. The series is named for one relation only; the edition read notes that a longer series of the expansions of love stands in the Ujjvalanīlamaṇi, which was not opened here."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GaudiyaVaisnava .

pmc:SamadhiIsWithdrawnAbsorption a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Sammlung liegt bei abgezogener Vertiefung"@de,
        "Concentration is placed at withdrawn absorption"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.7-I.11 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:withdrawnAbsorption ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:Samadhi ;
    pm:situationGround "The handbook names concentration as the condition of the direct knowledges and develops it as the four absorptions, which it describes as awake and turned from the senses to a single object. The coordinate is read off that development and not off the word itself; concentration in a weaker sense would not carry it."@en .

pmc:SamadhiPresupposesPalibodhaChedana a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Übung setzt voraus, dass die Hemmnisse abgeschnitten sind"@de,
        "The training presupposes that the impediments have been severed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga III.43, Pali PTS [93] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, p. 108; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga III.45, Pali PTS [94] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, pp. 109-110; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:note "The strength is set at stated necessity on the two items that carry a consequence, and it is worth saying that the other eight do not. For those the handbook prescribes the severing and says nothing about what follows from omitting it, which under SPEC §3.5 would be a prescribed order and not a necessity. They are not entered as eight further nodes at a weaker strength, because the handbook states them as one list with one instruction; what is entered instead is the list at pmc:DasaPalibodha and the qualification that most of them are relations and not properties."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "For two of the ten the handbook states the cost in the operative passage. Of the gains: from sunrise to the first watch of the night there is no interval in the coming and going, and he gets no opportunity to do the duties of an ascetic. Of building work: he falls into continual anxiety over what has been done and what has not been done. The first is a loss of the time the training needs; the second is the state of mind the training is to replace."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Samadhi ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:PalibodhaChedana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:SamadhiPresupposesSenasana a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Übung setzt eine Unterkunft ohne die benannten Fehler voraus"@de,
        "The training presupposes a lodging without the named faults"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.3, Pali PTS [119] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, p. 139; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.5, Pali PTS [119] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, p. 139; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:note "The strength is stated necessity and the dilapidated monastery is the reason it can be set that high. It is the one fault of the eighteen for which the handbook gives no releasing condition, and its stated consequence is the loss of the meditation subject, which is the thing the whole chapter is about."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Two of the eighteen faults are stated with their consequence in the operative passage. Of a dilapidated monastery: the meditation subject of the one who makes the repairs suffers. Of a large one: the duties are left undone, and the man who then does them goes late into the village and gets nothing because the almsgiving is over, while leaving them undone is an offence of wrongdoing in its own right. Both name a cost that falls on the training itself and not on the man's standing."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Samadhi ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:SenasanaPancanga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:SamanFromSramana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Wort selbst kommt von außen"@de,
        "The word itself comes from outside"@en ;
    dcterms:source "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXIII, sect. 98, pp. 276-277, sect. 99, pp. 277-278, and sect. 100, p. 284" ;
    skos:definition "The term saman is mentioned by the Chinese at the beginning of the twelfth century as a Nuichen word for sorcerer which they did not recognise as their own san-men, both being traced to the same source, sramana. The recorder proposes that wandering sramana, changed into samana, were transformed into the Nuichen and Manchu saman."@en ;
    skos:note "Beside it stands a second borrowing that needs no reconstruction: burkan, the term applied to a great number of spirits across the Tungus groups, is a Mongol word and a modification of Buddha, and where Buddha himself is mastered the ordinary word for a shaman's spirit is used of him."@en,
        "The etymology and the second sentence are of different weights and are kept apart here. That the Chinese record the word in that century, and that both forms are referred to sramana, is what the passage reports; that wandering monks were themselves the first bearers is the recorder's proposal and is his."@en,
        "This is the node for which the file exists. A category the whole comparative literature uses is named after a word its own bearers took from a Buddhist term for a wandering ascetic, in a transmission whose bearers say the art came from their neighbours. Whoever cites the Siberian case as the archaic form is citing the station of a transmission, not its source."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SiberianShamanism .

pmc:SamyamaHoldsOwnAgency a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Saṃyama behält die eigene Trägerschaft"@de,
        "Saṃyama holds its own agency"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.1-4" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:ownAgency ;
    pm:situatedNode pmp:Samyama ;
    pm:situationGround "The base text has the practitioner bind his own mind and hold it, and the capacities of the third chapter are said to follow from his doing so. Nothing in these sūtras has another speak or act through him; what is claimed is credited to the practice."@en .

pmc:SamyamaIsByEffort a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Saṃyama wird durch Anstrengung erlangt"@de,
        "Saṃyama is attained by effort"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra II.28",
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.1-4" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:byEffort ;
    pm:situatedNode pmp:Samyama ;
    pm:situationGround "The practice is the deliberate application of three named means to one object, and the text elsewhere makes the arising of knowledge conditional on the wearing away of impurity through the practice of the limbs. Nothing here arrives unworked for."@en .

pmc:SamyamaIsInnerToThePrecedingLimbs a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die drei inneren Glieder sind innerlicher als die vorhergehenden fünf"@de,
        "The three inner limbs are inner to the preceding five"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.7",
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.8" ;
    skos:note "The base text ranks the last three limbs as inner with respect to the preceding five, and then ranks even those three as outer with respect to the seedless absorption. It sets a relation of inner and outer, not a rule that one cannot be attempted before the other, and it says nothing about what follows from disregarding the ranking."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:Samyama ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:AstangaLimbs ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PatanjalaYoga .

pmc:SamyamaIsWithdrawnAbsorption a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Saṃyama ist abgezogene Vertiefung"@de,
        "Saṃyama is withdrawn absorption"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.1-4" ;
    skos:note "Numbering. The three members stand at III.1-3 and their combination at III.4 in the received division; the practice node in this file cites III.1-3 alone. See prima-materia#456."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:withdrawnAbsorption ;
    pm:situatedNode pmp:Samyama ;
    pm:situationGround "The three members are defined in sequence as the binding of the mind to one place, an unbroken flow of attention to that same place, and the same again with the object alone shining forth; the fourth sūtra names the three taken together on one object as saṃyama. That is the criterion this value states — awake, the senses off their objects, attention held on a single one — given in the base text rather than inferred from the capacities. The withdrawal of the senses is a separate limb preceding the three."@en .

pmc:SamyamaLocusNotStated a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Saṃyama gibt keinen Ort an"@de,
        "Saṃyama does not state a locus"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.1-4" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:locusNotStated ;
    pm:situatedNode pmp:Samyama ;
    pm:situationGround "The four sūtras were read for this question and are silent on it: they place the attention on an object and say nothing about where the awareness itself is held to be. That the collection has one capacity of displaced locus at III.38 does not settle the operator, which is applied to near and distant objects alike."@en .

pmc:SamyamaYieldsDivyaCaksus a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Saṃyama auf die Sonne bringt Weltkenntnis hervor"@de,
        "Saṃyama on the sun yields knowledge of the world"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.26" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Samyama ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PatanjalaYoga ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:DivyaCaksus .

pmc:SamyamaYieldsParakayaPravesa a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Saṃyama bringt den Eintritt in einen anderen Körper hervor"@de,
        "Saṃyama yields entry into another body"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.38 (Sanskrit sūtra text; received four-pāda numbering)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Samyama ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PatanjalaYoga ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ParakayaPravesa .

pmc:SanhedrinCalfCreatedAndEatenClaimed a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Schoepfungslehre soll das Kalb hervorbringen"@de,
        "The laws of formation are said to bring forth the calf"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Synhedrin 67b, the third class of Abaye's division, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. VII (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1903), p. 287" ;
    skos:definition "The pairing the source makes: occupying oneself with the laws of formation brings forth the calf that is then eaten. Entered as a node of its own because the claim is that a study produces an animal, and that is what a protocol would have to address."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:SanhedrinLawsOfFormationBeforeTheSabbath ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliSanhedrin ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:SanhedrinCalfCreatedAndEaten .

pmc:SanhedrinFieldFilledAndGatheredClaimed a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Wort soll das Feld fuellen und leeren"@de,
        "One word is said to fill and to empty the field"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Synhedrin 68a, R. Eliezer on his deathbed, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. VII (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1903), p. 289" ;
    skos:definition "The pairing the source makes: the single utterance brings forth the field of cucumbers, and a second utterance gathers them. The claim is reported by the operator about himself, in the hour of his death, to witnesses who are named."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:SanhedrinOneWordOverTheField ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliSanhedrin ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:SanhedrinFieldFilledAndGathered .

pmc:SantaAsSourceOfAllRasa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Ruhe als Quelle der übrigen ästhetischen Zustände"@de,
        "Calm as the source of the other aesthetic states"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Abhinavagupta, Abhinavabhāratī, on Nāṭyaśāstra VI" ;
    skos:definition "The commentator's claim that the calm state is not one aesthetic state beside the others but their source: the various feelings arise out of calm through their particular causes, and melt back into calm when those causes fall away."@en ;
    skos:note "Attestation is mediated and the reason is the language and not the reachability. The critical edition of the commentary is digitised and openly available, and so is the edition of the rival position named in prima-materia#363; the passage below was read in the English rendering of a study that prints and translates it. The mediated mode is therefore an open posting to be raised as soon as someone reads the Sanskrit, not a statement that the work cannot be had."@en,
        "The base treatise arranges its states by derivation, deity and colour and ranks none of them. This claim is the commentator's and belongs to him, which is why it stands as its own node and not as a property of the treatise."@en,
        "The reason it matters for this graph. It is a claim about the top of an order and not about an order running through: it makes one state the ground of the rest on metaphysical grounds, and it does not place unlike states on a common measure. Anyone citing it as evidence that the transmission ranks aesthetic states on a scale is reading it for something it does not say."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "J. L. Masson and M. V. Patwardhan, Śāntarasa and Abhinavagupta's Philosophy of Aesthetics (Bhandarkar Oriental Series 9, Poona: Bhandarkar Oriental Research Institute, 1969), where the passage is printed and translated" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SanskritDramaturgy .

pmc:SarTorah a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sar ha-Tora, der Fürst der Tora"@de,
        "Sar ha-Torah, the Prince of the Torah"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hekhalot Rabbati, chapter 31, trans. Morton Smith, corrected by Gershom Scholem, ed. Don Karr" ;
    skos:altLabel "Sar Torah"@en ;
    skos:definition "The angelic power under whose name the Law is said to be obtained. The transmission gives its own reason for needing him: Israel's understanding was obscured by the exile and the words of the Law became hard as brass and iron, and what the world lacks is this secret and this mystery which is not in it. The remedy is offered as a means of loftiness by which the people are to raise themselves."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HekhalotRabbati ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hekhalot .

pmc:SarTorahExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Das ohne Studium erlangte Gesetz"@de,
        "The Law obtained without study"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hekhalot Rabbati, chapters 30 and 31, trans. Morton Smith, corrected by Gershom Scholem, ed. Don Karr" ;
    skos:definition "A protocol against the claim that the seal replaces study. The claim is unusually well placed for examination and the transmission itself supplies the reason: what it promises is not an inner state but a public performance before witnesses who can judge it. A man who sits in an academy and expounds what is forbidden and permitted either holds up under that or does not, and the people around him are competent to say which."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at procedure without cases, and the reason is that the passages carrying the numbers were not opened. The regime is said elsewhere in this literature to run forty days with immersions counted by the day, and to be annulled from the first day by a single lapse of purity; none of that was read at the wording here and none of it stands in this file. See prima-materia#496."@en,
        "This is the rarest thing in the collection: a claim about an acquired capacity whose success condition the tradition itself locates outside the practitioner, in a room full of people qualified to contradict him. Most capacities in this graph are reported by the one who has them."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, protocol stated from the witness; no case series has been collected" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A recorded case of a man who completed the rite as the transmission prescribes it and could not afterwards expound what is forbidden and permitted better than before, judged by the academy the transmission itself makes the judge. The claim is that toil is replaced, so an outcome indistinguishable from the untrained falls due against it." ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HekhalotRabbati ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:TorahWithoutToil .

pmc:SarielTeachesCourseOfTheMoon a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Sariel offenbart den Lauf des Mondes"@de,
        "Sariel discloses the course of the moon"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:CourseOfTheMoon ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Sariel ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:SarielTeachesSignsOfTheMoon a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Sariel offenbart die Zeichen des Mondes"@de,
        "Sariel discloses the signs of the moon"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)" ;
    skos:note "Given under the name Sahriel; stands in the position the Ethiopic fills with the course of the moon."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:SignsOfTheMoon ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Sariel ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:SatesuSahassesuVaEko a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Nur einer unter Hunderten oder Tausenden, siebenmal hintereinander gesagt"@de,
        "Only one among hundreds or thousands, said at seven thresholds in a row"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.10 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, p. 435; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.8 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, pp. 434-435; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "Between the statement that the fourteenfold mastery cannot be skipped and the exception made for those of great past endeavour, the handbook runs one sentence seven times over. The preliminary work of the device is a burden to a beginner, and only one in a hundred or a thousand can do it; to one who has done it the production of the sign is a burden, and only one in a hundred or a thousand can do it; and so through the extending of the sign and the attaining of absorption, the taming of the mind in the fourteen ways, the transformation by the power itself, the rapid inducing of absorption, and finally the giving of protection to another, which is the seventh and stands a little apart from the other six."@en ;
    skos:note "The sentence is not a discouragement and does not read as one in its place. It stands in a passage arguing that the beginner must be taken through the fourteen ways like gold heated soft by a goldsmith or clay ground fine by a potter, and the two elders named in the middle of it are told to go about with their abilities perfected as soldiers go about with clean weapons. The numbers are there to say that the course is long, and the transmission is not counting anybody."@en,
        "This is the only place in the whole handbook where a rate of failure is put in numbers. The search that established that stands at pmp:VisuddhimaggaAttritionElsewhereSearch: the phrase occurs seven times in twenty-three chapters and all seven are here."@en,
        "Why it is worth a node. A transmission that states its own rate of attrition has said something that could in principle be checked against a population, and almost nothing in this holding does. What this one says, if the seven thresholds are taken one after another as the text takes them, is smaller than any population the transmission had; and the text plainly does not mean it that way. That tension is the finding and it is not resolved here."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:SattvikaBhava a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Unwillkürliche Körperzustände"@de,
        "Involuntary bodily states"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Nāṭyaśāstra VII" ;
    skos:definition "Eight bodily signs held to arise from the inner constitution rather than from will: paralysis, perspiration, horripilation, change of voice, trembling, change of colour, tears, and fainting."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SanskritDramaturgy .

pmc:SecondDegreeYieldsDistantTouch a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der zweite Grad bringt das Fernberühren hervor"@de,
        "The second degree yields touching at a distance"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 168" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:MideInitiationSecondDegree ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:MideDistantTouch .

pmc:SecondDegreeYieldsForesight a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der zweite Grad bringt den Blick in das Künftige hervor"@de,
        "The second degree yields the look into futurity"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 168" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:MideInitiationSecondDegree ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:MideForesight .

pmc:SecondWeekPresupposesFirst a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die zweite Woche setzt die erste voraus"@de,
        "The second week presupposes the first"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ignatius of Loyola, Spiritual Exercises 4" ;
    skos:note "The same annotation that sets the order removes any fixed duration from it: the weeks need not have seven or eight days, and one who needs longer in the first is to be given longer. The order is set, the schedule is not."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:SecondWeek ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:FirstWeek ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChristianAsceticDiscernment .

pmc:SeizureTakenAsCall a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein plötzlicher Anfall gilt als Ruf ins Amt"@de,
        "A sudden seizure is taken as a call to the office"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III" ;
    skos:definition "When a man or woman is struck by a sudden illness, loses judgement and is left as if mad, the senior ministers say that this has come because the huacas want him for their villac and priest; and on his coming to himself they make him fast and learn the office. The ground given is that they themselves lose judgement when they speak with the huacas. From this all who fall in fits are counted as chosen for these ministries."@en ;
    skos:note "The transmission gives its own explanation of the sign, and the explanation is an inference from the practitioner's state back to the novice's: the office is recognised by the symptom that the office produces. The recorder adds his own competing explanation, that the loss of judgement comes from the devil or from the strength of the chicha they drink before speaking with the huaca. Both are recorded; neither is the graph's."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmc:SelfRememberingAnchoring a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Selbsterinnern wird im äußeren Wachen ausgeführt"@de,
        "Self-remembering is performed in outward waking"@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:outwardWaking ;
    pm:situatedNode pmp:SelfRemembering ;
    pm:situationGround "The source sets the exercise in the middle of ordinary activity and reports it while walking a street, entering a shop and telephoning; withdrawal is refused rather than unmentioned, since the whole classification of ways in this teaching turns on not leaving life."@en .

pmc:SelfRememberingLocus a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Das Bewusstsein bleibt beim Selbsterinnern im Körper"@de,
        "Awareness stays in the body during self-remembering"@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:embodiedLocus ;
    pm:situatedNode pmp:SelfRemembering ;
    pm:situationGround "The one arrowhead of attention stays on the observed thing and the second turns on the observer, who is walking and speaking where he stands; nothing in the account has the practitioner perceive from a place other than his body."@en .

pmc:SelfRememberingPresupposesNonIdentification a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Selbsterinnern setzt das Nicht-Identifizieren voraus"@de,
        "Self-remembering presupposes not identifying"@en ;
    dcterms:source "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching, chapter eight, at the passage naming identifying the chief obstacle to self-remembering (one-volume English text as scanned in the Internet Archive full-text mirror; edition left unstated)" ;
    skos:note "This is the step that was missing while the graph held only the diagnosis. pmc:IdentifyingTakenForItsOpposite records that the state to be undone is regularly valued under names such as enthusiasm and inspiration; this node records what the same source prescribes against it, and names the exercise the diagnosis exists for."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "One who identifies with anything is unable to remember himself, and remains the slave of whatever can happen to him."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:SelfRemembering ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:IdentifyingTakenForItsOpposite ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:FourthWay .

pmc:SelfRememberingYieldsTheThirdState a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Selbsterinnern bringt den dritten Zustand hervor"@de,
        "Self-remembering brings about the third state"@en ;
    dcterms:source "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching, chapters seven and eight, at the definition of the third state and at the account of memory and self-remembering (one-volume English text as scanned in the Internet Archive full-text mirror; edition left unstated)" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that the divided attention of self-remembering is the third state of consciousness itself, that it occurs in ordinary life only as rare flashes, and that it can be made more or less lasting by deliberate training."@en ;
    skos:note "A second capacity is claimed in the same passage and is stated separately here because it is the one that could be counted: that memory retains only those moments in which a person remembered himself, and that the poverty of ordinary recollection follows from that. The recorder tests it against his own earliest memories and reports that they are moments of self-remembering. That is one case, reported by the person who formed the claim."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:SelfRemembering ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:FourthWay ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:FourStatesOfConsciousness .

pmc:SelfWarningStandsInsideThePrescription a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Warnung vor einer Praxis steht in der Stelle, die sie vorschreibt"@de,
        "The warning against a practice stands inside the passage prescribing it"@en ;
    skos:note "A finding of the opposite shape already stands in the holding and is not a counterexample to this one, because it concerns a different thing: prima-materia#640 records seven opened works carrying twelve announced signs of success and not one condition of failure. Signs of success and self-directed cautions are counted separately for the reason SPEC §15 gives, and a tradition can be generous with the first while saying nothing under the second. What this generalization claims is narrower and about the caution alone."@en,
        "Fourth witness, added on 2026-09-30 from the Rosicrucian manifestos, and it is the first in which the wrongly valued thing is the one the readership came for. The Fama states that men of discretion nowadays hold the transmutation of metals to be the highest point and fastigium in philosophy, and testifies publicly in the same passage that the true philosophers esteem little the making of gold, which is but a parergon, quoting the founder: aurum nisi quantum aurum. The warning is inside the work that announces the art and not in a commentary, and the Confessio adds an order rather than withdrawing it — the gift is in no manner to be despised, but excellent wits ought not to be drawn to the tincture of metals before they be exercised well in the knowledge of nature."@en,
        "It is falsified by a transmission that prescribes such a practice, whose earliest witness carries no warning against the practitioner's own approval, and where the warning is demonstrably first added by a commentator or a later recension. One such case refutes it. The candidates are not hard to name: the tantric material has a heavy commentarial layer, and whether a given caution belongs to the base text or to its readers is exactly the question the editions were read for in prima-materia#45."@en,
        "It satisfies the statement on the narrowest reading of it. The warning is against the very thing the practice was for, matter; it stands inside the prescribing passage and not in a commentary; and it is in the mouth of the writer who gave the prescription, a few lines after he gave it. What it adds is the placement: the earlier two witnesses warn against valuing something that occurs along the way, this one warns at the moment the practitioner judges herself finished, which is the one moment at which no further instruction was expected."@en,
        "No pm:Testing points at this node, and that is its position in the queue rather than a defect. Two attested claims is the floor; a third witness with no route to either would raise it out of the range where a shared physiology or a common institutional form could account for the agreement on its own."@en,
        "The two claims it is formed from have no route between them and say the same thing about their own practitioners. The Fourth Way holds that in identifying a person is absorbed into what occupies him and becomes a thing, and that those who observe the state in themselves regularly take it for a merit, calling it enthusiasm, zeal, passion or inspiration. The Mahānirvāṇa, in the verses prescribing the five elements of the heroic rite, says in the same breath that the men of this age, being greedy and lustful, will neglect the discipline, drink for the pleasure of it and lose the distinction of right and wrong."@en,
        "Third witness, added on 2026-09-27, and it moves the statement from two transmissions to three with no route proposed between the third and either of the first two. The Final Reckoning of Zosimos prescribes a course of conduct to a named addressee — stay at your hearth, rest the body, calm the passions, resist desire, pleasure, anger, grief and the twelve fatalities of death — says that operating thus she will obtain the fitting, authentic and natural tinctures, and then, in the next sentence of the same paragraph, warns her: but when you recognise that you have arrived at perfection, then fear the intervention of the natural elements of matter."@en,
        "Three near-misses from a fourth transmission, read at the wording on 2026-09-29 and recorded here rather than added to the derivation, because each of them fails the statement on a different word of it and together they show which word needs deciding. The Theravāda handbook warns three times against what it itself teaches. At pmc:IddhiPannayaPalibodha it tells the aspirant in its third chapter to cut off the supernormal powers as an impediment for insight, and devotes its twelfth and thirteenth chapters to them: that is a warning against valuing the thing wrongly, which the statement asks for, but it does not stand in the prescribing passage. At pmc:AnapanasatiDukkara and pmc:DibbacakkhuParipantha the warning does stand in the prescribing passage — the breathing subject is declared no field for inferior persons in the middle of the longest exposition the work gives it, and the divine eye is called a danger to the average man in the paragraph after the one describing how it is obtained — but neither warns against valuing it wrongly. One says the aspirant is probably not equal to it and the other that it will frighten him into madness."@en,
        "What makes it worth stating as a claim rather than as an observation is that it excludes something. It says the caution is not an outside correction: a tradition of this kind does not have to be caught by its critics, it catches itself, and it does so at the point of prescribing. A commentary layer supplying the warning afterwards would be a different history — the practice would then have been transmitted innocent and moralised later, which is what the reception literature usually assumes."@en,
        "What that forces, and it is a decision the statement cannot avoid much longer. Either the prescribing passage means the passage, in which case this handbook contributes two self-warnings of the wrong content and none of the right, or it means the same source, in which case the third-chapter warning is a fourth witness and the number of transmissions rises from three to four. The statement was written with the narrower reading and its own note says so — the warning is in the mouth of the writer who gave the prescription, a few lines after he gave it. This file keeps that reading and adds nothing to the derivation. A successor statement that widens it to the same source will be stronger and easier to break, which is the right direction, but it must then say what stops a work of nine hundred pages from counting as one passage."@en,
        "What the fourth witness adds beyond a fourth transmission. The same corpus enacts the warning as a deception of the practitioners who value the wrong thing: on the sixth day of the Chymische Hochzeit the guests not chosen for the last operation are set to work in gold, which the narrator says is indeed a piece of this art but not the most principal, most necessary and best, and are given a part of the ashes so that they should imagine the whole bird had been provided for the sake of gold. The other three witnesses warn the practitioner; this one also shows the transmission arranging matters so that the practitioner who cannot take the warning is kept busy. That is a further step and the statement above does not cover it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:IdentifyingTakenForItsOpposite,
        pmc:Panchatattva,
        pmc:RosicrucianAurumParergon,
        pmc:ZosimosCautioInPerfectione ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission prescribes a practice that the practitioner is liable to value for the wrong reason, the warning against that valuing stands inside the prescribing passage itself, in the same source, and is not supplied by a later commentator or an outside critic."@en .

pmc:SendingOrganProtocol a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der berührende Körperteil einen Unterschied macht"@de,
        "Whether the part in contact makes a difference"@en ;
    skos:definition "An examination of whether contact through the palms differs in its reported or measured effect from contact through another part."@en ;
    skos:note "Procedure without cases, and for the same reason as at pmc:HandSideTransferProtocol: the comparison needs no instrument the field lacks, and nobody has made it. What is absent is the case, not the means."@en,
        "The controls that would have to hold before a difference could be read as a property of the part: which part the practitioner was taught to use, which part the recipient expects, and the difference in ordinary touch between a palm and a forearm. A protocol without them would have measured the training and the expectation."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "Not devised here. The falsifying condition is the ordinary comparison the claim invites by naming one part and excluding the rest" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "Recipients blinded to the part in contact report or show no difference between contact through the palm and contact through another part beyond chance."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:PalmsAreTheSendingOrgan .

pmc:SerialReadingOfTheEightLimbs a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die serielle Lesart der acht Glieder"@de,
        "The serial reading of the eight limbs"@en ;
    dcterms:date "20th century" ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra II.29" ;
    skos:definition "The reading of the eight limbs as a ladder that must be climbed in order, each limb a precondition of the next."@en ;
    skos:note "The inner and outer categories come from the commentarial layer, and the hardening of the ranking into a rule of practice belongs to twentieth-century yoga pedagogy. Recorded here so that the reading is available as what it is. The base text at II.29 enumerates and does not order."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:compiledBy "The commentarial tradition, sharpened in twentieth-century yoga pedagogy" ;
    pm:ordersClaim pmc:AstangaLimbs .

pmc:SevenCentreQualityMapping a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Zuordnung von sieben Zentren zu Eigenschaften"@de,
        "The seven-centre quality mapping"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1927" ;
    dcterms:source "A. Avalon, The Serpent Power (1919)",
        "C. W. Leadbeater, The Chakras (1927)",
        "Christopher Hills, Nuclear Evolution (1977)",
        "Ken Dychtwald, Bodymind (1977)" ;
    skos:definition "The assignment of a fixed set of seven centres to spectral colours and to psychological qualities such as will, expression and security. An ordering imposed in the twentieth century on material that did not carry it."@en ;
    skos:note "Colour scheme in Leadbeater 1927, rainbow spectrum in Hills 1977, on the textual basis made available by Avalon's 1919 translation of the Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa. Recorded here so that the mapping is available in the graph as what it is, and cannot be mistaken for the tantric material it was built on."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:compiledBy "C. W. Leadbeater, extended by Christopher Hills and Ken Dychtwald" ;
    pm:ordersClaim pmc:Cakra .

pmc:ShamanizingYieldsKnowledgeOfTheHidden a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Schamanisieren bringt das Wissen um das Verborgene hervor"@de,
        "Shamanizing brings the knowledge of what is hidden"@en ;
    dcterms:source "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXVII, sect. 121, pp. 331-332" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:TungusShamanizing ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SiberianShamanism ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:TungusKnowledgeOfTheHidden .

pmc:ShamanizingYieldsRelief a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Schamanisieren bringt Befreiung der Befallenen hervor"@de,
        "Shamanizing brings relief to those afflicted"@en ;
    dcterms:source "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXX, sect. 140, p. 381" ;
    skos:note "The claim the whole institution rests on, and the one on which the source is least specific: it names no duration, no rate and no case in which the relief failed to come. That silence is the reason no protocol is written on it here."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:TungusShamanizing ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SiberianShamanism ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:TungusReliefOfSpiritAffliction .

pmc:ShamsielTeachesSignsOfTheSun a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Schamsiel offenbart die Zeichen der Sonne"@de,
        "Shamsiel discloses the signs of the sun"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:SignsOfTheSun ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Shamsiel ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:ShangqingCheshi a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Sehen und Hören über die Reichweite hinaus, und die Klausel für die ersten Versuche"@de,
        "Seeing and hearing beyond the range, and the clause that covers the first attempts"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 9.11b-9.12a (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 9",
        "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 9.5a-9.5b (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 9" ;
    skos:definition "Lying with the eyes closed, the breath made faint and the body settled so that a person beside one does not notice, the practitioner looks and listens inward to the four quarters and sets eye and ear ten thousand li beyond the body; long practice is said to bring sight of what is ten thousand li away, and concentrated practice sight of what is a million li away, with the ears constantly hearing the sound of metal and jade and of strings and pipes. A second passage gives the term for a related working of the eyes and ears: about a year for them to become acute, longer for sight through a thousand li."@en ;
    skos:note "The passage states its own null result in advance and disposes of it. It says that when this is first done there is really nothing, not even anything vague, and that with long persistence one enters the marvel of itself. That sentence is the whole of what the transmission offers a practitioner who finds nothing, and it is the reason this claim and the one above it are not of the same kind, although they stand four folios apart in the same section."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ShangqingCunsi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ShangqingCheshiCapacity .

pmc:ShangqingDanshi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die zwei, die das Elixier nahmen und starben, und die zwei, die daraufhin ablehnten"@de,
        "The two who took the elixir and died, and the two who then refused"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 5.8a-5.8b (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 5" ;
    skos:definition "Four men of the later Han are said to have studied in the Wangwu mountains for more than forty years and to have compounded the divine elixir together. The first took it and died; the second took it and died also. The remaining two, seeing this, did not dare to take it, abandoned the mountain and went home. Afterwards they saw the two dead men on the mountain, were astonished, asked them for the Way, were given a preparation of poria, and are said to be still alive after several hundred years, walking in the marchmounts. The corpus adds the verdict: these men knew that the divine elixir obtains the Way and did not perceive that the trial lay within it, and are therefore land-bound transcendents only, with no further hope of ascending to heaven."@en ;
    skos:note "It stands among five further cases in the same section and is not an outlier there. One man studied for four hundred years, thrice compounded the elixir, had it spoiled by a malign thing, and has not ascended because he failed too many trials. Another was bitten by a great serpent he had taken for a Perfected and lost his left eye to a demon, and his want of singleness of purpose is given as the cause. Another passed a hundred and forty trials and rose. The set is a doctrine of failure worked out in examples."@en,
        "The one passage in this holding where a transmission reports the observation that would refute it and scores the correct inference from that observation as the failure. Two deaths were seen; the survivors drew the conclusion a bystander would draw; and the drawing of it is what costs them the higher rank. Nothing is said about the deaths themselves except that they were not deaths."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:ShangqingEmengSannian a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Drei Jahre des Traumritus, und Träume, die sämtlich Vorschau sind"@de,
        "Three years of the dream rite, and dreams that are all foresight"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 9.16a-9.16b (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 9" ;
    skos:definition "Against bad dreams the corpus prescribes, on waking, treading the point beneath the nose twice seven times with the left hand, striking the teeth twice seven times, and an incantation naming the three cloud-souls and the seven white-souls; and it states the term and the outcome: the vapour that makes bad dreams is shut beneath the three passes, and after three years dreams come only when the spirits respond to a summons, every dream being as clear as a sight of what is to come, with no further ill omen or unlucky imagining."@en ;
    skos:note "Stated with a term, a stated end state and an observable that another person could ask after, which is what puts it in this file. The corpus names the three causes of recurrent bad dreams at the head of the same passage, so the claim has a doctrine behind it and is not a bare promise."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ShangqingCunsi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ShangqingMengjianCapacity .

pmc:ShangqingErshisishen a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die vierundzwanzig Geister, und was denen versagt bleibt, die sie nicht halten"@de,
        "The four and twenty spirits, and what is refused to those who do not hold them"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 9.2b (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 9" ;
    skos:definition "Citing two named registers, the corpus states that one who does not actualise the four and twenty spirits and does not know the names of the three times eight luminaries may not become a subject of the Great Peace and may not become a servant of the Later Sage."@en ;
    skos:note "The consequence is stated as an exclusion from a future dispensation rather than as an injury, which is the weaker of the two forms this corpus uses but is still a stated consequence and not a bare instruction. The passage stands inside a set of answers to questions the elder Xu had put, so the prerequisite is given as a reply to a practitioner and not as a rubric."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "One may not become a subject of the Great Peace and may not become a servant of the Later Sage." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ShangqingXuedao ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ShangqingCunsi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:ShangqingFangshiJisi a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Der Beischlaf während des Bewahrens des Dunklen und des Weißen"@de,
        "Lying together during the keeping of the dark and the white"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 10.2b (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 10" ;
    skos:definition "Of the keeping of the dark and the white the corpus states the prohibitions with their weights: the flesh of the six domestic animals and the five pungent flavours are especially forbidden, a separate sleeping place and quiet thought are required, and of lying together it says that it is especially to be avoided and that lying together means death at once."@en ;
    skos:note "Kept because of the form of the consequence. Most prohibitions in this corpus state a loss of merit, an interval of reckoning or an impediment; this one states immediate death, which is the strongest consequence the work attaches to breaking a rule of practice and is stated without qualification or interval."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ShangqingShouxuanbai ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:ShangqingFangyaoDijie a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Leiter der Methoden, und wovon jede ohne das andere nichts taugt"@de,
        "The ladder of the methods, and what each is worthless without"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 5.11b (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 5" ;
    skos:definition "The corpus sets its methods in a graded order and states the failure at each step. One who eats the drugs of plants and trees without knowing the chamber arts and the circulating of the breath and the guiding and pulling takes the drugs to no benefit. One who knows only the chamber arts, the guiding and pulling and the circulating of the breath, and does not know the method of the divine elixir, likewise does not obtain transcendence. One who obtains the golden liquor and the divine elixir requires no other art and becomes a transcendent at once. One who has the Great Cavern scripture requires not even the elixir: read ten thousand times, it suffices."@en ;
    skos:note "The chain is the clearest statement in the work of how it ranks what it teaches, and each link carries its own failure clause, which is why it is entered as a prerequisite and not as a classification. The compiler adds a gloss narrowing the first link: what is meant is that one who only takes the drugs and cultivates without engaging in intercourse will not obtain long life, and that one who abstains altogether and merely takes the drugs can obtain transcendence."@en,
        "The compiler's gloss is itself a disagreement with the sentence it glosses and is kept here rather than in a dispute node, because it turns on the reading of one clause and not on two witnesses to a claim."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Drugs of plants and trees taken without the other arts are said to be of no benefit; the other arts practised without the divine elixir are said not to obtain transcendence." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ShangqingFushi ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ShangqingSongjing ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:ShangqingFeizhenDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Die vier für unecht erklärten Stücke"@de,
        "The four items declared not genuine"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 8.12a (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 8",
        "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 8.14a, compiler's note (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 8" ;
    skos:definition "At the head of the last four items of juan 8 stands the line that what follows is not a true saying. The compiler's note at the end of them states the ground: they are in a different hand, they were obtained later at Dongyang by Lu Xiujing, they do not agree with the other traces, their wording is base and unlike the sense of the Perfected, and he suspects them of being a later man's work. He supports this from their content, observing that one person named in them is not the man he is made out to be and that a phrase in them belongs to a different reckoning."@en ;
    skos:note "The strongest instance of the compiler's criterion in operation, and one of the few places in this holding where a transmission prints matter and declares it spurious in the same breath rather than removing it. A neighbouring case stands at 10.17b-10.18a, where he suspects a sheet of tomb-siting rules to be a later fabrication built on a remark made earlier in the same corpus."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:TaoHongjingZhenwei ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:ShangqingFuyaoWuDao a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Vom Tod sprechen nach dem Einnehmen"@de,
        "Speaking of death after taking the drug"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 9.13b (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 9" ;
    skos:definition "The corpus prescribes that the drug of the transcendents be taken facing the quarter of one's natal cyclical sign and that after taking it one not speak of death, mourning or ill matters; and it states the consequence in one clause: to do so injures the embryo and harms the spirits, and the drug is then taken in vain and without benefit."@en ;
    skos:note "The clause is general, unbounded in time and attached to something the practitioner does daily without noticing. Anything that fails after the drug has been taken can be brought under it, and nothing in the passage limits how long after the taking it reaches."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ShangqingFushi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:ShangqingGongman a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Verdienstrechnung, und was auf Sohn und Enkel fällt"@de,
        "The merit reckoning, and what falls to the son and the grandson"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 5.16b-5.17a (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 5" ;
    skos:definition "Merit accumulated to a full thousand obtains transcendence even where there are faults; merit of three hundred, where the faults cannot be offset against it, obtains transcendence for the son; merit of two hundred, for the grandson. One without fault and also without merit obtains it on the strength of forebears' merit, which the corpus calls the surplus left over by those before. One without resolve and with many faults may become rich and honoured but is not to hope for transcendence."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as the corpus's own account of what becomes of a run that produced nothing in the practitioner's lifetime. The quantity is exact, the offsetting is explicit, and the outcome is displaced by one or two generations, so that the absence of any result in the person who did the work is provided for inside the doctrine. This is the same office that the trials perform elsewhere in the same section, reached by arithmetic instead of by narrative."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ShangqingJigong ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ShangqingYunjiaCapacity .

pmc:ShangqingHeyaoBujing a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Schuld auf das Rezept schieben"@de,
        "Blaming the recipe"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 10.3b-10.4a (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 10" ;
    skos:definition "In a passage of instructions on compounding, the corpus states that those who compound the drug without precision do not lay the blame on themselves but instead accuse the recipe of not working, and asks whether such a man can be said to be at fault; the reply is that he may be set to copying recipes rather than compounding them."@en ;
    skos:note "The transmission states the rule of assignment explicitly and in the first person: imprecision in the operator, not defect in the prescription. It states it, moreover, in the course of correcting the quantities of a particular preparation, so the rule is applied and not merely announced."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:ShangqingHuangguanzi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Neunundvierzig Jahre der Praxis der anderen Überlieferung, und was davon gesagt wird"@de,
        "Nine and forty years of the other tradition's practice, and what is said of them"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 5.7b-5.8a (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 5" ;
    skos:definition "A man called Huang Guanzi is said to have loved the Way from youth and to have served the Buddhist Way, bowing and knocking his head every morning and begging for long life, for nine and forty years; then to have taken to ingesting drugs, entered the Jiao mountains, passed the hundred and forty trials set by the Perfected of the Great Ultimate, taken the golden elixir, chanted the Great Cavern scripture and been appointed to a transcendent office. The corpus states the conclusion in five words: this is not what the Buddha is able to bring about."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a stated null result with a term, which is rare anywhere and rarer still about a named rival method. The corpus does not say the other practice is false; it says that nine and forty years of it produced nothing and that what produced the result was the elixir and the scripture. The compiler notes in the same place that this agrees with another passage about a man who served the Buddha."@en,
        "The passage bears on the contact route recorded on the tradition instance and pulls the other way from the borrowing at 6.6a-6.10a. A transmission that reproduces a Buddhist text in the mouths of its own Perfected and in the same juan denies that Buddhist practice achieves anything is doing two things at once, and both are in the file because both were read."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:ShangqingHuangtingBaiershi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Bis hundertzwanzig Jahre, danach das Elixier"@de,
        "Up to a hundred and twenty years, and after that the elixir"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangting neijing yujing 33 (Ganqi zhang) (DZ 331), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "In the chapter on the liver breath the work sets a limit on itself. Up to a hundred and twenty years a man may still be turned back; past that, to hold to the Way is truly very hard, and one must then wait upon the ninefold-cycled and the eight-gem elixir. It adds that one must still return to careful thought and hold the seven origins, and that the blossom of sun and moon rescues what is old and worn."@en ;
    skos:note "The clause also bears on the recitation claim above, and the two are not easy to reconcile. The first chapter promises that the chanting puts off old age and draws out the years without qualification; this chapter says the turning back is possible only up to a stated age. The work does not say which governs."@en,
        "Very little in this holding does what this sentence does. A transmission that names an age past which its own method no longer works, and refers the case to a different method belonging to a different transmission, has stated a boundary that a claim could fall outside. The referral is to the external alchemy, which this graph holds separately at traditions/waidan.ttl, and it is made without polemic."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:ShangqingHuangtingFangzhong a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Das eine, was das Werk dringend nennt"@de,
        "The one thing the work calls urgent"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangting neijing yujing 21 (Qiongshi zhang) (DZ 331), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile",
        "Huangting neijing yujing 22 (Changnian zhang) (DZ 331), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "Long life is most cautious and the chamber is the urgent matter: why act so as to die and make the spirits weep? To make light of it is the land of calamity and the three numina perish. Breathe in the breath and take down the essence, and a plot of a foot may be made to yield a living; but let the sea be breached and the hundred watercourses drained, the leaf goes and the tree withers and loses its green, the breath is gone and the fluid leaks away and the form is no longer one's own. The next chapter repeats it as an order: guard the chamber of the essence urgently and do not let it out at random, close it and treasure it and one may live long."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because the work states the consequence in its own terms rather than as a prohibition with a penalty attached. The form ceasing to be one's own is not a punishment imposed by an office; it is described as what happens to a tree whose root has been cut, which is the same register in which the rest of the work describes the body."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ShangqingHuangtingCunshen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:ShangqingHuangtingJinji a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Der Totenhauch, und das Korn des Bodens"@de,
        "The dead breath, and the grain of the ground"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangting neijing yujing 27 (Xuanyuan zhang) (DZ 331), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile",
        "Huangting neijing yujing 30 (Baigu zhang) (DZ 331), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "Two prohibitions stated as such. The chapter on the mysterious origin names what is most to be avoided: the breath of the dead and all that is defiled and base. The chapter on the hundred grains says that the fruit of the hundred grains is the essence of the soil and that the five flavours, fair on the outside, are the rankness of demons: they foul the spirits and the embryo-breath is emptied out, and how then should one turn back from age and return to the infant, when the three cloud-souls are unsettled and the white-souls crushed? It sets against them the eating of breath and the great harmony, by which one does not die."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ShangqingHuangtingCunshen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:ShangqingHuangtingLinjue a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Den Gott im letzten Atemzug rufen"@de,
        "Calling the god at the last breath"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangting neijing yujing 10 (Xinbu zhang) (DZ 331), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile",
        "Huangting neijing yujing 11 (Ganbu zhang) (DZ 331), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "Two chapters state the same claim of two different gods. Of the heart god the work says that at the point of expiring one calls upon him and even then revives. Of the liver god it says that when one is on the point of expiring, to think of the god brings the dead back to life, and that the cloud-souls are gathered and the white-souls returned so that there is no falling away. A third chapter, of the spleen lord, says that calling one's own name three times makes the god get through of itself."@en ;
    skos:note "The most checkable claim in the work and the one on which no protocol is written here. What would tell against it is a person at the point of death on whom the transmission's own people perform the actualisation and who dies; that is an observation and not an intervention, and it would require watching the dying, which is not a procedure this project devises. The claim is entered with its falsifying condition stated and the protocol left undevised rather than stubbed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ShangqingHuangtingCunshen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ShangqingHuangtingCapacity .

pmc:ShangqingHuangtingShen a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die dreizehn Götter, mit Namen und Beinamen"@de,
        "The thirteen gods, with their names and their courtesy names"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangting neijing yujing 7 (Zhidao zhang) (DZ 331), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile",
        "Huangting neijing yujing 8 (Xinshen zhang) (DZ 331), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "Two closed lists. Of the face and head, seven: the god of the hair, Azure Blossom, courtesy name Great Origin; of the brain, Essence Root, courtesy name Mud Pellet; of the eyes, Bright Above, courtesy name Blossoming Mystery; of the nose, Jade Mound, courtesy name Numinous Firmness; of the ears, Empty Leisure, courtesy name Hidden Field; of the tongue, Penetrating Fate, courtesy name Fivefold Order; of the teeth, Cliff Point, courtesy name Net of Thousands. All seven are subject to the Mud Pellet. Of the viscera, six: of the heart, Cinnabar Origin, courtesy name Guarding the Numen; of the lungs, White Blossom, courtesy name Empty Completion; of the liver, Dragon Smoke, courtesy name Holding Brightness; of the kidneys, Dark Obscurity, courtesy name Nurturing the Infant; of the spleen, Ever Present, courtesy name Resting Cloud-Soul; of the gall bladder, Dragon Radiance, courtesy name Awesome Brightness. The six receptacles and the five depots are said to have their spirit-bodies' essence all within the heart, turning the heavenly warp, and to be held there day and night for long life."@en ;
    skos:note "Every god carries two names, a name and a courtesy name, exactly as a person of the period would. Nothing in the chapter explains why, and the file does not supply a reason; what it records is that a transmission which elsewhere works with impersonal breaths here works with a named household."@en,
        "The count is thirteen and not twelve because the gall bladder is reckoned with the depots, which the chapter says in as many words. It is worth keeping because a roster with a stated membership is the kind of thing a later recension can be checked against, and this one can: the work devotes a further six chapters to the lung, heart, liver, kidney, spleen and gall bladder in turn, each with its own robe, its own outward correspondence and its own effect."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:ShangqingHuangtingWanbian a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Zehntausend Rezitationen, und was die Zahl bringen soll"@de,
        "Ten thousand recitations, and what the count is said to bring"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangting neijing yujing 1 (Shangqing zhang) (DZ 331), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile",
        "Huangting neijing yujing 36 (Muyu zhang) (DZ 331), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "The first chapter states the reward in a single line: chant it ten thousand times and ascend the Three Heavens, a thousand calamities are thereby dispelled and a hundred illnesses cured, one need not fear the ferocity of tiger and wolf, and old age is put off and the years drawn out. The last chapter states the procedure and the completion: recite in the chamber facing east until about ten thousand times, and the sense grows clear of itself; when the tally of readings is finished, the body and the spirits are refined, the Yellow Blossom Jade Maiden tells the practitioner her mind, the Perfected arrive and set the six ding at his service, and the Great Cavern scripture is then conferred on him."@en ;
    skos:note "It also states its own condition of failure in the closing chapter, and the condition is not about the count. Bathing and purity are required, the fat and the pungent are to be put aside, the hair is to be loosened and one is to be without desire; the five flavours are then said to come right and the correct breath to return. A practitioner who reached the count without these has not met the prescription, which is where a null result would be sent."@en,
        "The second witness in this holding to the ten thousand recitations, and it is the work the Zhen'gao prescribes at 9.23b, so the two are not independent. What this one adds is the sitting: ten readings and four bows make one pass, which puts the ten thousand at a thousand sittings and makes the count something a practitioner could keep track of."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ShangqingHuangtingSongjing ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ShangqingHuangtingCapacity .

pmc:ShangqingJiuhuan a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die neun Gebrechen des Lernenden"@de,
        "The nine afflictions of the student"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 5.12a-5.12b (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 5" ;
    skos:definition "The corpus names nine conditions it calls the great sicknesses of a student and says that whoever knows his own affliction is not far from transcendence: to have resolve and no occasion, occasion and no resolve, companions and no resolve, resolve and no enlightened master, a master and no discernment, discernment of the master and no diligence, diligence without keeping to the Way, resolve that is not firm, firmness that does not last. It adds that a man who loves the Way from youth and keeps his mind single needs neither companion nor prompting."@en ;
    skos:note "A catalogue of failure causes in which every item is a property of the student. The list is exhaustive by construction, since resolve, occasion, master, discernment, diligence, constancy and duration between them cover any course that did not succeed, and no item concerns the method, the instructions or the transmission."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ShangqingXuedao ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:ShangqingSanqiShinian a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Zehn Jahre der drei Dünste"@de,
        "Ten years of the three vapours"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 10.1a-10.1b (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 10",
        "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 13.7a (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 13" ;
    skos:definition "Of the three vapours drawn in from beneath the sun the corpus says that after ten years the three colours of vapour are present in the body of themselves and the practitioner thereupon attains transcendence, and it adds a judgement of the method that is unusual in this work: the method is meagre but the thing is much verified."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim stands twice, once in the section of instructions and once in the topographical section where the man it is told of is placed in his posthumous office, and the second telling repeats the verdict that the thing is much verified. The corpus nowhere says what the verification consisted in or who carried it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ShangqingFusanqi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ShangqingSanseqiCapacity .

pmc:ShangqingWeijingDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Die fünfzig auf Bestellung gemachten Rollen"@de,
        "The fifty scrolls made to order"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 19.11b-19.12b (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 19",
        "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 19.12a, compiler's note (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 19" ;
    skos:definition "The compiler relates that a man of talent, envious of the success of another family's fabricated scriptures, obtained the upper Clarity texts by persistence, judged that the highest matter could not be published as it stood, secretly added to and cut from it, ornamented the style, invented further works under the titles named in the received biographies, raised the pledges required for transmission in order to enhance the standing of his teaching, and produced more than fifty pieces in all; that these spread widely, so that new and old were mixed and could not easily be sorted, and that no one who had not seen the genuine writings could tell them apart. He adds that the holder of the genuine texts, impressed by the ornament and the income, came to despise his own copies and had the fabricator's versions copied for himself, so that the two ran in harness and true and false went side by side."@en ;
    skos:note "The compiler does not treat the episode as discrediting the transmission. His note supplies the reconciling clause: the rites of the Way were due to be published while the true wonders might not be broadly circulated, and the fabricator was therefore permitted to do what he did. The failure of the record is thus assigned, like every other failure in this corpus, to an intention that cannot be checked."@en,
        "The second source is the compiler's own note on the same folios, which is a separate testimony and not a gloss. It reports an eyewitness at second hand, a man who travelled by boat with the fabricator from the capital and saw him complete two scrolls of upper Clarity scripture before they had passed the first portage. It further states that the corpus was already mixed before this man: a named person found more than twenty sealed scrolls at Hailing in a year given by its cyclical characters, of which several were not genuine, so that not everything is the fabricator's work, only most of it."@en,
        "What this does to every node in this file is stated here and not left to be inferred. The corpus read is the one the compiler assembled after that mixing, by a criterion of handwriting whose evidence his edition does not reproduce. A citation to this work names a passage in the recension he made, and nothing further; where a stronger claim would be needed it is not made."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:YangxiShouzhen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:ShangqingXianzhibing a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Krankheit vor der Praxis behandeln"@de,
        "Treating the illness before the practice"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 10.17b-10.18b (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 10" ;
    skos:definition "The corpus states that one who would nourish life must first treat his illness, so that the body is not left with depleted blood, diminished marrow or fouled fluids, and that without this first step ingesting drugs and circulating the breath are of no benefit to the body."@en ;
    skos:note "The consequence is not stated abstractly but as a case with a name and a term, and the case is the reason this node is entered as a stated necessity rather than a prescribed order. A practitioner called Wang Zhongfu is said to have drawn in the two luminaries and fed on the aurora for more than forty years and to have felt no benefit at all, while a disciple of his ascended in daylight after eighteen. The Perfected of the Southern Peak then descended and told him the cause: a great illness in his body, his marrow palace diminished and his sinews unfed, so that consorting with the luminaries did his body no good. He treated the illness, practised a further eighteen years, and ascended."@en,
        "The structure is worth stating plainly, because the file turns on it in several places. Forty years of a prescribed practice produced nothing; the prescription was not revised; a defect in the practitioner, not discoverable by him and disclosed only afterwards by an authority, was supplied as the explanation; and the same practice then worked. The passage is at the same time the corpus's one useful clinical rule, which is why it stands here as a prerequisite and not only as a caution."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Ingesting drugs and circulating the breath are said to be of no benefit to the body, and the corpus illustrates this with a practitioner who felt no benefit whatever from more than forty years of the practice because of an illness he did not know he had." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ShangqingFuriyue ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ShangqingFushi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:ShangqingXiedao a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Vom Lernen selbst sprechen"@de,
        "Speaking of the study itself"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 10.8a (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 10",
        "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 15.10a (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 15" ;
    skos:definition "The corpus forbids speaking of studying the Way and states the consequence: where the study is spoken of, the demons of the Way attack the person, the undertaking does not stand, and the speaker falls ill, the reason given being that his body is not yet perfected. The compiler adds that the work says repeatedly that one word or one matter let out cuts the reckoning, not merely brings illness."@en ;
    skos:note "Its bearing on this holding is the same as the clause about speaking of death. A transmission that treats disclosure as a cause of failure has a reason available for every unsuccessful run that does not require the method to be examined, and this one supplies it twice over in adjacent passages."@en,
        "The prohibition reaches the ordinary conduct of a household. The same section forbids letting a person who is not of the Way see one comb one's hair, eat, or put on shoes, on the ground that the corpse-souls of the ordinary man come in at those moments and assail the spirits of the practitioner."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ShangqingXuedao ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:ShangqingXuHuiChaoJing a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Wer das Huangting aus der Überlieferung der Dame Wei abschrieb"@de,
        "Who copied the Huangting out of the Traditions of Lady Wei"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 20.4a (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 20" ;
    skos:definition "The compiler of the Zhen'gao, listing where the autographs of the three men had got to, records that the younger Xu copied the Huangting jing out of the Traditions of Lady Wei, together with some further sheets of received matter, that these were formerly with a man of the Shan mountains, and that after his death they should be with his woman disciple and a fellow student. The ascription is therefore not of the scripture to Lady Wei but of one copy of it to the hand of a named man, out of a named book, with the later whereabouts given."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the sentence that places the work in this file rather than in one of its own. It is an ascription of a copy and not of a composition, and the file does not take it for more: the scripture's own first chapter names the Most High Great Way Lord of the Jade Dawn as its speaker, not Lady Wei."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:ShangqingYeshu a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Nach einem Jahr Reiben bei Nacht schreiben"@de,
        "Writing by night after a year of the rubbing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 9.6b (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 9" ;
    skos:definition "The rubbing regimen performed daily is said to make the eyes clear of themselves, and the corpus states the term and the test together: in one year one can write in the dark. The same passage adds that it may be done unobtrusively among other people and that one should not say what one is doing."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered first among the intervals because of its form. The stated outcome is an ordinary act performed in ordinary conditions, it is dated to a term, and a person who has done the thing for a year either can do it or cannot. The corpus supplies no clause here that would cover the negative case, which distinguishes this claim from most of its neighbours."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ShangqingAnmo ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ShangqingYeshuCapacity .

pmc:ShangqingZhongsongDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Aus welcher Ecke des Grabes die Heimsuchung kommt"@de,
        "Which quarter of the tomb the affliction comes from"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 10.11b-10.12a (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 10",
        "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 10.12a, compiler's note (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 10" ;
    skos:definition "A revealed passage makes recurrent dreams of the north-east and north-west of a grave the sign of a suit brought by the tomb, assigns the north-east to a named calamity of the fate and the north-west to what it calls the nine straits, and prescribes an incantation against it. The compiler's note states that the family in question belongs to a different one of the five tone-classes, so that on the system being used the north-east is not that quarter for them at all, and adds that the term the nine straits does not occur in the burial literature."@en ;
    skos:note "The compiler is not disputing that tombs afflict the living, which he accepts throughout and acts on. He is disputing the correspondence rule, on the ground that it does not come out for the family it was given to and uses a term the technical literature does not have. That is an internal check of a revealed instruction against a body of ordinary craft knowledge, and it fails."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:YangxiShouzhen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:ShangqingZupuDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Ahn, den die Offenbarung nennt und das Register nicht"@de,
        "The ancestor the revelation names and the register does not"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 20.5a (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 20",
        "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 20.5a, compiler's note on the register (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 20" ;
    skos:definition "The revealed text states that the seventh-generation ancestor of the elder Xu bore a given name and courtesy name and had the merit of relieving a famine. The compiler, having the family register before him, states that the seventh-generation ancestor bore a different name and a different courtesy name, that the names do not agree with the revelation, and that the discrepancy is unexplained. He goes on to date the relief from the famine years recorded in the histories and to cite a memorial preserved in a book of Han offices in support of the ancestor's standing, so that the correction is made from documents and not from preference."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because of what is being checked against what. A revelation is here measured against a genealogy the compiler can read, found wanting on a matter of fact, and the disagreement is printed rather than harmonised. He does the same thing on the next folio with a date, where the autograph and the register differ by a year and he states the rule he applies, that the man's own record governs."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:YangxiShouzhen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:ShashengBuqisi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sie kann Lebende töten und keine Toten erwecken"@de,
        "It can kill the living and cannot raise the dead"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 60 (Yu ban), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 9" ;
    skos:definition "The chapter sets the small needle beside the five weapons and says that nothing is greater than the needle except them, that the five weapons are the equipment of death and not the instruments of life. Asked directly whether an instrument that can kill the living and cannot raise the dead might be turned round, the answer is that it cannot: it can kill the living and cannot raise the dead. The chapter compares it to a blade, which can kill a man, and to wine, which makes him drunk, and says that this can be known without examining anybody."@en ;
    skos:note "The asymmetry is stated by the corpus about its own instrument and is not a modern reading of it. It matters for this graph because the same corpus states the killing side with intervals and signs and the healing side with a criterion the operator feels at the needle. The transmission is more exact about what it can destroy than about what it can accomplish, and it says so."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmc:ShemihazahTeachesRootCutting a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Schemichasa offenbart das Wurzelschneiden"@de,
        "Shemihazah discloses root-cutting"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:RootCutting ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Shemihazah ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:ShemihazahTeachesSpellCasting a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Schemichasa offenbart die Beschwörung"@de,
        "Shemihazah discloses spell-casting"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:SpellCasting ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Shemihazah ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:ShenShengGongQiao a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Geist, Weiser, Könner, Geschickter: die vier Stufen des diagnostischen Wissens"@de,
        "Spirit, sage, craftsman, adept: the four grades of diagnostic knowing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Nanjing 61, in Wang Hanlin jizhu Huangdi bashiyi nanjing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 4" ;
    skos:definition "He who knows by looking is called spirit; he who knows by listening is called sage; he who knows by asking is called craftsman; he who knows by feeling the pulse is called adept. The text glosses each in turn: knowing by looking is seeing the five colours and knowing thereby which illness it is; knowing by listening is hearing the five tones and distinguishing the illness by them; knowing by asking is asking after the five flavours he desires and knowing thereby where the illness arose and where it lies; knowing by feeling the pulse is examining at the inch opening and seeing repletion and depletion, and thereby which depot or palace the illness is in. It closes by quoting the classic: to know it from without is called sage, to know it from within is called spirit."@en ;
    skos:note "It stands in the graph beside the Suwen chapter in which the practitioner alone sees where all are looking and the mouth cannot say it. That passage refuses explanation and this one supplies it, in the same corpus and about the same faculty, which is a difference worth having on record before either is used for anything."@en,
        "The grading is the reason for the node and the gloss is the reason it does not decide what a reader might want it to decide. Read as a scale of capacity, the highest grade is a knowing by sight alone and bears the word spirit; read with the gloss the recension prints, the same grade is the reading of complexion, and the commentators supply worked instances — a green complexion in the liver sector, a red one meaning the heart is riding the liver. The recension read here therefore carries both the exalted term and a deflationary explanation of it, side by side, and the graph holds the passage without settling between them."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmc:ShengxianZaiShendan a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Entscheidende im Atem liegt oder im Elixier"@de,
        "Whether the essential lies in the breath or in the elixir"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 4 (Jindan), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 4",
        "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 8 (Shizhi), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 8" ;
    skos:definition "One book holds both sides and the fourth chapter states its own plainly: the way of long life is not in sacrifices and the serving of ghosts and spirits, and not in guiding and pulling and in bending and stretching; the essential of ascending as an immortal is in the divine elixir. It says the same in another form at the outset, that though breathing and guiding-and-pulling and the taking of drugs from grasses and trees may lengthen the years, they do not exempt from death, whereas taking the divine elixir makes a man's span endless and ending only with heaven and earth. And it names a man who went the other way: Wang Tu, a military supervisor of the Wei, who knew nothing of the great drug and wanted to seek immortality by circulating the qi and entering the chamber, and made a book saying the way was complete in that, which the chapter calls a grave misleading of men. The eighth chapter of the same book, held in traditions/daoist.ttl, gives the circulation of the qi its own catalogue of capacities and names it among the essentials."@en ;
    skos:note "It is not a disagreement between schools but between two chapters written by one man, which makes it worth a node rather than a remark. Whoever cites the Baopuzi for the circulation of the qi is citing a book that says elsewhere the circulation does not exempt from death, and whoever cites it for the elixir is citing a book that also teaches the breathing at length."@en,
        "The demotion has a plain material cause and the file records it beside the doctrine. The great drug costs four hundred thousand and the breathing costs nothing. A ranking that puts the expensive operation at the top and the free one in the waiting room is not thereby wrong, but it is the ranking the author himself could not act on."@en,
        "The two chapters can be reconciled, and the fourth does the reconciling itself: the lesser methods are to be used to keep oneself going while the great drug is out of reach, since it is hard to bring off at short notice. That leaves the circulation of the qi as a holding measure and not as a way, which is a demotion the eighth chapter nowhere states."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:XingqiYieldsCapacities ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Waidan .

pmc:ShintoHarahi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Harahi, die Reinigung, die als Buße auferlegt wird"@de,
        "Harahi, the purification that is imposed as a penalty"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Nihongi, Book I, the Age of the Gods, in the English of W. G. Aston, vol. I, London 1896, pp. 45 and 49-50" ;
    skos:definition "The central operation of the transmission, and the chronicle introduces it as a sentence passed on an offender. After the brother's offences against the rice-fields, the new palace and the weaving hall, he is convicted and fined in the articles required for the ceremony of purification: things abhorrent of luck from the tips of his fingers and things abhorrent of calamity from the tips of his toes, white soft offerings of his spittle and blue soft offerings of his nose-mucus. A named deity is then charged with the Great Purification Liturgy and made to recite it, and the offender is banished."@en ;
    skos:note "Two things follow from the passage that a summary loses. The purification is a penalty with a tariff, paid in materials taken from the offender's own body; and the liturgy is not given here — the chronicle says only that it was recited and by whom, and the text of it stands in the work this file lists as unopened. The chronicle closes with an aetiology: this is the reason why the people of the world are careful in the disposal of their own nails."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Nihonshoki ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shinto .

pmc:ShintoImi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Imi, die Enthaltung, die für einen anderen geübt wird"@de,
        "Imi, the abstinence practised on behalf of another"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Nihongi, Book II, the Age of the Gods, in the English of W. G. Aston, vol. I, London 1896, pp. 82-83",
        "Nihongi, Book V, Sujin 7th year, 2nd month, in the English of W. G. Aston, vol. I, London 1896, p. 152" ;
    skos:definition "The standing condition on approach. Two named deities are told to take the heavenly divine fence down to the central land and there to practise religious abstinence on behalf of the sun goddess's descendants; the hereditary corporation descended from one of them is named from the same word. The rice of the first-fruits festival is grown in a plot fixed by divination and prepared under strict conditions of ceremonial purity, and the emperor who is not answered bathes and practises abstinence before praying again."@en ;
    skos:note "The construction worth recording is that the abstinence is performed on behalf of somebody else. The person who keeps the condition and the person for whom it is kept are different, which means the one who could tell whether it lapsed is not the one who bears the consequence. Compare generalizations/displaced-penalties.ttl; this is the same separation seen from the requirement side rather than from the penalty side."@en,
        "The editor of the translation quotes a Chinese book of about the third century which states the consequence of a lapse in plain terms — that when the Japanese fall ill or meet with disaster they set it down to the abstainer's failure to keep his vows and put him to death. That work was not opened in this run and no node here rests on it; the address is in prima-materia#742."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Nihonshoki ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shinto .

pmc:ShintoKukatachiExitus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der berichtete Ausgang, und der Grund, den die Chronik dafür angibt"@de,
        "The outcome reported, and the reason the chronicle gives for it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Nihongi, Book XIII, Ingyō 4th year, 9th month, in the English of W. G. Aston, vol. I, London 1896, p. 317" ;
    skos:definition "What the chronicle says happened. Those who were true remained naturally uninjured, and all those who were false were harmed. Then, in the next sentence: therefore those who had falsified their titles were afraid, and slipping away beforehand, did not come forward. From that time the houses and surnames were spontaneously ordered, and there was no longer anyone who falsified them."@en ;
    skos:note "The transmission reports a perfect result and, immediately after it, the mechanism that would produce a perfect result without the water doing anything: the people with something to hide did not take the test. It names its own selection effect, in its own voice, in the sentence after the verdict. Nothing else in this holding does that, and it is the reason this node is kept apart from the criterion above rather than folded into it."@en,
        "The two sentences also stand in the wrong order for the account they give. If the false had slipped away beforehand, there was nobody left to be harmed, and the clause saying that all those who were false were harmed has no subject. The chronicle asserts both and reconciles neither."@en,
        "What the passage does not carry: any number. Not how many came, not how many were harmed, not how many withdrew. The editor of the translation read here adds in a note that the measure can only have been applicable to a dominant caste and that the nation cannot all have been subjected to the ordeal; that is his and is entered as his."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Nihonshoki ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shinto .

pmc:ShintoKukatachiYieldsDiscrimen a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Probe scheidet die Wahren von den Falschen"@de,
        "The ordeal separates the true from the false"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Nihongi, Book XIII, Ingyō 4th year, 9th month, in the English of W. G. Aston, vol. I, London 1896, pp. 316-317" ;
    skos:note "Entered as the claim of the transmission. It is one of the few in this holding for which the transmission also supplies an outcome and a rival explanation of that outcome, both at pmc:ShintoKukatachiExitus, so a protocol on it would start further along than usual."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ShintoKukatachi ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Nihonshoki ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shinto ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ShintoKukatachiCriterium .

pmc:ShouyiYieldsCapacities a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Bewahren des Einen bringt jene Vermögen hervor"@de,
        "Guarding the One yields those capacities"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 18 (Dizhen), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 18" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Shouyi ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BaopuziNeipian ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ShouyiCapacities .

pmc:SiddhisAsObstacle a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Kräfte sind Hindernisse für samādhi"@de,
        "The powers are obstacles to samādhi"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.37" ;
    skos:definition "The capacities of the third chapter are named obstacles with respect to samādhi and attainments only with respect to the outward-turned state. The tradition that catalogues them is the same one that warns against them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:DivyaCaksus,
        pmc:DivyaSrotra,
        pmc:ParacittaJnana,
        pmc:ParakayaPravesa ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PatanjalaYoga .

pmc:SiguiDatePrediction a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Vorhersage der nächsten Zeremonie im Artikel selbst"@de,
        "The article's own prediction of the next ceremony"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on a dated prediction the account makes about itself. A footnote states that the next ceremony would probably fall in nineteen sixty-one or nineteen sixty-three if the reckoning holds, and adds that it was publicly known at the end of nineteen fifty that it would not be celebrated for another ten years or so."@en ;
    skos:note "A rare thing in this collection: a claim that fixes its own due date, published before the date fell. Whether the ceremony fell in those years is a matter of record, and the record was not read at the wording in this run. The date is therefore not entered and the state stands at procedure without cases. See prima-materia#254."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, protocol stated from the account's own footnote; the date of the ceremony has not been established from a work" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A dated report of the ceremony beginning in a year other than nineteen sixty-one or nineteen sixty-three refutes the reckoning as the account states it, and with it the claim that the interval is governed by a fifty-year period doubled." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-01"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:Sigui .

pmc:SiguiSeriesExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Masken- und Fermentträgerserien als datierbarer Nachweis der Zeremonie"@de,
        "The mask and ferment-holder series as a datable record of the ceremony"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the one part of the account that leaves objects a later worker can count and date without asking anybody: the great masks carved for each sixtieth-year ceremony and kept in rock shelters beside their predecessors, and the baobab-fibre ferment-holders woven for each and hung in the hogon's house. The account infers from series of nine, of eight and of fourteen that ceremonies were held back to the fifteenth and, on one reading, the twelfth century."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because it is the falsifiable half of the account and the half nobody examined. The astronomical claims cannot be tested against anything but memory; wood and fibre can be dated. The protocol asks whether the intervals between the objects of a single shelter are compatible with sixty years, and whether the oldest are as old as the inference requires."@en,
        "No case series has been collected here and none is known to this graph. The state is therefore procedure without cases and not cases without deviation: nobody has carried the examination, which is a different report from having carried it and found nothing. See prima-materia#253 for the radiocarbon and dendrochronological work on Bandiagara material that would have to be searched before this protocol can be advanced."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, protocol stated from the account; no case series has been collected" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A dated series from one shelter whose intervals depart from sixty years beyond what the dating method's error admits, or whose oldest object is materially younger than the count of objects requires. A single shelter dated cleanly against the count would equally carry the claim." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-01"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:Sigui .

pmc:SishiCini a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Zwölf Fehlnadelungen nach Jahreszeit und Tiefe, jede mit ihrem Ausgang"@de,
        "Twelve mis-needlings by season and depth, each with its outcome"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 64 (Si shi ci ni cong lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 18" ;
    skos:definition "The chapter first places the qi by season — in spring in the channels, in summer in the fine network vessels, in the long summer in the flesh, in autumn in the skin, in winter in bone and marrow — and then names, for each season, what follows from needling at the wrong depth. Spring, the network vessels: blood and qi overflow outward, the man is short of breath. Spring, the flesh: blood and qi turn back on themselves, the qi rises. Spring, sinew and bone: blood and qi settle inward, the belly distends. Summer, the channels: blood and qi are exhausted, the man is slack and weary. Summer, the flesh: blood and qi withdraw inward, the man is easily frightened. Summer, sinew and bone: blood and qi rise counter to their course, the man is easily angered. Autumn, the channels: blood and qi rise counter to their course, the man becomes forgetful. Autumn, the network vessels: the qi does not go outward, the man lies down and does not wish to move. Autumn, sinew and bone: blood and qi scatter inward, the man shivers. Winter, the channels: blood and qi are all lost, the eyes do not see clearly. Winter, the network vessels: the inner qi leaks outward, and there remains a great numbness. Winter, the flesh: the yang qi is cut off, the man becomes forgetful. All these, the chapter says, are the illnesses of great contrariety, and it adds that one who needles without knowing the seasons of the channels turns the with-the-grain into the against-the-grain."@en ;
    skos:note "Twelve clauses, each with a consequence stated in the same register as the prescription, and the whole indexed by a calendar. Beside the day-stem prohibitions of Lingshu 61 this is the corpus's second body of date-bound rules, and it is the more complete of the two, because these say what follows and those do not."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Cizhen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmc:SitaliYieldsCooling a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Einatmung über die Zunge bringt Kühlung hervor"@de,
        "Inhalation over the tongue yields cooling"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.57-58 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:note "Recorded because the opening through which the breath is taken is not incidental here but is what the practice consists in. Two of the eight retentions the manual lists take the breath in through the mouth and give it out through the nose; two others require the mouth held shut. The manual therefore assigns the opening per practice and does not set nasal breathing as a general rule, still less mark mouth inhalation as an error. See prima-materia#26."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Sitali ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:CoolingAndQuenching .

pmc:SituatingArsNotoriaPrognosisAgency a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Prognose wird bei abgetretener Handlung verortet"@de,
        "The prognosis is placed at ceded agency"@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:cededAgency ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:ArsNotoriaOratioYieldsCognitioAegritudinis ;
    pm:situationGround "The text states the coordinate itself rather than describing a condition from which it has to be inferred: know that this miracle proceeds not from your own nature, but from the nature and virtues of the holy angels, it being a part of their office to reveal these things to you. The operator says the words and asks the question; what is said to act is named as someone else." .

pmc:SituatingDaoWuGuishenAgency a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Der Weg ohne Geister wird bei eigener Trägerschaft verortet"@de,
        "The way without spirits is placed at own agency"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 25 (Bao ming quan xing lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 8" ;
    skos:note "The declining value was considered and does not fit. A tradition declines the distinction when it holds that acting and being acted through do not apply to what it teaches; this passage applies the distinction and rejects one of its terms, which is the opposite operation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:ownAgency ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:DaoWuGuishen ;
    pm:situationGround "The source does not leave the question open and does not set the distinction aside: it takes the side within it. What is denied is that a ghost or a spirit is at work, and what is put in its place is a procedure that answers a correspondence, with the operator ordering his own spirit first. That is the criterion this value names, and it is asserted here rather than assumed."@en .

pmc:SituatingDujianAnchoring a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Das Alleinsehen wird bei abgezogener Vertiefung verortet"@de,
        "Seeing alone is placed at withdrawn absorption"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 26 (Ba zheng shen ming lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 8" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:withdrawnAbsorption ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:Dujian ;
    pm:situationGround "The passage names the withdrawal of the ordinary channel itself and does not leave it to be inferred: the ear does not hear, and where all look he alone sees. The practitioner is awake and at work on a patient throughout, so what is set aside is the reliance on what the senses report and not waking itself. That is the criterion this coordinate names."@en .

pmc:SituatingEthiopianAsmatAgency a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Der Schutz der Namen wird bei abgetretener Handlung verortet"@de,
        "The protection of the names is placed at ceded agency"@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:cededAgency ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:EthiopianAsmatYieldsRestraintOfDevils ;
    pm:situationGround "The roll assigns the acting to others throughout: the angels protect and keep in safety, the names are the names of their God, the deliverance of the worn form comes by the will of God, and the seventh prayer rests the whole effect on an oath sworn to Christ by the adversary. What the person does is say or wear; what acts is named as someone else." .

pmc:SituatingEthiopianAsmatAnchoring a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Das Sprechen der Namen wird im nach außen gewandten Wachen verortet"@de,
        "The saying of the names is placed in outward waking"@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:outwardWaking ;
    pm:situatedNode pmp:EthiopianAsmatRecitedAtTheDoors ;
    pm:situationGround "The roll sets the saying at the front and at the doors of a house that is about to be entered, and the sixth section of the supplementary roll sets it at the moment a man raises a spear. Both are acts done with open eyes in the ordinary channel; nothing in either passage asks for withdrawal, sleep, vision or absorption, and no state is named as a condition." .

pmc:SituatingTaulipangSoulJourneyAcquisition a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Der Zustand der Seelenreise wird beim erarbeiteten Erwerb verortet"@de,
        "The state of the soul-journey is placed at acquired by effort"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Theodor Koch-Gruenberg, Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, Band III: Ethnographie (Stuttgart: Strecker und Schroeder, 1923), pp. 205, 207-208" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:byEffort ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VomRoroimaZumOrinocoIII ;
    pm:situatedNode pmp:TaulipangSoulLeavesByTheHairCord ;
    pm:situationGround "The transmission makes the state the end of a course of training it describes at length and quantifies - ten to twenty-five years, a week per bark, five or six months of nightly singing - and it says in as many words that the longer the study lasts the stronger the Zauberarzt becomes. It names no case of the state arriving unsought and no sign by which somebody is found to have it already. The office is also inherited in practice, the son usually learning from the father, but the inheritance is of the teaching and not of the state: where there is no son the Zauberarzt takes a boy he thinks suitable, and that boy goes through the same course."@en .

pmc:SituatingTaulipangSoulJourneyLocus a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Seelenreise wird beim verlagerten Ort verortet"@de,
        "The soul-journey is placed at displaced locus"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Theodor Koch-Gruenberg, Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, Band III: Ethnographie (Stuttgart: Strecker und Schroeder, 1923), pp. 204, 207" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:displacedLocus ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VomRoroimaZumOrinocoIII ;
    pm:situatedNode pmp:TaulipangSoulLeavesByTheHairCord ;
    pm:situationGround "The placement is as near to a quotation as this axis gets. The account says that the cord serves the shadow of the Zauberarzt as a ladder to climb up by at the cure while the body remains on the earth, and of the novices that their soul leaves the body and goes to the house of the Mauari, that they lie as if dead with only the heart beating, and that the body wakes before the soul returns. The transmission therefore locates the awareness away from the body and says where it has gone, which is the criterion this coordinate names."@en .

pmc:SixteenStepsOfBreathing a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die sechzehn Schritte der Atemachtsamkeit"@de,
        "The sixteen steps of mindfulness of breathing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Majjhima Nikāya 118" ;
    skos:note "Recorded at the weakest strength on purpose. The discourse sets out four tetrads of four steps and assigns them to the four foundations of mindfulness. It presents a progression; it does not say that a step is unreachable without the one before it, and the question whether the order is binding or expository is contested among practitioners to this day."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:Anapanasati ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MajjhimaNikaya ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:presentationOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:Anapanasati ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:SixteenSupports a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die sechzehn Stützen"@de,
        "The sixteen supports"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.73 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "A set of sixteen named stations of the body, called supports, which the seal at the throat is said to bind; the manual names the number in a compound and treats the binding of all sixteen as one operation."@en ;
    skos:note "Recorded because the number is the whole of the finding. A modern draft gives thirteen segments as the path along which the force is led; sixteen is what the base text names here, and other works of the wider corpus give eighteen. The count therefore varies across the transmission, which means there is no single path to be had, and a scheme that presents one number as the path has chosen a branch without saying so. The other witnesses to the sixteen and the eighteen have not been read at their wording and are not entered here. See prima-materia#35."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:SixthPalaceWaterTest a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Wasser, das keines ist, am Tor des sechsten Palastes"@de,
        "The water that is not there, at the gate of the sixth palace"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hekhalot Rabbati, chapter 26, trans. Morton Smith, corrected by Gershom Scholem, ed. Don Karr" ;
    skos:definition "A protocol the transmission states for itself, complete in every part. The doorkeepers of the sixth palace place upon the descender a thousand thousands of waves of water, and there is not in that place even a single drop. The test is what he does next: the fault is not a wrong answer but the question. If he says what is the nature of this water, they run after him and stone him, saying that he is of the seed of them that kissed the calf and is unfit to see the King and his throne in his beauty; a voice goes out from the seventh heaven confirming the sentence; and he does not move from the spot before they place on him a thousand thousands of pieces of iron."@en ;
    skos:note "Silence is the pass and the question is the failure, which inverts the shape every other examination in this graph has. It also makes the test unusually cheap to administer and impossible to prepare for by learning an answer, since there is none."@en,
        "What the protocol cannot survive is the same thing that undid the Maya examination in traditions/maya.ttl: the text that transmits the test also transmits the correct behaviour. From the moment this chapter is written down, the trial separates those who have read it from those who have not."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, protocol stated from the witness; no case series has been collected" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A recorded case in which a descender held to be fit asked about the water, or in which one held unfit kept silent and passed. The transmission claims the gate decides fitness, so a single clean case on either side falls due against it." ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HekhalotRabbati ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:HekhalotFitness .

pmc:SixtyYearReckoningPresupposesTheDiscovery a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Sechzigjahres-Comput setzt die Entdeckung des Same-Sterns voraus"@de,
        "The sixty-year reckoning presupposes the discovery of the seed-star"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 282-283" ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity because the consequence is quotable, and not because the sequence is historical. What the passage establishes is that the tradition itself makes the calendar depend on the star, which is the claim the outside reception took over and the claim the examination addresses."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The account states what held before the discovery: the year was not yet fixed as a unit, the only reckoning known was by seven harvests, and at the end of the seventh the supreme chief was sacrificed. The consequence of the prior step being absent is therefore named twice over, as a defect of the calendar and as a death." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Sigui ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:KnowledgeOfSeedStarCharacteristics ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Dogon .

pmc:SomnambulismeYieldsAutoDiagnostic a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der Somnambulismus bringt die Wahrnehmung der eigenen Krankheit hervor"@de,
        "Somnambulism brings the perception of one's own disease"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. V, pp. 74-75 and 80-82" ;
    skos:definition "In the somnambulic state the patient is held to perceive the seat of his own disorder and to be able to state its cause, name the crises that will bring the cure, and prescribe the means."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the yielding on which the practice's medical claim rests, and the source attaches to it the conditions that make it unexaminable: the somnambulist must be occupied with nothing but himself, must not be asked a question of curiosity, must not be shown to a physician unfamiliar with the practice, and must not be put to any proof. The capacity is asserted and its demonstration is prohibited."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:MagnetismeGrandsCourans ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:MagnetismeAutoDiagnostic .

pmc:SoulJourneyIsDisplaced a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Seelenreise ist ein verlagerter Ort"@de,
        "The journey of the soul is a displaced locus"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Herodotus, Histories IV.13-16",
        "Pliny, Naturalis Historia VII.174" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:displacedLocus ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HerodotiHistoriai,
        pmw:NaturalisHistoria ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:SoulJourney ;
    pm:situationGround "The reports have the soul leave the body and return to it. That is the criterion for this value, and it is what the accounts of Aristeas and Hermotimus turn on; whether anything of the sort occurred is a separate question and not this one."@en .

pmc:SpiritPresupposesBreath a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Verfeinerung des Geistes setzt die des Atems voraus"@de,
        "Refining spirit presupposes refining breath"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chen Zhixu, Jindan dayao, juan 4" ;
    skos:note "Recorded from the Yuan-period witness, not from the Han or Song works to which the sequence is commonly credited."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:NeidanThreeStages ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:JindanDayao ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Qi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:SpiritismControlExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Kontrolle, die sich nicht anwenden lässt"@de,
        "The control that cannot be applied"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Allan Kardec, L'Évangile selon le spiritisme, introduction, section II, sub-heading Contrôle universel de l'enseignement des Esprits",
        "Allan Kardec, Le Livre des Médiums, seconde partie, chapitre XXVI, section 288, item 3" ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the transmission's own rule of verification. Two passages by the same author, in two works, stand against each other in the way that matters here. One states a control and states it well: concordance of spontaneous teaching, from many mediums unknown to one another, in different places, restricted to fundamental principles, with the express refusal to count a single medium or a single centre. The other forbids the question put in order to test, and settles both outcomes in advance - the spirits addressed do not answer and withdraw, and a spirit that does answer such a question is thereby classed as imperfect."@en ;
    skos:note "The state stood at procedure without cases until prima-materia#499 was decided, and understated the protocol: pm:CasesRequireCounterSearchShape grouped the verdict on a claim's wording with the two states that report cases and demanded a counter-search that would have had nothing to search. The shape was corrected rather than the state faked, and the state is now the one the reading reached."@en,
        "The two rules do not contradict each other on their face and the finding is not that they do. The finding is what they leave: a criterion that can be satisfied by agreement and cannot be strained by a question. Agreement among mediums who share a doctrine, a vocabulary and an expectation is the outcome to be explained, not the evidence that explains it, and the one operation that would tell a real concordance from a shared expectation is the one the transmission rules out."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, protocol formed from two passages of the same author read at the wording; no case series has been collected and no external examination has been read" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A passage in this transmission in which a concordance of independent mediums is reported and then set aside as insufficient on some ground other than the moral quality of the communication; or a recorded case in which a test question was put, answered, and the answer accepted as evidence rather than as a mark of an imperfect spirit. Either would show that the control can be strained and that the exclusion of the test question is not doing the work described here." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:ControleUniversel ;
    pm:undividedOutcomes "A spirit that withdraws when tested and a spirit that was never present produce the same observation; a spirit that answers a test question is by that answer classed as imperfect. Agreement among mediums and agreement produced by a shared doctrine are likewise not separated, since concordance is admitted as the control and no operation is allowed that would strain it." .

pmc:SpiritismPrinciplePresupposesControl a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Grundsatz gehört zur Lehre erst nach der allgemeinen Kontrolle"@de,
        "A principle enters the doctrine only after the control of generality"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Allan Kardec, La Genèse, les miracles et les prédictions selon le spiritisme, chapitre I, article 55" ;
    skos:note "This is the passage on which the registration's judgement rested, and it holds: a transmission that writes down the condition under which it would change is doing something almost nothing else in this collection does. What the next section of this file records is what the same author did with that opening elsewhere."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The consequence is stated as a disqualification and is quotable: any principle that has not received the consecration of the control of generality cannot be considered an integral part of the doctrine. The same article adds the other half, that if new discoveries showed spiritism to be in error on a point, it would modify itself on that point." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:PrincipeDeLaDoctrine ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ControleUniversel ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Spiritism .

pmc:StatesAreNotOrdered a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Zustände werden nicht der Reihe nach erlangt"@de,
        "The states are not attained in order"@en ;
    dcterms:source "al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya",
        "al-Sarrāj, Kitāb al-Lumaʿ" ;
    skos:definition "A warning against treating the given states as a further ladder: they descend and pass, and no course of effort produces them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:Ahwal ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Sufism .

pmc:StationsPresupposeTheOneBelow a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Eine Station setzt die darunterliegende voraus"@de,
        "A station presupposes the station below it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya, chapter on the stations",
        "al-Sarrāj, Kitāb al-Lumaʿ" ;
    skos:note "The handbooks set turning back as the first station and hold that the one below must be kept before the one above is reached. The enumeration and the count of the stations differ between the handbooks; the precedence of turning back does not."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Maqamat ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Tawba ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Sufism .

pmc:SteinerAhnungAusUnbemerktemEindruck a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Ahnung war ein Zeitungsblatt auf einem Tisch, das niemand bemerkt hat"@de,
        "The presentiment was a newspaper on a table nobody noticed reading"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Rudolf Steiner, Wie erlangt man Erkenntnisse der höheren Welten?, chapter Über einige Wirkungen der Einweihung, on the ten-petalled organ, in the German text of 1918" ;
    skos:definition "Three worked examples, given to show the source of countless illusions. A man in a railway carriage is following a thought when it suddenly turns and he recalls an experience of years ago; he has not noticed that his eye was turned to the window and his glance fell on a person resembling somebody involved in that experience, so he believes the thing occurred to him of itself. A man cannot bear a certain colour and does not know that it is because a teacher who tormented him years ago had a coat of that colour. And a man reads in the paper of a death and maintains firmly that he foresaw it yesterday, having heard and seen nothing that could have brought him to the thought; he was in fact visiting an acquaintance a few hours before, a newspaper lay on the table which he did not read, and his eye fell unconsciously on the notice of that person's grave illness. He did not become conscious of the impression, but the effect was the presentiment. Truth is to be ascribed to the perceptions of the ten-petalled organ, the chapter concludes, only when one has become entirely free of the deceptions described."@en ;
    skos:note "The construction deserves its own name and this file does not have one for it. It is not the excusing clause of pmc:NoFailedRunCountsAgainstTheOperation, which disposes of an empty run; it is a condition built out of the strongest available objection to the claim, so that the objection becomes a stage of the training rather than a reason to doubt it. The neighbouring prescription in the same chapter follows from it: one must become master of what works on one from outside, so that impressions one does not wish to receive are really not received, and what one sees one must will to see."@en,
        "The transmission supplies the sceptical explanation of precognition, in more detail and with better examples than most of its critics, and then converts it into a prerequisite. Freedom from unnoticed impressions is made the condition under which the perceptions count as true — and it is a condition no third party can assess and the practitioner cannot assess either, since the impressions in question are by definition ones he did not notice."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SteinerWieErlangtMan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Anthroposophy .

pmc:SteinerBedingungenNieGanzErfuellbar a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Keine der Bedingungen ist ganz erfüllbar; verlangt ist nur das Streben"@de,
        "None of the conditions can be fully met; only the striving is required"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Rudolf Steiner, Wie erlangt man Erkenntnisse der höheren Welten?, chapter Die Bedingungen zur Geheimschulung, in the German text of 1918" ;
    skos:definition "The clause that governs the whole list of conditions for the schooling, printed immediately before the first of them: it must be emphasised that with none of these conditions is a complete fulfilment demanded, but merely the striving after such a fulfilment. Nobody can fulfil the conditions entirely; but everybody can set out on the way to fulfilling them. Everything depends on the will, on the disposition to set out on that way. The conditions themselves are called strict but not hard, since their fulfilment is not only permitted to be a free act but must be one."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also the answer to the first limb of pmc:TheFailureConditionFollowsWhatIsWithheld and the reason this transmission does not refute it. That limb says a transmission which writes its whole operation down names no further condition a possessor of the text could fail. This one names eight or more; but the falsifier asks for a condition a possessor could fail to meet and could be seen to have failed, and this clause removes the second half. The conditions are determinate as sentences and indeterminate as facts, and the book says so itself."@en,
        "The reason no condition in this file carries a value on pm:PrerequisiteStrength. The scale grades how firmly a transmission ties a step to a prior one — stated necessity, prescribed order, order of presentation — and it has no value for a condition declared unfulfillable in the act of being stated. The transmission has produced a prerequisite that is strict in wording and has no determinate state of affairs answering to it, so nobody, including the pupil, can establish whether it is met."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SteinerWieErlangtMan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Anthroposophy .

pmc:SteinerEinbildungStattWahrheit a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "In die eigene Einbildungskraft statt in höhere Welten"@de,
        "Into his own imagination instead of higher worlds"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Rudolf Steiner, Wie erlangt man Erkenntnisse der höheren Welten?, chapter Die Bedingungen zur Geheimschulung, first condition, in the German text of 1918" ;
    skos:definition "The stated consequence of not meeting the first condition, which is the striving after bodily and mental health. Nothing should lie further from the pupil than the inclination to the fantastic, to excitement, nervousness, exaltation and fanaticism; he should acquire a sound eye for all the circumstances of life and let things speak to him calmly. Were this condition not met, the pupil would come, instead of into higher worlds, into that of his own imagination, and favourite opinions would assert themselves in him in place of the truth. It is better for him, the chapter adds, to be sober than exalted and fantastical."@en ;
    skos:note "A sixteenth witness for pmc:NoFailedRunCountsAgainstTheOperation, and it is owed rather than entered: that file is being written in a parallel strand of the same run and this branch cannot touch it. What it adds to the fifteen is that the condition is not a defect of the procedure or of a material but a psychiatric property of the practitioner, named by the transmission in the vocabulary a critic would use. The debt is filed as an issue in the tracker and the note belongs at that generalization."@en,
        "The transmission prints the deflationary account of its own results and attaches it to a condition nobody can be shown to have met. Every case in which the pupil's perceptions are fantasy is provided for in advance, and the party at fault is the pupil, on a count — insufficient mental health, in the sense of freedom from exaltation — that has no criterion."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SteinerWieErlangtMan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Anthroposophy .

pmc:SteinerKeineAnderenHindernisse a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Es gibt keine anderen Hindernisse als die, die sich jeder selbst in den Weg wirft"@de,
        "There are no obstacles but those each man throws in his own way"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Rudolf Steiner, Wie erlangt man Erkenntnisse der höheren Welten?, chapter Praktische Gesichtspunkte, in the German text of 1918" ;
    skos:definition "The general form of the same construction, printed after the instruction every pupil is said to receive from his teacher at the beginning of the way. The pupil is told he must not expect to see and hear at once, that what he does only contributes to forming the higher senses, and to impress on himself the thought: it will one day come to me, what is to come to me, when I am ripe for it. Then: if he observes these instructions he perfects himself; if he does not observe them, all his working is in vain. But they are difficult only for the man who has no patience and steadfastness. There are no other obstacles than those which each man throws in his own way, and which each can also avoid if he really wills it."@en ;
    skos:note "It resembles the general clause of the Clavicula Salomonis, which this holding carries at pmc:ClaviculaDefectusOperantis, and it goes one step further. The grimoire says an unsuccessful experiment must have been false and defective and the operator must have failed in some matter. This book adds that no other kind of obstacle exists at all, and that whatever obstacle there was, the man could have avoided it had he really willed it. The second clause converts an excuse into an imputation."@en,
        "Three devices in one paragraph, and each of them closes a different exit. The result is postponed to ripeness, which is a term that cannot fall due. The absence of the result is assigned to the pupil's observance, which only he can report. And the possibility of any other obstacle is denied outright, so that a pupil who did observe the instructions and saw nothing has no case to make: by the wording there was nothing else it could have been."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SteinerWieErlangtMan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Anthroposophy .

pmc:StillnessExpectationProtocol a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Erwartung das Wirkende ist"@de,
        "Whether the expectation is what does the work"@en ;
    skos:definition "An examination of whether the state depends on the practitioner holding the expectation of physical stillness, or arises without it."@en ;
    skos:note "No procedure devised, and the obstacle is worth naming exactly because the condition itself is easy to write. It would read: practitioners who do not hold the expectation reach the state as often as those who do. Every term in that sentence can be arranged except the last. There is no way to establish that the state has been reached other than the practitioner's report, and his report is made by the same person whose expectation is the variable. The design collapses at its measuring end, not at its manipulating end."@en,
        "What would open it is an outward mark of the state that does not depend on the report — the kind of mark that made the inner-heat claim reachable, where a thermometer settled what testimony could not. Whether such a mark exists for this state is itself unexamined."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:noProcedureDevised ;
    pm:examinedBy "Nobody. The state records that no falsifying condition has been devised that could be run, which is a fact about the means and not about the claim" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:ExpectationOfStillnessIsTheOperator .

pmc:StillnessPresupposesRepentance a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Stille und Liebe setzen Buße und den Kampf gegen die Leidenschaften voraus"@de,
        "Stillness and love presuppose repentance and the labour against the passions"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John Climacus, The Ladder of Divine Ascent, steps 1-30" ;
    skos:note "The work is emphatic that neither stillness nor the love of God is reached without the prior repentance and the fight with the passions. It does not, however, state for every rung that the next is unreachable without it, and the image of the ladder is didactic before it is ontological, which is why the strength here is order and not necessity."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:LadderOfThirtySteps ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Praktike ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:DesertMonasticism .

pmc:SubidaCausaVariabilis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die genannte und die verschwiegene Bedingung"@de,
        "The condition that is stated and the condition that is kept silent"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Juan de la Cruz, Subida del Monte Carmelo II.XVIII (ed. Gerardo, Toledo 1912, pp. 196-200; II.20 in Lewis), read in the Spanish against Lewis 1906/1922 and the Carmelites of Paris, Oudin 1893" ;
    skos:definition "The second cause: God often says things founded on creatures and on effects of theirs which are variable and can fail, so that the words founded on them can also fail, because when one thing depends on another, the one failing the other fails too. Threats and revelations of this kind are threatening or conditional. The text then states the part that bears on every prediction of this transmission: that God sometimes says the saying or makes the revelation and keeps the condition silent, as he did to the Ninevites, to whom he said determinately that they were to be destroyed after forty days; and that at other times he declares it, as to Jeroboam. Whether he declares it or not, there is no securing oneself in the understanding."@en ;
    skos:note "The Spanish of the silent condition is dice el dicho o hace la revelacion, y calla la condicion algunas veces. The three cases the chapter gives are Nineveh, where the forty days passed and the city stood because the sin ceased; Ahab, where the threatened evil was moved to the days of his son because he humbled himself; and Eli, where a priesthood promised for ever was retracted because the zeal on which it was founded failed."@en,
        "The conclusion the chapter itself draws is the one a prediction cannot survive as evidence: no por esto es cierto cumplirse como suena, pues no es cierto el perseverar, it is not therefore certain that it will be fulfilled as it sounds, since the persevering is not certain. Any outcome whatever is thereby covered: fulfilment shows the condition held, non-fulfilment shows it failed, and the practitioner is told that he will often not know which, sino solo Dios."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SubidaDelMonteCarmelo ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinMysticism .

pmc:SubidaCautelaRevelationum a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Warnung vor den uebernatuerlichen Wegen, die dasselbe Werk beschreibt"@de,
        "The warning against the supernatural channels the same work describes"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Juan de la Cruz, Subida del Monte Carmelo II.XIX (ed. Gerardo, Toledo 1912, pp. 201-206; II.21 in Lewis), read in the Spanish against Lewis 1906/1922 and the Carmelites of Paris, Oudin 1893" ;
    skos:note "The caution stands inside the book that classifies these apprehensions at length and describes each kind. Souls are to flee such supernatural things with prudence; wanting to know things by a supernatural way is held worse than wanting other spiritual sweetnesses; the author says he does not see how the soul that seeks them avoids sinning at least venially, however good its ends and however advanced it is, and that whoever commanded or consented to it sins too; and he calls it cosa peligrosisima, mas que se decir, a most dangerous thing, more than I can say, adding that whoever has given weight to such things will understand him by experience."@en,
        "The consequence is in this note because pm:Cautioning cannot carry one; prima-materia#621. The consequence named is that the man will err greatly and often find himself much confused, and that it is temerity to deal with God in this way."@en,
        "The ground is not that the channels do not work. It is that they are not needed: there is natural reason, and law, and evangelical doctrine, by which a man can be sufficiently governed, and there is no difficulty or need that cannot be resolved by those means. The warning is therefore not a safety rule attached to a method but the withdrawal of the method, and the transmission that issues it is the one that holds the material it withdraws."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:SubidaVisionesVeraeFallaces ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SubidaDelMonteCarmelo ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinMysticism .

pmc:SubidaDeumNonInterrogandum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Gott antwortet, und zuernt darueber, gefragt zu werden"@de,
        "God answers, and is angered by being asked"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Juan de la Cruz, Subida del Monte Carmelo II.XIX (ed. Gerardo, Toledo 1912, pp. 201-205; II.21 in Lewis), read in the Spanish against Lewis 1906/1922 and the Carmelites of Paris, Oudin 1893" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that although God sometimes answers what is asked of him by a supernatural way, it is not a good procedure and he does not take pleasure in it but is displeased, and many times is angered and much offended. The reason given is that no creature may go outside the terms God has naturally ordained for its governance, and that to seek to find out and reach things by a supernatural way is to go outside the natural terms. Where God does answer, the text says it is for the weakness of the soul that wants to go that way, so that it may not be discouraged and turn back, and he gives as he finds each one, as a father gives a child the dish it asks for rather than the better one, with sadness."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the shape the holding keeps at generalizations/the-stronger-address.ttl and at examinations/the-stronger-address-tested.ttl, in an unusual form: the answer is granted and the granting is itself charged against the asker. Not added to either in this run, because SPEC 16 requires the counter-search against the holding before a generalization is written or enlarged and that counter-search was not carried here."@en,
        "Three cases are given of the anger that accompanies the answer: Saul, to whom Samuel appeared and who was at once reproved by him for having set about such a thing; the Israelites, who were given the flesh they asked for and had the wrath of God come down on them with the morsels still in their mouths; and Balaam, who was told to go and met the angel with the sword on the road, who told him his way was perverse and meant to kill him."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SubidaDelMonteCarmelo ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinMysticism .

pmc:SubidaIonasTerminus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Prophet, der seine eigene Frist abwartete und sie leer fand"@de,
        "The prophet who waited out his own deadline and found it empty"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Juan de la Cruz, Subida del Monte Carmelo II.XVIII (ed. Gerardo, Toledo 1912, pp. 199-200; II.20 in Lewis), read in the Spanish against Lewis 1906/1922 and the Carmelites of Paris, Oudin 1893" ;
    skos:definition "The case the text reports of a stated term that fell due and brought nothing, with the operator's own reaction. Jonah fled rather than prophesy because he knew the variety of God's sayings as men understand them and their causes, and so that they should not mock him when they did not see his prophecy fulfilled; and he waited out all forty days outside the city to see whether it would be fulfilled, and when it was not he was greatly distressed, so far as to ask God to take his life."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as its own node and not folded into pmc:SubidaCausaVariabilis because it is the rarest kind of material in this holding: a term named in advance, a wait carried out to the end of it, a stated non-event, and a reported reaction by the person who had announced it. The chapter cites the same prophet twice more on the cost of the office, that prophecy had become to him a reproach and a mockery all day long, and temor y lazos se nos ha hecho la profecia, fear and a snare has prophecy become to us."@en,
        "The same case is named by the second founder of the same reform, to the opposite end. See pmc:LatinMysticismCriterionDispute."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SubidaDelMonteCarmelo ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinMysticism .

pmc:SubidaMagisterContagio a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Neigung des Leiters, dem Geist des Schuelers eingepraegt"@de,
        "The director's own inclination printed on the disciple's spirit"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Juan de la Cruz, Subida del Monte Carmelo II.XVI (ed. Gerardo, Toledo 1912, pp. 181-185; II.18 in Lewis), read in the Spanish against Lewis 1906/1922 and the Carmelites of Paris, Oudin 1893" ;
    skos:note "The mechanism is named and it is the one this holding watches for. If the spiritual father is inclined to the spirit of revelations so that they weigh with him, he cannot help, even without understanding it himself, printing that same taste and estimation in the spirit of his disciple; the author says he does not know how easily a fullness and an estimation of these things attaches itself to the soul, sin ser en su mano, not being in its own power, and takes its eyes off the abyss of faith. And the thing the confessor does that causes it is named: he puts the principal part of spiritual language in these visions, dandoles indicios para conocer las visiones buenas y las malas, giving them marks by which to know the good visions from the bad. The text grants that it is good to know such marks and says there is no reason to put the soul to that work, care and danger except in some pressing need."@en,
        "The same chapter states the confounding of the criterion as well. Souls that act or believe according to what was revealed to them thought two things beforehand: first, that it was of God, pues tanto se les asentaba, because it settled so well with them, and the author says puede ser el natural inclinado a ello el que causaba aquel asiento, it may be the nature inclined to it that caused that settling; second, that being of God it had to come out as they understood it. So the subjective mark of divine origin is said, in the source, to be producible by the practitioner's own inclination, which the director has just been described as installing."@en,
        "Where a pm:Reworking node would stand and why it does not. This transmission is downstream of the discernment material in traditions/christian-discernment.ttl and traditions/desert-monasticism.ttl, and the differential diagnosis at pmc:SubidaSenalesCautela and the mechanism here are additions and not copies. But pm:Reworking requires pm:reworkedClaim to point at an entered node of the transmission received from, and the received claim in question, that the marks of a true vision can be taught, is not entered anywhere in this holding. Writing it would mean opening Cassian for that place, which is a strand of its own. Until it is entered this node stands as a caution and the reworking is not asserted."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:SubidaVisionesVeraeFallaces ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SubidaDelMonteCarmelo ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinMysticism .

pmc:SubidaMeditacionYieldsNoticia a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Wiederholte Akte liebender Kenntnis erzeugen den Habitus der allgemeinen liebenden Kenntnis"@de,
        "Repeated acts of loving knowledge engender the habit of general loving knowledge"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Juan de la Cruz, Subida del Monte Carmelo II.XII (ed. Gerardo, Toledo 1912, pp. 154-155; II.14 in Lewis), read in the Spanish against Lewis 1906/1922 and the Carmelites of Paris, Oudin 1893" ;
    skos:note "The mechanism is stated and is the ordinary one of habit formation: the end of meditation and reasoning on the things of God is to draw out some knowledge and love of God, each drawing is an act, and as many acts in anything come to engender a habit, so many acts of these loving knowledges come by use to be continued so far that a habit is made in the soul. The claim is therefore not that the practice produces what it is, which SPEC 11 forbids as circular, but that the repetition converts particular acts into a standing disposition."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:SubidaMeditacionDiscursiva ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SubidaDelMonteCarmelo ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinMysticism ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:SubidaNoticiaAmorosaGeneral .

pmc:SubidaProphetiaNonDiscriminans a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Eine wahre Zusage und ihr Gegenteil, als vereinbar ausgesprochen"@de,
        "A true promise and its own opposite, stated as compatible"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Juan de la Cruz, Subida del Monte Carmelo II.XVII (ed. Gerardo, Toledo 1912, pp. 193-195; II.19 in Lewis), read in the Spanish against Lewis 1906/1922 and the Carmelites of Paris, Oudin 1893" ;
    skos:definition "The two cases the text invents in order to show how far the first cause reaches. A holy man afflicted and persecuted is told by God: I will deliver thee from all thy enemies. The text says that this prophecy may be most true and that nevertheless his enemies may prevail and he may die at their hands, because God may have spoken of the true and principal deliverance and victory, which is salvation. A soul with great desires to be a martyr is told: thou shalt be a martyr, and is given great interior consolation and confidence that it will be so; the text says that it will nevertheless happen that she does not die a martyr and that the promise was true, because it will be fulfilled in what is principal and essential in it, by a prolonged martyrdom in labours whose continuance is more painful than dying."@en ;
    skos:note "And it draws the operative consequence, which is the opposite of the one a reader might expect: y si entonces obrara segun el entendia la profecia, pudiera errar mucho, and if he had then acted according to his understanding of the prophecy he might have erred greatly. The transmission therefore forbids acting on its own communications, on the ground that they do not say what they appear to say."@en,
        "The text draws the consequence for the bystander as well, at the Abraham case: those who saw him die without the land, having heard him say that God had promised it to him, quedaran confusos y creyendo haber sido falsa, would be left confused and believing it had been false. So the non-fulfilment is acknowledged to be indistinguishable, from outside, from falsity."@en,
        "This is the node the opened block was worth opening for. The source states, of its own transmission's communications, that the announced outcome and its contrary are both compatible with the communication having been true, and it states this not as an admission under pressure but as doctrine, with two constructed examples chosen to make the point as sharply as possible. In the vocabulary of this holding that is a claim that does not divide the outcomes, said by the transmission about itself."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SubidaDelMonteCarmelo ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinMysticism .

pmc:SubidaSituatAgency a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Handlungstraegerschaft im liebenden Aufmerken ist abgegeben"@de,
        "The agency in the loving attention is ceded"@en ;
    skos:note "The placement is worth making because the same transmission puts the step before it, at pmp:SubidaMeditacionDiscursiva, squarely on the practitioner's own labour, and the two placements together are what a stage map on this axis would consist of. Only the second is entered here; the first is not, because the source makes no statement about agency in meditation that would carry a placement rather than an inference about one."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:cededAgency ;
    pm:situatedNode pmp:SubidaAdvertenciaAmorosa ;
    pm:situationGround "The source says of the faculties in this state that if they sometimes work, it is not with force nor with much procured reasoning but with a sweetness of love, mas movidas de Dios que de la misma habilidad del alma, moved more by God than by the soul's own ability; and that the practitioner is to learn to let himself be quieted. The coordinate is the compiler's."@en .

pmc:SubidaSituatAnchoring a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Das liebende Aufmerken liegt in abgezogener Vertiefung"@de,
        "The loving attention is placed in withdrawn absorption"@en ;
    skos:note "Not pm:objectlessDepth. The source keeps an object throughout, namely a general and loving knowledge of God, and distinguishes that knowledge from the absence of knowledge at the very place where it warns against the soaking of a humour in which the man thinks of nothing."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:withdrawnAbsorption ;
    pm:situatedNode pmp:SubidaAdvertenciaAmorosa ;
    pm:situationGround "The source describes the state as being without acts or exercises of memory, understanding and will, at least without discursive ones, with the faculties at rest and not working, and with nothing given to the imagination or to its work; and it says that where they do work it is moved more by God than by the soul's own ability. The coordinate is the compiler's; the source names no axis."@en .

pmc:SubidaVeritasNonProbatOriginem a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Dass eine Vorhersage eintrifft, zeigt nicht, woher sie kam"@de,
        "That a prediction comes true does not show where it came from"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Juan de la Cruz, Subida del Monte Carmelo II.XIX (ed. Gerardo, Toledo 1912, pp. 203-206; II.21 in Lewis), read in the Spanish against Lewis 1906/1922 and the Carmelites of Paris, Oudin 1893" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that the fulfilment of a supernatural communication is no evidence of its source. The text says that among the locutions and visions that are of God there are ordinarily many that are of the devil, that he goes about with the soul in the same dress and manner as God does, and that because he says many things that are true and in conformity with reason and that turn out certain, souls are easily deceived in thinking that because it comes out true and hits what is to come, it can be nothing but God. The reason given is that whoever has a clear natural light can know many things that were or will be in their causes, and the two examples are a pestilence inferred from the disposition of earth, air and the course of the sun, and an earthquake inferred from the filling of the hollows of the earth with air. The text calls such a thing a prophecy of the devil and says it comes out true."@en ;
    skos:note "The operative rule the chapter draws from this is the sharpest methodological statement in the opened block and is quoted here because the node rests on it: of anything said supernaturally, only what is much in accordance with reason and the evangelical law is to be received, and then received no porque es revelacion, sino porque es razon, not because it is a revelation but because it is reason; and aun entonces conviene mirar y examinar aquella razon mucho mas que si no hubiese habido revelacion sobre ella, and even then the reason is to be examined much more than if there had been no revelation about it. A transmission that disarms its own confirming evidence and then raises the burden of proof where the evidence appeared is a rare thing in this holding."@en,
        "The text adds a second alternative explanation of a successful answer, in one clause: algunas veces responde el demonio, sometimes it is the devil who answers. Together with the layer above it the source therefore supplies two accounts of a correct answer that do not require the claimed source, and supplies them itself."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SubidaDelMonteCarmelo ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinMysticism .

pmc:SuccessPresupposesNadiPurification a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Erfolg der Praxis setzt die Reinigung der Kanäle voraus"@de,
        "Success in the practice presupposes purification of the channels"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.19-20 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.6 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:note "The text directs the breath practice at clearing the impurity of the central channel and gives the outward signs by which accomplished purification is known. It makes success depend on the purification without stating in these verses what befalls one who proceeds without it, which is why the strength here is order and not necessity."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:HathaPranayama ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:NadiPurification ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:SuiyueSuoshiDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob ein Viertel verzehrt werden kann, und ob ein aufgehängter Gegenstand es aufhielte"@de,
        "Whether a quarter can be eaten, and whether a hung object could stop it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Wang Chong, Lunheng, the fourth and last chapter of juan 23, against the year and month taboos on breaking ground, Sibu congkan facsimile",
        "Wang Chong, Lunheng, the treatise on objecting to the year star (Nan sui), juan 24, Sibu congkan facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "The attack the same chapter mounts, in arguments of four kinds and two cases. First, justice: the spirits of heaven and earth are of one mind, and to be punished without an offence is what the world calls a wrong; the man who breaks ground disturbs the body of the earth no less than the man who moves house, and yet it is the household at you, which has done nothing, that is eaten. Second, position and scale: the year star stands at the edge of heaven in the zi place while the builder stands within one province of the Middle Kingdom, and on Zou Yan's geography the whole of the known world lies in the southeast, so that a year eating at you eats the land of the western Qiang and the southeast can take no harm from it; and if the neighbour's house to the west is the you ground, then the builder's own compound has a you quarter too, which the spirit passes over in order to eat a stranger's. Third, the regress of spirits: if year and month have spirits, so has the day; days accumulate into months, months into seasons, seasons into years, of which one thousand five hundred and thirty-nine make a tong and four thousand six hundred and seventeen an era, and these are names for multiples and for the ends of reckonings and not for spirits; within a single day the twelve double-hours are laid on the same twelve branches and eat nothing, while the establishment of the month on those branches eats. Fourth, appetite and work: a spirit's mouth and belly are as a man's, and a man eats when he is hungry and stops when he is full, whereas this one eats only where building is done, so that in a lean age, with dwellings abandoned and building at an end, it would go hungry; and setting timber in a dug pit and raising a dike in a cut ditch are the same labour, yet the year eats the building of a house and not the working of a field. The two cases take the doctrine's own scale at its word: Meng Tian's wall for Qin used half the empire, so the disasters should be counted in tens of thousands, and the Qin people did not die in numbers; and the Duke of Zhou's building of Luo was work of the greatest extent, and what the record reports is that the people of the four quarters came together in concord, that the work was finished, and that no great mortality is heard of."@en ;
    skos:note "Both sources cited are the critic's, and one of them is the chapter that carries the doctrine as well. That is not the usual case in this file, where a dispute cites the transmission on one side and its opponent on the other, and it is recorded rather than concealed: the practice's own transmission of the year and month taboos on breaking ground has not been located in this holding, so the minimum of two sources is met by two chapters of one author and not by two sides."@en,
        "The argument against the countermeasure is the most transferable thing in the chapter, and it is not an objection from outside the doctrine. The conquest of the phases is granted: metal conquers wood, and yet wood beats metal where the wood is many and the metal few; pile up metal like a hill and light one charcoal under it and the metal will not melt. A cup of water on a burning Mount Tai, a handful of earth in a river breached for a thousand li, a five-foot child against Meng Ben. The doctrine fixes which phase conquers and says nothing of how much, while the practice chooses the object by direction alone; the mismatch is a defect in the doctrine's own terms."@en,
        "The shape of this dispute is not the shape of the one over chosen days. There the critic shows that the rule is not followed where it would be if it were believed, which leaves the doctrine standing for anyone willing to call his contemporaries careless. Here he takes the rule at its word, reads a consequence off its own scale, and looks for the consequence in the record. That is the only place in this file where a Chinese fate art is met with a quantity it committed itself to."@en,
        "The two cases have a different shape from the arguments and a weaker footing. Each is an argument from a silence in the record, and it is worth only what the record is worth. The same treatise says, in the chapter on distinguishing calamities that this file holds beside this one, that the practitioners of these arts keep the favourable cases back and report the unfavourable; a selection of that kind would work against an argument from what is not heard of just as well as it works against a count of confirmations."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:SuiyueSuoshi,
        pmp:Yansheng ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination .

pmc:SunDanceWeatherDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Wetter bei einem Sonnentanz je versagte"@de,
        "Whether the weather ever failed at a Sun dance"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Frances Densmore, Teton Sioux Music (Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 61, Washington, 1918), p. 99",
        "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 100 and p. 105" ;
    skos:definition "The two recorders of this transmission report opposite things about the same ceremony. The first prints a rule for the days on which the Sun does not show his face, assigns such a day to an offence in the camp, and provides that the day be struck from the count of four, which presupposes that such days occurred. The second reports that the medicine men prayed for fair weather for a month beforehand, that their efforts were always successful, and that the oldest men could not remember the falling of rain during a Sun dance."@en ;
    skos:note "Neither report is a study and neither can be preferred on its face. What separates them is not doctrine but memory of events: the first states a contingency and the second denies that the contingency ever arose. The dispute is entered because the second statement is checkable in principle against the recorded weather of the years and places at which the ceremony was held, and because the first would be untestable without it."@en,
        "The two are not equally situated. The first was written down at Pine Ridge from shamans who conducted the ceremony, the second at Standing Rock from men who danced in it, and the last tribal Sun dance the second witness names was held in eighteen eighty-one. A reader who wants to resolve this has to decide whether never having rained is a claim about the weather or a claim about what the rule on the other side was for."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:TheSunHidesHisFaceOverAnOffence .

pmc:SunDanceYieldsCommunicationFromTheSun a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Zuendetanzen bringt eine Mitteilung von der Sonne hervor"@de,
        "Dancing to completion yields a communication from the Sun"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 61 and p. 115 and p. 120" ;
    skos:note "The dancers of the buffalo dance are told the same thing in the praise the herald proclaims over them: that they now belong to the people of the Sun, that they will not have to pay the price when they take a wife, that they will have many children who will honour them, and that they may now receive a communication from the Sun. The four are given together, which is worth noting because only the last of them has a term."@en,
        "The strongest form in which this transmission makes a claim that could fail, and the reason the tradition was taken from the registry. The practice is public and its completion is witnessed by the camp; the promised effect is a communication; the term within which it is to arrive is named. What the corpus does not say is what follows if the term passes empty, and that silence is a property of the corpus and is recorded rather than filled."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:WiwanyangWacipi ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by named Oglala shamans, chiefly Sword, Tyon, Ringing-shield, Bad-wound and No-flesh, and by Finger on 25 March 1914; recorded by the agency physician J. R. Walker at Pine Ridge over his years of service there, closed December 1916; given in Lakota, part of it written out in Lakota by Sword and Tyon and translated by Burt Means, Thomas Mills, Bruce Means and Antoin Herman; the paper prints the translations only, so the Lakota wording is not carried." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LakotaRitual ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:CommunicationFromTheSun .

pmc:SunnaTiyaraLaTamnaka a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wenn ein böses Vorzeichen dich verwirrt, so halte es dich nicht von dem Werk ab, das du vorhattest"@de,
        "If a bad omen throws you into perplexity, let it not hinder the work you intended"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Mishkāt al-Maṣābīḥ, book xxi, chapter ii, as quoted in Thomas Patrick Hughes, A Dictionary of Islam, London 1885, pp. 87 and 125" ;
    skos:definition "Muʿāwiya ibn Ḥakam relates that he told the Prophet of three things done in the time of ignorance and asked after each. Of consulting diviners about future events he was told that now that he had embraced Islam he must not consult them. Of taking bad omens he was told: if from a bad omen you are thrown into perplexity, let it not hinder you from doing the work you had intended to do. Of drawing lines on the ground he was told that one of the prophets used to draw lines, so that if he could draw a line like him it is good and otherwise it is vain. Beside it stands the saying that one is not to put faith in a bad omen but rather take a good one, and the report of Qatʿan ibn Qabīṣa that the Prophet forbade taking omens from the running of animals, the flight of birds and the throwing of pebbles."@en ;
    skos:note "Limits, and they are severe. One paragraph, read in a compiler of 1885 who gives neither the Arabic nor a chapter number within the book he cites, and no isnād. The wording of the saying in the canonical collections was not checked against this. Nothing in this node should be cited as the text of a ḥadīṯ; it is what Hughes printed."@en,
        "Set beside the neighbouring answers, the shape is deliberate. The diviner is forbidden outright, and the answer on diviners elsewhere in the same compiler's entry gives a mechanism — one of the jinn steals away the truth and carries it into the diviner's ear, and to that the diviner adds a hundred lies. For the omen no mechanism is given and no prohibition either. The transmission can argue when it wants to; here it declines to, and prescribes instead."@en,
        "The ground is what makes this worth a node. The answer is not that the omen is false and not that the hearer is exempt from it. It is an instruction about what to do when the omen has already had its effect: go on with what you intended. The saying concedes the perplexity and refuses to let it govern the act, which is a rule of conduct and not a claim about the world at all."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MishkatAlMasabih ;
    pm:readVia "Thomas Patrick Hughes, A Dictionary of Islam, being a cyclopaedia of the doctrines, rites, ceremonies, and customs, together with the technical and theological terms, of the Muhammadan religion, London: W. H. Allen 1885, entry DIVINATION, p. 87, and entry FA'L, p. 125" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PropheticSunna .

pmc:SuperimpositionUnderliesEveryPath a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Überlagerung trägt jeden Weg, auch die Texte über die Befreiung"@de,
        "Superimposition underlies every path, including the texts on release"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Śaṅkara, Brahmasūtrabhāṣya, introduction, at the definition of superimposition as nescience and the statement that all practical distinctions including the texts on final release presuppose it (trans. G. Thibaut, Sacred Books of the East 34, Oxford 1890)" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that self and non-self are mutually superimposed, that this superimposition is what learned men call nescience, and that it is the presupposition of every practical distinction — those of ordinary life and those laid down in scripture alike — between means of knowledge, objects of knowledge and knowing persons, in texts of injunction and prohibition and in texts concerned with final release."@en ;
    skos:note "For the axes in prima-materia#52 this is neither a value nor a third position but a denial of the question's standing. It does not say that the bounded self is dissolved or kept; it says that the level at which either could be said is the level of nescience. Recorded so that a scheme which files it as a unitive telos can be checked against the argument. See prima-materia#66."@en,
        "The clause that decides the matter is the last one. Not only the ordinary distinctions but the texts on release itself are said to rest on the superimposition. A path, a choice between paths and the scripture that recommends one are therefore all inside what the argument calls nescience — not false, but valid only where the superimposition holds."@en,
        "The same section removes the one who would choose. Without the superimposition the self does not become a knowing agent at all, and where there is no knowing agent the means of knowledge do not operate. The agent that a choice between paths presupposes is thus itself among the things superimposed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AdvaitaVedanta .

pmc:SusrutaAprasruta a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wohin das Kapitel es legt, wenn kein Blut kommt"@de,
        "Where the chapter puts it when no blood comes"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Susruta Samhita, Sarirasthana VIII.23 and VIII.54, in the English of Kaviraj Kunjalal Bhishagratna, An English Translation of the Sushruta Samhita, vol. II, Calcutta 1911, pp. 202 and 205-206" ;
    skos:definition "The outcome in which the vein is opened and does not bleed is named four times and assigned a cause each time. Blood does not flow out from an incision made into a vein of an unconscious, a much frightened or a thirsty patient; an incision made without proper bandaging and raising up is attended with the same result; the defect called aprasruta is an incised vein unattended with bleeding owing to the patient's fright, coldness or loss of consciousness; and the defect called parisushka is an opened vein in an anaemic patient, with a total absence of bleeding, as if the flow had been dried up by the Vayu."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered separately from the twenty defects because this is the one outcome a practitioner would actually meet and because the holding is accumulating instances of where an empty run is put. Here it is put in two places and neither of them is the operation."@en,
        "The division is the finding. Of the causes the chapter names for an empty opening, three are states of the patient — fright, cold, unconsciousness, with anaemia as a fourth — and two are acts of the operator, the bandaging and the raising of the vein. What is not among them is the possibility that the vein was opened correctly in a fit patient and did not bleed. The chapter has no entry for that case."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SusrutaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmc:SusrutaAvyadhyapurusa a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Personen, deren Venen nicht geöffnet werden dürfen, und die zwei Fälle, in denen sie es doch werden"@de,
        "The persons whose veins are not to be opened, and the two cases in which they are opened anyway"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Susruta Samhita, Sarirasthana VIII.2-4, in the English of Kaviraj Kunjalal Bhishagratna, An English Translation of the Sushruta Samhita, vol. II, Calcutta 1911, pp. 198-199" ;
    skos:definition "The vein is not to be pierced or opened in an infant, an old man, a parched man, one fatigued and emaciated, a person of timid disposition, one used up with excessive drinking or sexual enjoyment or tired with a long journey, an intoxicated person, a patient treated with purgatives, emetics or enemas, a man who has passed a sleepless night, an impotent or emaciated person, a pregnant woman, or one afflicted with cough, asthma, high fever, phthisis, convulsions, paralysis, thirst, epilepsy or the effects of fasting. Nor is an incision to be made into veins unfit for opening, or into fit ones if they are invisible, or cannot be properly ligatured, or cannot be raised up when ligatured."@en ;
    skos:note "It is a long list and that is its value. Each item names a state of the patient at which the operation is said to go wrong, and four of them reappear below among the twenty defects as the reason a vein does not bleed — the frightened, the unconscious, the thirsty and the anaemic. A list of that length is checkable in a way a general caution is not."@en,
        "The waiver stands two verses later and runs in one direction only: venesection is to be performed even in the cases declared unfit for it, such as in an infant, in cases of blood-poisoning such as snake-bite and in fatal diseases. So the contraindication is set aside where the alternative is death, and nowhere else. The holding carries several transmissions that waive an outward requirement at both ends and keep the requirement on the body; here there is one end and the reason for it is stated."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:SusrutaSiravyadha ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SusrutaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmc:SusrutaCikitsapavada a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Klausel, die das Überleben der Behandlung zuschreibt"@de,
        "The clause that assigns the survival to the treatment"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Susruta Samhita, Sarirasthana VI.83, in the English of Kaviraj Kunjalal Bhishagratna, An English Translation of the Sushruta Samhita, vol. II, Calcutta 1911, p. 189" ;
    skos:definition "The medical authorities are said to have described the marmas as covering half the scope of surgery, inasmuch as a person hurt in any of the marmas dies presently, within seven days of the hurt; and the same verse continues that a deformity of the organ is sure to result from such an injury even if death be averted by a course of judicious and skilful medical treatment. The clause stands in the body of the verse and not in a note."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered separately because the form is the one this holding keeps finding and this is its medical instance. A term is named, and in the same breath the case in which the term passes and the patient lives is assigned to the physician's skill rather than to the claim. What remains after the clause has been applied is a deformity, which is the outcome that cannot be absent, because the verse on either side of it says that any injury to a marman produces one."@en,
        "It is worth saying what this is not. The clause is not a dodge invented for the occasion: a surgical handbook that said treatment never changes an outcome would be denying its own subject. What makes it a finding is that the transmission states the interval as a claim about the injury and then supplies, in the same sentence, the one explanation under which a passed interval says nothing about it."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SusrutaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmc:SusrutaDustavyadhana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die zwanzig benannten Arten, eine Vene schlecht zu öffnen"@de,
        "The twenty named ways of opening a vein badly"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Susruta Samhita, Sarirasthana VIII.52-55, in the English of Kaviraj Kunjalal Bhishagratna, An English Translation of the Sushruta Samhita, vol. II, Calcutta 1911, pp. 205-207" ;
    skos:definition "Twenty defects of an opened vein are named and each is defined by its cause. Durviddha, incised with too slender an instrument, with an unsatisfactory outflow and a very painful swelling; atividdha, the incision too large, so that no blood comes properly or it enters an internal channel; kunchita, incised in a curving manner; picchita, flattened by a blunt knife; kuttita, incised at the sides instead of in the body; aprasruta, unbleeding from the patient's fright, coldness or loss of consciousness; atyudirna, too wide, made with a sharp flat-edged instrument; ante-abhihata, the blood oozing in small quantity; parisushka, no bleeding at all in an anaemic patient; kunita, opened to only a quarter of the proper length; vepita, trembling because bandaged at a wrong place; anutthita-viddha, incised without being properly raised up; sastrahata, cut into two, with excessive bleeding and an inoperative organ; tiryag-viddha, incised with the instrument applied slantingly; apaviddha, incised several times with an improper instrument; avyadhya, a vein whose opening the sastras forbid; vidruta, opened carelessly and hastily; dhenuka, bleeding continuously from being repeatedly pressed and successively opened; punah-punar-viddha, pierced repeatedly in the same part with a very slender point; and marma-viddha, a vein opened at a snayu-, asthi-, sira- or sandhi-marman, in which severe pain, emaciation, deformity or even death may result."@en ;
    skos:note "The chapter closes the list with two sentences that bear on the whole of it. Practice does not give the necessary skill in operating on veins, because they are naturally unsteady and changing like fishes, so a vein is to be opened with the greatest care; and an opening made by an ignorant and unskilful surgeon is attended with the aforesaid dangers and many other distressing symptoms. The transmission therefore says in as many words that the twenty defects are properties of the operator, while four of the twenty it has just defined are properties of the patient."@en,
        "Twenty failure modes with a named cause for each is more than almost any other operative text in this holding supplies, and the completeness is the point of entering them as one list of named members rather than as prose: the question that can now be asked of the list is which of the twenty a practitioner could recognise before the operation and which only after. Most are recognised after."@en,
        "What the list would need in order to be a finding about the operation rather than about operators: one defect whose cause is the procedure. There is none. The nearest is avyadhya, opening a vein the sastras forbid, and that puts the cause in the authority of the text."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SusrutaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmc:SusrutaMarmaghataVivada a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob jede Verletzung eines marman Verunstaltung oder Tod bringt"@de,
        "Whether every injury to a marman brings deformity or death"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Susruta Samhita, Sarirasthana VI.84, in the English of Kaviraj Kunjalal Bhishagratna, An English Translation of the Sushruta Samhita, vol. II, Calcutta 1911, p. 189",
        "Susruta Samhita, Sarirasthana VI.87, in the English of Kaviraj Kunjalal Bhishagratna, An English Translation of the Sushruta Samhita, vol. II, Calcutta 1911, p. 190" ;
    skos:definition "Two verses of the same chapter give two answers. The one says that recovery is common in cuts anywhere in the body, and even where a whole hand or leg has been severed, provided the marmas are not hurt, and that the life is not to be despaired of in a crushed skull or a perforated intestine on the same proviso. The other says that an injury to a marman, whether it be severe or slight, is sure to bring deformity or death, and that an injury to the adjacent part of a marman — by cut, incision, blow, burn, puncture or any other cause — exhibits the same series of symptoms as an actually affected one. Taken together the second verse removes what the first one grants, because the adjacent part of a marman is where a surgeon's incision and a soldier's blade mostly land."@en ;
    skos:note "A protocol on the marman doctrine would have to begin by deciding which verse it is testing. Under the first, a man with a severed hand and intact marmas who recovers confirms the doctrine and one who dies tells against it. Under the second there is no such case, because any injury near a marman is covered and any outcome is either deformity or death."@en,
        "The second verse is the one that would make the doctrine unfalsifiable, and it is the one the transmission itself does not hold uniformly: the translator notes at that place that Gayadasa does not read this verse. That is a textual report read through the translation and not in the commentary, so it stands in this note and not in the graph; it is recorded because it says which of the two sentences the tradition had doubts about."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:SusrutaMarmasparsabhava ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SusrutaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmc:SusrutaMarmaparihara a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Untersuchung des marman kommt vor dem Messer"@de,
        "The examination of the marman comes before the knife"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Susruta Samhita, Sarirasthana VI.26-29 and VI.81, in the English of Kaviraj Kunjalal Bhishagratna, An English Translation of the Sushruta Samhita, vol. II, Calcutta 1911, pp. 178-179 and 187-188" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Stated twice and both times as a consequence rather than a degree. A careful examination of the affected marman is to precede all the acts of extricating a salya, because the Vayu aggravated by an injury to a marman blocks up the four classes of vessels in their entire course and gives rise to a great pain extending all over the body, and symptoms of syncope set in. And of the incision: men versed in surgery have laid down that the situation and dimension of each local marman be first taken into account and the incision made so as not to affect it, inasmuch as an incision even extending or affecting in the least the edge or the side of the marman may prove fatal."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:SusrutaSalyaharana,
        pmp:SusrutaSastrakarma ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SusrutaSamhita ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:SusrutaMarmapariksa ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmc:SusrutaMarmavedhaGambhirya a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Tiefe und Ort verschieben ein marman von einer Klasse in die nächste"@de,
        "Depth and place move a marman from one class to the next"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Susruta Samhita, Sarirasthana VI.30, with the translator's notes to that verse, in the English of Kaviraj Kunjalal Bhishagratna, An English Translation of the Sushruta Samhita, vol. II, Calcutta 1911, p. 179" ;
    skos:definition "The class of a marman is not fixed by the site alone. A marman of the sadyah-pranahara type perforated at its edge rather than in the centre brings on death at a later time, within seven days; a kalantara marman pierced at the side produces a deformity instead of death; a visalyaghna marman so perforated produces excruciating pain and distressful after-effects; and a rujakara marman pierced at the fringe produces an excruciating instead of a sharp pain. The translator's notes give the rule in the other direction: a deeply perforated kalantara marman brings on death within a day, a deeply perforated visalyaghna within seven days, and a deeply perforated rujakara brings excruciating pain — each behaving as a slightly injured marman of the class above it."@en ;
    skos:note "The direction of the rule matters for where it stands. The centre-and-edge half is in the body of verse 30; the depth half is in Bhishagratna's footnotes to that verse, which makes it his report of the doctrine and not a verse read at the wording. The definition marks which is which."@en,
        "The shift rule is the most consequential sentence in the chapter for anyone who wanted to test it, and it cuts both ways. It makes the doctrine finer, because the same site now predicts different things according to how it was struck; and it makes it harder to refute, because a sadya marman whose victim is alive after a day can be reassigned to the class below on the ground that the wound was at the edge. Both halves are in the text and the node keeps them together."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:SusrutaSalyaharana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SusrutaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmc:SusrutaPascatkarma a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Was der zur Ader Gelassene danach zu lassen hat, und wie lange die Autoritäten streiten"@de,
        "What the bled man must forgo afterwards, and for how long the authorities differ"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Susruta Samhita, Sarirasthana VIII.56, in the English of Kaviraj Kunjalal Bhishagratna, An English Translation of the Sushruta Samhita, vol. II, Calcutta 1911, p. 207" ;
    skos:definition "A man who has been anointed, diaphorised, vomited, purged, treated with either of the two enemas, or bled is to forgo anger, physical labour, sexual intercourse, sleep in the daytime, excessive talking, physical exercise, riding or driving, sitting on his haunches, frequent ramblings, exposure to cold, winds and the sun, and hardly digestible, uncongenial or incompatible food — until the strength is perfectly restored, or, according to some authorities, for a month."@en ;
    skos:note "A regimen with a disputed term and no stated penalty, and the shape of the dispute is what is worth keeping. The chapter's own measure is a state of the patient, until the strength is perfectly restored, which is open at the top and settled by the patient; the alternative it reports from other authorities is a month, which falls due. The transmission records that its own authorities disagreed about which kind of term to use."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:SusrutaSiravyadha ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SusrutaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmc:SusrutaRaktapramana a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Menge, und der Rest, der absichtlich bleibt"@de,
        "The quantity, and the remnant that is left on purpose"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Susruta Samhita, Sarirasthana VIII.24-26, in the English of Kaviraj Kunjalal Bhishagratna, An English Translation of the Sushruta Samhita, vol. II, Calcutta 1911, p. 202" ;
    skos:definition "A weak person, or one with an unusual derangement of the doshas, or one who has fainted under the operation, is not to be subjected to continuous blood-letting at one time; the vein is opened afresh in the same afternoon, or on the following day, or on the third day. An intelligent surgeon is not to allow the flow to an excess but is to stop it even with a remnant of the diseased blood left in the system, and to give soothing internal remedies for the purification of that remnant. Bleeding to the quantity of a prastha is deemed sufficient for a strong adult patient stuffed with a large quantity of deranged doshas; the translator's note gives the prastha in blood-letting as thirteen palas and a half."@en ;
    skos:note "The instruction to stop short is the interesting half, and it is the Indian counterpart of the Hippocratic aphorism on empyema and dropsy where a rapid and complete evacuation is said to be certainly fatal. Both transmissions make the completeness of the emptying the danger, and both state it as a rule about the operator's hand."@en,
        "The quantity is the one number in the chapter, and it is a number for a named kind of patient, which makes it the sort of claim a later station could revise. Whether the later Indian medical literature does revise it was not asked here."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:SusrutaSiravyadha ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SusrutaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmc:SusrutaSnehasvedapurvakatva a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Salbung und Schwitzkur kommen vor dem Öffnen"@de,
        "The anointing and the fomentation come before the opening"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Susruta Samhita, Sarirasthana VIII.5, in the English of Kaviraj Kunjalal Bhishagratna, An English Translation of the Sushruta Samhita, vol. II, Calcutta 1911, p. 199" ;
    skos:note "Entered at prescribed order and not higher on purpose. The chapter puts the preliminary first and gives it a purpose, and it does not say what follows from opening a vein that has not been prepared. The strength is a statement about the text and not about the procedure; the twenty defects below do name a failure for an unprepared opening of a different kind — an unraised or badly bandaged vein — and that is a different omission."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:SusrutaSiravyadha ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SusrutaSamhita ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:SusrutaSnehasveda ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmc:SusrutaSuviddhalaksana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Aussehen einer gut getroffenen Vene"@de,
        "The appearance of a well-pierced vein"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Susruta Samhita, Sarirasthana VIII.20-22, in the English of Kaviraj Kunjalal Bhishagratna, An English Translation of the Sushruta Samhita, vol. II, Calcutta 1911, pp. 201-202" ;
    skos:definition "A well and successfully pierced vein bleeds in streams, almost simultaneously with the thrusting of the knife, and stops spontaneously after a muhurta. The vitiated blood is seen to flow out of the opened vein first, like the drop of yellow pigment that comes first out of a kusumbha flower."@en ;
    skos:note "A sign of success, held apart from the conditions of failure as SPEC §15 requires, and this one is of the better sort: it is not announced to the patient but to the operator, it is about the blood and not about a sensation, and it is settled within a muhurta. A bystander could check it."@en,
        "The second half is the weaker half and is the part a protocol would have to drop. That the blood coming out first is the vitiated blood is not an appearance but an interpretation of one, and the comparison to the pigment of the kusumbha gives a colour and not a criterion."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SusrutaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmc:SusrutaVisalyaghna a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die drei Stellen, an denen das Herausziehen die tödliche Handlung ist"@de,
        "The three places at which the extraction is the lethal act"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Susruta Samhita, Sarirasthana VI.23 and VI.70-76, in the English of Kaviraj Kunjalal Bhishagratna, An English Translation of the Sushruta Samhita, vol. II, Calcutta 1911, pp. 176 and 186-187" ;
    skos:definition "Three marmas form a class of their own: the two Utkshepas and the one Sthapani. An extraction of a shaft or of any other extraneous pointed thing lodged in these results in the death of the patient, who on the contrary lives as long as the shaft is allowed to remain inside, or if the shaft comes out of itself after putrefaction. The chapter gives the mechanism with it: these marmas are of vataja property and arrest the escape of the vital Vayu, so that while the dart does not let the Vayu out the life is prolonged, and as soon as the dart is extracted the Vayu escapes and the wound necessarily proves fatal."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as the strongest statement of its kind so far found in the holding, because of the direction of the claim. Every other caution in this chapter forbids an act that would reach a vital place; this one forbids the act of repair, and it states the alternative in operational terms — leave the shaft in, or wait for it to come out by itself. A surgeon who followed it would be doing something unusual that the rule predicts, and the rule says what the other course leads to."@en,
        "The same chapter gives the class three members and no more, which means the rule is not a general caution against extraction but a claim about three named sites, two over the temples at the hairline and one between the eyebrows. A later run with the surgical book open can ask whether the extraction chapters repeat the prohibition."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:SusrutaSalyaharana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SusrutaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmc:SusrutaVyadhanakala a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die drei Zeiten, zu denen eine Vene geöffnet werden darf"@de,
        "The three times at which a vein may be opened"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Susruta Samhita, Sarirasthana VIII.6 and VIII.20, in the English of Kaviraj Kunjalal Bhishagratna, An English Translation of the Sushruta Samhita, vol. II, Calcutta 1911, pp. 199 and 201-202" ;
    skos:definition "Venesection is not to be performed on an extremely cold or hot, cloudy or windy day, and it is forbidden to open a vein without necessity, or in a healthy person, or in a disease in which the proceeding is prohibited. The positive rule is counted: an opening is to be effected on such a day in the rainy season as is devoid of the rumblings of a thunder-cloud, during the cold fourth part of the day in summer, and at noon in winter — and the chapter says these are the only three times of opening a vein."@en ;
    skos:note "A calendar with a stated exclusivity and no stated penalty. The chapter says that these are the only three times and does not say what follows from operating at a fourth, which puts the claim one step below the ones in this holding that bind an operation to a day and price the deviation. The weather clause is the sharper half: cold, heat, cloud and wind are conditions an operator cannot arrange, so the rule removes days rather than selecting them."@en,
        "The ban on opening a vein in a healthy person is worth separating from the rest. It is the clause that makes the operation unavailable as a prophylactic, and with it the transmission forgoes the one use under which an absent effect would have been visible as an absence."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:SusrutaSiravyadha ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SusrutaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmc:SwedenborgCaelumClausum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Himmel ist seit der ältesten Kirche verschlossen, und er ist zum Schutz des Menschen verschlossen"@de,
        "Heaven has been closed since the earliest church, and it is closed for the man's protection"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Swedenborg, Arcana coelestia, n. 784 (The Heavenly Arcana, trans. Potts, Standard Edition, vol. I, pp. 358-359)" ;
    skos:definition "Since the time of the most ancient church heaven has never been open as it was to its men, whose interiors were uncovered and opened from the Lord. Many afterwards spoke with spirits and angels, Moses and Aaron among them, but in an entirely different way. The reason heaven is now so closed that a man does not even know spirits are with him, and supposes himself alone when he is without company and thinking by himself, is that if it were not so closed to him while he is in no faith, still less in the truth of faith and still less in charity, it would be most perilous to him. He is nevertheless continually in the company of spirits who observe and perceive what he thinks and what he intends and devises, as fully and plainly as if it were manifest before all in the world, and of this he is ignorant."@en ;
    skos:note "It supplies the epochal form of the rarity that pmc:SwedenborgColloquiumPericulosum states as a present fact. The reason offered is a change in men — no faith, no charity — and not a change in the operation or in the other party, so the corpus locates the difference between the ancients and the present in the recipients."@en,
        "Two claims in one passage and they pull against each other, which is why both halves are in the definition. The closure is protective and complete; and the observation it was supposed to shut out continues unabated, without the man's knowledge, in every hour of his life. What the closure withdraws is his access to them and not theirs to him."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArcanaCoelestia ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Swedenborgian .

pmc:SwedenborgColloquiumPericulosum a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Rede mit Geistern wird jetzt selten gewährt, weil sie gefährlich ist"@de,
        "Speech with spirits is rarely granted now because it is dangerous"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Swedenborg, De caelo et inferno, n. 249 (Heaven and Hell, trans. Swedenborg Society, London 1885, pp. 114-115)" ;
    skos:definition "The work that consists of reports of such speech says of the practice that it is at this day rarely granted, because it is dangerous: the spirits then know they are with a man, which otherwise they do not, and evil spirits regard a man with deadly hatred and desire nothing more vehemently than to destroy him both soul and body. They effect this with those who have indulged much in phantasies. Those who live solitary and hear spirits speak do so without danger only because the spirits present with them are removed at intervals by the Lord lest they should learn they are with a man, and for that reason the man is not allowed to speak back to them."@en ;
    skos:note "A detail worth keeping because it inverts an expected sign. Of the enthusiastic spirits the passage says that they do not hurt the man with whom they communicate, because he pays them divine worship and honour. The absence of harm is therefore not a mark of a true communication in this corpus; it is the mark of a man who has submitted to a false one."@en,
        "The same passage names a way in which a man brings the state on himself, and it is an anti-instruction. Those who think much on religious subjects and dwell upon them from themselves, not breaking the current of their thoughts with various useful occupations, have those thoughts penetrate interiorly, become fixed, occupy the whole spirit and act upon the spirits of that world; such persons are visionaries and enthusiasts, and believe every spirit they hear to be the Holy Spirit. So the one procedure the corpus describes for producing the experience is described in order to be avoided."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:SwedenborgLoquelaCumSpiritibus ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeCaeloEtInferno ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Swedenborgian .

pmc:SwedenborgMemoriaAliena a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kein Geist darf aus seinem eigenen Gedächtnis sprechen, und ein Mensch könnte es nicht merken"@de,
        "No spirit may speak from its own memory, and a man could not tell if one did"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Swedenborg, De caelo et inferno, n. 246 (Heaven and Hell, trans. Swedenborg Society, London 1885, pp. 112-113)",
        "Swedenborg, De caelo et inferno, n. 256 (Heaven and Hell, trans. Swedenborg Society, London 1885, pp. 116-117)" ;
    skos:definition "It is not allowed any angel or spirit to speak with a man from his own memory, but only from the man's memory. The reason given is that spirits have memory as men do, and if a spirit were to speak with a man from its own memory the man would know no other than that the spirit's thoughts were his own; it would be like the seeming recollection of a thing which had never been heard or seen. The corpus explains the ancient doctrine of return into a former life this way: men occasionally had what seemed a recollection of things they had neither seen nor heard, and the appearance was produced by spirits whose influx proceeded from their own memory into the ideas of the man's thought. The reverse is reported too: an angel enters into all the man's memory so perfectly that he is almost led to suppose he knows of himself what the man knows, including the languages the man has learned."@en ;
    skos:note "The one diagnostic it yields is a negative one and is the sharpest thing in the corpus of that kind: a seeming recollection of what was never heard or seen is to be read as a spirit's influx and not as memory. That is a named, recognisable experience with a stated cause, and it is the corpus's own explanation of a doctrine it rejects."@en,
        "The passage states the very indistinguishability that makes every report from this corpus hard to weigh, and it states it as a rule of the other world rather than as a doubt about the reports. If a spirit speaking from its own memory would be taken by the man for his own thought, then the prohibition is the only thing separating the man's thinking from a foreign source, and the prohibition is not something he can verify."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeCaeloEtInferno ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Swedenborgian .

pmc:SwedenborgNullumSignumInVita a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kein Lebender weiß, ob die Grade in ihm geöffnet sind"@de,
        "No living man knows whether the degrees have been opened in him"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Swedenborg, Arcana coelestia, n. 3884 (The Heavenly Arcana, trans. Potts, Standard Edition, vol. V, p. 238)",
        "Swedenborg, Sapientia angelica de divino amore et de divina sapientia, n. 238 (Angelic Wisdom concerning the Divine Love and the Divine Wisdom, trans. Swedenborg Society, London 1912, pp. 85-86)" ;
    skos:definition "So long as a man lives in the world, the corpus says, he has no knowledge of the opening of these degrees within him. The reason given is structural and not a matter of degree: he is then in the natural degree, which is the ultimate, and from it he thinks, wills, speaks and acts; the spiritual degree does not communicate with the natural by continuity but by correspondences, and communication by correspondences is not sensibly felt. Which degree was opened appears when he puts off the natural degree, that is when he dies. The same withdrawal is made of the internal respiration, which is said to be internal and for that reason imperceptible to man."@en ;
    skos:note "It does not follow that the corpus admits no experience. The author reports a great deal of his own, dated and circumstantial. What he denies is that any of it settles the question the doctrine makes decisive, and the one thing he does report of the respiration is that it is led without his will and cannot be perceived."@en,
        "This is the configuration the file was written for. A transmission that describes a capacity at length and then states that its presence cannot be perceived by the person who has it has removed every sign a practitioner could report and every sign an observer could ask for, and it has done so in the operative doctrine rather than in an apology. The corpus is not silent about signs, which would be a different and weaker finding; it says in as many words that there are none to be had in life."@en,
        "What was searched, because a statement of this kind is only worth what the search behind it is worth. The eight volumes read were searched for any sign, mark or perception by which a living man might know the opening, in the author's own vocabulary as well as in the obvious English; nothing came back but the negative statement itself. The search stands at pmp:SwedenborgSignumAperitionisSearch."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArcanaCoelestia,
        pmw:DivinoAmoreEtSapientia ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Swedenborgian .

pmc:SwedenborgObsessioInterior a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Leibliche Besessenheit abgeschafft, innere Obsession häufiger als je, offener Verkehr selten gewährt"@de,
        "Bodily possession abolished, interior obsession more numerous than ever, open converse rarely granted"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Swedenborg, De caelo et inferno, n. 257 and its footnote (Heaven and Hell, trans. Swedenborg Society, London 1885, pp. 117-118)" ;
    skos:definition "A threefold division the corpus draws in one paragraph and its footnote. Certain spirits called natural and corporeal do not conjoin themselves with a man's thought as other spirits do but enter his body, occupy all his senses, speak through his mouth and act by his members, knowing no other than that the body and faculties are theirs; these are the spirits by whom men were formerly possessed, and they were cast into hell by the Lord and altogether removed, so that there are no such possessions at this day. The footnote adds that external or bodily obsessions do not exist at this day as formerly, but that internal obsessions, which are of the mind, are more numerous than formerly; a man is obsessed interiorly when he has filthy and scandalous thoughts concerning God and his neighbour and is withheld from publishing them only by external bonds, the fear of losing reputation, honour or gain, the dread of the law and the loss of life."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because the division decides what could ever be observed. The kind with a public sign is declared extinct; the kind declared more common than ever is identified by the content of a man's thoughts and by the fact that only fear stops him uttering them, which nobody can establish about anybody; and the third kind, the open converse, is the subject of this file and is said to be rarely granted. So of the three states the corpus distinguishes, exactly one had a mark a bystander could read, and that one is the one it says no longer occurs."@en,
        "The Arcana numbers the footnote cites for the second and third kinds — 1983, 2752, 4793 and 5990 — were not opened. What is entered here is the footnote's own statement, read at the wording in the copy named, and not those passages."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeCaeloEtInferno ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Swedenborgian .

pmc:SwedenborgProbatioSpiritus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Probe eines Geistes: den heiligen Namen verweigern und beobachten, was er tut"@de,
        "The test of a spirit: refuse the holy name and watch what it does"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Swedenborg, Diarium spirituale, n. 1366-1369 (The Spiritual Diary, trans. Bush and Smithson, vol. I, London 1883, pp. 429-430)" ;
    skos:definition "The one criterion in the reading that is carried out rather than stated. Every man sensibly acted upon by a spirit, and especially when the spirit speaks with him, thinks it is the Holy Spirit; all enthusiasts imagine this, whereas they are unclean spirits whom they then call the Holy Spirit and adore and worship, and such spirits love this and thus think themselves really to be the Holy Spirit. But the spirits who belong to the Lord, those in the heavens, are not willing to be called holy and do not call themselves the Holy Spirit, since the Lord alone is their holiness; therefore no holy spirit, by which angels or spirits are understood, is to be worshipped, still less adored. And then the trial: unclean and evil spirits were and are much incensed that he would not acknowledge them as the Holy Spirit, and were so angry as to threaten to punish him."@en ;
    skos:note "The criterion is the refusal and the reaction to it, which makes it the only thing in this corpus that has the shape of a test: an act with two possible outcomes, one of which the operator does not control. What it does not have is the shape of an instruction — it is reported of himself in a private journal, not prescribed to anybody, and the entries are dated 14 March 1748."@en,
        "The failure of the test is reported in the same entry and is the reason this node is worth more than the criterion. At first they threatened him so much that, before he knew how the case was, they greatly terrified him; afterwards, when he was instructed, he declared it boldly before the whole heaven. So the protection is knowledge held in advance of the encounter and not a capacity exercised within it, and the corpus records one occasion on which the knowledge was not yet there and the trial went the other way."@en,
        "The neighbouring criteria in this work stand in the lost portion and are index headings and not paragraphs, which is stated at pmw:DiariumSpirituale and is repeated here because these are the ones a later reader will want: that spirits put on the person of him whom a man adores and say they are the same, and especially wish to be called the Holy Spirit; that spirits put on the knowledges and memory of man and suppose them their own, but flow into his ideas only through his affections; that each spirit lives in his own cupidity and is not able to appropriate a man's cupidities to himself; and that dreams induced by angels are altogether different, beautiful, delightful, instructive, predictive. Numbers 3, 4, 6 and 8, all restored from the author's own Index Diarii."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DiariumSpirituale ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Swedenborgian .

pmc:SwedenborgProfanatioPostFidem a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Eine Beglaubigung, die gelingt und dann verleugnet wird, ist Entheiligung, und Entheiliger kommen in die tiefste Hölle"@de,
        "A demonstration that succeeds and is then denied is profanation, and profaners go to the lowest hell"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Swedenborg, De caelo et inferno, n. 456 (Heaven and Hell, trans. Swedenborg Society, London 1885, p. 239)" ;
    skos:definition "Asked from heaven why the state after death is not plainly revealed by visible appearances, the answer given is that such revelations could easily be made, but that those who have confirmed themselves in falses would not believe even the evidence of their senses, and that such demonstrations of the truth are dangerous to them, because they would first believe and afterwards deny it, and thus profane the truth itself. To believe the truth and afterwards deny it is profanation, and those who profane truths are thrust down into the lowest and most grievous of all the hells."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the strongest caution in the reading and it is a caution against the success of the thing. The danger is not that the demonstration fails; it is that it works and is then withdrawn from, and the penalty for that is the worst the corpus has. A transmission that attaches its heaviest penalty to a successful demonstration followed by disbelief has made the demonstration something no enquirer should want."@en,
        "Two things the passage grounds differently, and the difference is why this node and pmc:SwedenborgSignaNonCoguntFidem are separate. Here the refusal is grounded on a property of the persons asking — they have confirmed themselves in falses and would deny afterwards. At Arcana 10751 the refusal of the same thing is grounded on a property of the belief a sign produces, and that is a claim about the capacity and not about the asker."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:SwedenborgLoquelaCumSpiritibus ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeCaeloEtInferno ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Swedenborgian .

pmc:SwedenborgRespiratioProtegens a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die himmlische Atmung tritt im Augenblick des Angriffs als Schutz ein"@de,
        "The heavenly respiration arrives as a protection at the moment of the attack"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Swedenborg, Diarium spirituale, n. 458 (The Spiritual Diary, trans. Bush and Smithson, vol. I, London 1883, pp. 157-158)" ;
    skos:definition "A dated entry under the heading that in the heaven of spirits a conspiracy was formed to suffocate him. Being about to sleep he was told that certain spirits were conspiring against him with the intention of killing him, and because he was secure he cared nothing about it and fell asleep. About the middle of the night he awoke and felt that he did not breathe from himself but, as he believed, from heaven, which he manifestly perceived, so that the respiration was not his. It was then told him that the entire heaven of spirits had conspired against him with the intention of suffocating him, and immediately as they were about to carry out their attempt a heavenly respiration existed in him, and thus they were obliged to desist."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered beside pmc:SwedenborgRespiratioInterna because it settles what the internal respiration is in this corpus and it is not a faculty. It arrives, unasked and unpractised, at the moment of an attack, and it is the thing that ends the attack. A reader who takes the breathing for something the man had cultivated has to account for its being reported as an intervention."@en,
        "The entry is dated and falls in the surviving portion of the work, so it is the author's paragraph and not an index heading. The editorial caution about this translation, at pmw:DiariumSpirituale, still applies to its wording."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DiariumSpirituale ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Swedenborgian .

pmc:TapuBreachSign a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Die verletzte Kopfhaut als Anzeichen des gebrochenen Fastens"@de,
        "The cut scalp as the sign that the fast was broken"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 42" ;
    skos:definition "A protocol the tradition states for itself. During the hair-cutting of a child under tapu nobody attending may eat; the witness says that a breach is discovered at once, because the skin of the child's head is cut while the hair is being cut, and calls this a sure sign."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because the tradition names an outward, observable event as the detector of a hidden breach and calls it certain. That is a falsifying condition offered by the material itself, which is rare enough in this collection to be worth holding as a protocol rather than as a remark."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, protocol stated from the witness; no case series has been collected" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A recorded case in which the fast was kept and the scalp was cut all the same, or in which the fast was broken and the scalp was not cut. The tradition claims the sign is sure, so a single clean counter-case on either side falls due against it." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:Tapu .

pmc:TenDayTermProtocol a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Protokoll: die Zehntagesfrist des Gerufenen"@de,
        "Protocol: the ten-day term of the named man"@en ;
    dcterms:source "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), p. 93" ;
    skos:definition "A protocol against the claim that a man publicly named at the felling of a muamba tree dies within ten days, and that this never fails."@en ;
    skos:note "No case is entered and none is claimed. The work read supplies no list of namings and no follow-up, and the practice was already being suppressed by the colonial administrations at the time the work was written. What a later run would need is a source that names an occasion and a person rather than the rule, and the candidates are the Laman notebooks and the Mayombe literature, neither of which was opened here."@en,
        "Taken up because the claim states its own falsifying condition, which is rare enough in this holding to be worth a protocol even where no case can yet be entered. The condition is not the death but the term and the completeness: a single named man alive after ten days refutes the claim as the corpus states it, and the corpus states it as exceptionless."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia compiler, from the wording of Dennett 1906, p. 93" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A man publicly named at the felling of a muamba tree who is alive on the eleventh day, in a source that names the occasion and the person rather than the rule."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:TheNamedManDiesWithinTenDays .

pmc:TestingTheMarkedAnimals a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Prüfprotokoll: die an ihren Zeichen benannten Tiere"@de,
        "Protocol: the animals named by their marks"@en ;
    skos:definition "The claim states that animals bearing marks named in advance by the candidate will be sent and found. A condition of refutation exists and can be stated; no case has been recorded under it, because the source that carries the claim reports no case and no count."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim as the source words it is close to failing to discriminate, and the reason is inside it: where the named animals are not found, the spirit may be asked to send another. The falsifying condition below therefore has to be stated against the first designation and against the substitution separately, or the claim covers the finding of a marked animal and the finding of any animal alike. Whether the substitution is used often or rarely is exactly what the source does not say."@en,
        "The state is procedure without cases and not cases without deviation, although the source says the animals are always found. What it reports is a summary judgement without a number, and a protocol that entered it as a case series would be counting the recorder's confidence."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, protocol devised from the wording of Shirokogoroff 1935, sect. 130 I" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A recorded search in which the marks named by the candidate before the hunt are not found on any animal taken within the stated period, and the designation is neither withdrawn nor replaced by a second one. Where the substitution is admitted, the claim is refuted only if the marks of the second designation also fail."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-03"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:TungusMarkedAnimals .

pmc:TheEatersOfMen a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Menschenesser"@de,
        "The eaters of men"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III, on the cauchus or runapmicuc" ;
    skos:definition "A class named apart from all the other specialists and said to be the only ones who properly deserve the name of witch. They are said to gather at night under a master, who goes to a chosen house scattering a powder made of the bones of the dead, by which everyone in the house is put so deeply asleep that neither person nor animal stirs; he comes to the one he means to kill, draws a little blood with his nail from any part of the body and sucks what he can, for which they are also called suckers; what is so taken is carried to the assembly, where it is said to be multiplied into flesh, and is cooked and eaten, and the person dies within two or three days. The common phrase for the assembly is that tonight we are to eat the soul of such a one."@en ;
    skos:note "The recorder states how the class came to be known and the account is unusually traceable for this file: it was not established until one man volunteered it after his examination was over, naming others, who were then arrested and examined singly and against one another. Sixty-three were found culpable in four villages of the coast, none at all in the highland villages at that time, and more later in two other provinces. He also reports the confessions in the words of the accused, including one who said he had been made to eat by force and threatened with death if he did not come again."@en,
        "The whole of it was obtained under an examination conducted by a tribunal with the power to punish, and the recorder says the punishment given during the visitation was deliberately light in order to keep them quiet until a heavier one could be arranged. What such an examination produces is a well-known thing and the count of sixty-three is not evidence of sixty-three witches. It is entered because it is what the record says the transmission says about itself, and because the shape of the claim — the night assembly, the taking of a little blood, the eating of the soul, the death within two or three days — is the material a comparison is made from, not because the events are credited."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmc:TheEffectPutBeyondTheBystander a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Klausel, die die Wirkung dem Zuschauer entzieht"@de,
        "The clause that puts the effect beyond the bystander"@en ;
    skos:note "Falsified by a transmission that teaches an operation of this kind — an effect on the material world that any bystander would see if it occurred — and whose operative passage states that bystanders do see it, or that their not seeing it counts against the operation. Sharper still, and the shape to look for first: an operative text that prescribes the operation be performed before a stranger as a proof. The corpora likeliest to carry it are the ones in this holding where a feat is performed before an assembly as part of the office rather than as a display: the Siberian, Chukchee and Inuit material has not been asked this question, and the recorded duty of the great shaman to exercise his art before a gathering in order to convince people is the first place to put it."@en,
        "Its relation to pmc:TheUnsympatheticWitnessAsStatedCauseOfFailure and to pmc:TheRivalWhoWasNotInTheRoom. All three turn on whether the thing claimed can be established by somebody other than the parties to it, and they are three answers to the same pressure at three different places in a transmission: a clause in the instruction, a named cause of failure, and an account of a session. The order matters for a protocol. A transmission that carries the clause has nothing to explain afterwards, so the absence of the other two at such a transmission is not evidence that it is more candid."@en,
        "No pm:Testing points at this node, and that is its position in the queue and not a defect."@en,
        "The counter-search, and the case it returned. The holding carries a transmission that did put its operation before doubters as its normal business: an American mental operator of the eighteen-forties who travelled for years giving exhibitions to rooms that held the thing for a fraud and threatened him with mob violence, at pmp:QuimbyExhibitioPublica. It is not a counterexample, and the second clause of the statement is where it lands. What he exhibited was a subject describing a place someone in the audience was thinking of, or the inside of a body a physician could afterwards examine — outcomes a stranger reads off without the operator. The same volume records that he had earlier held the standard excluding condition, that a sceptic sitting too near made him fail, and dropped it in the same movement by which his outcomes became checkable."@en,
        "The fifth witness is a device the statement did not carry and now has to name: the bystander is put in the conditional. The ritual figures of the same instruction are to be practised until they appear in flame, in flame so near to physical flame that it would perhaps be visible to the eyes of a bystander, were one present. The clause were one present is in the text. The node is pmc:ThelemaSignumFlammae. It is not a reservation to the operator and not a reservation to a rare occasion; it is a sign whose only external check is granted and withdrawn in one subordinate clause, so that nothing was ever promised to anybody who could have looked. The same chapter adds the limiting case two sentences later: it is alleged that some persons have attained the power of actually kindling fire by these means, and whether this be so or not, the power is not one to be aimed at, which is entered at pmc:ThelemaIgnisAccensus and is the only claim in this holding that a single trial would settle."@en,
        "The first witness is the sharpest and the reason the statement was written. The operation it belongs to — a road becoming water, a river becoming ground — is the most public event anything in this holding claims; and the same paragraph that prescribes it withdraws it from every eye but one, while leaving the extension to other eyes at the operator's wish, which is itself unobservable. The handbook is not defending the claim. It has no opponent in view at that place and is giving instructions."@en,
        "The three witnesses, each with its own device. The Pāli handbook, instructing how to turn ground into water and water into ground, adds that only for him does the ground become water and only for him the water earth, that for everybody else it stays what it was and people go on ploughing and the fishes go on swimming, and that if he wishes it may be water for them too. The Chinese chapter, having stated a limit against its own side — that the meditative arts cannot lengthen a life or dispel an illness — reserves their worth to the violent ghost and the great epidemic, and concedes that where those do not arise, having the arts and not having them come to exactly the same. The Latin book, at the head of its third part, says that every magical experiment flees the public, seeks to be hid and is strengthened by silence, but is destroyed by publication, neither does any complete effect follow after."@en,
        "The weakness of the derivation, stated because a reader would otherwise have to find it. The three devices are not the same device, and a critic may fairly say that a reservation to the operator, a reservation to a rare occasion and a destruction by publicity have only their consequence in common. That is the claim: they have their consequence in common, and the consequence is the thing a protocol meets. But a fourth witness using a fourth device would strengthen it less than a second witness using the first device would, and the first device is the one the holding has only once."@en,
        "Two witnesses added on 2026-09-28, and they were added because this node asked for them. The note on the weakness of the derivation said that a fourth device would strengthen the statement less than a second witness of the first device would, and that the first device — reserving the perception of the effect to the operator — was the one the holding had only once. The Thelemic instruction of nineteen-nine supplies it, and supplies it with the distinction drawn in the text rather than by the compiler. Of the vibration of a god-name it gives two signs: that a single vibration entirely exhausts the practitioner's physical strength, makes him hot all over or perspire violently and leaves him hardly able to stand, which a bystander could see; and that he hears the name roared forth as by ten thousand thunders and seeming to come from the universe and not from himself, of which it says in the same sentence, though only by the student himself is it perceived. The node is pmc:ThelemaSignumVibrationis. That is the first device, in a transmission with no route to the Pāli handbook, stated as a property of the sign and inside the operative instruction."@en,
        "What it does not say. It says nothing about whether any of these operations produces anything, and nothing about the motives of anyone who wrote the clauses. It is a statement about the shape a transmission takes, it can be settled by reading, and that is why it stands here and not at a protocol."@en,
        "What makes the three one claim is not that they conceal. It is where the concealment is put. None of these is a rule of secrecy addressed to the practitioner about what he may tell; all three are statements about the operation, standing in the passage that prescribes it, and each of them has the consequence that a person who watched the operation and saw nothing has seen exactly what the text predicted he would see. That is a property of the claim and not of the practitioner, and it can be established by reading."@en,
        "What the two additions do to the statement. The consequent holds and gains its first repeated device, which is what the derivation most needed. The enumeration in the middle needs a fourth member, the counterfactual bystander, and the wording is not amended here: a claim is corrected where it is examined, and the correction belongs in a protocol beside it rather than in a tidied sentence. What is now known and was not is that the three devices are not a list of three expedients but at least two of them recur, so the agreement is about the consequence and not about the wording."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:AgrippaSilentium,
        pmc:GehongFangshuWuyi,
        pmc:TassaYevaSaPathavi,
        pmc:ThelemaSignumFlammae,
        pmc:ThelemaSignumVibrationis ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission teaches an operation whose effect, if it occurred, would be evident to anyone standing by, the operative passage itself carries a clause that puts the effect beyond a bystander's reach: by reserving the perception of it to the operator, by reserving its occasion to a case that may not arise in an ordinary life, or by making the effect depend on the operation being kept from the public. The clause stands as a property of the operation and not as an account of a session that produced nothing. Where instead the outcome a transmission stakes itself on is one a stranger can read off independently of the operator, no such clause appears and the operation is shown." .

pmc:TheExcuseThatStopsAtTheOfficiant a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Entschuldigung des Nichtwissens endet beim Vollzieher"@de,
        "The excuse of not knowing stops at the officiant"@en ;
    skos:note "Its relation to pmc:ThePenaltyLaidOnAnotherParty is neighbourhood and not containment, and the two are easy to confuse. That statement is about where a cost lands: away from the performer, on a family, a lineage, the dead, a class of beings. This one is about who may plead. They can hold at once and in the Mandaean material they do: the same corpus that refuses the officiant the plea of ignorance also, at Ginzā 15,1, calls a father to account for a daughter who went to the soothsayers. A transmission can move the cost and withhold the excuse, and the two devices leave the same person paying for something he did not do."@en,
        "The counter-search changed the wording twice and the changes are worth keeping. The Fon case removed any claim that the unwilled defilement is generally excused: the statement now says only that the transmission may relieve that participant, because plainly some do not. The Hawaiian case removed the word always from the second clause and put in its place the scope condition that the transmission be one which charges the officiant at all, since a transmission silent about the officiant neither confirms nor refutes."@en,
        "The counter-search over the compiled holding was carried in two directions. Looking for transmissions that excuse an officiant on the ground of ignorance: nothing. Looking for transmissions that handle unwilled defilement at all, three things came back and none of them falls inside the statement. The Hawaiian hula company restores a member defiled by contact with a corpse through a cleansing with sea water and turmeric, and a wilful transgression is not covered by it and requires penitence and confession: the transmission grades by will, exactly as the statement says such transmissions may, but it says nothing about the teacher who performs the cleansing, so it carries the antecedent and is silent on the consequent. The Fon material runs the other way and shows that the relief is not automatic anywhere: a man who kills a python of the sacred kind, even by accident, is by custom liable to be burned alive. And the Atharvavedic hymns carry the petition form — if knowing, if unknowing, we have committed sins, free us from them — which is a prayer about the state of souls and not a rule about the state of a rite. The Mandaean prayerbook carries the same petition in the same words and likewise states no rule; see pmp:MandaeanQolastaIrritumSearch."@en,
        "The limit of the derivation, stated because it is the weakness a later reader would otherwise have to find. All three witnesses come from one transmission, and all three reach the graph through one recorder, who reports two of them as rules she was told and the third as a rule with two named cases attached. What the three establish is therefore a property of Mandaean priestly practice as it was recorded in Iraq in the nineteen-thirties. Widening it does not mean restating it more boldly: it means opening another manual of ritual faults — the Zoroastrian Nērangestān, the expiation books of the Śrautasūtras, the Chedasūtras of the Jain penance order — and asking the one question above of each. All three are named in the holding and none of them is opened."@en,
        "The three cases, which are three different participants and one rule. An infant soils itself during its baptism: the transmission states in so many words that its baptism is not invalidated, because it sinned unwittingly, and the priest must nevertheless go through a complete baptism at the hands of a ganzibra and a priest. A candidate's wife menstruates, miscarries or gives birth during his consecration: the pollution invalidates the consecration and everyone concerned in it, beginning with three hundred and sixty-six baptisms for the officiating ganzibra, and nothing is said of her. Matrons examine a bride and are mistaken about her virginity, which the record says happens: the priest who then married her is debarred until he has received fifty full baptisms and the ganzibra three hundred and sixty, and the matrons are not mentioned again."@en,
        "What makes this a statement rather than a description of three accidents is the argument the transmission itself supplies in the first case and does not extend. It knows the distinction between a defilement willed and one not willed, it names it, and it applies it to the one participant who by no construction could be held to a standard — an infant — while the officiant, who was equally unable to prevent the event, is charged. The relief is available in principle and is not on offer to him."@en,
        "What would refute it, and it is a cheap thing to look for in any manual of ritual faults: a passage relieving an officiant of a prescribed penalty on the ground that he did not know of the defilement or could not have prevented it. One such passage settles it, because the statement is about what a transmission provides and not about what officiants did. The nearest place to look first is the part of the Alf Trisar Šuialia titled blow and healing, which is the Mandaean book of faults and their remedies and could not be obtained in the run that wrote this; see pmp:MandaeanManualisAccessusSearch."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:MandaeanInfansSineScientia,
        pmc:MandaeanPaisaq,
        pmc:MandaeanUxorisTamia ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission attaches a penalty to a defilement that arose during its own procedure through the body or the act of a participant who is not the officiant, it may relieve that participant on the ground that he did not know or did not will what he did, and it charges the officiant the stated penalty in every case, admitting no corresponding ground on his side even where the fact that triggered the penalty was one he could not have established and the event one he could not have prevented."@en .

pmc:TheFailureConditionFollowsWhatIsWithheldExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Prüfprotokoll zum Sitz der Misslingensbedingung"@de,
        "Protocol on where the failure condition sits"@en ;
    skos:definition "An attempt to fail the statement that a transmission's failure condition sits on whatever it keeps out of writing. The protocol is a reading protocol: what is claimed is a relation between two features of operative texts, so it fails or holds against texts and not against practitioners."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at procedure without cases. The procedure is in the falsifier and is cheap to run on any corpus already open: establish whether the transmission declares its written text sufficient, then find its stated failure condition and ask whether a man holding the text could meet it determinately. Nobody has yet gone through a corpus with that pair of questions."@en,
        "Two corpora in this holding are the obvious first cases and both are named in the counter-search note at the generalization: the Hekhalot handbook, whose preparation is stated in counts and whose penalty for breaking it is quotable, and the Clavicula, whose failure clause is general enough to cover everything. If either turns out to declare its text sufficient and to name a determinate further condition, the statement falls."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, protocol drawn from the wording of the six witnesses; no cases collected" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "An operative transmission that declares its written text the whole of its operation and nevertheless names a determinate further condition — a term, a bodily state, an observable sign — which a possessor of the text could fail to meet and could be seen to have failed; or one that expressly withholds part of its operation from writing and places its stated failure condition on the written part instead."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-19"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:TheFailureConditionFollowsWhatIsWithheld .

pmc:TheFirstMenstrualBundle a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Bündel des ersten Flusses und was seine Störung bewirken soll"@de,
        "The bundle of the first flow and what disturbing it is said to cause"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 143 and p. 162" ;
    skos:definition "The young woman wraps her first menstrual discharge and places the bundle in a plum tree, high enough that coyotes cannot reach it. The reasons given are three: the plum is the emblem of fruitfulness preferred by the Buffalo God; anyone or anything obtaining part of a first discharge thereby gains an influence over the woman that may be used to make her do foolish or shameful things; and the bundle has a potency of its own, so that if it is disturbed it may cause eruptive diseases of the skin and the falling of the hair."@en ;
    skos:note "The corpus adds elsewhere that the menstrual flow is very wakan and will cause diseases of the skin and the genitals, that some medicine men make a love medicine of it if they invoke the right spirits, and that its influence gives paralysis. The three passages are consistent with each other and are cited together."@en,
        "The last clause is the reason this node is in the file. The corpus does not leave the claim as a doctrine: it names the evidence it rests on, in the form see young men with pimply faces and many coyotes without hair. That is an appeal to a distribution in the world, made by the transmission itself, and it is the only one of its kind found in this run. It is also plainly checkable and plainly weak, and both belong in the record."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LakotaRitual .

pmc:TheIntervalAfterTheWound a pm:Converging ;
    rdfs:label "Der Verletzungsort, an eine gezählte Todesfrist gekoppelt"@de,
        "The wounded site coupled to a counted interval of death"@en ;
    skos:definition "Two medical transmissions do the same unusual thing with a wound: they take the site that was struck as the thing that fixes how long the dying takes, and they state the length in counted days. The needling-prohibition chapter of the Suwen gives six depots with six day-counts and pairs each with a bodily movement by which the strike is to be recognised. The sixth chapter of the Susruta's anatomical book sorts a hundred and seven marmas into five classes by the consequence of their injury and gives the two lethal classes their terms, seven days for the one and a fortnight or a month for the other. In both, the number rides on the place and not on the severity, and in both the interval is stated as doctrine rather than as a range of observations."@en ;
    skos:note "So what the other two transmissions share is narrower and better than this node first claimed, and it is still a finding. Not that a medical transmission couples a wound to a duration — all three do — but that the duration is stated as a number indexed on the wounded site. The Greek indexes the number on the season; the Chinese indexes six day-counts on six depots; the Indian indexes two terms on two classes of a hundred and seven junctions. On the narrower claim the Greek is a clean negative case rather than a merely silent one, which is stronger evidence than silence: a corpus that counts days after a wound and refuses to make the count depend on the place is a corpus that considered the question."@en,
        "That counter-comparandum was read in one section of one treatise, and the run of 2026-10-03 read the treatise the Aphorisms had left unopened. The finding survives and the statement it supports has to be narrowed by one word. On Injuries of the Head 19 does attach a counted term to a head wound — fever before the fourteenth day in winter and after seven days in summer, death before seven days in summer and before fourteen in winter — so the Greek corpus is not silent about how long the dying takes after an injury. What it does not do is index that number on the site. Chapter 2 grades the skull by site at length, gives the bregma the shortest dying and the back of the head the longest, and then says in one clause that in fatal cases a man will survive longer in winter than in summer, whatever be the part of the head in which the wound is situated. The place orders the duration and the calendar counts it, and the treatise states the separation itself."@en,
        "The counter-comparandum, which is the part of this node that was actually missing. Hippocrates, Aphorisms VI.18, names seven parts whose severe wounding is deadly — bladder, brain, heart, diaphragm, small intestines, stomach, liver — and attaches no interval to any of them, no sign, and no gradation. That matters because the same section of the same work counts days twice, at seven days for a sudden apoplectic seizure and at forty for a gouty inflammation. The Greek corpus can state a term and does not state one for a wound. So coupling a site to an interval is not simply what a medical transmission does, and the agreement between the other two is a finding rather than a genre."@en,
        "The identifier of the counter-comparandum is pmc:HippocratesVulneraLetalia in traditions/hippocratic.ttl, and it is deliberately not named with pm:convergingConcept: it is the case that does not converge, and a property that listed it among the converging concepts would report the opposite of the finding."@en,
        "Two further things the same reading puts beside the candidate, neither of them entered as converging. The Greek term is attached to a case the chapter defines by the surgeon having missed the injury, so the number measures a neglect and not an anatomy; nothing of the kind is reported of either of the other two. And the Greek declares the sign-set invariant to the age of the wound, which is the only explicit invariance statement any of the three makes. The nodes are pmc:HippocratesVulnusCapitisLocus, pmc:HippocratesVulnusCapitisTempusAnni, pmc:HippocratesVulnusCapitisSignaAequalia and pmc:HippocratesVulnusCapitisCulpaMedici in traditions/hippocratic.ttl, and they are deliberately not named with pm:convergingConcept for the same reason pmc:HippocratesVulneraLetalia is not."@en,
        "What is not shared, and it is more than what is. The Chinese list is of six organs and is reached by the needle; the Indian list is of a hundred and seven junctions of muscle, vessel, ligament, bone and joint, and is reached by a weapon or a surgeon's knife. The Chinese chapter pairs each interval with a sign; the Indian chapter pairs each class with a physiology of fiery and lunar virtues. The Indian one adds a rule that moves a site between classes according to the depth of the wound and whether it was struck in the centre or at the edge, and the Chinese one has nothing of the kind. A convergence of the form of a claim is all this is."@en,
        "Where the two readings weaken the candidate as the issue stated it. The Indian terms are not as firm as a summary makes them: the translation gives one term in a parenthesis at the enumeration of the classes and a different one in the body of the verse sixteen places later, and the chapter's own shift rule lets a site be reassigned according to how it was struck. The Chinese terms are in open dispute inside their own canon, which the holding already carries: three chapters of the Suwen give three sets of numbers and the earliest reachable witness disagrees with the received text at two of six places. A convergence between two disputed interval-sets is still a convergence of form, and the form is what is claimed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:convergingConcept pmc:CizhongWuzang,
        pmc:SusrutaMarmabheda ;
    pm:independenceGround "The ground is a date against a route and it is weaker than it looks, which is why it is stated at length rather than asserted. The needling-prohibition chapter belongs to the Suwen, whose material is placed by the specialist literature between the late Warring States and the Han; the marman doctrine belongs to a work whose composition Meulenbeld places over several centuries, beginning in the last centuries before the common era, with a redaction traditionally assigned to the third or fourth century. The documented channel between Indian and Chinese medicine runs through Buddhism from the second and third centuries and becomes effective in the Six Dynasties, which is later than the earlier end of both fixations and not later than the later end of either. So the independence holds against the documented route for the early stratum of each and is not established for the redaction of either, and neither fixation date was read at a primary source in this run — both are taken from the secondary literature and neither work's own dating was examined. A protocol on this node would have to settle the stratum before it settled anything else, and the holding's own dispute node on the Chinese intervals shows that the received text is not a single stratum."@en ;
    pm:independentAttestation pmt:Ayurvedic,
        pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmc:TheIntervalRidesOnTheClassification a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Frist hängt an der Klassifikation, nicht am Organ"@de,
        "The interval rides on the classification, not on the organ"@en ;
    skos:note "Its relation to the other statements in this directory. It is not a case of pmc:CountedThresholdNeverFallsDue, which is about counts of repetitions of an operation and what the transmission does when the count is reached without the promised effect. Here the count is not of acts but of days, and what is promised is harm and not benefit — which is why the device that statement found is absent here and a different thing is being claimed."@en,
        "The counter-search over the holding, carried before this was written and recorded at pmp:IntervalReleaseClauseSearch. Nine nodes in the holding couple a term of harm to a counted interval, and the one that refutes the statement this run first intended does not touch this one: the Kongo claim that a man whose name is called dies within ten days attaches its term to a name and not to a place on a body, so there is no classification of sites to be had and nothing to be classified. Of the rest, the bare-depot pulses of Suwen 19 are a second instance of the first half within the same canon, which makes them a strengthening of the Chinese witness and not an independent one; the ninth-day blessing of the Romanusbuechlein attaches a term to a break in a leg with no classification of sites anywhere in the booklet, which is the nearest thing in the holding to a refutation of the first half and falls short of it because a broken bone is not a wound at a site chosen from a list."@en,
        "The three instances. The Susruta sorts a hundred and seven marmas into five classes named by what an injury to them does — kills at once, kills after a term, kills when the shaft is drawn, maims, pains — and gives the terms to the classes and not to the sites, so that the Kshipra of the foot and the Stanamula of the chest share a term by sharing a class. The Suwen's needling-prohibition chapter classifies by depot, which is a classification of the body that the canon uses everywhere, and gives each depot its day-count. The Aphorisms name seven parts in a row with one predicate between them, deadly, and the same section states a term at seven days and another at forty for two courses of disease, so the corpus counts days where it has a course to count them along and does not where it has only a list of parts."@en,
        "The weakness, and it is the same weakness the holding's other generalizations carry. Three witnesses, of which the negative one is a translation with the Greek absent and one section of one work read; the Indian one is a translation in which the terms are stated twice and not alike; and the Chinese one stands in a dispute node of its own because three chapters of the same canon give three sets of numbers. The statement is about what transmissions do with intervals, so a disputed interval-set is still an instance; but a protocol on any of the three would have to settle the text before it settled anything else."@en,
        "What the statement is for. The convergence between the Indian and the Chinese interval-sets is a correspondence of form and could be a coincidence of two medical traditions both being thorough. If the interval is produced by the classification, it is not a coincidence but a consequence, and the question to ask of a third transmission is not whether it counts days but whether it sorts wounded sites into kinds. That question can be put to a text in an afternoon."@en,
        "What would refute it, in two halves because the statement has two. A transmission that lists fatal sites without classifying them and nevertheless gives each one a counted term refutes the first half. A transmission that sorts wounded sites into classes by the manner of their dying and gives no term to any class refutes the second. Either case settles it, and both are the kind of thing a reader of a medical or a ritual-surgical corpus would meet without looking for it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:CizhongWuzang,
        pmc:HippocratesVulneraLetalia,
        pmc:SusrutaMarmabheda ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission attaches a counted interval to a wound, it first sorts the wounded sites into classes by the manner of their dying and attaches the interval to the class; and where it only lists the sites whose wounding is fatal, it attaches no interval, even where the same text counts days for the course of a disease. The counted interval is therefore a property of a classification of sites and not of the sites themselves, and a transmission acquires it by classifying rather than by observing."@en .

pmc:TheKingSaysTheDivinersLied a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der König sagt öffentlich, die Wahrsager hätten ihn belogen"@de,
        "The king says publicly that the diviners lied to him"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. A. Skertchly, Dahomey as it is (London: Chapman and Hall, 1874), pp. 379-380" ;
    skos:definition "At a named ceremony in 1871, before the assembled army and the priesthood, the king of Dahomey complained that his Muslims with their Arabic inscriptions and charms had deceived him, that his cult people made all sorts of mistakes, and that the diviners who cast lots to learn the will of the gods told him many lies. He set the complaint against what he had paid: he gave them everything in his power and everything they asked for, cowries, cattle, goats, slaves and wives, and still they did not tell him the truth. The instance he named is a war: the diviners told him he would go out and meet an easy victory, and he was beaten back and disgraced."@en ;
    skos:note "The exit the king takes is the one this graph has now seen in three transmissions: the fault is put on the practitioners and not on the procedure. He says they lied, not that the casting does not answer. The Kongo corpus states the same division as a rule, that doubt of the practitioners costs nothing and doubt of the ordeal costs a life."@en,
        "The most valuable single passage found in this run. It is a failure of the oracle reported by the client, in public, with the prediction, the outcome and the fee all named, and it was taken down by a European who was present and had no stake in the complaint."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Vodun .

pmc:TheMissionEnteredTheNkisiCategory a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Mission trat in die Kategorie des geladenen Dings ein"@de,
        "The mission entered the category of the charged thing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), p. 240",
        "W. Holman Bentley, Pioneering on the Congo (London: Religious Tract Society, 1900), vol. I, pp. 236-237 and p. 281" ;
    skos:definition "Four statements from inside the corpus, all of them by missionaries reporting their own trade. The word chosen by the old Catholic missionaries for holy was nkisi, and the word they chose for church was nzo ankisi, which is the ordinary word for a grave and means the house of the charged things, so that they spoke of the church of Rome as the Roman grave. The Kongo name for the devil is stated to have stood for four hundred years. The image of the Virgin in the Catholic church at San Salvador is one of the places a couple goes for a child, the church itself being called the house of the charged things. And the crosses, metal discs and small pictures given by the priests to be worn are called by the same word as an ordinary protective amulet."@en ;
    skos:note "It also fixes the date. A term in liturgical use since the end of the fifteenth century cannot be treated as untouched in a corpus written down between eighteen eighty-seven and nineteen fourteen, and any agreement between this transmission and the Christian material this graph holds is reception until somebody shows otherwise."@en,
        "This is the node that carries the contact route of the whole file, and it is entered as a node rather than as a note on the tradition because a note would not survive a query. It shows the route in the direction that matters here: not that the mission borrowed a word, which would be a fact about the mission, but that the objects of the mission were classified by the transmission under its own category, which is a fact about the transmission."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:KongoNkisi .

pmc:TheObservationAnsweredByNarrowing a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Ueberlieferung bringt die Gegenbeobachtung selbst und antwortet, indem sie die Behauptung verschiebt"@de,
        "The transmission raises the counter-observation and answers it by moving the claim"@en ;
    skos:note "Falsified by a transmission of the kind described which puts against its own claim an observation introduced as something nevertheless seen, and then either withdraws the claim or names an observation that would count against it. One such case refutes it. The likeliest places to look are the transmissions in this holding whose texts carry a real internal check beside their claims, since a check is an observation already licensed to count: the Zande poison oracle with its corroborative second consultation, the forensic criteria of this same transmission at pmc:SanhedrinBloodAndDungAsTheTraceOfTheDeed, and the assays of the external alchemy. The second of these is the sharpest test available, because it would show one transmission doing both things, and it is the cheapest, because the pages are already read."@en,
        "The counter-search changed the wording once and the change is worth keeping. The statement first read that the transmission answers by restricting the claim, which the Aboda zara witness does not do: there the claim is left exactly as it was and the observation is given another cause. The third limb was added for it, and with it the statement stopped being about narrowing alone, which is why its own label is now narrower than its content. The label is left as it is because two of the three witnesses narrow and the file is named after them."@en,
        "The counter-search over the compiled holding, in full, because a statement with three witnesses from one transmission owes its negative results plainly. Looking for a transmission that raises an observation against its own claim and then withdraws the claim: nothing outside the bounding case above. Looking for transmissions that raise a counter-observation at all, five came back and none of them falls inside the statement. The internal alchemy puts the practitioner's own complaint that he has held to the work and seen no result at the head of a chapter and answers it with two conditions in the operator, which is an empty run and belongs to pmc:NoFailedRunCountsAgainstTheOperation. The Zhen'gao reclassifies the one observation that would end the practice, the practitioner's death, as the attainment, which is a rule of reading imposed in advance and not an answer to an objection. The Visuddhimagga states that the absorption does not arise in spite of his practising and supplies ten remedies, which is again an empty run. The Zande oracle prescribes a second consultation whose contradiction voids the verdict, which is the opposite construction and the holding's best instance of an outcome allowed to count. And the Confessio excludes a class of patients in advance, before any observation has been made. The distinction that separates all five from the three witnesses is the same one: they provide for the case before it arises, and these answer it after somebody has put it."@en,
        "The limit of the derivation, stated because it is the weakness a later reader would otherwise have to find. All three witnesses come from one transmission and from one of its works, the Babylonian Talmud, and two of the three from the same editorial layer that supplies the objections. What the three establish is therefore a property of the Babylonian discussion and not of transmissions in general, and the statement's reach is the reach of its witnesses. Widening it does not mean restating it more boldly: it means opening another corpus that argues with itself in the same manner and asking the one question above. The holding names three such places and has opened none of them - the Palestinian Talmud at these places, the commentarial apparatus of the Siku quanshu recensions that the Chinese divination file quotes at second hand, and the quaestio literature of the Latin schools."@en,
        "The three witnesses are three answers to the same form of objection and that is why they are held together. At Holin 7b the claim is that nobody who consulted R. Hanina about a wound from a white mule stayed alive; the discussion says that we see however that some stay alive, and the claim is read to mean, and is healed; it says that we see however that these heal too, and the claim is restricted to mules whose feet are white at the ends. At Aboda zara 55a the claim is that there is nothing in the idol; Zonin says that we see however that people go in broken and come back healed, and R. Aqiba grants the cure and assigns it to an appointment in time which the affliction was keeping anyway. At Sanhedrin 67b the claim is that there is none beside him even in a matter of sorcery; the objection is raised from the transmission's own saying that the practitioners defy the college above, and the answer is that it was different with R. Hanina, whose merits were great."@en,
        "What distinguishes the three answers, and the statement names all three because the holding should be able to tell them apart. The first restricts the predicate - dying becomes not healing. The second restricts the subject - white mules become mules with white feet-ends. The third and the Aboda zara case leave the claim alone and work on the observation instead, one by an exception in the person it happened to and one by a cause that was going to operate regardless. A successor statement that collapses them will lose the only interesting thing here, which is that a transmission under pressure has more than one move and reaches for different ones on consecutive pages."@en,
        "What the counter-search found, and it is a bounding case rather than a refutation. Two pages away from one of the witnesses the same transmission does something the statement forbids: at pmc:HolinThreeTimesAgainstMosesOnce a comparative claim is grounded on a count of three dividings of a river, the discussion objects that perhaps it happened only once, and the claim is given up rather than narrowed. The objection there is about the arithmetic of the evidence and not about an observation that contradicts the claim, so it falls outside the antecedent as worded; but it stands as proof that the editorial voice which narrows is capable of conceding, and that the move recorded here is a habit and not a mechanism. The statement is left standing and the case is recorded, on the principle the holding follows at the fifteenth and twentieth witnesses of pmc:NoFailedRunCountsAgainstTheOperation: a claim is corrected where it is examined."@en,
        "Why the objection being the transmission's own is the whole point, and the holding has been waiting for it. The same construction stands in the holding already, described from the outside, at pmc:BiansuiDispute: a first-century critic put a counter-example to a practitioner of the day-choosing arts three times and was answered three times with a fresh condition, ending at a clause that covers every remaining case. The note at that node says in as many words that a critic reporting the move is not a transmission supplying it, and the note at pmc:NoFailedRunCountsAgainstTheOperation repeats the reservation and declines to count it among its witnesses. The three witnesses here are the transmission supplying it, in its own canonical text, with the counter-observation raised by the editorial voice and not by an opponent, and in the Holin case twice within four lines. pmc:BiansuiDispute is therefore not a witness of this statement either, and it is now the outside description of something the holding can document from the inside."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:AbodaZaraAfflictionsSwornToTheirHour,
        pmc:HolinWhiteMuleClaimNarrowedTwice,
        pmc:SanhedrinWhetherTheArtPrevailsAgainstTheDecree ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission states a claim about what has or has not power, and the same transmission then puts against it an observation of its own, introduced as something that is nevertheless seen, it answers by restricting what the claim asserts, by restricting what the claim is about, or by granting the observation and reassigning its cause; it does not withdraw the claim, and it names no observation that would be allowed to count against it. Each answer leaves the claim covering a class the objector cannot enumerate or a cause he cannot reach."@en .

pmc:TheOmenDischargedOnTheBird a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Hahn wird getötet, damit das Vorzeichen an ihm selbst eintritt"@de,
        "The cock is killed so that the omen falls on itself"@en ;
    dcterms:source "A. B. Ellis, The Ewe-speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1890), p. 96 and p. 98" ;
    skos:definition "It is a bad omen for a cock to crow in the middle of the night, and the bird is immediately killed in order that the omen may be fulfilled at its own expense."@en ;
    skos:note "A procedure for discharging a prediction on a substitute, and thereby the cleanest case in this file of a claim that cannot come out wrong: the bad thing foretold has happened, to the bird. The graph holds nothing else of this shape."@en,
        "The witness draws his own conclusion two pages later, that to these people there is no such thing as an accident. That is his sentence and not the transmission's, and it is recorded here rather than entered as a claim."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Vodun .

pmc:TheOneWhoStaysOutsideTheState a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der zweite Beteiligte, der den Zustand nicht betritt"@de,
        "The second participant who does not enter the state"@en ;
    skos:note "Falsified by a transmission that has its practitioner enter such a condition, states a means of checking the operation that only the practitioner himself could apply, and names no second person — or, more sharply, by one that requires solitude for an operation in which the practitioner is said to lose command of himself. The second shape is the one to look for first, and the corpora most likely to hold it are the solitary vigil and the fast: the Lakota hanblapi, the incubation literature, and the desert material, all of which this holding already carries and none of which has been asked this question."@en,
        "The counter-search was carried against the compiled holding before this was written, and it came back full, which is why the statement has a second clause. The Ars notoria requires the orations to be pronounced alone, secretly, and in a voice barely audible to the speaker; the great Paris papyrus states that its rite is for one person alone, who is to raise himself, and that the mysteries may be handed on by a father to a son and to no one else. Both are genuine opposites of the first clause and neither is a counterexample to it, because in both the effect is bound to something the operator monitors himself: the exactness of the wording, the number of repetitions, the day of the moon and the hour. Where self-checking is possible the transmissions ask for solitude; where it is not, they ask for a second person. The statement was rewritten to say that, instead of the first clause alone, which the holding would have refuted on the day it was written."@en,
        "The three witnesses, each stating the condition in its own way and each stating a consequence. In Greenland the seeress asks for women who know the lore called Varðlokkur, none is found, the household is searched, and after the song is finally sung by a Christian woman who says she is no practitioner at all, the seeress reports that the spirits had before this wished to part from them and give them no obedience, and that things now lie plain that were hidden. Among the Chukchee the recorder puts it as an incapacity: without one who gives answering calls, a shaman considers himself unable to perform his calling in a proper way, and novices induce a brother or a sister to respond. In the Fourth Way material the impossibility is argued rather than reported: a person cannot awaken alone, and an agreement among twenty to wake one another is still insufficient, because all twenty may fall asleep at the same time and dream that they are waking."@en,
        "Weakened, without being refuted, if the three turn out to be reachable from one another. No route between them has been proposed and the two older ones lie far apart; the third is modern and its recorder was a man of letters in Saint Petersburg and Constantinople, so a route from the ethnographic literature of his own decades to his account cannot be excluded and has not been examined. That is the weakest of the three legs and the statement rests on it for the argued form of the claim rather than for the claim itself."@en,
        "What it does not say. It says nothing about whether these operations produce anything, and nothing about whether a second person makes any difference to what they produce. It is a statement about the shape a transmission takes, and it can be settled by reading, which is why it stands here and not at a protocol."@en,
        "What the three have in common is the reason, and in the third case the source states it: the sleeper cannot detect his own lapse, so the detection has to come from outside him. The same holds in the other two without being spelled out, because in both the practitioner is by then in the state the operation consists in. That is why the second person is not an assistant in the ordinary sense — nothing in any of the three has him fetch, hold or prepare anything. His office is to remain in the condition the practitioner has left."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:AwakeningPresupposesAnotherWhoDoesNotSleep,
        pmc:ChukcheePresupposesAnsweringCalls,
        pmc:NorseSeidrPresupposesVardlokkur ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission has its practitioner enter, during the operation, a condition in which he can no longer check whether the operation is going as it should, it names a second person who stays outside that condition and makes the outcome depend on him, and it states that without that person the operation does not come off. Where the transmission instead binds the effect to something the operator can check on himself while he does it — an exact wording, a count, a prescribed hour — it names no such person and may require him to be alone."@en .

pmc:TheOrdealGiverMustStandTheOtherOrdeals a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Betreiber muss die übrigen Proben selbst bestehen"@de,
        "The practitioner must stand the other ordeals himself"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), pp. 264-265" ;
    skos:definition "Beside the bark the corpus lists seven lesser ordeals with their Kikongo names, by hot knife, by boiling palm oil, by a bracelet in the oil, by knotted grass, by a bean on a twisted string, by a glass bead under the eyelid and by hot water. Of these the corpus states a condition on the practitioner: unless he is prepared to undergo each of them, the bark excepted, before the accused does, he is little respected and would soon lose his practice."@en ;
    skos:note "A qualification requirement that the practitioner meets in public, and the only one in the file. The corpus states in the same place how it is met: the practitioner rubs baobab juice on the arm of any accused person who pays for it, and on one who will not pay he rubs another decoction which is not protective, and the recorder adds that the practitioners undoubtedly know of juices that help them bear the tests and are willing to sell the knowledge."@en,
        "The bark is excepted, and the exception is the whole of the difference: the ordeal that kills is the one the practitioner does not have to take."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:KongoNkisi .

pmc:TheProtectionAndWhatWouldShowItFailedExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Prüfprotokoll zum Schutz und woran sein Versagen zu sehen wäre"@de,
        "Protocol on the protection and what would show it failed"@en ;
    skos:definition "An attempt to fail the statement that transmissions naming the harms their protections avert mix the recordable with the unrecordable and never make the recordable ones the test. The protocol is a reading protocol: what is claimed is a regularity in what operative texts say, so it fails or holds against texts."@en ;
    skos:note "A second and independent way to fail it: find a second transmission of the Fon kind, where a protection is attached to a single recordable harm with no companion. Two such cases would turn the other side of the statement from an existence proof into a class, and would raise the question the statement cannot now put — what distinguishes the transmissions that risk a checkable claim from those that do not."@en,
        "The counter-search is the run itself and not a separate act, because the protocol is a reading protocol: the way to find work that contradicts a claim about what texts say is to read texts. Both volumes of Campbell Thompson were searched with the three-part falsifier and with eleven patterns chosen to catch a prognosis, a term or a sign, and every hit was read in context. The search returned nothing against the statement. It is entered as found nothing rather than as not carried, and the note above says what was searched so that a reader can judge how much that is worth: one edition, two volumes, one series of tablets."@en,
        "The procedure is stated in the falsifier and is cheap to run: take an operative text that promises protection, list the harms it names, sort them by whether a bystander could tell the harm had occurred, and see whether the text sorts them too. It was entered at procedure without cases and moved to cases without deviation on the same day, when the first corpus was gone through with it."@en,
        "What the state now records and what it does not. One corpus has been gone through and shows no deviation, which is a great deal less than the statement asserts: the statement is about operative transmissions in general and one edition of one series is one case. The namburbi rituals proper, which are the corpus this protocol first named, are edited in a copyrighted work and were not reached; they are still the strongest place for a second run, and a run there that also came back empty would be worth more than the first."@en,
        "Where it was run first, and what came back. The apotropaic literature written for practitioners rather than for buyers was chosen on the reasoning that a manual which has to answer a dissatisfied client has a reason to say in advance what the protection does not cover. Campbell Thompson's two volumes of the Utukki Limnuti and the Fever Sickness and Headache series were read through on 2026-09-20 with the three-part falsifier in hand. No operative text in either volume names a sign, a term or a condition of failure, and the search instead produced a seventh witness on the mixing side. The run, its search terms and its limits are at pmp:ApotropaicHarmListSearch."@en ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundNothing ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Searched: R. Campbell Thompson, The Devils and Evil Spirits of Babylonia, Luzac's Semitic Text and Translation Series vols. XIV and XV, London 1903 and 1904, both volumes complete, on the whitespace-normalised text, with eleven patterns chosen to catch a prognosis, a term or a sign — shall recover, shall die, shall live, shall be healed, be cured, not be cured, thou shalt know, seventh day, third day, if he, unless — and with the protection formulae located by their own wording. Came back: nothing against the statement. No operative text in either volume names a sign, a term or a condition of failure; the only conditionals and the only terms in the two volumes stand in the editor's introduction and in the hemerology of unlucky days. The full run is at pmp:ApotropaicHarmListSearch." ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithoutDeviation ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, protocol drawn from the wording of the witnesses and run on 2026-09-20 against Campbell Thompson 1903-1904, both volumes complete; one corpus, no falsifier found" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "An operative transmission that names the harms its protection averts and does any one of three things: marks off the harms whose occurrence a bystander could establish from those whose occurrence nobody could record; names one of the recordable harms as the test of whether the protection held; or states a condition under which the protection is to be judged to have failed."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-20"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:TheProtectionAndWhatWouldShowItFailed .

pmc:TheProvisionForNotKnowingExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Prüfprotokoll zur Vorsorge für das Nichtwissen"@de,
        "Protocol on the provision for not knowing"@en ;
    skos:definition "An attempt to fail the statement that a corpus which makes an operation depend on identifying what is dealt with also carries a standing way of proceeding without the identification. A reading protocol: the claim is about what operative corpora contain, so it fails or holds against corpora."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at procedure without cases. The procedure: take a corpus in which the operator must name, identify or trace the origin of what he is treating; establish that the requirement is stated and not merely implied; then read the whole of that corpus for a covering disjunction, a formula for the unknown case, a key of identification, or an apparatus that works without the name. The statement fails if the reading comes back empty."@en,
        "One methodological trap, recorded because this run nearly fell into it. A corpus can look as though it has no provision simply because the holding entered only its strict passages. The Egyptian material carried the requirement for two weeks before the chapter that hands over the answers was read, and had the statement been written then it would have had a false refuter in it. The protocol therefore requires that the whole corpus be read, and not the nodes the holding happens to carry from it."@en,
        "Run it on the Zande poison oracle first, for the reason given at the statement: it is the one corpus in the holding whose procedure takes the name from outside itself, and the ethnography that would settle it is a single well-known monograph rather than a scattered literature. A second target with the opposite property is the Finnish collection, where the provision is already known to exist and the open question is the reverse one — how often the origin-words are actually supplied, against how often the formula for not knowing them is."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, protocol drawn from the wording of the witnesses; no cases collected" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "An operative corpus that makes an operation depend on naming, identifying or tracing the origin of what is being dealt with, states that requirement rather than implying it, and contains nowhere in itself a covering disjunction, a formula for the case of not knowing, a key by which the identification can be made, or an apparatus that answers the same encounters without a name."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-20"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:TheProvisionForNotKnowing .

pmc:ThePythonProhibition a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Das Töten einer Python, auch aus Versehen"@de,
        "Killing a python, even by accident"@en ;
    dcterms:source "A. B. Ellis, The Ewe-speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1890), pp. 57-59" ;
    skos:definition "Among the tribes of this worship a native who kills a python of the sacred kind, even by accident, is by custom liable to be burned alive, and a European to be beheaded. A child who touches or is touched by one of the pythons must be kept a whole year in the seminaries of the god at the expense of its parents and taught the songs and dances of the worship; adults, and particularly women, were formerly liable in the same way, and the witness states that the scandals this produced and the decline of priestly power have let the practice fall into disuse."@en ;
    skos:note "A named case is printed with it, from a French cleric on the coast: a young enslaved woman crushed a python under a door, the priests demanded her, her owner declined to pay the composition, and she and five men were shut in the hut. Four of the men had bribed the escort and were brought through the crowd unharmed with her; the fifth, who had not paid or could not, was beaten until he escaped into a cask in the lagoon. What the transmission prescribes and what it enforces are two different things, and the difference is measured here in bribes."@en,
        "The penalty is the finding, because the source records it in the act of becoming a form. Fifty years before the writing a native was invariably burned to death; at the time of writing the appearance of the sentence is preserved and the culprit escapes with his life. A hut of dried grass is built, the man is thrust inside unbound and the hut is fired, so that he breaks out long before the flames reach him, and the worshippers waiting outside beat him with clubs until he has purified himself by reaching running water."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:DanhGbi ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by Ewe and Fon informants at Whydah, unnamed, with one case taken from the published account of the Abbé Bouche and one from Des Marchais of 1731, both named by the recorder as he uses them; recorded by A. B. Ellis, colonial officer on the West African coast, published 1890; given in Ewe and Fon, of which the work prints the terms, the accounts in English." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Vodun .

pmc:TheRepairWithoutASignExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Prüfprotokoll zur Wiedergutmachung ohne Zeichen"@de,
        "Protocol on the repair without a sign"@en ;
    skos:definition "An attempt to fail the statement that a repair carries a sign exactly where the harm it repairs was observable. The protocol is a reading protocol and not a field one: what is claimed is a regularity in what operative texts say, so it fails or holds against texts."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at procedure without cases and not at no procedure devised. The procedure exists and is stated in the falsifier: open an operative text that prescribes a repair for a fault, find the harm the fault is said to cause, and see whether the text says how the practitioner would know the repair took. Nobody has yet gone through a corpus with that question."@en,
        "The first corpus to run it against is named at the generalization and is unopened: the Vedic expiation books of the Śrautasūtras. That is a good first case because the harm is already known from the exegesis, so only the second half of the question is open, and because a failure there would fall on the strongest of the four witnesses."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, protocol drawn from the wording of the four witnesses; no cases collected" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "An operative text that attaches an unobservable or deferred harm to a fault in its own procedure, prescribes a repair for the fault, and in the same text names either a sign by which the repair is known to have taken effect or a condition under which the repair fails."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-19"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:TheRepairWithoutASign .

pmc:TheRestrictionOnPassingItOn a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Überlieferung beschränkt ihre eigene Weitergabe, im operativen Text selbst"@de,
        "The transmission restricts its own passing on, inside the operative text"@en ;
    skos:note "A third witness was added on 2026-09-11, from a transmission with no route to either of the first two. It fits the statement in all three particulars: the restriction stands inside the instruction and not in an external rule, the permitted recipient is named as the right person and no other, and the restriction is referred to the scripture from which the method comes. It adds a feature the statement does not claim and which is worth watching for in further cases, a material pledge whose quantity is left to the giver and whose point is said to be the sincerity."@en,
        "Falsified by an operative text of the same kind whose restriction on transmission stands only in an external rule, a colophon or a later ordinance, with the working text itself silent. The grimoire literature and the Sanskrit ritual manuals are the corpora where such a case would turn up."@en,
        "It also bears on how such texts should be read. A restriction stated inside the working formula is part of the procedure as the text has it, so a modern reader who ignores it is not merely disobeying an outside rule but performing something the source says is not the practice. Whether that difference makes any difference is a question for a protocol and not for this node."@en,
        "The Ars Notoria puts the prohibition in the mouth of the angel who declared the oration: the holder is to presume neither to give it to another nor to expound anything out of it, neither himself nor any one by him nor any one after him. The Paris papyrus states in the opening prayer that these are mysteries a father alone may hand on, and that only to a son, an initiate worthy of the craft."@en,
        "This is the gate the project exists to remove, stated by the transmissions in their own words, and that is the reason to hold it as a claim rather than as a remark. A record that releases such material into the public domain is obliged to be exact about what it is overriding: not a later institution's secrecy, but an instruction the operative text carries about itself."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:ArsNotoriaSecretumKautel,
        pmc:PgmIvTraditioClausa,
        pmc:ShangqingMengjin ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Transmissions of an operative text state the restriction on passing it on inside the text itself rather than in an external rule, name the class of permitted recipient, and attribute the restriction to the authority the text claims for its own contents."@en .

pmc:TheRivalWhoWasNotInTheRoom a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Erklärung, die einen abwesenden Operierenden nennt, und dasselbe Korpus, das die Rolle lehrt"@de,
        "The account that names an absent operator, and the same corpus that teaches the part"@en ;
    skos:note "Falsified by an operative transmission that names an absent rival among its accounts of an empty result and nowhere in its corpus treats hostile operation as something a practitioner does — the excuse without the attack. It would show the account to be borrowed rather than entailed, which is the opposite of what the statement claims. Weakened, without being refuted, if the three here turn out to be reachable from one another; no route between the Solomonic, the Atharvavedic and the Zande material has been proposed, and the Zande contact question stands open in this holding as undecided."@en,
        "The counter-search was carried against the compiled holding before this was written, for transmissions that list the causes of an empty result. What it found on the other side is that the third-party account is not universal: the Chinese burial book assigns its adversities to the year, the season, the ground and the conduct of the descendants and names no rival geomancer; the internal alchemy assigns failure to the practitioner and to the absence of a teacher. The statement is accordingly worded to say what such a list contains where it contains this member, and not that every list does. Whether the presence of the member correlates with the corpus teaching hostile operations at all is the count a later run should make; the three here are consistent with it and three is not a count."@en,
        "The witnesses, and each carries the pair in a different way. The Key of Solomon, listing why the spirits may not have come, supposes that they were being conjured in some other place or part of the universe by some other exorcist by the same conjuration — and the book that supposes it is the book that other exorcist would be using, so the attack is the transmission's own procedure in another pair of hands. The Atharvaveda supplies the attack in the clearest form any corpus here offers: let perdition smite his offering before it comes true, let them smite his truth with untruth, let not that meet with success which he yonder offers — and, in the same collection, the hymn in which performers say that desiring to accomplish the sacrifice they did not accomplish it. The Zande list of the eight causes of an oracle contradicting itself contains witchcraft and sorcery, in a transmission whose whole account of misfortune is that witchcraft is something people have and sorcery something people do."@en,
        "What it does not say. It says nothing about whether such interference occurs. It is a statement about what a corpus makes available to its practitioners, which is why it can be settled by reading and why it belongs here rather than at a protocol."@en,
        "What makes the pairing more than an observation about human nature. An account of failure that named a natural obstacle — the weather, the season, a bad batch of poison — can be checked by anyone who was there, and the same three transmissions carry such accounts beside this one. This account cannot, and the reason is structural rather than rhetorical: the party named is defined by being elsewhere. A transmission reaches that shape not by evasion but by taking its own claims seriously, since a corpus that teaches an operation performed at a distance on a person who does not know it is being performed has thereby licensed the explanation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:BengeFailureCauses,
        pmc:ClaviculaCausaeNonAdventus,
        pmc:SacrificiumAlienumCorruptum ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where an operative transmission enumerates the accounts available for a session that produced nothing, one of the accounts is the action of a hostile or competing operator who was not present; and the same corpus elsewhere treats that action as an operation somebody deliberately performs, prescribing or describing how it is done. The excuse and the attack are transmitted together, so that the account is available to every practitioner of the transmission and can be neither confirmed nor refuted by anyone who attended the session."@en .

pmc:TheRuleAgainstReportingTheAbsence a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Regel trifft das Sagen, dass nichts geschah, nicht das Nichts"@de,
        "The rule falls on saying that nothing happened, not on the nothing"@en ;
    skos:note "A sixth witness added on 2026-09-26 from the Turba philosophorum, Dictum 27, and it is a variant of the third form rather than a plain instance, which is why the difference is stated here. The three forms this statement names all operate on an utterance: the saying causes the failure, the saying is an offence, the saying is evidence about the speaker. The Turba's case operates on an inference and an act. Gregorius reports a man who, when the redness was somewhat slow in appearing, imagined that he had made a mistake and so relinquished the work; the work records the inference as his error and the loss as his own, and the sentence immediately after it tells the reader that the redness does not delay in coming. Nothing is said about reporting anything. What is closed off is the reading of a delay as information, which is the same result reached one step earlier: a man who may not take the absence for a fact has nothing to report."@en,
        "Falsified by showing that the witnesses are not of one kind — that in each case the rule is against disclosing the method or against public complaint in general, rather than against the report that the result failed to come. The Chinese pair resists that reading because both rules name the content of the forbidden saying and it is in both cases that the thing did not work. Falsified in the other direction, and more cheaply, by a count: if a survey of the operative transmissions in this holding found rules of this shape in a small minority of those that make a checkable promise, the statement would be describing five cases and not a device, and should be withdrawn to a remark about them. That count has not been made and the statement claims a recurrence and not a proportion."@en,
        "The Turba case also shows the device without the penalty, and that is worth separating. The other five witnesses attach something to the utterance — a forfeited cure, a stated penalty, a fault found in the speaker. This one attaches nothing and needs nothing: the loss is supplied by the transmission's own assurance that the redness was on its way. A successor statement will have to decide whether the class is defined by the consequence or by the reading it forecloses, and this witness is the case that forces the question."@en,
        "The distinction to keep, because it is where this statement could be read too widely. A rule of secrecy about the method is not an instance. Transmissions that forbid disclosing the procedure are numerous in this holding and they say nothing about the outcome; what counts here is a rule that falls on the report of what came of it. The anthroposophical case is the near edge — it is a silence about the outcome and reads as a rule of method — and it is included on the ground that the text names the pupil's own formulation of his result as the thing to be suppressed. A run that wanted a narrower class would drop it and keep four."@en,
        "The pairing is what makes the two worth more than either. One transmission states the device as its own rule; a contemporary outside the other describes an identical device as a swindle and names the man who ran it. The graph therefore holds the same construction under both descriptions, from the same century and the same language area, without either text depending on the other. It is not a claim that the one transmission is the other's fraud, and nothing here says so."@en,
        "The three the counter-search added, each carrying a different form. The anthroposophical course puts the pupil's result under a rule of silence — be silent about your spiritual sights, indeed be silent about them even before yourself — after printing in advance the colours he is to see; the one act by which a perception could be compared with another pupil's or held against the announced colours is the act forbidden, and the prohibition reaches the pupil's own reflection. The Zande corpus states the third form as a ranking: doubt of the charged things and of the practitioners costs nothing, but to scout the power to bewitch or to sneer at the ordeal is held to prove the doubter a witch himself. And the Hekhalot descent attaches its whole penalty at the sixth palace not to a deed but to a question, so that asking about the nature of the water is the failure."@en,
        "The two that carry the first form, and they are the reason the statement was written. A fourth-century Chinese report of a fraud at Xinggu quotes the operative rule word for word: though you are not cured at once, tell people that you are cured, and you will certainly be cured; but if you tell anybody that you are not yet cured, then in the end you will never be cured — such is the law of the Way, and you must not fail to trust it. The chapter then records what the rule produced: those who came later asked those who had come before, the earlier ones always said they were cured, none dared say he was not, and within ten days the operator was immensely rich. The admonitions of the Celestial Masters, of the same century and a different transmission, state the second form as doctrine: one who on meeting calamity says that serving the Dao is of no benefit thereby commits an offence of three thousand, and an earlier passage of the same work says that a man who does not blame himself but cries out that serving the Dao is of no benefit deepens his transgression by it."@en,
        "Untested. No pm:Testing points here, which is the state and not an omission. What a protocol would have to fix first is the population, and the hard part is that the device is invisible where it works: a transmission whose adherents do not report failures leaves no trace of the rule unless the rule is written down. The five witnesses are the ones that wrote it down, and a protocol counting them against transmissions that did not would be counting the legible against the illegible."@en,
        "What the counter-search found on the other side, and it is the half that keeps the statement honest. Four transmissions in this holding do the opposite. A king of Dahomey complains in 1871 before the assembled army that the diviners took his cowries, cattle, slaves and wives and told him many lies, naming the war they promised him and the defeat he got — the client reporting the failure in public, with prediction, outcome and fee. The Zande poison oracle requires the second consultation and makes the validity of the first depend on it, so that the checking question is not forbidden but prescribed. A Kongo witness reports eating the forbidden thing without consequence, which is a member of the transmission reporting a negative result about its own prohibition. And a European blessing corpus admits a case in which the words were used without effect and a physician then cured the same complaint, entered as a case rather than reclassified as the physician's excuse. The device is therefore common and not universal, and a transmission that carries it is not thereby of a kind with the others."@en,
        "Why the device is worth a name of its own rather than a line in the statement about where a failed run is put. Those rules dispose of an outcome and leave the practitioner free to say what happened; this one leaves the outcome alone and closes the channel. The two can therefore be stacked, and in one transmission they are: the Celestial Masters both assign a relapse to the patient's bad character and make the complaint about the absence an offence. Where both are present, a run that produced nothing is explained and the explanation cannot be contradicted by the person it was applied to."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:CmYanhuiWuyi,
        pmc:GehongMaShi,
        pmc:SteinerSchweigeGebot,
        pmc:TurbaExemplumRelinquentis,
        pmc:WaterQuestionCaution,
        pmc:WhatMayBeDoubtedAndWhatMayNot ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "A recurring device in operative transmissions attaches a consequence not to a failed performance but to the practitioner's report that the result did not come. The consequence takes one of three forms: the saying is made the cause of the failure it would report, so that a man who says he is not cured has thereby forfeited the cure; the saying is made an offence in its own right, with a penalty stated in the same register as the transmission's other penalties; or the saying is made evidence about the speaker, so that the doubt is read as a fault in him rather than as information about the procedure. In every instance the rule stands in the operative text and not in a later gloss, and it operates on the one report that a transmission of this kind could be held against."@en .

pmc:TheSpecialistIsPaid a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Fachmann wird bezahlt, und gut bezahlt"@de,
        "The specialist is paid, and paid well"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulo VI, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces" ;
    skos:definition "The recorder states that the practitioner is paid very well for the consultation and is additionally courted with food and drink after their own fashion."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because payment is one of the gates this project counts, and because it stands beside the other one: the same chapter says the practice is hidden from outsiders. A transmission can be closed by price and by secrecy at once, and the two are not the same gate."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NahuaRitual .

pmc:TheTestOnTheIngredientExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Pruefprotokoll dazu, wohin das Handbuch die Probe legt, die es durchfallen kann"@de,
        "Protocol on where the handbook puts the test it can fail"@en ;
    skos:definition "An attempt to fail the statement that an operative handbook assays its materials and not its operation. The protocol is a reading protocol and not a field one: what is claimed is a regularity in what handbooks say, so it fails or holds against texts."@en ;
    skos:note "A second corpus was run through with the same question on 2026-09-25, the first volume of the Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, three hundred and fifty pages read at the wording. It produced three cases at once: one for the first limb, one against the second, and one for the second, all three in the same work. Two counter-cases from two corpora with no route between them are no longer a property of one booklet; the proportion this protocol wants is still not measured, and what is measured now is that both arrangements occur inside a single handbook."@en,
        "A third corpus was read through with the question on 2026-09-26 and it did not oppose the statement; it broke the statement's form instead. The Turba philosophorum states two assays, both of the operation, both provided for, and not one assay of a material. The protocol had been looking for a corpus that would refute the second limb and found one that empties the first, which is why the count of cases stays where it was and the generalization above now carries the note saying the successor statement cannot keep the default-and-exception shape. That is a result the falsifier did not name, and the state below is unchanged because the falsifying condition was not met."@en,
        "Entered at procedure without cases and not at no procedure devised. The procedure exists and is stated in the falsifier: open a handbook that prescribes operations with materials, list every act it states with a definite perceptible outcome, and for each one ask whether the thing decided is a material or the operation, and if the operation, whether a repetition rule or an external cause stands beside it. Nobody has yet gone through a corpus with that question; what has been done is a sweep of the nodes already entered, which is a sweep of what earlier runs happened to find worth entering and not a count."@en,
        "Moved from procedure without cases to cases with deviation on 2026-09-22. One corpus has been run through with the question and it produced one case, and the case is against the statement's second limb. That is a single case and it does not measure how common the arrangement is; what it does is remove the universal, which is all a single case can do and all this protocol claims."@en,
        "The first corpus the protocol named as its next step, the second bilingual handbook of the same Egyptian find, is still unopened and is still the right next step — now for a different question. The universal is gone; what is open is the proportion, and for that the protocol needs corpora counted rather than corpora sampled."@en,
        "The first corpus to run it against is named at the generalization and is unopened: the second bilingual handbook of the same find. That is a good first case because it is the nearest neighbour of the work that supplies the second witness, so a divergence there would be the cheapest possible refutation and a concurrence the cheapest possible strengthening — and because a handbook of the same milieu removes the objection that the arrangement belongs to a genre rather than to the transmissions."@en ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Searched a third time on 2026-09-26 in the Turba philosophorum, read complete at the wording in Waite's translation of 1896, for every act with a definite perceptible outcome and for the clause standing beside it. Two were found and both assay the operation, both with a repetition rule: the powder taken in the hand at Dictum 28, impalpable as water or else cook again, and the look for the powder at Dictum 13, a powder or else a stronger fire. Neither opposes the statement. No assay of a material was found anywhere in the work, and the whole of it was read, so the absence is of the corpus and not of a sample; the one sentence that comes near, nothing enters therein unless it be pure at Dictum 36, states a requirement and no way of meeting it. Also inspected and not assays: the forty-day bound on the blackness at Dictum 21, which is a term on a state and not an act performed to establish anything, and by the precedent this protocol set for the Romanus-Büchlein's ninth-day term is excluded on the same ground; the signs of success at Dictum 27, 37 and 69, which are appearances announced in advance and carry no act; and the several openings of the vessel, which report what will be found and state no alternative."@en,
        "Searched again on 2026-09-25 in Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, read at the wording throughout, for every act with a definite perceptible outcome and for the clause standing beside it. Six were found and they divide. Assays of a material, no excuse attached: the look of purified mercury, p. 38, and the four appearances of the swallowed morsels, p. 140. Assay of the operation with a repetition rule: the weight test after the swallowing of gold, pp. 86 and 134, boil it again until the weight reverts. Assay of the operation with no rule and no external cause: the five marks of incinerated mercury, p. 175, with the named failing class sajiva baddha at p. 314, which is declared not to possess the properties of incinerated mercury and not to be efficacious. That one opposes the statement's second limb and is the second case of its kind in this protocol. Also inspected and not counter-cases: the grass test for the heat of the sand apparatus, p. 259, which establishes a heat and not an operation, and the red colour seen through the flask at p. 100, which is an endpoint and not a verdict."@en,
        "Searched on 2026-09-22 in the Romanus-Büchlein, read at the wording throughout in the reprint in Scheible, Das Kloster III (Stuttgart 1846), pp. 489-523, for any act with a definite perceptible outcome by which one of its own operations is established, and for whatever clause stands beside such an outcome. One case came back and it opposes the statement: the demonstration against disbelief at the third blood-staunching, p. 503, which stabs an animal and predicts that it will not bleed, with nothing attached to the case in which it does. Two further passages were read as candidates and are not counter-cases: the shepherd's trial at pp. 511-512 vouches for a substitution of apparatus and states no outcome, and the ninth-day term for a broken leg at pp. 518-519 is a deadline on an effect and not an act performed to establish anything."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, protocol drawn from the wording of the four witnesses and from a sweep of the holding's assay nodes; three corpora since read through with the question, the Romanus-Büchlein on 2026-09-22, the first volume of the Rasa-Jala-Nidhi on 2026-09-25 and the Turba philosophorum on 2026-09-26" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "An operative handbook that states, of its own operation, an act with a definite perceptible outcome by which the operation is established to have worked or not to have worked, and attaches to the negative outcome neither a rule of repetition nor a cause lying outside the procedure."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-26"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:TheTestOnTheIngredient .

pmc:TheUnsympatheticWitnessAsStatedCauseOfFailure a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der anwesende Unbeteiligte als genannte Ursache des Misslingens"@de,
        "The person present who is not in sympathy, named as the cause of failure"@en ;
    skos:note "A case bearing on both clauses at once was read on 2026-09-22 and is deliberately not added to the witnesses, because it would misreport what it is. The American mental healer of the eighteen-forties held the excluding condition in the standard form — if a sceptic sat too near, he failed — and dropped it, along with the storm and the steel, in the same movement by which he made his results checkable: he adopted the rule that every command to his subject be given mentally, so that he could be sure of them, and he then spent years exhibiting the operation in public to rooms that held it for a fraud and threatened him with mob violence. One transmission, first clause and then second, with the transition visible. The nodes are pmp:QuimbyExhibitioPublica and pmc:QuimbyTonitruumNonObstat."@en,
        "A third witness was added on 2026-09-16, from a transmission with no route to either of the first two, and it answers the weakness this node states of itself further down. That complaint was that the derivation leaned on one rule and one report, a rule being the stronger kind of evidence for a claim about what transmissions provide. The Finnic case is a rule, and more than a rule: it is an operative formula with its own number in the collection, said against whoever looks with jealousy, looks pryingly with eyes askew, casts resentful glances or prepares some malevolence — its speaker opening with the admission that he cannot guard against envy nor shield himself from opponents while dealing with these toilsome tasks."@en,
        "Falsified by a transmission of the first kind — one person operating on another, witnesses admitted, the outcome certifiable only by operator or subject — which names no unsympathetic witness among its causes of failure, or which prescribes that the operation be shown to a doubter as a proof. Sharper still, and the shape to look for first: one which states that the effect holds in the presence of a hostile observer. The corpora likeliest to carry it are the ones in this holding where a practitioner heals another before a village, and the Azande witch-doctor material and the Kongo and Ifa corpora have not been asked this question."@en,
        "It fits the statement in the first clause and departs from it in the second, and the departure is the interesting part. The danger named is the same and it is located in the same place, in the disposition of a person present rather than in his presence: the formula asks that whoever looks without envy be blessed with honeyed eyes, so the onlooker as such is not the variable. What the transmission does about it is not exclusion. It curses the envious looker in advance — may the slag of Hiisi fill his eyes, may a fiery bung plug up his mouth — and blesses the other. Exclusion appears in this corpus only outside the songs, in the collector's account that the bath-house was heated as secretly as possible so that no envious person should get wind of it."@en,
        "It is not a refutation and the falsifier should not be read as satisfied by it. What that falsifier asks for is a transmission of the first kind which shows its operation to a doubter as a proof; here what was shown to doubters was of the second kind, and the healing practice proper — one man explaining to another until he admits the point, with the outcome certified by the patient — admitted no audience at all. The two halves of this man's career fall on the two sides of the statement, which is what makes the case worth recording and what stops it from settling anything."@en,
        "Its relation to pmc:TheOneWhoStaysOutsideTheState is inversion and not contradiction, and the two turn on one property between them. There the practitioner enters a condition in which he cannot check his own performance, so a second person outside it is required and the operation fails without him. Here the outcome cannot be checked from outside at all, so a second person outside the communication is excluded and the operation fails with him. Both statements are decided by where the capacity to check the operation lies, and a reader who wants to press either should press that: the first predicts a second person where the operator cannot monitor himself, the second predicts exclusion where nobody else can monitor the result."@en,
        "So the deciding property is not the strangeness of the operation, nor the presence of witnesses, but whether the thing claimed can be established by somebody other than the parties to it. Where it can, the transmission has nothing to lose by an audience and gains a public record; where it cannot, the audience is the one circumstance under which the absence of the effect would have to be admitted, and the transmission accounts for that absence in advance. The second clause of the statement is the counter-search written into the claim rather than appended to it."@en,
        "So the statement should now be read as two claims that have come apart in a third case: that such transmissions name the unsympathetic person as a cause of failure, which all three witnesses support, and that they prescribe his exclusion rather than a trial before him, which two support and the third meets by a different means. Whether acting on the onlooker and removing him are one provision or two is a question for a protocol and not for this node, and it is the first thing a protocol on this claim should settle."@en,
        "The counter-search over the compiled holding, and the finding that shaped the statement. The Zande poison oracle is a transmission in which one man questions and another administers the poison to a fowl, the consultation is held in public, the bystanders may interrupt the questioner to have a point put that he has left out, and the transmission names eight causes to which a failure of the oracle is attributed — the wrong variety of the creeper, a breach of taboo, witchcraft, the anger of the owners of the forest, the age of the poison, the anger of the ghosts, sorcery, and use. Not one of the eight is a person present who is not in sympathy. The same transmission requires a second, corroborative consultation and holds two tests indispensable wherever the relations between two persons are at issue. That is a genuine opposite of the first clause and no counterexample to it, and the reason is the outcome: the fowl dies or it does not, and every bystander sees which."@en,
        "The two witnesses, each stating the condition in its own terms and each stating what follows. Among the Tungus, where too many of those present are not susceptible the audience reacts too slowly and the performance may fail; it may still be carried out formally, as a sequence of well known actions, without attaining its aim; and the presence of a single person who remains outside may suffice to disturb the rest. In the French manual only one witness is to be admitted, always the same and in communication with the operator, and all the curious and especially all the incredulous are to be banished, because where the stranger is incredulous, suspects the subject's sincerity or makes a jest of what he sees, the subject is troubled and loses his lucidity."@en,
        "The weakness of the derivation, stated because a reader would otherwise have to find it. It stands on two claims, which is the floor the class sets and not a recommendation. The two are far apart and no route between them has been proposed, but they are not equally strong: the Tungus claim is a recorder's report of a stated cause of failure, with the recorder adding, as his own reading, that public opinion is nonetheless inclined to see the cause in the shaman; the French claim is a rule written by a practitioner for practitioners. A rule and a report are not the same kind of evidence for a statement about what transmissions provide, and the statement rests on the rule more heavily than on the report."@en,
        "What it does not say. It says nothing about whether any of these operations produces anything, and nothing about whether the presence or absence of such a person makes any difference to what they produce. It is a statement about the shape a transmission takes, it can be settled by reading, and that is why it stands here and not at a protocol."@en,
        "What that case does to the statement is to supply its mechanism rather than another instance of it, which is why it sits here as a note. This statement already says that the deciding property is whether the thing claimed can be established by somebody other than the parties to it. The American case shows the property operating inside a single practice over a few years: as the outcome became a thing a bystander could read off — a place somebody in the audience was thinking of, a body a physician could afterwards examine — the unsympathetic witness stopped being named and the doubters were charged admission. It also shows that the movement can run this way round, which the two witnesses could not: they are transmissions caught at one state each."@en,
        "What the two have in common is not a complaint about scepticism, and the French case makes that plain: the witness the manual most sharply excludes is a physician who is not hostile but merely unfamiliar with the practice, on the ground that the subject would wish to convince him, would conduct his conversation with address, and would lose the simplicity his clairvoyance requires. The author gives a case of his own in which a sympathetic expert — a pupil of Mesmer, invited to instruct him — deranged the whole course of the somnambulism without touching the subject. The rule is about a person outside the communication and not about a person with an opinion."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:CautionAgainstWitnesses,
        pmc:FinnicCautionAgainstTheEnvious,
        pmc:PerformanceFailsWithoutASusceptibleAudience ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission has its operation performed by one person upon or through another, admits witnesses to it, and makes the outcome a condition of a person that only the operator or the subject can certify, it names the presence of a person not in sympathy with the operation as a sufficient cause of its failure, and prescribes the exclusion of such persons rather than a trial conducted before them. Where instead the outcome is a discrete event that any bystander present can read off independently of the operator's report, it names no such cause, admits the bystanders, and may build the check into the procedure."@en .

pmc:TheWaiverRunsBothWays a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die äußere Forderung wird an beiden Enden erlassen, die an Körper und Verhalten an keinem"@de,
        "The outward requirement is waived at both ends, and the requirement on body and conduct at neither"@en ;
    skos:note "Falsified by an operative transmission that sets an outward requirement, releases its adepts from it, and gives the practitioner who cannot meet it no second route to the same result but exclusion; or by one that waives a bodily or conduct requirement — a fast, a washing, a term of continence, a rule of diet or of anger — for either class, at either end. Either of those breaks it, and the second would break the earlier statement with it."@en,
        "The count is four claims in three transmissions and one of the three supplies two of the four. Read as a spread that is three and not four, and the statement is worded as a recurring shape and not as a law."@en,
        "The counter-search was carried against the compiled holding before this was written, and it was put as two questions. First, whether any transmission here waives a bodily or conduct requirement for a class of practitioner — the search that would break the second clause. Second, whether any transmission, faced with a practitioner who cannot meet its outward requirement, gives him no second route but removes him. Both returned something and neither refutes."@en,
        "The other half of the same lesson is the second witness and it is stated in the next verses. That person who, without casting off at least the graver faults of both body and mind, uses rasayana never reaps the effects of rasayana; and rasayanic combinations succeed with those who are cleansed in respect of mind and body and who have their souls under control. Nobody is released from that, and it is the only thing in the lesson that accounts for a user in whom nothing happened. The lesson is willing to call its chamber optional and is not willing to call its conduct optional."@en,
        "The upward half comes from two transmissions that know nothing of each other or of the first. The Key of Solomon says that so exact a preparation of days and hours is not necessary for those who are adepts in the art but is extremely necessary for apprentices and beginners, and a search of both its books found no corresponding relief from the bath, the fast, the continence or the confession for anybody. The eighteenth chapter of the Baopuzi says that one who can guard the One need not divine the day or choose the hour, no longer consults the siting calendars and does not avoid the year star or the taboos of his year and fate — while the eighth chapter of the same book says that the circulation of the qi is of no benefit in the hours of dead qi, so that the same author sets the condition and grants the exemption from it."@en,
        "The witnesses, and the Indian one carries both halves at once, four sections apart in a single lesson. The rasayana lesson of the Caraka builds a chamber with thick walls, small openings and two outer rings of rooms, and binds the entry into it to the sun's northern course, the lighted fortnight, a propitious lunation and constellation and a favourable muhurta and karana. It then says that for those endued with strength, free from disease, intellectual, self-controlled, forgiving and having men and riches at their command, for them only is the ordinance about entering a hut beneficial; unto those of a different disposition the ordinance does not apply, and they take the rasayana exposed to sun and wind instead. The same section offers a third preparation expressly to those who seek ease and are unable to seek out herbs and undergo the discomforts of administration. The chamber and its calendar are therefore waived for the majority of users, and the result claimed is not reduced."@en,
        "To the first, the nearest cases are the ones the earlier generalization already records and one more. The Zhen'gao raises the question explicitly about a bodily condition and answers no: even a person entered in the jade registers is not thereby exempt from the loss that frequent emission causes. The Shangqing term of eighteen years attaches no condition that would exempt a practitioner of that standing who still casts a shadow at noon. No passage found waives a fast, a washing, a term of continence or a dietary rule for anyone."@en,
        "To the second, one clear case and it bounds the first clause. The Tungus material reports that a shaman who is old and physically weak cannot carry out very long and difficult performances, and that a shaman may be excused from attending only if she is female and pregnant or otherwise under a prohibition; without such an excuse he may lose his position. There the deficient practitioner gets no lighter route — he loses the office. The difference from the three witnesses is that the requirement he fails is not an outward one but his own strength, so the antecedent of the statement is not met; but the case shows what the alternative looks like and it is why the statement is worded about outward requirements and not about requirements in general."@en,
        "Weakened, without being refuted, if the three transmissions turn out to be doing the same thing for unrelated reasons, which is the likeliest way this fails. A medical handbook that offers an easier route is selling to more people; a grimoire that exempts its adepts is flattering them; a chapter arguing that one method replaces several thousand others has an interest in cancelling the others' rules. Three different motives could produce one pattern, and the pattern would then be a fact about who writes operative literature and not about what the literature holds. The statement is examinable either way: a fourth and fifth transmission with the same asymmetry and different motives would tell against the coincidence."@en,
        "What the four together show and what they do not. Three transmissions, no proposed route between any two of them, and in each the thing released is a time or a place and in each the thing not released is a state of the practitioner. That is the whole of the claim. It says nothing about whether either kind of requirement does anything; a transmission may be waiving the date because it never mattered or because the adept has grown past it, and this statement does not choose."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:AyurvedaDosatyagapurvakatva,
        pmc:AyurvedaKutyadhikarin,
        pmc:ClaviculaAdeptiExempti,
        pmc:ShouyiMianLiri ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where an operative transmission sets a requirement that is fixed outside the practitioner — a date, an hour, a place built for the purpose, a rarity of material — and then releases anyone from it, it releases at both ends: the practitioner who has attained and the practitioner who cannot meet it, the second by being given a second route to the same claimed result. Where the same transmission sets a requirement bearing on the practitioner's body or conduct, it releases nobody from it at either end, and that is the requirement it names when it accounts for a user in whom nothing happened."@en .

pmc:ThelemaCautioContraMagistrum a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Schließe dich einem Meister an und glaube ihm in nichts"@de,
        "Attach yourself to a master and believe him in no wise"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber E vel Exercitiorum sub figura IX, chap. VII, §§ 6-7, and chap. I, § 6, in The Equinox I,1 (London, March 1909), pp. 33-34 and 25" ;
    skos:definition "The instruction's closing advice and its immediate qualification: during the whole of this elementary study and practice the pupil will do wisely to seek out and attach himself to a master, one competent to correct him and advise him; and let him further remember that he must in no wise rely upon, or believe in, that master, but must rely entirely upon himself and credit nothing whatever but that which lies within his own knowledge and experience. The same instruction says earlier that the experimenter is encouraged to use his own intelligence and not to rely upon any other person or persons, however distinguished, even among ourselves."@en ;
    skos:note "The caution that matters most for the criterion this project works by, because it is aimed at the gate of authority from inside a tradition that operates one: the words even among ourselves are in the text. It sits uneasily beside the sanction that the order will take no official notice of experiments not recorded as prescribed, and both are entered."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ThelemaScriptumRecordum ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberEVelExercitiorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaCautioContraRealitatem a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Gegen das Fürwirklichhalten von irgendetwas davon"@de,
        "Against taking any of it for real"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber O vel Manus et Sagittae sub figura VI, chap. I, §§ 1-2, in The Equinox I,2 (London, September 1909), p. 13; quoted approvingly at Book 4 (Liber ABA), Part I: Meditation, chap. VI, p. 64 (Celephais Press electronic edition of 2004, following Magick: Book 4 Parts I-IV, York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1994)" ;
    skos:definition "The clause the ritual instruction places before all its practices: in this book it is spoken of things that may or may not exist; it is immaterial whether these exist or not; by doing certain things certain results will follow; students are earnestly warned against attributing objective reality or philosophical validity to any of them."@en ;
    skos:note "Two things at once, and the holding needs both. It is a caution against belief, issued from inside a transmission, and it is the sentence by which the same transmission places its own content beyond dispute: a claim that asserts nothing about what exists cannot be contradicted by anything that exists. The later exposition quotes it approvingly as the mark of an order whose members make no statement that cannot be verified in the usual manner, so the disclaimer is used there as a credential."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ThelemaAscensusInPlanis ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberOVelManusEtSagittae ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaCautioContraSamadhi a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Erreichung ist zu fürchten und zu meiden"@de,
        "The attainment is to be feared and shunned"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber HHH sub figura CCCXLI, note by Fra O.M. to chap. II (AAA), and chap. III (SSS), § 6, in The Equinox I,5 (London, March 1911), pp. 12-13" ;
    skos:definition "The note appended to the second meditation: at any time during this meditation the concentration may bring about the absorbed state, and this is to be feared and shunned, more than any other breaking of control, for it is the most tremendous of the forces which threaten to obsess. There is also some danger of acute delirious melancholia at the first point of the meditation."@en ;
    skos:note "The named psychiatric risk is attached to a specific numbered point of the practice, which is unusual: a caution with an address can be complied with, and a caution without one cannot."@en,
        "The strongest self-warning found in the corpus and the one worth having in the holding. The state the whole curriculum is directed at is here classed as a breaking of control and ranked as the worst of them, inside the instruction that produces it. The third meditation of the same instruction repeats the point in its own words: lose not control of the will, let not the absorbed state be thy master herein."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ThelemaDharanaNumerata ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberHHH ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaCautioContraSuamPraescriptionem a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Anweisung erklärt ihren eigenen vorigen Versuch für wenig wert"@de,
        "The instruction states that its own previous experiment has little value"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber O vel Manus et Sagittae sub figura VI, chap. VI, § 1, with chap. II ad fin., in The Equinox I,2 (London, September 1909), pp. 16 and 29" ;
    skos:definition "The opening of the last chapter of the ritual instruction: the previous experiment has little value and leads to few results of importance, its principal use being to familiarise the student with every kind of obstacle and every kind of delusion."@en ;
    skos:note "A self-warning of the narrow kind the specification asks to be harvested: a text warning against what it has just prescribed. It is not a retraction — the practice is kept and re-purposed as preparation — and that is what makes it worth a node. The same instruction also calls the methods of its two closing chapters dangerous while prescribing them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ThelemaAscensusInPlanis ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberOVelManusEtSagittae ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaCautioDeMorte a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Klausel, die das Sterben in der Stunde der Praxis abdeckt"@de,
        "The clause that covers dying in the hour of the practice"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber HHH sub figura CCCXLI, chap. III (SSS), § 12, in The Equinox I,5 (London, March 1911), p. 14" ;
    skos:definition "The closing section of the third meditation: if in this hour thou shouldst die, is it not written, blessed are the dead that die in the Lord? The practice it closes is the one whose stated signs include a body bathed in sweat or in sweat of blood and a forced cry."@en ;
    skos:note "It is entered as a caution because it names the outcome, and it is worth having because of what it does with it: the worst possible result of the practice is given a reading under which it is not a failure. That is the same move the clause about no student failing makes, at the other end of the scale."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ThelemaDharanaNumerata ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberHHH ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaCautioDeNimioLabore a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Gegen Überanstrengung, mit den leiblichen Zeichen dafür"@de,
        "Against overstrain, with the bodily signs that mark it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber E vel Exercitiorum sub figura IX, chap. IV, § 7, and chap. V, § 6, in The Equinox I,1 (London, March 1909), pp. 29-30; Liber RV vel Spiritus sub figura CCVI, § 9, in The Equinox I,7 (London, March 1912), p. 64; Liber Batrachophrenoboocosmomachia sub figura DXXXVI, § 1, in The Equinox I,10 (London, September 1913), p. 38" ;
    skos:definition "Three cautions on the same risk. In the breath practice: be very careful never to overstrain your powers, especially never to get so short of breath that you are compelled to breathe out jerkily or rapidly. In the concentration practice: avoid overstrain, this is very important. In the later breath instruction: the exercises should be carefully graduated to avoid overstrain and possible damage to the lungs. In the counting practice: care must be taken to detect the first symptom of fatigue and to stop, if possible, even before it threatens, and the practised psychologist learns to recognise even minute hesitations that mark the forcing of the attention."@en ;
    skos:note "The first of these is the only caution in the corpus that names a perceptible marker of the state it warns against — a jerky or rapid outbreath — and the last is the only one that names the marker in another person's attention. Both are therefore cautions that can be obeyed by observation rather than by resolve."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ThelemaDharanaNumerata,
        pmp:ThelemaNumerusUnoAspectu,
        pmp:ThelemaPranayamaNumerata ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberEVelExercitiorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaCautioDePericulis a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die genannten Gefahren: Ohnmacht, Besessenheit, Gedächtnisverlust"@de,
        "The dangers named: fainting, obsession, loss of memory"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber O vel Manus et Sagittae sub figura VI, chap. V, § 11, and chap. I, § 4, in The Equinox I,2 (London, September 1909), pp. 28 and 13-14" ;
    skos:definition "The named risks of the travelling practice: it might be dangerous to go too far or stay too long, for fatigue must be avoided; the danger spoken of is that of fainting, or of obsession, or loss of memory or other mental faculty. Earlier in the same instruction: the student must remain the master of all that he beholds, hears or conceives, otherwise he will be the slave of illusion and the prey of madness."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ThelemaAscensusInPlanis ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberOVelManusEtSagittae ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaClassesInstructionum a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die vier Publikationsklassen, und welche von ihnen geändert werden darf"@de,
        "The four classes of publication, and which of them may be altered"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1913" ;
    dcterms:source "A Syllabus of the Official Instructions of A∴A∴ Hitherto Published, in The Equinox I,10 (London, September 1913), p. 43" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission's own ordering of its instructions into four classes: class A consists of books of which not so much as the style of a letter may be changed, being beyond the criticism even of the visible head of the organisation; class B of books or essays that are the result of ordinary scholarship; class C of matter to be regarded as suggestive; class D of the official rituals and instructions. Some publications are composite and belong to more than one class."@en ;
    skos:note "It is entered as an ordering act and not as a doctrine because the classes are a classification imposed on texts already published, and because the same document that states them lists which books fall where, with a reason in each case."@en,
        "This is the ordering act that governs how every other node in this file must cite. Of the instructions read here, none is class A: Liber E, Liber O, Liber Turris, Liber RV and Liber DXXXVI are class B, Liber HHH and Liber III class D. For all of them the transmission itself asserts no textual invariance, and the edition read is therefore part of the claim and not a courtesy. The one book that does claim invariance is the received one, which this run did not open."@en ;
    pm:compiledBy "The A∴A∴, in the syllabus signed by its three officers" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SyllabusInstructionumAA ;
    pm:ordersClaim pmc:ThelemaSanctioNonNotitiae ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaDXXXVIOrdoPracticarum a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die dritte astronomische Praxis kommt erst nach den beiden ersten und der Lektüre"@de,
        "The third astronomical practice comes only after the first two and the reading"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber Batrachophrenoboocosmomachia sub figura DXXXVI, §§ 2-3, in The Equinox I,10 (London, September 1913), pp. 38-39" ;
    skos:definition "These practices A and B accomplished, and his studies in astronomy completed, let him attempt this practice C. The second of them is itself introduced with the assumption that the elementary difficulties of concentration have been thoroughly conquered and that mental pictures can be prevented from altering shape, size and colour against the will."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ThelemaNumerusUnoAspectu ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberBatrachophrenoboocosmomachia ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ThelemaDharanaNumerata ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaDXXXVIYieldsVisioMundi a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Zähl- und Bildpraxis sollen das Bild des Systems hervorbringen"@de,
        "The counting and picturing practices are said to yield the image of the system"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber Batrachophrenoboocosmomachia sub figura DXXXVI, §§ 0-3, in The Equinox I,10 (London, September 1913), pp. 37-40" ;
    skos:definition "The claim of the astronomical instruction: the figures of the science leave no direct impression with any precision upon the mind, and the two preparatory practices — naming a thrown number at a glance, and building a complete mental picture of oneself and one's surroundings outward to the whole field of vision — together with completed studies in astronomy, make the third practice possible."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ThelemaNumerusUnoAspectu ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberBatrachophrenoboocosmomachia ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ThelemaVisioMundiInMente .

pmc:ThelemaDisputatioDeLectione a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Intelligibly oder intelligently: für wen das Protokoll da ist"@de,
        "Intelligibly or intelligently: who the record is for"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber E vel Exercitiorum sub figura IX, chap. I, § 7, in The Equinox I,1 (London, March 1909), p. 25",
        "Liber E vel Exercitiorum, reprinted in Magick in Theory and Practice, Appendix VII, with the editor's note at that place (Ordo Templi Orientis electronic edition following the Castle Books edition, New York)" ;
    skos:definition "The two carriers of the same instruction give the requirement on the record differently. The first print reads that the written record should be intelligibly prepared so that others may benefit from its study. The reprint in the appendix of the later treatise reads intelligently prepared, and its editor notes at that place that the first print has intelligibly."@en ;
    skos:note "Not a spelling variant. Intelligibly puts the standard outside the writer: the record must be readable by others, and the purpose clause that follows says so. Intelligently puts it inside him: the record must be well made. The first is an auditable requirement and the second is a virtue, and this holding turns on the difference. Which of the two the author intended is not decided here."@en,
        "The comparison rests on two carriers of the instruction and not on three. The first print was read in the re-set edition of the ten numbers and in a page scan of the number, which agree on intelligibly. The reprint was read in an electronic edition following a later American reprint, not in the Paris print of 1929, which was sought and not obtained; the finding on that attempt stands at a search node. So the dispute is established between the first print and the reprint tradition, and a collation against the 1929 print is still owed."@en,
        "The same reprint drops the roman chapter numbers and the section numbers of the instruction altogether, so an address of the form chapter one section seven does not exist in that carrier. That is why every node in this file cites the first print."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmp:ThelemaScriptumRecordum ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberEVelExercitiorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaIgnisAccensus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Feuer entzünden durch die gezogene Figur, behauptet und beiseitegelegt"@de,
        "Kindling fire by the drawn figure, alleged and set aside"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber O vel Manus et Sagittae sub figura VI, chap. IV, § 2, in The Equinox I,2 (London, September 1909), p. 26" ;
    skos:definition "A capacity the instruction reports and immediately declines: it is alleged that some persons have attained the power of actually kindling fire by tracing the ritual figures; whether this be so or not, the power is not one to be aimed at."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also the only place where the corpus reports a capacity as hearsay within itself, in the passive and without naming the persons. Under the rules of this holding that would be an encounter and not a reading, and the instruction treats it the same way."@en,
        "The one claim in the corpus that would be settled by a single trial, and the instruction's response to it is to withhold judgment and remove it from the curriculum in the same sentence. It is entered because the move is the finding: a transmission that carries a testable claim and declines to test it has told a later reader something about its own priorities."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberOVelManusEtSagittae ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaOPraesupponitTriaPrima a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Ritualanweisung setzt Stellung, Atem und Konzentration voraus"@de,
        "The ritual instruction presupposes posture, breath and concentration"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber O vel Manus et Sagittae sub figura VI, chap. I, § 4, in The Equinox I,2 (London, September 1909), pp. 13-14" ;
    skos:definition "Before entering upon any of the practices of the ritual instruction, the student should be in good health and have attained a fair mastery of posture, of the regulation of the breath and of concentration."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity, although the neighbouring sentence names a danger. The danger is stated of failing to remain master of what one beholds and not of skipping the three preparatory practices, and reading it as the consequence of skipping would be the compiler's inference put into the source's mouth. The distinction is the one pm:prerequisiteStrength exists to keep."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ThelemaAscensusInPlanis,
        pmp:ThelemaVibratioNominum ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberOVelManusEtSagittae ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ThelemaDharanaNumerata,
        pmp:ThelemaPranayamaNumerata,
        pmp:ThelemaQuattuorSedes ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaQuinqueGeneraInterruptionum a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die fünf Klassen der Unterbrechung"@de,
        "The five classes of interruption"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1912" ;
    dcterms:source "Book 4 (Liber ABA), Part I: Meditation, chap. V, pp. 59-61 (first published London: Wieland & Co., 1912; read in the Celephais Press electronic edition of 2004, which follows the corrected text in Magick: Book 4 Parts I-IV, York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1994)" ;
    skos:definition "The classification of breaks the exposition imposes on the counted unit: physical sensations, which should have been overcome by the posture; breaks dictated by events immediately preceding the meditation; breaks partaking of the nature of reverie or day-dream, which are called the most insidious; a high class of break that is an aberration of the control itself, of the form how well am I doing it; and a fifth class with no discoverable source in the mind, which may take the form of an auditory hallucination."@en ;
    skos:note "The classification is what makes the count more than a tally: a constant total with a falling share of the first two classes is stated to be progress, so the instrument reports a direction and not only a number. That is an ordering imposed by the compiler of the exposition on the material of the earlier instruction, and it is entered as such."@en,
        "The fifth class carries the one piece of medical advice in the corpus: of such hallucinations it is said that they are infrequent and are recognised for what they are, and that otherwise the student had better see his doctor."@en ;
    pm:compiledBy "Frater Perdurabo and Soror Virakam, in the redacted dictation of 1911-1912" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberABA ;
    pm:ordersClaim pmc:ThelemaFractiCrescentesNonSuntCasus ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaResultatumRetentum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Ergebnisse werden der Anweisung entzogen, die sie verspricht"@de,
        "The results are withheld from the instruction that promises them"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber Turris vel Domus Dei sub figura XVI, § 16, in The Equinox I,6 (London, September 1911), p. 15" ;
    skos:definition "The closing section of the annihilation instruction: and of the difficulties of this practice and of the results that reward it, let these things be discovered by the right ingenium of the practitioner."@en ;
    skos:note "The instruction that claims the highest character for its results is the one that states none of them. A practice whose outcome is not described cannot be reported as absent, and the sentence is entered because that consequence is structural and not a matter of tone."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberTurrisVelDomusDei ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaSaltatioSineVoluntate a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Tanz wird unabhängig vom Willen"@de,
        "The dance becomes independent of the will"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber RV vel Spiritus sub figura CCVI, § 8, in The Equinox I,7 (London, March 1912), p. 64" ;
    skos:definition "The first of the stated results of concentration during the dance: the dance becomes independent of the will, and phenomena similar to the four stages of the seated breath practice occur, together with certain important mental results."@en ;
    skos:note "The instruction does not say what the important mental results are, here or elsewhere. That is the pattern of the whole instruction: the physical stages are described in detail and the mental ones are named and left."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberRVVelSpiritus ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaSignaScarabaeiAccipitrisLeonis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Käfer, Habicht und Löwe: drei Laute in fester Folge"@de,
        "The beetle, the hawk and the lion: three sounds in a fixed order"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber HHH sub figura CCCXLI, chap. II (AAA), §§ 11-13, in The Equinox I,5 (London, March 1911), p. 11" ;
    skos:definition "The signs of the second meditation, given as a sequence bound to stages of the practice: if the meditation has been well and properly performed, then on a sudden the drone and booming of a beetle will be heard; then, as the practitioner rises with rose and gold in the east, the cry of a hawk, shrill and harsh; and at the end, standing in mid-heaven, the mighty sound that holy men have likened to the roaring of a lion."@en ;
    skos:note "The most specific set of announced signs in the corpus, and specific in a way that matters: three distinct sounds, in a stated order, each tied to a stated stage. The conditional is the transmission's own — if thou hast well and properly performed this meditation — which makes the absence of the sound attributable to the performance and not to the claim. That is the shape this holding watches for."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberHHH ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaSignumMunditiaeEtSanctitatis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Gefühl von Reinheit und ein Gefühl von Heiligkeit"@de,
        "A feeling of cleanliness and a feeling of holiness"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber O vel Manus et Sagittae sub figura VI, chap. IV, § 3, in The Equinox I,2 (London, September 1909), p. 26" ;
    skos:definition "The stated signs of the two ritual operations: success in banishing is known by a feeling of cleanliness in the atmosphere, success in invoking by a feeling of holiness. The instruction adds in the same sentence that it is unfortunate that these terms are so vague."@en ;
    skos:note "The transmission grades its own sign as vague in the act of giving it, which is the pattern this file records at several places and the reason the withdrawal nodes below are entered as findings rather than as criticism."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberOVelManusEtSagittae ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaSignumSedis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Zeichen der Stellung: der Schmerz vergessen und jede andere Stellung unbequem"@de,
        "The sign of the posture: the pain forgotten and every other position uncomfortable"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book 4 (Liber ABA), Part I: Meditation, chap. I, pp. 26-27 (first published London: Wieland & Co., 1912; read in the Celephais Press electronic edition of 2004, which follows the corrected text in Magick: Book 4 Parts I-IV, York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1994)" ;
    skos:definition "The stated sign that the posture has been mastered: it will happen one day that the pain is suddenly forgotten and the fact of the presence of the body is forgotten, and one will realise that during the whole of one's previous life the body was always on the borderland of consciousness; and further, with an indescribable feeling of relief, that not only is this position the very ideal of physical comfort but that all other conceivable positions are uncomfortable. The exposition says outright: this feeling represents success."@en ;
    skos:note "Announced in advance and reportable only by the practitioner, which is what puts it here and not among the measures. It stands beside a criterion for the same practice that a bystander can apply — the saucer of water — and the pair is the clearest case in this holding of one transmission offering both kinds for one practice."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberABA ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaSignumSigillatum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Zeichen, verschlossen für jeden, der es nicht hatte"@de,
        "A sign sealed against anyone who has not had it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber HHH sub figura CCCXLI, chap. I (MMM), § 8, in The Equinox I,5 (London, March 1911), p. 8" ;
    skos:definition "The four coloured crosses placed within the imagined egg, of which the instruction says: herein is silence, for he that hath rightly performed the meditation will understand the inner meaning hereof, and it shall serve as a test of himself and his fellows."@en ;
    skos:note "A test whose criterion is withheld from everyone who has not passed it. It is entered because it is a stated test and because it is the limiting case of the kind: the transmission calls it a test of himself and his fellows, so the check is internal to the group by design, and no outsider can apply it or dispute a claim to have passed."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberHHH ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaSignumSudorisProprii a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Schweiß anderer Art als der der Anstrengung"@de,
        "A sweat of a different character from that of exertion"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber RV vel Spiritus sub figura CCVI, § 5 (a)-(b) with the plate and the editorial note to it, in The Equinox I,7 (London, March 1912), p. 62" ;
    skos:definition "The first of the four stated stages of the breath practice: if the breath is properly performed the body will first of all become covered with sweat, and this sweat is different in character from that customarily induced by exertion; if the practitioner rubs it thoroughly into his body he will greatly strengthen it. The second stage is stated as the ceasing of the tendency to perspire and the body becoming automatically rigid, and the instruction demands that this rigidity be described with minute accuracy."@en ;
    skos:note "The demand to describe the rigidity with minute accuracy is the only place in the corpus where a sign is to be characterised in writing rather than merely reported, and it is the point at which the sign could in principle be compared across practitioners. Whether any such comparison was ever made is not stated."@en,
        "The same instruction is accompanied by a photographic plate captioned as the breath properly performed, with an editorial note stating that it has been found necessary to show this because students were trying to do it without exertion and in other ways incorrectly. The note is the only report in the corpus of practitioners having got a practice wrong as a class."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberRVVelSpiritus ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaSignumSudorisSanguinis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "In Schweiß gebadet, oder in Blutschweiß"@de,
        "Bathed in sweat, or in a sweat of blood"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber HHH sub figura CCCXLI, chap. III (SSS), §§ 8-12, in The Equinox I,5 (London, March 1911), pp. 13-14" ;
    skos:definition "The sign of the third meditation at its height: the body bathed in sweat, or it may be in sweat of blood, with a cry forced from the practitioner; and at the last the movement of the light taken up by the brain and the spine independently of his will."@en ;
    skos:note "The independence from the will is the operative part and the one that could in principle fail to occur; the sweat of blood is offered as an alternative to ordinary sweat and so cannot be missed either way. The same section adds a clause on dying in that hour, which is entered as a caution below."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberHHH ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaTurrisPraesupponitEHHH a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Vernichtungspraxis setzt Stellung und frühere Meditationen voraus"@de,
        "The annihilation practice presupposes the posture and the earlier meditations"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber Turris vel Domus Dei sub figura XVI, § 0, in The Equinox I,6 (London, September 1911), p. 11" ;
    skos:definition "The instruction opens with the requirement: this practice is very difficult, and the student cannot hope for much success unless he have thoroughly mastered the posture and obtained much definite success in the meditation-practices of the two earlier instructions."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity because the consequence of skipping is quoted in the same sentence rather than inferred: the student cannot hope for much success. It is the only prerequisite among those read that states its own consequence, which is why the others below stand at the weaker strengths."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The student cannot hope for much success in the practice."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ThelemaAnnihilatioCogitationis ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberTurrisVelDomusDei ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ThelemaDharanaNumerata,
        pmp:ThelemaQuattuorSedes ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:TheopemptosCedesAgency a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Der gottgesandte Traum tritt die Trägerschaft ab"@de,
        "The god-sent dream cedes agency"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Iamblichus, De mysteriis III.2-3 (ed. des Places 1966)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:cededAgency ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:Theopemptos ;
    pm:situationGround "The dream is held to come from a god rather than from the sleeper, and the sleeper is described as receiving it. The axis records the account the source gives of who acts, not a verdict on that account."@en .

pmc:TheopemptosIsAtTheSleepThreshold a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Der gottgesandte Traum liegt an der Schlafschwelle"@de,
        "The god-sent dream is placed at the sleep threshold"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Iamblichus, De mysteriis III.2-3 (ed. des Places 1966)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:sleepThreshold ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:Theopemptos ;
    pm:situationGround "The account describes the condition and not the hour: sleep departing and waking beginning, or a condition between the two, with a voice, a light and an inability to move the eyes. Partial detachment of the sensory channel with the body already immobile is the criterion this coordinate names."@en .

pmc:TheopemptosLocusIsNotStated a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Der gottgesandte Traum gibt keinen Ort an"@de,
        "The god-sent dream states no locus"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Iamblichus, De mysteriis III.2-3 (ed. des Places 1966)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:locusNotStated ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:Theopemptos ;
    pm:situationGround "The passage was read for this question. It says where the communication comes from and says nothing about where the sleeper's awareness is while it arrives; nothing in it has the sleeper go anywhere. Recorded so that a later query can tell a source that was asked from a node nobody has reached yet."@en .

pmc:TheosophicalColourKeyToFeeling a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Lesung des Gefühls aus Farbe und Schwingung"@de,
        "The reading of feeling from colour and vibration"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1902" ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, Man Visible and Invisible (London: Theosophical Publishing Society, 1902), the frontispiece plate and the chapter Colours and their Meaning, pp. 81-82" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that every feeling has a colour, that the colour can be read off a body of subtle matter surrounding the person, and that the brightness of the colour measures the strength of the feeling."@en ;
    skos:note "Opened for prima-materia#361, on the suspicion that the modern one-dimensional ranking of emotions begins here. Read at the wording. The suspicion is only half borne out, and the half that fails is the more interesting one."@en,
        "What is here: a fixed key from feeling to colour, given as a list of the commonest hues; brightness and depth of colour stated outright as a measure of the strength and activity of the feeling; the vocabulary of vibration throughout; and an axis of self-reference applied to every colour alike, on which a thought with something of self in it turns downwards and one without turns upwards. The axis is the part that later scales inherit."@en,
        "What is not here, and the point of the node: a ranking of the feelings against one another. The plate is a grid of twenty-five fields, not a series, and the chapter introduces its colours as a list of the commonest ones. Where the text does rank, it ranks within a single colour by purity and luminosity, so that the same feeling has a lower and a higher form. That is a second dimension and not a scale. A later list that puts unlike feelings in one numbered order therefore does not have it from here, and prima-materia#12 cannot be answered by pointing at this node."@en ;
    pm:circulatesAs "An ancient science of the aura, reported as eastern in origin and as the common property of the older traditions." ;
    pm:originatedBy "C. W. Leadbeater; Annie Besant" .

pmc:TheosophyArousalYieldsClairaudientiam a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Feuer durch das Kehlzentrum zu fuehren bringt Hellhoeren hervor"@de,
        "Arousing the fire through the throat centre yields clairaudience"@en ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter Force-Centres, p. 296 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:TheosophyKundaliniArousal ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:TheosophyClairaudientia .

pmc:TheosophyArousalYieldsClairvoyantiam a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Feuer durch das Stirnzentrum zu fuehren bringt Hellsehen hervor"@de,
        "Arousing the fire through the brow centre yields clairvoyance"@en ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter Force-Centres, pp. 296-297 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" ;
    skos:note "The source states a partial case for this one and it is the device that keeps a failed attempt from counting: especially strong vibrations may at any time galvanize one or other of the centres into temporary activity without arousing the serpent-fire at all, or the fire may be partially roused, and in this way also partial clairvoyance may be produced for the time. See pmc:TheosophyIgnisStratificatus."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:TheosophyKundaliniArousal ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:TheosophyClairvoyantia .

pmc:TheosophyArousalYieldsConscientiam a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Feuer durch das Scheitelzentrum zu fuehren bringt ununterbrochenes Bewusstsein hervor"@de,
