pmc:TheosophyPraerequisitaArousal a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Sittliche Entwicklung, starker Wille und reines Denken, bevor das Feuer beruehrt wird"@de,
        "Moral development, a strong will and pure thought before the fire is touched"@en ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter The Serpent-Fire, pp. 299-301 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" ;
    skos:note "The prior step is the caution node and not a practice, because what the source requires first is not an exercise but a condition of the person and the presence of a teacher. The strength is pm:statedNecessity and not pm:prescribedOrder because the consequence of skipping is quoted and not inferred."@en,
        "The requirement has a second half which makes the whole unperformable from the text: the author says he has specially and intentionally refrained from explaining how the arousing is to be done or the order in which the force should be passed through the centres, and that this should by no means be attempted except at the express suggestion of a Master who will watch over his pupil during the stages of the experiment. A reader of this work therefore has the warning, the capacities and the prerequisite, and not the procedure."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The source states the condition and the consequence in adjacent sentences. It is far better to let the fire remain dormant until the man has made definite moral development, until his will is strong enough to control it and his thoughts pure enough to enable him to face its awakening without injury; and no one should experiment with it without definite instruction from a teacher who thoroughly understands the subject, because the dangers connected with it are very real and terribly serious. The consequences of acting without these are the injuries listed at pmc:TheosophyPericulumKundalini, of which the author says that to experiment with it without understanding it is far more dangerous than it would be for a child to play with nitroglycerine, and that no uninstructed man should ever try to awaken it." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:TheosophyKundaliniArousal ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:TheosophyPericulumKundalini ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy .

pmc:TheosophySituatAcquisitio a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Vermoegen liegen beim Erwerb durch Anstrengung"@de,
        "The capacities are placed on acquisition by effort"@en ;
    skos:note "The same chapter records glimpses of the astral world that arrive without the fire being aroused at all, through especially strong vibrations that galvanize a centre into temporary activity. Those are not placed here and would take the other value; they are a different claim and are held at pmc:TheosophyIgnisStratificatus."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:byEffort ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:TheosophyConscientiaContinua ;
    pm:situationGround "The source attributes the unbroken consciousness to the passing of the fire through all the centres in a certain order, the fire having been aroused by a determined and long-continued effort of the will, and says that the conquest has to be repeated in each incarnation. The coordinate is the compiler's." .

pmc:TheosophySituatAgency a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Das Wecken liegt in der eigenen Handlungstraegerschaft"@de,
        "The arousing is placed on the practitioner's own agency"@en ;
    skos:note "The placement is worth making because the same two chapters make the result depend on a Master who must suggest the attempt and watch over it, which would be a reason to read the agency as ceded. It is not: what the Master supplies is the instruction and the supervision, while the act is the pupil's own effort of will, and the text assigns the consequences of failure to the pupil at pmc:TheosophyNullaExceptio."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:ownAgency ;
    pm:situatedNode pmp:TheosophyKundaliniArousal ;
    pm:situationGround "The source says the arousing is done by a determined and long-continued effort of the will, that the will should be set in motion to arrest the fire where it rises of itself, and that the occultist is trained to guide it into a spiral course. Where it rises without that effort the text treats the event as accidental or spontaneous and not as given. The coordinate is the compiler's; the source names no axis." .

pmc:ThreeMastersInThreePlaces a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Drei Männer, die an mehreren Orten zugleich waren"@de,
        "Three men who were in several places at once"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 18 (Dizhen), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 18" ;
    skos:definition "The chapter ascribes the dividing of the form to three named men: Lord Zuo, Ji Zixun and the Transcendent Duke Ge. By it, it says, they could be in several tens of places in one day, so that there was a host in the hall speaking with a guest, and a host at the gate receiving a guest, and a host at the waterside with a fishing line, and the visitors could not tell which was the real host."@en ;
    skos:note "The ascription is to men the author counts among his own predecessors, and the third is his own great-uncle, to whom the eighth chapter also ascribes a day at the bottom of a pool. What is quotable here is not the feat but its stated form: several appearances at one time, indistinguishable, before guests who tried to tell them apart and could not. That is an outcome a witness could have reported, and the chapter names none."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:Fenxing ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BaopuziNeipian ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:ThreefoldAscentOrder a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Naturschau setzt asketische Praxis voraus"@de,
        "Contemplation of nature presupposes ascetic practice"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 1" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Physike ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Praktike ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:DesertMonasticism .

pmc:ThreefoldSeizure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die dreifache Heimsuchung"@de,
        "The threefold seizure"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 46-47" ;
    skos:definition "The office is not sought but suffered. The one marked for it is seized in infancy by an illness in which he is haunted by apparitions, and from these he learns, according to his years, what belongs to the art. Those seized a second time see more visions and gain greater knowledge. Those seized a third time — which is seldom without great torment or the utmost danger of life — arrive at the perfection of the art and become so knowing that without the drum they can see things at the greatest distances."@en ;
    skos:note "A graded scale with a stated cost at each step and a stated end state, which is the material an examination is made of. The recorder's own explanation of the seizure is that the devil takes a liking to the person in infancy; that explanation is his and is not entered as the transmission's."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SamiNoaidi .

pmc:ThresholdIsNotTheExecutionState a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der Schwellenzustand die Vermögen trägt"@de,
        "Whether the threshold state carries the faculties"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.11 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Diadochus of Photice, Gnostic Chapters 40",
        "Iamblichus, De mysteriis III.2-3 (ed. des Places 1966)" ;
    skos:definition "A disagreement about where the higher perceptual capacities are exercised. The handbooks of absorption place them in a waking, fully concentrated state and say so with a stated necessity; the ascetic literature marks the light half-sleep as the place of counterfeit consolation; the theurgic account, by contrast, treats the edge of sleep as the mark of a genuine divine communication."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. Two of the three sides name the edition or translation they were read in. For Diadochus the edition is not recoverable and is left unstated rather than guessed; the passage is to be reopened, and until then the chapter number stands on whichever numbering the earlier session used."@en,
        "Recorded as a dispute between sources and not as a verdict on the modern draft. A draft is not a side: it carries no attestation, so a claim it makes has no standing here. What the sources actually disagree about is narrower and more interesting than the draft's question, namely whether the threshold is evidential at all, and both sides of that are attested."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:Theopemptos .

pmc:ThresholdReceptionCorrespondence a pm:Converging ;
    rdfs:label "Die Schwelle als Ort des Empfangs"@de,
        "The threshold as a place of reception"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Iamblichus, De mysteriis III.2-3 (ed. des Places 1966)",
        "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005), the yoga of the clear light of sleep" ;
    skos:definition "The observation that several traditions mark the passage between sleeping and waking as the place where a communication is received, while none of them makes it the place where a faculty is exercised."@en ;
    skos:note "Two axes have to be held apart, and the modern draft that prompted this examination runs them together. On the reception axis the threshold is well attested: a voice, a light and an immobility of the eyes in the Neoplatonic account, the nightly ascent of the soul in the Kabbalistic one, the true dream in the Islamic one. On the execution axis, where sight, hearing and the mind-made body are actively exercised, the sources bind the capacities to waking absorption instead. No source found so far puts an actively exercised faculty in the threshold state."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:convergingConcept pmc:ClearLightOfSleep,
        pmc:Theopemptos ;
    pm:transmissionPath "The correspondence must not be counted as independent attestation. The Tibetan material reaches Tibet from the Indian mahāsiddha Nāropa through Marpa and is reception, as the file that holds it records. The Mediterranean witnesses are worse still for this purpose: the later Jewish and Islamic dream theory rests on a Neoplatonic substrate, so an agreement between the Greek, the Jewish and the Islamic account is a shared inheritance and not three independent observations. What would count as a finding is a tradition outside both blocks, and none has been located." .

pmc:ThresholdShutdownProtocol a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Lähmung eine Abschaltung ist und ob sie am Übergang geschieht"@de,
        "Whether the paralysis is a shutdown and whether it happens at the threshold"@en ;
    skos:definition "An examination of the posited claim in its two parts: whether the event is located at the transition into or out of sleep, and whether the mechanism is the switching off of motor control there."@en ;
    skos:note "How far the mechanism evidence reaches. The transmitter and receptor pathway was identified in rats, by pharmacological inactivation of trigeminal motoneurons; the atonia of the stage is a defining feature of it across the species studied, but the receptor-level account is not a human finding. The direction of the correction does not depend on that account, only on the atonia belonging to the stage rather than to the transition, which the human review also reports."@en,
        "Status of what follows. The state above is procedure without cases, and everything below is written in the voice of a finding. It is not one. The notes record what the evidence appeared to show before any search for opposing work was made, and they are kept in that voice because rewriting them would destroy the reasoning that has to be checked. Read them as the expected result of an examination nobody has finished, not as its outcome. See prima-materia#386."@en,
        "The location holds. Reported episodes occur on falling asleep or on waking and not in the middle of consolidated sleep, and the systematic review classes them accordingly."@en,
        "The mechanism does not hold as stated, and the correction is not a quibble. Motor control is not switched off at the transition: it is already off throughout the sleep stage in question, and the experience arises when that shutdown persists past the transition instead of ending with it. The claim has the direction backwards. What happens at the threshold is the failure to switch motor control back on, not the switching of it off."@en,
        "The word regular carries a second and separate deviation. The mechanism is regular, but the recurring form of the experience is classified as a parasomnia in the standard nosology, which is to say as a disorder. Both can be true at once and the claim does not distinguish them: a normal mechanism appearing at an abnormal moment. Whoever repairs the claim must say which of the two the word regular is doing."@en,
        "This is why the two posits in this file were kept apart. The universality claim survives its protocol; this one does not, on the same evidence, and a single node carrying both would have had to be entered as confirmed or as deviating and would have been wrong either way."@en ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchNotCarried ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Not carried. The receptor account and the nosological classification were taken as settled without any search for dissent, and the receptor work is an animal study whose translation is itself contested in the field."@en ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "American Academy of Sleep Medicine, International Classification of Sleep Disorders, 3rd edition (Darien, Illinois, 2014), recurrent isolated sleep paralysis among the parasomnias of the relevant sleep stage",
        "B. A. Sharpless and J. P. Barber, Lifetime prevalence rates of sleep paralysis: a systematic review, Sleep Medicine Reviews 15 (2011), no. 5, pp. 311-315, doi:10.1016/j.smrv.2011.01.007",
        "P. L. Brooks and J. H. Peever, Identification of the transmitter and receptor mechanisms responsible for REM sleep paralysis, Journal of Neuroscience 32 (2012), pp. 9785-9795, doi:10.1523/JNEUROSCI.0482-12.2012" ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "Not devised here. The deviation is read off the same two bodies of evidence that carry the neighbouring protocol, plus the standard nosological classification" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "The atonia is shown to begin at the transition rather than to persist across it; or reported episodes are found in consolidated sleep away from either threshold."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:ParalysisIsTheRegularShutdown .

