    rdfs:label "Das Reueverfahren bringt die Merkmale des Erloschenseins und öffnet die Vertiefung"@de,
        "The repentance procedure brings the marks of extinction and opens the absorption"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0463a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:TiantaiChanhui ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:TiantaiZhongzuiMiexiang .

pmc:TiantaiJingmeiYieldsXiemie a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Benennung des Kobolds bringt die Störung zum Erliegen"@de,
        "Naming the sprite makes the interference cease"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0470b, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:TiantaiJingmei ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:TiantaiJingmeiXiemie .

pmc:TiantaiShangenFaxiang a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die fünf Arten von Zeichen, an denen das Aufgehen der guten Wurzeln erkannt wird"@de,
        "The five kinds of sign by which the good roots are known to arise"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0469b to 0469c, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:definition "Five inward kinds, each with its own marks. In the breath kind, body and mind grow empty and still and the practitioner does not see the shape of either, and after one or two sittings or one or two days or one or two months, holding without losing or falling back, he suddenly feels body and mind move and the eight touches arise: pain, itch, cold, warmth, lightness, heaviness, roughness, smoothness, and while they arise body and mind are settled and the pleasure is beyond comparison; or he feels the breath long and short through all the pores and sees with the mind's eye the thirty-six things inside his own body as one sees beans in an opened granary. In the kind of the contemplation of the unclean he sees a corpse swell and rot, or his own bones joint by joint. In the kind of the loving mind he suddenly loves living beings and his face is constantly at peace to whomever he meets. In the kind of the contemplation of conditions understanding suddenly arises in him. In the kind of the recollection of the buddhas he remembers their merits, the body grows light and quick and he feels his own merit towering and loved by men."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted apart from the conditions of failure. Three of the five end in a state of the practitioner that only he reports; two end in something a bystander could notice, a face at peace and a body light and quick, and one, the seeing of the thirty-six things within the body, is a claim about a hidden matter of fact that could in principle be put against an anatomy. The work makes nothing of that and does not propose it as a check."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmc:TiantaiShierXiYieldsZhibing a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Von den zwölf Atemarten wird berichtet, sie behandelten die zwölf Leiden"@de,
        "The twelve breaths are reported to treat the twelve complaints"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0472a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:note "Third-party ascription and not textual attestation, because the work attributes the set to an unnamed master and does not take it up. The mode is the whole finding about these twelve: a manual that states its own failure conditions elsewhere in its own voice declines to own the one set of prescriptions in it that reads like a pharmacopoeia."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:TiantaiShierXi ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:TiantaiShierXiZhibing .

pmc:TiantaiXieweiChan a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die falsche und nachgemachte Vertiefung, mit den Merkmalen, die sie von der echten scheiden"@de,
        "The false and counterfeit absorption, with the marks that distinguish it from the true"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0470a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:definition "The work sets the two sides out one after the other. The marks of the false: the body itches or scratches, or is heavy as if pressed by a weight, or light as if about to fly, or as if bound, or sags and droops, or is parched with cold, or burning hot; or various strange realms are seen; or the mind is darkened; or evil thoughts arise; or thoughts scatter outward to miscellaneous good works; or there is joy and agitation; or grief and mourning; or an evil touch raises the hair of the body; or a great pleasure that stupefies. The marks of the true: none of the above occurs, and as each absorption arises the practitioner feels it answer to the settled state, empty and bright and pure, the inward mind glad, calmly happy, without covering; the good mind opens, faith and reverence increase, knowledge is clear, body and mind are soft, subtle and empty and still, the world is found wearisome, and there is no doing and no wanting, and the going in and the coming out are free. The work gives the criterion as a comparison: it is as with keeping company, where an evil man offends again and again and a good man is found fair the longer one sees him."@en ;
    skos:note "The consequence the work attaches to mistaking them is the third of its paths to madness: if a man loves and clings to this false absorption, he answers to the ninety-five ways of ghosts and spirits, and often loses his mind and becomes deranged; and the spirits, knowing that he clings to their way, add their strength, so that he brings forth false absorptions and false knowledge, eloquence and powers, and moves the world; the ignorant who see him say he has gained the fruit of the way and all believe and submit, while his own mind is upside down. Such a man at the end of life never meets a buddha and falls back into the way of ghosts and spirits, and if in his sitting he did much evil he falls into hell."@en,
        "The counterfeit of its own sign of success, set beside the sign, with a list on each side and a criterion between them, and the list of the false is the longer of the two. This is the material the holding keeps in examinations/the-sign-and-the-counterfeit.ttl, and this transmission supplies it unasked and for its own procedure."@en,
        "What the two lists do not do is divide the cases. Several of the false marks are bodily states that also stand in the list of the good roots at pmc:TiantaiShangenFaxiang: lightness, heaviness, cold, warmth, itch and pain are there called the eight touches and are a sign of the breath kind arising, and here they are the sign of a counterfeit. The work does not resolve it, and the criterion it does give, that the true absorption answers to the settled state and the false does not, is a judgement by the practitioner about his own condition. What the lists separate is therefore not available to anybody but him."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmc:TiantaiZhiguanIsOwnAgency a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Sitzpraxis liegt bei eigener Handlungsträgerschaft"@de,
        "The seated practice is placed at own agency"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0471a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:ownAgency ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:situatedNode pmp:TiantaiZhiguan ;
    pm:situationGround "Every instruction of the work is addressed to the practitioner as the one who acts: he regulates, he corrects, he retains or drops, and where a power outside him appears it appears as an interference to be dismissed and never as the agent of the result. The chapter on demonic affairs makes the point for the compiler: the work refuses to let even the harm be another's doing."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmc:TiantaiZhiguanIsWithdrawnAbsorption a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Sitzpraxis liegt bei abgezogener Vertiefung"@de,
        "The seated practice is placed at withdrawn absorption"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0465c, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:withdrawnAbsorption ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:situatedNode pmp:TiantaiZhiguan ;
    pm:situationGround "The work prescribes that the eyes be closed just enough to cut off the outer light, that the body be still as a set stone, and that the mind be bound to the nose tip or the navel without a scattered thought, and it treats dullness and sleep as faults to be corrected rather than as the medium of the practice. That is the criterion this coordinate names. The placement is of the seated practice; the same work carries the five turns into walking, working and speaking, and that extension is expressly a practice in the ordinary waking channel and is not placed here."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmc:TiantaiZhiguanYieldsShentong a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Ruhe und Schau bringt die Kräfte des Weges hervor"@de,
        "Calming and contemplation brings forth the powers of the way"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0466c, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:TiantaiZhiguan ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:TiantaiShentong .

pmc:TiantaiZhiguanYieldsSibaisibing a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Ruhe und Schau im Sitzen beseitigt die vierhundertvier Krankheiten"@de,
        "Seated calming and contemplation removes the four hundred and four illnesses"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0471b, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:TiantaiZhiguan ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:TiantaiSibaisibing .

pmc:TirokuddaGamana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Er geht ohne Widerstand durch Mauer, Wall und Berg"@de,
        "He goes feeling no obstruction through wall, rampart and hill"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.88-91 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, pp. 457-458; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "He enters the space device, emerges, adverts to the wall or rampart or hill, does the preliminary work and resolves that there be space; and there is space. If he wishes to go up or down there is a hollow, if he wishes to go through there is a hole."@en ;
    skos:note "A rule for two operators stands here and nowhere else in the corpus. If a hill or tree springs up on the way there is no need to resolve a second time, because a thing of the temperature order cannot arise against the strength of the first resolve; but if another possessor of the power has created it, the earlier creation is the stronger and the later comer must pass above it or below it. The transmission has thought about what happens when two of its practitioners operate on the same place, and it settles it by precedence in time."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:TokenBroughtBack a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Zeichen wird von dem Ort mitgebracht"@de,
        "A token is brought back from the place"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 55-57" ;
    skos:definition "He returns with a knife, a ring or some other token of his knowledge of what is done in those parts, and on rising shows what he has brought from thence, so that the account may seem certain. The recorder notes that the other report he uses mentions no such thing."@en ;
    skos:note "That the two reports the compiler used disagree about whether the token exists is recorded with the claim and not smoothed away. He names the report that lacks it. Which of the two is right is not decidable here, and the disagreement is itself the reason no examination node is written."@en,
        "This is the most examinable claim in the file. It is not a report of a perception but the production of a physical object said to have come from a named distant place, in front of witnesses, at a stated moment. What would refute it is obvious and requires nothing but the object and the place, and it is entered chiefly for that reason."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SamiNoaidi .

pmc:TrainingPresupposesPreliminaries a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Training setzt die Vorbereitungen voraus"@de,
        "The training presupposes the preliminaries"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:GenerationStage ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:Preliminaries ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:TibetanTantric .

pmc:TraversalPresupposesSequence a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Durchquerung setzt voraus, dass ihre Stationen der Reihe nach genommen werden"@de,
        "The traversal presupposes that its stations are taken in order"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Vijñānabhairava 29 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)",
        "Vijñānabhairava 56 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)" ;
    skos:note "Recorded at prescribed order and not at stated necessity, on purpose and against the reading a modern draft makes. The sources say kramaśaḥ and kramāt kramam, in sequence and step by step, and they say it more than once. What no source consulted says is what happens to one who skips a station. A prohibition needs a consequence somebody can quote, and this one has none; without it the claim is an instruction that a later reader hardened into a rule. See prima-materia#36."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:WorldPathTraversal ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:AscentThroughEachCentre ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmc:TreatmentPresupposesItsOwnContinuation a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Behandlung setzt ihre eigene Fortsetzung voraus"@de,
        "The treatment presupposes that it will be continued"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. VIII, pp. 168-170" ;
    skos:definition "A real danger of the practice is the interruption of a treatment begun and the failure to sustain a crisis that has been excited and that nature cannot develop and terminate unaided. It is nothing in slight and recent indispositions and very serious in organic and long-standing diseases; one ought not to attempt the action at all except when sure of continuing it as long as necessary."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity and it is the best-supported necessity in the file, because the source gives two dated cases in which the consequence followed and in the first of them names himself as the cause. A lady relieved of a monthly headache in half an hour was two days later seized with a fever that lasted six weeks; he does not doubt that it came of the humour he had displaced and would not have occurred had he continued for a month. A blind girl operated upon once with energy for an hour by a student who then broke off suffered eleven months of head pain."@en,
        "This is the one claim in the file whose falsifying condition is ordinary and within reach, and it runs against the tradition's interest: it predicts that interrupting a course of these operations is followed by an identifiable harm at a rate above that of never beginning one. No protocol is written on it here. Its address is this node."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "A person might do much mischief by magnetizing with energy only once to drive off an internal pain caused by a tumor, or by a humor which, for many years, attacks an organ at certain periods; and the accidents which have occurred by the rude interruption of a treatment ought not to be attributed to magnetism, but to the imprudence of the magnetizer."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:MagnetismeGrandsCourans ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:MagnetismeCrise ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:TroisIssuesALaPremiereQuestion a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die drei Ausgänge bei der ersten Frage"@de,
        "The three outcomes at the first question"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. V, pp. 71-72 and 74-75" ;
    skos:definition "Where the patient is tranquil, has closed his eyes and sleeps, the operator may pass his fingertips five or six times before the eyes and then ask how he is or whether he sleeps well. One of three things will take place: he will wake, he will not answer, or he will answer. If he wakes, somnambulism has not taken place and it is not to be thought of again during that sitting. If he continues to sleep without answering, there is reason to suppose he is entering the state. If he answers without waking and, after his waking, has no recollection of the operator having spoken to him, the somnambulism is real."@en ;
    skos:note "Its weakness is that the amnesia is asked for after the operator has spent the sitting telling the patient what to expect, and the same chapter has the operator forbid the somnambulist, while in the state, to meddle after waking with what he has said. A criterion of amnesia administered by the person who instructed the subject to forget is not a criterion. The transmission states both halves and does not connect them."@en,
        "The one discriminating test the work contains, and the only sign of the state anywhere in it that somebody other than the operator could read. Its criterion is not the answering but the amnesia afterwards, which is a report the patient makes in the ordinary state and which a second person in the room can take down. Everything else in this file is certified either by the operator's own perception or by the subject while in the state whose reality is at issue."@en ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A recorded series in which subjects who answer without waking are, after waking, as able to recall what was said to them as subjects given the same instructions and the same expectation without the passes; or in which the recall differs according to whether the operator had told the subject, during the state, not to occupy himself with it afterwards. The claim is that the absence of recollection establishes the reality of the state, so either outcome falls due against it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:TroisQuartsDesMalades a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Mindestens drei Viertel der Kranken spüren die Wirkung"@de,
        "At least three quarters of the patients feel the effects"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. I, §§ 21-22, p. 24" ;
    skos:definition "Not all persons are sensible to the action, and the same persons are more or less so according to their temporary disposition; the operation generally exercises no influence on persons in health, and there are diseases in which it is not perceived and others in which it is evident. The source states that it is not yet known enough to determine the cause of these anomalies or to pronounce beforehand whether the action will take place — that only certain probabilities are had — but that this should create no objection, since at least three quarters of the patients feel the effects of it."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim that persons in good health are generally not affected is the more interesting half, because it predicts that a trial on healthy volunteers should mostly fail — and that is precisely the population an examiner has. The transmission thus supplies, in advance, the explanation of a negative result in the only experiment that is easy to run."@en,
        "The one rate in the file, and the only sentence in the six chapters read that makes the transmission numerically due. It is offered as a reply to an objection and not as a finding, and no series behind it is named."@en ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A recorded series of sick patients operated on according to this work in which fewer than three quarters report effects, or in which the proportion reporting effects does not exceed the proportion among patients receiving the same attendance and the same expectation without the passes. A rate is asserted, so a counted series settles it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:TummoHeatExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Prüfung der Tummo-Hitze-Behauptung"@de,
        "Examination of the tummo heat claim"@en ;
    skos:definition "An examination of the claim that the tummo practice produces inner heat, taken up because this is the one pairing in the collection whose capacity has an outward side that instruments reach."@en ;
    skos:note "Status of what follows. The state above is procedure without cases, set when the counter-search requirement was introduced, because this node does not record whether a search for opposing work was made. Its notes are older than the requirement and are kept unchanged; whoever documents the search restores the case-bearing state. See prima-materia#386."@en,
        "What the measurements bear on is the somatic claim and nothing beyond it. Reported temperature rises are consistent with the practice producing heat; they say nothing about the cosmology the practice is embedded in, and the node must not be read as though they did. The reported effect also depends on the forceful-breath component together with the visualisation, which means the claim that is actually supported is narrower than the one the tradition makes."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:protocolledPractice ;
    pm:baseRate "Temperature change under comparable breathing effort without the practice, in matched participants." ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchNotCarried ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Not documented. This node predates the requirement; whether a counter-search was carried is not recoverable from it, and an undocumented search is treated as uncarried for the same reason pm:independenceGround reverses its burden."@en ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "H. Benson et al., Body temperature changes during the practice of g-tummo yoga, Nature 295 (1982), 234-236",
        "M. Kozhevnikov et al., Neurocognitive and somatic components of temperature increases during g-tummo meditation, PLoS ONE 8(3) (2013), e58244, doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0058244" ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "H. Benson and colleagues; M. Kozhevnikov and colleagues" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "Practitioners of the forceful-breath tummo method show no rise in measured body temperature beyond that produced by the breathing alone in matched non-practitioners." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-08-28" ;
    pm:tests pmc:TummoYieldsInnerHeat .

pmc:TungusFemaleShamanSuspension a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wann eine Schamanin nicht auftritt"@de,
        "When a female shaman does not perform"@en ;
    dcterms:source "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXVIII, sect. 128, pp. 349-350, and chap. XXX, sect. 140, p. 380" ;
    skos:definition "Female shamans do not perform at all during pregnancy and in the first weeks after the delivery, nor during the few days of menstruation each month. The consequence stated is practical and not ritual: she cannot help her clansmen at any moment when she might be needed, and this is given as one reason for a preference for male candidates."@en ;
    skos:note "The same passage carries a second, separate reason, given as the assertion of the Tungus themselves: that females are physically less resistant and therefore cannot carry out very long and difficult performances. The two are reported side by side and are not the same kind of claim."@en,
        "The suspension reappears as the one excuse the office admits. A shaman who is not very old and physically weak may be excused from attending only if she is a female and pregnant or otherwise under the prohibition; without such an excuse he may lose his position."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SiberianShamanism .

pmc:TungusFormalCharacters a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die fünf Merkmale des vollen Schamanentums"@de,
        "The five characters of shamanism in full function"@en ;
    dcterms:source "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXII, sect. 95, p. 274" ;
    skos:definition "The recorder's enumeration of what must all be present: the shaman is a master of spirits; he has a group of mastered spirits; there is a complex of methods and paraphernalia recognised and transmitted; there is a theoretical justification of the practice; the shamans assume a special social position."@en ;
    skos:note "He states in the same passage that all of these but the first two are met in complexes that are not shamanism at all — in the Manchu clan priesthood and in the Lamaist priest — and that he therefore restricts the term to complexes covering all five. The enumeration is his and is entered as his; what is not his is each of the five conditions, which the preceding sections carry as reports."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SiberianShamanism .

pmc:TungusOriginTestimony a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Überlieferung sagt, die Kunst sei nicht ihre eigene gewesen"@de,
        "The transmission says the art was not originally its own"@en ;
    dcterms:source "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXIII, sect. 98, pp. 276-277, and sect. 100, pp. 282-283" ;
    skos:definition "What the groups themselves state about the beginning of their shamanism. The Manchus refer it to a definite person and a definite year, 1033, and name him: a soldiers' commander who left his spirits to spread over three provinces, and who is himself now one of those spirits. The Reindeer Tungus of Manchuria state that they received shamanism, before their move to Manchuria in the first half of the nineteenth century, from subjects of the Emperor of China. The Birarcen name a Dahur or a Goldi as their first shaman, or say they had no shamans at all until the seven mastered spirits came from the Mongols. On the decisive point the recorder reports the groups as unanimous: the art of mastering spirits did not originally exist among them but was practised by their neighbours, and especially by lamas."@en ;
    skos:note "He also states why such testimony is usually missing from the record, and the remark is a finding about the discipline rather than about the Tungus: answers of this kind look to an investigator accustomed to the primeval character of shamanism like an error of the informer, and would most probably not even be recorded. That is his and is entered as his."@en,
        "This is testimony about itself and not a scholar's reconstruction, and it is the reason the node stands here rather than at an examination. The recorder separates it explicitly from folklore, which he holds unreliable for the question, and states that the groups themselves consider these statements historical facts."@en,
        "What he holds the older complex to have contained is reported with the same care: only the spirit of heaven, buga, which he says shows no direct sign of alien influence and is recognised by all Tungus as their own, and the managing of the souls of the dead. The Birarcen are quoted saying that before, the Tungus had only buga and buni. He then argues against his own inference, that other complexes may have been lost and forgotten, and calls the scepticism perhaps too great."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SiberianShamanism .

pmc:TungusShamanCannotHunt a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Amt und Jagd schließen einander aus"@de,
        "The office and the hunt exclude each other"@en ;
    dcterms:source "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXVIII, sect. 127, p. 345, and chap. XXX, sect. 140, pp. 378-381" ;
    skos:definition "The shaman cannot do two things, the hunting and a regular carrying out of the performances, and therefore needs the support of the junior members of his clan. It is given as one reason why father and son are not both shamans and why the office commonly skips a generation."@en ;
    skos:note "An economic condition stated as such, and one of the few places where a transmission in this graph says what its own office costs the household that carries it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SiberianShamanism .

pmc:TungusStateHoldsOwnAgency a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Der Zustand der Darbietung behält die eigene Trägerschaft"@de,
        "The state of the performance holds its own agency"@en ;
    dcterms:source "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXIX, sect. 134, pp. 362-363" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:ownAgency ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:TungusExtasy ;
    pm:situationGround "Producing the state is said to require a power of controlling the thinking mechanism and knowledge of the methods for entering it, maintaining it and ending it. A person in whom it turns into an uncontrolled fit is held to be possessed and for that reason cannot hold the office. The transmission thus places the office on the acting end of this axis and excludes the other end by name."@en .

pmc:TungusTrialInTheFrost a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Probe im Frost"@de,
        "The trial in the frost"@en ;
    dcterms:source "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXVIII, sect. 128, Case 2, p. 347" ;
    skos:definition "In the same clan history, an election carried out when nobody knew whom to select: a man from every generation put on a shaman's headdress, they went out and stayed in the frost while the act was carried on for a long time. Many of them could not stand it and ran away. The spirit descended into one of those who remained and declared that new shamans should not be initiated in winter but in spring."@en ;
    skos:note "A selection by endurance with an outcome the participants do not control, followed by a rule derived from the cost of the procedure. The transmission here corrects its own practice on the strength of what happened, which is the shape of an operative reworking rather than of a copied instruction."@en,
        "It stands inside a clan narrative told to the recorder and not in an account of a procedure he watched. The narrative also carries a live burial, a decapitation and a series of deaths, and no part of it is separable from the rest by anything in the text."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SiberianShamanism .

pmc:Tunkan a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Tunkan, die heiligen Steine"@de,
        "Tunkan, the sacred stones"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Frances Densmore, Teton Sioux Music (Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 61, Washington, 1918), pp. 204-206 and pp. 208-211" ;
    skos:definition "Small stones of native brown sandstone, usually spherical, regular in outline and untouched by any tool, found on the tops of high buttes and not buried. To dream of one was held to be a sign of great import, meaning that the dreamer who fulfilled the requirements of the dream would become possessed of a power exercised through the stones. The word is said to be an abbreviation of the word for grandfather and belongs to the sacred speech."@en ;
    skos:note "The stones are kept wrapped, one of them in eagle down, of which it was said that there is something between the down and the stone, because when surrounded by down it cannot get away. A stone sold to another remains subject to recall by the man who dreamed it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LakotaRitual .

pmc:TuoyangJiangui a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wer das Yang verloren hat, sieht Geister"@de,
        "He who has lost the yang sees ghosts"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Nanjing 20, in Wang Hanlin jizhu Huangdi bashiyi nanjing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 3" ;
    skos:definition "Doubled yang is mania; doubled yin is falling sickness; he who has lost the yang sees ghosts; he who has lost the yin goes blind. The commentary printed with the text ties each to a pulse: the commentator states that in loss of the yang there is no yang qi, that the pulse before the bar is extremely fine and faint, and that therefore the man sees wrongly within the eye and beholds ghostly things; and that in loss of the yin the depots no longer supply the eyes, so nothing is seen."@en ;
    skos:note "The graph carries a number of transmissions in which the seeing of beings is a capacity, acquired or given, and reported as an attainment. Here a canonical work of the same civilisation lists it among four disorders, between mania and blindness, and assigns it a pulse finding. That is a counter-attestation in the strict sense and not a difference of vocabulary: the same event, one register calling it an attainment and another a symptom."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmc:TurbaAcetumAnteIgnem a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Körper ohne Essig über das Feuer gesetzt verbrennt und verdirbt"@de,
        "The body set over the fire without the vinegar is burnt and corrupted"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 15 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    skos:definition "The condition Frictes states at Dictum 15: know that if ye place the body over the fire without vinegar, it will be burnt and corrupted; and know further that the first humour is cold, so be careful of the fire, which is inimical to cold."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the one prerequisite in the work whose stated consequence is a change in the matter that anybody present would see, rather than a failure to arrive at the result. It is therefore the kind of claim the work could be wrong about in a way that shows, and it is entered separately from the general dependence on the permanent water for that reason."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The body is burnt and corrupted."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:TurbaRegimenUniusRei ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:TurbaAquaPermanens ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy .

pmc:TurbaAlbedoAnteRubedinem a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Wenn ihr nicht weiß machst, könnt ihr nicht rot machen"@de,
        "Unless ye whiten ye cannot make red"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 12 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)",
        "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 15 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)",
        "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 17 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    skos:definition "The order Zimon states at Dictum 17 and Lucas repeats at Dictum 12: unless ye whiten, ye cannot make red; the beginning of the whole work is the whitening, to which the redness succeeds. At Dictum 12 the operator is told that if the fire blazes and the matter reddens early this profits him nothing, because at the beginning of the ruling he requires the white."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the firmest prerequisite in the work and the only one whose violation has a perceptible mark: a red matter at a point where the work requires a white one. It is therefore the single place where the work's order could be checked from outside, and it is the reason the colour sequence is held in this file as nodes of its own rather than folded into the regimen."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The redness reached before the whiteness profits nothing and the operator has to go back: the work says at Dictum 12 that in that case it becomes red prematurely, and this will profit you nothing, because in the beginning of the ruling you require the white."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:TurbaRubedo ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:TurbaAlbedo ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy .

pmc:TurbaAquaPermanensNecessaria a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Ohne das beständige Wasser darf niemand in die Kunst eintreten"@de,
        "Without the permanent water nobody may enter the art"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 19 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)",
        "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 37 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    skos:definition "The condition Dardaris states at Dictum 19 and Bonellus repeats at Dictum 37. One who is introduced to this art is to attempt nothing until he is familiar with the power of the permanent water; he who does not understand the permanent water and its indispensable regimen may not enter into this art; and the whole work and regimen does not take place except by water."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Nothing is effected without the permanent water — the work's own words at Dictum 19 — and at Dictum 37 what the operator looks for shall in no wise take place."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:TurbaRegimenUniusRei ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:TurbaAquaPermanens ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy .

pmc:TurbaAquaVitae a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Wasser des Lebens, worin was gemischt wird erst stirbt und dann jung wird"@de,
        "The water of life, in which whatever is mixed first dies and then becomes young"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 59 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    skos:definition "The claim Theophilus makes at Dictum 59 in continuation of the tree: the philosophers spoke the truth when they named a water of life, for whatsoever is mixed with that water first dies, then lives and becomes young."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered beside the tree because the two dicta stand together and the second is offered as the explanation of the first. The speaker breaks off immediately afterwards with the sentence that silence is better than this illumination, which is the work's ordinary way of closing a passage and is recorded here because it is the point at which the sequence of death and rejuvenation stops being expanded."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy .

pmc:TurbaCaveNeBalneumNimium a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Heizt das Bad nicht übermäßig, damit sie nicht Sinn und Bewegung verlieren"@de,
        "Do not kindle the bath excessively, lest they be deprived of sense and motion"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 29 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    skos:definition "The caution Diomedes gives at Dictum 29, stated of the matter under the figure of a married pair. Place the citrine with his wife after the conjunction into the bath; do not kindle the bath excessively, lest they be deprived of sense and motion; and beware lest ye evaporate them by burning them in too strong a fire. Venerate the king and his wife and do not burn them, since you know not when you may have need of these things."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:TurbaRegimenUniusRei ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy .

pmc:TurbaCaveNeComburatur a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Sorgt dafür, dass es nicht verbrennt und der Essig nicht zu Rauch wird und vergeht"@de,
        "Take care lest it be burnt, and lest the vinegar turn to smoke and perish"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 16 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)",
        "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 18 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    skos:definition "The cautions Socrates attaches to the long coction at Dictum 16. When the lead becomes dry it drinks up what remains of its humour swiftly, because it is burnt lead — take care, however, lest it be burnt; pound it with the sharpest vinegar and cook till it becomes thick, taking care lest the vinegar be changed into smoke and perish; and when the orpiment is mixed with the lead and cooked for seven days, take care lest the arcanum smoke away."@en ;
    skos:note "Three cautions in one speech and all three about the same loss: the volatile part leaving the vessel. The work returns to it at Dictum 18, where the operator is told to observe the vessel lest the composition fly and pass off in fumes, and at Dictum 58, where the vessel is to be closed internally and externally with clay. It is the one thing the work warns about more often than it warns about the fire."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:TurbaRegimenUniusRei ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy .

pmc:TurbaCaveNeExtrahasFestinanter a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Hütet euch, es eilig herauszuziehen, denn es verweilt"@de,
        "Beware lest you extract it hastily, for it lingers"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 26 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension, with the shorter recension in Waite's note)" ;
    skos:definition "The caution Zenon gives at Dictum 26 when the colours have run through to the purple: when ye are composing the Ixir, beware lest you extract the same hastily, for it lingers. The shorter recension makes the consequence explicit where the longer leaves it at the lingering: beware lest you extract the spirit in haste, for perchance it will perish."@en ;
    skos:note "The divergence between the recensions is recorded in the definition because it is the difference between a caution with a consequence and one without, and the strength a later reader would assign to the node depends on which recension he holds. Waite's text has the weaker form and his note the stronger."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:TurbaRegimenUniusRei ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy .

pmc:TurbaCaveNeIgnisArdeat a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Hütet euch, dass das Feuer auflodert, denn dann wird es zu früh rot"@de,
        "Beware lest the fire blaze up, for then it reddens too soon"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 12 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    skos:definition "The caution at Dictum 12, given with its consequence: cover the mouth of the vessel securely, and beware lest the fire blaze up, for in this case it becomes red prematurely, and this will profit you nothing, because in the beginning of the ruling you require the white. The same speech adds that the fire is to be gentle in the whitening until coagulation takes place."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:TurbaRegimenUniusRei ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy .

pmc:TurbaCaveNeIntendasIgnem a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Hütet euch, das Feuer zu verstärken, bevor die Elemente verbunden sind"@de,
        "Beware of intensifying the fire until the elements are conjoined"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 61 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)",
        "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 65 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    skos:definition "The caution Horfolcus gives at Dictum 65 for the beginning of the mixing: commingle elements which are crude, gentle, sincere and not cooked, over a gentle fire; beware of intensifying the fire until the elements are conjoined, for these should follow one another and be embraced in a complexion, whereby they are gradually burnt until they be desiccated in the said gentle fire. Dictum 61 states the same of the sand: unless ye have patience, ye err in ruling and corrupt the work, and if the composition be ruled more than it should be, its light is taken and extinguished."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:TurbaRegimenUniusRei ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy .

pmc:TurbaCaveNeMultiplicetis a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Hütet euch, die Dinge zu vervielfachen, denn die Neider haben vervielfacht, um zu täuschen"@de,
        "Beware lest ye multiply things, for the envious multiplied in order to deceive"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 56 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)",
        "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 72 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    skos:definition "The work's warning against its own literature, stated by Constans at Dictum 56. Beware, O all ye investigators of this art, lest ye multiply things, for the envious have multiplied and destroyed for you; they have also described various regimens that they might deceive; they have further likened it to the humid with all the humid and the dry with all the dry, by the name of every stone and metal, gall of animals of the sea, the winged things of heaven and reptiles of the earth."@en ;
    skos:note "The warning has a second half at Dictum 72 which states the purpose plainly: unless the names were changed, so that the vulgar might be deceived, many would deride our art. So the concealment is not defended as protection of the reader but as protection of the art's standing."@en,
        "This is the caution that gives the work its shape and it is stated a dozen times in different speakers' mouths: the envious wrote obscurely on purpose, the plurality of names is a device against the unworthy, and the reader who follows the names will find nothing. The work therefore instructs its reader to distrust the very corpus it belongs to, and it does so while adding to it. Its practical effect is that no reading of the work can be shown to be wrong from the work's own vocabulary."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:TurbaRegimenUniusRei ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy .

pmc:TurbaCaveNeOstendas a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Zeigt es den Bösen nicht, und hütet euch vor den Schlechten"@de,
        "Show it not to the evil, and beware of the wicked"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 16 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)",
        "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 21 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)",
        "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 64 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    skos:definition "The restriction on passing it on, stated inside the operative speeches. At Dictum 16, after the power of orpiment: do not show these unto the evil, for they will laugh. At Dictum 21, after the perfection of the work: I conjure you by God and your sure Master that you show not this great arcanum, and beware of the wicked. At Dictum 15 the ground is given: I swear to you that if kings were familiar with it, none of us would ever attain this thing."@en ;
    skos:note "Two of the three grounds the work gives for silence are about the holders and not about the recipients: that the vulgar would laugh, and that kings would take the thing away. Only the third — at Dictum 64, that God has concealed this from the crowd lest the world should be devastated — states a harm to anybody else."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:TurbaRegimenUniusRei ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy .

pmc:TurbaCaveVenenaOperantem a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Hütet euch vor der Trennung von den Wassern, damit die Gifte nicht an euch gelangen"@de,
        "Beware of separating from the waters, lest the poisons get at you"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 37 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension, with the shorter recension in Waite's note)" ;
    skos:definition "The one caution in the work whose stated victim is the operator's own body. Bonellus closes Dictum 37 with it: beware, however, of making a separation from the waters lest the poisons get at you, and the body perish with the other things which are in the vessel. The shorter recension reads that the body and soul which are in the vessel shall quickly perish, which removes the operator from the sentence."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also the work's only physical hazard, and the work states no precaution for it beyond the instruction not to separate: no ventilation, no distance, no covering, no antidote. A transmission that names a poison and prescribes nothing against it is stating a danger rather than managing one."@en,
        "The divergence decides what the node is about and is therefore in the definition rather than a note. On the longer recension the work warns a man that a vapour can reach him; on the shorter it warns him that his matter can be lost. Waite's text has the first and his note the second, and this holding records both because the count of transmissions that state a bodily danger to the practitioner is small and a mistaken entry would inflate it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:TurbaRegimenUniusRei ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy .

pmc:TurbaCorpusSolvendum a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Wird der Körper nicht zu einem untastbaren Geist zerlegt, ist die Arbeit umsonst"@de,
        "Unless the body is broken up into an impalpable spirit the labour is in vain"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 35 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)",
        "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 71 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    skos:definition "The condition stated at Dictum 35 and again at Dictum 71. Unless ye disintegrate this body, imbue and pound both cautiously and diligently until ye extract it from its grossness and turn it into a tenuous and impalpable spirit, ye have your labour in vain; and unless the body be withered up and so destroyed that it dies, and unless ye extract from it its soul, which is a tingeing spirit, ye are unable to tinge a body therewith. The work gives the formula in the received form: except ye turn bodies into not-bodies, and incorporeal things into bodies, ye have not yet discovered the rule of operation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The labour is in vain and the operator is unable to tinge a body."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:TurbaTincturaInvariabilis ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:TurbaRegimenUniusRei ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy .

pmc:TurbaDeusOstendetPrincipium a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Nach diesen Tagen wird Gott dir den Anfang dieses Steins zeigen"@de,
        "After these days God will show you the principle of this stone"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 21 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)",
        "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 54 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    skos:definition "The third of the work's announced signs, and the one that hands the outcome to somebody outside the procedure. Pandolfus at Dictum 21: cook the four permanently for forty or forty-two days; after these days God will show you the principle of this stone, which is the stone Atitos, of which favoured sight of God there are many accounts. The same speaker has the operator pray God that He may grant him the sight of this very great stone before the cooking begins."@en ;
    skos:note "The translator's own note says the authenticity of the name Atitos is extremely doubtful and that it is probably a misreading, so nothing here turns on the name. What the node claims is the form of the sentence: a count of days, and then a sight that God gives. An operator at whose forty-second day nothing has been shown has been told in advance that the showing was a favour and not a consequence."@en,
        "This is the clause the work reaches for wherever a term falls due, and it is not confined to one speaker: by the will of God at Dictum 19, God favouring at Dictum 65, God will accomplish the arcanum ye seek at Dictum 54, until God shall extract the colours for us at Dictum 27, according to the Divine pleasure at Dictum 32. Five speakers, one device."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy .

pmc:TurbaIuvenescentiaExArbore a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Hundertachtzig Tage der Frucht machen den Greis jung"@de,
        "A hundred and eighty days of the fruit make the old man young"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 58 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    skos:definition "The claim of effect at Dictum 58: the man of a hundred years shut in the dark house round the tree, eating of its fruit for a hundred and eighty days, becomes young."@en ;
    skos:note "It is one of the two claims in the work with a term that falls due: a hundred and eighty days, and at the end of them either the old man is young or he is not. No clause anywhere in the dictum provides for the second case. That silence is what makes the passage worth the node, and it is the reason this file's search for a stated term of abandonment was carried."@en,
        "The chain of report is three deep and the work states all three links, which is why the node is enterable. Balgus says that a certain person who had followed science notified to him after what manner he discovered the tree, extracted the fruit and ate of it; Balgus then asked him how the tree is nourished until it fructifies, and the answer is the prescription. So the work reports a conversation about somebody else's performance. Nothing in it is offered as the speaker's own."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:TurbaDomusCircaArborem ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:TurbaSenexIuvenescens .

pmc:TurbaLapisCoruscans a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Geführt, bis es wie Marmor in seinem Glanz blitzt"@de,
        "Ruled until it coruscates like marble in its splendour"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 12 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)",
        "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 27 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)",
        "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 37 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    skos:definition "The second sign the work announces, given three times in the same words. Gregorius at Dictum 27: the envious have called the venerable stone Efflucidinus and have ordered it to be ruled until it coruscates like marble in its splendour; the copper commingled with vinegar and ruled until it becomes water, then congealed, remains a coruscating stone with a brilliancy like marble, and when ye see it thus it is the most great arcanum. Dictum 12 has the flower of white salt boiled seven days until it becomes like gleaming marble, and Dictum 37 has the stone ruled until it becomes like marble in its splendour."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted with the signs and not with the assays, on the same ground as pmc:TurbaSignumPrimaeDecoctionis. The comparison is to a material anybody could hold up beside the vessel, which makes it the most nearly public sign in the work; what is missing is any statement of what a matter that never comes to that brilliance would establish."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy .

pmc:TurbaNaturaeVeritatisAnteOpus a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Tritt nicht unwissend an das Werk; es folgt nur Schaden"@de,
        "Do not approach the work in ignorance of the natures; nothing follows but harm"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 11 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    skos:definition "The condition Parmenides sets at Dictum 11. Having noticed the facts in this art, set your hands to the work; if indeed ye know not the natures of truth, do not approach the work, since there will follow nothing but harm, disaster, and sadness. The knowledge required is specified in the same speech: what putrefies the nature of truth and what renews it, what savour it has, what neighbours it has, and how after love enmity and corruption intervene."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered although the prior step is a body of knowledge and not an operation, because the work states the dependence with a consequence and the class takes what the source sets. The prior step is filled with the capacity itself, which is the closest the work comes to naming what is to be known; that is a choice of this file and is stated here so that a later reader can dispute it rather than discover it."@en,
        "The stated consequence is harm to the operator and not failure of the work, which puts this node in the small group in this holding where the penalty falls on the person. What the harm consists in is not said."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Nothing but harm, disaster, and sadness."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:TurbaRegimenUniusRei ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:TurbaTincturaInvariabilis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy .

pmc:TurbaNigredo a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Schwärze, die der Schlüssel des Werkes genannt wird"@de,
        "The blackness, which is called the key of the work"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 63 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)",
        "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 69 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    skos:definition "The first of the three states of the matter. The work has the operator cook until the matter is entirely black and says that when it is, whiteness is hidden in the belly of that blackness; Florus calls the blackening sulphur the key of the work at Dictum 69, and Agadimon at Dictum 63 states flatly that the things mixed and cooked once will show the prescribed blackness, that is, that they all become black."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy .

pmc:TurbaProbaPulveris a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sieh nach, ob es Pulver geworden ist; wenn nicht, ein stärkeres Feuer"@de,
        "Look and see whether it has become a powder; if not, a stronger fire"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 13 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    skos:definition "The work's second act of assay, at Dictum 13, in the same form as the first. Take that gum which is from the scoriae, mix with cinder of calx and with the faeces, moistening with permanent water; then look and see whether it has become a powder, but if not, roast in a fire stronger than the first fire, until it be pounded."@en ;
    skos:note "The same arrangement as at Dictum 28 with a different remedy: where the first prescribes more of the same, this one prescribes a hotter fire. Both are repetitions of the operation and neither leaves the failing outcome standing. Recorded separately because a count of how a corpus handles its own failing assays needs the cases separately, and these are the only two the work has."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy .

pmc:TurbaRegimenAgencyHolding a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Führung der einen Materie, auf der Handlungsträgerschaft verortet"@de,
        "The ruling of the one matter, situated on who holds the agency"@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:ownAgency ;
    pm:situatedNode pmp:TurbaRegimenUniusRei ;
    pm:situationGround "The placement is disputable and the ground says why it was made. Every operation in the work is the operator's: he mixes, closes, cooks, pounds, imbues, washes and opens, and no passage has another party act in his place. Against that, the work makes the result a gift at five separate places — God will show you the principle, by the will of God, God favouring, God will accomplish the arcanum, according to the Divine pleasure — and at Dictum 29 it makes a failure God's concealment. Those clauses condition the outcome rather than transfer the act, which is why the value is own agency; a reader who holds that a transmission whose outcome is given cedes agency would place it at the other value, and this file states the alternative rather than hiding it."@en .

pmc:TurbaRegimenSensoryAnchoring a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Führung der einen Materie, auf der Sinnesbindung verortet"@de,
        "The ruling of the one matter, situated on sensory anchoring"@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:outwardWaking ;
    pm:situatedNode pmp:TurbaRegimenUniusRei ;
    pm:situationGround "Every act the work prescribes is performed with the eyes on the vessel: look and see whether it has become a powder, take the powder in the hand, observe the vessel lest the composition fly, open the vessel and you will find, see that the blackness does not remain longer than forty days. Nothing in the seventy-two dicta asks the operator to withdraw attention from the sense channel, and the one condition it does place on his inner state — patience, freedom from haste, a faithful mind — is a condition on his conduct over months and not a state he enters. The value is therefore the ordinary waking one, and the placement is a reading of the work and not a term the work uses."@en .

pmc:TurbaRubedoExtrahenda a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die verborgene Röte zu ziehen ist, zwischen den beiden Rezensionen bestritten"@de,
        "Whether the hidden redness is to be extracted, disputed between the two recensions"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 69 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)",
        "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 69 (trans. Waite 1896, shorter recension as given in Waite's note)" ;
    skos:definition "Two opposite instructions at the same place. Of the redness hidden in the whiteness, the longer recension says that it does not behove the operator to extract it but rather to cook it until the whole becomes a most deep red; the shorter recension says that it does behove him to extract it. The dispute is between witnesses of one work and not between parties, and it governs what the operator does at the last stage."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a dispute rather than smoothed into the node above because SPEC §10 says an averaged node reports a text no witness gives, and here the average would be no instruction at all. Which recension is older is not decided here. Waite calls the shorter one the clearer and the less corrupt of the two and takes the longer for his text, so his own judgement and his own practice point different ways at exactly this place."@en,
        "This is the sharpest instance in the work of a question a later run can settle cheaply and this run did not: the 1610 Basel collection prints both recensions in one volume, so the divergence is a page turn away for anybody who reads the Latin. It is entered as a dispute and not as a debt because the divergence itself is the finding, and it survives whatever the Latin says about which reading is original."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:TurbaSignumPrimaeDecoctionis ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy .

pmc:TurbaSolEtUmbraNecessaria a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Kein färbendes Gift ohne die Sonne und ihren Schatten"@de,
        "No tingeing venom without the Sun and its shadow"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 21 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    skos:definition "The condition Pandolfus states at Dictum 21: no body is more precious or purer than the Sun, and no tingeing venom is generated without the Sun and its shadow. He, therefore, who attempts to make the venom of the philosophers without these already errs, and has fallen into that pit wherein his sadness remains; but he who has tinged the venom out of the Sun and its shadow has arrived at the highest arcanum."@en ;
    skos:note "The prior step is entered as the permanent water because the work identifies the Sun's moisture with it; what the Sun and its shadow are is not stated, and the translator's note that the shadow of copper means a protoxide is his and not the work's. What the node claims is the shape of the condition and not the identity of the substances."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The operator already errs and has fallen into the pit wherein his sadness remains. The consequence is stated as a condition of the man and not of the matter, which is the pattern this file records at pmc:TurbaCulpaInOperante."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:TurbaTincturaInvariabilis ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:TurbaAquaPermanens ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy .

pmc:TurbaTincturaExRegimine a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Führung der einen Materie bringt die unveränderliche Tinktur hervor"@de,
        "The ruling of the one matter brings forth the invariable tincture"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 9 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    skos:definition "The work's central claim of effect: the operator who turns the copper white and then red until a tincture results has the tincture, and imposing it on base coin makes gold; and the operator who does not turn it so, in the work's own words, accomplishes nothing."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim is stated in both directions in the same sentence, which is rarer in this holding than the positive half alone. At Dictum 9: unless you turn the copper into white and then again into redness until a tincture results, verily, ye accomplish nothing. The negative half is a condition on the procedure and not a criterion on the outcome, so it does not by itself make the claim testable; what it does is fix the operation so that a bystander could at least tell whether it had been carried out."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:TurbaRegimenUniusRei ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:TurbaTincturaInvariabilis .

pmc:TurbaVeritasPostErrorem a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Wahrheit wird nur nach dem Irrtum erkannt"@de,
        "The truth is not discerned except after error"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 39 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    skos:definition "The claim Bacsen states at Dictum 39, in the middle of the longest caution in the work: the philosophers have plainly stated that the truth is not to be discerned except after error, and nothing creates greater pain at heart than error in this art. He who plants trees does not look for fruit save in due season; how then should ye desire to attain this offering when ye have read but a single book, or have adventured only the first regimen?"@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a claim of its own and not as part of the case beside it, because it does something the other three accounts do not: it makes a failed attempt a required step. The other three say that a particular failure was the operator's fault; this one says that failure in general precedes the truth. An operator who accepts it has been given a reading not just of his last attempt but of any number of them."@en,
        "It is the nearest thing in the work to a statement about how many attempts are to be expected, and it names no number. The work's day-counts are all counts of cooking within one attempt; the count of attempts is never given, and the sentence above says why it could not be."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy .

pmc:Turiya a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Vierte"@de,
        "The fourth"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Māṇḍūkya Upaniṣad 7, in Robert Ernest Hume (trans.), The Thirteen Principal Upanishads (London: Humphrey Milford, Oxford University Press, 1921), p. 392" ;
    skos:definition "The fourth quarter of the self, given by negation throughout: not inwardly cognitive, not outwardly cognitive, not both, not a cognition-mass, neither cognitive nor non-cognitive; unseen, ungraspable, without distinguishing mark, unthinkable, not to be designated. Its positive terms are the cessation of development, tranquil, benign, without a second. The passage closes by identifying it with the self and saying it should be discerned."@en ;
    skos:note "Nothing in the verse gives a criterion by which a reader could decide of a described condition that it is this one. Every mark is a denial, and the one positive phrase, without a second, is a claim about reality rather than about what the practitioner meets. A coordinate has to be decidable from a text; this is not, and that is the second reason it is a claim here and not a value there."@en,
        "The separation of verse from commentary is drawn more sharply above than the transmission can bear, and the correction belongs in the node rather than only in a review. The Upaniṣad is transmitted inside the Kārikā and the question of an independent transmission is open; whether that changes what the verse alone supports is what prima-materia#443 has to settle at the wording. Until it does, the sentence above states what the printed verse says and not that verse and commentary reached us apart."@en,
        "What the verse does not say is as important as what it says, and it is why this node exists rather than a coordinate on an axis. The text enumerates the fourth alongside the other three as a fourth quarter, and does not say there that it underlies them or that the other three are its appearances. The substrate reading is carried by Gauḍapāda's Kārikā and by Śaṅkara after him and is not asserted here on the strength of this passage."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Upanisadic .

pmc:TwoPathsOfKnowledge a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Verzweigung der Menschheit in einen rechten und einen linken Pfad des Wissens"@de,
        "The branching of humanity into a right-hand and a left-hand path of knowledge"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1888" ;
    dcterms:source "H. P. Blavatsky, The Secret Doctrine I: Cosmogenesis (London 1888), in the discussion of the fourth race, at the sentence on the branching of humanity into the right- and left-hand paths of knowledge or Vidya" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that humanity divided at a datable point of its history into two opposed paths of knowledge, a right-hand and a left-hand one, and that the two magics called white and black were sown as seeds at that moment."@en ;
    skos:note "Checked against the earlier work rather than assumed from it. The full text of Isis Unveiled (1877), both volumes, carries no occurrence of a right-hand or a left-hand path, and none of the tantric vocabulary the names come from. The usual account, which dates the introduction of the terms to 1877, is wrong by eleven years: the passage stands in the work of 1888. See prima-materia#58."@en,
        "The claim is presented as a quotation from a commentary of unstated antiquity, and it is the commentary rather than the author that later reporting cites. That is what pm:circulatesAs records here, and it is why this node is an origination and not an ordering: an ordering names what it arranges, and this one arranges nothing."@en,
        "What is new here is not the pair of names, which stands in the orientalist literature from 1832 and is held as pmc:RightAndLeftHandNomenclature. What is new is that the pair becomes a history: two parties, a moment of origin, and a lineage of adepts on either side. Everything the later occult milieu inherits — that there are two camps and that a person may join one of them — rests on this move and not on the naming."@en ;
    pm:circulatesAs "The ancient meaning of the two paths, credited to a commentary of unstated age rather than to the work of 1888 in which it first stands." ;
    pm:originatedBy "Helena P. Blavatsky" .

pmc:TziteExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Los, das falsch lag"@de,
        "The lot that was wrong"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Popol Vuh: The Sacred Book of the Ancient Quiché Maya, English version by Delia Goetz and Sylvanus G. Morley from the Spanish translation of Adrián Recinos (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1950), part I, chapters 2 and 3" ;
    skos:definition "A protocol taken from the tradition's own report. The lot predicted that the men of wood would come out well and would speak on earth; the chapters that follow have them without memory of their maker, walking on all fours, and destroyed. The transmission preserves the failure of its own divination instead of removing it, which is what makes the passage worth holding."@en ;
    skos:note "The state is not raised above procedure without cases, and deliberately. One narrated instance inside a creation account is not a case in the sense this scale uses: nobody counted consultations, and the outcome is fixed by the story rather than observed. What the passage supplies is a procedure with a stated, missable outcome, which most divination material does not."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, protocol stated from the witness; no case series has been collected" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A series of castings recorded before their outcomes, in which the rate of answers met is no higher than the rate expected from the distribution of the seeds alone." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:TziteAnswer .

pmc:UbbegaPiti a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Erhebungsverzückung"@de,
        "Uplifting rapture"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.94ff. (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "The fourth of the five degrees of rapture, described as strong enough to lift the body."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:UdakeAbhijjamaneGamana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Er geht auf ungebrochenem Wasser"@de,
        "He walks on unbroken water"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.95-97 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, p. 459; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "Water one sinks into when trodden on is called broken; the opposite is unbroken. He enters the earth device, emerges, marks off so much water as is to become earth, does the preliminary work and resolves; and the water in the marked place becomes earth, and he walks on it. He does not only walk: he takes whatever posture he likes, and he may make gems, gold, rock or a tree instead of earth."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:UdughulPatientNotNamed a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Kranke der Serie ist keine Person"@de,
        "The patient of the series is not a person"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Udug-hul, form of address throughout the series, with the exception of the appendix to Tablet 1 and of Tablet 16 (canonical Utukku Lemnutu; critical edition M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons, BAM 8, De Gruyter 2016)" ;
    skos:definition "The form in which the series refers to the sick throughout: the man, son of his god, with no place where a name is to be inserted."@en ;
    skos:note "The contrast is drawn inside the edition read and is a matter of the wording on both sides. The apotropaic rituals of the other kind write So-and-so, son of So-and-so, at the place where the reciter was to say the name of the person in front of him. The sixteen tablets of this series do not, with the exception of an appendix to the first and of the sixteenth, where the sick man is the king. A formula with no slot for a name is a formula that was not addressed to a case, and whether it was ever recited over one is a question the series does not answer about itself."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmc:UdughulProhibitionsAddressTheDemon a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Verbote der Serie richten sich an den Dämon"@de,
        "The prohibitions of the series are addressed to the demon"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Udug-hul 4: 179-189', with the duplicates at 5: 69-74 and 6: 181'-187'; 6: 31-33; 9: 56', 94 and 111 (canonical Utukku Lemnutu; critical edition M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons, BAM 8, De Gruyter 2016)" ;
    skos:definition "The form every prohibition in the series takes: it is spoken to the demon and not to the operator or the patient. You must not place your head upon his head; you must not touch him with your hand; you must not enter the house with him; you must not lean out of the window to him; you must not look behind you; you must not blow in with the wind and heat towards him."@en ;
    skos:note "Read off the wording of every occurrence in the series and not from a statement about it. The prohibitions were searched for across all sixteen tablets in the edition read; each one found stands in the second person addressed to a demon, and none is addressed to the person carrying out the rite. What the operator must do and must not do is therefore stated nowhere inside the series and only in the appended ritual of an Assur tablet and in a colophon of the diagnostic series, both entered above."@en,
        "The consequence for examination is worth stating beside the observation, because this is the point at which the two halves of this transmission come apart. A prohibition addressed to a demon cannot be obeyed or broken by anybody whose conduct could be observed, so no case can be assigned to a breach of it. The prohibitions addressed to the exorcist can be, and there are two of them in the whole of what was read."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmc:UgarDuodecimAddresses a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Elf Adressaten, deren Eingreifen als nutzlos berichtet wird, und ein zwölfter, der handelt"@de,
        "Eleven addressees whose intervention is reported as useless, and a twelfth who acts"@en ;
    dcterms:source "RS 24.244:1-60 (KTU 1.100), in Pardee, Ritual and Cult at Ugarit, WAW 10, 2002, text 53, pp. 174-178" ;
    skos:definition "The structure of the work's first and longest part: the same message is commissioned twelve times in turn to twelve named deities, each at his named seat, with the same incantation in each; of the twelve only the last, Ḥôrānu, is presented as capable of acting effectively, and after each of the first eleven the text says that he binds the serpent, feeds the scaly serpent, draws up a chair and sits."@en ;
    skos:note "The editor adds a datum that sharpens it and that is his and not the text's: an administrative tablet lists rations for the horses of two of the deities this work names as ineffectual against serpents, so two of the eleven were, in their earthly cult, keepers of horses — and the sufferer in this work is a mare. He calls the effect somewhat ironic. That reading is recorded as his."@en,
        "This is the rarest thing the run found in either strand, and it is worth saying what makes it rare. Transmissions in this holding name what works. This one names eleven addresses that do not, by name and with their cult centres, and only then names the one that does. A practitioner reading it learns which eleven invocations to leave alone, which is a discrimination of exactly the kind the holding keeps looking for and almost never finds — and it is a discrimination the transmission makes against itself, because eleven of the twelve are principal deities of its own pantheon."@en,
        "What it is not. The eleven are not reported as having tried and failed; the repeated clause after each is the same and says what the addressee does, not that the venom remained. So this is a ranking of addresses and not a record of eleven failed attempts, and the difference matters for what the structure could be used to test."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RS24244 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:UgariticRitual .

pmc:UgarGzrTgrkm a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Starker am Tor, ein Krieger an den Mauern"@de,
        "A strong foe at the gate, a warrior at the walls"@en ;
    dcterms:source "RS 24.266:26'-27' (KTU 1.119), in D. Pardee, Ritual and Cult at Ugarit, WAW 10, Atlanta: Society of Biblical Literature 2002, text 46, p. 149" ;
    skos:definition "The condition of use the prayer states before it says what to do: when a strong one attacks your gate and a warrior your walls."@en ;
    skos:note "Two things make this condition unlike most entry conditions in this holding. It is a public event with a date, so a reader cannot be in doubt whether the case has arisen; and the text is formally addressed to human beings in the plural, which the editor notes is rare for this corpus and rarer still in poetry. The rite is therefore given to the city rather than prescribed to an officiant."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RS24266 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:UgariticRitual .

pmc:UgarHmtTluPlg a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Gift schwach wie in einem Bach, verteilt wie in einem Kanal"@de,
        "The venom weak as in a stream, dispersed as in a canal"@en ;
    dcterms:source "RS 24.244:68-69 (KTU 1.100), in Pardee, Ritual and Cult at Ugarit, WAW 10, 2002, text 53, p. 178" ;
    skos:definition "The stated outcome of the treatment, in two similes: the venom is weak as though in a stream, it is dispersed as though in a canal."@en ;
    skos:note "A second mechanism for the same affliction stands in the next tablet of the same corpus, where the venom is gathered rather than diluted, and the editor asks whether that text's invitation to the other gods was ironic. That tablet was not opened in this run, so the two mechanisms are not entered as a dispute; the work stands as pmw:RS24251 with the reason."@en,
        "The outcome is graded and not total, and the transmission says so in its own figures: the venom is diluted and carried off, not destroyed. A claim of dilution is harder to come out against than a claim of cure, because any surviving patient satisfies it and any dying one can be said to have had more than a stream's worth. What the work gains in caution it loses in checkability, and the two are the same sentence."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RS24244 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:UgariticRitual .

pmc:UgarLDgLKlb a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Reinige das Haus: kein Fisch mehr und kein Hund mehr"@de,
        "Cleanse the house: no more fish and no more dog"@en ;
    dcterms:source "RS 24.272:13-15 (KTU 1.124), in Pardee, Ritual and Cult at Ugarit, WAW 10, 2002, text 52, p. 172" ;
    skos:note "A second answer follows the prescription and is a prohibition on the household rather than an instruction to the operator: wipe the house, no fish and no dog. No consequence of keeping them is stated, which is why this is a caution and not a prerequisite with a stated necessity."@en,
        "Two named animals and nothing else. A prohibition that names its items instead of a class is checkable in a way a general injunction to purity is not, and it is the only exclusion of this shape the five opened tablets contain."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:UgarMrQhWSt ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RS24272 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:UgariticRitual .

pmc:UgarLbsIlYstk a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Gott kann dich kleiden, der Gott kann dich entblößen"@de,
        "The god can clothe you, the god can make you naked"@en ;
    dcterms:source "RIH 78/20:11-13 (KTU 1.169), in Pardee, Ritual and Cult at Ugarit, WAW 10, 2002, text 49, p. 160" ;
    skos:definition "The clause that follows the three prohibitions on the patient's body: the god can clothe you, the god can make you naked."@en ;
    skos:note "Both outcomes are reserved to the god in one line, and in the same construction. A statement that covers a result and its opposite excludes nothing, which is the condition the holding records as pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate — and it is stated here in the operative text rather than reached by a later examiner. The line stands immediately after three prohibitions addressed to the patient's own body, so the text assigns him the duties and the god both outcomes."@en,
        "What the line does not do, so that it is not read as more than it is. It is not a failure condition: it names no sign and no term, and it does not say what would show that the recitation had not worked. It is the opposite — the removal of any outcome that could show it."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RIH7820 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:UgariticRitual .

pmc:UgarMdrBl a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Gelübde, das nach der Erfüllung geschuldet ist, nicht vorher"@de,
        "The vow owed after delivery and not before"@en ;
    dcterms:source "RS 24.266:28'-34' (KTU 1.119), in Pardee, Ritual and Cult at Ugarit, WAW 10, 2002, text 46, p. 150" ;
    skos:altLabel "mdr"@en ;
    skos:definition "What the besieged promise, conditional on the god acting: a bull they will sanctify, a vow they will fulfil, a firstborn they will sanctify, a ḥtp-offering they will fulfil, a feast they will offer; to the sanctuary they will ascend, that path they will take."@en ;
    skos:note "The order of the two halves is the finding. In most of what this holding carries the offering is made first and the result is hoped for; here the sacrifice is owed only if the strong one is driven off, so the payment is a record of the outcome and not a precondition of it. A city that performed the rite and was not relieved would owe nothing, and a city that paid would be saying in public that the thing had worked."@en,
        "What that does not amount to. The text does not say that the vow is void if the god does not act; it simply makes the protasis the condition of the whole promise. Nothing in the eleven lines says what follows if the city falls, and no other work in this corpus was found that supplies it."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RS24266 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:UgariticRitual .

pmc:UgarMhrNhsm a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Brautpreis, in Schlangen bezahlt"@de,
        "The bride-price paid in serpents"@en ;
    dcterms:source "RS 24.244:70-76 (KTU 1.100), in Pardee, Ritual and Cult at Ugarit, WAW 10, 2002, text 53, pp. 178-179" ;
    skos:definition "The closing exchange: the healer asks that the house of incantation be opened to him and demands as his bride-price serpents, poisonous lizards and the sons of adders; and the answer given is that serpents and the sons of adders are hereby given as his bride-price."@en ;
    skos:note "The fee is paid in the thing that caused the harm. Like the Anatolian scapegoat sheep that is not killed and goes to the practitioner, this stands inside the operative text and not in a colophon or a rubric, and it is one of the few places in this holding where a transmission says what the healer gets."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RS24244 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:UgariticRitual .

pmc:UgarMntNtkNhs a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Beschwörung gegen Schlangenbiss und das Gift der schuppigen Schlange"@de,
        "The incantation for serpent bite and the scaly serpent's poison"@en ;
    dcterms:source "RS 24.244:4-6 and the eleven parallel passages (KTU 1.100), in Pardee, Ritual and Cult at Ugarit, WAW 10, 2002, text 53, pp. 174-178" ;
    skos:altLabel "mnt"@en,
        "ḥmt"@en ;
    skos:definition "The formula embedded identically in each of the twelve sections: my incantation for serpent bite, for the scaly serpent's poison — from it, O charmer, destroy; from it cast out the venom."@en ;
    skos:note "The editor classes the work as para-mythological rather than incantatory and notes that this formula inside it is conventionally incantatory, which is to say that the one operative element of the text is a quotation of a genre it otherwise is not. What is entered here is the formula and not the frame: the mare, her parentage and her messenger are the story in which the formula is set."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RS24244 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:UgariticRitual .

pmc:UgarMsr a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Aufrichtigkeit, das einzige Thema, dessen Terminus erhalten ist"@de,
        "Rectitude, the one theme whose term survives"@en ;
    dcterms:source "RS 1.002:26' and 35' (KTU 1.40), in Pardee, Ritual and Cult at Ugarit, WAW 10, 2002, text 22, pp. 78, 82-83" ;
    skos:altLabel "mšr"@en ;
    skos:definition "The quality the rite is to secure in its third pair of sections, named twice in the preserved text and assured by the slaughter of a donkey: the rectitude of the son of Ugarit, and of the daughter of Ugarit, together with the well-being of the foreigner within the walls and of the named members of the royal house."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the only one of the rite's three themes for which the term is preserved, and the editor says so. He deduces the expiatory character of the second pair of sections from the occurrence of the word for sin in them, and the communal character of the first pair from the general sacrificial term that stands first in each paragraph. Two of the three themes the literature names for this rite are therefore his reconstruction, and the node for the sin above rests on a word that is in the text while the theme it is assigned to is not."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RS1002 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:UgariticRitual .

pmc:UgarMtptYld a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der von Ditānu erbetene Entscheid für ein krankes Kind"@de,
        "The ruling sought from Ditānu for a sick child"@en ;
    dcterms:source "RS 24.272:1-12 (KTU 1.124), in Pardee, Ritual and Cult at Ugarit, WAW 10, 2002, text 52, pp. 171-172" ;
    skos:altLabel "mtpṭ"@en ;
    skos:definition "The frame of the work: a divine petitioner reaches Ditānu and asks him for a ruling for the child, and Ditānu answers with what is to be replied, so that the words carried back are a prescription for healing."@en ;
    skos:note "The purpose is not stated in the narrative frame and comes out only in the answer, as the editor notes: the child is ill and needs divine intervention. The term for the illness in the last preserved section is the word for bitterness, and he connects it to the descriptive term for serpent venom in the preceding tablet of the corpus, which would make this a poisoning; he offers that as a possibility."@en,
        "The reason this work is here is the editor's reading of what it was for, and it is recorded as his. He takes the prescription a deified ancestor gives for a sick divine child to have served as the model for human cases, and says there is no reason to doubt that the story served as a model for consulting the head of the dynasty when illness struck the young heir, and perhaps by extension any infant. On that reading the work is a procedure disguised as a story; on a stricter reading it is a story. Nothing in the tablet says which."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RS24272 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:UgariticRitual .

pmc:UgarRS24266AgencySituating a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Entlastung der belagerten Stadt liegt ganz beim Gott"@de,
        "The relief of the besieged city is placed entirely with the god"@en ;
    skos:note "An easy placement with one thing worth noting against it: the vow the speakers undertake is theirs and falls due only after the god has acted, so the transmission does give them something to do — afterwards. The axis asks who acts in the operation, and the answer is unambiguous; a reader who wanted the vow counted would be placing a different node."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:cededAgency ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:UgarRS24266Yielding ;
    pm:situationGround "The human part of the operation is to lift the eyes and to speak; the driving off of the besieger is stated as what the god will do, in the indicative and with him as the subject of both clauses. The source therefore assigns the whole of the operative act to the addressee and keeps none of it for the speakers."@en .

pmc:UgarRS24266AnchoringSituating a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Das Gebet an der Mauer wird im gewöhnlichen Wachzustand verrichtet"@de,
        "The prayer at the wall is performed in the ordinary waking state"@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because the negative case is worth having on this axis and not only the exotic ones. Almost everything the holding places at withdrawn absorption got there because the source prescribed a withdrawal; a source that prescribes none, in a situation that forbids one, is the control against which those placements read as findings rather than as the vocabulary of the compiler."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:outwardWaking ;
    pm:situatedNode pmp:UgarTsuEnkm ;
    pm:situationGround "The act is to lift the eyes to the god and speak, performed by the inhabitants of a city under attack and addressed to them in the plural. Nothing in the text withdraws the senses, prescribes a posture, a seclusion, a fast or a night, and the occasion is one in which the performers are necessarily attending to what is outside the walls."@en .

pmc:UgarThtaLGbk a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Solltest du gegen deinen eigenen Leib sündigen"@de,
        "Should you sin against your own body"@en ;
    dcterms:source "RIH 78/20:5-8 (KTU 1.169), in Pardee, Ritual and Cult at Ugarit, WAW 10, 2002, text 49, p. 160" ;
    skos:note "Four lines in the middle of an incantation that the editor describes as of a quasi-moral nature and as explaining how a man can open himself up to attack: should you sin against your body, should you commit evil against your body, you must eat moldy bread, drink fig-juice in oppression, on the heights and in the watered valleys, in the shadows and even at the sanctuary. The susceptibility is put in the patient's own conduct and the consequence is a penance diet."@en,
        "This node does not depend on which of the four readings of the work is right. The sinning, the evil against the body and the three items of the diet are in the preserved text on every reading; what the readings disagree about is the complaint the whole incantation treats."@en,
        "What this does for the work's own claim. An operation whose failure can be attributed to the patient's conduct, where the conduct is named only as sinning against his own body, has an excuse available in every case. The holding carries that device in its own statement at pmc:NoFailedRunCountsAgainstTheOperation, and this is a Levantine instance of it stated in the operative text. Not entered as a witness of that claim in this run: the claim was not re-read here and the counter-search SPEC 16 requires before enlarging a generalization was not carried for it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:UgarDbbm ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RIH7820 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:UgariticRitual .

pmc:UggahaNimitta a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das erfasste Zeichen, und das Merkmal, an dem es als entstanden erkannt wird"@de,
        "The learning sign, and the criterion by which it is known to have arisen"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.30, Pali PTS [125] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, p. 146; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "The first of the two signs. The practitioner develops the device until it comes into focus when he adverts to it with his eyes shut exactly as it does with his eyes open; at that point the learning sign is said to have been produced. Until it arises he is to develop in that way a hundred times, a thousand times, and more often still. Once it is produced he is not to sit in that place again but to return to his own quarters and go on there."@en ;
    skos:note "A sign of success and not a capacity, and it is entered and counted as one for the reason SPEC §15 gives. It is announced to the practitioner before he sits, which is exactly the condition under which a reported sign carries no base rate."@en,
        "The criterion is stated as something the practitioner checks on himself and nobody else can: the identity of what appears with the eyes shut and with the eyes open. The count attached to it is open at the top — a hundred, a thousand, and more often still — so no number of repetitions arrives at which he would have established anything."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:UnchosenSendingProtocol a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der ungewählte Zustand übergeht"@de,
        "Whether the unchosen state transmits"@en ;
    skos:definition "An examination of whether a sender's own affective state passes to a recipient in the absence of a deliberately chosen one."@en ;
    skos:note "Affect transfer between co-present people remains widely reported, but the mechanism the contagion literature proposes for it is not secure. The canonical test of the facial feedback step returned a rating difference of 0.03 units, confidence interval -0.11 to 0.16, across seventeen laboratories and 1,894 participants, where the original had reported 0.82. A claim that the state passes without either party choosing it therefore stands without its stated mechanism."@en,
        "The consequence for the draft, and it is not a refutation. What the claim describes occurs; it occurs through a channel that requires nothing the section posits. A practitioner who finds his own state arriving in the other person has not thereby found evidence for the transfer the section is about — he has found the effect that would be there anyway. The claim is confirmed and its confirmation supports nothing beyond itself."@en,
        "The earlier sentence here, that the states a person is not presenting are the ones that leak most reliably, is retracted. It reversed the meta-analytic finding."@en,
        "The leakage evidence is withdrawn. Ekman and Friesen 1969 is a theoretical paper with three illustrative analyses of films of forty female psychiatric inpatients, of whom deception could be certified for three interviews; the authors state that the studies do not directly test their formulation, and that the leakage they describe should be relatively easy to eliminate once a person is aware of it. Its channel prediction is not borne out either: over independent estimates numbering twenty-nine, twenty-eight, eighteen and thirty-two, hand movements stand at d = 0.00, foot and leg movements at d = -0.09, self-fidgeting at d = -0.01 and eye contact at d = 0.01. The body areas named as the reliable leakage sites sit at zero; what discriminates is verbal and vocal, and it is faint."@en,
        "The narrower claim that would carry the section is not examined here and is not touched by this node: that the unchosen state passes where the ordinary channels are closed, out of sight and out of earshot. That is a different protocol, it has no cases, and it must not borrow the confirmation recorded above."@en,
        "Two claims were run together here and are now separated. Detectability is the claim that an observer can read a state the sender is not presenting. Transmission is the claim that the recipient comes to be in that state. The evidence entered for the first was cited for the second, which is the same error as at the Kox case in prima-materia#383: the work was named correctly and stood for something it does not say."@en ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Carried 2026-09-03. Searched: the meta-analytic record on behavioural cues to deception, the accuracy of lie-truth judgments, and the replication status of the mimicry-and-feedback mechanism, in each case for work opposing the claim rather than supporting it. Found: the cue meta-analysis reports that many behaviours have no discernible or only weak links to deceit, with the hand, foot, fidgeting and gaze channels at or near zero; and the multi-laboratory replication of the facial feedback step recovered no effect. Both were read at the passage. Not yet opened: Hatfield, Cacioppo and Rapson 1994, which is carried in prima-materia as an unread citation, and the judgment-accuracy meta-analysis of Bond and DePaulo 2006, seen only in abstract."@en ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "B. M. DePaulo, J. J. Lindsay, B. E. Malone, L. Muhlenbruck, K. Charlton and H. Cooper, Cues to deception, Psychological Bulletin 129 (2003), no. 1, pp. 74-118",
        "E.-J. Wagenmakers et al., Registered Replication Report: Strack, Martin, & Stepper (1988), Perspectives on Psychological Science 11 (2016), no. 6, pp. 917-928" ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "Not devised here. The protocol reads the finding off the contagion literature and the work on involuntary nonverbal disclosure" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "Recipients in the presence of a sender in an unchosen state show no shift toward that state against a neutral condition."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-03"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:UnchosenSendingCarriesTheActualState .

pmc:UncreatedLightCedesAgency a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Das ungeschaffene Licht tritt die Trägerschaft ab"@de,
        "The uncreated light cedes agency"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Gregory Palamas, Triads in Defence of the Holy Hesychasts (trans. Gendle 1983)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:cededAgency ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:UncreatedLight ;
    pm:situationGround "The file's own note states the criterion: this is a claim about what shines through the practitioner, not about an emanation the practitioner produces or directs. The defence turns on the light being uncreated, which is what puts the acting side outside the practitioner."@en .

pmc:UnsoughtCapacityCannotBeLaidDown a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das ungesucht gekommene Vermögen lässt sich nicht ablegen"@de,
        "The capacity that came unsought cannot be laid down"@en ;
    skos:note "One asymmetry a later examination should not lose. The Tungus report gives reasons a person inside the transmission could state; the Sámi report gives a first-person complaint that the recorder heard and that includes, in the same breath, a claim of a verified hit about the recorder's own journey. The second is therefore both the better and the worse witness — better because it is the man himself, worse because the passage has an evident interest in being remarkable."@en,
        "The Tungus material states that once a person has become a shaman he must go on to death or to psychic invalidity, and gives three grounds: the spirits left without a master would harm the clan and will not easily submit to a new one, the condition that compelled him to take the office may return, and the clan holds that he must not give up. The Sámi material reports a man who surrendered his drum on complaint and confessed in tears that although he had parted with it and made no other he would have the same visions as before, and that he did not know how to use his eyes since distant things were presented to them. In the first, two of the three grounds lie outside the person and one is a rule; in the second there is no rule at all, only the capacity continuing after its instrument is gone."@en,
        "The counter-search over the holding found no case of an unsought capacity being laid down, and it found one thing that looks like a third witness and is not. The Inuit narrative reports second sight as a standing property of a person and expressly not as the outcome of an exercise, which satisfies the antecedent; it says nothing whatever about whether it could be given up, which is a silence and not a case. The acquired powers of the Indian material are ranked as obstacles to the goal of the practice, which is a different claim: an obstacle is something to be passed, not something that cannot be shed."@en,
        "The weakness of the derivation is the pairing. Both transmissions are circumpolar, both were written down by outsiders, and no route between them has been examined in this holding. Two witnesses from one region do not make a statement about transmissions in general, and the way to widen it is to ask the same question of the corpora that report an office entered through an illness which is resolved only by accepting it — the Korean material is registered here and carries no node."@en,
        "This is the inverse of nearly everything else in the holding, and that is why it is worth a node. The ordinary shape of a caution here is a warning against doing the thing wrongly or against doing it at all; these two warn against stopping. A transmission that holds its own central attainment to be irreversible has said something that bears on what the attainment is supposed to be, and it has said it against its own interest."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:CautionAgainstLayingDownTheOffice,
        pmc:CautionAgainstTheSightItself ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission holds that a capacity or an office comes to a person without being sought, it also holds that the person cannot be rid of it at will, and it locates the impossibility in the capacity or in what conferred it rather than in a rule the person could choose to break."@en .

pmc:UpakkilesaHatthiParikkha a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Elefantenprobe: ein Übender stellt fest, dass die Erlangung eines anderen nicht ist, wofür er sie hält"@de,
        "The elephant test: one practitioner establishes that another's attainment is not what he takes it for"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XX.111-112, Pali PTS [635]-[636] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part III, Pali Text Society, pp. 773-774; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "The test by which the error of the elder Mahānāga is established, carried out in the handbook's own narrative and not prescribed. Dhammadinna puts a thousand questions and every one is answered without a hitch; he asks when the attainment was reached and is told sixty years ago; he asks whether the elder can still use his concentration and is told it is not difficult; he has him create an elephant, and the elder creates one all white. Then he has him make the elephant come at him with ears fixed, tail stretched out, trunk thrust into its mouth and trumpeting terribly. The elder does so, sees the terrible form coming at speed, rises and begins to run. Dhammadinna catches him by the edge of the robe and asks whether there is such a thing as timidity in one purged of the cankers. The elder, knowing himself then to be an average person, asks Dhammadinna to be his protector, receives a meditation subject from him, ascends the terrace walk and reaches sanctity at the third stride."@en ;
    skos:note "It bears on pmc:TheTestOfAPersonIsRefused, which says that where a capacity is located in a person the deliberate test of it is forbidden, with the boundary clause that a trial between two practitioners is a different case. This is the second such trial in the holding beside the Sámi reindeer contest, and it differs from that one in that the two parties are not equals and the loser is corrected rather than ranked. What it does to that statement is set out at the generalization."@en,
        "Two things limit it and both are properties of the passage. The test is administered by a practitioner who himself has the analytical knowledges and travels through the air to reach the man, so the instrument is one of the capacities under examination; and it is narrated of two named men rather than prescribed to anybody, so the handbook does not make it a procedure. The thousand questions answered without a hitch are part of the finding: the doctrinal examination the candidate passed established nothing, and the involuntary reaction established everything."@en,
        "Why this belongs in a holding of failure conditions rather than among the anecdotes. It is a test of a claimed attainment with a stated criterion, an outcome in two directions, and a result that neither party controls: the flinch is involuntary, it is produced by the subject's own power turned against him, and it is visible to the person administering it. Nothing else in this transmission's part of the holding has that shape. The examination is not of a procedure but of a person, and it works."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:UpakkilesaSamathaVipassanaLabhi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Irrtum trifft den Übenden, der Ruhe und Einsicht hat, und drei Älteste werden genannt"@de,
        "The mistake falls on the practitioner who has both calm and insight, and three elders are named"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XX.108-111, Pali PTS [634]-[635] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part III, Pali Text Society, pp. 772-773; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "The class in which the handbook says this imperfection usually arises, with its mechanism and with named cases. It arises generally in one who has obtained calm and insight: because the defilements discarded by the attainments do not operate in him, he produces the thought that he is a saint. The three named are Mahānāga the elder resident at Uccavālika, Mahādatta the elder resident at Haṅkanaka, and Cūḷasumana the elder resident at Cittalapabbata. The illustration that follows is the elder Dhammadinna of Talaṅkara, a saint with the analytical knowledges, who reflected on whether his own teacher Mahānāga had finished his duties as a monk, saw that he was still an average person, judged that Mahānāga would die an average person if he did not go, rose into the air by his power and put a thousand questions to him."@en ;
    skos:note "The extent of the illumination is given on a scale in the same passage and it is the only quantity in it: in one monk it lights only the seat he sits on, in another the interior of his room, in another the whole monastery, then a quarter league, half a league, one, two and three leagues, and in another it makes one continuous light from the surface of the earth up to the Brahmā world. Two elders in a double-walled room at Cittalapabbata, on a moonless overcast night, report to each other flowers of five colours on the shrine terrace and fishes and turtles in the ocean a league away. The scale is stated without any suggestion that a larger light means a better practitioner, and the handbook uses it to show variety and not rank."@en,
        "The mechanism is a failure condition of the plainest kind and it is worth having separately from the three names: the suppression of the defilements by the attainments removes the evidence by which the practitioner would have known he was not finished. The better the calm, the fewer the marks against the conclusion."@en,
        "Three names and a fourth as the one who detected them is the most specific failure report in this transmission's part of the holding: named men, named residences, and a stated diagnosis of what was wrong with them. What makes the report unusable as a check is the last step — the error was detected by another practitioner exercising a supernormal power, so the instrument that establishes the mistake is one of the capacities in question."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:UpakkilesaSammaPatipannaNimittam a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Verunreinigung entsteht nur bei dem Übenden, der richtig vorgeht"@de,
        "The imperfection arises only in the practitioner who is going right"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XX.105-106, Pali PTS [633]-[634] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part III, Pali Text Society, pp. 771-772; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "The entry condition of the ten, stated twice and in both directions. The imperfections of insight do not arise in a noble disciple who has reached penetration of the truths, and they do not arise in persons erring in virtue, neglectful of their meditation subject and idlers. They arise only in a clansman who keeps to the right course, devotes himself continuously to his meditation subject, and is a beginner of insight."@en ;
    skos:note "A sign of success and counted apart from the failure conditions of this file, for the reason SPEC §15 gives. The illumination is announced to the practitioner in advance by the very handbook that then tells him not to value it."@en,
        "This is the finding of the passage and the reason it is entered apart from the list. The appearance is not a sign that something has gone wrong; its arising is evidence that the practitioner is on the right course, and it then ruins that course by being taken for the goal. So the transmission has constructed a mark that is at once the confirmation and the obstacle, and it says so."@en,
        "What it costs anybody who would test the practice by its signs. Neither the presence nor the absence of the ten tells the practitioner where he stands: present, they mean he is going right and is about to go wrong; absent, he is either an accomplished disciple or a careless one, and the transmission gives no mark distinguishing those two by this route. The mechanism by which the presence ruins the course is stated in the same passage from the Paṭisambhidā: he adverts to the illumination as a noble one's state, the distraction from that is agitation, and while his mind is seized by that agitation he does not correctly understand the appearance as impermanent, as painful, or as not-self."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:UpakkilesaTimsavidha a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Warum die zehn Verunreinigungen heißen, und wie sie dreißig werden"@de,
        "Why the ten are called imperfections, and how they become thirty"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XX.124-128, Pali PTS [637]-[638] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part III, Pali Text Society, pp. 776-778; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "The handbook's own qualification of the word it has used, and the multiplication that follows from it. Illumination and the rest are called imperfections because they are the bases of imperfection and not because they are unprofitable; attachment alone is both an imperfection and a basis of one. As bases they are ten. They become a full thirty by the way of seizing them: he who holds that his illumination has arisen seizes a view, he who holds that the illumination is delightful seizes conceit, and he who relishes it seizes craving — three ways for each of the ten. The remedy is stated in the same terms as the fault: the skilled and wary practitioner defines the illumination by understanding as impermanent, conditioned, generated through cause and liable to cessation, or reasons that were it the self it would be right to hold it so, and thereupon reviews it as not mine, not I, not my self, and no longer wavers. What the whole operation produces is the knowledge of what is the path and what is not: the states consisting in illumination and the rest are not the path, and insight knowledge free from imperfections and keeping to its course is the path."@en ;
    skos:note "The multiplication by three is not decoration. It says that the same appearance can be mistaken in three distinguishable ways, and it names them — view, conceit, craving. That is finer than anything else in this transmission's failure layer, and it is the place a later protocol should start if it wants to ask whether a transmission that grades its own errors also grades their remedies."@en,
        "The qualification matters for a holding that collects conditions of failure, because it withdraws the obvious reading. The ten are not faults and the handbook says so; what is a fault is one particular relation to them, and that relation is a thought the practitioner has about his own state. So the failure condition here lies neither in the practice nor in its result but in an interpretation, and the only person positioned to notice it is the one whose interpretation it is."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:VaidikaDarsapurnamasaSampad a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Vollmondopfer bringt jenen Wohlstand dem hervor, der dies weiß"@de,
        "The full-moon offering yields that prosperity to whoever knows this"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa I,6,3,15 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 12, Oxford 1882, p. 167" ;
    skos:note "The condition is double and the second half is the corpus's own: performing it in this wise, and knowing this. See pmc:VaidikaYaEvamVeda."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:VaidikaDarsapurnamasa ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:VaidikaIndrasampad .

pmc:VaidikaDosanivedananisedhah a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Der Priester im Sadas soll auf einen Fehler nicht aufmerksam machen"@de,
        "The priest in the Sadas must not call attention to a flaw"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Kauṣītaki Brāhmaṇa xxvi,4, in the English of Arthur Berriedale Keith, Rigveda Brahmanas, Harvard Oriental Series 25, Cambridge 1920, pp. 497-498" ;
    skos:definition "Prāgāhi holds that once the concluding call of the invitatory and offering verses has been made, a flaw noticed after that point has been passed over. Paiṅgya draws the rule from it: therefore the priest in the Sadas should not call attention to a flaw passed over at this time — he knocks into a pillar or falls into a pit or is put into one or perishes. Āruṇi holds that if the flaw is pointed out there must not be a repetition of what has been done; Śvetaketu, that there is loss of a secondary element. Paiṅgya repeats his rule twice more and adds: it is a tribulation to the sacrifice if the priest in the Sadas calls attention to a flaw passed over."@en ;
    skos:note "A prohibition on reporting an error, with a consequence named for the reporter and not for the error. The office described is the one office at the rite whose business is to watch it, and the rule addressed to him is to keep quiet."@en,
        "Worth keeping apart from the transmissions that forbid a testing question. Here nothing is being tested: the flaw is agreed to have occurred, and what is forbidden is saying so. The consequence — a pillar, a pit, perishing — falls on the man who speaks, which is the reverse of the arrangement everywhere else in this file, where the fault of one man is paid for by another."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:VaidikaNivid,
        pmp:VaidikaVasatkara ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:KausitakiBrahmana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual .

pmc:VaidikaDosavivadah a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob ein Fehler in der Rezitation zu heilen ist, oder ob es keinen gibt"@de,
        "Whether a flaw in the recitation has to be repaired, or whether there is none"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Kauṣītaki Brāhmaṇa xxvi,5, the position of Jātūkarṇya, in the English of Arthur Berriedale Keith, Rigveda Brahmanas, Harvard Oriental Series 25, Cambridge 1920, p. 498",
        "Kauṣītaki Brāhmaṇa xxvi,5, the position of Kauṣītaki, in the English of Arthur Berriedale Keith, Rigveda Brahmanas, Harvard Oriental Series 25, Cambridge 1920, p. 498" ;
    skos:definition "The corpus stages the question and names the parties. Daivodāsi Pratardana comes to a sacrificial session of the Naimiṣīyas and asks: if the priest in the Sadas should call attention to a flaw passed over, or any one of the priests should note it, how would you remove the flaw? They were silent. Their Brahman priest Alikayu Vācaspata says he does not know and will ask Jātūkarṇya, the aged teacher of those formerly. Jātūkarṇya answers that the mantra should be recited again, and, asked how much, that so much as is erroneous only need be repeated — a verse, or half verse, or quarter verse, or word, or letter. But Kauṣītaki used to say: he should not repeat the mantra, nor offer a libation; there is no flaw — for whatever flaw the Hotṛs make in the sacrifice through inattention, all that Agni as divine Hotṛ makes flawless."@en ;
    skos:note "Keith notes that this passage has no parallel in the Aitareya and none in the Śatapatha, and that the exact sense of Prāgāhi's sentence is uncertain. The uncertainty is about when the flaw counts as passed over and not about the two answers, which are plain."@en,
        "This is the most valuable thing the run read, and what makes it so is that both positions are held by named authorities inside one transmission. On one side the fault is real and repairable to the letter, which is as fine-grained a correction rule as any corpus in this holding states. On the other side there is no fault, because a god repairs whatever inattention produces. The second position is the immunising clause stated as doctrine and attributed by name, not reconstructed by an outsider."@en,
        "What the two positions do to the exactness rule is the point. The whole apparatus entered above — the empty vaṣaṭ, the three faults of the Nivid, the misspoken formula — presupposes that an error has an effect. Kauṣītaki's sentence removes the effect of every error made through inattention, and with it every occasion on which the rule could be seen to hold or fail. The corpus therefore carries the strictest form of the exactness claim and its complete cancellation, two sections apart."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:VaidikaNividdosah ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:KausitakiBrahmana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual .

pmc:VaidikaIndrasatru a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Wachse, der du Indra zum Feinde hast"@de,
        "Grow thou, having Indra for thy foe"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa I,6,3,8-10 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 12, Oxford 1882, pp. 165-166" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission's own demonstration that the wording of an offering formula decides the outcome. Tvaṣṭṛ, whose son Indra has killed and whose Soma Indra has drunk uninvited, pours what is left into the fire saying: Grow thou, having Indra for thy foe. What grows from the fire is Vṛtra. The passage then draws the lesson in one sentence: because he said Grow thou, having Indra for thy foe, therefore Indra slew him; had he said Grow thou, the foe of Indra, he would certainly have forthwith slain Indra."@en ;
    skos:note "What the Śatapatha says and what it does not. It makes the outcome turn on the wording and gives the two wordings; it does not say that the fault lay in the accent. That is supplied by Eggeling's note from the Taittirīya-Saṃhitā II,4,12,1, where the compound is said to have been accented on the first member instead of the second. The Taittirīya was not opened in this run, and the accent is therefore reported here as the translator's note and not as a reading of this holding. The same legend is told again at Śatapatha V,5,4,2 and following, which this run did not read."@en,
        "Who bears the fault is the finding. Tvaṣṭṛ speaks and Vṛtra dies. The speaker of the formula loses nothing by his mistake except the thing he produced with it, and the thing produced is what the mistake kills. See generalizations/displaced-penalties.ttl, where this is now a witness."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:VaidikaDarsapurnamasa ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual .

pmc:VaidikaIstakasamkhya a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die genaue Zahl der Steine und Ziegel, und die zwei Fehlschläge, die sie festsetzen"@de,
        "The exact count of stones and bricks, and the two failures that establish it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa X,4,3,4-8 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 43, Oxford 1897, pp. 356-357" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission states the condition by reporting its own failure twice. The gods perform the Agnihotra, the new and full-moon sacrifices, the seasonal offerings, the animal sacrifice and the Soma-sacrifice: they did not attain immortality. They build a fire-altar with unlimited enclosing-stones and unlimited bricks, as some do to this day saying that the gods did so: they did not attain immortality. Prajāpati then names the fault — ye either make me too large or leave me defective, therefore ye do not become immortal — and the count: three hundred and sixty enclosing-stones, three hundred and sixty bricks laid with formulas and thirty-six more, and ten thousand eight hundred filling bricks. The gods laid down accordingly and thereafter became immortal."@en ;
    skos:note "The numbers are the point and they are checkable in a way almost nothing else here is: a man either laid three hundred and sixty enclosing-stones or he did not. What is not checkable is the outcome attached to them, and the next paragraph of the same passage is where that is settled. See pmc:VaidikaNaSarirenaAmrtah."@en,
        "This is the only place the run found in either work where a defective performance is reported as having actually failed and the fault is then named. It is a report about gods and not about men, which is why it is entered as the ground of the condition and not as a case."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Ye do not lay down all my forms; but ye either make me too large or leave me defective: therefore ye do not become immortal. Stated by Prajāpati in the passage itself, as the reason for two reported failures."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:VaidikaAgnicayanaAmrtatva ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:VaidikaSamkhyapurnata ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual .

pmc:VaidikaMurdhanvyapatana a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Hättest du nicht so gesprochen, wäre dir das Haupt abgeflogen"@de,
        "If thou hadst not spoken thus, thy head would have flown off"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa XI,5,3,13 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 44, Oxford 1900, p. 85" ;
    skos:definition "The close of the contest. Śauceya, having admitted that he did not know the fifth answer, says: here are logs for fuel, I will become thy pupil. Uddālaka replies: if thou hadst not spoken thus, thy head would have flown off; come, enter as my pupil. He then initiates him and teaches him that pain-conquering utterance, Truth."@en ;
    skos:note "A penalty for asking beyond one's own knowledge, stated after the fact and cancelled by the submission that was offered before it was announced. The one act that averts it is acknowledging the other man as teacher, so the sanction protects the order of the school and not the rite."@en,
        "The same sanction is stated twice more in the corpus, and neither place was read at the wording in this run: at Śatapatha XI,4,1,9, to which Eggeling's note refers, and at XIV,6,9,28, where Vidagdha Śākalya's head falls off. The addresses are recorded here so the next run does not have to find them again."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:VaidikaAgnihotra ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual .

pmc:VaidikaNaSarirenaAmrtah a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Von nun an soll niemand mit dem Leibe unsterblich sein"@de,
        "Henceforward no one shall be immortal with the body"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa X,4,3,9-10 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 43, Oxford 1897, pp. 357-358" ;
    skos:definition "The clause that follows the successful piling, and it is spoken by Death. He objects that on this wise all men will become immortal and asks what share will then be his. The gods answer: henceforward no one shall be immortal with the body; only when Death has taken the body as his share shall he who is to become immortal, either through knowledge or through holy work, become immortal after separating from the body. The passage then identifies both the knowledge and the holy work with this same fire-altar."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also the sharpest instance in this holding of a construction that is usually reconstructed rather than read: a transmission that had a checkable promise, met an objection, and narrowed the promise in the same breath. The objector is a character in the story and the narrowing is presented as a concession to him."@en,
        "What the sentence does is worth stating exactly, because the transmission does not hide it and a summary would. Three paragraphs earlier the claim had an outcome anyone could look for: the performers of the rite would not die. Here the outcome is moved past the death of the body by a rule stated inside the same passage, and the reason given for moving it is that Death would otherwise have no share. From this point the claim excludes nothing that could be observed of a performer."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual .

pmc:VaidikaPranitavimocana a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Wasser wird ausgegossen, sonst verletzt das Opfer den Opferherrn"@de,
        "The water is poured out, or the sacrifice injures the sacrificer"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa I,9,2,32 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 12, Oxford 1882, p. 265" ;
    skos:definition "At the close of the new and full-moon offering the priest walks round to the south and pours out the praṇītā-water. The reason is stated as a mechanism: when he spreads the sacrifice he yokes it; but were he not to pour out the water, the sacrifice, being unyoked, would in moving backward injure the sacrificer. In this way, however, the sacrifice does not injure the sacrificer."@en ;
    skos:note "The plainest statement in the corpus of the shape this project looks for in a prerequisite: an act, an omission, and a named consequence of the omission, all in one sentence and with the consequence explained rather than asserted. The sacrifice is a yoked team; what is not unyoked at the end runs back over the man who drove it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "But were he not to pour out the praṇītā-water, the sacrifice, being unyoked, would, in moving backward, injure the sacrificer."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:VaidikaDarsapurnamasa ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:VaidikaPranitavisarjanam ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual .

pmc:VaidikaSkannamPrajananam a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die verschüttete Milch als Zeichen der Vermehrung gelesen"@de,
        "The spilt milk read as a sign of increase"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa XII,4,1,7 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 44, Oxford 1900, p. 180" ;
    skos:definition "The repair for spilling turns the mishap into a promise. He touches the spilt milk with the words: it hath been shed, it hath been implanted, birth hath ensued. The exegesis follows: this earth is a womb and the milk is seed, he thus implants seed in that womb, and forthwith that shed seed of him who so knows this is born forth. The paragraph ends by telling him what to conclude — let him therefore know that abundant production has accrued unto him, that he will be multiplied in offspring and cattle, and that he will become more prosperous."@en ;
    skos:note "It stands two paragraphs from the general assurance at pmc:VaidikaPrayascittaEffectus and does a different job. There the repair removes the harm; here the mishap is reclassified. Both are in the same chapter and neither is presented as an exception."@en,
        "The mishap and its opposite are now both favourable. Milk that stays in the vessel is a correct performance; milk that goes on the ground is abundant production accruing to the performer. The instruction is explicit that this is what he is to know from the event, so nothing about the event remains that could tell him the day went badly."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual .

pmc:VaidikaSvarenaKarma a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Durch die Tonhöhe des Rufes macht der Hotṛ den Opferherrn nach Belieben besser oder schlechter"@de,
        "By the pitch of the call the Hotṛ makes the sacrificer better or worse at will"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aitareya Brāhmaṇa iii,7 (xi,7), in the English of Arthur Berriedale Keith, Rigveda Brahmanas, Harvard Oriental Series 25, Cambridge 1920, p. 169" ;
    skos:definition "A capacity claimed for the officiant over the man who employs him, with the procedure stated. Should he consider the good and evil of the sacrificer whose Hotṛ he is? He should do to him herein as he may desire. If he desires that the man be as he was before sacrificing, he says the vaṣaṭ call in the same tone as he recites the verse. If he desires that he be worse, he recites the verse in a more raised tone and says the call in a more depressed tone. If he desires that he be better, he recites in a more depressed tone and says the call in a more raised tone."@en ;
    skos:note "Kept apart from pmc:VaidikaVasatkaraRiktah on purpose, although both turn on the delivery of the same call. One is a fault with a consequence, the other a technique with an intention; entering them as one node would have lost the fact that this corpus describes the same variation twice, once as damage and once as instrument."@en,
        "This is a capacity in the sense this project looks for and an unusually well specified one: the operation is a relative pitch between two utterances, it is performed in the open in the presence of the person it acts on, and the three intended outcomes are named. What is not named is any sign by which the sacrificer or anyone else would know which of the three was done to him, and the transmission puts the choice in the Hotṛ's discretion without requiring him to declare it."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AitareyaBrahmana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual .

pmc:VaidikaTulamana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wer dies weiß, besteigt die Waage schon in dieser Welt"@de,
        "He who knows this mounts the balance in this world"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa XI,2,7,33 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 44, Oxford 1900, pp. 45-46" ;
    skos:definition "The right edge of the altar-ground is the balance. Whatever good deed a man does is inside the altar-ground and whatever evil deed outside it; let him therefore sit down touching the right edge. For they place him on the balance in the world beyond, and whichever of the two rises he will follow, whether good or evil. And whosoever knows this mounts the balance even in this world and escapes being placed on the balance in the world beyond, for his good deed rises and not his evil deed."@en ;
    skos:note "A weighing whose outcome is fixed in advance for whoever knows how to sit. The passage states the general rule — the rising pan decides — and in the same breath names a condition under which the outcome is not in doubt. Eggeling's note refers this to the Indian ordeal literature, which was not opened here."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual .

pmc:VaidikaVasatkaraSantih a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Der Ruf ist ein Donnerkeil und ist nach jedem Gebrauch zu besänftigen"@de,
        "The call is a thunderbolt and must be appeased after every use"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aitareya Brāhmaṇa iii,8 (xi,8), in the English of Arthur Berriedale Keith, Rigveda Brahmanas, Harvard Oriental Series 25, Cambridge 1920, pp. 169-170" ;
    skos:definition "The vaṣaṭ call is a thunderbolt; it shines when hurled if not appeased. Of it not every man as it were knows the appeasing nor the support. From it even now there is often as it were death. The appeasing of it, the support, is Speech; therefore after each vaṣaṭ call the Hotṛ should recite as accompaniment: O vaṣaṭ call, do not injure me; let me not injure thee. Appeased, it injures him not."@en ;
    skos:note "The chapter then carries a disagreement about the countermeasure itself: an unnamed authority holds that the long formula is impotent and that Force, strength, force is what should accompany the call. Two remedies for one danger, with the second proposed against the first."@en,
        "Three things stand in this short passage that rarely stand together. A present-tense claim of harm — from it even now there is often as it were death — which is not set in myth and not deferred. An admission that the countermeasure is not generally known. And a countermeasure to be applied after every single use, which is what a transmission does when it treats its own danger as real rather than as emphasis."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:VaidikaVasatkara ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AitareyaBrahmana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual .

pmc:ValentinianConsummation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Vollendung als Hochzeit, nicht als Verschmelzung"@de,
        "The consummation as a marriage rather than a merging"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Irenaeus, Adversus Haereses I, in the account of the Valentinian consummation, at the passage on the mother's return, the brides of the angels and the removal of the creator (trans. J. Keble, Oxford 1872)" ;
    skos:definition "The account of a final state in which the mother returns within the fullness and receives the saviour as her spouse; those who call themselves spiritual are stripped of their souls, made purely intellectual spirits, and become brides of spiritual angels; the creator, being merely animal, moves into the mother's place; and the souls of the righteous rest in an intermediate place."@en ;
    skos:note "Placed on the telic axis as pm:conjugalTelos. The placement is a reading and carries the same burden as the claim it sits on: it rests on the bridal state reported in the passage, which has two parties and therefore neither one term nor an absolute. It is stated here rather than left implicit so that it can be contradicted."@en,
        "Recorded because it does not fit either end of the axis it is regularly assigned to. The spiritual ones are not dissolved: they are stripped and transformed, but a bridal state presupposes two, so what is transformed persists as a party to it. Nor are they made into absolutes of their own: they are married, not set apart. The end is a union that keeps what it unites, and that is a third case, distinct from the one in pmc:DistinctivenessAgainstDissolution, where the creature stays with itself against dissolution rather than entering a twoness. See prima-materia#70 and prima-materia#52."@en,
        "The passage is a report by an opponent and is attested as such. What can be said on this source is that the doctrine was ascribed in this form in the second century, not that its holders stated it so. See the caution on pmt:ValentinianGnosis."@en,
        "Two further things in the same passage that a summary loses. The demiurge is not destroyed but relocated, which makes the account less anticosmic than its modern reception; and the souls of the righteous, who are not of the spiritual class, are neither saved into the fullness nor lost, but given a place between. A scheme with two outcomes cannot hold either detail."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:telicOrientation pm:conjugalTelos ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ValentinianGnosis .

pmc:ValidConfessionPresupposesCompleteness a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Eine gültige Beichte setzt voraus, dass nichts verschwiegen wurde"@de,
        "A confession that holds presupposes that nothing was withheld"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V" ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity because the source names the outcome of the want and not merely the rule: the confession was not good and is to be made again. That the finding of the want is itself made by a randomising device is the point of the neighbouring node and does not weaken the necessity, which is stated independently of how it is detected."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Where the bead breaks in two rather than three, or the straws come out odd, the confession was not good and the penitent is told to confess his faults again."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ConfessionBeforeTheIchuri ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ConfessionValidityTest ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmc:ValidVerdictPresupposesCorroboration a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein gültiger Spruch setzt den zweiten Test voraus"@de,
        "A valid verdict presupposes the second test"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 138-139" ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity because the source names what fails to happen without the second test and not merely the order of the two. The qualification that a single test is often accepted stands on the practice node and does not weaken this: where the transmission requires the corroboration, it also says what the want of it costs."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Without the second test no judgement is accepted as valid; where both tests fall alike the verdict is contradictory and void and the matter must be put again on another occasion."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:BengeValidVerdictRule ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:BengeCorroborativeTest ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:VamaWorshipYieldsWorldlyPowers a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der heterodoxe Ritus bringt die weltlichen Vermögen hervor"@de,
        "The heterodox rite brings about the worldly powers"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Kālikāpurāṇa, at the verses on the fruits of the rite (135b-138a) (text, introduction and translation in English by B. N. Shastri, part III, Nag Publishers, Delhi 1991)" ;
    skos:note "Entered on the strength of the passage alone. Whether anything of the kind occurs is a separate question and would need a pm:Testing node; the claim here is that the source says so, and for that the place is the whole of the evidence."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:VamaWorship ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:WorldlyPowersOfTheLeftHandRite .

pmc:VengeanceSystemVeiledBySilence a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Racheverfahren trägt nur, solange die Opfer ungenannt bleiben"@de,
        "The vengeance system holds only while the victims are unnamed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 6" ;
    skos:definition "Every death is held to be caused by witchcraft and to be avenged by lethal magic, and the death of the witch so struck is in turn avenged by his own kin. The transmission keeps the names of those killed by avenging magic secret between the kin and the prince, and the source states that were the names known the whole procedure would stand exposed as futile. Some Azande are reported as doubting the honesty of the princes who hold the oracles, and a few as having seen that the system is fallacious."@en ;
    skos:note "The defence offered when the recorder put the objection is recorded with it: a prince whose oracle has declared that one man died of another's vengeance-magic will refuse to put the second death to his oracle at all, so that the chain is cut by the authority that holds the instrument."@en,
        "The strongest self-warning in this file, and one reported as spoken rather than inferred. It names the exact condition under which the transmission's own central procedure collapses, and names the arrangement by which that condition is kept from arising."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:VespasianHealing a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Heilung durch Berührung, Vespasian zugeschrieben"@de,
        "Healing by touch ascribed to Vespasian"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Suetonius, Divus Vespasianus 7",
        "Tacitus, Historiae IV.81" ;
    skos:definition "A capacity ascribed to a named ruler, in an account whose author remarks that those who were present still tell it although there is now no reward for the lie."@en ;
    skos:note "The remark about eyewitnesses is part of the ascription, not an endorsement of it. It records how the historian wanted the claim received."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:HealingByTouch ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Vespasian ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DivusVespasianus,
        pmw:TacitiHistoriae ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GraecoRoman .

pmc:VikubbanaIddhi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Er lässt die eigene Gestalt und nimmt eine andere an"@de,
        "He abandons his own appearance and takes another"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.137 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, p. 470; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "Success as transformation. He discards his normal form and shows the form of a boy, a serpent, a bird, a demon, a god, a spirit, Brahmā, the ocean, a hill, a lion, a tiger, a leopard, an elephant, a horse, a chariot, a foot soldier or a whole array of an army. The handbook distinguishes the two resolves: for a shape he becomes he resolves may I be such and such a boy, and for a shape shown outside him he resolves let there be an elephant, and not may I be an elephant."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:VitalEnergyCorrespondence a pm:Converging ;
    rdfs:label "Die Entsprechung der Lebensenergie"@de,
        "The vital-energy correspondence"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chāndogya Upaniṣad 7.15.1 (Sanskrit; received prapāṭhaka-khaṇḍa numbering)",
        "Xingqi yupei ming (jade inscription on circulating the qi)" ;
    skos:definition "The observation that several traditions name something conducted or borne in the body and susceptible of deliberate direction."@en ;
    skos:note "The correspondence is weaker than it looks. Pneuma is a principle of cosmic cohesion and not a faculty; the breath of the Sefer Yetzirah is a medium of formation and not a conduction through the limbs. Only the Chinese and the Indian material carries the operative sense the modern draft assumes."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:convergingConcept pmc:InnerHeat,
        pmc:Kundalini,
        pmc:Piti,
        pmc:Pneuma,
        pmc:Prana,
        pmc:Qi,
        pmc:Ruach ;
    pm:independenceGround "The two witnesses that carry the claim of independence are fixed long before any route between them. The jade inscription is dated to the fourth or third century before the common era on the reading this collection follows; the Chāndogya passage belongs to the early Upaniṣads and is older still. The earliest documented traffic that could have carried Indian breath technique into China is the Buddhist transmission of the first centuries of the common era, and both fixations precede it. Neither is an ethnographic gathering, so no question of a recorder's feedback arises; what each carries is the wording of a received text." ;
    pm:independentAttestation pmt:Daoist,
        pmt:Upanisadic ;
    pm:transmissionPath "The Tibetan material reaches Tibet from the Indian mahāsiddha Nāropa through Marpa the translator and is therefore reception, not independent attestation. Buddhist and Daoist exchange along the trade routes is documented and forbids treating the Chinese and Buddhist testimony as wholly separate. The Greek and Hebrew items are neither route-linked nor operatively parallel." .

pmc:WatchfulnessIsUnbound a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Wachsamkeit ist an keinen Zustand gebunden"@de,
        "Watchfulness is bound to no state"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Philokalia I, Hesychios of Sinai, On Watchfulness and Holiness" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:anchoringUnbound ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:Watchfulness ;
    pm:situationGround "The guarding of the heart is set as an unbroken attention to what arises in it, and its whole point is that it does not stop when the occasion changes. A watchfulness held only in a session would not be the thing the literature asks for."@en .

pmc:WhatCeremonyCannotReach a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Götter, die keine Zeremonie beeinflussen kann"@de,
        "The gods that ceremony cannot influence"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 82 and p. 83 and p. 88 and p. 158" ;
    skos:definition "The corpus names three limits on its own means. The Moon fixes the time for the more important undertakings of mankind but is indifferent to ceremonies and cannot be influenced by them, and her potency can be imparted to nothing. The Wind cannot be influenced by sacrifice or ceremony and his potency can be imparted to nothing. And the Sun's potency abides in fire and can be imparted to no other thing, so that since fire cannot be clothed in wrappings, a fetish having the potency of the Sun cannot be prepared at all; a second informant puts the same thing as a refusal rather than an impossibility, saying that no one ever had the potency of the Sun for his own, for the Sun will not be that for anyone."@en ;
    skos:note "A fourth is recorded beside them without being one of them: a fetish carrying the potency of the Great Spirit is said to be able to prevail in all things and to be not permissible to mankind. That is a prohibition and not a limit of the means, and it is not entered as one."@en,
        "Statements of what does not work are scarcer in this graph than statements of what does, and they are worth more per line, because a transmission that names the boundary of its own instrument is making a claim that its own practice could refute. Here there are three of them in a corpus that elsewhere ascribes very wide powers to a shaman."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LakotaRitual .

pmc:WhatLiesStillWhileHeTravels a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Was da stillliegt"@de,
        "What it is that lies still"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, p. 56" ;
    skos:definition "Three positions are reported side by side. Some hold that those who cast themselves down are not really but only in appearance dead. Others hold that the soul departs from the body and, after its travel abroad, returns again. The compiler holds both to be false, on the ground that it is impossible for either man or devil to restore a soul to the body it has once left, and concludes that the devil only stifles the faculties of the soul for a time and hinders their operations."@en ;
    skos:note "No dispute node is written, and the reason is a rule of this repository rather than a judgement about the material. A dispute cites each side from its own work; here all three positions stand in one compilation, reported by one man, and two of them without an owner. What the passage establishes is that the question was open among those the compiler drew on, not who held what."@en,
        "The compiler's own position is reached from a doctrine and not from the material: he rejects the departure of the soul because it would be theologically impossible, not because anything he was told tells against it. That is worth recording, because the same move settles the question for him everywhere else in the chapter."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SamiNoaidi .

pmc:WhetherTheHuacaSpeaks a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Huaca spricht oder der Priester für sie"@de,
        "Whether the huaca speaks or the priest speaks for it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Cristóbal de Molina, Relación de las fábulas y ritos de los incas, ed. Paloma Jiménez del Campo, transcr. Paloma Cuenca Muñoz (Madrid: Iberoamericana / Frankfurt: Vervuert, 2010), fol. 6v",
        "Felipe Guaman Poma de Ayala, Nueva corónica y buen gobierno (1615), Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, GKS 2232 4°, p. 280 [282], the chapter on the sorcerers of this kingdom",
        "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III, on the huacapvillac" ;
    skos:definition "A disagreement between the recorders about what happens at the moment the whole transmission turns on. The indigenous chronicler of sixteen fifteen says outright that these priests deceived men, saying that the huacas eat and drink and speak, which they did not do. The extirpation account of sixteen twenty-one says that the one who speaks with the huaca answers the people what he pretends it tells him, and then adds that sometimes the devil does speak to them through the stone. The earlier report of about fifteen seventy-five says without qualification that they had many huacas and temples where the devil gave his answers."@en ;
    skos:note "The chronicler's denial has a provenance he states himself: he knows what he reports about the priesthood because he served the visitor general who destroyed the huacas of the kingdom. Whether that makes his denial better evidence, as an insider's, or worse, as a man writing for the side that was burning them, is exactly what a later reader has to weigh, and it is why the sentence is in this note."@en,
        "The disagreement is not the ordinary one between a believer and a sceptic. All three recorders are Christians who hold the huacas to be nothing or to be demons, and the one who denies the speaking outright is the one who is himself Andean. What separates them is whether the office rests on an event or on a performance, and that is a question about the transmission and not about their theology."@en,
        "What the three do agree on is that few laymen ever saw the principal huaca and that only the specialist went forward and spoke. That much is common ground between the denial and the two affirmations, and it is entered separately as its own node."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmp:MocharTheHuaca .

pmc:WhetherTheSelfIsKeptOrSurrendered a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Bewahren des Selbst das Ziel des Weges ist oder sein Scheitern"@de,
        "Whether keeping the self is the goal of the path or its failure"@en ;
    dcterms:source "A. Crowley, Magick Without Tears, chapter XII, on the left-hand path and the black brothers (electronic text of the collected letters)",
        "M. A. Aquino, The Temple of Set (Temple of Set, San Francisco), in the account of the Word Xeper and its Egyptian derivation" ;
    skos:definition "A disagreement about what becomes of the bounded self at the end of the path, carried on inside the modern occult literature with the same term on both sides. One party holds that a self which is not surrendered ossifies and is consumed by time, and calls that the second death; the other holds that a self brought forth and made to endure is the attainment, and names it with a word of self-generation."@en ;
    skos:note "One asymmetry the record should not smooth. The earlier side values the matter from a unitive end and treats the surviving self as a remainder that time consumes; the later values it from a separative end and treats the same survival as the work itself. Neither is a misreading of the other. A continuity story running from the one to the other as a single tradition with a changing mood reports a break as a development."@en,
        "That is why the disagreement is worth a node. Read as a quarrel about practices it dissolves into nothing, because neither side defines the path by practices. Read as a quarrel about the end state it is sharp and not decidable from inside either work, because each describes the same movement and calls it by the opposite name. See prima-materia#61 and prima-materia#68."@en,
        "The sides do not differ over means, and both say so. The one states that the signpost of a right-hand and a left-hand path is barely legible, that the two look alike for a long way, and that the question is at which point they diverge; he puts the divergence at the crossing of the Abyss and separates the sorcerer from the brother of the left-hand path as sharply as a thief from a tyrant. The other derives his word from an Egyptian term for coming into being and applies it to the transformation of the will, not to any particular practice. Whatever is at issue between them, it is not method."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:XeperAsSelfGeneration .

pmc:WhoseTheDrumIs a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Trommel der Sonne gehört oder Gott"@de,
        "Whether the drum belongs to the sun or to God"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 47-48",
        "Knud Leem, An Account of the Laplanders of Finmark, in John Pinkerton (ed.), A General Collection of the Best and Most Interesting Voyages and Travels, vol. I (London: Longman, Hurst, Rees and Orme, 1808), chap. XXI, pp. 476-477" ;
    skos:definition "The two recorders describe the same instrument and disagree about what is invoked over it. The compilation of sixteen seventy-four has the drum made from a tree whose grain turns with the course of the sun, because the tree is held acceptable to the sun, which they worship under the image of Thor; the whole art is treated as heathen and the answers as the devil's. The account of seventeen sixty-seven reports a named practitioner who, going to use his drum, marked himself and it with the sign of the cross as an earnest of good success, said the Lord's Prayer, invoked God the Father, the Son and an angel to assist him, and in the middle of the work broke out into a prayer to the God who created the heaven and the earth, calling himself a man liable to error, old and unworthy, and saying it were better that he should die than that God should deny help to the one on whose behalf they entreated."@en ;
    skos:note "Ninety years lie between them and the second recorder had read the first, so this is not two independent descriptions of one moment. What it is, is a transmission caught in the middle of taking something over: the instrument, the hammer, the ring and the rule of the sun's course are unchanged, and what is said over them has been replaced. The reading rule survived the theology that was given for it, which says something about which of the two the practice actually rested on."@en,
        "That the later station added something to what it received is exactly the shape pm:Reworking is for, and no reworking node is written: the addition here is Christian and the transmission it came from is not the one that performed the act. The class as it stands wants one tradition taking over from another, and this is a tradition taking over from the mission that was suppressing it. The gap is recorded rather than forced."@en,
        "The second recorder notices one detail against himself and reports it: that in calling the Holy Ghost an angel the man used a word he had never heard the Lapps use for it anywhere he knew, where they say Passe Vuoign. He records the anomaly instead of correcting it, which is why the passage can be used at all."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmp:RunicDrumConsultation .

pmc:WilledDisplacementProtocol a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der Ortswechsel so unmittelbar ist, wie die Behauptung verlangt"@de,
        "Whether the displacement is instantaneous in the way the claim requires"@en ;
    skos:definition "An examination of the claim against what the transmissions that report the journey say about its duration."@en ;
    skos:note "A transmission in this holding says the opposite, and it says it in the one form that can be compared. pmc:DurationTracksDistance records that the time the practitioner lies still is stated to depend on how near or far the place is, an hour or two for a near one, with an upper bound of a day however great the distance. A journey whose duration follows the distance is not a displacement by willing; it is a movement with a speed."@en,
        "How much weight this bears, stated plainly. It is a seventeenth-century report of what practitioners said, not a measurement, and it is one transmission among several. It does not refute the posit. What it does is show that the draft's account is not the one its sources give, at a point where the sources happen to be specific — and specificity of that kind is rare enough in this field that it should not pass unremarked."@en,
        "No procedure devised, for the same reason as at the neighbouring node: the duration of the journey is only reachable through the traveller's report, and the claim is about what that report describes. The falsifying condition would require an independent fix on when the traveller arrived, which is the whole question."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:noProcedureDevised ;
    pm:examinedBy "Nobody. The comparison with the transmission is recorded in the notes and is not an examination; no protocol has been devised that could be run" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:IntentionReplacesMusculature .

pmc:WindAsSourceProtocol a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob Wind eine Quelle ist"@de,
        "Whether wind is a source"@en ;
    skos:definition "An examination of the claim that moving air supplies the practitioner with something over and above respiration."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered on no procedure devised, and the reason is worth stating rather than passing over. The neighbouring claim about cold names a boundary condition that instruments already measure, so it could be turned around within an afternoon. This one names no quantity that distinguishes it from breathing in still air, and until it does, there is nothing to fail."@en,
        "What would make it examinable is a difference the claim itself must state: the same breathing, in moving and in still air, with the two conditions expected to come apart in some named respect. That is a small addition to the claim and it is the whole difference between this node and its neighbour."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:noProcedureDevised ;
    pm:examinedBy "Nobody. The state records that no falsifying condition has been stated, which is a fact about the means of examination and not about the claim" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:EnergyTakenFromWind .

pmc:WitchcraftDreamIsExperienceNotSymbol a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein böser Traum ist eine Erfahrung der Hexerei, kein Zeichen für sie"@de,
        "A bad dream is an experience of witchcraft, not a sign of it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), appendix II, pp. 230-231" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission divides dreams into witchcraft-dreams and oracular dreams, a nightmare being of the first kind and a pleasant dream of the second. A bad dream is held to be not a symbol of witchcraft but an actual undergoing of it: in waking life a man learns that he was bewitched only from a subsequent misfortune or from an oracle, but in a dream he sees the witches themselves and may speak with them. The soul is held to leave the body in sleep, and the witch to send the soul of his witchcraft to eat the soul of his victim while both sleep."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because it stands squarely against a claim this graph holds open elsewhere, that the figures met at the threshold of sleep are the signature of the sleeper's own state and not visitors. Here the transmission asserts the opposite in as many words. It is one side and not a refutation; the other side is not in this file, so no dispute node is written."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:WoodwardCakkaInterpolatio a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "Die sieben Kraftzentren mit ihren Farben, und die Gefahr unterhalb des Nabels"@de,
        "The seven force-centres with their colours, and the danger below the navel"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1916" ;
    dcterms:source "F. L. Woodward, Manual of a Mystic (London: Pali Text Society Translation Series VI, 1916), translator's footnote at pp. 13-15, compiled from C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that the body carries seven centres of force at the base of the spine, the navel, the spleen, the heart, the throat, between the eyebrows and the top of the head; that each has a stated number of radiations and a predominant colour; that the base of the spine houses a serpent-fire; that the centre between the eyebrows gives clairvoyance and the centre at the throat the hearing of distant sounds when stimulated; that the centre at the crown, called the thousand-petalled lotus, gives unbroken consciousness on all planes; and that arousing the centres below the navel is a process attended with great danger."@en ;
    skos:note "It is not the same case as pmc:TheosophicalColourKeyToFeeling in originations/affect-scales.ttl and the two should not be merged. That one is a doctrine circulating under its own steam as ancient wisdom; this one is a doctrine printed physically inside a translation of a datable manuscript, which is a stronger form of the same thing and a rarer one. The two share an author and that is worth recording: the same writer supplies the colour key that later affect scales inherit and the force-centre system that gets read back into a Theravāda manual."@en,
        "Measured against the source on 2026-10-08, the source having been opened for the first time in this holding at traditions/theosophy.ttl and the measurement recorded at pmp:TheosophyMarkerVerificationSearch in examinations/searches-theosophy.ttl. The note is a faithful digest on most of what it carries: the count of seven, the locations, the five radiation counts, the colours and the three faculties all stand in the chapter it names, and the three faculties are the source's own and not the translator's, which this node did not previously establish. pmc:TheosophyClairaudientia, pmc:TheosophyClairvoyantia, pmc:TheosophyMagnificatio and pmc:TheosophyConscientiaContinua hold them."@en,
        "The diagnostic markers, so that a later presentation can be measured against them. The count: seven. The radiation counts the note gives: four, ten, six, twelve and sixteen for the first five centres, and nine hundred and sixty for the thousand-petalled lotus. The sixth centre is the one the note gives without a radiation count, as two halves, one rose-coloured and the other purplish blue. An earlier version of this line listed two as a radiation count beside the others; it is not one, in the note or in the work the note names, and the correction was made on 2026-10-08 when that work was opened. The assignments the note makes to the manual's own vocabulary: the throat centre to the element of mobility and the brow centre to the element of heat. Those two assignments are the join, and they are the translator's, not the manuscript's; the manual assigns its elements to raptures and colours and never to a centre of force."@en,
        "The sentence that matters for this holding is the last of the note: it will be noted that the Buddhist method here does not seek to arouse the centres below the navel, a process attended with great danger. That is a caution about the practice of the manual, expressed as an observation about it, and it is the only caution in the whole volume. The manual itself states no caution and no danger anywhere — established by reading it complete and searched term by term at pmp:BoranPericulumSearch in examinations/searches-boran-kammatthana.ttl. So the one safety statement a reader finds in the book is an inference drawn in 1916 from a theosophical work of 1911 about a system the manuscript does not describe."@en,
        "Two things do not survive the measurement and the second is the one that matters. The sixth count: the source gives ninety-six for the brow centre as its own figure and attributes the figure two to the Indian books, saying that a count of undulations of the same character as the others gives forty-eight in each half; the note keeps the two halves and drops the ninety-six, so a reader of the note has the Indian books figure in the place where the source put its own. And the warning, which is the whole reason this node exists: the source does not warn against the centres below the navel. It warns against three other centres, which it says Blavatsky calls the lower centres, which are not among its seven, and whose awakening it calls the greatest of misfortunes. In the source the base of the spine and the navel are the first two of the seven that are used in occult development, and the arousing of the fire at the base of the spine is the means by which the rest are vivified. The one safety statement in the volume therefore warns against the first two steps of the method it is digesting, and it does so in a sentence shaped as an observation about a Sinhala manuscript that describes neither. pmc:TheosophyPericulumKundalini holds the source side."@en,
        "What makes the case sharp is that the translator marks his source and the marking is easy to miss. The footnote begins by saying he has compiled it from Mr. C. W. Leadbeater's The Inner Life, and it then runs for a page and a half in the same typeface as his philological notes, alongside notes that do render the manuscript. Nothing in the layout separates the compiled system from the translated text."@en ;
    pm:circulatesAs "A note in a Pali Text Society translation of an eighteenth century Sinhala meditation manual, printed at the foot of the page on which that manual places the five raptures in the body, and introduced with the words that it shows the centres according to Yoga. A reader taking the volume for the transmission it translates reads the seven centres, their colours and the warning as part of what the manual teaches." ;
    pm:originatedBy "C. W. Leadbeater, whose book the translator names as the source he compiled the note from; with G. Sutcliffe for the identification of the four great elements with four densities of modern physics, cited from The Theosophist of September 1914" .

pmc:WoundsSharedWithTheAnimal a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Mensch trägt die Wunden, die das Tier davontrug"@de,
        "The man is found with the wounds the animal took"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulo I, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that the man and his animal are one body for the purpose of injury: after a threatened person had a fight in the river with a caiman or in the open with some other animal, the man who had uttered the threat was afterwards found bearing the same wounds the animal had taken from the fight, although he had been elsewhere while it happened."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the most examinable claim in what was read, and it is examinable in the ordinary way: it asserts a coincidence of injuries between two bodies at a distance, with a stated time relation. Nothing about it requires an opinion on whether the transformation occurs. No protocol stands on it yet; see prima-materia#231."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NahuaRitual .

pmc:Wuduo a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die fünf Beraubungen, in denen nicht abgeleitet werden darf"@de,
        "The five deprivations, in which nothing may be drained"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 61 (Wu jin), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 9" ;
    skos:definition "Five states of prior loss in which the draining operation is forbidden outright: the flesh already lost; after a great loss of blood; after a great sweating; after a great purging; and after childbirth or a great flow of blood. The chapter closes the list with the sentence that in all of these one may not drain."@en ;
    skos:note "Held apart from the seven states of Suwen 52 and the twelve of Lingshu 9 because the criterion is different. Those forbid needling in a condition of the moment, drunkenness or anger or hunger; these forbid one half of the operation after a loss that has already happened, and the prohibition is on draining and not on needling as such. A transmission that separates the two halves of its own operation this way is saying something about the operation."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Cizhen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmc:Wuguo a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Beim Füllen und Ableiten das Maß nicht überschreiten"@de,
        "In supplementing and draining, do not exceed the measure"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 61 (Wu jin), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 9" ;
    skos:definition "The whole of what the chapter says about the five excesses. Asked what the five excesses of needling are, the answer is one clause: in supplementing and in draining, do not go beyond their measure."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the corpus's general rule of measure against its own operation, and it is remarkable for how little it carries. It names no quantity, no sign by which the measure would be recognised, and no consequence of exceeding it, while the same chapter states the five prohibitions and the five deprivations with places and states attached. A rule of measure with nothing to measure by is entered as what the corpus says and not as a procedure."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Cizhen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmc:WuliErshiwu a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Fünfmal fünf am Wuli, und die Zuflüsse sind erschöpft"@de,
        "Five times five at the wuli, and the transports are drained"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 60 (Yu ban), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 9" ;
    skos:definition "The one counted threshold in this corpus. Asked whether there is a number to it, the answer is that one meets it at the wuli and stops midway; five arrivals and that is all; five goings and the qi of a depot is exhausted; therefore five fives, twenty-five, and the transports are drained. The chapter calls this robbing a man of his heaven-given qi and denies that it is a cutting short of his allotted span. It adds the two cases: he who needles peering in from the gate, the man dies in the house; he who enters the gate and needles, he dies in the hall. The chapter ends by ordering the matter written on jade tablets and handed to later ages as a prohibition of needling, so that the people dare not transgress it."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because of its shape and not its plausibility. Every other lethal claim in this corpus attaches to a place — a depot, a vessel, a region — and this one attaches to a number of repetitions at a single named point. A dose is a different kind of claim from a location: it can be exceeded by degrees, and the transmission that states one has said what an operator would have to count."@en,
        "The two gate clauses are the part that resists any reading as observation. They make the interval to death depend on where the operator was standing, which no arrangement of the body could produce, and they stand in the same passage as the count. What the graph holds here is therefore one claim with two halves of very different kinds, and it does not separate them, because the source does not."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Cizhen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmc:WuniYuban a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die zwei Fünfergruppen der Widersetzlichkeit, jede mit ihrer Frist"@de,
        "The two sets of five counter-flows, each with its term"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 60 (Yu ban), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 9" ;
    skos:definition "Three lists of five in one chapter. The first is for a wound gone to pus: the white of the eye blue and the black of the eye small; vomiting what is given by mouth; pain in the belly with great thirst; the shoulder and neck not free; the voice hoarse and the colour gone. Except for these five, the case runs with the grain. The second set of five carries a term: a distended belly with a hot body and a large pulse; the belly rumbling and full with the four limbs cold and purging, and the pulse large; nosebleed that will not stop, and a large pulse; coughing with blood in the urine, the flesh gone, and a small taut pulse; coughing, the flesh gone, the body hot, and a small quick pulse. Of these the chapter says that such a one dies within fifteen days. The third set carries a shorter term: a greatly distended belly with the four extremities cold, the flesh gone and severe purging; a distended belly with blood in the stool and a large pulse that intermits; coughing with blood in the urine, the flesh inwardly gone, and a beating pulse; vomiting blood with a full chest drawing to the back, and a small quick pulse; coughing and vomiting with a distended belly and undigested stools, and the pulse cut off. Of these it says that such a one dies within a single hour. It adds that a workman who does not examine for these and needles anyway is performing a treatment against the grain, and elsewhere in the chapter that once the pus has formed, ten die and one lives."@en ;
    skos:note "The chapter states no exception and names no case in which such a patient lives. That is what distinguishes it from the year-prognosis of the red vessels, which names the finding under which it predicts nothing at all."@en,
        "This is the most examinable prognostic material read in this corpus, and its form is why. Each item is a conjunction of findings that a second examiner could be asked to confirm, the two terms are short enough to be observed out, and the two terms are different, so the lists are not a single undivided claim about the gravely ill. Against that stands the pulse, which is the finding two examiners are least likely to take alike, and which appears in nine of the ten."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmc:WushiWuxu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die fünf Füllen und die fünf Leeren und die zwei Bedingungen ihres Überlebens"@de,
        "The five repletions and the five depletions, and the two conditions of surviving them"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 19 (Yu ji zhen zang lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 6" ;
    skos:definition "The chapter states that the five repletions kill and the five depletions kill, and then names both. Repletion: the pulse full, the skin hot, the belly distended, front and back stopped, and the sight and mind clouded. Depletion: the pulse fine, the skin cold, the qi scant, purging and diarrhoea, and food and drink not going in. Asked why some nevertheless live, the answer names one condition for each. Where gruel enters the stomach and the pouring purge stops, the depleted one lives; where the body sweats and the bowels then move, the replete one lives. These, the chapter says, are the signs of it."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the one prognosis read in this corpus that names its own rescue condition in the same breath as the death sentence, and names it as an event rather than as a state. Both conditions are things that either happen or do not, and both are observable by anyone at the bedside without a pulse. That combination is rare enough here to be the reason for the node."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmc:XingqiInscriptionDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Datierung und Lesung der Jadeinschrift"@de,
        "The dating and reading of the jade inscription"@en ;
    dcterms:source "D. Harper, Early Chinese Medical Literature (1998), p. 125",
        "H.D. Roth, Original Tao (1999)",
        "J. Needham, Science and Civilisation in China II (1956), p. 143" ;
    skos:definition "A disagreement among editors about the age of the inscription on circulating the qi and about how its opening is to be construed. One dating places it in the late fourth or early third century before the common era, another in the middle of the sixth; one reading takes the opening characters as a title and begins the text at the second line, another counts and construes differently."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as a node rather than as a remark, because the dating decides whether this is the earliest datable witness to deliberate circulation of the qi or one witness among several, and because a citation that names only the inscription conceals that anything is at issue."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmp:Xingqi ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XingqiYupeiMing .

pmc:YangshengYieldsDubaisui a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Regimen bringt die volle Spanne hervor"@de,
        "The regimen yields the full span"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 1 (Shang gu tian zhen lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 1" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ZhidaoYangsheng ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:DubaisuiNaiqu .

pmc:YaodongGuBing a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Mittel wirkt, darum die Krankheit"@de,
        "The drug is moving, therefore the illness"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Han Yu, epitaph for the late Erudite of the Grand Academy Li, in Wubaijia zhu Changli wenji, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text, juan 34" ;
    skos:definition "What the takers say, reported by the man who assembled the case series against them, and reported as two sentences that come at different moments. Of the dead who went before: those who died all failed to get the method; I am not like them. Of their own sickness, at its onset: the drug is moving, and that is why the illness; when the illness goes and the drug takes effect, one will not die. And when they are themselves about to die, he adds, they repent again."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as a claim of this transmission although no text of it printed here says so, because the whole of the file above is a transmission and this is the sentence that keeps it standing. The two moves are complementary and between them cover every case: the deaths of others are assigned to their method, and one's own symptoms are assigned to the drug working. Nothing can then be a failure of the drug."@en,
        "One instance is reported and not merely the rule. When the commissioner of Xiangyang had been ill a year, a member of his household said that the drug he had taken before was the wrong one, that he was about to purge it, and that when purged he would be well. He was ill two years and died."@en,
        "The same machinery is printed as instruction at pmc:JinchengYaocheng, where a run that yields no gold is to be fired again, and at pmc:HedanTiaojian, where a drug that does not form may be blamed on an unbeliever's slander. Here it is not the instruction but the speech of the men taking it, written down by an outsider who had watched seven of them die. The graph therefore holds this form from three sides: as recipe, as excuse, and as observation."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Waidan .

pmc:YinshuBaJingYieldsWuBing a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Ziehen der acht Schüsse bringt Krankheitsfreiheit hervor"@de,
        "The pull of the Eight Warps yields freedom from illness"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhangjiashan Yinshu, slips 104 to 105, in Vivienne Lo (trans.), How to do the Gibbon Walk: A Translation of the Pulling Book (ca. 186 BCE), Needham Research Institute Working Papers 3 (Cambridge: Needham Research Institute, 2014)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:YinshuBaJing ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Yangsheng ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:YinshuWuBing .

pmc:YinshuBingYuan a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Warum ein Mensch krank wird"@de,
        "Why a person becomes ill"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhangjiashan Yinshu, slips 103 to 104, in Vivienne Lo (trans.), How to do the Gibbon Walk: A Translation of the Pulling Book (ca. 186 BCE), Needham Research Institute Working Papers 3 (Cambridge: Needham Research Institute, 2014)" ;
    skos:definition "The list the third text opens with, given as invariable: the reasons why a person becomes ill are heat, damp, wind, cold, rain and dew; the opening and closing of the patterns of the skin; lack of harmony in eating and drinking; and not being able to respond to cold and heat in getting up and going to sleep. It adds that between the four seasons chaotic qi bump against each other, so that people cannot avoid being in the middle of it themselves."@en ;
    skos:note "Four of the five members are outside the person and the fifth is his conduct, and the closing clause says that the outside part cannot be avoided. An aetiology built that way leaves the conduct as the only thing the instruction can address, which is what the rest of the text does."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Yangsheng .

pmc:YinshuDaoyinYieldsShanYu a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Ziehen bringt den Nutzen für den ihm zugeordneten Teil hervor"@de,
        "Pulling yields the benefit of the part named for it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhangjiashan Yinshu, slips 99 to 102, in Vivienne Lo (trans.), How to do the Gibbon Walk: A Translation of the Pulling Book (ca. 186 BCE), Needham Research Institute Working Papers 3 (Cambridge: Needham Research Institute, 2014)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:YinshuDaoyin ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Yangsheng ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:YinshuShanYu .

pmc:YinshuGuiJianYiBing a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Vornehme und der Geringe erkranken aus verschiedenen Gründen, und beide aus dem eigenen"@de,
        "The noble and the lowly fall ill for different reasons, and both for their own"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhangjiashan Yinshu, slips 106 to 110, in Vivienne Lo (trans.), How to do the Gibbon Walk: A Translation of the Pulling Book (ca. 186 BCE), Needham Research Institute Working Papers 3 (Cambridge: Needham Research Institute, 2014)" ;
    skos:definition "The passage that closes the third text, which divides the causation of illness by rank. The nobility fall ill because they do not harmonise their joys and their passions: if they are joyful the Yang qi is in excess, if they are angry the Yin qi is in excess; those who follow the Way exhale the warm breath quickly when joyful and puff out the moist breath the more when angry, in order to harmonise it. The lowly fall ill from exhaustion at their labour, from hunger and thirst, and because when the hundred sweats cease they plunge themselves into water and then lie down in a cold and empty place; they do not know to put on more clothes, and they also do not know to expel air and breathe out to get rid of it, and on account of this they have many illnesses and die easily. Before both stands the general statement that people are born into their nature and do not know how to be sparing with their qi, and therefore often become ill and die easily."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also a social claim and not only a medical one, and the graph should not smooth that away. The text explains why the people who work are the people who are ill, and it explains it by their ignorance rather than by their labour, although it names the labour first."@en,
        "The operative word in both halves is knowing, and it is where the failure is put. The noble man's illness is a failure of moderation and the lowly man's is a failure of knowledge — he does not know to dress warmly, he does not know to breathe it out. Every illness the passage accounts for is thereby accounted for by something the sick person did or did not know, and the remedy in both halves is the same breath the text has just taught. The corpus has stated its own immunising clause in the plainest possible form, and it stated it in the second century before this era."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Yangsheng .

pmc:YinshuPengzuDao a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Erzeugen, wachsen, ernten, bewahren: der Weg des Peng Zu"@de,
        "Generate, grow, harvest, preserve: the way of Peng Zu"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhangjiashan Yinshu, slip 1, in Vivienne Lo (trans.), How to do the Gibbon Walk: A Translation of the Pulling Book (ca. 186 BCE), Needham Research Institute Working Papers 3 (Cambridge: Needham Research Institute, 2014)" ;
    skos:definition "The opening line of the work, which puts the whole regimen under one figure: in the spring generate, in the summer grow, in the autumn harvest, in the winter preserve, this is the way of Peng Zu."@en ;
    skos:note "The four terms are the year of a plant and the year of a farmer, applied to a body. The work names no other authority anywhere, and it names this one in its first sentence, which is where a transmission with a lineage would put the lineage."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Yangsheng .

pmc:YinshuRiyongYieldsChangshou a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die jahreszeitliche Tagesordnung bringt langes Leben hervor"@de,
        "The day's order in its season yields long life"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhangjiashan Yinshu, slip 2, in Vivienne Lo (trans.), How to do the Gibbon Walk: A Translation of the Pulling Book (ca. 186 BCE), Needham Research Institute Working Papers 3 (Cambridge: Needham Research Institute, 2014)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:YinshuSijieRiyong ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Yangsheng ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:YinshuChangshou .

pmc:YinshuShiRi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Tausendmal viermal am Tag, nach zehn Tagen aufhören"@de,
        "A thousand times four times a day, stop after ten days"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhangjiashan Yinshu, slips 40 to 42 and slip 48, in Vivienne Lo (trans.), How to do the Gibbon Walk: A Translation of the Pulling Book (ca. 186 BCE), Needham Research Institute Working Papers 3 (Cambridge: Needham Research Institute, 2014)" ;
    skos:definition "The one place where the work puts a term in days on a treatment, and it does it twice with the same numbers. For feet whose steps cannot be coordinated, with the knees tending to hurt and the lower legs to cold, a shaved piece of wood four chi long is hung on new twist four chi from the ground; the patient sits on it and jumps the legs alternately a thousand times in the morning, a thousand at midday, a thousand in the evening and a thousand at midnight, and stops after ten days. For two hands with too little qi in them, the arms unequal and the fingertips tending to numbness, the elbows are held as if bound to the sides and swung vigorously, a thousand times in the morning, at midday and at midnight, and stops after ten days."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the shape from which a failure condition can be made, and it is rare enough in this holding to be worth naming as such. A count, a frequency and a term together say what would have to be done before anyone could say the treatment had been given its chance. What the work does not add is the other half: it nowhere says what follows if the ten days pass and the steps are still uncoordinated."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Yangsheng .

pmc:YinshuYiZeShangQi a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Mehr davon schädigt das Qi"@de,
        "Increasing it will harm the qi"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhangjiashan Yinshu, slips 3, 5 and 7, with the autumn paragraph at slip 6, in Vivienne Lo (trans.), How to do the Gibbon Walk: A Translation of the Pulling Book (ca. 186 BCE), Needham Research Institute Working Papers 3 (Cambridge: Needham Research Institute, 2014)" ;
    skos:definition "The sanction the work attaches to the timed member of the day's order, in the same five graphs each time and set on its own slip: when it is time, cease; increasing it will harm the qi."@en ;
    skos:note "It stands three times and not four. Spring, summer and winter each carry it; the autumn paragraph carries no hour, no stopping rule and no sanction, and says instead that the body's desires are to be indulged in eating and drinking and that entering the chamber whenever the body derives comfort from it is the way of benefit. The asymmetry is in the text and is not a lacuna: the autumn slip is whole and ends with its own closing formula. A transmission that suspends its one sanction for one quarter of the year has said something about what the sanction is for."@en,
        "What is named as harmed is the qi and not the practitioner's chances. The cost of exceeding is a state of the body, which is the same place three other transmissions in this holding put it, and it is entered here in its own right rather than as a case of that."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:YinshuRushi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Yangsheng .

pmc:YinshuZaoShiHanRe a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Welcher Atem welchem Wetter antwortet"@de,
        "Which breath answers which weather"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhangjiashan Yinshu, slips 111 to 112, in Vivienne Lo (trans.), How to do the Gibbon Walk: A Translation of the Pulling Book (ca. 186 BCE), Needham Research Institute Working Papers 3 (Cambridge: Needham Research Institute, 2014)" ;
    skos:definition "The closing rule of the work, which sets the breath against the state of the air in four cases: if it is dry, breathe the dry breath out frequently and lie down frequently; if it is damp, puff out the moist breath frequently, do not lie down, and make the Yin substantial; if it is hot, concentrate exclusively on exhaling the warm breath; when it is cold, work the body. This, it says, is the way to align dry, damp, cold and heat with one another."@en ;
    skos:note "A rule with four branches and a criterion the practitioner does not set, which is unusual in this holding: the weather decides which branch applies, and the weather is the same for everybody present. It is the nearest thing the work offers to a procedure another person could check him against."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Yangsheng .

pmc:YinshuZhuRen a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die zweite Person im Raum, und was geschieht, wenn der Kranke sich nicht heben kann"@de,
        "The other person in the room, and what is done when the patient cannot rise"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhangjiashan Yinshu, slips 32, 49, 60 and 72, in Vivienne Lo (trans.), How to do the Gibbon Walk: A Translation of the Pulling Book (ca. 186 BCE), Needham Research Institute Working Papers 3 (Cambridge: Needham Research Institute, 2014)" ;
    skos:definition "Four treatments require somebody else and name what he does. For the neck that cannot turn, others raise the head from in front and behind as far as it will go, and then the patient is slowly straightened and rested. For bowel flushing, the patient lies prostrate with the chin on a pillow and instructs a person to step on the small of his back. For urine retention, another person is directed at the small of the back while the patient embraces a column. For abdominal pain, someone raises a strung wooden board to a height of one chi for the patient to stand on. The treatment for bowel flushing then states what to do where the illness has gone so far that the patient cannot raise himself: the other person raises the buttocks using the clothes."@en ;
    skos:note "The last clause is the reason for the node. A prescription that provides for the case in which the patient is too ill to do his own part is providing for its own procedure failing at the body of the person it is addressed to, and it answers with a substitution rather than with an excuse. This holding carries a great deal of the second kind and very little of the first."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Yangsheng .

pmc:YuruguPursuit a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Yourougou in Verfolgung Yasiguis"@de,
        "Yourougou in pursuit of Yasigui"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), p. 292" ;
    skos:definition "The account in which one twin tore away a piece of the placenta that became the earth, was turned into the pale fox for it, and communicated his own impurity to the earth so that it became dry and barren; his fate is to pursue his twin, who is also his female soul, until the end of time. On the mythical plane the seed-star is this fox, held in space and turning tirelessly around Sirius, that is around the pursued twin, without ever reaching her."@en ;
    skos:note "The pursuit that never arrives is the same figure as the orbit that never closes on its centre. Whether the star is the image of the myth or the myth the reading of the star is exactly the question the article declines to raise."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Dogon .

pmc:ZandeOracleSetDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Welche Orakel die Überlieferung als die ihren zählt"@de,
        "Which oracles the transmission counts as its own"@en ;
    dcterms:source "A. de Calonne-Beaufaict, Azande: introduction à une ethnographie générale des bassins de l'Ubangi-Uele et de l'Aruwimi (Bruxelles: Maurice Lamertin, 1921), chapter V, Procédés divinatoires (mantique), pp. 183-184",
        "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), appendix I, p. 228, and p. 164" ;
    skos:definition "A disagreement between the two recorders about the extent and the naming of the Zande set of oracles. The monograph gives the generic term as soroka and names four principal oracles: the poison, the rubbing-board, the termites and a three-stick oracle called mapingo. The earlier Belgian ethnography gives the generic term as lora, lists eight procedures, and assigns the pile-of-sticks oracle to the Abarambo, Mangbetu and Bakango and to Azande of those origins rather than to the transmission at large. It also describes the termites procedure differently, as a plaited grass braid laid in a small pit and read from whether the termites cut it through or gnaw only its underside."@en ;
    skos:note "The disagreement matters beyond terminology. Which procedures belong to the transmission and which were taken over from neighbours is exactly the question a contact route turns on, and the two recorders answer it differently for the same set. Neither was read against the other in a modern study in this run."@en,
        "The two accounts also differ on who receives the poison. The Belgian ethnography, written sixteen years earlier and on the other side of the border, gives the ordeal as administered to a suspected person or to a fowl, in the present tense and without qualification. The monograph gives administration to human beings as a thing of the past and very rare even then. Whether that is a change over sixteen years, a difference between the two administrations, or a difference of observation is not decidable from the two passages."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:OracleRankOrder .

pmc:ZangshuDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Wahl von Grab und Tag das Geschick der Nachkommen entscheidet"@de,
        "Whether the choice of grave and day decides the fortune of the descendants"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Jiu Tangshu 79, the memorial of Lü Cai on the burial books, juan 79, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text",
        "Wang Chong, Lunheng, the treatise on frugal burial (Bo zang), juan 23, Sibu congkan facsimile",
        "Zangshu, inner and miscellaneous sections, in the recension of Wu Cheng, one juan, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text" ;
    skos:definition "A memorial preserved in the dynastic history sets seven numbered objections against the burial books, on an imperial commission to revise the divinatory literature. The rites fix the interval between death and burial by rank, so the burial has a set time and no year or month is to be chosen; the books call one day of the sexagenary cycle the most inauspicious of all for a burial, and a check of the classical annals turns up more than twenty burials on that day, so the day is not chosen either; the hour is fixed by what each dynasty honoured, so the hour is not chosen; one house kept its succession and another lost its sacrifices without either turning on a burial; the ancients buried north of their capitals in one fixed place, without the surname doctrine, and their descendants were not the poorer for it; a grave once made is never altered while rank rises and falls without rest, so office is not what a burial confers; and the practice makes mourners choose ground in hope of an office and a season in hope of money, so that the books corrupt the customs."@en ;
    skos:note "A second and much older objector was added on 2026-09-10, and it attacks the premise rather than the practice. The first-century treatise on frugal burial argues that the dead have no knowledge: that while a parent lives his child seeks the diviner and calls the physician, in hope that the misfortune may be dispelled and the drug do good, but that once he is dead, though the diviner were as discerning as Wu Xian and the physician as skilled as Bian Que, he does not live again, because with the qi cut off there is no further benefit at all; and that the soul of a dead parent assuredly knows nothing. On that account there is no channel from the bones to the grandson, which is the premise this file holds as the ground of the whole art. The treatise is arguing against lavish burial and not against siting, so the objection is a consequence of its position and not its target; that is why it is entered here as a side and not as its own dispute."@en,
        "The defending side is the book itself and the commentary printed with it, which cites two cases the other way: a reader of graves who saw a general's tomb and said the clan would be wiped out, and it was; and a duke of the annals who moved his capital against a prognostication that it would benefit the people and not the ruler, and died in the fifth month. Both are anecdotes reported by the defence and neither was traced to the works they name."@en,
        "The sixth objection is the one worth naming, because it is an argument of a form the graph can reuse: the cause is invariant and the effect fluctuates, therefore the cause is not that cause. The second is of another form again and is treated as evidence rather than as testimony, in the protocol beside this file."@en,
        "The two objectors do not agree with each other and the graph should not merge them. The memorial concedes that a grave is chosen and argues that the choice decides nothing; the treatise denies the mechanism that would make any choice matter. A defender who has answered one has not answered the other."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:YitiShouyin ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination .

pmc:ZangshuRecensionHistory a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wie das hier gelesene Buch entstanden ist"@de,
        "How the book read here was made"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1781" ;
    dcterms:source "Zangshu, the editors' prefatory summary in the Wenyuange Siku quanshu text, dated the tenth month of the forty-sixth year of Qianlong" ;
    skos:definition "The account the editors of the imperial collection give of the text they are printing. The attribution to Guo Pu of the fourth century is a received one and the book is not among the works his biography names; a burial book under his name first appears in the Song bibliography. Practitioners afterwards competed in embellishing it until it ran to twenty pian; Cai Yuanding, finding it overgrown, struck out twelve and kept eight; Wu Cheng, holding that Cai had not gone to the bottom of it, took what was purest as the inner section, what was half fine and half coarse as the outer, and what should have gone but was kept for the time being as the miscellaneous. Liu Zezhang of Xinyu received the text from Wu and annotated it, though whether the annotation printed here is his cannot be determined. The Song bibliography called it the burial book, and later admirers renamed it the burial classic, a change the editors decline to follow."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because every node in this file that cites the burial book cites a text of that history, and because the ordering is not a scholarly aside but the thing being cited. The material a reader meets as one doctrine was selected twice, by two named men four generations apart, on the criterion of purity; what was cut is not preserved, so the graph cannot say what the transmission said before the cuts."@en,
        "The miscellaneous section carries the outcome sentences and the six adversities, that is to say the two bodies of material this file draws most heavily on. In Wu Cheng's own arrangement that is the section he judged coarse, kept provisionally and would have removed. The citations name the section for that reason."@en ;
    pm:compiledBy "Cai Yuanding, who reduced twenty pian to eight; Wu Cheng, who divided the remainder into inner, outer and miscellaneous sections; and the editors of the Siku quanshu, who report both and settle the title" ;
    pm:ordersClaim pmc:HuoBuXuanri,
        pmc:SanjiLiuxiong,
        pmc:WuBukezang,
        pmc:XingshiJixiong .

pmc:ZangshuZhuDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Der mitgedruckte Kommentar widerspricht den Ausgängen des Buches selbst"@de,
        "The commentary printed with the book contradicts the book's own outcomes"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zangshu, miscellaneous section, in the recension of Wu Cheng, one juan, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text",
        "Zangshu, the annotation printed beneath the miscellaneous and inner sections in the Wenyuange Siku quanshu text of the Wu Cheng recension" ;
    skos:definition "The annotation printed beneath the text in this edition does not defend the outcome sentences; at several of them it names cases going the other way and tells the reader not to follow the book. Where the text says that a form like a crosswise stool cuts off the sons and kills the grandsons, the note answers that where the true stool form is obtained it is a transformation of water and produces literary distinction and success in the examinations, and names three families whose ancestral graves were of that form: the forebear of a chief councillor Lu, of the prodigy Yang, and of a eunuch Fang. Where the text says that a form like a lying sword brings execution and extermination, the note answers that there are sword forms that have produced the eminent, and names one. Where the text places the flying serpent among the inauspicious, the note asks how many sites of serpent form have been used from antiquity to now, and whether they can all be set aside as dangerous. The same hand qualifies four of the five prohibited mountains in the same way, each time with the formula that there is a further kind which is not to be rejected on that ground."@en ;
    skos:note "The form of the qualification is the part worth having. The note never denies the rule; it denies that the case at hand was an instance of it, on the ground that the form was only apparently that form. That move preserves every sentence of the text against every counter-example, and it is the same machinery this file already records at the six adversities, one level up: there the failures of a correct burial are absorbed, here the successes of an incorrect one."@en,
        "This is a dispute inside one book and one printing, and the two sides are the text and its annotation. It is entered because the graph would otherwise carry the outcome sentences as the position of this transmission, when the transmission as it is actually handed on carries the sentences and their systematic qualification on the same page."@en,
        "Who wrote the annotation is not established, and the edition itself says so. The prefatory summary of the imperial collection states that Cai Yuanding cut the twenty pian to eight, that Wu Cheng afterwards divided what he had into the inner, outer and miscellaneous sections, that Liu Zezhang of Xinyu received the text from Wu and annotated it, and that whether the note in this printing is Liu's cannot be determined. The graph therefore has the counter-examples as an anonymous later hand and not as a named party."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:XingshiJixiong ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination .

pmc:ZhanbuDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob Stengel und Schale überhaupt antworten"@de,
        "Whether the stalks and the shell answer at all"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Wang Chong, Lunheng, the treatise on divining, juan 24, Sibu congkan facsimile",
        "Zhouyi, Xici A, the sections on prognostication and on what the Changes are without, in the Wang Bi and Han Kangbo recension; read in the digital text of the Kanseki Repository, which names no printing" ;
    skos:definition "A treatise of the first century sets itself against the claim that the Changes penetrate the grounds of everything under heaven. Its arguments, in the order it gives them: the names of the stalk and the shell mean the aged and the old, so that consulting them means consulting the aged, and neither is spiritlike; heaven, if it has no ears, is not a body but a vapour, and a vapour cannot tell anybody anything; the dead stalk and the dead shell are asked about the living; and, reading the very passage of the great expansion, the division into two images heaven and earth, the counting by fours images the seasons and the remainder images the intercalation, which is imaging by likeness in order to set up the number of the figures, and says nothing about heaven and earth answering a man. Its own account of the hits is coincidence: what the shell is said to meet and the stalks to encounter is what was met with, not what was brought about, and following the answer does not necessarily bring fortune nor going against it misfortune."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as a dispute and not as a refutation because that is what it is: two works, one asserting and one denying, both read at the wording in this run, neither carrying a case. The value of having it as a node is that the denying side reads the same passage the asserting side rests on, and reads it as a construction by analogy rather than as a channel."@en,
        "The strongest move in the denying work is not an argument but a condition, and it is held separately as a protocol in examinations/. It is not in this node because a dispute records two positions and a condition is a third kind of thing."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:JishuZhilai ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination .

pmc:ZhenrenZhirenShengrenXianren a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die vier gestuften Personen des ersten Kapitels"@de,
        "The four graded persons of the first chapter"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 1 (Shang gu tian zhen lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 1" ;
    skos:definition "Four grades, each with a stated reach and each ascribed to a conduct rather than to a birth. The true persons of high antiquity lifted and held heaven and earth, grasped the yin and the yang, breathed the essence-qi, stood alone and guarded the spirit, and their flesh was as one, so that their years wore out heaven and earth and had no ending time. Next the perfected persons, whose virtue was whole, who were in accord with yin and yang and in tune with the four seasons, who left the age and departed from custom, who accumulated the essence and made the spirit whole, who roamed between heaven and earth and whose seeing and hearing went beyond the eight reaches. Next the sages, who dwelt in the harmony of heaven and earth and followed the pattern of the eight winds, whose bodily form was not worn out and whose essence and spirit were not scattered, and who likewise could be counted in hundreds. Last the worthies, who took heaven and earth for their rule and modelled themselves on sun and moon, and who could lengthen their years but had a limit."@en ;
    skos:note "Nothing in the chapter says that the two upper grades were observed, and nothing says how one would tell a perfected person from a worthy. The grades are entered as what the corpus claims and not as a scale the graph can place anyone on."@en,
        "The two upper grades carry the claims that make this a capacity list rather than a moral one: a lifespan coextensive with heaven and earth and without end, and a seeing and hearing that reaches beyond the eight quarters. The two lower grades carry no such claim and are bounded in the text itself, the third at a count in hundreds and the fourth explicitly at a limit."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmc:ZhenxiaReHan a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wärme und Kälte unter der Nadel"@de,
        "Heat and cold under the needle"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 54 (Zhen jie pian), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 14" ;
    skos:definition "A second sign, stated in the chapter that explains the nine needles: where emptiness is to be filled, it is warm under the needle, for the qi becomes full and then it is warm; where fullness is to be drained, it is cold under the needle, for the qi becomes empty and then it is cold. The same chapter makes the warmth the criterion for withdrawal, saying that in needling an emptiness one waits for the fullness, and that when the yang qi has arrived in force and it is warm under the needle, then the needle is taken out."@en ;
    skos:note "Held apart from the vessel criterion above, and the difference is the point. This one is felt by the operator at the needle and is not stated to be checkable by anybody else; the other is a rule over a finding that at least two people can take. A tradition that carries both is a tradition that has not settled which of them decides, and the file does not settle it either."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmc:Zhenzangmai a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Pulse der bloßen Depots und die gestuften Fristen"@de,
        "The bare depot pulses and the graded terms"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 19 (Yu ji zhen zang lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 6" ;
    skos:definition "Five pulses named as the depot appearing bare, each given as a thing felt under the finger with a colour beside it. The liver: taut inside and out, as though running along a knife's edge, as though pressing the string of a zither; blue and white without lustre, the hair breaking, then death. The heart: hard and beating, as though running along strung job's-tears seeds; red and black without lustre. The lung: large and empty, as a feather brushing the skin; white and red without lustre. The kidney: beating and cut off, as a finger flicking a stone; black and yellow without lustre. The spleen: weak and now quick now sparse; yellow and green without lustre. Of all five the chapter says that where the bare depot pulses appear, all die and are not to be treated. Before them it grades the whole case by term: the great bones withered, the great flesh sunken, the chest full and the breathing not free, and the qi moving the body, six months; with inward pain drawing to shoulder and neck, one month; with a hot body, the flesh gone and the muscle bulk broken, within ten months; with the marrow of the shoulder melting away and movement failing further, one year; and with the eye sockets sunken and the patient not seeing people, immediate death, while one who does see people dies at the time of the phase that overcomes him. Where the bare depot appears, the chapter says, the day may then be given."@en ;
    skos:note "The clause about giving the day is what makes this a prognostic system and not a list of grave signs. The interval comes from the general state and the bare depot pulse is what licenses naming a date, so the two findings do different work and the chapter keeps them apart."@en,
        "The last of the graded cases carries its own escape. One who does not see people dies at once; one who does see people dies at the time of the overcoming phase, which is a term the chapter does not bound. A protocol would have to fix that before anything here could fail."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmc:ZhuyouGujinYi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sie wirkte im Altertum und wirkt jetzt nicht mehr"@de,
        "It worked in antiquity and does not work now"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 13 (Yi jing bian qi lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 4" ;
    skos:definition "The chapter opens by asking why the ancients treated illness by shifting the essence and transforming the qi, so that the incantation after the cause sufficed, while now drugs are used within and needle and stone without, and some are cured and some are not. The answer is a difference of age and not of technique. In high antiquity men lived among the birds and beasts, moved to keep off the cold and dwelt in the shade to keep off the heat, had within them no burden of longing and without them no obligation of office; in that untroubled age the evil could not enter deeply, so drugs could not treat within and needle and stone could not treat without, and shifting the essence and the incantation were enough. The present age is not so: cares run within, the toil of the body injures without, the compliance with the four seasons is lost and the fitness of cold and heat gone against, the thieving wind comes often and the empty evil morning and evening, reaching within to the depots and the marrow and injuring without the orifices and the flesh, so that a small illness must become grave and a great one must kill. Therefore, the chapter concludes, the incantation can no longer bring anything to an end."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also a transmission reporting a decline of a capacity from inside, which the graph carries in other traditions as a claim about lineages and initiations. Here the cause named is not a broken lineage but a changed way of living, which is at least the kind of antecedent a later reader could dispute with evidence."@en,
        "The form of this claim is what the graph should hold it for. It neither asserts nor denies that the practice works; it dates the end of its working and gives a reason lying outside the practice, in the condition of the age. A statement of that shape covers both outcomes in advance: where the incantation fails it is confirmed, and where it succeeds the age was not yet so far gone."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmc:ZiqelTeachesSignsOfShootingStars a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Ziqel offenbart die Zeichen der Sternschnuppen"@de,
        "Ziqel discloses the signs of the shooting stars"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)" ;
    skos:note "Stands in the position the Ethiopic fills with Ezeqeel and the knowledge of the clouds."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:SignsOfShootingStars ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Ziqel ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:ZoroastrianReturnPresupposesWatch a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Rückkehr setzt die ununterbrochene Wache über dem Körper voraus"@de,
        "The return presupposes the unbroken watch over the body"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The Book of Arda Viraf, ed. and trans. Martin Haug, E. W. West and Hoshang Jamaspji Asa (Bombay and London: Trübner, 1872), 2.18-33 and 3.2" ;
    skos:note "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity, and the difference is the finding rather than a formality. The transmission prescribes the watch in full detail and never says what would follow if it lapsed. There is no sentence anywhere in the chapters read about what happens if the traveller does not return, none about a condition under which the journey fails, and none about a body left unwatched. The risk is conceded in practice, by the testament made before drinking and by the undertaking given to the sisters, and it is not conceded in doctrine. A procedure this exact about what to do, and this silent about what goes wrong, is the shape a claim takes when nobody has been in a position to report a failure."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:WirazReturn ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:WirazWatch ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Zoroastrian .

pmc:ZosimosAraQuindecimGraduum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Altar in Bechergestalt, mit fünfzehn Stufen"@de,
        "The altar in the form of a cup, with fifteen steps"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zosime, die Vision vom Altar, § 2, französische Übersetzung in M. Berthelot und Ch.-Em. Ruelle, Collection des anciens alchimistes grecs, seconde livraison, Paris 1888, S. 111" ;
    skos:definition "The opening of the first lesson. Having said these things, the writer falls asleep and sees a sacrificer standing before him at the top of an altar in the form of a cup; the altar has fifteen steps to climb; the priest stands upon it, and a voice from above says that it has accomplished the action of descending the fifteen steps, walking towards the darkness, and the action of climbing the steps, going towards the light, that it is the sacrificer who renews it by casting off the thick nature of the body, and that, consecrated priest thus by necessity, it becomes a spirit."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as what the source says and not as what it is said to accomplish. The whole of it is spoken by a figure inside a reported dream, and the speaker is the one being operated on, not the operator. A pm:Yielding formed from this would credit the transmission with a claim it makes in the mouth of a dream."@en,
        "The number is fifteen in both directions and the two directions are named as two actions, one towards darkness and one towards light. Whatever else the passage is, it is a count with a stated sense: the descent is not the failure of the ascent but its first half."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Cheirokmeta ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GraecoEgyptianAlchemy .

pmc:ZosimosDisciplinaYieldsTincturas a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Haltung am Herd bringt die natürlichen Tinkturen hervor"@de,
        "The conduct at the hearth yields the natural tinctures"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zosime, Compte final, § 8, französische Übersetzung in M. Berthelot und Ch.-Em. Ruelle, Collection des anciens alchimistes grecs, seconde livraison, Paris 1888, S. 192-193" ;
    skos:note "The claim is the reverse of the circular ones this holding usually has to refuse. It does not say that working on matter produces a state of soul; it says that a state of soul produces a working on matter, and it names the product in the terms of the craft. What would tell against it is a tincture obtained without the conduct, and the letter says nothing about that case."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ZosimosSedeAdFocum ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LogosTeleutaios ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GraecoEgyptianAlchemy ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ZosimosTincturaeNaturales .

pmc:ZosimosIonDilaniatus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Priester Ion, zergliedert nach den Regeln der Zusammensetzung"@de,
        "Ion the priest, dismembered according to the rules of the combination"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zosime, die Vision vom Altar, § 3, französische Übersetzung in M. Berthelot und Ch.-Em. Ruelle, Collection des anciens alchimistes grecs, seconde livraison, Paris 1888, S. 111" ;
    skos:definition "What the figure on the altar answers when asked who he is. He is Ion, the priest of the sanctuaries, and he suffers an intolerable violence: someone came in the morning in haste and did violence to him, running him through with a sword and dismembering him according to the rules of the combination; he took the whole skin from his head with the sword he held; he mixed the bones with the flesh and burned them with the fire of the treatment; and it is thus, by the transformation of the body, that he learned to become a spirit. As he goes on speaking and is pressed to speak further, his eyes become like blood and he vomits all his flesh; and the dreamer sees him changed into a small misshapen man, tearing himself with his own teeth, and sinking down."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the passage on which the reputation of the corpus rests, and the corpus does nothing with it: nothing in what was read attaches a procedure, a condition or a sign to it. It is a report of a dream inside a treatise on tinctures."@en,
        "Two things in the wording are worth keeping apart from the famous image. The dismemberment is said to follow rules — the rules of the combination — so the violence is presented as an operation and not as an assault. And the collapse comes after the dreamer forces him to keep speaking, which puts the undoing of the figure on the questioner and not on the treatment."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Cheirokmeta ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GraecoEgyptianAlchemy .

pmc:ZosimosPraesuppositaDuoGradus a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Versuch des Werkes setzt die Kenntnis der zwei Stufen voraus"@de,
        "Attempting the work presupposes knowing the two grades"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zosime, französische Übersetzung in M. Berthelot und Ch.-Em. Ruelle, Collection des anciens alchimistes grecs, seconde livraison, Paris 1888, S. 172 und 193" ;
    skos:definition "The order the letter sets: if you know the two grades, do not fear to try, carrying out in detail all the things I have set out to you. The knowing is put before the attempting and is put as its condition, in a conditional clause and not as a warning."@en ;
    skos:note "Set at prescribed order and not at stated necessity, because no consequence of proceeding without the knowledge is given anywhere in what was read. The strength is the honest one: an instruction stands there, and a reader who took it for a necessity would be supplying the missing half himself."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ZosimosOpusTemptare ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Cheirokmeta ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ZosimosDuoGradusTincturarum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GraecoEgyptianAlchemy .

pmc:ZosimosSacerdotesNaturaliaOccultaverunt a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sie verbargen die natürlichen Verfahren, weil sie von den Opfern lebten"@de,
        "They hid the natural procedures because they lived on the sacrifices"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zosime, Compte final, § 7, französische Übersetzung in M. Berthelot und Ch.-Em. Ruelle, Collection des anciens alchimistes grecs, seconde livraison, Paris 1888, S. 192" ;
    skos:definition "And the letter gives the ground: it was not only out of jealousy against us, but because they were anxious for their existence and did not want to expose themselves to being beaten with rods, driven out, and dying of hunger, once they ceased to receive the offerings of the sacrifices. They hid the natural procedures and put forward their own, which were of the supernatural order; they represented to their priests that the common people would neglect the sacrifices if they no longer had recourse to the supernatural procedures; and thus, by the effect of custom, of law and of fear, their sacrifices were well attended. They no longer even carried out their lying announcements. When their sanctuaries came to be deserted and their sacrifices neglected, they still got the men who had stayed with them to give themselves to the sacrifices, flattering them with dreams and other deceits and with certain counsels. And the letter closes the passage on the addressee: they want to win you to their cause too, through their false prophet; they flatter you; being starved, they covet not only the sacrifices but your soul."@en,
        "The account the letter gives of why the working procedures of the art are not available. Those who brought the colours fabricated by supernatural means, being set aside, counselled the men of standing to act against all of us, the learned, who operate by natural actions; they did not want to be set aside by men, but to be entreated and adjured to yield what they had made, in return for offerings and sacrifices. They therefore kept hidden all the natural procedures, those which give the results without artifice."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as one side. The priests are not heard here, and the letter is the only witness read; what it reports about their motives is its claim and not a finding of this holding. What is not in question is that the distinction between natural and supernatural procedures is the letter's own, and that it is the distinction on which the whole accusation rests."@en,
        "This is an internal critic of an operative tradition, and what he locates the withholding in is neither ignorance nor a rule of silence but an income. The procedures that work are hidden because the ones that do not work are what the sacrifices are paid for. A transmission that says this about its own custodians is saying that the unavailability of its working part is a fact about who lives by it."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LogosTeleutaios ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GraecoEgyptianAlchemy .

pmc:ZosimosTemporaOperationum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Fristen, die der Korpus zählt, in Stunden und in Tagen"@de,
        "The intervals the corpus counts, in hours and in days"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zosime, in M. Berthelot und Ch.-Em. Ruelle, Collection des anciens alchimistes grecs, seconde livraison, Paris 1888, französische Übersetzung, S. 114, 132, 143, 166 und 196" ;
    skos:definition "The counted intervals read in the translation of the Zosimos pieces: taking the pearl, put it to decoction with oil in an unstopped vessel, without a lid, for three hours, over a moderate fire; leave the whole for three times twenty-four hours, until the product is cooked; cook until the colour is spread through the whole mass, some for nine hours, others for two days; the time of the cooking for all products, notably when one operates on plates, is not less than nine hours; and when you want to operate so as to bring the product to yellow, leave it in the furnace for six hours."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted, stated as a minimum in one case, and nowhere attached to a sign. The corpus says how long and does not say what is to be seen when the time is up; the two are different kinds of statement and this transmission makes only the first. Where the interval is given as a lower bound — not less than nine hours — nothing is said about the upper. The search for a sign is at pmp:ZosimosSignumSearch."@en,
        "The edition itself records that one of these intervals is transmitted in more than one figure: at the place that gives nine hours or two days it notes a witness reading one day and a night and another reading twelve hours. A number that the manuscripts give three ways is a number about which the corpus has no single claim, and it is entered here with that said."@en,
        "The piece numbers of these pages were not established, and the node therefore cites the pages of the edition alone. That is enough to find the places and not enough to say which treatise carries them; a later run that settles the numbering should carry it back here."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Cheirokmeta ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GraecoEgyptianAlchemy .

pmc:ZuniEfficacyPresupposesExactness a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das öffentlich gesprochene Gebet setzt buchstabengetreue Wiedergabe voraus"@de,
        "The public prayer presupposes letter-perfect delivery"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ruth L. Bunzel, Zuni Ritual Poetry, in Forty-Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of American Ethnology 1929-1930 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1932), p. 616" ;
    skos:note "Entered one step below the Māori and Navajo parallels and for a reason that is a property of the passage and not of the tradition: the requirement is stated, the consequence of failing it is not. Raising this to stated necessity would need a place in the corpus that says what a misrecited prayer does, and the body of the collection was not read. See the convergence node and prima-materia#112."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ZuniPrayerEfficacy ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ZuniPrayerExactness ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ZuniRitual .

pmc:ZuniPrayerPossession a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Gebet als Besitz"@de,
        "The prayer as a possession"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ruth L. Bunzel, Zuni Ritual Poetry, in Forty-Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of American Ethnology 1929-1930 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1932), p. 615" ;
    skos:definition "Prayers held to carry power in themselves, so that having them is a source of power and losing or impairing them is a great danger."@en ;
    skos:note "The nearest thing the passages read give to a stated consequence of getting a prayer wrong, and deliberately not entered as one: the sentence speaks of loss and impairment of the prayer as a possession, not of the effect of a misrecited performance."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ZuniRitual .

pmc:ZuyuaExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Zuyua-Verhör als Abstammungsprobe"@de,
        "The Zuyua interrogation as a test of descent"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ralph L. Roys, The Book of Chilam Balam of Chumayel (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1967), chapter IX, pp. 89-98" ;
    skos:definition "A protocol the tradition states for itself. The claim is that the questions separate men of the ruling lineage from men who are not, and the tradition acts on the claim to the point of execution. The outcome is binary and is applied on the day."@en ;
    skos:note "A route by which a European examination genre could have reached this material exists and is documented: a Yucatec translation of the Spanish question-and-answer book of the emperor Adrian and the boy Epitus stands in a colonial Yucatec manuscript. Nobody has set the Zuyua catalogue against that literature. See prima-materia#460."@en,
        "What the protocol cannot survive is the document that preserves it. The manuscript that reports the examination also prints the answers, so from the moment of that writing the procedure separates those who have read this book from those who have not, and no longer those of the lineage from the rest. A test whose key is published measures access to the key. This is a property of the record and not a modern objection to the tradition, and it is the reason the state stands at procedure without cases rather than higher."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, protocol stated from the witness; no case series has been collected" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A recorded case in which a man of the ruling lineage failed the questions, or in which a man not of that lineage answered them. The tradition claims the questions decide descent, so a single clean case on either side falls due against it." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:ZuyuaNaat .

pmc:ZuyuaThanAnchoring a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Das Zuyua-Verhör findet im gewöhnlichen Wachen statt"@de,
        "The Zuyua interrogation is carried out in ordinary waking"@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:outwardWaking ;
    pm:situatedNode pmp:ZuyuaThan ;
    pm:situationGround "The candidate walks in, is spoken to by the first head-chief, fetches the objects demanded and hands them over, and the outcome is executed on the spot. Nothing in the chapter withdraws the senses, alters the state of either party, or places the exchange anywhere but in the public day." .

<https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology> a owl:Ontology ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/> ;
    dcterms:title "prima-materia core ontology"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Consciousness-first meta-ontology. Processes, not substances."@en .

pm:Manifesting a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Manifestieren"@de,
        "Manifesting"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "The emergence of a form out of a prior undifferentiated ground."@en .

pm:Perceiving a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Wahrnehmen"@de,
        "Perceiving"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "The act of apprehending form within an awareness context."@en .

pm:Relating a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Beziehen"@de,
        "Relating"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "The act of placing one concept in relation to another."@en .

pm:acquisitionDeclined a pm:AwarenessValue,
        pm:StateAcquisition ;
    rdfs:label "Erwerbsunterscheidung abgelehnt"@de,
        "Acquisition distinction declined"@en ;
    skos:definition "The tradition holds that the distinction between what is worked for and what is given does not apply to what it teaches."@en .

pm:acquisitionNotStated a pm:AwarenessValue,
        pm:StateAcquisition ;
    rdfs:label "Erwerb nicht angegeben"@de,
        "Acquisition not stated"@en ;
    skos:definition "The source was read for this question and does not answer it."@en .

pm:addedElement a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "hinzugefügtes Element"@de,
        "added element"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Reworking ;
    skos:definition "The node carrying what this station added and the exemplar does not have. Required: an addition that cannot be pointed at is an assertion about a chain and not a link in it."@en .

pm:agencyNotStated a pm:AgencyHolding,
        pm:AwarenessValue ;
    rdfs:label "Trägerschaft nicht angegeben"@de,
        "Agency not stated"@en ;
    skos:definition "The source was read for this question and does not answer it."@en .

pm:analogueConcept a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "analogue concept"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:range pm:Conceptualizing ;
    skos:definition "A cross-tradition structural analogue, weaker than owl:sameAs and distinct from skos:closeMatch in that it asserts role-in-process similarity rather than lexical overlap."@en .

pm:anchoringDeclined a pm:AwarenessValue,
        pm:SensoryAnchoring ;
    rdfs:label "Sinnesbindung als Unterscheidung abgelehnt"@de,
        "Anchoring distinction declined"@en ;
    skos:definition "The tradition holds that the distinction this axis draws does not apply to what it teaches: nothing is to be withdrawn from and no state is to be entered, because whatever arises is taken as the way."@en ;
    skos:note "A positive claim about a tradition and not a gap in the record. It needs a passage like any other claim; the same shape as pm:normDistinctionDeclined and pm:selfBoundaryDeclined on the two orientation axes."@en .

pm:ascribedCapacity a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "zugeschriebenes Vermögen"@de,
        "ascribed capacity"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:range pm:Conceptualizing ;
    skos:definition "The capacity that an attribution ascribes."@en .

pm:ascribedTo a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "zugeschrieben an"@de,
        "ascribed to"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Attributing ;
    skos:definition "The bearer to whom the capacity is ascribed: a named figure, a class of beings, humanity before a stated event."@en .

pm:attestedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "bezeugt durch"@de,
        "attested by"@en ;
    rdfs:range pm:Attesting ;
    skos:definition "Declares by which route a claim entered the record. Mandatory on every attribution, so that non-evidence cannot be forgotten."@en .

pm:axisValue a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Achsenwert"@de,
        "axis value"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:range pm:AwarenessValue ;
    skos:definition "The coordinate the node is placed at. One value per node; a claim placed on two axes is two placements, and a claim placed at two values on one axis is either two claims or a dispute."@en .

pm:baseRate a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Basisrate"@de,
        "base rate"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Testing ;
    skos:definition "The rate against which observed outcomes are compared, with the population it was taken from."@en .

pm:byPractice a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "durch Praxis"@de,
        "by practice"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:range pm:Practicing ;
    skos:definition "The practice a yielding claim proceeds from."@en .

pm:caseCount a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Fallzahl"@de,
        "case count"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    skos:definition "The number of cases recorded under this protocol so far."@en .

pm:cautionsAbout a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "warnt vor"@de,
        "cautions about"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Cautioning ;
    skos:definition "The capacity, practice or pursuit a warning is directed at."@en .

pm:circulatesAs a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "zirkuliert als"@de,
        "circulates as"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Originating ;
    skos:definition "The ascription under which the claim is commonly reported: the tradition, the age or the authority it is credited to and does not have."@en .

pm:claimedSpeaker a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "behaupteter Sprecher"@de,
        "claimed speaker"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Receiving ;
    skos:definition "The speaker the work claims for the wording, given as the work gives it. A literal and not a link to a named being: what is recorded is the claim, and typing it as a bearer would grant the claim the thing it is asserting."@en .

pm:compiledBy a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "geordnet von"@de,
        "compiled by"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Systematizing ;
    skos:definition "The named compiler who performed the ordering act. Without it, an order made in the present is indistinguishable from an order found in the source."@en .

pm:conformingMethod a pm:MethodOrientation ;
    rdfs:label "Ordnungstreue Methode"@de,
        "Conforming method"@en ;
    skos:definition "The operative means keep the norm of the order the path is transmitted in: what is prohibited there stays prohibited here."@en .

pm:consequenceOfSkipping a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Folge des Überspringens"@de,
        "consequence of skipping"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Presupposing ;
    skos:definition "What the source says happens if the prior step is passed over."@en ;
    skos:note "Required wherever the strength is pm:statedNecessity. Claiming that a text sets a necessity obliges the claimant to say what the text says goes wrong without it; a necessity nobody can quote a consequence for is an order read as a necessity."@en .

pm:contactRoute a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "contact route"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:range pm:ContactRoute ;
    skos:definition "The state of the contact question between this tradition and those the graph already holds."@en .

pm:convergingConcept a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "konvergierender Begriff"@de,
        "converging concept"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Converging ;
    rdfs:range pm:Conceptualizing ;
    skos:definition "A concept that participates in the observed correspondence."@en .

pm:corpusWork a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Korpuswerk"@de,
        "corpus work"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:range dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    skos:definition "A work in which this transmission is chiefly carried, as an identifier. The identifier counterpart of pm:principalCorpus, which stays as the readable listing."@en ;
    skos:note "Says nothing about whether the work was read. That follows from whether any node points at the work with pm:fromWork."@en .

pm:counterSearch a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Gegensuche"@de,
        "counter-search"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:range pm:CounterSearchState ;
    skos:definition "How far the search for work opposing the cited evidence has been carried."@en ;
    skos:note "Required wherever pm:evidenceFrom is set, enforced by pm:CounterSearchRequiredShape. A protocol that records no state here is not neutral about the question; it has not asked it."@en .

pm:counterSearchNote a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Notiz zur Gegensuche"@de,
        "counter-search note"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Testing ;
    skos:definition "What was searched for and what came back, stated so that the search itself can be judged."@en ;
    skos:note "Required wherever pm:counterSearch is set, enforced by pm:CounterSearchNoteRequiredShape. Without it, pm:counterSearchFoundNothing is indistinguishable from a search nobody ran, and it is the state a hasty protocol would reach for."@en .

pm:coverageState a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "coverage state"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:range pm:CoverageState ;
    skos:definition "How far the corpus of this tradition has been carried into the graph."@en .

pm:dependentStep a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "abhängiger Schritt"@de,
        "dependent step"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Presupposing ;
    skos:definition "The later step: the practice, state or capacity said not to be reached without the prior one."@en .

pm:disputedClaim a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "strittiger Gegenstand"@de,
        "disputed claim"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Disputing ;
    skos:definition "What the disagreement is about."@en .

pm:durationDeclined a pm:AwarenessValue,
        pm:StateDuration ;
    rdfs:label "Dauerunterscheidung abgelehnt"@de,
        "Duration distinction declined"@en ;
    skos:definition "The tradition holds that the distinction between a passing and an abiding condition does not apply to what it teaches."@en .

pm:evidenceFrom a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Evidenz aus"@de,
        "evidence from"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Testing ;
    skos:definition "A modern work bearing on the claim under examination, given as a bibliographic reference or a work identifier."@en ;
    skos:note "Deliberately not dcterms:source. A modern study is never a witness to a tradition and must never appear as the source of one of its claims; it bears on a claim only as evidence inside an examination. Keeping the two predicates apart is what allows research to enter the record at all without loosening the rule that sources are received works."@en .

pm:examinationState a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Prüfstand"@de,
        "examination state"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:range pm:ExaminationState ;
    skos:definition "How far the attempt to fail the claim has been carried."@en .

pm:examinedBy a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "geprüft von"@de,
        "examined by"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Testing ;
    skos:definition "Who devised or carried the protocol. A protocol without a named author is unaccountable, and an unaccountable protocol is not evidence of anything."@en .

pm:falsifiedBy a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "widerlegt durch"@de,
        "falsified by"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Testing ;
    skos:definition "The stated condition under which the claim counts as refuted."@en .

pm:fieldRecord a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Aufnahmelage"@de,
        "field record"@en ;
    skos:definition "How the cited passage was taken down: who spoke, or that the speaker is expressly withheld; who recorded; when and where; in which language; and whether the wording in the language of the telling is carried in the work or only a translation."@en ;
    skos:note "It names the gathering of this passage, not the standing of the corpus. The situation of a whole ethnographic tradition — span of the fieldwork against the year of publication, how many informants, whether the same people were revisited, whether material was delivered between visits, whether the recorder stood in a milieu already expecting what he found — belongs as a skos:note on the pm:Tradition instance. See prima-materia#260. The two are needed together: one says how far this sentence stands from its speaker, the other how far the corpus stands from the transmission."@en,
        "Required wherever pm:attestedBy is pm:fieldAttestation, enforced by pm:FieldAttestationShape. Prose and not a scale, for the reason pm:independenceGround is prose: there is no threshold at which a gathering becomes sound, and a scale would counterfeit a check. What the sentence is worth is decided by whoever reads it."@en .

pm:fromWork a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "aus Werk"@de,
        "from work"@en ;
    rdfs:range dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    skos:definition "The work that a dcterms:source of this node lies in. A node whose sources lie in two works carries both."@en ;
    skos:note "It does not replace dcterms:source. The source keeps the passage and the edition; this carries only the identity of the work, which a literal cannot carry without a string comparison."@en .

pm:generalizedFrom a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "verallgemeinert aus"@de,
        "generalized from"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Generalizing ;
    skos:definition "An attested claim the generalization was formed from. At least two are required, and each must itself name a work."@en ;
    skos:note "Two is a floor and not a target. How many attested claims a generalization rests on, and how far apart their traditions stand, are the numbers that make one worth examining before another — the same distinction pm:Converging draws between many witnesses on one cable and many on three."@en .

pm:generalizedStatement a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "verallgemeinerte Aussage"@de,
        "generalized statement"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Generalizing ;
    skos:definition "The claim itself, stated in the abstract and worded so that a result could contradict it."@en ;
    skos:note "A wording that covers an outcome and its opposite alike is the failure pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate records after the fact. Here the cost is lower: it can be caught while the sentence is being written."@en .

pm:hasSymbolicForm a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has symbolic form"@en ;
    rdfs:range pm:Symbolizing ;
    skos:definition "Associates a concept with one or more symbolic representations (sigils, diagrams, glyphs)."@en .

pm:independenceGround a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Grund der Unabhängigkeit"@de,
        "ground of independence"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Converging ;
    skos:definition "The statement that carries a claim of independent attestation: when the testimony relied on was fixed or gathered, and against which publication or route that date stands."@en ;
    skos:note "Required wherever pm:independentAttestation is set, enforced by pm:IndependenceGroundShape. The burden is reversed on purpose. Feedback from a published account back into the field it describes can almost never be demonstrated, so a predicate that marked only the demonstrated cases would let the ordinary case pass in silence — the failure pm:CoverageState and pm:ExaminationState exist to prevent. A claim of independence must instead carry the fact that makes it checkable."@en,
        "The date alone does not settle it, and the sentence must say more where more is at stake. Feedback within a single enquiry — few informants, several years, the same team returning, material gathered by an informant between visits — precedes any publication and no date catches it. Where the corpus was fixed that way, the ground states the gathering situation as well. Whether it does so honestly is settled at the merge and not by a shape; naming that limit is better than a guard that pretends to check it."@en .

pm:independentAttestation a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "unabhängige Bezeugung"@de,
        "independent attestation"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Converging ;
    rdfs:range pm:Tradition ;
    skos:definition "A tradition attesting the correspondence for which no route of contact is documented, so that its testimony is not the same statement counted a second time."@en ;
    skos:note "The earlier definition called this the only kind of attestation that makes a correspondence a finding. It is not: it is the kind that makes the count honest. A correspondence carried by reception is still a finding about what was carried; what it may not be is a tally of independent witnesses."@en .

pm:locusDeclined a pm:AwarenessLocus,
        pm:AwarenessValue ;
    rdfs:label "Ortsunterscheidung abgelehnt"@de,
        "Locus distinction declined"@en ;
    skos:definition "The tradition holds that the distinction between an awareness in the body and an awareness apart from it does not apply, because it does not grant the localisation the axis presupposes."@en .

pm:methodNotStated a pm:MethodOrientation ;
    rdfs:label "Ausrichtung der Methode nicht angegeben"@de,
        "Method orientation not stated"@en ;
    skos:definition "The source was read for this question and does not answer it: it prescribes its means without placing them for or against the norm of the order it stands in."@en ;
    skos:note "The counterpart of pm:normDistinctionDeclined from the other side, and required on every subject axis by SPEC §17. Without it a source that was asked and stayed silent is indistinguishable from a node nobody has reached, and any query of the form 'only conforming methods' reports the state of our reading instead of the state of the traditions. Silence is common here and is worth recording: a great many operative texts simply do not raise the question."@en .

pm:methodOrientation a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Ausrichtung der Methode"@de,
        "method orientation"@en ;
    rdfs:range pm:MethodOrientation ;
    skos:definition "Places a tradition, a practice or a claim on the axis of method. Asserting it is a claim about a source and carries the same burden as any other: a passage, an edition, and the counter-search."@en ;
    skos:note "No domain is declared, deliberately. The axis is stated of whatever a source states it of — a transmission as a whole, a single practice, a stage within one — and a declared domain would force the level before the material has settled it."@en .

pm:nameForm a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Namensform"@de,
        "name form"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Naming ;
    skos:definition "The name as it stands in the edition named by dcterms:source, in that edition's transliteration."@en ;
    skos:note "Further forms of the same bearer go in skos:altLabel, so that a reader who met the name elsewhere finds the node. The form that decides identity is the one here."@en,
        "The edition is not decoration here. A name is carried by its letters, and transliteration conventions differ enough that Šemiḥazah and Semjaza are the same bearer under two scholarly hands. Recording the form without the edition it comes from would make a later comparison unfalsifiable."@en .

pm:nameRole a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Rolle des Benannten"@de,
        "name role"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Naming ;
    skos:definition "What the naming tradition takes the bearer to be, in that tradition's own term: watcher, angel, demon, daimon, god, spirit, ancestor."@en ;
    skos:note "Deliberately a literal and not a controlled scale. A fixed vocabulary would have to decide whether a Greek daimon is an angel or a demon, and that decision belongs to no tradition in the record: it is the harmonisation this project exists to avoid. Where two traditions call a shared name differently, the difference is the finding, and a scale would have deleted it."@en .

pm:normDistinctionDeclined a pm:MethodOrientation ;
    rdfs:label "Abgelehnte Normunterscheidung"@de,
        "Norm distinction declined"@en ;
    skos:definition "The source holds that the distinction does not apply to its path: nothing is kept and nothing is broken, because it denies that anything needs to be renounced, purified or transformed."@en ;
    skos:note "The value exists because a path that declines the axis is not the same as one whose position on it is unknown, and a scale without it would force the first to be recorded as the second. It is asserted only where a source says so."@en .

pm:objectlessDepth a pm:AwarenessValue,
        pm:SensoryAnchoring ;
    rdfs:label "Gegenstandslose Tiefe"@de,
        "Objectless depth"@en ;
    skos:definition "A condition in which no object at all is apprehended, whether reached in sleep or in a cessation described by a handbook of absorption."@en ;
    skos:note "The Māṇḍūkya calls this suṣupti. A source that has something exercised here is worth a second look before it is entered, because most traditions place their capacities short of it."@en .

pm:ordersClaim a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "ordnet Behauptung"@de,
        "orders claim"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Systematizing ;
    skos:definition "A claim that this ordering act groups. The claims keep their own attestation; only the grouping belongs to the compiler."@en .

pm:originatedBy a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "aufgestellt von"@de,
        "originated by"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Originating ;
    skos:definition "The named author with whom the claim begins."@en .

pm:positedIn a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "gesetzt in"@de,
        "posited in"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Positing ;
    skos:definition "Where the claim was put forward, given as an identifier rather than as a bibliographic reference: an issue number, a draft designation. Deliberately not dcterms:source, because what is named here is not a work a reader can obtain and must never be counted as one."@en .

pm:prerequisiteStrength a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Stärke der Voraussetzung"@de,
        "prerequisite strength"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:range pm:PrerequisiteStrength ;
    skos:definition "How firmly the source sets the prerequisite."@en .

pm:principalCorpus a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "principal corpus"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Tradition ;
    skos:definition "The works in which this transmission is chiefly carried, named so that a later reader can find them."@en ;
    skos:note "Deliberately not dcterms:source. A source carries a passage that was read, with its place and its edition; this names a corpus that was not read. Using dcterms:source here would let a registration claim a textual knowledge it does not have. The literature rule applies all the same: a work, never a URL."@en,
        "Kept as the readable listing once a tradition names its works with pm:corpusWork. The literal is what a reader sees; the identifiers are what a query can join."@en .

pm:priorStep a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "vorausgesetzter Schritt"@de,
        "prior step"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Presupposing ;
    skos:definition "The earlier step the later one is said to require."@en .

pm:protocolUpdated a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Protokollstand"@de,
        "protocol updated"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:range xsd:date ;
    skos:definition "The date on which the count was last carried forward. A protocol without this date claims a scrutiny it does not document."@en .

pm:readVia a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "gelesen über"@de,
        "read via"@en ;
    skos:definition "The work in which the passage was actually read, given as a bibliographic reference, where that work is not the one carrying the claim."@en ;
    skos:note "Deliberately separate from dcterms:source, for the same reason pm:evidenceFrom is. dcterms:source names what a reader must obtain to check the claim; this names what the writer of the node had in front of him. A record that fused them would report second-hand reading as first-hand and would lose the one distinction that makes an unreachable source admissible at all."@en,
        "Required wherever pm:attestedBy is pm:mediatedAttestation. Carries a bibliographic reference and never a URL, enforced by pm:ReadViaIsLiteratureShape."@en .

pm:receivedBy a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "empfangen durch"@de,
        "received by"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Receiving ;
    skos:definition "The person through whom the wording was received, named as the work names them."@en .

pm:receivedFrom a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "übernommen von"@de,
        "received from"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Reworking ;
    rdfs:range pm:Tradition ;
    skos:definition "The transmission the claim was taken over from. The work and the place stay in dcterms:source; this names the transmission, so that the depth of a chain can be counted."@en .

pm:receptionCircumstance a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Umstand des Empfangs"@de,
        "reception circumstance"@en ;
    skos:definition "The circumstances of the receiving: the state the receiver is said to have been in, who put the questions, who took the wording down, and whether a record of the proceeding itself exists beyond the edited text."@en ;
    skos:note "Required on every receiving, enforced by pm:ReceivingShape. Without it the node says only that a speaker is claimed, which a reader could have guessed from the title; with it the claim can be weighed against the ones this literature makes about itself."@en .

pm:reworkedClaim a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "bearbeitete Behauptung"@de,
        "reworked claim"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Reworking ;
    skos:definition "The claim that was taken over, as it stands in the tradition performing the reworking."@en .

pm:scaleFamily a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "scale family"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Scale ;
    rdfs:range pm:ScaleFamily ;
    skos:definition "The family a scale belongs to."@en .

pm:searchOutcome a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Ausgang der Suche"@de,
        "search outcome"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:range pm:SearchOutcome ;
    skos:definition "What the search returned."@en .

pm:searchTerms a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Suchbegriffe"@de,
        "search terms"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Searching ;
    skos:definition "The terms actually used, in the original language and in the transliterations tried. What decides whether a negative finding is worth anything: a search in one transliteration is a statement about that spelling."@en .

pm:searchYieldedNode a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Suche ergab Knoten"@de,
        "search yielded node"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Searching ;
    skos:definition "The node the found passage became. Required wherever the outcome is pm:searchFoundPlace, enforced by pm:SearchFoundPlaceRequiresNodeShape."@en ;
    skos:note "Its absence is what makes the class useful. The searches carrying no node are the gaps the holding knows about and has not filled, and they are addressable by anyone — which is a gate removed, in the sense of SPEC §0: a stranger can see where the work stopped and take it up."@en .

pm:searchedBy a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "gesucht von"@de,
        "searched by"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Searching ;
    skos:definition "Who carried the search. An unaccountable negative finding is worth nothing, for the same reason an unaccountable protocol is."@en .

pm:searchedCorpus a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "durchsuchter Korpus"@de,
        "searched corpus"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Searching ;
    skos:definition "The corpus that was searched, named as literature so that a later reader can repeat the search in the same place."@en .

pm:searchedFor a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "gesucht nach"@de,
        "searched for"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Searching ;
    skos:definition "The question that was put to the corpus, stated so that somebody else could put the same one."@en ;
    skos:note "A literal and not a reference, deliberately. The typical object of a fruitful search is a claim the holding does not contain, and a property pointing at a node would restrict the class to questions already answered."@en .

pm:selfBoundaryDeclined a pm:TelicOrientation ;
    rdfs:label "Abgelehnte Selbstgrenze"@de,
        "Self boundary declined"@en ;
    skos:definition "The source holds that the question is malformed for its path: there is no bounded self that could either be dissolved or preserved, so neither end state is what it aims at."@en ;
    skos:note "As with pm:normDistinctionDeclined, this is a positive claim about a source and not a place to put an undetermined case."@en,
        "The value was first written as the single place for everything that is neither dissolution nor separation, and the material has since shown that too coarse: a marriage is not a non-position, and a non-position is not a claim about which level the question belongs to. The two clearest of those have their own values below. What remains here is the plain form — the source denies that there is a bounded self to be dealt with either way — and the case where a source instead declares the whole level of the question superseded, which is a further distinction still to be made when a second instance of it appears."@en .

pm:separativeTelos a pm:TelicOrientation ;
    rdfs:label "Absonderndes Ziel"@de,
        "Separative telos"@en ;
    skos:definition "The path claims that the bounded self is to be strengthened and kept as its own absolute, and treats its dissolution as what is to be avoided."@en ;
    skos:note "Distinct from self-assertion, ambition or the pursuit of power, none of which say anything about an end state. What places a path here is a claim about where it ends, not about how its practitioners behave."@en .

pm:situatedNode a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "verorteter Knoten"@de,
        "situated node"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Situating ;
    skos:definition "The node being placed: a practice, a capacity claim, a concept or a prerequisite claim."@en .

pm:situationGround a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Verortungsgrund"@de,
        "situation ground"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Situating ;
    skos:definition "What in the source carries the placement, in the compiler's words: the condition the passage describes, and why it is read as this coordinate."@en ;
    skos:note "Required. Without it a placement is a bare assignment, and a later reader can neither check it nor contradict it — the same reason pm:counterSearchNote is required as soon as a counter-search state is set."@en .

pm:telicOrientation a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Ausrichtung des Zieles"@de,
        "telic orientation"@en ;
    rdfs:range pm:TelicOrientation ;
    skos:definition "Places a tradition, a practice or a claim on the axis of the end state. Asserting it is a claim about a source and carries the same burden as any other."@en ;
    skos:note "Where a source claims one end state for a stage and another for the whole, both are stated on their own nodes rather than averaged. An averaged orientation reports a path no source teaches, in the same way an averaged reading reports a text no witness gives."@en .

pm:telosNotStated a pm:TelicOrientation ;
    rdfs:label "Ziel nicht angegeben"@de,
        "Telic orientation not stated"@en ;
    skos:definition "The source was read for this question and does not answer it: it sets out a practice and its results without saying what becomes of the bounded self at the end."@en ;
    skos:note "Required on every subject axis by SPEC §17, and the case it covers is the ordinary one. A handbook of technique may name a dozen capacities and never state an end state at all; recording that as absence would put it in the same bucket as every text nobody has opened yet."@en .

pm:tests a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "prüft"@de,
        "tests"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Testing ;
    skos:definition "The claim this protocol attempts to fail."@en .

pm:transgressiveMethod a pm:MethodOrientation ;
    rdfs:label "Übertretende Methode"@de,
        "Transgressive method"@en ;
    skos:definition "The operative means take what the surrounding order prohibits and use the force of the prohibition itself as the vehicle. The transgression is prescribed, not incidental, and it is bounded by the rite that prescribes it."@en ;
    skos:note "Not the same as licence. A path is transgressive on this axis only where a source sets the breach as a means; a practice that merely fails to observe a norm is not oriented by it at all."@en .

pm:transmissionPath a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Übertragungsweg"@de,
        "transmission path"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Converging ;
    skos:definition "A documented route of historical contact between the traditions in which the correspondence appears."@en ;
    skos:note "The earlier definition here said that the presence of a route downgrades the correspondence from convergence to reception. That was a judgement wearing the clothes of a definition, and it is withdrawn. A route says how a claim travelled, not whether anything was in it. Survival through many hands is selection, because most claims do not survive it, and a record that treats transmission as noise throws away the only evidence it has about what kept being worth copying."@en,
        "What the route does decide is narrower and stays in force: an agreement reached along it may not be counted as independent attestation, because the same statement would be counted twice. Both may stand on one node, and on several they already do. The route bars a particular way of counting; it does not devalue the correspondence."@en .

pm:undividedOutcomes a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "nicht geschiedene Ausgänge"@de,
        "undivided outcomes"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Testing ;
    skos:definition "The differing outcomes that the claim treats alike, named so that the charge of saying nothing is itself checkable."@en ;
    skos:note "Required wherever the state is pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate, enforced by pm:UndividedOutcomesRequiredShape. The burden falls on the examiner for the same reason it does at pm:independenceGround: a verdict that a claim says nothing is easy to reach and hard to contest, and one that names no pair of outcomes it fails to separate is an opinion about the claim rather than a finding about it."@en .

pm:unitiveTelos a pm:TelicOrientation ;
    rdfs:label "Vereinigendes Ziel"@de,
        "Unitive telos"@en ;
    skos:definition "The path claims that the bounded self is dissolved into, or united with, something that exceeds it, and treats the persistence of the separate self as what is to be given up."@en .

pm:uses a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "verwendet"@de,
        "uses"@en ;
    rdfs:range skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A thing this process calls for by name: a substance, an instrument, or a fixed form of words. An identifier for joining, never a claim."@en ;
    skos:note "Its absence means nobody has entered the materials of that procedure, not that it calls for none. The same asymmetry pm:Testing and pm:Searching keep, and for the same reason: stubs would destroy it."@en,
        "Says nothing about whether the thing is necessary, whether it works, or what it does. Necessity is a pm:Presupposing node with its stated consequence; what the source says the thing is for stays in the definition of the process that calls for it."@en .

pm:withinTradition a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "within tradition"@en ;
    rdfs:range pm:Tradition ;
    skos:definition "Associates a process instance with the tradition in which it is situated."@en .

pm:yieldsCapacity a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "bringt Vermögen hervor"@de,
        "yields capacity"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:range pm:Conceptualizing ;
    skos:definition "The capacity a yielding claim arrives at. That the tradition says so is the claim; that the practice produces it is not asserted."@en .

pmp:AegyptiaAccipiterNovilunii a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Falke mit der weißen Krone und neun Göttern in Gelb auf neuer Tafel"@de,
        "The hawk with the white crown and nine gods in yellow on a new plaque"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 134, rubric, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: An English Translation of the Chapters, Hymns, etc., of the Theban Recension, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II" ;
    skos:definition "Recite over a hawk standing and having the white crown upon his head, and over figures of Tem, Shu, Tefnut, Seb, Nut, Osiris, Isis, Suti and Nephthys painted in yellow colour upon a new plaque, which shall be placed in a model of the boat of the sun along with a figure of the deceased. These shall be anointed with cedar oil, incense shall be offered to them on the fire, and feathered fowl shall be roasted."@en ;
    skos:note "One colour for nine gods, one oil, one burnt offering and one roast. The rubric closes by calling the whole an act of praise to Ra as he journeyeth, which is the only place in the rubrics read where the compiler names the genus of the act rather than its effect."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmp:AegyptiaAnimaAurea a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die goldene Seele mit Edelsteinen, auf der Brust"@de,
        "The soul of gold inlaid with precious stones, on the breast"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 89, rubric, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: An English Translation of the Chapters, Hymns, etc., of the Theban Recension, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II" ;
    skos:definition "Say these words over a soul of gold inlaid with precious stones and placed on the breast of Osiris."@en ;
    skos:note "The whole rubric, complete. One sentence, one object, one placement, no time, no outcome, no condition. It is the shortest execution rubric in the three volumes and shows what the form reduces to when nothing is promised."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmp:AegyptiaAnnulusAnkham a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Ring aus Anch-am-Blüten und der Ring im Byssus"@de,
        "The ring of ankham flowers and the ring in byssus"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 13, rubric, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: An English Translation of the Chapters, Hymns, etc., of the Theban Recension, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. I" ;
    skos:definition "Recite over a ring made of ankham flowers, which shall be laid on the right ear of the khu, together with a second ring wrapped up in a strip of byssus cloth, on which the name of the deceased shall be written, on the day of sepulture."@en ;
    skos:note "Two objects, one material each, two placements, one day. No outcome is stated at all: the rubric ends with the day of sepulture and says nothing about what the rings obtain. It is one of the shortest in the corpus and one of the few that promise nothing."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmp:AegyptiaChartaNavis a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Zeichnung auf unbeschriebenem Papyrus mit Tinte aus grünem Abut und Anti-Wasser"@de,
        "The design on unused papyrus in ink of green abut and anti water"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 100, rubric, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: An English Translation of the Chapters, Hymns, etc., of the Theban Recension, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II" ;
    skos:definition "Recite over the design which has been drawn above; it shall be written upon papyrus which has not been written upon, with ink made of grains of green abut mixed with anti water, and the papyrus shall be placed on the breast of the deceased. It shall not touch his members."@en ;
    skos:note "The only negative handling instruction in the rubrics read: the papyrus is placed on the breast and must not touch the body. Nothing is said about what follows if it does."@en,
        "The stated outcome: he shall go forth into the boat of Ra every day, and Thoth shall take account of him as he comes forth and goes in, regularly and continually, and he shall set up the Tet and stablish the buckle and sail with Ra wherever he wishes."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmp:AegyptiaCoronaVictoriae a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Siegeskranz auf dem Gesicht, zweimal bei Tagesanbruch gesprochen"@de,
        "The chaplet of victory on the face, said twice at dawn"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 19, rubric, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: An English Translation of the Chapters, Hymns, etc., of the Theban Recension, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. I" ;
    skos:definition "Recite over the divine chaplet which is laid upon the face of the deceased, and cast incense into the fire on his behalf. The chapter is to be said twice at dawn, regularly and continually."@en ;
    skos:note "One of the very few rubrics that fixes a time of day and a number of repetitions. It also makes the only claim of its kind in the corpus read by this run: Budge renders it a never-failing charm. No sign of the charm working is named, and no case of it failing could be, since the claim excludes one."@en,
        "The stated outcome is double and of two kinds: triumph over his enemies, dead or alive, and a place among the followers of Osiris with a hand stretched out to him bearing meat and drink."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmp:AegyptiaDeusManusElatae a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Gott der erhobenen Hand, in Lapislazuli und Gamai-Wasser gemalt, über dem Herzen"@de,
        "The god of the lifted hand painted in lapis-lazuli and gamai water, over the heart"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 165, rubric, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: An English Translation of the Chapters, Hymns, etc., of the Theban Recension, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. III" ;
    skos:definition "Recite over a figure of the god-of-the-lifted-hand having plumes upon its head, its legs wide apart and its middle portion in the form of a beetle, painted blue with lapis-lazuli mixed with gamai water; and over a second figure with a head like that of a man, its hands and arms stretched away from its body, with the head of a ram above each shoulder. Paint the first figure upon a piece of linen immediately over the heart, and the second over the breasts."@en ;
    skos:note "Two placements on the body given by organ rather than by garment, which is unusual: the amulet rubrics say at the neck, and this one says over the heart and over the breasts."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmp:AegyptiaFasciaRegia a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Binde aus königlichem Leinen, mit Anti beschrieben, am Hals am Tag des Begräbnisses"@de,
        "The bandlet of royal linen written with anti, at the neck on the day of burial"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 101, rubric, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: An English Translation of the Chapters, Hymns, etc., of the Theban Recension, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II" ;
    skos:definition "Recite over a bandlet of the fine linen of kings upon which the chapter has been written with anti, which shall be laid upon the neck of the perfect khu on the day of the burial."@en ;
    skos:note "The outcome is stated at unusual length and reaches into what an onlooker could in principle check: he shall be stablished as a star face to face with Septet (Sothis), the goddess Menqet shall make plants to germinate upon his body, and Thoth shall make the light rest upon his corruptible body at will. The first of these is an astronomical claim about a dead man and the second a claim about his corpse."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmp:AegyptiaFiguraInNavi a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Bild des Toten in der Modellbarke, und die Waschung des Rezitierenden"@de,
        "The figure of the deceased in the model boat, and the washing of the reciter"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 136A, rubric, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: An English Translation of the Chapters, Hymns, etc., of the Theban Recension, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II" ;
    skos:definition "Say over a figure of the deceased which shall be placed in a model of the boat of the sun. He that reciteth it shall be washed and shall be ceremonially pure, and shall have burnt incense before Ra and offered wine, cakes and roasted fowl for the journey of the deceased in the boat of Ra."@en ;
    skos:note "One of the few rubrics that puts a condition on the living performer rather than on the dead beneficiary, and the only one in the group of boat rubrics that does. The condition is washing and ceremonial purity; no consequence of omitting it is stated."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmp:AegyptiaLaterMeridionalis a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der beschriftete Ziegel mit Weihrauch in der Mitte, mit Pech bestrichen und entzündet"@de,
        "The inscribed brick with incense in its middle, smeared with pitch and set alight"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 137A, the third of the four appended brick rubrics, Papyrus of Nu, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: An English Translation of the Chapters, Hymns, etc., of the Theban Recension, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II" ;
    skos:definition "Recite over a brick of green clay upon which the chapter has been inscribed. Place incense in the middle of it, smear it with pitch or resin and set light to it. Make a cavity in the south wall, turn the front of the brick towards the north within it, and wall it up. Words are then given to be spoken."@en ;
    skos:note "The object is set on fire and then immured. Nothing in the rubric says how long it is to burn, or whether it is still burning when the cavity is closed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmp:AegyptiaLaterOrientalis a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Anubis aus grünem Ton mit Weihrauch geknetet, in die Ostwand vermauert, nach Westen gewandt"@de,
        "The Anubis of green clay kneaded with incense, walled into the east wall facing west"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 137A, the second of the four appended brick rubrics, Papyrus of Nu, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: An English Translation of the Chapters, Hymns, etc., of the Theban Recension, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II" ;
    skos:definition "Recite over a figure of Anubis made of green clay kneaded with incense and set up on a brick of green clay upon which the chapter has been inscribed. Make a cavity in the east wall, turn the face of Anubis towards the west within it, and wall it up. Words are then given to be spoken."@en ;
    skos:note "The material is a compound and the rubric says how it is made: green clay kneaded with incense. It is the only object in the corpus read here whose substance is prescribed as a mixture rather than named as a stone or a metal."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmp:AegyptiaLiberFestorum a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Buch, an sieben genannten Festen rezitiert"@de,
        "The book recited on seven named festivals"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 190, rubric, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: An English Translation of the Chapters, Hymns, etc., of the Theban Recension, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. III" ;
    skos:definition "This book shall be recited on the first day of the month, on the festival of the six, on the festival of Uak, on the festival of Thoth, on the birthday of Osiris, on the festival of Sekri, and on the festival of the night of Haker."@en ;
    skos:note "Budge notes that this chapter may be merely the rubric of the preceding chapter in the Papyrus of Nu, given a title of its own; he prints it separately for convenience. The node follows his printing and records his doubt rather than resolving it."@en,
        "Seven occasions named and nothing else prescribed: no object, no material, no place. It is the only rubric in the corpus read that fixes a calendar and leaves everything else open."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmp:AegyptiaMutTricipita a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die dreiköpfige Mut mit Flügeln und Löwenklauen auf Leinenbinde, zwischen zwei Zwergen"@de,
        "The three-headed Mut with wings and lion's claws on a linen bandage, between two dwarfs"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 164, rubric, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: An English Translation of the Chapters, Hymns, etc., of the Theban Recension, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. III" ;
    skos:definition "Recite over a figure of Mut having three heads: the first like that of Pekhat and bearing plumes, the second like that of a man and bearing the crowns of the South and the North, the third like that of a vulture and bearing plumes. The figure shall have a phallus, a pair of wings and the claws of a lion, and shall be painted with ant and powder of watch mixed with yellow colour upon a bandage of fine linen. One dwarf shall stand in front of it and another behind; each shall wear plumes, shall have one hand and arm raised, shall have two faces, one of a hawk and one of a man, and shall have a fat body."@en ;
    skos:note "The longest single specification in the corpus and the one least like a prescription for an act. Nothing is done to the figure and nothing is placed anywhere: the whole rubric describes what is to be drawn. It is a drawing instruction under the name of a rite."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmp:AegyptiaNavisPicta a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Barke des Ra, an reinem Ort gemalt, mit den beiden Booten an ihren Seiten"@de,
        "The boat of Ra painted in a pure place, with the two barques on its sides"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 130, rubric, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: An English Translation of the Chapters, Hymns, etc., of the Theban Recension, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II" ;
    skos:definition "Recite over a boat of the god Ra which has been painted in colours in a pure place. Place a figure of the deceased in the bows of it, paint a Sektet boat upon its right side and an Atet boat upon its left side, and make offerings to them of bread, cakes, wine, oil and every kind of fair offering upon the birthday of Osiris."@en ;
    skos:note "A prescribed orientation — one barque to the right, one to the left — and a prescribed date, the birthday of Osiris. The corpus fixes a day here and almost nowhere else."@en,
        "The stated outcome: his soul shall have existence and shall live for ever and shall not die a second time. The Saite recension adds to this chapter a promise of knowing the hidden things of the underworld and a find legend under the reign of Semti; Budge prints that separately and it is not entered here."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmp:AegyptiaNavisPorcellana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Barke aus grünem Porzellan, vier Ellen lang, mit Natron und Weihrauch gereinigt"@de,
        "The boat of green porcelain, four cubits long, purified with natron and incense"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 133, rubric, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: An English Translation of the Chapters, Hymns, etc., of the Theban Recension, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II" ;
    skos:definition "Recite over a boat four cubits in its length and made of green porcelain, on which have been painted the divine sovereign chiefs of the cities, with a heaven and its stars also made. The whole shall be made ceremonially pure by means of natron and incense. An image of Ra in yellow colour shall be made upon a new plaque and set at the bows, and an image of the khu whom you wish to make perfect shall be placed in the boat and made to travel about in it."@en ;
    skos:note "A measurement, and the manuscripts disagree about it: Budge's base text gives four cubits and the Papyrus of Ani gives seven. A prescription with a number in it is the kind that two copies can contradict, and here two do."@en,
        "Two purifying agents are named, natron and incense, and they are applied to an object rather than to a person. The corpus purifies the man at chapter 125 and the boat here in the same vocabulary."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmp:AegyptiaPicturaPrincipum a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Fürsten in gelber Tinte auf die Barke des Ra gezeichnet, und das Bild, das an jedes Tor geschickt wird"@de,
        "The sovereign chiefs drawn in yellow ink on the boat of Ra, and the figure sent to each gate"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 144, rubric, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: An English Translation of the Chapters, Hymns, etc., of the Theban Recension, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II" ;
    skos:definition "Say over a drawing of the divine sovereign chiefs done in yellow ink on the boat of Ra, with offerings and oblations made to them and sacrifices of feathered fowl, and incense burnt before them. Make a figure of the deceased in their presence and make it come forth towards every one of the gates which are painted above. Recite the chapter at the door of each of the arits painted above, and at each one make offerings, namely the thigh, the head, the heart and other portions."@en ;
    skos:note "The only rubric read in this corpus that prescribes a movement: the figure of the deceased is made to come forth towards each painted gate in turn, and the chapter is recited at each door. Everything else in the rubrics is a placing or an anointing."@en,
        "The stated outcome connects it to the apparatus of names: he shall neither be repulsed nor turned back at the pylons of the underworld. See pmc:AegyptiaExaminatioRerum."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmp:AegyptiaScarabaeusViridis a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Skarabäus aus grünem Stein in Kupfer und Silber, am Hals"@de,
        "The scarab of green stone in copper and silver, at the neck"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 30B, rubric, Papyrus of Amen-hetep, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: An English Translation of the Chapters, Hymns, etc., of the Theban Recension, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. I" ;
    skos:definition "Say these words over a scarab of green stone encircled with a band of refined copper and having a ring of silver, which shall be placed on the neck of the khu."@en ;
    skos:note "The heart scarab of basalt set in gold is a different prescription and is held at pmp:AegyptiaScarabaeusCordis in traditions/egyptian-ritual.ttl."@en,
        "Three materials named exactly and one placement. The rubric then turns into the find legend of the chapter, which is held separately at pmc:AegyptiaInventioLibri; the object and the legend travel together in this manuscript and are separated here only because they make different kinds of claim."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmp:AegyptiaSerpensUtchat a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Schlange mit Beinen und die beiden geflügelten Utchats, mit Anti auf Stein oder Leinen geschrieben"@de,
        "The serpent with legs and the two winged Utchats, written with anti on stone or linen"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 163, rubric, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: An English Translation of the Chapters, Hymns, etc., of the Theban Recension, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. III" ;
    skos:definition "Recite over a serpent having legs and wearing a disk and two horns, and over two Utchats having both eyes and wings. In the pupil of one Utchat there shall be a figure of the god-of-the-lifted-hand with the face of the divine soul, having plumes and a back like a hawk; in the pupil of the other, a figure of the same god with the face of the goddess Neith, having plumes and a back like a hawk. The chapter shall be written with anti either upon a meh stone or upon mother-of-emerald of the south dipped in water of the western lake of Qemt, or upon a bandage of watchet linen with which every limb should be swathed."@en ;
    skos:note "Three alternative supports are offered for the same writing — a stone, a treated stone, a linen bandage — and the rubric does not rank them or say what turns on the choice. Alternatives without a criterion are rare here and worth marking: elsewhere the material is the prescription."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmp:AegyptiaTabulaMaati a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die neue Tafel aus Erde, von keinem Schwein und keinem Tier betreten, in Farbe gezeichnet"@de,
        "The new tile of earth untrodden by pig or beast, drawn in colour"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 125, rubric, Papyrus of Nu, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: An English Translation of the Chapters, Hymns, etc., of the Theban Recension, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II" ;
    skos:definition "The making of the representation of what shall happen in the Hall of the two Maati. The chapter is to be said after the deceased has been cleansed and purified, arrayed in apparel, shod with white leather sandals, his eyes painted with antimony and his body anointed with unguent of anti, and when he offers oxen, feathered fowl, incense, cakes, ale and garden herbs. Then the representation shall be drawn in colour upon a new tile moulded from earth upon which neither a pig nor any other animal has trodden, and the book written upon it."@en ;
    skos:note "The most specified prescription in the corpus. Six preparations of the person, six classes of offering, one material for the ground, and one condition on that material which is a condition about the past of a lump of clay: no pig and no other animal may have walked on the earth it was moulded from. Whether that condition holds of a given tile cannot be established by looking at the tile."@en,
        "The outcome is the one at pmc:AegyptiaRubricaScientiae: he shall flourish, his children shall flourish, his name shall never fall into oblivion, he shall be as one who filleth the heart of the king and of his princes, and he shall not be turned back at any door in Amentet."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmp:AegyptiaTetAureus a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das goldene Tet auf Sykomore, in Anch-am-Blütenwasser eingelegt, am Hals"@de,
        "The Tet of gold on sycamore steeped in water of ankham flowers, at the neck"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 155, rubric, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: An English Translation of the Chapters, Hymns, etc., of the Theban Recension, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II" ;
    skos:definition "Recite over a Tet of gold set in a plinth of sycamore wood which has been steeped in water of ankham flowers, and place it at the neck of the deceased on the day of the funeral."@en ;
    skos:note "The stated outcome is a state and a calendar: he shall become a perfect khu in the underworld, and at the new year festivals he shall be like those in the following of Osiris, continually and for ever."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmp:AegyptiaTorquisAureus a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der gravierte goldene Halskragen, am Hals am Tag des Begräbnisses"@de,
        "The collar of gold engraved, at the neck on the day of the funeral"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 158, rubric, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: An English Translation of the Chapters, Hymns, etc., of the Theban Recension, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II" ;
    skos:definition "Say over a collar of gold upon which the chapter has been engraved, and place the collar on the neck of the deceased on the day of the funeral."@en ;
    skos:note "The whole rubric. Object, material, inscription, placement, day; nothing promised."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmp:AegyptiaUatchSmaragdi a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Uatch aus Smaragdmutter, beschriftet, am Hals"@de,
        "The Uatch of mother-of-emerald inscribed, at the neck"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 159, rubric, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: An English Translation of the Chapters, Hymns, etc., of the Theban Recension, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II" ;
    skos:definition "Say over an Uatch of mother-of-emerald upon which the chapter has been inscribed, and place the Uatch on the neck of the deceased."@en ;
    skos:note "The last rubric of the second volume, and the barest: no day, no preparation, no outcome."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmp:AegyptiaUtchatPlena a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Utchat aus Lapislazuli oder Mak-Stein, beim vollen Utchat im Monat Pert"@de,
        "The Utchat of lapis-lazuli or mak stone, at the full Utchat in the month of pert"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 140, rubric, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: An English Translation of the Chapters, Hymns, etc., of the Theban Recension, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II" ;
    skos:definition "Recite over an Utchat of real lapis-lazuli or of mak stone plated with gold, before which every kind of fair and pure oblation shall be offered when Ra shows himself on the last day of the second month of the season pert. Make another Utchat of jasper and place it upon such part of the dead man's body as you please. While the chapter is recited and the offerings made, at the time when the Utchat is full, four altars shall be lighted for Ra-Tem and four for others."@en ;
    skos:note "The most precisely dated prescription in the corpus: the last day of the second month of the season pert, at the moment Ra shows himself. It is also the only one whose placement is left to the officiant — the second Utchat goes on whatever part of the body he pleases — so the same rubric fixes the calendar to the day and leaves the anatomy open."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmp:AegyptiaVaccaAurea a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Kuh aus Feingold am Hals und das Kapitel auf neuem Papyrus unter dem Kopf"@de,
        "The cow of fine gold at the neck and the chapter on new papyrus under the head"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 162, rubric, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: An English Translation of the Chapters, Hymns, etc., of the Theban Recension, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. III" ;
    skos:definition "Recite over the image of a cow made in fine gold and placed at the neck of the deceased, and written upon new papyrus and placed under his head."@en ;
    skos:note "The rubric grounds the prescription in a precedent rather than in an authority: it says the thing has exceedingly great protective power because it was made by the cow for her son Ra when he was setting and his habitation was surrounded by a company of beings of fire. A single stated case, and the case is a god's."@en,
        "The stated outcome is the only one in the rubrics read that is a bodily state and not a status: abundant warmth shall be in him throughout, even like that which was in him when he was upon earth. It is also the only promised effect in this bundle that would be plain to anyone who touched the body."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmp:AegyptiaVulturAureus a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der beschriftete goldene Geier, am Hals am Tag des Begräbnisses"@de,
        "The vulture of gold inscribed, at the neck on the day of the funeral"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 157, rubric, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: An English Translation of the Chapters, Hymns, etc., of the Theban Recension, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II" ;
    skos:definition "Say over a vulture of gold upon which the chapter has been inscribed, and place the vulture on the neck to protect the perfect deceased one on the day of the funeral, continually and regularly."@en ;
    skos:note "One of four consecutive rubrics — 157, 158, 159 and 155 — that differ only in the object and the metal or stone. Set side by side they show the rubric as a form being filled rather than a report of anything done."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmp:AgrippaSuccessSignSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die drei Buecher ein wahrnehmbares Zeichen dafuer nennen, dass eine Verrichtung gewirkt hat"@de,
        "Whether the three books name a perceptible sign that an operation has worked"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-22" ;
    skos:note "Not one sign of an operation. The fifteen terms produce seventeen hits in the whole volume and every one of them is something else: the translator's own preface telling the buyer that he shall see the possibility of these things confirmed by reason and example; the chapters on astrological judgement, where what shall appear is a prodigy or a configuration to be interpreted and not the result of anything done; a passage on extracting the name of a genius, where what shall appear is the star that governs a person; and scriptural quotations about seeking and finding and about faith as a grain of mustard seed."@en,
        "The comparison that makes the count readable rather than impressive. The Greek magical papyrus this holding opened at the same time announces a dozen sights in order and states one forfeiture; this work states seven kinds of failure and no sight. They are opposite arrangements of the same silence, and neither gives the practitioner a criterion: the papyrus tells him what he will see and never what would show it had failed, and this work tells him what will have gone wrong and never what it looks like when nothing has."@en,
        "What the empty result is worth, set against what the same volume does say. This work states that a firm belief is required and that doubt breaks the operation; that a constant credulity works miracles even in false operations; that the operator must be dignified and that many prosecute the art in vain for want of it; that nothing will be done without the assistance of the moon; that neglect of the true motions defrauds many of their desired effect; that publication destroys the experiment; and that a reader who does not obtain his desire should accuse himself. Seven places where a failure is accounted for, and no place where a success is recognised. A transmission arranged that way cannot be wrong at any point its own text admits."@en,
        "Where a later run should go, and what it must not conclude. The fourth book that circulates under this author's name is reported to supply exactly what is missing here, in the form of the shapes in which the spirits of the planets appear; it is a work of 1559, twenty-four years after his death, it was not opened in this run, and nothing about it is claimed. If it does carry the signs, the interesting fact will be that the pseudepigraph supplies what the authentic work withholds."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search of the whole volume, case-insensitive, for: thou shalt see, you shall see, shall appear unto, will appear unto, sign that, a sign of, token of, thou shalt know, you shall know, shalt perceive, shall perceive, thou shalt find, shall feel, shall hear, if it be done rightly. Every hit read in its place."@en ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-22" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Agrippa, De occulta philosophia, the whole of the volume in the English translation of 1651 — the three books, the address to the reader, the two letters and the censure printed after book III (trans. J. F., London 1651)."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage telling the operator what he would perceive when an operation of the work has taken effect: an appearance, a light, a warmth, a voice, a state of the body, any observable announced in advance as the mark of success. The work states conditions of success in quantity and conditions of failure in quantity; whether it anywhere states a sign had to be established across the whole volume, because a run that read only the chapters on dignification and on celestial opportunity would have found none and learnt nothing from that."@en .

pmp:AjivikaSelfWitnessSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob irgendetwas von den Ājīvikas selbst erreichbar ist"@de,
        "Whether anything the Ājīvikas themselves produced is reachable"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-07" ;
    skos:note "So the registration's sentence that this transmission has no corpus of its own stands unconfirmed by this run, and is the one thing in this file that a reader should not take on its authority. The next run on this transmission can settle it in one reading, and the place to go is named in AGENTS.md once archive.org answers again."@en,
        "The search was carried at the first of the two stages only, and that is stated rather than glossed over: it established that no transmitted work of the Ājīvikas is named as reachable, and it did not open a second-hand account to confirm it, because the standard study of the transmission could not be reached on the day of the run. That is a property of the access path and not of the work: archive.org, which is the regular route of this holding and carries that study, answered every request of this run with a global outage page. The reading this file does rest on did not need it; the claim that nothing of their own survives still does."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Ājīvika, Ājīvaka, Āgīvika, Ājīvikas with the terms text, canon, scripture, inscription, surviving, lost; the names Nanda Vaccha, Kisa Saṅkicca, Makkhali Gosāla, Maskarin Gośāliputra; and the Tamil and Sanskrit forms of the name."@en ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-07" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Not a corpus but the question of whether one exists: the search was for a text, an inscription or a documentary record transmitted by the Ājīvikas themselves rather than by their opponents, the registration of this transmission having asserted that there is none."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A self-witness of any kind, against which the ascriptions of this file could be checked. Without one, every node of traditions/ajivika.ttl rests on a canon that is hostile to it, and the holding cannot distinguish the doctrine from the report of the doctrine."@en .

pmp:AjivikaWitnessSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der einzige Zeuge die Lehre bei einem Lehrer hält"@de,
        "Whether the sole witness keeps the doctrine attached to one teacher"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-07" ;
    skos:note "It does not. Three places and three arrangements. The Sāmaññaphalasutta gives the enumeration with the ball of thread to Makkhali Gosāla and the seven unmade bodies with the sword that takes no life to Pakudha Kaccāyana, in two separate sections six paragraphs apart. The Mahādiṭṭhisutta runs both together as one view and names no teacher for it. The Chaḷabhijātisutta gives the six classes of birth, which the Sāmaññaphalasutta has inside Makkhali's own enumeration, to Pūraṇa Kassapa, and has Gosāla appear in that scheme as a member of its highest class instead of as its author."@en,
        "Recorded as opposing material and not as a find, because what it opposes is the holding's own ascriptions. It does not show the doctrine false and it does not show it not Ājīvika; it shows that the one canon this transmission is reached through does not treat these blocks as the property of one teacher. Every node of traditions/ajivika.ttl carries pm:thirdPartyAscription for that reason, and the dispute itself is entered at pmc:AjivikaChalabhijatiVivada rather than left in this note."@en,
        "The witness's stance, from the same search and from the same canon: the Kesakambalasutta calls this doctrine the worst of all the doctrines of ascetics and brahmins and its teacher a trap for humans. A hostile witness that also shuffles the material between teachers is the weakest kind of source this holding admits, and it is the only kind available here."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchTerms "The shared clauses themselves rather than keywords: niyatisaṅgatibhāvapariṇatā, cuddasa yonipamukhasatasahassāni, aparipakkaṁ kammaṁ paripācessāmi, suttaguḷe khitte, sattime kāyā akaṭā akaṭavidhā, chaḷābhijātiyo; each sought across the canon and each occurrence read with the heading above it."@en ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:AjivikaAnattributaDitthi,
        pmc:AjivikaChalabhijati,
        pmc:AjivikaChalabhijatiVivada ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-07" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The same places in the same edition, with the three doctrinal blocks read in the Pali side by side: Dīgha Nikāya 2 at the headings Makkhaligosālavāda and Pakudhakaccāyanavāda, Saṁyutta Nikāya 24.8, and Aṅguttara Nikāya 6.57."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "Whether the enumeration and the denial of austerity that the Sāmaññaphalasutta ascribes to Makkhali Gosāla stand elsewhere in the same canon, and under whose name. This is the counter-search to every ascription in traditions/ajivika.ttl: a report whose own canon moves the doctrine between teachers is a weaker witness than one that does not, and that has to be established before the ascriptions are written and not after."@en .

pmp:AjivikaYieldingSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob eine Quelle berichtet, die Ājīvikas hätten ein durch Übung erreichbares Vermögen behauptet"@de,
        "Whether any source reports the Ājīvikas asserting a capacity reached by practice"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-07" ;
    skos:note "Nothing, and the nothing is a doctrine and not a gap. The passages found say the opposite in the plainest words the canon has: there is no cause and no condition for the purification of beings, no act of one's own and none of another, no power, no energy, no human strength, no human exertion; and there is no such thing as by this precept or vow or austerity or holy life I shall ripen an unripe deed or wear away a ripe one. Node pmc:AjivikaNaTapasaParipaka. The Aṅguttara's own summary of the doctrine is three words long and is the same: natthi kammaṁ, natthi kiriyaṁ, natthi vīriyaṁ."@en,
        "So traditions/ajivika.ttl carries no pm:Yielding and cannot. That is the only file in this holding of which that is true for a reason rather than for want of reading, and the reason is worth more to the project than a capacity would have been: the holding now has one registered transmission whose reported position is the denial of every other transmission in it."@en,
        "What was found instead and is not a capacity: an end that arrives without being reached. Fools and the wise alike make an end of suffering after running through the enumerated round, as a thrown ball of thread runs on only while it unwinds. Nodes pmc:AjivikaSamsarasuddhi and pmc:AjivikaSuttagulaUpama. A later run must not read that as an attainment; the text makes it independent of anything anybody does."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "In the Pali: iddhi, abhiññā, tapa, tapas, brahmacariya, sīla, vata, paripācessāmi, byantiṁ karissāmi, vīriya, purisaparakkama, attakāra, parakāra; and the names makkhali, gosāla, ājīvaka, ājīvika in every spelling the canon uses, each hit opened in its sutta."@en ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-07" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Every place in the Pali canon that names the bamboo-staffed ascetic Gosāla or the Ājīvaka ascetics, located by a search of the whole canon and read in the Pali of the Mahāsaṅgīti Tipiṭaka Buddhavasse 2500 with the English of Bhikkhu Sujato: seventeen places for the name of the teacher and thirty for the name of the ascetics, of which the doctrinal ones are Dīgha Nikāya 2, Saṁyutta Nikāya 24.8, Aṅguttara Nikāya 3.137 and 6.57, and Majjhima Nikāya 76; the rest are monastic-rule passages, verses and name lists."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "The form of claim this holding collects: a practice, however described, that the transmission is reported to have held brings about a state, a power or an attainment. The question is put to this transmission because its own registration called it the limiting case and because the structure-driven entrance sends a run to places nobody asked about."@en .

pmp:AndeanDreamDivination a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Wahrsagen durch Schlafen auf dem Kleidungsstück des Fragenden"@de,
        "Divining by sleeping on the questioner's garment"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III, on the moscoc" ;
    skos:definition "The diviner by dreams. Someone comes to ask whether he will recover or die, or whether a lost horse will be found. If the questioner is a man the diviner asks for the band from his head, or his bag, or his cloak, or some other part of his clothing; if a woman, for her sash or the like. He takes it home, sleeps on it, and answers according to what he dreams. Where the question is about love, he asks for the hair or the clothing of the person he is to divine about."@en ;
    skos:note "The identifier carries the name of the tradition because the plain one was already taken by a practice of another transmission in this graph. That is a property of a flat identifier space and not of the practice; see prima-materia#275."@en,
        "The instrument is a possession of the person the question is about, not of the questioner, wherever the two differ. That distinction is stated in the source and is the only place in this file where the procedure names what it is a procedure on."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmp:AnguiShi a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Probe an der Schildkröte"@de,
        "The pressing of the turtle"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 73 (Guan neng), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 11" ;
    skos:definition "The procedure the chapter gives for deciding whether a person has the poisonous hand. A turtle is placed under a vessel and the person presses on the vessel from above. If his hand is poisonous, the turtle is dead in fifty days; if his hand is sweet, it lives on as before."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a practice in its own right because of what it is: a discrimination procedure, stated by the tradition, over a claim about a person, with a fixed term and two outcomes that are not the same. Nothing else read in this corpus in this run has that shape, and the reason for opening the corpus from the testability side was the expectation of finding it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmp:AnthroposophyStatedTermSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Schulung eine Frist nennt, zu der ihr Ergebnis fällig ist"@de,
        "Whether the schooling states a term by which its result is due"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-22" ;
    skos:note "No term anywhere. The periods the text gives are after some time, after an appropriate time, and perhaps only after many attempts — each of them the length of time the thing takes, stated as the thing taking time. The one place where a term is nearly set is the instruction to impress on oneself that it will one day come to me, what is to come to me, when I am ripe for it, which makes ripeness the condition and the arrival of the result its only evidence."@en,
        "Set beside the two corpora this holding opened in the same week, the pattern is worth recording even though three cases are not a count. A booklet of household charms with no doctrine sets a broken leg straight by the ninth day. A volume arguing for four hundred pages that its healing is a science sets no term. A book publishing a graded training of perception sets no term and adds a clause explaining why the pupil should not expect one. The elaboration of the doctrine and the presence of something that falls due run in opposite directions in all three, and whether that holds generally is a question for a statement and not for this file."@en,
        "The book provides in advance for the pupil who finds nothing, and this is the clause the search was really looking for. He must always keep before his eyes, it says in the chapter on inner calm, that he may already have made quite considerable progress after some time without its showing itself to him in the way he perhaps expected; whoever does not consider this will easily lose steadfastness and give up all attempts after a short time. Progress that does not show is thereby made the normal case, and the absence of the expected sign is given its account before any pupil can report it."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search for: nach einiger Zeit, nach angemessener Zeit, kurze Zeit, lange Zeit, Wochen, Monate, Jahre, Jahrzehnte, wie lange, Frist, reif, Reife, sogleich, bald. Every hit read in its place."@en ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-22" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Rudolf Steiner, Wie erlangt man Erkenntnisse der höheren Welten?, the same text and the same reading."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A stated period after which the pupil should have the perception, or after which its absence would mean something: a count of days, weeks or years, a number of repetitions, a stage that falls due. The question is put because this holding has just found that the transmission with the most elaborate claim to being a science set no term anywhere, and the comparison is worth having twice."@en .

pmp:AnthroposophyThirdPartyCheckSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob jemand außer dem Schüler feststellen kann, daß er etwas erlangt hat"@de,
        "Whether anybody other than the pupil can establish that he has attained"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-22" ;
    skos:note "One place and it closes the circle. In the chapter on the effects of initiation the book states that only he can speak about instruction for the development of supersensible faculties who has himself experienced in himself everything he is to develop in another, and who is fully in a position to recognise whether his instructions lead to the entirely correct result. So a competent judge is provided for, the qualification for the office is possession of the faculty in question, and the criterion by which he recognises the correct result is nowhere stated. Nobody outside can occupy the position and nobody inside has to show his working."@en,
        "The one check the book does name is self-examination, and it names a question somebody else could have answered. The pupil is to examine again and again whether he has fallen out of his equilibrium and whether he has remained the same man within the circumstances in which he lives. His household could answer that; it is put to him alone. That is the sharpest thing this search found and it is entered at pmp:SteinerUebungAmSamen rather than here."@en,
        "What the search did not find, listed because the absences are what a later reader will want. No examination, no conferred grade, no confirmation by a teacher of a pupil's perception, and no role for a second person at any stage. The teacher appears thirteen times in the text read and in none of them does he verify anything: he gives advice, which the book says is all he can give; he receives, perhaps, the pupil's first account of a perception — if one happens to have a teacher; and he is otherwise a figure whose authority the preface of 1918 is at pains to reduce, saying that what matters is a wholly immediate relation to the objective spiritual world rather than a relation to the personality of a teacher."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search of the whole text for: Lehrer, Prüfung, prüfen, bestätigen, Zeugnis, Grad, Mitschüler, beglaubigen, nachprüfen, feststellen, Kennzeichen, Merkmal, erkennen ob, Kontrolle, Selbstkontrolle. Every hit read in its place."@en ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:SteinerPruefungDurchUrteilskraft ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-22" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Rudolf Steiner, Wie erlangt man Erkenntnisse der höheren Welten?, in the German text of 1918, in a plain-text transcription in the full-text archive: the prefaces of 1909 and 1918 and the chapters Bedingungen, Innere Ruhe, Die Stufen der Einweihung, Praktische Gesichtspunkte, Die Bedingungen zur Geheimschulung, and Über einige Wirkungen der Einweihung, read in order."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "Any provision by which a party other than the pupil establishes that a stage has been reached: an examination, a confirmation, a grade conferred, a sign another could read, a criterion a fellow pupil or a household could apply. The question is put because the book publishes the whole method to strangers, which removes the initiatory gate, and a transmission without that gate has to put its verification somewhere."@en .

pmp:ApotropaicHarmListSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob ein apotropäisches Korpus die Schäden sortiert, die es nennt"@de,
        "Whether an apotropaic corpus sorts the harms it names"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-20" ;
    skos:note "A confirming case instead, and a sharper one than the statement had. The fixed litany of the Utukki Limnuti mixes the two kinds in the enumeration of what is adjured — pestilence, fever, sickness, death and disease standing in one list with spirit, demon, ghost, devil, god, fiend, Phantom of Night and Wraith of Night — and again in the enumeration of what is asked to be kept off: unto my body may they not draw nigh, before me may they wreak no evil, nor follow behind me, into my house may they not enter, my fence may they not break through, into my chamber may they not enter. A fence broken through is among the plainest facts any witness in this holding names, and it is set beside a wreaking of evil in the abstract with nothing to separate them."@en,
        "Limits of this run, and they are the ordinary ones. One edition, and an old one: Campbell Thompson's readings of 1903 and 1904 are superseded almost everywhere, and the holding's other Mesopotamian nodes cite Geller's critical edition, which this run could not obtain. What the search establishes is a fact about an enumeration and not about a verb, and an enumeration is the part of a text that survives a bad translation best. The namburbi rituals proper, which the protocol also named, are edited in a copyrighted work and were not reached; they remain the strongest place for a second run."@en,
        "No falsifier. Neither volume contains, in any operative text, a sign by which a protection would be known to have held, a term within which the harm would otherwise have come, or a condition under which the adjuration is to be judged to have failed. The search was designed to find one: the second volume is titled for fever, sickness and headache, and Babylonian diagnostic literature elsewhere is full of he shall recover and he shall die, so a prognosis attached to an incantation was the likely refutation. There is none in either volume. Every hit for shall recover and shall die was zero; the three hits for seventh day and the three for if he and unless all fall in the editor's introduction or in the hemerology of unlucky days, which is a calendar and not a test of a protection."@en,
        "One thing the search found that the statement did not anticipate. Tablet III does not list what is present but disjoins what might be — whether thou be an evil Spirit or an evil Demon, or sickness, or death, or evil pestilence — so the operation is written not to require that the officiant establish which harm he is treating. That is a second and independent way of making the claim unfailable, and it is not the mixing of kinds; it is a refusal to identify the kind at all. It is held at pmc:AsipuNominisIncertitudo and is a reason to widen the protocol rather than to narrow the statement."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of both volumes on the whitespace-normalised text. For a term or a sign: shall recover, shall die, shall live, shall be healed, be cured, not be cured, thou shalt know, seventh day, third day, if he, unless. For the protection formulae themselves: come not nigh, draw not nigh, may they not enter, get thee, shall not approach, be thou removed. Every hit of every pattern was read in context. The recurring litany was located by its own wording and counted."@en ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:AsipuLitaniaMalorum,
        pmc:AsipuNominisIncertitudo,
        pmc:AsipuUtukkiYieldsRemotio ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-20" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "R. Campbell Thompson, The Devils and Evil Spirits of Babylonia, being Babylonian and Assyrian incantations against the demons, ghouls, vampires, hobgoblins, ghosts and kindred evil spirits which attack mankind, Luzac's Semitic Text and Translation Series vols. XIV and XV (London: Luzac, 1903 and 1904), both volumes complete: the Utukki Limnuti series in volume I and the Fever Sickness and Headache series in volume II, with their introductions" ;
    pm:searchedFor "The falsifier stated at pmc:TheProtectionAndWhatWouldShowItFailedExamination, in the corpus that protocol names as the place to run it first. Three things would have failed the statement: an operative text that marks off the harms whose occurrence a bystander could establish from those whose occurrence nobody could record; one that names a recordable harm as the test of whether the protection held; or one that states a condition under which the protection is to be judged to have failed." .

pmp:ArsNotoriaPrognosisExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Antwort des Kranken entscheidet, was die Kunst sagt"@de,
        "Whether the answer of the sick man decides what the art says it decides"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that after the oration of the physical art is said three times, the sick man's answer to the question how he feels tells without doubt whether he will recover or die."@en ;
    skos:note "One escape the corpus takes elsewhere is absent here. The art bars the man impeded by crime from saying the orations at all, and that condition is recorded as a caution; nothing in this passage says that a wrong prognosis shows the speaker was unworthy. Whether a fuller recension closes that gap is unread and would change the state of this protocol rather than its subject."@en,
        "The obstacle to cases is not the difficulty of the procedure but who would keep the record. The judgement has to be written before the outcome and by someone who is not the person nursing the patient; the corpus offers no such series, and none has been looked for outside this graph."@en,
        "The third branch is excluded from the falsifying condition on purpose. A patient who says he does not know is covered by dying and by getting worse together, and no fate he can meet falls outside that pair. Testing the rule as a whole would let that branch absorb every case the other two got wrong, which is exactly how a claim that says nothing comes to look confirmed."@en,
        "Which reading is being tested has to be said, and this protocol tests the English. The Latin of the same printing says of the badly answering patient only that he will die shortly; Turner says he will die on the morrow. Shortly has no last day and therefore no outcome that could arrive too late, so the Latin branch is not examinable at all. The divergence stands as pmc:ArsNotoriaTerminusMortisDisputatus and it is the reason this protocol names its witness rather than the tradition."@en ;
    pm:baseRate "Unknown and not a single number: whether a person who lies languishing recovers depends on the illness, and the rule is stated for illness in general. Any protocol has to fix the population before it starts, because a rule applied to a ward of the nearly recovered and a rule applied to a ward of the dying score differently without either being right." ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, working from the passages recorded in traditions/ars-notoria.ttl" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "One sick person who answers that he is at good ease or begins to mend and then dies of that illness, or who answers that he is grievously ill or worse and worse and then recovers, with the answer and the judgement written down before the outcome. The first two branches each name one outcome and exclude the other, and the text says without doubt and doubtless, so a single such case refutes the rule as stated." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-07" ;
    pm:tests pmc:ArsNotoriaOratioYieldsCognitioAegritudinis .

pmp:ArsNotoriaSexPredictionExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Oration das Geschlecht des ungeborenen Kindes offenbart"@de,
        "Whether the oration reveals the sex of the unborn child"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that saying the oration while touching the pulse of a pregnant woman reveals whether she will bring forth a male or a female. It is the claim in this corpus that comes closest to being ready for a count: a binary outcome, a known base rate, a public result, and a prediction that can be fixed in writing beforehand."@en ;
    skos:note "No case has been collected, and the obstacle here is not the record but the era: the claim is from a work in print since sixteen fifty-seven and nobody appears to have counted. That is a property of the attention the claim has had and not of the claim, which is why the state is procedure-without-cases."@en,
        "The passage that carries this claim also carries the sentence that would explain a failure away, and it explains it away in the wrong direction: the miracle proceeds not from the operator's nature but from the angels, whose office it is to reveal these things. That locates the acting elsewhere but sets no condition on the operator, so it does not absorb a wrong answer. Nothing in the passage does."@en,
        "What a protocol would have to guard against is not the operator's honesty but the moment of prediction. Touching the pulse puts the operator in front of the woman, and a prediction made late in a pregnancy by someone who can see her is not a prediction from the oration. The prediction has to be sealed and the point in the pregnancy fixed, or the protocol measures ordinary observation."@en ;
    pm:baseRate "About one half. The sex ratio at birth in human populations runs a little above parity for males, so an operator predicting at random scores close to fifty in a hundred, and a series has to be long enough to distinguish a real excess from that. This is the only claim the run met whose base rate is known in advance rather than having to be estimated from the same data that test the claim." ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, working from the passage recorded in traditions/ars-notoria.ttl" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A series of predictions recorded before each birth, by an operator keeping the conditions the text sets, whose proportion correct does not exceed what guessing would give on the same series. The claim is stated without degree and without exception, so a single wrong prediction refutes it as it stands; the weaker form, that the operator does better than chance, is what a series would actually settle, and it is stated here because the strong form is the one the text makes and the weak form is the one worth measuring." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-07" ;
    pm:tests pmc:ArsNotoriaOratioYieldsSexusInfantis .

pmp:ArtefactPenaltySearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob eine andere Auflage im Bestand von dem Zustand eines gemachten Dings beantwortet wird"@de,
        "Whether any other observance in the holding is answered by the state of a made object"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-02" ;
    skos:note "Nothing of the kind. Of the eighty-three stated consequences in the holding, the five that mention an object failing are all something else. The Inuit case puts crooked arms, legs, neck or back on the person who refuses the office, which is the practitioner's own body. The Maya case says the fasts were not dared to be broken for fear of evil upon the faster or upon his house, which names no object and no failure. The Inuit sewing prohibition explains a failed return by a garment sewn of new caribou skin, which is an act and not a damaged thing. The Jain case lists seven consequences and all seven are states of the practitioner. The one that comes nearest is the Christian confession test, where the bead breaking in two rather than three means the confession was not good — and that is a divinatory criterion read off an object, not the penalty of an observance."@en,
        "So pmc:JibaroTandaraAbstinence is the first case in the holding of a rule on the maker whose stated penalty is the condition of the made thing, and the Canelos variant beside it is the second. Two cases are not a generalization and none is written. What the next run can do is ask the question of the corpora that would most likely have it and that this holding already carries: the alchemical operations, where a batch either forms or does not; the Rasashastra chapters on the eighteen operations on mercury and their doctrine of faults; the instrument-making of the ritual handbooks. None of those was asked here."@en,
        "The method, with its hole named. The first pass read a single property across the whole graph, which is exhaustive for that property and blind to a consequence stated in a skos:note instead; the second pass caught those but returned eighty-two subjects of which most were about something else, and they were sorted by reading the names rather than the nodes. A consequence of this kind hidden in a note of a node whose name does not suggest it would have been missed."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "All eighty-three pm:consequenceOfSkipping literals of the merged graph, read in full, filtered for: crack, burst, warp, split, moth, rot, spoil, useless, crooked, shatter, break, broken, flaw, fail to set, fail to form, fail to harden, does not set, does not form, not form, unusable, blacken, curdle. Five matched and all five were read at their nodes. The same patterns were then run against every literal of the graph paired with a maker's vocabulary — make, forge, carve, fashion, cast, mould, smith, weave, vessel, drum, shield, blade, amulet, image — which returned eighty-two subjects, all of them inspected by name." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-02" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The compiled holding of prima-materia as of main at bf46350, all directories, queried over the merged graph" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Whether the holding already carries a transmission in which a rule laid on the conduct of a practitioner has, as its stated consequence of being broken, the physical failure of an object he is making or has made — a split, a warp, a rot, a worm, an unusable result. The question matters because almost every stated consequence in this holding falls on the practitioner himself, where nobody but he can settle it, and a consequence that falls on a piece of wood is settled by looking." .

pmp:AsipuEruKakku a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Halten des e'ru-Szepters und des Dattelpalmwedels"@de,
        "Holding the e'ru-wood sceptre and the date-palm frond"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Udug-hul 3: 66 and 152-154 (canonical Utukku Lemnutu; critical edition M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons, BAM 8, De Gruyter 2016)" ;
    skos:definition "Holding in the hand, during the recitation, the mighty e'ru-wood sceptre of Anu, together with the plans of Ea and the date-palm frond of the major rituals."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmp:AsipuSuluhSebisu a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Siebenmaliges Reinigen des Kranken mit Flusswasser"@de,
        "Cleansing the patient seven times with river water"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Udug-hul 1: 39' (canonical Utukku Lemnutu; critical edition M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons, BAM 8, De Gruyter 2016)" ;
    skos:definition "Purifying and cleansing the patient seven times over with a hand-washing rite of water, namely with water of the Tigris and water of the Euphrates."@en ;
    skos:note "A counted repetition with a named number and named material, which is the shape a later run can compare against the counted thresholds the holding carries elsewhere. Nothing in the passage says what follows if the count is short."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmp:AsipuZisurru a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das an Tor und Tür gestreute Mehl"@de,
        "The flour scattered at the gate and the door"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Udug-hul 7: 49-51 (canonical Utukku Lemnutu; critical edition M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons, BAM 8, De Gruyter 2016)" ;
    skos:altLabel "zidubdubbu"@en,
        "zisurru"@en ;
    skos:definition "Scattering the zisurru-flour, called the mighty guardian, and scattering the flour of the magic circle at the outer gate; then sprinkling the zidubdubbu-flour at the door of the sick man's house and at the door bolt."@en ;
    skos:note "A boundary laid on a building and not on a person, and the only instruction of the series read here that takes effect where the patient is not. The flour is addressed as a guardian in its own right, so the line is both an instruction and an invocation of the material used."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmp:AtharvanRecitationFaultSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Atharvaveda-Saṃhitā einen Rezitationsfehler zur Ursache eines misslungenen Zaubers macht"@de,
        "Whether the Atharvaveda Saṃhitā makes a faulty recitation the cause of a failed charm"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-12" ;
    skos:note "Limits. One translation, one recension, and a search in English over a text whose technical vocabulary is Sanskrit; the Paippalāda was not searched at all, and a verse carrying the doctrine there would not have been reached. The negative finding is about the Śaunaka vulgate as Whitney renders it."@en,
        "The finding is a layer boundary and it is worth having as a node because the corpus is routinely cited as a whole. The Saṃhitā knows that a rite can fail — 6.114 says in the first person that the performers desired to accomplish the sacrifice and did not — and it knows a mechanism of spoiling, at 7.70, where an enemy's truth is smitten with untruth by outside agents. What it does not do anywhere in twenty books is trace a failure to the performer's own words. That doctrine, and the priestly office built on it, sit in the layer above the hymns."@en,
        "The one passage that might have been read as a condition of failure is 6.29.1, and reading it at the wording is what decided against it. Whitney renders: them yonder let the winged missile come upon; what the owl utters, be that to no purpose. That is an imprecation asking that the omen be void, not a rule that the operation fails if the owl calls; a rendering that gives it as a conditional makes the hymn say the opposite of what it asks. Recorded here rather than entered as a node."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of both volumes for: mispronounced, wrongly pronounced, ill-pronounced (none); in vain (one hit, of the Brahmans' arrows, which are said not to be in vain); fruitless (one, in the editor's preface); to no purpose (two, one an editorial remark on a simile, one at 6.29.1 of what the owl utters); without success and meet with success (one each, both at 7.70 and both of an enemy's offering being spoiled by others); not accomplish (two, both at 6.114 of the performers' own shortfall). The words defective, blunder, omission and faulty occur in quantity and belong without exception to the editorial apparatus on manuscripts and metre." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-12" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Atharvaveda Saṃhitā, Śaunaka recension, both volumes entire, in the translation of W. D. Whitney revised by C. R. Lanman, Harvard Oriental Series 7 and 8, Cambridge, Mass. 1905; searched over the whole text and read at the wording where a hit fell" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A verse of the collection itself stating that a charm did not work, or will not work, because it was spoken wrongly — a mispronunciation, an omitted word, a disordered order. The doctrine is standard in the Brāhmaṇa and Sūtra layer of this corpus and is the office of the Atharvan priest at the solemn sacrifice; the question here is whether the hymns say it, because a claim carried by the manual is not a claim carried by the collection." .

pmp:AyurvedaKalanirapeksaSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Lektion irgendwo ein Rasayana vom Kalender löst"@de,
        "Whether the lesson anywhere frees a rasayana from the calendar"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-21" ;
    skos:note "Limits. The calendrical apparatus the lesson uses — ayana, paksa, tithi, naksatra, muhurta, karana — belongs to a literature this lesson does not cite and this run did not open, and the translator's explanations of muhurta and karana come from there. What the lesson supplies is the binding; what would say why these units and not others is elsewhere."@en,
        "Six windows, and not one penalty. The entry into the hut takes four calendar conditions at once — the sun's northern course, the lighted fortnight, a propitious lunation and constellation, a favourable muhurta and karana — and the other five bind the handling of a drug: the amalaka powder buried through the rains, the nagabala roots dug in Magha or Phalguna in an auspicious hour, the bhallataka gathered in Suci or Sukra and administered in Sahas or Sahasya, the amalakas plucked by hand in Magha or Phalguna, the forest entered on the full moon of Pausa, Magha or Phalguna after three days of fasting. At no one of the six does the lesson say what befalls a user who acts outside it."@en,
        "To the second question the direct answer is no and the structural answer is yes, and the difference matters. Nowhere does the lesson say that a rasayana works whatever the day; the search terms for that returned nothing. What it does is offer, in the same verse that names the hut, a second route that carries no date — the route of wind and sun — and assign it in the fourth section to everyone who cannot meet the conditions of the first. It then adds a third, the rasayana of conduct, which has neither drug nor chamber nor date. The release is by omission and not by denial."@en,
        "Why that is worth a node rather than a remark. This holding carries a generalization that transmissions binding an operation to a calendar state the condition as a date and locate any stated failure in the time; its counter-material so far has come from a rival stream of the same tradition or from another work of the same author. Here the binding and the release stand in one lesson, for one operation, four sections apart, and the release is offered to the majority of users. See pmc:AdmissibilityBoundToTheCalendar, to which this lesson is a ninth witness and its own qualification."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of the normalised lesson for: month (20), season (10), full moon (2), new moon (2), lunar (2), constellation (1), muhurta (2), karana (9), auspicious (12), inauspicious (3), hour (2), day of (4), fortnight, northern course, uttarayana, udagayana; and for the other question: whatever the (0), regardless (0), at all times (0), any time (0), at any season (0), indifferent (0). Every date expression was read at its place." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmp:AyurvedaVatatapika ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-21" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana, Lesson I in its four sections, in the English of Avinash Chandra Kaviratna, parts XXXIII to XXXV, Calcutta 1904-1905, pp. 1015-1070, complete" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Two questions at once, and they were put separately. First, every place in the lesson at which an operation or the preparation of a drug is bound to a date, a month, a fortnight, a lunar day, a constellation or an hour, and whether any of them states what follows from acting outside the window. Second, whether the lesson anywhere says of a rasayana that it works whatever the day — a statement that the time is indifferent, or a route that carries no date at all." .

pmp:AyurvedaRasayanaLingaSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Rasayana-Lektion ein Zeichen dafür nennt, dass die Kur gewirkt hat"@de,
        "Whether the rasayana lesson names a sign that the course has taken effect"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-21" ;
    skos:note "Eight occurrences of the word sign and all eight are the same phrase: one lives for a period of a hundred years without any sign of decrepitude. That is the absence of a mark and not the presence of one, and it is an absence stated over a century. The lesson therefore names no criterion that any user of any of its some forty preparations could apply to his own case at any point during the course."@en,
        "Limits. One translation, one lesson, one work. The lesson refers five times to groups of drugs set out in the fourth lesson of the Sutrasthana, which this run did not open, and the Cikitsasthana has twenty-nine further lessons. Whether the Susruta or Vagbhata name such a sign is a different question and was not asked here."@en,
        "What it does name, and the contrast is the finding. The preliminary cleansing runs until the bowels are clear and the stools regular, and physicians conversant with age and constitution then certify that it has been properly done; the amalaka plucked under the recitation is to turn sweet in the hand; the man in the wooden vessel is to waste to nothing and grow again in six months. The transmission is perfectly capable of stating an observable endpoint. It states one for a preliminary, one for a recitation, one for a procedure whose herbs it concedes were already unobtainable — and none for the operation the lesson is about."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of the normalised lesson for: sign (8 hits, every one inspected); symptom (1); token (0); know that (0); he knows (0); thereby he knows (0); mark of (0); appears (inspected); perceive, perceives (0); within a month, within a year, within six months, after a fortnight, on the seventh day (the date expressions were inspected and all belong to the preparation of a drug, not to its effect). In addition the closing verses of all four sections and the two comparison passages at I.4.49-51 were read continuously." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-21" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana, Lesson I in its four sections — Abhayamalakiya, Pranakamiya, Karapracitiya and Ayurvedasamutthaniya — in the English of Avinash Chandra Kaviratna, Charaka-Samhita translated into English, parts XXXIII to XXXV, Calcutta 1904-1905, pp. 1015-1070, complete" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Anywhere in the lesson: a mark, sign, token or perceptible event by which a user of a rasayana would know, before the promised span had elapsed, that the course had taken effect; or a term within which such a mark should appear; or an instruction to the physician on what to look for while the course runs. The question decides whether the lesson's promises are examinable at all by anyone who takes one of its preparations, since every stated outcome is a span of a hundred years or more." .

pmp:AyurvedaRasayanaPariksaSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Lektion hinter ihren fünf Erprobungsbehauptungen einen Fall berichtet"@de,
        "Whether the lesson reports a case behind its five claims of having been tested"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-21" ;
    skos:note "Behind them, nothing. No person is reported as having taken a rasayana and been observed. The named users are Cyavana, the Vaikhanasas, the Valakhilyas, Vasistha, Kasyapa, Angiras, Jamadagni, Bharadvaja and Bhrgu, all of them figures of the remote past, and what is reported of them is the outcome and never the observation. The one thing in the lesson said to have been seen by men is that the seeds of kulattha break stone."@en,
        "Limits. The absence of a reported case in a handbook is weak evidence about a practice and strong evidence about a genre, and only the second is claimed here. One translation, one lesson."@en,
        "The five claims, in the order they stand: six combinations of rasayanas well tested by experience, closing the first section; seventeen well-tested preparations, closing the second; the administration of every one of these extends the period of life as has been fully tested by experiment, of the gold and silver preparations; the potency of this rasayana has been well tested, of the Aindri; and sixteen well-tested combinations, closing the third. Two more preparations are called well tried."@en,
        "This is the exhibited gap the class exists for, and it is a gap of a particular kind. It is not that the transmission was indifferent to trial — it uses the vocabulary of trial in six separate places and reserves it for some preparations and not others, which is what a writer does who thinks the distinction matters. It is that the trials, if there were any, were not written down, and the corpus that would carry them is not this one. A run that wants to know whether the physician's literature ever recorded a case goes to the Vimanasthana, which sets out how a physician and a treatment are to be judged, and this run did not open it."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of the normalised lesson for: tested (5), test (8, of which three are the word in another sense), experiment (1), tried (2), proved (1), observed and observation (12, all inspected), seen by men (1), case, instance (inspected). All hits read at their places. In addition the five summary verses that close the sections and the narrative frame of the fourth section were read continuously." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-21" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana, Lesson I in its four sections, in the English of Avinash Chandra Kaviratna, parts XXXIII to XXXV, Calcutta 1904-1905, pp. 1015-1070, complete" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A report, anywhere in the lesson, of a rasayana having been administered to a named or unnamed person with a stated outcome: a case, an observation, a trial, a comparison, a failure. The lesson asserts five times that its preparations are well tested by experience or by experiment, and the value of that assertion depends entirely on whether anything stands behind it." .

pmp:AyurvedaVyapattiSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Lektion einen Schaden nennt, den ein Rasayana selbst anrichten kann"@de,
        "Whether the lesson names a harm that a rasayana itself can do"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-21" ;
    skos:note "Four places and no more. A counted ceiling on bhallataka, thirty fruits a day and a thousand in all, with the drug described as keen and like fire and made nectar only by being given according to the ordinances. An interdiction of the seeds of kulattha with silajatu, grounded on an argument from what those seeds do to stone. A commentator's remark, reported by the translator, that the fourteen or twenty-six fruits by which two versions of the long-pepper course overshoot a thousand are not injurious — an answer to a question the text does not raise. And the one general clause: if in consequence of any deviation from the rules diseases set in, the course is suspended and the diseases treated."@en,
        "Limits. The lesson is a section on the healthy and the Cikitsasthana's other twenty-nine lessons treat the sick; the literature on what a drug does wrong may well sit there, and the Kalpasthana and Siddhisthana exist for the management of purgatives that overshoot. Nothing here is a statement about the corpus."@en,
        "The count is the result. Some forty preparations, among them iron, gold, silver, copper, coral, lapis, mercury-free but aconite-bearing compounds, and one drug the lesson itself likens to fire — and four statements in the whole lesson that any of it could go wrong, of which only the last is general. Against that stand five claims of having been tested and three claims that poison loses its force on the user's body. The registration's expectation of double examinability is half met: the promise is stated in falsifiable-looking figures, the toxicity is conceded in one drug and one pulse and nowhere else."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of the normalised lesson for: injur (3), harm (12), excess (6), should not (10), must not (0), never (9), avoid (3), abstain (1), forbid (1), interdict (2), hostile (3), baneful (2), suspend (1), deviation (1), poison (8), fire (inspected at the bhallataka verses). All hits read at their places." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:AyurvedaVyapadvidhi ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-21" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana, Lesson I in its four sections, in the English of Avinash Chandra Kaviratna, parts XXXIII to XXXV, Calcutta 1904-1905, pp. 1015-1070, complete" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Every place in the lesson at which a harm, an injury, an excess, an interdiction or a limit is attached to one of its own preparations, as against the harms it attributes to food, conduct and the excitement of the humours. The registration of this transmission named its double examinability — the promised outcome and the toxicity of the means — and the second half of that is only there if the transmission itself concedes that its drugs can hurt." .

pmp:BihuSaiyou a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Tür und Fenster schließen und wiederholt fragen"@de,
        "Shut the door and the window, and ask him repeatedly"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 13 (Yi jing bian qi lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 4" ;
    skos:definition "The procedure the same chapter names as the whole of the art when asked what the one thing is to which treatment comes down. Shut the door and stop the window; bind yourself to the sick man; ask him repeatedly about his circumstances, and go with what he wants. He who obtains the spirit flourishes; he who loses it perishes."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as a practice because the chapter states it as an instruction with a setting, a repetition and a stopping rule, and because it is the corpus's own answer to the question of what decides. It is also the counterpart to the passage in which a patient's report of relief is refused as evidence: here the patient's account is what is sought, and there it is what is set aside. The corpus uses the sick man's word for the diagnosis and not for the outcome, and the graph keeps both."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmp:BinaryPredatesItsReportersExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Zweiteilung älter ist als ihre Berichterstatter"@de,
        "Whether the two-way division is older than the reports of it"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that the naming of two Śākta branches as a right-hand and a left-hand ritual reports a division the Indian material carries, rather than imposing one it does not."@en ;
    skos:note "The state moved from procedure-without-cases past cases-without-deviation and to non-discrimination, and the middle step was deliberately not taken. A case has now been read, so the first state no longer fits; but reporting it as a case without deviation would say the claim survived a test, when what happened is that the test could not tell the two possible worlds apart. The finding is about the wording of the claim and not about the Indian material."@en,
        "Three divisions now stand in the graph and no two of them agree: seven ascending ācāras with the left above the right (pmc:AcaraLadder), three dispositions deciding competence (pmc:BhavaClassification), and two methods assigned by the deity worshipped (pmc:KalikapuranaLeftAndRightMethods). That is the shape of the actual answer, and it is more interesting than either yes or no: the material carries divisions in abundance, and none of them is the one the West received."@en,
        "What is still not established. The corpus has not been searched exhaustively, the tantras of the Kaula stream have not been opened for this question, and the modern scholarship on the construction of the category has been used only through the one work cited at pm:evidenceFrom. A reformulated claim is owed before this protocol can return anything but non-discrimination. See prima-materia#71."@en ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Searched for the opposite of the claim: a pre-colonial Indian source carrying the two-way division. Opposition was found and is now in the graph. Urban's account of the Kālikāpurāṇa led to the passages, which were then read in the Shastri translation rather than taken from him; the purāṇa announces the orthodox and heterodox methods as a pair, prescribes the left hand method for particular deities, and closes by saying both have now been stated. Urban also reports that across most Tantric traditions the pair marks a literal against a symbolic reading of the same prohibited substances — a second division, again without moral polarity. What was not found in either is a criterion of union against self-deification, or a division of practitioners into camps."@en ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "H. B. Urban, The Power of Tantra: Religion, Sexuality and the Politics of South Asian Studies (I.B. Tauris, London 2010), in the chapter on the sacrifice of desire, at the section on the origins of sexual rites and the right and left-hand paths" ;
    pm:examinationState pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, working from the passages recorded in traditions/saiva-sakta.ttl and from the Kālikāpurāṇa in the Shastri translation" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A pre-colonial Indian source that presents dakṣiṇa and vāma as a clean two-way division with a moral polarity, using union with the divine against self-deification as the criterion that separates them. One such passage refutes the claim that the division was imposed rather than found." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-09" ;
    pm:tests pmc:RightAndLeftHandNomenclature ;
    pm:undividedOutcomes "The claim treats two findings alike that are not alike. In the one, no pre-colonial text divides at all, and the naming invents its object. In the other, a pre-colonial text does announce a two-way division of methods in these very terms, and the naming keeps the pair while attaching a moral charge the text does not carry. The falsification condition below is met by neither, so the protocol returned the same verdict for both — and the second is now the established case (pmc:KalikapuranaLeftAndRightMethods)."@en .

pmp:Biqi a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Verschließen des Atems"@de,
        "Closing off the breath"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 8 (Shizhi), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 8" ;
    skos:definition "The beginner draws the qi in at the nose and closes it off, counting silently in the mind to one hundred and twenty, and then lets it out at the mouth. Neither the drawing in nor the letting out is to be audible to the practitioner himself, and always more is to be taken in than given out. With practice the count is raised by degrees, and in the long run it may reach a thousand; at a thousand, the passage says, the old grow younger and a day is given back for a day."@en ;
    skos:note "Correction, and the reason the edition rule exists. The earlier wording of this definition said that the retention is counted in heartbeats and gave no number. The chapter counts in the mind, not at the pulse, and it names both ends of the scale, one hundred and twenty at the start and a thousand in the long run. Neither could be checked while the citation named no edition, which is the harm SPEC §10 describes; the citation now names the one that was read. See prima-materia#45."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BaopuziNeipian ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmp:BlueVitriolToothacheExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das gezählte Zahnschmerzmittel die Spontanbesserung übertrifft"@de,
        "Whether the counted toothache remedy beats remission"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that a piece of blue vitriol placed in a hollow tooth drives out the severest toothache, held against the fact that toothache remits of itself. The count the source gives is the tradition's own and is treated here as the claim, not as the examination."@en ;
    skos:note "No modern work has been opened for this protocol, and none is cited. The state is therefore procedure-without-cases: naming a state that reports cases would claim a reading that has not been done, and the dental and toxicological literature on copper sulphate is the obvious place to begin and belongs to whoever takes it up."@en,
        "The author states that he does not know whether the remedy works on teeth that are not hollow. That limit is part of the claim and narrows the protocol rather than weakening it: a series run on sound teeth would test something the source never asserted."@en,
        "The population the count runs over is unsettled between the two printings and is entered as a dispute beside the claim. Until that is decided the ratio cannot be compared with anything, because a man counting his own toothaches and a healer counting other people's are not measuring the same thing."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, working from the passage recorded in traditions/european-folk-magic.ttl" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A series of applications on hollow teeth in which the pain is recorded before the application and at a fixed interval after it, with the failures kept alongside, showing no larger fall than in teeth left untreated over the same interval. The source's own ratio of one failure in more than sixty is the figure to beat and is not itself such a series, because it carries no untreated arm and no interval." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-05" ;
    pm:tests pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicVitriolYieldsStillung .

pmp:BodilyConditionDirectionExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Aufnahmebedingung immer als Verbot gesetzt ist"@de,
        "Whether the intake condition is always stated as a prohibition"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that where a transmission prescribes an operation on a body and addresses any rule to the persons taking part, those rules include states of recent intake and of strong feeling under which the operation is not to be performed. The question put to the holding was not whether the variable recurs — the claim was formed from four witnesses that it does — but whether the direction does."@en ;
    skos:note "One corpus carries both directions and it is already one of the four witnesses, which is the sharpest form the deviation could take. The Ars Notoria warns that no man presume to say the oration after too much drinking or luxury, nor fasting, without great reverence and discretion; and the same corpus prescribes fasting for the first day of a rehearsal. The node that carries the warning said so when it was written and left the two passages unreconciled. A claim formed from that witness and reading only its prohibitive half took the witness at half its word."@en,
        "The deviation, stated as the cases. The Solomonic operator does not avoid an empty stomach, he is required to reach one: nine days of continence before the circle, the last three on fasting diet once a day, better bread and water only, and nothing at all on the last, with the general clause of the book quoted as the cost of any defect (pmc:ClaviculaNovemDies). Whoever enters a greater Andean office fasts a month, in other places six, in others a year, without salt and without chilli (pmp:FastBeforeTakingOffice, pmc:OfficePresupposesTheFast). The Maya fast before the first month has a stated floor of thirteen days, with continence and the giving up of salt and chili added, and a stated consequence for breaking one begun (pmp:MayaNewYearFast, pmc:MayaSafetyPresupposesUnbrokenFast). The Jain transmission counts its fasting in meals rather than days and puts the abstention from well-dressed food and from eating to excess among the ten conditions of its celibacy, with a named consequence at each (pmp:JainaMahaviraUpadhana, pmp:JainaDashaBrahmacaryasamadhi, pmc:JainaBrahmacaryabhangaPhala)."@en,
        "The finding about this project's procedure, which is the same one prima-materia#667 recorded and is therefore not new but recurrent. The counter-search for this claim was carried before it was written, as SPEC §16 requires, and it was carried in the wrong layer: it asked which transmissions name no such condition and it did not ask which name it the other way round. A counter-search that looks only for the absence of the antecedent will never find a case that satisfies the antecedent and reverses the consequent. The general form is worth keeping: for a claim of the shape where X, the rule is Y, the negation to search is not only no rule but also the rule not-Y."@en,
        "The third sentence of the claim is untouched by this protocol and is not thereby confirmed. It says that where such a transmission states what follows from proceeding anyway, it states a disorder of the body and not a failure of the operation. Two of the deviating cases state a consequence and neither is a bodily disorder: the Solomonic clause makes the experiment not verified, and the Maya one makes evil come upon the faster or his house. That is contrary material and it is recorded here rather than folded into the deviation above, because the two clauses fail for different reasons and a later reader should be able to take them apart."@en,
        "What survives. The first two sentences of the claim stand and are strengthened rather than weakened: the variable is recent intake and strong feeling, it attaches to a person present rather than to the day, the place or the material, and it recurs across transmissions with no route between them — now across nine and not four. The clause that fails is the one that fixes the direction, and it fails because it was read off a set of witnesses selected from the caution layer. A claim formed from cautions will find prohibitions, which is a property of where one looked and not of what transmissions do."@en,
        "What the protocol is worth for examination, which is the reason to run it at all. The reformulated variable is still the one an experiment can set, and the deviating cases make it more so, because several of them carry a countable term: nine days with three inside them, thirteen days at the least, one month or six or a year, the sixth meal or the eighth or the tenth. One of them is even checked after the fact by the transmission itself — the Andean guinea-pig inspection that reads off whether the fast was kept (pmc:FastKeptWellIsAlsoChecked). A transmission that checks its own participants supplies the procedure a protocol would otherwise have to invent."@en ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Searched the holding for every transmission carrying a rule about eating, drinking, continence or strong feeling addressed to a person taking part in an operation, by term over all tradition files and by reading the nodes returned. Twelve files carry such rules. Of those read, four state the condition as a prohibition and are the witnesses the claim was formed from; five state it as a prescription and are the deviation; one corpus states it in both directions within itself. Not searched: the grimoire and ritual literature outside this holding, where the falsifying case the claim names would have to be found, and no modern study was consulted, so no pm:evidenceFrom is set."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-16" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A transmission that prescribes a bodily operation, carries rules for the persons performing it — purity, dress, posture, hour — and carries none about what they have eaten, drunk or felt. That is the condition the claim itself named, and it was not met by anything found here. The deviation reported below falsifies a different clause, the one that fixes the direction."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-16" ;
    pm:tests pmc:TheBodyOfThePartiesIsAStatedCondition .

pmp:BonFailureConditionSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der gZi brjid irgendeinen Ausgang nennt, unter dem von seinen eigenen Verfahren kein Erfolg zu erwarten ist"@de,
        "Whether the gZi brjid names any outcome under which its own procedures are not to be expected to work"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-09" ;
    skos:note "Four conditions of failure and one prerequisite with a quotable consequence, which is more than the six works opened before this one carry between them. Their distribution is the second finding: three of the four are in the causal Ways, the ones with a client and a fee, and none is in the five Ways of the fruit. A transmission states the terms on which its operations can be judged exactly where somebody is paying for one."@en,
        "Limits. One translation and one editor, and no second translation: the gZi brjid has been excerpted into a European language once, by Snellgrove in 1967, so the two-translation requirement of SPEC §15 cannot be met for this work at all and the reason is a property of the work and not of the run. The Tibetan is printed facing the English and was used to check that the terms at issue are single clauses and not the translator's expansions, but the run does not read Tibetan and says so. A stopping rule could also be phrased without any of the twenty-four terms above, and against that stands the first Way, which was read continuously and in full."@en,
        "The third finding is what the conditions do. Of the four, three assign the failure to a person and none to the procedure: to the patient by his karma, to the operator by his unperfected skill, to the offerer by his mistake over the items. Only the fourth, the demons who receive the ransom and may themselves err, puts a possible failure outside both parties, and it is forestalled by an explanation rather than corrected. The holding's own statement of this shape is at pmc:NoFailedRunCountsAgainstTheOperation and at pmc:TheExcuseThatStopsAtTheOfficiant; neither is enlarged by this run, because the counter-search against the holding that SPEC §16 requires before a generalization is touched was not carried."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of the volume over a whitespace-normalised copy, the first Way then read continuously in full and every hit elsewhere read at its place. Terms with no hit: of no avail, in vain, will not succeed, does not succeed, fails, not be able, if the rite, wrongly. Terms with hits: useless (two; one is the karmic cut-off at the close of the first Way, the other is a doctrinal use in the Supreme Way), will fail (one, the officiant and the deceased in the Way of the Shen of Existence), no effect (two, both doctrinal in the Supreme Way), mistake (six; one is the offerer over the ransom items in the second Way, one is the demons who receive them, the rest are the editor on the scribe and one is the behaviour of a perfected sage being mistaken for madness), signs of death (two, both the prognostic from the appearance in the first Way), before long (one, the sick man who dies under unpractised hands), if you do not (five; two are the lords of the soil in the Way of the Primeval Shen, one is the purity clause of the Thug-khar lore, two are meditation instructions), error (nine; one is the claim that the scheme contains none, the rest are doctrinal or the editor's), omit, incorrect (the editor on the manuscript)." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:BonGludMaNor,
        pmc:BonLasChad,
        pmc:BonPresupposesSaBdag,
        pmc:BonSmanDug,
        pmc:BonSoSkye ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-09" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "gZi brjid, the nine doctrinal chapters on the Nine Ways, Tibetan text and English translation entire, in D. L. Snellgrove, The Nine Ways of Bon: Excerpts from gZi-brjid, London Oriental Series 18, London: Oxford University Press, 1967" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage naming a result of a correctly conducted operation under which the operator is to conclude that it did not work or cannot work: a term after which nothing further is to be expected, a state of the patient that puts the case beyond the procedure, a degree of accomplishment below which the rite is not to be attempted, or a mistake with a named consequence. This is the material a pm:falsifiedBy can be formed from out of a transmission's own mouth, and the same question was put to the demotic manual at pmp:PdmFailureConditionSearch and to the Clavicula at pmp:ClaviculaFailureConditionSearch, both without result." .

pmp:BonRtsis a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Berechnung des Horoskops auf dem gefelderten Brett"@de,
        "The calculation of the horoscope on the squared board"@en ;
    dcterms:source "gZi brjid, the Way of the Shen of Prediction (phya-gshen theg-pa), the second of its four parts, on calculation, in D. L. Snellgrove, The Nine Ways of Bon: Excerpts from gZi-brjid, London Oriental Series 18, London: Oxford University Press, 1967" ;
    skos:definition "The second part. On a cloth of brocade silk the squared calculating board is set with its white and black pieces; the operator worships Ye-srid lha-dbang rgyal-po, requites the goddesses of the Elements and the Time-Periods, and prays to dBang-chen bdag-po. He looks in the mystic mirror of the horoscope, works the sPar-kha sMe-ba circle, calculates the cycles of the Elements and the Time-Periods and examines the combinations by the method ju-zhag. What he is to identify is listed: the past and present state, how it comes about from major and minor causes, how events and prayers have corresponded, the ways of change in Time and the Elements and the Four Seasons, the strength and weakness of gods, demons and klung-rta, an estimate of good and bad and of the length of life, and the characteristics of increase and decrease by years, months, days and hours."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GziBrjid ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bon .

pmp:BoranAnapanasati a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Atemübung für geistige Klarheit, mit dem Zeichen bis zum Meru und zurück"@de,
        "The breathing exercise for mental clarity, with the sign extended to Meru and back"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Yogāvacara's Manual, breathing exercise for mental clarity (trans. Woodward 1916, p. 67-71)" ;
    skos:definition "The exercise the work calls the chief aim of meditation. After the concentration is established the three thought-forms of the element of space are withdrawn from the nose-tip, observed inch by inch, and the line of observation is extended: twelve inches to a span, two spans to a cubit, seven carpenter's cubits to a pole, twenty poles to a cast, eighty casts to a league, a hundred and then a thousand leagues until it seems like the ocean, then the Seven Great Rocks, then the peak of Mount Meru, then the six heavens, the sixteen Brahmā worlds and Endless Space; and then the same scale is returned in order to a single inch and placed in the nostril. The same is done for each of the ten quarters."@en ;
    skos:note "The ladder is a scale of extent and the only quantified thing in the work besides the candle and the count. It is stated in units a carpenter would use at the bottom and in cosmological distances at the top, and the practitioner is to pass through all of them in both directions for each of ten quarters."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:YogavacarasManual ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BoranKammatthana .

pmp:BoranAnulomaPatiloma a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Jede Station in rechter Folge und dann in umgekehrter Ordnung"@de,
        "Every station run in right succession and then in reverse order"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Yogāvacara's Manual, the order due (trans. Woodward 1916, p. 6-16)" ;
    skos:definition "The rule of motion that governs the whole work. Each state is attained anuloma, in the way of right succession, and then paṭiloma, in reverse order; the thought-forms are taken from the nose-tip, fixed in the heart, then in the navel, and then placed one inch above the place of the station before, upward through the five raptures to the heart; and the reverse run puts each back where the forward run had placed it. The work applies the double run to the five raptures, to the twelve sub-exercises, to the five jhānas, to the ten devices, to the ten quarters of space and to the ten forms of knowledge."@en ;
    skos:note "The double run is what makes the work long and what makes it short to describe. It is also the only structural feature of the manual that states a completeness condition of any kind: a station is done when it has been held in both directions. Nothing says what a practitioner is to do if the reverse run does not come."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:YogavacarasManual ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BoranKammatthana .

pmp:BoranCustosForisSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Manual jemanden nennt, der außerhalb der Vertiefung bleibt"@de,
        "Whether the Manual names anybody who stays outside the absorption"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-26" ;
    skos:note "Not entered as a counter-case to pmc:TheOneWhoStaysOutsideTheState and the reason is in that statement's second limb. It provides for the transmission that binds the effect to something the practitioner can check on himself while he does it — an exact wording, a count, a prescribed hour — and says that such a transmission names no such person and may require him to be alone. This work has all three: a fixed request formula, a count of five to ten, and a candle clock he can hear. So it falls on the statement's own other side and confirms rather than opposes it. What the search establishes is that it falls there, which was not obvious from a corpus whose practitioner has his eyes shut."@en,
        "The living transmission, on the other hand, supplies exactly the figure the manual does not. The report at pmc:BoranSisyaUmmattako holds that the practice must not be taught by a guru who is not fully versed in it and able to see clairvoyantly what effect each meditation is having on his pupil, and names a pupil who went mad and died. That is a watcher outside the state, with the outcome made to depend on him, stated by the last people to hold the practice and not by the book they held. The split between a manual that prescribes solitude and a lineage that requires a watcher is the finding of this search."@en,
        "The search found material that runs against the expectation rather than a plain absence, which is why the outcome is the third value and not the first. One second person is named: a bhante addressed three times for pardon at the opening of the fivefold zest. His office then ends — the editor of 1916 states that the confessor's function ends at the foot of that page — and the recurring okāsa afterwards is, on her reading, the solitary adjuring himself. The preamble has the practitioner go to a solitary forest, the foot of a tree, or a lonely place far from the haunts of men. So the work names the person the statement predicts, at the door, and then sends him away and prescribes solitude."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Read at the wording and searched for: sir, bhante, confessor, teacher, guru, guide, master, pupil, sisya, attendant, companion, alone, solitary, and every occurrence of the formula Give leave and of okāsa."@en ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:BoranOkasaBhante ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-26" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Yogāvacara's Manual, the whole work as above (trans. Woodward 1916), and the translator's prefatory note of the same volume for the comparison."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A second person on whom the outcome of the operation is made to depend: a teacher who watches, an attendant who reads a sign the practitioner cannot read, a witness, anybody at all who is required to be present. The question is put because the holding carries pmc:TheOneWhoStaysOutsideTheState, which holds that a transmission whose practitioner enters a condition where he can no longer check the operation names such a person and states that without him it does not come off; and this practice puts its practitioner in exactly that condition, with a clock made audible because he cannot see it."@en .

pmp:BoranItipandamPuja a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Wachskerzenopfer: acht Stäbchen in der Kerze, jede Station endet an einem Geräusch"@de,
        "The wax-taper offering: eight sticks in the candle, each station ending at a sound"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Yogāvacara's Manual, the way of making wax-taper offerings for the fivefold rapture (trans. Woodward 1916, p. 24-27)" ;
    skos:definition "The exercise that measures the work. Pieces of wood are set into a wax taper at intervals of about an inch, the first section about two inches; the practitioner develops the thought-forms of one element and continues meditating until the taper has burned down to the first stick and the stick falls audibly, whereupon he rises, squats without touching the ground, speaks the formula for the next station, and continues. Eight sticks carry the forward run and eight the reverse run, and the eighth is the token by which he is to know that the way of attainment in right succession is complete."@en ;
    skos:note "It is one of the two features of the work for which the editors state that no route to Indian or Burmese Buddhism is known, which is why it is entered as a practice of its own rather than folded into the rubric it serves."@en,
        "The device is acoustic by design and the editor of 1896 says why: the practitioner cannot see the stick fall, so he is made aware of it by the noise. A meditation whose attention is withdrawn from the eye is given a clock it can hear. That is the most concrete thing in the transmission and it is the only measurement in it that a second person in the room could also take."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:YogavacarasManual ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BoranKammatthana .

pmp:BoranPericulumSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Yogāvacara-Manual irgendeine Kautel oder Gefahr nennt"@de,
        "Whether the Yogāvacara's Manual states any caution or danger"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-26" ;
    skos:note "Nothing of the kind in the work. The word fail occurs some ninety-five times and every one of them is the rubric that he has without fail performed the first duties, which is a formula of completed preparation and not a diagnosis. The word danger occurs five times: once in the translator's theosophical footnote, and four times inside the similes of the ten forms of knowledge, where the dangers are the content of the insight — the forest of wild beasts, the tank of water-sprites, the snake caught by the tail — and not hazards of the practice. Must not occurs once, in a note of the translator's about a technical term. Mad occurs once, in the translator's prefatory note, in the report carried at pmc:BoranSisyaUmmattako."@en,
        "The one caution the volume does carry is not the manual's and is held elsewhere for that reason: the translator's footnote compiled from a theosophical work of 1911, which says that arousing the centres below the navel is a process attended with great danger. It stands at pmc:WoodwardCakkaInterpolatio in originations/boran-theosophical-insertion.ttl. A run that searched the volume rather than the work would have found a caution and credited it to an eighteenth century manuscript."@en,
        "What the empty result is worth. The work is a liturgy of rubrics and every rubric is in the indicative: the element appears to him, the two images appear to him, the perception of the mental states appears. There is no conditional anywhere in it and no alternative branch. A practitioner using this manual has been told what will happen and has been given no sentence to read if it does not, which is a different thing from being given an excuse: the case is not covered, it is not raised."@en,
        "Where a run should go next. The Siamese branch of the transmission is reported to carry what is missing here: a manual from Wat Ratchasittharam whose pīti section has been published with translation, a manual ascribed to King Taksin dated 1775, and the Khmer material edited by Bizot, of which one text is reported to state a colour condition outright — that the embryonic objects must be red like a ball of blood, and that if another colour is mixed with it the form cannot be born. If that is at the wording it is the failure condition this manual lacks, in the same transmission, and the pair would be worth more than either alone. None of it was opened in this run and nothing about it is claimed."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "The whole work read at the wording, then searched case-insensitively over the full text for: beware, danger, dangerous, harm, harmful, injure, injury, must not, should not, do not, avoid, careful, caution, forbidden, unfit, unwholesome, and for the negative forms cannot, unable, in vain, fail, fails, failed, does not appear, do not arise, no sign, wrong, mad. Every hit read in its place."@en ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-26" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Yogāvacara's Manual, the whole work — preamble, invocation, the meditation of the fivefold zest with its twelve sub-exercises, the six pairs, the meditation on happiness, the breathing exercise, the exercise by devices, the ten foul things, the bodily parts, the ten recollections, the immaterial realms, the four highest states and the ten forms of knowledge (trans. Woodward 1916, PTS Translation Series VI)."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A caution addressed to the practitioner: a danger, a harm, a contraindication, a state of body or mind in which a station is not to be attempted, an instruction not to do something, a consequence of doing it wrongly. The question is put because this corpus states signs in greater number and greater detail than anything else in this holding, and a transmission that grades its stages by appearances has the most to gain from a rule about which appearances are not to be trusted."@en .

pmp:BoranTerminusSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Manual sagt, was folgt, wenn ein Zeichen nicht kommt"@de,
        "Whether the Manual says what follows when a sign does not come"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-26" ;
    skos:note "Nothing. The work has three quantified things and none of them is a term of that kind. The wax-taper clock measures how long a station lasts and its eighth stick is the token that the forward run is complete, which says when to stop meditating and nothing about whether anything came. The count in the breathing exercise — not less than five times nor more than ten — is a closed count with no provision for the tenth repetition passing empty, carried at pmc:BoranPancaDasaVara. The extension ladder measures distance. Repeat and again occur only in the rubric that the station is to be run in reverse order."@en,
        "One qualification on the negative, stated because it is the honest boundary of a search in a translation. The manual is a liturgy whose repetitions the printed text abbreviates with et cetera, and the elided parts are given in full earlier on the same page or the page before, as the translator's own footnote says. A condition of failure hidden inside an elided repetition is therefore possible in principle, and it is very unlikely: the elisions are of the request formula and the posture rubric, both of which are printed in full more than once, and a condition would have had to stand in a passage the editors chose to abbreviate as identical to another."@en,
        "The words counterfeit, false, illusion, deception, obstacle, hindrance and defilement do not occur in the manual at all. Imperfection occurs once and it is the translator's gloss on a term, not a state of the practice. That is the finding this run was looking for and the reason the protocol at examinations/the-sign-and-the-counterfeit.ttl was opened: the transmission the Visuddhimagga's ten imperfections belong to has a meditation manual of its own in which no imperfection is named and the appearances are the whole of the criterion."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "The whole work read at the wording, then searched for: again, repeat, once more, if not, otherwise, until, counterfeit, false, illusion, deception, imperfection, obstacle, hindrance, defilement, upakkilesa, and for every numeral and every occurrence of times, days and hours in the rubrics. Every hit read in its place."@en ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-26" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Yogāvacara's Manual, the whole work as above (trans. Woodward 1916)."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage telling the practitioner what he is to conclude or do when a station has been held and the appearance stated for it has not arisen: a number of sittings after which he is to go back, a rule for repeating a station, a state that would show the station was not reached, a false or counterfeit appearance, an obstacle of the kind the Theravāda commentary names as the ten imperfections of insight."@en .

pmp:BothSidesOfTheCriterionExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das unbeherrschbare Kriterium stets seine Misslingensseite trägt"@de,
        "Whether the uncontrollable criterion always carries its failing side"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that a transmission which fixes the judgement of a single performance to a physical event neither party can produce at will states what the event would have to be for the performance to have failed, and that a transmission whose criterion is an appearance announced beforehand states the succeeding side alone. The population is the set of criterion statements in the compiled holding; every node whose wording fixes an outcome of a single performance to a perceptible event was drawn and read at its place, and each was classified on two axes, whether a party to the operation could produce the event at will and whether the text states the other side."@en ;
    skos:note "A second opposition to the second clause was found on 2026-09-18, in a corpus this holding had opened at one piece and not at this work, and it is harder than the first. The demotic manual of London and Leiden carries a direct inquiry ascribed to a named priest in which the two answers are bound to two distinct appearances before anything is seen: if the thing shall happen the god is to come in his form of a priest, in his figure of a servant of the temple; if it shall not come to pass, in his form of a Kalashire. The criterion is an announced appearance, which puts it in the second column; both sides are stated, in consecutive clauses, which the second clause says does not happen there; and no further attempt is prescribed that would dissolve the reading, so the escape the corrected statement above left open is not available here. See pmc:PdmRamshauFormaDuplex and pmp:PdmBothSidesSearch."@en,
        "A tenth predecessor entered the claim on the day this protocol was written, from the insular Celtic satire whose stated outcome falls on the poets if the poets were in the wrong; it arrives with prima-materia#733 and is not counted among the cases above, because a witness added in the same run as the examination is not a case the examination found."@en,
        "Eight confirming cases beyond the seven the claim was built on, all found in the first search and all read at their places. The verdict rule of the Lower Congo ordeal names both outcomes and then names four further ways of not clearing oneself, which is more of the failing side than the claim requires. The Maya cane planted before a journey states both readings in advance and instructs the grandmother what to say in each case. The Mithraic report gives the highest initiation to the candidate who is still alive after the ordeals and an honourable burial to the one who has succumbed. The proxy dosing of the accused states what follows when none of the boys shows the signs, and what follows is not an acquittal but a fresh appointment of representatives. The reversed rule for theft and adultery states both sides of the same bark with the sides exchanged. The severed-channel prognosis of the Lingshu pairs each channel with a sign and a day. And the Sámi drum rule of sixteen seventy-four states health for the ring travelling with the sun and sickness for it travelling against."@en,
        "The case this protocol named as the refutation of the claim was found on 2026-09-27, in a corpus the holding had opened at nine nodes and not at this material. The second book of Jeu fixes each of its three baptisms to an event no party to the rite can produce at will — the wine to the right of the sacrifice becoming water, a sign in the fire of the incense, a sign in the offering — asks for it in the prayer on a stated condition, reports it as having come in the same moment, and says nothing at any of the three places about its absence. That is the falsifying condition of this protocol word for word. See pmc:JeuSignaTriaPetita and pmp:JeuSignumAbsensSearch, and prima-materia#602 for the run that read it."@en,
        "The corrected statement, written out here and not carried back into the node, because a claim is corrected where it is examined. Where a transmission fixes the judgement of a single performance to an event no party can produce at will, it states the other side; where the criterion is an appearance announced beforehand, it either states no other side or states one that a further attempt dissolves. What distinguishes the two columns is therefore not whether the other side is stated but whether the transmission lets a single performance settle anything, and in both columns the cases in which it does are the cases in which somebody other than the operator reads the event."@en,
        "The deviation, and it is a third thing the claim's two columns do not have room for. The assay in the fourth chapter of the Baopuzi fixes the judgement of the whole operation to a physical event: two hundred and forty zhu of the elixir joined with a hundred catties of mercury and put to the fire make gold, and where the gold forms the drug is complete. Nobody can make gold form at will, so the criterion is in the first column. The chapter then states the other side and cancels it in the same clause: where the gold does not form, the drug is to be sealed again and fired again for the same count of days, and then there is no case in which it does not form. The failing side is named and abolished, which is neither stating it nor omitting it."@en,
        "The opposition to the second clause, and it is the more interesting of the two findings because the claim asserts a silence and a silence is cheap to break. The magnetic literature announces to the operator in advance what he will perceive at the first question, and it names three outcomes and not one: the subject wakes, or he continues to sleep without answering, or he answers without waking. Of the first it says outright that somnambulism has not taken place and is not to be thought of again during that sitting. That is an announced criterion with its failing side stated, which the second clause says does not happen."@en,
        "The same search brought two witnesses for the first clause out of the same manuscript, and they bound the finding rather than adding to it. The pregnancy test of the verso — the woman's water on a herb overnight, scorched for no conception and flourishing for conception — and the assay that tells a genuine stone by whether a little of it ground with water removes the skin are both uncontrollable criteria with both sides stated, and both stand in the medical and pharmacological half of the work. The transmission that cannot register a failed inquiry states a falsifiable prognosis three columns later, and the difference is not the culture and not the century but what kind of thing is being judged."@en,
        "The two corrections this protocol had written out and left here — the failing side abolished by a prescribed repetition, and the failing side made non-binding by a further attempt — have been carried into the statement at the same time, because a correction that lives only in the protocol is invisible to whoever loads the graph. The protocol stays at cases with deviation: cases were collected, one deviates, and the deviation has now cost the claim a clause."@en,
        "Three near-misses, recorded because they bound the claim without touching it. The Sámi drum rule is given by two recorders and the later one weakens the negative side, augury of a favourable event with the sun and a doubt arising against it, so that two-sidedness can be a property of a recorder rather than of a transmission. The Shangqing account of the four men who compounded the elixir has two of them die of it and reads their death as the attainment, which is the failing side of an uncontrollable criterion being reinterpreted after the event rather than before it. And the sign of the closed breath in the eighth chapter of the Baopuzi, a goose down that does not stir, was set aside as outside the population: the operator controls his own breath, so the criterion is in neither column."@en,
        "What survives of the second clause after it, and it is less than after the magnetic case. The corrected statement said that an announced criterion either states no other side or states one that a further attempt dissolves; this witness states one and dissolves nothing. What can still be said is narrower and is about who reads the event: the two forms appear in a vision or a dream belonging to one person, that person is the operator himself, and the manual makes no provision for the case in which neither form comes. So the criterion discriminates in its wording and is certified by the only party with an interest in it — which is the property the corrected statement identified as the real divider, arrived at here from the second column instead of the first."@en,
        "What the protocol does not establish, and it is the same limit the claim states about itself. The sample is this holding, the holding was assembled by runs that found stated criteria interesting, and a criterion stated one-sidedly is exactly the kind a compiler passes over. The state is set at cases-with-deviation and not higher for that reason: what has been shown is that within a body of material selected for its criteria, the uncontrollable ones carry their other side, and that at least one of them carries it in a form that decides nothing."@en,
        "What the same corpus does with that statement two works later is the reason the second clause survives in a narrowed form. It rarely happens, the other work says, that the magnetic sleep is produced at the first sitting, and the first effects are often so little marked that they pass unnoticed, which is no reason to conclude the subject incapable; the operator is to begin again the following day, and the day after, for eight consecutive days, and only then may he form a definitive judgment. The failing side of the first sitting is therefore stated and then made non-binding, exactly as the alchemical assay makes its own non-binding. The two findings of this protocol are the same finding, arrived at from the two halves of the claim."@en,
        "What was done with it, and it is a narrowing and not a discard. The claim now carries an exception for the case in which the event is asked for as a favour of the party whose granting is at issue, and the ground is stated at the node: in the seven witnesses the event settles something between parties present at the operation and the loser has no say in it, while here the event is in the gift of the addressee, so that the operator cannot be wrong about the outcome but only unanswered."@en,
        "Why that is a deviation and not a refutation, stated exactly. The claim says that the transmission states what the event would have to be for the performance to have failed; this transmission does state it, in the words where the gold does not form, and then denies that the case exists. A reader of the claim would expect from those words a judgement against the operation and would not get one. The correction the protocol recommends is to split the first column in two: criteria whose failing side is a verdict, and criteria whose failing side is a repetition. This holding already carries the second shape under its own generalization about counted thresholds, and the two statements meet here."@en,
        "Why this is the more useful of the two shapes of failure this protocol has now seen. The alchemical assay names the failing side and abolishes it by prescribing repetition, which a reader can notice while reading the sentence. Here nothing is abolished, because nothing is said: the transmission narrates its own single successful performance in the passage that prescribes the rite, three times over, and a reader looking for the failing side finds no sentence to weigh. A silence is cheaper to produce than a cancellation and harder to see, and the first clause of the claim had no room for it."@en ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Two searches, run as two questions and not as one. The first went over the compiled holding for every node whose wording fixes an outcome to a perceptible event, classified each by controllability, and asked of the uncontrollable ones whether the failing side stands in the text; twenty-two nodes were drawn and read at their places. The second asked the opposite question of the announced-appearance half: which announced criterion states what the absence of the appearance would show. It returned one, from a nineteenth-century corpus, and that is the opposition recorded here. Not searched: anything outside this holding. The claim is about the wording of transmissions and the holding carries some ninety corpora, so the cheap search is the one that was run; a transmission outside it that states an uncontrollable criterion one-sidedly would still refute the claim, and nothing here makes that less likely."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-17" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A transmission that fixes the judgement of a performance to an event no party can produce at will and states the succeeding side alone, with no wording for the case in which the event does not occur. That is the condition the claim names for itself. It was not met: every uncontrollable criterion read in this protocol says something about the other side, and the deviation below is that one of them says something that is not a failing verdict."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-27" ;
    pm:tests pmc:TheCriterionNoPartyControls .

pmp:BrahmajalaLowArtsGroundSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Welchen Grund die erste Lehrrede dafür angibt, sich des Wählens der Tage zu enthalten"@de,
        "What ground the first discourse gives for holding aloof from the choosing of days"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-21" ;
    skos:note "Limits. One discourse in one translation of 1899, and the commentary of Buddhaghosa, which the translator quotes constantly in his notes, was not read as a text of its own."@en,
        "No ground of any of the kinds looked for. The clause is the same in all seven paragraphs and it is about earning: while living on food provided by the faithful, earn their living by wrong means of livelihood. The choosing of days is refused as a trade and the discourse says nothing about whether the trade delivers what it promises."@en,
        "One place in the same volume argues instead of listing, and it is not in this discourse. The translator's note at the paragraph on clothes gnawed by mice points to a birth story that laughs the superstition out of court, and another note points to a birth story that mocks the interpreters of dreams. The corpus therefore has the argumentative form available and puts it in its narrative literature, which is exactly where the forty-ninth birth story stands. The doctrinal text lists; the story argues."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of the volume for: lucky (inspected at every hit), unlucky, nakshatra, constellation, auspicious, low arts (the seven framing sentences read in full), wrong means of livelihood, holds aloof (all occurrences inspected), and the translator's notes at every one of the paragraphs in question. The lists at paragraphs 21, 22, 23, 24, 25, 26 and 27 were read item by item." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:TheravadaNakkhattaLowArt ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-21" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Digha Nikaya 1, Brahmajala Sutta, the Cula-, Majjhima- and Mahasila in full, paragraphs 8 to 27, DN i.4-12, in the English of T. W. Rhys Davids, Dialogues of the Buddha, Part I, Sacred Books of the Buddhists vol. II, London 1899, pp. 4-26, read continuously; the remainder of the discourse, on the sixty-two views, was read only as far as was needed to fix where the paragraphs on conduct end" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Any ground at all, anywhere in the seven paragraphs of the low arts, for holding aloof from the arranging of lucky days and the foretelling by constellations: a statement that the art does not work, an occasion on which it failed, a cosmology that rules it out, an exemption granted to the attained, or a reason of any other kind beside the recurring clause about livelihood. The question decides whether this passage falsifies pmc:TheRefusalAndWhatItRestsOn, which names it as a likely counter-case." .

pmp:BrahmaviharaBhavana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Kultivierung der göttlichen Verweilungen"@de,
        "Cultivation of the divine abidings"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IX (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "The deliberate raising and sustaining of a chosen affective state as a meditation subject in its own right."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmp:BurialDayExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob Tag und Jahreszeit der Bestattung etwas entscheiden"@de,
        "Whether the day and season of a burial decide anything"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the second of the six adversities of the burial book, that a going wrong of the year and the season makes the burial adverse however favourable the ground. A memorial of the seventh century, written on an imperial commission to revise the divinatory literature, sets against it a count: the burial books call one day of the sexagenary cycle the most inauspicious of all for a burial, and a check of the classical annals turns up more than twenty burials on that day."@en ;
    skos:note "The count is treated as evidence and not as testimony, and the reason is the functional line SPEC §14 draws rather than the date. It is a procedure with an outcome that could have gone otherwise: the annals might have shown no burial on that day, and then the memorial would have had nothing. That it was carried in the seventh century does not make it a witness to a transmission; it makes it an early instance of the only kind of check this doctrine admits."@en,
        "The state stays at procedure-without-cases all the same, and the distinction is the one this protocol exists to hold. What the count establishes is that the classical corpus, which the doctrine claims as its warrant, buried on the forbidden day more than twenty times. That is a case against the antiquity of the rule and not against its effect: the memorial does not say what became of those twenty houses. Setting the state to cases-with-deviation here would report the reach of a philological check as a result about the world."@en,
        "Two things a later run would have to establish before the count could be used at all, and neither was done here. Which day of the cycle the memorial names, and whether the burial books it checked are the recension of the burial book this graph holds — it speaks of a hundred and twenty houses of the art, and the text in the graph is a redaction of the fourteenth century. Until both are settled the evidence points at the doctrine and not yet at this node."@en ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Searched for the defending side within the works opened, and it is there. The burial book itself supplies the sixth adversity, whose commentary lists the delays, quarrels, poverty, wars, lawsuits and neglect through which a burial fails to happen, so that a favourable grave without a fortune is always attributable; and the commentary cites two cases the other way, a reader of graves who foretold the extinction of a general's clan and a duke of the annals who moved against a prognostication and died in the fifth month. Neither was traced to the works it names. Not searched: whether anyone answered the memorial itself in writing, and whether the later burial literature took up its count." ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "Jiu Tangshu 79, the memorial of Lü Cai on the burial books, juan 79, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text" ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "Lü Cai, in the memorial on the burial books recorded in Jiu Tangshu 79, seventh century; recorded here from the passage in traditions/chinese-divination.ttl" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A comparison of what followed burials made on the day the books forbid with what followed burials made on days they allow, over a set of burials fixed before the outcomes are known. The doctrine claims a difference; no difference falls against it, and a difference in the wrong direction falls against it twice." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-09" ;
    pm:tests pmc:SanjiLiuxiong .

pmp:CalendarFailureLocationExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob eine kalendergebundene Überlieferung das Misslingen in die Zeit legt"@de,
        "Whether a calendar-bound transmission puts the failure in the time"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that transmissions binding an operation to a calendar state the condition as a date rather than as a state of a party, and that where such a transmission states a consequence for acting outside the window it locates the failure in the time. The population is every node in the compiled holding that binds an operation, or the handling of the material an operation needs, to a date, an hour, a month, a lunar phase, a season or a constellation. It was drawn by a whole-text scan of traditions/, convergences/ and originations/ for the vocabulary of dates, and every candidate was read at its place and classified on two axes: whether the condition is a date or a state of a party, and whether the transmission states any consequence for missing it."@en ;
    skos:note "Eight confirming cases for the first clause beyond the predecessors, all found in the first search and all read at their places. The seventeenth chapter of the Baopuzi lays three calendars over one another for the entering of the mountains — the third and ninth months when the mountain opens, each of the five peaks closed in the years and months of its own phase, then a list of forbidden days by month and a third list of six days in a long month and five in a short one. The handbook of European folk magic prints a table of some thirty-two unlucky days of the year. The science of letters prescribes seven days of the lunation for its orations. The Key of Solomon fixes four of the seven capacities of its first book to a month, a planetary day and hour, a date in June, a phase of the moon and a span between midnight and daybreak. The rubric of the hundred and fortieth chapter of the Book of the Dead fixes its offering to the last day of the second month of the season pert, at the moment the sun shows himself. And the rasayana lesson of the Caraka binds six separate operations to a window. In every one of them the condition is a date and in none of them is it a state of a party."@en,
        "Five cases were considered and set outside the population, and they are recorded because the boundary is where this protocol could be wrong. Two are dates attached to something other than admissibility: the twentieth chapter of the Suwen reads the hour of a patient's death off the pulse by way of a season, which dates an outcome and not a permission; and the rubric of the hundred and first chapter of the Book of the Dead is tied to the day of the burial, which is an event and not a calendar. One is bound to a place and not a time, the prohibition on sewing new caribou skin on the island of Oqshoriaq. And two are the releases the claim already records at its own node: the fire phases that use no hour, and the man who guards the One and need not divine the day. To these the present run adds a third release, the route of wind and sun in the rasayana lesson, which is entered at the node rather than here."@en,
        "For the second clause the harvest is thin, and the thinness is the first half of the finding. Of the fifteen transmissions in the population, four state a consequence for acting outside the window and eleven state none. The four are the ones the claim was built on: the hours of dead qi in which the circulation of the qi is of no benefit, the going wrong of the year and the season that makes a burial adverse however correct the rite, the prohibited days of the Zhen'gao whose harm is done before the person notices it, and the mbattal days of the Mandaean reckoning. The chapter on entering the mountains joins them and is the eighth witness for that clause: whoever enters on such a day will assuredly be tested by the spirit of the mountain, will not obtain what he sought and will not accomplish what he set out to do, and the ordinary man who enters on those days meets with harm and falls in with tigers and wolves. The failure is located in the day."@en,
        "One case belongs to neither side and is the sharpest thing in the neighbourhood. The first-century treatise against the choosing of days argues that King Wu won his battle on a jiazi day and the tyrant lost his on the same jiazi day, the two armies facing one another with their standards in sight: one day and two fates. That is not a transmission of the kind the claim describes but a critic of them, and it is held at its own dispute node. It is named here because a protocol on this claim should say where the discriminating argument is, and it is not inside any of the fifteen."@en,
        "The correction this protocol recommends, written out here and not carried back into the node, because a claim is corrected where it is examined. The statement should be split in two, and the two halves have very different standing. First: transmissions that bind an operation to a calendar state the condition as a date and not as a state of a party — carried by every one of the fifteen cases, with no exception found, and strong. Second: where such a transmission states a consequence for acting outside the window, it locates the failure in the time — carried by five and unmet by ten, and true of a class so small that it should be stated as what it is, an observation about five transmissions, rather than as a regularity. A third statement is now available and the holding does not have it: a transmission that binds an operation to a calendar and elsewhere enumerates the causes of a failed session does not put the calendar among them."@en,
        "The deviation, and it is a case the claim's two columns have no room for. The sixty-fourth chapter of the Suwen indexes the depth of needling by the season and then names, for each season and each wrong depth, what follows: twelve clauses, each with a consequence stated in the same register as the prescription. The condition there is not a date and not a state of a party but a pairing of the two, the season with the depth; and the failure is located neither in the time nor in either party but in the mismatch, which the chapter names in so many words when it says that one who needles without knowing the seasons of the channels turns the with-the-grain into the against-the-grain. A transmission can therefore bind to a calendar, state its consequence, and put the failure in a relation rather than in a place."@en,
        "The opposition, and it is the reason this protocol stands at cases-with-deviation rather than at cases-without. The Key of Solomon binds its operations to days and hours — a whole chapter of the first book is given to them — and it is also the transmission in this holding that says at the greatest length where a failure lies. If having performed an experiment with due observance of days, hours and requisite solemnities thou shalt find it unsuccessful, it says, thou must assuredly have failed in some matter; and it names four causes for a spirit that does not come, the operator's fault, a troubled and windy sky, a rival exorcist elsewhere in the world, and the pure nature of the spirits themselves. Not one of the four is the time. A transmission that binds to the calendar as firmly as any in the population, and enumerates the accounts available for a session that produced nothing, leaves the calendar off the list entirely."@en,
        "What this protocol does not establish. The population is this holding, and the holding was assembled by runs that found stated conditions interesting, so a transmission that binds to a calendar without saying so in a quotable sentence is exactly the kind a compiler passes over. The state is set at cases-with-deviation and not higher for that reason. The ninth witness added on the same day is not counted among the eight confirming cases above, because a witness a run enters is not a case the run's own examination found."@en,
        "Why that is an opposition and not a refutation, stated exactly. The clause examined says that where such a transmission states a consequence for acting outside the window, it locates the failure in the time. The Key of Solomon states no consequence for acting outside the window; it states a consequence for acting inside it and getting nothing. The antecedent is therefore not met and the clause survives. What the case shows is how narrow the clause is: it describes the one kind of failure these transmissions almost never discuss, and it is silent about the kind they discuss constantly. Eleven of the fifteen say nothing about the window, and at least one of those eleven has a full apparatus for assigning blame that does not mention it."@en ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Two searches over the compiled holding, run as two questions. The first drew every node whose text carries the vocabulary of dates — day, hour, lunar day, lunation, new and full moon, fortnight, named weekday, season, month, solstice, equinox, constellation, planetary, auspicious and unlucky day, stem, calendar, zodiac — and classified each by whether the condition it states is a date or a state of a party; some forty nodes were drawn and the fifteen that bind an operation were read at their places. The second asked the opposite question of the same population: which of them says anything at all about what follows from missing the window, and where it puts the failure. That is where the opposition came from. Not searched: anything outside this holding. The claim is about the wording of transmissions and the holding carries some ninety corpora, so the cheap search is the one that was run; a transmission outside it that binds an operation to a calendar and blames the operator for a session held inside the window would tell against the second clause exactly as the case found here does."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-21" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "For the first clause: a transmission that binds an operation to a calendar and states the condition as a state of the operator or of the person operated on rather than as a date. Not met; nothing in the population does this, and the two cases that come closest are releases from the calendar rather than statements of it. For the second clause: a transmission that states a consequence for acting outside its own window and locates that consequence in a party rather than in the time. Not met either, and the reason is the finding of the protocol — almost no transmission in the population states a consequence for acting outside the window at all."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-21" ;
    pm:tests pmc:AdmissibilityBoundToTheCalendar .

pmp:CalfUnderTheVirginExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Prüfung des im Zeichen der Jungfrau geborenen Kalbes"@de,
        "Examination of the calf born under the Virgin"@en ;
    skos:note "Against the claim stands a plain confound that the protocol must carry: in the northern hemisphere the sun stands in Virgo from late August to late September, and in Scorpio from late October to late November, so the two signs pick out autumn calvings. Any difference in first-year survival between autumn and spring calves would produce the stated result without the sign doing anything. A refutation must therefore compare within season and not across it."@en,
        "It has a second condition the first one lacks, and the two are ordered: the claim says not only that the calf of the Virgin does not live a year, but that the calf of the Scorpion dies sooner still. An ordering between two outcomes is harder to satisfy by chance than either outcome alone."@en,
        "The cheapest examination available anywhere in this holding. A herd book carries dates of calving and dates of death; the sign follows from the date; the deadline is one year and is stated by the claim itself. Nothing has to be done to an animal and nobody has to believe anything."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-13" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "Calves whose birth falls in the sun's passage through Virgo surviving their first year at the rate of calves born in any other sign, and calves born under Scorpio surviving at least as long as calves born under Virgo."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-13" ;
    pm:tests pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicKalbZeichen .

pmp:CarriedBookletProtectionExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der Schutz des getragenen Büchleins etwas ausschließt"@de,
        "Whether the protection of the carried booklet excludes anything"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that the bearer of the booklet cannot drown, cannot burn, cannot die without the whole corpus Christi and cannot have an unjust sentence spoken over him. It is examinable because the claim is stated as an impossibility over named outcomes and not as an increase in safety."@en ;
    skos:note "No case has been collected in either direction, and the obstacle is stated rather than left to be inferred: the bearers of a cheap handbook are not a recorded set, and the claim is therefore refutable in principle and unattended in fact. That is a property of the record and not of the claim, which is why the state is procedure-without-cases and not no-procedure-devised."@en,
        "The claim carries over unchanged from the booklet of seventeen eighty-eight into the Reading printing of eighteen twenty, and in both it refers to the booklet in the reader's hand. A protocol therefore has to name which booklet a bearer carried, and both answers are covered by the same wording."@en,
        "The escape the wording does not take is worth naming, because the same corpus takes it elsewhere. Nothing here says the protection fails if the bearer sins, doubts or acquires the booklet wrongly. Where such a clause is present, as it is in the passages that assign a failure to a broken prohibition, every outcome is absorbed and no protocol is possible. Here the sentence stands bare."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, working from the passages recorded in traditions/european-folk-magic.ttl" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "One bearer, carrying the booklet at the time, who drowned or burned to death, or who died without the sacrament, or over whom a sentence later held to be unjust was pronounced. The wording admits no degree, so a single such case refutes the claim as it stands. A weaker form, that bearers meet these ends less often than others, would require the bearers to be counted before the outcomes are known." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-05" ;
    pm:tests pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicBuechleinSchutz .

pmp:CelticCriticalEditionSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die maßgebliche kritische Ausgabe des Glossars erreichbar ist"@de,
        "Whether the standard critical edition of the glossary can be reached"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-17" ;
    skos:note "A second and free path was found for the manuscript wording and is named here because it is not a work and cannot be cited as one: the Early Irish Glossaries Database of the Cambridge department, which prints diplomatic transcriptions of all witnesses with their manuscript places. It is a way to the readings and never a citation, by the same rule that keeps every URL out of a source."@en,
        "The ways SPEC 15 names were tried. Other volumes of the same series are digitised and were found, which is what makes the absence of this one a fact about the item and not about the series. Another transliteration of the title was tried in five forms. The series is cited inconsistently in the literature, as volume IV of 1912 and as volume 5 of 1913, and both were searched. The only digital record found is a catalogue entry for a modern reprint, in limited view, which is a rights restriction on a twentieth-century edition and not a path."@en,
        "What this costs the file, stated so that a later reader can size it. The Irish text of the entry was read in Stokes's edition of 1862 from the Leabhar Breac and the English in O'Donovan's translation of 1868; both are printed from the shorter recension, and every citation in traditions/insular-celtic.ttl names which. What has not been checked is whether the longer recension carries the same wording of the term, of the prohibition, and of the clause about the ends of the bones, all three of which this run found to be places where the translations diverge."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:corpusUnreachable ;
    pm:searchTerms "Searched under Sanas Cormaic, Sanas Chormaic, Cormac's Glossary, Anecdota from Irish Manuscripts, Anecdota IV, Anecdota volume 4, Anecdota volume 5, Meyer 1912, Meyer 1913, and under the companion volumes of the same series, which are present."@en ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-17" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Kuno Meyer (ed.), Sanas Cormaic. An Old-Irish Glossary compiled by Cormac úa Cuilennáin, in Anecdota from Irish Manuscripts IV (Halle and Dublin: Hodges and Figgis, 1912), pp. 1-128, which alone prints the longer recension of the Yellow Book of Lecan and is the edition the field cites by section number."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A readable text of the edition that carries the entry as section 756, so that the Irish entered in traditions/insular-celtic.ttl from the shorter Leabhar Breac recension could be checked against the longer one. The two recensions differ in length by about six hundred entries, and a holding that has read only the shorter one has read the shorter one."@en .

pmp:CelticEfficacyDenialSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das inselkeltische Material irgendwo bestreitet, dass die Verfahren wirken"@de,
        "Whether the insular Celtic material anywhere denies that the procedures work"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-17" ;
    skos:note "Nothing came back in that direction, and the absence is the finding. Both prohibiting witnesses give a ground and both grounds are about the offering. The glossary: whoever should practise them should have neither heaven nor earth, because it was renouncing baptism. The law prologue: they were abolished as profane rites, for teinm laegha and imus forosna could not be performed without offering to idol-gods. Neither says that the procedures failed, that they revealed anything false, or that the poets were pretending."@en,
        "The one thing in the neighbourhood that looks like a denial is not one. The editor of the verse tract remarks in 1891 that the whole treatise makes a young and arbitrary impression and doubts whether the description of the glám dícenn rests on old tradition, adding that the passage speaks in the imperfect and therefore of something no longer usual. That is a modern scholar on the age of a description, not a source on the working of a procedure, and it is recorded at pmc:TalamDiaSlugadh rather than entered."@en,
        "What that leaves standing is the strongest attestation of efficacy in the material, and it comes from the party with the least reason to give it. The one operation the prohibition keeps, díchetal do chennaib, is kept on a ground about its mechanism and not about its results: it requires no offering to demons, and it was acquired through great knowledge and application. A transmitting hand that sorts three procedures by what they require and forbids two of them without ever saying they do not work has attested them in the act of suppressing them."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Searched for: false, falsehood, lie, lying, deceit, deceive, deception, fraud, imposture, vain, in vain, no effect, without effect, nothing, avail, availed, pretend, pretended, feign, folly, foolish, empty, idle, superstition, superstitious, credulous, believe not. Every hit read in its place, and the whole of the glossary entry, the whole of the law prologue between pages 24 and 46, and the whole of the Welsh chapter read in full."@en ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-17" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Sanas Cormaic in the Irish of Whitley Stokes, Three Irish Glossaries (London 1862), and in the English of John O'Donovan, Cormac's Glossary, ed. Stokes (Calcutta 1868), both searched whole. Ancient Laws of Ireland, vol. I, the Senchas Már with its prologue and the editors' notes (Dublin: HMSO, 1865), searched whole. Rudolf Thurneysen, Mittelirische Verslehren, in Irische Texte III/1 (Leipzig 1891), searched whole, text and commentary. Kuno Meyer, The Triads of Ireland (Dublin 1906). Kuno Meyer, Finn and the Man in the Tree, Revue Celtique 25 (1904). Giraldus Cambrensis, Descriptio Kambriae I.16, in Thomas Wright's translation (London 1863)."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage in which the transmission, or the churchmen who transmit it, say that the procedures do not work: that they had been tried and failed, that what they revealed was false, that the poets deceived, or that the revelation came from the poet's own invention. The prohibition is the natural place for such a statement, and this holding needed to know whether it is there or whether the ground is something else entirely."@en .

pmp:CelticSuccessSignSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das inselkeltische Material ein wahrnehmbares Zeichen des Gelingens nennt"@de,
        "Whether the insular Celtic material names a perceptible sign that a procedure has worked"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-17" ;
    skos:note "One consequence for the file. The absence of a success sign is what makes the outcome of the glám dícenn stand out: it is the only place in this material where the transmission names an event by which a performance could be judged, and it is an event that falls on somebody rather than an appearance shown to somebody."@en,
        "The finding is entered as opposing rather than as a find because it tells against the question as it was put. The transmission puts its bodily signs on the fault of a judge, where a bystander can read them, and puts none on the success of a seer, where a bystander cannot. That division is worth more than the sign would have been: it shows the transmission is capable of stating a legible criterion and does not do it for the operations this file is about."@en,
        "The question came back answered about the wrong office. No sign of a successful divination was found anywhere: the glossary says that everything is revealed to the poet and stops, the law prologue says that he discovered every unknown thing proposed to him and stops, and the Welsh chapter has the answer buried in a turn of a word that a skilful observer must pick out, which is the opposite of a sign. What the search did find is a set of signs attached to judging: three permanent blotches on Sencha's face for each false judgment, a chain that tightened on Morann's neck when he judged falsely and expanded when he judged truly, and perfect fruit on the trees when Fachtna judged truly."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Searched for: sign, signs, token, mark, marks, blotch, blotches, blemish, light, shining, radiance, glow, heat, warmth, cold, sound, voice, appears, appeared, apparition, vision, knew, knows, revealed, revelation, colour, face, countenance, swelling, blister. Every hit read in its place."@en ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:BolgaGubrethe ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-17" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The same corpus as pmp:CelticEfficacyDenialSearch: Sanas Cormaic in Stokes 1862 and O'Donovan 1868; Ancient Laws of Ireland, vol. I (Dublin 1865); Thurneysen, Mittelirische Verslehren, Irische Texte III/1 (Leipzig 1891); Meyer, The Triads of Ireland (Dublin 1906); Meyer, Finn and the Man in the Tree, Revue Celtique 25 (1904); Giraldus, Descriptio Kambriae I.16, trans. Wright (London 1863)."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage telling the operator or a bystander what would be perceived when one of these procedures has taken effect: a light, a warmth, a sound, a mark on the body, a change in the sleeper. This holding treats signs of success as their own harvest category and counts them apart from conditions of failure, and a transmission with four named operations and no sign at all would be a finding of its own."@en .

pmp:ChannelPurification a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Reinigung der Kanäle"@de,
        "Purification of the channels"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)" ;
    skos:definition "A preliminary in which the channels are cleared by visualisation before the heat practice proper; the tradition's own form of the notion that a blockage is a place the energy does not reach."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:TibetanTantric .

pmp:ClaviculaFailureConditionSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der Schlüssel irgendeinen Ausgang nennt, der gegen seine Operationen zählte"@de,
        "Whether the Key states any outcome that would count against its operations"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-12" ;
    skos:note "Limits. English text, one editor, seven manuscripts blended; and a failure condition could be phrased without any of the twenty terms above — as a time after which the operator departs, say. Against that, the chapters that would carry such a rule if any did, the three graded conjurations of the first book and the first chapter of the second, were read continuously and not only searched."@en,
        "Nothing came back, and the shape of what came back instead is the finding. Every hit that touches an operation at all is an assignment rather than a condition: a fault in one point, a troubled air, a rival exorcist, the purity of the spirits, and — from the introduction, which the search turned up and the reading had not — the profanation of the secrets by disclosure, after which no effect can follow. Five routes out and no exit. The nearest thing to a stopping rule in either book runs the other way: we shall not desist from our work until the accomplishment of our will."@en,
        "The search is the ground of pmp:ClaviculaVerificationExamination and the reason that protocol could be set at pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate rather than at the weaker pm:procedureWithoutCases. Without a node saying which terms were put and what came back, the verdict would rest on an impression formed while reading."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of both books for: in vain (five hits, none of an operation: four are the imprecation against taking the Name of God in vain, one is the angel telling Solomon his prayer is not in vain); fruitless, of no effect, faileth, shall fail, leave off, give over (none); no effect (one hit, in the introduction, of secrets profaned by disclosure); useless (two, of speech); avail (four, none of an operation's outcome); wilt fail (one, of the operator's hand in writing characters); failure (one, inside a conjuration, of the spirits executing the will without failure); abandon, cease from, desist (four, of God abandoning the unbeliever, of the treasure-diggers being warned to cease, and of the exorcist who shall not desist until the accomplishment of his will)." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-12" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Clavicula Salomonis, both books entire, in the English of S. L. MacGregor Mathers, The Key of Solomon the King, London: George Redway, 1889; searched over the whole text and read at the wording where a hit fell" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage naming a result of a correctly prepared operation under which the operator is to conclude that the operation did not work — a term after which he is to stop, a sign that the spirits will not come, a number of attempts beyond which the art is not to be pressed. This is the material SPEC §15 calls a condition of failure and the only material from which a pm:falsifiedBy can be formed out of a transmission's own mouth." .

pmp:ClaviculaPurificationWaiverSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der Schlüssel seine Reinigung für Adepten ebenso erlässt wie seine Stunden"@de,
        "Whether the Key waives its purification for adepts as it waives its hours"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-12" ;
    skos:note "Limits, and they are not small. The text searched is the editor's synthesis of seven manuscripts and the chapters of the first book after the eighth are absent from one of them entirely; a waiver standing in a branch Mathers did not follow would not have been reached. The search was carried in English, so a term of art surviving only in the Latin or Italian witnesses was out of reach by construction. What the node establishes is that this text, which is the one most readers have, contains no such passage."@en,
        "The finding is an asymmetry and not a silence, which is why it is worth a node. The book does relieve the adept, in terms, and the relief it grants is bounded by its own wording to the preparation of days and hours; the ground it gives is that beginners lack faith in the experiments. Everything the same chapters require of the body — nine days, three days of fasting, the last day without food, the bath in exorcised water, the confession — is left standing for everyone. A transmission willing to call its own astrology a pedagogy and unwilling to say the same of its purification is saying something about which of the two it takes to be doing the work."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of both books for: Adept and adept (two hits, both in the one sentence of Book II chapter I); not necessary (two hits, one the same sentence, one distinguishing the experiments that need no circle); need not (one hit, of a sportsman killing no game); dispense (one hit, of what may be added to or left out of an experiment's apparatus, with no object named); exempt (one hit, of companions exempt from perils on condition of obedience); unnecessary (none). Further reading at the wording of every occurrence of fast, bath, continence and purify." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-12" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Clavicula Salomonis, both books entire, in the English of S. L. MacGregor Mathers, The Key of Solomon the King, London: George Redway, 1889; searched over the whole text and read at the wording where a hit fell" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage relieving anyone — adept, master, or operator in haste — of the nine days of continence, the fast of the last three days, the bath, the confession or the white linen, corresponding to the relief the second book grants from the exactness of days and hours. The question decides whether the transmission treats its purification as a condition of the operation or, like its astrology, as a discipline for beginners." .

pmp:ClaviculaThiefMarkExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das im Wasser geschnittene Zeichen am Dieb erscheint"@de,
        "Whether the mark cut in the water appears on the thief"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that after the sieve has been spun and the water stilled, the gazer sees the form of the thief, and that a sign cut with the sword of art across the face seen in the water shall be really found thereafter upon the person of that man. The procedure is fully specified by the chapter and the outcome lies outside the session: a named person, not present, is said to bear afterwards a mark he did not bear before."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at procedure-without-cases because that is exactly what it is. The book supplies the procedure and the outcome; this holding supplies no run, no report of a run, and no trial record in which the claim was put. The state says so rather than letting the absence read as a verdict."@en,
        "Provenance of the chapter matters more here than elsewhere. Mathers notes that the remainder of the chapter, which is where this procedure stands, comes from Lansdowne 1203 alone. The claim is therefore one manuscript's, and a run that found nothing would have refuted a branch rather than the transmission."@en,
        "The chapter offers no way out that the other chapters would have offered. There is no clause here that a wrongly done point voids the sighting, no term within which the mark must be sought, and no statement that an unmarked face means the operator erred. That is unusual for this book and it is the reason the protocol could be written."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-12; the condition is the chapter's own, no case is on record here" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "The person whose face was seen, examined after the operation by someone who did not attend it, bearing no mark where the cut was made; or bearing one where the operator cut nothing. The claim as the chapter states it leaves no room for degree: the sign shall be really found upon his own person." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-12" ;
    pm:tests pmc:ClaviculaFurisVisio .

pmp:ClaviculaVerificationExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob eine misslungene Operation überhaupt gegen den Schlüssel zählen könnte"@de,
        "Whether an unsuccessful operation could count against the Key at all"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that the operation of the circle brings the spirits to appear and obey. The verdict is read off the wording of the claim and not off cases, and the wording is the book's own: an experiment performed with due observance of days, hours and requisite solemnities which is found unsuccessful must in some manner be false, ill-arranged and defective, and the operator must assuredly have failed in some matter. The same clause stands twice more, in the chapter on invisibility and in the chapter on favour. Beside it the book sets three further assignments for a non-appearance — wind, a rival exorcist elsewhere in the universe, and the difficulty pure spirits have with impure men — and a series of conjurations in which non-appearance is the instruction to proceed to the next stage rather than the end of the run."@en ;
    skos:note "The one place this verdict does not reach is in the same book, and it is the second protocol below. The chapter on theft states that a mark cut with the sword in the water where the thief's face appears shall be really found thereafter upon his own person. That is an outcome outside the session and it discriminates. A transmission can hold an unfalsifiable general claim and a falsifiable particular one at once, and this one does."@en,
        "The state is set from the claim and not from evidence, which is what pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate is for. No study was cited and none was needed: the chapter that carries the clause says outright that the whole Key depends upon it, so the transmission is not being convicted of an implication it would disown."@en,
        "What a later run should look for before treating this as a property of the transmission rather than of one edition. The clause was read in a text assembled by an editor in 1889 from seven manuscripts; it should be checked against a single manuscript, and against the Colorno and Abognazar branches separately, since the chapters of the first book after the eighth are absent from Lansdowne 1202 altogether."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-12, from the chapters read at the wording in traditions/solomonic-grimoire.ttl" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A passage in any witness of this transmission that names an outcome of a correctly prepared operation under which the operation is to be counted as having failed, without assigning that outcome to the operator, to the weather, to a third party or to the nature of the spirits. One such passage would put the claim back among those an outcome can reach, and this verdict would fall." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-12" ;
    pm:tests pmc:ClaviculaOperatioYieldsImperium ;
    pm:undividedOutcomes "A session prepared and conducted as the third chapter of the first book prescribes, in which the participants perceive an appearance, and a session prepared and conducted in the same way in which they perceive nothing. The book covers both: the first is the claim coming true, the second is proof that a point was done ill, or that the air was troubled, or that another exorcist was calling them, or that the spirits found the men too impure — and in any case the instruction is to conjure again." .

pmp:CmChengfuSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der Korpus der Himmelsmeister die Lehre von der ererbten Last fuehrt"@de,
        "Whether the Celestial Masters corpus carries the inherited-burden doctrine"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-23" ;
    skos:note "Not one occurrence of the compound in any of the four. The corpus displaces penalties onto forebears in its own vocabulary and with its own reckoning — nine generations at the pledge rule, seven at the petition for the five tombs — and it does so without the term under which the neighbouring movement systematised the idea. That is the evidential half of the corpus correction; the doctrinal half is that the foundational document of this transmission calls that movement's founder a heterodox way, which is entered at pmc:CmZhangjueXiedao."@en,
        "What the empty result does not establish. It does not show that the two transmissions had no contact; the passage at pmc:CmXijiaoHu shows the corpus reasoning about its relation to a neighbouring teaching, and the outside chronicle at pmc:CmBuxindaoDispute treats the two movements as one kind. It shows that the scripture was not this transmission's canon, which is the narrower claim the file now makes."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of all four works for 承負, and for 承 and 負 separately in order to catch a split writing across a page break or an interposed particle."@en ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-23" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The four Daoist works of this transmission in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile: Zhengyi fawen tianshi jiaojie kejing (DZ 789), one juan; Lu Xiujing, Lu xiansheng daomen kelüe (DZ 1127), one juan; Chisongzi zhangli (DZ 615), six juan; Nüqing guilü (DZ 790), six juan. Whole-text search over all four, in the Chinese."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "The term chengfu, the doctrine that a person's misfortune is the burden received from the faults of his predecessors and passed on to his successors. The registration of this tradition named the Taiping jing among its principal works, and chengfu is that scripture's characteristic teaching; if the term stood in these four works, the registration would have had a ground. The question is not whether this transmission displaces a penalty onto ancestors — it does, and the places are entered at pmc:CmZhangxinSanfen and pmc:CmWumuJiuzu — but whether it does so under the doctrine and the word of the other movement."@en .

pmp:CmYanSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob dem Kranken gesagt wird, was er wahrnimmt, wenn die Eingabe gewaehrt ist"@de,
        "Whether the sick person is told what he would perceive when the petition has been granted"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-23" ;
    skos:note "Nothing for the sick person, and a detailed answer for the officiant. The search returned one sustained passage of prescribed perception and it belongs entirely to the other party: the code of visualisation at 2.23b tells the officiant what he will see on the way up with the sealed petition — the red pneuma from his own heart, the hundred li, the jewel trees, the yellow road of sun and moon, the purple clouds, the gate of heaven at one zhang eight chi. That is entered at pmc:CmCunsiKe and counted among the signs of success, separately from the failure material, as SPEC §15 requires. Nowhere is the party whose illness is at issue told anything he would perceive."@en,
        "The asymmetry is the finding and it runs against the grain of the transmission's own bookkeeping. This corpus is otherwise meticulous about the sick person: it fixes what he confesses, what his household pays, which days he may not be attended on, how many days a death in his house suspends the rite, and what is to be concluded if his illness returns. The one thing it does not give him is a way of telling, other than by getting better, that anything was done. The only prescribed perception in the procedure is the officiant's, it is specified down to the measure of the gate, and it is therefore a report by the one party whose competence the failure rules make the alternative explanation."@en,
        "The two hits on 效驗 are both negative and both belong to the failure side. At 009b of the code the outcome of an incompetent officiant is frailty and no proof of effect; at 2.22a of the manual the twenty-four titles of the body's officers are said to be all efficacious when called upon as the case requires, which is a blanket assertion and not a sign. A corpus whose only uses of the word for proof of effect are one denial and one blanket claim has not given the term any work to do."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search for 驗, 效, 應, 徵, 見, 夢, 光, 香氣, 瑞, and for the collocations 有驗, 無效, 效驗. Every hit read in its place."@en ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:CmCunsiKe ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-23" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Zhengyi fawen tianshi jiaojie kejing (DZ 789) and Lu Xiujing, Lu xiansheng daomen kelüe (DZ 1127), both complete; Chisongzi zhangli (DZ 615), juan 1 and 2 complete, juan 3 to 6 by term. Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, in the Chinese."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A sign of success announced in advance to the party the rite is performed for: something the sick person or his household would see, feel or hear by which the granting of the petition could be known apart from the recovery itself. The corpus states failure signs in quantity, so the question is whether the two sides are stated at the same strength."@en .

pmp:CmZhangqiSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Eingabe eine Frist traegt, innerhalb derer sie beantwortet wird"@de,
        "Whether the petition carries a term within which it is answered"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-23" ;
    skos:note "No term anywhere. The counted days that the search turns up are of two other kinds and of no other. They are pollution intervals, which say when a petition may be sent and not when it will be answered, and they are calendar prohibitions, which say on which days it may not be sent. The manual counts a hundred days for a death in the house, forty for a year's mourning, three for entering a birth room, seven before a household may enter the parish after a beast has given birth, a hundred before a newborn child may; and it prints five separate tables of forbidden days. Not one of these counts forward from the rite."@en,
        "What the absence sits next to is what makes it worth an exhibited gap. The failure of a petition is not merely unmeasured in this corpus, it is the trigger for the next petition: the citation of the Taizhen code at pmc:CmBuyuJizhang runs through some fourteen stages, each entered on the failure of the one before, and at no stage does it say how long one waits before deciding that a stage has failed. The officiant therefore has a complete instruction for what to do when nothing happens and no instruction for when to conclude that nothing has happened. A term would have supplied the one thing that could close the series, and the corpus that counts pollution to the day does not state one."@en,
        "Where a later run should look before treating this as settled. The Nüqing guilü was searched by term and not read, and juan 3 to 6 of the manual were searched and not read; the terms above would have caught an explicit interval but not a term stated by circumlocution. The registers and ordination texts of the Zhengyi fawen group, nine of which survive under separate titles, were not opened at all."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search of the three works for 三日, 七日, 百日, 日中, 期, 限, 剋期, 效, 驗, 差, 愈, 不愈, 不差, and every numeral followed by 日 in the opening two juan of the manual. Every hit read in its place."@en ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-23" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Zhengyi fawen tianshi jiaojie kejing (DZ 789), one juan, and Lu Xiujing, Lu xiansheng daomen kelüe (DZ 1127), one juan, both read complete; Chisongzi zhangli (DZ 615), juan 1 and 2 read complete and juan 3 to 6 searched by term. Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, in the Chinese."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A stated interval between the sending up of a petition and the effect it is sent for: a number of days after which the sick person is expected to mend, a term after which the petition is held to have failed, or any period at the end of which a verdict could be taken. The corpus counts days in quantity elsewhere — a hundred days of pollution for a death in the house, three days for entering a birth room, one day for a beast — so a term, if the transmission had one, would be stated in the same way."@en .

pmp:ColorisLocusHypothesisSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Misslingensbedingung dem Ort der Farbe folgt"@de,
        "Whether the failure condition follows where the colour appears"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-26" ;
    skos:note "A second case bounds it from the other side and was inspected in the same pass. pmc:NeidanZhengyan grades by an announced sequence of appearances, several of them colours, and the same work carries its failure side in the adjacent chapter together with the most exactly quantified tolerance in this holding at pmc:NeidanSanwanke. It is not a counter-case to the drafted wording, because its sequence is graded by events rather than by a colour scale, but it shows that the class the drafted statement was carving out would have contained two texts and excluded everything that looks like them."@en,
        "The refuting case is pmp:SteinerUebungAmSamen in traditions/anthroposophy.ttl and it was found on the first pass. Its colours are inner and expressly not seen as physical eyes see colours — a flame-formation felt as lilac-blue in the middle and bluish at the edge from the seed, greenish-blue and yellowish-red from the dying plant — and they are announced in advance in the same sentence as the instruction, which is the condition the drafted statement made its whole point of. And the same chapter states what the drafted statement said such a transmission does not: that many will take it for illusion and some will fall away, that everything then depends on having the courage to press forward, and that the pupil must continually cultivate the sense which distinguishes truth from deception and examine again and again whether he has fallen out of his equilibrium. Two cautions in the holding already point at that practice. So an inner colour sequence can carry a caution, an authenticity test and an operator-located excuse, and the location of the colour does not decide anything."@en,
        "The statement was therefore not written, and this node is what the run has instead. That is the point of recording it: a hypothesis refused before it was entered leaves nothing in the holding unless the refusal is entered, and the next run would form the same hypothesis from the same two works. The one thing that does survive the search is not a generalization but a single case, and it is carried as a protocol: this manual grades by inner appearances and names no counterfeit, which refutes pmc:TheSignTakenForTheAttainment. See examinations/the-sign-and-the-counterfeit.ttl."@en,
        "What the search says about the method and not about the colours. The drafted statement was formed from the two works read in one batch, which is the exact situation SPEC §16 warns about: whoever generalizes from what he has just read has read the class the evidence comes from and not the class the counter-case lies in. The counter-case here came from a tradition file opened four days earlier by another run, and one query over the compiled graph returned it."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Query over the compiled graph for every node whose skos:definition contains colour or color, which returned thirty-six; each read at the node. Then the nodes returned were sorted by whether the colour appears in a material or in the practitioner, and those in the second class were read for any caution, criterion, term or excuse standing beside them."@en ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:BoranPancajhanaVanna ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-26" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The compiled holding of this graph as of 2026-09-26, every node whose definition names a colour, read at the node; and the wording of the two works read in this batch, the Turba philosophorum and the Yogāvacara's Manual."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "Material that would refute a statement this run had drafted and did not write: that where an operative transmission grades the stages of its own operation by a sequence of colours, whether it states a condition under which a stage counts as not reached follows where the colour appears — a transmission whose colours appear in a material outside the practitioner stating such a condition, and one whose colours appear within the practitioner stating the sequence alone. The counter-search required by SPEC §16 before a generalization is written, run against the holding as that section requires and not against the literature."@en .

pmp:CountedThresholdExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob eine Überlieferung, die eine Zahl festsetzt, auch für deren Ablauf vorsorgt"@de,
        "Whether a transmission that fixes a count also provides for the count running out"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that where a transmission fixes a number of repetitions at which a prescribed operation is to take effect, and the effect is not establishable apart from the operation itself, the same transmission provides in advance for the number being reached without the effect, either by prescribing continuation past the number or by assigning the shortfall to a nameable act of the operator. Two questions were put: whether a transmission inside the antecedent supplies no provision at all, which is the falsifying case; and whether one supplies a provision of a third kind the claim's enumeration does not carry."@en ;
    skos:note "A second limitation, and it is about the reading rather than the tablet. This work has no reachable translation; the two editions that carry one are in print. The absence of a conditional clause in its fourth column was established on the critical transliteration read against the editors' own German summary of contents, which confirms the order of acts and the counts and does not translate the wording. A negative claim about a text read without a translation is weaker than a positive one, and this is a negative claim. The search is at pmp:HittSecondTranslationSearch."@en,
        "The deviating case, and what it is exactly. The ritual of Ḫantitaššu is performed against years that have been set in motion, which is a condition no outcome of the rite could establish, and it fixes five counts: the recitation three times, the incantation seven times, three libations at the pit and at each offering, seven days of the patient carrying the enchanted things, and the conclusion on the ninth day. It names no sign of success and no sign of failure. And it supplies nothing for the case of the counts being completed without the effect: the fourth column, which is where such a clause would stand, runs from the last offering through the disposal of the bundle and the restrictions on where and by whom the rite may be performed to the colophon, without a conditional clause of any kind. The node is pmc:HittHantitassuCounts."@en,
        "The limitation on the deviating case, so that it is not claimed harder than it can bear, and it is the one thing that could still save the claim. The fourth column of this ritual is preserved to the end in both exemplars and in the second version on a third manuscript, so the absence is established where it matters most. The first and second columns and the third have gaps, and a clause of this kind would be unusual but not impossible there: the recitation counts stand in the second column, and a provision attached to a count is more often beside it than at the end of the tablet. What a later run would have to do is read the gaps of the two further versions of the same ritual, CTH 395.2.2 and CTH 395.3, against the preserved text of this one."@en,
        "What the pair is worth beyond the verdict, and it is the reason the run did not simply enter the witness and move on. The two tablets come from one tradition, neither practitioner is from the other's city, the afflictions are unrelated, and the two do opposite things with a count. That is the strongest form a deviation can take, because it cannot be explained by the tradition: whatever makes a transmission provide for its own empty run, it is not something a tradition has or lacks as a whole."@en,
        "What this case does to the claim, stated without softening it. The claim's consequent is a disjunction of two provisions, and this transmission supplies neither. So the claim is false as a universal statement about transmissions inside its antecedent, and the honest reading of it from here on is as a recurring device rather than a rule. That is the second reading the holding has already had to adopt once, for a neighbouring claim, and it is what prima-materia#812 is about: a middle clause that enumerates forms is the part of these statements that keeps failing, and the enumeration here did not even have to be widened, because the case supplies nothing to enumerate."@en,
        "What was not done and should be. The three witnesses the claim was formed from are Chinese and were not re-read here; this protocol takes them as the claim's author entered them. If any of the three turns out on re-reading to supply its provision less plainly than the Anatolian witness does, the claim loses its spread as well as its universality, and the queue of SPEC 16 would then want it re-formed rather than examined. That is a reading of three nodes and not of three corpora, and it is the cheapest next step from here."@en ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "The confirming witness first, because it is the one that sets the standard the deviating case is measured against. The ritual of Paškuwatti fixes three days of invocation with three entreaties a day, announces its sign as the deity appearing in the dream and sleeping with the patient, and closes with the case in which that does not happen: the practitioner keeps performing the rite. It is the clearest of the four witnesses of the claim, because the continuation is stated by the practitioner in the first person about her own practice, and not inferred from a rubric. The node is pmc:HittIssiskemiPat."@en,
        "The search was over the five Anatolian practitioner rituals read at the wording in this run, each of which fixes at least one count, and over the whole ritual section of a machine-readable corpus of the language for the clause shapes. It asked both questions. For the first it returned a case at once, in the second work of the five. For the second it returned nothing: no Anatolian witness assigns a shortfall to a party the claim's enumeration does not already allow. Not searched: the rest of this holding, whose transmissions were read for the claim when it was written; and nothing outside it."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-06" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A transmission that fixes a count at which a prescribed operation is to take effect, whose effect is not establishable apart from the operation, and that supplies no provision for the count being completed without the effect, neither by prescribing continuation nor by assigning the shortfall to an act of the operator. The condition is met, by the ritual of Ḫantitaššu, and the limitation on that case is stated below."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-10-06" ;
    pm:tests pmc:CountedThresholdNeverFallsDue .

pmp:CritiqueRigoureuse a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die strenge Kritik einer Mitteilung"@de,
        "Rigorous critique of a communication"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Allan Kardec, Le Livre des Médiums, seconde partie, chapitre XXIV, sections 266-267" ;
    skos:definition "The operation the founder offers as the means of that distinction, and he claims for it both exclusivity and certainty in one sentence: this means is the only one, but it is infallible, because there is no bad communication that can withstand a rigorous critique. The marks it works with are given as a numbered list, among them that good spirits never command but advise; that they do not flatter, while bad ones praise extravagantly and stimulate vanity while preaching humility; that an elevated spirit does not contradict itself across time and place; and that good spirits say only what they know and are silent or confess their ignorance, while bad ones speak of everything with a false assurance."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim of infallibility is the strongest single sentence this transmission makes and the one most open to examination, because it asserts that no false communication can survive the procedure. It is entered in the graph as it stands, without softening, since the value of holding it is that it can be held against cases."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Spiritism .

pmp:DicetalDoChennaib a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Díchetal do chennaib, der Vortrag aus den Enden"@de,
        "Díchetal do chennaib, the recital from the ends"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Prologue to the Senchas Már, in Ancient Laws of Ireland, vol. I (Dublin: HMSO, 1865), p. 45",
        "Sanas Cormaic s.v. Imbas forosnai, Irish text in Stokes, Three Irish Glossaries (London 1862), p. 25, English in Cormac's Glossary, trans. O'Donovan (Calcutta 1868), p. 95" ;
    skos:altLabel "dicetal do chendaib"@en,
        "díchetal do chennaib"@en ;
    skos:definition "The third of the three, and the one the prohibition leaves standing. The glossary says it was left to be composed in right of the art, and gives the reason: it is not necessary in it to make an offering to demons, but there is a revelation at once from the ends. The law prologue describes what remained after the abolition: when the poet sees the person or thing before him he makes a verse at once with the ends of his fingers, or in his mind without studying, and he composes and repeats at the same time."@en ;
    skos:note "The Irish of the glossary at this place reads do chennaib cnáime, from the ends of the bones, where the English of 1868 has from the ends of the poet's fingers. The difference is not settled here; it is recorded because the two readings put the operation in different parts of the body and because the name of the procedure is built on the word at issue."@en,
        "The exemption is argued and not merely declared, and the argument is about the mechanism. Two procedures are forbidden because they require an offering; this one is kept because it does not, and the law prologue adds a second ground, that it was acquired through great knowledge and application. A transmission that sorts its own operations by what they require rather than by what they achieve has stated a criterion a later reader can apply to the other two."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InsularCeltic .

pmp:DisplacedPenaltyCounterSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob eine verschobene Strafe die Regel dem Prüfen entzieht"@de,
        "Whether a displaced penalty puts the rule beyond checking"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-11" ;
    skos:note "Limits. This was a search of the holding and not of any corpus, so every proportion in it is a proportion of what has been entered, which is a selection made by earlier runs of this project for reasons of their own. A shape that is a minority in the holding may be the majority in the world, or the reverse. The statement is accordingly falsified by a count and says so."@en,
        "The answer to the first question was that the displaced form is real and that the witnesses cluster in two corpora, the burial literature and the needling canon, with scattered instances elsewhere. The answer to the second was a proportion: four of forty stated consequences of a skipped prerequisite displace outright, and nine to eleven of some fifty cautions, so the displaced form is a substantial minority and not the rule. Both answers went into the statement."@en,
        "The answer to the third question was yes, and it removed the half of the statement that had been the reason for writing it. A purification performed by the unqualified is said to make the sickness and the death in the world stronger than before, and the holding already notes that a claim of active worsening is decidable where a claim of failure is not. A burial doctrine that fixes a class of its effects within a day of a public dated event had a count of more than twenty such burials set against it in a seventh-century memorial. A felling prohibition names the man, sets ten days and denies the natural explanation in advance. The common feature is a direction and a term, and that feature became the closing clause of the statement instead of the claim it replaced."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Sweep of all sixty-six tradition files and the four further data directories, over the forty instances of pm:consequenceOfSkipping and the roughly fifty nodes of class pm:Cautioning, and a literal-level scan for the co-occurrence of a fault with a harm borne by a named other party, using among others: falls on, ill-luck, penalty, harm, descendants, ancestors, generations, family, the dead, rebirth, debt, curse, misfortune, barren, sickness." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:ThePenaltyLaidOnAnotherParty ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-11" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The compiled prima-materia holding as it stood on 2026-09-11: all tradition files, the examinations, the generalizations, the convergences and the originations, read at the literals of skos:definition, skos:note and pm:consequenceOfSkipping" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Three questions put together before writing pmc:ThePenaltyLaidOnAnotherParty. First, further witnesses in which the stated cost of a faulty or improperly transmitted performance falls on someone other than the performer. Second, the counter-cases in which it falls squarely on the performer, in order to learn the proportion and so the strength the statement could carry. Third, and this is the one that mattered, whether the holding contains a case in which a displaced penalty is nevertheless checked, reported back or made observable, which would falsify the second half of the statement as drafted." .

pmp:DisqualifyingFaultOutsideTheOperationSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der Fehler, der dem Übenden sein Vermögen kostet, stets außerhalb des Verfahrens liegt"@de,
        "Whether the fault that costs a practitioner his faculty always lies outside the operation"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-17" ;
    skos:note "The candidate was refuted and was not written. Two nodes that had stood in the holding for weeks put the disqualifying fault squarely inside the operation. At the gate of the sixth palace the descender is stoned and declared unfit for asking a question about the water, which is an act performed in the middle of the procedure and nowhere else. And the appended instruction of the Mithras rite states that the immortalization takes place three times in the year and that whoever, having received the instruction, disregards it, will have it come about for him no longer — a forfeiture attached to the frequency of the operation itself."@en,
        "The verdict is worth keeping because the mistake behind it is repeatable. The two witnesses the candidate was formed from were both read in the same two runs, one of them this one, and both happened to be transmissions in which the operative capacity belongs to an office with a legal or monastic standing beside it. Generalizing from them was generalizing from the class the reading had just been in, which is the exact confusion SPEC 16 records for the four generalizations of 2026-09-09."@en,
        "What survives of the candidate is narrower and is not a generalization. Two transmissions in this holding attach the loss of an operative capacity to a fault adjudicated in another forum, and the insular Celtic case is the sharper of the two because the fault is a false judgment given in court and the loss is of imbas forosnai and teinm laída by name. That is a pair and not a pattern, and it is recorded here so that a third case would be recognised if one turns up."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchTerms "The compiled graph searched over every literal for: forfeit, forfeited, disqualify, disqualified, unfit, deprived, debarred, no longer, loses, lost the, may not practise, ceases to be. Every hit read at its node."@en ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:HekhalotDescentPresupposesFitness,
        pmc:PgmIvTerInAnno ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-17" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The compiled holding as of 2026-09-17, queried over all traditions, examinations, convergences and originations for claims in which a practitioner is said to lose, forfeit or be barred from an operative capacity."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "Counter-material to a generalization this run had formed and had not yet written: that where a transmission makes an operative capacity depend on the standing of the practitioner, the fault that costs him the capacity is one committed and judged outside the operation — a judgement given in court, a motive for exercising the art, a breach of the rule of an order — rather than an error in the performance. The insular Celtic law prologue and the Jain penalty on living by the arts had supplied the two witnesses; SPEC 16 requires that the counter-search run against the holding before the node is written."@en .

pmp:DivinationByThrownThings a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Wahrsagen durch geworfene Dinge"@de,
        "Divination by things thrown on the ground"@en ;
    dcterms:source "A. B. Ellis, The Ewe-speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1890), p. 97" ;
    skos:definition "The commoner form outside the oracle proper: short sticks a few inches long, pieces of knotted cord, or a handful of cowries or nuts are thrown on the ground and the answer is drawn from the position in which they fall. A less common mode is named beside it, in which a fowl or an animal is impaled and the answer drawn from how long it takes to die."@en ;
    skos:note "The second mode is the reason for the node. An answer read off the time a creature takes to die is fixed to an outcome the operator does not steer, like the poison ordeal and the casting, but it is measured in duration rather than in a binary sign, and this graph holds no other case of that."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by Ewe informants of the western and eastern tribes, unnamed; recorded by A. B. Ellis, colonial officer on the West African coast, from his own residence and from earlier printed reports he names as he uses them, published 1890; given in Ewe, of which the work prints the terms with etymologies, the accounts in English." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Vodun .

pmp:EgyptianAssessorNameSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Zweiundvierzig von Kapitel 125 ein fester Namensbestand sind"@de,
        "Whether the forty-two of chapter 125 are a fixed set of names"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-19" ;
    skos:note "A second result of the same search, and the reason it was carried at all. Thirty-nine of the forty-two Ani forms are confirmed under a vocabulary headword, which is what made hand repair of the scanned transliteration line possible at all; three are not, and those three stand on the 1895 line alone. The identifiers written at traditions/egyptian-names.ttl are reproducible from the two named volumes together and from neither alone."@en,
        "Not a fixed set. Budge's vocabulary to the Theban recension marks as one of the Forty-two Judges a number of names that do not occur anywhere in the Ani sequence: Uatch-nesert, Green Flame; Uarekht; Utent; Maa-ha-f, he whose two eyes are like knives; Neb-nebu, Lord of lords; Then-ret; Serekhi; and the entry Budge glosses phallus. The vocabulary was compiled from the same recension as the papyrus, so these are names other manuscripts of that recension put in the forty-two places. The number is a count of slots."@en,
        "What was not searched. The hieroglyphic text, which would settle every one of the three unconfirmed forms and the assignment of the two Maa- names in a morning, and any manuscript of the recension other than the one Budge printed in 1895. A run with a reader of Egyptian closes this."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of the vocabulary on the whitespace-normalised text for the marker Budge attaches to these entries, the name of one of the Forty-two Judges in the Hall of Osiris, in the spellings Forty-two Judges, Forty two Judges, forty-two Judges and Forty-two gods; twenty-nine intact markers were returned and the damaged windows around them were read by hand with three hundred characters of left context to recover the headword. Each recovered headword was then looked up in the vocabulary's own index for its page, and searched for in the chapter 125 sequence of 1895 in its own spelling and in the spellings the scan produces for it."@en ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:AegyptiaConfessioNegativa ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-19" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "E. A. Wallis Budge, A Hieroglyphic Vocabulary to the Theban Recension of the Book of the Dead, London: Kegan Paul, 1911, complete, with its index; against the address sequence of chapter 125 in Budge, The Book of the Dead: The Papyrus of Ani, London: British Museum, 1895, plates XXXI-XXXII, complete" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Whether the forty-two gods of the hall of the two Maati are a fixed set of forty-two names, or forty-two places filled differently by different manuscripts. The question decides what the naming nodes may claim: a fixed set would make the list a name to be learned, and a variable one makes it a count." .

pmp:EgyptianChapterTitleSurvey a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Wofür jedes Kapitel des Totenbuchs sich selbst erklärt"@de,
        "What every chapter of the Book of the Dead says it is for"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-19" ;
    skos:note "Limits, and they bound the counts rather than the finding. The counts are of recovered titles, so each is a floor. Budge's arrangement and numbering are Lepsius's of 1842 and not the order of any manuscript, so a class here is a class of the printed edition. And a title is what a chapter says it is for, which need not be what it does: chapter 190 carries a title and may be, as Budge says, only the rubric of the chapter before it."@en,
        "One hundred and thirty-two usable titles out of a denominator of one hundred and seventy chapter numbers, the denominator itself established by two independent passes over the running heads which agreed on one hundred and seventy. The shortfall of thirty-eight has three causes and the search does not separate them: headings lost in the scan, chapters that carry no title in the Theban recension, and cross-references to another chapter."@en,
        "The result that changed a standing claim. The registration described this corpus as a list of transformations. Eight transformation titles were recovered; fifteen titles are about food, drink and air; twenty-one about passing, entering or journeying; and thirty-two state their capacity in the negative. The largest class is provisioning, which the registration did not name at all."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "Two passes on the whitespace-normalised text of all three volumes. The first took the tables of contents, splitting on the chapter number followed by a dash and taking the text up to the next number. The second took every occurrence of CHAPTER followed by a roman numeral and a bracket, and read forward for the phrase THE CHAPTER OF in Budge's several typographic renderings of it. The two results were merged, the table-of-contents form preferred where both had a chapter, and entries that are not titles were dropped by hand: See Chapter X, Another chapter, Without title, and the vignette-only entry for chapter 16."@en ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:AegyptiaMalaAversa,
        pmc:AegyptiaTituliCapacitatum,
        pmc:AegyptiaVictusInferorum ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-19" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The Book of the Dead, Theban recension, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vols. I to III: the tables of contents of all three volumes and every chapter heading in the body of all three" ;
    pm:searchedFor "The capacity each chapter states for itself in its own title, for all the chapters Budge prints and not for a sample. The registration of this tradition claimed it was a capacity list in the strict sense; a claim of that shape is answerable only against the whole list, and until this search the holding had read fewer than twenty chapters." .

pmp:EgyptianMillionFormulaSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die gemeinfreien Übersetzungen die Formel führen, ein Spruch sei millionenfach erprobt"@de,
        "Whether the public-domain translations carry the formula that a spell is proved a million times"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-19" ;
    skos:note "Not in either translation as an efficacy claim. The one place in Budge 1901 with the words for millions of times is the rubric of chapter 72, where it modifies what the dead man shall do continually and regularly, not how often the spell has been tried. At the place in the rubric of chapter 137A where a later translator reads the formula, Budge has the words it is a great struggle — a rendering that shares nothing with it and that suggests the two are reading the same consonants differently."@en,
        "The way to close it is named: a modern critical translation, or the hieroglyphic text of Budge 1898 and 1910 with a reader of Egyptian. Both are public domain; neither was usable by this run."@en,
        "This is an exhibited gap in the access and not in the corpus, and the difference matters. The finding is that a claim widely reported of this tradition cannot be grounded from any translation this project may use. It is entered so that the next run does not spend the search again, and so that nobody enters the formula on the strength of having read about it."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of all five volumes on the whitespace-normalised text for: million times; a matter a million times; proved a million; truly effective; hath been proved; tested; for millions of times; millions of years. The last two are frequent — one hit in Budge 1901 vol. II and a hundred and nine for millions of years across the five volumes — and every one inspected proved to be a duration and not an assertion of efficacy."@en ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-19" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The Book of the Dead, Theban recension, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London 1901, vols. I to III, complete; Budge, The Papyrus of Ani, London 1895, complete; and Renouf and Naville, The Egyptian Book of the Dead, London 1904, complete" ;
    pm:searchedFor "The efficacy formula that later translators render as a thing proved a million times or truly effective a million times, which would be the only statement in this corpus asserting that its prescriptions have been tested rather than that they work. Whether the public-domain translations carry it decides whether this holding can enter the claim at all." .

pmp:EgyptianNegativeRubricSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob eine Nachschrift sagt, was folgt, wenn ihre Bedingung nicht erfüllt ist"@de,
        "Whether any rubric says what follows from not meeting its condition"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-19" ;
    skos:note "Limits. Two translations, both public domain, both a century and more old, and one of them by a translator whose English is not now considered reliable. The modern standard translations are under copyright and were not obtained, so a rubric that a later translator renders as a threat and Budge renders otherwise would not appear in this search. One such place is known and is entered at pmp:EgyptianMillionFormulaSearch."@en,
        "The second find is not a rubric and is worth more than the first. In the hall of the two Maati the doorway itself refuses passage a dozen times over in the same form — I will not let thee enter, or pass over, or open to thee, or make mention of thee, unless thou tell my name — and that is a stated failure condition for the whole operation, given as dialogue rather than as postscript. A run that had searched only the rubrics would have reported a clean negative and missed the place where the corpus does say what happens to a man who does not know."@en,
        "Two finds and one large silence, and the silence is where the run expected the finds. Among the rubrics proper there is exactly one two-sided statement in five volumes: the rubric printed as Appendix 1 to chapter 76 in the Papyrus of Ani, which says that if the chapter be known the dead man shall come forth by day and go in again, and that if it be not known he shall not enter in after he hath come forth, nor shall he come forth by day. No other rubric read in either translation states an adverse outcome for its own condition, and the purity condition of chapter 64, which is repeated three times over, states none at all."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of all five volumes on the whitespace-normalised text for: be not known; if he know not; if he knoweth not; who knoweth not; if it be not done; shall be of no effect; without effect; in vain; shall fail; doth not know this; knoweth it not; unless he; unless thou; if thou doest not; if he do not. Every hit was inspected. The four chapters that carry the densest condition lists — 30B, 64, 125, 137A — were read continuously in Budge 1901, and the transformation chapters 76 to 88 were read through in the same volume."@en ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:AegyptiaPortaeNomina,
        pmc:AegyptiaRubricaBilateralis ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-19" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The Book of the Dead, Theban recension, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vols. I to III, complete; Budge, The Book of the Dead: The Papyrus of Ani, London: British Museum, 1895, complete; and Peter le Page Renouf and Édouard Naville, The Egyptian Book of the Dead, London: Society of Biblical Archaeology, 1904, complete" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A rubric that states an adverse outcome for the case in which its own condition is not met — the chapter not known, the object not made of the prescribed material, the purity not kept, the secrecy broken. The corpus states what knowing and doing obtain on almost every other page; whether it anywhere states the other half decides whether its claims exclude anything." .

pmp:EmptyConsultationExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Zeichen erscheint, wenn die Befragung nicht ernst gemeint ist"@de,
        "Whether the sign appears when the consultation is not meant"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that the counting of the stalks makes known what is coming. The condition is not this project's: a treatise of the first century puts it as the decisive case. Let a diviner bore the shell emptily and count the stalks in vain, making sport of heaven and earth, and he still gets an omen and a number; let a man curse heaven while he bores and strike the earth while he counts, and he still gets an omen and a number. The treatise draws the conclusion that the omen and the number are therefore not an answer, and adds the counterfactual that if they were, the fire should go out, the hand be scorched and the fingers shake so that the count is spoiled."@en ;
    skos:note "The state is procedure-without-cases and the reason is exact. The treatise says that the empty and the abusive consultation still yield a figure; it does not report having made one. The wording is a supposition put to the reader, and a supposition is not a case however confident it sounds. Whoever raises this protocol beyond its present state has to make the runs, not to quote the passage more loudly."@en,
        "The two obvious refinements are both blocked from inside the transmission, and that is the finding this protocol carries. Asking the same question twice and comparing is forbidden by the judgement on the fourth hexagram, which calls repetition defilement and withholds the answer. Consulting where the answer is already known is excluded by the Supreme Mystery, which says that who does not doubt does not consult. A control arm therefore has no sanctioned form in either work, and the treatise's empty consultation is the only one that was ever proposed."@en,
        "What the protocol would not settle even if it ran. That a figure appears under mockery tells against the figure being an answer given by something; it does not tell against the weaker claim that the figures of earnest consultations correspond to what comes. That weaker claim needs outcomes and a count, which is the other protocol beside this one."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "Wang Chong, in the treatise on divining of the Lunheng, first century; recorded here from the passage in traditions/chinese-divination.ttl" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A run of the counting performed emptily, in mockery or under abuse, which yields no figure, or yields figures distinguishable as a class from those of consultations meant in earnest. The claim as the classic states it — without thought, without doing, touched and thereupon penetrating — offers no place for the manner of the consultation to enter, so a difference between the two classes would fall against the treatise and no difference falls against the classic." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-09" ;
    pm:tests pmc:JishuZhilai .

pmp:EthiopianSpokenNamesSpearExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die gesprochenen Namen einen Speer zurückwenden"@de,
        "Whether the spoken names turn a spear back"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that if a man wishes to thrust a spear into the speaker and the speaker repeats the names, the spear shall be turned back. It is examinable because the claim names a single public event at a single moment and attaches no condition to it beyond the speaking."@en ;
    skos:note "No case has been collected in either direction, and the obstacle is a property of the record: the men who carried these rolls are not a recorded set, and neither the deaths nor the survivals of the eighteenth-century owners of Ethiopian parchment rolls were written down anywhere the claim could be checked against. Refutable in principle, unattended in fact, which is why the state is procedure-without-cases and not no-procedure-devised."@en,
        "One escape is nevertheless built into the object rather than into the sentence, and a protocol has to meet it: every prayer of every roll read names the person it was written for. A bearer who was struck can always be said to have carried a roll made out to somebody else. That is recorded at pmc:EthiopianOwnerNamedInEveryPrayer and it is a condition of the object, not of the claim."@en,
        "The escape the wording does not take is the point of entering this at all. Nothing here says the spear returns only if the speaker is worthy, only if he believes, or only if God wills it — and the second half of the same sentence says exactly the last of those about the other form. The bare half was not written by an author who had no such clause available."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, working from the passage recorded in traditions/ethiopian-orthodox-magic.ttl" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "One man who repeated the names as the thrust came and was struck by the spear. The wording admits no degree, names no further condition, and reserves nothing, so a single such case refutes the claim as it stands. A weaker form, that speakers are struck less often than others, would require the speakers to be counted before the outcomes are known, and no such count exists for any transmission in this holding." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-07" ;
    pm:tests pmc:EthiopianSpokenAsmatYieldsTurnedSpear .

pmp:EthiopianWornNamesSpearExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die getragenen Namen etwas ausschließen"@de,
        "Whether the worn names exclude anything"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that the names worn upon the breast shall deliver the wearer from his enemy and that he shall escape by the will of God. The verdict is reached from the wording and not from cases: the closing clause covers the outcome and its opposite."@en ;
    skos:note "A falsifying condition had to be entered here saying that there is none. pm:FalsifierRequiredShape exempts only pm:noProcedureDevised, and pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate is the one verdict whose content is that no falsifier exists; the shape therefore demands of it exactly what it reports as absent. Recorded rather than worked around, and filed as its own finding."@en,
        "The state is not a milder version of the one beside it and does not report a weaker claim. The spoken form says something and no cases have been gathered; the worn form gathers nothing because there is nothing that would count. The difference is three words at the end of one sentence."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, working from the passage recorded in traditions/ethiopian-orthodox-magic.ttl" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "None is available from the wording as it stands, and that is the finding rather than an omission of this protocol. Were the deliverance stated without the closing clause, one wearer struck while carrying the names would refute it, exactly as in the spoken form beside it. With the clause in place there is no outcome to look for." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-07" ;
    pm:tests pmc:EthiopianWornAsmatYieldsDeliverance ;
    pm:undividedOutcomes "A wearer of the names who escapes his enemy, and a wearer of the names who is struck down by him. The first is deliverance by the will of God; the second is a deliverance that was not willed. The sentence covers both and forbids neither, so no observation of a wearer's fate can bear on it." .

pmp:FeatherAtTheNostrilExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der Flaum an Nase und Mund stillsteht"@de,
        "Whether the down at nose and mouth stays still"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the mark the eighth chapter of the Baopuzi sets on the closing off of the breath: a goose down is laid on the nose and the mouth, and that it does not stir while the qi is let out is the sign. The passage gives this as the criterion by which the practitioner knows he is doing the thing, alongside the rule that he is not to hear his own breathing and that he is always to take in more than he gives out."@en ;
    skos:note "The scale beside the sign belongs in any run. The chapter starts the beginner at a silent count of one hundred and twenty and says the count may in the long run reach a thousand, and it attaches to the thousand a claim that does not belong to the down at all: that the old grow younger and a day is given back for a day. A protocol that measured the count and reported the second claim as tested would be measuring one thing and reporting another."@en,
        "The state is procedure-without-cases and not no-procedure-devised, and here the procedure was devised by the source rather than by this project. Nothing stands in the way of running it: the down is cheap, the observation needs no instrument, and the criterion is binary. What has not been done is the running."@en,
        "What such a run would and would not settle. It would settle whether a person can exhale below the threshold at which a goose down moves, which is a question about airflow and not about the qi; the chapter treats the two as one, and this protocol does not. It would not touch the second and larger claim of the same passage, the embryonic breathing that uses neither nose nor mouth, for which the down criterion is satisfied by construction and therefore says nothing."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, working from the passage recorded in traditions/daoist.ttl" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A practitioner who claims the attainment and whose down stirs on the exhalation, observed by someone other than himself. The source states the sign without qualification and attaches no condition of hour, place or state to it, so a stirring falls against the claim as the chapter makes it. The converse does not establish the doctrine: a down that lies still shows a slow exhalation and nothing about the qi." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-09" ;
    pm:tests pmc:InaudibleAndUnevenBreath .

pmp:FinnicExactnessSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der finnische Bestand die genaue Rezitation zur Bedingung macht"@de,
        "Whether the Finnic corpus makes exact recitation a condition"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-16" ;
    skos:note "Nothing came back. In a corpus whose central line makes the expulsion follow from pronouncing a deep origin, words that are handed down, no passage read states that the words must be exact, and none names a consequence for the operator who errs, forgets or does not know them. The nearest thing to a rule for not knowing is the formula to discover the cause, which is a remedy and not a penalty."@en,
        "The one statement in the volume about exactness runs the other way and is the editor's, not the transmission's: he holds that one or only a very few of the songs stand in the shape in which they were sung, and that the singers, through failure of memory, did not recite them precisely the same on each occasion but unconsciously made small changes. That is an observation about the practice by the man who printed it, and it is recorded here as his."@en,
        "Why the empty result is worth having. The holding asserts a convergence at pmc:ExactnessAsConditionOfEfficacy over four transmissions in which exactness is stated as the condition of efficacy. This is a fifth transmission of the same kind — spoken words, an operator, a claimed effect — in which the condition is not stated at all. That does not refute a convergence, which claims agreement among its members and not universality; it fixes the boundary of the agreement, and a later run that wants to say anything general about exactness now has a case outside it to account for."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search of the whole volume, on the normalised text, for: wrongly, mistake, mistaken, forget, forgets, forgetting, forgot, wrong word, err, erred, error, fail, fails, failed, failure, unless, if he does not, who knows not, not know. Every hit read in its place." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-16" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "John Abercromby, The Pre- and Proto-historic Finns, vol. II: The Magic Songs of the West Finns (London: David Nutt, 1898), searched whole by term and the hits read at the wording; the editor's account of the collection at pp. 1-6 read in full" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage stating that the words must be said exactly, or naming what follows if the operator errs, forgets or does not know them. The holding carries a convergence asserting that exactness is set as the condition of efficacy in four transmissions, and this corpus makes the handed-down words the whole ground of the operation, so it is the obvious place to ask." .

pmp:FinnicSignOfSuccessSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob ein finnischer Spruch ein wahrnehmbares Zeichen des Wirkens nennt"@de,
        "Whether a Finnish charm names a perceptible sign of having worked"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-16" ;
    skos:note "Everything else the search returned was a mark on a body, a heraldic or weather word in a simile, or the editor's own prose. The volume has no equivalent of the ordered sign-sequences this holding carries from the Chinese inner alchemy, and no equivalent of a warmth or a light reported by the practitioner."@en,
        "Exactly one passage in the volume demands a sign, and it demands it in the one place where nobody is standing. The evil, sent home to whoever fashioned it, is to give a sign on arrival, a secret signal, a crash like a thunder-clap and a glare like a lightning-flash — at that house, not at the bath-house where the operation was performed. The count for this corpus is therefore one sign of success, and it is not one the operator could report absent."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search of the whole volume, on the text normalised to single spaces because the scan breaks lines inside words, for: sign, signal, token, omen, mark, appear, appears, glare, crash, flash, smoke, warmth, light. Every hit read in its place." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:FinnicMerkkiKotona ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-16" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "John Abercromby, The Pre- and Proto-historic Finns, vol. II: The Magic Songs of the West Finns (London: David Nutt, 1898), searched whole by term and the hits read at the wording" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage telling the operator or the patient what will be perceived when a charm has taken effect, which SPEC §15 requires to be harvested and counted separately from the conditions of failure." .

pmp:FinnicStatedTermSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob ein finnischer Spruch eine Frist setzt, bis zu der er gewirkt haben muss"@de,
        "Whether a Finnish charm sets a term by which it must have worked"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-16" ;
    skos:note "One term came back and it is set on the wrong event. The evil is to reach its master's seat ere the close of day, ere the setting of the sun; nothing is said about when the sick man is to be better, and nothing about what follows if the sun sets and he is not. The formula's own provision for that case is the conditional two clauses later, which escalates instead of concluding."@en,
        "This is the same finding the Jain run of the same day made in another corpus and it is worth stating as a pattern to be watched rather than a coincidence: terms are common, terms on the result are not. What a transmission times is the journey of the thing expelled, the length of the preparation, the number of the repetitions — and not the interval within which the promised effect is to appear."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search of the whole volume, on the normalised text, for: ere the close, ere the setting, sunset, sundown, before the sun, three days, three times, nine days, nine times, within a, by the morrow, by morning, this night. Every hit read in its place." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:FinnicAurinkoonMennessa ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-16" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "John Abercromby, The Pre- and Proto-historic Finns, vol. II: The Magic Songs of the West Finns (London: David Nutt, 1898), searched whole by term and the hits read at the wording" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A term after which the charm is to have taken effect, which is the form the testability-driven entrance of SPEC §14 looks for and which would make a failure condition constructible." .

pmp:FinnicUhkaus a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Drohformel, gesprochen nach einer Austreibung, die nichts bewirkt hat"@de,
        "The menacing formula, said after an expulsion that produced nothing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John Abercromby, The Pre- and Proto-historic Finns, vol. II (London: David Nutt, 1898), § 16 a, pp. 92-93, with the occasion stated at p. 3" ;
    skos:altLabel "uhkasanoja"@en ;
    skos:definition "A formula with its own number and its own occasion in the collection: it is used after an expulsion formula, if the expulsion has proved of no avail."@en ;
    skos:note "The occasion is the point. This is not a stronger version of the expulsion, to be chosen instead of it; it is what is said afterwards, in the case the expulsion was performed and nothing followed. The transmission has given that case a name, a number and a text."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:FinnicIncantation .

pmp:FireAndWaterRite a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Ritus mit Feuer und Wasser bei einer Geburt"@de,
        "The rite of fire and water at a birth"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulo I, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces" ;
    skos:definition "The whole work of fire and water at a birth is committed to the specialist who holds it as his office, and who among these people ordinarily bears the name and the office of physician."@en ;
    skos:note "The sentence is a statement about an institution: the office exists, it has a name, and the rite is not performed by the family. That is the part a later reader can check, and it is why the passage is entered as a practice rather than as an item of belief."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NahuaRitual .

pmp:FireOrWaterClauseInLeechdomsSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die angelsächsischen Heilbücher den Ausschluss von Feuer und Wasser führen"@de,
        "Whether the Anglo-Saxon leechdoms carry the fire-or-water exclusion"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-13" ;
    skos:note "No exclusion of that shape anywhere in the three volumes. What came back instead is the same disease with the condition put in a different place: chapter sixty-one of the Old English Herbarium gives berries of the wort called asterion for the falling sickness, to be eaten when the moon is on the wane and when the sun stands in the Virgin, which the text resolves into the month of August, with the wort hung on the sufferer's neck. The condition is on the calendar and not on the sufferer."@en,
        "See prima-materia#520. The corpus label korpus:volksmagie now carries a search of its Anglo-Saxon half, which it did not before."@en,
        "That is the productive outcome and it changes what the Hohman clause looks like. Both transmissions bind this one remedy to a window; Hohman's is a weekday and a phase of the moon, the Herbarium's is a phase of the moon and a month of the year; only Hohman adds a condition on the person. An exclusion stated on the sufferer is therefore not the normal form even within the corpus this handbook stands in."@en,
        "The other three hits on falling sickness are remedies without any condition at all: a mountain goat's brain drawn through a golden ring and given before the child tastes milk, and a boar's testicles worked into a drink. They are noted and not entered, because this run opened the volumes for one question and read the hits, not the works."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search of all three volumes for: falling sickness (four hits, all read); fylleseoc (one hit, in the glossary of volume three); fallen into, into fire, fire or water (none); epilep (eighteen hits, all in editorial matter or in the Latin); lunatic (twelve hits); devil sickness (five hits)." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicAsterionYieldsHeilung ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-13" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Oswald Cockayne, Leechdoms, Wortcunning and Starcraft of Early England, three volumes (Rolls Series 35, London 1864-1866), holding the Old English Herbarium in volume one, Bald's Leechbook in volume two and the Lacnunga in volume three; all three volumes searched by term and the hits read at the wording" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A remedy for the falling sickness restricted to a sufferer who has not yet fallen into fire or water, or any other exclusion stated before the application and determinable on the sufferer beforehand — the shape of pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicFallendeKrankheitAusschluss." .

pmp:FireOrWaterClauseInRomanusSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Romanus-Büchlein den Ausschluss von Feuer und Wasser führt"@de,
        "Whether the Romanus-Büchlein carries the fire-or-water exclusion"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-13" ;
    skos:note "See prima-materia#520. The finding bears on the question of whose clause it is: in the printed transmission behind Hohman it is not there, which leaves the American printing of eighteen twenty as its earliest witness in this holding."@en,
        "The booklet has no remedy for the falling sickness at all, so the exclusion has nothing in it to attach to. That is a stronger silence than a remedy without the clause would have been: the affliction is missing and not only the condition."@en,
        "The limit of the search is the edition. Peterson's text withholds two of the seventy-six charms, numbers seventy-five and seventy-six, so the negative finding covers seventy-four and not the whole. No printing of seventeen eighty-eight was reached, and no copy of the booklet was found in any full-text archive under either spelling of its title."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of the German and the English side for: fallende, Fallsucht, fallende Krankheit, Krankheit, Feuer oder Wasser, geboren; falling, falling sickness, epilep, fire or water. The only hit on falling is in a wound charm, where it is the falling off of the flesh." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-13" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Romanus-Büchlein (Glatz: Franz Pompejus, 1788), in the bilingual edition of Joseph H. Peterson, 2006, seventy-four numbered charms; whole text searched" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A remedy for the falling sickness, and in particular the clause that restricts such a remedy to a sufferer who has not yet fallen into fire or water — the exclusion at pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicFallendeKrankheitAusschluss, which Hohman prints in the heading of his prescription." .

pmp:FourthWayStatesAntecedentSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der Vierte Weg für seine vier Zustände einen Vorgänger nennt"@de,
        "Whether the Fourth Way names an antecedent for its four states"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-09" ;
    skos:note "Its weight is the weight of the terms above and no more. A search in Latin transliteration finds nothing about a doctrine the author might have named in Russian, and the scanned text is an OCR whose failures are unknown. Anyone with a better copy can overturn this in an afternoon, which is the point of writing it down."@en,
        "The finding is a silence and not a refutation. The work names no antecedent for the states, while naming four terminologies for the bodies in the same volume; a route to the theosophical or Vedantic material may still exist outside it. What the node records is that this corpus was asked and did not supply one, which is a different thing from nobody having asked. See prima-materia#653."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Full-text search of the whole work for: Theosoph (ten occurrences, all concerning bodies, planes or societies); Vedanta, Vedantic; turiya, Turiya; Mandukya; Upanishad." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-09" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching (one-volume English text as scanned in the Internet Archive full-text mirror)" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Whether the work derives its fourfold ordering of states of consciousness from an earlier source, as it derives its fourfold division of bodies from four named terminologies at pmc:FourBodiesAcrossTerminologies." .

pmp:GehongCasusPositivusSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Kapitel einen Fall nennt, in dem ein Opfer geholfen hat"@de,
        "Whether the chapter names a case of a sacrifice that helped"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-23" ;
    skos:note "Seventeen named persons and not one of them a case for the other side. The nearest thing is the recoveries inside the four etiologies — the man whose eyes mended under the plum tree, the sufferers who rubbed the stone figure, the traveller whose sores healed in the lime-water — and all three are named as having happened of themselves, with the antecedent supplied. Those are not offered as cases to be accounted for; they are the chapter's explanation of how such cases are mistaken."@en,
        "The empty result is why the protocol at pmc:GehongBaLi stands at pm:procedureWithoutCases rather than at a state reporting cases. Cases were offered, in quantity and with names, and what is missing is any sign that the other column of the ledger was looked for. A selection made by the arguer, with no stated population and no counter-instance, is a rhetorical exhibit and not a count — and the chapter's own argument at 2b, that if prayer lengthened life the rich would necessarily live long, would have been far harder to answer if it had been accompanied by the cases that told the other way and a reason for setting them aside."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Read at the wording rather than searched by term, because a positive case would not carry a fixed vocabulary. Every named person in the chapter was collected and classified: King Ling of Chu, the Prince of Guangling, Emperor Wen, the Fifth Lord, the Lujiang of Song, Wen Weng, Cao Cao, the Runan snare-setter, the fish dealer, Zhang Zhu of Nandun, the old woman at the Peng tomb, the traveller at the Luoyang tomb, the water seller, Ma of Xinggu, Li A, Li Kuan, and the author himself."@en ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-23" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 9 (Daoyi), complete, in the Zhengtong Daozang text as reproduced in the Hanfenlou facsimile, DZ 1185, juan 9, read at the wording."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A named instance of a person who sacrificed, prayed or petitioned and whose illness or destiny then turned, offered by the chapter as a case it had to account for. The chapter names eight cases against the practice — four who sacrificed and were not helped, four who abolished a cult and prospered — and one control group of its own acquaintance, so the question is whether it exhibits the other side of its own ledger anywhere."@en .

pmp:GehongXiaoyanSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Kapitel, das über Wirksamkeit streitet, ein Wort für Wirksamkeit hat"@de,
        "Whether the chapter that argues about efficacy has a word for efficacy"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-23" ;
    skos:note "Not one occurrence of either character in the whole chapter. What the chapter uses instead is the ordinary vocabulary of getting better — yu, to recover, fifteen times, and chai, to mend, three times — and the adverb ou, of itself or by chance, which it attaches to three of the four recoveries in its etiologies of a healing cult. The argument is therefore carried entirely in the language of outcomes and never in the language of demonstration."@en,
        "The comparison that makes the silence legible. The Celestial Masters manual opened in the same run uses the word for proof of effect twice, once to deny it to an incompetent officiant and once as a blanket claim that the twenty-four officers are all efficacious; and the transmission's code states the outcome of a failed treatment as frailty and no proof of effect. So the vocabulary existed in Chinese operative writing of the period, in the very practice this chapter attacks, and this chapter does not use it. A critic who argues from cases and never asks for a demonstration has conceded the frame of the argument to the party he is attacking, which is visible in what he does two folios later at pmc:GehongYuer."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-chapter search for 驗, 效, 效驗, 有驗, 無效, 應驗, 靈驗, 徵. Each term searched in the unspaced text, so that a compound would be caught by either of its characters."@en ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-23" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 9 (Daoyi), complete, in the Zhengtong Daozang text as reproduced in the Hanfenlou facsimile, DZ 1185, juan 9. Whole-chapter search in the Chinese."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "The vocabulary in which a Chinese operative text states that something has been shown to work: yan, proof of effect; xiao, effect; xiaoyan, the two together; and the related terms for a verified case. The chapter is a sustained argument against the efficacy of sacrifice and petitionary prayer, conducted with named cases on both sides, so the question is whether it reaches for the technical vocabulary the neighbouring transmissions use."@en .

pmp:GeneralRuleAgainstExcessSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der medizinische Kanon eine allgemeine Maßregel gegen das Übermaß der Praxis nennt"@de,
        "Whether the medical canon states a general rule against excess of practice"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-10" ;
    skos:note "See prima-materia#240. The issue keeps its korpus:huangdi-neijing label and stays open, because four further corpora named there have not been asked."@en,
        "The result is a general rule and not the general rule the claim asks for, and the difference is worth keeping. The five exhaustions state that a thing done too long injures, name the injury in each case, and stand twice in the corpus; but the five things are looking, lying, sitting, standing and walking, which are postures and not prescribed techniques. The corpus's own rule about its own operation is a single clause with no quantity and no consequence, and it is entered separately."@en,
        "What the search settles for this corpus is therefore narrower than the issue asks and sharper than a silence would have been. The transmission does have the form of claim the question is after, applies it to ordinary conduct, and declines to give it any content where its own technique is concerned. That asymmetry is a finding about the corpus and would not have been visible from either half alone."@en,
        "Written a day after the search, because the node it names could not be reached from any branch on which it would have validated. The guard in tests/test_publication.py refused a part that points at a node it does not name, which is the condition of SPEC §1 and which held. What that shows about this class is worth keeping: a pm:Searching with a find is bound to the node it yields and travels with it, so such a search belongs in the same change as its find and not in a batch of searches."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of both classics for: 過度 (six hits, all about excess of exertion or of pleasure and cold and heat, none about a practice); 無過其度 (one hit, Lingshu 61); 五勞所傷 (Suwen 23); 久視傷 (Lingshu 78, the same five clauses); 不妄作勞 (Suwen 1)." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:WulaoSuoshang ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-10" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Huangdi neijing, Suwen in the Chongguang buzhu recension and Lingshu in the Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, both in the Sibu congkan facsimile; searched over both classics in the Chinese, with the chapters carrying a hit read at the wording" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A general rule, not a single case, that too much of a prescribed practice injures and that a measure is therefore to be kept — the claim of prima-materia#240, which reaches this corpus among five." .

pmp:GradedTrialExclusionExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob ein Satz von Proben je den glatten Ausschluss für teilweises Versagen nennt"@de,
        "Whether a set of trials ever states plain exclusion for partial failure"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that where a transmission makes a set of trials or obstacles the condition of its highest attainment, it also states an outcome for the candidate who clears only part of the set, and that outcome is a lesser rank within the same system rather than exclusion from it; and that the sets are so constructed that a candidate cannot establish in advance which trials he faces or how many he has cleared, so the grading is assigned after the fact. The question put was the falsifier the claim names: a set of trials that states a plain exclusion for partial failure."@en ;
    skos:note "One feature of the case bears on the claim's own reason for existing and cuts the other way. The claim's second note says that a graded trial produces a set of people who each attained something, so that the transmission's own records contain no failures at all. This transmission's record contains nothing but failures, counted: seven who raised one weight, twenty-one who raised two, thirty-five three, thirty-five four, twenty-one five, seven six, one who reached the seventh and could not raise it, many who wholly failed, and few who raised them all. The numbers are the binomial coefficients and are therefore constructed rather than counted, which is entered at pmc:RosicrucianDistributioPonderum and does not affect this protocol: what matters here is that the transmission publishes a failure rate at all, and no other transmission in this holding does."@en,
        "That is the claim's second clause exactly inverted. The grading is there and it is finer than in either of the claim's two witnesses, which have twelve trials and nine difficulties and one lesser rank each; here seven weights generate a punishment for every combination. But every grade is a way of being put out. Nobody who failed a weight receives a lesser rank inside the system, and the dismissal says so in its own words: you cannot be present at his Majesty's feast of honour, neither is your reputation hereby prejudiced although you be rejected by this our Order. The transmission grades the exclusion and not the attainment."@en,
        "The case. On the third day of the Chymische Hochzeit a balance of gold is hung in the hall and seven weights are set out; two hundred armed men are divided into seven bands by the number of the weights; each guest is weighed against them one by one, and he who cannot raise a weight is flung out of the scale, bound, laughed at and delivered to the band of the weight he failed. The sentence read the same day assigns the outcome by which weights were failed: those who did not stand up to the first, third and fourth are stripped stark naked and sent forth; those too light in the second and fifth are besides stripping branded with one, two or more marks according as each was lighter or heavier; those drawn up by the sixth or seventh and not by the rest are dealt with somewhat more graciously; for every combination a certain punishment was ordained; and the cheaters who could move no weight at all are punished in body and life with the sword, halter, water and rods."@en,
        "The third clause fails too and more cheaply. The claim says the sets are so constructed that a candidate cannot establish in advance which trials he faces or how many he has cleared. Here he is told the evening before that the artist's scales will be set on the morrow's morn, wherein each one shall be weighed and found what he has forgotten; the weighing is public, in the middle of the hall, before the whole company; and each man's result is recorded, the narrator saying he numbered them as they stood before him and noted them in his table-book. What he cannot establish in advance is what the weights measure, which is a different defect and the one pmp:RosicrucianDignitasSearch exhibits."@en,
        "What the deviation costs and what it leaves. The claim's antecedent is a set of trials conditioning a highest attainment; its consequent has three parts, and this case breaks the second and the third while confirming the first. So the surviving statement is thin: transmissions with sets of trials state an outcome for partial clearing. Whether that outcome is a lesser rank or an exclusion now has both values in the holding, and the interesting question is what predicts which — the two witnesses of the lesser rank are Chinese cultivation lineages in which the candidate is already inside the transmission, and the case here is an admission trial at the threshold of one. A candidate who is already in cannot be excluded and a candidate at the door cannot be demoted, which would make the claim's consequent a function of where the trial sits. That is a statement worth forming and it is not formed here, because two witnesses on one side and one on the other is not enough and because a claim is not to be replaced in the protocol that broke it."@en ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundNothing ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "The counter-search looked for a reading under which the weighing is not a set of trials, which is the escape the claim used against the Mithraic material: that case was set aside as a single ordeal and the claim is about sets. Here the escape is unavailable. There are seven weights, they are put in one after another, they are numbered, the sentence of the same day grades its punishments by which of them a guest failed, and the work publishes a distribution over the number cleared. A second reading was looked for as well — that the guests who fail are not candidates for the highest attainment but gatecrashers, and so outside the antecedent. It does not hold either: they came on the bridegroom's invitation, were admitted at the gates on his tokens, and the sentence dismisses some of them as lords and potentates expressly rejected by this our Order, which is a rejection of candidates and not the removal of intruders. Searched besides over the compiled holding for any further set of trials stating plain exclusion, in the Mithraic, Midewiwin, Ars notoria and solomonic material the claim names as the places to widen it; none of the four was found to state one, so this protocol adds a case and not a class."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-30" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A transmission with a set of trials that states a plain exclusion for partial failure — the candidate obtains nothing, is not of the company, is sent away. That is the condition the claim itself named. It is met, and the exclusion is itself graded."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-30" ;
    pm:tests pmc:TheTrialWithNoFailingGrade .

pmp:HarbaDeMosheCautioSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Werk irgendwo vor den schädigenden Verrichtungen warnt"@de,
        "Whether the work anywhere warns against the operations that do harm"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-24" ;
    skos:note "Nothing anywhere. The operation for killing a man is set out with the same evenness as the one for a painful nerve, between an instruction for catching fish and one for sending plagues, and no sentence in the work distinguishes them. What the work does guard is its own handling: three days of purity, secrecy about the intention, a prohibition on touching before the preparations, and at the close a threat of strangulation for an amulet written without its opening formula."@en,
        "One search that was named and not carried, and it is the gap of this run rather than of the work. The rabbinic condemnation of magic — the Mishnah at Sanhedrin 7:11 with its distinction between the sorcerer who does a deed and the one who dazzles the eyes, the discussion at Bavli Sanhedrin 67a to 68a, and Shabbat 75a on learning from a magician — is the counter-transmission to this corpus, and public-domain English translations of all of it are reachable by the regular route: Danby's Tractate Sanhedrin of 1919 for the Mishnah and the Tosefta beside it, Rodkinson's Talmud for the rest. None of it was opened at the wording in this run, and no node here rests on it. It is the first thing the next run at this corpus should do, and it is cheap."@en,
        "That asymmetry is the finding and it is stated here rather than in a note on some node, because it is a property of the whole work and not of any one entry. The transmission takes great care about the operator's condition and none at all about what he does with it."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of the translation, normalised to bare letters, for: take heed (one hit, and it is in the second recension printed as an appendix, not in this work, and it is about oaths and modesty); beware (no hits at all); do not use (none); forbidden (one, and it is the editor's introduction about the Tetragrammaton); sin (one, in a prayer of supplication); warn (two, one the editor on a responsum of Hai Gaon and one an optical misreading of was heard); not lawful (none). The harmful operations were then read again in place to see whether a caution stood beside any of them without those words." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-24" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The Sword of Moses, the framing part and the whole of part III, ed. and trans. Moses Gaster (London: David Nutt, 1896)" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A caution, a restriction or a condition of any kind attached to the operations that injure: killing a man with an image and a bow, sending plagues, sending an evil spirit, making a woman miscarry, making a man ill, destroying a neighbour's house, making him disliked, taking a rich man's riches, making the bystanders kill one another. The question matters because the same work fences its own use with three days of purity, a secrecy rule and a prohibition on touching, and because the holding carries several transmissions in which the self-warning stands inside the prescribing passage." .

pmp:HarbaDeMosheDefectusSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob eine der hundertsechsunddreißig Verrichtungen eine Misslingensbedingung nennt"@de,
        "Whether any of the hundred and thirty-six operations states a condition of failure"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-24" ;
    skos:note "A hundred and thirty-six operations and not one condition of failure. The form of the entries is uniform throughout: do this to this material, say the named section, and the thing follows. The verb is never conditional. Two entries do have two outcomes — the lettuce whose leaves close against the journey at number 65, and the sick man who turns his face towards the operator or away at number 90 — but both are questions put to the world and not tests of the operation, and in both the operation is held to have worked either way."@en,
        "It also settles what the sanction at pmc:HarbaDeMosheCaution is and is not. That sentence threatens the operator with strangling if he leaves a formula off an amulet; it says nothing about the amulet working or not working, and this search establishes that nothing else in the work does either."@en,
        "The limit of the finding. Part two, the list of names the operations refer to, is not printed in this translation and could not be searched; if a condition of failure stands anywhere in the work it would have to stand there, among names, which is not where a condition of failure is put. The critical edition of 1997 rests on a fuller manuscript and was not obtainable in this run."@en,
        "This is the gap the search exists to exhibit, and it is a large one. The holding's whole stage three rests on transmissions that say what would count against them, and this corpus, which is as operative as anything here and more specific about its materials than most, says nothing of the kind anywhere in its hundred and thirty-six entries. Whoever wants to know how many claims of this corpus are testable now has the number, and it is not an estimate."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "The whole of part three was read through continuously, and then searched with the text normalised to bare letters, for: if it does not (no hits); will not work (none); fail in any form (none at all); in vain (none); unless (none); does not succeed (none); if he has not (none); be of no avail (none); try again (none); nothing will (one hit, and it is a promise — say this in the fall and nothing will hurt thee); repeat it (one, and it is an instruction to repeat a remedy for three days, not on failure)." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-24" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The Sword of Moses, part III in full, numbers 1 to 136 in the editor's numbering, ed. and trans. Moses Gaster (London: David Nutt, 1896)" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Any sentence attached to any single operation saying what would show that it had not worked, what to do if it did not, or under what circumstance it does not hold: a term after which nothing more comes, a botched ingredient, an unfit operator, a repetition on failure. The work carries two stated consequences, one in its framing part and one at its close, and both are about the operator rather than the operation; the question is whether the operations themselves carry anything of the kind." .

pmp:HawaiianRedundantMemorising a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Auswendiglernen eines Gesangs durch mehrere Rezitatoren"@de,
        "Memorising a chant with more than one reciter"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Martha Warren Beckwith, The Kumulipo: A Hawaiian Creation Chant (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), chapter 7, p. 35" ;
    skos:definition "The guard the transmission puts against its own condition of failure. A master of song usually gathered two or more of his fellows to edit and to memorise the lines, and genealogies in particular had to be memorised by more than one reciter."@en ;
    skos:note "Worth holding separately from the rule it answers, and for a reason that goes beyond this tradition. A transmission that names a failure condition and stops has stated a threat; one that names the failure condition and then builds a redundancy against it has treated its own rule as a real risk and acted on it. The second is evidence about the practice that the first is not, and the graph has very few instances of it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HawaiianRitual .

pmp:HippocraticIntervalSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die hippokratischen Aphorismen einer Wunde eine gezählte Frist beigeben"@de,
        "Whether the Hippocratic Aphorisms attach a counted term to a wound"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-02" ;
    skos:note "Limits, and they are not small. One section of one work of the collection, in one nineteenth-century translation, with the Greek absent. On Wounds in the Head is the treatise of this corpus that handles an injury at a named site at length and it was not opened; until it is, the finding is about the Aphorisms and not about Hippocratic medicine. Both unopened works are named as identifiers in traditions/hippocratic.ttl so that queries/unopened-works.rq shows them."@en,
        "The answer to both halves. The list of seven fatal parts at VI.18 carries no interval, no sign and no gradation; and the same section states a term twice, at seven days for a man suddenly seized with head pains and laid down speechless, and at forty days for a gouty inflammation. So the silence at the wound is not an incapacity of the corpus. That is the finding the convergence needed and it is the reason this search is entered as a find and not as an empty one."@en,
        "The one counted term the section does attach to a sudden event comes with a release in the same sentence: the patient dies in seven days unless fever come on. A later run comparing release clauses across the holding should start there rather than at the wound list."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "Section VI read continuously, aphorism by aphorism, in Adams's English; within it, whole-text search of the volume for: deadly, mortal, fatal, dies in, die in, death in, within, days, seventh day, forty days, interval, term. Every hit inside Section VI inspected at its place, and the two counted terms outside the wound material — VI.49 and VI.51 — read in full. The Greek was not searched and is not present on this route." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:HippocratesVulneraLetalia ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-02" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Hippocrates, Aphorisms, Section VI, all sixty aphorisms, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. II, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, read continuously" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Whether the Greek corpus, at the place where it lists the parts whose wounding is fatal, says anything about how long the dying takes; and, if not, whether the absence is a property of the genre or of the subject — that is, whether the same section counts days at all. The question decides what the agreement between the Indian marman classes and the Chinese depot intervals is worth, since an agreement in a thing every medical transmission does is not a finding." .

pmp:HippocraticVulnusStatusSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob diese zwei Traktate einen Eingriff in benannten Zuständen des Menschen verbieten"@de,
        "Whether these two treatises forbid an operation in named states of the patient"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-03" ;
    skos:note "Not one member of the catalogue is a condition on an operation in either treatise. Where the terms do occur they occur as something else, and the three near things are worth naming because each fails on a different word. Thirst appears four times in the Prognostic, every time as a prognostic sign of the patient's state and never as a bar to a measure. Wine appears once in the head-wounds treatise, in the prohibition on putting anything liquid on a head wound, which is a rule about the dressing and not about the patient. And the one place that does make a measure conditional on the patient's conduct is chapter 20 of the head-wounds treatise, where the purge is given only if the erysipelatous swelling be not connected with any error in the regimen — a condition on the reading of a sign, not on the permission to operate."@en,
        "What the two treatises do condition an operation on, so that the negative finding is readable against something. The state of the part: the uvula is not to be cut while red and large. The place: no incision at the temple, no trepan on a suture, the trepan only where the bone is thickest. The time since the wound: the three days of chapter 14. The weather: more especially if it be hot. The age of the patient: a small trepan and the greatest precautions in children. And the surgeon's own history with the case, twice. The conditions are on the site, the instrument, the calendar and the operator — and not once on a state of the person operated on."@en,
        "Where the Greek side of prima-materia#610 probably is, stated so that the next run does not search here again. On Regimen in Acute Diseases stands in the same volume at pp. 271-336 and is where this corpus treats baths, evacuation and the condition of the patient under which a measure is or is not applied; the Aphorisms carry the seasonal rules for venesection, two of which are already in this holding at pmc:HippocratesVenaeSectioVere. Neither was read for this question. Celsus, whom the issue names for the Graeco-Roman side, is a different corpus and is not in this holding at all."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of both chapter ranges after whitespace normalisation, case-insensitively, for: drunk, intoxicat, wine, weari, fatigue, exhaust, satiet, repletion, full meal, after eating, hunger, fasting, abstinen, thirst, fright, terror, coition, venery, sexual, journey, travel, anger, wrath, bath, vomit, purg, venesect, bleed, blood-letting, phlebotom, open a vein, season, summer, winter, spring, autumn. Every hit read at its place; the twenty-nine and twenty hits on anger are the substring in dangerous and were discarded." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-03" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Hippocrates, On Injuries of the Head, chapters 1 to 21, and The Book of Prognostics, chapters 1 to 25, both read continuously in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, pp. 445-466 and 234-256" ;
    pm:searchedFor "The catalogue of states of the person in which an operation on a vessel is forbidden — drunkenness, anger, exhaustion, having just eaten, hunger or fasting, thirst, fright, having just had intercourse, having travelled — which prima-materia#610 puts as a convergence candidate across four transmissions and which it requires to be read at the wording on the Graeco-Roman side. The question is whether these two treatises, which are the surgical and the prognostic treatise of this corpus, carry any part of it." .

pmp:HippocraticVulnusTerminusSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die hippokratische Sammlung einer Wunde eine gezählte Frist beigibt"@de,
        "Whether the Hippocratic collection attaches a counted term to a wound"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-03" ;
    skos:note "A correction this search owes about itself, made by the run's own closing check. An earlier draft of the note above said that Adams's notes report, from the Epidemics, trepanned children dying on the twenty-fourth and the sixteenth day. Neither half of that was established here: the numbers come from the research report that preceded this run, which gives them from Withington's introduction to the Loeb volume and not from Adams, and the Epidemics were not opened in this run at all. Two day-counts outside the seven-and-fourteen frame of chapter 19 would be the strongest thing anyone could put against pmc:HippocratesVulnusCapitisTempusAnni, which is exactly why they may not be carried on a report. The address is kept and the claim is withdrawn."@en,
        "The answer is yes and it is not the yes the question was built for. The counted term exists — fever before the fourteenth day in winter and after seven days in summer, death before seven days in summer and before fourteen in winter — and it is indexed on the season. The site is graded at length in the second chapter and given no number at all, and the same chapter states in one clause that a man survives longer in winter than in summer whatever be the part of the head in which the wound is situated. So the corpus separates the two questions and answers them differently: the place orders the dying, the calendar counts it."@en,
        "Two further findings of the same search, both entered as nodes. The term is attached to a case the chapter defines by the surgeon's omission, not by the injury: the bone was broken and by mistake not discovered and the case neglected as if it were sound. And the sign-set is declared invariant to the age of the wound in the same chapter. A transmission that counts days and says which variables the count does not depend on is a better object for a protocol than one that merely counts."@en,
        "What this does not settle. Two treatises of a collection of some sixty, in one nineteenth-century English translation, with the Greek absent for the one that carries the term. The Epidemics stand in the same volume and were not read, and they are the place a run should open that wants to know whether this corpus held its own term against its own cases."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "Both treatises read chapter by chapter in Adams's English. Within the volume, whole-text search of the two chapter ranges, after whitespace normalisation, for: deadly, mortal, fatal, dies, die, death, survive, live longer, sooner, later, shorter, interval, term, day, days, seventh, fourteenth, twenty, forty, sixty, summer, winter, spring, autumn, season. Every hit inside the two ranges read at its place. The running-head sequence of the volume was checked for monotonicity over both ranges before any page number was taken from it, and is monotone from 226 to 256 and from 424 to 466 with one single-digit slip. The Greek of the Prognostic was read alongside at the decisive clauses in the Loeb volume hippocrates02hippuoft; the Greek of the head-wounds treatise is not present in its Loeb full text at all, measured as zero characters in U+0370-U+03FF and U+1F00-U+1FFF." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:HippocratesVulnusCapitisTempusAnni ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-03" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Hippocrates, On Injuries of the Head, chapters 1 to 21, and The Book of Prognostics, chapters 1 to 25, both read continuously in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, pp. 445-466 and 234-256" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Whether anywhere in these two treatises a wound at a named site is coupled to a counted number of days until death or until a crisis; and, if such a count exists, what it is indexed on. The question was left open by the run of 2026-10-02, which found the list of fatal parts in the Aphorisms carrying no interval and recorded in its own note that On Injuries of the Head was the place that would decide whether that silence belonged to the Aphorisms or to the collection." .

pmp:HittFailureConditionSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Praktikerrituale eine Bedingung nennen, unter der der Ritus nicht gewirkt hat"@de,
        "Whether the practitioner rituals name a condition under which the rite has not worked"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-06" ;
    skos:note "One of the five names it, and the one that names it is the one that also announces a sign of success. The ritual of Paškuwatti ends with the case in which the deity is not seen in the dream and does not sleep with the patient, and says what follows: the practitioner keeps performing the rite. So the corpus does carry the thing the holding was short of, and it carries it attached to its own neutralisation."@en,
        "The negative half of this search is therefore not an empty result and should not be read as one. What the four works lack is a failure condition; what they have in its place is entered, node by node, in traditions/hittite-ritual.ttl. A later run that wants the count of transmissions without a failure condition must not add these four to it without the qualification."@en,
        "What the other four carry instead, and the shape is uniform. The ritual of Tunnawiya counts twelve parts of the body and never lists them. The ritual of Maštigga declares the words cut off and seals the washing water where it cannot be fetched back. The ritual of Ḫantitaššu fixes five counts and ends with the bundle in the seal-house that nobody opens again. The ritual of Uḫḫamuwa makes its second entry condition the action of an unidentified hostile deity. Four different ways of leaving no outcome that would come out against the rite, and not one of them is the absence of a clause: each is a clause doing that work."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "Each tablet read paragraph by paragraph in the editors' numbering rather than searched by term, because four of the five are under fifty paragraphs. Where a term search was used over the transliteration it was for the negation in its two Hittite forms and the Akkadographic one, for the conditional particle, for the prohibitive, for the iterative suffix in the first person, and for the Sumerographic writings of day and of the numerals; every hit read in its place."@en ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:HittIssiskemiPat ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-06" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Five Anatolian practitioner rituals read in full at the wording: CTH 404.1.II (KBo 2.3+) and CTH 406 (KUB 7.8+KUB 9.27(+)KUB 7.5) and CTH 395.1 (KBo 11.14; KUB 43.57+KBo 18.174) and CTH 410 (HT 1 Vs. II 17-47) in the critical editions of the Mainz Beschwörungsrituale corpus, and CTH 409 (KUB 7.53+KUB 12.58) in Goetze and Sturtevant, American Oriental Series 14, 1938."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A clause telling the operator or the patient what would show that the rite had not worked, and what then follows: an interval after which nothing more comes, a sign of failure, a count completed without effect, an outcome the rite excludes. The holding had twelve announced signs of success against no condition of failure when this run opened, and the entrance of SPEC 14 that was taken sends a run to the corpora that would carry one."@en .

pmp:HittProvisionSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die anatolischen Rituale die Vorsorge für das Nichtwissen führen"@de,
        "Whether the Anatolian rituals carry the standing provision for not knowing what is being dealt with"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-06" ;
    skos:note "A second instance in the same corpus, found on the second pass over the middle of the ritual of Tunnawiya and therefore after this search was first run: the Old Woman covers the setting of the uncleanness with a triple disjunction — whether anyone made the patient unclean before the gods, or before the dead, or before mankind — and leaves the agent under the same construction. That is the closest thing in the Anatolian material to the Ugaritic instance of the same run, and it is in the operative speech. Node pmc:HittPaprahtaTriplex. It changes nothing about the decision not to enlarge the generalization and it does strengthen the observation underneath it: within one corpus and across three unrelated rituals the device appears three times, in three different forms, which is not what a borrowed formula looks like. That is an argument about the contact route and it is not strong enough to settle it."@en,
        "It found the device in the plague ritual, which addresses whichever deity of the enemy land has caused the death and never names one, and it found a weaker form in the sorcery material of the same corpus where the untwining is performed in both directions so that the operator need not know which way the sorcerer twined. The node is entered; the generalization is not enlarged."@en,
        "What would settle it, named so that a later run does not repeat the search. Not more witnesses of the device: a reading of the one position in the literature that asks of each proposed borrowing whether it is specific, exceptional and co-occurrent with others. That position was reached in the landscape stage of this run and the work is in print."@en,
        "Why the generalization was not enlarged, and this is the finding of the search rather than its by-product. One of the six witnesses already standing is the Mesopotamian exorcistic craft, and the Mesopotamian channel into the Anatolian ritual corpus is one of the live positions in the contact-route dispute of pmt:HittiteRitual. A witness whose independence of an existing witness is the open question of its own tradition cannot raise a count of witnesses. The Ugaritic strand of the same run found a second candidate of the same form in the expiation text RS 1.002 and it has the same defect, in the same direction and for the same corpus."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "The indefinite and relative pronouns in the address formulae of the five works, read in place; and over the rebuilt corpus lines the relative pronoun together with the word for deity and the words for the enemy land."@en ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:HittKuisDingirKurLukur ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-06" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The five works read in full at the wording, and the same CTH 380 to CTH 500 section of the Thesaurus Linguarum Hethaeorum digitalis Beta 0.2 for the address formulae."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "The device in generalizations/the-provision-for-not-knowing.ttl: where a transmission makes an operation depend on identifying what is being dealt with, a standing provision for proceeding without the identification, in the form of a disjunction over the candidates, a form of words for the case of not knowing, or an apparatus that needs no name."@en .

pmp:HittSealOpeningSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob ein anderes hethitisches Ritual seinen Rückstand versiegelt und sagt, wann er geöffnet werden darf"@de,
        "Whether any other Hittite ritual seals its residue and says when it may be opened"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-06" ;
    skos:note "It found one, in a ritual the run had already opened for another reason, and in both of its manuscripts and in a second version on a third manuscript. The ritual of Ḫantitaššu lays the things the patient has carried for seven days into his linen chest, which they do not open, puts the chest in the seal-house, and says that nobody opens it again. So the corpus states the irreversibility twice, once as a condition that cannot be met and once flatly, in two rituals that share no practitioner, no home city and no affliction."@en,
        "Outside those two the verb to open occurs eleven times in the section searched and never of a seal: it opens an offering pit, a vessel, a kneading trough, a grave-pit, and in one case a door. The seal-house occurs three times more and in each case as a storeroom something is simply put into, with no clause about opening. The device is therefore not a formula of the genre but a thing two rituals do."@en,
        "The first run of this search returned nothing, and the nothing was false. The extractor read only the normalised transliteration attribute that the glossed files of the corpus carry; the unglossed files, which include the main manuscript of the Maštigga ritual, carry their transliteration as element content and have no such attribute, so those documents came out as lines with no words at all. The search then found no instance of a clause that was sitting in the corpus it was searching. It was caught by putting the known clause in as a positive control and seeing the control fail. The rule is now in AGENTS.md; the point for a reader here is that a negative result from a corpus of this shape is worth nothing without a control."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "Over the rebuilt lines: the Sumerographic writing of seal with its stone determinative, the seal-house, and every spelling of the verb to open attested in the corpus in both its initial signs, together with the negation and the indefinite pronoun. Hits counted per CTH number and every hit read in its line and in its paragraph, and the paragraphs around the hits read in the critical edition where one exists."@en ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:HittEKisibUlKinuzi ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-06" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The whole ritual and magic section of the Thesaurus Linguarum Hethaeorum digitalis, Beta 0.2, Müller, Prechel, Rieken and Schwemer, deposited 2025-03-07 under CC-BY, read as the deposited XML: every manuscript filed under CTH 380 to CTH 500, 66,530 tablet lines in 1,061 documents, rebuilt into transliterated lines with their tablet loci."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A second instance of the device at pmc:HittKisibApiyakku: a ritual that puts something away under seal and states a condition for its being opened, or states that it is not to be opened. The question was raised by the Maštigga clause and the search was run before anything was written about it, as the counter-search rule requires."@en .

pmp:HittSecondTranslationSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob für die ohne Übersetzung gelesenen Werke eine zweite Übersetzung erreichbar ist"@de,
        "Whether a second translation is reachable for the works read without one"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-06" ;
    skos:note "For the ritual of Ḫantitaššu the two editions that carry a translation, of 1996 and of 2016, are in print and neither is open. For the ritual of Tunnawiya the only further translation named in the current bibliography is in print. The portal publishes a translation page for each text and for these it is an empty shell. The freely posted back-volumes of the series stop at a volume number below the two monographs that would carry the rest."@en,
        "The outcome is entered as the corpus being unreachable rather than as nothing found, because the works exist, are named, and are closed. That is the distinction the scale was built for, and prima-materia#691 is the standing finding that the depth rule can be unsatisfiable; this run is a second case of it and is recorded there."@en,
        "What was read instead, so the shortfall is exact and not general. For the ritual of Tunnawiya: the 1938 transliteration and English translation, checked against an independent modern transliteration with interlinear glossing published under CC-BY-SA. For the ritual of Ḫantitaššu: the critical transliteration of both exemplars and the second version, checked against the editors' own German summary of contents paragraph by paragraph, which is a check on the order of acts and the counts and not on the wording. For the ritual of Paškuwatti: the critical transliteration and one English translation of 1969 that reads the practitioner's name differently."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:corpusUnreachable ;
    pm:searchTerms "The works named in the two critical editions as editions and translations were each looked for by author and title on the open full-text archive, on the publisher-hosted open-access collections, in the freely posted back-volumes of the series the editions appear in, and in the portal's own translation pages."@en ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-06" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The reachable editions and translations of CTH 409 and of CTH 395, as the bibliographies of the critical editions and the concordance of the Hittite portal name them, and the open-access full-text routes this holding uses."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A second translation of the ritual of Tunnawiya beside the one of 1938, and any translation at all of the ritual of Ḫantitaššu. The depth rule of SPEC 15 asks for the primary text and at least two translations, and this run could meet it for two of the five works and not for the other three."@en .

pmp:HohmanTestimonialsExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Prüfung der Vierundzwanzig-Stunden-Frist der Zeugnisse"@de,
        "Examination of the twenty-four hour interval in the testimonials"@en ;
    skos:note "Against it stands the base rate, and it is large. A shot-blister on the eye, a sore finger, a headache and a mouth sore remit without treatment, most of them within a day. The section reports no case that failed and gives no denominator, so what it offers is fifteen successes out of an unknown number of applications. That is the condition under which the state stays at procedure without cases: a series of confirmations with no count of the attempts is not a set of cases."@en,
        "No counter-search state, because no evidence from outside the transmission has been taken in. The counter-search here would be the record of Berks and Bucks County between eighteen twelve and eighteen nineteen, which nobody has opened for these names."@en,
        "The interval is what makes the section examinable, and it is the transmission's own. Eleven of the fifteen cases give one: a little more than twenty-four hours, in a short time, in some seven or nine hours, by the following morning. A claim with a deadline can be missed; a claim of healing without one cannot."@en,
        "The mortification case is the one that could not be explained by remission and is the one that ought to be checked first, if any record of it survives. It is also the case the preface is written against, since the preface pronounces a woe on whoever lets a man lose life or limb to mortification when this booklet could have helped."@en,
        "Two of the fifteen are not the author's own work. The boy of Johann Bingeman, in whom mortification had already set in, and the erysipelas on Bingeman's wife were both treated by the author's wife; the case of Jacob Staufer's child was performed by Staufer himself after his neighbour urged it. The last is the strongest of the fifteen for the transmission rather than for the man, because there the book worked in a hand that was not its author's."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-13" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "An application of the kind the section reports after which the complaint persists beyond the interval the section states for it, that is beyond a little more than twenty-four hours for the eye and the limb, beyond seven to nine hours for the erysipelas on the cheek, and beyond the following morning for the erysipelas on the finger."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-13" ;
    pm:tests pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicSchussblatterHeilung .

pmp:HuacaFeast a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Fest der Huaca"@de,
        "The feast of the huaca"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V" ;
    skos:definition "The senior specialist gives notice when the time comes, the chicha is brewed, and he goes through the households collecting the offerings. On the appointed day he goes with his assistants to the principal huaca and makes the offering, then to the lesser huacas and the ancestor bodies. On his return nobody sleeps that night: they sing, dance and tell tales, and children are beaten if they fall asleep. This night is called the pacaricuc, and from it the fast begins — no salt, no chilli, no sleeping with wives — lasting five days in some places and longer in others according to different traditions. Three such feasts are held in the year, tied to the Pleiades, to the first rains and to the maize harvest."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmp:HuangtingKouchiSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Huangting neijing jing das Zähneklappern vorschreibt"@de,
        "Whether the Huangting neijing jing prescribes the tooth-clapping"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-11" ;
    skos:note "Limits. This establishes an absence in one recension of one scripture and nothing more. The outer scripture stands in the same volume of this reproduction and was not searched; neither were the commentaries, which is where the practice is expected to be and where a positive finding would be no surprise. An absence in a work of some two and a half thousand characters of verse is a weak thing to build on, and it is entered as a statement about the wording of this text, not about the transmission that carries it."@en,
        "What the work has in the place where the clapping would stand is the swallowing, and it is counted: the thirty-six swallowings in the Jade Pond by which the hundred vessels open. The one number the received practice and the canon text share is therefore attached in the text to a different act from the one the practice attaches it to."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of all thirty-six chapters for the compound 叩齒 (no hits) and for the single graph 齒 (five hits, every one of them read: the tooth god and his courtesy name in chapter seven, the teeth as the granary attended by the two bright lads in chapter eight, the washing of hair and teeth in chapter twenty, the thirty-six swallowings that follow the essence of hair and teeth in chapter twenty-one, and the teeth made firm and the hair kept black in the same chapter). Searched further for 扣 and 擊 as alternative graphs for the striking, with no hits, and for the counts 三十六 and 二十四 in order to see what the work does count." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:ShangqingHuangtingKouchi ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-11" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Huangting neijing yujing, thirty-six chapters in one juan (DZ 331), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile; searched over the whole work in the Chinese, with every hit read at the wording, the work being short enough that the search and the reading cover the same text" ;
    pm:searchedFor "The tooth-clapping, with its counts of three, twelve, twenty-four and thirty-six, which the compiler expected to find in this work and did not. The question put was whether the canon text of the scripture prescribes it at all. Where the practice does come from, if not from here, is a further question and one this search does not answer, because it was carried in the canon text alone and in no commentary." .

pmp:HuangtingLocationSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Huangting neijing jing sagt, wo der Gelbe Hof liegt"@de,
        "Whether the Huangting neijing jing says where the Yellow Court is"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-11" ;
    skos:note "Limits. A negative finding on a spelling is a finding about that spelling, and this work is written in a deliberately allusive register in which a thing may be named by a figure rather than by its word: a line that says where the Yellow Court is without saying Yellow Court would not be reached by any term list. What the search does establish is that the two places where the work uses the words of its own title do not carry a location and are not adjacent to one."@en,
        "The work is not vague about the body. It gives the upper palace nine chambers of one square inch each, it names thirteen organ gods with their own courtesy names and colours of dress, it routes the fluid from the dark gullet through the bright hall to the throat. The term in its title is the one thing in it that receives no place, and it is unlocated not by omission in a thin text but by omission in a text that locates everything else."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of all thirty-six chapters for 黄庭 in both graphs of the first character, seven hits, of which five are the titles at the head and foot of the volume and the chapter heading, leaving two in the verse. Searched then for the spatial terms the work does use, in order to see whether any of them stands beside it: 泥丸 and its variant spelling 泥九 (eight hits between them), 明堂 (four), 洞房 (two), 丹田 (one), 三田 (one), 脾 (eight), 寸 (six). Each hit was read at the wording and the two verse occurrences of the title term were read in their chapters entire." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:ShangqingHuangtingKouchi ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-11" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Huangting neijing yujing, thirty-six chapters in one juan (DZ 331), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile; searched over the whole work in the Chinese, with every hit read at the wording" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage in which the work locates the thing it is named after, whether in the head, the heart, the spleen, the lower field or anywhere else. The question was put because the secondary accounts disagree about it and each of them cites this scripture." .

pmp:Huikala a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Huikala"@de,
        "Huikala"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Nathaniel B. Emerson, Unwritten Literature of Hawaii: The Sacred Songs of the Hula, Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 38 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1909), chapter II, p. 15" ;
    skos:definition "The cleansing by which a member defiled through contact with a corpse is restored to the company: the teacher offers prayer, sprinkles the offender with sea water and turmeric, and orders a bath in the sea, after which the person is clean. A wilful transgression is not covered by it and is restored only after penitence and confession."@en ;
    skos:note "The distinction between accidental defilement and wilful breach is drawn by the transmission itself and drawn at the point where it matters: the rite removes the first and does not touch the second. A restoration procedure that admits it cannot cover every case is rarer in this collection than one that claims to cover all."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HawaiianRitual .

pmp:IbogaFirstDescriptionSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Notizen von 1901, die das Alkaloid zuerst isolierten, im Wortlaut erreichbar sind"@de,
        "Whether the 1901 notes that first isolated the alkaloid are reachable as wording"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-05" ;
    skos:note "Neither is reachable as wording and the ways tried are these. The Académie volume is on Gallica as a public-domain digitisation, but it has no text layer: the full-text index returns nothing even for Académie and science, and the per-page OCR route answers 500 for every view of it. The series is on archive.org issue by issue for other years - fifty-three items for 1907 alone - and returns nothing at all for 1901. The Linnean bulletin volume is in the Biodiversity Heritage Library, which answers 403 to every machine request from this sandbox behind a challenge page, and no mirror of the volume was found; the article index of that library's own article service does not cover it."@en,
        "One substitute path was found, read, and deliberately not used for a node. The Bulletin des sciences pharmacologiques for 1905 is reachable in full and its bibliographic section reports the matter in a few lines: that Landrin isolated from the root of Tabernanthe iboga H. Bn. an alkaloid, l'ibogine, said to be physiologically a little different from the ibogaïne extracted at the same period by Haller. That is a report of two publications and not either of them, and it would at most let a later run cite the chain through a named intermediary with pm:readVia; it establishes nothing about what the 1901 notes say. It does show that the two alkaloid determinations were in dispute from the start, which is worth knowing before anybody takes the pharmacological clause of Trilles' footnote at face value."@en,
        "The access finding this leaves for the table in AGENTS.md: on Gallica the presence of a text layer is testable in one request, by putting a word that must occur in any volume of the kind through the full-text search service; a volume that returns nothing for such a word is a picture book, however complete its digitisation. The per-page OCR route works where the layer exists and answers 500 where it does not, and the plain text route is closed to scripts by a challenge."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:corpusUnreachable ;
    pm:searchTerms "iboga, ibogaine, ibogaïne, Tabernanthe, Dybowski, Landrin, Baillon, run against the Gallica full-text index of the volume and against archive.org identifiers for the series and for the Linnean bulletin; the Gallica text layer was tested by running the trivial terms Académie and science against the same volume" ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-05" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Comptes rendus hebdomadaires des séances de l'Académie des sciences, tome 133 (July to December 1901), for Dybowski and Landrin, Sur l'iboga, ses propriétés excitantes, sa composition et sur l'alcaloïde nouveau qu'il renferme, pp. 748-750; and the Bulletin mensuel de la Société linnéenne de Paris, nouvelle série, volume 2 (1889), p. 783, for Baillon's first description of Tabernanthe iboga" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Whether the two foundational notes the initiation literature rests on can be read at the wording, so that the pharmacological character Trilles attributes to ibogaïne at page 271 can be checked against the publication he is repeating rather than taken from him. The question is not about the transmission; it is about whether a modern identification inside a field record is traceable to its source." .

pmp:IntervalReleaseClauseSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob jede gezählte Schadensfrist im Bestand eine Entlastungsklausel trägt"@de,
        "Whether every counted interval of harm in the holding carries a release clause"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-02" ;
    skos:note "The intended generalization is refuted and was not written. The counter-case is pmc:TheNamedManDiesWithinTenDays in traditions/kongo-nkisi.ttl: the corpus states that a man whose name is called at the felling of the tree dies, certainly within ten days, that the named one never fails to die, and that all idea of poisoning or of the death being hurried on materially is repudiated, the practitioner being possibly miles away. That is a counted interval of harm with an explicit claim of a perfect record and no clause that releases a case from it. A second, weaker counter-case stands at pmc:RomanusNonusDies in traditions/european-folk-magic.ttl, where a blessed broken leg is to be straight again by the ninth day with nothing said about the ninth day passing — though the same blessing pairs it with a term that never falls due at all."@en,
        "The method has a known hole and it is the one this holding keeps finding: the query matched literals, so a node that states an interval in words the pattern does not cover was not reached. Nine nodes out of twenty-eight thousand triples is a thin yield for a holding this size, and the likeliest reading is that the pattern is narrow rather than that the holding carries nine."@en,
        "Two near misses that are not counter-cases and are recorded so that the next run does not re-read them. The bare-depot pulses of Suwen 19 give graded terms and the last of them is explicitly unbounded, one who sees people dying at the time of the overcoming phase, so the release is in the text. The dispute node on the Chinese intervals reports a strike through the diaphragm after which the patient dies within the year however well he seems to recover, which is the inverse of a release — a clause that takes an apparent recovery out of the counter-evidence — and it belongs to the same family rather than against it."@en,
        "What survived the search is narrower and is in the holding as pmc:TheIntervalRidesOnTheClassification. The refuted statement was about release clauses; the surviving one is about where the interval comes from, and the Kongo case does not touch it because the site there is a name and not a place on a body."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Query over every literal of the compiled graph for a term of harm and a counted interval in the same literal: wound, wounded, strike, struck, stab, pierce, injure, cut, needle, blow, bite, bitten, poison, sting, each against in or within or after or on plus a number from one to forty plus day, days, hour, hours, month, months, fortnight, night, nights, or against the third, fifth, seventh, ninth, tenth or fortieth day. Nine nodes matched and all nine were read at their nodes." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-02" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The compiled holding of prima-materia as of main at bf46350, all directories, queried over the merged graph" ;
    pm:searchedFor "The counter-search SPEC §16 requires before a generalization is written, and the generalization it was carried for was this: that where a transmission couples an injury at a named site to a counted interval of death, it also states, in the same place, a clause that takes a case out of the interval. The question put to the holding was its negation — is there a counted interval of harm in the holding stated with no release of any kind, so that the arrival of the term without the outcome would tell against the claim and nothing in the passage covers it." .

pmp:InuitMeatForkDivination a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Wahrsagung mit der Fleischgabel"@de,
        "Divination with the meat fork"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 24" ;
    skos:definition "A procedure used to decide whether a sick person will live: the meat fork is set up in the cooking pot, and the way it comes to rest is read as the answer."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InuitAngakkuq .

pmp:InuitReturnFailureExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Wiederaufleben in der berichteten Fassung überhaupt scheitern kann"@de,
        "Whether the return of the dead can fail at all as the tradition reports it"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that a dead man over whom the words were spoken can come back among the living, held here to record that the one reported failure was assigned to a broken prohibition and that no condition for failing the claim itself is available."@en ;
    skos:note "No falsifying condition is given, and this is the permitted omission: the state says outright that none has been devised. Stating one here would misreport the situation, because the obstacle is not that nobody has looked but that the claim as transmitted has no failing case by construction. Whether a condition can be recovered by fixing the interval and the announcement in advance is an open question and belongs to whoever takes it up."@en,
        "The asymmetry with the protocol above is worth naming. Both claims come from the same narrator on adjacent pages; one is universal and therefore refutable by a single case, the other is hedged by a clause that absorbs every case. The difference is in the form of the claim and not in the strength of the testimony behind it."@en,
        "The passage is unusual in reporting a failure at all. What it does with the failure is the finding: the attempt is said to have miscarried because a third person had sewn new caribou skin where that is forbidden, so the practitioners and the claim are both left untouched. Success shows the power, failure shows the breach, and no outcome is left over that could count against the claim."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:noProcedureDevised ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-08-31" ;
    pm:tests pmc:WordsAtBirthYieldReturnAfterDeath .

pmp:InuitSecondSightExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Vorherwissens-Behauptung ihre eigene Allgemeinform übersteht"@de,
        "Whether the foreknowledge claim survives its own universal form"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that a named person always knows in advance when anything remarkable is going to happen. The claim is examinable because it is universal and because the tradition itself counts the announcement, not the interpretation, as the thing that happens."@en ;
    skos:note "One confirming case is related in the same passage and no failing case is. The case does not count against the condition and does not count for it either: it was told after the event it is said to have foretold, by the wife of the man it is told about, and a dream of a friend eaten by his kin is a description that the encounter afterwards fitted rather than a prior announcement of that encounter."@en,
        "The remainder of the volume was not read in the run that opened this protocol, and it is precisely there that a failing case would be found if one is recorded at all. The state is therefore procedure-without-cases and not cases-without-deviation: the search has begun and has not been carried."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, working from the passage recorded in traditions/inuit-angakkuq.ttl" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "One recorded remarkable event in the bearer's presence that he did not announce beforehand. The claim says always, so a single unannounced case refutes it as stated. A weaker form, that such announcements occur more often than chance would give, would require announcements collected before the events they name, with the failures kept alongside the hits." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-08-31" ;
    pm:tests pmc:HusbandHasSecondSight .

pmp:IuratusFailureLocusSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob eine Klausel des Werkes einen Fehlschlag auf die Kunst, das Buch oder das Verfahren legt"@de,
        "Whether any clause of the work lays a failure on the art, the book or the procedure"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-07" ;
    skos:note "Nothing, and the nothing is uniform. Every failure clause in the work lands on the operator: he is unclean, he is in deadly sin, he is troubled with worldly things, he does not hold the catholic faith, he is not a Christian, or God has refused him and he must ask again. The two occurrences of nulla and the one of imperfecte are all in the passage on pagan and Jewish operators and say that their works are null, which is the same direction. The book is never once at fault in its own account."@en,
        "This is the exhibited gap of this run and it is worth more than a filled one. A transmission that states a visible penalty for the wrong operator and no outcome at all for the right operator who gets nothing has put its whole examinability on one side of the operator. The holding carries the shape as a generalization at generalizations/the-excuse-that-stops-at-the-officiant.ttl and at generalizations/displaced-penalties.ttl from other transmissions; this witness was not added to either, because the first work of this same tradition stands behind pmc:ArsNotoriaCrimenKautel and two works of one transmission do not count twice."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "In the Latin: defectu, defectus, ars non, liber non, falsa, frustra, nihil, nulla, imperfecte, and the names of the art and the book with a negation in the same sentence. In the English: fault, defect, the art is, the book is, will not work, cannot, and the two occurrences of plague and the one of madness read in their chapters. All hits of nulla and imperfecte read in place."@en ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-07" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The same two witnesses of the Liber iuratus Honorii: Royal MS 17 A. xlii in the English of the digital edition of Joseph H. Peterson, and Sloane MS 3854 in the Latin of Boudet's critical edition of 2002."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A clause that attributes a failure to anything other than the operator: a defect of the art, a corrupt exemplar, a procedure that does not always work, a case the book admits it cannot deliver. The question is the counter-search to every prerequisite node of this file, since a prerequisite of stated necessity is only examinable if the source does not also leave a second explanation open."@en .

pmp:IuratusTerminusSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob ein zweites Werk der Notoria-Gattung einen verstreichbaren Termin und ein Misslingenszeichen trägt"@de,
        "Whether a second work of the notory genre carries a term that can elapse and a sign of failure"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-07" ;
    skos:note "And the same search found the device that takes it back, which no part of the question anticipated. The chapter that states the verdict also states what is done when the verdict is a refusal: the three days again with alms for the Sunday fast, then the first moon, the second, the third, the fourth and so on to the end, and in the later prayers the petition is exchanged, from the petition for the grant to the petition for mercy. The falsifiable outcome and its neutralisation are four sentences apart. Node pmc:IuratusRepulsaIteratio."@en,
        "The genre carries both, and more sharply than the work that raised the question. The three days of the first purification end in a verdict delivered on a named night by an angel and stated as a pair, concessus vel repulsa, so the outcome is binary and falls due. Working the vision in deadly sin is said to leave the operator mad ever after, with a mechanism given and with an appeal to cases the author claims to have seen. Saying the orations without chastity and devotion is said to bring an open plague from God. So the answer to prima-materia#640 is that the absence it reported was a property of the seven works it had, not of this field."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "In the English: fast, day, days, thirteenth, moon, thrice, revealed, granted, denied, put back, mad, plagued, despair, every hit read in its chapter. In the Latin: dies, diebus, luna, lunatio, hora, terminus, repulsa, concessus, insanus, demens, amens, plaga, frustra, nihil proficiet, cavendum, and the conditional and negative particles, every hit read in its paragraph and in its folio."@en ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:IuratusConcessusVelRepulsa,
        pmc:IuratusInsania,
        pmc:IuratusPlagaKautel ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-07" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The Liber iuratus Honorii in two witnesses read at the wording: the sixteenth-century English translation of London, British Library, Royal MS 17 A. xlii, complete as far as it runs, in the digital edition of Joseph H. Peterson, 1998/2009, rev. 2024; and the Latin of London, British Library, Sloane MS 3854 in the critical edition of Jean-Patrice Boudet, Melanges de l'Ecole francaise de Rome. Moyen Age 114/2 (2002), pp. 851-890, read page by page over its thirty-nine edited sections."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "The question left open at prima-materia#640: whether the term within which an effect is to appear is a property of the Ars Notoria alone or of its genre, to be tested at the cheapest place, namely a second work of the same genre; and with it a condition under which the operator would know the thing had not worked."@en .

pmp:IuratusVisionIntervalSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Werk eine Frist nennt, nach der die Schau selbst nicht gekommen ist"@de,
        "Whether the work states an interval after which the vision itself has not come"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-07" ;
    skos:note "So the two negatives of this run point the same way. The work is examinable about who may operate and unexaminable about whether the operation worked, and both are stated rather than left out."@en,
        "The counted days run up to the operation and stop there. The operator sleeps on the thirteenth day and the text goes straight from the sleep to the sight, with no interval and no provision for a sleep that gives nothing. The interval this work carries belongs to the petition and not to the vision: the verdict of pmc:IuratusConcessusVelRepulsa falls thirteen days before the operation and says whether the petition is granted, not whether the sight came."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "The thirteenth day and the Thursday in both witnesses, then every numeral and every word for night, day, moon, hour and time in the four chapters of the operation, read in place; in the Latin dies, nox, nocte, hora, luna, usque ad, infra, post, quousque."@en ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-07" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The same two witnesses, with chapters XCVIII to CII read in full in the English of Royal MS 17 A. xlii and sections 13 to 16 of Boudet's edition read in full in the Latin of Sloane MS 3854."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A number of nights, a count of attempts or a date by which the sight of the Deity is to have appeared, and a statement of what follows if it has not. The thirteen counted days are the term of the preparation; the question is whether the term of the effect is given too."@en .

pmp:JainaAnasanaItvara a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Zeitweiliges Fasten in sechs gezählten Anordnungen"@de,
        "Temporary fasting in six counted arrangements"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXX.9-11 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:altLabel "anaśana"@en,
        "itvara"@en ;
    skos:definition "The first of the external austerities in its temporary form, divided into six kinds by the figure the successive fasts make: in the form of a line, of a square, of a cube, of a sixth power, of a twelfth power, and of any arrangement. The lecture adds that temporary fasting can be practised for different objects which one has in mind, and distinguishes the form in which a desire for food is present from the form in which none is."@en ;
    skos:note "A counted practice whose count is stated as a figure rather than as a number of days, and the only place in what was read where the transmission builds a schedule out of an arithmetic. The translator supplies the arithmetic from the commentary and remarks that the last two classes would require hundreds of thousands of years; that computation is his and the commentary's, and only the six names and the two distinctions are entered here."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmp:JainaConsequenceOfOmissionSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob eine jainistische Voraussetzung nennt, was dem widerfährt, der sie überspringt"@de,
        "Whether a Jain prerequisite states what befalls the one who skips it"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-16" ;
    skos:note "The search was carried twice on the same day and the two passes disagree, which is recorded here rather than tidied away. The first pass, on the term list above, came back empty: none of the conditionals it found attaches a consequence to an omitted step, and what they govern is conduct as a whole — if an ordained monk through carelessness does not strictly keep the great vows, then his fetters will not be completely cut off; in vain he adopts nakedness, who errs about matters of paramount interest. Both of those are entered at pmc:JainaLingamNaTrayate and both are about the man and not about the order of his acts."@en,
        "The second pass read a lecture the first had only seen in a search hit, and the passage is there. The sixteenth lecture of the Uttarādhyayana attaches to each of its ten conditions of celibacy, singly and by the same formula, a list of seven named outcomes of the breach, one of which — a dangerous illness of long duration — a bystander could record. It is entered at pmc:JainaBrahmacaryabhangaPhala and it carries the one pm:statedNecessity in traditions/jain.ttl."@en,
        "What the correction leaves standing. The other prerequisites entered from this transmission keep pm:prescribedOrder or pm:presentationOrder, because no passage attaches a consequence to them; the sixteenth lecture is the exception and not the rule, and the difference between it and the rest is now a fact of the holding rather than an artefact of one term list."@en,
        "Why the term search missed it is the useful part and is a finding about the method rather than about the corpus. The formula carries none of the markers the list was built from: it does not say if he does not, or unless, or otherwise, or in vain. It says that the preceptor has explained the reason for this, and then gives the outcomes as a chain of alternatives joined by or. A consequence stated as a list of outcomes, with no conditional marker in the sentence, is invisible to a search built on conditional markers, and this holding should expect that wherever a corpus states its reasons as consequences."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search of both volumes for: if he does not, unless, otherwise, will not attain, in vain, fails, fail, without which, without it, he cannot. Every hit read in its place."@en ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:JainaBrahmacaryabhangaPhala,
        pmc:JainaDashaPresupposesCelibacy ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-16" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Hermann Jacobi, Jaina Sūtras Part II, Sacred Books of the East 45 (Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010) and Jaina Sūtras Part I, Sacred Books of the East 22 (Oxford 1884; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 1964), both volumes searched whole by term; the ordered sequences at Uttarādhyayana XXIX.72, XXX.7-11 and XXX.30-36 and at Ācārāṅga I.7.8 read in full."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage attaching a named consequence to the omission of a step or to taking a prescribed sequence out of order. Every prerequisite claim entered from this transmission is set at pm:prescribedOrder rather than pm:statedNecessity, and under pm:StatedNecessityShape that setting has to rest on the absence of a quotable consequence rather than on not having looked."@en .

pmp:JainaLabdhiSucipatraSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der durchnummerierte Vermögenskatalog in Übersetzung erreichbar ist"@de,
        "Whether the numbered catalogue of capacities can be reached in translation"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-17" ;
    skos:note "One correction the attempt did produce, and it is recorded here rather than entered as a node because it was not read at the wording. The tenfold laddhi of the fifth Anga at the place prima-materia#721 names is, in the analytical index of that book that could be obtained, a list of the soul's basic faculties — knowledge, belief, conduct, giving, gain, enjoying, using, willing and the senses — and not a list of operative powers. The word is the same and the thing is not, which is a good reason for a later run to reach the catalogue claim through the works rather than through the term."@en,
        "The finding is therefore about the state of the field and not about the transmission, which is what this outcome value is for. Whether the Jain corpus carries a numbered and closed catalogue of capacities is open, and the note that would have to accompany any answer is that the works said to carry it are readable only by someone who reads Prakrit."@en,
        "The ways SPEC 15 requires before unreachability may be asserted were tried and are named. Another identifier for the same work in the same archive: Leumann's edition of the Aupapātika of 1883 is there in full text and is Prakrit text with a German glossary, with no translation, and a second edition of the same work of 1931 likewise carries only the text. An older or public-domain translation: none exists in any language, and no translation volume to Leumann's text was ever published. Another transliteration of title or author: all the forms above were tried. The work inside a collection or a commentary printing it entire: the Tiloyapaṇṇatti stands in the Sholapur edition of 1951 with a Hindi introduction and no translation of book 4. Another digitisation project: the field's own text collections carry romanised Prakrit and no translation. Nothing was blocked at the access path; the translations do not exist."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:corpusUnreachable ;
    pm:searchTerms "Searched under the title forms Aupapātika, Aupapātika Sūtra, Ovavāiya, Uvavāiya, Ovavāiya Suttam, Tiloyapaṇṇatti, Tiloya Pannatti, Trilokaprajñapti, Pravacanasāroddhāra, Viśeṣāvaśyaka Bhāṣya, and under the author forms Yativṛṣabha, Yativrishabha, Jadivasaha, Nemicandra, Jinabhadra; and for the item names khelosahi, jallosahi, vipposahi, amosahi, savvosahi, kottha-buddhi, bija-buddhi, pada-buddhi, padanusari, sambhinnasoya, akkhina-mahanasiya, ujjumai, vipulamai, veuvviya, carana-labdhi, labdhi, laddhi, rddhi, riddhi."@en ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-17" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The works named in prima-materia#721 as carrying the catalogue: the Aupapātika Sūtra (Ovavāiya), first Upāṅga; the Tiloyapaṇṇatti of Yativṛṣabha, book 4; the Pravacanasāroddhāra of Nemicandra; and the Viśeṣāvaśyaka Bhāṣya of Jinabhadra. Searched for an English or German translation, in the digitised collections, in the nineteenth-century oriental series and in the journals of the field."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A translation in which the numbered catalogue of capacities obtained by austerity could be read at the wording, so that the items could be entered with their places. The claim that the Jain transmission carries such a catalogue came from outside this holding and is the reason prima-materia#721 stands."@en .

pmp:JainaSignOfSuccessSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der jainistische Bestand ein wahrnehmbares Zeichen des Gelingens nennt"@de,
        "Whether the Jain corpus names a perceptible sign that a practice has worked"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-16" ;
    skos:note "Nothing came back. Every occurrence of light, lustre and radiance in the two volumes belongs to one of four things that are not signs of a practice: the gods and their lustre, the mountain Sudarśana and the similes built on it, the lights and portents at the birth and death of the founder, and the fourteen dreams of his mother. The nearest thing to a sign is the statement that the twenty-ninth lecture makes at its close about absolute knowledge penetrating the whole universe, which is the attainment itself and not a mark of it."@en,
        "The one passage that speaks of a perception during a practice speaks against believing it: the man dying by the fast is told not to trust in the delusive power of the gods. That is entered at pmc:JainaDevamaya, and it makes the absence sharper rather than softer. The transmission expects something to be seen and tells the practitioner in advance that it proves nothing."@en,
        "What follows for a later run is where to look next rather than what to conclude. The sign literature of this transmission, if it has one, would be in the works with no public-domain English translation — the Aupapātika, the Pravacanasāroddhāra, the Tiloyapaṇṇatti — and the negative finding is about the four works read and says so."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search of both volumes, case-insensitive, for: sign, signs, token, tokens, radiance, radiant, lustre, fragrance, fragrant, warmth, glow, glowing, vision, appears, appeared, appearance, mark of, he will know, knows that he. Every hit read in its place."@en ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-16" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Hermann Jacobi, Jaina Sūtras Part II, Sacred Books of the East 45 (Oxford: Clarendon 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010), the Uttarādhyayana and the Sūtrakṛtāṅga; and Jaina Sūtras Part I, Sacred Books of the East 22 (Oxford: Clarendon 1884; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 1964), the Ācārāṅga and the Kalpa Sūtra. Both volumes searched whole by term; the hits read at the wording, and the lectures named in the coverage note of pmt:Jain read in full."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage in which the transmission tells the practitioner what he will perceive when a practice has taken effect: a light, a warmth, a fragrance, an apparition, a bodily change, anything at all by which he would know rather than be told. This holding carries such signs from the Chinese inner alchemy and from the Tibetan heat practice and treats them as their own harvest category, so their absence in a transmission with a capacity catalogue of its own had to be established and not assumed."@en .

pmp:JainaStatedTermSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob eine jainistische Praxis durch eine genannte Frist begrenzt ist"@de,
        "Whether a Jain practice is bounded by a stated term"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-16" ;
    skos:note "Four kinds of term came back and none of them is the kind that was looked for. A bound in births, at most three, on the purity of faith. A term the practitioner sets himself, the twelve years of neglecting the body. A date for a single attainment, given down to the muhūrta and the direction of the shadow. And counts of fasting stated in meals rather than in days, the sixth meal, the eighth, the tenth, the twelfth."@en,
        "The absent kind is the useful one. Nowhere does a passage say that a practice carried out for so long should have produced its result by then, which is the form from which a failure condition can be made. The nearest approach runs the other way: the two and a half days of fasting without water that stand immediately before the attainment in the Kalpa Sūtra are a circumstance of one event and not a rule."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search of both volumes for: within a, within one, within two, within three, within six, within twelve, six months, twelve years, after a day, after a month, after a year, after one night, muhurta, muhirta, fortnight, month, months, in due time. The hits read in their places; the numerals in the enumerations of the lifespans of classes of beings set aside as not being terms of a practice."@en ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:JainaKevalaprapti,
        pmc:JainaTribhavaAvadhi,
        pmp:JainaDvadashavarshaNirodha,
        pmp:JainaMahaviraUpadhana ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-16" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Hermann Jacobi, Jaina Sūtras Part II, Sacred Books of the East 45 (Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010) and Jaina Sūtras Part I, Sacred Books of the East 22 (Oxford 1884; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 1964), both volumes searched whole by term."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage that puts a term on a practice: a number of days, months or years within which the result is to appear, or after which it is not to be expected. The testability-driven entrance of SPEC §14 looks for exactly this, and the registration of this transmission was made on the expectation that a catalogue of capacities would carry one."@en .

pmp:JainaTejolesyaTermSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das fünfzehnte Buch dem Erwerb eines Vermögens eine genannte Frist setzt"@de,
        "Whether the fifteenth book puts a stated period on the acquisition of a capacity"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-17" ;
    skos:note "The corpus this was found in was reached only in abridged translation, and every node it yielded carries pm:mediatedAttestation with pm:readVia for that reason. A later run with the Prakrit edition should read these three places again before anything is built on them."@en,
        "Three kinds of term came back and the first two are the kind the earlier search missed. A course of asceticism followed for six months, after which the capacity is said to have arrived. A regimen of six months with an appearance dated to its last night and two named outcomes turning on what the practitioner does then. And a pair of predictions of death, six months against seven days, uttered by two men about each other in each other's presence, with a public said to have argued about which would prove true."@en,
        "What the find does not establish. None of the three is laid down as a rule for a practitioner: the first is narrated of one man, the third is a prediction about named persons, and only the second is stated as a general course. The gap the earlier search exhibited was for a period after which a result should have arrived and could be missed; the second of these comes closest to it and still puts the branch point in an act of the practitioner on the final night rather than in the arrival or non-arrival of a result."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "Both texts read whole; then searched for six months, seven days, seven nights, sixteen years, within, after the lapse of, at the end of, last night, term, period, fever, and for the Prakrit and Sanskrit forms of the capacity, teya-lessa, teyalesse, tejolesya, tejolesyah, tejolabdhi, sankhitta-viula, sanksipta-vipula. Every hit read in its place."@en ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:JainaSanmasaTejolesyaprapti,
        pmc:JainaSuddhapanayaAntyaratri,
        pmc:JainaUbhayamaranavacana ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-17" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Viyāhapannatti (Bhagavaī), book XV, in the two translations from the Prakrit that could be obtained: Ernst Leumann, Appendix I: Extracts from Bhagavatī XV on the Intercourse Between Mahāvīra and Gosāla Maṅkhaliputta, in W. W. Rockhill, The Life of the Buddha and the Early History of his Order (London: Trubner 1884), pp. 249-255; and A. F. Rudolf Hoernle, The Uvāsagadasāo, vol. II, Appendix I: The History of Gosāla Maṅkhaliputta, briefly translated from Bhagavatī saya XV (Bibliotheca Indica, Calcutta 1888), pp. 1-14. Both read in full and at the wording."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage putting a term on a practice: a number of days, months or years within which a result is to appear. The search that stands at pmp:JainaStatedTermSearch had reported this kind of term absent from the four works of the Jacobi volumes, and had said in its own note that the absent kind was the useful one. This search put the same question to a fifth work, named in prima-materia#722 and not opened by that run."@en .

pmp:JainaTejolesyaVidhiSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die erreichbaren Übersetzungen die Askese abdrucken, durch die die Feuerkraft erlangt wird"@de,
        "Whether the reachable translations print the discipline by which the fiery power is obtained"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-17" ;
    skos:note "Barnett and Barua were searched for the same thing because both handle this material and both are public domain. Barnett has no Gosāla narrative at all and gives only a note on the capacity; Barua reports the episode at length and likewise does not print the discipline."@en,
        "Neither translation prints it, and both mark the place where it would stand. Hoernle: Mahāvīra then explained to him the severe ascetic discipline by which he had obtained it, with the page of the Calcutta print against the sentence. Leumann: I explained to him the austere discipline which it required, and he thought of undergoing it. The sentence is there in both and its content is in neither."@en,
        "This is a finding about two translations and not about the transmission, and the distinction is the reason the search is recorded rather than the absence simply noted. Both renderings are condensations made by philologists working from the Prakrit, and both condense at exactly the sentence a reader of this holding wants. What the Prakrit carries at that place has not been established here."@en,
        "What the search did find is the posture, and it is entered at pmc:JainaAtapanabhumi: the man who already has the power is shown sitting with upraised arms and upturned face in the glare of the sun. That is one element of the reported formula, standing in the same episode and predicated of the wrong person. A reader may join it to the discipline; neither translation does."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Searched in all four works for: discipline, austerity, austere, asceticism, penance, fast, fasting, beans, bean, pulse, handful, water, palm, sun, solar, arms, raised, upraised, exposure, three days, every third day. Every hit read in its place."@en ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-17" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Viyāhapannatti (Bhagavaī), book XV, in Ernst Leumann's extracts in Rockhill, The Life of the Buddha (London: Trubner 1884), pp. 249-255, and in A. F. Rudolf Hoernle, The Uvāsagadasāo, vol. II, Appendix I (Bibliotheca Indica, Calcutta 1888), pp. 1-14. Also searched, for the same question: L. D. Barnett, The Antagaḍa-dasāo and Anuttarovavāiya-dasāo (Oriental Translation Fund NS 17, London 1907), and B. M. Barua, The Ājīvikas, Part I (University of Calcutta 1920)."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "The content of the discipline the book says Mahāvīra taught to Gosāla: whether either translation prints what it consisted of. The secondary literature reports a formula in the Prakrit with continuous two-day fasts, a handful of beans, a cupped handful of water and exposure to the sun with raised arms, and prima-materia#722 was opened on that report."@en .

pmp:JainaWarningAgainstThePowersSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der jainistische Bestand vor den Vermögen warnt, die er nennt"@de,
        "Whether the Jain corpus warns against the capacities it names"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-16" ;
    skos:note "The passage the landscape stage named is not the passage it was said to be, and the search is recorded partly for that. Uttarādhyayana XXXV.18 in the edition read forbids the monk to desire flowers, a seat, an eloquent greeting, presents or a magnificent welcome; it is about honours and says nothing of powers. The warning that was looked for stands elsewhere and in a stronger form, in the catalogue of the Sūtrakṛtāṅga and in the two dispositions of Uttarādhyayana XXXVI."@en,
        "What was found is not a warning against the capacities but a penalty on living by them, and the difference decides how the nodes are worded. In every one of the four places the condition is a motive: for the sake of food, drink, clothes, a lodging, a bed and objects of pleasure; for the sake of pleasure, amusement or power; as a sinful living. The transmission never says that the arts do not work, and in one place it says outright that they do."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search of both volumes for: supernatural, magic, magical, sorcery, miraculous, riddhi, labdhi, iddhi, augury, omen, omens, fortune-telling, spell, spells, incantation, charm, charms, exorcism, occult, prognostics. Every hit read in its place, and Uttarādhyayana XXXV read in full to check the passage the landscape stage named."@en ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:JainaAbhiyogikabhavana,
        pmc:JainaCikitsavidya,
        pmc:JainaNimittavidya,
        pmc:JainaPapavidya,
        pmc:JainaPapavidyaphala ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-16" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Hermann Jacobi, Jaina Sūtras Part II, Sacred Books of the East 45 (Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010) and Jaina Sūtras Part I, Sacred Books of the East 22 (Oxford 1884; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 1964), both volumes searched whole by term and the hits read at the wording."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage in which the transmission warns against the operative capacities themselves rather than against their misuse, which is the position the secondary literature ascribes to it and which the landscape stage of this run located at Uttarādhyayana XXXV.18 and at a work outside these two volumes."@en .

pmp:JeuAscensusPerAeones a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Form einer Station des Aufstiegs durch die Äonen"@de,
        "The form of a station of the ascent through the aeons"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Das zweite Buch Jeû, Handschriftenseiten 77-78, deutsche Übersetzung in Carl Schmidt, Gnostische Schriften in koptischer Sprache aus dem Codex Brucianus, Texte und Untersuchungen VIII 1-2, Leipzig 1892, S. 215" ;
    skos:definition "The unit the text repeats for each of the twelve aeons and beyond them. When you come out of the body and reach the aeon, and the archons of that aeon step before you — they are named — seal yourselves with this seal, whose name is given and drawn, say its name only once, grasp this cipher with both your hands; and when you have sealed yourselves with it and said its name only once, say these apologies: withdraw, and here the names of the archons of that aeon, for I invoke, and here three or four further names. When the archons of the aeon hear these names they are greatly afraid, scatter, flee to the west to the left, and you go up."@en ;
    skos:note "Three features of the unit are worth holding apart from the names. The count is fixed and small and is stated as a count: only once, at every station, and at the tenth aeon the text says it twice in one sentence, once of the name and once of the sealing. The cipher is held and not counted with: it is grasped with both hands or laid in them. And the apology is addressed to the beings of the station by name and gives as its whole ground that the passer is invoking other names, which are not translated and not explained."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu .

pmp:JeuBaptismaAquae a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Taufe des Lebenswassers"@de,
        "The baptism of the water of life"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Das zweite Buch Jeû, Handschriftenseiten 59-61, deutsche Übersetzung in Carl Schmidt, Gnostische Schriften in koptischer Sprache aus dem Codex Brucianus, Texte und Untersuchungen VIII 1-2, Leipzig 1892, S. 198-200" ;
    skos:definition "A sacrifice is laid out with one vessel of wine to its left and one to its right; juniper berries, kasdalanthos and nard are laid on; all the disciples are wrapped in linen garments, kynokephalon is put in their mouth, the cipher of the seven voices, 9879, and heliakon are laid in both their hands; linen cloths are spread at a place with a cup of wine on them and loaves according to the number of the disciples, olive branches are laid at the place of the offering and all are crowned with olive branches; they are sealed with a seal whose name is sazapharas and whose interpretation is thezozaz; they turn with him to the four corners of the world, each setting his feet close together, and he speaks the prayer."@en,
        "The first rite, with the whole of its apparatus as the text prescribes it. The disciples are sent up to Galilee to find a man or a woman in whom the greater part of the wickedness has died — if a man, one who holds no intercourse; if a woman, one who has ceased with the manner of women and holds no intercourse — and to receive two vessels of wine from the hands of such a person and to bring vine branches."@en ;
    skos:note "The condition on the wine is a condition on a third party who is not in the rite. What is required of him is a state of the body sustained over time, and the text gives two forms of it, one for each sex; it is not a purification he performs for the occasion. Nothing is said about how the disciples were to establish it."@en,
        "The prayer names fifteen servants of the seven light-virgins who are set over the baptism of life, and gives all fifteen names. It is the only place in what was read where a numbered class of beings is listed out in full and the list is legible from end to end."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu .

pmp:JeuBaptismaIgnis a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Feuertaufe"@de,
        "The baptism of fire"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Das zweite Buch Jeû, Handschriftenseiten 62-65, deutsche Übersetzung in Carl Schmidt, Gnostische Schriften in koptischer Sprache aus dem Codex Brucianus, Texte und Untersuchungen VIII 1-2, Leipzig 1892, S. 200-202" ;
    skos:definition "The second rite. Vine branches are brought and an incense laid on, with juniper berries, myrrh, frankincense, mastic resin, nard, kasdalanthos, terebinth and stakte; linen cloths are spread at the place of the offering with a cup of wine and loaves according to the number of the disciples; all put on linen garments and are crowned with upright peristereon, kynokephalon is put in their mouth, the cipher of the seven voices, 9879, and chrysanthemon are laid in both their hands and polygonon under their feet; they are set before the incense with their feet close together; he steps behind the incense and seals them with a seal whose name is thozaees and whose interpretation is zozazes; then he turns with them to the four corners of the world and speaks the prayer, which asks that Zorokothora Melchisedek come in secret and bring out the water of the baptism of fire of the Light-Virgin, the judge."@en ;
    skos:note "The prayer also enumerates what is to be forgiven, and the list is a list of deeds and not of states: slanders, curses, false oaths, thefts, lies, false accusations, fornications, adulteries, desires, covetousness, and what they have done from childhood until the present day."@en,
        "Two things distinguish this rite from the first in the wording. The prayer asks for a sign and not for a substance to appear in a vessel, and the sealing afterwards is on the forehead, with the seal of the Light-Virgin, which is said to be what has them reckoned to the kingdom of light."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu .

pmp:JeuBaptismaSpiritus a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Taufe des heiligen Geistes"@de,
        "The baptism of the holy spirit"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Das zweite Buch Jeû, Handschriftenseiten 65-67, deutsche Übersetzung in Carl Schmidt, Gnostische Schriften in koptischer Sprache aus dem Codex Brucianus, Texte und Untersuchungen VIII 1-2, Leipzig 1892, S. 203-204" ;
    skos:definition "The third rite. The incense of the baptism of the holy spirit is laid on, with vine branches, juniper berries, kasdalanthos, the remains of saffron, mastic resin, cinnamon, myrrh, balsam and honey; two vessels of wine are set up, one to the right of the incense and one to the left, with loaves on them according to the number of the disciples; the disciples are sealed with a seal whose name is zakzoza and whose interpretation is thozonoz; they are clothed in linen garments and set before the incense while the cipher of the seven voices, 9879, is in both their hands; he calls with a loud voice, speaks the prayer, and asks for a sign in the offering. He performs the rite while they are clothed in linen and crowned with myrtle, with kynokephalon in their mouth and a single shoot of artemisia in both their hands, their feet set close together, turning to the four corners of the world."@en ;
    skos:note "The one cipher of the three baptisms is the same at all three, 9879, and the text calls it the cipher of the seven voices. What changes from rite to rite is the incense, the wreath, the plant in the hands and under the feet, and the seal. The apparatus is therefore not a set of keys to three different places but one number carried through three operations."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu .

pmp:JeuChrismaSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die geistige Salbung in den Büchern des Jeu irgendwo vollzogen wird"@de,
        "Whether the spiritual anointing is anywhere performed in the Books of Jeu"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-27" ;
    skos:note "A negative finding about a damaged manuscript whose end is lost, and it is weaker for that than the shape of the passage suggests. What it establishes is that the announcement is not followed by the rite within the pages read, and that the recapitulation which is followed by the ascent does not name it. What it cannot establish is that the rite was never in the work."@en,
        "One occurrence of the word in the pages read, and it is the announcement. The four rites that follow are the three baptisms and the removal of the archons' wickedness, all four described with their full apparatus; the recapitulation four leaves later lists the mystery of the twelve aeons and those four, and not the anointing. The finding stands at pmc:JeuChrismaNonTraditum."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Read at the wording; looked for in that reading: Salbe, Salbung, geistige Salbe, chrisma, and the recapitulation of the rites received, which was read in full at manuscript page 77." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-27" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Carl Schmidt, Gnostische Schriften in koptischer Sprache aus dem Codex Brucianus, Texte und Untersuchungen VIII 1-2, Leipzig 1892, the German translation of the second book of Jeû; read at the wording over manuscript pages 55 to 70 and 76 to 78, that is pages 194 to 208 and 213 to 215" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage prescribing or describing the spiritual anointing, the fifth of the five things announced at manuscript page 56: an incense, a seal, a cipher, a prayer, or a report that it was performed." .

pmp:JeuClavisUniversalisSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob ein stationsweiser Aufstieg im Bestand sonst ebenfalls ein Stück trägt, das alle seine Stationen öffnet"@de,
        "Whether a station-by-station ascent elsewhere in the holding also carries one item that opens all its stations"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-27" ;
    skos:note "Not searched: anything outside this holding, and within it the traditions whose ascent material has no node yet. The holding carries some ninety corpora and the question is about the wording of transmissions, so the cheap search is the one that was run."@en,
        "The candidate was refuted at once and by two of the five. The Mithraic ladder of seven gates carries no item that opens all seven, and the Hekhalot palaces carry seals given palace by palace and no master seal. Two witnesses would have supported it — the one great name of the first book of Jeu, which is said to make the watchers and curtains of all sixty treasuries scatter, and the light-garment of the Pistis Sophia, which carries the name of every mystery and every order below — and both belong to the same transmission family, with a documented route between them. A statement resting on one family is a description of that family, and it was not written."@en,
        "What the same search did produce is the statement at pmc:ThePassageThatPutsNoQuestion, and it is worth more than the candidate was. The division the material actually carries is not between one key and many, but between stations that hand the passer a token and stations that put him a question: the first kind states no test and no failed case, the second states what failing costs. The counter-case that killed the first candidate is a witness for the second, which is the ordinary shape of a counter-search that was carried early enough."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Query over the compiled graph for every node with pm:withinTradition in the five traditions named, read at the label; then the definitions of pmc:PistisSophiaVestisNominum, pmc:PistisSophiaTransitusVisu, pmc:MithraicKlimaxHeptapylos, pmc:MithraicProbatioLetalis, pmc:WaterQuestionCaution and pmc:HekhalotDescentPresupposesFitness read in full." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:ThePassageThatPutsNoQuestion ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-27" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The compiled holding of prima-materia as of 2026-09-27, over the traditions that carry a graded passage: the Books of Jeu, the Pistis Sophia, the Hekhalot material, the Mithraic reports, and the Egyptian gate and arit material" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A counter-case and a confirming case for a candidate generalization formed in this run and not written: that a transmission teaching a passage through many guarded stations, each opened by its own name, seal and number, also carries one single item said to open all of them at once. Sought were transmissions with many stations and no such item." .

pmp:JeuPoenaProditionisSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Geheimhaltungsgebot der Bücher des Jeu eine Folge nennt"@de,
        "Whether the command of secrecy in the Books of Jeu names a consequence"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-27" ;
    skos:note "The nearest thing to a consequence anywhere in this corpus falls on the wrong party. The first book warns that one who bears the garment without knowing him is given over to destruction, which is a penalty on the unworthy bearer and not on whoever handed the garment over. Where a transmission of an operative text states its restriction in its own words and states no penalty, that is a fact about the restriction, and it is only countable if the asking is recorded."@en,
        "Three pages of prohibitions with nothing attached to their breach. The passage is unusually specific about whom the mysteries may not reach — it names a rival cult by its rite and its god, with the god's rank, name and face — and unusually silent about what follows from reaching them. That is why pmc:JeuMysteriaNonTradenda is a caution and not a prerequisite with a stated necessity: the wording does not carry the consequence the class would require."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Read at the wording over the whole of the command of secrecy, the list of the excluded, the definition of the worthy recipient and the ethical commands; looked for in that reading: wehe, Gericht, Strafe, verlieren, nicht wirken, zugrunde, Verderben." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-27" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Carl Schmidt, Gnostische Schriften in koptischer Sprache aus dem Codex Brucianus, Texte und Untersuchungen VIII 1-2, Leipzig 1892, the German translation of the second book of Jeû; read at the wording over manuscript pages 55 to 58, that is pages 194 to 197" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A stated consequence for the disciple who passes the mysteries to somebody the text excludes, or for the recipient who takes them without meeting the conditions: a penalty, a loss of the effect, a harm, a term." .

pmp:JeuSignumAbsensSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Bücher des Jeu sagen, was das Ausbleiben des erbetenen Zeichens zeigen würde"@de,
        "Whether the Books of Jeu say what the absence of the requested sign would show"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-27" ;
    skos:note "The finding is the shape of the three passages and not a silence at the edge of them. Each prayer names the condition — if the Father has heard, if he has forgiven, if they are counted among the heirs — and then asks for the sign as the consequence of that condition being met. The one-sided conditional is therefore not an oversight of wording: the text has the apparatus to say what a missing sign would show, uses it to say what a present sign shows, and stops there. Three times."@en,
        "This search is what pmc:JeuSignaTriaPetita rests on, and through it the case recorded against pmc:TheCriterionNoPartyControls at examinations/both-sides-of-the-criterion.ttl."@en,
        "What the search cannot say. It was carried over the pages named and not over the whole corpus; the ascent through the taxeis at manuscript pages 70 to 76, the third to the ninth aeon, and the diagram chapters of the first book were not read. A clause of this kind in one of them would overturn the finding, and the place to look first is the end, which is lost: the text breaks off before delivering the one mystery it has three times promised."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Read at the wording; looked for in that reading: Zeichen, Wunder, nicht, wenn nicht, falls, erhöre, and any conditional construction following the three prayers. The three sign passages were read in full, from the opening of each prayer to the sentence reporting the outcome, on pages 200, 202 and 204." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-27" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Carl Schmidt, Gnostische Schriften in koptischer Sprache aus dem Codex Brucianus, Texte und Untersuchungen VIII 1-2, Leipzig 1892, the German translation of the two books of Jeû; read at the wording over manuscript pages 39, 43 to 47, 55 to 70, and 76 to 86, that is pages 179, 183 to 186, 194 to 208, and 213 to 215, 219, 220 and 223" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A clause anywhere in the four rites, or in the pages read around them, stating what is to be done, or what would be shown, if the sign the prayer asks for does not come: the wine that does not become water, the sign that does not appear in the fire, the sign that does not appear in the offering." .

pmp:JibaroNatemaUmartinyu a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Trinken des natema beim Kopftrophäenfest"@de,
        "The drinking of the natema at the head-trophy feast"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Rafael Karsten, Blood Revenge, War, and Victory Feasts among the Jibaro Indians of Eastern Ecuador, Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 79 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1923), pp. 70-72" ;
    skos:definition "Pieces of the stem of Banisteria caapi, a vine specially cultivated, are crushed with clubs, parted into thinner fibres and boiled in water for a couple of hours — at the trophy feast for about one hour — and tobacco water boiled at a second fire is poured in. The slayer himself has to assist, his wrist held by the officiant, so that the power he is held to carry passes into the drink; two rings of samiki bark the size of his index finger are tied to a piece of manioc stem and set on the boiling tobacco pot to catch and carry that power. All who want to dream may drink, the slayer and his wife and daughter among them. Each drinker empties a dish of a little over half a litre, goes out and vomits it, and repeats this three times; they have eaten and drunk nothing beforehand and fast strictly afterwards until they have slept and dreamed."@en ;
    skos:note "The account of what the rings are for — to catch the slayer's power and transfer it to the manioc and the tobacco pot — is the recorder's statement of the object of the ceremony and reads as his summary of what the Jibaros hold. It is reported here and not made a claim of its own."@en,
        "The sequence is exact enough to repeat and the quantities are given, which is unusual in this holding for an operation of this kind. What it does not give is a term: nothing is said about how soon the dream must come or what follows if it does not."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:KarstenBloodRevenge ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JibaroShuara .

pmp:Karakia a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Karakia"@de,
        "Karakia"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), pp. 10-11, and the vocabulary entry karakia, p. 108" ;
    skos:definition "An address to the spirits of dead ancestors, invoked in genealogical order and closing in a petition that the invoked power give the address its effect. The witness insists that the ordinary rendering as charm is wrong and that invocation comes nearer."@en ;
    skos:note "The genealogies are said to be preserved because they form the ground-work of the invocation, so that the descent recited and the efficacy claimed are the same fact for this tradition rather than two."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Maori .

pmp:Kuahu a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Weihe des Kuahu"@de,
        "The consecration of the kuahu"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Nathaniel B. Emerson, Unwritten Literature of Hawaii: The Sacred Songs of the Hula, Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 38 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1909), chapter II, pp. 19-21" ;
    skos:definition "The setting up and consecration of the hula altar. The plants that may decorate it are a fixed short list held acceptable to the goddess, among them maile, ieie, lehua, ohia, ferns, hau, breadfruit, the native banana, ti and ilima; the recorder marks non-native flowers as repugnant to the tradition. During the consecration the voice of prayer must continue to be heard until the work is entirely accomplished, and the teacher may repeat a prayer or begin another to keep it so."@en ;
    skos:note "The condition here is on continuity and not on wording: what must not happen is a silence, and the teacher is expressly free to change the prayer in order to avoid one. That is the opposite of the exactness rule this same tradition sets for the chanted genealogy, and the two stand side by side in one transmission. The graph should not read either as the tradition's general doctrine about speech."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HawaiianRitual .

pmp:LatinMysticismCriterionSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob irgendetwas in derselben Ueberlieferung haelt, dass eine goettliche Sprache verbuergt, was sie sagt"@de,
        "Whether anything in the same transmission holds that a divine locution secures what it says"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-08" ;
    skos:note "Found, and in the sharpest possible place. The second founder of the same reform, writing for the same houses within about eight years, makes a certainty that no contrary outcome removes the third and most reliable sign that a locution came from God, and states its behaviour under disconfirmation in so many words: though it seem that everything goes contrary and years pass, the thought does not leave her that God will seek other means, and in the end it is done. The two positions are entered as a dispute at pmc:LatinMysticismCriterionDispute and neither is preferred."@en,
        "The find that made the search worth the second opening is not either position but the shared case. Both authors reach for Jonah at Nineveh, and they use him for opposite purposes: one to show that a prediction may pass its term unfulfilled without having been false, the other as what the soul is reminded of countless times when it fears the word it was given will be made false. A search that had stopped at the first work would have had the stronger half of the holding's best material on announced terms and would not have known that the same corpus contradicts it."@en,
        "What the search did not establish, said plainly. The Moradas were opened at one chapter and the rest of that work is unread, so this is not a survey of Teresa's position across her writings; the chapter itself refers to her Vida and to her Relaciones for cases, and neither was opened. The dispute entered here rests on one chapter against six, and a later run that opens the Vida may widen or narrow it."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchTerms "In the Spanish: senales, certidumbre, seguridad, asegurarse, cumplirse, no se cumple, verdaderas en si, locuciones, hablas, revelaciones, profecia, enganar, antojo, imaginacion, demonio; and the name Jonas with Ninive, which is what joined the two places." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:LatinMysticismCriterionDispute,
        pmc:MoradasCertitudoSuperstes,
        pmc:MoradasSenalesHablas ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-08" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The two works of the registered corpus of this transmission that treat locutions and revelations as a subject: Juan de la Cruz, Subida del Monte Carmelo, Libro II, the whole run of chapters on the apprehensions of the understanding, in the Spanish of the Gerardo edition, Toledo 1912; and Teresa de Jesus, Las Moradas, the sixth dwelling places, in the Spanish of the Navarro Tomas edition, Madrid 1910, with chapter 3 read complete." ;
    pm:searchedFor "The negation of pmc:SubidaVisionesVeraeFallaces and pmc:SubidaCausaVariabilis: a passage holding that a communication known to come from God does give security about what it says, so that non-fulfilment would count against it. The question was put to the same transmission first because that is where a counter-position would be most expensive to this holding and cheapest to find, and because SPEC 13 says a finding is relative to the search and not to the stock." .

pmp:LungReading a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Lesen an der Lunge des getöteten Tieres"@de,
        "Reading the lungs of the killed animal"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Cristóbal de Molina, Relación de las fábulas y ritos de los incas, ed. Paloma Jiménez del Campo, transcr. Paloma Cuenca Muñoz (Madrid: Iberoamericana / Frankfurt: Vervuert, 2010), fol. 6v, on the calparigu" ;
    skos:definition "The office named first in the earlier report: those who see the fortune and outcome of what is asked of them. For this they killed birds, lambs and llamas, and blowing through a certain vein into the lungs found in them signs by which they said what was to happen."@en ;
    skos:note "The one procedure in this file that reads a variable natural object rather than a count. It is the Andean counterpart of the guinea-pig inspection that the later account gives under two other names, and the two together make entrail reading the best-witnessed technique of the transmission."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmp:MagneticActionWithoutKnowledgeExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die magnetische Wirkung einen Menschen erreicht, der nichts von ihr weiss"@de,
        "Whether the magnetic action reaches a subject who does not know it is being applied"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. I, § 26, p. 26, and chap. VIII, pp. 170-171" ;
    skos:definition "The examined claim is the one the transmission needs in order not to be an account of expectation: that the operator's action produces its effects in the subject, and produces them at the places to which it is directed, independently of the subject's belief that it is being applied. The manual read for traditions/mesmerism.ttl asserts it in the form that confidence is necessary in the operator and not in the person operated upon, and that one can act equally upon those who believe and those who do not."@en ;
    skos:note "Four results, all read at the wording. A woman who had been the one of eleven persons sensible to one operator's action reported, blindfolded, sensations that no longer answered to the places towards which the action was directed: operated upon the stomach and the back she felt only heat in her head and pain in both eyes and the left ear. Blindfolded again with the operator removed, silence enjoined and the subject induced to believe the operation was being performed, the effects were the same though no operation near or distant was performed; and when after a quarter of an hour a sign was made for the operation to begin she felt nothing, the sensations diminishing instead of augmenting. A servant persuaded that he was being operated upon felt a nearly universal heat, emotions in the belly, extreme heaviness, and grew drowsy. A medical man believing the operation had been proceeding for ten minutes before it had begun felt a heat in his loins he compared to a stove, and felt nothing on several occasions when it was performed."@en,
        "The conclusion is stated in the form that makes it evidence and not opinion, and it is quoted here because the wording is the finding: that the imagination without the magnetism produces convulsions, and that the magnetism without the imagination produces nothing. The commissioners add that the existence of the fluid is destitute of proof, that having no existence it can have no use, and — an observation of their own, and a claim in its own right that this protocol does not examine — that the compressions and the repeated action of the imagination may be hurtful and that every public process employing these means must in the end produce pernicious effects."@en,
        "The design the commissioners used is the one that answers it and it is worth stating as a design, because it is better than the century it comes from. A bandage was made of two rubber cups filled with eiderdown, sewn into circles of cloth and tied behind the head, leaving room for the nose and full liberty of breathing and admitting no light above, below or through. Subjects already known to be sensible to the operation were blindfolded; for the most part they were not operated upon at all, and were put questions framed so as to point out the answer. The commissioners state the logic themselves: where no operation was performed the sole answer ought to have been that no sensation was felt, and where it was performed the impression and not the form of the question ought to have dictated the reply."@en,
        "The fifth is the one usually remembered and it belongs here because of who chose the subject. The commissioners required that the trial be made on a person of the practitioner's own choosing whose susceptibility he was already convinced of; he brought a boy of about twelve. An apricot tree in Franklin's orchard at Passy, standing clear of the others, was operated upon by the practitioner alone. The boy, blindfolded, was brought to four trees that had not been operated upon and made to embrace each for two minutes, the mode of communication the practitioner had himself prescribed, with commissioners standing between him and the boy so that no signal could pass. At the first tree the boy reported perspiring in large drops, coughing, spitting and a slight pain in the head; at the second stupefaction and head pain; at the third the two increased and he said he believed he was approaching the operated tree; at the fourth, some twenty-four feet from it, he fell into a crisis and fainted."@en,
        "The three checks SPEC requires of an evidence citation, carried one at a time. The work is named with translator, place, printer and year from its own title page, read as a whole. It supports the claim it is cited for and not a neighbouring one: the blindfold series is about the reach of the action and not about whether the cures happened, and this protocol says nothing about the cures. The population is the commission's own — eleven persons under one operator, of whom one was sensible; the servant and the medical man at that operator's house; and the boy of twelve chosen by the practitioner who was being examined. That last is the strongest single feature of the design and the one a reader should weigh: the subject was selected by the party with an interest in success."@en,
        "Why the state is not stronger than it is, and the reason is a date and a person. This commission examined one practitioner's practice in 1784; the manual examined in traditions/mesmerism.ttl was written in 1825 by a man who had read the report, refers to 1784, and holds that at that time the true principles were not yet known. The two are not the same operator and not the same method, and the commission's subjects were the practitioner's own choice under the older doctrine. The blindfold design nevertheless tests the claim and not the doctrine, because the claim is that the action reaches a subject who does not know of it, and that is what the bandage removes. Deviation was found, in five subjects, with a design whose outcome could have gone the other way, and that is what this state asserts."@en ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Searched for, and found at the wording: the transmission's own reply, in the work read for traditions/mesmerism.ttl, that at the time Mesmer first connected patients around the baquet he knew neither how to direct the agent nor how to calm the crises, that since 1784 the true principles being known the scenes which then made so much noise are no longer reproduced, and that the convulsive effects arise where the practice is used out of curiosity, to exhibit power or to obtain singular effects. That is a genuine objection to the generality of the 1784 conclusion and it is the reason this protocol's state is not stronger. Searched for and not opened, therefore named here and not counted: Jussieu's separate publication as the member of the parallel commission of the Société royale de médecine who refused to sign; the reports of the Academy of Medicine of 1826 and 1831, which reached the opposite conclusion and of whose printed text the collating witness of 1841 alleges notable variations from Husson's manuscript; and Bertrand's account of 1826. Each is a party with standing and each is reachable; until one of them is read at the wording this protocol reports one commission and says so."@en,
        "The commissioners are themselves a party and the file says where. They examined the practice of one practitioner and drew a conclusion about the magnetism in general, and they answer the objection in a footnote by appeal to the king's intention rather than to their design. Their historical section, which rests on a separate enquiry by another hand, concludes that the doctrine is an old falsehood revived, and their closing paragraph is a recommendation about public practice. None of that is evidence and none of it is counted here; what is counted is the blindfold series."@en ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "Report of Dr. Benjamin Franklin, and Other Commissioners, Charged by the King of France, with the Examination of the Animal Magnetism, as now Practised at Paris, translated from the French, with an historical introduction (London: J. Johnson, 1785), the blindfold experiments and the conclusions" ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:examinedBy "Commission of the Faculty of Medicine and the Royal Academy of Sciences, Paris 1784 — Franklin, Bailly, Lavoisier, Majault, Sallin, d'Arcet, de Bory, Le Roy and Borie; protocol entered by prima-materia, 2026-09-14" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A blindfold series of the same form in which the reported sensations follow the places to which the action is directed, and in which subjects falsely told the operation is proceeding report nothing. The claim under examination is that the action reaches a subject who does not know of it, so a series in which the report tracks the action rather than the belief falls due in its favour and against this protocol."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-14" .

pmp:MandaeanCasusIrritiSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der Feldbericht je eine gescheiterte Weihe zählt"@de,
        "Whether the field record ever counts a consecration that failed"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-20" ;
    skos:note "No number, and only three named cases in the whole book, none of them a voiding: two men who went through the expiation for marrying a woman who was not a virgin, and one purification the recorder watched at Litlata in 1934. The recorder says of the recovery case that it happens rarely and gives no instance, and of the fallen crown nothing at all."@en,
        "The gap is exhibited rather than deplored, because it is the same gap the holding has everywhere and it is unusually visible here. This transmission states more conditions of failure than almost anything else in the graph, and states none of the frequencies. A reader who wanted to know whether the severity is lived or recited would have to count consecrations, and nobody has."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search, normalised to bare letters, for: postponed for a year (one hit, the rule itself and no case); was invalidated (none); had to be repeated (none); how often (none); cases of (two, neither of them a case of a voided rite); I have known, I knew of, happened once, never happened (none); has happened (one, and it is about men returning from death); twice in, three times in (seven, none of them about a rite)." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-20" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran: Their Cults, Customs, Magic, Legends, and Folklore (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), the whole volume" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A figure, or even a single dated case, for how often the voiding conditions this transmission states are actually met: a consecration invalidated by the wife's uncleanness, a crown that fell, a year's postponement, a candidate for the high priesthood whose dying man recovered. The rules are stated with great precision, and a rule that is never triggered and a rule that is triggered constantly look identical in a book of rules." .

pmp:MandaeanGinzaTaxatioSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der Ginzā irgendwo einen Ritualfehler beziffert"@de,
        "Whether the Ginzā anywhere prices a ritual fault"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-20" ;
    skos:note "Nothing anywhere. The scripture threatens, and it threatens at length — the blazing fire, the boiling cauldrons, the second death that does not come — but it never converts a fault into a number, and it never says that a performance did not count. The whole quantified apparatus of this transmission lies outside the two books this run could open."@en,
        "The limit that matters here is one of translation and not of coverage. The search was carried over a German translation of 1925, so it would miss a penalty that its translator rendered in words the search did not try. Against that stands the shape of the finding: the absence is not of one term but of a whole form of sentence, and a translator who had met such sentences would have had to render them somehow."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of the volume, with the text normalised to bare letters first so that the scan's broken umlauts would not hide a spelling. Searched for: Taufen (nineteen hits, every one of them the rite and none a penalty); sechzig Taufen, dreihundert (ten, all of them numbers of heavenly dwellings or ages, none a penalty); 366 (twenty-one, all of them the count of dwellings or of days, the nearest being the head of the three hundred and sixty-six dwellings); Buße and Bue and Busse (thirteen, all of them repentance and none of them a tariff); ungültig in three spellings (none); wiederholen (four, none ritual); Strafe der Taufe (none)." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-20" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Ginzā, right and left side, in the German of Mark Lidzbarski, Ginzā: Der Schatz oder das große Buch der Mandäer (Göttingen: Vandenhoeck und Ruprecht, 1925)" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage in which the scripture itself states a counted penalty for a fault in a rite, or declares a performance void: the sixty and fifty and three hundred and sixty-six baptisms that the field record of 1937 reports as the price of a broken cult object, of a nocturnal emission on a given day, or of a marriage performed for a woman who was not a virgin. The question decides how the tariff in traditions/mandaean.ttl is to be read: as the transmission's own written rule, or as priestly practice that stands beside the scripture." .

pmp:MandaeanManualisAccessusSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das priesterliche Fehlerhandbuch erreichbar war"@de,
        "Whether the priestly manual of faults could be reached"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-20" ;
    skos:note "Entered on the fourth outcome and not on the second, which is the distinction the scale exists for: this corpus was not read and found silent, it could not be opened. Two nodes in the tradition file rest on it at second hand through the field monograph, and they are marked as such."@en,
        "What actually worked in this run is worth leaving beside the failure, because it is the reason the failure can be called one. The prayerbook was first found as a lending copy too, and a second identifier of the same edition in the same archive turned out to carry an open text. The same move was tried here and there is no second identifier. That is the difference between a statement about a run and a statement about a work."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:corpusUnreachable ;
    pm:searchTerms "The ways SPEC §15 requires before unreachability may be asserted were worked through rather than assumed. Searched the full-text archive under the title, under the transliterated title in two spellings, and under the editor's name; one scan of the edition of 1960 exists and is a lending copy, whose text file answers 401 to a direct request. Searched for a second identifier of the same work in the same archive, which is how the prayerbook was got in this run, and found none. The transliteration held by the Comprehensive Aramaic Lexicon refuses automated retrieval by its own notice. No other digitisation was found." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-20" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Alf Trisar Šuialia, in the edition of E. S. Drower, The Thousand and Twelve Questions (Alf Trisar Šuialia): A Mandaean Text (Berlin: Akademie-Verlag, 1960)" ;
    pm:searchedFor "The text of the part titled Mhita u-Asuta, blow and healing, which is the transmission's own book of ritual faults and their remedies, and of the part on unintentional defilement and its healing. Every counted penalty in traditions/mandaean.ttl presumably stands there, and stands there in the transmission's own words rather than in a recorder's summary." .

pmp:MandaeanQolastaIrritumSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Gebetbuch irgendwo eine Handlung für ungültig erklärt"@de,
        "Whether the prayerbook anywhere declares a performance void"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-20" ;
    skos:note "The word for a void performance does not occur in the book of the performance. What the liturgy has instead is a standing formula in which the celebrant asks that sins, trespasses, follies, stumblings and mistakes be loosed — a petition, addressed upward, about the state of souls, and not a rule about the state of a rite. The two ways of handling a fault sit in two different books: the prayerbook asks for it to be forgiven, and the manual, which could not be got, prices it."@en,
        "This is the finding of the run that a later reader is most likely to want and least likely to expect, so it is stated plainly: the transmission's famous severity about ritual error is not visible in its liturgy. Whoever opens the Alf Trisar Šuialia should ask the reverse question there, whether the manual ever asks for forgiveness or only ever charges."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of the volume, normalised to bare letters. Searched for: invalid (no hits at all); must repeat (none); repeat the (two, both of them ordinary instructions to say a prayer again in sequence); mistake (twenty-five, and every one of them inside the same liturgical formula, sins, trespasses, follies, stumblings and mistakes, asking that they be forgiven); error (nine, all of them the editor's notes on the manuscripts); fault (twelve, likewise); defil (one); pollut (six, all of them the state of a person and none the state of a rite); penalt (one, in an editor's note); omit (eighty-three, all of them the editor recording what a manuscript leaves out); sixty (thirty-five, all of them counts of prayers, loaves or days)." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-20" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Qolastā, in the English of E. S. Drower, The Canonical Prayerbook of the Mandaeans (Leiden: E. J. Brill, 1959), the whole volume including the rubrics" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A rubric in the liturgical book stating that a performance is invalid, is to be broken off, or is to be repeated, and any statement of what the celebrant is to do when something goes wrong. The prayerbook is the text actually in the celebrant's hands and carries bracketed rubrics throughout, so if the fault rules were anywhere in the liturgy this is where they would be." .

pmp:MandaeanSacerdotissaeSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das dreizehnte Buch Priesterinnen nennt, wie der Feldbericht sagt"@de,
        "Whether the thirteenth book mentions priestesses, as the field record says"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-20" ;
    skos:note "The place is there and it says the opposite of what it was cited for. At text 288,24 the translation has a feminine plural rendered as die Jüngerinnen, the women disciples, and a footnote hanging on it which says that the Mandaic word cannot mean priestesses, since there are none among the Mandaeans. The only occurrence of the word priestesses in the whole translation is thus a denial that it belongs in the translation."@en,
        "What the search turned up is therefore not a missing passage but a decision taken inside the standard translation, on a ground that assumes the answer: the reading is excluded because the community has no priestesses, which is precisely what the passage would bear on. It became pmc:MandaeanSacerdotissaeDisputatae, and the search is recorded here because the node would otherwise look like a finding of the reading rather than a finding about how the reading was closed off."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-volume search for Priesterin and Priesterinnen, which returns exactly one place in six hundred pages, and it is a footnote. The thirteenth book was then read continuously around that place rather than searched, because the word sought was known not to stand in the running text. Also searched: Jüngerin, Frauen, Töchter." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:MandaeanSacerdotissaeDisputatae ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-20" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Ginzā, right side, book 13, in the German of Mark Lidzbarski, Ginzā (Göttingen 1925), pp. 283-288, read continuously, with a whole-volume search beside it" ;
    pm:searchedFor "The passage the field monograph of 1937 cites when it says that there is a mention of Mandaean priestesses in the thirteenth book of the right side. The citation matters because it is the only textual support offered for a claim the same page reports as contested in the community and forbidden by the priestly manual." .

pmp:MenWithoutShameInSeidrSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der altnordische Bestand Männer kennt, die seiðr ohne Schande üben"@de,
        "Whether the Norse corpus has men practising seiðr without shame"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-13" ;
    skos:note "The search is recorded because its outcome changed what was written and not only what was known. Without it the caution would have gone into the holding as a rule of the transmission; with it, it goes in as one side of a dispute that the transmission carries within a single chapter. This is the case the class exists for on the productive side."@en,
        "Three passages came back and two of them from the same chapter as the caution. The sentence that introduces the craft says Óðinn performed it himself; two sentences after the clause about shame, the same chapter says he taught most of his crafts to the blótgoðar, who are men. The third is in the later saga: the king's own son learned sorcery and became a seiðmaðr, and Vitgeirr in Hǫrðaland answered the royal order to stop with a public verse pointing at him."@en,
        "What it does not settle. Vitgeirr's answer shows a man not ashamed; the same chapter ends with the king's other son burning the first indoors together with eighty seiðmenn, and the deed much praised. A norm enforced by killing is evidence for the norm. The dispute node says so and does not resolve it."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search of both sagas for: seið, seiðr, seiðmaðr, seiðmenn, fjölkyngi, fjölkunnig, ergi, gyðjum, blótgoðum, Vitgeirr, Rögnvaldr; the chapters carrying the hits read in full." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:NorseSeidrErgiStrittig ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-13" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Snorri Sturluson, Heimskringla, in the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson: Ynglinga saga read at the wording in chapters six and seven, Haralds saga ins hárfagra searched by term over the whole saga and the hits read at the wording" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage telling against the caution at pmc:NorseSeidrErgi, which states that so much ergi goes with the craft that men could not deal with it without shame and that it was therefore taught to the priestesses: that is, men practising it, and practising it without the shame the clause asserts." .

pmp:MesmericAbandonedTreatmentSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das mesmeristische Handbuch einen Fall berichtet, in dem seine eigene Abbruchregel angewandt wurde"@de,
        "Whether the mesmeric manual reports a case in which its own stopping rule was applied"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-14" ;
    skos:note "A negative finding of this shape is weaker than a positive one and the reason is worth stating: a manual is not a case register and is not obliged to report failures. That is exactly why the absence matters here rather than being excused by it — the work does report failures, of a kind that reflect on the operator's prudence, and does not report the one kind that would reflect on the agent."@en,
        "Limited to one work, one translation and six of its ten chapters. Chapter VII on the application to particular diseases was not read at the wording and is the chapter likeliest to hold such a case; chapters IV, IX and X were also not read. The search covered the term vocabulary of the whole of Deleuze's text, so a case reported without any of those five words would have been missed."@en,
        "The finding is an asymmetry and not a silence about nothing. The work reports its cures in detail — a paralysed soldier, a caretaker's wife cured in fifteen days, a blind girl restored in five sittings, a lady whose catarrh went at the first sitting — and reports its accidents in detail, two of them with the author named as their cause. What it does not report once, in six chapters that state four stopping rules, is a patient for whom the rule was reached and the practice put aside. The rules are prospective throughout."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search over the whole of Deleuze's text for: renounce, renounced (thirteen); abandon (seven); discontinue, discontinued (three); ceased to magnetize (one); gave up (none). Each occurrence read in place." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-14" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), Deleuze's text only, pages 17 to 216" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A reported case in which one of the work's own stopping rules was in fact applied and a treatment given up as ineffective: a five-day trial that ended in the presumption that the action does not take place, three or four sittings of irritation after which the operator desisted, or a month without effect after which the patient renounced the practice. The question is not whether the rules are stated — they are, and they carry their own nodes — but whether the transmission anywhere records the rule being obeyed." .

pmp:MesmericBaseRateSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das mesmeristische Handbuch den Bericht eines Somnambulen je gegen eine Zufallsrate stellt"@de,
        "Whether the mesmeric manual anywhere compares a somnambulist's report against a rate of chance"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-14" ;
    skos:note "The nearest approach is a single clause and it goes the other way: the author says he has seen professional somnambulists succeed where the disease had characteristics too rare for them to come at the truth by conjecture or chance. The rarity is asserted and not counted, and it is asserted of cases selected for having succeeded. That is the step the search was for, taken in one direction only."@en,
        "The same author writes, of the same consultants, that there is not one he has not known to commit errors and not one that has not shown him proofs of clairvoyance. Both halves are there and neither is numbered. An author who had both the successes and the errors before him, and says so in one sentence, is one arithmetical step from the finding — and this search is the record that the step is not in the work."@en,
        "What it does not establish. This is one work of a large literature, and the literature does contain counted series: the Academy's commission of 1831 is reported to have searched for clairvoyant diagnoses and found one success. Nothing of that was opened here, and this node says only that the manual which taught the practice to its household operators contains no such reasoning."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "The occurrences of chance, proportion, number of cases and how many, each read in place; plus statistic, coincidence, average, percent (none anywhere in the text)." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-14" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), Deleuze's text only, pages 17 to 216" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Any reasoning that sets a somnambulist's correct report against the number of reports made, or against what an uninformed guess would have produced — the base-rate step without which a collection of successful diagnoses says nothing." .

pmp:MesmericSomnambulismAcquisitionSituating a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Der magnetische Somnambulismus auf der Erwerbsachse verortet"@de,
        "The magnetic somnambulism placed on the acquisition axis"@en ;
    skos:note "The placement is on the subject's side and not the operator's, which is the only way the axis reads here: the operator does exert effort, and the question the axis asks is whether the source presents the state as won or as given. To the person who enters it, this transmission presents it as given — and it is one of the very few in the holding to forbid working for it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:notByEffort ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:MagnetismeSomnambulisme ;
    pm:situationGround "The work forbids seeking the state and has it let come naturally, holds that where nature is disposed to this crisis the fluid will of itself be carried to the brain, and states that a slumber obtained by charging the head is forced, of no utility and not without danger. It also states that the state occurs only in a small number of cases and cannot be pronounced upon beforehand. A condition the source says may not be worked for, and comes where nature is disposed to it, is not presented as earned."@en .

pmp:MesmericSomnambulismAgencySituating a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Der magnetische Somnambulismus auf der Handlungsachse verortet"@de,
        "The magnetic somnambulism placed on the agency axis"@en ;
    skos:note "Held against the rest of the holding this is the distinguishing feature of the tradition. Almost everything else here has the practitioner act upon himself, so the agency question has one answer and no interest. Here two people are involved and the source assigns the action to the one who is not in the state — which is why the claims of this tradition were, uniquely in this holding, examinable by a commission, and why the file beside this one could be written."@en,
        "The transmission sets a limit to the ceding in as many words, and the limit is not a reason to place the node elsewhere. The subject is said to preserve his reason and the use of his will, to be unable to be made to reveal a secret he has reason to conceal or to act against the principles he holds awake, and to revolt into convulsions at a reprehensible act of volition. That is a boundary on what may be commanded, not a claim that the subject is the one acting; the axis asks who acts, and by this work's account the operator does."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:cededAgency ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:MagnetismeSomnambulisme ;
    pm:situationGround "The work states that the subject is subject to the influence of his operator, that in a treatment there ought to be only one will active to which all others are subordinate, that the operator is to oppose the subject's caprices by letting him know his will and never suffer him to get the upper hand, and that the operator is for him a just and inflexible providence. The operative will is the operator's throughout and the subject is required to yield himself passively, making no resistance."@en .

pmp:MesmericSomnambulismAnchoringSituating a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Der magnetische Somnambulismus auf der Sinnesachse verortet"@de,
        "The magnetic somnambulism placed on the sensory axis"@en ;
    skos:note "The value was preferred to pm:sleepThreshold although the transmission calls the state a sleep and reaches it through drowsiness. The threshold value is for practices carried out at the edge of falling asleep; here the subject is described as asleep and answering, which is not an edge but a withdrawal. The choice is contestable on the transmission's own vocabulary and is recorded as contestable."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:withdrawnAbsorption ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:MagnetismeSomnambulisme ;
    pm:situationGround "The work states that the external organs of sense are all, or nearly all, asleep, that the eyes are closed, and that the subject generally understands only those put in communication with him — and in the same sentence that he nevertheless experiences sensations by another means, an internal sense being roused in him. Withdrawal from the ordinary channel with awareness continuing is what this value holds, and the passage states both halves of it."@en .

pmp:MesmericSomnambulismDurationSituating a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Der magnetische Somnambulismus auf der Dauerachse verortet"@de,
        "The magnetic somnambulism placed on the duration axis"@en ;
    skos:note "The work reports the opposite case and it does not unsettle the placement: persons long in the practice retain a susceptibility that returns them to an imperfect somnambulism at the slightest action, and some have been seen habitually in the magnetic state. That is reported as an inconvenience to be prevented, not as the state's own duration, which is why the value stands as it does and the report stands here."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:transientState ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:MagnetismeSomnambulisme ;
    pm:situationGround "The work has the operator end the state within the sitting, wake the subject perfectly so that no intermediate point remains between the ordinary and the somnambulic condition, and will that the faculties cease as soon as the patient is cured; and it states that somnambulism too much prolonged would of itself become a disease. A condition the transmission requires to be terminated, and calls a disease where it persists, is transient by its own account."@en .

pmp:MesmericVerificationProcedureSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das mesmeristische Handbuch ein Verfahren zur Prüfung der behaupteten Hellsicht vorschreibt"@de,
        "Whether the mesmeric manual prescribes any procedure for verifying the lucidity it claims"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-14" ;
    skos:note "The node the search yielded carries the prohibition. The examination beside it, pmp:MagneticActionWithoutKnowledgeExamination, is a protocol on the claim this prohibition protects, and it was carried by a commission from outside the tradition — which is the only way it could have been carried at all."@en,
        "The productive outcome, and it is the whole reason the search was worth carrying rather than assumed. Nothing in the work resembles a verification procedure, and the absence is not a silence: the single occurrence of put to the proof is the author's own footnote saying that he never permitted himself to consult somnambulists to put them to the proof, and the general rule of the chapter on somnambulism forbids any attempt to prove the lucidity at all. A tradition that has no test and a tradition that prohibits testing are different findings, and a search that had stopped at counting hits would have reported the first."@en,
        "The vocabulary of proportion is worth the space it takes here. Three occurrences of the word and all three are figures of speech; no occurrence anywhere of a rate, an average or a coincidence; and the one genuine number in the six chapters read — at least three quarters of the patients feel the effects — is offered as a reply to an objection with no series behind it. The work has the arithmetic vocabulary of its century available and does not use it on its own claims."@en,
        "What the search cannot say. It was carried in one English translation of one work, so a French term with no English cognate in Hartshorn's rendering would not have been found, and the scanned text is an optical transcription whose failures are unknown. The author's own Histoire critique of 1813, which he cites in this work for the case of his first somnambulist, was not opened; if a verification procedure exists anywhere in his writing it is likeliest there, and that is the address for whoever takes this further."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search over the whole of Deleuze's text for: verify, verified (six, none of them a procedure: fluid drawn off the hands, a dietary observation any one can easily verify, a somnambulist in whom there is nothing which cannot be verified, two cases the author verified himself, and the laws of matter verified by experiment); proof (seventeen); experiment (thirty-seven); trial (ten); test (one); proportion (three, all of them idiomatic — in proportion as one doubts, in proportion to the interest displayed, cures in proportion to strength); chance (four, one of them the phrase too rare for them to come at the truth by conjecture or chance); statistic, coincidence, average, percent (none); number of cases (one, of the rarity of somnambulism); autopsy, post-mortem, dissection (none)." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:CautionAgainstProvingTheLucidity ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-14" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), Deleuze's text only, pages 17 to 216; the translator's appendices from page 217 were excluded from the search as a different author and a later date" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Any passage prescribing how the clairvoyance of a somnambulist is to be verified: a comparison against a case established otherwise, a rate, a count of successes against failures, a check of a diagnosis against a physician's finding or a post-mortem, or any procedure by which a reader could distinguish a lucid somnambulist from one who is not." .

pmp:MithraicCoronaRecusata a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der auf dem Schwert dargebotene Kranz, mit einem Satz zurückgewiesen"@de,
        "The crown offered on a sword, refused with a saying"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tertullian, De corona 15, with De praescriptione haereticorum 40 (trans. A. S. Geden, Select Passages Illustrating Mithraism, London 1925)" ;
    skos:definition "The initiation of the soldier grade as Tertullian describes it to shame Christian soldiers: in a cave, a crown is offered to the candidate on the point of a sword and set on his head, whereupon he is required to lift it off with his own hand and move it to his shoulder, saying that Mithras is his crown. From then on he is never crowned; and if he is ever put on trial about his religion, this is the mark by which he is known — he throws the crown away and says that he has his crown in his god."@en ;
    skos:note "The nearest thing to a passing formula that this graph holds outside the gnostic material, and it is the shape the Passierformeln project is looking for: an assertion of what the speaker has, not a request for anything. Nothing is asked of Mithras and nothing is granted by him. The sentence does not open a door; it identifies the speaker, and it does so under interrogation, where a wrong answer costs something."@en,
        "The same author elsewhere adds, from memory and saying so, that Mithras marks his soldiers on the forehead, celebrates an offering of bread, brings in an image of resurrection, opens the way to the crown with a sword, restricts his high priest to a single marriage, and keeps virgins and continents. Held here rather than as separate nodes because the author hedges the whole list."@en,
        "The witness is hostile and says why he is telling it: he wants Christians shamed by a comparison with a soldier of Mithras. That cuts both ways for reliability. He has no motive to invent a rite that makes his opponents look steadfast, and every motive to sharpen the detail that does. What he reports as an enduring mark — never crowned again — is the kind of claim a contemporary reader could have checked."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mithraic .

pmp:MithrasFailureSignSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Stueck ein wahrnehmbares Zeichen dafuer nennt, dass der Ritus nicht gewirkt hat"@de,
        "Whether the piece names a perceptible sign that the rite has not worked"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-22" ;
    skos:note "Nothing of the kind, and the three negations that do occur are not it: one stands in the prayer, where the speaker says it is not attainable for him as a mortal to rise with the flame-glance of the immortal light, and two stand in the botanical note and in the apparatus. The single clause that says the thing will not come about is the forfeiture already entered at pmc:PgmIvTerInAnno, and it names a cause in the operator's conduct rather than anything he would perceive."@en,
        "The one thing in the whole piece that could come out the other way is a test of an ingredient and not of the rite, and it was found in the same reading: the feather whose barbs fall off at pmc:PgmIvProbatioKentritidis. That is the finding the two searches produce together, and it is the material of the generalization in generalizations/the-test-on-the-ingredient.ttl."@en,
        "What the empty result is worth. It settles that the asymmetry recorded at pmc:PgmIvSignaSuccessus is a property of the piece and not of the translation that was read first, which was the open point: a reading that stops at P 723 cannot distinguish the two. It does not settle anything about the rite, and it does not mean the operator had no way of telling. It means the text gave him none."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search of the whole text and translation part for the German of the editor — nicht wirk, vergeblich, umsonst, misslingen, wenn nicht, sonst, versagt, ausbleiben, ohne Erfolg, nichts geschieht, wird nicht, verwirkt, nicht mehr — and for the Greek negations in the forms the optical transcription of this copy produces for ouketi, ouk and oux. Every hit read in its place, and the appendix pp. 16-22 read through in order for an unmarked clause."@en ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-22" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "PGM IV.475-834 in full, Greek text with apparatus and the editor's German translation, in A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig and Berlin: Teubner 1910, pp. 1-22."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage telling the operator what he would perceive if the rite had failed: a sight that does not come, an interval after which nothing further arrives, a state of the body that shows the breath was taken wrongly, an outcome that excludes the announced one. The piece announces a dozen sights in order and this holding has recorded the imbalance twice; whether the other side is entirely absent had to be established in the edition and not in the translation, because the translation stops before the appendix."@en .

pmp:MithrasGreekLayerSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob ein erreichbares Exemplar der Ausgabe ihr Griechisch als Griechisch traegt"@de,
        "Whether any reachable copy of the edition carries its Greek as Greek"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-22" ;
    skos:note "Not one Greek character in any of the three. The optical transcription renders the Greek throughout in visually similar letters of the Latin alphabet with diacritics, so that the opening invocation comes out as a line of Latin letters. The German is clean and the apparatus is clean, which is why the reading this run did was possible at all; what is not possible from this copy is a single name spelt out."@en,
        "The distinction the outcome makes is the point of entering it. The work was read, the operative content of the appendix was harvested from it, and nineteen nodes in traditions/greek-magical-papyri.ttl stand on it. One layer of the same work is unreachable on this path, and it is precisely the layer the issue wanted. A node saying the work was not reachable would be false; a silence would leave the next run to pay for the same three downloads."@en,
        "Where a later run should go. Not to a fourth copy of the same volume on the same archive — three is enough to establish that the transcription engine is the cause and not the scan. The remaining ways are an edition whose Greek was set rather than photographed, a corpus of Greek documentary texts that carries PGM IV in its own transcription, and the printed apparatus of the standard edition of the papyri. Which of these exists as machine-readable text is an open question and stands in the issue."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:corpusUnreachable ;
    pm:searchTerms "For each of the three copies the whole full-text file was taken and the characters counted by block: the Greek and Coptic block, the Greek extended block, and the Latin letters. Counts of Greek characters: nil, nil and nil, against 373279, 412885 and 376632 Latin letters. The metadata of each copy was read for the list of files it carries, and the alternative text layers were looked for there."@en ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-22" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Three digitised copies of the edition on the full-text archive that is this holding's regular way to public-domain volumes: the first edition of 1903, the second of 1910, and a lending copy of the second catalogued as carrying both German and ancient Greek. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, Leipzig and Berlin: Teubner, 1st ed. 1903 and 2nd ed. 1910."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "The letter-exact wording of the barbarous names, the vowel chain and the fourteen names of the two ranks, which is what prima-materia#643 was opened for. The edition prints them; the question was whether any copy the machine can read reproduces the Greek script rather than substituting letters of the Latin one."@en .

pmp:MithrasNamesInAddendaSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Nachtraege der Ausgabe die vierzehn Namen dem Ritus zusprechen"@de,
        "Whether the addenda of the edition argue the fourteen names belong to the rite"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-22" ;
    skos:note "One sentence found in passing decides more than the search did, and it is the editor's own: the preamble to his word-index to the liturgy states that the voces mysticae are not registered. So the edition indexes the liturgical vocabulary word by word with page and line and deliberately leaves out the one class of word a reconstruction of the sounds would need. The material is in the text and out of the apparatus that makes the text usable."@en,
        "The addenda do not return to the question. Every hit falls in the two registers at the end of the volume and not in the addenda: the subject index lists the pole-rulers, the Fates, the seven vowels, the magical words and the hissing and smacking, each with the page of the commentary where they were already treated in 1903. Nothing between 1903 and 1909 that the editor thought worth reprinting bore on whether these fourteen names are the rite's own."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search of pp. 219-248 for Namen, vox, voces, mystica, Jungfrauen, Polherrscher, Tychai, Vokal, and for the transcriptions the copy produces for the Greek words for name and for the Fates. Every hit read in its place."@en ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-22" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The addenda and the two registers of A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig and Berlin: Teubner 1910, pp. 219-248, which collect what had been printed on the piece between 1903 and 1909 together with Wuensch's own notes."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "An argument, in the addenda rather than in the apparatus, that the names of the seven maidens and of the seven pole-rulers stood in the liturgy the magicians took over, as against being their own insertions. The holding asserts at pmc:PgmIvSalutatioNominatim that the editor holds names must have stood in these two places and that this rests on the shape of the passage and not on a mark in the manuscript. That assertion had been read off the apparatus alone."@en .

pmp:MorallyPolarisedBinarySearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob eine vorkoloniale indische Quelle die beiden Methoden moralisch polt"@de,
        "Whether a pre-colonial Indian source polarises the two methods morally"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-09" ;
    skos:note "One corpus, one translation, one spelling set. The corpora that would carry the polarity if anything does — the Kaula tantras, the Śākta digests of the Bengal schools — were not opened for this question, and this node says so rather than letting the silence of the tracker say it."@en,
        "The search was for the polarity and found the pair without it. That is the productive outcome and the one an unrecorded search loses entirely: a run that only enters what it set out to find reports its own question back to itself. The passages became pmc:KalikapuranaLeftAndRightMethods and the four nodes around it."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Full-text search of the translation for: vama, vamacara, vamachara, daksina, daksinacara; left hand, right hand, left-hand, orthodox, heterodox." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:KalikapuranaLeftAndRightMethods ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-09" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Kālikāpurāṇa (text, introduction and translation in English by B. N. Shastri, part III, Nag Publishers, Delhi 1991)" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A pre-colonial Indian passage presenting dakṣiṇa and vāma as a clean two-way division with a moral polarity, using union with the divine against self-deification as the criterion that separates them — the falsifying condition of pmp:BinaryPredatesItsReportersExamination." .

pmp:MuambaFelling a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Fällen des Muamba-Baums für eine Nagelfigur"@de,
        "The felling of the muamba tree for a nail figure"@en ;
    dcterms:source "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), p. 93" ;
    skos:definition "The making of a nail figure begins in the wood. A council is held first to decide whose life is to preside over the figure, and a boy of great spirit or a great and daring hunter is chosen; the party then goes into the bush and calls his name, the practitioner fells the tree, and blood is said to come from the cut. A fowl is killed and its blood mixed with what comes from the tree."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by Bavili at Loango, unnamed in this passage; recorded by the trader R. E. Dennett during his residence on the Loango coast from 1879, the book published in 1906; given in the Vili dialect, whose orthography the recorder sets out himself and whose terms he prints, the accounts themselves in English only." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:KongoNkisi .

pmp:NassauBwetiSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob ein dritter Aufzeichner in einer dritten Sprache die Gesellschaft nennt"@de,
        "Whether a third recorder in a third language names the society"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-05" ;
    skos:note "The name is there and nothing else is. Page 248 places Bweti among the Bakele in an enumeration of the governing societies of the Gabun region; the table of contents of chapter XV lists it in the same series. Of the initiation, the root, the mirror and the criterion there is nothing, and iboga and eboga do not occur in the volume. The twenty hits for mbwiri are Ombwiri, a class of spirit treated in a different chapter, and are not the society."@en,
        "What the find is worth, stated narrowly so that it is not later read for more. It is independent evidence that a society of this name existed in the region before 1904 and was taken for an instrument of government, by a recorder who assigns it a third people. It is no evidence about anything the initiation nodes claim. The one detailed initiation account in the book is an initiation into Malinda, and was not read in this run."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "bweti, bwiti, bouiti, mbwiri, ombwiri, iboga, eboga, melan, run case-insensitively over the whole volume, 961,833 characters; the two hits for bweti and the twenty for mbwiri were read, and the distinction between the society and the guardian spirit Ombwiri was made at the wording and not from the spelling." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:BwetiAmongTheBakele ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-05" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Robert Hamill Nassau, Fetichism in West Africa: Forty Years' Observation of Native Customs and Superstitions (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1904), complete as full text" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Whether an English-language recorder of forty years' residence in the same district, citing neither French recorder, names a society of the Bwiti name, and whether he connects it with a root. The question was put because both accounts of the initiation in the holding come from Catholic missionaries of one congregation and a third name would be the first witness outside that circle." .

pmp:NavajoSacredSong a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Navajo-Kultgesang"@de,
        "Navajo sacred song"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Washington Matthews, Navaho Legends, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society V (Boston and New York: Houghton, Mifflin, 1897), section 41, p. 24" ;
    skos:definition "The songs belonging to a rite, held by the priest of the rite and his assistants, containing expressions that are obsolete or without current meaning and that have therefore to be carried by memory rather than by sense."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NavajoCeremonial .

pmp:NeidanCalendarSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die innere Alchemie ihr Feuer an den Kalender bindet"@de,
        "Whether the internal alchemy binds its fire to the calendar"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-11" ;
    skos:note "A by-product of the term list is worth keeping, because it settles a reading. The literature circulates the thirteenth quatrain in two forms, one with the fire phases not using the hour and one with them not requiring it and the solstice questioned rather than denied. The recension read has the first and not the second: the search for the other form over the whole work returned nothing. A variant that is absent from the witness cited should not be quoted from it."@en,
        "The search was expected to confirm and instead split the corpus. The Zhong-Lü chuandao ji binds throughout and makes ignorance of the times a sufficient cause of failure; the Jindan sizi denies the binding in four lines and the commentary under them says one may not be bound to the four seasons; and the Wuzhen pian does both within one juan of one recension, mocking those who practise by the hexagram-breaths on one folio and setting the fire by the solstices and the second and eighth months, with a stated consequence, twenty-five folios later. The dispute node carries the passages."@en,
        "What this search does not establish. It was carried in three works of one transmission and cannot say what the rest of the internal alchemy does; the later literature that made the staged terms canonical was not opened. It also cannot say which side was earlier, since the Jindan sizi survives only in a recension of twelve forty-one and the Zhong-Lü chuandao ji only in later ones."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of all three works for: 冬至 (three in the Jindan sizi, eight in the Zhong-Lü chuandao ji, six in the Wuzhen pian); 夏至; 子時 and 午時; 卯酉; 四時; 時候; 卦氣 and the variant 卦炁 (one each in the Jindan sizi and the Wuzhen pian); 不用時 (one, the Jindan sizi's thirteenth quatrain); 不須時 (none, which settles a variant reading in the literature)." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:NeidanHuohouDispute ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-11" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Zhang Boduan (ascr.), Jindan sizi with Huang Ziru's commentary (DZ 1081); Zhang Boduan, Wuzhen pian in Wuzhen pian zhushi with Weng Baoguang's commentary (DZ 145), three juan; Zhong-Lü chuandao ji in the Chongkan Daozang jiyao recension (JY 149), three juan. Searched over all three in the Chinese, with every chapter carrying a hit read at the wording" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Whether this transmission sets the timing of its operation by the calendar, as pmc:AdmissibilityBoundToTheCalendar would predict of a transmission that binds an operation to a time at all, or by a state of the practitioner." .

pmp:NeidanChoutian a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Abziehen und Zusetzen"@de,
        "Withdrawing and adding"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhang Boduan (ascr.), Jindan sizi, verse 17, with Huang Ziru's commentary (DZ 1081), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, fol. 11a" ;
    skos:definition "The adjustment of the two components against each other during the run, the corpus giving it weights and measures and warning that it must be held to carefully."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Neidan .

pmp:NeidanHundredPacesExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der Übende auf hundert Schritt ein feines Haar sieht"@de,
        "Whether the practitioner sees a fine hair at a hundred paces"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the item in the sign sequence which states that the practitioner comes to see, with the eye, a fine autumn hair at a hundred paces. It is examinable because both terms are given: a distance and a target, and both are of the kind an optometrist measures without needing to know anything about the practice."@en ;
    skos:note "No case has been collected. The obstacle is the staging and not the measurement: the sequence is ordered and the transmission's own criterion for placing a practitioner at a given item is his report of the items before it, several of which are private. Any protocol therefore has to take the placement from the tradition and measure only the last step, which is what the falsifying condition above does."@en,
        "The physiological limit is worth stating before any case is collected, because it bounds what a positive result could mean. A hundred paces is on the order of a hundred and fifty metres; resolving a hair at that distance would be an acuity far outside the range the human eye is built for, so a positive result would not be a strong version of ordinary good sight but a different claim altogether. That is a reason to run the test, not against it."@en,
        "The second checkable item of the same sequence is deliberately not made a protocol here: old scars and blemishes vanishing of themselves is observable, but scars do fade, and a protocol would have to fix in advance what counts as vanishing. It is recorded at the claim node and left there."@en,
        "Why this sequence and not the rest of it. The same chapter states, immediately above the sequence, that a practitioner who holds to the work and sees no result has either taken the wrong master or misjudged the times — see pmc:NeidanFeidaofuren. That clause covers every negative outcome of every item in the list, which is why the protocol is set on the one item where a negative outcome can be established without the practitioner's cooperation."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, working from the sequence recorded at pmc:NeidanZhengyan" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A practitioner whom the transmission places at or beyond this item of the sequence — that is, one for whom the earlier items are affirmed by his own teachers — and whose visual acuity at a hundred paces, measured against a standard target, does not exceed that of matched controls of the same age. The claim names a distance and an object and attaches no condition to the observer, so a group of such practitioners not differing from controls settles it." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-11" ;
    pm:tests pmc:NeidanZhengyan .

pmp:NeidanMethodBlamedSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die innere Alchemie zulässt, dass die Methode schuld sein kann"@de,
        "Whether the internal alchemy allows that the method may be at fault"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-11" ;
    skos:note "Limits, and one of them is sharp. Three works of one transmission, one recension each, and two of the three are Song or later redactions of texts ascribed to earlier men. A different recension could carry a different sentence at this place. What the search establishes is that in the recensions that circulate, the phrase for the Way failing a man occurs exactly twice and both times as something to be denied."@en,
        "The search found the question already put and already answered, which is better than a silence and is the reason the outcome is entered as opposing rather than as nothing found. The two hits on the phrase stand one sentence apart at the head of the chapter on the signs of verification: a man who toils and sees no merit — it is not that the Way has failed him, but that his master was not enlightened; a man who practises by the Law and sees no merit — it is not that the Way has failed him, but that he did not know the times. The transmission raises the possibility this search was looking for, names it in four characters, and denies it twice."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of all three works for: 非道負人 (two hits, both in the Zhong-Lü chuandao ji, adjacent); 負人 (two there, one in the Wuzhen pian); 不見功 (four in the Zhong-Lü chuandao ji); 徒勞 (one each in the Zhong-Lü chuandao ji and the Wuzhen pian); 不成功; 無成; 咎 (one, in the Jindan sizi); 歸咎, 法之誤, 法非 (none anywhere). The chapters carrying the hits were then read at the wording." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:NeidanFeidaofuren ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-11" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Zhang Boduan (ascr.), Jindan sizi with Huang Ziru's commentary (DZ 1081); Zhang Boduan, Wuzhen pian in Wuzhen pian zhushi with Weng Baoguang's commentary (DZ 145), three juan; Zhong-Lü chuandao ji in the Chongkan Daozang jiyao recension (JY 149), three juan. Searched over all three in the Chinese, with the passages carrying a hit read at the wording" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage admitting that a practitioner who held to the work and saw no result may have been given a method that does not work, as against a passage locating the cause in him. The same question was put to the Zhen'gao on 2026-09-11 and found no such passage there either; see pmp:ZhengaoMethodBlamedSearch." .

pmp:NeidanMuyu a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Baden"@de,
        "The bathing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhang Boduan, Wuzhen pian, in Wuzhen pian zhushi (DZ 145), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 2, fol. 31b-32a" ;
    skos:definition "The pause in the fire, prescribed at two points of the cycle, during which the heat is to be withheld; the corpus states that adding fire at that point overturns the work."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Neidan .

pmp:NewThoughtPatientsBeliefBlamedSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob ein misslungene Behandlung je dem fehlenden Glauben des Kranken zugeschrieben wird"@de,
        "Whether an unsuccessful treatment is ever put down to the patient's want of belief"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-22" ;
    skos:note "One case contradicts the clean reading and is kept, because a search that reports only its own conclusion is worthless. A patient improving under treatment bought an advertised medicine for catarrh, took it, and ran down very rapidly; the healer reports the deterioration and puts it on the patient's belief in the advertisement. So a failure is assigned to the patient once, and what is blamed is not the absence of belief in the healer but the presence of belief in a rival remedy. The distinction matters for the register: a transmission can decline the want-of-faith clause and still have a construction that covers every case in which the patient consulted anyone else."@en,
        "The excuse is not used, and the opposite construction is stated twice. Of the mesmeric practice: if a failure occurred the fault was the operator's and not that of the subject. Of the healing: I plead his case, and if I get the verdict the criminal is set at liberty; if I fail, I lose the case. A transmission which holds that the sick person's belief is the disease, and which declines to hold that his belief is the reason the cure did not take, has left the easiest immunising clause in the register on the table."@en,
        "The result is entered as opposing and not as an ordinary finding because of what it does to pmc:NoFailedRunCountsAgainstTheOperation. It does not refute that statement — the operator is one of the four places the statement names, and this transmission does put the failure there. It removes something else: the impression, which fourteen witnesses could easily give, that the place is chosen for the convenience of the transmission. Here it is chosen against the convenience of the man who wrote it down."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search for: want of faith, lack of faith, if you do not believe, unless you believe, if the patient believes, the patient is to blame, fault of the patient, your want, your lack, did not believe enough, doubted, doubt. Every hit read in its place. The doctrinal chapters on belief were read at the passages the hits fell in."@en ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:QuimbyCulpaOperantis ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-22" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Horatio W. Dresser (ed.), The Quimby Manuscripts, 2nd ed. (New York: Thomas Y. Crowell, 1921), the same text and the same reading."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "The construction this holding collects at pmc:NoFailedRunCountsAgainstTheOperation in the form it takes most often: a clause assigning an empty run to the person operated on. In a transmission whose entire doctrine is that belief makes and unmakes disease, the patient's insufficient belief is the excuse that lies nearest to hand, and the question is whether it is used."@en .

pmp:NewThoughtStatedTermSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Behandlung eine Frist nennt, in der die Heilung eintreten soll"@de,
        "Whether the treatment states a term within which the cure is to appear"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-22" ;
    skos:note "No term is prescribed anywhere. The treatment is an explanation given until the patient admits the point, and it ends when he admits it; the healer describes it as pleading a case before a judge who is the patient's own judgment, which has no calendar. The one place a period appears is an editorial footnote saying the healer was skilful in telling how long his absent work would take before the patient would be up and about — a skill attributed to him rather than a rule stated by him, and no instance of such a prediction with its period is printed."@en,
        "The empty result is worth keeping for the contrast it draws with the neighbouring corpus. The Romanus-Büchlein, a booklet of charms with no doctrine at all, sets a beast's broken leg straight again by the ninth day; this volume, which argues for four hundred pages that its healing is a science, sets no term anywhere. The transmission that calls itself a science is the one with nothing that falls due."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search for: in three weeks, within a week, in a few days, number of sittings, how many visits, by the time, appointed time, how long, would take. Every hit read in its place."@en ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-22" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Horatio W. Dresser (ed.), The Quimby Manuscripts, 2nd ed. (New York: Thomas Y. Crowell, 1921), the same text and the same reading."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A stated period after which the treatment would be at an end and its outcome readable: a number of sittings, a count of days, a date by which the patient would be up. The question is put because a deadline that falls due is the cheapest examinable feature a healing transmission can have, and this holding found exactly one in the last corpus it opened."@en .

pmp:NewThoughtUncuredPatientSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der Band einen behandelten und nicht geheilten Kranken nennt"@de,
        "Whether the volume reports a patient treated and not cured"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-22" ;
    skos:note "Not one case. The hits on death are about the death of Jesus, the death of the natural man, the deaths of people before they came, or the healer's own death in 1866; the hits on failure are about the mesmeric experiments of the eighteen-forties and about the world's failure to understand the science; the hits on worse are about what doctors do. The chapters of letters are testimonials of gratitude without exception."@en,
        "The finding is about the volume and not about the practice, and the distinction is the whole of what this search is worth. A collection of papers edited by a follower sixty years after the fact, published into a dispute about priority, is exactly the document in which uncured patients would not appear, and the absence therefore establishes nothing about how many there were. What it does establish is that the transmission, at the moment it was made into a book, carried no record of its own negative cases — so nobody reading it then or since could have counted."@en,
        "Two near-misses are worth naming because they are what a later run will find and think it has found more. A correspondent reports a partial relapse a year after her recovery, which is handled by a further absent treatment and reported as successful. And an editorial footnote under the letters says the chief difficulty of former patients who depended solely on the new Science was avoiding old fears when meeting sceptical friends, which concedes that some difficulty existed and names no case. Both are entered in the tradition file, the second at pmc:QuimbyAmiciIncreduli."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search of the whole text for: fail, failure, failed, not cured, could not cure, cannot cure, no benefit, without success, no better, gave up the case, declined, refused to take, relapse, grew worse, worse, died, death of, want of faith, lack of faith, unsuccessful. Every hit read in its place and assigned to the hand that wrote it."@en ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-22" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Horatio W. Dresser (ed.), The Quimby Manuscripts, 2nd ed. (New York: Thomas Y. Crowell, 1921), in the optical transcription of a copy in the full-text archive; all five kinds of text in the volume, with the chapters of contemporary testimony, letters from patients, letters to patients and inquirers, and disease and healing read in place."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "Any report, from any of the five hands the volume prints, of a person who came under this treatment and did not recover: a case broken off, a patient who died under care, a treatment declared unsuccessful, or a refusal to take a case on the ground that it could not be cured."@en .

pmp:NippurBoundFigures a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Den Dämon gebunden und gefesselt auf die Schale zeichnen"@de,
        "Drawing the demon bound and hobbled on the bowl"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, introduction § 11 on the designs, with texts 4, 8, 15 and 20, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 53-55 and 200-201)" ;
    skos:definition "The figures on the bowls are part of the operation and not decoration. Most represent the demons, drawn bound and hobbled in the words the incantation uses of them; liliths are so represented; one bowl shows a demon with arms and legs manacled, with the words prohibition and permission written on either side of it and the client's name inside the body on the side of the prohibition; one shows the serpent with its tail in its mouth; and rectangular figures divided into compartments stand for the walls of protection the charm casts about the client."@en ;
    skos:note "One bowl depicts not the demon but the operator, waving a magic bough in his hand. The editor sets it beside the branch of datepalm or tamarisk held aloft in the Babylonian rite and notes that the Assyriologist who published those texts had already pointed at this design."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmp:NippurInscribedSkull a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Den Zauber auf einen menschlichen Schädel schreiben"@de,
        "Inscribing the charm on a human skull"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 41 with its introductory note, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 256-257)" ;
    skos:definition "The text the volume publishes as an appendix is inscribed not on a bowl but on the top of a human skull, in two inscriptions, one running across the length of the left-hand side from front to back. The editor calls it unique and says enough is legible to indicate that it is a magical inscription, doubtless of the same order as those on the bowls."@en ;
    skos:note "No node of this file carries a capacity from this witness, and the reason is a property of the object: the skull is well preserved but the text is sadly worn and obscured through the shaling of the surface, and only a few detached words are legible. What it yields is that this craft wrote on a skull, which no bowl could have told us, and nothing about what it wrote there."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmp:NotoryArtOneMonthExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Wissenschaften binnen des genannten Monats eintreffen"@de,
        "Whether the sciences arrive within the month the art names"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that whoever pronounces the orations at the appointed times, setting all other occasions aside, profits in all the sciences within one month. It is examinable because the claim names a regimen, a term and an outcome that a third party can measure."@en ;
    skos:note "No case has been collected. The obstacle is named rather than left to be inferred, and it is not the difficulty of the regimen: the witness in print omits the notae, so nobody working from it can keep the procedure the text prescribes. A negative result obtained from this witness alone would therefore not tell against the art, only against half of it. That is a property of the transmission and belongs in the protocol, because it is exactly the kind of gap a later reader would otherwise supply on his own."@en,
        "The clause that would absorb every outcome is present in the corpus but not in this claim. Elsewhere the text bars the man impeded by crime from saying the orations at all; here nothing says that a failure shows the speaker was unworthy. Whether a fuller recension closes that gap is unread, and it would change the state of this protocol rather than its subject."@en,
        "The measurement is the weak point and is stated so: profiting in all the sciences is not a quantity, and any protocol has to choose one art and one instrument before the month begins. Choosing them afterwards would let the outcome pick its own measure, which is the failure mode pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate records after the fact and which is cheaper to prevent here."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, working from the passages recorded in traditions/ars-notoria.ttl" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "One person who keeps the full regimen for a lunation — the orations on the fourth, eighth, twelfth, sixteenth, twenty-fourth, twenty-eighth and thirtieth day of the moon, at the four hours, alone and in the prescribed manner — and who at the end of the month scores no higher on a test of a liberal art set before the month began than a person matched for prior knowledge who did not keep it. The claim is universal in form and admits no degree, so a single such case refutes it as stated." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-05" ;
    pm:tests pmc:ArsNotoriaOratioYieldsFacundia .

pmp:OrdealByOdumDecoction a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Ordal mit dem Sud aus Odum-Holz"@de,
        "The ordeal by a decoction of odum wood"@en ;
    dcterms:source "A. B. Ellis, The Ewe-speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1890), p. 97" ;
    skos:definition "Where human ingenuity fails to establish the truth of a charge, the decision is transferred to the gods. The commonest ordeal among these tribes is stated to be the drinking of a decoction of odum wood."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered although the source gives no reading rule, because the absence is itself informative: the graph now holds a poison ordeal from the Lower Congo with twenty-seven doses and a stated verdict rule, and here the same institution is named in one sentence by a witness who describes much else in detail. What is missing is the rule, not the practice."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by Ewe informants of the western and eastern tribes, unnamed; recorded by A. B. Ellis, colonial officer on the West African coast, from his own residence and from earlier printed reports he names as he uses them, published 1890; given in Ewe, of which the work prints the terms with etymologies, the accounts in English." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Vodun .

pmp:PalmsAsSendingOrganSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der Baopuzi die Handflächen zum Sendeorgan macht"@de,
        "Whether the Baopuzi makes the palms the organ of sending"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-10" ;
    skos:note "Added after the seventeenth juan was read at the wording later the same day: the rule stands there a second time, in a different operation. Blowing the qi at a snakebite ten and more li away, the practitioner calls the man's surname and name and invokes over his own left hand for a man and his own right for a woman, exactly as in the eighth. What the search found is therefore not a formula of one chapter but a rule the work sets twice, and the office it assigns to the hand is the same both times."@en,
        "Recorded as an outcome and not as a refutation. The claim may still stand in the corpora the issue names beside this one; what this node says is that this work was asked and answered with something else. See prima-materia#314."@en,
        "The chapter has the hand in exactly one operation and puts it in the other role. The practitioner who has heard that someone at a distance was struck by a venomous creature blows and incants over his own hand, the left for a man and the right for a woman, and the other is healed though a hundred li away. The hand is what the blowing is directed at and not what does the sending; the sending is the blowing, which the same passage uses on water, on fire, on a tiger and on a wound with no hand in it at all. What the search found is a near neighbour of the claim that assigns the office elsewhere."@en,
        "The laterality is the part of the find that matters to the issue, and it points away from it as well. Left and right are distinguished by the sex of the patient and not by a function of the two hands, so the passage cannot be cited for a rule about which hand transmits. The node it yielded carries the wording."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchTerms "The whole of juan 8 read at the wording; term search over all twenty juan for 掌, 掌心, 手心, 勞宮, 出氣, 發氣, 以手." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:XuZhu ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-10" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 8 (Shizhi), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 8; read at the wording, with the remaining nineteen juan searched by term" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage making the palms the place at which the qi leaves the body in an operation on another person or thing — the claim of prima-materia#314." .

pmp:PangweErythrophloeumSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Was dieselbe Monographie mit der zweiten Pflanze des mélan macht"@de,
        "What the same monograph does with the second plant of the mélan"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-05" ;
    skos:note "The opposing material is therefore not a denial but a reassignment, which is the harder case and the reason the dispute at pmc:WhatMelanIsDisputed names three readings rather than two. One recorder has the plant inside a graded initiation at its second degree; the other has it inside a judicial ordeal the accused undertakes himself, and gives neither an initiation nor a degree."@en,
        "The ordeal claim was read at the wording and is not entered as a node of its own, and the reason is a rule and not an omission. It is not an initiation, so it does not fall under the registered definition of pmt:Bwiti; and a Fang tradition is a new heading, which SPEC sections 11 and 14 reserve to the human. The wording is therefore carried inside the dispute so that nothing read is lost, and the registration question goes to the human with a recommendation."@en,
        "The plant is there and in a different institution. Volume I gives it twice in plant lists, as elün, the Leguminosa with the striking red bark, the poison tree, and once among the five trees whose charcoal is strong enough for iron smelting. Volume II gives it in wound and backache treatments, in a bird call, and then at pages 241 and 242 in the custom of the voluntary proof of truth, undertaken without invocation of any moral power by a person who believes himself falsely accused: he goes to an Erythrophlœum tree, strikes off some bark and chews it with water, three times, and publicly declares his innocence. The stated criterion is that in real innocence the poison is brought up again, and otherwise stays in the body and acts fatally."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Erythro, Erythrophloeum, Erythrophlœum, elun, elün, Giftbaum, Wahrheitsbeweis, Ordal, run case-insensitively over both volumes; every one of the eleven hits for the plant was read in context, and the two that stand in an institution rather than in a plant list were read at the wording with their printed page numbers taken from the running heads." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:WhatMelanIsDisputed ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-05" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Günter Tessmann, Die Pangwe, two volumes (Berlin: Ernst Wasmuth A.-G., 1913), both volumes complete as full text; the passage read at the wording is volume II, pages 241 and 242" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Whether the plant Trilles names for the second degree of the Fang initiation, Erythrophlœum guineense, appears in the Pangwe monograph at all, and if so in what institution. The search was the counter-search to pmc:FangMelanIsTwoPlantsUnderOneName and was run because a negative result on the society name alone would have left the first recorder's claim untouched." .

pmp:PangweIbogaSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die ausführlichste frühe Monographie über die Fang Iboga oder die Bouiti-Gesellschaft nennt"@de,
        "Whether the fullest early monograph on the Fang names iboga or the Bouiti society"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-05" ;
    skos:note "Nothing under any of the five society spellings and nothing under iboga, eboga or Tabernanthe, in either volume. The three hits for boga are all inside other words and were read. The men's cults the work does give by name over many pages are the Sso, Bokung, Elong, Schok, Ngi and Odschöe, with the Ngi named a hundred and twenty-seven times in volume II alone, and the women's cults Mawungu, Nkang and Endaluma. A transmission the holding now carries for this people under the Bwiti name is therefore absent from the fullest early account of it."@en,
        "The gap this exhibits, in the sense of SPEC section 13: whoever can read the Fang chapters of either work in the vernacular, or can obtain Fernandez 1982, can settle in an afternoon what this holding has had to leave as a dispute. The two candidate answers are both consequential and neither is expensive to test."@en,
        "What the search does not establish. It is a search over spellings and not over descriptions: a Fang initiation by a root preparation reported under a name none of these five terms covers would not have been caught. The one check made against that is the plant, and it points the other way - the second of the two plants Trilles names for the mélan is in this work and is prominent in it, which is why the outcome of pmp:PangweErythrophloeumSearch is opposing material rather than nothing."@en,
        "Why this is a finding about the corpus and not about access, which is the distinction that decides what the silence is worth. The recorder was admitted to a great deal: he gives the cults by name and in sequence, describes the Ngi feast day by day, prints the region's plants with their Pangwe names beside their Linnean ones, and names Erythrophlœum guineense eleven times across the two volumes. A transmission missing from a work of that reach is not one its author was kept away from, or not demonstrably so, and the burden falls on whoever wants to say it was."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "iboga, eboga, boga, bwiti, bouiti, bweti, mbwiri, melan, mélan, Erythro, Tabernanthe, run case-insensitively over the whole of both volumes, 671,686 and 1,085,767 characters. The vernacular spellings were chosen to cover the Fang, Mitsogo, Loango, Eshira and Mpongwe forms current in the literature; boga alone was included as the bare stem so that a prefixed form would be caught. The German transliteration conventions of the work were allowed for by searching the stem without its prefix and by checking every hit of boga by hand." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-05" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Günter Tessmann, Die Pangwe. Völkerkundliche Monographie eines westafrikanischen Negerstammes, Ergebnisse der Lübecker Pangwe-Expedition 1907-1909 und früherer Forschungen 1904-1907, two volumes (Berlin: Ernst Wasmuth A.-G., 1913), both volumes complete as full text" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Whether the most extensive early ethnography of the Fang, from a resident expedition of two years published one year after Trilles, names the root Tabernanthe iboga or a society of the Bwiti name anywhere. The question decides whether the Fang half of the transmission entered at traditions/bwiti.ttl is attested by a second witness, and if it is not, whether that silence is a finding about the corpus or about the recorder's access." .

pmp:ParikammaAdhitthana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Vorbereitung und die Entschließung"@de,
        "The preliminary work and the resolve"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.57-58 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, pp. 448-449; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "The operative sequence the chapter repeats at every capacity and varies only in the device used. He attains the absorption that is the basis of the direct knowledge; he emerges from it; he adverts to what is to be effected and does the preliminary work upon it, marking off the extent where an extent is in question; he attains the absorption again and emerges; and then he resolves once, by a single rising of knowledge, and what he resolved is the case simultaneously with the resolving consciousness."@en ;
    skos:note "The preliminary consciousness has the thing to be effected for its object, not a concept of it; the resolving consciousness is of the fourth absorption of the realm of form and arises once, immediately after the adoption. This is the only technical description of how the operations of this chapter are carried out, and it is the same for all of them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmp:PdmBothSidesSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Handbuch irgendwo beide Seiten eines Kriteriums nennt"@de,
        "Whether the manual anywhere states both sides of a criterion"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-18" ;
    skos:note "The one in the divinatory half is the reason this search was worth carrying. pmc:TheCriterionNoPartyControls asserts that where a criterion is an appearance announced to the participants beforehand, the transmission states the success side alone; here two distinct appearances are announced in advance and each is bound to one of the two answers before anything is seen. That is opposition to the second clause and is recorded at the protocol on it, pmp:BothSidesOfTheCriterionExamination, where a claim is corrected."@en,
        "Three places in a manual of sixty-two columns, and their distribution is the finding. The pregnancy test and the assay of the stone are in the medical and pharmacological half of the verso, where nothing is being invoked; the third, the priest's inquiry at VIII/12-16, is in the divinatory half and is the only one there. Against those three stand the seven or eight announced signs of arrival at pmc:PdmSignaAdventus, none of which has a failing side, and the six escalations at pmc:PdmCompulsioNonAdventus, which make one impossible."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "Every conditional clause of the translation was read at its place; searched additionally for: if he says, if it be that, if you find, if thou, then she will, will not, shall not come to pass, otherwise, on the contrary. Three hundred and more conditionals stand in the work and almost all of them are instructions, not readings." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:PdmProbatioGraviditatis,
        pmc:PdmProbatioLapidis,
        pmc:PdmRamshauFormaDuplex ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-18" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), recto columns I to XXIX and verso columns I to XXXIII entire, in the translation of F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage that fixes an outcome to a perceptible event and says what the event would be in each of the two cases — the form pmc:TheCriterionNoPartyControls describes, and in particular the form its second clause says does not occur, an appearance announced to the participant beforehand that carries the failing side as well as the succeeding one." .

pmp:PdmFailureConditionSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das demotische Handbuch irgendeinen Ausgang nennt, der gegen eine Befragung zählte"@de,
        "Whether the demotic manual names any outcome that would count against an inquiry"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-18" ;
    skos:note "It is the ground of the first clause of pmc:TheStrongerAddress, and it is recorded rather than summarised for the reason the class exists: without a node saying which terms were put and what came back, that clause would rest on an impression formed while reading, and a reader who suspects the impression would have to read the manual again."@en,
        "Limits. One translation, one editor, one optical transcription, and a stopping rule could be phrased without any of the twenty-three terms above — as an hour at which the operator departs, say. Against that, the nine datings collected at pmc:PdmHoraEtLuna were read continuously for exactly that possibility and none of them states a consequence, and the columns that carry the graded compulsions were read in full and not only searched."@en,
        "Nothing came back, and what came back instead is the finding. Every hit that touches an inquiry at all is an escalation or an assignment: the compulsion, the further cry, the name said nine times until they answer truthfully, and the two general assignments to purity and to the boy's fright. The one place where a session is broken off is broken off because of the medium and not because of the god. The manual takes up the empty session six times and does not once treat it as a result."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of the translation, over a whitespace-normalised copy, for: in vain, fruitless, of no effect, no effect, useless, unsuccessful, leave off, give over, give up, break off, desist (none of them occurs); fail (two hits, one inside an invocation of the speaker's own strength, one the phrase without fail); faileth (one, inside an invocation); cease (eight; the only two that touch an operation are at XVII/17, where the operator ceases from the inquiry because the boy has looked away, and the clause about the gods going away and the boy ceasing to see them); abandon (one, in the editors' introduction); stop (four, all of stopping a vessel's mouth or staunching blood); not succeed (one, the purity clause at XVII/26); delay, obduracy, obstinacy, slow to come, do not come, not come (thirteen between them, every one of which is answered by a further formula and none by an end)." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-18" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), recto columns I to XXIX and verso columns I to XXXIII entire, in the translation of F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage naming a result of a correctly prepared inquiry under which the operator is to conclude that it did not work: a term after which he is to stop, a number of addresses beyond which the god is not to be pressed, or a sign that nothing will come. This is the material from which a pm:falsifiedBy can be formed out of a transmission's own mouth, and the same question was put to the Clavicula at pmp:ClaviculaFailureConditionSearch." .

pmp:PdmPraescriptaEnumerataSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Wie viele Vorschriften dieses Handbuch ausserhalb seines divinatorischen Teils fuehrt und wie viele von ihnen ein Zeichen oder eine Misslingensbedingung nennen"@de,
        "How many prescriptions this manual carries outside its divinatory half, and how many of them name a sign or a condition of failure"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-09" ;
    skos:note "Limits. One translation, one editor, one optical transcription, and the count of prescriptions depends on where a reader cuts: five places in the work give the same prescription twice and are entered once with both loci, and five more stand once with a named outcome and once with the formula that it does its work, entered once with both. A reader who counted the doublets separately would reach eighty-seven. The cut taken here is the prescription as the work distinguishes it, and the places where it does not distinguish are named at the nodes."@en,
        "The fourteen entries of col. XXIV and col. verso XXX whose whole content is an ingredient and a back-reference were counted as prescriptions, because the back-reference does the work of the instruction and the manual means them to be used. Omitted with reason, and named here so that the omission is not silent: the eye-paint at col. verso XXI/4-6 and the star at col. verso XXVIII/1-2, both too damaged to carry a stated outcome, and the clause for making the skin disease troublesome at col. XIII/25, whose ingredient is lost."@en,
        "The two counts, which are the answer prima-materia#747 asked for and which are now over the whole work and not over its divinatory part alone. Announced signs of success: seven or eight, every one of them in the divinatory half and collected at pmc:PdmSignaAdventus; in the seventy-seven prescriptions outside it, none. Conditions of failure, meaning an outcome under which the operator is to conclude that it did not work: none, in either half. What the non-divinatory half has instead is thirty-two clauses of outcome, listed in the terms above, and they are outcomes and not signs: the claim and the thing to be observed are the same event, so there is nothing for the operator to look for on the way and nothing he could see that the clause excludes."@en,
        "Three things the count did turn up, and they are the material the examinability-driven entrance of SPEC §14 is after. A graded threshold at pmc:PdmCataplexiaCapitisAsini, where four days of the same operation are to produce sleep and seven days death, which is the only place in the work where a quantity decides between two stated outcomes. A stopping rule at pmc:PdmMorsusCanisPrimus, where the wound is addressed daily until it is well, which is the only end set by a condition of the patient rather than by a count. And a question put at a term at pmc:PdmPodagraInterrogatio, already entered. Against them stand the four stages of pmc:PdmAquaMulierisPrima through pmc:PdmAquaMulierisQuarta, nineteen days of treatment with no criterion between any two stages."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "The whole translation of the named columns was read continuously, not searched, and each prescription entered as its own node before the count was taken; the resulting enumeration is traditions/demotic-recipes.ttl with seventy-seven nodes. The clause of outcome was then counted mechanically over a whitespace-normalised copy of the translation with the inline line numbers stripped, because the line numbers stand inside the sentences and break every phrase search: then he dies 4, then she dies 1, then he is blinded 3, then he sleeps 1, then he is upset 1, then her body falls 1, then they are separated 1 (as the clause without agreeing again for ever), shall not be healed 1, then she has a passion 2, then she feels 1, then it brings you 2, then she comes at once 1, comes to pass instantly 1, then it makes dreams 1, then the bone comes up 1, then it stops 1, then he recovers 1, then it is cured 1, then she is cured 1, and it is safe 1, then it is troublesome 1, then it does its work 4 with it does its work also 1 and it does its work very much 1." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-09" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), recto columns IV, XI to XVI, XIX to XXI, XXIII to XXVI and verso columns II to XXXIII, in the translation of F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel and Co., 1904. The divinatory columns were excluded because their signs and escalations were counted in the search of 2026-09-18." ;
    pm:searchedFor "A count rather than a place: every single prescription of the non-divinatory half, read at its own column and line, and for each of them whether the work names an announced sign by which the operator would know it had worked and whether it names any outcome under which he would conclude it had not. SPEC §15 requires the two to be counted separately and over the whole holding of a work, and prima-materia#747 asked for the figures for this work once the list node was broken up." .

pmp:PdmProbatioAurium a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Prüfung des Knaben an seinen Ohren"@de,
        "The testing of the boy in his ears"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. III/16-20, with col. X/14-15, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A test carried on the boy before the vessel is used, to learn whether he will be profitable in going to the vessel. The operator recites a short formula down into the boy's head seven times, asking that he be purified in the manner of the noble ibis, falcon and hawk; then the boy's ears speak. If both ears speak he is very good; if it is his right ear, he is good; if it is his left ear, he is bad."@en ;
    skos:note "The graded outcome is the reason this is a practice of its own and not a clause of the vessel-inquiry. A later column requires that the child brought to the vessel be one that has been tested in his ears before, so the test is a standing precondition and not a flourish of one recipe."@en,
        "What the test measures is not the boy's accuracy about anything. No question with a knowable answer is put to him, and nothing he reports is checked against a fact; the whole of it is an event in his own body, reported by himself, in three grades, after the operator has told him what the grades are. That is worth stating because the procedure looks at first like a control and works like an initiation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmp:PdmProbatumQuidSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Handbuch sagt, worin seine neun Erprobungen bestanden"@de,
        "Whether the manual says what its nine trials consisted in"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-18" ;
    skos:note "Not one of the six marks carries anything behind it. The mark is attached to the recipe and never to an outcome; no question is named, no answer is checked, and no trial is described. In one of the two numbered cases the number is not even the compiler's own — it is what he reports the informant as having said. The claim of nine trials therefore stands in a manual that pmp:PdmFailureConditionSearch shows has no way of counting an unsuccessful one."@en,
        "The finding is entered as a search and not as a node because it is an absence in the work, and the node it bears on, pmc:PdmProbatumNovies, carries what the work does say. The distinction matters here more than usual: a reader who meets tested nine times in a translation will supply the missing half by himself unless something in the record says it is missing."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search for: tested, tried, proved, approved, tries, trial, times tried, nine times, true and tried. The six marks are at IV/23, V/3, VIII/12, XIV/31, XXIV/1 and XXIX/1; each was read at its place together with the recipe it stands over and the recipe following it. Read as well: the editors' note to VIII/12, which compares the phrase with XIV/31 and XXIX/1 and holds nine times tried to be proverbial." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-18" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), recto columns I to XXIX and verso columns I to XXXIII entire, in the translation of F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    pm:searchedFor "At the six places where the manual marks a procedure as tested, tried or well tested, and at the two where it gives the number nine: who carried the trials, on what question, against what the answer was afterwards set, and what an unsuccessful trial would have looked like." .

pmp:PdmReworkingSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der Zusammensteller sagt, was er an einem übernommenen Verfahren geändert hat"@de,
        "Whether the compiler says what he changed in a procedure he received"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-18" ;
    skos:note "The compiler names his donors and sets his variants beside one another, and nowhere says that he changed anything or why one reading is preferred. The two comparative judgments the work does make are about procedures and not about versions: that this vessel-inquiry of the lamp is better than the beginning, and that there is none better than the amulet of the sixteen-thread band. Neither names what the worse one lacked."@en,
        "The finding bears on how much a chain of copies is worth. This manual is at the near end of the demotic and Greek handbook tradition and at the far end of the grimoire stream, and it hands on procedures from at least four named persons without recording a single alteration. pm:Reworking counts the stations at which somebody took an instruction, carried it out and wrote down what was not in the exemplar; on the evidence of this work, this station is not one of them."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Every place where the work names a source or a variant was read at its wording: gave to me (I/1 and XVIII/7), said to me (XIV/1), he tells it (VIII/12), another roll says, another book says, another volume saith, another says, as says another book, otherwise said. Searched additionally for: I added, I found, I corrected, I have proved, better than, stronger than, is better." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-18" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), recto columns I to XXIX and verso columns I to XXXIII entire, in the translation of F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    pm:searchedFor "An addition the compiler makes to a procedure he says he received from somebody else — a term, a caution, a sign, a condition of failure that the donor's version is said not to have had. That is the material of pm:Reworking, and a manual that names four donors and cites competing rolls is the likeliest place in this holding to find it." .

pmp:PdmShenPre a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Sonnenbefragung"@de,
        "The inquiry of the sun"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XXIX/1-30, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A divination made facing the rising sun. A young pure boy is stood on a new brick at the moment the sun rises and comes up entire with the whole disk, a new mat of linen behind him and his eyes shut; the operator stands over him, recites down into his head, strikes down on his head with the Ra-finger of his right hand, and has first filled the boy's eye with a prepared paint. A variant has the boy gaze at the sun when it fills the uzat instead of at its rising, and a further variant has the operator do it alone with the paint in his own eyes and his eyes open towards the sun."@en ;
    skos:note "The last words of the recto are the plainest statement in the work of what it holds the whole apparatus to depend on, and they are said of this procedure: its chief point is purity, and it is profitable for the boy and profitable for the operator acting alone. The same sentence therefore states the condition and says that the condition is indifferent to which of the two persons carries the perception."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmp:PdmUnctioOculorum a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Füllen der Augen, das den Knaben entbehrlich macht"@de,
        "The filling of the eyes, which lets the operator dispense with the boy"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. V/24-31, with col. IV/23-24 and col. verso XXII/2-8, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A preparation put into the eyes so that the operator himself perceives what the boy would otherwise perceive. Several are given. The chief one is made from flowers of the Greek bean bought from the garland-seller or the lupin-seller, shut into a glass vessel in a hidden dark place for twenty days, at which point a pair of testicles with a phallus is said to be found in it, and for forty days more, at which point it is said to have become bloody; the blood is what the eyes are filled with. Others are a hawk's egg with myrrh, the head and blood of a hoopoe, and a paint of green eye-paint and stibium ground with further ingredients."@en ;
    skos:note "It is the hinge of the whole apparatus. Wherever the papyrus offers to do an inquiry alone it offers this and nothing else in place of the boy, so the second person is replaceable by a substance while the purity, the hour, the new brick and the count are not."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmp:PgmIvConsecratioScarabaei a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Weihe des Skarabaeus, begonnen wenn der Mond nicht zu sehen ist"@de,
        "The consecration of the scarab, begun when the moon is not to be seen"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the teaching of the operation (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 17 line 7 to p. 18 line 5)" ;
    skos:definition "The preparation the appendix calls the teaching of the operation. A solar scarab with twelve rays is taken; at the seizure of the moon it is made to be thrown into a deep turquoise vessel together with the seed of the lotus pith, ground up with honey into a cake. The beetle comes to it and eats. It is then taken up and put into a glass vessel of the best rose oil, consecrated sand is strewn beneath, the vessel is set upon it, and the name is said over the vessel for seven days while the sun stands at the meridian. On the seventh day the beetle is taken out, dipped in myrrh, Mendesian wine and byssos, and laid away in a bean field in bloom; the ointment is then put away in purity, after a meal taken in company, for the immortalization."@en ;
    skos:note "A dated start, a term of seven days, a daily hour, and a seventh-day act. The start is fixed by a state of the moon and not by a day of a calendar, which is a harder condition than a date because it cannot be anticipated by counting from an arbitrary point. The hour is fixed by the sun standing at the meridian, so the recitation is tied twice over."@en,
        "The materials are named by kind and not left open: the twelve-rayed solar beetle, the deep turquoise vessel, the seed of the lotus pith, honey, the best rose oil, consecrated sand, myrrh, Mendesian wine, byssos, and a bean field in bloom. SPEC section 3 parameter 1 provides for these as identifiers under a namespace of their own and no such identifier exists in the holding yet, so they stand here in the definition of the procedure. Nothing about their working is claimed by naming them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmp:PgmIvPhylakteriaDuo a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Zwei Amulette, eines auf der Haut eines schwarzen und eines auf der eines weissen Schafs"@de,
        "Two amulets, one on the skin of a black sheep and one on that of a white"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the amulets (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 20 lines 3-8)" ;
    skos:definition "The making of the amulets the rite has the operator kiss at two of its stages. The right one is written on the membrane of a black sheep with myrrh-black ink, and is bound round with sinews of the same animal; the left one on the membrane of a white sheep, and is used in the same way. The left is stated to be for those well disposed."@en ;
    skos:note "The liturgy proper has the operator kiss the amulets twice and says nothing of where they come from; the appendix supplies the material and the writing. Colour is made to carry the difference between the two, and the binding is required to come from the same animal as the skin."@en,
        "The text that is written on them is given as four Homeric hexameters, on which see pmc:PgmIvVersusHomerici. The right amulet is not given a purpose in the text as it stands, and the editor notes that the formula for the left one appears to be missing."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmp:PoisonousHandTurtleExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Probe an der Schildkröte die giftige Hand unterscheidet"@de,
        "Whether the pressing of the turtle discriminates the poisonous hand"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that a person whose hand is poisonous kills a turtle within fifty days by pressing on the vessel it is held under, while a person whose hand is sweet leaves it living as before. The claim is examinable in the form the source gives it: the term is fixed, the arrangement is described, and the two outcomes are stated as different."@en ;
    skos:note "A protocol that ever ran would also have to say what the corpus does not: whether the term begins at the first pressing or at the last, and how often the pressing is repeated within the fifty days. The passage gives neither, and a run that fixed them would be fixing them, not reading them."@en,
        "No case has been collected, and the obstacle is not the scarcity of turtles. It is that the source does not say what it is about the pressing that is supposed to act, so the arrangement it describes has no condition under which the pressing is present and the poison absent. A run of the procedure would establish that a turtle held under a pressed vessel for fifty days dies or does not; whether the hand made the difference needs a second arm the source does not describe, and devising one is the work this state is waiting on."@en,
        "The state is procedure-without-cases and not no-procedure-devised, and the difference is the whole reason this node exists. The tradition devised the procedure; nobody has carried it. Recording that as though nobody had thought of a test would report the reach of this project's instruments as a property of the corpus."@en,
        "What the claim does not do is the thing most claims of this kind do. It does not say that the poisonous hand harms only where the intent is wrong, or only in a person unprepared, or only where the practitioner is not seen. There is no absorbing clause in the sentence, and that absence is what makes the fifty days mean anything."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, working from the passage recorded in traditions/chinese-medical-canon.ttl" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A person named as having the poisonous hand who presses as described and whose turtle is alive and unchanged after fifty days; or a person named as having the sweet hand whose turtle dies within the term. The source states the outcomes as a pair and attaches no clause under which either could still be counted as confirming, so a case on either side falls against the claim." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-09" ;
    pm:tests pmc:Shoudu .

pmp:PostMortemForWitchcraftSubstance a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Leichenschau auf die Hexereisubstanz"@de,
        "The post-mortem for witchcraft substance"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 3-4" ;
    skos:definition "The belly of a dead man is opened and the substance looked for. A post-mortem in which none is found clears his paternal kin of the suspicion, and cases so decided are quoted afterwards by that kin in their defence."@en ;
    skos:note "A procedure inside the transmission with an outcome that could have fallen otherwise, applied to a claim about a class of persons, and reported as having been used to settle actual accusations. It is the second reason this tradition was opened."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmp:Pranayama a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Prāṇāyāma"@de,
        "Prāṇāyāma"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra II.49-53" ;
    skos:definition "The regulation of the breath as a distinct limb of practice, following posture and preceding the withdrawal of the senses."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PatanjalaYoga .

pmp:ProxyDosingOfTheAccused a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Dosieren von Stellvertretern zur Auswahl des Beschuldigten"@de,
        "The dosing of substitutes to choose the accused"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), p. 261" ;
    skos:definition "Where two or three persons are implicated in one charge, the witch-finder takes as many small boys, one to represent each suspected adult, gives a small quantity of the ordeal to each and watches the result. If the symptoms shown by one or two of the boys warrant it, the adults they represent are accused and must take the ordeal properly; if none of the boys shows the signs, others are suspected and fresh representatives are appointed. Only members of the same family are held able to bewitch one another, and only boys of the family of the deceased are used, and they are paid for it."@en ;
    skos:note "A screening step before the ordeal proper, and the only case in the file where the transmission runs the procedure on a person who is not the party to the question. It is also where the physiology of the substance is made to do the work of the answer, since what is watched is the effect of a small dose on a child."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by Bakongo of the Lower Congo, unnamed in this passage; recorded by the Baptist missionary John H. Weeks over some thirty years of residence, the book closed in 1913; given in Kikongo, of which the work prints the terms while the accounts are printed in English only." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:KongoNkisi .

pmp:QuimbyExperimentumMentale a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Jeder Auftrag an das Medium nur gedanklich, nie laut"@de,
        "Every command to the subject given mentally and never aloud"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Horatio W. Dresser (ed.), The Quimby Manuscripts, 2nd ed. (New York: Thomas Y. Crowell, 1921), pp. 50-51, with the operator's own statement of the ground at p. 60" ;
    skos:definition "The rule the operator adopted at the outset and held for the four years of his association with the subject: never to let the subject know what was expected of him save mentally. Even a wish that the subject give him his hand was asked mentally and never audibly. All communication during an experiment was silent, the subject answering as if spoken to aloud."@en ;
    skos:note "It is entered as a procedure because it is stated as one and kept as one, and it is the reason the rest of this file is worth entering. The operator gives his own ground for it in the first person: being a sceptic he would not believe anything his subject did if there was any chance for deception, so all his experiments were carried on mentally. A control against his own credulity, built into the procedure by the person whose claim it protects, is a thing this holding has almost none of."@en,
        "What it does and does not exclude. It removes the spoken cue and nothing else: the operator was in the room, holding the subject's hands, and whatever passed between them that was not speech passed freely. The volume itself supplies the limit a page later, where the operator finds that his own unspoken belief about silk moved the subject's hand. So the rule bought him certainty against one channel and left him inside another, and he says so."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:QuimbyManuscripts ;
    pm:readVia "The editor's connecting narrative in Horatio W. Dresser (ed.), The Quimby Manuscripts, 2nd ed. (New York 1921), which summarises the mesmeric years from the papers, the journal and the notices rather than printing them at the place" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought .

pmp:RabbinicCounterTransmissionSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Ueberlieferung, die die Kunst verbietet, ein Kriterium fuer eine behauptete Verrichtung nennt"@de,
        "Whether the transmission that forbids the art names a criterion for a claimed performance"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-01" ;
    skos:note "A finding about the editions, which cost the run a comparison and should save the next one the same. The public-domain English of 1902 is defective for this subject in three measured places: at the permission to learn in tractate Sabbath it omits the whole proof, which its own explanatory remarks announce as method, since where the original gives two statements separated by a formula of alternative it translates only the second; at the name of the art it loses the acrostic and renders the ground of the name as an aside; and the dispute it attaches names the wrong authority to the wrong word. Goldschmidt's German is complete at all three, prints the Aramaic beside it, and carries an apparatus with a printed key to its sigla. For this corpus the German is the edition and the English is not."@en,
        "An open question the search raised and did not settle. Goldschmidt's apparatus carries a note at the word of liability in the first clause of the Mishnah, giving it for the Munich manuscript, while his base text, the censorship-free Bomberg edition of Venice 1520 to 1523, is rendered without it, and Danby's English likewise reads the clause as a definition. With the key alone the direction of the variant cannot be settled, and whether the first clause of this Mishnah is a definition or a penal norm turns on it. The place where it is decidable is Strack's edition of the tractate of 1910, which prints the Mishnah from manuscripts and old prints and was not found on the regular route in this run."@en,
        "The find is a criterion of a kind the holding did not carry. At pmc:SanhedrinBloodAndDungAsTheTraceOfTheDeed a performance is judged by the residue a real deed would have left, by a man who did not see it, from a report by a man who did. Every other criterion in this holding is a sign announced to a practitioner beforehand, which he afterwards reports. This one is negative, cheap, and cannot be supplied by expectation."@en,
        "The second find is that the transmission does not keep one criterion. The same discussion also carries the water that dissolves an enchantment at a market in Alexandria, on the opposite premise: there the test ends a transformation the text treats as having occurred. The two are not harmonised in the text, and a reader who meets only one of them will misreport the transmission."@en,
        "What the search does not establish. Neither of these texts names the handbook and the handbook names neither of them; the juxtaposition is the compiler's, and no node of this run claims otherwise. What is established and dated is that the Mishnah was fixed around the year two hundred and the Babylonian discussion is carried by names of the third and fourth centuries, while every dating of the handbook now defended in the field puts it later; only the editor's own early dating of 1896 would reverse that. The handbook's silence about failure is therefore a silence against a known norm and not a state of innocence. The datings themselves are modern scholarship and enter this holding only as evidence at an examination, never as a source here; they are named in the issues of this run."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "Danby's volume was read at the subject heading for the sorcerer and the two paragraphs under it, found by searching the full text for Sorcerer, and the pages before and after were read for the context and the page sequence. Goldschmidt's seventh volume was located by the Hebrew of the Mishnah, the strings for the one who holds the eyes, for the sorcerer, for the cucumbers and for the holding of the eyes in the Aramaic, and then read continuously in German from the running head 285 to the running head 291, with the Hebrew column stripped so that the two did not run together. The permission to learn was located by the Hebrew for thou shalt not learn, for to understand and for to teach. The German column was also searched for the household above, for the drum, for the camel and for the eye-deception. Rodkinson's English of 1902 was fetched for comparison and measured against Goldschmidt at three places." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:SanhedrinBloodAndDungAsTheTraceOfTheDeed,
        pmc:SanhedrinLearningIsPermittedToUnderstandAndToTeach,
        pmc:SanhedrinNothingBesideHimEvenInSorcery,
        pmc:SanhedrinSorcererIsTheOneWhoDoesTheDeed,
        pmc:SanhedrinSorceryContradictsTheHeavenlyRetinue,
        pmc:SanhedrinTheSorcererIsStoned,
        pmc:SanhedrinThreeHundredRulesOnTheWitch,
        pmc:SanhedrinThreefoldDivisionOfTheLaw,
        pmc:SanhedrinWaterDissolvesTheTransformation,
        pmc:SanhedrinWhatIsBeyondTheDemons,
        pmc:SanhedrinWhetherTheArtPrevailsAgainstTheDecree ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-01" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Mishnah Sanhedrin VII.11 and Tosefta Sanhedrin XI.5c, trans. Herbert Danby, Tractate Sanhedrin: Mishnah and Tosefta (London: Society for Promoting Christian Knowledge, 1919), pp. 106-107; and Babylonian Talmud, Synhedrin folios 67a to 68a, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. VII (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1903), pp. 285-290" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Whether the rabbinic legal transmission, which prohibits the operations an operative handbook of the same language and region prescribes, states any criterion by which a performance claimed to have worked is judged: a distinction between a deed and an appearance, a residue a real deed would leave, a test anybody could apply. The question was put because the handbook in traditions/hekhalot.ttl carries a hundred and thirty-six operations and, by the search at pmp:HarbaDeMosheDefectusSearch, no condition of failure anywhere among them, and because the counter-transmission to that silence had been named and not read." .

pmp:RabbinicDefectusSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der gelesene Teil irgendeine Bedingung nennt, unter der eine Verrichtung misslungen waere"@de,
        "Whether the part read states any condition under which a procedure would have failed"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-01" ;
    skos:note "Nothing, and the shape of the nothing is informative. This transmission is unusually well supplied with tests and unusually empty of conditions of failure, and the two are not in tension: its tests are forensic. They are put to a performance that has been exhibited, by somebody deciding whether a deed was done, and that is a different instrument from a condition told to a practitioner in advance. Of the two performances it reports of its own teachers, the calf and the field of cucumbers, neither carries a condition of any kind."@en,
        "The limit of the finding, and it is a real one. The search covers seven printed pages of one tractate and no more. The place in this transmission where a numbered condition is known to stand is the rule of the proven amulet in tractate Sabbath, where a thing counts as proven when it has healed and repeated and done so a third time; that place was not opened in this run and no node rests on it. It is named in the issues as the next and cheapest continuation, and in the public-domain English it is reachable as well as in the German."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "The German column of the part read, with the Hebrew and Aramaic stripped out so that the two did not run together, searched for: misslingt, misslang, Misslingen, wirkt nicht, vergeblich, umsonst, gelingt nicht, nicht gelingen, gelingt, wiederhol, Frist, unwirksam, nichts geschieht, vermag nicht, Probe, all of them with no hit at all; Zeichen, one hit, and it is a sign of puberty in the following chapter; pruefen, one hit, and it is the water at the market; kannst, one hit, and it is R. Hanina telling the woman to try. The part was then read through again to see whether such a condition stood anywhere without those words." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-01" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Babylonian Talmud, Synhedrin folios 67a to 68b, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. VII (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1903), pp. 285-291" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Any sentence in the part read saying what would show that one of the operations this transmission reports had not worked, what to do if it did not, a term after which nothing more comes, a repetition on failure, or a defect in the operator or the material that would prevent the effect. The two criteria the discussion does carry judge a performance somebody has already seen; the question here is whether anything judges a procedure beforehand." .

pmp:RabbinicObservedSuccessDefectusSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die drei Stellen eine Bedingung nennen, unter der eine Verrichtung misslungen waere"@de,
        "Whether the three places name a condition under which an operation would have failed"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-04" ;
    skos:note "The limit of this search, stated in the same terms as its predecessor's. It covers five printed pages of one tractate, four of a second and twelve of a third, out of three tractates running to several hundred folios, and it reaches no part of this transmission outside the Babylonian Talmud. The place in it where a numbered condition is known to stand, the proven amulet of tractate Sabbath, has now been named by two searches and opened by neither."@en,
        "Two finds, of very different strength, and the weaker one is the one the search was shaped to catch. The strong find is the amulet of Pesahim 111b, where a wrong number in the formula is the stated cause of the failure and the right number repairs it. The weak find is R. Hanina's words at Holin 7b, take it, your purpose will not succeed, which is a prediction that a particular operation by a particular person will fail; it is a condition of failure of somebody else's operation and not of one this transmission prescribes, and the discussion immediately makes the general claim safe by the man's merits, so nothing in the transmission turns on whether the prediction held."@en,
        "What still did not come back, after three tractates. No term, no count of attempts, no repetition on failure, and no defect in a material. The remedies the Pesahim part prescribes are given as lists of alternatives any one of which suffices - five for the thirst at night, three for the two women in the street - and a list of sufficient alternatives is the opposite of a condition of failure: it says what else to try and never when to stop. Of the operations the two earlier files report of this transmission's own teachers, the calf, the field of cucumbers, the river and the ass, not one carries a condition of any kind."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "The separated German of all three parts was searched for misslingt, misslang, Misslingen, wirkt nicht, vergeblich, umsonst, gelingt, gelingt nicht, nicht gelingen, unwirksam, wiederhol, Frist, Probe, pruefen, Zeichen, vermag nicht, nichts geschieht, nuetzt nichts, and for the numerals one to sixty spelt out in the Pesahim part, where numbers carry the rule. Each part was then read through continuously so that a condition standing without any of those words would be seen." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:HolinTakeItYourPurposeWillNotSucceed,
        pmc:PesahimAmuletAgainstSixtyDemons ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-04" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Babylonian Talmud, Aboda zara folios 54b to 56a, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. VII (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1903), pp. 990-992; Holin folios 7a to 7b, vol. VIII (Leipzig: Otto Harrassowitz, 1909), pp. 819-822; Pesahim folios 110a to 112b, vol. II (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1901), pp. 703-714" ;
    pm:searchedFor "The question pmp:RabbinicDefectusSearch put to tractate Sanhedrin on 2026-10-01 and answered with nothing, put again to the three places opened here: any sentence saying what would show that a prescribed operation of this transmission had not worked, what to do if it did not, a term after which nothing more comes, a repetition on failure, or a defect in the operator or the material that would prevent the effect." .

pmp:RabbinicObservedSuccessSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Was die Ueberlieferung mit einem beobachteten Erfolg macht"@de,
        "What the transmission does with a success somebody has seen"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-04" ;
    skos:note "A finding about the way, entered here as well as in the table of access routes because it cost this run a search that should not have to be repeated. The identifier pattern derbabylonischet<NN>gold holds for the seventh volume of this translation and for no other; derbabylonischet02gold, derbabylonischet08gold and derbabylonischet01gold have no item behind them. The whole translation is reachable as derbabylonischet01unse to derbabylonischet08unse, found by searching the archive's own catalogue for the title and the translator rather than by guessing an identifier. A second, later translation by the same man exists on the same archive as twelve items named band-1 to band-12, published between 1929 and 1936 with a different volume division; its first volume carries no full text, and its pagination is not the pagination of the volumes cited here. Citing a page from the one in the belief that it is the other is the error this note exists to prevent."@en,
        "The answer is that it never denies the success, in any of the three places, and that it has three different things to put in place of a denial. At Aboda zara 55a the cure is granted and the causal connection is cut, by an appointment in time that the cure was keeping anyway. On the same page the rain is granted and the connection is left standing while its sign is reversed, so that the operation works and working is the punishment. At Holin 7b and in the Pesahim material the claim itself is moved instead: narrowed after a counter-observation, discharged for whole classes of case, or qualified clause by clause until what remains is reached by several coincidences."@en,
        "The strongest single result is not an answer to the question asked. It is the amulet at Pesahim 111b, which supplies what the search at pmp:RabbinicDefectusSearch looked for in tractate Sanhedrin and did not find: an operation with a quantity in it, a wrong value of the quantity as the named cause of failure, a sign of the failure delivered by the party operated against, and a second performance with the right value that succeeds. That search's own note said its limit was that it covered one tractate and named Sabbath 61a as the next place; the place that answers it was three tractates away in another direction."@en,
        "What the search corrected rather than added. The note at pmc:SanhedrinNothingBesideHimEvenInSorcery reported from the secondary literature that the parallel at Holin 7b gives R. Hanina a flatter answer and that this would amount to a denial of efficacy rather than a personal exception. The flatter answer is there; the consequence is not, because the same objection and the same resolution by the man's merits follow it in this tractate too. The reading therefore refuted the conjecture that had been entered in place of the reading, which is the case AGENTS.md records at prima-materia#76 and the reason the second stage of a search is not optional."@en,
        "What was not read, so that the next run knows where this one stopped. Of Aboda zara only folios 54b to 56a; the tractate runs to folio 76b and its opening chapters on the festivals of the nations were not opened. Of Holin only folios 7a to 7b of a tractate of a hundred and forty-two folios. Of Pesahim only folios 110a to 112b, which is the end of the tenth chapter; the account of the four cups that the pairs material hangs off begins earlier in the chapter and was not read. Of the three places the lease named, all three were read. The proven amulet at Sabbath 61a, which both this search and pmp:RabbinicDefectusSearch name as the place where a numbered condition stands, is in the first volume of this translation and was not opened in this run either."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "The German column of each part was separated from the Hebrew and Aramaic before reading, by keeping only lines above a measured proportion of Latin letters, because the two stand side by side and the full text interleaves them line by line. Aboda zara was located by the running head of the tractate and then by the folio head for 55a, and the whole of folios 54b to 56a was read continuously. Holin was located by the folio heads for 7a and 7b and read continuously. Pesahim was located by the folio heads for 110a to 112b and read continuously, and then searched in the German for geschieden, Geschiedene, entliess, Zauber, behex, achtgab, Paare, and in the surrounding part for the case report the lease named. The page sequence of all three parts was checked against the running heads before any page was cited." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:AbodaZaraAfflictionsSwornToTheirHour,
        pmc:AbodaZaraRainFollowsTheSlaughter,
        pmc:AbodaZaraRainOnDemandOfTheIdol,
        pmc:AbodaZaraTheIdolatersAreTalkedOver,
        pmc:HolinBloodFromTheStubbedToeAtones,
        pmc:HolinTakeItYourPurposeWillNotSucceed,
        pmc:HolinTheBeastRefusesTheUntithed,
        pmc:HolinThePiousAreGreaterAfterDeath,
        pmc:HolinThreeTimesAgainstMosesOnce,
        pmc:HolinWhiteMuleClaimNarrowedTwice,
        pmc:PesahimAmuletAgainstSixtyDemons,
        pmc:PesahimFourActsEachWithAnExit,
        pmc:PesahimPairsExemptions,
        pmc:PesahimTheCountingContestToAHundredAndOne,
        pmc:PesahimTheDangerOfPairs,
        pmc:PesahimTheDivorcedWifesSpell,
        pmc:PesahimTheForgottenItemOfTheList,
        pmc:PesahimTwoWomenFacingEachOther,
        pmc:PesahimWhoeverHeedsIsHeeded,
        pmp:AbodaZaraSlaughterForRain,
        pmp:HolinCommandingTheRiverToDivide,
        pmp:PesahimAmuletForSixtyDemons,
        pmp:PesahimCounterFormulaAgainstTheSorceresses,
        pmp:PesahimSabririFormula ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-04" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Babylonian Talmud, Aboda zara folios 54b to 56a, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. VII (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1903), pp. 990-992; Holin folios 7a to 7b, vol. VIII (Leipzig: Otto Harrassowitz, 1909), pp. 819-822; Pesahim folios 110a to 112b, vol. II (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1901), pp. 703-714" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Whether the rabbinic legal transmission, which holds the art capital and holds in several places that it has no power against a man of merit, anywhere meets a reported success of a rival practice by denying that the success occurred; and if it does not, what it does instead. The question was put because the run of 2026-10-01 read tractate Sanhedrin and found a transmission that argues at length about whether the art works and never once about a case anybody watched, and because its own notes named these three places and said that no node rested on them." .

pmp:RasaAnuvasana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Wiederherstellung des Quecksilbers"@de,
        "Rehabilitation of mercury"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, anubasanam, pp. 71-73" ;
    skos:altLabel "anubasanam"@en ;
    skos:definition "The ninth operation. Stimulated mercury is mixed with lime juice and exposed to the sun for a day in an earthen or stone pot, or boiled with sour asava or with the juices of a named list of some fifty herbs called the rasamulika. The stated purpose is the intensifying of its potency."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmp:RasaBhasmikarana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Veraschung des Quecksilbers"@de,
        "Incineration of mercury"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, bhasmikaranam, pp. 155-175" ;
    skos:altLabel "bhasmikaranam"@en ;
    skos:definition "The sixteenth operation, given in twenty-five processes, some following on killing and some expressly without it. Dead mercury is burnt by putapaka after rubbing with named oils, or heated forty-eight hours between two basins packed with the five muds, or sealed in a ball of sulphur and boiled seven days. The products are described by colour — white as camphor, red as the morning sun, like an emerald, like smelted silver."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmp:RasaDiksha a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Einweihung des Schülers"@de,
        "The initiation of the disciple"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. I, pp. 6-7, and ch. II, initiation of disciple, pp. 17-20" ;
    skos:definition "At a moment when the moon stands with a benefic star and both are strong, a water jar containing gold, gems and fruits is set before the mercurial phallus and covered; the image is worshipped, a fire is made in a triangular pit and given oil, ghee, milk-rice and lotuses with the two hymns; the disciple is then called, bathed, sprinkled with the water of the jar, and the science of controlling mercury is imparted to him. He pays a fee according to his ability and then recites each hymn a hundred thousand times."@en ;
    skos:note "The qualifications are stated on both sides beforehand — the preceptor wise, courageous, a worshipper of Śiva, master of his senses and fond of disciples; the disciple modest, truthful, persevering, retentive and devoted — and the transmission adds that the science is not to be taught to those who are not disciples, to atheists, or to the dishonest."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmp:RasaMardana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Reiben des Quecksilbers"@de,
        "Rubbing of mercury"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, mardanam, pp. 53-56" ;
    skos:altLabel "mardanam"@en ;
    skos:definition "The third operation. Mercury is rubbed three days with soot, brick powder, white cumin, burnt ram's fur, molasses and rock salt, each at a sixteenth of its weight and each with kanji, then washed in hot kanji after every rubbing. The stated purpose is the removal of dirt, and the text adds an observable: in the course of being rubbed this way mercury assumes a clearer appearance at every rubbing."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmp:RasaMulagranthaSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der Band die Werke nennt, aus denen er kompiliert"@de,
        "Whether the volume names the works it compiles"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-25" ;
    skos:note "Limits. The Sanskrit verses were not read, and it is conceivable that a verse names its own provenance where the English does not. The four later volumes were not searched; volume V is reported to carry an introduction with a list of authors, and a run that opens it should put this question again."@en,
        "No verse in the volume is attributed to any work. The titles that do occur are all in the preface, where they are named as ancient books assigned to Rāma and Rāvaṇa or as works of which only names survive, and four of the five occurrences of Rasendra are the word as a synonym of mercury. What the body of the volume does instead is print the phrase according to another version some thirty times, and once say that the expert chemists use an ingredient although it is not mentioned in the original text — an acknowledgement that there is an original text, without naming it."@en,
        "This is a search whose empty result is a working instruction and not a lament. Because the volume attributes nothing, no node in traditions/rasashastra.ttl may cite the Rasārṇava or the Rasaratnasamuccaya, and none does; the three classical works stand there as unopened work identifiers. Somebody has done the identification — Meulenbeld's History of Indian Medical Literature sets the borrowings against the chapters of the Rasaratnasamuccaya, the Rasārṇava and the Rasendracūḍāmaṇi — and that is secondary literature and a way to the works, never a source."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of the normalised English for: Rasarnava (0), Rasaratna (0), Rasahrdaya (0), Rasendra Chintamani (1), Rasa Chintamani (1), Rasendra (5), Rama rajiya (1), Arka Prakasha (2), authorities (3), according to another version (inspected throughout, some thirty occurrences), commentators (inspected), original text (1). Every hit read at its place; the section headings of chapters III to VIII inspected for attributions." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-25" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, complete, including the preface, the introduction, every footnote and the glossary" ;
    pm:searchedFor "An attribution, anywhere in the volume, of any printed verse or passage to a named work: the Rasārṇava, the Rasaratnasamuccaya, the Rasahṛdayatantra, the Rasendracūḍāmaṇi, the Rasendracintāmaṇi or any other title. The question decides how every node taken from this volume may be cited, and it had to be settled before a single one was written." .

pmp:RasaMurchana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Ohnmacht des Quecksilbers"@de,
        "The swooning of mercury"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, murchhanam, pp. 98-125" ;
    skos:altLabel "murchhanam"@en ;
    skos:definition "The eleventh operation, given in some twenty-three processes, of which the best is stated to be heating with six times the weight of sulphur. Mercury rubbed with sulphur gives up its subtleness, turns to a black dust and is then said to be in a state of swoon."@en ;
    skos:note "The transmission gives the operation a definition by its effect and not by its procedure, and the definition is a clinical one: mercury is in a state of swoon when it succeeds in curing diseases without producing any after-effect. That makes the absence of an adverse effect the criterion of a preparatory step, which is as close as the volume comes to defining an operation by an observation on a patient."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmp:RasaNisphalatvaSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der Band sagt, was ein Anwender schließen soll, wenn nichts geschieht"@de,
        "Whether the volume says what a user should conclude if nothing happens"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-25" ;
    skos:note "Limits. One volume, the compiler's English, and the search was for a statement about failure and not for an implicit one. Whether the later volumes state a stopping rule was not asked."@en,
        "That is the shape of the whole apparatus around the course. Three hundred pages prescribe, sixty prohibitions hedge, ten signs announce a broken rule, three antidotes repair a mistake, and no sentence anywhere tells the user at what point to stop believing the medicine. The one place where a term and an outcome meet — cure within a week — is therefore the cheapest examination this file makes available, and it is available because the transmission stated a number, not because it invited the question."@en,
        "To the first question the answer is yes and the terms are short: all diseases cured in a week by taruna baddha, the diseases of serious vices in three weeks by kumara baddha, purgation within six hours, sexual intoxication in three weeks, senility destroyed in six months, the ball held a year. To the second the answer is nothing at all. The three occurrences of failure are the compiler's word for a chemist's failure to understand the science, the failure to digest the mercury after a broken rule, and the failure of mercury to cure a disease in a man who has fasted — all three of them failures of the user or the reader, and none a case in which the medicine was given correctly and did not work."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of the normalised English for the first question: in a week (1), in three weeks (2), fortnight (2), within a month (0), for one month (inspected), in six hours (1), for three days (inspected), for six months (1), for one year (1). For the second: if it does not (0), in case it does not (0), fails (0), failure (3, all inspected), unsuccessful (0), does not cure (0), no benefit (1), does not work (0), abortive (1), proves harmful (1), remains uncured (0)." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-25" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV in full, pp. 176-220, with the classes of solidification at ch. VII pp. 311-318" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Two questions, put separately. First, every place at which the volume states a term within which an effect of a preparation should appear. Second, whether it anywhere says what follows if the term passes and the effect does not come: a rule for stopping, a second attempt, a judgement that the medicine was wrong, or any statement at all about a course that did nothing." .

pmp:RasaNiyamana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Bändigung des Quecksilbers"@de,
        "Restraint of mercury"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, niyamanam, pp. 65-69" ;
    skos:altLabel "niyamanam"@en ;
    skos:definition "The seventh operation, and the repair of the sixth. Confinement is said to make mercury gain in strength and thereby subtle; restraint removes the subtleness by rubbing three days with the decoction of gandhanakuli, tamarind bark, bitter karkoti, bhringaraja, lotus and kanaka dhustura, or by boiling in kanji, or by heating in crucibles cut from lumps of rock salt. The stated effect is stability."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmp:RasaPatana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Sublimation des Quecksilbers, aufwärts, abwärts und geneigt"@de,
        "Sublimation of mercury, upward, downward and inclined"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, urdhvapatanam, adhahpatanam and tirjak patanam, pp. 57-63" ;
    skos:altLabel "patanam"@en ;
    skos:definition "The fifth operation, in three directions. Upward: mercury rubbed with copper and lime juice into a lump, heated under a water-filled upper vessel so that the metal collects on its convex side. Downward: the lump painted on the inner surface of an inverted upper vessel with fire above and water below, so that the mercury falls into the water. Inclined: the lump placed in a vessel with a bent tube through which the mercury passes into water."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmp:RasaRanjana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Färbung des Quecksilbers"@de,
        "Dyeing of mercury"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. V, ranjanam and sankramanam, pp. 221-235" ;
    skos:altLabel "ranjanam"@en ;
    skos:definition "The seventeenth operation. Exhausted mercury is put in a long crucible with the red oil and heated over cowdung cakes, twice or three times over; the dyeing is stated to be what prepares it for the eighteenth operation, and wax, honey, tallow, blood, borax, earthworm, incinerated lead and oil are named as the things that impart the power of transformation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmp:RasaRodhana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Einschließen des Quecksilbers"@de,
        "Confinement of mercury"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, rodhanam, pp. 63-65" ;
    skos:altLabel "rodhanam"@en ;
    skos:definition "The sixth operation. Mercury is put into a glass bottle or a coconut shell under a decoction of bala and riddhi or a solution of rock salt and buried three days in a hole a yard deep. The stated purpose is exact and is a repair: mercury acquires impotency by being sublimated, and confinement frees it from that impotency."@en ;
    skos:note "The sixth operation exists to undo a damage the fifth is stated to do. A transmission that builds a repair step into its own series is saying, in the order of the steps, that its earlier step has a cost; that is worth holding beside the places where it says nothing can go wrong."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmp:RasaSancharana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Bewegung des Quecksilbers"@de,
        "The movement of mercury"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, movement of mercury, p. 129" ;
    skos:altLabel "sancharanam"@en ;
    skos:definition "The twelfth operation, and the shortest in the volume. Mercury already able to swallow metals, rubbed in a hot mortar with mica, gold and iron at a triti each in sour juice or old kanji, moves like an earthworm."@en ;
    skos:note "An operation whose whole content is an observable: the material is said to be seen to move. It is one of the few places where the volume states a perceptible outcome and attaches no condition and no excuse to it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmp:RasaSvedana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Kochen des Quecksilbers"@de,
        "Boiling of mercury"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, swedanam, pp. 50-53" ;
    skos:altLabel "svedanam"@en ;
    skos:definition "The second operation. Mercury bound in a four-folded cloth is boiled a day or three days in kanji with the trikatu, the triphala, chitraka roots, rock salt, rye and kanya, in a dola jantram. Four processes are given; the stated purpose is the removal of the remnants of the blemishes that purification left."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmp:RasaUtthapana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Erheben des Quecksilbers"@de,
        "Raising of mercury"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, utthapanam, pp. 56-57" ;
    skos:altLabel "utthapanam"@en ;
    skos:definition "The fourth operation. Mercury is rubbed with a quarter of its weight of powdered turmeric and the juice of kanya and sublimated in a patana jantram; in the second process it is rubbed in the sun with lime juice and dried, and whatever cannot be collected is recovered by sublimation in a damaru or vidyadhara apparatus."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmp:RasaVedhaPariksaSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Verwandlungsbehauptung irgendwo einer öffentlichen Probe unterworfen wird"@de,
        "Whether the transmutation claim is anywhere put to a public test"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-25" ;
    skos:note "Limits. Sixteen processes for making gold and silver were read in chapter VIII and none was carried out. Whether any of them would yield a metal a goldsmith would call gold is an examination this holding has not opened, and the materials — orpiment, realgar, arsenic — make it one that would need a laboratory and not a reading."@en,
        "The one invitation in the volume is to the reader and is about the other claim: let my readers perform faithfully the operations described in the present volume, and then form their own judgment as to the truth of the statement — the statement being that mercury swallows gold without gaining weight. That sentence is the reason pmc:RasaGrasanaParyaptiPariksa is held as the most examinable node in this file. For the transmutation itself no test is offered, no assay is reported and no goldsmith appears."@en,
        "Three places and they pull in different directions. The chapter on the making of gold states the fineness of its products in sixteenths and in carats, which is the language of an assay and is the one measure in the volume a buyer could dispute. The single occurrence of market is the instruction that base metals transformed into gold should be kept buried in the earth for a fortnight before they are taken out for sale, which delays the encounter with a buyer and gives no reason. And the preface has the compiler saying that the transformation was demonstrated to him by his preceptor and that he is confident he could carry it out if he were given a laboratory and the opportunity, which is a claim about a demonstration he witnessed and not one he offers."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of the normalised English for: goldsmith (1), market (1), assay (0), touchstone (0), witness (0), carat (2), parts fine (8), sixteenth (inspected), buried (inspected), sale (1), sell (0), demonstrated (1), form their own judgment (1)." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:RasaHemabhasaVibheda ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-25" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. V pp. 221-249 and ch. VIII pp. 321-350, read continuously, with the preface" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Whether the volume, which claims that prepared mercury turns a hundred to ten million times its weight of base metal into gold, anywhere submits that claim to somebody outside the transmission: an assay, a goldsmith, a sale, a witness, a weighed demonstration, a stated fineness that a buyer could dispute." .

pmp:RasaVrttantaSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der Band einen Fall berichtet, in dem jemand eines seiner Präparate genommen hat"@de,
        "Whether the volume reports a case of anybody who took one of its preparations"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-25" ;
    skos:note "In three hundred and fifty pages of instruction there is not one case. Every hit for case of in the instructional text is the phrase in another sense — in case of fever, a case of swallowing, in case of constipation — and the four occurrences of observed are all the word in the sense of a rule being observed. The two occurrences of observation and the two of experiment are the same sentence of the preface, and it is the sentence that denies both: this is not a collection of experiential truths or inductive generalizations based on observation and experiment."@en,
        "Limits. One volume of five. Volumes II to V treat sulphur, mica, the metals and the gems, and whether a case is reported there was not asked. The Sanskrit verses printed above each English passage were not read and nothing here is a statement about them."@en,
        "The cases are all in the back matter, and the search is entered as having found a place because of them. Some ten signed statements of cure stand after the glossary, with names, offices, addresses and dates. They name the practitioner and not the preparation: only one of them names a medicine at all, none states a dose, and every one is written by the person cured or by a relative. The division is the finding — three hundred pages of procedure with no outcome, and eight leaves of outcome with no procedure."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of the normalised English for: case of (11, every one inspected), instance (1), was cured (3), cured by (5, of which three are the word procured or an indication), patient of mine (0), my patient (0), I saw (0), I have seen (0), observed (4, all inspected), observation (2), experiment (2), experience (1), trial (1, and it is the word industrial), tested (0), no effect (2). All hits read at their places, and in addition the closing sections of chapters III to VIII and the whole of the preface read continuously." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:RasaPramanapatra ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-25" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi or Ocean of Indian Chemistry and Alchemy, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, the compiler's English throughout, preface to closing advertisement, complete" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A report, anywhere in the volume, of a named or unnamed person who took one of these preparations or underwent one of these operations, with a stated outcome: a case, an observation, a trial, a comparison, a failure. The volume prescribes some fifty preparations with doses in ratis and mashas and states cure-times in weeks, so the question is whether anything behind the prescriptions was written down." .

pmp:RasaVyapattiVrttantaSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der Band einen Fall des Schadens berichtet, den sein eigenes Material nach seiner Aussage anrichtet"@de,
        "Whether the volume reports a case of the harm it says its own material does"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-25" ;
    skos:note "Limits. The absence of a reported case in a handbook is weak evidence about a practice and strong evidence about a genre. The compiler's clinical papers, if he wrote any, and the case literature of the college he directed are not this work; the testimonials at the back of this volume report cures and no harm at all, which is what an advertisement does."@en,
        "Sixteen occurrences of death and not one of them an event. Fourteen are the promise — the conqueror of death, freedom from senility and death, a killer of diseases and death — and two are the threat: the action leads to death where the body was not prepared, and hatha bandha gives rise to death or a very serious disease. The three occurrences of died are a physician who died fifty years ago and twice the word remedied. The one death in the volume that happened to anybody is the death of a Kaviraja who had no faith in metallic medicines."@en,
        "This is the exhibited gap, and it is the more striking half of the reading. A transmission that lists eight faults of mercury against eight diseases, in a fixed order and one to one, has made a claim that a single case would sharpen or break; it writes three antidote regimens with terms of three and seven days, which is what a practitioner writes who has seen the condition; and it reports no patient. The knowledge that the material harms is held as doctrine at exactly the same distance from a case as the knowledge that it cures."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of the normalised English for: died (3), death (16, every one inspected), fatal (0), poisoned (0), autopsy (0), was killed (0 in this sense), accident (0), injured (0), suffered from (inspected), and the names of the eight ailments of the blemish list — carbuncle, leprosy, swoon, inflammation, loss of vigour, inertness, eruptions — read at every occurrence." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-25" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, complete, with the toxicological passages at ch. IV pp. 35-38, 134, 196, 206-210 and ch. VII pp. 311-314 read continuously" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A report of an actual injury or death from one of these preparations: somebody who took impure mercury and got one of the eight diseases named for it, somebody who took the mercury solidified without purification that is said to cause death or serious disease, somebody who took mercury without preparing the body and died as the volume says he will. The registration of this transmission named its double examinability, the promised outcome and the toxicity of the means, and the second half turns on whether the stated harms were ever seen or only taught." .

pmp:RefusalGroundExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob eine Zurückweisung des Kalenders auf einem Fall oder auf einem Zustand des Übenden ruht, oder auf keinem von beiden"@de,
        "Whether a refusal of the calendar rests on a case or on a state of the practitioner, or on neither"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that a transmission rejecting the choosing of a day from outside the practice grounds the rejection in a case, and that one rejecting it from inside grounds it in a state of the practitioner. The population is every node in the compiled holding whose wording rejects the choosing of a day, together with the three addresses the claim's own falsifier names as unread. The six nodes already in the holding were classified when the claim was formed; this protocol opened two of the three named addresses at the wording and classified what it found."@en ;
    skos:note "The correction this protocol recommends. The claim should be restated over a narrower population and the third ground should be named beside the other two. First, over transmissions that dispute the art: those that dispute it from outside argue from an occasion, and this survives with the three witnesses it was built on and no exception found. Second, over transmissions that release a practitioner from an art they otherwise teach: they ground the release in a state of the practitioner, and this too survives with its three. Third, and new: a transmission may decline the art without disputing or teaching it, and then it grounds the declining in a rule of its own — of livelihood, or of how to act when already perplexed — and asserts nothing at all about whether the art works. Two witnesses in two spheres, both read on the day of this protocol."@en,
        "The first deviation. The Mahasila runs seven paragraphs of one shape — whereas some recluses and brahmans, while living on food provided by the faithful, earn their living by wrong means of livelihood, by low arts, such as these, and then a list, and then Gotama the recluse holds aloof from such low arts. The choosing of days stands in two of the seven lists, among some two hundred items that include palmistry, snake charming and bringing forth flames from one's mouth. The discourse never says that the days do not fall out as the almanac has them. Its ground is that one who lives on the food of the faithful does not earn by this, which is a rule about the refuser and not a judgement on the art."@en,
        "The second deviation, and it is the sharper of the two because it concedes what it refuses. Asked what to do about the bad omens he had taken in the time of ignorance, the Prophet is reported as answering: if from a bad omen you are thrown into perplexity, let it not hinder you from doing the work you had intended to do. The perplexity is granted. What is refused is that it should govern the act. That is neither an occasion nor an exemption but an instruction, and beside it in the same compiler stands the outright prohibition of diviners with a mechanism attached — one of the jinn steals away the truth and carries it into the diviner's ear — which shows that the transmission argues when it chooses to and declines to here."@en,
        "What this protocol does not establish, stated plainly because the finding is fresh and thin. Two addresses out of three, one of them through a compiler and not at the wording of the source; the third address untouched and the transmission it belongs to absent from this holding. Two witnesses are enough to show that the claim's two grounds are not exhaustive and not nearly enough to say how the third ground distributes. The state is set at cases-with-deviation and not higher because both deviating cases were found by this run's own reading rather than by a search of the assembled holding, and a run that finds its own counter-cases has not yet been checked by anyone."@en,
        "Why both count as deviations rather than as confirmations of the outside half. The claim's outside half says the refusal is grounded in a case: an occasion on which one date carried two outcomes, or one outcome many dates, or the rule was followed exactly and the follower lost what he sought. Neither of these two adduces any occasion. They also do not do what the falsifier expected, which was to ground the refusal in doctrine; a doctrine at least asserts something about the world, and neither of these asserts anything about the world. The right description of both is abstention, and the holding did not have the word."@en ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Two corpus searches were carried, each recorded as its own node. The first read the whole of the Mahasila of the first discourse of the Digha in Rhys Davids's English and asked what ground the discourse gives for holding aloof from the choosing of days; the answer is the recurring clause wrong means of livelihood and nothing else, and the passage became pmc:TheravadaNakkhattaLowArt. The second searched a nineteenth-century compiler's whole text for the prophetic traditions on omens and found the entries FA'L and DIVINATION, which yielded pmc:SunnaTiyaraLaTamnaka; the same search for the tradition against the ill omen of the month of Safar came back empty in every edition reachable, and that failure is recorded as its own node with the outcome corpus-unreachable rather than as an absence in the corpus. Not searched: the Hebrew Bible, which is the third address the falsifier names and which this holding does not carry as a transmission at all. That is the largest thing this protocol did not do."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-21" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A transmission that rejects the choosing of a day from outside the practice and grounds the rejection in doctrine alone, adducing no occasion; or one that rejects it from inside and does adduce an occasion. Neither was met. What was met instead was a third ground the claim has no room for, in two of the two addresses opened: the refuser's own rule of livelihood, and a rule of conduct for one already perplexed by an omen. The claim is not refuted by these, because it states what a refusal rests on and these do not rest on either of its two grounds; it is shown to be a statement about a narrower class than the one it appears to describe."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-21" ;
    pm:tests pmc:TheRefusalAndWhatItRestsOn .

pmp:RomanusApprobatumSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Wirksamkeitsetiketten des Büchleins auf den Segen oder auf das Mittel fallen"@de,
        "Whether the booklet's efficacy labels fall on the charms or on the remedies"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-22" ;
    skos:note "No division. The labels fall on both kinds without a pattern: a certain fire-blessing that helps at all times and a right approved shot-stelling are spoken charms, the approved plaster for the broken leg is a preparation, and the bundle buried under the threshold, which is a preparation, is called quite certain and truthful by God's power. The one instance that suggested a rule is the only one of its kind."@en,
        "The empty result is worth carrying because it removes a reading that was available and attractive. It would have been a tidy finding that this transmission labels as tried whatever it could actually try, and it is not so: the labels are distributed over what can be watched and what cannot alike, and the marks of certainty are heaviest exactly where the claim reaches furthest, on the bundle warranted for as long as the house stands."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search of the whole text for: approbirt, probirt, Probe, bewährt, es hilft, hilft gewiß, ganz gewiß, gewisser, gewisse, allezeit hilft, wahrhaftig, richtige, rechte. Every hit read in its place and assigned to the kind of item it stands on."@en ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-22" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Romanus-Büchlein in full, all thirty-five pages, in the reprint in Johann Scheible (ed.), Das Kloster, weltlich und geistlich, vol. III (Stuttgart 1846), pp. 489-523."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "The distribution of the booklet's own marks of tried efficacy — approved, certain, helps certainly — across the two kinds of thing it prints: the spoken blessings and stellings on one side, the prepared plasters, bundles and draughts on the other. The question was put because at the broken leg the label approved stands on the plaster and the blessing beside it carries the deadline, and a regular division would say something about what the transmission thought was at stake in each half."@en .

pmp:RomanusEpistulaSanguinis a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der auf die Wunde gelegte Brief"@de,
        "The letter laid on the wound to staunch blood"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Romanus-Büchlein, in Johann Scheible (ed.), Das Kloster, weltlich und geistlich, vol. III (Stuttgart 1846), pp. 489-523, here p. 503" ;
    skos:definition "The third of the booklet's blood-staunchings, and the one it calls entirely certain. Where the blood will not stand, or where a vein is opened, the letter carrying the prescribed line of letters is laid upon the place, and the blood stands from that hour. Whoever carries it about him, the booklet adds, can stand before all his enemies; and a woman in labour, or in any distress of heart, is to take the letter to her."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as its own procedure because the assay below is attached to it and to nothing else in the booklet. The words written are a line of letters and abbreviations which the optical transcription of the copy used does not render reliably, and none of them is reproduced here."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RomanusBuechlein ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmp:RomanusFeuernothBuendel a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Bündel unter der Hausschwelle gegen Feuersnoth"@de,
        "The bundle buried under the threshold against fire"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Romanus-Büchlein, in Johann Scheible (ed.), Das Kloster, weltlich und geistlich, vol. III (Stuttgart 1846), pp. 489-523, here p. 492-493" ;
    skos:definition "A black hen is taken from the nest morning or evening and its throat cut, the stomach taken out and left as it is; a piece of cloth the breadth of a plate is got from the shift of a girl who is still a maid, from the part most stained; and an egg laid on Maundy Thursday. The three pieces are wrapped together with wax, put in a small pot, covered and buried under the house threshold; and so long as a stake of the house stands, fire before or behind the dwelling can do the operator and his children no harm. For a fire already broken out, a whole shift of a maid, or a sheet a woman has borne a child in, is thrown wrapped together into the fire in silence."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered for the shape of its claim rather than its contents. The buried bundle is warranted for as long as the house stands, which is a term no run can close; the thing thrown in silence is warranted for the moment, and the booklet says of both that they help quite certainly, of the first adding that it is by God's power also quite certain and truthful. Two quite different exposures, one label."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RomanusBuechlein ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmp:RomanusOperationAssaySearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Büchlein eine Probe einer eigenen Verrichtung nennt"@de,
        "Whether the booklet states a test of one of its own operations"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-22" ;
    skos:note "One case, at p. 503, and it opposes the statement. After the blood-staunching it calls entirely certain, the booklet turns to the reader who will not believe it and tells him to write the letters on a knife, stab an unreasoning animal, and see that it does not bleed. Nothing is said about an animal that bleeds. That is an assay of the operation with no escape attached, which is what the generalization declared impossible."@en,
        "Two passages were read as candidates and set aside. The shepherd's trial on the nobleman at pp. 511-512 is a report that somebody performed a variant and vouches for the apparatus rather than for an outcome. The ninth-day term for a beast's broken leg at pp. 518-519 is a deadline on an effect and not an act performed in order to establish something; it is entered separately at pmc:RomanusNonusDies because a deadline that falls due is scarce in this holding for its own reasons."@en,
        "What the found case does not settle. It removes a universal and it does not measure a proportion: this is one booklet and one clause. It also has a property worth carrying forward — the demonstration is addressed to a doubter and not to the practitioner, and the only comparable clause this holding has, the assay of the white stone in the demotic handbook, is likewise a way of settling something rather than a step of an operation. Whether every assay of an operation in these corpora turns out to be aimed at a sceptic is the question the protocol now holds."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchTerms "The whole of pp. 489-523 read in order, and a term search of the same text for: Probe, probirt, approbirt, versucht, bewährt, gewiß, ganz gewiß, hilft gewiß, glauben, nicht glauben, wer es nicht glauben will, so wirst du sehen, so vergehet es, bis auf den, Tag. Every hit read in its place."@en ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:RomanusProbaAdIncredulum ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-22" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Romanus-Büchlein in full, all thirty-five pages, in the reprint in Johann Scheible (ed.), Das Kloster, weltlich und geistlich, vol. III (Stuttgart 1846), pp. 489-523."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "An act with a definite perceptible outcome by which one of the booklet's own operations is established to have worked or not worked, together with whatever clause stands beside the refuting outcome. The question comes from pmc:TheTestOnTheIngredient, which states that handbooks assay their materials and not their operations, and which names this shape as the thing that would refute it."@en .

pmp:RosicrucianDignitasSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Korpus irgendein Merkmal nennt, an dem Würdigkeit vor der Probe feststellbar wäre"@de,
        "Whether the corpus gives any mark by which worthiness could be established before the trial"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-30" ;
    skos:note "Limits. Three works of four named in the corpus, two of them in one seventeenth-century English translation. The Vaughan print is a translation whose own reviser calls it prolix and careless, so a criterion stated loosely in the German could in principle be lost in it; against that, the German of 1615 was consulted at the six articles and the Latin of the cedars tablet is printed in the German 1616 edition and was read there.",
        "Nothing, and the corpus is unusually explicit that there is nothing. The Confessio says outright that the worthiness of those who shall be accepted is not esteemed and known by man's carefulness but by the rule of their revelation, which is a statement that no mark is to be had; the invitation names the criterion of the weighing two days before the weighing and does not say what it measures; and the one weight of seven that is identified is identified in a parenthesis about the narrator's own modesty. The nearest thing to a mark in the whole corpus is the Bible: he that taketh all his pleasures therein shall know that he prepared for himself an excellent way to come in to the Fraternity. That is a preparation and not a criterion, and it is entered nowhere as one.",
        "Why the gap is worth exhibiting rather than noting. Four claims of this corpus have the same shape and the same defect, and a reader who has the shape can see at once which of the corpus's promises could fail and which could not. The mark, if it is anywhere, is in the Latin Confessio of 1615, in the Allgemeine Reformation, or in Andreae's own later writings, none of which was opened; and whoever opens them closes this in an afternoon." ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of the three works for: worthy, worthiness, unworthy (fifty-one hits across the three, every one of them either an assertion that somebody is or is not worthy or the requirement that a successor be worthy, none a criterion); merit, deserve, deserved, demerit; fit, fitter, unfit; prepared, prepare; qualified; too light, lighter, heavier, weigh, weighed, weight; destinated, destined; pure-hearted, false-hearted; eyes of an eagle; token, sign, mark, character. Read continuously and not only searched: the verse of the invitation, the Latin tablet of the cedars, the virgin's address on the eve of the weighing, the whole of the weighing and the sentence, the Confessio's passage on offering the treasures freely, and the conclusions of both manifestos." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-30" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Fama Fraternitatis and Confessio Fraternitatis, complete, in the English of Thomas Vaughan, The Fame and Confession of the Fraternity of R: C: (London, 1652); Chymische Hochzeit Christiani Rosencreutz, complete, in a modern reset English checked against the Strasbourg print of 1616" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage stating, in advance of an outcome, what makes an applicant worthy or unworthy, light or heavy, destined to be plagued or not. The corpus turns on the distinction at four places — the invitation's him who is too light, the seven weights, the medicine withheld from the destined, and the unworthy who cry a thousand times and are not heard — and every one of them assigns a person to a class without saying how the class is recognised. If a mark exists anywhere, all four become testable at once." .

pmp:RosicrucianOperandiModusSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Korpus für irgendeines der sechs verheissenen Vermögen eine Praxis nennt"@de,
        "Whether the corpus names a practice for any of the six promised capacities"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-30" ;
    skos:note "Limits. The Book M, the Rota Mundi, the Protheus, the book H, the vocabulary of Theophrastus and the parchment book I are named in the Fama as the works in which the Fraternity's content stands, and not one of them exists. So the absence found here may be an absence in the manifestos rather than in the transmission they announce — which is itself the finding, because a transmission whose method is in books nobody has is one no reader can work.",
        "No practice for any of the six. The corpus contains exactly one procedure, the work of the seven storeys of the tower on the sixth day of the Chymische Hochzeit, and what that procedure produces is not one of the six: it produces two living bodies, and the office conferred at the end of that narrative is authority over ignorance, poverty and sickness, which is the first of the six turned into a rank rather than obtained by a method. Everything else the corpus says about how its capacities are come by is that the founder learned them from the wise of Damascus and of Fez, that they stand in a Book M he translated, and that they are given by God to whom he pleases.",
        "This is the gap that decides how much of this corpus this holding can ever test, and it is the reason there is one pm:Yielding node in the tradition file and not seven. A capacity with no practice cannot carry a yielding claim: there is nothing for pm:byPractice to point at, and a node that supplied one would be reporting the compiler's guess at a method as the tradition's." ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of the three works for: pray, prayer, prayers, meditate, meditation (numerous, and every hit is either the narrator praying in the narrative or the injunction to read the Bible); fast, fasting; breath, breathe; posture, kneel; recite, recitation, invoke, invocation, conjure; number, seven times, thrice; hour, midnight, dawn; abstain, diet; instrument (all hits are the founder's mathematical instruments or musical ones); operation, process, processes (all hits in the Chymische Hochzeit and all of them the work of the tower). Read continuously: the whole of the Confessio, which is where the six stand; the Fama's account of the founder's journeys and of the first four brethren; and the sixth and seventh days of the Chymische Hochzeit." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-30" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Fama Fraternitatis and Confessio Fraternitatis, complete, in the English of Thomas Vaughan (London, 1652); Chymische Hochzeit Christiani Rosencreutz, complete, in a modern reset English checked against the Strasbourg print of 1616" ;
    pm:searchedFor "For each of the six capacities the Confessio promises — freedom from fear of hunger, poverty, sickness and age; living as from the beginning of the world to its end; nothing hidden by those beyond the Ganges or in Peru; one book read and all books understood; singing that draws pearls and moves princes; foreknowing the obscurations of the Church — a passage naming what is done to obtain it: a posture, a recitation, a breath, an interval, a diet, a number, an instrument, a place." .

pmp:RosicrucianPonderaSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die sieben Gewichte der Waage irgendwo benannt werden"@de,
        "Whether the seven weights of the balance are named anywhere"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-30" ;
    skos:note "One of the seven is named and six are not, and the one is named incidentally. At the banquet of the third day the narrator declines to say what his place in the order was, if perhaps it might not be interpreted as pride in him, which is expressly against the fourth weight. That is the whole identification the work makes. The search is entered as a find rather than as a gap because the find changes what the trial is: a scale with one named division is a scale, and a reader now knows that the divisions are virtues severally and that pride is the fourth of them.",
        "What the search also settled, and it is the reason to record the terms. The five articles sworn on the seventh day are the nearest candidate for the missing six, and the fourth of them — that the knights desire not to employ this honour to worldly pride and high authority — is about pride, as the fourth weight is. The work never says the articles are the weights, there are five of the one and seven of the other, and this holding does not assert the identification. It is the cheapest next question at this corpus and it needs the critical edition's commentary and not another reading of the print." ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of the reset English for: weight, weights (forty-one hits, read one by one); scale, scales, balance; first weight, second weight, third weight, fourth weight, fifth weight, sixth weight, seventh weight; virtue, virtues; pride, proud. The 1616 German was normalised — long s to s, combining e to following e, double hyphen dropped — and searched for: Gewicht (nineteen hits, read one by one), Wag, Wage, zu leicht (three), Pondera, Libra. The passages of the weighing, of the sentence, of the banquet and of the five articles of the seventh day were read continuously in both." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:RosicrucianSeptemPondera ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-30" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Chymische Hochzeit Christiani Rosencreutz, complete, in a modern reset English and in the Strasbourg print of 1616" ;
    pm:searchedFor "An identification of the seven weights against which the guests are measured on the third day. The sentence of the same day grades its punishments by which weights each guest failed — first, third and fourth; second and fifth; sixth and seventh — so the numbering is operative, and if the seven are named the trial has a stated criterion and the whole corpus changes character." .

pmp:RosicrucianTerminusSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Korpus eine Frist nennt, nach deren Ablauf die verheissene Reformation ausgeblieben wäre"@de,
        "Whether the corpus names any term after which its promised reformation would have failed to come"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-30" ;
    skos:note "Every figure in the corpus is retrospective and every prospective statement is undated. The hundred and twenty years of the vault is a term that had elapsed before the work was printed, and it is stated as fulfilled; the hundred years of secrecy is a term the Fraternity set itself and reports as expired. On the other side: the Lord's Sabbath is almost at hand, a general reformation is to come, the door to Europe already doth begin to appear, the time shall come, ere long, in short time, presently thereupon shall ensue. Not one of them carries a year, and the eclipse analogy that would require one is drawn two pages from the end of the Confessio and left there.",
        "The asymmetry is the finding and it is exact: the corpus dates what it says has happened and does not date what it says will. A reader who wants the falsifying case has to supply the term himself, and the Confessio has already explained in advance why he will not get one, because the manifestation waits on the especial pleasure of God." ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of both manifestos for: year, years (thirty-one hits: the founder's ages and journeys, the hundred years of secrecy, the hundred and twenty of the vault, the hundred years within which the world is said to be much amended, and the year 1378); 120, hundred and twenty, hundred years; how long (one hit, the eclipse passage itself); last, shall last; before her end, before the end; time, times, in short time, ere long, shortly, presently, at hand, already, begin to appear; sixth candelabrum, Miranda sextae aetatis, Lord's Sabbath. Both works were also read continuously with the question in hand." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-30" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Fama Fraternitatis and Confessio Fraternitatis, complete, in the English of Thomas Vaughan (London, 1652)" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A date, a span of years or an event by which the general reformation of divine and human things, the opening of the door to Europe, or the obscurations of the Church and how long they shall last, could be judged not to have occurred. The Confessio itself draws the eclipse analogy, which commits it to a date and a duration, so this is the corpus's own standard and not one imposed on it." .

pmp:SafarOmenSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Überlieferung gegen das Vorzeichen des Safar in einer hier verwendbaren Ausgabe erreichbar ist"@de,
        "Whether the tradition against the ill omen of Safar is reachable in an edition this holding may use"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-21" ;
    skos:note "The outcome is about the access path and not about the transmission. The tradition is well known to stand in the canonical collections; what could not be reached is a rendering of it that this holding may reproduce. The standard English versions are in copyright, the early nineteenth-century translation of the Mishkat did not resolve in the archive, and the 1885 compiler who does print many traditions on omens does not print this one under any of the words searched."@en,
        "The outcome is set at corpus-unreachable and it is weaker than the term sounds, because the first stage of this search was thin and the run says so rather than letting the state stand unqualified. No survey was made of which English renderings of the canonical collections are out of copyright at all; three identifiers were tried in the open scan archive on the strength of names already known, and when they failed the run went to a compiler it happened to remember. A proper landscape stage would ask the question the other way round — which renderings may be reproduced, and which of them index the traditions on omens — and it was not asked. Anyone reopening this should treat the state as not reached rather than as not reachable."@en,
        "This is an exhibited gap of the access kind and it should be closed by anyone who can reach a quotable text. Until then no node in this holding may be cited as carrying the wording of that tradition, and none does."@en,
        "What the same search did find, and it is why the run did not come away empty. The dictionary's entries on omens and on divination print three sayings and one exchange on exactly the question at issue, and the exchange became pmc:SunnaTiyaraLaTamnaka. A refusal of the omen was therefore recovered from this sphere; it is simply not the one the falsifier named."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:corpusUnreachable ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of the dictionary for: Safar (12 hits, every one inspected, all of them about the month as a calendar unit or about the feast of its last Wednesday), evil omen, no infection, contagion, bird of ill, omens, augury, auguries, lucky and unlucky. In the scan archive: Mishkat-ul-Masabih English translation Matthews; Sahih Bukhari English translation; A Manual of Hadith. The two scans that resolved carried no usable text." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-21" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Thomas Patrick Hughes, A Dictionary of Islam, London: W. H. Allen 1885, whole text, some four and a half million characters; plus a search of the open scan archive for a public-domain English rendering of the canonical collections or of the Mishkat al-Masabih" ;
    pm:searchedFor "The tradition in which the Prophet denies contagion, the bird of ill omen, the owl and the ill omen of the month of Safar, in any English version that a CC0 holding may quote. The claim's falsifier names this tradition as one of three places where pmc:TheRefusalAndWhatItRestsOn is likeliest to break, so its wording matters and a paraphrase from memory would not do." .

pmp:Sagdid a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Sagdīd, der Blick des Hundes"@de,
        "Sagdid, the gaze of the dog"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Vendidad, fargard 8.16-18, trans. James Darmesteter, Sacred Books of the East 4, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon, 1895)" ;
    skos:altLabel "sag-did"@en,
        "sagdid"@en ;
    skos:definition "The driving out of the corpse-demon by having a dog look at the body. The dog is specified: a yellow dog with four eyes, or a white dog with yellow ears. The way is to be traversed three times; if the dog will not go, six times; and if it still will not, nine times. The transmission states what follows: the demon flies away to the regions of the north in the shape of a raging fly, with knees and tail sticking out."@en ;
    skos:note "A rare case of a procedure whose success is defined by an event nobody can observe and whose failure is defined by one that anybody can: the demon's flight is unobservable, but the dog's refusal to walk the way is not, and the refusal is what multiplies the requirement from three to six to nine. The transmission has built its escalation on the one part of the operation that is public."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Zoroastrian .

pmp:SecondSingerOutsideTheNorthSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob eine Überlieferung außerhalb des Nordens eine Sitzung an einen Zweiten bindet"@de,
        "Whether a transmission outside the north binds a séance to a second person"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-13" ;
    skos:note "Not searched, and they are the obvious next places: the Koryak volume of the same expedition, which the same recorder's colleague wrote and which reports a neighbouring practice; the Yukaghir material, where the secondary literature reports the practitioner's wife as his supporting staff; and the Iglulik records, where the drum is named as that by which the spirits are called up. All three were reached in the landscape stage and none was opened."@en,
        "The difference between the two cases is in the finding and is kept in the node. Greenland states an outcome: the spirits withheld obedience and the things now plain were hidden before. Bogoras states an incapacity: the practitioner considers himself unable to perform properly. The second is the weaker of the two and the generalization is worded so that it carries both."@en,
        "The find was entered rather than left as an address, which meant registering and opening a transmission this holding did not have. Three pages of one work stand in traditions/chukchee.ttl and the coverage state says work opened and nothing more."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search of the whole volume, with the text normalised to letters because the digitisation damages the transcription of Chukchee words, for: ocitkolin, ecitkolin, citkolin, answering call, assistant, respond, shamanistic performance, unable to perform, encouraging the zeal. The Chukchee term itself was not findable in any spelling and the passage was reached through the English gloss." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:ChukcheePresupposesAnsweringCalls ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-13" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Waldemar Bogoras, The Chukchee, Memoir of the American Museum of Natural History vol. VII (Leiden: E. J. Brill, 1904-1909), searched by term over the whole volume and the section on the shamanistic performance, pp. 433-435, read at the wording" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A transmission outside the North Atlantic sphere in which a proceeding of this kind is stated to depend on a second, competent participant, as Eiríks saga rauða states of the song called Varðlokkur — the outside-the-sphere search SPEC §15 requires before pmc:NorseSeidrPresupposesVardlokkur may be generalized from." .

pmp:SeparativeEndStateExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der absondernde Endzustand überhaupt geprüft wurde"@de,
        "Whether the separative end state has been examined at all"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that a self brought forth and made to endure is the attainment of the path, held here to record that no procedure for failing it has been devised."@en ;
    skos:note "No pm:falsifiedBy is given, and the omission is the permitted one: the state says outright that no procedure has been devised. Everywhere else its absence would let a claim drift from unexamined to examined-and-unsupported."@en,
        "The asymmetry is worth naming because it is easy to mistake for evidence. States of dissolution have measurable correlates and a literature; the end state claimed on the other side has neither instrument nor operationalisation. That is a fact about the instruments. Whoever infers from it that one path is better attested than the other has read the reach of the measuring apparatus as a finding about the paths. See prima-materia#73."@en,
        "This node exists to prevent a confusion, not to report a result. Nobody has stated a condition under which the claim would count as refuted, and that is a statement about the means of examination and not about the claim. A record that left the protocol out entirely would say the same thing by silence; a record that reported the claim as unsupported would say something else and something false."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:noProcedureDevised ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-08-29" ;
    pm:tests pmc:XeperAsSelfGeneration .

pmp:ShangqingAnshanyuan a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Drücken der Bergquelle"@de,
        "Pressing the mountain source"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 10.8b-10.9a (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 10" ;
    skos:definition "The tongue turned back, the fluid swallowed three times, then the second and third fingers of the left hand set in the hollow beneath the nose, inside the septum, and pressed thirty-six times without lifting or losing the count, followed by seven grindings of the teeth, the palm laid over the nose and an incantation. Prescribed on entering a dangerous road or a shrine and wherever the practitioner has a misgiving."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmp:ShangqingBambooExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob Bambus nördlich der inneren Kammer Nachkommen bringt"@de,
        "Whether bamboo north of the inner chamber brings offspring"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the instruction that bamboo be planted outside the north wall of the inner chamber and the finer stems walked among, whereupon heaven is moved to grant offspring, the pregnancy is preserved and the child is long-lived; which the passage calls a secret rule and of which it says that those who carry it out much verify it."@en ;
    skos:note "No case has been collected and none has been searched for. The claim is entered with a protocol rather than without one because of its form and not its plausibility: it is the only instruction in this work whose antecedent and consequent are both facts about a household, so that neither has to be taken on the practitioner's word."@en,
        "One asymmetry should be recorded before any case is collected. A household that plants bamboo after years of childlessness is a household selected for a long prior interval, and regression alone would raise its subsequent rate. That is why the falsifying condition names the matching and not the bare rate."@en,
        "The passage's own claim of verification is not evidence and is not treated as any. It says that those who carry it out much verify it, names nobody, counts nothing, and stands in a reply to a patron who wanted heirs. A transmission that claims verification in that form is reporting its own confidence."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, working from the passage recorded at pmc:ShangqingZhongzhu" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A set of households that carried out the planting and the walking after a period without conception, whose subsequent rate of live births does not exceed that of households matched on the duration of the prior period and on the age of the woman. The claim names an arrangement of a dwelling and an outcome of a household, both of which are observable without the participants' report, and it attaches no condition to either party." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-11" ;
    pm:tests pmc:ShangqingZhongzhu .

pmp:ShangqingFuwu a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Einnehmen des Nebels"@de,
        "Ingesting the mist"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 10.1b-10.2a (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 10" ;
    skos:definition "Performed at dawn in the sleeping chamber: the eyes closed and the five depots looked at inwardly, the breath expelled twenty-four times, the five colours of vapour seen gathering on the face and taken in through the mouth fifty times, sixty swallowings, an incantation, then seven grindings of the teeth and seven swallowings. Given as transmitted by the jade consort of the eastern sea to a woman of the Han."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmp:ShangqingNoonShadowExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob achtzehn Jahre der Sonne im Herzen den Schatten nehmen"@de,
        "Whether eighteen years of the sun in the heart take away the shadow"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that the sun held in the heart three times daily brings, at one year the end of illness, at five years a lustre on the body, and at eighteen years the necessary obtaining of the Way, walking in the midst of the sun without casting a shadow. Only the third term is examinable, and it is examinable in the strongest sense available to this holding: the outcome is a public fact about a body in daylight and the claim states the moment at which it is due."@en ;
    skos:note "No case has been collected, and the reason is the term. Eighteen years of a daily practice is a cohort that would have to be identified at its beginning, and the transmission that would identify it has no living body of practitioners performing this particular actualisation to the stated schedule, so far as this holding knows. The obstacle is the recruitment and not the measurement, which is the rarer and the better position of the two."@en,
        "The first two terms are deliberately not tested. Illness gone in a year and a lustre on the body in five have no stated measure and a high base rate, and a protocol that tested them would be choosing its own instrument after the fact. They are recorded in the claim node and left there."@en,
        "What would not count against the claim is worth stating, because this corpus supplies such clauses generously elsewhere and does not supply one here. Nothing in the passage says that a practitioner who still casts a shadow was impure, was under trial, broke a prohibition or missed a day. That absence is the whole reason this claim is examinable and the neighbouring ones are not."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, working from the passage recorded at pmc:ShangqingRizaixin" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "One person who has performed the stated actualisation three times daily for eighteen years and who casts a shadow when standing in direct sunlight. The passage states the outcome with a word of necessity and attaches no condition to the practitioner, so a single such case refutes the third term as stated." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-11" ;
    pm:tests pmc:ShangqingRizaixin .

pmp:ShangqingShijieExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Regel, einen Leichnam zu lesen, irgendeinen Ausgang ausschließt"@de,
        "Whether the rule for reading a corpse excludes any outcome"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the rule by which a death is read as release through the corpse. The protocol finds that the rule admits each state of a corpse and its opposite, so that no observation of a dead practitioner is left over which the rule would have to call an ordinary death."@en ;
    skos:note "The distinction the rule does draw is recorded so that the state is not read as stronger than it is. The corpus ranks the manner of going, placing daylight departure above midnight and dawn or dusk lowest, and says that release through the corpse does not command the flowered canopy or the flying dragon. It discriminates between grades of success. What it does not do is leave any outcome outside the set of successes."@en,
        "The sharpest single case is not part of the rule and is recorded at pmc:ShangqingDanshi: two men took the elixir and died, two who saw it refused, and the refusal is scored as the failure. The rule above is the doctrine of which that narrative is the application."@en,
        "The state is set on the reading of the rule and not on a case series, and it is set without evidence from any modern work, so no counter-search over the literature is owed. What was owed and was carried is the search inside the transmission for a passage that would count a practitioner's death against the operation; it found the opposite and is recorded as a search node."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, working from the passages recorded at pmc:ShangqingShijieZhengbian and from the counter-search at pmp:ZhengaoShijieCountsSearch" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A state of a dead practitioner's body which the rule as stated would classify as an ordinary death and not as release through the corpse. The protocol has not found one, and the finding that it has not is what the state below records." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-11" ;
    pm:tests pmc:ShangqingShijieZhengbian ;
    pm:undividedOutcomes "A body that looks like a living man, and a body whose form and bones are lost entirely. Feet that are not blue and skin that is not wrinkled, and hair that has fallen out completely. Eyes whose light is undamaged, and a corpse that has putrefied. A body found whole, a body found with head and feet in different places, a body that crawls with worms, a body that stinks at a distance, a body that is not found at all, and clothes left behind with no body in them. The preceding passage supplies named adepts for each of the disfiguring cases and counts every one of them as an attainment." .

pmp:ShintoDivinationMethodSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Chronik ihre eigene Große Wahrsagung beschreibt"@de,
        "Whether the chronicle describes its own Greater Divination"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-18" ;
    skos:note "The chronicle names the procedure and never describes it. Everything this holding knows about how it was done comes from two footnotes by the translator of 1896, who also states there that the tortoise-shell named in the chronicle is the Chinese practice and the deer's shoulder-blade the Japanese one. That is a scholar's distinction imposed on a text that draws none, and pmp:ShintoFutomani says so at the node."@en,
        "The gap is worth exhibiting because of what hangs on it. A divination whose method is unknown cannot be placed on either side of the division pmc:TheCriterionNoPartyControls draws — nobody can say whether the reader of the cracks had an interest in the outcome, or whether anyone but him could see them. Whoever opens the Kojiki or the Kogoshūi can close this in an afternoon."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of volume I for: Greater Divination (three hits, all of them namings and none a description); futomani, futo-mani (none — the translation gives the term only in English); shoulder-blade (one hit, and it is in an editor's footnote); crack (three, of which two are in editor's footnotes and one is a metaphor about a carriage); tortoise (seven, of which one is the Sacred Tortoise proposed by Sujin, two are editor's footnotes, and the rest are animals); deer (forty, all of them animals, hunts and place-names); scapula, seared (none)." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-18" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Nihongi, Books I to XVI, in the English of W. G. Aston, Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697, vol. I, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage in which the chronicle says, in its own voice, what the Greater Divination consisted in: what was burnt, what was read off it, who read it, and what a reading looked like. The procedure is named four times and used to settle the cause of a failed rite, a time, a plot of ground and an appointment, so the method decides how much any of those findings is worth." .

pmp:ShintoFutomani a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Große Wahrsagung"@de,
        "The Greater Divination"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Nihongi, Books I and II, the Age of the Gods, in the English of W. G. Aston, vol. I, London 1896, pp. 15, 83 and 86" ;
    skos:definition "The procedure the chronicle names when a question is put to the gods: futomani, the Greater Divination. It is used to find the cause of a failed rite, to fix a time for a second attempt, to choose the plot of ground in which the rice of the first-fruits festival is grown, and, at the sending down of the heavenly grandchild, as the service a named house is appointed to perform."@en ;
    skos:note "The work names the procedure and does not describe it. What it consisted in — the roasting of a deer's shoulder-blade and the reading of the cracks — is supplied by the editor of the translation read here, who also states that the tortoise-shell used elsewhere in the same chronicle is the Chinese practice and not the Japanese one. The method is therefore entered as his and not as the chronicle's, and a run that wants it from inside the transmission has to go to another work."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Nihonshoki ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shinto .

pmp:ShintoOrdealCountSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Chronik für die Probe des siedenden Wassers irgendeine Zahl nennt"@de,
        "Whether the chronicle gives any number for the ordeal of boiling water"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-18" ;
    skos:note "Limits. One volume of two and one translation. Volume II carries Books XVII to XXX, and the later reigns were not searched; a further ordeal with figures in them would change the finding and nothing here makes that unlikely."@en,
        "No number anywhere. The chronicle says that everybody was told to go, that the true were uninjured and the false harmed, and that those who had falsified their titles slipped away beforehand and did not come forward. It counts nobody in either group, so the report of a perfect result rests on no enumeration, and the same passage supplies the reason there may have been nothing to enumerate. See pmc:ShintoKukatachiExitus."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of volume I for: Kuka-tachi (one hit, the naming sentence); caldron (three, all in the one passage); boiling water (six, of which four are in this passage and two in the earlier two-party ordeal of Ōjin); boiling mud (one); red-hot (one); uninjured (two); harmed (two); scalded, persons underwent (none). The whole passage and the two pages around it were read continuously, and so was the Ōjin ordeal." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-18" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Nihongi, Books I to XVI, in the English of W. G. Aston, Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697, vol. I, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896" ;
    pm:searchedFor "At the ordeal of the fourth year of Ingyō, which reports that the true were uninjured and the false harmed: how many came to the cauldrons, how many were harmed, how many stayed away. Any figure at all would make the report a count rather than a verdict, and the passage is the only place in this holding where a transmission states a criterion on both sides and then says what happened." .

pmp:ShintoUkehi a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Eid, der eine Frage durch das entscheidet, was unter ihm hervorgebracht wird"@de,
        "The oath that settles a question by what is produced under it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Nihongi, Book I, the Age of the Gods, in the English of W. G. Aston, vol. I, London 1896, pp. 35-36" ;
    skos:definition "The procedure by which the sun goddess and her younger brother settle whether his heart is pure. The two stand opposite one another, agree on what the outcome is to mean, and then each takes an object, rinses it in the true-well of heaven, chews or bites it and blows it away, whereupon deities are produced from the mist of the breath. The sexes of the deities produced are the outcome read."@en ;
    skos:note "Its shape is what makes it valuable to this holding. The criterion is fixed by agreement before anything is done; the event that decides it is the sex of children, which neither party is in a position to determine; and the transmission states what each of the two possible outcomes would mean. That combination is the first column of pmc:TheCriterionNoPartyControls, and this is the first witness for it from the Japanese material."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Nihonshoki ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shinto .

pmp:ShintoUkehiVerdictSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Chronik den Eid irgendwo zu einem Urteil auflöst"@de,
        "Whether the chronicle anywhere settles the oath into one verdict"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-18" ;
    skos:note "That is the productive outcome the class was built for. A run that had only checked whether the criterion names both sides would have entered a clean witness for the first clause of pmc:TheCriterionNoPartyControls and moved on; what the reading found is a third way for a two-sided criterion to decide nothing, and it is recorded at the node it became."@en,
        "The search was for a verdict and found four incompatible ways of reaching one. The physical outcome is the same in every version — three female deities and five or six male ones — and the verdict varies with who is said to have produced which, which is a rule none of the four states in advance. Two versions declare the brother vindicated, two draw no verdict at all, and the main narrative reassigns the children by the ownership of the material after they are born."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchTerms "The whole of Books I and II was read continuously rather than searched, because the variants are printed as running text and a term search would have returned the same words from four passages without telling them apart. Searched in addition over the whole volume for: oath (nine hits), covenant, pledge, I have won, proof of his victory, red heart, black heart, impure heart." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:ShintoUkehiVariaeLectiones ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-18" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Nihongi, Books I and II, the Age of the Gods with all their variant versions, in the English of W. G. Aston, Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697, vol. I, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage deciding, for the oath between the sun goddess and her brother, which of the two produced the male children and therefore whether the criterion was met — the rule of attribution that the criterion presupposes and does not state." .

pmp:SiguiMaterialRecord a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Bewahrung des Sigui in Holz und Faser"@de,
        "Keeping the Sigui in wood and fibre"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 277-279" ;
    skos:definition "The keeping of the great mask carved for each Sigui in a rock shelter beside those of the preceding ceremonies, and of the baobab-fibre ferment-holders woven for each Sigui in the hogon's house. Both accumulate as series, and the article treats the series as the firmer evidence that a ceremony took place at all."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the one part of the account that leaves an object a later worker could count and date independently of any informant. The article reports nine masts at Ibi in 1931 with traces of three more, and eight or fourteen ferment-holders at Sanga and Arou. Held as a practice here and as a protocol at pmc:SiguiSeriesExamination in examinations/dogon-sirius.ttl."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Dogon .

pmp:SiguiReckoning a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Auszählen des Sigui-Abstands"@de,
        "Reckoning the interval between two Sigui"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 275-277" ;
    skos:definition "The counting of the sixty-year interval by means that are stated to be mnemonic rather than astronomical: thirty biennial millet-beer drinkings, each marked with a cowry laid down by the eldest; a millet-porridge figure above the sanctuary door in which a point is added each year; and a painted line of chevrons called sigi lugu, count of the Sigui, each tooth standing for twenty years."@en ;
    skos:note "The recorders report that the rule is theoretical and that the practice departed from it: between the Sigui of the beginning of the century and 1931 only one drinking was held, and the cowries for the missing thirty years were laid down together in a single heap afterwards. The reckoning that is described as regular is thus reconstructed in arrears, and the article says so."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Dogon .

pmp:Sitkari a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Sītkārī"@de,
        "Sītkārī"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.54-56 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "A retention in which the breath is drawn in through the mouth with the tongue between the lips, producing a hissing sound, and is not let out the same way."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmp:StrongerAddressExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Eskalation ohne Frist auf Überlieferungen beschränkt ist, deren Ergebnis eine Erscheinung ist"@de,
        "Whether the escalation without a term is confined to transmissions whose result is an apparition"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the two-clause claim that a transmission whose result is the appearance of a being meets a non-appearance with a further and stronger address and states no term, while a transmission whose result is a reported change in the condition of a second person states such a term as a count of sittings or of days. The population is every node in the compiled holding that prescribes what an operator is to do when the result of an operation has not arrived, together with every node that states a count of attempts, sittings or days at the end of which a verdict is to be taken. It was drawn by a whole-text scan of traditions/, convergences/ and originations/ for the vocabulary of failure set beside the vocabulary of repetition, and every candidate was read at its place and classified on two axes: what kind of thing the transmission makes its result, and whether it names a point at which the operator is to stop."@en ;
    skos:note "One thing the protocol did not do, and it bears on how much the finding is worth. The case was not found by a search devised against this claim; it came out of the harvest of a corpus opened for another reason, and the connection to the claim was made afterwards. SPEC §16 asks the counter-search of a generalization to run against the holding before the statement is written, and that was done when this statement was written — the holding then did not contain the refuting case. The lesson is the one already recorded at pmc:AdmissibilityBoundToTheCalendar: against a statement drawn from few witnesses, the counter-search that finds something is usually the next corpus and not the next query."@en,
        "The absence of a term was established by search and not by reading the one passage. The question was put to the whole of the two one-juan works and the first two juan of the manual, with the remaining four juan searched by term, for any interval between the sending of a petition and the effect it is sent for; it came back empty, and the empty result is exhibited at pmp:CmZhangqiSearch. What the search did return is the reason the absence is worth something: this corpus counts days with unusual care in two other places, a hundred days of pollution after a death in the house and five tables of days on which a petition may not be sent, so a term would have been stated in the same register if the transmission had one. A corpus that counts pollution to the day and does not count the wait is not silent by inadvertence."@en,
        "The first clause stands and gains nothing. The population returned no transmission making an apparition its result and naming a stopping point, and the near-misses the statement already records — the one-month term of the Ars Notoria, the years of the Shangqing sun held in the heart, the forfeiture clause of the Greek ascent — were re-read and are still of the third kind, in that what falls due at the term is a state of the operator and not the arrival of a being. Two of this run's own findings belong to the first clause and confirm it without adding a tradition: the Latin grimoire's series and the demotic manual's six take-ups are the witnesses the statement was built on, and nothing came back beside them."@en,
        "The second clause is met by the case its own falsification note named, and the case is a transmission the holding did not have when the statement was written. The Celestial Masters make their result the recovery of a sick person — a change in the condition of a second person, reported by that person and by his household, and one that no party to the rite controls. The manual of petitions cites the Taizhen code for what to do when the rite has not worked: a sequence of some fourteen petitions in which the failure of each is the sole trigger of the next, running from the petition of self-accusation through the releasing of the interrogation, of the dead forebears' punishment and of the five tombs, to the request for life at noon and midnight and the pledged text for prolonging life when the patient is in extremity. It is the escalation of the first clause, stated with a count, in an operative manual, for a result of the second kind. The node is pmc:CmBuyuJizhang."@en,
        "What the deviation costs the statement, stated exactly. It does not touch the first clause, which is about the other column. It refutes the second clause as a universal — a transmission of the second kind does not always state a term — and it leaves the asymmetry the statement was built on standing as an observation about two particular corpora rather than about two kinds of result. The clause should be rewritten so that it claims what the evidence carries: that the holding contains transmissions of the second kind which state a term, and contains at least one which does not, so that the kind of result does not by itself decide the matter. That rewriting is made at the node; this protocol records why."@en,
        "What would decide it, and what a later run should do rather than adding witnesses. The second clause rested on one transmission and now rests on two that disagree, which is not a class but a pair. The cheapest discriminating question is not how many further transmissions state a term but what the two that differ have that the other lacks: the animal magnetism operates on a patient in the operator's presence over a sitting whose length he fixes, and the Celestial Masters operate by a document sent to an office whose answer arrives, if it arrives, outside the rite altogether. A term can be stated where the operator is present at the end of the attempt and cannot easily be stated where he is not. If that holds across further cases, the dividing feature is the location of the outcome relative to the rite and not the kind of result at all, and the statement should be reformed on it. The population for that question is the transmissions of this holding whose operation is a petition, a letter, a burial deposit or any other despatch, and it has not been drawn."@en ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Two searches over the compiled holding. The first drew every node whose wording sets a term of failure beside a term of repetition — fail, no effect, does not heal, produced nothing, comes to nothing, set against again, repeat, a second, a further, once more, renew, until — and returned some eighty nodes, each read at its place. The second put the question the other way round and asked which transmissions of the holding make their result a change in the condition of a second person reported by that person, so that the population of the second clause could be counted rather than assumed. That is where the opposition came from: the count is two, and the second of them behaves like the first clause and not like the second. Not searched: anything outside the holding. The claim is about the wording of transmissions, and the cheap search was the one run; what a further corpus would decide is stated below."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-23" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "For the first clause: a transmission making the appearance of a being its result and naming a term, a count of addresses or a sign at which the operator concludes that nothing will come. Not met; nothing in the population does this. For the second clause: a transmission whose result is a reported change in the condition of a second person which prescribes, in place of any term, a stronger address repeated without limit. Met, by the petition sequence of the Celestial Masters, and the second clause does not survive it as worded."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-23" ;
    pm:tests pmc:TheStrongerAddress .

pmp:SubidaFalsificationSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Subida ein Zeichen nennt, an dem ein Uebender erkennen koennte, dass der Uebergang falsch vollzogen war"@de,
        "Whether the Subida names a sign by which a practitioner could tell that the transition had been made wrongly"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-08" ;
    skos:note "A second limit, and it is the one that keeps the find from being a falsifier in the sense of pm:falsifiedBy. The criterion tells a practitioner whether he may leave meditation; it does not say what would show that the contemplation he entered is not happening. Nothing in the opened block states an outcome whose absence would count against the practice itself."@en,
        "Found, and the registration was right. The work states a three-part conjunctive criterion, states that fewer than three do not suffice, and names the two states that counterfeit a partial set: distraction or lukewarmness for the first sign alone, and melancholy or a humour settled in the brain or the heart for the first and second together. It also states the consequence of acting on an incomplete set, which is to go backwards and to have lost the remote means without reaching the proximate one. That is a named misidentification with a named differential diagnosis, and it is the thing this holding most often looks for and does not find."@en,
        "The limit of the find, which is in the source and not a reservation of this run. The sign that does the discriminating work, the third, is said to be almost imperceptible at the beginning of the state, most subtle and delicate and as it were unfelt, and the text gives two reasons why. So the criterion is weakest exactly at the point where a practitioner would need it, and the work says so. A protocol that wanted to use this criterion would have to fix that first, and nothing in the opened block offers a way."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "In the Spanish: senal, senales, juntas, no basta, distraccion, tibieza, melancolia, humor, cerebro, corazon, embelesamiento, volver atras, perderse, errar, dano, enganar, antes de tiempo, a su tiempo." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:SubidaSenalesCautela,
        pmc:SubidaSenalesConiunctae ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-08" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Juan de la Cruz, Subida del Monte Carmelo, Libro II, chapters XI to XIX in the Gerardo numbering, read complete in the Spanish of the Gerardo edition, Toledo 1912, against the English of Lewis rev. Zimmermann and the French of the Carmelites of Paris." ;
    pm:searchedFor "A stated condition under which the practice this work prescribes would be shown not to have worked: a term after which nothing more comes, a mark of a state wrongly identified, a consequence of proceeding on the wrong sign. This is the question of the testability-driven entrance of SPEC 14, and this transmission was chosen for it because its registration named it as the western material with the plainest failure modes." .

pmp:SubidaSignaUtEvidentiaSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Subida irgendwo eine Vision, eine Sprache oder eine Erfuellung als Nachweis zaehlen laesst"@de,
        "Whether the Subida anywhere lets a vision, a locution or a fulfilment count as evidence"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-08" ;
    skos:note "Nothing, and the nothing is doctrine. Every candidate the search found is a place where the work takes the evidential value away. A communication true in itself is not thereby true for us. A prediction that comes out right shows nothing, because whoever has a clear natural light can read effects from causes, and the text gives a pestilence and an earthquake as worked examples and calls the result a prophecy of the devil that comes out true. The marks by which good visions are told from bad ones are granted to be worth knowing and the soul is to be kept from the work, the care and the danger of using them. And what is said supernaturally is to be received not because it is a revelation but because it is reason, with the reason examined much more than if no revelation had occurred."@en,
        "So the exhibited gap here is of the useful kind. This transmission supplies, from inside its own operative text, the two things this holding usually has to supply from outside: an alternative account of a correct answer, and a rule that refuses the hearer's certainty as a ground. A later run looking for a transmission that argues against its own confirming evidence should start here, and the nodes are pmc:SubidaVeritasNonProbatOriginem and pmc:SubidaProphetiaNonDiscriminans."@en,
        "What was not searched: the third book of the Subida and the Noche oscura, where the treatment of the memory and the will continues and where a permission of this kind could in principle stand. Both are unopened, and pmw:NocheOscura is carried by queries/unopened-works.rq for that reason."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "In the Spanish: asegurarse, asegurar, cierto, ciertas, salir verdadero, sale verdad, acierta en lo que esta por venir, probar, indicios, conocer las visiones buenas y las malas, no porque es revelacion sino porque es razon, examinar." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-08" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Juan de la Cruz, Subida del Monte Carmelo, Libro II, chapters XVI to XIX in the Gerardo numbering, read complete in the Spanish of the Gerardo edition, Toledo 1912, with chapters XI to XV read for the same question." ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage in which the work permits an announced sign, a vision, a locution or the fulfilment of a prediction to be taken as showing that something is so. The question matters because a transmission whose signs are all withdrawn supplies no confirming evidence at all, and that is a different object for this holding than a transmission whose signs are weak." .

pmp:SusrutaChedana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das sofortige Abtrennen des Glieds nach einem Treffer in das Kshipra- oder Tala-marman"@de,
        "Amputating the limb at once after a piercing of the Kshipra or Tala marman"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Susruta Samhita, Sarirasthana VI.82, in the English of Kaviraj Kunjalal Bhishagratna, An English Translation of the Sushruta Samhita, vol. II, Calcutta 1911, pp. 188-189" ;
    skos:definition "The one positive instruction chapter VI gives for a marman that has been struck: in a case of piercing of or injury to the Kshipra or the Tala, the hand or the leg is to be amputated immediately, at the wrist or at the ankle respectively."@en ;
    skos:note "The reasoning is given with it and is an argument from a contrast the chapter states twice. The amputation of a hand or a leg may not prove fatal, whereas a wound in any of the marmas situated therein is sure to bring on death: in a cut limb the vessels contract and the bleeding is scantier, so the limb comes off like the branch of a tree, while a man pierced in the Kshipra or the Tala meets his doom like a tree whose roots have been severed."@en,
        "What the chapter does not say, and the silence is the examinable part. It does not say that the amputation saves the patient, it sets no term within which the amputation must be done for it to work, and it reports no case. The instruction stands on the contrast and on nothing else."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SusrutaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:SusrutaMarmaLingaSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das marman-Kapitel einen Fall berichtet"@de,
        "Whether the marman chapter reports a case"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-02" ;
    skos:note "Limits. Two chapters of an anatomical book. The surgical book of the same work, which stands in volume I and was not opened, is where a case would be if there were one, and the Uttaratantra is where a later station would have added one. Neither was asked."@en,
        "No case. The two chapters report no wounded person, no bled patient and no outcome observed in anyone. What the hits give instead is a dense apparatus of disagreement among authorities about the doctrine: some are of opinion that the marmas are junctions of five factors and others of fewer, and the chapter rejects the second view by an argument from bleeding joints; Gayadasa is reported on the measure of the Indravasti, on the distances to be left in an incision and as not reading one verse at all; Vagbhata is reported as placing one marman in a different class; Dallana is reported as differing from the text on which disease a vein near the temple is opened for. The transmission argues about the classification at length and never adduces a patient."@en,
        "This is the exhibited gap and it is of the same kind as the one this holding records for the rasayana lesson of the Caraka, which claims five times to have been tested and reports no case. The two works are the two halves of the same corpus, and in both the vocabulary of authority is rich and the vocabulary of observation is absent. That is a finding about the genre of the Indian medical handbook, and only that is claimed."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of the whitespace-normalised chapters for: seen, observed, observation, experience, case of a, instance, it has been found, we have seen, reported, tested, tried, example; and for the names by which the work elsewhere credits an authority: Gayadasa, Bhoja, Vagbhata, Dallana, Jejjata, authorities, some are of opinion, several authorities. Every hit read at its place." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-02" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Susruta Samhita, Sarirasthana VI and VIII, in the English of Kaviraj Kunjalal Bhishagratna, An English Translation of the Sushruta Samhita, vol. II, Calcutta 1911, pp. 173-190 and 197-208, read continuously" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Anywhere in the two chapters: a report of a person wounded at a named marman with a stated outcome, or a patient bled with a stated result — a case, an observation, a series, a failure, an attribution to a named physician's experience. The chapters state a hundred and seven sites with their consequences, five classes with their terms, twenty named defects of an opened vein and a quantity in palas, which is a great deal of detail about outcomes; what is at issue is whether anything behind it is reported as having been seen." .

pmp:SwedenborgPraxisSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Korpus irgendwo eine Operation angibt, durch die der Verkehr herbeizuführen wäre"@de,
        "Whether the corpus anywhere gives an operation by which the converse could be brought about"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-29" ;
    skos:note "It also settles what the internal respiration is in this corpus, against the whole later reception of it. There is no breathing exercise here: the respiration is reported as led from within without any exertion of the will, as imperceptible, and at one place as arriving unasked at the moment of an attack and ending it. The nodes are pmc:SwedenborgRespiratioInterna and pmc:SwedenborgRespiratioProtegens."@en,
        "Nothing. In eight volumes there is no operation, and that is why traditions/swedenborgian.ttl carries no pm:Practicing node. The one passage that comes nearest is a prohibition and it runs the other way: those who think much on religious subjects and do not break the current of their thoughts with various useful occupations become visionaries and enthusiasts who take every spirit they hear for the Holy Spirit. The corpus describes the way into the experience in order to forbid it."@en,
        "This is the finding that makes the pair with the Theravada handbook read in the same batch worth having. That one is operation throughout and states its conditions of failure as properties of the circumstances; this one has capacities, prerequisites, cautions and a criterion, and nothing to do. Two transmissions at opposite ends of the same axis, both now in the holding at the wording, and the axis itself — whether a transmission supplies a procedure — is not yet anything the graph can be asked about."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "All eight volumes normalised to bare letters, then searched for: let him who desires (no hits anywhere); if any one wishes to speak with spirits (none); in order to speak with spirits he must (none); by means of abstinence (none); regulate his breathing (none); method by which (two hits, both in Arcana volume XI and both inside the exposition of the curtains of the tabernacle, neither an instruction); hold the breath (one, in Angelic Wisdom, and it is an argument that a man who holds his breath altogether can think only in his spirit, not an exercise); fasting (one, in Arcana volume XII, and not of this); discipline (three, one of them a translator's preface about the march of the truths of heaven); posture (seven, every one of them either a printing of imposture or a description of how a body lay or was tormented); exercise and practise (about eighty between them, all read at the hit and every one of them either a faculty being exercised, a device being practised upon somebody, or a virtue being practised)." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-29" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Swedenborg, De caelo et inferno, in the English of the Swedenborg Society, Heaven and Hell, London 1885, and in the Society's edition of 1893; Swedenborg, Arcana coelestia, volumes I, V, XI and XII of the Standard Edition in the English of John Faulkner Potts; Swedenborg, Sapientia angelica de divino amore et de divina sapientia, in the English of the Swedenborg Society, London 1912; Swedenborg, Diarium spirituale, volume I, in the English of Bush and Smithson, London 1883 — eight volumes, each searched as one text" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Any instruction by which a second person could set about the thing the corpus reports: a posture, a regulation of the breath, an abstinence, a fast, a vigil, a recitation, a formula, a period of preparation, a discipline of any kind, or a sentence of the form let him who desires this do that. The question decides whether this transmission carries a pm:Practicing node at all, and therefore whether it belongs among the transmissions this holding can compare operation for operation with the others." .

pmp:SwedenborgSignumAperitionisSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Korpus ein Zeichen nennt, an dem ein Lebender die Öffnung seines Inneren erkennt"@de,
        "Whether the corpus names a sign by which a living man knows his interiors are opened"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-29" ;
    skos:note "Nothing, and the one hit is the corpus saying so. So long as a man lives in the world he has no knowledge of the opening of these degrees within him, because he is in the natural degree and the spiritual does not communicate with it by continuity but by correspondences, and communication by correspondences is not sensibly felt. The same withdrawal is made of the internal respiration, which is called internal and for that reason imperceptible to man. The node is pmc:SwedenborgNullumSignumInVita."@en,
        "The limit of the search is that it was carried over English translations and over four of the twelve Arcana volumes. Against that stands what the corpus does state in the volumes read: not a silence but an explicit denial, which a translator would have had to render somehow and which a further volume would have to contradict rather than merely supplement."@en,
        "Why this is an exhibited gap and not bookkeeping. A reader who works forward through these volumes meets a great many reported experiences and will not notice that none of them is offered as a criterion; the absence is only visible if somebody asks for the criterion and comes back empty. Anybody who owns the Latin, or the eleven volumes of the Arcana that were not opened, can now try to close it, and the question is stated precisely enough to be answered."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "All eight volumes normalised to bare letters first, then searched for: may know that his interiors (no hits in any volume); knows that his interiors (none); sign of the opening (none); know whether the spiritual degree (none); perceive the opening (none); is sensible of the opening (none); no knowledge of the opening (one hit, in Angelic Wisdom, and it is the negative statement itself). Then the passage that hit was read continuously together with the thirty numbers around it, 232 to 262, and the four Arcana places on the internal respiration were read for the same question." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-29" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Swedenborg, De caelo et inferno, in the English of the Swedenborg Society, Heaven and Hell, London 1885, and in the Society's edition of 1893; Swedenborg, Arcana coelestia, volumes I, V, XI and XII of the Standard Edition in the English of John Faulkner Potts; Swedenborg, Sapientia angelica de divino amore et de divina sapientia, in the English of the Swedenborg Society, London 1912; Swedenborg, Diarium spirituale, volume I, in the English of Bush and Smithson, London 1883 — eight volumes, each searched as one text" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Any mark, sign, perception or test by which a man still living could establish that the spiritual or celestial degree has been opened in him, or that his interiors have been opened so that he may speak with spirits and angels. The doctrine makes that opening the condition of everything the corpus claims, and the question is whether it also gives the man a way of knowing where he stands. If it does not, then the central claim of the largest body of first-person reports in this holding is one its own author says nobody can check on himself." .

pmp:TaulipangDefectusSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Ueberlieferung eine Bedingung nennt, unter der das Vermoegen zerstoert wird"@de,
        "Whether the transmission names a condition under which the capacity is destroyed"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-04" ;
    skos:note "A third find of a different kind: the cure is never performed during rain, and a thunderstorm breaking out during the work ends it at once, to be begun again from the beginning the following night. That is the only condition in the part read which lies outside both the practitioner and the person operated on, and it is the only one that carries a resumption rule."@en,
        "The strongest single passage was not what the search was looking for. At pmc:TaulipangTheTrunkWasEmpty a practitioner reports an operation, a verification he performed on it - he locked the thing he had caught in a trunk and opened the trunk in the morning - and an empty result, which he then accounts for by the nature of what he had caught. The holding carries containers and residues applied to a performance by other people; this is the only one applied by the operator to himself, in private, with both halves reported by him."@en,
        "Two conditions, and both are of the strong kind the search was shaped for. The leaves of wolele-yeg, brewed into the drink pelakali and forbidden for the whole apprenticeship, would burn the novice's whole throat so that he could never sing again; and piranha, the one food still forbidden after release, would cut the ladder to the sky so that he could never ascend again. Each names the substance, the mechanism and the faculty lost, each is announced in advance, and each is permanent. Between them they cover both faculties the office consists of."@en,
        "What the two conditions do not establish, stated so that the find is not overstated. Neither is tested and neither is said ever to have happened to anybody. A prohibition with a cited consequence is a falsifiable claim and not a verified one, and no pm:Testing node is written for either; the botanical identification wolele-yeg would need is not in the passage, while the fish is identified by the recorder as Serrasalmo sp."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "The German body text of the part read was read continuously from the running head 194 to the running head 221, the chapter having been located by its running heads because the chapter headings are destroyed in the optical reading. It was then searched for verbrennen, abschneiden, niemals, nie mehr, verlieren, verliert, Kraft verlieren, misslingt, vergeblich, umsonst, nicht gelingen, unwirksam, Frist, Probe, Pruefung, Verbot, verboten, darf nicht, essen, trinken, Fasten, abbrechen, Gewitter, Regen. The page sequence of the running heads was checked before any page was cited." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:TaulipangImmunityInferredFromTheAnimal,
        pmc:TaulipangJaguarCertifiedByTheBystanders,
        pmc:TaulipangLongerStudyStrongerShaman,
        pmc:TaulipangMauariCrystalFallsIntoThePocket,
        pmc:TaulipangNeverOneNoviceAlone,
        pmc:TaulipangNoCureDuringRain,
        pmc:TaulipangPiranhaCutsTheSkyLadder,
        pmc:TaulipangReleaseWhenManyHaveFallen,
        pmc:TaulipangSoulRetrievedByTheMaster,
        pmc:TaulipangSpellsNeedNoInitiation,
        pmc:TaulipangTheTrunkWasEmpty,
        pmc:TaulipangWoleleLeavesBurnTheThroat,
        pmp:TaulipangJaguarTransformation,
        pmp:TaulipangSoulLeavesByTheHairCord,
        pmp:TaulipangZauberarztApprenticeship ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-04" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Theodor Koch-Gruenberg, Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, Band III: Ethnographie (Stuttgart: Strecker und Schroeder, 1923), pp. 194-221" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Any sentence naming what would destroy, prevent or undo a capacity of this transmission, or what would show that an operation had not worked: a substance that disqualifies, a term after which nothing more comes, a repetition on failure, a defect in the operator, a sign of failure announced in advance. The question was put to this corpus rather than another because the holding is shortest of exactly this and because the registered transmissions outside the Eurasian contact space had so far yielded none of it." .

pmp:TaulipangSignOfFailureSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob dem Vollzieher je gesagt wird, woran er erkennt, dass eine Verrichtung nicht gewirkt hat"@de,
        "Whether a practitioner is ever told how to recognise that an operation did not work"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-04" ;
    skos:note "Nothing, and the shape of the nothing is the finding. This transmission is unusually well supplied with conditions that disqualify a practitioner permanently and carries not one sign that a particular performance miscarried. The two are not the same instrument: a disqualification is about the man and is checked once, a sign of failure is about an occasion and would be checked every time."@en,
        "The limit of the search. Twenty-eight printed pages of a volume of some four hundred, and none of the eleven spells, which are the one part of this corpus printed with its original wording and which carry, by the recorder's own description, an instruction for application at the end of each. If a sign of failure is anywhere in this corpus it is most likely there, and that is where the next run at it should go."@en,
        "Three places where such a sign could have stood and does not. Three men died of dysentery at Koimelemong while a Zauberarzt was singing over the sickness, and the account of it reports the wrestling and the empty trunk and says nothing about the deaths counting for or against the cure. The novices lie as if dead and their body wakes before their soul returns, which is an interval in which something could be read, and nothing is read in it. And a cure may run several nights in succession with no number given, so there is no count at which it ends."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "The same continuously read pages, searched for the terms listed at pmp:TaulipangDefectusSearch and in addition for Zeichen, erkennen, erkennt, umsonst, wirkungslos, half nicht, starb, Tod, gestorben, wiederholen, nochmals, von neuem, and for the numerals in the account of the cure. The three reported cases in the part read - the three deaths at Koimelemong, the empty trunk, the jaguar at the dance feast - were each read through for a sentence of this kind." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-04" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Theodor Koch-Gruenberg, Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, Band III: Ethnographie (Stuttgart: Strecker und Schroeder, 1923), pp. 194-221" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Whether the part read anywhere names a sign by which the practitioner or a bystander would know that a cure, a soul-journey or a transformation had failed; a number of attempts after which it is given up; or a condition in the operator or the material that accounts for an operation that produced nothing. This is the other half of the question above: not what destroys the capacity, but what shows that an exercise of it miscarried." .

pmp:TeinmLaida a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Teinm laída"@de,
        "Teinm laída"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Sanas Cormaic s.v. Orc tréith, in Cormac's Glossary, trans. O'Donovan, ed. Stokes (Calcutta 1868), pp. 129-130" ;
    skos:altLabel "teinm laegda"@en,
        "teinm laída"@en ;
    skos:definition "The second of the two procedures the prohibition names, and the glossary nowhere describes it. Where it is shown at work, it is shown as a gesture and not as a course: Finn, finding a headless body, puts his thumb into his mouth, and when he takes it out again he speaks through teinm laegdha and names the dead man and says that enemies have taken his head."@en ;
    skos:note "The entry that prohibits it treats it as a thing everyone knows; the entry that shows it gives one motion and no preparation, no offering, no sleep and no watcher. If the two are the same operation, the glossary has described the one and demonstrated the other."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InsularCeltic .

pmp:TermForAPromisedOutcomeSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob eine Überlieferung, die einen Nutzen zu einer Frist verspricht, sagt, was bei Fristablauf geschieht"@de,
        "Whether a transmission that promises a benefit by a term says what happens at the term"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-15" ;
    skos:note "The lesson of the search is the one SPEC §16 draws and is worth repeating from a second case. The candidate was formed while reading the class its examples came from, and the class that refutes it — the promises of the outer alchemy and of the notory art — was elsewhere and cost one query. Reading the class the evidence comes from does not count as reading the holding."@en,
        "Two nodes in the holding refute the candidate outright and a third weakens it. The nine elixirs of the outer alchemy each carry a term and each carries the benefit at that term: the first is made in thirty-six days and in seven days one is an immortal; the second acts in a hundred days; the eighth acts on the day it is taken. That is a promised outcome with a stated term and no persevering. The notory art promises that whoever says the orations at the appointed times profits in all the sciences within one month, which is why the holding already carries a protocol on it with a falsifying condition written out. The Sámi journey states an upper bound of twenty-four hours on the time the practitioner lies still, whatever the distance, which is a term with an outcome of a third kind. The candidate is therefore false as stated and was not written."@en,
        "What survives of it is smaller and is not a generalization. The mesmeric manual does name two terms and does state no outcome at either — five days before one may presume the operation does not act, nearly a month for the case of no effect at all — and the holding's own nodes say why: the sentence after the number replaces the criterion, and the paragraph after the month removes the only case that could end the treatment quickly. That is a statement about one transmission, and one transmission is not a generalization. It is left where it already stands, at the nodes themselves."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Query over the merged graph for definitions containing: within a month; in one month; thirty days; twenty-four hour; in three days; seven days; nine days; hundred days; a year and. Then each hit read at its node. The candidate's own side was drawn from pmc:PresqueUnMois, pmc:CinqJoursAvantDePresumer and pmc:MaidenDeclarationDecidedByASnake, each of which names a term and states no outcome at it; the harm side from pmc:CizhongWuzang and pmc:JingmaiQijue." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-15" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The compiled holding of prima-materia as of main at e2a66ae, all directories, queried over the merged graph" ;
    pm:searchedFor "The counter-case to a candidate generalization: that where a transmission fixes a term to an outcome it promises, it states how long to persevere and not what follows if the term passes, while a term fixed to an outcome it does not want — an injury, a death — carries a stated outcome at the term. The counter-case would be a transmission that promises a benefit by a named term and states the outcome at that term." .

pmp:TestingProhibitionExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob Überlieferungen, die eine Auskunft aus einem Verfahren beziehen, die prüfende Frage verbieten"@de,
        "Whether transmissions obtaining an answer from a procedure forbid the testing question"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that transmissions obtaining an answer from a procedure state a condition excluding the repeated or testing question, and give the spoiling of the answer as the reason rather than the unworthiness of the questioner."@en ;
    skos:note "It is worse for the claim than an ordinary counterexample, because the Zande rule inverts the mechanism the generalization described. There the second asking spoils the answer; here the absence of a second asking leaves the answer without standing. Two transmissions obtaining an answer from an outcome the operator does not control take opposite positions on the same question, so the position is not a property of that kind of procedure at all."@en,
        "The deviation. The Zande poison oracle requires a second, corroborative consultation and makes the validity of the verdict depend on it: across the two tests one fowl must die and the other must live. If both die or both live, the verdict is contradictory and void, and the matter is put to the oracle again on another occasion. That answers every clause of the falsifying condition — repetition required rather than forbidden, the outcome of the pair read as a finding about the verdict, and the response to a voided consultation being to ask again."@en,
        "The finding about this project's own procedure is the more expensive one and belongs here rather than in a report nobody reads. The counter-search for the four generalizations of prima-materia#665 was not carried before they were written; had it been, this case would have surfaced from the holding at no cost. SPEC §15 requires the search for the negation of every claim, and a generalization is a claim like any other. The three remaining nodes of that file stand under the same suspicion until each has been searched against the holding, and the searches are owed."@en,
        "What survives and what does not. The two witnesses the generalization was formed from say what they were reported as saying; the Zhouyi and the spiritist manual are untouched, and their nodes stand. What fails is the quantifier over kinds of transmission. A narrower claim about a subset — perhaps those whose answer is delivered by a speaker rather than read off an outcome — might hold, and would have to be formed and examined on its own rather than rescued from this one by adding a clause."@en ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Searched the holding's own oracular material for a transmission requiring rather than forbidding the second question, by walking every node of the Zande, Ifá, Fon, Chinese divinatory, Nahua, Maya and Kongo registrations. The Zande case answers the falsifying condition on all three counts and stands at pmc:BengeValidVerdictRule and pmp:BengeCorroborationProtocol. Not searched: the wider divinatory literature outside this holding, and no modern study was consulted, so no pm:evidenceFrom is set."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-09" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A transmission of the same kind whose earliest witness permits or requires repeated consultation on the same matter, and treats agreement or disagreement between the consultations as a finding about the procedure rather than about the questioner. One such case refutes the claim."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-09" ;
    pm:tests pmc:TestingTheProcedureVoidsItsAnswer .

pmp:TheDirectionOfTheMeasureExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob eine Überlieferung mit Maß die ungünstige Richtung zählen lässt"@de,
        "Whether any transmission with a measure lets its unfavourable direction count"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that where a transmission supplies a measure of whether the practice is working, it supplies a rule for reading a change in the unfavourable direction as confirmation and no rule for the converse. Two questions were put to the compiled holding, not one: whether a transmission with such a measure states a reading of it that counts against the practice, which is the falsifying case the claim names; and whether a transmission supplies the re-reading in a place the claim's third clause does not allow, that is where one person judges the result obtained for another."@en ;
    skos:note "No modern work is cited under pm:evidenceFrom and none was read. The claim is about what transmissions say and not about what happens to practitioners, so the evidence layer is not where it would be settled — but the neighbouring empirical question is worth naming for whoever wants it: whether a self-administered count of lapses in attention rises or falls over the first months of such a practice has been measured outside this holding, and a protocol that cited such work would be testing a different claim than this one."@en,
        "The state is cases with deviation and not cases without, and the deviation is the one named in the second counter-search note. It is recorded as a deviation rather than folded into the statement because a claim is corrected where it is examined: the third clause of the statement carries the correction, and the protocol beside it carries the case that forced it. A later reader should be able to see that the clause was not there first."@en,
        "What the protocol establishes and what it cannot. It establishes by reading that in the three transmissions of this holding that supply a measure of the practitioner's own state, the unfavourable direction is provided for and the favourable one is not; and it establishes that the holding contains no transmission of that kind whose measure is allowed to count against the practice. It cannot establish that none exists, because the search was over this holding and the holding is thin in exactly the respect that matters: a counted measure is rare in it, and three of the five nodes come from two authors in one milieu."@en ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Not searched: anything outside this holding. The claim is about a form of wording and the holding carries some ninety corpora, so the cheap search is the one that was run. A transmission outside it that refutes the claim would still refute it, and the falsifier above says where to look first."@en,
        "The second search asked where the re-reading sits and returned the case that shaped the third clause. In the same corpus as the second witness, the device is named and rejected: against operators who suppose that remedies which did not succeed were not exactly followed and regard remedies that did mischief as a necessary crisis. The node is pmc:CautionAgainstTrustingThePrescription. This is a deviation and it is entered as one, because the claim as first drafted said the corpus supplies the re-reading and nothing about where. What the deviation shows is that the same corpus can hold the device and its refusal at once, sorted by who is being judged; and it stops short of the step that would settle anything, since the prescriptions go to a candid physician who is not to be put in consultation with the somnambulist, so no judgement is ever recorded against another in a form anybody could count."@en,
        "Two searches over the compiled holding on 2026-09-28, then at the nodes returned. The first asked which transmissions carry a measure and a stopping rule keyed to it, searching every tradition file case-insensitively for: grows worse, the worse, worse instead, renounce, leave off, discontinue, desist, abandon the practice, give up the practice, aggravation, crisis, is the sign that it is working, proof that it works, counted as proof, in vain, wasted, of no benefit. Every hit was read in its place. It returned one stopping rule and the rule is cancelled in its own corpus: the magnetic instruction tells the patient to renounce the practice after some days and use ordinary medicine if he grows worse, and the paragraph immediately following removes that case, requiring careful attention before one can say with certainty that the disease is rendered worse and warning that a person might be deceived by appearances and renounce at the moment the practice is on the point of doing the most good. The node is pmc:PresqueUnMois and the cancellation is recorded there. It is not a refutation; it is the strongest stopping rule the holding has, and it lasts eight pages."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-28, over the compiled holding at the head of this branch" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A transmission that gives its practitioner a counted quantity, a bodily state or a change in his condition by which to judge the practice, and states a value or a direction of that measure at which the practice is to be judged not to work. A term at which to persevere longer does not meet it, and neither does a rule charging the shortfall to the practitioner: the reading of the measure itself has to go against the practice. Where such a passage stands in a corpus that elsewhere supplies the re-reading, the claim is not refuted but its first clause is reduced to a tendency and has to say so." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-28" ;
    pm:tests pmc:TheDirectionOfTheMeasure .

pmp:TheSignAndTheCounterfeitExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob eine Überlieferung, die den Fortschritt an inneren Erscheinungen misst, immer eine Fälschung benennt"@de,
        "Whether a transmission measuring progress by inner appearances always names a counterfeit"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that transmissions measuring progress by inner appearances name those appearances as the chief obstacle to further progress, on the ground that the practitioner takes them for the attainment, and place the mark of the counterfeit in the circumstances of the experience rather than in its intensity. The protocol is a reading protocol: what is claimed is a regularity in what transmissions say, so it fails or holds against texts and not against practitioners."@en ;
    skos:note "The lineage makes the case stronger than a distant one would have been, and it is worth saying why rather than leaving it as a coincidence. The claim's second witness is the Visuddhimagga's ten imperfections of insight, entered in this holding from Theravāda XII. The refuting manual belongs to the transmission whose ordination line was fetched from Siam and returned to Ceylon in 1753, uses the Pali vocabulary of jhāna, kasiṇa, nimitta, appanā and upacāra throughout, and cites the same canonical scheme of ten insights. A holding that had only asked distant corpora would have concluded that the Theravāda names its counterfeits; what it names is one text's counterfeits, and another text of the same lineage names none."@en,
        "The one thing the transmission did say about danger it did not say in writing, and the protocol records it because it bears on where such rules live rather than on whether they exist. A Sinhala informant in 1916 held that the practice must not be taught by a guru who is not fully versed in it and able to see clairvoyantly what effect each meditation is having on his pupil, and named a pupil who went mad and died. That is at pmc:BoranSisyaUmmattako with pm:fieldAttestation and its gathering named. So the last holders of the practice carried a grave caution and the book they held carried none, which suggests that the successor statement should ask not only which genre names the counterfeit but whether the rule was ever in a book at all."@en,
        "What survives, and it is a narrower claim that this protocol does not write into the graph, because a claim is corrected where it is examined and a tidied sentence would hide the case. The two original witnesses are both commentarial or directorial literature: a handbook of discernment and a commentary on the path, both written to instruct somebody about somebody else's experience. The refuting case is a liturgy, written to be recited by the practitioner himself while he does the thing. That is the distinction a successor statement should try: the counterfeit is named where a transmission addresses a supervisor and not where it addresses the operator. If that holds, the claim was about a genre and not about inner appearances, and the two witnesses were selected by the genre without anybody noticing."@en,
        "What the case is, in the transmission's own terms. The manual grades the five raptures by a triad of colours each, and the five jhānas by a single ascending scale of light: the ecstasy of the first jhāna is like a firefly, of the second like the halo of the moon, of the third like the colour of the medicine star, of the fourth like the halo of the sun, of the fifth like white flowers. That is the whole criterion by which the stages are distinguished. The appearances are announced before the practitioner sits — the formula of request names the state he is to attain and the rubric that follows states which element appears and in which colours — and they are stated in the indicative, as what appears to him, with no conditional and no alternative branch anywhere in the work. The nodes are pmc:BoranPancapitiVanna and pmc:BoranPancajhanaVanna in traditions/boran-kammatthana.ttl."@en,
        "What the refutation costs the claim, stated precisely, because a refutation that is allowed to cost too much is as bad as one that is allowed to cost nothing. Both halves of the claim fail against this case and they fail differently. The first half says such transmissions name the appearances as the chief obstacle to further progress; this one names no obstacle of any kind, so the first half fails by absence. The second half says the mark of the counterfeit is placed in the circumstances rather than the intensity; this one has no counterfeit, so the second half is not contradicted but vacuous. The claim's own note said the second half is the part that could fail and is therefore the part worth having, and it identified the failure mode as a tradition that measured the counterfeit by intensity. That is not what happened. What happened is a tradition with no counterfeit at all, and it grades by intensity — of light — without ever using the grading to reject anything."@en,
        "Where the protocol should go next, and it is a named address rather than a class. The Siamese and Khmer branches of the same transmission are published in part and were not opened: the pīti section of the Kammatthan Matchima Baep Lamdap from Wat Ratchasittharam, the manual ascribed to King Taksin of 1775, and the Khmer material edited by Bizot, of which one text is reported to state outright that the embryonic objects must be red like a ball of blood and that if another colour is mixed with it the form cannot be born. If that is at the wording, the same transmission holds both arrangements — a colour condition that can fail in one branch and none in another — and the successor statement can be tested inside one lineage instead of across the holding."@en ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "A second search, over the compiled holding rather than the work, asked whether the holding already carried a transmission of this kind with no counterfeit, so that the case would not be the first. Every node whose definition names a colour was read, thirty-six of them, and every node whose definition names a sign or an appearance in the traditions that grade by them. It did not: pmp:SteinerUebungAmSamen announces inner colours in advance and states the authenticity test the claim predicts — that the sense distinguishing truth from deception must be continually cultivated and the pupil must examine whether he has fallen out of his equilibrium; pmc:NeidanZhengyan announces its sequence in advance and the same work carries the difficulties and the demons in the adjacent chapter; the desert discernment literature and the Visuddhimagga are the claim's own two witnesses. So the manual is the first case of its kind in this holding and the claim held against everything that had been entered before this run."@en,
        "Searched on 2026-09-26 in the Yogāvacara's Manual, read complete at the wording in Woodward's translation of 1916, for any way of telling a true appearance from a counterfeit and for any statement that an appearance is an obstacle. The whole work was read and then searched case-insensitively for counterfeit, false, illusion, deception, imperfection, obstacle, hindrance, defilement, upakkilesa, danger, beware, must not, and for the negative forms of appearing. Counterfeit, false, illusion, deception, obstacle, hindrance and defilement do not occur in the manual at all. Imperfection occurs once as a translator's gloss on a technical term and not as a state of the practice. Danger occurs five times: once in the translator's theosophical footnote and four times inside the similes of the ten forms of knowledge, where the danger is the content of the insight and not a hazard of the practice. The search is recorded at pmp:BoranTerminusSearch and pmp:BoranPericulumSearch in examinations/searches-boran-kammatthana.ttl."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-26; the reading protocol is the falsifier's own and the corpus that met it was chosen because the falsifier named its class" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A transmission whose practice produces inner appearances of the kind described, whose earliest witness treats them as reliable evidence of attainment, and which names no way of telling a true appearance from a counterfeit. The condition is the one the claim named for itself. It has been met."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-26"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:TheSignTakenForTheAttainment .

pmp:ThelemaConditioDefectusSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der Bestand eine Bedingung nennt, unter der das Verfahren und nicht der Übende gescheitert ist"@de,
        "Whether the corpus names a condition under which the procedure, rather than the practitioner, has failed"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-28" ;
    skos:note "Recorded as a search rather than left as an observation because the shape is transferable and the holding should be able to query for it. A transmission can carry a demand for measurement and a rule that makes the measurement unfalsifiable without either being a concession by the other, and neither passage reads as a retraction. Only a search that asked for the failure condition and came back with its opposite makes that visible."@en,
        "The outcome is opposing material and not a find, and the distinction is the point. One genuine failure condition came back — the inharmonious error of the card test, which names a class of result the claim excludes — and everything else the search turned up runs the other way: no practitioner can fail to get some result; a rising count of interruptions is increased accuracy of observation; ease in the posture is incapacity; no simple and certain test of success exists; and where a result is subtle the practitioner decides for himself whether to be satisfied with it. Four of these five are in the same two instructions as the measures the other search found."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search for: fail, fails, failed, failure, in vain, useless, wasted, mistake, error, sources of error, discouraged, if he do not, unless, cannot hope, no result, nothing, wrong, incorrectly, not nearly good enough. Every hit read in its place."@en ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:ThelemaErrorInharmonicus,
        pmc:ThelemaFacilitasEstIndiciumErroris,
        pmc:ThelemaFractiCrescentesNonSuntCasus,
        pmc:ThelemaIudiciumApudStudentem,
        pmc:ThelemaNemoDeficit,
        pmc:ThelemaNullumProbandumCertum ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-28" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The Equinox, volume one, numbers one to ten (London 1909-1913), searched whole by term; and Book 4 (Liber ABA), Part I: Meditation, chapters I, V, VI and VII, read at the wording in the Celephais Press electronic edition of 2004, which follows the corrected text in Magick: Book 4 Parts I-IV, York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1994."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage stating what would show that a practice of this transmission does not work: a term after which a result should have arrived and has not, an outcome the claim excludes, a reading of the record under which the procedure is at fault. A transmission that counts its own failures on a string of beads was the most promising place in the holding to look for one."@en .

pmp:ThelemaCountAtAGlanceExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Zählpraxis die auf einen Blick nennbare Zahl hebt"@de,
        "Whether the counting practice raises the number nameable at a glance"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the practice in which an assistant throws an unknown number of small similar objects on a bare table and gathers them up again, the practitioner declares the number at the glance, and the assistant confirms it by his count; the maximum rises by one at each session provided that not a single mistake is made, and the practice is to run at a quarter of an hour thrice daily for at least one year."@en ;
    skos:note "No case has been collected. Unlike the card practice, this one has an established comparison outside the transmission — the ordinary limit on how many objects a person can enumerate at a glance — and a protocol that used it would be stronger than one that compared the practitioner only with himself. That literature was not read in this run and nothing is cited under pm:evidenceFrom."@en,
        "The confound is in the instruction and was not noticed by it: the quickness of the assistant in gathering up the objects is expected to increase with time, so the exposure duration is not held constant across the year. A rise in the named maximum against a falling exposure would be a stronger result than the instruction claims, and a rise against a rising exposure would be no result at all. The protocol therefore requires the exposure to be fixed or measured, which the instruction does not."@en,
        "This is the only practice in the holding whose advancement rule is enforced by a second person in the course of the practice itself. The assistant is not a witness brought in to check a claim afterwards; he is part of the procedure, and the tally he keeps decides whether the difficulty rises. That makes the practice self-recording in a way nothing else here is."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, working from the passage recorded in traditions/thelema.ttl; the cadence, the error rule and the term are the instruction's own" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A practitioner who keeps the practice at the stated cadence for the stated year, with the assistant's count recorded at every session, and whose maximum reliably named number at the end of the year is no higher than the maximum he reached in his first week; or a series in which the rise in the maximum is not distinguishable from the rise attributable to the assistant becoming quicker at gathering the objects, which the instruction expects and does not control for. The claim is a claim about an increase and a series without an increase refutes it." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-28" ;
    pm:tests pmp:ThelemaNumerusUnoAspectu .

pmp:ThelemaEditioPrincepsMCMXXIXSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der Pariser Druck von 1929 gegen den Erstdruck der Anweisung kollationiert werden kann"@de,
        "Whether the Paris print of 1929 can be collated against the first print of the instruction"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-28" ;
    skos:note "A property of the access route and not of the work. The 1929 print exists in libraries and is out of copyright in its place of publication; what blocks it is the rights state recorded on the digitised copies. The next ways, both untried here, are a physical copy and a library with its own full-text access. Until one of them is used, the dispute at pmc:ThelemaDisputatioDeLectione is established between the first print and the later reprint tradition and not against Crowley's own second edition."@en,
        "Entered as a search rather than as an issue because the gap is the kind a stranger can close: anybody holding the Paris print can settle it in one minute, and an exhibited gap can be closed by a stranger while an unexhibited one cannot."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:corpusUnreachable ;
    pm:searchTerms "Archive identifiers tried: the Wellcome Library copy dated 1929, whose full text and pdf both answer with an authorisation requirement and which is marked access-restricted; the Dover reprint of 1976, likewise restricted to lending; and an open electronic edition which states that it follows a later American reprint and carries its editor's own notes. Also tried: the HathiTrust full-text search, which answers with a refusal to automated requests."@en ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-28" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Crowley, Magick in Theory and Practice (Book 4, Part III), Paris: Lecram Press 1929, sought as a full text in order to collate the reprint of Liber E in its seventh appendix against the first print in The Equinox I,1."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "The wording of Liber E chapter I section seven in the 1929 print, and whether the section numbering of the instruction survives in it. The first print reads intelligibly prepared and a later reprint tradition reads intelligently, which changes whether the record is answerable to other people; the 1929 print stands between them in time and would decide where the change entered."@en .

pmp:ThelemaLevitationSequenceExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die vier Stufen der Atempraxis eintreten, und in der genannten Folge"@de,
        "Whether the four stages of the breath practice occur, and in the stated order"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that the lengthened breath cycle brings about, in order, a sweat of a character different from that of exertion, then the ceasing of the sweat with automatic rigidity, then unconscious spasmodic movement in which the body hops gently from place to place, and last the body rising into the air and remaining there from a second to an hour or more."@en ;
    skos:note "No case has been collected and the first stage is the one to start from, not the fourth. A sweat of a character different from that of exertion is a claim about a secretion and could be examined without any of the rest; the instruction even demands that the rigidity of the second stage be described with minute accuracy, which is the point at which reports from different practitioners could be compared. Nobody has compared them here."@en,
        "The claim is unusually exposed for this holding and the reason is the order. A practitioner who reaches the second stage and not the third has an outcome the instruction did not predict, and the instruction says nothing about what to conclude from it. Most of what this holding carries promises an end state and leaves the route unnumbered, so that no intermediate result can be wrong."@en,
        "The instruction's own framing helps rather than hinders: the stages are put as statements to be investigated and the text calls what it asks for a record of research. That is the transmission inviting the protocol, and it is entered here as such."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, working from the passages recorded in traditions/thelema.ttl; the four stages, their order and the requirement of an assistant are the instruction's own" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A practitioner who reaches the cycles the instruction names and holds them under the conditions the instruction names — including the assistant it requires to be present throughout — and in whom the third and fourth stages do not occur, the assistant reporting no displacement of the body from its place and no rise from the ground. The fourth stage is stated as a bodily event of stated duration in the presence of a second person, so its absence in a practitioner who has reached the stated cycles refutes the sequence as stated." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-28" ;
    pm:tests pmc:ThelemaPranayamaYieldsCorpusLevatum .

pmp:ThelemaLiberABAPrimaEditioSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der erste Teil von Book 4 als Scan des Wieland-Drucks von 1912 vorliegt"@de,
        "Whether the first part of Book 4 exists as a scan of the Wieland print of 1912"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-28" ;
    skos:note "A property of the access route, and the consequence is carried in the sources rather than hidden. Every node in traditions/thelema.ttl taken from the first part names the 1912 first publication and the 2004 electronic edition following the corrected text of 1994, because that is what was read. The pagination of the first print is carried by that edition and was used for the page numbers."@en,
        "One consequence is a naming trap worth recording. The 1912 half-title of the part reads Meditation; Mysticism is the name of the later editions. A citation of Book 4 Part I Mysticism names an edition that did not exist in 1912, and this holding therefore cites the part as Meditation."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:corpusUnreachable ;
    pm:searchTerms "Archive identifiers tried for the first part: three separate items, of which one returns an optical transcription in Cyrillic characters throughout and is unusable at any wording, one follows a corrected text of 1994 by its own statement, and one covers the later parts only and follows an edition of 1969. The full-text file of the second part was reached under the standard path and read; no equivalent exists for the first."@en ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-28" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Crowley and Sturges, Book 4 (Liber ABA), Part I, London: Wieland & Co. 1912, sought as a scan of the first edition."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A carrier of the first part that is the 1912 print itself, so that the nodes taken from it need not cite a later edition. The second part was found as a first-edition scan from a university library, dated 1913, with its errata leaf and the note signed by the second author; the first part was not."@en .

pmp:ThelemaMensuraExternaSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der thelemische Bestand ein Maß nennt, das ein anderer als der Übende anlegen kann"@de,
        "Whether the Thelemic corpus states a measure a person other than the practitioner can apply"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-28" ;
    skos:note "Five places, and they are not of one kind. A stated chance rate with a threshold above it; a saucer of water that spills or does not; a count kept by an assistant, with the advancement of the practice gated on his tally; a printed verdict of failure on a named practitioner under two named criteria; and a prediction with a time, an ambient temperature and a named instrument. Four of the five are in the earliest instructions of the series and one is in the last, so the demand is not a late addition."@en,
        "What the search also settled, by the absence of hits rather than by their presence: no passage anywhere in the ten numbers submits a claim of this transmission to a person outside the order. The examiner, the assistant and the instrument are all internal to it. That is a limit on the external measures the search did find, and it belongs with them."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search of all ten numbers, case-insensitive, for: test, tests, tested, examination, examined, admitted for, record, records, recorded, count, counted, number of, chela, bystander, witness, respirometer, instrument, measure, prove, proof, verify, verified, per cent, times in, once in. Every hit read in its place; the hits in the verse, the reviews and the fiction set aside."@en ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:ThelemaFidelitasEtVigilantia,
        pmc:ThelemaMensuraPatinae,
        pmc:ThelemaRatioCasus,
        pmc:ThelemaSignumSudorisDecemMinutorum,
        pmp:ThelemaNumerusUnoAspectu ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-28" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The Equinox, volume one, numbers one to ten (London 1909-1913), all ten numbers searched whole by term; the instructions among them read at the wording. Read in the Celephais Press and Ordo Templi Orientis re-setting, which reproduces the original pagination, with number one collated against a page scan of that number."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage in which this transmission puts the judgment of a result in the hands of somebody other than the person judged: a bystander, an assistant, an examiner, an instrument, or a number fixed in advance. The testability-driven entrance of SPEC §14 looks for exactly this, and a transmission that demands a written record was the obvious place to look for it."@en .

pmp:ThelemaPranayamaInAmbulatione a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Atem im Gang und im Tanz"@de,
        "The breath in the walk and in the dance"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber RV vel Spiritus sub figura CCVI, §§ 3 and 6-8, in The Equinox I,7 (London, March 1912), pp. 61-64" ;
    skos:definition "Practising the deep full breathing during walks, with a sacred sentence or a count so that the footfall beats accurately with its rhythm; beginning with an indrawing of the breath for four paces and a breathing out for four, increasing to six, eight, twelve, sixteen and twenty-four, then to the proportion four to eight, six to twelve, eight to sixteen, twelve to twenty-four; then quickening the sentence and the pace until the walk develops into a dance, and practising mental concentration during the dance."@en ;
    skos:note "The nine practices of this instruction are written as statements to be investigated rather than as promises, and the word the text uses of its own contents is research: let him investigate the following statements, and prepare a careful record of research. That is the most nearly experimental framing anywhere in the corpus, and it is the frame in which the strongest physical claims of this transmission are made."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberRVVelSpiritus ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmp:ThelemaProbatioPentagrammatis a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Prüfung einer Erscheinung mit dem Pentagramm"@de,
        "Testing an apparition with the pentagram"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber O vel Manus et Sagittae sub figura VI, chap. V, § 9, in The Equinox I,2 (London, September 1909), p. 28" ;
    skos:definition "During the rising, carefully testing the truth of all with whom one speaks, using the appropriate figures and signs: a hostile being may appear clothed with glory, and the appropriate pentagrams will in such a case cause him to shrivel or decay."@en ;
    skos:note "The one operational test in the corpus with a stated outcome that could fail to occur. It is a test of the object and not of the practitioner, and its result is a change in the apparition rather than a feeling in the observer, which distinguishes it from every other sign this instruction gives. Whether the observer and the judge are the same person is not addressed, and that is the limit of it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberOVelManusEtSagittae ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmp:ThelemaTarotRateExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Kartenpraxis das Nennen über die vom Text selbst genannte Rate hebt"@de,
        "Whether the card practice raises naming above the rate the instruction itself states"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that the card practice develops the faculty it measures, up to a suit called correctly three times in four and the card named aright fifteen or twenty times in seventy-eight. It is examinable without any decision by the compiler, because the instruction supplies both the chance rate and the threshold."@en ;
    skos:note "Modern work bearing on forced-choice card guessing exists in quantity and none of it was read in this run, so nothing is cited under pm:evidenceFrom and the state stays at a procedure without cases. Entering a state that reports cases without having read the work would be the error AGENTS.md names: a state beyond a devised procedure presupposes the reading."@en,
        "No case has been collected, and the obstacle is not the difficulty of the trial, which is an afternoon's work. It is that the instruction fuses training and measurement: the same series is the exercise and the test, so a rate at chance is open to the reading that the training was not yet complete. The instruction gives no number of trials and no date by which the threshold is to be reached, and that is the one number it withholds."@en,
        "The protocol therefore requires the number of trials to be declared before the series begins. Choosing it afterwards would let the outcome pick its own measure, which is the failure mode pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate records after the fact and which is cheaper to prevent here. The same reasoning stands in examinations/notory-art-one-month.ttl and is not new; what is new is that here the threshold is not the compiler's."@en,
        "Two clauses of the same corpus would absorb a negative outcome if they were allowed to, and they are named so that a later reader can see whether they were: that no student however idle or stupid will fail to get some result, and that according to one's powers will it be demanded of one. Neither is part of the claim this protocol tests, and both stand at nodes of their own."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, working from the passages recorded in traditions/thelema.ttl; the rate, the threshold and the exclusion are the instruction's own" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A practitioner who keeps the practice as the instruction prescribes it — a pack of seventy-eight, shuffled and cut, the card drawn and named before it is seen, each call and each actual card written down — under the exclusion the instruction itself demands, that is with the pack handled so that shape, position and edge of the pips cannot be felt, and whose rate over a pre-declared number of trials is not above the one-in-seventy-eight the instruction states for the full call and the two-in-seven it states for the suit called as trumps every time. The instruction admits no degree at its own threshold: at three-in-four for the suit the practitioner is to be admitted for examination, so a series at chance after the training the instruction describes refutes the claim as the instruction makes it." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-28" ;
    pm:tests pmc:ThelemaProbatioTarotYieldsClarividentia .

pmp:ThelemaTerminusStatutusSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob eine thelemische Praxis eine Frist trägt, nach der das Ergebnis eingetreten sein soll"@de,
        "Whether a Thelemic practice is bounded by a term after which the result should have come"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-28" ;
    skos:note "The nearest thing to a term on a result is not in an instruction at all but in an editorial: the claim that in three years of the order's experience three months' work has not failed to produce at least one notable result in any man. That is a falsifiable statement with a term, and it was found and deliberately not entered as a node in this run, because it names no practice and no result and the editorial was read only at that place. It is the first thing a later run should pick up in these volumes."@en,
        "The separation was the work and the result is mixed. The corpus states many durations of exercise — an hour of breath, a week of each avoidance, a quarter of an hour thrice daily for at least one year, half an hour of concentration — and almost no term on a result. The two exceptions found are the saucer of water held for a whole hour, where the duration is itself the criterion, and the ten minutes at seventeen degrees, where a stated time produces a stated effect."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search for: for a whole hour, one hour, half an hour, ten minutes, a quarter of an hour, a week, a week or more, six months, one year, at least one year, three months, a year's study, after, within, until, proceed not, but not before. Every hit read in its place and sorted into duration of exercise and term on a result."@en ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:ThelemaMensuraPatinae,
        pmc:ThelemaPranayamaGradusInterdicti,
        pmc:ThelemaSignumSudorisDecemMinutorum,
        pmp:ThelemaNumerusUnoAspectu ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-28" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The Equinox, volume one, numbers one to ten (London 1909-1913), searched whole by term; and Book 4 (Liber ABA), Part I, chapters I, V, VI and VII, in the edition named at the nodes."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A term of the form the specification calls for: a period after which a result is to have appeared, or after which it is not to be expected. The corpus is full of durations, so the search had to separate the duration of an exercise from a term on its result."@en .

pmp:ThelemaTriplexIugum a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das dreifache Joch auf Rede, Handlung und Gedanke"@de,
        "The triple yoke on speech, action and thought"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber III vel Jugorum, chaps. I, II and III, §§ 1-3, in The Equinox I,4 (London, September 1910), pp. 11-14" ;
    skos:definition "Three sets of self-imposed avoidances, each to last a week or more. On speech: avoid a common word such as and, the or but; avoid a letter of the alphabet; avoid the pronouns and adjectives of the first person; and devise others. On action: avoid lifting the left arm above the waist; avoid crossing the legs. On thought: avoid a definite subject that commonly occupies much thought and is frequently stimulated by the senses or by the conversation of others; or create two personalities by a device such as changing a ring from one finger to another, the thoughts of each within limits entirely different from the other."@en ;
    skos:note "On each occasion of being betrayed into the thing sworn to avoid, the practitioner cuts himself sharply upon the wrist or forearm with a razor, and the instruction states the reason in a sentence that makes the practice an instrument: thine arm then serveth thee both for a warning and for a record. The daily progress is also written down."@en,
        "The self-inflicted injury is entered because it is the operative part of the stated procedure and because the count is carried on the body. This holding reports what the instruction prescribes; it does not recommend it, and the caution the instruction itself lacks here is one of the absences worth noting about it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberIIIVelJugorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmp:TheosophyCounterSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob irgendetwas im geoeffneten Werk die Zentren, die Vermoegen oder den Mechanismus bestreitet"@de,
        "Whether anything in the opened work denies the centres, the faculties or the mechanism"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-08" ;
    skos:note "Nothing denies the centres or the faculties, and two places qualify them from inside. The author declines the reading that each petal represents a moral quality, on the ground that he has met no facts confirming it and has met persons in whom the centres were active and the moral development was not; and in the second work he says the factors determining the number of spokes in a chakra are not yet known and that hundreds of observations and comparisons must be made, repeated and verified over and over again before much more can be said. Both are entered, the first as its own node and the second at pmc:TheosophyChakrams."@en,
        "The counter-search outside this transmission was not carried and the reason is that it would be a different strand: the claims of this file are reports of clairvoyant observation, and the material that bears against them is modern anatomy and the literature on the history of the subtle body, which enters this holding only through pm:evidenceFrom at a pm:Testing node and under the counter-search duty that SPEC 3 attaches to it. No pm:Testing node is written here, and its absence says what it should: nobody has taken up the examination."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchTerms "In the English: not, no facts, I have not, cannot, by no means, do not yet know, unexplained, must be made, repeated and verified, suggested, I am not able to see." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:TheosophyChakrams,
        pmc:TheosophyPetalaNonMoralia ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-08" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapters Force-Centres and The Serpent-Fire, pp. 286-303, read complete in the Theosophical Press printing of Chicago 1922, with C. W. Leadbeater, The Chakras, chapter II, pp. 10-16." ;
    pm:searchedFor "The negation of the claims entered in traditions/theosophy.ttl, sought first inside the opened work as SPEC 15 requires of every claim: a passage denying the seven centres, denying a faculty attributed to one of them, or withdrawing the mechanism by which the faculties are said to arise." .

pmp:TheosophyFalsificationSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das geoeffnete Werk eine Bedingung nennt, unter der das Wecken als nicht gelungen erwiesen waere"@de,
        "Whether the opened work names a condition under which the arousing would be shown not to have worked"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-08" ;
    skos:note "No falsifier, and the three places that stand where one would be are the reason the outcome is recorded as opposing and not as nothing. The fire is said to exist in seven layers, so that a man who exerts his will may affect one only, and when he thinks he has done the work may find it ineffective and have to do it again many times; the attainment is deferred, for most people, past the present incarnation and for the majority to a later round altogether; and the expectation of an exception is raised in advance and answered by locating the cause of a bad outcome in the practitioner's impatience. Each of the three accounts for an attempt that produced nothing, and together they leave no outcome that the practice could fail to produce."@en,
        "The one place where the source lets an observation count against a claim of its own is not about the practice but about the petals: the author reports the suggestion that each petal represents a moral quality, says he has met no facts confirming it, and reports persons in whom centres were in full activity though the moral development was by no means excellent. That is a stated counter-instance and it is entered at pmc:TheosophyPetalaNonMoralia. It is listed here because a search for falsifiers that reported only the three devices would miss the one thing in these chapters that behaves like evidence."@en,
        "What was not searched: Isis Unveiled and The Secret Doctrine, both named in the registration's principal corpus, both reachable, neither opened. A falsifier for this practice could in principle stand in either, and queries/unopened-works.rq carries them."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchTerms "In the English: ineffective, fail, failure, no result, over and over, again, many times, layers, degrees of force, partial, partially, temporary, exception, favouritism, incarnation, later round, due course, not yet met with any facts, by no means excellent." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:TheosophyDilatioIncarnationum,
        pmc:TheosophyIgnisStratificatus,
        pmc:TheosophyNullaExceptio ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-08" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapters Force-Centres, pp. 286-297, and The Serpent-Fire, pp. 298-303, both read complete in the Theosophical Press printing of Chicago 1922." ;
    pm:searchedFor "A term after which nothing further is to be expected, a sign of a failed attempt, a stated outcome whose absence would count against the practice: anything from which a pm:falsifiedBy could be formed. This is the question of the testability-driven entrance of SPEC 14, put to a transmission chosen on the structure-driven one." .

pmp:TheosophyMarkerVerificationSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Fussnote von 1916 mit dem Werk uebereinstimmt, das sie als Quelle nennt"@de,
        "Whether the 1916 footnote agrees with the work it names as its source"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-08" ;
    skos:note "Measured, and the result is mixed in a way worth having. The count of seven, the locations, the five radiation counts for the first five centres, the colours and the three faculties all stand in the named chapter, so the footnote is a faithful digest of them and the earlier node's reading of it is confirmed. The two assignments to the manual's own vocabulary, the throat centre to the element of mobility and the brow centre to the element of heat, are not in the source and were already recorded in the earlier node as the translator's own join; that part needed no correction."@en,
        "Recorded as opposing material rather than as a find, because what it opposes is this holding's own earlier reading. It does not show the 1916 footnote fabricated; it shows that the one safety statement in a Pali Text Society volume is a digest that moved its source's warning onto the steps its source prescribes. That is a sharper version of the finding the earlier node was written for and it is now checkable, because the source is open."@en,
        "Two points do not stand and both were corrected in originations/boran-theosophical-insertion.ttl in the same run. First, the sixth count. The source gives ninety-six for the brow centre as its own figure, attributes the figure two to the Indian books, and says that a count of undulations of the same character as the others gives forty-eight in each half; the footnote keeps two halves and drops the ninety-six, and the earlier node's marker list turned that into a radiation count of two standing beside the others. Second, and more consequential, the warning. The source warns against three centres that Blavatsky is said to call the lower centres and that are not among its seven; the footnote turns this into a warning against the centres below the navel, which in the source are the first two of the seven that are used and the first of which is where the fire is roused."@en,
        "What the search did not settle. The footnote names The Inner Life without an edition and this run read the Chicago printing of 1922, six years after the footnote; which printing the compiler had is therefore unestablished, and so is whether his wording tracked a different one. The two chapters are identical in substance to the treatment in The Chakras of 1927 on every count checked, which makes a divergent earlier printing unlikely but does not exclude it."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchTerms "In the English of both works: force-centre, force-centres, chakram, chakras, spokes, petals, radiations, undulations, serpent-fire, kundalini, base of the spine, navel, solar plexus, spleen, heart, throat, between the eyebrows, top of the head, thousand-petalled, nine hundred and sixty, ninety-six, clairvoyance, clairaudient, at a distance, magnification, unbroken, continuous, lower centres, great danger, greatest of misfortunes." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:TheosophyChakrams,
        pmc:TheosophyClairaudientia,
        pmc:TheosophyClairvoyantia,
        pmc:TheosophyConscientiaContinua,
        pmc:TheosophyMagnificatio,
        pmc:TheosophyPericulumKundalini ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-08" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter Force-Centres, pp. 286-297, read complete in the Theosophical Press printing of Chicago 1922, against the footnote in F. L. Woodward, Manual of a Mystic (Pali Text Society Translation Series VI, 1916), pp. 13-15, read complete; with C. W. Leadbeater, The Chakras, chapter II, pp. 12-16, read for the counts alone." ;
    pm:searchedFor "Whether each of the diagnostic markers that pmc:WoodwardCakkaInterpolatio in originations/boran-theosophical-insertion.ttl lists for the 1916 footnote stands in the work the footnote names as its source: the count of seven, the radiation counts, the colours, the faculties, the two assignments to the manual's own vocabulary, and the warning. That node was written to make a later presentation measurable against the markers, and nobody had opened the source." .

pmp:TokenGateFailureExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob ein Tor, das seinen eigenen Ausweis übergibt, je ein Misslingen an ihm nennt"@de,
        "Whether a gate that hands over its own token ever states a failure at it"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the first limb of the claim that where a transmission carries a graded passage in which the passer is admitted at each station by producing a token that the station itself or the transmission supplies him, it states no test at any station and names no case in which a passer failed to be admitted. The question put was the one the claim names as its own falsifier: whether the holding carries a token-handing passage that nevertheless states a test, names a refusal, or says what a wrong token would cost."@en ;
    skos:note "And the same passage states two failures at those stations. The gate shuts: as the traveller lingers with the second porter the castle bell rings, the porter tells him to run or all the pains and labour he has hitherto undergone will serve to no purpose, the lights above begin to go out, he gets in beside the virgin who extinguishes them, the gate is clapped to, part of his coat is locked out and left behind, and neither he nor they who stood ready without and called at the gate could prevail with the porter to open it again. The porter then hands the keys to the virgin. Second, and stated as a class rather than an event: the first porter tells him how easily he might have forgotten the letter of invitation, as it also chanced to others. That is a set of applicants who came to a token-handing station and lacked the token the transmission had issued them."@en,
        "The case, in the terms the claim uses. The second day of the Chymische Hochzeit carries a graded passage of three gates. At the first the porter demands the letter of invitation — which the bridegroom sent the traveller, so the transmission supplied it — is satisfied by it, sells him a golden token marked S.C. for his bottle of water, and gives him besides a sealed letter to the second porter. At the second the porter reads that letter, drives back a chained lion, and sells a token marked S.M. for his salt. At the third his name is written in a vellum book, the true guest token S.P.N. is given him, smaller and much heavier than the others, and new shoes are given while the old are to be handed to the poor. Nothing the traveller brought of himself opens any gate. The arrangement is the one the claim describes: the token of the next station is issued at the last."@en,
        "The second feature of the case is one the claim did not ask about and it belongs in the record. The refusal is put beyond the officiant: the porter cannot reopen the gate, and he divests himself of the keys in the same sentence. So the transmission states a failure at its own station and simultaneously removes the person at the station from the power to remit it. Both halves are in the operative passage, and this holding already carries the second half as a form of its own at pmc:TheExcuseThatStopsAtTheOfficiant, where it works the other way round."@en,
        "What the deviation costs the claim, put as narrowly as it can be. The first limb is falsified as written: there exists a token-handing passage that names a refusal at a station. What survives is the observation the limb was built on, which is that the four ascent witnesses state none — and the correction the case suggests is not that the limb be widened but that it be split. In the Jeu and Pistis Sophia material the token is read off the passer by the watchers, who do not speak with him; here it is sold to him at a counter and the transaction takes time. A station that transacts can be reached too late, and a station that reads cannot. That is a property of the gate and not of the tradition, and it is the shape a rewritten statement should have."@en,
        "What this protocol does not settle, and it is the part a later reader should not take from it. Whether the Chymische Hochzeit's gates have a documented route to the Hekhalot and kabbalistic material this holding carries was not established and no search for one was carried; the corpus is late, Western and syncretistic, and the Fama names its own antecedents freely. The falsification does not need independence — one clean case of the named kind ends the limb whatever its lineage — but a rewritten statement that wanted to count transmissions rather than cases would need it, and it is not here."@en ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundNothing ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "The counter-search here is a search for a reading under which the case is not a case, and it was carried before the verdict was set. Three ways out were looked for and none holds. First, that the gates of the Chymische Hochzeit are not token-handing: they are, and more strictly than the four witnesses of the first limb, because the token of each station is bought from that station for a consumable the traveller happens to be carrying — the bottle of water at the first, the salt at the second — and the letter to the second porter is written by the first. Second, that the refusal is not at the station: it is, the gate being the thing that shuts. Third, that the refusal stands in a frame and not in the operative passage: it does not, it stands in the same paragraph as the issuing of the second token and is reported of persons, those who stood ready without and called at the gate. Searched over the compiled holding besides for any further token-handing passage carrying a stated failure, over the five ascent traditions the claim's own counter-search went through; none was found, so this protocol adds one case and not a class."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-30" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A transmission in which a station admits the passer on a token the station or the text supplies, and which nevertheless states a test there, or names a case in which somebody was refused, or states what a wrong token would cost. That is the condition the claim itself named. It is met."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-30" ;
    pm:tests pmc:ThePassageThatPutsNoQuestion .

pmp:TurbaCeleritasNocetSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Turba eine körperliche Schädigung durch zu schnelles Vorgehen nennt"@de,
        "Whether the Turba states a bodily injury from proceeding faster than prescribed"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-26" ;
    skos:note "It also fixes the position of Dictum 37 in the holding's count of transmissions that name a physical hazard to the practitioner. The count is small, the passage is textually uncertain, and it is entered at pmc:TurbaCaveVenenaOperantem with both readings so that the count is not inflated by a variant."@en,
        "No such statement. Haste is warned against four times — Dictum 26, 29, 39 and 61 — and in every one of them what is damaged is the work, not the man: it becomes red prematurely, the spirit perishes, they are deprived of sense and motion, the light is extinguished, the work is corrupted. The one place where the operator's own body is at risk is Dictum 37, and the cause there is a wrong separation and not a wrong pace, and even that reading holds only on the longer recension; the shorter one has the body in the vessel perish and not the operator."@en,
        "Why the empty result is a finding and not a mismatch of scope. The statement about bodily injury from haste is held of transmissions prescribing a technique performed on the practitioner's own body, and the Turba's operation is performed on matter in a vessel. So this search establishes the boundary of that statement from the outside rather than testing it: a transmission with graded stages, day-counts and repeated warnings against haste states no bodily injury when the graded thing is not the body. That is a difference the statement predicts and had not been shown."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Read at the wording and checked by search for: hasty, haste, hastily, speedily, over speedily, patience, patient, weary, sickness, sick, dies, death, perish, poison, poisons, venom get at, harm, disaster, sadness, injure, injury, body perish."@en ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-26" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Turba philosophorum, the whole work as above (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A statement that the operator who goes faster than the work's grades is injured in his body, and in particular that the injury is the disorder the work claims to remove. The question is put because the holding carries a statement to that effect about transmissions prescribing a graded bodily technique, and the Turba prescribes graded stages with day-counts and warns repeatedly against haste, so it is the nearest thing to a case outside the bodily techniques."@en .

pmp:TurbaMateriaeProbaSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Turba irgendeine Weise nennt, zu wissen, dass ein Stoff das ist, wofür er gilt"@de,
        "Whether the Turba states any way of knowing that a material is what it is called"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-26" ;
    skos:note "Nothing of the kind anywhere in the work. The two acts of assay the Turba does state — the powder taken in the hand at Dictum 28 and the look for the powder at Dictum 13 — both judge the state of the work in progress and neither judges an ingredient. The one sentence that comes near is at Dictum 36, nothing enters therein unless it be pure, and it states a requirement without any way of meeting it. Dictum 16 requires lead of the best quality and says nothing about how best quality is recognised."@en,
        "What the empty result is worth, and it is worth more than an absence usually is. This holding has carried since 2026-09-22 the statement that an operative handbook assays its materials and not its operation, with the assays of the operation as the provided-for exception. The Turba is the mirror image: two assays, both of the operation, and not one of a material. It is the first handbook in the holding of which that is true, and the finding is not that the statement is refuted but that a handbook can consist entirely of what the statement treats as the exceptional case. Recorded at pmc:TheTestOnTheIngredient."@en,
        "Where a run should go next, and it is named in the file. The Summa perfectionis magisterii is reported to carry exactly the catalogue that is missing here, nine assays of the metal announced as a list and then worked through, and it stands unopened at pmw:SummaPerfectionisMagisterii in traditions/latin-alchemy.ttl. If the report holds, the pair Turba-Summa is one transmission holding both arrangements, which is the sharpest evidence a corpus can give that the device is chosen and not generic."@en,
        "Why the absence is a property of the work and not of the translation. A test of a material is a physical act with an outcome, and such acts survive translation better than anything else in an alchemical text; Waite's English renders the two assays of the operation without difficulty, so it would have rendered an assay of a material. The Latin was not read and the finding is stated against Waite's English, which is what the date and the corpus line say."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "The whole work was read at the wording, and the reading was checked by term search of the transcription, case-insensitive, for: know that it is, thou shalt know, ye shall know, to test, the test, prove it, proof, if ye find, look and see, take in the hand, true nature of, genuine, pure, unless it be pure, and every occurrence of know, test and find in the operative dicta. Every hit was read in its place."@en ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-26" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Turba philosophorum, the whole work — the epistle of Arisleus, the seventy-two dicta and the conclusion of Agmon (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension, with the shorter recension in Waite's notes)."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "An act with a definite perceptible outcome by which the operator establishes that one of the substances the work names is genuine, pure, or the thing the work means by the name: a test of the quicksilver, of the vinegar, of the orpiment, of the lead, of the copper the work calls ours. The question is put because the work prescribes upwards of thirty named substances under a vocabulary it itself says was multiplied in order to deceive, so a way of knowing that what is in the vessel is what the recipe called for would be the first thing an operator needed."@en .

pmp:TurbaTerminusDesistendiSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Turba eine Frist, Zahl oder ein Zeichen nennt, bei dem der Übende aufhören soll"@de,
        "Whether the Turba names a term, count or sign at which the operator is to stop"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-26" ;
    skos:note "Everything else the search turned up is a duration and not a term of abandonment, and the distinction is the whole point. The work has: seven days at Dictum 12 and 16, twenty-one and ten and ten at 16, a hundred and fifty at 16, forty at 18 twice, forty and forty-two at 21 and 22, a hundred at 23, forty at 26 and 33 and 37 and 72, forty-two at 68, a hundred and eighty at 58, and the second work performed between the tenth of September and the tenth degree of Libra at 68. Every one of them says how long to continue. Not one says when to stop."@en,
        "Nor does the work leave the case unaddressed; it addresses it four times and each time in the operator. Blame no one save yourselves at Dictum 17, God has concealed the truth from you at 29, the man who mistook the slow redness for an error at 27, the man who failed of haste and sadness at 39, and over all of them the sentence at 39 that the truth is not discerned except after error. Those are the nodes pmc:TurbaCulpaInOperante, pmc:TurbaExemplumRelinquentis, pmc:TurbaFestinantiaCausaDefectus and pmc:TurbaVeritasPostErrorem, and together they are why no term of abandonment could stand in this work: a term would have to survive the sentence that error precedes the truth, and it could not."@en,
        "One passage and one only, and it is half of what was sought. Dictum 21 puts an upper bound on a state of the matter — see that the blackness does not remain longer than forty days — which is the single sentence in the work at which a dated fact about a batch could be established by the operator alone. What the work does not do anywhere is say what follows from the bound being exceeded. So the outcome of this search is a find, and the find is a bound without a consequence."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "The whole work read at the wording, and the reading checked by term search for: if not, but if, otherwise, unless, in vain, profit you nothing, accomplish nothing, effect nought, err, error, relinquish, give up, cease, desist, forty days, forty-two, hundred days, hundred and fifty, one hundred and eighty, and every numeral in the operative dicta. Every hit read in its place."@en ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:TurbaNigredoNonUltraQuadraginta ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-26" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Turba philosophorum, the whole work as above (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage telling the operator what he is to conclude, or to do, or to stop doing, when a stated term has passed and the stated result has not come: a number of attempts after which the recipe is wrong, a state of the matter that settles that this batch will not come to anything, a sign of failure as against the several signs of success the work does give."@en .

pmp:Tzite a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Losen mit Maiskörnern und Tzite-Samen"@de,
        "Casting the lot with maize grains and tzite seeds"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Popol Vuh: The Sacred Book of the Ancient Quiché Maya, English version by Delia Goetz and Sylvanus G. Morley from the Spanish translation of Adrián Recinos (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1950), part I, chapter 2" ;
    skos:definition "A divination performed with grains of maize and the seeds of the tzite tree, carried out by the diviner pair Xpiyacoc and Xmucane at the request of the makers, in order to learn whether the men to be carved of wood would turn out. The seeds are addressed in the second person and told to come together, to take hold of one another and to speak, so that the answer is given by things spoken to and not by things merely read."@en ;
    skos:note "The same procedure survives in the highlands as the office of the ajq'ij, the daykeeper who counts tzite seeds. The colonial spellings tzite and tzité and the modern tz'ite' stand for one word and one seed of the coral tree; a search under only one of them misses most of the literature."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MayaRitual .

pmp:UdughulFailureConditionSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die kanonische Dämonenserie eine Misslingensbedingung nennt"@de,
        "Whether the canonical evil-demon series names a condition of failure"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-15" ;
    skos:note "One interval was found and it is not a term: the twelfth tablet has an operation persist for one day and the scapegoat drawn to the body in the evening. A duration prescribed for an act is not a deadline after which the act counts as failed, and the difference is the whole of what this search was for."@en,
        "The reading of the must not occurrences is the part of this search worth keeping, and it became a node of its own at pmc:UdughulProhibitionsAddressTheDemon. A prohibition spoken to a demon cannot be obeyed or broken by anyone whose conduct could be observed, so no case can ever be assigned to a breach of it. The series therefore has prohibitions in quantity and none that bears on whether a performance was correct."@en,
        "The two conditions the craft does put on its operator were found outside the series and are entered at the tradition: the ritual appended to an Assur tablet, which has the exorcist anoint himself before approaching, and the colophon of the second tablet of the diagnostic series, which forbids approaching before the spell has been cast on oneself. Both protect the exorcist and neither says anything about whether the treatment took."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-volume search of the English translation and apparatus, in both the spaced and the unspaced normalisation, for: in vain; no avail; without success; fruitless; ineffective; has no effect; does not work; if it does not; if he does not; fail; failure; unsuccessful; repeat the ritual; repeat the incantation; recite it again; a second time; twice more; if the demon returns; returns again. Zero hits for every term except a second time, which occurs twice, both times in the editor's notes on manuscript duplication and not in a rite. Separately, every occurrence of must not in the volume was read in context: all of them are addressed to the demon and none to the operator or the patient." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-15" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Udug-hul / Utukku Lemnutu, all sixteen canonical tablets in the complete critical edition with English translation: M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons: Canonical Udug-hul Incantations (BAM 8, Boston/Berlin: De Gruyter 2016), searched over the whole volume in the open-access full text" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A place at which the series says what it would look like for one of its rites not to have worked: a sign of non-effect, a term after which nothing further is to be expected, an instruction to repeat because the first attempt did not take, or a named fault in the performance that voids it. Anything, that is, from which a pm:falsifiedBy could be formed." .

pmp:UdughulPrognosisSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die kanonische Dämonenserie eine Prognose stellt"@de,
        "Whether the canonical evil-demon series states a prognosis"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-15" ;
    skos:note "Its weight is the weight of the terms and of one translation. Everything searched was English: a prognosis phrased in the Akkadian of the partitur without an English rendering beside it would not have been caught, and neither would one the editor rendered in wording nobody would search for. The negative is entered at that strength and not above it."@en,
        "The finding is a silence and a sharp one, because the same craft has a prognostic corpus and this series is not it. The diagnostic series, redacted inside this tradition and attributed to a named editor, consists of little else than statements of the form he will recover and he will die. The two stand in one curriculum, listed one after another in the exorcist's manual. What the search establishes is that the prediction lives in one of the two and not in the other, so that the craft's examinable half and its operative half are different texts."@en,
        "What the search does not cover, and it is the more important gap: the diagnostic series itself was not opened. Its standard edition and its modern re-edition are both print-only for this workstation, and only the catalogue of the series is open access. The silence recorded here is the silence of the incantation series, never of the craft."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-volume search of the English translation and apparatus for: recover; he will live; he will die; will get well; get well; be well; become well; well again; convalesce; survive; restore to health; third day; seventh day; on the third; after three days; prognosis; apodosis; death of the patient. Each term was run twice, once over the text normalised to letters and spaces and once over the text with all spacing removed, because the full text loses word boundaries in places and renders some capitals as lower-case letters, so that a search on a spaced form alone would report a silence that belongs to the scan. Three hits for be well, none of them a prediction; two for prognosis, both in the editor's own discussion of the diagnostic series; zero for every other term, in both passes." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-15" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Udug-hul / Utukku Lemnutu, all sixteen canonical tablets in the complete critical edition with English translation: M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons: Canonical Udug-hul Incantations (BAM 8, Boston/Berlin: De Gruyter 2016), 729 pages, searched over the whole volume in the open-access full text" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A place in the series at which the outcome of an affliction or of a treatment is predicted: that the patient will recover, that he will live, that he will die, or that either will happen by a stated day. This is the form the diagnostic series of the same craft is built out of, and the question is whether the incantation series carries it too." .

pmp:UgarDbhTaNkt a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die drei Opferhandlungen des Einheitsritus"@de,
        "The three sacrificial acts of the unity rite"@en ;
    dcterms:source "RS 1.002:23'-26' and 32'-35' (KTU 1.40), in Pardee, Ritual and Cult at Ugarit, WAW 10, 2002, text 22, pp. 82-83" ;
    skos:definition "The sequence each section of the rite performs after its enumeration of sins: the sacrifice is sacrificed, the tʻ-sacrifice is offered on a ram, the slaughtering is done; and the result is to be borne to the father of the sons of ʼIlu, to the Circle and the Assembly of the Sons of ʼIlu and to Tukamuna-wa-Šunama, with the animal named at the end — here is the ram, here is the donkey."@en ;
    skos:note "The three acts stand in the same order in every section, which the editor reads as the vertical structure of the text crossing its horizontal division into paragraphs for the male and the female inhabitants. The donkey of rectitude is the one whose term survives, and the editor connects its killing to an old Amorite practice at the making of covenants."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RS1002 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:UgariticRitual .

pmp:UgarFailureConditionSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das ugaritische Ritualkorpus eine Bedingung nennt, unter der ein Ritus nicht gewirkt hat"@de,
        "Whether the Ugaritic ritual corpus names a condition under which a rite has not worked"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-06" ;
    skos:note "Nothing of the kind, and the editor's own framing is consistent with that: he states of the expiation rite that there are no indications in the texts themselves as to its frequency and that chronological data on the liturgy are not forthcoming, which is a different absence of the same kind. What the corpus supplies in place of a failure condition is an apparatus for finding out whether things stand well at all — the divination section prints liver and lung models, an astrological report and four omen manuals — and that apparatus is addressed to the question before the rite and not to the outcome after it."@en,
        "What the empty result is worth, and it is worth more than it looks. The holding has been counting transmissions that announce signs of success against transmissions that name conditions of failure, and the count is only meaningful if a negative is established on a read corpus rather than assumed from an unread one. This corpus is now read in its one complete open edition and contributes a zero that is a property of the corpus. It does not mean the practitioners had no way of telling; it means the texts gave them none."@en,
        "What was not searched, so the negative is bounded. The second substantially preserved exemplar of the expiation rite and its four fragments are not printed in this edition. The Rapiuma tablets are not in it at all, for the reason at pmp:UgarRapiumaSearch. And the corpus of letters, legal and administrative texts, which is the bulk of what Ugarit produced, is outside this edition and was not looked at; a clause of this kind would be a surprise there but is not excluded."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "The five tablets read paragraph by paragraph. Across the whole volume, term search over the extracted text for fail, failure, unsuccessful, in vain, repeat, repetition, again, second time, if not, otherwise, remedy, expiation of a failed, and for the editor's own vocabulary of outcome; the section headings of all nine sections read, and the divination section read through its table of contents, since that is where a sign of whether things stand well would sit in this corpus."@en ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-06" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The complete open-access critical edition of the corpus: D. Pardee, Ritual and Cult at Ugarit, Writings from the Ancient World 10, Society of Biblical Literature 2002, 314 pages, transliteration and translation with introductions and notes, read as the publisher's own posted file; its table of contents read in full, its nine sections identified, and five tablets read at the wording — RS 24.266, RS 1.002, RS 24.244, RS 24.272 and RIH 78/20."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A clause anywhere in the corpus telling the operator or the beneficiary what would show that a rite had not worked, and what then follows: a term after which nothing further comes, a sign of failure, a count completed without effect, a stated remedy for a failed performance, a provision for repetition. The same question was put to the Anatolian corpus in the strand that ran beside this one and found one case there."@en .

pmp:UgarHrnArar a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die vier genannten Mittel, mit denen das Gift ausgetrieben wird"@de,
        "The four named materials with which the venom is driven out"@en ;
    dcterms:source "RS 24.244:61-67 (KTU 1.100), in Pardee, Ritual and Cult at Ugarit, WAW 10, 2002, text 53, p. 178" ;
    skos:definition "What the twelfth addressee does when the message reaches him: he goes to a named place in the east, casts a tamarisk from among the trees and the tree of death from among the bushes, and with the tamarisk he expels the venom, with the fruit stalk of a date palm he banishes it, with the succulent part of a reed he makes it pass on, and with the carrier he carries it away."@en ;
    skos:note "Four materials and four verbs, one each, and the materials are ordinary plants that a practitioner could have. That is what distinguishes this section from the eleven addresses above it: the first part of the work names whom to ask, and this part names what to use."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RS24244 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:UgariticRitual .

pmp:UgarProvisionSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das ugaritische Korpus die Vorsorge für das Nichtwissen führt"@de,
        "Whether the Ugaritic corpus carries the standing provision for not knowing what is being dealt with"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-06" ;
    skos:note "The first form, and the most complete instance of it read anywhere in this holding. The expiation rite enumerates, three times over and in each of its three sections, every party whose statement could have caused the sin — six foreign peoples and two of the kingdom's own underclasses and one further name — then every disposition in which it could have been committed, then both of the cultic acts in which it could have been committed. The officiant needs to know none of it."@en,
        "The node is entered and the generalization is not enlarged, and the reason is the same one the Anatolian strand recorded on the same day for its own candidate. One of the six witnesses already standing is the Mesopotamian exorcistic craft; this corpus names Tuttul and Mari among the seats of the deities it addresses in another of its tablets, so its contact with that material is in its own text. A witness that cannot be shown independent of a standing witness raises no count. What it could raise is the count of stations with a reworking, and that needs the added element entered first."@en,
        "What the two candidates of this run do establish together, which is not a count. The device appears, in the same century and in two neighbouring kingdoms that name each other, in a plague ritual that addresses an unidentified hostile deity and in an expiation that enumerates every possible accuser. Whether that is one technique twice or two inventions is exactly the question the contact route of both traditions leaves open, and both are at pm:contactRouteDisputed."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "The disjunctive construction of the expiation rite read in all three of its sections and in all three of its enumerations; and over the volume, term search for whether, be it, whoever, whatever, unknown, and for the editor's renderings of the interrogative particle of this corpus."@en ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:UgarHta ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-06" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The same complete open-access edition of 2002, with the five tablets read at the wording and the expiation rite's three parallel sections read in all their repetitions."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "The device in generalizations/the-provision-for-not-knowing.ttl, in any of its three forms: a disjunction that names every candidate at once so that the identification need not be made, a form of words for the case of not knowing, or an apparatus of objects that answers the same encounters without a name."@en .

pmp:UgarRapiumaSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob eine Transliteration der Rapiuma-Tafeln erreichbar ist"@de,
        "Whether a transliteration of the Rapiuma tablets is reachable"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-06" ;
    skos:note "Entered as the corpus being unreachable and not as nothing found, because the works exist, are named, are numbered and are closed. The distinction is the one the scale was built for: a corpus that was read and is silent and a corpus that could not be opened are opposite findings about the same gap."@en,
        "Nothing. The 2002 edition is a volume of cult and ritual and does not contain the narrative poetry, and the companion volume of the same series that does is lending-restricted; so are the 1963 corpus in both volumes, the three handbooks, the 1956 collection and the concordance. The directory of the posting publisher answers with a refusal while the single file beneath it answers normally, and the filename pattern that reaches the one volume does not reach the other. The one reachable treatment of these three tablets gives a translation with notes and no transliterated text, and the standard corpus edition of the language is not open at any address tested."@en,
        "The two routes that remain, named so that a later run does not repeat this search. A library copy of the lending volume; or the edition project that lists these fragments in its own plan with a date of 2026. Not a further copy of the same volumes on the same archive: the restriction is on the item and not on the scan."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:corpusUnreachable ;
    pm:searchTerms "Each candidate work looked for by author and title, and for every hit the archive's own metadata read before the full text was requested, because the restriction flag in that metadata predicts the authorisation error and saves the attempt. The 2002 edition's contents list checked for the three tablets by excavation siglum and by corpus number. The series directory of the posting publisher probed for a second posted volume by the filename pattern that works for the first."@en ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-06" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The reachable editions of the Ugaritic corpus: the open-access critical edition of 2002 and its table of contents; the open full-text archive, by author and title for the standard corpus of alphabetic cuneiform texts of 1963 in both its text and its plate volume, for the three successive handbooks and textbooks of the language, for the 1956 collection of myths and legends, for the concordance of 1972 and for the modern volume of narrative poetry; the publisher-hosted series directory under which the 2002 edition is posted; a mirrored manual of the language with autograph hand copies; the page-image and optical-text routes of a journal archive for the editiones principes; and the project site of the edition now in preparation, with its tablet concordance."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A continuous transliteration of KTU 1.20, 1.21 and 1.22, the three tablets the registration of this tradition named as the Rapiuma texts and which are one of the two reasons it was registered."@en .

pmp:Ujjayi a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Ujjāyī"@de,
        "Ujjāyī"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.51-53 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "A retention in which the mouth is held closed and the breath is drawn through both nostrils so that it grazes the throat and sounds as it passes."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmp:UnluckyDaysExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Prüfung der Tafel der unglücklichen Tage"@de,
        "Examination of the table of unlucky days"@en ;
    skos:note "No cases and therefore no counter-search state. Setting one would claim a search of the opposing literature that has not been carried."@en,
        "The claim is unusually well protected against the commonest objection to statistical folklore, that the days are too many to be missed. They are about thirty-two out of three hundred and sixty-five, unevenly distributed, with seven in January and one each in June and October. The unevenness is what makes the count informative: a uniform sprinkling would be hard to distinguish from chance in a small sample, and this is not uniform."@en,
        "Three separate conditions and not one, because the table attaches three different consequences to the same set of days and any of the three could hold while the others fail. A protocol that folded them together would report a single verdict on three claims."@en,
        "What the protocol cannot do is decide whether the claim belongs to this transmission at all. The book marks the table as drawn from a hundred-year almanac out of Germany and signs the marking; the almanac is the bearer and the handbook the reprinter. An examination that refuted the table would refute the almanac, and the handbook would be left having quoted it accurately."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-13" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "No raised rate of poverty among those born on the listed days, no raised rate of failure to recover among those who fall ill on them, and no raised rate of later destitution among those who become engaged or marry on them, measured against all other days of the same year in the same population."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-13" ;
    pm:tests pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicUngluecklicheTage .

pmp:UnluckyDaysTableInRomanusSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Tafel der unglücklichen Tage aus dem Romanus-Büchlein stammt"@de,
        "Whether the table of unlucky days comes from the Romanus-Büchlein"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-13" ;
    skos:note "Not carried: the hundred-year almanac itself, in any of its printings. That is the corpus this table belongs to, and it is not in this holding under any label. See prima-materia#519."@en,
        "The booklet has no calendar of any kind. Its uses of unglück are all in charm titles against misfortune, and its uses of geboren are all in the formula about the hour in which Christ was born."@en,
        "The question the search was carried for is in any case answered by the handbook itself, which names its own source for this one piece: a hundred-year almanac coming out of Germany, marked as such and signed with the author's name. The search records that the other candidate in the issue was put the question and gave nothing, which the handbook's self-attribution does not by itself establish."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search for: unglück, Unglückstage, Tage, Tag, geboren, Kalender, hundertjährig, Ader, Aderlass, Zeichen, Jungfrau; unlucky, days, born, almanac, calendar, bleed, bloodletting, zodiac, Virgin, Scorpion." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-13" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Romanus-Büchlein (Glatz: Franz Pompejus, 1788), in the bilingual edition of Joseph H. Peterson, 2006; whole text searched" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A calendar of named unlucky days with consequences for birth, illness, betrothal or travel — the source of pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicUngluecklicheTage, which prima-materia#519 left undecided between the booklet, a bloodletting calendar and the medieval Egyptian days." .

pmp:UptakeThroughTheHandSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der Baopuzi ein Aufnehmen von Qi aus einem anderen Menschen über die Hand kennt"@de,
        "Whether the Baopuzi has qi taken up out of another person through the hand"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-10" ;
    skos:note "See prima-materia#328. The issue keeps its korpus:daoistisch label and stays open, because the label is the record of coverage and this search adds one corpus to it rather than exhausting the plausible ones."@en,
        "The direction is the finding, and it is uniform. Every operation in this work in which the hand and the qi appear together sends outward: the blowing over one's own hand at a distance in juan 8, and the subduing of a snake in juan 17, where the practitioner faces the sun, takes three breaths and holds them, props the tongue against the palate and pinches a hand posture. Nothing takes anything in, and nothing takes anything out of a person. A transmission that describes the outward operation in detail and never mentions the inward one is saying something, and it is not the same thing as a transmission nobody has asked."@en,
        "What the search cannot say. It was carried in the Chinese over one edition and one set of characters, and a technical term for the intake that this vocabulary does not contain would not have been found. The one result that would carry beyond this work is the absence of 勞宮 anywhere in it, since that is the term the claim would be expected to attach to; but the term belongs to the medical corpus, and its absence here bears on this work only."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of all twenty juan for: 掌 (palm, seven hits, all figurative or lexical: ruling in the palm, holding in the palm, dragon's liver and tiger's palm as a drug name); 掌心 and 手心 (palm centre, none); 勞宮 (the point in the palm centre, none); 取人 (four hits, all about taking a man's position, wealth or wife, in the list of transgressions of juan 6 and in juan 10); 受人, 布炁, 奪人炁 (none); 以手 (one hit, juan 17); 摩 (rubbing, five hits, all pharmaceutical or lexical)." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-10" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian, twenty juan, in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185; searched over the whole work in the Chinese, and read at the wording where a hit fell" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage in which qi, energy or vitality is taken up out of another person, with the hand as the place of entry — the claim of prima-materia#328, whose second half is that the entry point is the left hand." .

pmp:VedicExpiationSignSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Brāhmaṇas ein Zeichen dafür nennen, dass eine Sühne gewirkt hat"@de,
        "Whether the Brāhmaṇas name a sign that an expiation has taken effect"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-19" ;
    skos:note "Limits, and they are real. The performance literature was not opened: the expiation is a book of its own in the Śrautasūtras, Kātyāyana 25 and Āpastamba 9, and Eggeling's notes refer to Kātyāyana at exactly the places searched. A manual written for the man who has to do the thing may well say when he may stop. The finding is about the exegesis and says nothing about the sūtras."@en,
        "No sign anywhere, and one sentence in the opposite direction. The standing form of the expiation book is a question — they also say, if such and such were to happen, what rite and what expiation would there be in that case? — an act, and the closing formula this, then, is the rite performed in that case. Some twenty-two of these stand in four chapters. The only statement about efficacy the run found is general and unconditional: no hurt and no harm of any kind befalls where that expiation is made. Beside it stands the clause for everything not enumerated, the three utterances which the text calls all-expiatory and of which it says that he thus makes expiation with all this universe."@en,
        "This is the exhibited gap the class exists for, and it is not a gap in the reading. A corpus that lists its accidents this carefully has plainly attended to the question of what can go wrong; the silence is about how one would know it had been put right, and it is complete over six volumes."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of all six volumes on the whitespace-normalised text for: expiation (69 in the five Śatapatha volumes, 130 in Keith); expiatory (36 and 5); in vain (9 and 1, every one inspected and none about a rite that did not take); of no avail, did not avail, availed not, unavailing (none anywhere); failed (6 and 0, all inspected — five are the Vaiśvānara dialogue of Śatapatha X,6,1 and one is a simile about a stronghold gate); sign of (11 and 12); token (2 and 1); thereby he knows, he knows that the expiation, sign of the expiation (none). In addition the four chapters of the expiation book, Śatapatha XII,4,1 to XII,4,4, were read continuously, and so were Aitareya vii,2 to vii,12 in outline." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-19" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa (Mādhyandina) in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 12, 26, 41, 43 and 44, Oxford 1882-1900, kāṇḍas I to XIV complete; and Aitareya and Kauṣītaki Brāhmaṇas in the English of Arthur Berriedale Keith, Rigveda Brahmanas, Harvard Oriental Series 25, Cambridge 1920, complete" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Anywhere in either corpus: a sign, mark or event by which a performer would know that a prescribed expiation had taken effect; or an interval within which the harm the expiation averts would otherwise have arrived; or a case in which an expiation was made and failed. The expiation is the corpus's own repair for its own accidents, and it is prescribed case by case with great care; whether it is also given a criterion decides whether the whole apparatus of stated failure conditions is examinable or only enumerable." .

pmp:VedicPenaltyCaseSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Brāhmaṇas einen Fall berichten, in dem der angedrohte Schaden wirklich eintrat"@de,
        "Whether the Brāhmaṇas report a case of the threatened harm actually arriving"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-19" ;
    skos:note "Limits. One translation each, and the Kāṇva recension of the Śatapatha, which is not a variant of the Mādhyandina numbering but a separate text, was not obtained in any form. Eggeling's rendering decides the search terms: he writes recurring death where Keith writes repeated death, and a search in either wording alone loses half the corpus."@en,
        "One case in six volumes, and the search is the only reason it is in the holding: it stands in a chapter on the heads of the victims that no part of this run had any reason to read. Some obtain the heads without performing the animal sacrifice and put them on the altar; they who do this become mortal carcases; in this way they did put them on for Aṣāḍhi Sauśromateya, but quickly indeed he died after that."@en,
        "What the negative half of the finding is worth. Four occurrences of the word died in fourteen kāṇḍas, of which one is a dyed ox-hide, one a wind dying away, one the immortality passage of X,4,3, and one this case. A corpus this insistent about what follows a fault records the following of it once. That is a statement about the genre — the Brāhmaṇas argue from equivalences and not from instances — and it bounds what any protocol built on them can expect to find."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of all six volumes on the whitespace-normalised text for: died (4 in the Śatapatha, 3 in Keith, every one inspected); he perished, came to ruin, was ruined, his cattle died, his eldest son died, the sacrificer died, his head flew off, head fell off (none anywhere); before long (5 in the Śatapatha, all in the dialogue of Śauceya and Uddālaka at XI,5,3, all of them statements of the rule and none a report); within a year, within the year, within a month, within ten days, on the tenth day (15 across the six volumes, inspected, none of them a term set to a penalty). The line-based counts before normalisation were 1 per volume for died, which is the artefact described in the header of this file."@en ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:VaidikaAsadhimrtyu ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-19" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa (Mādhyandina) in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 12, 26, 41, 43 and 44, Oxford 1882-1900, kāṇḍas I to XIV complete; and Aitareya and Kauṣītaki Brāhmaṇas in the English of Arthur Berriedale Keith, Rigveda Brahmanas, Harvard Oriental Series 25, Cambridge 1920, complete" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage reporting that a named human being committed one of the ritual faults this corpus attaches a consequence to, and that the consequence followed. The corpus states such consequences by the dozen — the eldest son dies, the master of the house dies, the cattle die, the line fails, the sacrificer becomes worse, is confused, is likely to die — and a single reported instance is worth more to an examination than any number of further statements of the rule." .

pmp:VedicPunarmrtyuWordingSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Entkommen vom wiederkehrenden Tod noch etwas anderem als dem Altar zugeschrieben wird"@de,
        "Whether the escape from the death that comes again is credited to anything besides the altar"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-19" ;
    skos:note "Not searched: the Kāṇva recension, and the Upaniṣadic parts of kāṇḍa XIV beyond the passages the other searches touched, where the term is known to recur in a different argument."@en,
        "Spread, and over a wide range of cost. Nine places in the six volumes, every one read at the wording. In the Śatapatha: the piled altar with its ten thousand eight hundred bricks at X,4,3; the altar built above the sun rather than on this side of it, in kāṇḍa X, where the building is said to be done thereabove by the builder's knowledge; two places in the same kāṇḍa where the escape is credited to knowing an equivalence and to nothing else, one of them adding that he attains the whole life; the daily recitation of one's lesson at XI,5,6,9, where a single divine word suffices on a day when nothing else is possible; a place in kāṇḍa XII where passing from the imperishable to the imperishable does it; and the Sautrāmaṇī at XII,9,3,11 and 12, which wards off the recurring death of a man's cattle and of his dead, and which does so for him for whom the rite is performed as well as for him who knows. In the Kauṣītaki, the Viṣuvant day: without it are hunger and repeated death, and they conquer hunger and repeated death who perform it, at xxv,1 in Keith's page 486."@en,
        "The finding bears on the condition and not on the claim. X,4,3 is the only place where the corpus reports its own failures and names an exact count as what ends them, and it is the passage a reader would take for the corpus's considered position. Four other places give the same result for operations that meet no such condition. Whatever the count at X,4,3 is doing, it is not the condition on which escape from the death that comes again is generally made to depend."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of all six volumes on the whitespace-normalised text for: recurring death and recurrent death, which is Eggeling's rendering; repeated death, which is Keith's; death again, dies again, come to life again; immortality (29 in the Śatapatha volumes, inspected around the passages found). Eggeling's and Keith's renderings do not overlap, and a search in one alone returns nothing from the other corpus." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:VaidikaSvadhyayaPunarmrtyujaya ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-19" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa (Mādhyandina) in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 12, 26, 41, 43 and 44, Oxford 1882-1900, kāṇḍas I to XIV complete; and Aitareya and Kauṣītaki Brāhmaṇas in the English of Arthur Berriedale Keith, Rigveda Brahmanas, Harvard Oriental Series 25, Cambridge 1920, complete" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Every operation to which either corpus credits escape from punarmṛtyu, the death that comes again. The question is whether the claim is tied to the one expensive operation that states an exact condition — the piling of the fire-altar with its counted bricks — or is spread over operations of very different cost, because the second would mean that the condition stated at X,4,3 governs nothing." .

pmp:VisuddhimaggaAttritionElsewhereSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Handbuch außerhalb des zwölften Kapitels irgendwo eine Ausfallzahl nennt"@de,
        "Whether the handbook puts a number on failure anywhere but in the twelfth chapter"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-24" ;
    skos:note "The form stands alone. A handbook that grades everything else it treats — the absorptions, the kinds of persons, the sixteen steps of breathing, the ten imperfections — declines to grade its own success rate everywhere except here, and here it does it seven times in a row. That is a gap worth exhibiting rather than a curiosity: whoever wants to know how often these transmissions concede that their instruction fails has, in this corpus, exactly one place to go."@en,
        "The search does not settle whether the sentence is Buddhaghosa's own or taken from the Sinhalese commentaries he abridges, and this run could not settle it, because those commentaries are lost. It is noted because the answer would change what the passage is evidence of."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search, normalised to bare letters. Searched for: one in a hundred (seven hits, and all seven stand in the twelfth chapter, six in one paragraph and the seventh two paragraphs later); one in a thousand (no hits, the phrase being carried inside the first); out of a hundred (none); hundreds or thousands (none); few can (none); rarely (two, neither about a rate of attainment). The three further occurrences of hundred or a thousand in the same chapter were read and are the number of forms the operator creates, not a proportion." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-24" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga, the whole of the twenty-three chapters, in the English of Ñāṇamoli, The Path of Purification, Colombo 1956, searched as one text" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A second place in the handbook where a proportion of those who attempt a practice is said to succeed or to fail. The handbook describes some forty meditation subjects and the whole graded course to sanctity, so if the rate of attrition stated seven times over in the twelfth chapter were a habit of this author rather than a single passage, a second place would exist. The answer decides whether pmc:SatesuSahassesuVaEko is a form this transmission uses or a sentence it used once." .

pmp:VisuddhimaggaBelowFourthAbsorptionSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Kapitel sagt, was dem widerfährt, der die Kräfte unterhalb der vierten Vertiefung versucht"@de,
        "Whether the chapter says what happens to one who attempts the powers from below the fourth absorption"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-24" ;
    skos:note "That matters for what the chapter can be tested against. A transmission that named an injury, a wrong result or a delay would have said something a run could fail at; this one says that the unqualified simply do not get started, which is not an outcome anybody could observe from outside."@en,
        "The note at pmc:IddhiPresupposesFourthJhana is a property of the chapter and not of the two places it cites. In a chapter that states a rate of attrition at seven thresholds and prescribes what to do when a resolve does not take, there is no sentence at all about what befalls one who tries this from below the required absorption. The one negative the chapter does state is a bare impossibility: the attempt cannot even begin."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Continuous reading of the chapter in the first translation; then a search of the chapter in the second, normalised to bare letters, for: not possible (two hits, both the statement that the beginner who has not tamed his mind in the fourteen ways cannot begin); impossible (one, the same statement); fails and failure (no hits at all); does not succeed (one, and it is the instruction to repeat the preliminary work and the resolve); he cannot (none); injury, injures, hurt, harm in the sense of harm to the operator (none in this chapter)." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-24" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga, chapter XII, in the English of Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, London 1931, pp. 432-471, read continuously; and the same chapter in the English of Ñāṇamoli 1956, searched as one text" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A stated consequence of attempting the operations of the chapter without the fourth absorption, or without the eight attainments in the eight devices: an injury, a delay, a wrong result, a partial result, anything at all beyond the statement that the attempt cannot begin. The holding carries pmc:IddhiPresupposesFourthJhana at the weaker strength of a prescribed order, with a note saying that the handbook does not say what happens to one who attempts it from elsewhere, and this search was carried to establish whether that note is a property of the two places already cited or of the whole chapter." .

pmp:VisuddhimaggaDeclineSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Handbuch irgendwo sagt, die Erlangungen seien nicht mehr zu haben"@de,
        "Whether the handbook anywhere says the attainments are no longer to be had"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-24" ;
    skos:note "Nothing. In nine hundred pages of instruction the handbook never concedes that any part of it has become unreachable, and it never dates itself against the passing of its founder. The gap is exhibited because it is the kind that cannot be found by reading forwards: a reader who works through the chapters will meet no such sentence and will not notice that he has not met it."@en,
        "The limit of the finding is the translation. It was carried over an English rendering, so it would miss a statement that Ñāṇamoli rendered in words none of the terms above would catch. Against that stands the shape of what was sought: not a term but a kind of sentence, and a translator who had met one would have had to render it somehow."@en,
        "The other side of the question was then put to a different work of the same transmission and found at once, which is why this search matters. See pmc:PancaAntaradhanani and pmc:IddhiAdhigamaVivada."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search with the text normalised to bare letters first, so that the scan's broken characters would not hide a spelling. Searched for: nowadays (no hits at all); at the present day (none); in these days (none); no longer (twenty-six hits, every one of them read — sixteen are the translator's or editor's remarks about commentaries and manuscripts that are lost, and the remaining ten are inside meditation instructions and describe what a meditator no longer does at a given stage; not one is about availability); have vanished (none); disappear and disappeared (one hundred and five hits, of which the ones falling in the chapters on the direct knowledges were read and concern the light that disappears when the preliminary work runs out, not the attainment)." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-24" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga, the whole of the twenty-three chapters, in the English of Ñāṇamoli, The Path of Purification, Colombo 1956, searched as one text; the twelfth chapter additionally in the English of Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, London 1931" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A sentence anywhere in the handbook stating that any of the attainments it prescribes has ceased to be reachable, that the age no longer produces those who reach it, or that the instruction is preserved rather than in use. The question decides how the twelfth chapter is to be read: as instruction addressed to its reader, or as a record of what was once done. It also decides whether the disagreement entered at pmc:IddhiAdhigamaVivada is a disagreement at all, since a handbook that conceded the point would not be a second side." .

pmp:VisuddhimaggaSukkhavipassakaSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Handbuch sagt, der Trockeneinsichtsübende könne die höheren Erkenntnisse nicht erlangen"@de,
        "Whether the handbook says the bare-insight worker cannot reach the higher knowledges"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-29" ;
    skos:note "So the exclusion of the bare-insight worker from the powers is a consequence of XII.1 and XII.2 and not a sentence of the handbook, and pmc:IddhiPresupposesFourthJhana stays at the strength it has. The finding matters because the modern literature states the exclusion as though the handbook did: what the handbook does is require the fourth absorption and the eight attainments, and leave the reader to draw the inference."@en,
        "The handbook says two neighbouring things and not this one. At XXI.112 it explains that a man who has not obtained absorption but has bare insight is of dry insight, being not soaked in the juice of absorption; and at XXIII.18 it excludes bare-insight workers, together with ordinary men, stream-enterers and once-returners, from the attainment of cessation, naming only non-returners and arahants who have the eight attainments. Neither passage says anything about the supernormal powers."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Both texts normalised to bare letters, then searched for: sukkhavipassaka (no hits in either English text, the term surviving only in a Pali quotation in a footnote of Part III); bare insight and bare-insight (three hits in Ñāṇamoli, one in Part II, three in Part III); dry insight (one in Ñāṇamoli, three in Part III); dry-visioned (none); insight alone (three in Ñāṇamoli); without jhana (four in Ñāṇamoli, two in Part II). Every hit was read." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-29" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga, the whole of the twenty-three chapters, in the English of Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Parts II and III, Pali Text Society, and in the English of Ñāṇamoli, The Path of Purification, both searched as one text each" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A statement that one who reaches the goal by insight without the absorptions does not obtain the supernormal powers and the other higher knowledges. The question decides how pmc:IddhiPresupposesFourthJhana is to be read. If the handbook says it outright, the condition is a necessity with a named class of people excluded by it; if it does not, the exclusion follows only from the requirement of the fourth absorption and is the compiler's inference and not the transmission's statement." .

pmp:VisuddhimaggaTerminusSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Handbuch einen Punkt nennt, an dem der Übende schließen soll, dass das Zeichen nicht kommt"@de,
        "Whether the handbook names a point at which the practitioner is to conclude the sign will not come"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-29" ;
    skos:note "Nothing, and the shape of the nothing is the yield. Every one of the four places where the handbook admits that the sign has not come or has gone answers with a further operation: go back to the device and apprehend it again; guard it as an embryo is guarded; work through the lodgings three days at a time; and if it still does not arise in spite of his practising, bring to pass the tenfold skill in absorption. The last of these is explicitly the second station, and there is no third. In nine hundred pages there is no sentence at which a practitioner is to stop."@en,
        "One exception stands and it is not in these chapters. In the twelfth chapter the resolve is allowed once or twice, on the authority of the commentary on the Saṃyutta, and nothing is said about the second time failing. That is the only closed count in the handbook and it is the one place where the series of recourses ends without stating an outcome. The node is pmc:AdhitthanaDvikkhattum, and what it does to pmc:CountedThresholdNeverFallsDue is set out there."@en,
        "Why the gap is worth exhibiting rather than merely noting. A transmission that never lets the practitioner stop has made its central claim untestable by him: no run he can perform has an outcome that would count against it, because every empty run is met by another instruction. That is a property of the text and it is now queryable; a reader who wants to press it can ask the same question of any other handbook in this holding and compare."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Both texts normalised to bare letters, then searched for: should give up (one hit, in Part III and about giving up a line of reasoning in a scholastic objection); gives up the meditation (none); abandon the meditation subject (none); is not for him (none); not suited for this subject (none); cease the endeavour (none); desist (none); hopeless (none); no use continuing (none); can never attain (none); should conclude (three hits, all three about how a passage is to be understood or where an anatomical survey is to end, none about stopping a practice); will never (three, none of them addressed to a practitioner). Then the four passages that do treat the failure were read continuously in both translations: IV.29, IV.34, IV.41-43 and VI.59-63." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-29" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga, the whole of the twenty-three chapters, in the English of Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Parts II and III, Pali Text Society, and in the English of Ñāṇamoli, The Path of Purification, both searched as one text each; chapters III, IV and VI additionally read continuously" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A sentence anywhere in the handbook telling the practitioner to stop: to give up a meditation subject as not his, to conclude after some number of attempts or some length of time that the absorption will not arise, or to take the absence of the sign as establishing anything about the practice rather than about his circumstances. The handbook is unusually rich in failure conditions and states at four separate places what to do when the sign does not come or is lost; the question is whether the series ever ends." .

pmp:VisuddhimaggaThirdSignSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Handbuch neben dem erfassten und dem Gegenzeichen ein drittes, vorbereitendes Zeichen kennt"@de,
        "Whether the handbook knows a third, preparatory sign beside the learning and counterpart signs"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-29" ;
    skos:note "The handbook states the number twice and in the affirmative, so this is a positive finding and not a silence. Chapter VI quotes the old commentary in the words that here the sign is twofold, the learning sign and the counterpart sign; and Chapter III, listing what a teacher is to expound of the earth device, names the twofold sign among nine headings. The node is pmc:NimittaDuvidha."@en,
        "The limit of the finding is the translation, as with every search of this kind carried over an English text. Against that stands the shape of what was sought: a numbered term in a scholastic handbook, which both translators render with a number, and both numbers are two."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "Both texts normalised to bare letters first, so that the scan's broken characters could not hide a spelling, then searched for: parikammanimitta and parikamma nimitta (no hits in either text); preliminary sign (none); threefold sign (none); three signs (two hits in Ñāṇamoli and one in Pe Maung Tin, all of them the canonical passage on the three signs of concentration, exertion and equanimity, which is a different list); tividha (one hit in Ñāṇamoli, in a footnote and not of a sign); sign is twofold (one hit in Ñāṇamoli); twofold sign (four hits in Pe Maung Tin)." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:NimittaDuvidha ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-29" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga, the whole of the twenty-three chapters, in the English of Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Parts II and III, Pali Text Society, and in the English of Ñāṇamoli, The Path of Purification, both searched as one text each" ;
    pm:searchedFor "The term parikamma-nimitta, the preparatory or preliminary sign, as a member of a triad with the learning sign and the counterpart sign. The triad is standard in the later manuals and in every modern account of this practice, and those accounts cite this handbook. If it is not here, then a reader who brings the triad to the handbook and finds it confirmed has confirmed the later systematisation, and the number of intermediate marks a practitioner of this transmission can report is one and not two." .

pmp:VoSisa a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Vo-sisa, was bei schlechtem Spruch zu tun ist"@de,
        "Vo-sisa, what is done when the oracle answers badly"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Richard F. Burton, A Mission to Gelele, King of Dahome (2nd ed., London: Tinsley Brothers, 1864), vol. II, pp. 143-144" ;
    skos:definition "When the oracle predicts evil the enquirer must perform the remedy. Ground is cleared near the house or in the bush, a mat is spread and a short thick peg driven through it, and the enquirer with his practitioner, who taps a small cymbal with an iron rod, pours on the wood first water and then the blood of a fowl. The body of the fowl falls to the practitioner. The witness states that there are many different forms of the remedy."@en ;
    skos:note "The remedy is what turns a bad prediction into an action, and it is the point at which the oracle stops being falsifiable by the event: a predicted evil that does not occur is then the remedy working, and one that occurs is the prediction. The same witness reports the saying that the practitioner who is most cunning takes to the oracle, because it pays best."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by Fon informants and interpreters at Abomey, unnamed in this passage and following an order the recorder says his interpreters dictated; recorded by Richard F. Burton on the mission of 1863 to 1864; given in Fon, of which the work prints the terms, the account itself in English." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Vodun .

pmp:Whakahokitu a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Whakahokitu"@de,
        "Whakahokitu"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 35" ;
    skos:definition "The forms of makutu employed to turn back the working of another tohunga, that is, a counter-operation of the same kind rather than a protection of a different kind."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Maori .

pmp:Whakanoa a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Aufheben des Tapu"@de,
        "Making noa"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 27" ;
    skos:definition "The rite by which a person or place under tapu is made common again, in the case witnessed by the eating of food cooked in an oven opened for the purpose and distributed to everyone present."@en ;
    skos:note "The rite the recorder describes as an eyewitness was already carried out with a Christian morning service inside it, and he remarks that the teacher had done wisely in keeping so much of the old ceremonial. The passage is the clearest single reason the contact route of this tradition is entered as disputed rather than absent."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Maori .

pmp:WhereTheEmptyRunIsPutExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der leere Durchgang immer einer Partei zugewiesen wird"@de,
        "Whether the empty run is always assigned to a party"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that where a transmission prescribes an operation whose success cannot be established apart from the operation itself, the same transmission supplies a condition under which a run that produced nothing counts as no failure, and locates that condition in the operator, in the person operated on, in the material or in a third party. Two questions were put to the holding and not one: whether a transmission of the kind described supplies no such condition at all, which is the falsifying case the claim names; and whether one supplies it and puts it somewhere the claim's enumeration does not allow."@en ;
    skos:note "A third near-miss, added on 2026-09-26, and it is of a shape the first two did not have. The two recorded above are conduct without a rule and a limit set before the operation. This one is silence: a transmission inside the antecedent that neither supplies an excuse nor lets an outcome count, because it never puts the question. The Yogavacara's Manual has a closed count — not less than five times nor more than ten, and then the perception appears — so the moment does arrive at which the practitioner has done the maximum and either has the perception or has not, and the work says nothing whatever about the second case. The nodes are pmc:BoranPancaDasaVara and pmc:BoranPancajhanaVanna in traditions/boran-kammatthana.ttl."@en,
        "One qualification on the negative, so the case is not claimed harder than it can bear. The manual is a single manuscript at two removes, read in translation, and its printed text abbreviates its repetitions with et cetera. A condition hidden inside an elided repetition is possible in principle and unlikely in fact, for the reason given at pmp:BoranTerminusSearch: the elisions are of the request formula and the posture rubric, both printed in full more than once. The Siamese and Khmer branches of the same transmission are published in part, were not opened, and one Khmer text is reported to state a colour condition that can fail — which would make the transmission carry both arrangements and is the cheapest next test."@en,
        "The deviation, and it stands inside a witness. The thirteenth chapter of the same medical canon asks why the ancients cured by shifting the essence and transforming the qi, so that the incantation after the cause sufficed, while now drugs are used within and needle and stone without, and some are cured and some are not. Its answer is not a defect of the operator, of the patient, of the material or of a third party. It is the age: in high antiquity men lived among the birds and beasts with no burden of longing within and no obligation of office without, the evil could not enter deeply, and the incantation was enough; the present age is not so, and therefore the incantation can no longer bring anything to an end (pmc:ZhuyouGujinYi). The empty run is assigned, and it is assigned to a fifth place the claim's enumeration does not carry."@en,
        "The first address, and it is the strongest of the three because the sign is one a second person can observe. The eighth chapter of the Baopuzi lays a goose down on the nose and the mouth and makes the sign that it does not stir while the breath is let out (pmc:InaudibleAndUnevenBreath). Nothing in that chapter says what follows if the down stirs. What the same work supplies instead stands in its eighteenth chapter: to know the One is not hard, the hard thing is in the ending, and where a man forgets the guarding and is harmed by the hundred ghosts or pressed down in his sleep, he is to go into the courtyard, look at the assistant star and guard the One again, with a fallback for overcast weather (pmc:ShouyiZhongnan). The failure is in the operator's persistence and the remedy is more of the practice. The same chapter eight binds the circulation to the hours of living qi and says that in the hours of dead qi it is of no benefit, which assigns a further class of empty runs to the hour the operator chose."@en,
        "The procedural point, and it is the reason to run the second search separately in future. The first search asked which transmissions carry no excuse and it found nothing, which is what it would have found in the run that wrote the claim. The second asked which carry an excuse pointed the wrong way, and it returned a case in one query, from a corpus that had been a witness for weeks. The recommendation in prima-materia#726 was written before this protocol was run and this is its first test: the second counter-search is not a refinement of the first, it is a different question, and the material it reaches is not reachable from the terms of the first."@en,
        "The second address is the one where the sign and the excuse are printed in the same chapter, and it is the first witness of the claim. The fourth chapter of the Baopuzi gives the assay: joined with mercury and put to the fire, the elixir makes gold, and where the gold forms the drug is complete. Where it does not form, the drug is to be sealed and fired again for the same count of days, and then there is no case in which it does not form (pmc:JinchengYaocheng). The observable sign the claim sent the protocol to look for — the body turning gold in colour when the golden liquor enters the mouth (pmc:JinyeFenliang) — stands four columns away from that clause and is covered by it."@en,
        "The third address is the medical canon, and there the sign and the excuse are in adjacent chapters of one work. The fourteenth chapter names the case in which the body is worn out, the blood exhausted and the treatment establishes nothing, and gives it a name: the spirit does not lend itself (pmc:Shenbushi). The node entered from it already says that the passage supplies no mark by which the condition could have been established beforehand. That is the excuse for the ten-day term and for everything else in the corpus that could come out empty."@en,
        "Two near-misses, recorded because they bound the claim rather than break it. The Sami material reports two men who set a term, named the sign they demanded, received nothing and destroyed the idol and the drum without explaining the failure away (pmc:AbandonmentAfterFailure); the counter-search that preceded the claim had already found it, and it does not refute, because it reports conduct and not a rule, and because both cases stand in a missionary's chapter on conversion. The Oglala corpus names three things its means cannot reach at all — the Moon and the Wind cannot be influenced by ceremony, and the potency of the Sun can be imparted to nothing, so that a fetish of the Sun cannot be prepared (pmc:WhatCeremonyCannotReach). That is a limit set before the operation rather than an excuse supplied after it, and a transmission that forbids the attempt never has an empty run to place. It is the nearest thing in this holding to a corpus that lets an outcome count, and it works by not operating."@en,
        "What the deviation does and does not cost the claim. The first clause stands: the transmission does supply a condition, it does supply it in advance, and the condition is not an outcome a further run could establish — an age is even further out of reach of a further run than a defect of the operator, because no run can be moved into it. What fails is the enumeration in the middle, which was read off nine witnesses that all happened to name a party. The corrected wording would say that the condition is located anywhere other than in an outcome a further run could establish, and would name the four parties and the age as the values found so far. The statement is not rewritten here: a claim is corrected where it is examined, and what a later reader needs is the protocol beside it and not a tidied sentence."@en,
        "What the protocol is worth for the next examination. The claim survives with a wider consequent and is now harder to falsify than it was, which is a loss and should be said plainly: every widening of where the excuse may sit moves the claim further from anything that could come out against it. The place where it could still fail is the one the first search could not reach — a transmission outside this holding that names a term, lets it run out and then says of its own method that it does not work. The Pulling Book of the second century before this era, opened in the same run, was read with that question in hand and answered it the other way: knocking the teeth on waking prevents decay, and where there is decay already, the instruction is to knock them more (pmc:YinshuKouchi). That is the same two-clause shape as the assay of the fourth chapter, four hundred years earlier, and it is entered as the tenth witness rather than as a counter-case."@en,
        "Why the silence matters to this protocol specifically, and it is the thing the first search could not have reached. That search asked which transmissions name a sign or a term and then supply no excusing condition, and found no case; it found none because every corpus then in the holding raised the question somewhere. The claim's force comes from the excuse being always supplied, and a corpus that never asks is indistinguishable, in the claim's wording, from a corpus that supplies one. So the claim is vacuously true of this manual and the protocol cannot currently tell a provided-for transmission from an unreflective one. That is a defect of the wording rather than of the claim, and the correction it needs is a scope clause: the statement holds of transmissions that contemplate an empty run, and how many do is a second question nobody has counted."@en ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Searched again on 2026-09-26 in the Yogavacara's Manual, read complete at the wording in Woodward's translation of 1916, with the first of the two questions this protocol puts: whether a transmission of the kind described supplies no excusing condition at all. It supplies none, and it is the first case the protocol has found. The work grades its stages by inner colours, states three quantified things — an audible candle clock of eight sticks, a closed count of not less than five and not more than ten repetitions, and a ladder of extension — and states every appearance in the indicative as what appears to the practitioner. Searched case-insensitively for beware, danger, harm, injure, must not, do not, avoid, caution, counterfeit, false, illusion, deception, obstacle, hindrance, defilement, in vain, fail, cannot, unable, does not appear, if not, otherwise, again, repeat. The word fail occurs some ninety-five times and every occurrence is the rubric that he has without fail performed the first duties. Counterfeit, false, illusion, deception, obstacle, hindrance and defilement do not occur. It does not refute the claim, whose falsifier requires the transmission also to name an outcome that would count against the operation, and this one names no outcome at all."@en,
        "Two searches, in two layers, over the compiled graph and then at the nodes returned. The first asked which transmissions in this holding name an observable sign of success or a stated term and then supply no excusing condition; it went first to the three addresses the claim names for itself and then over every tradition file by term. It found no case. The second asked which transmissions supply an excusing condition and put it outside the four places the claim enumerates; it found one, and the corpus it stands in is one of the nine witnesses. Not searched: anything outside this holding. The claim is about a form of wording and the holding now carries some ninety corpora, so the cheap search is the one that was run; a transmission outside it that refutes the claim would still refute it."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-16; a further corpus read through with the first question on 2026-09-26, the Yogavacara's Manual" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A transmission of the kind described whose earliest witness names an outcome that would count against the operation and supplies no condition assigning a failed run to the operator, the person operated on, the material or a third party. That is the condition the claim itself named. It was not met."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-26" ;
    pm:tests pmc:NoFailedRunCountsAgainstTheOperation .

pmp:WhereThePenaltyLandsExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Wohin die verlagerte Strafe fällt, über den ganzen Bestand gezählt"@de,
        "Where the displaced penalty lands, counted over the whole holding"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that where a transmission states what a defective or improperly transmitted performance costs, the cost is in a substantial minority of cases laid not on the performer but on a third party — the family or lineage the rite is done for, the dead ascendants of the person transmitting, or a class of beings to whom a debt is owed. Three questions were put and all three by enumeration rather than by sampling: what proportion of the holding's stated costs displace; whether the displaced ones are confined to the burial and needling literatures; and whether the party they land on is one of the three the statement names."@en ;
    skos:note "A second limit, on the sharper of the three questions. Whether a cost lands on the performer or on somebody else is sometimes a judgement and not a reading. Where a transmission says only that the rite is of no avail, the party who loses is whoever the rite was for, and this count did not treat that as displacement: pmc:MaoriEfficacyPresupposesExactRecitation, pmc:NavajoEfficacyPresupposesExactness and pmc:InipiPresupposesTheCustomaryBuild were read as loss of efficacy and left out, although each of the three is performed for a person who is not the reciter. Counting them in would add three to the forward-displaced twelve and strengthen the deviation; they are left out because the statement asks what the transmission says the cost is, and these three name no harm."@en,
        "How the count was made, so that a later run can repeat it rather than trust it. Two classes of clause in the compiled graph state a cost: the values of pm:consequenceOfSkipping, which the shapes make quotable wherever a prerequisite claims stated necessity, and the pm:Cautioning nodes. There were 81 of the first and 182 of the second. All 81 were read one by one. The 182 were reduced by a term filter — family, families, household, ancestor, ancestors, descend, son, sons, grandson, children, child, patient, the sick, bystander, onlooker, village, town, people, kin, lineage, wife, husband, father, mother, parent, generation, generations, heir, cattle, clan, assistant, companion, others, another, third party, those present, relative, community, tribe, neighbour, neighbor — which returned 57, and all 57 were read in full. A caution that displaces to a party named by none of those words would have been missed, which is the one hole in the method and is stated here rather than left for a reader to find."@en,
        "One case would not classify and is recorded rather than forced. At pmc:VaidikaIndrasatru the wording of an offering formula decides which of two parties is slain: Tvaṣṭṛ pours the remainder saying grow thou, having Indra for thy foe, what grows is Vṛtra, and Indra slays him — where had he said grow thou, the foe of Indra, Vṛtra would have slain Indra. The fault is the reciter's, the bearer is the being the formula produced, and the passage exists in order to show that the accent decided it. It is counted among the thirty-one because the bearer is not the reciter; it belongs to none of the five values, and a reader who throws it out is left with thirty and the same finding."@en,
        "Six clauses run backward and they are worth naming, because the statement's second note already predicted this shape from a single Celestial Masters case and said a later run might want to say that displacing either term achieves the separation. This count supplies five more. Agrippa has the prating of a companion, his incredulity and unworthiness, hinder and disturb the effect in every operation. The mesmeric rule banishes the incredulous stranger for the same reason. The Baopuzi's compounding conditions forbid letting unbelievers know of the work, because if they slander the divine drug the drug will not form. A Sámi report has the man never recover out of the trance if the singers cease or anyone stirs him with hand or foot. And an Inuit account has the moon refuse to let a man come back to life because his mother had sewn garments of new caribou skin where it is not allowed. In every one of the five the fault is another party's and the cost the practitioner's, and in the last the practitioner is dead."@en,
        "The cluster fear is refuted, and it should be said plainly because the statement's own third note raises it as the likeliest way the claim would turn out to be about two corpora. The 31 clauses come from sixteen transmissions: the magnetic literature with seven, the Celestial Masters with three, the Vedic ritual texts with four, the Maya and the Siberian material with two each, and one each from the Azande, Hawaiian, Inuit, Sámi, Kongo, Fon, Shangqing, Śaiva-Śākta, Mandaean, Renaissance magic, the Baopuzi and the Turba-adjacent operative alchemy. The burial literature supplies one, at pmc:WuBukezang, and the needling canon one, at pmc:WuliErshiwu. The two clusters the statement was careful not to rest on together supply two of thirty-one."@en,
        "The largest cluster is now a different one and it is the interesting part of the count. Seven of the thirty-one stand in the magnetic literature, and all seven have the same shape: the operator's fault and the subject's harm. Rheumatic pains, nervous affections and organic diseases pass from magnetiser to magnetised; an operator who does not wake the subject perfectly leaves him habitually in the magnetic state; one who rouses him roughly produces convulsions, violent pains or the permanent loss of the faculty; one who operates across the sexes in a long treatment wounds decency and meets crises a man ought not to witness; and an incredulous witness troubles the somnambulist and he loses his lucidity. A transmission that puts a second person under the operation for hours at a time generates this form as a matter of course, and that is a property of the arrangement and not of the doctrine."@en,
        "The proportion, and it is close to what the earlier counter-search reported at a third of the present size. It found four of forty stated consequences displacing and nine to eleven of some fifty cautions; this count finds eleven of eighty-one and twenty of a hundred and eighty-two. Both give a minority in the low teens of a percent, and the holding has roughly tripled between them. The statement's substantial minority is therefore not an artefact of the corpora that were in it when it was written, which is the strongest thing a count of this kind can establish."@en,
        "What the protocol does not establish, and it bounds every number above. The two classes counted are the clauses in which this holding has recorded a cost, and the holding is not the world: a corpus that states costs and has not been opened contributes nothing, and the magnetic literature is over-represented here for the plain reason that it was read closely and recently. The count therefore measures the shape of the recorded clauses and not the shape of the transmissions, and the claim it tests is about transmissions. That is the same limit the earlier counter-search stated for itself, and repeating the count on a holding three times larger has not removed it — it has only shown that the proportion did not move while the holding grew, which is evidence of a different and weaker kind than a survey of corpora would be."@en,
        "Which yields the correction the statement needs, and it is a correction of the middle clause only. The displaced party is not to be enumerated as family, ascendants or creditor-beings; those are three of at least five values, and the one the enumeration omits is the commonest. What the five have in common is the thing the statement's first and last clauses already say: the bearer is someone other than the person positioned to notice that the fault occurred. A corrected wording would name that separation and give the five values as what has been found, which is what pmc:NoFailedRunCountsAgainstTheOperation was told to do on 2026-09-16 for the same reason. The statement is not rewritten here."@en ;
    pm:baseRate "31 displaced clauses out of 263 operative clauses that state a cost, being 11 of 81 values of pm:consequenceOfSkipping and 20 of 182 pm:Cautioning nodes. The population is the compiled holding at main, 2026-09-30, and it is the whole of it and not a sample: both classes were enumerated, every value of pm:consequenceOfSkipping was read, and the cautions were reduced by a term filter whose terms are named below and every hit then read in full."@en ;
    pm:caseCount 31 ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "The opposition is to the statement's enumeration and it came back on the first pass. Of the 31 displaced clauses, six run backward — a fault committed by somebody other than the practitioner, with the cost on the practitioner or his work — and twenty-five forward. Of the twenty-five forward, twelve land on a living person the operation is performed upon or for: the magnetised subject, the sick person in the hands of an incompetent officiant, the patient of a needler who has passed the counted threshold, the patron of a sacrifice, the person or family a chant honours. That class is not among the three the statement names, and it is the largest class in the count. The remainder land on a household, family or lineage, on dead ascendants, or on a whole settlement or clan. Searched, in addition, for the third falsifier the claim names — a displaced penalty with a stated means of confirming its arrival and no direction or term — and none was found, so that limb of the statement stands unchallenged by this count."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-30" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "The claim names two count-based falsifiers. That a survey of the operative clauses across the holding's corpora finds the displaced form to be the majority rather than the minority, in which case the statement should be rewritten as a rule; or that it finds the displaced form confined to the burial and needling clusters and absent everywhere else, in which case the statement should be withdrawn to a remark about two corpora. Neither is met. It also names a third, from the other direction: an operative text that lays the penalty on a third party and in the same clause gives the performer a means of confirming that it arrived, without a direction or a term. None was found."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-30" ;
    pm:tests pmc:ThePenaltyLaidOnAnotherParty .

pmp:WhoMayTestAPersonExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Verweigerung einer Prüfung im Vermögen und nicht im Fragenden begründet ist"@de,
        "Whether the refusal of a test is grounded in the capacity and not in the asker"@en ;
    skos:definition "A reading protocol on the claim that where a transmission locates a claimed capacity in a person rather than in a procedure anyone could work, it states a rule withdrawing that person from a deliberate test, grounds the rule in a property of the capacity or of the party exercised through it and not in the unworthiness of whoever proposes the test, and that where two practitioners instead measure their capacities against one another it fixes a criterion and does not forbid the trial. What is claimed is a regularity in what transmissions say, so the protocol fails or holds against texts and not against practitioners."@en ;
    skos:note "A limit of this protocol, stated because it decides what the next one should do. Three of the four witnesses of the claim now have a documented route between them — the Swedenborgian corpus is upstream of both the magnetic and the spiritist literature — so the confirmation of the core rests on two independent lines and not four: that block, and the Pali discourse. The Theravada case that breaks the second qualification comes from the same transmission as one of those two lines, which makes it a cheap find and a strong one at once: the transmission that refuses the outsider's test narrates an insider's test approvingly, in the same corpus, four chapters apart."@en,
        "The successor statement this protocol recommends, written here and deliberately not written into the graph, because a claim is corrected where it is examined and a tidied sentence would hide the cases. It should be about WHO may test rather than about what the refusal is grounded in: where a transmission locates a capacity in a person, a test proposed by an enquirer or an onlooker is refused, while a test proposed from inside the transmission is admitted, and it is admitted whether the parties are equals or not. If that holds, what these rules protect is not the capacity from examination but the boundary of the transmission, and the ground given for the refusal — the thing or the asker — is then a rhetorical choice and not the structure. That is a sharper claim than the present one, it is easier to break, and it can be broken by a single transmission that refuses a test proposed by one of its own."@en,
        "What fails, first qualification. The statement says the rule is grounded in a property of the capacity or of the party exercised through it AND NOT in the unworthiness of whoever proposes the test. One corpus does both, in two works, and neither passage marks the difference. The word that has to change is the negation: not, becomes not only. That is a real loss, because the excluded ground was doing work — a transmission that blames the asker has said something about persons rather than about the capacity, and the statement's interest was that these transmissions do not take that easy road. The finding is that one of them takes both roads."@en,
        "What fails, second qualification, and the failure is more interesting than the first. The last sentence of the statement exempts the case of two practitioners measuring their capacities against one another. With three cases that exemption is no longer a boundary but a second class, and the three do not share the property the wording names. In the Sami case the parties are equals and the outcome is a rank. In the Theravada case they are not equals and the outcome is a correction that the loser accepts and acts on within the hour. In the Swedenborgian case the party tested is not a practitioner at all but the interlocutor whose identity is at issue. What all three share is that the test is proposed from inside the transmission by somebody who wants to know, and what the three original witnesses share is that the test is proposed from outside by somebody who wants to be convinced."@en,
        "What holds, and it is the part worth having. In everything read the core of the statement was confirmed: no transmission in this holding that locates a capacity in a person invites an outsider to test it and states what a negative outcome would be. The Swedenborgian corpus is the strongest confirmation yet, because it is the one corpus that had the least need of such a rule — its author was believed by nobody in particular and had no lineage to protect — and it refuses the demonstration twice over anyway, at some length, and adds that a demonstration which succeeded and was afterwards denied carries the lowest hell."@en,
        "Why the outcome is cases with deviation and not a refutation. The claim's own falsifier is the core and it was not met; what was met are the two qualifications, which the claim had added to save itself from cases it already knew about. A statement whose qualifications fail while its core holds is not broken, it is under-described, and the right response is a narrower successor and not a deletion. The precedent this holding has for the other outcome is examinations/the-sign-and-the-counterfeit.ttl, where the falsifier itself was met and the claim fell."@en ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Searched on 2026-09-29 across the two corpora read at the wording in this run and then across the holding. In the Swedenborgian corpus the question was put to eight volumes: whether the refusal of visible proof is grounded in the thing or in the asker. Both grounds are there and in two different works. Arcana 10751 grounds it in the thing — appearances are rare now lest they compel men to believe, for a compulsory faith such as enters by miracles does not cleave to the man — and that is the witness at pmc:SwedenborgSignaNonCoguntFidem. Heaven and Hell 456 grounds it in the asker — those who have confirmed themselves in falses would not believe the evidence of their senses, and such demonstrations are dangerous to them because they would first believe and afterwards deny — and that is pmc:SwedenborgProfanatioPostFidem. The two passages refuse the same demonstration and do not agree about why."@en,
        "Second search, over the second corpus of this run, for the boundary clause. The Visuddhimagga narrates at XX.111 a deliberate trial of one practitioner's attainment by another: Dhammadinna puts a thousand questions and every one is answered, then has the elder Mahanaga create an elephant, has him drive it at himself, the elder flinches and runs, and the flinch establishes that an attainment of sixty years' standing was not what its holder took it for. The node is pmc:UpakkilesaHatthiParikkha. The trial is narrated approvingly and is not forbidden, and the doctrinal examination that preceded it established nothing while the involuntary reaction established everything."@en,
        "Third search, over the holding rather than over a corpus, asking whether the boundary clause already had more cases than the one it names. It did not name them and it has them. Before this run the clause rested on the Sami drum contest, two practitioners painting reindeer to see whose wins. This run adds two, and neither fits the wording: the Swedenborgian test at pmc:SwedenborgProbatioSpiritus is put by a practitioner to his interlocutor and not to a second practitioner, and the Theravada test is put by one practitioner to another who is his senior and his own former teacher, with correction and not ranking as the outcome. So the clause now carries three cases of three different shapes, and what they share is not that the parties are equals but that the person proposing the test stands inside the transmission."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-29; the protocol is a reading of the two corpora opened in this run plus a search of the holding, and the falsifiers of the two qualifications were formulated before the cases were looked for" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "For the core of the statement: a transmission claiming a capacity of a person whose earliest witness invites an outsider to test it, states what a negative outcome would be, and treats such an outcome as telling against the capacity rather than against the conditions of the trial. That condition is the claim's own and has NOT been met. For the first qualification: a transmission of the same kind that grounds its refusal in the unworthiness of whoever proposes the test. That has been met. For the second: a deliberate trial of such a capacity, proposed from inside the transmission, that the transmission does not forbid and does not treat as a contest between equals. That has been met twice."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-29"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:TheTestOfAPersonIsRefused .

pmp:WordsBeforeDoctorsExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Prüfung des Satzes über Worte und Doktoren"@de,
        "Examination of the rejoinder about words and doctors"@en ;
    skos:note "The falsifying condition is written out even so, because SPEC's fifth state sends the claim back to the wording rather than to the bin. The sentence could be restated in a form that does discriminate, and the restatement would be a different claim with the same content: that words help in cases where medicine does not. That version can fail and this one cannot."@en,
        "The state is not a verdict on the strength of the evidence. It says that the claim, as the preface words it, treats an outcome and its opposite alike, and that the third outcome, the only one that could tell against it, is answered before it can occur. What decides the state is not how convinced a reader is but whether there is a result the claim would have ruled out."@en,
        "Where the sentence stands matters for how it is read. It is in the preface, that is at the place where a book says what it defends itself against, and the paragraph around it is a chain of rhetorical questions about what no physician has ever driven out. The immunising move is the transmission's own account of its failures, which is exactly the evidence SPEC §14 wants when it asks whether a transmission is a copying chain or a series of trials."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-13" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A case in which blessing words were used without effect and a physician then cured the same complaint, admitted as a case rather than reclassified as the physician's excuse."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-13" ;
    pm:tests pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicDoctorsAusrede ;
    pm:undividedOutcomes "The sufferer does not recover after the words and the physician does help, which is the one outcome the sentence would have to exclude; the same paragraph disposes of it in advance by calling the physicians' account of it their excuse."@en,
        "The sufferer does not recover after the words and the physician does not help either, which the sentence takes as confirming itself."@en,
        "The sufferer recovers after the words, which the sentence takes as confirming the words."@en .

pmp:XiaoZhiguanCaseReportSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Xiao zhiguan einen einzigen Fall berichtet, in dem jemand seine Verfahren ausführte, und was daraus wurde"@de,
        "Whether the Xiao zhiguan reports a single instance of somebody carrying out its procedures and what came of it"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-10" ;
    skos:note "The gap this exhibits is the one that matters most for the work's standing. The transmission that supplies this holding with its most answerable clauses supplies no case, and the absence is not an accident of the genre: the author ran a meditation community, the work names his lay brother as its addressee, and the chapter on illness says that an illness treated at once is easily cured, which is a claim about what happens to people. Somebody was there, and nothing is reported."@en,
        "What would close it is not a further search in this work. It is the Mohe zhiguan, which stands unopened at pmw:MoheZhiguan, the biographical literature of the school, and the Tiantai manuals that followed; a reader who owns those can say whether the school ever wrote down an outcome. The search is entered so that the next run does not repeat it here."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "有人 a certain man, two, one a simile about a man in a jewel mountain without hands and one inside the denial that any man has been seen eaten by a demon; 昔有 formerly there was, nil; 嘗有 there once was, nil; 某甲 so-and-so, nil; 一人 one man, nil; 驗 verification, nil; 效驗 proof of effect, nil. The named persons in the work are Bimbisāra, Udayana, the five hundred immortals of the Snow Mountains, the novice who loved the taste of curds, the one-horned seer, Śāriputra, Kāśyapa, Śakra and Devadatta, all of them from the scriptures and all of them illustrating a desire or a fault and never a performance of the ten chapters. The preface names Chen Zhen, the lay elder brother for whom the work was drawn up, and says nothing about what he did with it." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-10" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46 no. 1915, pages 462a to 473a, the whole work in the Chinese of the Taishō text as the CBETA edition carries it, read entire, with the preface of Yuanzhao of 1095" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A named or unnamed person said to have performed one of the work's procedures, with an outcome: a cure, a sign, a failure, a death. Not an exemplum from scripture and not a doctrinal illustration, but a case the author or his tradition is in a position to have witnessed. The holding is short of these everywhere, and a manual written by a teacher for a pupil is the most likely place for one to turn up." .

pmp:XiaoZhiguanFailureConditionSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Xiao zhiguan irgendeinen Ausgang nennt, unter dem von seinen eigenen Verfahren kein Erfolg zu erwarten ist"@de,
        "Whether the Xiao zhiguan names any outcome under which its own procedures are not to be expected to work"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-10" ;
    skos:note "Eleven nodes, which is more than the eight works opened before this one carry between them, and the reason is the form of the work and not its doctrine: it is a manual addressed to a named beginner, and a manual that tells a beginner what to do has to tell him what will go wrong. The transmissions this holding opened earlier are revealed biographies, canonical collections and grimoires, and none of them is addressed to a learner who might come back and complain."@en,
        "The second finding is where the consequences land. Of the eleven, six are consequences in the practitioner's own body — a swollen belly and an urgent breath from too much food, a weak body and a loose mind from too little, a pain upward in the chest from a tight mind, saliva at the mouth from a loose one, headache and stiffened joints from an abrupt exit, and the four hundred and four illnesses from the wrong use of the mind. This holding's difficulty is almost always that only the operator can say what happened. Two of these six are visible to a bystander: the head hanging low at pmc:TiantaiJiKuanBing and the saliva at the mouth in the same node."@en,
        "The third finding is that the work keeps the two apart in a way the holding has not met. The failure conditions are all conditions on the performance, and the method itself is nowhere said to be able to fail; the sentence at pmc:TiantaiZhixinYichu says of the method that there is nothing it does not accomplish. A transmission that states eleven ways for its practitioner to fail and none for its procedure has said something about where it is willing to be answerable, and it has said it without being asked."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "Chinese terms, with the number of occurrences in the whole work. 發狂 madness, two, both of a wrong performance; 失心顛狂 derangement, one; 狂亂 disorder of the spirit, one; 名之曰狂 called mad, one, of one-sided practice; 即墮邪倒 falls into the false and upside down, one; 往往發狂 often brings on madness, one; 成患致死 falls ill and comes to death, one; 大命 the great life, one; 因之發生 arise from it, one, of the four hundred and four illnesses; 非得定之道 not the way to gain the absorption, one; 無由近道 no way to come near the way, one; 有妨 there is obstruction, one; 難攝 hard to gather in, one; 頭痛 headache, three, one of them the consequence of the omitted exit procedure; 百骨節彊 the hundred joints stiff, one; 治之難愈 hard to heal by treatment, one; 墮聲聞 falls to the ground of the hearers, one; 不成就 does not accomplish, one, of the borrowed visualizations. Terms that produced nothing of this kind: 驗, 效驗, 證驗 for a verification, all nil." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:TiantaiBingJiuNanYu,
        pmc:TiantaiJiKuanBing,
        pmc:TiantaiJingmeiXiemie,
        pmc:TiantaiPianxiuXieDao,
        pmc:TiantaiPresupposesChuding,
        pmc:TiantaiPresupposesShanzhishi,
        pmc:TiantaiPresupposesTiaoshi,
        pmc:TiantaiPresupposesYishi,
        pmc:TiantaiPresupposesZhengshen,
        pmc:TiantaiShouFeng,
        pmc:TiantaiWuGai,
        pmc:TiantaiYizhiFakuang,
        pmc:TiantaiYongxinShisuo,
        pmc:TiantaiZhuKongLuoDi ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-10" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46 no. 1915, pages 462a to 473a, the whole work in the Chinese of the Taishō text as the CBETA edition carries it, read entire; the English of Lu Kuan Yu (Charles Luk), The Secrets of Chinese Meditation, London: Rider, 1964, chapter 4, read beside it as a control" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage naming a result of a correctly conducted operation under which the operator is to conclude that it did not work or cannot work: a term after which nothing further is to be expected, a state of the practitioner or the patient that puts the case beyond the procedure, a degree of accomplishment below which it is not to be attempted, or a mistake with a named consequence. This is the material a pm:falsifiedBy can be formed from out of a transmission's own mouth. The same question was put to the demotic manual at pmp:PdmFailureConditionSearch, to the Clavicula at pmp:ClaviculaFailureConditionSearch and to the gZi brjid at pmp:BonFailureConditionSearch." .

pmp:XiaoZhiguanUnconditionalEfficacySearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob dasselbe Werk eine Wirkung behauptet, die kein Ausgang enttäuschen könnte"@de,
        "Whether the same work claims an effect that no outcome could disappoint"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-10" ;
    skos:note "The counter-search found more than the question asked for. The one unconditional guarantee in the work, that what is treated will certainly have effect and not be in vain, is conditional on ten things, and the tenth of those ten forbids the report of a benefit and forbids doubt where harm is not yet shown, while the seventh removes the term. A practitioner who kept the ten could not have produced a counter-instance to the guarantee they protect. Both clauses stand at pmc:TiantaiZheZhang."@en,
        "The outcome is entered as opposing material found and not as a place found, although both searches read the same text and both yielded nodes. The distinction is the one the scale is for: the first search asked what the transmission concedes and the second what it withholds, and a corpus that answers yes to both is a different finding from a corpus that answers yes to one. Five of the nodes of this search and eleven of the first are disjoint sets except for pmc:TiantaiSibaisibing, which states the effect and its failure in one sentence and is therefore named by both."@en,
        "Two further devices came out of the same search and they work on the other side. At pmc:TiantaiFeiMoSuoWei the work denies that the interferences it has just catalogued ever do anything and lays the whole damage on the practitioner's want of wisdom; at pmc:TiantaiJinshiBuchengjiu it says of the prescriptions it has just reported that the men of the present time are too dull to accomplish them. In both the failure is real, admitted, and assigned to a person. Nothing in this work is ever assigned to the method."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchTerms "必定有効 will certainly have effect, one; 不虛 and not in vain, one, in the same sentence; 無事不辦 there is nothing that is not accomplished, one; 自然除差 removed of themselves, one; 不計日月 the days and months are not to be counted, one; 不向外說 not to be spoken of outwardly, one; 未損不生疑謗 where there is not yet harm no doubt and no disparagement, one; 非魔所為 it is not what the demon did, one; 未曾見 it has never been seen, two, both in the same passage; 世不流傳 it is not transmitted in the world, one; 根機淺鈍 the capacities are shallow and dull, one; 自致其患 brings the trouble on himself, one; 行人無智受患 the practitioner suffers through want of wisdom, one." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:TiantaiFeiMoSuoWei,
        pmc:TiantaiJinshiBuchengjiu,
        pmc:TiantaiKongWuSuoHuo,
        pmc:TiantaiSibaisibing,
        pmc:TiantaiWuGai,
        pmc:TiantaiZheZhang,
        pmc:TiantaiZhixinYichu ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-10" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46 no. 1915, pages 462a to 473a, the whole work in the Chinese of the Taishō text as the CBETA edition carries it, read entire" ;
    pm:searchedFor "The negation the first search owes under SPEC §15.2, put to the same corpus rather than to another: a guarantee without a condition, a rule that forbids the report of an absence, a provision that removes the term, or a clause that assigns every failure to the practitioner so that none can fall on the procedure. The counter-search of a transmission that states its own failure conditions is not another transmission denying them but the same text taking them back." .

pmp:YangshengContactSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Ziehbuch eine Quelle außerhalb seiner selbst nennt"@de,
        "Whether the Pulling Book names any source outside itself"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-16" ;
    skos:note "Not one of the terms occurs. The work names exactly one person, in its first sentence, and names him as the owner of the way rather than as a teacher who handed it on: in the spring generate, in the summer grow, in the autumn harvest, in the winter preserve, this is the way of Peng Zu. Everything else it grounds in heaven, earth and the four seasons. There is no colophon, no line of transmission, no scripture cited and no authority appealed to."@en,
        "What the empty result is worth, and what it is not worth. It does not prove that nothing reached this material from outside; an absence of names is what most technical manuals of any period show. What it does is remove the one thing that would have settled the question the other way. The manuscript went into a tomb between 186 and 156 before this era and came out in 1984, so a route of contact, if there is one, has to be argued from the practices themselves and cannot be read off a citation. For a holding whose every convergence so far has been open to the objection of reception, that is the condition it has been looking for and it is why this corpus was opened before the two that were not reachable."@en,
        "Where a later run should go next with this. The two Mawangdui manuscripts named in the registration, the Daoyin tu and the Quegu shiqi, are from the same century and the same practice, and neither was reachable in a public-domain or freely readable English edition in this run. They would double the witness. The Huangting jing would not: it is centuries later and stands downstream of the traffic the early manuscripts stand before."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search of the whole translation, case-insensitive, for: India, Indian, Buddha, Buddhist, yoga, Sanskrit, prana, foreign, barbarian, western regions, Hu, monk, master, teacher, transmitted, received from, handed down. Every occurrence of a personal name in the translation read in its place."@en ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-16" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The Zhangjiashan Yinshu in full, all 113 slips, in Vivienne Lo (trans.), How to do the Gibbon Walk: A Translation of the Pulling Book (ca. 186 BCE), Needham Research Institute Working Papers 3 (Cambridge: Needham Research Institute, 2014)."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "Any naming of a source, an authority or an origin for the regimen that lies outside the work: a teacher, a lineage, a received scripture, a foreign people, a place beyond the Chinese world, or a technical term this holding carries from the Indian material. The registration of this transmission rested on the burial date rather than on the wording, and the wording had not been checked."@en .

pmp:YangshengPrerequisiteSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob eine Praxis des Ziehbuchs zur Bedingung einer anderen gemacht wird"@de,
        "Whether one practice of the Pulling Book is made the condition of another"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-16" ;
    skos:note "Order is everywhere in the work and it is always order inside one prescription. Extend the left foot, then raise the right arm; do the counted part, then hold the breath until the sweat comes; on rising make the movement wide, on lying down pull as far as it will go. Every occurrence of must governs a treatment and not a career: at this moment you must treat it with the pull of the Eight Warps. Nothing says that a practitioner who has not done one thing may not do another."@en,
        "The finding is about the shape of the transmission and not about a gap in the reading. A corpus of forty-one exercises each indexed to a complaint does not need a ladder, because nothing in it is reserved: the entry condition for any one of them is having the complaint it treats. The graded sequences this holding carries from the Baopuzi, from the Shangqing material and from the internal alchemy are later, and they come with what this text has none of — a lineage, a transmission restricted to the worthy, and an attainment at the top. An exhibited gap here is therefore worth more than a found passage would have been."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search of the whole translation for: before, first, only after, unless, must, cannot, not until, when you have, having. Every hit read in its place, and the three texts read through in order for an unmarked sequence."@en ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-16" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The Zhangjiashan Yinshu in full, all 113 slips, in Vivienne Lo (trans.), How to do the Gibbon Walk: A Translation of the Pulling Book (ca. 186 BCE), Needham Research Institute Working Papers 3 (Cambridge: Needham Research Institute, 2014)."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage making one of the work's practices the prerequisite of another: an exercise to be mastered before a second may be attempted, a purification before the breath, a stage that cannot be reached without a prior one. Prerequisite chains are one of the four kinds the harvest owes, and this holding carries them in quantity from the Chinese material of later centuries, so their absence in the earliest witness had to be established rather than assumed."@en .

pmp:YangshengSignOfSuccessSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Ziehbuch ein wahrnehmbares Zeichen des Gelingens nennt"@de,
        "Whether the Pulling Book names a perceptible sign that a treatment has worked"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-16" ;
    skos:note "One sign and it is a sign of the procedure rather than of the cure. Under held breath, after a while, sweat comes out of the patterns of the skin, and that is the point at which the instruction turns from a count to as long as possible. Everything else stops at a number: ten times, thirty, three hundred, a thousand, or ten days. The distinction matters for what can be checked. Sweat under a held breath follows from doing the thing correctly, so a practitioner who sees it knows he performed the procedure and knows nothing yet about his neck."@en,
        "The second-nearest thing was read and set aside as not a sign. Three outcomes are stated of the wall-and-staff treatment as things that will be the case when the qi of the head flows downward — the foot not numb, the head not swollen, the nose not blocked — and they are the complaints themselves in the negative. A transmission that says the treatment works when the symptom is gone has named a criterion and not a sign, and the difference is that a criterion of that kind is the ordinary one any medicine offers."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search of the whole translation for: sweat, sign, know, feel, appears, until, then the, will not, cured, recover, better, warm, light. Every hit read in its place; the stopping formulae of all forty-one exercises compared."@en ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:YinshuHanChu ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-16" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The Zhangjiashan Yinshu in full, all 113 slips, in Vivienne Lo (trans.), How to do the Gibbon Walk: A Translation of the Pulling Book (ca. 186 BCE), Needham Research Institute Working Papers 3 (Cambridge: Needham Research Institute, 2014)."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage telling the practitioner what he will perceive when a treatment has taken effect, as against how many times to do it. The five kinds the harvest owes count signs of success separately, and this holding has had to record their absence in several corpora with full capacity catalogues."@en .

pmp:ZhengaoBuddhistStratumSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Wo das Zhen'gao buddhistisches Material führt"@de,
        "Where the Zhen'gao carries Buddhist material"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-11" ;
    skos:note "Limits, and they are not small. The received text is a recension made a century and a quarter after the sessions by a compiler who says himself that later hands added and cut; what is in it is evidence about the recension first of all. A search cannot show that more Buddhist matter did not stand there once, only that this much stands there now."@en,
        "The terms found the frame and not the body, which is the finding that mattered. The explicit Buddhist vocabulary clusters where the corpus talks about Buddhism as a subject, at the emperor's dream and the Fangzhu islands in juan 9. The block of sayings in juan 6 carries almost none of it, because the renunciant has been replaced by the man of the Way throughout and the sayings put in the mouths of named Perfected; it is recognisable only by reading it, and it is the place where the borrowing actually is."@en,
        "What the transmission says about the direction is the opposite of what the search establishes about it. The Fangzhu passage makes the eastern islands the older home of that teaching, with monks, stupas and men who do not die, so that the borrowing is presented as a recovery of the original. A route is a route whichever way the claim points, and the tradition instance records it as documented on that ground."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of all twenty juan for: 佛 (thirty-four hits, concentrated at 9.19b-9.21b and recurring in juan 1, 5, 12 and 14); 沙門 (three, at 9.20b, 14.8b, 14.19a); 浮圖 (one, 9.21a); 四十二章 (one, 9.20a); 比丘 (one, 9.21b); 菩薩 (one, 9.21b); 三惡道 (two, 6.6b and 6.7a); 愛欲 (one, 6.6a); 宿命 (seven). The block at 6.6a-6.10a, which the term list reaches only through the last three, was read entire." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:ShangqingZhonglingJiaojieDispute ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-11" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao, twenty juan (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile; searched over the whole work in the Chinese, with every hit read at the wording" ;
    pm:searchedFor "The places at which this corpus carries Buddhist matter, in order to decide the contact route of the tradition from the text rather than from the secondary literature." .

pmp:ZhengaoMethodBlamedSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Zhen'gao ein Misslingen je der Methode zuweist"@de,
        "Whether the Zhen'gao ever assigns a failure to the method"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-11" ;
    skos:note "The one exception to that, and it is not about a method, is the compiler's own work: at 8.14a and at 10.17b-10.18a he declares passages spurious on internal grounds, and at 20.5a he corrects a revealed genealogy against a register. The transmission can be wrong about its own text in his hands. It is never wrong about what a practice does."@en,
        "The question as put was not answered, and the node records the nearest thing rather than a silence. Neither passage allows that an instruction might be wrong; the second merely moves the blame from the spirits back to the practitioner. In twenty juan containing at least seven narratives of a failed course, no passage was found in which the prescription is revised, withdrawn or doubted on the strength of an outcome."@en,
        "The two places where the corpus discusses the assignment of blame at all are adjacent in the term list and opposed in content, and both were entered. At 10.3b it says that those who compound without precision do not blame themselves but accuse the recipe of not working, which is the rule of assignment stated as a rule. At 7.13b it says that students bring a hundred ailments on themselves by carelessness and then blame the spirits, and calls them fools. The second is the node the search yielded."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of all twenty juan for: 咎 (ten hits); 歸咎 (one, 7.13b); 自咎 (one, 10.3b); 責方 (one, 10.3b); 不驗 (one, 10.3b); 無驗; 法誤; 非法; 過在 (one, 6.14a); 由我 (one, 11.5b). The two folios carrying the compound terms were read at the wording, as were the failure narratives of juan 5." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:ShangqingGuijiuShenling ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-11" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao, twenty juan (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile; searched over the whole work in the Chinese, with the passages carrying a hit read at the wording" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage assigning an unsuccessful run to a defect in the prescription, the recipe or the instruction rather than to the practitioner, his conduct, his purity, his ancestors or an unrecognised trial." .

pmp:ZhengaoShijieCountsSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Zhen'gao den Tod eines Übenden gegen die Operation zählt"@de,
        "Whether the Zhen'gao counts a practitioner's death against the operation"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-11" ;
    skos:note "Limits. One work, one recension, one set of characters, and a vocabulary that may name the thing otherwise: a passage treating a death as a plain failure could use no term in the list above. What the search establishes is that the terms the transmission itself uses for leaving the body all fall on one side."@en,
        "One near miss should be recorded because it is the closest the work comes to the passage that was looked for and still is not it. At 5.8a two of four men who compounded the elixir took it and died, and the two who saw this and refused are said to have obtained the lesser rank only, precisely because they did not perceive that the trial lay in the deaths. The observation that would count against the operation is reported, and the inference from it is scored as the failure."@en,
        "The search found the rule that makes the question unanswerable inside this transmission, which is a stronger outcome than a silence. Every state of a corpse is assigned to release through the corpse, including the states that would ordinarily be read as poisoning, and the preceding folio supplies named adepts whose bodies putrefied, bred worms, stank at a distance or were found in pieces, each counted as an attainment."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of all twenty juan for: 尸解 (twenty-four hits, concentrated in juan 4 and recurring in juan 5, 12, 13 and 14); 解形; 白日解; 而死 (nine hits, of which 5.8a is the elixir case); 死者; 不得仙 (three hits, none of them about a death); 不成; 敗. The juan 4 block and the juan 5 narratives were then read at the wording." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:ShangqingShijieZhengbian ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-11" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao, twenty juan (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile; searched over the whole work in the Chinese, with the passages carrying a hit read at the wording" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage in which the death of a person who carried out a prescribed operation is treated as evidence against the operation, rather than as a passage out of the world by another route. This is the counter-search owed by the reading of pmc:ShangqingShijieZhengbian and by the protocol built on it." .

pmp:ZosimosDefectusSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die letzte Abrechnung sagt, worauf ein ertraglos gebliebener Durchgang zu schieben ist"@de,
        "Whether the Final Reckoning says what a run that produced nothing is to be assigned to"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-27" ;
    skos:note "Beside it stands the one thing in this corpus that does read like a condition, and it points the other way: the warning at the point of attainment, which tells the addressee to fear the natural elements of matter once she recognises that she has reached perfection. It is a condition on what follows success and not on what explains its absence. The node is pmc:ZosimosCautioInPerfectione."@en,
        "Not searched: the technical treatises and the commentators of the same volume, which is the larger part of it, and the Greek text facing the translation. A condition of failure in one of those would not have been caught by these terms."@en,
        "What came back is not a condition of failure but the place where the corpus puts the outcome: the exposition has as its purpose to engage the addressee to try whether fortune is favourable to her. Fortune is not a state of anybody or anything, nothing establishes it beforehand and nothing shows it to have been absent, so an operation that produced nothing has an account available to it that no outcome can contradict. That is the opposing finding, and it is stronger than the silence the search expected."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-volume search for perfection, surnaturel, sacrifices, demons, essayer, Pasteur, Theosebie, marches, heures, cuisson and déchirer, all in the French of the edition; the paragraphs returned for each were read in full, and the four pages named were read through." ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:ZosimosFortunaFavens ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-27" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Zosimos, the Final Reckoning and the pages around it, in the French translation in M. Berthelot und Ch.-Em. Ruelle, Collection des anciens alchimistes grecs, seconde livraison, Paris 1888; read at the wording on pages 172, 190, 192 and 193, with the whole volume searched by term" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A stated condition of failure: a state of the operator, of the material, of the day or of a third party to which the corpus assigns an operation that did not come off, or a term after which nothing more is to be expected." .

pmp:ZosimosSignumSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Zosimos-Übersetzung ein Zeichen nennt, an dem das Gelingen des Werkes erkannt wird"@de,
        "Whether the Zosimos translation names a sign by which the work is known to have succeeded"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-27" ;
    skos:note "A second limit: the word for a sign need not be signe. The search would not have caught indice, marque, preuve, or a construction that names the appearance without naming it as a sign. The finding is as strong as the term, which is the whole reason the term is written down."@en,
        "The finding sits beside what the same corpus does carry, and that is what makes it worth a node. The intervals are exact and repeated — three hours, three times twenty-four hours, nine hours, six hours, not less than nine hours — and they are the only thing that tells the operator when to stop. A transmission that counts the time and never names the sign has made the clock its whole criterion, and that is a fact about the transmission and not a gap in this search."@en,
        "What the search cannot say, and it is a real limit. It was carried in one word in one language over one livraison. A sentence of exactly the shape sought is reported to stand in the third livraison of the same edition, where the Greek has, when you see the complete goldening, then know that you have made the complete iosis; that volume was not opened in this run and that sentence was not read. If it stands there, the finding above is a statement about this volume and not about Zosimos."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-volume search for signe, in the French of the edition. Seven hits, all of them in the apparatus to the Greek text, pages 9, 10, 12, 13, 19, 26 and 30, and every one of them about a manuscript abbreviation sign for a metal or a colour. No hit anywhere in the French translation of the Zosimos pieces, pages 111 to 197." ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-27" ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "M. Berthelot und Ch.-Em. Ruelle, Collection des anciens alchimistes grecs, seconde livraison, Paris 1888; searched over the whole volume, and read at the wording where a hit fell" ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage in which the corpus states what is to be seen, heard or found when an operation has succeeded: a colour reached, an appearance in the vessel, an assay with a perceptible outcome — anything that would let an operator tell a completed work from an incomplete one." .

pmp:ZuniPrayer a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Zuni-Festgebet"@de,
        "Zuni fixed prayer"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ruth L. Bunzel, Zuni Ritual Poetry, in Forty-Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of American Ethnology 1929-1930 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1932), p. 616" ;
    skos:definition "A prayer of stereotyped phrasing, held by an appointed keeper for each kiva and taught over four nights after each planting of prayer sticks, the instructor intoning while the pupils join in by degrees and afterwards reciting it back to him."@en ;
    skos:note "The teaching method is itself a check: the pupil returns the prayer to the keeper, so that a divergence is caught before it is carried into the ceremony. Because the phrasing is stereotyped the witness names the sequence, and not the wording, as the hard part of learning a long prayer."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ZuniRitual .

pmt:Alvar a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Ālvār-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Ālvār transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Tamil devotional transmission of the twelve Ālvār, in which the relation to the deity is sung in the first person and the states it passes through are named."@en ;
    skos:note "The earliest of the devotional corpora and the one on which the later theory of devotional states rests. Worth registering ahead of the others for the same reason the Gauḍīya file was opened: the states are named, not only described."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Nālāyira Divya Prabandham, in particular the Tiruvāymoḻi of Nammāḻvār" .

pmt:Amazonian a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Amazonische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Amazonian transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The lowland South American transmission of songs used to direct the effect of a plant preparation, and of a body that can be exchanged for another."@en ;
    skos:note "No corpus in its own language and no writing before contact. What exists is transcribed song and ethnographic report, which this project cannot yet admit as a source. Registered so that the exclusion is visible rather than silent."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteAbsent ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "No transmitted written corpus; the published icaro transcriptions and the ethnographic record stand in its place" .

pmt:AnalyticalPsychology a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Analytische Psychologie"@de,
        "Analytical psychology"@en ;
    skos:definition "Jung's transmission of an objective psyche and of active imagination as a procedure for engaging figures encountered in it, with a record kept of the engagement."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered as a tradition and not as science on the rule stated at the head of this file: its central claims rest on testimony, including the author's own record of his experiment, and not on procedures that could have come out otherwise. Where it does produce evidence, that evidence enters at an examination node like any other."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Jung, Liber Novus; Jung, Septem Sermones ad Mortuos; Jung, Mysterium Coniunctionis" .

pmt:ArabicAlchemy a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Arabische Alchemie"@de,
        "Arabic alchemy"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the balance, of elixirs and of the generation of life by art, carried under the name of Jābir by a corpus larger than one man."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Jābirian corpus; al-Rāzī, Sirr al-Asrār" .

pmt:AshtarTransmission a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Ashtar-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Ashtar transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of messages ascribed to a command or council of non-terrestrial beings charged with the oversight of the earth, received after the first by many transmitters who do not share a lineage."@en ;
    skos:note "The motif of a federation or confederation of worlds enters general circulation here and is afterwards found in corpora that never name Ashtar. That spread is a reception history and belongs in the graph as one, before anybody reads it as agreement between independent witnesses."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Van Tassel, the Ministry of Universal Wisdom publications; the published Ashtar message collections" .

pmt:AustralianDreaming a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Australische Aboriginal-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Australian Aboriginal transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Australian transmission of an ancestral order laid into country, held in song and in the routes the song follows."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered and deliberately left at corpus named. There is no corpus in the project's sense, much of the material is restricted by rules that are still enforced, and a public-domain dedication of restricted knowledge would be a taking and not an opening. The gate here protects ownership, not authority, and the project's criterion does not apply to it unchanged."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteAbsent ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "No transmitted written corpus; the ethnographic record stands in its place and much of it is restricted" .

pmt:BalineseTantra a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Balinesische Lontar-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Balinese lontar transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Balinese palm-leaf transmission of healing, protective diagrams and the composition of the person, at the far edge of the Indian sphere of influence."@en ;
    skos:note "Written, catalogued and barely used by comparative work. The contact question is genuinely open: how much is Indian import and how much is local is undecided in the literature."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteUndecided ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Usada healing lontar; Kanda Pat; the Aji Saraswati texts" .

pmt:BalticSlavicCharm a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Baltische und slawische Besprechungstradition"@de,
        "Baltic and Slavic charm transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The eastern European transmission of spoken charms for healing, protection and binding, fixed in writing only by nineteenth-century collectors."@en ;
    skos:note "The late fixation is the whole difficulty: the corpus is a collection and not a work, and the project has no attestation mode for that yet."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteUndecided ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The published zagovory collections; the Latvian dainas collections" .

pmt:BasharTransmission a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Bashar-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Bashar transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission received by Darryl Anka since 1984, teaching that acting on the strongest available excitement is the means by which one comes to occupy a different reality."@en ;
    skos:note "Unusually operative for a channelled corpus: the teaching is given as steps with stated conditions of failure, in front of audiences, on record."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Anka, Bashar: Blueprint for Change; the recorded session transcripts" .

pmt:BurmeseWeikza a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Burmesische Weikza-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Burmese weikza transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Burmese transmission of an accomplished practitioner who prolongs life by alchemy, diagram and mantra in order to await a future buddha."@en ;
    skos:note "A living tradition whose claims are made about named contemporaries, which is rare and worth more to an examination than any ancient ascription."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The published weikza and dhat-lonn literature in Burmese" .

pmt:Candomble a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Candomblé"@de,
        "Candomblé"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Brazilian transmission of the orixás, with an initiation that seats a spirit in the head of the initiate."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Bastide, Le candomblé de Bahia; Verger, Notes sur le culte des Orisa et Vodun" .

pmt:CastanedaToltec a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Castanedas toltekische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Castaneda's Toltec transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of a body of practice concerning attention, dreaming and the assemblage point, presented as the teaching of a Yaqui man of knowledge."@en ;
    skos:note "The fieldwork is not credible and the informant is not attestable, and the practices are nonetheless transmitted today by people who follow them. Both facts belong in the graph, which is why the tradition is registered rather than dismissed."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan; Castaneda, The Art of Dreaming" .

pmt:CayceReadings a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Die Cayce-Readings"@de,
        "The Cayce readings"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of several thousand readings given in a sleep-like state, most of them addressed to a named person about a stated condition, and taken down stenographically."@en ;
    skos:note "The one corpus in this block built for examination without intending to be: individual readings, individual recipients, dated, indexed, and with outcomes that were sometimes followed up. Whatever it yields, it yields countably."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The indexed Cayce readings held by the Association for Research and Enlightenment; Sugrue, There Is a River" .

pmt:ChaldeanOracles a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Chaldäische Orakel"@de,
        "Chaldean Oracles"@en ;
    skos:definition "The fragmentary hexameters that the later Platonists treated as revealed, and on which their theurgic practice was built."@en ;
    skos:note "The graph already holds the theurgy that rests on this text without holding the text."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Chaldean Oracles, transmitted in fragments through Proclus, Damascius and Psellos" .

pmt:Chan a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Chan"@de,
        "Chan"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Chinese transmission holding that awakening is sudden and that the mind is not to be polished but recognised."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Platform Sūtra; Linji lu; Biyan lu" .

pmt:ChaosMagic a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Chaosmagie"@de,
        "Chaos magic"@en ;
    skos:definition "The late twentieth century transmission treating belief as a tool to be adopted and discarded, and holding that the symbolic system employed is arbitrary if the technique is sound."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim that content is interchangeable and only technique carries effect is directly examinable, and if it held it would reorganise every convergence question the project asks."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Spare, The Book of Pleasure; Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut" .

pmt:ChristianTheosophy a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Christliche Theosophie"@de,
        "Christian theosophy"@en ;
    skos:definition "Böhme's transmission of a divine self-disclosure through opposition, reported as having been seen rather than reasoned."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Böhme, Aurora; Böhme, De signatura rerum" .

pmt:ContacteeTransmission a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Contactee-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Contactee transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The mid twentieth century transmission of teachings ascribed to visitors from other worlds, reported first as bodily meetings and later as messages received without one."@en ;
    skos:note "The shift from a claimed physical encounter to a claimed interior reception happens inside this literature and is datable. It is the hinge between the older revealed corpora and the later channelled ones."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Adamski, Inside the Space Ships; Menger, From Outer Space to You; van Tassel, I Rode a Flying Saucer" .

pmt:CopticMagic a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Koptische Zaubertradition"@de,
        "Coptic magical transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "Christian Egyptian operative texts in which biblical names, angelic invocation and older Egyptian procedure stand side by side without apology."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Coptic magical papyri and ostraca in the standard collections; the Testament of Solomon as its adjacent Greek witness" .

pmt:CourseInMiracles a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Kurs in Wundern"@de,
        "A Course in Miracles"@en ;
    skos:definition "A scribed corpus in three parts, teaching that the perceived world is a projection of guilt, and carrying a workbook of three hundred and sixty-five daily exercises in fixed order."@en ;
    skos:note "The workbook is a stated curriculum with an order and a duration, which is rare in this block and puts the corpus closer to the manual traditions than to the revealed cosmologies."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "A Course in Miracles: Text, Workbook for Students, Manual for Teachers" .

pmt:Druze a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Drusische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Druze transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "A closed transmission teaching the transfer of the soul into a new body at death, with an internal literature restricted to the initiated."@en ;
    skos:note "A case where the gate the project exists to remove is still shut and still enforced. Registration says so rather than pretending the corpus is available."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteUndecided ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Rasāʾil al-Ḥikma" .

pmt:Dzogchen a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Dzogchen"@de,
        "Dzogchen"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Tibetan transmission of a primordially free awareness, with a practice whose stages are counted by visions appearing under stated conditions of posture, light and darkness."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Nyingthig cycles; Longchenpa, Seven Treasuries" .

pmt:GoldenDawn a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Hermetic Order of the Golden Dawn"@de,
        "Hermetic Order of the Golden Dawn"@en ;
    skos:definition "The late Victorian transmission that welded kabbalistic, grimoire and Egyptian material into a graded initiatory curriculum with examinations."@en ;
    skos:note "The syncretism is documented in its own papers, which makes it the best available record of how a modern system is assembled from older parts and then presented as one."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The cipher manuscripts and knowledge lectures; Regardie, The Golden Dawn" .

pmt:HaitianVodou a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Haitianisches Vodou"@de,
        "Haitian Vodou"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Haitian transmission of spirits who arrive in a named person and speak, with a liturgy that assigns each an appearance, a rhythm and a manner."@en ;
    skos:note "Deren's account is a first-person report of being mounted by a spirit written by the observer herself, which is a rare form: participant testimony with the observation intact."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Métraux, Le vaudou haïtien; Deren, Divine Horsemen" .

pmt:Hasidism a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Chassidismus"@de,
        "Hasidism"@en ;
    skos:definition "The eastern European transmission of devekut as a continuous attachment attainable within ordinary life, and of contemplation as its means."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Toledot Yaakov Yosef; Keter Shem Tov; Shneur Zalman, Tanya" .

pmt:Hoodoo a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Hoodoo"@de,
        "Hoodoo"@en ;
    skos:definition "The African American transmission of workings for protection, luck, love and revenge, carried by practitioners without a priesthood or a central corpus."@en ;
    skos:note "The Hyatt collection is thousands of practitioner statements taken down verbatim with the speakers identified only by place. As a body of first-person practice testimony it has no equal in the project's reach."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Hyatt, Hoodoo, Conjuration, Witchcraft, Rootwork, five volumes" .

pmt:Huna a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Huna"@de,
        "Huna"@en ;
    skos:definition "An early twentieth century system of three selves and directed vital force, presented as the secret teaching of Hawaiian priests."@en ;
    skos:note "Constructed by an outsider from theosophical and New Thought material and rejected by Hawaiian scholars as a representation of anything Hawaiian. Registered as the transmission it actually is, so that the ascription can be entered as false without the material vanishing from the record."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles" .

pmt:Illuminationism a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Illuminationismus"@de,
        "Illuminationism"@en ;
    skos:definition "Suhrawardī's transmission of knowledge by presence and of an intermediate world of images held to be neither material nor mental but a place with its own standing."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim that the imaginal world is a place and not a faculty is an ontological commitment the graph will have to sort against every tradition that treats vision as internal."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Suhrawardī, Ḥikmat al-Ishrāq; Mullā Ṣadrā, al-Asfār al-Arbaʿa" .

pmt:InnerAsianTengrism a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Innerasiatische Tengri-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Inner Asian sky transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Turkic and Mongol transmission of a sky power that confers mandate and fortune, and of practitioners who address it."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteUndecided ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Secret History of the Mongols; the Orkhon inscriptions; the published Manas cycle" .

pmt:IslamicLetterMagic a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Islamische Buchstaben- und Namensmagie"@de,
        "Islamic letter and name magic"@en ;
    skos:definition "The operative transmission of the science of letters and of the divine names, with squares, talismans and hours of working."@en ;
    skos:note "The ascription of the best known handbook to al-Būnī is contested, and the corpus grew after him under his name. The dispute is the first node this tradition should carry."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Šams al-maʿārif corpus ascribed to al-Būnī; Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm" .

pmt:IsmailiCosmology a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Ismailitische Kosmologie"@de,
        "Ismaili cosmology"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Shia transmission of a graded emanation and of an inner meaning disclosed by initiation, together with the encyclopaedic Neoplatonism of the Brethren of Purity."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Sijistānī, Kashf al-Maḥjūb; Nāṣir-i Khusraw, Jāmiʿ al-Ḥikmatayn; Rasāʾil Ikhwān al-Ṣafāʾ" .

pmt:JapaneseZen a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Japanischer Zen"@de,
        "Japanese Zen"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Japanese transmission of seated practice as enactment rather than means, together with a literature on the illnesses that practice itself produces."@en ;
    skos:note "Hakuin's account of meditation sickness is a self-warning of the strongest kind the project can hold: a text prescribing a practice and describing its casualties, with a remedy."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Dōgen, Shōbōgenzō; Hakuin, Yasenkanna; Hakuin, Orategama" .

pmt:Kabbalah a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Kabbala"@de,
        "Kabbalah"@en ;
    skos:definition "The medieval and early modern Jewish transmission of the sefirot as the structure of emanation, and of the human act as bearing on it."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Sefer ha-Bahir; Zohar; Cordovero, Pardes Rimmonim; Vital, Ez Ḥayyim and Šaʿar ha-Gilgulim" .

pmt:Kalacakra a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Kālacakra"@de,
        "Kālacakra"@en ;
    skos:definition "The tantric transmission of a sixfold yoga whose completion is marked by a sequence of signs appearing in a darkened retreat."@en ;
    skos:note "The sign sequence is stated in fixed order and is therefore comparable with the Dzogchen visions and with the phosphene literature, which is a convergence question the graph cannot currently pose."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Vimalaprabhā; Sekoddeśa and its commentary" .

pmt:KeylonticScience a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Keylontic Science und die MCEO-Lehren"@de,
        "Keylontic Science and the MCEO teachings"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of a detailed geometry of subtle anatomy together with a claimed ancient lineage of guardianship, delivered through a living transmitter and taught in graded courses with their own technical vocabulary."@en ;
    skos:note "Two claims to keep apart. That it transmits is a fact: texts, a transmitter, courses, students, successors, and it is registered on that ground alone. That it descends from an ancient priesthood is an ascription with a modern author and a modern date, attested nowhere outside the corpus, and it enters as a pm:Originating node when it enters. The material is under active copyright, so nothing is reproduced; the definitions here are the compiler's own paraphrase, as everywhere in this graph."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Ashayana Deane, Voyagers I and Voyagers II; the published MCEO Freedom Teachings course materials" .

pmt:KoreanMuism a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Koreanischer Musok"@de,
        "Korean muism"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Korean transmission of the mudang, whose office is entered through a sickness resolved only by accepting it, and whose ritual is a negotiation carried out in the voice of the dead."@en ;
    skos:note "Chiefly oral. The corpus is a collection of transcribed ritual songs, which the project has no attestation mode for yet."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteUndecided ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The published muga song transcriptions; Akiba and Akamatsu's collected materials" .

pmt:LucumiSanteria a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Lucumí"@de,
        "Lucumí"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Cuban transmission of the orichas, carrying the Yoruba divinatory corpus in a shortened form alongside Catholic imagery."@en ;
    skos:note "Its relation to Ifá is a documented case of a corpus surviving a forced transfer with measurable loss, which is examinable rather than merely regrettable."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Cabrera, El Monte; the published libretas" .

pmt:Manichaean a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Manichäische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Manichaean transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission founded on Mani's own account of a companion who appeared to him and disclosed what he taught."@en ;
    skos:note "The Cologne Mani Codex carries a first-person account of the syzygos, which is rare for late antiquity and rarer still with a name attached."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Kephalaia; Cologne Mani Codex; the Coptic psalms and the Turfan fragments" .

pmt:ModernVipassana a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Moderne Vipassanā-Bewegung"@de,
        "Modern vipassanā movement"@en ;
    skos:definition "The twentieth century Burmese reform transmission of insight practice as a lay technique detachable from monastic life, taught in fixed courses."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered separately from the canon and the commentaries because it is a distinct transmission with its own founders and manuals, and because its relation to the older material, in particular its treatment of absorption, is contested inside the tradition itself."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Mahāsi Sayādaw, Manual of Insight; Ledi Sayādaw, the Manuals of Buddhism" .

pmt:ModernWicca a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Moderne Wicca"@de,
        "Modern Wicca"@en ;
    skos:definition "The mid twentieth century transmission of an initiatory craft with a coven structure, a seasonal calendar and an inherited book copied by hand."@en ;
    skos:note "Presented at its founding as the survival of an old religion. The construction is documented in detail, so the tradition and the refutation of its pedigree can be held together without either being lost."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Gardner, Witchcraft Today; the Book of Shadows in the published recensions" .

pmt:Nath a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Nāth-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Nāth transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The lineage of Gorakhnāth, teaching a body perfected by practice and a state in which mind is said to be absent rather than concentrated."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Siddha-Siddhānta-Paddhati; Gorakṣaśataka; Amanaska" .

pmt:NeoConfucianCultivation a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Neokonfuzianische Selbstkultivierung"@de,
        "Neo-Confucian self-cultivation"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of quiet sitting and of the investigation of things as a discipline of attention that claims no transcendent object."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered as a control condition. A contemplative practice with the same postures and much of the same phenomenology, and without the metaphysical claim, is what any examination of the others needs to have alongside them."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Zhu Xi, Zhuzi yulei on jingzuo; Wang Yangming, Chuanxilu; Mencius 2A.2 on the flood-like qi" .

pmt:Neoshamanism a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Neoschamanismus"@de,
        "Neoshamanism"@en ;
    skos:definition "The modern transmission of a journey undertaken to a drumbeat and taught in workshops, presented as a technique abstracted from many indigenous traditions at once."@en ;
    skos:note "The abstraction claim is the point of interest: it asserts that a core method survives the removal of every particular tradition. The graph can hold that assertion beside the traditions it claims to abstract from, which is exactly what it was built to do."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Harner, The Way of the Shaman" .

pmt:Onmyodo a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Onmyōdō"@de,
        "Onmyōdō"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Japanese office of yin-yang divination and calendrical prohibition, practised by a licensed bureau of the state."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Hoki naiden; the onmyō provisions of the Engishiki" .

pmt:PaloMonte a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Palo Monte"@de,
        "Palo Monte"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Cuban transmission of Kongo derivation, working through a charged vessel and a pact with a named spirit of the dead."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Cabrera, Reglas de Congo" .

pmt:Pancaratra a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Pāñcarātra"@de,
        "Pāñcarātra"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Vaiṣṇava tantric transmission, in which the practitioner's body is dissolved and rebuilt by placement before any worship may proceed."@en ;
    skos:note "The body-construction sequence is a prerequisite chain of unusual explicitness and belongs in the graph for that reason before any doctrine of the school does."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Jayākhya Saṃhitā; Lakṣmī Tantra; Sātvata Saṃhitā" .

pmt:PropheticKabbalah a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Prophetische Kabbala"@de,
        "Prophetic Kabbalah"@en ;
    skos:definition "Abulafia's transmission of letter permutation, breath and head movement as a technique for reaching a state he calls prophecy."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered apart from Kabbalah because it is a manual and not a doctrine. On the level of stated procedure it is the closest western counterpart to the Yogasūtra that exists, including its warnings about what befalls the unprepared."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Abulafia, Or ha-Sekhel; Abulafia, Ḥayyei ha-Olam ha-Ba" .

pmt:RaMaterial a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Das Ra-Material"@de,
        "The Ra material"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of one hundred and six sessions received between 1981 and 1984 by a group of three working in fixed roles, teaching a cosmology of densities, a choice between service to others and service to self, and a harvest at the close of a cycle."@en ;
    skos:note "The best documented reception in the block. Questioner, instrument and scribe are named, the sessions are numbered and dated, the working conditions are described including the instrument's physical decline, and the transmission continued under a different contact after this one ended. The cosmological claims are largely undecidable; the claims about the conditions of contact and about what the work cost the instrument are not, and that is where an examination would begin."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Elkins, Rueckert and McCarty, The Law of One, Books I to V; the L/L Research session transcripts" .

pmt:Raelism a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Raëlismus"@de,
        "Raëlism"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission founded on Claude Vorilhon's reported meeting of 1973, holding that humanity was made by a non-terrestrial people and reading scripture as the record of that making."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered because it performs the characteristic move of the block in its clearest form: it rereads received texts as accounts of contact. That reinterpretation is a claim about the older corpora and belongs in the graph beside them."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Vorilhon, Le livre qui dit la vérité; Vorilhon, Les extra-terrestres m'ont emmené sur leur planète" .

pmt:Reiki a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Reiki"@de,
        "Reiki"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Japanese transmission of a healing energy conveyed by the hands, transmitted through an attunement conferred by a teacher in a stated lineage."@en ;
    skos:note "Attunement is a transmission claim in the strict sense: the capacity is said to pass from person to person by a discrete act. That is unusually well suited to examination, because the tradition itself distinguishes those who have received it from those who have not."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Usui Reiki Ryōhō Hikkei; Hayashi, Reiki Ryōhō Shishin" .

pmt:Samkhya a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Sāṃkhya"@de,
        "Sāṃkhya"@en ;
    skos:definition "The enumerative transmission of the evolutes of nature and of a witnessing principle distinct from them, on which the yoga of the graph already rests."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Īśvarakṛṣṇa, Sāṃkhyakārikā; Yuktidīpikā" .

pmt:SantNada a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Sant- und Nāda-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Sant and inner-sound transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The north Indian transmission of an inner sound held to be audible under instruction, and of union without image or rite."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Kabīr granthāvalī; Ādi Granth; Nāda-bindu Upaniṣad" .

pmt:SethMaterial a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Das Seth-Material"@de,
        "The Seth material"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission received by Jane Roberts from 1963, teaching that reality is constructed by belief and that the constructing can be observed and altered by the one who does it."@en ;
    skos:note "States its central claim in a form that asks for a procedure: beliefs are to be examined and changed, and the change is said to show. The sessions were numbered and dated from the start."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Roberts, Seth Speaks; Roberts, The Nature of Personal Reality; The Early Sessions" .

pmt:Shingon a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Shingon"@de,
        "Shingon"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Japanese esoteric transmission claiming buddhahood in the present body, achieved through the coordination of gesture, speech and visualisation."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Kūkai, Sokushin jōbutsu gi; Kūkai, Hizō hōyaku" .

pmt:Shugendo a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Shugendō"@de,
        "Shugendō"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Japanese transmission of mountain austerity, in which power is held to follow from ordeals performed at named places in a fixed order."@en ;
    skos:note "The self-mummification practice gives the project something almost nothing else does: a stated procedure with a documented outcome and documented failures, both counted."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Shozan engi; the Shugendō shūyō himitsu shū" .

pmt:Srivaisnava a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Śrīvaiṣṇavische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Śrīvaiṣṇava transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission descending from Rāmānuja, which gives the devotional relation a scholastic form and disputes within itself over whether the devotee acts or is acted upon."@en ;
    skos:note "The internal dispute of the two schools, over whether surrender requires an act of the devotee or none, is a candidate for a pm:Disputing node with two attested sides inside one transmission."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Rāmānuja, Śrībhāṣya and Gadyatraya; Vedānta Deśika, Rahasyatrayasāra; Piḷḷai Lokācārya, Śrīvacanabhūṣaṇa" .

pmt:Srividya a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Śrīvidyā"@de,
        "Śrīvidyā"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the goddess as sound and diagram, with an initiatory sequence tied to the levels of a single mantra."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Yoginīhṛdaya; Nityāṣoḍaśikārṇava; Bhāskararāya, Setubandha" .

pmt:SyriacAsceticism a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Syrische Askese"@de,
        "Syriac ascetic transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The East Syriac transmission of solitary prayer, of the stages of stillness and of the wonder held to interrupt prayer rather than complete it."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Isaac of Nineveh, Ascetical Homilies; John of Dalyatha, Letters; Evagrius, Kephalaia Gnostica in the Syriac recension" .

pmt:TamilSiddhar a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Tamilische Siddhar-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Tamil Siddhar transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Tamil transmission of bodily perfection, rejuvenation regimens and a deliberately obscure poetic idiom that resists the commentary it invites."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Tirumūlar, Tirumandiram; the Bogar and Agastyar corpora; the kāya-kalpa literature" .

pmt:TheravadaCommentarial a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Theravādische Kommentarhandbücher"@de,
        "Theravāda commentarial manuals"@en ;
    skos:definition "The manual layer of Theravāda: the objects of concentration, the marks by which each absorption is recognised, and the higher knowledges said to follow from mastery."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered apart from the canon because the canon states that the attainments occur and the manuals state how to reach them and how to tell whether one has."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga; Upatissa, Vimuttimagga; Paṭisambhidāmagga" .

pmt:UrantiaBook a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Das Urantia-Buch"@de,
        "The Urantia Book"@en ;
    skos:definition "A single large revealed cosmology published in 1955, presenting itself as a series of papers by named celestial authors and describing a graded administration of the universe."@en ;
    skos:note "The human circumstances of its production were withheld on purpose by the group that published it, which leaves its provenance the weakest in a block whose provenance is otherwise its strength."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Urantia Book" .

pmt:VaisnavaSahajiya a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Vaiṣṇava-Sahajiyā-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Vaiṣṇava Sahajiyā transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Bengali transmission that takes the devotional relation as a bodily practice, holding that the states named by the aesthetic theory are to be produced rather than contemplated."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered because it is the one station of this sphere that turns a doctrine of states into a procedure, which is what SPEC §14 puts first. Its corpus is small, partly clandestine, and the attribution of several works is contested; that is to be settled before the file is opened, not after."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Vaiṣṇava Sahajiyā padāvalī; Ānanda-bhairava; Vivarta-vilāsa attributed to Ākiñcana-dāsa" .

pmt:Varkari a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Vārkarī-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Vārkarī transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Marathi devotional transmission of the pilgrimage to Paṇḍharpūr, carried in the songs of its poet-saints rather than in a treatise."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Jñāneśvarī; the abhaṅga of Tukārām, Nāmdev and Eknāth" .

pmt:Yarsan a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Yarsanische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Yarsan transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "An Iranian transmission teaching successive bodily lives as a purification, carried in Gorani verse."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteUndecided ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Kalām-e Saranjām" .

pmt:Yazidi a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Jesidische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Yazidi transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "A Kurdish transmission carried chiefly in hymn and recitation, teaching successive returns of the soul."@en ;
    skos:note "The two books circulated as its scripture are held by most scholarship to be forgeries of the early twentieth century, which makes the corpus question itself the first thing to decide."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteUndecided ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Qewls (hymns) in the published collections; the disputed Mišefa Reš and Kitêba Cilwe" .

pmt:Yogacara a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Yogācāra"@de,
        "Yogācāra"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of consciousness-only doctrine together with the most detailed Indian description of meditative signs and the stages at which they appear."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Asaṅga, Yogācārabhūmi, particularly the Śrāvakabhūmi; Vasubandhu, Triṃśikā" .

pmc:AbodaZaraRainFollowsTheSlaughter a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Wenn sie ihm einen Menschen geschlachtet haben, kommt Regen"@de,
        "When they have slaughtered a man for it, rain comes"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Aboda zara 55a, Raba b. Jishaq to R. Jehuda, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. VII (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1903), p. 991" ;
    skos:definition "The claim Raba b. Jishaq brings to R. Jehuda, and he brings it as something that happens rather than as something taught: the idol promises rain for a human sacrifice and the rain comes. It is reported by a man of the place to a teacher as a difficulty, which is what makes it an unusual witness in this holding - an efficacy claim about a rival practice, carried into the record by somebody who holds the practice to be capital."@en ;
    skos:note "The capacity and the practice are kept apart from the account the transmission then gives of them, which stands at pmc:AbodaZaraTheIdolatersAreTalkedOver. The sequence rain-after-slaughter is what the speaker says he sees; that God lets it happen in order to condemn them is what his teacher says about it. A node holding both would make the explanation part of the observation, and the whole interest of the passage is that the two are separable."@en,
        "Why pm:firstPersonReport and not pm:textualAttestation. The mode asks how the claim entered the record, and here a named man says that this is so in his own town. The written transmission of that report is the medium and not the testimony. Nothing in the passage says he watched a slaughter."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AbodaZaraSlaughterForRain ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliAbodaZara ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AbodaZaraRainOnDemandOfTheIdol .

pmc:AbodaZaraTheIdolatersAreTalkedOver a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Wirkung wird zugegeben und einer absichtlichen Taeuschung der Goetzendiener zugeschrieben"@de,
        "The effect is granted and assigned to a deliberate deception of the idolaters"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Aboda zara 55a, R. Jehuda on Deuteronomy 4:19 and Resh Laqish on Proverbs 3:34, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. VII (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1903), p. 991" ;
    skos:definition "R. Jehuda's answer to the rain, and it is the second reassignment of the same page. He does not say that the rain fails to come. He grounds it in the verse that the Lord allotted the host of heaven to all the peoples, read so that the allotting is a talking-over: God smoothed the idolaters with words in order to drive them out of the world. Resh Laqish is attached with the verse that he scorns the scorners and gives grace to the humble, and with the rule that to him who would defile himself the doors are opened and to him who would purify himself help is given. The effect is real, it is produced, and it is produced against the interest of the people it is produced for."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the harder of the two accounts on the page and the two should be compared, which is why both are nodes. At pmc:AbodaZaraAfflictionsSwornToTheirHour the connection between the operation and the outcome is cut: the cure was coming anyway. Here the connection is left standing and its sign is reversed: the operation works and working is the punishment. The first account can be put to a test in principle, because it says the cure would have arrived without the visit; the second cannot be put to any test at all, because every outcome of the operation confirms it. A transmission that holds both has an answer for a success and an answer for a failure and needs neither to be true."@en,
        "What this does to the counter-transmission the first file built. There the question was whether a performance did something, and the criteria were forensic - blood and dung as the trace of a real deed, water that dissolves a transformation. Here the question is what follows from its having done something, and the transmission answers it without using any of those criteria. The two halves of the same transmission therefore stand at different distances from a test, and a reader who meets only the forensic half will overstate how far it goes."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliAbodaZara ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmc:AdmissibilityBoundToTheCalendar a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Über die Zulässigkeit des Vorgangs entscheidet das Datum und keine der beiden Seiten"@de,
        "The date, and not either party, decides whether the operation may be done"@en ;
    skos:note "A counter-doctrine was found on 2026-09-11 and is recorded here rather than in a file of its own, because it bounds this statement without refuting it. The internal alchemy denies the binding in the stream where the binding is otherwise general: the thirteenth quatrain of the Jindan sizi holds that the fire phases use no hour and that the winter solstice is not at zi, and its commentary adds that one may not be bound to the four seasons. That is not a transmission that binds an operation to a calendar and would have to say where the failure lies; it is a transmission that refuses to bind and puts the condition in the practitioner. The statement above is about the first kind and this is the second, so the antecedent is not met — but a stream that contains both is a reminder that the class is not a property of a culture. What makes the case sharp is that the same corpus holds both positions: the Zhong-Lü chuandao ji binds throughout and makes ignorance of the times a sufficient cause of total failure, and the Wuzhen pian does both within one juan of one recension. See pmc:NeidanHuohouDispute."@en,
        "A fourth witness was added on 2026-09-11 from the Zhen'gao, and it does not merely repeat the others. Its prohibited days are given with the reckoning behind them, the woman's palace in shen and the man's in yin standing in mutual punishment, so the condition is located in the date and in the relation of the two signs and in neither party; and the passage adds that the person does not notice the harm while the body has already been damaged, which places the failure beyond the reach of either party's report as well. That is the statement above in its strongest available form."@en,
        "A ninth witness was added on 2026-09-21 from the Indian medical literature, and it is the densest single statement of the first clause the class has. The rasāyana lesson of the Caraka binds the entry into its built chamber to four calendar conditions at once, the sun in its northern course, the lighted fortnight, a propitious lunation and constellation, a favourable muhūrta and karaṇa, on an auspicious day; and the same lesson binds five further operations to a month or a full moon: the powder buried through the rains, the roots dug in Māgha or Phālguna in an auspicious hour, the bhallātaka gathered in Śuci or Śukra and given in Sahas or Sahasya, the fruits plucked by hand in Māgha or Phālguna, the forest entered on the full moon of Pauṣa, Māgha or Phālguna after a three-day fast. Six windows in one lesson, every one of them a date and none of them a state of a party."@en,
        "A seventh witness was added on 2026-09-18, and it tests the statement rather than repeating it. The demotic manual of London and Leiden binds nine of its operations to a time — a half-month of the moon from the fourth day to the fifteenth, the fifteenth day when the moon fills the uzat, the third and the fourteenth of the lunar month, midday, the seventh hour of the day, the third hour of the evening, the moment of the sun rising entire, a named constellation, and the moon in Leo — and the condition is in every case the date and never a state of either party, which is the first half of the statement. The second half it does not supply: it states no consequence for acting outside any of the nine windows. The witness therefore confirms the antecedent nine times over and leaves the consequent unattested, which is worth entering because the statement is a conditional and a case that meets the antecedent without meeting the consequent is where it would break. It does not break here — the statement claims only that where such a consequence is stated it lies in the time — but the case shows how much of the class is silent."@en,
        "An eighth witness was added on 2026-09-20 from the Mandaean material, and it is the first that carries both clauses in one word. The days it names are mbattal, and the transmission uses that word of a day in exactly the sense it would use of an operation: useless. On such a day no undertaking is to be begun and no religious ceremony performed; the condition is the date of the month and nothing else; and the failure is located in the day, which is the second clause stated as plainly as the class allows. The witness also supplies the thing the seventh did not, a consequence with a repair attached: a man who dies on such a day must have the blessed oblation performed for him on a substitute at the spring festival, so the transmission both names the loss and prices its recovery."@en,
        "Both new witnesses carry the first clause and neither carries the second, so they change the count and not the balance: the handbook names the day and the quarter and says nothing about what befalls an act done outside them, and the Herbarium does the same. The holding therefore has four witnesses for how the condition is stated and two for where the failure is located, and the second clause still rests on the Chinese material alone."@en,
        "Eleventh witness, added on 2026-09-23 from the Celestial Masters, and it is the densest single case the class holds. The petition manual fixes the sending of a petition by five separately computed prohibitions at once: a monthly table with a named stem-branch day and a further count of days for each of the twelve months, plus eight branch-days; the days of heaven's father, the third, ninth, fifteenth and twenty-seventh, on which no petition is sent up; the days of heaven's mother; a monthly table of the days of the killing of heaven, which a petition must avoid; the days on which the officers of heaven attend the audience at heaven's gate, on which none may be presented; and the days on which heaven and earth are shut. Every one is a date and none is a state of a party. It carries the first clause and not the second: the manual says the petition is not to be sent on those days and nowhere says what befalls one sent on them."@en,
        "Falsified by a transmission of this kind whose earliest witness names a compensating act — a substitute rite, a correction, a second attempt — that restores the effect for an operation performed outside the window, or one that makes the window itself depend on a state of the operator, since either would put the decision back into a party's hands. Weakened, without being refuted, if the windows of two such transmissions turn out to be derived from one another rather than arrived at separately; the two here are both Chinese and a route between them is not excluded, which is why the statement claims a recurring form and not an independent attestation."@en,
        "It matters for the spread as well as for the count. Six of the seven earlier witnesses stand in the Chinese and European spheres and the seventh in the Graeco-Egyptian; this one is West Asian, its contact route to the others is disputed rather than documented, and a run that wants to know whether the form is a property of a culture now has a fourth sphere to reckon with. What it does not do is make the second clause independent: the Mandaean rule reaches the graph through a field record of the nineteen-thirties, not through the scripture, and a search of the Ginzā and of the prayerbook for the same rule came back empty. See pmp:MandaeanGinzaTaxatioSearch."@en,
        "It narrows the statement and does not refute it, and the difference is worth being exact about. The chapter does not say that an operation performed outside its window works; it says that a man of this attainment no longer chooses days at all, which is a claim about who stands under the rule. Had it said that the practice takes effect in the hours of dead qi for such a man, the first clause would be gone."@en,
        "One of the two also marks a limit that the earlier witnesses did not show, and it is recorded here rather than left for a reader to notice. The handbook's slip against the falling sickness carries, in the same heading, a condition on the sufferer: the remedy is for one who has not yet fallen into fire or water. A calendar condition and a person-condition therefore stand on one prescription. That does not touch the statement, which says how a calendar condition is stated and not that a transmission may state nothing else; but it shows that the two kinds are not alternatives in practice, and a later run that reads the first clause as an exclusion of person-conditions would be reading it too widely."@en,
        "Tenth witness, added on 2026-09-22 from Renaissance learned magic. The second book requires that a planet be placed in its dignities, fortunate, powerful and ruling in the day, in the hour and in the figure of the heaven, and that the moon be opportunely directed, and then says flatly that thou shalt do nothing without the assistance of the moon. The condition is a position of the luminaries and nothing is asked of either party; the consequence is stated as an impossibility and not as a diminution, which is the strong form of the second half of this statement. It comes with the difficulty the statement does not cover: the same page offers three separate celestial conditions, so a single failure has a choice of causes and the operator is given no way to tell which failed him."@en,
        "That narrowness is the reason for the two witnesses added on 2026-09-13, and they are the first from outside the Chinese sphere. The European folk-magic handbook binds a whole class of its acts to a named weekday, an hour before sunrise and a named quarter of the moon — Friday before sunrise in the waning moon for the knotting of twigs, the first Friday in the new light for the slip against the falling sickness, the last Friday in the old light for the letter against rheumatism. The Old English Herbarium binds one remedy for the same disease to two calendar conditions at once, the waning moon and the sun in the Virgin, which the text itself resolves into the month of August. Both were read at the wording; the second was found by a counter-search that was looking for something else. The spread is now three transmissions and two spheres between which no route has been proposed."@en,
        "The consequent it does not supply at all, and that silence is now the commonest case in the class. At none of the six does the lesson say what befalls a user who acts outside the window, which makes this the second witness after the demotic manual to confirm the antecedent many times over and leave the consequent unattested. Of nine witnesses four now state where the failure lies and five do not. A statement whose second clause is carried by fewer than half its predecessors should be read as two statements, and a later run may want to split it."@en,
        "The counter-search was run against the compiled holding before this was written, for transmissions that bind an operation to a time and for transmissions that refuse to. Nothing in the holding says of an operation of this kind that it works whatever the day. What the search did turn up is the case that keeps the second clause from being stated more strongly: the operative transmission of the science of letters prescribes seven days of the lunation on which its orations are to be said, and the holding already carries that at prescribed order rather than at stated necessity, on the ground that the text does not say what follows if a day is missed. A window with no stated failure is therefore a real and already recorded shape, and the statement is worded so that such a case does not falsify it."@en,
        "The density is itself a finding and it belongs with the note on Renaissance magic above, which records the same difficulty at three conditions. Here there are five, laid over one operation, computed by different reckonings and not reduced to one another. An officiant whose petition brought nothing has five available dates to have fallen foul of, and no procedure for telling which, since the manual names no consequence for any of them. A calendar of that density stops being a condition on the operation and becomes a supply of explanations for its absence."@en,
        "The finding is also a lesson about the counter-search this class requires. It ran against the compiled holding, as SPEC §16 prescribes, and came back empty; the case that limits the claim was in the unread part of a work already cited three times in this graph. Against a generalization drawn from two or three witnesses, the cheapest remaining counter-search is not another query but reading the rest of the works the witnesses come from. See prima-materia#674."@en,
        "The nearest counter-case was found on the day this node was written, and it was not in the holding at the time of the counter-search. It stands in the eighteenth chapter of the work whose eighth chapter supplies the first witness: one who can guard the One, it says, need not divine the day or choose the hour, no longer consults the siting calendars, does not avoid the year star or the taboos of his year and fate, and in the end never meets with calamity. The exemption is granted by a state of the operator, which is the thing the first clause says a calendar-bound condition is not stated in terms of. It is what removed the word alone from that clause, and it is the reason the clause now says how the condition is stated rather than what it depends on."@en,
        "The one thing the search found on the other side is not a transmission but a critic. The seventh-century memorial on the burial books argues that the rites fix the interval between death and burial by rank, so that no year or month is chosen, and counts burials in the classical annals on the day the books call worst of all. That bears on whether the second witness is right and not on what it claims, and it is held where it belongs, at the dispute node beside the burial book."@en,
        "The same run supplied the class its first outright refusal, and it is the strongest counter-material this statement has met. The counter-doctrine recorded below from the internal alchemy declines to bind and puts the condition in the practitioner; the code of the Celestial Masters forbids the binding and assigns it to the opposing party. In dwelling, in settling a tomb, in moving and in every undertaking one does not divine the day or enquire after the hour but acts as the heart directs, with nothing avoided and nothing sought; the books of siting are to be driven out by petition; and the keeping of a calendar and the picking of days and hours is named among the present abuses as a folly grown worse which the correct code has made plain and men will not follow. The refusal is placed in a list whose other members are sacrificing to ghosts and telling fortunes, so the calendar is classed with the practices the transmission defines itself against. The node is pmc:CmBukaoRiwen."@en,
        "The shape of these claims is worth stating beside the claim, because it runs in two directions at once and this is the class in which both are visible in one sentence. A condition fixed by a calendar is the most examinable kind this holding carries: it is public, it is settled before the case, and no party to the operation can adjust it afterwards. The same condition is also the cheapest available defence, because any session, burial or needling that produced nothing can be assigned to the hour after the fact. Which of the two a given transmission intends is not decided here and probably cannot be decided from the texts."@en,
        "The three witnesses. The Baopuzi says that the circulation of the qi is to be done in the hours of living qi and not in the hours of dead qi, and that in the hours of dead qi it is of no benefit; the boundary is midnight and noon and nothing else enters it. The burial book names as the second of six adversities a going wrong of the year and the season, which makes the burial adverse however favourable the ground and however correct the rite. The Lingshu binds five needling operations to the stems of the day: on jia and yi days not the head, on bing and ding days not shoulder and throat, and so through the cycle."@en,
        "The two clauses of the statement are not equally carried, and the division is the useful part. All three witnesses carry the first: the condition is a date, and no examination of anybody could establish or remove it. Only two carry the second, because the Lingshu names the days and the places and stops, without saying what befalls one needled at the head on a jia day. A fourth place in the same corpus, the twelve mis-needlings by season, does state consequences, which suggests the silence belongs to that chapter rather than to the transmission; that is a reason to look further and not a reason to count it."@en,
        "The witness also brings its own qualification, and it is sharper than the one the internal alchemy supplied. The same lesson offers, in the verse that names the chamber, a second route carrying no date at all, the route of wind and sun, and assigns it in its fourth section to everyone who is not strong, healthy, intellectual, self-controlled, forgiving and possessed of means; it then offers a third, the rasāyana of conduct, which has neither drug nor chamber nor date, and claims the same result for it. The binding and the release stand in one lesson, for one operation, and the release is given to the larger class of users. What the lesson does not do is say that the operation works on any day: the release is by omission and not by denial. See pmp:AyurvedaKalanirapeksaSearch, which put both questions, and pmc:TheWaiverRunsBothWays, which is the generalization formed from the release."@en,
        "Two of the three witnesses stand in the same tradition. The spread is therefore two transmissions and not three, and the claim is correspondingly weaker than the count of predecessors suggests. See prima-materia#666."@en,
        "What makes that refusal cost something rather than merely bound the statement. It does not stand outside the transmission that supplies the eleventh witness; it stands inside it, in a different work, and the two are set against one another at pmc:CmRiwenDispute. One transmission both forbids the calendar as the mark of heterodoxy and prints forty folios of it for its own central rite. The antecedent of this statement is therefore not met by a tradition but at most by a work, and a run that counts witnesses by tradition is counting the wrong unit."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:AgrippaSineLuna,
        pmc:AyurvedaKutipravesaKala,
        pmc:CmZhangjiaoJiri,
        pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicAsterionPresupposesFenster,
        pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicMondfenster,
        pmc:MandaeanDiesMbattal,
        pmc:PdmHoraEtLuna,
        pmc:SanjiLiuxiong,
        pmc:ShangqingJinri,
        pmc:ShengqiSiqi,
        pmc:Wujin ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Transmissions that bind an operation to a calendar state the condition as a date and not as a state of the operator or of the person operated on; and where such a transmission states a consequence for acting outside the window, it locates the failure in the time rather than in either party."@en .

pmc:AdornmentAndCosmetics a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Schmuck und Kosmetik"@de,
        "Adornment and cosmetics"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:1 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "Knowledge of bracelets and ornaments, of antimony and the painting of the eyelids, of precious stones and colouring tinctures."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:AegyptiaAritaeTransitus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Weder zurückgewiesen noch abgewiesen vor Osiris"@de,
        "Neither turned back nor repulsed before Osiris"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 147, closing rubric of the Papyrus of Thenna, printed as a footnote in E. A. Wallis Budge, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II, p. 479" ;
    skos:definition "What the chapter's closing rubric says the recitation obtains. If these words be recited by the deceased when he cometh to the seven arits and entereth into the pylons, he shall neither be turned back nor repulsed before Osiris; he shall be made to have his being among the blessed Khus and to have dominion among the principal followers of Horus; and if these things be done for any deceased person he shall have his being there like a lord of eternity in one body along with Osiris, and at no place shall any great fight be made concerning him."@en ;
    skos:note "The formulation is negative on one side — neither turned back nor repulsed — which is as close as this chapter comes to naming the failure it averts. It still does not say what happens to a man who recites nothing."@en,
        "The rubric is not in the Papyrus of Ani. Budge prints it in a footnote as an addition of the Papyrus of Thenna the scribe, which is the ordinary state of the rubrics in this corpus and the reason every source string here names a manuscript. A node that cited chapter 147 without the manuscript would assert of Ani's papyrus something Ani's papyrus does not contain."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmc:AegyptiaConfessioNegativa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die zweiundvierzig Anreden: ein Gott, eine Verneinung"@de,
        "The forty-two addresses: one god, one denial"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 125, the address to the forty-two gods, Papyrus of Ani, in the transliteration and English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London: British Museum, 1895, plates XXXI-XXXII, pp. 198-203" ;
    skos:definition "The form the second half of chapter 125 takes in the Papyrus of Ani. Forty-two times the dead man says Hail, followed by a name, followed by the place the god comes forth from, followed by one thing he has not done. Nothing else stands in the unit: no petition, no offering, no act. The denial is bound to the god by position alone, and the binding is the whole content of the address."@en ;
    skos:note "Two counts that do not agree, and they are in the text rather than read into it. There are forty-two addresses and there are forty-four denials, because address 33 carries I have not harmed and I have not done evil, and address 41 carries I have not carried off the food of the infant and I have not sinned against the god of my town. The apparatus is built to a number and the material does not divide by it."@en,
        "Two denials are also repeated word for word: addresses 20 and 21 both say I have not defiled the wife of a man, and addresses 17 and 31 both say I have not been an eavesdropper. A list assembled to fill forty-two places behaves like one."@en,
        "What the form is for is stated at pmc:AegyptiaPortaeNomina and not here: the man passes because he can say the name. The denial is what he says once he has the name right, and no part of the chapter makes the passage depend on the denial being true."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmc:AegyptiaCorNeTestetur a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Mein Herz, meine Mutter: dass es sich nicht von mir trenne vor dem, der die Waage hält"@de,
        "My heart my mother: let it not part from me before him who keepeth the scales"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 30B, Papyrus of Ani with additions from the Papyrus of Nebseni, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London 1901, vol. I, pp. 150-151" ;
    skos:definition "The words said over the scarab. The heart is addressed as mother and as the speaker's own ka, and asked for four things: that nothing stand up to oppose him at his judgment, that there be no opposition to him in the presence of the sovereign princes, that it not part from him in the presence of him that keepeth the balance, and that the officers of the court not cause his name to stink. The Nebseni manuscript adds: let not that which is false be uttered against me before the great god, the lord of Amentet."@en ;
    skos:note "The construction is what this holding is built to record. The corpus stages a judgment whose instrument is the man's own heart, and then sells him an object, worn at the neck or laid in the body, whose stated purpose is to keep that instrument from testifying against him. The examination and the means of defeating it are transmitted in the same book and often on the same sheet."@en,
        "Whether the object is asked to make the heart silent or to make it truthful is not decided by the wording read: the request is that it not part from him and that no lies be spoken. A run with a modern translation should take this up, because the two readings differ in whether the amulet is a defence against a false accusation or a defence against a true one."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmc:AegyptiaFlammaeSignum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Götter und die Toten sollen ihn in der Gestalt des Herrschers von Amenti sehen"@de,
        "The gods and the dead shall see him in the form of the Governor of Amenti"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 137A, rubric, Papyrus of Nu, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London 1901, vol. II, pp. 417-418" ;
    skos:definition "The outcome the rubric names, and it names it as something seen by others: when this ceremony hath been performed, the gods, and the Khus, and the dead shall see him in the form of the Governor of Amenti, and he shall have power and dominion like this god. The rubric adds a second criterion in the same place: he shall not have sentence of evil passed upon him on the day of the weighing of words before the god Osiris, for ever and ever. It also promises that he shall never, never fail, that he shall enter in through the hidden pylons and not be turned back, and that no limit to his journeying shall be set."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the nearest thing to a criterion of success that the run found in the corpus, and it is worth exactly what its witnesses are worth: the parties who would see him are gods, transfigured spirits and the dead. The second criterion is better placed, because the weighing of words is a procedure the same corpus describes in detail and with a verdict; see pmc:AegyptiaLibraIudicium. What the rubric does not supply, here or anywhere else in the volumes read, is a sign that the ceremony failed."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmc:AegyptiaNavisRubrica a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Er wird hervorgehen nach Sechet-Aaru, und die Gefolgsleute des Horus werden für ihn ernten"@de,
        "He shall come forth into Sekhet-Aarru, and the followers of Horus shall reap for him"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 99, rubric, Papyrus of Nu (British Museum No. 10,477, sheets 21 and 22), in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II, pp. 301-302" ;
    skos:definition "What the rubric of chapter 99 says the knowing of it obtains. If this chapter be known by the deceased he shall come forth into Sekhet-Aarru; bread and wine and cakes shall be given unto him at the altar of the great god; and fields and an estate sown with wheat and barley, which the followers of Horus shall reap for him. He shall eat of that wheat and barley, his limbs shall be nourished therewith, his body shall be like unto the bodies of the gods, and he shall come forth into Sekhet-Aarru in any form whatsoever he pleaseth, regularly and continually."@en ;
    skos:note "The condition is knowing and not doing, as at pmc:AegyptiaRubricaScientiae, and the chapter it attaches to is a list of names. What is promised is an estate with other people working it, which is the most concrete thing any rubric read in this corpus promises and the one least available to inspection."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmc:AegyptiaRubricaBilateralis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die eine Nachschrift, die sagt, was aus dem Nichtwissen folgt"@de,
        "The one rubric that states what follows from not knowing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 76, the rubric printed as Appendix 1 to that chapter, immediately before the chapter of changing into a golden hawk, Papyrus of Ani, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: The Papyrus of Ani, London: British Museum, 1895, translation section, between the pages headed 329 and 333" ;
    skos:definition "Appended to the chapter of making the transformation into whatever form the speaker pleaseth, in the Papyrus of Ani: if this chapter be known by the deceased, he shall come forth by day in Neter-khert, and he shall go in again after he hath come forth. If this chapter be not known, he shall not enter in after he hath come forth, nor shall he come forth by day."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also the weakest kind of two-sided statement, and the file does not pretend otherwise. The adverse outcome happens after death to a man who cannot report it, and the condition that produces it is the absence of an item of knowledge in that same man. Nothing here could be observed by anyone at any time. The search that found it is at examinations/searches-egyptian.ttl."@en,
        "This is the only rubric in the volumes read that names both outcomes, and it was found by searching for it rather than by reading. Everywhere else the corpus states what knowing obtains and is silent about not knowing; here the two halves stand in one sentence, and the second is the exact negation of the first. What makes it worth a node of its own is that it shows the silence elsewhere to be a property of those rubrics and not of the genre: the form was available."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmc:AegyptiaTituliCapacitatum a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Was die Titel aussagen: die Klassen von Vermögen über die gewonnenen Kapitelüberschriften"@de,
        "What the titles state: the classes of capacity across the recovered chapter headings"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-19" ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, the chapter titles of the Theban recension, from the tables of contents and the chapter headings of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: An English Translation of the Chapters, Hymns, etc., of the Theban Recension, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vols. I to III" ;
    skos:definition "One hundred and thirty-two chapter titles were recovered from Budge 1901 and read for the capacity each states. The classes, with the count of recovered titles falling under each: provisioning with food, drink or air, fifteen; passing, entering, journeying or sailing, twenty-one; the soul, its unity with the body and its freedom, ten; the heart, its giving, its keeping and its not being carried off, eight; coming forth by day, ten; transformation into a named creature or state, eight; amulets given to the deceased, three. Thirty-two of the hundred and thirty-two state the capacity in the negative, as a harm not to be suffered or an assailant driven back; these overlap the other classes rather than standing beside them."@en ;
    skos:note "Compiled by this project. The titles are Budge's; grouping them into classes and counting the classes is an act of this holding, with its date, and no Egyptian source arranges them so."@en,
        "The counts are of recovered titles and not of chapters. Thirty-eight of the hundred and seventy chapter numbers yielded no title to this run, and the transformation group is the clearest casualty: chapters 76 to 88 are the transformation chapters in Budge's arrangement and only eight of them came back with a legible heading. A reader who took eight as the number of transformations in the corpus would be reading the scan and not the book."@en,
        "The registration of this tradition described it as a capacity list in which each chapter states what the speaker becomes and what that state permits. The titles bear that out for the transformation group and for little else. What the majority of them state is not what the speaker becomes but what he keeps, reaches, eats, or is not subjected to."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:compiledBy "prima-materia" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmc:AegyptiaVictusInferorum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Brot, Wasser und Luft: die größte Klasse der Titel"@de,
        "Bread, water and air: the largest class of titles"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, the chapter titles of the Theban recension, chapters 53 to 63, 148, 182 and 189, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vols. I to III" ;
    skos:definition "Fifteen of the recovered titles are about provisioning, which makes it the largest single class. They cover the giving of air and of water in the underworld, the snuffing of the air, the drinking of water at the stream, the providing of the deceased with food, the eating of cakes and ale at the altar of the great god, and the two chapters that state the same concern negatively: of not eating filth and of not drinking foul water, and of not letting a man make a journey being hungry."@en ;
    skos:note "The same concern runs through the outcomes of the rubrics: bread, wine and cakes at the altar of the great god at chapter 99, an estate sown with wheat and barley which the followers of Horus reap for him, cakes and ale at chapter 125. The class is not an artefact of the titles."@en,
        "This is the class the registration of the tradition did not mention, and it is larger than the transformations it did. What the book is most often for, judged by its own headings, is that the dead man should breathe, drink and eat."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmc:AffectCouplingShiftsTheCarrier a pm:Positing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Kopplung eines Zielzustands an einen starken Affekt verschiebt den Koppelnden"@de,
        "Coupling an intended state to a strong affect shifts the one who couples"@en ;
    skos:definition "The claim that binding an intended state to a strong affect shifts what the person attends to, how ready they are to act, how they carry themselves, and where their threshold for risk lies."@en ;
    skos:note "Deliberately the weak reading. The neighbouring claim of the same section holds that the coupled state acts outside its carrier; that one is not examined here and nothing below bears on it. Separating them is the point: the weak reading is testable and the strong one is not, and running them together would let evidence for the first be read as evidence for the second."@en ;
    pm:positedIn "prima-materia#141" .

pmc:AgrippaCulpaLectoris a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Erlangt er sein Verlangen nicht, so klage er sich selbst an"@de,
        "If he does not obtain his desire, let him accuse himself"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Agrippa, De occulta philosophia III.65 (trans. J. F., London 1651)" ;
    skos:definition "The clause closing the whole work. If any therefore through his incredulity or dulness of intellect does not obtain his desire, let him not impute the fault of his ignorance to the author, or say that he has erred or purposely written falsely and lied, but let him accuse himself, who does not understand the writings; for they are obscure and covered with divers mysteries, by which it will easily happen that many err and lose their sense. The author adds that he has folded up the truth of the science with many enigmas and dispersed it in divers places, and has delivered it in words which necessarily blind the foolish."@en ;
    skos:note "It is placed where it does the most work: at the end, after two hundred chapters, as the conclusion of the whole work. Whoever has read that far and has nothing to show for it meets this sentence and no other account."@en,
        "The failed run is assigned to the reader, and the two causes named — incredulity and dulness of intellect — are both properties of whoever complains. Neither can be cleared in advance and neither is anything the work undertakes to test. This is the same construction the holding carries from the burial book, from the elixir chapter and from the internal alchemy, arriving here from a learned author writing in print rather than from a lineage."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:AgrippaPraeparatioOraculi ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeOccultaPhilosophia ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RenaissanceMagic .

pmc:AgrippaFides a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Glaube als festgesetzte Absicht, und der Handelnde, der gar nicht zweifeln darf"@de,
        "Faith as a fixed intention, and the worker who must not doubt at all"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Agrippa, De occulta philosophia I.66 (trans. J. F., London 1651)" ;
    skos:definition "The condition stated of every operation in the first book. The mind effects things by faith, which the work defines as a firm adhesion, a fixed intention and a vehement application of the worker or the receiver to that which co-operates; in every work one is therefore to affect vehemently, imagine, hope and believe strongly. He that works in magic must be of a constant belief and not at all doubt of obtaining the effect. The medical analogy stands beside it: a strong belief, an undoubted hope and love towards the physician conduce much to health, sometimes more than the medicine itself."@en ;
    skos:note "One word of the translation carries more than the Latin will bear and is therefore not built into this node. The print of 1651 has the worker be credulous, which in that English means trusting and in present English means gullible. What the node claims is the shape of the condition — firm belief required, doubt named as the cause of failure — which does not turn on that word."@en,
        "The other half is the failure clause: distrust and doubting dissipate and break the virtue of the mind of the worker, whence it happens that he is frustrated of the desired influence of the superiors. A run that produced nothing therefore has an account waiting for it that does not touch the procedure, and the account is in the instructions."@en,
        "The sentence that decides the standing of the whole work stands in the same paragraph and is not an aside: a firm and strong belief works wonderful things, the work says, although it be in false works. So the transmission states, of its own operations, that the condition it requires would produce the effect even where the operation is worthless — and it states this as an argument for the condition."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeOccultaPhilosophia ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RenaissanceMagic .

pmc:AgrippaSineLuna a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Ohne den Beistand des Mondes wirst du nichts zustande bringen"@de,
        "Thou shalt do nothing without the assistance of the Moon"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Agrippa, De occulta philosophia II.29 (trans. J. F., London 1651)" ;
    skos:definition "The rule on celestial opportunity in the second book. In every work the situation, motion and aspect of the stars and planets in signs and degrees are to be observed, and how these stand to the length and latitude of the climate. When working anything belonging to a planet, that planet must be placed in its dignities, fortunate and powerful, and ruling in the day, in the hour and in the figure of the heaven; and the moon must be observed as opportunely directed to the work, for, the work says, thou shalt do nothing without the assistance of the moon."@en ;
    skos:note "A condition of failure fixed by the date and not by either party, which is the family of claims the holding has been collecting; and it is stated as a flat impossibility rather than as a diminution. What the clause does not carry is an account of how the operator would tell afterwards which condition had failed him, and the work supplies several candidates a page apart — the planet not in its dignities, the aspect not friendly, the moon not directed — so any one failure has a choice of causes."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:AgrippaPraeparatioOraculi ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeOccultaPhilosophia ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RenaissanceMagic .

pmc:AjivikaAnattributaDitthi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Dieselbe Aufzählung, mit der Lehre eines anderen verschmolzen und niemandem zugeschrieben"@de,
        "The same enumeration, fused with another teacher's doctrine and ascribed to nobody"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Saṁyutta Nikāya 24.8, Mahādiṭṭhisutta 1.3-1.15 (Mahāsaṅgīti Tipiṭaka Buddhavasse 2500; segments sn24.8:1.3-1.15), read with the English translation of Bhikkhu Sujato, against Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphalasutta 20.7-20.11 and 26.2-26.10 in the same edition" ;
    skos:definition "The third place in the same canon, and the one that unsettles the other two. The Mahādiṭṭhisutta presents, as a view that arises from grasping at form, feeling, perception, choices and consciousness, a doctrine that joins two things the Sāmaññaphalasutta keeps apart: the seven unmade bodies with the clause that one who cuts off a head takes no life, which the Sāmaññaphalasutta gives to Pakudha Kaccāyana, and the whole enumeration of wombs, aeons and dreams with the denial of austerity and the ball of thread, which it gives to Makkhali Gosāla. In the Saṁyutta the two stand as one view and no teacher is named for it."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because it is the strongest statement this holding can make about the quality of its only witness to this transmission. The canon's materials for the rival doctrines circulate: the same block appears split between two named teachers in one work, fused and unattributed in another, and its six classes given to a third teacher in a third. An ascription drawn from it is therefore an ascription to a position the witness itself does not keep attached to one person, and every node of this file is to be read with that reservation."@en,
        "The reading was done in the Pali of all three places: the seven bodies at dn2:26.2-26.10 under the heading Pakudhakaccāyanavāda, the enumeration at dn2:20.7-20.11 under the heading Makkhaligosālavāda, and the fusion at sn24.8:1.3-1.15 under no heading of a teacher at all. The wording of the shared clauses is near identical across the places."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SamyuttaNikaya ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ajivika .

pmc:AjivikaChalabhijatiVivada a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Wessen Lehre die sechs Klassen sind: der Kanon sagt beides"@de,
        "Whose scheme the six classes are: the canon says both"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aṅguttara Nikāya 6.57, Chaḷabhijātisutta 1.3-9.4 (Mahāsaṅgīti Tipiṭaka Buddhavasse 2500; segments an6.57:1.3-9.4)",
        "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphalasutta 20.7 (Mahāsaṅgīti Tipiṭaka Buddhavasse 2500; segment dn2:20.7)" ;
    skos:note "No side is preferred here and no resolution is proposed. What the dispute establishes is a property of the witness rather than of the doctrine: the canon that is the sole source for the Ājīvika transmission attributes one of its two reported doctrines to two different teachers, so an ascription from it carries that much uncertainty before anything else is asked. The same uncertainty attaches to every node of this file."@en,
        "The ground on which the Aṅguttara rejects the scheme is worth recording and is not a second side of this dispute. The Buddha asks whether the whole world authorised Pūraṇa Kassapa to describe these classes, says it did not, and compares it to forcing a cut of meat on a penniless man and demanding payment: the classes were described without the consent of the ascetics and brahmins put into them. That is an objection to the procedure of classifying and not to the content of the classes, and it is the only objection of that form found in either place."@en,
        "The one witness to this transmission disagrees with itself about who taught what. The Dīgha Nikāya carries the six classes of birth, chaḷābhijātiyo, inside the enumeration it puts in the mouth of Makkhali Gosāla. The Aṅguttara Nikāya has Ānanda report the same six classes, named and filled out, as the scheme of Pūraṇa Kassapa, and has Gosāla appear in it as a member of its highest class rather than as its author. Both places were read in the same edition and in translation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:AjivikaChalabhijati ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AnguttaraNikaya ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ajivika .

pmc:AjivikaNiyati a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Schicksal, Umstände und Natur, und die Leugnung jeder Anstrengung"@de,
        "Destiny, circumstance and nature, and the denial of every exertion"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphalasutta 20.2-20.6 (Mahāsaṅgīti Tipiṭaka Buddhavasse 2500; segments dn2:20.2-20.6), read with the translations of Bhikkhu Sujato and Sabbamitta" ;
    skos:altLabel "niyati"@en,
        "niyati-saṅgati-bhāva"@en,
        "niyatisaṅgatibhāvapariṇatā"@en ;
    skos:definition "The doctrine the Dīgha Nikāya puts in the mouth of Makkhali Gosāla. There is no cause and no condition for the corruption of beings: beings are corrupted without cause and without condition. There is no cause and no condition for the purification of beings: beings are purified without cause and without condition. There is no act of one's own, no act of another, no act of a person; there is no power, no energy, no human strength, no human exertion. All beings, all breathing things, all creatures, all souls are without mastery, without power, without energy, bent by destiny, by circumstance and by nature, and experience pleasure and pain in just six classes of birth."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as the report it is. The witness is the canon of a competing movement, in a set speech that the same text puts in the mouths of five other teachers in the same form, and a modern reader cannot check it against anything the Ājīvikas wrote, because nothing they wrote survives. pm:thirdPartyAscription and not pm:textualAttestation for exactly that reason: the wording is textually attested, the doctrine is ascribed."@en,
        "The Pali of the central phrase is niyatisaṅgatibhāvapariṇatā and the sevenfold denial that precedes it is natthi attakāre, natthi parakāre, natthi purisakāre, natthi balaṁ, natthi vīriyaṁ, natthi purisathāmo, natthi purisaparakkamo. Both translations read render the denial as covering power, energy, strength and vigour, which is the vocabulary this holding uses for what a practice is supposed to produce."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DighaNikaya ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ajivika .

pmc:AmuletFailureIsAlwaysExcused a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Für ein versagendes Amulett findet sich immer eine Ausrede"@de,
        "A failed amulet is never at a loss for an excuse"@en ;
    dcterms:source "A. B. Ellis, The Ewe-speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1890), p. 92" ;
    skos:definition "The witness states the failure case in its sharpest form and reports the response as a rule: if the amulet fails to defend the wearer from the one danger it was made to avert, so that an amulet consecrated to the fire god fails to protect its wearer from a disaster by fire, they are never at a loss for some excuse to account for its inefficacy."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also the recorder's summary rather than a quoted informant, and it is entered as such. What would settle it is a named excuse in a named case, and none was found in the works opened."@en,
        "The passage names the response and not its content, so the graph can hold the shape of the move and not the move itself. That is worth having, because it is the third transmission in this holding of which the same shape is recorded: the Kongo corpus assigns a failed object to neglect or exhaustion, the Lakota corpus assigns a stone that will not obey to the owner's divided faith, and here the excuse is reported without being specified."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Vodun .

pmc:AngelOfLightDeception a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Täuschung, die als Licht erscheint"@de,
        "The deception that appears as light"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Diadochus of Photice, Gnostic Chapters 40",
        "Evagrius Ponticus, On Thoughts 39" ;
    skos:definition "The warning that an experience of luminosity or consolation may be counterfeit, and that the counterfeit is recognised by its circumstances rather than by its brightness."@en ;
    skos:note "Diadochus distinguishes the true consolation, which comes with the body awake and in warm love, from the false, which comes in a light half-sleep."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:DiscernmentOfOrigins ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChristianAsceticDiscernment .

pmc:AppanaPresupposesSappaya a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Vertiefung setzt die sieben Zuträglichkeiten voraus"@de,
        "The absorption presupposes the seven suitabilities"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.36-41, Pali PTS [127]-[128] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, pp. 147-149; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:note "The passage closes the condition from the other side as well, and that is what makes this the load-bearing prerequisite of the chapter: avoiding the seven unsuitable and following the seven suitable, the absorption arises in a short time in him who so practises and is devoted to the sign. The claim therefore has both directions in one place, which very little in this holding does."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The handbook states the cost twice in the same passage and both times as an event in the practice. Of an unsuitable lodging: the sign does not arise there, or the sign that had arisen perishes. Of the thirty-two kinds of worldly talk: they lead to the vanishing of the sign. Of unsuitable company: he loses what he has already got, let alone finds the sign."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:FourAbsorptions ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:SattaSappaya ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:Araqiel a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Araqiel"@de,
        "Araqiel"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "A Watcher named as the one who disclosed the signs of the earth."@en ;
    skos:note "Charles brackets the clause naming Araqiel and Shamsiel as absent from part of the Ethiopic tradition; the bracketing belongs to the text as printed and is recorded here rather than resolved."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:nameForm "Araqiêl" ;
    pm:nameRole "watcher" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:Armaros a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Armaros"@de,
        "Armaros"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:altLabel "Hermani"@en ;
    skos:definition "A Watcher named in the teaching list as the one who disclosed the undoing of spells."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:nameForm "Armârôs" ;
    pm:nameRole "watcher" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:ArsNotoriaCognitioAegritudinis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Vollkommene Kenntnis, ob eine Krankheit zum Tod oder zum Leben führt"@de,
        "Perfect knowledge whether a disease tends to death or life"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), §§ 26, 30a-30b, pp. 24-25, ed. Peterson" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity claimed for the oration of the physical art: to have the perfect knowledge of any disease, whether the same tend to death or to life, including for a sick person who is far off and named only, and for one who dissembles and hides his infirmity."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:ArsNotoriaEbrietasKautel a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Nicht nach Trunk oder Ausschweifung, und nicht nüchtern ohne Bedacht"@de,
        "Not after drink or luxury, and not fasting without discretion"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), p. 44, ed. Peterson" ;
    skos:definition "The warning that no man presume to say any of the oration after too much drinking or luxury, nor fasting, without great reverence and discretion, and that it be treated only at certain determinate and appointed times."@en ;
    skos:note "Fasting appears on both sides of the corpus: it is prescribed for the first day of a rehearsal and warned against here without reverence and discretion. The two passages are held apart rather than reconciled; whether the witness contradicts itself or distinguishes two situations is not decided from what was read."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ArsNotoriaOratio ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:ArsNotoriaGradatioFacundiae a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Nach der Zahl der Wiederholungen zugemessene Beredsamkeit"@de,
        "Eloquence measured out by the count of repetitions"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), pp. 46-47, ed. Peterson" ;
    skos:definition "The graded effect the text attaches to the number of repetitions: said once by a man troubled or involved in a great business, eloquence shall be added to him as much as is needed; said twice, great eloquence shall be given to him; said three times by a man who desires to be made eloquent, he shall be made eloquent."@en ;
    skos:note "A dose and its effect, which the corpus this graph holds gives almost nowhere else. The interest is not the promise but the monotony: more of the same act is said to produce more of the same effect, and a monotony can be examined without the underlying claim being settled first."@en,
        "The order in the passage does not rise. Three times stands for the man who wishes to become eloquent, once and twice for the man under pressure, so the text may be setting out three separate applications rather than one scale. Which of the two it is decides whether there is a dose-response claim here at all, and the passage read does not decide it; the Latin beside it is no clearer."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:ArsNotoriaInterrogatioAegroti a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Entscheidungsregel an der Antwort des Kranken"@de,
        "The rule of decision on the sick man's answer"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), § 30a, pp. 24-25, ed. Peterson" ;
    skos:definition "The rule the text lays down for reading the answer: if he says he is at good ease or begins to mend, judge without doubt that he shall recover; if he says he is grievously ill or worse and worse, conclude that he will die; if he says he does not know whether he is better or worse, he will either die or the disease will change for the worse. Where the sick man is a child too young to answer, or too ill to answer, the oration is said three times and what first arises in the speaker's mind is the judgement."@en ;
    skos:note "The fallback for the silent patient moves the sign from the sick man to the speaker, and with it the whole character of the procedure: what is read is then the operator's own first thought and not anything the patient did."@en,
        "Three answers, and only two of them decide. The third covers dying and getting worse together, so no fate the sick man can meet falls outside it, and that branch alone cannot fail. The first two are not like that: each names one outcome and excludes the other."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:ArsNotoriaLunationes a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Mondtage, an denen die Orationen gesprochen werden"@de,
        "The days of the moon on which the orations are said"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), § 15, p. 12, ed. Peterson" ;
    skos:definition "The seven days of the lunation on which the orations are to be put in operation: the fourth, the eighth, the twelfth, the sixteenth, the twenty-fourth, the twenty-eighth and the thirtieth day of the moon."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:ArsNotoriaOratioYieldsFacundia a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Orationen bringen die Wissenschaften binnen eines Monats"@de,
        "The orations confer the sciences within one month"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), § 14, p. 12, ed. Peterson" ;
    skos:note "The term is the part that matters. Whoever pronounces the words at the determinate appointed time, setting all other occasions aside, shall profit in all the sciences in one month. A promise with a stated interval can be missed; the same promise without one cannot, and the corpus this graph holds gives intervals very rarely."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ArsNotoriaOratio ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ArsNotoriaFacundia .

pmc:ArsNotoriaOratioYieldsSexusInfantis a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Oration offenbart das Geschlecht des ungeborenen Kindes"@de,
        "The oration reveals the sex of the unborn child"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), § 30b, p. 25, ed. Peterson" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ArsNotoriaOratioPhysicae ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ArsNotoriaSexusInfantis .

pmc:ArsNotoriaSecretumKautel a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Das unentschuldbare Verbot der Weitergabe"@de,
        "The inexcusable prohibition on passing it on"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), § 17, p. 16, ed. Peterson" ;
    skos:definition "The warning, ascribed in the text to the angel who declared the oration, that the holder presume neither to give it to another nor to expound anything out of it, neither himself, nor any one by him, nor any one after him."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the gate the project's criterion is directed at, stated by the transmission in its own words and attributed to an angel so that no holder can lift it. It stands here as a claim of the tradition and not as an instruction; the text it guards has been in print since sixteen fifty-seven."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ArsNotoriaOratio ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:ArsNotoriaSexusInfantis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Geschlecht des ungeborenen Kindes am Puls"@de,
        "The sex of the unborn child at the pulse"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), § 30b, p. 25, ed. Peterson" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that if the same oration is said while touching the pulse of a woman with child, it shall be revealed whether she shall bring forth a male or a female."@en ;
    skos:note "The most examinable claim the run found in this corpus, and it is examinable for a reason that has nothing to do with how strongly it is asserted: the outcome is binary, its base rate is known and close to even, it becomes public within months, and the prediction can be recorded before it does. Nothing in the passage makes the result depend on the operator's worthiness."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:ArsNotoriaSolitudo a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Allein, und kaum sich selbst hörbar"@de,
        "Alone, and scarcely audible to himself"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), p. 14, ed. Peterson" ;
    skos:definition "The condition that the oration be spoken secretly, that the speaker be alone, and that he pronounce it with so low a voice that he scarcely hears himself; with fasting on the first day of the rehearsal and a chaste and devout life."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also not the same requirement as the one it most resembles in this graph. The European folk-magic condition recorded at pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicUnbeschrauen forbids the act to be called at and spoken of, that is it regulates what other people say about it; this one regulates how loudly the operator says the words himself. One shields the act from other voices, the other shields the voice from other ears, and a correspondence between them would be built out of the single word silence. Held apart here rather than joined, which settles the second question of prima-materia#530 in the negative; whether either has a counterpart outside the European sphere is unsearched."@en,
        "The condition is on the manner of speaking and not on the words. It is the counterpart of the exactness demands the graph holds elsewhere: there the wording carries the effect, here it is the inaudibility that is prescribed, and the two are not the same requirement."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:AscentThroughEachCentre a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Aufstieg, der in einer Bewegung jedes Zentrum durchläuft"@de,
        "The ascent that passes through every centre in one movement"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Vijñānabhairava 28 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)",
        "Vijñānabhairava 29 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)" ;
    skos:definition "The power is imagined rising in the form of lightning, at each centre in turn and step by step, upward as far as three fists, and at the end of that ascent stands the great awakening."@en ;
    skos:note "The two phrases that carry this are praticakram, at each centre, and kramāt kramam, step by step. Recorded because it settles two questions at once. It gives a body measure in the base text, three fists for the upward reach, where a modern draft gives an arm's length; and it makes the passage through several centres a single exercise, where the same draft allows one centre per session and calls a change of centre a break. See prima-materia#38 and prima-materia#40."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmc:AsipuKapirigEridu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Beschwörer von Eridu, dessen Mund gereinigt ist"@de,
        "The exorcist of Eridu whose mouth is cleansed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Udug-hul 3: 124-126 (canonical Utukku Lemnutu; critical edition M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons, BAM 8, De Gruyter 2016)" ;
    skos:altLabel "KA.PIRIG"@en,
        "asipu"@en ;
    skos:definition "The office the speaker of the third tablet claims for himself in the first person: the incantation priest who heals the land, the chief exorcist who walks about the city, the exorcist of Eridu whose mouth is cleansed."@en ;
    skos:note "The cleansed mouth is a qualification and not a metaphor here: the manuscripts vary between the cleansed hand and the cleansed mouth, and the same variation runs through the rubrics of the mouth-washing ritual, which is itself listed in the exorcist's manual as one of the compositions to be learned. What the office is therefore defined by, in its own words, is a completed procedure performed on the operator."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmc:AsipuLemnuAnaAsipiTehu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Was den Kranken befällt, kann den Beschwörer treffen"@de,
        "What afflicts the patient may come upon the exorcist"@en ;
    dcterms:source "KAR 31 (VAT 8803) rev. 29, rubric (M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons, BAM 8, De Gruyter 2016, p. 40)" ;
    skos:definition "The danger the rubric of the Assur incantation names: that the Sheriff-demon, the Bailiff-demon, epilepsy, the accessory-to-evil demon, the Alu-demon, the falling illness and whatever else of the kind should approach the exorcist himself."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim is about the practitioner and not about the patient, and it is the reason the craft has a procedure performed on the operator before any procedure is performed on the sick. It is also the only respect in which this transmission makes its own operation hazardous: nothing read here says that a failed treatment harms the patient further."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmc:AsipuLemnuAnaSerika a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Jeder Dämon in seine eigene Steppe"@de,
        "Each demon to its own steppe"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Udug-hul 7: 152-153 (canonical Utukku Lemnutu; critical edition M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons, BAM 8, De Gruyter 2016)" ;
    skos:definition "The outcome the dismissal states, one clause to each named being: evil Utukku-demon to your steppe, evil Alu-demon to your steppe, evil ghost to your steppe, evil Sheriff-demon to your steppe."@en ;
    skos:note "Stated in the imperative and to the demon, like the prohibitions of the series entered below, and with the same consequence for examination: a departure commanded of a being nobody can see is not an event at which anything could be found to have gone wrong. What distinguishes it from the prohibitions is only that the flour and the provisions are things the operator does, and those could be observed."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmc:AsipuLemnuIntoTheNetherworld a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das mit dem Fell hinabgetragene Übel"@de,
        "The evil carried down with the hide"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Udug-hul 12: 108-112 (canonical Utukku Lemnutu; critical edition M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons, BAM 8, De Gruyter 2016)" ;
    skos:definition "The outcome claimed for carrying off the hide: that Whatever Evil be removed into the Netherworld, that the enormous be turned into the trivial, and that the evil Alu-demon inflicted on the man be taken into the Netherworld."@en ;
    skos:note "The transfer is stated as a wish throughout and never as a report. Every clause of the passage is precative, and the goat that is to hold the evil is asked not to be released from the patient's head. A transmission that states its own outcome in the optative has said nothing that a case could contradict, and that observation belongs beside the passage rather than in a protocol, because it is a property of the wording."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmc:AsipuUtukkiYieldsRemotio a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Beschwörung entfernt, was da ist, aus dem Leib des Mannes"@de,
        "The adjuration removes whatever is present from the body of the man"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Utukki Limnuti, Tablet V, lines 45-55, in the English of R. Campbell Thompson, The Devils and Evil Spirits of Babylonia, Luzac's Semitic Text and Translation Series vol. XIV (London: Luzac, 1903), vol. I" ;
    skos:note "Entered as the claim of the transmission. Two volumes of this edition were searched through for a sign, a term or a condition of failure attached to any adjuration, and there is none; the finding is at pmp:ApotropaicHarmListSearch. The claim is of the same shape as pmc:AsipuMimmaLemnuLaTehi, which says of the anointed exorcist only that nothing bad will approach him."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AsipuUtukkiLimnutiTuru ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AsipuLitaniaMalorum .

pmc:AsiputuIskaruMasmassuti a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Korpus der Beschwörungskunst, vollständig benannt"@de,
        "The corpus of exorcism, named in its entirety"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The Exorcist's Manual, KAR 44: 1-35 (M. J. Geller, 'The Exorcist's Manual (KAR 44)', in U. Steinert (ed.), Assyrian and Babylonian Scholarly Text Catalogues, BAM 9, Berlin and Boston: De Gruyter 2018, pp. 296-302)" ;
    skos:definition "What the manual presents itself as: the incipits of the exorcism compositions, established for recensions and for reading, named in their entirety. The list runs from the brick-laying and mouth-washing rituals through the prayer genres, the diagnostic and physiognomic omen series, the incantation series including the evil-demon series and the burning series, the rituals of the bath house and the house of enclosures, the spells for undoing witchcraft and a broken oath, to the treatises on paralysis, palsy and the afflictions named Hand of the god, Hand of the goddess and Hand of a ghost."@en ;
    skos:note "A transmission that publishes its own syllabus and claims completeness for it. Four centuries and four cities are represented in the manuscripts of this one tablet — Assur in the seventh century, Sippar, Babylon in the fourth, Uruk at the end of the fifth — so the claim was not the property of one library."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmc:AsiputuMithurtu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Erwägen der widerstreitenden Ansichten"@de,
        "Considering the conflicting views"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The Exorcist's Manual, KAR 44: 40 (Geller, in Steinert (ed.), Assyrian and Babylonian Scholarly Text Catalogues, BAM 9, De Gruyter 2018, p. 301)" ;
    skos:altLabel "mithurtu"@en ;
    skos:definition "The capacity the manual places at the end of the whole curriculum and after everything else has been understood: to think through and weigh the views that conflict."@en ;
    skos:note "The last thing the craft asks of its own practitioners is not a power and not a purity but a judgement between disagreeing authorities, and it is placed after mastery rather than before it. The holding has no second transmission that ends its syllabus this way, and that is the reason the node is entered separately from the practice it belongs to rather than folded into its definition."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmc:Astrology a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Astrologie"@de,
        "Astrology"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "The reading of terrestrial import from the position of the heavenly bodies."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:AvertingTheAngerOfTheHuacas a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Abwenden des Zorns der Huacas und der Ahnen"@de,
        "Turning aside the anger of the huacas and the ancestors"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V" ;
    skos:definition "What the confession is made for: the faults of a man are held to anger his ancestors and his huacas, and the anger to be the reason he falls sick. The confession, the blowing of the powders, the rubbing with the pardon-stone and the washing of the head are done so that the anger is turned aside and the sickness with it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmc:AwakeningPresupposesAnotherWhoDoesNotSleep a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Erwachen setzt einen voraus, der nicht schläft"@de,
        "Awakening presupposes another who does not sleep"@en ;
    dcterms:source "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching, chapter eight, at the passage on the impossibility of awakening alone (one-volume English text as scanned in the Internet Archive full-text mirror; edition left unstated)",
        "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching, chapter eleven, at the talk on the chief feature and the teacher (one-volume English text as scanned in the Internet Archive full-text mirror; edition left unstated)" ;
    skos:note "The prerequisite is not a rule of etiquette towards teachers but follows from the caution below: the sleeper cannot detect his own lapse, so the detection has to come from outside him. A second and narrower form of the same claim stands in the talks on groups, where the chief fault of a person is said to be findable only by a teacher and never by the person himself, and the source calls that practically a law."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "A person cannot awaken alone; and an agreement among twenty to wake one another is still insufficient, because all twenty may fall asleep at the same time and dream that they are waking. Without someone who does not fall asleep as easily and who is charged with waking them, awakening is held to be impossible."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:SelfRemembering ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:FourStatesOfConsciousness ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:FourthWay .

pmc:AyurvedaDuragamana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Tausend Yojana in einem Zug zurücklegen"@de,
        "Traversing a thousand yojanas at a stretch"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.4.29, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, p. 1061" ;
    skos:definition "Claimed of the preparation taken lying in a covered wooden vessel: one can traverse a thousand yojanas at a stretch, and one's period of life is extended to full ten thousand years uninterrupted by any ailment. The distance and the span are given as figures and neither is qualified."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because the strict completeness SPEC §15 demands of capacities is completeness and not selection, and a handbook that claims this beside a dose in counted fruits is telling a reader something about itself. The same section states that the herbs from which it is made were already unobtainable and unidentifiable in the compiler's own day, which the translator notes in so many words."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmc:AyurvedaKutipravesaKala a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Vier Kalenderbedingungen zugleich für den Eintritt in die Hütte"@de,
        "Four calendar conditions at once on the entry into the hut"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.1.21-23, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIII, Calcutta 1904, pp. 1017-1018" ;
    skos:definition "The one place in the lesson where the operation itself, and not the gathering or the maturing of a drug, is bound to a date, and it is bound fourfold. During the period of the sun's northern course, in the lighted fortnight, when the lunation and the constellation are propitious, when the muhurta and the karana are favourable, on an auspicious day, one should after a shave enter the chamber — with strength of understanding and memory, with faith and concentrated attention, having dispelled all mental faults, with mind dwelling on love of all creatures, having worshipped the deities and Brahmans and circumambulated images of gods, of the twice-born and of kine."@en ;
    skos:note "The translator explains the two technical terms: a muhurta is a division of time of forty-eight minutes, a karana a division of which two make a lunar day, eleven in all, some auspicious and some not. The explanation is his and the grid it belongs to is the calendrical literature, which this lesson does not cite."@en,
        "Two kinds of condition stand in one sentence and they behave differently. The ayana, the fortnight, the lunation, the constellation, the muhurta and the karana are fixed outside every party and no examination of anybody could establish or remove them; the faith, the attention and the dispelled faults are states of the user and only he can report them. The transmission does not mark the difference, and a reader who takes the passage for a calendar rule alone has dropped half of it."@en,
        "What the passage does not say is as much the finding as what it says: no consequence is attached to entering on another day. The lesson names five further calendar conditions — the jar buried through the rains, the nagabala roots dug in Magha or Phalguna in an auspicious hour, the bhallataka gathered in Suci or Sukra and given in Sahas or Sahasya, the amalakas plucked in Magha or Phalguna, the forest entered on the full moon of Pausa, Magha or Phalguna after a three-day fast — and attaches a consequence to none of them either. Six windows and no penalty anywhere."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:AyurvedaKutipravesika ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:AyurvedaRasayana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmc:AyurvedaKutyadhikarin a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Für wen die Hütte ist, und was die Übrigen stattdessen tun sollen"@de,
        "Who the hut is for, and what the others are to do instead"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.4.49-50, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXV, Calcutta 1905, p. 1065" ;
    skos:definition "For those that are endued with strength, free from diseases, intellectual, with their minds under control, of a forgiving disposition, and having men and riches at their command, for them only is the ordinance about entering a hut beneficial. Unto those of a different disposition the ordinance does not apply; they, while taking the rasayana, should expose themselves to both sun and wind. Of the two methods the former is certainly superior, and it should however be stated that the superior method is exceedingly difficult of observance."@en ;
    skos:note "Six conditions on the person, of which the last is money. A transmission that states what its best route costs, and names an inferior route for those who cannot pay it, is being unusually plain about the distribution of its own promise."@en,
        "The comparison is made on a ground the holding's generalization about comparative judgements predicts: difficulty of observance, a property of the procedure and of what it demands, and not the dignity of the persons who work it. The same passage is the reason the calendar at the entry to the hut governs one route and not the operation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:AyurvedaKutipravesika ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:AyurvedaVatatapika ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmc:AyurvedaVaksiddhi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Vermögen, die Rede wahr zu machen"@de,
        "The power of making speech true"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.1.7-8 and I.3.26-27, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, parts XXXIII-XXXIV, Calcutta 1904-1905, pp. 1018 and 1047" ;
    skos:definition "Named in the opening enumeration of what rasayana confers and stated again of the preparation made with iron: whatever he utters becomes accomplished. The translator glosses it as vaksiddha, one who has attained to success of speech, and says of such a man that if he were to say at midday that it is night, the sun would hide itself; that his benedictions and curses bear immediate fruit; and that this is one of the eight siddhis a yogin acquires by penances."@en ;
    skos:note "The gloss is the translator's and the enumeration of the eight siddhis with it; the lesson names the capacity and does not describe it. The distinction matters because the gloss imports the yoga literature into a medical text that does not cite it, and a later run that wants the capacity in its Indian breadth should go to the Yogasutra places this holding already carries rather than to this note."@en,
        "Worth marking as the point at which a medical handbook crosses into the material this project is looking for. Everything else in the lesson is a claim about a body; this is a claim that a man's sentences determine what is the case, stated in the same list and with no change of register."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmc:AyurvedaVyapadvidhi a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Setzt aus einer Abweichung Krankheit ein, wird die Kur ausgesetzt und die Krankheit behandelt"@de,
        "If disease sets in from a deviation, the course is suspended and the disease treated"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.4.51, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXV, Calcutta 1905, p. 1065" ;
    skos:definition "The lesson's one statement of what a departure from its own rules costs, and of what is then to be done. If in consequence of any deviation from the rules regarding the use of rasayana diseases set in, the taking of the rasayana should be suspended and the treatment should be taken up of those diseases."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also the only sentence in a hundred and forty thousand characters from which a condition of failure could be built, and it does not quite reach one. It says that a deviation can produce disease; it does not say at what point the user should conclude that the rasayana is not working, and it names no term. The distance between this clause and a falsifier is the whole finding of the run, and it is exhibited at pmp:AyurvedaRasayanaLingaSearch rather than argued here."@en,
        "Read beside the verse eight lines further on — he who has not cast off the graver faults never reaps the effects — the lesson has two accounts of a user in whom nothing happened, and they point in opposite directions. The first says a deviation makes him ill and is noticed; the second says a fault of character leaves him as he was and is not."@en,
        "Three things stand in this sentence and they are worth separating. The harm is named and it is a present condition anybody can see, disease. The cause is named and it is the user's own departure from the rule. And the repair is neither a repetition nor an expiation but a suspension: the operation stops and something else begins. That combination is rare in this holding, where a repair is usually a second attempt."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:AyurvedaRasayana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmc:BarasnumCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Gegen die Reinigung durch einen Unkundigen"@de,
        "Against undertaking the purification without knowing the rites"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Vendidad, fargard 9.47-49, trans. James Darmesteter, Sacred Books of the East 4, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon, 1895)" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission's warning against the man who takes on the purification without the knowledge for it. The penalty stated for him is death: they shall cut the head off his neck and give over his corpse to the vultures. The reason given is not that the rite is wasted but that it works the wrong way: through such cleansing the sickness and the death that were in the world become stronger than they were before."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the strongest thing of its kind the graph holds. Most transmissions say that a botched procedure fails; this one says that it makes the harm greater than if nothing had been done, and it stakes a man's life on the rule. A claim that a wrong performance actively worsens the world is decidable in a way that a claim of mere failure is not, because the two predict opposite directions of the same measurable thing."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Barasnum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Zoroastrian .

pmc:BeautyItself a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Schönheit selbst"@de,
        "Beauty itself"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Plato, Symposium 211c-212a" ;
    skos:definition "The final object of the ascent, seen only by one who has climbed by way of the intervening rungs."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Platonic .

pmc:BeautyOfOneBody a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Schönheit eines Körpers"@de,
        "The beauty of one body"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Plato, Symposium 210a-211c" ;
    skos:definition "The first object of the ascent, from which the climb begins."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Platonic .

pmc:BolgaGubrethe a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Drei Flecken für jedes falsche Urteil, und eine Kette, die sich zusammenzieht"@de,
        "Three blotches for each false judgment, and a chain that tightens"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Prologue to the Senchas Már, in Ancient Laws of Ireland, vol. I (Dublin: HMSO, 1865), pp. 24-25" ;
    skos:definition "The marks the same passage reports of the judges. Sencha mac Aililla never pronounced a false judgment without getting three permanent blotches on his face for each judgment. Morann never pronounced a judgment without having a chain around his neck: when he pronounced a false judgment the chain tightened round his neck, and when he passed a true one it expanded down upon him. Of Fachtna Tulbrethach the same passage says that when he gave a true judgment the fruit was perfect on the trees."@en ;
    skos:note "Three claims of the form this holding looks for and rarely finds: a fault of a named kind produces a physical mark that a person other than the judge can see, and in two of the three the mark is present in both directions, tightening for the false and expanding for the true. There is no announcement to the participants beforehand, and nothing in the sign depends on the judge's report of his own state."@en,
        "What weakens them is that they are told of three named men in the past and not laid down as a rule of the office. The text is explaining how false judgment used to be caught, not prescribing a test a court could run. A later reader who wants to build on it should notice that the passage which states the loss of the faculty and the passage which states the marks are the same passage, and that the marks are on the judges and not on the poets."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InsularCeltic .

pmc:BonGludMaNor a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Der Opfernde selbst darf sich beim Loskauf nicht irren"@de,
        "The offerer himself must make no mistake over the ransom"@en ;
    dcterms:source "gZi brjid, the Way of the Shen of the Visual World (snang-gshen theg-pa), the third portal, on ransom by equal exchange, in D. L. Snellgrove, The Nine Ways of Bon: Excerpts from gZi-brjid, London Oriental Series 18, London: Oxford University Press, 1967" ;
    skos:note "From the Way of the Shen of the Visual World, at the portal of ransom by equal exchange. With regard to the sacred ransom and pledge of life, and the secret quittance with its outer adornment of figurines, the offerer himself must make no mistake; and so that the demons who receive the items do not make mistakes, the intention of the profound meditation must be explained by means of the exposition."@en,
        "Two parties can spoil the transaction and both are named: the offerer and the receiving demons. The second is the part worth keeping, because it puts a possible mistake outside the operator and still inside the rite, which is a shape this holding had not met: a failure that is nobody's fault and is forestalled by an explanation rather than by a correction."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:BonTsheBslu ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GziBrjid ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bon .

pmc:BonLasChad a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ist er durch die Wirkungen seiner Taten abgeschnitten, sind Ritus und Diagnose nutzlos"@de,
        "If he is cut off by karmic effects, ritual and diagnosis are useless"@en ;
    dcterms:source "gZi brjid, the Way of the Shen of Prediction (phya-gshen theg-pa), the fourth of its four parts, on diagnosis, in D. L. Snellgrove, The Nine Ways of Bon: Excerpts from gZi-brjid, London Oriental Series 18, London: Oxford University Press, 1967" ;
    skos:definition "The passage that closes the fourth part. If you are sure you see signs of death, urge him to the practice of virtue. If he is cut off by karmic effects, ritual and diagnosis are useless. If it is certain his time of death has come, even food which should nourish the body may be his life's enemy. But if it is not such a case, and he suffers from an accident or a sudden disease, you will save him by treatment and medicine."@en ;
    skos:note "This is a condition of failure in the strict sense, and it is the first one an opened work of this holding has stated: the work names an outcome of a correctly conducted operation under which the operator is to conclude that the operation cannot work. It is also the most complete immunisation in the holding, and the two things are the same sentence. Whether a patient is cut off by his karma is decided by whether he recovers, so every death falls under the clause and no death falls outside it; the carve-out for accident and sudden disease marks the only cases in which the treatment is claimed to work at all."@en,
        "What it does to the prognostic beside it. pmc:BonRtagsMtshan states both sides of a criterion on a patient, which would make the first Way the examinable one; this clause means that the side that reads death is also the side on which nothing is owed. The pair belongs beside generalizations/the-provision-for-not-knowing.ttl and generalizations/no-failed-run-counts.ttl, and is not entered as a witness of either: the counter-search against the holding that SPEC §16 requires before a generalization is enlarged was not carried in this run."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GziBrjid ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bon .

pmc:BonPresupposesSaBdag a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Ritualplatz setzt die Herren des Bodens voraus, bei Strafe einer Wurzel des Übels"@de,
        "The ritual place presupposes the lords of the soil, on pain of a root-cause of evil"@en ;
    dcterms:source "gZi brjid, the Way of the Primeval Shen (ye-gshen theg-pa), on the preliminaries, in D. L. Snellgrove, The Nine Ways of Bon: Excerpts from gZi-brjid, London Oriental Series 18, London: Oxford University Press, 1967" ;
    skos:note "Stated necessity and not prescribed order, because the consequence is quotable and is quoted twice within a few lines, once as the anger of the named powers and once as a root-cause of evil and harm. The dependent step is entered as the gto rites because the passage says whichever Bon way of the Nine Vehicles you practise, and the gto rites are the ones this holding has entered; the scope in the source is wider than the node claims."@en,
        "What makes the clause worth having is the fraction beside it. The same passage says that fertile fields, good harvests, the extent of royal power and the spread of dominion are ordained by previous actions for about half, and that the other half comes from the lords of the soil. A transmission that divides the credit in halves has said something a later reader can hold it to, and this is the only place in the excerpts where it does."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "If you fail to give milk-offerings and pure sacrificial cakes to the powerful lords of this world, if you do not ask them for a site for your palace of the Blessed Ones, these powerful lords, the lords of the soil, the serpents and the furies are irascible, however much they may still protect the doctrine. And: if you do not know how to act methodically in this matter, a root-cause of evil and harm springs from this."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:BonGto ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GziBrjid ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:BonSaBdag ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bon .

pmc:BonSaBdag a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Herren des Bodens vor jedem Bau um den Platz bitten"@de,
        "Asking the lords of the soil for the site before anything is built"@en ;
    dcterms:source "gZi brjid, the Way of the Primeval Shen (ye-gshen theg-pa), on the preliminaries, in D. L. Snellgrove, The Nine Ways of Bon: Excerpts from gZi-brjid, London Oriental Series 18, London: Oxford University Press, 1967" ;
    skos:definition "The preliminary the Way of the Primeval Shen requires before the place of ritual is constructed, whichever of the Nine Ways is practised: milk-offerings and pure sacrificial cakes are given to the powerful lords of this world and they are asked for a site for the palace of the Blessed Ones."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GziBrjid ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bon .

pmc:BonSmanDug a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Du wirst keine Medizin erzeugen, sondern Gift, und er wird bald sterben"@de,
        "You will not produce medicine, but poison, and he will die before long"@en ;
    dcterms:source "gZi brjid, the Way of the Shen of Prediction (phya-gshen theg-pa), the fourth of its four parts, on diagnosis, in D. L. Snellgrove, The Nine Ways of Bon: Excerpts from gZi-brjid, London Oriental Series 18, London: Oxford University Press, 1967" ;
    skos:note "Entered as a caution and not as a prerequisite with a stated necessity, although the consequence is quotable and is quoted. The reason is that the class cannot hold it: pm:Cautioning has no slot for the consequence of the thing it warns against, which is prima-materia#621, and a pm:Presupposing would need a prior step, while what the text requires here is not a step but a degree of accomplishment it does not measure. The consequence therefore stands in this note and is not queryable, which is the cost the open finding describes."@en,
        "The closing warning of the first Way, and it is a self-warning of the art against its own practitioner: if your skill and cleverness of method have not been perfected by practice, you will not produce medicine, but poison; you will not cure the sick man and he will die before long; so skill and cleverness of method are very important."@en,
        "The failure lands on the operator and the penalty lands on the patient. A transmission that says its own remedy becomes poison in unpractised hands has given an account of every death under its treatment that leaves the treatment intact, and it has done so in the sentence that also makes the strongest case for training. Both readings are in the text."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:BonDpyad ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GziBrjid ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bon .

pmc:BonSoSkye a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Beide, der Vollziehende und der Verstorbene, werden scheitern"@de,
        "Both the officiant and the deceased will fail"@en ;
    dcterms:source "gZi brjid, the Way of the Shen of Existence (srid-gshen theg-pa), on the rites for the dead, in D. L. Snellgrove, The Nine Ways of Bon: Excerpts from gZi-brjid, London Oriental Series 18, London: Oxford University Press, 1967" ;
    skos:definition "From the Way of the Shen of Existence, on the rites for the dead. Except for the Best of Shen, expert in knowledge and whose meditation is vast like the heavens, this ability in the affairs of living beings is no sphere of activity for ordinary people; both the officiator and the deceased will fail, and there is great danger of connecting up with evil rebirths. One who has been continuously purified from previous times and who has entered the stream of the four measureless virtues is able to benefit living beings."@en ;
    skos:note "The third of the work's conditions of failure, and the one with the widest scope: it does not fail the rite in a particular case but withdraws the whole operation from anybody below a named level of attainment, and it attaches a penalty to the attempt that falls on the dead man as well as on the officiant. A reader who wants to know whether the rite works has first to establish that the man who performed it was a Best of Shen, which the transmission does not say how to establish."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GziBrjid ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bon .

pmc:BonTsheLoGsum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Obwohl er im Sterben liegt, wird sein Tod um drei Jahre aufgeschoben"@de,
        "Although he is about to die, his death is delayed three years"@en ;
    dcterms:source "gZi brjid, the Way of the Shen of Prediction (phya-gshen theg-pa), the third of its four parts, on the rites, in D. L. Snellgrove, The Nine Ways of Bon: Excerpts from gZi-brjid, London Oriental Series 18, London: Oxford University Press, 1967" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity the work claims for the life-ransom, stated with a number: although your patient is about to die, you can delay his death for the space of three years. The sentence stands at the end of the Exchange Rite, after the items have been offered and explained truly, and is followed by the remark that the patient's people will make the operator happy with offerings and fees."@en ;
    skos:note "A term with a due date, which almost nothing in this holding has. Three years from a named rite on a named dying man is answerable by anybody who is still there, and the work neither names a sign by which the delay would be recognised at the time nor an outcome under which it would be judged not to have happened."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GziBrjid ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bon .

pmc:BoranOkasaBhante a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Erlaubnis von einem Beichtvater erbeten, dessen Amt vor der Meditation endet"@de,
        "Leave asked of a confessor, whose office ends before the meditation begins"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Yogāvacara's Manual, the meditation of the fivefold zest, the order due (trans. Woodward 1916, p. 6-7)" ;
    skos:definition "The one second person the work names. The meditation of the fivefold zest opens with a request for pardon addressed to a bhante, to be said three times: O sir, grant pardon for the transgressions we may have done in youthful ignorance, with mind confused through lack of skill, for the sake of our guidance hereafter. The formula of request then continues without an addressee, and the editor of 1916 states that the confessor's function ends at the foot of the page, after which the recurring okāsa is probably the self-adjuring of a solitary."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because of what follows from it. This transmission has its practitioner enter a condition in which he cannot check whether the operation is going as it should — the eyes are closed, the attention is withdrawn, and the clock has to be audible because he cannot see it. A second person is named at the door and dismissed before the first station. Nobody stays outside to read the outcome, and the work does not say that anybody should."@en,
        "The living transmission said the opposite, and the difference is recorded at pmc:BoranSisyaUmmattako rather than smoothed into this node: a Sinhala informant in 1916 held that the practice must not be taught by a guru who is not fully versed in it and able to see clairvoyantly what effect each meditation is having on his pupil. That is exactly the person the manual does not name."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:YogavacarasManual ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BoranKammatthana .

pmc:BoranPancajhanaVanna a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die fünf Vertiefungen, nach dem Grad des Lichts geordnet"@de,
        "The five jhānas, graded by degree of light"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Yogāvacara's Manual, breathing exercise for mental clarity (trans. Woodward 1916, p. 69)" ;
    skos:definition "The second catalogue, given in the breathing exercise under its own question: what are the three thought-forms of the five jhānas? The ecstasy of the first is like a firefly; of the second like the halo of the moon; of the third like the colour of the medicine star; of the fourth like the halo of the sun; of the fifth like white flowers. The preamble and the access of each are stated to be like those of the fivefold zest."@en ;
    skos:note "It is the sentence that makes this work a counter-case to pmc:TheSignTakenForTheAttainment. That statement holds of transmissions measuring progress by inner appearances that they name the appearances as the chief obstacle to further progress and place the mark of the counterfeit in the circumstances of the experience. This work measures progress by inner appearances and by nothing else, names no obstacle, and has no counterfeit. The protocol is examinations/the-sign-and-the-counterfeit.ttl."@en,
        "This is a scale and it is a scale of brightness: firefly, moon's halo, a star, sun's halo, white. The stages of the transmission's highest attainment are therefore graded by how much light the practitioner sees, with nothing else distinguishing them, and the work states no upper or lower bound at which a light would be the wrong one."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:YogavacarasManual ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BoranKammatthana .

pmc:BouitiSuspensionAboveTheGround a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Eingeweihte des Bouiti bleiben über einen Meter über dem Boden länger als zehn Minuten schweben"@de,
        "Initiates of the Bouiti remain suspended a metre above the ground for more than ten minutes"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Le Scao, Au pays de Sette Cama: la religion des habitants. Les fétiches: le Bouiti, Messager du St Esprit, 1908-1909, the section on the prodigies" ;
    skos:definition "A capacity ascribed to initiates and reported at second hand inside the article: others affirm having seen initiates of the Bouiti remain suspended a metre from the ground for more than ten minutes. The mediator adds that still stranger things were cited to him."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because of its form and not because of its content. It is the rare case of a claim about a capacity that is quantified in both dimensions a bystander could check - a metre of height and ten minutes of duration - and that requires no initiation to observe. Almost everything else in these pages is either inside the secret or inside the candidate."@en,
        "The counter-statement stands in the same passage and is the reason this node is worth more than the claim. The recorder writes that neither he nor his colleagues were witnesses of such facts, and gives the reason in the same sentence: that they are never admitted to these ceremonies. So the negative is explicitly not an observation of absence but a statement about access, and it must not be counted as a counter-witness. He then leaves the question open - faut-il les admettre? beaucoup le croient - and refers to a bishop's collection of analogous facts rather than deciding."@en,
        "What would make it testable is exactly what the passage withholds. The claim is of a kind that admits a protocol, and the holding has none for it; what is missing is not an instrument but admission. No pm:Testing node is written here, because none has been carried, and the absence of one is the statement SPEC section 11 wants it to be."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AuPaysDeSetteCamaLeBouiti ;
    pm:readVia "H. Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan (Münster i. W.: Aschendorffsche Verlagsbuchhandlung, 1912), p. 51" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bwiti .

pmc:BrowContactOpensTheBrow a pm:Positing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Brauenöffnung eines anderen erfolgt durch Kontakt zwischen dessen Brauen"@de,
        "The brow of another is opened by contact between the brows"@en ;
    skos:definition "The claim that the opening of the brow centre in another person is brought about by contact applied at that point."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the sharpest of the three, and the reason is what the transmission does not say. The śaiva material holds pmc:Saktipata, the descent of power from teacher to disciple, and it makes that descent the condition of everything that follows — but it names no place on the body where it enters. The draft supplies one. The addition is entirely the draft's, and it is the addition that can be checked."@en ;
    pm:positedIn "prima-materia#315" .

pmc:BwetiAmongTheBakele a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Bei den Bakele Bweti, genannt als eine der regierenden Gesellschaften der Gabun-Region"@de,
        "Among the Bakele, Bweti, named as one of the governing societies of the Gabun region"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Robert Hamill Nassau, Fetichism in West Africa: Forty Years' Observation of Native Customs and Superstitions (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1904), p. 248" ;
    skos:definition "The whole of what the third recorder says of the society. In the Gabun region of the equator there is, among the Shekani, Mwetyi; among the Bakele, Bweti; among the Mpongwe-speaking tribes, Inda and Njembe; and Ukuku and Malinda in the Batanga regions. He names them among the societies whose primary object was the good one of government, holding the fetich in terror, and which in one aspect of their influence were before the intrusion of foreign powers the only authority that could settle a fierce personal dispute or enforce intertribal peace."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because the name is the only thing it confirms, and that is worth having precisely. A missionary of forty years' residence in the same region, writing in English and citing neither of the French recorders, puts the society in the Gabun region under the form Bweti and assigns it a people - the Bakele, a third people, neither the Fang of Trilles nor the Bavili of Sette Cama. Nothing of the initiation, nothing of the root, and no occurrence of iboga anywhere in the volume."@en,
        "What it is therefore evidence of, and what it is not. It is independent evidence that a society of this name existed in this region before 1904 and was taken to be an instrument of government. It is no evidence at all about the initiation, the root, the mirror or the criterion, and it must not be counted as a second witness to any of them. The recorder gives a detailed account of an initiation elsewhere in the book, and it is an initiation into Malinda and not into Bweti."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by Robert Hamill Nassau, an American Presbyterian missionary of forty years' residence in the Gabun district, in English, published 1904. No informant is named for the enumeration; the recorder states elsewhere in the same chapter that he came into contact with two of the societies he lists himself. The vernacular names are given in his own transcription and no vernacular sentence is carried." ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:FetichismInWestAfrica ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bwiti .

pmc:Cakra a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Cakra"@de,
        "Cakra"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pūrṇānanda, Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa (1577)" ;
    skos:definition "A centre of the subtle body, described in the source with its seed syllables and its position along the central channel."@en ;
    skos:note "The count varies across the older tantras, which give four, five or six. The tradition does not assign the centres the psychological qualities that modern presentations attach to them."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmc:CastingYieldsForeknowledge a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der Wurf bringt das Vorauswissen hervor"@de,
        "The casting yields foreknowledge"@en ;
    dcterms:source "A. B. Ellis, The Yoruba-Speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1894), chapter II, the section on Ifa" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:IfaCasting ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ifa ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:IfaForeknowledge .

pmc:CautionAgainstLayingDownTheOffice a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Das Amt lässt sich nicht ablegen"@de,
        "The office cannot be laid down"@en ;
    dcterms:source "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXX, sect. 140, pp. 380-381" ;
    skos:definition "Once a man or woman has become a shaman, he or she must go on to death or to psychic invalidity. Three reasons are given: by giving up control the shaman would leave the dis-mastered spirits to harm the clansmen, since in the presence of the old master the spirits will not easily submit to a new one; the person may again be affected by the condition that compelled him to become a shaman in the first place; and the clan holds that a shaman must not give up, so that breaking with it puts him in still greater trouble."@en ;
    skos:note "He closes the section with a judgement of his own — that a shaman's life is a continuous self-sacrifice. That is his and is recorded as his."@en,
        "The warning is against the release and not against the practice, which inverts the usual shape. The recorder states in the same section that if the shaman performs too often he may become the prey of his own spirits, so the office is hedged on both sides: too much practice and its abandonment carry the same named outcome."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:TungusShamanizing ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SiberianShamanism .

pmc:CautionAgainstOperatingAfterEating a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Nicht nach reichlicher Mahlzeit magnetisieren"@de,
        "Do not operate after eating heartily"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. III, pp. 46-47" ;
    skos:definition "One is not to operate after having eaten heartily, nor during digestion, though it is often useful to take something before the sitting to increase one's strength; whoever undertakes a treatment ought in general to live temperately, avoiding all excesses and guarding against whatever tends to disturb the exercise of his physical and moral powers. Where the patient may have a contagious disease the operator is to remain active throughout so as always to impart and never receive, to avoid immediate contact, and to have himself demagnetized for some minutes after each sitting."@en ;
    skos:note "The plainest contraindication in the file and the only one addressed to the body of the operator rather than to his character. It is carried because a rule of this shape can be complied with or not by a stranger reading the page, which is more than can be said of will, confidence and benevolence."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:MagnetismeGrandsCourans ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:CautionAgainstTheFireReaders a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Feuerleser galten am wenigsten, weil sie immer logen"@de,
        "The fire-readers were least esteemed because they always lied"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Cristóbal de Molina, Relación de las fábulas y ritos de los incas, ed. Paloma Jiménez del Campo, transcr. Paloma Cuenca Muñoz (Madrid: Iberoamericana / Frankfurt: Vervuert, 2010), fol. 6v, on the virapiricoc" ;
    skos:definition "The report adds, of this class and of no other in its list of specialists, that these were the least esteemed, because they always lied."@en ;
    skos:note "It bears directly on the claim, open elsewhere in this project, that the old traditions test origin and fruits and never a rate of hits. Here a transmission is reported as having formed a standing comparative judgement about a whole class of practitioners on the ground that they were always wrong, which is an aggregation over cases however loosely it was arrived at. Whether the judgement was reached by counting or was itself a piece of received esteem is not decidable from the one sentence, and no examination node is written on it."@en,
        "The most valuable sentence found in this run. It is a ranking of divinatory techniques by reliability, made inside the transmission, with the reason given as repeated failure rather than as impurity, wrong descent or broken taboo. Every other failure in this file is attributed to something about the person; this one is attributed to the method."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:FireReading ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmc:CautionAgainstTheRubbingBoard a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Das Reibbrett lässt sich vom Betreiber führen"@de,
        "The rubbing-board can be worked by the operator"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 173 and p. 153" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission holds the rubbing-board inferior to the other oracles because the man who works it can cheat with it, and only he may work it. The charge is raised in practice when the oracle has spoken against the questioner or when he dislikes the operator. Of the poison oracle, by contrast, the source records that a manipulation is never even suggested."@en ;
    skos:note "The warning names the failure mode of an oracle whose outcome one party controls, and names it from inside the transmission rather than from the recorder's side. It is the counterpart of the passage on the second test that is not made."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:RubbingBoardConsultation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:CautionAgainstTheSightItself a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Das Sehen kommt ungerufen und lässt sich nicht ablegen"@de,
        "The sight comes unbidden and cannot be laid down"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 46-47" ;
    skos:definition "Those who have reached the third degree are said to see distant things even against their will. A first-person report is given: a man who surrendered his drum to the recorder on complaint confessed with tears that though he parted with it and made no other, he would have the same visions as before; he instanced the recorder himself, giving a true and particular account of what had happened to him on his journey to Lapland; and he complained that he did not know how to make use of his eyes, since things altogether distant were presented to them."@en ;
    skos:note "The same passage carries a claim of a verified hit — the man is said to have told the recorder correctly what had happened on his own journey — and the recorder reports it without endorsing it. It is a claim standing in the work and no more; nothing about the account can be checked, and the man is not named."@en,
        "The warning is not against misuse and not against the anger of a spirit. It is that the capacity, once complete, is not under the will and cannot be given up with the instrument. That is a caution of a kind this graph holds very few of, and it is the transmission warning against the thing it exists to produce."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:DistantSight ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SamiNoaidi .

pmc:CautionAgainstWitnesses a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Der anwesende Unbeteiligte lässt die Operation misslingen"@de,
        "The person present who is not in sympathy makes the operation fail"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. V, pp. 76-79" ;
    skos:definition "Only one witness is to be admitted, always the same person, in communication with the operator and taking an interest in the patient. All useless witnesses are to be banished, all who are excited by curiosity, and especially all the incredulous. Where the stranger is incredulous, suspects the somnambulist's sincerity, or makes a jest of what he sees, the somnambulist is troubled and loses his lucidity. Where many witnesses are present the fluid of each acts on him and, the fluids not being in harmony, he experiences discordant effects; and every time a spectator occupies himself with something else he breaks the communication, producing shocks that disturb the state."@en ;
    skos:note "The condition of failure is located outside both the operator and the subject, which is rare in this holding and is the reason this node is carried into a generalization. The same chapter extends it to the one witness a reader would most expect to be welcome: nothing is said to be more hurtful than the presence of a physician not familiarised with the practice, because the somnambulist would wish to convince him, would conduct his conversation with address, and would lose the simplicity necessary for his clairvoyance."@en,
        "The source gives a case of his own for it. Being a novice, he invited a pupil of Mesmer to instruct him; the visitor did not touch the subject, and yet the course of the somnambulism was entirely deranged, a young somnambulist who had shown extraordinary clairvoyance for several days ceasing to manifest his faculties. The case is entered because it is the transmission applying the rule to a sympathetic expert witness and not only to a hostile one, which makes the rule wider than a complaint about scepticism."@en,
        "What the rule costs the tradition is not remarked on in the work: taken together with the prohibition on proving the lucidity, it makes the claim unobservable by anyone who does not already hold it, since the only admissible observer is one in communication with the operator and desiring the cure."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:MagnetismeLucidite ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:CedarWardsTheWingedGod a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zedernholz hält den Geflügelten Gott fern"@de,
        "Cedar keeps the Winged God away"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 70 and p. 110 and p. 133" ;
    skos:definition "The rack set at the foot of the candidate's bed of sage is of cedar because the cedar is favoured by the Winged God, who will not visit one protected by it nor cause such a one to act foolishly. Cedar leaves and twigs are also sprinkled over the altar in the dance lodge, where they are said to ward against the contrary conduct of the Winged God and of the heyoka."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim is worth holding because it is the counter-move to the obligation on the neighbouring node: the same transmission that says a communication can bind a man for life also names a material that prevents it. A third means is named in the Hunka ceremony, where the waved wands are said to shield a man from seeing the person of the Winged God and so from being made heyoka. Three protections against one outcome, from three different rites, all in the same paper."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LakotaRitual .

pmc:ChukcheeAnsweringCalls a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Antwortrufe und der, der sie gibt"@de,
        "The answering calls and the one who gives them"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Waldemar Bogoras, The Chukchee, Memoir of the American Museum of Natural History vol. VII (Leiden: E. J. Brill, 1904-1909), p. 434" ;
    skos:definition "The exclamations a listener throws in from time to time during the performance — an interjection of wonder, of course, certainly — for which the transmission has a word of its own, to give answering calls, and a participle naming the one who gives them."@en ;
    skos:note "The Chukchee word is given twice on the page and the digitised copy used here damages it in both places, once as oTitkek for the verb and once as ocitkedm for the participle. The secondary literature reads the participle ocitkolin. What the reading here rests on is the English gloss, which is unambiguous, and not on the transcription."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChukcheeShamanism .

pmc:CiceroGeminiDiscrepantes a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Eine Geburtsstunde und zwei Geschicke, ein Geschick und viele Geburtsstunden"@de,
        "One nativity and two fates, one fate and many nativities"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Cicero, De divinatione II.43 and II.47, in the English of C. D. Yonge, The Treatises of M. T. Cicero, London: Bohn 1878, pp. 237-238 and 240" ;
    skos:definition "The two cases the second book puts against the calculation of nativities, and they cut in opposite directions. First, two born at one moment whose lives diverge: Procles and Eurysthenes, twin kings of the Lacedaemonians, did not live the same number of years, for Procles died a year before his brother and much excelled him in the glory of his actions. Second, many born at many moments who meet one end: were all those slain in the battle of Cannae born under the same constellation, since they met with one and the same end? The passage adds a third of the same form, that a multitude is born at every moment and yet no one has ever been like Homer."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as what the transmission says and not as evidence. Nothing here is counted; two twins and one battle are instances adduced in an argument, and a protocol that wanted a rate would have to build it. SPEC §14 keeps such a work on the testimony side as long as it argues instead of counting, and this one argues."@en,
        "The shape is what this holding is short of. Neither case argues that the stars have no power; each takes the practice at its word, finds a pair the rule must classify alike, and points at the outcome. That is a criterion the practice itself supplies, applied to a case it cannot have chosen. The treatise also names the arguments as Panaetius's and not Carneades's, which places them inside the Stoa rather than outside it."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeDivinatione ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GraecoRoman .

pmc:CinqJoursAvantDePresumer a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Fünf Tage, bevor auf Unwirksamkeit geschlossen wird"@de,
        "Five days before presuming that it does not act"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. III, p. 42" ;
    skos:definition "Patients on whom the operation does not act exist but are said to be very rare; it is less rare that the communication cannot be established until after several sittings, whence one cannot presume that it does not act until after a trial of five days. To found the presumption even after that lapse of time it is not enough that the patient has felt nothing: it is to be considered whether he has experienced no change in his condition, whether he does not find himself better, and whether his disease is not rendered worse by pursuing the ordinary course."@en ;
    skos:note "A stopping rule with a number, stated in the chapter on effects and their indications, and the second sentence takes it back in the same paragraph. The number would make the claim due on the sixth day; the qualification replaces the criterion of sensation with a criterion of condition, which the same chapter says may improve without any sensation at all. What is left is a term after which the operator is to consult a sign the transmission has already declared unnecessary."@en,
        "It is nonetheless a real rule and the reason this file exists: it names a term, and a term can be kept. Anyone holding a register of five-day trials with the patients' own reports of change could settle what the passage asserts, and the register is what the transmission never keeps."@en ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A recorded series of five-day trials, carried by operators conforming to the conditions this work sets, in which the proportion of patients reporting either sensation or a change in condition does not exceed the proportion among matched patients given the same attendance without the passes. The claim is that the trial establishes something about the agent, so an outcome indistinguishable from attendance alone falls due against it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:ClaviculaCausaeNonAdventus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die vier Ursachen, denen das Buch ein Ausbleiben zuschreibt"@de,
        "The four causes to which the book assigns a non-appearance"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Clavicula Salomonis, Book II chapters I and XXI, with Book I chapters III and VII, in the English of S. L. MacGregor Mathers, The Key of Solomon the King, London: George Redway, 1889" ;
    skos:definition "Besides the fault of the operator the book names three further causes, and no others. The weather: they will not come when the air is troubled or agitated by winds, seeing that spirits have neither flesh nor bones and are created of different substances, and the three last days of preparation should therefore be calm, without wind and without clouds rushing over the sky. A rival: supposing that they were being conjured in some other place or part of the universe by some other exorcist by the same conjuration, the master should add that they at least send messengers to declare where they are, how employed, and the reason why they cannot come. And their nature: even if the angels and spirits appear not at the consecration of the book, be not thou astonished thereat, seeing that they are of a pure nature and have much difficulty in familiarising themselves with men who are inconstant and impure; the ceremonies being correctly carried out with perseverance, they will be constrained to come."@en ;
    skos:note "Not one of the four is a property of the procedure. Two lie in the operator, one in the sky, one in a person elsewhere in the world whom nobody present can identify. Between them they cover every way a session can end without a spirit in it."@en,
        "The rival clause is the only one of the four that leaves anything behind. It concedes that the spirits may be genuinely unable to come and therefore requires a substitute deliverable — messengers who state where they are and why — so that the session still ends with something the operator must receive. The concession and the repair are in the same sentence."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ClaviculaSalomonis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SolomonicGrimoire .

pmc:ClaviculaCeteraeVirtutes a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die weiteren Vermögen des ersten Buches"@de,
        "The further capacities named in the first book"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Clavicula Salomonis, Book I chapters X, XI, XII, XIII, XIV, XV and XVI, in the English of S. L. MacGregor Mathers, The Key of Solomon the King, London: George Redway, 1889" ;
    skos:definition "Entered together because the first book names them in one run of chapters and states each in a sentence. To become invisible at will, by an image of yellow wax made in January in the day and hour of Saturn, buried in a cavern and carried in the left pocket. To hinder a sportsman from killing any game for the rest of a day, by a stick of green elder laid in his path. To travel far in a few days and without fatigue, by garters of stag-skin filled with mugwort gathered before sunrise on the twenty-fifth of June. To hear distinctly the answer to whatever is asked, by lying prostrate under a carpet of new white wool between midnight and daybreak at full or new moon. To be made partaker in a treasure the earth-spirits hold. To obtain any grace or favour so that it shall not be denied. To deceive the senses of onlookers so that they seem to see what they see not and hear what they hear not."@en ;
    skos:note "Four of the seven carry a term the operator cannot move: a month, a day and hour of a planet, a date in June, a phase of the moon, a span between midnight and daybreak. Two of the seven carry an outcome another person could check within a day — the sportsman who kills nothing, the journey accomplished — and both were left by the text without any statement of what would follow if the outcome were otherwise."@en,
        "Mathers records chapters XI, XII, XIII and XIV as taken from Lansdowne 1203 alone, and chapter XV from Add. 10862 alone. The run of capacities is therefore not the common stock of the transmission but the sum of what several manuscripts each contribute."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ClaviculaSalomonis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SolomonicGrimoire .

pmc:ClaviculaDefectusOperantis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein misslungenes Experiment beweist, dass der Operierende in irgendeinem Punkt versagt hat"@de,
        "An unsuccessful experiment proves the operator failed in some matter"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Clavicula Salomonis, Book II chapter I, with Book I chapters X and XV, in the English of S. L. MacGregor Mathers, The Key of Solomon the King, London: George Redway, 1889" ;
    skos:definition "If by chance it should happen that having performed an experiment with due observance of days, hours and requisite solemnities, thou shalt find it unsuccessful, it must be in some manner false, ill-arranged and defective, and thou must assuredly have failed in some matter; for if thou doest ill in one single point, these experiments or these arts will not be verified. Upon this chapter, the text adds, depends the whole Key: although every solemnity be rightly observed, no experiment will be verified unless the meaning of this chapter is penetrated."@en ;
    skos:note "A fifth assignment stands in the introduction and was found by the search rather than by the reading: what is revealed to any must be kept by them to themselves, otherwise the secrets are profaned and no effect can follow. Disclosure thus joins the operator's fault, the wind, the rival and the purity of the spirits, and it is the one of the five that can be alleged of a session by someone who was not at it."@en,
        "It is not a single lapse. The same clause stands twice more in the first book, in the chapters on invisibility and on favour: if thou lettest any of these things escape thee, or if thou despiseth them, never shalt thou be able to arrive at thy proposed end; and, but if thou doest not the experiment carefully and rightly, assuredly thou shalt not succeed in any manner. Three chapters, three manuscript groups, one rule."@en,
        "The sentence is a biconditional in the guise of an instruction. It takes the case in which the conditions were met and nothing happened, and rules that case out by assigning it to an unnoticed fault. There is then no state of the world under which the arts could be found not to work, and the chapter says so of itself: the whole Key depends on it."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ClaviculaSalomonis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SolomonicGrimoire .

pmc:ClaviculaFurisVisio a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Dieb wird im Wasser gesehen, und das dort geschnittene Zeichen findet sich an seinem Leib"@de,
        "The thief is seen in the water and the mark cut there is found on his body"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Clavicula Salomonis, Book I chapter IX, in the English of S. L. MacGregor Mathers, The Key of Solomon the King, London: George Redway, 1889" ;
    skos:definition "A sieve is hung by a cord with which a man has been hanged, characters written within its rim in blood; a brass basin is filled with spring water; at the words Dies mies yeschet benedoefet Donnima Metemauz the sieve is spun with the left hand and the water stirred the contrary way with a twig of green laurel. When the water is still and the sieve no longer whirls, the gazer sees the form of him who committed the theft; and, that he may be recognised the more easily, the gazer marks him somewhere on the face with the sword of art, for that sign which has been cut in the water shall be really found thereafter upon his own person."@en ;
    skos:note "Mathers notes that the remainder of this chapter, which is where both procedures stand, is from Lansdowne 1203 alone."@en,
        "The chapter carries a second and weaker procedure with the sieve and shears: at the naming of the guilty person the sieve turns of itself without its being able to stop it. That outcome is inside the session and depends on two people holding the rings on their thumbnails, so it discriminates nothing; it is recorded here and not given its own node."@en,
        "This is the most examinable sentence in the book and the only one in the chapters read that states an outcome outside the session. Everything else the transmission promises is established inside the operation by the operator; here a mark made in water is said to appear on the body of a person who was not present, and a bystander could look at the face named and see whether it is there. The examination node beside this file takes it as the falsifying condition for the transmission as a whole, and it is the reason that node could be written at all."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ClaviculaSalomonis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SolomonicGrimoire .

pmc:ClaviculaGradusConiurationum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die gestuften Beschwörungen, jede betreten, weil die vorige nichts hervorbrachte"@de,
        "The graded conjurations, each entered because the last produced nothing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Clavicula Salomonis, Book I chapters V, VI and VII, in the English of S. L. MacGregor Mathers, The Key of Solomon the King, London: George Redway, 1889" ;
    skos:definition "The book sets its conjurations in a series and makes the trigger of each the failure of the one before. If they then immediately appear, it is well; if not, the master uncovers the pentacles. If they do not yet appear, he strikes the air with the knife and repeats a stronger conjuration. This he says facing east, and if they appear not he repeats it to the south, the west and the north, four times in all. If they appear not even then, he signs the foreheads of his companions with Tau and says a further one. If they appear not yet, he is told not to lose courage, for there is nothing in the world stronger to overawe the spirits than constancy; he re-examines and reforms the circle, casts dust to the four quarters, kneels, and conjures again. The last conjuration is declared to be of such efficacy that even spirits bound with chains of iron and fire could not delay to come."@en ;
    skos:note "The series has no term at which the operator is told to stop. Non-appearance never counts as an outcome of the procedure; it is the instruction to proceed to the next stage, and the stages run out into a curse and a burning of names which, the text says, brings them assuredly and in great haste. A transmission built this way cannot register a failed run, because a run is not over until it has succeeded."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ClaviculaSalomonis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SolomonicGrimoire .

pmc:ClaviculaImperiumSpirituum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Geister kommen, sprechen wie ein Freund zum Freunde und geben, was verlangt wird"@de,
        "The spirits come, speak as friend to friend, and give what is asked"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Clavicula Salomonis, Book I chapters VI and VII, in the English of S. L. MacGregor Mathers, The Key of Solomon the King, London: George Redway, 1889" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity the whole book is built around. When the conjuration succeeds, they will immediately talk with thee as a friend speaketh unto a friend; ask of them all that thou desirest, with constancy, firmness and assurance, and they will obey thee. At the final stage the master uncovers the pentacles and demands all that he shall wish of the king of the spirits, and dismisses them with a license to depart."@en ;
    skos:note "Nothing in the chapters read limits what may be asked. The one restriction the text places is on the conditions of asking, never on the content of the request, and that is a difference from the transmissions in this holding that bound a petition to a class of subject matter."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ClaviculaSalomonis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SolomonicGrimoire .

pmc:ClaviculaSignaAdventus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Zeichen, an denen das Kommen der Geister angesagt wird"@de,
        "The signs by which the coming of the spirits is announced"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Clavicula Salomonis, Book II chapters XIII and I, in the English of S. L. MacGregor Mathers, The Key of Solomon the King, London: George Redway, 1889" ;
    skos:definition "The book tells the operator in advance what he will perceive. When the pentacles are shown to each quarter, there shall be noises and rushings. The manner of coming is said to differ by the substance the spirits were created from: those of fire come always with great noise and with the terrible nature of fire; those of water with great rains, thunder, hail, lightning and thunderbolts; those of clouds with great deformity, in a horrible form, to strike fear into the invocator; those of wind with exceeding swift motion; those of air with a kind of gentle breeze; those of solar vapours in a very beautiful and excellent form but filled with pride and vanity. The master is to exhort the companions beforehand that they shall behold kings of kings and emperors of emperors with a great crowd of followers and all sorts of musical instruments, and that nothing should be feared."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered under the separate count that SPEC §15 requires for signs of success, and this is the case the rule was made for. The signs are announced to the participants by name before the session and include the whole range of weather and noise a night in the open can supply. Read as evidence they can only confirm; the reason they are here is that a transmission which announces its signs in advance is a fact about the transmission."@en,
        "Mathers notes that the chapter carrying the noises and rushings and the speech of the emperor of the spirits is given in Add. 10862 alone."@en,
        "The count for this work: one sign of success against no condition of failure. Against the six kinds of appearance and the promised noises and rushings, the chapters read state not one observation that would show an operation had not worked. Every non-occurrence is assigned by pmc:ClaviculaDefectusOperantis or pmc:ClaviculaCausaeNonAdventus before it can become one."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ClaviculaSalomonis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SolomonicGrimoire .

pmc:CmBinglaiFuchai a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Kehrt die Krankheit nach dem Weichen wieder, ist der Mensch böse und wird nicht weiter behandelt"@de,
        "If the illness returns after clearing, the man is bad and is not to be treated again"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhengyi fawen tianshi jiaojie kejing 018b (DZ 789), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "The admonitions instruct: where a libationer treats an illness and the illness comes and then clears, and having cleared comes again, this person is a bad person; do not treat him medically any further."@en ;
    skos:note "It stands in direct contradiction to the escalation at pmc:CmBuyuJizhang, where a failure to heal is answered by a further and stronger petition through some fourteen stages. The two are in works of the same transmission and are set against one another at pmc:CmZhiliaoDispute."@en,
        "The most exact failure rule in the file, and it does two things at once. It names a countable outcome — one relapse after one remission — which is the sort of criterion this holding is looking for and almost never finds; and it assigns that outcome to the moral quality of the patient rather than to the procedure, and closes the case by withdrawing the procedure. The sick person who relapses is thereby removed from the series before he can relapse a second time, so the rule is also the mechanism by which the series keeps its record clean. Entered as a witness for pmc:NoFailedRunCountsAgainstTheOperation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout "A patient whose illness returns after it has once cleared" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ZhengyiFawenTianshiJiaojieKejing ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CelestialMasters .

pmc:CmBuyuJizhang a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Heilt es nicht, so sende die nächste Eingabe hinauf"@de,
        "If it does not heal, send up the next petition"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chisongzi zhangli 2.18b-19a (DZ 615), citing the Taizhen ke, Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 2" ;
    skos:definition "The manual cites the Taizhen code for the order of petitions in illness: first the petition of self-accusation; if it does not heal, the petition releasing the interrogation; if it does not heal, the petition releasing the punishment of the dead forebears; if it does not heal, the petition of transferral; if the patient sinks, the petition driving off the killers and gathering the infusing ghosts; if he collapses, the great petition releasing calamity and evil; if it does not mend, the petition releasing the punishment of the five tombs; if it does not mend, the great petition sustaining the failing and crossing the straits; if it does not heal, the petition returning the cloud-soul and restoring the white-soul; if it does not heal, the petition settling the tomb and releasing the five soils' wasting harm; if it does not heal, the petition settling the dwelling, quieting the gods and expelling the gathered ghosts; if it does not heal, the petition dividing and releasing the great interrogation within and without; if the case is critical, the petitions of the noon and midnight request for life and of driving off the three officers' release by death; if he goes back and forth, the petition looking up in thanks to the thirty-two heavens; and if he is in extremity, the pledged text for prolonging life, and after that the seizing of life, the separate release of the entailed, the lamps, the twenty-eight lodges, and the eighty-one petitions for the dividing and release of the dead forebears."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted from the citation: fourteen stages before the residual list, each introduced by the failure of the one before it, and the whole of it a prescription for what to do when the procedure has not worked. There is no stage at which the series terminates in a verdict against the procedure; the terminal case is the one in which the patient is in extremity, and the answer to that is a further petition. The failure of each stage is the sole condition for entering the next, so that the sequence converts every negative outcome into an instruction and none of them into evidence."@en,
        "This is the passage that makes the transmission worth having in this holding. The shape it exhibits — a procedure whose prescribed response to its own failure is another instance of itself — is stated here with a stated count, in an operative manual, with the stages named. It is entered as the sixth witness of pmc:TheStrongerAddress, and the protocol at pmp:StrongerAddressExamination records what it does to that statement's second clause."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout "A petition for illness that has not brought recovery" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ChisongziZhangli ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CelestialMasters .

pmc:CmCunsiKe a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Was der Vollzieher auf dem Weg hinauf mit der Eingabe sieht"@de,
        "What the officiant sees on the way up with the petition"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chisongzi zhangli 2.23b (DZ 615), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 2" ;
    skos:definition "The manual gives, as the code of visualisation, what the officiant is to see once the petition is sealed: he lies down before the table and visualises a red pneuma issuing from his own heart and rising to heaven; in a moment it is as though he had travelled a hundred li; the road of red pneuma is broad and without flaw or shadow on either side and set with many jewel trees; he sees a yellow road, which is the yellow road of the sun and the moon; five or six li past it he sees purple clouds faintly, and coming to them the gate of heaven, the gate measuring one zhang eight chi, with the attendants at it."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered among the signs of success and counted separately from the failure material, as SPEC 15 requires. The content of the vision is prescribed in advance and in detail, down to the measure of the gate, so that an officiant who reports it is reporting what he was told he would see; the transmission supplies no sign that would distinguish a vision that arrived from one that was produced, and states no consequence for an officiant who sees nothing."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:CmShoufuYinshui ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ChisongziZhangli ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CelestialMasters ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity "The vision of the ascent with the petition, as far as the gate of heaven" .

pmc:CmLibuweishi a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Beamten gehorchen nicht, das Pneuma steigt nicht herab, und am Ende ist kein Wirksamkeitsnachweis da"@de,
        "The officers do not serve, the pneuma does not descend, and in the end there is no proof of effect"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Lu Xiujing, Lu xiansheng daomen kelüe 009a-009b (DZ 1127), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "The code describes the officiant who has no code or precept to go by and no means of telling the genuine from the false, who works from corrupted old petitions and garbled talismans whose head and tail do not answer to one another, who guesses and alters at will, who writes in a scrawl, who eats meat and drinks wine, who says whatever comes into his mouth, who arrests innocent ghosts and indicts spirits that are not the cause; and it states the outcome: for such men the officers and soldiers do not serve, the pneuma of the Dao does not descend, the interrogation grows daily more pressing, the sick person turns weaker and heavier by the day, and in the end there is frailty and no proof of effect. Host and guest are deluded together, do not perceive their error, and fall to slander, resenting the Dao and blaming the gods, some of them burning their registers and wrecking their chambers."@en ;
    skos:note "The most complete failure protocol in the file. It is remarkable for stating the negative outcome in the terms an examination would use — the patient measurably worse, and no proof of effect — and then locating the cause in the officiant's competence, which is assessed by the same authority that assesses the outcome. It also records the end state of the sequence: the people who conclude that the thing does not work destroy the apparatus, and the text treats that conclusion as their further error."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout "An officiant working without the code, from corrupted petitions and garbled talismans" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LuxianshengDaomenKelue ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CelestialMasters .

pmc:CmWumuJiuzu a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die gegenwärtige Krankheit wird den angehäuften Sünden von sieben Generationen angelastet"@de,
        "The present illness is charged to the accumulated sins of seven generations"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chisongzi zhangli 4.7a (DZ 615), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 4" ;
    skos:definition "The petition for releasing the punishment of the five tombs states its own premise: that the petitioner has been stricken with an illness which goes back and forth and does not mend and he fears he will not stay alive, and that this is because his household's three great-grandfathers, five ancestors and seven generations of forebears accumulated sins in their lifetimes and drew down the calamity, or because there is harm from litigation within the tombs, or because the ridge of the ground has been cut through, the pines and cypresses felled by others, the bricks dug out, or a road driven past encroaching on the burial place."@en ;
    skos:note "The failure of the earlier petitions is here given a cause, and the cause is placed in parties who are dead and in a state of the ground that the petitioner did not make. Entered as a witness for pmc:ThePenaltyLaidOnAnotherParty, at the far end of its range: the bearer of the fault is seven generations back and the sufferer is the one alive to report it, which is the displacement of the earlier witnesses run in reverse."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout "An illness that goes back and forth and does not mend after earlier petitions" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ChisongziZhangli ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CelestialMasters .

pmc:CmYanhuiWuyi a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Zu sagen, der Dienst am Dao nütze nichts, ist selbst ein Vergehen von dreitausend"@de,
        "Saying that serving the Dao brings no benefit is itself an offence of three thousand"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhengyi fawen tianshi jiaojie kejing 017b and 002b (DZ 789), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "The admonitions state that one who, on meeting calamity and harm, turns instead to resentment and falsely says that serving the Dao is of no benefit, thereby commits an offence of three thousand. The general admonition earlier states the same in another form: that when guilt arrives and a man does not blame himself and name his earlier fault, but instead cries out that serving the Dao is of no benefit and holds a grievance against august heaven, his transgression is deepened thereby."@en ;
    skos:note "The rule makes the one report that would count as evidence against the procedure into a further offence, and it is stated in the operative text rather than in a commentary. Nothing else in this file so directly forecloses the practitioner's own testimony; the failure rules above explain the report away, and this one penalises it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout "Saying, after a calamity, that serving the Dao brings no benefit" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ZhengyiFawenTianshiJiaojieKejing ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CelestialMasters .

pmc:CmZhangjiaoJiri a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Tage, an denen keine Eingabe hinaufgesandt werden darf"@de,
        "The days on which a petition may not be sent up"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chisongzi zhangli 2.8a, 2.9b, 2.12b-13a and 2.16b (DZ 615), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 2" ;
    skos:definition "The petition manual fixes the sending of a petition to a calendar. It gives a monthly prohibition with a named stem-branch day and a further count of days for each of the twelve months, and eight further branch-days, above which no petition or offering may be made; the days of heaven's father, being the third, ninth, fifteenth and twenty-seventh, on which no petition is sent up; the days of heaven's mother; a table of the days of the killing of heaven by month, which a petition must avoid; the days on which the officers of heaven attend the audience at heaven's gate, on which no petition may be presented; and the days on which heaven and earth are shut, on which petition and offering are not used."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a witness for pmc:AdmissibilityBoundToTheCalendar. It carries the first clause of that statement fully — every condition is a date, none is a state of either party — and it does not carry the second: the manual states that the petition is not to be sent on those days and does not say what befalls one sent on them. It is also the densest instance the holding has, in that the same operation is fixed by five separate and independently computed prohibitions at once, so that a petition that produced nothing has five available dates to have fallen foul of."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep "That a petition may be sent up on a given day" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ChisongziZhangli ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep "That the day is not among the monthly prohibitions, the days of heaven's father or mother, the days of the killing of heaven, the days of the audience at heaven's gate, or the days on which heaven and earth are shut" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CelestialMasters .

pmc:CmZhangxinSanfen a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Drei Zehntel des Pfandes, und der Rest an die Armen"@de,
        "Three parts in ten of the pledge, and the rest to the poor"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chisongzi zhangli 1.2a (DZ 615), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 1" ;
    skos:definition "The manual cites the code: the goods of the pledge are to be distributed among the poor and hidden virtue thereby practised, and the master may not use the whole of them; reckoning by ten parts, what the master may take in is three parts and no more. It adds that the code of heaven is severe, that one who offends is found guilty before the three officers, that the calamity reaches to the nine ancestral generations, and that he becomes for ever a lower ghost."@en ;
    skos:note "The sanction is displaced twice over. The officiant who overcharges is punished in his own posthumous standing, which lies past any report, and in nine generations of ancestors, who cannot be examined at all. The one party in a position to know that the rule was broken — the officiant himself — is thus the party from whom the consequence is furthest removed, and the family charged with it cannot be observed. Entered as a witness for pmc:ThePenaltyLaidOnAnotherParty."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout "An officiant keeping more than three parts in ten of the pledge" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ChisongziZhangli ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CelestialMasters .

pmc:CommunicationFromTheSun a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Eine Mitteilung von der Sonne"@de,
        "A communication from the Sun"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 61 and pp. 119-120" ;
    skos:definition "What one who has danced the Sun dance to its completion may expect: a communication granted by the chief of the gods, in a vision, at any place and time before the dispersion of the next winter camp. The corpus also states what the completion is worth in degrees: tearing the sticks from the wounds unaided is the most effective manner, being freed after a swoon the least effective, and being released at the dawn of the next day counts as a rescue and is as meritorious as an escape."@en ;
    skos:note "The term is the reason this node is here. A promise with a deadline can fail; a promise without one cannot. The corpus fixes it as the dispersion of the next winter camp, which is a date that everyone in the camp shares and that arrives whether or not anything has come."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LakotaRitual .

pmc:CompletionStage a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Vollendungsstufe"@de,
        "Completion stage"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)" ;
    skos:definition "The second of the two stages, to which the six yogas belong."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:TibetanTantric .

pmc:ConfessionValidityTest a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Test, der entscheidet, ob eine Beichte galt"@de,
        "The test that decides whether a confession held"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V, the account of the confession before the ichuri" ;
    skos:definition "Concealing a fault in confession is held a great fault, and the confessor makes great efforts to find it out, by different ceremonies in different places. In some, the penitent first calls on the hills around, the plains, the condors that fly, the owls, saying that he wishes to confess his faults; he says them holding a small bead of shell fixed on a thorn between two fingers of the right hand, the thorn raised; when he has finished he gives it to the confessor, who drives the thorn into his cloak and presses until the bead breaks, and looks at how many parts it broke into. If it broke into three the confession was good; if into two it was not good, and he tells him to confess his faults again. In other places a small bundle of the grass called ichu, from which the confessor's name is taken, is divided in two and straws are drawn one from each side until it is seen whether they come out even, and then the confession was good, or odd, and it was bad. In others they divine it by the blood of guinea pigs."@en ;
    skos:note "A falsifying condition stated by the transmission, applied by its own practitioners to its own act, with a stated outcome and a stated remedy: the confession is void and must be made again. It is not a test of whether the specialist is right; it is a test of whether the layman held something back, so its target is the penitent and not the instrument. That is the opposite arrangement from the one this graph holds for the Zande poison oracle, and the difference is worth more than the resemblance."@en,
        "The same passage records what the procedure is when it fails to persuade: in one village the penitent's hands were tied behind him and squeezed with a cord until he told the truth; a man said before the visitor that his confessor beat him with a stick and another that he was beaten with a rope. A validity test with a coercive fallback is not an oracle, and both are in the same paragraph."@en,
        "The three variants are three different randomising devices — a break count, a parity count and an inspection of blood — attached to one function. That the function is stable across three devices, and that a fourth device with the same parity mechanism is used for divination proper elsewhere in the same corpus, is the finding here."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmc:Constellations a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Sternbilder"@de,
        "The constellations"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "Knowledge of the fixed groupings of stars."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:ContestOfTwoNoaidi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Wettstreit zweier Praktiker"@de,
        "The contest of two practitioners"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Leem, An Account of the Laplanders of Finmark, in John Pinkerton (ed.), A General Collection of the Best and Most Interesting Voyages and Travels, vol. I (London: Longman, Hurst, Rees and Orme, 1808), chap. XXI, p. 477" ;
    skos:definition "When two noaidi wished to know which of them excelled the other in the art, they painted on a drum two reindeer striking each other with their horns, and he whose reindeer had the victory was the better."@en ;
    skos:note "A procedure with a stated criterion, applied by practitioners to practitioners, with an outcome that could fall either way and that neither controls. Nothing else in this file, and very little in this graph, has that shape."@en,
        "What the source does not say is how the victory of a painted reindeer is determined — whether by the ring, by the movement of the drum, or otherwise. Without that the procedure cannot be reconstructed, only recorded, and no protocol node is written on it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SamiNoaidi .

pmc:CoolingAndQuenching a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kühlung und das Stillen von Hunger und Durst"@de,
        "Cooling and the quenching of hunger and thirst"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.58 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "The state ascribed to the cooling retention: fever, bile, hunger, thirst and the effect of poisons are said to be put down."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:CountedThresholdNeverFallsDue a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die gezählte Schwelle wird nie fällig"@de,
        "The counted threshold never falls due"@en ;
    skos:note "A fourth witness, added on 2026-10-06, and it is the first from outside the Chinese stream whose narrowness this file records two notes down. The Anatolian ritual of Paškuwatti fixes three days of invocation with three entreaties a day, announces its sign — the deity seen in the dream and sleeping with the patient — and closes with the case in which the sign does not come: in the practitioner's own first person and with the emphatic particle, she just keeps performing it. It is the plainest instance of the first device the statement names, because the continuation is stated by the operator about her own practice and not read off a rubric. The two negations in the conditional are restorations by the editors of the critical edition and the rest of the clause is preserved; the exposure that creates is stated at the node. The node is pmc:HittIssiskemiPat in traditions/hittite-ritual.ttl."@en,
        "A second silence, added on 2026-09-26, and it is sharper than the first one this file records. The Yogavacara's Manual states, in its breathing exercise, that to him who practises the concentration doing it not less than five times nor more than ten times, the perception of the mental states appears. That is a closed count. The Notory Art's counted repetitions, already noted here, have no ceiling, so a practitioner may always do one more and the moment never arrives; here the tenth repetition is the last the work allows, and the work says nothing whatever about its passing empty — no continuation past the number and no act of the practitioner to carry the shortfall. It is not a counter-case, because the falsifier of this statement requires the transmission to say that the completed count establishes something about the operation, and this one says nothing. It is the first closed count in the holding with neither device, and a successor statement will have to decide whether an unaddressed ceiling counts as providing for the case or as failing to. The node is pmc:BoranPancaDasaVara in traditions/boran-kammatthana.ttl and the search is pmp:BoranTerminusSearch."@en,
        "A third silence, added on 2026-09-29, and with three of them the question this file has twice deferred can no longer be. The Theravāda handbook allows the resolve of its twelfth chapter to be made a second time if the first does not take effect, and grounds the ceiling on an outside authority, the commentary on the Saṃyutta, which it says allows one to attain once, or twice. Nothing follows. The work states no continuation past the second resolve and no act of the operator to carry the shortfall, and this is the only closed count in nine hundred pages whose every other repetition is open at the top — a hundred times, a thousand times, and more often still. It is not a counter-case, for the same reason the Yogāvacara ceiling is not one: the falsifier requires the transmission to say that the completed count establishes something about the operation, and this one says nothing at all. The node is pmc:AdhitthanaDvikkhattum in traditions/theravada-vism-samadhi.ttl and the search that established the surrounding silence is pmp:VisuddhimaggaTerminusSearch."@en,
        "And the counter-case, found in the same reading and in the same tradition, which is the more important half and is why this file was touched at all. The Anatolian ritual of Ḫantitaššu is performed against years that have been set in motion, which no outcome of the rite could establish, and it fixes five counts: three recitations, seven incantations, three libations, seven days of carrying, conclusion on the ninth. It supplies neither device. Its fourth column, where such a clause would stand, is preserved to the colophon in both exemplars and in a second version on a third manuscript, and carries no conditional clause at all. This is not a silence of the kind the three notes below record: those are closed counts whose transmissions say nothing about the completed count establishing anything, and the falsifier of this statement asks for exactly that. What this case does is simpler and worse for the statement — it is a transmission inside the antecedent that supplies no provision, which is the first clause of the statement and not its enumeration. The statement is therefore false as a universal and stands from here on as a recurring device. It is not rewritten here: a claim is corrected where it is examined, and the protocol is examinations/the-count-that-does-not-fall-due.ttl. The node is pmc:HittHantitassuCounts."@en,
        "Its relation to pmc:NoFailedRunCountsAgainstTheOperation is containment and not duplication, and one node is shared. That statement is about operations whose success cannot be established apart from them at all; this one is about the narrower class that also names a number, and it says something the broader one cannot: that the device is supplied at the exact point where the claim had made itself checkable."@en,
        "The counter-search over the holding found two things that come close and neither of which falls inside the scope. The Kongo corpus reports a charm sold as making the purchaser proof against the gun, and records that when it was seen that the purchasers died like ordinary mortals the traffic ceased; that is a failure not explained away, but it is a single trial and no count is named. The Mithraic ascent material, in a hostile report, says what happens if the operation does not work and the answer is that the operator dies; again no count. Both are entered in the holding and neither is a counted threshold, which is why the scope clause of the statement above says what it says."@en,
        "The limit of the derivation, stated because it is the weakness a later reader would otherwise have to find. Two of the three claims come from one transmission and the third from another with which a route is not in dispute, both Chinese and both within a few centuries. What the three establish is therefore a property of that stream. The way to widen it is not to restate it more boldly but to look for counted thresholds elsewhere: the counted repetitions of the Notory Art are in this holding and attach no rule of either kind to a completed count, which is a silence and not a third case."@en,
        "The three claims do the same work by two different devices. The recitation of the five thousand words is to fetch the cloud chariot at ten thousand readings, and the same sentence says that a reader who has completed the ten thousand and not departed is to read it twice a month until it comes. The elixir is finished when the gold forms, and where the gold does not form the batch is to be sealed and fired again for the same count of days, whereupon, the chapter says, there is no case in which it does not form. The third works the other way: three brothers recited the same book, one reached ten thousand and rose, and the other two are reported at nine thousand seven hundred and thirty-three, the shortfall caused by their having set the book down to look at a deer and made irrecoverable by the book's destruction. The exact number is stated so that the failure has a place to attach to."@en,
        "What the pair settles that neither half could. Both tablets belong to one tradition, the practitioners are from different cities, the afflictions are unrelated, and the two do opposite things with a count. So whatever makes a transmission provide for its own empty run is not a property a tradition has or lacks, and a later run that groups by tradition when it looks for the device will group by the wrong thing."@en,
        "What would refute it, and it is an ordinary thing to look for: a transmission that names a count, and says that a practitioner who completes the count without the effect has established something about the operation — that it does not work, that it is not for him, that the recipe is to be revised — rather than something about himself. The statement is about what the transmission provides, not about what practitioners did, so a single passage of that kind settles it."@en,
        "Why three silences are worth more than two. The Notory Art count has no ceiling, so the moment never arrives; the Yogāvacara count has one and the work is a liturgy that raises no case of any kind; this one has a ceiling in a scholastic handbook that elsewhere states failure conditions in abundance, names the empty run in as many words, and supplies a second station of recourse for it. So the silence here is not the silence of a text that does not think about failure. That removes the readiest explanation of an unaddressed ceiling and makes the successor question a real one: whether a ceiling with nothing beyond it is a third device of the same family as the two the statement names, or the one place where these transmissions let a count fall due and say nothing."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:HittIssiskemiPat,
        pmc:JinchengYaocheng,
        pmc:ShangqingWanbianYunjia,
        pmc:ShangqingZhoushiSanxiong ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission fixes a number of repetitions at which a prescribed operation is to take effect, and the effect is not establishable apart from the operation itself, the same transmission provides in advance for the number being reached without the effect: either by prescribing that the operation be continued past the number, or by assigning the shortfall to a nameable act of the operator. It does not leave the completed count without effect standing as an outcome that would tell against the claim."@en .

pmc:CourseOfTheMoon a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Lauf des Mondes"@de,
        "The course of the moon"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "Knowledge of the moon's path, the calendrical rather than divinatory form of lunar knowledge."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:DanhGbi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Danh-gbi, die heilige Python"@de,
        "Danh-gbi, the python held sacred"@en ;
    dcterms:source "A. B. Ellis, The Ewe-speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1890), pp. 57-58" ;
    skos:definition "A python of one particular kind, held to be a god and kept in a temple at Whydah with free exit through holes in the walls, so that the animals move about the town and the bush. They are carried back only when they have entered a profane place, such as the house of a European, and then by a priest who purifies himself by rubbing fresh green leaves between his palms and prostrates himself before the animal before taking it up. Every python of this kind is to be treated with veneration wherever met, and a man of Whydah who meets one in his path prostrates himself, rubs his forehead in the earth and addresses it as master, father and mother."@en ;
    skos:note "Held apart from the boa said to be the messenger of the rainbow god, of which the same source states that only certain individuals are to be respected. The distinction matters for the prohibition on the neighbouring node: it applies to a kind and not to a single animal."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Vodun .

pmc:DaoWuGuishen a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Weg hat keine Geister und keine Dämonen"@de,
        "The way has no ghosts and no spirits"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 25 (Bao ming quan xing lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 8" ;
    skos:definition "Of the needling of the present age the chapter says that where the depleted are filled and the full are drained, that is what all the ordinary workmen know alike; but that to take heaven for law and earth for model, to move as the correspondence answers, so that harmonising with it is like an echo and following it like a shadow — the way has no ghosts and no spirits, it comes alone and goes alone. The same chapter names the five things that are to be hung out for all under heaven, of which the first is the ordering of the spirit, and instructs that in every true needling the spirit must first be ordered."@en ;
    skos:note "The sentence is a denial of the very thing the graph most often finds asserted: that in an operation of this kind something other than the operator acts. It is denied inside a corpus which elsewhere assigns the incanting of illnesses as an office and which holds a chapter dating the efficacy of that incantation to antiquity. The line does not therefore run between the two classics but between passages, and the dispute node beside this one carries all four places."@en,
        "The spirit that must be ordered in the same chapter is not the spirit denied. What is set in order is the operator's own, and the corpus elsewhere describes it as a perception preceding the senses; what is refused is an agent standing outside the procedure. Keeping the two apart is the whole reading of the passage and is the reason it is entered rather than paraphrased."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmc:DasaAppanakosalla a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die zehnfache Fertigkeit in der Vertiefung, verordnet für den Fall, dass die Vertiefung nicht eintritt"@de,
        "The tenfold skill in absorption, prescribed for the case in which the absorption does not arise"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.41-43, Pali PTS [128]-[129] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, pp. 149-151; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "A set of ten expedients the handbook introduces by naming the failure they answer. Having said that the absorption arises in a short time in him who avoids the seven unsuitable things and follows the seven suitable, it continues: but if it does not arise in spite of his practising, then he should bring to pass the tenfold skill in absorption. The ten are making the basis clean, keeping the faculties balanced, skill in the sign, exerting the mind when it should be exerted, restraining it when it should be restrained, gladdening it, looking on at it with equanimity, avoiding unconcentrated persons, keeping company with concentrated ones, and resoluteness upon the absorption."@en ;
    skos:note "And what it does not do. The ten are themselves prescriptions, so the passage answers a failure with more operation, and the handbook names no third station. There is no sentence saying what the practitioner is to conclude if the absorption does not arise after the tenfold skill either."@en,
        "The first of the ten carries its own failure condition with a cause and a simile: when the internal basis is unclean, the hair and nails long or the body soaked with sweat, or the external basis unclean, the robe old and dirty or the lodging full of rubbish, then the knowledge arising in his consciousness is unpurified, as the light of a lamp is with an unpurified bowl, wick and oil; formations do not become evident, and the meditation subject he devotes himself to does not come to growth, increase or fulfilment."@en,
        "Why this is the most useful passage in the chapter for a project that collects failure conditions. It is a provision made in advance for the prescribed course not working, it is stated as the second thing to do and not as the first, and it names the failure in the plainest terms the handbook uses anywhere: it does not arise in spite of his practising. Almost nothing else in this holding admits that in the operative text."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:DavisReceptio a pm:Receiving ;
    rdfs:label "Die Behauptung des Schreibers, der Wortlaut stamme aus der Geisterwelt"@de,
        "The scribe's claim that the wording came from the spiritual world"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Davis, The Principles of Nature, 3rd edn., New York 1847, An Address to the World, pp. 1-4",
        "Davis, The Principles of Nature, 3rd edn., New York 1847, Scribe's Introduction, pp. xiv-xviii" ;
    skos:definition "The claim the volume makes about its own authorship in the Scribe's Introduction: that the book must have been dictated by some other and higher source of information than that accessible to the physical senses, and that the source is the Spiritual World. The scribe specifies it: the associated spirits and angels of the Second Sphere are, as one grand Man, in sympathetic communication with the subject to transmit knowledge to mankind on earth."@en ;
    skos:note "The receiving is the one thing in this volume that could in principle still be checked, because the apparatus is physical. The manuscripts with their two hundred and sixty-seven signatures are declared preserved; whether they survive, and whether the sheets dated 15 and 16 March 1846 are among them, is the question that decides the dating claim at pmc:DavisPlanetaeDatum. Nothing in the holding can settle it and the work names the place to look."@en,
        "This node records the claim and not its truth, and SPEC §10 built the class for exactly this corpus. Without it the ascription would have had to ride in the source line, and a source of the form Second Sphere, lecture 41 would have decided the contested question by the shape of a citation. The source line names the work and the editors a reader can obtain; the speaker the work claims stands here as a literal."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:claimedSpeaker "The Spiritual World; specified by the scribe as the associated spirits and angels of the Second Sphere, as one grand Man" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DavisPrinciplesOfNature ;
    pm:receivedBy "Andrew Jackson Davis, called the Poughkeepsie Seer on the title page, aged twenty at the first sitting" ;
    pm:receptionCircumstance "A hundred and fifty-seven sittings between 28 November 1845 and 25 January 1847, each of forty minutes to about four hours and yielding three to fifteen folio pages. Davis was magnetized by Dr Silas Smith Lyon, a physician of Bridgeport who gave up his practice for the work and whom Davis says he chose while in the state; an earlier operator, William Levingston of Poughkeepsie, had magnetized him from about October 1843. The wording was taken down by William Fishbough, informed of his part by letter from Lyon on 27 November 1845 and writing from the next day. Three witnesses chosen in advance were present, Rev. J. N. Parker, Theron R. Lapham and Dr T. Lea Smith, with twenty-three further occasional witnesses named with their towns. The pencilled manuscripts of a hundred and forty of the sittings carry two hundred and sixty-seven witness signatures, seventeen sittings carry none, and the manuscripts are declared to be carefully preserved. The scribe states his own editorial reach: grammar, the striking of redundancy, the clearing of obscure sentences, three transpositions of contiguous paragraphs, and all the footnotes but one; otherwise the work is offered as paragraph for paragraph and word for word as delivered." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SpiritualistAutomaticWriting .

pmc:DetectionOfTheNdoki a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Aufdecken des ndoki"@de,
        "The detection of the witch"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John H. Weeks, Notes on Some Customs of the Lower Congo People, in Folk-Lore, vol. XX (London: The Folk-Lore Society, 1909), pp. 51-52" ;
    skos:definition "The power ascribed to the ordeal of showing whether an accused person is a ndoki, a witch, the corpus stating that the ordeal is firmly held to be the surest means of detecting one, and that the accused is thereafter either honoured or killed according to the effect the poison has had on his stomach."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:KongoNkisi .

pmc:DiscernmentOfBitterAndSweet a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Unterscheidung von bitter und süß"@de,
        "Discernment of bitter and sweet"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 69:8 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "The disclosure of the distinction of bitter from sweet, given in the text alongside the secrets of wisdom."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:DisplayOfPowersRefused a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Zurschaustellung der Kräfte verweigert"@de,
        "Display of powers refused"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Dīgha Nikāya 11 (Kevaṭṭa Sutta)",
        "Vinaya, Cūḷavagga V.8, Vin ii.110-112, in the English of T. W. Rhys Davids and Hermann Oldenberg, Vinaya Texts, Part III, Sacred Books of the East vol. XX, Oxford 1885, pp. 78-81" ;
    skos:definition "The discourse distinguishes three marvels, of psychic power, of mind-reading and of instruction, and holds only instruction to be reliable; the display of power for the sake of persuasion is refused as something to be ashamed of."@en ;
    skos:note "The Vinaya place was read at the wording on 2026-09-24 and its edition is now named; see pmw:VinayaPitaka. What stands there is a case and not a doctrine. A merchant hangs a sandalwood bowl on a pole and offers it to any recluse or brahman who is a saint and possessed of the power; six teachers of other schools ask for it in turn and are told to fetch it down themselves; an elder rises into the air, takes it and goes three times round the town; a crowd follows him shouting; and the Blessed One, hearing the shouting, rebukes him in the terms the definition gives, forbids the display before laypeople on pain of a wrongdoing, and orders the bowl broken up and ground to powder for eye ointment. The comparison drawn is with a woman who displays herself for a coin."@en,
        "The Vinaya rule against displaying superhuman states to laypeople stands alongside it."@en,
        "Two things in that account bear on this file and were not in it. The offer was made to the holder of the power by a layman, and taken up by six named teachers before any monk touched it, so the act forbidden is one the surrounding world expected. And the ground given for the prohibition is neither that the power is unreal nor that it is dangerous: it is that the display will not convert the unconverted and will turn back the converted. The rule is about what the display does to an audience."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:AkasenaGamana,
        pmc:Cetopariyanana,
        pmc:Dibbacakkhu,
        pmc:Dibbasota,
        pmc:Manomayakaya ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DighaNikaya,
        pmw:VinayaPitaka ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:DistantPerceptionFromAVoluntaryState a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wahrnehmung des Unerreichbaren aus einem willentlich betretenen Zustand"@de,
        "Perception of what is out of range, from a state entered at will"@en ;
    skos:note "One consequence bears on the statement above and is recorded here rather than acted on. In the one data set in which the contrast holds, it holds only for participants selected as believers or practitioners: for unselected participants there is no difference between the conditions at all. The clause people who have not entered such a state therefore covers, in the evidence actually in hand, a comparison between selected people in a state and unselected people not in one. Narrowing the statement to say so is a change to a generalization and belongs to a run that takes that decision deliberately, not to the protocol that noticed it."@en,
        "Seven attested claims from five transmissions: the Sámi journey made while the body lies still, two applications of saṃyama in the Yogasūtra, two of the graded degrees of the Midewiwin, the Iglulik spirit calling, and the Lakota sending of the sacred stones. Each names its own work and passage; this node names none, because none of them says what it says."@en,
        "The derivation is contestable at two points and both are left visible. First, whether the four transmissions describe one kind of practice at all: the Sámi journey, saṃyama, the Midewiwin degrees and the Iglulik calling agree in that the state is entered deliberately and differ in almost everything else. Second, whether reporting on a distant place and reading another mind are one effect or two — the sources treat them as neighbouring and this node treats them as one, which is a decision and not a finding."@en,
        "The seventh claim was added after the sixth and its addition is the only one that changed the shape of the derivation, so it is recorded rather than absorbed. The Lakota case is the first in this set that comes with narrated cases attached: a stone sent to find a rifle dropped into a river at a named place, and a stone sent to bring a buffalo in front of a fur trader who had staked ten articles against it. Cases do not make evidence — nobody devised the procedure and nobody recorded an outcome that could have been checked by a third party — but they are what a protocol would have to address, and no other claim under this node has any. Against that stands the same objection as for the Midewiwin material, since the recording is of nineteen eleven to nineteen fourteen; and beside it the fact that the same transmission names the cause to which a failure of the instrument is attributed, so that its own successes cannot be counted as a run."@en,
        "This node headed the queue of untested generalizations, which SPEC §16 makes the queue for the examining layer, because it rests on more attested claims and more widely separated transmissions than anything else the holding can currently form. The protocol was taken up on 2026-09-20 and stands at examinations/distant-perception-against-a-control.ttl with the state pm:casesWithDeviation. The sentence that used to stand here, that no protocol pointed at this node and that this was its function rather than a defect, was true until then and is kept in this form rather than deleted, because a reader who meets the node first should know what changed and when."@en,
        "What the protocol found, in one sentence, so that it is not read off the state alone: the comparison clause has been tested about four times in sixty years, the results do not agree, and the largest body of work that looks as though it tested the clause tested something else — performance against chance rather than performance against a control condition. The examinable part of this statement is therefore smaller than the number of predecessors suggests, and the protocol names the wording change that would make it larger."@en,
        "Why the wording is as heavy as it is. The traditions say that the capacity is reached; a statement that repeated that could not fail at anything. What can fail is a comparison, so the statement is written as one, with the comparison group named — people working from the same prior information without the state. Whoever thinks the sentence overreaches should attack that clause, because it is the clause that carries the whole claim."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:FourthDegreeYieldsThoughtReading,
        pmc:JourneyYieldsDistantSight,
        pmc:SamyamaYieldsDivyaSrotra,
        pmc:SamyamaYieldsParacittaJnana,
        pmc:SecondDegreeYieldsDistantHearing,
        pmc:SpiritCallingYieldsRecoveryOfLostThings,
        pmc:TunkanYieldsDistantKnowledge ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a practitioner enters a stated inner state at will and then reports on a person, place or object outside the range of ordinary perception, the reports agree with independently established facts more often than reports produced, from the same prior information, by people who have not entered such a state."@en .

pmc:DogonKnowledgeOfSiriusCompanion a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Dogon kannten den Begleiter des Sirius"@de,
        "The Dogon knew the companion of Sirius"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1950" ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 274, 282 with its first footnote, and 287" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that a people without instruments held, as transmitted knowledge, the movements and characteristics of a star that cannot be seen: its period around Sirius, its extreme density, and its priority in creation. The claim is put forward in an article of nineteen fifty which sets the question of how such knowledge could exist and expressly declines to answer or even to pose it."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as an origination and not as a dispute, because a dispute needs two sides that each say something about the matter. Here one side says nothing: no transmission carries the identification, and the tradition file holds only the seed-star. The identification begins in the work named below."@en,
        "The decisive sentence is the one the reception never quotes. On p. 274 the recorders write that the question of how men without instruments know the movements and certain characteristics of practically invisible bodies has not been decided, nor even raised. That sentence grants the premise and withholds the enquiry in one move, and everything the claim later became rests on the granted premise."@en,
        "The later form of the claim, in which the knowledge is explained by contact with visitors from the Sirius system, begins in a work of nineteen seventy-six that has not been read at the wording in this run and is therefore not entered. See prima-materia#251."@en,
        "The second decisive sentence is a footnote. Where the article gives the orbital period as some fifty years, the note observes that this period is very close to that of the companion of Sirius and cites a French astronomical periodical of nineteen thirty-one for it. The identification is therefore the recorders' and is made in the recorders' own apparatus; the body of the text never says Sirius B. What the informants are reported as saying and what the note makes of it are separable on the page, and separating them is what this node exists for."@en ;
    pm:circulatesAs "ancient Dogon astronomy: a pre-contact African tradition holding the existence, period and density of Sirius B, cited both as evidence of a lost indigenous science and as evidence of extraterrestrial contact" ;
    pm:originatedBy "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen" .

pmc:DouleursCritiquesCommePreuve a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der von der Operation verursachte Schmerz gilt als Beweis ihres Wirkens"@de,
        "The pain the operation causes is counted as proof that it works"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. I, § 31, p. 27, chap. III, pp. 48-49, and chap. VI, p. 111" ;
    skos:definition "The operation often excites pain in the part where the disease is located and renews old and slumbering pains; these are produced by the efforts nature makes to triumph over the malady, are but transient, and the patient always finds himself better after having experienced them, which is what distinguishes critical pains from those produced by the progress of the disease. The patient is told that these pains prove the action is powerful, that they are necessary to subdue the disease, that he is to bear them without speaking of them to anyone, and that he is not even to ask the operator to calm them."@en ;
    skos:note "The distinguishing criterion offered — that critical pains are transient and followed by improvement — is retrospective, so it cannot be applied at the moment when the patient has to decide. A patient in pain on the tenth day cannot tell from the passage whether he is in the case that requires fortitude or the case that requires renunciation, and the transmission does not supply a test that would tell him."@en,
        "The first of the three clauses that cancel the stopping rules, and the most direct. Under the rule of nearly a month the patient is to renounce the practice if he grows worse; under this one, growing worse is the sign that it is working. The two instructions are eight pages apart in the same chapter sequence and are addressed to the same person."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:DrumWoodTurningWithTheSun a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Holz, das sich mit dem Sonnenlauf dreht"@de,
        "Wood that turns with the sun's course"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, Of the magicall Ceremonies of the Laplanders, pp. 47-48" ;
    skos:definition "The qualifying material of the drum. It must be a pine, fir or birch grown in a particular place and turning directly according to the sun's course, which the recorder defines: the grain of the wood, running from the bottom of the tree to the top, winds itself from the right hand to the left. A tree so grown is held acceptable to the sun."@en ;
    skos:note "A second condition on the same material stands in the same passage: the piece must be taken from the root, cleft asunder and hollowed on one side, and the skin is stretched over the hollow. It is entered here rather than as a node of its own because it names no criterion by which a piece could fail, only a manner of working."@en,
        "The direction is stated twice in this file in frames that do not obviously agree. Here the sun's course is the grain winding from the right hand to the left as it rises; at pmc:SunCourseRule it is the ring travelling round towards the right hand. Whether a spiral seen along a rising trunk and a ring seen on a flat skin describe one direction or two is not decidable from the passages, and is left standing rather than harmonised."@en,
        "The recorder gives the sun-acceptability as his own conjecture — 'from this perhaps they believe' — not as something told to him. The requirement is reported; its reason is his."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SamiNoaidi .

pmc:DubaisuiNaiqu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die vollständig gelebten hundert Jahre"@de,
        "The hundred years fully lived out"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 1 (Shang gu tian zhen lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 1" ;
    skos:definition "The outcome the same chapter attaches to that conduct: form and spirit together, the heaven-given years carried to their end, a hundred years passed through and then departure. Against it stands the stated outcome of the counter-case, decline at half a hundred."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as a capacity claim and not as a piece of cosmology because the chapter states both arms and states them in the same terms. It is nonetheless not a claim any protocol reaches from here: the antecedent is a manner of living over a lifetime and the corpus supplies no way of establishing that it was kept."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmc:Dujian a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Allein sehen, wo alle hinsehen"@de,
        "Seeing alone where all are looking"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 26 (Ba zheng shen ming lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 8" ;
    skos:definition "The corpus is asked what it means by spirit and answers with a description of a perception that precedes the senses. The ear does not hear; the eye is bright and the heart opens and the intent goes first; he understands alone and clearly and the mouth cannot say it; all look and he alone sees; it is as though dark, and he alone is bright, as when wind blows the clouds away. The chapter sets this against the corresponding account of form, where what is sought is found by asking after the illness and searching it in the vessels."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because it is the clearest statement in this corpus that a capacity of the practitioner is meant and not a technique. It is also the least examinable: the passage says in the same breath that the mouth cannot say it, so nothing in the description could be set against an outcome."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmc:Ea a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Ea"@de,
        "Ea"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Udug-hul 3: 152 and 8: 25-26, 30-31; KAR 31 (VAT 8803) obv. 1-4 (M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons, BAM 8, De Gruyter 2016)" ;
    skos:altLabel "Enki"@en ;
    skos:definition "The god named in the series as the one whose plans the exorcist holds, whose incantation is placed in the exorcist's mouth, and who instructs Marduk in the recurring dialogue that introduces the rituals."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:nameForm "Ea" ;
    pm:nameRole "the god who devises the incantations and instructs the exorcist" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmc:EkoHutvaBahu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Einer seiend wird er viele"@de,
        "Being one he becomes many"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.57-67 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, pp. 448-452; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "The first capacity of the formula. Having done the preliminary work and resolved, the meditator becomes a hundred, a thousand or a hundred thousand. The created forms resemble the creator and do whatever he does, standing, sitting, speaking or keeping silent; but if he wishes he may make them differ, some young and some old, some with shaven and some with unshaven heads, some in robes of one colour and some of another, some reciting, some preaching, some putting questions and some answering them, some dyeing, stitching and washing robes."@en ;
    skos:note "The handbook states what to do when the resolve does not take: if he does not succeed by so much, he is to repeat the preliminary work, the entering and emerging, and the resolve. That sentence is the only place in the chapter where the operation is allowed to fail, and it prescribes repetition rather than any reading of the failure."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:EmmeYaTolo a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Emme ya tolo, der Weibliche-Hirse-Stern"@de,
        "Emme ya tolo, the Female Sorghum star"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 287-288" ;
    skos:definition "A second companion said to run a wider course in the same direction and the same time as the seed-star, four times lighter and larger than it, called the sun of the women, and named as the seat of the female souls of all beings living or to come. It is said to have a satellite of its own, the star of the Women."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Dogon .

pmc:EnergyTakenFromAPersonThroughTheLeftHand a pm:Positing ;
    rdfs:label "Energie lässt sich aus einer Person über die linke Hand aufnehmen"@de,
        "Energy can be taken from a person through the left hand"@en ;
    skos:definition "The claim that something passes from one person to another during contact, and that it enters through one named hand rather than the other."@en ;
    skos:note "Two claims in one sentence, and they are not equally reachable. That something passes is the general transfer claim of the section; that it enters through the left hand and not the right is a statement about sides, and a statement about sides is the kind that can be checked."@en ;
    pm:positedIn "prima-materia#328" .

pmc:EnergyTakenFromCold a pm:Positing ;
    rdfs:label "Energie lässt sich aus Kälte aufnehmen"@de,
        "Energy can be taken in from cold"@en ;
    skos:definition "The claim that exposure to cold is a source from which the practitioner draws, rather than a condition he works against."@en ;
    skos:note "The narrowest of the draft's uptake claims and the only one that names a measurable boundary condition, which is why it is entered first."@en ;
    pm:positedIn "prima-materia#133" .

pmc:EnergyTakenFromWind a pm:Positing ;
    rdfs:label "Energie lässt sich aus Wind aufnehmen"@de,
        "Energy can be taken in from wind"@en ;
    skos:definition "The claim that moving air is a source from which the practitioner draws, beyond what breathing it supplies."@en ;
    pm:positedIn "prima-materia#134" .

pmc:EpreuveCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Gegen die Prüfung der Geister"@de,
        "Against putting the spirits to the test"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Allan Kardec, Le Livre des Médiums, seconde partie, chapitre XXVI, section 288, item 3" ;
    skos:definition "The instruction that follows from it. Questions asked from a motive of curiosity or of test are not to be put; the spirits addressed do not answer them and go away, and the transmission adds that such questions please the imperfect spirits, who are amused by them."@en ;
    skos:note "The rule is self-sealing in a way the graph should record plainly rather than argue about. A superior spirit that withdraws when tested and an absent spirit produce the same observation; and a spirit that does answer a test question is by that answer classed as imperfect. Both outcomes are accounted for in advance, so the test yields no information either way."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Evocation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Spiritism .

pmc:ErectTrunkInSwastikasana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der gerade Rumpf als Merkmal einer Sitzhaltung"@de,
        "The straight trunk as the mark of a seat"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I.21 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "A seat defined by sitting evenly with the body straight; the straightness is part of what the posture is, not a separate instruction."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:EthiopianAsmat a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die abwehrenden Namen"@de,
        "The names that hold off"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, prayers I-IX, pp. 216-220" ;
    skos:altLabel "asmat"@en ;
    skos:definition "The long lists of divine and angelic names that stand at the head of each prayer of the roll and are called the glorious names, the names of their God. They are the operative part: what the prayer asks for is asked by their might, and the roll gives them without explanation, as things to be said or carried rather than understood."@en ;
    skos:note "The identifier keeps the Geez term of the genre rather than an English rendering, because the volume read gives no single term for the lists and calls the whole class magical prayers, which is the editor's name for it and not the transmission's."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic .

pmc:EthiopianAsmatYieldsRestraintOfDevils a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die an der Tür gesprochenen Namen halten die Teufel ab"@de,
        "The names said at the door hold the devils off"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, prayer I, p. 216" ;
    skos:note "The wording makes the protection follow from knowing the names rather than from saying them at any particular moment: it is to him that knoweth these that no devil draws nigh. The instruction to say them at the door is given first and the guarantee is stated after it without repeating the condition, so the two do not close on each other."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:EthiopianAsmatRecitedAtTheDoors ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:EthiopianKeepingOfTheDevilsInRestraint .

pmc:EthiopianDeliveranceByTheWillOfGod a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Errettung vom Feind nach dem Willen Gottes"@de,
        "Deliverance from the enemy by the will of God"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers written for Za-Walda Hawaryat (London: W. Griggs, 1900), section 6, p. LVIII" ;
    skos:definition "The outcome claimed for the worn form: the names shall deliver the wearer from his enemy, and he shall escape from him by the will of God."@en ;
    skos:note "The clause at the end is the whole difference between this node and the one beside it. A man who wore the names and was struck has not shown that they failed; he has shown that it was not willed. The same volume states the same protection three lines earlier without that clause."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic .

pmc:EthiopianSpearTurnedBack a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der zurückgewendete Speer"@de,
        "The spear turned back"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers written for Za-Walda Hawaryat (London: W. Griggs, 1900), section 6, p. LVIII" ;
    skos:definition "The outcome claimed for the spoken form: the spear of the man who wishes to thrust it shall be turned back."@en ;
    skos:note "Stated without condition and without reservation. Nothing is said about the speaker's worthiness, his belief, the state of the roll, or the will of God, and the outcome named is a single public event at a single moment."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic .

pmc:EthiopianSpokenAsmatYieldsTurnedSpear a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die gesprochenen Namen wenden den Speer zurück"@de,
        "The spoken names turn the spear back"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers written for Za-Walda Hawaryat (London: W. Griggs, 1900), section 6, p. LVIII" ;
    skos:note "The passage is printed twice in immediate succession in the edition read, word for word, which the editor does not remark on. Whether that is a repetition in the parchment or a compositor's doubling is not decidable from the volume, and it changes nothing about the claim."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:EthiopianAsmatSpokenAgainstTheSpear ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:EthiopianSpearTurnedBack .

pmc:EthiopianWearingUntilChildbearingEnds a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Getragen, bis die Jahre des Gebärens enden"@de,
        "Worn until the years of childbearing are ended"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, prayer VII, p. 218" ;
    skos:definition "The term set on the wearing of the seventh prayer: it must be written down and worn by the woman who has children to suckle and bring up until the years of childbearing are ended."@en ;
    skos:note "A stated term, which is rarer in this graph than a stated effect. It gives the claim a span rather than a moment, and it says when the wearing may stop without the protection being forfeited."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic .

pmc:EthiopianWornAsmatYieldsDeliverance a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die getragenen Namen erretten vom Feind"@de,
        "The worn names deliver from the enemy"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers written for Za-Walda Hawaryat (London: W. Griggs, 1900), section 6, p. LVIII" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:EthiopianAsmatWornUponTheBreast ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:EthiopianDeliveranceByTheWillOfGod .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicAsterionPresupposesFenster a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Essen setzt abnehmenden Mond und die Sonne in der Jungfrau voraus"@de,
        "The eating presupposes the waning moon and the sun in the Virgin"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Herbarium Apuleii Platonici, chapter LXI, in Oswald Cockayne, Leechdoms, Wortcunning and Starcraft of Early England, vol. I (Rolls Series 35, London 1864), p. 165" ;
    skos:note "The second calendar window this file now holds for the same disease. Hohman's slip against the falling sickness is hung on at the first Friday in the new light; the Anglo-Saxon berries are eaten at the waning moon in August. The two windows are incompatible with each other and neither text knows of the other, which is a finding about the shape of the prescriptions and not about the moon."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicFallendeKrankheitHeilung ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LeechdomsCockayne ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicAsterionBeeren ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicAsterionYieldsHeilung a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Beeren in ihrem Monat nehmen die fallende Krankheit"@de,
        "The berries in their month take away the falling sickness"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Herbarium Apuleii Platonici, chapter LXI, in Oswald Cockayne, Leechdoms, Wortcunning and Starcraft of Early England, vol. I (Rolls Series 35, London 1864), p. 165" ;
    skos:note "Two calendar conditions on one remedy, and they are of different kinds: a phase of the moon, which recurs monthly, and a position of the sun, which the text itself resolves into a single month of the year. Together they leave a window of a few days in August. The prescription says nothing about what happens outside it, which is why the placing below is a prescribed order and not a stated necessity."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicAsterionBeeren ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LeechdomsCockayne ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicFallendeKrankheitHeilung .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicBrandNehmen a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Den Brand herausnehmen, so dass der Arm nicht wehe wird"@de,
        "Taking the burn out so that the arm does not go sore"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Abschnitt »Zeugniße«",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 7" ;
    skos:definition "The outcome claimed for a scald: the burn is taken out on the spot, and thereafter the arm does not go sore at all but heals in a very short time."@en ;
    skos:note "The only case in the section in which the author states that he was present and saw the injury happen. It is therefore also the only one in which the interval between injury and application is fixed by the report itself."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1820,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicBuechleinSchutz a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Schutz des getragenen Büchleins"@de,
        "The protection of the carried booklet"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 90",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 57",
        "Romanus-Büchlein, in Johann Scheible (ed.), Das Kloster, weltlich und geistlich, vol. III (Stuttgart 1846), pp. 489-523, here p. 517" ;
    skos:definition "The claim, printed after the charm against powder and ball, that whoever carries this booklet is safe from all his enemies, visible and invisible; that whoever has it with him cannot die without the whole corpus Christi, cannot drown in any water, cannot burn in any fire, and that no unjust sentence can be spoken over him."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim stands word for word in the Romanus-Büchlein of seventeen eighty-eight and again in the Reading printing of eighteen twenty, and in both it says this booklet. The referent therefore moves with the reprinting: the sentence that guarantees the bearer of one book is carried over unchanged into another and guarantees the bearer of that one. Nothing in either printing marks the transfer."@en,
        "This is the passage the transmission is most reliably misreported on. It is regularly given as a Pennsylvania German claim of eighteen twenty; it was in print in Silesia thirty-two years earlier. The title page of the Reading printing does not in fact claim otherwise: it says the greater part is still little known and appears in print in America for the very first time, which is a claim about America and not about first publication. The English title page of eighteen fifty drops in America from the first half of that sentence and thereby makes the wider claim the German never made."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1820,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850,
        pmw:RomanusBuechlein ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicDoctorsAusrede a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Was mit Worten nicht zu heilen ist, kann ein Doktor erst recht nicht heilen"@de,
        "What words cannot cure, no doctor can cure"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Vorrede zur ersten Ausgabe dieses Büchleins, S. 3-4",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), preface, pp. 3-4" ;
    skos:definition "The rejoinder printed in the preface: that the objection, according to which medical treatment no longer helps after the blessing words have been used in vain, is merely the physicians' excuse, because whatever cannot be cured by words can certainly be cured still less by a doctor."@en ;
    skos:note "It stands in the preface, that is at the place where a book says what it is defending itself against. A holding that takes in the prescriptions and leaves out the defence loses the record of how the transmission handles its own failures, and that record is where a copying chain and a series of trials come apart."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1820,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicKalbZeichen a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das im Zeichen der Jungfrau geborene Kalb lebt kein Jahr"@de,
        "The calf born under the Virgin does not live a year"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Ephrata, Pa., 1828), S. 70",
        "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Abschnitt »Unglückliche Tage«, im unpaginierten Schlussteil vor dem Register",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 58" ;
    skos:definition "The claim, printed immediately below the table of unlucky days, that a calf whose cow calves in the sign of the Virgin does not live one year, and that one which falls in the Scorpion dies sooner still; from which the book draws the instruction to wean no calf in those signs, nor in Capricorn or Aquarius, so that they do not easily take the deadly inflammation."@en ;
    skos:note "It comes from the same hundred-year almanac as the table: the author's sentence about the almanac stands after both and covers the whole piece."@en,
        "The most examinable sentence on the page and the one the tracker passed over. It names an animal, a determinable date of birth, a deadline of one year and an outcome that either occurs or does not, and it needs no practitioner, no state and no condition. Where the table of days above it requires a run of human lives to test, this requires a herd book."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1820,
        pmw:HohmanFreund1828,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicKalterBrandVertreibung a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Vertreiben des kalten Brandes"@de,
        "The driving out of mortification"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Abschnitt »Zeugniße«",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 6" ;
    skos:definition "The outcome claimed for a boy badly burnt in whom mortification had already set in: it is driven out in a short time and he is restored."@en ;
    skos:note "The preface makes this the gravest of the claims: it pronounces a woe on whoever is at fault that a man lose his life through mortification, or lose a limb or the sight of an eye, when help out of this booklet could have been given. The testimonial is the case that sentence is written against."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1820,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicKuhFaelltTodt a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Kuh fällt todt zur Erde"@de,
        "The cow falls dead to the ground"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Ephrata, Pa., 1828), Anhang, S. 71-72",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), Appendix, pp. 59-60" ;
    skos:definition "The stated course of the transfer: the animal draws the whole disease to itself little by little, takes the convulsive attacks, and when the man has entirely lost them the cow falls dead to the ground."@en ;
    skos:note "An outcome and not a description of a state. It happens in a stable, to an animal, before witnesses, and it excludes the case in which the sick man and the cow both stay as they were. That is why the passage is here and not left in the tracker."@en,
        "The disease is the falling sickness, not gout. The heading of the German appendix reads Mittel wider die Fallende-Krankheit, and what passes to the cow are the gichterische Anfälle, that is convulsive fits; the Harrisburg translation renders gichterisch as arthritic and so produces a claim about gout that the German does not make. The translation error is recorded here because the English printing is the one usually read."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1828,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicRadKautel a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Treib das Rad nicht gar zu stark um"@de,
        "Do not drive the wheel too hard"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 84",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 53" ;
    skos:definition "The warning against turning the wheel too hard, printed inside the spoken charm and addressed to the operator rather than to the thief the surrounding words address."@en ;
    skos:note "The place of the sentence is the finding. A caution set inside the words to be spoken cannot be read aloud without being obeyed or noticed, and an operator working from the page meets it at the moment it applies. What it does not say is what turning too hard would do."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicRadNabe ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1820,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicRheumaBrief a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der eingenähte Brief gegen das Reissen"@de,
        "The sewn letter against rheumatism"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 53",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 32" ;
    skos:definition "A written letter sewn into a piece of cloth and hung around the neck, where cloth, tape and thread must never have been in water, the thread must carry no knot, and cloth and tape must have been spun by a child of not more than seven years; the hanging on is unbeschrauen and falls in a named Friday of the moon, and the Lord's prayer and the articles of faith are said immediately after."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1820,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicSchelwurzelVorzeichen a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Weinen für die Genesung, fröhliches Singen für den Tod"@de,
        "Weeping for recovery, glad singing for death"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Abschnitt »Die Schelwurzel«",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 20" ;
    skos:definition "The sign the laying on is performed to obtain: if the sick man is to recover he weeps, and if he is to die he sings with a glad voice."@en ;
    skos:note "The sharpest claim in the handbook and the only one in it whose two outcomes exclude each other. It requires no interpretation, it is read off the sick man within the same hour, and any case in which the one who weeps dies or the one who sings recovers tells against it. Most of the book promises an effect and leaves the absence of the effect unaccounted for; this predicts, and a prediction can miss."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1820,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicUnbeschrauen a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Unbeschrauen"@de,
        "Unbeschrauen"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 14, 27, 28, 29, 53, 83",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), pp. 17, 18, 32" ;
    skos:definition "The condition, attached to a procedure and not to a formula, that the act be carried out without being called at and without the doing being spoken of. It is set on cutting a sod, on knotting twigs, on hanging a written letter around the neck, on passing water into a vessel and on going to a wagon, that is on acts of every kind, and never on the words themselves."@en ;
    skos:note "The eighteen fifty translation renders the term as unbeshrewedly, a coinage with no currency in English, which is why the identifier keeps the German word of the printing that carries it."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1820,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicUngluecklicheTage a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Tafel der unglücklichen Tage"@de,
        "The table of unlucky days"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Ephrata, Pa.: gedruckt bey J. B., 1828), S. 69",
        "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Abschnitt »Unglückliche Tage, wie solche in jedem Monat sich befinden«, im unpaginierten Schlussteil vor dem Register",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), pp. 57-58" ;
    skos:definition "A fixed list of about thirty-two days of the year, printed month by month, to which four consequences are attached: whoever is born on one of them is unlucky and suffers poverty; whoever falls ill on one of them seldom regains his health; whoever becomes engaged or marries on one of them comes into great poverty and misery; and on such a day one should not move house, not travel, not trade and not begin a lawsuit."@en ;
    skos:note "The book names its own source for this one piece and for no other: it was drawn from a hundred-year almanac coming out of Germany, and, the sentence adds, many believe in it. The author signs that sentence with his name. The table is therefore not his claim but a quotation he marks as such, and the transmission it belongs to is the printed almanac and not the handbook."@en,
        "The days as the Reading printing of eighteen twenty gives them: January one, two, three, four, six, eleven, twelve; February one, seventeen, eighteen; March fourteen, sixteen; April ten, seventeen, eighteen; May seven, eight; June seventeen; July seventeen, twenty-one; August twenty, twenty-one; September ten, eighteen; October six; November six, ten; December six, eleven, fifteen. The Ephrata printing of eighteen twenty-eight agrees except at July, where it prints seven and twenty-one."@en,
        "Two clauses stand with the table and are not part of it. On the day of the Annunciation, of Simon and Jude, and of the Apostle St Andrew no blood is to be let; and the signs of the zodiac are to be observed by the course of the month as the common almanac gives them daily. The eighteen fifty translation drops the bloodletting clause and keeps the zodiac one."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1820,
        pmw:HohmanFreund1828,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicZahnschmerzStillung a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Vertreiben der allerheftigsten Zahnschmerzen"@de,
        "The driving out of the severest toothache"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 46",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 28" ;
    skos:definition "The effect claimed for the vitriol, stated for hollow teeth and left open by the author for teeth that are not hollow."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1820,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:ExactnessAsConditionOfEfficacy a pm:Converging ;
    rdfs:label "Die Genauigkeit der Rezitation ist als Bedingung ihrer Wirksamkeit gesetzt"@de,
        "The exactness of the recitation is set as the condition of its efficacy"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aitareya Brāhmaṇa iii,11 (xi,11), in the English of Arthur Berriedale Keith, Rigveda Brahmanas, Harvard Oriental Series 25, Cambridge 1920, pp. 171-172",
        "Aitareya Brāhmaṇa iii,7 (xi,7), in the English of Arthur Berriedale Keith, Rigveda Brahmanas, Harvard Oriental Series 25, Cambridge 1920, p. 169",
        "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 11",
        "Martha Warren Beckwith, The Kumulipo: A Hawaiian Creation Chant (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), chapter 7, pp. 35-36",
        "Ruth L. Bunzel, Zuni Ritual Poetry, in Forty-Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of American Ethnology 1929-1930 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1932), pp. 615-616",
        "Washington Matthews, Navaho Legends, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society V (Boston and New York: Houghton, Mifflin, 1897), sections 41-42, pp. 24-25" ;
    skos:definition "Four transmissions make the same claim about spoken ritual language: the effect is not obtained unless the words are rendered as they stand. Three of them also say what a lapse does. One holds that an error, and equally a hesitation, is fatal to the efficacy of the address. Another holds that an error in a song may be fatal to the ceremony and that in some cases a single syllable does irreparable harm. A third requires the publicly chanted parts to be letter perfect and, in the passages read, states no consequence. The fourth names a breath taken before the close of a phrase alongside mistake and hesitation, and says the ill-luck falls on the party the chant honours rather than on the reciter. The fifth and sixth come from one corpus and grade the fault: a syllable lost from the closing call empties the reciter and the man he recites for, and passing over, inverting or uniting the sentences of a formula are three faults with three consequences of rising severity, the last of them the likely death of the sacrificer."@en ;
    skos:note "A defect in the vocabulary shows at this node and is recorded rather than worked around. pm:contactRoute is a property of a single tradition, but contact is a relation between two. The value pm:contactRouteAbsent as the registry uses it means that no route runs to what the graph already held at the time of registration; it cannot answer whether a route runs between two registered traditions that are later brought together in one correspondence, which is exactly the question a convergence asks. See prima-materia#101 for the vocabulary defect and prima-materia#102 for the registry entries it affects."@en,
        "A fourth witness was added on 2026-09-04 and it is expressly not a fourth independent one. The Hawaiian transmission holds that a breath taken before the close of a phrase, a mistake, or even hesitation in pronouncing a word is a sign of ill-luck. It stands beside the Maori witness and not beyond it: both descend from one East Polynesian dispersal, dated archaeologically to the thirteenth century, so for the independence question the two Polynesian legs count as one. The count of transmissions carrying the rule goes from three to four; the count of independent ones does not move. Holding both numbers is what SPEC section 14 asks for, and this is the case that shows why one number alone would mislead."@en,
        "The counter-search did not produce a tradition that denies the claim. What it produced is of a different kind and belongs here: the recorded versions of the same Māori address differ from one another across the eighteen-forties to the early nineteen-hundreds, and which rendering was valid was disputed among Māori at the time. A doctrine of one correct wording and a transmission that visibly carries several are not a contradiction, but they are the point at which the claim becomes examinable instead of edifying. The place that says so has not been read at the wording and stands at prima-materia#110."@en,
        "The sixth witness also carries the sharpest counter-material this node has met, and it stands in the same corpus two books away. The Kauṣītaki reports a session at which the question is put — if a flaw is noticed, how would you remove it? — and prints two named answers. Jātūkarṇya: recite again, and only so much as was erroneous, a verse or half verse or quarter verse or word or letter. Kauṣītaki: he should not repeat the mantra, nor offer a libation, there is no flaw, for whatever flaw the Hotṛs make through inattention, all that Agni as divine Hotṛ makes flawless. The second answer cancels the rule this node describes, inside the transmission that states the rule most precisely. It is entered at pmc:VaidikaDosavivadah and it is a reason to read this correspondence as a claim about what these transmissions say rather than about what any of them settles."@en,
        "Three witnesses agreeing on the form of a rule is a weaker finding than it looks unless the alternative is stated. The alternative exists and is common: traditions in which the sense of a prayer carries it and the wording may be varied, and traditions in which improvisation is the mark of the competent practitioner. That the three named here all fall on the fixed side is what the correspondence asserts. Where the rule holds and where it is absent has not been surveyed; the spheres left unsearched stand at prima-materia#116, the Indic parallel at prima-materia#109."@en,
        "Two further witnesses were added on 2026-09-19 from the Vedic Brāhmaṇas, and they are expressly not two further independent ones: the contact route of that transmission is documented and it stands inside the sphere where this holding is already densest. The count of transmissions carrying the rule goes from four to six; the count of independent ones does not move. What they add is of three kinds. First, grading: where the other four state that an error is fatal, the Aitareya distinguishes passing over a sentence, inverting two and uniting two, and assigns them a break in the sacrifice, confusion of the sacrificer, and the likelihood of his death, in that order. A doctrine that only warned would not need to rank the ways of getting it wrong. Second, a mechanism: the sacrifice is a yoked team, and the call is a thunderbolt that must be appeased after every use with a formula the corpus says not every man knows. Third, the same corpus makes the very variation that is a fault when it happens into a technique when it is intended — by reciting the verse in a raised tone and the call in a depressed one the Hotṛ makes the man he sacrifices for worse, and by the reverse better, at his own discretion and without declaring it."@en,
        "What the fourth witness does add is a different thing, and it sharpens the correspondence rather than padding it. First, it names who suffers: the ill-luck falls on the person or family the chant honours and not on the reciter who made the fault, so the party that could report the lapse and the party that bears it are different people. Second, it names a countermeasure the transmission itself takes: a master of song gathers two or more of his fellows to edit and memorise the lines, and genealogies must be memorised by more than one reciter. A transmission that builds redundancy against its own stated failure condition has treated the condition as a real risk, which is evidence about the practice that a bare threat is not. Third, the same tradition sets the opposite rule for a different kind of utterance in the same file: at the consecration of the hula altar what must not lapse is the continuity of the voice, and the teacher is expressly free to change the prayer to avoid a silence. The exactness rule is therefore not a general doctrine about ritual speech even where it holds, and the correspondence should not be read as one."@en,
        "What the node claims and what it does not. Independent attestation is asserted for the two North American transmissions with respect to the Polynesian one and with respect to everything else this collection holds; no route of contact between the Pacific and the American Southwest is available for the periods in which any of these corpora were fixed. It is not asserted between those two: they are neighbours in one region, and a route between Pueblo and Athabaskan ceremonialism is neither absent nor established here. The Māori witness is therefore the third leg and not a third independent one, because its own contact route is entered as disputed."@en ;
    pm:convergingConcept pmc:HawaiianChantExactness,
        pmc:MaoriExactRecitation,
        pmc:NavajoSongExactness,
        pmc:VaidikaNividdosah,
        pmc:VaidikaVasatkaraRiktah,
        pmc:ZuniPrayerExactness ;
    pm:independenceGround "Not a claim of priority in time. The two American corpora were fixed in eighteen ninety-seven and, from fieldwork of the nineteen-twenties, in nineteen thirty-two, both after the Polynesian witness was printed in eighteen eighty-two. What carries the independence is that no route of contact between the Pacific and the American Southwest is available for the period in which any of the three corpora were fixed, so agreement between them cannot be one statement counted twice. Two things this ground does not settle and does not hide: both American corpora were gathered by ethnographers working in a discipline to which the rule of exact recitation was already familiar, and neither passage says whether the rule was volunteered in the field or asked for. The second is the gathering question and it stands open." ;
    pm:independentAttestation pmt:NavajoCeremonial,
        pmt:ZuniRitual .

pmc:ExpectationOfStillnessIsTheOperator a pm:Positing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Erwartung, dass sich physisch nichts bewegt, ist der eigentliche Operator"@de,
        "The expectation that nothing moves physically is the operator"@en ;
    skos:definition "The claim that what brings the state about is not the technique but the practitioner's settled expectation that his body will stay where it is."@en ;
    skos:note "Unusual among the draft's claims because it is about a mechanism rather than a capacity, and unusual in its direction: it places the working part in the practitioner's expectation, which is where a sceptical account would also place it. A draft that says this about its own method is saying something against interest, and that is worth recording whatever else follows."@en ;
    pm:positedIn "prima-materia#304" .

pmc:Ezeqeel a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Ezeqeel"@de,
        "Ezeqeel"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "A Watcher named in the Ethiopic teaching list as the one who disclosed the knowledge of the clouds."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:nameForm "Ezêqêêl" ;
    pm:nameRole "watcher" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:FaYieldsForeknowledge a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der Wurf bringt die Antwort hervor"@de,
        "Casting the nuts yields the answer"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. A. Skertchly, Dahomey as it is (London: Chapman and Hall, 1874), pp. 475-476" ;
    skos:note "The same form of claim the graph holds from the Zande poison oracle, the Ifá casting and, since the Kongo file, the bark ordeal: the answer is read off an outcome the operator is said not to steer. Here the route to one of the others is documented and stated on the tradition instance, so this node may not be counted as a further independent witness for it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Fa ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by Fon practitioners at Abomey and Whydah, unnamed in this passage; recorded by the naturalist J. A. Skertchly during his detention in Dahomey in 1871, published 1874; given in Fon, of which the work prints the figure names and a few terms, the account itself in English." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Vodun ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ForeknowledgeFromFa .

pmc:FailureIsSulkinessOrLostPower a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Ding, das versagt, schmollt oder ist erschöpft"@de,
        "A charged thing that fails is either sulky or weak"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), p. 235 and pp. 230-231 and p. 250" ;
    skos:definition "The corpus states the diagnosis as a pair of alternatives with a remedy for each. A charged thing that fails to perform its office must either have a sacrifice offered to it or have its power renewed by the practitioner who first made it, or by a member of the same cult: it is either sulky because it has been neglected, or weak from loss of power. The sacrifice removes the sulkiness and the practitioner restores the power."@en ;
    skos:note "The same corpus records the second, wider form of the move: when a man is not helped, he as a rule does not blame the practitioner but concludes that the diagnosis was wrong and that his trouble is not under the control of that particular charged thing, and simply changes practitioners, his confidence in the order untouched. The transmission thus has two exits from a failure before any question about the practice arises."@en,
        "This is the node the file is built around. It is a complete account of non-occurrence that never reaches the claim: whatever happens, the outcome is assigned to the state of the instrument and not to the question whether the instrument works. It costs a fee in either branch."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:KongoNkisi .

pmc:FangEyenShowsWhatIsHidden a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das éyèn zeigt eine Einweihung, einen Dieb oder jede andere verborgene Sache"@de,
        "The éyèn shows an initiation, a thief, or any other hidden fact"@en ;
    dcterms:source "H. Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan (Münster i. W.: Aschendorffsche Verlagsbuchhandlung, 1912), p. 355" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity ascribed to the mirror: it serves in very many circumstances and great confidence is placed in it, and what it is held to show is an initiation, the identity of thieves, or any other hidden fact."@en ;
    skos:note "The recorder's inspection of the instrument stands in the same sentence as the confidence placed in it, and it is the sharpest counter-observation in the passages read. He writes that most of the time this mirror is in such bad condition or so frightfully tarnished that only the imagination, violently overexcited by the absorption of certain substances or vegetable alkaloids - he names ibogaïne, ouabaïne, strophantine - can permit anything at all to be seen in it. The claim and the state of the thing the claim runs on are therefore both attested by the same witness, and he is a witness who handled the objects."@en,
        "The trade mirror is the second finding at this place and it bears on the contact route. The Fang obtain mirrors easily from the little pomade boxes covered with a glass that are sold in quantity on the coast, and these have everywhere replaced the mica sheets formerly used. The instrument of the central act is therefore an import of the trade era, and the practice as described cannot be older than the glass in it, whatever is true of the practice that used mica."@en,
        "Why that is not entered as a dispute and not as a protocol. It is one source, so pm:Disputing cannot hold it; and no procedure was devised and no case counted, so pm:Testing would be a stub of the kind SPEC section 11 forbids. What it is, is a condition under which the claim would be shown to be about something other than the mirror - that the mirror cannot carry an image - and that condition is met, by the recorder's own report, most of the time. Whoever writes the protocol has the falsifying condition already stated and needs a count."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by H. Trilles among the Fang of the French Congo, published 1912, in his own voice, with no informant named for the claim. The recorder's own inspection of the mirrors and his procuring of a single mica specimen are reported by him as his own observation and are kept distinct from the ascription in the notes above. The Fang term éyèn is carried; no Fang sentence is." ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LeTotemismeChezLesFan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bwiti .

pmc:FangMelanIsTwoPlantsUnderOneName a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zwei sehr verschiedene Arten liefern den mélan, und der Grad entscheidet, welche"@de,
        "Two very different species furnish the mélan, and the degree decides which"@en ;
    dcterms:source "H. Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan (Münster i. W.: Aschendorffsche Verlagsbuchhandlung, 1912), p. 271, footnote 1" ;
    skos:definition "The claim the whole passage turns on, stated by the recorder as a fact about the word: two very different species furnish the mélan. The first degree takes Tabernanthe iboga; the second degree takes Erythrophlœum guineense under the same name. One name therefore covers two plants, and which one is meant is fixed by the stage of the initiation and by nothing in the name."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as its own node because the holding has nothing else of this shape and because every later claim about this transmission inherits it. A procedure named by its substance, whose substance changes with the grade while the name does not, cannot be identified from the name alone - so a report that initiates ate the mélan does not say what they ate, and two reports that agree on the word may disagree on the plant without either being wrong."@en,
        "It is also the place where the dispute at pmc:WhatMelanIsDisputed bites. The second of the two plants is the one the Pangwe monograph of 1913 names repeatedly and puts to an entirely different use; the first is the one that monograph does not name at all. If that monograph is right about the Fang, the claim here is a claim about one plant with a second one attached, and the attachment is the recorder's."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by H. Trilles, Holy Ghost missionary, resident among the Fang of the French Congo for fifteen years, published 1912. No speaker is named; the statement stands in the recorder's own voice in a footnote, and the botanical identifications in it are his. The Fang term mélan is carried; no Fang sentence is." ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LeTotemismeChezLesFan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bwiti .

pmc:FinnicAurinkoonMennessa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Vor dem Ende des Tages, vor dem Untergang der Sonne"@de,
        "Ere the close of day, ere the setting of the sun"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John Abercromby, The Pre- and Proto-historic Finns, vol. II (London: David Nutt, 1898), § 17 g, p. 97" ;
    skos:definition "The one term in what was read. The exorcism orders the evil to go, if it has a master, to its master's seat, and if a mistress, to its mistress's feet, to the mouth of its brother's door and the end of its sister's floor — ere the close of day, ere the setting of the sun."@en ;
    skos:note "A term set on the departure and not on the recovery of the patient. Nothing in the passage says what is to be concluded if the sun sets and the sick man is unchanged; the formula's own provision for that case is the conditional two clauses further on."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:FinnicIncantation .

pmc:FinnicCautionAgainstTheEnvious a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Gegen den neidischen Zuschauer"@de,
        "Against the envious onlooker"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John Abercromby, The Pre- and Proto-historic Finns, vol. II (London: David Nutt, 1898), § 3 a-b, pp. 70-71" ;
    skos:note "The caution is an operative formula and not a rule about who may attend, which is the form this holding has the same claim in from the French magnetic manual. Here there is no instruction to exclude anyone; there is a curse laid on the envious onlooker in advance — may the slag of Hiisi fill his eyes and a fiery bung plug up his mouth — and a blessing on the one who looks without envy. The transmission answers the same danger by acting on the onlooker rather than by removing him."@en,
        "The collection adds the exclusion elsewhere and by a different route, in the editor's account of how the work was done rather than in a song: the vapour-bath house was heated as secretly as possible so that no envious or malevolent person should get wind of it and injure the operation. That sentence is the compiler's and is entered at pmc:LonnrotJarjestys, not here."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:FinnicKateus ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:FinnicIncantation .

pmc:FinnicKateus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der neidische Blick, der die Arbeit verdirbt"@de,
        "The envious look that spoils the work"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John Abercromby, The Pre- and Proto-historic Finns, vol. II (London: David Nutt, 1898), § 3 a-c, pp. 70-72" ;
    skos:definition "What the formula against envy names: whoever looks with jealousy, looks pryingly with eyes askew, casts resentful glances or prepares some malevolence — and, in the second variant, whoever keeps turning round with a jealous eye, causes bewitchment with his mouth or imprecates with words. The speaker opens by saying that he cannot guard against envy nor shield himself from opponents while dealing with these toilsome tasks."@en ;
    skos:note "The distinction the formula draws is between the envious look and the look without envy, and it is drawn inside the same passage: whoever looks without envy is to be blessed so that he shall have honeyed eyes, and whoever happens to overlook or stares from one side is asked to look with a honeyed glance. The disposition of the onlooker and not his presence is what the formula treats as the variable."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:FinnicIncantation .

pmc:FinnicMerkkiKotona a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Zeichen wird gefordert, und anderswo gefordert"@de,
        "The sign is demanded, and demanded elsewhere"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John Abercromby, The Pre- and Proto-historic Finns, vol. II (London: David Nutt, 1898), § 17 g, pp. 97-98" ;
    skos:definition "The one sign in what was read. The same exorcism tells the evil that on its arrival at the abode of him that fashioned it, it is to give a sign, a secret signal on reaching home, to cause a crash like a thunder-clap and a glare like a lightning-flash."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered under the separate count SPEC §15 requires for signs of success, and it is the case that shows why the count is kept separate. The sign is perceptible, it is named in advance, and it is to occur at the house of whoever sent the evil — that is, at a place where neither the operator nor the patient is, and at an hour when they are elsewhere. A transmission that names its only confirming event outside the room has stated something no participant could report absent."@en,
        "It is the counterpart of the deadline above and belongs beside it: the term is set on the same journey, and the sign is what would mark its completion. Together they are a complete verification procedure with no one at the place of verification."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:FinnicIncantation .

pmc:FinnicPahanPoisto a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das unnötige Übel wird ausgetrieben"@de,
        "The needless evil is expelled"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John Abercromby, The Pre- and Proto-historic Finns, vol. II (London: David Nutt, 1898), § 210 a, p. 332" ;
    skos:definition "The outcome the song states of the recitation, in one line and without a condition attached: by pronouncing a deep origin, words that are handed down, a needless evil is expelled."@en ;
    skos:note "The line is the whole of the transmission's own statement of its central doctrine, and it is worth noticing how little it says. It names no interval, no repetition, no sign, and no state of either party. Everything else this holding will carry about the synty comes from what the operator does when it does not work."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:FinnicIncantation .

pmc:FirstWeek a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die erste Woche"@de,
        "The first week"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ignatius of Loyola, Spiritual Exercises 4" ;
    skos:definition "The purgative part of the exercises, given over to the consideration of sin."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChristianAsceticDiscernment .

pmc:FiveHindrances a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die fünf Hemmnisse"@de,
        "The five hindrances"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)" ;
    skos:definition "Sensual desire, ill will, dullness and drowsiness, restlessness and remorse, and doubt, whose abandoning the discourse describes immediately before entry into the first absorption."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DighaNikaya ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:ForcedBreathCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Ein erzwungener statt allmählich gezähmter Atem tötet den Übenden"@de,
        "Breath forced rather than tamed by degrees kills the practitioner"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.15 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.16-17 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "A warning that the breath is to be mastered by degrees as a lion, an elephant or a tiger is mastered, and that it destroys the one who proceeds otherwise; performed wrongly, the practice is said to generate the very ailments it removes when performed rightly."@en ;
    skos:note "The named consequences are hiccough, laboured breathing, cough, and pain in head, ears and eyes. This is the strongest thing the manual says about the manner of breathing, and it concerns pace and force, not the opening the breath passes through. See prima-materia#26."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:HathaPranayama ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:ForeknowledgeFromFa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Was Fa kundtun soll"@de,
        "What Fa is said to make known"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. A. Skertchly, Dahomey as it is (London: Chapman and Hall, 1874), pp. 475-476",
        "Richard F. Burton, A Mission to Gelele, King of Dahome (2nd ed., London: Tinsley Brothers, 1864), vol. II, p. 143" ;
    skos:definition "Two things, according to the witnesses. Where a mother is cast, the day denoted by its symbol is the propitious day for the business asked about, and where a child is cast no day is more propitious than another; and the oracle is said to predict evil, in which case a prescribed remedy follows. Simple answers of yes and no are said to be obtainable by anybody, while more stands with the practitioner."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Vodun .

pmc:FourthDegreeYieldsThoughtReading a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der vierte Grad bringt das Gedankenlesen hervor"@de,
        "The fourth degree yields the reading of thoughts"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 170" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:MideInitiationFourthDegree ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:MideThoughtReading .

pmc:Gadreel a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Gadreel"@de,
        "Gadreel"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 69:6 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "A figure named in the Book of Parables as the one who led Eve astray and disclosed the weapons of death."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:GehongFangshuWuyi a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Künste zeigen ihren Wert nur in dem Fall, der ausbleiben kann"@de,
        "The arts show their worth only in the case that may not arise"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 9 (Daoyi) 10b-11a, in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 9" ;
    skos:definition "The chapter states where the arts are to be relied on and where not. For guarding the person and warding off harm one is to cultivate the prohibitions that guard the form and to wear the talismans and sword of the celestial writ; sacrifice and prayer are of no benefit. One should rely on one's own being unassailable and not on the spirits' not assailing one. And it states a limit: pondering the mystery, holding the One and enclosing the light about the body can turn aside evil and carry one past the inauspicious, but it cannot lengthen the span of life or dispel a sickness of the body. One who follows nature and has no arts is not necessarily prevented from reaching his allotted years; but he cannot meet the violence of a fierce ghost or the passage of a great epidemic, and against those he will have nothing to turn them aside. The chapter closes the argument with an analogy: one who stores armour and keeps a straw cloak does so against war and against rain, and if by good fortune there is no campaign and the sky does not close over, then having them and not having them come to exactly the same; but when arrow and stone gather like fog and the flying points cross like smoke, then the straits of the naked man are known, and when the rain comes down as though a river were tipped out and the snow fills the sky, then the plight of the man standing on the road is felt."@en ;
    skos:note "Three things stand in this passage and they should not be run together. The first is a limit stated against the author's own side and worth having on that account: the meditative practice is said not to lengthen life and not to dispel bodily illness, which is a narrower claim than the chapter's readers would have expected. The second is a concession that the unequipped may well reach their full span. The third is the reservation that makes the first two costless — the arts show their worth only against a violent ghost or a great epidemic, events that may not arise in a given life, so that in the ordinary case having them and not having them are conceded to come to the same. A claim reserved to the exceptional case cannot be told from a claim about nothing by any ordinary run of lives, and the author has arrived at that position in the course of refuting people who did the same."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout "Judging the arts of self-protection by ordinary years, in which the case they are for does not arise" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BaopuziNeipian ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:GehongFuguiChangsheng a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Wenn Gebet das Leben verlängerte, müssten die Reichen zwangsläufig lange leben"@de,
        "If prayer lengthened life the rich would necessarily live long"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 9 (Daoyi) 2b-3a, in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 9",
        "Lu Xiujing, Lu xiansheng daomen kelüe 001b-002a (DZ 1127), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "The chapter puts a conditional against the claim it is disputing: good fortune is not obtained by servile reverence and calamity is not averted by sacrifice; for if a destiny could be extended by repeated prayer and an illness removed by lavish offering, then the wealthy would of necessity live long and the noble would be without sickness. It adds that the spirits do not partake of what is not of their kin and that ghosts do not accept illicit offerings, and concludes that they are not to be moved by artful speech and not to be sought by dressed-up bribes."@en ;
    skos:note "This is an argument of the form the holding rarely gets from inside a transmission: it derives from the claim a consequence about a class of people, observes that the consequence does not hold, and rejects the claim. The class is besides the one with the most to spend on the remedy, which makes the observation cheap to check and hard to explain away. Entered as a dispute because the other side is in the holding: the transmission at traditions/celestial-masters.ttl makes exactly the claim this argument attacks, and the chapter's own remedy is exposed to the same argument at pmc:GehongFangshuWuyi."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim "Whether prayer and offering lengthen a life or remove an illness" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BaopuziNeipian ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:GehongMaShi a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Anweisung des Heilers: sage, du seist geheilt, auch wenn du es nicht bist"@de,
        "The healer's instruction: say you are cured even if you are not"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 9 (Daoyi) 9b-10a, in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 9" ;
    skos:definition "The chapter reports a case from Xinggu. The prefect Ma had a relation come to him in need; he had the man lodge outside and gave out that he was a divine man and a Daoist who treated illness, with none who was not cured on the spot, and set persuasive speakers to circulate the report that he could make the blind see and the lame walk at once. People gathered from the four quarters and thronged like a market, and money and silk were already heaped up like a hill. And he further instructed all who came for treatment: though you are not cured at once, you are to tell people that you are cured — do it so and you will certainly be cured; but if you tell anybody that you are not yet cured, then in the end you will never be cured; such is the law of the Way, and you must not fail to trust it. So those who came later asked those who had come before, and the earlier ones always told them they were already cured, and none dared say he was not. Within ten days the man had come into an immense fortune."@en ;
    skos:note "The mechanism by which an efficacy report produces itself, set out in a named case, with the operative rule quoted, and described by a contemporary as a deliberate fraud. It does two things at once. It forbids the negative report by making the report itself the cause of the failure it would report, so that a man who says he is not cured has thereby forfeited the cure; and it turns every earlier patient into a witness for the next. The chapter's own comment is that what people call gods are all of the sort of Ma's deception of people, and that he records these several cases as a warning to those who have not yet seen through it."@en,
        "The rule has a counterpart in a transmission that states it as doctrine rather than as fraud: at pmc:CmYanhuiWuyi the admonitions of the Celestial Masters make it an offence of three thousand to say, after a calamity, that serving the Dao brings no benefit. Two Chinese sources of the same century thus carry the same device, one as a crime committed by a swindler and one as a rule of the Way, and neither depends on the other. Generalized at pmc:TheRuleAgainstReportingTheAbsence."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout "A rule that makes the report of a failure the cause of that failure" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BaopuziNeipian ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:GreaterMysteries a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die großen Mysterien"@de,
        "The greater mysteries"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Athenian decree concerning Eleusis, IG I³ 6, face C" ;
    skos:definition "The rite at Eleusis, held in the month Boedromion."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EleusinianMysteries .

pmc:HalauConduct a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die von der Hula-Schar geforderte Führung"@de,
        "The conduct required of the hula company"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Nathaniel B. Emerson, Unwritten Literature of Hawaii: The Sacred Songs of the Hula, Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 38 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1909), chapter II, p. 15" ;
    skos:definition "Five requirements, given together: to maintain the greatest propriety of demeanour, to suppress all rudeness of speech and manner, to abstain from all carnal indulgence, to deny themselves specified articles of food, and above all to avoid contact with a corpse."@en ;
    skos:note "The list mixes conditions of different kinds without marking the difference, and the mixture is itself the finding. Propriety and rudeness are matters of judgement and could be argued about; contact with a corpse is an event that either happened or did not. A rule set in which only some members are decidable is the ordinary case in this collection, and it is what makes a transmission look stricter than it is testable."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HawaiianRitual .

pmc:HarbaDeMosheGeneralisClausula a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Klausel, die alles deckt, was die Liste nicht genannt hat"@de,
        "The clause that covers everything the list did not name"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The Sword of Moses, ed. and trans. Moses Gaster (London: David Nutt, 1896), part III, no. 136 in the editor's numbering" ;
    skos:definition "The hundred and thirty-sixth and last operation, which is not an operation but a rule about the other hundred and thirty-five: for every other thing that has not been mentioned, say this section and read on to the end of the Sword."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered separately from the operation of the same number because of what it is. A list that ends by covering everything it left out cannot be exhausted, and no reader can ever establish that the work does not provide for a case. It is also the clause on which the operation at number 124 depends, which conjures a heavenly prince by reading from here to the end."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HarbaDeMoshe ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hekhalot .

pmc:HarbaDeMosheSecrecy a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Verschwiegenheit, die das Schwert verlangt"@de,
        "The secrecy the Sword requires"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The Sword of Moses, ed. and trans. Moses Gaster (London: David Nutt, 1896), the opening instructions" ;
    skos:definition "A condition of a different kind from the counts. No one is to know that the user intends to use the Sword, and it is not to be communicated except to the chaste and the pure, because in it are the mysteries of the universe and they are practised only in secret."@en ;
    skos:note "The secrecy clause is the gate this project exists to remove, stated by the transmission in its own words, and it is worth holding as a node rather than as background: it names the criterion of admission, and the criterion is moral rather than lineal."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HarbaDeMoshe ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hekhalot .

pmc:HauOfTheGift a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Hau der Gabe"@de,
        "The hau of the gift"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tamati Ranapiri, letter to Elsdon Best, 23 November 1907, Polynesian Society Papers, Alexander Turnbull Library, MS-Papers-1187, folder 271" ;
    skos:definition "The account, given by a named Māori authority in a letter, that what comes back from a third party for a valuable passed on is the hau of the valuable first received, and that keeping it back for oneself would be fatal to the keeper."@en ;
    skos:note "Held at mediated attestation because the letter is an archival manuscript and was read in the study that prints and collates it, not in the archive. The published version of 1909 could not be opened in this run either; the paths tried are recorded in prima-materia#106. This node is therefore an open post and is to be raised to textual attestation when either the manuscript or the 1909 printing is read."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "Manuka Hēnare, \"Ko te hau tēnā o tō taonga…\": The words of Ranapiri on the spirit of gift exchange and economy, Journal of the Polynesian Society 127/4 (2018), pp. 455-457" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Maori .

pmc:HawaiianChantWelfare a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Wohlergehen der besungenen Person oder Familie"@de,
        "The good fortune of the person or family the chant honours"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Martha Warren Beckwith, The Kumulipo: A Hawaiian Creation Chant (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), chapter 7, p. 35" ;
    skos:definition "What the correct rendering is held to secure. The transmission places the outcome not with the reciter but with the party the chant is made for, which is the unusual part of this witness."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HawaiianRitual .

pmc:HawaiianWelfarePresupposesExactChanting a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Wohlergehen der besungenen Familie setzt den fehlerlosen Gesang voraus"@de,
        "The good fortune of the honoured family presupposes a chant without fault"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Martha Warren Beckwith, The Kumulipo: A Hawaiian Creation Chant (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), chapter 7, p. 35" ;
    skos:note "The transfer of the harm is what distinguishes this witness from the others the graph holds on the same rule. Where the Maori address is simply said to lose its efficacy and the Navajo ceremony is broken off, here the reciter is not the one who suffers. That makes the rule harder to test from inside the tradition, because the person who could report the fault and the person who bears the consequence are different people."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "A breath taken before the close of a phrase, a mistake, or even hesitation in pronouncing a word was a sign of ill-luck to the person or family thus honoured. The harm is named, it is transferred, and it falls on the party who did not commit the fault." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:HawaiianChantWelfare ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:HawaiianChantExactness ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HawaiianRitual .

pmc:HawkinsConsciousnessScale a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Ordnung der Affekte nach gemessener Stufe"@de,
        "The ranking of emotions by measured level"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1995" ;
    dcterms:source "D. R. Hawkins, Power vs. Force (1995)" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that emotional and mental states occupy fixed positions on a single numbered scale, ascending from shame through fear, anger and pride to reason, love, joy, peace and enlightenment, and that the position can be established by testing."@en ;
    skos:note "An earlier form of this note held that no transmission ranks affects on a single ordered scale. That was withdrawn: the ground for it had been a search across the holdings of this graph, which says what has been entered here and not what the transmissions say. The search that had not been carried out finds one at once. Rūpa Gosvāmin ranks the five primary forms of devotional love in a single ascending order in the sixteenth century, in the verse itself, and traditions/gaudiya-vaisnava.ttl now holds it. The finding of this node is therefore not that ranking affects is modern. It is narrower and survives: the particular scale below, with its count, its order and its claim of measurability, begins here."@en,
        "The diagnostic markers, given so that any later list presenting itself as old can be measured against a dated first appearance. The count: seventeen steps. The order of the lower band: shame, guilt, apathy, grief, fear, desire, anger, pride. The turn at the middle: courage, then neutrality, then willingness in eleventh place. The upper band: acceptance, reason, love, joy, peace, enlightenment. A list that shares the count, the relative order of shame, guilt, fear and anger, and the eleventh position of willingness has not arrived at them independently."@en,
        "The nearest older relative is pmc:TheosophicalColourKeyToFeeling, and the comparison sharpens rather than dissolves this node. That transmission supplies the vibration vocabulary and an axis of self-reference on which a feeling rises or falls, and it was read at the wording for this purpose. It supplies no ranking of unlike feelings against one another. The single numbered order is therefore not inherited, and the question of where it does come from is open in prima-materia#12."@en ;
    pm:circulatesAs "A perennial map of consciousness, reported as the distilled agreement of the mystical traditions." ;
    pm:originatedBy "David R. Hawkins" .

pmc:HealingByTouch a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Heilung durch Berührung"@de,
        "Healing by touch"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Suetonius, Divus Vespasianus 7",
        "Tacitus, Historiae IV.81" ;
    skos:definition "The restoration of a withered hand and of sight, ascribed to the touch of a ruler in Alexandria."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DivusVespasianus,
        pmw:TacitiHistoriae ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GraecoRoman .

pmc:HeatWithoutForcefulBreath a pm:Positing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Hitze entsteht ohne den kraftvollen Atem"@de,
        "The heat arises without the forceful breath"@en ;
    skos:definition "The claim that the gentler form of the practice, without the forceful breathing, produces the same effect as the form the tradition prescribes."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because it is the point where the method draft parts company with the transmission it draws on. The transmission prescribes the forceful breath; the draft says it can be left out. That is a claim about the draft's own variant and belongs to nobody else, which is why it is posited and not sourced."@en ;
    pm:positedIn "prima-materia#318" .

pmc:HekhalotDescentPresupposesFitness a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Bestehen am sechsten Palast setzt die Eignung zum Abstieg voraus"@de,
        "Passing the sixth palace presupposes fitness to descend"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hekhalot Rabbati, chapter 26, trans. Morton Smith, corrected by Gershom Scholem, ed. Don Karr" ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity because the consequence is quotable and because the passage sets no lesser outcome beside it: no second attempt, no purification that would restore the failed descender, no return."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The transmission states the consequence in a sequence and states it as violence, not as exclusion. The doorkeepers run after him and stone him; they name him of the seed of them that kissed the calf and unfit to see the King and his throne in his beauty; a voice from the seventh heaven confirms that they have said well; and he does not move from the spot before they place on him a thousand thousands of pieces of iron." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:YeridatMerkavah ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HekhalotRabbati ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:HekhalotFitness ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hekhalot .

pmc:HermeticDescensusPerHarmoniam a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Jeder Herrscher gibt einen Anteil seiner eigenen Ordnung"@de,
        "Each ruler gives a share of his own ordering"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Corpus Hermeticum I.13-15 (trans. G. R. S. Mead)" ;
    skos:definition "The account of the descent: Man, changing his state to the formative sphere, gazed on the creatures of his brother, and they loved him and each gave him a share of his own ordering; having learned their essence and become a sharer in their nature, he broke through the boundary of their spheres and showed the fair form of God to downward Nature. He is then said to be above the Harmony and within the Harmony a slave."@en ;
    skos:note "The descent is not a fall through disobedience and is not punished; it is granted by the Father and driven by love. The vices given back at the ascent are therefore not sins acquired against the order but shares of an ordering that was handed over. That distinction is lost in every summary that calls this a fall."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hermetic .

pmc:HermeticDissolutioCorporis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Was vor den Zonen abgegeben wird"@de,
        "What is given up before the zones"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Corpus Hermeticum I.24 (trans. G. R. S. Mead)" ;
    skos:definition "The three surrenders that precede the ascent: the body is given to the work of change and the form one had vanishes; the way of life, void of its energy, is given to the daimon; and the body's senses pass back into their sources, becoming separate and rising again as energies, while passion and desire withdraw to the nature that is void of reason."@en ;
    skos:note "The way of life goes to a daimon before the zones are reached, and the treatise does not say which daimon or whether it is the avenging one of I.23. The silence is left standing here rather than filled."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hermetic .

pmc:HermeticFinisBonus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Eins werden mit Gott"@de,
        "To be made one with God"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Corpus Hermeticum I.26 (trans. G. R. S. Mead)" ;
    skos:definition "The end named for those who have gained gnosis: they go in a band to the Father, of their own selves make surrender of themselves to the powers, and becoming powers are in God."@en ;
    skos:note "Placed on the telic axis as pm:unitiveTelos would be a reading and is not made here; the wording carries both a becoming-powers and a being-in-God, and which of the two is the end is exactly what a placement would have to decide. Left unsituated deliberately, so that the absence means nobody has decided rather than that the source is silent."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hermetic .

pmc:HeterodoxWorshipNeverClearsTheDebt a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die heterodoxe Methode tilgt die Schuld nie"@de,
        "The heterodox method never clears the debt"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Kālikāpurāṇa, at the verses stating that heterodox worship never clears the debt (132-133a) and that liberation comes only after a very long time with repeated rebirth (133b-135a) (text, introduction and translation in English by B. N. Shastri, part III, Nag Publishers, Delhi 1991)" ;
    skos:definition "The warning that because the goddess takes for herself the share offered to others, worship performed in the heterodox method never results in clearing the debt to ancestors, sages, gods and the human race; and that one who becomes an expert in the tripurā practice attains liberation only after a very long time and is reborn on this earth again and again, having been visited by the sin arising from the uncleared debt."@en ;
    skos:note "A failure condition of the kind this project looks for, and an unusually exact one: the source states the price of its own path, names the mechanism, and says what remains unattained. It does not warn against the rite. It says what the rite costs, which is the harder thing for a tradition to write down about itself."@en,
        "It also settles what the two methods are weighed on in this text. Not purity, not moral standing, not the direction of the will: the debt. The orthodox method discharges it and is rewarded in due course; the heterodox method leaves it standing and pays immediately instead. Whoever takes the left hand path here for the faster route has read half of it — it is the faster route to power and the slower one to liberation, and the source says both in the same breath."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:VamaWorship ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmc:HippocratesAnaitios a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Nicht als die Ursache zu gelten"@de,
        "Being held not to be the cause"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, The Book of Prognostics 1, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, p. 235; the Greek from Hippocrates, vol. II, ed. and trans. W. H. S. Jones, Loeb Classical Library 148, London 1923, p. 6" ;
    skos:altLabel "ἀναίτιος"@grc ;
    skos:definition "The state the treatise names as what the announcing secures for the physician: foreknowing and foretelling those who will die and those who will be saved, he would be blameless. The Greek word is the one used of a party who is not the cause of what happened; Adams renders the clause as escaping censure."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the capacity the opening chapter actually argues for, and it is not a capacity over the disease. The chapter has just conceded that some die before the physician is called and some immediately after, before he could bring his art to counteract the disease, and that it is therefore necessary to know how far such affections are above the powers of the constitution. The announcing is what converts that concession into a position: a death that was announced is a death the announcer is not answerable for."@en,
        "What would tell against reading it this way. The same clause can be read as saying only that a physician who predicts correctly will not be blamed for predicting, which is a statement about reputation and not about liability. The Greek does not decide it: ἀναίτιος is used both ways. What does decide it is the sentence before, which is about cases the art cannot reach, and the words the translator chose, escape censure; both point at answerability for the outcome."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PrognosticonHippocratis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine .

pmc:HippocratesDiesCritici a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Tage, an denen Fieber zur Krise kommen sollen"@de,
        "The days on which fevers are said to come to a crisis"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, The Book of Prognostics 20, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, pp. 251-252; the Greek from Hippocrates, vol. II, ed. and trans. W. H. S. Jones, Loeb Classical Library 148, London 1923, pp. 42-43" ;
    skos:altLabel "διὰ τεσσάρων ἐς τὰς εἴκοσιν ἐκ προσθέσιος"@grc ;
    skos:definition "A series stated as doctrine and not as a range of observations: fevers come to a crisis on the same days as to number on which men recover and die. The mildest end on the fourth day or earlier and the most malignant kill on the fourth day or earlier; the second class is protracted to the seventh, the third to the eleventh, the fourth to the fourteenth, the fifth to the seventeenth and the sixth to the twentieth, so that the periods ascend by fours up to twenty. After these, in the same progression, come periods of thirty-four, forty and sixty days. Quartans and the crises of women after parturition are said to follow the same order."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because it is the one thing in the treatise that fixes a moment at which a prediction has either held or not, and because the holding already carries counted terms from four other transmissions and had none from this one. What rides on the number here is the course of a disease and not a site, which is the distinction the counter-comparandum at pmc:TheIntervalAfterTheWound turns on."@en,
        "The series is reported to be in open dispute inside its own collection, and that report is NOT a reading. The research that preceded this run gives Aphorisms IV.36 as making the critical days of sweats an odd-numbered series with a twenty-first and a twenty-seventh in it, which would conflict with the tetrads here; that section stands in the second volume of this translation and was not opened in this run, and the citation is therefore second hand and enters no node. Whoever opens it writes the pm:Disputing. The sentence is kept because it names the place, which is what a report is good for."@en,
        "Two things stand in the same chapter's apparatus and were read there, in the translator's footnote to this chapter. Adams states that the treatise On Critical Days is in all probability spurious, referring to his own Preliminary Discourse; and he reports Galen as not hesitating to declare Hippocrates the first who treated of the critical days, while doubting whether Galen had any competent authority for pronouncing it. He also remarks that the subject is not touched upon in the Coan Prognostics, so that this chapter is either original or taken from a source now unknown — which is the only chapter of the treatise he says that of."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PrognosticonHippocratis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine .

pmc:HippocratesFaciesMoribunda a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Gesicht, an dem der Tod nahe sein soll"@de,
        "The face by which death is said to be at hand"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, The Book of Prognostics 2, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, pp. 235-236" ;
    skos:definition "The sign the chapter puts first and describes item by item: a sharp nose, hollow eyes, collapsed temples, the ears cold and contracted with their lobes turned out, the skin about the forehead rough and distended and parched, and the colour of the whole face green, black, livid or lead-coloured. The best countenance is stated in the same place and in the same form: like those of persons in health, and better still like itself."@en ;
    skos:note "A sign and not a cause, and the chapter is explicit that the inference runs from the ensemble: if the countenance be such at the commencement of the disease and this cannot be accounted for from the other symptoms, then the further questions follow. The eye signs of the same chapter are entered with it rather than separately, since the chapter states them as one examination: shunning the light, involuntary weeping, squinting, unequal size, red or livid whites or black veins in them, gum, restlessness, protrusion or hollowness; and, in sleep, a portion of the white appearing because the lids are not closed, which the chapter calls very deadly."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PrognosticonHippocratis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine .

pmc:HippocratesVulnusCapitisTempusAnni a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Zahl hängt an der Jahreszeit, und ausdrücklich nicht am Ort"@de,
        "The number rides on the season, and expressly not on the site"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, On Injuries of the Head 2 and 19, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, pp. 447 and 463-464" ;
    skos:definition "Two statements, in the second and the nineteenth chapter, and they agree. Chapter 2: in fatal cases a man will survive longer in winter than in summer, whatever be the part of the head in which the wound is situated. Chapter 19, of a wound whose broken or contused bone was not discovered and which was neglected as if the bone were sound: fever will generally come on before the fourteenth day if in winter, and in summer the fever usually seizes after seven days; and they die before the end of seven days if in summer, and before fourteen if in winter."@en ;
    skos:note "A second term stands in chapter 14 and is a window rather than an outcome: when the raspatory shows that the wound in the bone requires the operation, you must not postpone it for three days but do it during this period, more especially if the weather be hot. The season enters there as an intensifier and not as a boundary, which is why that place is entered as a prerequisite below and not as a term here."@en,
        "The counted term exists in this corpus and it is indexed on the calendar. Chapter 2 does not merely leave the site unnumbered, it says in one clause that the thing which is counted does not depend on the site at all. The two chapters therefore divide the work between them: the site decides the order, the season decides the number."@en,
        "What this does to the convergence candidate in prima-materia#612. The agreement between the needling-prohibition chapter of the Suwen and the marman chapter of the Susruta is not that a medical transmission couples a wound to a duration — the Greek does that too — but that the duration is given as a number indexed on the site. The Greek indexes the number on the season and the order on the site, and says so expressly. The note at pmc:TheIntervalAfterTheWound is corrected accordingly in the same bundle."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeVulneribusCapitis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine .

pmc:HittEKisibUlKinuzi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das im Siegelhaus niedergelegte Bündel, das niemand wieder öffnet"@de,
        "The bundle deposited in the seal-house that nobody opens again"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Ḫantitaššu, CTH 395.1, KBo 11.14 Rs. IV 15'-19' and KUB 43.57+KBo 18.174 Rs. IV 14'-17' (critical edition, exemplars A and B, hethiter.net/: CTH 395.1, textus 2015-06-16)" ;
    skos:definition "The disposal of the rite: when the seventh day comes the ritual patron takes off the things he has carried and lays them in his linen chest, which they do not open, and they put it inside the seal-house; and nobody opens it again."@en ;
    skos:note "The two exemplars differ in the last verb, and the difference is worth keeping. The general version has nobody set it in motion again, which is the verb of the affliction in the incipit of the same tablet; the version made for the king has nobody open it again. The second version of the ritual, on a separate manuscript, has they carry it into the seal-house and nobody opens it again, and reaches the point after six days rather than nine."@en,
        "This is the flat form of what the ritual of Maštigga states as a condition. Here nothing is said about any case in which the thing would be opened; there the opening is provided for and fixed to the return of the olden kings. A transmission that forbids reopening leaves the rite unrepeatable and unexaminable in the same act, and that it does so twice in one corpus, in two unrelated rituals, is the finding of the counter-search at pmp:HittSealOpeningSearch."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hantitassu ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual .

pmc:HittHalluwai a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Streit zwischen Verwandten, gegen den der Ritus gilt"@de,
        "The quarrel between kin that the rite is performed against"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Maštigga ritual against domestic quarrel, CTH 404.1.II, KBo 2.3+ Vs. I 1-4 and Rs. IV 14-18 (critical edition, exemplar A, hethiter.net/: CTH 404.1.II, Expl. A, textus 2012-05-07)" ;
    skos:altLabel "ḫalluwai-"@en ;
    skos:definition "The condition named in the incipit and repeated in the colophon: a father and a son, or a husband and his wife, or a brother and a sister are to quarrel, and the practitioner sets them together again."@en ;
    skos:note "Three pairs and no others, each one a pair within a household, and the same three in the same order at both ends of the tablet. The rite is performed on two persons at once throughout — the plural of the ritual patron is used of them in every paragraph — so the party treated is the quarrel and not either quarreller."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Mastigga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual .

pmc:HittKisibApiyakku a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Siegel, das erst zu öffnen ist, wenn die einstigen Könige wiederkommen"@de,
        "The seal to be opened only when the olden kings come back"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Maštigga ritual against domestic quarrel, CTH 404.1.II, KBo 2.3+ Rs. IV 7-13 (critical edition, exemplar A, hethiter.net/: CTH 404.1.II, Expl. A, textus 2012-05-07)" ;
    skos:definition "The last act of the rite: the water the pair have poured over their heads and rinsed their hands and eyes with is poured into an ox horn and sealed, and the Old Woman says that when the olden kings come back and take account of the custom of the land, only then is this seal to be opened."@en ;
    skos:note "The condition under which the rite could be undone is set at an event that the transmission nowhere expects. The clause is not a prohibition — it names a case in which the seal is to be opened — and that is what makes it worth entering: a flat prohibition would be a rule about conduct, whereas this is a stated condition of release which cannot be met, so the irreversibility is produced by the condition rather than asserted."@en,
        "The restrictive particle is what carries it: the adverb of place is extended by a particle that limits it to that case, and both reachable translations of the tablet agree that something is restricted to that moment and disagree about everything else. The disagreement is at pmc:HittKisibApiyakkuDisputatum, and it is the reason this node states the clause and not a reading of it."@en,
        "The same device, with the bundle of the rite deposited where nobody opens it again, stands in the ritual of Ḫantitaššu at pmc:HittEKisibUlKinuzi. There it is stated flatly and not as a condition; here it is a condition that does not fall due. The two are the Hittite pair for the claim in generalizations/counted-thresholds.ttl, and the counter-search that found the second is recorded at pmp:HittSealOpeningSearch."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Mastigga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual .

pmc:HittNattaAmmelUddar a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Dies sind nicht meine Worte"@de,
        "These are not my words"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Ḫantitaššu, CTH 395.1, KBo 11.14 Vs. II 20-26 (critical edition, exemplar A, hethiter.net/: CTH 395.1, textus 2015-06-16)" ;
    skos:definition "The clause with which the officiant disclaims the incantation he has just said: these are not my words, they are the words of the Sun-god and of Kamrušepa, and they are the incantations of the son of mankind."@en ;
    skos:note "The officiant denies authorship of the operative words at the moment of saying them. Two consequences for this holding, and the second is the one that makes the node worth more than its curiosity. First, nothing in the rite can be laid to his formulation, because the formulation is not his. Second, the clause stands immediately after the two similes that state what the rite is to achieve — the grain escaping from the grindstone, the rear wheel that does not catch the front one — so what is disclaimed is precisely the predictive part."@en,
        "The reading of the last line of the clause rests on a restoration from a parallel manuscript of a different version of the same ritual; the editors mark it as such. The two preceding lines, which carry the disclaimer and the two divine names, are preserved."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hantitassu ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual .

pmc:HittParkunuwarUzuUr a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die zwölf Körperteile gereinigt"@de,
        "The twelve parts of the body purified"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Tunnawiya, CTH 409, KUB 7.53+KUB 12.58 Rs. III 32 and Rs. IV 8-13, 34-38 (Goetze–Sturtevant, AOS 14, 1938, pp. 7, 23-25)" ;
    skos:definition "The attainment the rite claims for the person it is performed on: the twelve parts of the body are cleansed and purified, and the generative faculty is re-established, so that the house is filled with sons and daughters, grandchildren and great-grandchildren."@en ;
    skos:note "The closing formula is partly broken and the restoration is the editors' of 1938. What stands in the preserved text is that the twelve parts have been purified; that the generative faculty was re-established is supplied by them from the context. The node is entered with the restoration named because the whole point of the tablet's opening is procreation, and the restoration is not what carries the capacity: the fruit-tree address earlier in the same column asks in the preserved text that the patient be covered like the tree and fill the house with grandchildren and great-grandchildren."@en,
        "The twelve parts are counted and never listed. The number occurs six times in the tablet — in the charge to the genius of the river bank, twice in the Old Woman's addresses over the patient, in the combing formula, in the address at the gate of alanza wood and in the closing formula to the Sun-god; nowhere is there an enumeration of which twelve. A claim with a count and no list cannot come out short."@en,
        "What is taken away is a list and not one thing, and the list grows as the rite proceeds. The formulae name, across the tablet, evil uncleanness, sorcery, sin, the anger of the god or of the gods, the terror of the dead, the evil gossip of all mankind, curses, the gossip of the crowd, bad dreams, a third affliction whose term the 1938 edition leaves untranslated, and an early death. Eleven named items, and no formula carries all of them: each one names a selection. A rite whose object is an open list of this kind cannot be said to have missed its object, because the object is whatever of the list the operator named that day."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Tunnawiya ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual .

pmc:HittPaskuwattiYielding a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Anrufung der Uliliyašši soll Männlichkeit zurückgeben und Söhne und Töchter bringen"@de,
        "The invocation of Uliliyašši is said to restore manhood and bring sons and daughters"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Paškuwatti, CTH 406, KUB 7.8+KUB 9.27(+)KUB 7.5 Vs. I 26-29 with Vs. II 1-13 (critical edition, exemplar A, hethiter.net/: CTH 406, Expl. A, textus 2012-05-07)" ;
    skos:note "The second half of the claim is in the invocation and is addressed to the deity rather than asserted of the man: come down to this man, be a wife to him for a child, assign him your handmaid, let him take his wife and beget sons and daughters for himself — and they will be your servants and your handmaids and will keep giving you rituals, thick breads, meal and libations. The payment named is the children's future service, so what the rite promises and what it charges are the same thing."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:HittMugessarUliliyassi ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Paskuwatti ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:HittSaklaiLutar .

pmc:HittRiverBankGenius a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Genius des Flussufers"@de,
        "The genius of the river bank"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Tunnawiya, CTH 409, KUB 7.53+KUB 12.58 Vs. I 26-32 (Goetze–Sturtevant, AOS 14, 1938, pp. 5-7)" ;
    skos:definition "The addressee at the bank, to whom the rite is announced with bread, tallow cake, porridge and a libation of wine before the clay is taken, and who is asked to take the clay in hand and purify the twelve parts of the patient's body with it."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Tunnawiya ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual .

pmc:HittSaklaiLutar a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Weiblichkeit genommen und Männlichkeit zurückgegeben"@de,
        "Womanhood taken away and manhood given back"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Paškuwatti, CTH 406, KUB 7.8+KUB 9.27(+)KUB 7.5 Vs. I 26-29 (critical edition, exemplar A, hethiter.net/: CTH 406, Expl. A, textus 2012-05-07)" ;
    skos:altLabel "šaklai-"@en ;
    skos:definition "The attainment asserted at the reed gate, in the first person and in the perfect: I have taken womanhood away from you and given manhood back to you; you have cast off the custom of a woman, take up the custom of a man."@en ;
    skos:note "The assertion is in the perfect at the moment of the act and is not conditional on anything. It is made before the three days of invocation begin and before the night of the sign, so the rite asserts its own success twice and on two different grounds: here by performance, and at the sign by observation."@en,
        "The word for what is cast off and taken up is the standing Hittite term for custom or usage, and it is the same word that stands in the sealing clause of the ritual of Maštigga, where the olden kings are to take account of the custom of the land. The transmission thus treats the man's condition as a usage rather than as a state of the body, and the exchange of the spindle and mirror for the bow and arrows is the usage being changed."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Paskuwatti ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual .

pmc:HittTentSite a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der unbebaute Ort am Fluss"@de,
        "The uncultivated place beside the river"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Tunnawiya, CTH 409, KUB 7.53+KUB 12.58 Vs. I 40-42 (Goetze–Sturtevant, AOS 14, 1938, p. 9)" ;
    skos:definition "The site at which the reed tent for the rite is erected: beside the river, where no cultivation is near and the plough does not come."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Tunnawiya ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual .

pmc:HolinBloodFromTheStubbedToeAtones a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Blut eines Stosses suehnt wie das Blut eines Brandopfers, unter drei Bedingungen"@de,
        "Blood from a stubbed toe atones like the blood of a burnt offering, under three conditions"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Holin 7b, R. Eleazar on the blood of a stubbing and Raba's three conditions, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. VIII (Leipzig: Otto Harrassowitz, 1909), p. 822" ;
    skos:definition "R. Eleazar says that the blood drawn by a stubbing atones in the same way as the blood of a burnt offering. Raba attaches three conditions to it: it holds of the thumb of the right hand, and of the second stubbing rather than the first, and only where the stubbing happens on the way to a pious deed."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because the harvest rule owes the qualified claims of an opened page as well as its capacities, and because this is the cleanest small instance on these pages of a stated effect narrowed by conditions nobody asked for. The three conditions are of three different kinds - a part of the body, an ordinal position in a series, and the purpose of the journey - and the last cannot be established by anybody other than the man himself."@en,
        "It is not a witness of pmc:TheObservationAnsweredByNarrowing. No observation is put against the claim; the conditions are supplied unprompted, as at pmc:PesahimFourActsEachWithAnExit, and the generalization rests only on the cases where the transmission raises the counter-observation itself."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliHolin ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmc:HolinTheBeastRefusesTheUntithed a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Esel, der die Gerste nicht frass, mit drei ausgeschlossenen Alternativen vor dem Schluss"@de,
        "The ass that would not eat the barley, with three alternatives excluded before the conclusion"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Holin 7a, the ass of R. Pinhas b. Jair at the inn, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. VIII (Leipzig: Otto Harrassowitz, 1909), p. 821" ;
    skos:definition "The criterion the same passage reports, and the one place in it where something is handled like an experiment. The ass of R. Pinhas b. Jair is given barley at an inn and will not eat it; it is sieved and the ass will not eat it; it is cleaned and the ass still will not eat it; he asks whether perhaps it has not been tithed, it is tithed, and the ass eats. The sentence drawn from it is that the poor beast is going to do the will of its maker and they are feeding it what is not tithed."@en ;
    skos:note "What the passage will not carry, and the pages before it say so plainly. The surrounding discussion asks four times over whether the earlier story of this man eating an untithed leaf can be explained some other way - that he forgot, that he had tithed from elsewhere, that he aimed at one part and ate another - and each is met by an argument rather than by a check. So the same discussion that excludes two alternatives mechanically for the ass excludes four for the man by inference. A reader taking the barley sequence as the transmission's method will overstate it."@en,
        "Why the sequence matters more than the claim. Two ordinary accounts of a refusing animal are dirt and chaff, and both are tried and removed before the third is put; the third is then confirmed by an intervention that changes only the one thing. The holding contains a great many claimed signs and very few that are approached in this order, and the order is in the text and not supplied by the reading."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliHolin ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmc:HolinThePiousAreGreaterAfterDeath a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Frommen sind nach ihrem Tod bedeutender als zu ihren Lebzeiten, und der Beweis ist ein Leichnam, der aufstand"@de,
        "The pious are greater after their death than in their life, and the proof is a corpse that stood up"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Holin 7b, R. Hanina on the pious after death, with 2 Kings 13:21 and the objection of R. Papa to Abajje, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. VIII (Leipzig: Otto Harrassowitz, 1909), p. 822" ;
    skos:definition "R. Hanina's saying, with the case the transmission attaches to it. The pious are greater after their death than in their life, because it is written that when they were about to bury a man they saw a raiding party and threw the man into Elisha's grave, and when he touched the bones he came to life again and stood on his feet. R. Papa puts to Abajje that this may have happened only so that Elijah's blessing of a double portion should be fulfilled in Elisha, that is for a reason other than the dead prophet's merit; the answer given is R. Johanan's, that Elisha had already healed Naaman of leprosy, which counts as the equivalent of death, so the double portion was discharged and the revival stands to his credit."@en ;
    skos:note "Harvested because it is a capacity and the harvest rule holds capacities to strict completeness. It is located in the remains of a dead man rather than in a living practitioner, which is unusual in this holding, and it requires no operation at all: the contact was accidental and the man who benefited was a corpse himself. Nothing is prescribed, nothing is prepared, and nobody intends anything."@en,
        "The argument around it is the same instrument as at pmc:HolinThreeTimesAgainstMosesOnce and it is worth noting that it appears twice within one page. A claim is grounded on a case; the objection is that the case may have had another sufficient cause; and the answer removes that cause by accounting for it elsewhere. Here the claim survives, where two pages earlier it was given up. The two together show the editorial voice testing its own grounds and reaching opposite results, which is the reason pmc:TheObservationAnsweredByNarrowing is careful to call the narrowing a habit and not a mechanism."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliHolin ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmc:HolinThreeTimesAgainstMosesOnce a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Zahl gegen die Zahl gestellt, und zurueckgenommen"@de,
        "The count put against the count, and withdrawn"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Holin 7a, R. Joseph on the three dividings and the objection to the count, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. VIII (Leipzig: Otto Harrassowitz, 1909), p. 821" ;
    skos:definition "What the transmission does with the three dividings, and it is an argument from a count. R. Joseph says that this man is so much greater than Moses with his sixty myriads, for there it happened once and here three times. The discussion objects that perhaps here too it happened only once, and the answer concedes: rather, this man is like Moses with his sixty myriads. A comparative claim grounded on a number is put, met with a doubt about the number, and given up."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because the holding has very little of this. Counting is the instrument the project itself reaches for, and transmissions that count their own successes are rare in it; here one counts, is asked whether the count is right, and drops the conclusion rather than defending it. The doubt raised is not that the river did not divide but that the three requests may have been one event with three beneficiaries, which is a question about how an occurrence is individuated. That is the same question a protocol has to settle before it can count anything."@en,
        "It sits oddly beside pmc:HolinWhiteMuleClaimNarrowedTwice on the pages either side of it. There a claim meets a counter-observation and is narrowed until nothing bears on it; here a claim meets a doubt about its arithmetic and is withdrawn. The same editorial voice does both within two pages, so neither can be read as the transmission's settled habit."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliHolin ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmc:HuitJoursConsecutifs a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Acht aufeinanderfolgende Tage, und was das Urteil dann entscheiden darf"@de,
        "Eight consecutive days, and what the judgment may then decide"@en ;
    dcterms:source "A. Teste, A Practical Manual of Animal Magnetism, trans. D. Spillan (London: H. Bailliere, 1843), chap. VI, Of the Number and the Hour of the Sittings, pp. 171-172" ;
    skos:definition "From a second work of the tradition. It rarely happens that the magnetic sleep is produced at the first sitting, and the first effects are often so little marked that they pass unnoticed, which is no reason to conclude the subject incapable of entering somnambulism. The operator is to begin again the following day, then the day after, then eight days consecutively, and only then will he be able to form a definitive judgment. Each sitting ought to last twenty minutes at least, and the experiments are to be made every day at the same hour."@en ;
    skos:note "It stands beside pmc:TroisOuQuatreSeances and pmc:PresqueUnMois as a third witness inside one tradition for the same structure, and it is the strongest of the three because it is the most explicit. Whether the three together can carry a generalization of their own is the open question named in the pull request that entered this file; they are three nodes from two works of one transmission, which is thin for the class, and the second witness a generalization would need is being sought in prima-materia#708."@en,
        "Only this section of the work was read. The volume carries a large catalogue of claimed capacities in its chapter on lucid somnambulism, with named witnesses per sitting, and none of it was opened here."@en,
        "The sharpest stopping rule the tradition produced, and it is sharper than the four in Deleuze because it names both a count and what the count settles. What it settles, however, is stated in the same sentence and is the whole finding: the judgment should involve only one single fact — the degree of the operator's magnetic power, or of his complete want of power relatively to such a person. Eight days of nothing therefore establish something about this operator with this subject, and nothing whatever about the agent."@en,
        "Two clauses in the same pages keep even that from being reached. The operator is warned on every occasion against allowing himself to be discouraged by one or two failures, and especially against betraying any sign of the discouragement, since that would deprive him of the confidence others might have in him. And the beginner is instructed, as emphatically as the author can put it, to attempt his experiments first only on persons who present favourable conditions for succeeding, or he will yield to discouragement and be stopped on his way. A rule that counts to eight, then reads the result as a fact about the operator, and is applied only to subjects selected in advance for their likelihood of success, cannot return a negative that means anything."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:HulaKnowledgeRetained a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Gesänge und die Schulung bleiben beim Schüler"@de,
        "The songs and the training stay with the pupil"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Nathaniel B. Emerson, Unwritten Literature of Hawaii: The Sacred Songs of the Hula, Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 38 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1909), chapter V, p. 34" ;
    skos:definition "What the school promises the pupil who keeps his vows: that memory will be strong and that the training, the knowledge and the songs will stay."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HawaiianRitual .

pmc:HuoBuXuanri a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Unheil dreht keinen Tag"@de,
        "The misfortune does not turn a day"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zangshu, outer section, in the recension of Wu Cheng, one juan, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text" ;
    skos:definition "The one interval the book states. Where the configuration is favourable and the shape unfavourable, one may still hope for a single day of fortune; where the configuration is unfavourable and the shape favourable, the misfortune does not turn a day. The commentary says that this is put so strongly in order to say how fast the answer comes, and elsewhere the book has a burial made at the hollow in the morning and wept for in the evening."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the most examinable sentence found in the transmission and the reason the corpus was opened from the testability side. A doctrine that placed its effects a generation away could never be caught; this one places one class of them within a day of the burial, and the burial is a public and dated event."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination .

pmc:HusbandHasSecondSight a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Dem Mann der Erzählerin wird das zweite Gesicht zugeschrieben"@de,
        "The narrator's husband is said to have the gift of second sight"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 29" ;
    skos:definition "The narrator states of her husband Padloq, whom she calls by a name she glosses as the one who keeps her alive, that he has the gift of second sight and always knows when anything remarkable is going to happen. The case she gives is a dream, on a journey from Iglulik to Tununeq, that a friend of his had been eaten by his nearest kin; on that journey they met the woman who had eaten her own family."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim is universal in form, which is what makes it examinable: he always knows. One confirming case is related and no failing case is; the case is retrospective and was told after the meeting it is said to have foretold. The protocol stands at examinations/inuit-second-sight.ttl."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:InuitSecondSight ;
    pm:ascribedTo "Padloq, the narrator Takornâq's husband" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InuitAngakkuq .

pmc:HuwuZhiming a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Den Namen des Dinges zu wissen nimmt ihm die Macht"@de,
        "Knowing the thing's name takes away its power"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 17 (Dengshe), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 17" ;
    skos:definition "A table by which a thing met in the mountains is identified from the branch of the day and the title it gives itself, so that it may be called by its true name. On a yin day one calling himself the tiger warden is a tiger and one calling himself lord of the road is a wolf; on a mao day one calling himself old man is a hare, one calling himself father of the eastern five is an elk, one calling himself queen mother of the west is a deer; on a chen day the rain master is a dragon, the earl of the river a fish, the gutless gentleman a crab; and so through the twelve branches to the chou day, on which one calling himself a student is an ox. The chapter closes the list with the rule: only know the thing's name, and it cannot do harm. The same operation is given for the mountain sprites, which are listed by shape rather than by day — one like a small child with a single foot that runs backwards, one like a drum and red with one foot, one nine feet tall in a fur robe and a hat, one like a dragon of five colours with red horns — with the same instruction, call them by the name and they dare do no harm."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim is that a name is an operator and not a label, and it is stated as a rule rather than as an anecdote. What makes it examinable in principle is that it is a rule of identification with a stated key: the day and the self-designation together determine the answer, so two practitioners given the same encounter should return the same name. What makes it unexaminable in practice is that the encounters the key applies to are the ones in dispute."@en,
        "The other half of the chapter answers the same encounters with objects rather than names: five talismans in cinnabar on peachwood at the door and the four quarters, a seal of jujube heartwood two inches square, the seal of the yellow spirit with its hundred and twenty characters, the mirror above, and realgar for the snakes. The naming rule is entered separately because it alone claims that knowledge does the work."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BaopuziNeipian ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:IdentificationOfTheWitch a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Benennung dessen, der gerade behext"@de,
        "Naming the one who is bewitching at this moment"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 4 and pp. 35-36" ;
    skos:definition "What the poison oracle is chiefly consulted for: to learn whether witchcraft threatens an undertaking or a person's health, and if so which neighbour is at this moment exercising it, so that he may be approached and asked to withdraw it. The question is never whether a man is a witch but whether he is now bewitching the questioner."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:IfaForeknowledge a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Vorauswissen über den Ausgang einer Sache"@de,
        "Foreknowledge of how a matter will fall out"@en ;
    dcterms:source "A. B. Ellis, The Yoruba-Speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1894), chapter II, the section on Ifa" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity the oracle is consulted for: to learn beforehand how a matter stands and will fall out. The answer is delivered through the channel of parables, and the account of eighteen ninety-four reports that nothing of importance is undertaken without the consultation."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ifa .

pmc:IfaSixteenMothers a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die sechzehn Figuren, Mütter genannt"@de,
        "The sixteen figures called mothers"@en ;
    dcterms:source "A. B. Ellis, The Yoruba-Speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1894), chapter II, the section on Ifa" ;
    skos:definition "The sixteen figures the casting can produce, named mothers in the account of eighteen ninety-four. A figure formed by taking one column from one mother and one column from another is named a child."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ifa .

pmc:ImperfectionsOfInsight a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Unvollkommenheiten der Einsicht"@de,
        "The imperfections of insight"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XX.105, Pali PTS [633] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part III, Pali Text Society, p. 771)",
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XX.105ff. (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "Ten states arising in practice, among them luminosity, rapture, tranquillity, pleasure and resolve, which are not defilements in themselves but become obstacles when taken as proof of attainment."@en ;
    skos:note "The handbook illustrates the error with the monk who held himself awakened for sixty years. This is the tradition's own answer to the question of how a practitioner mistakes a state for a result."@en,
        "The passage was read complete at the wording on 2026-09-29 and the citation above is completed accordingly with an edition and a locator; the note about the monk of sixty years was checked against it and stands. What the reading added is not entered here but beside it, in traditions/theravada-vism-samadhi.ttl: the entry condition at pmc:UpakkilesaSammaPatipannaNimittam, the class of practitioner and the three named elders at pmc:UpakkilesaSamathaVipassanaLabhi, the handbook's own qualification of the word imperfection and the thirtyfold multiplication at pmc:UpakkilesaTimsavidha, and the test by which the error was established at pmc:UpakkilesaHatthiParikkha. This node is left as it stood because it is cited from generalizations/what-the-cautions-share.ttl and from the protocol that refuted that claim, and a node named in a refutation is not rewritten under it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:Piti ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:InaudibleAndUnevenBreath a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der für den Übenden selbst unhörbare Atem, kürzer aus als ein"@de,
        "The breath inaudible to its own bearer and shorter out than in"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 8 (Shizhi), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 8" ;
    skos:definition "Two marks set on the practice: one is not to hear the sound of one's own breathing, and a feather held before nose and mouth is not to stir; and one is always to give out less than one has taken in."@en ;
    skos:note "Reopened and confirmed at the wording, with the test made exact. The chapter names the material and the place: a goose down is laid on the nose and the mouth, and that it does not stir while the qi is let out is the sign. Nothing is said about the intake. That is an outcome a second person can observe, which is rare enough in this graph to carry a protocol of its own."@en,
        "The intake is at the nose and the release at the mouth. The distribution is the reverse of the one a modern draft assigns, which treats mouth breathing as a fault on the intake and says nothing of the release. The passage also fixes the pace by an external test rather than by a count of seconds, which is what makes it examinable at all. See prima-materia#26."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BaopuziNeipian ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:IntentionReplacesMusculature a pm:Positing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Absicht ersetzt die Muskulatur, der Ortswechsel geschieht durch Wollen des Ortes"@de,
        "Intention replaces the musculature and the change of place happens by willing it"@en ;
    skos:definition "The claim that in the state, movement is accomplished by willing the destination, with no counterpart to the effort and the duration that bodily movement requires."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim carries an implication it does not state: if willing the place is what moves, the movement has no duration that depends on the distance. That implication is what makes it comparable to what the transmissions report."@en ;
    pm:positedIn "prima-materia#305" .

pmc:InuitForkSignOfSurvival a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das an der Gabel gelesene Zeichen"@de,
        "The sign read from the fork"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 24" ;
    skos:definition "The stated reading of the divination: if the fork falls the sick person will die, if it stays upright she will live. In the case related the fork settled upright and the sick child lived."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because the sign is stated in advance and in both directions, which is rare in the material this graph holds. A divination that names its own failure condition before the outcome is known is examinable; one that is interpreted afterwards is not. The single case related is of course selected by the narrator's survival."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InuitAngakkuq .

pmc:InuitRecoveryOfLostThings a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Wiederfinden verlorener Dinge"@de,
        "The recovery of lost things"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 28" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity to bring back an object that has been lost, named in the narrative for iron and steel knives, which were rare at the time and often lost."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InuitAngakkuq .

pmc:InuitSewingProhibitionOnOqshoriaq a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Verbot, auf Oqshoriaq neues Karibufell zu nähen"@de,
        "The prohibition on sewing new caribou skin on Oqshoriaq"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), pp. 28-29" ;
    skos:definition "A prohibition attached to a named place: garments of new caribou skin may not be sewn on the island of Oqshoriaq."@en ;
    skos:note "The prohibition is local and specific, which distinguishes it from the general taboo system the later chapters of the volume set out. Whether it is a case of that system or a rule of its own cannot be decided from the portion read."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InuitAngakkuq .

pmc:IuratusFidesCatholica a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der katholische Glaube als Bedingung der Schau"@de,
        "The catholic faith as the condition of the vision"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber iuratus Honorii, cap. XIII, oratio 7, the Athanasian creed (Royal MS 17 A. xlii; the inserted clauses noted there from Hedegard's Latin), ed. Peterson" ;
    skos:definition "The condition set in the form of the Athanasian creed and with its clause carried over: whoever wishes to be saved and to have the divine vision must before all things hold the catholic faith, which unless a man believe faithfully and firmly he shall perish and shall not have the divine vision."@en ;
    skos:note "The witness is the oration the book numbers as its seventh, which takes the creed and inserts the vision into its promise and into its threat. The insertion is the claim: the book makes its own operative outcome part of what is lost by unbelief."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberIuratusHonorii ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:IuratusPlagaKautel a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Wer es anders tut, wird offen von Gott geschlagen"@de,
        "He that doth otherwise shall be openly plagued of God"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber iuratus Honorii, cap. L (Royal MS 17 A. xlii, fol. 45v), ed. Peterson" ;
    skos:definition "The warning attached to the orations of the first purification: he that will say those prayers or such like must be chaste and clean and must say them with great devotion, and he that doth otherwise shall be openly plagued of God; for in those Greek, Hebrew and Chaldaic prayers there are the most sacred names of God and of his angels, which ought not to be spoken by man but only through the mercy of God."@en ;
    skos:note "Openly plagued, not merely unrewarded. The warning puts a visible outcome on the wrong performance of the words, which is the second clause of this work with an observable consequence and the reason the genre comes out of prima-materia#640 better than its first witness did."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:IuratusMundacioPrima ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberIuratusHonorii ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:IuratusSequestratio a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Absonderung von den weltlichen Dingen, und die Armen vor den Reichen"@de,
        "Sequestration from worldly things, and the poor before the rich"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber iuratus Honorii, the conditions of the divine vision (Sloane MS 3854, fol. 115vb), section 13 of Boudet's critical edition, at p. 879: Ex hoc patet quod pauperes citius et verius operantur quam divites; and cap. CI in the English of Royal MS 17 A. xlii, fol. 62r, ed. Peterson" ;
    skos:definition "The condition on the operator's life. He is to be truly penitent and confessed, sequestered from women and from their sight, separated from evil and from sick men, his life led cleanly, his garments not foul but new or most cleanly washed, and never idle. No man troubled with worldly things can do this thing, for the soul is by earthly blindness sequestered from all godly secrets; and if he who works for a part of this science must be sequestered from worldly things, how much more he who works for the whole."@en ;
    skos:note "The first inference is the one this project's criterion is about. A transmission that makes poverty an advantage in its own operation is removing the gate its own apparatus would otherwise set up, and it does so in the chapter that fixes the conditions rather than in a preface."@en,
        "Two inferences the book draws from this and neither is incidental. The first: Solomon meant by new garments the virtues, since God and the blessed angels do not care about worldly things, from which it is plain that the poor operate sooner and more truly than the rich. The second runs the other way for the angelic operation that follows, because those angels dwell with men and are clean and therefore want clean clothes. The book states both and does not reconcile them; held apart here rather than joined."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberIuratusHonorii ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:IuratusSolusChristianus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Nur der Christ wirkt wahrhaft"@de,
        "Only the Christian operates truly"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber iuratus Honorii, prologue and first book (Sloane MS 3854, fols. 114ra-114rb), sections 8 and 9 of Boudet's critical edition, at pp. 875-876: Solus igitur christianus potest in hac visione et in omnibus aliis veraciter operari" ;
    skos:definition "The book's division of operators into three kinds and its verdict on two of them. Pagans sacrifice to unclean aerial and earthly spirits and do not constrain them: the spirits only feign to be constrained by the words of the pagans' law, so that they keep faith with idols, and because their faith is bad their works are null. Jews cannot operate in this vision at all, having lost the gift at the coming of Christ, and their invocations do not come to effect unless they put their faith in Christ; the spirits are compelled to come by the holy names but, because the Jew is not signed with the sign of the cross and of the faith, they will not answer him truly. Therefore only the Christian can operate truly in this vision and in all the others."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also the clause that makes the whole first book unexaminable from outside the faith it requires. Every failure by a non-Christian operator is predicted and accounted for in advance, so no such failure can tell against the procedure. Entered as the claim of the source, with no judgement on it here."@en,
        "The argument in the middle of this passage is the part worth keeping, because it reasons from an outcome. The book quotes a prophecy that the anointing of the Jews would cease when the king of kings came, and infers that it would never have ceased if the art had had true efficacy through them. That is a transmission arguing from a historical observable against a rival's efficacy, and it is the only such argument found in either witness."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberIuratusHonorii ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:JainaAbhiyogikabhavana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Zustand dessen, der Zauber um der Macht willen wirkt"@de,
        "The disposition of the one who works spells for power"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXXVI.263 and XXXVI.265 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:definition "Two of the dispositions the thirty-sixth lecture lists among those that fix a lower birth. Those who practise spells and besmear their body with ashes for the sake of pleasure, amusement, or power, realise the Abhiyogika disposition. And he who is continuously angry, and who puts his faith in prognostics, realises the Asuratva disposition."@en ;
    skos:note "The condition is again the motive and it is named three ways: pleasure, amusement, power. The second sentence puts faith in prognostics beside continual anger, which is a judgement on the client of the art and not only on its practitioner — the one place in what was read where consulting rather than performing is what is charged."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaBrahmacaryabhangaPhala a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die sieben Dinge, die dem Bruch folgen sollen"@de,
        "The seven things said to follow the breach"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XVI.1, with the same formula at XVI.2-10 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:definition "The consequence the lecture attaches to each of the ten, in one formula repeated at every item: then, though he be chaste, there may arise a doubt with regard to his chastity, or a sensual desire, or a feeling of remorse, or he will break the rules, or he will become a slave to passion, or he will acquire a dangerous illness of long duration, or he will desert the faith which the Kevalin has proclaimed."@en ;
    skos:note "The formula is introduced by the sentence that the preceptor has explained the reason for this, and then gives the consequence in place of a reason. The transmission therefore treats the consequence as the reason, which is what makes the clause usable: it is not a threat appended to a rule but the ground the rule is said to rest on."@en,
        "This is the only stated consequence of an omission anywhere in the four works read, and it is the reason the prerequisite below carries pm:statedNecessity while every other prerequisite in this file carries pm:prescribedOrder or less. Six of the seven outcomes are states of the man and unobservable from outside; the sixth is not. A dangerous illness of long duration is an event a bystander could record, and it is the only item in the list of which that is true."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaDashaPresupposesCelibacy a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die zehn gehen der Keuschheit voraus, deren Bedingungen sie sind"@de,
        "The ten come before the chastity they are conditions of"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XVI.1-10 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:note "Stated necessity, and the only one in this file. What earns it is that the consequence is quotable and attached to each item singly rather than to the set, so that the omission of one condition and not only the abandonment of the whole discipline has a named cost."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Though he be chaste, there may arise a doubt with regard to his chastity, or a sensual desire, or a feeling of remorse, or he will break the rules, or he will become a slave to passion, or he will acquire a dangerous illness of long duration, or he will desert the faith which the Kevalin has proclaimed. The lecture states this consequence separately at each of the ten conditions and states it in place of a reason."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:JainaBrahmacaryasamadhiPhala ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:JainaDashaBrahmacaryasamadhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaDevamaya a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die trügerische Macht der Götter"@de,
        "The delusive power of the gods"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ācārāṅga Sūtra I.7.8.24 (Jacobi, SBE 22, Oxford 1884; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 1964)" ;
    skos:definition "What the twenty-fourth verse of the eighth lesson tells the dying man not to trust: he should be enlightened with eternal objects and not trust in the delusive power of the gods."@en ;
    skos:note "A warning about an appearance during the practice, and the only one in what was read. It says that something may be seen or offered in the course of the fast that is not what it presents itself as, and it gives no mark by which the practitioner should tell the one from the other. Held as the counterpart of the search for a sign of success: this transmission names no perceptible sign of the practice working and does name a perceptible appearance that must not be believed."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaJalasiddhi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Vollendung durch Salz, durch Wasser, durch Feuer"@de,
        "Perfection by salt, by water, by fire"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Sūtrakṛtāṅga Sūtra I.7.12-13 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:definition "The three claims the seventh lecture of the first book reports of others before attacking them: some say that perfection is reached by abstaining from the seasoner of food, others by the use of cold water, that is by ablutions, and others again by tending a fire. The passage adds that those who hold the second touch water in the morning and evening, and that those who hold the third light a fire in the morning and evening."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as its opponent reports it, and the citation says so. No transmission of these practices has been run down in this holding; what stands is a Jain report of what others were doing, quoted closely enough that the argument against it can be checked against it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaJanmashatanivarana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Viele Hunderte von Geburten verhindert"@de,
        "Many hundreds of births prevented"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX.40 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:definition "The result the fortieth item states in one clause and without qualification: by renouncing all food he prevents his being born again many hundreds of times."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaJivitamaranaAkanksha a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Verlangen nach Leben und der Wunsch nach Tod"@de,
        "The longing for life and the wish for death"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ācārāṅga Sūtra I.7.8.4-5 (Jacobi, SBE 22, Oxford 1884; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 1964)" ;
    skos:definition "The two states the eighth lesson excludes in one line: he should not long for life, nor wish for death; he should yearn after neither, life or death. The next verse says of the man who is indifferent and wishes for the destruction of Karman that he should continue his contemplation."@en ;
    skos:note "The condition on the act is a state of the man and not an act of his, and there is nothing in the passage by which a bystander could tell whether it holds. That is the same shape this holding carries from the Chinese medical canon, where a failure is located in the patient without a mark that was recognisable beforehand, and it is worth noting that here the transmission does it to the practitioner rather than to the person practised on."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaKarmasthitiHrasa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Dauer des Karman verkürzt und seine Kraft gemildert"@de,
        "The duration of the karman shortened and its power mitigated"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX.22, with the eight kinds of Karman at XXXIII.2-3 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:definition "The result the twenty-second item states: the soul loosens the firm hold which seven of the eight kinds of Karman have upon it, all but the one that determines the length of life; it shortens their duration where it was to be a long one and mitigates their power where it was intense; it may either acquire the life-determining Karman or not, but it accumulates no more of the kind that produces unpleasant feelings."@en ;
    skos:note "The exception is what makes the claim worth holding. Seven kinds are said to be shortened and the eighth, the one fixing the span of the present life, is named and excluded. A transmission that exempts exactly the quantity an outsider could measure has drawn the line where a test would have to begin, and it has drawn it explicitly rather than by silence."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaLingamNaTrayate a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die äußeren Zeichen retten den sündigen Asketen nicht"@de,
        "The outward tokens will not save a sinful ascetic"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Sūtrakṛtāṅga Sūtra I.2.1.9 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)",
        "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra V.21-22 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)",
        "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XX.41-43 and XX.49 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:definition "The statement four passages of three works make in the same terms. Bark and skin of a goat, nakedness, twisted hair, baldness: these outward tokens will not save a sinful ascetic, and a sinner though he be a mendicant will not escape hell. He who walks about naked and lean, he who eats only once after a month, if he is filled with deceit, will be born an endless number of times. One who for a long time wears a shaven crown and mortifies himself, but who is careless with regard to the vows and neglects penance and self-control, will not be a winner in the battle; he is empty like a clenched fist and like an uncoined false coin. In vain he adopts nakedness, who errs about matters of paramount interest."@en ;
    skos:note "One of the three sources is text-critically doubtful and the doubt is recorded rather than passed over. The editor of the edition read holds verses 38 to 53 of the twentieth lecture to be apparently a later addition, on two grounds he states: their subject is not connected with what precedes, and they are composed in a different metre. The verses drawn on here, 41 to 43 and 49, fall inside that block. The statement does not rest on them — the fifth lecture and the Sutrakritanga carry it without them — but a reader who accepts the editor's judgement should read this node as resting on two witnesses and not on three."@en,
        "The consequence for examination is that this transmission cannot be embarrassed by a case. Any practitioner who fails is, on these verses, a practitioner who was filled with deceit, and the verses supply no way of having known that beforehand. The same clause protects the promise and empties it."@en,
        "This is the finding the file was worth opening for, and it is repeated too often to be an aside. The transmission prescribes nakedness, the plucked or shaven head and the month-long fast, and then states in four places that a man may have all of them and be nowhere. What decides is named as deceit, carelessness about the vows and error about what matters, and none of the three has an outward mark anywhere in what was read."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaPanchahrasvaksharakala a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Zeit, die das Aussprechen von fünf kurzen Silben braucht"@de,
        "The time required for pronouncing five short syllables"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX.72 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:definition "The measure the seventy-second item gives for the final meditation: when his life is spent up to less than half a muhūrta he discontinues to act, and during the time required for pronouncing five short syllables he is engaged in the final pure meditation, in which all functions have ceased and the four remaining kinds of Karman are annihilated simultaneously."@en ;
    skos:note "Two measures in one sentence, one of them stated in a unit of the calendar and one in an act of speech, and both are shorter than anything else the lecture measures. A transmission that fixes the length of its own last operation by how long it takes to say five syllables has made a statement of a kind nothing else in this file makes, and it has made it about the one moment no witness is present for."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaPapashramana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der schlechte Asket, in zwanzig Zügen gezeichnet"@de,
        "The bad ascetic, drawn in twenty marks"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XVII.1-20 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:altLabel "pāpaśramaṇa"@en ;
    skos:definition "The portrait the seventeenth lecture draws of the man who has entered the order, learned the Law, received religious discipline and obtained the benefit of Bodhi, and afterwards begins to live as he likes. He sleeps, eats and drinks as much as he likes and lives comfortably; he despises the learning and discipline his teachers taught him; he hurts living beings, seeds and sprouts and does not control himself though he believes himself well-controlled; he uses bed, plank, chair, seat or duster without having wiped them; he walks in haste and without care; he inspects things carelessly; he is deceitful, talkative, arrogant and greedy; he perverts the truth and delights in quarrels; he sits down on a shaking seat wherever he lists; he sleeps with dusty feet; he eats milk, curds and what is made from milk and does not practise austerities; he eats after sunset and answers admonition with anger; he leaves his own teacher and follows heretical ones, changing his school continuously; he busies himself in another's house and lives by fortune-telling; he eats the food of his relations and reposes on the seat of the householder."@en ;
    skos:note "Every one of the twenty marks is observable by somebody standing next to the man, which is exactly what the marks of the sinful ascetic at pmc:JainaLingamNaTrayate are not. The transmission therefore has a fully external portrait of the bad monk and a purely internal account of the monk whose practice fails, and it does not connect them."@en,
        "The nineteenth of the twenty marks is the one this holding should keep: living by fortune-telling. The transmission puts the practice of a divinatory art into its portrait of the failed ascetic, alongside sleeping too long and answering back, and the same practice appears in the prohibitions of the fifteenth lecture and in the catalogue of the Sūtrakṛtāṅga. Three different literary forms in three places, one rule."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaPapavidya a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Katalog der bösen Wissenschaften"@de,
        "The catalogue of evil sciences"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Sūtrakṛtāṅga Sūtra II.2.25-27 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:definition "The longest list of operative arts in either volume, given in the second book of the Sūtrakṛtāṅga under the heading of men's success by occult sciences. Divination from terrestrial accidents, from strange phenomena, from dreams, from phenomena in the air, from changes in the body, from sounds, from mystical signs, from seeds; the interpretation of the marks of women, men, elephants, cows, partridges, cocks, ducks, quails, of wheels, parasols, shields, sticks, swords, precious stones and jewels; the art to make one happy or miserable, to make a woman pregnant, to deprive one of his wits; incantations; conjuring; oblations of substances; the martial arts; the courses of the moon, the sun, Venus and Jupiter; the falling of meteors; great conflagration; divination from wild animals, from the flight of crows, from showers of dust and from rain of blood; the Vaitālī and Ardhavaitālī arts; the art of casting people asleep and of opening doors; the arts of Caṇḍālas, Śabaras, Draviḍas, Kaliṅgas, Gauḍas and Gandhāras; and the spells for making somebody fall down, rise or yawn, for making him immovable or cling to something, for making him sick or sound, and for making him go forth, disappear or come."@en ;
    skos:note "Fifty-odd named capacities in three sentences, and the whole of it stands inside a prohibition. This is the shape in which this transmission carries the material the holding is looking for: not as a promise with a procedure but as an enumeration with a penalty. A later reader should not take the list for a Jain claim about what can be done; it is a Jain claim about what was being done."@en,
        "Several of the arts named here are in the holding already from other transmissions, under their own names and with their own procedures. The overlap is at the level of the list only, and no convergence is asserted from it: the passage names the arts without describing any of them, so there is nothing here to match against a procedure."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaTirthakaranamagotra a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Karman, das Namen und Geschlecht eines Tīrthakara bewirkt"@de,
        "The karman that brings about the name and family of a Tīrthakara"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX.43 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:definition "The result the forty-third item states, and the only item of the seventy-three whose result is a particular station rather than a removal: by doing service the soul acquires the Karman which brings about for him the name and the family of a Tīrthakara."@en ;
    skos:note "Worth keeping beside the rest because it runs the other way. The lecture otherwise states results as subtractions — Karman destroyed, obstacles removed, births not taken. Here a Karman is acquired on purpose, and the station it produces is the one the transmission reserves for the founders of its own order."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaTrisalyaMukti a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Freiheit von den drei Dornen"@de,
        "Freedom from the three thorns"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX.5 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:definition "The result the fifth item states: the soul gets rid, as it were of thorns, of deceit, of misapplied austerities and of wrong belief, which obstruct the way to final liberation and cause an endless migration of the soul; and it obtains simplicity, so that the soul free from deceit acquires no Karman resulting in carnal desire and annihilates such as it had acquired before."@en ;
    skos:note "Misapplied austerity stands in the list of what confession removes, and the translator names the term behind it, the austerity undertaken for a reward. A transmission that counts a wrongly motivated austerity among the obstacles alongside deceit and wrong belief has said that its own principal practice can be performed in a way that adds to what it is meant to remove."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaTrividhamaranakrama a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Drei Sterbeweisen in aufsteigender Schwierigkeit"@de,
        "Three deaths in an ascending order of difficulty"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ācārāṅga Sūtra I.7.8.7-25, with the comparatives at I.7.8.11, 19 and 20 (Jacobi, SBE 22, Oxford 1884; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 1964)" ;
    skos:definition "The grading the eighth lesson states in its own words as it passes from one method to the next. The first is the fast without food in which the man may bear the pains where he lies. Of the second the text says that a more exalted method follows for a well-controlled and instructed monk, in which he gives up all motions except his own and may walk, stand or sit as he tires. Of the third it says that this is a still more difficult method, not to stir from one's place while checking all motions of the body, and that this is the highest law, exalted above the preceding method."@en ;
    skos:note "The order is the text's and not a reader's: each step is introduced by a comparative. A transmission that ranks its own terminal procedures by difficulty has stated something that can be held against its own reports of who performed which, which is what makes the grading worth a node of its own."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaUbhayamaranavacana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sechs Monate gegen sieben Tage"@de,
        "Six months against seven days"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Viyāhapannatti (Bhagavaī) XV, Calcutta edition pp. 1250a-1250b" ;
    skos:definition "The exchange the book puts immediately after the rebound. Gosāla, believing he has struck home, tells Mahāvīra that he will now die of bilious fever within six months. Mahāvīra replies that so far from dying within six months he will live sixteen years longer as a Jina, and that Gosāla, having been hit by his own power, will perish of bilious fever within seven days."@en ;
    skos:note "It is a claim narrated by the winning side about the loser, in a book that the scholarship holds to be redacted centuries after the events and to be hostile to the sect whose founder it describes. The dating is decidable in form and unchecked in fact, and the node claims only the first."@en,
        "The book then supplies what the form needs and what such passages almost never have: a public. The rumour of the dispute is said to spread through the town and there is much discussion among the people as to whose threat would prove true, with the better sort maintaining that Mahāvīra spoke the truth. The transmission therefore records that the question was treated as open at the time, by people who would see the answer, and it records which way the report has it settled."@en,
        "Three dated statements in four sentences, each of them decidable by waiting, and two of them mutually exclusive on the same illness. This holding carries few claims of that shape from any transmission, and it carries none in which the two sides are uttered in each other's presence."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "A. F. Rudolf Hoernle, The Uvāsagadasāo, vol. II, Appendix I (Bibliotheca Indica, Calcutta 1888), pp. 6-7" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JeuAthanasiaDiscipulorum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sie wurden unsterblich und folgten ihm in jeden Ort"@de,
        "They became immortal and followed him into every place"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Das zweite Buch Jeû, Handschriftenseiten 68-69, deutsche Übersetzung in Carl Schmidt, Gnostische Schriften in koptischer Sprache aus dem Codex Brucianus, Texte und Untersuchungen VIII 1-2, Leipzig 1892, S. 205-206" ;
    skos:definition "The outcome the text reports for the fourth rite: in the same moment in which he had sealed them with that seal, the archons took their whole wickedness away from the disciples; and when the wickedness of the archons had left them, the disciples became immortal, following him into all the places they wished to go."@en ;
    skos:note "The one capacity in this corpus that is reported as having been conferred rather than promised, and it is reported in a single sentence with no sign attached to it. It is also the only one of the four rites whose effect the text describes as something the disciples act on themselves: they go where they wish."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu .

pmc:JeuCustodesTacent a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Wächter reden nicht mit euch, sie übergeben"@de,
        "The watchers do not speak with you, they hand over"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Das zweite Buch Jeû, Handschriftenseite 70, deutsche Übersetzung in Carl Schmidt, Gnostische Schriften in koptischer Sprache aus dem Codex Brucianus, Texte und Untersuchungen VIII 1-2, Leipzig 1892, S. 207-208" ;
    skos:definition "What the text says happens at the gates of the light-treasure and at every rank beyond them. The watchers of the gates see the mystery of the forgiveness of sins that the arrivals have performed, and its apologies and all its commandments, and they see the seal on their forehead and the cipher in their hands; then the nine watchers open the gates and they go in; the watchers do not speak with them, but give them their seals and their mystery; and at each further rank the rank gives its seal, its mystery and the great name, and they pass into its inner."@en ;
    skos:note "Two things are inspected and neither is spoken. What is read off is a seal on the forehead and a number in the hands, both visible, and what the rank then does is hand over the tokens of the next stage. The passage contains no question, no answer and no test, which sets this ascent against the Hekhalot material of this holding, where a wrong answer at the sixth palace destroys the one who gives it. The division is stated at pmc:ThePassageThatPutsNoQuestion."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu .

pmc:JeuLiberatioAbArchonte a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Vor dem Archon dieses Äons gerettet werden"@de,
        "Being saved from the ruler of this aeon"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The First Book of Jeu, chapter 2 (trans. V. MacDermot), in the excerpt published by the Gnostic Society Library" ;
    skos:definition "The outcome claimed: that the hearer is saved from the ruler of this aeon and his persecutions, to which there is no end; that the ruler will not fight with him, having found no commandment of his own in the speaker; and that the hearer is made free and becomes whole through a freedom in which there is no blemish."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu .

pmc:JeuRemissioOmniumPeccatorum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Jede Sünde von Kindheit an ausgetilgt, und das Reich schon auf der Erde ererbt"@de,
        "Every sin from childhood blotted out, and the kingdom inherited while on earth"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Das zweite Buch Jeû, Handschriftenseite 58, deutsche Übersetzung in Carl Schmidt, Gnostische Schriften in koptischer Sprache aus dem Codex Brucianus, Texte und Untersuchungen VIII 1-2, Leipzig 1892, S. 197" ;
    skos:definition "What the one performance is said to effect: all the sins a man has committed knowingly and unknowingly since his childhood until the present day, and all he has committed since the foundation of the world until the present day, are blotted out; he is made a pure light and taken up into the light of the lights. Those who have received it have inherited the kingdom of God already, since they are on the earth; they have a portion in the light-treasure; and they are immortal gods. When they come out of the body all the aeons scatter one after another and flee to the west to the left, until the souls reach the gates of the light-treasure and the watchers of the gates open to them."@en ;
    skos:note "The clause that makes this a blanket and not a remission is the double reach of it: the sins of the man's own life and the sins committed since the foundation of the world, in one sentence. Nothing is exempted and no fault of any earlier rite is excluded, which is the reason the same text can make this the condition of everything else."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu .

pmc:JeuRemissioPraesupposita a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Eingang in den Lichtschatz setzt das Mysterium der Sündenvergebung voraus"@de,
        "Entering the light-treasure presupposes the mystery of the forgiveness of sins"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Das zweite Buch Jeû, Handschriftenseiten 58, 69 und 86, deutsche Übersetzung in Carl Schmidt, Gnostische Schriften in koptischer Sprache aus dem Codex Brucianus, Texte und Untersuchungen VIII 1-2, Leipzig 1892, S. 197, 206 und 223" ;
    skos:definition "The prerequisite the text states three times and in the strongest form it uses anywhere: whoever performs the mysteries of the five trees, the seven voices and the great name needs no further mystery of the kingdom of light except the mystery of the forgiveness of sins; when the ascending disciples reach the six great aeons they will be held fast until they receive it; and at the place of the first archon of the light they cannot enter the inner until they have received it."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also the place where this corpus answers a question its own apparatus raises. The disciples have by then been given the seal, the name and the cipher of every station up to the fourteenth aeon, and the text says in so many words that this is not enough. What is missing is not a further token of the same kind but the one operation for which no token is given at all. That is why this node is a witness at generalizations/the-text-and-what-is-withheld.ttl."@en,
        "The distribution of this clause is what makes it a necessity and not an order. It stands once before the rites are performed, once in the account of the soul leaving the body, and once at the last station the text reaches, and in the third place it is put as a reassurance: do not be afraid that you cannot enter, because they will only hold you. A transmission that repeats a condition at three removes and softens its consequence at the third is stating a requirement and not a sequence."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Being held fast short of the goal, and nothing worse. The six great aeons will hold them until they receive it; the inhabitants of the place of the first archon of the light will seize them there, because those inhabitants have not received it either, so that the arrivals must perform with them the mysteries they have received; and the text adds that there is no place of chastisement in those places and that they cannot be chastised there. The stated consequence of lacking the withheld mystery is therefore detention without punishment, at a station short of the treasury."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:JeuTransitusOmniumThesaurorum ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:JeuMysteriumRemissionis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu .

pmc:JeuThesaurusTransitusForma a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Form eines Schatzkammerdurchgangs"@de,
        "The form of one treasury passage"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Das erste Buch Jeû, Handschriftenseiten 39 und 43, deutsche Übersetzung in Carl Schmidt, Gnostische Schriften in koptischer Sprache aus dem Codex Brucianus, Texte und Untersuchungen VIII 1-2, Leipzig 1892, S. 179 und 183" ;
    skos:definition "The unit the account repeats for each treasury it reaches: when you come to this place, seal yourself with this seal, whose name is given and drawn; while this cipher is in your hand, say this name, once at one station and three times at another; whereupon the watchers, the ranks and the curtains scatter, until you come to the place of their father, and he gives you his own name and his own seal, and you cross to the gate inside his treasury; and the watchers there see the seal of their father and scatter, because they have recognised it."@en ;
    skos:note "The seal is not the passer's own. He is sealed with the seal of the station, and what opens the next gate is the seal the father of the station has just given him, which the watchers there recognise as their father's. The apparatus is therefore not transferable to somebody who begins in the middle: each token is handed over inside the passage, and the text says of the watchers that they scatter because they knew it."@en,
        "The two ciphers of these two stations are printed as Coptic-Greek letter numerals and Schmidt leaves them in that form, unlike the ciphers of the second book, which he gives as arabic numerals and several of which he marks with a question mark of his own."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu .

pmc:JibaroArutamaSeen a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Alten gesehen zu haben, und was es verleihen soll"@de,
        "Having seen the Old Ones, and what it is said to confer"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Rafael Karsten, Blood Revenge, War, and Victory Feasts among the Jibaro Indians of Eastern Ecuador, Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 79 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1923), pp. 2-3 and 24" ;
    skos:definition "The arutama, the Old Ones, are the souls of the ancestors and appear to the drinker in the dream as jaguars, eagles, giant snakes and other wild animals, or in the lightning, the rainbow and meteors. They speak to the young man and teach him in all manly business and first of all in warlike deeds. Only the youth who has seen the arutama in the dream and has been spoken to by them can expect to become a valiant and successful warrior, to kill many enemies, and himself to secure long life. The capacity is stated with an outward mark: when he afterwards meets a jaguar, an eagle or a boa in real life he shows no fright and does not flee but challenges it with his lance, and in the fight he meets his enemy without fear."@en ;
    skos:note "A chant in the language is printed for it, Arutama weinikama, given as I have seen the Old Ones."@en,
        "Entered as a capacity and not as a sign. The holding keeps signs of success apart because a sign announced in advance is reported by the person who wants it; this one is announced in advance too, but what is announced is a behaviour in company and not an inner event, and it is the claim rather than its confirmation."@en,
        "The claim has two halves and they are not equally checkable, which is why both are in the definition. Long life and success in war are settled by a career; the absence of fright before a jaguar is settled by one meeting and in front of witnesses. The transmission supplied the second itself and it is the nearer thing to a criterion."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:KarstenBloodRevenge ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JibaroShuara .

pmc:JibaroTsantsaPlenty a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Mehrung der Schweine und der Felder"@de,
        "The increase of the swine and the fields"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Rafael Karsten, Blood Revenge, War, and Victory Feasts among the Jibaro Indians of Eastern Ecuador, Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 79 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1923), pp. 46-47" ;
    skos:definition "What the head trophy is said to bring: that the domestic animals and the fields grow and develop not in a normal way but with an extraordinary force. The soul of the killed enemy, the wakáni, turned by the conjuration into the slayer's obedient slave, instructs him in his dreams how the swine and the chickens are to be fed and tended so that they grow quickly, get fat and increase, and how the plantations are to be cleaned and irrigated so that they bear abundantly; and the slayer, with the trophy round his neck, passes those instructions on to the women, whose work this normally is."@en ;
    skos:note "And the account states both outcomes. If everything has turned out well, the swine full grown and fat and the fields abundant, the warrior begins the preparations for the proper feast; if he has been negligent in the fasting, the swine die — the wakáni biting them as a poisonous snake, or killing them otherwise — and the plantations dry away."@en,
        "The outcome named is fat pigs and a full manioc field, which is as public an outcome as this holding carries anywhere, and the term is given by the thing itself: the breeding of swine to full growth takes about two years, which is why the great feast waits that long. A claim with a two-year term and a harvest at the end of it is in principle answerable by anyone who lives in the house."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:KarstenBloodRevenge ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JibaroShuara .

pmc:JindanYieldsCapacities a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Goldelixier bringt jene Vermögen hervor"@de,
        "The golden elixir yields those capacities"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 4 (Jindan), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 4" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Jindan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Waidan ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JindanCapacities .

pmc:Jingshen a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Essenzgeist und Absicht des Kranken"@de,
        "The essence-spirit and the intent of the sick person"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 8 (Ben shen), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 2" ;
    skos:definition "The condition of the patient on which the corpus makes the whole operation depend: the essence and the spirit, and with them the intent, held to be present or absent and to be won or lost. The Lingshu instructs the needler to observe the bearing of the sick person in order to know whether essence, spirit, hun and po are present or gone; where these five are damaged, it says, the needle cannot treat him."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmc:JourneyYieldsDistantSight a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Reise bringt das Wissen um den fernen Ort hervor"@de,
        "The journey yields the knowledge of the distant place"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 55-57" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:NoaidiJourney ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SamiNoaidi ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:DistantSight .

pmc:KalikapuranaLeftAndRightMethods a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die rechts- und linkshändige Methode, wie das Purāṇa sie angibt"@de,
        "The right and left hand methods as the purāṇa states them"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Kālikāpurāṇa, at the announcement of the orthodox and heterodox (right and left hand) methods following the yonimudrā verses (text, introduction and translation in English by B. N. Shastri, part III, Nag Publishers, Delhi 1991)",
        "Kālikāpurāṇa, at the closing statement of the Tripurā-kavaca chapter that the left hand and right hand methods of worshipping the goddess have now been stated (text, introduction and translation in English by B. N. Shastri, part III, Nag Publishers, Delhi 1991)",
        "Kālikāpurāṇa, at the verses on the Bhairava image (204-205a) and on the left images of Sūrya and Gaṇeśa (212-218) (text, introduction and translation in English by B. N. Shastri, part III, Nag Publishers, Delhi 1991)" ;
    skos:definition "A pair of methods of worship announced by the text as the orthodox and the heterodox, and closed by it as the right and left hand methods of worshipping the goddess. The text defines both terms itself. Worship performed strictly by the prescribed rules, with the five sacrifices that repay the debts to sages, gods, ancestors, men and the elements, is called the state of dakṣiṇa, and it is so called because the worshipper becomes humble towards gods and ancestors and the goddess is favourably disposed towards him. Vāmā is the goddess who, when worshipped, takes the share of the sacrifice for herself before all other gods, and one who worships in that method should himself be a vāma, whether or not he performs the five sacrifices. Which method applies is often decided by the deity rather than by the worshipper: Bhairava, who says he assumed his form for the sake of meat, wine and intercourse at will, is to be worshipped in the left hand method always; the left images of Sūrya and Gaṇeśa likewise; and the text keeps the five great brahmanical sacrifices standing alongside."@en ;
    skos:note "Chapter numbering is not recoverable from the copy read, which is an OCR of a printed translation whose running heads the scan does not carry reliably. Work, translator, part and verse numbers are named, and the passages sit in the chapters on the worship of Tripurā; a later reader working from another edition should expect a different chapter count. Same limitation as at pmc:AcaraLadder. See prima-materia#45."@en,
        "It stands beside two other divisions already held here and agrees with neither. pmc:AcaraLadder gives seven ascending steps with the left above the right; pmc:BhavaClassification gives three dispositions that decide what a person is competent for. This purāṇa gives two methods assigned by the object of worship. Three texts, three different shapes, and the only element common to all three is that none of them is the pair as the West received it."@en,
        "This is the passage the project had been unable to produce, and it changes the state of the question rather than settling it. A pre-colonial Indian text does announce a two-way division in these terms, so the sharpest form of the claim that the pair is an entirely Victorian imposition does not hold. What the text does not supply is the thing the Western pair carries: there is no moral polarity between the two, no camp a person joins, and no criterion of union against self-deification. The division sorts deities and rites, not practitioners."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmc:Kasdeja a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kasdeja"@de,
        "Kasdeja"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 69:12 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "A figure named in the Book of Parables as the one who disclosed the striking of spirits, the killing of the embryo and the serpent's bite."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:KhuddikaPiti a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Geringe Verzückung"@de,
        "Minor rapture"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.94ff. (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "The least of the five degrees of rapture, described as able to raise the hairs of the body."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the passage that attests piloerection as a recognised concomitant of a meditative state, not as its object."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:KindledInnerFire a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das entfachte innere Feuer"@de,
        "The kindled inner fire"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.66-68 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "A heat said to arise when the raised wind reaches the region of fire, lengthening its flame, until the sleeping power is warmed through and straightens."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:Kiyoka a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kiyoka, das Verbrennen aller geladenen Dinge"@de,
        "Kiyoka, the burning of all the charged things"@en ;
    dcterms:source "W. Holman Bentley, Pioneering on the Congo (London: Religious Tract Society, 1900), vol. I, pp. 290-292" ;
    skos:definition "A movement of about eighteen hundred and seventy-two, brought through the country by men from Luanda, which induced town after town to destroy every charged thing on a stated argument: since sickness and death come by the black art, there would be no more suffering and death if every charm were destroyed and none made again. Houses were searched and hiding places ransacked, and the movement proclaimed a code of law beside the burning."@en ;
    skos:note "A prediction about the world, made inside the transmission, acted on at the scale of whole districts, and refuted by the event. The corpus states the outcome and the response in one movement: the prevalence of sickness and death as before convinced the people that their hopes were not realised, and they concluded that many had kept their charms in spite of everything, so that the correctness of the theory was unquestioned and the failure was put down to the treachery of those who had hidden them."@en,
        "The account was first printed in the compiler's dictionary appendix of eighteen ninety-five under the entry kiyoka and is repeated in the narrative of nineteen hundred; the second recorder quotes it from the appendix rather than reporting it himself, so there is one witness here and not two."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:KongoNkisi .

pmc:KnowledgeOfClouds a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wolkenkunde"@de,
        "Knowledge of the clouds"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "The reading of weather and import from cloud formations."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:KrtyaReditAdAuctorem a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Zauber geht zu dem zurück, der ihn gemacht hat, und zu niemandem sonst"@de,
        "The spell goes back to whoever made it, and to nobody else"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Atharvaveda Saṃhitā 5.14 verses 4, 5, 6, 7 and 9, and 10.1 verses 3, 5 and 7, in the translation of W. D. Whitney, revised by C. R. Lanman, Harvard Oriental Series 7 and 8, Cambridge, Mass. 1905" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity claimed for the rite and the restriction claimed on it, stated together. Be the witchcrafts for the witchcraft-maker, the curse for him that curses; if woman or if man has made witchcraft in order to evil, it we conduct unto him; whether thou art god-made or made by man, thee we lead back. And, in the same hymn: O practised piercer, pierce him; whoever made it, him do thou smite; we do not sharpen thee up to slay him who has not made it. The second hymn puts the restriction twice more — let evil be to the evil-doer, a curse to the curser; and, him do thou return against, seek not us who are innocent — and names the classes of possible maker without deciding between them: Śūdra-made, king-made, woman-made, made by Brahmans."@en ;
    skos:note "The restriction is the part worth having and it is stated as a limit on the weapon rather than as a wish. A returned spell is a thing that can be aimed, and the text twice says whom it must not reach. That makes the claim narrower than the general run of counter-sorcery and, in the same movement, unexaminable from outside: a person who falls ill after such a rite was the maker, and a person who does not was not."@en,
        "Whitney records that verses 3 and 5 are wanting in the Paippalāda and that verses 9 and 13 stand there in the second book, so that the verse carrying the restriction is transmitted in both recensions but in different places. The claim is the transmission's and not one recension's."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Atharvavedic .

pmc:LadderOfThirtySteps a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Leiter der dreißig Stufen"@de,
        "The ladder of thirty steps"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John Climacus, The Ladder of Divine Ascent, steps 1-30" ;
    skos:definition "An ascent in thirty steps, running from renunciation and exile through the active virtues to stillness, prayer, freedom from the passions and love, and passing in its course from human labour to what is given."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:DesertMonasticism .

pmc:LatinMysticismCriterionDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob eine als goettlich erkannte Sprache verbuergt, was sie sagt"@de,
        "Whether a locution known to be from God secures what it says"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Juan de la Cruz, Subida del Monte Carmelo II.XVII-XVIII (ed. Gerardo, Toledo 1912, pp. 186-200; II.19-20 in Lewis)",
        "Teresa de Jesus, Las Moradas sextas 3 (ed. Navarro Tomas, Madrid 1910, pp. 168-175)" ;
    skos:note "No side is preferred and the dispute is not resolved. What it establishes is a property of the transmission rather than of the locutions: this transmission does not have one criterion. A later reader who takes either author for the Carmelite position on the discernment of locutions has taken half of it, and the two halves cannot both be used in one argument."@en,
        "The hinge is the same case on both sides, and that is what makes the pair worth more than either half. The Subida uses Nineveh to prove that a prediction with a term may pass its term and stand unfulfilled without having been false, and reports the prophet's distress at it. The Moradas names the same prophet at the same city as the thing the soul is reminded of countless times when it fears the word it was given will be made false. One reads the non-event as the normal case and the expectation as the error; the other reads the non-event as the trial and the expectation as the fidelity."@en,
        "The narrow point of contact, so that the dispute is not read more widely than the reading supports. Both subordinate the content to Scripture and to a learned confessor, both distinguish the imagination and the devil from God, and both warn against seeking such things. The disagreement is over the evidential standing of the hearer's own certainty about a thing to come, and over what follows when the thing does not come. On that the two statements are incompatible as they stand."@en,
        "Two founders of one reform, writing within about eight years of one another, in the same language and for the same houses, take opposite positions on the one question that decides whether anything in this class is checkable. One says that although the words of God are always true in themselves we cannot secure ourselves in them, that they do not infallibly happen as they sound, that acting on the understanding of them may be a great error, and that nothing is to be received because it is a revelation. The other makes a certainty that no contrary outcome removes the third and most reliable sign that the words were God's, and reports that the certainty is right because in the end the word is fulfilled."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:MoradasSenalesHablas ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SubidaDelMonteCarmelo ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinMysticism .

pmc:LesserMysteries a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die kleinen Mysterien"@de,
        "The lesser mysteries"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Athenian decree concerning Eleusis, IG I³ 6, face C" ;
    skos:definition "The preparatory rite at Agrai, held in the month Anthesterion."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EleusinianMysteries .

pmc:LightKnowledge a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Leichte Kenntnis"@de,
        "Light knowledge"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), p. 274" ;
    skos:definition "The first stage of a secret knowledge, named with a Bambara expression. The account states that the rules of the Sirius system revealed to initiates at this stage are simplified in some parts and complicated in others, in order to turn attention away from calculations that remain more secret still."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Dogon .

pmc:LightOfKnowledge a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Leuchten der Erkenntnis"@de,
        "The light of knowledge"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra II.28" ;
    skos:definition "What is said to arise, on the wearing away of impurity, and to continue up to discriminative discernment."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PatanjalaYoga .

pmc:LoosingOfSpells a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Lösen von Beschwörungen"@de,
        "Loosing of spells"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "The undoing of a binding laid by another, held in the text as a distinct art from the laying of it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:MagnetismeCrise a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Krise"@de,
        "The crisis"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. III, pp. 47-49" ;
    skos:definition "Any state differing from the natural one and resulting from the magnetic influence, manifested by sharp pains in a part of the body, a sense of suffocation, nervous movement, spasms, considerable perspiration, the impossibility of opening the eyes, drowsiness, sleep or somnambulism. The operator is to let it develop, calm the spasm by degrees, concentrate the action on the seat of the pain and then draw it off, and on no account leave the patient or let anyone disturb him until the state has entirely ceased."@en ;
    skos:note "The source reports and then qualifies Mesmer's rule that there are no cures without crises: read as meaning that recovery requires a change of state it is trivially true, read as meaning that the cure is worked by a sudden change with evident symptoms it is not always true, since many diseases are cured by slow amelioration in which no moment can be marked. That is a transmission correcting its own founder on the point where his claim was strongest, and it is entered as the correction it is."@en,
        "The word is used here in a narrower sense than physicians use it, and the source says so: operators came to call somnambulism the crisis and somnambulists crisiacs. A reader comparing this with the medical literature of the period is comparing two senses of one word."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:MagnetismeDeplacementDeLaDouleur a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Verlagerung des Schmerzes"@de,
        "The displacement of the pain"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. III, pp. 44-45" ;
    skos:definition "One of the most ordinary effects: the operation dislodges the pain and makes it pass down in the direction of the current given to the fluid. Where the dislodged pain does not at first reach the extremities it is to be forced thither in subsequent sittings; where gout seated in the head descends and is arrested at the breast or stomach the action is to be continued until it is conducted to the feet. The displacement is always a proof of the efficacy of the operation."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also, in a narrow way, the one claim here a bystander could check in a single sitting: whether a patient who located a pain in the head locates it in the knees twenty minutes later is a report anybody in the room can take down. The examination beside this file does not use it, because the 1784 commission did not test it; the address is recorded here for whoever does."@en,
        "The last sentence is the one to hold. A movement of the reported site of a pain is taken as establishing that the agent acted, and the same chapter has the operator prescribe where the pain is to go next — so the sign is produced by the same hand that reads it. This is the cheapest of the three unfalsifiability clauses in the file and the only one the source states as a rule rather than as a qualification."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:MandaeanCautioAdversusMagiam a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Lernet nicht des Satans Zauberwerk"@de,
        "Learn not the sorcery of Satan"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ginzā, right side, book 1, commandment 97 (text 13,11-14,20), and book 2, first piece, commandment 32 (text 35,3-36,8), in the German of Mark Lidzbarski, Ginzā (Göttingen 1925), pp. 16 and 35" ;
    skos:definition "The prohibition standing in the commandments of the first book and repeated in the parallel recension of the second: learn not the sorcery of Satan and bear no witness of falsehood; every one who perverts the law is consumed by the blazing fire. The neighbouring commandment warns that the song of Satan is full of sorcery, deception and seduction, and perverts the mind of the perfect."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered with the fact that stands against it, because the fact is in the same book that carries the prohibition and was recorded by the same person who transcribed the practice. The recorder states that many Mandaean priests, in spite of the Ginzā's prohibition of such practices, derive part of their income from writing amulets and from sorcery when their lawful fees are insufficient. The corpus of lead rolls and incantation bowls in Mandaic therefore stands in a field its own scripture condemns, and a reader who takes either the prohibition or the practice alone has half of it."@en,
        "The prohibition is printed twice in the Ginzā in two recensions of the same commandment list, once numbered ninety-seven in the first book and once thirty-two in the second, and the two differ in wording but not in the clause. That is a repetition inside one work and not a second witness."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:MandaeanNomenEtSignum ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GinzaRabba ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean .

pmc:MandaeanDiesMbattal a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Tage, die mbaṭṭal sind, unnütz"@de,
        "The days that are mbaṭṭal, useless"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), p. 88, with the marriage case at p. 60" ;
    skos:definition "Days on which no undertaking is to be begun and no religious ceremony performed, because the day is mbaṭṭal, useless and inauspicious: the twenty-second of the month, the twenty-fifth of Nuna, the first four days of Taura, and the day following the Panja festival. If a man dies on a mbaṭṭal day, a blessed oblation must be performed for him on a substitute at Panja."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a witness for generalizations/efficacy-bound-to-the-calendar.ttl, where it is the eighth and the first from this transmission and the first from outside the Chinese, European and Graeco-Egyptian material that carries the other seven."@en,
        "The condition is a date and nothing else. It is not a state of the person operating, not a state of the person operated on, and not a condition that anyone could meet by preparing better; and the term the transmission uses for the day is the same word it would use for a ritual that did not work. The repair, for the one case where the timing cannot be chosen, is performance at the right season upon a stand-in."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by E. S. Drower in Iraq and published in 1937, in her chapter on cosmogony, astrology and holy-days; the rule is given as current observance, and she notes in her marriage chapter that a wedding was split across two days so as to avoid the day after Panja."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean .

pmc:MandaeanInfansSineScientia a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Kind sündigte unwissentlich, und der Priester zahlt"@de,
        "The child sinned unwittingly, and the priest pays"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), pp. 45-46" ;
    skos:definition "If during the baptism of an infant the child soils or wets its clothing, the priest continues the ceremony as though nothing had happened, but must afterwards go through a complete baptism at the hands of a ganzibra and a priest. The child's baptism is not invalidated, because it sinned unwittingly, and if it dies it is entitled to full burial rites; if it lives and reaches an age of decent behaviour it atones by being baptized in a new garment at the appointed feast."@en ;
    skos:note "The excuse is also dated forward rather than waived: the child atones later, when it can speak. What is remitted is the invalidity of the rite, not the debt."@en,
        "The transmission draws the distinction between a defilement willed and one not willed, states it in so many words, and applies it to the party whose body produced the defilement. It does not apply it to the officiant, who could no more have prevented the event than the child could, and who is charged a complete baptism. The distinction exists here and is not available to him."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by E. S. Drower in Iraq and published in 1937, in her chapter on lay life, in an account of infant baptism she describes as a rigorous ordeal during which the baby often dies; the reason given for the child's exemption is quoted by her in English as the priests gave it."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean .

pmc:MandaeanIngirthaConvalescentia a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Genest der Sterbende, ist die Weihe ungültig"@de,
        "If the dying man recovers, the consecration is void"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), p. 171" ;
    skos:definition "What the transmission says about the case in which the rite does not come off. It happens, rarely, that the person thought to be dying recovers. In that case a masiqta is read in his name, but the ceremonies as far as the candidate to the high priesthood is concerned are invalid, and he must wait until another suitable person is near death."@en ;
    skos:note "Both sides of the criterion are named, and the criterion is an event no party to the rite controls. The death makes the consecration; the recovery unmakes it, and the transmission says so in the same breath rather than finding the candidate at fault or providing a repair. That is the shape pmc:TheCriterionNoPartyControls describes, and this is the first Mandaean witness for it."@en,
        "What keeps it from being a clean experiment, and it should be said at the node rather than left for a reader to notice: the parties are not indifferent to the outcome, the dying person is handled in ways the recorder says hasten the end, and the recorder also notes that Mandaeans are acute at recognizing the signs of imminent death. The event is uncontrolled; the selection of the case is not."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by E. S. Drower in Iraq and published in 1937, from priestly informants; she reports the case as one that occurs rarely rather than as one she saw."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean .

pmc:MandaeanPaisaq a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Priester, der eine Nichtjungfrau getraut hat"@de,
        "The priest who married a woman who was not a virgin"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), p. 175, with the exclusion of the children at p. 61" ;
    skos:definition "Should a priest assist at the marriage of a woman who is not a virgin, he is debarred from all his duties until he has received fifty full baptisms in new garments, and the ganzibra who officiated must go through three hundred and sixty before he may return to his office. The record names two men who went through this expiation. A priest so reduced is called paisaq and may thereafter perform no ceremony except the marriage of women who are not virgins, and the children of such a union are excluded from the priesthood for three generations."@en ;
    skos:note "The consequence outlasts the man by three generations and attaches to children who did nothing. Entered beside generalizations/displaced-penalties.ttl for that reason, although the node is not used there: the cost to the priest is the tariff, and the cost to the descendants is a separate and permanent one."@en,
        "The fact that triggers all of this is not known to the priest and is not his to establish: matrons examine the bride, and the record says in the same sentence that they sometimes make a mistake about her virginity. The penalty is nevertheless his, and theirs is not mentioned. It is the second of the three passages in this file where relief on the ground of not knowing is available in principle and is not extended to the officiant."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by E. S. Drower in Iraq and published in 1937; she names Shaikh Dukhayil and Shaikh Rumi as having gone through the expiation, and gives the term paisaq with its definition in her own glossary."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean .

pmc:MandaeanPoenaPatris a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Tochter geht zu den Wahrsagern, und der Vater wird zur Rechenschaft gezogen"@de,
        "The daughter goes to the soothsayers and the father is called to account"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ginzā, right side, book 15, piece 1 (text 301,15-302,8), in the German of Mark Lidzbarski, Ginzā (Göttingen 1925), p. 299" ;
    skos:definition "The clause in the fifteenth book on the daughter who has separated herself from the Nasoraean way: if she prepares offerings for the temples, judgement will be passed upon her father; if she goes to the soothsayers and Chaldeans, he will be called to account at the throne of the raging Nerig."@en ;
    skos:note "The fault is hers and the reckoning is his, and the passage says so twice in two lines with two different faults. It is the plainest case of a displaced penalty this run found, and it is displaced in the direction the holding already knows from the Vedic material, from the transmitting party to the party who did not act."@en,
        "The same piece puts the same structure the other way round a few lines further on, where each man who smells the odour of the soothsayers will be made to smell the odour of decay. There the penalty stays with the one who acted. The transmission carries both and does not say what decides which."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GinzaRabba ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean .

pmc:MandaeanSacerdosIntegerCorpore a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Augenlicht des Priesters muss fehlerlos sein"@de,
        "The sight of the priest must be faultless"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Alf Trisar Šuialia, on the qualifications of a priest, quoted in E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), p. 147" ;
    skos:definition "The condition the priestly manual sets on the body of a priest, as quoted in the field record: according to the Alf Trisar Šuialia the priest's sight must be faultless. The surrounding rule of current practice is that the body must be pure, sound and perfect, that a circumcised man, an impotent man and a eunuch are debarred, and that a priest already ordained who takes an injury destroying his manhood or costing him a limb can no longer officiate."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered second-hand and marked as such, because the manual could not be obtained; the surrounding practice is field-recorded and stands at the source string beside the manual. The reason for keeping the two apart is that they disagree: the recorder notes that the loss of an eye is apparently not now counted, while the manual she quotes requires faultless sight. A later reader who conflates them will read a rule as current that the field record reports as lapsed."@en,
        "The intermediary is named as a literal and not as the work identifier this file otherwise uses, because pm:readVia is declared as a literal and its value shape rejects anything else. That is a gap in the vocabulary and not a decision of this node: the work identifiers introduced on 2026-09-18 exist so that two files naming the same work find each other without a string comparison, and a node read through an intermediary is exactly the case where a later reader wants to ask which works the holding has read through. It is filed as prima-materia#759."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlfTrisarSuialia ;
    pm:readVia "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), p. 147" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean .

pmc:MandaeanSacerdotissaeDisputatae a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob diese Überlieferung Frauen geweiht hat"@de,
        "Whether this transmission consecrated women"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), p. 147",
        "Ginzā, right side, book 13 (text 287,10-288,24), in the German of Mark Lidzbarski, Ginzā (Göttingen 1925), p. 285 with his footnote 1" ;
    skos:definition "Three statements that cannot all be taken at face value. The priestly manual says, in the words the field record quotes: beware lest ye consecrate a woman as priest. The field record states that sex is not a bar, that there have been women priests and high priestesses, that a woman of the high rank may perform only one kind of marriage, and that the names of such women occur in the ancestor lists; and in the same paragraph that the recorder never met one or heard of one existing, but was assured there had been some in the past. And the thirteenth book of the right Ginzā, which the field record cites as mentioning Mandaean priestesses, has at that place a feminine plural which its translator renders as women disciples and refuses in a footnote to render as priestesses, on the ground that there are none among the Mandaeans."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered against pmc:MandaeanSacerdosIntegerCorpore because that is the node carrying the manual's conditions on who may be consecrated, and the dispute is about the extent of those conditions. The manual could not be obtained, so both of its sentences in this file stand at second hand through the same intermediary, which is a limit on the whole dispute and not only on one side of it."@en,
        "The prohibition in the manual points the same way as the disputed reading and not away from it. A rule forbidding an act is not usually made against an impossibility, and the recorder quotes this one immediately after reporting that she was assured there had been such women. What none of the three gives is a case: no consecration of a woman is described anywhere in the works read here."@en,
        "The reason this is a node and not a remark is the translator's footnote, which was read at the wording in this run. The ground he gives for excluding the reading is the present state of the community; and the present state of the community is precisely what the passage would bear on if the other reading were right. A textual question is being decided by an appeal to the fact in question, in a work that is the standard translation and is cited by everything downstream of it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:MandaeanSacerdosIntegerCorpore ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran,
        pmw:GinzaRabba ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean .

pmc:MandaeanTaxatioPollutionis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Dasselbe unwillkürliche Ereignis, nach dem Tag seines Eintritts berechnet"@de,
        "The same involuntary event, priced by the day it falls on"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), p. 154" ;
    skos:definition "The gravest contamination during the week in the hut is nocturnal emission, and for that reason the candidate is not allowed to sleep for the whole week and a perpetual din is kept up to prevent him falling asleep. If it happens nevertheless, the price is fixed by the day: on the first three days and nights the novice and his master incur three hundred and sixty-six baptisms, on the fourth sixty, on the fifth fifty, on the sixth a triple baptism by three priests and the recitation of twenty-one rahmis. If it happens before the crowns have been put on him, the whole consecration is postponed for a year."@en ;
    skos:note "Stated so that it is not read as more than it is: the graded scale is the recorder's report of what priests told her, it is given without a case, and the manual in which it presumably stands could not be obtained. What would settle whether the grading is a rule or a recollection is the Mhita u-Asuta section of the Alf Trisar Šuialia."@en,
        "This is the passage the file was assembled around. One and the same event, involuntary and unobservable by anyone but the sleeper, costs five different amounts according to the stage the rite has reached, and the largest consequence is not the largest number but the one attached to the earliest point: before the investiture there is no price at all, only a year's delay. The transmission is not grading the fault by its gravity. It is grading it by how far the procedure had got."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by E. S. Drower in Iraq and published in 1937, from priestly informants; she notes in the same paragraph that if the candidate nods a little latitude is given, which is her own observation of how the rule is handled rather than part of it."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean .

pmc:MandaeanUxorisTamia a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Frau wird unrein, und die Weihe ist ungültig"@de,
        "The wife becomes unclean and the consecration is void"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), p. 148" ;
    skos:definition "If the wife of a married candidate menstruates, miscarries or gives birth during his consecration, the resulting pollution invalidates the consecration and everyone concerned in it, beginning with three hundred and sixty-six baptisms for the officiating ganzibra. If it is the wife of the ganzibra who becomes unclean during the rite, he incurs the same three hundred and sixty-six baptisms and is in addition debarred for life from ever becoming rišh ʿama."@en ;
    skos:note "The event that voids the rite is involuntary, it happens to a person who is not performing it, and it is not in the knowledge or the power of anyone who is. The cost falls entirely on those performing. No relief is offered on the ground that they could not have known or prevented it, and the transmission does offer such relief elsewhere, to a child: see pmc:MandaeanInfansSineScientia, where the very same argument is made and made about somebody else."@en,
        "Two prices in one sentence and they are of different kinds. The baptisms are a count that can be worked through, and pmc:MandaeanBaptismataMultiplicata shows how; the bar on the highest office is permanent and attaches to the accident and not to any act."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by E. S. Drower in Iraq and published in 1937, from priestly informants in her account of the consecration; no speaker is named at this place and the rule is given as a rule and not as a case."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean .

pmc:MaoriKarakiaEfficacy a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Wirksamkeit der Karakia"@de,
        "The efficacy of the karakia"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), pp. 11-12" ;
    skos:definition "The effect an invocation is held to obtain, which the closing petition asks the invoked ancestor to grant."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Maori .

pmc:MayaAjSign a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das in der Mitte des Hauses gepflanzte Rohr"@de,
        "The cane planted in the middle of the house"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Popol Vuh: The Sacred Book of the Ancient Quiché Maya, English version by Delia Goetz and Sylvanus G. Morley from the Spanish translation of Adrián Recinos (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1950), part II, chapter 7" ;
    skos:altLabel "Aj"@en ;
    skos:definition "A sign set up before a journey from which the traveller may not return. Each of the two plants a cane in the middle of the house and states both readings in advance: if it dries, that is the sign of their death; if it sprouts again, they are living. The grandmother who stays behind is told what to say in each case."@en ;
    skos:note "Both branches are named before the outcome, the reader is a third party who was not present at the event, and the sign is not the thing it reports. That combination is rare in this collection: most signs a tradition names are read by the practitioner himself, during the practice, after being told what to expect."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MayaRitual .

pmc:MayaHouseholdUnharmed a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Unversehrtheit an Person und Haus"@de,
        "Remaining unharmed in person and household"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Diego de Landa, Yucatan Before and After the Conquest, translated by William Gates (Baltimore: The Maya Society, publication 20, 1937), section XL, p. 70" ;
    skos:definition "The state the faster is held to keep by carrying a fast through, and to lose by breaking one he has begun. The witness reports the belief without endorsing it and without naming what the harm would be."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MayaRitual .

pmc:MayaIxRemedyYieldsAverting a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Abwehr befreit von den geweissagten Übeln"@de,
        "The conjuring frees from the ills foretold"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Diego de Landa, Yucatan Before and After the Conquest, translated by William Gates (Baltimore: The Maya Society, publication 20, 1937), section XXXVII, pp. 65-66" ;
    skos:note "Entered with its own weakness stated, because the weakness is the finding. The witness names no sign by which the freeing would be recognised, no interval within which the ills would otherwise have come, and no consequence of omitting the procedure. The ills themselves are drawn wide enough to include drought and political discord together. Nothing in the passage marks out an outcome the claim would exclude, and the harvest rule keeps signs of success apart from conditions of failure exactly because a transmission can be rich in the first and empty of the second. This one is."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:MayaIxYearRemedy ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MayaRitual ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:MayaAvertingOfForetoldIlls .

pmc:Metalworking a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Metallverarbeitung und Waffenherstellung"@de,
        "Metalworking and weapon-making"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:1 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "Knowledge of the metals of the earth and of working them into blades, shields and body armour."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:MideForesight a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Blick in das Künftige"@de,
        "Looking into futurity"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 168" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity to see what is to come, ascribed to a priest of the second degree and marked in the pictographic chart by lines drawn upward from the eyes."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin .

pmc:MithraicGradusSeptem a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sieben Grade, benannt nach den Bildern, die ein Präfekt zerschlug"@de,
        "Seven grades, named from the images a prefect destroyed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Jerome, Epistula 107.2, to Laeta (trans. A. S. Geden, Select Passages Illustrating Mithraism, London 1925)" ;
    skos:definition "The list Jerome gives in passing while urging a mother not to despair of her father's conversion: that her kinsman Gracchus, as prefect of the city, overthrew and broke up a cave of Mithras with all the monstrous images by which the initiates are named — raven, a second name, soldier, lion, Persian, sun-runner and father — and sent them ahead of him as hostages to obtain baptism."@en ;
    skos:note "The most quoted list in the study of this cult, and the second name in it is not transmitted. The manuscripts read nymphius, bridegroom, or gryphus, griffin; the standard edition prints cryphius, hidden one, on the strength of inscriptions and against the manuscripts. That is the same shape of thing as the seven-gated ladder at Contra Celsum VI.22: two of the load-bearing data points of this tradition rest on emendations made because the emended text agrees better with what is known from elsewhere."@en,
        "The translations wobble too. One English rendering of the passage produces eight items by splitting one grade in two, so a reader working from that version counts eight grades. The seven are secure only because the epigraphic and iconographic material has seven; the text alone would not settle the number."@en,
        "What the passage does not do is assign planets. Jerome names the grades and nothing else — no tutelary star, no order of ascent, no doctrine. Every table pairing a grade with a planet is drawing on the mosaics and inscriptions, not on this witness. That matters for prima-materia#595, because it means the two ancient literary sources for anything sevenfold in this cult, VI.22 and this letter, do not overlap: one has planets and no grades, the other grades and no planets."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mithraic .

pmc:MithraicKlimaxHeptapylos a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Leiter mit sieben Toren und einem achten darüber"@de,
        "The ladder of seven gates with an eighth above it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.22 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV)" ;
    skos:definition "The symbol Celsus reports from the mysteries: a ladder of gates with a further gate at its top, standing for the two celestial revolutions — that of the fixed sphere and that of the planets — and for the passage of the soul through them. The first gate is of lead, the second of tin, the third of bronze, the fourth of iron, the fifth of an alloy, the sixth of silver, the seventh of gold."@en ;
    skos:note "It is a representation and not an instruction. The witness says what the symbol shows; he does not say that anyone climbs it, what is said at a gate, or what happens to a soul that fails at one. Beside the ophite speeches, which give the words, this is the same architecture with the operative half missing."@en,
        "The count of seven rests on a conjecture. The manuscript reading is a ladder of lofty gates; a seven-gated ladder is Bouherel's emendation, printed in the notes of the translation used and repeated ever since without the qualification. Seven metals are then listed, so the emendation is well motivated — but the phrase in which the number is usually quoted is not the transmitted one, and every argument that counts this ladder as a seven-station ascent is counting the metals, not reading the word."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mithraic .

pmc:MithraicProbatioLetalis a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Übersteht er es, die höchste Weihe; erliegt er, ein Begräbnis"@de,
        "If he survives, the highest initiation; if he succumbs, a burial"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Nonnus the Mythographer, commentary on Gregory Nazianzen, with Historia Augusta, Life of Commodus 9 (trans. A. S. Geden, Select Passages Illustrating Mithraism, London 1925)" ;
    skos:definition "The outcome attached to the ordeals in the same report: after passing through all of them in succession the candidate at last receives the highest initiation if he is still alive, and an honourable burial if he has succumbed."@en ;
    skos:note "A stated condition of failure, and the scarcest kind in this graph: not a sign to watch for but an outcome that excludes the other. Almost everything else the ascent material holds tells the practitioner what he will see; this tells him what happens if it does not work, and the answer is that he dies. That it comes from a late and hostile commentator is the reason it is held here with the caution and not as an established practice — but a Christian polemicist has no incentive to invent an ordeal his opponents survive."@en,
        "The Augustan History cuts the other way and is worth holding beside it: the rites, it says, normally involved saying or feigning something that would produce an impression of terror, and Commodus defiled them by killing for real. If that is right, the ordeals were mimed and the deaths were not expected — and the eighty-grade report has mistaken the mime for the thing."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:MithraicProbationes ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mithraic .

pmc:MithraicPylaiPlanetarum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Jedes Tor einem Planeten zugeordnet, mit einer Begründung aus seinem Metall"@de,
        "Each gate assigned to one planet, with a reason from its metal"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.22 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV)" ;
    skos:definition "The assignment Celsus reports gate by gate: the leaden gate to Saturn for the slowness of that star, the tin one to Venus for brightness and softness, the bronze one to Jupiter as firm-based and solid, the iron one to Mercury because iron and Mercury alike endure every work, make money and are laborious, the alloyed one to Mars because a mixture is uneven and varied, the silver one to the Moon and the golden one to the Sun, after the colours of the two."@en ;
    skos:note "Celsus gave the reason for the sequence and the witness withheld it. Origen reports that he went on to examine why the stars stand in this order, that musical reasons out of Persian theology were adduced, and that a second musical explanation followed — and then declines to quote any of it as beneath him. What is lost is exactly the part that would say whether the order is arbitrary, harmonic or observational. The project's own criterion names this gate: an order held back is a gate, and here it was held back by the transmitter rather than by the tradition."@en,
        "The order is the test, not the resemblance. Saturn, Venus, Jupiter, Mercury, Mars, Moon, Sun is neither the received planetary sequence by distance nor the order in which the ophite speaker meets his gates. A transfer from here to there would have had to change the order while keeping the idea, which is a stronger claim than a borrowing and is not made by anyone yet."@en,
        "This is the only place in the whole ascent material of this graph where a late antique witness distributes planets over seven gates one at a time. The ophite diagram does not: it counts by gates and realms, and the single heavenly body named anywhere in it is Phainon, in sympathy with the lion-shaped ruler of the other, differently named hebdomad. Irenaeus I.30.9 equates the hebdomad with the planets collectively and never pairs a name with a star. Every modern table that seats Ialdabaoth on Saturn is therefore filling one witness from another, and this is the nearest candidate for where the filling came from — nine chapters earlier in the same book, in a passage whose author expressly invites the comparison of the two diagrams. See prima-materia#595 and prima-materia#641."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mithraic .

pmc:MoradasCertitudoSuperstes a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Gewissheit, die jeden widrigen Ausgang ueberlebt"@de,
        "The certainty that survives every contrary outcome"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Teresa de Jesus, Las Moradas sextas 3 (Las moradas, ed. Tomas Navarro Tomas, Madrid: Ediciones de La Lectura, 1910, Clasicos castellanos III, pp. 171-173), read in the Spanish against the English of the Benedictines of Stanbrook rev. Zimmerman, 2nd edition 1912, Sixth Mansions chapter III, and the French of Marcelle Auclair" ;
    skos:definition "What the third sign is said to do when the thing does not happen. There is a security that cannot be overthrown: though it seem to her that everything goes contrary to what she understood, and years pass, the thought does not leave her that God will seek other means that men do not understand, and that in the end it has to be done, and so it is that it is done. The text adds that with however many bad outcomes there are to show that it cannot be fulfilled, and though the confessors with whom these things are treated tell the person herself that they are nonsense, there remains a spark somewhere, so alive, that it will be, that although all the other hopes are dead she could not if she wished keep that spark of security from being alive."@en ;
    skos:note "The Spanish of the hinge is anque le parezca que vaya todo al contrario de lo que entendio, y pasan anos, no se le quita aquel pensar que Dios buscara otros medios que los hombres no entienden, mas que, en fin, se ha de hacer, y ansi es que se hace; and of the spark, queda una centella no se donde, tan viva, de que sera, anque todas las demas esperanzas esten muertas."@en,
        "The chapter then names the case that makes this the counter-position and not merely a strong sign: Infinitas veces se acordaba cierta persona de Jonas, profeta, sobre esto, cuando temia no habia de perderse Ninive, a certain person was reminded countless times of the prophet Jonah in this matter, when he feared that Nineveh was not to be destroyed. The reason given is a fidelity owed to the Spirit of God, in desiring that he who is the supreme truth should not be held false; and the text says the soul would grieve less at being caught in a lie of its own."@en,
        "What this holding should not do with the node. It is not evidence that the certainty is unfounded, and the chapter is not naive about the alternatives: it distinguishes the imagination, where none of the signs is present, and it records persons so absorbed in the prayer of quiet that they are as if asleep and think they are spoken to. The finding is narrower and is about the criterion: a sign whose stated behaviour under disconfirmation is to remain unchanged cannot be used to tell a true locution from a false one, and the source states that behaviour itself."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MoradasDelCastilloInterior ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinMysticism .

pmc:MulabandhaYieldsInnerFire a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Wurzelsiegel bringt das entfachte innere Feuer hervor"@de,
        "The root seal yields the kindled inner fire"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.66-68 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:note "The pairing is set at the fire and not at the reversal on purpose. The manual defines the root seal as the drawing upward of the downward wind, so a claim that the seal produces that upward movement would assert only that the practice is what it is. The heat, the lengthened flame and the waking of the power are consequences the verses state beyond the definition, and they are what a protocol could address."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Mulabandha ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:KindledInnerFire .

pmc:NavajoCeremonyEfficacy a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Wirksamkeit der Zeremonie"@de,
        "The efficacy of the ceremony"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Washington Matthews, Navaho Legends, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society V (Boston and New York: Houghton, Mifflin, 1897), section 41, p. 24" ;
    skos:definition "The effect a chantway is held to obtain, which the witness says an error in a song may destroy."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NavajoCeremonial .

pmc:NeidanChengdi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Was denen zufällt, die nur einen Teil davon ablegen"@de,
        "What is obtained by those who clear only some of them"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhong-Lü chuandao ji, Lun monan 17, in the Chongkan Daozang jiyao recension (JY 149), juan 3, fol. 54a-54b" ;
    skos:definition "The chapter grades the outcome of partial failure rather than refusing it. One who leaves the dust of the world and lives hidden with his will set on the gate of mystery, but cannot wholly clear the nine difficulties, or is attached to one or two of the ten demons, is not thereby without the Way: within the Way he obtains the middling or the lesser completion, and among the immortals he becomes a human immortal or an earth immortal. Only one who clears the difficulties and the demons entirely passes through the signs of verification in order, rises step by step, and returns to the three islands."@en ;
    skos:note "The same construction stands in traditions/shangqing.ttl at pmc:ShangqingShieryanshi, where three failures out of twelve trials cost the higher rank and leave the lesser. Two transmissions, both Chinese, both grading the failure rather than recording it, and in both the effect is that no course of practice ends in a plain failure. The generalization formed from the pair stands in generalizations/graded-failure.ttl."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Neidan .

pmc:NeidanChuanyaoBuchuanhuo a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Weisen überliefern die Arznei und nicht das Feuer"@de,
        "The sages transmit the medicine and not the fire"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhang Boduan, Wuzhen pian, in Wuzhen pian zhushi (DZ 145), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 2, fol. 30a",
        "Zhang Boduan, Wuzhen pian, in Wuzhen pian zhushi (DZ 145), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 2, fol. 32a" ;
    skos:definition "The Wuzhen pian states that one may know cinnabar and black lead and still, not knowing the fire phases, be as idle as before; that the whole thing rests on the force of the practice; and that a hair's breadth of difference makes no elixir. Weng Baoguang's comment draws the rule: the making of the golden elixir rests wholly on the metal, a hair's breadth of difference is a thousand li apart, and therefore the sages transmit the medicine and do not transmit the fire. A second poem in the same juan says that the pacts, treatises, scriptures and songs expound the ultimately true and do not set the fire phases down in writing, and that to know where the oral instruction reaches through to the mystery one must discuss it in detail with an immortal; the comment adds that the six hundred chapters of the Record of the Fire are each like the other, that what heaven keeps hidden no sage transmits in writing, and that one must meet a true master and not blunder on alone."@en ;
    skos:note "The transmission is not concealing this; it is arguing for it, and the argument is about secrecy and not about failure. That the two come to the same thing is an observation of this file and is not in the text."@en,
        "This is the structural counterpart of the thirty thousand marks. There the tolerance is nil; here the parameter against which the tolerance would be measured is by doctrine not written down. Together they make a system in which no written instruction can ever be shown to have been followed correctly, and in which a failure therefore always has somewhere to go."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Neidan .

pmc:NeidanHuohouDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Feuer nach dem Kalender gesetzt wird"@de,
        "Whether the fire is set by the calendar"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhang Boduan (ascr.), Jindan sizi, verse 13, with Huang Ziru's commentary (DZ 1081), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, fol. 9a",
        "Zhang Boduan, Wuzhen pian, in Wuzhen pian zhushi (DZ 145), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 2, fol. 31b-32a",
        "Zhang Boduan, Wuzhen pian, in Wuzhen pian zhushi (DZ 145), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 2, fol. 5b-6b",
        "Zhong-Lü chuandao ji, Lun zhengyan 18, in the Chongkan Daozang jiyao recension (JY 149), juan 3, fol. 55a-55b" ;
    skos:definition "Two transmitted works of the same transmission answer the question differently, and a third contradicts itself. The Zhong-Lü chuandao ji takes the timings literally: it makes the times a numbered matter, the year following the rise and fall of yin and yang in heaven and earth, the month following the count of the comings and goings of sun and moon, and the day carrying the four cardinal points, the eight trigrams, the ten stems, the twelve branches, a hundred marks and sixty divisions, all to be divided out according to the Law; and it names not knowing the times as one of the two sufficient causes of a practitioner seeing no result at all. The Jindan sizi denies precisely this: the fire phases use no hour, the winter solstice is not at zi, the bathing hours are an empty likeness, and one may not be bound to the four seasons."@en ;
    skos:note "A second disagreement of the same kind runs beside it, over the length of the work. The Wuzhen pian commentary, on the poem about the first stirring of the yang, says that the working is to be seized at once and can be completed to term, that it is within a single double-hour, and asks what nine years or three years have to do with it. The staged terms that the later literature made canonical are therefore already being denied inside this corpus, and the one term this file enters, the year of pmc:NeidanYizaiShengtai, comes from the work that denies them."@en,
        "The Wuzhen pian is the third party and it holds both positions within one juan of one recension. On one folio it mocks those who take the images literally: the images set in the hexagrams were meant as models, and having got the image one forgets the words; later deluded followers are mired in the images and practise by the hexagram-breaths in the hope of flying up. Weng Baoguang's comment sharpens it — once the fire phases are understood, the hexagram images may all be forgotten and are of no use; today's students instead are lost in them, practise the hexagram-breaths, toil in body and thought, and hope to fly up, which he calls pitiable; one who has the fish forgets the trap, and they hold the trap for the fish and are further from the Way than before. Twenty-five folios later the same commentary sets the fire by the calendar without qualification: from the winter solstice, the first yang, the zi hour, the yang fire is advanced; from the summer solstice, the first yin, the wu hour, the yin tally; and of the second and eighth months it states a consequence — at those two months the fire is to be left off, and adding it will certainly overturn and the returned elixir will run away."@en,
        "The disagreement is not a matter of emphasis, because a consequence hangs on it. The literal side names ignorance of the times as a cause of total failure and names a month in which adding fire destroys the work; the other side says the times are an empty likeness. A practitioner cannot follow both, and the transmission does not say which to follow."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmp:NeidanHuohou ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Neidan .

pmc:NeidanThreeStages a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die drei Stufen des inneren Elixiers"@de,
        "The three stages of the inner elixir"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chen Zhixu, Jindan dayao, juan 4" ;
    skos:definition "Refining essence into breath, breath into spirit, and spirit back into emptiness."@en ;
    skos:note "A dating warning belongs on this node. The sequence is routinely attributed to the Cantong qi or to the Wuzhen pian, but neither carries it as a fixed numbered progression; the Wuzhen pian sets only a precedence of vital force before inner nature, and of doing before non-doing. The earliest ordered witness located for the full triad is the Yuan-period Jindan dayao."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:JindanDayao ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:NeidanYiqiao a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die eine Öffnung, die aus einem Mund kommen muss"@de,
        "The one aperture, which must be had from a mouth"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhang Boduan (ascr.), Jindan sizi, preface, with Huang Ziru's commentary (DZ 1081), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, fol. 4a-4b" ;
    skos:definition "The preface makes one thing the condition of everything else. There is in the body an aperture called the mysterious female; it is not the heart, not the kidneys, not the mouth or nose, not the spleen or stomach, not the grain passage, not the bladder, not the cinnabar field and not the upper cinnabar field. Whoever knows this one aperture has in it the winter solstice, the medicine, the fire phases, the bathing and the binding of the embryo. And the condition on the condition: this aperture may not be settled by one's own notion, it must be handed on from mind to mouth, and whoever does otherwise acts at random."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a stated necessity because the consequence of missing it is stated for the whole work at once: without it the run is at random, which is the preface's own word. The construction is worth setting beside the fire-phase node below, since between them they remove both halves of the operation from the written record — the place is not to be found by thinking and the timing is not to be written down."@en,
        "The nine denials are the operative part and are kept in the definition rather than summarised. A list that says where the aperture is not, and gives no positive location, cannot be followed by a reader; that is what makes the oral transmission necessary, and the necessity is stated in the same passage that creates it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Whoever settles the aperture by his own notion rather than receiving it from mouth to mind is said to be acting at random; and since solstice, medicine, fire phases, bathing and the binding of the embryo are all said to lie in it, the whole operation goes with it." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:NeidanHuohou ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:NeidanZhengyanXingchi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Neidan .

pmc:NginduLeafNullifiesEveryCharm a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Blatt hebt jedes geladene Ding im Haus auf"@de,
        "One leaf annuls every charged thing in the house"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), p. 241" ;
    skos:definition "A leaf of the ngindu-ngindu tree is held to counteract the influence of all charms and charged things, so that a practitioner who finds one in his house must reinvigorate everything he owns, the presence of the leaf having rendered them useless."@en ;
    skos:note "Recorded because it is a physical and checkable condition of failure rather than a moral one, and because it is general: it does not annul one charged thing but the whole stock of them. What it shares with the other failure conditions is that it is discovered after the fact."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:KongoNkisi .

pmc:NimittaDuvidha a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Handbuch kennt zwei Zeichen und nicht drei"@de,
        "The handbook knows two signs and not three"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga III.117-121, Pali PTS [113]-[114] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, pp. 132-134; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga VI.81, Pali PTS [196] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, p. 222; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "Where the later Theravāda manuals and the modern accounts derived from them run a triad of preparatory, learning and counterpart sign, this handbook states two. Chapter III lists what a teacher is to expound of the earth device and names the twofold sign among nine headings; Chapter VI quotes the old commentary in the words: here the sign is twofold, the learning sign and the counterpart sign."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because the difference decides what can be asked of the text. A three-stage sign gives a practitioner two intermediate marks to report; a two-stage sign gives him one. Whoever brings the triad to this handbook and finds it confirmed has found the later systematisation and not this work, and the search that established the absence stands at pmp:VisuddhimaggaThirdSignSearch."@en,
        "The same Chapter VI passage carries a second restriction of the same kind: there is no counterpart sign in the four divine abidings and in the ten kinds of foulness, and Chapter III reckons that only twenty-two of the forty meditation subjects have one at all. So the mark by which progress is judged is not available for eighteen of the subjects the handbook teaches, and it does not say what is to serve instead."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:NippurArmasaHermes a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Armasa, der Hermes der mystischen Kulte"@de,
        "Armasa, the Hermes of the mystical cults"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, texts 2: 2, 7: 8, 19: 7 and 25: 4-5, with the commentary to 3: 1 and introduction § 13, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 99, 120, 123, 147, 196 and 206)" ;
    skos:definition "A foreign power naturalised into this craft under several spellings. One operator comes clad in iron and fire, vested with the garments of Armasa and the Word, with his strength in him who created heaven and earth. Another invokes Ermes the great Lord beside Gabriel, Michael, Raphael and the angel Asiel. A pagan bowl invokes the Lord, the Word and Leader and Armasa. A fourth sets the name in a series with Metatron."@en ;
    skos:note "The editor identifies the form as one of the Syriac spellings of Hermes and takes the being to be the Hermes of the Egypto-Grecian mystical theosophy, welded together in this corpus with the esoteric Jewish Metatron; and he doubts that these magicians knew what the name meant. The identification is his, the four places are the texts'."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:nameForm "Armasa",
        "Ermes" ;
    pm:nameRole "the great lord whose garment the operator puts on and whose name he invokes; called the Word and Leader in a pagan bowl of the same collection, and identified with Metatron in a third" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurFiveCommissioningDeities a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Sames, Sin, Bel, Nannai und Nirig"@de,
        "Sames, Sin, Bel, Nannai and Nirig"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 36: 3-6 with the commentary, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 237-239)" ;
    skos:note "The editor observes that all of these except Nannai survived in the Mandaean religion as evil spirits, the spirits of the seven planets, while this charm confesses their benevolent power and carries no Mandaic trace; and he takes Nirig for the ancient Nergal. The same bowl later sends the demon away from the presence of holy angels, so the two hierarchies stand in one text without comment."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:nameForm "Bel",
        "Nannai",
        "Nirig",
        "Sames",
        "Sin" ;
    pm:nameRole "the five deities who commission the exorcist of the pagan bowl: the lord Sames the Sun charges him, Sin the Moon sends him, Bel commands him, Nannai speaks to him, and Nirig gives him power to go against the evil spirit" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText10HouseholdSealedWithTheSealsOfAdamAndNoah a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Hausstand gesiegelt mit den Siegeln des ersten Adam und Noahs"@de,
        "A household sealed with the seals of the First Adam and of Noah"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 10: 1-6, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, p. 165)" ;
    skos:definition "An amulet for the salvation of a named woman, her husband, her son, her house and her whole threshold. The house and threshold are sealed and countersealed; the woman and her son are sealed with that seal with which the First Adam sealed Seth his son and he was preserved from demons and devils and tormentors and satans; and the three of them are sealed and countersealed again with that seal with which Noah sealed the ark from the waters of the Deluge, that the spirits may fly and cease and go forth and remove from them and from their house and their abode and their bed-chamber, from this day and forever."@en ;
    skos:note "Both warrants are sealings that are said to have worked once before, and both name what they preserved against. The editor notes that this is one of the two bowls in which the father of the client is named instead of the mother, and that here the woman’s husband and father bore the same name."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText11WomanSavedFromFeverBarrennessAndAbortion a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Eine Frau gerettet vor der Lilith, Fieber, Unfruchtbarkeit und Fehlgeburt"@de,
        "A woman saved from the lilith, fever, barrenness and abortion"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 11: 1-9, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, p. 170)" ;
    skos:definition "Salvation from heaven for a named woman, that she be saved by the love of heaven from the Lilith and the Tormentor; that these and Fever and Barrenness and Abortion fly and refrain and remove from her, in the name of him who controls the demons and devils and liliths. The bowl is for the binding of Bagdana, their king and ruler; it adjures the named lilith who dwells in her house and plagues boys and girls; it has written her a divorce and separated her as the demons do who write divorces for their wives and do not return to them; and it tells her to take her divorce, not to appear by night or by day, not to lie with the woman and not to kill her sons and daughters."@en ;
    skos:note "One of four nearly identical inscriptions, the others being text 18 of this collection, a Mandaic bowl published by another editor and the very first bowl of this class ever published; the editor restores the broken halves of each from the others. The four harms are named as four separate things, which is as near as this corpus comes to a list of complaints."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText13BarrenWifeLovedAndBearing a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Eine unfruchtbare Frau vom Mann geliebt und zum Gebären gebracht"@de,
        "A barren wife loved by her husband and brought to bear"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 13: 1-13, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 178-179)" ;
    skos:definition "The charm a childless and neglected wife has made for herself. The mouths of all races, legions and tongues are closed from her; three named angels pity and love and compassionate and embrace her, enter in before all the sons of Adam, clothe her from their clothing with the garment of the grace of God and sit with her on this side and on that; and on the exterior: quickly be enamoured, be enamoured and come, her named husband, to the marrow of his house and to the marrow of his wife; as she was a virgin travailing and bearing not, so may she be fresh myrtle for crowns."@en ;
    skos:note "One of the two love-charms of the collection, and the editor marks the difference between them: here the barren forsaken wife speaks, not the passionate lover. The same couple is the subject of text 1, and the editor reads the liliths bound there as what had prevented the fruit of the union, affecting the man’s love and virility as well as the woman. The last line is broken where a preparation with leaven is named, which he takes for an aphrodisiac recipe."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText14NamedAndUnnamedDemonsCastDown a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Eine Frau gesiegelt gegen Dämonen, deren Namen geschrieben sind und deren nicht"@de,
        "A woman sealed against demons whose names are written and whose are not"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 14: 1-8, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, p. 183)" ;
    skos:definition "The bowl is made for the sealing of a named woman. It adjures the evil one in the name of holy Agrabis and of further names, and him who lodged his Shekina in the temple of light and hail; it invokes Michael and Gabriel, Sariel, Seraphiel, Suriel, Sarsamiel, Gadriel, Peniel and Nahriel; and it binds all blast-demons and evil injurers, whose names are recorded in this bowl and whose names are not recorded in this bowl, to be cast down and to sit within the glowing light and fiery flame."@en ;
    skos:note "The clause covering the unrecorded names is the craft’s own provision against the insufficiency of its instrument, and it is this bowl that states it most plainly. The editor reads several of the opening names as letter-substitutions and anagrams for the divine name rather than as beings."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText15HouseholdBoundWithBrassIronAndFire a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Hausstand durch einen Reimspruch und ein Feuersiegel von seinen Geistern befreit"@de,
        "A household banished of its spirits by a rhyming couplet and a seal of fire"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 15: 1-9, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 185-186)" ;
    skos:definition "Salvation of heaven for the house and dwelling of five named persons. The operative part is a doggerel couplet printed in Latin letters, repeated three times with variation, after which the operator says: I scan and rhyme against you, Spirits and Goblins and Plagues and Howlers and Strokes and Circlet-spirits and evil arts and mighty works and idol-spirits and the evil Lilith; and I bind you with bonds of brass and iron and seal you with the figure of a seal of fire; and I banish you from the five, in the name of eight named angels, and by the seal of Yhwh Sebaoth it is established forever."@en ;
    skos:note "The editor says the virtue of this charm lies in the couplet, which is the only place in the collection where the working words are a measured verse rather than a list of names or a formula of binding. The figure in the centre of the bowl is the serpent with its tail in its mouth. The five clients may be the members of a lodging house."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText16ChildrenLongLifeAndNoInjurerTouching a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kinder, langes Leben und kein Schädiger der Welt, der eine große Familie berührt"@de,
        "Children, long life and no injurer in the world touching a large family"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 16: 1-14, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 188-189)" ;
    skos:definition "Salvation from heaven for the same family of nine, their house and their property, that they may have children and may live long and be established, and that no injurer in the world may touch them. It works by the great name whereby the holy God is called, which suppresses darkness under light, plague under healing, destruction under construction, injury under ban and anger under repose; by it all the sons of darkness are suppressed under the throne of God, and a list of twenty-odd kinds of hostile power, ending with the seven tormentors of night and day, are bound, suppressed and laid away from the family, their house, their property and all their abode, from this day forever."@en ;
    skos:note "The five pairs of the great name are the only statement in this corpus of how the binding is supposed to work rather than of what it binds, and each pair sets a good over an evil in the same construction. The list of species that follows is the longest in the collection."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText17WomanDivorcesTheLilithsHerself a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Eine Frau scheidet die Lilithen selbst und erlangt Lösung für ihre Söhne"@de,
        "A woman divorces the liliths herself and obtains release for her sons"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 17: 1-12, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 190-191)" ;
    skos:definition "In the first person: this day above any day, years and generations of the world, I, a named woman, have divorced, separated, dismissed thee, thou Lilith, Lilith of the Desert, Hag and Ghul; the three of you, the four of you, the five of you; naked are ye sent forth, nor are ye clad, with your hair dishevelled behind your backs. They are to hear and go forth and not trouble her in her house, nor her two named children; they are warded off with the curse which Joshua bar Perahia sent; and the bowl binds and seals with the seal of El Shaddai and with the seal of Joshua bar Perahia the healer, healing and release from heaven for three named sons, and all injurers thwarted and frustrated."@en ;
    skos:note "The client is the operator, which happens in four bowls of this collection and in none of them more completely than here: she dates the writ, pronounces the divorce in the first person and names the master only as the authority behind the ban. Her children appear in two different groups, and the editor cannot resolve them into one family."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText18PrayerForOffspring a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Nachkommenschaft, erbeten in der vierten der gleichlautenden Scheidungsschalen"@de,
        "Offspring prayed for in the fourth of the duplicate divorce bowls"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 18: 2, with the commentary, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, p. 193)" ;
    skos:definition "The fourth of the nearly identical inscriptions collated under text 11. The editor gives no translation, saying the bowl is badly written and mutilated and would be in large part unintelligible without the other texts, and that it presents little that is new; of its second line he states that a prayer for offspring is here expressed."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because the bowl carries a statement of effect even though the volume prints no translation of it, and because leaving it out would make the count of the collection’s claims depend on how much of each bowl the editor chose to render. What the node rests on is one sentence of the commentary and not a read passage of the text; that is said here and not smoothed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText19BodySealedHeadToToe a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Mann mit besetztem Leib gesiegelt vom Scheitel bis zu den Zehen"@de,
        "A man whose body is infested sealed from the top of his head to his toes"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 19: 1-20, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 196-197)" ;
    skos:definition "The whole person and the bedchamber of a named man are charmed and sealed and countersealed with the seven spells which may not be loosed and with the eight seals which may not be broken. More than half the text is an invocation of named deities and powers, the sixty male gods and the eighty female goddesses and the seventy exalted priests of Bagdana among them. Against all demons, devils and evil satans this charm and bowl is sure and its seals established, from whose charm none ever goes forth and from whose control none sallies forth; the demon and the evil lilith which are in his body are bound by charms in earth and by seals in heaven, and the man is sealed from the top of his head to the toes of his feet."@en ;
    skos:note "The one bowl in which the affliction is located in the client’s body rather than in his house, and the one that denies most explicitly that its work could be undone. The charming and the sealing are repeated six times over, each time in the name of a different pair of powers, and the whole series ends in the same clause of expulsion from his body."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText1CoupleAndHouseKeptFromLiliths a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Heil für ein Ehepaar und sein Haus vor den Lilithen und ihren Bruten"@de,
        "Salvation for a couple and their house from the liliths and their broods"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 1: 1-15, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 117-118)" ;
    skos:definition "The first bowl of the collection calls itself an amulet wherein shall be salvation for a named man and his wife, and an amulet against the liliths that haunt their house. It adjures all species of liliths in respect of their posterity, begotten by demons and liliths to the children of light who go astray, and closes: with the formula TWM 5S GS GSK have I written against thee, evil Lilith, whatsoever name be thine; we have written; and his name shall save thee, Ephra, forever and ever."@en ;
    skos:note "The harms the adjuration lists are the fullest catalogue in the corpus: the liliths do violence and trample and scourge and mutilate and break and confuse and hobble and dissolve the body like water, and they appear to men in the likeness of women and to women in the likeness of men. The same couple is the subject of the love-and-fertility charm at text 13, which the editor reads as answering the same trouble from the other side."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText20GhostsExorcisedFromAWoman a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Dämon, Teufel, Satan, Fluchgeist und Lilithen von einer Frau gebannt"@de,
        "The demon, devil, satan, curse-spirit and liliths exorcised from a woman"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 20: 1-5, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, p. 201)" ;
    skos:definition "In the name of a sixfold repeated letter, the Demon and the Devil and the Satan and the Curse-spirit and the evil Liliths which appear by night and appear by day, and appear to a named woman, are exorcised and sealed, in the name of Gabriel, Michael and Rophiel."@en ;
    skos:note "The shortest text of the collection and the one whose figure carries the claim: a demon drawn with arms and legs manacled, with the word prohibition written in the space at its right and permission at its left, and the client’s name inscribed in the lower part of the body on the side of the prohibition. The editor reads the picture as presenting graphically that the demon has no power over the lady, and calls the inscription itself very illiterate."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText21HouseAndThresholdSealedFirstOfFour a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Haus und Schwelle gesiegelt vor jeder Art Lilith, die erste eines Satzes"@de,
        "House and threshold sealed from every kind of lilith, the first of a set"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 21: 1-4, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 203-204)" ;
    skos:definition "The first of three surviving bowls of one set for one woman. Sealed and countersealed are the house and threshold of a named woman from all evil plagues, from all evil spirits, from the tormentors, from the liliths and from all injurers, that they approach not to her, to her house and threshold, which is sealed with three signets and countersealed with seven seals from every kind of lilith and from all injurers, from this day and forever."@en ;
    skos:note "The three bowls are kept as three claims and not as one, because what they are is three deposits of a single operation and the corpus makes the count of deposits part of the operation. The editor translates the longest of the three and notes that the writer of the second had more room and made a longer inscription."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText22HouseAndThresholdSealedSecondOfFour a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Haus und Schwelle gesiegelt vor jeder Art Lilith, die zweite eines Satzes"@de,
        "House and threshold sealed from every kind of lilith, the second of a set"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 22: 1-5, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 203-204)" ;
    skos:definition "The second and longest of the three surviving bowls of the set, and the one the volume translates. Sealed and countersealed are the house and threshold of the same woman from all evil plagues, from all evil spirits, and from the tormentors, and from the liliths, and from all injurers, that ye approach not to her, to the house and threshold, which is sealed with three signets and countersealed with seven seals from every kind of lilith and from all injurers, that ye approach not to her, and from all evil spirits and from all evil injurers, from this day and forever."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText23HouseAndThresholdSealedThirdOfFour a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Haus und Schwelle gesiegelt vor jeder Art Lilith, die dritte eines Satzes"@de,
        "House and threshold sealed from every kind of lilith, the third of a set"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 23: 1-4, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 203-204)" ;
    skos:definition "The third of the three surviving bowls of the set, carrying the same sealing of the house and threshold of the same woman against the same list of powers, with slight variations of wording. All three are roughly and illegibly written, and in all three a grammatical peculiarity recurs which the editor finds again in the Mandaic bowls."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText24FirstSisterSavedFromFeverAndSweating a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Eine Schwester gerettet vor Fieber und Schweißkrankheit"@de,
        "One sister saved from fever and from sweating"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 24: 1-3, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, p. 205)" ;
    skos:definition "The first of three statements of effect on one bowl, each for a different daughter of one woman and each naming its own malady: salvation from heaven be for the first, that she be saved by the love of heaven from Fever and from Sweating."@en ;
    skos:note "This bowl is counted as three claims and not one, because it states three different outcomes for three named persons in three separate formulas each closed with its own Amen. The editor calls it a charm for three daughters made out in their names severally and for specific maladies. The names of the two diseases are broken at the third term."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText24SecondSisterFreedOfDisturbingDreams a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Eine zweite Schwester befreit von störenden Träumen und dem bösen Geist"@de,
        "A second sister freed of disturbing dreams and the evil spirit"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 24: 3-4, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, p. 205)" ;
    skos:definition "The second statement of effect on the same bowl: salvation from heaven be for the second daughter, that there cease from her disturbing dreams and the evil spirit and evil satans."@en ;
    skos:note "The disturbing dream is the one complaint in this corpus that is both the harm and the observable: elsewhere the charms forbid the demon to appear in dream by night, and here the dream itself is what the bowl is to stop."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText24ThirdSisterBroughtToTheBirth a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Eine dritte Schwester zur Geburt ihres Kindes gebracht"@de,
        "A third sister brought to the birth of her child"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 24: 4-6, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, p. 205)" ;
    skos:definition "The third statement of effect on the same bowl: salvation for the third daughter, that she be saved by the love of heaven, to wit that she bring to the birth her child."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText25FamilyGhostsKeptFromTheLiving a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Paar bewahrt vor der Erscheinung seiner eigenen Toten"@de,
        "A couple kept from the apparition of their own dead"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 25: 1-7, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, p. 207)" ;
    skos:definition "Salvation from heaven for a named man and his wife, that there vanish from them in their dwelling the demons and devils by the mercy of heaven. Then the clause the bowl exists for: whoever here has dead, who shall become alive to them here, and shall approach and are found to be actually dead, from these you are kept and these are kept from you. A message is to be sent to a named one of the departed about the contentions of them all; and five named angels, who bring salvation to all the children of men, will come and go forth with the salvation of the house and property and dwelling and the sons and daughters and all the people in the house, from this day even for the sphere of eternity."@en ;
    skos:note "The one bowl of the collection directed against the apparition of a household’s own dead rather than against a class of spirit, and the only one that proposes to negotiate: the dead are to be told to cease their contentions with the living. The editor compares two bowls of other collections and notes that the name given to the departed is a fancy name and not recognisable as a person’s."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText26LilithDismissedAtEveryHourAndSeason a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Eine Lilith von einem Paar verabschiedet, zu keinem Abend, Morgen, keiner Zeit und Jahreszeit"@de,
        "A lilith dismissed from a couple at no evening, morning, time or season"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 26: 1-6, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, p. 209)" ;
    skos:definition "After the opening sentence of the Shema and two further scriptural passages: bound and held art thou, evil spirit and mighty lilith, that thou appear not to a named man and a named woman, neither by day nor by night, nor at any evening or morning, nor at any time whatsoever, nor at any seasons whatsoever; but flee from their presence and take thy divorce and thy separation and thy writ of dismissal; I have divorced thee, even as demons write divorces for their wives and return not to them."@en ;
    skos:note "The enumeration of times is the most exhaustive in the corpus and is the mirror image of the formula that singles one moment out: where the operation is set in this day out of all days, the prohibition covers every day there is. The bowl is also the one that opens with the words still carried in the house phylactery of the Jews."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText27ClientsBecomeTheOperators a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Klienten einer früheren Schale wirken den Zauber im eigenen Namen"@de,
        "The clients of an earlier bowl work the charm in their own names"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 27: 1-12, with the commentary, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 212-213)" ;
    skos:definition "The bowl repeats the wording of text 2 almost exactly for the length of ten lines, with the difference that the man who was the subject of the charm at text 7 is here the one operating it, and that in the eighth line his wife takes up the exorcism as well. In the ninth line the sorcerer says that he laid the ban upon Hermon. The remaining twelve lines are so mutilated or obscure that the editor presents no connected passage from them."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as its own claim because it is its own bowl and its own operation: the same words in the mouth of a different operator are a second assertion that they work, and it is the one case in the collection where a client of one bowl is the operator of another. What the node cannot carry is the second half of the text, which is illegible."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText29WomanAndLodgersBoundOfTwoDozenAfflictions a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Eine Frau, ihre Kinder und ihre Mieter von genannten Übeln gelöst"@de,
        "A woman, her children and her lodgers bound of named afflictions"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 29: 1-12, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 218-219)" ;
    skos:definition "An amulet in the name of Yhwh Sebaoth for a named woman, from whom, from her children, from her house and from all her dwelling the evil plagues and evil demons and the lilith and the necklace-spirits and menstruation and tormentors and the hags of the wild and impurities and epilepsy are bound; and then an adjuration of whatsoever evil thing lodges in the house and dwelling of three further named men, one of them a foreigner: leprosy, plague, stroke, the demons, ghostly shades, all goblins and evil injurers whose names I have mentioned and whose names I have not mentioned."@en ;
    skos:note "The editor reads the long list of male names in one bowl of this kind as showing that the woman who procured it was landlady of a lodging house. The tone of piety in this one he calls superior to the rest: the incantation is in the name of Yhwh Sebaoth alone, whose praises are dwelt upon in almost liturgical fashion."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText2TwoOperatorsWardEachOther a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zwei Handelnde schlagen einander die Unholde und schirmen Haus und Habe"@de,
        "Two operators smite the fiends for each other and ward house and property"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 2: 1-7, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 121-122)" ;
    skos:definition "A mutual charm in which each of two men works his powers for the other in the two halves of the text. The operator comes and has smitten the evil fiends and the malignant adversaries; he says that if at all they sin against the other man and his wife and his sons, he will lay upon them the spell of the Sea and the spell of the monster Leviathan, and bend the bow against them and stretch the bow-string at them; and wherein soever they sin against the house and property and all the people of the house, he will bring down upon them the curse and the proscription and the ban which fell upon Mount Hermon and upon Leviathan and upon Sodom and upon Gomorrha."@en ;
    skos:note "The nearest thing in the corpus to a conditional threat rather than a binding: the penalty is stated as following upon a further offence and not upon the present state. The editor asks whether the two men are professional magicians or laymen who felt they could make a stronger defence standing shoulder to shoulder; the first half of text 27 repeats the same wording with the parties reversed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText30HouseKeptFromTheSunAndTheEye a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Haus und drei Leben bewahrt vor Sonne, Hitze und dem Auge"@de,
        "A house and three lives kept from the sun, the heat and the eye"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 30: 1-5, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, p. 221)" ;
    skos:definition "Bound and sealed are the house and the life of two named men and a named woman, from the Sun and Heat, from the devil, the satan, the male demon, the female lilith, evil spirits, the impious amulet-spirit and the lilith-spirit male or female; the Eye of man or woman; the Eye of contumely; the Eye which looks right into the heart; the mystery which belongs to the evil potency, that impious lord; from the evil hateful potency; from disturbing vision; from evil spirits; from that impious lord."@en ;
    skos:note "The only bowl that names the sun itself among the powers to be bound, and the editor notes that in the Mandaean religion the sun is likewise an evil genius and that the word used is the Mandaic spelling. The three forms of the eye are the fullest statement of the evil eye in this corpus. The inscription breaks off in the middle of a formula of invocation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText31TormentorCurseAndEvilDreamsRemovedSyriac a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Peiniger, Fluch und die sehr bösen Träume aus einem Haus entfernt"@de,
        "The tormentor, the curse and the very evil dreams removed from a house"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 31: 1-9, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, p. 223)" ;
    skos:definition "The first of the Syriac bowls. This bowl is designated for the sealing of the house of a named man, that from him and his house may remove the Tormentor and the Curse and the very evil Dreams; charmed, fortified and confirmed, corroborated, strengthened and sealed and guarded are these bowls for the sealing of the house, that they may not lodge together with them; and sealed and guarded shall be the house and wife and sons of the man, that there may remove from him and his house the Tormentor and the Curse and evil Dreams."@en ;
    skos:note "Made for the same man as texts 12, 16 and 33, so the same house is covered by four bowls in three scripts. The plural, these bowls, stands in the text and the editor takes it as a reference to the four duplicates of a set. The three evils named are the standing triad of the Syriac bowls of this collection."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText32BanWritForAllTimeSyriac a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Haus gesiegelt durch einen Bannbrief, der für alle Zeit gilt"@de,
        "A house sealed by a ban-writ that is for all time"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 32: 1-12, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 225-226)" ;
    skos:definition "The bowl is designated for the sealing of the house and the wife and the children of a named man, that there remove from him the Tormentor and evil Dreams. The operator deposits and sinks the bowl, a work made like that which Rab Jesus bar Perahia sat and wrote against them, a ban-writ against all the demons and devils and satans and liliths and latbe in the house; and again he wrote against them a ban-writ which is for all time, by the virtue of names and a writing within a writing, through which heaven and earth and the mountains were subjected and the hostile powers fettered, and through which he passed over from this world and climbed above them to the height of heaven and learned all counter-charms. Charmed and sealed and countersealed is it, even as ancient runes fail not."@en ;
    skos:note "The clause about learning all counter-charms is the corpus admitting that there are such things and locating the knowledge of them in the master it works under. Of the four duplicates, this one carries additional matter at the beginning and the end which the parallel text 33 does not."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText33SameSealingForASecondClientSyriac a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Dieselbe Siegelung eines Hauses, auf einen anderen Klienten ausgestellt"@de,
        "The same sealing of a house, made out for another client"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 33: 1-11, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 230-231)" ;
    skos:definition "A Syriac bowl practically identical in wording with text 32, made out in the name of a different client, and lacking the additional matter at the beginning and the end of that text. The editor prints the inscription and supplies its lacunae from the parallel; the two bowls were written in the same hand."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as its own claim for the same reason text 27 is: the identity of the wording is not the identity of the operation, and a formula asserted again for a second household is a second assertion. The wife of this bowl’s client is the subject of text 35."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText34BodyHouseCattleAndGoodsSealedUntilTheDissolution a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Mann, sein Haus, sein Vieh und seine Habe gesiegelt gegen Schaden und Armut"@de,
        "A man, his house, his cattle and his goods sealed against harm and poverty"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 34: 1-13, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 231-232)" ;
    skos:definition "The bowl is designated for the sealing of the house of a named man by the power of the virtue of Jesus the healer and by the virtue of the operator’s mighty relative. His dwelling, abode, house, wife, sons and daughters are charmed and sealed as Moses commanded the Red Sea and the waters stood up like a wall on both sides, and by the seal of the mountains and heights, of heaven and earth, sun and moon, stars and signs; all evil in his body and in his house and wife and sons and daughters and cattle and property is sealed by the signet of Arion son of Zand, the seal of King Solomon son of David, the seal of El Saddai and Abraxas, and a seal against Harm and Constraint, that they shall not at all enter in; three further named kinds are charmed by the spell of fire and the enchainment of water until the dissolution of heaven and earth; and there depart from him the Injurer and evil Dreams and the Curse and the Vow and Arts and the Tormentor and Damages and Losses and Failures and Poverty. A named woman is sealed and protected in the same terms, and the ban-spirit who thwarts her in her hand and foot is charmed, that it may not approach nor afflict her."@en ;
    skos:note "The widest claim in the corpus and the only one that names economic loss: damages, losses, failures and poverty stand in the same list as the tormentor and the curse. It is also the only bowl in which the ban-spirit is said to thwart a person in a named part of the body, the hand and the foot, which recurs for the same formula in text 35."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText35WomanGuardedFromVowsAndRitesOfMankind a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Eine Frau bewacht vor Gelübden, Anrufungen und Riten der Menschen"@de,
        "A woman guarded from the vows, invocations and rites of mankind"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 35: 1-12, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 236-237)" ;
    skos:definition "The bowl is appointed for the sealing and guarding of the house and sons and property and body of a named woman, that she may be guarded from demons, plagues, devils, satans, seducers and diaboli, and from any vows and invocations and rites of mankind, of men and of women, and of idol-spirits who are known and who are not known by name. Five named angels, commissioned along with Moses to wardship, will guard and seal her from everything evil for the ages forever; she is sealed and guarded from the Tormentor and evil Dreams and the Curse and the Vow, and the ban-spirit who thwarts her in her hand and foot is charmed that it may not approach her; and the house and wife and son and property of a named man are guarded in the same terms."@en ;
    skos:note "The one bowl that distinguishes in so many words between diabolic arts and human machinations, and names the second as a thing to be guarded against in its own clause: the vows and invocations and rites of mankind, of men and of women. The editor marks the distinction at that word."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText37HouseholdAndCattleHealedOfAMurderousSpirit a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Hausstand und sein Vieh geheilt von einem mordenden Hausgeist"@de,
        "A household and its cattle healed of a murderous house spirit"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 37: 1-11, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, p. 242)" ;
    skos:definition "The bowl is designated for the salvation and healing of the house and threshold, the wife, the sons and daughters, the cattle and all that is his and whatsoever shall belong to a named man, confirmed by the virtue of the word of God, the Mystery of heaven and the Mystery of the assembled waters and the Mystery of earth. All that is in the house is enjoined: arts and the tormentor, the image-spirits of idolatry, all the legions and the amulet-spirits and the Ishtars and all the demons and all mighty liliths. Of the spirit it says that he sits in the house, eating and devouring, drinking and quaffing, that he is a slayer of children, and that angels come against him in wrath with sabres and sword, standing before him and ready to kill him. It closes with peace from the male gods and from the female Ishtars, and victorious peace is set."@en ;
    skos:note "The most mutilated of the bowls whose claim can still be read. The editor calls it pagan and reads the demon as carousing upon the blood of his victims; the threefold division of heaven, water and earth and the closing phrase about victorious peace he connects with the Mandaic doxology. The quarterings of the seal in the centre carry letters of the Tetragrammaton."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText38HouseVesselsCattleAndChildrenCharmedAndArmed a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Hausstand mit Geschirr und Vieh bezaubert, gewappnet und gerüstet"@de,
        "A household with its vessels and cattle charmed, armed and equipped"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 38: 1-14, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 244-245)" ;
    skos:definition "The first Mandaic bowl. Charmed, armed and equipped are the house, the dwelling and mansion and barn, and the sons and daughters, and the cattle and household vessels of a named woman and of her husband and her sons and daughters. A named lilith and all her broods, even the three hundred and sixty broods, are charmed by the word and command of a named angel and by the seal of a second, whose word none transgresses; all the gods and temple-spirits and shrine-spirits and idol-spirits and Ishtars are charmed from the bodies of her named sons and from her and her house and her bed; the mighty Istar and the three hundred and sixty broods are charmed and confined and restrained and hobbled, dismissed one after the other; and the amulet-spirits that dwell in the houses of men and waste them are charmed and hobbled and suppressed and covered and squeezed under the feet of the named sons and of their mother. The stated outcome: life, abundance, health and arming and sealing and protection to their body, and their wives and their sons and their daughters and their cattle and the people of their houses, both those entering and departing."@en ;
    skos:note "The only bowl in the collection that names the household vessels among what is covered, and the one whose claim reaches furthest beyond the family: those entering and departing with the household are included by name of their relation and not by their own names. The editor notes that the word for mansion is here reduced from the Babylonian sense of temple or palace to that of a private house."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText39UnbornChildAndWombSealed a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Leib, Seele, ungeborenes Kind und Mutterleib einer Schwangeren gesiegelt"@de,
        "The body, soul, unborn child and womb of a pregnant woman sealed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 39: 1-12, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, p. 248)" ;
    skos:definition "Health and arming and sealing and protection be for the body and soul and the unborn child and womb of a named pregnant woman. Charmed are the sorcery-spirits in stocks of iron; charmed the lilith in chains of lead; charmed the empoisoning male devils and the empoisoning female liliths; charmed the arts of evil men and hostile beasts, and evil mysteries and the magic circle of malignant masters and sages and doctors, and the melting of wax figures of him who is alive; charmed the lilith that appears to her in the shape of her sister’s daughter, who is named; charmed all the defiling ghosts that have entered, which appear to her in dreams of night and in visions of day; charmed and sealed with the seal of King Solomon. And again: health and arming and sealing be for the womb and the parturition."@en ;
    skos:note "The one bowl that names the rival operations it must overcome as operations: a magic circle worked by masters, sages and doctors, and the melting of a wax figure of a living person. The editor notes that both lead and tin were used in such workings and that his word could be either. The claim is stated twice over, the second time for the womb and the birth alone."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText3HouseholdKeptFromTheDestroyer a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Hausstand bewahrt vor dem Verderber, der Mann von Frau trennt"@de,
        "A household kept from the destroyer who parts husband from wife"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 3: 1-11, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 127-128)" ;
    skos:definition "The bowl is designated a spell and mystery and strong seal for the sealing of a named household, that the evil demon and the evil satan called the Mighty Destroyer depart and remove: that he not kill off the man from his wife nor the wife from her husband nor their sons and daughters, whether those they have or those they shall have, from this day and forever, neither by night nor by day."@en ;
    skos:note "The operative part is a chain of mystical syllables called the great name, before which the angel of death is afraid and from which, hearing it, he flees frightened and is swallowed up; the same clause is set out four times over in the one text, each time ending in the same word of sealing. The claim covers children not yet born, which is the ordinary reach of these charms and is stated in every bowl that mentions offspring."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText40HouseholdAndLivestockArmedFromLife a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Hausstand, sein Anwesen, seine Scheune und sein Vieh gewappnet aus dem Leben"@de,
        "A household, its mansion, its barn and its livestock armed from Life"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 40: 1-26, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 252-253)" ;
    skos:definition "In the name of Life, that health and armament be to the body and wife and male sons and female daughters, and the house and abode, the mansion and the barn of the cattle, the ass, bull and goat, the property of a named man, from Life. The operator swears and adjures by Life; he has broken the powers in the gate of the house; health and armament and healing and guarding are to be to the sons and daughters and the house and dwelling and mansion and barn, and to a second named woman; a named lilith and all the goddesses and the three hundred and sixty tribes are charmed by the word of the granddaughter of a named angel and by the seal of a second, whose word none transgresses; the gods and temple-spirits and shrine-spirits and goddesses are charmed from the bodies and the house; the Ishtar and the three hundred and sixty tribes are charmed, shut up, confined and hobbled, dismissed one after the other; all the amulet-spirits which lodge in their houses and devastate them are charmed; and the satan and the plague are charmed and hobbled and suppressed and covered from the bodies and the buildings. The text ends: and Life is victorious."@en ;
    skos:note "The longest and most repetitious charm of the collection, with about half its inscription on the exterior of the bowl, and the only one that names the species of livestock it covers: the ass, the bull and the goat. The whole of it is sworn by Life, which is the Mandaic name of the deity, and it is the only text here that both opens and closes with it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText4HouseWalledWithBronze a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Haus, an den vier Ecken gesiegelt und mit Bronze umwallt"@de,
        "A house sealed at its four corners and walled about with bronze"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 4: 1-7, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 133-134)" ;
    skos:definition "The general charm against all evil spirits for a named man and all the people of his house. It opens by calling the bowls covers to hold in sacred angels and all evil spirits and the tongue of impious amulet-spirits, declares them charmed and sealed in each one of the four corners of his house, and states the ground: because well sealed is his house and well armed, and with a great wall of bronze have I surrounded it. The operator adds of himself: what I desire I grasp, and what I ask I take."@en ;
    skos:note "The bowl whose opening lines settled what the whole corpus was for: the editor says they definitely establish the use of these vessels, and it is here that the inversion is read as holding the spirits in. The seals it borrows are the charm with which Enoch was charmed by his wicked brothers and the seal with which the Seven Stars and the Twelve Signs of the Zodiac were charmed unto the great day of judgment."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText5ManAndSistersKeptFromTheEvilEye a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Mann und seine zwei Schwestern bewahrt vor dem bösen Blick und dem mächtigen Satan"@de,
        "A man and his two sisters kept from the evil eye and the mighty satan"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 5: 1-6, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 138-139)" ;
    skos:definition "All evil liliths and all demons and devils and spells and idol-spirits, and the Vow and the Curse and the Invocation, and evil arts and mighty works and everything hostile are to depart from a named man and his two sisters. They are bound with the seven spells and sealed with the seven seals, and the great Prince is conjured to keep the man and one sister from the Evil Eye and from the mighty Satan and from the many Satyrs in the road of Hamad."@en ;
    skos:note "A named road, which is the only topographical specification in the collection, and a named class of spirit attached to it. The bowl closes with two scriptural passages in Hebrew, the march at the mouth of Yhwh and the rebuke of Satan, the second of which recurs in three other bowls of this collection."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText6DemonsPressedDownFromACouple a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Dämonen eines Paares niedergepresst, blind für sie in Traum und Schlaf"@de,
        "The demons of a couple pressed down, blind to them in dream and sleep"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 6: 1-11, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 141-142)" ;
    skos:definition "A press which is pressed down upon the demons, devils, satans, amulet-spirits, familiars, counter-charms and liliths male and female that attach themselves to a named couple, dwell in their archways, lurk by their thresholds, appear to them in one form and another, and strike and cast down and kill. The stated outcome: that they appear to the couple neither in dream by night nor in sleep by day, that they approach neither their right side nor their left, that they kill not their children, and that they have no power over their property, what they have and what they shall have, from this day and forever."@en ;
    skos:note "The bowl that names the vessel a press and uses the verb of pressing of its own operation four times. The pressing is done by seven words, each attached to a named power, by which heaven and earth are charmed; the seventh of those names is itself the word for presses."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText7LifeHouseAndBodyBoundAndSealed a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Leben, Haus, Habe und Schlafkammer eines Mannes gebunden und gesiegelt"@de,
        "The life, house, property and bedchamber of a man bound and sealed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 7: 1-9, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 147-148)" ;
    skos:definition "The bowl binds, seals and counterseals the life, house and property of a named man in the name of the great God and with the seal of Shadda El, by the splendour of Sebaoth and by the great glory of the Holy One, that all demons and all mighty satans remove and betake themselves and go out from the house and from the dwelling and from the whole body of the man; and then binds, seals and counterseals the same again, together with his bedchamber, in the name of Gabriel and Michael and Raphael, of the angel Asiel and Ermes the great Lord, of the great Abbahu and of the great Abraxas, the guardian of good spirits and destroyer of evil."@en ;
    skos:note "The best preserved text of the collection is a replica of this one published two years earlier from another bowl of the same museum, and the editor prints the two in parallel columns throughout. The clients of this bowl are the operators of text 27, where the man works the charm in his own name instead of receiving it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText8LilithsDivorcedFromAMarriedPair a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Lilithen von einem Ehepaar geschieden und am Wiederkehren gebunden"@de,
        "The liliths divorced from a married pair and bound not to return"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 8: 1-17, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 155-156)" ;
    skos:definition "The charm for a named man and his wife, made out in the form of a divorce-writ against the liliths. The liliths are to hear and obey and come forth from the house and the dwelling of the couple; they are not to appear to them in their house nor their dwelling nor their bedchamber, nor in dream by night nor in slumber by day; the writ renders them their divorce and their separation; they are sealed with the signet of El Shaddai, with the signet of the house of Joshua bar Perahia and by the Seven that are before him; and they are not to return from this day and forever. The close reads: and be they preserved for life."@en ;
    skos:note "The fullest divorce bowl in the collection and the one from which the abbreviated text 17 derives. It is also where the writ states its own authority twice, once from heaven and once from across the sea, and where the parentage of the liliths is given so that the instrument has two named parties."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText9DivorcesWrittenForAllTheLiliths a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Scheidebriefe für alle Lilithen, die einem Mann und seiner Frau erscheinen"@de,
        "Divorces written for all the liliths that appear to a man and his wife"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 9: 1-11, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 161-162)" ;
    skos:definition "The operator deposits and sinks the bowl, operates the work in the fashion of Rabbi Joshua bar Perahia, and writes divorces for all the liliths who appear to a named couple in dream by night and in slumber by day: a writ of separation and divorce, by virtue of letter abstracted from letter and letters from letters, of word from words and of pronunciation from pronunciations, whereby heaven and earth are swallowed up, the mountains are uprooted and the heights melt away. He has mounted up over them to the celestial height and brought a destroyer against them, to bring them forth from the house and dwelling and threshold and bedchamber."@en ;
    skos:note "The one bowl that describes its own deposition in the first person at the head of the text, which is how the position of the excavated vessels and the wording of the corpus were brought together. The letter-from-letter clause is the corpus at its most explicit about where it locates the power: in the manipulation of the written sign and the pronunciation, not in the being invoked."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurWritOfHeavenlyProvenance a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Brief, der vom Himmel und von jenseits des Meeres kam"@de,
        "A writ that came down from heaven and from across the sea"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 8: 7-11 with the commentary, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 153-155 and 159)" ;
    skos:definition "The authority the divorce-writ claims for itself. It is said to have come down to us from heaven and to have come for the demons from across the sea, and to have something found written in it for their advisement and their terrification; the commissioners and witnesses to it are the holy angels, and a named rabbi is at hand to seal it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:Nkisi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Nkisi, das geladene Ding"@de,
        "Nkisi, the charged object"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), pp. 234-235",
        "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), p. 92" ;
    skos:definition "A bundle or a carved figure into which ingredients have been put, held thereby to bring a named spirit under the control of whoever owns it and to perform one named office: to make a thief invisible or to detect him, to give a child or to withhold one, to make a gun shoot straight or another gun shoot crooked. The corpus states that there is no good wish and no evil desire for which one cannot be bought, and that a new complaint brings a new cult with a new charged thing to control the power responsible for it."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a container and not as an object of worship because the corpus says so in as many words: there is held to be no idea of worship in the sacrifice made to one, and no prayers are offered with it. The Vili witness makes the same separation in his own terms, dividing the people who serve the charged figures from the priests of the sacred groves and holding that the old Portuguese took the first for the whole."@en,
        "The name is written nkisi by Weeks, nkixi in Bentley's dictionary of eighteen eighty-seven, and nkici with the plural zinkici by Dennett, who sets out his own orthography and warns that his x stands for a different sound than Bentley's. The identifier follows the form of the Lower Congo works."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:KongoNkisi .

pmc:NoFailedRunCountsAgainstTheOperation a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der misslungene Durchgang wird anderswohin zugewiesen als an die Operation"@de,
        "The failed run is assigned somewhere other than to the operation"@en ;
    skos:note "A bounding case from the same run, and it is the sharpest the statement has met, because it uses the register's commonest place and does not have its commonest property. At pmc:PesahimAmuletAgainstSixtyDemons an amulet written against one demon fails where sixty were present; the failure is announced by the demons themselves in a song mocking the man who wrote it; and a second scholar who knows that a service-tree near a town holds no fewer than sixty writes it against sixty, whereupon they leave. The condition is in the operator, as it is in most of the twenty-three. But in every one of the others that condition is either unestablishable in advance or unestablishable in principle - incredulity, dullness, haste, impurity, insufficient merit, a decree about the patient - and here it is a matter of fact anybody could settle by asking the man one question. So the failed run does count, against the performance rather than against the art, and it is diagnosed and repaired within the story. The statement is about where a failed run is sent and it survives; what the case shows is that the four places are not all equally safe, and that a successor statement should distinguish a condition in the operator that can be checked from one that cannot. Three of the twenty-three would then fall on the checkable side at most."@en,
        "A bounding case, not a witness and not a refutation, entered on 2026-09-26 and named here because a statement with nineteen witnesses needs its vacuous cases recorded as much as its instances. The Yogavacara's Manual falls inside the antecedent — its success is an inner appearance that nobody but the practitioner can establish — and it supplies no condition of any kind under which a run that produced nothing counts as no failure. It does not supply the excuse; it never raises the case. There is no caution in it, no danger, no counterfeit and no conditional sentence, established by reading the whole of it and searching it term by term at pmp:BoranPericulumSearch and pmp:BoranTerminusSearch. The falsifier of this statement is not met, because it requires the transmission to name an outcome that would count against the operation and this one names none. The statement is therefore vacuously true of the most sign-rich manual in this holding, and that is a weaker thing than its witness count suggests."@en,
        "A ninth witness was added on 2026-09-12 from the Clavicula Salomonis, and it is the limiting case of the statement rather than another instance of it. The others supply a condition under which an empty run counts as no failure; this one supplies the condition in advance and in general, for every operation in the book: an experiment performed with due observance of days, hours and requisite solemnities which is found unsuccessful must in some manner be false and defective, and the operator must assuredly have failed in some matter. The same clause stands in three chapters of the work. Where the other eight name a party or a circumstance to which a particular non-occurrence goes, this one names the operator for all of them at once and adds that the whole book depends on the chapter that says so."@en,
        "A seventh witness was added on 2026-09-11 and it is the limiting case of the statement. Where the others supply a condition under which a run that produced nothing counts as no failure, the Zhen'gao supplies a rule of reading under which the outcome that would end the practitioner's participation altogether — his death — is classified as the attainment, and it does so for every state a corpse can be found in, the intact and the putrefied alike. The condition has there been moved from the run to the observation, which is one step further than any of the six."@en,
        "A tenth witness was added on 2026-09-16 and it is the oldest of them by four centuries. A single slip of the Pulling Book from tomb 247 at Zhangjiashan carries the instruction in two clauses: on waking, knocking the teeth prevents tooth decay; if there is tooth decay, knock them more. The first clause offers an outcome any practitioner could look for in his own mouth and the second says what to do when it is not there, and what it says is to do more of the same. That is the construction of the assay of the fourth chapter of the Baopuzi in miniature, and the two are separated by some four hundred years. The witness is worth listing apart from the other nine for one reason: it was buried between 186 and 156 before this era and read in 1984, so nobody added the second clause later in answer to anyone. The immunising clause is not a defensive move made when critics appeared; it was already in the instructions."@en,
        "A twelfth and a thirteenth witness were added on 2026-09-18 from the Japanese chronicle of 720, and they were owed rather than found: both stood in the same run as the eleventh, in a strand whose branch could not touch this file. The debt is prima-materia#744 and it is settled here. What the two add is one thing the other eleven do not have. In the rite at the pillar of heaven the excusing clause is not asserted by anybody — the rite produces a leech, the heavenly gods are asked by the Greater Divination why, and the answer, that the woman spoke first, is itself the output of a second procedure whose outcome nobody can establish either. And in the reign of Sujin the emperor worships exactly as the oracle instructed him and it is without effect, whereupon he bathes, keeps abstinence, purifies the hall and asks whether his ceremonies are not yet complete; a dream then supersedes the first instruction without withdrawing it. In eleven witnesses the transmission supplies the excuse; in these two it divines it."@en,
        "An eighth witness was added on 2026-09-11 and it is the only one that states the rule instead of instancing it. The internal alchemy puts the practitioner's question — he has held to the work and seen no result — at the head of its chapter on the signs of verification, and answers it twice in four characters: it is not that the Way has failed the man. In its place it supplies two conditions, both in the operator, an unenlightened master and ignorance of the times. Where the other seven have to be read off a doctrine, this one is the doctrine, and it names the possibility it excludes."@en,
        "An eleventh witness was added on 2026-09-18 from the demotic magical manual of London and Leiden, and it is the most repetitive of them. The work takes up the empty session six times in six different wordings and never once lets it stand: the boy who does not see the light, obduracy, obstinacy with the god unseen, a god who delays so as not to come in, a light that has not come forth, a light slow to come within, and gods who are inside and give no answer are each met by a further and stronger form of address. Where it does name a cause it names purity, which it calls its chief matter twice, and the fright of the boy who looked away. Both lie in the two people in the room and neither is anything an onlooker could check. What it adds to the ten is the count: this is not one excusing clause in a doctrine but the standing shape of every recipe in a working manual."@en,
        "Counter-search, carried against this holding before the statement was written, as SPEC §16 requires. Two near-misses and no refutation. The Sami material reports two practitioners who set a period, named in advance the sign they demanded, received nothing and destroyed the instrument without explaining the failure away; it does not refute, because it reports conduct and not a rule of the transmission, and the node itself flags that both cases stand in a missionary's chapter on conversion. The Zande material prescribes a corroborative second test whose contradiction voids the verdict, which is an outcome a further run can establish and looks at first like the counter-case; it does not refute either, because the same transmission carries the eight causes beside it, so that the internal check and the external excuse coexist. What that pair shows is worth more than a clean confirmation: a transmission can hold a real check and an unlimited excuse at once, and the check then bounds nothing."@en,
        "Falsified by a transmission of the kind described whose earliest witness names an outcome that would count against the operation and supplies no condition assigning a failed run to the operator, the person operated on, the material or a third party. One such case refutes it. The likeliest places to look are the transmissions this holding carries that already name an observable sign of success, since a sign that can be present can also be absent: the feather at the nostril of the eighth chapter of the Baopuzi, the gold-coloured body of the golden liquor, and the ten-day term of the medical canon."@en,
        "Fifteenth witness, added on 2026-09-22 from the American mental healing of the eighteen-sixties, and it is the only one that costs the person who states it anything. The four places the statement names are the operator, the person operated on, the material and a third party; thirteen of the fourteen earlier witnesses use one of the last three, or an operator understood as some other operator, whoever he may be. This one names the operator who is speaking. Of the mesmeric years: if a failure occurred, the fault was the operator's and not that of the subject. Of the healing: I plead his case, and if I get the verdict the criminal is set at liberty; if I fail, I lose the case."@en,
        "Fourteenth witness, added on 2026-09-22 from Renaissance learned magic, and it is the first from a printed book with a named author writing for a public. The conclusion of Agrippa's three books says that if any, through his incredulity or dulness of intellect, does not obtain his desire, he should not impute the fault to the author or say that the author erred or lied, but accuse himself, who does not understand the writings. The two causes named are properties of whoever complains, neither can be cleared in advance, and the sentence stands at the end of some two hundred chapters, where whoever has worked through them and has nothing to show meets it and no other account. What the witness adds to the statement is that the construction does not need a lineage, an initiation or a secret: it works in print, addressed to strangers."@en,
        "One further thing this witness supplies that none of the nineteen does, and it belongs here rather than at either node. The handbook states the recourse for the empty run twice over and the second time as the second station: first the seven suitabilities, and then, if it does not arise in spite of his practising, the tenfold skill in absorption. There is no third station and no sentence anywhere in the work at which the practitioner is to stop; the search that established that over twenty-three chapters stands at pmp:VisuddhimaggaTerminusSearch. So this transmission does not merely give the empty run a home, it gives it two in succession, which is the same construction one level up."@en,
        "The claim is about where the failure is put and not about whether the operations work. A transmission that supplies such a clause is not thereby making a false claim; it is making one that the ordinary check cannot reach, because the check's negative outcome has been given a home in advance. That is the difference this project exists to record, and it is a property of the wording rather than of the world."@en,
        "The consequence for the statement, put as a question the next protocol can take up. The wording presupposes that a transmission of this kind contemplates an empty run, and every one of the nineteen witnesses does. A transmission that does not contemplate it at all is a third arrangement beside supplying the excuse and letting the outcome count, and the protocol at examinations/where-the-empty-run-is-put.ttl already records two near-misses of a different shape — conduct without a rule, and a limit set before the operation. This is the third shape and it is the one closest to the statement's blind spot: silence is not an excuse, and the statement cannot tell the two apart."@en,
        "The form was described from the outside, at the same period as two of the witnesses, by a first-century critic whose account this holding carries at pmc:BiansuiDispute: he put a counter-example to a practitioner three times and was answered three times with a fresh condition. Seeing the same form printed as instruction at pmc:JinchengYaocheng is what made the generalization worth forming. The description from outside is not counted among the witnesses, because a critic reporting the move is not a transmission supplying it."@en,
        "The transmission supplies a third thing the fifteen earlier witnesses do not, and it is worth recording here rather than at either node. It penalises the report. At pmc:CmYanhuiWuyi one who meets calamity and says that serving the Dao is of no benefit thereby commits an offence of three thousand. The statement above is about where an empty run is assigned; this is one step further out, at the practitioner's testimony about the empty run, and it is stated in the operative text rather than in a gloss. A later run looking for a name for that construction should note that the same corpus also describes, at pmc:CmLibuweishi, what those who conclude that the thing does not work go on to do — burn their registers and wreck their chambers — and treats that conclusion as their further error."@en,
        "The witnesses are not of equal standing and the difference runs along one line. In the three Chinese cases the excusing clause is printed by the transmission itself, as part of the instruction or of the commentary that travels with it: nobody is being answered, and no outside observer is involved. In the three African cases it is the report of a European recorder, and two of the three recorders say so in their own voice — one holds that the practitioners themselves are responsible for the prohibitions because a prohibition leaves a reasonable excuse, and another summarises an attitude rather than quoting an informant. Observers of that period were looking for exactly this shape. The generalization therefore rests on the Chinese witnesses, where the clause is self-attested, and is corroborated with that caveat by the others."@en,
        "Twenty-fourth witness, added on 2026-10-05 from the Gabonese Bouiti, and it is here for the first half of the statement and against the second. The first half holds squarely and in the operative sentence: a candidate who looks into the mirror and does not report what is required is not told that the root was too weak or the procedure wrong, but that the fetish did not want to show itself to him and refused him, and tout est a recommencer - the whole procedure is run again. An empty run therefore counts as no failure of the operation, which is what the statement claims, and the transmission supplies the clause itself rather than leaving it to be inferred."@en,
        "Twenty-third witness, added on 2026-10-04 from the rabbinic law, and it is the one in which the lapse is quantified. A man was protected against his divorced wife's sorcery for an unstated length of time because he was careful about even numbers; one day he drank a great deal, was clear-headed to the sixteenth cup and careful, and from then on neither, and went out having drunk an even number, whereupon he burst. The condition is in the person protected, which is the second of the four places the statement names. What it adds to the twenty-two is the shape of the record it produces: a long run in which the operation was performed repeatedly and produced nothing counts as evidence for the protection, and the one run that produced something counts as evidence for the operation, so both halves are read as confirmations of two different claims. The number sixteen is supplied by the story that needs it and nobody counted for him."@en,
        "Two further properties of this witness, both of which make it worse than the register's average and neither of which the statement covers. The content of the criterion is secret and its disclosure carries a death penalty, so a candidate cannot know what he was asked and a later reader cannot check whether the same thing was asked twice: the condition is not merely unestablishable in advance, like incredulity or haste, but unobtainable in principle by anyone outside. And the repetition is unbounded - no number of attempts is fixed - so the series has no term at which the procedure could be said to have failed for a given man. The statement is about where an empty run is sent and it survives both; what they show is that a fifth place is available and that it is the least checkable of the five."@en,
        "Two witnesses added on 2026-09-23 from the Celestial Masters, and they are worth having separately because they put the empty run in two of the four named places within one transmission. At pmc:CmBinglaiFuchai the admonitions instruct that where a libationer treats an illness and it clears and then returns, this person is a bad person and is not to be treated further: the run is assigned to the person operated on, and the assignment is also the mechanism by which the case leaves the series before it can relapse twice. At pmc:CmLibuweishi the code assigns it to the operator, in the fullest failure protocol the holding carries — the officers do not serve, the pneuma does not descend, the interrogation grows daily more pressing, the sick person turns weaker and heavier by the day, and in the end there is frailty and no proof of effect — with the cause in the officiant's competence, which the same authority assesses."@en,
        "Two witnesses added on 2026-09-26 from the Turba philosophorum, read complete at the wording, and the pair is worth more than the count because the second one closes off the escape the first leaves open. At Dictum 17 and Dictum 29 the work says blame no one save yourselves and, in the same breath, that a reader who remains ignorant has had the truth concealed from him by God because his mind was not faithful — the condition in the operator, twice over, with a divine cause standing behind it. At Dictum 39 the same device is stated of a man of whom the work expressly says he knew the elements as well as the speaker, so knowledge is closed off as the variable and what carries the failure is four states of his soul: sadness, ignorance in ruling, excessive eagerness and haste. A holding that only had the first kind could be told that the transmissions are charging ignorance, which is at least a fact about a man that somebody could check. The second kind is not checkable by anybody and the work constructs it that way."@en,
        "Two witnesses added on 2026-09-29 from the Theravāda handbook, and they come from the class the falsifier of this statement named as the likeliest place to break it: transmissions that already name an observable sign of success, since a sign that can be present can also be absent. This one names two, the learning sign and the counterpart sign, and it does not let their absence count. At pmc:AppanaPresupposesSappaya the seven suitabilities are the excusing apparatus: a lodging in which the sign does not arise or in which the arisen sign perishes is thereby an unsuitable lodging; the thirty-two kinds of worldly talk lead to the vanishing of the sign; and he who keeps company with a man given to such talk loses what he had got, let alone finds the sign. At pmc:DasaAppanakosalla the handbook says in as many words that the absorption does not arise in spite of his practising, and the first of the ten remedies puts the cause in the operator: with head hair, nails and body hair long, the body soaked with sweat, an old dirty robe or a lodging full of rubbish, the meditation subject does not come to growth, increase or fulfilment."@en,
        "What distinguishes it from pmc:Shenbushi, which uses the same place. The medical canon locates the failure in a state of the patient — the body worn out, the blood exhausted, the spirit not lending itself — and a physician can at least look at the patient. Here the class is defined by a decree about the patient, so the condition is not merely unestablishable beforehand but unestablishable in principle, and its only evidence is the failure it excuses. The Confessio then runs the same construction twice more, on the Fraternity's own findability and on the seeker's disposition, and there the second outcome is death: he shall sooner lose his life in seeking and searching for us than find us. Those are entered at the node and not here, because the statement above is about an operation and not about an approach."@en,
        "What each witness supplies, in the transmission's own terms. The golden elixir: where the assay yields no gold the drug is to be sealed and fired again for the same count of days, and then it never fails to form; and where it still does not form, an unbeliever may have slandered it. The medical canon: where the treatment establishes nothing, the spirit does not lend itself, and the passage supplies no mark by which the absence could have been seen beforehand. The burial book: the commentary on the sixth adversity enumerates the ways in which nothing happens, and every one of them is a circumstance of the family rather than of the site. The Kongo corpus: a charged thing that fails is either sulky and wants a sacrifice or has lost its power and wants renewing, and both branches cost a fee. The Zande oracle: eight named causes for a self-contradiction, from the wrong variety of creeper to the anger of the ghosts, and the recorder's summary that the errors as well as the sound judgements are taken as proof of its accuracy. The Slave Coast amulet: where the thing fails at the single danger it was made against, they are never at a loss for an excuse."@en,
        "What the fifteenth witness adds is a distinction the statement can carry and did not yet display. The doctrine here is immunised exactly as the others are — a lost case says nothing against the science — and the man is not. That the transmission whose entire content is that belief makes and unmakes disease never once puts a failed treatment down to the patient's want of belief was searched for and is recorded at pmp:NewThoughtPatientsBeliefBlamedSearch; the easiest immunising clause in this register was available to it and is not used. One case in the same volume runs the other way, and the excuse there is the patient's belief in a rival advertised remedy rather than his want of belief in the healer, which is a narrower door than any of the other fourteen opens."@en,
        "What the twentieth witness costs the statement, and it is a widening rather than a refutation. The statement names four places the empty run can be sent to — the operator, the person operated on, the material, a third party. Of the two witnesses added here one uses the first and one uses the fourth, so both fall inside the enumeration. But the same passage sends the empty run to three further places that are none of the four: the lodging, the season and the posture, each of which is to be judged by whether the sign arises in it and each of which is settled by a probation of three days. A circumstance is not an operator, a subject, a material or a third party, and the statement as worded cannot hold them. The sentence is left standing and the gap is recorded, on the same principle as the fifteenth and nineteenth witnesses: a claim is corrected where it is examined, and a successor statement should read the fourth place as any party or circumstance other than the operation itself."@en,
        "Where it falls outside the statement is the middle clause, and that clause is the one prima-materia#812 reports as having fallen three times in three examinations while the edges never fell. This is the fourth fall and it is a new place rather than a near miss. The condition is not in the operator - the Ganga conducts the procedure and is not blamed; not in the person operated on - nothing says the candidate lacked merit, was impure or was careless, and he is not disqualified; not in the material - the root did its work, the candidate was dead drunk for three days; and not in a third party, because nobody else is present in the clause. It is in the invoked power itself, and the invoked power is the thing whose efficacy the operation would demonstrate. That makes the assignment circular in a way none of the twenty-three is: the authority that explains why the procedure produced nothing is the authority the procedure exists to reach. The two Celestial Masters cases come nearest, where the officers do not serve and the pneuma does not descend, and they are not the same, because there the non-arrival is the symptom and the cause is put in the officiant's competence, which the same authority assesses. Here there is no further cause and none is sought."@en,
        "Witness added on 2026-09-30 from the Confessio Fraternitatis, and it is the shortest statement of the construction this holding carries: although there be a medicine to be had which might fully cure all diseases, nevertheless those whom God hath destined to plague with diseases, and to keep them under the rod of correction, such shall never obtain any such medicine. The condition is in the person operated on, which is the second of the four places the statement names, and it is supplied in advance and in the same sentence as the promise."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:AgrippaCulpaLectoris,
        pmc:AmuletFailureIsAlwaysExcused,
        pmc:AppanaPresupposesSappaya,
        pmc:BengeFailureCauses,
        pmc:BouitiMirrorReportDecidesAdmission,
        pmc:ClaviculaDefectusOperantis,
        pmc:CmBinglaiFuchai,
        pmc:CmLibuweishi,
        pmc:DasaAppanakosalla,
        pmc:FailureIsSulkinessOrLostPower,
        pmc:JinchengYaocheng,
        pmc:NeidanFeidaofuren,
        pmc:PdmCompulsioNonAdventus,
        pmc:PesahimTheDivorcedWifesSpell,
        pmc:QuimbyCulpaOperantis,
        pmc:RosicrucianMedicinaDestinatisNegata,
        pmc:SanjiLiuxiong,
        pmc:ShangqingShijieZhengbian,
        pmc:Shenbushi,
        pmc:ShintoFutomaniCausaDefectus,
        pmc:ShintoSujinIteratio,
        pmc:TurbaCulpaInOperante,
        pmc:TurbaFestinantiaCausaDefectus,
        pmc:YinshuKouchi ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission prescribes an operation whose success cannot be established apart from the operation itself, the same transmission supplies a condition under which a run that produced nothing counts as no failure of the operation, and it locates that condition in the operator, in the person operated on, in the material, or in a third party, rather than in an outcome that a further run could establish."@en .

pmc:NorseAsaOkAlfaSkil a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Kennen der Unterschiede aller Asen und Alben"@de,
        "Knowing the distinctions of all gods and elves"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hávamál 159 (Guðni Jónsson; 160 in Bellows), in Eddukvæði. Sæmundar-Edda, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:definition "The fourteenth song: if the speaker must tell over the gods before a company of men, he knows the distinctions of all the Æsir and elves; few unwise men know so."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmc:NorseEggjarDeyfi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Stumpfen der Schneiden des Feindes"@de,
        "Blunting the edges of the enemy"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hávamál 148 (Guðni Jónsson; 149 in Bellows), in Eddukvæði. Sæmundar-Edda, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:definition "The third song, for when there is great need of a fetter against foemen: the speaker blunts the edges of his adversaries, and neither weapon nor staff bites on them."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmc:NorseFjarlaegLond a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "In einem Augenblick in ferne Länder fahren"@de,
        "Going in one moment to far lands"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Snorri Sturluson, Ynglinga saga, chapter 7, in Heimskringla, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity claimed for the shape-changing: to go in a single moment to distant lands, on one's own errands or on another man's, in the form of a bird, a beast, a fish or a serpent."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmc:NorseFjoturrSprettr a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Fessel springt von den Füßen"@de,
        "The fetter springs from the feet"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hávamál 149 (Guðni Jónsson; 150 in Bellows), in Eddukvæði. Sæmundar-Edda, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:definition "The fourth song, for when men lay bonds on the speaker's limbs: he chants so that he may walk, the fetter springs from his feet and the shackle from his hands."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmc:NorseGedOkGaman a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Gewinnen des ganzen Sinnes und Vergnügens einer klugen Frau"@de,
        "Winning the whole mind and pleasure of a wise woman"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hávamál 161 (Guðni Jónsson; 162 in Bellows), in Eddukvæði. Sæmundar-Edda, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:definition "The sixteenth song: if the speaker will have the whole mind and pleasure of a wise maid, he turns the thought of the white-armed woman and changes all her feeling."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmc:NorseHallaerisSpa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Teuerung währt nicht länger als diesen Winter"@de,
        "The dearth will not last beyond this winter"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Eirik the Red's Saga, chapter 4, translated by J. Sephton (Liverpool: D. Marples, 1880)",
        "Eiríks saga rauða, chapter 4, in Íslendinga sögur, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:definition "The prediction given after the song: that this dearth will not hold longer than through the winter and the season will better as spring comes, and that the sickness which has lain on them will also mend sooner than looked for."@en ;
    skos:note "A prediction with a term in it, which is what separates it from the rest of the chapter. It names a season by which the change must have come and a comparison, sooner than expected, for the second half. Both could have failed within the year, in front of the household that heard them."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmc:NorseHatrBaett a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Beilegen des Hasses unter den Fürstensöhnen"@de,
        "Bettering hate among the sons of princes"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hávamál 153 (Guðni Jónsson; 154 in Bellows), in Eddukvæði. Sæmundar-Edda, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:definition "The eighth song, which the poem says it is useful for all to learn: wherever hate grows among the sons of a prince, the speaker can better it straightway."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmc:NorseHeilirHildar a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Heil zur Schlacht und heil von ihr"@de,
        "Whole to the battle and whole from it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hávamál 156 (Guðni Jónsson; 157 in Bellows), in Eddukvæði. Sæmundar-Edda, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:definition "The eleventh song: if the speaker must lead old friends into battle, he chants under the shields, and they fare in power whole to the fight and whole from the fight, and come whole from wherever they go."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmc:NorseHjalp a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Hilfe gegen Klagen und Sorgen"@de,
        "Help against suits and sorrows"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hávamál 146 (Guðni Jónsson; 147 in Bellows), in Eddukvæði. Sæmundar-Edda, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:definition "The first song, called Help, which will help against suits and sorrows and every kind of grief. The stanza adds that the song is known neither to a king's wife nor to any man's son."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmc:NorseLaeknar a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Was die brauchen, die als Heiler leben wollen"@de,
        "What those need who would live as leeches"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hávamál 147 (Guðni Jónsson; 148 in Bellows), in Eddukvæði. Sæmundar-Edda, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:definition "The second song, which the sons of men need who would live as leeches. The stanza states the need and not the effect, and is the only one of the eighteen that does so."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmc:NorseMeinAftrSnuit a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Übel frisst den Absender statt mich"@de,
        "The harm eats the sender rather than me"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hávamál 151 (Guðni Jónsson; 152 in Bellows), in Eddukvæði. Sæmundar-Edda, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:definition "The sixth song: if a man wounds the speaker with the roots of a raw tree, the harm eats that man who called down hatred on him rather than the speaker."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmc:NorseSalrLogi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Löschen des Hallenbrandes über den Bankgenossen"@de,
        "Quenching the hall-fire over the benchmates"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hávamál 152 (Guðni Jónsson; 153 in Bellows), in Eddukvæði. Sæmundar-Edda, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:definition "The seventh song: if the speaker sees a high flame over the hall of his benchmates, it does not burn so broad that he cannot save them; he knows that charm to chant."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmc:NorseSeidrErgi a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "So viel ergi folgt der Kunst, dass Männer sie nicht ohne Schande ausüben konnten"@de,
        "So much ergi goes with the craft that men could not use it without shame"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Snorri Sturluson, Ynglinga saga, chapter 7, in Heimskringla, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson",
        "The Heimskringla, translated by Samuel Laing (London 1844), Ynglinga saga, chapter 7" ;
    skos:definition "The caution Snorri attaches to the craft: with this sorcery, when it is performed, goes so much ergi that it did not seem to men without shame to deal with it, and the craft was taught to the priestesses."@en ;
    skos:note "Laing renders ergi as weakness, which is too mild to carry the clause: the word denotes unmanliness in the sexually passive and dishonouring sense, which is why the consequence is shame and not incapacity. The German label keeps the Norse word for the same reason the identifier does."@en,
        "The consequence named is social and not operative: not that the craft fails in a man's hands, but that a man cannot handle it skammlaust, without shame. What follows from it in the text is a redirection of the teaching and not a limit on the effect."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:NorseSeidrIthrott ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmc:NorseSeidrErgiStrittig a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob Männer die Kunst ausübten"@de,
        "Whether men practised the craft"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Snorri Sturluson, Haralds saga ins hárfagra, chapter 36, in Heimskringla, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson",
        "Snorri Sturluson, Ynglinga saga, chapter 7, in Heimskringla, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson",
        "The Heimskringla, translated by Samuel Laing (London 1844), Ynglinga saga chapter 7 and Harald Harfager's saga chapter 36" ;
    skos:note "The counter-search was carried against the caution and it came back full, from the same author and in part from the same chapter. Three passages tell against it. The sentence that introduces the craft says that Óðinn performed it himself, ok framdi sjálfr, immediately before the clause about shame. Two sentences after the clause, the same chapter says he taught most of his crafts to the blótgoðar, the sacrificing priests, who are men, and that they were next to him in all lore and sorcery. And Haralds saga ins hárfagra has male practitioners in the open: Rǫgnvaldr réttilbeini, the king's own son, learned sorcery and became a seiðmaðr on Haðaland, and Vitgeirr in Hǫrðaland answered the king's order to stop with a public verse saying it is small harm that we practise it, children of churls and of old women, when Rǫgnvaldr réttilbeini does, Haraldr's own son, on Haðaland."@en,
        "The dispute is not resolved here and the two sides are not of the same kind. What the caution states is a norm; what the narratives show is the norm being broken and enforced, since the same chapter ends with Eiríkr burning his brother indoors together with eighty seiðmenn, and the deed much praised. A norm with a death penalty attached is evidence for the norm and not against it."@en,
        "The part that is genuinely against the caution is Vitgeirr's answer, because he is not ashamed. He replies in verse, publicly, to a king, and his defence is not denial but comparison. That is a man treating the craft as ordinary and saying so where it could be heard."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:NorseSeidrErgi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmc:NorseSeidrOrlogOkUordna a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Wissen um die Schicksale der Menschen und das noch nicht Geschehene"@de,
        "Knowing men's fates and what has not come to pass"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Snorri Sturluson, Ynglinga saga, chapter 7, in Heimskringla, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson",
        "The Heimskringla, translated by Samuel Laing (London 1844), Ynglinga saga, chapter 7" ;
    skos:definition "What Snorri says the craft called seiðr gave: by it he could know the fates of men and things that had not yet happened, and also bring men death, misfortune or sickness, and take wit or strength from men and give it to others."@en ;
    skos:note "Four capacities in one sentence and they fall into two kinds: knowing, and transferring. The second kind is the only place in this transmission where a quantity is said to be taken from one person and given to another."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmc:NorseSeidrPresupposesVardlokkur a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Der seiðr setzt einen zweiten Kundigen voraus, der das Lied kann"@de,
        "The seiðr presupposes a second person who knows the song"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Eirik the Red's Saga, chapter 4, translated by J. Sephton (Liverpool: D. Marples, 1880)",
        "Eiríks saga rauða, chapter 4, in Íslendinga sögur, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:note "The hostile frame strengthens rather than weakens the node. The same chapter has Guðríðr refuse at first because she is a Christian woman and has her father leave the house while such hindrvitni is practised. A recorder who disapproves has no reason to invent a condition that makes the proceeding depend on a Christian woman's help."@en,
        "The load-bearing node of this file and the reason the work was opened. A practice whose outcome is stated to depend on a second, competent participant is rare, and this one names the failure twice: once as a condition asked for in advance and once as a report of what the spirits did before it was met."@en,
        "The one who supplies the condition is not a practitioner. Guðríðr says outright that she is neither versed in magic nor a woman of knowledge, and she agrees only under pressure from her host and after being told she would be no worse a woman for it. What the condition requires is the song, not the office."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The seeress states the consequence in her own words after the fact: before the song, the spirits wished to part from us and to give us no obedience, and the things now plain to her were hidden. The narrative states it a second time by acting on it, since the séance halts and the whole farm is searched for anyone who knows the song."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:NorseAudsynir ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:NorseVardlokkur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmc:NorseSeintFirrask a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die junge Maid wird mich nur langsam meiden"@de,
        "The young maid will be slow to shun me"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hávamál 162 (Guðni Jónsson; 163 in Bellows), in Eddukvæði. Sæmundar-Edda, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:definition "The seventeenth song, that the young maid will be slow to shun him; to which the stanza adds that Loddfáfnir will long be in want of these songs, though they would be good for him if he got them, useful if he took them, needful if he received them."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmc:NorseThjodrerisLjod a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kraft den Asen, Ruhm den Alben, Verstand dem Hroptatýr"@de,
        "Might to the Æsir, fame to the elves, thought to Hroptatýr"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hávamál 160 (Guðni Jónsson; 161 in Bellows), in Eddukvæði. Sæmundar-Edda, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:definition "The fifteenth song, which the dwarf Þjóðrerir chanted before the doors of Delling: he chanted might to the Æsir, fame to the elves, and understanding to Hroptatýr."@en ;
    skos:note "The only one of the eighteen attributed to a named singer other than the speaker, and the only one whose beneficiaries are not men."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmc:NorseTunridurVillar a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Irreführen der Zaunreiterinnen aus ihren eigenen Gestalten"@de,
        "Sending the hedge-riders astray from their own shapes"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hávamál 155 (Guðni Jónsson; 156 in Bellows), in Eddukvæði. Sæmundar-Edda, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:definition "The tenth song: if the speaker sees hedge-riders playing aloft, he so works that they go astray from their own home-shapes and their own home-minds."@en ;
    skos:note "The one song in the catalogue whose object is another practitioner in flight, and the only place in it where the poem names a state the counterpart must return to: a home-shape and a home-mind. The displacement is assumed and the harm is that the return fails."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmc:NorseVardlokkur a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Lied namens Varðlokkur"@de,
        "The song called Varðlokkur"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Eiríks saga rauða, chapter 4, in Hauksbók, AM 544 4to, in the diplomatic edition of Finnur Jónsson (Copenhagen 1892-1896)",
        "Eiríks saga rauða, chapter 4, in Íslendinga sögur, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:definition "The lore needed for the seiðr, called Varðlokkur, which the seeress asks for by name and which no woman on the place knows; Guðríðr has it from her foster-mother Halldís in Iceland and calls herself neither versed in magic nor a woman of knowledge."@en ;
    skos:note "Hauksbók calls what Halldís taught a kvæði, a poem, and Skálholtsbók calls it fræði, lore. The difference matters for what is required of the second participant: a poem can be recited from memory, lore has to be understood."@en,
        "Hauksbók writes Varðlokkvr with a double k, which is the reading verified here in the diplomatic print of Finnur Jónsson; Skálholtsbók is written varð lokr in two words and is normalised by its editor. The two spellings carry two derivations — from loka, to lock the spirits in, or from lokka, to entice them — and the narrative supports the second, since the spirits are said to have come to it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmc:NorseVatniVerpa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der mit Wasser Besprengte fällt nicht in der Schlacht"@de,
        "The man sprinkled with water does not fall in battle"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hávamál 158 (Guðni Jónsson; 159 in Bellows), in Eddukvæði. Sæmundar-Edda, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:definition "The thirteenth song: if the speaker must sprinkle a young man with water, he shall not fall though he come into the host, and that warrior shall not sink before swords."@en ;
    skos:note "The one claim in the catalogue with an outcome that a later generation could check, because the man either falls in battle or does not. The sprinkling is done once and the protection is stated without a term."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmc:NorseVindKyrring a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Stillen des Windes und Einschläfern der See"@de,
        "Stilling the wind and putting the sea to sleep"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hávamál 154 (Guðni Jónsson; 155 in Bellows), in Eddukvæði. Sæmundar-Edda, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:definition "The ninth song: if need presses the speaker to save his ship afloat, he stills the wind on the wave and puts the whole sea to sleep."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmc:NorseVirgilnaMaelir a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Gehenkte geht und spricht mit mir"@de,
        "The hanged man walks and speaks with me"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hávamál 157 (Guðni Jónsson; 158 in Bellows), in Eddukvæði. Sæmundar-Edda, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:definition "The twelfth song: if the speaker sees a corpse of the gallows swinging up in a tree, he so cuts and colours in runes that the man walks and speaks with him."@en ;
    skos:note "The only one of the eighteen that names the operation as well as the effect, and it is carving and colouring rather than singing. It is also the only one whose outcome is a report from a second party, which is what makes it the most examinable of the catalogue in principle and the least reachable in fact."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmc:OccultaVidere a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sehen, was sich verbirgt, und jeden sehen"@de,
        "Seeing what conceals itself, and seeing everyone"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Atharvaveda Saṃhitā 4.20 verses 2, 4, 6, 8 and 9, in the translation of W. D. Whitney, revised by C. R. Lanman, Harvard Oriental Series 7, Cambridge, Mass. 1905" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity the hymn asks of the plant, in three widths. All beings: three skies, three earths, and these six directions severally — by thee let me see all beings. Every person: may the thousand-eyed god set it in my right hand; with it do I see every one, both who is Śūdra and Aryan. And the concealed in particular: show me the sorcerers, show the sorceresses, show all the piśācas; with this intent I take hold of thee, O herb. The hymn closes by claiming the act done — I have seized out of his shelter the sorcerer, the kimīdin — and demands that whatever flies through the atmosphere, creeps across the sky, or thinks the earth a refuge be shown forth."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because it is the plainest claim of an acquired perception this corpus offered in the hymns opened, and because it is of a kind the holding already carries from elsewhere: a state in which a practitioner reports on what is not available to ordinary sight. It differs from those in the means, which is an object worn and not a state entered, and in having no condition of any kind attached to it."@en,
        "The seeing is its own sign of success and there is no other. The hymn names nothing the wearer should observe to know that the amulet is working, and nothing he should observe to know that it is not; the sorcerers it reveals are by definition those nobody else can see. Counted on the sign side and not on the condition side, per SPEC §15."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Atharvavedic .

pmc:OfficeEnteredThreeWays a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Drei Wege in das Amt"@de,
        "Three ways into the office"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III" ;
    skos:definition "By succession, the son inheriting from the father, and where the heir is too young the nearest kinsman standing in until he is fit. By election, where succession fails, the other ministers choosing whom they judge fittest with the agreement of the lords. Or by a man taking the office himself, which is said to be ordinary among the old for the lesser offices of diviner and healer, and to be done for a living."@en ;
    skos:note "The third way is entered with the others because the recorder gives it the same weight and because it is the one a later reader would drop. A transmission that admits self-appointment for its lesser offices is saying where its own boundary is soft."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmc:OloliuhquiAnswer a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wonach das Orakel befragt wird"@de,
        "What the oracle is asked for"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulo VI, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity the consultation is undertaken for: to be told whatever the questioners wish to know, with no restriction of subject stated in the passage."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NahuaRitual .

pmc:OphiteHebdomasNominata a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die sieben Namen der Hebdomas und ihre Tore"@de,
        "The seven names of the hebdomad and their gates"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.31-32 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)" ;
    skos:definition "The set of seven names the diagram gives the rulers of the gates: Ialdabaoth, Iao, Sabaoth, Adonaeus, Astaphaeus, Aloaeus and Horaeus. Origen adds that three of them, Ialdabaoth, Astaphaeus and Horaeus, are taken from magic, and four, Iao, Sabaoth, Adonaeus and Eloaeus, from the Hebrew scriptures, where they are names of one and the same God."@en ;
    skos:note "Aloaeus and Eloaeus are taken here as one bearer under two spellings of the printed text, since VI.31 addresses Aloaeus and VI.32 lists Eloaeus among the seven while omitting Aloaeus. The identification is the compiler's and is stated so that it can be denied."@en,
        "The gates are counted from below. Horaeus holds the first, Aloaeus the second, Astaphaeus the third, Sabaoth the fifth realm, and Ialdabaoth is addressed as first and seventh. The fourth and the sixth are never numbered in the passage."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:OphiteGnosis .

pmc:OphiteMichael a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Michael, der Löwengestaltige"@de,
        "Michael the lion-like"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.30 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:nameForm "Michael" ;
    pm:nameRole "first of the seven ruling demons of the diagram, lion-like; honoured in scripture as the angel of the Creator" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:OphiteGnosis .

pmc:OpposedDirectionsOfPranaAndApana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die beiden Winde werden zugleich in entgegengesetzte Richtungen getrieben"@de,
        "The two winds are driven in opposite directions at once"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I.50 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.47 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "The apāna is raised from below while the prāṇa is pressed down from the throat, and the two are brought together in the region of the navel."@en ;
    skos:note "The manual assigns direction to the two winds, not to the two phases of breathing. Both directions run in the same operation and against each other, so a scheme that reads inhalation as upward and exhalation as downward, or the reverse, has no purchase here. See prima-materia#27 and prima-materia#28."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:OracleRankOrder a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Rangordnung der Orakel"@de,
        "The graded order of the oracles"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 164" ;
    skos:definition "Where more than one oracle is consulted the lesser is always consulted before the greater, in the order rubbing-board, termites, poison. A verdict of the termites is not put to the rubbing-board for confirmation, and a verdict of the poison oracle is not put to the termites. On all important matters the decision of the termites must be corroborated by the poison oracle."@en ;
    skos:note "The order is a rule about the direction in which confirmation may run and not merely a ranking by esteem. It is what makes the cheaper oracles usable at all: a man narrows the field with the termites and spends poison only on the final decision."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:PalmsAreTheSendingOrgan a pm:Positing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Handflächen sind das Sendeorgan"@de,
        "The palms are the organ of sending"@en ;
    skos:definition "The claim that what passes from one person to another passes through the palms, and not through some other part indifferently."@en ;
    skos:note "The force of the claim is in the exclusion. That the hands are used in transmission is reported widely and is not at issue; that they are the organ, so that another part would not serve, is the draft's addition and the part that can fail."@en ;
    pm:positedIn "prima-materia#314" .

pmc:Panchatattva a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die fünf Tattvas des heroischen Ritus"@de,
        "The five tattvas of the heroic rite"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Mahānirvāṇa Tantra I.58-59 (trans. A. Avalon, Tantra of the Great Liberation, London 1913)",
        "Mahānirvāṇa Tantra I.61 (trans. A. Avalon, Tantra of the Great Liberation, London 1913)" ;
    skos:definition "The five elements of the rite belonging to heroic practice, named in the source as wine, meat, fish, parched grain, and the union of man and woman."@en ;
    skos:note "The fourth element is parched grain and not a ritual gesture. Wilson renders the same list in 1832 with mudrā glossed as gesticulations of the fingers and maithuna as women; the divergence is his and not the text's, and it matters because grain makes that element a food where a gesture would make it an act. Recorded so that the current gloss can be checked against a verse instead of against another summary. See prima-materia#53."@en,
        "The same passage carries its own caution, and the caution is not a Western addition: the source says that the men of this age, being greedy and lustful, will neglect the discipline, drink for the pleasure of it, and lose the distinction of right and wrong. The warning against the rite stands inside the text that prescribes it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmc:ParalysisIsTheRegularShutdown a pm:Positing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Lähmung ist die reguläre Abschaltung der Motorik am Schlafübergang"@de,
        "The paralysis is the regular shutdown of movement at the threshold of sleep"@en ;
    skos:definition "The claim that what is called sleep paralysis is the ordinary switching off of motor control that happens as sleep is entered, and therefore a regular part of the architecture of sleep rather than a disorder."@en ;
    skos:note "The weaker of the two claims in this file and separated from the other on purpose. This one places the event and calls it regular; pmc:ParalysisPrecedesItsNotice adds that it is universal and that only its noticing is rare. The two are examined apart because they fail apart, and one of them does."@en ;
    pm:positedIn "prima-materia#137" .

pmc:ParalysisPrecedesItsNotice a pm:Positing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Lähmung liegt bei jedem vor, selten ist nur ihr Bemerken"@de,
        "The paralysis is present in everyone and only its noticing is rare"@en ;
    skos:definition "The claim that the motor paralysis at the threshold of sleep is a regular feature of every sleeper rather than a disorder of some, and that it becomes noticeable only where waking awareness outlasts the body's entry into it."@en ;
    skos:note "Split deliberately from the weaker claim that sleep paralysis is the regular shutdown of motor control at the sleep transition, which is prima-materia#137 and stands on its own. This one adds the two things that make it testable: a universality and an explanation of why a universal thing is rarely reported."@en ;
    pm:positedIn "prima-materia#138" .

pmc:PdmHoraEtLuna a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Stunde und Mondtag, an denen der Vorgang geschehen darf"@de,
        "The hour and the day of the moon on which the operation may be done"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. III/15, col. VI/7, col. IX/17, col. X/21-22, col. XXI/19, col. XXIII/22-23, col. XXVII/37, col. XXIX/2-3 and col. verso IX/12, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "The work fixes several of its operations to a time and not to a state of either party. The vessel-inquiry with the eye-ointment is done from the fourth day of the lunar month until the fifteenth, which is the half-month when the moon fills the uzat. The divination opposite the moon is addressed to it on the fifteenth day when it fills the uzat, the operator having been pure for three days beforehand. The inquiry of the lamp must be done at midday in a place without light. The vessel-inquiry with the boy is carried until the time of the seventh hour of the day; another is done at the third hour of the evening. The inquiry of the sun is made at the moment the sun rises and comes up entire with the whole disk. Outside the inquiries: the priest's procedure is said opposite the Shoulder constellation on the third day of the month; the scarab is done at the rising of the sun, and repeated on the third of the lunar month; a love charm is done on the fourteenth of the lunar month; and the amulet for a gouty foot is made when the moon is in the constellation of Leo."@en ;
    skos:note "Nine datings in one manual, and not one of them is stated with a consequence for acting outside the window. The work binds the operation to a time as firmly as any transmission in this holding and says nothing at all about what going outside the time would produce, which is the half of the construction that a reader supplies by himself if the node does not say so."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmProbatioGraviditatis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wie man an einer Frau erkennt, ob sie schwanger ist"@de,
        "The way to know of a woman whether she is with child"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso V/4-8, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A test standing among the medical recipes of the verso. The woman passes her water on a named herb in the evening; when the morning comes, if the plant is found scorched she will not conceive, and if it is found flourishing she will conceive. Both readings are stated, in consecutive clauses, and the outcome is a state of a plant on the following morning."@en ;
    skos:note "The identity of the herb is not established here. The translation says on this herb as above again, referring back to a plant described in the preceding entries of the same column, and the editors note a similar prescription in a nineteenth-dynasty text. What the node claims is the form of the test and its two readings, which is what the wording carries; the botanical identification and the modern work on this test were not opened in this run and are prima-materia#740."@en,
        "The most checkable claim in the whole work and the only one in it whose criterion neither party can produce at will, whose two sides are both stated, and whose verdict falls due at a named time without any further address being prescribed. It stands in the same manuscript as the inquiries, written by the same hand, which is why it is worth a node beside them rather than in a file of medicine: the transmission that cannot register a failed inquiry can state a falsifiable prognosis three columns later."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmPuerIntactus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Knabe, der noch nicht bei einer Frau war"@de,
        "The boy who has not yet gone with a woman"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. III/11, col. XXV/20-21 and col. XXVII/15, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "The condition fixed on the medium and repeated in three separate recipes: a boy, pure, before he has gone with a woman; a boy who has not yet gone with a woman is he whom you make come before you; the boy standing before the lamp, he being pure and not having gone with a woman. A fourth passage requires in addition that the child be one who has been tested in his ears before."@en ;
    skos:note "A condition of the body that cannot be acquired and that the boy himself is the only witness to. Set beside the ear test, the two requirements on the medium are a state he cannot demonstrate and a sign he reports from inside his own head; neither is anything an onlooker could check, and together they are the whole of his qualification. Nothing in the work asks whether he has answered correctly before."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmPuritas a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Reinheit, zweimal als die Hauptsache genannt"@de,
        "Purity, named twice as the chief matter"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XVII/26 and col. XXIX/30, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "The condition the work names above all others, and the only one for which it states the consequence of the want in plain words: if it be that you do not apply purity to it, it does not succeed, its chief matter is purity; and at the end of the recto, of the inquiry of the sun, its chief point is purity. What the word covers in the recipes is a room cleansed with natron water, new and untouched objects, a boy who has not gone with a woman, and an operator who is pure and who in several places has spoken to no one on earth since rising."@en ;
    skos:note "The papyrus nowhere says how purity is to be ascertained, by whom, or what would show that it had lapsed. It is therefore the perfect assignment for an empty run: available in every case, verifiable in none, and named by the text itself as the one thing everything depends on."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmRamshauFormaDuplex a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Komm als Priester, wenn es geschieht, als Kalashire, wenn nicht"@de,
        "Come as a priest if it will happen, as a Kalashire if it will not"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. VIII/12-16 and col. IX/17-18, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A direct inquiry ascribed to the voice of Pasash the priest of Kes, which the informant is said to report as tested nine times. The operator declares himself Ramshau son of Tapshau and lays down in advance which of two appearances is to mean which answer: if the thing in question shall happen, the god is not to come with his face of Pekhe but in his form of a priest, in his figure of a servant of the temple; but if it shall not come to pass, he is to come in his form of a Kalashire. The formula is said seven times opposite the Shoulder constellation on the third day of the month, over a clove of three-lobed white garlic pierced by three iron needles, which is then put at the operator's head; then he attends to the operator and speaks with him."@en ;
    skos:note "The criterion is announced to the participant before the session and states both sides, which is the combination this holding had found nowhere. In the ten witnesses gathered at pmc:TheCriterionNoPartyControls the two-sidedness always belongs to the other column, the one where the deciding event is a physical process; where the criterion is an announced appearance the transmissions state the success side alone. Here the appearance is announced, the two forms are distinct and mutually exclusive, and each is tied to one of the two answers before anything is seen."@en,
        "What weakens it, stated so that it is not read as more than it is. The appearance occurs in a dream or a vision belonging to one person, so nobody but the operator can say which form came; there is no provision for the case in which neither form comes; and the promise that he attends to you and speaks with you means the session can still be carried by speech if the forms fail. The finding is therefore about the wording of the criterion and not about a practice that could be checked, which is the limit pmc:TheCriterionNoPartyControls states of itself."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PerformanceFailsWithoutASusceptibleAudience a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Vorstellung misslingt, wenn das Publikum nicht folgen kann"@de,
        "The performance fails if the audience cannot follow"@en ;
    dcterms:source "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXVII, sect. 122, pp. 332-333" ;
    skos:definition "Where too many of those present are not susceptible, the audience reacts too slowly and the performance may fail. It may still be carried out formally, as a sequence of well known actions, without attaining its aim. The presence of a single person who remains outside may suffice to disturb the rest."@en ;
    skos:note "He notes in the same section that a performance staged outside its milieu is psychologically impossible to bring off, because there is no audience cooperating and no purpose in shamanizing, and describes such a staging before an ethnographical society as a farce. That is a warning by the recorder against the conditions under which his own discipline usually saw the thing it described."@en,
        "The condition of failure is located outside the performer, which is rare. The recorder adds that public opinion is nonetheless inclined to see the cause in the shaman, and that this is one of the reasons shamans resort to tricks in order to gain an indifferent audience. Both of those are his reading; the failure and its stated cause are reported."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SiberianShamanism .

pmc:PesahimFourActsEachWithAnExit a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Vier Handlungen, die das Leben verwirken, jede mit ihrem Ausweg"@de,
        "Four acts that forfeit a life, each with its way out"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Pesahim 110b-111a, Resh Laqish on the four acts, with the qualifications attached to each, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. II (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1901), pp. 707-708" ;
    skos:definition "Resh Laqish names four things whose doer has his blood on his own head and forfeits his life: relieving oneself between a palm and a wall, passing between two palms, drinking borrowed water, and stepping over spilt water, even water his wife spilt in his presence. The discussion then attaches to each a set of conditions under which there is nothing in it: four cubits between palm and wall, or another way available; a public road separating the two palms; water borrowed by an adult rather than a child, or borrowed in town rather than in the field, or wine and beer rather than water; earth strewn on the spilt water, or spittle on it, or the sun having passed over it."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered for its form and not its content. A danger is stated in the strongest available terms and then dismantled clause by clause by the same discussion, until what is forbidden is a narrow case reached by several coincidences. This is the third shape of the same movement on these pages - narrowing after an objection at pmc:HolinWhiteMuleClaimNarrowedTwice, discharge by class at pmc:PesahimPairsExemptions, and here exhaustive qualification of a stated penalty - and the three together are the material pmc:TheObservationAnsweredByNarrowing is formed from."@en,
        "The difference from the other two is that here no counter-observation is reported. Nobody says, we see however that people do this and live. The qualifications are supplied without being asked for, which makes this the weakest of the three as a witness and the reason the generalization does not rest on it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliPesahim ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmc:PesahimPairsExemptions a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wen und was die Paare nicht bedrohen"@de,
        "Whom and what the pairs do not threaten"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Pesahim 110b, R. Awira on dishes and loaves and what follows, with the limitation of the numerical exemption to the danger from demons, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. II (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1901), pp. 706-707" ;
    skos:definition "The list of discharges the transmission attaches to the danger. R. Awira: with dishes and loaves pairs are not to be feared, and the rule is that what was made by human hands is exempt while what was made by heaven is not; in a tavern they are not to be feared; if one intends to drink another they are not to be feared; a guest need not fear them; a woman need not fear them, though a woman of rank must. Beside these stand the numerical discharges - ten, eight, six and four do not count as pairs - and the scope limit the discussion then puts on them: that limit holds only for the danger from demons, while sorcery is to be feared even at a large number."@en ;
    skos:note "The scope limit is the sharpest part and it runs the other way. Having discharged the higher numbers, the discussion re-imposes them for sorcery, so the same count is safe against one agency and unsafe against another, with no way of telling which agency one is dealing with before the harm. The case at pmc:PesahimTheDivorcedWifesSpell is then cited as the proof of exactly that clause, which makes the case load-bearing rather than illustrative."@en,
        "The structure is what the node is for. A danger is asserted, and then every class of case in which it would be easy to look for it is discharged: domestic settings, hospitality, half the population, and any number above three. What remains inside the rule is a man drinking alone at home and keeping count. The transmission is not hiding this; it is reasoning its way to it clause by clause, and each clause is given a ground."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliPesahim ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmc:PesahimTheCountingContestToAHundredAndOne a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Zaehlwettstreit, der bis hundertundeins lief, und der Daemon barst"@de,
        "The counting contest that ran to a hundred and one, and the demon burst"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Pesahim 110a, the remedy of the crossed thumbs and the case that ran to a hundred and one, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. II (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1901), pp. 704-705" ;
    skos:definition "The remedy for a man who has forgotten and gone out having drunk an even number, and the one case the transmission reports of its outcome. He takes the thumb of his right hand in his left and the thumb of his left in his right and says: you and I are three. If he hears it said, you and I are four, he calls back, you and I are five; if he hears, you and I are six, he calls back, you and I are seven. The report attached is that such a case once ran up to a hundred and one, and the demon burst."@en ;
    skos:note "Nothing says what to do if the exchange does not terminate, and nothing says what a man should conclude who hears no answer at all. The second silence is the larger of the two: the whole remedy presupposes that he hears the other party counting."@en,
        "The structure is a contest in which one party always holds the odd number and wins by outlasting the other, and it is the one place on these pages where an operation of this transmission has a definite perceptible outcome that somebody could have seen. The holding's register of escalating address stands at generalizations/the-stronger-address.ttl, whose statement is that a transmission meeting a non-appearance with a further and stronger address of the same kind states no term, count or sign at which the operator is to stop. This is the same escalation with the opposite ending: no term is stated here either, and one reported run terminated, with a count and a result."@en,
        "What makes the count worth entering rather than merely quoting. A hundred and one is odd, which is the side the human holds, so the number is not a round figure but the parity the procedure requires; and it is reported of one case rather than prescribed, so the transmission is not promising that a hundred and one will do it. A later protocol reading this as a fixed threshold would be reading a case report as an instruction."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliPesahim ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmc:PesahimTheDangerOfPairs a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Gefahr der Paare bei Speise und Trank"@de,
        "The danger of even numbers in what is eaten and drunk"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Pesahim 110a-110b, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. II (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1901), pp. 703-707" ;
    skos:definition "The warning the whole of this part of the tractate turns on: an even number of cups, eggs, nuts or cucumbers exposes the person who consumed them to harm, from demons and from sorcery, and the harm reported in the cases is death. The transmission carries it as a live rule with remedies, counts, exemptions and case reports attached, and not as a saying."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as a caution rather than as a concept because the passage is addressed to conduct throughout: it tells the reader what to do when he has lost count, what to say, whom it does not threaten and where it is not observed. The capacity it defends against is not named in the usual way; what is named is an occasion."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:PesahimWhoeverHeedsIsHeeded,
        pmp:PesahimCounterFormulaAgainstTheSorceresses ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliPesahim ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmc:PesahimTheForgottenItemOfTheList a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zwei Eier, zwei Nuesse, zwei Gurken und noch etwas, und das noch etwas ging verloren"@de,
        "Two eggs, two nuts, two cucumbers and something else, and the something else was forgotten"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Pesahim 110b, R. Dimi on what was handed down at Sinai, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. II (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1901), pp. 705-706" ;
    skos:definition "R. Dimi's report of what the rule of pairs rests on, with its own gap in it. Of two eggs, two nuts, two cucumbers and something else it was handed down to Moses at Sinai; and because the sages did not know what that something else was, they imposed a prohibition on all pairs on account of it. A received list of four is transmitted with its fourth member lost, and the loss is handled by extending the prohibition over everything the lost member might have been."@en ;
    skos:note "A transmission reporting its own loss and saying what it did about it. The first file carries the other instance of the same honesty at pmc:SanhedrinThreeHundredRulesOnTheWitch, where three hundred rules on one verse are claimed and two are said to have reached the next generation. The two differ in what follows from the loss. There the loss is simply stated; here it generates law, and the law is wider than anything the lost item could have required. A rule whose scope is set by the size of a gap in its own warrant cannot be narrowed by any later finding, because the finding would only identify the fourth member and leave the other three in force."@en,
        "It also fixes what the exemptions at pmc:PesahimPairsExemptions are exemptions from. The underlying danger is a list of named foodstuffs in pairs; everything beyond that list is there because of the forgotten member, which is why the discussion can discharge whole classes - what human hands made, a tavern, a guest, a woman - without touching the warrant."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliPesahim ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmc:PesahimTwoWomenFacingEachOther a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zwei Frauen, die sich einander zuwenden, befassen sich zweifellos mit Zauberei"@de,
        "Two women sitting opposite each other are without doubt engaged in sorcery"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Pesahim 111a, the two women in the street and the three remedies, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. II (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1901), pp. 708-709" ;
    skos:definition "A criterion applied from outside, by a passer-by, to decide that an operation is going on. If two women sit in the street, one on one side and the other on the other, and turn their faces towards each other, they are without doubt occupied with sorcery. The remedy is graded by what is available: if there is another way, take it; if there is not and somebody else is present, the two of them take each other by the hand and pass; if nobody else is there, one says, Agrath, Azlath, Usij and Belusij are slain by arrows."@en ;
    skos:note "Nothing in the passage says what would show that the two women were not doing it, and the remedies are graded by the bystander's circumstances rather than by any feature of the women. So the criterion decides every case the same way and the response is scaled to the observer. It belongs with pmc:HolinWhiteMuleClaimNarrowedTwice as the other end of the same transmission's range: a claim that excludes nothing, here from the start rather than after two narrowings."@en,
        "The first file's finding was that this transmission supplies a criterion applied to a claimed effect from outside, by somebody who is neither the operator nor the person operated on. This is the same instrument put to a different question. There the question was whether a deed had been done and the criterion was a residue; here the question is whether a deed is being done and the criterion is a posture. The second is much weaker - two women facing each other across a street is a common sight and the sentence says without doubt - and the pair is useful because it shows the transmission using the same forensic habit at two very different strengths."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliPesahim ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmc:PgmIvMemoriaInfallibilis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Erschoepft an der Seele, und das Orakel ohne Fehl erinnernd"@de,
        "Drained in soul, and remembering the oracle without a slip"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the close of the rite and the instruction for magical use (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 16 lines 1-7)" ;
    skos:definition "What the appendix says of the operator once the god answers: he prophesies at once; the operator will be drained in soul and not in himself while he is being answered; the god speaks the oracle in verses and departs, and the operator stands there speechless. He will take all of it in of itself, and will then remember without deviation what the archangel said, even if the oracle ran to ten thousand verses."@en ;
    skos:note "A capacity stated with a quantity, which is rare enough to enter on that ground alone. Unerring recall of an utterance of any length is checkable in principle by anyone who has the utterance, and unlike the announced sights it is not a private appearance: the oracle comes in verses, and verses can be written down and compared."@en,
        "It is also the only place in the piece where the operator is said not to be in himself. The liturgy proper has him acting throughout — drawing breath, hissing, bellowing, greeting each power by name — and here, at the one moment when something is received rather than done, the agency is stated to be gone."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PgmIvProbatioKentritidis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wie die Pflanze zu erkennen ist: die Feder, deren Fahne bei der Beruehrung abfaellt"@de,
        "The way the herb is known: the feather whose barbs drop off at a touch"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the teaching of the operation (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 19 lines 9-11)" ;
    skos:definition "The recognition of the plant, given after its season and its habitat and a comparison with the upright vervain. The knowledge of it comes about thus: the black tip of an ibis feather is slackened and smeared with the juice, and at the mere touching the barbs fall off."@en ;
    skos:note "How the plant came to be known is reported as a revelation and the test is not: the lord pointed it out, and the plant was found in a named district at a named place beside another named plant. The finding is credited to the god; the way of checking a specimen is given as a procedure anybody could run."@en,
        "It is a test of the ingredient and not of the operation, and that is where the interest lies. The same corpus carries a second of the kind, the assay of the white stone at pmc:PdmProbatioLapidis, and there too the test falls on what is bought rather than on what is done. Neither work states any comparable test for the apparition, the far-shining sight or the immortalization."@en,
        "The botanical identification is not part of the claim. The editor's friend, printed in the addenda of this same edition, argues the plant is a species of Ruscus and states that the name occurs nowhere else in the literature he knows. That is modern work and enters this holding only as pm:evidenceFrom at a protocol, never as a source here."@en,
        "The one place in this piece where something is stated that could come out the other way. It is a test of a material with a definite outcome on an object neither the operator nor a client controls: either the barbs fall or they do not, and the text attaches the identity of the plant to that and to nothing else. The whole rest of the piece tells the operator what he will see; here it tells him what to do to find out."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PgmIvTerInAnno a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Dreimal im Jahr, und verwirkt für den, der die Anweisung überhört"@de,
        "Three times a year, and forfeited by whoever disregards the instruction"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the appended instruction for magical use (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wünsch, Leipzig 1910)" ;
    skos:definition "The clause appended to the rite: this immortalization takes place three times in the year, and if anyone should wish, after the instruction has been given, to disregard it, it will no longer come about for him."@en ;
    skos:note "It stands outside the liturgy proper, in the instruction for magical use that follows it in the papyrus, which is why a reading that stops at the end of the liturgy reports the rite as carrying no condition of failure. The same appendix later has the god himself change the frequency, and that revision is now a node of its own at pmc:PgmIvUsusRepetitusEtCorrectio, where the author also reports having worked the operation many times. A rule that the text alters within itself is a rule that somebody was following; where the authority for the alteration comes from is the second half of the finding and belongs at the node, not in a remark here."@en,
        "The one stated condition of failure in this piece, and it is not a sign to be watched for but a forfeiture. It does not say that the operator will see something different when it fails; it says that the thing will not happen at all, and it names the cause — disregard of the instruction after receiving it. That is checkable in the weak sense that the tradition can be held to it, and it is more than the announced sights offer, since none of them can fail to arrive in a way the text acknowledges."@en,
        "Two further constraints stand with it and are not conditions of success: for a second person to hear what is said, that person breathes with the operator for seven days and abstains from animal food and the bath; and the operator, when the god answers, is drained in soul and not in himself, while remembering unerringly what was said even if the oracle ran to ten thousand lines."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:PgmIvSpiritusTractus ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PgmIvTraditioClausa a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Vater darf es weitergeben, und nur an einen Sohn"@de,
        "A father may hand it on, and only to a son"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.475-829, the father's prayer (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1907)" ;
    skos:definition "The restriction stated in the opening prayer: that these are mysteries which a father alone may hand on, and that only to a son, an initiate worthy of the craft; the rite is for one person alone, who is to raise himself."@en ;
    skos:note "It sits oddly beside the rite itself, which is expressly solitary and needs no officiant. What is withheld is therefore the text and not the performance, and a text that has been in print since nineteen hundred and seven is no longer withheld by it."@en,
        "The same gate the project's criterion is aimed at, in a third transmission after the Ars Notoria and the ophite requirement of a curse before admission. The restriction here is narrower than either: not an order, not a school, but one person to one person."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:PgmIvSpiritusTractus ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PgmIvVisusTelaugos a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Er wird weithin leuchtend sehen, so dass du dich wundern wirst"@de,
        "He will see far-shiningly, so that you will marvel"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the teaching of the operation (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 18 lines 6-9)" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity claimed for the anointing of another's face: that person will see far-shiningly, the text says, so that the operator will marvel; and the author adds that he has found no greater operation than this in the world."@en ;
    skos:note "The superlative is the author's own and not a later editor's, and it is worth keeping because it sits next to the one condition of failure this piece states. A writer who calls something the greatest operation in the world has said what he thinks is at stake and has not said what would count against it."@en,
        "What the promised seeing is remains unstated. The word says far-shining or bright to a distance and the text names no object, no duration and no way of telling the seeing from ordinary sight. Beside the ordered list of announced sights in the liturgy proper, this is the same kind of claim with the list removed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PistisSophiaTransitusVisu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Herrscher sehen ihren eigenen Namen, und ihre Bande lösen sich"@de,
        "The rulers see their own name and their bonds fall away"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pistis Sophia 11-14 (trans. C. Schmidt, GCS 13, Leipzig 1905)" ;
    skos:definition "The account repeated at every stage of the ascent: the gates open of themselves, the rulers are shaken by the great light, they look at the light-garment and see on it the mystery that holds their names, they are struck with fear, all their bonds loose, each leaves his own order, and they fall down and worship, saying that the Lord of the All has passed through them without their knowing it."@en ;
    skos:note "The sequence is stated four times with the same wording, at the firmament, the first sphere, fate and the twelve aeons, with the light increasing forty-ninefold at each step. A repetition that exact is a form, and the form says that passage here is recognition and not negotiation."@en,
        "This is the same event as an ophite gate and its opposite in method. There the passer addresses the ruler by name and office and asks to pass; here nothing is said at all. The ascending one is not even seen — the sources say the disciples and the rulers saw only the light — and what releases the bonds is the ruler reading his own name on someone else's clothing."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PistisSophia .

pmc:Pneuma a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Pneuma"@de,
        "Pneuma"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chrysippus, in Galen, De placitis Hippocratis et Platonis V.3.8" ;
    skos:definition "A mixture of air and fire that pervades bodies and, by its tension, holds them in cohesion; a principle of the world rather than a capacity of a practitioner."@en ;
    skos:note "The associated doctrines are that of tension and that of total mixture, by which two bodies occupy the same place throughout."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:StoicPhysics .

pmc:PneumaticVehicle a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das pneumatische Seelenfahrzeug"@de,
        "The pneumatic vehicle of the soul"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Synesius of Cyrene, De insomniis" ;
    skos:definition "A subtle vehicle carried by the soul, whose purity is held to decide whether what appears in a dream is clear and true or clouded."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NeoplatonicTheurgy .

pmc:PoTolo a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Pô tolo, der Digitaria-Stern"@de,
        "Pô tolo, the Digitaria star"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 284 and 287" ;
    skos:definition "A star named after the fonio grain, said to be white where Sirius is seen red, to be the first thing created, to be the egg of the world out of which everything visible and invisible developed, and to consist of a metal called sagala. It is called at once the smallest of all things and the heaviest, weighed in the account at four hundred and eighty donkey loads, so that all earthly beings together could not lift it."@en ;
    skos:note "The number four hundred and eighty is glossed in the article itself as the product of the base eighty and the count of tens in the older base sixty, and is said to be used as the symbol of the greatest of numbers. A reader who takes it for a measured mass is reading against the note printed beneath it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Dogon .

pmc:PoToloAsAxis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Same-Stern als Achse der Welt"@de,
        "The seed-star as the axis of the world"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), p. 287" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that the orbit of the seed-star lies at the centre of the world, that without its movement no heavenly body could hold its place, and that it orders the position of Sirius in particular, which is called the most disordered of the stars."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Dogon .

pmc:PopDzam a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Matte und der Thron"@de,
        "The mat and the throne"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ralph L. Roys, The Book of Chilam Balam of Chumayel (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1967), chapter IX, pp. 92-93" ;
    skos:definition "The office of a town chief, named in the source by the two objects handed over with it, together with the first wand of office. The candidate who has understood comes before his lord on bended knees and receives them."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MayaRitual .

pmc:Prana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Prāṇa"@de,
        "Prāṇa"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chāndogya Upaniṣad 1.11.5",
        "Chāndogya Upaniṣad 7.15.1 (Sanskrit; received prapāṭhaka-khaṇḍa numbering)" ;
    skos:definition "Vital breath, presented as that on which the parts of the person rest and out of which beings come forth, in the image of a wheel resting on its hub."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Upanisadic .

pmc:PrincipeDeLaDoctrine a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Zugehörigkeit eines Grundsatzes zur Lehre"@de,
        "A principle's standing as part of the doctrine"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Allan Kardec, La Genèse, les miracles et les prédictions selon le spiritisme, chapitre I, articles 51-54" ;
    skos:definition "What the control decides. The transmission holds that the teaching had to be collective and not individual, that the spirits divided the work by scattering the subjects of study across many places, and that a centre formed of itself to compare what came in and to distinguish an individual opinion from the general teaching."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Spiritism .

pmc:QizhiYouxiao a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Ankommen des Qi und das zurückgewiesene Zeichen"@de,
        "The arrival of the qi, and the sign that is refused"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 9 (Zhong shi), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 2" ;
    skos:definition "The Lingshu states what it means for the qi to have arrived and to have had effect, and states it as a rule over the vessel and not over the patient. Where the operation drained, the vessel is to be as large as before but no longer hard; if it is hard as before, then although the patient says it is as it was, the illness has not gone. Where the operation supplemented, the vessel is to be as large as before and harder; if it is as before and not hard, then although the patient says he feels relieved, the illness has not gone. The chapter closes the rule from the other side: even where the pain does not follow the needle, the illness must be in retreat."@en ;
    skos:note "The reason for entering this rather than the many other success signs in the corpus. A success sign told to the practised beforehand is worth little, because he will report it; this passage names such a report and refuses it twice over, in the same sentence in which it gives the criterion that replaces it. The corpus therefore holds a stated distinction between what a patient says and what counts as the operation having taken, and the distinction runs against the interest of the operator."@en,
        "What the passage does not supply is a way of reading the vessel that two examiners would read alike. The criterion is hardness under the finger, which is why this is entered as a sign the tradition names and not as a procedure the graph can use."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmc:RasaGuptavidya a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Geheim gehalten ist sie stark, und ein dem Publikum bekanntes Mittel wird unwirksam"@de,
        "Kept secret it is strong, and a medicine known to the public becomes ineffective"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. II, p. 25, with ch. I, p. 7, ch. I, p. 11 footnote, and the preface, p. ix" ;
    skos:definition "The knowledge of metallurgy is to be kept secret from those who are not disciples. It becomes powerful if cultivated in secret; otherwise it loses much of its strength. The preparation and composition of a medicine should not be taught to a patient or to the public in general. A medicine which is very familiar to a patient and to the public becomes ineffective. The same chapter forbids teaching the science to those who are not disciples, to atheists and to the dishonest, and the compiler notes at the worship of the two deities that for the detailed procedure a disciple should depend upon the instructions given by his preceptor."@en ;
    skos:note "Read beside the compiler's account of his own learning, the arrangement is complete: he says he learnt from a yogi much more than could be found in the existing books, each of which is incomplete, incoherent, incorrect and in many cases misleading. The part that is not in the books is with a person, and the part that is in the books is declared weaker for being there."@en,
        "The clause does not say that a published medicine will be misused or debased; it says the medicine stops working. That puts the efficacy of the substance into the relation between the patient and the knowledge, and it does so in a printed book sold for six rupees which prints the composition of some fifty preparations. The volume therefore holds at once that the operation is in the book and that what is in the book loses its strength, and it does not reconcile them."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RasaHemabhasaVibheda a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Gemachtes Gold und Metall, das nur wie Gold aussehen gemacht wird"@de,
        "Gold that is made, and metal that is only made to look like gold"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. V, pp. 238 and 247, with ch. VIII, pp. 321-337" ;
    skos:definition "Mercury exhausted with a quarter of its weight of the amalgam and without any gold or silver can make base metals appear like gold, but cannot transform them into real gold. The chapter on the making of gold states the fineness of each product in sixteenths — ten sixteenths, twelve carats, fourteen sixteenths — and says what must be added to bring it to purity."@en ;
    skos:note "Beside it stands the instruction that base metals transformed into gold by mercury should be kept buried in the earth for a fortnight before they are taken out for sale in the market. The volume gives no reason for the delay and none is supplied here."@en,
        "The transmission drawing the distinction that its critics would draw, inside its own instructions, and then stating its results in a measure any goldsmith of its time could check. A claim reported in sixteenths of fineness is a claim about an assay, and this is the sharpest example in the volume of an outcome stated in a quantity somebody outside the transmission could dispute."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RasaPramanapatra a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die am Ende des Bandes abgedruckten Zeugnisse"@de,
        "The testimonials printed at the end of the volume"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, the unnumbered advertisement leaves following the glossary" ;
    skos:definition "The volume closes with some ten signed statements of cure, each with the writer's name, office and address and most with a date: a magistrate whose blood pressure was found normal after fifteen days of treatment, two professors of Lucknow University, a company secretary whose father's five-year blood pressure yielded in two months, a cashier whose granddaughter's three-year fever and cough were cured, two brothers cured of malignant typhoid, a relative cured of kala-azar with an enlarged spleen, a man cured of rheumatic pain culminating in almost complete paralysis, and a chronic asthma of twenty-five years cured by a named preparation. The compiler's own note is appended to one of them, and the section ends: it is needless to produce more proofs of the unprecedented success with which we have been curing all sorts of diseases; no disease is incurable with us."@en ;
    skos:note "The arrangement — an operative handbook that states no case in three hundred pages and prints signed testimonials in its back matter — is the one this holding already records from the printed household charm-books of Europe, at examinations/hohman-testimonials.ttl. Two printed handbooks from two centuries and two continents with the same division of labour between the text and its advertisement is worth the comparison, and the comparison is not made here."@en,
        "The cases the body of the volume does not contain are all here, and they are of a different kind: none of them names a preparation of the eighteen operations except one, none states what was taken or in what dose, and every one is written by the person cured or by a relative. They report the practitioner and not the procedure. That is why they stand in a node of their own and are not counted among the claims of the transmission."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:RetentionOfTheDescendingNectar a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Zurückhalten des herabfließenden Nektars"@de,
        "The retention of the descending nectar"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.51-52 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.77 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "The stopping of a fluid held to fall from above and to be consumed below, whose loss the manual makes responsible for ageing and death."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:ReturnIsImmediate a pm:Positing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Rückkehr erfolgt unmittelbar durch Denken an den Körper"@de,
        "The return is immediate, by thinking of the body"@en ;
    skos:definition "The claim that the practitioner comes back at once by turning his attention to his body, without the passage the outward journey required."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered separately from the movement claim because it asserts something the movement claim does not: an asymmetry between going and returning. If the outward journey also happened by willing, there would be nothing to say about the return that was not already said."@en ;
    pm:positedIn "prima-materia#306" .

pmc:RightAndLeftHandNomenclature a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Wiedergabe der beiden Śākta-Zweige als rechtshändiges und linkshändiges Ritual"@de,
        "The rendering of the two Śākta branches as a right and a left hand ritual"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1832" ;
    dcterms:source "H. H. Wilson, Sketch of the Religious Sects of the Hindus, Asiatic Researches XVII (Calcutta 1832), the sections headed Dakshinas, or Bhaktas and Vamis, or Vamacharis" ;
    skos:definition "The English naming of the dakṣiṇācāra and vāmācāra divisions of the Śākta sect as the followers of a right hand and a left hand ritual, together with the characterisation of the second as impure, as running contrary to what is usual, and as something its adherents will not publicly avow."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as an ordering act because the naming is regularly credited to Blavatsky, who wrote in 1877 and in 1888. It stands in Wilson forty-five years earlier, in the volume of the Asiatic Researches for 1832, in those terms and with the moral charge already attached. Whoever first coined it, it was not she, and a record that repeats the later attribution reports a reception as an origin. See prima-materia#57."@en,
        "No pm:ordersClaim is given, and the reason has changed. It used to be that the division this naming orders stood nowhere in the graph as a claim of its own. Since pmc:KalikapuranaLeftAndRightMethods it does, in a pre-colonial text, and the naming is therefore no longer the only witness to a pair. What is still missing is the link: Wilson orders what he found in Bengali Śākta circles of his own century, and that it is the same division the Assamese purāṇa states has not been shown. Asserting the link would make a shared vocabulary into a shared claim, which is the error this file exists to record. See prima-materia#53."@en,
        "The same section carries a ranking the naming does not survive. Wilson translates a passage he ascribes to the Kulārṇava that grades the Veda lowest and ascends through the Vaiṣṇava and the Śaiva to the right hand Śākta, then sets the left hand above the right, the Siddhānta above that, and the Kaula above all. In the material being ordered, the left is therefore the higher of the two; the English rendering keeps the pair and reverses the valuation. The verse is not located in an edition of the Kulārṇava here and is not held as a claim of that work. See prima-materia#56."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:compiledBy "Horace Hayman Wilson" .

pmc:Romanca a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Haaraufrichtung"@de,
        "Horripilation"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhagavadgītā 1.29",
        "Nāṭyaśāstra VII" ;
    skos:definition "The raising of the body hair, counted among the eight involuntary signs and treated as a mark of an inner state, not as something to be produced for its own sake."@en ;
    skos:note "Attested outside the dramaturgical treatise in the epic corpus, where trembling and horripilation are named together as signs of an overwhelming state."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SanskritDramaturgy .

pmc:RomanusProbaAdIncredulum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wer es nicht glauben will: steche ein Thier, es wird nicht bluten"@de,
        "For whoever will not believe it: stab an animal and it will not bleed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Romanus-Büchlein, in Johann Scheible (ed.), Das Kloster, weltlich und geistlich, vol. III (Stuttgart 1846), pp. 489-523, here p. 503" ;
    skos:definition "The demonstration the booklet offers against disbelief, printed immediately after the blood-staunching it belongs to: but whoever will not believe it, let him write the letters on a knife and stab an unreasoning animal, and it will not bleed."@en ;
    skos:note "It refutes the second limb of pmc:TheTestOnTheIngredient, written into this holding on 2026-09-19 and named there as falsifiable by exactly this shape. The protocol at pmc:TheTestOnTheIngredientExamination carries the deviation. What survives of that statement is the first limb and an observation about where handbooks put their assays most of the time; what does not survive is the claim that an assay of the operation always comes with its escape."@en,
        "The cost of the test falls on a third party that cannot refuse it, and the holding should say so plainly: the thing that establishes the charm is a wound inflicted on an animal for the purpose of settling an argument. That is a property of the demonstration and not a reason to leave it out; a transmission which will put its claim at risk, and which puts it at risk in this way, has told two things about itself at once."@en,
        "This is a test of the operation itself and not of a material it consumes, it names an outcome that could come out the other way, and the booklet attaches to that outcome neither a rule of repetition nor a cause lying outside the procedure. Nothing is said about what a bleeding animal would mean. The sentence is addressed to the doubter, which is what makes it a demonstration rather than an instruction: the operator who already believes has no occasion to run it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RomanusBuechlein ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:RootCutting a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wurzelschneiden"@de,
        "Root-cutting"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "The gathering and preparation of plant material for pharmacological or operative use."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:RosicrucianAurumParergon a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Goldmachen ist ein Nebenwerk"@de,
        "The making of gold is a by-product"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Fama Fraternitatis, in the English of Thomas Vaughan (London, 1652), the passage on the accursed gold-makings; with Confessio Fraternitatis, the passage on the tincture of metals, and Chymische Hochzeit (Strasbourg, 1616), the Sixth Day, the seventh storey" ;
    skos:definition "The Fama's judgement on the art its readers were expecting. Men of discretion nowadays hold the transmutation of metals to be the highest point and fastigium in philosophy; the Fraternity publicly testifies that the true philosophers are far of another mind, esteeming little the making of gold, which is but a parergon, for besides that they have a thousand better things. The founder's saying is quoted for it: aurum nisi quantum aurum. The philosopher does not rejoice that he can make gold, nor that the devils are obedient unto him, but that he sees the heavens open and his name written in the book of life."@en ;
    skos:note "The Chymische Hochzeit puts the same judgement as an event rather than a rule, at the seventh storey: the other artists were well pleased because they were to work in gold, which is indeed a piece of this art but not the most principal, most necessary and best, and they were given a part of the ashes so that they should imagine the whole bird had been provided for the sake of gold. The warning of the manifestos is there enacted as a deliberate deception of the practitioners who value the wrong thing."@en,
        "The Confessio does not withdraw this and does not simply repeat it either; it adds an order. The gift is in no manner to be set at naught or despised, but because it does not always bring the knowledge of nature with it, excellent wits ought not to be drawn to the tincture of metals before they be exercised well in the knowledge of nature. So the two works together demote the art and then prescribe when it may be taken up."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:FamaFraternitatis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmc:RosicrucianImaginesAnimatae a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zwei kleine menschliche Körper gemacht und dann beseelt"@de,
        "Two small human bodies made and then given souls"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chymische Hochzeit Christiani Rosencreutz (Strasbourg, 1616), the Sixth Day, the seventh storey of the Tower of Olympus" ;
    skos:definition "The one operative capacity the corpus narrates being exercised. On the seventh floor of the tower the ashes of the beheaded bird are moistened, heated, cast in two moulds, and opened as two transparent images four inches long, a male and a female, lithe and fleshy and without life; they are then fed the bird's blood drop by drop until they reach full human size, and the soul is afterwards let into each of them three times through a kindled trumpet directed at a hole in the roof."@en ;
    skos:note "The same passage says of the other guests that they were told they were to work in gold, which is indeed a piece of this art but not the most principal, most necessary and best, and that they were given a part of the ashes so that they should imagine the whole bird had been provided for the sake of gold. That is the Fama's parergon clause put as an event, and it stands at pmc:RosicrucianAurumParergon."@en,
        "What the narrator says about the lights on the table is the reason this node is here rather than in a file of allegories. He states that the old man set the torches so that the four workers should not observe when the soul entered, that they would not have noticed it, and that he himself saw the hole open and a bright stream of fire pass down the tube because he had twice before seen the flames — and that he let his three companions keep their own belief. A text that distinguishes what the operator was allowed to see from what happened has made a claim about a procedure and not only told a story."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:NuptiaeChymicae ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmc:RosicrucianMedicinaDestinatisNegata a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die allgemeine Medizin erlangt nie, wer zur Plage bestimmt ist"@de,
        "The universal medicine is never obtained by those destined to be plagued"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Confessio Fraternitatis, in the English of Thomas Vaughan (London, 1652), the conclusion; with Fama Fraternitatis, the closing passage" ;
    skos:definition "The Confessio's clause on its own remedy, stated in one sentence: although there be a medicine to be had which might fully cure all diseases, nevertheless those whom God hath destined to plague with diseases, and to keep them under the rod of correction, such shall never obtain any such medicine."@en ;
    skos:note "The Fama states the third member of the series in the same form, and there the outcome is named: he that is false-hearted, or only greedy of riches, shall not be able in any manner of wise to hurt us, but bring himself to utter ruin and destruction; while whosoever shall earnestly and from his heart bear affection unto the Fraternity, it shall be beneficial to him in goods, body and soul. Two outcomes, assigned by a disposition of the seeker, and no way to establish the disposition except from the outcome."@en,
        "The immediately following sentence extends the same construction from the medicine to the Fraternity itself: even in such manner, although we might enrich the whole world and endue them with learning, yet shall we never be manifested and made known unto any man without the especial pleasure of God; yea, it shall be so far from him whosoever thinks to get the benefit without and against the will of God, that he shall sooner lose his life in seeking and searching for us than find us. So the seeker who does not find has the same two outcomes available to him as the sick man who is not cured, and one of them is death."@en,
        "The sentence is constructed so that no case of the medicine failing can arise. If a man is cured the medicine worked; if he is not, he was destined to be plagued, and the destination is knowable only from his not being cured. The work supplies no antecedent mark of the destined class, and the search that establishes that absence is at pmp:RosicrucianDignitasSearch."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ConfessioFraternitatis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmc:RosicrucianQuatuorViae a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Vier Wege angeboten, drei begehbar, und kein Weg zurück"@de,
        "Four ways offered, three passable, and no way back"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chymische Hochzeit Christiani Rosencreutz (Strasbourg, 1616), the Second Day, the Latin tablet on the three cedars, Quatuor viarum optionem per nos tibi sponsus offert" ;
    skos:definition "The tablet on the cedars, printed in Latin in the 1616 edition, offers the traveller a choice of four ways to the royal court. The first is short but dangerous and leads into rocky places scarcely passable. The second is longer and takes him about; it is plain and easy if by help of the magnet he turns neither left nor right. The third is the royal way, which affords a joyful journey, and it has so far scarcely been allotted to one in a thousand. By the fourth no man shall reach the place, because it is a consuming way, practicable only for incorruptible bodies. Whichever he enters is the one destined for him by immutable fate, and he cannot go back in it save at great peril to life."@en ;
    skos:note "The stated rate is what makes this passage examinable at all, and it is also the place to be careful. One in a thousand is said of the allotment of the third way and not of the outcome of the journey, so it is a claim about how the ways are distributed among travellers and not about how many arrive. The work gives no figure of the second kind until the third day, where it gives one for the weighing instead."@en,
        "Three things in this passage are of a kind this holding rarely gets from one place. A stated rate: the royal way has scarcely been allotted to one in a thousand, in the German of 1616 das vnter tausent ich eben der sein solte der den Koeniglichen Weg erwehlte. A way declared impassable in advance and offered anyway. And an irreversibility with a stated cost, which the narrator then tests: having entered a way by accident, he turns and finds a wind so strong against him that he concludes it would cost him his life, and goes on."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:NuptiaeChymicae ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmc:RosicrucianSeptemPondera a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sieben Gewichte, von denen eines benannt ist und sechs nicht"@de,
        "Seven weights, of which one is named and six are not"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chymische Hochzeit Christiani Rosencreutz (Strasbourg, 1616), the Third Day, the seven weights, with the parenthesis on the fourth weight at the banquet of the same day" ;
    skos:definition "What the work says the weights are. It does not say. The seven are described by size and by their disproportionate heaviness, and the sentence of the following judgement grades the punishments by which of them a guest failed, so the numbering is operative. Only one is identified, and incidentally: the narrator declines to name his own place in the order lest it be interpreted as pride in him, which he says is expressly against the fourth weight."@en ;
    skos:note "The asymmetry is the finding. A trial whose grading is published weight by weight, and whose weights are named once out of seven and only in a parenthesis, gives its candidates a scale they cannot prepare against and gives its readers a scale they cannot reconstruct. That is the same construction as the smallest fault against unseen laws at the cedars, one level in."@en,
        "Various eminent virtues beyond the narrator's hopes were found in many: one could stand out this, the second another, some two, some three, four or five, but few could attain to the just perfection. So the weights measure virtues, in the plural and severally, and the work says so without saying which."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:NuptiaeChymicae ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmc:Rtsa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kanäle"@de,
        "Channels"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)" ;
    skos:definition "The channels of the subtle body through which the winds are held to move; Tsongkhapa's presentation counts one hundred and twenty principal ones."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:TibetanTantric .

pmc:Ruach a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ruach"@de,
        "Ruaḥ"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Sefer Yetzirah 1:9-12 (Short Version; numbering of Kaplan 1997)",
        "Sefer Yetzirah 6:7 (Short Version; numbering of Kaplan 1997)" ;
    skos:definition "Breath or spirit, named as the first of the ten emanations, from which air issues, from air water, and from water fire."@en ;
    skos:note "The citation was extended from 1:9-10 to 1:9-12 when the passage was reopened: in this numbering the first emanation is breath, the second breath from breath, and only the third and fourth carry water from breath and fire from water. The earlier range stopped two verses short of the sequence the definition states. The numbering is recension-dependent throughout, and the long and Saadia versions divide the same material otherwise."@en,
        "The text also has the patriarch stirring the letters with breath, which is the closest the source comes to breath as an operative medium; it remains an act of formation, not a conduction through the body."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SeferYetzirah .

pmc:SacrificiumAlienumCorruptum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sie sollen seine Wahrheit mit Unwahrheit schlagen, und es soll nicht gelingen"@de,
        "Let them smite his truth with untruth, and let not that meet with success"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Atharvaveda Saṃhitā 7.70 verses 1, 2 and 4, in the translation of W. D. Whitney, revised by C. R. Lanman, Harvard Oriental Series 7, Cambridge, Mass. 1905" ;
    skos:definition "The same failure seen from the other side, and taught as an operation. Whatsoever he yonder offers with mind, and what with voice, with sacrifices, with oblation, with sacred formula, that let perdition, in concord with death, smite, his offering, before it comes true. The sorcerers, perdition, also the demon — let them smite his truth with untruth; let the gods, sent by Indra, disturb his sacrificial butter; let not that meet with success which he yonder offers. And: with the fury of divine Agni, therewith have I smitten thine oblation."@en ;
    skos:note "The consequence for the examination of anything in this corpus is direct. Once a transmission teaches that a rite can be spoiled from outside by an unseen party, every rite in it that produced nothing has an account available which no bystander can check, and the account is the transmission's own and not a later apology. The counterpart hymn, imprecating against enemies who thwart holy work, was not read at the wording in this run."@en,
        "This is the finding the run did not go looking for and the reason the registration entrance exists. The corpus states a mechanism by which a rite correctly performed by somebody else is destroyed, names the substance of the attack — untruth put in the place of the truth the rite is made of — and names the moment, before it comes true. Where other transmissions in this holding assign an empty result to the operator, to the time or to the material, this one supplies a third party who was working to produce exactly that result, and it supplies him from the attacker's desk."@en,
        "Whitney records that only the first two verses are found in the Paippalāda, and that both stand also in the Taittirīya Brāhmaṇa with variants. The mechanism is therefore attested outside this collection as well as inside it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Atharvavedic .

pmc:Saktipata a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Herabkunft der Kraft"@de,
        "Descent of power"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Abhinavagupta, Tantrasāra XI",
        "Abhinavagupta, Tantrāloka XIII",
        "Mālinīvijayottaratantra" ;
    skos:definition "The transfer of power from one who has it to one who does not, graded in the Kashmirian exegesis into nine degrees."@en ;
    skos:note "The sources give transfer by glance, by touch and by word. The specifically manual modality of the modern draft is not attested here."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmc:SamyamaYieldsDivyaSrotra a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Saṃyama bringt das göttliche Ohr hervor"@de,
        "Saṃyama yields the divine ear"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.41" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Samyama ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PatanjalaYoga ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:DivyaSrotra .

pmc:SamyamaYieldsParacittaJnana a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Saṃyama auf die Vorstellung bringt Kenntnis fremden Bewusstseins hervor"@de,
        "Saṃyama on the presented idea yields knowledge of another mind"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.19" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Samyama ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PatanjalaYoga ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ParacittaJnana .

pmc:SanhedrinBloodAndDungAsTheTraceOfTheDeed a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "War hinterher Blut und Mist da"@de,
        "Was there blood and dung afterwards"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Synhedrin 67b, Rav's report of the Arab and the camel and R. Hiyya's question, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. VII (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1903), p. 287" ;
    skos:definition "A criterion stated as a question put to an eyewitness. Rav told R. Hiyya that he had seen an Arab cut a camel to pieces with a sword, strike a drum before it, and the camel rise. R. Hiyya asked him whether afterwards there was blood and dung; since there was not, it was nothing further than a holding of the eyes. The performance is judged not by its effect but by the residue a real deed would have left."@en ;
    skos:note "The limits of it, stated because the node is strong. It discriminates one class of performance, the transformation or destruction of a body, and nothing else; it is applied here once, by one authority, to one report; and the report it is applied to is a first-person account of a show seen in passing, so what the criterion decides is the status of a memory and not of a repeatable procedure. The transmission draws no rule from it either: nothing in the part read says that a court must ask this question, and the next node shows the same discussion applying a different criterion with the opposite premise."@en,
        "This is what the counter-search was carried for, and it is the node the holding did not have. Every other criterion in the holding is a sign announced to a practitioner beforehand, which he then reports; this one is a trace, it is named by a third party who did not see the performance, it is negative, it costs nothing to check, and the man who saw the show cannot supply it by expecting it. It therefore belongs beside examinations/the-sign-and-the-counterfeit.ttl and not among the signs of success."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliSanhedrin ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmc:SanhedrinCalfCreatedAndEaten a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein dreijaehriges Kalb, erschaffen und dann gegessen"@de,
        "A three-year-old calf, created and then eaten"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Synhedrin 67b, the third class of Abaye's division, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. VII (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1903), p. 287" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity claimed for that practice: the creation of a three-year-old calf, which the two then ate. The eating is the part that carries the claim, because it is what distinguishes the calf from an appearance; nothing else in the sentence says the animal was real."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliSanhedrin ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmc:SanhedrinFieldFilledAndGathered a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Feld, mit Gurken gefuellt und wieder geleert"@de,
        "A field filled with cucumbers and emptied again"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Synhedrin 68a, R. Eliezer on his deathbed, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. VII (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1903), p. 289" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity claimed: a whole field filled with cucumbers at one word, and the same cucumbers gathered into one place at a second. Both directions are stated, which is unusual; the reversal is asked for by the pupil as a second lesson and granted."@en ;
    skos:note "R. Eliezer says in the same speech that he knows three hundred rules, by another reading three thousand, on the planting of cucumbers, and that nobody ever asked him about them except Akiba. The capacity is thus reported as the surviving fragment of a body of technique, which is the same shape as the Tosefta's report at pmc:SanhedrinThreeHundredRulesOnTheWitch, with a different object."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliSanhedrin ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmc:SanhedrinLearningIsPermittedToUnderstandAndToTeach a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Es zu tun darfst du nicht lernen, wohl aber darfst du es lernen, um zu verstehen und zu lehren"@de,
        "To do it you may not learn, but you may learn it to understand and to teach"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Synhedrin 68a, the answer that study is different, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. VII (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1903), p. 290" ;
    skos:definition "The answer the discussion gives to its own objection against R. Eliezer. It is different when it is done for the sake of study, for the master said: thou shalt not learn to do it; to do it you may not learn, but you may learn it in order to understand and to teach. The exception thus covers not only the learning but the performing, since what it is brought in to excuse is a performance."@en ;
    skos:note "The public-domain English omits the sentence at the parallel in tractate Sabbath, where the same teaching carries a whole proof about what a magos is. Goldschmidt's seventh volume carries it here in full. That omission is the reason this file is built on the German and is recorded in the search node."@en,
        "This is the gate that SPEC section 0 is about, found in the transmission that built the fence. It removes the gate for the student and for the teacher and leaves it for the practitioner, and it is the oldest permission of its kind in the holding. Its origin is tannaitic and lies in Sifre Deuteronomium at the commandment not to learn the abominations of the nations; the Babylonian Talmud quotes it as a known teaching, without a name, and does not coin it. That earlier work has no public-domain translation in any language and was not opened; the issues of this run say so."@en,
        "What the sentence rests on and what it does not. The ground is a reading of an infinitive in the prohibition and nothing else: no purpose is argued, no necessity of the court is invoked. That the permission exists so that judges can recognise the art is a reasonable inference from its standing next to the rule about who is liable, and it is not in the wording; it is therefore not claimed here."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliSanhedrin ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmc:SanhedrinNothingBesideHimEvenInSorcery a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Es gibt keinen ausser ihm, und das reicht bis in die Zauberei"@de,
        "There is none beside him, and this reaches as far as sorcery"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Synhedrin 67b, the saying of R. Hanina on Deuteronomy 4:35 and the woman who sought earth from beneath his feet, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. VII (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1903), p. 286" ;
    skos:definition "R. Hanina applies the verse that there is none beside him to sorcery as well, and the case reported next is his own: a woman tried to take earth from under his feet in order to bewitch him, and he said to her, if you can, then do it, for it is written, there is none beside him. The claim is that the art has no power against him, and the ground is a verse and not a technique."@en ;
    skos:note "The parallel at Holin 7b was opened on 2026-10-04 and it refuted the second half of what this note used to say. The note reported from the scholarship that the parallel gives R. Hanina a flatter answer, that the woman's business will not succeed, and inferred that this would be a denial of efficacy rather than a personal exception. The flatter answer is there in the wording. The inference is wrong: the same objection from R. Johanan's saying and the same resolution by R. Hanina's great merits follow it in that tractate too, so the personal exception is a feature of the case and not of this tractate's wording. The reading stands at pmc:HolinTakeItYourPurposeWillNotSucceed, and what the difference in wording does change is recorded there - take it, your purpose will not succeed is a prediction a bewitching could have contradicted, where if you can, then do it is an invitation no outcome could disappoint."@en,
        "The resolution the discussion gives is what makes the claim worth holding as a node. The objection is raised from the preceding saying, that the art contradicts the household above; the answer is that it was different with R. Hanina, whose merits were very great. The claim is thereby rescued at the cost of becoming untestable against any single case: whoever is struck had too little merit. The holding has other claims of that shape and this is the plainest of them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliSanhedrin ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmc:SanhedrinTheSorcererIsStoned a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Kunst ist todeswuerdig, und gestritten wird ueber die Todesart"@de,
        "The art is capital, and the discussion is about which death"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Synhedrin 67a-67b, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. VII (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1903), pp. 285-286" ;
    skos:definition "The warning of this transmission against the art is a sentence of death, and the part of it that is argued is which death. R. Jose the Galilean derives the sword from the wording of the prohibition; R. Akiba derives stoning from another verse; Ben Azzai derives stoning from the commandment that stands next to it. The teaching that the feminine wording of the prohibition carries the masculine too, and that it is feminine only because women are mostly found in sorcery, is stated before the dispute."@en ;
    skos:note "What is warned against is the performance and not the capacity, which is the opposite emphasis from most of the cautions in this holding. Nothing here says the art does not work, and the next two nodes say in as many words that it does. A transmission that puts a man to death for an act it holds effective is making a different claim from one that forbids a delusion."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:SanhedrinSorcererIsTheOneWhoDoesTheDeed,
        pmp:SanhedrinOneWordOverTheField ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliSanhedrin ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmc:SanhedrinThreeHundredRulesOnTheWitch a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Dreihundert Lehren zum Vers ueber die Zauberin, von denen zwei gelernt wurden"@de,
        "Three hundred rules on the verse about the witch, of which two were learnt"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tosefta Sanhedrin XI.5c, trans. Herbert Danby, Tractate Sanhedrin: Mishnah and Tosefta (London: Society for Promoting Christian Knowledge, 1919), p. 107" ;
    skos:definition "R. Akiba says that R. Eliezer used to expound three hundred halakhoth on the commandment not to suffer a witch to live, and that he himself learnt only two things from it: the two cucumber pickers, and that the one who does the deed is liable while the one who only appears to is innocent. A body of law of that size on one verse is claimed, and the amount of it that reached the next generation is stated as two sentences."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also the clearest statement in the holding of a transmission reporting its own loss in numbers. Two of three hundred, said by the man who received them."@en,
        "The Babylonian Talmud reports the same figure with a different subject: there the three hundred, or by another reading three thousand, are rules about the planting of cucumbers, that is about the technique, and not about the verse. The Tosefta read here has the verse. Which way the substitution ran is not decided here, and the difference matters, because one version makes the lost body of law legal doctrine and the other makes it operative instruction."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ToseftaSanhedrin ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmc:SanhedrinThreefoldDivisionOfTheLaw a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Drei Klassen, von denen eine von vornherein erlaubt ist"@de,
        "Three classes, of which one is permitted from the outset"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Synhedrin 67b, the saying of Abaye on the law of sorcery, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. VII (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1903), p. 287" ;
    skos:definition "Abaye says the law of sorcery is like the law of the Sabbath: some of it is punished by stoning, some of it leaves the doer free while the act stays forbidden, and some of it is permitted from the outset. He assigns the classes: the one who performs an act is stoned, the one who merely holds the eyes is free but the act is forbidden, and permitted from the outset is what R. Hanina and R. Oshaya used to do."@en ;
    skos:note "The division settles that liability does not follow efficacy in this transmission. The third class is the one that proves it: what those two did was to create a calf and eat it, which is as real an effect as the corpus claims anywhere, and it is permitted. What is graded is the means and the warrant, not whether anything happened."@en,
        "The middle class is where the Mishnah stopped. It says only that the man is free; that the act is nevertheless forbidden is Abaye's addition, and the holding records it here rather than at the Mishnah so that the two are not read as one sentence."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliSanhedrin ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmc:SanhedrinWaterDissolvesTheTransformation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Niemand kauft hier etwas, ohne es durch Wasser zu pruefen"@de,
        "Nobody buys here without testing it by water"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Synhedrin 67b, Zeeri's ass at Alexandria and the rule of the sellers, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. VII (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1903), p. 287" ;
    skos:definition "The competing criterion, in the same discussion and on the opposite premise. Zeeri bought an ass in Alexandria; when he went to water it the enchantment dissolved and he was left sitting on a plank of a bridge. The sellers told him that were he not Zeeri they would not have given the money back, for there is nobody here who buys anything without testing it by water. Here the test does not expose an illusion as empty; it undoes a transformation the discussion treats as having taken place."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also the only place in the part read where a criterion is reported as a market custom rather than as a saying of a named authority: the sellers state it as what everybody does. That makes it the one criterion here with a claim to having been applied more than once."@en,
        "The two criteria are held side by side in the text and are not harmonised there. At the camel the absence of a trace proves that nothing happened; at the ass the water proves that something did and ends it. Any statement about what this transmission takes a test to be has to carry both, and a reader who meets only one of them will get the transmission wrong."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliSanhedrin ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmc:SanhedrinWhatIsBeyondTheDemons a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Was den Daemonen unmoeglich ist, als Mass angegeben"@de,
        "What is beyond the demons, given as a measure"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Synhedrin 67b, the sayings of R. Eliezer and Rav Pappa on what a demon can create, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. VII (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1903), p. 287" ;
    skos:definition "A stated limit of the art, with a quantity. R. Eliezer infers from the plague of lice that a demon cannot create a creature smaller than a barley grain. Rav Pappa sharpens it: not even one the size of a camel can they create; what they can do is gather such things together, and the small ones they cannot. Before it stands the distinction that where weight is laid on a particular implement the thing is done by a demon, and where no weight is laid on an implement it is sorcery."@en ;
    skos:note "Rav Pappa's second half is the more consequential: it reinterprets every apparent creation as transport. A claim that something was made is thereby converted into a claim that something was fetched, and the two differ in what would show them false."@en,
        "This is the only place in the part read that draws a boundary of efficacy with a measure in it, and the measure is the wrong way round from any physical expectation: the small is harder than the large. The reason is that the boundary is derived from a scriptural episode and not from a trial."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliSanhedrin ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmc:SattaSappaya a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die sieben Zuträglichkeiten, drei davon durch eine Probe von drei Tagen geprüft"@de,
        "The seven suitabilities, three of them tested by a probation of three days"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.35-41, Pali PTS [127]-[128] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, pp. 147-149; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "Seven things named in a verse as to be avoided where they are unsuitable and followed where they are suitable: lodging, alms resort, speech, person, food, season and posture. The criteria are given. A lodging in which the sign does not arise, or in which the arisen sign perishes, is unsuitable; therefore in a monastery with many quarters he is to live three days in each and stay where his mind becomes collected. An alms village within one and a half kosa, to the north or the south so that he does not walk against the sun, and where food is easily had, is suitable. Of speech, the thirty-two kinds of worldly talk are unsuitable because they lead to the vanishing of the sign, and the ten permitted subjects are to be used in moderation. Of a person, one given to worldly talk is unsuitable, and he who keeps company with such a man loses what he has got, let alone finds the sign. Food, season and posture are individual, and posture likewise is settled by a probation of three days."@en ;
    skos:note "The one case the handbook adduces on this head is positive and carries a number: five hundred monks in the Cūḷanāga cave on Tambapaṇṇi are said to have reached sanctity there because the lodging was suitable. No case is given of a practitioner who worked through a monastery three days at a time and found no suitable quarter."@en,
        "Two things here are rare in this holding and are the reason for the node. The criterion of suitability is the behaviour of the sign, so the practitioner is told to judge his lodging and his posture by whether the practice works in them; and the judgement is given a term, three days, twice. That is a condition of failure with a clock on it, stated of the circumstances and not of the man."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:SecondDegreeYieldsDistantHearing a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der zweite Grad bringt das Fernhören hervor"@de,
        "The second degree yields hearing at a distance"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 168" ;
    skos:note "One node per pairing, following traditions/patanjala-yoga.ttl, because a protocol would have to take the pairings one at a time and because the four capacities of this degree are not equally examinable."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:MideInitiationSecondDegree ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:MideDistantHearing .

pmc:SecondWeek a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die zweite Woche"@de,
        "The second week"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ignatius of Loyola, Spiritual Exercises 4" ;
    skos:definition "The part given over to the life of Christ up to Palm Sunday, and to the following of it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChristianAsceticDiscernment .

pmc:SenasanaPancanga a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die fünf Merkmale einer Unterkunft, in der die Übung getan werden kann"@de,
        "The five marks of a lodging in which the training can be done"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.19, Pali PTS [122] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, pp. 142-143; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "Against the eighteen faults the handbook sets five marks, quoted from the canon: the lodging is not too far and not too near and there is a path for going and coming; it is little frequented by day; there is little noise and clamour by night; there is little contact with gadflies, mosquitoes, wind, heat and creeping things; and the four requisites are easily obtained there and elders of experience are living in it, whom he can ask and who will explain what is unexplained, clear what is unclear and remove his doubt."@en ;
    skos:note "The fifth mark is two conditions and the second of them is a person. That is the only one of the five a place cannot supply by itself, and it is the one the eighteenth fault names in the negative: a monastery where no good friend is to be had is reckoned a great disadvantage. So the handbook's account of a suitable place ends at a second party, and the account of the practice done in it does not."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MajjhimaNikaya,
        pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:Shamsiel a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Schamsiel"@de,
        "Shamsiel"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "A Watcher named as the one who disclosed the signs of the sun."@en ;
    skos:note "Charles brackets the clause naming Araqiel and Shamsiel as absent from part of the Ethiopic tradition; the bracketing belongs to the text as printed and is recorded here rather than resolved."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:nameForm "Shamsiêl" ;
    pm:nameRole "watcher" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:ShangqingCheshiCapacity a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Durchdringendes Sehen und Hören"@de,
        "Penetrating sight and hearing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 9.5a-9.5b (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 9" ;
    skos:definition "Seeing what lies beyond the range of the eye and hearing what lies beyond the range of the ear, given in this corpus in stated distances and paired with a standing auditory sign, the constant sound of metal and jade in the ears."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:ShangqingGuijiuShenling a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die eine Stelle, an der der Bestand die Zuweisung töricht nennt"@de,
        "The one place where the corpus calls the assignment foolish"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 7.13b (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 7" ;
    skos:definition "One of the Perfected states that students of the Way are commonly careless in their affairs, bring a hundred ailments on themselves, and then lay the blame on the spirits; that they brave the wind and lie in the damp and then blame others for the want of a cover; and that all such are fools, to whom nothing of the profound may be told."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because it is the counter-instance inside the same transmission, and it was what a search for one turned up; see pmp:ZhengaoMethodBlamedSearch. The corpus assigns failure to the practitioner throughout, and here it names a case in which assigning it elsewhere is the error — but the direction is the same as everywhere else, since what is blamed wrongly are the spirits and what is blamed rightly is again the practitioner. The passage is not a statement that a method might be at fault, and this file does not read it as one."@en,
        "Its value is that it shows the transmission capable of the move at all. A corpus that never distinguishes a self-inflicted failure from a failure of the operation cannot be asked which it means; this one makes the distinction once, on the side that costs it nothing."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:ShangqingHuangtingMengjin a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Pfand bei der Übergabe, und wer für eine falsche zahlt"@de,
        "The pledge at the handing on, and who pays for a wrong one"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangting neijing yujing 36 (Muyu zhang) (DZ 331), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "The closing chapter sets the terms of transmission. The one who gives is called master and the one who receives swears; cloud-brocade, phoenix gauze and a golden clasp are bound up in place of cutting the hair, so that skin and flesh stay whole; the two go up the mountain hand in hand and smear the cinnabar liquid, and only then may the golden writing and jade scene be declared. And the sanction: let the handing on be examined and reported to the three palaces, and let it not be that seven ancestral generations receive the affliction."@en ;
    skos:note "The penalty for a transmission carried out wrongly falls on the dead ascendants of the transmitter and not on him, which is the feature that distinguishes this clause from the oath in gold at pmc:ShangqingMengjin and from the two witnesses of pmc:TheRestrictionOnPassingItOn. A sanction on seven generations already dead cannot be observed by anyone and cannot be reported absent. It is on this feature that the clause is one of the three witnesses of pmc:ThePenaltyLaidOnAnotherParty."@en,
        "The textile pledge replacing the cutting of the hair is stated as a substitution in the text itself, which is rare enough to keep: the work says in one clause what the older form was and that this one stands in its place."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ShangqingHuangtingSongjing ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ShangqingXuedao ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:ShangqingJinri a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die verbotenen Tage, und der Schaden, den man nicht bemerkt"@de,
        "The forbidden days, and the harm one does not notice"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 10.20a (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 10",
        "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 10.25a-10.25b (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 10" ;
    skos:definition "The corpus binds several prohibitions to dates. On the jiayin and gengshen days the corpse-demons contend and the spirits are fouled, and one may neither share a mat with wife or husband nor meet face to face. On the five mao days one enters the chamber facing east. A further rule names four dates in the year on which one seeking transcendence must not be in the presence of a woman, gives the reckoning behind it, the woman's palace standing in shen and the man's in yin and the two in mutual punishment, and states that on those days the corpse-souls of each sex call to the other, so that spirit is chipped and vapour departs and death follows from the accumulation."@en ;
    skos:note "The dates themselves are of the ordinary kind and are the third witness in this holding for a transmission binding an operation to the calendar rather than to a state of either party; see pmc:AdmissibilityBoundToTheCalendar."@en,
        "The last clause is the reason the node is here. The passage says that the person does not notice it himself and yet the body has already been damaged. A harm defined as imperceptible to the person who suffers it cannot be reported absent by him, which removes the only witness the rule has."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ShangqingXuedao ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:ShangqingJisiCapacity a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Fortsetzung der Linie"@de,
        "Continuation of the line"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 8.10a (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 8" ;
    skos:definition "Offspring obtained where they were wanting, with the pregnancy preserved and the child reaching a long life; given in this corpus as something a household arrangement can procure and not as something the practitioner's own cultivation procures."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:ShangqingMengjianCapacity a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Träume als Sicht des Kommenden"@de,
        "Dreams as sight of what is to come"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 9.16b (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 9" ;
    skos:definition "A state of dreaming in which, the corpus says, dreams arise only at the prompting of the spirits and each is as distinct as a view of a coming thing; given as the end point of a three-year practice and not as an inborn faculty."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:ShangqingMengjin a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Gold hinterlegt, bevor die Methode weitergegeben wird"@de,
        "Gold laid down before the method is transmitted"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 9.7b-9.8a (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 9" ;
    skos:definition "Before the rubbing regimen may be practised the corpus requires an oath sworn on gold, the quantity left to the giver and the sincerity the point, and states that the method is not transmitted to one who is not the right person. A second method in the same section requires blue as well as gold, and a third requires an oath sworn on blue metal, the compiler working out the quantities the pledge should run to."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a prescribed order and not as a stated necessity, because the passage attaches no consequence to practising without the oath. What it states is a rule about who may receive, and the corpus elsewhere says that a scripture obtained without proper transmission fails: at 19.11a a man who copied one without receiving it is said to have had his house burned by the mountain spirit and his recitation stopped by sudden rain that soaked the paper, and the compiler's note assigns both to his having copied it without the transmission."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ShangqingAnmo ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ShangqingXuedao ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:ShangqingRizaixin a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Jahr, fünf Jahre, achtzehn Jahre, und kein Schatten am Mittag"@de,
        "One year, five years, eighteen years, and no shadow at noon"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 9.22a (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 9" ;
    skos:definition "Of the sun held in the heart the corpus states a graded timetable: performed three times a day, in one year illness is gone, in five years the body has a lustre, in eighteen years the Way is necessarily obtained, one walks in the midst of the sun casting no shadow, and a hundred demons and a thousand baleful influences are kept off."@en ;
    skos:note "The first two terms are of another kind and are kept in the same node because the passage states them in one breath. Illness gone in a year and a lustre on the body in five are outcomes with a high base rate and no stated measure, and nothing in the passage says how either is to be established."@en,
        "The third term is the most checkable claim in the work. Casting no shadow at noon is a public fact about a body in daylight, the term after which it is to hold is stated exactly, the word used of the outcome is one of necessity, and no condition is attached that would exempt a practitioner of eighteen years' standing who casts one. The protocol built on it stands in examinations/shangqing-intervals.ttl."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ShangqingFuriyue ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ShangqingWuyingCapacity .

pmc:ShangqingSanseqiCapacity a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die drei Farben des Dunstes im Körper"@de,
        "The three colours of vapour present in the body"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 10.1b (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 10" ;
    skos:definition "A state in which the green, white and red vapours are said to be in the body of themselves and no longer to require drawing in, given as the condition immediately preceding transcendence."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:ShangqingShieryanshi a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die zwölf Proben, und dass sie nicht zu erkennen sind"@de,
        "The twelve trials, and that they are not to be recognised"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 5.5a-5.6a (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 5" ;
    skos:definition "The corpus states that the great methods are transmitted only to one who has passed twelve trials, that the Perfected of the Great Ultimate come in person to look on, and that the trials are set at precisely what the candidate's mind does not grasp and his feelings do not reach. Failing to recognise the tester counts as failing the trial, and so does taking a demon for a Perfected. The corpus adds that a man who passed nine of the twelve and failed three obtained the elixir and rose, but was made a transcendent only and not a Perfected."@en ;
    skos:note "The construction is worth setting out because it is the strongest case of its kind in this holding. The test is defined as unrecognisable, so no candidate can prepare for it and none can be shown to have passed it except by the outcome; failure is therefore always available as an explanation after the fact and is never available as a prediction before it. The corpus supplies six worked examples in the same section, each of which assigns a failure to a trial recognised only afterwards."@en,
        "The grading is the one part of it that is not circular, and it should be kept. Three failures out of twelve are said to cost a rank and not the whole attainment, which means the doctrine has a scale and is not a bare pass and fail."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ShangqingXuedao ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:ShangqingWanbianYunjia a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Zehntausend Lesungen, und die Regel für den Fall, dass der Wagen ausbleibt"@de,
        "Ten thousand readings, and the rule for when the chariot does not come"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 9.23b (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 9" ;
    skos:definition "Of the five thousand words of the Daode jing the corpus says that at ten thousand readings the cloud chariot comes to fetch the reader; and it states in the same sentence what is to be done if the ten thousand are complete and the reader has not departed, namely to read it twice a month until the chariot arrives and then go."@en ;
    skos:note "The corpus gives a second and independent reckoning of the same operation immediately below, and its own compiler checks the arithmetic and finds it wrong. The night-time reading of the inner scripture of the Yellow Court, once a night for twenty-one years, is said to make ten thousand times; the compiler notes that once a night for twenty-one years does not come to ten thousand and proposes either that the figure of twenty-one is corrupt or that the true rate is two or three a night. The same passage stands again at 15.10a with the same note."@en,
        "The second half is the reason this node exists. The claim names an exact quantity, which makes it the kind of statement a run could fail, and the same sentence removes the failure by converting the threshold into a standing obligation. What would have been the falsifying outcome, ten thousand readings and no chariot, is provided for as a normal case before anyone has reached it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ShangqingSongjing ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ShangqingYunjiaCapacity .

pmc:ShangqingWuyingCapacity a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Keinen Schatten in der Sonne werfen"@de,
        "Casting no shadow in the sun"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 9.22a (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 9" ;
    skos:definition "Walking in the midst of the sun without a shadow, given in this corpus as the mark of a body that has taken the sun into itself and as one of the things obtained at the end of a stated term."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:ShangqingYeshuCapacity a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Sehen, um im Dunkeln zu schreiben"@de,
        "Seeing to write in the dark"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 9.6b-9.7a (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 9" ;
    skos:definition "The ability to write without light, given in this corpus as the plain consequence of clear eyes and not as a vision or a state. A saying of the Perfected quoted in the same passage puts it as a maxim: if you would write by night, cultivate the constant abode, the name the corpus gives to the hollow behind the eyebrows."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:ShangqingYijieQingyao a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Beischlaf löscht ein Jahr der Arznei"@de,
        "One act cancels one year of the drug"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 10.18b-10.19a (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 10",
        "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 10.23b (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 10" ;
    skos:definition "The corpus states the loss in a countable form: for one who is nourishing life, one act of intercourse overturns one year of the drug's force, two acts overturn two years, and beyond three the whole of what has been taken is lost from the body. A second passage in the same juan gives a parallel reckoning from another hand: one act overturns one year of the drug's force, and one who practises the chamber arts while taking nothing at all has thirty years struck from his reckoning. A third states that frequent emission fouls the vapour, destroys the spirit and exhausts the essence, so that even a person entered in the jade registers is not thereby exempt."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered for its arithmetic. A rule in this form makes any null result recoverable: a practitioner who has taken the drug for years and has nothing to show has only to be asked about his conduct, and the balance can be made to come out at nothing without the drug being questioned. It is the same device as the merit reckoning at pmc:ShangqingGongman, applied to the failure side instead of to the success side."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ShangqingFushi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:ShangqingZhonglingJiaojieDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die versammelten numinosen Mächte es gesagt haben"@de,
        "Whether the assembled numinous powers said it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 6.10a, compiler's note (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 6",
        "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 6.6a-6.10a (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 6" ;
    skos:definition "The block of sayings at 6.6a-6.10a is ascribed in the text to five named Perfected, three male and two female, each speaking in turn, and is closed with the rubric that these are the teachings and admonitions of the assembled numinous powers. The compiler's note directly beneath it holds that those five are among the most exalted and descend very seldom, that he fears the block is those speakers recounting old matter, and that it was not necessarily received by Yang Xi in person."@en ;
    skos:note "The reading that makes the disagreement sharp is available to anyone who reads the block: it is, clause for clause, a Buddhist text, with the renunciant of the original replaced by the man of the Way and the sayings distributed among Daoist speakers. The compiler does not say that in this note. He says the equivalent about the neighbouring passage on the emperor's dream at 9.20a-9.20b, where he observes that the account agrees roughly with the outer books and may be Yang Xi copying an old story, and adds that the writings of the Perfected do repeatedly treat Buddhist matters."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:YangxiShouzhen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:ShangqingZhongzhu a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Bambus nördlich der inneren Kammer gepflanzt"@de,
        "Bamboo planted north of the inner chamber"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 8.9b-8.10a (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 8" ;
    skos:definition "A Perfected woman, asked about a want of heirs, cites a text for the doctrine that bamboo is the essence of the northern pivot, receives its vapour from a named lodge, and is accordingly hollow within, holds a doubled shade, and sets root and bears seed in great number; and she directs that bamboo be planted outside the north wall of the inner chamber and the finer ones walked among, whereupon heaven will be moved to grant offspring, the pregnancy will be preserved and the child long-lived. The passage calls this the secret rule of the men of the dark and says that those who carry it out much verify it."@en ;
    skos:note "One of the few claims in the corpus whose antecedent and consequent are both public. Households either plant bamboo north of an inner room or they do not, and they either have children afterwards or they do not, and the passage attaches no condition to the practitioner's state. The protocol built on it stands in examinations/shangqing-intervals.ttl."@en,
        "The reasoning is stated and is worth keeping with the claim, because it is the form of inference the corpus runs on throughout: the plant is fertile and is assigned to the north, the north is the quarter of the pivot, therefore the plant set in the north of a dwelling transfers its fertility to the household. Nothing in the passage rests on an observation of households."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ShangqingCunsi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ShangqingJisiCapacity .

pmc:ShangqingZhoushiSanxiong a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Neuntausendsiebenhundertdreiunddreißig"@de,
        "Nine thousand seven hundred and thirty-three"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 5.6a-5.6b (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 5" ;
    skos:definition "Three brothers of the Zhou are said to have studied in the Chang mountains for ninety-seven years and to have been given seven scrolls of a plain-silk book, which they recited together. A white deer appeared at the mountain's edge; the two younger set the book down to look at it, the elder did not break off. The elder completed ten thousand recitations and flew off as a transcendent; the two younger had reached nine thousand seven hundred and thirty-three, and when they took up the book again the stone chamber burst into flame and burned it, so that they did not obtain transcendence and are still walking in the Chang mountains."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because of the number. A transmission that sets a threshold ordinarily leaves the shortfall unspecified; this one names it to the unit and attaches the failure to it, which makes the threshold a real edge rather than an encouragement. The story does a second thing at the same time: the loss of the book is the punishment for the interruption, so the shortfall is at once the cause of the failure and its effect, and no one who stopped short could afterwards make up the difference."@en,
        "The corpus is consistent here. The same section states at 5.14b-15a that the Great Cavern scripture read ten thousand times makes a transcendent and calls this the highest of the methods of transcendence, and at 5.11b that one who has that scripture needs no elixir. The threshold in this story is the same quantity applied to a different book."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:Shenbushi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Geist gibt sich nicht her"@de,
        "The spirit does not lend itself"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 14 (Tang ye lao li lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 4" ;
    skos:definition "The term the corpus gives to the case in which the body is worn out and the blood exhausted and the treatment establishes nothing. Asked why that happens, the answer is that the spirit does not lend itself. Needle and stone, the passage continues, are a way; where the essence-spirit does not go forward and the intent is not ordered, the illness cannot be cured; and where the essence is ruined and the spirit gone, the constructive and defensive qi can no longer be gathered back, because desire is without end and grief without cease."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the failure condition of this corpus that is hardest to test and most worth recording, because it is located in the patient and not in the operation. Any outcome of a treatment can be assigned to it after the fact, and the passage supplies no mark by which its presence could be established beforehand. Recorded as the tradition's own account of why a correct procedure fails, and marked here as the shape of claim that absorbs every outcome."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmc:ShengqiSiqi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die lebendigen und die toten Stunden"@de,
        "The living hours and the dead hours"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 8 (Shizhi), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 8" ;
    skos:definition "The circulation is to be done in the hours of living qi and not in the hours of dead qi. The day and night hold twelve hours; the six from midnight to noon are living qi and the six from noon to midnight are dead qi, and in the hours of dead qi, the chapter says, circulating the qi is of no benefit."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also, read the other way, the cheapest possible defence: any session without result can be assigned to the hour. Both readings are true of the same sentence, and which of them the transmission intends is not decided here."@en,
        "This is the sentence for which the chapter was worth opening. It states a condition under which the practice does nothing, it binds the condition to the clock rather than to the practitioner, and it therefore names an outcome the practice excludes: a benefit obtained in the afternoon. Almost nothing else in this file does that."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BaopuziNeipian ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:ShierjinZhongshi a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die zwölf Verbote und was ihr Bruch bewirken soll"@de,
        "The twelve prohibitions, and what is said to follow from breaking them"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 9 (Zhong shi), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 2" ;
    skos:definition "The needling chapter of the Lingshu states the same prohibitions as a two-sided rule and adds a state the other list does not have. Newly after intercourse do not needle, and newly needled do not lie together; already drunk do not needle, and already needled do not get drunk; newly angered, newly exhausted, already filled, already hungry, already thirsty, each in the same double form. On great fright and great fear the rule is not a prohibition at all but a condition: settle the qi first, then needle. Two further clauses set waiting times by the manner of arrival, the time of a meal for one who came by cart and the time of walking ten li for one who came on foot. The chapter then names what breaking the twelve does: the vessels are disordered and the qi scattered, a yang illness enters the yin and a yin illness comes out as yang, the evil qi is generated afresh, and the text calls it hewing at the body, wasting the brain marrow and losing the qi."@en ;
    skos:note "The relation to the Suwen list is not similarity but disagreement, and the Song collation note printed with the Suwen chapter says so in as many words: it states that the seven clauses there and the Lingshu clauses go in and out of one another, and it prints the Lingshu wording beside them so that a reader can see it. The two differ on fright, which one forbids and the other admits after a delay, and on intercourse, which only the Lingshu has."@en,
        "This is also the passage in this corpus that comes closest to a stated failure condition for the operator rather than the patient. It says what the transgression produces, in the body, and names the direction of the change."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Cizhen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmc:ShintoFutomaniCausaDefectus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Wahrsagung, die sagt, warum der Ritus einen Blutegel hervorbrachte"@de,
        "The divination that says why the rite produced a leech"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Nihongi, Book I, the Age of the Gods, in the English of W. G. Aston, vol. I, London 1896, pp. 15-16" ;
    skos:definition "The first use the chronicle makes of the procedure, and it is put to a rite that went wrong. The two deities set up the pillar of heaven, agree to go round it from opposite sides, and meet; the female speaks first. Their first child is a leech, which is put in a reed-boat and set adrift, and their next is not counted among their children either. They return to heaven and report the circumstances, and the heavenly gods divine the matter by the Greater Divination and instruct them: it was by reason of the woman's having spoken first. They then divine a time, go down again, and the second attempt succeeds."@en ;
    skos:note "Every element of the shape this holding calls the excusing clause is here and none of it is a later gloss: a procedure whose outcome cannot be judged apart from itself, an outcome that plainly did not come off, a cause located in a nameable act of one of the two operators, and a repetition that succeeds. What makes it worth a node rather than a mention is that the cause is not asserted by the operators but produced by a second procedure whose own outcome nobody could establish either. The excuse is itself divined."@en,
        "The time of the second attempt is divined as well, which puts a calendar condition on the repetition. The chronicle does not say what would have followed from going down at another time."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Nihonshoki ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shinto .

pmc:ShintoSujinIteratio a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Er verehrte, wie ihm gesagt war, und es blieb ohne Wirkung"@de,
        "He worshipped as he was told, but without effect"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Nihongi, Book V, Sujin 7th year, 2nd and 8th months, in the English of W. G. Aston, vol. I, London 1896, pp. 151-153" ;
    skos:definition "The chronicle's plainest case of an empty run. Calamities befall the country; the emperor assembles the deities and inquires by divination; a princess is inspired to answer that reverent worship would pacify the land of itself, and names the god who speaks. The emperor worshipped as he was told, but without effect. He then bathes, practises abstinence, purifies the interior of the hall, and prays again, asking whether the observance of due ceremonies is not yet complete and calling the non-acceptance cruel. That night the same god appears in a dream with a further instruction: let a named man be made master of the worship, and there will be peace at once. Three other people report the same dream, the man is found, the appointment is made, and the chronicle then says the country was at peace."@en ;
    skos:note "The confirmation by three independent dreamers is the other half and is worth keeping in view: the second instruction is corroborated by three further people having the same dream on the same night. That is a check the transmission itself supplies, and it checks the instruction and not the outcome."@en,
        "The sequence is the one generalizations/no-failed-run-counts.ttl describes, in narrative rather than in doctrine. An instruction is given by the procedure, carried out, and produces nothing; the emperor's own reading of the nothing is that his ceremonies were incomplete; and the remedy prescribed is a further and stronger address of the same kind, ending in a further instruction that supersedes the first without withdrawing it. At no point is the possibility raised that the first answer was wrong."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Nihonshoki ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shinto .

pmc:ShintoUkehiVariaeLectiones a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Derselbe Ausgang, vier Fassungen, und das Urteil wandert mit der Zurechnungsregel"@de,
        "The same outcome, four versions, and the verdict moves with the attribution rule"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Nihongi, Book I, the Age of the Gods, main narrative, in the English of W. G. Aston, vol. I, London 1896, pp. 35-36",
        "Nihongi, Book I, the Age of the Gods, the three versions introduced as in one writing it is said, in the English of W. G. Aston, vol. I, London 1896, pp. 37-40" ;
    skos:definition "The chronicle prints the episode four times and the physical outcome is the same in all of them: three female deities and five or six male ones. What differs is who is said to have produced which, and the verdict follows that and not the outcome. In the main narrative the goddess takes her brother's sword and produces the three females while he takes her jewels and produces the five males; she then assigns the children by the ownership of the material, so that the males are hers and the females his, and the narrative draws no verdict at all. In the first variant each uses his or her own object, and the passage concludes that he had thereby acquired proof of his victory and that she learnt his intentions were free from guilt. In the second variant the two first exchange objects by covenant, she produces the females from his jewels and he the males from her sword, and the passage closes with the words such is the story and no verdict. In the third variant she states the criterion herself over the children he will produce, she produces the females from her own swords and he six males from his own jewel-strings, he says truly I have won, and she is said to have known exactly that his intentions had been honest from the first."@en ;
    skos:note "That is a third way for an announced criterion to decide nothing, and this holding did not have it. pmc:TheCriterionNoPartyControls describes criteria that state one side only, and its protocol at examinations/both-sides-of-the-criterion.ttl added a second shape, the failing side named and then abolished. Here the criterion is two-sided, the event is uncontrollable, the outcome is fixed and identical across the witnesses, and the verdict still varies — because the attribution was underdetermined. A reader who wants to know whether a stated criterion can settle anything has to ask not only whether both sides are named but whether the mapping is."@en,
        "The disagreement is not about what happened. It is about the rule that maps the event onto the criterion, and that rule was not fixed when the criterion was agreed. Two of the four versions therefore return a verdict and two return none, out of one and the same outcome."@en,
        "Worth stating so it is not read as more than it is: the four versions are four recensions of one episode in one work, not four transmissions. What the node establishes is that the compiler of 720 had before him readings that disagreed on the verdict and printed them side by side without choosing."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:ShintoUkehiCriterium ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Nihonshoki ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shinto .

pmc:Shoudu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die giftige Hand"@de,
        "The poisonous hand"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 73 (Guan neng), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 11" ;
    skos:definition "A property of a person's hands, named in the chapter that assigns offices according to fitness: some have bitter nails and poisonous hands and in doing things are apt to injure, and such a person is to be set to pressing on accumulations and holding down numbness. The chapter opposes it to the sweet hand. It stands in a list beside the assignment of the well-jointed and supple-sinewed to the leading and pulling of the qi, and of the sharp-tongued and slighting to the spitting on abscesses and the incanting of illnesses."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim is that the difference is a property of the person and not of what he does, since the chapter assigns offices by it and says that where the person is not the right one the work does not succeed and his teacher goes without a name."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmc:ShouyiCapacities a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Was das Bewahren des Einen vermögen soll"@de,
        "What guarding the One is said to accomplish"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 18 (Dizhen), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 18" ;
    skos:definition "On land it turns aside evil beasts, on water it repels the jiaolong; there is no fear of the wangliang or of venomous creatures, ghosts dare not approach and a blade dares not strike. With the bare blade at the neck, thinking the One obtains life. In a ghost temple, under the trees of a mountain, in a place of great pestilence, among graves, in the thicket of tigers and wolves, in the haunt of vipers, guarding the One without slackening drives every evil far off. Where soldiers or bandits have surrounded a man and there is no ground of life left, he enters at once into the yin of the six jia, lies hidden and guards the One, and the five weapons cannot reach him."@en ;
    skos:note "The list is of the same kind as the eight things the circulation of the qi is said to accomplish in the eighth chapter, and it has the same mixture: some items name an outcome a witness could report, the blade that does not cut and the encirclement escaped, and others are dispositions of the world that nothing could count against. It is recorded whole for the same reason as the other."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BaopuziNeipian ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:ShouyiZhongnan a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Nicht das Wissen ist schwer, sondern das Durchhalten"@de,
        "Knowing it is not the hard part; ending in it is"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 18 (Dizhen), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 18" ;
    skos:definition "The chapter states the difficulty as a matter of persistence and not of understanding: to know the One is not hard, the hard thing is in the ending; guard it without losing it and one may be without limit. It names what happens when it lapses and what is then to be done. If a man happens to forget the guarding and is harmed by the hundred ghosts, or is pressed down in his sleep, he goes out into the middle of the courtyard, looks at the assistant star, closes his hands and guards the One, and the ghost departs; and if it is overcast or raining he stays in the room, faces north and thinks the assistant star, and that is all."@en ;
    skos:note "The remedy is the part worth having, and it is the shape a claim takes when the transmission expects its own practice to fail intermittently. It names the failure, names a sign of it that the practitioner does not have to interpret, prescribes a corrective act, and provides a fallback for the case in which the corrective act cannot be performed. Very little in this holding sets out a repair rather than a prohibition."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Shouyi ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BaopuziNeipian ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:SigneMaterielCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Kein materielles Zeichen beweist, wer spricht"@de,
        "No material sign proves who is speaking"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Allan Kardec, Le Livre des Médiums, seconde partie, chapitre XXIV, section 268, questions 3-6 and 23" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission's own warning against the evidential test. Superior spirits, it says, have no signs by which to be recognised other than the superiority of their ideas and their language, and every spirit can imitate a material sign. It adds that spirits take the names of saints and of famous persons, and that this is not fraud where the intention is good, so that the name is no proof either."@en ;
    skos:note "Two of the three things an outside investigator would reach for - a signature or handwriting, and a name - are ruled out by the transmission itself before anyone else can propose them. What is left is the third, the quality of the language, which is the one an outsider cannot measure."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Evocation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Spiritism .

pmc:SignsOfLightning a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zeichen der Blitze"@de,
        "Signs of the lightning flashes"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)" ;
    skos:definition "The reading of import from lightning, given in place of astrology in the Aramaic-Greek witness."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:SignsOfShootingStars a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zeichen der Sternschnuppen"@de,
        "Signs of the shooting stars"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)" ;
    skos:definition "The reading of import from meteors, given in place of the knowledge of the clouds in the Aramaic-Greek witness."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:SignsOfTheEarth a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zeichen der Erde"@de,
        "Signs of the earth"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "The reading of import from terrestrial phenomena."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:SignsOfTheMoon a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zeichen des Mondes"@de,
        "Signs of the moon"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)" ;
    skos:definition "The reading of import from the moon, given in place of its course in the Aramaic-Greek witness."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:SignsOfTheStars a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zeichen der Sterne"@de,
        "Signs of the stars"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)" ;
    skos:definition "The reading of import from the stars, given in place of the constellations in the Aramaic-Greek witness."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:SignsOfTheSun a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zeichen der Sonne"@de,
        "Signs of the sun"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "The reading of import from the sun."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:Sila a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sittlichkeit"@de,
        "Virtue"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.1-I.8 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "The first of the three trainings, on which the handbook is built as a commentary on the verse about the tangle."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:SpellCasting a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Beschwörung"@de,
        "Spell-casting"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "The binding of persons or events by spoken formula."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:SpiritCallingYieldsRecoveryOfLostThings a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Geisterrufen soll Verlorenes wiederbringen"@de,
        "Spirit calling is said to recover what has been lost"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 28" ;
    skos:definition "The claim, made by the narrator about her second husband, that when a knife was lost he would hold a spirit calling and in that way recover it."@en ;
    skos:note "The most examinable claim in the portion read, and the reason it is entered separately from the capacity it names. It states a repeatable occasion, a named class of object, and an outcome that either occurred or did not; it is not a claim about a state of the practitioner. What it does not state is any case in which the calling was held and the knife stayed lost."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:InuitSpiritCalling ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InuitAngakkuq ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:InuitRecoveryOfLostThings .

pmc:SteinerPruefungDurchUrteilskraft a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein großer Teil ist mit gesunder Urteilskraft prüfbar, und kein wissenschaftliches Ergebnis widerspricht"@de,
        "A large part is checkable by sound judgement, and no scientific result contradicts it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Rudolf Steiner, Wie erlangt man Erkenntnisse der höheren Welten?, Vorrede zur dritten Auflage (1909), in the German text of 1918" ;
    skos:definition "The offer the preface makes to the reader who will not walk the path. Whoever wants to observe the facts must ascend to supersensible knowledge; but one who does not perceive them can still obtain a sufficient conviction of the truth of the communications. A large part of them is checkable straight away by applying sound judgement in a really unprejudiced way. It is added in the same paragraph that in truth there is no scientific result which contradicts spiritual research, and that whoever believes some scientific judgement does not agree has not consulted the scientific results impartially and from all sides. A further part withdraws more or less from mere intellectual judgement, and for that part healthy feeling can be a judge of truth, where the feeling is not driven by sympathy or antipathy."@en ;
    skos:note "A check is offered and its outcome is fixed in the same breath. The reader is invited to apply his judgement, told in advance what the judgement will find, and given the account of every other finding: prejudice, or a one-sided consultation of the sciences. Where the intellect will not come to the stipulated result, the office of judge passes to feeling, and the feeling that judges otherwise is disqualified as sympathy or antipathy."@en,
        "This is a different construction from the fifteen the holding collects at pmc:NoFailedRunCountsAgainstTheOperation and should not be filed with them. Those excuse a run of the operation that produced nothing. This one excuses a failed check by a third party who never ran the operation at all — it is the immunising clause moved from the practitioner's result to the outsider's verdict. The holding has no statement about that move and this is the first witness for one; whether there is a second is the question, and the register's other public transmissions with a printed doctrine are where to look."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SteinerWieErlangtMan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Anthroposophy .

pmc:SteinerSchweigeGebot a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Schweige über deine geistigen Gesichte, sogar vor dir selber"@de,
        "Be silent about your spiritual sights, even before yourself"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Rudolf Steiner, Wie erlangt man Erkenntnisse der höheren Welten?, chapter Die Stufen der Einweihung, in the German text of 1918" ;
    skos:definition "The rule given immediately after the colours of the astral image are named. One does best to speak of such a perception at first to nobody except perhaps one's teacher, if one has such a one; for in trying to describe such an appearance in clumsy words one mostly gives oneself over to bad deceptions, using ordinary words which are not meant for such things and are too coarse and heavy for them, with the result that one is seduced by one's own attempt at wording into mixing all kinds of fantasy-dazzle into the true perceptions. Then, as a rule for the pupil: understand how to be silent about your spiritual sights. Indeed, be silent about them even before yourself. Do not try to clothe what you behold in spirit in words or to fathom it with the clumsy understanding; give yourself to your perception without prejudice and do not disturb it by much thinking about it, for your thinking is at first not at all equal to your seeing."@en ;
    skos:note "It is the third arrangement of the pair at pmc:TheFailureConditionFollowsWhatIsWithheld and the statement has no room for it yet. The first limb is a transmission that writes the whole operation down; the second is one that keeps part of the operation out of writing. Here the whole operation is written down and published, and what is withheld is the result: the pupil receives every step in print and is forbidden to put his outcome into words. The failure condition then sits neither on the text nor on an orally transmitted part, but on the pupil's silence. That is a distinction for a later run at that statement and not a refutation of it."@en,
        "The neighbouring authority clause belongs with it. The directions of thought given here, the same chapter says, have been tested and practised in the secret schools since primeval times, and only such are communicated; whoever would apply others of another kind, which he forms himself or of which he hears or reads here or there, must go astray and will soon find himself on the path of shoreless fantasy. So the pupil may not word his results, may not devise his own exercises, and may not take them from elsewhere."@en,
        "The outcome of the operation is placed under a rule of silence, and the ground given for the silence is the very risk of self-deception the same book elsewhere analyses at length. Wording the perception is said to corrupt it; so the one act by which a perception could enter a record, be compared with another pupil's, or be held against the colours announced in advance, is the act the rule forbids. That the prohibition extends to the pupil's own reflection closes the last channel."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:SteinerUebungAmSamen ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SteinerWieErlangtMan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Anthroposophy .

pmc:SteinerVerkehrteSchulung a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Hellsehend und blinder als vorher"@de,
        "Clairvoyant and blinder than before"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Rudolf Steiner, Wie erlangt man Erkenntnisse der höheren Welten?, chapter Über einige Wirkungen der Einweihung, in the German text of 1918" ;
    skos:definition "The warning attached to a schooling undertaken without the prescribed ordering of life. He could then enter the higher worlds only with a defective eye of thought, and instead of knowing the truth would be subject to nothing but deceptions and illusions; he would become clairvoyant in a certain respect but would at bottom be subject to a greater blindness than before, since formerly he at least stood firm within the world of the senses and had a definite hold on it, whereas now he sees behind it and goes astray in it before standing securely in a higher world; he may then no longer be able to distinguish truth from error at all and loses all direction in life. The same chapter states the consequences of a distorted development of one of the organs: not only illusions and fantastic notions where a clairvoyant gift appears, but also aberration and unsteadiness in ordinary life, so that one may become fearful, envious, vain, arrogant and self-willed, having had none of these properties before."@en ;
    skos:note "It is entered as a caution and not as a claim about outcomes because that is its grammatical form; but it carries a statement of fact about what happens to people, which no node in this file can examine. Whether any case of it was ever recorded is a question for the biographical and institutional literature of this transmission and not for the book."@en,
        "The transmission names, precisely and in its own voice, the outcome an outside observer would report of a failed case — illusion, loss of the hold on the sensory world, inability to tell truth from error, a change of character for the worse — and assigns it to a wrong or premature schooling. It is the most complete self-diagnosis in this holding and it is perfectly immunising: an observer who reports exactly these things about a practitioner reports something the book has already classified as a departure from the method."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:SteinerUebungAmSamen ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SteinerWieErlangtMan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Anthroposophy .

pmc:StrikingOfSpirits a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Schlagen der Geister und der Leibesfrucht"@de,
        "Striking of spirits and of the womb"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 69:12 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "The disclosure of harmful operations: the striking of spirits and demons, the killing of the embryo in the womb, the serpent's bite and the stroke of the noonday heat."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:SubidaNoticiaAmorosaGeneral a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die allgemeine liebende Kenntnis als Habitus"@de,
        "The general loving knowledge held as a habit"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Juan de la Cruz, Subida del Monte Carmelo II.XII (ed. Gerardo, Toledo 1912, pp. 154-155; II.14 in Lewis), read in the Spanish against Lewis 1906/1922 and the Carmelites of Paris, Oudin 1893" ;
    skos:altLabel "habito y sustancia de una noticia amorosa general"@en ;
    skos:definition "The state the practice is said to produce: what the soul used to draw piecemeal by the labour of meditating into particular knowledges has become in it, by use, a habit and substance of one general loving knowledge, not distinct and not particular as before. On placing itself in prayer it drinks without labour and in sweetness, as one who has the water brought to him and need not draw it up through the conduits of past considerations and forms and figures."@en ;
    skos:note "The text adds that God also works this in many souls without the means of those acts, or at least without many having preceded, putting them straight into contemplation and love. So the habit is claimed to arise both by repetition and without it, and the source says both in the same sentence."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SubidaDelMonteCarmelo ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinMysticism .

pmc:SubidaSenalesCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Zwei Zustaende, die die Zeichen nachahmen: Zerstreuung, und ein Saft im Hirn oder im Herzen"@de,
        "Two conditions that counterfeit the signs: distraction, and a humour in the brain or the heart"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Juan de la Cruz, Subida del Monte Carmelo II.XI (ed. Gerardo, Toledo 1912, pp. 151-152; II.13 in Lewis), read in the Spanish against Lewis 1906/1922 and the Carmelites of Paris, Oudin 1893" ;
    skos:note "The caution is a differential diagnosis and it is the most useful thing in the opened block. The first sign alone is not enough, because the inability to imagine and meditate on the things of God as before may come from the practitioner's own distraction and little recollection; the mark that distinguishes the two is the second sign, since a man whose inability comes from distraction or lukewarmness at once has an appetite to put the imagination on other things and a motive for leaving. The first and the second together are not enough either, because the inability to reason joined to the absence of any appetite for other things may proceed de melancolia o de otro algun jugo de humor puesto en el cerebro o corazon, from melancholy or some other humour settled in the brain or the heart, which produce in the sense a certain soaking and suspension that make the man think of nothing and have no wish to, but stay in that savoury absorption. Against that the third sign is required, the loving knowledge and attention in peace."@en,
        "The caution names its own hardest case in the same place: at the beginning of the state the loving knowledge is almost imperceptible, for two reasons the text gives, so the third sign is at its weakest exactly where it has the most work to do. A practitioner in the soaking of a humour and a practitioner at the threshold of contemplation are told apart by a sign that is at that moment subtle, delicate and as it were unfelt. That is stated by the source and not inferred here."@en,
        "The consequence of this caution is in the note and not in a predicate, because pm:Cautioning cannot hold one; prima-materia#621 is the finding and this is a fresh case for it. Commented there in the run of 2026-10-08."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:SubidaTresSenales ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SubidaDelMonteCarmelo ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinMysticism .

pmc:SubidaSenalesConiunctae a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die drei Zeichen zusammen, und nicht weniger, sind vor dem Verlassen der Meditation gefordert"@de,
        "The three signs together, and not fewer, are required before meditation may be left"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Juan de la Cruz, Subida del Monte Carmelo II.XI-XII (ed. Gerardo, Toledo 1912, pp. 149-157; II.13-14 in Lewis), read in the Spanish against Lewis 1906/1922 and the Carmelites of Paris, Oudin 1893" ;
    skos:note "The Spanish of the conjunction is Estas tres senales ha de ver en si juntas por lo menos el espiritual para atreverse seguramente a dejar el estado de meditacion y del sentido y entrar en el de contemplacion y del espiritu: all three together, at the least, before he may venture safely. The strength is pm:statedNecessity and not pm:prescribedOrder because the consequence of skipping is quoted in the source and not inferred."@en,
        "The prior step is the practice and not the signs, because the signs are the criterion by which the step is judged complete and not a step that is performed. pmc:SubidaSenalesCautela holds what the text says goes wrong when fewer than three are present."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Going back. The text states the necessity in both directions in one sentence: as it is fitting to leave the imaginary meditation at its time so as not to be hindered, so it is necessary not to leave it before its time, para no volver atras, in order not to go backwards. It then describes what going backwards is: the practitioner looks for the rind of discourse, does not find it because it has been taken away, so enjoys neither the substance nor the meditation, and troubles himself thinking that he is going back and losing. The apprehensions of the sensitive faculties are not a proximate means of union for the advanced, but they remain a remote means for beginners, by which the spirit is disposed and the lower temporal and secular images emptied; a practitioner who leaves them before that work is done has lost the means and not reached the end."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:SubidaAdvertenciaAmorosa ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SubidaDelMonteCarmelo ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:SubidaMeditacionDiscursiva ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinMysticism .

pmc:SubidaTresSenales a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die drei Zeichen fuer das Verlassen der diskursiven Meditation"@de,
        "The three signs for leaving discursive meditation"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Juan de la Cruz, Subida del Monte Carmelo II.XI (ed. Gerardo, Toledo 1912, pp. 150-152; II.13 in Lewis), read in the Spanish against Lewis 1906/1922 and the Carmelites of Paris, Oudin 1893" ;
    skos:altLabel "las senales que ha de ver en si el espiritual"@en,
        "tres senales"@en ;
    skos:definition "The signs the practitioner is to observe in himself in order to know the time at which he may leave discursive meditation. The first is to find that he can no longer meditate or work with the imagination and takes no pleasure in it as he used to, finding dryness where he used to fix the sense and draw relish. The second is that he has no inclination to put the imagination or the sense on other particular things, outward or inward: not that it does not come and go, which it does even in great recollection, but that the soul takes no pleasure in putting it there on purpose. The third and most certain is that the soul takes pleasure in being alone with a loving attention to God, without particular consideration, in interior peace and quiet and rest and without acts of the faculties."@en ;
    skos:note "The Spanish of the heading is Las senales que ha de ver en si el espiritual para dejar la meditacion discursiva son tres. The first sign carries its own restriction in the same sentence: en tanto que hallare jugo y pudiere discurrir en la meditacion, no la ha de dejar, so long as he finds relish and can reason in meditation he is not to leave it."@en,
        "The chapter number is edition-dependent and both numbers are given at every node of this file. In the Gerardo text of Toledo 1912 the three signs stand at the end of Libro II, capitulo XI, pp. 150-152, with the running head LIBRO II, CAP. XI, and capitulo XII proves them; in Lewis they are chapter XIII and the proof is chapter XIV. The cause is a division and not a renumbering: what Gerardo carries as one chapter, on the imaginary and natural apprehensions and then the signs, Lewis divides into XII and XIII. The offset of two holds through the rest of the book, so Gerardo XVI, XVII, XVIII and XIX are Lewis XVIII, XIX, XX and XXI."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SubidaDelMonteCarmelo ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinMysticism .

pmc:SuiyueSuoshi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Viertel, das Jahr und Monat verzehren"@de,
        "The quarter the year and the month eat"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Wang Chong, Lunheng, the fourth and last chapter of juan 23, against the year and month taboos on breaking ground, Sibu congkan facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "The doctrine of the common practice of the first century, as the treatise that attacks it reports it. When earth is broken and building begun, the year and the month each have a quarter they eat, and in the quarter eaten there will certainly be deaths. The example runs by the branches: with the year star at zi the year eats at you, and with the first month established at yin the month eats at si, so that work begun in the zi or the yin ground is eaten out of the households standing at you and at si. The harm is graded by the size of the work: a labour of three feet eats within one pace, a labour of ten zhang eats a li away. Those who find themselves eaten hang up against it an object of the phase that conquers, metal where the west is eaten and charcoal where the east is; or they set out a sacrifice to remove the ill; or they leave the dwelling empty and move away."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as its opponent reports it, and the citation says so. No transmission of the practice itself has been run down in this holding: what stands is a first-century critic's account of what his contemporaries did, quoted closely enough that the branch arithmetic can be checked against the doctrine's own rule. Where a doctrine enters a graph only through the man attacking it, the graph should say which sentences are his summary and which are his argument, and that is what the separation of this node from the dispute below is for."@en,
        "The graded scale is the part worth keeping. It is the only quantitative claim in the material this file holds about the year and month taboos: it fixes a distance from a size, and it is what makes the counter-cases possible at all. Without the scale, the wall of Qin and the building of Luo would say nothing about the doctrine, because nothing would follow from the size of the work."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination .

pmc:SunCourseRule a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Regel des Sonnenlaufs"@de,
        "The rule of the sun's course"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, p. 57",
        "Knud Leem, An Account of the Laplanders of Finmark, in John Pinkerton (ed.), A General Collection of the Best and Most Interesting Voyages and Travels, vol. I (London: Longman, Hurst, Rees and Orme, 1808), chap. XXI, pp. 474 and 477" ;
    skos:definition "The single rule by which the drum is read. If the ring travels round towards the right hand, with the course of the sun, health, fortune and increase of men and beasts are promised. If it goes the contrary way, towards the left, sickness and all the evils of ill success are expected. The later recorder gives the same rule in weaker terms on the negative side: with the sun it augured a favourable event, against the sun a doubt then arose of a prosperous one."@en ;
    skos:note "One further reading is given by the later recorder alone, from a named practitioner: that if the copper bird laid on the skin falls from the drum to the earth, it is a sign that the person enquired of will soon die. That is a third outcome beside the two directions, and it is entered here because a binary instrument with a third outcome is not binary."@en,
        "The difference between them is not nothing. The earlier makes the contrary turn a prediction of evil; the later makes it only a doubt. Whether that is a change in the practice, a difference between regions, or the difference between a compiler stating a rule and a missionary reporting what a man told him is not decidable from the two passages."@en,
        "Two recorders, ninety years apart, one compiling from priests' reports in Uppsala and one living among the people, give the same reading rule tied to the same physical direction. It is the best-attested single claim in this file."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SamiNoaidi .

pmc:SusrutaMarmasparsabhava a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Klausel, die einen Ausgang ausschließt: ein abgetrenntes Glied heilt, wenn kein marman getroffen ist"@de,
        "The clause that excludes an outcome: a severed limb heals if no marman is touched"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Susruta Samhita, Sarirasthana VI.84, in the English of Kaviraj Kunjalal Bhishagratna, An English Translation of the Sushruta Samhita, vol. II, Calcutta 1911, p. 189" ;
    skos:definition "The life of the patient is not to be despaired of even in a fracture or crushing of a bone of the Koshtha, the Sirah or the Kapala, or in a perforation of the intestines, if the local marmas are found not to be hurt. Recovery is common in cuts in the Sakthi, the Bhuja, the Pada and the Kara or in any other part of the body, and even where a whole leg or hand is found to be severed and carried away, if the marmas are not in any way hurt."@en ;
    skos:note "It also stands against the verse six places later, which says that an injury to a marman, whether severe or slight, is sure to bring deformity or death. The two sentences are not compatible in their strongest readings and the chapter prints both. The dispute node beside this one holds that and does not resolve it."@en,
        "This is the half of the doctrine that makes the other half mean something, and it is the reason the chapter is in this holding at all. A claim that wounds to vital places kill is cheap. A claim that a crushed skull, a perforated gut and a severed limb are commonly survived provided no marman was touched is not: it names a class of cases that would tell against the doctrine, and it names them in a form a surgeon sees."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SusrutaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmc:SwedenborgRespiratioInterna a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die innere Atmung, von innen geführt und dem Menschen nicht wahrnehmbar"@de,
        "The internal respiration, led from within and imperceptible to man"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Swedenborg, Arcana coelestia, n. 3884 (The Heavenly Arcana, trans. Potts, Standard Edition, vol. V, pp. 237-238)" ;
    skos:definition "One of four general operations of heaven the author reports perceiving while in the body: it entered into the respiration of the lungs and led his breathing gently but from within, so that he had no need to draw breath or respire by any exertion of his will. The respiration of heaven, he says, is internal and for that reason imperceptible to man, and inflows by correspondence into the external respiration of the body; were a man deprived of that influx he would instantly fall down dead. The pulse in that state was about three beats to each period of respiration."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because this is the one place where the corpus touches the thing a reader of the modern occult literature comes to it for, and it is not what that literature made of it. There is no exercise here. The breathing is led, the will is not used, and the author states that the internal respiration cannot be perceived at all. Whoever reads this passage as a technique has read the later reception; the search for an instruction anywhere in the corpus stands at pmp:SwedenborgPraxisSearch."@en,
        "The author's own footnote elsewhere names the further Arcana numbers on this subject, among them 3894 on the wicked being unable to breathe in heaven and suffocating if they enter. That number was not opened and nothing here rests on it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArcanaCoelestia ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Swedenborgian .

pmc:SwedenborgSignaNonCoguntFidem a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Erscheinungen werden vorenthalten, weil ein erzwungener Glaube dem Menschen nicht anhaftet"@de,
        "Appearances are withheld because a compelled faith does not cleave to the man"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Swedenborg, Arcana coelestia, n. 10751 (The Heavenly Arcana, trans. Potts, Standard Edition, vol. XII, p. 654)" ;
    skos:definition "Having listed those to whom spirits appeared in human form — Abraham, Lot, the inhabitants of Sodom, Manoah and his wife, Joshua, Mary, Elizabeth and the prophets generally — the corpus says that now this rarely happens, lest such things should compel men to believe; for a compulsory faith, such as enters by means of miracles, does not cleave to the man, and might also be an injury to those with whom faith could be implanted through the Word in a state that is not compulsory."@en ;
    skos:note "It is the fourth witness of pmc:TheTestOfAPersonIsRefused and the note there says what it adds and what it does not. It is not independent of two of the other three: the magnetic and spiritist literatures are later than this corpus and knew it."@en,
        "The ground is a property of what a sign produces and not a property of whoever asks for one, and that is what makes this passage usable. It says that the class of evidence an outsider would demand is withheld on purpose, because evidence of that class produces a belief that does not hold. Whatever one thinks of the doctrine, the consequence for examination is exact: the corpus has given a reason why no demonstration will be offered, and the reason does not depend on the enquirer."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArcanaCoelestia ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Swedenborgian .

pmc:Taixi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Embryonalatmung"@de,
        "Embryonic breathing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 8 (Shizhi), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 8" ;
    skos:definition "The chapter names it the great essential of the whole art: whoever has obtained the embryonic breathing can breathe without using nose or mouth, as though he were within the womb, and then the way is complete."@en ;
    skos:note "The definition is stated as a threshold and not as a degree, which is unusual in this collection: not better breathing but breathing without the two openings. It is also the point at which the chapter stops being examinable from outside, because the sign it gives for the lower stage, the down that does not stir, would be satisfied trivially by someone who had reached this one."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BaopuziNeipian ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:TaoHongjingZhenwei a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Regel des Kompilators für die Scheidung der echten Blätter"@de,
        "The compiler's rule for telling the genuine sheets from the rest"@en ;
    dcterms:date "499" ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 19.5b-19.7b (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 19" ;
    skos:definition "The order the work is in was made afterwards and by a stated method, which the compiler sets out. The criterion is the hand: he distinguishes the three men's writing, marks each item with whose hand it is in, and where he cannot recognise a hand he says so and labels the unidentified hands with the cyclical characters so that a reader can keep them apart. He states that later hands have added, cut and altered words, that most of this is the work of the younger Xu and his son, and that much of it is empty. He states that he does not reproduce the hands in facsimile, so that the distinction he makes cannot be checked from his book; that where a character is wrong he leaves it and rings it rather than correcting it; and that where sheets had rotted away he has joined what was left and marked the joins."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as an ordering act and not as a testimony, because that is what it is: no Perfected said any of it. Its value is that it is one of the few places in this holding where a transmitter states his criterion of authenticity, states its limits, and concedes in the same breath that his edition does not carry the evidence the criterion runs on."@en ;
    pm:compiledBy "Tao Hongjing 陶弘景, the recluse of Huayang" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:Tapu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Tapu"@de,
        "Tapu"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), pp. 25-26" ;
    skos:definition "The state of being sacred and thereby prohibited, held to be communicated from an atua or a sacred person to whatever they touch, and from that object onward to whatever it touches in turn."@en ;
    skos:note "The re-transmission by contact is the part later theory generalised. A record that carries only the prohibition and not the transmissibility loses exactly what the nineteenth-century comparativists picked up and turned into an impersonal magical fluid. See prima-materia#103 and prima-materia#104 for the originations that are not yet entered."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Maori .

pmc:TassaYevaSaPathavi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Nur für ihn wird der Boden zu Wasser und nur für ihn das Wasser zu Erde"@de,
        "Only for him does the ground become water, and only for him the water earth"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.94 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, p. 459; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.97 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, p. 459; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "The clause the handbook attaches to both of the two operations that change a surface. Only for him does that ground become water; for everybody else it stays ground, and people walk on it, drive over it and plough it as before. Only for him does that water become earth; for everybody else it stays water, and fishes, turtles and water birds go about in it as they like. The restriction is not a limit on the power: if he wishes that it be water for them too, it becomes water for them too, and the same in the other direction. And when the determined time has run out, the marked extent goes back to what it was, except for water that was in pots and ponds to begin with."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a node of its own, and not as a remark on the two capacities, because of what it does to a witness. An operation that turns a road into water is the most public event a transmission could claim; this clause makes it, by default, an event that no passer-by can see, and leaves the extension of the perception to a wish of the operator's that nobody else can observe him making. A bystander who saw nothing has seen exactly what the text says he would see."@en,
        "The wording is not weaker in the second translation. Pe Maung Tin has only for him is the ground turned into water, for others it remains ground; Ñāṇamoli has it is only for him that that earth becomes water, not for anyone else. Both carry the permission to extend it, and both carry the reversion at the end of the determined time."@en,
        "What it is not. It is not an account of a failure and does not stand where failures are accounted for. It stands in the operative instruction, between the resolve and the time limit, as a property of the operation; the handbook is not explaining away an absence but describing what it holds to happen. That is the difference between this clause and the family of excuses the holding collects at pmc:TheRivalWhoWasNotInTheRoom, and it is the reason this one is worth more: the excuse is made after a session, and this was written before any."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:TaulipangImmunityInferredFromTheAnimal a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Tier frisst das Schaedliche und wird nicht geschaedigt, also muss es einen Zauberspruch kennen"@de,
        "The animal eats the harmful thing and is not harmed, therefore it must know a spell"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Theodor Koch-Gruenberg, Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, Band III: Ethnographie (Stuttgart: Strecker und Schroeder, 1923), pp. 220-221" ;
    skos:definition "The reasoning the transmission gives for which animal is invoked against which complaint, and it is an argument from an observed exception. Ulcers are held to arise from eating the large game - tapir, deer, wild pig. To drive them out the various kinds of jaguar are invoked, because the jaguars can frighten the ulcers: they eat all this game and yet do not suffer from ulcers, so they must possess a magic power, must know a spell that protects them from the bad influence of this food. The same for the otters, invoked against the diarrhoea of newborns because they live on the big fish that are forbidden to the parents of a newborn and their young come to no harm; and for the two dogs invoked against intestinal worms, because dogs often have worms without dying of them."@en ;
    skos:note "Its relation to pmc:TheObservationAnsweredByNarrowing, and why it is not entered as a witness of it. That statement is about a transmission that puts an observation against its own claim and answers by restricting the claim or reassigning the cause of the observation, and this is the second kind of answer exactly. It would be the first witness from a transmission with no route to the other three, which is what that statement most needs. It is not claimed as one here for one reason: the inferential step - they must therefore know a spell - is the recorder's rendering of the Indians' reasoning and is carried by his own therefore, with his aside, as we would say, immune to it. A witness of that statement has to be a transmission saying the thing, and whether this one says it is decidable only from the spells themselves, which are printed with their original wording in the same volume and were not read in this run. That is named as the next step and not as a result."@en,
        "Three instances of one inference, and the inference is from a negative instance to a concealed cause. The transmission holds an aetiology; it observes a class of eaters in which the cause is present and the effect absent; and instead of giving up the aetiology it posits in the exception a hidden agent that protects it - and then borrows that agent. The animal is functioning as a control group and the conclusion drawn from the control is a spell."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Reported by Theodor Koch-Gruenberg in his own voice as the belief and the reasoning of the Taulipang, with the phrase wie die Indianer sagen marking the part he had from them; recorded in the Roroima region between 1911 and 1913, published 1923. No individual speaker is named. The original wording of the spells these notes introduce is printed in the same volume and was not read." ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VomRoroimaZumOrinocoIII ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Taulipang .

pmc:TaulipangJaguarCertifiedByTheBystanders a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Er erfuhr von seiner eigenen Verwandlung am anderen Tag von den anderen, und schaemte sich"@de,
        "He learnt of his own transformation from the others the next day, and was ashamed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Theodor Koch-Gruenberg, Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, Band III: Ethnographie (Stuttgart: Strecker und Schroeder, 1923), pp. 200-201" ;
    skos:definition "The claim and the case that goes with it, and the case runs the other way from almost everything in this holding. When the Zauberaerzte are very drunk they turn into jaguars without knowing it. Akuli told the recorder that he had himself turned into a jaguar at a great dance feast at Roroima, in the dance house, in front of everybody's eyes; that the people fled and barricaded the house; and that he climbed up a house post and fell down. When the people told him about it the next day, he was very ashamed."@en ;
    skos:note "A second case on the same page is the recorder's and is the clearest confound explanation in this corpus. Katura reported that the Taulipang chief Dailawo had met him as a jaguar while he was fishing alone, and that he had shot arrows into its collarbone, after which Dailawo was ill for some time. The recorder writes that Katura evidently shot a real jaguar, and that because Dailawo fell ill at the same time the two were brought into connection. That reassignment is the recorder's and not the transmission's, and it is recorded here rather than in the definition for that reason."@en,
        "The reason this is a find. The holding's standing complaint about claimed capacities is that the transmission reserves the perception of the effect to the operator, which generalizations/the-effect-put-beyond-the-bystander.ttl collects. Here the effect is reserved to the bystanders and withheld from the operator: the man did not know it happened, everybody else saw it, and his only evidence is their testimony. A capacity whose occurrence only others can certify is in principle the easier kind to test, because the operator's expectation cannot produce the report."@en,
        "What the case nevertheless does not give, and it must be said because the shape is so promising. The event reported is not a jaguar: it is a very drunk man at a feast who climbed a post and fell off it, and the bystanders' flight is as consistent with his behaviour as with his shape. So the report is of an occasion rather than of a transformation, and the one witness whose account would separate the two - somebody who says what he saw rather than what it was - is not in the passage. The recorder himself attaches a parallel from an eighteenth-century missionary among the Abipones, where the practitioners threaten to turn into tigers and begin by imitating the roar, and those around them run."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by Akuli, a named Taulipang, of himself; recorded by Theodor Koch-Gruenberg in the Roroima region between 1911 and 1913 and published 1923, in the recorder's German rendering of the oral account in reported speech, with the Taulipang wording not carried beside it. The second case named in the notes was spoken by Katura, likewise named." ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VomRoroimaZumOrinocoIII ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Taulipang .

pmc:TaulipangLongerStudyStrongerShaman a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Je laenger das Studium waehrt, desto staerker wird der Zauberarzt"@de,
        "The longer the study lasts, the stronger the Zauberarzt becomes"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Theodor Koch-Gruenberg, Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, Band III: Ethnographie (Stuttgart: Strecker und Schroeder, 1923), p. 205" ;
    skos:definition "The claim the transmission makes about its own training, stated as a rule and not as encouragement. The apprenticeship lasts as a rule about ten years, sometimes sixteen to twenty, even twenty-five; and the longer the study lasts, the stronger the Zauberarzt becomes."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as its own node because of its form, which is rare in this holding. It is a monotonic claim relating a quantity somebody could measure - the years a man studied - to a magnitude the transmission treats as real, and it is not hedged by a threshold or a sufficiency clause. Almost every claim about attainment in this holding either names a term after which the thing is had or names no quantity at all; this one says more is more, without end."@en,
        "What it would take to put it to a test is also what makes it safe. Strength here is not given any mark: nothing in the passage says what a stronger Zauberarzt does that a weaker one cannot, and the one measure of competence the corpus does supply - the number of Mauari that have fallen - is a criterion for release and not for strength. So the claim is comparative without a comparand, and the node records the absence rather than supplying one."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by Akuli, a named Taulipang, of his own training; recorded by Theodor Koch-Gruenberg in the Roroima region of northern Brazil between 1911 and 1913 and published by him in 1923. The language of the gathering was the recorder's German rendering of Akuli's oral account, which the recorder says at the head of the passage he gives exactly as it was told to him; the Taulipang wording is not carried beside it, and the technical terms are printed in Taulipang throughout." ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VomRoroimaZumOrinocoIII ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Taulipang .

pmc:TaulipangMauariCrystalFallsIntoThePocket a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Jede Nacht kommt ein Mauari als Kristallstein und faellt in die Tasche des Novizen"@de,
        "Each night a Mauari comes as a rock crystal and falls into the novice's pocket"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Theodor Koch-Gruenberg, Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, Band III: Ethnographie (Stuttgart: Strecker und Schroeder, 1923), p. 208" ;
    skos:definition "The sign of attainment, announced to the novice before it happens. After five or six months of nightly singing and morning dancing, every night one Mauari comes for each novice, arriving as a rock crystal and falling into his pocket, a hunting bag of jaguar or otter skin or knitted cotton. The Mauari then passes out of the crystal into the boy's body. After a time a second comes, after a time a third, and so on, until each boy has a whole number of them."@en ;
    skos:note "This is a sign of success of the kind SPEC section 15 says must be counted separately: it is told to the novice in advance, so he reports it, and the base rate is unknown because nobody measures the same sign in anyone who was not told to expect it. It is entered and it is not counted among the conditions of failure."@en,
        "Two things nevertheless make it stronger than most of its register. The crystals are real objects, found in several colours at Roroima and at the Serra das Cristaes, and the recorder says he repeatedly watched Zauberaerzte carry them about and blow tobacco smoke on them at night while murmuring formulas - so the thing that arrives is an object a bystander can see, even if its arrival is not. And the arrival is countable, which is what makes the criterion of release at pmc:TaulipangReleaseWhenManyHaveFallen possible at all."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by Akuli, a named Taulipang, of his own training; recorded by Theodor Koch-Gruenberg in the Roroima region between 1911 and 1913, published 1923, in the recorder's German rendering of the oral account. The recorder adds in his own voice, in square brackets, that he several times observed the crystals in use on the journey; that part is his own observation and not Akuli's testimony." ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VomRoroimaZumOrinocoIII ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Taulipang .

pmc:TaulipangNeverOneNoviceAlone a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Niemals ein Novize allein, und bei vier stets zwei Lehrmeister"@de,
        "Never one novice alone, and with four of them always two masters"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Theodor Koch-Gruenberg, Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, Band III: Ethnographie (Stuttgart: Strecker und Schroeder, 1923), p. 205" ;
    skos:definition "A rule about the composition of the group rather than about the operation. A Zauberarzt is never alone: he always has a companion, whom he often brings up himself, and some have three or four. It is never one novice; at least two are taught together, sometimes three, sometimes four, and with four novices there are always two masters."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because the holding has little of this and the ratio is stated rather than implied. What it is for is not said. Two readings are open and the passage decides neither: that the office requires a second person in the work, which the cure does, since another Zauberarzt is the one who brings the novices' souls back at pmc:TaulipangSoulRetrievedByTheMaster; or that teaching two at once is simply how it is done. The first would make the pairing part of the capacity and the second part of the schooling."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by Akuli, a named Taulipang; recorded by Theodor Koch-Gruenberg in the Roroima region between 1911 and 1913, published 1923, in the recorder's German rendering of the oral account, with the Taulipang wording not carried beside it." ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VomRoroimaZumOrinocoIII ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Taulipang .

pmc:TaulipangNoCureDuringRain a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Niemals bei Regen, und ein Gewitter bricht die Kur sofort ab"@de,
        "Never during rain, and a thunderstorm breaks the cure off at once"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Theodor Koch-Gruenberg, Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, Band III: Ethnographie (Stuttgart: Strecker und Schroeder, 1923), p. 195" ;
    skos:definition "The one circumstance the transmission lets interrupt an operation in progress. The Zauberarzt never exercises his office during rain, and if a thunderstorm suddenly breaks out while he is at work he breaks the cure off immediately and begins it again from the beginning the following night."@en ;
    skos:note "It also supplies a resumption rule, which conditions of this kind usually do not: not later the same night, but the following night, and from the beginning. A cure may run several nights in succession in any case, so the cost of the interruption is one night and the transmission says so. Nothing is said about what the interruption does to the sick man in the meantime, and nothing names a number of interruptions after which the attempt is given up."@en,
        "Worth a node because of the shape and not the weather. The condition is outside both parties - neither the practitioner nor the sick man brings on a thunderstorm - and it is the only condition in the part read that is. The holding's register of conditions set outside the practitioner is generalizations/what-attainment-relieves.ttl and generalizations/the-waiver-that-runs-both-ways.ttl, and both are about requirements a transmission then releases somebody from; this is a requirement with no release named for anybody."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:TaulipangSoulLeavesByTheHairCord ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Reported by Theodor Koch-Gruenberg in his own voice as the practice of the Taulipang Zauberaerzte, in the Roroima region between 1911 and 1913, published 1923; no individual speaker is named for this sentence. At the same place the recorder states what he himself witnessed: he several times listened to the conjurations of Katura, one of the most respected Zauberaerzte of the Taulipang, at Koimelemong, separated from him only by the thin leaf wall of the hut, and Katura later entrusted his magical songs to the phonograph, behaving exactly as if he had a sick man in front of him." ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VomRoroimaZumOrinocoIII ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Taulipang .

pmc:TaulipangPiranhaCutsTheSkyLadder a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Pirayafische wuerden ihm die Himmelsleiter abschneiden, so dass er nie mehr in die Hoehe steigen koennte"@de,
        "Piranha would cut his ladder to the sky so that he could never ascend again"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Theodor Koch-Gruenberg, Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, Band III: Ethnographie (Stuttgart: Strecker und Schroeder, 1923), p. 209" ;
    skos:definition "The one prohibition that outlasts the training, and the transmission says so by naming everything else as released. Once his master has released him, the young Zauberarzt may again drink and eat everything except piranha, for these would cut off his ladder to the sky, so that he could never ascend again."@en ;
    skos:note "The mechanism is stated and it is mechanical, which is what makes this worth a node rather than a line. The ladder is not a figure: it is the karauali, the cord twisted from the master's wife's hair and drawn through the novice's nostrils, which the account says serves the shadow of the Zauberarzt as a ladder to climb up by while the body stays on the earth, and the step-shaped liana kapeyenkuma(x)pe serves the same purpose. A piranha is the fish whose teeth the Taulipang use as cutting tools, which the recorder notes at the place. So the reason the fish is forbidden is that it cuts, and what it would cut is the instrument of the capacity."@en,
        "The recorder's footnote widens it and should not be read as part of the claim. He notes that among some neighbouring peoples the finished practitioner is also under food prohibitions - the Galibi may not eat certain fish and game or he would lose his power, the Warrau may not eat the flesh of larger animals - and those are other peoples reported by other authors. The Taulipang claim is the one named fish and the one named mechanism."@en,
        "Why the pairing with pmc:TaulipangWoleleLeavesBurnTheThroat is the finding and not either one alone. Between them they cover both faculties the office consists of - singing and ascending - and each is destroyed by one named food by one named physical action. A transmission that can be disabled in two specified ways has told a practitioner something he could act on and an observer something he could look for, and that is a different kind of statement from a danger in general. Both are also releases in reverse: the first binds only during the training, the second only after it, so the two together say that the capacity is vulnerable at every stage and in a different way at each."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Denn diese wuerden ihm die Himmelsleiter abschneiden, so dass er nie mehr in die Hoehe steigen koennte - for these would cut off his ladder to the sky, so that he could never ascend again." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:TaulipangSoulLeavesByTheHairCord ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by Akuli, a named Taulipang, of his own training; recorded by Theodor Koch-Gruenberg in the Roroima region between 1911 and 1913, published 1923, in the recorder's German rendering of the oral account, with the fish identified by the recorder as Serrasalmo sp. in his own footnote. The Taulipang wording of the sentence is not carried beside it." ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VomRoroimaZumOrinocoIII ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:TaulipangZauberarztApprenticeship ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Taulipang .

pmc:TaulipangSoulRetrievedByTheMaster a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Seele des Lehrmeisters holt die Seelen der Novizen zurueck, und der Koerper erwacht, bevor die Seele zurueckkommt"@de,
        "The master's soul fetches the novices' souls back, and the body wakes before the soul returns"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Theodor Koch-Gruenberg, Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, Band III: Ethnographie (Stuttgart: Strecker und Schroeder, 1923), p. 207" ;
    skos:definition "What the transmission makes the outcome of the soul-journey depend on. The novices lie as if dead with only the heart beating and their whole soul gone. The master orders named leaves to be bound together so that he can blow on them, that is cure them; then he begins to sing, and his own soul goes away to the house of the Mauari, where the boys' souls are dancing with the Mauari's daughters, and he brings the souls of the boys back. Before the soul comes back the body wakes; only then does the Zauberarzt arrive with the soul. Then the boys get up and speak again, and dance."@en ;
    skos:note "The construction is the one generalizations/the-one-who-stays-outside.ttl is about, with one part of it reversed, and the difference is the reason for this node. There a transmission puts its practitioner into a condition in which he can no longer check the operation and names a second person who stays outside it and on whom the outcome depends. Here the outcome does depend on a second person, and the novices cannot come back without him - but he does not stay outside. He enters the same condition, sends his own soul after theirs, and the retrieval happens inside the state rather than from the bank. So the dependency holds and the safeguard does not: there is nobody in the hut who is awake."@en,
        "The one sentence that would be a check is also the one with nothing to check against. The body wakes before the soul returns, which gives an interval in which a bystander would see a waking body that the transmission says has no soul in it yet. Nothing says how long that interval is, what the body does in it, or what it would mean if the soul never arrived. The absence is recorded and not filled."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by Akuli, a named Taulipang, of his own training; recorded by Theodor Koch-Gruenberg in the Roroima region between 1911 and 1913, published 1923, in the recorder's German rendering of the oral account, with the Taulipang wording not carried beside it." ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VomRoroimaZumOrinocoIII ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Taulipang .

pmc:TaulipangSpellsNeedNoInitiation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Zaubersprueche koennen nicht nur von Begnadeten, sondern von jedem mit Erfolg angewandt werden"@de,
        "The spells can be used with success not only by the gifted but by anybody"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Theodor Koch-Gruenberg, Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, Band III: Ethnographie (Stuttgart: Strecker und Schroeder, 1923), p. 220" ;
    skos:definition "The statement that separates this part of the corpus from the office described in the rest of it. The Taulipang have numerous spells which can be used with success not only by specially gifted people such as Zauberaerzte but by anybody, and on all sorts of occasions: against wounds, ulcers, snakebite, the sting of a ray, for and against inflammation of the throat, against pustules, intestinal worms, the vomiting and diarrhoea of newborns, to make birth harder and to make it easier, and to turn enemies into friends."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because of the gate criterion in SPEC section 0, which the holding is built to record: valuable is what removes a gate. Beside a ten-to-twenty-five-year apprenticeship with named emetics and two permanent disqualifications, the same transmission puts a body of operations that need no initiation, no lineage and no teacher, and says in as many words that anyone can work them successfully. The two stand in one people and one volume, so the contrast is not between transmissions but inside one."@en,
        "One thing works the other way and is in the same paragraph. The recorder says the spells are rarely spoken in full public but usually murmured in the intimacy of the family, and that the Indian does not readily give them up to a European, probably for fear of weakening the magic power or losing it altogether. So the operations are open to everybody in the people and withheld from outside it, and the ground given for withholding is a claim about the operation rather than about authority. The recorder then prints them anyway, which is the self-warning this corpus carries against what it itself does."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Reported by Theodor Koch-Gruenberg in his own voice of the Taulipang as a people, from the Roroima region between 1911 and 1913, published 1923; no individual speaker is named. The spells themselves are printed in the same volume with the original wording and an interlinear German rendering, and were not read in this run." ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VomRoroimaZumOrinocoIII ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Taulipang .

pmc:TaulipangTheTrunkWasEmpty a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Er sperrte den Vater der Krankheit in einen Koffer, und am Morgen war nichts darin"@de,
        "He locked the father of the sickness in a trunk, and in the morning there was nothing in it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Theodor Koch-Gruenberg, Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, Band III: Ethnographie (Stuttgart: Strecker und Schroeder, 1923), p. 200" ;
    skos:definition "A practitioner's own report of a check he made and of what he concluded when it came back empty. In August 1911 three Taulipang died one after another of a kind of dysentery at Koimelemong. As the sickness was dying down, Katura said that he had sung in the night, that a big strong man had come whom he had wrestled with, that he had finally overcome him and locked him in a trunk - and that when he looked in the next morning, there was nothing in it. It was probably the father of the sickness, Katura said."@en ;
    skos:note "The reading does not claim that Katura devised the check as a test. Nothing in the passage says why he looked, and a man who has locked something in a trunk may open it for any reason. What is established is that the looking happened, that it is reported by the man who did it, and that the result did not count against the catch."@en,
        "This is the rarest thing read in this corpus and the reason it is entered on its own. A practitioner performed an operation, took a step that would verify it - he put the thing in a container he could open - and opened the container. The outcome was negative and perceptible to anybody. He did not treat it as a failure; the emptiness was taken to show what kind of being had been caught. The verification was real and was absorbed by a property assigned to the object after the fact."@en,
        "Where it belongs in the holding, and it does not belong in the register it most resembles. pmc:NoFailedRunCountsAgainstTheOperation is about a run that produced nothing and where the transmission sends it; here the run is said to have produced something, and what produced nothing is the check on it. That is one step further out, and the holding has nothing else of the kind: the containers and the residues it carries - the blood and dung of the rabbinic forensic test, the water that dissolves an enchantment - are all applied by somebody other than the operator. This one the operator applied to himself, in private, and reported both halves."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by Katura, a named Taulipang and one of the most respected Zauberaerzte of his people, of himself, at Koimelemong in the Roroima region; the events are dated by the recorder to August 1911 and the telling to the time when the sickness was abating. Recorded by Theodor Koch-Gruenberg, who states that he was absent at Sao Marcos while the three deaths happened, and published by him in 1923 in his own German rendering in reported speech, with the concluding sentence given in quotation marks as Katura's words and the Taulipang wording not carried beside it." ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VomRoroimaZumOrinocoIII ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Taulipang .

pmc:TaulipangWoleleLeavesBurnTheThroat a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Blaetter von wolele-yeg wuerden ihm die Kehle verbrennen, so dass er niemals wieder singen koennte"@de,
        "The leaves of wolele-yeg would burn his throat so that he could never sing again"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Theodor Koch-Gruenberg, Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, Band III: Ethnographie (Stuttgart: Strecker und Schroeder, 1923), p. 208" ;
    skos:definition "The sharpest of the two prohibitions of the apprenticeship. During the whole of the training the novice may drink only light kaschiri made from manioc cakes, called sa'buru, and no other kaschiri, no payua, and above all no pelakali. The last is brewed with the leaves of the plant wolele-yeg, which grows in the forest. Those leaves, the account says, would burn his whole throat, so that he could never sing again."@en ;
    skos:note "Its limit, and it is real. The claim that these leaves burn the throat is not tested here and nothing in the passage says that it ever happened to anybody. A prohibition with a stated consequence is a falsifiable claim and not a verified one, and whether this plant does what the transmission says it does is a question for a pm:Testing node that nobody has written. The identification of wolele-yeg is not given botanically in the passage, which is what a test would need first."@en,
        "This is a condition of failure of the kind SPEC section 15 holds to be the most valuable and the holding is shortest of, and it has every part of one. The substance is named and identified by where it grows. The mechanism is physical and stated. The loss is permanent - never again - and it is the loss of the one faculty the office is carried out with, since the Zauberarzt works by singing and the emetics of the training have powdered cicada nests mixed into them precisely to give him a beautiful voice. A man who drank it would not have performed the operation badly; he would have ceased to be able to perform it."@en,
        "What makes it unlike the excusing clauses of pmc:NoFailedRunCountsAgainstTheOperation, and the difference is worth stating because the two are easy to confuse. Those clauses account for a run that produced nothing, after the fact, by a condition nobody can check. This one is announced in advance, names what must not be done, and the consequence it names is observable by anybody: a man either sings or he does not. It does not excuse a failure; it predicts one."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Diese Blaetter wuerden ihm die ganze Kehle verbrennen, so dass er niemals wieder singen koennte - these leaves would burn his whole throat, so that he could never sing again." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:TaulipangReleaseWhenManyHaveFallen ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by Akuli, a named Taulipang, of his own training; recorded by Theodor Koch-Gruenberg in the Roroima region of northern Brazil between 1911 and 1913 and published by him in 1923, in the recorder's German rendering of the oral account, with the plant and the drink named in Taulipang. The Taulipang wording of the sentence is not carried beside it." ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VomRoroimaZumOrinocoIII ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:TaulipangZauberarztApprenticeship ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Taulipang .

pmc:Tawba a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Umkehr"@de,
        "Turning back"@en ;
    dcterms:source "al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya, chapter on the stations" ;
    skos:definition "Repentance, placed first among the stations in the handbook enumeration."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Sufism .

pmc:TestingOfThoughts a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Prüfung der Gedanken"@de,
        "The examination of thoughts"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ignatius of Loyola, Spiritual Exercises 334",
        "Ignatius of Loyola, Spiritual Exercises 336",
        "John Cassian, Collationes I.20-22" ;
    skos:definition "A four-part examination of an inner movement, set out in the image of the money-changer who tests a coin: for the metal, for the stamp, for the lawfulness of its provenance, and for its weight."@en ;
    skos:note "Ignatius adds the rule that the whole course of a train of thought is to be examined, since a bad end reveals the origin, and that a consolation arriving after the fact is to be examined well before it is believed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "John Cassian, transmitting Abba Moses; supplemented by Ignatius of Loyola" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A movement fails the test if its metal is false, its stamp is not that of right teaching, its provenance is unlawful, or it weighs light of the fear of God and heavy of human vanity; and it fails if the end of the train of thought is bad, however good its beginning." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-08-28" ;
    pm:tests pmc:DiscernmentOfOrigins ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChristianAsceticDiscernment .

pmc:TestingTheProcedureVoidsItsAnswer a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Verfahren verbietet die Frage, die es prüfen würde"@de,
        "The procedure forbids the question that would test it"@en ;
    skos:note "Falsified by a transmission of the same kind whose earliest witness permits or requires repeated consultation, and treats disagreement between consultations as a finding about the procedure rather than about the questioner. One such case refutes it, and the divinatory literature is wide enough that it may well exist."@en,
        "Read as a claim about the traditions it is unremarkable; read as a claim about examinability it is sharp. A rule of this shape makes the ordinary test — ask again and compare — a violation of the procedure, so that a failure to replicate is always attributable to the asking. It is not evidence that the procedures are empty; it is a statement about why the question has stayed open."@en,
        "Refuted on the day it was written, from material this holding already carried. pmp:TestingProhibitionExamination reports the deviation: the Zande poison oracle requires the second consultation and makes the verdict's validity depend on it, which inverts the mechanism this claim describes. The node stands because a refuted claim with its protocol beside it is a finding, and deleting it would leave the next reader to form it again."@en,
        "The most consequential of the four for this project, because it names the mechanism that keeps a procedure out of reach of examination. The Zhouyi attaches exactly one condition to consulting: at the first asking it tells; asked twice and three times, that is defilement, and defiled it does not tell. The spiritist manual instructs that questions put from curiosity or from a motive of test are not to be answered, and that the spirits addressed withdraw from them."@en,
        "The two witnesses are far apart in time and unequal in kind: a canonical judgement fixed in the first millennium BCE and a French manual of 1861. A route between them is not documented and is also not needed for the claim, which asserts a recurring form and not an independent attestation. Whoever wants the second must show the manual's author had no access to the sinological literature of his century."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:EpreuveCaution,
        pmc:ZaisanDu ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Transmissions that obtain an answer from a procedure state a condition that excludes the repeated or testing question, and give as the reason that the answer is spoiled by it rather than that the questioner is unworthy."@en .

pmc:TheBodyOfThePartiesIsAStatedCondition a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Regeln an den Ausführenden beginnen bei dem, was er gegessen hat, und wie ihm zumute ist"@de,
        "The rules addressed to the operator begin with what he has eaten and how he feels"@en ;
    skos:note "Falsified by a transmission that prescribes a bodily operation, carries rules for the persons performing it — purity, dress, posture, hour — and carries none about what they have eaten, drunk or felt. The grimoire literature is the place to look, because it is dense in operator rules of every other kind, and one such case refutes the statement."@en,
        "Four transmissions in four corpora with no route among them. The Chinese medical canon names seven states in which the needle is not to be used: greatly drunk, greatly angered, greatly exhausted, newly filled with food, greatly hungry, greatly thirsty, greatly startled. The nourishing-life chapter of the Baopuzi forbids eating much and forbids anger to the man circulating his breath. The Ars Notoria warns that no man presume to say the oration after too much drinking or luxury, nor fasting, without reverence and discretion. The magnetic manual instructs that one is not to operate after having eaten heartily nor during digestion, and that whoever undertakes a treatment ought in general to live temperately."@en,
        "Not yet examined, and no pm:Testing points at it. Of the two statements in this file it is the one an examination could actually address, because the condition it names is one an experiment can set."@en,
        "The counter-search was run against this holding before the statement was written. It found one transmission that prescribes an operation on a body and names no such condition: the canonical Mesopotamian incantation series, in which every prohibition read is addressed to the demon and not to the operator, and in which what the operator must do and must not do is stated nowhere inside the series. That is why the antecedent carries the clause about addressing any rule at all to the persons taking part — the exorcistic series fails it, and the statement is about transmissions that have operator-facing rules in the first place. The other near miss is the whole class of calendar-bound conditions, which state the condition of the day rather than of a person and are generalized separately at pmc:AdmissibilityBoundToTheCalendar."@en,
        "The last clause is the part that can fail and is therefore the part worth having. Two of the four state a consequence and both state it as a disorder of the body: needling the greatly drunk makes the qi disordered, needling the greatly angered makes the qi go against its course, and much anger disorders the qi so that it cannot flow over and may set a man coughing. The other two state no consequence at all. A transmission of this kind whose penalty for the full stomach is that the operation does not come off, rather than that the practitioner is harmed, would break the clause without touching the rest."@en,
        "This is the class of condition a protocol can control for, and that is the whole reason to hold the statement. The other conditions this holding carries in quantity — a defect of the operator nobody noticed, a state of faith, the sympathy of the bystanders, a trial set at what the candidate's mind does not grasp — cannot be established before the operation or checked after it. Time since the last meal, quantity of drink and a reported state of anger or fright can be. A run that wanted to build an examination of any of these four transmissions would have here the only entry the sources themselves supply."@en,
        "Which person the condition is stated of varies and the statement is worded to carry the variation. In the medical canon it is the person needled; in the nourishing-life chapter and in the Latin oration it is the practitioner working on himself; in the magnetic manual it is the operator working on another. What does not vary is that the condition attaches to a body in the room."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:ArsNotoriaEbrietasKautel,
        pmc:CautionAgainstOperatingAfterEating,
        pmc:WucijinQizhuang,
        pmc:XingqiProhibitions ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission prescribes an operation on a body, whether the operator's own or another's, and addresses any rule at all to the persons taking part in it, those rules include states of recent intake and of strong feeling — drink, a full stomach, hunger, thirst, exhaustion, anger, fright — under which the operation is not to be performed. The condition is stated of a person present, and not of the day, the place or the material. Where such a transmission states what follows from proceeding anyway, what it states is a disorder of the body and not a failure of the operation."@en .

pmc:TheCriterionNoPartyControls a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wo keine Partei den Ausgang bestimmen kann, werden beide Seiten des Kriteriums genannt"@de,
        "Where no party can fix the outcome, both sides of the criterion are stated"@en ;
    skos:note "A further witness was added on 2026-09-20 from the Mandaean material, after the protocol at examinations/both-sides-of-the-criterion.ttl was written rather than before it, and it is entered with that said so that nobody reads the protocol as having weighed it. It is unusual in the set because the event is not an ordeal or a sign but a death, and not the death of a party to the rite. A priest may be raised to the high priesthood only while some pious aged person is near death and carries the message to Abathur; if that person recovers, the ceremonies as far as the candidate is concerned are invalid and he must wait for another. Both sides are stated, in one sentence, and neither party can produce the event."@en,
        "An eighth witness was added to the first clause on 2026-09-17, from a transmission that had no node in this holding when the statement was written, and it is the strongest of them. The insular Celtic satire performed on a hill at the boundary of seven lands fixes its judgement to the earth of the hill opening, and it names both sides in consecutive clauses: if it were they that were in the wrong, the earth of the hill would swallow them up; if it were the king that was in the wrong, the earth would swallow up him and his wife and his son and his horse and his arms and his dress and his hound. Nobody can make the earth of a hill open, the poets are the operators, and the failing side of the criterion falls on them."@en,
        "Falsified by a transmission that fixes the judgement of a performance to an event no party can produce at will and states the success side alone — a divination whose text says what the favourable fall means and is silent on the unfavourable one, an ordeal that names the sign of guilt and not the sign of innocence. Falsified in the other direction by a set of announced signs of verification that states, in the same passage, what the absence of a sign would show about the work. Weakened, without being refuted, if the seven turn out to be selected by the reading: five of the six transmissions entered the holding through ethnographic corpora, and a recorder who found a stated criterion interesting is a plausible filter on what got written down."@en,
        "For none of the three does the text say what the absence of the sign would show, prescribe anything for that case, or contemplate it. That is the falsifying condition of this node as it stood, in the words of its own last note: an event outside the control of every party, with only the success side named. See pmc:JeuSignaTriaPetita and pmp:JeuSignumAbsensSearch."@en,
        "It also shows the clause about control doing real work rather than dividing cleanly. The event is uncontrolled and the selection of the case is not: the record says that Mandaeans are acute at recognizing the signs of imminent death, and that the shock of the cold water and the exertion usually hasten the end. A transmission can therefore satisfy the antecedent of this statement while arranging that the criterion almost always falls the same way, and the statement says nothing about how often it falls each way. That is a limit on what the generalization licenses and not a case against it."@en,
        "Its second pass removed a stronger clause that the holding refutes: that a transmission stating both sides is one that abides by the verdict. The ordeal tree says otherwise in the same passage that supplies the witness. The recorder states that although the omens may be in favour of the accused the ordeal-giver proceeds all the same, that the results are not infrequently quite contrary to the omens, and that the belief is unaffected by the discrepancy. Two-sidedness is therefore a property of the wording and not of the practice, and the statement above claims only the wording. That limit is the most useful thing in this node, because it marks exactly how far a reader may carry a criterion found in a text."@en,
        "The claim as first written did not separate an uncontrolled event from an event in the gift of the addressee, and this case is what made the difference visible. A reader who holds that separation to be a repair rather than a discovery has a refutation and should enter it."@en,
        "The counter-search was run against the compiled holding before this was written, and its first pass removed the word public from the statement. The criterion of the Lingshu is the hardness of a vessel under a finger, which is not public and is read by one person, and the criterion of the drum contest is the victory of a painted reindeer, which the source does not say how anybody determined. What the seven share is not that a bystander could check them but that nobody who has an interest in the outcome can produce the event; that is a weaker property and it is the one the witnesses actually carry."@en,
        "The exception in the first clause was not there before 2026-09-27, and it is there because the case this node named as its own refutation was found. The second book of Jeu performs three rites and fixes each of them to a physical event no party can produce at will: wine becoming water, a sign in the fire of an incense, a sign in an offering. Each is asked for in a prayer on a stated condition — if the Father has heard, has forgiven the sins, has counted the disciples among the heirs — and each is reported as having happened in the same moment."@en,
        "The second of the two is the oath between the sun goddess and her brother, and it opens a third way for a stated criterion to decide nothing. The criterion is fixed by agreement beforehand and names both sides: children produced under the oath that are males show a pure heart, females an impure one. The event is a sex ratio, which neither party can fix. And the chronicle prints the episode four times with the same physical outcome in every version — three female deities and five or six male ones — while the verdict moves from version to version, because what varies is who is said to have produced which, and that rule was never part of the criterion. Two versions declare the brother vindicated; two draw no verdict at all. See pmc:ShintoUkehiCriterium and pmc:ShintoUkehiVariaeLectiones."@en,
        "The seven witnesses of the first clause, and what each of them fixes the judgement to. The fork of the Iglulik divination falls or stands: if it falls the sick person will die, if it stays upright she will live, and the reading is given before the fork is dropped. The kite spirit of the Manchu clan history eats the liver of an ox and the shaman must then leave his assistant; if he falls down without being able to keep on his legs, the sacrifice counts as rejected. The two Sámi practitioners who wish to know which of them excels paint two reindeer striking each other on a drum, and he whose reindeer has the victory is the better. The needling chapter of the Lingshu states what it is for the qi to have arrived as a rule over the vessel and then refuses the patient's own report twice in the same sentence: although he says it is as it was, although he says he feels relieved, the illness has not gone. The prohibition chapter of the Suwen pairs each depot with an interval and a bodily sign, so that a man struck in the heart who is alive on the third day is not covered by the sentence. The ordeal-giver of the Lower Congo cuts the tree with a formula that names both outcomes, the blade bending if the accused is a witch and entering if he is not. And the Zande poison oracle requires a second fowl told what the first answer was, which must come out the opposite way."@en,
        "The spread after the addition: eight transmissions for the first clause, and the Japanese one has no proposed route to the Inuit, Tungus, Sámi, Congo or Zande witnesses. Its route to the Chinese material, on the other hand, is not in doubt — the chronicle is written in Chinese and its own translator marks one of its two divinatory techniques as Chinese — so this witness does not add an independent one against the Lingshu and the Suwen."@en,
        "The spread, stated so that the count of predecessors does not overstate it. Seven witnesses for the first clause, but the Lingshu and the Suwen are one transmission, so the count of transmissions is six: Iglulik Inuit, Manchu-Tungus, Sámi, the Chinese medical canon, Lower Congo and Zande. No route has been proposed between the first, the third, the fourth, the fifth and the sixth, which makes this the widest spread in this directory. The two witnesses of the second clause are a Latin grimoire and a Chinese internal alchemy, between which no route has been proposed either. The wide spread is the reason the statement is worth having and is also its own warning: a form that turns up in six unconnected places is a candidate for a structural necessity of the thing rather than for a shared insight, and SPEC §14 names that as one of the three sources of agreement independence does not protect against."@en,
        "The transmission it comes from is one for which no route has been proposed to any of the six, so the spread of the first clause is now seven transmissions. Its contact route in this holding is entered as documented, but the route that is documented runs to the Christian material and not to the Inuit, Tungus, Sámi, Chinese, Congo or Zande witnesses, and it therefore does not touch the independence of this agreement."@en,
        "Two further witnesses for the first clause were added on 2026-09-18, both from the Japanese chronicle of 720, and the second of them does something to the claim that none of the earlier ten did. The ordeal of boiling water of the fourth year of Ingyō is the plainest instance the holding carries: the company is washed and under abstinence, each calls the gods to witness, and everyone is told before the first hand goes in that he who tells the truth will be uninjured and he who is false will assuredly suffer harm. Nobody can make boiling water spare a hand, both sides are named in one sentence, and — almost uniquely here — the chronicle then reports the outcome. See pmc:ShintoKukatachiCriterium and pmc:ShintoKukatachiExitus."@en,
        "Untested. No pm:Testing points here, which is the state and not an omission; the queue it belongs to is derivable from the absence and is not to be written down. What a protocol would have to do first is name the population, and that is the hard part: the unit is a transmission and not a case, so the denominator is the set of transmissions that fix a judgement to an uncontrollable event, which nobody has enumerated."@en,
        "What that adds to this statement, said exactly so it is not read as a refutation. The claim is about whether both sides are stated, and in this witness they are; it holds. What the witness shows is that stating both sides is not sufficient for a criterion to settle anything, because a criterion also needs a rule mapping the event onto its two sides, and that rule can be left open while the criterion looks complete. The protocol at examinations/both-sides-of-the-criterion.ttl already found two shapes in which a stated other side decides nothing — the failing side abolished by a prescribed repetition, and the failing side made non-binding by a further attempt. This is a third, and it is the cheapest of the three to overlook, because nothing is missing from the sentence."@en,
        "What the eighth witness adds beyond a count. In the seven it joins, the failure side falls on the person operated on or on nobody in particular; here it falls on the operator, and the transmission says so in the same breath as the success. That is the form of a trial rather than of a curse, and it narrows a possible objection to the first clause: a reader could have said that stating the failure side is cheap where the failure costs the operator nothing. In this witness it costs him everything, and the clause is stated anyway."@en,
        "What the narrowing costs, said plainly. The exception is not free: any transmission that asks a higher party for its own criterion now falls outside the first clause, and petitionary rites are common, so the clause claims less than it did and what it claims is about criteria between parties present at the operation. The second clause has at the same time absorbed the two corrections that the protocol at examinations/both-sides-of-the-criterion.ttl had written out and left at the examination. They are in the statement now, because a statement whose corrections live only in its protocol is read as uncorrected by whoever loads the graph."@en,
        "What the search did not find, and this is what makes the first clause a claim rather than an observation. No transmission in the holding fixes a judgement to an event outside every party's control and then names only the success side. Nine candidates were read at the node and none of them does; four are named here: the maiden's declaration decided by a snake states that the challenger loses if he refuses the same test; the Andean confession test states that the confession is void and must be made again; the Māori hair-cutting names the cut scalp as the detector of a breach nobody admitted; the Sámi journey states an upper bound of twenty-four hours as well as the rule that the time tracks the distance. If a case exists it will be a plain refutation, and a reader who has one needs nothing from this graph to bring it."@en,
        "What the two witnesses of the second clause do instead, and why they are in the same node rather than in a file of their own. The Solomonic book tells the operator in advance what he will perceive when the pentacles are shown — noises and rushings, and six manners of coming sorted by the substance the spirits were made from, from great rains and thunderbolts to a gentle breeze. The internal alchemy announces its signs of verification in order, each introduced as coming next after the one before. Both are events nobody can produce at will either; a night in the open supplies weather, and a body under a regimen supplies sensations. What separates them from the seven is not controllability but that they are announced to the participant beforehand, and neither states what would have to appear for the work to have failed. The clause is in the statement because without it the first clause would be a remark about a handful of cases instead of a claim about a division."@en,
        "Why the statement was narrowed rather than dropped, and the ground is a property of the witness. In the seven witnesses of the first clause the event settles something between parties who are both present to the operation, and the party who stands to lose by it has no say in whether it occurs: a fork falls, boiling water scalds, a blade bends, a hill opens. In this witness the event is requested from the one party whose favour is the whole question, and it is that party who would supply it. The operator can therefore not be wrong about the outcome; he can only be unanswered, and an unanswered petition is not a verdict on a performance."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:CizhongWuzang,
        pmc:ClaviculaSignaAdventus,
        pmc:ContestOfTwoNoaidi,
        pmc:InuitForkSignOfSurvival,
        pmc:MandaeanIngirthaConvalescentia,
        pmc:NeidanZhengyan,
        pmc:QizhiYouxiao,
        pmc:ShintoKukatachiCriterium,
        pmc:ShintoUkehiCriterium,
        pmc:TalamDiaSlugadh,
        pmc:TungusSacrificeRejected,
        pmp:BengeCorroborativeTest,
        pmp:NkasaTreeCutting ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission fixes the judgement of a single performance to a physical event that neither the operator nor the person operated on can produce at will, it states what the event would have to be for the performance to have failed and not only what it would be for the performance to have succeeded — unless the event is asked for as a favour of the party whose granting is the very thing at issue, in which case the transmission may narrate the sign as given and say nothing whatever about its absence; where the criterion is instead an appearance announced to the participants before the session, the transmission either states the success side alone or states an other side that a further attempt dissolves."@en .

pmc:TheDirectionOfTheMeasure a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Richtung, in der das Maß gelesen wird"@de,
        "The direction in which the measure is read"@en ;
    skos:note "Falsified by a transmission that gives its practitioner a measure of the kind described and states a value or a direction of that measure at which the practice is to be judged not to work — not a term at which to persevere longer, and not a rule assigning the shortfall to the practitioner, but a reading of the measure itself that counts against the practice. The corpora likeliest to carry it are the ones where a measure is kept for somebody else: the medical canon with its ten-day terms, the Ayurvedic and Greek surgical literature with its staged prognoses, and the alchemical assay literature, where the thing measured is a material and not a person. The last is the nearest, and it is already in the holding as pmc:TheTestOnTheIngredient; whether an assay of an operation ever carries an unexcused negative reading is the first question to put."@en,
        "The first witness, and the one that shows the device at its most defensible. The Thelemic exposition of nineteen twelve has the practitioner count the number of times his thought wanders, on his fingers or on a string of beads, with a notebook and a watch before him. It then rules that if the breaks seem to become more frequent instead of less frequent, the student must not be discouraged, for this is partially caused by his increased accuracy of observation; and it offers as its analogy that the introduction of vaccination raised the apparent number of cases of smallpox, because people began to tell the truth about the disease instead of faking. The reasoning is sound. A rising count on a self-administered measure really is ambiguous between worse control and better observation. What the passage does not supply is any condition under which the ambiguity would be resolved the other way, and its only exit is the assurance that control will soon improve faster than observation and that the improvement will then become apparent in the record — which moves the criterion to a later point that is not named."@en,
        "The fourth is the same instrument read from the other end, and it is what makes the statement's first clause more than a complaint about one passage. The same Thelemic exposition rules that the beginner to whom the described difficulties do not occur need not imagine he is meditating, since it shows merely that he is incapable of concentrating his mind in the very smallest degree; and that the apparent simplicity of the practices is such that he is likely to wonder what all the fuss is about and to think himself specially gifted. Difficulty is progress and ease is incapacity, so no reading of that measure tells against the practitioner's progress. Each rule is defensible alone; the pair closes the instrument."@en,
        "The second clause, and it is the part that keeps the statement from being a statement about occultism in general. The same instruction of nineteen-nine that demands a written record as the only possible check upon error also states a chance rate — the right card once in seventy-eight times, and a correct suit twice in seven by calling trumps every time — and a threshold above it, and then names a pattern of result that is worthless even if the numbers came out: to call the Lord of Love for the Lord of Strife would show that you were getting nothing right. Where a level is fixed before the practice begins, the transmission does name an excluded outcome. The re-reading appears where the measure is the practitioner's own report of his own state, and not where it is a count against a number nobody can move."@en,
        "The second witness is bodily and older. The magnetic instruction of the eighteen-twenties states that the operation often excites pain in the diseased part and renews old and slumbering pains, that these are the efforts nature makes to triumph over the malady, that they are transient, and that the patient always finds himself better after them. The patient is told that the pains prove the action is powerful, that they are necessary to subdue the disease, that he is to bear them without speaking of them to anyone, and that he is not even to ask the operator to calm them. The distinguishing mark offered — that critical pains are transient and followed by improvement — is retrospective, so the patient in pain on the tenth day cannot apply it at the moment he would have to."@en,
        "The third clause came out of the counter-search and is the sharpest thing here. The corpus that supplies the second witness rejects the identical device by name — when its adherents use it about somebody else. Against the blind confidence of enthusiastic operators, it says, who believe their somnambulists infallible, suppose that remedies which did not succeed were not exactly followed, and regard remedies that did mischief as a necessary crisis: where the prescription appears unsuitable the operator is to object, to have the organs examined in succession, to ask the reasons for the choice, and to present the medicine to be touched and tasted. Nothing in that passage is retracted eight pages earlier where the patient's own pains are declared proof. The device is admitted for the practitioner judging himself and refused for the practitioner judging another, and the corpus does not notice the difference."@en,
        "The third witness is the change in condition, and it is the widest. The anthroposophical manual of nineteen eighteen names, in its own voice, precisely what an outside observer would report of a failed case — illusion, the loss of the hold on the sensory world, the inability to tell truth from error, and a change of character for the worse, so that one becomes fearful, envious, vain, arrogant and self-willed having had none of these properties before — and assigns all of it to a schooling undertaken wrongly or too early. An observer who reports exactly these things about a practitioner reports something the book has already classified as a departure from the method."@en,
        "The weakness of the derivation, stated because a reader would otherwise have to find it. Three of the five nodes come from two authors writing within a hundred years of each other in the same European occult milieu, and the contact routes between magnetism, theosophy, anthroposophy and Thelema are documented throughout this holding. So the agreement is not evidence of independent invention and nothing here claims it is. What a fourth witness would have to add is a transmission with no route to these three that keeps a counted measure at all — and a counted measure is itself rare in this holding, which is the honest reason the witnesses cluster."@en,
        "What the statement does not say. It says nothing about whether any of these practices does anything, and it imputes nothing to anyone's motives: the first witness is a piece of correct statistical reasoning applied without a stopping rule, which is harder to see than an evasion and is the reason it is entered first rather than last. It is a statement about the shape a transmission takes, and it can be settled by reading."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:DouleursCritiquesCommePreuve,
        pmc:SteinerVerkehrteSchulung,
        pmc:ThelemaErrorInharmonicus,
        pmc:ThelemaFacilitasEstIndiciumErroris,
        pmc:ThelemaFractiCrescentesNonSuntCasus ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission gives the practitioner something by which to judge whether the practice is working — a counted quantity, a bodily state, or a change in his own condition — the same corpus supplies a rule under which a change in the unfavourable direction is to be read as a mark that the practice is working, or as a mark of the practitioner's improved observation, and supplies no rule under which a change in the favourable direction would count against it. Where a transmission instead states a rate against a level fixed before the practice begins, the same passage does name a pattern of result that would count against the claim. And the re-reading is placed where the practitioner judges his own state: where the same corpus has one person judge the result obtained for another, it may name the identical device and reject it." .

pmc:TheFailureConditionFollowsWhatIsWithheld a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Misslingensbedingung sitzt auf dem, was die Überlieferung aus dem Text heraushält"@de,
        "The failure condition sits on whatever the transmission keeps out of the text"@en ;
    skos:note "A third arrangement was read on 2026-09-22 which neither limb covers, and it is entered as a note because it is a gap in the statement and not a case against it. Steiner's book of the schooling publishes the whole operation — every exercise, every condition, the announced colour of every perception — to anyone who buys it, says that the teacher can give nothing but advice, and mentions a teacher only as somebody one may happen to have. By the first limb it should therefore name no further condition a possessor of the text could fail. It names many; but what it withholds is not a part of the operation, it is the result. The pupil is told to understand how to be silent about his spiritual sights, to be silent about them even before himself, and not to clothe what he beholds in words, on the ground that the wording mixes fantasy into the true perception. The failure condition then sits on the pupil's silence: a perception put into words is corrupted by the wording, so no outcome can enter a record, be set beside another pupil's, or be held against the colours the book announced in advance. The nodes are pmc:SteinerSchweigeGebot and pmp:SteinerUebungAmSamen."@en,
        "And it declares that apparatus insufficient in its own words, three times: whoever performs the mysteries of the five trees, the seven voices and the great name needs no further mystery except the mystery of the forgiveness of sins; the ascending will be held fast at the six great aeons until they receive it; and at the place of the first archon of the light they cannot enter the inner before they have it. The failure condition therefore sits entirely on the withheld part, and what it is — detention short of the treasury, with the text adding that there is no place of chastisement there — is stated at each of the three places. The node is pmc:JeuRemissioPraesupposita."@en,
        "Counter-search, carried against the compiled holding before the statement was written, as SPEC §16 requires. Two near-misses and no refutation. The Hekhalot handbook states the preparation in exact counts — three days free of pollution, one bath, three prayers, a hundred names — and then names the penalty for the user who does not keep it, which is a failure condition a possessor of the text could meet; it does not refute, because the same corpus also withholds, and the run did not read the passages where names and seals are the condition of passage. The Clavicula supplies the general clause that an unsuccessful experiment must have been false and defective and the operator must have failed in some matter, which puts a failure condition on a possessor of the text; it does not refute either, because the clause names nothing in particular and so is not a condition anyone could fail determinately. Both are addresses for the run that presses this further."@en,
        "Eighth witness, added on 2026-09-25, and it falls on the second limb and pushes past its far edge. The first volume of Mookerjee's Rasa-Jala-Nidhi is a printed book with a price on the title page which sets out the eighteen operations and the composition of some fifty preparations, and it states three things in one chapter: the knowledge of metallurgy is to be kept secret from those who are not disciples; it becomes powerful if cultivated in secret, otherwise it loses much of its strength; and the preparation and composition of a medicine should not be taught to a patient or to the public in general, because a medicine which is very familiar to a patient and to the public becomes ineffective. The part kept from writing is named elsewhere in the same volume — for the detailed procedure of the worship a disciple is to depend upon the instructions of his preceptor, and the compiler says he learnt from a yogi much more than could be found in the existing books, each of which he calls incomplete, incoherent, incorrect and in many cases misleading. Three elements of the limb stand there: a part received from a person, the written text declared insufficient, and the failure condition off the written part. The node is pmc:RasaGuptavidya."@en,
        "Falsified by either of two cases. A transmission that puts its whole operation into a text obtainable by purchase or inheritance, and that nevertheless names a determinate further condition — a term, a bodily state, a sign — which a possessor of the text could fail to meet and could be seen to have failed. Or a transmission that withholds part of its operation from writing and nevertheless places its stated failure condition on the written part rather than on what it withheld. One clean case of either kind ends the statement."@en,
        "Ninth witness, added on 2026-09-27, and it falls on the second limb in the cleanest form the holding has yet carried. The second book of Jeu writes out its whole apparatus: an incense, a wreath, a plant, a seal with its name and its interpretation, a cipher and a prayer for each of four rites, and then, station by station, the seal, the name said once, the cipher and the apology for every aeon of the ascent up to the fourteenth."@en,
        "Seventh witness, added on 2026-09-22, and it falls on the second limb. The head of Agrippa's third book says that the secrets which are among a few wise men and communicated by mouth only are not fit to be publicly written, asks the reader's pardon for passing over in silence many and the chiefest secret mysteries of ceremonial magic, and then puts the failure condition on exactly the withheld part: every magical experiment is strengthened by silence but is destroyed by publication, neither does any complete effect follow after. All three elements the limb requires stand in one chapter — the part kept out of writing, the requirement that it be received from a person, and the declared insufficiency of what is written. The witness matters because it is the first on this limb from a transmission with no initiatory order behind it: the withholding is done by an author in a printed book, which makes the arrangement a property of the claim rather than of a school."@en,
        "The one thing this witness cannot settle, and it is the same limit the eighth carried. The manuscript is damaged and its end is lost, so a deferral and a lacuna are not fully distinguishable here. What is not in doubt is that the declaration of insufficiency stands three times in the surviving text, at three removes from each other, and that the third time it is put as a reassurance: do not be afraid that you cannot enter, they will only hold you. A transmission that softens the consequence of lacking the withheld part is not one that forgot to supply it."@en,
        "The withholding is therefore done by deferral and not by prohibition, and the effect on the reader is the same: the written text is declared insufficient, and what would complete it has to come from the person who promised it. A limb that sorted transmissions by whether they forbid publication would miss this one entirely."@en,
        "Thin where it is thin. Two transmissions on the first side and one on the second, all three read in the last week, and the third from a corpus whose own contradictions about the fire phases this holding already records. Whether the relation holds across the holding would need a pass over every transmission that states both how much it writes and where the failure lies, and that pass has not been made."@en,
        "What each side supplies, in the transmissions' own terms. On the first side: the Egyptian corpus, whose chapters close with the clause that if this chapter be known such and such will follow, and whose gates refuse passage unless the dead man tells the doorway its name — and every one of those names is printed on the same sheet as the question. And the Vedic corpus, whose expositions close with the clause that he who knows this obtains a full life, offspring, cattle, or escape from the death that comes again, and whose catalogue of mishaps at the fires states that the eldest son dies in the case of him who would not know this, while the man who knows has by dint of knowledge prevailed. Neither names a condition that a man holding the text could fail. On the second side: the internal alchemy, whose preface says that the one aperture may not be settled by one's own notion but must be handed on from mind to mouth, and whose commentary draws the rule that the sages transmit the medicine and do not transmit the fire, because a hair's breadth of difference in the fire phases makes no elixir. There the text is declared insufficient in the text, and the failure has been placed exactly where no reader can check it."@en,
        "What this witness adds beyond a count, and it is a second mechanism of withholding that the limb did not separate. In the earlier witnesses on this limb the operative part is kept out of writing by a rule: the aperture may not be settled by the practitioner alone but must pass from mind to mouth; the chief secret mysteries are not fit to be publicly written; the knowledge is to be kept from those who are not disciples. Here there is no such rule. The mystery of the forgiveness of sins is promised, with its apologies, its seal and its cipher, three separate times, and the text breaks off without delivering it."@en,
        "Where this witness goes past the limb, and it is worth a successor statement rather than a repair. Agrippa says that a magical experiment is destroyed by publication, which puts the failure on the operator's having spoken. This transmission says that a medicine familiar to the patient and to the public becomes ineffective, which puts it on a third party's knowledge — on how widely the composition of a substance is known. That is a failure condition placed neither on the withheld part nor on the written one but on the distribution of the writing, and it makes the effect of a substance depend on who else has read about it. The statement above does not cover it and cannot be stretched to it, exactly as with the third arrangement recorded from the book of the schooling. Both gaps point the same way: the limbs sort transmissions by how much they write, and the cases that escape them sort by what the writing then does in the world."@en,
        "Why that is a gap and not a refutation. The falsifier asks, on the first limb, for a determinate further condition which a possessor of the text could fail to meet and could be seen to have failed. Every condition this book states is removed from that description by a clause printed immediately before the list of them: with none of these conditions is a complete fulfilment demanded, but merely the striving, nobody can fulfil them entirely, and everything depends on the will. The conditions are determinate as sentences and indeterminate as facts, which is why the case leaves the statement standing and shows that its two limbs do not exhaust the field. A transmission can publish its operation whole and still place its failure condition out of reach, by withholding the report of the outcome instead of a part of the procedure. A successor statement has to name that third position; this one cannot be stretched to it."@en,
        "Why this is worth more than the correspondence it comes from. That two corpora make knowing the condition can be read as a family resemblance and excludes nothing. What the statement above claims is a relation between two features of the same transmission — how much it writes down, and where it puts the failure — and that relation can be looked for and can come out wrong. It also supplies the structural explanation the convergence node had to leave hanging: a transmission whose product is the text has every reason to make holding the text sufficient, and one that lives by initiation has every reason not to. The two witnesses on the first side are both funerary or exegetical corpora sold or performed for a client; the witness on the second is a lineage."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:AegyptiaPortaeNomina,
        pmc:AegyptiaRubricaScientiae,
        pmc:AgrippaSilentium,
        pmc:JeuRemissioPraesupposita,
        pmc:NeidanChuanyaoBuchuanhuo,
        pmc:NeidanYiqiao,
        pmc:RasaGuptavidya,
        pmc:VaidikaJnanenaJitam,
        pmc:VaidikaYaEvamVeda ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission puts the whole of its operative content into a text that the practitioner obtains from others, it states the condition of the attainment as knowing that text and the condition of failure as not knowing it, and it names no further condition that a possessor of the text could fail to meet. Where a transmission instead keeps part of its operative content out of writing and requires that part to be received from a person, it states the failure condition on the withheld part, and the written text is then explicitly declared insufficient."@en .

pmc:TheNamedManDiesWithinTenDays a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Gerufene stirbt binnen zehn Tagen"@de,
        "The man whose name is called dies within ten days"@en ;
    dcterms:source "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), p. 93" ;
    skos:definition "It is forbidden, at the felling, for anyone to call another by his name, for the man so called will die and his spirit will pass into the tree and preside over the figure. The corpus states the term and the record together: the named one then dies, certainly within ten days, and they say that the named one never fails to die. It states in the same place that they repudiate all idea of his being poisoned or of his death being hurried on in any material way by the practitioner, who they say may be miles away."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim of a perfect record is the transmission's own and is entered as such. Whether any case of a named man surviving was ever recorded is not answered by this work; the same work states that the caller who names a man carelessly answers with his life to the dead man's relations, which is a reason why the naming is not done carelessly and also a reason why a failure would be inconvenient to report."@en,
        "The most examinable claim in the file, and the reason it is examinable is that the transmission itself supplies the three parts: an outcome, a term of ten days, and an explicit denial of the obvious naturalistic explanation. Nothing else read in this run puts a number of days on anything."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:KongoNkisi .

pmc:TheOrderOfExitsForADisease a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Rangordnung der Wege, auf denen eine Krankheit ausgetrieben wird"@de,
        "The ranked ways a disease is driven out"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Frances Densmore, Teton Sioux Music (Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 61, Washington, 1918), pp. 251-252",
        "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 163" ;
    skos:definition "All diseases are things that have got into the body and do violence to it, and the thing to do is to get them out. The medicines drive the disease out in the sweat, in the breath, in the defecation, in the urine or in the vomit, and the corpus ranks these: in the sweat is the best and easiest, in the breath the next best, then the defecation, then the urine, and the vomit is very hard although some diseases will come out no other way. Where the cause is an influence a shaman cures best; where it is something else, a medicine man does; and if the sickness is of long duration someone should seek a communication and learn what to do."@en ;
    skos:note "A ranked therapeutics with a stated criterion, given by a named informant whose father was a medicine man. It is entered because a ranking of routes is a claim that can be wrong in a way that a list cannot."@en,
        "The second witness has the same rule from a different man on a different reservation, put as a regime rather than a ranking: the first thing a sick person should do is take a sweat bath so that the body will respond to remedies, then a lighter diet and rest, and no unnecessary exertion. He adds a limit that the first witness does not — that if the disease is not too far advanced the person usually recovers — and a caution that the sick person did not jump into cold water afterwards, as is sometimes stated, but was covered with furs and allowed to cool gradually."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LakotaRitual .

pmc:TheProtectionAndWhatWouldShowItFailed a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Schäden, die ein Schutz abwendet, werden in beiden Arten zugleich genannt, und die feststellbaren sind nie die Probe"@de,
        "The harms a protection averts are named in both kinds at once, and the recordable ones are never the test"@en ;
    skos:note "A seventh witness was added the same day, and it was added by running the protocol rather than by browsing. The examination named the Mesopotamian apotropaic literature as the first corpus to attempt a refutation in; both volumes of Campbell Thompson 1903 and 1904 were read through for a sign, a term or a condition of failure, and there is none in any operative text of either. What came back instead was the sharpest instance of the mixing in the holding. The fixed litany of the Utukki Limnuti enumerates what is to be kept off in one breath: unto my body may they not draw nigh, before me may they wreak no evil, nor follow behind me, into my house may they not enter, my fence may they not break through, into my chamber may they not enter. A broken fence is as plain a fact as this holding contains, and it stands unseparated beside a wreaking of evil in the abstract. The run is at pmp:ApotropaicHarmListSearch."@en,
        "Counter-search, carried against the compiled holding before the statement was written, as SPEC §16 requires. Every node in the traditions whose wording names an averting and also names a sign, a test or a term was read. No refutation and three near-misses. The Kongo kandu announces a curse before the theft and states a rule of fairness requiring the announcement, which is a two-sided arrangement — but it announces a harm rather than averting one, and the corpus itself notes that a curse announced in advance is one the announcement could bring about. The Ethiopian prayer for the suckling sets a term, until the years of childbearing are ended, but it is a term of the wearing and not a term within which the harm would otherwise have come; the holding already marks this at pmc:EthiopianSucklingProtectionPresupposesTheWearing, which is prescribed order and carries no consequence of breaking off. And the Lakota transmission names three different protections against one and the same outcome in three different rites, which multiplies the means without ever stating what would show any of them had failed."@en,
        "Independence, and one confound named. Six witnesses from six transmissions with no contact route asserted between any pair in this holding. Five recorders: Budge, Landa through Gates, Walker, Geller, Ellis. Two of the six — the Egyptian and the Ethiopian — reach the graph through the same editor and translator, and the claim here is a claim about wording, so for this statement that pair counts as something closer to one witness than to two. The Lakota witness was sought for exactly that reason and is the one that carries the mixing side independently of Budge."@en,
        "The one witness on the other side, and it is the reason the statement is not a truism about religious language. The Fon vo-sesa is bound to crops standing in the bush and to goods left for sale beside a frequented path, with a few cowries on each article to show the price, and they are said to be quite safe from pillage. One harm, no companion, and the harm is a theft or the absence of one. That claim is about a distribution in the world and could have come out against the transmission on any market day."@en,
        "The same run turned up a second arrangement that this statement does not cover and should not be stretched to cover. Tablet III of the same series does not name what is present but disjoins what might be — whether thou be an evil Spirit or an evil Demon, or sickness, or death, or evil pestilence — so that the officiant need never establish which harm he is treating. That is not a list mixing two kinds; it is a refusal to identify the kind. It makes the claim unfailable by a different route and is held at pmc:AsipuNominisIncertitudo, where the next statement in this direction should start."@en,
        "The strongest thing the counter-search found is not a near-miss but a contrast, and it bears on whether the silence is a property of these sources or of these claims. The same holding, and in the Inuit case the same kind of report by the same kind of recorder, does carry two-sided signs where the operation is a divination: the fork that falls means the sick person dies and the fork that stands upright means she lives, stated in advance and in both directions. A recorder who wrote that down would have written down a stated failure condition for a protection if the transmission had supplied one. It appears that they do not supply one."@en,
        "Thin where it is thin. The other side of the statement rests on a single case, so what is established is that the arrangement is avoidable and not how often it is avoided. A count of the protections in the holding, sorted by whether the harms they name are recordable, has not been made and would settle the proportion."@en,
        "What each witness supplies, in the transmission's own words. The Book of the Dead states thirty-two of its recovered chapter titles as harms averted and mixes them without a mark: not letting the head of a man be cut off, which a mourner would see, stands in the same list as not dying a second time, which nobody can report. The Ethiopian roll does it inside one sentence — that no devil shall draw nigh, and that the man shall be smitten neither by the arrows of his adversary nor by the stone hurled at him nor by the spear. The Lakota cedar does it in one clause and its continuation: the Winged God will not visit one protected by it, nor cause such a one to act foolishly; the visit is between the god and the man, the foolish conduct is what the camp sees and names. The Maya conjuring frees from the ills indicated as to come, and the ills run from drought to political discord. The Mesopotamian anointing has no list at all and says only that nothing bad will approach him, which is the same silence reached by the shorter road."@en,
        "Why the mixing matters and what it is not. It is not an accusation of evasion. Nothing suggests the compilers arranged the lists so as to be safe; the Ethiopian prayer is plainly trying to cover everything that can happen to a nursing child, and covering everything is exactly what produces the mixture. The consequence holds regardless of the intention: once the unrecordable member is in the list, the recordable member never has to carry the claim, and a spear that does strike is not a refutation of a sentence that also promised that no devil would draw nigh."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:AegyptiaMalaAversa,
        pmc:AsipuLitaniaMalorum,
        pmc:AsipuMimmaLemnuLaTehi,
        pmc:CedarWardsTheWingedGod,
        pmc:EthiopianKeepingOfTheDevilsInRestraint,
        pmc:MayaAvertingOfForetoldIlls,
        pmc:VoSesa ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where an operative transmission names the harms that its protection averts, it joins in one sentence or one list, and without marking the difference, harms whose occurrence any bystander would notice — a spear striking, a man behaving as a contrary, a head cut off, a drought, a named disease — to harms whose occurrence nobody could record — a devil drawing nigh, a god paying a visit, ills indicated as to come, anything bad approaching. The protection is claimed as satisfied whole, so no recordable harm is ever made the test of it, and none of these transmissions states a condition under which the protection would be judged to have failed. Where only one harm is named it is of the unrecordable kind. A protection attached to a single recordable harm and to nothing else occurs in the holding, and occurs once."@en .

pmc:TheProvisionForNotKnowing a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Überlieferung, die alles vom Namen abhängen lässt, führt auch einen Weg, ohne ihn zu arbeiten"@de,
        "The transmission that makes everything depend on the name also carries a way of working without it"@en ;
    skos:note "Counter-search, carried against the compiled holding before the statement was written, as SPEC §16 requires. Every node in the traditions whose wording makes knowing a name or an origin the condition of an operation was listed and read, and each was checked for a clause covering the case of not knowing. No refutation among those read, and two near-misses that are worth naming because both were candidates. The Mandaean watch-houses demand a name at every station, but the name demanded is the traveller's own and is conferred by a ceremony performed while alive, so there is nothing for him to fail to know; the holding already marks this at pmc:MandaeanNomenEtSignum. And the Shangqing requirement to know the names of the three times eight luminaries carries a stated consequence for not knowing them and no provision at all — but what is to be known there is the practitioner's own pantheon and not the identity of anything he is dealing with, so it falls outside the statement rather than against it."@en,
        "The Egyptian corpus supplies both ends and is the reason the statement is worth making. At the hall of the two Maati every element of the doorway refuses passage unless told its name, which is the requirement in its strictest form; and chapter 147 hands the dead man the name of the doorkeeper, the watcher and the herald of each of the seven arits before he is asked for any of them. The provision there is not a clause but the book: what is sold to the buyer is the answer sheet to an examination the same book describes as unpassable without it. Twenty-one names entered at traditions/egyptian-arits.ttl are the whole content of that chapter."@en,
        "The three forms, one witness each. The disjunction: the Babylonian adjuration runs whether thou be an evil Spirit or an evil Demon, or sickness, or death, or evil pestilence, and then removes whichever it is. The formula for not knowing: the Finnish collection makes the pronouncing of the origin the operation itself and treats it as a thing the operator either has or lacks, and it carries a separate formula for lacking it — one whose speaker says outright that he does not know who the maker of the hurt is, and which has a number and a place in the order rather than standing as an emergency measure. The apparatus of objects: the eighteenth chapter of the Baopuzi gives a key by which a thing met in the mountains is named from the day-branch and the title it gives itself, closes the list with the rule that only knowing the name takes away the harm, and then answers the same encounters in its other half with talismans in cinnabar on peachwood, a seal of jujube heartwood, the mirror and realgar, none of which requires the name."@en,
        "Thin where it is thin, and it is thin in two places. Three traditions carry the three forms and a fourth carries both ends, which is four corpora and not a survey; and two of the four, the Babylonian and the Egyptian, were read in this same September, so the pattern was found by a reader who had just been looking at one of them. That is a reason to state the falsifier precisely and to run it in a corpus nobody chose for this purpose."@en,
        "What the statement does not say. It does not say that the identification is a pretence, and it does not say the provisions were added later by people who found the requirement inconvenient; neither would follow from the wording and neither was investigated. It says that in every corpus read here the two stand together, and that the requirement therefore never bears the weight a reader of it alone would give it."@en,
        "Where the statement is most likely to break, named here rather than left for a reader to find. The Zande poison oracle exists to name the one who is bewitching at this moment, and the question is put to it about a person the questioner has already named; a transmission whose whole apparatus needs a name supplied from outside is the shape that would refute this, if it also turns out to supply nothing for the case of having no name to put. The holding does not carry enough of the Zande consultation procedure to decide that, and a run that reads it would settle the statement one way or the other faster than any other corpus now in the graph."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:AegyptiaPortaeNomina,
        pmc:AsipuNominisIncertitudo,
        pmc:HuwuZhiming,
        pmp:AegyptiaSeptemAritae,
        pmp:FinnicSynnynLukeminen,
        pmp:FinnicSyynTiedustus ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission makes an operation depend on identifying what is being dealt with — the name of the adversary, the origin of the hurt, the name of the thing met on the road — the same corpus carries a standing provision for proceeding without that identification, and the provision is not an emergency measure but has a place of its own. It takes one of three forms: a disjunction that names every candidate at once, so that the identification need not be made; a form of words for the case of not knowing, with a number and a fixed position in the order of recitation; or an apparatus of objects that answers the same encounters and does not require a name at all. In no case read here does the corpus that states the requirement leave the practitioner who cannot meet it with nothing."@en .

pmc:TheRefusalAndWhatItRestsOn a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Zurückweisung von außen stützt sich auf einen Fall, die von innen auf einen Zustand des Übenden"@de,
        "The refusal from outside rests on a case, the refusal from inside on a state of the practitioner"@en ;
    skos:note "Examined on the day it was written, at two of the three addresses its falsifier names, and it did not come through unchanged. Both addresses deviate, and both in the same direction: the first discourse of the Dīgha refuses the choosing of days on the ground of livelihood, and the prophetic tradition on omens refuses it with a rule for one already perplexed. Neither grounds the refusal in a case and neither grants an exemption; neither says anything about whether the art works. A third ground is therefore in the holding that this statement has no room for, and the statement should be read as describing transmissions that dispute or teach the art, not every transmission that declines it. See pmp:RefusalGroundExamination, which carries the finding and the recommended restatement."@en,
        "Falsified by a transmission that rejects the choosing of a day from outside the practice and grounds the rejection in doctrine alone, adducing no occasion — a cosmology, a scriptural prohibition, an argument from the nature of the heavens with no instance under it; or by one that rejects it from inside and does adduce an occasion, naming a practitioner who acted outside the window and reporting what followed. Either breaks it, and the first is the likelier: the scriptural prohibitions of the Hebrew Bible, the ḥadīṯ against the ill omen of the month of Ṣafar and the treatment of naksatra prognostication among the low arts in the first discourse of the Dīgha are all refusals from outside, none of them was read at the wording in this run, and any of them may well be doctrine without a case. They are named here so that the statement can be attacked at its weakest point rather than at its strongest."@en,
        "The consequence for this holding, and it is the reason the node was written. The counter-material against pmc:AdmissibilityBoundToTheCalendar consisted, until 2026-09-21, of the two refusals from inside and of one critic. That is a body of counter-material that cannot discriminate: an exemption for the attained says nothing about whether an operation done on the wrong day works, and both of the earlier cases say in so many words that it does not. The three from outside do discriminate, and two of the three were read at the wording on the day this node was written. See pmp:CalendarFailureLocationExamination, whose own finding was that the sharpest argument in the holding came from a single Chinese critic."@en,
        "The count is six claims in six transmissions and four spheres — Pāli, Latin, Chinese in two separate streams, and the Latin grimoire — with no proposed route between the three that argue from cases. That is a better spread than most statements here, and it is still six."@en,
        "The three that refuse from inside, and not one of them names an occasion. The thirteenth quatrain of the Jindan sizi says that the fire phases use no hour and that the winter solstice is not at zi, and its commentary adds that once a man has started the fire on the circuit within his own body the withdrawing and adding are there of themselves and one may not be bound to the four seasons: the condition has been moved from the calendar into the body. The eighteenth chapter of the Baopuzi says that one who can guard the One need not divine the day or choose the hour and no longer consults the siting calendars: the exemption is granted by an attainment. The Key of Solomon says that so exact a preparation of days and hours is not necessary for those who are adepts in the art but is extremely necessary for apprentices and beginners, and gives as the ground that beginners lack faith in the experiments: the exemption is granted by a state of mind, and the text says so."@en,
        "The three that refuse from outside, and all three argue from an occasion. The forty-ninth Jātaka tells one story twice: a family fixes a wedding day, asks its household ascetic afterwards whether the stars are favourable, is told they are not, stays at home, and loses the bride to somebody else — and the stanza that closes it says that the fool may watch for lucky days yet luck shall always miss, for it is luck itself that is luck's own star. The second book of Cicero's treatise sets two cases against the calculation of nativities and they cut in opposite directions: twin kings of the Lacedaemonians of whom one died a year before the other and much excelled him in glory, and the dead of Cannae, who met one end and cannot all have been born under one constellation. The first-century treatise against the choosing of days puts King Wu winning his battle on a jiazi day and the tyrant losing his on the same jiazi day, the two armies facing one another with their standards in sight."@en,
        "Weakened, without being refuted, if the division turns out to track the genre and not the position of the speaker. Two of the three outside witnesses are narrative and argumentative literature, which is where cases live anyway, and two of the three inside ones are operative manuals, which state rules and not stories. A transmission that refuses from outside in the form of a manual, or from inside in the form of a story, would separate the two readings, and this holding carries neither."@en,
        "Why the division is worth stating rather than the refusal. A refusal from outside has to persuade somebody who is still in the practice, and the only currency that buys that is an outcome the practice itself would have had to predict; a refusal from inside does not have to persuade anybody, because it is addressed to those who already accept the teacher, and it can therefore be granted as a privilege. The two are not weak and strong versions of one move. They are different moves, and only the first produces anything a protocol can take over."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:CiceroGeminiDiscrepantes,
        pmc:ClaviculaAdeptiExempti,
        pmc:NeidanHuohouBuyongshi,
        pmc:ShouyiMianLiri,
        pmc:TheravadaNakkhatta,
        pmc:ZeriDispute ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission rejects the choosing of a day from outside the practice that chooses it, it grounds the rejection in a case: an occasion on which one date carried two outcomes, or one outcome many dates, or the rule was followed exactly and the follower lost what he sought. Where a transmission rejects the same choosing from inside the practice that teaches it, it grounds the rejection in a state of the practitioner — his attainment, his faith, the condition moved into his body — and adduces no case at all."@en .

pmc:TheRepairWithoutASign a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Wiedergutmachung trägt genau dann ein Zeichen, wenn der Schaden, den sie behebt, einer war, den der Übende sehen konnte"@de,
        "The repair carries a sign exactly where the harm it repairs was one the practitioner could see"@en ;
    skos:note "Counter-search, carried against the compiled holding before the statement was written, as SPEC §16 requires. Two near-misses and no refutation. The Kongo diagnosis of a charged thing that fails — either it is sulky and wants a sacrifice or it has lost its power and wants renewing — is a repair for an unobservable harm and names no sign, so it falls on the same side as the witnesses rather than against them; it was left out of the derivation only because the harm there is the failure of the instrument and not a fault in a procedure. The Shangqing rule that reads a corpse in any condition as the attainment is the harder case, because it does supply a sign; but the sign is read off an event that ends the practitioner's participation, so it is not a sign the practitioner has. Neither breaks the statement."@en,
        "Falsified by an operative transmission that attaches an unobservable or deferred harm to a fault in its own procedure, prescribes a repair, and in the same text names a sign by which the repair is known to have taken effect, or a condition under which the repair fails. One such case refutes it. The likeliest place to look is the performance literature rather than the exegesis: the Vedic expiation books of the Śrautasūtras, Kātyāyana 25 and Āpastamba 9, stand unopened in traditions/vedic-ritual.ttl, and a manual written for the man who has to do the thing has a reason to say when he may stop that a commentary explaining what the thing means does not."@en,
        "The claim is about the wording and not about whether repairs work. A transmission that names no sign is not thereby making a false claim; it is making one whose failure has nowhere to show, and it usually does so without any sign of intending to — the Vedic book is manifestly trying to be thorough, and thoroughness about the list of mishaps is exactly what makes the silence about the outcome visible."@en,
        "Thin where it is thin, and the thinness is on the observable side. Three witnesses stand on the silent side and one on the other, so what the statement really establishes is that the silence is not a necessity of the form. Whether it is the normal case would need a count across the holding's repairs, and no such count has been made."@en,
        "Two cases in the holding were checked as possible refutations and are not. The Zande oracle prescribes a corroborative second consultation whose contradiction voids the verdict, which is a check with a stated outcome — but it is a check on an answer and not a repair of a fault, and nothing is being put right. The Navajo ceremony broken off as fruitless when a detected error is caught is the opposite arrangement and belongs to the other statement: there the fault is observed and no repair is offered at all."@en,
        "What each witness supplies, in the transmission's own terms. The Vedic expiation book enumerates twenty-two mishaps of the daily milk oblation — the cow lying down, the cow giving blood, the milk spilt before and after ladling, the vessel overturned or broken, a fire gone out, the fires mingling, the sun risen over an uncarried offering — and prescribes an act for each; for what is not enumerated it supplies three syllables which it calls all-expiatory; and it closes with the sentence that no hurt and no harm of any kind befalls where that expiation is made. The harms it repairs are the death of the eldest son, of the master of the house, of the cattle, or the failure of the line, all of them stated as coming before long and none of them with a term. The Maya conjuring of the ills foretold for an Ix year answers ills that run from drought to political discord, and the witness names no sign by which the freeing would be recognised and no interval within which the ills would otherwise have come. The Kongo leaf renders every charged thing in a house useless, which is a condition discovered only after the fact, and the prescribed reinvigoration of the whole stock carries nothing to show it took. Against these the Baopuzi: the practitioner who forgets the guarding of the One is harmed by the hundred ghosts or pressed down in his sleep, which he notices himself; he goes into the courtyard, looks at the assistant star and guards the One, and the ghost departs; and if it is overcast he stays indoors, faces north and thinks the star, and that is all."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:MayaIxRemedyYieldsAverting,
        pmc:NginduLeafNullifiesEveryCharm,
        pmc:ShouyiZhongnan,
        pmc:VaidikaPrayascittaSarvam ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where the harm a transmission attaches to a fault in its own procedure is one the practitioner cannot observe — deferred past a term the text does not fix, laid on another party, or consisting in the mere absence of an effect — the repair it prescribes for that fault is asserted to work without exception and is given no sign by which it is known to have taken effect and no condition under which it fails. Where the harm is instead a present condition the practitioner notices in himself, the same kind of transmission states the repair's success as the ending of that condition, and provides for the case in which the repair cannot be carried out."@en .

pmc:TheSignTakenForTheAttainment a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Erscheinung wird als das benannt, was den für sie gehaltenen Fortschritt anhält"@de,
        "The appearance is named as what stops the progress it is taken for"@en ;
    skos:note "Falsified by a transmission whose practice produces such appearances, whose earliest witness treats them as reliable evidence of attainment, and which names no way of telling a true one from a counterfeit. Candidates are the visionary literatures that grade experiences by degree of light."@en,
        "It stands beside pmc:SelfWarningStandsInsideThePrescription and is not the same claim. That one says the caution is in the prescribing passage; this one says what the caution is about. A transmission could satisfy either without the other."@en,
        "Refuted on 2026-09-26, and the case came from the class the falsifier named. The Yogavacara's Manual, the one surviving published witness of the Boran kammatthana transmission, was read complete at the wording; it grades the five jhanas by an ascending scale of light — firefly, the moon's halo, the medicine star, the sun's halo, white flowers — grades the five raptures by a triad of colours each, states every appearance in the indicative as what appears to the practitioner, and names no counterfeit, no obstacle and no imperfection anywhere. The words counterfeit, false, illusion, deception, obstacle, hindrance and defilement do not occur in it. The nodes are pmc:BoranPancajhanaVanna and pmc:BoranPancapitiVanna, and the protocol is examinations/the-sign-and-the-counterfeit.ttl."@en,
        "The refutation is close to home, which makes it worth more than a distant one. The second witness of this claim is the Visuddhimagga's ten imperfections of insight. The refuting manual is Theravada, uses the same Pali vocabulary of jhana, kasina, nimitta, appana and upacara, cites the same scheme of ten insights, and belongs to the transmission whose ordination line came from Siam to Ceylon in the middle of the eighteenth century. So what this claim had caught was not a property of transmissions that grade by inner appearances; it was a property of one text of that tradition, and another text of the same tradition does the opposite."@en,
        "The second half of the statement is the part that could fail and is therefore the part worth having. Both sources refuse to grade the experience by its own force: what decides is the setting, the sequence, what precedes and follows. A tradition that measured the counterfeit by intensity would break the claim without touching its first half."@en,
        "Two transmissions with no documented route between them say the same thing about the same class of experience. The desert discernment literature warns that luminosity or consolation may be counterfeit and that the counterfeit is recognised by its circumstances and not by its brightness. The Theravāda commentary names ten states arising in practice — luminosity, rapture, tranquillity, pleasure, resolve among them — which are not defilements in themselves and become obstacles precisely when taken as proof of attainment."@en,
        "What the successor statement should try, recorded here rather than substituted for the sentence above. Both original witnesses are literature addressed to somebody who supervises another person's practice — a handbook of discernment and a commentary on the path. The refuting case is a liturgy addressed to the practitioner and recited by him while he works. The distinction to test is therefore the addressee and not the appearances: the counterfeit may be named where a transmission instructs a supervisor and absent where it instructs the operator. If that holds, the two witnesses were selected by a genre nobody had noticed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:AngelOfLightDeception,
        pmc:ImperfectionsOfInsight ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Transmissions that measure progress by inner appearances name those appearances as the chief obstacle to further progress, on the ground that the practitioner takes them for the attainment; and they place the mark of the counterfeit in the circumstances of the experience rather than in its intensity."@en .

pmc:TheStrongerAddress a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wo das Ergebnis eine Erscheinung ist, gibt es keinen Punkt zum Aufhören"@de,
        "Where the result is an apparition, there is no point at which to stop"@en ;
    skos:note "Counter-search against the compiled holding, carried before this was written. Every node whose wording prescribes what to do when a promised result does not arrive was drawn and read at its place, and the terms searched over the holding were cease, desist, abandon, stop, leave off, give up, in vain, definitive judgment, does not come and never comes. No transmission of the first kind was found that names a term. Three near-misses came back and none of them meets the antecedent."@en,
        "Examined on 2026-09-23 at pmp:StrongerAddressExamination, which found cases with deviation. The note that follows records the state before that protocol and is left standing as the record of it. What a protocol would have to do first is fix the population, and the hard part is the second column: this holding carries exactly one transmission in it, so the clause is at present a statement about the animal magnetism wearing the grammar of a class. A second transmission whose result is a reported change in a second person would settle whether there is a division here or only a contrast between two files."@en,
        "Falsified by a transmission that makes the appearance of a being its result and states a term, a count of addresses or a sign at which the operator is to conclude that nothing will come — a grimoire that sends the company home at dawn, a manual that gives the inquiry three attempts and no more. One such case refutes the first clause as stated. Falsified in the other direction by a transmission whose result is a reported change in a second person and which prescribes, in place of any term, a stronger address repeated without limit. The likeliest places to look for the first are the transmissions this holding carries that already fix an operation to an hour or a day, since a bounded hour is the nearest thing to a bounded session: the demotic manual itself carries nine such datings at pmc:PdmHoraEtLuna and states no consequence for any of them."@en,
        "The near-misses, because each of them looks at first like a refutation. The Ars Notoria states a term of one month and an outcome due at the end of it, but its result is knowledge in the operator and not the appearance of anything, so it belongs to neither column; the protocol on it stands at examinations/notory-art-one-month.ttl. The Shangqing sun held in the heart names one year, five years and eighteen years, and what is due at the term is a property of the practitioner's own body in daylight, which is again a third kind. And the ascent ritual of the Greek papyrus carries a forfeiture clause — three times in the year, and no longer coming about for whoever disregards the instruction after it has been given — which is a rule about who may have it and not a rule about a session that produced nothing; the same piece prescribes nothing at all for a non-appearance in either direction, and that silence is recorded as its own search rather than counted here."@en,
        "The route between the two witnesses of the first clause is not clean and is stated rather than passed over. The demotic manual of the third century and the Latin grimoire of the late Middle Ages stand in one stream: the Greek and demotic handbooks of Egypt, the Byzantine Hygromanteia and the Latin Clavicula are connected by a transmission history that this holding does not claim to have established but does not pretend is absent either, and the tradition instance of the grimoire carries pm:contactRouteDocumented. Two witnesses on one cable is the floor of SPEC §16 and no more than the floor. The three witnesses of the second clause stand in a nineteenth-century European medical polemic with no proposed route to either, so the division the statement asserts runs across a contact route and not along one, and that is what makes the division worth stating at all."@en,
        "The second clause was rewritten on 2026-09-23 after the protocol at pmp:StrongerAddressExamination met it with the case its own falsification note had named. The clause said that a transmission whose result is a reported change in the condition of a second person states a term, as a count of sittings or of days. The Celestial Masters make their result the recovery of a sick person, and the petition manual answers a failure to heal with a further and stronger petition through some fourteen named stages, the failure of each being the sole trigger of the next, with no term anywhere in the corpus — an absence established by search and exhibited at pmp:CmZhangqiSearch, in a corpus that counts a hundred days of pollution after a death in the house and prints five tables of forbidden days. The clause as it stood is therefore false as a universal, and what survives is entered above: the column divides, and the kind of result does not decide it. The sixth witness is pmc:CmBuyuJizhang and it stands on the first clause rather than the second, which is the whole of the correction."@en,
        "The three witnesses of the second clause, all from the animal magnetism and two of its works. Five days before one may presume that the operation does not act, and then a second criterion to consult. Three or four sittings of nervous irritation, after which one may presume that the operation is not good for this patient and ought not to persist. Eight consecutive days of twenty minutes at the same hour, after which the operator will be able to form a definitive judgment. All three name a count; all three say what the count settles; and what it settles in every case is something about this operator or this pairing and never about the agent, which is the finding those three nodes already carry."@en,
        "The two witnesses of the first clause and what each does with an empty session. The demotic manual takes it up in six places and every one of them prescribes speech: the boy who does not see the light is made to close his eyes and called to again; obduracy is met by the summons, which is the god's compulsion; obstinacy with the god unseen is met by the compulsion; a god who delays so as not to come in is met by a further cry; a light that has not come forth is met by having the boy address the lamp himself; a light slow to come within is met by a name said seven times, incense, and a great name uttered forwards and backwards; and gods who are inside and give no answer are met by another name said nine times until they inquire truthfully. The Latin grimoire sets its conjurations in a series and makes the trigger of each the failure of the one before, running through the four quarters, the signing of the foreheads, the re-forming of the circle and a final conjuration declared strong enough that spirits bound with chains of iron and fire could not delay to come."@en,
        "What the correction leaves open and where a later run should go. Two transmissions of the second kind now disagree, which is a pair and not a class. The candidate distinguishing feature, stated at the protocol and not established here, is not the kind of result but where the outcome falls relative to the rite: the animal magnetism works on a patient present through a sitting whose length the operator fixes, and the Celestial Masters send a document to an office whose answer, if it comes, arrives outside the rite altogether. A term is statable in the first case and hard to state in the second. The population that would decide it — the transmissions whose operation is a despatch rather than an encounter — has not been drawn."@en,
        "What the statement does not claim. It does not say that the transmissions of the second column abide by their terms, and the three witnesses show they largely do not: the five-day rule replaces its own criterion in the following sentence, the three-or-four-sittings rule prescribes trying two or three further operators, and the eight-day rule is to be applied only to subjects selected beforehand for their likelihood of succeeding. What the second clause claims is that the term is stated, which is a property of the wording; the first clause claims that in the other column it is not stated at all, and that is the asymmetry."@en,
        "What the two have in common beyond the escalation is the absence, and the absence is the half that does the work. Neither names a number of addresses after which the operator is to stop, neither names a sign that the being will not come, and neither gives the session a length. A run is therefore not over until it has succeeded, which is why no run can be counted as having failed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:CinqJoursAvantDePresumer,
        pmc:ClaviculaGradusConiurationum,
        pmc:CmBuyuJizhang,
        pmc:HuitJoursConsecutifs,
        pmc:PdmCompulsioNonAdventus,
        pmc:TroisOuQuatreSeances ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission makes the appearance of a being the result of its operation, it meets a non-appearance with a further and stronger address of the same kind and states no term, count or sign at which the operator is to conclude that nothing will come. The holding also contains transmissions whose result is a change in the condition of a second person which that person reports, and these divide: some state such a term, give it as a count of sittings or of days, and name what the term settles, while at least one meets a failure to heal with a further and stronger despatch of the same kind through some fourteen stages and states no term at all. The kind of result does not by itself decide which of the two a transmission does."@en .

pmc:TheSunHidesHisFaceOverAnOffence a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Sonne verbirgt ihr Gesicht, solange ein Unwürdiger im Lager ist"@de,
        "The Sun hides his face while an unworthy person is in the camp"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 100 and p. 105" ;
    skos:definition "It is proclaimed at the dawn of each holy day that one who knows himself unworthy to appear before the face of the Sun must not enter the ceremonial camp circle, because if such a one appears the Sun will hide his face with a veil of clouds until the offender withdraws or until the Winged God sweeps or washes away the offence. If the face is hidden at sunrise the marshals seek the cause of offence and expel it, and the people wait until the Sun shows his face. If on any day he does not show it, that day is not counted as a holy day, and enough days are counted to make four."@en ;
    skos:note "A sign read off the weather, with a stated cause, a stated remedy and a stated procedure for the case where the sign appears. It is the closest thing in this file to a falsifying condition applied in public, and it is also the clearest case in this file of a condition that cannot fail: an overcast morning is assigned to an offender, and if no offender is found the day is struck from the count instead. Both halves are in the corpus and both are entered."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LakotaRitual .

pmc:TheTestOfAPersonIsRefused a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wo das Vermögen in einer Person liegt, ist seine absichtliche Prüfung verboten"@de,
        "Where the capacity is in a person, the deliberate test of it is forbidden"@en ;
    skos:note "Examined on 2026-09-29. The protocol is pmp:WhoMayTestAPersonExamination in examinations/who-may-test-a-person.ttl, and the outcome is pm:casesWithDeviation: the core of the statement held against everything read and both qualifying clauses failed. The claim's own falsifier — a transmission of this kind that invites an outsider to test and lets a negative outcome count — was not met. What was met are the two clauses the statement had added to protect itself: one corpus grounds the refusal both in the thing and in the unworthiness of the asker, so the negation has to become not only; and the exemption for a trial between two practitioners now has three cases of three different shapes, which is a second class and not a boundary. The successor statement the protocol recommends is about who may test rather than about what the refusal is grounded in, and it is written out there rather than substituted for the sentence above."@en,
        "Falsified by a transmission claiming a capacity of a person whose earliest witness invites an outsider to test it, states what a negative outcome would be, and treats such an outcome as telling against the capacity rather than against the conditions of the trial. The nineteenth-century literature on both sides of the magnetic dispute is where such a case would most likely be found, and it has not been opened in this holding."@en,
        "Fourth witness, added on 2026-09-29 from the Swedenborgian corpus, and it is the earliest of the four by a century. Having listed the people to whom spirits appeared in human form — Abraham, Lot, Manoah and his wife, Joshua, Mary, Elizabeth, the prophets generally — the Arcana says that now this rarely happens, lest such things should compel men to believe; for a compulsory faith, such as enters by means of miracles, does not cleave to the man, and might also be an injury to those with whom faith could be implanted through the Word in a state that is not compulsory. The class of evidence an outsider would ask for is withheld on purpose, and the ground is a property of the belief such evidence produces."@en,
        "It is not independent of two of the other three and the direction of the route is worth stating, because it runs the other way from the usual case in this holding. Swedenborg published from 1749 and died in 1772; the magnetic manual and the spiritist manual that are witnesses one and three are later, and both milieux knew him. So this corpus is the earlier station of a documented route into two of the witnesses, not a fourth voice arriving from elsewhere. What it adds is age and not independence: the form is older than the nineteenth century by more than the Pali discourse alone established, and it is found in a printed doctrine addressed to strangers rather than in a manual for operators."@en,
        "It is not the claim at pmc:TestingTheProcedureVoidsItsAnswer and the difference is the antecedent. That one is about transmissions that obtain an answer from a procedure — a cast, a consultation — and says the repeated or testing question is excluded. This one is about transmissions whose claim is a capacity of a named person, where there is no procedure a stranger could work and therefore nothing to repeat. A transmission can satisfy either without the other, and the spiritist manual happens to satisfy both, in two different chapters and for two different reasons."@en,
        "The counter-search was run against this holding before the statement was written, and it found one case that bounds it. Among the Sámi material, when two practitioners wished to know which of them excelled the other in the art, they painted two reindeer striking each other on a drum and he whose reindeer had the victory was the better. That is a deliberate trial of a capacity located in a person, with a criterion, an outcome that could fall either way and no party controlling it — and it is prescribed rather than forbidden. What distinguishes it from the three witnesses is who proposes the test: there it is the practitioners themselves and the loser remains a practitioner, whereas the rule in all three witnesses answers an enquirer or an onlooker who wants to be convinced. The last sentence of the statement carries that boundary, and a second case of the same shape would strengthen the statement rather than break it."@en,
        "The same corpus also supplies the first case in this holding of a transmission that refuses the test on BOTH grounds, and that is what this witness costs the statement. The statement says the rule is grounded in a property of the capacity and not in the unworthiness of whoever proposes the test. At Arcana 10751 it is the first. At Heaven and Hell 456 it is the second: the reason given for withholding visible proof of the state after death is that those who have confirmed themselves in falses would not believe the evidence of their senses, and that such demonstrations are dangerous to them because they would first believe and afterwards deny, which is profanation and carries the lowest hell. Both passages refuse the same demonstration and they do not agree about why. The node is pmc:SwedenborgProfanatioPostFidem, and the consequence for the statement is that its discriminating clause should read not only rather than not: a corpus can ground the refusal in the thing and also, elsewhere, in the asker, and this one does."@en,
        "The three witnesses say it in three idioms and the ground is a property of the thing claimed in each. The magnetic manual makes it a general rule from which the operator is never to depart, never on any account to permit any attempt to prove the somnambulist's lucidity, and derives the prohibition in the same chapter from the prerequisite that lucidity requires an undivided attention: the reason the faculty cannot be tested is the reason it is said to work. The spiritist manual states that superior spirits have no sign by which to be recognised other than the superiority of their ideas, that every spirit can imitate a material sign and that the names of saints are taken freely, so that neither a sign nor a name is proof. The Pali discourse distinguishes three marvels, of psychic power, of mind-reading and of instruction, holds only the third to be reliable, and refuses the display of power for the sake of persuading anyone as something to be ashamed of."@en,
        "Two further cases of the boundary clause were read in the same batch, and with four instances that clause is now carrying more than a boundary usually bears. The last sentence of the statement says that where two practitioners measure their capacities against one another a criterion is fixed and the trial is not forbidden; the Sami reindeer contest above was the one case. The Spiritual Diary reports a test carried out on the other party — refuse to acknowledge a spirit as the Holy Spirit and observe what it does, anger and threats marking the unclean one — with two outcomes and neither in the operator's hand; that is pmc:SwedenborgProbatioSpiritus, and it differs from the Sami case in that the party tested is not a second practitioner but the interlocutor. And the Theravada handbook narrates a trial between two practitioners that is not a contest but a correction: Dhammadinna answers nothing and asks everything, has the elder Mahanaga create an elephant and then charge him with it, the elder flinches and runs, and the flinch establishes that his attainment of sixty years' standing was not what he took it for. That is pmc:UpakkilesaHatthiParikkha. In both the test works, in both it is proposed by somebody inside the transmission, and in neither is it forbidden."@en,
        "Two of the three witnesses have a documented route between them: the spiritist manual of 1861 comes out of the magnetic milieu of the preceding century and its author knew that literature. They are therefore not two independent witnesses, and no independence is claimed here — a generalization asserts a recurring form and not a count of witnesses. The Pali discourse is unconnected to both, and it is the witness that decides whether the form is older than the nineteenth century."@en,
        "What the four instances of that clause suggest, stated as the question rather than as a rewritten sentence. The distinction the statement draws may not be between the capacity and the asker at all, but between an enquirer who wants to be convinced and a practitioner who wants to know: the first is refused on every ground the transmissions can find, the second is admitted and given a criterion. If that is right, then what these transmissions protect is not the capacity but the boundary of the transmission, and the successor statement should be about who is allowed to test rather than about what the refusal is grounded in."@en,
        "What the statement costs the claims it is about is worth saying plainly, because it is the reason to hold it. A rule of this shape makes every negative result attributable to the testing, and one of the three witnesses states that consequence itself: where an incredulous person consults in order to put the somnambulist to the proof, he is said to exert, without knowing it, an influence that furnishes him with new reasons for his incredulity. That is a prediction that a failed test confirms the claim, and it is the point at which a transmission stops being examinable from outside."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:CautionAgainstProvingTheLucidity,
        pmc:DisplayOfPowersRefused,
        pmc:SigneMaterielCaution,
        pmc:SwedenborgSignaNonCoguntFidem ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission locates a claimed capacity in a person rather than in a procedure that anyone could work, it states a rule withdrawing that person from a deliberate test, and it grounds the rule in a property of the capacity or of the party exercised through it — that the undivided attention it requires is destroyed by the testing, that the party addressed withdraws from a question put as a test, that no material sign it could produce would settle anything — and not in the unworthiness of whoever proposes the test. The rule governs a test proposed by an enquirer or an onlooker. Where a transmission instead has two practitioners measure their capacities against one another, it fixes a criterion and does not forbid the trial."@en .

pmc:TheTestOnTheIngredient a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Probe, die ein Handbuch durchfallen kann, prueft, was es einkauft, und nicht, was es tut"@de,
        "The test a handbook can fail is a test of what it buys, not of what it does"@en ;
    skos:note "A third corpus was read through with the question on 2026-09-26, counted and not sampled, and it produced the case the file had not allowed for: a handbook whose assays are all of the second kind and none of the first. The Turba philosophorum, seventy-two dicta read complete, states two acts with a definite perceptible outcome, and both judge the work in progress. At Dictum 28 the powder is taken in the hand and, if it is impalpable as water, is most excellent; otherwise the cooking is repeated until it is brought to the required condition. At Dictum 13 the operator looks to see whether it has become a powder and, if not, roasts in a fire stronger than the first until it is pounded. Both carry the repetition rule the second limb predicts, which makes the fifth witness. What the work does not state anywhere — checked against the whole of it and recorded as an exhibited gap at pmp:TurbaMateriaeProbaSearch — is any way of knowing that a material is what it is called, for any of the thirty-odd substances it names under a vocabulary it itself says was multiplied in order to deceive."@en,
        "A third demonstration of the same shape was read on 2026-09-22, in a corpus as far from the first two as the register reaches, and it bears on the residue rather than on the statement. An American healer receives a patient who has come after hearing him called a spiritualist and a mesmerist, tells her he never wishes a patient to tell him her feelings, takes her hand, names a sharp pain in one vertebra and a pain in the left temple affecting the sight of that eye, and only then explains his theory. Like the demotic assay and like the booklet's knife, it is offered to somebody who does not believe, it can come out the other way, and no clause covers the outcome in which it does. The node is pmc:QuimbyProbaAdAegrum. Three cases from three transmissions with no route between them is no longer a coincidence of two manuscripts, and the question the protocol carries — whether every assay of an operation in these corpora is aimed at a sceptic — now has a third case supporting it and still no count."@en,
        "A third thing the same volume shows, and it belongs beside the second limb rather than against it: the handbook does assay its own operation with a rule of repetition where the material allows one. After the swallowing of gold the mercury is to weigh what it weighed before, and if it weighs more it is to be boiled again until it reverts to its original weight. So one work carries both arrangements — an assay of the operation with a repetition rule at the tenth operation, and an assay of the operation without one at the sixteenth — which is the sharpest evidence in the holding that the device is chosen case by case and is not a property of the genre. The node is pmc:RasaGrasanaParyaptiPariksa."@en,
        "Counter-search, carried against the compiled holding before the statement was written, as SPEC section 16 requires. Every node whose definition states an assay, a way of knowing, a genuine article or a counterfeit was read. Three classes of near-miss and no refutation. The ordeals and the divinatory criteria that this holding carries in quantity — the sinking or floating in boiling water, the corroborative second consultation of the poison oracle, the fork at the shaman's seance, the recovery of the sick after the immersion, the felling of the ordeal tree — all state both sides, and they were the material of pmc:TheCriterionNoPartyControls. They are excluded by the wording and not by exception: in each the outcome is the answer that was asked for, not a verdict on whether the procedure was done correctly. The contests, which the same neighbouring statement already separates out, are excluded on the same ground. And the discernment literature, which teaches that a consolation may be counterfeit and is recognised by its circumstances, names no act with an outcome and so states no assay at all."@en,
        "Falsified by an operative handbook that states, of its own operation, an act with a definite perceptible outcome by which the operation is established to have worked or not worked, and attaches to the negative outcome neither a repetition rule nor a cause outside the procedure. One such case refutes it. The likeliest place to look is the pharmacological and alchemical literature rather than the ritual, because a text that already assays its ingredients has the form to hand: the Leiden papyrus of the same find stands unopened at pmw:PgmXII in traditions/greek-magical-papyri.ttl, and of the two corpora this sentence named as unopened the second, the mercurial alchemy of India, has since been opened and produced the case recorded above."@en,
        "It was refuted from the place the falsifier did not name. The falsifier sent a later run to the pharmacological and alchemical literature; the counter-case came from a booklet of household charms that this file had cited three times without opening. That is worth recording about the falsifier and not only about the statement: the corpus most likely to refute a claim about handbooks was the handbook the holding already had a citation to."@en,
        "Refuted in its second limb on 2026-09-22, three days after it was written, and the case is recorded rather than the statement quietly repaired. The Romanus-Büchlein, opened for the first time in this holding, prints after its third blood-staunching the sentence: but whoever will not believe it, let him write the letters on a knife and stab an unreasoning animal, and it will not bleed. That is an assay of the operation itself, with an outcome that could come out the other way, and the booklet attaches to the refuting outcome neither a rule of repetition nor a cause lying outside the procedure. The node is pmc:RomanusProbaAdIncredulum and the protocol below carries the deviation."@en,
        "The address the falsifier named was opened on 2026-09-25 and it paid, on both limbs. The mercurial alchemy of India was registered and unopened when the sentence below was written; the first volume of Mookerjee's Rasa-Jala-Nidhi has now been read at the wording, and it supplies a witness for the first limb and a second counter-case for the second. First limb: the look of purified mercury, deep blue inside and bright as the mid-day sun outside, decides the material and carries no excuse, and the failing material goes back through the operation. Second limb: the five marks of incinerated mercury — lustreless, light, white, a killer of metals, devoid of subtleness, and it does not sublimate if placed upon fire — are an assay of the handbook's own sixteenth operation, and the volume names the failing outcome in its own right and gives it a verdict: mercury that sublimates out of the crucible during the incineration is called sajiva baddha, does not possess the properties of incinerated mercury, and is not efficacious. No repetition rule and no external cause stands beside it. The nodes are pmc:RasaSuddharasalaksana and pmc:RasaBhasmalaksana in traditions/rasashastra.ttl."@en,
        "The proportion this protocol has been asking for is therefore now measurable in one direction and the first number is in. Across the two corpora read through completely with the question — the first volume of the Rasa-Jala-Nidhi on 2026-09-25 and the Turba philosophorum on 2026-09-26 — the count of assays of a material is two and zero, and the count of assays of the operation is two and two. The Turba's contribution to the first column is zero, and it is the only one of the corpora read whose author warns his reader that the names of his materials were changed to deceive him. Those two facts belong together and the successor statement should try them as one claim: a handbook assays its materials where it believes its names hold, and stops assaying them where it says they do not."@en,
        "The same run produced a fourth outcome the first limb does not provide for, and it is recorded because the successor statement will have to handle it. The first limb says a handbook assays its materials and finds them genuine or counterfeit. This healer assayed a material of his own — he believed silk attracted his subject, and found the subject's hand moved toward a lady with a silk apron even when she stood behind him — and concluded neither that the silk was good nor that it was false, but that his own belief had produced the effect and that no material had been involved. An assay whose result is that the assayed thing was never in the procedure is a third result, and it moves the variable into the operator's mind, where nothing further can reach it. The node is pmc:QuimbySericumOperantis."@en,
        "Thin where it is thin, and the thinness is on the first limb. Two of its four witnesses are two works of one corpus, found together and written within a century or two of one another, so for the first limb this holding has one tradition and not two. That is the reason the protocol below is entered as a procedure without cases rather than as cases without deviation: what has been established is that the arrangement exists and is not accidental to one manuscript, not that it is the normal case."@en,
        "This second counter-case settles the residue the file had been carrying, and it settles it against the guess. After the Romanus-Büchlein and the American healer the open question was whether every assay of an operation in these corpora is addressed to somebody who does not believe. This one is not. It stands in a chapter of definitions between the twenty-five classes of solidified mercury, it is addressed to the operator at his own bench, and the person it would disappoint is the man who did the work. The successor statement therefore cannot be rescued by restricting it to demonstrations offered to sceptics, and the second limb is not weakened but simply gone."@en,
        "Two cases were checked as possible refutations and are not. The signs of verification of the internal alchemy do state what success looks like, and the same chapter answers the practitioner who asks what happens if it does not come by naming two defects in the man — his choice of teacher and his knowledge of the times — which is the second limb and not a counter-case. The Egyptian gate material makes knowing the names the condition of passage and says a dozen times over what happens to the man who does not know, which looks like an assay of the operation until one asks who would observe it: the outcome falls after death and no bystander is given a criterion."@en,
        "What each witness supplies, in the transmission's own terms. The great Paris papyrus gives the recognition of the herb its operation depends on: the black tip of an ibis feather is slackened and smeared with the juice and at the mere touching the barbs fall off. That is stated flat, with no alternative and no excuse, and it decides the identity of the plant. The same appendix states of the far-shining sight only that the man anointed will see so that the operator marvels, and adds that no greater operation was found in the world. The demotic handbook of the same find gives the way to know that the white stone is genuine: a little is ground with water and rubbed on a man's skin for a short time, and it takes the skin off. The same work's divinations have seven or eight announced signs of arrival and, as this holding has already recorded, not one verdict on the operation. Against these the fourth chapter of the Baopuzi, which does assay the elixir: elixir joined with mercury and put to the fire makes gold, and where the gold forms the drug is complete — and where it does not form, the drug is to be sealed and fired again for the same count of days, after which, the chapter says, there is no case in which it does not form. And the Pulling Book, whose preventive states an outcome anybody could look for in his own mouth and then says, of the case in which the outcome is missing, to do more of the same."@en,
        "What survives and what does not. The first limb stands: the assays of materials in this holding are still exact and still carry no excuse. The second limb does not stand as a universal, and the successor statement will have to say less — that where a handbook assays its own operation it usually attaches an escape, and that the exception is addressed to a doubter rather than to the operator. That last part is the interesting residue: the two clauses in this holding that put an operation at risk are both demonstrations offered to somebody who does not believe, and neither is an instruction to the practitioner. Whether that is the rule was not established and is the question the protocol now carries."@en,
        "Why that is a finding about the statement and not a refutation of it. The first limb says that where such a handbook states an assay, the thing assayed is a material and not the operation, and the second limb provides for the handbook that does assay its operation. The Turba is a handbook consisting of nothing but the provided-for exception, and the statement as written has no place for it: read strictly, its first limb is false of every assay the Turba states, while its second limb is true of both. So the two limbs are not a rule and an exception but two arrangements, and a corpus can be built entirely out of either. A successor statement has to say that and cannot keep the default-and-exception form."@en,
        "Why the two limbs belong in one statement and not in two. An ingredient is bought, found or grown, so a failing assay of it costs the practitioner a specimen and leaves the procedure untouched; an assay of the operation would cost the procedure its standing. The statement says that handbooks are exact in the first place and provided-for in the second, and it is the pair that carries the claim. A holding that only recorded the immunising clauses would be reporting that these transmissions cannot state a testable thing, and that is false: they can, and they do it where it is safe."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:JinchengYaocheng,
        pmc:PdmProbatioLapidis,
        pmc:PgmIvProbatioKentritidis,
        pmc:TurbaProbaImpalpabilis,
        pmc:YinshuKouchi ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where an operative handbook states a procedure of assay — an act with a definite perceptible outcome by which something is established — the thing assayed is a material the operation consumes and not the operation itself, and the assay is stated without any clause that would excuse a negative result. Where such a handbook does state an assay of its own operation, it attaches in the same passage a clause that removes the negative result, either by prescribing repetition of the operation until the outcome arrives or by assigning the absent outcome to a cause outside the procedure. The statement is about assays a single practitioner performs on his own materials or his own work. It does not govern a contest in which two practitioners measure their capacities against one another, nor a divination whose outcome is the answer sought rather than a verdict on the procedure; both of those are held elsewhere in this holding and both do state criteria on two sides."@en .

pmc:TheTrialWithNoFailingGrade a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Probe ohne Durchfallen"@de,
        "The trial with no failing grade"@en ;
    skos:note "One asymmetry between the two witnesses should not be lost in the generalization. The Shangqing trials are set by persons, the Perfected of the Great Ultimate coming in person to look on, and are therefore in principle reportable by those persons; the neidan difficulties are circumstances of a life and the demons are appearances, so nobody sets them and nobody can report on them. The second is the harder case for anyone wanting to check the grading, and it is the one that comes with an exact catalogue."@en,
        "The Shangqing material has twelve trials, set at exactly what the candidate's mind does not grasp and his feelings do not reach, with failure to recognise the tester counting as failure; and it reports a man who passed nine and failed three, obtained the elixir and rose, and was made a transcendent only and not a Perfected. The internal alchemy has nine difficulties and ten demons, the demons to be met without acknowledgement and the difficulties covering the whole of a person's circumstances; and it states that one who cannot wholly clear the nine, or is attached to one or two of the ten, is not thereby without the Way but obtains the middling or the lesser completion and becomes a human immortal or an earth immortal."@en,
        "The weakness is the pairing and it is the same weakness as at pmc:CountedThresholdNeverFallsDue: two Chinese transmissions, one of which names the other's stream as its own antecedent. What the two establish is a property of that stream. The obvious place to widen it is the graded initiations this holding already carries — the Mithraic grades, the Midewiwin degrees, the Ars notoria's conditions on the man impeded by crime — and none of those was examined for this question, because none of them was open."@en,
        "What would refute it: a transmission with a set of trials that states a plain exclusion for partial failure — the candidate obtains nothing, is not of the company, is sent away. The counter-search over the holding found one thing close to that and it is not within scope: the Mithraic material, in a hostile report, has an ordeal in which the candidate who survives receives the highest initiation and the one who succumbs receives a burial. That is pass or fail with no middle, and it would be a counter-case if it were a set of trials; it is a single ordeal, and the statement above is about sets."@en,
        "Why the grading matters more than the trials. A pass-or-fail ordeal produces, over a population, a set of people who failed and who could in principle be counted; a graded one produces a set of people who each attained something, and the transmission's own records then contain no failures at all. Both nodes are held in this graph beside narratives in which named men are said to have fallen short and to be still walking in the mountains — which is what the lesser grade looks like from inside."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:NeidanChengdi,
        pmc:ShangqingShieryanshi ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission makes a set of trials or obstacles the condition of its highest attainment, it also states an outcome for the candidate who clears only part of the set, and that outcome is a lesser rank within the same system rather than exclusion from it. The sets are so constructed that a candidate cannot establish in advance which trials he faces or how many he has cleared, so the grading is assigned after the fact and from the outcome."@en .

pmc:ThelemaClarividentiaPhysica a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Physische Hellsicht, wie diese Überlieferung sie bestimmt"@de,
        "Physical clairvoyance, as this transmission defines it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber E vel Exercitiorum sub figura IX, chap. II, in The Equinox I,1 (London, March 1909), pp. 26-27" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity the card test is held to measure: naming a drawn card whose face has not been seen, at a rate above the chance rate the same instruction states. The instruction's own heading for the chapter is physical clairvoyance."@en ;
    skos:note "Defined by its measurement and by nothing else, which is why it is the one capacity in this file that a count could settle. No mechanism is offered and none is needed for the claim as stated."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberEVelExercitiorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaCorpusLevatum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Körper steigt in die Luft"@de,
        "The body rises into the air"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber RV vel Spiritus sub figura CCVI, § 5 (d), in The Equinox I,7 (London, March 1912), p. 63" ;
    skos:definition "The fourth of four stated stages of the breath practice: as a development of the state in which the body hops gently from place to place, the body rises into the air and remains there for an appreciably long period, from a second to an hour or more."@en ;
    skos:note "The strongest physical claim in the corpus and the one stated with the widest bound. It is put as a statement to be investigated, and the conditions under which the investigation is to run include an assistant present throughout, so the claim is in principle witnessed. No case is reported anywhere in what was read."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberRVVelSpiritus ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaCorpusSaltans a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Körper hüpft von Ort zu Ort ohne den Willen"@de,
        "The body hops from place to place without the will"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber RV vel Spiritus sub figura CCVI, § 5 (c), in The Equinox I,7 (London, March 1912), p. 62" ;
    skos:definition "The third stated stage: the state of automatic rigidity develops into a state characterised by violent spasmodic movements of which the practitioner is unconscious but of whose result he is aware, the result being that the body hops gently from place to place. After the first two or three occurrences the posture is not lost, and the body is said to appear, on another theory, to have lost its weight almost completely and to be moved by an unknown force."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered separately from the rising because the instruction numbers them separately and because they differ in what would count as a witness: a hop is observable from outside, a loss of weight is a theory about it. The instruction offers the second as an alternative description and not as an observation."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberRVVelSpiritus ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaFidelitasEtVigilantia a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Treue und Wachsamkeit, und ein veröffentlichtes Scheitern"@de,
        "Fidelity and vigilance, and a published failure"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber III vel Jugorum, plate ARATUM SECURUM with its caption, in The Equinox I,4 (London, September 1910), p. 10" ;
    skos:definition "The two criteria under which a performance of the triple yoke is judged, given in the caption to the plate that accompanies the instruction: a named practitioner after one week of avoiding the first person, of whom the caption says that his fidelity is good, his vigilance bad, and that he is not nearly good enough to pass."@en ;
    skos:note "The single most unusual thing the run found in the whole corpus, and the reason this entry was worth making. A transmission that publishes a photograph of a practitioner's forearm together with a verdict of failure on him has exposed its own standard to inspection: the number of cuts is visible, the criteria are named, and the judgment is recorded against a person rather than described in general. Nothing else in this holding does that."@en,
        "The two criteria are not defined further, and the distinction between them has to be read off the case: fidelity is keeping the oath, vigilance is noticing when one has broken it. A practitioner can score well on the first and badly on the second only if the cuts record breaches he did not catch at the time, which is what the caption is reporting."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberIIIVelJugorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaIudiciumApudStudentem a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Urteil über den Erfolg liegt bei dem, über den geurteilt wird"@de,
        "The judgment of success is left to the one being judged"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber O vel Manus et Sagittae sub figura VI, chap. IV, § 5, in The Equinox I,2 (London, September 1909), pp. 26-27" ;
    skos:definition "The instruction's disposal of the question for the verbal invocations: success in these is so subtle a matter, and its grades so delicately shaded, that it must be left to the good sense of the student to decide whether or not he should be satisfied with his result."@en ;
    skos:note "The one passage that closes the loop. Where a sign is private, an outsider cannot check it; where the verdict is the practitioner's own, there is no verdict to check. The corpus that carries this sentence also publishes a printed verdict of failure on a named practitioner, so the transmission held both practices at once and did not reconcile them."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberOVelManusEtSagittae ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaNemoDeficit a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kein Übender scheitert; nur seine Deutung des Ergebnisses kann fehlgehen"@de,
        "No practitioner fails; only his reading of the result can go wrong"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber O vel Manus et Sagittae sub figura VI, chap. I, § 5, in The Equinox I,2 (London, September 1909), p. 14; the clause on individual capacity at Liber E vel Exercitiorum sub figura IX, chap. VI, § 9, in The Equinox I,1 (London, March 1909), p. 32" ;
    skos:definition "The clause that removes the failure condition from the procedure: there is little danger that any student, however idle or stupid, will fail to get some result, but there is great danger that he will be led astray, obsessed and overwhelmed by his results, even though it be by those which it is necessary that he should attain; and too often he mistaketh the first resting-place for the goal."@en ;
    skos:note "A second clause of the same instruction pulls in the same direction by attaching the standard to the person: it is desirable that the student should never attach to any result the importance which it at first seems to possess. And the earlier instruction ties the demand itself to individual capacity — according to your powers will it be demanded of you — so that there is no level at which two practitioners are held to the same figure."@en,
        "The finding this entry exists for, stated in the transmission's own words. If no practitioner can fail to get a result, then no result counts against the procedure, and every miscarriage is located in the practitioner. This holding has the same shape from other transmissions and holds it as a class; here it is put as plainly as anywhere."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberOVelManusEtSagittae ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaNullumProbandumCertum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Es gibt keine einfache und sichere Erfolgsprobe"@de,
        "No simple and certain test of success exists"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber O vel Manus et Sagittae sub figura VI, chap. III, § 2, in The Equinox I,2 (London, September 1909), p. 16" ;
    skos:definition "The sentence the ritual instruction sets immediately after the practice of assuming a god-form: it is a matter for very great regret that no simple and certain tests of success in this practice exist."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a node because it is a statement by the transmission about its own examinability and because it stands three sections from the demand for a record as the only possible check upon error. Both are in the same corpus, both are first-hand, and neither is a later retraction of the other. A reader who has only one of them has the wrong picture of this transmission."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberOVelManusEtSagittae ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaPranayamaGradusInterdicti a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Jede Atemstufe ist gesperrt, bis die vorige leicht ist"@de,
        "Each breath stage is barred until the last is easy"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber E vel Exercitiorum sub figura IX, chap. IV, §§ 1-5, in The Equinox I,1 (London, March 1909), p. 29" ;
    skos:definition "The gating clause of the breath instruction, repeated at each increase: when this is quite easy to you, increase the periods; when this is quite easy to you, but not before, change the pattern; when you can do this with perfect ease and comfort for a whole hour, go on."@en ;
    skos:note "Prescribed order and not a necessity: the instruction gives no consequence of going faster, and the caution against overstrain that stands four sections later is a caution and not the stated outcome of skipping a stage. The two are entered separately for that reason."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ThelemaPranayamaNumerata ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberEVelExercitiorum ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ThelemaQuattuorSedes ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaPranayamaYieldsCorpusLevatum a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der verlängerte Atemzyklus soll den Körper vom Boden heben"@de,
        "The lengthened breath cycle is said to bring the body off the ground"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber RV vel Spiritus sub figura CCVI, § 5 (a)-(d), in The Equinox I,7 (London, March 1912), pp. 62-63" ;
    skos:definition "The claim as the instruction makes it: the practitioner accomplished in the postures and in the breath is to master a cycle of ten, twenty, forty or sixteen, thirty-two, sixty-four and then to increase the period further, and the four stages follow in order — a sweat of a different character from that of exertion, then the ceasing of the sweat and automatic rigidity, then unconscious spasmodic movement with the body hopping, then the body rising and remaining in the air."@en ;
    skos:note "The four stages are given as an ordered series, so the claim is not only that the last occurs but that it occurs after the other three. That makes it unusually exposed: a practitioner who reaches the second and not the third has a result the instruction did not predict, and the instruction does not say what to conclude from it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ThelemaPranayamaNumerata ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberRVVelSpiritus ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ThelemaCorpusLevatum,
        pmc:ThelemaCorpusSaltans .

pmc:ThelemaProbatioTarotYieldsClarividentia a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Kartenpraxis soll das Vermögen ausbilden, das sie misst"@de,
        "The card practice is said to develop the faculty it measures"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber E vel Exercitiorum sub figura IX, chap. II, §§ 8-9, in The Equinox I,1 (London, March 1909), p. 27" ;
    skos:definition "The instruction is written as a progression and not only as a test: as you progress, you will find that you are able to distinguish the suit correctly three times in four, and in seventy-eight experiments to name the card aright as many as fifteen or twenty times. The practice is thus held to bring about the capacity, and at the threshold the practitioner is admitted for examination and given more complex exercises."@en ;
    skos:note "The double role is worth holding as such. A procedure that is at once the training and the measurement cannot separate an improvement in the faculty from an improvement in the procedure, and the instruction does not distinguish them. That is a property of the claim and not a defect of the reading."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ThelemaProbatioTarot ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberEVelExercitiorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ThelemaClarividentiaPhysica .

pmc:ThelemaRatioCasus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die genannte Zufallsrate und die Schwelle darüber"@de,
        "The stated chance rate, and the threshold above it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber E vel Exercitiorum sub figura IX, chap. II, §§ 3, 5 and 8-9, in The Equinox I,1 (London, March 1909), pp. 26-27" ;
    skos:definition "The instruction states the rate to be expected without any clairvoyance at all: the right card once in seventy-eight times; and, since seventy-eight cards yield twenty-two trumps and fourteen of each other suit, a correct suit roughly twice in seven times by calling trumps every time. Against that it sets the threshold at which the practitioner may be admitted for examination: the suit correctly three times in four, very few inharmonious errors, and the card named aright as many as fifteen or twenty times in seventy-eight."@en ;
    skos:note "The second of the two chance figures is also a stated strategy for beating a naive score without any faculty at all, which means the transmission had thought about the base rate rather than merely quoting one."@en,
        "This is the only passage the run found anywhere in the corpus that puts a number on chance and a second number on success. Both are stated by the transmission and neither is the compiler's. It is what the testability-driven entrance of the specification is looking for, and it is here rather than in a tradition with a longer pedigree."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberEVelExercitiorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaSanctioNonNotitiae a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Was nicht protokolliert ist, wird nicht zur Kenntnis genommen"@de,
        "What is not recorded is not noticed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber E vel Exercitiorum sub figura IX, chap. I, § 4, in The Equinox I,1 (London, March 1909), p. 25" ;
    skos:definition "The sanction attached to the record: the order states that it will take no official notice of any experiments which are not properly recorded in the manner the three preceding sections set out."@en ;
    skos:note "The rule is entered separately from the practice because it is the part that can be complied with or broken and because it is the transmission's own answer to the question this holding asks of every claim. It makes the record the condition of admissibility rather than a matter of diligence, which is the same move a protocol makes here."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberEVelExercitiorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaSignumFlammae a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die gezogene Figur erscheint in Flamme"@de,
        "The drawn figure appears in flame"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber O vel Manus et Sagittae sub figura VI, chap. IV, § 2, in The Equinox I,2 (London, September 1909), p. 26" ;
    skos:definition "The stated sign for the ritual figures: they should be practised until the figures drawn appear in flame, in flame so near to physical flame that it would perhaps be visible to the eyes of a bystander, were one present."@en ;
    skos:note "The sign is stated in the conditional and the witness is absent by construction: the clause were one present is in the text. A sign formulated this way cannot be missed, because nothing was promised to anyone who could check it."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberOVelManusEtSagittae ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaSignumVibrationis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Zeichen der Vibration, eines leiblich und eines nur vom Übenden gehört"@de,
        "The signs of the vibration, one bodily and one heard only by the practitioner"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber O vel Manus et Sagittae sub figura VI, chap. III, §§ 5-6, in The Equinox I,2 (London, September 1909), p. 17" ;
    skos:definition "Two signs given for the same practice. The first: it is a sign that the student is performing it correctly when a single vibration entirely exhausts his physical strength, causes him to grow hot all over or to perspire violently, and so weakens him that he finds it difficult to remain standing. The second: it is a sign of success, though only by the student himself is it perceived, when he hears the name of the god vehemently roared forth as if by the concourse of ten thousand thunders, and it appears to him as if that great voice proceeded from the universe and not from himself."@en ;
    skos:note "The transmission marks the difference between the two itself, in the clause though only by the student himself is it perceived. That clause is the reason this holding can keep the two kinds of sign apart in this corpus without the compiler drawing the line: here the source drew it."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberOVelManusEtSagittae ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaVisioMundiInMente a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Sonnensystem in einem Bild und in wahrer Größenordnung halten"@de,
        "Holding the solar system in one mental image at its true relative size"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber Batrachophrenoboocosmomachia sub figura DXXXVI, §§ 2-3, practices B and C, in The Equinox I,10 (London, September 1913), pp. 38-40" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity the astronomical practice aims at: a mental picture of the earth, then of the earth and moon with their relative sizes and distance, then of the planets one by one, then of the whole system with each body in its proper motion, in which the apparent size approximates to the real. The instruction states that unless the apparent size approximates to the real, the practice is wasted."@en ;
    skos:note "A stated waste condition, which is as close as this practice comes to a failure condition, and a stated obstacle with it: the final trick of the mind is said to be a constant disappearance of the image and its reappearance on a smaller scale."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberBatrachophrenoboocosmomachia ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:Theologike a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Theologikē"@de,
        "Knowledge of God"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 1" ;
    skos:definition "The third of the three parts, the knowledge of God."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:DesertMonasticism .

pmc:TheosophyDilatioIncarnationum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Erreichung auf eine spaetere Inkarnation und eine spaetere Runde verschoben"@de,
        "The attainment deferred to a later incarnation and a later round"@en ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter The Serpent-Fire, p. 303 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that the development of the higher force-centres will come to all in due course, but that most people cannot gain it during the present incarnation if it is the first in which they have begun to take these matters seriously in hand; that some might succeed, whose bodies are by heredity more adaptable than most others; and that it is really for the majority the work of a later round altogether. The conquest of the serpent-fire has besides to be repeated in each incarnation, since the vehicles are new each time, though after it has once been thoroughly achieved the repetitions are said to be an easy matter."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered for the same reason as the node above it. The term on which the capacities of this file are promised is placed beyond any life in which a witness could check it, and the text says so plainly rather than leaving it to be inferred. Together with the seven layers this is the second of two devices in the same two chapters by which no attempt and no lifetime can count as a failure, and the holding keeps that shape at generalizations/the-count-that-does-not-fall-due.ttl and generalizations/counted-thresholds.ttl. Not added to either in this run and for the same reason."@en,
        "The hereditary clause is recorded as stated and not reworded. The source says that some Indians might succeed in doing so, as their bodies are by heredity more adaptable than most others. It is an assertion about bodies and descent made by a modern writer and it belongs at this node because it is part of the same sentence that defers the attainment."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy .

pmc:TheosophyIgnisStratificatus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Feuer in sieben Schichten, sodass ein Fehlversuch nur eine erreichte Schicht bedeutet"@de,
        "The fire in seven layers, so that a failed attempt means only one layer reached"@en ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter Force-Centres, p. 297 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that the fire exists in seven layers or seven degrees of force, and that it often happens that a man who exerts his will in the effort to arouse it may succeed in affecting one layer only, so that when he thinks he has done the work he may find it ineffective and may have to do it all over again many times, digging gradually deeper and deeper until not only the surface is stirred but the very heart of the fire is in full activity."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because of what it does and not because of what it describes. It is the explanation the transmission supplies in advance for an attempt that produced nothing, and it is stated in the source as a general case rather than as a diagnosis of a particular failure. Under it no number of attempts without result can count against the practice, since each is accounted for as a layer reached, and the text says so in the very words a practitioner would use to report a failure, that he thinks he has done the work and finds it ineffective."@en,
        "The same page supplies a second account of a result that arrives without the practice: especially strong vibrations may at any time galvanize one or other of the centres into temporary activity without arousing the serpent-fire at all, or the fire may be partially roused, and in that way partial clairvoyance may be produced for the time. So the source covers both a nothing and a something without the procedure having worked."@en,
        "This is the shape the holding keeps at generalizations/graded-failure.ttl and generalizations/no-failed-run-counts.ttl. Not added to either in this run, because SPEC 16 requires the counter-search against the holding before a generalization is enlarged and that search was not carried here. A later run has a clean candidate and this node is the witness."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy .

pmc:TheosophyInfluentiaeAstrales a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Astrale Einfluesse spueren, ohne zu wissen warum"@de,
        "Feeling astral influences without knowing why"@en ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter Force-Centres, p. 295 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity attributed to the second centre. When the centre at the navel comes into activity the man begins in the physical body to be conscious of all kinds of astral influences, vaguely feeling that some of them are friendly and others hostile, or that some places are pleasant and others unpleasant, without in the least knowing why."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy .

pmc:TheosophyMemoriaItinerum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sich der astralen Reisen erinnern"@de,
        "Remembering the astral journeys"@en ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter Force-Centres, p. 296 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity attributed to the third centre. When the centre at the spleen is awakened the man is enabled to remember his vague astral journeys, though sometimes only very partially; and the effect of a slight and accidental stimulation of this centre is often to produce a half-remembrance of a blissful sensation of flying through the air."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy .

pmc:TheosophyNullaExceptio a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der vorweg erhobene Einwand, und die Antwort gegen den Uebenden"@de,
        "The objection raised in advance, and answered against the practitioner"@en ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter The Serpent-Fire, pp. 301-302 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" ;
    skos:definition "The passage in which the transmission raises the practitioner's own expectation and disposes of it. The author says that in matters such as these students so often seem to think that some special exception to the laws of nature will be made in their case, that some special intervention of providence will save them from the consequences of their folly; that assuredly nothing of that sort will happen, and that the man who wantonly provokes an explosion is quite likely to become its first victim; that it would save much trouble and disappointment if students could be induced to understand that in all matters connected with occultism we mean just exactly and literally what we say, and that it is applicable in every case without exception, for there is no such thing as favouritism in the working of the great laws of the universe."@en ;
    skos:note "Kept as its own node because it is the place where the failure is assigned. The transmission anticipates the report of a bad outcome and locates its cause in the person before any case arises: everybody wants to try all possible experiments, everybody is convinced that he is quite ready for the highest possible teaching, and no one is willing to work patiently at the improvement of character. The holding keeps this shape at generalizations/displaced-penalties.ttl and generalizations/the-observation-answered-by-narrowing.ttl; not added to either in this run and for the reason given at the two nodes above."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy .

pmc:TheosophyPetalaNonMoralia a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Gegenfall, den der Autor gegen die sittliche Lesart seiner eigenen Ueberlieferung berichtet"@de,
        "The counter-instance the author reports against his own tradition's moral reading"@en ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter Force-Centres, pp. 291-292 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" ;
    skos:definition "The author reports having heard it suggested that each of the different petals of the force-centres represents a moral quality and that the development of that quality brings the centre into activity. He says he has not yet met with any facts which confirm this, nor is he able to see exactly how it can be, because the appearance is produced by definite and easily recognizable forces and the petals in a centre are either active or not active according as those forces have or have not been aroused; that their development seems to him to have no more connection with morality than has the development of the biceps; and that he has certainly met with persons in whom some of the centres were in full activity though the moral development was by no means excellent."@en ;
    skos:note "Not entered as a pm:Disputing node. The suggestion the author rejects is unattributed in the source, so only one side has a place and a work, and SPEC requires at least two sources for a dispute. A later run that finds the suggestion in a named theosophical work has the second side and can convert this node."@en,
        "The rarest kind of place in this holding and the reason the chapter was read to its end. A writer of a transmission that states a prerequisite of moral development for the same practice reports, in the same chapter, cases in which the practice had worked and the moral condition was absent, and declines a reading that would have made the two inseparable. The two statements are not formally in conflict, because the prerequisite is about safety and this is about the mechanism of the appearance; but they pull in opposite directions, and the holding has few sources that record their own counter-instances at all."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy .

pmc:TheosophySensusAlienorum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Freuden, Leiden und Schmerzen anderer spueren"@de,
        "Feeling the joys, sorrows and pains of others"@en ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter Force-Centres, p. 296 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity attributed to the fourth centre. Stimulation of the centre at the heart makes the man instinctively aware of the joys and sorrows of others, and sometimes even causes him to reproduce in himself by sympathy their physical aches and pains."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy .

pmc:TheravadaNakkhatta a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Tor mag auf günstige Tage achten, und das Glück wird ihm immer entgehen"@de,
        "The fool may watch for lucky days, and luck shall always miss"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Jātaka 49, Nakkhatta-Jātaka, Fausbøll i.257-259, in the English of Robert Chalmers, ed. E. B. Cowell, The Jātaka or Stories of the Buddha's Former Births, vol. I, Cambridge University Press 1895, pp. 125-126" ;
    skos:definition "The forty-ninth story, told twice, once of the present and once of the past, and both times as a run with an outcome. A family has fixed a wedding day and afterwards asks its household ascetic whether the stars are favourable for it. The ascetic, piqued at having been consulted second, answers that they are not and that great misfortune would follow; the family stays at home; the other side, having prepared everything, marries the girl to somebody else; and the party that obeyed the constellation goes home with nothing. A wise man passing through puts the argument in one stanza: the fool may watch for lucky days, yet luck shall always miss; it is luck itself that is luck's own star, and what can mere stars achieve."@en ;
    skos:note "The motive matters and is stated in the text twice. The ascetic knows the stars are indifferent and says otherwise because he was asked second. A transmission that explains a false reading of the calendar by the reader's pique has located the failure in a party — which is exactly the location the holding's generalization about calendar-bound operations says these transmissions do not use. It does not refute that statement, because this is not a transmission that binds an operation to a calendar; it is one that reports somebody else's binding. The difference is recorded here so that a later run does not count it on the wrong side."@en,
        "What makes this worth a node rather than a proverb is that the refusal is carried by a case and not by a doctrine. The transmission does not argue that constellations have no power; it reports an occasion on which the rule was followed exactly and the follower lost the thing he wanted, and it names the mechanism of the loss — an offended interpreter with a motive. The same corpus that supplies this puts nakṣatra prognostication among the low arts elsewhere, which is the doctrinal form; this is the other form, and it is the one a protocol can use."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Jataka ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:TheravadaNakkhattaLowArt a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Wählen der Tage unter den niederen Künsten, und der Grund ist der Lebensunterhalt"@de,
        "The choosing of days among the low arts, and the ground is livelihood"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Dīgha Nikāya 1, Brahmajāla Sutta, the Mahāsīla, paragraphs 21 to 27, DN i.9-12, in the English of T. W. Rhys Davids, Dialogues of the Buddha, Part I, Sacred Books of the Buddhists vol. II, London 1899, pp. 16-26; the days at paragraph 26, pp. 22-23, the constellations at paragraph 24, pp. 20-21" ;
    skos:definition "The Mahāsīla of the first discourse runs seven paragraphs of the same shape: whereas some recluses and brahmans, while living on food provided by the faithful, earn their living by wrong means of livelihood, by low arts, such as these — a list — Gotama the recluse holds aloof from such low arts. Two of the seven lists carry the choosing of days. The twenty-sixth paragraph opens with arranging a lucky day for marriages in which the bride or bridegroom is brought home, arranging a lucky day for marriages in which the bride or bridegroom is sent forth, and fixing a lucky time for the conclusion of treaties of peace, for the outbreak of hostilities, for the calling in of debts and for the expenditure of money. The twenty-fourth lists the foretelling of eclipses of the moon, of the sun and of a star, of aberrations of the sun, the moon and the stars, and of their return to their usual paths."@en ;
    skos:note "Read for the falsifier of pmc:TheRefusalAndWhatItRestsOn, which named this passage as one of three places where that statement is likeliest to break, and it does deviate — but not in the way the falsifier predicted. The falsifier expected a refusal from outside grounded in doctrine alone. What stands here is grounded in neither doctrine nor case: the recurring clause is wrong means of livelihood, and the list is a list of things a recluse holds aloof from. The discourse does not say that the days do not fall out as the almanac has them. It says that one who lives on food provided by the faithful does not earn by this."@en,
        "That is a third kind of refusal and the holding did not have it named. A refusal from outside grounded in a case argues that the art fails; a refusal from inside grounded in a state of the practitioner grants an exemption from an art that works; this one withdraws from the art without judging it, on a ground that belongs entirely to the refuser's own vocation. It is abstention and not refutation, and a protocol can take nothing from it, because nothing here is a claim about the world."@en,
        "Two further things the passage settles for anyone who counts it. It is a list and not an argument: the seven paragraphs enumerate some two hundred items, from palmistry and snake charming to bringing forth flames from one's mouth, and the choosing of days has no special standing among them. And the enumeration is of what others do for money, so even the reading that has the discourse rejecting the art reads it as rejecting a trade."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DighaNikaya ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:TiantaiBingJiuNanYu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sogleich behandelt ist die Krankheit leicht zu heilen, lange gelassen schwer"@de,
        "Treated at once the illness is easily cured, left long it is hard to heal"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0471c, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text",
        "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0472a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:definition "The term the work sets on its own remedy: illnesses of this kind, treated as soon as they are got, are very easily cured; if a long time passes the illness is formed, the body is weak and the illness knotted, and it is hard to heal by treatment. Three causes of illness are distinguished before the term is stated and the remedy differs by cause: the increase and decrease of the four elements and the five organs, illness made by ghosts and spirits, which calls for a strong mind with spells to assist, and illness as the recompense of deeds, which requires the cultivation of merit and repentance."@en ;
    skos:note "A term with a direction, which is rarer in this holding than a term with a date. It does not say when the remedy stops working, only that the chance of it falls as the illness ages, and that is enough to make a late failure expected rather than a counter-instance. The three causes do a second thing of the same kind: two of them take the case out of the reach of the breath and visualization altogether."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmc:TiantaiFeiMoSuoWei a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Schaden wird durch die eigene Unwissenheit des Übenden erlitten und ist nicht das Werk des Dämons"@de,
        "The harm is suffered through the practitioner's own want of wisdom and is not the demon's doing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0471a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:definition "The work's own correction of the chapter it has just written. If the demonic realms do not withdraw, no grief is to arise, and if they are seen to withdraw, no joy either. For what reason: it has never yet been seen that a man in sitting saw a demon turn into a tiger or a wolf and come to eat him, nor has it ever been seen that a demon turned into a man or a woman and became his wife or husband. When they work their illusions the foolish man does not understand, his mind gives rise to fright or to clinging, and through this his mind is disordered, he loses the absorption and becomes deranged and brings the trouble on himself. All of this is the practitioner suffering through want of wisdom, and it is not what the demon did."@en ;
    skos:note "A transmission that has just given a bestiary of interferences by the hour of the day then says that none of them has ever been seen to do anything, and lays the whole of the damage on the practitioner. It is the most complete displacement of a failure in this holding so far, and it is more complete than the ones already entered because it does not merely move the blame but denies that the other party acts at all. The holding's statement of the shape is at generalizations/the-excuse-that-stops-at-the-officiant.ttl and generalizations/displaced-penalties.ttl; neither is enlarged by this run, for the reason given at pmc:TiantaiZheZhang."@en,
        "The same passage is also the strongest negative report in the work, and it is a negative report about a class of events, which SPEC §13 counts the expensive kind: it has never been seen, said of a tradition's own demonology by the man who wrote its manual. What it rests on is not stated, and the work does not say who looked."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmc:TiantaiJiKuanBing a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Krankheit des straffen und die des lockeren Geistes, je an ihrem Körperzeichen"@de,
        "The disease of the tight mind and the disease of the loose mind, each by its bodily sign"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0466a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:definition "Two faults of regulating the mind, each named with the sign by which it is known and the correction. The mark of the disease of a tight mind: from gathering the mind and applying recollection in the sitting, and entering the absorption by it, there is therefore an urgent pain upward in the chest and breast; the mind is then to be loosened and the air thought to flow downward, and the trouble heals of itself. The mark of the disease of a loose mind: the mind feels slack and scattered, the body inclines to sag, or saliva runs from the mouth, or at times there is darkness; the body is then to be drawn in and the recollection made urgent so that the mind abides in its object. Beside them stand the mark of sinking, a dark mind with the head hanging low, and the mark of floating, a drifting mind with the body not at rest."@en ;
    skos:note "Four misadjustments, each with an outward sign, and two of the four signs are visible to a bystander: the head hanging low and the saliva at the mouth. In a holding whose usual trouble is that only the operator can say what happened, a transmission that names a sign a third person can see is the thing to keep, and this work names two without being asked for any."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmc:TiantaiJinshiBuchengjiu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Menschen der Gegenwart bringen diese Vorstellungsübungen meist nicht zustande"@de,
        "People of the present time mostly do not accomplish these visualizations"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0472a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:definition "The withdrawal the work makes immediately after reporting the twelve breaths, the six sounded breaths and the borrowed visualizations: but the capacities of the men of the present time are shallow and dull, and making these visualizations they mostly do not accomplish them, and it is not transmitted in the world. The sentence goes on to forbid the obvious substitute: nor is one to go on from this to learn breath arts and the giving up of grain, for fear of giving rise to deviant views. Mineral and herbal medicines, where they answer to the illness, may be taken."@en ;
    skos:note "The reason the twelve prescriptions are entered as one claim and not as twelve, and the reason that decision is defensible. The work reports them, declines to own them, says they mostly fail now, and says that the failure is in the men and not in the method. That last move is the same one as at pmc:TiantaiFeiMoSuoWei, applied to a technique instead of to a demon, and it is what keeps a set of prescriptions in a manual after the manual has stopped expecting them to work."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmc:TiantaiKongWuSuoHuo a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Auch wenn man gar nichts erlangt, kehrt man nicht um"@de,
        "Even if one gains nothing at all, one never turns back"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0466c, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:definition "The fifth of the five expedients, single-mindedness, defined by what no outcome may change. Seeing clearly that the world is to be feared and hated, and knowing well that the merit of the absorption and the wisdom is to be honoured and held dear, one is then to cultivate calming and contemplation single-mindedly and with decision, with a mind like adamant, so that the demons of heaven and the outer ways cannot ruin it; and supposing it be empty and one gains nothing at all, one will in the end not turn back and change. That is called single-mindedness."@en ;
    skos:note "Found on the second pass. It is the strongest of the devices in the work, and unlike the clause at pmc:TiantaiZheZhang it protects no particular promise: it is written into the definition of a required disposition that the absence of any result whatever is not to change the practice. A practitioner who has it cannot stop for a negative outcome, because stopping for one would mean he never had the fifth expedient and so was never doing the thing properly."@en,
        "What keeps it from being merely an exhortation is where it stands. The five expedients are the fifth chapter of ten and are required before the main practice begins; so the work states the condition that no outcome counts before it has stated a single outcome. Beside it, the sentence two chapters later that there is nothing the gathered mind does not accomplish, at pmc:TiantaiZhixinYichu, reads differently: the guarantee is made to a practitioner who has already agreed that its failure would prove nothing."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmc:TiantaiPianxiuXieDao a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Nur eines der beiden zu üben fällt in das Falsche und Verkehrte"@de,
        "Practising one of the two alone falls into the false and the upside down"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0462b, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:definition "The condition the work sets on its own pairing, at the head of the whole treatise: these two methods are like the two wheels of a cart and the two wings of a bird, and if they are practised one-sidedly one falls into the false and the upside down. The scripture is quoted for the two outcomes: he who cultivates the absorption and its merit one-sidedly and does not learn wisdom is called stupid, and he who learns wisdom one-sidedly and does not cultivate the absorption and its merit is called mad. The faults of madness and stupidity differ a little, but in the turning round of false views there is no difference at all."@en ;
    skos:note "A condition of failure on the method as a whole, and the one place in the work where the two halves of the method are said to be dangerous apart. It is also the first of five places where this work puts madness at the end of a wrong performance of what it teaches. The other four are the forcing of the abiding mind at pmc:TiantaiYizhiFakuang, the clinging to a counterfeit absorption at pmc:TiantaiXieweiChan, the fright or the clinging at a demonic show at pmc:TiantaiFeiMoSuoWei, and the beginner without a teacher at pmc:TiantaiPresupposesShanzhishi, where the derangement ends in death. Beside them stands a sixth use that is not of this kind: the five hundred immortals of the Snow Mountains who heard the kiṃnara woman sing, lost their absorption and were drunk and mad, which is an exemplum from outside the practice and is not entered."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmc:TiantaiPresupposesChuding a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Verlassen der Sitzung setzt das Austrittsverfahren voraus, bei Strafe von Kopfschmerz und erstarrten Gelenken"@de,
        "Leaving the sitting presupposes the exit procedure, on pain of headache and stiffened joints"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0466b, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:note "The clearest falsifiable clause in the work and the one a reader could act on without believing anything else in it. The procedure is short, the omission is a single act, the consequence is named, bodily, and arrives in the same session or the next, and the work states it of every sitting and not of a special case. Nothing in this holding has stood this close to a statement that could be checked by doing the opposite twice."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "If he does not do so, then although he may have gained the abiding mind in the sitting, his coming out being abrupt and hurried, the fine state has not dispersed and remains in the body, and it makes the man headache and the hundred joints of the bones stiff, as it were a wind exhaustion; and in the sittings after it he is vexed and not at rest. Therefore when the mind means to come out of the absorption, attention is needed every time."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:TiantaiZhiguan ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:TiantaiChuding ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmc:TiantaiPresupposesShanzhishi a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Anfänger setzt einen Lehrer in der Nähe voraus, bei Strafe von Verwirrung und Tod"@de,
        "The beginner presupposes a teacher at hand, on pain of derangement and death"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0471a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:note "The fifth of the five outward conditions is the only one whose want the work costs at the practitioner's life, and the sentence is in the chapter on demonic affairs and not in the chapter of the conditions. The second half is the more interesting one: the work describes a practitioner who acquires persuasive powers and a following, and enters that among the outcomes to be feared rather than among the capacities to be sought."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Therefore the practitioner of a beginning mind must keep near a good friend, because there are difficulties of this kind: this is the demon entering a man's mind, and it can make the practitioner's mind and spirit deranged, now glad and now grieving, and through this he falls ill and comes to death. Or it may make him gain false absorptions, false knowledge, powers and spells, so that he preaches and teaches and all believe and submit to him, and afterwards he ruins the good works of other men and destroys the true teaching."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:TiantaiZhiguan ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:TiantaiWuyuan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmc:TiantaiPresupposesTiaoshi a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Vertiefung setzt das Maß der Nahrung voraus, bei Strafe eines benannten Körperzustands"@de,
        "The absorption presupposes the measure of food, on pain of a named bodily state"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0465b, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:note "What makes this clause worth a node is that the consequence is in the body and not in the result. A reader who wants to know whether the practitioner had the condition can ask about the swollen belly and the urgent breath, and the answer does not depend on whether the absorption came."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "These two are both not the way to gain the absorption: if the food is too full, the breath is urgent and the body swollen, the hundred vessels do not pass, the mind is shut and the thought in sitting is not at rest; if the food is too little, the body is weak, the mind hangs loose and the intention is not firm. Food that does not suit stirs an old illness and sets the four elements against one another."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:TiantaiZhiguan ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:TiantaiTiaoshi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmc:TiantaiPresupposesYishi a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Praxis setzt hinreichende Kleidung und Nahrung und die Freiheit von Geschäften voraus"@de,
        "The practice presupposes sufficient clothing and food and freedom from occupations"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0463b, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:note "Stated necessity and not prescribed order, because the work asks its own question after each of the two conditions and answers it with the consequence of the want. The two are entered under one node because the consequence is the same sentence in different words and the prior step is the same list."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Of the second condition: for what reason? Without these conditions the mind is not at rest, and there is obstruction to the way. Of the fourth: for what reason? If the occupations are many, then the business of practising the way is abandoned, the mind is disordered and hard to gather in. And of too much possession under the second: if one is greedy for more and piles it up, then the mind is disordered and the way is hindered."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:TiantaiZhiguan ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:TiantaiWuyuan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmc:TiantaiPresupposesZhengshen a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Atemregelung setzt den gerade gerichteten Körper voraus, bei Strafe grober Atmung und zerstreuten Geistes"@de,
        "The regulation of the breath presupposes the straightened body, on pain of a coarse breath and a scattered mind"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0465c, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text",
        "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0466b, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:note "Stated necessity and not prescribed order, and the distinction matters for the question that is answered here. The work does not merely put the body first in a list: it gives the mechanism by which an unregulated body spoils the breath and an unregulated breath the mind, and it states the general ground of the sequence. A reader who wanted to dispute the order would have to dispute the mechanism, which is what a prescribed order does not offer."@en,
        "This carries the claim in prima-materia#29, which asked whether the upright seat is set as a condition of the breath work and where the sources state it. The answer is from outside the sphere the issue expected: it went to the haṭhayogic and the yogasūtra material, and the clause with the mechanism is in a Chinese Buddhist manual of the sixth century. That is point five of the depth rule in SPEC §15 working as intended, and it is also the beginning of an independent attestation rather than one: nothing here is claimed as independent, since the contact route between this transmission and the Indian meditation literature is documented and the work quotes its sources by name."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "If what is done is coarse and rough, then the breath follows it and is coarse; the breath being coarse, the mind scatters and is hard to record, and the sitting is besides vexed and the mind not at ease. And the ground of the order: entering the absorption is from the coarse into the fine, and since the body is the coarse of the three and the breath stands in the middle and the mind is the finest and stillest, the coarse is regulated towards the fine so that the mind is at rest; this is the first expedient of entering the absorption."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:TiantaiTiaoxi ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:TiantaiZhengshen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmc:TiantaiShentong a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Mit der Vertiefung vermag man die Kräfte des Weges hervorzubringen"@de,
        "Attaining the absorption one is able to bring forth the powers of the way"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0466c, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text",
        "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0472c, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity the chapter on the five expedients holds out as the reason for valuing the absorption above the world: if one gains the absorption, one is then able to bring forth in full the undefiled knowledges, all the transcendent powers and strength of the way, to accomplish the right and equal awakening and to deliver living beings widely. At the end of the work the same thing is said of the completed middle contemplation: the six senses become pure, all the teachings of the buddhas appear before one, the recollection absorption is accomplished and one abides in the Śūraṅgama absorption, which is the absorption of the universal manifestation of bodily form."@en ;
    skos:note "The only capacities in the work that are not healing or quiet are stated here and nowhere else, and both times as a consequence of a stage and never of an operation anyone could repeat. The work gives no sign by which either would be recognised from outside."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmc:TiantaiShierXiZhibing a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Jede der zwölf Atemarten behandelt das ihr zugewiesene Leiden"@de,
        "Each of the twelve breaths treats the complaint assigned to it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0472a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity ascribed to the twelve breaths, with the pairing complete as the work gives it: upward against sinking heaviness, downward against empty suspension, full against wasting, scorching against swelling, growing against debility, destroying against excess, warm against cold, cold against heat, thrusting against stoppage, holding against trembling, harmonizing against the disharmony of the four elements, supplementing against their decline. Used well, the work says of them, these breaths can treat all complaints throughout."@en ;
    skos:note "Twelve prescriptions, each with its indication, and the harvest rule of SPEC §15 counts a prescription as a capacity and asks for strict completeness. They are entered here as one capacity with the twelve pairings complete rather than as twelve nodes, and the ground is not economy: the work does not claim them. It reports them as the opinion of an unnamed master among several it sets side by side, and three lines later it withdraws them for its own time. Twelve nodes would have recorded twelve claims of this transmission where the transmission makes none. The alternative was available and is named here so that a later reader can take it: the pairings are in the definition and nothing has to be read again to split them."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmc:TiantaiShouFeng a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wind bewahren zerstreut, Keuchen bewahren verknotet, Luft bewahren erschöpft"@de,
        "Guarding wind scatters, guarding panting knots, guarding air exhausts"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0466a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:definition "The four outcomes the work attaches to the four breath marks, one line each: to guard the wind is to scatter, to guard the panting is to knot, to guard the air is to exhaust, to guard the breath is to be settled. Where the first three marks are present in sitting it is called unadjusted, and if the practitioner then uses his mind on it, it becomes a trouble of the mind as well and the mind too is hard to settle. The mark of a regulated breath is given as a rule a practitioner can apply to himself: neither rough nor slippery."@en ;
    skos:note "The most answerable thing in the work after the exit procedure, and of a different kind: it is a sign available before the operation has an outcome. The practitioner is told to listen for a sound at his own nostrils and to feel whether the breath knots, and the work predicts what follows from sitting on with each of the three. Three predictions, each with an antecedent anybody present could also check."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmc:TiantaiTiaoshi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Regelung der Nahrung, mit den vier Weisen ihres Fehlgehens"@de,
        "Regulating food, with the four ways it goes wrong"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0465b, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:definition "Food is taken to support the body and further the way. If it is too full, the breath is urgent and the body swollen, the hundred vessels do not pass, the mind is shut and the thought in sitting is not at rest. If it is too little, the body is weak and the mind hangs loose and the intention is not firm. Unclean food makes the consciousness dark and confused. Food that does not suit stirs an old illness and sets the four elements against one another. The work calls this the beginning of cultivating the absorption and says it is deeply to be guarded against."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmc:TiantaiWuGaiList a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die fünf zu verwerfenden Deckel, und warum diese fünf und nicht mehr"@de,
        "The five coverings to be discarded, and why these five and not more"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0464a to 0465b, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:definition "The third chapter. Five states are to be discarded as coverings over the good mind: craving, which arises here in the inner faculty and not in the outer objects; anger, which the work calls the root of losing the dharma and the great thief of the good mind; sleep, which is singled out as the worst because the other coverings can be removed once they are noticed, while sleep is like death and has no noticing in it, so that it is hard to remove, and against which the meditation weight and staff are prescribed where it lies heavy; agitation in body, mouth and mind, which becomes a covering only when regret follows it, since while a man is agitated he is not yet at his object; and doubt. The work puts its own objection and answers it: asked why, since bad states are numberless, only five are discarded, it answers that these five contain the three poisons and their equal share, that the four are the root, and that eight thousand four hundred vexations are gathered under them, so that discarding these five is discarding all that is not good."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered on the second pass. Two things in it are worth keeping apart from the prerequisite that stands on it. The work ranks its own five by how hard each is to remove and gives the criterion, which is whether the state can be noticed while it holds; and it answers the question why five, with an arithmetic. A list that justifies its own length is rarer in this holding than a list."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmc:TiantaiYizhiFakuang a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Das Erzwingen des Verweilens bringt häufig Wahnsinn hervor"@de,
        "Forcing the mind to abide often brings on madness"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0467a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:note "Entered as a caution and not as a prerequisite, because the thing to be avoided is not the omission of a step but a degree of force in performing the prescribed step, and the work gives no measure of that degree. The consequence is quotable and is quoted here; pm:Cautioning has no slot for it, which is prima-materia#621."@en,
        "The clause stands immediately after the work has quoted the Awakening of Faith on gathering the scattered mind back into right recollection, and it is the work's own comment on that instruction: for one of a beginning mind in training, abiding is not yet to be had; to press it so that it abides often brings on madness. It is like the method of archery, where one hits the mark after long practice. A self-warning inside the passage prescribing the thing warned against, and the consequence is named."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:TiantaiZhiguan ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmc:TiantaiYongxinShisuo a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Falsch gebrauchte Praxis bringt die Krankheiten hervor und kann das Leben kosten"@de,
        "The practice wrongly used brings on the illnesses and may cost the life"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0471b, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:note "The opening of the healing chapter, and it is addressed to the practitioner about his own operation: as the practitioner settles his mind and cultivates the way, the four elements may have an illness, and because he now uses contemplation the mind and breath beat against it and stir up the underlying illness; or at times he cannot adjust the three matters of body, mind and breath well, and transgresses inwardly or outwardly, and so there is illness. If the mind is used well the four hundred and four illnesses are removed of themselves, and if the use of the mind misses its place the four hundred and four illnesses arise from it. Once an illness is stirred, not only is there obstruction to practising the way, but the great life itself is to be feared for."@en,
        "This is the shape SPEC §15 calls a self-warning of a text against what it prescribes, and it is unusually complete: the work names the mechanism, the magnitude, the two directions and the worst outcome, and then devotes the longest chapter of the ten to the remedy. What it does not name is a sign by which the right use could be told from the wrong one before the illness appears."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:TiantaiZhiguan ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmc:TiantaiZheZhang a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Nenne niemandem den Nutzen, und zweifle nicht, bevor Schaden erwiesen ist"@de,
        "Tell no one the benefit, and do not doubt before harm is shown"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0472b, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:definition "The tenth of the ten things the work requires beside the healing in the sitting, and the sentence that follows it. What is the recognition of hindrances: it means that the benefit gained is not spoken of outwardly, and that where there is not yet harm no doubt and no disparagement is to arise. If one follows these ten things, then what is treated will certainly have effect and will not be in vain."@en ;
    skos:note "The one unconditional guarantee in the work, and it is made conditional on ten things of which the tenth forbids both the report of a success and the doubt of a failure. A practitioner who followed the ten could not have produced a counter-instance: a cure he may not mention, and a case that has not yet done him harm he may not doubt. The guarantee and the device that protects it are the same sentence, as at pmc:BonLasChad in the Bön file, and here the device is the more complete of the two because it closes both directions."@en,
        "The other eight are given here so that the ten can be counted and the two in question not read as the whole: faith that this method can cure; use, meaning constant use at the time; diligence, meaning to use it with undivided attention until a cure is got; abiding in the object, meaning a fine mind following the method thought by thought without another object; distinguishing the cause of the illness; skill in means, meaning that the breathing out and in and the directing of the mind be well accomplished and not miss what is fitting; long practice; and the knowledge of what to retain and what to drop, meaning to be diligent where there is benefit and to drop and adjust where there is harm; and the care that discerns and avoids discordant causes."@en,
        "The seventh of the same ten removes the term as well: what is long practice, it means that if the use of it brings no benefit at once, the days and months are not to be counted, and it is to be practised constantly and not given up. This belongs beside the holding's statement of that shape in generalizations/no-failed-run-counts.ttl, generalizations/the-rule-against-reporting-the-absence.ttl and examinations/the-count-that-does-not-fall-due.ttl, and it is not entered as a witness of any of the three: the counter-search against the holding that SPEC §16 requires before a generalization is enlarged was not carried in this run."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmc:TiantaiZhengshen a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Den Körper gerade richten: das Rückgrat weder gekrümmt noch hochgezogen, Nase und Nabel gegenüber"@de,
        "Setting the body straight: the spine neither bent nor hunched, nose and navel facing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0465c, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:definition "The first of the three things regulated at the beginning of a sitting, and it is the uprightness and not merely the firmness of the seat that is prescribed. The seat is settled, the legs crossed half or full, the belt loosened, the hands laid one on the other; then the body is to be set straight: first shaken and all its joints moved seven or eight times as in a rubbing down, so that hand and foot are not out of place; then made upright, the bones of the spine neither bent nor hunched; then the head and neck set right, so that the nose and the navel face each other, neither leaning nor aslant, neither low nor raised, the face level and rightly set. Only after this is the stale air expelled through the open mouth and the breath regulated, and only after that the mind."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because the question put in prima-materia#29 is narrower than a holding of postures can answer: whether the sources require the upright seat, that is the erection of the spine, as a condition of its own, or only a firm seat. This work requires it as a condition of its own and names three places on the axis of the body, the spine, the neck and the plane of the face, with a tolerance in each direction, and it does so before the breath is touched. The Haṭhayogapradīpikā node the holding already carries demands firmness and a measured diet and is silent on the erection, which is the difference the issue asked about."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmc:TiantaiZhixinYichu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Halte den Geist an einem Ort, und nichts bleibt unvollbracht"@de,
        "Hold the mind in one place and there is nothing that is not accomplished"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0469c, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:definition "The quotation with which the work closes the chapter on the arising of the good roots, after listing the five kinds and adding that the whole range of the gates of the teaching may arise in the same way: therefore the scripture says, hold the mind in one place and there is nothing that is not accomplished."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because it is the counter-weight to everything in this file and stands in the same work, two chapters before the healing chapter puts the four hundred and four illnesses on both sides of the same operation. The two are not in contradiction, and that is the finding: what this transmission holds open to failure is the performance, and what it holds closed is the method."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmc:TiantaiZhongzuiMiexiang a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Merkmale, an denen das Erloschensein einer schweren Schuld erkannt wird"@de,
        "The marks by which the extinction of a grave offence is known"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0463a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:definition "The work asks its own question, how is the mark of the extinction of a grave offence known, and answers with a list: if the practitioner repents with a mind thus set, he feels of himself that body and mind are light and quick, and gets good auspicious dreams; or he sees numinous and unusual signs; or he feels the good mind opening; or in the sitting he feels the body as a cloud or a shadow, and by this gradually attains and reaches the fields of the absorptions; or understanding suddenly arises, he knows the marks of the teachings well, and grasps the sense of whatever scripture he hears, and from this joy in the teaching his mind is without grief or regret."@en ;
    skos:note "A sign of success in the strict sense of SPEC §15, told to the practitioner in advance of the practice that is to produce it, and therefore counted apart from the conditions of failure in this file and in the harvest note. The first item is the state of the body as the practitioner himself feels it, the second is a dream, the third a vision, and none of the five is available to anybody else."@en,
        "The term is worth noting beside it. The confession is to be carried for one or three weeks, a month, three months or up to a year, and it stops when extinction is reached. The stopping rule is the sign, so the term is not a term: a practitioner who has not yet seen the marks has not reached the end of the procedure, and there is no length of time after which the procedure is to be called finished without them."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmc:TiantaiZhuKongLuoDi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Verweilt man in der ersten Schau, fällt man auf den Boden der Hörer"@de,
        "Abiding in the first contemplation one falls to the ground of the hearers"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0472b, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:definition "The failure the last chapter attaches to the first of its three contemplations: if one abides in this contemplation, one falls to the ground of the hearers and the solitary buddhas. The reason given is that the one who sees the unconditioned and enters the right position will in the end not be able to bring forth the awakening mind, this being an excess of the power of the absorption, by which the buddha nature is not seen. Of the second contemplation the same form is used with the other excess: abiding there, the power of wisdom is great, and so although the buddha nature is seen it is not seen clearly."@en ;
    skos:note "A failure condition of a stage and not of an operation, and it has the shape the work uses everywhere: the fault is an excess of one of the two halves, and the remedy is the other half. It is worth keeping because it is a failure that only shows in what the practitioner cannot do next, which is the hardest kind for an outsider to check and the easiest for a transmission to assert."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmc:TorahWithoutToil a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Erlernen des Gesetzes ohne Mühe"@de,
        "Learning the Law without toil"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hekhalot Rabbati, chapter 30, trans. Morton Smith, corrected by Gershom Scholem, ed. Don Karr" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity claimed. The transmission puts it as a contrast and not as a degree: the voice of the academies sounds, and not with toil and not with weariness do they learn, but with the name of this seal. What is promised beside it is public standing of a specific kind - to seat academies, to expound what is prohibited and what is permitted, to declare the impure impure and the pure pure, to determine the months and the intercalation of years, to appoint judges and to anoint patriarchs."@en ;
    skos:note "Set against it in the same passage is what the ordinary student must do without it, and the transmission puts that in the students' own prayer: may that which we have read remain in our possession, may that which we have studied remain in our possession. The claim is therefore not that learning is easier but that a different faculty is being offered in place of memory."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HekhalotRabbati ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hekhalot .

pmc:TreideNeimthigedarFilid a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Drei Dinge, die einen Dichter ausmachen"@de,
        "Three things that constitute a poet"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The Triads of Ireland, section 123, ed. and trans. Kuno Meyer (Dublin: Hodges and Figgis, 1906), pp. 16-17" ;
    skos:definition "The triad that makes the three operations the qualification of the office rather than an accomplishment beside it: three things that constitute a poet, the knowledge that illumines, teinm laeda, improvisation."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because it fixes the standing of the other nodes in this file. These are not powers a poet might have; they are what the transmission says a poet is. That is what makes the loss of them at pmc:GubrethForfeitsImbas a loss of the office and not of an ornament."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InsularCeltic .

pmc:TroisOuQuatreSeances a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Drei oder vier Sitzungen mit Reizung, dann aufhören"@de,
        "Three or four sittings of irritation, then stop"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. III, pp. 50-51" ;
    skos:definition "Where the operation produces in a patient a nervous irritation that leaves him uneasy after the sittings, the most soothing action is to be used and the operator is to act from a distance. If after three or four sittings the same effect takes place, one may presume that the operation is not good for this patient, or that the fluid of this operator does not agree with him, and one ought not to persist in it. One might merely try, two or three times, other operators."@en ;
    skos:note "That structure is what generalizations/counted-thresholds.ttl describes from three Chinese claims, arrived at here independently in a French work of 1825, and it widens that generalization beyond the stream it was formed in. The widening is recorded on this node rather than by editing the generalization, because its stated scope is a count at which an effect is to take effect and this is a count after which absence of effect is to be acted on. The two are mirror images and a later reader should decide whether they belong under one statement."@en,
        "The cleanest stopping rule in the work: a count, an outcome to look for, and an instruction to desist. It is also the clearest case in this holding of a rule that names a count and assigns the shortfall away from the operation — the presumption offered is that the agent is not good for this patient or that this pairing does not suit, never that the agent does nothing. And the last sentence prescribes continuation with two or three further operators, so the count is reached and the trial goes on."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:TungusCandidateCondition a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Zustand des Anwärters"@de,
        "The condition of the candidate"@en ;
    dcterms:source "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXVIII, sect. 128, pp. 346-350" ;
    skos:definition "A child whose dreams differ from ordinary ones, who is subject to strong emotions and changes of mood and whose behaviour is unlike that of other children is taken for a candidate, especially where there were shamans in the direct ancestral line. Such a child is told of the possibility and gradually prepared. Between sixteen and twenty the first entering of the spirit occurs. Before it the youth refuses food, wanders about without purpose, sleeps too long or cannot sleep; after two weeks or more he jumps up on the bed during the night and begins to sing as the shamans do. If a real state follows, he is considered a future shaman."@en ;
    skos:note "The stated boundaries of the age are two and they differ in kind. That nobody becomes a shaman before fifteen is reported as an absence in the record the author gathered; that candidates are exceptionally rare after full maturity is reported with a reason attached, namely that whoever must look after a family has no leisure for it."@en,
        "The transmission announces the signs to the candidate in advance and then reads them off him. The recorder says of one case that the woman acted according to the candidates' model — she trembled, ran away, climbed a tree, refused food, forgot everything — and of another candidate that the entering of the spirit was seemingly a ritualistic performance. These are signs of success in the strict sense of SPEC §15, told beforehand and reported afterwards, and they are held apart from the conditions of failure in this file for that reason."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SiberianShamanism .

pmc:TungusKnowledgeOfTheHidden a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Wissen um Gedanken und Taten der Anwesenden"@de,
        "Knowing what those present are thinking or doing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXVII, sect. 121, pp. 331-332" ;
    skos:definition "A capacity claimed in the performance and not outside it: the shamans, though not all of them, may say what the persons present are thinking or doing, and in almost all performances they divine in some form."@en ;
    skos:note "Both explanations are the recorder's and neither was tested. The claim is entered because it is the one capacity in this file that could be examined against a base rate, and the passage is the address at which such an examination would start."@en,
        "The recorder gives two cases and a counter-explanation for each. In the first a shaman accused a young man of having eaten the kidney of a sacrificial animal and ordered it given back, and the man vomited into the drum; the recorder believes the shaman could not have seen the act and offers instead that he could infer which of the young men present was likely to have done it. In the second a shaman took a coin from a sceptic and the coin was afterwards found in the sceptic's own hand; the recorder proposes that it was handed back unnoticed. He states in the same passage that in a great number of cases he did not find the shamans conscious of tricking their audience."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SiberianShamanism .

pmc:TungusListOfSpirits a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Schamane braucht eine Liste von Geistern"@de,
        "The shaman must have a list of spirits"@en ;
    dcterms:source "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXII, sect. 95 B, pp. 271-272" ;
    skos:definition "The number of mastered spirits is the measure of the shaman's power. A beginner has at least one, usually a complex one, with whose help he masters others; among the Tungus of Manchuria beginners are stated to have five or six, and by the end of a good career all spirits are mastered directly or through other spirits. The number is expected to increase with the years practised."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim is quantified and the quantity is watched by other people, which is unusual in this graph. It also has a stated downward direction: a shaman who fails to master enough spirits is called a small shaman, not a real one, and when the practice is given up for lack of new spirits the person is said to be no more a shaman."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SiberianShamanism .

pmc:TungusMarkedAnimals a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die an ihren Zeichen benannten Tiere"@de,
        "The animals named by their marks"@en ;
    dcterms:source "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXVIII, sect. 130 I, p. 351" ;
    skos:definition "Among the Reindeer Tungus of Transbaikalia the decisive moment of the trial: the candidate, in the state, names particular marks of the animals which the spirits will send for the making of his costume — a white spot on a leg, white hoofs, a white head, a mutilated ear. A clansman then hunts for animals so marked. The search may take several months. If the animals named cannot be found, the spirit may be asked to send another animal instead."@en ;
    skos:note "A prediction with a stated content, made before witnesses, checkable by anyone who brings back the animal. It is the most examinable claim in this file, and it comes with its own escape clause in the same sentence."@en,
        "The recorder closes the passage with four words that are the whole finding: the needed animals are always found. He gives no count and reports no failure."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SiberianShamanism .

pmc:TungusParaphernalia a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Gerät ist unerlässlich und wandelbar"@de,
        "The paraphernalia are indispensable and variable"@en ;
    dcterms:source "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXII, sect. 95 D, pp. 272-273" ;
    skos:definition "It is held that without paraphernalia shamanizing is impossible, so that a person who has none cannot function as a shaman. The composition varies greatly: from a brass mirror and a drum up to several complete costumes with headdress, coat, trousers, apron, shoes and many placings for spirits. Equipment made for a single occasion is admitted, so the lack of permanent paraphernalia does not by itself mean the complex is absent."@en ;
    skos:note "The variability is stated together with a floor: reduction of the paraphernalia to zero is held responsible for the disintegration of shamanism, and the recorder names cases where local authorities forbade shamanizing and destroyed the equipment. Where the equipment is limited the power is said to be reduced in a specific way — among the Nercinsk and Barguzin Tungus a shaman cannot go into the lower world without a complete harness."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SiberianShamanism .

pmc:TungusReliefOfSpiritAffliction a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Befreiung der von Geistern Befallenen"@de,
        "Relief of those afflicted by spirits"@en ;
    dcterms:source "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXX, sect. 140, p. 381" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity for which the office exists: to cure people affected by the spirits, to relieve sick people also where the disease is not of that order, and by his presence to give assurance to the people that they will not suffer."@en ;
    skos:note "The recorder adds his own explanation, that this is the result of an empiric experimentation of the Tungus on themselves reached through a long adaptation, and that the shaman functions as a safety valve of the clan. That reading is his. What is reported is the claim that the office relieves and the fact that people seek it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SiberianShamanism .

pmc:TungusSacrificeRejected a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Zeichen des zurückgewiesenen Opfers"@de,
        "The sign that a sacrifice was refused"@en ;
    dcterms:source "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXVIII, sect. 128, Case 2, p. 347" ;
    skos:definition "In the Manchu clan history recorded by the author, the kite spirit is said to be able to eat the whole liver of an ox. After eating, the shaman must leave his assistant; if he falls down without being able to keep on his legs, the sacrifice is considered as rejected by the spirit."@en ;
    skos:note "A condition of failure with a physical criterion, stated in advance, decidable on the spot by everyone present, and not in the performer's gift. Very little in this graph has that shape, and it is the reason the passage is entered although it stands inside a narrative."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SiberianShamanism .

pmc:TungusShamanDrum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Trommel als Werkzeug der Selbsterregung"@de,
        "The drum as the instrument of self-excitation"@en ;
    dcterms:source "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXII, sect. 95 D, p. 272" ;
    skos:definition "The second member of the observed minimum. Its stated function is not signalling and not accompaniment but the shaman's own self-excitation, without which he cannot bring himself into the state the performance requires."@en ;
    skos:note "Stated as a function and not as a symbol, which is what makes it usable. The same passage names the toli as a placing for spirits and the drum as the means of self-excitation, so the two indispensable objects have two different offices and are not interchangeable."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SiberianShamanism .

pmc:TungusToli a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Toli, der Bronzespiegel"@de,
        "Toli, the brass mirror"@en ;
    dcterms:source "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXII, sect. 95 D, p. 272, and chap. XXIII, sect. 99, p. 278" ;
    skos:definition "A Chinese brass mirror with pendants, used as a placing for spirits and the one object that cannot be dispensed with. Where there is no costume the shaman can perform with the mirror alone; where there is no mirror or a substitute for it, no performance is possible. The word is the same across all Tungus dialects and Manchu and stands beside the Mongol, Buriat and Dahur forms."@en ;
    skos:note "In the same passage he lists what stands beside it: a headdress with five images of Buddha among the Tungus of Manchuria, sometimes replaced by ornaments of Chinese style; the swastika and the Christian cross used by all shamans; the boa constrictor symbolised in the costume although unknown in the region; and Manchu shamans with weapons familiar to Buddhism and pictures reproducing the costume of Buddhist priests."@en,
        "The mirror carries the contact route on its face. The recorder states that it is widely used in Lamaism as one of the indispensable components of the altar, and that mirrors are either found in the earth, or received from predecessor shamans, or bought from the Mongols, who receive them from China and Tibet. The animals on the back are interpreted by each group according to its own imagination."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SiberianShamanism .

pmc:TunkanDistantKnowledge a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wissen um das, was jenseits des Blicks liegt, durch die Steine"@de,
        "Knowledge of what lies beyond sight, through the stones"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Frances Densmore, Teton Sioux Music (Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 61, Washington, 1918), p. 205 and p. 210" ;
    skos:definition "The power said to follow the fulfilled dream of a sacred stone, given by the recorder in three parts: an ability to cure sickness, to predict future events, and to tell the location of objects beyond the range of the possessor's natural vision."@en ;
    skos:note "The first case has a sceptic, a stake and a witness, which is as close to a set procedure as anything in this file; the second has a locatable object and a named place. Neither is evidence in the sense of SPEC §14 — no procedure was devised and no outcome was recorded that could have gone otherwise for a third party — and both are entered as what they are, first-person reports taken down at the time."@en,
        "The third part is the one that matters to this graph, because it names a fact of the world that somebody else can check. Two cases are printed with it, both told in the first person by the man who performed them. In one, a fur trader who had said that all the medicine men did was sleight of hand offered ten articles if a buffalo were called to the spot where they stood; the stones were sent, the trader watched through field glasses, and a buffalo came near enough to be shot from where they stood. In the other, a white man dropped a rifle from a ferry into the river near the present site of Pierre in South Dakota and made no effort to recover it; the teller took the stones out on the water, dove where they told him to, found the rifle on the bed of the river, and afterwards returned it to its owner."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LakotaRitual .

pmc:TunkanYieldsDistantKnowledge a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Aussenden der Steine bringt Wissen um Unerreichbares hervor"@de,
        "Sending the stones yields knowledge of what is out of range"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Frances Densmore, Teton Sioux Music (Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 61, Washington, 1918), p. 210" ;
    skos:note "Entered at first-person report and not at field attestation, because the two cases that carry it were told by the man who says he did them, in his own words, to the recorder. What the recorder supplies is the setting; the claim is his informant's."@en,
        "This is the outside-sphere witness this graph has been short of. The generalization at pmc:DistantPerceptionFromAVoluntaryState rested on four transmissions, of which one was North American; this adds a second, from a different language family and a different region, with a stated instrument and two reported cases. The independence is not thereby established: the recording is of nineteen eleven to nineteen fourteen and the objection raised about the Midewiwin material applies here too. What it is not subject to is the mission route that governs the doctrinal nodes of this file, since the recorder notes of one of these informants that the missionaries had made no impression on him."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:TunkanSending ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LakotaRitual ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:TunkanDistantKnowledge .

pmc:TurbaAlbedo a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Weiße, aufs Feinste aus der Schwärze gezogen"@de,
        "The whiteness, extracted most subtly from the blackness"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 52 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)",
        "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 69 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    skos:definition "The second state. The whiteness hidden in the belly of the blackness is to be extracted most subtly, put in a vessel with its instruments and cooked gently until it becomes completely white; the work says at Dictum 67 that to whiten is to burn, and at Dictum 52 that sulphur alone whitens copper and cannot whiten it until it has itself been whitened in the work."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy .

pmc:TurbaCulpaInOperante a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wenn ihr irrt, klagt niemanden an als euch selbst"@de,
        "If ye err, blame no one save yourselves"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 17 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)",
        "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 29 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    skos:definition "The work's standing account of a failure, stated by two speakers in the same terms. Zimon at Dictum 17, having given the rule of the four seasons: having, therefore, given this example, rule the tingeing natures, but if ye err, blame no one save yourselves. Diomedes at Dictum 29, closing his speech: happy are ye, if ye understand, but if not, I have still performed my duty, and that briefly, so that if ye remain ignorant, it is God who hath concealed the truth from you — blame not, therefore, the Wise, but yourselves, for if God knew that ye possessed a faithful mind, most certainly he would reveal unto you the truth."@en ;
    skos:note "A witness for pmc:NoFailedRunCountsAgainstTheOperation in generalizations/no-failed-run-counts.ttl, on the limb that locates the condition in the operator."@en,
        "The second of the two sentences is the complete device. It states in one breath that the failure is the reader's, that the cause is God's concealment, and that the concealment is warranted by a defect in the reader's mind. Each of the three would account for a run that produced nothing; together they leave no outcome that could be charged to the instructions."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy .

pmc:TurbaExemplumRelinquentis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Mann, der die ausbleibende Röte für einen Fehler nahm und das Werk aufgab"@de,
        "The man who took the slow redness for a mistake and gave up the work"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 27 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    skos:definition "The case Gregorius reports at Dictum 27, immediately after saying that an operator who rules ignorantly will see none of the colours. I knew a certain person who commenced this work, and operated the natures of truth, who, when the redness was somewhat slow in appearing, imagined that he had made a mistake, and so relinquished the work. The sentence that follows tells the reader what to do instead: observe how ye make the conjunction, for the redness does not delay in coming."@en ;
    skos:note "A witness for pmc:TheRuleAgainstReportingTheAbsence in generalizations/the-rule-against-reporting-the-absence.ttl, on the third of the three forms that statement names: the doubt is read as a fault in the man rather than as information about the procedure. What is unusual here is that the consequence attaches to acting on the doubt rather than to uttering it, and that the man's loss — he gave up a work that was about to succeed — is supplied by the transmission rather than observed by anybody."@en,
        "It is not a commentator's gloss and not a critic's observation. It stands in an operative speech, between the instruction to rule the stone until it reddens and the instruction about the weight, and it is told as the speaker's own acquaintance."@en,
        "This is the most exact instance in the holding of a transmission instructing its reader how to read the absence of its own announced sign. The sign is the redness; the operator did not get it when he expected it; and the work records his inference that something had gone wrong as the error, not the missing redness. After this passage no delay can be evidence, because delay has been named in advance as the thing a man mistakes for evidence."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy .

pmc:TurbaFestinantiaCausaDefectus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Er kannte die Elemente so gut wie ich und scheiterte an Eile, Trauer und Begehren"@de,
        "He knew the elements as well as I and failed of haste, sadness and eagerness"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 39 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    skos:definition "The second case, reported by Bacsen at Dictum 39. I once met with a person who was as well acquainted with the elements as I myself, but when he proceeded to rule this disposition, he attained not to the joy thereof by reason of his sadness and ignorance in ruling, and excessive eagerness, desire, and haste concerning the purpose. The speech puts the general rule beside it: ye can reach no useful result without a patient, laborious and solicitous soul, persevering courage and continuous regimen; he who desires to learn over speedily must not have recourse to our books."@en ;
    skos:note "The case is constructed so that knowledge is explicitly not the variable: the man knew the elements as well as the speaker. What is left to carry the failure is four states of his soul, none of which anybody could have established before the attempt and all four of which anybody could find afterwards. It is the same displacement as pmc:TurbaCulpaInOperante with the knowledge condition deliberately closed off."@en,
        "The same speech states the other half of the arrangement, and it is the sentence that makes a term impossible: the truth is not to be discerned except after error, and nothing creates greater pain at heart than error in this art, while each imagines that he has almost the whole world and yet finds nothing in his hands. Error is thereby made a station on the way rather than an outcome. See pmc:TurbaVeritasPostErrorem."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy .

pmc:TurbaNigredoNonUltraQuadraginta a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Schwärze darf nicht länger als vierzig Tage bleiben"@de,
        "The blackness must not stay longer than forty days"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 21 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    skos:definition "The one term in the work whose overrun would be a failure the operator could date. Pandolfus at Dictum 21: rule it thus for the white until it becomes Kuhul, closed up with blackness, which blackness see that it does not remain longer than forty days. The same speech then has the four cooked permanently for forty or forty-two days, after which God will show the operator the principle of the stone."@en ;
    skos:note "And what the work does with that fact is nothing. No sentence anywhere in the seventy-two dicta says what the operator is to conclude, do or abandon when the forty days pass and the blackness stands. The bound is stated and left, which is why the search for a term of abandonment was carried over the whole work and is recorded as an exhibited gap at pmp:TurbaTerminusDesistendiSearch."@en,
        "The Latin of the passage, as Waite gives it in his note, reads that the pure blackness lasts only forty days, which is an assertion about what happens rather than an instruction about what is allowed. His English makes it an instruction. Which of the two the work says is a question for the Latin and it is left open here; the node claims the bound and not its grammatical mood."@en,
        "What makes the sentence unusual in this holding is that it is an upper bound on a state and not a duration of an operation. Every other number in the work says how long to cook; this one says how long the matter is allowed to stay as it is. An operator whose matter is still black on the forty-first day has a fact about his own batch, dated, and needing nobody's report but his own."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy .

pmc:TurbaProbaImpalpabilis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Pulver in die Hand genommen, untastbar wie Wasser, sonst nochmals kochen"@de,
        "The powder taken in the hand, impalpable as water, else cook again"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 28 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    skos:definition "The work's plainest act of assay, stated by Custos at Dictum 28. When the water has entered the body it turns it first into earth and next into powder; to test the perfection of which take in the hand, and if ye find it impalpable as water, it is then most excellent; otherwise, repeat the cooking until it is brought to the required condition."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a claim of the work and not as a protocol, because nobody in this holding has performed it. It is a witness for pmc:TheTestOnTheIngredient in generalizations/the-test-on-the-ingredient.ttl, on the limb about a handbook that assays its own operation."@en,
        "It has everything an assay needs and one thing besides. The act is definite — take the powder in the hand; the criterion is perceptible and needs no instrument; and the negative outcome is named rather than passed over. The one thing besides is what the negative outcome licenses: not a verdict on the operation but a repetition of it, until the outcome arrives. So the act can establish that this batch is not yet finished and cannot establish that the operation does not work."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy .

pmc:TurbaRubedo a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Röte von tyrischer Farbe, mit der sich nichts vergleichen lässt"@de,
        "The redness, of a Tyrian colour, with which nothing can compare"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 67 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)",
        "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 69 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    skos:definition "The third and last state. When the whiteness appears and flows over all, the work says the operator is to be certain that redness is hid in that whiteness, and to cook until the whole becomes a most deep red with which nothing can compare; the finished redness is called the Tyrian colour, and at Dictum 67 making red is called making alive."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy .

pmc:TurbaSenexIuvenescens a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Greis, der jung wird, und der Vater, der zum Sohn wird"@de,
        "The old man made young, the father made into the son"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 58 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity claimed of the fruit of that tree: the old man shall become young, the soul of the old man is transformed into a juvenile body, and the father is made into the son. Whoever eats of the fruit shall hunger nevermore."@en ;
    skos:note "Read as what it is and not as an allegory of the matter, because the passage gives a number of days and a closed room and the work elsewhere gives numbers of days for the matter as well; nothing in the dictum marks this one as figurative that does not equally mark the others. Whether the compiler meant it so is a question about the compiler. What the graph holds is that the work states it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy .

pmc:TurbaSignumPrimaeDecoctionis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Zeichen der Güte der ersten Kochung ist das Ziehen ihrer Röte"@de,
        "The sign of the goodness of the first decoction is the extraction of its redness"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 69 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    skos:definition "The work's stated sign of success, given by Florus at Dictum 69. The sign of the goodness of the first decoction is the extraction of its redness. When ye see that the matter is entirely black, know that whiteness has been hidden in the belly of that blackness; and when ye shall perceive that whiteness appear and flowing over all, be certain that redness is hid in that whiteness."@en ;
    skos:note "Held separately from the two acts of assay, and separately counted, for the reason SPEC §15 gives: a sign is announced to the operator in advance, so he reports it, and the base rate is unknown. The difference from the assays above is exact. At Dictum 28 the operator does something and reads an outcome that could have come out otherwise; here he watches for a colour he has been told to expect, and the instruction that accompanies it is to be certain that the next colour is already hidden in the one he sees."@en,
        "The clause that removes the sign's force stands in the same sentence as the sign. Told that whiteness is hidden in the blackness and redness in the whiteness, an operator who sees only blackness has been given the reading that the whiteness is there and not yet out. There is no state of the matter that the sentence would count as the absence of progress."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy .

pmc:TziteAnswer a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Antwort des Loses über die Holzmenschen"@de,
        "The answer the lot gave about the men of wood"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Popol Vuh: The Sacred Book of the Ancient Quiché Maya, English version by Delia Goetz and Sylvanus G. Morley from the Spanish translation of Adrián Recinos (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1950), part I, chapter 2" ;
    skos:definition "The outcome of that casting, given as a plain prediction: the figures of wood will come out well, they will speak and talk on earth. The prediction is about a future state of affairs and not about a duty, so it is the kind of answer that an outcome can meet or miss."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MayaRitual .

pmc:UgarDbbm a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Peiniger, die die Rezitation austreibt"@de,
        "The tormenters the recitation casts out"@en ;
    dcterms:source "RIH 78/20:1-2 and 9-10 (KTU 1.169), in Pardee, Ritual and Cult at Ugarit, WAW 10, 2002, text 49, pp. 159-160" ;
    skos:altLabel "dbbm"@en ;
    skos:definition "What the opening line of the work says the recitation does: it casts out the tormenters of a young man; and in the lines that follow, the sorcerers and the tormenters are those whom Ḥôrānu will drive out, together with the companions and a further named group."@en ;
    skos:note "The identification of the affliction is the open question of this work and four reachable treatments answer it four ways: the edition read here titles it male sexual dysfunction, a second translation the exorcism of a possessed adolescent, the editio princeps a promise of divine help against tormenting demons, and a fourth treatment sorcery by a spoken word. What is not in dispute is that something is being cast out of a person and that the agent named for it is Ḥôrānu, who is the same deity that acts in RS 24.244."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RIH7820 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:UgariticRitual .

pmc:UgarHta a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Sünde, deren jede mögliche Quelle auf einmal genannt wird"@de,
        "The sin whose every possible source is named at once"@en ;
    dcterms:source "RS 1.002:19'-23', with the parallel sections at 28'-32' and 36'-40' (KTU 1.40), in Pardee, Ritual and Cult at Ugarit, WAW 10, 2002, text 22, pp. 81-83" ;
    skos:altLabel "ḥṭʼ"@en ;
    skos:definition "The condition the rite expiates, stated as a triple disjunction repeated in each section: whether you sin according to the statement of the Qaṭian, of DDMY, of the Hurrian, of the Hittite, of the Alashian, of GBR, of your oppressed ones, of your impoverished ones, of QRZBL; whether you sin in your anger, in your impatience, in some turpitude you should commit; whether you sin as concerns the sacrifices or as concerns the tʻ-sacrifice."@en ;
    skos:note "Not entered as a witness of that claim, and the reason is the same one the Anatolian strand recorded for its own candidate on the same day. One of the six witnesses already standing is the Mesopotamian exorcistic craft, and this corpus names its contact with upper Mesopotamia in the text of another of its tablets. A witness that cannot be shown independent of an existing witness does not raise a count; what it could raise is the count of stations with a reworking, and that needs an added element entered first. The search is at pmp:UgarProvisionSearch."@en,
        "The other side of the same device, which is what makes it worth a node rather than a remark. A rite that covers every possible cause also covers every possible outcome: there is no case in which a later misfortune could show that the expiation had missed its object, because no object was named. The enumeration buys completeness at the price of discrimination, and it does it in the operative text and not in a commentary."@en,
        "Three enumerations, each closed by the construction that takes them all at once, and between them they leave nothing outside: every foreign party the kingdom dealt with and both of its own underclasses, every disposition in which an offence could be committed, and both of the cultic acts in which it could be committed. The officiant does not need to know what was done, by whom, or in what spirit. That is the device the holding records at pmc:TheProvisionForNotKnowing in its first form, a disjunction that names every candidate so that the identification need not be made, and this is the most complete instance of it read anywhere in the holding."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RS1002 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:UgariticRitual .

pmc:UgarRS24266Yielding a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Gebet an der Mauer soll Baʻlu den Belagerer vertreiben lassen"@de,
        "The prayer at the wall is said to make Baʻlu drive the besieger off"@en ;
    dcterms:source "RS 24.266:26'-36' (KTU 1.119), in Pardee, Ritual and Cult at Ugarit, WAW 10, 2002, text 46, pp. 149-150" ;
    skos:note "Entered as the claim of eleven lines and not of a corpus. The obverse of the same tablet is a sacrificial calendar for a named month and the editor states that the prayer is appended without an explicit link to it; whether the city understood the prayer to work on its own or only on top of that calendar is not said anywhere in the tablet."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:UgarTsuEnkm ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RS24266 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:UgariticRitual ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:UgarWSmBl .

pmc:UgarWSmBl a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Und Baʻlu wird euer Gebet hören"@de,
        "And Baʻlu will hear your prayer"@en ;
    dcterms:source "RS 24.266:34'-36' (KTU 1.119), in Pardee, Ritual and Cult at Ugarit, WAW 10, 2002, text 46, p. 150" ;
    skos:definition "The closing clause, which is not a petition but a statement: and Baʻlu will hear your prayer, he will drive the strong foe from your gate, the warrior from your walls."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the shape the holding has least of: a flat predictive statement in the indicative, addressed to the people who are to perform the act, about an outcome a third party could observe. It is not an optative and it is not conditional on the vow; the vow was conditional on it. A transmission that says this has made itself checkable in the only way that counts — the siege is lifted or it is not — and has done so without any of the devices the rest of this holding uses to prevent that."@en,
        "What is preserved and what is not, because a predictive clause is worth exactly what its text is worth. The verb of hearing is restored by the editor, who marks it as a restoration, and the words for the prayer and for the two halves of the outcome are preserved, the second with a break at the end of the last line. A reader who rejects the restoration still has the two lines that state what the god will drive away from the gate and from the walls, which is the predictive part; what the restoration supplies is the hearing that introduces them."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RS24266 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:UgariticRitual .

pmc:UnchosenSendingCarriesTheActualState a pm:Positing ;
    rdfs:label "Senden ohne gewählte Signatur überträgt den eigenen Ist-Zustand"@de,
        "Sending without a chosen signature carries the sender's actual state"@en ;
    skos:definition "The claim that where no state is deliberately chosen for transmission, the one the sender is in transmits instead."@en ;
    skos:note "A claim about a failure mode rather than about a capacity, and that is what makes it the most tractable of the three. It says what happens when the practice is done wrong, and a claim about going wrong names an outcome that can be looked for."@en ;
    pm:positedIn "prima-materia#316" .

pmc:UncreatedLight a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ungeschaffenes Licht"@de,
        "Uncreated light"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Gregory Palamas, Triads in Defence of the Holy Hesychasts (trans. Gendle 1983)" ;
    skos:definition "A light held to be uncreated, and held to shine through the whole person, body as well as soul; the defence is explicitly that the body participates and is not merely the site of an event in the soul."@en ;
    skos:note "This is a claim about what shines through the practitioner, not about an emanation the practitioner produces or directs."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hesychasm .

pmc:VaidikaAgnicayanaAmrtatva a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der geschichtete Altar bringt das Entkommen vom wiederkehrenden Tod hervor"@de,
        "The piled altar yields escape from the death that comes again"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa X,4,3,8-10 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 43, Oxford 1897, pp. 357-358" ;
    skos:note "Entered as the claim of the transmission. What makes it worth more than the ordinary promise of a rite is that the passage reports two failures of its own before it succeeds, which almost nothing else in this holding does. See pmc:VaidikaIstakasamkhya."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:VaidikaAgnicayana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:VaidikaPunarmrtyu .

pmc:VaidikaAgnyupasamana a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Was eintritt, wenn ein Feuer erlischt, Feuer für Feuer"@de,
        "What befalls when a fire goes out, fire by fire"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa XI,5,3,8-12 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 44, Oxford 1900, pp. 82-85" ;
    skos:definition "Śauceya Prācīnayogya puts five cases to Uddālaka Āruṇi in turn and Uddālaka names the damage for each. If the fires are taken out and the vessels brought down and the offering fire then goes out, before long the eldest son would die. If at that moment the householder's fire goes out, before long the master of the house would die. If the southern cooking fire goes out, before long all the cattle would die. If all the fires go out, before long the family would be without heirs. If all the fires go out and there is no wind blowing, unpleasant things would be seen in this world and unpleasant things in the world beyond. Each answer names an item of knowledge and a repair beside the damage."@en ;
    skos:note "A graded catalogue of failure conditions for one rite, given in the transmission's own voice, with the damage named in each case and rising from one person to the whole household. Nothing else this holding has read states the consequences of a single kind of mishap in five degrees."@en,
        "The damage falls on the eldest son, on the master of the house, on the cattle and on the line — never on the officiant who is speaking. Where the sacrificer is himself the offerer the second case reaches him, and it is the only one of the five that does."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:VaidikaAgnihotra ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual .

pmc:VaidikaIndrasampad a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Wohlstand, zu dem Indra gelangte"@de,
        "The prosperity Indra came to have"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa I,6,3,14-15 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 12, Oxford 1882, p. 167" ;
    skos:definition "The attainment the full-moon offering is said to obtain, defined by the legend it closes. Agni and Soma come over to Indra for the cake on eleven potsherds, and after them go all the gods, all the sciences, all glory, all nourishment, all prosperity: thus by offering that cake Indra became what Indra now is. The claim follows: he who, knowing this, performs the full-moon offering in this wise attains to the same state of prosperity, becomes thus endowed with glory, becomes such a consumer of food."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because the harvest rule counts attainments strictly and this is one, although it is the vaguest kind: prosperity, glory and the consumption of food, with no term, no sign and no amount. It is worth having beside the sharp claims of the same corpus rather than instead of them — the same book that fixes an altar at ten thousand eight hundred bricks also promises this."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual .

pmc:VaidikaPranitavisarjanam a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Ausgießen des Praṇītā-Wassers als das Ausspannen"@de,
        "The pouring out of the praṇītā-water as the unyoking"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa I,9,2,32-33 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 12, Oxford 1882, p. 265" ;
    skos:definition "The closing act. Having walked round from the north of the offering fire to the south, he pours the praṇītā-water out on the altar, with the words: who unyokes thee? he unyokes thee! for whom does he unyoke thee? for him he unyokes thee! for plenty! He pours it out with the same vessel with which he brought it forward, because the team is unyoked with the same tie by which it is yoked."@en ;
    skos:note "The identification is what carries the prescription: spreading the sacrifice is yoking it, and the vessel is the yoke-tie. The prohibition at pmc:VaidikaPranitavimocana follows from the image and is stated as its consequence."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual .

pmc:VaidikaPrayascittaEffectus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kein Schaden und kein Übel irgendwelcher Art trifft den, bei dem jene Sühne vollzogen wird"@de,
        "No hurt and no harm of any kind befalls where that expiation is made"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa XII,4,4 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 44, Oxford 1900, p. 196" ;
    skos:definition "The effect claimed for the repair, in the transmission's own closing words to the chapter on the sun rising or setting over an uncarried offering: the import is the same as before, and verily, no hurt and no harm of any kind befalls where that expiation is made."@en ;
    skos:note "The generality is in the sentence and not in the reading of it. It does not say that the particular harm of the particular mishap is averted; it says that no harm of any kind befalls. A claim of that shape has no outcome left over that could count against it."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual .

pmc:VaidikaPrayascittaSarvam a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Sühne wirkt, in jedem Falle und ohne Zeichen"@de,
        "The expiation is said to work, in every case and without a sign"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa XII,4,1,8 and XII,4,4 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 44, Oxford 1900, pp. 180 and 196" ;
    skos:note "Entered with its weakness stated, because the weakness is the finding. Twenty-two enumerated mishaps, a prescribed act for each, one general formula that covers whatever is not enumerated — the three utterances bhūr bhuvaḥ svar, of which the text says that these utterances are all-expiatory and that he thus makes expiation with all this universe — and one general assurance: no hurt and no harm of any kind befalls where that expiation is made. In four chapters the run found no sign by which a completed expiation is known to have taken effect, no interval within which the averted harm would otherwise have come, and no case in which an expiation failed. The search is at examinations/searches-vedic.ttl."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:VaidikaPrayascitta ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:VaidikaPrayascittaEffectus .

pmc:VaidikaRudhiradoha a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Kuh, die Blut gibt, und der Mann, dem das Übel angeheftet wird"@de,
        "The cow that gives blood, and the man the evil is fastened on"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa XII,4,2,1 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 44, Oxford 1900, p. 183" ;
    skos:definition "One of the enumerated mishaps, with a repair that moves the harm to a named person. If the Agnihotra cow were to milk blood, he says Disperse, has the southern fire enclosed, boils the blood on it and offers it silently and in an undefined way, because Prajāpati is undefined and the undefined means everything, so that he makes atonement with everything. At this offering he should give that cow to a Brahmaṇa whom he does not intend to visit; for she who milks blood milks it after perceiving the sacrificer's suffering and evil, and he thus fastens that suffering and evil upon this Brahmaṇa. He then offers with what other milk he can procure."@en ;
    skos:note "The mishap is treated as a symptom and not as an accident: the cow is said to have perceived an evil that was already in the sacrificer. The repair does not remove the evil, it transfers it, and the instruction to choose a Brahmaṇa he does not intend to visit says plainly that the recipient is not to be seen again. This is the most explicit transfer of a ritual penalty to an uninvolved third party that this holding has read."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual .

pmc:VaidikaSamkhyapurnata a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Vollständigkeit der Zahl"@de,
        "The completeness of the count"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa X,4,3,8 and X,4,3,12-13 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 43, Oxford 1897, pp. 357-358" ;
    skos:definition "The condition Prajāpati names and the gods then meet: three hundred and sixty enclosing-stones, three hundred and sixty bricks laid with formulas and thirty-six more, and ten thousand eight hundred filling bricks. The transmission reads each class against a division of the year — the stones for the nights, the formula-bricks for the days, half-moons, months and seasons, the filling bricks for the hours — and distributes the stones by hearth: twenty-one round the householder's fire, seventy-eight round the subsidiary hearths, two hundred and sixty-one round the offering fire."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as its own node because it is the one condition in this file that an onlooker could check while the work was going on. Everything else the corpus makes efficacy depend on is either a state of mind or an event after death."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual .

pmc:VaidikaSvadhyayaPunarmrtyujaya a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die tägliche Rezitation befreit vom wiederkehrenden Tod"@de,
        "The daily recitation frees from the death that comes again"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa XI,5,6,9 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 44, Oxford 1900, p. 99" ;
    skos:note "The same outcome the fire-altar is said to obtain, obtained here by recitation alone, with an occasion attached: the four vaṣaṭ calls of this sacrifice are the wind blowing, the lightning, the thunder and the rumbling, and he who knows this should study when they occur so as not to lose them. And a floor is set under it — should he be altogether unable to study, let him read a single divine word, and thus he is not shut out from beings."@en,
        "Worth holding beside pmc:VaidikaAgnicayanaAmrtatva rather than merged with it. Two operations of very different cost are credited with the same result in the same corpus, and one of them can be discharged by one word on a day when nothing else is possible."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:VaidikaSvadhyaya ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:VaidikaPunarmrtyu .

pmc:VaidikaVasatkaraRiktah a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Der leere Ruf, in dem die Silbe ausfällt"@de,
        "The empty call, in which the syllable fails"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aitareya Brāhmaṇa iii,7 (xi,7), in the English of Arthur Berriedale Keith, Rigveda Brahmanas, Harvard Oriental Series 25, Cambridge 1920, p. 169" ;
    skos:definition "The third of the three vaṣaṭ calls and the defective one: that one wherein the sat fails is the empty; he empties himself, he empties the sacrificer; the sayer of vaṣaṭ becomes worse, he becomes worse for whom he says vaṣaṭ. Therefore he should not desire it."@en ;
    skos:note "A stated condition of failure located in a single syllable, with the damage named on both sides: on the reciter and on the man he recites for. The commentator Sāyaṇa, as Keith reports him, takes the loss to lie in a lowered pronunciation, so the fault is one of delivery and not of wording."@en,
        "The same chapter makes the call an instrument of deliberate harm and benefit, which is entered separately at pmc:VaidikaSvarenaKarma. Read together, the two say that the same variation which is a fault when it happens is a technique when it is intended."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:VaidikaVasatkara ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AitareyaBrahmana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual .

pmc:Vespasian a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Vespasian"@de,
        "Vespasian"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tacitus, Historiae IV.81" ;
    skos:definition "The Roman emperor to whom the Alexandrian healings are ascribed by two independent historians."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TacitiHistoriae ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GraecoRoman .

pmc:VoSesa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Vo-sesa, das gebundene Amulett"@de,
        "Vo-sesa, the bound amulet"@en ;
    dcterms:source "A. B. Ellis, The Ewe-speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1890), pp. 92-93" ;
    skos:definition "An amulet belonging to a god, of which every god has his own kind. Its power is said not to rest on an indwelling spirit but on its consecration: it lets the god recognise and protect his own. Neither prayer nor sacrifice is offered to it. Only the priests of a god know how to make the amulets of that god, and they sell them at a high price. The word is derived from the verb to bind, which is used only of amulets, because the priest binds them on the person or the property of the buyer."@en ;
    skos:note "The transmission attaches a public and checkable claim to these: crops standing in the bush and goods left for sale beside a frequented path are put under an amulet on a stick, with a few cowries on each article to show the price, and are said to be quite safe from pillage. Whether that held is a question about a distribution in the world, and it is the kind of claim this graph is short of."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Vodun .

pmc:WalkerSystematizationOfTheGods a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Vier-mal-vier-Schema der Götter als Ordnungsakt"@de,
        "The four-times-four scheme of the gods as an ordering act"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1917" ;
    dcterms:source "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 56 and pp. 79-81 and pp. 154-155" ;
    skos:definition "The arrangement of the Lakota gods into four classes of four, the superior, the associate, the subordinate and the god-like, gathered again into four groups of four called the Chief God, the Great Spirit, the Creator and the Executive, and those four taken as one. The compiler states that the shamans recognized a scheme and a system but had never formulated them into a single whole, that no one shaman was found who could give them in a comprehensive or sequential manner, that the scheme was formulated with their aid, and that it was afterwards approved by every informant to whom it was submitted."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because the corpus says so itself, in the introduction, and because a reader who takes the scheme for a transmitted doctrine will read every node that mentions a god wrongly. Approval after the fact is not authorship: the informants were asked whether an arrangement was right, which is a different act from producing it."@en,
        "The interview with Finger, printed in the same paper and taken a few days before the compiler left the reservation, does not reproduce the scheme. Asked how many there are he names eight, then says there is but one, then says there are four and that the other four are the same as those four. The compiler notes of that interview that there was no opportunity for verifying the information."@en ;
    pm:compiledBy "J. R. Walker, agency physician at Pine Ridge, with the Oglala shamans who approved the scheme after it was formulated" .

pmc:WeaponsOfDeath a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Waffen des Todes"@de,
        "The weapons of death"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 69:6 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "Shield, breastplate and the sword for slaughter, disclosed as instruments of killing rather than as craft."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:WhatMayBeDoubtedAndWhatMayNot a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Was bezweifelt werden darf und was nicht"@de,
        "What may be doubted and what may not"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John H. Weeks, Notes on Some Customs of the Lower Congo People, in Folk-Lore, vol. XX (London: The Folk-Lore Society, 1909), pp. 51-54" ;
    skos:definition "The corpus sets out a ranking of what the people hold, in the order of its binding force: witchcraft and evil spirits first, the ordeal second, prohibition third, divination fourth, omens fifth, the practitioners sixth, and the charged things last. On the first two it states that doubt is not permitted, since to scout the idea of the power to bewitch or to sneer at the ordeal is held to prove the doubter a witch himself; on the last two it states that doubt costs nothing, that a man may snub a practitioner and talk slightingly of his charms and suffer no inconvenience, and that no one believes in all the charged things but each keeps his own few and regards the rest with contempt."@en ;
    skos:note "The corpus states one consequence of this ranking as an observation of the recorder: the practitioner of a village is seldom if ever engaged by the people of that village, who know too much about him and see that his charms cannot keep himself, his wife, his children or his animals in health, and who send instead for the practitioner of another village of whom they know little."@en,
        "The most valuable node in the file for what the project is for, because the transmission itself distinguishes the propositions that carry the system from the ones that do not. It also states the reason the ordinary man cannot check the charged things against each other: if a thief is not found out his charm was strong, and if he is caught the other man's was stronger."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:KongoNkisi .

pmc:WhatMelanIsDisputed a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der mélan der Fang-Einweihung Iboga ist, und ob die Fang die Gesellschaft überhaupt hatten"@de,
        "Whether the mélan of the Fang initiation is iboga, and whether the Fang had the society at all"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Günter Tessmann, Die Pangwe (Berlin: Ernst Wasmuth A.-G., 1913), vol. II, pp. 241-242",
        "H. Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan (Münster i. W.: Aschendorffsche Verlagsbuchhandlung, 1912), p. 271, footnote 1, with the identity of Biéri and Bwiti asserted at pp. 47 and 49" ;
    skos:definition "A disagreement between two monographs on the same people published one year apart. One says that two species furnish the mélan, that the first ordeals use the bark of Tabernanthe iboga and the second degree that of Erythrophlœum guineense, and that the Fang Biéri is the same thing as the Fiote Bwiti. The other, from a resident expedition of 1907 to 1909 with earlier work from 1904, names Erythrophlœum guineense repeatedly under the Pangwe name elün, calls it the poison tree, and gives it a use that is not an initiation at all: the custom of the voluntary proof of truth without invocation of any moral power, in which a person who believes himself falsely accused chews the poisonous bark with water three times and publicly declares his innocence, the opinion being that in real innocence the poison is vomited up again, and otherwise stays in the body and acts fatally. In two volumes of that monograph the words iboga, eboga, bwiti, bouiti and bweti do not occur."@en ;
    skos:note "The ordeal claim in the second work is of the form this project values most and it does not belong to this tradition. Innocence is to produce vomiting and guilt death, which is a stated criterion with two observable outcomes and the penalty on the person; the recorder adds, as his own explanation and not as the transmission's, that people who believe the accusation drive the accused into the state they call olü'n until he either kills himself or ends the talk by undertaking the proof. The ordeal is not an initiation and falls outside the registered definition of this tradition, so it is carried here only as the second side of the dispute, and the question whether a Fang tradition of its own is to be registered goes to the human with a recommendation."@en,
        "Three readings are open and the holding decides none of them. That the Fang branch of the transmission is later than 1913, which is what the second monograph would imply and which would make the first recorder's identification of Biéri with Bwiti a claim about the Fiote projected onto the Fang. That the first recorder had the plant wrong and the mélan of the Fang is the Erythrophlœum alone, in which case his two species are one species with a European identification attached. Or that the second recorder's informants withheld it, which his own method makes harder to assert than it would be of a traveller. What would decide it is the work this file leaves unopened."@en,
        "What is at issue and what is not. Both works agree that Erythrophlœum guineense is in Fang hands and is deadly. They disagree about what the first ordeals of the initiation use, about whether the people had a society of this name, and about what the plant is for: a graded initiation in one, a judicial ordeal undertaken by the accused in the other. Neither contradicts the other on the Bouiti of Sette Cama, which lies outside the second recorder's region."@en,
        "Why silence counts here and not at pmc:BouitiSuspensionAboveTheGround. There the recorder gave a reason for his own ignorance that is a property of access - he was never admitted. Here the second recorder was admitted to a great deal, gives the men's cults of the people by name over many pages, lists the plants of the region with their Pangwe names and their Linnean ones, and names this plant eleven times; a transmission he does not mention under any of its five spellings is not a transmission he was kept away from. The silence is therefore a finding about the corpus and is recorded as such at pmp:PangweIbogaSearch."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:FangMelanIsTwoPlantsUnderOneName ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DiePangwe,
        pmw:LeTotemismeChezLesFan .

pmc:WirazReturn a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Rückkehr des Reisenden am siebten Tag"@de,
        "The return of the traveller on the seventh day"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The Book of Arda Viraf, ed. and trans. Martin Haug, E. W. West and Hoshang Jamaspji Asa (Bombay and London: Trübner, 1872), 3.2-3 and 3.15-26" ;
    skos:definition "The outcome the undertaking names. On the seventh day the traveller rises, and the transmission compares it to rising from a pleasant sleep. He asks first for food and drink, and after that for a writer who is wise and learned."@en ;
    skos:note "The order of the two requests is worth keeping: the body first, the record second. The writing down is fixed at the moment of return and not at the end of the vision, which makes the report a part of the procedure rather than a later literary act."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Zoroastrian .

pmc:WordsAtBirthYieldReturnAfterDeath a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die bei der Geburt gesprochenen Worte sollen das Wiederaufleben ermöglichen"@de,
        "Words spoken at birth are said to make the return after death possible"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 28" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that those at whose birth such words were uttered can, if they die, come to life again and return among people."@en ;
    skos:note "The interval between the act and its effect is a whole life, which puts the claim beyond any protocol that could be run on it and is worth recording as a property of the claim rather than as a defect of the record."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:InuitWordsSpokenAtBirth ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InuitAngakkuq ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:InuitReturnAfterDeath .

pmc:WorldPathTraversal a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Durchquerung des Weltenpfads"@de,
        "The traversal of the path of worlds"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Vijñānabhairava 56 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)",
        "Vijñānabhairava 57 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)" ;
    skos:definition "The whole is contemplated in the form of the path of worlds and the other paths, in sequence, in its coarse, subtle and supreme condition, until the mind dissolves; and the same method carried to the limits of the universe is said to bring the great awakening."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the old form of the claim that attention placed in a centre opens an assigned layer. The layers here are worlds, bhuvana, and they belong to a cosmology written into the body, not to a list of psychological qualities. The distance between the two is the whole point of the mapping node above. See prima-materia#39."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmc:WorldlyPowersOfTheLeftHandRite a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die weltlichen Vermögen, die der heterodoxe Ritus verleihen soll"@de,
        "The worldly powers the heterodox rite is said to confer"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Kālikāpurāṇa, at the verses on the fruits of the rite (135b-138a) (text, introduction and translation in English by B. N. Shastri, part III, Nag Publishers, Delhi 1991)" ;
    skos:definition "A set of capacities named as the fruit of the rite: happiness and prosperity, standing everywhere, a body resembling Madana, the subduing of kings together with their kingdoms and subjects, the drawing of all women, the subduing of lions, tigers, hyenas, bhūtas, pretas and piśācas, and movement everywhere like the wind."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as one set and not split into eight nodes, because the source gives them as a single list in one place and attaches no separate condition to any of them. Splitting would make the graph report eight claims where the text makes one."@en,
        "They are worldly and immediate, and that is the contrast the same passage draws. The orthodox side is not said to confer them; it is said to clear the debts and to be rewarded in due course. The difference between the two methods is stated here as a difference of what is obtained and when, not as a difference of moral standing. That is a fourth criterion beside rank, competence and deity, and it comes closer than any of them to what the Western pair was later made to carry — without ever carrying it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmc:WritingWithInk a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Schreiben mit Tinte"@de,
        "Writing with ink"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 69:9 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "The art of fixing speech in ink on paper, treated in the text as a transgression rather than a gift."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:WuBukezang a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die fünf Berge, auf denen nicht bestattet werden darf"@de,
        "The five mountains on which one may not bury"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zangshu, inner section, in the recension of Wu Cheng, one juan, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text" ;
    skos:definition "Five prohibitions, each stated as a consequence of a premise the book has already laid down. Because the qi comes about through the harmonious generation of things, a bald mountain may not be buried on. Because the qi comes by way of the form, a broken mountain may not be buried on. Because the qi travels by way of the earth, a stone mountain may not be buried on. Because the qi is stopped by the configuration, a mountain that merely passes through may not be buried on. Because the qi gathers where the dragons meet, a solitary mountain may not be buried on. The classic is then quoted for the conclusion: bald, broken, stone, passing and solitary generate new misfortune and extinguish the fortune already there."@en ;
    skos:note "The closing formula is stronger than a warning and is why this is entered as one. It does not say that such a burial fails to help; it says that it generates new misfortune and cancels fortune the family already had, so the claim is that the operation done wrongly is worse than not done."@en,
        "The five are the only prohibitions in this book whose ground is stated, and each ground is a clause of the doctrine rather than a rule of its own. That makes them the place where the transmission is most nearly deductive: given what it has said the qi is, these five follow, and a reader who accepted the premises and rejected a prohibition would be contradicting himself and not merely disagreeing."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Zang ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination .

pmc:WucijinQizhuang a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die sieben Zustände, in denen nicht genadelt wird"@de,
        "The seven states in which one does not needle"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 52 (Ci jin lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 14" ;
    skos:definition "Seven conditions of the person are named in which the needle is not to be used, and the first two carry their consequence with them: do not needle one greatly drunk, it makes the qi disordered; do not needle one greatly angered, it makes the qi go against its course; do not needle one greatly exhausted, nor one newly filled with food, nor one greatly hungry, nor one greatly thirsty, nor one greatly startled."@en ;
    skos:note "The consequence is stated for the first two and withheld for the remaining five, which the file keeps as it stands rather than extending the pattern. Where a later chapter of the same corpus states consequences for the whole list, that is a second and differently worded passage and it is entered separately."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Cizhen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmc:Wujin a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die fünf Verbote nach dem Tagesstamm"@de,
        "The five prohibitions bound to the day stem"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 61 (Wu jin), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 9" ;
    skos:definition "A second system of prohibitions, independent of the one in Suwen 52 and bound not to a state of the person but to the day. On jia and yi days, when they mount themselves, do not needle the head and do not open the darkness at the inner ear; on bing and ding days do not shake the dust at shoulder, throat and lianquan; on wu and ji days, when they mount the four seasons, do not needle the belly and do not remove the claw to drain the water; on geng and xin days do not needle the joints at thigh and knee; on ren and gui days do not needle foot and shin. The chapter calls these the five prohibitions and opens by defining a prohibition as forbidding what may not be needled."@en ;
    skos:note "The reason for entering this rather than the many other prohibitions of the corpus. Every other prohibition read here attaches to something about the person before the needle — drunk, angry, exhausted, newly fed, the spirit not present. This one attaches to the date and to nothing else, and the date is fixed by a calendar that stands before the case and outside the operator's judgement. That makes it the one prohibition in this corpus a third party could check without examining anybody."@en,
        "What it does not carry is a consequence. The chapter names the days and the places and stops; it does not say what happens to one needled at the head on a jia day. The named operations it forbids are the ones the corpus elsewhere calls the five sections, so the prohibition is stated over a technical vocabulary and not over the body's parts alone."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Cizhen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmc:WulaoSuoshang a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die fünf Erschöpfungen und was jede schädigt"@de,
        "The five exhaustions and what each injures"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 78 (Jiu zhen lun), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 12",
        "Huangdi neijing suwen 23 (Xuan ming wu qi), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 7" ;
    skos:definition "The corpus's one general statement that a thing done too long injures, with the injury named for each: looking long injures the blood, lying long injures the qi, sitting long injures the flesh, standing long injures the bone, walking long injures the sinews. The Suwen closes the list by naming it the five injuries of exhaustion; the Lingshu prints the same five clauses and calls them the illnesses of the five long exhaustions."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as the answer this corpus gives to a question the graph asks of many transmissions: whether the excess of a practice is said to harm and whether a measure is prescribed. The answer here is general in form and has a stated consequence for each case, which is more than most. What it does not say is that the excess of a prescribed practice harms; the five things it names are ordinary postures and not techniques, and the corpus's rule about its own operation is the bare clause about measure entered beside this."@en,
        "The doublet is not a variant. Both classics carry the same five clauses with the same organs and the same order, one under the heading of the five exhaustions and one in the chapter on the nine needles; the wording of the closing formula differs and the list does not."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ZhidaoYangsheng ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmc:XibalbaSurvival a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das lebende Verlassen der Häuser von Xibalbá"@de,
        "Coming out of the houses of Xibalba alive"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Popol Vuh: The Sacred Book of the Ancient Quiché Maya, English version by Delia Goetz and Sylvanus G. Morley from the Spanish translation of Adrián Recinos (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1950), part II, chapter 9" ;
    skos:definition "The outcome the ordeals decide. The transmission treats it as the thing at stake in each house and reports both outcomes for named persons rather than only the successful one."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MayaRitual .

pmc:XingqiCapacities a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Was die Zirkulation des Qi vermögen soll"@de,
        "What the circulation of the qi is said to accomplish"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 8 (Shizhi), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 8" ;
    skos:definition "A list given in one breath: circulating the qi may cure the hundred illnesses, may take one into a place of pestilence, may hold off snakes and tigers, may stop the blood of a wound, may let one stay in water, may let one go on water, may keep off hunger and thirst, and may lengthen the years."@en ;
    skos:note "Eight items, of which the middle ones are decidable and the outer ones are not. Staying in water, going on water and holding off a tiger are outcomes a witness could report; curing the hundred illnesses and lengthening the years are not, in this form. The list is recorded whole rather than filtered, because a transmission that puts both kinds in one sentence is telling something about itself."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BaopuziNeipian ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:XingqiDepth a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Tiefe als erste Stufe"@de,
        "Depth as the first stage"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Xingqi yupei ming, in D. Harper, Early Chinese Medical Literature (1998), p. 125" ;
    skos:definition "The opening stage of the inscription: the breath is taken deeply, whereupon it gathers."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XingqiYupeiMing ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:XingqiProhibitions a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Nicht viel essen, und nicht zürnen"@de,
        "Not much food, and no anger"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 8 (Shizhi), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 8" ;
    skos:definition "Two prohibitions with their stated consequences. One is not to eat much, and not to eat raw vegetables or fat and fresh things, because they make a man's qi strong and hard to close off. And anger is forbidden: much anger disorders the qi, and since it then cannot flow over, it may set a man coughing. The chapter adds that few can do the thing at all, because men are by nature restless and few can be still enough to cultivate the way."@en ;
    skos:note "Both consequences are physical and immediate and neither is a misfortune sent from outside, which distinguishes these from most of the prohibitions this graph holds. The second also names a mechanism: anger does not spoil the practice directly, it makes the qi unable to overflow, and the cough is what follows from that."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:XingqiBaopuzi ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BaopuziNeipian ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:XingqiYieldsCapacities a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Zirkulation des Qi bringt jene Vermögen hervor"@de,
        "Circulating the qi yields those capacities"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 8 (Shizhi), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 8" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:XingqiBaopuzi ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BaopuziNeipian ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:XingqiCapacities .

pmc:XuZhu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Anblasen von Wasser, Feuer, Tieren und Wunden"@de,
        "Blowing on water, fire, beasts and wounds"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 8 (Shizhi), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 8" ;
    skos:definition "What one who is good at using the qi is said to do. He blows at water and the water runs backwards several paces; he blows at fire and the fire goes out; he blows at a tiger or a wolf and it crouches and cannot rise; he blows at a snake or a viper and it coils and cannot go. If another has been wounded by a blade, he blows at it and the blood stops at once. Where he hears that someone has been struck by a venomous creature, he blows and incants at a distance without seeing the person, over his own hand, the left for a man and the right for a woman, and though the other be a hundred li away he is healed on the spot. And for a sudden seizure it is enough to swallow the qi of the three nines, and it passes at once."@en ;
    skos:note "The distant healing is stated with a distance and an immediacy, a hundred li and on the spot, and with no condition attached to the patient. That makes it the most examinable claim in the chapter and the one furthest from anything the chapter offers to check it with."@en,
        "The laterality is the part this graph should not lose: the practitioner blows over his own left hand for a male patient and his own right for a female. A rule of that shape is not a description of an experience but an instruction that can be got wrong, and it is the kind of detail a later station adds rather than invents."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BaopuziNeipian ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:YinjunDexian a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Gelehrte aus Xinye, der es durch dieses Elixier erlangt haben soll"@de,
        "The scholar of Xinye who is said to have got it by this elixir"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 4 (Jindan), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 4" ;
    skos:definition "The chapter names one man. Lord Yin of Xinye, in recent times at the end of the Han, obtained immortality by this Great Clarity elixir. He was by origin a Confucian scholar, of talent and reflection, and wrote poems and a eulogy on the elixir scriptures with a preface, setting out the beginning and end of his own study under his teacher and listing the men he knew of who had obtained immortality, more than forty of them, very clearly."@en ;
    skos:note "The one detail that would count differently is that the man is said to have written down the beginning and end of his own study under a named teacher. An account of an operation by the person who ran it is the thing this collection everywhere lacks, and this node records that such a document is named as having existed."@en,
        "This is the whole of the evidence the chapter offers that anybody ever succeeded, and its shape is worth keeping. The immortality of the one man is asserted; the forty are a list he is said to have made, reported at second hand by an author who did not perform the operation himself. What is offered as confirmation is therefore a document about a document, and the chapter presents it as very clear."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:JindanCapacities ;
    pm:ascribedTo "Lord Yin of Xinye, a Confucian scholar of the end of the Han" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Waidan .

pmc:YinshuChangshou a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Dadurch kann man lange leben"@de,
        "By this you may live a long life"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhangjiashan Yinshu, slip 2, in Vivienne Lo (trans.), How to do the Gibbon Walk: A Translation of the Pulling Book (ca. 186 BCE), Needham Research Institute Working Papers 3 (Cambridge: Needham Research Institute, 2014)" ;
    skos:definition "The outcome the first text states of the morning order, once, in the spring paragraph and not repeated in the other three: go to meet the purity of the early morning dew, receive the quintessence of heaven, and after drink one cup of water, and in this way you may live a long life."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Yangsheng .

pmc:YinshuHanChu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Bis der Schweiß aus den Mustern der Haut tritt"@de,
        "Until the sweat comes out of the patterns of the skin"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhangjiashan Yinshu, slips 31 to 32 and slips 36 to 37, in Vivienne Lo (trans.), How to do the Gibbon Walk: A Translation of the Pulling Book (ca. 186 BCE), Needham Research Institute Working Papers 3 (Cambridge: Needham Research Institute, 2014)" ;
    skos:definition "The one stopping rule in the work that is set by something perceived rather than by a count. In the treatment for a neck that hurts and cannot be turned, after the counted part is done the patient covers the mouth forcibly and does not breathe, and after a while sweat comes out of the patterns of the skin; the instruction is then to do it as long as possible and stop. Two further outcomes are stated of another treatment in the same terms, as things that will be the case when the qi of the head flows downward: the foot will not be immobile and numb, the head will not swell, and the nose will not be stuffed up."@en ;
    skos:note "A sign of success and an observable one, which the harvest counts separately and which this holding has often had to record as absent. It is worth noticing what kind of sign it is. Sweat under held breath is a thing the body does, so the sign is available to anyone who performs the procedure correctly and says nothing about whether the complaint is gone. The corpus never names a perceptible mark of the cure itself."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Yangsheng .

pmc:YinshuQiXiaXing a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Qi des Kopfes geht abwärts, und die Haltung schickt es"@de,
        "The qi of the head goes downward, and the posture is what sends it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhangjiashan Yinshu, slips 36 to 37, in Vivienne Lo (trans.), How to do the Gibbon Walk: A Translation of the Pulling Book (ca. 186 BCE), Needham Research Institute Working Papers 3 (Cambridge: Needham Research Institute, 2014)" ;
    skos:definition "The one place in the work that states a direction taken by the qi inside the body and the outcome of its taking it. The patient grasps a staff in the right hand, faces a wall and does not breathe; with the left foot he treads on the wall and rests when tired, then the same with the hands and feet exchanged. When the qi of the head flows downwards, the foot will not be immobile and numb, the head will not swell, and the nose will not be stuffed up. Whenever there is free time, it adds, practise it frequently."@en ;
    skos:note "The direction is not carried by a phase of the breath. The breath is held throughout, and what is varied is the posture, the wall, the staff and the side. The claim in convergences/breath-direction.ttl is that direction inside the body is assigned to a graded course and not to inhaling and exhaling, and this passage assigns it to a posture instead, which is the same denial from a third corpus and an early one."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Yangsheng .

pmc:YinshuShanYu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wofür jede Bewegung gut ist"@de,
        "What each movement is good for"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhangjiashan Yinshu, slips 99 to 102, in Vivienne Lo (trans.), How to do the Gibbon Walk: A Translation of the Pulling Book (ca. 186 BCE), Needham Research Institute Working Papers 3 (Cambridge: Needham Research Institute, 2014)" ;
    skos:definition "The catalogue that closes the second text, in which some twenty of the named exercises are each assigned a part of the body: holding the breath is good for the coital sinew; Praying Mantis for the constant channel; Snake Shake for the great brain; Wild Duck Bathing for the head; Encircling Channels for the patterns of the skin and for the heels and head; Follow to the Side for the ear; Looking in a Yang direction for the eyes; looking up with the mouth open for the nose; roaring without emitting a sound for the mouth; stroking the heart and raising the chin for the throat and gullet; Owl Shivers for the neck; Tiger Looks Back for the neck and buttocks; Pull the Back for the shoulder muscles; Limbs Falling for the armpits; Chicken Stretch for the shoulders and arms; Backward Waving for the abdomen and heart; Backwards Swirling for the two ribs; Bear Warp for the muscles of the back; Repeating Holds for the small of the back; the Pace of Yu for between the thighs; Forward Stumble for the thighs and knees; Rotating the Ankles for the sole of the foot; Squeezing the Toes for the qi of the feet; Knocking the Heels for the chest. For these, it says, stop after three times."@en ;
    skos:note "This is a capacity catalogue in the strict sense the harvest asks for: it is closed, it is stated by the transmission itself rather than read off its practice, and every item has the same form. What it does not contain is as uniform as what it does. No item names a faculty, a perception, a power over anything outside the practitioner's own body, or an outcome beyond the part treated. A transmission that lists twenty-four capacities and lists no capacity of that other kind has stated a boundary, and it is the boundary that makes the corpus worth the independence question."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Yangsheng .

pmc:YinshuWuBing a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Und deshalb wird es keine Krankheit geben"@de,
        "And therefore there will be no illness"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhangjiashan Yinshu, slips 105 and 108, in Vivienne Lo (trans.), How to do the Gibbon Walk: A Translation of the Pulling Book (ca. 186 BCE), Needham Research Institute Working Papers 3 (Cambridge: Needham Research Institute, 2014)" ;
    skos:definition "The outcome the third text states of the pull of the Eight Warps, in one clause and without a term or a qualification: by these means seek conformity with heaven and earth, and therefore there will be no illness. The same claim is made a second time of the breath alone: if they breathe in the quintessential qi of heaven and earth to make the Yin substantial, then they will be able to avoid illness."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Yangsheng .

pmc:YiyaoBaizhe a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Die sieben Männer, die das Mittel zugrunde richtete"@de,
        "The seven men ruined by the drug"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Han Yu, epitaph for the late Erudite of the Grand Academy Li, in Wubaijia zhu Changli wenji, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text, juan 34" ;
    skos:definition "A case series assembled in 823 against the claim that mineral elixirs confer deathlessness, and stated as such. The author writes that he does not know from what age the doctrine of taking these things arose, that the men it has killed cannot be counted, and that the world's longing for it only grows. He then states his inclusion rule: what is recorded in documents and what is heard at second hand he passes over, and takes only those he saw with his own eyes and personally associated with and who were ruined by the drug, six or seven gentlemen, to serve as a warning to the age. He names seven, all of them men of rank whom the world knows. The Erudite for whom the epitaph is written met a fangshi while serving in the southeast, received the drug method, took it, passed blood repeatedly, and after four years, the illness growing acute, died at forty-eight; the method is given as a cauldron filled with lead, hollowed in the middle and filled with mercury, sealed at the four edges and fired to make cinnabar. Of the seven: the Minister of Works, having eaten mercury, fell ill and described it himself as a burning iron staff driven from the crown down through him, breaking into fire and shooting out at the apertures and joints, so that he howled with mad pain and begged to be let die; mercury was found on his bedding, and when it broke out the pain stopped; he spat blood for more than ten years and so perished. The Palace Censor died of an abscess that broke out on his back. The Minister of Justice, dying, said to the author: I was misled by the drug. That man's younger brother, a Vice-Minister, died in a single morning with no illness. The commissioner of Xiangyang met the author by boat, sent the others away, said that he had obtained a secret drug and could not alone escape death, and left him a vessel of it to be taken as pills with jujube flesh; a year later he was ill, and someone from his household reported that the drug he had taken before was the wrong one, that he was about to purge it, and that when purged he would be well; he was ill two years and died. The Censor-in-chief of Dongchuan, dying, passed blood and flesh with his urine, in pain past bearing, and begged for death. The General of the Golden Guard ate the same fangshi's drug and died in exile by the sea."@en ;
    skos:note "The author says six or seven and names seven. The approximation is left as he wrote it rather than corrected to the count, because a series whose author was not sure of his own number is reporting from memory and not from a list, which is a fact about the series."@en,
        "The edition's commentary carries the case that would have counted most and that the epitaph itself leaves unsaid. The fangshi who supplied the drug was recommended to the reigning emperor through a favourite; he prepared the cinnabar preparation; the emperor took it and, in the commentary's phrase, suddenly abandoned the myriad states; on the next reign the fangshi was handed to the metropolitan prefecture and put to death, and the man who had recommended him was demoted and died of the drug in his place of exile. That the ruling house both took the preparation and executed its maker is the sharpest thing in the apparatus, and it is in the apparatus and not in the text."@en,
        "The inclusion rule is the reason this is a protocol and not a polemic. It excludes exactly the two classes of report that make a case series worthless: what stands written elsewhere, and what the author was told. Nothing in this collection, ancient or modern, states a cleaner criterion, and it is stated in the ninth century by a man writing a funeral inscription."@en,
        "What the series can and cannot settle. It reports seven men who took mineral preparations and died, four of them with a course of illness that the report ties to the taking, and two with symptoms that a modern reader will recognise without the graph needing to say so. It does not establish a rate, because the denominator is missing: how many took such preparations and did not die of them is not asked, and the author's own selection is of the ruined. What it does establish is that the outcome the transmission excludes did occur, repeatedly, in men the author knew."@en ;
    pm:baseRate "The span and manner of death of men of the same rank and period who did not take mineral preparations, which this series does not supply." ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Carried on 2026-09-10 over the two corpora this file rests on and recorded as a node at pmc:FushiSuccessSearch. Two things came back. In the transmission itself, one success is claimed: the fourth chapter of the Baopuzi names a scholar of the end of the Han said to have become an immortal by the Great Clarity elixir and to have listed more than forty others, which this graph holds at pmc:YinjunDexian and which is second-hand by its own account. In the witness's own collected works, a poem to a friend says that since their parting he knows the friend has had the golden elixir transmitted to him, and begs a knife-point of it to save his sick body; the edition's commentators argue over whether that shows the author of this series took mineral drugs himself. Both findings weaken the series, the first by supplying a claimed positive case and the second by unsettling the witness, and both are recorded rather than set aside."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:examinedBy "Han Yu, in the epitaph for the late Erudite of the Grand Academy Li, written in 823; the protocol is his and not the compiler's" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "Men who take the mineral preparations live at or beyond the ordinary span and die of causes unconnected with the taking, with no course of illness following it." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-10" ;
    pm:tests pmc:JindanYieldsCapacities ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Waidan .

pmc:ZaisanDu a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Zwei- und dreimal fragen entweiht, und entweiht antwortet es nicht"@de,
        "Asking twice and three times defiles, and defiled it does not tell"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhouyi, hexagram 4, the judgement and the commentary on the judgement, in the Wang Bi and Han Kangbo recension; read in the digital text of the Kanseki Repository, which names no printing" ;
    skos:definition "The judgement on the fourth hexagram states the only condition the canon attaches to consulting: at the first consultation it tells; asked twice and three times, that is defilement, and defiled it does not tell. The commentary on the judgement repeats the words and glosses the defilement as defiling the ignorance itself."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as a caution and not as a prerequisite because nothing is said to be reached or missed. The consequence is stated, unusually for this corpus, and the file records it in the definition for want of a predicate; see prima-materia#621."@en,
        "This is the node that decides how far this transmission can be examined at all, and it is worth having precisely because it is not a procedural rule. Nothing here says that a second casting is void, or wrong, or that the operator has erred. It says that repeating the question is an offence and that the answer is then withheld. The obvious test of any oracle is to put the same question twice and compare; the canon forbids exactly that and attaches no cost to the doctrine when the second answer differs, because by its own rule there should not be a second answer."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Dieshi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination .

pmc:ZangYieldsYin a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Bestattung auf dem lebendigen Qi bringt die Beschirmung der Nachkommen hervor"@de,
        "Burial on the living qi yields the shading of the descendants"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zangshu, inner section, in the recension of Wu Cheng, one juan, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Zang ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:YitiShouyin .

pmc:ZeriDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der gewählte Tag über irgendetwas entscheidet"@de,
        "Whether the day chosen decides anything"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Wang Chong, Lunheng, the treatise on censuring days (Ji ri), juan 24, Sibu congkan facsimile",
        "Wang Chong, Lunheng, the treatise on interrogating the art (Jie shu), juan 25, Sibu congkan facsimile",
        "Wang Chong, Lunheng, the treatise on objecting to the year star (Nan sui), juan 24, Sibu congkan facsimile",
        "Wang Chong, Lunheng, the treatise on the four taboos (Si hui), juan 23, Sibu congkan facsimile",
        "Zangshu, miscellaneous section, in the recension of Wu Cheng, one juan, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text" ;
    skos:definition "A treatise of the first century attacks the choosing of days across four chapters, and the arguments are of three kinds. First, comparisons within the practice: encoffining, which puts the corpse in the box, is done on no chosen day, while burial, which puts the box in the ground, is; and grave, field and dwelling are equally the ground of a man's fortune, yet only the westward extension of a dwelling is forbidden. Second, a count: in the age of the annals the deaths of kings, lords and great officers ran to hundreds and thousands, and their burial days do not agree with the calendars; the rites fix the interval by rank, so that the parity of the month follows from the rank and not from a choice; and where a burial was postponed the annals give rain as the reason and not a day. Third, a case in which one day carries two outcomes: King Wu won his battle on a jiazi day and the tyrant lost his on the same jiazi day, the two armies facing one another with their standards in sight, one day and two fates. The same treatise adds that washing, combing, cutting cloth, building and moving house each have their own calendar, that water and wood are both of the five phases yet only the one is subject to a day, and that a man who kills is punished whatever day he chose."@en ;
    skos:note "It is a party and not an instance, and the difference matters for the claim it disputes. The treatise argues; it does not count anything it gathered itself, except the burials in the annals, and that count is a reading of a text and not a series of cases. Where it counts, it is evidence and belongs at a protocol; where it argues, it is a witness like the book it attacks, and that is how it is held here."@en,
        "The dispute has a shape worth noting beside the burial book's own. The critic never says that the ground or the hour has no effect and then names what would show it; he shows that the rule is not followed where it would be if it were believed. That leaves the doctrine intact for anyone willing to say that his contemporaries were careless, which is the answer the burial book's own annotation gives in a different connection."@en,
        "The third argument is the one this graph should keep, because it is the only one that discriminates rather than compares. Two parties, one date, opposite outcomes: whatever the day is supposed to decide, it did not decide that. The others show that the practice is applied inconsistently, which embarrasses the practitioners without touching the doctrine."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:SanjiLiuxiong ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination .

pmc:Ziqel a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Ziqel"@de,
        "Ziqel"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)" ;
    skos:definition "A Watcher named in the Aramaic-Greek teaching list as the one who disclosed the signs of the shooting stars. The position he occupies is held by Ezeqeel in the Ethiopic; no identification of the two is asserted here."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:nameForm "Ziqel" ;
    pm:nameRole "watcher" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:ZosimosCautioInPerfectione a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Wenn du erkennst, dass du zur Vollendung gelangt bist, dann fürchte die Elemente der Materie"@de,
        "When you know you have reached perfection, then fear the elements of matter"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zosime, Compte final, § 8, französische Übersetzung in M. Berthelot und Ch.-Em. Ruelle, Collection des anciens alchimistes grecs, seconde livraison, Paris 1888, S. 193" ;
    skos:definition "The warning that closes the prescription, and it is placed at its point of success: but when you recognise that you have arrived at perfection, then fear the intervention of the natural elements of matter; descending towards the Shepherd and plunging into meditation, ascend thus to your origin."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also the one place in this corpus where a stated attainment is made into a danger rather than an end. What it prescribes against the danger is a descent and a meditation, and the thing descended to is named and not described; the letter has used the same name nowhere else in what was read."@en,
        "The warning stands inside the prescribing passage, in the same letter, in the sentence after the one that says what the practice obtains, and it warns against the very material the practice was for. It is not supplied by a later commentator and not by an outside critic, and there is no other passage in what was read that could be taken for its source."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ZosimosSedeAdFocum ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LogosTeleutaios ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GraecoEgyptianAlchemy .

pmc:ZosimosFortunaFavens a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Glück dir günstig ist"@de,
        "Whether fortune is favourable to you"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zosime, französische Übersetzung in M. Berthelot und Ch.-Em. Ruelle, Collection des anciens alchimistes grecs, seconde livraison, Paris 1888, S. 172" ;
    skos:definition "The clause in which the letter states what the attempt is for: the exposition has as its purpose to engage the addressee to try whether fortune is favourable to her. The wording admits two readings and the witness does not decide between them — that she should try, if fortune is favourable, or that the trying is itself the way of finding out whether fortune is favourable. On either reading the outcome of a single attempt is placed in fortune."@en ;
    skos:note "Nothing else in what was read of this corpus states a condition of failure at all. The search is at pmp:ZosimosDefectusSearch, and this node is what it returned in place of what it was looking for."@en,
        "This is what a run that produced nothing is assigned to in this corpus, and it is worth naming exactly because it is so cheap. Fortune is not a state of the operator, not a state of the material, not a day and not a third party; nothing establishes it in advance and nothing can be shown to have been absent. A transmission that puts its own failures there has stated a condition that no outcome can contradict."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Cheirokmeta ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GraecoEgyptianAlchemy .

pmc:ZosimosTincturaeNaturales a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Tinkturen, die schicklich, echt und natürlich sind"@de,
        "The tinctures that are fitting, authentic and natural"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zosime, Compte final, § 8, französische Übersetzung in M. Berthelot und Ch.-Em. Ruelle, Collection des anciens alchimistes grecs, seconde livraison, Paris 1888, S. 193" ;
    skos:definition "What the letter says the prescribed conduct obtains: operating thus, you will obtain the tinctures that are fitting, authentic and natural. The same letter has already distinguished those from the colours fabricated by supernatural means, which the priests of the temples produced and sold in return for offerings."@en ;
    skos:note "A material result made to depend on a course of conduct, and the letter states it without a bridge. Between resting the body and obtaining a tincture there is one clause, in operating thus, and nothing else: no apparatus, no substance, no interval. That is the whole of the mechanism this transmission gives at this place."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LogosTeleutaios ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GraecoEgyptianAlchemy .

pmc:ZuniPrayerEfficacy a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Wirksamkeit des Zuni-Gebets"@de,
        "The efficacy of the Zuni prayer"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ruth L. Bunzel, Zuni Ritual Poetry, in Forty-Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of American Ethnology 1929-1930 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1932), p. 615" ;
    skos:definition "The effect the fixed prayer is held to obtain when it is delivered as required."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ZuniRitual .

pmm:HotHerbsCalledSunwort a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Die scharfen Kräuter, die man Sonnenkraut nennt"@de,
        "The hot herbs called sunwort"@en ;
    skos:note "Named in the love-charm beside a further plant and peppers. The editor's rendering of the plant-name carries his question mark, and the words on either side of it are broken."@en .

pm:Symbolizing a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Symbolisieren"@de,
        "Symbolizing"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "The act of one form standing for another within awareness."@en .

pm:agencyDeclined a pm:AgencyHolding,
        pm:AwarenessValue ;
    rdfs:label "Trägerschaftsunterscheidung abgelehnt"@de,
        "Agency distinction declined"@en ;
    skos:definition "The tradition holds that the distinction between acting and being acted through does not apply to what it teaches."@en .

pm:casesWithoutDeviation a pm:ExaminationState ;
    rdfs:label "Fälle ohne Abweichung"@de,
        "Cases without deviation"@en ;
    skos:definition "Cases have been recorded and show no deviation from the stated base rate."@en .

pm:conjugalTelos a pm:TelicOrientation ;
    rdfs:label "Bräutliches Ziel"@de,
        "Conjugal telos"@en ;
    skos:definition "The path claims that the bounded self is neither dissolved nor kept apart but carried into a union of two that preserves both parties to it, so that what is transformed persists as one side of a relation rather than as an absolute or as nothing."@en ;
    skos:note "Distinct from pm:separativeTelos for the same reason from the other side: what persists here is not its own absolute but a party to a bond, and it is transformed on the way rather than fortified."@en,
        "Distinct from pm:unitiveTelos, and the distinction is not verbal. A dissolution has one term at the end; a marriage has two. A record that files a bridal end state as unitive reports the disappearance of something the source keeps."@en .

pm:corpusHarvested a pm:CoverageState ;
    rdfs:label "Korpus geerntet"@de,
        "Corpus harvested"@en ;
    skos:definition "The harvest has been carried out for the works actually opened, and what was left unread is recorded. The state claims completeness for what was opened, never for the corpus."@en .

pm:dreamingWithin a pm:AwarenessValue,
        pm:SensoryAnchoring ;
    rdfs:label "Im Traum"@de,
        "Dreaming within"@en ;
    skos:definition "Awareness of objects generated inwardly, with the sensory channel detached. Whether the dreamer knows he dreams is a separate question and not this axis."@en ;
    skos:note "The Māṇḍūkya calls this svapna. Lucidity is deliberately not a value here: it varies within the state rather than replacing it, and a source that trains recognition inside the dream is placed here and says so in its own words."@en .

pm:durationNotStated a pm:AwarenessValue,
        pm:StateDuration ;
    rdfs:label "Dauer nicht angegeben"@de,
        "Duration not stated"@en ;
    skos:definition "The source was read for this question and does not answer it."@en .

pm:individuatingTelos a pm:TelicOrientation ;
    rdfs:label "Individuierendes Ziel"@de,
        "Individuating telos"@en ;
    skos:definition "The path claims that the creature's proper movement is to hold itself as itself against a dissolution that would end it, while denying that the movement is towards difference or towards becoming an absolute; what is sought is the creature's own being and not its distinction from others."@en ;
    skos:note "Between the two older values and reducible to neither. It refuses dissolution, so it is not unitive; and it refuses the striving after difference, so it is not separative. The distinction is fine and load-bearing: a scheme that reads every refusal of dissolution as self-deification cannot state it, and that misreading is common enough to be worth a term."@en .

pm:sourceModalityScale a pm:ScaleFamily ;
    rdfs:label "Quellenmodalitätsskala"@de,
        "Source modality scale"@en ;
    skos:definition "A scale that reports how firmly a source sets what it says. Its values are read off the wording of the source: they are neither a judgement about the subject matter nor a judgement about the source's reliability."@en .

pm:workOpened a pm:CoverageState ;
    rdfs:label "Werk geöffnet"@de,
        "Work opened"@en ;
    skos:definition "A work of the corpus has been obtained and read and its edition is settled, but no passage has yet been entered as a node."@en .

pmp:AegyptiaFibulaCarneoli a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Schnalle aus Karneol, in Anch-am-Blütenwasser eingelegt, am Hals"@de,
        "The buckle of carnelian steeped in water of ankham flowers, at the neck"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 156, rubric, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: An English Translation of the Chapters, Hymns, etc., of the Theban Recension, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II" ;
    skos:definition "Say over a buckle of carnelian which has been steeped in water of ankham flowers and set in a plinth of sycamore wood, and place it at the neck of the deceased on the day of the funeral."@en ;
    skos:note "It carries its own secrecy clause, and a narrower one than most: thou shalt not let any person who is with thee see it. Held with the others at pmc:AegyptiaSecretumOperis."@en,
        "The same preparation as the Tet of gold at chapter 155, with the stone changed. The corpus treats the treatment as transferable and the material as not."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmp:AegyptiaLaterOccidentalis a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Tet aus Kristall auf grünem Ziegel, in die Westwand vermauert, nach Osten gewandt"@de,
        "The Tet of crystal on a green brick, walled into the west wall facing east"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 137A, the first of the four appended brick rubrics, Papyrus of Nu, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: An English Translation of the Chapters, Hymns, etc., of the Theban Recension, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II" ;
    skos:definition "Recite over a Tet of crystal set up upon a brick made of green clay upon which this chapter has been inscribed. Make a cavity in the west wall, turn the front of the Tet towards the east within it, and wall it up with earth moistened with cedar juice. It will repulse the enemies of Osiris who set themselves at the east wall. Words are then given to be spoken."@en ;
    skos:note "One of four, and the four are the most exactly specified objects in the corpus. Each names a material, a support, an inscription, a wall, a facing, and the quarter whose enemies it turns back; the four together box the chamber. The other three are pmp:AegyptiaLaterOrientalis, pmp:AegyptiaLaterMeridionalis and pmp:AegyptiaLaterSeptentrionalis."@en,
        "The facing and the wall do not agree in the way a reader expects: the Tet goes into the west wall and faces east, and repulses those at the east wall. The rubric is consistent with itself and states the relation each time; whether the relation is the same for all four is a question this holding can now put to the four nodes rather than to the page."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmp:AegyptiaLaterSeptentrionalis a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der beschriftete Ziegel mit einer Palme von sieben Fingern Höhe, deren Mund geöffnet wird"@de,
        "The inscribed brick with a palm tree seven fingers high, its mouth opened"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 137A, the fourth of the four appended brick rubrics, Papyrus of Nu, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: An English Translation of the Chapters, Hymns, etc., of the Theban Recension, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II" ;
    skos:definition "Recite over a brick of green clay upon which the chapter has been inscribed, along with a figure of a palm tree seven fingers in height; and open its mouth. Make a cavity in the north wall, turn the face of the figure towards the south within it, and wall it up."@en ;
    skos:note "A measure in fingers, and a rite performed on a tree: the opening of the mouth, which the corpus elsewhere performs on a corpse, is here performed on a model palm."@en,
        "This rubric carries the purity condition for all four; it is held at pmc:AegyptiaPuritasOperantis."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmp:AegyptiaNavisNomina a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Boot herbeiführen: jedem seiner Teile den Namen sagen"@de,
        "Bringing along the boat: answering every part of it by name"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 99, Papyrus of Nu (British Museum No. 10,477, sheets 21 and 22), in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II, pp. 297-302" ;
    skos:definition "The operation of chapter 99. After an address to those who bring the boat over the back of Apep, the parts of the vessel put their question one after another, each in the same words — tell me my name, or tell us our names — and each is answered. The sequence runs through the wood, the rudder, the rope, the oar-rests, the hold, the mast, the lower deck, the upper post, the sail, the pieces of leather, the paddles, the matchabet, the planks of the hull, the bows, the hull, the rudder a second time, the keel, the sailor, the wind, the river, the river banks and the ground. A closing address to the divine beings with beautiful Kas is then recited before them."@en ;
    skos:note "Budge's vocabulary to the same recension confirms the reading of the chapter from outside it, and supplies the corpus's own term for the vessel. It carries several of these answers as headwords in their own right and glosses them as parts of what it calls the magical boat: Ari-nef-tchesef as a plank or peg in it, Arisi as the leather bands in it. So the answers are not epithets invented for the dialogue; they are the names the tackle is held to have."@en,
        "The examination is put by the equipment. The corpus applies to a rope and a plank the form it applies at the gate of the underworld, and it draws no distinction of kind between them: the keel asks in the same words as the doorkeeper and is answered out of the same book."@en,
        "The rudder asks twice and is answered differently each time — Leg of Hapiu at the first asking, Aqa, the true one, at the second. Nothing in the chapter marks the repetition as an error or as two different rudders."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmp:AegyptiaPeretEmHeru a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Herausgehen am Tage: das Rezitieren und das Wissen der Kapitel"@de,
        "Coming forth by day: the recitation and the knowing of the chapters"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 64, short version, rubric, Papyrus of Nu, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London 1901, vol. I, pp. 217-218" ;
    skos:definition "The operation the corpus is built around. Its own title for itself is the chapters of coming forth by day, and what it prescribes is of two kinds: the recitation of a named chapter, sometimes over a named object and at a named time, and the knowing of it. The two are not the same, and the rubrics keep them apart: some say that this chapter shall be recited over such and such a thing, and others that if this chapter be known by the deceased, such and such will follow."@en ;
    skos:note "The distinction is the reason this tradition was worth taking off the register. An operation that consists in knowing a text has no performance an onlooker could watch and no step that could be done wrongly in a way anyone could see. The corpus nevertheless states a failure condition for it, and states it in the same terms; see pmc:AegyptiaPortaeNomina."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmp:AegyptiaQuattuorVentorum a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die vier Winde auf den Sarg gemalt, je einem von vier Göttern zugeordnet"@de,
        "The four winds painted on the coffin, one to each of four gods"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 161, rubric, from the Saite recension as Budge prints it, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: An English Translation of the Chapters, Hymns, etc., of the Theban Recension, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. III" ;
    skos:definition "Every sahu for whom these divine figures have been painted upon his coffin shall make his way through these four entrances into heaven. That of the north wind belongs to Osiris, that of the south wind to Ra, that of the west wind to Isis, and that of the east wind to Nephthys. Each of these winds shall breathe into his nostrils as he enters in his daily course."@en ;
    skos:note "A complete assignment: four quarters, four gods, one each, with no remainder and no overlap. It is the tidiest scheme in the corpus and one of the very few that can be stated without loss as a table."@en,
        "It carries the strongest secrecy clause in the three volumes: let none who is outside know it, thou shalt not do this in the presence of any person except thy father or thy son, or thyself alone. Budge adds it from the Saite recension; it is not in his Theban base text. Held at pmc:AegyptiaSecretumOperis."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmp:AegyptiaScarabaeusCordis a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Herzskarabäus aus grünem Stein"@de,
        "The heart scarab of green stone"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 30B, rubric, Papyrus of Amen-hetep, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London 1901, vol. I, pp. 151-152" ;
    skos:definition "A scarab of green stone encircled with a band of refined copper and having a ring of silver, placed on the neck of the transfigured dead, over which the words of the chapter are said. The rubric of chapter 64 prescribes the same object in another form — a scarab of green stone washed with gold, placed in the heart of the man, which will perform for him the opening of the mouth, anointed with anti unguent and recited over with the words that follow."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmp:AegyptiaTabulaCibi a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Götter auf ein Brett gemalt, und die Speise vor ihnen, wenn Ra erscheint"@de,
        "The gods painted on a board, and the food set before them when Ra appears"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 148, rubric, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: An English Translation of the Chapters, Hymns, etc., of the Theban Recension, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II" ;
    skos:definition "These words shall be said when Ra appears, over figures of the gods written or painted in colour upon a board, with offerings and tchefau food placed before them: cakes, ale, flesh, feathered fowl and incense."@en ;
    skos:note "The rubric closes with a secrecy clause: thou shalt not recite this book of Un-nefer in the presence of any person except thine own self. It is one of four such clauses in the corpus and is held with them at pmc:AegyptiaSecretumOperis."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmp:Ailolo a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Ailolo"@de,
        "Ailolo"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Nathaniel B. Emerson, Unwritten Literature of Hawaii: The Sacred Songs of the Hula, Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 38 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1909), chapter V, pp. 31-35, and the glossary, s.v. ai-lolo" ;
    skos:definition "The graduation rite of the hula school, named after the brain of the victim that is eaten in it. The offering is a black suckling without spot or blemish; the parts consumed are the snout, the ear-tips, the tail, the feet and portions of the vital organs, above all the brain. Two foods are shut out of the feast, the cooked taro leaf and the drupe of the pandanus. In the days before it no pupil may leave the house except under the most stringent necessity, then only with the head muffled so as not to be recognised, and may hold no conversation whatever outside."@en ;
    skos:note "The rite decides admission: the glossary of the same work defines ailolo as a critical ceremonial sacrifice whose conditions must be met before a novice can be admitted as a practitioner. What the passages read do not give is a way of failing it that is stated in advance, which is why no protocol node stands beside this one."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HawaiianRitual .

pmp:ArsNotoriaOratioRepetita a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die ein-, zwei- oder dreimal wiederholte Oration"@de,
        "The oration repeated once, twice or three times"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), pp. 46-47, ed. Peterson" ;
    skos:definition "Pronouncing the oration on any day on which the speaker is not hindered by a criminal sin, with confession of heart and mouth preceding and early in the morning: three times if he desires to be made eloquent, once if an evil thing troubles him or he is involved in some great business, twice for the greater measure."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmp:AsipuMashultuppu a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der an den Leib des Kranken gebrachte Sündenbock"@de,
        "The scapegoat drawn to the patient's body"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Udug-hul 12: 77-98 and 108-112 (canonical Utukku Lemnutu; critical edition M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons, BAM 8, De Gruyter 2016)" ;
    skos:altLabel "mas-hul-dub-ba"@en ;
    skos:definition "Making a healthy goat into a scapegoat and sacrificing it as one; reciting an incantation over its hide; drawing the goat to the body of the man in the evening and binding its sash on his head; then carrying the hide off from the man's body and removing it quietly from the square."@en ;
    skos:note "The one interval anywhere in the tablets read: the preceding operation is to persist for one day, and the goat is drawn to the body in the evening. It is a duration for the act and not a term after which the treatment counts as failed, which is the distinction the two searches at this tradition turn on."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmp:AsipuNaruqqaLeqe a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die mit Beutel und Zehrung fortgeschickten Geister"@de,
        "The ghosts sent off with their pouch and their rations"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Udug-hul 7: 152-156 (canonical Utukku Lemnutu; critical edition M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons, BAM 8, De Gruyter 2016)" ;
    skos:definition "Dismissing the demons and ghosts by naming each one and sending it to its own steppe, and telling it to take its leather pouch, to take its daily food rations, and to take its leather bag."@en ;
    skos:note "Provisioning and not compulsion, and the difference is worth holding because the rest of the series drives the demon out by force. Here the departure is furnished: the thing addressed is given a bag and a day's food for the road. It is the one place in the tablets read where the transmission treats the affliction as a party with needs rather than as an enemy with none."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmp:AsipuSiptaAnaRamaniNadu a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Beschwörung über sich selbst sprechen"@de,
        "Casting the spell upon oneself"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Sakikku (diagnostic series) Tablet 2, colophon (R. Labat, Traite akkadien de diagnostics et pronostics medicaux, Paris and Leiden 1951, pp. 18-19 n. 27; re-edited N. Heessel, Archiv fuer Orientforschung 48/49, 2001-02, p. 37)" ;
    skos:definition "Reciting the incantation over oneself before going in to a patient, given as the standing instruction in the colophon of the second tablet of the diagnostic series: if you are to approach a patient, until you cast the spell on yourself, you should not approach the patient."@en ;
    skos:note "Second-hand, and the reason is a property of the works and not of this run. The edition that carries the colophon and the monograph that re-edits it are both print-only for this workstation; the passage was read in the quotation given by the edition of the incantation series, which prints it in transliteration and in translation. The node is an open post and the mode is to be raised to textual attestation when either volume is obtained."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons: Canonical Udug-hul Incantations (BAM 8, Boston/Berlin: De Gruyter 2016), p. 40, quoting the colophon in transliteration and translation" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmp:AsipuUtukkiLimnutiTuru a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Austreibung der bösen Geister: die Beschwörungsformel"@de,
        "The exorcism of the evil spirits: the formula of adjuration"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Utukki Limnuti, Tablet III, lines 170-186, and Tablet V, lines 26-38, in the English of R. Campbell Thompson, The Devils and Evil Spirits of Babylonia, Luzac's Semitic Text and Translation Series vol. XIV (London: Luzac, 1903), vol. I" ;
    skos:definition "The operation the series carries. The exorcist declares who he is — a sorcerer that giveth life unto the land, a potent wizard that patrolleth the city, a sorcerer of Eridu whose mouth is purified — and then adjures whatever is present to depart, by Heaven and by Earth and by a named succession of gods: Bel lord of the world, Beltis lady of the world, Ninib son of Esharra, Ishtar mistress of the world who enlighteneth the night. The adjuration closes with a term of deprivation rather than of time: until thou art removed, until thou departest from the body of the man, thou shalt have no food to eat, thou shalt have no water to drink."@en ;
    skos:note "The one clause in the formula that looks like a term is not one. Until thou art removed, thou shalt have no food and no water sets a condition on the thing being expelled and not on the exorcist, and nothing in the passage says how long the withholding may go on or what is to be concluded if the sick man is no better."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmp:AyurvedaAindrirasayana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Aindri-Rasayana, mit Eisenhut und Gold nach Gerstenkorngewicht"@de,
        "The Aindri rasayana, with aconite and gold by weight of barleycorns"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.3.28-33, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, pp. 1047-1048" ;
    skos:definition "Aindri, matsyaksika, brahmi, sweet flag, brahmasuvarcala, long pepper and rock salt, each three yavas; gold two yavas; aconite two tilas; ghee one pala. All mixed and taken in proper measure; when digested, boiled rice with honey and a copious measure of ghee."@en ;
    skos:note "The only prescription in the lesson whose quantities are given in barleycorns and sesame seeds, and the only one containing a drug the translation names as Aconitum ferox. The measure of the aconite is the smallest quantity stated anywhere in the lesson, and nothing is said about what a larger one would do."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:AyurvedaAmalakacurna a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Amalaka-Pulver, durch die Regenzeit vergraben"@de,
        "The amalaka powder, buried through the rains"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.2.21-22, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, p. 1034" ;
    skos:definition "An adhaka of amalaka powder soaked twenty-one nights in the juice of a thousand amalakas, mixed with two adhakas of honey and ghee, an eighth of an adhaka of long pepper powder and a fourth of sugar, placed in a ghee-soaked jar and kept buried in a heap of ashes in the rainy season, in the month of Asadha or Sravana, and taken up after the rains have ended."@en ;
    skos:note "One of the five places in the lesson where a calendar enters, and it enters at the making and not at the taking. What is bound to a season here is the maturing of the drug in the jar, and the passage states no consequence for doing it in another month."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:AyurvedaAmalakaghrtaSahasrapaka a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das hundert- oder tausendfach gekochte Amalaka-Ghee"@de,
        "The amalaka ghee cooked a hundred or a thousand times"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.2.10-12, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, pp. 1031-1032" ;
    skos:definition "An adhaka of ghee cooked with the expressed juice of amalaka and a paste of white punarnava; then with the juice of vidari and a paste of jivanti; then with fourfold milk and the cold infusion of bala and atibala and a paste of satavari. The three stages together are one round, and the round is repeated a hundred or a thousand times, sugar and honey being added each time. Kept in a vessel of gold, silver or clean strong earth."@en ;
    skos:note "The prescription's cost is the point of it. The translator sets out the arithmetic of the rounds in a long note and the quantities are in tens of seers; a hundred rounds of a three-stage cooking is a year's work for somebody. Nothing in the lesson says what the hundredth round does that the tenth does not."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:AyurvedaAmalakarasayanaCaturtha a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das vierte, das in der Kammer genommene Amalaka-Rasayana"@de,
        "The fourth, the amalaka rasayana taken in the chamber"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.1.74, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIII, Calcutta 1904, pp. 1026-1027" ;
    skos:definition "Emblic with chebulic, or emblic with belleric, or all three myrobalans in equal numbers, wrapped in the bark of butea and plastered with soft clay, steamed in a perforated vessel over water; the seeds thrown out, a thousand palas of the kernel pounded, mixed with curds, ghee, honey, powdered sesame, oil and sugar. Taken on an empty stomach, having entered the chamber described; after it, barley gruel until the constitution is restored, then the body rubbed with ghee and with barley powder; then two meals a day with soups, milk and ghee; after that the user may bear himself as he pleases."@en ;
    skos:note "The lesson calls this method of administration the best, and it is the only preparation in the first section whose ordinance sets out what the user does after the course as well as during it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:AyurvedaAmalakavaleha a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Amalaka-Extrakt"@de,
        "The amalaka extract"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.2.17-19, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, p. 1033" ;
    skos:definition "A thousand amalakas with a thousand long peppers drenched in water in which the ashes of butea wood are dissolved, dried in the shade, the seeds thrown out, powdered together, mixed with honey and ghee at four times the measure of the powder and sugar at a fourth, kept in a ghee-soaked jar underground for six months. Taken in the morning in a measure agreeable to strength and appetite; no food in the afternoon or evening while the course lasts."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:AyurvedaAmalakavalehaDvitiya a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der zweite Amalaka-Extrakt, in einem Gefäß aus rohem Butea-Holz geröstet"@de,
        "The second amalaka extract, roasted in a vessel of raw butea wood"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.2.24-26, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, pp. 1035-1036" ;
    skos:definition "A thousand amalakas heated in a vessel of raw butea wood covered so that no vapour escapes, over a fire of dried cowdung from fields and forests; cooled, the seeds thrown out, powdered; an adhaka each of that powder, of long pepper and of embelia, an adhaka and a half of sugar, with thirty-two seers each of sesame oil, honey and ghee, kept twenty-one days in a clean strong ghee-soaked jar."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:AyurvedaAmalakayasarasayana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Amalaka-Eisen-Rasayana, aus mit der Hand gepflückten Früchten"@de,
        "The amalaka and iron rasayana, from fruits plucked by hand"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.3.2-7, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, pp. 1042-1044" ;
    skos:definition "Amalakas plucked with the hand and not by shaking the branches, in the month of Magha or Phalguna, seedless, sun-dried, powdered, and made to absorb twenty-one times the expressed juice of other fruits of the same kind: an adhaka. With an adhaka of the five groups of drugs called life-lengthening, nourishing, milk-producing, semen-increasing and age-arresting, with the chipped stems of six trees and seven further drugs. Boiled in a hundred and sixty seers of water down to sixteen, strained, the amalaka powder thrown in, heated over a fire of dried cowdung or split bamboo until the liquid has gone, dried on an iron plate, pounded on a stone laid on a black deerskin, kept in an iron vessel, mixed with an eighth of its measure of iron powder and given with honey and ghee."@en ;
    skos:note "The name of the section comes from this prescription: karapracita, plucked with the hand. Three of its conditions are on the manner of gathering, one on the month, one on the surface the powder is pounded on. None of them is given a reason and none a consequence."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:AyurvedaBhallatakaksaudra a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der in ein vergrabenes Gefäß herabgezogene Bhallataka-Saft, mit Honig"@de,
        "The bhallataka juice drawn down into a buried jar, with honey"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.2.41-42, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, p. 1040" ;
    skos:definition "Bhallataka fruits of the kind and collection already described, pounded and kept in a vessel with a hole in the bottom, which is set on a strong ghee-soaked jar buried to its neck in the ground; the upper vessel is sealed with an earthen cup and black earth and heated with a fire of dried cowdung, so that the juice descends into the lower jar. The juice is mixed with honey at an eighth of its measure and ghee at twice, both a year old and the ghee from cow's milk."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:AyurvedaBhallatakataila a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das hundertfach gekochte Bhallataka-Öl"@de,
        "The bhallataka oil cooked a hundred times"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.2.44, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, pp. 1040-1041" ;
    skos:definition "A patra of the oil drawn off in the preceding manner, mixed with cow's milk and with an aksha of liquorice paste, and cooked a hundred times; the milk is evaporated at each round and replaced. The manner of administration, the diet during and after the course, and the effects are those already stated."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:AyurvedaBhallatakavidhi a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die zehn Formen der Bhallataka-Gabe, und der Arzt, der unter ihnen zu wählen hat"@de,
        "The ten forms in which bhallataka is given, and the physician who must choose among them"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.2.45-48, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, pp. 1041-1042" ;
    skos:definition "Bhallataka in milk, in honey, in oil, in treacle, in soup, in ghee, in flesh, in flour, in salt and in tarpana: the lesson names ten forms and describes three. The physician conversant with diseases, with the nature of the human constitution and with the laws of assimilation is to select the method of administration; the remaining seven, the translator notes, are not laid down and the physician must learn to make them."@en ;
    skos:note "A transmission that names ten forms of its most dangerous drug and describes three of them is not withholding a secret; it is recording that the operative knowledge was elsewhere. Where a transmission keeps part of its operative content out of writing this holding has a generalization for it, and the case is noted rather than counted, because the lesson states no condition of failure for the seven it omits."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:AyurvedaBrahmarasayanaDvitiya a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das zweite Brahmya-Rasayana"@de,
        "The second Brahmya rasayana"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.1.57, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIII, Calcutta 1904, pp. 1022-1023" ;
    skos:definition "A thousand emblic myrobalans steamed over boiling milk, dried in the shade, the kernels powdered, macerated seven days in the juice of a thousand more of the same, twenty further drugs added at an eighth of the powder's measure, macerated seven days again in the juice of a thousand palas of nagabala, dried in the shade, mixed with twice its measure of ghee or of ghee and honey, made into a ball and kept in a ghee-soaked jar buried under a heap of ashes for a fortnight. Taken up after the fortnight by a physician conversant with the Atharvans, mixed with a globular mass of gold, silver, copper, coral and black iron at an eighth of its measure, and administered every morning with the dose increased by half a karsha a day."@en ;
    skos:note "Two conditions here are on the operator and not on the drug: the jar is to be taken up by a physician conversant with the Atharvans, and the dose is raised by a fixed step every day. The first is the only place in the lesson where the person who handles a preparation must have a qualification of that kind."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:AyurvedaBrahmarasayanaPrathama a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das erste Brahmya-Rasayana"@de,
        "The first Brahmya rasayana"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.1.41-56, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIII, Calcutta 1904, pp. 1020-1022" ;
    skos:definition "Ten palas of each of the five groups of five roots boiled in tenfold water down to a tenth, strained, with a thousand chebulic and three thousand fresh emblic myrobalans pounded in, thirteen further powders at four palas each, eleven hundred palas of sugarcandy, two adhakas of oil and three of ghee, boiled in a copper vessel over a mild fire until it will bear licking, honey added when cool, kept in a ghee-soaked earthen jar. Administered at the proper time in a measure the physician thinks judicious; when digested, rice of the sixty-day variety with milk."@en ;
    skos:note "The one preparation of the lesson for which the promised span is not a figure. What is claimed is immeasurable longevity, and it is claimed of the Vaikhanasas and Valakhilyas rather than of a user."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:AyurvedaCyavanaprasa a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Cyavanaprasa"@de,
        "Cyavanaprasa"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.1.61-69, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIII, Calcutta 1904, pp. 1024-1025" ;
    skos:definition "Some thirty drugs at one pala each with five hundred emblic myrobalans boiled in a drona of water; when the juices have come out the vessel is taken down, the seeds of the myrobalans thrown away, their kernels fried in twelve palas of ghee and oil, the fried mass boiled in the decoction with pure raw sugar; honey added when cool, with bamboo manna, long pepper, cinnamon bark, cardamom, cinnamon leaf and mesua."@en ;
    skos:note "Four of the drugs are given with a named substitute because the original is stated to be unobtainable — guduci for jivaka, bamboo manna for rsabhaka, asvagandha for meda, kustha for puskara. A prescription that names its own substitutions is telling a later reader that its materia was already incomplete when it was written down, and it is the one place in the lesson where the transmission concedes it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:AyurvedaDronipravesika a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Weg, der in ein hölzernes Gefäß eintritt"@de,
        "The route that enters a wooden vessel"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.4.18-26, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, pp. 1059-1061" ;
    skos:definition "Eight herbs are described by their shape and their exudation — brahmasuvarcala with golden milk and lotus leaves, adityaparni with a flower like the disc of the sun, nari whose leaves look like a goat, kasthagodha shaped like an iguana, sarpa shaped like a snake, soma with fifteen leaves that wax and wane with the moon, padma like the lotus, aja, and nila with blue milk. Of whichever can be obtained the expressed juice is drunk to the measure of gratification. The user then lies naked within a covered vessel built of the raw wood of butea and soaked in ghee, and is fed on goat's milk; the translator reports Gangadhara's direction that the milk is poured drop by drop through a hole in the lid."@en ;
    skos:note "The procedure with the longest term, the most extreme claim and the least available material in the lesson. The translator observes of the herbs that many are not to be found, that Agnivesa's own way of speaking of them shows they were obsolete and unidentifiable even in his day, and that they are named by Indra to seers on a mountain. A prescription whose ingredients the compiler could not obtain is not a prescription anyone could have run."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:AyurvedaHaritakirasayana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das fünfte, das Haritaki-Rasayana"@de,
        "The fifth, the haritaki rasayana"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.1.75, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIII, Calcutta 1904, p. 1027" ;
    skos:definition "A kumbha of ghee boiled with the decoction of the three myrobalans and the five groups of five roots, with a paste of long pepper, liquorice, bassia flowers, kakoli, ksirakakoli, mucuna, jivaka, rsabhaka and ksirasukla, with the expressed juice of vidari and eight times its measure of cow's milk. Administered with an eye to the strength of the digestive fire; when digested, rice with milk and ghee, and hot water to drink after."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:AyurvedaIndropadistarasayana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Rasayana, das Indra auf dem Himavat lehrte"@de,
        "The rasayana Indra taught on the Himavat"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.4.14-17, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, pp. 1058-1059" ;
    skos:definition "Aindri, brahmi, ksirakakoli, ksirapuspi, sravani, mahasravani, satavari, vidari, jivanti, punarnava, nagabala, sthira, sweet flag, chatraticchatra, meda, mahameda and the other drugs of the life-lengthening group, made into a paste and used with milk for six months."@en ;
    skos:note "Prefaced by the only date the lesson gives for the operation as a whole rather than for a drug: this is the auspicious time for rasayanas, Indra says, and the herbs born on Himavat have now attained to maturity and potency. The statement is made once, in a narrative frame, and no other passage refers back to it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:AyurvedaKevalamalaka a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das allein genommene Amalaka, im Wald, unter der Rezitation der Silbe"@de,
        "The amalaka taken alone, in the forest, under the recitation of the syllable"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.3.13-17, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, pp. 1045-1046" ;
    skos:definition "One lives a year on milk alone among kine, mentally reciting the Savitri, in sexual continence and with the senses under control. After the year, on the day of the full moon of Pausa, Magha or Phalguna, having fasted three days, one enters an amalaka forest, climbs a large tree rich in fruit, plucks a few fruits with the hand, and holding them mentally recites the pranava, the syllable Om, until nectar comes into them. One then eats until hunger is gratified."@en ;
    skos:note "The only procedure in the lesson whose central operation is a recitation and not a compounding, and the only one that states a perceptible sign of its own working. It is also the only one with a full-moon date, a three-day fast and a year of preliminary observance, so that a user who wanted to try it would have to begin twelve months before the one day on which it can be done."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:AyurvedaLauhadirasayana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das siebenmal abgeschreckte Eisenblatt"@de,
        "The iron leaf quenched seven times"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.3.19-25, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, pp. 1045-1046" ;
    skos:definition "A leaf of keen iron four fingers long and as thick as a sesame seed, made red hot and quenched in turn in the juice of the three myrobalans, in cow's urine, in water with the ashes of green barley spikes, in salt water, in water with the ashes of ingudi wood and in water with the ashes of kimsuka wood, until it takes a hue as black as collyrium; then pounded, mixed with honey and amalaka juice to a lickable paste, placed in a ghee-soaked vessel and kept a year under a heap of barley, the physician stirring the whole of the contents once a month. Administered after the year with honey and ghee every morning."@en ;
    skos:note "Six quenchings are named and the heating before the first makes seven operations; the sequence is fixed and the endpoint is a stated colour. It is the nearest thing in the lesson to a procedure with a criterion inside it, and the criterion governs the making of the drug and not its working."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:AyurvedaLauhaghrta a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das sechste, das Ghee mit Eisenpulver, drei Jahre lang genommen"@de,
        "The sixth, the ghee with powdered iron, taken for three years"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.1.76, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIII, Calcutta 1904, p. 1028" ;
    skos:definition "The decoction of the three myrobalans with turmeric, sthira, sweet flag, embelia, tinospora, dry ginger, liquorice, long pepper and white acacia, boiled with milk and ghee, honey and sugar; a hundred palas of emblic powder macerated in the juice of its own species added, and powdered iron at a fourth of that measure. Two tolas of the ghee every morning, having entered the chamber; in the evening rice with mung soup, milk and ghee."@en ;
    skos:note "The only preparation in the lesson with a term longer than a year, and the one whose claim is stated as a conditional with a figure on each side: if one takes the ghee continually for three years, one lives for a hundred years."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:AyurvedaMedhakararasayana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Rasayanas, die den Verstand stärken"@de,
        "The rasayanas that invigorate the understanding"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.3.34-35, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, pp. 1048-1049" ;
    skos:definition "The expressed juice of mandukaparni; liquorice reduced to powder; the juice of guduci — each used with milk; and the paste of the roots and blossoms of sankhapuspi used with the liquid. Four preparations under one heading, of which the lesson singles out the last."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:AyurvedaNagabalarasayana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Nagabala-Rasayana, mit der längsten Drogenvorschrift der Lektion"@de,
        "The nagabala rasayana, with the longest specification of a drug in the lesson"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.2.27-30, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, pp. 1036-1037" ;
    skos:definition "The roots of nagabala growing in a dry region overgrown with kusa grass, on soil oily, sweet and black or gold-coloured, free from the faults of poison, beasts of prey, wind, water and fire, never cultivated, free from anthills, with no crematorium upon or adjacent to it, no chaitya tree upon it and no alkaline salts, exposed to such wind, water and sun as are agreeable whatever the season; the plants unharmed, not overgrown, neither young nor old, undiminished in virtue, having put forth new leaves after casting the old, and not having borne fruit. The roots are extracted in the month of Magha or Phalguna by a person in a state of purity and observing self-restraint, after worship of the deities and the benediction of Brahmans, in an auspicious hour. Only the bark of the root is used, pounded to paste and given in milk every morning for a year."@en ;
    skos:note "Fourteen conditions on the place and the plant and three on the gatherer, and every one of them checkable by anybody standing there. This is the densest specification in the lesson and it is entirely on the material and its collection; nothing of comparable precision is said anywhere about what the drug then does or when one would know."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:AyurvedaPippalirasayana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Langpfeffer-Rasayana, fünf bis zehn Früchte ein Jahr lang"@de,
        "The long pepper rasayana, five to ten fruits for a year"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.3.36-39, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, pp. 1049-1050" ;
    skos:definition "Five, six, seven or ten fruits of long pepper with honey and ghee, used for a whole year. Alternatively a handful of the fruits soaked in water in which the ashes of kimsuka wood are dissolved, fried, and three of them taken three times a day, before and after meals, with honey and ghee. Gangadhara, the translator notes, has the soaking repeated seven times and the second preparation continued for a whole year."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:AyurvedaPippalivardhamana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die steigende Langpfeffer-Kur, tausend Früchte in neunzehn Tagen"@de,
        "The rising course of long pepper, a thousand fruits in nineteen days"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.3.40-43, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, pp. 1050-1051" ;
    skos:definition "Ten fruits of long pepper with milk on the first day, the number raised by ten each day to the tenth, and from the eleventh lowered by ten each day: a thousand fruits in nineteen days. Every day, when the medicine is digested, rice of the sixty-day variety with milk and ghee. The powder for a strong man, the decoction for one of average strength, the cold infusion for one of little strength; and the course beginning with six fruits is called tolerable, that beginning with three the least in efficacy and the one a weak man should use."@en ;
    skos:note "The lesson's second counted ramp, and the one whose arithmetic the translator works out: ten to a hundred and back gives a thousand, six gives one thousand and fourteen, three gives one thousand and twenty-six. Gangadhara is quoted as remarking that the excess over a thousand is not injurious. A commentator who has to say that an excess does no harm is answering a question the text left open, and the question is the only one in the neighbourhood that a body could settle."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:AyurvedaSamsodhana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Reinigung, die vorausgeht, und das Zeichen, an dem sie vollständig ist"@de,
        "The cleansing that goes before, and the sign at which it is complete"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.1.24-27, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIII, Calcutta 1904, pp. 1018-1019" ;
    skos:definition "The preliminary the lesson sets out in four verses. The body is first rubbed with oil and made to sweat; then a powder of chebulic myrobalan, rock salt, emblic myrobalan with raw sugar, sweet flag, embelia, turmeric, long pepper and ginger is drunk in hot water; then light food is given, and gruel of old barley with ghee for three, five or seven nights, until the bowels become clear and the stools regular. Only when physicians conversant with age, constitution and power of assimilation have ascertained that the bowels are properly cleansed is the rasayana itself administered, and it is chosen to suit the individual."@en ;
    skos:note "The only place in the lesson where an operation is carried until a stated observable condition is reached rather than for a stated number of days. Three, five or seven nights is the range; what ends it is the state of the stools. That is a criterion, it is checkable, and it belongs to the preliminary and not to the operation the preliminary is for."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:AyurvedaSuvarnarajatarasayana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Rasayanas aus Gold und Silber, auf dieselbe Weise bereitet"@de,
        "Rasayanas of gold and of silver, prepared in the same manner"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.3.19-25, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, p. 1047" ;
    skos:definition "Agreeably to the method laid down for the iron leaf, rasayanas are to be prepared of gold and of silver. The lesson gives the direction in one sentence and adds that the administration of every one of these rasayanas extends the period of life, as has been fully tested by experiment, and dispels all sorts of disease."@en ;
    skos:note "Two further preparations in a single clause, and the clause carries the lesson's most explicit appeal to trial. What is claimed as tested by experiment is a set of preparations for which no procedure beyond by the same method is given."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:AyurvedaSvarasarasayana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Presssäfte, nach der Weise der Nagabala-Wurzel genommen"@de,
        "The expressed juices taken in the manner of the nagabala root"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.2.31-32, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, p. 1038" ;
    skos:definition "The expressed juice of bala, atibala, sandal, agallochum, dhava, tinisa, catechu, sissoo and asana, and the juice of the ten plants named as capable of preventing the effects of age — tinospora, chebulic myrobalan, emblic myrobalan, vanda, white clitoria, jivanti, satavari, mandukaparni, sthira and punarnava — each taken like the rasayana of nagabala, with the same directions and, the translator adds, the same virtues. Where the expressed juice is not obtainable, an adhaka of the powder steeped a day and a night in an adhaka of water and strained is to be used in its place."@en ;
    skos:note "Nineteen drugs under one heading, each of which the section counts separately towards its own total of seventeen preparations. The node follows the heading; the discrepancy between the two counts is noted at the head of this block."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:AyurvedaTriphalarasayana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Rasayanas der drei Myrobalanen"@de,
        "The rasayanas of the three myrobalans"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.3.45-51, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, pp. 1051-1052" ;
    skos:definition "One chebulic fruit before the meal, two belleric, and four emblic just after it, all with honey and ghee, taken for a whole year. Or a new iron leaf laved a day and a night with the paste of the three myrobalans, the paste then used with honey and water. Or the three used with powdered liquorice, or with bamboo manna, or with long pepper, or with honey and ghee, or with sugar; or with iron powder, or with gold, or with sweet flag, or with embelia, or with rock salt."@en ;
    skos:note "Under one heading the lesson gives twelve combinations and calls five of them well tried. The five are named as a group and no one of them is distinguished from the rest."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:AyurvedaVaidikarasayana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das vedische Rasayana, für die, die die Kräuter nicht suchen können"@de,
        "The Vedic rasayana, for those unable to seek out the herbs"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.4.34-45, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, parts XXXIV-XXXV, Calcutta 1905, pp. 1062-1064" ;
    skos:definition "Offered expressly to those who seek ease and are unable to seek out herbs and undergo the discomforts of administration. The juices of four groups of ten drugs and of some twenty-six further drugs, with the ashes of butea wood, cooked with cow's milk, sesame oil and ghee; an adhaka of amalaka powder soaked a hundred times in its own juice, new honey, sugar, bamboo manna and long pepper added; kept fifteen days in a ghee-soaked jar. Taken with a sixteenth part of powdered gold, copper, coral, ivory, crystal, pearl, lapis, conch and silver. While the course lasts the user refrains from toil and from sexual congress."@en ;
    skos:note "The third route the lesson opens, after the hut and the wind and sun, and it is opened on the same ground as the second: the user cannot meet what the harder way asks. The lesson grades its own procedures by what a user can do and says so in plain words, here and at the comparison of the two methods."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:AyurvedaVidangavaleha a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Vidanga-Extrakt"@de,
        "The vidanga extract"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.2.23, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, pp. 1034-1035" ;
    skos:definition "An adhaka of embelia grains powdered, an adhaka of long pepper powdered, an adhaka and a half of white sugar, and four adhakas each of ghee, sesame oil and honey, mixed, placed in a ghee-soaked jar, buried in ashes at the beginning of the rains and taken up after them."@en ;
    skos:note "The measures of the three liquids are not given in the text; the translator supplies them from Gangadhara's rule that a liquid is taken at twice the stated solid and doubled again for the direction about time. That is a commentator's arithmetic standing where a quantity should be, and it is marked here because the prescription is otherwise exact."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:BabalawoPupilage a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Lehrzeit des Anwärters auf das Amt des Babalawo"@de,
        "The pupilage of a candidate for the office of babalawo"@en ;
    dcterms:source "James Johnson, Yoruba Heathenism, the account of the training of a candidate for the office of Babalawo" ;
    skos:definition "A candidate for the office is expected to be the pupil of a babalawo known for wide knowledge of the religion and for skill in divining, for at least three years, and the course is divided into three parts covering those years. In the first the candidate learns the names of all the signs and the marks that stand for them and shows his command of them in practice; in the second he learns the traditions attached to them."@en ;
    skos:note "What the third year requires was not recovered from the portion of the extract delivered in this run. The gap is a property of the retrieval and not of the work, and it is recorded so that the next reader opens the appendix at that place rather than repeating the whole."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), appendix, extract from Yoruba Heathenism" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ifa .

pmp:Barasnum a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Barašnūm der neun Nächte"@de,
        "The Barashnum of the nine nights"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Vendidad, fargard 9.7-14, 9.31-38, trans. James Darmesteter, Sacred Books of the East 4, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon, 1895)" ;
    skos:altLabel "Barasnum i no shab"@en,
        "barasnum"@en,
        "baresnum"@en ;
    skos:definition "The great purification, stated throughout in numbers. Six holes are dug for the bull's urine and three more for the water, a pace apart; twelve furrows are drawn around them; the liquid is poured with a spoon of iron or of lead, so that the purifier does not touch. At the first water hole there is one washing, at the second two, at the third three. The whole runs nine nights, in three periods of three, with a washing of body and clothes after each. The fee is graded by station: for a priest a blessing of the just, for a lord of a province a camel of high value, for a master of a house a cow of three years."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Zoroastrian .

pmp:BauKiaNkasa a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Bau kia nkasa, das Rindenordal"@de,
        "Bau kia nkasa, the ordeal by the bark"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), pp. 262-264",
        "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), p. 25 and p. 83",
        "W. Holman Bentley, Pioneering on the Congo (London: Religious Tract Society, 1900), vol. I, pp. 276-277" ;
    skos:definition "The pounded bark of the nkasa tree, given to a person accused of witchcraft. The corpus gives the setting and the dose: the accused is taken to the bare top of a hill, a hut of palm fronds is built with a lath across it, and a stone is pushed towards him bearing twenty-seven small heaps of the ordeal, each ground to powder and fed to him in turn, during which he must hold his hands spread out and touch nothing. A curse is then laid on him, that if he is a witch he will die by the ordeal. A second witness has the accused drink cup after cup of the bark paste mixed with water, in the market place and on market day, standing on a stone or a marked spot he may not leave."@en ;
    skos:note "The Vili witness at Loango has the same substance in the same office, administered at the ruler's court by a practitioner of the ordeal, and adds what happens to the condemned there: the body is burnt and the ashes scattered, and no burial is granted."@en,
        "The corpus states that it is not the medicinal property of the bark that acts but the spirit in the tree, which reveals the witchcraft or does not. That is the claim the procedure rests on, and it is what makes the case a candidate for the class of answers fixed to an outcome the operator does not control: the operator is expressly said not to be the one who decides."@en,
        "The same second witness reports that the mixture is supposed to be the bark only, and that a practitioner who considers it desirable to kill mixes one or two other poisons with it, which the recorder says he was told by a man who had himself been of the trade. The two statements stand in the file together on purpose: the transmission asserts an outcome nobody steers, and a witness from inside states how it was steered."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by Bakongo of the Lower Congo, in this passage largely unnamed, and in the second work by a man who had himself administered the ordeal; recorded by the Baptist missionaries John H. Weeks and W. Holman Bentley during their residence at San Salvador, Wathen and in the Ngombe Lutete district from 1879 onwards; given in Kikongo, of which Bentley compiled the dictionary of 1887, and printed in English with the Kikongo terms." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:KongoNkisi .

pmp:BengeTaboos a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Tabus vor der Berührung des Orakelgifts"@de,
        "The taboos kept before touching the oracle poison"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 132-133" ;
    skos:definition "Whoever comes into contact with the poison abstains from sexual relations, from the flesh of the elephant, from fish, from two glutinous vegetables named in the source, and from smoking hemp. Sexual abstinence is set at five or six days and abstinence from the forbidden foods at three or four, though the source records that the period is not fixed and that men give different estimates."@en ;
    skos:note "A man asked to operate who has not kept the sexual taboo says that he has eaten the first of the two vegetables, and everyone understands the euphemism. The source adds that he may use the same words when he simply does not want the work, which makes the phrase a refusal as well as a confession."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmp:BonDpyad a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Diagnose nach den Kanälen, dem Harn und dem Aussehen"@de,
        "Diagnosis by the channels, the urine and the appearance"@en ;
    dcterms:source "gZi brjid, the Way of the Shen of Prediction (phya-gshen theg-pa), the fourth of its four parts, on diagnosis, in D. L. Snellgrove, The Nine Ways of Bon: Excerpts from gZi-brjid, London Oriental Series 18, London: Oxford University Press, 1967" ;
    skos:definition "The fourth part. The physician raises his thought to the four immeasurable virtues, takes refuge in the hosts of buddhas, offers a mandala, and worships the medicine king Vaidūrya with his eight fellow buddhas. He then diagnoses the major and minor causes in everything visible, identifies the disease by the connecting channels, diagnoses from the urine what is of benefit and what of harm, and from the appearance the signs of death and the signs of cure. Having identified the disease as heat or cold, phlegm or bile or a combination, he applies the medicine, cooling, warming or equalising, as powder, pill, syrup, potion, ointment or butter-mould; treatment is of four kinds and curing of four kinds, by medicine, by bleeding and branding, and by tranquillising with method and spells. The work ends the part by requiring that the three diagnoses coincide."@en ;
    skos:note "Let the result coincide is the only instruction in the four parts that puts one of the work's own procedures under a test: three independent readings are taken and are to agree, and a disagreement would be visible to the physician before he treats anybody. Nothing is said about what he is to do when they disagree."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GziBrjid ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bon .

pmp:BonGto a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die gto-Riten, die Heilung durch Ritus"@de,
        "The gto rites, the cure by ritual"@en ;
    dcterms:source "gZi brjid, the Way of the Shen of Prediction (phya-gshen theg-pa), the third of its four parts, on the rites, in D. L. Snellgrove, The Nine Ways of Bon: Excerpts from gZi-brjid, London Oriental Series 18, London: Oxford University Press, 1967" ;
    skos:definition "The third part, a set of named rites each with its own occasion. The Awry Rite is performed when sPar-kha, the Year-Cycle, the sMe-ba sphere and the antagonistic elements are in disarray: a circle is drawn with sand of five colours, twigs with coloured wools and silk of five colours are set up, a cake of different grains and the three milk and three sweet substances are offered, the goddesses of the Elements and the Time-Periods worshipped and true expositions recited, and the disarrayed elements are thereby quietened. The Striking Rite is performed for long life and good fortune: a swastika is drawn in grain on a clean base, the implements and talismans prepared, libations and consecrated chang offered and the eight gods of Prediction and Good Fortune worshipped. The Stinging Rite, which works by knowledge of prognostic signs, is used when beings are struck with an impediment and come near to death."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GziBrjid ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bon .

pmp:BonMo a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Losbefragung, die erste und vornehmste der vier"@de,
        "Sortilege, the first and foremost of the four"@en ;
    dcterms:source "gZi brjid, the Way of the Shen of Prediction (phya-gshen theg-pa), the first of its four parts, on sortilege, in D. L. Snellgrove, The Nine Ways of Bon: Excerpts from gZi-brjid, London Oriental Series 18, London: Oxford University Press, 1967" ;
    skos:definition "The first of the four parts of the Way of the Shen of Prediction, and the one the work calls foremost. On a piece of white felt as the base the operator places sprinklings of green barley, sets up the symbol of life, a bronze-tipped arrow with a turquoise ornament, marks the way of the incense with wafts of smoke, and worships with a heap of barley-flour and butter and with consecrated chang. He worships the god Phu-wer dkar-po, invokes the knot-sortilege of Ye-srid-hphrul, produces clairvoyance within himself, reflects upon a dream and effects the soothsaying of Ye-dbang-lha. He then names everything in the client's past, sets in order everything of his future, and writes down the evils and benefits to come and the length of his life."@en ;
    skos:note "The procedure ends in a written document about a named person, which is the form in which a prognostic can be checked afterwards, and the work says in the same passage that truth and falsehood there may be but that true distinction is to be made. It does not say what is to happen if the document turns out wrong."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GziBrjid ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bon .

pmp:ChukcheeShamanisticPerformance a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Vollzug im geschlossenen Innenraum"@de,
        "The performance in the closed inner room"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Waldemar Bogoras, The Chukchee, Memoir of the American Museum of Natural History vol. VII (Leiden: E. J. Brill, 1904-1909), pp. 433-434" ;
    skos:definition "The proceeding after the evening meal: the kettles and trays are carried out, everyone who wishes to be present enters the inner room, which is closed for the night; the practitioner sits at the master's place by the back wall with free space left around him, goes over the drum and tightens its head, strips to the waist and often takes off shoes and stockings; the light is put out, and he beats the drum and sings tunes without words, at first low and then filling the room, using the drum to change the place his voice appears to come from, until after a quarter of an hour or more the spirits first appear."@en ;
    skos:note "The darkness and the closed room are stated as conditions of the proceeding and not as atmosphere: the light is put out before the operating begins, and the recorder describes what the sound does to listeners who can no longer place its source."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by Waldemar Bogoras among the Reindeer and Maritime Chukchee of northeastern Siberia in the field of the Jesup North Pacific Expedition and published in English between 1904 and 1909; the speakers are not named at this place and the account is the recorder's own from attendance rather than a quoted informant; the Chukchee terms are carried in his transcription beside the English, and a separate volume of texts in the language stands beside the monograph."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChukcheeShamanism .

pmp:DavisMagnetization a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Magnetisieren des Subjekts in den Vortragszustand"@de,
        "Magnetizing the subject into the lecturing state"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Davis, The Principles of Nature, 3rd edn., New York 1847, Scribe's Introduction, pp. xvii-xviii" ;
    skos:definition "The procedure the scribe sets out step by step. The magnetizer and the subject are seated in easy positions facing each other; the ordinary manipulations are performed, requiring three to five minutes; a sudden convulsion of the muscles, such as is produced by an electric shock, indicates that the subject is duly magnetized, and his eyes are then bandaged against the light. He remains speechless some four or five minutes, with occasional convulsions, one of which brings him to external consciousness and gives him command of speech. He then inclines to the right or the left and passes into the fourth condition. In the state itself a few words only are uttered at a time, which the clairvoyant requires the operator to repeat back so that he may know he is understood."@en ;
    skos:note "The procedure carries a sign of success and the sign is stated in advance, which SPEC §15 requires be counted separately from a condition of failure: the convulsion like an electric shock is announced as the indicator that the state has been reached. One announced sign of success, and the conditions of failure are at pmc:DavisManipulatorNecessarius and pmc:DavisInterferentiaInsensibilis."@en,
        "The repetition requirement is worth noticing because it is the one point at which the operator is inside the production of the text. The scribe states it as a convenience of the state; it means that every sentence of the volume passed through the magnetizer's mouth before it reached the scribe's pencil. That consequence is drawn here from the passage and is the compiler's reading of it, not a statement of the scribe's."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DavisPrinciplesOfNature ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SpiritualistAutomaticWriting .

pmp:DreamDivination a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Traumdivination"@de,
        "Dream divination"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Synesius of Cyrene, De insomniis" ;
    skos:definition "The deliberate use of dreams as a source of knowledge, treated in this literature as available to anyone and requiring no instrument beyond the sleeper."@en ;
    skos:note "The absence of an instrument and of an office is the point at which this tradition differs from the incubation cult: no temple, no priest, no fee."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NeoplatonicTheurgy .

pmp:EthiopianAsmatSpokenAgainstTheSpear a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die beim Speerstoß wiederholten Namen"@de,
        "The names repeated at the thrust of the spear"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers written for Za-Walda Hawaryat (London: W. Griggs, 1900), section 6, p. LVIII" ;
    skos:definition "Repeating the names of the fifth section of the roll at the moment when a man wishes to thrust a spear into the speaker."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic .

pmp:EthiopianAsmatWornUponTheBreast a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die auf der Brust getragenen Namen"@de,
        "The names worn upon the breast"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers written for Za-Walda Hawaryat (London: W. Griggs, 1900), section 6, p. LVIII" ;
    skos:definition "Wearing the written names upon the breast instead of uttering them firmly with the voice. The roll gives this as the second case of one instruction and not as a separate procedure: it is what is done by the man who will not speak them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic .

pmp:EthiopianSusenyosPrayerWorn a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das niedergeschriebene und getragene Gebet des Susenyos"@de,
        "The prayer of Susenyos written down and worn"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, prayer VII, p. 218" ;
    skos:definition "Writing down the seventh prayer and wearing it, by the woman who has children to suckle and bring up, for the removal of disease of every kind from those who suck at their mothers' breasts."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic .

pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicBlauerVitriol a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Blauer Vitriol im hohlen Zahn"@de,
        "Blue vitriol in the hollow tooth"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 46",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 28" ;
    skos:definition "Putting a small piece of blue vitriol into the hollow tooth when the pain begins, not too much, and spitting out the water that collects rather than swallowing it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1820,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicGichterKnoten a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Knüpfen dreier Schüsse gegen die Gichter"@de,
        "The knotting of three twigs against the fits"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 29",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 18" ;
    skos:definition "Going onto another man's land, saying that one goes before another court and knots one's seventy-seven kinds of fits, cutting three shoots there and making a knot in each, on a Friday before sunrise in the waning moon and unbeschrauen, then making the crosses over the body at the place where the fits are felt."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1820,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicKuhstallUebertragung a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Bettstelle über dem Kuhstall"@de,
        "The bed built over the cow stable"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Ephrata, Pa., 1828), Anhang, S. 71-72",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), Appendix, pp. 59-60" ;
    skos:definition "Setting up a bedstead for the sick man over the cow stable and keeping him there by day as well, leaving a good opening in the ceiling of the stable so that the exhalation of the stable passes into the room and is breathed by the sick man, and the exhalation of the sick man is breathed by the cow; not mucking out the stable during the operation, though fresh straw may be laid in, and pouring away the cow's milk as unusable for as long as she still gives any."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1828,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicRadNabe a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die drey Spänlein in der Rad-Nabe"@de,
        "The three chips in the wheel hub"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 83-84",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), pp. 52-53" ;
    skos:definition "Cutting three chips at the place the thief went out, going to a wagon unbeschrauen, taking off a wheel, putting the chips into the hub and driving the wheel backwards while the compelling words are spoken."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1820,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicSchelwurzelProbe a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Schelwurzel und Maulwurfsherz auf das Haupt des Kranken gelegt"@de,
        "Celandine and a mole's heart laid on the sick man's head"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Abschnitt »Die Schelwurzel«; die gedruckte Seitenzahl ist im benutzten Exemplar nicht lesbar, der Abschnitt steht zwischen S. 32 und dem Abschnitt »Ein Mittel, Schmerzen zu nehmen«",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 20, Abschnitt »Swallow-wort«" ;
    skos:definition "Taking the herb that grows at the time when the swallows build their nests or the eagles breed, together with the heart of a mole, and laying both on the head of a sick man."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1820,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmp:Fa a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Fa, der Wurf der sechzehn Palmnüsse"@de,
        "Fa, the casting of the sixteen palm nuts"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. A. Skertchly, Dahomey as it is (London: Chapman and Hall, 1874), pp. 474-476" ;
    skos:definition "Palm nuts are held in one hand and thrown into the other, one or two being dropped on purpose during the throw; if one nut is left a single mark is made and if two are left a double mark. Sixteen principal figures are called mothers and are set down with their marks and a symbol apiece; further figures called children are formed by taking the right side of one mother and the left side of another. The first and the last of the mothers are held to be the most powerful, and their children the most important."@en ;
    skos:note "The figure names are given by both witnesses in nearly the same forms, from the first, called the mother of all, to the sixteenth, called the father of all. They are the names of the Ifá figures, which is one half of the reason the contact route on this tradition is entered as documented."@en,
        "The second witness, who says he was himself taught the craft, gives the same casting with a differently proportioned board and puts the marks the other way round, adding of his own accord that the contrary may be the case. Two travellers who did not work from one another thus agree on the procedure and disagree on the reading rule, and the file keeps both."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by Fon practitioners at Abomey and Whydah, unnamed in this passage; recorded by the naturalist J. A. Skertchly during his detention in Dahomey in 1871, published 1874; given in Fon, of which the work prints the figure names and a few terms, the account itself in English." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Vodun .

pmp:FangEyenMirrorConsultation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Blick in das éyèn im mvul-Horn auf die Gestalten, die vorbeiziehen"@de,
        "Looking into the éyèn in the mvul horn at the figures that pass"@en ;
    dcterms:source "H. Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan (Münster i. W.: Aschendorffsche Verlagsbuchhandlung, 1912), p. 355" ;
    skos:definition "The practice the recorder reveals as the secret of the mvul, the great antelope horn that is with the bones of ngure and mvi the most important part of the akaméyôn. The end of the mvul is closed by a mirror, éyèn or éyèna. It is in this mirror that the chief, after the prescribed ceremonies and rites, looks at the figures that pass, whether the matter is an initiation, or the discovery of thieves, or any other hidden fact. Inside the horn are a fragment of the skull of one of the ancestors, a fragment of the mvamayôn, ashes from the sacrifice, and nail parings, hair and dried blood and mucus of the bearer himself."@en ;
    skos:note "The practice belongs to the same complex as the Bouiti mirror and is reported by the other of the two recorders, in his own voice and for the other people. In both the vision is read in a mirror set in a fetish, in both an initiation is one of the occasions, and in both the mirror is the instrument of the decision. Whether that makes two reports of one procedure or one procedure and a parallel is the question the tradition note leaves open."@en,
        "The recorder says of the secret that the chief Esôya had not revealed it to the earlier author he names, and that he is giving it here; and he declines to go into the mirror itself on the ground that it is of a purely fetishistic order and not his subject. So the fullest statement in the book about the instrument of the vision is made in passing and against the grain of the chapter it stands in."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by H. Trilles among the Fang of the French Congo, published 1912, in his own voice. The informant for the content of the horn is not named; the chief Esôya is named as the man who had withheld the secret from an earlier author. The Fang terms mvul, éyèn, éyèna, akaméyôn and mvamayôn are carried; no Fang sentence is. The recorder adds an observation of his own: he was able to procure only a single specimen of the mica sheets the trade mirrors have everywhere replaced." ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LeTotemismeChezLesFan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bwiti .

pmp:FangMelanEvomInitiation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Essen des mélan beim evôm: ein Teller geriebener Rinde bei den ersten Proben"@de,
        "Eating the mélan at the evôm: a plateful of grated bark at the first ordeals"@en ;
    dcterms:source "H. Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan (Münster i. W.: Aschendorffsche Verlagsbuchhandlung, 1912), p. 271, footnote 1" ;
    skos:definition "The Fang initiation as the recorder gives it in his own voice. At the initiation feast, the evôm, those who are to be initiated still today eat the mélan. In the initiation feasts proper, at the first ordeals, the patients are made to absorb a plateful of grated mélan bark, and that is the bark of Tabernanthe Iboga (Baillon), a shrub of one to two metres, whose alkaloid ibogaïne is soporific and aphrodisiac. In the ordeals and initiations of the second degree, under the same name mélan, the bark of Erythrophlœum guineense is used."@en ;
    skos:note "The recorder is not a botanist and the identifications are his. He gives the authority for the iboga determination - Baillon - and gives the alkaloid of each plant and the pharmacological character of each, which is the vocabulary of the pharmacological literature of about 1901 to 1905 and not of the Fang. What belongs to the transmission in this passage is the word mélan, the name of the feast, and the fact of the eating; the rest is the identification of a European reader."@en,
        "This is the only place in the read passages where Tabernanthe iboga is named, and it is named for the Fang and not for the Bouiti of Sette Cama, whose root the same book identifies as Strychnos icaja. The transmission the registration describes - an initiation carried by a root preparation - is therefore attested at the wording twice over, with two different plants, by two recorders in one volume."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by H. Trilles, a Holy Ghost missionary of fifteen years' residence among the Fang of the French Congo, and published by him in 1912. The speaker is not named: the passage stands in the recorder's own voice as a footnote to a Fang song he prints, and he says of the custom that it is still practised today. The language of the gathering is Fang; the recorder prints the Fang terms mélan and evôm and the Fang wording of the song he is annotating, but the note itself is his French and carries no Fang sentence." ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LeTotemismeChezLesFan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bwiti .

pmp:FastBeforeTakingOffice a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Fasten vor dem Antritt eines höheren Amtes"@de,
        "The fast before entering a major office"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III" ;
    skos:definition "Whoever is to enter one of the greater offices fasts: a month, in other places six, in others a year, eating neither salt nor chilli, not sleeping with his wife, not washing and not combing. In some places he must not bring his hands to his body, and one who kept it said that in the time of his fast he scratched himself with a little stick."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmp:FinnicLuonnonNostatus a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Erheben der eigenen Natur vor der Arbeit"@de,
        "Raising one's own Nature before the work"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John Abercromby, The Pre- and Proto-historic Finns, vol. II (London: David Nutt, 1898), § 12 a-c, pp. 83-84, with the occasion stated at p. 3" ;
    skos:altLabel "haltia"@en,
        "luonnon nostatus"@en,
        "luonto"@en ;
    skos:definition "A formula the operator says over himself and not over the patient. He calls his Nature from its hole and his genius from under a fallen tree, from beneath a stone, from out the moss; he calls his Nature that is hard as stone and his hair that is tough as iron, and the Nature of his mother, his father and his ancestor in addition to his own; he asks to be clothed with a burning coat and with furs of fiery red. The collection names the occasion: it is said for steeling the nerves while conjuring the sick and removing obstacles."@en ;
    skos:note "Two of its lines are the transmission's own account of what the operator brings to the work, and they are inherited rather than acquired: his mother's Nature and his father's and his ancestor's are called up beside his own. That is a prerequisite located in descent, which is not a condition anyone could set, and it stands in the same formula as the one condition anyone could — that the man says these words before he begins."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:FinnicIncantation .

pmp:FireReading a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Lesen am Verbrennen von Fett und Coca"@de,
        "Reading the burning of fat and coca"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Cristóbal de Molina, Relación de las fábulas y ritos de los incas, ed. Paloma Jiménez del Campo, transcr. Paloma Cuenca Muñoz (Madrid: Iberoamericana / Frankfurt: Vervuert, 2010), fol. 6v, on the virapiricoc" ;
    skos:definition "Those who burned llama fat and coca in the fire and saw in the shapes and the marks made at the time of the burning what was to come, and told it to whoever consulted them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmp:FourteenfoldMastery a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Beherrschung des Geistes in vierzehn Weisen"@de,
        "Control of the mind in fourteen ways"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.2 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "A numbered course of mastery over the meditation objects, among them the order of the kasiṇa and its reverse, skipping the absorption, skipping the object, and the definition of the object."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmp:Hanblapi a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Hanblapi, das Suchen einer Mitteilung"@de,
        "Hanblapi, seeking a communication"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 68 and p. 218" ;
    skos:definition "The seeker strips and wears only a robe, a breechclout and moccasins, takes a pipe, smoking material and a knife, and goes to the top of a high place where others are not likely to intrude. There he clears a space of every living or growing thing large enough to sit or lie on, and on entering it invokes the Four Winds that they may not bring inclement weather upon him. He may stand, sit or lie, awake or asleep, and may smoke as often as he wishes, but he must not leave the space he has prepared and must neither eat nor drink while the quest lasts."@en ;
    skos:note "The second account in the same volume, given as the origin of the Buffalo ceremony, prescribes four passages through the vitalizing lodge first and sends the seeker up naked with a robe, a pipe and willow bark, and has him invoke four named gods and then call the spirit of the buffalo. The two accounts differ in the clothing and in the number of purifications and agree on the solitude, the high place, the fasting and the waiting. Both are printed in the same paper and neither is preferred here."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by named Oglala shamans, chiefly Sword, Tyon, Ringing-shield, Bad-wound and No-flesh, and by Finger on 25 March 1914; recorded by the agency physician J. R. Walker at Pine Ridge over his years of service there, closed December 1916; given in Lakota, part of it written out in Lakota by Sword and Tyon and translated by Burt Means, Thomas Mills, Bruce Means and Antoin Herman; the paper prints the translations only, so the Lakota wording is not carried." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LakotaRitual .

pmp:HippocratesAlvusAperienda a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das sanfte Öffnen des Leibes"@de,
        "Opening the bowels gently"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, The Book of Prognostics 23, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, p. 254" ;
    skos:definition "Named in the same chapter only as a step preceding the operation on the uvula, and not described: the bowels are to be opened gently beforehand. The treatise gives neither the means nor a measure for it here, and it is entered as a node because the prerequisite claim above has to point at something that a later reader can go and look for in the rest of the collection."@en ;
    skos:note "What was read gives this no procedure of its own. The place where this corpus does describe evacuating is the Aphorisms, Section VI, already in this file at pmp:HippocratesIncisioEtCauterium and pmc:HippocratesEvacuatioCeleris, and that is about emptying an abscess and not the bowels. A run that opens On Regimen in Acute Diseases, which stands in this same volume, should look there."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PrognosticonHippocratis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine .

pmp:HippocratesIncisioCapitis a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Eröffnen einer Kopfwunde durch Schnitt"@de,
        "Laying open a wound of the head by incision"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, On Injuries of the Head 13 and 14, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, pp. 457-459" ;
    skos:definition "Performed where the bone is denuded of flesh and the wound has not length and breadth enough for the bone to be inspected, so that it cannot be seen what mischief the blow has done. The incision is made proportionate to the wound, the flesh is separated from the bone, and the whole wound is filled with a tent which expands it by the next day; a hollow wound is cut up where a medicine cannot reach, and a circular one is given a double incision lengthways to make it long."@en ;
    skos:note "The purpose stated is inspection and not drainage, which is what ties this practice to the scraping and to the three-day window: the treatise builds a sequence of three steps whose point is to find out what cannot be seen before deciding whether to open the bone."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeVulneribusCapitis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine .

pmp:HippocratesIncisioEtCauterium a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Behandlung von Empyem oder Wassersucht durch Schnitt oder Brenneisen"@de,
        "Treating empyema or dropsy by incision or the cautery"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, Aphorisms VI.27, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. II, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849" ;
    skos:definition "The operation the section names only in order to state the condition under which it kills: cases of empyema or dropsy treated by incision or by the cautery, so that the collected water or pus is let out. Nothing is said about where the incision is made, with what, or how much is to be taken."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because the caution beside it needs something to warn about and because the act is named in the text rather than supplied here. The thinness is the same thinness as at the venesection node and has the same explanation: the Aphorisms assemble sentences and leave the procedures to the treatises they were assembled from."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AphorismiHippocratis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine .

pmp:HippocratesPrologesis a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das vorherige Ansagen, wer stirbt und wer durchkommt"@de,
        "Announcing beforehand who will die and who will recover"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, The Book of Prognostics 1 and 25, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, pp. 234-235 and 255-256" ;
    skos:altLabel "προλέγειν"@grc ;
    skos:definition "The operation the treatise prescribes: having formed a judgment from the whole of the signs, the physician says in advance, in the presence of the sick and of those around, which patients will die, which will recover, and in which cases the disease will be protracted. It is performed before the outcome and in public, and the treatise gives twenty-four chapters of signs as its means."@en ;
    skos:note "Separated from the capacity because the treatise separates them: chapter 1 says what the knowledge is for and chapter 25 says what the announcing requires, which is a judgment from the ensemble of the signs weighed against one another, together with the epidemic influx and the constitution of the season."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PrognosticonHippocratis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine .

pmp:HippocratesTrepanumRefrigeratio a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Kühlen des Trepans"@de,
        "Cooling the trepan"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, On Injuries of the Head 21, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, p. 465" ;
    skos:definition "The interrupting step the twenty-first chapter requires of the operation it describes: the instrument is frequently taken out of the bone and plunged in cold water, and is then returned to the sawing."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeVulneribusCapitis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine .

pmp:HippocratesVenaeSectio a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Öffnen einer Vene"@de,
        "Opening a vein"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, Aphorisms VI.36 and VI.47, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. II, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849" ;
    skos:definition "The operation as the section states it, which is in two sentences and no more: venesection cures dysuria, and the internal veins of the arm are the ones to open; and persons who are benefited by venesection or purging should be bled or purged in spring. No posture, no quantity, no instrument, no sign of a good opening and no list of persons who are not to be opened."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered with the thinness showing, because the thinness is the comparison. The Susruta devotes a whole lesson of its anatomical book to this same operation — the persons unfit for it, the three times of day and year at which it may be done, the appearance of a well-pierced vein, the quantity to be taken, twenty named ways of opening a vein badly — and the Aphorisms give two lines. Whether the rest of the Hippocratic collection carries the apparatus is a question for the treatises this file names and does not open."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AphorismiHippocratis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine .

pmp:HittTaksanTittanumar a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Wiederzusammensetzen des streitenden Paares"@de,
        "Setting the quarrelling pair together again"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Maštigga ritual against domestic quarrel, CTH 404.1.II, KBo 2.3+ Vs. I 5-Rs. IV 6 (critical edition, exemplar A, hethiter.net/: CTH 404.1.II, Expl. A, textus 2012-05-07)" ;
    skos:definition "The procedure performed over both quarrellers together: tongues of wax, of dough and of clay, hands of dough and figures of dough are waved over them and thrown into the hearth; salt, tallow and tissue are flattened by them with the left hand; water and dough are waved over them and they are declared clean of mouth and tongue; a white sheep, a black sheep and a piglet are presented as substitutes and the pair spit into the mouth of each; a kneading-bowl of clay with black cumin, and a second with oil and a severed thread of blue wool, are waved over them; a ḫupuwai-vessel filled with wine, oil, honey, figs, raisins, tissue, salt and tallow is poured into the hearth and broken; a further sheep is presented to the Sun-god as the scapegoat; seven stone pillars set between two fires are overturned by the pair with their feet and thrown into the fire; their fine garments and the vessel from their heads are taken off them and broken; their limbs are rubbed down with the Sun's hay; and finally they pour salted water over their heads and rinse their hands and eyes."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Mastigga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual .

pmp:HittUdukranzaPenniyawar a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Treiben des bekränzten Widders auf die Feindstraße"@de,
        "Driving the crowned ram onto the enemy's road"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Uḫḫamuwa, CTH 410, HT 1 Vs. II 20-47 (critical edition, exemplar A, hethiter.net/: CTH 410, textus 2013-12-09)" ;
    skos:definition "The procedure: a ram is driven up, blue, red, yellow, black and white wool are twined together into a wreath and the ram is crowned with it, and the ram is driven out onto the road leading to the enemy with a speech addressed to whichever deity of the enemy land has caused the death, that the crowned ram has been driven to that deity for peace-making, and that as the strong one makes peace toward this ram, so the deity should make peace toward the land of Ḫatti and turn back to it in favour; then the crowned sheep is driven away into the enemy land. Fodder and mutton fat are brought for the deity's horses with a speech that the horses should eat and be satisfied and the chariot be greased and the deity turn to its own land and in favour to Ḫatti; and finally a he-goat is offered to the Heptad, a sheep is killed and cooked, and cheese, sour bread, a pulla-vessel, a huppar of wine, a huppar of beer and fruit are prepared for the deity of the road."@en ;
    skos:note "Read in the original and in one translation. The translation, which is of a different manuscript of the same ritual, has the goat consecrated to the Heptad and one sheep consecrated to the Sun-god; the manuscript read here has the goat to the Heptad and the sheep killed and cooked, with no Sun-god in the clause. The divergence is of the kind a second exemplar explains and the second exemplar was not collated."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Uhhamuwa ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual .

pmp:HolinCommandingTheRiverToDivide a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Einen Fluss anreden und ihn teilen lassen"@de,
        "Addressing a river and ordering it to divide"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Holin 7a, R. Pinhas b. Jair at the river Ginaj, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. VIII (Leipzig: Otto Harrassowitz, 1909), pp. 820-821" ;
    skos:definition "R. Pinhas b. Jair, travelling to ransom captives, meets the river Ginaj and says: Ginaj, divide your water for me, that I may pass through you. The river answers that he may or may not accomplish his errand while it certainly accomplishes its own; he replies that if it does not divide he will decree that water shall never flow through it again, and it divides. He then has it divide twice more, once for a man carrying wheat for Pesah and once for an Arab travelling with them, the second on the ground that nobody should be able to say that this is how a companion is treated."@en ;
    skos:note "The operation is harvested because the pages were open and because a capacity passed over is something that was there to be learnt and that nobody will notice again. It is a plain address, with no preparation, no formula, no time and no instrument, and the one condition anywhere near it is in the river's objection rather than in the procedure."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliHolin ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmp:IniTiConstruction a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Errichten der Ini ti"@de,
        "Building the ini ti"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), pp. 66-67" ;
    skos:definition "The making of the lodge itself: slender saplings thrust into the ground in a circle whose diameter is a little longer than the height of a tall man, their tops bent and bound to form a dome, the whole covered with robes so as to confine the vapour, and an opening large enough for a man to crawl through made anywhere on the border except toward the north, and closable."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by named Oglala shamans, chiefly Sword, Tyon, Ringing-shield, Bad-wound and No-flesh, and by Finger on 25 March 1914; recorded by the agency physician J. R. Walker at Pine Ridge over his years of service there, closed December 1916; given in Lakota, part of it written out in Lakota by Sword and Tyon and translated by Burt Means, Thomas Mills, Bruce Means and Antoin Herman; the paper prints the translations only, so the Lakota wording is not carried." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LakotaRitual .

pmp:Inipi a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Inipi, die Belebungshütte"@de,
        "Inipi, the vitalizing lodge"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Frances Densmore, Teton Sioux Music (Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 61, Washington, 1918), p. 98",
        "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), pp. 66-67 and p. 156" ;
    skos:definition "Saplings set in a circle a little wider than a tall man is high, bent and bound into a dome and covered so as to hold the vapour, with an opening anywhere on the border except toward the north. The one who vitalizes strips and crawls in naked with a pipe and tobacco; assistants heat stones outside, pass them and the water in and close the opening; those inside place the stones at the centre and pour water on them at intervals, and what rises is held to be the released spirit-like of the water, which cannot escape upward and must therefore enter the bodies exposed to it. What comes out again on the skin carries the harmful things with it and is wiped or washed away. In the simplest form it lasts as long as two pipefuls take to smoke, and it may be prolonged for a day and a night or longer."@en ;
    skos:note "The name is given in the corpus both as Inipi and as Ini kaga, and the lodge as ini ti. The second witness calls it the vapor lodge and reports one built by each band beside its camp at the Sun dance for the use of those who took part."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by named Oglala shamans, chiefly Sword, Tyon, Ringing-shield, Bad-wound and No-flesh, and by Finger on 25 March 1914; recorded by the agency physician J. R. Walker at Pine Ridge over his years of service there, closed December 1916; given in Lakota, part of it written out in Lakota by Sword and Tyon and translated by Burt Means, Thomas Mills, Bruce Means and Antoin Herman; the paper prints the translations only, so the Lakota wording is not carried." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LakotaRitual .

pmp:InuitWordsSpokenAtBirth a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die bei der Geburt gesprochenen Worte"@de,
        "The words spoken at a birth"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 28" ;
    skos:definition "The uttering of efficacious words over a person at birth, said to determine that the person will be able to come to life again after dying."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InuitAngakkuq .

pmp:JainaAlocana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Sündenbekenntnis vor dem Lehrer"@de,
        "Confession of sins before the teacher"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX.5-7 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:altLabel "ālocanā"@en ;
    skos:definition "The fifth item: the confession of one's sins in the presence of the Guru, distinguished in the same list from repenting of them to oneself and from repenting of them before the Guru, which are the sixth and the seventh."@en ;
    skos:note "Three neighbouring items that a summary would collapse into one. The lecture keeps them apart and gives each a different result: confession removes the thorns, self-repentance produces an ascending scale of virtues, repentance before the teacher produces humiliation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmp:JainaAnupreksha a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Nachsinnen über das Gelernte"@de,
        "Pondering on what has been learned"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX.22, with the fivefold division at XXX.34 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:altLabel "anuprekṣā"@en ;
    skos:definition "The twenty-second item, one of the five parts into which the same lecture and the thirtieth divide study: saying or learning the lesson, questioning the teacher, repetition, pondering, and religious discourse."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmp:JainaMahaviraUpadhana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das eigene Regime des Stifters aus Fasten und Aussetzung"@de,
        "The founder's own regimen of fasting and exposure"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ācārāṅga Sūtra I.8.4.3-7 (Jacobi, SBE 22, Oxford 1884; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 1964)" ;
    skos:definition "The regimen the ninth lecture of the Ācārāṅga reports of Mahāvīra in the first person of the tradition: in the cold season he meditated in the shade, in summer he exposed himself to the heat and sat squatting in the sun; he lived on rice, pounded jujube and beans, and sustained himself on these three for eight months; sometimes he did not drink for half a month or a month, sometimes for more than two months or even six months, day and night; sometimes he ate only the sixth meal, or the eighth, the tenth, the twelfth."@en ;
    skos:note "The counted meal is the unit and not the day: the sixth, the eighth, the tenth, the twelfth meal. That is the same kind of count the temporary fast is built from and it is the form in which this transmission states a quantity when it states one at all."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmp:JainaPapavidyabhyasa a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Ausüben der Künste für den Unterhalt"@de,
        "Practising the arts for one's keep"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Sūtrakṛtāṅga Sūtra II.2.27 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:definition "The act the same passage condemns, and it condemns it under a stated condition: these and similar sciences are practised by some men for the sake of food, drink, clothes, a lodging, a bed, and various objects of pleasure."@en ;
    skos:note "The motive is in the sentence and it is what the sentence turns on. What is named is not the exercise of the art but living by it, and the verdict that follows — they practise a wrong science, the unworthy, the mistaken men — is passed on men so described."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmp:JainaSamlekhanaItvara a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das für den religiösen Tod ausgebreitete Stroh"@de,
        "The straw spread for the religious death"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ācārāṅga Sūtra I.7.6.3-4 (Jacobi, SBE 22, Oxford 1884; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 1964)" ;
    skos:altLabel "itvara"@en ;
    skos:definition "The procedure the sixth lesson prescribes. If the thought occurs to a monk that he is sick and not able at this time to regularly mortify the flesh, that monk should regularly reduce his food, and reducing his food and diminishing his sins should take proper care of his body, being immovable like a beam. He should then beg for straw, retire with it to a secluded spot, repeatedly examine and clean the ground where there are no eggs, living beings, seeds, sprouts, dew, water, ants, mildew, waterdrops, mud or cobwebs, spread the straw on it, and there and then effect the death called itvara."@en ;
    skos:note "The entry condition is an incapacity and not an achievement: the procedure begins where the ordinary austerity can no longer be carried on. The transmission therefore states the end of its own principal practice as the occasion for its last one."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmp:JainaSamvega a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Verlangen nach Erlösung"@de,
        "The longing for liberation"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX.1 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:altLabel "saṃvega"@en ;
    skos:definition "The first item of the lecture: the longing for liberation, held as something the soul does rather than something that befalls it, since the lecture asks what the soul obtains by it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmp:JainaTejolesyaTapas a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die strenge Askese, durch die die Feuerkraft erlangt wird"@de,
        "The severe ascetic discipline by which the fiery power is obtained"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Viyāhapannatti (Bhagavaī) XV, Calcutta edition p. 1220" ;
    skos:definition "The discipline the book names and does not describe in either translation read. Gosāla, terrified by what he has just been told, wishes to know how the man had acquired his power; Mahāvīra explains to him the severe ascetic discipline by which he had obtained it. Leumann's extract renders the same sentence as: I explained to him the austere discipline which it required, and he thought of undergoing it."@en ;
    skos:note "A practice node whose content is a blank is unusual and is entered deliberately. The transmission asserts that the power is acquired by a nameable course, that a particular person taught it to another on a particular occasion, and that the other then carried it out; the two translations that this holding could obtain withhold what the course consists of. Leaving the node out would make the withholding invisible, and the withholding is what a later reader with the Prakrit edition can repair."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "A. F. Rudolf Hoernle, The Uvāsagadasāo, vol. II, Appendix I (Bibliotheca Indica, Calcutta 1888), p. 3; and Ernst Leumann, Appendix I: Extracts from Bhagavatī XV, in W. W. Rockhill, The Life of the Buddha (London: Trubner 1884), p. 251" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmp:JainaVaiyavrittya a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Dienst an den zehn"@de,
        "Doing service to the ten"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX.43, with the ten kinds at XXX.33 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:altLabel "vaiyāvṛttya"@en ;
    skos:definition "The forty-third item: giving one's assistance as well as one is able, in ten kinds according to whom it is rendered to, the teacher being the first of them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmp:JeuMysteriaTradere a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Mysterien weitergeben"@de,
        "Handing the mysteries on"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Das zweite Buch Jeû, Handschriftenseiten 55-56, deutsche Übersetzung in Carl Schmidt, Gnostische Schriften in koptischer Sprache aus dem Codex Brucianus, Texte und Untersuchungen VIII 1-2, Leipzig 1892, S. 194-195" ;
    skos:definition "The act the text regulates before it teaches anything: the disciples are to keep the mysteries and give them to those who are worthy of them, that is, to those who have left the whole world and all its affairs and its gods and deities behind them and hold no other faith than the faith in the light after the manner of the children of the light, listening to one another and subordinating themselves to one another as children of the light."@en ;
    skos:note "The class of permitted recipient is defined by what has been given up and by a form of conduct towards other recipients, and not by descent, office or initiation already held. That is worth recording because the same page forbids passing the mysteries to whole classes of people and names them by their cult and not by their birth."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu .

pmp:JeuMysteriumContraKakian a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Mysterium, das die Unbill der Archonten beseitigt"@de,
        "The mystery that removes the wickedness of the archons"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Das zweite Buch Jeû, Handschriftenseiten 67-68, deutsche Übersetzung in Carl Schmidt, Gnostische Schriften in koptischer Sprache aus dem Codex Brucianus, Texte und Untersuchungen VIII 1-2, Leipzig 1892, S. 204-205" ;
    skos:definition "The fourth rite. A censer is built on thalassia; vine branches, juniper, malabathron, a plant Schmidt could not identify, asbestos, agate and frankincense are laid on; all the disciples are wrapped in linen garments and crowned with artemisia; frankincense is put in their mouth and the cipher of the first Amen, 530, in their hand; they set their feet close together and remain before the incense. They are sealed with a seal whose true name is zezezoiazoz and whose interpretation is zozozai; then the prayer is spoken, which invokes the imperishable names of the light-treasure and asks the Father to compel Sabaoth Adamas and all his leaders to come and take their wickedness away from the disciples; and after the prayer they are sealed with the seal of the second Amen, whose true name is zachozakos and whose interpretation is zchozozo."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the rite whose cipher is not 9879. It carries the cipher of the first Amen, and it is the only one of the four whose prayer asks the Father to compel a named archon rather than to send a power or a sign. It is also the only one of the four that is sealed twice, before the prayer and after it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu .

pmp:JeuNomenMagnum a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der eine Name, der in jedem Ort gesagt wird"@de,
        "The one name that is said in every place"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Das erste Buch Jeû, Handschriftenseiten 45-47, deutsche Übersetzung in Carl Schmidt, Gnostische Schriften in koptischer Sprache aus dem Codex Brucianus, Texte und Untersuchungen VIII 1-2, Leipzig 1892, S. 185-186" ;
    skos:definition "The procedure the disciples ask for and receive: they had asked for a single name that would suffice for all the places, and were told they would be given it once they had been shown all the places. Standing in the place of the Outer, they are to name it, seal themselves with this seal, whose name is given, grasp the cipher 7856 in the hand, say that name first, then turn to the four corners of the treasury they are in, seal themselves and say its name with the cipher in the hand, then say the name once, invoking it and turning to the four corners; and then say the formula addressing the watchers of the sixty treasuries."@en ;
    skos:note "It is expressly not the name of the Father. The disciples ask whether it is the great name of the Father who exists from the beginning, and the answer is no: it is the name of the great power that is in all places."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu .

pmp:Khecari a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Khecarī"@de,
        "Khecarī"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.32-33 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.37 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "A seal in which the tongue is turned back and set above the palate while the gaze rests between the brows; the manual describes a months-long preparation of the tongue before the position can be held."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmp:Kimenga a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Kimenga, das erneuernde Opfer"@de,
        "Kimenga, the sacrifice that renews the charged thing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), pp. 235-236" ;
    skos:definition "A fowl or a goat is killed and its blood poured over the figure or the bundle, or the toe of a fowl or of a frog is cut and the blood allowed to fall on it. The corpus gives the rule as a schedule and a scale: to be kept at full power the sacrifice should be made regularly, at every new moon or on certain market days, and the larger the favour expected the more costly the sacrifice must be, so that a charged thing receiving only the drippings from a frog's toe is not to be expected to confer large favours. The flesh may not be sold but is eaten by the man who offers it and his clan, the offering is made at sunset with the face turned to the sun, and a pig is never offered."@en ;
    skos:note "Recorded with its scale because the scale is what makes the practice answerable: a transmission that ties the size of the favour to the size of the offering has said something that its own practitioners can find false, and the corpus elsewhere records exactly that complaint in the form of a charged thing that failed."@en,
        "The recorder adds that his informants could not say why the pig is excluded, and he supplies a guess of his own. The guess is his and is not entered."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by Bakongo of the Lower Congo at San Salvador and in the Ngombe Lutete district, mostly unnamed in this passage; recorded by the Baptist missionary John H. Weeks over some thirty years of residence, the book closed in 1913; given in Kikongo, of which the work prints the terms in the text while the accounts themselves are printed in English only." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:KongoNkisi .

pmp:Krtyapratiharana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Zurückwenden des Zaubers auf seinen Urheber"@de,
        "The turning back of the witchcraft upon its maker"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Atharvaveda Saṃhitā 5.14 and 10.1, in the translation of W. D. Whitney, revised by C. R. Lanman, Harvard Oriental Series 7 and 8, Cambridge, Mass. 1905" ;
    skos:definition "A rite performed with a plant, addressed to the hostile spell itself as to a person. The spell is taken by the hand and led away; it is set straight in front of its maker that it may smite him; it is conducted to him like a horse by a halter; it is told to go as a son to a father, to bite like a trampled constrictor, to fly straighter than an arrow, to run back like an easy chariot. The second and longer hymn adds that the spell is bathed off the person it was set upon, that it is to go to the one who put its joints together, and that the person it was sent against is unknown to it."@en ;
    skos:note "The operation is not a defence. Nothing in either hymn asks that the spell be dissolved, absorbed or made harmless; both ask that it arrive, intact and working, somewhere else. What is transmitted is a redirection, and the thing redirected is assumed throughout to be real, individuated and in motion."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Atharvavedic .

pmp:Makutu a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Makutu"@de,
        "Makutu"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 31" ;
    skos:definition "The art of bringing the resentment of another person's own family atua down upon them, worked upon something that has been part of them or has touched them: a portion of spittle, or what is left of their food."@en ;
    skos:note "The operation runs through the same transmissibility by contact that tapu runs through, which is why the two belong in one file and not in two."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Maori .

pmp:MandaeanTarmidaTarasa a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Aufsetzen der Krone: die Weihe eines Tarmida"@de,
        "The setting up of the crown: consecration of a tarmida"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran: Their Cults, Customs, Magic, Legends, and Folklore (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), pp. 146-165" ;
    skos:definition "The consecration of a priest of the first degree, which the transmission calls the setting up of the crown on him. Seven priests at least and a ganzibra baptize one another; a sheep is killed beside the water as a ransom for the novice; the assembled priests examine his body to establish that it is without blemish; a reed hut is built and the novice recites the whole Book of Souls from memory while the priests follow with open copies to see that no mistake is made. He and his master keep vigil in the hut through the night. At dawn the hut is pulled down and he enters a second hut, where he is invested with ring, crowns, wreath and staff and remains a week without sleeping. Sixty days of purity follow, in which he lives apart, prepares his own food, immerses three times a day and receives three secret words each day. At the end he celebrates a masiqta alone and by heart in the name of the ganzibra who taught him."@en ;
    skos:note "The recital by heart is a condition and not a display: a novice must be word perfect in the Book of Souls, in the whole ritual of the masiqta and in the rahmi before he is considered worthy of priesthood, and at the masiqta the ganzibra de-consecrates his own dress so as to become a spectator and stands by holding the text in his hand to prompt his pupil if he stumbles. The transmission builds its own error protection into the rite, and it does so at the one point where the candidate is alone."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by E. S. Drower in Iraq and published in English in 1937. She states at this place that she had not witnessed the ceremonies herself but had several informants who related them in great detail and whose descriptions tallied; the Mandaic terms are carried in her transliteration beside the English. The rite as she gives it is therefore reported speech from named and unnamed priests and not observation, and she says so."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean .

pmp:MangIWistaspan a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Trunk aus Wein und dem Mang des Wischtasp"@de,
        "The drinking of wine and the mang of Wishtasp"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The Book of Arda Viraf, ed. and trans. Martin Haug, E. W. West and Hoshang Jamaspji Asa (Bombay and London: Trübner, 1872), 1.28-43 and 2.18-31" ;
    skos:altLabel "bang"@en,
        "mang"@en,
        "mang i Wishtaspan"@en ;
    skos:definition "A procedure for sending a soul out of a living body and getting a report back. The transmission states it as a sequence with numbers. From the whole assembly seven men without doubt are set apart, from the seven three, from the three one; the lot is cast three times, under the names good thought, good word and good deed, and all three fall to the same man. He prays for the departed, eats, makes his testament, washes head and body, puts on new clothes, perfumes himself, has a clean carpet spread on a prepared couch, consecrates the dron and remembers the souls, and eats again. Then three golden cups of wine and the mang of Wishtasp are given him, one spoken over with each of the three names."@en ;
    skos:note "The order in which the preparation is given differs slightly between the verses as numbered and the sequence a reader reconstructs; the testament stands close to the prayer, and the second meal after the consecration. What is fixed and not reconstructed is the count: seven, three, one, three lots, three cups."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Zoroastrian .

pmp:MayaIdolCarving a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Schnitzen der Götterbilder im Monat Mol"@de,
        "The carving of idols in the month Mol"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Diego de Landa, Yucatan Before and After the Conquest, translated by William Gates (Baltimore: The Maya Society, publication 20, 1937), section XL, pp. 76-77" ;
    skos:definition "The making of new wooden images, begun by the priest, the chacs and the carver together with a fast. During the work they were not to consort with their wives, the witness adds even in thought, and nobody could enter the place where they worked. Blood was drawn from the ears and incense burned."@en ;
    skos:note "The condition that reaches furthest is the one on thought. A rule about an act can be kept and seen to be kept; a rule about what may pass through the mind of the worker has no observer, and a failure of the work can always be referred back to it. That is the shape of a claim that cannot fail, and it is worth marking where a transmission takes it on."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MayaRitual .

pmp:MayaNewYearFast a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Fasten vor dem Neujahr"@de,
        "The fast before the new year"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Diego de Landa, Yucatan Before and After the Conquest, translated by William Gates (Baltimore: The Maya Society, publication 20, 1937), section XL, p. 70" ;
    skos:definition "A fast kept by the chiefs, the priests and the devout before the festival of the first month. The witness gives a floor and not an average: none for less than thirteen days. Some began three months before, some two, others as they chose. To continence was added the giving up of salt and of chili in the food, which the witness says was counted a grave penitential act."@en ;
    skos:note "The same section of the same work records a second and separate fast, kept on receiving duties for the festivals and on undertaking duties of state, which it says at times lasted as long as three years. That is a different practice with a different occasion and is not entered here; it stands in prima-materia#462."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MayaRitual .

pmp:MideInitiationFirstDegree a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Einweihung in den ersten Grad"@de,
        "Initiation into the first degree"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 164" ;
    skos:definition "The entry rite of the society: instruction under a paid teacher, often carried over years, gifts to the officiating priests, a feast, and the shooting of the shell into the candidate by the officiating priests."@en ;
    skos:note "The report says candidates sometimes spend a number of years in preparation and impoverish themselves in the payment of fees and the preparation of the feast. The cost is stated as a fact of the institution and not as a warning."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin .

pmp:MithraicProbationes a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Gestufte Proben durch Feuer, Kälte, Hunger und Durst"@de,
        "Graded ordeals of fire, cold, hunger and thirst"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Nonnus the Mythographer, commentary on Gregory Nazianzen, Invectives against Julian (trans. A. S. Geden, Select Passages Illustrating Mithraism, London 1925)" ;
    skos:definition "The training a late Greek commentator reports: that nobody may share in the rites without first passing through the disciplines in succession, of which eighty are counted, ascending and descending, easier at first and then harder — fasting alone in a desert place, immersion in deep water for many days, throwing oneself into fire, prolonged exertion, and the like. One version gives a schedule: fasting about fifty days, then two days of extreme heat, then twenty days in snow, the severity increased by stages."@en ;
    skos:note "Eighty grades, not seven. The number is irreconcilable with every other account and the source is late, derivative and hostile, so the graph holds it as what it is: a claim about this cult made centuries after it ended, by someone commenting on an invective. Two lexica repeat it in abbreviated form, which multiplies the copies and not the witnesses."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mithraic .

pmp:MocharTheHuaca a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Mochar: der Akt der Anrede"@de,
        "Mochar: the act of address"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V" ;
    skos:definition "The act the whole transmission turns on. The priest sits on the ground, extends the left hand towards the huaca, names it, and makes with the lips the sucking sound that is what the word properly means; he says that he comes and brings what its children and creatures offer it, asks it to receive them and not be angry and to give life and health and good fields, pours chicha before it and sometimes over it, smears it with the blood of guinea pigs or llamas, and burns or blows the rest of the offerings."@en ;
    skos:note "The recorder states expressly that to have mochado a huaca does not mean to have seen it or knelt before it, because the adoration does not consist in that. It consists in having given the offering to the priest at the appointed place and joined the invocation with the left hand raised and open. That correction is his, and it is the most useful sentence in the chapter: it says that the act is participation and not sight, which is what makes the next node possible."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmp:Mulabandha a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Wurzelsiegel"@de,
        "The root seal"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.61-63 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "A seal made by pressing the perineum with the heel and contracting the anus, by which the downward wind is drawn upward."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmp:NeidanNeiguan a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Innenschau"@de,
        "Inner observation"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhong-Lü chuandao ji, Lun neiguan 16 and Lun monan 17, in the Chongkan Daozang jiyao recension (JY 149), juan 3, fol. 51a-51b" ;
    skos:definition "The stage at which the practitioner observes inwardly to gather the yang spirit, named as the sixteenth of the eighteen discourses and as the condition under which the spirit is sent out through the heavenly gate."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Neidan .

pmp:NippurAngelCompelledByHornAndHoney a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Eine Hornform kneten und Honig darauf gießen"@de,
        "Kneading a shape of a horn and pouring honey on it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 12: 5-9 with the commentary to 12: 5, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 173-176)" ;
    skos:definition "The one rubric that stands inside the wording of an incantation in this collection. In the course of adjuring the angel that descends from heaven, the text breaks off into a direction for an operation performed on a simulacrum: there being kneaded something in the shape of a horn, on which honey is poured. The adjuration then resumes and the angel so compelled is the one who will frustrate and ban the charms, curses, invocations, knockings and rites listed after it."@en ;
    skos:note "The editor reads the line as a rubric directing an operation that compels the presence of the angel through a simulacrum and its manipulation, and sets it beside the rubrics of the Babylonian magic; he adds the query whether the sorcerer was not reciting a form he did not understand. The identity of the kneaded substance is not given."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmp:NippurPropitiousSeason a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Verrichtung in eine ausgezeichnete Zeit setzen"@de,
        "Setting the operation in a singled-out time"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 6: 5-6, with the mutilated form at 17: 1 and the commentary in introduction § 11, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 55-56 and 141)" ;
    skos:definition "The assumption of a fitting season for the exorcism, carried in a formula that singles the moment out of each larger unit in turn: this day out of all days, this month out of all months, this year out of all years, this season out of all seasons."@en ;
    skos:note "The editor reports the same formula from the Hellenistic papyri, year out of years, month out of months, day out of days, hour out of hours, and holds the bowl form to be a reminiscence of it. He also judges the references in these texts to be quite conventional and concludes that no horoscopes were cast by these sorcerers. A named day, the first of Nisan, stands in a bowl of another collection and not in these."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmp:NippurSpiritSentToTheBridalChamber a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Den Geist in die Brautkammer schicken, zu essen und zu trinken"@de,
        "Sending the spirit to the bridal chamber to eat and drink"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 36: 6-7 with the commentary, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 237-239)" ;
    skos:definition "The close of the pagan charm for a bride: the evil spirit, having been driven from the engraved seal, is directed to go to the bridal chamber and eat, and moreover to drink a libation, and then to depart from the woman."@en ;
    skos:note "An expulsion that ends by provisioning what it expels, and the only one of its kind in what was read. The editor supposes the food and drink would in some way incapacitate the spirit's powers and sets the passage beside the book of Tobit, where Raphael performs a charm against the demon of the bridal chamber. The text is badly obscured at the point where the food is named."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmp:NippurWritingTheLilithNames a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Namen aufschreiben, die die Lilith von sich angab"@de,
        "Writing out the names the Lilith gave of herself"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 42 with the commentary, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 259-261)" ;
    skos:definition "The operation of the childbirth amulet: the fifteen names the Lilith named of herself when she was restrained are written out, together with the opening names, the adjuration of Elija and the enumeration of what she is not to do. The text makes the writing the working part, and the writing is done for a woman in childbirth named in the formula as so-and-so daughter of such-a-one."@en ;
    skos:note "The amulet is the one piece in this collection that is not a bowl and not deposited: it is a written thing whose being written and seen is what the text says binds her. The original of this text was no longer in the Museum when the volume was made, and the editor prints it from a copy that came to his hands."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmp:NkasaTreeCutting a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Schlagen des Ordalbaums, mit Formel und Zeichen"@de,
        "The cutting of the ordeal tree, with its formula and its sign"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), p. 262" ;
    skos:definition "The tree is never cut for any other purpose, and only the ordeal-giver may cut it, using a set formula: that if the person for whom he is cutting is really a witch the blade should bend at the stroke, and if he is not, the blade should enter and the wind stop blowing. The corpus adds the reading of the sign in the world: the air is often quite still for two or three hours before a storm, and that stillness is credited to someone, somewhere, cutting the ordeal tree."@en ;
    skos:note "Two conditions of failure are stated here before the ordeal begins, and the corpus then records what is done with them. The recorder states that although the omens may be in favour of the accused the ordeal-giver proceeds all the same, that the results are not infrequently quite contrary to the omens, and that the belief is unaffected by the discrepancy. A sign that is read, contradicted and kept is a different thing from a sign that is never tested, and the file holds it as the first."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by Bakongo of the Lower Congo, unnamed in this passage; recorded by the Baptist missionary John H. Weeks over some thirty years of residence, the book closed in 1913; given in Kikongo, of which the work prints the terms while the accounts are printed in English only." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:KongoNkisi .

pmp:NkindaAEvata a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Nkind' a evata, das Setzen des Ortszaubers"@de,
        "Nkind' a evata, the setting up of a town charm"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), pp. 220-221" ;
    skos:definition "The inhabitants of a town club together to fee a practitioner of the luck-giving order. A post of a named wood is cut, a hole made in its top and filled with the charm, a hole dug beside the road the women take to the water, a goat killed and its head laid in the hole under the post, the earth rammed round it and the goat's blood poured over the charm. No sacrifice is then made on a schedule; the charm is refreshed with more blood when a misfortune befalls the town."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by Bakongo of the Lower Congo at San Salvador and in the Ngombe Lutete district, mostly unnamed in this passage; recorded by the Baptist missionary John H. Weeks over some thirty years of residence, the book closed in 1913; given in Kikongo, of which the work prints the terms in the text while the accounts themselves are printed in English only." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:KongoNkisi .

pmp:NorseBidjaOkBlota a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Bitten und Opfern, Senden und Schlachten"@de,
        "Asking and offering, sending and slaying"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hávamál 144 (Guðni Jónsson; 145 in Bellows), in Eddukvæði. Sæmundar-Edda, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:definition "The four operations the poem puts as questions in one stanza, beside carving, reading and colouring the runes: knowest thou how one shall ask, how one shall offer, how one shall send, how one shall slay."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmp:NorseHamskipti a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Gestaltwechsel, während der Leib wie schlafend daliegt"@de,
        "Changing shapes while the body lies as if asleep"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Snorri Sturluson, Ynglinga saga, chapter 7, in Heimskringla, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson",
        "The Heimskringla, or Chronicle of the Kings of Norway, translated by Samuel Laing (London 1844), Ynglinga saga, chapter 7" ;
    skos:definition "The operation Snorri ascribes to Óðinn: he changed shapes, and then the body lay as if asleep or dead, while he himself was a bird or a beast, a fish or a serpent, and went in one moment to far lands on his own errands or those of other men."@en ;
    skos:note "A third-party ascription and not a textual attestation of a practice: Snorri is writing in the thirteenth century about a figure he is euhemerising into a king, and the chapter is a catalogue of what the man is said to have known. What it reports is the tradition's claim about him and not an instruction anyone could follow."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmp:OfferingAndWoundBeforeTheDance a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Gabe an die Sonne und die blutende Wunde"@de,
        "The offering to the Sun and the wound that bleeds"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 61 and pp. 116-117" ;
    skos:definition "What is done before a candidate begins to dance: an offering acceptable to the Sun, of as much value as the candidate can afford, and a wound cut in his person that will cause his blood to flow while he dances. In the first form the wound must not be smaller than that made by cutting away a bit of skin as large as a louse and may be as large as the candidate wills; in the other three the wounds are those through which the sticks are thrust."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by named Oglala shamans, chiefly Sword, Tyon, Ringing-shield, Bad-wound and No-flesh, and by Finger on 25 March 1914; recorded by the agency physician J. R. Walker at Pine Ridge over his years of service there, closed December 1916; given in Lakota, part of it written out in Lakota by Sword and Tyon and translated by Burt Means, Thomas Mills, Bruce Means and Antoin Herman; the paper prints the translations only, so the Lakota wording is not carried." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LakotaRitual .

pmp:OloliuhquiConsultation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Trinken des Ololiuhqui, um es zu befragen"@de,
        "Drinking the ololiuhqui in order to question it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulos II and VI, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces" ;
    skos:definition "A seed resembling a lentil is drunk; the recorder says the draught takes away the judgement, being very vehement. Those who drink it consult it as an oracle for everything they wish to know. Some hold the drinking of the seed for such consultations as their office, and such a specialist bears his own name."@en ;
    skos:note "Three things stand in the passage and are kept apart here: that the substance is drunk, that the drinker is questioned as an oracle, and that there is an office for it. Only the third makes this a transmission rather than a usage."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NahuaRitual .

pmp:OphiteTransitusOratio a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die an jedem Tor gesprochene Rede"@de,
        "The speech said at each gate in passing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.31 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)" ;
    skos:definition "Saying, after passing what the text calls the fence of wickedness, a set speech to the ruler of each gate in turn: addressing him by name and office, naming what the speaker brings or has been purified by, asking to be let through, and closing with a request for grace."@en ;
    skos:note "The instruction is given as something the passer is taught beforehand. Origen introduces the speeches as the teaching received after the fence of wickedness, that is as material learned in life for use at the gates, not as words spoken in the moment by whoever happens to arrive."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:OphiteGnosis .

pmp:PalibodhaChedana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Abschneiden der Hemmnisse, mit einem Maß an vier von ihnen"@de,
        "The severing of the impediments, with a measure attached to four of them"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga III.43-50, Pali PTS [93]-[95] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, pp. 108-111; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "The operation the handbook prescribes against each impediment, and it is graded rather than uniform. Against the gains: leave the company and wander alone where one is not known. Against the class of pupils: if they have learnt much, finish with them; if little, hand them to another teacher living not further off than a league; and only if that fails, take leave of them. Against building work: if little remains, finish it; if much and it belongs to the Order, hand it over to the Order; and if there is nobody to hand it to, leave it and go. Against illness: take medicine, and if after some days it has not been relieved, address the body in the terms the handbook gives and go on with the training."@en ;
    skos:note "Three of the four remedies are stated with a measure — a distance of one league, a quantity of work remaining, a number of days — and the fourth with an addressee. That is unusual in this holding and is the reason the practice is entered separately from the list: an instruction with a measure in it can be followed wrongly, and one without cannot."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmp:PdmShenHbs a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Lampenbefragung"@de,
        "The lamp-inquiry"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. VI/1-10, with col. XXV/8-22 and col. XXVII/13-19, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A divination at a lamp. A clean dark cell without light is taken and a new hole dug in an east wall; a white lamp in which no minium or gum-water has been put is filled with clean genuine oil and a clean wick; the spells of praising Ra are recited at dawn and the lit lamp is held opposite the sun and recited to four times; the lamp and the boy are then carried into the cell, the operator being pure and the boy also, and the spells are said seven times to the boy with his eyes closed and the operator's finger on his head. The boy then opens his eyes towards the lamp and sees the shadow of the god about the lamp, and inquires on the operator's behalf. The wick and the oil are varied by the business: a wick of sail-cloth and butter for a damned spirit, a clean wick and pure oil for anything else, ointment of roses to bring a woman to a man."@en ;
    skos:note "The one place in the procedure where the operator's choice is said to matter to the outcome is the wick and the filling, and it is fixed by the kind of question and not by the operator's state. That is an unusually specific instruction and it is also the only one in the three procedures that could be got wrong without anybody noticing."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmp:PesahimAmuletForSixtyDemons a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Amulett fuer die Zahl der anwesenden Daemonen schreiben"@de,
        "Writing an amulet for the number of demons present"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Pesahim 111b, the sixty demons of the service-tree and the question in what respect this matters, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. II (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1901), pp. 710-711" ;
    skos:definition "The operation the passage makes a technical parameter explicit for. Having said that no fewer than sixty demons are to be found at a service-tree near a town, the discussion asks in what respect this matters, and answers: for the writing of an amulet. The amulet is written against a stated number of demons, and the number is to be the number actually present at the place where the seizure happened."@en ;
    skos:note "The question and answer are what make this an operation rather than a piece of demonology. The count is not offered as a fact about the world for its own sake; the discussion asks what turns on it and names the procedure that the count enters as a quantity."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliPesahim ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmp:PesahimSabririFormula a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der abnehmende Name gegen die Blendung beim Wassertrinken in der Nacht"@de,
        "The diminishing name said against blindness when drinking water at night"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Pesahim 112a, the prohibition on drinking water at night and the remedies for thirst, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. II (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1901), pp. 712-713" ;
    skos:definition "The remedy prescribed for a man who must drink water at night from a river or a cistern, where the danger named is blindness. If somebody else is present one says to him, N. son of N., I am thirsty, and then drinks. If nobody is present one says to oneself: my mother told me, beware of the blindness, Sabriri; and then the name is said again with its first letters dropped away one by one. Alternatives given beside it are to recite the verses in which the word voice occurs seven times, or to say a second formula of four names, or to rattle the lid on the mouth of the jug, or to throw something into the water, and then drink."@en ;
    skos:note "Harvested because the pages were open and because the holding counts operations. Two things in it are worth a later reader's attention: the remedy is a list of five alternatives of very different kinds for one danger, any of which suffices, and the first and cheapest of them is simply to speak to another person who is awake. Goldschmidt's note at the place says that the form of the diminishing name in the editio princeps and the other old prints is a misprint, so the number of steps in the sequence is not secure in this edition."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliPesahim ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmp:PgmIvPurificatioTriduana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Drei Tage vorher rein bleiben"@de,
        "Keeping pure for three days beforehand"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the constituting of the great god (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 18 line 15)" ;
    skos:definition "The requirement standing before the meeting with the great god: having kept pure for three days, the operator is to come at dawn towards the east."@en ;
    skos:note "Stated as a span and a direction and nothing else. What purity consists in is not said here; the appendix says elsewhere, of a second person, that he abstains from animal food and the bath, and that is the nearest the piece comes to a content."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmp:PgmIvSystasisMagni a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Zusammentreten mit dem grossen Gott bei der Konjunktion im Loewen"@de,
        "The constituting of the great god at the conjunction in the Lion"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the constituting of the great god (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 18 lines 11-19)" ;
    skos:definition "The operation by which the great god is brought into relation: carrying the aforesaid herb, at the conjunction that occurs in the Lion, the juice is taken and mixed with honey and myrrh; the eight-letter name is written with it on a leaf of the persea; the operator comes at dawn towards the east and licks off the leaf while showing it to the sun, and thus, the text says, the god will hear him perfectly. The consecration is to be begun at the new moon in the Lion, and the leaf is rolled up and put into the rose oil afterwards."@en ;
    skos:note "The stated result is that the god will hear perfectly. That is an improvement of a channel and not an event, so nothing about it could be seen not to have happened, and the piece supplies no sign for it."@en,
        "Two calendar conditions in one operation and they are not the same condition: the juice is taken at a conjunction in a named sign, and the consecration is begun at a new moon in the same sign. Both fix the time by where the luminaries stand and not by a date anyone keeps."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmp:PgmIvUnctioOculorum a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Gesicht eines anderen mit dem Saft der Pflanze und Rosenoel salben"@de,
        "Anointing another person's face with the juice of the herb and rose oil"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the teaching of the operation (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 18 lines 6-8)" ;
    skos:definition "The operation the appendix gives for showing the thing to someone else: the operator takes the juice of the herb called kentritis and anoints with it, together with rose oil, the face of whomever he wishes."@en ;
    skos:note "The only procedure in this piece performed on a second person's body rather than on the operator's own, and the only one whose announced result is a change in what that second person sees. The rite proper is expressly solitary; this is its outward-facing half."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmp:PracticeOfTheLimbs a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Übung der Glieder"@de,
        "Practice of the limbs"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra II.28" ;
    skos:definition "The performance of the eight limbs, taken together as one course of practice."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PatanjalaYoga .

pmp:Preliminaries a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Vorbereitungen"@de,
        "The preliminaries"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patrul Rinpoche, Kun bzang bla ma'i zhal lung (19th century)",
        "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)" ;
    skos:definition "The general and the tantra-specific preliminaries, placed before the training in either stage."@en ;
    skos:note "A dating warning belongs here. The preliminaries are set before the training in the fifteenth-century treatise, but the sharpest codification of them as an obligatory course is nineteenth-century, in Patrul Rinpoche's manual. Their presence in the older text does not license reading the later regime back into it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:TibetanTantric .

pmp:QuequmaHa a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Probe im Finsteren Haus"@de,
        "The ordeal of the Dark House"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Popol Vuh: The Sacred Book of the Ancient Quiché Maya, English version by Delia Goetz and Sylvanus G. Morley from the Spanish translation of Adrián Recinos (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1950), part II, chapter 9" ;
    skos:definition "The first of the ordeals of Xibalba. A lighted torch of pine and cigars are handed to those under trial at nightfall; they must be returned at dawn and returned whole, that is, not burned down. The houses named in the chapter beside it are the House of Cold, the House of Jaguars, the House of Bats and the House of Knives."@en ;
    skos:note "The condition is unusual in this collection because it is arithmetical rather than moral: what is required is that a thing that burns be given back unconsumed after a stated interval. The failure is measurable by anyone holding the object, and the interval is named."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MayaRitual .

pmp:QuimbyExhibitioPublica a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Verrichtung öffentlich vor einem Publikum, das sie für Betrug hielt"@de,
        "The operation shown in public to an audience that thought it fraud"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Horatio W. Dresser (ed.), The Quimby Manuscripts, 2nd ed. (New York: Thomas Y. Crowell, 1921), p. 31, with the subject's journal at p. 44 and the abandoned condition at p. 50" ;
    skos:definition "For several years the operator travelled with his subject through Maine and New Brunswick giving exhibitions, which attracted much attention and were noticed in the newspapers. The volume states what the audiences were: at that time the phenomenon was looked upon as a deception, a fraud, a humbug, the operator was vilified and frequently threatened with mob violence, and the exhibitions smacked too strongly of witchcraft to suit the people. The subject's own journal records that the people seemed very bitter upon the subject and that they had nevertheless satisfied a great many, some very hard cases, and as a general thing satisfied all sceptics beyond a doubt."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered for what it does to pmc:TheUnsympatheticWitnessAsStatedCauseOfFailure, whose falsifier names as the first thing to look for a transmission that prescribes its operation be shown to a doubter as a proof. This transmission did that as its normal business, for years, for money, before hostile rooms — and it had previously held the excluding condition in the standard form, that if a sceptic sat too near he failed. The two facts are in the same volume about the same man."@en,
        "It does not refute that statement, and the reason it does not is the statement's own second clause. What was exhibited was the subject describing a place someone in the audience was thinking of, or the inside of a body a physician could afterwards examine: an outcome a bystander could read off, which is precisely the case in which the statement predicts the bystanders will be admitted and no unsympathetic witness named. So the transmission passes from the first clause to the second as its outcomes become checkable, and the excluding condition falls away in the same movement as the steel and the storm."@en,
        "The claim that all sceptics were satisfied beyond a doubt is the subject's own journal entry and is treated here as a claim about the exhibitions and not about their success. He was the person whose performance was at stake and he was seventeen."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:QuimbyManuscripts ;
    pm:readVia "The editor's connecting narrative in Horatio W. Dresser (ed.), The Quimby Manuscripts, 2nd ed. (New York 1921), which summarises the mesmeric years from the papers, the journal and the notices rather than printing them at the place" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought .

pmp:RasaAghoraMantra a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Rezitieren der Aghora- und Rasankusi-Hymnen"@de,
        "The chanting of the Aghora and Rasankusi hymns"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. I, the rasalingam, pp. 10-11, and ch. IV, p. 41" ;
    skos:definition "The two hymns that accompany every operation. Every mercurial operation and the worship of mercury are to be performed with the Aghora mantram; the disciple recites each of the two a hundred thousand times at his initiation; the goddess addressed by the second is said to be the sovereign force behind mercury and to be equal to it, which is given as the reason it is worth while to learn how to control mercury."@en ;
    skos:note "The volume prints the Sanskrit of the hymn in a footnote and says expressly that no translation is attempted. What a later reader can check is therefore the instruction and not the words."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmp:RasaDipana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Anregung des Quecksilbers"@de,
        "Stimulation of mercury"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, dipanam, pp. 69-71" ;
    skos:altLabel "dipanam"@en ;
    skos:definition "The eighth operation. Mercury is kept in an earthen vessel and heated three days with metals, stones and vegetables — green vitriol, five salts, rye, pepper, drumstick seed, borax and kanji. The stated purpose is to make mercury fit for swallowing metals; the vocabulary throughout is appetite."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmp:RasaGarbhadruti a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Innere Verflüssigung des Quecksilbers"@de,
        "Internal liquefaction of mercury"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, garbhadruti, pp. 129-131" ;
    skos:altLabel "garbhadruti"@en ;
    skos:definition "The thirteenth operation, defined against the tenth by an observation: when metals thrown upon mercury disappear altogether it is swallowing, and when they turn into liquids without any heat being applied it is internal liquefaction. It is effected with half the weight of the essence of mica and half of the essence of pyrites, or with gold or silver first rubbed with pyrites and sour juices."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmp:RasaKalinipatni a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Kalini-Gattin, und die mit Schwefel vorbereitete Vertretung"@de,
        "The kalini wife, and the substitute prepared with sulphur"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. II, a kalini wife, pp. 18-19" ;
    skos:definition "The operator is to have a wife of a described kind, whose body is warm in winter and cold in summer and who begins her menstruation while the moon is on the wane; she is said to be capable of success in mercurial operations, efficient in preparing mercurial compounds, in applying mercury to patients, and in preparing the medicines against premature old age. Where such a wife is not available, any beautiful lady may serve the purpose if she takes every morning for three weeks one tola of purified sulphur mixed with clarified butter."@en ;
    skos:note "The description is a list of bodily marks, and the substitution rule turns the whole of it into something a course of sulphur can supply in three weeks. A transmission that states a qualification and then states its price is easier to examine than one that does not, and this is the second place in the volume where the operator is selected by marks on the body; the first is the chemist chosen by the lines of his palm."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmp:RasaKshetrikarana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Vorbereitung des Bodens, einen Monat bevor Quecksilber genommen wird"@de,
        "The preparation of the soil, one month before mercury is taken"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, preparations for taking mercury, pp. 192-194" ;
    skos:altLabel "kshetrikaranam"@en ;
    skos:definition "For one month before he begins to take mercury the user is purged and made to vomit, then, his strength recovered, takes every morning long pepper, chebulic myrobalan, dried ginger, rock salt and chitraka root in tepid water to maintain the power of digestion, and every night for that month incinerated mica and magnetic iron with long pepper, myrobalan, honey and ghee. The body is soothed with ghee and rock salt every third day, purged, made to vomit with the juice of bacha, freed of worms with palasa seed and bidanga in molasses, and strengthened afterwards with sali rice and meat juice. The transmission calls this the preparation of the soil, and the body the field that receives the seed of mercury."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmp:RasaMarana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Tötung des Quecksilbers"@de,
        "Killing of mercury"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, maranam, pp. 141-155" ;
    skos:altLabel "maranam"@en ;
    skos:definition "The fifteenth operation. Exhausted mercury is rubbed with the herbs called controllers of mercury, enclosed in a crucible painted inside with their paste, and heated by mild fire for a day, or eight times over in a bhudhara jantram with fresh rubbing each time, or solidified with sulphur oil and heated twelve hours under alternately melting and setting lead."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmp:RasaMrtasanjivaniGutika a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Mrita-sanjivani-gutika, die im Mund gehaltene Kugel"@de,
        "Mrita-sanjivani-gutika, the ball held in the mouth"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, mrita-sanjivani-gutika, pp. 211-212" ;
    skos:definition "Magnetic iron, mica, pyrites, diamond, gold and incinerated mercury rubbed a week with kanji and made into a ball, coated with a paste of gojihva, kakamachi, myrobalan, nirgundi, honey and rock salt, and heated in the bhudhara apparatus over khadira wood in a crucible painted inside with barley, tamarind, the ash of palasa wood, bibhitaki, rye, cotton seed and tanduliyaka, alum and white glass being added from time to time; the ball is dry in fifteen days. It is then held for one year inside the mouth."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmp:RasaParpati a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Parpati-rasa, das auf das Blatt gegossene schwarze Quecksilber"@de,
        "Parpati-rasa, the black mercury poured on the leaf"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, another preparation of black mercury, p. 113" ;
    skos:definition "One pala of dried sulphur melted over a mild fire in an iron or copper pot, three palas of mercury stirred into it with an iron ladle; when the two are commingled the mass is poured on a banana leaf laid over fresh cowdung and pressed lightly with a second leaf, and the black mercury is used when cool and solid."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmp:RasaRasasindura a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Rasasindura, die rote Verbindung"@de,
        "Rasasindura, the red compound"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, second and sixth processes of murchhanam, pp. 100 and 104" ;
    skos:definition "One part of mercury, three of sulphur and an eighth part of incinerated lead rubbed to a black mixture, put into a glass flask stopped with chalk and lime, plastered with rags and mud and heated in the sand apparatus for three days over a gradually increasing fire. In the principal process mercury is rubbed with six times its weight of sulphur and heated until the surplus sulphur is burnt out and the compound takes the appearance of red vermilion, which is stated to be noticeable from outside the flask."@en ;
    skos:note "The colour seen through the glass is a second assay of the kind this file separates out: an endpoint the operator watches for rather than a time he waits out."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmp:RasaSarvangasundara a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Sarbangasundara-rasa, das gelbe Quecksilber"@de,
        "Sarbangasundara-rasa, the yellow mercury"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, eighth process, p. 111" ;
    skos:definition "Equal quantities of mercury and sulphur rubbed steadily for seven days with the juice of hastishundi or bhudhatri, the compound put in a crucible in the sand apparatus and held at a mild heat for twenty-four hours until the mercury turns yellow. Dose one gunja with a betel leaf."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmp:RasaSvarnasindura a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Swarna-sindura, das Goldrot"@de,
        "Swarna-sindura, the gold red"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, twelfth process, p. 116" ;
    skos:definition "One pala of mercury, one of sulphur and an eighth of a pala of gold reduced to ashes, rubbed three hours each with the juice of the aerial roots of the banyan and with the juice of kanya, then heated in a glass flask in the sand apparatus; the red powder is collected from the upper part of the flask and prescribed according to the constitution, the power of digestion and the age."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmp:RasaTalaka a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Rasa-talaka, Quecksilber mit Auripigment"@de,
        "Rasa-talaka, mercury with orpiment"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, tenth process, p. 114" ;
    skos:definition "Mercury, sulphur, orpiment and red arsenic in equal quantities rubbed together, put into a glass flask and heated twelve hours in the sand apparatus; the product is yellow."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmp:RasaVajrapanjara a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Bajra-panjara-rasa, der Diamantkäfig"@de,
        "Bajra-panjara-rasa, the diamond cage"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, bajrapanjara rasa, p. 190" ;
    skos:definition "Incinerated diamond and an equal quantity of killed mercury rubbed with the juice of hansapadi and burnt by putapaka, the ash rubbed again with the same quantity of killed mercury and burnt again, the product rubbed with kanji and used to coat four times its weight of gold leaf, which is then killed in the usual manner. Taken with chitraka root, ginger, rock salt, bacha and suvarchala at half a rajika a day, raised gradually to one masha, the whole course amounting to one pala."@en ;
    skos:note "The one preparation of the volume with a stated total quantity for the whole course. A course with an end is a course that could be seen to have finished without result, which is what makes the total worth entering."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmp:RasaVedhana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Verwandlung der unedlen Metalle"@de,
        "The transformation of base metals"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. V, bedhanam and the six kinds, pp. 236 and 247-248" ;
    skos:altLabel "bedhanam"@en ;
    skos:definition "The eighteenth and last operation. Mercury is dyed and exhausted and dyed and exhausted again, seven times over, and is then said to be competent to turn base metals into gold. Six modes are distinguished by how the prepared mercury reaches the metal: by painting, by casting into the melt, by holding in a forceps, by smoke, by a sound blown through the mouth, and by touch."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmp:RosicrucianLibraAurea a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Wägung auf der goldenen Waage"@de,
        "The weighing on the golden balance"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chymische Hochzeit Christiani Rosencreutz (Strasbourg, 1616), the Third Day, the Inquisition at the golden balance" ;
    skos:definition "Announced the evening before and carried out on the morning of the third day. A balance entirely of gold is hung up in the middle of the hall; on a table covered with red velvet stand seven weights, first a fairly big one, then four little ones, then two great ones, and they are said to be heavier in proportion to their bulk than any man can believe. Two hundred armoured men are divided into seven bands by the number of the weights, one man of each band taking charge of his weight and carrying a rope beside his sword. Each guest steps up in his order and the weights are put in against him one by one; he who cannot raise a weight is flung out of the scale, bound, laughed at by the bands and delivered to the band of the weight he failed."@en ;
    skos:note "The marginal glosses of the 1616 print name the parts: Libra aurea for the balance, Pondera for the weights, Satellites for the armed men. The identifiers of this file are taken from them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:NuptiaeChymicae ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmp:RubbingBoardConsultation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Befragung des Reibbrettorakels"@de,
        "Consulting the rubbing-board oracle"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 170-171",
        "Georg Schweinfurth, The Heart of Africa, trans. Ellen E. Frewer (London: Sampson Low, Marston, Low and Searle, 1873), vol. II, chapter XIII, the account of the Niam-niam praying-machine and auguries" ;
    skos:definition "Two pieces of wood, the upper rubbed across the moistened surface of the lower. Sticking gives one answer and smooth running the other, and a lid that runs from side to side or alternately sticks and runs is taken as a refusal to answer. The instrument is the cheapest and most frequently used of the oracles and the one least trusted."@en ;
    skos:note "The earliest witness describes the same instrument two generations before the monograph, under the name borru, made from the wood of a tree he names, and reports that it was consulted before any undertaking of importance and hidden from the Muslim traders. The two descriptions of the mechanism agree; the names do not, and the divergence stands on the dispute node at the end of this file."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmp:Sadampuspa a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Anbinden des immerblühenden Amuletts"@de,
        "The binding on of the ever-flowering amulet"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Atharvaveda Saṃhitā 4.20, in the translation of W. D. Whitney, revised by C. R. Lanman, Harvard Oriental Series 7, Cambridge, Mass. 1905" ;
    skos:definition "An amulet of the plant called ever-flowering is bound on, and the hymn is spoken to the plant as to a divinity with a thousand eyes and as the eye-pupil of the heavenly eagle."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Atharvavedic .

pmp:SaktipataTransmission a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Übertragung der Herabkunft der Kraft"@de,
        "Conferring the descent of power"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Abhinavagupta, Tantrasāra XI",
        "Abhinavagupta, Tantrāloka XIII" ;
    skos:definition "The initiatory act by which the descent of power is conferred."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmp:SanhedrinLawsOfFormationBeforeTheSabbath a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Sich am Vorabend des Sabbats mit der Schoepfungslehre befassen"@de,
        "Occupying oneself with the laws of formation on the eve of the Sabbath"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Synhedrin 67b, the third class of Abaye's division, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. VII (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1903), p. 287" ;
    skos:definition "The practice Abaye names as permitted from the outset: R. Hanina and R. Oshaya occupied themselves every eve of the Sabbath with the laws of formation, created a three-year-old calf and ate it. The operation is study of a doctrine, performed at a fixed time of the week, and it is not distinguished in the text from other learning by anything except what follows from it."@en ;
    skos:note "The parallel a little earlier in the same tractate names the object of their study as a book title where this place names rules, which touches traditions/sefer-yetzirah.ttl directly. That earlier place was not read at the wording in this run and the difference is therefore recorded and not decided."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliSanhedrin ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmp:ShamanicInterpretation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Deuten einer Mitteilung durch einen Schamanen"@de,
        "Having a communication interpreted by a shaman"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), pp. 68-69 and p. 135" ;
    skos:definition "The consultation in which a shaman renders what a communication means. The doctrine holds that shamans are the vicars of the gods, that communications from them are usually in mystic form, and that the shaman's interpretation is authoritative."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by named Oglala shamans, chiefly Sword, Tyon, Ringing-shield, Bad-wound and No-flesh, and by Finger on 25 March 1914; recorded by the agency physician J. R. Walker at Pine Ridge over his years of service there, closed December 1916; given in Lakota, part of it written out in Lakota by Sword and Tyon and translated by Burt Means, Thomas Mills, Bruce Means and Antoin Herman; the paper prints the translations only, so the Lakota wording is not carried." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LakotaRitual .

pmp:ShangqingFusanqi a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Einnehmen der drei Dünste"@de,
        "Ingesting the three vapours"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 10.1a-10.1b (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 10" ;
    skos:definition "Green, white and red vapour seen as threads coming from beneath the sun in the east straight into the mouth, drawn in ninety times until sated, performed every morning with the eyes half closed."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmp:ShangqingJigong a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Ansammeln von Verdienst"@de,
        "Accumulating merit"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 5.16b-5.17a (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 5" ;
    skos:definition "Acts of benefit performed and reckoned up, treated in this corpus as a countable quantity that stands in a fixed relation to what is obtained and that passes to descendants when it falls short."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmp:ShangqingShouxuanbai a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Bewahren des Dunklen und des Weißen"@de,
        "Keeping the dark and the white"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 10.2a-10.2b (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 10" ;
    skos:definition "Black vapour held in the upper cinnabar field, white in the heart, yellow at the navel, the three grown into a cloud that covers the body, turned to fire and made to circle it until inside and outside are one, followed by a hundred and twenty ingestions of breath."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmp:ShintoKukatachi a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Kukatachi, die Probe des siedenden Wassers"@de,
        "Kukatachi, the ordeal of boiling water"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Nihongi, Book X, Ōjin 9th year, 4th month, in the English of W. G. Aston, vol. I, London 1896, p. 258",
        "Nihongi, Book XIII, Ingyō 4th year, 9th month, in the English of W. G. Aston, vol. I, London 1896, pp. 316-317" ;
    skos:definition "The procedure ordered in the fourth year of the emperor Ingyō to settle which houses and surnames were falsely claimed. The people of the various houses and surnames are to wash themselves and practise abstinence, and then, each one calling the gods to witness, to plunge their hands into boiling water. Cauldrons were placed on a named spur of a named hill and everybody was told to go there. The chronicle gives two variants of the same ordeal: mud was sometimes put in a cauldron and made to boil and the bared arms used to stir it, and sometimes an axe was heated red-hot and placed on the palm of the hand. Those taking it put on straps of tree-fibre before coming to the cauldrons."@en ;
    skos:note "The preparation is the same purification the rest of this file turns on — washing and abstinence — and it is required of everyone rather than of an officiant. An earlier reign carries the same ordeal in its two-party form: two ministers who wrangled so that the right and the wrong could not be ascertained were sent to the bank of a river to undergo the ordeal of boiling water, and one of them was victorious. That passage states no criterion; it states a winner."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Nihonshoki ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shinto .

pmp:SingingTheSongsOfAPotency a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Singen der Lieder, die zu einer Potenz gehören"@de,
        "Singing the songs that belong to a potency"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Frances Densmore, Teton Sioux Music (Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 61, Washington, 1918), p. 211",
        "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 88 and pp. 158-159" ;
    skos:definition "The songs attached to a particular imparted potency, learned with it and sung when a request is made of it. The corpus states that a shaman preparing a fetish devises a formula which must be repeated to invoke its powers, that a medicine man knows the songs of his medicines and that those songs are his potency, and that a stone transferred from one man to another was transferred together with the song that was to be sung whenever a request was made of it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by named Oglala shamans, chiefly Sword, Tyon, Ringing-shield, Bad-wound and No-flesh, and by Finger on 25 March 1914; recorded by the agency physician J. R. Walker at Pine Ridge over his years of service there, closed December 1916; given in Lakota, part of it written out in Lakota by Sword and Tyon and translated by Burt Means, Thomas Mills, Bruce Means and Antoin Herman; the paper prints the translations only, so the Lakota wording is not carried." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LakotaRitual .

pmp:Sitali a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Śītalī"@de,
        "Śītalī"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.57-58 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "A retention in which the breath is drawn in over the tongue, protruded past the lips, and released through the nostrils."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmp:SusrutaMarmapariksa a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das marman in Rechnung stellen, bevor operiert wird"@de,
        "Taking account of the marman before operating"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Susruta Samhita, Sarirasthana VI.26-29 and VI.81, in the English of Kaviraj Kunjalal Bhishagratna, An English Translation of the Sushruta Samhita, vol. II, Calcutta 1911, pp. 178-179 and 187-188" ;
    skos:definition "The step the chapter puts before both the knife and the extraction: a careful examination of the affected marman, and in the case of an incision, taking the situation and the dimension of each local marman into account first. The chapter supplies what the examination is against — the hundred and seven sites, their classes, their measured extents in fingers' widths, and the clear space to be left about each — and does not describe how the examination is carried out."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because the prerequisite needs a step to point at and because the step is the whole practical content of the chapter. A surgeon who had read only this chapter would have learnt a map and a set of distances, which is an instruction to do something before cutting and not an instruction in cutting."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SusrutaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:SusrutaSnehasveda a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Salbung, Schwitzkur und flüssige Kost, bevor die Vene geöffnet wird"@de,
        "Anointing, fomenting and the liquid diet before the vein is opened"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Susruta Samhita, Sarirasthana VIII.5, in the English of Kaviraj Kunjalal Bhishagratna, An English Translation of the Sushruta Samhita, vol. II, Calcutta 1911, p. 199" ;
    skos:definition "The preliminary the chapter prescribes: the patient is duly fomented and anointed with oily preparations, and is first given a liquid food or a diet of articles antidotal to the bodily principles that engendered the disease, or gruel. The translator's note gives the reason the chapter does not: a liquid food is recommended in order to liquefy the blood so that it bleeds easily."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as its own step because the prerequisite below needs one and because the chapter treats it as a stage with its own purpose. Nothing here is the whole Ayurvedic doctrine of sneha and sveda, which this run did not open; the node says only what this chapter says about them in this place."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SusrutaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:TaulipangJaguarTransformation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Kleid des Jaguars anziehen"@de,
        "Putting on the dress of the jaguar"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Theodor Koch-Gruenberg, Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, Band III: Ethnographie (Stuttgart: Strecker und Schroeder, 1923), pp. 200-201" ;
    skos:definition "The Zauberaerzte are firmly convinced that they can turn into jaguars by putting on the dress of the jaguar, kaikusa-zamatale. In doing so they turn the whole body round, so that the belly is turned upward and the back comes underneath as the belly; the hands and feet become round and armed with claws like a jaguar's feet, and turn backwards."@en ;
    skos:note "The description is of an anatomical reversal and not of a disguise, which is why it is entered as an operation with a stated procedure rather than as a belief. What it does not carry is any preparation, any formula, any time of day, or any condition - the dress is put on and the body turns."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VomRoroimaZumOrinocoIII ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Taulipang .

pmp:ThelemaAnnihilatioCogitationis a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Vernichtung des Gedankens an seinem Entstehungspunkt"@de,
        "The annihilation of thought at its point of origin"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber Turris vel Domus Dei sub figura XVI, §§ 0-9, in The Equinox I,6 (London, September 1911), pp. 11-12" ;
    skos:definition "Six points, each a separate achievement. Discovering for oneself the apparent position of the point in the brain where thoughts arise, or, if there is no such point, the position of the point where thoughts are judged; developing in oneself a will of destruction, even of annihilation, and identifying oneself with it even to the loss of oneself; letting that will watch the point vigilantly and annihilating every thought as it is perceived or judged; then inhibiting every thought in its inception; then discovering and annihilating the causes or tendencies that would otherwise end in thoughts; and last unmasking and annihilating the true cause of all."@en ;
    skos:note "The instruction claims for this practice something it claims for no other: that any success in it is of an exceedingly high character, and that the student is less liable to illusion and self-deception in this than in almost any other that the order makes known. The claim is asserted and not argued, and no test is offered with it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberTurrisVelDomusDei ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmp:ThelemaProbatioTarot a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Kartenprobe mit tabelliertem Ergebnis"@de,
        "The card test with a tabulated result"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber E vel Exercitiorum sub figura IX, chap. II, §§ 1-4, in The Equinox I,1 (London, March 1909), pp. 26-27" ;
    skos:definition "Take a pack of seventy-eight tarot cards, shuffle, cut, draw one card, and without looking at it try to name it. Write down the card named and the actual card; repeat and tabulate the results. The easier form, guessing only the suit, is to be tried first, and all possibilities of obtaining the knowledge through sight, touch or smell are to be excluded."@en ;
    skos:note "The exclusion clause is not a formality and the instruction says why: there was once a man whose fingertips were so sensitive that he could feel the shape and position of the pips and so judge the card correctly. A stated source of error with a stated mechanism is rare in this holding."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberEVelExercitiorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmp:ThelemaVibratioNominum a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Vibration der Gottesnamen"@de,
        "The vibration of god-names"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber O vel Manus et Sagittae sub figura VI, chap. III, §§ 3-4, in The Equinox I,2 (London, September 1909), pp. 17-18" ;
    skos:definition "Standing with arms outstretched, breathing in deeply through the nostrils while imagining the name of the god entering with the breath; letting the name descend from the lungs through heart, solar plexus, navel and generative organs to the feet; at the moment it appears to touch the feet, advancing the left foot about twelve inches, throwing the body forward and shooting out the hands from beside the eyes, while imagining the name rushing up through the body and breathing it out through the nostrils; then withdrawing the foot and placing the forefinger on the lips. All of it done with all the force of which the practitioner is capable."@en ;
    skos:note "It follows the assumption of god-forms, of which the same chapter says that no simple and certain test of success exists. The vibration, by contrast, is given two signs, and the two differ from each other in exactly the way this holding needs to distinguish: one is a bodily state a bystander could in principle observe, the other is stated to be perceptible only to the practitioner."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberOVelManusEtSagittae ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmp:TiantaiChanhui a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Reue, die das Hindernis gebrochener Gelöbnisse beseitigt"@de,
        "The repentance that clears the obstruction of broken precepts"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0462c to 0463a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:definition "The procedure prescribed for the third kind of practitioner, the one who has broken precepts heavy and light. Ten things assist it: clear belief in cause and effect, the arising of grave fear, deep shame, the seeking of a method of extinction, the disclosure of the earlier offence, the cutting of the continuing mind, the raising of a mind that protects the teaching, the making of a great vow to deliver living beings, the constant recollection of the buddhas of the ten directions, and the contemplation of the offence as unborn. The place of practice is adorned, the body washed, clean clothes put on, incense burned and flowers scattered, and the confession carried on single-mindedly before the three jewels for one or three weeks, or a month, or three months, or up to a year, and it stops when extinction is reached."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmp:TiantaiChuding a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Verfahren für das Herauskommen aus der Sitzung"@de,
        "The procedure for coming out of the sitting"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0466b, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:definition "Before leaving the seat the mind is first released to another object, the mouth opened and the air let go with the thought of it dispersing from all the vessels at will; then the body is moved a little, then the shoulders, arms, hands, head and neck, then both feet until they are soft; then the pores are rubbed over with the hands, the hands rubbed warm and laid over both eyes, and the eyes opened after that. The seat is left only when the heat of the body has subsided."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmp:TiantaiJingmei a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Den Kobold an der Doppelstunde seines Kommens erkennen und ihn benennen"@de,
        "Identifying the sprite by the double-hour at which it comes, and naming it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0470b, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:definition "The first of the three kinds of interference and the only one of them with a procedure. The twelve beasts of the twelve double-hours change into shapes and colours of every kind, a young girl, an old man, a shape to be feared, to vex and confuse the practitioner, and each comes at its own hour, so that the hour tells which it is: at the third double-hour a tiger, at the fourth a hare or deer, at the fifth a dragon or turtle, at the sixth a snake, at the seventh a horse, ass or camel, at the eighth a sheep, at the ninth an ape, at the tenth a cock or crow, at the eleventh a dog or wolf, at the twelfth a pig, at the first a rat, at the second an ox. If the practitioner sees that it constantly comes at this hour, he knows it for that beast's sprite, and on his speaking its name and reproaching it, it withdraws at once."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered on the second pass, and it belongs in the file because it is the one place in the work where a claim is made with an antecedent a second person in the room could check. The hour is on the clock, the identification is a prediction from the hour, and the stated effect of naming the beast is that the interference ends at once. The work says in the same chapter, at pmc:TiantaiFeiMoSuoWei, that no such interference has ever been seen to do anything; the two stand four pages apart and the work does not reconcile them."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmp:TiantaiShierXi a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die zwölf Atemarten, durch Vorstellung gegen benannte Leiden geführt"@de,
        "The twelve breaths worked by visualization against named complaints"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0471c to 0472a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:definition "Twelve breaths, all of them produced from the visualizing mind, each assigned to one complaint: the upward breath treats sinking heaviness, the downward breath empty suspension, the full breath wasting, the scorching breath swelling, the growing breath debility, the destroying breath excess, the warm breath cold, the cold breath heat, the thrusting breath stoppage and blockage, the holding breath trembling, the harmonizing breath the general disharmony of the four elements, and the supplementing breath their decline. Beside them the work gives six sounded breaths matched to the organs in a verse: he to the heart, chui to the kidneys, hu to the spleen, si to the lungs, xu when heat comes from the liver, and xi where the triple burner is congested."@en ;
    skos:note "The work does not teach these in its own voice. Each set is introduced with a master says, the masters are unnamed, and four such opinions on healing by calming and three on healing by contemplation are set side by side without the work deciding between them. What the work adds in its own voice is the withdrawal entered at pmc:TiantaiJinshiBuchengjiu."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmp:TiantaiTiaoxi a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Atemregelung nach ihren vier Merkmalen"@de,
        "Regulating the breath by its four marks"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0466a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:definition "The breath has four marks and three of them are marks of maladjustment. Wind is when the breath is audible at the nostrils in sitting. Panting is when it is silent but knotted and does not pass. Air is when it is neither audible nor knotted but not fine. Breath proper is when it is neither audible nor knotted nor coarse, passing continuously as if present and as if absent. Where one of the first three is found, three measures are prescribed: settle the mind downward, loosen the body, and think the air passing in and out through all the pores without obstruction."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmp:Tummo a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Tummo"@de,
        "Tummo"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)" ;
    skos:definition "The generation of inner heat, presented in three components: the channels, the syllables, and the heat meditation itself."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:TibetanTantric .

pmp:TunkanSending a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Aussenden der heiligen Steine"@de,
        "Sending the sacred stones"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Frances Densmore, Teton Sioux Music (Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 61, Washington, 1918), pp. 205-207" ;
    skos:definition "A medicine man who has dreamed of the stones sends them long distances to obtain information. After a time they return and give it to him; he alone can understand what they say and repeats their message to the man who asked the question. During a treatment of the sick in a darkened lodge the stones are said to fly through the air and sometimes to strike those who have refused to believe in them."@en ;
    skos:note "The men who furnished this were named and were distinguished by the recorder according to their relation to the stones: those who had dreamed of them and used them, those who owned one and believed themselves helped by it, one who owned one and could not command it, one who had sung at another man's demonstrations, and those who had only witnessed them. That classification is the recorder's and is unusually careful for the material, which is why it is repeated here."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by named Teton men who had themselves danced or doctored, among them Red Weasel, Chased-by-Bears, Brave Buffalo, Goose, Charging Thunder and Two Shields; recorded by Frances Densmore for the Bureau of American Ethnology at Standing Rock, 1911 to 1914, with Robert P. Higheagle interpreting; given in Lakota, and the bulletin carries the Lakota of the song texts but prints the spoken accounts in English only." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LakotaRitual .

pmp:TurbaDomusCircaArborem a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das um den Baum gebaute Haus, mit einem Hundertjährigen darin verschlossen"@de,
        "The house built round the tree, with a man of a hundred years shut inside"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 58 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    skos:definition "The operation reported at Dictum 58 in the words of a man who said he had found the tree and eaten of its fruit. Take that tree and build a house wholly surrounding it, circular, dark, encircled by dew; place in it a man of a hundred years; shut and secure the door so that neither dust nor wind reaches them; and after a hundred and eighty days send them away to their homes. The man is not to cease eating of the fruit until the number of the days is complete."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a practice although it is framed as a report, because the passage gives an apparatus, a tenant, a closure condition and a term in days, which is what a prescription consists of. The frame matters and is recorded at the yielding node: the speaker did not do it, he asked somebody who said he had."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy .

pmp:UgarMrQhWSt a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Myrrhe in zwei Götterhäuser und ein drittes Mittel in das Haus des Kindes"@de,
        "Myrrh in two divine houses and a third substance in the child's"@en ;
    dcterms:source "RS 24.272:5-10 (KTU 1.124), in Pardee, Ritual and Cult at Ugarit, WAW 10, 2002, text 52, pp. 171-172" ;
    skos:definition "The prescription Ditānu gives, in the imperative: take a skin-bottle of myrrh and put it in the house of Ḥôrānu; take a new bottle of myrrh and put it in the house of Baʻlu; take the third named substance and put it in the child's house, and it will bring his illness to a head."@en ;
    skos:note "The reading of the third substance and of the verb of the effect are both uncertain in the edition; the editor marks the verb as partly restored and the substance as a word whose meaning is among the work's principal problems. What is secure is the two placements of myrrh in two named divine houses."@en,
        "Three placements and a stated effect, and the effect is the interesting half: the substance is not said to cure the child but to bring the illness to a head. That is a prediction with a direction, and it is the kind of statement a bystander could have expected to see come true or not."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RS24272 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:UgariticRitual .

pmp:VaidikaAgnicayana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Agnicayana, die Schichtung des Feueraltars"@de,
        "Agnicayana, the piling of the fire-altar"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa X,4,3,8-13 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 43, Oxford 1897, pp. 357-358" ;
    skos:definition "The construction of a brick altar in five layers, treated by the transmission as the building of a body for the year, for death and for the sacrificer at once. The exegesis makes its efficacy depend on the completeness of the count: enclosing-stones for the nights, bricks laid with formulas for the days, half-moons, months and seasons, and plain filling bricks for the hours."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual .

pmp:VaidikaPrayascitta a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Prāyaścitta, die Wiedergutmachung eines Zwischenfalls im Ritus"@de,
        "Prāyaścitta, the repair of a mishap in the rite"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa XII,4,1 to XII,4,4 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 44, Oxford 1900, pp. 177-196" ;
    skos:definition "The prescribed repair for a mishap during the performance. The transmission sets it out case by case in a standing form: they also say, if such and such were to happen, what rite and what expiation would there be in that case? — followed by the act to be done, and closed with: this, then, is the rite performed in that case. The cases read include a team or persons passing between the fires, unclean animals passing between them, milk spilt at the milking and milk spilt after ladling, the cow giving blood instead of milk, the vessel overturning or breaking, the fire going out after the offering, the loss of the householder's fire, the fires touching one another or an unclean fire, and the sun rising or setting before the offering is carried out."@en ;
    skos:note "Some twenty-two of these stood in the four chapters read, the great majority in the standing form quoted above. What they show as a set is a corpus that has thought about its own accidents in detail; what they do not show is entered at pmc:VaidikaPrayascittaSarvam."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual .

pmp:VaidikaSvadhyaya a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Svādhyāya, die tägliche Rezitation des eigenen Pensums"@de,
        "Svādhyāya, the daily recitation of one's own lesson"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa XI,5,6,9 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 44, Oxford 1900, p. 99" ;
    skos:definition "The daily study and teaching of the Veda, which the transmission calls a sacrifice to the Brahman and sets out in the same terms as a fire-rite: it has its own oblations and its own vaṣaṭ calls, and it is to be performed whether or not any fire is lit."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual .

pmp:WirazWatch a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die siebentägige Wache über dem Körper"@de,
        "The seven days' watch over the body"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The Book of Arda Viraf, ed. and trans. Martin Haug, E. W. West and Hoshang Jamaspji Asa (Bombay and London: Trübner, 1872), 2.18 and 2.31-33" ;
    skos:definition "What is done while the traveller is gone. The dasturs give the seven sisters an undertaking in a number: they will deliver him back safe and sound in seven days. The body remains on the carpet; the dasturs, the priests and the seven sisters keep watch around it for seven days and nights, with fire and fumigation, reciting the Avesta and the Zand, going through the Nasks and chanting the Gathas, and they keep watch in the dark."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the part of the procedure that an outside observer could check, and it is stated in a form that could fail: a named man, a named place, a stated number of days, and a promise made to named women that he will be given back alive."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Zoroastrian .

pmp:Xingqi a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Zirkulation des Qi"@de,
        "Circulating the qi"@en ;
    dcterms:source "H.D. Roth, Original Tao (1999)",
        "Xingqi yupei ming, in D. Harper, Early Chinese Medical Literature (1998), p. 125",
        "Xingqi yupei ming, in J. Needham, Science and Civilisation in China II (1956), p. 143" ;
    skos:definition "A breath practice inscribed in forty-five characters on a twelve-sided jade block, setting out the deliberate movement of qi through the body."@en ;
    skos:note "The two axes of disagreement belong to the claim and are recorded with it. Dating: Harper places the inscription in the late fourth or early third century BCE, Needham in the middle of the sixth. Reading: Harper takes the opening two characters as the title and begins the text at the second line, where Roth counts and construes differently. A citation that names only the inscription conceals both."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XingqiYupeiMing ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmp:XuanShu a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Abzählen des Höchsten Geheimnisses"@de,
        "The counting of the Supreme Mystery"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Yang Xiong, Taixuanjing, Xuan shu, the eleventh, with the commentary of Fan Wang, juan 8, Sibu congkan facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "A parallel counting on a different modulus. Thirty-six are looked to for the stalks; heaven divides by three and ends at six completions, hence eighteen, and since heaven does not give and earth does not complete, the number is doubled and earth leaves three empty against heaven's eighteen. One is separated and hung on the little finger of the left hand, the rest is divided in the middle and searched through by threes, and the remainder is set beside the notch. After one notch the remainder is counted: seven counts as one, eight as two, nine as three, and at six reckonings the way of the stalks is exhausted. The four positions so obtained give one of eighty-one heads, and each head carries nine appraisals ranked as thought, fortune and misfortune, each in a lower, middle and upper degree."@en ;
    skos:note "The interest is the contrast with the Changes, and it is exact: the same act of reading a figure off an ungoverned remainder, on a modulus of three instead of four, over four positions instead of six, into eighty-one figures instead of sixty-four. Two traditions in one language, three centuries apart, differing in the arithmetic and agreeing in the form. That is the strongest internal evidence this file holds that the form is not the content."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination .

pmp:Yansheng a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Aufhängen der überwindenden Wandlungsphase"@de,
        "Hanging up the conquering phase"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Wang Chong, Lunheng, the fourth and last chapter of juan 23, against the year and month taboos on breaking ground, Sibu congkan facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "The countermeasure taken by a household that finds itself in the quarter eaten, as the same chapter reports it. An object of one of the five phases is hung up in the house: metal where the west is eaten, charcoal where the east is. Beside it stand two other remedies, a sacrifice set out to remove the ill and the vacating of the dwelling until the danger has passed. The chapter says the practice was copied from neighbour to neighbour and that everyone held it to be so."@en ;
    skos:note "The procedure is decided by direction alone. It names which phase is to be hung and says nothing of how much of it, which is the opening the critic goes through and the reason this practice is held as a node of its own rather than as a clause of the doctrine."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination .

pmp:YinshuBaJing a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Ziehen der acht Schüsse, mit den drei Atemarten"@de,
        "The pull of the Eight Warps, with the three breaths"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhangjiashan Yinshu, slips 104 to 106, with the same procedure prescribed for incipient exhaustion at slip 33, in Vivienne Lo (trans.), How to do the Gibbon Walk: A Translation of the Pulling Book (ca. 186 BCE), Needham Research Institute Working Papers 3 (Cambridge: Needham Research Institute, 2014)" ;
    skos:definition "The general procedure the third text prescribes against every cause of illness it has just listed: treat it with the pull of the Eight Warps — puff out the moist breath, exhale the warm breath, breathe the quintessential qi of heaven and earth, extend the belly and straighten the small of the back, vigorously extend the hands and feet, stroke the heels and curl the fingers and toes. On rising the movement is to be made wide and large, and on lying down the pull is to be taken as far as it will go. The three breaths are then given counts that change with the season: on spring days the warm breath twice, the dry breath once, the moist breath once; on summer days the dry breath twice, the warm once, the moist once; on winter days the moist twice, the warm once, the dry once."@en ;
    skos:note "The autumn count is not on the slips. Spring, summer and winter are given and the fourth is absent, in the same text whose first part suspends its sanction in autumn."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Yangsheng .

pmp:YinshuDaoyin a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Ziehen"@de,
        "Pulling"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhangjiashan Yinshu, slips 8 to 102, in Vivienne Lo (trans.), How to do the Gibbon Walk: A Translation of the Pulling Book (ca. 186 BCE), Needham Research Institute Working Papers 3 (Cambridge: Needham Research Institute, 2014)" ;
    skos:altLabel "daoyin"@en,
        "yin"@en ;
    skos:definition "The forty-one set exercises of the second text. Each is a named posture or sequence — Crossing the Thigh, the Inch Worm, Wild Duck Bathing, Tiger Looks Back, the Gibbon Hold, Bear Warp, the Pace of Yu — carried out for a stated count and then stopped: ten times, thirty, three hundred, a thousand. Where an exercise treats a named complaint it is called pulling that complaint, and the instruction takes the form of a prescription: which limb to extend, which hand holds what, how many times, and when to stop. Several require an implement — a wooden staff, a board, a column, a piece of shaved wood hung on new twist at a fixed height — and several require another person."@en ;
    skos:note "The counts are the part a later reader will most easily pass over and they are the most exact thing in the work. The complaints are addressed by site and by side: where the pain is in the left foot the left foot is extended and the right thigh raised, and where it is in the right the instruction is given again with the sides exchanged, in full, rather than by saying to reverse it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Yangsheng .

pmp:YinshuRushi a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Eintreten in die Kammer zur Stunde, die die Jahreszeit ihm gibt"@de,
        "Entering the chamber, in the hour the season gives it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhangjiashan Yinshu, slips 2, 4, 6 and 7, in Vivienne Lo (trans.), How to do the Gibbon Walk: A Translation of the Pulling Book (ca. 186 BCE), Needham Research Institute Working Papers 3 (Cambridge: Needham Research Institute, 2014)" ;
    skos:definition "The one member of the day's order that the work times differently in each season and attaches a stopping rule to. In spring and in summer: enter the chamber between dusk and midnight. In winter: between dusk and the hours before midnight. In autumn the hour is not given at all, and the instruction is instead to enter whenever the body is nourished and derives comfort from it."@en ;
    skos:note "The Chinese phrase is the early verbal form of a standing euphemism for sexual activity, and the translator says so at the first occurrence. It is entered as a practice and not as a circumstance because the work times it, bounds it and states what follows from exceeding it, which is what it does for its exercises and for nothing else in the day's order."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Yangsheng .

pmp:YinshuSijieRiyong a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Tagesordnung, nach der Jahreszeit gesetzt"@de,
        "The day's order, set by the season"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhangjiashan Yinshu, slips 2, 4, 6 and 7, in Vivienne Lo (trans.), How to do the Gibbon Walk: A Translation of the Pulling Book (ca. 186 BCE), Needham Research Institute Working Papers 3 (Cambridge: Needham Research Institute, 2014)" ;
    skos:definition "The regimen of the first text, given four times over with the same members in a different arrangement. On spring days: rise early, pass water, cleanse the hands and mouth, clean the teeth, breathe out, loosen the hair, ramble in front of the house, go to meet the purity of the early morning dew, receive the quintessence of heaven, and afterwards drink one cup of water. On summer days: wash the hair more and bathe less, do not sleep late, eat more vegetables, then the same morning order and a cup of water after a while. On autumn days: bathe and wash the hair frequently, and in drinking and eating your fill indulge the body's desires. On winter days: bathe and wash the hair frequently, keep the hands cold and the feet warm, the face cold and the body warm, rise late from rest, and in lying down and stretching be straight."@en ;
    skos:note "The four are not four different regimens but one regimen with the dials turned. What moves between them is the frequency of washing, the hour of rising, the temperature the body is to be held at and the licence given to appetite. That is a claim a reader can check against the text and not a summary of it: the members recur verbatim."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Yangsheng .

pmp:Yubu a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Schritt des Yu"@de,
        "The pace of Yu"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 17 (Dengshe), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 17" ;
    skos:definition "A gait set out step by step. Stand upright with the right foot in front and the left behind; next advance the right foot and bring the left up beside it, and that is one pace; next advance the right foot, then the left, and bring the right up beside the left, and that is two paces; next advance the right foot and bring the left up beside it, and that is three paces; and with that the way of the pace of Yu is complete. The chapter gives the length of a pace as seven feet, notes a variant reading, and says the walker looks back at nine footprints. Of its scope it says that in performing any of the hundred arts under heaven one ought to know the pace of Yu, and not for this matter alone."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because of that last clause. The chapter treats the gait as a component shared by a whole class of operations rather than as a rite of its own, which is the sort of statement that lets a graph ask whether the class has anything else in common. It is also given with an exactness — which foot, in which order, brought up beside which — that almost nothing else in this work carries."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BaopuziNeipian ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmp:Zhuyou a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Beschwörung nach der Ursache"@de,
        "The incantation after the cause"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 13 (Yi jing bian qi lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 4" ;
    skos:definition "The treatment the corpus ascribes to antiquity: shifting the essence and transforming the qi, for which, it says, the incantation after the cause was enough. It is named as an office elsewhere in the corpus, where the chapter on assigning duties by fitness gives the spitting on abscesses and the incanting of illnesses to the sharp-tongued and slighting."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmp:ZosimosOpusTemptare a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Werk im einzelnen versuchen, wie es dargelegt ist"@de,
        "Attempting the work in detail, as it has been set out"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zosime, französische Übersetzung in M. Berthelot und Ch.-Em. Ruelle, Collection des anciens alchimistes grecs, seconde livraison, Paris 1888, S. 172" ;
    skos:definition "What the writer tells the addressee to do with the exposition he has given: this exposition gives rise to no ambiguity, but its purpose is to engage you to try whether fortune is favourable to you. And, at the same place: if you know the two grades, those of the Jews and of the others, do not fear to try, carrying out in detail all the things I have set out to you."@en ;
    skos:note "The second half of the first sentence is damaged in the witness read: the naming of the second party to the two grades breaks off in the printed text, and this node carries the clause as far as the edition gives it and no further."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Cheirokmeta ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GraecoEgyptianAlchemy .

pmp:ZuyuaThan a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Verhör in der Sprache von Zuyua"@de,
        "The interrogation in the language of Zuyua"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ralph L. Roys, The Book of Chilam Balam of Chumayel (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1967), chapter IX, pp. 89-98; the same chapter is pp. 42-50 in the first edition, Carnegie Institution of Washington Publication 438, 1933" ;
    skos:definition "An examination held at the close of one katun and the opening of the next, in which the first head-chief demands of each town chief a series of objects named figuratively, and the candidate must produce the thing actually meant. The demands are answered by everyday things: the sun is a large fried egg, the brains of the sky are copal gum, the house six spans long is a very large hat, the things that stop the bottom of the cenote are white and yellow jicamas."@en ;
    skos:note "The source names the moment exactly and not vaguely: the katun that ends is 3 Ahau and the one that begins is 1 Ahau, so the procedure carries a date rather than an occasion."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MayaRitual .

pmt:AdvaitaVedanta a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Advaita Vedānta"@de,
        "Advaita Vedānta"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Śaṅkara, Brahmasūtrabhāṣya, introduction (trans. G. Thibaut, Sacred Books of the East 34, Oxford 1890)" ;
    skos:definition "The non-dual commentarial tradition on the Vedānta corpus, represented here by the commentary on the Brahmasūtra."@en ;
    skos:note "Kept separate from the Upaniṣadic file although it comments on that corpus. A commentary is a later act of reading and states things its base text does not: what is held here is the commentator's argument, and a record that filed it under the older heading would date a ninth-century position to the age of the verses it expounds."@en,
        "The three decisions preceding this file. Route of contact: none is documented towards the Greek or Chinese material; a correspondence with either would count as a finding, and none is asserted yet. Edition: the Thibaut translation of 1890 in the Sacred Books of the East, named on the node. Number of citable passages: one, read in the introductory section on superimposition."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Śaṅkara, Brahmasūtrabhāṣya" .

pmt:Madhyamaka a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Madhyamaka"@de,
        "Madhyamaka"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Nāgārjuna, Mūlamadhyamakakārikā (trans. Th. Stcherbatsky, The Conception of Buddhist Nirvāṇa, Leningrad 1927)" ;
    skos:definition "The middle-way school of Buddhist philosophy founded on the verses of Nāgārjuna and its commentarial literature."@en ;
    skos:note "The three decisions preceding this file. Route of contact: to the Śaiva and Śākta material a route exists and is not in dispute, so a correspondence with it would be reception and not a finding; towards the Greek or the Western esoteric material none is documented. Edition: the verses are read in Stcherbatsky's translation of 1927, named on the node, which carries the commentary of Candrakīrti alongside them; the Sanskrit has not been consulted. Number of citable passages: one."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Nāgārjuna, Mūlamadhyamakakārikā" .

pmt:PropheticSunna a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Die prophetische Überlieferung über Vorzeichen und Wahrsager"@de,
        "The prophetic tradition on omens and diviners"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Mishkāt al-Maṣābīḥ, book xxi, as quoted in Thomas Patrick Hughes, A Dictionary of Islam, London: W. H. Allen 1885, pp. 125 and 87" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of sayings ascribed to the Prophet, here only in the part that bears on omens, auguries, the consulting of diviners and the taking of a day or a sign as favourable or unfavourable."@en ;
    skos:note "Opened in this run at one place only and through a compiler: the entries FA'L and DIVINATION in Thomas Patrick Hughes, A Dictionary of Islam, London 1885, which print the traditions in Hughes's English and refer them to the Mishkāt al-Maṣābīḥ, book xxi. Nothing of the canonical collections was read. The transmission is entered at pm:placesEntered rather than pm:corpusNamed because a passage was read and taken in, not because the corpus was surveyed."@en,
        "What this holding does not have, and it is most of what there is. The two ṣaḥīḥ collections, the four sunan, the Muwaṭṭaʾ and the Musnad are all unopened. The tradition this run originally went looking for — the denial of the ill omen of the month of Ṣafar, which stands in the canonical collections beside the denials of contagion, of the bird of ill omen and of the owl — was not found in any edition this run could reach and is not entered. Its absence is recorded at pmp:SafarOmenSearch and not papered over here."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:MishkatAlMasabih ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MishkatAlMasabih ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Ṣaḥīḥ al-Buḵārī; Ṣaḥīḥ Muslim; Mishkāt al-Maṣābīḥ" .

pmt:SeferYetzirah a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Sefer Jetzira"@de,
        "Sefer Yetzirah"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Sefer Yetzirah 1:9-12 (Short Version; numbering of Kaplan 1997)" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the Book of Formation and its commentaries, in which the world is formed through letters, number and breath."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Sefer Yetzirah, Short Version" .

pmt:StoicPhysics a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Stoische Physik"@de,
        "Stoic physics"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chrysippus, in Galen, De placitis Hippocratis et Platonis V.3.8" ;
    skos:definition "The Stoic doctrine of a fiery breath that pervades matter and holds it together."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. The edition of Galen the fragment was read in is not recoverable and is left unstated rather than guessed. The section numbering of De placitis Hippocratis et Platonis differs between the older editions and the critical one, so V.3.8 locates the passage only in the latter."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Chrysippus, as reported in Galen, De placitis Hippocratis et Platonis" .

pmt:ValentinianGnosis a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Valentinianische Gnosis"@de,
        "Valentinian gnosis"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Irenaeus, Adversus Haereses I (trans. J. Keble, Oxford 1872)" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the Valentinian school of the second century, known in this file through the account of an opponent and not yet through its own writings."@en ;
    skos:note "A caution governs everything read out of this file at present. What is held here comes from a refutation: the heresiologist reports the doctrine in order to defeat it, selects what serves that end, and is under no obligation to state it as its holders would. Every node therefore carries pm:thirdPartyAscription and not textual attestation, and none of them may be read as a Valentinian self-statement until the same claim is found in a text of the school itself."@en,
        "The three decisions that precede a tradition file, stated rather than left implicit. Route of contact: none is documented towards the Indian or Chinese material, so a correspondence with either would count as a finding rather than as reception — but no such correspondence is asserted here yet. Edition: the account is read in the Keble translation of 1872, named on each node; the Nag Hammadi witnesses are not yet read and their translation is not yet chosen. Number of citable passages: one. That is thin, and deliberately so, on the model of traditions/daoist.ttl — a file grows in passages, and a heading without passages under it would be a claim about the corpus that the corpus does not carry."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Irenaeus, Adversus Haereses I, which is the heresiological report through which this transmission is reached" .

pmw:Abramelin a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Das Buch Abramelin"@de,
        "The Book of Abramelin"@en .

pmw:Ambazzi a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Das Ritual der Ambazzi"@de,
        "The ritual of Ambazzi"@en ;
    skos:note "Not opened. The critical edition of CTH 391.1 is reachable as a transliteration and was downloaded in this run and not read; no translation of it is reachable, and the monograph that is the standard treatment of this ritual tradition is in print. This work stands here as the warranted next place in this corpus, and queries/unopened-works.rq lists it."@en .

pmw:Anniwiyani a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Das Ritual der Anniwiyani"@de,
        "The ritual of Anniwiyani"@en ;
    skos:note "Not opened. Two treatments are reachable: the critical edition of CTH 393 as a transliteration, and the autograph with transliteration, translation, commentary and glossary in E. H. Sturtevant and G. Bechtel, A Hittite Chrestomathy, Philadelphia: Linguistic Society of America 1935, pp. 100-126. It is therefore the cheapest of the unopened works of this corpus and the one with the best apparatus; it was left for the reason that this run's lease was five works and not seven. queries/unopened-works.rq lists it."@en .

pmw:ApastambaSrautasutra a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Āpastamba-Śrautasūtra"@de,
        "Āpastamba Śrautasūtra"@en ;
    skos:note "Named here as a work of the corpus and unopened. Its ninth praśna is the expiation book of the other school. Garbe's edition of 1882 to 1902 is public domain."@en .

pmw:Astangahrdaya a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Vagbhata, Astangahrdaya"@de,
        "Vagbhata, Astangahrdaya"@en ;
    skos:note "Named here as a work of the corpus and unopened. Its Uttarasthana carries the same rasayana material one stage further on, which makes it the place to ask whether a later station of this transmission added a term, a sign or a condition of failure to what the lesson read here states — the question pm:Reworking exists for."@en .

pmw:AuroraConsurgens a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Aurora consurgens"@de,
        "Aurora consurgens"@en ;
    skos:note "Named here as a work of the corpus and unopened, with the same kind of warning as the Rosarium. The work has two parts of entirely different character and availability: the seven parables ascribed to Thomas Aquinas, which never entered the printed collections and are edited by Marie-Louise von Franz in 1957, and a second, operative tract printed in Artis auriferae I under the title Aurora consurgens quae dicitur Aurea hora. The parables are allegory throughout; a reconnaissance reports that it is the second part that carries the terms in days and the cautions, and that it is the freely readable one."@en .

pmw:BenjaminMaior a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Benjamin maior"@de,
        "Benjamin maior"@en ;
    skos:note "Unopened. Richard of St Victor, named in the registration as part of the principal corpus of this transmission."@en .

pmw:BwitiEthnographyOfTheReligiousImagination a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Fernandez, Bwiti: An Ethnography of the Religious Imagination in Africa"@de,
        "Fernandez, Bwiti: An Ethnography of the Religious Imagination in Africa"@en ;
    skos:note "A copy outside the lending restriction was found and deliberately not used. A work put up without its publisher's leave is not a path to the wording in the sense of SPEC section 15 - the question the access rules ask is whether a later reader can obtain the work, and an unauthorised upload answers that question for nobody."@en,
        "The work the registration named as principal corpus, Princeton University Press 1982, and the one that would answer the question this file leaves open about Fang and Fiote. Not opened, and the reason is a property of the work: it is in copyright and the archive copy is lending-restricted, answering 401 and 500 to the full-text route. Declared as a work of the corpus and left unopened so that queries/unopened-works.rq reports it."@en .

pmw:CoffinTexts a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Die Sargtexte"@de,
        "The Coffin Texts"@en ;
    skos:note "Named here as a work of the corpus and unopened. It is the layer between the Pyramid Texts and the Book of the Dead, and the transition is dated by both editors to the coffins of Horhotep and of Queen Mentuhotep."@en .

pmw:DeUmbrisIdearum a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Bruno, De umbris idearum"@de,
        "Bruno, De umbris idearum"@en ;
    skos:note "Named here as a work of the corpus and unopened. It carries the memory art of this transmission, which nothing in the holding yet touches."@en .

pmw:DeVitaLibriTres a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Ficino, De vita libri tres"@de,
        "Ficino, De vita libri tres"@en ;
    skos:note "Named here as a work of the corpus and unopened. It is the source the literature names for a part of the doctrine entered above, so it is the first place a run should go to ask whether the belief condition is this author's own or taken over."@en .

pmw:DiePangwe a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Tessmann, Die Pangwe"@de,
        "Tessmann, Die Pangwe"@en ;
    skos:note "Access: archive.org identifiers diepangwevlkerku01tess, 671,686 characters, and diepangwevlkerku02tess, 1,085,767 characters, both over the regular _djvu.txt route. Both tails are library catalogue stamps after the back matter, so neither is truncated. What was read at the wording: volume II, pages 241 and 242, the voluntary truth-ordeal; volume I at the three places that name Erythrophloeum guineense and volume II at its eight. The rest of some nine hundred printed pages is not opened, the Ngi cult of volume II among them."@en,
        "Not a work of the Bouiti corpus, and that is the finding rather than an omission. It is a monograph on the same people from a resident expedition of two years, published one year after Trilles, and it carries no occurrence of iboga, eboga, bwiti, bouiti or bweti in either volume. It is declared here because nodes cite it, not with pm:corpusWork, because pm:corpusWork says a transmission is carried in a work and this one is not."@en,
        "The edition as the title page in the full text gives it: Die Pangwe, Völkerkundliche Monographie eines westafrikanischen Negerstammes, Ergebnisse der Lübecker Pangwe-Expedition 1907-1909 und früherer Forschungen 1904-1907, von Günter Tessmann, Verlegt bei Ernst Wasmuth A.-G., Berlin 1913, two volumes."@en .

pmw:DrasaDYahia a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Drāšā d-Yahia, das Johannesbuch"@de,
        "Drāšā d-Yahia, the Book of John"@en ;
    skos:note "Named here as a work of the corpus and unopened. Lidzbarski's German of 1915 is public domain and its chapter of admonitions is reported to carry the same prohibition of sorcery that the Ginzā carries, which would make it a second witness inside the corpus for pmc:MandaeanCautioAdversusMagiam. Not opening it was a decision of this run and not a property of the work."@en .

pmw:EckhartPredigten a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Meister Eckhart, deutsche Predigten"@de,
        "Meister Eckhart, German sermons"@en ;
    skos:note "Unopened. A corpus rather than a single work, named in the registration; the identifier stands so that a later run does not mint a second one."@en .

pmw:EngishikiNorito a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Die Norito des Engishiki"@de,
        "The norito of the Engishiki"@en ;
    skos:note "Named here as a work of the corpus and unopened. It is where the purification liturgy entered below stands in full; the chronicle only says that it was recited and by whom. The English of E. M. Satow in the Transactions of the Asiatic Society of Japan of 1879 to 1881 is public domain."@en .

pmw:Heptameron a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Heptameron"@de,
        "Heptameron"@en ;
    skos:note "The tradition node records that the nine days of continence were read at the wording in the Heptameron of the Turner collection of 1655, but no node names it as a source. Until one does, the work reads as unopened, which is what the graph can vouch for."@en .

pmw:HopkinsScientificChristianMentalPractice a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Emma Curtis Hopkins, Scientific Christian Mental Practice"@de,
        "Emma Curtis Hopkins, Scientific Christian Mental Practice"@en ;
    skos:note "Named by the registration of this tradition and unopened. It is the work that turns the founder's practice into a taught method with pupils, so it is where the question of pmc:QuimbyCulpaOperantis has to be put next: whether the admission that the operator can fail survives into a transmission with a school behind it."@en .

pmw:IsisUnveiled a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Isis Unveiled"@de,
        "Isis Unveiled"@en ;
    skos:note "Unopened. Named in the registration's principal corpus."@en .

pmw:KatyayanaSrautasutra a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Kātyāyana-Śrautasūtra"@de,
        "Kātyāyana Śrautasūtra"@en ;
    skos:note "Named here as a work of the corpus and unopened. Its twenty-fifth adhyāya is the expiation book, and Eggeling's notes to the passages entered below refer to it by section at exactly the points where the Brāhmaṇa states a repair. Whoever opens it can say whether the performance manual carries a sign of a completed expiation where the exegesis carries none. Weber's edition of 1859 is public domain."@en .

pmw:Kojiki a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Kojiki, die Aufzeichnung von 712"@de,
        "Kojiki, the record of 712"@en ;
    skos:note "Named here as a work of the corpus and unopened. It carries its own version of the oath-divination entered below, and a run that wants to know whether the disagreement about how to read that criterion is confined to one chronicle goes here first. The English of B. H. Chamberlain of 1882 is public domain."@en .

pmw:LaoziXiangerZhu a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Laozi Xiang'er zhu"@de,
        "Laozi Xiang'er zhu"@en ;
    skos:note "Named here as a work of the corpus and unopened. It survives in the single Dunhuang manuscript Stein 6825, covering the upper half of the Laozi from the end of chapter 3 to the end of chapter 37, and the scholarly citation of it runs by the line numbers of a photographic reproduction rather than by chapter, because the manuscript carries no chapter division. Neither that reproduction nor a critical edition was reachable, and an uncredited transcription on an open wiki is not an edition. The unreachability is a property of the work's transmission and not of this run."@en .

pmw:Lemegeton a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Lemegeton (Clavicula Salomonis Regis)"@de,
        "Lemegeton (Clavicula Salomonis Regis)"@en ;
    skos:note "Unopened in this holding. The harvest of its third book, the Ars Paulina, and of the Ars Nova is prima-materia#736."@en .

pmw:LiberALVelLegis a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Liber L vel Legis sub figura CCXX"@de,
        "Liber L vel Legis sub figura CCXX"@en ;
    skos:note "Class A, the only class of which the transmission claims that not so much as the style of a letter may be changed. Printed with a facsimile in the seventh number. Unopened in this holding: the received-wording question belongs to pm:Receiving and was not carried in this run."@en .

pmw:LiberGraduumMontisAbiegni a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Liber XIII vel Graduum Montis Abiegni"@de,
        "Liber XIII vel Graduum Montis Abiegni"@en ;
    skos:note "Unopened in this holding. Printed in the third number and reachable; it carries the examination order of the whole curriculum, which is the prerequisite chain this file enters only in the fragments the other instructions state for themselves."@en .

pmw:LiberXXXAerum a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Liber XXX Aerum vel Saeculi (Die Vision und die Stimme) sub figura CCCCXVIII"@de,
        "Liber XXX Aerum vel Saeculi (The Vision and the Voice) sub figura CCCCXVIII"@en ;
    skos:note "Unopened in this holding. Printed as the supplement to the fifth number and therefore reachable; the syllabus grades it A-B and says the cries of the angels are to be regarded as accurate, which makes it the place where this transmission's claim of textual reliability is strongest and least examined."@en .

pmw:MagickInTheoryAndPractice a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Magick in Theory and Practice (Book 4, Teil III)"@de,
        "Magick in Theory and Practice (Book 4, Part III)"@en ;
    skos:note "Unopened at the wording of its own print. The Lecram Press edition, Paris 1929, was sought and not obtained; the finding stands at a search node. What was consulted instead is an electronic edition following a later reprint, and it is used in this file for one purpose only, as the second side of a dispute about a reading."@en .

pmw:MoheZhiguan a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Mohe zhiguan, die große Abhandlung über Ruhe und Schau"@de,
        "Mohe zhiguan, the great treatise on calming and contemplation"@en ;
    skos:note "Named first by the registration and unopened. The reason is the size and not the access: the text is reachable in the same edition as the work opened here, at T46 no. 1911 in twenty juan, and reading it is a run of its own. The Xiao zhiguan was taken first because it is the author's own summary of it and could be read through."@en .

pmw:MonasHieroglyphica a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Dee, Monas Hieroglyphica, mit den geistlichen Tagebüchern"@de,
        "Dee, Monas Hieroglyphica, with the spiritual diaries"@en ;
    skos:note "Named here as a work of the corpus and unopened. The registration singled the diaries out for a reason that still holds: they are a working log with dates, which makes them a different kind of witness from the treatises around them, and the only one in this corpus that could report what happened on a given day."@en .

pmw:NocheOscura a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Dunkle Nacht"@de,
        "Noche oscura"@en ;
    skos:note "Unopened. Named in the registration and in the same volume as the work opened here."@en .

pmw:NubesIgnorantiae a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Die Wolke des Nichtwissens"@de,
        "The Cloud of Unknowing"@en ;
    skos:note "Unopened. Named in the registration. The identifier is the Latin of the title because the work is anonymous and its Middle English title is itself a translation of the phrase it takes from the Dionysian corpus."@en .

pmw:NuqingGuilu a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Nüqing guilü"@de,
        "Nüqing guilü"@en ;
    skos:note "Six juan, DZ 790. Named here as a work of the corpus and searched by term only; not read at the wording. It is the demonological code of the transmission and is the place a later run should go for the names of the beings the petition addresses."@en .

pmw:Oahspe a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Newbrough, Oahspe: Eine neue Bibel in den Worten Jehovihs"@de,
        "Newbrough, Oahspe: A New Bible in the Words of Jehovih"@en ;
    skos:note "A warning for whoever opens it. The archive.org copies of this work are user uploads without catalogue metadata, and the fullest of them ends its pagination at 1132, which does not match the collation of the 1882 edition. Cite it by book, chapter and verse, which is how the work refers to itself, and not by page."@en,
        "Named here as a work of the corpus and unopened. It is the second and later layer of this transmission and the registration names it beside the first, and the question it carries is the mirror of what the first work answers at length: the 1882 volume declines to describe its own production. Its editor's preface asks why anything should be said about how this book was written, and the familiar account of a typewriter is not in it. When and by whom that account was first published is the question for the run that opens it, and the place to look is the spiritualist press of 1883 and the prefaces of the editions from 1891."@en .

pmw:PapyrusWestcar a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Papyrus Westcar"@de,
        "Papyrus Westcar"@en ;
    skos:note "Named here as a work of the corpus and unopened. It is the one item of the registered corpus that reports magical acts as events with witnesses rather than prescribing them, and it is therefore the first place to go for a case rather than a claim."@en .

pmw:PgmXII a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "PGM XII / PDM xii, der Leidener Papyrus J 384"@de,
        "PGM XII / PDM xii, the Leiden papyrus J 384"@en ;
    skos:note "Named here as a work of the corpus and unopened. It is the second of the bilingual handbooks of the same find and stands to the Papyrus of London and Leiden as its nearest neighbour; a run that wants a second demotic witness for anything entered from PDM xiv goes here first."@en .

pmw:PgmXIII a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "PGM XIII, der Leidener Papyrus J 395"@de,
        "PGM XIII, the Leiden papyrus J 395"@en ;
    skos:note "Named here as a work of the corpus and unopened."@en .

pmw:PyramidTexts a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Die Pyramidentexte"@de,
        "The Pyramid Texts"@en ;
    skos:note "Named here as a work of the corpus and unopened. It is the oldest layer, inscribed from the fifth dynasty onward, and about a third of the Coffin Text spells are said to descend from it. Whoever opens it can say whether the knowledge rubric entered below is as old as the claims it attaches to, or whether it is a later addition to older spells — which is the question the editors of 1901 and 1904 both raise and neither answers."@en .

pmw:Qolasta a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Qolastā, das kanonische Gebetbuch"@de,
        "Qolastā, the canonical prayerbook"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened on 2026-09-20 in the English of E. S. Drower of 1959, the edition that numbers the prayers from one to four hundred and fourteen. It was opened for one question and searched over the whole: whether the liturgical book itself anywhere states that a performance is void or is to be repeated. It does not. See pmp:MandaeanQolastaIrritumSearch."@en .

pmw:RS2019 a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Die Tafel RS 2.[019]"@de,
        "The tablet RS 2.[019]"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "CTA 21"@en,
        "KTU 1.21"@en ;
    skos:note "Not opened, for the reason given at pmw:RS3348, which is a property of the group. The square brackets in the siglum mark a reconstructed excavation number; the tablet is the second of the three Rapiuma texts and was found with the others in the house of the high priest on the acropolis."@en .

pmw:RS2024 a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Die Tafel RS 2.[024]"@de,
        "The tablet RS 2.[024]"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "CTA 22"@en,
        "KTU 1.22"@en ;
    skos:note "Not opened, for the reason given at pmw:RS3348, which is a property of the group. The third of the three Rapiuma texts, from the same find-spot."@en .

pmw:RS24251 a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Die Tafel RS 24.251"@de,
        "The tablet RS 24.251"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "KTU 1.107"@en ;
    skos:note "Not opened. It is printed in the same open-access edition as text 54 and is therefore as reachable as the five that were opened; the reason it was left is that this run's lease was five tablets. Its introduction was read and it matters for one node: it treats the same affliction as RS 24.244 by a different mechanism, the venom being gathered rather than diluted, and the editor asks whether its invitation to the other gods was ironic. The two mechanisms side by side would be a dispute inside one corpus and it cannot be entered as one until this tablet is read at the wording. queries/unopened-works.rq lists it."@en .

pmw:RS3348 a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Die Tafel RS 3.348"@de,
        "The tablet RS 3.348"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "CTA 20"@en,
        "KTU 1.20"@en ;
    skos:note "Not opened, and the reason is a property of the work and not of this run. This is the first of the three tablets the registration of this tradition named as the Rapiuma texts, and it is the one group of the corpus for which no transliteration is reachable at all. The open-access edition this file rests on is a volume of cult and ritual and does not include the narrative poetry; the one modern volume that does is lending-restricted and answers every full-text request with an authorisation error, and so is the 1963 corpus of alphabetic cuneiform texts, in both its text and its plate volume. The one further treatment read in translation only gives a translation with notes and no continuous transliterated text. The edition project that announces a fresh edition of these fragments gives 2026 as its date."@en,
        "What this costs the holding and why it is worth saying at the work. The registration named these texts as one of the two things this corpus was registered for, and they are the half that could not be delivered. A run that wants them has two routes and neither is a download: the lending volume through a library, or the announced new edition when it appears. queries/unopened-works.rq lists all three."@en .

pmw:RS34126 a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Die Tafel RS 34.126"@de,
        "The tablet RS 34.126"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "KTU 1.161"@en ;
    skos:note "Not opened. It is the best-served work of this corpus for a run that wants three independent treatments of one text: the open-access edition prints it as text 24, a second reachable translation of 1998 gives it independently, and the French editio princeps of 1982 with its autograph copy is reachable through the page-image route of a journal archive. It is therefore the warranted next place in this corpus and the one where the depth rule of SPEC 15 could actually be met. queries/unopened-works.rq lists it."@en .

pmw:Rasahrdayatantra a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Rasahṛdayatantra"@de,
        "Rasahṛdayatantra"@en ;
    skos:note "Named by the registration and unopened. Same state as the Rasārṇava: the Sanskrit is in print, no complete translation into a western language exists. The attribution of its author Govinda to the teacher of Śaṅkara is rejected in the philological literature, so a run that opens it should not take the registration's naming as a dating."@en .

pmw:Rasaratnasamuccaya a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Rasaratnasamuccaya"@de,
        "Rasaratnasamuccaya"@en ;
    skos:note "Named by the registration and unopened. Its first eleven chapters exist in English in modern editions that are not open access; chapters twelve to thirty, the whole iatrochemical part, have never been translated. The ascription to the Vāgbhaṭa of the Aṣṭāṅgahṛdaya is rejected in the philological literature and the dating runs between the thirteenth and the sixteenth century, so the name in the registration is a title and not an author."@en .

pmw:Rasarnava a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Rasārṇava"@de,
        "Rasārṇava"@en ;
    skos:note "Named by the registration as a principal work of the corpus and unopened. The Sanskrit is in print and reachable — the Bibliotheca Indica edition of Ray and Kaviratna, Calcutta 1910 — and no complete translation into any western language exists, which is a property of the work and not of the run: what has been translated is its first chapter and scattered verses in the histories."@en .

pmw:ReformatioGeneralis a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Allgemeine Reformation der gantzen Welt (1614)"@de,
        "Allgemeine Reformation der gantzen Welt (1614)"@en ;
    skos:note "Named here as a work of the corpus and unopened. It is printed in front of the Fama in the Kassel edition of 1614 and is a German translation of a chapter of Boccalini's Ragguagli di Parnaso; a run that wants to know how much of the Fama's programme is Boccalini's goes here. The Wellcome scan b28759345 of the 1781 reprint carries it with the Fama and the Confessio."@en .

pmw:RosariumPhilosophorum a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Rosarium philosophorum"@de,
        "Rosarium philosophorum"@en ;
    skos:note "Named here as a work of the corpus and unopened. A bibliographic warning belongs with the identifier, because the title covers two different works that stand next to each other in the same collections and share passages verbatim: the late medieval florilegium first printed with its woodcuts at Frankfurt in 1550 and reprinted without them in Artis auriferae II, and the treatise ascribed to Arnald of Villanova as Thesaurus thesaurorum seu Rosarium philosophorum, which is divided into numbered capitula. A run that opens this work must say which of the two it read, and the chapter numbers that circulate in the literature belong to the second."@en,
        "Why it is the next place to go. A reconnaissance reports that the florilegium cites the assay catalogue of the Summa perfectionis by name as Geber's book of tests, and prints the formula that the work is to be repeated if the fire test is not passed. If that is at the wording, the holding gains the link between an allegorical florilegium and an operative assay canon, which is the question pmc:TheTestOnTheIngredient now carries."@en .

pmw:ScotDiscoverieOfWitchcraft a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Reginald Scot, The Discoverie of Witchcraft"@de,
        "Reginald Scot, The Discoverie of Witchcraft"@en .

pmw:SextumSeptimumMosis a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Sechstes und Siebtes Buch Mosis"@de,
        "Sechstes und Siebtes Buch Mosis"@en ;
    skos:note "Named here as a work of the principal corpus and unopened. A contemporary letter printed in the study of 1904 names it beside Hohman as a foundation of the practice, and it does so with a weaker warrant than it gives Hohman: the writer says he understands the one and knows the other. That gradation is the reason this work is named and not yet used."@en .

pmw:SteinerGeheimwissenschaft a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Steiner, Die Geheimwissenschaft im Umriss"@de,
        "Steiner, Die Geheimwissenschaft im Umriss"@en ;
    skos:note "Named by the registration of this tradition and unopened. It carries the cosmology the book of the schooling refers to and does not state, so it is where the content of the perceptions is to be found rather than the method of obtaining them."@en .

pmw:SummaPerfectionisMagisterii a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Summa perfectionis magisterii"@de,
        "Summa perfectionis magisterii"@en ;
    skos:note "Its ascription is disputed and the dispute matters for the contact route. The work circulated as Geber's, that is as Jabir ibn Hayyan's; the received position since Newman's edition of 1991 is that it is by Paul of Taranto, a Latin Franciscan of the late thirteenth century, and the minority position of al-Hassan holds to an Arabic original. If the received position is right, the assay catalogue is a Latin addition to the transmission and not something the Latin took over, which would make it a reworking in the sense of pm:Reworking rather than a copy. Nothing of that is claimed here."@en,
        "Not named by the registration and added to the corpus here, unopened, because the run that read the Turba found the gap this work fills. The Turba states two acts of assay and both judge its own operation; it never states a way of knowing whether a material is what it is called. The Summa is reported to carry a closed catalogue of assays of the metal — cupellation, cementation, ignition, fusion, exposure to sharp vapours, quenching, mixture with sulphur, calcination and reduction, and the taking up of quicksilver — announced as a list and then worked through one by one. Whether that is at the wording decides a question this holding is carrying, so the work belongs in the corpus even though nobody here has opened it."@en .

pmw:TheChakras a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "The Chakras"@de,
        "The Chakras"@en ;
    skos:note "Named in the registration's principal corpus. Opened at its second chapter, The Force-Centres, for one question only: whether the radiation counts are stable across the author's own works. They are, and the later work adds the author's own comment on the leap from sixteen to ninety-six and from ninety-six to nine hundred and seventy-two. The rest of the work is unread."@en .

pmw:TheSecretDoctrine a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "The Secret Doctrine"@de,
        "The Secret Doctrine"@en ;
    skos:note "Unopened. Named in the registration's principal corpus."@en .

pmw:TrowardEdinburghLectures a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Thomas Troward, The Edinburgh Lectures on Mental Science"@de,
        "Thomas Troward, The Edinburgh Lectures on Mental Science"@en ;
    skos:note "Named by the registration of this tradition and unopened. It is the work through which the transmission passes into its twentieth-century form, and the one a run should open to find out whether the conditions dropped here were later put back."@en .

pmw:Vimuttimagga a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Upatissa, Vimuttimagga"@de,
        "Upatissa, Vimuttimagga"@en ;
    skos:note "Declared here because one node of this file cites it and a work a node points at must have an identifier. It is deliberately NOT listed under pm:corpusWork of pmt:Theravada: the Pali original is lost, the work survives in a Chinese translation of the sixth century and in Tibetan citations, its school is disputed, and it stands in the principal corpus of the separately registered pmt:TheravadaCommentarial. That the same corpus is registered twice in this holding, once as a registration and once as the read corpus of this transmission's files, is a defect of the holding and is filed as such rather than resolved by a note."@en,
        "Opened on 2026-09-29 at the sixth chapter only, on temperaments, and read at the wording in the English of N. R. M. Ehara, Soma Thera and Kheminda Thera as revised by Nyanatusita, The Path of Freedom, Buddhist Publication Society, which prints the column numbers of the Taishō edition of the Chinese in the running text; the passage used is at Taishō 410a10 to 410a22. The rest of the work was not read."@en .

pmw:Viyahapannatti a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Viyāhapannatti (Bhagavaī)"@de,
        "Viyāhapannatti (Bhagavaī)"@en ;
    skos:note "Declared here for the same reason, and unopened for this transmission. traditions/jain.ttl reads its fifteenth book in second hand for the narrative of Gosāla; the doctrine reported in this file is not taken from there."@en .

pmw:WoodwardManualOfAMystic a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Woodward, Manual of a Mystic"@de,
        "Woodward, Manual of a Mystic"@en ;
    skos:note "The identifier exists because the alternative was worse. Pointing those two nodes at the manual's identifier would have made the manual read as the source of a caution and a chakra system it does not contain, which is exactly the confusion the volume's own layout invites. A reader who follows pm:fromWork now lands on the right book."@en,
        "The volume of 1916 that translates the manual, declared as a work of its own and deliberately NOT listed under pm:corpusWork of the transmission. It is not a work of this transmission; it is the book in which the transmission was translated, and it contains three layers besides the manual: the editor's preface, the translator's prefatory note, and the translator's footnotes. Two things in this holding lie in those layers and not in the manual, and both point here rather than at pmw:YogavacarasManual: the account of the pupil who went mad, at pmc:BoranSisyaUmmattako, which the prefatory note is the first fixation of; and the compiled system of force-centres, at pmc:WoodwardCakkaInterpolatio in originations/boran-theosophical-insertion.ttl."@en .

pmw:ZaubersprucheDerTaulipangIndianer a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Koch-Gruenberg, Zaubersprueche der Taulipang-Indianer"@de,
        "Koch-Gruenberg, Zaubersprueche der Taulipang-Indianer"@en ;
    skos:note "The primary publication of the spells, named by Koch-Gruenberg himself in the footnote at page 220: Zaubersprueche der Taulipang-Indianer, in Archiv fuer Anthropologie, Neue Folge, Band XIII, Heft 4, pages 371 ff., Braunschweig 1915. Declared as a work of the corpus and deliberately left unopened in this run, so that queries/unopened-works.rq reports it."@en,
        "Why it is not the work to prefer. The eleven spells are printed in volume III of 1923 in three parts each - the myth that leads to the spell, the original text with interlinear German, and the instruction for applying it - and the article of 1915 is the shorter publication. Which of the two carries the complete set is not established here and is the first question for whoever opens it."@en .

pmw:ZhangZhungNyanGyud a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Zhang Zhung Nyan Gyud, die mündliche Überlieferung von Zhangzhung"@de,
        "Zhang Zhung Nyan Gyud, the oral transmission of Zhangzhung"@en ;
    skos:note "Named as the first work of this corpus and unopened. The route was tested in this run and the finding is about the route and not about the work: what is freely available in full text is a transcription of oral teachings given on these texts by Yongdzin Lopon Tenzin Namdak, edited by C. and D. Ermakova, which is a commentary and therefore a work in which the text has been met and not read. A critical edition with the Tibetan was not reached."@en .

pmc:AbodaZaraAfflictionsSwornToTheirHour a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Zuechtigungen sind auf Tag und Stunde vereidigt, daher beweist die Heilung im Goetzentempel nichts"@de,
        "The afflictions are sworn to a day and an hour, so the cure at the idol's temple proves nothing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Aboda zara 55a, the exchange of Zonin and R. Aqiba with R. Johanan on Deuteronomy 28:59, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. VII (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1903), pp. 990-991" ;
    skos:definition "The passage the whole of this file was opened for. Zonin puts it to R. Aqiba that the two of them know there is nothing in the idol, and that they nevertheless see people go in broken and come back healed, and he asks how that comes about. R. Aqiba does not deny the observation. He answers with a parable of a trustworthy man who will not break his custom because one fool deposited without witnesses, and then applies it: when afflictions are sent upon a man they are put under oath to go only on a named day, only at a named hour, only through a named man and only by a named medicine; and when the day on which they are to go comes round and the man happens to be standing in the idol's temple, they go anyway, saying that by rights they should not, but that they will not break their oath because this fool is behaving improperly. R. Johanan's proof text is attached in the same breath: bad and faithful illnesses means bad in their mission and faithful in their oath."@en ;
    skos:note "Not modelled as pm:Disputing. The text does not stage two sides over one claim; it stages a question and an answer, and both speakers agree on the premise that the idol is nothing. The disagreement the first file found at pmc:SanhedrinWhetherTheArtPrevailsAgainstTheDecree is of the other kind and the two should not be read as the same construction."@en,
        "The limit of the claim, and the transmission does not notice it. The oath fixes the day, the hour, the man and the medicine in advance, so on its own terms somebody could ask which day was named and check. Nothing in the passage says who may know the appointed day, and nothing makes the appointment retrievable, so the account is unfalsifiable in practice while sounding like a schedule. That asymmetry is the node's contribution to pmc:TheObservationAnsweredByNarrowing and is why it is not simply filed as a dispute."@en,
        "What makes this worth a node of its own rather than a line in the first file. The holding carries many claims that are safe against a counter-case, and it carries them because the transmission supplies an excuse for a run that produced nothing. This is the opposite operation on the opposite material: the rival's run produced something, the something is granted, and what is withdrawn is the causal connection. The mechanism offered is a coincidence in time between a cure that was going to happen and a visit that did not cause it, and it is stated as a general rule and not as a remark about one case. A modern protocol calling a reported success confounded is making the same move; the point of entering this is that the move is thirteen or fourteen centuries older than the protocol and is made by a party with everything to gain from denying the cure outright."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliAbodaZara ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmc:AbodaZaraRainOnDemandOfTheIdol a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Regen, der auf Verlangen eines Goetzen eintritt"@de,
        "Rain brought on at the demand of an idol"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Aboda zara 55a, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. VII (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1903), p. 991" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity the reported sequence claims: rain in a time of drought, following a slaughter the idol itself asked for in a dream. It is held as its own node because the holding counts capacities and because this one has a property the weather claims in it mostly lack - the occasion is set by the other party and not by the practitioner, so the operation cannot be run at will and cannot be repeated to order."@en ;
    skos:note "That property is also what makes the claim cheap. Rain is demanded when the world is in want of it, which is when rain is in any case overdue, and the transmission that reports the sequence does not notice this although its own account of the matter would have been strengthened by it. The observation belongs to the compiler and is recorded here rather than put into the definition."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliAbodaZara ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmc:AegyptiaMalaAversa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Kapitel des Nicht: Vermögen, als abgewendete Schäden ausgesprochen"@de,
        "The chapters of not: capacities stated as harms averted"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, the chapter titles of the Theban recension, chapters 5, 27, 28, 30, 30A, 31, 32, 34, 39, 40, 41, 42, 43, 44, 45, 46, 47, 53, 61, 63A, 85, 91, 93, 101, 154, 163, 175, 176, 182 and 189, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vols. I to III" ;
    skos:definition "Thirty-two of the hundred and thirty-two recovered titles name their capacity by the harm it prevents. Among them, in Budge's wording: of not letting the deceased do work in the underworld; of not letting the heart be taken from him; of not letting the head of a man be cut off from him; of not letting the soul of a man be taken from him; of not letting the soul be captive; of not letting the seat and throne be taken away; of not perishing and of becoming alive; of not dying a second time, which the corpus states twice under two numbers; of not eating filth and of not drinking foul water; of not being bitten by snakes; of not being burnt by fire; of not sailing to the east; of not allowing the body of a man to moulder away; of not letting a man make a journey being hungry. Beside them stand the chapters of driving back and repulsing: the eater of the ass, the serpent Rerek, the crocodile that cometh to carry off the charm, the slaughterings performed in the underworld, and Apshait."@en ;
    skos:note "The count is of recovered titles. Where a title is lost to the scan its capacity is unclassified, and the proportion given here is a floor and not a measurement of the corpus."@en,
        "The shape of the claim is what is entered here, not a judgement on it. A capacity whose whole content is that something does not happen is satisfied by the ordinary course of things and cannot be distinguished from it by any observation the corpus names. Read together with pmc:AegyptiaRubricaeAnatomia, which found that no rubric in the three volumes states a sign or a term, this is the same absence approached from the other side of the book: the ends are stated so that nothing would count against them, and the means are stated so that nothing would count for them."@en,
        "Two of the negatives are unlike the rest and worth marking. Not eating filth and not drinking foul water name a state a living person could report of himself, and chapter 189 pairs the second with not making a journey being hungry. These are the only averted harms in the group that belong to the body rather than to the fate of the dead."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmc:Agape a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Liebe"@de,
        "Love"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 81",
        "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 84" ;
    skos:definition "Called the offspring of apatheia and the keeper of the door to the contemplation of created things."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:DesertMonasticism .

pmc:AgrippaSilentium a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Durch Schweigen gestärkt, durch Veröffentlichung zerstört, und keine vollständige Wirkung folgt"@de,
        "Strengthened by silence, destroyed by publication, and no complete effect follows"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Agrippa, De occulta philosophia III.2 (trans. J. F., London 1651)" ;
    skos:definition "The rule at the head of the third book. Those secrets which are among a few wise men and communicated by mouth only are not fit to be publicly written, and the author asks the reader's pardon for passing over in silence many and the chiefest secret mysteries of ceremonial magic; he supposes he has done enough if he opens what is necessary to be known. He then warns that every magical experiment flees the public, seeks to be hid and is strengthened by silence, but is destroyed by publication, neither does any complete effect follow after; and that the prating of a companion, his incredulity and unworthiness, hinders and disturbs the effect in every operation."@en ;
    skos:note "The clause about the companion is the same displacement as the clause about the reader at the conclusion, one degree closer to the operation: a second person's talk, disbelief or unworthiness accounts for a failure, and none of the three is anything the operator could establish beforehand."@en,
        "The failure condition sits on what the text keeps out of itself, which is the arrangement this holding already carries from three other transmissions. Here it is put twice over in one chapter: the chief mysteries are withheld, and the withholding is itself made a condition of the working. A reader who has the printed book therefore holds, by the book's own rule, a thing whose effect publication has destroyed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:AgrippaPraeparatioOraculi ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeOccultaPhilosophia ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RenaissanceMagic .

pmc:AjivikaChalabhijati a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die sechs Geburtsklassen, mit den Ājīvikas an der Spitze"@de,
        "The six classes of birth, with the Ājīvikas at the top"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aṅguttara Nikāya 6.57, Chaḷabhijātisutta 1.3-8.1 (Mahāsaṅgīti Tipiṭaka Buddhavasse 2500; segments an6.57:1.3-8.1), read with the English translation of Bhikkhu Sujato" ;
    skos:altLabel "chaḷābhijāti"@en ;
    skos:definition "The classification the Aṅguttara Nikāya reports: a black class of slaughterers, hunters, fishers, bandits, executioners, butchers and jailers and any others of a cruel livelihood; a blue class of mendicants whose life is thorny and any others who teach the efficacy of deed and action; a red class of the Jain ascetics who wear one cloth; a yellow class of the white-robed householders who are disciples of the naked ascetics; a white class of the Ājīvaka ascetics, male and female; and an ultimate white class consisting of Nanda Vaccha, Kisa Saṅkicca and the bamboo-staffed Gosāla."@en ;
    skos:note "The scheme puts the transmission that denies the efficacy of deeds above the one that teaches it, and names its own three founders as the single highest class. That is a scale of attainment with persons at its top and no practice between the classes, which is consistent with the doctrine of pmc:AjivikaNaTapasaParipaka and is the only ranking this transmission is reported to have had."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AnguttaraNikaya ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ajivika .

pmc:AjivikaSamsarasuddhi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Läuterung durch das Umherwandern"@de,
        "Purification by running through the round"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphalasutta 20.7 and 20.9 (Mahāsaṅgīti Tipiṭaka Buddhavasse 2500; segments dn2:20.7, dn2:20.9), read with the translations of Bhikkhu Sujato and Sabbamitta" ;
    skos:altLabel "saṁsārasuddhi"@en ;
    skos:definition "The end the doctrine does hold out, and the terms on which it is reached. Pleasure and pain are measured out; the round has its limit set; there is no increasing it and no diminishing it, no raising and no lowering. Fools and the wise alike, having run on and wandered through, will make an end of suffering. The running through is enumerated: one million four hundred thousand principal wombs, and six thousand, and six hundred; five hundred kinds of deed, and five, and three; a deed and a half-deed; sixty-two paths and sixty-two sub-aeons; six classes of birth and eight stages of a human life; four thousand nine hundred Ājīvaka ascetics, four thousand nine hundred wanderers, four thousand nine hundred abodes of serpents; two thousand faculties, three thousand hells, thirty-six elements of dust; seven embryos with perception, seven without, seven knotless; seven gods, seven men, seven goblins, seven lakes, seven cast-offs and seven hundred, seven precipices and seven hundred, seven dreams and seven hundred; and eight million four hundred thousand great aeons."@en ;
    skos:note "Also the one place where the Ājīvika report concedes a goal that is reached: the end of suffering, by everyone, fools and wise alike. The ball of thread at pmc:AjivikaSuttagulaUpama is the image the text gives for it."@en,
        "Not added to either generalization in this run. SPEC 16 requires the counter-search against the holding before a generalization is written or enlarged, and the counter-search that would have to run here is the one for transmissions that put a count on something and let it come due. It was not carried."@en,
        "This is the shape the holding keeps at generalizations/the-count-that-does-not-fall-due.ttl and at generalizations/counted-thresholds.ttl, in its purest form. The count is exact, the terms are enumerated to the last seven hundred dreams, and the total cannot fall due inside a life or inside any number of lives a witness could follow. A number that precise and that unreachable does the work of having no number at all, and the text says in its next sentence that nothing can shorten it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DighaNikaya ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ajivika .

pmc:Apatheia a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Apatheia"@de,
        "Apatheia"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 81" ;
    skos:definition "Freedom from the disturbance of the passions, called the flower of ascetic labour."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:DesertMonasticism .

pmc:ArsNotoriaOratioYieldsCognitioAegritudinis a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Oration der Heilkunst bringt die Kenntnis von Tod oder Leben hervor"@de,
        "The oration of the physical art yields the knowledge of death or life"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), §§ 26-30a, pp. 24-25, ed. Peterson" ;
    skos:note "This is the shortest way in the witness from an act to an outcome a third party can check. The outcome is binary, it arrives in days, it is recorded anyway, and the judgement can be written down before it arrives. The claim the tradition is known for, the sciences within a month, needs a measuring instrument that has first to be chosen; this one needs none."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ArsNotoriaOratioPhysicae ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ArsNotoriaCognitioAegritudinis .

pmc:Asana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sitzhaltung"@de,
        "Posture"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "The seat, treated in the first chapter as the first limb of the practice."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:AsipuMimmaLemnuLaTehi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Dem gesalbten Beschwörer naht nichts Böses"@de,
        "Nothing bad approaches the anointed exorcist"@en ;
    dcterms:source "KAR 31 (VAT 8803) rev. 31 (M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons, BAM 8, De Gruyter 2016, p. 40)" ;
    skos:definition "The outcome the ritual states for the anointing: that when he approaches the patient, nothing bad will approach him."@en ;
    skos:note "Stated without reservation and without a condition on the operator's state, his worthiness or the will of a god. The only condition in the sentence is that the anointing come first."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmc:AsipuNominisIncertitudo a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wer immer du seist: die Beschwörung, die sich nicht festlegen muss"@de,
        "Whether thou be: the adjuration written so as not to have to decide"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Utukki Limnuti, Tablet III, lines 160-165, in the English of R. Campbell Thompson, The Devils and Evil Spirits of Babylonia, Luzac's Semitic Text and Translation Series vol. XIV (London: Luzac, 1903), vol. I" ;
    skos:definition "The form Tablet III gives the litany. It is not a list of things present but a disjunction over what might be: whether thou be an evil Spirit or an evil Demon, or an evil Ghost or an evil Devil, or an evil God or an evil Fiend, or sickness, or death, or Phantom of Night, or Wraith of Night, or disease, or evil pestilence — be thou removed from before me. The operation is stated so that the exorcist need not establish which of the twelve he is dealing with."@en ;
    skos:note "The reason given for the form is the editor's and not the tablet's, and is entered as his. Campbell Thompson writes that the exorcist names every spirit any one of which may be the cause of the sickness, and by so doing impresses the demon that he is in possession of his name. Nothing in the translated lines says that, and a reader who took it from this node as the tradition's own account would be taking an 1903 explanation for an Assyrian one."@en,
        "This is the strongest single thing the reading found, and it bears on the whole apparatus of naming that this holding has been entering from the Egyptian side. At the gate of the hall of the two Maati the dead man must produce the right name or he does not pass; here the officiant recites every name there is, and the recitation works whichever one is true. The two arrangements put the name to opposite uses: as a test that can be failed, and as a disjunction that cannot."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmc:AyurvedaAtitajnana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Rede, die das Vergangene offenbart"@de,
        "Speech that reveals the past"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.4.28 and I.4.46-48, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, parts XXXIV-XXXV, Calcutta 1905, pp. 1061 and 1064-1065" ;
    skos:definition "Claimed of the preparation taken lying in a covered wooden vessel and again of the Vedic rasayana of the fourth section: one gets such an excellence that one's speech reveals even the past; it imparts to one the power of knowing the past. Eyes and ears are said in the same place to become like those of the immortals."@en ;
    skos:note "A capacity about knowledge of fact, claimed by a medical text, and therefore of the kind a protocol could get hold of if the transmission stated any procedure by which a report is produced and checked. It states none. The claim is that the user has the power, not that anybody ever put a question to him."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmc:AyurvedaDosatyagapurvakatva a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Ohne das Ablegen der gröberen Fehler von Körper und Geist erntet er die Wirkungen nie"@de,
        "Without casting off the graver faults of body and mind, he never reaps the effects"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.4.58-59, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXV, Calcutta 1905, p. 1066" ;
    skos:definition "The lesson's own condition on all its promises, stated immediately after the rasayana of conduct. That person who, without casting off at least the graver faults of both body and mind, uses rasayana, never reaps the effects of rasayana; and rasayanic combinations, which have for their objects the lengthening of life and the prevention of decrepitude and disease, succeed with those who are cleansed in respect of mind and body and who have their souls under control."@en ;
    skos:note "The corresponding statement for the drug taken in the wooden vessel is harder still and runs the same way: the effects of these celestial herbs can be borne by persons like yourselves, and cannot be borne by sinful souls. Spoken by Indra to seers, in the section where the procedure is given."@en,
        "This is the condition that decides what the calendar and the hut and the counted fruits are worth. Every outward condition of the lesson can be met or missed in public; this one is a state of the user, it is stated as a necessary condition of the whole class, and its absence accounts in advance for any user in whom nothing happened."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "He never reaps the effects of rasayana. Stated in the text as the outcome for one who uses rasayana without casting off at least the graver faults of body and mind; the effect is withheld entire and no partial effect is named."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:AyurvedaRasayana ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:AyurvedaAcararasayana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmc:Azazel a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Azazel"@de,
        "Azazel"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:1 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:altLabel "Asael"@en ;
    skos:definition "A leader among the Watchers, named in the Book of the Watchers as the one who disclosed metallurgy, weaponry and adornment."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:nameForm "Azâzêl" ;
    pm:nameRole "watcher" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:Baraqel a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Baraqel"@de,
        "Baraqel"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:altLabel "Baraqijal"@en ;
    skos:definition "A Watcher whose teaching concerns celestial signs; the two recensions differ on which."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:nameForm "Barâqîjâl" ;
    pm:nameRole "watcher" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:BengeFailureCauses a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die acht Ursachen, denen ein Versagen des Orakels zugeschrieben wird"@de,
        "The eight causes to which a failure of the oracle is attributed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 155 and pp. 155-157" ;
    skos:definition "Where the oracle contradicts itself the failure is attributed to one of eight causes: the wrong variety of the creeper having been gathered, the breach of a taboo, witchcraft, the anger of the owners of the forest in which the creeper grows, the age of the poison, the anger of the ghosts, sorcery, and use. Two varieties of the creeper are distinguished by name, and poison that kills without discrimination is set aside for some months to cool and is thrown away if it does not."@en ;
    skos:note "The list is the reason the falsifying condition on the neighbouring node does not function as one inside the transmission. Every occurrence of the condition is assigned to a cause that leaves the oracle itself untouched, and the recorder reports that the errors as well as the sound judgements are taken as proof of its accuracy. That sentence is the recorder's summary of an attitude and not a quoted Zande statement, and it is entered as such."@en,
        "This is also the passage on which the whole later argument about whether Zande thought is closed against refutation rests. None of that argument was read at the wording in this run; it stands as an issue with the works named."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:BengeValidVerdictRule a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Huhn muss sterben und das andere leben"@de,
        "One fowl must die and the other live"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 138-139, the four cases lettered A to D" ;
    skos:definition "The rule by which an oracular verdict stands or falls: across the two tests one fowl must die and the other must live. If both die or both live the verdict is contradictory and therefore void, and the matter must be put to the oracle again on another occasion."@en ;
    skos:note "This is a falsifying condition stated by the transmission itself, applied by its own practitioners, and printed with four worked cases. It is the reason this tradition was opened before others in the registry. What the transmission does with the condition when it is met is a separate matter and stands on its own node below."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:BengeYieldsIdentificationOfTheWitch a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Giftorakel bringt die Benennung des Hexers hervor"@de,
        "The poison oracle yields the naming of the witch"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 35-36 and pp. 141-143" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:BengeConsultation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:IdentificationOfTheWitch .

pmc:BouitiMirrorReportDecidesAdmission a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Er muss bestimmte Dinge sehen und einzeln nennen; andernfalls hat der Fetisch ihn abgewiesen und alles beginnt von neuem"@de,
        "He must see certain things and detail them; if he does not, the fetish refused him and all begins again"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Le Scao, Au pays de Sette Cama: la religion des habitants. Les fétiches: le Bouiti, Messager du St Esprit, 1908-1909, the section on the initiation" ;
    skos:definition "The criterion of admission, and the whole of it. At the moment he looks into the mirror he must see certain things and detail them to the Ganga. If he says it rightly he is received into the Bouiti; if he does not say what is required, it is that the fetish did not want to show itself to him, refused him, and everything is to begin again. What he must see is the secret of the Ganga and of the initiates."@en ;
    skos:note "Tout est à recommencer is the operative clause and it is not a penalty. A candidate who fails the vision is not disqualified and not punished; the procedure is simply run again, which means the three days of the root are run again. Nothing in the passage fixes a number of attempts, and the only outcome the passage does make terminal is the one in the caution at pmc:BouitiDibohaMayNotReturn."@en,
        "What makes it worse than the excusing clauses the holding already carries is the withholding. The content of the criterion is secret, so a candidate cannot know what he is being asked and a later reader cannot check whether the same thing was asked twice. The mediator says he could get nothing out of even the converts: they are full of reticence and seized with terror at the subject, and the accounts that do circulate - that the initiate sees sometimes a goat, sometimes an elephant - are called incoherent by the man reporting them. A criterion whose content is unobtainable is unauditable by construction, and the three animals named are the one place where its content shows at all."@en,
        "Why this node is the reason the work was opened. The criterion has the shape the holding is shortest of and the defect the holding has catalogued: the outcome is announced in advance, it is decided by one man who already knows the answer, and a negative outcome is attributed to the fetish rather than to the procedure or to the root, so that no failed run counts against either. The transmission states the excuse itself and does not leave it to be inferred."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AuPaysDeSetteCamaLeBouiti ;
    pm:readVia "H. Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan (Münster i. W.: Aschendorffsche Verlagsbuchhandlung, 1912), p. 51" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bwiti .

pmc:CautionAgainstProvingTheLucidity a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Keinen Versuch zulassen, die Hellsicht des Somnambulen zu beweisen"@de,
        "Permit no attempt to prove the somnambulist's lucidity"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. V, pp. 75 and 77, and chap. VIII, pp. 166 and 179" ;
    skos:definition "A general rule from which the operator is never to depart: never, on any account, to permit any question of curiosity, any attempt to prove the lucidity of the somnambulist; to speak to him solely of his disease; to direct all his attention to the means of restoring his health. Every experiment is said to be extremely injurious, and the source states that he never permitted himself to consult a somnambulist to put him to the proof."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also the sentence against which the whole examination literature of the period is to be read. The 1784 commission's design was exactly the thing forbidden here, and Braid's objection of 1852 — that a faculty this capricious would be worthless even if real, because one could not tell a true enunciation from a false one — is the same observation made from outside. Neither work was opened in this run for this node; the two addresses stand in the examination file beside it and in an issue."@en,
        "The most consequential node in this file for the purposes of this holding. A transmission that claims a faculty and prohibits its demonstration has removed itself from examination by its own rule, and has done so in the language of care for the subject rather than of evasion. The prohibition is not incidental: it is derived, in the same chapter, from the stated prerequisite that lucidity requires an undivided attention, so the reason the faculty cannot be tested is the same reason it is said to work."@en,
        "Two further clauses make it complete. Experiments to show that a somnambulist can be made to obey the will are called experiments of curiosity, without moral danger but very imprudent, since they fatigue the patient uselessly and may prevent his restoration. And where an incredulous person consults with the aim of putting the somnambulist to the proof, the source holds that he will, without knowing it, exert an influence furnishing him with new reasons for his incredulity — a prediction that a failed test is evidence for the claim."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:MagnetismeLucidite ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:Cetopariyanana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kenntnis fremden Bewusstseins"@de,
        "Knowledge encompassing another mind"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XIII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "Knowledge of the state of another consciousness."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:ChukcheePresupposesAnsweringCalls a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Vollzug setzt einen voraus, der antwortet"@de,
        "The performance presupposes someone who answers"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Waldemar Bogoras, The Chukchee, Memoir of the American Museum of Natural History vol. VII (Leiden: E. J. Brill, 1904-1909), p. 434" ;
    skos:note "The limit of the claim, and it is the difference from the North Atlantic case. What the recorder says is that the practitioner holds himself unable to perform properly. He does not say that the spirits stay away, and nothing here states an outcome the absence of the answerer would produce. The seeress in Greenland says exactly that, and this transmission does not."@en,
        "What the passage adds beyond the rule is the behaviour that follows from it: novices learning the practice usually induce a brother or a sister to respond, so as to encourage the zeal of the performer. A rule that people arrange their households around is a rule that was in force, and the arrangement is reported without being asked for."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The recorder states it as an incapacity and not as a preference: without one who gives answering calls, a Chukchee shaman considers himself unable to perform his calling in a proper way. The sentence names the office, not the crowd."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChukcheeShamanisticPerformance ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by Waldemar Bogoras among the Reindeer and Maritime Chukchee of northeastern Siberia in the field of the Jesup North Pacific Expedition and published in English between 1904 and 1909; the statement is given as what a Chukchee shaman considers, without a named speaker, in the recorder's English with the Chukchee term beside it."@en ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChukcheeAnsweringCalls ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChukcheeShamanism .

pmc:ClaviculaOperatioYieldsImperium a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Operation im Kreis bringt hervor, dass die Geister erscheinen und gehorchen"@de,
        "The operation of the circle brings the spirits to appear and obey"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Clavicula Salomonis, Book I chapters III, VI and VII, in the English of S. L. MacGregor Mathers, The Key of Solomon the King, London: George Redway, 1889" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ClaviculaOperatioCirculi ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ClaviculaSalomonis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SolomonicGrimoire ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ClaviculaImperiumSpirituum .

pmc:ControleUniversel a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die allgemeine Kontrolle der Geisterlehre"@de,
        "The universal control of the teaching of the spirits"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Allan Kardec, L'Évangile selon le spiritisme, introduction, section II, sub-heading Contrôle universel de l'enseignement des Esprits" ;
    skos:altLabel "contrôle universel de l'enseignement des Esprits"@fr ;
    skos:definition "The rule of verification the transmission sets for itself: concordance in the teaching of the spirits is the best control. It is stated with its conditions and with its limits. The revelations must come spontaneously, through a large number of mediums unknown to one another, and from different places. A single medium questioning many spirits is expressly not a sufficient guarantee, because one obsessing spirit could present the same content under different names; and mediums of a single centre are not sufficient either, because they may stand under a common influence. The criterion is restricted to what attaches to the principles of the doctrine and does not extend to communications on secondary matters."@en ;
    skos:note "Both exclusions are the founder's own and both are exactly the ones an outside critic would raise. That is worth marking, because it makes the rule better than the practice that followed it: the same author describes elsewhere how the scattered material was gathered and weighed at a single centre, and that centre was himself."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Spiritism .

pmc:Dibbasota a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Göttliches Ohr"@de,
        "Divine ear"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XIII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "Hearing beyond the range of the physical ear."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:Diksa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Initiation"@de,
        "Initiation"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Abhinavagupta, Tantrāloka XV",
        "Mālinīvijayottaratantra VIII" ;
    skos:definition "The rite by which a person is admitted to the practice, set out in the tantra and reproduced with commentary in the Kashmirian exegesis."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmc:EthiopianKeepingOfTheDevilsInRestraint a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die abgehaltenen Teufel und die fehlgehende Waffe"@de,
        "The devils held off and the weapon missing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, prayer I, p. 216" ;
    skos:definition "The effect claimed for the names at the door: that no devil shall draw nigh, that the man shall be smitten neither by the arrows of his adversary nor by the stone hurled at him nor by the spear, and that the angels shall protect him and keep him in safety under their wings."@en ;
    skos:note "Two kinds of harm stand in one sentence, and the second is the one that can be looked at. Whether a devil drew nigh is not an event anybody records; whether a man was struck by a stone or a spear is."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic .

pmc:EthiopianProtectionOfTheSuckling a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Bewahrung des Kindes an der Brust"@de,
        "The preserving of the child at the breast"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, prayer VII, p. 218" ;
    skos:definition "The effect claimed for the worn prayer: preservation and protection from the disease caused by the evil eye of Barya, from coughs, bronchitis, pleurisy, inflammation of the lungs and bowels, diarrhoea, colic, dysentery, the disease caused by worms, fistula, the cracking of the skin, and the evil eye of the sar, of the sorcerer and enchanter, and of a list of named others."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicMondfenster a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Fenster aus Wochentag und Mond"@de,
        "The window of weekday and moon"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 27, 29, 53",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), pp. 17, 18, 32, 41" ;
    skos:definition "The condition that an act fall on a named weekday, before sunrise, and in a named quarter of the moon: Friday before sunrise in the waning moon for the knotting of twigs, the first Friday in the new light for the written slip against the falling sickness, the last Friday in the old light for the letter against rheumatism, Thursday morning before sunrise and in the open air for the binding of a thief."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1820,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicVitriolYieldsStillung a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der Vitriol vertreibt die Zahnschmerzen"@de,
        "The vitriol drives out the toothache"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 46",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 28" ;
    skos:note "The author also states outright that he does not know whether the remedy works on teeth that are not hollow. The limit is his and is kept here rather than widened."@en,
        "The rare part of this claim is not the remedy but the number attached to it. The author states more than sixty applications and says that on one of them it did not help. A transmission that keeps its own failure in the count is uncommon in this graph, and it is what makes the claim examinable rather than merely asserted; what the number does not carry is a base rate, since toothache remits without treatment."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicBlauerVitriol ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1820,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicZahnschmerzStillung .

pmc:Fenxing a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Teilung der Gestalt"@de,
        "The dividing of the form"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 18 (Dizhen), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 18" ;
    skos:definition "Guarding the mysterious One, the chapter says, one thinks one's own body divided into three persons; when the three are seen one adds to them, up to several tens, all as one's own body, and the hiding and showing of them has its own oral instruction. This is what is called the way of dividing the form. The chapter adds that guarding the One together with the cultivating of the bright mirror gives the same, the way of the mirror being able to divide the form into several tens of persons, all alike in dress and face."@en ;
    skos:note "The mysterious One is distinguished from the true One in the same chapter and the distinction bears on the practice: the true One has a surname, a name, a length and a colour of dress, and the mysterious One has none of that but is first sought in the midst of the sun. It is said to be easier to guard than the true One, and to be got after a hundred days of purification and then not more than three or four days of waiting."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BaopuziNeipian ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:FourthJhana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die vierte Vertiefung"@de,
        "The fourth absorption"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.1 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.49 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "The absorption from which the handbook has the supernormal powers proceed, and which it calls their natural plane."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:GenerationStage a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Erzeugungsstufe"@de,
        "Generation stage"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)" ;
    skos:definition "The first of the two stages of the highest tantra, treated before the completion stage in the training."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:TibetanTantric .

pmc:HawaiianChantExactness a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Gesang ohne Atemzug, Fehler und Zögern"@de,
        "The chant rendered without breath, mistake or hesitation"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Martha Warren Beckwith, The Kumulipo: A Hawaiian Creation Chant (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), chapter 7, pp. 35-36" ;
    skos:definition "The delivery a master of song is held to owe: no breath taken before the close of a phrase, no mistake, and no hesitation in pronouncing a word. The three are named together and on the same footing, so that pausing counts as failing rather than as taking care. The manner reported beside it is a tone almost on one note with every word enunciated distinctly."@en ;
    skos:note "Beckwith gives the rule in her own voice and names her authorities for it: Kamakau for the manner of delivery, and Pukui's article of 1949 for the practice. Neither was opened in this run; the article is behind a paywall and the newspaper series was not reachable. The node therefore stands on Beckwith and says so."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HawaiianRitual .

pmc:HekhalotFitness a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Eignung zum Abstieg zur Merkava"@de,
        "Fitness to descend to the Merkavah"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hekhalot Rabbati, chapter 26, trans. Morton Smith, corrected by Gershom Scholem, ed. Don Karr" ;
    skos:altLabel "hashmal"@en ;
    skos:definition "The property the transmission holds to be decidable and to be decided at a particular place. The hashmal is said to divide, among those who descend, between those who were fit to descend and those who were not."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HekhalotRabbati ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hekhalot .

pmc:HermeticRedditioEnergiarum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Was an jeder der sieben Zonen zurückgegeben wird"@de,
        "What is surrendered at each of the seven zones"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Corpus Hermeticum I.25 (trans. G. R. S. Mead)" ;
    skos:definition "The list given for the upward journey through the Harmony: to the first zone the energy of growth and waning; to the second, the device of evils, now de-energized; to the third, the guile of the desires, de-energized; to the fourth, domineering arrogance, de-energized; to the fifth, unholy daring and the rashness of audacity, de-energized; to the sixth, the striving for wealth by evil means, deprived of its aggrandizement; and to the seventh, ensnaring falsehood, de-energized."@en ;
    skos:note "Read against the ophite speeches, this is the complement and not the parallel. There the passer names what he brings and the gate opens; here he names nothing and leaves one thing behind at each stage. One is a liturgy of assertion, the other an inventory of divestment, and the sequence of seven is what they have in common."@en,
        "The repeated de-energized is the load-bearing word and is easy to read past. What is handed over is not the vice but the vice already stripped of its working: the surrender is described as the last step of something that has happened, not as the act that undoes it. Whether the undoing is the dying itself or the life that preceded it, the passage does not say."@en,
        "The zones are not named, numbered to planets, or assigned rulers here, although the same treatise says at I.9 that the seven rulers govern what men call Fate. The pairing of zone with planet is therefore as absent in this witness as the pairing of archon with planet is in the ophite one, and for the same kind of reader it is supplied just as readily."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hermetic .

pmc:HippocratesVulneraLetalia a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Teile, deren schwere Verwundung tödlich ist, ohne Frist genannt"@de,
        "The parts whose severe wounding is deadly, named without a term"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, Aphorisms VI.18, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. II, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849" ;
    skos:definition "Seven parts are named and the claim made of each is the same: a severe wound of the bladder, of the brain, of the heart, of the diaphragm, of the small intestines, of the stomach, and of the liver is deadly. The sentence gives a site and an outcome. It gives no interval, no sign by which the case is to be recognised while the outcome is pending, and no gradation among the seven."@en ;
    skos:note "And the absence is not a limit of the genre, which is why it is worth entering rather than merely noting. The same Section VI counts days twice: a man suddenly seized with pains in the head and laid down speechless dies in seven days unless fever come on, and gouty inflammation subsides in the course of forty days. This corpus can state a term. It states none for a wound."@en,
        "This node is in the holding for what it does not say. Two other transmissions couple a wounded site to a counted interval of death — the needling-prohibition chapter of the Suwen, with six organs and six day-counts, and the marman doctrine of the Susruta, with five classes and their terms — and the agreement between those two is worth nothing until it is known whether coupling a site to an interval is simply what a medical transmission does with a wound. Here is one that does not."@en,
        "What the aphorism would need in order to fail, and it has it: a severe wound of one of these seven parts that is not deadly. Galen, as Adams reports him, says exactly that of four of the seven, and the holding keeps his qualification out of the graph because he was read through the translator and not opened."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AphorismiHippocratis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine .

pmc:HittIssiskemiPat a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kommt das Zeichen nicht, führt die Ausführende es einfach weiter aus"@de,
        "If the sign does not come, the practitioner simply keeps performing it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Paškuwatti, CTH 406, KUB 7.8+KUB 9.27(+)KUB 7.5 Rs. IV 19-21 (critical edition, exemplar A, hethiter.net/: CTH 406, Expl. A, textus 2012-05-07)" ;
    skos:altLabel "iššiškemi-pát"@en ;
    skos:definition "The last paragraph of the tablet: if he does not see her in bodily form, and the deity does not sleep with him in the dream, then — in the practitioner's first person and with the emphatic particle — I just keep performing it."@en ;
    skos:note "The device is the one examined in generalizations/counted-thresholds.ttl, where the three witnesses entered before this run are all Chinese. This is the first witness from a corpus with no plausible contact route to them, and it is the plainest of the four because the continuation is stated by the practitioner about her own practice rather than inferred from a rubric. The protocol is examinations/the-count-that-does-not-fall-due.ttl."@en,
        "This is a stated negative outcome of a stated sign, and it is the only one the five works read in this corpus contain. What makes it worth the whole run is the second half: the negative outcome is named and then disposed of without being allowed to count. The rite has no end at which it could have failed, because the case in which the sign does not come is answered by continuing."@en,
        "What is preserved and what is not, stated exactly, because the claim turns on it. The conditional frame, the words for his body and for the dream and for being with him, and the final verb with its particle are preserved. The two negations are restorations by the editors of the critical edition, supplied from the sense and marked as restorations in their text. A reader who rejects the restorations is left with a conditional paragraph whose protasis repeats the preceding paragraph's sign and whose apodosis says that the practitioner keeps performing — which carries the same consequence by a different route, since a sign already obtained would need no continuation."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Paskuwatti ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual .

pmc:HittKuisDingirKurLukur a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Welche Gottheit des Feindlandes dies auch verursacht hat"@de,
        "Whichever deity of the enemy land has caused this"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Uḫḫamuwa, CTH 410, HT 1 Vs. II 24-25 (critical edition, exemplar A, hethiter.net/: CTH 410, textus 2013-12-09)" ;
    skos:definition "The addressee of the rite, named by description and not by name: whichever deity of the enemy land has brought this death about."@en ;
    skos:note "The operation is addressed to a party the operator cannot identify, and the transmission says so in the address itself rather than treating the gap as an obstacle. That is a standing provision for proceeding without an identification, and this holding carries the device in three other forms at pmc:TheProvisionForNotKnowing. Whether this is a fourth witness of that claim is not decided here and is not asserted, for the reason in the note below."@en,
        "Why it is not entered there in this run. The claim about the provision for not knowing is a generalization, and a generalization may not be enlarged without the counter-search SPEC 16 requires and without the independence of the new witness being settled. The contact route of this tradition is disputed, and one of the six witnesses already standing is the Mesopotamian exorcistic craft, which is a candidate source for the Anatolian technique. Adding this witness would therefore risk counting one claim twice. The Ugaritic strand run beside this one found a second candidate of the same form and the same difficulty; both are recorded as candidates at pmp:HittProvisionSearch."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Uhhamuwa ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual .

pmc:HittMuNininkantes a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die in Bewegung gesetzten Jahre eines Menschen"@de,
        "The years of a person set in motion"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Ḫantitaššu, CTH 395.1, KBo 11.14 Vs. I 1-2 with Rs. IV 24 (critical edition, exemplar A, hethiter.net/: CTH 395.1, textus 2015-06-16)" ;
    skos:altLabel "MU.KAM.ḪI.A ninininkanteš"@en ;
    skos:definition "The condition named in the incipit and repeated in the colophon: for a person, man or woman, the years have been set in motion, and the lord of the word performs the rite for that person."@en ;
    skos:note "The same verb closes the rite in one of the two exemplars, and that is why the condition is worth entering under its own term rather than as shortened life. In exemplar A the bundle that has carried the affliction away is one that nobody sets in motion again; in exemplar B it is one that nobody opens again. The first reading makes the last line of the ritual answer its first in the same word."@en,
        "The verb is the one the editors render as disturbed and which elsewhere means to set in motion or mobilise. Nothing in the text says how the condition would be recognised, and nothing says by whom the diagnosis is made. What the rite is against is therefore stated in the terms of its own vocabulary and not in terms of anything observable."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hantitassu ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual .

pmc:HittZashiyaTuekkissi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das angesagte Zeichen: die Gottheit im Traum in leiblicher Gestalt"@de,
        "The announced sign: the deity seen in bodily form in the dream"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Paškuwatti, CTH 406, KUB 7.8+KUB 9.27(+)KUB 7.5 Rs. IV 1-10 (critical edition, exemplar A, hethiter.net/: CTH 406, Expl. A, textus 2012-05-07)" ;
    skos:altLabel "zašḫiya tuekkišši"@en ;
    skos:definition "The sign the rite announces in advance and then looks for: the ritual patron sleeps, and if he sees the deity in bodily form in the dream, and she comes to him, and she sleeps with him; and while the practitioner invokes her for three days, he keeps telling whatever dreams he sees, whether the deity shows him her face and whether she sleeps with him."@en ;
    skos:note "A sign of success in the strict sense, and one of the clearest in this holding, because the text states both the content of the sign and the procedure for collecting it: the patient reports every dream of the three nights and the practitioner hears them. It is also announced before the nights begin, which is the condition under which a reported sign tells nothing about the rite. The holding counts signs of success separately from conditions of failure for this reason, and this work is the one case where the same tablet carries both."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Paskuwatti ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual .

pmc:HolinTakeItYourPurposeWillNotSucceed a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Nimm, deine Absicht gelingt dir doch nicht: die Parallele gelesen, und sie bestreitet die Wirksamkeit nicht"@de,
        "Take it, your purpose will not succeed: the parallel read, and it is not a denial of efficacy"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Holin 7b, R. Hanina on Deuteronomy 4:35 and the woman who took earth from beneath his feet, with the objection from R. Johanan and the answer from his merits, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. VIII (Leipzig: Otto Harrassowitz, 1909), p. 822" ;
    skos:definition "The same case as at Sanhedrin 67b in the other wording. R. Hanina applies the verse that there is none beside him to sorcery as well; a woman sets about taking earth from under his feet in order to bewitch him; and here he says to her, take it, your purpose will not succeed, because it is written that there is none beside him. Sanhedrin has him say, if you can, then do it. The objection from R. Johanan's saying that the practitioners are so named because they defy the college above stands in this tractate too, and so does the answer: it was different with R. Hanina, whose merits were great."@en ;
    skos:note "A pointer the two wordings supply and neither tractate notices. Goldschmidt's note at this place refers the explanation of the word for the practitioners to the seventh volume, page 286, note 527, which is the Sanhedrin passage the first file worked from. The cross-reference is the translator's and is recorded here because it is the only thing in either place that treats the two as one discussion."@en,
        "This node exists because it refutes the conjecture that was entered in its place. The note at pmc:SanhedrinNothingBesideHimEvenInSorcery reported from the secondary literature that the parallel here falls flatter and that this would be a denial of efficacy rather than a personal exception. The first half is right and the second is wrong, and the wording shows why: the flatter wording is followed by the same objection and the same resolution, so the personal exception is not a feature of the Sanhedrin wording but of the case. The lease that asked for this reading guessed the consequence and the guess did not survive the page. That is the use of the second stage of a search, and it is the third time this holding has recorded it; the first stands in prima-materia#76."@en,
        "What the difference in wording does change, and it is worth more than what it does not. If you can, then do it is an invitation that no outcome could disappoint, because a failure shows the speaker right and a success was invited. Take it, your purpose will not succeed is a prediction about a particular operation by a particular woman, and a bewitching that then took effect would have contradicted the man who said it. This tractate therefore carries, in one clause, the only thing in this transmission that looks like a stated condition of failure of somebody else's operation - and the discussion immediately covers the general claim with the merits of the man, so that nothing turns on whether the prediction held."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliHolin ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmc:HolinWhiteMuleClaimNarrowedTwice a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Nie fragte mich jemand ueber die Verwundung durch ein weisses Maultier und blieb am Leben: die Behauptung zweimal unter Gegenbeobachtung eingeschraenkt"@de,
        "Nobody ever asked me about a white mule's wound and lived: the claim narrowed twice under counter-observation"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Holin 7b, R. Jehosua b. Levi on the name of the mules and R. Hanina on the wound, with the two objections and the two answers, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. VIII (Leipzig: Otto Harrassowitz, 1909), p. 822" ;
    skos:definition "The most compact instance in this holding of a claim being restricted by its own transmission in answer to what people see, and it happens twice in four lines. R. Hanina says that nobody in his life ever consulted him about a wound from a white mule and stayed alive. The discussion objects: but we see that some do stay alive. The answer is to read the saying as meaning, and is healed. The discussion objects again: but we see that these heal too. The answer is that the saying speaks of mules whose feet are white at the ends. After the second narrowing the claim covers a class the objector cannot enumerate and excludes no case he can point to."@en ;
    skos:note "The shape of the two narrowings is not the same and the difference is the useful part. The first changes what the claim asserts - from dying to not healing - and so is a reinterpretation of the words. The second changes what the claim is about - from white mules to mules with white feet-ends - and so is a restriction of the subject. Both are introduced by the same formula, we see however that, and neither is marked in the text as a retreat. A later protocol counting immunising moves will need both kinds and this is the cheapest place in the holding to take them from."@en,
        "What was not found on the same page, and it matters for the reading. Nothing says how many cases R. Hanina had seen, nothing fixes a term after which a wound counts as healed, and nothing says who decides whether a mule's feet are white at the ends. The claim therefore has no count, no term and no adjudicator, and it was stated as an exceptionless autobiographical report. The absence is the compiler's observation and is not in the passage."@en,
        "Why this is a find and not a curiosity. The holding already carries this construction described from the outside, by a first-century critic who put a counter-example to a practitioner three times and was answered three times with a fresh condition; that stands at pmc:BiansuiDispute, and the note there says in as many words that a critic reporting the move is not a transmission supplying it. Here the transmission supplies it, in its own voice, in its own canonical text, with the counter-observation raised by the editorial discussion itself rather than by an opponent. The gap the holding named is closed by this passage."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliHolin ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmc:IdentifyingTakenForItsOpposite a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "Der aufzulösende Zustand wird für den erstrebten gehalten"@de,
        "The state to be undone is mistaken for the state to be sought"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1949" ;
    dcterms:source "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching (London 1950), in the passage on identifying and the names given to it" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that in identifying a person is absorbed into what occupies them and becomes a thing, and that those who observe this state in themselves regularly take it for a merit, calling it enthusiasm, zeal, passion, spontaneity or inspiration, and believing that good work is possible only in it."@en ;
    skos:note "It stands beside the self-caution recorded at pmc:Panchatattva, where a text prescribes a rite and warns in the same breath that men of the age will take it up for pleasure. Two traditions with no route between them both place the danger inside the practitioner's own approval. Still not a convergence, which would assert that the concepts correspond; the pattern is now stated as a claim that can fail, at pmc:SelfWarningStandsInsideThePrescription."@en,
        "Recorded as a caution that names its own misrecognition, which is the form worth collecting. The warning is not that the state is unpleasant — it is that the state is valued, under names that make it sound like the goal. A caution of this shape cannot be tested by asking practitioners how they feel, since the claim predicts they will report it favourably."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:circulatesAs "An observation of the school, reported as long known." ;
    pm:originatedBy "G. I. Gurdjieff, in the record of P. D. Ouspensky" .

pmc:ImbasForosnaiFaillsiugud a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Es erschließt alles, was der Dichter offenbaren will"@de,
        "It discovers everything the poet wishes to manifest"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Sanas Cormaic s.v. Imbas forosnai, in Stokes, Three Irish Glossaries (London 1862), p. 25, and in Cormac's Glossary, trans. O'Donovan (Calcutta 1868), p. 94" ;
    skos:definition "What the glossary states the procedure is for, in the sentence that opens the entry: it discovers everything which the poet likes and which he desires to manifest."@en ;
    skos:note "An unrestricted object. The entry names no class of question the procedure cannot answer, no condition on what may be asked, and no case in which the answer would be withheld. What bounds it in the entry is the term and the watching, not the subject matter."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InsularCeltic .

pmc:InuitHiddenThings a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die verborgenen Dinge"@de,
        "Hidden things"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), pp. 19, 24" ;
    skos:definition "What is not open to ordinary sight: the state of the sick, the whereabouts of what is lost, the presence of the dead among the living. The tradition treats this as a domain that some persons perceive and that others are ill advised to form opinions about."@en ;
    skos:note "Relevant to prima-materia#127, which asks whether a perception of the distant, hidden or concealed is attested outside the spheres already held. The passage here attests the domain and one case of its perception; it does not attest a procedure for acquiring it, and that part is not in the portion read."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InuitAngakkuq .

pmc:InuitReturnAfterDeath a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Wiederaufleben nach dem Tod"@de,
        "Coming to life again after death"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 28" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity ascribed to certain persons of coming to life again after death and returning to their place among people, held to belong to those over whom particular words were spoken at their birth."@en ;
    skos:note "The narrative gives the being a name in a phonetic transcription that the digitised text renders unreliably; the spelling is therefore not carried into this node, and the term is to be taken from a clean copy of the page before it is asserted anywhere. The claim itself does not depend on the spelling."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InuitAngakkuq .

pmc:IuratusConcessusVelRepulsa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Gewährung oder Abweisung, offenbart in der folgenden Nacht"@de,
        "The grant or the refusal, revealed in the night following"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber iuratus Honorii, cap. L (Royal MS 17 A. xlii, fol. 44v; Latin of Sloane MS 3854 as given in Peterson's edition: tunc in nocte sequenti in somnis revelabitur tibi per Angelum concessus vel repulsa)" ;
    skos:definition "The outcome the first purification is said to produce: after the third of the three days, when the operator has prayed three times, it is revealed to him in the night following, by an angel and in sleep, whether he will obtain his petition or not."@en ;
    skos:note "Concessus vel repulsa, the grant or the refusal. This is the rarest shape in this holding: a stated outcome that is binary, that falls due on a named night, and that can come out against the operator. Nothing is left to the operator's reading of his own state; an angel says yes or no."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberIuratusHonorii ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:IuratusInsania a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Danach für immer wahnsinnig"@de,
        "Mad ever after"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber iuratus Honorii, cap. CI (Royal MS 17 A. xlii, fol. 64v), ed. Peterson; Latin of Sloane MS 3854 in Boudet's edition, section 15, at p. 880, with the apparatus of three manuscripts: Sed cavendum est operanti ne sit in peccato mortali, quia de cetero esset insanus" ;
    skos:definition "The outcome the book states for the operator who works the vision while in deadly sin: he shall be mad ever after. The mechanism is given with it. The understanding proceeds from that part of the soul which intends to see the one in whom it delights, and when through the impediment of sin it does not see him with delight, it will thereafter think of nothing else, so that as regards his body there is in him no human rationality. The text then appeals to observation: and likewise we see it in many who are made witless, in that the understanding proceeding from the soul does not reach what it wanted."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the condition of failure prima-materia#640 reported absent across seven opened works, and it is stronger than the shape that issue was looking for. It names the breach, it names the outcome, the outcome is observable by a third party, and the text claims the observation has been made in more than one case. A procedure whose misuse is said to leave a visible mark on the operator is examinable in a way that an unfulfilled promise is not."@en,
        "What it does not do, and the distinction decides whether it can carry a falsifier. The madness is said to follow the breach of a condition, not the failure of the rite; it tells a reader what happens to the wrong operator and not what happens to the right one who gets nothing. The work carries no clause of the second kind. A search of both witnesses for a clause that lays any failure on the art, the book or the procedure returned nothing: every failure clause in this work lands on the operator, as pmp:IuratusFailureLocusSearch records."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberIuratusHonorii ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:JainaBrahmacaryasamadhiPhala a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Was das Hören und Verstehen der zehn bringen soll"@de,
        "What hearing and understanding the ten is said to bring"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XVI, opening formula (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:definition "The outcome the lecture states twice, in its opening and in its restatement of the question: by hearing and understanding these ten conditions the monks will reach a high degree of self-discipline, of saṃvara and of contemplation, will be well protected, will guard their senses, will guard their chastity, and will thus never be remiss in the attendance on their religious duties."@en ;
    skos:note "Stated of the hearing and understanding and not of the keeping, which is unusual enough to keep as it stands rather than to smooth. The lecture then spends its whole length on the keeping."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaCikitsavidya a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Heilkünste, von denen sich der wahre Mönch enthält"@de,
        "The healing arts the true monk abstains from"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XV.8 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:definition "The list that follows in the next verse of the same lecture: spells, roots, every kind of medical treatment, emetics, purgatives, fumigation, anointing of the eye, and bathing, together with the patient's lamentation and his consolation. He who abstains from all these things is a true monk."@en ;
    skos:note "The last two items are not treatments. Attending to the patient's lamentation and consoling him stand in the same prohibition as the emetic and the spell, which means the rule is against the whole office of the healer and not against the efficacy of any particular means."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaKevalaprapti a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die auf die Muhūrta datierte Erlangung"@de,
        "The attainment dated to the muhūrta"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Kalpa Sūtra 120, with the parallel at Ācārāṅga Sūtra II.15.25 (Jacobi, SBE 22, Oxford 1884; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 1964)" ;
    skos:definition "The event the Kalpa Sūtra reports with every circumstance it has: during the thirteenth year, in the second month of summer, in the fourth fortnight, the light fortnight of Vaiśākha, on its tenth day called Suvrata, in the muhūrta called Vijaya, outside the town Jṛmbhikagrāma on the bank of the river Ṛjupālikā, not far from an old temple, in the field of the householder Sāmāga, under a Sāl tree, while the moon was in conjunction with the asterism Uttaraphalgunī, when the shadow had turned towards the east and the first wake was over, in a squatting position with joined heels, exposing himself to the heat of the sun, after fasting two and a half days without drinking water, being engaged in deep meditation, he reached the highest knowledge and intuition, called Kevala."@en ;
    skos:note "The most fully specified single attainment in what was read, and the specification is of everything except the man. Year, month, fortnight, day, muhūrta, asterism, direction of the shadow, watch of the day, place, tree, posture, exposure and the length of the immediately preceding fast are all named; nothing is said of any perceptible mark by which the attainment announced itself, and the account gives none afterwards either."@en,
        "The two and a half days without water are the one operative condition in the sentence that a later reader could repeat. Everything else in it is a coordinate."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaNimittavidya a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Wahrsagekünste, von denen der wahre Mönch nicht lebt"@de,
        "The divinatory arts the true monk does not live on"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XV.7 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:definition "The list the fifteenth lecture gives, in a verse whose subject is what a monk must not profess or live on: divination from cuts and shreds, from sounds on the earth or in the air, from dreams, from diagrams, from sticks, from the properties of buildings, from changes in the body, and from the meaning of the cries of animals."@en ;
    skos:note "The translator remarks on what the list does not contain: there is no astrology in it, and he infers that Greek nativity had not yet risen to importance where and when the lecture was composed. The inference is his; the absence is the text's and is entered as such, because a catalogue of forbidden arts is also a catalogue of the arts that were there to forbid."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaPapavidyaphala a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Geburt unter den Asuras, danach taub und stumm"@de,
        "Born among the Asuras, and then deaf and dumb"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Sūtrakṛtāṅga Sūtra II.2.27 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:definition "The outcome the same passage states for the men who live by those sciences: after having died at their allotted time they will be born in some places inhabited by Asuras and evildoers, and when they are released therefrom they will again be born deaf and dumb, or night-blind."@en ;
    skos:note "A stated consequence in two stages, the second of them a named bodily condition. It is the clearest case in this file of a transmission naming what a practice produces, and what it names is a penalty. Held beside the holding's other yields for the sake of the comparison: the same arts that are promised elsewhere are here costed."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaSanmasaTejolesyaprapti a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Nach sechs Monaten erlangte er die Kraft"@de,
        "After six months he acquired the power"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Viyāhapannatti (Bhagavaī) XV, Calcutta edition p. 1222" ;
    skos:definition "The term the book puts on the acquisition. Having separated from Mahāvīra, Gosāla followed the course of asceticism previously explained to him and, after six months, succeeded in acquiring the power. Leumann's extract has the same sentence: after the lapse of six months he himself acquired magical power by means of the austere discipline."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the shape the testability-driven entrance of SPEC 14 looks for and the shape the earlier search through the four works of the Jacobi volumes reported as absent from them: a practice, a stated period, and a result that could have failed to arrive. It is here in a fifth work, and the gap that pmp:JainaStatedTermSearch exhibited is filled from outside the corpus that exhibited it."@en,
        "Two translators independently rendering the same Prakrit give the same period, which is as much corroboration as a reading in second hand can have. It is not corroboration of the event."@en,
        "What it is not. The six months are narrated of one man on one occasion and are not laid down as a rule for anyone else; the book does not say that six months is what the discipline takes, nor what is to be concluded if the period passes and nothing comes. The difference between a narrated interval and a prescribed term is the same difference this holding records at the two and a half days of the Kalpa Sūtra, and it is why no falsifying condition is formed from this passage."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "A. F. Rudolf Hoernle, The Uvāsagadasāo, vol. II, Appendix I (Bibliotheca Indica, Calcutta 1888), p. 4; and Ernst Leumann, Appendix I: Extracts from Bhagavatī XV, in W. W. Rockhill, The Life of the Buddha (London: Trubner 1884), p. 251" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JainaTribhavaAvadhi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Vollendung in höchstens drei Geburten"@de,
        "Perfection within three births at the most"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX.1 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:definition "The outcome the first item states, and it states it as a bound: by the purity of faith some will reach perfection after one birth, and nobody who has got this purity will be born more than thrice before he reaches perfection."@en ;
    skos:note "The one claim in the lecture that a count could contradict, and the only bound anywhere in what was read that is stated as a maximum rather than as a promise. It is not open to a count that anybody could carry out, because the unit is a birth; the shape is nevertheless the shape of a falsifiable statement, and it is worth holding as the case where a transmission put an upper limit on its own remedy."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JeuTransitusOmniumThesaurorum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Alle Örter stieben auf einmal davon, vom ersten bis zum letzten"@de,
        "All the places scatter at once, from the first of them to the last"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Das erste Buch Jeû, Handschriftenseiten 46-47, deutsche Übersetzung in Carl Schmidt, Gnostische Schriften in koptischer Sprache aus dem Codex Brucianus, Texte und Untersuchungen VIII 1-2, Leipzig 1892, S. 185-186" ;
    skos:definition "What the one name is said to effect: when you say it, all the places in the treasuries scatter, from the first of them to the last, as far as the treasury of the true God; the watchers and the ranks and the curtains scatter; and in the moment in which the name and the words and the mystery are said and the speaker turns to the four corners, the watchers of the gates and the ranks of the treasuries and their curtains, which are drawn before these fathers, all scatter from within, out of all of them, from the first of them to the last."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu .

pmc:JindanCapacities a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Was die Elixiere verleihen sollen"@de,
        "What the elixirs are said to confer"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 4 (Jindan), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 4" ;
    skos:definition "A catalogue in the enumerated form, and the items are of three kinds. Deathlessness: the taker does not age and does not die, his span becomes endless and ends with heaven and earth, and he rides the clouds and drives dragons up and down the Great Clarity. Powers over the world: walking on water with the elixir smeared under the feet; the destruction of the three corpses and the nine worms; the healing of the hundred illnesses; warding off the five weapons, and off thieves, tigers and wolves; the attendance of jade women and of the spirits of mountains and rivers, seen in human shape; the raising of a man dead not more than three days, by washing him with the blue elixir in water and putting a knife-point of it in his mouth; and a man of ninety fathering children. Transmutation: a knife-point of the elixir with a catty of mercury makes silver, and with the fire it makes gold, and vessels made of that gold cause whoever eats from them to live long."@en ;
    skos:note "The catalogue is what the registration of this transmission was made for: it is an explicit list of attainments in the same enumerated form as the siddhi lists of the Indic material this collection holds, compiled without access to them. Whether the resemblance is a route or a form is not decided here and is not to be decided by resemblance."@en,
        "The three kinds are not equally reachable by an examination, and the difference is in the record rather than smoothed away. The raising of the dead and the fathering of children at ninety are outcomes a witness could report; the walking on water is a public event; the endless span and the attendance of jade women are not, in this form. The transmutation of mercury into silver is the only item on the list that names a material operation with a visible product, and the chapter uses exactly that item as its assay."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Waidan .

pmc:KnowledgeOfSeedStarCharacteristics a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kenntnis der Merkmale des Same-Sterns"@de,
        "Knowledge of the characteristics of the seed-star"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 282 and 284" ;
    skos:definition "The knowledge of what the seed-star is made of, how heavy it is and how it moves, said in the account to have been unavailable to men while the star's existence was known but its characteristics unrevealed."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Dogon .

pmc:Kokabel a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Kokabel"@de,
        "Kokabel"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "A Watcher whose teaching concerns the stars; the two recensions differ on whether it is the constellations or the signs of the stars."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:nameForm "Kôkabêl" ;
    pm:nameRole "watcher" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:Kundalini a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kuṇḍalinī"@de,
        "Kuṇḍalinī"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pūrṇānanda, Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa (1577)" ;
    skos:definition "A power held to rest coiled at the base of the subtle body and to rise through it when roused."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmc:MagnetismeAutoDiagnostic a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Somnambule erkennt den Sitz seiner eigenen Krankheit"@de,
        "The somnambulist perceives the seat of his own disease"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. V, pp. 74, 81 and 84-85" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity on which the practice turns: the somnambulist perceives the seat of his own disorder, sees the lesion in one part, states the nature, cause and consequences of his complaint, names the crises that will bring on the cure, prescribes the remedial means and the hour at which he is to be operated upon, and describes the effects his prescriptions will produce."@en ;
    skos:note "And the failure mode is named with it: where several diseases are present the somnambulist fixes on the one that gives him most uneasiness and may prescribe for it something injurious to another. One worked case is given, of a patient with affected lungs and an impaired stomach who was talked out of her own prescription and thanked the operator a fortnight later. So the transmission holds, of the faculty it rests on, that its bearer is to be overruled."@en,
        "The boundary of the capacity is stated and it is a real one: it is rare that he sees the situation, form and tissue of his organs, especially of those not affected, so anatomical questions yield vague and perhaps erroneous views. The source draws the line exactly where it is useful — he will not mistake in foretelling a crisis, pointing out a remedy or describing its effects, but could easily give explanations that would be ridiculous in proportion to the interest shown in hearing them."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:MandaeanNomenEtSignum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Übergib uns deinen Namen und dein Zeichen"@de,
        "Hand over thy name and thy sign"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ginzā, right side, book 5, piece 3 (text 181,8-184,8), in the German of Mark Lidzbarski, Ginzā (Göttingen 1925), pp. 184-186" ;
    skos:definition "What is demanded at each watch-house and what is given. The keepers say: thou man, hand over to us thy name and thy sign, which thou hast fetched out of the waves of the water, out of the treasures of radiance and out of the great baptism of light. The soul names its name and its sign, and the keeper bows deeply and lets it pass."@en ;
    skos:note "What is handed over is not the keeper's name but the soul's own, and it is identified in the text by where it came from: the baptism. The pass at every station is therefore a rite performed while alive, and the itinerary is a statement about the efficacy of that rite made in the form of a journey."@en,
        "Worth setting beside the Egyptian gate material this holding already carries, and not by way of a convergence, which this run did not establish and which the disputed contact route would not support in any case. The difference is the point: there the traveller must know the names of the gatekeepers, here he must produce his own. The first is knowledge of a text and can be lost by forgetting; the second is a state conferred by a ceremony and, as pmc:MandaeanAblatioSacramentorum shows, can be taken away."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GinzaRabba ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean .

pmc:MaoriExactRecitation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wiederholung ohne Fehler und ohne Zögern"@de,
        "Repetition without error or hesitation"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 11" ;
    skos:definition "The rendering of an invocation in which neither a mistake nor a pause occurs. The tradition sets the two on the same footing: hesitating is treated as failing, not as taking care."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Maori .

pmc:MayaAvertingOfForetoldIlls a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Befreiung von den angekündigten Übeln"@de,
        "Being freed from the ills indicated as to come"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Diego de Landa, Yucatan Before and After the Conquest, translated by William Gates (Baltimore: The Maya Society, publication 20, 1937), section XXXVII, pp. 65-66" ;
    skos:definition "The effect claimed for the procedure. The witness reports that the revels and the sacrifices were held to be very acceptable to the idols and remedial for freeing them from the ills indicated as to come."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MayaRitual .

pmc:MideDistantHearing a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Hören dessen, was in großer Ferne geschieht"@de,
        "Hearing what happens at a great distance"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 168" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity to hear what is transpiring at a great distance, ascribed to a priest of the second degree and marked in the chart by lines drawn from the ears."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin .

pmc:MideDistantTouch a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Berühren in der Ferne, zum Guten wie zum Bösen"@de,
        "Touching at a distance for good or ill"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 168" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity to touch friends and enemies at a distance, however remote, for good or for evil, marked in the chart by lines drawn from the hands; alongside it the ability to traverse all space, marked by lines from the feet."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin .

pmc:MideSoulSeizing a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Herausrufen des Schattens und sein Festhalten"@de,
        "Calling forth the shadow and holding it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 170" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity ascribed to a priest of the fourth degree to call forth the shadow, that is the soul, and to retain it within his grasp at pleasure."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin .

pmc:MideThoughtReading a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Lesen der Gedanken und Absichten anderer"@de,
        "Reading the thoughts and intentions of others"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 170" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity ascribed to a priest of the fourth degree, marked in the chart by the shell placed at the top of the head."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin .

pmc:MoradasSenalesHablas a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die drei Zeichen, an denen eine Sprache als von Gott erkannt wird"@de,
        "The three signs by which a locution is known to be from God"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Teresa de Jesus, Las Moradas sextas 3 (Las moradas, ed. Tomas Navarro Tomas, Madrid: Ediciones de La Lectura, 1910, Clasicos castellanos III, pp. 168-175), read in the Spanish against the English of the Benedictines of Stanbrook rev. Zimmerman, 2nd edition 1912, Sixth Mansions chapter III, and the French of Marcelle Auclair" ;
    skos:altLabel "las mas ciertas senales que se pueden tener"@en ;
    skos:definition "The signs the second founder of the same reform gives as the most certain that can be had. The first and truest is the power and lordship the words bring with them, which is in speaking and in working: a soul in interior tribulation and darkness and dryness is told only do not grieve, and is left quiet and without grief and with great light, so that it seems to her that all the world and all the learned men gathered to give her reasons could not have taken that affliction from her. The second is a great quietness left in the soul, with devout and peaceful recollection and a disposition to the praises of God. The third is that these words do not pass from the memory for a very long time and some never, unlike the words we hear from men, and that there remains from them a very great certainty."@en ;
    skos:note "The same chapter sets limits that are not in dispute and are recorded so that the dispute is not read more widely than it is. Do not think that even if they are of God you will be the better for it, since he spoke much to the Pharisees; make no more account of any that does not go very much according to Scripture than if you had heard it from the devil itself; and whatever is grave, or is to be put into act, is never to be done or even entertained without the opinion of a learned, careful confessor who is a servant of God, however clearly it seems to be of God."@en,
        "The third sign is stated with its application to predictions, and that is the point at which it meets the other founder: ni tampoco, si son en cosas por venir, las creemos como a estas, que queda una certidumbre grandisima, nor, if they concern things to come, do we believe those as we believe these, from which there remains a most great certainty. The text also records the negative case: if they are of the imagination none of these signs is there, neither certainty nor peace nor interior joy."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MoradasDelCastilloInterior ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinMysticism .

pmc:NadiPurification a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Reinigung der Kanäle"@de,
        "Purification of the channels"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.19-20 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.6 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "The clearing of impurity from the channels, at which the breath practice is said to aim, and whose accomplishment the text says shows outwardly in the body."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:NavajoSongExactness a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Genauigkeit der gesungenen Worte"@de,
        "Exactness of the sung words"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Washington Matthews, Navaho Legends, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society V (Boston and New York: Houghton, Mifflin, 1897), section 41, p. 24" ;
    skos:definition "The requirement that the songs be known in a most exact manner, down to the syllable, no important mistake being tolerated."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NavajoCeremonial .

pmc:NeidanFeidaofuren a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Nicht der Weg hat den Menschen im Stich gelassen"@de,
        "It is not that the Way has failed the man"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhong-Lü chuandao ji, Lun zhengyan 18, in the Chongkan Daozang jiyao recension (JY 149), juan 3, fol. 54b-55a" ;
    skos:definition "The chapter on the signs of verification opens by putting the question of the practitioner who has held to the work and seen no result, and answers it twice in the same form. If a man toils with a resolute will and in the end sees no merit, it is not that the Way has failed him: it is that he did not follow an enlightened master, so that what he received was not the Law. If a man practises according to the Law and in the end sees no merit, it is not that the Way has failed him: it is that he did not know the times, and so came to nothing. Where a man has met an enlightened master, obtained the Law, practises the great method and keeps to the times, the chapter asks what cause there could then be for the signs not to appear."@en ;
    skos:note "The nearest thing to it in this holding is pmc:ShangqingGuijiuShenling, where a transmission calls it foolish to blame the spirits for a self-inflicted illness. That one moves the blame from the gods back to the man; this one moves it from the method back to the man, which is the harder and the more consequential move, and it is the only place in this holding where a transmission makes it in so many words."@en,
        "The second of the two defects binds this node to the dispute below. Not knowing the times is given as a sufficient cause of a null result, and the corpus does not agree with itself about what the times are: the chapter that states this rule reads them off the year, the month and the hundred marks of the day, while the Jindan sizi holds that the fire phases use no hour at all. A practitioner told that his failure lies in the times has been told something the transmission cannot make precise."@en,
        "This is the rule of assignment stated as a rule, and stated at the one place where it has to be: at the head of the chapter that lists what success looks like, immediately after the practitioner has asked what happens if it does not come. The transmission names the possibility that the Way has failed the man, names it twice, and denies it twice, offering in its place two defects located in the man — his choice of teacher and his knowledge of the times."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Neidan .

pmc:NeidanHuohouBuyongshi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Feuerphasen brauchen keine Stunde"@de,
        "The fire phases use no hour"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhang Boduan (ascr.), Jindan sizi, verse 13, with Huang Ziru's commentary (DZ 1081), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, fol. 9a" ;
    skos:definition "The thirteenth quatrain states it in four lines: the fire phases use no hour, the winter solstice is not at zi, and as for the method of the bathing, the mao and you hours are an empty likeness. The commentary spells it out: in general the fire phases are not the zi hour and the winter solstice, nor the wu hour and the summer solstice; and the bathing is not the mao hour and the spring equinox, nor the you hour and the autumn equinox. Once a man has started the fire on the circuit within his own body, the withdrawing and adding and the bathing are there of themselves, and one may not be bound to the four seasons."@en ;
    skos:note "Its bearing on pmc:AdmissibilityBoundToTheCalendar is stated at that node and not claimed here. This is not a transmission that binds an operation to a calendar and locates the failure in the time; it is a transmission that refuses the binding, in a stream where the binding is otherwise general, and it puts the condition in the practitioner instead."@en,
        "The doctrine has the preface behind it and is not an isolated aphorism. The preface has already said that whoever knows the one aperture has the winter solstice in it, the medicine in it, the fire phases in it and the bathing in it; the quatrain is the verse form of that sentence. The condition has been moved from the calendar into the body, and with it the answer to the question of when."@en,
        "The wording of the received text is worth recording because it is contested in the literature. The Hanfenlou facsimile of DZ 1081 reads that the fire phases do not use the hour and that the winter solstice is not at zi; the variant that has them not requiring the hour and asks whether the solstice is at zi at all is not what this recension prints. The reading was checked at the wording for this reason."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Neidan .

pmc:NeidanShengtaiCapacity a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der heilige Embryo"@de,
        "The sage embryo"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhang Boduan (ascr.), Jindan sizi, preface and verse 20, with Huang Ziru's commentary (DZ 1081), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, fol. 3b and fol. 11b" ;
    skos:definition "A body said to form within the practitioner as the work proceeds, described in the preface as flesh and bone growing clear, the skin becoming transparent, substance arising where there was none, and the embryo-breath congealing until the infant shows its form; and in the twentieth quatrain as a child born after one year who rides the crane out through the upper cinnabar field."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Neidan .

pmc:NeidanZhengyan a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Zeichen der Bewährung, der Reihe nach"@de,
        "The signs of verification, in order"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhong-Lü chuandao ji, Lun zhengyan 18, in the Chongkan Daozang jiyao recension (JY 149), juan 3, fol. 55b-57a" ;
    skos:definition "An ordered sequence of signs said to follow the work, each introduced as coming next after the one before. Lewd conduct ceases and the outward observances are kept; the metal essence fills and the mind clears; the fluid of the heart rises and there is a sweet taste in the mouth; yin and yang strike together and a sound of wind and thunder is heard in the belly; the cloud-souls and white-souls are unsettled and dreams are full of alarm; small ailments of the six receptacles and the four limbs heal without treatment; the cinnabar field warms of itself at night and the face is clear by day; in a dark room the eyes show a light of their own; in dreams one is bold, nothing can harm one, and one comes back carrying an infant; the golden bar and jade lock are sealed and there is no more emission in sleep; a single clap of thunder, the joints connect, and one starts awake bathed in sweat; the fluid is decocted to a curd; the numinous liquid becomes a paste and one comes to shun the smell of flesh; the bones lighten, the yang spirit changes, the step outruns a galloping horse; one meets things without desire; the true breath entering a thing can cure another's illness; inner observation is bright and no longer dim; the pupils are like lacquer, the lined face fills out and dark hair grows again; the true breath fills so that one seems always sated, eats little, drinks without measure and is never drunk; the body has a lustre, a strange fragrance stays about the mouth and nose; one sees a fine hair at a hundred paces; old scars and blemishes vanish of themselves, and there is no more spittle, tears or sweat; the embryo is complete and food is left off; the nine worms below and the three corpses above die; there are no more dreams and no difference between night and day; the frame does not feel cold or heat; life and death cannot reach one; the merit is full and the hidden reckoning answers, and coming events can be known beforehand; the embryo-immortal appears as a body outside the body; the breath is pure yang and can dry mercury outside the body by being breathed on it; an auspicious light arises in the sleeping room; music is heard in stillness; ordinary men, however rich, smell rank at close quarters; a golden colour shows through the bones; the spirits come of themselves to pay court and go where they are bidden; purple mist fills the eyes and a golden light covers the body; the body turns into a fire dragon and flies, which is the shedding of the frame; and thereafter coloured clouds, strange flowers falling as rain, a jade maiden descending with a heavenly writ, and the return to Penglai."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as signs and counted as signs, kept apart from the failure side above, which is what SPEC §15 requires and which this corpus makes easy by doing it itself: the difficulties and the demons are chapter seventeen, the signs are chapter eighteen, and the practitioner's question at the head of eighteen is what joins them."@en,
        "The list also contains its own refutation of the idea that these are private experiences. Drying mercury outside the body by breathing on it, curing another's illness with the breath, spirits coming when bidden, and a golden colour showing through the bones are all public; and the last item but one has the practitioner's body turn into a fire dragon in front of whoever is there."@en,
        "The sequence is announced to the practitioner in advance and in order, which is precisely the condition under which a sign is reported because it was expected. Two items in it are of another kind and are worth separating out, because a bystander could check them: seeing a fine hair at a hundred paces, and the disappearance of old scars. The protocol built on the first stands in examinations/neidan-signs.ttl."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Neidan .

pmc:NippurCommissionFromTheFiveDeities a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Beauftragung des Beschwörers durch fünf genannte Gottheiten"@de,
        "The exorcist's commission from five named deities"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 36: 3-4, with introduction §§ 9 and 13, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 47, 100 and 237)" ;
    skos:definition "The authority one pagan bowl of this collection presents before it acts: the lord Sames the Sun has charged me against thee, Sin the Moon has sent me, Bel has commanded me, Nannai has said to me, and Nirig has given me power to go against the evil spirit."@en ;
    skos:note "The one place in this corpus where the operator states where his power comes from in the form of a received commission rather than of a borrowed name. The editor takes it for one of the latest survivals of the old Babylonian priestly formula and prints the parallels from the Utukku and Maqlû series beside it; those works were not opened in this run and are not cited here."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurJoshuaBarPerahia a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Rabbi Joshua bar Perahia, der große Heiler"@de,
        "Rabbi Joshua bar Perahia, the great healer"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, texts 8: 6-12, 9: 2, 17: 8-12, 32: 4-5 and 34: 2, with introduction § 9, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 46, 153-155, 160, 189-191, 224 and 229)" ;
    skos:definition "Six bowls of this collection work in his name, three in the Rabbinic script and three in the Syriac, where he is Rab Jesus bar Perahia. The texts say that he sent the ban against the liliths, that so has he spoken to them, that he sat and wrote them a ban-writ which is for all time, and that the binding is sealed with the signet of El Shaddai and with the signet of the house of Joshua bar Perahia. One bowl has the sorcerer call himself a cousin of his, and another refers to his house, that is to his school."@en ;
    skos:note "The person named is one of the Zugoth or Pairs who handed down the tradition of the Law from the Great Synagogue, of the early first century before this era. Whether the magical tradition attached to him is authentic the editor calls dubious, and takes the case as an instance of magic appealing to ancient masters and using their names as though their powers went with them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:nameForm "Joshua bar Perahia",
        "Rab Jesus bar Perahia" ;
    pm:nameRole "the master in whose name the charm is worked: a great healer, who sent the ban against the liliths, who sat and wrote the ban-writ against the demons, and whose signet and whose house seal the operation" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurOperatorNamesHisOwnWork a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wie diese Kunst ihr eigenes Werk nennt und wie nicht"@de,
        "What this craft calls its own work, and what it will not call it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, introduction § 11, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 51-53)" ;
    skos:definition "The craft distinguishes the physical operation from the spoken word, as the Babylonian distinguished the work from the incantation. Of the operation it uses the root for working and serving, the same root West-Semitic uses for the service of the gods, and the exorcist speaks of his working; of the written incantation it uses a word for a bound charm and once a Persian word for a document; of the spoken word it uses words for words and a term for the invocation."@en ;
    skos:note "The editor's further observation, which rests on his count of the vocabulary and not on a statement of the texts: the exorcist gives himself none of the technical names of the forbidden side of the art, avoids the derived noun that denotes a magical practice while using the plain noun of his own working, and his favourite term for what he does is the root for binding, exactly the Greek and Latin words for the same act. Montgomery states that these magicians will work only white magic and that their whole effort is for the salvation of their clients."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText12OffspringAndPreservation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Nachkommen, Bestand und Bewahrung für eine Familie von neun"@de,
        "Offspring, long standing and preservation for a family of nine"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 12: 1-12, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 174-175)" ;
    skos:definition "Salvation from heaven for a named man, his wife, their seven named children, their house and their property, that they may have offspring and may live and be established and be preserved from demons and devils and plagues and satans and curses and liliths and tormentors which may appear to them. The angel adjured, and compelled by the rubric inside the text, together with those like him, will frustrate and ban all familiars and countercharms and necklace-spirits and curses and invocations and knockings and rites and words and demons and devils and plagues and liliths and idol-spirits and tormentors and everything whatsoever evil, that they flee and depart from the household and its dwelling from this day and forever."@en ;
    skos:note "The same family stands in text 16 and in the two Syriac bowls 31 and 33, so four bowls of three dialects were made for one house. The exterior of this bowl carries an endorsement naming where it was to lie: of the inner room, of the hall."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText28ManInflamedWithLove a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Mann entflammt, entzündet und zum Brennen nach einer Frau gebracht"@de,
        "A man inflamed and kindled and set burning after a woman"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 28: 1-5, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 213-214)" ;
    skos:definition "The bowl is appointed to the account of a named man, that he be inflamed and kindled and burn after a named woman. The woman is to sprinkle upon him a named substance with hot herbs called sunwort, a further plant and peppers, and the rites of love, until that he be inflamed and burn after her, in lust and in the mysteries of love; and pieces are to be taken from his heart. It closes in the name of the angel Rahmiel and of Dlibat the passionate, the gods, the lords of all the mysteries."@en ;
    skos:note "The one aggressive operation in this collection that works on a person rather than on a spirit, and the editor says as much: with text 13 these two are unique among early Semitic incantations in being love-charms, and this is the one for a passionate woman to bring her lover to her side. The text is mutilated at each point where a substance is named, and the bowl was cracked and repaired after an earlier copyist had read more of it than remained."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText36BridalMotherhoodSecured a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Eine Braut gesichert gegen den Erwürger der Kinder im Mutterleib"@de,
        "A bride secured against the strangler of children in the womb"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 36: 1-8, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, p. 238)" ;
    skos:definition "The bowl is designated and the murderess, daughter of murderess, is turned away. Commissioned by five named deities, the operator goes against the evil spirit called the Strangler, who kills the young in the womb of their mothers; it is to go from the presence of these holy angels, that sons may come to birth to their mothers and little children to their fathers; because he has been given a name by which he shall drive it forth, it is to go from the presence of the angels and depart from this engraved seal, and go to the bridal chamber and eat, and moreover drink a libation, and depart from the named woman."@en ;
    skos:note "The editor calls the purpose of this charm the insurance of a bride against the goblin which would destroy her powers of motherhood, and it is the one bowl made out for a bridal chamber. The text is badly obscured where the woman is named and where the food is named."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NippurText42ChildbedProtectedByTheWrittenNames a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Eine Gebärende und ihr Kind geschützt durch die geschriebenen Namen"@de,
        "A woman in childbirth and her child protected by the written names"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 42, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 259-260)" ;
    skos:definition "The amulet whose warrant is a narrative. Elija meets the Lilith on the road with all her band, going, as she says, to the house of the woman in childbirth who is in pangs, to give her the sleep of death, to take the child she is bearing, to suck his blood, to suck the marrow of his bones and to devour his flesh. He restrains her with a ban from the Name; she swears to let go this business in the case of this woman in childbirth and the child to be born to her and every inmate, so as to do no injury, and states the condition: every time that they repeat or I see my names written, it will not be in the power of me or of all my band to do evil or harm. She then names fifteen names of herself, and Elija adjures her and all her band by a list of powers that they come not to injure the woman or the child, nor drink his blood nor suck the marrow of his bones nor devour his flesh, nor touch them neither in their two hundred and fifty-six limbs nor in their three hundred and sixty-five ligaments and veins."@en ;
    skos:note "The only text in the collection in which the being bound states the condition of its own binding, and the only one whose efficacy is made to depend on an act of the client rather than on the operator’s work: the names must be repeated or seen written. The adjuration carries four numbers, the gematriac sum six hundred and thirteen, the ten holy Seraphs, the Ten Books of the Law, and the two hundred and fifty-six limbs against the three hundred and sixty-five ligaments and veins."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NorseAudsynir a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Was verborgen war, wird offenbar"@de,
        "Things become plain that were hidden"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Eiríks saga rauða, chapter 4, in Íslendinga sögur, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:definition "The effect the seeress states after the song: many spirits have now come to it and thought it fair to hear, who before wished to part from us and give us no obedience, and many things are now plain to her that were hidden before, and many others besides."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmc:NorseFleinnStodvadr a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Anhalten des fliegenden Schaftes"@de,
        "Staying the shaft in flight"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hávamál 150 (Guðni Jónsson; 151 in Bellows), in Eddukvæði. Sæmundar-Edda, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:definition "The fifth song: if the speaker sees a shaft shot in malice flying into the host, it does not fly so hard that he cannot stay it, provided he sees it with his eyes."@en ;
    skos:note "The condition of sight is stated in the stanza itself and is the sharpest failing condition in the catalogue: the song does not work on what is not seen. It is entered as its own prerequisite below."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmc:NorseHamskiptiYieldsFjarlaegLond a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der Gestaltwechsel trägt ihn in ferne Länder"@de,
        "The shape-changing carries him to far lands"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Snorri Sturluson, Ynglinga saga, chapter 7, in Heimskringla, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:NorseHamskipti ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:NorseFjarlaegLond .

pmc:OphiteSymbolumOstensum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Was der Sprechende ans Tor mitbringt"@de,
        "What the speaker brings to the gate"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.31 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)" ;
    skos:definition "The thing each speech names as already belonging to the speaker: the symbol of life borne in the character of a type at the seventh; his own beard as symbol at the second Iao; a faultless symbol preserved by the stamp of an image, and a body liberated by a pentad, at the fifth; purification with the spirit of a virgin at the third gate; the symbol of the mother at the second; and at the first, a symbol sculptured on the figure of the tree of life and formed in the likeness of innocence."@en ;
    skos:note "That reading must not be pressed further than the wording bears, and the wording pulls both ways. Every one of the speeches ends with a request: let grace be with me, O father, let it be with me. A description of the speeches as assertion of identity rather than supplication holds for their body and not for their close, and a file that recorded only the first half would be selecting."@en,
        "The speeches are not petitions to be granted something. Each of them states a condition the speaker is already in and asks that it be seen: look upon me, seeing thou beholdest, bearing now mine own beard as symbol. Whether the ruler could withhold what is asked is a question the passage never raises."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:OphiteGnosis .

pmc:PdmCompulsioNonAdventus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Es erschien nichts, und was der Text vorschreibt, ist ein stärkerer Name"@de,
        "Nothing appeared, and what the text prescribes is a stronger name"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. II/3-4, col. VI/21-22, col. VIII/1 and VIII/27-28, col. XVII/12-15, col. XXIX/16-18 and col. verso XXVI/4-8, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "The work takes up the case of an inquiry that produces nothing in at least six places and never once treats it as an outcome. If the boy opens his eyes and does not see the light, he is made to close them and the operator calls again. If obduracy takes place, the operator rises and recites the summons, which is the god's compulsion. If obstinacy appears, the boy not having seen the god, the operator turns round and pronounces the compulsion. If the god delays so as not to come in, a further cry is given. If the light has not come forth, the boy himself is made to speak to the lamp, and then the god reveals himself to him in the moment named. If the light is slow to come within, a seventh-times name is said, frankincense is put on the brazier, and a great name is uttered forwards and backwards four times. And if the gods give no answer once they are in, another name is recited nine times until they inquire for the operator truthfully."@en ;
    skos:note "There is no term anywhere in the work at which the operator is told to stop. Non-appearance is never an outcome of the procedure; it is the instruction to proceed to a stronger address of the same kind, and the strongest of them is a formula of compulsion that asserts the speaker's identity with the god. A session is not over until it has succeeded, and the transmission therefore cannot register a failed run."@en,
        "Where the work does assign a cause, it assigns it to the operator or to the medium and never to the procedure. The general assignment is purity at pmc:PdmPuritas; the particular one is at pmc:PdmCautioAspectus, where a boy who has looked away is afraid and the session is broken off. Both are conditions the only witnesses to which are the two people in the room."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmProbatioLapidis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wie man erkennt, dass der Stein echt ist"@de,
        "The way to know that the stone is genuine"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. verso III/7-13, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "An assay of a material, given in the plant and drug list of the verso. Of the white stone called foam of the moon the text says that there is another sort which is made into lime, and that the way to know it that it is genuine is this: you grind a little with water, you rub it on the skin of a man for a short time, then it removes the skin."@en ;
    skos:note "A stated procedure with a stated outcome on a body other than the operator's, applied to the ingredient rather than to the operation. The work thus contains the form of a test and applies it to what it buys and not to what it does. That contrast is the finding, and it is the same one the pregnancy test makes from the other direction."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:PdmResponsumVerum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Gott kommt herein und antwortet auf jede Frage wahrhaftig"@de,
        "A god comes in and answers every question truly"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. XXIII/2-5, with col. IX/28-29, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity the procedures are for: that a named god enters, gives his name when asked for it, and answers whatever the operator asks, in truth and without falsehood. One passage puts the scope of the answer in full — that the gods do not depart before they have been questioned concerning every word and have told the answer about heaven, earth and the underworld, a distant inquiry, water and the fields."@en ;
    skos:note "The phrase truly without falsehood recurs through the work as a fixed demand of the invocations, together with the demand that the god not substitute a face for a face or a name for a true name. The transmission therefore knows the failure mode of a false answer from an impostor and addresses it by asking the god not to commit it, which is not a check."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:Penemue a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Penemue"@de,
        "Penemue"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 69:8 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "A figure named in the Book of Parables as the one who disclosed the distinction of bitter and sweet and the art of writing."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:PesahimAmuletAgainstSixtyDemons a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Amulett fuer einen Daemon misslang, weil sechzig da waren, und die Daemonen sagten es"@de,
        "The amulet written for one demon failed because sixty were there, and the demons said so"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Pesahim 111b, the town official at the service-tree and the two amulets, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. II (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1901), pp. 710-711" ;
    skos:definition "The one condition of failure in this transmission that a bystander could check, with the defect named and the correction carried out. A town official passed a service-tree near the town, sixty demons entered into him and he came into danger. He went to a scholar who did not know that sixty demons are found at such a tree, and that man wrote him an amulet against one demon. Then he heard them performing a dance and singing that the master wears a gown like a scholar but when one examines him he does not even know how to say the blessing. Afterwards a scholar came who knew that sixty demons are found at a service-tree, and wrote an amulet against sixty demons; and then he heard them call, clear your gear out of here."@en ;
    skos:note "The sign of failure is the part the holding has least of. The amulet that was wrong did not merely fail to work: the demons mocked the man who wrote it, in a song, in his hearing, and the content of the mockery is that he is unqualified. The holding's signs are overwhelmingly signs of success announced to a practitioner in advance, which he then reports; this is a sign of failure delivered by the party the operation was directed against. Nothing says what a man should conclude who hears nothing at all."@en,
        "What the passage does not supply, and it bounds the find. It gives no way of determining the number of demons at a place other than the rule about service-trees near towns, and the rule gives a floor and not a count - no fewer than sixty. An amulet written for sixty where there were more would be wrong by the same logic and the text does not say what then happens. So the parameter is checkable in the one case the story tells and not in general."@en,
        "What this answers. The search at pmp:RabbinicDefectusSearch read seven printed pages of tractate Sanhedrin for any sentence saying what would show that one of this transmission's operations had not worked, and came back with nothing at all; its own note said the limit of the finding was that it covered one tractate, and named the proven amulet at Sabbath 61a as the next place to look. The place that answers it is here instead, three tractates away, and it answers more than was asked: the operation has a quantity, a wrong value of the quantity makes it fail, the failure is announced by the opposing party in words, and the operation performed with the right value succeeds. That is a condition of failure with a diagnosis and a repair."@en,
        "Why it is not simply a twenty-third witness for pmc:NoFailedRunCountsAgainstTheOperation, although it uses that register's commonest place. The excuse there is a condition in the operator which, in every one of the twenty-two witnesses, is either unestablishable in advance or unestablishable in principle - incredulity, dullness, impurity, insufficient merit, a decree about the patient. Here the condition in the operator is a matter of fact that anybody could settle by asking him: does he know how many demons are at a service-tree near a town. The failed run therefore does count against the operation as performed, and it is the performance and not the art that the failure is charged to. A bounding note to that effect is entered at the generalization rather than only here."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliPesahim ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmc:PesahimTheDivorcedWifesSpell a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Zauber, der nicht wirkte solange er zaehlte, und wirkte als er sich verzaehlte"@de,
        "The spell that did not take while he kept count, and took when he lost it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Pesahim 110b, the case of the divorced wife and the shopkeeper, cited for the rule that sorcery is to be feared at a large number too, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. II (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1901), pp. 706-707" ;
    skos:definition "The case the discussion brings as proof that sorcery is to be feared even at a large number. A man divorced his wife and she married a shopkeeper. He used to go out every day and drink wine, and she wanted to bewitch him, and it did not succeed with her, because he was careful about the pairs. One day he drank a great deal and did not know how much; up to the sixteenth cup he was still clear-headed and careful, from then on he was no longer clear-headed and not careful, and he went out having drunk an even number. As he went an Arab met him and said, there goes a dead man. He then clasped a palm, the palm withered and he burst."@en ;
    skos:note "A protection with a failure the transmission accounts for, and the account is the oldest form in the register: the protection did not fail, the man stopped applying it. The holding carries twenty-two witnesses of that construction at pmc:NoFailedRunCountsAgainstTheOperation, and this is one more, with the condition in the person protected. What it adds to the twenty-two is that the lapse is dated and quantified within the story - sixteen cups, clear-headed to that point - so the account is specific while remaining impossible to check: nobody counted for him, and the number is produced by the narrative that needs it."@en,
        "The Arab who says there goes a dead man is the only outside observer in the story and he is a bystander with no part in it. The holding has an interest in such figures - see generalizations/the-unsympathetic-witness.ttl - and this one is the opposite case: he is not unsympathetic, he is uninvolved, and his sentence functions as a sign announced before the outcome rather than as a judgement after it. Whether it is meant as foreknowledge or as a remark on a very drunk man is not decidable from the wording."@en,
        "The structure of the case is worth separating from its content. For an unnamed length of time an operation was performed repeatedly and produced nothing, and that long run of nothing is not evidence against the operation but evidence for the protection. Then one occasion produced something and that is evidence for the operation. Both halves of the record are read as confirmations, of two different claims, and the transmission does not notice that the second reading is only available because the first was granted."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliPesahim ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmc:PesahimWhoeverHeedsIsHeeded a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wer darauf achtet, auf den achten auch jene, und wer nicht, auf den nicht"@de,
        "Whoever heeds them is heeded by them, and whoever does not is not"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Pesahim 110b, the rule of attending and the note that in the West they did not attend to the pairs, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. II (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1901), pp. 705-706" ;
    skos:definition "The rule the transmission states for the danger of pairs, in its own words, as a rule: whoever attends to it, they attend to him; whoever does not attend to it, they do not attend to him; and nevertheless it is advisable to take it into account. The efficacy of the danger is thereby made to depend on whether the person believes in it, and the sentence that says so is immediately followed by advice to observe it anyway. The geographical note stands beside it: in the West they did not attend to the pairs."@en ;
    skos:note "The geographical clause is the closest thing in the passage to a controlled comparison and the transmission draws nothing from it. If the danger holds only for those who attend to it, a region that does not attend to it should show no harm, and the text reports exactly such a region in one half-sentence and goes on. Whether it had any count from the West is not said. The inference is the compiler's and the observation that it is not drawn is what the node records."@en,
        "This is an explicitly self-fulfilling structure of efficacy, stated as such by the transmission, and the holding has little of it. What makes it unusually clean is the third clause. The first two make the danger a function of attention, which would license anyone to escape it by not attending; the third withdraws that licence without withdrawing the rule, so the reader is told both that his disregard would protect him and that he should not try it. No reason is given for the third clause."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliPesahim ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmc:PistisSophiaVestisNominum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Kleid, auf dem die Namen aller Ordnungen stehen"@de,
        "The garment on which the names of all orders are written"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pistis Sophia 10 (trans. C. Schmidt, GCS 13, Leipzig 1905)" ;
    skos:definition "The light-garment sent from the last mystery and put on before the ascent. It is said to carry the glory of the name of every mystery and every emanation of the orders below: the treasury and its saviours, the seven amens, the seven voices, the five trees, the three amens, the twin saviour, the nine watchers of the three gates, the great invisible and the three thrice-powerful ones, the twelve aeons with their archons, and everything in fate and in the spheres."@en ;
    skos:note "The garment is an inventory rather than a vesture: what is listed on it is exactly the sequence that will be passed through. Whatever else the ascent is, the thing worn is a list of the names of what stands in the way."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PistisSophia .

pmc:PoToloOrbit a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Bahn des Pô tolo um Sirius"@de,
        "The course of pô tolo around Sirius"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 281-283" ;
    skos:definition "The course the seed-star is said to run around Sirius: perpendicular to the horizon, of a period of some fifty years counted double to a hundred because Sigui are paired as twins, with the star turning on itself once a year. Sirius is said to brighten when the seed-star is nearest and the seed-star to scintillate so that an observer believes he sees several stars when it is farthest."@en ;
    skos:note "The article calls the same course the figure of circumcision and excision: the near and far points of the orbit are the foreskin and the knife. The astronomical reading and the ritual reading are the same drawing, and the account gives no priority to either."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Dogon .

pmc:PrayerOfTheHeart a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Herzensgebet"@de,
        "The prayer of the heart"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Philokalia IV, Gregory of Sinai, On Stillness and the Two Ways of Prayer",
        "Philokalia IV, Nikephoros the Monk, On Watchfulness and the Guarding of the Heart" ;
    skos:definition "The continuous inward prayer that gives the tradition its practice."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hesychasm .

pmc:QuimbyCulpaOperantis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Bei einem Misslingen lag die Schuld beim Operateur, nicht beim Medium"@de,
        "If a failure occurred the fault was the operator's, not the subject's"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Horatio W. Dresser (ed.), The Quimby Manuscripts, 2nd ed. (New York: Thomas Y. Crowell, 1921), p. 55, and the founder's own wording at p. 195",
        "Horatio W. Dresser (ed.), The Quimby Manuscripts, 2nd ed. (New York: Thomas Y. Crowell, 1921), pp. 290-291, the case of the advertised medicine" ;
    skos:definition "The rule the editor states of the mesmeric years: the subject would see or do what was commanded once the operator gained his attention, and until the subject gave his full attention nothing resulted; if a failure occurred, the fault was the operator's and not that of the subject. The founder states the same division in his own voice of the healing practice: he pleads the patient's case as a lawyer does, and if he gets the verdict the patient is released, and if he fails he loses the case."@en ;
    skos:note "A witness for pmc:NoFailedRunCountsAgainstTheOperation and a peculiar one, because the place it assigns the empty run to is the only one of the four named places that costs the transmission anything. Fourteen witnesses put the failure on the person operated on, on the material, on a third party or on an operator understood as some other operator; this one puts it on the operator who is speaking, by name, in the first person. It immunises the doctrine exactly as the others do and it does not immunise the man."@en,
        "One case in the volume runs the other way and is recorded here rather than left for a later reader to find. A gentleman under treatment who thought he had consumption was improving; he read a newspaper advertisement for a medicine against catarrh, bought a bottle, and instead of the discharge lessening it grew worse and he ran down very rapidly. The healer reports the deterioration and assigns it to the patient's belief in the advertisement, adding that there is more virtue or misery in the advertisement than in the medicine. So the operator takes the failure of his own operation and the patient takes the relapse, and the dividing line is whether a rival remedy was believed in. The admission below should be read with that limit on it."@en,
        "The difference is worth stating precisely, because it is easy to overstate. Losing the case is not an outcome that counts against the claim: the science is untouched by it and only the pleader has lost. What the wording does supply that the others do not is an admission, published, that the thing can fail at all and that no property of the sick person will be produced to explain it. Whether that admission survives into the later transmission is a question for the works of Hopkins and Troward, which are named at the foot of this file and unopened."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:QuimbyManuscripts ;
    pm:readVia "The editor's connecting narrative in Horatio W. Dresser (ed.), The Quimby Manuscripts, 2nd ed. (New York 1921), which summarises the mesmeric years from the papers, the journal and the notices rather than printing them at the place" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought .

pmc:RasaVajradeha a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Körper, so hart wie Stahl gemacht"@de,
        "A body made as hard as steel"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. III, pp. 31-32, and ch. IV, mrita-sanjivani-gutika, p. 212" ;
    skos:definition "The bodily condition claimed of mercury that has passed the eighteen operations: the frame made hard, firm and unbreakable, proof against the weapons of enemies in the strongest statement of it, and held to be the condition without which concentration and therefore knowledge cannot be had."@en ;
    skos:note "The transmission argues for this one rather than asserting it. Emancipation depends on knowledge, knowledge on concentration of mind, concentration on a firm physique, and a firm physique cannot be had from any vegetable elixir because a vegetable drug is itself unstable and combustible. That chain is the ground on which mercury is put above the rest of the pharmacopoeia, and it is an argument from the properties of the material to the properties of the body."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:SanhedrinSorcererIsTheOneWhoDoesTheDeed a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Zauberer ist der, der die Handlung vollbringt, nicht der, der die Augen haelt"@de,
        "The sorcerer is the one who does the deed, not the one who holds the eyes"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Synhedrin 67a, the Mishnah with the apparatus, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. VII (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1903), pp. 285-286",
        "Mishnah Sanhedrin VII.11, trans. Herbert Danby, Tractate Sanhedrin: Mishnah and Tosefta (London: Society for Promoting Christian Knowledge, 1919), pp. 106-107" ;
    skos:definition "The sentence the whole law of this subject hangs on. A sorcerer is the one who accomplishes some deed and not the one who merely deceives the eyes; and the illustration attributed to R. Akiba in the name of R. Jehoshua is of two men picking cucumbers, of whom the one who actually picked is liable and the one who only appeared to is innocent. The capital list of the chapter puts the sorcerer among those who are stoned; this sentence says which of two men in the same posture that list reaches."@en ;
    skos:note "The first clause is a definition in the edition read and a penal norm in the manuscript. Goldschmidt's base text, the censorship-free Bomberg edition of Venice 1520 to 1523, carries the first clause without the word of liability, and his German renders it accordingly: the sorcerer, only when he performs an act, not however when he merely puts on a deception of the eyes. His apparatus carries a note at that very word, giving it for the Munich manuscript. Danby's English renders the clause the same way as Goldschmidt's base text. The second clause, the summary after the cucumbers, carries the word of liability in both. Which direction that variant runs is not decidable from the sigla key alone and is an open question of this run, named in its issues; what is established is that the standard public-domain English and the standard public-domain German both read the first clause as a definition."@en,
        "What the Mishnah does not say is as important as what it says. It says the one who holds the eyes is free, and it does not say that what he does is allowed. The gap is filled only in the Babylonian discussion, at pmc:SanhedrinThreefoldDivisionOfTheLaw, and from a different verse."@en,
        "Why this is the node the counter-search went looking for. The operative handbook in traditions/hekhalot.ttl sets out a hundred and thirty-six operations and, as the search at pmp:HarbaDeMosheDefectusSearch established, names no condition of failure for any of them. This sentence is the earliest place in the same language and the same region that distinguishes a performance which did something from a performance which only looked as though it did, and it makes the distinction decide a man's life. Neither text names the other; the juxtaposition is the compiler's and is recorded as such in the search node."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MishnahSanhedrin ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmc:SanhedrinSorceryContradictsTheHeavenlyRetinue a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zauberei ist nach dem Widerspruch gegen das Gefolge oben benannt"@de,
        "Sorcery is named from contradicting the retinue above"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Synhedrin 67b, the saying of R. Johanan on the name of the art, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. VII (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1903), p. 286" ;
    skos:definition "R. Johanan's answer to why the practices are called what they are called: because they contradict the household above. The translation read gives it as contradicting the heavenly college, and its note gives the word as an acrostic of that phrase. It is the strongest claim of efficacy this transmission makes, and it is made as the ground of the name itself."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim is not that the art produces an effect in the world but that it prevails against a decision already taken above, which is a stronger claim and a different one. The word rendered household is a loan from the Latin for a great man's following."@en,
        "The public-domain English loses this. Rodkinson gives it as a remark that witchcraft may affect even against heavenly decrees, without the acrostic, so that the ground of the name disappears and the sentence reads as an aside."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliSanhedrin ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmc:SanhedrinWhetherTheArtPrevailsAgainstTheDecree a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Kunst gegen das oben Beschlossene durchdringt"@de,
        "Whether the art prevails against what is decided above"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Synhedrin 67b, the objection from that saying against R. Hanina and the answer from his merits, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. VII (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1903), p. 286",
        "Babylonian Talmud, Synhedrin 67b, the saying of R. Johanan on the name of the art, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. VII (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1903), p. 286" ;
    skos:definition "A disagreement the text stages itself and does not leave to a reader. One saying grounds the very name of the art in its contradicting the household above; the next holds that there is none beside him even in a matter of sorcery. The discussion puts the second against the first in as many words and resolves it by an exception in the person: it was different with R. Hanina, whose merits were very great. The general claim of efficacy is left standing and the exception is personal."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as a node because the resolution is the finding and not the disagreement. A transmission that answers a counter-case by the merit of the man it happened to has made its claim safe against every single case, and that is a property of the claim which no amount of further material changes. It is also the reason the two sayings must not be read as one position."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:SanhedrinSorceryContradictsTheHeavenlyRetinue ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliSanhedrin ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmc:Sariel a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Sariel"@de,
        "Sariel"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:altLabel "Sahriel"@en ;
    skos:definition "A Watcher whose teaching concerns the moon; the Ethiopic gives its course, the Aramaic-Greek witness gives its signs under the name Sahriel."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:nameForm "Sariêl" ;
    pm:nameRole "watcher" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:SeeingWitchcraft a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Sehen der Hexerei und das Weissagen"@de,
        "Seeing witchcraft and prophesying"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 88-89" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity ascribed to the witch-doctors: to see what is unseen, to tell who the witches are, to see the emanations of witchcraft floating about as small lights invisible to the uninitiated, and to resist a fatigue that the source says could not otherwise be borne."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:ShangqingHuangtingCapacity a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Name oben eingetragen, unten gestrichen"@de,
        "The name entered above and struck out below"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangting neijing yujing 24 (Yinying zhang) (DZ 331), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile",
        "Huangting neijing yujing 34 (Feizhi zhang) (DZ 331), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "What the work says is obtained: the name enters upper Clarity and is struck from the register of the dead; the white head is turned back to the infant; the body brings forth light and a breath fragrant as orchid; the teeth are firm and the hair black without turning white; the colour of the face has a lustre of gold and jade; the bones are green, the sinews red and the marrow like frost; and ten thousand spirits are set to work and pay court to the three origins."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:ShangqingHuangtingKouchi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Was die Schrift nicht enthält"@de,
        "What the scripture does not contain"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangting neijing yujing 3 (Kouwei zhang) (DZ 331), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile",
        "Huangting neijing yujing 34 (Feizhi zhang) (DZ 331), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "The tooth-clapping that is commonly associated with this scripture is not in this work. The thirty-six chapters were searched for it at the wording and it does not occur once. What the work has instead is the swallowing: the mouth as the Jade Pond and hall of great harmony, where rinsing and swallowing the numinous fluid keeps calamity off; the dark spring and the hidden gate-towers; holding and rinsing the golden must and swallowing the jade blossom; the knotted pearl that holds the essence fast, the mouth closed and the tongue bent to feed on the embryo-fluid; drawing the fluid from the dark gullet into the bright hall to water the throat; and the one counted instance, thirty-six swallowings in the Jade Pond, by which the hundred vessels are opened and the blood and fluid begin."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a finding about the work and not as a claim of the work, which is why it carries no yielding and no caution. What the node establishes is exactly one thing: the canon text of the inner scripture does not contain the act. Where the practice does come from is not established here, because neither the commentaries nor the liturgical manuals were opened in this run, and the file does not assert a layer it has not read. The one count the canon text does carry is the thirty-six swallowings, and it is in the definition above. The search that establishes the absence is pmp:HuangtingKouchiSearch."@en,
        "The same holds for the location of the Yellow Court itself, which the work names in its title and never fixes. Nothing in the thirty-six chapters says where it is. That the later tradition places it variously in the brain, the heart, the spleen, the lower field or nowhere at all is not recorded here as a claim of this work; what is recorded is that the work leaves its own central term without a referent. In the verse the term stands twice. At the opening it names what the seven-syllable lines themselves are, the inner chapters made out of the transformation of the myriad spirits; in the fourth chapter it names a dweller, the person within, robed in brocade. The work has a full spatial vocabulary for the body and uses it freely, the upper palace and its nine chambers of one square inch, the bright hall, the cavern chamber, the cinnabar field, the three fields; none of it is ever attached to the term in the title. The search is pmp:HuangtingLocationSearch."@en,
        "The wording of this node was narrowed by the self-check at the close of the run of 2026-09-11. Two earlier formulations quantified over things the run had not read, that the act stands in every account of this transmission, and that its counts belong to the ritual and commentary layer. Both were written from recollection and neither had a passage under it, which is the condition of admission this project works to. They are struck rather than sourced, because the negative finding does not need them: an absence in a text that was read is worth exactly what the reading is worth, and an assertion about texts that were not read would have cost it that."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:ShangqingYunjiaCapacity a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Vom Wolkenwagen geholt werden"@de,
        "Being fetched by the cloud chariot"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 9.23b (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 9" ;
    skos:definition "Departure from the world in a conveyance said to be sent for the practitioner, given in this corpus as the outcome of a counted recitation and distinguished from release through the corpse, which it ranks below."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:Shemihazah a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Schemichasa"@de,
        "Shemihazah"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 6:3, 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:altLabel "Amezarak"@en,
        "Semjaza"@en ;
    skos:definition "The leader of the Watchers who binds the others by oath on Mount Hermon and is named as the teacher of spells and root-cutting."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:nameForm "Semjâzâ" ;
    pm:nameRole "watcher" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:ShintoKukatachiCriterium a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wer die Wahrheit sagt, bleibt unversehrt; wer falsch ist, nimmt gewiss Schaden"@de,
        "He who tells the truth will be uninjured; he who is false will assuredly suffer harm"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Nihongi, Book XIII, Ingyō 4th year, 9th month, in the English of W. G. Aston, vol. I, London 1896, p. 317" ;
    skos:definition "The criterion, announced to the whole company before the ordeal and in the same words to everyone: he who tells the truth will be uninjured, he who is false will assuredly suffer harm."@en ;
    skos:note "Both sides stated, in one sentence, of an event nobody present can produce at will, and announced before the first hand goes in. It is as clean an instance of the first column of pmc:TheCriterionNoPartyControls as the holding carries, and unlike most of them it is followed by a report of the outcome."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Nihonshoki ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shinto .

pmc:ShintoUkehiCriterium a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Töchter für ein unreines Herz, Söhne für ein reines"@de,
        "Females for an impure heart, males for a pure one"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Nihongi, Book I, the Age of the Gods, in the English of W. G. Aston, vol. I, London 1896, p. 35" ;
    skos:definition "The criterion as the main narrative states it, in the brother's own words and before anything is produced: let us make an oath together; while bound by this oath we shall surely produce children; if the children which I produce are females, then it may be taken that I have an impure heart, but if they are males, then it must be considered that my heart is pure."@en ;
    skos:note "Both sides are named in one sentence, and the failing side falls on the speaker who proposes the test. That is the rarer of the two shapes: a criterion whose adverse reading costs the operator, stated by the operator."@en,
        "What the sentence leaves open is which children count as his, and the rest of the passage turns on exactly that. The criterion says the children which I produce; it does not say what produces a child — the breath that blows the fragments away, or the object the fragments came from. See pmc:ShintoUkehiVariaeLectiones."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Nihonshoki ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shinto .

pmc:Sicun a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sicun, verliehene Potenz"@de,
        "Sicun, imparted potency"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), pp. 87-88 and p. 158" ;
    skos:definition "The potency of a human being and the emitted potency of a god. One is imparted to each person at birth and stays with him until death, enabling him to do what the beasts cannot and giving courage and fortitude; it may be pleased or displeased with its possessor and may be operative or inoperative at its own pleasure, may be invoked by ceremony or prayer, and can be imparted to no other person or thing. Most gods can emit their potencies, and an emitted potency can be put into a material thing by a shaman with a proper ceremony correctly performed. A potency so imparted is operative only when it is wrapped and hidden; wrapping, material and potency together make a wasicun, which the corpus renders as fetish."@en ;
    skos:note "A man may choose further potencies during his life, and the corpus says an evil man cannot secure a good one but may secure an evil one. The shaman who prepares a fetish devises a formula that must be repeated to invoke its powers."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LakotaRitual .

pmc:SubidaVisionesVeraeFallaces a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Visionen und Sprachen von Gott sind in sich wahr und nicht immer wahr fuer uns"@de,
        "Visions and locutions from God are true in themselves and not always true for us"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Juan de la Cruz, Subida del Monte Carmelo II.XVII (ed. Gerardo, Toledo 1912, pp. 186-195; II.19 in Lewis), read in the Spanish against Lewis 1906/1922 and the Carmelites of Paris, Oudin 1893" ;
    skos:definition "The claim the chapter sets out to prove: that although the visions and locutions of God are true and always certain in themselves, they are not always so with respect to us; and that this happens for two causes. The first is our defective manner of understanding them. The second is the causes or grounds on which they rest, which are at times variable."@en ;
    skos:note "The Spanish is Por dos cosas dijimos, que aunque las visiones y locuciones de Dios son verdaderas y siempre en si ciertas, no lo son siempre para con nosotros. La una es por nuestra defectuosa manera de entenderlas. Y la otra es por las causas o fundamentos de ellas, que a veces son variables. The edition records that the earlier printings read the second cause as threatening and as it were conditional, and that the manuscripts give variable; the editor prints variable and argues for it in a note of his own. The distinction is editorial and is recorded here because the node depends on the second cause."@en,
        "The first cause is argued from four scriptural cases in which a divine word did not come out as the hearers expected: the land promised to Abraham, which he never held and his descendants held four hundred years later; the promise to Jacob that God would bring him out of Egypt again, who died there; the two defeats of Israel against Benjamin after God had told them to go up and fight, with twenty-two thousand and then eighteen thousand of their own dead; and the prophecies of a temporal dominion read literally of Christ. On the third the text states the device plainly: God had not told them that they would conquer but that they should fight, and he meant by the defeats to punish a certain carelessness and presumption in them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SubidaDelMonteCarmelo ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinMysticism .

pmc:SusrutaMarmabheda a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die fünf Klassen der marman, und die Frist, die jede trägt"@de,
        "The five classes of marman, and the term each one carries"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Susruta Samhita, Sarirasthana VI.15, VI.16-21, VI.23 and VI.31, in the English of Kaviraj Kunjalal Bhishagratna, An English Translation of the Sushruta Samhita, vol. II, Calcutta 1911, pp. 175-176 and 179" ;
    skos:definition "A hundred and seven marmas are divided, by the consequence of their injury, into five classes with their counts: sadyah-pranahara, nineteen places; kalantara-pranahara, thirty-three; visalyaghna, three; vaikalyakara, forty-four; rujakara, eight. The terms stand in a verse of their own and not only in the class names: an injured marman of the sadyah-pranahara type terminates in death within seven days of the injury, one of the kalantara type within a fortnight or a month from the date of the hurt according to circumstances, and a case of injured Kshipra-marman seldom proves fatal before that time. An injured marman of the visalyaghna or vaikalyakara group may prove fatal if it is severely injured."@en ;
    skos:note "The chapter also supplies a reason for the difference in the terms, which is a second claim and not an observation: the sadya marmas are of fiery virtue and fiery virtue is easily enfeebled; the kalantara marmas are fiery and lunar at once, and cooling virtues take a considerable time to be enfeebled, so these prove fatal in the long run. Nothing in the reason could be checked without first granting the classification."@en,
        "The intervals are stated twice in the chapter and not in the same words, and the difference is a property of the translation that cannot be resolved on this route. Where the classes are first enumerated, the translator renders sadyah-pranahara parenthetically as fatal within twenty-four hours and kalantara-pranahara as fatal within a fortnight or a month; sixteen verses later the body of the text gives seven days for the first and a fortnight or a month for the second, and the individual marmas of the sadya class are each said to end fatally within the day. A reader of the Sanskrit would be able to say whether the chapter contains two terms or one term and a gloss. A reader of this translation cannot, and the node states both rather than choosing."@en,
        "Why the class system is worth more than the list. The transmission is not claiming that a wound to a vital place kills; it is claiming that the place decides how long it takes, and it assigns a hundred and seven places to five answers. That is a claim with a great many ways to be wrong, and it is the form the Suwen's needling-prohibition chapter has at six organs and the Hippocratic list of fatal parts has at none. The comparison is made at the convergence node and not here."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:SusrutaSastrakarma ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SusrutaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmc:SwedenborgTresGradus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Drei getrennte Grade in jedem Menschen von Geburt, nacheinander geöffnet"@de,
        "Three discrete degrees present in every man by birth, opened in succession"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Swedenborg, Sapientia angelica de divino amore et de divina sapientia, n. 236-237 (Angelic Wisdom concerning the Divine Love and the Divine Wisdom, trans. Swedenborg Society, London 1912, pp. 84-85)" ;
    skos:definition "Three degrees of altitude, natural, spiritual and celestial, are in every man by birth and can be opened successively; as they are opened the man is in the Lord and the Lord in the man. Within each there are continuous degrees of latitude by which it increases. The natural degree comes first and grows by knowledge up to the rational; the spiritual is not opened by that means but by the spiritual love of uses, which is love towards the neighbour; and even by the truths of that love the celestial is not opened, which is opened by the celestial love of use, that is love to the Lord, defined as committing the precepts of the Word to life."@en ;
    skos:note "The corpus says twice in one paragraph that understanding does not open the higher degree, and it is the sharpest thing in the reading for a project that collects prerequisites: the condition is not knowledge, which can be examined, but a love shown in conduct, which the same corpus then says is not felt while it is being acquired."@en,
        "The state is not a rank conferred by a lineage and is not an initiation. Every man has the three by birth; what is at issue is whether they open. That is why this file carries no degree structure and no admission."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DivinoAmoreEtSapientia ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Swedenborgian .

pmc:TaulipangReleaseWhenManyHaveFallen a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Zauberarzt darf die Novizen entlassen, wenn viele Mauari herabgefallen sind"@de,
        "The master may release the novices when many Mauari have fallen down"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Theodor Koch-Gruenberg, Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, Band III: Ethnographie (Stuttgart: Strecker und Schroeder, 1923), p. 208" ;
    skos:definition "The criterion that ends the apprenticeship, and the whole of it: then the Zauberarzt can release the novices, when many Mauari have fallen down. The training is over and the novices are finished Zauberaerzte who can now exercise the office."@en ;
    skos:note "A count without a number, and that is the finding. The apprenticeship is the most heavily quantified procedure in this part of the holding - a week per bark, five days of rest, ten days of drying, five or six months of singing, ten to twenty-five years in all - and the one quantity that decides whether a man is finished is left as many. Who judges that it is many is the master, and the thing judged is in the novice's body."@en,
        "It is therefore not a condition of failure and must not be read as one. Nothing says what happens to a novice in whom few Mauari fall, nothing fixes a term after which the master gives up, and nothing names an outcome that would end the training without the office. The search at pmp:TaulipangDefectusSearch looked for such a term and did not find one."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by Akuli, a named Taulipang, of his own training; recorded by Theodor Koch-Gruenberg in the Roroima region between 1911 and 1913, published 1923, in the recorder's German rendering, with the decisive clause given in quotation marks as Akuli's own words." ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VomRoroimaZumOrinocoIII ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Taulipang .

pmc:ThePassageThatPutsNoQuestion a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wo das Tor den Ausweis übergibt, nennt keine Überlieferung ein Misslingen an ihm"@de,
        "Where the gate hands over the token, no transmission states a failure at it"@en ;
    skos:note "Counter-search, carried against the compiled holding before the statement was written, as SPEC 16 requires, and it changed what was written. The search was set up for a different candidate — that a many-stationed passage always carries a single master item — and the Mithraic ladder of seven gates and the Hekhalot palaces refuted it, the first carrying no such item and the second issuing seals palace by palace. Both then read as witnesses for the division above. The same search found no token-handing passage anywhere in the holding that states a failure at a station, which is what the first limb claims; five traditions were gone through node by node."@en,
        "Falsified by either of two cases. A transmission in which a station admits the passer on a token the station or the text supplies, and which nevertheless states a test there, or names a case in which somebody was refused, or states what a wrong token would cost. Or a transmission in which a station puts a question or an ordeal to the passer and the text states only the passing side, with nothing about failing it. One clean case of either kind ends the statement; the Egyptian material above is the nearest thing the holding has to the first, and it is nearest because its classification is arguable and not because its wording is."@en,
        "The case that bounds the statement and does not break it, and it is the reason the first limb says supplied rather than possessed. The Egyptian gate material of this holding has the doorway ask the dead man its name, and the names are printed on the same sheet as the question; the consequence of not answering is refusal of passage and is stated. Read as a question it is a witness for the second limb; read as a token supplied by the book it is a counter-case to the first. The statement takes it as the second, on the ground that the wording is a question put to the passer, and it says so here rather than leaving the decision unmarked. Anybody who reads it the other way has a refutation of the first limb and needs nothing from this graph to bring it."@en,
        "The four witnesses of the first limb, and what each of them supplies. In the Books of Jeu the watchers of the gates of the light-treasure read a seal off the forehead of the passer and a cipher out of his hands, open, and expressly do not speak with him; each rank beyond them then gives him its seal, its mystery and the great name, so that the token of the next station is issued at the last."@en,
        "The spread, stated so that six witnesses do not read as six transmissions. The Books of Jeu and the Pistis Sophia are one family with a documented route — the second cites the first by name, twice — so the first limb rests on two transmissions and not four. The second limb rests on the Hekhalot material and on a Greek report about the Mithraic mysteries, between which no route has been proposed. Four transmissions in all, and the weakest point is that the two behind the first limb are as close as two transmissions in this holding get."@en,
        "The treasury passages of the first book of Jeu give the same arrangement one level down: the passer is sealed with the seal of the station, the father of the station gives him his own name and seal, and the watchers of the next gate scatter because they have recognised their father's seal. In the Pistis Sophia the gates open of themselves, the rulers look at the light-garment, see on it the mystery that holds their own names, and their bonds loose without a word being exchanged; and the garment itself was put on before the ascent began and carries the name of every mystery and every order below. None of the four states a test, and none names a case in which a passer was refused."@en,
        "Untested. No pm:Testing points here, which is the state and not an omission. What a protocol would have to settle first is the population, and here that is unusually tractable: the unit is a station in a transmission that carries a graded passage, and the ascent material of this holding is small enough to enumerate. The harder question is the one the first limb hides — whether the absence of a stated failure is a property of these transmissions or of the state of their texts, several of which break off."@en,
        "What the two witnesses of the second limb do instead. The Hekhalot descent puts one question at the sixth palace, about the nature of the water, and the whole penalty of the corpus hangs on it — and on the question and not on a deed: what the descender must not do there is ask, and the transmission attaches the consequence to that speech act. The Mithraic report has graded ordeals of fire, cold, hunger and thirst, and states both outcomes in one sentence: the highest initiation to the candidate still alive at the end, an honourable burial to the one who has succumbed. Both stand in the operative or reporting passage and not in a later gloss."@en,
        "Why the division is worth stating rather than the observation that some gates test and some do not. What the two limbs together claim is a relation between two features of the same transmission: whether the passer supplies anything out of himself, and whether the transmission admits that the passage can fail. A handbook that hands over every token has written the answer to its own question, and it then has nothing to say about a wrong answer — not out of carelessness but because in its own terms there is no such case. That is falsifiable in one direction by a token-handing passage that states a failure, and in the other by an ordeal or a question stated without a cost."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:JeuCustodesTacent,
        pmc:JeuThesaurusTransitusForma,
        pmc:MithraicProbatioLetalis,
        pmc:PistisSophiaTransitusVisu,
        pmc:PistisSophiaVestisNominum,
        pmc:WaterQuestionCaution ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission carries a graded passage in which the passer is admitted at each station by producing a token that the station itself or the transmission supplies him — a seal, a name, a number, a garment — it states no test at any station and names no case in which a passer failed to be admitted; where instead a station puts a question to the passer or subjects him to an ordeal, the same transmission states in the operative passage what failing it costs."@en .

pmc:ThePenaltyLaidOnAnotherParty a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Strafe für den Fehler wird dem auferlegt, der ihn nicht begangen hat"@de,
        "The penalty for the fault is laid on someone who did not commit it"@en ;
    skos:note "A fourth, fifth, sixth and seventh witness were added on 2026-09-19 from the Vedic Brāhmaṇas, and they form a cluster of the kind the third note warns about — in that corpus the displaced form is not a minority but the ordinary arrangement, and the four are listed so that the cluster is visible rather than so that the count looks better. What they add beyond number is four things the first three did not have. A grading: passing over a sentence of the formula makes the sacrificer worse, inverting two confuses him, uniting two makes him likely to die, and in all three the fault is the reciter's and the damage the patron's. A range: if the offering fire goes out the eldest son dies, if the householder's fire goes out the master of the house dies, if the southern fire goes out the cattle die, if all of them go out the family is without heirs. A prescribed transfer rather than a stated one: where the cow gives blood instead of milk, the repair is to give her to a Brahmaṇa whom the sacrificer does not intend to visit, because she milked blood after perceiving his suffering and evil, and he thus fastens that suffering and evil upon the recipient. And, once, a report: the heads of the victims obtained without the sacrifice were put on the altar for Aṣāḍhi Sauśromateya, by others, and quickly indeed he died after that."@en,
        "An eighth witness was added on 2026-09-20 and it breaks the cluster the third note warns about. It is neither burial nor needling nor Vedic, it is scriptural rather than field-recorded, and it displaces across a relation the earlier witnesses do not use: the fifteenth book of the right Ginza says of the daughter who has left the Nasoraean way that if she prepares offerings for the temples, judgement will be passed upon her father, and that if she goes to the soothsayers and Chaldeans, he will be called to account at the throne of the raging Nerig. Two faults, one bearer, and the bearer is alive and nameable, which puts this witness at the near end of the range the first note sets out rather than at the seven-generations end."@en,
        "Falsified by a count. If a survey of the operative transmission clauses across the holding's corpora found the displaced form to be the majority rather than the minority, the statement would have understated the shape and should be rewritten as a rule; if it found the displaced form confined to the two clusters named above and absent everywhere else, the statement would have overstated it and should be withdrawn to a remark about two corpora. Falsified in the other direction by an operative text that lays the penalty on a third party and in the same clause gives the performer a means of confirming that it arrived, without a direction or a term: that would break the condition the fourth note states."@en,
        "The backward case is a kind the statement did not have and it bears on the first half rather than the second. The earlier witnesses displace a consequence away from a performer who knows the fault; here a consequence already present in a living and reporting party is given a cause in parties who are dead. The bearer is the one alive to report, and what is out of reach is the fault and not the harm. That is the mirror image of the arrangement the statement describes, and it produces the same result by the other route: the practitioner cannot check the attribution, because the sins of seven generations cannot be enumerated and their absence cannot be shown. A later run may want to say that the statement is about the separation of the knower of the fault from the bearer of the cost, and that displacing either term achieves it."@en,
        "The consequence for how such material is read is small and exact. A rule whose penalty is displaced cannot be tested by asking the practitioner whether it came true, because he is not the one it would come true to, and the absence of a report of the harm is not evidence that the harm did not occur. Anyone building a protocol on a transmission of this kind has to find the bearer before he can find the outcome, and in the case of the dead ascendants there is no bearer to find. That is a statement about what can be examined and not about what is the case."@en,
        "The same piece also carries the undisplaced form a few lines later, where each man who smells the odour of the soothsayers is made to smell the odour of decay. A transmission holding both within one passage is the sharpest available illustration of why the statement says a substantial minority: the displacement is a device the transmission reaches for and not a grammar it is bound by, and this corpus does not say what decides which of the two it uses."@en,
        "The second half of an earlier draft of this statement said that the displacement puts the rule beyond checking, and the counter-search killed it. Three kinds of case in the holding refuse it. A purification performed by the unqualified is said to make the sickness and death in the world stronger than they were, which is a directional claim about a countable thing and therefore decidable in a way that mere failure is not. A burial doctrine that placed its effects a generation away could never be caught, but one line of it fixes a class of effects within a day of a burial, and burials are public and dated; a seventh-century memorial accordingly set a count of more than twenty recorded burials against it. And a felling prohibition names the man who will die, sets ten days, and denies the obvious natural explanation in advance. What all three have that the three witnesses lack is a direction and a term. That is the condition under which a displaced consequence comes back within reach, and it is stated here as a finding and not as a hope."@en,
        "The seventh witness bears on the fifth note rather than on the statement. That note says what an unfalsifiable displaced claim looks like when a tradition tries to confirm it, and names two anecdotes supplied by a defending commentary and never traced. The Vedic case is of the same kind and is better placed: it stands in the chapter that forbids the shortcut, names one man and one outcome, gives the interval as quickly, and reports no instance in which the shortcut was taken and nothing followed. One anecdote in fourteen books, told by the rule against those who break it."@en,
        "The three witnesses displace to three different distances, and the range is the point. In the Hawaiian case a breath taken before the close of a phrase is ill-luck to the person or family the chant honours, who are alive and can be watched, so that only the attribution and not the observation is out of reach. In the Chinese transmission clause the affliction of a wrongly conducted handing on is reported to the three palaces and falls on seven generations of ancestors, who cannot be examined at all and cannot be reported unafflicted. In the Sanskrit case the debt to ancestors, sages, gods and the human race is simply never cleared, and the price is paid in repeated rebirth, which places the consequence outside the life in which the fault was committed."@en,
        "The witnesses also cluster rather than distribute, and a reader should not take this for a cross-traditional regularity. Two corpora carry the displacement as their normal form: the burial literature, in which a badly sited or badly dated grave cuts off the sons and kills the grandsons, and the needling canon, in which the operator's error is paid for by the patient within a stated number of days. Outside those two the instances are scattered, and the three witnesses above were chosen from three unconnected transmissions in order not to rest the statement on either cluster."@en,
        "Two further cases bear on the first half rather than the second and are worth naming so that the claim is not read too strongly. In one tradition the audience at the public night ceremony knows the songs well enough to catch a deviation, so the fault is reported by people other than the performer even though the displaced ill-luck is not; and in another the displaced consequence is reported by the transmission itself as having been seen to arrive, in two anecdotes that the defending commentary supplies and that were never traced to the works they name. The first shows that displacement of the consequence does not entail displacement of the observation of the fault. The second shows what an unfalsifiable displaced claim looks like when the tradition does try to confirm it."@en,
        "Two witnesses added on 2026-09-23 from the Celestial Masters, and they matter for the shape rather than for the count, because between them they run the displacement in both directions inside one transmission. At pmc:CmZhangxinSanfen the officiant who keeps more than three parts in ten of the pledge is found guilty before the three officers, the calamity reaches nine ancestral generations, and he becomes for ever a lower ghost: the displacement runs forward from the one party certain to know the fault to a family that cannot be examined, which is the ordinary direction of the earlier witnesses. At pmc:CmWumuJiuzu the petition for releasing the punishment of the five tombs runs it backward: the illness that earlier petitions did not mend is charged to the sins accumulated in the lifetimes of three great-grandfathers, five ancestors and seven generations of forebears, or to a cut ridge, a felled cypress or a road driven past the graves."@en,
        "What the counter-search established about proportion should be kept with the claim, because it is the reason the statement says a substantial minority and not a rule. Of the forty stated consequences of a skipped prerequisite in the holding, four displace outright; of some fifty cautions, nine to eleven do. The commoner form by a wide margin is the penalty that falls on the practitioner's own body, memory, standing or attainment, and where that form is used the tradition can in principle be contradicted from inside, as one of the holding's own nodes says of itself."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:CmWumuJiuzu,
        pmc:CmZhangxinSanfen,
        pmc:HawaiianWelfarePresupposesExactChanting,
        pmc:HeterodoxWorshipNeverClearsTheDebt,
        pmc:MandaeanPoenaPatris,
        pmc:ShangqingHuangtingMengjin,
        pmc:VaidikaAgnyupasamana,
        pmc:VaidikaAsadhimrtyu,
        pmc:VaidikaNividdosah,
        pmc:VaidikaRudhiradoha ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission states what a defective or improperly transmitted performance costs, the cost is in a substantial minority of cases laid not on the performer but on a third party: the family or lineage the rite is done for, the dead ascendants of the person transmitting, or a class of beings to whom a debt is owed. The displacement separates the person positioned to notice the fault from the party who bears its consequence, and it does so in the operative text and not in a later gloss. It does not by itself make the rule unexaminable. It removes the consequence from the reach of the one person certain to know that the fault occurred, which is a different and weaker thing, and the check is restored wherever the displaced consequence is given a direction and a term."@en .

pmc:ThelemaErrorInharmonicus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der unharmonische Fehler, und was er zeigen soll"@de,
        "The inharmonious error, and what it is said to show"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber E vel Exercitiorum sub figura IX, chap. II, §§ 6-7, in The Equinox I,1 (London, March 1909), pp. 26-27" ;
    skos:definition "The stated pattern of a result that is worthless: some cards are harmonious, so that calling the five of Swords for the ten of Swords would not be a bad error, but to call the Lord of Love for the Lord of Strife would show that you were getting nothing right. The harmonies are to be thoroughly learnt from the tables of the correspondence book."@en ;
    skos:note "This is a failure condition in the strict sense the specification asks for: a class of outcome that the claim excludes. A run at the chance rate would already be a failure by the stated numbers; a run whose errors are inharmonious is declared worthless even if the numbers were to come out. The transmission thus names two independent ways for the same practice to fail."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberEVelExercitiorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaFacilitasEstIndiciumErroris a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Leichtigkeit wird als Fehlerzeichen gelesen"@de,
        "Ease is read as an error indicator"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book 4 (Liber ABA), Part I: Meditation, chaps. I and V, pp. 26 and 56 (first published London: Wieland & Co., 1912; read in the Celephais Press electronic edition of 2004, which follows the corrected text in Magick: Book 4 Parts I-IV, York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1994)" ;
    skos:definition "The rule on the other side of the same instrument: in these practices the apparent simplicity is such that the beginner is likely to wonder what all the fuss is about and perhaps to think that he is specially gifted; and anyone to whom the described difficulties do not occur need not imagine that he is meditating, since it shows merely that he is incapable of concentrating his mind in the very smallest degree."@en ;
    skos:note "Taken with the rule on the rising count, the pair leaves no reading of the measurement that tells against the practitioner's progress: difficulty is progress, and ease is incapacity. Each rule is defensible alone; together they close the instrument. Entered as a separate node because the two passages are separate and a later reader should be able to dispute one without the other."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberABA ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaMensuraPatinae a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Untertasse Wasser als Maß der Stellung"@de,
        "The saucer of water as the measure of the posture"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber E vel Exercitiorum sub figura IX, chap. III, § 9, in The Equinox I,1 (London, March 1909), p. 28; the wording collated between the re-setting and a page scan of the number" ;
    skos:definition "The stated criterion for the posture: a saucer filled to the brim with water and poised upon the head does not spill one drop during a whole hour, and no tremor can any longer be perceived in any muscle. At that point the practitioner is admitted for examination."@en ;
    skos:note "A criterion a bystander can apply, unlike almost everything else this transmission offers as a sign, and one that fails visibly. It is entered as its own node for that reason: the posture and the measure of the posture are separable, and only the second is examinable."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberEVelExercitiorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:ThelemaSignumSudorisDecemMinutorum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zehn Minuten und eine genannte Temperatur"@de,
        "Ten minutes and a stated temperature"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber RV vel Spiritus sub figura CCVI, § 9, in The Equinox I,7 (London, March 1912), p. 64" ;
    skos:definition "The one quantified prediction in the breath instruction: ten minutes of such practice is ample to induce profuse sweating in any place of a temperature of seventeen degrees centigrade or over. The progress of the practitioner in acquiring depth and fullness of breath is to be tested by the respirometer."@en ;
    skos:note "A prediction with a time, a threshold and an ambient condition, and an instrument named for the underlying quantity. It is the least exalted claim in the corpus and the most nearly checkable, and the two facts belong together."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberRVVelSpiritus ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:TheosophyChakrams a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die sieben Kraftzentren, ihre Strahlen und ihre Farben"@de,
        "The seven force-centres, their radiations and their colours"@en ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Chakras, chapter II, The Force-Centres, pp. 12-16 (Theosophical Publishing House monograph reprint)",
        "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter Force-Centres, pp. 286-291 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" ;
    skos:altLabel "chakram"@en,
        "force-centre"@en ;
    skos:definition "The claim that each of the vehicles carries force-centres, points of connection at which force flows from one vehicle to another, visible in the etheric double as saucer-like depressions on its surface, a quarter of an inch beyond the outline of the denser matter and not inside the body. The centres usually employed in occult development are seven and are placed at the base of the spine, the navel, the spleen, the heart, the throat, the space between the eyebrows and the top of the head. A primary force rushes into each at right angles and radiates out again in straight lines like the spokes of a wheel, and the number of spokes determines the number of petals the centre exhibits: four at the base of the spine, ten at the navel, six at the spleen, twelve at the heart, sixteen at the throat, ninety-six at the brow in two halves of forty-eight, and nine hundred and sixty in the outer circle of the crown, which has besides a subsidiary whirlpool of its own with twelve undulations. Secondary forces sweep round each saucer at right angles to the spokes and weave themselves under and over them, and the crossing of undulations of different sizes produces the appearance the Hindu books call petals. Each centre has a predominant colour, fiery orange-red at the base, a blending of shades of red with much green at the navel, radiant and sun-like at the spleen, glowing gold at the heart, blue with a silvery gleaming at the throat, rose with much yellow in one half and purplish-blue in the other at the brow, and indescribable chromatic effects at the crown."@en ;
    skos:note "The author states the status of the counts himself in the second work, and it is worth keeping because it is unusual in this holding. He calls the leap from sixteen to ninety-six sudden and the variation from ninety-six to nine hundred and seventy-two even more startling, says that the factors determining the number of spokes in a chakra are not yet known, and says that before more can be said hundreds of observations and comparisons must be made, repeated and verified over and over again. A transmission that reports its own figures as unexplained and in need of replication is reporting the state of its evidence and not only its doctrine."@en,
        "The counts are the diagnostic markers that originations/boran-theosophical-insertion.ttl asked to have measured, and they are measured here. Five agree with the 1916 footnote exactly. The sixth does not: the source gives ninety-six as its own figure and attributes two petals to the Indian books, adding that each half is subdivided into forty-eight. The seventh agrees on nine hundred and sixty and the footnote drops the subsidiary twelve."@en,
        "Three others are named and excluded in the same paragraph, and the exclusion is the part the 1916 footnote lost. The author says Blavatsky speaks of three other centres which she calls the lower centres, that there are schools which use these, and that the dangers connected with them are so serious that their awakening is to be considered the greatest of misfortunes. These three are not among the seven and are not located in the text. See pmc:TheosophyPericulumKundalini."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy .

pmc:TheosophyClairaudientia a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Stimmen, Musik und ferne Laute hoeren"@de,
        "Hearing voices, music and distant sounds"@en ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter Force-Centres, p. 296 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity attributed to the fifth centre. The arousing of the centre at the throat enables the man to hear voices, which sometimes make all kinds of suggestions to him, and sometimes music or other less pleasant sounds; and when it is fully working it makes him clairaudient as far as the etheric and astral planes are concerned."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the faculty the 1916 footnote in originations/boran-theosophical-insertion.ttl renders as the power to hear sounds at a distance, and the rendering is faithful to the source."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy .

pmc:TheosophyClairvoyantia a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wachvisionen, und Hellsehen beim vollen Erwachen"@de,
        "Waking visions, and clairvoyance on the full arousing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter Force-Centres, pp. 296 and 302-303 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity attributed to the sixth centre. When the centre between the eyebrows becomes vivified the man begins to see things, to have various sorts of waking visions, sometimes of places and sometimes of people; in its earlier development, when it is only just beginning to be awakened, it often means nothing more than half-seeing landscapes and clouds of colour; and the full arousing of it brings about clairvoyance."@en ;
    skos:note "The same chapter reports a further claim about this centre from another work of the transmission and supplies a mechanism for it: it says that The Voice of the Silence holds that when the serpent-fire reaches the centre between the eyebrows and fully vivifies it, it confers the power of hearing the voice of the Master, which the author glosses as the voice of the ego or higher self, and that the reason is that when the pituitary body is brought into working order it forms a perfect link with the astral vehicle. Not entered as its own capacity because the source for it is another work which this run did not open."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy .

pmc:TheosophyMagnificatio a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Winzige Dinge nach Belieben vergroessern und verkleinern"@de,
        "Magnifying and contracting minute objects at will"@en ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter Force-Centres, pp. 296-297 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" ;
    skos:definition "A second capacity attributed to the sixth centre and distinct from the first. Through it the power of magnification of minute physical objects is exercised: a tiny flexible tube of etheric matter is projected from its centre, resembling a microscopic snake with an eye at the end of it, and the eye at the end can be expanded or contracted, the effect being to change the power of magnification according to the size of the object examined. To examine an atom one develops an organ of vision commensurate in size with the atom."@en ;
    skos:note "Kept as its own capacity rather than folded into pmc:TheosophyClairvoyantia because the text treats it as a second way in which the centre is connected with sight, gives it a different organ and a different mechanism, and the 1916 footnote carries it separately as the power of magnifying and contracting objects."@en,
        "The claim carries its own identification with an older one, and the identification is the author's: this, he says, is what is meant in ancient books when mention is made of the capacity to make oneself large or small at will, and the little snake projecting from the centre of the forehead was symbolized upon the head-dress of the Pharaoh of Egypt, who as chief priest of his country was supposed to possess this among many other occult powers. Both halves are ordering acts of a modern writer about older material and are recorded here as his, not as the older material's."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy .

pmc:TheosophyPericulumKundalini a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Gefahren des Schlangenfeuers, mit benannten Schaeden"@de,
        "The dangers of the serpent-fire, with the injuries named"@en ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapters Force-Centres and The Serpent-Fire, pp. 287 and 299-301 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" ;
    skos:note "The author also states the ground of his own report, which is the part a later reader has to weigh: he says he is not in any way exaggerating the horror of this thing as a person to whom it was all a matter of hearsay might unwittingly do, that he has himself been consulted by people upon whom this fate has already come, and that he has seen with his own eyes what happened to them. The node carries pm:firstPersonReport for that reason and not pm:textualAttestation. The cases are not identified and no number is given, so nothing here is a protocol."@en,
        "The consequence is in these notes because pm:Cautioning cannot carry one, prima-materia#621. This case is unusual for that finding in that the consequences are many and graded, so a single consequence predicate would not have held it either; commented there in the run of 2026-10-08."@en,
        "The correction this run owed to originations/boran-theosophical-insertion.ttl is here. The warning in the source is about three centres that are not among its seven: the author says Blavatsky speaks of three others which she calls the lower centres, that there are schools which use them, and that their awakening is to be considered the greatest of misfortunes; and he adds that there is a school of black magic which purposely uses the downward rush in order to vivify those lower force-centres, which are never used by the followers of the Good Law. The 1916 footnote turns this into the statement that the Buddhist method does not seek to arouse the centres below the navel, a process attended with great danger. In the source the base of the spine and the navel are the first two of the seven that are used, and the arousing of the fire at the base of the spine is the means by which the others are vivified. The footnote therefore warns against the first two steps of the method it is digesting."@en,
        "The injuries are named and are not left general, which is why the caution is worth a node. Some are purely physical: the uncontrolled movement often produces intense physical pain, and it may readily tear tissues and even destroy physical life. That is called the least of the evils, because it may do permanent injury to vehicles higher than the physical. One very common effect of rousing it prematurely is that it rushes downwards in the body instead of upwards and excites the most undesirable passions to a degree at which it becomes absolutely impossible for the man to resist them; such men are said to become satyrs and monsters of depravity, probably to gain certain supernormal powers which bring them into touch with a lower order of evolution, and to escape from that thraldom may take them more than one incarnation. Even apart from this, premature unfoldment intensifies everything in the nature and reaches the lower qualities more readily than the good, bringing with it a great intensification of intellect together with an abnormal and satanic pride."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:TheosophyKundaliniArousal ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy .

pmc:TiantaiJingmeiXiemie a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Benannt und gescholten, weicht der Kobold auf der Stelle"@de,
        "Named and reproached, the sprite withdraws at once"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0470b, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity claimed for the naming: if the practitioner sees that the thing constantly comes at this double-hour, he knows it for that beast's sprite, and on his speaking its name and reproaching it, it withdraws at once. The second kind of interference is met by the same means with a fixed form of words: eyes shut and single-minded, the practitioner curses it inwardly and says, I know you now, you are such and such a kind in this world, a stealer of fire and smeller of incense, of false views, glad to break the precepts; I keep the precepts and in the end do not fear you; and a monk is to recite the precept text and a layman the three refuges and five precepts, whereupon the thing goes off backwards, crawling."@en ;
    skos:note "A misfiring condition is stated with it and is the reason this is the most answerable claim in the chapter: the procedure is to be applied when the thing comes constantly at one hour, so a thing that comes at no fixed hour is not to be met this way. The work therefore names a case in which its own identification does not apply, which is the shape of a condition of failure, in a chapter it afterwards says describes nothing that happens."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmc:TiantaiSibaisibing a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die vierhundertvier Krankheiten vergehen von selbst, oder werden hervorgebracht"@de,
        "The four hundred and four illnesses are removed of themselves, or brought on"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0471b, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:definition "The claim the healing chapter opens with, stated in both directions in one sentence: as for the method of seated meditation, if a man is able to use the mind well, then the four hundred and four illnesses are removed of themselves; if his use of the mind misses its place, then the four hundred and four illnesses arise from it. The number is the canonical one, a hundred and one for each of the four elements."@en ;
    skos:note "The strongest single claim of the work and the one place where it states its own failure mode in the same breath as its effect, with the same magnitude on both sides. It is answerable in principle on a named person, and the work supplies no sign by which the two directions could be told apart in advance."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmc:TiantaiWuGai a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Zweifelt er an sich, wird der Übende die Vertiefung am Ende nicht hervorbringen"@de,
        "Doubting himself, the practitioner will in the end not bring the absorption forth"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0465a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:note "Found on the second pass over the work and not on the first, which is the reason the second pass was made. The clause is the sharpest prerequisite in the work after the exit procedure and it is of a different kind: what must be removed is not an act or a condition of life but the practitioner's own estimate of himself, and the consequence is that the thing he is practising for will never arise. A transmission that makes a man's doubt of his own fitness the reason his practice fails has put the one thing he cannot report truthfully in the position of the cause."@en,
        "It is also the fourth of the work's devices, though it was not written as one. Doubt of the teaching keeps the teaching from dyeing the mind, doubt of the teacher obstructs the absorption, doubt of oneself keeps the absorption from arising at all; so a practitioner who sat and got nothing is told in advance that his doubt is a sufficient explanation. Beside the prohibition on reporting a benefit at pmc:TiantaiZheZhang and the clause at pmc:TiantaiFeiMoSuoWei that nothing but his own want of wisdom harmed him, this completes the account: the method cannot be answered because every outcome is about him."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Of the fifth covering, doubt, in its three kinds. Doubting himself: he thinks, my faculties are dark and dull, my guilt and defilement deep and heavy, am I the man for this. Making this doubt of himself, the dharma of the absorption will in the end not be brought forth; so if he would cultivate the absorption he must not make light of himself, since the roots of good of a former life are hard to measure. Doubting the teacher: he thinks, this man's bearing and appearance are so and so, he has no way in himself, how should he teach me. Making this doubt and contempt, that is an obstruction to the absorption. Doubting the dharma: men mostly hold to their own minds and cannot at once believe and receive with a reverent mind what is given them, and if the mind gives rise to hesitation, the dharma does not dye the mind. And of the first covering: a man of craving is very far from the way, for where the mind clings to craving there is no way to come near the way."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:TiantaiZhiguan ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:TiantaiWuGaiList ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmc:TiantaiWuyuan a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die fünf äußeren Bedingungen, die vor dem Beginn vorliegen müssen"@de,
        "The five outward conditions to be had before the practice begins"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0462c to 0463b, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:definition "First the precepts kept pure, with the repentance procedure for the one who has broken them. Second clothing and food sufficient, in one of three measures of clothing and four of food. Third a quiet dwelling, of which three are allowed: deep mountain away from people, an ascetic retreat three or four li from the nearest settlement so that the sound of herding is gone, and a clean monastery away from lay dwellings. Fourth the cutting off of occupations, in four kinds: livelihood, social relations, crafts and skills including medicine, spells, divination and reckoning, and the business of learning, reciting and lecturing. Fifth the nearness of good friends, in three kinds: the outward protector who provides, the fellow practitioner who practises the same way, and the teacher who shows the gates of the practice inward and outward."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmc:TummoYieldsInnerHeat a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Tummo bringt innere Hitze hervor"@de,
        "Tummo yields inner heat"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)" ;
    skos:note "The one pairing in this file that names a capacity with an outward, measurable side. Any later protocol has a clearer target here than anywhere else in the collection."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Tummo ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:TibetanTantric ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:InnerHeat .

pmc:TungusExtasy a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Zustand hat zwei Grenzen, und beide sind verfehlbar"@de,
        "The state has two limits, and both can be missed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXIX, sect. 134, pp. 362-363" ;
    skos:definition "The state produced in the performance is bounded on both sides: it must not turn into an uncontrolled fit and it must not be suppressed. Within those limits it varies in degree. Producing it requires the ability to double, a power of controlling the thinking mechanism, and knowledge of the methods for entering the state, maintaining it and ending it as the performance requires."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the passage that decides the reading of the whole file. A person in whom the state turns into a fit, who cannot control himself, is held to be possessed by the spirits and for that reason cannot become a shaman: he must be treated. A person who cannot produce the state at all cannot become a shaman either, but may become a clan priest. The transmission thus excludes on both sides of a band, and the popular identification of the office with uncontrolled trance names precisely one of the two exclusions."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SiberianShamanism .

pmc:TungusMasteringOfSpirits a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Meisterung der Geister"@de,
        "Mastering of spirits"@en ;
    dcterms:source "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXII, sect. 95 A, p. 271" ;
    skos:definition "The condition that makes an ordinary man a shaman: he is a master of spirits, at least of a group of them. The mastered spirits are not guardians and do not elect him; the master must feed them and handle them, and introduces them into himself when he wants to. The shaman uses his own body as a placing for spirits."@en ;
    skos:note "The discrimination the transmission itself draws, and the one most reliably lost in later reports of it. The difference between a person possessed by spirits and a shaman is that the shaman introduces them at his own will. The recorder states that the Tungus are always very careful as to the diagnosis, whether the person is possessed by the spirits or the spirits are possessed by the person, and that nobody would recognise anyone as a shaman who could not possess spirits in that sense."@en,
        "The relation is named by a term the recorder gives in five dialect forms at once. The digitised copy used is an optical transcription and its rendering of these forms is not trustworthy at the level of single letters, so the forms are not reproduced here as if they had been read cleanly. What is reported without doubt is that one term covers the relation across the Manchu, Birarcen, Kumarcen, Nercinsk and Khingan dialects, and that the spirits are said to use it of their master through the shaman's mouth."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SiberianShamanism .

pmc:VaidikaAsadhimrtyu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Man tat es so für Aṣāḍhi Sauśromateya, und bald darauf starb er"@de,
        "They did so for Aṣāḍhi Sauśromateya, and quickly he died after that"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa VI,2,1,37 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 41, Oxford 1894, pp. 170-171" ;
    skos:definition "The victims' heads that go into the fire-altar are to come from an animal sacrifice performed for the purpose. The chapter states the rule by naming the shortcut and its price: some, having obtained those heads in that way — somehow or other, by buying or begging them, as the commentator glosses it — put them on the altar, thinking that either way they are animals; but they who do this become mortal carcases, for unpropitiated are those heads of theirs. Then the case: in this way, indeed, they did put them on for Aṣāḍhi Sauśromateya; but quickly indeed he died after that. The chapter goes on to reject gold heads as false bricks and earthen ones as a substitute for the man who knows neither the practice nor the theory."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the only place in the five volumes read where the corpus reports that a stated consequence of a ritual fault actually arrived, and it came out of a search rather than a reading: the whole run was looking for such a case and had provisionally concluded there was none. The search and its terms stand at examinations/searches-vedic.ttl."@en,
        "What the case is worth, stated exactly. It names a man, it names the fault, it names the outcome, and it gives an interval — quickly, and nothing more precise. It reports one instance and no others, gives no number of men who used bought heads and lived, and is told inside the chapter that forbids the shortcut. Nothing here is a count; it is the transmission's own confirming anecdote, which is a different thing and the reason this holding keeps a class for cases and a class for claims."@en,
        "Who bore it is worth noting beside the rest. The heads were put on for Aṣāḍhi, by others; the sentence is in the plural for the doing and the singular for the dying. The fault is the officiants', the death is the patron's, which is the arrangement generalizations/displaced-penalties.ttl describes, here with the consequence reported as having been seen to arrive."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual .

pmc:VaidikaJnanenaJitam a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kraft des Wissens habe ich selbst obsiegt"@de,
        "By dint of knowledge I myself have prevailed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa XI,5,3,8-12 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 44, Oxford 1900, pp. 82-85" ;
    skos:definition "The clause attached to every one of the five cases, in the same words each time. The damage is stated for him who would not know this; and the man questioned adds of himself: but by dint of knowledge I myself have prevailed. The knowledge in each case is an identification of one breath with another — the breath of the mouth has entered the upward breathing, and so on — and the repair is an offering moved to a different fire."@en ;
    skos:note "It is not a later apology either. The clause is not attached to a complaint that the rite failed; it stands in the first statement of the danger, in the mouth of the authority who states it, as part of what makes him an authority. The excusing condition and the threat are the same sentence."@en,
        "This is the finding of the passage and it is the reason the tradition was worth taking off the register. The same mishap, the same fire, the same hour: the eldest son dies or he does not, and what decides it is what the offerer knows. No observation of the mishap distinguishes the two cases, and the knowledge is not a skill anybody could see exercised — it is an equivalence held in the mind and reported only by the man who holds it."@en,
        "What the passage does supply against itself, and it is unusual: it says outright that the knowledge is rare. The exchange is a contest in which one party is repeatedly told that this much they know in common, until at the fifth case Śauceya answers that he did not know this. See pmc:VaidikaMurdhanvyapatana for what follows."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual .

pmc:VaidikaPunarmrtyu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Punarmṛtyu, der wiederkehrende Tod"@de,
        "Punarmṛtyu, the death that comes again"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa X,4,3,10 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 43, Oxford 1897, pp. 357-358" ;
    skos:definition "The condition the transmission offers to remove: not death but its recurrence. Those who neither know nor perform the work come to life again when they have died, and become the food of Death time after time; those who know or perform it come to life again and come to immortal life."@en ;
    skos:note "Eggeling renders the term throughout as recurring or recurrent death and Keith as repeated death, so a search in one wording misses the other half of the corpus. This was the case in the searches carried in this run and is recorded at examinations/searches-vedic.ttl."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual .

pmc:VaidikaYaEvamVeda a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ya evaṃ veda: was dem versprochen wird, der dies weiß"@de,
        "Ya evaṃ veda: what is promised to whoever knows this"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aitareya Brāhmaṇa iii,7, iii,8 and iii,10 (xi,7, xi,8 and xi,10), in the English of Arthur Berriedale Keith, Rigveda Brahmanas, Harvard Oriental Series 25, Cambridge 1920, pp. 169-171",
        "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa X,4,3,1-2, XI,2,7,33, XI,5,6,9 and I,6,3,15 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 43, Oxford 1897, p. 356, and 44, Oxford 1900, pp. 45-46 and 99, and 12, Oxford 1882, p. 167" ;
    skos:definition "The recurring clause of this corpus, which attaches an attainment not to the performance but to the knowing of what the performance means. Every passage read in this run carries it, and the attainments are these. He who knows the year to be Death — that year does not destroy his life before old age, and he attains his full extent of life. He who knows the balance mounts it in this world and escapes being placed on it in the world beyond. He who studies at the four thunder-calls is freed from the death that comes again and attains community of nature with the Brahman. He who knows the meaning of the full-moon offering attains Indra's prosperity, is endowed with glory, and becomes such a consumer of food. In the Aitareya: he who knows the continuity of the vaṣaṭ call is united with offspring and cattle; he who knows its appeasing lives a full life; he who knows the placing of the Nivid is propagated with offspring and cattle and shines on all sides with the decoration of the sacrifice."@en ;
    skos:note "Held in one node and not in seven, with the reason stated because the harvest rule of SPEC §15 is against exactly this kind of economy. What that rule protects is the count of distinct claims a corpus makes, and these are not distinct claims: they are one clause with one small set of outcomes — a full life, offspring, cattle, prosperity, glory — reattached at the end of each exposition to whatever that exposition explained. The distinct attainments of this corpus, escape from the death that comes again and the fixing of the balance, have their own nodes; what is held here is the form and the list of places, so that a later run can count them without reading the passages again."@en,
        "The count is of the passages read and not of the corpus. Searching Eggeling's five volumes for knows this and knowing this, on the whitespace-normalised text, returns 268 occurrences: 74 in kāṇḍas I to II, 17 in III to IV, 14 in V to VII, 57 in VIII to X, 106 in XI to XIV. Nobody has gone through them, and this node does not stand for that work. The distribution is itself worth a run: the clause is commonest where the corpus turns the rite into knowledge, and it is nearly as common in the first two kāṇḍas, where it does not."@en,
        "Why the form matters more than any one of its instances. The clause makes the attainment turn on an item of knowledge and not on anything done, so two men who perform identically are promised different things, and nothing about the performance shows which. That is the same construction as pmc:VaidikaJnanenaJitam on the side of the harm, and the two together are the corpus's characteristic move: knowledge decides, and knowledge is reported only by the man who has it."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual .

pmc:Watchfulness a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wachsamkeit"@de,
        "Watchfulness"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Philokalia I, Diadochus of Photice, Gnostic Chapters",
        "Philokalia I, Hesychios of Sinai, On Watchfulness and Holiness" ;
    skos:definition "The guarding of the heart and the attention to what arises in it, treated as the groundwork on which the prayer of the heart is built."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hesychasm .

pmc:WaterQuestionCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Frage nicht nach dem Wasser"@de,
        "Do not ask about the water"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hekhalot Rabbati, chapter 26, trans. Morton Smith, corrected by Gershom Scholem, ed. Don Karr" ;
    skos:definition "A caution whose object is a speech act and not a deed. What the descender must not do at the sixth palace is not touch, not turn back and not doubt, but ask; the transmission attaches the whole penalty to the question about the nature of the water."@en ;
    skos:note "A rule against asking is the form a tradition takes when the thing to be hidden is that there is nothing there. This is not offered as a reading of the passage's intent, which the graph cannot establish, but as the shape the rule has: the prohibition falls exactly on the act that would reveal the absence."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:YeridatMerkavah ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HekhalotRabbati ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hekhalot .

pmc:Wowihanble a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wowihanble, eine empfangene Mitteilung"@de,
        "Wowihanble, a communication received"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 68 and p. 156" ;
    skos:definition "What the corpus calls a supernatural communication and the English of the recorders calls a vision: a thing seen or heard, waking or asleep, in the form of anything that breathes or of something inanimate, which makes known by what it says or does that it is the thing sought. It may speak intelligibly, or in words the receiver does not understand, or in the language of birds and beasts."@en ;
    skos:note "The term is printed in the corpus as Wowihanble and the seeking as hanblapi. The digitised copies read from render the second word with an initial th, which is a known confusion of the scanning and not a variant of the print; the identifier follows the reading hanblapi. A check against a printed copy is held as an issue."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LakotaRitual .

pmc:XeperAsSelfGeneration a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "Xeper als das Wort der Selbsthervorbringung"@de,
        "Xeper as the word of self-generation"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1975" ;
    dcterms:source "M. A. Aquino, The Temple of Set (Temple of Set, San Francisco), in the account of the Word Xeper and its Egyptian derivation" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that the Egyptian term for becoming, coming into being, personified in the scarab god and signifying self-generation and the dawn, names the transformation and evolution of the will, and that it is uttered as the word of a new age succeeding the age of indulgence."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as an origination because of the shape of the claim rather than its content. The word is Egyptian and its lexical sense is not in dispute; what begins in 1975 is its use as the formula of a path, in a sequence of ages with named predecessors. The Egyptological reference is present in the source and does not make the doctrine ancient."@en,
        "This is the end state the milieu sets against dissolution, and it is worth recording in the author's own terms rather than in the summaries: not the preservation of an ego as ordinarily meant, but a self-generation modelled on a god who brings himself forth. A record that reports it as mere ego-preservation reports the criticism and not the claim."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:circulatesAs "A doctrine recovered from Egyptian antiquity, reported as the teaching the word is drawn from rather than as a formula first uttered in 1975." ;
    pm:originatedBy "Michael A. Aquino" .

pmc:XingqiDescent a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Abstieg als Stufe der Folge"@de,
        "Descent as a stage of the sequence"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Xingqi yupei ming, in D. Harper, Early Chinese Medical Literature (1998), p. 125" ;
    skos:definition "The stage at which the gathered and extended breath goes downward, after which it settles and becomes firm; the sequence then turns and rises."@en ;
    skos:note "The inscription assigns direction to a place in a sequence, not to a phase of breathing. Descent follows gathering and extension; ascent follows sprouting and growing. The closing lines set the two directions against the two powers, the dynamism of heaven showing in what rises and that of earth in what descends. Nothing here binds a direction to inhaling or to exhaling. See prima-materia#27 and prima-materia#28."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XingqiYupeiMing ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:XingshiJixiong a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Gestalt und Lage, jede mit benanntem Ausgang"@de,
        "The configuration and the form, each with a named outcome"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zangshu, miscellaneous section, in the recension of Wu Cheng, one juan, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text" ;
    skos:definition "The book's longest run of decidable sentences, in two series. By configuration: like ten thousand horses coming down from heaven, the burial of a king; like great waves with ridge behind ridge, the burial of a lord of a thousand chariots; like a descending dragon with the water winding and the clouds attending, rank and emolument of the three highest offices; like storeyed houses with lush grass and tall trees, the opening of a government and the founding of a state; like a startled snake, crooked and slanting, the ruin of house and state; like spear and halberd, death by arms and imprisonment of the form; like running water, the living all become ghosts. By form: like a screened seat with a ridge standing in the middle, buried where it stops, princes and lords arise; like a swallow's nest, buried at the bend, land is granted and rank divided; like a fallen mound with the ridge coming from far behind and the answer in front curving round, the nine thorns and three sophoras of high office; like an upturned cauldron, its ridge can make rich; like a planted cap, lasting prosperity and joy; like scattered counting rods, all affairs are confounded; like disordered clothing, jealous daughters and licentious wives; like a bag of ashes, the house burns and the granary with it; like an upturned boat, the women fall ill and the men go to prison; like a crosswise stool, the sons are cut off and the grandsons die; like a lying sword, execution and extermination; like an upturned knife, misfortune and flight into hiding. The series closes with rules over the two: where configuration and form run together it is auspicious and where they run against each other inauspicious; a good configuration with a bad form leaves one hope in a hundred; a bad configuration with a good form brings the misfortune before the day turns. And of the whole art it says that the configuration is the hardest to judge, the form next, and the compass direction last of all."@en ;
    skos:note "The clause about a bad configuration with a good form is a second interval claim beside the one this file already holds. It places the misfortune within a day, in a different pairing from the one at the node about the misfortune that does not turn a day, and the commentary glosses it as saying that the response is immediate. Two independent places of the same book therefore put one class of its effects inside twenty-four hours of the burial."@en,
        "These are the most examinable single sentences this graph holds about any practice. Each names a visible feature of ground and a public, datable event in a family — an office, an execution, a fire, a line dying out — and the ground is there to be seen before the event. What decides whether they could ever be tested is not the outcome but the antecedent: who fixes which of these forms a given hill has, and when. The book itself says the configuration is the hardest of the three to judge, which is as close as it comes to conceding the point."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination .

pmc:YinshuKouchi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Zähne schlagen; und ist schon Karies da, mehr schlagen"@de,
        "Knock the teeth; and if there is decay already, knock them more"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhangjiashan Yinshu, slip 98, in Vivienne Lo (trans.), How to do the Gibbon Walk: A Translation of the Pulling Book (ca. 186 BCE), Needham Research Institute Working Papers 3 (Cambridge: Needham Research Institute, 2014)" ;
    skos:definition "A single slip carrying both halves of one instruction: on waking, knocking the teeth prevents tooth decay; if there is tooth decay, knock them more."@en ;
    skos:note "Two clauses, and the second withdraws what the first offers to a check. The first states a preventive with an outcome anybody could look for in his own mouth. The second says what to do when the outcome is not there, and what it says is to do more of the same, so that no mouth full of decay ever tells against the knocking. This holding carries the identical construction from the assay of the fourth chapter of the Baopuzi — where the gold does not form, seal it and fire it again for the same count of days, and then there is no case in which it does not form — and this slip is some four hundred years older and came out of a closed tomb."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Yangsheng .

pmc:YitiShouyin a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Gebeine empfangen das Qi und die Nachkommen werden beschirmt"@de,
        "The bones receive the qi and the descendants are shaded"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zangshu, inner section, in the recension of Wu Cheng, one juan, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text" ;
    skos:definition "The claim the whole doctrine rests on: a person receives his body from his parents, and when the root bones obtain the qi the body left behind is shaded. The classic is quoted for it — the qi is touched and responds, and the fortune of the dead reaches the living — and the resonance is illustrated by the bronze mountain that collapsed in the west while the numinous bell answered in the east."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim is transitive across a generation and across death, which is what makes it examinable in principle: it predicts something about descendants from something about a grave. What it does not carry is a way of telling the shading from its absence in any single case, and the commentary printed with the text takes the transitivity further rather than bounding it, extending the shading to adopted children and to the sons of a second mother."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination .

pmc:ZosimosDuoGradusTincturarum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zwei Stufen der Tinkturen, und die gekochte ist kürzer und verlangt mehr Geschick"@de,
        "Two grades of tinctures, and the cooked one is shorter and needs more skill"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zosime, französische Übersetzung in M. Berthelot und Ch.-Em. Ruelle, Collection des anciens alchimistes grecs, seconde livraison, Paris 1888, S. 193" ;
    skos:definition "The comparison the letter draws between the two ways of working: there are two grades of fitting tinctures, according to whether one operates on the raw or on the cooked species. The procedure of cooking is free of a great fatigue; it needs a great skill and it is shorter, as the divine Mary has said."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also the only comparative judgement of working in what was read of this corpus, which is why it is entered although it decides nothing about the tinctures themselves."@en,
        "The page is the page after the one that closes the prescription of the Final Reckoning, and whether this sentence still belongs to that letter was not established. The node therefore cites the page and is filed under the collective work identifier."@en,
        "Three grounds are given and all three are properties of the procedure and not of whoever works it: it costs less labour, it demands more dexterity, it takes less time. Nothing is said about the fitness, the rank or the initiation of the operator, and the judgement is attributed to a named earlier authority rather than to the writer."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Cheirokmeta ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GraecoEgyptianAlchemy .

pmc:ZuniPrayerExactness a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Buchstabengetreue Wiedergabe"@de,
        "Letter-perfect delivery"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ruth L. Bunzel, Zuni Ritual Poetry, in Forty-Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of American Ethnology 1929-1930 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1932), p. 616" ;
    skos:definition "The requirement, set on the parts of the ceremony chanted aloud and in unison, that the words be rendered letter perfect."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ZuniRitual .

pmc:ZuyuaNaat a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Verstehen der Sprache von Zuyua"@de,
        "Understanding the language of Zuyua"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ralph L. Roys, The Book of Chilam Balam of Chumayel (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1967), chapter IX, pp. 89-93" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity to hear a figurative demand and produce the thing meant. The witness does not treat this as a skill beside descent but as the same fact: what the examination asks is whether the candidate can explain how the ruling men came and whether he is of the lineage of rulers."@en ;
    skos:note "The text says nothing about where this language is learned or whether the answers are taught. That silence is the whole difficulty of the procedure as a test and is taken up at pmc:ZuyuaExamination."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MayaRitual .

pm:abidingState a pm:AwarenessValue,
        pm:StateDuration ;
    rdfs:label "Bleibend"@de,
        "Abiding"@en ;
    skos:definition "The source has the condition remain with the one who has it, whether it was worked for or not."@en .

pm:anchoringNotStated a pm:AwarenessValue,
        pm:SensoryAnchoring ;
    rdfs:label "Sinnesbindung nicht angegeben"@de,
        "Anchoring not stated"@en ;
    skos:definition "The source was read for this question and does not answer it."@en ;
    skos:note "Not the same as an untagged node, and the difference is what makes a query of the form 'only in this state' readable at all. No placement means nobody asked; this value means somebody asked and the source is silent."@en .

pm:anchoringUnbound a pm:AwarenessValue,
        pm:SensoryAnchoring ;
    rdfs:label "An keine Sinnesbindung gebunden"@de,
        "Not bound to an anchoring"@en ;
    skos:definition "The source prescribes the practice across the states this axis distinguishes: not in a set-apart session but through work, speech and sleep alike."@en ;
    skos:note "Deliberately not added to the other three axes. There is no case in the holdings for an awareness that is unbound as to its locus or its agency, and a value written for a case nobody has met is vocabulary running past the matter rather than ahead of it."@en,
        "Distinct from pm:anchoringDeclined, and the difference is which claim the tradition makes. A tradition that declines the axis denies that the distinction applies to it at all; a tradition that prescribes unbroken prayer grants the distinction and says the practice spans it. The hesychast literature is the clear case: it knows perfectly well that a man sleeps and wakes, and requires the prayer through both. See prima-materia#440."@en .

pm:embodiedLocus a pm:AwarenessLocus,
        pm:AwarenessValue ;
    rdfs:label "Im Körper"@de,
        "Embodied"@en ;
    skos:definition "Awareness is located where the body is, whatever it perceives at a distance."@en ;
    skos:note "Distant sight reported from a motionless practitioner is embodied. The axis asks where the seer is, not how far he sees."@en .

pm:protocolledPractice a pm:Attesting ;
    rdfs:label "Protokollierte Praxis"@de,
        "Protocolled practice"@en ;
    skos:definition "A practice repeated under a stated procedure that records its failures alongside its successes."@en .

pm:transientState a pm:AwarenessValue,
        pm:StateDuration ;
    rdfs:label "Vergehend"@de,
        "Transient"@en ;
    skos:definition "The source has the condition pass of itself, so that it must be entered again rather than merely kept."@en ;
    skos:note "A capacity said to come and go is a different object of examination from one said to last: it is tested against frequency and occasion, not against a course of training."@en .

pmp:AbodaZaraSlaughterForRain a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Einen Menschen fuer den Goetzen schlachten, der im Traum darum bat"@de,
        "Slaughtering a man for the idol that asked for it in a dream"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Aboda zara 55a, Raba b. Jishaq to R. Jehuda on the temple in their locality, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. VII (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1903), p. 991" ;
    skos:definition "The operation Raba b. Jishaq reports from his own locality, in the second person to R. Jehuda and as a present practice: there is a temple of an idol there which, when the world is in want of rain, appears to them in a dream and tells them to slaughter a man for it, and they do so. The procedure is named by its occasion, its prompting and its one act; no officiant, no formula and no time of day is given, and the report gives none because it is a question about an observed sequence and not an instruction."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a practice although the transmission that reports it forbids it absolutely and on pain of death. The class says what somebody does and not whether a transmission approves; the prohibition stands at pmc:SanhedrinTheSorcererIsStoned in the first file, and the condemnation is the reason the report is credible as a report: the speaker is not advertising."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliAbodaZara ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmp:AegyptiaQuattuorFlammae a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Zeremonie der vier lodernden Flammen"@de,
        "The ceremony of the four blazing flames"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 137A, rubric, Papyrus of Nu, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London 1901, vol. II, pp. 417-419" ;
    skos:definition "Four fires made of afma cloth anointed with hatet unguent of Thehennu are placed in the hands of four men who have the names of the pillars of Horus written upon their shoulders, and are burnt in the beautiful rays of Ra. The chapter is recited over them. Four further rubrics hang on the same chapter, each prescribing an object walled into one side of the tomb: a Tet of crystal on a green clay brick in the west wall, and three more."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmp:AegyptiaSeptemAritae a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Durchgang der sieben Arits: die drei Namen wissen und die Anrede sprechen"@de,
        "The passage of the seven arits: knowing the three names and saying the address"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 147, Papyrus of Ani (British Museum No. 10,470, sheets 11 and 12), in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II, pp. 473-479" ;
    skos:definition "The operation chapter 147 prescribes. At each of the seven arits the dead man is given three names — the doorkeeper, the watcher, the herald — and then says an address of his own, in which he states what he has done for Osiris and asks that the way be opened. The naming and the address are the whole of it. No object is handled, no material is named, no time of day is set, and no assistant is required."@en ;
    skos:note "The structure is that of chapter 125 with the dialogue removed. There the doorway asks and the dead man answers; here the answers are supplied in advance and there is no question. Which of the two is the older arrangement this run cannot say, and the note is here rather than in a claim because nothing in either translation bears on it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmp:ArsNotoriaOratioPhysicae a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Oration der Heilkunst, vor dem Kranken gesprochen"@de,
        "The oration of the physical art said before the sick"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), §§ 26-30a, pp. 24-25, ed. Peterson" ;
    skos:definition "Standing before a sick person who lies languishing and saying the oration of the physical art three times with great reverence, and then asking him how he feels himself."@en ;
    skos:note "The asking is part of the procedure and not an afterthought. Without it the oration yields nothing that could be read, and with it the reading is fixed in advance: the text sets out the answers and what each of them decides."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmp:AsipuAnaMarsiTehu a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Abfolge am Krankenbett"@de,
        "The bedside sequence at the patient"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Udug-hul 3: 129-136 (canonical Utukku Lemnutu; critical edition M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons, BAM 8, De Gruyter 2016)" ;
    skos:definition "The ordered series of acts the exorcist names in the first person as what he does at the patient: he approaches him, studies his flesh, examines his limbs, sprinkles the water of Ea on him, frightens him, slaps his cheek, calls out over him, and casts the Eridu incantation."@en ;
    skos:note "Eight acts in one uninterrupted conditional chain, each introduced by when. Two of them are examination and not treatment — studying the flesh and examining the limbs — and they stand before every operative act in the list. That is the order the diagnostic tradition of this craft presupposes, stated inside an incantation rather than in a handbook."@en,
        "Two of the acts are done to the patient and not for him: he is frightened and his cheek is slapped. The edition read remarks that it is not clear why the exorcist needed to frighten the patient and suggests it was to alarm the demon. That is the editor's reading and not the text's, and it is recorded here as his and not entered as a claim."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmp:AsipuRamanaPasasu a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Sich salben, bevor man an den Kranken herantritt"@de,
        "Anointing oneself before approaching the patient"@en ;
    dcterms:source "KAR 31 (VAT 8803) rev. 30-31 (M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons, BAM 8, De Gruyter 2016, p. 40)" ;
    skos:definition "The ritual appended to the Assur incantation: grinding up male and female nikiptu-plant, mixing it in honey and ghee, and anointing oneself with it at the moment of approaching the patient."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmp:AsiputuLamadu a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Korpus studieren, bis es beherrscht ist"@de,
        "Studying the corpus until it is mastered"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The Exorcist's Manual, KAR 44: 36 (Geller, in Steinert (ed.), Assyrian and Babylonian Scholarly Text Catalogues, BAM 9, De Gruyter 2018, p. 301)" ;
    skos:definition "The first stage of the training the manual prescribes: studying the listed compositions until the student masters the entire corpus of purification and discovers the secrets."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmp:AsiputuSitulti a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die zweite Stufe: die Listen, dann die widerstreitenden Ansichten"@de,
        "The second stage: the lists, then the conflicting views"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The Exorcist's Manual, KAR 44: 37-40 (Geller, in Steinert (ed.), Assyrian and Babylonian Scholarly Text Catalogues, BAM 9, De Gruyter 2018, p. 301)" ;
    skos:definition "What the manual prescribes afterwards: by way of the lexical lists, the translations and the synonym lists, to grasp how to examine Sumerian and Akkadian rituals; and when the tithes, the chills, the anxiety, the celestial omen series and the terrestrial omen series are understood, to think through and consider the conflicting views."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmp:AyurvedaBhallatakaksira a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Bhallataka-Kur in Milch, mit ihrer Rampe gezählter Früchte"@de,
        "The bhallataka course in milk, with its ramp of counted fruits"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.2.33-39, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, pp. 1038-1039" ;
    skos:definition "Bhallataka fruits unharmed, undiseased and full in juice, size and energy, coloured like ripe jambolan, gathered in the month of Suci or Sukra, buried in a heap of barley or of phaseolus for four months and taken out in the month of Sahas or Sahasya. Before the course the body is nourished with cooling, oily and sweet food. Ten fruits are powdered and cooked in eight times their measure of water down to an eighth, strained and drunk with milk, the mouth first wetted with ghee. The number is raised by one fruit a day until it reaches thirty and then lowered by one a day until it reaches ten again."@en ;
    skos:note "The one prescription in the lesson that states a quantity as a count, a schedule as a series and a ceiling as a number. It is also the drug the lesson calls keen and like fire, which is the nearest the section comes to saying that a rasayana can do harm."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:AyurvedaKutipravesika a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Weg, der in die Hütte eintritt"@de,
        "The route that enters the hut"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.1.16-20 and I.4.49-50, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, parts XXXIII-XXXV, Calcutta 1904-1905, pp. 1017 and 1065" ;
    skos:definition "The first of the two methods. A chamber is built on good ground in an inhabited place, to the east or north of it, wide and spacious and high, enclosed by two outer sets of apartments, its eyes — its doors and windows — small, its walls thick, adapted to the season, not gloomy, pleasing to the mind, impenetrable to all inauspicious sounds, barred entirely against the access of women, and equipped with physicians, medicines and Brahmans. The user enters it shaven, after worship, on a day the calendar has qualified, and takes the rasayana inside it."@en ;
    skos:note "The lesson calls this method certainly superior and adds in the same sentence that it is exceedingly difficult of observance. The comparison is made on the ground of difficulty and of what the user can meet, not on a ground of dignity, which is the shape the holding's generalization about comparative judgements describes."@en,
        "The specification is of a place and not of a state, and every item of it can be checked by a bystander: the orientation, the thickness of the wall, the size of the openings, the two outer rings of rooms. That is unusual in this holding and it is worth saying plainly, because the outcome the place is built for cannot be checked at all."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:AyurvedaSilajaturasayana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Silajatu-Kur, mit ihren vier Arten, drei Längen und drei Gaben"@de,
        "The silajatu course, with its four kinds, its three lengths and its three doses"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.3.52-65, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIV, Calcutta 1905, pp. 1052-1054" ;
    skos:definition "Silajatu, held to be the impurity of gold, silver, copper and black iron dissolved by the heat of the sun on the mountain, each kind identified by taste, colour, potency and post-digestive taste, and all of them smelling of cow's urine. It is soaked in a lukewarm decoction of drugs that allay the excited humours, dried in the sun, and soaked again, seven days in all; then taken with iron powder and milk. The course is of three lengths — seven weeks, three weeks, one week — and the dose of three measures — one pala, half a pala, two tolas — the length and the measure chosen by the strength of the user."@en ;
    skos:note "The one preparation in the lesson with a stated grading on both axes at once, length and quantity, and with a rule for reading the grade off the user. It is also the one with an interdicted food attached; see pmc:AyurvedaSilajatukulattha."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:AyurvedaVatatapika a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Weg von Wind und Sonne, der nichts an einen Tag bindet"@de,
        "The route of wind and sun, which binds nothing to a day"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.1.16 and I.4.49-50, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, parts XXXIII-XXXV, Calcutta 1904-1905, pp. 1017 and 1065" ;
    skos:definition "The second of the two methods, named in the same verse as the first and defined by the translator as its reverse: vata is wind and atapa the heat of the sun, and the user is not commanded to shut himself up within any closed chamber. The lesson assigns it in the fourth section to everyone who does not meet the conditions of the first — those who are not strong, not free from disease, not intellectual, not self-controlled, not forgiving, not possessed of men and riches — and states that for them the ordinance about entering a hut does not apply."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the finding of the run for the calendar question and it is worth stating exactly. The whole of the lesson's calendar apparatus — the northern course of the sun, the lighted fortnight, the propitious lunation and constellation, the favourable muhurta and karana, the auspicious day — stands at the entry into the hut and at no other point of the operation. The second route is prescribed in the same lesson, for the larger class of users, and carries no date at all. A transmission that binds an operation to a calendar here also carries its own release from it, and the release is not a later gloss or a rival school but the alternative the same passage offers."@en,
        "What it does not say, and the distinction decides how much it is worth. It does not say that the operation works on any day. It says that a user who cannot meet the conditions of the hut takes the drug outside, exposed to sun and wind, and it states no calendar for that. The release is by omission and not by denial, which is exactly the difference between this case and a transmission that refuses the binding in so many words."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:BengeCorroborativeTest a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der bestätigende zweite Test am Giftorakel"@de,
        "The corroborative second test at the poison oracle"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 138-140" ;
    skos:definition "A second question, put with a second fowl, in which the oracle is told what its first answer was and asked to confirm it by the opposite outcome. The first test is called bambata sima and the second gingo. If the first fowl died the second must survive, and if the first survived the second must die."@en ;
    skos:note "The two tests may be made at one seance where the poison suffices, and are often separated by weeks or months. The transmission names six situations in which a single test is accepted instead, among them the case where the answer came decisively and the case where a lesser oracle had already been asked; but two tests are said to be indispensable in any question concerning the relations between two persons, and especially where a legal issue is involved."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmp:BonTsheBslu a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Lebensloskauf und der Tausch zweier gleicher Dinge"@de,
        "The life-ransom and the exchange of two equal things"@en ;
    dcterms:source "gZi brjid, the Way of the Shen of Prediction (phya-gshen theg-pa), the third of its four parts, on the rites, in D. L. Snellgrove, The Nine Ways of Bon: Excerpts from gZi-brjid, London Oriental Series 18, London: Oxford University Press, 1967" ;
    skos:definition "The rite within the third part that the work describes at most length. Against devils, fiends, she-demons, spirits of death, the devils that attack a man's length of days, the sprites that cause impediments and the devils that attack the life-force, the operator establishes life-ransoms, life-pledges and amulets, and pays the debts of evil with a life-ransom as payment for life. For the Exchange Rite he prepares the ritual devices and items: a figurine of the right size as ransom for the patient's body, the sky symbol, the tree symbol, the arrow, the distaff, the ritual stakes, a male and a female figure, the rock-plant mtshe and mustard-seed, a model of the house and its wealth and the things one desires. He salutes the hosts of buddhas, offers and prays for refuge, and then offers the items of ransom, explaining them truly."@en ;
    skos:note "The work states the condition on which the exchange works and it is a condition on the items and not on the operator's state: if they are exchanged as equal things the ransom will be good, if they are transposed as equivalents they will be chosen as payment. A ransom in the wrong measure is therefore a named way for the rite to be wrong, and it is checkable by anyone who can see the figurine and the body."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GziBrjid ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bon .

pmp:BouitiDibohaInitiation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Eintritt in den Bouiti: drei Tage Diboha-Wurzel, dann der Spiegel"@de,
        "Entry into the Bouiti: three days of the Diboha root, then the mirror"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Le Scao, Au pays de Sette Cama: la religion des habitants. Les fétiches: le Bouiti, Messager du St Esprit, 1908-1909, the section on the initiation" ;
    skos:definition "The procedure as the article gives it. A man decided to enter the Bouiti goes to the Ganga, who fixes a date. For three days the Ganga has the postulant chew roots of a shrub called Diboha and has him drink a decoction of the same roots in repeated doses. The postulant soon grows drowsy and falls into a state of lethargy and complete absorption; he is dead drunk. A liana is then passed round his neck. Only after three days does he come a little to himself, unless he never comes back. Whoever has borne the ordeal of the Diboha more valiantly is then invited by the Ganga to look into the mirror set in the belly of the fetish."@en ;
    skos:note "The identification of the plant is the mediator's and it contradicts the one the same transmission is known for. Trilles' footnote to the word reads: Strychnos Icaja, of the Strychneae; a dangerous poison at high dose, at low dose its effects are analogous to those of indian hemp. Tabernanthe iboga is not named here, and where Trilles does name it, at page 271, it is for the Fang mélan and not for this. Whether the Diboha of Sette Cama is iboga under a cognate name or a second plant is not decided by anything read in this run."@en,
        "The vernacular name is an optical reading and not a settled spelling. The full text gives Diboha twice, in the account and in the case of the boy at Koja. The confusions of b with h and of b with g are both common in this scan, and the cognate forms current in the Gabonese languages are of the shape diboga and dibuga, so the printed form may be Diboba or Diboga. The identifier follows the reading as the file delivers it; what would settle it is a printed copy, which is the same open item the holding already carries for pmp:Hanblapi."@en,
        "Three days is said twice and means two different things. The Ganga administers the root for three days, and the postulant returns to himself only after three further days. Nothing in the passage says what is done with him in the interval beyond the liana round the neck, and nothing says who decides that he has borne it valiantly."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AuPaysDeSetteCamaLeBouiti ;
    pm:readVia "H. Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan (Münster i. W.: Aschendorffsche Verlagsbuchhandlung, 1912), p. 51, where the article is printed in quotation with Trilles' own footnotes" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Bwiti .

pmp:ConfessionBeforeTheIchuri a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Beichte während des Fastens"@de,
        "The confession made during the fast"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V" ;
    skos:definition "During the fast everyone confesses to the holder of that office, both sitting on the ground, at places appointed in the open country. What is confessed is theft, ill-treatment of others, having more than one woman, adultery, and failure to attend to the huacas; inward faults are not confessed, and simple fornication is not held a fault at all. Powders of the offerings are laid on a flat stone and the penitent is made to blow them; a small stone called pasca, which means pardon, is rubbed on his head with ground white maize, and the head is washed in a stream or where two rivers meet, which is called tincuna."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also done outside the feasts, when a man is sick, because they understand that their ancestors and huacas are angry at their faults and that this is why they fall ill. Some purify themselves without telling anyone, by rubbing the head with the pasca and washing it in a river, saying that the water carries the faults away; and one man said the specialist took him and his wife to a large ditch, washed them, made them put on new clothes and leave the old ones on the road, saying that their faults stayed there and that whoever took the clothes would take them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmp:Dieshi a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Abzählen der Schafgarbenstengel"@de,
        "The counting of the yarrow stalks"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhouyi, Xici A, the section beginning with the number of the great expansion, in the Wang Bi and Han Kangbo recension; read in the digital text of the Kanseki Repository, which names no printing" ;
    skos:definition "The number of the great expansion is fifty, and forty-nine of them are used. They are divided into two to image the two; one is hung between the fingers to image the three; they are counted through by fours to image the four seasons; the remainder is returned between the fingers to image the intercalary month, and because five years hold two intercalations there are two such returns before the next hanging. Four operations complete a change, eighteen changes complete a hexagram, and the eight trigrams are a small completion."@en ;
    skos:note "The chapter as it stands does not say what the remainder means. It gives four operations and eighteen changes and nowhere says that three changes make one line, nor how a remainder is read off as a value; the string for three changes does not occur in the section at all. The rule that makes the procedure executable is later, and stands in the Song commentary rather than in the canon. This file records what the canon gives and leaves the completion to the issue that carries it, because a node that printed the Song rule as canonical would report as ancient a step the classic does not take."@en,
        "Two numbers stand in the same passage and only one of them is the famous one. The stalks of the two parts are eleven thousand five hundred and twenty, matching the number of the things; that is in the text. Four thousand and ninety-six is not, and belongs to a commentary."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination .

pmp:DreamYoga a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Traumyoga"@de,
        "Dream yoga"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005), the yoga of the dream state" ;
    skos:definition "The training of awareness within the dream, set out in the treatise as a course of exercises in apprehending, multiplying and transforming what appears."@en ;
    skos:note "Tibetan rmi lam, transliterated milam. Older witnesses stand behind the fifteenth-century treatise: the dream-yoga manuals in the collected works of Gampopa (1079-1153) and the root verses ascribed to Tilopa. That ascription is traditional and only partly secured philologically. Note also that the list of the six is unstable between witnesses, and that the name six yogas is a western coinage for what the tradition calls six dharmas."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:TibetanTantric .

pmp:EthiopianAsmatRecitedAtTheDoors a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Namen, gesprochen vorn und an den Türen"@de,
        "The names said at the front and at the doors"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, prayer I, p. 216" ;
    skos:definition "Reciting the list of names at the front and at the doors before entering a house which is old, or in ruins, or unclean."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic .

pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicAsterionBeeren a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Beeren des asterion, gegessen bei abnehmendem Mond in der Jungfrau"@de,
        "Berries of asterion eaten at the waning moon in Virgo"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Herbarium Apuleii Platonici, chapter LXI, in Oswald Cockayne, Leechdoms, Wortcunning and Starcraft of Early England, vol. I (Rolls Series 35, London 1864), Old English text and translation, p. 165" ;
    skos:definition "Taking the berries of the wort called asterion and giving them to be eaten when the moon is on the wane, and that when the course of the sun is in the constellation named the Virgin, which is the month called August; and hanging the same wort on the sufferer's neck."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LeechdomsCockayne ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicFallendeKrankheitZettel a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der rückwärts geschriebene Zettel gegen die fallende Krankheit"@de,
        "The reversed slip against the falling sickness"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 27",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 17" ;
    skos:definition "Writing backwards on a slip of paper the words that all is accomplished, once only and not more; laying the writing in a little scarlet-red cloth and a linen cloth over it; and hanging it on unbeschrauen on the first Friday in the new light, with the linen cloth and the thread unbleached."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1820,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicWachholderbusch a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der gebogene Wachholderbusch"@de,
        "The bent juniper bush"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 90",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 57",
        "Romanus-Büchlein, in Johann Scheible (ed.), Das Kloster, weltlich und geistlich, vol. III (Stuttgart 1846), pp. 489-523, here p. 516" ;
    skos:definition "Going before sunrise to a juniper bush, bending it towards the rising sun with the left hand while telling it that it will be bent and pressed until the thief has carried the stolen goods back, then laying a stone on the bush and the skull of a malefactor beneath stone and bush."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1820,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850,
        pmw:RomanusBuechlein ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmp:FinnicSyynTiedustus a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Formel, wenn der Ursprung nicht bekannt ist"@de,
        "The formula said when the origin is not known"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John Abercromby, The Pre- and Proto-historic Finns, vol. II (London: David Nutt, 1898), § 5 a-c, pp. 73-75" ;
    skos:definition "The formula for the case in which the operator does not know what he is dealing with. Its speaker says outright that he does not know who the maker of the hurt is, and undertakes to get to know its origin and to ascertain its birthplace; it then puts the alternatives to the hurt itself, whether it is the Creator's work or a man's."@en ;
    skos:note "A transmission whose doctrine is that knowing the origin gives power, and which carries a standing procedure for not knowing it, has admitted the ordinary case into its own apparatus. The formula is not an emergency measure: it has a number and a place in the order of recitation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:FinnicIncantation .

pmp:GlamDicenn a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Glám dícenn, die Satire auf der Höhe der sieben Gebiete"@de,
        "Glám dícenn, the satire performed on the hill of seven lands"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Mittelirische Verslehren III, section 155, ed. Rudolf Thurneysen, Irische Texte III/1 (Leipzig: Hirzel, 1891), pp. 96-97, with his German at pp. 124-125; the same passage from the Book of Ballymote p. 284a, ed. and trans. Whitley Stokes, The Second Battle of Moytura, Revue Celtique 12 (1891), pp. 119-121" ;
    skos:altLabel "glam digend"@en,
        "glám dícenn"@en ;
    skos:definition "The procedure the verse tract sets out for the case in which a prince withholds from the poet the fixed reward for a poem delivered. There is fasting on the land of the king for whom the poem was composed, and a council of thirty laymen and thirty bishops and thirty poets about the making of the satire afterwards; and it was a crime for them to prevent the satire once the reward had been refused. The poet then goes in a company of seven, himself and six holding the six poetic grades, at sunrise, to a hilltop on the boundary of seven lands. The face of each grade is turned towards his own land and the face of the ollam towards the land of the king he would satirise; the backs of all are turned to a hawthorn growing on the top of the hill; the wind is from the north; each man holds a slingstone and a thorn of the hawthorn in his hand; each sings a stave in the prescribed metre into the two objects, the ollam first and the rest together after him; and each puts his stone and his thorn at the butt of the hawthorn."@en ;
    skos:note "Six conditions that can be got wrong and that a bystander could check: the hour, the place, the number and grades of the company, the direction each man faces, the direction of the wind, and the objects in the hands. The transmission attaches no consequence to getting any one of them wrong, which is why the prerequisite below is entered at prescribed order; but it has specified enough that a performance could be said not to have been this performance."@en,
        "The occasion is a debt and not an enmity. The procedure is triggered by the refusal of a stipulated payment, the council is convened about making the satire and not about whether to make it, and preventing it after the refusal is itself an offence. The transmission has put the operation inside a legal remedy."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InsularCeltic .

pmp:HermeticVitaPia a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das fromme Leben unter der Gegenwart des Mind"@de,
        "The pious life under the presence of Mind"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Corpus Hermeticum I.22 (trans. G. R. S. Mead)" ;
    skos:definition "The conduct the treatise describes for those with whom Mind is present: living piously, pure and merciful, winning the Father's love by pure lives, giving thanks and chanting hymns intent on him with ardent love, and turning from the body's sensations before giving it up to its proper death."@en ;
    skos:note "Mead's Mind is here an agent and not a faculty: it is said to be present with the good, to become an aid, to close up the entrances as a door-keeper and to cut off the mental actions that base energies induce. What the practitioner does and what is done to him are not separated in the passage."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hermetic .

pmp:HippocratesOssisRadulatio a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Abschaben des Knochens mit dem Schabeisen"@de,
        "Scraping the bone with the raspatory"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, On Injuries of the Head 10 and 14, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, pp. 452-453 and 459-460" ;
    skos:definition "The step that establishes whether the operation is required, and the treatise insists on it because the thing to be found cannot be seen. The bone is scraped to a depth and length proportionate to the wound and again transversely, for the sake of the fractures which are not seen and the contusions which are not discovered; where an indentation is present, the dint itself and the surrounding bone are scraped. The chapter also gives a staining step for the case that remains unclear: black ink is put into the wound, the wound is dressed, and on the next day the bone is cleaned and scraped again, when a fracture line takes the colour."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the one place in the two treatises where the corpus states a procedure for making an invisible finding visible, rather than a sign to be read off. It is also where the sounding probe is expressly ruled out: chapter 10 says the probe will not find out a contusion or a fissure that does not show, with the exception of sounding — that is, it names its own instrument as the wrong one for this question."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeVulneribusCapitis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine .

pmp:HippocratesUvulaeResectio a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Abtragen oder Skarifizieren der vergrößerten Uvula"@de,
        "Cutting or scarifying the enlarged uvula"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, The Book of Prognostics 23, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, p. 254" ;
    skos:definition "The one operation the Prognostic prescribes with a time condition on it: the enlarged uvula is cut off or scarified, but only once it has changed in a stated way. The treatise distinguishes the variety it calls Uva, which is to be operated on when the whole of it is converted into an abscess, from the one it calls Columella, which is to be operated on when its extremity is larger and round and its upper part thinner."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PrognosticonHippocratis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine .

pmp:HittHukmaiHantitassu a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der neuntägige Ritus des Herrn des Wortes bei verkürzten Jahren"@de,
        "The nine-day rite of the lord of the word for shortened years"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Ḫantitaššu, CTH 395.1, KBo 11.14 Vs. I 3-Rs. IV 23 (critical edition, exemplar A, hethiter.net/: CTH 395.1, textus 2015-06-16)" ;
    skos:definition "The procedure: a table is set on a roof facing the Sun-god and loaded with soldier-bread strewn with every kind of seed, a thick loaf of fine wheat, named precious stones and metals, beer-bread, malt, measured handfuls of kappi-grain, and the cut-off tip of the horn of a living plough-ox; incense of cedar, butter, honey and a fourth substance is burnt in a vessel before the table and flour and salt are strewn on it; the lord of the word recites to the Sun-god, the recitation is said three times at one place and the incantation seven times at another; at evening something is deposited in the inner chamber of a house, at night a pit is dug by the bolt-wood and a piglet is slaughtered down into it with meat, pastry and a threefold libation of wine for the olden gods; and the ritual patron carries the enchanted things for seven days, after which they are taken off, laid in his linen chest and deposited in the seal-house, the whole ending on the ninth day with a washing."@en ;
    skos:note "Read without a translation beside it. Both editions that carry one are in print; what was read beside the original is the editors' German summary of contents, paragraph by paragraph, which confirms this order of acts and the counts. Where the summary and the transliteration do not reach, this description stops."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hantitassu ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual .

pmp:HittMugessarUliliyassi a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die dreitägige Anrufung der Uliliyašši mit dem Durchgang durch das Schilftor"@de,
        "The three-day invocation of Uliliyašši with the passage through the reed gate"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Paškuwatti, CTH 406, KUB 7.8+KUB 9.27(+)KUB 7.5 Vs. I 4-Rs. IV 1 (critical edition, exemplar A, hethiter.net/: CTH 406, Expl. A, textus 2012-05-07)" ;
    skos:definition "The procedure: rations are heaped and loaded with sweet loaves, figs, dried grapes, the god's meal, the fleece of an unblemished sheep, a jug of wine and the man's own headdress or shirt; a girl of the household carries them, the ritual patron walks behind having bathed and bathes again, and they are taken out to open country; a gate of reeds is built and a cord of red and white wool twined; a spindle and a mirror are put in the patron's hand and he passes under the gate, and as he comes out they are taken from him and a bow and arrows given to him, with the words that womanhood has been taken from him and manhood given back; the deity is then invoked three times a day, at dawn, at midday and at nightfall, for three days, with the same words and a fresh set of loaves each time; on the third day a sheep is consecrated to her and cut up before the table; and at night a bed is made for the patient in front of the table and the garment that lay on the rations is spread out, and he lies down."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Paskuwatti ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual .

pmp:IfaCasting a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Wurf der sechzehn Palmnüsse"@de,
        "Casting the sixteen palm nuts"@en ;
    dcterms:source "A. B. Ellis, The Yoruba-Speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1894), chapter II, the section on Ifa" ;
    skos:definition "The operation by which a figure is obtained: sixteen palm nuts are held loosely in one hand and thrown through the half-closed fingers into the other. One nut left behind is marked with a double stroke and two nuts left behind with a single stroke, and the throw is repeated until two columns of four marks stand on the whitened board."@en ;
    skos:note "The mark is set by what remains in the hand and not by what the diviner judges, so the figure is not chosen. The source describes the mechanism and does not draw that consequence itself; the observation is recorded here and not asserted as the source's."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ifa .

pmp:InuitSpiritCalling a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Geisterrufen"@de,
        "Spirit calling"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), pp. 24, 28" ;
    skos:definition "The session in which a shaman calls up his helping spirits and puts a question to them, held in the household on the occasion that requires it rather than at a fixed time."@en ;
    skos:note "The portion read gives the occasions and the outcomes and not the procedure. Nothing was found here about the posture, the words, the darkness or the binding that the later chapters of this volume are known to describe, and none of that is asserted by this node."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InuitAngakkuq .

pmp:IuratusMundacioPrima a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die erste Reinigung"@de,
        "The first purification"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber iuratus Honorii, cap. L (London, British Library, Royal MS 17 A. xlii, fols. 44v-45r), in the sixteenth-century English translation as printed in the digital edition of Joseph H. Peterson, 1998/2009, rev. 2024, with the Latin of the same chapter given there from Sloane MS 3854" ;
    skos:definition "The three-day opening of every operation of the book: on a Friday, after true penitence and confession, a fast of bread and water; before daybreak the ten orations numbered twenty-three to thirty-two said gently and intently; then a pause in which the petition is rehearsed, then the eight terms with a pause and the petition rehearsed at the end of each; the whole said again about the third hour and again about midday, after which the operator may dine. The Saturday repeats the Friday. The Sunday repeats it without the fast, with fish or flesh eaten moderately."@en ;
    skos:note "The hour-count is the part a later reader will want: three performances a day over three days, with the petition rehearsed at the end of each of the eight terms. The book is specific about this and vague about almost nothing else in the sequence."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberIuratusHonorii ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmp:JainaDvadashavarshaNirodha a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die zwölf Jahre der Vernachlässigung des Leibes"@de,
        "The twelve years of neglecting the body"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Kalpa Sūtra 117, with the parallel at Ācārāṅga Sūtra II.15.23-24 (Jacobi, SBE 22, Oxford 1884; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 1964)" ;
    skos:definition "The resolution the Kalpa Sūtra and the fifteenth lecture of the second book of the Ācārāṅga put in the founder's mouth on entering the order: I shall for twelve years neglect my body and abandon the care of it; I shall with equanimity bear, undergo and suffer all calamities arising from divine powers, men or animals."@en ;
    skos:note "A term the practitioner sets himself before he begins, stated as a number of years and not as a condition to be reached. It is the only practice in what was read whose duration is fixed in advance."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmp:JainaSuddhapanaya a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der reine Trank: sechs Monate reine Nahrung auf Erde, Brettern und Darbha-Gras"@de,
        "The pure drink: six months of pure food on earth, planks and darbha grass"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Viyāhapannatti (Bhagavaī) XV, Calcutta edition p. 1257" ;
    skos:altLabel "suddha-pāṇaya"@en ;
    skos:definition "The last of the things the book has Gosāla lay down as substitutes for drink, and the only one of them that is a course rather than a gesture: one eats pure food for six months, lying successively, for two months at a time, on the bare earth, on wooden planks and on darbha grass."@en ;
    skos:note "Attributed by the book to the founder of another sect and reported by his opponents, which is how nearly everything in the fifteenth book stands. The other seven substitutes are momentary — clutching a cool or wet vessel without drinking from it, pressing an unripe fruit or a green bean with the mouth without drinking its juice — and this one runs half a year and ends in a named event. The list therefore puts a regimen among gestures, which is the reason it is entered separately."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "A. F. Rudolf Hoernle, The Uvāsagadasāo, vol. II, Appendix I (Bibliotheca Indica, Calcutta 1888), pp. 8-9" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmp:JeuMysteriumRemissionis a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Mysterium der Sündenvergebung, einmal vollzogen"@de,
        "The mystery of the forgiveness of sins, performed once"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Das zweite Buch Jeû, Handschriftenseiten 58 und 69-70, deutsche Übersetzung in Carl Schmidt, Gnostische Schriften in koptischer Sprache aus dem Codex Brucianus, Texte und Untersuchungen VIII 1-2, Leipzig 1892, S. 197 und 206-207" ;
    skos:definition "The one operation the text counts. Every man who wishes to believe in the kingdom of light must perform the mystery of the forgiveness of sins only once; whoever receives any of these mysteries must receive it; and it is called the great mystery of the Unapproachable, which is in the treasuries of the inner of the inner, and the whole salvation of the soul. What the operation consists in is nowhere stated: no incense, no seal, no cipher and no prayer is given for it, and the promise to give its apologies, its seal, its cipher and their interpretations is repeated three times and never kept, the text breaking off before it."@en ;
    skos:note "A count of one is a count, and it is the only one in this corpus that is not a cipher to be held in the hand. It is also the reason the omission is a finding rather than an accident of length: an operation prescribed once, called the whole salvation of the soul, and withheld, is the shape of something reserved for delivery by a person."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu .

pmp:JeuVerbumCognoscere a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Wort mit Gewissheit erkennen"@de,
        "Knowing the word with certainty"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The First Book of Jeu, chapter 2 (trans. V. MacDermot), in the excerpt published by the Gnostic Society Library" ;
    skos:definition "What the living Jesus tells the apostles to do: to receive his word with certainty and to know it, to fulfil it, and thereby to receive the soul from the race of the understanding mind so that it ceases to be earthly and becomes understanding."@en ;
    skos:note "No procedure is given here, and that was worth recording rather than filling: the part of the text this node was read from prescribes none of the seals, ciphers and vowel chains the corpus is famous for. The second book does prescribe them, in detail, and they stand below. What remains true of this passage is that the condition it states is a knowing and a fulfilling, and that the apparatus is nowhere offered as a substitute for it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BooksOfJeu .

pmp:JibaroMaikoaUmartinyu a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Trinken des maikoa in der Traumhütte"@de,
        "Drinking maikoa in the dreaming ranch"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Rafael Karsten, Blood Revenge, War, and Victory Feasts among the Jibaro Indians of Eastern Ecuador, Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 79 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1923), pp. 2-3 and 17" ;
    skos:definition "The operation by which the ancestors are consulted before a killing. The drink called maikoa is prepared from the rind of the bush Datura arborea. The man retires to the forest, remains there three days and three nights fasting strictly, and sleeps in a small dreaming ranch called ayámdai, usually many miles from the houses. At puberty the drink is obligatory and follows the feast kusupani, which lasts three or four days; an older warrior takes it when he wants to know who his enemies are and whether he will be victorious."@en ;
    skos:note "Two drinks are named for the same purpose and the work distinguishes them. Natema, from the stem of the vine Banisteria caapi, is also drunk for divination; maikoa, whose effects are said to be much stronger, is the particular narcotic of warriors. Asked why he drinks maikoa, a Jibaro answers: I do it in order to kill my enemies — Shuara matinyu."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:KarstenBloodRevenge ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JibaroShuara .

pmp:JibaroTandaraMaking a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Anfertigen des Schildes"@de,
        "Making the shield"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Rafael Karsten, Blood Revenge, War, and Victory Feasts among the Jibaro Indians of Eastern Ecuador, Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 79 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1923), pp. 6-7" ;
    skos:definition "The shield, tandara, is wrought of one single piece of a wood the Jibaros call kamaka and the Quichua-speaking Canelos lanchama, light and strong at once, and is always round. While the rough work is being done the maker partly fasts and abstains from his wives. The Canelos observance for the same work is given in full beside it: the rough work takes four days, the man works every day from early morning until noon, fasts and takes neither food nor drink through the whole forenoon, drinks manioc beer only when the day's work is finished, keeps strict silence towards everybody while working, and does not lie with his wife during the four days and nights."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because the rule attached to it is the most nearly falsifiable claim in the work: what is at stake is the condition of a wooden object, and the object is there to be looked at."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:KarstenBloodRevenge ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JibaroShuara .

pmp:Jindan a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Bereiten und Einnehmen des Goldelixiers"@de,
        "Compounding and taking the golden elixir"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 4 (Jindan), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 4" ;
    skos:definition "The compounding of mineral preparations and their ingestion, in the two forms the chapter calls the great essentials: the returned elixir, cycled in a sealed vessel over a graded fire for a counted number of days, and the golden liquor, in which a catty of gold and named accessory substances are laid in the flowery pool for a hundred days without fire until they turn to liquid. The chapter says it has surveyed more than a thousand juan of books on nourishing the nature and on long vision, and that none of them fails to take these two as the great essential."@en ;
    skos:note "The author's own commitment is unusually exposed and is quoted here because the whole file hangs on it: take these and fail to become an immortal, he says, and there have never been immortals since antiquity. A transmission that stakes its existence on one operation is easier to hold to account than one that does not, and this is the strongest such sentence located in the collection so far."@en,
        "The two forms are not equally hard, and the chapter says which is which. The nine elixirs need many accessory drugs, to be had only while the four quarters are open and not when the realm is divided, and a fire kept up day and night for tens of days whose strength must never depart from the fit measure. For the golden liquor only the gold is hard to come by; no fire is raised at all, and the counted days in the pool are enough. The chapter recommends the second on grounds of feasibility and not of power."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Waidan .

pmp:MayaIxYearRemedy a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Abwehr der für ein Ix-Jahr geweissagten Übel"@de,
        "The conjuring of the evils foretold for an Ix year"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Diego de Landa, Yucatan Before and After the Conquest, translated by William Gates (Baltimore: The Maya Society, publication 20, 1937), section XXXVII, pp. 65-66" ;
    skos:definition "The procedure prescribed against the ills foretold for a year whose bearer is Ix. An image of Kinch-ahau Itzamna is set in the temple, incense and many offerings and prayers are made to it, blood is drawn to anoint the stone called Sac-acantun, there is much dancing in which old women take part, and a new oratory is built for the god or the old one renewed."@en ;
    skos:note "The ills the procedure answers are named in the same section and are of two kinds. Some are conditions of the world: shortage of water, hot spells withering the maize, hunger and the theft and enslavement that follow, locusts, a bad year for bread and a good one for cotton. Others are conditions of the polity: discords within the town or with other towns, changes of rule through war, and the failure of men seeking a chieftaincy. A remedy addressed to both at once is not falsified by either."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MayaRitual .

pmp:MideInitiationFourthDegree a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Einweihung in den vierten Grad"@de,
        "Initiation into the fourth degree"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 170" ;
    skos:definition "The last entry: further feasts, the personation of the bear spirit, the sacred drum and the chanted prayers."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin .

pmp:MingjingRushan a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Spiegel, der ins Gebirge mitgenommen wird"@de,
        "The mirror carried into the mountains"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 17 (Dengshe), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 17" ;
    skos:definition "A bright mirror of nine inches across or more, hung at the back, on the stated ground that the old things of the world can borrow a human shape to deceive the eye but cannot alter their true form in a mirror. Where something comes to test the traveller, he looks into the mirror: a transcendent or a good spirit of the mountain shows there as a human form, a bird, a beast or a sprite shows as what it is. The chapter adds a second criterion for the case where the visitor withdraws, which it says such a thing always does backwards: turn the mirror after it and look at its back, and if it is an old sprite it will have no heels, while a thing with heels is a spirit of the mountain."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the second procedure of its kind found in this graph and the first outside the medical canon. It states a fixed instrument, a threshold on that instrument, an act to be performed, and two outcomes that are not the same, and it says what each outcome means. Its criteria differ in strength: the reflection is a claim about what a mirror does, the heels are a claim about what such a thing lacks, and the second could be checked without a mirror at all."@en,
        "Two cases are given with it, and both are third-hand. Zhang Gaita and Ou Gaocheng, refining their thought in a stone chamber on Mount Yuntai in Shu, were visited by a man in a yellow silk gown and a hemp cap; they looked in the mirror, saw a deer, and asked it why an old deer of the mountain dared take a human form, whereupon it became a deer and fled. Xi Boyi, lodging in the haunted pavilion below Mount Linlü where guests had been dying, sat up with the lamps lit reciting a scripture; at midnight ten and more people came and gambled with him; he lit them secretly with the mirror, saw a pack of dogs, singed one with the lamp so that it smelt of burnt hair, then stabbed one, which cried out as a man and died as a dog. The chapter says of the second that it was the power of the mirror."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BaopuziNeipian ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmp:NeidanZhengyanXingchi a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der gestufte Gang der zwölf Verfahren"@de,
        "The graded course of the twelve methods"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhong-Lü chuandao ji, Lun zhengyan 18, in the Chongkan Daozang jiyao recension (JY 149), juan 3, fol. 55a" ;
    skos:definition "The course the corpus sets out as twelve numbered methods in three vehicles, from the matching of yin and yang through the gathering and dispersing of water and fire, the coupling of dragon and tiger, the firing of the medicine, the flying of the metal essence up the spine, the two returns and the two refinings of the form, the refining of breath at the court of the origin, the exchange in inner observation, to the shedding and division of the form."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Neidan .

pmp:NippurHotHerbsOnTheImage a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Scharfe Kräuter auf das Bild streuen und den Namen aussprechen"@de,
        "Sprinkling hot herbs on the image and pronouncing the name"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 28: 2-5 with the commentary, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 211-214)" ;
    skos:definition "The operation of the love-charm, so far as the broken text carries it: take a named substance and hot herbs which they call sunwort, with a further plant and peppers, and the rites of love, and sprinkle them upon the person to be inflamed; and take pieces from his heart. The woman who commissions the bowl is the one who sprinkles."@en ;
    skos:note "The editor reads a simulacrum behind it, because the heart is to be torn in pieces, and takes the herbs for a salad of hot plants expressive of the passion wanted, with the beloved's name formally pronounced over them. The text is mutilated at each of the three points where the substance is named."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:uses pmm:HotHerbsCalledSunwort ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmp:NippurNamingOfClientAndMother a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Den Klienten samt seiner Mutter nennen"@de,
        "Naming the client together with the mother"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, introduction § 10, with texts 10: 1, 12: 1, 8: 6 and 17: 6, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 49-50)" ;
    skos:definition "The individuals a charm is to cover are specified exactly, and the specification is a name plus the name of the mother: so-and-so son or daughter of such-a-one. The father appears instead where the mother is not known."@en ;
    skos:note "The editor sets the rule beside the Talmudic maxim that all repetitive incantations are in the name of the mother, beside the Mandaean use in which a person's sacred name includes the mother's, and beside the same rule in the Greek defixiones. Those parallels are his and are not entered here as places."@en,
        "The same rule holds for the demon when the demon is to be bound: a named lilith is given her parents, and in the divorce-writ the father and mother of the liliths are named as the two parties of a writ must be. Two bowls of this collection name the father of the client rather than the mother."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmp:NorseFremjaSeidinn a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Vollziehen des seiðr vom Gerüst"@de,
        "Performing the seiðr from the platform"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Eirik the Red's Saga, chapter 4, translated by J. Sephton (Liverpool: D. Marples, 1880)",
        "Eiríks saga rauða, chapter 4, in Íslendinga sögur, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:definition "The proceeding at Þorkell's farm in Greenland in a year of dearth: the seeress is fetched and seated on a high seat with a cushion stuffed with hen's feathers; she comes in a blue mantle set with stones to the hem, glass beads at her neck, a black lambskin hood lined with white catskin, a staff with a knob mounted in brass and set with stones, a belt of touchwood carrying a great pouch in which she keeps the charms she needs for her knowledge, shaggy calfskin shoes with long thongs and great tin knobs, and catskin gloves white and furred within; she is given porridge of kid's milk and the hearts of every kind of beast on the place; on the following evening the gear she needs is set up for her, the women strike a ring about the platform and she sits upon it, and the song is sung."@en ;
    skos:note "The belt is a hnjóskulindi, a belt of touchwood with the pouch on it, and not a belt with a tinder-box. Sephton renders it a girdle of soft hair and Vigfusson a girdle of puff-balls; Reeves has touch-wood, which is the word. The divergence is noted because the object is regularly listed in secondary accounts from the weaker translations."@en,
        "The platform is a hjallr in the text of Guðni Jónsson, which follows Hauksbók; the compound seiðhjallr, the word usually quoted for it, is the reading of Skálholtsbók. The Hauksbók line is the one read here and the plain word is what it gives."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmp:NorseSeidrIthrott a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Kunst, die seiðr heißt"@de,
        "The craft called seiðr"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Snorri Sturluson, Ynglinga saga, chapter 7, in Heimskringla, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:definition "The craft Snorri names as the one with which the greatest power went and which Óðinn performed himself, called seiðr, taught by him to the priestesses and, in the same chapter, most of his crafts to the sacrificing priests."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmp:PdmShenHne a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Gefäßbefragung"@de,
        "The vessel-inquiry"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv), col. III/5-15 and col. X/9-19, in F. Ll. Griffith and H. Thompson, The Demotic Magical Papyrus of London and Leiden, vol. I, London: H. Grevel & Co., 1904" ;
    skos:definition "A divination into a vessel of oil. A dark clean room is sprinkled with river sand or purified with natron water; a new dish is filled with clear oil, or a bronze cup with water finished on its surface with oil; new bricks that have not been turned are set under the vessel and under the boy; seven clean loaves and seven lumps of salt are arranged around it; a boy who is pure and has not gone with a woman is laid or seated at the vessel with his eyes shut, and the operator recites the invocation down into his head seven times with his hand over the boy's eyes. The boy then opens his eyes and is asked what he sees, and the questioning proceeds through Anubis, who is sent to fetch the other gods, seat them at a table, give them food and drink, and produce the one god who will answer."@en ;
    skos:note "The procedure is given in this papyrus more than a dozen times with variations of the vessel, the oil, the wick, the hour and the invocation, and the editors' own classification counts eleven places for the vessel alone. What is constant across them is the apparatus: a new or unused object at every point of contact, an odd count of repetitions, a boy at the vessel and the operator behind him."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmp:PesahimCounterFormulaAgainstTheSorceresses a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Was man sagt, wenn man Zauberfrauen begegnet"@de,
        "What to say on meeting sorceresses"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Pesahim 110a, Amemar on what the chief of the sorceresses told him, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. II (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1901), pp. 704-705" ;
    skos:definition "The formula Amemar reports, and he reports it from the chief of the sorceresses herself. On meeting sorceresses one says: hot dung in perforated baskets into your mouths, you sorcerous women; may your bald patch grow balder; may your breadcrumbs be blown away; may your spices fly off; may the wind carry away the fresh saffron you hold, you sorcerous women. The second half of what she told him is a statement of her own position: while they were gracious to me and I was careful I did not come among you; now that I have come among you they are not gracious to me, but I am careful."@en ;
    skos:note "The counter-measure is credited to the opposing party, which is rare enough to be the reason for the node. The holding carries many protections against practitioners and this is one the practitioners are said to have supplied, so the transmission is reporting that the people it condemns told its own teacher how to defend himself. Whether that is a claim about an informant or a literary device is not decidable from the passage, and the node does not decide it."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliPesahim ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmp:RasaJarana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Erschöpfung des Quecksilbers"@de,
        "Exhaustion of mercury"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, jaranam, pp. 132-141" ;
    skos:altLabel "jaranam"@en ;
    skos:definition "The fourteenth operation, and the one the volume treats at most length. The essence of mica and steel ash, each a sixty-fourth of the weight of the mercury, are worked in little by little in a hot mortar; the mercury is then wrapped in a painted birch leaf, boiled in kanji, dried, mixed with an eighth of its weight of bida and heated in a kachchapa jantram. It is repeated with a larger morsel and a stronger fire at each turn."@en ;
    skos:note "The operation carries two checkable clauses. Its success is judged by weight and by fire — properly swallowed, the mercury retains its original quantity and can stand the heat of fire — and it names materials that must not be used in it, with the harm they do."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmp:RasaKarpuraRasa a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Karpura-rasa, das kampferweiße Quecksilber"@de,
        "Karpura-rasa, the camphor-white mercury"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, seventh process, pp. 108-109" ;
    skos:definition "Purified mercury rubbed twelve hours with equal quantities of red ochre, brick, chalk, alum, rock salt, the mud raised by white ants, kshari salt and potter's clay, all finely powdered and sifted; the whole put in a pot covered by a second, the joint closed seven times with rags and mud, dried, and kept on a fire burning ninety-six hours at a stretch. The camphor-white compound is collected from the upper pot."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmp:RasaMakaradhwaja a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Makaradhwaja, die Goldzubereitung"@de,
        "Makaradhwaja, the gold preparation"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, eleventh process, pp. 115-116" ;
    skos:definition "One pala of gold purified and reduced to ash, eight palas of mercury and sixteen palas of sulphur rubbed with the juice of kanya into a black powder, dried, put into a glass flask and heated three days in the sand apparatus; the pollen-like red dust is taken when the apparatus is completely cool. Dose one jaba with a betel leaf."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmp:RosicrucianExaminatioAnteNuptias a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Wache halten und sich selbst betrachten vor der Hochzeit"@de,
        "Keeping watch and examining oneself before the wedding"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chymische Hochzeit Christiani Rosencreutz (Strasbourg, 1616), the First Day, the verse of the invitation" ;
    skos:definition "What the invitation requires of the invited, in five lines of verse and in the imperative: keep watch, examine thyself, and bathe thoroughly. The German of 1616 runs Halt Wacht, Dich selbst betracht, Wirstu dich nit fleissig baden, die Hochzeit kan dir schaden."@en ;
    skos:note "The narrator performs it as an examination of his own understanding, conduct and motives, and finds himself wanting on all three; the bathing is not reported as carried out. What the practice consists in is therefore stated by the invitation and shown by the response, and the two do not fully agree."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:NuptiaeChymicae ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmp:SanhedrinOneWordOverTheField a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Wort ueber ein Feld sprechen"@de,
        "Saying one word over a field"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Babylonian Talmud, Synhedrin 68a, R. Eliezer on his deathbed, trans. Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud, vol. VII (Berlin: S. Calvary und Co., 1903), p. 289" ;
    skos:definition "The operation R. Eliezer reports of himself on his deathbed. Akiba asked him on the road to teach him something about the planting of cucumbers; he said one thing, and the whole field filled with cucumbers; Akiba then asked to be shown how they are pulled up, and he said one thing, and they were all gathered into one place. No material, no implement, no preparation and no time of day is named: the operation is a single utterance, twice."@en ;
    skos:note "Technically this is the same operation as the hundred and thirty-six of the handbook in traditions/hekhalot.ttl, which likewise consist in reciting a named section and letting the effect follow, with the difference that here the words are not written down. The transmission that forbids the art on pain of death reports its own teachers performing it, and the discussion notices: it asks how he was permitted to do this, since we have learnt that the one who performs an act is liable, and answers with the permission to learn."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TalmudBavliSanhedrin ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RabbinicHalakhah .

pmp:ShangqingAnmo a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Reibeordnung"@de,
        "The rubbing regimen"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 9.6b-9.7b (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 9" ;
    skos:definition "A fixed sequence of manipulations of the face and head performed on waking and before sleeping: the palms rubbed hot and drawn over the face, the small hollow behind the eyebrows pressed thrice nine times, the hands drawn up the forehead into the hairline, the ears turned, the eyes pressed at their four corners, the hair combed many times. The corpus gives it as the whole content of a scripture of the Most High and hangs several of its stated intervals on it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmp:ShangqingFuriyue a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Einnehmen der Bilder von Sonne und Mond"@de,
        "Ingesting the images of sun and moon"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 9.22a-9.22b (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 9" ;
    skos:definition "An image the size of a coin held red in the heart with nine rays rising from it to the teeth and turning back into the stomach, the eyes half closed until heart and stomach are seen clearly, then breath expelled and the fluid swallowed thirty-nine times, three times a day; the moon performed at night in the upper cinnabar field with ten white rays. The corpus gives several versions of this and says at 9.21b that the Perfected who have already obtained the Way go on performing it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmp:ShangqingHuangtingSongjing a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Huangting bis zur Zahl rezitieren"@de,
        "Reciting the Huangting to its count"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangting neijing yujing 36 (Muyu zhang) (DZ 331), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "The closing chapter sets the procedure: bathe thoroughly and put aside the fat and the pungent, enter the chamber facing east and recite the jade text; loosen the hair and be without desire; ten readings and four bows make a sitting, the Most High saluted first and the Thearch after, then a turn to the north. The count to be reached is ten thousand."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmp:ShangqingSongjing a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Rezitieren einer Schrift bis zur Zahl"@de,
        "Reciting a scripture to a count"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 9.23b (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 9" ;
    skos:definition "Reading a named text through a stated number of times, the number being the operative quantity: the five thousand words of the Daode jing ten thousand times, the inner scripture of the Yellow Court once each night before sleep, the Great Cavern scripture ten thousand times. The corpus counts the readings rather than the years and converts between the two."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmp:SteinerUebungAmSamen a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Übung am Samenkorn und an der sterbenden Pflanze, mit vorher genannten Farben"@de,
        "The exercise on the seed and the dying plant, with the colours named in advance"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Rudolf Steiner, Wie erlangt man Erkenntnisse der höheren Welten?, chapter Die Stufen der Einweihung, in the German text of 1918" ;
    skos:definition "Two exercises given in sequence. One places oneself before a seed and fills oneself with the thought that what one does not now see will grow from it; what was sensibly invisible reveals itself in a spiritually visible way, and there appears a spiritual flame-formation whose middle is felt as lilac-blue and whose edge is felt as bluish. The other places oneself before a plant at full development and fills oneself with the thought that it will die and leave seeds; after an appropriate time a force develops in the soul which becomes a new perception, and a larger flame grows out of the plant, to be felt as greenish-blue in its middle part and yellowish-red at its outer edge. It is expressly said that these are not colours seen as physical eyes see colours, but that spiritual perception feels something similar to a physical colour impression."@en ;
    skos:note "That self-examination is the one thing in the book which somebody other than the pupil could carry out, and the book does not ask anybody to. Whether he has remained the same man inside his own circumstances is a question his household could answer; it is put to him alone."@en,
        "The book faces the objection and answers it with resolve rather than with a criterion. It is understandable, the same chapter says, that many a man will take all this for illusion, and many will say what are such visions, such phantasms to me, and some will fall away and not continue the path; and it is exactly there that everything depends on not confusing fantasy with spiritual reality, and on having the courage to press forward. The instruction for telling them apart, given two paragraphs later, is that the sound sense which distinguishes truth from deception must be continually cultivated, that the pupil must never lose full conscious mastery of himself, and that he should examine again and again whether he has fallen out of his equilibrium and remained the same man within the circumstances in which he lives."@en,
        "The sign is announced before the exercise, in the same sentence as the instruction, down to the two colours and their position in the figure. Whatever a pupil then reports, the report was prescribed. That is a property of the procedure and not an accusation: the book is a manual and a manual says what is to be expected. What the holding should record is that this transmission, which publishes its whole method, has no way left of distinguishing a pupil who perceived the flame from one who was told what to perceive."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SteinerWieErlangtMan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Anthroposophy .

pmp:SubidaMeditacionDiscursiva a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Diskursives Meditieren an vorgestellten Formen und Figuren"@de,
        "Discursive meditation on imagined forms and figures"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Juan de la Cruz, Subida del Monte Carmelo II.XI-XII (ed. Gerardo, Toledo 1912, pp. 147-157; II.13-14 in Lewis), read in the Spanish against Lewis 1906/1922 and the Carmelites of Paris, Oudin 1893" ;
    skos:altLabel "discurso"@en,
        "meditacion discursiva"@en ;
    skos:definition "The practice of drawing a knowledge and a love of God by reasoning from imagined forms, images and figures: the operation of the sensitive faculties, in which the practitioner goes from one particular to another and finds relish in it. Each such act is one act; the work is the repetition of them."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SubidaDelMonteCarmelo ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinMysticism .

pmp:SusrutaSastrakarma a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Schnitt, in gemessenem Abstand von einem marman gelegt"@de,
        "The incision, placed at a measured distance from a marman"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Susruta Samhita, Sarirasthana VI.81, in the English of Kaviraj Kunjalal Bhishagratna, An English Translation of the Sushruta Samhita, vol. II, Calcutta 1911, pp. 187-188" ;
    skos:definition "The surgical operation as chapter VI constrains it. An incision is to be made a finger's width away from the Urvi, Kurchcha-sirah, Vitapa, Kaksha and a Parsva marman; a clear space of two fingers is to be left about the Stanamula, Manivandha or Gulpha; three fingers about the Hridaya, Vasti, Kurchcha, Guda or Nabhi; four fingers about the four Sringatakas, the five Simantas and the ten marmas of the neck; and half a finger in respect of the remaining fifty-six."@en ;
    skos:note "The distances are a procedure and not a warning, which is why they are entered here and the consequence of ignoring them at the prerequisite node. The chapter states them as a measured table and then states the rule behind the table: the situation and dimension of each local marman are to be taken into account first and the incision made so as not to affect it."@en,
        "The translator reports two disagreements on the figures and both are left out of the graph because the commentaries were not opened. Some are said to hold that a palm's measure should be left in the case of the remaining fifty-six; and Gayadasa, having learnt from Bhoja, is reported as requiring two fingers for ten named marmas. The disagreement is about the numbers and not about the rule."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SusrutaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:ThelemaScriptumRecordum a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das schriftliche Protokoll jedes Versuchs"@de,
        "The written record of every experiment"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber E vel Exercitiorum sub figura IX, chap. I, §§ 1-4 and 7-9, and chap. VII, § 8, in The Equinox I,1 (London, March 1909), pp. 25 and 34; read in the Celephais Press and O.T.O. re-setting of the ten numbers and collated against a page scan of number one" ;
    skos:altLabel "the magical record"@en ;
    skos:definition "The practice the instruction opens and closes with. Every experiment is to be recorded in detail during or immediately after its performance; the physical and mental condition of the experimenter, the time, the place, the state of the weather and all conditions that might act on the result or be sources of error are to be noted; and the more scientific the record, the better, while the emotions are to be noted as being among the conditions."@en ;
    skos:note "Two features make this more than an exhortation. It carries a sanction: the order will take no official notice of experiments not thus recorded. And it names a published model of the form, a diary printed in the same number of the journal, so that the standard is exhibited rather than described. The instruction returns to it at its end and calls the written record the only possible check upon error derived from the various qualities of the experimenter."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberEVelExercitiorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmp:TungusFormalRecognition a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die förmliche Anerkennung"@de,
        "The formal recognition"@en ;
    dcterms:source "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXII, sect. 95 F, pp. 273-274, and chap. XXVIII, sect. 129-130, pp. 350-352" ;
    skos:definition "The social act by which a person becomes a clan shaman. The candidate must show a will and a proved ability to master spirits in at least one successful performance; the clansmen must want him. There follows a period of trial in which he must help sick clansmen, practise divination and interpret dreams. Only afterwards is he assisted in making the paraphernalia and in offering the regular sacrifice, and only then do people seek his assistance."@en ;
    skos:note "It is an act with an outcome that can go the other way. Among all Tungus groups, he states, there are many persons who pretend to be shamans and are not recognised, so that people do not seek their assistance; a candidate may be rejected before the final trial on grounds of character, and among two candidates one may be elected and the other rejected and cured."@en,
        "The recorder rejects the words consecration and initiation for this act and gives his reason: nothing in it is sacred, and the candidate is already initiated before he attends. The term formal recognition is his and is entered as his; the procedure it names is reported."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SiberianShamanism .

pmp:UgarTsuEnkm a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Augen zu Baʻlu erheben und die Worte sprechen"@de,
        "Lifting the eyes to Baʻlu and saying the words"@en ;
    dcterms:source "RS 24.266:27'-29' (KTU 1.119), in Pardee, Ritual and Cult at Ugarit, WAW 10, 2002, text 46, pp. 149-150" ;
    skos:definition "The prescribed act: the besieged are to lift their eyes to Baʻlu and speak the address that follows, which asks him to drive the strong one from the gate and the warrior from the walls and promises what will be given if he does."@en ;
    skos:note "The whole operation is one posture and one speech. No officiant, no material, no interval and no count, which is why this work is the one in the corpus whose claim could in principle be followed to an outcome: there is nothing in the procedure that could have been done wrongly."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RS24266 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:UgariticRitual .

pmp:VaidikaAgnihotra a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Agnihotra, die tägliche Milchspende"@de,
        "Agnihotra, the daily milk oblation"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa XII,4,1,1 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 44, Oxford 1900, p. 177" ;
    skos:definition "The oblation of milk poured into the fire morning and evening, which the transmission calls a sacrificial session entered upon for life and describes as ensuring death in old age. Three fires are kept for it: the offering fire, the householder's fire and the southern cooking fire."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual .

pmp:VaidikaNivid a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Rezitation der Nivid"@de,
        "The recitation of the Nivid"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aitareya Brāhmaṇa iii,10-11 (xi,10-11), in the English of Arthur Berriedale Keith, Rigveda Brahmanas, Harvard Oriental Series 25, Cambridge 1920, pp. 171-172" ;
    skos:definition "The insertion of a short formula of set sentences into a litany — before it at the morning pressing, in the middle at midday, at the end at the third pressing. The Aitareya calls the Nivids the embryos and the ornaments of the litanies, and recites them by sentences because the gods gathered the sacrifice together by quarters."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AitareyaBrahmana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual .

pmp:VamaWorship a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Verehrung in der heterodoxen Methode"@de,
        "Worship in the heterodox method"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Kālikāpurāṇa, at the verses defining vāmā and prescribing that the worshipper be a vāma while worshipping in that method (130-131) (text, introduction and translation in English by B. N. Shastri, part III, Nag Publishers, Delhi 1991)" ;
    skos:definition "The rite performed as vāma: the worshipper takes the position of one whose goddess claims the share of the offering before all other gods, and performs the worship in that method whether or not he also performs the five sacrifices."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmp:WitchDoctorMedicineEating a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Essen der Medizinen der Hexendoktoren"@de,
        "Eating the medicines of the witch-doctors"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 88-89 and pp. 96-97" ;
    skos:definition "Before a seance the performers eat some of their medicines. The medicine goes into the stomach and is shaken through the body by the dancing, where it is held to become an active agent. The whole apprenticeship consists in the slow transfer of the knowledge of which plants the medicines are made from, paid for in a long string of fees, and a plant is known by stalk and leaf and not by root, which is why laymen may sit at the communal meal without learning anything."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmp:XingqiBaopuzi a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Zirkulation des Qi, wie das achte Kapitel sie darstellt"@de,
        "The circulation of the qi as the eighth chapter sets it out"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 8 (Shizhi), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 8" ;
    skos:definition "One of three things the chapter names as the essentials, beside the guarding of the essence and the taking of the one great medicine. It says of itself that there are several methods within it, that no method is given whole at the first teaching, and that the essential is told only to one who has not slackened."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BaopuziNeipian ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmp:YeridatMerkavah a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Abstieg zur Merkava"@de,
        "The descent to the Merkavah"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hekhalot Rabbati, chapter 26, trans. Morton Smith, corrected by Gershom Scholem, ed. Don Karr" ;
    skos:altLabel "yordei merkavah"@en,
        "yored merkavah"@en ;
    skos:definition "The passage through the palaces, called a descent although the traveller goes up. The transmission does not explain the word anywhere in the passages read."@en ;
    skos:note "The term is kept in the tradition's own form and not translated as ascent, because a rendering that corrected the direction would remove the only trace of whatever the word once meant. That it is nowhere explained is itself worth recording: a technical term that its own literature never glosses has usually come from somewhere else."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HekhalotRabbati ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hekhalot .

pmp:ZhidaoYangsheng a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Regimen derer, die den Weg kannten"@de,
        "The regimen of those who knew the way"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 1 (Shang gu tian zhen lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 1" ;
    skos:definition "The conduct the opening chapter ascribes to the people of high antiquity who knew the way: they took the yin and the yang as their model and were in accord with the arts and the numbers, they were measured in food and drink and regular in rising and resting, and they did not work recklessly. The chapter states the counter-case in the same breath, of the people of the present who take wine for water, take the reckless for the regular, enter the bedchamber drunk, exhaust their essence in desire and are without measure in rising and resting."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmp:ZosimosSedeAdFocum a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Sitze an deinem Herd, ruhe den Körper, widerstehe den zwölf Verhängnissen"@de,
        "Sit at your hearth, rest the body, resist the twelve fatalities"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zosime, Compte final, § 8, französische Übersetzung in M. Berthelot und Ch.-Em. Ruelle, Collection des anciens alchimistes grecs, seconde livraison, Paris 1888, S. 192-193" ;
    skos:definition "What the letter prescribes to Theosebeia in place of what the priests offer her. Do not let yourself be seduced; do not start wandering in search of God, but stay seated at your hearth, and God will come to you, who is everywhere and is not confined to the lowest place as the demons are. Rest your body, calm your passions, resist desire, pleasure, anger, grief, and the twelve fatalities of death. Directing yourself thus, you will call the divine being to you, and the divine being will come to you, who is everywhere and nowhere. Without being called, offer sacrifices — not the sacrifices that profit those men and are meant to feed them and please them, but such as drive them off and destroy them. Do these things until you have become perfect in your soul."@en ;
    skos:note "The counter-prescription is the interesting half. Sacrifices are not forbidden, they are redirected: the ones to offer are those that drive off and destroy the men who live by sacrifices, and the letter names an authority for them. A transmission that answers a cult by prescribing a different sacrifice is saying that the operation was never the problem."@en,
        "The list of what is to be resisted is four states and then a number: desire, pleasure, anger, grief, and the twelve fatalities of death. The four are conditions a person can notice in himself; the twelve are not enumerated in what was read, and the passage gives no way of telling whether one of them is present."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LogosTeleutaios ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GraecoEgyptianAlchemy .

pmt:GaudiyaVaisnava a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Gauḍīya-vaiṣṇavische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Gauḍīya Vaiṣṇava transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Rūpa Gosvāmin, Bhaktirasāmṛtasindhu 2.5.113-115 (trans. Haberman 2003)" ;
    skos:definition "The Bengali Vaiṣṇava transmission descending from Caitanya, which takes over the aesthetic vocabulary of Sanskrit dramaturgy and applies it to devotion, treating the relations in which a devotee stands to the deity as rasa and ranking them."@en ;
    skos:note "Read in this run: the fifth wave of the Southern Quadrant and the third wave of the Western Quadrant of the Bhaktirasāmṛtasindhu, at the wording, in the bilingual edition of two thousand and three, whose Sanskrit stands on the left page and the translation on the right. Left unread: the Eastern and Northern Quadrants entirely, and the remaining waves of the two quadrants opened. The Ujjvalanīlamaṇi and the Prīti-sandarbha were not opened; both are named in the notes of the edition read and would carry the fuller series of the expansions of love."@en,
        "The contact route is documented and not undecided, and it runs to a transmission this graph already holds. The rasa vocabulary of this corpus is taken over from the dramaturgical treatise: the same terms, the same distinction of foundational and transitory states, the same eight secondary rasa. An agreement between this file and traditions/sanskrit-dramaturgy.ttl therefore counts as reception and never as independent attestation. What is worth looking for is what this later station added, and it added the ranking: the dramaturgical treatise arranges its rasa by derivation, deity and colour and does not rank them."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Rūpa Gosvāmin, Bhaktirasāmṛtasindhu; Rūpa Gosvāmin, Ujjvalanīlamaṇi; Jīva Gosvāmin, Prīti-sandarbha" .

pmw:AlfTrisarSuialia a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Alf Trisar Šuialia, die tausendundzwölf Fragen"@de,
        "Alf Trisar Šuialia, the Thousand and Twelve Questions"@en ;
    skos:note "Named here as a work of the corpus and not obtainable in this run. It is the priestly manual, and one of its parts carries the title Mhita u-Asuta, blow and healing, that is, ritual faults and their remedy. Drower's edition of 1960 exists in print; the lending scan answers 401 to its text file and the transliteration at the Comprehensive Aramaic Lexicon refuses automated retrieval. Two claims below are entered second-hand through the field monograph because of this, and they are the two that would be worth checking first once a copy is at hand."@en .

pmw:FetichismInWestAfrica a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Nassau, Fetichism in West Africa"@de,
        "Nassau, Fetichism in West Africa"@en ;
    skos:note "Access: archive.org identifier fetichisminwesta00nass, 961,833 characters over the regular _djvu.txt route. What was read at the wording: page 248, the enumeration of the secret societies of the Gabun region in chapter XV. The volume carries no occurrence of iboga or eboga. The rest is not opened."@en,
        "The edition as the title page in the full text gives it: Fetichism in West Africa, Forty Years' Observation of Native Customs and Superstitions, by Robert Hamill Nassau, forty years a missionary in the Gabun district of Kongo-Francaise, New York, Charles Scribner's Sons, 1904, published October 1904."@en .

pmw:Jataka a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Jātaka"@de,
        "Jātaka"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened on 2026-09-21 at story 49 only, in the English of Robert Chalmers under E. B. Cowell's editorship, volume I of 1895. The remaining five hundred and forty-six stories were not read. All six volumes are public domain and reachable by the regular route, which makes this the cheapest continuation for anything the canon states as doctrine and the Jātaka reports as a case."@en .

pmw:SamyuttaNikaya a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Saṁyutta Nikāya"@de,
        "Saṁyutta Nikāya"@en ;
    skos:note "Declared here because this run is the first to cite it, and for the same reason as the Aṅguttara: it is a work of the Theravāda canon and stands under pm:corpusWork of this transmission because the opponents' canon is the only corpus this transmission has."@en .

pmw:SyllabusInstructionumAA a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "A Syllabus of the Official Instructions of A∴A∴ Hitherto Published"@de,
        "A Syllabus of the Official Instructions of A∴A∴ Hitherto Published"@en ;
    skos:note "The transmission's own catalogue of its instructions, with the four classes that decide whether a text may be altered. It is the document that makes the edition of every other node in this file a necessary part of the citation."@en .

pmw:ToseftaSanhedrin a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Tosefta, Traktat Sanhedrin"@de,
        "Tosefta, tractate Sanhedrin"@en ;
    skos:note "Read in Danby's translation beside the Mishnah, in the same volume of 1919. Danby's reference for this passage is T. XI.5c; the Vilna division numbers the same passage 11:2."@en .

pmw:VinayaPitaka a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Vinaya Piṭaka"@de,
        "Vinaya Piṭaka"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened at Cūḷavagga V.8, and the edition is now recoverable. The passage was read at the wording on 2026-09-24 in the English of T. W. Rhys Davids and Hermann Oldenberg, Vinaya Texts, Part III, The Kullavagga IV-XII, Sacred Books of the East vol. XX, Oxford 1885, pp. 78-81, which is public domain and reachable by the regular route. That closes the Vinaya half of prima-materia#45; the Dīgha Nikāya 11 half is still open."@en .

pmc:Ahwal a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zustände"@de,
        "States"@en ;
    dcterms:source "al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya",
        "al-Sarrāj, Kitāb al-Lumaʿ" ;
    skos:definition "Conditions that descend on the wayfarer as a gift and pass, distinguished in the handbooks from the stations precisely by not being attainable through effort."@en ;
    skos:note "No prerequisite claim is entered for these. The tradition holds that they cannot be worked for, which is a claim about the limits of any ordering and not an omission in this record."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Sufism .

pmc:AkasenaGamana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Er reist mit gekreuzten Beinen durch die Luft wie ein beschwingter Vogel"@de,
        "He travels cross-legged through the air like a winged bird"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.98-99 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, pp. 459-460; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "He enters the earth device, emerges, and marks off a place of the size he needs — of a seat if he would travel seated, of a cot if lying, of a path if on foot — and resolves that there be earth, and it becomes earth. In the sky he then walks to and fro, stands, sits and lies down."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:ArsNotoriaFacundia a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Zunehmen in allen Wissenschaften"@de,
        "Profiting in all the sciences"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), § 14, p. 12, ed. Peterson" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity claimed for the art: to profit in all the sciences and to attain them in an extraordinary manner, with memory, understanding and eloquence increased and established in the speaker."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:AsipuLitaniaMalorum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die feste Litanei: was die Beschwörung nennt, und in welcher Gesellschaft"@de,
        "The fixed litany: what the adjuration names, and in what company"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Utukki Limnuti, Tablet III, lines 100-107 and 160-165, and Tablet V, lines 26-27 and 40-55, in the English of R. Campbell Thompson, The Devils and Evil Spirits of Babylonia, Luzac's Semitic Text and Translation Series vol. XIV (London: Luzac, 1903), vol. I" ;
    skos:definition "The recurring enumeration of what is adjured, which the edition calls the constantly recurring line and which appears ten times in the first volume alone: evil Spirit, evil Demon, evil Ghost, evil Devil, evil God, evil Fiend. Where the series amplifies it, the additions are of two sorts at once. Tablet V sets before the six: pestilence and fever that ravage the land, sickness and woe that oppress the land, harmful to the flesh, unclean to the body; and after them: evil man, evil face, evil mouth, evil tongue. Tablet III sets the same six in a disjunction that runs on into sickness, death, Phantom of Night, Wraith of Night, disease, and evil pestilence."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a witness at generalizations/the-protection-and-what-would-show-it-failed.ttl, where it is the seventh and the only one that names an intrusion into a building beside a wreaking of evil in the abstract."@en,
        "What the litany asks to be kept off is enumerated in the same manner and is the part worth reading slowly. Unto my body may they not draw nigh; before me may they wreak no evil, nor follow behind me; into my house may they not enter; my fence may they not break through; into my chamber may they not enter. Three of those six are states of affairs nobody could establish and three are physical intrusions — a fence broken through is as plain a fact as this holding contains — and the series puts them in one breath and marks no difference between them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmc:AstangaLimbs a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die acht Glieder"@de,
        "The eight limbs"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra II.29" ;
    skos:definition "Restraint, observance, posture, breath regulation, withdrawal of the senses, concentration, meditation and absorption, listed together in a single sūtra."@en ;
    skos:note "The sūtra names them and says nothing about traversing them in order."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PatanjalaYoga .

pmc:AyurvedaKrtyapratighata a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Unangreifbarkeit durch Beschwörung und durch den Geist des Unglücks"@de,
        "Immunity from incantation and from the spirit of adversity"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.1.75 and I.3.28-33, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, parts XXXIII-XXXIV, Calcutta 1904-1905, pp. 1027 and 1048" ;
    skos:definition "Claimed of two preparations: that the user is freed from fear of incantations, and that no krtya performed by Atharvan rites can injure a man who takes the medicine, nor can Alaksmi, the spirit of adversity, approach him. The translator explains krtya as a fire ignited with formulas of the Atharvaveda by which a person may be brought under subjection, deprived of reason, or killed."@en ;
    skos:note "A medical handbook that promises protection against an operation performed by somebody else is doing two things at once: claiming a capacity and conceding that the operation works. The lesson does not describe the incantation and does not deny that it has effect; it sells the defence. Where a corpus transmits the attack and the defence together the holding has a generalization for it, and this is not quite that case — the attack is not transmitted here but in another Veda — so it is entered as what it is and not counted there."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmc:AyurvedaOjovrddhi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Mehrung des Ojas beim Gesunden"@de,
        "The increase of ojas in a healthy person"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.1.2-6, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIII, Calcutta 1904, pp. 1015-1017" ;
    skos:definition "The division the lesson opens with, and the one that places the whole of it. Medicine is of two kinds: some has for its purpose the increase of the ojas of a healthy person, some the alleviation of disease. What increases the ojas of a healthy person is again of two kinds, aphrodisiac and rasayana. Rasayana is therefore defined in the text as an operation on the healthy and not as a treatment, and the lesson adds that of almost all diseases it is regarded as alleviative besides."@en ;
    skos:note "The same division is what the translator's own preface to the part singles out: no system of medicine except this one, he writes, gives such importance to the treatment of the healthy. That is his judgement and not the text's, and it is recorded here as his."@en,
        "Worth holding as its own node because it is the claim that makes every other claim in the lesson unanswerable by an ordinary clinical observation: the population is people who are not ill, the outcome is a quantity not otherwise measured, and the span over which it is asserted is the rest of a life. A transmission that had wanted its promises checked would have started somewhere else."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmc:ClaviculaAdeptiExempti a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Genauigkeit von Tagen und Stunden ist für Adepten nicht nötig"@de,
        "The exactness of days and hours is not necessary for adepts"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Clavicula Salomonis, Book II chapter I, in the English of S. L. MacGregor Mathers, The Key of Solomon the King, London: George Redway, 1889" ;
    skos:definition "So exact a preparation of days and hours is not necessary for those who are adepts in the art, but it is extremely necessary for apprentices and beginners, seeing that those who have been little or not at all instructed do not have as much faith in the experiments as those who are adepts and have practised them. The wise should observe only the precepts of the art which are necessary."@en ;
    skos:note "The exemption is asymmetrical, and the asymmetry was the object of a search carried in this run. It covers days and hours and nothing else. No passage of the chapters read waives the bath, the fast, the continence or the confession for anyone, adept or beginner. The transmission is willing to call its astrology a pedagogy and is not willing to call its purification one. See pmp:ClaviculaPurificationWaiverSearch."@en,
        "The ground the text gives for the exemption is the faith of the operator and not anything about the hours. That makes the astrological term, by the book's own account, a device for producing confidence in beginners rather than a condition of the world — and it is the sharpest statement against its own timing rules that this corpus contains."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ClaviculaSalomonis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SolomonicGrimoire .

pmc:ClearLightOfSleep a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das klare Licht des Schlafs"@de,
        "The clear light of sleep"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005), the yoga of the clear light of sleep" ;
    skos:definition "A luminosity held to arise at the passage into sleep, which the practitioner is trained to recognise and hold rather than to pass through unconscious."@en ;
    skos:note "Tibetan 'od gsal, Sanskrit prabhāsvara. The state is not that of a body whose motor system has shut down while the mind stays awake; it is a luminosity placed at the moment of falling asleep, and its purpose in the sources is the recognition of emptiness and the preparation for dying, not the acquisition of a faculty."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:TibetanTantric .

pmc:DiscernmentOfOrigins a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Unterscheidung der Herkunft"@de,
        "Discernment of origins"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Evagrius Ponticus, On Thoughts 8",
        "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 80" ;
    skos:definition "The examination of an inner movement for its origin, distinguished threefold as angelic, human or demonic."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChristianAsceticDiscernment .

pmc:DistantSight a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Sehen des Entfernten"@de,
        "Seeing what is at a distance"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 46-47 and pp. 55-57" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity the whole transmission is consulted for: to know what is happening at a place too far off to be seen or reached, and to report it on returning."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SamiNoaidi .

pmc:DivyaCaksus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Göttliches Auge"@de,
        "Divine eye"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.26" ;
    skos:definition "Perception of what is distant, hidden or obstructed."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PatanjalaYoga .

pmc:EmptyingOfTheHeart a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Fasten des Herzens"@de,
        "Fasting of the heart"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhuangzi 4, in James Legge (trans.), The Sacred Books of China: The Texts of Tâoism, Part I, Sacred Books of the East vol. XXXIX (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1891), Book IV, par. 2, pp. 208-210" ;
    skos:definition "A graded emptying set out in three steps. The pupil is told not to wait for the hearing of his ears but for the hearing of his mind; then not to wait even for the hearing of his mind, but for the hearing of the third term. The hearing of the ears is to rest with the ears and the mind to rest in its own verification; the third term is free of all pre-occupation and so waits for things to appear. Where the way is, there is that freedom, and that freedom is the fasting of the mind."@en ;
    skos:note "The name is a second case of the same thing. The tradition's term is xinzhai, the fasting of the xin, and the label here says heart where the edition read says mind. Both are the same character, and the graph carries the German and English labels of an earlier run beside a source that translates it otherwise. The label is left standing and the divergence is recorded, because renaming it would hide that the term does not resolve into either word."@en,
        "The third term is where the translations part, and the passage was reopened in this run because of it. The edition read renders it 'the spirit' in the body of the text and states in its own footnote that the character standing there is qi, 'the breath'. Whoever reads only the translation gets a graded ascent from ears to mind to spirit; whoever reads the footnote gets an ascent that ends in the breath, which is what the whole of this file is about. The earlier wording of this definition ended with the way gathering in emptiness alone, which is a third rendering again and follows a translation this file could not name. All three are recorded rather than harmonised."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Zhuangzi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicKopfwehStillung a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Stillen heftiger Schmerzen am Haupte"@de,
        "The stilling of severe pain in the head"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Abschnitt »Zeugniße«",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), pp. 6-7" ;
    skos:definition "The outcome claimed four times in the testimonials: severe pain in the head goes away in a short time."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1820,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicRothlaufStillung a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Stillen des Rothlaufs"@de,
        "The stilling of the erysipelas"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Abschnitt »Zeugniße«",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), pp. 6-7" ;
    skos:definition "The outcome claimed three times in the testimonials: a spreading red inflammation of leg, cheek or finger goes away, in one case within some seven or nine hours, in another by the following morning."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1820,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:FourStatesOfConsciousness a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die vier Bewusstseinszustände"@de,
        "The four states of consciousness"@en ;
    dcterms:source "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching, chapter eight, at the talk on the four states of consciousness (one-volume English text as scanned in the Internet Archive full-text mirror; the scan does not carry its imprint, so the edition is left unstated rather than guessed)" ;
    skos:definition "An ordering of four states held to be possible for a person: sleep; the state ordinarily called waking, which the source holds to be a second form of sleep in which people walk the streets, write books and kill one another; self-consciousness, which is self-remembering; and an objective state in which things are seen as they are. The source holds that ordinary people live in the two lowest only, that flashes of the third occur but cannot be produced by wish or decision, and that the fourth is reached solely through the third."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim carries its own account of why it is resisted: the chief obstacle is said to be that people believe they already possess the third state, together with a permanent I, will and the ability to do. A claim of this shape cannot be tested by asking people whether they have the state, because it predicts the affirmative answer."@en,
        "The ordering is fourfold and is not the Indian one. It does not count waking, dream and deep sleep with a fourth beyond them; it counts degrees of presence within waking, and it puts the ordinary waking state on the lower half of its own scale. Two of its four terms therefore have no counterpart on pm:SensoryAnchoring, whose values were drawn from the Māṇḍūkya. See prima-materia#653."@en,
        "The source names no antecedent for this ordering, and that silence is itself worth recording. The same book gives the fourfold division of bodies under four sets of names and asserts that comparable tables stand in most systems, which is held at pmc:FourBodiesAcrossTerminologies. For the states it names nobody. Whether a route to the theosophical or Vedantic material can be shown is open; the work does not supply one."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:FourthWay .

pmc:InnerHeat a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Innere Hitze"@de,
        "Inner heat"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)" ;
    skos:definition "Heat held to be generated within the body by deliberate practice rather than received from outside it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:TibetanTantric .

pmc:InuitSecondSight a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zweites Gesicht"@de,
        "Second sight"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 29" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity to know in advance that something remarkable is about to happen, reported in the narrative as a standing property of a person rather than as the outcome of an exercise."@en ;
    skos:note "The English term is the translator's. What the node carries is the claim reported under it and not the term's own history in Scottish and Scandinavian usage; whether the Inuktitut word behind it has that range is not decidable from the portion read."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InuitAngakkuq .

pmc:JainaSuddhapanayaAntyaratri a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "In der letzten Nacht kommen die beiden Devas und fassen ihn an"@de,
        "On the last night the two devas come and take hold of him"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Viyāhapannatti (Bhagavaī) XV, Calcutta edition p. 1257" ;
    skos:definition "What the book says happens at the end of the six months. On the last night the two devas Puṇṇabhadda and Māṇibhadda appear to him and clutch his limbs with cool and wet hands. He who submits to their touch furthers the work of serpents; he who does not submit, in his body arises a fire which consumes his body, and thus he attains death and perfection."@en ;
    skos:note "A term with an event at its end and two named outcomes, of which the transmission says which is the attainment. That is the fullest form of a testable claim this holding has yet taken from the Indian material: the period is counted, the appearance is dated to its last night, the branch point is an act of the practitioner, and each branch has a stated result."@en,
        "The fire here is not the fiery power of the rest of the book. That one is shot outward at a target and is obtained by a discipline; this one arises in the practitioner's own body at the end of a fixed period and consumes him. The two are entered apart for that reason, and a later reader who wants to join them is joining them himself."@en,
        "The success branch ends in the practitioner's death by a fire arising in his own body, so the outcome that counts as the attainment is also the outcome after which no one can be questioned. The failure branch leaves a living man who furthers the work of serpents, and the transmission gives no mark by which that condition would be recognised. What can be observed is the death; what cannot is which of the two branches produced a survivor."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "A. F. Rudolf Hoernle, The Uvāsagadasāo, vol. II, Appendix I (Bibliotheca Indica, Calcutta 1888), pp. 8-9" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmc:JinchengYaocheng a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ist das Gold entstanden, ist das Mittel fertig"@de,
        "If the gold forms, the drug is finished"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 4 (Jindan), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 4" ;
    skos:definition "The assay the chapter gives for the first elixir, and with it for the operation as a whole. Two hundred and forty zhu of the elixir joined with a hundred catties of mercury and put to the fire make gold. Where the gold forms, the drug is complete. Where the gold does not form, the drug is to be sealed again and fired again for the same count of days, and then, the chapter says, there is no case in which it does not form."@en ;
    skos:note "The two halves of this sentence do different work and the difference is the reason for the node. The first half is a criterion: a visible product decides whether an invisible one is finished, which is more than most of the material this collection holds ever offers. The second half withdraws it, because a failure that requires only repetition is not an outcome the operation can have. What remains after both halves is a rule under which no run ever counts against the recipe."@en,
        "This is the same structure the collection holds from the burial book and from the treatise against the year and month taboos, and it arrives here from the inside rather than from a critic. Nobody is being answered; the immunising clause is printed as part of the instructions."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Waidan .

pmc:JishuZhilai a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Zahlen erschöpfen, um das Kommende zu wissen"@de,
        "Exhausting the numbers to know what is coming"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhouyi, Xici A, the sections on prognostication and on what the Changes are without, in the Wang Bi and Han Kangbo recension; read in the digital text of the Kanseki Repository, which names no printing" ;
    skos:definition "What the Changes are said to do, stated twice in the same treatise. To exhaust the numbers and thereby to know what is coming is called prognostication, and what yin and yang do not sound out is called spirit. And: the Changes are without thought and without doing, still and unmoving; touched, they thereupon penetrate the grounds of everything under heaven. The same section says that whoever is not the most spiritlike thing under heaven could not do this, and that the Changes receive a charge as an echo receives a sound."@en ;
    skos:note "The formulation is the strongest in this file and the least examinable, and both for the same reason. Without thought and without doing means that nothing about the operator's state is offered as a condition, so no failure can be assigned to him; and knowing what is coming is not tied to any statement that could turn out false. What the corpus does supply against this is the one condition on repetition, below, and the counter-argument from a work of the first century, held as a dispute."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination .

pmc:MagnetismeRapport a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Rapport, die hergestellte Verbindung"@de,
        "Rapport, the established communication"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. I, § 8, p. 22, and chap. II, pp. 31-32" ;
    skos:definition "The induced condition in which the operator exerts an influence upon the patient, there being between them a communication of the vital principle. It is established by taking the thumbs, and strengthened by setting the ten fingers against the patient's so that the balls touch. Once well established it is renewed in each succeeding sitting at the instant of beginning, so that touching is no longer needed; it is sometimes established at once and sometimes only after a long trial; and it is rare not to have it established at the first sitting."@en ;
    skos:note "The pivot of the whole transmission, and the place where its testability is decided. Rapport is what makes the action selective — the source has the patient understand only those put in communication with him, and later makes the breaking of the communication by a distracted spectator the cause of shocks. Every claim in this file that could be checked from outside runs through it, and so does every excuse for a check that fails."@en,
        "The source states that experienced operators generally perceive in themselves when it takes place. That locates the criterion in the operator and nowhere else, and it is the reason the three-outcome test below matters: it is the one place where the book offers a sign of the state that somebody other than the operator could read."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:Manomayakaya a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Geistgemachter Körper"@de,
        "Mind-made body"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta, DN i.77 (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)" ;
    skos:definition "A second body, formed and complete in its faculties, said to be produced from this one; the classical form of the claim the modern draft calls exteriorisation."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DighaNikaya,
        pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:Maqamat a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Stationen"@de,
        "Stations"@en ;
    dcterms:source "al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya, chapter on the stations",
        "al-Sarrāj, Kitāb al-Lumaʿ" ;
    skos:definition "Stages of the path attained by effort and held by the one who reaches them, enumerated in the handbooks in a set order."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Sufism .

pmc:NippurDemonsDepartAndDoNotReturn a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Geister fahren aus und erscheinen nicht wieder, nicht bei Nacht und nicht bei Tag"@de,
        "The spirits go out, and do not appear again by night or by day"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, texts 6: 9-11, 7: 5-6, 8: 11 and 8: 16, 11: 8, 20: 4 and 26: 5-6, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 142, 147, 155-156, 170, 200 and 206)" ;
    skos:definition "The outcome nearly every bowl of this corpus states, and it is stated as an observable. The spirits are to remove and betake themselves and go out from the house, the dwelling and the whole body of the client; they are not to appear to him in dream by night nor in slumber by day; they are not to approach his right side nor his left; they are not to kill his children; they are to have no power over his property, what he has and what he shall have; and they are not to return to him from this day and forever."@en ;
    skos:note "Five named things, each of which either happens or does not: an appearance in sleep, an approach, the death of a child, a loss of property, a return. That is the whole of what this corpus offers a later reader to hold it to, and it offers it in the form of a prohibition rather than of a sign."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmc:NorseRunarUppNema a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Aufnehmen der Runen"@de,
        "Taking up the runes"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hávamál 139 and 141 (Guðni Jónsson; 140 and 142 in Bellows), in Eddukvæði. Sæmundar-Edda, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:definition "The acquisition claimed for the hanging: the runes are taken up from below, and with them, in the stanzas that follow, a thriving and a growing wise in which word sought word for him and work sought work."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmc:OphiteTransitusLiber a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Freier Durchgang durch das Reich eines Herrschers"@de,
        "Free passage through the realm of a ruler"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.31 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)" ;
    skos:definition "The outcome the speeches ask for: that the gate be opened and the speaker pass through the realm of its ruler, in the wording of the first of them, again in freedom."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:OphiteGnosis .

pmc:PgmIvSublatio a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Emporgehoben werden, nichts Sterbliches hören und sehen"@de,
        "Being raised aloft, hearing and seeing nothing mortal"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.475-829, first instruction (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1907)" ;
    skos:definition "The effect announced for the breathing: the operator will see himself raised aloft and soaring towards the height, so that he seems to be in the midst of air; he will hear nothing, neither man nor beast, and will see nothing of the sights upon the earth in that same hour, but everything he sees will be immortal."@en ;
    skos:note "Stated as what will happen and not as what may. The withdrawal of hearing and of earthly sight is given first and the vision second, which makes the sensory cut the condition rather than a side effect."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmc:Physike a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Physikē"@de,
        "Contemplation of nature"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 1" ;
    skos:definition "The second of the three parts, the contemplation of created things."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:DesertMonasticism .

pmc:Piti a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Pīti"@de,
        "Pīti"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.94ff. (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "Rapture as a bodily felt state, graded in the handbook into five degrees of increasing pervasion."@en ;
    skos:note "The five degrees are khuddikā, khaṇikā, okkantikā, ubbegā and pharaṇā."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:Praktike a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Praktikē"@de,
        "Ascetic practice"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 1" ;
    skos:definition "The first of the three parts of the way, consisting in the keeping of the commandments and the labour against the passions."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:DesertMonasticism .

pmc:Qi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Qi"@de,
        "Qi"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Xingqi yupei ming (jade inscription on circulating the qi)" ;
    skos:definition "The vital breath or energy held to circulate in the body and to be susceptible of deliberate direction."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XingqiYupeiMing ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:ShangqingShijieZhengbian a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Regel, einen Leichnam zu lesen"@de,
        "The rule for reading a corpse"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 4.15b-4.16a (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 4",
        "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 4.17a (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 4" ;
    skos:definition "The corpus gives a rule for telling release through the corpse from ordinary death, and the rule admits each state and its opposite. If the dead man's form is as of a living man, that is release through the corpse. If the feet are not blue and the skin not wrinkled, that too is release through the corpse. If the light of the eyes is undamaged and there is no difference from a living man, that too. If the hair has fallen out entirely and form and bone are lost, those too are all release through the corpse. A related passage lists the ways of going: dying and living again, being beheaded and going out at the side, vanishing before the laying out, the form remaining with no bones in it, clothes left with the form gone, hair shed and the form lost; and grades them by the hour, departure in daylight ranking highest, at midnight lowest, and at dawn or dusk placing one among the administrators of the underworld."@en ;
    skos:note "A preceding passage supplies the cases the rule is built to cover, and they are the cases that would otherwise be read as poisoning. Named adepts are said to have drunk the essence and had their corpses putrefy; to have swallowed the jade blossom and had worms crawl out of the door; to have swallowed the golden liquor and stunk for a hundred li; to have taken the cloud powder and been found with head and feet in different places; to have taken the stone marrow and gone into fire. The section closes with the remark that the traces of obtaining the Way have no fixed form."@en,
        "The corpus does rank the outcome rather than treating all as equal, which keeps it from being simply a blanket. Release through the corpse is said not to command the flowered canopy, ride the flying dragon, ascend to the Great Ultimate or roam the nine palaces; and daylight departure is said not to be a case of release through the corpse at all."@en,
        "This is the node the counter-search was carried for and the one it found; see pmp:ZhengaoShijieCountsSearch. The protocol that names the outcomes the rule treats alike stands in examinations/shangqing-intervals.ttl."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmc:ShouyiMianLiri a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wer das Eine bewahrt, steht nicht mehr unter dem Kalender"@de,
        "He who guards the One is no longer under the calendar"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 18 (Dizhen), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 18" ;
    skos:definition "The chapter states an exemption and states it as a consequence of the attainment. One who can guard the One travels ten thousand li, goes into an army, crosses great rivers, and need not divine the day or choose the hour; in beginning works, in moving house, in entering a new dwelling, he no longer consults the siting calendars; he does not avoid the year star, the general of the great yin, the killing and wasting spirits of the month establishment or the taboos of his year and fate; and in the end he never meets with calamity. It closes by calling this a way that the worthies of former times tested one after another and found to hold."@en ;
    skos:note "It also names the interest the exemption serves inside the work. The chapter is arguing that guarding the One replaces several thousand other methods, and an exemption from the calendar is one more thing the reader no longer has to do. That is a reason for caution about the claim and not a reason to leave it out."@en,
        "This is the passage that keeps the same work from speaking with one voice about the calendar. Its eighth chapter binds the circulation of the qi to the hours of living qi and says that in the hours of dead qi it is of no benefit; its seventeenth binds the entering of mountains to years, months and days and states the harm that follows; and its eighteenth says that a man of one particular attainment is under none of it. The exemption is granted by a state of the operator, which is exactly what a calendrical condition is otherwise said not to depend on."@en,
        "What it does not say is that an operation done outside its window works. It says that this man no longer chooses days at all, which is a claim about who stands under the rule and not about what the rule does. The difference decides whether it refutes the generalization in generalizations/efficacy-bound-to-the-calendar.ttl or only narrows it, and the note there records that it narrows it."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BaopuziNeipian ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:Sigui a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sigui"@de,
        "Sigui"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 274 and 280" ;
    skos:definition "A ceremony celebrated once in sixty years whose stated purpose is the renewal of the world. The star that governs its reckoning is named after it: sigi tolo, star of the Sigui."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Dogon .

pmc:SoulJourney a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Seelenreise ohne den Körper"@de,
        "Journey of the soul apart from the body"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Herodotus, Histories IV.13-16",
        "Pliny, Naturalis Historia VII.174" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that the soul leaves the body and returns to it, reported of Aristeas of Proconnesus and of Hermotimus of Clazomenae."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HerodotiHistoriai,
        pmw:NaturalisHistoria ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GraecoRoman .

pmc:TalamDiaSlugadh a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Erde verschlingt, wer im Unrecht war"@de,
        "The earth swallows whichever side was in the wrong"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Mittelirische Verslehren III, section 155, ed. Thurneysen, Irische Texte III/1 (Leipzig 1891), pp. 96-97, English in Stokes, The Second Battle of Moytura, Revue Celtique 12 (1891), pp. 120-121" ;
    skos:definition "The outcome the tract states in two clauses. If it were they that were in the wrong, the earth of the hill would swallow them up. But if it were the king that was in the wrong, the earth would swallow up him and his wife and his son and his horse and his arms and his dress and his hound. The tract then distributes the seven curses over the seven objects: the satire of the mac fuirmid falls on the hound, of the fochloc on the dress, of the doss on the arms, of the cana on the wife, of the clí on the son, of the anrad on the land, and of the ollam on the king."@en ;
    skos:note "The distribution of the seven curses over the seven objects is the part that makes an outcome readable. It says which member of the household is answerable to which grade of poet, so that a partial outcome would be interpretable rather than merely disappointing, and it commits the procedure to effects on a hound, a dress and a horse that anybody could look at."@en,
        "The editor of the tract doubted in 1891 that the description rests on old tradition, and gave a reason worth carrying: the passage speaks in the imperfect, of what used to be done and is no longer usual. That is a judgement about the age of the description and not about its content, it is a modern scholar's and not a source's, and it is recorded here rather than entered as a claim."@en,
        "This is the form the project looks for and finds seldom. The criterion is a single physical event; it is not announced to the participants as a sign to be recognised, it is not a state either party could report of himself, and above all it is two-sided: the same event decides against the operators if they were the ones in the wrong. A satire that can swallow the satirist is not a curse but a trial, and the transmission has made it one by naming the losing side on both ends."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InsularCeltic .

pmc:ThelemaFractiCrescentesNonSuntCasus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein schlechter werdender Zählwert wird als geschärftes Instrument gelesen"@de,
        "A worsening count is read as a sharpening instrument"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book 4 (Liber ABA), Part I: Meditation, chap. V, pp. 58-59 (first published London: Wieland & Co., 1912; read in the Celephais Press electronic edition of 2004, which follows the corrected text in Magick: Book 4 Parts I-IV, York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1994)" ;
    skos:definition "The rule the exposition lays over the counted breaks: if these breaks seem to become more frequent instead of less frequent, the student must not be discouraged, for this is partially caused by his increased accuracy of observation. The analogy offered is that the introduction of vaccination resulted in an apparent increase in the number of cases of smallpox, because people began to tell the truth about the disease instead of faking."@en ;
    skos:note "Nothing in the passage is dishonest and that is the point. The self-immunising step here is a piece of good statistical reasoning applied without a stopping rule, which is harder to see than an evasion and is why it is entered as its own node."@en,
        "This is the most instructive passage in the corpus for the purposes of this holding, because the reasoning is correct and the effect is nonetheless to immunise the measurement. A rising count is genuinely ambiguous between worse control and better observation. The exposition resolves the ambiguity in one direction by rule, and states no condition under which a rising count would be read the other way. The only proposed exit is that control will soon improve faster than observation and the improvement will become apparent in the record, which relocates the criterion to a later and unnamed point."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberABA ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmc:TheosophyConscientiaContinua a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Den Koerper bei vollem Bewusstsein verlassen, und ununterbrochenes Bewusstsein"@de,
        "Leaving the body in full consciousness, and consciousness unbroken"@en ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter Force-Centres, p. 297 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity attributed to the seventh centre. When it is awakened the man is able, by passing through it, to leave his body in full consciousness and to return to it without the usual break, so that his consciousness is continuous through night and day; and when the fire has been passed through all the centres in a certain order, which varies for different types of people, the consciousness becomes continuous up to the entry into the heaven-world at the end of the life on the astral plane, no difference being made by either the temporary separation from the physical body during sleep or the permanent division at death."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the faculty the 1916 footnote renders as the power of unbroken consciousness on all planes, and the rendering is faithful to the source."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy .

pmc:TurbaAquaPermanens a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das beständige Wasser, genannt Gummi, scharfer Essig und geistliches Blut"@de,
        "The permanent water, called gum, sharp vinegar and spiritual blood"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 15 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)",
        "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 18 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)",
        "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 19 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    skos:definition "The one substance the work makes every operation depend on. It is described as a water that does not evaporate, whose force is a spiritual blood, which turns a body into spirit when ground with it; the work identifies it in turn with the gum, with a very sharp vinegar that makes gold into sheer spirit, with the water of our sea and with the water of sulphur, and states that without it neither whiteness nor blackness nor redness nor rust can be made."@en ;
    skos:note "The identification is the work's own and is made explicitly at four places, which is why one node carries the several names rather than several nodes carrying one name each. At Dictum 18 the gum is none other than permanent water; at Dictum 15 the nature is a very sharp vinegar; at Dictum 21 the despised stone is a permanent water; at Dictum 51 the water of our copper is the fiery venom. What the substance is in a laboratory is not stated anywhere and is not guessed at here."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy .

pmc:VaidikaNividdosah a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Drei Weisen, die Nivid zu verfehlen, und drei abgestufte Folgen"@de,
        "Three ways of misreciting the Nivid, and three graded consequences"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aitareya Brāhmaṇa iii,11 (xi,11), in the English of Arthur Berriedale Keith, Rigveda Brahmanas, Harvard Oriental Series 25, Cambridge 1920, pp. 171-172" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission grades the fault and grades the damage with it. He should not pass over a sentence of the Nivid; if he were to pass over one, he would make a break in the sacrifice, and as the break grows the sacrificer becomes worse. He should not invert two sentences; if he were to invert two, he would confuse the sacrifice, and the sacrificer would be confused. He should not unite two sentences; if he were to unite two, he would contract the life of the sacrifice, and the sacrificer would be likely to die. A fourth follows the same form: inserting the Nivid at the third pressing with two verses left over instead of one injures the propagative power and deprives people of embryos."@en ;
    skos:note "The grading is what makes this more than a threat. Omission, inversion and fusion are three different things a reciter can do to the same text, and the transmission assigns them consequences of different severity in a fixed order — worse, confused, likely to die. A doctrine that only warned would not need to rank them."@en,
        "Three degrees of a fault in a recitation, three degrees of harm, and in all four cases the harm falls on the sacrificer and not on the reciter who made it. The reciter is the only person in a position to know that he passed over a sentence, and he is the one person named as suffering nothing."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:VaidikaNivid ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AitareyaBrahmana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual .

pm:Reworking a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Bearbeiten"@de,
        "Reworking"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "The act, within one tradition, of taking over a claim from another and adding to it something the source it came from does not contain: a term, a consequence, a caution, a sign, a condition of failure."@en ;
    skos:note "Deliberately expensive: pm:addedElement points at a node, so the addition has to be entered before the reworking can be claimed. A station that added something and cannot say what added nothing that this graph can use. As with pm:Testing, absence is informative and is not to be stubbed: no instance means nobody has shown an addition, not that none was made."@en,
        "Distinct from pm:Systematizing, which is an ordering act performed by an editor in the present and named as his. A reworking is an act inside a transmission, attested in a work like any other claim, and it belongs to the tradition that performed it."@en,
        "The class exists because reception is two things and the record could not tell them apart. Textual transmission is somebody copying a sentence, and it shows that the sentence was handed on. Operative uptake is somebody taking the instruction, carrying it out, and afterwards writing what was not in the exemplar. Only the second turns a chain of copies into a series of attempts, and only the second is proof against feedback: what makes the Dogon Sirius case weak is not that it is received but that nothing was added at the second station which was not already in the question."@en,
        "This is therefore the quantity worth counting, and it is not the number of independent witnesses. A tradition that takes over a claim about an effect and attaches a condition of failure to it has delivered more than three that pass it on word for word — and the addition is the material a pm:falsifiedBy is made from, so the examining layer comes out of the reception history rather than against it."@en .

pm:locusNotStated a pm:AwarenessLocus,
        pm:AwarenessValue ;
    rdfs:label "Ort nicht angegeben"@de,
        "Locus not stated"@en ;
    skos:definition "The source was read for this question and does not answer it."@en .

pm:presentationOrder a pm:PrerequisiteStrength ;
    rdfs:label "Darstellungsreihenfolge"@de,
        "Presentation order"@en ;
    skos:definition "The source only presents the material in a sequence. Any necessity is the reader's, and belongs to whoever read it that way."@en .

pmp:AyurvedaAcararasayana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Verhalten als Rasayana, ohne Droge, Kammer und Termin"@de,
        "Conduct as a rasayana, without drug, chamber or date"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.4.52-57, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXV, Calcutta 1905, pp. 1065-1066" ;
    skos:definition "The section the translator heads Rasayana of conduct. A list of some thirty dispositions and practices — truthfulness, freedom from wrath, abstention from wine and from sexual indulgence, absence of animosity, labour that does not fatigue, tranquillity, amiable speech, silent recitation, purity, gravity, charity, penance, honouring of teachers and of the aged, aversion to cruelty, regularity of sleep and waking, the taking of milk and ghee, knowledge of time and place, freedom from pride, senses turned towards the self, devotion to the scriptures — and the conclusion that such a person should be known as a user of rasayana. The translator's note draws the consequence the passage implies: such a person does not require the aid of any rasayana of drugs, and attains through his conduct to all the effects of rasayana."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a practice of its own and not as a remark, because it is the lesson's own alternative to everything else in it and because it carries no date, no chamber, no purification and no drug. Whatever the calendar at the entry to the hut is doing, it is not a condition on the effect the lesson names; the same effect is claimed here for conduct alone."@en,
        "The drawing of the consequence is the translator's, in a footnote, and the text says only that such a person is to be known as a user of rasayana. The difference is small and it is the difference between a claim the passage makes and a claim a reader makes for it, so it is marked."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:BengeConsultation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Befragung des Giftorakels"@de,
        "Consulting the poison oracle"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 136-138 and p. 153" ;
    skos:definition "A questioner addresses the oracle while a second man, the operator, administers a measured number of doses of the poison benge to a fowl. The address alternates: sentences framing the matter so that an affirmative answer is asked for and ending with the command that the oracle kill the fowl, then sentences framing it so that a negative answer is asked for and ending with the command that it spare the fowl. Whether the death of the fowl means yes or no is therefore decided by the form of the question and not by the death."@en ;
    skos:note "The operators are generally boys between twelve and sixteen, old enough to keep the food taboos and young enough to keep the sexual one. The consultation is held in public, and the bystanders may interrupt the questioner to have a point put that he has left out."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmp:BoranParikamma a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Vorbereitung: der Atem an der Nasenspitze unter dem Wort Arahan"@de,
        "The preparation: the breath watched at the nose-tip under the word Arahan"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Yogāvacara's Manual, the preamble (trans. Woodward 1916, p. 1-2)" ;
    skos:definition "The operation on which everything in the work is built, stated once in the preamble and then presupposed by every later rubric. The practitioner goes to a solitary place, sits cross-legged and holds the body straight, sets mindfulness in front of him with Nibbāna as his goal, and breathes in and out knowing of each breath that it is long or quick. He grasps the path of the breath from the nose-tip down to the navel and up again, fixes his sight on the tip of the nose, grasps that he sees the nose-tip with the sight-mind, and fixes in the heart the thing he is attending to. The syllables of the word Arahan are assigned to the three treasures: A the Law, RA the Buddha, HAN the Brotherhood."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as the practice and not as a series, because the work treats it so: every later section opens with the rubric that he sits down cross-legged and prepares himself with the word Arahan, abbreviated with et cetera after the first statement of it. What changes between sections is the object and the colour triad, not the operation."@en,
        "The syllable assignment is worth recording because it is the one place where this manual and the Vimuttimagga, the work the literature most often sets beside it, are reported to differ: the distribution of A, RA and HAṀ over the three treasures is inverted between them. That is a textual detail and it is the kind of thing a route between two works is argued from, so it is noted here and not built into a claim."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:YogavacarasManual ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:BoranKammatthana .

pmp:Evocation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Evokation"@de,
        "Evocation"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Allan Kardec, Le Livre des Médiums, seconde partie, chapitre XXVI, sections 286-288" ;
    skos:altLabel "évocation"@fr ;
    skos:definition "The putting of questions to a spirit through a medium. The founder makes the form of the question part of the operation: it is to be clear, precise and put in a logical order, and the answer given depends on how it was asked."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Spiritism .

pmp:HarbaDeMoshe a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Schwert des Mose"@de,
        "The Sword of Moses"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The Sword of Moses, ed. and trans. Moses Gaster (London: David Nutt, 1896), the opening instructions" ;
    skos:altLabel "Harba de-Moshe"@en,
        "Sword of Moses"@en ;
    skos:definition "An operative handbook whose opening instructions state the preparation in numbers. The user must free himself three days beforehand from accidental pollution and from everything unclean; eat and drink once every evening; take his bread from a pure man or wash his hands first in salt, a clause the translator marks as uncertain; drink only water; bathe once on the first day and no more; and pray three times daily, reciting a named blessing after each prayer. He carries during the three days a piece of leather written with ink."@en ;
    skos:note "Corrected on 2026-09-24 against the whole work. Two clauses stood here that do not belong to the preparation. The hundred sacred names summing to three hundred and four stand in the appendix Gaster prints after the work, which is a different recension from a different manuscript, and not in these instructions. And not approaching a woman is part of the description of the men to whom the messenger was sent, not part of what the user is told to do; it stands in the charge God gives the messenger, some paragraphs later. Both are removed. The leather written with ink, which does belong here, was missing and is added."@en,
        "Three days, once a day, water only, one bath, three prayers. A preparation given entirely in counts is checkable in a way a preparation given in dispositions is not, and this one has no clause about the state of mind of the user at all."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HarbaDeMoshe ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hekhalot .

pmp:HittSiskurPaprannas a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Unreinheitsritual, Flussritual genannt"@de,
        "The ritual of uncleanness, called the ritual of the river"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Tunnawiya, CTH 409, KUB 7.53+KUB 12.58 Vs. I 7-II 20 (Goetze–Sturtevant, AOS 14, 1938, pp. 4-13)" ;
    skos:altLabel "SISKUR.SISKUR paprannaš"@en ;
    skos:definition "The procedure the Old Woman performs over the person who is experiencing uncleanness: black animals and black vessels are assembled and the patient is dressed in black and stops the ears with black wool; at nightfall the Old Woman goes to the river bank to announce the rite to its genius and takes clay from the bank and from a spring; a tent of reeds stands ready beside the river; at dawn the patient comes to the tent, and the Old Woman lifts the black sheep, the piglet, the puppy, the clay tongues, the two figures and the other prepared objects over the patient in turn, pronouncing the charm belonging to each; the figures of wax and of mutton tallow are lifted last; the blue and red wool are taken off the body and the black clothing is torn from top to bottom, the gaiters drawn off the feet and the wool taken out of the ears; an empty pot is swung over the patient and broken; the patient bathes; a temple girl combs the patient once with each of nine boxwood combs while the Old Woman declares that she is scrubbing all the limbs; the combs, the brush, the wing and the black clothing go into the river behind her, the remnants and the clay figure are thrown into the river, and the puppy and the piglet are carried to another place and burnt; two gates are then built at the tent, one of ḫatalkeššar wood covered with white wool on the upper side and one of alanza wood covered with black wool on the lower side, and the patient passes through both with a simile spoken at each and a thin loaf thrown away after each; and finally the clay of the bank and of the spring is declared to have cleansed and purified the twelve parts of the body, and the horn of a fertile cow is seized for the address to the Sun-god."@en ;
    skos:note "The second gate works by a different argument and it is worth separating. The first says that a gate is a thing things are stripped at; the second says that this wood cleanses a thousand or ten thousand shepherds and cowherds, so let it cleanse this one too. The first is an analogy from the act, the second an appeal to a quantity nobody counted."@en,
        "The sequence of charms is not in this tablet. After the clay oxen the text says that the respective charms are already put down in a tablet, and that tablet is not this one. What is recoverable here is therefore the order of acts and not the words said over most of them."@en,
        "The two gates are the part of this rite that connects it to another work of the same corpus, and the connection was not looked for. The patient is made to pass under a gate and something is declared taken off him on the far side — the same construction as the reed gate of the ritual of Paškuwatti at pmp:HittMugessarUliliyassi, where a spindle and a mirror are exchanged for a bow and arrows as he comes through. Here the simile is from animals: the buck goes through you and you take off his hobble, the bull goes through you to his pasture and you take off his shackle, so take off from this sacrificer in the same way. Two practitioners from different ends of Anatolia, two unrelated afflictions, one device."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Tunnawiya ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual .

pmp:JainaBhaktapratyakhyana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Verzicht auf alle Nahrung"@de,
        "Renouncing all food"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX.40 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)",
        "Ācārāṅga Sūtra I.7.8.7-11 (Hermann Jacobi, Jaina Sūtras Part I, Sacred Books of the East 22, Oxford: Clarendon 1884; read in the Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 1964)" ;
    skos:altLabel "bhaktapratyākhyāna"@en ;
    skos:definition "The fortieth item of the lecture, and the first and least severe of the three methods of the religious death in the Ācārāṅga: lying down without food, examining the ground and spreading straw on it, bearing the pains that attack, not killing the animals that feed on the body and not rubbing the wound, and not stirring from the position while they destroy it."@en ;
    skos:note "The same act stands in both works and is entered once. The Uttarādhyayana names it in the list of what obtains a result; the Ācārāṅga describes how it is carried out and what distinguishes it from the two harder methods."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmp:JainaDashaBrahmacaryasamadhi a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die zehn Bedingungen zur Verwirklichung der Keuschheit"@de,
        "The ten conditions for the realisation of celibacy"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XVI.1-10, with the verses XVI.1-14 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" ;
    skos:altLabel "brahmacaryasamādhi"@en ;
    skos:definition "The ten avoidances the sixteenth lecture sets out in prose and repeats in verse, attributed to the venerable Sthaviras. A Nirgrantha should not occupy places for sleep or rest frequented by women, cattle or eunuchs; should not converse with women; should not sit with women on the same seat; should not look at or contemplate the charms and beauties of women; should not, behind a screen or curtain or wall, listen to their screeching, screaming, singing, laughing, giggling or crying; should not recall to memory the pleasures and amusements he enjoyed with women in the past; should not eat well-dressed food; should not eat or drink to excess; should not wear ornaments or adorn his body, lest he become an object of desire; and should not care for sounds, colours, tastes, smells and feelings."@en ;
    skos:note "Two of the ten are about food and not about women, and they stand in the same list and carry the same consequence: well-dressed food is said soon to raise sensuality, and eating to excess is forbidden beside the prescribed quantity and the right time. The transmission therefore makes the diet a condition of the celibacy and not a separate discipline."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain .

pmp:NorseHangaAVindgaMeidi a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Hängen am windigen Baum"@de,
        "Hanging on the windy tree"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Henry Adams Bellows, The Poetic Edda (New York: American-Scandinavian Foundation, 1923), Hovamol 139-140",
        "Hávamál 138-139 (Guðni Jónsson; 139-140 in Bellows), in Eddukvæði. Sæmundar-Edda, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:definition "Hanging nine whole nights on a windy tree, wounded with a spear and given to Óðinn, oneself to oneself, on the tree of which no man knows from what roots it runs; then peering down and taking up the runes, taking them up screaming, and falling back from there."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmp:RasaGrasana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Verschlucken der Metalle durch das Quecksilber"@de,
        "The swallowing of metals by mercury"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, grasanam, pp. 75-98" ;
    skos:altLabel "grasanam"@en ;
    skos:definition "The tenth operation, given in fifteen processes and with as many recipes for the bida, the reagent that is said to open the mouth of the mercury. Sulphur is swallowed first, then mica, then gold, and last lead, brass, iron, copper and zinc, each reduced to ashes and each equal in weight to the mercury; gold leaf smeared with peacock's bile is rubbed into the mercury a sixty-fourth at a time and the whole is then boiled three days in salted kanji."@en ;
    skos:note "The operation carries the volume's central claim and its one measurable test: after the gold has been swallowed the mercury is to weigh what it weighed before, and if it weighs more it is boiled again until it does. See pmc:RasaGrasanaParyaptiPariksa."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmp:RunicDrumConsultation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Befragung der Trommel"@de,
        "Consulting the drum"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 47-48 and p. 57",
        "Knud Leem, An Account of the Laplanders of Finmark, their Language, Manners and Religion, in John Pinkerton (ed.), A General Collection of the Best and Most Interesting Voyages and Travels, vol. I (London: Longman, Hurst, Rees and Orme, 1808), chap. XXI, Of the Magic Arts of the Laplanders, pp. 474-477" ;
    skos:definition "A bowl of hollowed pine, fir or birch with a skin stretched over it, painted with figures of the gods, the angels, the evil spirits, the associates of the noaidi, the sun, the dwellings, the sacred mountains and the beasts of the country. A ring or a brass cover is laid on the skin, the skin is struck with a hammer of reindeer horn, and the ring travels across the painted figures. Where it comes to rest, or which way it runs, gives the answer."@en ;
    skos:note "The two recorders describe the same instrument ninety years apart and their descriptions of the mechanism agree in every part that matters: the hammer of horn, the ring or cover laid on the skin, the painted field, the reading from where the ring goes. The later adds what the earlier does not: that the drum is not thought fit for use unless got by inheritance, and that women may not touch it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SamiNoaidi .

pmp:ShangqingHuangtingCunshen a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Körpergötter beim Namen vergegenwärtigen"@de,
        "Actualising the gods of the body by name"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangting neijing yujing 7 (Zhidao zhang) (DZ 331), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "Holding the resident gods of the parts of the body before the inner sight, each by its name and its courtesy name, each in its own chamber of a square inch, all robed in purple with flying gauze skirts. The work states that it is enough to think of the whole company for the lifespan to be without end, that they do not dwell apart but are ranged in order within the brain facing outward, and that where they are held in the heart they answer to one another of themselves."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmp:Shouyi a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Bewahren des Einen"@de,
        "Guarding the One"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 18 (Dizhen), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 18" ;
    skos:definition "The practice the chapter calls the whole of warding off evil, as the golden elixir is the whole of long life. He who would live long guards the One and must think it clearly: to the One at hunger the One gives food, to the One at thirst the One gives drink. The One has a surname and a personal name, a dress and a colour; the male is nine fen long and the female six; and it is either in the lower cinnabar field two inches four fen below the navel, or in the crimson palace and golden gate that is the middle cinnabar field below the heart, or between the brows, one inch in being the bright hall, two inches the cavern chamber and three inches the upper cinnabar field. The chapter says this is what the school holds most precious and that its surname and name have been transmitted from mouth to mouth over the generations with the smearing of blood."@en ;
    skos:note "The chapter also states what the practice replaces. There are, it says, several thousand methods of holding things in mind for warding off harm, and some make one think up several thousand objects to guard oneself with, which is mostly troublesome and wearies the mind; whoever knows the way of guarding the One puts all of that aside. A transmission that names the number of its own alternatives and calls them laborious is describing its own economy."@en,
        "The condition of transmission is stated as an operative condition and not as an etiquette: the blood of a white victim is smeared, the day is one of the ruling and assisting phases, white silk and white silver are the pledge, a metal tally is cut and divided, and where it is spoken of lightly or handed on wrongly, the spirit does not work. That is a stated failure condition attached to the transmission rather than to the performance, and this graph holds few of those."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BaopuziNeipian ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmp:SubidaAdvertenciaAmorosa a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Im liebenden Aufmerken auf Gott verweilen, ohne Akte der Vermögen"@de,
        "Resting in loving attention to God without acts of the faculties"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Juan de la Cruz, Subida del Monte Carmelo II.XI (ed. Gerardo, Toledo 1912, pp. 148-151; II.13 in Lewis), read in the Spanish against Lewis 1906/1922 and the Carmelites of Paris, Oudin 1893" ;
    skos:altLabel "advertencia amorosa en paz"@en,
        "atencion amorosa"@en,
        "noticia amorosa general"@en ;
    skos:definition "The practice the text prescribes in place of the other: to stay alone with a loving attention to God, without particular consideration, in interior peace and quiet and rest, without acts or exercises of memory, understanding and will, at least without discursive ones, and to give nothing to the imagination or to its work. Where the faculties do act, it is without force and without procured reasoning, moved more by God than by the soul's own ability."@en ;
    skos:note "The Spanish of the prescription is que aprendan a estarse con atencion y advertencia amorosa en Dios en aquella quietud, y que no se den nada por la imaginacion ni por la obra de ella."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SubidaDelMonteCarmelo ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinMysticism .

pmp:SusrutaSalyaharana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Herausziehen eines steckenden Geschosses"@de,
        "Extracting a lodged shaft"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Susruta Samhita, Sarirasthana VI.26-29, in the English of Kaviraj Kunjalal Bhishagratna, An English Translation of the Sushruta Samhita, vol. II, Calcutta 1911, pp. 178-179" ;
    skos:definition "The removal of a salya — a shaft, dart or other piercing matter — from the body. Chapter VI does not describe the extraction, which belongs to the surgical book; what it adds is the condition on it, that a careful examination of the affected marman is to precede all the acts of extricating a salya from its inside."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because two of the chapter's sharpest claims are about this act and not about the wound: the examination that must come first, and the class of marmas at which the extraction is itself the lethal event. A practice node is needed for both to hang on."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SusrutaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:TaulipangZauberarztApprenticeship a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Werdegang des Zauberarztes: die gestufte Folge von Brechmitteln, Fasten und Tabak"@de,
        "The making of a Zauberarzt: the graded course of emetics, fasting and tobacco"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Theodor Koch-Gruenberg, Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, Band III: Ethnographie (Stuttgart: Strecker und Schroeder, 1923), pp. 203-208, the accounts of Akuli and Katura" ;
    skos:definition "The account Akuli gave of his own training, which the recorder says he reproduces exactly as it was told to him. Preparation begins between the tenth and twelfth year under an older Zauberarzt. The boys first drink water until they bring it up again, then rest five days. Then come emetics made from the finely ground bark of named trees stirred into water, each taken daily for a whole week in a fixed order: prle-yeg, mure-yeg, dzalaura-yeg, peune-yeg, mese-yeg, maualualekalu-yeg, the cinnamon tree maipaime, the wild cashew zoloi-yeg, then the liana kozokozo which is said to cut through all the entrails so that the novices bring up much blood, then the step-shaped liana kapeyenkuma(x)pe, then the bark makalaula, then elikaua, and last ayug. They vomit into a canoe and drink from it again and again. Powdered tube-shaped cicada nests are mixed into the emetics so that the boys get a beautiful voice. Finally they drink strong tobacco brew through the mouth and very strong tobacco brew through the nose, for one day only. The master also twists two cords from his wife's hair, puts them into the boys' nostrils and draws them slowly out through the mouth until blood comes."@en ;
    skos:note "A second and much shorter account of the same thing stands beside it from Katura: five nights of bark dissolved in water, a different mixture each night, vomiting each time, then tobacco brew, eating nothing and growing very thin, then gathering named leaves, binding them into the bundle the Zauberarzt later uses at a cure, climbing up with it, returning, and being a Zauberarzt. The recorder calls it very summary and says little can be done with it. Both are kept here in one node because they are two renderings of one procedure by two men of the same people, and the difference between ten years and five nights is a difference in the telling and not in the office."@en,
        "The diet runs through the whole of it: only small birds, small fish and light kaschiri made from manioc cakes. The cakes are dried ten days in the sun; the master cuts a round hole in the middle, and the novice eats outward from the middle crumb by crumb so that the hole stays round, until it is about as wide as two hands can span. The recorder says he cannot explain this last practice and names two neighbouring usages of the same shape on other occasions, which is the right way round: the observation stands and the interpretation is marked as missing."@en,
        "Two things in the procedure are instruments rather than ordeals, and the transmission says so. The cicada nests are for the voice, and the voice is what the office is carried out with. The hair cord is the ladder the shadow climbs; it is the same device that gives a youth at puberty and a hunter their power, by the same method of transfer, and it is named karauali."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VomRoroimaZumOrinocoIII ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Taulipang .

pmp:ThelemaQuattuorSedes a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die vier Stellungen, mit jedem Muskel angespannt"@de,
        "The four postures, held with every muscle tense"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber E vel Exercitiorum sub figura IX, chap. III, §§ 1-8, in The Equinox I,1 (London, March 1909), pp. 27-28" ;
    skos:definition "Learning to sit perfectly still with every muscle tense for long periods, in one of four named positions: the God, seated in a chair with head up, back straight, knees together, hands on knees and eyes closed; the Dragon, kneeling with the buttocks on the heels and the toes turned back; the Ibis, standing and holding the left ankle with the right hand, the free forefinger on the lips; the Thunderbolt, seated with the left heel pressing up the anus, the right foot poised on its toes and the arms stretched over the knees. No garment is worn that interferes with the posture."@en ;
    skos:note "The duration of the practice, the severity of the pain if any, and the degree of rigidity attained are to be noted down, so the posture is one of the places where the record has something countable to hold."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberEVelExercitiorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmp:VaidikaDarsapurnamasa a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Neu- und Vollmondopfer"@de,
        "The new and full-moon offering"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa I,6,3,15 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East 12, Oxford 1882, p. 167" ;
    skos:definition "The paired offerings at the two turns of the moon, which the transmission treats as the model performance and to which it attaches both the legend of the misspoken formula and the closing act of unyoking. The exegesis states what the full-moon offering is worth to the performer in the same breath as the legend: he who, knowing this, performs it in this wise attains to the same state of prosperity as Indra did."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual .

pmp:VaidikaVasatkara a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Vaṣaṭ-Ruf"@de,
        "The vaṣaṭ call"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aitareya Brāhmaṇa iii,7 (xi,7), in the English of Arthur Berriedale Keith, Rigveda Brahmanas, Harvard Oriental Series 25, Cambridge, Massachusetts 1920, p. 169" ;
    skos:definition "The call with which, at the end of the offering formula, the oblation is poured into the fire. The Aitareya treats it as an instrument in the Hotṛ's hand and distinguishes three: the thunderbolt, made aloud and forcibly, which is hurled at a rival; the hider of his abode, even and continuous and without loss of part of the verse, on which offspring and cattle depend; and the empty, in which the syllable sat fails."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AitareyaBrahmana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:VedicRitual .

pmp:WiwanyangWacipi a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Wiwanyang wacipi, der Sonnenschautanz"@de,
        "Wiwanyang wacipi, the sun-gazing dance"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Frances Densmore, Teton Sioux Music (Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 61, Washington, 1918), pp. 88-90 and pp. 98-99",
        "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), pp. 60-62 and pp. 92-93 and pp. 116-120" ;
    skos:definition "A ceremony in four graded forms, undertaken to fulfil a vow, to secure supernatural aid for another, to secure it for oneself, or to gain supernatural powers for oneself; the fourth purpose requires the fourth form. The candidate takes a mentor, is vitalized, seeks a communication, is consecrated with his tipi and implements, keeps eight rules until the dance begins, fasts and takes no drink from the beginning of his last day in the sacred lodge, and dances gazing at the sun and sounding a whistle while bound by thongs passed through wounds cut in his flesh, until he tears himself free. The forms differ only in the wounds and in what the thongs are made fast to: nothing, buffalo skulls, four stakes, or the sacred pole, with or without the dancer's feet leaving the ground."@en ;
    skos:note "The corpus distinguishes the ceremony from the dance and calls the dance the culminating rite of it. The whole is timed by four signs read off the world and not by a count of days: when the buffalo are fat, when new sprouts of sage are a span long, when the chokecherries are ripening, and when the moon is rising as the sun is going down. The second witness gives the time as the full moon of midsummer, when the trees are in full leaf, the June berries are ripe and the wild sage is fully grown."@en,
        "The second witness names the men who danced and how often, with the counts they gave: one who acted as intercessor four times and danced four times, once suspended; one who received a hundred cuts on his arms at twenty-four; one who danced four times at twenty-one, twenty-three, twenty-four and twenty-five, with about twenty of the last hundred cuts made on the chest for want of room on the arms. Counts of this kind are rare in this graph and are recorded because they are what a later reader can weigh."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by named Oglala shamans, chiefly Sword, Tyon, Ringing-shield, Bad-wound and No-flesh, and by Finger on 25 March 1914; recorded by the agency physician J. R. Walker at Pine Ridge over his years of service there, closed December 1916; given in Lakota, part of it written out in Lakota by Sword and Tyon and translated by Burt Means, Thomas Mills, Bruce Means and Antoin Herman; the paper prints the translations only, so the Lakota wording is not carried." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LakotaRitual .

pmp:Zang a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Bestattung, die das lebendige Qi reitet"@de,
        "Burial that rides the living qi"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zangshu, inner section, opening, in the recension of Wu Cheng, one juan, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text" ;
    skos:definition "The book opens with the sentence that burying is riding the living qi. The five qi run within the earth and issue forth to generate the ten thousand things; the qi scatters when it rides the wind and halts where it is bounded by water, and because the ancients gathered it so that it would not scatter and moved it so that it would halt, the thing is called wind and water. The living is a gathering of qi which congeals into bone and remains alone after death, so that burying is the method of returning the qi into the bone in order to shade what it generated."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination .

pmt:EleusinianMysteries a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Eleusinische Mysterien"@de,
        "Eleusinian mysteries"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Athenian decree concerning Eleusis, IG I³ 6" ;
    skos:definition "The Attic mystery cult of Demeter and Kore, with a lesser rite at Agrai in the spring and the greater rite at Eleusis in the autumn."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. The citation names the corpus in which the inscription is edited, which for an epigraphic source is the edition; what is not recorded is whether the text was read in that corpus or in a secondary report of it, and that is to be established before the reading of face C is relied on."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Athenian decree concerning Eleusis, IG I³ 6" .

pmt:Platonic a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Platonische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Platonic tradition"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Plato, Symposium 210a-212a" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the Platonic dialogues and the ascent they describe from particular to intelligible beauty."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. The edition the Symposium was read in is not recoverable and is left unstated rather than guessed. The Stephanus pagination is stable across the modern editions, so the citations locate the text; what is unknown is whether the Greek or a translation was read."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Plato, Symposium" .

pmw:Anagatavamsa a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Anāgatavaṃsa, die Geschichte des Kommenden"@de,
        "Anāgatavaṃsa, the history of what is to come"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened on 2026-09-24 at the passage on the five disappearances only. The text read is the recension Minayeff marked B, a mixed prose and verse copy of a Rangoon manuscript, printed by him in the Journal of the Pali Text Society for 1886, pp. 33-36, and rendered into English by Henry Clarke Warren, Buddhism in Translations, Harvard 1896, §102, pp. 481-486. The remainder of the work, which is about the next Buddha, was not read. The recension matters and is named because Minayeff printed more than one and they do not agree."@en .

pmw:AnguttaraNikaya a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Aṅguttara Nikāya"@de,
        "Aṅguttara Nikāya"@en ;
    skos:note "Declared here because this run is the first to cite it. It is a work of the Theravāda canon and not of this transmission; it stands under pm:corpusWork of pmt:Ajivika for the same reason the Dīgha Nikāya does, namely that the opponents' canon is the only corpus this transmission has. When pmt:Theravada opens it, the identifier is already minted and does not need to be invented twice."@en .

pmw:DeDivinatione a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Cicero, De divinatione"@de,
        "Cicero, De divinatione"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened on 2026-09-21 at Book II, chapters XLII to XLVII, in the English of C. D. Yonge, The Treatises of M. T. Cicero, Bohn's Classical Library, London 1878. Read continuously; the remainder of both books was not read. Unlike the other four works of this corpus the edition is recoverable, because this passage was read in this run and not inherited."@en .

pmw:DiariumSpirituale a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Swedenborg, Diarium spirituale"@de,
        "Swedenborg, Diarium spirituale"@en ;
    skos:note "Read here in the English of George Bush and John H. Smithson, The Spiritual Diary of Emanuel Swedenborg, volume I, numbers 1 to 1538, James Speirs, London 1883. The entries are numbered in the author's own hand from the manuscript and are dated; the dates of the entries used here fall in January to March 1748."@en,
        "Second, the translation is disowned. The editor of the successor volume writes that on first taking Professor Bush's manuscript in hand he was struck with astonishment at the number of evident errors, some of the most elementary character and the most glaring description, that the further he advanced and the greater the care he exercised the more numerous and grave the errors he detected, and that he grew convinced Bush had never intended the manuscript to reach the printer without a further careful revision by himself. A holding that collects conditions of failure should carry the condition of failure of its own source, and this is it."@en,
        "Two textual cautions, both read at the wording in this volume, and every node resting on this work carries whichever of them applies. First, the beginning is lost. The editors' footnote states that the portion from 1 to 148 inclusive is wanting in the Latin as Tafel published it, that the subsequent publication of Swedenborg's own Index Diarii proves the missing numbers stood in a separate manuscript which has not been discovered and is feared irrecoverably lost, and that the heads or subjects of nearly all of them have been restored from that Index and occupy the place of the missing text. So numbers 3 to 8 and 148, which carry most of the criteria for telling a deceiving spirit, are index headings and not the author's paragraphs."@en .

pmw:DivusVespasianus a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Sueton, Divus Vespasianus"@de,
        "Suetonius, Divus Vespasianus"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened at chapter 7. The edition is not recoverable; see prima-materia#45."@en .

pmw:JindanDayao a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Chen Zhixu, Jindan dayao"@de,
        "Chen Zhixu, Jindan dayao"@en ;
    skos:note "Named here as a work of the corpus and unopened. Its juan division is itself edition-dependent, which is why the two nodes citing it name a juan without an edition and are flagged in the file's edition note."@en .

pmw:KausitakiBrahmana a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Kauṣītaki-Brāhmaṇa"@de,
        "Kauṣītaki Brāhmaṇa"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened on 2026-09-19 in the same volume and translation as the Aitareya. Read at the wording: chapter xxvi, sections 4 to 6, which carry the named disagreement about whether a detected flaw in the recitation has to be repaired at all. The registration of this tradition did not name this work; it is added to the corpus at the instance because the reading found it there, and the coined identifier of the tradition is kept unchanged."@en .

pmw:LiberIIIVelJugorum a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Liber III vel Jugorum"@de,
        "Liber III vel Jugorum"@en ;
    skos:note "Class D, an official ritual and instruction. Published with a photographic plate of a practitioner's forearm and a printed verdict on his performance."@en .

pmw:MishkatAlMasabih a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Mishkāt al-Maṣābīḥ"@de,
        "Mishkāt al-Maṣābīḥ"@en ;
    skos:note "Not opened. The work is known to this holding only through the passages Hughes quotes from its twenty-first book, and the quotation is his English and not a translation this run can check against an original."@en .

pmw:MishnahSanhedrin a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Mischna, Traktat Sanhedrin"@de,
        "Mishnah, tractate Sanhedrin"@en ;
    skos:note "Danby prints Mishnah and Tosefta in alternating paragraphs under subject headings of his own, and says in his second footnote that this arrangement is not that of the original texts. The subject number is therefore his and is not cited here; what is cited is his reference to the Mishnah and the Tosefta and the page of his volume."@en,
        "Read in the translation of Herbert Danby, Tractate Sanhedrin: Mishnah and Tosefta, the judicial procedure of the Jews as codified towards the end of the second century A.D., in the series Translations of Early Documents, Series III, Rabbinic Texts, London, Society for Promoting Christian Knowledge, 1919. The edition is named from the title pages in the full text and not from a catalogue line."@en,
        "The chapter and paragraph number of this passage is not one number. It is 7:11 in the Babylonian division, which Danby follows; the Palestinian division of the same chapter runs to thirteen halakhot and puts it at 7:13; Rodkinson's English prints it as Mishnah XII. The place is therefore given with the edition and never as a bare number."@en .

pmw:MoradasDelCastilloInterior a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Die Wohnungen der inneren Burg"@de,
        "Las Moradas del castillo interior"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened at one chapter only, Moradas sextas 3, and opened for the counter-search of pmc:SubidaVisionesVeraeFallaces rather than for its own sake. The rest is unread, so this work is not harvested and a later run opening it will find most of it standing."@en .

pmc:AegyptiaPortaeNomina a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ich lasse dich nicht vorbei, wenn du nicht meinen Namen nennst"@de,
        "I will not let thee pass unless thou tell my name"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 125, the address to the gods of the hall of the two Maati, Papyrus of Nu, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London 1901, vol. II, pp. 375-377" ;
    skos:definition "The failure condition of the whole apparatus, stated as a dialogue and repeated part by part. At the hall of the two Maati every element of the doorway speaks in turn and refuses passage on the same terms: the bolt, the right lintel, the left lintel, the threshold, the fastening, the socket of the fastening, the guardian, the doorposts, the floor of the hall, the doorkeeper. Each says that it will not let him enter, or pass over, or open to him, or make mention of him, unless he tells its name; each name is then given; and each concludes that since he knows it he may pass. The floor of the hall adds a condition of its own — it will not be trodden on by one who does not know the names of its two feet — and the doorkeeper, who is called the discerner of hearts and searcher of the reins, will not announce him until he has named a further god."@en ;
    skos:note "Here the corpus says what happens to a man who does not know, and it says it a dozen times in one chapter: he does not pass. Set beside pmc:AegyptiaRubricaScientiae this completes the construction — knowing is the operation, not knowing is the failure, the gates are the assay, and the book is what the buyer acquires against the assay. Nothing in the arrangement is left to chance except whether the man has the text."@en,
        "Two things the passage does not do. It never says that anyone failed; the dialogue is written from the answer sheet, with every name supplied in the same breath as the question. And the same corpus prescribes an amulet to keep the heart from testifying at the weighing that follows, so the one element of the examination which the candidate cannot answer is provided for by an object rather than by knowledge. See pmc:AegyptiaCorNeTestetur."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmc:AegyptiaRubricaScientiae a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wenn dieses Kapitel gewusst wird: was das Wissen erlangt"@de,
        "If this chapter be known: what knowing is said to obtain"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapters 64, 83, 86 and 125, rubrics, Papyrus of Nu, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London 1901, vol. I, pp. 217-218 and 221-222, and vol. II, pp. 269, 276-277 and 377-378" ;
    skos:definition "The recurring postscript that attaches an attainment to the knowing of a chapter. The places read carry these. Chapter 64: if this chapter be known, he shall be victorious both upon earth and in the underworld, he shall do whatsoever a man doeth who is upon the earth, and he shall perform all the deeds which those do who are alive; the longer version adds that he shall not be repulsed at any gate of the underworld, either in going in or in coming out, that he shall perform all the transformations which his heart shall desire, and that he shall not die. Chapter 83: he shall come forth pure by day after his death, he shall perform whatsoever transformations his heart desireth, and no evil thing whatsoever shall have dominion over him for ever and ever. Chapter 86: he shall come forth by day, he shall not be turned back at any gate in the underworld, and he shall make his transformation into a swallow regularly and continually. Chapter 125, after its conditions: he shall flourish, his children shall flourish, his name shall never fall into oblivion, and he shall be as one who filleth the heart of the king and of his princes."@en ;
    skos:note "Read beside pmc:VaidikaYaEvamVeda in traditions/vedic-ritual.ttl, which is the same construction in a corpus with which no contact route is available. The correspondence is asserted at convergences/knowing-as-the-condition.ttl and not here."@en,
        "Three things about the form, all of them in the wording and none of them read into it. The condition is knowledge and not performance, so two men buried with the same papyrus are promised different things and nothing distinguishes them. The attainments are of two kinds and the corpus does not separate them — passage and transformation after death, which nobody can report, and flourishing children and the favour of the king, which anybody can. And the rubric is attached to the chapter rather than contained in it, which matters because the rubrics move: the editors of 1901 and 1904 both note chapters whose rubric stands elsewhere in another manuscript, and one of them holds that the rubric of chapter 1 was originally the rubric of chapter 72."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmc:AyurvedaVisavighata a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Gift, das bei Berührung des Körpers seine Kraft verliert"@de,
        "Poison losing its force on contact with the body"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.1.58-60, I.1.76 and I.3.28-33, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, parts XXXIII-XXXIV, Calcutta 1904-1905, pp. 1024, 1028 and 1048" ;
    skos:definition "Claimed of three preparations in the same wording: if poison comes in contact with his system it loses its force; if poison comes in contact with his body it fails to affect him; poison cannot affect him. It is stated as a standing property of the user and not as a remedy applied after a poisoning."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the most nearly falsifiable capacity in the lesson and the only one of the set that a single trial would settle, which is presumably why no trial is reported. The lesson elsewhere prescribes a drug it calls keen and like fire and sets a counted ceiling on it, so the transmission is not indifferent to what a substance does to a body; the immunity is claimed beside that ceiling and not instead of it."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmc:CizhongWuzang a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Der Treffer ins Depot: die Frist und das Zeichen"@de,
        "Striking a depot: the interval and the sign"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 52 (Ci jin lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 14" ;
    skos:definition "A needle driven into one of the depots is said to kill after a stated interval, and each interval is paired with a bodily sign by which the strike is to be recognised. The heart: death in one day, the movement belching. The liver: five days, speech. The kidney: six days, sneezing. The lung: three days, coughing. The spleen: ten days, swallowing. The gall bladder: a day and a half, vomiting. The same chapter continues with strikes that maim rather than kill and names the outcome for each: into the brain through the occipital opening, death on the spot; the vessel in the face, blindness; the marrow between the vertebrae, a bent back; the knee cap, lameness; the arm's great yin vessel, immediate death from loss of blood."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because of the form and not the content. This is a claim that names an outcome it excludes: a person struck in the heart who is alive on the third day is not covered by any weaker reading of the sentence, and the sign attached to each interval says what the strike is supposed to look like while the interval runs. Very little in this graph is stated that way."@en,
        "The intervals are not stable across the witnesses, and the transmitted text says so about itself. That is held in the dispute node below rather than in this definition, which gives the numbers as the recension read prints them."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Cizhen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmc:Dibbacakkhu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Göttliches Auge"@de,
        "Divine eye"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XIII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "Sight beyond the range of the physical eye."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:DivyaSrotra a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Göttliches Ohr"@de,
        "Divine ear"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.41" ;
    skos:definition "Hearing beyond the range of the physical ear."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PatanjalaYoga .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicFallendeKrankheitHeilung a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Weichen der fallenden Krankheit"@de,
        "The ceasing of the falling sickness"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 27",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 17" ;
    skos:definition "The effect the hung slip is worn for: that the falling sickness leaves the sufferer."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1820,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicGichterHeilung a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Weichen der Gichter"@de,
        "The ceasing of the fits"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 29",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 18" ;
    skos:definition "The effect the knotting is performed for: that the fits, counted by the charm as seventy-sevenfold, leave the body at the place the crosses are made."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1820,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicSchmerzStillungAmGlied a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Stillen der Schmerzen an einem Glied oder im Munde"@de,
        "The stilling of pain in a limb or in the mouth"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Abschnitt »Zeugniße«",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), pp. 5-6" ;
    skos:definition "The outcome claimed four times in the testimonials for an arm, a finger, a leg with a running sore and a child's very sore mouth: the pain is relieved or the part healed, in a little more than twenty-four hours or in a short time."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1820,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:FourAbsorptions a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die vier Vertiefungen"@de,
        "The four absorptions"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)" ;
    skos:definition "A graded series in which each stage is reached by letting go of a factor still present in the one before it."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DighaNikaya,
        pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:MagnetismeLucidite a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Lucidité, die Hellsicht des Somnambulen"@de,
        "Lucidity, the clairvoyance of the somnambulist"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. V, pp. 84-85, and chap. VIII, pp. 177-178 and 185" ;
    skos:definition "A faculty developed in some somnambulists: seeing without the aid of the eyes, hearing without the ears, seeing at a distance, reading the thoughts, appreciating time with rigorous exactitude, and having a presage of the future. It requires the somnambulist to concentrate his faculties upon a single object without distraction and without any foreign influence changing the direction of his mind. It is not always accompanied by a perfect clairvoyance, is often relative to a certain order of ideas, and is variable in its intensity from one moment to another."@en ;
    skos:note "The source grants the faculty and then removes, one by one, every condition under which it could be demonstrated: it is rare, it is not extended equally to all objects, it varies between moments, it is destroyed by an unsympathetic witness, it is destroyed by the presence of a physician not familiar with the practice, it declines in somnambulists consulted by several operators, and it is not to be put to any proof. What remains is a capacity asserted together with the grounds for dismissing every failed demonstration of it."@en,
        "The source's own judgement of the professional consultants it examined is the most useful sentence in the chapter and is entered as his: among those he observed there is not one he has not known to commit errors, and not one that has not shown him proofs of clairvoyance. He also reports having been for three days the dupe of a feigned somnambulism."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:ParacittaJnana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kenntnis fremden Bewusstseins"@de,
        "Knowledge of another mind"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.19" ;
    skos:definition "Knowledge of the contents of another consciousness, said to follow from saṃyama on the presented idea."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PatanjalaYoga .

pmc:TurbaTincturaInvariabilis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die unveränderliche Tinktur, die einen Körper färbt und das Feuer nicht flieht"@de,
        "The invariable tincture that colours a body and does not flee the fire"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 25 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)",
        "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 9 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity the work is for. A tincture is reached whose colour does not vary, does not flee from the fire and cannot be rubbed out; imposed on the coins of the vulgar it turns them into gold. The work states of it that it colours nothing until it has itself been coloured, that a dense thing does not tinge a tenuous one, and that the tincture is a soul extracted from a body and then put back into a body."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim as the work makes it has one property that decides how it could ever be examined: the outcome is a metal in somebody's hand, which any bystander can weigh, hammer and look at. It is one of the few capacities in this holding whose success would be readable by a stranger without the operator's report. What the work does not state is any act by which the stranger should read it — see pmp:TurbaMateriaeProbaSearch in examinations/searches-latin-alchemy.ttl."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy .

pmc:YangxiShouzhen a pm:Receiving ;
    rdfs:label "Yang Xi schreibt nieder, was die Vollkommenen diktieren"@de,
        "Yang Xi writes out what the Perfected dictate"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 19.9b-19.10a (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 19" ;
    skos:definition "The work states of itself that the upper Clarity scriptures entered the world in the second year of the Xingning period of the Jin, a jiazi year, when the Lady Wei of the Southern Peak descended to the retainer Yang and had him write out in clerical script what was dictated, for transmission to the guard commandant Xu and his third son; and that the surviving autographs amount to some ten scrolls of scripture and forty-odd scrolls of received matter."@en ;
    skos:note "The interest of the node is not the claim but its bookkeeping. The corpus dates its sessions to the night, names the human hand that wrote, names the two men the writing was for, and the compiler four hundred years later works from the sheets themselves. Very little in this holding is transmitted with that much of its own machinery visible, and the machinery is what makes the dispute nodes below possible at all."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:claimedSpeaker "The Perfected: the Lady Wei of the Southern Peak, the Lady of Purple Tenuity, the Lady Right Blossom of Cloud Forest, the Lord of the Western Citadel, the two younger Mao lords in their offices of Fate and Register, and others named at their several descents" ;
    pm:receivedBy "Yang Xi 楊羲, retainer of the Wang ducal establishment, writing in clerical script" ;
    pm:receptionCircumstance "Stated as nocturnal descents at which the Perfected spoke and Yang Xi wrote; the writing was then copied out again for the elder Xu, so that a session leaves an original hand and a fair copy. The compiler says the fair copies are the tidy ones and the hurried, blotted and corrected sheets are the ones written while the words were coming. Dates are given for many sessions and the compiler orders the whole by them where he can, saying at 19.4b that beyond one year the dates run out of sequence and that sheets carrying no date at all he leaves where they were." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pm:AgencyHolding a owl:Class,
        pm:Scale ;
    rdfs:label "Trägerschaft der Handlung"@de,
        "Agency Holding"@en ;
    skos:definition "The axis on which a source's account of who acts is placed: the practitioner, or another to whom the practitioner has yielded."@en ;
    pm:scaleFamily pm:subjectAxis .

pm:AwarenessLocus a owl:Class,
        pm:Scale ;
    rdfs:label "Ort des Bewusstseins"@de,
        "Awareness Locus"@en ;
    skos:definition "The axis on which a source's account of where the awareness is located during the act is placed: with the body, or apart from it."@en ;
    pm:scaleFamily pm:subjectAxis .

pm:CounterSearchState a owl:Class,
        pm:Scale ;
    rdfs:label "Stand der Gegensuche"@de,
        "Counter-Search State"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "How far the attempt to find work opposing the evidence has been carried."@en ;
    skos:note "A study cited under pm:evidenceFrom is a party and not an instance. It carries its own claim, its own method and its own critics, and a protocol that admits it without looking for opposition has not examined the claim against the world but against one report of the world. That is the same error as testing a transmission against this holding instead of against its source, which SPEC §13 forbids, and it is easier to commit because a study looks like a verdict."@en,
        "The state exists because the prose rule did not hold. It was written into AGENTS.md on 2026-09-02 after evidence was entered unread, and within the same session evidence was entered again without any search for opposition. A rule that has to be remembered at the moment of writing is a rule that fails at the moment of haste."@en ;
    pm:scaleFamily pm:recordScale .

pm:PrerequisiteStrength a owl:Class,
        pm:Scale ;
    rdfs:label "Stärke der Voraussetzung"@de,
        "Prerequisite Strength"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "How firmly a source sets a prerequisite: as a necessity, as an instruction, or merely by the order in which it presents its material."@en ;
    skos:note "The three must not collapse. A list of eight limbs in the order given is not the same claim as a sentence saying that the eighth is unreachable without the seventh, and reading the first as the second is the commonest way a modern system acquires an ancient warrant it never had."@en ;
    pm:scaleFamily pm:sourceModalityScale .

pm:Receiving a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Empfangen"@de,
        "Receiving"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "The claim, made in or about a work, that its wording was received from a speaker other than the person who wrote or spoke it down."@en ;
    skos:note "Distinct from pm:Originating, which names the author with whom a claim begins and the ascription it circulates under. A receiving makes no finding about who really composed the material; it records that the work claims a speaker, so that the claim is addressable, countable and disputable instead of riding invisibly in a citation."@en,
        "Whether an agreement between two such corpora can ever be more than reception is a separate question and a hard one. The route is documented across the whole block: the vocabulary runs on from Theosophy. A convergence there needs the demonstration that a receiver did not know the earlier literature, and that demonstration is expensive and rare."@en .

pm:ScaleFamily a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Skalenfamilie"@de,
        "Scale Family"@en ;
    skos:definition "The kind of question a scale answers. Three families are distinguished because a statement about the state of the record, a statement about the firmness of a source and a statement about the subject matter must never be read alike."@en .

pm:StateAcquisition a owl:Class,
        pm:Scale ;
    rdfs:label "Erwerb des Zustands"@de,
        "State Acquisition"@en ;
    skos:definition "The axis on which a source's account of how a condition comes to someone is placed: through effort directed at it, or not."@en ;
    pm:scaleFamily pm:subjectAxis .

pm:StateDuration a owl:Class,
        pm:Scale ;
    rdfs:label "Dauer des Zustands"@de,
        "State Duration"@en ;
    skos:definition "The axis on which a source's account of how long a condition holds is placed: passing of itself, or abiding once it is there."@en ;
    pm:scaleFamily pm:subjectAxis .

pmp:Anapanasati a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Ānāpānasati"@de,
        "Ānāpānasati"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga VIII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Majjhima Nikāya 118" ;
    skos:definition "Mindfulness of in-breath and out-breath, set out as a sixteen-step progression in the discourse and as a meditation subject in the handbook."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MajjhimaNikaya,
        pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmp:ArsNotoriaOratio a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Sprechen der Orationen"@de,
        "The saying of the orations"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), §§ 14-15 and pp. 13-14, ed. Peterson" ;
    skos:definition "Pronouncing the orations of the art on the prescribed days of the moon, at the prescribed hours and with the prescribed number of repetitions, alone, secretly and in a voice barely audible to the speaker, having fasted on the first day and living chastely."@en ;
    skos:note "The procedure the text prescribes is larger than this node: the orations accompany the notae, and the witness read carries none of them. What is entered is the part the witness gives."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmp:ClaviculaOperatioCirculi a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Operation im Kreis"@de,
        "The operation within the circle"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Clavicula Salomonis, Book I chapter III, in the English of S. L. MacGregor Mathers, The Key of Solomon the King, London: George Redway, 1889" ;
    skos:definition "The book divides its experiments in two: those that can be performed without a circle, for which only the chapter of the particular operation is to be observed, and those that cannot be brought to perfection without one. The second kind runs: nine days of continence for master and disciples; after the sixth day frequent prayer and confession; on the seventh the master alone bathes in exorcised water and puts on white linen, then pours the same water over the naked disciples; the last three days a fast; on the last day a washing at running water, the confession, the kiss, the absolution, the distribution of the instruments; then the circle, traced with the consecrated knife, with the letter Tau at the four quarters, four hexagonal pentacles and the divine names between them, two squares circumscribed and four further circles at the outer angles; then the fumigation, the placing of the disciples at the four quarters, and the conjurations."@en ;
    skos:note "The second kind is what makes the transmission examinable at all: it fixes in advance what counts as having done the thing, down to the number of days, the material of the garment and the position of each participant. A procedure specified to that depth can be departed from in a way a witness could name. What follows below is the account the book gives of what happens when it is not departed from and nothing occurs."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ClaviculaSalomonis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SolomonicGrimoire .

pmp:CmShoufuYinshui a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Talismanwasser trinken, beichten und die Eingabe hinaufsenden"@de,
        "Swallowing the talisman water, confessing, and sending up the petition"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Lu Xiujing, Lu xiansheng daomen kelüe 001b-002a and 008a (DZ 1127), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile",
        "Sanguozhi 8.28a-28b, Dianlüe quoted in Pei Songzhi's commentary, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text (Zhonghua shuju 8.264)" ;
    skos:definition "The procedure prescribed for illness: no needling, no moxa and no decoction, but the swallowing of a talisman in water, the confession of the faults committed from the year of birth onward, a change of conduct, and a petition sent up to the offices of heaven. The outside chronicle describes the confession in its earliest form as three written copies of the sick person's name and statement of submission to the fault, one placed on a mountain for heaven, one buried for earth and one sunk in water, called the handwritings to the three officers."@en ;
    skos:note "The two descriptions are a century and more apart and agree on the core. What the later one adds is the exclusion of medicine, stated as a boundary of the transmission rather than as a preference: at 008a the author lists the swallowing of talismans, the confession and the petition as what the covenantal authority permits, and names the needle, the moxa and the decoction as what it does not."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LuxianshengDaomenKelue,
        pmw:Sanguozhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:CelestialMasters .

pmp:FinnicSynnynLukeminen a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Aussprechen des Ursprungs der Sache"@de,
        "Pronouncing the origin of the thing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John Abercromby, The Pre- and Proto-historic Finns, vol. II (London: David Nutt, 1898), § 210 a, p. 332" ;
    skos:altLabel "synnyn sanat"@en,
        "synty"@en ;
    skos:definition "Reciting, to the cause of a hurt, the account of how it came to be: its birth, its parentage and its descent, in the words that are handed down. The songs treat it as a thing the operator either has or lacks, and the collection carries a separate formula for the case in which he lacks it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:FinnicIncantation .

pmp:HippocratesTrepanatio a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Trepanieren des Schädels"@de,
        "Trepanning the skull"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, On Injuries of the Head 9, 14, 17, 18 and 21, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, pp. 451-452, 459, 462-463 and 465-466" ;
    skos:definition "The operation the treatise is written around: the bone is sawed or perforated to give issue to the blood and to reach the injury that the raspatory has shown. Two instruments are distinguished — a serrated or crown trepan which is used when the case is taken over from another and is carried at once down to the membrane, and a perforator which is not to be carried through — and the chapter prescribes a different procedure according to whether the surgeon has had the management of the case from the first."@en ;
    skos:note "The indication is stated as a limit and not as a licence, which is unusual enough to record: chapter 9 says that a hedra without fracture and contusion does not require trepanning, that a large and wide notch does not, and that those bones which are most pressed and broken require trepanning the least. Chapter 17 adds that depressed fragments are not to be interfered with."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeVulneribusCapitis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine .

pmp:IuratusOpusVisionis a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Werk der göttlichen Schau"@de,
        "The work of the divine vision"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber iuratus Honorii, capp. XCVIII-CI (Royal MS 17 A. xlii, fols. 58v-62r), ed. Peterson; the Latin of the perfectio operis in Boudet's edition of Sloane MS 3854, section 14, at p. 879" ;
    skos:definition "The operation of the second chapter of the first book, as the witness gives it. After the first purification the operator keeps himself clean and remembers God, then fasts a further twelve days on bread and water with no other refection, as he did on the earlier days, until he comes to the thirteenth day, which must be a Thursday; on that day he purges any sin he perceives in himself and receives the body of Christ. On the Thursday early in the morning he says what he said before, makes a couch of hay, strews clean sifted ashes about it and writes in them the hundred names of God; washes in cold spring water; puts on a haircloth next his skin and black clothes, sits in the choir and says the psalter with the litany and the proper prayers. Then he sleeps, and speaks no more."@en ;
    skos:note "The thirteenth day is a Thursday and the operation is performed on it, so the whole sequence is pinned to a weekday and cannot be started on an arbitrary morning. The witness for this chapter is the English of Royal MS 17 A. xlii; the Latin of Sloane MS 3854 for the perfectio operis is in Boudet's edition and agrees on the Thursday, the ashes and the hundred names."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberIuratusHonorii ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmp:JibaroIhambratinyu a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Fasten des Töters"@de,
        "The slayer's fasting"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Rafael Karsten, Blood Revenge, War, and Victory Feasts among the Jibaro Indians of Eastern Ecuador, Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 79 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1923), pp. 19 and 34-35" ;
    skos:definition "Nothing is prescribed while the warriors prepare for the war and march to it; the fasting begins the moment an enemy has been killed. On the way home the slayers eat only boiled and mashed manioc, nauma, cooked by themselves and by no other man and still less a woman, and taken with small wooden pins and never with the fingers, the hands being polluted with blood. They may not bathe or wash in any way until they reach home, and arrive soiled with blood, as do their loin cloth and the lance and the knife. At home the diet is named: nauma, the root fruit sangu which the Quichua call mandi, the dish ambi of manioc and sangu leaves; forbidden are pork, chicken, the large wild hog unta pakki and any kind of monkey; allowed are the small peccary yankipi, the agouti kayuka, only the small forest birds taken with blowpipe and unpoisoned arrows, and only the smallest fish, sardines and the small shellfish chumakai shachma. Manioc beer is forbidden for the first two days. For the first month he may not sleep in his house and passes the nights in a small ranch on the river bank; at the end of the month he stands under a waterfall, paccha or sasa, and lets the water fall on his naked body, and may then sleep in the house but in the men's fore-room tangámasha and not with his wife. The diet and the abstinence run for two months, or among some Jibaros three."@en ;
    skos:note "A counted observance with a counted release inside it, which is rare in this holding: the month that ends at the waterfall is a station, and the two or three months are the whole. The work records its own variation — two months or three — without resolving it, and that is the transmission's variation and not the recorder's uncertainty, since he states it as a difference between Jibaros."@en,
        "The restrictions after a killing inside the tribe are stated to be much milder than those after killing an enemy of a foreign tribe. What is entered here is the milder set, read at pages 19 and 34 to 35; the stricter set belongs to the chapters this run did not read."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:KarstenBloodRevenge ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JibaroShuara .

pmp:MideInitiationSecondDegree a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Einweihung in den zweiten Grad"@de,
        "Initiation into the second degree"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 168" ;
    skos:definition "The second entry: three feasts to the instructing and officiating priests, three chanted prayers to the named manidō, and a second shooting of the shell."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin .

pmp:NoaidiJourney a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Reise bei stillliegendem Leib"@de,
        "The journey made while the body lies still"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 55-56" ;
    skos:definition "The practitioner beats the drum and sings, others present singing with him in higher and lower notes. He then casts himself on the ground, grows black in the face and lies as if dead, the drum laid as near as possible on his head, sweating. Those present do not leave off singing for the whole time. When he awakes he relates all the circumstances of the business enquired after."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SamiNoaidi .

pmp:PgmIvSpiritusTractus a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Atem aus den Strahlen ziehen, dreimal so tief wie möglich"@de,
        "Drawing breath from the rays, three times as deeply as one can"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.475-829, first instruction and fifth and eighth utterances (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1907)" ;
    skos:definition "The first instruction of the rite: to take breath from the sun's rays, inhaling three times as deeply as the operator is able. Later stages prescribe the counter-movement — a prolonged bellowing like a horn-note, expelling the whole breath with pressure on the ribs, and a second bellowing with pressure on the belly to set the senses going all at once."@en ;
    skos:note "The most concrete bodily instruction in the whole of this graph's ascent material. It names the direction of the breath, its depth, its count and, at the exhalation, where the pressure is applied. Nothing in the ophite, hermetic or Jeu material comes near this, and it is the reason the register singled this corpus out."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri .

pmp:ShangqingCunsi a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Vergegenwärtigen"@de,
        "Actualising"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 9.5a-9.5b (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 9" ;
    skos:definition "Holding a thing before the inner sight until it is present: the sun in the heart, the moon in the upper cinnabar field, the spirits of the body at their stations, the five planets descending into the chamber. The corpus treats it as an operation with a prescribed posture, a prescribed count of swallowings and grindings of the teeth, and a stated period after which its effect is to appear."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmp:ShangqingFushi a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Einnehmen der Mittel"@de,
        "Ingesting the drugs"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 10.4a-10.4b (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 10" ;
    skos:definition "The compounding and taking of prepared substances: atractylodes boiled down with honey and dates, sesame oil rendered with leek, mineral preparations taken by the knife-point measure. The corpus gives quantities, sequences of steps, daily doses and a graded timetable of effects, and it gives beside them the conditions under which the same drug is said to do nothing."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmp:ThelemaAscensusInPlanis a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Steigen auf den Ebenen"@de,
        "Rising on the planes"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber O vel Manus et Sagittae sub figura VI, chap. V, §§ 1-12, and chap. VI, §§ 1-6, in The Equinox I,2 (London, September 1909), pp. 27-30" ;
    skos:definition "After the preliminary purifications, banishings and invocations, imagining one's own figure as enveloping the physical body or standing in front of it, transferring the seat of consciousness to that figure so that one seems to see with its eyes and hear with its ears, causing it to rise to a great height above the earth, stopping, looking about, speaking to the figures that approach and insisting on being answered. The developed form omits the stopping: the practitioner continues to rise until fatigue almost overcomes him and, whatever threatens or allures, forces his way upward until his whole being is swallowed up in fatigue and he sinks into unconsciousness, writing down soberly and accurately a record of all that has occurred on coming to himself."@en ;
    skos:note "The instruction calls the transfer of consciousness the great difficulty of the operation and, a page later, calls the whole experiment perfectly easy to perform, adding that two or three attempts should render the student confident and even expert. Both sentences are in the same chapter."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberOVelManusEtSagittae ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmp:ThelemaPranayamaNumerata a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die gezählten Atemzyklen"@de,
        "The counted breath cycles"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber E vel Exercitiorum sub figura IX, chap. IV, §§ 1-9, in The Equinox I,1 (London, March 1909), p. 29" ;
    skos:definition "At rest in one of the postures, the right nostril closed with the thumb, breathing out slowly and completely through the left nostril while the watch marks twenty seconds, and in through the same nostril for ten, then changing hands and repeating with the other nostril, continuous for one hour. Then, when each stage is quite easy and not before, the periods rise: thirty and fifteen; out fifteen, in fifteen, held fifteen; out forty, in twenty; out twenty, in ten, held thirty."@en ;
    skos:note "Two conditions are stated with it. Food in the stomach, even in small quantities, makes the practice very difficult; and the practitioner is never to overstrain, in particular never to get so short of breath that he is compelled to breathe out jerkily or rapidly. The second is both a caution and a failure indicator, and it is entered again as a caution below."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberEVelExercitiorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmt:ChukcheeShamanism a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Tschuktschische Schamanenüberlieferung"@de,
        "Chukchee shamanic transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Waldemar Bogoras, The Chukchee, Memoir of the American Museum of Natural History vol. VII (Leiden: E. J. Brill, 1904-1909), pp. 433-435" ;
    skos:definition "The northeastern Siberian transmission of a performance held in a closed and darkened sleeping-room, in which the practitioner drums and sings without words until the spirits appear, and in which a second person present answers him."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered and worked in the same act, which SPEC §14 allows and which is stated here rather than glossed over: a registration claims only that the transmission exists and where it lies, and this file goes past that for three pages and no further."@en,
        "The contact route is entered as documented and toward one transmission this graph already holds. The recorder states that the use of unmixed tobacco as a stimulant before the performance is copied from the Tungus shamans, and pmt:SiberianShamanism is the Tungus file. That is a route in the material and not a geographical guess; it does not touch the North Atlantic question, for which no route of any kind is known in either direction."@en,
        "The lie of the corpus. One recorder, working in the field of the Jesup North Pacific Expedition around the turn of the twentieth century among the Reindeer and the Maritime Chukchee, publishing in English with the Chukchee terms in his own transcription and with a separate volume of texts in the language. He is the first fixation in writing of what he reports, which is what makes him a witness here and not a secondary summary; what he is not is a chorus, and a second recorder would be worth more than a second reading of him."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:workOpened ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Waldemar Bogoras, The Chukchee, Memoir of the American Museum of Natural History vol. VII (Jesup North Pacific Expedition vol. 7), Leiden and New York 1904-1909; Bogoras, Chukchee Materials" .

pmt:Hesychasm a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Hesychasmus"@de,
        "Hesychasm"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Gregory Palamas, Triads in Defence of the Holy Hesychasts" ;
    skos:definition "The Byzantine tradition of the prayer of the heart and of the vision of the uncreated light, defended in the fourteenth century against the objection that such a light must be created."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. For every citation in this file the edition or translation the passage was read from is not recoverable, and it is left unstated rather than guessed. The Philokalia exists in Greek and in several translations whose contents and numbering differ, so a citation naming only the volume does not yet locate a text."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Philokalia, volumes I and IV; Gregory Palamas, Triads in Defence of the Holy Hesychasts" .

pmt:SanskritDramaturgy a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Sanskritische Dramaturgie"@de,
        "Sanskrit dramaturgy"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Nāṭyaśāstra VII" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the Nāṭyaśāstra and the theory of aesthetic states that follows from it."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. For both citations the edition or translation the passage was read from is not recoverable, and it is left unstated rather than guessed. The chapter division of the Nāṭyaśāstra differs between the recensions, so the number seven does not by itself locate the passage on the bodily signs."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Nāṭyaśāstra; Abhinavagupta, Abhinavabhāratī" .

pmt:Upanisadic a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Upaniṣadische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Upaniṣadic tradition"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chāndogya Upaniṣad" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the Upaniṣads as the speculative layer of the Vedic corpus."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. Chāndogya 7.15.1 carries the edition it was read from and was reopened in the Sanskrit for that purpose. For 1.11.5 the edition is not recoverable and is left unstated rather than guessed."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Chāndogya Upaniṣad; Māṇḍūkya Upaniṣad" .

pmw:LiberTurrisVelDomusDei a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Liber Turris vel Domus Dei sub figura XVI"@de,
        "Liber Turris vel Domus Dei sub figura XVI"@en ;
    skos:note "Class B."@en .

pmw:MajjhimaNikaya a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Majjhima Nikāya"@de,
        "Majjhima Nikāya"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened at sutta 118. The edition is not recoverable; see prima-materia#45."@en .

pmw:NaturalisHistoria a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Plinius, Naturalis Historia"@de,
        "Pliny, Naturalis Historia"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened at VII.174. The edition is not recoverable; see prima-materia#45."@en .

pmw:RIH7820 a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Die Tafel RIH 78/20"@de,
        "The tablet RIH 78/20"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "KTU 1.169"@en ;
    skos:note "A convergence this work invites and that is not entered. The Anatolian ritual of Paškuwatti, read in the strand beside this one, is performed for a man who has no procreation or is not a man toward a woman, and on the reading of the edition used here this tablet treats the same complaint. Two reasons not to write the node. The contact route between the two corpora is documented and named inside this corpus, so the convergence could not carry pm:independentAttestation; and the identification of this work's subject is the open question just recorded, so the convergence would rest on a reading rather than on two wordings. A convergence is asserted and not found, and this one would be asserted on one editor's title."@en,
        "Opened at the wording on 2026-10-06 in Pardee, Ritual and Cult at Ugarit, WAW 10, 2002, text 49, transliteration and translation, with the editor's introduction and his notes read. The editio princeps is Bordreuil and Caquot's in Syria 57, 1980, pp. 346-350, whose page images are reachable and whose optical transcription of the transliteration is not character-reliable; it was not used as a source."@en,
        "What this work is about is unsettled and the disagreement is not marginal. The edition read here titles it an incantation against male sexual dysfunction; a second reachable translation of 1998 titles it an incantation for the exorcism of a possessed adolescent; the editio princeps reads it as a promise of a god's help against tormenting demons; and a fourth treatment reads it as against sorcery by a spoken word. The author of the 1998 translation states in his own introduction to it that scholars disagree on most matters of interpretation and that his translation is provisional. Two of the places entered below do not depend on which reading is right, and the node that does says so."@en .

pmw:RS1002 a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Die Tafel RS 1.002"@de,
        "The tablet RS 1.002"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "CTA 32"@en,
        "KTU 1.40"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened at the wording on 2026-10-06 in Pardee, Ritual and Cult at Ugarit, WAW 10, 2002, text 22, pp. 77-83, transliteration and translation, with the editor's introduction read in full. The editio princeps is Virolleaud's plate of 1929."@en,
        "The editor states three things about this work that the nodes depend on, and they bear on how much of it can be held. First, the rite is attested in an unparalleled number of exemplars for this corpus, spread over three buildings and several scribal hands, which he takes to show that the texts were not the product of a single school and that the rite was performed with some regularity. Second, the key term survives for only one of its three themes, and the expiatory character of two sections is his deduction from the occurrence of the word for sin. Third, he avoids the word atonement for it, because the Hebrew root usually so translated does not occur here. The node for the one preserved term is entered as his reading and marked as such."@en,
        "What was not read. The second substantially preserved exemplar of the same rite, and the four further fragments, are named in the edition and are not printed in it; the differences between performances that the editor says they show were therefore not read. The duplicate passages a 2017 article lists in three further tablets were not read either."@en .

pmw:RS24272 a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Die Tafel RS 24.272"@de,
        "The tablet RS 24.272"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "KTU 1.124"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened at the wording on 2026-10-06 in Pardee, Ritual and Cult at Ugarit, WAW 10, 2002, text 52, transliteration and translation, with the editor's introduction read in full."@en,
        "The work is short and almost entirely divine speech, and the editor's reading of what it is for is the reason it is here: he takes the prescription a deified ancestor gives for a sick divine child to have served as the model for human cases, and says there is no reason to doubt that the story served as a model for consulting the head of the dynasty when illness struck the young heir. That is his inference and the node says so. The identity of the figure who seeks the ruling is disputed in the literature he cites and nothing below depends on it."@en .

pmw:TacitiHistoriae a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Tacitus, Historien"@de,
        "Tacitus, Historiae"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened at IV.81. The edition is not recoverable; see prima-materia#45."@en .

pmc:AyurvedaDehasamhanana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Körper dicht gemacht und von keinem Geschöpf angreifbar"@de,
        "The body made compact, and unassailable by all creatures"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.1.58-60, I.1.76 and I.3.26-27, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, parts XXXIII-XXXIV, Calcutta 1904-1905, pp. 1024, 1028 and 1047" ;
    skos:definition "Claimed in three wordings: the compactness of the body becomes like that of a mountain and the user's energy like that of the wind; his body becomes as compact as stone, he becomes irresistible to view and unassailable by all creatures; and, of the preparation made with iron, that he is endued with the strength of an elephant and never succumbs to aggressions of violence committed on his person, nor to diseases, nor to the decrepitude of age, nor to death itself."@en ;
    skos:note "The third wording is the place where the lesson's strongest claim stands in its plainest form: not that death is postponed but that the user does not succumb to it. It is claimed of a preparation of iron leaf quenched seven times and kept a year under barley, that is, of something a reader could make."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmc:DavisIndependentClairvoyance a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Unabhängiges Hellsehen"@de,
        "Independent clairvoyance"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Davis, The Principles of Nature, 3rd edn., New York 1847, Scribe's Introduction, pp. xvi-xvii",
        "Davis, The Principles of Nature, 3rd edn., New York 1847, pp. 43-44" ;
    skos:definition "The state the work claims for itself, described by the scribe from the outside and by Davis from the inside, and the two descriptions agree on the physiology. The subject becomes cold, rigid, motionless and insensible to all external things; the pulsations become feeble and the breathing is apparently almost suspended; and the condition, on Davis's own explanation, corresponds almost precisely to that of physical death. Davis adds what the state is for: passing into it he does not observe entities as they would naturally be known to exist, but receives the reality of what he had a previous desire to investigate."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because the claim is a state and not a power, and because the work makes the state the whole of its evidence: the scribe says that if the account of Davis's past life and opportunities is correct, then it irresistibly follows that the book must have been dictated by some other and higher source of information than that accessible to the physical senses. The argument runs from the state and the man's ignorance to the source, and never from the contents."@en,
        "What the state is said to leave behind, and the claim is total. The mind is said to be entirely freed from the sphere of the body and consequently from all preconceived ideas, from all theological isms, and from all influences of education and local circumstances. A transmission that claims its own freedom from the influence of education is claiming the one thing an outside reader would most want to test, and the work's answer to that test is biographical and not textual."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DavisPrinciplesOfNature ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SpiritualistAutomaticWriting .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicSchussblatterHeilung a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Heilen einer Schußblatter am Auge"@de,
        "The healing of a shot-blister on the eye"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Abschnitt »Zeugniße«",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), pp. 5-6" ;
    skos:definition "The outcome claimed four times over in the testimonials: an eye suffering from what the book calls a Schußblatter becomes as good as the other, in a little more than twenty-four hours."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1820,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:Iddhividha a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Arten übernatürlicher Kraft"@de,
        "The kinds of supernormal power"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "Transformation by supernormal power, the first of the direct knowledges treated in the handbook."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:KevalaKumbhaka a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Alleiniges Anhalten"@de,
        "Retention alone"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.71-74 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "Retention held at will without the filling and the emptying that ordinarily frame it. The manual calls it the accomplished form of the practice and says nothing in the three worlds is beyond one who holds it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:Samadhi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sammlung"@de,
        "Concentration"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.7-I.11 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "The second of the three trainings, named in the handbook as the condition for the six direct knowledges."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:SanjiLiuxiong a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Drei Glücksfälle und sechs Unglücksfälle"@de,
        "Three felicities and six adversities"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zangshu, miscellaneous section, in the recension of Wu Cheng, one juan, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text" ;
    skos:definition "The book closes with three conditions under which a hollow is felicitous and six under which the burial is adverse. The six: a discordance of yin and yang; a going wrong of the year and the season; a small strength that plans a great thing; a reliance on present fortune and standing power; an overreaching above or a falling short below; and a strange appearance among the responses. The classic is quoted for the conclusion that a felicitous hollow with an adverse burial is the same as abandoning the corpse."@en ;
    skos:note "The second adversity is separately consequential, because it is the one an outside witness attacked with a count. It requires the year and the season of the burial to be right, and the memorial recorded in the dynastic history sets against that a tally of burials in the classical annals on the day the burial books call most inauspicious."@en,
        "The sixth adversity is the reason this node stands here and not among the ordinary cautions. The commentary printed with it enumerates what the strange appearances are, and the list is a list of ways in which nothing happens: the site is right and the family delays the burial; a quarrel prevents it; poverty and sickness together make it impossible; the master dies before he can return; the coffin stands for years and a war intervenes; the descendants do not agree; a lawsuit or a prison ends it; the days pass in neglect until the thing is abandoned. Every one of these is a reason a favourable grave produced no fortune, and every one is available after the fact."@en,
        "What the graph gains here is not a warning but a shape of claim. Beside the interval that does not turn a day, this file now holds the machinery that keeps that interval from ever being read as a refutation, in the same book and a few folios apart."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Zang ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination .

pmc:SwedenborgLoquelaCumSpiritibus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Rede mit Geistern und Engeln noch im Leibe"@de,
        "Speech with spirits and angels while still in the body"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Swedenborg, Arcana coelestia, n. 9438 (The Heavenly Arcana, trans. Potts, Standard Edition, vol. XI, pp. 359-360)",
        "Swedenborg, De caelo et inferno, n. 246, 248 (Heaven and Hell, trans. Swedenborg Society, London 1885, pp. 112-114)" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity the whole corpus rests on. A man whose interiors have been opened can speak with spirits and with angels while he lives in the world, because as to his interiors a man is a spirit and an angel and the body serves him only for activity in the natural sphere. The speech is not a local motion of sound: the angel's words fall into the man's thought and thence into words of his own language, and the man hears them as his own speech would be heard."@en ;
    skos:note "Stated as rarely granted at the present day and as formerly common: the ancients spoke with spirits and angels frequently, and on some earths spirits appear in human form and speak with the inhabitants. The corpus therefore holds that the capacity is human and not personal, and that the present rarity is a condition of the age."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArcanaCoelestia,
        pmw:DeCaeloEtInferno ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Swedenborgian .

pm:ContactRoute a owl:Class,
        pm:Scale ;
    rdfs:label "Kontaktweg"@de,
        "Contact route"@en ;
    skos:definition "Whether a route of transmission is known between a tradition and those the graph already holds. It decides whether an agreement may be read as independent attestation or only as reception, and it is therefore a property of the tradition and not of the individual correspondence."@en ;
    pm:scaleFamily pm:recordScale .

pm:CoverageState a owl:Class,
        pm:Scale ;
    rdfs:label "Erschließungsstand"@de,
        "Coverage state"@en ;
    skos:definition "How far the corpus of a tradition has been carried into the graph. The scale exists so that a transmission nobody has opened and a transmission whose corpus is genuinely thin never read alike."@en ;
    pm:scaleFamily pm:recordScale .

pm:MethodOrientation a owl:Class,
        pm:Scale ;
    rdfs:label "Ausrichtung der Methode"@de,
        "Method Orientation"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "How a path stands to the norm of the order it is transmitted in: whether its operative means keep that norm, deliberately break it, or decline the distinction. An instance of this class is a value on that axis and never a path."@en ;
    skos:note "Held apart from pm:TelicOrientation deliberately, and that separation is the whole reason both classes exist. The two vary independently: a path can take the forbidden as its vehicle and still aim at dissolution into what exceeds the self. A scheme with one axis cannot state that case, and the terms that carry one axis — right-hand path, left-hand path — therefore report a transgressive means as though it were a separative goal. See prima-materia#52."@en,
        "Three cases, and they must not read alike. Absence of pm:methodOrientation on a node means nobody has put the question to the source. pm:methodNotStated means somebody has, and the source does not answer it. pm:normDistinctionDeclined means the source answers by rejecting the distinction, which is a positive claim and carries the same burden as any other. None of the three is the middle value; there is no middle value."@en ;
    pm:scaleFamily pm:subjectAxis .

pm:SearchOutcome a owl:Class,
        pm:Scale ;
    rdfs:label "Ausgang der Suche"@de,
        "Search Outcome"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "What a carried search returned. Four outcomes, of which three are findings about the corpus and one is a finding about access to it."@en ;
    skos:note "The fourth value is the reason the scale is not a boolean. A corpus that could not be reached and a corpus that was read and stayed silent are opposite findings, and without a value for the first they would both be entered as the second — which would report as a property of the transmission what is a property of a library."@en ;
    pm:scaleFamily pm:recordScale .

pm:byEffort a pm:AwarenessValue,
        pm:StateAcquisition ;
    rdfs:label "Durch Anstrengung"@de,
        "By effort"@en ;
    skos:definition "The source has the condition attained by practice directed at attaining it."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the end of the axis where prerequisite chains are to be expected, and where their absence is worth a question. The Sufi handbooks call these maqāmāt and enumerate them in a set order."@en .

pm:contactRouteAbsent a pm:ContactRoute ;
    rdfs:label "Kein Kontaktweg"@de,
        "Contact route absent"@en ;
    skos:definition "No route is available for the period in which the corpus was fixed. Agreement with traditions already held is a candidate for independent attestation."@en .

pm:counterSearchFoundNothing a pm:CounterSearchState ;
    rdfs:label "Gegensuche ohne Fund"@de,
        "Counter-search found nothing"@en ;
    skos:definition "A search for opposing or non-replicating work was carried and returned nothing. This is a statement about the search, and the note must say what was searched."@en .

pm:noProcedureDevised a pm:ExaminationState ;
    rdfs:label "Kein Verfahren ersonnen"@de,
        "No procedure devised"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nobody has stated a condition under which the claim would count as refuted. This is a statement about the means of examination, not about the claim."@en .

pm:notByEffort a pm:AwarenessValue,
        pm:StateAcquisition ;
    rdfs:label "Nicht durch Anstrengung"@de,
        "Not by effort"@en ;
    skos:definition "The source has the condition arrive without being worked for: given, inherited, conferred at birth, or descending on the wayfarer as a gift."@en ;
    skos:note "Where a source says this, a missing prerequisite claim is a doctrine and not an omission. The Sufi handbooks say it outright of the aḥwāl; the Inuit narrative says it of second sight in another way, by reporting the capacity as something a person has."@en .

pm:outwardWaking a pm:AwarenessValue,
        pm:SensoryAnchoring ;
    rdfs:label "Nach außen gerichtetes Wachen"@de,
        "Outward waking"@en ;
    skos:definition "Awareness turned to the senses and to the world they report, with the body in ordinary use."@en ;
    skos:note "Set this where the source has the act performed openly, in the world, among others: an invocation spoken aloud, a divination read off an object, a healing performed by touch. The Māṇḍūkya calls this jāgrat."@en .

pm:sleepThreshold a pm:AwarenessValue,
        pm:SensoryAnchoring ;
    rdfs:label "Schlafschwelle"@de,
        "Sleep threshold"@en ;
    skos:definition "The passage between waking and sleeping in either direction, in which the sensory channel is partly detached and the body may already be immobile while awareness continues."@en ;
    skos:note "Set this where the source describes the condition rather than the clock: sleep departing and waking beginning, an inability to move, a luminosity at the moment of falling asleep. The holdings already carry the finding that this state is well attested as a place of reception and not as a place where a faculty is exercised; see convergences/threshold-of-sleep.ttl."@en .

pmp:AgrippaPraeparatioOraculi a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Wie sich einrichtet, wer Orakel empfangen will"@de,
        "How one who would receive oracles disposes himself"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Agrippa, De occulta philosophia III.53 (trans. J. F., London 1651)" ;
    skos:definition "The preparation prescribed in the third book for whoever, desiring to come to the supreme state of the soul, goes to receive oracles. He must go chastely and devoutly disposed, pure and clean, his soul polluted with no filthiness and free from all guilt; he must purify mind and body as far as he may from all diseases and passions and all irrational conditions, which adhere as rust to iron, by rightly composing what belongs to the tranquillity of the mind. By this means, the work says, he shall receive the truer and more efficacious oracles. Three chapters follow on cleanness, on abstinence, fastings, chastity and solitariness, and on penitence."@en ;
    skos:note "The four chapters were read at their headings and the first at its wording; the contents of the chapters on cleanness, abstinence and penitence were not read, and what they prescribe in detail is not entered."@en,
        "The stated effect of the preparation is comparative and not a threshold: truer and more efficacious oracles, with no line below which an oracle would not be received at all. So the condition cannot fail in the way the moon clause fails, and a poor oracle is evidence of insufficient purity rather than of anything about the procedure."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeOccultaPhilosophia ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:RenaissanceMagic .

pmp:HathaPranayama a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Atemregulierung im Haṭhayoga"@de,
        "Breath regulation in Haṭhayoga"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "The breath practice of the second chapter, directed at the clearing of the central channel."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmp:ImbasForosnai a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Imbas forosnai, das erleuchtende Wissen"@de,
        "Imbas forosnai, the knowledge that illumines"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Sanas Cormaic s.v. Imbas forosnai, Irish text in Whitley Stokes, Three Irish Glossaries (London: Williams and Norgate, 1862), p. 25, English in Sanas Chormaic: Cormac's Glossary, trans. John O'Donovan, ed. Whitley Stokes (Calcutta 1868), pp. 94-95" ;
    skos:altLabel "himbas forosnai"@en,
        "imbas forosnai"@en ;
    skos:definition "The procedure the glossary sets out in order and in the present tense. The poet chews a piece of the flesh of a red pig, or of a dog, or of a cat. He puts it afterwards on the flagstone behind the door. He chants an incantation over it and offers it to idol-gods. He calls his idols to him and does not find them on the morrow. He chants incantations over his two palms and calls his idol-gods to him again, that his sleep may not be disturbed. He lays his two palms on his two cheeks and falls asleep. He is watched, so that no one may turn him over and no one disturb him, until everything he is engaged upon is revealed to him."@en ;
    skos:note "One sentence in the sequence is read two ways by the two translators and neither reading is adopted here. Stokes has the idols brought to him, and he finds them not on the morrow. O'Donovan has him invoke his idols, and if he obtains not his desire on the day following, he pronounces incantations over both his palms. The second makes the palm-chant a second attempt after a failure and the first makes it the next step; the difference decides whether the procedure contains a built-in repetition, and the Irish as printed does not settle it here."@en,
        "The glossary closes the entry with its own etymology, and the etymology is of the posture and not of the knowledge: therefore it is called imbas, that is, his two palms about him, a palm over and a palm from the other side upon his cheeks. A transmission that derives the name of the faculty from the position of the hands has made the posture the defining element rather than the flesh or the offering."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InsularCeltic .

pmp:NippurFourCornerCircuit a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Vier Schalen in die vier Ecken des Hauses setzen"@de,
        "Setting four bowls at the four corners of the house"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, introduction § 8 with texts 4: 1-2 and 21-23, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 42 and 202-204)" ;
    skos:definition "The quadruple use of the vessel: four bowls carrying the same inscription for the same client are deposited at equidistant points of the dwelling, and the text declares the spirits bound and sealed in each one of the four corners of the house."@en ;
    skos:note "Three of one such set of four are published together as texts 21, 22 and 23, all made out for one woman, and the editor takes them for three of the four of a single house. The fourth is not in the collection. The practice accounts for the duplicate inscriptions that run through the whole corpus."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmp:RasaRasasevana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Einnahme des Quecksilbers"@de,
        "The taking of mercury"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, dose of incinerated mercury and special directions, pp. 194-198" ;
    skos:definition "The course itself. Incinerated mercury exhausted with the essence of mica, with magnetic iron, with copper, brass or steel is taken at one gunja a day, raised gradually to one masha; exhausted with gold at one rati, with silver at two, with copper at three. It is taken in the morning, the meal following just after midday, with a betel leaf against the constipation it causes, and with the physician greeted as one greets the god."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmp:RasaShodhana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Reinigung des Quecksilbers"@de,
        "Purification of mercury"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, the first to eleventh processes of purification, pp. 41-50" ;
    skos:altLabel "shodhana"@en ;
    skos:definition "The first operation. Mercury is sifted through cloth and rubbed in a hot mortar, one blemish at a time, with a substance named for that blemish — ram's fur, turmeric, brick powder, soot and lime juice against the lead blemish, the root of bisala and ankotha against the tin blemish, the kernel of aragbadha against foreign matter, chitraka root against the fire blemish, black dhustura against subtleness, the triphala against the poison, the trikatu against the stone blemish, the trikantaka against the inability to stand fire — each at a sixteenth of the weight of the mercury, with a washing in hot aranala after every rubbing. Eleven processes are given and any one of them is stated to suffice."@en ;
    skos:note "The one-to-one correspondence is the structure of the whole operation: eight faults, eight drugs, each drug named against its own fault. A later reader should notice that the eleventh process, the extraction from cinnabar, removes all eight at once and is declared to exempt the product from the eight otherwise indispensable operations. The transmission thus carries both a remedy per fault and a shortcut past all of them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmp:SusrutaSiravyadha a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Öffnen einer Vene"@de,
        "Opening a vein"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Susruta Samhita, Sarirasthana VIII.5, VIII.7-19 and VIII.27-39, in the English of Kaviraj Kunjalal Bhishagratna, An English Translation of the Sushruta Samhita, vol. II, Calcutta 1911, pp. 199-204" ;
    skos:definition "The operation in the detail the chapter gives it. The patient is seated or laid down and the part bound neither too loosely nor too tightly; for a vein of the head he sits on a stool of one aratni's height facing the sun, legs drawn up, elbows on the knee-joints, thumbs closed in his fists and placed on the Manyas, with the binding linen cast over the fists and a second man tying the bandage from behind, and he holds his breath until the operation is finished. The posture is given separately for the leg, the arms, the back, the chest, the sides, the penis, the tongue and the palate. The ligature is tied four fingers above the place to be incised. The incision is made to the depth of a barley-corn with a Vrihimukha instrument in the muscular parts and half that in other places, and over a bone with a small axe to half the depth of a barley-corn."@en ;
    skos:note "The chapter also gives the place of the opening by disease, and the places are stated as distances from marmas: two fingers above the Kshipra for a group of foot and skin complaints, four fingers above the Gulpha in the wind disorders, two fingers below the Indravasti in scrofula, four fingers above or below the Janu in sciatica. That is the sixth chapter's table applied, and it is the reason the two chapters are harvested together."@en,
        "What the chapter claims for the operation: venesection properly performed gives speedier relief than medicated oil or plaster, and is half of the treatment described in surgery as enematic measures are half of therapeutics. That is a claim about a share of a discipline and not about an outcome in a patient, and nothing in the chapter reports a case."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SusrutaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:TaulipangSoulLeavesByTheHairCord a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Den Schatten an der Haarschnur hinaufschicken, waehrend der Koerper auf der Erde bleibt"@de,
        "Sending the shadow up the hair cord while the body stays on the earth"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Theodor Koch-Gruenberg, Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, Band III: Ethnographie (Stuttgart: Strecker und Schroeder, 1923), pp. 204, 207" ;
    skos:definition "The operation the whole training is for. Very strong tobacco brew is drunk through the nose; the practitioner loses consciousness and sleeps, and his soul leaves the body and goes to the house of the Mauari. The cord called karauali, twisted from the master's wife's hair and drawn through the nostrils during the training, serves the shadow of the Zauberarzt as a ladder to climb up by at a cure, while the body remains on the earth; the step-shaped liana kapeyenkuma(x)pe serves the same purpose and is used at the cures."@en ;
    skos:note "Before it the novice says to his relatives: do not weep, I know what I am doing, let me be. That is the only sentence in the passage addressed to bystanders, and it is a warning about what they are about to see rather than a condition of the operation: they then lie as if dead, and only the heart still beats."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VomRoroimaZumOrinocoIII ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Taulipang .

pmp:TungusShamanizing a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Schamanisieren"@de,
        "Shamanizing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXVII, sect. 119-121, pp. 325-332, and chap. XXIX, sect. 134, pp. 362-364" ;
    skos:definition "The performance: the shaman drums, continually increasing and decreasing tempo and intensity in a definite rhythm, brings himself into the bounded state, introduces the spirits into himself, and acts and speaks as the spirits. Assistants must know the art of drumming and must understand the shaman's movements. The performance may begin out of a sleep during which the spirit comes into him."@en ;
    skos:note "The recorder holds that the audience is a component of the operation and not its public: bringing the audience into the state is not the aim but the means by which the suggestion is made. That claim is his reading and is entered as his; what is reported is the practice."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SiberianShamanism .

pmt:NeoplatonicTheurgy a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Neuplatonische Theurgie"@de,
        "Neoplatonic theurgy"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Iamblichus, De mysteriis III (ed. des Places 1966)" ;
    skos:definition "The late antique Platonic transmission concerned with the ascent of the soul and with the criteria by which a divine communication is told from a human one."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. The De mysteriis citations name the edition they were read in. For Synesius, De insomniis neither edition nor translation is recorded, and the citation names no chapter; it is left unstated rather than guessed and the passage is to be reopened."@en,
        "The file records that this layer underlies the later Jewish and Islamic dream theory, so agreement among those three may not be counted as independent."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Iamblichus, De mysteriis; Synesius of Cyrene, De insomniis" .

pmt:Sufism a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Sufismus"@de,
        "Sufism"@en ;
    dcterms:source "al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the Islamic mystical path, systematised in the handbooks of the tenth and eleventh centuries."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. For both works the edition or translation the passages were read from is not recoverable, and it is left unstated rather than guessed. Neither citation carries a page or section number, and the printed editions of the Kitāb al-Lumaʿ and the Risāla divide their material differently, so the passages are to be reopened before anything in this file is cited by place."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "al-Sarrāj, Kitāb al-Lumaʿ; al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya" .

pmw:DivinoAmoreEtSapientia a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Swedenborg, Sapientia angelica de divino amore et de divina sapientia"@de,
        "Swedenborg, Sapientia angelica de divino amore et de divina sapientia"@en ;
    skos:note "First published at Amsterdam in 1763. Read here in the English of the Swedenborg Society, Angelic Wisdom concerning the Divine Love and the Divine Wisdom, London 1912, a translation based on the recommendations of the Society's revision committee. The numbering runs 1 to 432; numbers 232 to 262 were read continuously."@en .

pmw:HerodotiHistoriai a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Herodot, Historien"@de,
        "Herodotus, Histories"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened at IV.13-16. The edition is not recoverable; see prima-materia#45."@en .

pmw:LeechdomsCockayne a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Das altenglische Herbarium des Pseudo-Apuleius, in Cockaynes Leechdoms Bd. I"@de,
        "The Old English Herbarium of pseudo-Apuleius, in Cockayne's Leechdoms vol. I"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened at one chapter, on the herb asterion against the falling sickness, in the edition of 1864 which prints the Old English with a facing translation. The rest of the volume, and the Lacnunga and Bald's Leechbook that the same principal corpus names, are unopened."@en .

pmw:LiberABA a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Book 4 (Liber ABA)"@de,
        "Book 4 (Liber ABA)"@en ;
    skos:note "Published in parts: Part I, Meditation, Wieland 1912, and Part II, Magick, Wieland 1913. The title page and the opening note name two authors and state that the book is not the work of the first of them but was written down from his dictation by the second and rearranged by her, and that it was read before printing by several persons of less than average intelligence so that anything unclear to them could be elucidated. A node from this work reports a redacted dictation with a third party interposed for intelligibility, not an author's text."@en .

pmw:LiberBatrachophrenoboocosmomachia a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Liber Batrachophrenoboocosmomachia sub figura DXXXVI"@de,
        "Liber Batrachophrenoboocosmomachia sub figura DXXXVI"@en ;
    skos:note "Class B."@en .

pmw:Uhhamuwa a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Das Ritual des Uḫḫamuwa"@de,
        "The ritual of Uḫḫamuwa"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened in full at the wording on 2026-10-06 in the critical edition hethiter.net/: CTH 410, textus 2013-12-09, exemplar A, which is HT 1 (BM 108548), where the ritual runs on the obverse second column from line 17 to line 47 and is, as the editors state, completely preserved. Exemplar B, KUB 9.31, was downloaded and its divergences were not collated. A second translation was read beside the original: A. Goetze, Ritual against Pestilence, in Pritchard (ed.), ANET, 3rd ed. 1969, pp. 347-348."@en,
        "The editors note that all four witnesses are four-column collective tablets carrying three rituals each, those of Zarpiya, of Uḫḫamuwa and of Ašḫella. The work is therefore not a tablet but a section of one, and the places entered from it are cited as such."@en .

pmw:Zhuangzi a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Zhuangzi"@de,
        "Zhuangzi"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened at chapter 4 only, in Legge's translation of 1891. The remaining thirty-two chapters are unopened, and the inner chapters in particular carry further material of the kinds this holding owes — the cook, the swimmer at the falls, the ferryman — which is claims about acquired capacity in the register this file collects."@en .

pmc:IuratusVisioDivina a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Schau der Gottheit in diesem Körper"@de,
        "The sight of the Deity in this body"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber iuratus Honorii, cap. CI (Royal MS 17 A. xlii, fol. 64r), ed. Peterson; Latin of Sloane MS 3854 in Boudet's edition, section 15, at p. 880: Et sic fiet quod videbit celeste palatium et majestatem in gloria sua, 9 ordines angelorum et agmina spirituum beatorum" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity the first book claims: that the operator shall see the celestial palace and the majesty of God in his glory, the nine orders of angels and the companies of all blessed spirits, while he lives and in this corruptible body, his spirit caught up and his body fed in the earth with angelic food."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberIuratusHonorii ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:ParakayaPravesa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Eintritt in einen anderen Körper"@de,
        "Entering another body"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.38 (Sanskrit sūtra text; received four-pāda numbering)" ;
    skos:definition "The transfer of the seat of awareness into a body other than one's own."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PatanjalaYoga .

pmc:RasaSarvarogahara a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Heilung jeder Krankheit, durch die für sie gewählte Beigabe"@de,
        "The cure of every disease, by the accompaniment chosen for it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, ch. IV, how incinerated mercury is to be used in all sorts of diseases, pp. 177-187" ;
    skos:definition "The rule that governs some forty of the volume's prescriptions: the ashes of mercury cure every disease if taken with that particular anupanam or accompaniment which is efficacious in that disease, and the volume then runs through the accompaniments one complaint at a time — fever with the decoction of musta, cough with kantakari and long pepper, jaundice with liquorice, piles with elephant-foot yam burnt and mixed with oil and rock salt, and so on through some forty named conditions."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as one node and not as forty, and the departure is deliberate and is flagged here so that a later reader can contest it. What the passage varies is the accompaniment and never the preparation, and the claim it makes is a doctrine about accompaniments rather than forty recipes: the same substance is said to reach any disease provided the vehicle is chosen for it. The named preparations of the volume, which do carry their own materials and their own claims, stand below one by one."@en,
        "The doctrine has a consequence the volume does not draw. If the accompaniment decides the indication, then a preparation that failed can always be said to have had the wrong accompaniment, and no course of the medicine can come out against the medicine. That is the same immunising shape this holding records elsewhere under the displacement of the failure, reached here by a pharmacological route."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pmc:Theopemptos a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der gottgesandte Traum"@de,
        "The god-sent dream"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Iamblichus, De mysteriis III.2-3 (ed. des Places 1966)" ;
    skos:definition "A dream held to come from a god rather than from the sleeper, and distinguished from the ordinary dream not by its content but by the state in which it arrives."@en ;
    skos:note "The state is described as the one in which sleep is departing and waking beginning, or as a condition between the two, accompanied by a voice, by a light, and by an inability to move the eyes. This is the closest description in the collection to what modern accounts call the hypnopompic state, and the immobility is named as part of the sign rather than as an obstacle. What arrives in it is a communication received, not a faculty exercised."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NeoplatonicTheurgy .

pm:ExaminationState a owl:Class,
        pm:Scale ;
    rdfs:label "Prüfstand"@de,
        "Examination State"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "The standing of a protocol: how far the attempt to fail a claim has actually been carried."@en ;
    skos:note "The first two states and the third must never collapse into one another. A claim nobody has devised a way to examine is not a claim that has been examined and found wanting. A record that reports both as unsupported would not be reporting on the claims but on the reach of its own instruments."@en ;
    pm:scaleFamily pm:recordScale .

pm:recordScale a pm:ScaleFamily ;
    rdfs:label "Aufzeichnungsskala"@de,
        "Record scale"@en ;
    skos:definition "A scale that reports how far the work of recording, searching and examining has been carried. Its values describe what has been done to the subject matter, never the subject matter itself."@en ;
    skos:note "The cardinal error this family exists to prevent is the collapse of a state that reports absent work into one that reports a finding: a claim for which no procedure was devised and a claim examined and unsupported are different findings and must never read alike."@en .

pmp:AyurvedaRasayana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Rasayana, die Gabe dessen, was beim Gesunden das Ojas mehrt"@de,
        "Rasayana, the administration of what increases ojas in a healthy person"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.1.7-8, I.1.14 and I.1.51-52, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIII, Calcutta 1904, pp. 1018 and 1021-1022" ;
    skos:definition "The operation the lesson is about: a course of compounded drugs, taken on an empty stomach in the morning in a dose fixed by the strength of the user's digestive fire, followed when digested by a prescribed food of rice with milk and ghee, and continued for a stated term — a year for most of the preparations, three years for one, six months for two. The lesson derives the name from rasa and ayana and glosses it as that which imparts superior rasa and the other constituents of the body."@en ;
    skos:note "Two features of the prescription are what make the whole set unexaminable from inside, and both are stated as care rather than as vagueness. The dose is not a quantity but a relation — such a measure as may not prevent one from taking one's ordinary meals, a measure regulated by the strength of one's digestive fire — and the term is a year. A course with an unstated dose and a year's term has no point before its end at which anything could be said to have gone wrong."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:NeidanHuohou a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Feuerphasen"@de,
        "The fire phases"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhang Boduan, Wuzhen pian, in Wuzhen pian zhushi (DZ 145), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 2, fol. 30a" ;
    skos:definition "The regulation of the work by a schedule of advancing and withdrawing: the yang fire raised from one point of the cycle, the yin tally applied from the opposite point, the whole run through in a set number of intervals. The corpus treats the schedule as the operative part of the operation and as the part that is not written down."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Neidan .

pmp:RosicrucianOpusTurris a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Werk der sieben Geschosse des Turms"@de,
        "The work of the seven storeys of the tower"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chymische Hochzeit Christiani Rosencreutz (Strasbourg, 1616), the Sixth Day, the first six storeys of the Tower of Olympus" ;
    skos:definition "The graded procedure of the sixth day, carried by the guests under the direction of an old man and a virgin: the dissolving of the beheaded bodies by water heated through a wrapped head; the drawing of the matter into a golden globe; the heating of the globe by sunlight thrown from mirrors on all sides; the cutting of the cooled globe with a pointed diamond and the finding of a white egg; the ripening of the egg in warm sand; the feeding of the hatched bird with the blood of the beheaded, through which it passes from black to white to variegated; the bath in which it loses its feathers and the blue stone the bath boils down to; the bird's own beheading at the third conjunction; and the burning of its body to ashes."@en ;
    skos:note "The colour sequence is stated as an effect of successive feedings and not of successive heatings, which is how it is usually given in the alchemical literature, and the work names the food: the blood of the beheaded diluted with prepared water, and then perhaps the blood of another of the royal persons. The uncertainty is the narrator's and is left here as his."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:NuptiaeChymicae ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rosicrucian .

pmp:ThelemaDharanaNumerata a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Konzentration mit gezählten Unterbrechungen"@de,
        "Concentration with the interruptions counted"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber E vel Exercitiorum sub figura IX, chap. V, §§ 1-6, in The Equinox I,1 (London, March 1909), p. 30, with the counting procedure and the expected profile at Book 4 (Liber ABA), Part I: Meditation, chap. V, pp. 58-61 (first published London: Wieland & Co., 1912; read in the Celephais Press electronic edition of 2004, which follows the corrected text in Magick: Book 4 Parts I-IV, York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1994)" ;
    skos:definition "Constraining the mind to a single simple imagined object, then to combinations, then to simple moving objects and to combinations of moving objects, with no other thought allowed to intrude. Seated in the posture, with notebook and pencil at one's side and a watch in front of one, at first for not more than ten minutes at a time; the duration, the number and nature of the intruding thoughts and the tendency of the object to depart from the course laid out for it are noted. The number of times the thought wanders is counted, on the fingers or on a string of beads."@en ;
    skos:note "It also supplies the one case in the holding of a break being externally observable. A footnote states that the grosser kind of break can be detected by another person, that it is accompanied by a flickering of the eyelid and can be seen by him, and that with practice he could detect even very small breaks."@en,
        "This is the practice that turns the record into an instrument. The counted unit is the break, and the count is kept while the practice runs rather than reconstructed afterwards. The later exposition states the profile expected at a given stage: an average practice of about half an hour with between ten and twenty breaks."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberEVelExercitiorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmp:ThelemaNumerusUnoAspectu a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Eine geworfene Zahl auf einen Blick nennen, von einem Zweiten bestätigt"@de,
        "Naming a thrown number at a glance, confirmed by a second person"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber Batrachophrenoboocosmomachia sub figura DXXXVI, § 1, practice A, in The Equinox I,10 (London, September 1913), pp. 37-38" ;
    skos:definition "Seated before a bare square table, an unknown number of small similar objects is thrown by an assistant from time to time upon the table and hastily gathered up again. The practitioner declares the number at the glance and the assistant confirms it by his count. A quarter of an hour thrice daily; the maximum number of objects is at first seven, and the maximum increases by one at each practice provided that not a single mistake is made. The practice is to continue assiduously for at least one year."@en ;
    skos:note "The assistant's own performance is expected to change with time — his quickness in gathering up the objects is expected to increase — which means the difficulty of the task is not held constant across the year. The instruction does not notice this."@en,
        "The only practice in the corpus whose result is checked by another person as a matter of procedure rather than as an afterthought, and the only one whose progression is gated on an error count kept by that other person. The failure condition is stated in the same breath as the advancement rule: one mistake and the maximum does not rise."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberBatrachophrenoboocosmomachia ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema .

pmt:NavajoCeremonial a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Navajo-Zeremonialüberlieferung"@de,
        "Navajo ceremonial transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Washington Matthews, Navaho Legends, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society V (Boston and New York: Houghton, Mifflin, 1897), pp. 24-25" ;
    skos:definition "The Diné transmission of chantways: long ordered ceremonials for restoring a condition named as order, with the sequence fixed and its errors held to be consequential."@en ;
    skos:note "Read for the first time in the introduction to the legends, sections 41 and 42 on the sacred songs, printed pages 24 and 25. The legends themselves and the ceremonial texts were not read, and the fuller ceremonial account of the night chant published in nineteen hundred and two was not opened in this run."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteAbsent ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Wyman, Blessingway, with the recorded Navajo texts; Matthews, The Night Chant" .

pmt:ZuniRitual a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Zuni-Ritualüberlieferung"@de,
        "Zuni ritual transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ruth L. Bunzel, Zuni Ritual Poetry, in Forty-Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of American Ethnology 1929-1930 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1932), pp. 611-835" ;
    skos:definition "The Zuni transmission of fixed prayer, memorised verbatim and held to be ineffective if altered."@en ;
    skos:note "A tradition that states its own falsifying condition: the words must be exact. Whether exactness is in fact required is examinable."@en,
        "Read for the first time in the introduction to the ritual poetry, pages 611 to 620 of the printed report. The body of the collection, the prayers themselves, was not read. The corpus stands in the language of its speakers with a facing translation, which is what makes it admissible here at all; the recorder is an outsider and the question of ethnographic first fixation raised at prima-materia#91 is not settled by this file."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteAbsent ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Bunzel, Zuni Ritual Poetry, with the Zuni texts" .

pmw:LiberHHH a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Liber HHH sub figura CCCXLI"@de,
        "Liber HHH sub figura CCCXLI"@en ;
    skos:note "Class D."@en .

pmw:LogosTeleutaios a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Die letzte Abrechnung"@de,
        "The Final Reckoning"@en ;
    skos:note "The one piece of this corpus whose title was read in this run. The table of contents of the volume gives it as Le Premier Livre du Compte Final de Zosime le Thebain, and the piece itself opens, four pages before the passages entered below, with the salutation Zosime a Theosebie, salut. It is a letter, and it is the one place in what was read where the writer states a procedure, a warning and an accusation in the same breath."@en .

pmw:RS24244 a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Die Tafel RS 24.244"@de,
        "The tablet RS 24.244"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "KTU 1.100"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened at the wording on 2026-10-06 in Pardee, Ritual and Cult at Ugarit, WAW 10, 2002, text 53, transliteration and translation of all seventy-nine lines including the left edge, with the editor's introduction read in full. The editio princeps is of 1968."@en,
        "The editor classes this work as para-mythological rather than incantatory, on the ground that its protagonists are divine throughout, and notes that an incantatory formula is nevertheless embedded in each of its twelve parallel sections. The places entered below are from the embedded formula, from the twelve-fold address and from the operative section at the end; what is not entered is the cosmology of the mare's parentage, which carries no claim of the kind this graph holds."@en .

pmw:Sanguozhi a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Chen Shou, Sanguozhi, mit dem Kommentar des Pei Songzhi"@de,
        "Chen Shou, Sanguozhi, with Pei Songzhi's commentary"@en ;
    skos:note "Read at juan 8, the biography of Zhang Lu, in the Wenyuange Siku quanshu text. It enters this corpus as the report of an outside observer and not as a witness the transmission would own: the chronicle calls the movement the rice bandits and its teaching the way of ghosts. Two layers are to be kept apart in it, and the citations do. Chen Shou's own text describes the practice; the Dianlüe quoted by Pei Songzhi describes it again and adds a judgement on whether it worked. The Dianlüe's description is besides of two movements at once, Zhang Jue's and Zhang Xiu's, and says of the second that its method was roughly the same as the first with the quiet chamber added."@en .

pmw:TalmudBavliAbodaZara a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Babylonischer Talmud, Traktat Aboda zara"@de,
        "Babylonian Talmud, tractate Aboda zara"@en ;
    skos:note "Access: the archive.org identifier is derbabylonischet07gold, full text over the regular route. The file keeps its line breaks, so the warning about the single very long line does not apply to it; the Aramaic and the German stand in two columns which the full text interleaves line by line, and a reading that does not separate them will run the two together."@en,
        "Read in the translation of Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud mit Einschluss der vollstaendigen Misnah, seventh volume, Synhedrin, Makkoth, Sebuoth, Aboda zara, Horajoth, Edijoth, Aboth, Berlin, S. Calvary und Co., 1903. The same physical volume carries tractate Sanhedrin and is the volume traditions/rabbinic-halakhah.ttl is worked from; the tractate is the work and the volume is the edition, so it is declared separately."@en,
        "The part read is folio 54b to 56a, pages 990 to 992 of that volume. The running heads of the tractate were checked over the whole of chapter one and the part read before anything was cited: they run 796, 799, 801, 806, 807, 809, 813, 819, 822, 827, 833, 837, 839, 842, 845, 852, 862 and on to 992 without going backwards. The check is the one AGENTS.md requires after prima-materia#829, where a scan gave the sequence 753, 759, 757, 799, 761 and a page number formed from it would have been invented."@en .

pmc:RasaAjaraAmara a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Freiheit von Alter und Tod"@de,
        "Freedom from old age and from death"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, introduction p. 3 and ch. III, the attributes of mercury, pp. 28-29" ;
    skos:altLabel "ajara-amara"@en ;
    skos:definition "The condition the transmission puts at the head of everything it promises: the arrival of premature old age prevented and death put off, the body kept from decay while the man goes on living in it. It is claimed of mercury in every one of its prepared forms and is the reason the science is said to have been introduced into India."@en ;
    skos:note "The promise is stated in the same breath as the cure of disease and is not distinguished from it. That matters for any later examination: a preparation that relieves a complaint and a preparation that suspends ageing are claimed in one sentence and with the same words, so a user who found relief would have no way of telling which of the two he had obtained."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Rasashastra .

pm:TelicOrientation a owl:Class,
        pm:Scale ;
    rdfs:label "Ausrichtung des Zieles"@de,
        "Telic Orientation"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "What a path says becomes of the bounded self at its end: whether the self is to be dissolved into what exceeds it, strengthened and preserved as its own absolute, or whether the path declines the question. An instance of this class is a value on that axis and never a path."@en ;
    skos:note "The values name what a source claims, not what happens. Nothing on this axis is examined by holding it; where somebody takes up the examination, that is a pm:Testing node pointing at the claim, and the examination states of the two values are not symmetric."@en,
        "This is the axis the Western terms were built to carry, and the axis they carry badly, because they carry the other one at the same time. A record that wants to state where a path claims to end has to be able to state that without also stating how it proceeds. See prima-materia#52."@en ;
    pm:scaleFamily pm:subjectAxis .

pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate a pm:ExaminationState ;
    rdfs:label "Behauptung unterscheidet nicht"@de,
        "Claim does not discriminate"@en ;
    skos:definition "Cases have been recorded, they show no deviation, and the claim is nonetheless uninformative: it is worded so that outcomes which differ from one another all satisfy it equally."@en ;
    skos:note "The distinction that decides which of the two applies is not how strong the evidence is but whether an outcome exists that the claim would have ruled out. If the claim covers a result and its opposite alike, it discriminates nothing and belongs here, however well the cases fit."@en,
        "The fifth state and the least obvious. It is not a milder pm:casesWithoutDeviation. There the claim said something and the cases bore it out; here the cases bear it out because the claim did not say enough to be borne out against anything. Recording such a protocol as cases without deviation would enter a success where an examination in fact found the claim empty, and a reader counting confirmations would count this one."@en,
        "This state exists because a case forced it. A claim that coupling an intended state to a strong affect shifts the threshold for risk is confirmed by the evidence and useless to whoever acts on it, because fear and anger are both strong and move that threshold in opposite directions. See prima-materia#141."@en .

pm:ownAgency a pm:AgencyHolding,
        pm:AwarenessValue ;
    rdfs:label "Eigene Trägerschaft"@de,
        "Own agency"@en ;
    skos:definition "The practitioner acts, and what comes of it is credited to the practice."@en .

pm:subjectAxis a pm:ScaleFamily ;
    rdfs:label "Sachachse"@de,
        "Subject axis"@en ;
    skos:definition "A coordinate on which a tradition, a practice, a capacity or a claim is placed. The axis belongs to no tradition; sources describe a condition and do not name a coordinate, so every placement is an act of the compiler and carries pm:compilerInference."@en ;
    skos:note "Two values are owed on every subject axis beyond the substantive ones. A declining value, for a tradition that rejects the distinction the axis draws: without it a rejection is recorded as an omission, which turns a doctrine into a gap. And a not-stated value, for a source that was read for the question and does not answer it: without it a node nobody tagged cannot be told from a source that was asked and stayed silent, and every query of the form 'only in X' reports the state of the tagging instead of the state of the traditions."@en .

pmp:SelfRemembering a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Selbsterinnern"@de,
        "Self-remembering"@en ;
    dcterms:source "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching, chapter seven, at the account of the first attempts at self-remembering and the division of attention (one-volume English text as scanned in the Internet Archive full-text mirror; edition left unstated)" ;
    skos:definition "An exercise in dividing attention: attention is directed at the observed thing and at the one observing at the same time, so that the sense of I is held alongside the perception rather than lost in it. It is performed in the middle of ordinary activity, walking a street or entering a shop, and not in withdrawal."@en ;
    skos:note "A sign of success is named and is kept apart from the exercise on purpose: a state of inner peace and confidence said to follow great efforts of this kind. It is recorded as a sign and not as evidence. The practitioner is told beforehand what to expect, so his report of it is not independent of the instruction, and there is no base rate for the same state without the instruction."@en,
        "The novelty of the exercise is disputed inside the source itself. A contemporary told the recorder that it is apperception as Wundt defines it and nothing further; the recorder answers that Wundt came close and went elsewhere. Held as a note and not as a pm:Disputing node, because Wundt has not been read here and a dispute node would need both sides at their own wording."@en,
        "The recorder distinguishes it in the same passage from two neighbouring things, and both distinctions are the tradition's own. It is not the stopping of thought described in the yoga literature he had already tried, because there the whole of attention goes to the effort of admitting no thoughts, whereas here attention is split, one part to that effort and one to the feeling of self. And it is not self-analysis or self-feeling."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:FourthWay .

pmp:ShangqingXuedao a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Gang des Lernens"@de,
        "The course of study"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao 5.12a-5.12b (DZ 1016), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 5" ;
    skos:definition "The whole undertaking as this corpus conceives it: a course entered under a master, held to over decades, sustained against the trials, and assessed at its end by an office that grades the outcome. The corpus speaks of it as a single thing with its own conditions of failure, distinct from any one of the techniques carried out within it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Shangqing .

pmt:ChristianAsceticDiscernment a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Christliche Unterscheidung der Geister"@de,
        "Christian ascetic discernment"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John Cassian, Collationes I-II" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the practice of discerning the origin of inner movements, running from the desert monastic literature through the Byzantine ascetic corpus into the Latin spiritual exercises."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. For every citation in this file the edition or translation the passage was read from is not recoverable, and it is left unstated rather than guessed. The gap is load-bearing here: Evagrius is transmitted in Greek and in Syriac with divergent chapter counts, the Collationes are numbered differently in the older editions, and the marginal numbering of the Spiritual Exercises belongs to a modern editorial layer and not to the autograph."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos and On Thoughts; John Cassian, Collationes; Diadochus of Photice, Gnostic Chapters; Ignatius of Loyola, Spiritual Exercises" .

pmw:AitareyaBrahmana a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Aitareya-Brāhmaṇa"@de,
        "Aitareya Brāhmaṇa"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened on 2026-09-19 in the English of Arthur Berriedale Keith, Rigveda Brahmanas: The Aitareya and Kauṣītaki Brāhmaṇas of the Rigveda, Harvard Oriental Series 25, Cambridge, Massachusetts 1920. Read at the wording: the third pañcikā, chapters 7 to 11, on the vaṣaṭ call and the Nivid. The remaining seven pañcikās were searched and not read. Keith gives both the pañcikā count and the continuous adhyāya count in brackets, and the places below give both because the older literature uses the second."@en .

pmw:DeCaeloEtInferno a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Swedenborg, De caelo et inferno"@de,
        "Swedenborg, De caelo et inferno"@en ;
    skos:note "A textual detail that bears on citation and was seen at the wording. The author's own footnote to number 249 lists the Arcana places where speech with spirits is treated, and the two editions read give different numbers in the first of its two lists: the 1885 copy prints 734 where the second list of the same footnote prints 784. The second list, which is the one this file uses, reads 784, 9438, 10751 in both copies. Whoever follows the first list to 734 will find a passage on another subject."@en,
        "Published anonymously at London in 1758 as De Coelo et ejus Mirabilibus, et de Inferno, ex Auditis et Visis. Read here in the English of the Swedenborg Society, Heaven and Hell, London 1885, and checked against the Society's edition of 1893; the numbering runs 1 to 603 and did not differ at any place read between those two and the editions of 1778, 1784 and 1883, which were also consulted for the footnotes."@en,
        "That the work appeared without its author's name is not a disputed attribution and is not entered as one, but it is part of the citation history and belongs here: Swedenborg published his theological works anonymously until 1768 and named himself on a title page for the first time in 1771."@en .

pmw:HohmanFreund1828 a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Ephrata, Pa. 1828)"@de,
        "Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Ephrata, Pa. 1828)"@en ;
    skos:note "The second German printing, opened for its appendix and for the day list its scan carries whole. Its title page says it is printed a second time on request, which dates it against the Reading original."@en .

pmw:RS24266 a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Die Tafel RS 24.266"@de,
        "The tablet RS 24.266"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "KTU 1.119"@en ;
    skos:note "One place in the calendar that was read and is not entered, recorded so that a later run does not take the omission for an absence. The edition's glossary puts the purification verb of this corpus — the D-stem that it glosses as restoring to a non-cultic state — at line 23' of this tablet, three lines before the prayer begins, and a 2017 article makes that line the hinge of the whole archive by reading the officiant's function from it. The line itself was not read at the wording in this run, only the glossary entry and the article's abstract, so nothing is entered from it. It is the first place a later run should go in this work."@en,
        "Opened at the wording on 2026-10-06 in the critical edition that prints transliteration beside translation: D. Pardee, Ritual and Cult at Ugarit, Writings from the Ancient World 10, Society of Biblical Literature 2002, where the tablet appears twice — as text 13, among the prescriptive sacrificial rituals, and as text 46, among the prayers, for the eleven lines of its reverse that this file was opened for. The editio princeps is Herdner's of 1978."@en,
        "The editor prints it twice on purpose and that is itself a statement about the tablet: the obverse is a calendar of sacrifices for a named month and the last eleven lines of the reverse are a prayer with no explicit link to the sacrificial text above it. He classes those lines as a prayer appended, and notes that actual prayers are extremely rare in this corpus and that this is the clearest example. The nodes below are from the appended lines; the calendar was read and nothing from it is entered, because a list of offerings per day carries no claim this graph can hold."@en .

pmc:MagnetismeSomnambulisme a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Magnetischer Somnambulismus"@de,
        "Magnetic somnambulism"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. V, pp. 68-69, and chap. VIII, pp. 165-166" ;
    skos:definition "A mode of existence in which the person appears asleep, answers his operator without waking, can execute movements, and on returning to the natural state retains no remembrance of what passed. The eyes are closed; he generally understands only those put in communication with him; the external organs of sense are all or nearly all asleep, and yet he experiences sensations by another means, an internal sense being roused in him which is perhaps the centre of the others. He is subject to the influence of his operator, and that influence may be useful or injurious according to the operator's disposition and conduct."@en ;
    skos:note "It is stated to occur only in a small number of cases, and the source devotes a chapter to it precisely because it changes the manner of operating. That rarity is worth holding beside the fact that it is the state on which every capacity in this file depends."@en,
        "The dependence on the operator is given a stated limit in the same work: the somnambulist preserves his reason and the use of his will, cannot be made to reveal a secret he has reason to conceal or to do things contrary to the principles he holds awake, and a reprehensible act of volition would make him revolt and throw him into convulsions. The transmission thus asserts obedience and its boundary in the same breath, and the placement on the agency axis below says so rather than picking one half."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pm:Attesting a owl:Class,
        pm:Scale ;
    rdfs:label "Bezeugen"@de,
        "Attesting"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "The act by which a claim entered the record. Instances of this class are modes of entry, not claims; a claim points at one of them through pm:attestedBy."@en ;
    skos:note "Orthogonal to the subject axes of pm:subjectAxis, which say where the matter lies on a coordinate rather than by which route the assertion reached the record. The note named pm:AwarenessContext until 2026-10-07; that class was struck with prima-materia#436 and its two questions were separated into the five axes of ontology/consciousness.ttl, which are attached to a node through pm:Situating and not through an edge on the assertion."@en ;
    pm:scaleFamily pm:recordScale .

pm:SensoryAnchoring a owl:Class,
        pm:Scale ;
    rdfs:label "Sinnesbindung"@de,
        "Sensory Anchoring"@en ;
    skos:definition "The axis on which the relation of awareness to the ordinary sensory channel is placed, from outward waking perception through withdrawal, the edge of sleep and dreaming to a condition in which no object is apprehended."@en ;
    pm:scaleFamily pm:subjectAxis .

pm:displacedLocus a pm:AwarenessLocus,
        pm:AwarenessValue ;
    rdfs:label "Verlagert"@de,
        "Displaced"@en ;
    skos:definition "The source has the awareness, or a body made of mind, leave the place of the physical body and act elsewhere."@en ;
    skos:note "The holdings already gather the material for this value across traditions; see convergences/stream.ttl and examinations/displacement.ttl."@en .

pmt:Ajivika a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Ājīvika-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Ājīvika transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphalasutta 19-21, in the Mahāsaṅgīti Tipiṭaka Buddhavasse 2500 edition of the Pali, read with the English translation of Bhikkhu Sujato (2018) and the German of Sabbamitta" ;
    skos:definition "A lost renunciant transmission teaching a fixed destiny through which every being passes, known only through the reports of its opponents."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered as the limiting case: a tradition with no surviving corpus of its own. Whatever it yields will be an ascription by hostile witnesses and must be recorded as one. Taken off pm:corpusNamed on 2026-10-07, and the ascriptions are recorded as such: every node of this file carries pm:thirdPartyAscription and names the canon that reports it."@en,
        "The corpus of this transmission is the corpus of its opponents, which is why pm:corpusWork here names the Dīgha Nikāya, the Aṅguttara Nikāya, the Saṁyutta Nikāya and the Jain Viyāhapannatti. The last is unopened for this transmission: it is read in traditions/jain.ttl in second hand for the narrative of Gosāla and his fiery power, which is a claim of the Jain transmission about a person and not a report of the Ājīvika doctrine. queries/unopened-works.rq will therefore carry it, and that is accurate."@en,
        "The one finding that no other registered transmission in this holding can produce: the doctrine denies the form of claim this project collects. There is no cause and no condition, no agency of one's own or of another, no power and no exertion, and no austerity that shortens the round. So this file has no pm:Yielding and cannot have one, and the holding now has a registered transmission whose place in it is to stand against the rest."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:AnguttaraNikaya,
        pmw:DighaNikaya,
        pmw:SamyuttaNikaya,
        pmw:Viyahapannatti ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DighaNikaya ;
    pm:principalCorpus "No transmitted corpus; the doctrine is reported in the Buddhist and Jain canons, chiefly the Sāmaññaphalasutta of the Dīgha Nikāya, the Chaḷabhijātisutta and the Kesakambalasutta of the Aṅguttara Nikāya, the Mahādiṭṭhisutta of the Saṁyutta Nikāya and the Viyāhapannatti" .

pmt:Anthroposophy a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Anthroposophie"@de,
        "Anthroposophy"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Rudolf Steiner, Wie erlangt man Erkenntnisse der höheren Welten?, Vorrede zur dritten Auflage (1909), in the German text of 1918",
        "Rudolf Steiner, Wie erlangt man Erkenntnisse der höheren Welten?, chapter Die Bedingungen zur Geheimschulung, in the German text of 1918" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission holding that a schooling of the inner life develops organs of perception for a supersensible world, and that the schooling can be set out in full and followed without a teacher."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at places entered on 2026-09-22 from the book of the schooling. The claim that distinguishes it in this register is the second half of the definition: the training is published entire, and the book says of itself that the teacher can give nothing but advice and that one speaks of one's perceptions to a teacher only if one happens to have one."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:SteinerGeheimwissenschaft,
        pmw:SteinerWieErlangtMan ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SteinerWieErlangtMan ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Steiner, Wie erlangt man Erkenntnisse der höheren Welten; Steiner, Die Geheimwissenschaft im Umriss" .

pmt:FourthWay a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Der Vierte Weg"@de,
        "The Fourth Way"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of Gurdjieff's teaching: a claim that ordinary waking is a form of sleep, and a work on attention conducted in the middle of ordinary life."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding. The passages in traditions/fourth-way.ttl were read in the Internet Archive full-text scan of the one-volume English text, which carries no imprint and no reliable pagination. Work and chapter are named, which meets findability; the edition is left unstated rather than guessed, as in traditions/saiva-sakta.ttl. The five origination nodes drawn from the same work cite London 1950 and were entered before this rule was applied to them; the discrepancy stands until somebody opens a copy with an imprint."@en,
        "Only Ouspensky has been opened. Beelzebub's Tales stands in the principal corpus and is unread, so the state below claims places entered and not a harvested corpus."@en,
        "The graph already holds the Fourth Way anthropology as an origination, because material from it circulates under ancient ascription. Registering the transmission does not touch that finding: the tradition transmits, and the pedigree remains unsupported."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous; Gurdjieff, Beelzebub's Tales to His Grandson" .

pmt:GraecoRoman a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Griechisch-römisches Zeugnis"@de,
        "Graeco-Roman testimony"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Herodotus, Histories IV.13-16" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of Greek and Latin historiographical and doxographical reports of extraordinary capacities, and of the argumentative literature that disputes them."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. For every citation in this file the edition or translation the passage was read from is not recoverable, and it is left unstated rather than guessed. The book and chapter numbers are stable across the modern critical editions, which is why the loss is smaller here than elsewhere; it is still a loss, because it is unknown whether the passages were read in the original or in translation."@en,
        "The file records that a route to Indian material has been proposed for the older Greek transmission and not shown. Disputed is therefore the state of the question, not a hedge."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:DeDivinatione,
        pmw:DivusVespasianus,
        pmw:HerodotiHistoriai,
        pmw:NaturalisHistoria,
        pmw:TacitiHistoriae ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HerodotiHistoriai ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Herodotus, Histories; Tacitus, Historiae; Suetonius, Divus Vespasianus; Pliny, Naturalis Historia; Cicero, De divinatione" .

pmw:AuPaysDeSetteCamaLeBouiti a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Le Scao, Au pays de Sette Cama: les fétiches, le Bouiti"@de,
        "Le Scao, Au pays de Sette Cama: les fétiches, le Bouiti"@en ;
    skos:note "Named by Trilles himself in the footnote at page 49: Au pays de Sette Cama, La religion des habitants. Les fétiches: le Bouiti, par le R. P. Le Scao, 1908-1909, Messager du St Esprit. The Bouiti material of pages 49 to 52 is this article, printed by Trilles in quotation marks."@en,
        "Not opened, and the reason is a property of the work: the Messager du Saint-Esprit is a congregational mission periodical of 1908-1909 for which no digitisation was found. Ways tried: archive.org by title and by the author name, Gallica by title and through the periodical catalogue. The article is reachable only through Trilles, which is why the nodes drawn from it carry pm:mediatedAttestation and name him with pm:readVia."@en,
        "Trilles warns about the vocabulary of this article himself, and the warning is worth more than the usual note on a mediator: he says the study was destined for a religious review, whence certain terms and comparisons. The words Grand Fétiche, statue and secret society in the passages below are therefore the mediator's register, and the transmission's own terms survive only where the article prints them - Bouiti, Ganga, Nzimba, Diboha, Dindimba, Likoundou."@en .

pmw:Cheirokmeta a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Die unter dem Namen des Zosimos überlieferten Schriften"@de,
        "The works transmitted under the name of Zosimos"@en ;
    skos:note "A collective identifier, and it says so rather than pretending to be a work. What Berthelot prints as the third part of his translation is a series of numbered pieces attributed to Zosimos; the number and the title of an individual piece were not established in this run, because the printed headings were not among the paragraphs the retrieval returned. The places entered under this identifier therefore cite the page of the edition and, where the printed paragraph carried one, its section number."@en,
        "The piece that carries the visions is transmitted under the title On Virtue and is the part of this corpus that is read and cited everywhere. That title is not entered as a work identifier here, because the heading itself was not read; a later run that reads it should split this identifier and carry the split back to every node below."@en .

pmw:Hantitassu a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Das Ritual der Ḫantitaššu"@de,
        "The ritual of Ḫantitaššu"@en ;
    skos:note "No translation of this work is reachable. The two editions that carry one, of 1996 and of 2016, are in print. What was read beside the original is the editors' own German paragraph-by-paragraph summary of contents printed with the edition, which is a check on the structure and not a translation of the wording. Every place entered from this work therefore rests on the original read without a translation beside it, and that is stated at each node that needs it. See prima-materia#691."@en,
        "Opened in full at the wording on 2026-10-06 in the critical edition hethiter.net/: CTH 395.1, textus 2015-06-16, both exemplars: A is KBo 11.14, a four-column tablet whose reverse fourth column is preserved with its colophon, and B is KUB 43.57 with KBo 18.174. The second version of the same ritual, hethiter.net/: CTH 395.2.1, whose single manuscript is KUB 57.79, was read in its preserved fourth column for the same places."@en,
        "The editors state two things about the two exemplars that the nodes below depend on. A is a general version and B was made for the king. And in the colophon A designates Ḫantitaššu as a man while B designates her as a woman."@en .

pmw:LiberRVVelSpiritus a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Liber RV vel Spiritus sub figura CCVI"@de,
        "Liber RV vel Spiritus sub figura CCVI"@en ;
    skos:note "Class B. Published with a photographic plate and an editorial note stating why the plate was necessary."@en .

pmw:Paskuwatti a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Das Ritual der Paškuwatti"@de,
        "The ritual of Paškuwatti"@en ;
    skos:note "No colophon is preserved. The name is taken from the incipit, where the text names itself as the word of Paškuwatti, woman of Arzawa, who dwells in Parašša."@en,
        "Opened in full at the wording on 2026-10-06 in the critical edition hethiter.net/: CTH 406 exemplar A, textus 2012-05-07 with the introduction of 2017-01-12, whose manuscripts are KUB 7.8 joined with KUB 9.27 indirectly and with KUB 7.5. The editors state that the indirect join was established by collation of the originals in 2009 and that their line numbering therefore departs from the editio princeps of 1987; the loci cited in this file are theirs."@en,
        "Script dating: the concordance catalogues the fragments of this composition as New Hittite."@en,
        "The name is the one this file had to correct. The reachable English translation, A. Goetze in Pritchard (ed.), ANET, 3rd ed. 1969, pp. 349-350, reads the practitioner's name as Pissuwattis; the critical edition and the editio princeps read fPaskuwatti. The identifier follows the leading transliteration of the edition used, as the rule at the instance requires, and the earlier form is recorded here so that a reader coming from the 1969 translation finds the node."@en .

pmw:TalmudBavliHolin a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Babylonischer Talmud, Traktat Holin"@de,
        "Babylonian Talmud, tractate Holin"@en ;
    skos:note "Read in the translation of Lazarus Goldschmidt, eighth volume, Zebahim, Menahoth, Holin, Leipzig, Otto Harrassowitz, 1909. The edition is copied from the title page in the full text, which names the eighth volume and the Leipzig imprint; the seventh volume of the same translation carries a different publisher and year, so the volume cannot be cited from the series alone."@en,
        "The part read is folio 7a to 7b, pages 819 to 822. The running heads over folios 6a to 9a run 819, 820, 822, 824, 826 without going backwards, so the page numbers cited are the printed ones. The tractate is spelt Holin on the title page and in the running heads, which render it HOLIN; the common English spelling is Hullin and the place is the same."@en,
        "This is the volume the first file said it had not opened and would need, because this translator's German is the only translation of this tractate in the public domain: Rodkinson did not translate it and Soncino is not free. Access was the obstacle and not the language. The identifier derbabylonischet07gold follows the pattern derbabylonischet<NN>gold for the seventh volume and for no other; the eighth is derbabylonischet08unse, in a series of identifiers running from derbabylonischet01unse to derbabylonischet08unse which carries this whole translation. That is a finding about the way and is entered in the table of access routes in AGENTS.md, so that no later run has to search for it again."@en .

pmw:XingqiYupeiMing a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Xingqi yupei ming, die Jadeinschrift über die Zirkulation des Qi"@de,
        "Xingqi yupei ming, the jade inscription on circulating the qi"@en ;
    skos:note "Not a book but an inscribed object of some forty-five characters, read here through the transcriptions and renderings of three modern works because no facsimile was reached. Its date is the subject of pmc:XingqiInscriptionDispute and the range proposed runs across three centuries, which is why the dispute node and not the concept nodes carries the question."@en .

pmc:AyurvedaVayahsthapana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Aufhalten des Alters, und die Rückkehr der Jugend zu einem bereits Alten"@de,
        "The arresting of age, and the return of youth to one already old"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.1.54-55 and I.1.71-73, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIII, Calcutta 1904, pp. 1022 and 1025-1026" ;
    skos:definition "The second capacity, and the one the lesson separates from mere length of life: not that age is slowed but that a body already decrepit is exchanged for a young one. The seers are said to have been freed from decayed bodies and to have obtained bodies that were foremost of their kind and youthfulness of years; Cyavana, who had become very old, is said to have become youthful once more through the preparation that carries his name; and of the same preparation it is said that one who is even decrepit, entering the chamber and taking it, soon casts off his decrepit form and acquires that of prime of youth."@en ;
    skos:note "A group of ten drugs is named in the lesson by the technical term vayahsthapana, capable of preventing the effects of age, and referred back to a list in the fourth lesson of the Sutrasthana which this run did not open. The capacity is entered from the places in this lesson that state it and not from that list."@en,
        "This is the half of the claim that would be settled in a season rather than in a century, and the lesson states it of named figures of the remote past — the Vaikhanasas, the Valakhilyas, Cyavana, Vasistha and the rest — and of nobody living. The distance between the strongest form of the claim and the nearest reported instance is the whole of the tradition's own prehistory."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pm:counterSearchNotCarried a pm:CounterSearchState ;
    rdfs:label "Gegensuche nicht geführt"@de,
        "Counter-search not carried"@en ;
    skos:definition "No attempt has been made to find work that contradicts, fails to replicate, or supersedes the evidence cited."@en .

pmp:Cizhen a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Nadelung"@de,
        "Needling"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 52 (Ci jin lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 14" ;
    skos:definition "The insertion of a needle at a named place on the body to a stated depth, held to move the qi of a depot or a vessel either towards fullness or towards emptiness. The corpus treats it throughout as an operation that can go wrong, and states the going wrong in the same register as the going right."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmp:MagnetismeGrandsCourans a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Magnetisieren mit grossen Strömen"@de,
        "Magnetizing by the long pass"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. II, pp. 28-33, and chap. III, p. 46" ;
    skos:definition "The operator seats the patient, places himself facing him with the patient's knees between his own, and takes the patient's thumbs between his fingers so that the inner surfaces touch, remaining so from two to five minutes or until he perceives an equal degree of heat between his thumbs and the patient's. He then raises his hands to the head, lays them on both shoulders for about a minute, draws them down the arms to the fingertips, repeats that pass five or six times, holds them a moment on the head, brings them down before the face at a distance of one or two inches to the pit of the stomach, lets them remain there about two minutes, and descends slowly to the knees or the feet. A pass from head to feet takes about half a minute. Passes are magnetic descending and not ascending, which is why the hands are turned obliquely when raised. The sitting is closed by passes prolonged beyond the hands and feet with the fingers shaken at each, transverse passes before the face and breast at three or four inches, and, with the patient standing, seven or eight passes from above the head to the floor with one hand before the body and one behind."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at this length because the length is the point. This is the most exactly specified operation in the holding: every element carries either a count or a duration in minutes, the direction of each movement is prescribed, and the reason for the direction is stated. A transmission that specifies to this degree can be performed by a stranger from the page, which is the condition under which any of its claims could be put to a trial at all."@en,
        "The session length and its frequency stand with it: the first sittings about an hour, and once the communication is established half an hour or three quarters, the sittings periodical and equal in duration, at the same hour every day, and above all the agreed hour not changed for many days in succession. Where the operator can give only two or three sittings a week the source has magnetized water supply the direct action."@en,
        "The source states in the same chapter that the choice of process is not itself essential, that other operators follow their own, and that the results are nearly the same; and at chapter I it says the will suffices to give the fluid its direction. The exactness above therefore does not belong to the doctrine — it is offered as a method to adopt so as not to be embarrassed. That tension is the transmission's own and is carried here rather than resolved: the same book that prescribes half a minute for a pass says that the passes could be otherwise."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmp:Samyama a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Saṃyama"@de,
        "Saṃyama"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.1-3" ;
    skos:definition "The combined application of dhāraṇā, dhyāna and samādhi to a single object, presented as the general operator from which the capacities of the third chapter follow."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PatanjalaYoga .

pmt:NahuaRitual a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Nahua-Ritualüberlieferung"@de,
        "Nahua ritual transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulo I, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces" ;
    skos:definition "The central Mexican transmission of address to the powers in things, of the day count, of an animal companion given at birth, and of a seed drunk in order to be questioned."@en ;
    skos:note "Read in this run: the first treatise of the manual of sixteen twenty-nine, chapters one to seven, in the running text. The remaining eight treatises, which carry the incantations themselves and the calling back of the tonal, were not reached: the reproduction used delivers its running text only to that point. That is a property of the access path and not of the work, and the next run resumes there rather than at the beginning. See prima-materia#228."@en,
        "The contact route is entered as undecided and no longer as absent, and the change is a correction rather than a finding. The registry recorded absent, which asserts that no route exists for the period in which the corpus was fixed. But this corpus was fixed a century after the conquest, by a Spanish priest holding an ecclesiastical office, among people who had been under mission for three generations, and the recorded material carries Christian elements in places. Whether what he wrote down transmits the pre-conquest practice, and in what proportion, is a question nobody in this graph has carried out. Undecided says that; absent said something stronger that was never earned."@en,
        "The edition is not fully stated, and that is a defect this file carries openly. The reproduction used gives the Spanish in an old orthography and does not name the edition it reproduces. Every source line below says so. Settling it is the first thing to do before more nodes are written on this tradition, because a later change of edition would touch all of them."@en,
        "The incantations were taken down in Nahuatl in order to be suppressed, so the words survive in the language they were spoken in inside a hostile frame. Both facts belong in the record."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteUndecided ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Sahagún, Florentine Codex, especially books VI and XI; Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas (1629); Codex Borgia" .

pmt:Spiritism a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Spiritismus"@de,
        "Spiritism"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Allan Kardec, Le Livre des Médiums, seconde partie, chapitre XXIV, sections 263-268 (French text in the standard numbering; the digital witness read does not name its printing)" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission founded by Kardec on answers obtained through mediums and arranged as a doctrine, with a stated rule for admitting a principle into that doctrine and a stated rule for judging which spirit is speaking."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered at corpus named until this file was written, and moved here on the first passages actually read. What was read at the wording: Le Livre des Médiums, chapter XXIV, sections 263 to 268, and chapter XXVI, sections 286 to 288; L'Évangile selon le spiritisme, the introduction, part II, under the heading of the universal control of the teaching of the spirits; La Genèse, chapter I, articles 51 to 55. Not opened: Le Livre des Esprits, the chapter on obsession in Le Livre des Médiums, and the Revue Spirite. The reasons stand in the issues named in the harvest note of prima-materia#497."@en,
        "The contact route stays at documented, as the registration had it. Nothing in this transmission is a candidate for independent attestation: it is a nineteenth-century European construction with named authors, a dated founding text and a documented reception, and its value to this graph lies elsewhere - in being the one body of material in the collection that states its own rule of verification in writing."@en,
        "The editions are a difficulty this file does not hide. Kardec renumbered and enlarged Le Livre des Esprits between 1857 and 1860, from about five hundred questions to about a thousand, and everything since is cited by the later numbering. The digital witnesses read here carry the standard numbering and do not name the printing they were set from. The sections cited are therefore findable but the edition is not stated, which SPEC section 10 requires. See prima-materia#498."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Kardec, Le Livre des Esprits; Kardec, Le Livre des Médiums; Kardec, L'Évangile selon le spiritisme; Kardec, La Genèse; the Revue Spirite, 1858-1869" .

pmw:FamaFraternitatis a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Fama Fraternitatis (1614)"@de,
        "Fama Fraternitatis (1614)"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened on 2026-09-30 and read complete in the English of Thomas Vaughan, London 1652. The work carries no internal division — no chapters, no numbered paragraphs — so a place in it is given by the passage and by the page of a named edition, and this file gives the passage. The German print of 1615 is available in the Internet Archive scan famafraternitati00andr and was consulted for the six articles only."@en .

pm:corpusUnreachable a pm:SearchOutcome ;
    rdfs:label "Korpus nicht erreichbar"@de,
        "Corpus unreachable"@en ;
    skos:definition "The corpus could not be obtained, and the note states which ways were tried. A finding about access and never about the transmission."@en ;
    skos:note "SPEC §15 requires that unreachability be established rather than assumed, and lists the ways that must be exhausted first. This value is where that finding is recorded; entering it without the note that names the attempts turns a first failed attempt into a verdict about a work."@en .

pmp:TheosophyKundaliniArousal a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Schlangenfeuer wecken und durch die Zentren fuehren"@de,
        "Arousing the serpent-fire and sending it through the centres"@en ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapters Force-Centres and The Serpent-Fire, pp. 295-303 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" ;
    skos:altLabel "kundalini"@en,
        "serpent-fire"@en,
        "the Fiery Power"@en ;
    skos:definition "The procedure to which the capacities of this file are attributed. The serpent-fire sleeps in the etheric centre at the base of the spine; to bring that first centre into full activity is precisely to awaken it, and the arousing is done by a determined and long-continued effort of the will. Once aroused it is by its force that the other centres are vivified, and the course through which it ought to move is a spiral one, in an order which varies for different types of people. Its function in connection with occult development is to vivify the centres and make them available as gates of connection between the physical and the astral bodies."@en ;
    skos:note "The operative core is withheld in the text and the withholding is stated: the author says he has specially and intentionally refrained from explaining how this arousing is to be done, or mentioning the order in which the force should be passed through the centres, because that should by no means be attempted except at the express suggestion of a Master who will watch over his pupil during the various stages of the experiment. So the work describes what the practice does, names its danger at length, and does not say how it is performed. This holding keeps that shape at generalizations/the-text-and-what-is-withheld.ttl; not added to it in this run, because SPEC 16 requires the counter-search against the holding before a generalization is enlarged and that search was not carried here."@en,
        "Where a pm:Reworking node belongs and why it is not written. The receipt is documented in the text itself: the practice is named kundalini in Sanskrit, it is called the Fiery Power and the World's Mother after The Voice of the Silence, and the chapter closes its warning by quoting the Hathayogapradipika iii.107, that it gives liberation to yogis and bondage to fools. pm:Reworking requires pm:reworkedClaim to point at an entered node of the transmission received from, and traditions/hathayoga.ttl carries no node for the serpent-fire; it is also not yet migrated to work identifiers, so touching it would oblige this run to migrate it, which is a strand of its own. queries/work-migration-backlog.rq carries that transmission. The verse number is the one the author gives and was not checked against the vulgate in this run; pmc:HathapradipikaRecensionDispute in traditions/hathayoga.ttl holds why that matters."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy .

pmt:Hermetic a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Hermetische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Hermetic transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Corpus Hermeticum I (Poimandres), in the translation of G. R. S. Mead" ;
    skos:definition "The Graeco-Egyptian transmission of an ascent through the spheres in which the ascending one sheds a vice at each, and of a rebirth reported as having happened to the speaker."@en ;
    skos:note "Mead's diction is Victorian and interpretive in places where the Greek is disputed; his zone list in particular has been rendered differently by later translators. What is entered here is what one named translation says, which is the standard this project sets, and it is why the edition stands on every node rather than in this header alone."@en,
        "The Discourse on the Eighth and the Ninth is an initiation with a sequence, a moment of change and a demand that the initiate confirm what he now sees. It has no equivalent in what the graph currently holds."@en,
        "The three decisions that precede a tradition file. Route of contact: documented in every direction that matters here — the corpus is Graeco-Egyptian of the first three centuries, and the traditions it would be compared with in this graph, the ophite diagram and the magical papyri, stand in the same milieu. Nothing entered from this file may be counted as an independent witness against them. Edition: read in the translation of G. R. S. Mead, public domain, named on every node; Nock and Festugière and the translation of Copenhaver were not consulted, and where Mead's wording carries a decision it is flagged. Number of citable passages: the first treatise only, and within it the ascent."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Corpus Hermeticum; Asclepius; the Stobaean excerpts including Korē Kosmou; Nag Hammadi VI,6" .

pmt:NewThought a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "New Thought"@de,
        "New Thought"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Horatio W. Dresser (ed.), The Quimby Manuscripts, 2nd ed. (New York: Thomas Y. Crowell, 1921), pp. 30-35",
        "Horatio W. Dresser (ed.), The Quimby Manuscripts, 2nd ed. (New York: Thomas Y. Crowell, 1921), pp. 50-61" ;
    skos:definition "The American transmission holding that mental states determine bodily and material conditions, and that the determination can be exercised deliberately."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim is stated in a form that invites counting, and it is the ancestor of most of what circulates today as manifestation."@en,
        "What was read: the collected papers of 1921 at twelve passages — pp. 30 to 35, the account of the mesmeric years and of the founder's own recovery; p. 44 from the subject's journal; pp. 50, 51, 55, 60 and 61, the editor's account of the conditions that were dropped and of the rule of mental command; pp. 104 to 106, the absent treatment at Wayne with the husband's letter; p. 115 with its editorial footnote; p. 195 from the manuscript headed Christ or Science; pp. 290 to 291, the case of the advertised medicine; and pp. 301 to 302, a patient's account of a first interview. Nothing else of the volume is opened, and nothing of Hopkins or Troward."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:HopkinsScientificChristianMentalPractice,
        pmw:QuimbyManuscripts,
        pmw:TrowardEdinburghLectures ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:QuimbyManuscripts ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Quimby, The Quimby Manuscripts; Emma Curtis Hopkins, Scientific Christian Mental Practice; Troward, The Edinburgh Lectures on Mental Science" .

pmt:SpiritualistAutomaticWriting a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Spiritistisches automatisches Schreiben"@de,
        "Spiritualist automatic writing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Andrew Jackson Davis, The Principles of Nature, Her Divine Revelations, and a Voice to Mankind, 3rd edn., New York: published by S. S. Lyon and Wm. Fishbough, 1847, Scribe's Introduction, pp. xvii-xviii" ;
    skos:definition "The nineteenth century transmission of extended texts produced by a writer who reports not composing them, and issued as scripture rather than as message. Taken here at its earliest layer, where the production is documented at length by the people who conducted it: a man put into a state by a named operator, questioned by nobody, taken down by a named scribe in the presence of three chosen witnesses, over a hundred and fifty-seven sittings."@en ;
    skos:note "And the title page states the ambiguity the whole file turns on, in three words. The work is BY AND THROUGH Andrew Jackson Davis. Which of the two it is, the volume answers twice and differently, and the disagreement is between its author and its scribe; it stands at pmc:DavisFonsDisputatus."@en,
        "Read at the wording on 2026-10-03: Davis, The Principles of Nature, third edition, New York 1847, at the title and copyright page, the Scribe's Introduction pp. iii-xxii, the Address to the World pp. 1-4, the chapters on animal magnetism and clairvoyance pp. 29-57, and the astronomical part at pp. 160-173. Not read: the greater part of the volume, named in the file header. Not opened at all: Newbrough, Oahspe, 1882, which this registration also names and which stands below as an identifier so that queries/unopened-works.rq shows it."@en,
        "What kind of witness this is, and it is an unusual one for this holding. Most transmissions here are read at a place and the circumstances of their fixation are lost. This one documents its own production more fully than any other corpus in the holding: it gives the operator's name, the scribe's name, three witnesses chosen in advance and named with the reason each was chosen, a list of twenty-three further occasional witnesses with their towns, the count of sittings at a hundred and fifty-seven, the count of manuscripts carrying signatures at a hundred and forty, the number of signatures at two hundred and sixty-seven, the range of sittings any one person attended as one to seventy-three, and the number of sittings with no signature at all as seventeen. A corpus that counts its own witnesses that precisely is making a claim that can be checked, and the manuscripts are declared to be preserved."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:DavisPrinciplesOfNature,
        pmw:Oahspe ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DavisPrinciplesOfNature ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Davis, The Principles of Nature, Her Divine Revelations, and a Voice to Mankind; Newbrough, Oahspe: A New Bible in the Words of Jehovih" .

pmt:Zoroastrian a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Zoroastrische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Zoroastrian transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The Book of Arda Viraf, ed. and trans. Martin Haug, E. W. West and Hoshang Jamaspji Asa (Bombay and London: Trübner, 1872), chapters 1-5" ;
    skos:definition "The Iranian transmission of a dualist cosmology, of a soul journey undertaken under a named preparation and reported on return, of an accounting at a bridge whose width answers to the traveller, and of purifications stated in numbers of holes, washings and nights."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered at corpus named until this file was written, and moved here on the first passages actually read. What was read at the wording: Arda Wiraz Namag, chapters 1 to 5, in the Haug and West translation; Hadhoxt Nask, fragments 2 and 3, in Darmesteter's translation; Vendidad, fargards 8 and 9, in Darmesteter's translation. Not opened: Yasht 19 on the glory, Menog i Xrad, Dadestan i Denig, the Denkard, and the Kirdir inscriptions. The reasons stand in the issues named in the harvest note of prima-materia#489."@en,
        "The contact route stays at disputed and is not moved, but the reason is now sharper than at registration. The earliest dated Iranian witness of an otherworld journey is not literary but epigraphic, in the inscriptions of the mage Kirdir under Wahram the Second, that is 274 to 293; the Book of the Watchers is third or second century before the era. Every Pahlavi book read here reached its surviving shape in the ninth century or later. Whoever wants to read the Iranian material as the source of the Jewish and Christian ascent literature must argue from the Avestan passages and not from these books. See prima-materia#490."@en,
        "Two editions stand behind the wording used here and they are of different weight. Haug and West of 1872 rests on a single, partly Pazand-contaminated witness; Gignoux 1984 and Vahman 1986 are the critical editions and were not reachable in full. Where a number in this file matters, it was read in the Haug and West text and the note on the node says so."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Avesta (Gathas, Yasna, Vendidad, Yashts); Hadhoxt Nask; Arda Wiraz Namag; Bundahisn; Denkard; Menog i Xrad; Dadestan i Denig" .

pmw:ArcanaCoelestia a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Swedenborg, Arcana coelestia"@de,
        "Swedenborg, Arcana coelestia"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened at four places only and not read through: numbers 784, 3884 to 3886, 9438 to 9439 and 10751. The author's own footnotes name a further set that was not opened — 1983, 2752, 4793 and 5990 on obsession and 3894 on the suffocation of the wicked in heaven — and the nodes below that depend on them say so."@en,
        "Read here in the English of the Standard Edition, Arcana Coelestia: The Heavenly Arcana, translated from the Latin and thoroughly revised and edited by John Faulkner Potts, American Swedenborg Printing and Publishing Society, New York; volumes 1, 5, 11 and 12 were opened. The numbering runs 1 to 10837 and is stable across the three English sets consulted; the bracketed subdivisions such as [2] and [3] are Potts's editorial work and not the author's, so a reference to a bracketed part is a reference to that edition."@en .

pmw:GinzaRabba a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Ginzā Rabbā, der große Schatz"@de,
        "Ginzā Rabbā, the Great Treasure"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened on 2026-09-20 in the German of Mark Lidzbarski of 1925, which is the translation the scholarship cites and which carries the page and line of Petermann's Mandaic text of 1867 in its running heads. Every source string below therefore gives the Petermann reference and the page of the German, because the two count differently and a later reader needs both. Of the two the Petermann reference is the safer: the copy read here is a scan whose optical reading damages the page numerals in the running heads, turning twenty-two into ninety-two and one hundred and eighty-four into a brace and eighty-four, while the text references beside them survive. The page numbers below were reconstructed from the sequence of the heads and not read off single ones."@en .

pmw:HekhalotRabbati a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Hekhalot Rabbati"@de,
        "Hekhalot Rabbati"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened at chapters 26, 30 and 31 in the translation of Morton Smith, corrected by Gershom Scholem and edited by Don Karr, read against the Hebrew of the Wertheimer recension. The paragraph numbering of this literature is Schäfer's, from an edition that could not be reached; see prima-materia#493. The Sar Torah paragraphs that carry the numbers were not opened."@en .

pmw:LeTotemismeChezLesFan a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan"@de,
        "Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan"@en ;
    skos:note "Access: archive.org identifier letotemismechezl00tril, full text over the regular _djvu.txt route, 1,690,570 characters. The tail is a Princeton Theological Seminary shelf stamp after the index, so the file ends where the book ends and is not one of the truncations the access table warns about. The running heads over the parts read run 49, 50, 51, 52, 53 and 271, 272 and 355, 356 without going backwards, so the pages cited are the printed ones."@en,
        "The edition as the title page in the full text gives it: Le Totémisme chez les Fan, par le R. P. H. Trilles, C.S.Sp., avec préface de Mgr. A. Le Roy, évêque d'Alinda, Sup. Gén. C.S.Sp., Münster i. W. 1912, Aschendorffsche Verlagsbuchhandlung, in the series Internationale Sammlung ethnologischer Monographien; the signature line at the foot of page 49 gives the series number as Bibliothèque Anthropos I/4."@en,
        "The title page spells the people Fan and the body of the book spells it Fân throughout. The work identifier follows the title page, as SPEC section 10 requires the edition to be the one read; the people are called Fang in the English literature and Pangwe in the German, which is why pmw:DiePangwe is a work about the same people under a third name."@en,
        "What was read at the wording: pages 47 and 48, the comparison of the Fang Biéri with the Fiote Bwiti; pages 49 to 52, the whole of the section Bouiti and the opening of Le Mboïo; page 271 with its first footnote; page 355 with the account of the mirror in the mvul horn. The volume runs to some five hundred pages. Not opened: the hundred and three places at which the Ngi cult is named, the chapters on the totem proper, and the Mboïo section beyond its first page."@en .

pmw:Mastigga a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Das Ritual der Maštigga"@de,
        "The ritual of Maštigga"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened in full at the wording on 2026-10-06 in the critical edition of the second version, hethiter.net/: CTH 404.1.II exemplar A, textus 2012-05-07, whose main manuscript is KBo 2.3 joined with KBo 45.191 and IBoT 4.13 and collated against KUB 12.34, KUB 15.39, KUB 12.59, IBoT 2.109, IBoT 4.12 and KUB 41.12. Forty-four paragraphs, read in order."@en,
        "The composition has three versions in the catalogue, CTH 404.1.I, .II and .III, and they are not duplicates. The first is edited from KBo 39.8 and is catalogued as Middle Hittite in script; the second, read here, is the one the two reachable translations treat. A redactional history of the group was published in 2004 and is in print and was not read. Which version is prior is therefore not asserted anywhere in this file."@en,
        "Two translations of this same version were read beside the original and they disagree at the place this file was opened for. English: A. Goetze, Ritual against Domestic Quarrel, in J. B. Pritchard (ed.), Ancient Near Eastern Texts Relating to the Old Testament, 3rd ed. 1969, pp. 350-351, which names KBo 2.3 and the same duplicates as its text. German: F. Hrozný, Hethitische Keilschrifttexte aus Boghazköi. In Umschrift, mit Übersetzung und Kommentar, Boghazköi-Studien 3, Leipzig: J. C. Hinrichs 1919, pp. 60-89, the editio princeps of this tablet, with transliteration and translation. The disagreement is at pmc:HittKisibApiyakkuDisputatum."@en .

pmw:SteinerWieErlangtMan a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Steiner, Wie erlangt man Erkenntnisse der höheren Welten? (deutscher Text von 1918)"@de,
        "Steiner, Wie erlangt man Erkenntnisse der höheren Welten? (German text of 1918)"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened on 2026-09-22 and read at the wording in the prefaces of 1909 and 1918 and in six of its chapters. Assembled by its author from separate essays, as the preface of 1909 says; the prefaces of 1914 and 1918 revise the presentation without withdrawing the method, and the preface of 1918 states that whoever strives for this path must give himself over to no deceptions, and that the soul experience meant here is misplaced when it is put in the same rank as mediumism and other degenerations of human striving."@en,
        "The transcription used carries no pagination, so the citations in this file name chapters. A run that needs a page must acquire the collected edition, which was not obtained here. This is a debt of the run and not a property of the work."@en .

pmc:AyurvedaSmrtimedha a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Gedächtnis und Verstand, und das Behalten des einmal Gehörten"@de,
        "Memory and understanding, and the retaining of what is heard once"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.1.58-60 and I.1.76, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, part XXXIII, Calcutta 1904, pp. 1023-1024 and 1028" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity claimed for almost every preparation in the lesson and stated twice in a form that is not a matter of degree: that the user becomes able to retain all he hears, and that whatever he hears even once he succeeds in remembering. The translator notes at the second place that some Bengali renderings make the user a srutidhara, one capable of reproducing whatever he hears even once, and rejects that reading for the passage he is annotating while adopting it at the other."@en ;
    skos:note "Of the whole set of capacities this is the one a trial could touch tomorrow, and the lesson gives it no term, no measure and no procedure. It is also the one place where the wording of the claim is disputed inside the translation itself, which is why the translator's note is reported here rather than smoothed away."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmp:TiantaiZhiguan a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Ruhe und Schau im Sitzen"@de,
        "Calming and contemplation in seated practice"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0465c to 0469a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:definition "The paired procedure the work teaches. Calming is the first gate for subduing the bonds, contemplation the main instrument for cutting delusion; the work calls them the two wheels of a cart and the two wings of a bird. Seated, the practitioner takes the half or full cross-legged posture, loosens the belt, lays the left hand on the right, straightens the spine so that nose and navel face each other, expels the stale breath three times through the open mouth, closes the mouth with the tongue to the palate and the eyes just enough to cut the outer light, and sits as still as a set stone. Within one sitting the three matters of body, breath and mind are watched and corrected as each goes out of adjustment; five turns of calming and contemplation are then applied according to which fault appears, and the same five are carried into walking, standing, sitting, lying, working and speaking, and into each of the six senses."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai .

pmt:Atharvavedic a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Atharvavedische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Atharvavedic transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Atharvaveda Saṃhitā 5.14, in the translation of W. D. Whitney, revised by C. R. Lanman, Harvard Oriental Series 7, Cambridge, Mass. 1905" ;
    skos:definition "The operative Vedic corpus: spells for healing, protection, harm, love and kingship, with the ritual manual that tells how each is employed."@en ;
    skos:note "Dating. The redaction is later than that of the Rigveda and the language of the hymns younger than the tenth book of the Rigveda, while much of the sorcery material may be older than either; the manual is later again and its own tradition names neither time nor place for its composition. This file therefore dates nothing and cites hymns by book and hymn, which is the citation the edition supports."@en,
        "Edition decision. The hymns were read in the translation of W. D. Whitney, revised and edited by C. R. Lanman, Harvard Oriental Series volumes 7 and 8, Cambridge 1905, which is the critical translation of the Śaunaka vulgate and prints its variants against the Paippalāda recension hymn by hymn. That apparatus is used here: where Whitney records that a verse is absent from the Paippalāda or stands there in another order or another book, the note says so, because a claim carried by one recension is not the same as a claim carried by the transmission."@en,
        "Read in this run at the wording, all in the Whitney translation: 4.20 entire; 5.14 entire; 6.27, 6.28, 6.29 entire; 6.108 verses 1 to 4; 6.114 entire; 6.115 verses 1 and 2; 7.70 entire; 10.1 verses 1 to 10; 18.2 verse 38; 19.67 entire. Kauśikasūtra 19.15 to 19.21 was read in Bloomfield's rendering and not in the Sanskrit, and the node that rests on it says so. Not read: everything else, which is the great bulk of a collection of twenty books. This file claims completeness for the hymns it opened and for nothing beyond them."@en,
        "The contact route is kept at documented and was not re-decided here. The corpus stands inside the Vedic transmissions this holding already carries, and the question worth asking of it — whether the returning of a spell upon its maker is shared with the Mesopotamian anti-witchcraft literature by contact or by convergence — was not carried at the wording in this run and no node claims it."@en,
        "The recension decision follows from the edition and is not free. This file holds the Śaunaka vulgate. The Paippalāda is not a variant reading of it but a second collection, in places closer to the Rigveda and in places more archaic, and its hymns are differently ordered and differently bounded; a node built from both would be a node about neither."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Atharvaveda Saṃhitā (Śaunaka recension); Kauśikasūtra" .

pmt:DesertMonasticism a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Wüstenmönchtum"@de,
        "Desert monasticism"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the ascetic literature of the Egyptian desert and its Byzantine continuation, in which the path is set out as an ordered ascent."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. For every citation in this file the edition or translation the passage was read from is not recoverable, and it is left unstated rather than guessed. The chapter numbering of the Praktikos and the step division of the Ladder both vary between editions, so the numbers here stand on nothing until the passages are reopened."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos; John Climacus, The Ladder of Divine Ascent" .

pmt:Ifa a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Ifá"@de,
        "Ifá"@en ;
    dcterms:source "A. B. Ellis, The Yoruba-Speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1894), chapter II, the section on Ifa" ;
    skos:definition "The Yoruba divinatory transmission: an enumerated set of signs obtained by casting, each sign carrying narratives that a diviner is expected to hold by memory and to recite on the sign falling."@en ;
    skos:note "Read in this run: the section on Ifa in chapter II of the account of eighteen ninety-four, and its chapter IV for what that author says about the origin of the god; and the appendix of the volume of nineteen hundred and six, which reprints an extract on Ifa by a Yoruba bishop and a second extract by a Sierra Leonean author on geomancy. Left unread: the remaining chapters of both volumes, and the twentieth-century monographs named in the principal corpus, which are in copyright and available only as lending copies. The text of eighteen ninety-four was read in an electronic reproduction that carries no printed pagination, so the places are given by chapter and section and not by page."@en,
        "The closest thing to a written corpus that was never written. It is fixed, enumerated, internally ordered and transmitted through a pupilage of stated length, which is what made it the entry point chosen from the registry."@en,
        "The contact route is entered as disputed and no longer as absent, and the correction is the main finding of the run that opened this file. The registry had recorded no route because none was looked for. One of the two extracts read here identifies the sixteen figures of the oracle with the apparatus of geomancy — twelve houses of the heavens, two witnesses, a judge and a further judge — which is the apparatus of the Arabic sand science and, through it, of material this graph already holds on its Mediterranean side. Until that identification is settled, an agreement between this tradition and anything already in the graph counts as neither reception nor independent attestation. The dispute stands as its own node below, which is what pm:contactRouteDisputed requires before an agreement may be weighed at all."@en,
        "Two of the works cited in this file were read at second hand, in the appendix that reprints them. Neither extract carries an imprint there, so neither citation gives place or year. A separately published copy of the bishop's pamphlet was searched for and not found: under both the author's name and the confusable name of the Yoruba historian, in the large text archives and in a library catalogue aggregator, and in a bibliography of early Yorùbá books. The nodes resting on those extracts therefore carry pm:mediatedAttestation and name the appendix with pm:readVia, and they remain open posts."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Odù corpus as published in Bascom, Ifa Divination, and Abimbola, Ifá: An Exposition of Ifá Literary Corpus" .

pmw:AphorismiHippocratis a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Hippokrates, Aphorismen"@de,
        "Hippocrates, Aphorisms"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened on 2026-10-02 at Section VI, read continuously in the English of Francis Adams, vol. II, London 1849. Sections I to V and VII were not read and stand in the same volume, so the cheapest continuation of this file is the same volume: Section VII carries the acute prognostic signs and Section V the material on women and on the seasons, and both bear on questions this holding already asks of other corpora."@en,
        "The Greek is not on this route. Anyone who needs the wording of a term here goes to Littré or to the Loeb, and until someone does, every node formed from this work is a node about a nineteenth-century English rendering of a Greek sentence."@en .

pmw:Tunnawiya a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Das Ritual der Tunnawiya"@de,
        "The ritual of Tunnawiya"@en ;
    skos:note "A second philological treatment of the same tablet is reachable and was used as the check on the 1938 transliteration: the independent transliteration with interlinear glossing in the Thesaurus Linguarum Hethaeorum digitalis, hethiter.net/: TLHdig KUB 7.53+ (2021-12-31). It is not a second translation, and no second translation of this work is reachable; the only one named in the current bibliography is in print. What was read at the wording is therefore one translation beside the original, and that is less than the five points of SPEC 15 require. See prima-materia#691."@en,
        "Opened in full at the wording on 2026-10-06 in the only edition that carries transliteration and translation together: A. Goetze, interpreted in cooperation with E. H. Sturtevant, The Hittite Ritual of Tunnawi, American Oriental Series 14, New Haven: American Oriental Society 1938, with its text, translation, commentary and glossary read through, and the title page read in the full text and not in the catalogue entry. The tablet is the join the edition itself states, Bo 2062 (KUB 7.53) with Bo 2589 (KUB 12.58), which in the modern form is KUB 7.53+."@en,
        "The script dating of this composition is late. Of the fragments the concordance assigns to CTH 409 the great majority are catalogued as New Hittite copies, with a single Middle Hittite fragment; the tablet read here is in the late group. The composition is therefore attested essentially only in copies made long after the practitioner it names."@en,
        "This work carries a title of its own, which the others do not, and it is in the colophon: the first tablet of the ritual of uncleanness and of the ritual of the river, this being the one of Tunnawiya the Old Woman, complete, written by Pikku. The identifier still follows the rule stated at the instance, because the rule has to produce one identifier for a corpus in which four of five colophons give only the name."@en .

pm:contactRouteUndecided a pm:ContactRoute ;
    rdfs:label "Kontaktweg unentschieden"@de,
        "Contact route undecided"@en ;
    skos:definition "The question has not been carried out. Registered explicitly rather than left absent, because an unstated contact route and a route shown to be absent are the difference between a witness and a candidate."@en .

pmp:NippurGetAgainstDemons a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Dem Dämon einen Scheidebrief schreiben"@de,
        "Writing the demon a writ of divorce"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, texts 8: 7-16, 9: 3-10, 11: 7-8, 17: 1-11 and 26: 6, with the commentary to 8: 7, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 153-160 and 170-191)" ;
    skos:altLabel "the magical Get"@en ;
    skos:definition "The operation that separates a lilith from the person she lies with by divorcing her in the forms of the rabbinic divorce court. The bowl carries a divorce-writ: the date stands at its head, both parties are named with their parentage, the writ is declared to have come down from heaven and to have come from across the sea, the demon is told to take her writ, and the separation is pronounced in the words of the court, I have divorced, separated, dismissed thee."@en ;
    skos:note "The editor states that he knows of no occurrence of this magical Get outside the bowls, and calls it the magicians' application to the unions of demons and mortals of the power of binding and loosing that the rabbis claimed. His own verdict on the logic stands in his commentary and is his: the procedure was very simple, if only the liliths were as submissive to divorce as their human sisters."@en,
        "The warrant the text gives itself for divorcing a spirit is that demons do the same among themselves: I have separated thee, like the demons who write divorces for their wives, and do not return to them. Two texts put it in those words and a third repeats it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmt:GraecoEgyptianAlchemy a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Griechisch-ägyptische Alchemie"@de,
        "Graeco-Egyptian alchemy"@en ;
    skos:definition "The earliest alchemical transmission, in which the operation on matter is reported together with dreams that interpret it, and in which a named practitioner writes to a named woman both a procedure and an accusation against the priests of his own art."@en ;
    skos:note "A limit of the access path, and it decides what this file may quote. One retrieval of the same interface returned the French paragraphs translated into German without saying so, while six others returned the original. Every passage a node below rests on came back in French; three paragraphs that came back in another language were treated as not read and carry nothing. See prima-materia#799."@en,
        "Edition: the French translation in M. Berthelot und Ch.-Em. Ruelle, Collection des anciens alchimistes grecs, seconde livraison, Paris 1888, read through the full-text hit interface of the digitisation, which returns whole paragraphs. It is textually superseded — Martelli's critical work has moved beyond it — and it is the only complete edition of the corpus there is, and it is out of copyright. Where a later editor would read otherwise, this witness says what Berthelot read."@en,
        "Number of citable passages: small, and the corpus is the reason. What survives under the name of Zosimos is a conglomerate — fragments of the Cheirokmeta, the visions, and Byzantine accretions — and Berthelot says so in his own preface, announcing the works or rather the fragments attributed to Zosimos. Twelve places are entered here, and the number of pieces the third part of the translation carries was not counted."@en,
        "The page numbers below are the printed pages, and that had to be established rather than assumed: the interface returns a leaf index. For this volume the two coincide, verified against the scan metadata and, independently, against the printed table of contents, which puts the pieces numbered 3.-41, 3.-43, 3.-49 and 3.-51 on the pages where those headings actually stand."@en,
        "The pieces are numbered in the edition and their numbers are not used below. The printed headings were not among the paragraphs the retrieval returned, so which numbered piece carries which of the pages read was not established; the one exception is the Final Reckoning, whose title stands in the table of contents and whose salutation was read. Everything else is filed under the collective work identifier, and a later run that reads the headings should split it."@en,
        "Three decisions before the first node. Route of contact: documented, and it was documented at the registration — this material stands at the head of the chain that runs through the Arabic and the Latin alchemy the holding already carries, so nothing here counts as an independent witness against them. The relation to the Egyptian temple milieu is a different question and the Final Reckoning speaks to it from inside."@en,
        "What was read at the wording: the first lesson of Sur la Vertu, pages 111 and following, at the vision of the altar and of the priest Ion; the Final Reckoning at its sections 7 and 8, pages 192 and 193, and at the two grades of tinctures, page 193; the passage about attempting, page 172; and the intervals on pages 114, 132, 143, 166 and 196. Not read: everything else in the eighty-seven pieces, including the whole of the technical treatises and the commentators, and the Greek text facing the translation."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:Cheirokmeta,
        pmw:LogosTeleutaios ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Zosimos of Panopolis, the Visions and the authentic memoirs; M. Berthelot und Ch.-Em. Ruelle, Collection des anciens alchimistes grecs, Paris 1887-1888" .

pmt:HawaiianRitual a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Hawaiische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Hawaiian transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Martha Warren Beckwith, The Kumulipo: A Hawaiian Creation Chant (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), chapter 7, pp. 35-36" ;
    skos:definition "The Hawaiian transmission of a chanted genealogy of the world, of the mastery of chanting as an office with a stated condition of failure, and of the house of the hula with its conduct, its cleansing and its graduation."@en ;
    skos:note "Coined as pmt:HawaiianCosmogony and renamed to pmt:HawaiianRitual on 2026-09-04 under prima-materia#471. The old identifier was written for the Kumulipo alone, and the reading widened the tradition, not the transmission: the file carries the mastery of chanting and the house of the hula, and a query that trusted the name would have taken a transmission of rite and office for a cosmogony. Renaming a coined identifier needs a compelling reason, and the reason here is that the name asserted something about the subject matter that the holdings contradict. The rename was carried in one pass, which is what SPEC §11 requires; nothing outside this repository is known to have referred to the old name."@en,
        "Registered at corpus named until this file was written, and moved here on the first passages actually read. What was read at the wording: Beckwith's Kumulipo, chapters one, three and seven; Emerson's Unwritten Literature of Hawaii, chapters two, four and five. Not opened: Malo's chapters on necromancy and on the kuni rite, the Fornander volume on the order of sorcery, Kamakau's newspaper series, and Pukui's article of 1949 which Beckwith cites for the chanting rule. The reasons and the paths tried stand in the issues named in the harvest note of prima-materia#470."@en,
        "The contact route is entered as documented and no longer as absent, and it is documented twice over. First against the Maori transmission this graph already holds: both descend from one East Polynesian dispersal, dated archaeologically to the thirteenth century, so a correspondence between them is common inheritance and not two witnesses. Second against the Christian material: the mission arrives in 1820, the seminary at Lahainaluna is founded in 1831, and every work read here was written or printed after that by men trained in that school or by their successors. Absent was true of the islands before contact and false of the corpus this graph can cite."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Kumulipo in the Hawaiian text with Beckwith's edition; Malo, Moolelo Hawaii, in the Emerson translation and in the Langlas and Lyon edition; Emerson, Unwritten Literature of Hawaii; the Fornander Collection of Hawaiian Antiquities and Folk-Lore; Kamakau's series in Ke Au Okoa" .

pmt:Maori a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Māori-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Māori transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882)" ;
    skos:definition "The Māori transmission of mana as an efficacy that attaches to persons and things, of tapu as its restriction, and of karakia as the form of address."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered at corpus named until this file was written, and moved here on the first passages actually read. What was read is Shortland 1882, chapters II to V and the appendix, in the 1882 London edition. Chapters VI and VII, on claiming and naming land, were not read; nothing else of the principal corpus was opened."@en,
        "The contact route is entered as disputed and no longer as absent, and the reason is a rule of this project rather than a judgement about Polynesia. pm:contactRouteAbsent asks whether a route was available for the period in which the corpus was fixed. This corpus was fixed in writing from the eighteen-forties onward, a generation after the first mission stations, by Māori writers and by European recorders working with mission orthography. Shortland himself reports a rite of removing tapu that was carried out with a Christian morning service inside it and adds that the teacher had done wisely in keeping so much of the old ceremonial; that is not a suspicion about the material but an observation printed in the witness. The transmission is older than the contact; the corpus this graph can cite is not. See prima-materia#102 for the same question across the rest of the registry, and prima-materia#108 for the source criticism that decides it."@en,
        "The word from which every western theory of an impersonal magical force was built. The graph uses the notion throughout and has never held the tradition it came from."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Ngā Mōteatea; Best, The Maori; Best, Tuhoe" .

pmt:PistisSophia a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Pistis Sophia"@de,
        "Pistis Sophia"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pistis Sophia 1-18 (trans. C. Schmidt, Koptisch-gnostische Schriften I, GCS 13, Leipzig 1905)" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the Askew Codex, in which the risen Jesus puts on a garment of light inscribed with the names of every order below, ascends through firmament, sphere, fate and the twelve aeons, and afterwards explains to his disciples what he did to the rulers on the way."@en ;
    skos:note "Route of contact: documented and close. The work cites the two Books of Jeu by name as writings of its own circle, so nothing entered here may be counted as an independent witness beside traditions/books-of-jeu.ttl. Both belong, on Schmidt's account, to the same Egyptian group that the heresiologists split into Ophites, Sethians, Cainites and Archontics — which is the same reason nothing here counts independently beside traditions/ophite.ttl either."@en,
        "The work names itself otherwise. Schmidt shows that the title Pistis Sophia is a convenience: the manuscript's own subscription reads a part of the books of the Saviour, and the figure of the Pistis Sophia does not appear in the second half at all. The name is kept here because it is the one under which the work is found, and this note is the correction."@en,
        "Two editions in one file, and the edition therefore stands on every node rather than in this header. Their transliterations differ where it matters most for a later comparison: Schmidt writes Jeu, Mead writes Yew, for the same figure who is also the Jeu of traditions/books-of-jeu.ttl. Anyone tracing this name through the literature meets all three spellings before meeting a disagreement of substance."@en,
        "What was read: chapters 1 to 18, in the German translation of Carl Schmidt, Koptisch-gnostische Schriften I (GCS 13, Leipzig 1905), the volume that also carries the Books of Jeu; and chapter 25 separately, in the English of G. R. S. Mead (London 1921), because the first volume was read only as far as chapter 18. Chapters 19 to 24, everything from 26 onwards, the repentances of the Sophia and the whole of the fourth book with its rites are unread."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Pistis Sophia in the Askew Codex, British Museum MS Add. 5114; ed. and trans. Carl Schmidt, Koptisch-gnostische Schriften I, GCS 13 (Leipzig 1905)" .

pmt:RenaissanceMagic a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Gelehrte Magie der Renaissance"@de,
        "Renaissance learned magic"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Agrippa, De occulta philosophia I.66 (trans. J. F., London 1651)",
        "Agrippa, De occulta philosophia III.4 (trans. J. F., London 1651)" ;
    skos:definition "The early modern attempt to give magic a philosophy: sympathies, images, memory as an operative art, and angelic communication kept as a record."@en ;
    skos:note "The Latin of 1533 is reachable and was not read, and the distinction matters for what a later run has to do. Three digitised copies of the Latin print were taken and found legible in the body at the sentence, with the usual noise of an early modern scan; a run that wants to know whether a condition entered below stands in the Latin as it stands in the English has the text and needs no new acquisition. The chapters at issue are also the ones where that question has teeth: the literature reports that the manuscript of 1510 does not contain the chapters carrying the dignification doctrine, so the belief condition and the sentence about false operations may belong to the print of 1533 and not to the work as its author first gave it away."@en,
        "Two layers stand between these nodes and the work, and both are named because the holding has been caught by an unnamed second layer before. The first is the translation of 1651, which is a translation and not the text; the second is the digital transcription of that print in which it was read, which carries the translator's English with the transcriber's corrections marked in brackets against the Latin. Nothing in bracket is treated here as the print's wording. As a control, the optical transcriptions of two copies of the same print of 1651 were taken from the full-text archive; both render the long s as f and break words badly enough that they are usable to confirm that a passage exists and not to read it, and they were used for that and nothing more."@en,
        "What the translation of 1651 is good enough for, and what it is not. The nodes below turn on whether a condition is stated, on what is made to depend on what, and on where a failure is assigned. Those survive a translation. A node turning on a single word would not, and none is written; the one place where a single word decides something — the translator's credulous, which in 1651 means trusting and now means gullible — is recorded at the node itself rather than built into a claim."@en,
        "What was read: Agrippa, De occulta philosophia, in the English translation of 1651, at nine places — the address to the reader in full; book I chapters 20, 66 and 67; book II chapters 29 and 34; book III chapters 2, 3, 4, 53 and 65; and the opening of the piece headed as the author's censure or retraction, printed after book III. Nothing else of the work is opened, and nothing of Ficino, Bruno or Dee."@en,
        "Why it is not an independent witness, stated at the registration and unchanged. The author names his predecessors himself, and among them the Spanish Picatrix, so an agreement between this work and the Arabic astral magic is reception with a route and not two witnesses. What the work can carry is the other thing SPEC §14 asks for: it states conditions, terms, hours and failures, and it states them about operations it prescribes."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:DeOccultaPhilosophia,
        pmw:DeUmbrisIdearum,
        pmw:DeVitaLibriTres,
        pmw:MonasHieroglyphica ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeOccultaPhilosophia ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Ficino, De vita libri tres; Agrippa, De occulta philosophia; Bruno, De umbris idearum; Dee, Monas Hieroglyphica and the spiritual diaries" .

pmt:Shinto a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Shintō"@de,
        "Shintō"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Nihongi, Book I, the Age of the Gods, in the English of W. G. Aston, Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697, vol. I, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896, pp. 35-40" ;
    skos:definition "The Japanese transmission of kami, of purification as the central operation, and of formal address preserved in the imperial liturgies."@en ;
    skos:note "Read in this run at the wording: the chronicle of 720 in the English of W. G. Aston of 1896, volume I, which covers Books I to XVI, from the Age of the Gods to the reign of Seinei. Read closely and in full: Books I and II, the two Books of the Age of the Gods with all their variant versions; and within Books V, IX, X and XIII the passages on divination, on the ordeal and on abstinence. Not read: the reigns of Books III, IV, VI to VIII, XI, XII, XIV to XVI except where a search fell in them, and the whole of volume II, Books XVII to XXX. Not opened at all: the Kojiki and the norito of the Engishiki, which its registration names beside the chronicle."@en,
        "The contact route stays at disputed and this run did not settle it, but it did read what the work itself says about the question. The chronicle is written in Chinese, dates by the Chinese cycle, and quotes Chinese books; and at the place where an emperor proposes to commit a question to the Sacred Tortoise, the editor notes that the ancient Japanese divination was by roasting a deer's shoulder-blade and that the tortoise-shell is the Chinese practice. The work therefore carries two divinatory techniques at once, one of which its own editor marks as foreign, and the borrowing is visible inside the source rather than only in the scholarship. What the run did not do is establish which parts of the doctrine travel with the technique, and no node here claims it."@en,
        "What kind of witness this work is. It is a court chronicle compiled in 720 out of earlier material, and it says so of itself by printing its variants: the Age of the Gods is given as a main narrative with up to eleven further versions introduced as in one writing it is said, and the versions differ on matters this project cares about, including who produced which children in the oath below. A transmission that prints its disagreements is a better witness than one that smooths them, and it is also a warning: a claim read off the main text alone is a claim about one recension."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:EngishikiNorito,
        pmw:Kojiki,
        pmw:Nihonshoki ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Nihonshoki ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Kojiki; Nihon shoki; the norito of the Engishiki" .

pmw:ChisongziZhangli a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Chisongzi zhangli"@de,
        "Chisongzi zhangli"@en ;
    skos:note "Six juan, DZ 615, in the memorials class of the Dongxuan section. Juan 1 and 2 read complete, juan 4 read at the petition for the five tombs, the remaining juan searched by term. It is the working manual of the petition: the tariff of pledges per petition, the calendar of days on which a petition may and may not be sent, the liturgy of the officers invoked, the pollution intervals, and the forms of the petitions themselves. The work as it stands is later than the other two and its own preface says at 001a that of what once existed ten parts in one or two survive."@en .

pmw:ConfessioFraternitatis a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Confessio Fraternitatis (1615)"@de,
        "Confessio Fraternitatis (1615)"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened on 2026-09-30 and read complete in the English of Thomas Vaughan, London 1652, which prints it with the preface announcing thirty-seven reasons. The work divides into fourteen capita and the division is stable across prints, so a place in it is given by caput where the passage sits in one; the Vaughan print of 1652 runs the text without caput numbers, and this file therefore locates by the passage."@en .

pmw:DavisPrinciplesOfNature a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Davis, Die Prinzipien der Natur, ihre göttlichen Offenbarungen"@de,
        "Davis, The Principles of Nature, Her Divine Revelations, and a Voice to Mankind"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened on 2026-10-03 in the third edition, New York 1847, over the archive.org full text principlesofnatu00davi. Read in this run at that copy: the title page and the copyright page, which give the edition and the printer Redfield and Savage who stereotyped it, and the running-head sequence from 3 to 782, checked for monotonicity before any page number below was taken from it."@en,
        "The cheapest continuation is the part of the same volume that was not read, and the work itself names the reason to go there: the scribe writes that the evidence for the claimed source will be apparent on a perusal of the whole volume and especially of pp. 33 to 57, which is read. Six further pages are worth opening on a report and not on a reading: the research that preceded this run gives pp. 121, 237, 242, 263, 270 and 312 as the passages Podmore attacks on geological and palaeontological grounds. Podmore was not opened in this run, so those six numbers are an address and nothing more. A run that wants a protocol rather than more claims opens Podmore and then those six places, in that order."@en,
        "The stereotype plates make the pagination common to the editions from 1847 into the eighteen-eighties, and that is a second-hand statement in this file rather than a reading of this run. The research that preceded the run reports the same wording found at the same page in four further scans — the tenth edition of 1852, the twentieth of 1855, the thirty-second of 1871 and an undated thirty-fifth — and this run did not repeat the comparison. The copyright page it did read states the stereotyping, which is the ground of the inference and not the measurement. The consequence for a reader is small and should be said anyway: a page number from this file is reliable for this copy, and reliable for the others on a report."@en .

pmw:QuimbyManuscripts a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Dresser (Hrsg.), The Quimby Manuscripts, 2. Aufl., New York 1921"@de,
        "Dresser (ed.), The Quimby Manuscripts, 2nd ed., New York 1921"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened on 2026-09-22 at fourteen places. Not one text but five: the editor's connecting narrative, the founder's manuscripts printed verbatim, newspaper notices of 1840 to 1847, letters from patients, and the private journal of the mesmeric subject beginning 26 December 1843. Each node above says which of the five it stands on, and the attestation modes differ accordingly."@en,
        "The volume was published into a dispute over what the founder of Christian Science owed to this healer, whose patient she had been, and its preface says the manuscripts had been withheld from publication for many years although earnestly sought. That is the reason the editor's narrative is taken here as an interested account and the manuscripts he prints are not."@en .

pmt:Dogon a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Dogon-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Dogon transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 273-294" ;
    skos:definition "The Dogon transmission of a renewal of the world celebrated once in sixty years, of the ways by which the interval is counted, and of a seed-star said to be the smallest and the heaviest of things."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered at corpus named until this file was written, and moved here on the first passages actually read. What was read is Griaule and Dieterlen 1950 in full, pp. 273-294 of the first printing, in the digitised page images of the journal. Nothing else of the principal corpus was opened: neither Dieu d'eau, nor Masques dogons, nor Le renard pâle, nor Les âmes des Dogon. The Sirius article was chosen first because it is the single text the outside reception rests on."@en,
        "The contact route is entered as disputed and no longer as absent. The reason is in the article and not in a later judgement about it: on p. 282 the recorders attach to the fifty-year orbit a footnote observing that this period lies very close to that of the companion of Sirius, and they cite a French astronomical periodical of 1931 for it. Whatever the informants said, the account that reached print was written by readers of European astronomy, and the central enquiry ran from 1946 to 1950 in a region under French administration with a mission station in it. A route to the material this graph already holds therefore exists at the point where the corpus was fixed, and it runs through the recorders. See prima-materia#102 for the same question across the rest of the registry."@en,
        "The registration named van Beek's re-study as part of the principal corpus. That was wrong under SPEC §14: a field re-study is a procedure with an outcome that could have gone otherwise, which makes it evidence and not testimony. It has been removed from the corpus here and stands at pm:evidenceFrom on the examination node instead."@en,
        "The tradition most widely reported outside itself, and reported wrongly. Registered in this graph from the start as the standing warning of the whole registry, because a body of teaching that circulated for decades as ancient African astronomy could not be found by later workers among the people it was ascribed to."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Griaule, Masques dogons; Griaule, Dieu d'eau; Griaule and Dieterlen, Le renard pâle; Dieterlen, Les âmes des Dogon; Griaule and Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius" .

pmt:SolomonicGrimoire a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Salomonische Grimoire-Tradition"@de,
        "Solomonic grimoire transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Clavicula Salomonis, Book II chapter I, in the English of S. L. MacGregor Mathers, The Key of Solomon the King, London: George Redway, 1889" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of ritual handbooks for the constraint of spirits, carrying preparation, circle, seal, adjuration and dismissal as a fixed sequence."@en ;
    skos:note "Dating and attribution. The ascription to Solomon is not held by any modern position and is contradicted by the material record: the oldest witnesses of the work are late medieval. This was not read at the wording in this run and is not entered as a node; what the run did read is a work of 1584 that rejects the ascription in terms, and that stands below as one side of the dispute. The question of a Greek antecedent — whether the Hygromanteia is the ancestor of the Latin and Italian text or a parallel branch — was not decided here and no node claims it."@en,
        "Edition decision, and it is the awkward one. The text read is the English of S. L. MacGregor Mathers, London 1889, and that is not an edition of a work but a text assembled by its editor out of seven manuscripts of the British Museum: Add. 10862 in Latin, Sloane 1307 in Italian, Sloane 3091, Harleian 3981, King's 288, Lansdowne 1202 and 1203 in French. No critical edition of the Latin Clavicula with a stemma exists. The citations here therefore name the book and chapter and say in the same breath whose text it is, and where Mathers records that a passage stands in one manuscript only, the note says which. Anyone who obtains a single manuscript can overturn a node here, and the notes are written so that he can see which one to obtain."@en,
        "Read in this run at the wording, all in the Mathers text: Book I chapters I, III, V, VI, VII, IX, X, XI, XII, XIII, XIV, XV, XVI, XVII; Book II chapters I, III, IV, XIII, XXI. Not read: Book I chapters II, IV, VIII, XVIII, and Book II chapters II, V to XII, XIV to XX, XXII, which carry the tables of hours, the prayers, the pentacles, the instruments and their consecrations. The chapters left unread are the apparatus; the chapters read are the ones that state what the operations do, on what terms, and what follows when they do not."@en,
        "What the citations cannot do. Mathers' apparatus is thin and his own datings are disputed: he places Add. 10862 at the end of the sixteenth century and holds it the oldest witness, while the modern description puts it in the seventeenth. Two chapters entered here stand in one manuscript each by his own footnote, and are marked as such at the node. The single-manuscript chapters are not weaker as claims about what a Solomonic book says; they are weaker as claims about what the transmission holds in common."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:Abramelin,
        pmw:ClaviculaSalomonis,
        pmw:Heptameron,
        pmw:Lemegeton ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ClaviculaSalomonis ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Clavicula Salomonis; Lemegeton; Heptameron; the Book of Abramelin" .

pmw:LuxianshengDaomenKelue a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Lu Xiujing, Lu xiansheng daomen kelüe"@de,
        "Lu Xiujing, Lu xiansheng daomen kelüe"@en ;
    skos:note "One juan, DZ 1127, in the Taiping section. Read complete. The work is a list of defects: at 002a the author says that in the present observance everything is upside down and nothing is not the reverse of what it should be, and that he will accordingly set out its illnesses below. It is therefore a witness of a particular kind — the norm it states is stated against a practice it says departs from it, so that every rule in it comes with a report that the rule is broken."@en .

pmw:TalmudBavliPesahim a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Babylonischer Talmud, Traktat Pesahim"@de,
        "Babylonian Talmud, tractate Pesahim"@en ;
    skos:note "Read in the translation of Lazarus Goldschmidt, second volume, Erubin, Pesahim, Joma, Berlin, S. Calvary und Co., 1901, identifier derbabylonischet02unse. The edition is from the title page in the full text."@en,
        "The part read is folio 110a to 112b, pages 703 to 714. The running heads run 703, 706, 708, 710, 713, 714 without going backwards; one head came back from the scan as 7111 and is an artefact of the optical reading rather than a page, and no citation here rests on it."@en,
        "Why this edition and not the free English. The lease for this work warned that Rodkinson's English is defective for the material on sorcery at three measured places, which the search node in examinations/searches-rabbinic-sorcery-law.ttl established for tractate Sanhedrin. The German was therefore used as the text and the English was not consulted at all for this tractate, which means the comparison the first file carried for Sanhedrin has not been carried here and is not claimed."@en .

pmw:ZhengyiFawenTianshiJiaojieKejing a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Zhengyi fawen tianshi jiaojie kejing"@de,
        "Zhengyi fawen tianshi jiaojie kejing"@en ;
    skos:note "One juan, DZ 789, in the precepts class of the Dongshen section. Read complete. It is a composite: a general admonition to folios 012a, then the Dadao jia lingjie from 012a, then a seven-word instruction to the libationers, then the Yangping parish instruction, then a set of five-word verses. The Dadao jia lingjie dates itself at 016b to the seventh day of the first month of the second year of Zhengyuan, and the Yangping instruction at 020b gives the founding as the first day of the fifth month of the first year of Han'an."@en .

pm:Positing a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Setzen"@de,
        "Positing"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "A claim held in the graph solely so that a protocol has something to address. It witnesses no transmission, claims no source, and belongs to no tradition."@en ;
    skos:note "The class exists because a protocol needs an object and because the claims most worth examining are often the ones no received work carries. It is deliberately narrow: an instance may exist only where a pm:Testing points at it, enforced by pm:PositingRequiresExaminationShape. Without that restriction the class would become the channel through which an unpublished draft enters the record after all, and the direction the project runs in would be reversed."@en .

pm:cededAgency a pm:AgencyHolding,
        pm:AwarenessValue ;
    rdfs:label "Abgetretene Trägerschaft"@de,
        "Ceded agency"@en ;
    skos:definition "The source has another speak or act through the practitioner, who is described as receiving, carrying or being ridden rather than as doing."@en ;
    skos:note "The axis records the account the source gives, not a verdict on it. A tradition that credits a spirit and one that credits a trained faculty differ in what they claim, and that difference is worth holding whatever either claim is worth."@en .

pmt:BoranKammatthana a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Borān kammaṭṭhāna"@de,
        "Borān kammaṭṭhāna"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Yogāvacara's Manual, the meditation of the fivefold zest (trans. Woodward 1916, p. 6-10)",
        "Yogāvacara's Manual, the preamble (trans. Woodward 1916, PTS Translation Series VI, p. 1-3)" ;
    skos:definition "The esoteric Theravāda transmission of Southeast Asia, working with syllables placed in the body and a nimitta led along an interior path."@en ;
    skos:note "Largely displaced by the modern reform movements and therefore poorly represented in every general account of Buddhist meditation. The displacement is itself dated and named: the Dhammayuttika reform of the eighteen-thirties in Siam, and then the spread of the Burmese insight movement, after which the older practice survived in a few temple lines and in manuscripts."@en,
        "The shape of the work decides how much of it can be cited and how little needs to be. It is a liturgy with rubrics: a formula of request, a posture instruction, and a rubric saying which element appears and in which three colours, repeated through five raptures, then through twelve exercises, then through five jhānas, then through ten devices, in right succession and in reverse order each time. Rhys Davids reckoned it at a hundred and twelve mental states by twelve ways, doubled by the two directions. The printed text abbreviates most of the repetitions with et cetera, and what varies between them is the element and the colour triad. So the citable content is a small number of rubrics and a large number of repetitions of them, and this file carries the rubrics."@en,
        "What was read: the Yogāvacara's Manual complete in Woodward's translation of 1916 — the preamble, the invocation, the meditation of the fivefold zest with its twelve sub-exercises including the wax-taper offering and the task of self-control, the six pairs, the meditation on happiness, the breathing exercise for mental clarity, the exercise by devices, the ten foul things, the bodily parts, the ten recollections, the immaterial realms, the four highest states and the ten forms of knowledge. The two editors' prefaces and the translator's prefatory note were read as apparatus; Jayatilaka's appendix on the Sinhala revival was read for the dating and carries nothing here. Nothing of the Siamese or Khmer branches of the transmission is opened."@en,
        "Why it is not an independent witness, and where the qualification bites. For anything Theravāda — the jhānas, the kasiṇas, the ten insights, the thirty-two parts of the body — the route to the Pali commentaries is documented and this corpus counts for nothing additional. For the wax-taper clock and for the colour triads the editor of 1916 states that no route is known to Indian or Burmese Buddhism. That is an absence of a documented route and not a documented absence, so it does not license pm:independentAttestation, and none is claimed anywhere in this file."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:YogavacarasManual ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:YogavacarasManual ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Yogāvacara manuals of Sri Lanka and Siam in the published editions" .

pmt:JibaroShuara a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Jibaro-Überlieferung (Shuara)"@de,
        "Jibaro (Shuara) transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Rafael Karsten, Blood Revenge, War, and Victory Feasts among the Jibaro Indians of Eastern Ecuador, Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 79 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1923), pp. 1-3" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the Jibaro of eastern Ecuador, who call themselves Shuara, taken here at the part of it this work records: the narcotic drink by which the ancestors are seen and consulted before a killing, the observances a slayer is bound to afterwards with their stated terms and their stated penalty, and the rule that what a man eats and does passes into the thing he is making."@en ;
    skos:note "Read at the wording in this run: the introduction and the chapters on the weapons, on the origin and conduct of the feuds, on the observances after a killing, on the washing of the blood, on the feast suamartinyu, and the second day of the tsantsa feast — printed pages 1 to 19, 34 to 37, 46 to 47 and 70 to 73. Not read: the preparation of the head trophy (pages 28 to 33), the first and third to seventh days of the tsantsa feast, the concluding remarks on the feast at page 87, and the plates. The work is 94 printed pages and roughly a third of it was read."@en,
        "The position of the corpus, which SPEC §10 asks for beside the gathering of each passage. The work names no informants, gives no dates of collection and reports no interpreter; its letter of transmittal is dated 1 February 1921 and bounds the gathering from above. Karsten writes throughout as someone who was present and had the Jibaro terms, and he prints three chants in the language with translation, so the wording of the transmission is carried for those and not for the rest. He is also writing after three centuries of mission and trade contact that he himself describes — firearms are received from the whites in exchange for a head trophy — so this is a corpus fixed inside contact and not before it."@en,
        "What this file does not do. It does not take pmt:Amazonian down from the register, it does not claim that the Jibaro material stands for the lowland, and it does not assert any convergence. Its purpose is that the first lowland South American transmission in this holding exists as something other than a registration."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:KarstenBloodRevenge ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:KarstenBloodRevenge ;
    pm:principalCorpus "No transmitted written corpus; Karsten, Blood Revenge, War, and Victory Feasts among the Jibaro Indians of Eastern Ecuador, Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 79; Karsten, The Head-Hunters of Western Amazonas; Up de Graff, Head Hunters of the Amazon, as the account of an outsider" .

pmt:TibetanTantric a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Tibetisch-tantrische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Tibetan tantric tradition"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the Indian tantric completion-stage practices in Tibet, of which the six yogas of Nāropa are the best documented cycle."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. The Tsongkhapa and Bar do thos grol citations name the translation they were read in. For Patrul Rinpoche's Kun bzang bla ma'i zhal lung neither edition nor translation is recorded, and the citation names no chapter; it is left unstated rather than guessed and the passage is to be reopened."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations; the Bar do thos grol in the Karma Lingpa recension; Patrul Rinpoche, Kun bzang bla ma'i zhal lung" .

pmt:Vodun a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Fon-Vodun-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Fon vodun transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "A. B. Ellis, The Ewe-speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1890), p. 97" ;
    skos:definition "The Fon and Ewe transmission of powers received into a person and of bound objects that act, together with the Fa oracle, in which sixteen palm nuts are cast until a figure is read off the count that remains in the hand."@en ;
    skos:note "Chosen from the registry on the independence entrance, and the reading corrected that entrance. The contact route is entered as documented and no longer as absent, and the route runs to a transmission this graph already holds: the eastern Ewe are said to divine after the manner of the Yoruba god of divination, and the Fa practitioner is identified by a second and independent witness with the Egba babalawo. An agreement between the Fa figures and the Ifá figures is therefore reception and may not be counted twice."@en,
        "Read in this run at the wording: Ellis 1890, printed pages fifty-seven to fifty-nine, ninety-one to ninety-eight; Skertchly 1874, printed pages three hundred and seventy-nine to three hundred and eighty and four hundred and seventy-three to four hundred and seventy-six; Burton 1864, volume two, printed pages one hundred and forty-three to one hundred and forty-five. The remainder of each work was searched by term and not read continuously."@en,
        "The recorder problem. Two of the three are visitors of weeks or months who wrote for a European public and did not command the language; the third is a colonial officer and linguist who corrects the other two on terminology and who was himself in the country. Where they agree the agreement is worth something, because they did not work from one another on these points; where they differ, as they do on the width of the divining board and on the reading of the marks, the difference is recorded rather than smoothed."@en,
        "Two works that carry the centre of this transmission were not reachable in this run and the corpus names them all the same. The chapter in which Burton describes the casting in detail stands in volume one, and neither reachable digitisation of that volume carries it; the volume-two text read here refers to it. The systematic work on the oracle, Maupoil 1943, has no free full text at all, and the Fa chapter of Herskovits 1938 stands in a volume two that is available only on loan. The file therefore rests on two travellers and one missionary-linguist, which is a thinner base than the Kongo file has, and says so."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Ellis, The Ewe-speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa; Burton, A Mission to Gelele, King of Dahome; Skertchly, Dahomey as it is; Spieth, Die Ewe-Stämme, and Die Religion der Eweer in Süd-Togo; Le Hérissé, L'ancien royaume du Dahomey; Maupoil, La géomancie à l'ancienne Côte des Esclaves; Herskovits, Dahomey: An Ancient West African Kingdom" .

pmw:DeOccultaPhilosophia a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Agrippa, De occulta philosophia libri tres"@de,
        "Agrippa, De occulta philosophia libri tres"@en ;
    skos:note "The three books, first printed complete in 1533 and read here in the English translation of 1651. Opened at nine places out of some two hundred chapters; the rest is unopened. The Latin print of 1533 was located in three digitised copies whose Latin body is legible at the sentence and was not read at those nine places; that is a debt and not an unreachability. The critical edition of 1992 is access-restricted, and until somebody has it the citations of this file name book and chapter of the English print without the edition's page."@en,
        "The volume of 1651 contains more than the three books, and two of the additions are cited here: the author's address to the reader, and the piece headed as his censure or retraction, printed after book III together with two letters. The fourth book that circulates under the author's name is not part of this volume; it is first printed in 1559, twenty-four years after his death, and nothing of it is entered here."@en .

pmw:Nihonshoki a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Nihon shoki (Nihongi), die Chronik von 720"@de,
        "Nihon shoki (Nihongi), the chronicle of 720"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened on 2026-09-18 in the English of W. G. Aston, volume I of 1896, covering Books I to XVI. Volume II, Books XVII to XXX, was not obtained in this run; that is a statement about the run and not about the work."@en .

pmw:PrognosticonHippocratis a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Hippokrates, Prognostikon"@de,
        "Hippocrates, Prognostics"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened on 2026-10-03, chapters 1 to 25, read continuously in the English of Francis Adams, vol. I, London 1849, pp. 234-256, with the Greek alongside from the Loeb volume of 1923. Not read: the Argument Adams sets before it, pp. 223-233, and the Appendix he prints after it, pp. 257-270, which he himself holds to be spurious. The question this work was named for is answered and the answer is in the other work of this run: the term for a wound is in On Injuries of the Head and not here, and what rides on a number here is the course of a disease."@en,
        "The cheapest continuation from this work is its own appendix and the treatise that stands before it in the same volume. On Regimen in Acute Diseases, pp. 271-336, is where this corpus describes evacuating, bathing and the conditions of the patient under which a measure is or is not to be applied, and the holding has an open question that goes exactly there: the catalogue of states in which an operation on a vessel is forbidden, searched for in these two treatises and not found, at pmp:HippocraticVulnusStatusSearch."@en .

pmw:RomanusBuechlein a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Romanus-Büchlein"@de,
        "Romanus-Büchlein"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened on 2026-09-22 and read at the wording throughout, in the reprint in Johann Scheible (ed.), Das Kloster, weltlich und geistlich, vol. III (Stuttgart 1846), pp. 489-523, which presents itself in a footnote as a verbatim reprint of the booklet once offered for sale at fairs and carries the fictitious imprint Printed at Venice. The whole of those thirty-five pages was read; the printed page numbers survive in the optical transcription and the citations here are made from them."@en,
        "What is known about the booklet's own printing history is not known from a copy. The earliest dated printing is given as Glatz 1788, from the printer named on its title page, and that date reaches this holding through a modern digital edition which takes it from the philological commentary of 1958; neither the printing of 1788 nor that commentary was seen here. The witness that was read is fifty-eight years later than that date and twenty-six years later than Hohman. Anything this file says about what stood in print before 1820 therefore rests on a dating nobody here has checked, and the three nodes that used to name the printing of 1788 as their source now name the reprint that was actually read."@en,
        "Why the distinction is worth this much space. The holding asserts at pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicBuechleinSchutz that the sentence guaranteeing the bearer of the booklet was in print in Silesia thirty-two years before Hohman reprinted it, and that assertion is the sharpest thing this file says about how the transmission travelled. It stands or falls with the date of 1788, and the date is the one element of the claim that was taken on trust. The passage itself is now confirmed against a witness independent of the edition that supplied it."@en .

pm:Scale a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Skala"@de,
        "Scale"@en ;
    skos:definition "A class whose instances form a closed set of permitted values. A property whose range is a scale takes one of that scale's declared instances and nothing else."@en ;
    skos:note "Closure is the point. rdfs:range states an intention and constrains nothing; until pm:ScaleValueShape was written, a misspelt or invented value passed validation in silence and was indistinguishable from a declared one."@en .

pm:withdrawnAbsorption a pm:AwarenessValue,
        pm:SensoryAnchoring ;
    rdfs:label "Abgezogene Vertiefung"@de,
        "Withdrawn absorption"@en ;
    skos:definition "Awake and not asleep, with the senses withdrawn from their objects and attention held on a single one. The condition the handbooks of concentration describe and require."@en ;
    skos:note "Set this where the source binds the act to a named degree of absorption, to a suspension of the breath, or to a prayer of the heart held past the point at which the outward senses report. This value has no counterpart in the fourfold list, which is the main reason that list could not be used."@en .

pmp:TurbaRegimenUniusRei a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Führung der einen Materie durch Schwarz, Weiß und Rot"@de,
        "The ruling of the one matter through black, white and red"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 53 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)",
        "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 70 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)",
        "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 9 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    skos:definition "The single operation the work prescribes under many names. The operator takes what the work calls our copper together with the permanent water, closes the vessel, and cooks, imbues, washes and grinds by turns over a fire that must be gentle at first and stronger later, until the matter turns black, then white, then red. The work insists that the many names — salting, sublimating, washing, pounding, whitening, turning into rust, the confection of Ethel — all name this one operation, that nothing foreign is added to it at any point, and that a single decoction and a single grinding would suffice if the operator knew it."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as one practice and not as a series, because the work says so itself at Dictum 53 and again at Dictum 70: there are many names and one regimen, the master began with one and finished one. A holding that entered a node for each named operation would be reporting the vocabulary of the concealment rather than the procedure."@en,
        "The circularity SPEC §11 forbids is avoided here by what the practice is said to produce: not our copper, which is the material it starts from, but a tincture that colours a body it is imposed on. What the operation makes is claimed to be different in kind from what it works with, and that is what makes the claim enterable at all."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LatinAlchemy .

pmt:Bwiti a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Bwiti"@de,
        "Bwiti"@en ;
    dcterms:source "H. Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan (Münster i. W.: Aschendorffsche Verlagsbuchhandlung, 1912), pp. 49-52" ;
    skos:altLabel "Bouiti"@de,
        "Bouiti"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Gabonese transmission of an initiation carried by a root preparation, in which the initiate is expected to meet the dead and to report the meeting. In the earliest reachable account the report is made into a mirror set in the belly of the fetish and is the criterion of admission: a candidate who does not say what is required is told that the fetish refused him, and the whole procedure begins again."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at places read out of pm:corpusNamed, where it stood from its registration. The registration named Fernandez 1982 as principal corpus and that work is still not opened; what was read instead is a public-domain monograph of 1912 which prints a mission article of 1908-1909 in quotation, a monograph of 1913 on the same people, and a missionary's enumeration of 1904. All three recorders are outsiders, two of them resident missionaries, and the registry file this instance leaves says in its own header that this is the condition most of its entries are in."@en,
        "One: the contact route, and it is undecided rather than absent. The registration entered absent, and nothing had been looked for. Against a route: the vernacular terms of the procedure are not Christian, the plant is local, the society is reported under three distinct names in three languages by three recorders who do not cite each other, and nothing in the read passages has a European antecedent. For a route: the only account of the initiation is by a Catholic missionary resident at Sette Cama, written for a religious review, and the mediator says so; the fetish house is described in the vocabulary of a church, place of honour and all; and the mirror the vision is read in is a trade mirror from the coast, which the Fang account says has everywhere replaced the mica sheets used formerly, so that the instrument of the central act is an import datable to the trade. Absent would be false and documented would overstate. Until the question stands as its own node this tradition may not be counted as a party to a convergence."@en,
        "The lease that holds this work is the branch claude/ernte-trilles-totemisme-fan, and the harvest note stands in the body of its pull request. No issue holds it, as AGENTS.md requires of an opened work."@en,
        "Three: the number of citable places. Seven, carrying sixteen nodes: Trilles pages 47, 49, 51, 271 and 355, Tessmann volume II pages 241 and 242, and Nassau page 248, all read at the wording in this run. What was left unread is recorded at each work."@en,
        "Two: recension and edition. Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan, Münster i. W., Aschendorffsche Verlagsbuchhandlung, 1912, from the title page in the full text. The Bouiti section is Le Scao's French, printed by Trilles with his own footnotes and his own bracketed additions, and the vernacular wording is not carried beside it; the Fang material of pages 271 and 355 is Trilles' own. No original-language text of any part of this transmission was read in this run, and none is printed in the works that were."@en,
        "Whether this is one transmission or two is not decided here and the decision was not this run's to take. Trilles asserts the identity - comparons le Biéri fân et le Bwiti fiot, on en verra l'identité - and derives it from a sound correspondence of r and t in Fang and from a common origin; the Fang material and the Fiote material therefore stand under one tradition in this file, following the opened work. Against the identity stands the thing the same run found: the Fang monograph of 1913 names neither Bouiti nor the root, so if the two are one transmission, the Fang branch of it is invisible in the fullest early account of the Fang. The question is the first one for whoever opens Fernandez."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteUndecided ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:AuPaysDeSetteCamaLeBouiti,
        pmw:BwitiEthnographyOfTheReligiousImagination,
        pmw:FetichismInWestAfrica,
        pmw:LeTotemismeChezLesFan ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LeTotemismeChezLesFan ;
    pm:principalCorpus "James W. Fernandez, Bwiti: An Ethnography of the Religious Imagination in Africa (Princeton: Princeton University Press, 1982); H. Trilles, Le Totémisme chez les Fan (Münster i. W.: Aschendorffsche Verlagsbuchhandlung, 1912), which prints Le Scao, Au pays de Sette Cama: les fétiches, le Bouiti, Messager du St Esprit, 1908-1909; and Robert Hamill Nassau, Fetichism in West Africa (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1904)" .

pmt:FinnicIncantation a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Finnische Zaubersprüche"@de,
        "Finnic incantation"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John Abercromby, The Pre- and Proto-historic Finns, vol. II: The Magic Songs of the West Finns (London: David Nutt, 1898), § 210 a, p. 332, translating Elias Lönnrot (ed.), Suomen Kansan Muinaisia Loitsurunoja (Helsinki: SKS, 1880)" ;
    skos:definition "The Finnish and Karelian transmission of the tietäjä, in which power over a thing follows from knowing and reciting its origin."@en ;
    skos:note "Kalevala is a nineteenth-century compilation and belongs in the record as an ordering act, not as the corpus. The corpus is the collected incantations."@en,
        "Read in one work and at the wording of its English: John Abercromby, The Pre- and Proto-historic Finns, vol. II, The Magic Songs of the West Finns (London: David Nutt, 1898), which translates 639 pieces of Elias Lönnrot's collection of 1880. Read in full: the editor's account of the eighteen classes of formula and of the order of recitation, pp. 2-6; § 3 Against Envy, pp. 70-72; § 5 To Discover the Cause, pp. 73-75; § 12 Falling into Ecstasy, pp. 83-84; § 16 Menacing, pp. 92-94; § 17 Exorcisms at a to h, pp. 94-99; § 210 a, p. 332. The whole volume searched by term for signs of success and for stated terms, and every hit read in its place."@en,
        "The contact route stays disputed and the reading moved it only by adding the translator's own judgement, which is recorded as his and not entered as a claim. He states that the origin or genealogy passage occurs about a hundred and fifty times in the songs and is also found in Estonian charms, which closely resemble the Finnish, but not elsewhere so far as he knows — and that a different structural element, the narrative precedent, is probably borrowed from the Latvians. That is a scholar comparing corpora in 1898 and belongs at a protocol or in an issue, not in a node of this file."@en,
        "The edition read is a translation of a compilation, and the difficulty is in the compilation and not in the translation. Lönnrot gives no place of recording and contaminates as many as twenty variants into one text, which the translator states in his own opening pages. A node in this file therefore names a formula type and not an aufzeichnung: it says that this is how the type runs in the standard collection, not that any one singer sang it so. The corpus that would carry the second claim is Suomen Kansan Vanhat Runot, which prints the recordings singly with their place, collector and date, and it was not opened in this run because no public-domain English translation of it exists."@en,
        "What was not read, and it is the larger part. Of the volume's 233 numbered sections, seven were read; the origins themselves, §§ 183-233, were read only at § 210. Volume I was not opened. Neither Suomen Kansan Vanhat Runot nor Lönnrot's own edition was opened. The Kalevala was deliberately not used, for the reason the registration gives: it is an ordering act and not the corpus."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Suomen Kansan Vanhat Runot; the loitsurunot collections" .

pmt:Mithraic a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Mithras-Mysterien"@de,
        "Mysteries of Mithras"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.22 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV)",
        "Porphyry, De antro nympharum (trans. Thomas Taylor, London 1917)" ;
    skos:definition "The initiatory cult of the Roman empire, organised in graded degrees and practised in artificial caves, whose own transmission is almost entirely monumental and epigraphic rather than literary."@en ;
    skos:note "The lopsidedness of the corpus is the first thing to say about it. This tradition left several hundred sanctuaries, reliefs and inscriptions and no book of its own; every connected account of what it taught comes from someone outside it, and mostly from someone attacking it. That is the opposite of every other tradition in this graph's ascent material, where a text survives and the material remains do not, and it is the reason the tradition can matter here as a witness independent of the heresiological channel — once the material rather than the reports is opened."@en,
        "The name Mithras appears in this graph twice over, and the two occurrences are not the same thing. Here it is the cult. In traditions/greek-magical-papyri.ttl it is a modern title given in 1903 to PGM IV.475-829, and whether that piece is Mithraic at all is disputed in the scholarship. Nothing in this file rests on that identification."@en,
        "What was read: Origen, Contra Celsum VI.22, in the Ante-Nicene Fathers translation with the Greek of the disputed words in its notes; and Porphyry, De antro nympharum, complete, in Thomas Taylor's translation (London 1917). Neither is a Mithraic text. The first is a report about Mithraists inside a Christian refutation; the second is a Platonist reading Homer, who reaches for the cult four times because it suits his argument about the descent of souls."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The monuments and inscriptions of the cult in their standard corpus; the literary testimonia, among them Origen, Contra Celsum VI.22 (reporting Celsus), Porphyry, De antro nympharum, Tertullian, De corona 15, Jerome, Epistula 107.2, and Firmicus Maternus, De errore profanarum religionum 5" .

pmt:Swedenborgian a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Swedenborgische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Swedenborgian transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Swedenborg, De caelo et inferno, n. 249 (Heaven and Hell, trans. Swedenborg Society, London 1885, pp. 114-115)" ;
    skos:definition "A transmission resting on one man's sustained reports of conversation with the dead and with angels, kept as a dated journal over decades and published as doctrine."@en ;
    skos:note "Quantitatively the largest body of first-person reports available to the project, and dated throughout, which makes internal consistency checkable in a way most such material is not. The registration said so and the reading bears it out: two of the contradictions entered below were found by reading two of this author's own works against each other."@en,
        "The contact route runs out of this corpus and not into it. Swedenborg published from 1749 and died in 1772; the magnetic and spiritist literatures this holding already carries are later and knew him. An agreement between this file and either of those is reception with this corpus as the earlier station, and no pm:independentAttestation is claimed anywhere here."@en,
        "The corpus carries no operation, and that is a property of it and not of the reading. It has capacities, prerequisites, cautions, a criterion for telling a deceiving spirit and a statement that the state cannot be known in life, and it has no instruction by which anybody could set about the thing. The search stands at pmp:SwedenborgPraxisSearch."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:ArcanaCoelestia,
        pmw:DeCaeloEtInferno,
        pmw:DiariumSpirituale,
        pmw:DivinoAmoreEtSapientia ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeCaeloEtInferno ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Swedenborg, De caelo et inferno; Swedenborg, Diarium spirituale; Swedenborg, Arcana coelestia" .

pmt:Taulipang a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Taulipang-Ueberlieferung"@de,
        "Taulipang transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Theodor Koch-Gruenberg, Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, Band III: Ethnographie (Stuttgart: Strecker und Schroeder, 1923), pp. 203-209" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the Taulipang, a Carib-speaking people of the Roroima region on the border of northern Brazil and Venezuela, in which a Zauberarzt is made over ten to twenty-five years by a graded course of emetics, fasting and tobacco, receives helping spirits that arrive as rock crystals, sends his shadow up a cord or a liana while his body stays on the ground, and may be permanently unmade by two named foods."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at places read and not registered first, because the passages were opened in the same run in which the tradition was decided on. The three decisions SPEC section 14 requires before the first node were made and are the three notes that follow."@en,
        "One: the contact route, and it is undecided rather than absent, on evidence in the work itself. Against a route: the corpus is Carib, the region is the Guiana shield, the account of the training comes from two named Taulipang and carries no Christian or European element anywhere in it, and the recording is of 1911 to 1913. For a route: the same volume places a Benedictine mission on the upper Surumu with a Taulipang chief living at it and the padres' horses and cattle in the story, and the recorder's own comparative apparatus is built from nineteenth-century missionaries in the neighbouring country - Brett in British Guiana, Quandt the Moravian in Surinam, Schomburgk, Barrere, Rochefort. So contact with Christian missions is documented for the period of fixation, while no route to any magical corpus in this holding is shown. Absent would be false and documented would overstate; undecided is the honest value and it is the same correction the Azande, Nahua and Maori entries received. Until the question stands as its own node this tradition may not be counted as a party to a convergence."@en,
        "The lease that holds this work is prima-materia#838, and it was opened after the reading rather than before it: this run chose its work from the registration state and the witness position rather than from a standing lease. The harvest note stands at that issue."@en,
        "Three: the number of citable places. Fourteen, from pages 194 to 221, all read at the wording in this run. The volume runs to some four hundred pages, and the chapters on the neighbouring Schiriana, Waika and Yekuana - the last with a Zauberarzt chapter of its own at pages 380 to 388 - are not opened. Grown in places and not in names, as traditions/daoist.ttl is the model for."@en,
        "Two: recension and edition. Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, volume III, Stuttgart, Strecker und Schroeder, 1923, from the title page in the full text and not from the catalogue line, which is wrong. The account of the training is the recorder's German rendering of what Akuli told him orally, and the recorder says at the head of it that he gives it exactly as it was told to him; the original wording is not printed beside it. The spells are the one part of this corpus where the original is printed, and they were not read in this run."@en,
        "Why this transmission and not another. It was chosen under the testability-driven entrance, which SPEC section 14 ranks first: it names two conditions under which a capacity is destroyed, each tied to a named substance and a named mechanism, and that is the material a pm:falsifiedBy can be formed from. The independence-driven entrance is the second reason and the weaker one, because the route is undecided."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteUndecided ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:VomRoroimaZumOrinocoIII,
        pmw:ZaubersprucheDerTaulipangIndianer ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VomRoroimaZumOrinocoIII ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Theodor Koch-Gruenberg, Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, Band III: Ethnographie (Stuttgart: Strecker und Schroeder, 1923); and the same author's Zaubersprueche der Taulipang-Indianer, Archiv fuer Anthropologie, Neue Folge XIII.4 (Braunschweig, 1915)" .

pmw:DeVulneribusCapitis a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Hippokrates, Über die Kopfwunden"@de,
        "Hippocrates, On Wounds in the Head"@en ;
    skos:note "Littre's third volume, BIUSante_34859x03, was fetched and measured: it carries 200955 characters in U+0370-U+03FF and 40953 in U+1F00-U+1FFF, so the Greek is present as Greek there. Whether it is accurate was NOT established in this run — the research that preceded the run reports it as systematically misread, with rho for delta and kappa for chi, and no Greek word of this treatise was compared against a second witness here. Anyone needing the wording of a term goes to the critical edition, and the first thing to do on that route is to check that report."@en,
        "Opened on 2026-10-03, chapters 1 to 21, read continuously in the English of Francis Adams, vol. I, London 1849, pp. 445-466. It was named as the strongest candidate in this corpus for a stated interval after an injury and it carries one; the finding changed shape in the reading rather than collapsing, and what it is now stands at pmc:HippocratesVulnusCapitisTempusAnni. Not read: Adams's Argument before it, pp. 423-444, and the three plates of instruments at the end of the volume."@en,
        "The Greek is not on this route for this work, and the failure is the dangerous kind. The Loeb volume hippocrates03hippuoft prints the treatise with its Greek, and its archive.org full text carries zero characters in U+0370-U+03FF and U+1F00-U+1FFF: the OCR has rendered every Greek word in lookalike Latin letters, so the file looks clean and the original language is absent. Measured in this run, and the title of the treatise in that file reads EPI TQN EN KE and a box and AAHI TPOMATON."@en .

pmw:LiberOVelManusEtSagittae a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Liber O vel Manus et Sagittae sub figura VI"@de,
        "Liber O vel Manus et Sagittae sub figura VI"@en ;
    skos:note "Class B. The syllabus says of it that its instructions are too loose to find place among the official rituals, which is the transmission's own grading of the text this file leans on most."@en .

pmw:YogavacarasManual a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Yogāvacara's Manual"@de,
        "Yogāvacara's Manual"@en ;
    skos:note "Dating: no colophon date and no author. The editor of 1916 states in as many words that there is no certain evidence of either date or authorship, the final colophon consisting of pious aspirations still in vogue. The Sinhala diction was judged fairly modern and possibly of the seventeenth or even sixteenth century, and the same judge withdrew the early date on the ground that samādhi and jhāna practice had by then died out in Ceylon, proposing instead the reviver Saraṇaṅkara or a pupil of his in the eighteenth century. Jayatilaka's appendix supports the later date with the revival's history and the Siamese ordination mission that returned in 1753. This file uses the eighteenth century and says that it is an editors' inference."@en,
        "Read here complete in F. L. Woodward, Manual of a Mystic, being a translation from the Pali and Sinhalese work entitled The Yogāvachara's Manual, edited with an introductory essay by Mrs. Rhys Davids, London: Pali Text Society Translation Series VI, 1916, from the text T. W. Rhys Davids edited in 1896. Page references in this file are to the translation of 1916, which gives the manuscript pages in brackets."@en,
        "The bilingual layout is a fact about which claims are citable. Larger type from page 3 renders Pali, smaller type from page 8 renders Sinhala, and the preamble is Sinhala but for its first paragraph. The colour triads, which are what this transmission is chiefly known for, stand in the Sinhala descriptive paragraphs, which the translator states outright he was not equipped to render alone and for which he had the help of a Sinhala pandit at his college. That is a fourth layer and it is named here rather than left for a later reader to find."@en,
        "The textual base is a single copy of a single manuscript and the editors say so at length. The palm-leaf original is at the Bambara-galla Wihāra, Teldeniya, near Kandy; its existence was ascertained in 1892, a copy was made by a scribe engaged for the purpose, and that copy — not the original — reached Europe in 1893 and was returned afterwards. Rhys Davids found it unsatisfactory as a copy and unsatisfactory in its source, the copy showing hasty and inaccurate work and the source suggesting an earlier scribe conversant with Sinhala but unfamiliar with Pali. No second manuscript was obtainable. Every claim in this file therefore rests on one witness at two removes, and nothing here turns on a single word."@en,
        "The title is the editor's and not the work's, and that matters for anybody tracing citations. The manuscript bears no title at either end; Rhys Davids named it after the person addressed in it and added the words of Indian Mysticism, of which his co-editor writes in 1916 that mysticism occurs not at all in the work and that there is no Pali equivalent. In Sinhala terms the work would be called a vidarsana-pota."@en .

pmt:InuitAngakkuq a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Inuitische Angakkuq-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Inuit angakkuq transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), pp. 19-32" ;
    skos:definition "The Inuit transmission of an office whose holder is said to perceive what is hidden, to call up helping spirits and to act through them, acquired through solitude and privation and exercised for a household rather than for an institution."@en ;
    skos:note "Contact route, stated rather than assumed. No route is proposed by anyone between the Iglulik Inuit and the literate transmissions this graph already holds, and none is available for the doctrinal content. The corpus was nevertheless fixed in nineteen twenty-one and twenty-two, well after contact: the narrative itself names whalers, and the region was under mission influence at the time of the recording. The value here is therefore contact-route-disputed and not contact-route-absent, following the correction made for the Māori entry, and the tradition may not be counted as a party to a convergence until the dispute stands as its own node. See prima-materia#101 and prima-materia#102."@en,
        "Read in the opening of the volume: the account of the working method, the first meeting near Repulse Bay, the old singer Ivaluardjuk, and the autobiography of Takornâq, printed pages ten to thirty-two. The chapters on the shamans, on the spirits and on the taboo system, which carry the terms this tradition is usually reported by, were not reached in this run. The limit is one of the access path and not of the work: the volume is digitised and public, and the portion delivered by the tool in use broke off at printed page thirty-two. See prima-materia#99 and prima-materia#100."@en,
        "The material read is testimony of an unusual kind for this graph. It is not a doctrinal corpus but a life story told in the first person by a woman who is herself said to have practised, recorded by an ethnographer who spoke a related language and worked without an interpreter. What that buys is a speaker attached to each claim; what it costs is that almost every capacity is ascribed by the narrator to somebody else, which is why the nodes below stand on third-party ascription and not on first-person report."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos; Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Caribou Eskimos" .

pmt:PatanjalaYoga a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Pātañjala-Yoga"@de,
        "Pātañjala Yoga"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the Yogasūtra of Patañjali and its commentarial literature."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. Only Yogasūtra III.38 carries the edition it was read from; it was reopened in the Sanskrit for that purpose. For every other citation in this file, and for the Yogabhāṣya, the edition or translation the passage was read from is not recoverable: the session that entered them did not record it, and it cannot be reconstructed from the wording of the nodes. It is stated here rather than guessed, because an edition entered by inference would be a claim about the compiler's work that is not true. Until the passages are reopened, the verse numbers in this file stand on the received four-pāda division and nothing further."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Patañjali, Yogasūtra" .

pmt:SamiNoaidi a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Samische Noaidi-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Sámi noaidi transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, Of the magicall Ceremonies of the Laplanders, pp. 45-47" ;
    skos:definition "The Sámi transmission of a drum read by the travel of a ring across painted figures, of an office entered through a threefold seizure, and of a journey to a distant place undertaken while the body lies as if dead and from which a token is brought back."@en ;
    skos:note "Read in this run, both at the wording: the compilation of sixteen seventy-four in its Oxford English edition, chapter VIII on the religion and chapter XI on the magical ceremonies; and the account of Danish Lapland of seventeen sixty-seven in the English translation printed in a collection of eighteen hundred and eight, its chapter on the magic arts. Left unread: the remaining thirty-three chapters of the first and the remaining twenty-two of the second, and the treatise on the pagan religion of the Finns and Lapps printed with the second."@en,
        "The contact route is entered as documented and no longer as undecided, and the ground is inside the practice rather than around it. By seventeen sixty-seven a named practitioner marked himself and his drum with the sign of the cross, said the Lord's Prayer over it and invoked God the Father, the Son and an angel before striking it. That is a route to the Christian material this graph already holds, visible in the operation itself and not inferred from proximity. It says nothing about the route to the Siberian material or to the Norse, which is the question the registry entry meant and which is still not carried out; the predicate is single-valued and cannot hold both answers, so it holds the stronger. See prima-materia#101."@en,
        "The recorder of seventeen sixty-seven applies source criticism to his own material, and it is entered here because it is rare and because it bears on how the rest of this file is to be read. Of the confessions of witchcraft in the Norwegian trials he says that it is probable they were extorted, partly by violence and fear, and that it appears from the law books that some were put to the torture before they would confess. He then separates that material from what he goes on to report of the Sámi themselves. The separation is his and it is the reason the trial material is not in this file."@en,
        "The two are of different kinds and that is why both were opened. The first was written by a professor at Uppsala who never went to Lapland, on the commission of the Chancellor of Sweden, out of the written reports of parish priests whom he names in the text — so what it carries is at one remove and its author says so. The second was written by a missionary who lived among the people he describes and who reports named practitioners in their own words. The first is a compilation with a stated method; the second is first-hand and confessional. Where the two agree, the agreement is worth something; where the first stands alone, it stands on reports it names but this graph has not seen."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Schefferus, Lapponia, in the English edition The History of Lapland; Leem, Beskrivelse over Finmarkens Lapper; the eighteenth-century missionary manuscripts of Skanke and Randulf; Jessen, De religione paganorum Fennorum et Lapponum" .

pmt:Waidan a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Waidan, äußere Alchemie"@de,
        "Waidan external alchemy"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Chinese transmission of elixirs compounded from mineral substances, with an explicit catalogue of the capacities each elixir is said to confer."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition note. Read in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, in the same digitisation as the chapters held in traditions/daoist.ttl, so that the two files cite one text and any divergence between them is a divergence in the work and not in the copy."@en,
        "Ge Hong's inner chapters list attainments in the same enumerated form as the siddhi lists and were compiled without access to them. The comparison is one of the sharpest tests the project can set itself."@en,
        "Read at the wording on 2026-09-10, in the Chinese, for the examination and not for the transmission: Han Yu's epitaph for the late Erudite of the Grand Academy Li, and the poem to the supernumerary of Suizhou with the commentary printed with it, both in the Wubaijia zhu Changli wenji in the Wenyuange Siku quanshu text. What they carry stands in examinations/elixir-deaths.ttl. The rest of that collection was searched by term and not read."@en,
        "Read at the wording on 2026-09-10, in the Chinese: Baopuzi neipian 4, on the golden elixir, whole. The remaining chapters bearing on this transmission, above all 16 on gold and silver and 11 on the drugs of immortality, are searched by term and not read; the Zhouyi cantong qi is not opened. The harvest still owed at the opened work is recorded at prima-materia#633."@en,
        "The finding of the opening run, and it is one sentence set against another in the same chapter. The author states that he received the elixir scriptures more than twenty years ago and has never compounded them, having not so much as a picul of means, and that all he has is a long sigh. Thirteen leaves later he prices the operation: about four hundred thousand cash for one preparation, enough for eight men. The chapter that calls the golden elixir the utmost of the way therefore rests, in its own author's hands, on an operation he could not afford to perform. Nothing in the file below is reported by a man who carried it out."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian; Zhouyi cantong qi" .

pmw:ClaviculaSalomonis a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Clavicula Salomonis"@de,
        "Clavicula Salomonis"@en .

pmw:KarstenBloodRevenge a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Karsten, Blood Revenge, War, and Victory Feasts among the Jibaro Indians"@de,
        "Karsten, Blood Revenge, War, and Victory Feasts among the Jibaro Indians"@en ;
    skos:note "A United States government publication, archive.org bloodrevengewarv00kars, 318391 characters of full text, running heads unbroken. The Jibaro terms are carried in the recorder's transcription throughout and three chants are printed in the language."@en,
        "Opened on 2026-10-02 at the pages named in the nodes of this file. The remainder is reachable by the same route and the cheapest continuation: the chapters on the preparation of the head trophy and on the remaining days of the tsantsa feast carry the ceremonies whose failure the observances are said to guard against, and the concluding remarks at page 87 are where the recorder states what he thinks the feast is for."@en .

pmw:TalmudBavliSanhedrin a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Babylonischer Talmud, Traktat Synhedrin"@de,
        "Babylonian Talmud, tractate Synhedrin"@en ;
    skos:note "Read in the translation of Lazarus Goldschmidt, Der babylonische Talmud mit Einschluss der vollstaendigen Misnah, seventh volume, Synhedrin, Makkoth, Sebuoth, Aboda-zara, Horajoth, Edijoth, Aboth, Berlin, S. Calvary und Co., 1903. The title page states what the translation rests on: the first, censorship-free Bomberg edition, Venice 1520 to 1523, with the variants of the later revised editions and of the Munich Talmud manuscript. The edition is copied from that title page in the full text."@en,
        "Rodkinson's English of 1902 was fetched and compared and is not used. It is defective for exactly this subject: his own explanatory remarks state that where the original gives two statements separated by a formula of alternative he translates only the second, and at the place of the permission to learn the whole proof runs on that formula. The comparison stands in the search node in examinations/searches-rabbinic-sorcery-law.ttl."@en,
        "The page sequence of the part read was checked before the reading: the running heads run 285, 286, 287, 288, 289, 290, 291 without a gap, so a term not found in this part is a statement about the work and not about the scan."@en,
        "The volume prints the Aramaic and Hebrew beside the German and carries an apparatus with a printed key to its sigla: M is the Munich manuscript after Rabbinowicz's Variae lectiones, P the editio princeps, B the later revised editions, V readings of some editions. That key is what makes the variant at pmc:SanhedrinSorcererIsTheOneWhoDoesTheDeed readable at all, and it is the reason this volume was preferred to the public-domain English."@en .

pm:Converging a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Konvergieren"@de,
        "Converging"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "A structural correspondence observed across traditions, held as a node so that it can carry its routes of transmission and its counterexamples instead of asserting universality."@en ;
    skos:note "Every instance is an ordering made by a compiler and carries pm:compilerInference, never textual attestation. No source says that the concepts correspond; the correspondence is the reading, and it is held as a node so that it can be argued with. Its sources are the sources of the concepts it brings together, and they attest those concepts and not their correspondence."@en,
        "Independence is also a weaker instrument than it looks, and this is the reason breadth is held beside it rather than under it. It guards against none of the three commonest sources of agreement: a shared physiology that produces the same experience wherever the same thing is done to a body, a structural necessity of the subject matter that makes any competent version converge, and the form of graded institutions, which resemble one another because institutions with grades do. What decides in the end is what can be reproduced, and independence is a way of sorting candidates for that, not a verdict."@en,
        "Most apparent universality in the Western esoteric record is diffusion from few sources. A graph that counts a citation chain as independent confirmation measures reception history and reports it as structure."@en,
        "That is a warning against one way of counting and not against reception itself. Reception history is a finding of its own: how many transmissions carry a claim at all, and how deeply the paths between them branch, are two separate numbers and neither cancels the other. Many witnesses on one cable is a different map from many witnesses on three, and both are more informative than the earlier arrangement, in which the first case was simply not shown. The one thing the graph must be able to do is say which of the two maps a reader is looking at: dense transmission and strong effect must never look alike, or the gate of authority returns in statistical dress."@en .

pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicGebrauchen a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Für jemanden aus diesem Buch gebrauchen"@de,
        "Using out of this book for a sufferer"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Abschnitt »Zeugniße, welche zu einiger Zeit beweisen können, daß ich, Hohman, für sie aus diesem Buch gebraucht habe«",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), pp. 5-8" ;
    skos:definition "The act the testimonials report without naming the prescription used: the author, or in two cases his wife, applies to a named sufferer what the book contains, and an outcome follows within a stated interval. The book calls this brauchen, using, and holds it apart from the physician's craft throughout the preface."@en ;
    skos:note "Deliberately unspecific, because the section is. Only one of the fifteen cases names the means, the twenty-five letters sold with a book to Jacob Staufer; the other fourteen say only that the author used out of this book. A node that guessed the prescription would report the compiler's reading as the transmission's claim."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HohmanFreund1820,
        pmw:HohmanLongLostFriend1850 ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmw:DighaNikaya a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Dīgha Nikāya"@de,
        "Dīgha Nikāya"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened at suttas 1, 2 and 11. For sutta 1 the edition is recoverable, because the Mahāsīla was read at the wording in this run: T. W. Rhys Davids, Dialogues of the Buddha, Part I, Sacred Books of the Buddhists vol. II, London 1899, pp. 16-26. For suttas 2 and 11 the edition is not recoverable and prima-materia#45 records that."@en .

pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (1937)"@de,
        "Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (1937)"@en ;
    skos:note "Not a work of this corpus and deliberately not listed under pm:corpusWork. It is the field record: the first fixation in writing of rules the recorder was told by priests and of proceedings she attended in Iraq between the nineteen-twenties and 1937. It is named here because the nodes that rest on it must be able to say which work their source lies in, and because a reader who sees the tariff below should be able to tell at a glance that it comes from this book and not from the scripture."@en .

pmw:LiberEVelExercitiorum a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Liber E vel Exercitiorum sub figura IX"@de,
        "Liber E vel Exercitiorum sub figura IX"@en ;
    skos:note "Class B by the syllabus of the transmission, that is, no claim of textual invariance. Sections and roman chapter numbers are carried by the Equinox print and are absent from the reprint in the appendix of Magick in Theory and Practice, which makes the Equinox print the only carrier with a usable internal address."@en .

pmt:Bon a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Bön"@de,
        "Bön"@en ;
    dcterms:source "gZi brjid, the Way of the Shen of Prediction (phya-gshen theg-pa), in D. L. Snellgrove, The Nine Ways of Bon: Excerpts from gZi-brjid, London Oriental Series 18, London: Oxford University Press, 1967" ;
    skos:definition "The Tibetan transmission that claims descent from Zhangzhung rather than from India, with its own dzogchen cycles and its own founder."@en ;
    skos:note "Read in this run at the wording: the gZi brjid in the Tibetan text and facing English translation of D. L. Snellgrove, 1967. Read closely and in full: the first of the Nine Ways, the Way of the Shen of Prediction, with all four of its parts. Read in part, by a search for conditions of failure carried over the whole volume and then read at every hit: the Way of the Shen of the Visual World, the Way of the Shen of Existence and the Way of the Primeval Shen. Not read: the Way of the Shen of Illusion, the Way of the Virtuous Adherers, the Way of the Great Ascetics, the Way of Pure Sound and the Supreme Way, except where the search fell in them. Not opened at all: the Zhang Zhung Nyan Gyud, which its registration names first."@en,
        "The contact route stays at disputed and this run did not settle it, but the reading made the question concrete rather than abstract. The excerpts are saturated with Buddhist vocabulary used without apology: the officiant is to raise his Thought towards Enlightenment before every operation, the hosts of buddhas are the refuge of the physician, the god of medicine is Vaidūrya with his eight fellows, the diseases are the molestations that Sanskrit calls kleśa, and the ways are counted as vehicles. What the run did not establish is whether the procedures travelled with the vocabulary or the vocabulary was laid over procedures already there, and no node here claims either."@en,
        "The independence claim is the point. If it holds, Bön is a witness beside Tibetan Buddhism rather than a variant of it; if it fails, the failure is one of the better documented cases of a tradition constructing its own antiquity. Either outcome is a finding."@en,
        "What kind of witness this work is. The gZi brjid is the long biography of Shenrab and is held by Bön tradition to have been dictated in the fourteenth century; Snellgrove took his excerpts from a manuscript collected at Samling in Dolpo in 1961 and prints the Tibetan with the scribe's errors marked. A transmission whose doctrinal summary is a revealed biography is not a practitioner's manual, and the difference shows: the four parts of the first Way are described in the order in which they are taught, with their items and their conditions, and never once with a case in which somebody did them."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:GziBrjid,
        pmw:ZhangZhungNyanGyud ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GziBrjid ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Zhang Zhung Nyan Gyud; the Bön dzogchen cycles in the published Bön canon; gZi brjid" .

pmt:LatinMysticism a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Lateinische Mystik"@de,
        "Latin mystical transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Juan de la Cruz, Subida del Monte Carmelo II.XI (Obras del mistico doctor San Juan de la Cruz, ed. Gerardo de San Juan de la Cruz, Toledo 1912, pp. 149-152; II.13 in Lewis), read in the Spanish against the English of David Lewis rev. Zimmermann (London: Thomas Baker, 1906; fourth impression 1922) and the French of the Carmelites of Paris (Paris: Oudin, 1893)" ;
    skos:definition "The western Christian transmission of graded interior states, with criteria for telling one state from another and warnings against taking consolation for progress."@en ;
    skos:note "Four works of the registered corpus stand as pm:corpusWork without pm:fromWork and are therefore carried by queries/unopened-works.rq. That is accurate and not a defect of the run: the Noche oscura is the continuation of the work opened here and the Gerardo edition argues in its own introduction that it is the third and fourth parts of it, which is an editorial claim and not this holding's; Richard of St Victor, the Cloud of Unknowing and the German sermons of Eckhart were not opened."@en,
        "Registered with the note that Teresa's dwellings and John of the Cross's nights are the western material closest to a stage map with failure modes. Taken off pm:corpusNamed on 2026-10-08 at the Subida del Monte Carmelo, with one chapter of the Moradas opened for the counter-search. The contact route is unchanged at documented: this transmission is downstream of the discernment material this holding already carries, and nothing here is offered as an independent witness."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:BenjaminMaior,
        pmw:EckhartPredigten,
        pmw:MoradasDelCastilloInterior,
        pmw:NocheOscura,
        pmw:NubesIgnorantiae,
        pmw:SubidaDelMonteCarmelo ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SubidaDelMonteCarmelo ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Richard of St Victor, Benjamin Major; Meister Eckhart, German sermons; The Cloud of Unknowing; Teresa of Avila, Las Moradas; John of the Cross, Subida del Monte Carmelo and Noche oscura" .

pmw:SubidaDelMonteCarmelo a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Subida del Monte Carmelo"@de,
        "Subida del Monte Carmelo"@en ;
    skos:note "The work opened by this run, in the Gerardo de San Juan de la Cruz edition, Toledo 1912. It breaks off unfinished inside its third book, and the commentary on the last six stanzas that the author announces is lost; the Gerardo introduction argues further that the Noche oscura is the third and fourth parts of this same work. Both are properties of the work and are recorded here rather than at a node, because no node of this file depends on either."@en .

pm:Originating a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Urheben"@de,
        "Originating"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "The act of first putting forward a claim that no transmission carries, in a work that can be named and dated, where the claim afterwards circulates as though it were old."@en ;
    skos:note "Distinct from pm:Systematizing, which arranges material that was already there and names what it arranges. An origination arranges nothing: the claim begins with it. Holding the two apart is what keeps the record from reporting an invention as an ordering of something ancient, which is the commonest way a modern construction acquires a warrant."@en,
        "The circulating ascription is the point of the class, not an addendum to it. A node that names only the author and the work is a bibliography; a node that also names what the claim is taken for is the finding, and it is the kind of finding this project exists to make."@en .

pm:Systematizing a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Systematisieren"@de,
        "Systematizing"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "The act of imposing an order on material that did not carry it: a list, a sequence, a correspondence table. Named, dated, and attributed to the compiler who performed it, never to the source that is being ordered."@en .

pmp:NorseLjodatal a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Können und Singen der achtzehn Lieder"@de,
        "Knowing and singing the eighteen songs"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Henry Adams Bellows, The Poetic Edda (New York: American-Scandinavian Foundation, 1923), Hovamol 147-165",
        "Hávamál 146-163 (Guðni Jónsson; 147-165 in Bellows), in Eddukvæði. Sæmundar-Edda, the normalised Old Norse text of Guðni Jónsson" ;
    skos:definition "The operation the catalogue reports without giving its content: the speaker knows a song and, at need, sings, chants or carves. Three of the eighteen add a condition to the singing — that the flying shaft be seen with the eyes, that the runes be cut for the hanged man, that the young man be sprinkled with water — and the remaining fifteen state the knowing alone."@en ;
    skos:note "That the words are withheld is the transmission's own arrangement and not a loss in the manuscript. The poem says at the end of the catalogue that all is better which one alone knows, and it names an addressee, Loddfáfnir, who is told the effects and not the songs."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NorseSeidr .

pmt:MayaRitual a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Maya-Ritualüberlieferung"@de,
        "Maya ritual transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ralph L. Roys, The Book of Chilam Balam of Chumayel (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1967), chapter IX, pp. 89-98" ;
    skos:definition "The Maya transmission of creation, of the calendar as a moving structure of obligation, of divination by counting seeds, and of an examination in a figurative language by which the right to rule was said to be decided."@en ;
    skos:note "A witness of this corpus is therefore a witness for the period of its fixation and not for the period it describes. The strongest case is Landa: the autograph of 1566 is lost, what survives is an anonymous copy, and the thesis that the surviving text is a compilation of the seventeenth or eighteenth century out of Landa's papers is in the literature and not answered here. See prima-materia#461."@en,
        "Registered at corpus named until this file was written, and moved here on the first passages actually read. What was read at the wording: the Chumayel, chapter IX, in the Roys translation; Landa in the Gates translation, sections XXVII, XXXVII and XL; the Popol Vuh in the Goetz and Morley version of the Recinos translation, part I chapter 2 and part II chapters 7 to 9. What was not opened: the Ritual of the Bacabs, the Dresden Codex, the Tizimin and Mani books, and Landa section XXVI. The reasons stand in the issues named in the harvest note of prima-materia#458."@en,
        "The contact route is entered as disputed and no longer as absent, and that correction is the finding of this run rather than a formality. Absent asks whether a route was available in the period in which the corpus was fixed, and every work read here was fixed after 1524: Landa's account survives only in a later copy, the Popol Vuh only in the hand of a Dominican of 1701 to 1703, the Chumayel in a compilation signed at Chumayel in 1782. That the Chilam Balam books received European material is not a suspicion but a philological result, demonstrated for the almanac and zodiac material of the Kaua book against named Spanish printed sources. Against that stands an argument for continuity in the incantation genres, which was not decided here. See prima-materia#459 and prima-materia#460."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Popol Vuh in the Ximenez manuscript; the Books of Chilam Balam; the Dresden Codex; the Ritual of the Bacabs; Landa, Relacion de las cosas de Yucatan, as the account of an outside observer" .

pmt:UgariticRitual a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Ugaritisch-levantinisches Ritual"@de,
        "Ugaritic and Levantine ritual"@en ;
    dcterms:source "RS 24.266:26'-36' (KTU 1.119), the prayer appended to the sacrificial ritual, in D. Pardee, Ritual and Cult at Ugarit, Writings from the Ancient World 10, ed. T. J. Lewis, Atlanta: Society of Biblical Literature 2002, text 46, pp. 149-150" ;
    skos:definition "Late Bronze Age Levantine ritual and incantation, including the address to the deified dead."@en ;
    skos:note "The consequence of that for the counting, and it is the reason this note is at the instance and not in a comment. A convergence asserted between this tradition and the Mesopotamian or the Anatolian material may not be entered as pm:independentAttestation on the strength of distance, because the distance is not there. It needs a pm:independenceGround that addresses the named contact, and the strongest form such a ground could take here is not geography but reworking: a station that took the claim and added a term, a caution or a sign that was not in what it received. See traditions/hittite-ritual.ttl, where the Anatolian strand of the same run records the same limitation from the other side."@en,
        "What the editor of the open-access edition says about the dating, and it is unusually frank. The tablets are of the Late Bronze Age and the kingdom is documented from the middle of the fourteenth to the early twelfth century. But of the expiation rite he states that there are no indications in the texts themselves as to its frequency, nor, if annual, as to the moment in the year, and that comparison with the biblical day of atonement must wait until chronological data on the Ugaritic liturgy are forthcoming. The internal dating of the ritual tablets is therefore open on his own statement, and nothing in this file turns on it."@en,
        "What the reading added to the contact route, which stays disputed. This corpus states its own reach, and it states it twice. The expiation text names the parties whose statement may have caused the sin it expiates and the list is a tour of the Late Bronze Age: the Qaṭian, DDMY, the Hurrian, the Hittite, the Alashian, GBR, and then the kingdom's own oppressed and impoverished and one further name. And the serpent text commissions its messages to twelve deities and names each one's seat, from Caphtor in the west through central Anatolia and central Syria to Tuttul and Mari in upper Mesopotamia and across the Jordan. So the question is not whether this transmission was in contact with the others this holding carries. It was, and says so."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:RIH7820,
        pmw:RS1002,
        pmw:RS2019,
        pmw:RS2024,
        pmw:RS24244,
        pmw:RS24251,
        pmw:RS24266,
        pmw:RS24272,
        pmw:RS3348,
        pmw:RS34126 ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RS24266 ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The KTU corpus, including the Rapiuma texts and the serpent incantations" .

pmw:VomRoroimaZumOrinocoIII a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Koch-Gruenberg, Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, Band III: Ethnographie"@de,
        "Koch-Gruenberg, Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, volume III: Ethnographie"@en ;
    skos:note "Access: identifier bub_br_1918_01011130, full text over the regular route, 1,174,957 characters. The tail is the publisher's own back matter followed by a notice of the Brasiliana Digital collection of the University of Sao Paulo, so the file ends where the book ends and is not one of the truncations the access table warns about. The running heads over the part read run 197, 198, 199, 201, 202, 203, 204, 205, 206, 207, 208, 210, 211, 212, 213, 215, 216, 217, 218, 219, 220, 221 without going backwards, so the page numbers cited are the printed ones. The optical reading of the body text is good; the chapter headings and the table of contents are badly damaged, and the chapter was therefore located by its running heads and not by a heading."@en,
        "The catalogue line of the archive copy is wrong in three particulars and the title page was preferred, as SPEC section 10 requires. The catalogue gives the title as Vom Roraima, the publisher as Berlin, Dietrich Reimer (Ernest Vohsen), and the year as 1917; the file delivers Vom Roroima, Stuttgart, Strecker und Schroeder, 1923. This is the same failure the holding recorded at intellectualcult00rasm, where a catalogue line named a different volume than the file delivered."@en,
        "The edition as the title page in the full text gives it: Vom Roroima zum Orinoco, Ergebnisse einer Reise in Nordbrasilien und Venezuela in den Jahren 1911-1913, unternommen und herausgegeben im Auftrage und mit Mitteln des Baessler-Instituts in Berlin von Theodor Koch-Gruenberg, Dritter Band: Ethnographie, Verlag Strecker und Schroeder in Stuttgart, 1923."@en,
        "What was read at the wording: pages 194 to 221, that is the end of the chapter on the cure, the whole of the chapter on the Zauberarzt including the account of his training, and the opening of the chapter on the spells. The volume runs to some four hundred pages and the rest is not opened."@en .

pm:casesWithDeviation a pm:ExaminationState ;
    rdfs:label "Fälle mit Abweichung"@de,
        "Cases with deviation"@en ;
    skos:definition "Cases have been recorded and show a deviation from the stated base rate. What deviates is what the protocol measured, which is rarely the whole of what the claim asserts."@en .

pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition a pm:CounterSearchState ;
    rdfs:label "Gegensuche mit Fund"@de,
        "Counter-search found opposition"@en ;
    skos:definition "A search returned work that contradicts the evidence, fails to replicate it, or narrows its reach. What was found belongs in the note and, where it bears on the finding, in the protocol's own notes."@en .

pmt:InsularCeltic a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Inselkeltische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Insular Celtic transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Sanas Cormaic s.v. Imbas forosnai, Irish text in Whitley Stokes, Three Irish Glossaries (London: Williams and Norgate, 1862), p. 25" ;
    skos:definition "The Irish and Welsh transmission of poetic knowledge as an acquired faculty, with named divinatory procedures and the ecclesiastical prohibitions attached to them."@en ;
    skos:note "One philological point governs every citation in this file and is stated once here. The two public-domain translations of the glossary entry render the same Irish clause differently, and the clause is the one that carries the term. The Irish of the Leabhar Breac text printed by Stokes reads cocend nómaide no a dó no a tri. Stokes renders it to the end of a nómad (?) or two or three, leaving the unit untranslated and marking it doubtful; O'Donovan renders it a minute or two or three; the editors of the Ancient Laws print a minute at the parallel place while noting in their own footnote that the reading there is corrupt and that the glossary has the right one. This holding does not decide the length of a nómad, which would need a dictionary it has not opened. It records that a unit stands there, that it is counted, and that the two renderings a reader can obtain disagree about what it is."@en,
        "Read in the public-domain editions, 2026-09-17, and at the wording of the Irish where the Irish is printed. Sanas Cormaic s.v. Imbas forosnai: the Irish text in Whitley Stokes, Three Irish Glossaries (London: Williams and Norgate, 1862), p. 25, with Stokes's own rendering in the preface at p. xxxvi, and the English of John O'Donovan in Sanas Chormaic: Cormac's Glossary, ed. Whitley Stokes (Calcutta: O. T. Cutter, 1868), pp. 94-95. The same glossary s.v. Orc tréith, at 1868 pp. 129-130. The prologue to the Senchas Már in Ancient Laws of Ireland, vol. I (Dublin: HMSO, 1865), pp. 24-25 and 44-45 with the editors' notes there. The instance of imbas forosnai from the version of the Senchas Már in the vellum codex H. 3. 18, printed and translated by Kuno Meyer, Finn and the Man in the Tree, Revue Celtique 25 (1904), pp. 344-349. The glám dícenn at Mittelirische Verslehren III, section 155, in Rudolf Thurneysen's edition, Irische Texte III/1 (Leipzig 1891), pp. 96-97, with his German at pp. 124-125, and the same passage from the Book of Ballymote in Whitley Stokes, The Second Battle of Moytura, Revue Celtique 12 (1891), pp. 119-121. The triad at Kuno Meyer, The Triads of Ireland (Dublin: Hodges and Figgis, 1906), sections 123, pp. 16-17. Giraldus Cambrensis, Descriptio Kambriae I.16, in the English of Thomas Wright, The Historical Works of Giraldus Cambrensis (London: Bohn, 1863), pp. 501-502."@en,
        "The contact route is moved from disputed to documented, and the move is a finding of this reading rather than a tidying. Everything entered here reaches us inside a Christian Latin frame that the sources do not conceal: the glossary is an etymological work in the Isidorean manner, the law text is a prologue written to place the native law under a Christian settlement, and the prohibition of two of the three procedures is attributed in both to Patrick by name. A route between this transmission and the Christian material this holding already carries is therefore not merely plausible but present in the witnesses themselves. Agreement with that material counts as reception. What the move does not decide is the age of what the frame carries, and nothing here is entered as pre-Christian."@en,
        "The prohibitions are in the same manuscripts as the procedures, so procedure and counter-testimony arrive together."@en,
        "What was not read, and it is the larger part. The standard critical edition of the glossary, Kuno Meyer's Sanas Cormaic in Anecdota from Irish Manuscripts IV (Halle 1912), which alone prints the longer recension of the Yellow Book of Lecan, is not digitally available and was not obtained; that search stands at pmp:CelticCriticalEditionSearch. Uraicecht na Ríar, Bretha Nemed in either recension, and the Corpus Iuris Hibernici are in print only and were not opened, so the legal grading of the poets is here only as the law prologue and the verse tracts give it. The immrama and the Book of Taliesin, both named in the registration as principal corpus, were not opened: the one available public-domain translation of the Taliesin poems is judged unusable by the field and would have supplied words without warrant."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The legal and glossarial material on imbas forosnai, teinm laída and díchetal do chennaib; the immrama; the Book of Taliesin" .

pmt:Midewiwin a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Midewiwin"@de,
        "Midewiwin"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), pp. 143-300" ;
    skos:definition "The Anishinaabe medicine society, with graded degrees and a mnemonic record incised on birchbark."@en ;
    skos:note "Read in the report of nineteen ninety-one for the first time, pages 143 to about 180 of the printed text: the account of the society, of the initiation, of the four degrees and of the pictographic charts. The song scrolls, the medicine formulas and the extensive appended vocabularies were not read. The recorder worked among the Ojibwa in eighteen eighty-seven to eighteen eighty-nine; his chief informants are named in the report, the principal one a second-degree priest of Mille Lacs first initiated about eighteen thirty."@en,
        "The birchbark scrolls are a written transmission by their own means, which puts this tradition closer to the project's source rule than most of the registry it came from."@en,
        "The contact route is entered as disputed and not as absent. What is disputed is the age of the society itself, not a route to any tradition this graph holds: one side reads it as a nativistic formation of the contact period, the other points to a dated scroll fragment from before any documented European contact in the region. The dispute stands as its own node below, which is what pm:contactRouteDisputed requires before an agreement may be weighed at all. Note that the axis of the dispute and the axis a convergence asks about are not the same axis; see prima-materia#101."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The birchbark scroll record; Hoffman, The Midewiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa" .

pmt:Yangsheng a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Yangsheng-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Yangsheng nourishing-life transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhangjiashan Yinshu, slip 1, in Vivienne Lo (trans.), How to do the Gibbon Walk: A Translation of the Pulling Book (ca. 186 BCE), Needham Research Institute Working Papers 3 (Cambridge: Needham Research Institute, 2014), translating the transcript of Zhangjiashan Han mu zhujian (Beijing: Wenwu chubanshe, 2001)" ;
    skos:definition "The early Chinese transmission of breath circulation, guided stretching and dietary regimen for the prolongation of life, with illustrated postures."@en ;
    skos:note "Read in one work and read whole: the Zhangjiashan Yinshu, in Vivienne Lo's annotated translation of 2014, which prints the Chinese transcript of the Wenwu edition of 2001 slip by slip beside the English. All 113 slips were read in the English, and the three texts the translator distinguishes were read as three: the year's regimen in four seasons at slips 1 to 7, the forty-one exercises at slips 8 to 102, and the treatise on the causes of illness at slips 103 to 113. The whole translation was searched by term for cautions, stated terms, signs and failure conditions, and every hit read in its place."@en,
        "The contact route stays absent, and after the reading that is a finding and no longer an assumption. The work names one authority for its regimen, Peng Zu, and otherwise grounds itself in heaven, earth and the four seasons. Nothing in the 113 slips names a teacher, a lineage, a foreign source or a term this holding carries from the Indian material; the search that establishes it stands at pmp:YangshengContactSearch. The burial date makes the question decidable in a way it almost never is: whatever agreement this material has with practice elsewhere cannot have been received after 156 before this era, because the slips were in the ground."@en,
        "The source is an excavated manuscript and not a transmitted work, which is new in this holding and is stated here rather than left to be noticed. The rule of SPEC 10 asks for a work with the place that carries the claim and excludes links, videos, wikis and summaries standing in for a passage; an excavated text in a published transcript with a published translation meets what the rule is for, and it meets it better than a transmitted text does in one respect, because nothing was copied into or out of it after the tomb was closed. What it lacks is a transmission history, so a node from it says what stood on those slips and not what a lineage taught."@en,
        "What was not reached, and it is the part the registration was made for as much as this one. The Mawangdui Daoyin tu and Quegu shiqi were not opened: no public-domain or freely readable English edition of either was found, and the transcripts stand in modern scholarship this run could not obtain. The Huangting jing was not opened. The coverage state therefore says places entered and not corpus harvested, and it says it of one manuscript out of three named."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteAbsent ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Mawangdui manuscripts, including Daoyin tu and Quegu shiqi; the Zhangjiashan Yinshu; Huangting jing" .

pmw:GziBrjid a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "gZi brjid, die lange Lebensbeschreibung des Shenrab"@de,
        "gZi brjid, the long biography of Shenrab"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened on 2026-10-09 in the excerpts of D. L. Snellgrove, 1967, which give the Tibetan text of nine chapters with a facing English translation and a glossary. The excerpts are what was opened and not the work: the gZi brjid runs to twelve volumes, and the nine chapters are the doctrinal summary of the Nine Ways and not the narrative."@en .

pmc:AyurvedaDirghayus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Hundert Jahre ohne Verfall"@de,
        "A hundred years without decrepitude"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.1.7-8 and I.2.20, in the English of A. C. Kaviratna, parts XXXIII-XXXIV, Calcutta 1904-1905, pp. 1018 and 1034" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity the lesson claims most often and in the most nearly checkable form: that the user lives out a stated span, and lives it without the signs of age. The span is given as a figure and the figure moves — a hundred years for most of the preparations, a thousand for the seers of old, ten thousand for the one that is taken lying in a covered wooden vessel — and where it is a hundred it is the span the same work elsewhere assigns to human life, so that the claim at its commonest is that the user reaches the full term rather than that he exceeds it."@en ;
    skos:note "The form of the claim decides what could ever be done with it. A span of a hundred years is stated of a person and is settled only by that person's death, which is the outcome the user cannot be present for and the physician will not outlive. Every shorter criterion the lesson might have given — a term at which the effect should be noticeable, a sign that it has begun — is absent, and its absence was searched for rather than assumed. See pmp:AyurvedaRasayanaLingaSearch."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ayurvedic .

pmt:ChineseDivination a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Chinesische Divinations- und Siedlungslehre"@de,
        "Chinese divinatory and siting transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhouyi, Xici A, the section beginning with the number of the great expansion, in the Wang Bi and Han Kangbo recension; read in the digital text of the Kanseki Repository, which names no printing" ;
    skos:definition "The Chinese transmission of divination by counting stalks down to a remainder, of a parallel counting on a different modulus, and of the siting of graves on the doctrine that the bones of the parents receive the qi of the ground and that the descendants are shaded by it."@en ;
    skos:note "A second reason the old value could not stand. The works read span the first century before the common era to a redaction of the fourteenth: the burial book as read is the recension Wu Cheng made from the eight sections Cai Yuanding had left of twenty, and it is first attested in the Song bibliography. Absent asks whether a route was available at the time the corpus was fixed, and for that end of the corpus the answer is not the same as for the Xici."@en,
        "Chosen from the registry on the testability entrance, and the reading bore the registration out on both halves. Fristen and stated outcomes are dense here: the burial book says that where the configuration is inauspicious the misfortune does not turn a day, and it names what a given shape of ground brings to the descendants, down to the extinction of the line. What the registration did not say is that the same book carries six adversities under which a favourable site produces nothing, and that the commentary on the sixth enumerates the delays, disputes, poverty and wars through which a burial fails to happen at all. Every non-occurrence has a place to go."@en,
        "Edition note. The Changes were read in the digital text of the Kanseki Repository in the Wang Bi and Han Kangbo recension; that text names no printing, and the citations say so rather than inventing one. The Taixuanjing and the Lunheng were read in the Sibu congkan facsimiles, the Zangshu and the Jiu Tangshu in the Wenyuange Siku quanshu text."@en,
        "Read at the wording on 2026-09-10, in addition to the passages above and all of them in the Lunheng: the treatise on frugal burial and the treatise on the four taboos in juan 23, the treatises on censuring days, on distinguishing calamities and on objecting to the year star in juan 24, the treatise on interrogating the art in juan 25, and the fourth and last chapter of juan 23, which argues against the year and month taboos on breaking ground. With that the chapters of the work bearing on the arts this file holds are read at the wording; the remainder of the Lunheng, and the Taixuanjing outside the counting chapter, are still searched by term only."@en,
        "Read in this run at the wording, in the Chinese: Zhouyi, Xici A, sections on the great expansion and on what the Changes are without; Zhouyi, hexagram 4; Taixuanjing, Xuan shu, the eleventh; Zangshu, the whole of the single juan of the Siku recension, inner, outer and miscellaneous sections with its interlinear commentary; Lunheng 24, the treatise on divining; Jiu Tangshu 79, the memorial on the burial books. The Taixuanjing outside the counting chapter, and the rest of the Lunheng, were searched by term and not read continuously."@en,
        "The contact route is entered as disputed and no longer as absent, and the dispute is in the literature rather than in the sources. The graph already holds three transmissions that read a figure off an ungoverned remainder — the Ifá casting, the Fa casting and the geomantic tradition — and the counting of the Changes has the same form on a different modulus. One side derives the Arabic sand art from Chinese material through Needham's transmission routes; the other reconstructs it inside Abbasid Mesopotamia and states that the Chinese derivation was tried and dropped. Neither side was read at the wording in this run, and nothing here decides it; what the value records is that the question is contested and open, which is the opposite of what absent asserted."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Zhouyi with the Xici commentary; Yang Xiong, Taixuanjing; Zangshu, in the recension of Wu Cheng; Wang Chong, Lunheng; the memorials of Lü Cai in Jiu Tangshu 79" .

pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Äthiopisch-orthodoxe Zaubertradition"@de,
        "Ethiopian Orthodox magical transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. A. Wallis Budge (ed.), The Miracles of the Blessed Virgin Mary, and the Life of Hanna (Saint Anne), and the Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (Lady Meux Manuscripts Nos. 2-5; London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, pp. 216-220" ;
    skos:definition "The Geez transmission of protective prayers written on strips of parchment and worn on the body, in which lists of divine and angelic names are said to keep off named harms, and in which the name of the person the roll was written for stands in every prayer."@en ;
    skos:note "Contact route kept at documented, and now carried twice over. The registration argued it from the Enochic book, whose fullest witness is the Ethiopic one and which this graph already holds. The reading adds a second and closer route: the story that grounds the seventh prayer, in which Susenyos on horseback kills the child-killer Werzelya and takes an oath from her, is the Ethiopian station of a legend transmitted in Greek, Coptic, Syriac, Armenian and Slavonic. That the parallels exist was established in the landscape stage of this run and not read at the wording; the comparative works are named in an issue rather than asserted here."@en,
        "Read for the first time in one edited volume: the nine prayers of Lady Meux MS No. 5, written for the woman Aheta Mikael, and the three supplementary rolls printed with them in the same volume. The English translations were read at the wording, page by page, from p. 216 to p. 220 for the Meux roll and from p. LIV to p. LXV for the three supplementary rolls, the third of which is a litany that runs on past the point where the summaries of the other two end. What was not read: the Geez texts printed at the end of the volume, because the reading is of the translation and the edition is named accordingly; and the greater part of the volume, which carries the Miracles of the Blessed Virgin Mary and the Life of Hanna and is a different corpus."@en,
        "The remaining works named in the principal corpus were not opened in this run. The Lefafa Sedeq is the chief absence and it is a large one: Budge printed it separately in 1929 and that volume is in full view on HathiTrust under the identifiers of the Indiana and Michigan copies, so it is reachable in the sense of SPEC 15 and its absence here is a property of this run and not of the work."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Lefafa Sedeq; the asmat scroll corpus; the Geez canon" .

pmt:Neidan a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Neidan, innere Alchemie"@de,
        "Neidan internal alchemy"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhang Boduan, Wuzhen pian, in Wuzhen pian zhushi with Weng Baoguang's commentary (DZ 145), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 2, fol. 5b-6b" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission that relocates the alchemical work into the body, with a staged refinement of essence, breath and spirit and an embryo said to form and depart; carrying with it an unusually explicit doctrine of what makes the work fail, an ordered catalogue of the signs by which it is said to be succeeding, and an internal disagreement about whether its timings are read off the calendar or off the body."@en ;
    skos:note "Read at the wording, in the Chinese, on 2026-09-11: the Jindan sizi entire, preface, twenty quatrains and commentary; the Zhong-Lü chuandao ji, chapters seventeen and eighteen entire, with the chapter headings of the other sixteen and the two postfaces; and of the Wuzhen pian the passages cited below in juan 2 of DZ 145, with juan 1 and 3 gone through by term over the five owed kinds. The remainder of the Zhong-Lü chuandao ji and the greater part of the Wuzhen pian are not harvested."@en,
        "The contact route stands as documented and the ground is inside the works that were read, not in the scholarship. Weng Baoguang's commentary states that the author of the Cantong qi read the Changes, understood that the workings of the golden elixir are one with the Changes, and composed his book to set out the images of the Changes so as to make the great elixir plain; and the commentary then carries the whole apparatus over — the tripod and furnace as Qian and Kun, the medicine as Kan and Li, two hexagrams a day for thirty days to make sixty, the four central hexagrams added to make sixty-four, three hundred and eighty-four lines answering to the three hundred and eighty-four grains of a catty. The route to the external alchemy is therefore not an inference from similarity but the transmission's own account of where its instrument came from."@en,
        "What that route does to any convergence built on this file. The Waidan file in this holding rests on the Baopuzi and on the Cantong qi; this one names the Cantong qi as its source. An agreement between the two is reception at the level of the instrument, whatever may hold of the practice. The same caution applies to this file and traditions/shangqing.ttl, which share a vocabulary of body gods and inner vision without a route having been examined here."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Zhang Boduan, Wuzhen pian; Zhong-Lü chuandao ji; Jindan sizi; Xingming guizhi" .

pmt:SaivaSakta a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Śaiva- und Śākta-Tantra"@de,
        "Śaiva and Śākta tantra"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pūrṇānanda, Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa (1577)" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the Śaiva and Śākta tantric corpus and its Kashmirian exegesis."@en ;
    skos:note "A caution governs everything read out of this tradition. What is old is a set of subtle centres carrying seed syllables, and the older tantras vary in how many they count. The fixed system of seven centres with spectral colours and assigned psychological qualities belongs to the twentieth century and to no tantra; it is held in this file as pmc:SevenCentreQualityMapping so that it is available as what it is, and it must not be read back into the material it was built on."@en,
        "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. The Vijñānabhairava citations name the numbering they were read in. For the Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa, the Tantrāloka, the Tantrasāra and the Mālinīvijayottaratantra the edition is not recoverable and is left unstated rather than guessed; those passages are to be reopened, and the chapter divisions of the Kashmirian exegesis are edition-dependent."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Vijñānabhairava; Mālinīvijayottaratantra; Abhinavagupta, Tantrāloka and Tantrasāra; Kulārṇava Tantra; Kālikāpurāṇa; Mahānirvāṇa Tantra; Pūrṇānanda, Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa" .

pmw:NuptiaeChymicae a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Chymische Hochzeit Christiani Rosencreutz (1616)"@de,
        "Chymische Hochzeit Christiani Rosencreutz (1616)"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened on 2026-09-30 and read complete, all seven days, in a modern reset English and checked passage by passage against the Strasbourg print of 1616. It is the only one of the three works that narrates a procedure, and the only one that states what happens to those who fail at its stations. Its canonical locus is the day."@en .

pm:contactRouteDisputed a pm:ContactRoute ;
    rdfs:label "Kontaktweg strittig"@de,
        "Contact route disputed"@en ;
    skos:definition "A route has been proposed and is contested in the scholarship. Agreement counts as neither reception nor independent attestation until the dispute is entered as its own node."@en .

pmt:Theosophy a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Theosophie"@de,
        "Theosophy"@en ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter Force-Centres, pp. 286-291 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" ;
    skos:definition "The modern transmission of root races, planes and masters, presented as the recovery of a single ancient doctrine underlying all religions."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered with the note that the pedigree claim is the tradition's own central assertion and is contested, that it belongs in the graph as a claim about origin and not as a fact about transmission, and that much of what circulates today as the doctrine of older traditions entered general circulation here. Taken off pm:corpusNamed on 2026-10-08 at The Inner Life, with The Chakras opened at one chapter. The contact route is unchanged at documented and nothing in this file is offered as an independent witness."@en,
        "The registration's second sentence is confirmed in the narrow way a reading can confirm it. The seven force-centres with their radiation counts and colours, as this transmission states them, stand verbatim inside a 1916 Pali Text Society translation of a Sinhala meditation manual, attributed there to Yoga; the manual itself places five raptures in the body and knows nothing of them. The case is held at pmc:WoodwardCakkaInterpolatio in originations/boran-theosophical-insertion.ttl, which this run corrected against the source it names."@en,
        "Two works of the registered corpus stand as pm:corpusWork without pm:fromWork and are carried by queries/unopened-works.rq: Isis Unveiled and The Secret Doctrine. Neither was opened. The reason is the scope of the strand and not a property of the works, both of which are reachable as full text."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:IsisUnveiled,
        pmw:TheChakras,
        pmw:TheInnerLife,
        pmw:TheSecretDoctrine ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Blavatsky, Isis Unveiled; Blavatsky, The Secret Doctrine; Leadbeater, The Chakras" .

pmw:SusrutaSamhita a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Susruta Samhita"@de,
        "Susruta Samhita"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened on 2026-10-02 at two chapters of the Sarirasthana, VI on the marmas and VIII on venesection, read continuously in the English of Kaviraj Kunjalal Bhishagratna, An English Translation of the Sushruta Samhita, vol. II, Calcutta 1911, pp. 173-190 and 197-208. The two open claims that turned on it are answered at the wording: the graded death-terms of the marman and the states in which an operation is forbidden. Not read: chapters I to V, VII, IX and X of the same Sarirasthana, and the Sutra-, Nidana-, Chikitsita-, Kalpa- and Uttara-sthana. The same volume carries the Nidana-, Chikitsita- and Kalpa-sthana and is the cheapest continuation; the surgical book, which is where the extraction of a shaft is actually described, stands in volume I."@en,
        "The scan read is the archive.org full text englishtranslati02susr, and the title page in it gives Calcutta, published by the author, 1911, with the printer's year 1907 on the verso. The Sanskrit is not on this route: a character count by Unicode block over the whole file returns nothing outside Latin, so every term entered from this work is Bhishagratna's transliteration and every interval is his English."@en .

pmt:Andean a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Andine Überlieferung"@de,
        "Andean transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. II, the summary of what is worshipped" ;
    skos:definition "The Andean transmission of huacas as powers that are addressed and answer, of camac as the making and animating that a huaca is invoked by, and of the graded specialists who speak with them, fast for them and hear confession before them."@en ;
    skos:note "Left unread: chapters VIII to XIII and XV to XX of the extirpation account, which are about the causes of idolatry and the conduct of the visitation rather than about what is transmitted; the whole of the report on the Incas apart from the section on the specialists; and the rest of the chronicle of sixteen fifteen. The Quechua prose of Huarochirí, which is the one document of this transmission written in its own language, was not opened at all: neither the manuscript nor any of its three standard translations could be reached in full text, which is a property of the works and is recorded as its own issue."@en,
        "Read in this run, all three at the wording: the account of the extirpation of idolatry of sixteen twenty-one, chapters I to VII and XIV, in a digitised copy of the first edition; the report on the fables and rites of the Incas of about fifteen seventy-five, in the critical edition of two thousand and ten, at the folios given; and the chapter on the sorcerers of this kingdom in the autograph chronicle of sixteen fifteen, in the transcription published with the manuscript by its holding library, at the author's own page numbers."@en,
        "The contact route is entered as undecided and no longer as absent. The registry's absent was not wrong about the Andes before fifteen thirty-two; it was wrong about the corpus. Every word of this transmission that can be read was written down after the conquest, and two of the three recorders wrote in order to destroy what they described. The categories they carried in — sin, confession, penance, priesthood, the devil — are the categories under which the material is reported, and the confession complex entered below is exactly where that matters most. Whether a validity test for a confession is Andean, or Christian confession seen through an Andean procedure, or an Andean procedure seen through Christian confession, is not decidable from these three witnesses, and the entry says so rather than settling it by silence."@en,
        "The first edition of the extirpation account carries folio numbers that its digitisation does not reproduce reliably, so the places below are given by chapter and by the passage within it. A modern transcription of the same text was held alongside to disambiguate the reading of the sixteen twenty-one type; the two give the same wording at every place used here. No place rests on the transcription alone."@en,
        "The third recorder states his own provenance and it cuts both ways. He writes that he knows what he reports about the priesthood because he served the visitor general who destroyed the huacas of the kingdom. That makes him a witness from inside the transmission who learned much of what he reports while helping to extinguish it, which is a stronger warrant than either priest for the terminology and a weaker one for the practice."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteUndecided ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Huarochirí manuscript; Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú; Molina, Relación de las fábulas y ritos de los incas; Guaman Poma de Ayala, Nueva corónica y buen gobierno; Polo de Ondegardo, Los errores y supersticiones de los indios; Cobo, Historia del Nuevo Mundo, book XIII" .

pmw:ArsNotoria a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Ars Notoria"@de,
        "Ars Notoria"@en ;
    skos:note "One identifier for the work across its recensions and printings. The witness a node was read in stays in its dcterms:source; the divergence between the Latin of the printing and Turner's English is held as a dispute and not as two works."@en .

pmw:LiberIuratusHonorii a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Liber Iuratus Honorii"@de,
        "Liber Iuratus Honorii"@en .

pmw:TheInnerLife a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "The Inner Life"@de,
        "The Inner Life"@en ;
    skos:note "Theosophical talks at Adyar, published in two series. The first series carries the chapters Force-Centres, pp. 286-297, and The Serpent-Fire, pp. 298-303, which are the two chapters this run read complete. Not named in the registration's principal corpus; declared here because it is the work the 1916 footnote in originations/boran-theosophical-insertion.ttl names as its source, and because the printing read carries the chapter the footnote names. The footnote gives the title without an edition, so which printing its compiler had is not established by this run and is a property of that footnote."@en .

pm:AwarenessValue a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Wert einer Bewusstseinsachse"@de,
        "Awareness Axis Value"@en ;
    skos:definition "A value on one of the awareness axes. The class exists so that a single constraint can keep pm:axisValue to the declared coordinates; every value below carries it alongside the axis it belongs to."@en .

pmt:Mandaean a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Mandäische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Mandaean transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ginzā, right side, book 15, piece 7 (text 319,1-320,16), in the German of Mark Lidzbarski, Ginzā: Der Schatz oder das große Buch der Mandäer (Göttingen: Vandenhoeck und Ruprecht, 1925), pp. 322-324" ;
    skos:definition "A living gnostic transmission with its own scripture and priesthood, teaching an ascent of the soul past stations of examination."@en ;
    skos:note "Not read: the left Ginzā and the greater part of the right; the Drāšā d-Yahia, which was not opened in this run although Lidzbarski's German of 1915 is public domain; the burial and ritual-meal chapters of the field monograph. Not obtainable: the Alf Trisar Šuialia, the priestly manual in which the fault rules of this transmission actually stand. Its edition of 1960 exists only in print and in a lending copy whose text file answers with 401, and the transliteration held by the Comprehensive Aramaic Lexicon refuses automated retrieval. That is a property of the work and not of this run, and it is the reason the tariff below is entered from the field record rather than from the manual."@en,
        "Read in this run at the wording, on 2026-09-20. Three works were opened. First, the Ginzā in the German of Mark Lidzbarski of 1925, read closely at the right side, book one, the commandments; book two, first piece, the parallel commandments; book five, third piece, the journey past the watch-houses, in full; book thirteen, the priest's prayer and the warnings; book fifteen, first and seventh pieces. Second, the Qolastā in the English of E. S. Drower of 1959, searched over the whole and read at the baptism and masiqta prayers and their rubrics. Third, Drower's field monograph of 1937, read closely at chapters nine and ten, the consecration of the tarmida and of the ganzibra, in full, and at the passages on the insignia, on the baptism of children, on marriage, and on the calendar."@en,
        "The contact route stays at disputed and this run did not settle it, but the shape of the dispute is worth stating, because the received summary of it is not the one the material supports. The question is usually put as west against east, a Palestinian origin against a Mesopotamian one. What actually divides the scholarship is a question about evidence: no manuscript of this corpus is older than the sixteenth century, and everything claimed for an earlier state of the text rests on the copyist chains named in the colophons. Whoever holds those chains to be reliable dates the transmission to the second or third century; whoever holds them to be constructions follows the late dating. A reader of this file should know that the works read here do not decide it and that no node below claims to."@en,
        "What kind of witness each work is, since they are not the same kind. The Ginzā and the Qolastā are transmitted books and are cited as such. The monograph of 1937 is the first fixation in writing of rules which its author was told by priests and of proceedings she attended; its passages are entered with pm:fieldAttestation and the gathering is named at each node. Where she quotes the priestly manual, the claim is second-hand and says so. The three are not interchangeable, and the file does not let the field record stand in for the scripture: the searches at examinations/searches-mandaean.ttl were carried precisely to find out which of the rules below the scripture itself states, and the answer is none of them."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:AlfTrisarSuialia,
        pmw:DrasaDYahia,
        pmw:GinzaRabba,
        pmw:Qolasta ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GinzaRabba ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Ginzā Rabbā; Drāšā d-Yahia; Qolastā" .

pmt:AzandeOracle a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Azande-Orakelüberlieferung"@de,
        "Azande oracular transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), appendix I, the list of Zande terms, pp. 226-228" ;
    skos:definition "The Zande transmission of witchcraft as an inherited substance in the body, of a graded set of oracles by which it is located, and of a corroborative second question by which an oracular verdict is held valid or void."@en ;
    skos:note "Chosen from the registry on the testability entrance rather than on demand. The transmission states a condition under which its own principal oracle delivers no verdict at all, names eight causes to which a failure is attributed, and prints two consultations at length so that a reader may count the outcomes for himself. Very little else in this graph names a failure condition its own practitioners apply."@en,
        "Read in this run: the abridged edition of nineteen seventy-six in full for chapters I, III, V, VI, VIII, IX and X and for the appendix of terms and the appendix on dreams. Left unread in that edition: chapters IV, VII, XI, XII and XIII, on whether witches are conscious agents, on the place of witch-doctors in the society, on magic and medicines, on the association for communal magic, and on the situation of death; and the third appendix. The unabridged edition of nineteen thirty-seven was not opened: it is in copyright and stands in the large archives only as a lending copy or as a search-only index, which is a property of the work and not of the search. Every place below is therefore given from the abridged edition, and the abridgement is named in each reference because an abridgement is a selection and a later reader must be able to see that he is looking at one."@en,
        "The contact route is entered as undecided and no longer as absent, and the correction is not a finding but the removal of one: the registry had recorded no route because none had been looked for. What a search finds is on both sides. Against a route: the poison oracle stands already in the earliest account, taken down at the first sustained European contact, so it cannot have entered with that contact. For a route: the same monograph places the belief in a witchcraft substance in the body among many peoples of Central and West Africa and calls Zandeland the north-eastern limit of its distribution, which is an areal connection to the West African sphere in which this graph already holds a transmission; and the corridor from the Islamic north was open from the eighteen-sixties, since the earliest witness travelled in with an ivory company out of Khartoum. Neither has been carried out for the oracular material in particular. Until it is, an agreement between this tradition and anything already in the graph counts as neither reception nor independent attestation."@en,
        "Two further witnesses were opened for the counter-search and are cited where they carry something: the travel account of eighteen seventy-three, which is the earliest European description of these oracles and predates the anthropological literature by two generations, and the Belgian ethnography of nineteen twenty-one, which describes the same transmission from the other side of the colonial border and sixteen years earlier than the monograph. Both were read at the wording in full-text reproductions of the printed editions named in the references."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteUndecided ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande; de Calonne-Beaufaict, Azande; Larken, An account of the Zande, in Sudan Notes and Records; Lagae, Les Azande ou Niam-Niam" .

pmt:Hathayoga a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Haṭhayoga"@de,
        "Haṭhayoga"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the Haṭhayogapradīpikā of Svātmārāma and the manuals around it."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. Every verse cited in this file was read in the Sanskrit of the widely circulated vulgate, in the recension that the 1915 edition and translation of Sinh carries, and each citation now says so. The critical edition drawn from the manuscript tradition numbers differently in places; pmc:HathapradipikaRecensionDispute holds what is at issue."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā" .

pmw:SatapathaBrahmana a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Śatapatha-Brāhmaṇa"@de,
        "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened on 2026-09-19 in the Mādhyandina recension, in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East volumes 12, 26, 41, 43 and 44, Oxford 1882 to 1900, covering kāṇḍas I to XIV. Five passages were read at the wording; the rest of the fourteen kāṇḍas was searched and not read. The Kāṇva recension is a separate text for citation purposes and was not obtained."@en .

pm:Process a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Prozess"@de,
        "Process"@en ;
    skos:definition "The root class of all ongoing acts modelled by prima-materia. Every substantive class in the ontology is a subclass of pm:Process."@en .

pmt:KongoNkisi a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Kongolesische Nkisi-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Kongo nkisi transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), pp. 230-231" ;
    skos:definition "The Lower Congo and Loango transmission of composed objects charged to act, whose power is held to be renewed by sacrifice and destroyed by the breaking of a stated prohibition, and of a poison ordeal by which an accusation of witchcraft is decided at an outcome the operator is not supposed to control."@en ;
    skos:note "Chosen from the registry on the independence entrance. What the reading found is that the entrance was mistaken for this transmission and the correction is the chief finding of the run: the contact route is entered as documented and no longer as absent, and it is documented from inside the corpus and not from the scholarship. The word the transmission uses for a charged object is the word the old Catholic mission chose for holy, and the mission called its own churches nzo ankisi; the Kongo name for the devil is stated to have stood for four hundred years; the image of the Virgin in the church at San Salvador is addressed for a child like any other charged thing, and the crosses, discs and pictures given out by the priests are called by the same word as an amulet. A transmission whose central term was in liturgical use for four centuries cannot be counted as a witness without a route."@en,
        "One work could not be read where it should have been. The digitised text of Bentley 1900 volume one that was reachable breaks between printed page two hundred and thirty-seven and printed page two hundred and seventy-four, and the chapter on the charged objects themselves lies inside that gap, as does the passage in which a retiring witch-doctor is said to have put a number on her own false denunciations. The gap belongs to the copy and not to the work, so the pages are owed and stand as an issue, not as a property of the work."@en,
        "Read in this run at the wording: in Weeks 1914 the two chapters on magic and on charms and their prohibitions, printed pages two hundred and sixteen to two hundred and fifty-one, and the chapter on divination and the ordeal, printed pages two hundred and sixty-one to two hundred and sixty-five; in Weeks, Folk-Lore volume twenty, printed pages fifty-one to fifty-eight; in Bentley 1900 volume one, printed pages two hundred and thirty-six to two hundred and thirty-seven and two hundred and seventy-four to two hundred and ninety-three; in Dennett 1906, printed pages twenty-four to twenty-five, seventy-three to seventy-seven, eighty-three, eighty-six to ninety-three and one hundred and forty-seven to one hundred and forty-eight. The rest of each work was searched by term for the owed kinds and was not read continuously; that is a decision and not an exhaustion."@en,
        "The corpus is widened at the instance and the coined identifier is kept. The registration named the Laman notebooks in Kikongo and Laman, The Kongo; neither was opened. The four volumes are in any case a derivative rather than a source, since the notebooks were sorted, cut and fused in making them, and what a later run must obtain is the individual notebook with its author and district. What was read instead is four public-domain works of the years eighteen ninety-five to nineteen fourteen, three of them by men resident in the country for decades."@en,
        "The recorder problem is stated here rather than left in the sources. All four works are by outsiders: two Baptist missionaries at San Salvador and in the Ngombe Lutete district, and a trader on the Loango coast. Two consequences run in opposite directions and both are kept. The missionaries report the transmission as an evil to be ended, and every judgement of theirs is to be weighed separately from their observations. But their reports of fraud, of doubt among the people, and of promises that failed are reports against the interest of the thing they were describing and not for it, and they are the part of the corpus this graph can least afford to lose."@en,
        "Two speech communities stand in one file and the file says so rather than smoothing it. Weeks and Bentley record Kikongo-speaking Bakongo of the Lower Congo around San Salvador, Wathen and Ngombe Lutete; Dennett records Vili-speaking Bavili of Loango, whose orthography differs and who write nkici where the others write nkisi or nkixi. Dennett states that the overlord of Maluango was the ruler of Kongo, so the two stand in one documented sphere; whether the Loango transmission is to be registered as its own is a decision that has not been taken and is held as an issue."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo; Weeks, Notes on Some Customs of the Lower Congo People, in Folk-Lore, vols. XIX-XX; Bentley, Pioneering on the Congo, and the Appendix to the Dictionary and Grammar of the Kongo Language; Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind, and Notes on the Folklore of the Fjort; the Laman notebooks in Kikongo, with Laman, The Kongo, as the derivative made from them" .

pmt:SiberianShamanism a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Sibirische Schamanenüberlieferung"@de,
        "Siberian shamanic transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXII, sect. 95, pp. 271-274" ;
    skos:definition "The Tungus and Manchu transmission of an office whose holder is defined as a master of spirits rather than as one they possess, entered through an illness that resolves only on acceptance, carried by a drum and a brass mirror, and conferred by a formal recognition that a candidate can fail."@en ;
    skos:note "Read in this run at the wording: the monograph of nineteen thirty-five, chapters XXII to XXXI, that is the whole of the part on shamanism, pages 268 to 402, in the London edition of that year. Left unread: parts one to three, pages 49 to 240, on positive knowledge, on the hypotheses and on the practical consequences of the hypotheses, which carry the system of spirits this part presupposes; the introduction, pages 1 to 48, which is missing from the digitised copy used; and the whole of the Sakha material named in the registration, which the author does not carry."@en,
        "That correction is the reason the entry was worth opening first. The registration noted that this is the region the word shaman was taken from and that the graph used the category while holding no witness from its source. What the source says is that the complex it names is comparatively recent and largely borrowed. Every convergence in this graph that leans on a Siberian parallel is affected by it, and the affected nodes are exactly the ones nobody would have checked."@en,
        "The author is one recorder and not a chorus. He worked among the Tungus of Manchuria and Transbaikalia and among the Manchus over some twenty years, in their languages, and he says at the outset which groups he saw and which he did not. Where he gives his own reconstruction rather than what he was told, the notes on the nodes below say so."@en,
        "The contact route is entered as documented and no longer as undecided, and this is the finding of the run rather than a formality. The route runs through the instruments and through the word. The brass mirror without which no performance is possible is bought from the Mongols, who receive them from China and Tibet, and it is the same object that Lamaism uses on its altars; the headdress of the Tungus of Manchuria carries five images of Buddha; the term burkan, applied to a great number of spirits, is the Mongol modification of Buddha; and the word saman itself is traced by the author to sramana through the Chinese san-men. A tradition whose indispensable instrument, whose spirit vocabulary and whose own name run to a corpus this graph already holds cannot be counted as an independent witness for anything they share."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus; Shirokogoroff, Social Organization of the Northern Tungus; the published Olonkho epics; Anisimov's field records" .

pmp:NippurBowlInversion a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die beschriftete Schale mit der Öffnung nach unten niederlegen"@de,
        "Depositing the inscribed bowl mouth downward"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, introduction § 8, with texts 4: 1-2, 6: 1 and 9: 1, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 41-44)" ;
    skos:definition "The operation the whole corpus is built on: a bowl is inscribed on its inner surface with the incantation, then sunk just below the surface of the earth with its mouth downward, so that what the text has bound is held under it. One text names the vessel a press and says of itself: this press I press down upon them. Another opens by calling the bowls covers to hold in sacred angels and all evil spirits, and announces to the demons that they are bound and sealed in each one of the four corners of the house."@en ;
    skos:note "A second deposition is attested for other sites and not for Nippur: bowls inscribed for the cemetery, found in a graveyard. None of the Nippur bowls is so marked."@en,
        "The one operation in this holding whose performance is attested by the excavated object and not only by the text that prescribes it. Of the hundred bowls dug while the excavator was on the scene, most lay upside down in the ground; the position is photographed in his report, and bowls were also found set mouth to mouth in pairs."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic .

pmt:LakotaRitual a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Lakota-Ritualüberlieferung"@de,
        "Lakota ritual transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), pp. 55-59" ;
    skos:definition "The Lakota transmission of a vitalizing lodge that precedes every other rite, of a solitary fasting quest undertaken for a communication, of a graded sun-gazing ceremony whose completion is said to be answered within a stated term, and of small stones held to be sent out and to bring back word of what lies beyond sight."@en ;
    skos:note "A second witness was opened for the counter-search and is cited wherever it carries something: the bulletin of nineteen eighteen, recorded on the Standing Rock reservation from named men who had themselves danced. It is a different recorder, a different reservation and a partly different division of the tribe, and on one point it contradicts the first witness outright. Read in it at the wording: the Sun dance chapter, printed pages eighty-four to one hundred and fifty-one, the chapter on the sacred stones, printed pages two hundred and four to two hundred and eleven, and the account of Goose on printed pages two hundred and fifty-one and two hundred and fifty-two. The remainder, which is mostly musical analysis, was not read."@en,
        "Chosen from the registry on the testability entrance rather than on demand. This transmission states a term within which the promised communication is to arrive, provides in as many words for the case in which the quest yields nothing, hangs the validity of its greatest ceremony on a sign in the weather, decides a contested declaration by an ordeal with a named outcome, and names the cause to which a failure of its instrument is attributed. Little else in this graph carries a deadline and a stated non-occurrence side by side."@en,
        "Read in this run at the wording: the paper of nineteen seventeen in its expository part in full, printed pages fifty-one to one hundred and sixty-three, that is the Sun dance from the choosing of the mentor to the completion of the ceremony, the Hunka ceremony, the Buffalo ceremony, and the translated texts of Sword, Tyon, No-flesh and One-star together with the interview with Finger; and printed pages two hundred and seventeen to two hundred and nineteen, the two legends of the origin of the Buffalo ceremony. Left unread at the wording: the narratives on printed pages one hundred and sixty-four to two hundred and sixteen, which were passed over with a search for the four owed kinds and yielded none that the expository part does not already carry. That is a decision and not an exhaustion, and it is recorded so that a later reader can overturn it."@en,
        "The contact route is entered as documented and no longer as absent, and the correction is the finding of this run. It is documented three times over and each time from inside the corpus. The compiler of the paper of nineteen seventeen states that Wakan Tanka, two words in the old speech designating a class of gods, has become one word in modern Lakota designating the God of the Christians, so the central term of the doctrine was already contaminated when it was written down. His chief informant wrote an autobiography saying that in war with the white people he found their Wakan Tanka superior, that he took the name Sword, joined the church and is a deacon in it. And the editor of the volume identifies the Hunka ceremony with the Pawnee Hako and notes that the Lakota year name which dates its present form corresponds to eighteen hundred and five and turns on the tail of a horse, so that the ceremony took that form after the horse. The route is single-valued and the value entered is the one that governs the doctrinal nodes; that the informants of the second witness on the sacred stones were medicine men on whom the missionaries had made no impression is recorded on those nodes and does not change it."@en,
        "The recorder problem is stated here rather than left in the sources. The compiler writes that no one shaman was found who could give the mythology in a comprehensive or sequential manner, that the scheme was formulated with their aid, and that it was afterwards submitted to the informants for approval. A scheme formulated by the recorder and approved afterwards is an ordering act and not a transmitted doctrine, and it is entered below as one. The lodge, the quest, the ceremony, the ordeal and the stones carry their own passages and are untouched by it."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota, Anthropological Papers of the American Museum of Natural History, vol. XVI, part II; Densmore, Teton Sioux Music, Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 61; Brown, The Sacred Pipe; DeMallie, The Sixth Grandfather, as the record of the mediation" .

pm:searchFoundOpposing a pm:SearchOutcome ;
    rdfs:label "Suche mit Gegenfund"@de,
        "Search found opposing material"@en ;
    skos:definition "A passage was found that tells against the question as it was put, rather than answering it. The commonest shape of a productive search and the one an unrecorded search loses entirely."@en .

pmt:CelestialMasters a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Himmelsmeister"@de,
        "Celestial Masters"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhengyi fawen tianshi jiaojie kejing 014a (DZ 789), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile" ;
    skos:definition "The earliest organised Daoist transmission, in which illness is treated as the consequence of fault, healing proceeds by written confession and by petition to a bureaucracy of spirits, and the practitioner's standing rests on an entry in a register kept by his own master."@en ;
    skos:note "Contact route, left where the registration put it and now with a ground from inside the text. At 013b-014a the admonitions have the Dao go west among the barbarians and hand them the law of the Way, with the prohibition made severe there, no union of yin and yang and no killing, until the barbarians knocked their heads ten thousand times, shaved their heads and trimmed their beards, and the true Way arose among them. That is the conversion-of-the-barbarians claim, and it is a claim about a contact route made by the party that would benefit from it: it lets every resemblance between this transmission and Buddhism be read as borrowing in the other direction. It does not settle the route and it is why the route stays disputed. What the reading does establish is that the quiet chamber, the practice most often derived from Buddhist precedent, is already in the earliest outside description of the movement, where the Dianlüe of the third century ascribes it to Zhang Xiu as his addition to a method otherwise shared with Zhang Jue."@en,
        "Corpus correction, and it is the first finding of this reading. The registration named the Taiping jing as one of three principal works. It is not a work of this transmission, and the corpus says so about itself: at 014a of the admonitions the Yellow Turban rising of Zhang Jue — the movement whose scripture the Taiping jing is — is called a heterodox way that set the later heirs to dividing the pneuma and governing the people, and it is named as the cause of the disaster the text is written in the wake of. A transmission that calls another movement heterodox in its own foundational document does not hold that movement's scripture as its own canon. The term chengfu, the inherited burden that is the Taiping jing's characteristic doctrine, was searched for across all four works of this file and does not occur once; the search is entered at pmp:CmChengfuSearch. The principal corpus is restated here without it."@en,
        "What the file does not settle. The Xiang'er commentary is unopened, and it is the work on which the internal dating of the whole transmission rests, because the admonitions quote it and are themselves dated in their own text to the seventh day of the first month of the second year of Zhengyuan. The admonitions were read and that date stands at 016b; the quotation could not be checked against the commentary, and this file therefore carries the date as the corpus states it and not as a result."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:ChisongziZhangli,
        pmw:LaoziXiangerZhu,
        pmw:LuxianshengDaomenKelue,
        pmw:NuqingGuilu,
        pmw:Sanguozhi,
        pmw:ZhengyiFawenTianshiJiaojieKejing ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ZhengyiFawenTianshiJiaojieKejing ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Zhengyi fawen tianshi jiaojie kejing (DZ 789), containing the Dadao jia lingjie; Lu Xiujing, Lu xiansheng daomen kelüe (DZ 1127); Chisongzi zhangli (DZ 615); Nüqing guilü (DZ 790); Laozi Xiang'er zhu, in the Dunhuang manuscript Stein 6825; Sanguozhi 8, with the Dianlüe quoted in Pei Songzhi's commentary, as the report of an outside observer" .

pmt:OphiteGnosis a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Ophitische Gnosis"@de,
        "Ophite gnosis"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Irenaeus, Adversus Haereses I.30 (trans. A. Roberts and W. Rambaut, Ante-Nicene Fathers I, Buffalo 1885)",
        "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.24-38 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission behind a diagram of circles and gates in which the soul, rising after death, says a set speech to the ruler of each gate, naming what it brings and asking to pass."@en ;
    skos:note "Origen states that the bearers are not Christians, that they admit nobody who has not first cursed Jesus, and that they name one Euphrates as the author of their opinions; he also says the sect is very small and that he never met anyone anywhere who believed the diagram. The size claim comes from a hostile witness with a reason to make it and is recorded, not adopted."@en,
        "Read in the Ante-Nicene Fathers translation of Frederick Crombie. Neither the critical Greek of Koetschau nor Chadwick's translation was consulted, and no claim in this file rests on a word whose Greek was checked. The edition is named on every node for that reason."@en,
        "The caution that governs every node in this file. The whole of it is read out of an opponent. Origen is refuting Celsus and holds both of them in contempt; he says he obtained the diagram himself out of a love of learning, and he prints the speeches in order to show that he knows what he rejects. Every node therefore carries pm:thirdPartyAscription, and none of them may be read as a statement the bearers of this transmission made about themselves."@en,
        "The report is nonetheless of an unusual kind, and the difference is worth stating because it is what makes the file worth having. Origen is not summarising a doctrine: he is copying out of a document he possessed, and he says twice that he is doing so where Celsus could not or would not. What is entered here is closer to a transcribed liturgy inside a polemic than to a heresiological paraphrase."@en,
        "The second witness was opened afterwards and is a different kind of report. Irenaeus gives no speeches: he tells a myth, and the seven names appear in it as a chain of generations rather than as gatekeepers. Where the two accounts touch, they are entered side by side and their divergences are held as disputes, because an averaged account would report a witness neither of them gives."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.24-38; Irenaeus, Adversus Haereses I.30" .

pmt:VedicRitual a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Vedische Ritualüberlieferung"@de,
        "Vedic ritual transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa X,4,3,4-8 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, The Śatapatha-Brāhmaṇa, Sacred Books of the East 43, Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1897, pp. 356-357" ;
    skos:definition "The solemn ritual and its exegesis, in which efficacy rests on stated equivalences between the act, the body and the world."@en ;
    skos:note "Not opened at all: the Śrautasūtras, which the registration names as the third part of the principal corpus, and which carry the expiation as a book of its own — Kātyāyana 25, Āpastamba 9. Eggeling cites Kātyāyana at exactly the places entered below, so the address is known and the work is not."@en,
        "Read in this run at the wording, on 2026-09-19. The Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa in the Mādhyandina recension, in the English of Julius Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East volumes 12, 26, 41, 43 and 44, Oxford 1882 to 1900. Read closely: I,6,3 (the misspoken formula and Vṛtra); I,9,2 (the unyoking of the sacrifice); X,4,3 (the count of bricks and immortality); XI,2,7,33 (the balance); XI,5,3 (the dialogue of Śauceya and Uddālaka on what befalls when a fire goes out); XI,5,6,9 (the daily recitation); XII,4,1 to XII,4,4 (the expiations of the Agnihotra). The Aitareya and Kauṣītaki Brāhmaṇas in the English of Arthur Berriedale Keith, Rigveda Brahmanas, Harvard Oriental Series 25, Cambridge 1920: read closely Aitareya iii,7 to iii,11 (the vaṣaṭ call and the Nivid) and Kauṣītaki xxvi,4 to xxvi,6 (the flaw in the recitation). Everything else in both works was searched but not read: the five Eggeling volumes hold fourteen kāṇḍas and the Keith volume two further Brāhmaṇas entire. This is five passages out of a corpus of some four thousand pages, and no claim of exhaustion is made for it."@en,
        "The contact route stays at documented and this run found nothing to move it. What it did find is worth recording beside the value: the nearest formal parallel to this corpus's characteristic claim, that the knower of a formula obtains a named result, is the knowledge rubric of the Egyptian funerary literature, and the nearest parallel to its expiation book is the Mesopotamian namburbû. Neither was opened and no node here rests on either."@en,
        "The recension is part of the citation and not a detail. Eggeling translates the Mādhyandina; the Kāṇva recension divides into seventeen kāṇḍas against fourteen and its numbering does not convert, so a Kāṇva place has to be given on its own. No free full translation of the Kāṇva was found in this run, and the Kāṇva readings entered below are those Eggeling reports in his notes and not readings this run has seen."@en,
        "What kind of witness this is. The Brāhmaṇas are not a liturgy but the commentary that travels with one: they state what each act is for, what it is the counterpart of, and what happens if it is done wrong. That makes them unusually good evidence for what the tradition claimed and no evidence at all for what was done — the sūtras hold the performance and were not opened. A claim read here is a claim of the exegesis."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:AitareyaBrahmana,
        pmw:ApastambaSrautasutra,
        pmw:KatyayanaSrautasutra,
        pmw:KausitakiBrahmana,
        pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatapathaBrahmana ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa; Aitareya Brāhmaṇa; the Śrautasūtras" .

pmw:PgmIv a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "PGM IV, der grosse Zauberpapyrus von Paris (Bibliotheque nationale suppl. gr. 574)"@de,
        "PGM IV, the great magical papyrus of Paris (Bibliotheque nationale suppl. gr. 574)"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened at one piece, the ascent ritual at lines 475 to 829, in two layers: the English of Mead of 1907 and the edition of Dieterich in the second edition of 1910. The codex runs to thirty-six pages and the rest of it is unopened."@en .

pmt:BooksOfJeu a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Die Bücher des Jeu"@de,
        "The Books of Jeu"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The First Book of Jeu, chapters 1-10 (trans. V. MacDermot), in the excerpt published by the Gnostic Society Library" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission in the Bruce Codex in which the living Jesus sets out the emanation of the treasuries of the light, each rank with its name, its character, its three watchers at the gate and its twelve emanations, performs four rites on the disciples, and then hands over, station by station, the seal, the name and the cipher by which each aeon of the ascent is said to be passed."@en ;
    skos:note "A limit of the medium, not of the text, and it decides what this file can hold. The seals are drawn and not transliterated: Schmidt prints the figure and then gives its name and its interpretation as two strings, so a node can carry the two names and never the figure. Where the figure stood alone the printed page carries a blot and this file carries nothing. Every claim about a drawn seal in this corpus is, from this witness, a claim about something reproduced as a picture."@en,
        "And from the ascent the passage form at the first two aeons, the tenth to the fourteenth aeon and the closing page, manuscript pages 76 to 86, that is pages 213 to 215, 219, 220 and 223."@en,
        "Edition: Schmidt 1892, the German translation, read page by page in the Munich digitisation; the manuscript pagination Schmidt prints in the margin is given with every source, so that the place is findable in MacDermot's English and in Crégheur's French as well. Number of citable passages: the second book is dense and the first is mostly diagrams."@en,
        "The chapter numbers by which this corpus is usually cited are not in the edition read. Schmidt introduced them in 1905, in the Griechische christliche Schriftsteller volume, expressly to make citation easier; the 1892 translation has no chapters and carries the manuscript pagination in the margin instead. The sources below therefore cite the manuscript page and the page of the 1892 translation, and no chapter."@en,
        "Three decisions before the first node, restated because the edition changed. Route of contact: documented and dense — the corpus is cited by name in the Pistis Sophia, twice and with Enoch as its scribe, and stands in the same Coptic-Egyptian transmission as the ophite and hermetic material this graph already holds, so nothing here counts as an independent witness against them."@en,
        "What was not read, and it is not little: the diagram chapters of the first book, manuscript pages 5 to 34; the hymn fragment at 35 to 38; the treasury passages between 39 and 43; the ascent through the taxeis at manuscript pages 70 to 76 except its opening; the third to the ninth aeon; the appendix at 87 and 88. The nine nodes that stood here before this run were read in an online excerpt of MacDermot's English and keep that citation."@en,
        "What was read on 2026-09-27, in Schmidt's German translation of 1892. From the first book the treasury passages and the great name, manuscript pages 39 and 43 to 47, that is pages 179 and 183 to 186. From the second book the command of secrecy, the announcement of the rites, the ethical commands, the mystery of the forgiveness of sins, the three baptisms, the mystery against the wickedness of the archons and the recapitulation, manuscript pages 55 to 70, that is pages 194 to 208."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Books of Jeu and the Untitled Text in the Bruce Codex, ed. Carl Schmidt, trans. Violet MacDermot, Nag Hammadi Studies 13; Carl Schmidt, Gnostische Schriften in koptischer Sprache aus dem Codex Brucianus (1892)" .

pmt:HippocraticMedicine a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Hippokratische Medizin"@de,
        "Hippocratic medicine"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, Aphorisms VI.18, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. II, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849" ;
    skos:definition "The Greek medical transmission of the collection that travels under the name of Hippocrates, taken here at the part of it that states outcomes: which conditions and injuries are said to be fatal, which signs are said to decide a case, and at what term, if any, the outcome is said to arrive."@en ;
    skos:note "A correction to the run of 2026-10-02, and it is the kind that costs a later reader an hour. The note above says the Prognostics and On Wounds in the Head stand in the same two volumes, which is true, but the work notes below had left a reader to guess which; both are in volume I and only the Aphorisms are in volume II. Volume II of this translation is also the copy whose running-head digits over Section VI are noise, while volume I in the copy read on 2026-10-03 has a monotone head sequence over both treatises, so page numbers are used below and were not used above."@en,
        "Read at the wording in the run of 2026-10-02: Aphorisms, Section VI, all sixty aphorisms, in the English of Francis Adams, vol. II, London 1849. Not read then: Sections I to V and VII of the same Aphorisms, which stand in the same volume and were opened only far enough to establish where Section VI begins and ends; and the rest of the collection, among it the Prognostics, On Wounds in the Head, On the Articulations and the Epidemics, which the same two volumes carry. The works below say which of them were opened."@en,
        "Read at the wording in the run of 2026-10-03: The Book of Prognostics, chapters 1 to 25, and On Injuries of the Head, chapters 1 to 21, both continuously, in the English of Francis Adams, vol. I, London 1849, pp. 234-256 and 445-466, over the archive.org full text genuineworksofhi01hippuoft; the Greek of the Prognostic alongside from the Loeb volume hippocrates02hippuoft. Two of the three named works are now read and the third, the Aphorisms, is read in one section of eight. The coverage state stays at places entered: a collection of some sixty treatises of which three chapters-worth has been opened is not a harvested corpus, and the two treatises added here are the two that were already named as identifiers rather than two chosen freely."@en,
        "The commentary is not the text. Adams prints Galen's remarks under most aphorisms and sometimes his own judgment on whether the sentence still holds; at VI.18 Galen is reported as saying that wounds of the brain, liver, bowels and bladder are not necessarily fatal and as citing lithotomy against the bladder. That is a later station qualifying the claim, it is reported here and not entered as a node, and the reason is that Galen was read through Adams and not opened. A run that wants the qualification as a node opens the commentary."@en,
        "What kind of witness this is. The Aphorisms are a list of sentences with no argument between them, assembled from the other treatises of the collection and transmitted as a set to be learnt; Adams's own preliminary discourse in volume I treats the eighth section as not belonging to the rest. A sentence here is therefore a claim that the transmission thought worth fixing in a form that could be memorised, which is a different thing from a claim it thought worth demonstrating, and nothing in this file should be read as the latter."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:AphorismiHippocratis,
        pmw:DeVulneribusCapitis,
        pmw:PrognosticonHippocratis ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AphorismiHippocratis ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Corpus Hippocraticum: the Aphorisms; the Prognostics; On Wounds in the Head; On the Articulations; the Epidemics; and the remaining treatises of the collection" .

pmt:HittiteRitual a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Hethitisch-anatolisches Ritual"@de,
        "Hittite and Anatolian ritual"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Maštigga ritual against domestic quarrel, CTH 404.1.II, KBo 2.3+ Vs. I 1-4 (critical edition, exemplar A, hethiter.net/: CTH 404.1.II, Expl. A, textus 2012-05-07)" ;
    skos:definition "Anatolian purification and substitution rituals transmitted under the names of the practitioners who dictated them, most of them women."@en ;
    skos:note "A second route question the reading opened and did not close, and it bears on the strand that was run beside this one. The Ugaritic expiation text RS 1.002 names the Hittite among the parties whose statement may have caused the sin it expiates. That establishes contact between the two corpora and nothing about transmission of technique; but it is enough that a convergence asserted between pmt:HittiteRitual and pmt:UgariticRitual may not be entered as pm:independentAttestation without a pm:independenceGround that addresses it. See traditions/ugaritic-ritual.ttl."@en,
        "The registration's clause that most of the practitioners are women held for four of the five works read, and the fifth is the case that shows what the clause rests on. The two manuscripts of the ritual of Ḫantitaššu disagree about it inside a single line: the exemplar made for general use writes the female determinative before the name and then the sign for man after it, while the exemplar the editors identify as made for the king writes the female determinative and then the sign for woman. The editors of the critical edition state the divergence. The clause is therefore kept as the registration wrote it, and the one case against it is entered at pmc:HittKolophonHantitassuDisputatum rather than smoothed away."@en,
        "What the reading added to the contact route, which stays disputed. The practitioners are named in the texts themselves as foreign to the capital, and in two directions at once: Maštigga is the woman of Kizzuwatna in her incipit and the woman of Kummanni in her colophon, which is the cult centre of that same south-eastern land; Paškuwatti is the woman of Arzawa who dwells in Parašša, and Uḫḫamuwa is the man of Arzawa, which lies to the west. Ḫantitaššu is of Ḫurma. So the corpus records its own rituals as imported from both ends of Anatolia, and that is a datum for the dispute rather than a resolution of it: it establishes that the texts present themselves as received, and leaves open whether the technique came with the name. The position that would settle it asks of each proposed Hurrian or Mesopotamian element whether it is specific, exceptional and co-occurrent with others; that position was reached in the landscape stage of this run and not read at the wording, so it is not asserted here."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:Ambazzi,
        pmw:Anniwiyani,
        pmw:Hantitassu,
        pmw:Mastigga,
        pmw:Paskuwatti,
        pmw:Tunnawiya,
        pmw:Uhhamuwa ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Mastigga ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The CTH ritual corpus, including the rituals of Tunnawiya (CTH 409), Maštigga (CTH 404), Ḫantitaššu (CTH 395), Paškuwatti (CTH 406), Uḫḫamuwa (CTH 410), Ambazzi (CTH 391) and Anniwiyani (CTH 393)" .

pmt:LatinAlchemy a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Lateinische Alchemie"@de,
        "Latin alchemy"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 39 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)",
        "Turba philosophorum, Dictum 9 (trans. Waite 1896, longer recension)" ;
    skos:definition "The western alchemical transmission, in which the stages of the work are described at once as operations and as states of the operator."@en ;
    skos:note "The double reading is not a modern imposition and not a settled fact either. It is a dispute the corpus carries in itself and the graph should record as one. The work read here is evidence on both sides in one volume: it prescribes weights, vessels, fires and day-counts for matter that can be handled, and it makes the whole outcome depend on the operator's patience, his freedom from haste, and God's willingness to show him the stone."@en,
        "The shape of the work decides how it may be cited. It is a staged colloquy: seventy-two speeches under the names of graecised presocratics, each answered by the assembly, with the operative instruction embedded in the speech. There is no continuous procedure and no chapter apparatus beyond the numbering of the dicta, so the dictum is the smallest unit a later reader can find, and it is the unit used in every source below."@en,
        "What was read: the Turba philosophorum in Waite's translation of 1896, complete — the epistle of Arisleus, all seventy-two dicta, and the conclusion of Agmon that stands as the last dictum of the shorter recension. The editorial preface and the footnote apparatus were read as apparatus and carry nothing here. Nothing of the Rosarium philosophorum and nothing of the Aurora consurgens is opened."@en,
        "Why it is not an independent witness, stated at the registration and unchanged. The compiler worked in Arabic or Hebrew out of Greek material, the translation used here prints the Greek parallels alongside, and the transmission from there to the Latin collections is not in dispute. What the work can carry is the other thing SPEC §14 asks for, and it carries a great deal of it: terms in days, cautions with named consequences, and prerequisites whose omission the text says makes the work come to nothing."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:AuroraConsurgens,
        pmw:RosariumPhilosophorum,
        pmw:SummaPerfectionisMagisterii,
        pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Turba philosophorum; Rosarium philosophorum; Aurora consurgens" .

pmt:Mesmerism a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Mesmerismus"@de,
        "Mesmerism"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. I, pp. 21-27" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of an animal magnetism held to pass between bodies and to be directed by the will of an operator through passes of the hand, together with the induced states its practitioners described: the crisis, the magnetic sleep, and the somnambulism in which the subject is held to perceive the seat of his own disease."@en ;
    skos:note "A second work of the tradition was opened for one question and is entered accordingly. Of Teste's manual in Spillan's English of 1843, the section on the number and the hour of the sittings was read at the wording; the rest of that volume was not, and pmc:HuitJoursConsecutifs is the only node drawn from it. It is named here rather than left to the node, because a file that carries two works must say how much of each it has seen."@en,
        "Read in this run at the wording: Deleuze's manual of 1825 in Hartshorn's English of 1843, chapters I, II, III, V, VI and VIII, that is the principles, the processes, the effects and their indications, somnambulism, the choice of an operator, and the dangers. Left unread in that volume: chapter IV on accessory means, chapter VII on the application to particular diseases, chapter IX on developing the faculty in oneself, chapter X on the studies, and the whole of the appendices."@en,
        "The contact route stands as documented and this is not a formality. It runs in both directions and both are stated inside the corpus. Backwards: the operation is the laying on of hands with a theory attached, the 1784 commission's own historical introduction traces the doctrine to physicians of the preceding age, and Deleuze names Mesmer and Puységur as his predecessors and takes the whole of his doctrine from the latter. Forwards is the heavier one: this is the transmission out of which the nineteenth-century hypnotism literature and, through it, a great part of what now circulates as the ancient doctrine of older traditions came. A parallel between this tradition and a later one is reception until shown otherwise."@en,
        "The edition question has to be settled before any citation from this work can be read, and it is the reason every source line below carries a chapter and a page. Hartshorn's revised edition of 1843 prints Deleuze to page 216 and then an Appendix and a New Appendix of the translator's own notes and of letters from American correspondents, which are not Deleuze, are forty years later, and are editorialised by Hartshorn inside their own pages. Nothing below is drawn from beyond page 216."@en,
        "The translation is named in each source line for the same reason. Deleuze wrote in French in 1825; what was read is Hartshorn's English. Where the wording of a stopping rule decides a node — the five days, the three or four sittings, the nearly a month — a reader who wants to press it should set the French first edition beside this, and that collation has not been made here."@en,
        "What this tradition is good for in this holding, and it is unusual: the operator is not the subject. Almost everything else here has a practitioner who does something to himself and reports the result. Here one person acts and another reports, which makes the reports checkable in a way the rest are not — and makes the presence of a third person a question the transmission has to answer. It answers it, at length, and the answer is the material of generalizations/the-unsympathetic-witness.ttl."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Mesmer, Mémoire sur la découverte du magnétisme animal; Puységur, Mémoires pour servir à l'histoire du magnétisme animal; Deleuze, Instruction pratique sur le magnétisme animal" .

pmt:MesopotamianExorcism a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Mesopotamische Beschwörungskunst"@de,
        "Mesopotamian exorcistic craft"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Udug-hul (canonical Utukku Lemnutu) Tablets 1, 3 and 12, and KAR 31 (VAT 8803), in M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons: Canonical Udug-hul Incantations (Die babylonisch-assyrische Medizin in Texten und Untersuchungen 8, Boston/Berlin: De Gruyter 2016)" ;
    skos:definition "The scribal craft of the āšipu: a trained office with its own curriculum, treating affliction as an assault to be countered by named procedure."@en ;
    skos:note "Read for the first time in two modern critical editions, both open access and both read at the wording of the English translation printed beside the text. First, the complete edition of the canonical series Udug-hul / Utukku Lemnutu in all sixteen tablets: Chapter One on the ritual content of the incantations, Chapter Three on the exorcist as diagnostician with its edition of KAR 31, and the partitur of Tablets 1, 3 and 12 at the places entered here. Second, the edition of the exorcist's manual KAR 44 in the volume of scholarly text catalogues, read in full from line 1 to the colophons."@en,
        "The contact route stays disputed and the reading did not move it. The registration argued the dispute from the age of the corpus alone, which settles the direction of any derivation without settling whether there was one. The position the graph would need in order to decide it is the one that asks of each proposed borrowing whether it is specific, exceptional and co-occurrent with others, and reports that most claimed dependencies of the Greek magical papyri on this material fail those tests; that position was reached in the landscape stage of this run and not read at the wording, so it is named in an issue and not asserted here."@en,
        "The oldest procedural corpus in writing anywhere, and older than every tradition the graph currently holds. Whatever it shares with the Greek and Jewish material is therefore a question of derivation running one way only."@en,
        "What was not read, and it is the larger part. Of the principal corpus, Maqlu and Surpu were not opened: the standard editions of both are the ones a reader would have to be given, and neither is open access, while the nineteenth-century editions that are — Tallqvist for Maqlu, Zimmern for Surpu — are superseded and would enter the graph as an edition nobody now cites. The Diagnostic Handbook, which is where a prognosis in this craft would stand, was not opened either and for the same reason; only its second tablet's colophon is entered here, and that one at second hand. Ludlul bel nemeqi and Enuma Anu Enlil were not opened. Of the work that was opened, the partitur of Tablets 2, 4 to 11 and 13 to 16 was read only where a search led and not line by line. The whole of it was searched, which is a different claim and is made as such at the two search nodes."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Maqlû; Šurpu; the exorcist's manual KAR 44; Ludlul bēl nēmeqi; Enūma Anu Enlil" .

pmw:BaopuziNeipian a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian"@de,
        "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian"@en ;
    skos:note "Twenty juan, DZ 1185 in the Zhengtong Daozang, read in the Hanfenlou facsimile. Five chapters are now read at the wording: 4 on the golden elixir, 8 on releasing the obstruction, 17 on climbing into the mountains, 18 on the earthly truth, and 9 on the intention of the Way, read on 2026-09-23. Of chapter 8 a part was read in an earlier run and the remainder of that chapter is still owed. Fifteen chapters of the inner book and the whole of the outer book are searched by term only; the harvest still owed is recorded at prima-materia#633."@en .

pmt:Rosicrucian a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Rosenkreuzerische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Rosicrucian transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Fama Fraternitatis, in the English of Thomas Vaughan, The Fame and Confession of the Fraternity of R: C: (London, 1652), the six articles of agreement" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission begun by three anonymous works printed between 1614 and 1616, which announce an order, a corpus and a set of capacities, and which state at every point of admission what befalls the applicant who is not fit for it."@en ;
    skos:note "One consequence of the edition situation should be stated where a reader will meet it. The Chymische Hochzeit exists in three incompatible paginations — the Strasbourg print of 1616 to p. 143, the Foxcroft English of 1690 to p. 193, and the modern reset to p. 88 — so every source in this file locates by day first and by page of a named edition second. The last page of the work is a printer's note that about two leaves in quarto are missing, and the narrator's fate is therefore reported in a bracket and not by the narrator."@en,
        "Read at the wording on 2026-09-30, and the editions are named because they differ in a way that decides what may be cited. The Chymische Hochzeit was read complete, all seven days, in the modern reset English printed as The Chymical Wedding of Christian Rosenkreutz (archive.org identifier ChymicalWeddingOfChristianRosenkreutz), and every passage a node rests on was then checked against the German original, Chymische Hochzeit: Christiani Rosencreutz. Anno 1459 (Strasbourg: Lazarus Zetzner, 1616), read in the Internet Archive scan chymischehochzei00rose. The Fama and the Confessio were read complete in the English of Thomas Vaughan, The Fame and Confession of the Fraternity of R: C: (London, 1652), in the proofread Wikisource transcription of that print. A. E. Waite's Real History of the Rosicrucians of 1887 was deliberately not used as the witness for any node: Waite states at his chapter four that his Fama and Confessio are a searching revision of Vaughan rather than a translation, and at his chapter five that his Chymische Hochzeit is compressed by the omission of all irrelevant matter and dispensable prolixities. A compressed text cannot carry an absence."@en,
        "The contact route stays at documented and the reading did not move it. These works name their own antecedents in their own voice: the wise of Damascus and of Fez, the Book M, Theophrastus by name and with the statement that he was not of the Fraternity but had read the Book M, Adam, Moses and Solomon as holders of the same philosophy, Plato, Aristotle and Pythagoras as partial hitters of the mark. A transmission that lists its sources is not an independent witness to anything they carry, and no node here is offered for a convergence."@en,
        "The corpus is dated earlier than its printing and the difference matters for any claim of independence. Adam Haslmayr had the Fama in manuscript in 1610 and printed his answer to it in 1612, two years before the Fama itself; Benedictus Figulus had a copy in the same year. Both facts are taken from de Vries at second hand for the reason given in the note above and were not read at the page; unlike the judgement about the authorship they are not contested, and the Haslmayr print of 1612 is itself the primary witness for them, unopened here. So the text is at latest of 1610, which puts it before every printed response to it and therefore before the reception this holding would otherwise have to discount."@en,
        "What kind of witness these works are, and it has to be said before any node is read. All three are anonymous, none reports a practice the writer says he carried out, and the third is narrated as a dream inside a dream. The attribution is contested in the current literature rather than settled, and that it is contested is all this file asserts. The reference for the state of the question is Lyke de Vries, Reformation, Revolution, Renovation (Leiden: Brill, 2021), and the reference is second-hand: the work was not opened. Its Open Access copy at OAPEN answered every address tried with a proof-of-work challenge, one of them with a status of 200 and the challenge page in the body, and the finding is at prima-materia#814. What is reported of it — that it calls the authorship a highly contested topic, postpones the question programmatically, heads its third chapter Authorship in Question, and uses the plural the authors throughout — comes from a research report and not from the page, and the page numbers that report gave are deliberately not repeated here. No node of this file rests on de Vries: it is neither a dcterms:source nor a pm:evidenceFrom, and if it were, the reading would not be finished. Johann Valentin Andreae claimed only the third work, and called it a ludibrium. This file therefore enters no pm:Attributing node for any of the three: an ascription to Andreae or to the Tübingen circle is a scholarly position and belongs at a protocol or in an issue."@en,
        "What was not read. The Allgemeine Reformation printed with the Fama in 1614, which is a translation of Boccalini and stands in front of it, was not opened. The Latin Confessio of 1615 was not compared with the German. Andreae's Vita ab ipso conscripta, which is where the word ludibrium stands, was not opened; it is named in this file only as reported by de Vries. The critical edition of the corpus, Johann Valentin Andreae, Gesammelte Schriften, vol. 3, Rosenkreuzerschriften, ed. Roland Edighoffer (Stuttgart: frommann-holzboog, 2010), with its variant apparatus and its commentary volume 3.1, was not obtained; where this file records a reading of the 1616 print it means that print and not the critical text."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:ConfessioFraternitatis,
        pmw:FamaFraternitatis,
        pmw:NuptiaeChymicae,
        pmw:ReformatioGeneralis ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:FamaFraternitatis ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Fama Fraternitatis; Confessio Fraternitatis; Chymische Hochzeit Christiani Rosencreutz" .

pmw:MegasLogosKataMysterion a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Das Buch vom grossen Logos kata mysterion"@de,
        "The Book of the Great Discourse according to the Mystery"@en ;
    skos:note "The title the manuscript itself carries, printed after the closing hymn of what Schmidt divided as the first book. Schmidt made two books of it and the whole of the modern literature followed him; the corpus is therefore cited as the two Books of Jeu by almost everyone who cites it. One work identifier is used here because the manuscript gives one title, and because the division is contested: Crégheur's edition of the Bruce Codex separates three tracts where Schmidt saw two books plus an introduction."@en,
        "Whichever division wins, the passages cited below are the same passages, and the marginal manuscript pagination Schmidt prints is what makes them findable in any of them. The decision between one identifier and three is laid out with options and a recommendation in prima-materia#798."@en .

pmw:TurbaPhilosophorum a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Turba philosophorum"@de,
        "Turba philosophorum"@en ;
    skos:note "Dating and ascription. The translator states on his first page that the work is the oldest extant Latin treatise on alchemy and was not written in Latin, and that its compiler wrote in Arabic or Hebrew. The literature puts the Arabic composition in the ninth century in Egypt and disputes when the Latin translation was made; that dispute was not settled in this run and nothing here turns on the date. The ascription of the speeches to Iximidrus, Anaxagoras, Pythagoras, Socrates, Plato, Democritus and the rest is a device of the compilation and is not treated here as an attribution; no pm:Attributing node is written on it."@en,
        "Read here complete in Arthur Edward Waite, The Turba Philosophorum, or Assembly of the Sages, London: George Redway 1896, which translates the longer of the two Latin recensions and gives the shorter one's readings in notes. Seventy-two dicta plus the epistle of Arisleus and the conclusion of Agmon."@en,
        "The Latin is reachable and was not read. The work stands in the Artis auriferae collections of 1572, 1593 and 1610 and in Manget's Bibliotheca chemica curiosa of 1702, several of them digitised and freely readable; the 1610 Basel print carries the Turba twice, in both recensions, which makes it the cheapest place to settle the two divergences above. That the Latin was not read is a debt of this run."@en,
        "The two recensions diverge in ways that touch the operations, not only the wording, and two of the divergences are recorded in the holding. At Dictum 69 the longer recension says of the redness hidden in the whiteness that it does not behove the operator to extract it and the shorter says that it does — two opposite instructions at the same place, carried as pmc:TurbaRubedoExtrahenda. At Dictum 16 the washing is twenty-one days with dew and rain, ten with salt and ten with fresh water in the longer recension, and twenty-nine, twenty and ten in the shorter. A run that wants to decide either has to go to the Latin."@en .

pm:Generalizing a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Verallgemeinern"@de,
        "Generalizing"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "The act of forming, from several attested claims, one statement that no source makes: a claim about a kind of practice or a kind of effect, worded so that an outcome could contradict it."@en ;
    skos:note "It carries a derivation instead of an origin, and the derivation is checkable in the same way a source is. pm:generalizedFrom points at the attested nodes it was formed from, so a reader can go back to the passages and disagree with the step rather than with the conclusion alone."@en,
        "No instance may carry dcterms:source or pm:withinTradition. A generalization that named a work would report as transmitted what no transmission says, and that is the one confusion this layer exists to prevent."@en,
        "That is also the whole safeguard against the direction the project must not run in. An unpublished draft has no attested predecessors: a sentence from a method draft cannot be generalized into the graph, because there is nothing for pm:generalizedFrom to point at. The class is therefore open where pm:Positing had to be locked, and locked where pm:Positing is open."@en,
        "The absence of a pm:Testing pointing at a generalization is not a defect and is deliberately not enforced. It is the queue: the generalizations nobody has taken up are exactly the ones a run should reach for, and a shape that required a protocol would make the queue unwritable, as it does for pm:Positing."@en .

pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Chinesischer medizinischer Kanon"@de,
        "Chinese medical canon"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 52 (Ci jin lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 14" ;
    skos:definition "The canonical Chinese transmission of needling, of the qi and the vessels, and of the spirits held to reside in the depots, set out as dialogues between the Yellow Lord and his interlocutors and carrying, alongside the doctrine, its own prohibitions, its own intervals to death and its own signs by which an operation is said to have taken."@en ;
    skos:note "Chosen from the registry on the independence entrance, and the reading corrected that entrance. The contact route is entered as documented and no longer as absent, and the route runs to a transmission this graph already holds. The received text is not the Han-period text but the recension Wang Bing completed in seven hundred and sixty-two as the editors of ten sixty-seven passed it on, and that recension is constituted by its commentary: the commentary printed with the opening chapter quotes the Laozi twice and names the Heshanggong commentary on it, and the commentary elsewhere in the same juan quotes the Zhuangzi. The Laozi and the Zhuangzi are the corpus the Daoist file in this graph rests part of its material on. An agreement between this file and that one is therefore not two witnesses at the level of the transmitted text, whatever may hold of the stratum beneath it."@en,
        "Edition note. Both classics were read in the Sibu congkan facsimiles, which is the received text and not a critical edition: for the Suwen the Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen with Wang Bing's commentary and the Song collation notes, for the Lingshu the Huangdi suwen lingshu jing. The digitisation used names the collection and not the printing within it, and the citations say so by naming the collection. Where a reading is contested the contest is entered as a node and not smoothed into the citation. The Nanjing was read in the Sibu congkan facsimile of Wang Hanlin's collected commentaries on the Classic of Eighty-One Difficulties, which prints the text together with the notes of Ding Deyong, Yang Xuancao and Yu Shu; where a node rests on the commentary rather than on the text, it says so. One Lingshu chapter had to be read in a second edition because the Sibu congkan digitisation breaks off inside it, and that citation names the other edition."@en,
        "Read at the wording, in the Chinese, on 2026-09-10, in addition to the passages of the first run: Suwen 13, 19, 20, 23, 25 and 64; Lingshu 10, 32, 60, 61, 70 and 78; the Lingshu chapter on examining the forearm, in a second edition for the reason given at the node that rests on it; and Nanjing 20 and 61 with the commentary apparatus printed with them. The Nanjing enters this file with those two places and had until then been searched by term only."@en,
        "Read in the first run at the wording, in the Chinese: Suwen 1, 14, 16, 26, 52 and 54, and Lingshu 8, 9 and 73, each in the juan named in the citations below. The Song editors' collation notes printed with Suwen 16 and Suwen 52 were read with them and carry the dispute node in this file. The parallel in the Zhenjiu jiayi jing was opened for the same passage and is cited as the second witness there. The remainder of both classics, and the whole of the Nanjing, were searched by term and not read continuously; what that turned up and did not verify stands in the issues named in the harvest note, not here."@en,
        "The correction is narrower than the value can express, and the difference matters for any convergence built on this file. What is documented is a route at the point where the corpus was fixed in the form read, in the eighth century and again in the eleventh. Whether the first-century material that the compilation rests on had such a route is a separate question, and it is not decided here; the predicate is single-valued and cannot hold both answers. The corresponding question for the Indian and the Greek material was searched and stands as its own issue."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Huangdi neijing (Suwen and Lingshu); Nanjing; Huangfu Mi, Zhenjiu jiayi jing; Yang Shangshan, Huangdi neijing taisu" .

pm:Attributing a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Zuschreiben"@de,
        "Attributing"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "The act of ascribing a capacity, property, or deed to a subject. A lost faculty is never a finding; it is an ascription made in a work, and the work is what the graph can hold."@en .

pm:mediatedAttestation a pm:Attesting ;
    rdfs:label "Vermitteltes Zeugnis"@de,
        "Mediated attestation"@en ;
    skos:definition "The claim stands in a received work at a passage that can be named, but the passage was read in a second work that quotes or reports it, because the first could not be obtained."@en ;
    skos:note "Admissible only where the work itself is out of reach — not digitised, out of print, held internally, in a language not commanded. Effort is not a reason: the mode exists so that unreachable material can enter at its true strength, not so that reachable material can enter cheaply. See SPEC §10 and SPEC §15."@en,
        "Every claim carrying this mode must name the intermediary with pm:readVia, enforced by pm:MediatedAttestationShape. dcterms:source keeps naming the work that carries the claim, because that is what a later reader must obtain; pm:readVia names what the writer of the node actually had in front of him. Collapsing the two would hide exactly the fact this mode exists to record."@en,
        "Weaker than pm:textualAttestation and stronger than nothing, and the record has to be able to say which of the two it has. A claim read through an intermediary carries that intermediary's selection, its translation and its silence; a reader who cannot see that the passage came second-hand cannot weigh any of it."@en .

pmt:RabbinicHalakhah a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Rabbinische halachische Ueberlieferung"@de,
        "Rabbinic halakhic transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Mishnah Sanhedrin VII.11, trans. Herbert Danby, Tractate Sanhedrin: Mishnah and Tosefta (Translations of Early Documents III, Rabbinic Texts; London: Society for Promoting Christian Knowledge, 1919), pp. 106-107" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of Jewish law in the Mishnah, the Tosefta and the two Talmuds, which legislates on sorcery, grades it, and applies criteria to a claimed performance in order to decide a case."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at places read and not registered first, because the passages were opened in the same run in which the tradition was decided on. The three decisions SPEC section 14 requires before the first node were made and are these. The contact route is documented and dense: this transmission and the operative Jewish material already in the holding share a language, a region and several centuries, and the handbook whose counter-transmission this is was composed, on every dating anyone now defends, after the Mishnah was fixed and while the Babylonian discussion was being redacted. The recension and edition are Danby for the Mishnah and the Tosefta and Goldschmidt for the Babylonian Talmud, both named at the works. The number of citable places is eleven, in three works, all read at the wording in this run."@en,
        "Three further tractates were added to the corpus on 2026-10-04 and the places read in them stand in traditions/rabbinic-observed-success.ttl, which is the second file of this tradition. They are the three places this entry named as unopened and left to the next run. What they establish is a side of this transmission that tractate Sanhedrin does not show: Sanhedrin argues whether the art works and never about a case anybody watched, while Aboda zara, Holin and Pesahim take up successes that were seen and never once deny them."@en,
        "What was read at the wording: Mishnah Sanhedrin VII.11 and Tosefta Sanhedrin XI.5c in Danby, pages 106 and 107, with the title pages; and the Babylonian Talmud Synhedrin from folio 67a to folio 68a in Goldschmidt, pages 285 to 290, with the key to his sigla. What was not opened: the Palestinian Talmud at this place, the Tosefta in Hebrew, Sifre Deuteronomium 170, which is where the permission to learn originates, Strack's edition of the Mishnah with its apparatus, and the places of this transmission outside tractate Sanhedrin that bear on the subject, of which the proven amulet at Sabbath 61a and the ash strewn around the bed at Berakhoth 6a are the two the next run should take first. They are named in the harvest note and in the issues of this run and in no node."@en,
        "Whether this transmission belongs in the holding as a tradition is a decision of the human and is put to him, with options and a recommendation, in the pull request of this run. The functional test of SPEC section 14 is what it was entered on: a tradition delivers testimony, a place where somebody says what is the case, and this transmission does that about sorcery at length. It is not entered as an authority over the corpora it condemns."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:MishnahSanhedrin,
        pmw:TalmudBavliAbodaZara,
        pmw:TalmudBavliHolin,
        pmw:TalmudBavliPesahim,
        pmw:TalmudBavliSanhedrin,
        pmw:ToseftaSanhedrin ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MishnahSanhedrin ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Mishnah, tractate Sanhedrin; Tosefta, tractate Sanhedrin; Babylonian Talmud, tractate Sanhedrin; Palestinian Talmud, tractate Sanhedrin; Sifre Deuteronomium" .

pmt:Tiantai a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Tiantai"@de,
        "Tiantai"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0462b, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" ;
    skos:definition "The Chinese transmission of calming and contemplation, with a systematic account of the obstacles, illnesses and demonic states that arise in practice."@en ;
    skos:note "Read in this run at the wording, from the preface of Yuanzhao of 1095 to the last line: Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao, also called Tongmeng zhiguan and Xiao zhiguan, T46 no. 1915, in the Taishō text as the CBETA edition carries it. All ten chapters were read: the five outward conditions, the reproach of the five desires, the discarding of the five coverings, the regulating of the five matters, the five expedients, the main practice, the arising of the good roots, the recognition of demonic affairs, the curing of illness and the final realization. Nothing of this work was left unread."@en,
        "The English of Lu Kuan Yu (Charles Luk), The Secrets of Chinese Meditation, London: Rider, 1964, chapter 4, was read beside the Chinese and used as a control on the clauses this file turns on. It is one translator and not two: the two-translation requirement of SPEC §15 is not met for this work and the reason is given at the search node in examinations/searches-tiantai.ttl."@en,
        "The contact route stays at documented and this run did not change it. The work quotes the Lotus Sūtra, the Mahāprajñāpāramitā treatise, the Daśabhūmika, the Awakening of Faith and a Miaosheng ding jing by name, and takes the four breath marks and the five good roots from the meditation literature it inherits; nothing here is proposed as an independent witness. What the reading did establish is that the three clauses at the end of the healing chapter are this work speaking in its own voice and not a quotation, because the quotations in it are marked and these are not."@en,
        "What kind of witness this work is. It is a manual written by the founder of the school for his lay elder brother, and it reads as instructions to a named beginner: it says what to do, what will go wrong, what that will feel like in the body, and what to do then. That form is the reason it carries what this holding is short of. It also means it reports no case: in the whole work there is not one instance of a person who did these things and what came of it."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:MoheZhiguan,
        pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusHarvested ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Zhiyi, Mohe zhiguan; Zhiyi, Xiao zhiguan" .

pm:searchFoundPlace a pm:SearchOutcome ;
    rdfs:label "Suche mit Fund"@de,
        "Search found a place"@en ;
    skos:definition "A passage was found and entered. The node it became is named with pm:searchYieldedNode, so that a claimed find is checkable against the holding."@en .

pmw:XiaoZhiguan a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao, die kleine Abhandlung über Ruhe und Schau"@de,
        "Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao, the small treatise on calming and contemplation"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened and read entire on 2026-10-10 in the Taishō text, T46 no. 1915, pages 462a to 473a, as the CBETA edition of that text carries it. One juan, ten chapters, with the preface Yuanzhao of Yuhang wrote for the printing of 1095. The preface is itself a source for the work's standing: it names the four treatises on calming and contemplation of the Tiantai master and says this is the fourth of them, drawn up for the master's lay elder brother Chen Zhen as the outline of the large one."@en .

pmt:ArsNotoria a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Ars Notoria"@de,
        "Ars Notoria"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ars Notoria: The Notory Art of Solomon, trans. Robert Turner (London: J. Cottrel, 1657), in the digital edition of Joseph H. Peterson" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of a set of orations, said on prescribed days of the moon and at prescribed hours, held to confer the liberal arts, eloquence, memory and understanding within a stated term, and bound to conditions of solitude, silence, fasting and freedom from crime."@en ;
    skos:note "Read for the first time in Robert Turner's English translation, London 1657, in the digital edition of Joseph H. Peterson, which carries the printed pagination and the Latin of the Agrippa text beside it; the wording of every passage cited here was additionally checked against the optical text of the 1657 printing itself. The critical Latin edition of Véronèse was not opened."@en,
        "The second work of this corpus has since been opened and stands in traditions/liber-iuratus-honorii.ttl: the Liber iuratus Honorii in the sixteenth-century English of Royal MS 17 A. xlii and in the Latin of Sloane MS 3854 as Boudet edited it in 2002. It answers the question prima-materia#640 left open about this genre, and it answers it against the first work: where the Ars Notoria names a term and no sign, the second work names a term, a verdict delivered on a named night, and two visible penalties on the operator."@en,
        "The version read is incomplete in a way that bears on every claim in this file. Both Turner's translation and the Latin of the Agrippa printing omit the notae, the figures which the text itself calls the centre of the art; the orations are given and the drawings they accompany are not. What was read is therefore the verbal half of a procedure whose other half is missing, and no claim entered here can be carried out from this witness alone."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:ArsNotoria,
        pmw:LiberIuratusHonorii ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Ars Notoria in the medieval recensions; Liber Juratus Honorii" .

pmt:Daoist a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Daoistische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Daoist tradition"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Xingqi yupei ming (jade inscription on circulating the qi)" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of Chinese texts and practices concerned with the cultivation and circulation of qi."@en ;
    skos:note "Deliberately not documented. From the arrival of Buddhism in China the route is plain and the file's material lies mostly after it; for the earlier strata it is not, and the whole independence value of the Chinese material hangs on where that line falls."@en,
        "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. The citations of the jade inscription name the work they were read in. Three passages stood without an edition because it was no longer recoverable, and the file said so rather than guessing. Two of the three have since been reopened: Zhuangzi 4 was read at the wording in Legge's translation of eighteen ninety-one and now carries it, and the reopening turned up three renderings of the term the passage ends on; and Baopuzi neipian 8 was read at the wording in the Daozang text on 2026-09-09, which corrected one reading and added ten nodes below. Chen Zhixu's Jindan dayao is still unopened, and for it the juan division is itself edition-dependent."@en,
        "Read at the wording on 2026-09-10, in the Chinese: Baopuzi neipian 17, on climbing into the mountains, and Baopuzi neipian 18, on the earthly truth, both in the Daozang text the citations name. What the two chapters gave stands at the nodes below and is listed in the note at the head of this file. Three of the twenty chapters of the inner book are now read at the wording; the remaining seventeen, and the whole of the outer book, are searched by term only, and the harvest still owed at the opened work is recorded at prima-materia#633."@en,
        "The file is no longer thin only by necessity. The eighth chapter of the Baopuzi, opened on 2026-09-09, gave what the earlier runs had gone looking for elsewhere: a list of what the circulation of the qi is said to accomplish, an external sign of the breathing, a condition under which the practice is said to have no benefit at all, two prohibitions with a stated consequence, and a passage in which the author sets his own tradition against the Zhuangzi this file also holds. What stays out is what stayed out before: the absorption of solar and stellar qi and the abstention from grain are at prima-materia#17, not here."@en,
        "The first note above counts two places that could be run down to a text naming its own words. It states the position of the run that wrote it and is left standing as that record; the holding now rests on the jade inscription, on Zhuangzi 4 and on three chapters of the Baopuzi neipian, and the thinness it reports is no longer the same thinness."@en,
        "Thin on purpose, and the thinness is the finding. Of the material this tradition is credited with, two places could be run down to a text that names its own words: the jade inscription, which is the earliest datable witness located for a deliberate circulation of qi through the body, and the eighth chapter of the Baopuzi, which states the manner of the breathing in terms an examination could take hold of. How early the inscription is remains contested, and the dispute node holds that. What is widely reported and was not run down to a citable passage stays out rather than being approximated: the absorption of solar, lunar and stellar qi (fuqi) and the abstention from grain (bigu) are recorded at prima-materia#17, not here."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:BaopuziNeipian,
        pmw:JindanDayao,
        pmw:XingqiYupeiMing,
        pmw:Zhuangzi ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XingqiYupeiMing ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Xingqi yupei ming, the jade inscription on circulating the qi; Zhuangzi; Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian; Chen Zhixu, Jindan dayao" .

pmt:Enochic a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Henochische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Enochic tradition"@en ;
    dcterms:source "G.W.E. Nickelsburg, 1 Enoch 1 (Hermeneia, 2001), introduction" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the Enoch literature: the Book of the Watchers, the Astronomical Book, the Book of Dreams, the Epistle and, in the Ethiopic canon alone, the Book of Parables."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "1 Enoch, in the Ethiopic text and in the Aramaic fragments from Qumrân Cave 4" .

pm:procedureWithoutCases a pm:ExaminationState ;
    rdfs:label "Verfahren ohne Fälle"@de,
        "Procedure without cases"@en ;
    skos:definition "A falsifying condition is stated and no case has yet been recorded against it."@en .

pmt:Thelema a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Thelema"@de,
        "Thelema"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Liber E vel Exercitiorum sub figura IX, chap. I, in The Equinox I,1 (London, March 1909), p. 25" ;
    skos:definition "Crowley's transmission of a received book, of the discovery of one's own will as the central task, and of a practice curriculum with a demand for recorded results."@en ;
    skos:note "Book 4, Part I was read in a text that reaches the first edition through a later one, and the nodes from it say so in the source. No scan of the Wieland print of 1912 was found; the one archive identifier offering that part returns an optical transcription in Cyrillic characters and is unusable. The part title also differs between the editions: the 1912 half-title reads Meditation, and Mysticism is the later name. A node citing Book 4 Part I Mysticism would be citing an edition that did not exist in 1912."@en,
        "Read in this run at the wording. The Equinox, volume one, numbers one to ten, London 1909 to 1913: Liber XXX Librae, Liber IX E vel Exercitiorum whole, Liber VI O vel Manus et Sagittae whole, Liber III vel Jugorum whole with its plate, Liber CCCXLI HHH whole, Liber XVI Turris vel Domus Dei whole, Liber CCVI RV vel Spiritus whole with its plate and the editorial note to it, Liber DXXXVI Batrachophrenoboocosmomachia whole, and A Syllabus of the Official Instructions of A∴A∴ Hitherto Published at number ten. Book 4, Part I: Meditation, chapters I, V, VI and VII. Not read at the wording: the second part of Book 4 beyond its preliminary matter, the whole of The Temple of Solomon the King, Liber CCCCXVIII, Liber XIII vel Graduum Montis Abiegni, Liber CCCCXII A vel Armorum, Liber CC Resh vel Helios, the Class A books, and the verse, prose and reviews that make up most of the ten numbers."@en,
        "The contact route stays documented and the reading did not move it. The route is in the corpus itself and needs no secondary literature: the course of reading at Liber E VII, 2 prescribes the Upanishads, the Bhagavadgītā, the Shiva Saṃhitā, the aphorisms of Patañjali, the Haṭhayogapradīpikā, Vivekananda's Raja Yoga, the Voice of the Silence, Lévi, the Abramelin book and the Goetia by name; Liber E III names four postures and Liber E IV the alternating nostril cycles; Liber CCVI names Pingala, Ida, Suṣumṇā and the Viśuddhi cakra; the fifth number prints a treatise by Ananda Metteyya in the same issue as Liber HHH; and the second and third numbers print the neophyte ritual of the Golden Dawn, which the fourth number's editorial defends having published. A transmission whose reading list, whose postures, whose breath vocabulary and whose ritual forms all run to corpora this holding already carries cannot be counted as an independent witness for anything they share."@en,
        "Two carriers were used for the Equinox and the reading rests on both. The first is the Celephais Press and Ordo Templi Orientis re-setting of the ten numbers, which reproduces the title pages, the running heads and the original pagination and states its own provenance in the text. The second is a page scan of number one. Where a wording matters it was collated between them; Liber E I, 7 reads intelligibly prepared in both, and the reading of Liber E III, 9 on the saucer of water is identical in both. The re-setting renders the arabic nine as g throughout, so a section number taken from it alone is not to be trusted, and every section number in this file was checked against its neighbours in the sequence."@en,
        "What the entry is for, and it is not the capacity list. This transmission is the one in the holding that makes the demand for a written record from inside itself, states a base rate against which a result is to be judged, publishes a negative assessment of a named practitioner, and then, in the same volumes, withdraws the testability it demanded. Both halves are entered. The withdrawal is not a later recantation and not the work of a critic: it stands three pages from the demand, in the same instruction."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:LiberABA,
        pmw:LiberALVelLegis,
        pmw:LiberBatrachophrenoboocosmomachia,
        pmw:LiberEVelExercitiorum,
        pmw:LiberGraduumMontisAbiegni,
        pmw:LiberHHH,
        pmw:LiberIIIVelJugorum,
        pmw:LiberOVelManusEtSagittae,
        pmw:LiberRVVelSpiritus,
        pmw:LiberTurrisVelDomusDei,
        pmw:LiberXXXAerum,
        pmw:MagickInTheoryAndPractice,
        pmw:SyllabusInstructionumAA ;
