pm:searchFoundNothing a pm:SearchOutcome ;
    rdfs:label "Suche ohne Fund"@de,
        "Search found nothing"@en ;
    skos:definition "The corpus was read for this question and carries no passage answering it. A statement about a search, and only as strong as the search the note describes."@en .

pmt:Rasashastra a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Rasaśāstra"@de,
        "Rasaśāstra"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi or Ocean of Indian Chemistry and Alchemy, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, introduction, pp. 3-4" ;
    skos:definition "The Indian alchemical transmission of mercurial preparations held to make the body proof against decay, with named substances, processes and durations."@en ;
    skos:note "Among the most testable claims available anywhere in the corpus, and testable in two directions at once: the promised outcome and the toxicity of the means."@en,
        "Read in this run at the wording, 2026-09-25: Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi or Ocean of Indian Chemistry and Alchemy, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, in the compiler's own English, read continuously from the preface through chapters I to VIII and the glossary to the closing advertisement. Not read: the Sanskrit verses the volume prints above each passage, which were not consulted and on which nothing here turns; and volumes II to V of the same work, which treat sulphur, mica, the metals and the gems. Not opened at all: the Rasārṇava, the Rasahṛdayatantra and the Rasaratnasamuccaya, which the registration named. They stand below as work identifiers and appear from now on in queries/unopened-works.rq, which is the state the question follows from and the reason no issue is written for it."@en,
        "The compiler's own dating is inside the work and is not a dating in the sense this holding uses. The introduction puts the beginning of the present kalpa at about 1,600,000,000 years ago and the composition of the book in the year 5026 of the kali age; the preface assigns two works to Rāma of the Rāmāyaṇa and one to Rāvaṇa. It is entered here as a property of the witness so that no node below is read as carrying a date."@en,
        "The contact route stays at documented and this reading did nothing to move it. The transmission shares its sphere and its vocabulary with the material this holding already carries from India, and the claim of an external route — that the oral ingestion of cinnabar came to India from China — is a claim of modern scholarship and not of this work, which derives its science from Śiva and dates it to the beginning of the present kalpa. A witness from here raises the count of files and not the count of independent witnesses."@en,
        "What kind of witness this work is, and it is the first thing a reader of the nodes below needs. It is a compilation made in 1926 by a practising physician, principal of a college of Ayurveda in Calcutta, who says he learnt what is not in the books from an ascetic preceptor and arranged the rest out of the existing books, which he calls incomplete, incoherent, incorrect and in many cases misleading. It prints Sanskrit verse and its own English beside it and attributes no verse to any work. It is therefore a station of the transmission and not a text edition, and every node here is a claim of that station. Whether the same claim stands in a work of the eleventh or the sixteenth century is a question this file does not answer and does not pretend to."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi,
        pmw:Rasahrdayatantra,
        pmw:Rasaratnasamuccaya,
        pmw:Rasarnava ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:RasaJalaNidhi ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Rasārṇava; Govindabhagavatpāda, Rasahṛdayatantra; Vāgbhaṭa, Rasaratnasamuccaya" .

pmw:RasaJalaNidhi a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Rasa-Jala-Nidhi"@de,
        "Rasa-Jala-Nidhi"@en ;
    skos:note "A compilation that names no source for any verse it prints. That is a property of this work and is the reason no node in this file cites a classical title."@en,
        "Opened on 2026-09-25 at the whole of its first volume, Calcutta 1926, in the compiler's own English. Volumes II to V of the same work carry sulphur, mica, the metals, the gems and the poisons, and every one of the nodes below that says see volume II is a forward reference of the compiler's own; they are the cheapest continuation from here and need no new acquisition."@en .

pm:Situating a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Verorten"@de,
        "Situating"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "The placement of a claim, a practice or a capacity at a value on an awareness axis. The node records the compiler's identification of a described condition with a coordinate, not the description itself."@en ;
    skos:note "Normally attested by pm:compilerInference. Textual attestation is available only where the source itself draws the distinction the axis draws — the Sufi division of what descends from what is attained is the clear case — and even then the wording that carries it belongs in pm:situationGround."@en .

pm:firstPersonReport a pm:Attesting ;
    rdfs:label "Erfahrungsbericht"@de,
        "First-person report"@en ;
    skos:definition "An experiential account given in the first person by the one who had it, recorded once."@en .

pm:prescribedOrder a pm:PrerequisiteStrength ;
    rdfs:label "Angeordnete Reihenfolge"@de,
        "Prescribed order"@en ;
    skos:definition "The source instructs that one comes before the other, without saying what follows from disregarding it."@en .

pmw:CarakaSamhita a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Caraka Samhita"@de,
        "Caraka Samhita"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened on 2026-09-21 at one lesson, Cikitsasthana I in its four sections, in the English of Avinash Chandra Kaviratna, Calcutta 1904-1905. The remainder of the work was not read. The same translation carries the whole of the Samhita and is the cheapest continuation: a run that wants the vajikarana lesson, the Sutrasthana groups of drugs this lesson refers to by name, or the Vimanasthana account of how a physician is to be judged, opens the same volume and needs no new acquisition."@en .

pm:Testing a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Prüfen"@de,
        "Testing"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "An ongoing attempt to fail a claim under a stated condition. A protocol that names no condition under which the claim would be refuted is not a test of it."@en ;
    skos:note "An instance exists only where somebody has taken the examination up, and its absence is therefore informative rather than empty: it says that no procedure has been taken up, not that the claim has been examined and found wanting. A stub for every claim in the collection would double the graph and say nothing the absence does not already say."@en .

pm:placesEntered a pm:CoverageState ;
    rdfs:label "Stellen eingetragen"@de,
        "Places entered"@en ;
    skos:definition "At least one passage has been read in a named edition and entered as a node. This is the state in which a tradition begins to witness."@en .

pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Papyrus von London und Leiden (PDM xiv)"@de,
        "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv)"@en ;
    skos:note "One manuscript in two museums, of the third century of this era, twenty-nine columns on the recto and thirty-three short columns on the verso. Opened on 2026-09-18 in the edition of Griffith and Thompson, volume I of 1904, which prints transliteration, translation and notes; the translation was read throughout and the transliteration was not used. The editors state that the language makes it difficult to believe that any part of the work in its present redaction is more than a century or two older than the papyrus itself."@en,
        "The nodes read off it stand in traditions/demotic-magical-papyri.ttl."@en,
        "What the reading covered and what it left: the whole of the translation of the recto, columns I to XXIX, and of the verso, columns I to XXXIII, was read at the wording. Not read: the transliteration on the facing pages and the philological notes beneath both, except where a note bears on a passage entered; the introduction, apart from its account of the manuscript and its table of contents; and the glossary volume of 1909, which was not obtained. The digitised copy used is an optical transcription and its rendering of the transliterated demotic is not trustworthy at the level of single letters, which is why no node in this holding reproduces a demotic form from it."@en .

pm:statedNecessity a pm:PrerequisiteStrength ;
    rdfs:label "Ausdrücklich gesetzte Notwendigkeit"@de,
        "Stated necessity"@en ;
    skos:definition "The source says what fails to happen without the prior step: that the later step is not possible, or does not arise, or turns harmful."@en .

pmt:Ayurvedic a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Ayurvedische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Ayurvedic transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana I.1.1-15, in the English of Avinash Chandra Kaviratna, Charaka-Samhita translated into English, part XXXIII, Calcutta 1904, pp. 1015-1017" ;
    skos:definition "The Indian medical transmission, including the rasayana section that claims restoration of vigour and extension of life by regimen, by compounded drugs and by conduct."@en ;
    skos:note "One thing about the drugs, because it bounds every node below. The translation identifies the plants by nineteenth-century botanical names and the translator marks several as unidentifiable in his own day and some as unidentifiable already in the compiler's. Nothing in this file turns on the identification of a plant, and no node here should be read as asserting one."@en,
        "Read in the run of 2026-09-21 at the wording: the Caraka Samhita, Cikitsasthana, Lesson I, the lesson on rasayana, in all four of its sections — Abhayamalakiya, Pranakamiya, Karapracitiya and Ayurvedasamutthaniya — in the English of Avinash Chandra Kaviratna, parts XXXIII to XXXV, Calcutta 1904-1905, pages 1015 to 1070. Read continuously and in full, from the definition of medicine at the head of the lesson to its closing colophon. Not read: Lesson II on vajikarana, which begins on page 1071 and was opened far enough to establish where the lesson read here ends; the remaining twenty-eight lessons of the Cikitsasthana; and the Sutra-, Nidana-, Vimana-, Sarira-, Indriya-, Kalpa- and Siddhisthana. Not opened at all: the Susruta Samhita and Vagbhata's Astangahrdaya, which the registration names beside the Caraka."@en,
        "Second reading, 2026-10-02, at the second of the three works this registration names. The Susruta Samhita was opened at Sarirasthana VI and VIII in the English of Bhishagratna, vol. II, Calcutta 1911, and both chapters were read continuously; what they yielded stands at the end of this file and the harvest note is at prima-materia#829. The coverage state is unchanged at places entered, because a tradition whose three named works are now one read lesson and two read chapters out of three works is not a harvested corpus. Vagbhata is still unopened."@en,
        "The contact route stays at documented and this run did nothing to change it. The lesson names its own descent inside itself — from Brahman to Daksa to the twin Asvins to Indra to the seers on the Himavat — which is a claim of the transmission about itself and not a route in the sense this holding uses; and the Indian sphere is the one in which this holding is already densest, so a witness from here raises the count of files and not the count of independent witnesses. It was taken from the register for its examinability and for nothing else."@en,
        "What kind of witness this work is. It is a handbook for a practitioner, arranged as a teaching by Atreya Punarvasu to his pupil Agnivesa and redacted by Caraka, and the redaction is visible: the fourth section retells the origin of the science in a form the translator marks as differing from the account in the first lesson of the Sutrasthana of the same work. The colophon of this lesson names Agnivesa and Caraka and not Drdhabala, whose supplement covers other lessons of the same Cikitsasthana; that is the transmission's own division of its layers and it is entered here as such, not as a dating."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:Astangahrdaya,
        pmw:CarakaSamhita,
        pmw:SusrutaSamhita ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:CarakaSamhita ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Caraka Samhita; Susruta Samhita; Vagbhata, Astangahrdaya" .

pm:contactRouteDocumented a pm:ContactRoute ;
    rdfs:label "Kontaktweg belegt"@de,
        "Contact route documented"@en ;
    skos:definition "A route of transmission to traditions already held is documented. Agreement with them counts as reception."@en .

pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Griechische und demotische Zauberpapyri"@de,
        "Greek and demotic magical papyri"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.475-829, in the translation of G. R. S. Mead, A Mithriac Ritual (London 1907)" ;
    skos:definition "The working handbooks of late antique Egypt: procedures with materials, hours, purity requirements, spoken formulae and stated signs of success."@en ;
    skos:note "A second work of this corpus was opened on 2026-09-18 and its nodes stand in traditions/demotic-magical-papyri.ttl: the Papyrus of London and Leiden, read throughout in the translation of Griffith and Thompson of 1904. The registration named the demotic papyri from the start and nothing of them had been entered, so the tradition stood at pm:placesEntered on one piece of one Greek papyrus. The works are named as identifiers here from that date, which is what makes an unopened work of an opened tradition visible to a query at all (prima-materia#737)."@en,
        "Dieterich says himself where he is certain that the received text already carried its own names, and the list is short: the vowel groups for the seven planets, the point where the gods are appeased, the opening of the disk, the appearance of the sun god, the protective cry at the gates, and the adjuration that the god remain in the operator's soul. Those places are the ones on which a reconstruction of the sounds may be attempted; the rest of the barbarous names he holds to be interchangeable stock that the magicians supplied from their own apparatus, so that even a perfect reading of the papyrus would not recover what the rite said there."@en,
        "Going behind Mead was done and lands on a third layer rather than on the papyrus. His source is Dieterich's edition, opened here in the second edition of 1910; Dieterich's source is Wessely's first reading of Paris papyrus 574. What Mead removed, Dieterich had already set in small type as the magicians' insertions, on his own judgement and not on any mark in the manuscript, and Mead's obeli are Dieterich's typography carried into English. The distinction between the liturgy and its magical redaction is therefore an editorial reconstruction from 1903, and every node in this file that speaks of what belongs to the rite is standing on it."@en,
        "The name is a modern label twice over. Dieterich called the piece a liturgy in 1903; Mead objects that a liturgy has several participants while this is a rite for one person alone, and whether it is Mithraic at all is disputed in the scholarship. Neither question is settled here."@en,
        "Two properties of the translation first used bear on the nodes that cite it, and Mead states both himself. He removes the barbarous names he judges to be later insertions by an Egyptian magical school and marks the rest with obeli, so most of the names in the ritual are not in the text read; the one long vowel chain that is printed survives because he holds it to belong to the ritual. And he renders one of the two prescribed sounds as puff rather than pop because he takes it to be connected with breath, which is a reading and not a translation."@en,
        "What was read: the ascent ritual at PGM IV.475-829, in the translation of G. R. S. Mead, A Mithriac Ritual (Echoes from the Gnosis VI, London and Benares 1907), public domain. Nothing else of the corpus is opened, and this is a single piece out of a codex of thirty-six pages."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden,
        pmw:PgmIv,
        pmw:PgmXII,
        pmw:PgmXIII ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Papyri Graecae Magicae and Papyri Demoticae Magicae, including the ascent ritual at PGM IV.475-829" .

pm:Naming a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Benennen"@de,
        "Naming"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "The act, within a tradition, of naming an addressable being: the name as a received work gives it, together with what that work takes the bearer to be."@en ;
    skos:note "Not a substance class in disguise. What is held is the act of naming and what a transmission says under that name, not a being asserted to exist. The graph reports that a work names Asael and calls him a watcher; it asserts no watcher."@en,
        "Three things must not collapse into one, and this class holds only the first two. The bearer and the name form belong here. The operative claim — that invoking under this name does something — stays a pm:Yielding whose practice is the invocation, because that claim needs its own falsifying condition and its own protocol."@en,
        "Variants between recensions are not smoothed into a lead form. Where two editions give different names for what a reader takes to be one bearer, that is a pm:Disputing node, by the same rule SPEC §10 sets for diverging text: an averaged node reports a witness nobody gives."@en,
        "pm:ascribedTo deliberately keeps no rdfs:range. Where the bearer is a named being it points at an instance of this class; where it is humanity before a stated event, or a class of beings the source does not name, it points elsewhere, and a range would have forced those cases out of the record or into a name nobody gave them."@en .

pmt:EgyptianRitual a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Pharaonisch-ägyptisches Ritual"@de,
        "Pharaonic Egyptian ritual"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 137A, rubric, Papyrus of Nu (British Museum 10.477), in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: An English Translation of the Chapters, Hymns, etc., of the Theban Recension, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. II, pp. 417-418" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of heka as an operative power held by gods, kings and the transfigured dead, carried in mortuary and temple literature."@en ;
    skos:note "Not opened: the Pyramid Texts, the Coffin Texts and Papyrus Westcar, which the registration names as parts of the principal corpus. They stand at the work identifiers as unopened rather than under the coverage state."@en,
        "Read in this run at the wording, on 2026-09-19. The Theban recension in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: An English Translation of the Chapters, Hymns, etc., of the Theban Recension, three volumes, London 1901, whose base manuscript changes from chapter to chapter and is named in each heading. Read closely: chapter 30B with its rubric (vol. I); the three rubrics of chapter 64, the short version and the two of the long version (vol. I); chapters 83 and 86 with their rubrics and the surrounding transformation spells (vol. II); the doorkeeper dialogue and the rubric of chapter 125 (vol. II); chapter 137A with its rubric and the four appended brick rubrics (vol. II). Beside them the Papyrus of Ani in Budge's English of 1895, plate III, the weighing of the heart with the verdict of Thoth, and the appendix rubric to chapter 76. The translation of Peter le Page Renouf and Édouard Naville, The Egyptian Book of the Dead, London 1904, was searched throughout and read at the places where it disagrees with Budge."@en,
        "The contact route stays at disputed and this run did not settle it. What it did establish is which question the dispute is about. The demotic magical manuscripts of the Roman period share the trade, the ritual technique and part of the technical vocabulary of the pharaonic priesthood, and the graph holds them separately; what is not established anywhere the run looked is a textual filiation from the mortuary literature into the later magical corpora, and the strongest argument on the other side is that a priest writing for a hellenistic readership had reason to perform an expected Egyptianness. The dispute is about the direction and the mode of the contact and not about whether there was any."@en,
        "The edition is the whole citation here and not a formality, because the two public-domain translations disagree in ways that change the claim. At chapter 137A Budge has the secrecy clause admit the father and the son — perform this before no human being except thine own self, or thy father, or thy son — while Renouf, from the Turin text and under chapter 161, has it exclude them: reveal it to no one, may he be thy father or thy son, except thyself. A node that gave the place without the edition would assert one of these against the other by accident. Every source string below therefore names translator, volume and page."@en,
        "What kind of witness this is, and it is an unusual one. There is no Book of the Dead in the sense of a work: no manuscript carries all the chapters, the selection was made by the family and, as the editor of 1904 says in his introduction, by the number of chapters corresponding to the price they wished to pay. A fixed order appears only under the Saite kings, and the numbering used everywhere goes back to Lepsius of 1842 on one late Turin papyrus. A claim read here is a claim of one manuscript in one recension, and the sources say which."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:BookOfTheDead,
        pmw:CoffinTexts,
        pmw:PapyrusWestcar,
        pmw:PyramidTexts ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Pyramid Texts; Coffin Texts; Book of the Dead; Papyrus Westcar; the demotic magical papyrus of London and Leiden" .

pmw:BookOfTheDead a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Das Totenbuch"@de,
        "The Book of the Dead"@en ;
    skos:note "Budge's translations are the weakest part of this reading and the file says so where it matters. The criticism is old and specific: contemporaries called his books filled with egregious errors, and the modern verdict is that the hieroglyphic editions remain usable while the English is not. Two places in this file rest on him alone because no public-domain alternative exists, and both carry a note. The standard modern translations — Faulkner 1972, Allen 1974, Hornung 1979, Quirke 2013 — are under copyright and were not obtained."@en,
        "Not a work in the ordinary sense and the file treats it accordingly. Chapter numbers are Lepsius's of 1842, taken from one late Turin papyrus; Naville's edition of 1886 extends them from the manuscripts of the New Kingdom; Budge follows Naville and subdivides further, so that 30A and 30B, 81A and 81B, 136A and 136B, 137A and 137B are his divisions. A place is therefore given here as chapter number plus manuscript plus translation plus page, and nothing less will locate it."@en,
        "One place in this file is given without a page number, and the reason is a property of the copy and not of the work: in the scanned text of Budge 1895 the running heads are legible on some pages and not on others, and the rubric to chapter 76 falls in a stretch where they are not. It is located instead by chapter, by its printed heading Appendix 1, and by the chapter it stands immediately before. SPEC §10 measures a place by findability and not by numbering, and a page number guessed by interpolation would have looked better and been worth less."@en,
        "Opened on 2026-09-19 in two public-domain English translations: Budge 1901, three volumes, the Theban recension, base manuscripts named chapter by chapter, dominant among them the Papyrus of Nu, the Papyrus of Ani and the Papyrus of Nebseni; and Budge 1895, the Papyrus of Ani alone, with text, transliteration and translation. Renouf and Naville 1904, which translates from Naville's synoptic edition and refers to its manuscripts by sigla rather than by name, was used as the second translation and as the check on Budge."@en .

pmw:HarbaDeMoshe a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Ḥarba de-Moshe, das Schwert des Mose"@de,
        "Ḥarba de-Moshe, the Sword of Moses"@en ;
    skos:note "Opened on 2026-09-24 in the English of Moses Gaster, London 1896, made from a single manuscript of his own collection, now British Library Or. 10678. The work has three parts: a framing part with the heavenly hierarchy, the revelation to Moses and the conditions of use; the Sword itself, a list of names in no identifiable language; and a part in Aramaic with the operations that refer back to the names. The first and the third were read entire. The second is not printed in the translation, only described in square brackets, and cannot be read from this edition."@en,
        "The division of part three into a hundred and thirty-six numbered items is Gaster's, made so that the operations could be set beside the corresponding stretches of the name-list, and it is his numbering that the later literature cites. The source strings of the operations below therefore give that number and say whose it is. It is the locus and not a second identity scheme in the sense SPEC §11 forbids, and it is the one thing two independent runs would both produce."@en,
        "The edition has a critical apparatus in no sense of the word. Gaster prints one manuscript, records no variants, and nowhere states the grounds of his readings; at several points his own translation carries a question mark where he could not read the word. A critical Hebrew edition on a fuller manuscript exists — Yuval Harari, Jerusalem 1997 — and was not obtainable in this run. Where Gaster marks a word illegible the node below says so."@en,
        "Two defects of the copy read, both properties of the copy and not of the work. Its English part is a transcription rather than a scan of the print and replaces every mystical name with a bare X, which Gaster set in Hebrew letters inside the English sentence; and it carries no page numbers. A reader who needs pages needs the print: Gaster, Studies and Texts, vol. 1 (London: Maggs Bros., 1925-28), pp. 288-337, where the translation begins at p. 311."@en .

pmt:JewishPracticalMagic a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Jüdische Praxismagie"@de,
        "Jewish practical magic"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, introduction §§ 1 and 8-15 and texts 1-42, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913)" ;
    skos:definition "The operative Jewish transmission of adjurations, amulets and bowls, distinct from the speculative literature and older than it in its written witnesses."@en ;
    skos:note "Of the four works the principal corpus names, one now stands opened here and one stands opened under pmt:Hekhalot in traditions/hekhalot.ttl, which is where the Ḥarba de-Moshe was read entire. The Sefer ha-Razim and the Cairo Genizah magical fragments remain unopened and are carried as no work identifier, because a work identifier without a read place would report an opening that did not happen."@en,
        "Raised from pm:corpusNamed on the first passages actually read, which are in the fourth of the four works its principal corpus names. What was read at the wording: the whole of the introduction's third part, on the purpose of the bowls, the exorcists, the clients and the incantations, and its fourth part on the age of the bowls; and the translation and commentary of every one of the forty-two texts. What was not read line by line: the three glossaries, the general index, the register of the bowls and the forty-one plates, and the two parts of the introduction on the sources hitherto published and on script and language, which were read where a question led."@en,
        "The clientele is mixed and the magic is indifferent to it. The great majority of the client names are Persian; there are two Syriac Christian names, one Greek, probable Indian ones, and few typical Jewish ones, and the editor concludes that a Jewish-Aramaic text implies neither a Jewish exorcist nor Jewish clients. A Jewish text, a Syriac text and a Mandaic text of the same place name the same persons and families."@en,
        "What this corpus is for the holding, and it is not its quantity. It is the one body of operative material the graph holds that was excavated rather than copied: the bowls were found in situ in ruined houses at Nippur, most of them upside down in the ground, so the praxis they prescribe is attested by the position of the object and not only by its text. Against that, the operator's share is the smallest in the holding. The incantation is nearly the whole of it, and the volume says so in as many words."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Sefer ha-Razim; Ḥarba de-Moshe; the Cairo Genizah magical fragments; the Aramaic incantation bowls" .

pm:compilerInference a pm:Attesting ;
    rdfs:label "Schluss des Bearbeiters"@de,
        "Compiler inference"@en ;
    skos:definition "A conclusion drawn by an editor or compiler that the source does not state."@en ;
    skos:note "Admissible as attestation of an ordering act, never of the claim being ordered. Enforced by pm:AttributionShape."@en .

pmt:Hekhalot a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Hekhalot- und Merkava-Literatur"@de,
        "Hekhalot and Merkavah literature"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hekhalot Rabbati, chapters 26, 30 and 31, trans. Morton Smith, corrected by Gershom Scholem, ed. Don Karr, against the Hebrew of the Wertheimer recension" ;
    skos:definition "The Jewish transmission of a descent through palaces whose gatekeepers test the descender, and of an adjuration by which knowledge of the Law is obtained without study."@en ;
    skos:note "How old the practice behind these texts is, and whether there is a practice behind them at all, is the open question of the field and is not decided here. The oldest manuscripts are of the thirteenth century and later, at least half a millennium after the composition anyone claims for the material. Two positions stand against each other and neither was read at the wording. See prima-materia#495."@en,
        "Read again on 2026-09-24, and this time the whole of the Sword of Moses and not its opening instructions. What was read: the framing part entire, and part three entire, the hundred and thirty-six numbered operations. Part two, the list of names the operations refer to, is not printed in Gaster's translation and could not be read. The reading corrected three statements this file already carried; the corrections stand at the nodes and say so."@en,
        "Registered at corpus named until this file was written, and moved here on the first passages actually read. What was read at the wording: Hekhalot Rabbati, chapters 26, 30 and 31 in the Smith and Scholem translation edited by Karr, against the Hebrew of the Wertheimer recension; and the opening instructions of the Sword of Moses in Gaster's translation. Not opened: the Sar Torah paragraphs that carry the numbers, Maaseh Merkavah, Hekhalot Zutarti, Shiur Qomah, Sefer ha-Razim and 3 Enoch. The reasons stand in the issues named in the harvest note of prima-materia#492."@en,
        "The corpus is carried as work identifiers from 2026-09-24. Two works are named: the Hekhalot Rabbati, opened at chapters 26, 30 and 31, and the Sword of Moses, now opened entire except for its name-list. The remaining five works of the principal corpus are named in the string and carried as no identifier, because none of them has been opened and a work identifier without a read place would report an opening that did not happen."@en,
        "The paragraph numbering of this literature is Schäfer's, from an edition that could not be reached, and the translations read carry it in brackets. Where this file gives a chapter it is the chapter of the digital witness actually read, and where it gives a paragraph the paragraph is taken from that witness and not from the edition itself. That is a weaker citation than the rest of the graph uses and it is marked rather than smoothed. See prima-materia#493."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:HarbaDeMoshe,
        pmw:HekhalotRabbati ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:HekhalotRabbati ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Hekhalot Rabbati; Hekhalot Zutarti; the Sar Torah material; Maaseh Merkavah; Shiur Qomah; 3 Enoch; the Sword of Moses" .

pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Aramäische Beschwörungstexte aus Nippur"@de,
        "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur"@en ;
    skos:note "The copy read is the Internet Archive full text of the identifier aramaicincantati00mont, digitised in 2017 with funding from the Getty Research Institute. Three further copies of the same volume were fetched and measured; this one has the best optical reading of the running heads, 202 of them in an unbroken sequence from p. 8 to p. 326, and the file runs to the last plate, plate XLI."@en,
        "The dating is the editor's and is a terminus ad quem only: not later than the sixth or the beginning of the seventh century, approximately 600 A. D., argued from the Cufic coins in the upper stratum of the Jewish houses at Nippur, from the absence of any name of Arabic origin among the many Persian, Jewish, Syriac Christian, Greek and probable Indian ones, and against the epigraphical datings of Chwolson, Schwab and Nöldeke, which the volume reports and rejects. Those earlier positions are modern scholarship and enter the holding only as evidence at an examination, never as a source here."@en,
        "The edition, copied from the title page inside the full text and not from the archive's catalogue line: University of Pennsylvania, The Museum, Publications of the Babylonian Section, vol. III. Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, by James A. Montgomery, Professor at the Philadelphia Divinity School and Assistant Professor at the University of Pennsylvania. Eckley Brinton Coxe Junior Fund. Philadelphia, published by the University Museum, 1913."@en,
        "The numbering of the texts and the line numbers inside them are Montgomery's, and the source strings below give them because they are the locus of this corpus and the one thing two independent readings would both produce. The bowls also carry their museum numbers, which the volume prints beside each text."@en,
        "The work is a corpus and an edition at once. Forty bowls from Nippur are published with transliteration, translation and notes as texts 1 to 40, with an inscribed human skull as text 41 and a form of the Lilith legend as text 42, whose original was no longer in the Museum when the volume was made. The Museum catalogue counted over 150 numbers of this class; the rest were fragments, too defaced, too illiterate, or not Semitic."@en,
        "Two statements of the praxis stand in the volume as the editor's report of Pognon's bowls and not as a place in a Nippur text: that the bowl used was a new one, and that the sorcerer sat upon an uncleft rock. Pognon's Inscriptions mandaites was not opened in this run, so no node below carries either; both are named in the harvest note as the next place to go."@en,
        "What that copy cannot give, and it is the greater part of the volume. The Aramaic, Syriac and Mandaic of the texts is set in three non-Latin scripts, and the optical reading has rendered all three in look-alike Latin characters: the file holds 0.9925 ASCII and not one character in the Hebrew block U+0590 to U+05FF, none in Syriac U+0700 to U+074F, none in Greek. Apparatus, translation and commentary are usable at the wording; the original language is not present on this route at all. Every node below therefore cites the translation, and no node carries a term in the language of the bowls."@en .

pm:Searching a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Suchen"@de,
        "Searching"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "A record of a question put to a named corpus and of what came back: a place, a contradiction, silence, or a corpus that could not be reached. It states what was searched and asserts nothing about the subject matter."@en ;
    skos:note "Distinct from pm:counterSearch, which is a state on a protocol and asks whether the work opposing a piece of evidence was looked for. This asks whether a transmission was put a question at all, and it stands on its own rather than as a field of something else."@en,
        "Like pm:Testing, an instance exists only where somebody carried the search, and its absence stays informative: no node means nobody asked, not that the corpus is silent. Stubbing every unasked question would fill the holding with protocols about nothing and destroy the very distinction the class was built for."@en,
        "Unlike pm:Testing, it needs no object in the graph. What was searched for is a literal, because the point of the commonest case is precisely that the thing sought is not in the holding. A property pointing at a node would make the negative finding unwritable."@en .

pm:Tradition a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Tradition"@de,
        "Tradition"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "A knowledge tradition understood as an ongoing act of transmission rather than a static corpus."@en .

pm:Presupposing a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Voraussetzen"@de,
        "Presupposing"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "The claim, made within a tradition, that one step of a path presupposes another: that a practice, state or capacity is not reached except by way of something else."@en ;
    skos:note "A node and not an edge, for the same reason as pm:Attributing and pm:Yielding. A prerequisite claim differs enormously in what carries it, and the difference is not visible on an edge. It matters whether a text sets an order or a reader inferred one from the order of the chapters."@en .

pm:Cautioning a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Warnen"@de,
        "Cautioning"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "The act, within a tradition, of warning against a capacity, a practice, or the pursuit of either."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as a node rather than as commentary, because what a tradition warns against is part of what it teaches. A record that carries only the capacities and not the cautions misreports the tradition it claims to hold."@en .

pm:Yielding a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Hervorbringen"@de,
        "Yielding"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "The claim, made within a tradition, that a named practice produces a named capacity."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as a node and not as an edge for the same reason pm:Attributing is: only a node can carry its own source, its own mode of attestation, and a protocol that tries to fail it. A bare edge would make every such claim look alike, whatever it rests on, and would leave nothing for pm:Testing to point at. The question of which capacities a practice actually develops is answerable only where the pairing itself is addressable."@en .

pm:Practicing a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Praktizieren"@de,
        "Practicing"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "An operative procedure enacted within a tradition."@en .

pm:Conceptualizing a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Begriffsbildung"@de,
        "Conceptualizing"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "The formation of a concept as an act within awareness; instances of this class are concept nodes such as pmc:Pleroma or pmc:Hen."@en .

pm:textualAttestation a pm:Attesting ;
    rdfs:label "Textzeugnis"@de,
        "Textual attestation"@en ;
    skos:definition "The claim stands in a received work, at a passage that can be cited and read independently of this graph."@en .

